ior form of its reception; therefore it is neither an objective receptacle nor a private fiction.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MME-03","CL_5P0L_05:BSD-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Memory and Scripture convert absence into present efficacy: the one makes traces of life operate, the other the Word through temporal words.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GA-04","CL_5P0L_05:GA-11","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Charity is the higher form of attention, because it simultaneously directs the objects of desire, meanings of the text, and works of the community toward a common end.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GA-08","CL_5P0L_05:XD-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-06","text":"Confession and exegesis are mechanisms of recursive correction: each places action under truth, excludes false closure, and opens broader reception.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GNX-01","CL_5P0L_05:ALT-B05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Grace constitutes created action, but responsibility for the same action is retained; dependence and imputability therefore cohere without a fully formalized mode of cooperation.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GA-10","CL_5P0L_05:DBN-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-08","text":"The creative Word and Christ the mediator effect one direction: the donation of being continues into formative calling, mortal reconciliation, and return to justice.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GA-12","CL_5P0L_05:GA-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-09","text":"Multiplicity is the locus of bifurcation: separated from source and end it disperses; ordered under unity of truth it constitutes the fecundity of things and understandings.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:DBN-04","CL_5P0L_05:GA-10","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-10","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-10","text":"Present works contain their own obsolescence, because they begin healing but are ordered toward peace and Sabbath, which close succession.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BAS-08","CL_5P0L_05:GA-13","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"The asymmetry of creation and grace generates one law of participation: as being is given, so self-knowledge, formation, and perseverance are received.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BSD-05","CL_5P0L_05:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"The subject is a temporally distributed capacity, whose identity is composed from appetite, memory, attention, signs, and the orientation of love.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BSD-01","CL_5P0L_05:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"History is not neutral succession, but the apparatus through which the will is reformed, works pass away, and the creature is prepared for a non-temporal state.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BSD-03","CL_5P0L_05:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"The Church externalizes interior gathering, because the community of confession, interpretation, and distribution of means corporeally exercises the same formation that the mind receives.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:CGD-09","CL_5P0L_05:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Ontological plurality and hermeneutical plurality become homologous: each presupposes a determinate capacity for receiving several true forms.","refs":["CL_5P0L_05:XD-01","CL_5P0L_05:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Evil is removed from the field of substances and transferred into the direction of created good, by which attention usurps a means or charity refers it to the source.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GNX-05","CL_5P0L_05:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Salvation is the reconfiguration of all created temporality, not flight from it: mediation assumes succession, orders it, and leads it to irreversible rest.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BSD-06","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-08","text":"Participated knowledge joins certainty of principle with provisionality of application; truth remains stable, while understanding and community are corrigible.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BSD-07","CL_5P0L_05:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-09","text":"Aporia becomes a positive theological discipline, because ignorance of powers, time, and causality is transferred from the simulation of comprehension to confession of dependence.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:XD-10","CL_5P0L_05:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Opacity generates confession, confession seeks illumination, illumination opens a broader capacity, and renewed capacity requires a new confession.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:RLE-01","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Gift constitutes the capacity to act, operation manifests wound and receptivity, and this manifestation amplifies reception of further grace.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:RLE-04","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Created good draws attention; disordered love turns it into an end; dispersion increases; mercy requested restores the order of love.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:RLE-02","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Sound is referred to interior judgment, judgment seeks the Word, the Word opens several true meanings, and these in turn demand examination by charity.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:RLE-05","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Interior formation generates community, community recapitulates the order of creation, and recapitulation again interprets interior condition under a broader history.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:CGD-09","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"Plurality of meanings arouses examination of truth; exclusion of the false forms the concord of charity; concord produces new semantic fecundity.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:DBN-04","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Expectation passes through attention into memory, repeated transitions produce the history of formation, and history increases desire for its consummation.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:RLE-03","CL_5P0L_05:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"Divine unity is a recursive attractor within time, but rest without evening is the terminal attractor that finally closes the recursion of works.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:RLE-07","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"God self-sufficient + creation from nothing → universal dependence → impossibility of ontological and epistemic autonomy.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GA-02","CL_5P0L_05:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Capacity not yet formed + calling of the Word → reception of form → ordered change → created time.","refs":["CL_5P0L_05:GA-04","CL_5P0L_05:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Temporal creature + memory + attention + expectation → distributed identity → distention or history of formation.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BSD-01","CL_5P0L_05:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Exterior sign or Scripture → interior judgment → multiple true meanings → exclusion of the false → concord of charity.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:RLE-05","CL_5P0L_05:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Created good + disordered attention → capture → dispersion → confession of limit → petition for illumination.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:RLE-02","CL_5P0L_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Grace + mediation + participatory causality → created action → interior and ecclesial gathering → renewed capacity for reception.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GA-10","CL_5P0L_05:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Temporal works + intention toward one → healing begun → future peace → Sabbath without evening.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BAS-08","CL_5P0L_05:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Divine sufficiency → non-chronological origin → created capacity → formative calling → ordered changes → time.","refs":["CL_5P0L_05:BAS-04","CL_5P0L_05:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Signs and traces → interior judgment → opacity and distention → confession → illumination → charity → common formation.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BAS-04","CL_5P0L_05:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Ordered multiplicity → temporal works → gathering toward one → mediation of Christ → future peace → rest without evening.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GA-11","CL_5P0L_05:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-01","text":"Ontological dependence becomes the common map of being, knowing, willing, interpreting, and persevering.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:CGD-08","CL_5P0L_05:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-02","text":"Interiority changes from a space of autonomy into a place of reception, where the ontology of the creature and the discipline of interpretation intersect.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GA-05","CL_5P0L_05:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Time is reclassified from a universal container into the effect of change and the distribution of present operations.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MME-03","CL_5P0L_05:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Multiplicity ceases to be either pure dispersion or error, because the order of the end converts it into ontological and hermeneutical fecundity.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:DBN-04","CL_5P0L_05:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Authority is transferred from possession of meaning into corrigible ministry under truth and canon.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GNX-04","CL_5P0L_05:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Evil is reclassified from a property of things into a mode of direction, by which created good is turned away from the source and makes attention captive.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MME-04","CL_5P0L_05:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-07","text":"The Church becomes the historical extension of interior formation and the place where the grammar of creation can be recapitulated through signs, ministries, and understandings.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:CGD-09","CL_5P0L_05:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-08","text":"The goodness of temporal things is assessed not from their perpetuity, but from their ordered passage to fruition and rest.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:GNX-08","CL_5P0L_05:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level presents confession, memory, temptation, creation, the words of Genesis, the Church, and Sabbath as distinct objects.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:ALT-B01","CL_5P0L_05:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level composes capacity, memory, attention, form, change, charity, and grace into a mechanism of reception and gathering.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:ALT-B02","CL_5P0L_05:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level transfers the contest over autonomy or a single meaning into a discipline of dependence, true plurality, and corrigible authority.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:ALT-B03","CL_5P0L_05:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level establishes God as eternal cause, the creature as mutable reception, and history as the order of formation.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:ALT-B04","CL_5P0L_05:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level accomplishes by return, variation, and negation what it affirms: it transfers the reader from dispersion to intention and from closure to corrigibility.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:ALT-B05","CL_5P0L_05:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Time oscillates between affection of the mind, effect of change, and medium of the history of salvation; the ontological relation of these three states is not fully resolved.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:FL-03","CL_5P0L_05:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Eternal act produces created succession, but the passage from non-chronological origin to ordered effects resists formalization.","refs":["CL_5P0L_05:FL-03","CL_5P0L_05:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Grace constitutes every good capacity, while the will retains responsibility; the nexus of participatory causality and imputability remains incomplete.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:FL-05","CL_5P0L_05:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-04","text":"Interior truth provides a certain criterion to an opaque subject, while participated and corrigible ecclesial authority distributes the same judgment socially.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:FL-02","CL_5P0L_05:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Plurality of meanings is limited by authority, Spirit, and charity, but no full rule of compatibility among true meanings is exhibited.","refs":["CL_5P0L_05:FL-05","CL_5P0L_05:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-06","text":"The analogy between formlessness and distention is fruitful, but threatens conflation of ontological capacity with moral fault.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MP-02","CL_5P0L_05:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-07","text":"The mediation of Christ with the creative Word closes the global structure, although the necessity of this conjunction is not deduced from the phenomenology of time alone.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:FL-06","CL_5P0L_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-08","text":"The creature remains naturally mutable, but terminal rest excludes the return of change; the mode of stabilization without abolition of nature is fragile.","refs":["CL_5P0L_05:FL-02","CL_5P0L_05:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Formlessness changes from privative capacity into a figure of aversion, then is joined with distention as a need for gathering.","refs":["CL_5P0L_05:MP-01","CL_5P0L_04:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Attention changes from the measure of succession into normative intention, which under charity also governs interpretation and ontological movement.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MP-03","CL_5P0L_05:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Memory changes from the operation of retaining into a model of mediation, which Scripture and community amplify toward the presence of the Word.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MP-01","CL_5P0L_05:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Time changes from the measurement of interior passage into a pedagogical interval of faith, action, and common institution.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:BSD-02","CL_5P0L_05:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-05","text":"Rest changes from the stabilizing direction of present life into a terminal transformation that closes all temporal works.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MP-04","CL_5P0L_05:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a mechanism of donation and conversion around an immutable center, in which mutable dependencies receive form, direction, and rest.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:HSI-01","CL_5P0L_05:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a receptive and porous node, whose unity is effected from memory, attention, appetite, signs, and the weight of love.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:HSI-02","CL_5P0L_05:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is at once a river of passage and a workshop of formation, extended between origin without succession and rest without evening.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:HSI-03","CL_5P0L_05:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Memory and Scripture are two prismatic surfaces through which absence is not removed, but refracted into trace, meaning, and present operation.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MME-02","CL_5P0L_05:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-05","text":"Navigation is possible not as flight from the created condition, but as the direction of attention and love through confession, the Word, grace, and the mediator.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:HSI-05","CL_5P0L_05:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore the mechanism that coordinates creation, interiority, time, interpretation, the Church, and rest under one law of participation.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MRT-B01","CL_5P0L_05:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the asymmetry between God self-sufficient and the creature to which being, truth, action, unity, and rest are given.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MRT-B02","CL_5P0L_05:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"The relation of time to affection of the mind, the status of formlessness, and the causality by which eternal act produces succession are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MRT-B03","CL_5P0L_05:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"The full rule for discerning multiple meanings and translating the order of creation into the Church still resists axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MRT-B07","CL_5P0L_05:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B05","text":"The coordination of grace, action, and responsibility coheres as a systemic postulate, but is not yet reduced to a complete causal formula.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MRT-B04","CL_5P0L_05:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B06","text":"The bridge between the creative Word and the mediation of Christ sustains the global closure, although it relies on revealed premises and analogy more than on internal deduction.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MRT-B05","CL_5P0L_05:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B07","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if it retains dependence, recursion, ordered plurality, and terminal rest, and does not flatten the images of memory, formlessness, and Sabbath into identity.","refs":["CL_5P0L_04:MRT-B08","CL_5P0L_05:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_5P0L_L1_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_5P0L_L1_02:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"5P0L L1 02"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Confessiones","title":"5P0L L1 02","context":"Chapters 10–13 · Book Ten. · Book Eleven. · Book Twelve. · Book Thirteen.","original_title":"5P0L L1 02","original_context":"Chapters 10–13 · Liber Decimus. · Liber Undecimus. · Liber Duodecimus. · Liber Decimus Tertius."},"original_title":"5P0L L1 02","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"WB_5P0L","layer":"WB","title":"Confessions","order":1,"parents":["MC_5P0L_L1_01","MC_5P0L_L1_02"],"revision":"WB_5P0L@R002","sha256":"6982ab63e4b82c09b4ece5b8ea3127d618eede3243c7f4110bc59af54a792c0f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo creatura principio suo alienata, sibi opaca et temporaliter dispersa culpam agnoscat atque unitatem, veritatem et requiem quas sibi dare nequit recipiat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis creationem et reformationem eadem asymmetria donationis regit: creatura bona est quia esse recipit, sanabilis quia forma eius non deleta est, et indiget gratia quia principium reditus in se non possidet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio confessionem affectivam, refutationem ontologicam, analysim temporis et exegesim componit, ut historia subiecti et historia creationis unius dependentiae receptivae magnitudines fiant.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-04","text":"Motus ab exteriori dispersione per iudicium interius procedit, sed interioritatem ipsam transcendit ad Verbum, mediationem communem et finem qui successioni non subiacet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-05","text":"Progressio simul spiralis et liminalis est: illuminationes repetitae opacitatem altius detegunt, conversio voluntatem punctiformiter integrat, et formatio post limen recursive continuatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-06","text":"Conversio biographica non est clausura prioris partis, sed exemplar locale creationis reformativae, quod posterior investigatio originis, temporis et Verbi ad legem universalem participationis amplificat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RMD-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-07","text":"Confessio machinam totius libri efficit: memoriam et Scripturam sub veritate ponit, causalitatem redistribuit et subiectum una cum communitate interpretante reformat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-08","text":"Clausura praesens aperta manet, quia conversio et opera pacem anticipant sed non possident; clausura terminalis est sabbatum sine vespera, quod recursionem temporalem non prolongat sed terminat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Deus immutabilis est fons essendi, mensura veritatis et finis quietis, nec principium adversum, tempus aut opus creatum eum continet vel perficit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Omne creatum qua creatum bonum est, sed ex nihilo factum causam essendi, unitatis, cognitionis et perseverantiae in se non possidet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Malum caret substantia propria, sed ut aversio boni, directio inordinata et consuetudo sedimentata efficaciam temporalem realem acquirit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Bona creata et multiplicitas bonitatem retinent; dispersiva fiunt quando medium pro fine usurpatur, fecunda autem quando ad fontem et finem communem referuntur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Subiectum est nodus receptivus et diachronicus, cuius identitas memoria, attentione, appetitu, habitu, signis et relationibus socialibus constituitur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Veritas interior et Verbum subiecto intimiora sunt sed illud transcendunt, errores iudicant atque capacitatem cognoscentem ad formam vocant.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Cognitio est actus participatus et moraliter ordinatus; certitudo principii non tollit necessitatem humilitatis, correctionis affectus et provisionalitatis applicationis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Tempus est modus creatus mutationis, distentionis et receptionis, qui obiecta dissolvit, habitus sedimentat, formationem explicat et historiam salutis sustinet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Mediatio per societatem, auctoritatem, Scripturam, sacramentum et Ecclesiam est necessaria sed ambivalens: secundum ordinem receptionis aut captat aut sanat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Gratia et causalitas divina facultatem boni actus antecedunt atque constituunt, voluntatem sanantes sine consensu eius aut responsabilitate abolendis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-10","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Conversio est limen quod voluntatem integrat, non consummatio quae temporalitatem aufert; eius actus in disciplina, communione et reformatione continua explicatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-11","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Confessio et interpretatio veritatem non tantum referunt, sed memoriam, affectum, intellectum et communitatem sub eius iudicio performative reformant.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-13","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Unitas vera est ordo pluralitatis per caritatem, non suppressio differentiae nec licentia sensuum vel appetituum indiscriminata.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-15","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Sapientia et quies in bono immutabili inquietudinem, cognitionem, conversionem et opera temporalia dirigunt, quamvis intra praesentem vitam stabiliter non possideantur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Sabbatum sine vespera terminaliter claudit successionem operum, ubi pax et Sapientia non amplius per vestigia, dilationem aut mutationem recipiuntur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-14","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-01","text":"Creatio ex nihilo ex propositione ontologica in legem cognitionis, voluntatis, interpretationis et conversionis amplificatur, ita ut omnis reformatio formam receptionis retineat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-02","text":"Dispersio, informitas et aversio circa absentiam unitatis propriae recurrunt, sed non coincidunt: informitas capacitatem formatam nondum habet, dispersio unitatem amittit, aversio culpabiliter directionem pervertit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-03","text":"Interioritas ex loco examinationis biographicae in commissuram ontologiae et hermeneuticae mutatur, deinde a Verbo transcenditur ne in autonomiam reflexivam claudatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-04","text":"Memoria ab origine morali reconstruenda ad mediationem temporalem absentiae transit, cui Scriptura respondet ut memoria communis Verbi in sermone temporali.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RMD-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-05","text":"Attentio ex operatione mensurandi successionem in directionem amoris mutatur, atque sub caritate fit norma simul moralis, hermeneutica et ontologica.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-06","text":"Conversio ex clausura biographica in exemplar reformationis universalis transfertur, dum nexus Verbi creatoris et mediatoris eventum personalem ad structuram creationis refert.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RMD-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-07","text":"Mediatio vim invertit: initio captationem socialem et doctrinalem distribuit, post correctionem fit medicina, ac tandem structuram originis, interpretationis et formationis ecclesialis constituit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-08","text":"Requies ex stabilitate affectiva quaesita in transformationem terminalem mutatur, quae non unum statum temporalem confirmat sed ipsam successionem vesperamque excludit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-10","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-09","text":"Multiplicitas ex signo dispersionis in locum bifurcationis transit: sine fine uno attentionem dividit, sub fonte indiviso pluralitatem rerum et sensuum fecundam reddit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-01","text":"Infantia, furtum, luctus, taxonomiae memoriae et materia informis manent machinae locales; ad gradum totius libri ascendunt tantum ut exempla receptionis, aversionis, vestigii aut formationis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:CGD-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-02","text":"Ascensus interiores et aporiae temporis non uniuntur in doctrinam regionalem, sed coniunctim demonstrant cognitionem veritatis a possessione stabile et praesentiam ab exhaustiva sui comprehensione distingui.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:CGD-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-03","text":"Polemicae anti-Manichaeae, anti-Donatisticae et exegeticae recedunt ut controversiae locales, dum earum exclusiones contra malum substantiale, iudicium autonomum et mensuram creatam globaliter persistent.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:CGD-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-04","text":"Scholae, sodalitates et exempla singula amplificantur in theoriam formationis communis, cui communitas confessionis addit distributionem interpretationis, caritatis et operum.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:CGD-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-05","text":"Christus mediator et Verbum creatorium transcendunt conclusiones regionales, quia pontem portant inter donationem essendi, sanationem voluntatis et directionem mortalitatis ad iustitiam.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-06","text":"Eventus conversionis transitum maiorem efficit cum sub lege creationis et gratiae reponitur: punctum consensus fit condensatio dependentiae quae totam actionem creatam regit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-07","text":"Dubia de affectione temporali, informitate et causalitate aeterna non solvuntur per elevationem gradus; synthesis tantum eorum functionem limitativam et analogicam coordinat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-01","text":"Nulla exterioritas ontologica, materia coaeterna aut tempus independens datur ex quo Deo oppositio substantialis constitui possit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-02","text":"Malum neque natura creata neque materia nec proprietas corporis est; eius efficacia directioni privativae et temporaliter sedimentatae tribuitur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-03","text":"Nulla creatura sui causa, illuminatrix, reparatrix aut consummatrix esse potest, quamvis proprios actus vere agat et eorum culpam gerat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-04","text":"Nullum bonum finitum damnatur ut substantia, sed nec corpus, societas, doctrina aut aliud medium mutabile finem stabilem desiderii praebet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-05","text":"Scientia, eloquentia, introspectio et analysis temporis neque salutem neque ascensum immediatum efficiunt, si capacitas cognoscentis et ordo amoris immutati manent.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-06","text":"Mediatio neque neutralis est neque impedimentum dispensabile: voces, societates et signa iam receptionem formant, quamvis ex se salutem praestare nequeant.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-07","text":"Unitas non est univocitas, nec multiplicitas per se veritatem vel ordinem efficit; identitas absoluta et licentia indiscriminata pariter excluduntur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-08","text":"Nulla conversio, virtus, institutio aut series operum intra mortalitatem clausuram consummatam constituit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet creationem et conversionem duas economias separatas esse, aliter bonitas sanabilis creaturae et asymmetria gratiae communem fundamentum amitterent.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet mentem esse principium propriae veritatis aut interioritatem plenam sui transparentiam praebere.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:DBN-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem divinam et actionem creatam competitive distribui, sive gratia post actum accedat sive voluntatem coactive substituat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:DBN-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet cognitionem termini secum ferre facultatem perveniendi aut veritatem ab affectu et intentione subiecti produci.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:DBN-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet dilationem medium vacuum esse aut successionem creatam rationem voluntatis divinae continere.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:DBN-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet introspectionem vel correctionem obiecti cogniti sufficere, quia impedimentum ad capacitatem recipientem et ordinem amoris transfertur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-06","MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse oportet homologiam inter informitatem, distentionem et dispersionem demonstrare identitatem ontologicam culpae aut necessitatem immediatam translationis creationis in Ecclesiam.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas radicaliter indisponibilis est creatura quae se totam possideat, se regeneret et principium stabilitatis ex propria natura producat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-02","text":"Nullum extra causalitatem divinam datur in quo creatura Deum fugiat vel oppositionem substantialem, esse aut veritatem independentem accipiat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-03","text":"Prospectus creatus qui animam, providentiam, tempus et Scripturam simul exhauriat indisponibilis est; conversio cognitionem auget sine hac absentia abolenda.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-04","text":"Nulla neutralitas stabilis inter aversionem et conversionem, aut inter attentionem ad fontem et usurpationem medii, conceditur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-05","text":"Sapientia stabiliter possessa et finis sine vespera non sunt momenta intra seriem praesentem; eorum indisponibilitas vim terminalem toti motui praebet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-06","text":"Innocentia absoluta et meritum clausum ontologice atque narrative excluduntur, ut misericordia, sacrificium et intercessio formas ultimorum actuum maneant.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-01","text":"Reformatio subiecti est creatio participata in magnitudine morali: sicut esse ex se non producitur, ita facultas reditus non a voluntate iam divisa generatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01","WB_5P0L:GA-02","WB_5P0L:GA-10","WB_5P0L:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-02","text":"Malum privativum ontologica mutabilitate utitur, sed informitati non aequatur: receptivitas est condicio formae, aversio autem directio culpabilis capacitatis iam donatae.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-02","WB_5P0L:GA-02","WB_5P0L:GA-03","WB_5P0L:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-03","text":"Tempus coniungit distentionem phenomenologicam et causalitatem moralem, quia eadem successio voluntatem dividit, consuetudinem accumulat et correctionem gradualiter recipiendam sustinet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-03","WB_5P0L:GA-05","WB_5P0L:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-04","text":"Memoria et Scriptura absentiam in praesentem efficaciam convertunt, dum confessio per vestigia vitae et exegesis per verba temporalia causalitatem sub iudicio Verbi reordinant.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-04","WB_5P0L:GA-06","WB_5P0L:GA-09","WB_5P0L:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-05","text":"Conversio punctiformis et illuminatio spiralis non sunt formae aemulae: eventus divisionem voluntatis claudit, dum capacitas renovata novas opacitates et receptiones aperit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-01","WB_5P0L:GA-07","WB_5P0L:GA-11","WB_5P0L:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-06","text":"Ecclesia emergit ut apparatus duplicis formationis, quia regimen affectuum et credibilitatis cum communi interpretatione, memoria et distributione mediorum coniungit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-04","WB_5P0L:GA-05","WB_5P0L:GA-09","WB_5P0L:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-07","text":"Tensionem inter gratiam et responsabilitatem tota ontologia participationis iterat: actio vere creaturae imputatur, quamvis facultas boni agendi sibi non sufficiat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-07","WB_5P0L:GA-02","WB_5P0L:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-08","text":"Auctoritas medicinalis et Verbum creatorium eundem circuitum capacitatis efficiunt: principium transcendens format facultatem quae postea signum eius agnoscere et iudicare potest.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-08","WB_5P0L:GA-06","WB_5P0L:GA-09","WB_5P0L:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-09","text":"Inquietudo personalis et unitas creationis in eundem attractorem convergunt, sed Sapientia quaesita in requiem quae successionem terminaliter claudit amplificatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-08","WB_5P0L:GA-13","WB_5P0L:GA-14","WB_5P0L:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-10","text":"Confessio biographiam in doctrinam creationis et doctrinam creationis in novam lectionem biographiae recursive convertit, ita ut communitas eundem motum exteriorizet et perpetuet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-05","WB_5P0L:GA-05","WB_5P0L:GA-12","WB_5P0L:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-01","text":"Autobiographia et cosmologia se mutuo determinant: historia conversionis ostendit quid creaturae dependentia temporaliter faciat, dum creatio originem et mensuram ipsius historiae retrospective reconstituit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum fit receptivitas creata quae se actibus propriis ligare potest, sed tantum dono, mediatione et memoria confessionali ad unitatem recollegitur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-03","text":"Tempus unam structuram multiplicem constituit: est effectus mutationis, distentio animi, sedimentum habitus et medium historiae qua creatura ad statum non temporalem formatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-04","text":"Malum fit parasitica directio doni, quae bonitatem creatam non delet sed eam in finem falsum claudit atque historia, repetitione et mediatione amplificat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-05","text":"Epistemologia, hermeneutica et soteriologia unam disciplinam receptivitatis efficiunt, in qua veritas stabilis intellectum, affectum et communitatem semper corrigibilia format.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-06","text":"Ecclesia est memoria et attentio distributa, quae conversionem personalem in sacramentum, interpretationem, cantum, ministerium et communem exspectationem exteriorizat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-07","text":"Opera temporalia structuram propriae obsolescentiae continent: bona sunt ut media formationis, sed consummatio eorum cessationem potius quam perpetuationem postulat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-10","MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-08","text":"Forma libri lectorem ex spectatore in subiectum confessans et interpretans mutat, quia narratio retractata et exegesis corrigibilis actum receptionis quem describunt simul exigunt.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-01","text":"Donum facultatem operandi constituit; operatio defectum vel receptivitatem manifestat; manifestatio confessionem aperit; confessio ampliorem receptionem gratiae postulat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-02","MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-02","text":"Fames boni immutabilis bonum finitum absolutizat; insufficiencia eius dispersionem auget; dispersio misericordiam invocat; misericordia medium ad fontem restituit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-03","text":"Laus auctoritatem, auctoritas imitationem et imitatio novam laudem generant; communitas convertit hunc circuitum captivum in propagationem exempli, confessionis et collectionis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-04","text":"Actus perversus consuetudinem, consuetudo divisionem et divisio dilationem gignunt; eadem successio, per attentionem reordinata, historiam formationis et desiderium consummationis producit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-05","text":"Veritas impotentiam detegit, impotentia confessionem generat, confessio illuminationem petit, et illuminatio capacitatem auget quae novam opacitatem agnoscit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-06","text":"Signum ad iudicium interius remittit, iudicium Verbum quaerit, Verbum pluralitatem sensuum aperit, et pluralitas examen veritatis atque caritatis renovat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-07","text":"Eventus per confessionem providentiam manifestat, narratio formationem communem parit, et communitas sub ampliore historia creationis eventum iterum interpretatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-08","text":"Sapientia stabilis recursionem temporalem allicit sine praesentia plena, dum sabbatum sine vespera attractorem terminalem praebet qui circuitum operum tandem claudit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B01","text":"Fons immutabilis → creatio ex nihilo → bonitas recepta → mutabilitas et dependentia universalis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B02","text":"Desiderium boni immutabilis → absolutizatio medii → attentio captiva → dispersio affectiva et ontologica.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B03","text":"Dispersio → repetitio temporalis → habitus et distentio → subiectum diachronice divisum et sibi opacum.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B04","text":"Vox, auctoritas, memoria et Scriptura → iudicium interius → detectio falsi → receptio mediatica veritatis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B05","text":"Veritas et Verbum → refutatio obiecti falsi → revelatio habitus intacti → confessio impotentiae recipientis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B06","text":"Gratia antecedens et mediator → consensus integratus → conversio → capacitas actionis participatae et reformationis continuae.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-10","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B07","text":"Confessio, baptismus, exegesis et communitas → memoria et attentio distributae → pluralitas sub caritate collecta.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-13","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B08","text":"Opera temporalia → sanatio incohata → desiderium propriae consummationis → Sapientia et sabbatum sine vespera.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-14","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:AFM-B01","text":"Donum essendi → bonum creatum male finitum → dispersio affectiva → captatio socialis et doctrinalis → iudicium interius → responsabilitas voluntatis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AFM-B02","text":"Refutatio falsi → detectio impotentiae → gratia integrans → conversio → confessio performativa → reinterpretatio memoriae sub grammatica creationis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AFM-B03","text":"Conversio → formatio ecclesialis → opera temporalia et pluralitas ordinata → mediatio Christi → pax futura → sabbatum sine vespera.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-01","text":"Malum reclassificatur ex natura adversa in directionem parasiticam quae bonum donatum a fonte avertit et per tempus, habitum atque mediationem efficaciam acquirit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-02","text":"Subiectum ex anima sibi praesente in receptivitatem historicam mutatur, cuius unitas donis, actibus, vestigiis, signis et relationibus constituitur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-03","text":"Tempus ex recipiente neutro in structuram multiplicem mutationis, mensurationis, sedimentationis moralis et institutionis salutaris transfertur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-04","text":"Interioritas ex spatio autonomiae in interface mutatur ubi veritas transcendens, vestigia memoriae, signa exteriora et iudicium communitatis concurrunt.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-05","text":"Auctoritas ex opposito libertatis vel possessione sensus in ministerium medicinalis formationis transfertur, quod sub veritate manet examinabile et corrigibile.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-06","text":"Multiplicitas desinit esse pura dispersio: unitas caritatis eam in fecunditatem ontologicam, socialem et hermeneuticam convertit sine differentia abolenda.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-07","text":"Conversio ex substitutione opinionis in formam localem creationis reformativae expanditur, qua facultas recipiens ad consensum, actionem et perseverantiam instauratur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-08","text":"Narratio et exegesis reclassificantur ut interventionum apparatus: una memoriam culpabilem, altera creationem et Ecclesiam reordinat, utraque lectorem sub veritate format.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei biographiam, memoriam, tentationem, conversionem, tempus, creationem, Scripturam, Ecclesiam et sabbatum tamquam diversa loca unius quaestionis de receptione ordinata exhibet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis donationem, aversionem, dispersionem, mediationem, memoriam, gratiam et collectionem in machinam formationis temporalis componit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus certamen ab electione doctrinarum ad formationem facultatis transfert, quae amare, attendere, credere, interpretari et consentire potest.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis realitatem ut participationem constituit, creaturam ut receptivitatem mutabilem et historiam ut transitum ordinatum inter originem sine tempore et finem sine successione.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus per confessionem, repetitionem, variationem et exegesim lectorem a dispersione ad intentionem atque a falsa clausura ad corrigibilitatem transfert.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-01","text":"Gratia facultatem boni consensus constituit et voluntas culpam retinet, sed modus quo prioritas divina libertatem efficit sine coactione formaliter incompletus manet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-02","text":"Providentia actus pravos ad bonum ordinat et actus aeternus effectus successivos producit, sed distinctio inter ordinationem, efficientiam et imputabilitatem firmius affirmatur quam explicatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-03","text":"Veritas interior auctoritatem medicinalem iudicat, dum ipsa facultas iudicandi auctoritate et communitate formatur; circuitus criterio omnino externo caret.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-04","text":"Bona creata et multiplicitas legitima manent, sed eadem captationem et dispersionem sustinent; discrimen ordinis ex fine et caritate pendet quae non semper operative definiuntur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-05","text":"Conversio actum integrum postulat, dum natura mutabilis et formatio ulterior perseverant; requies terminalis stabilitatem promittit cuius modus sine abolitione creaturae fragilis manet.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-06","text":"Homologia informitatis, distentionis et dispersionis systema comprimit, sed capacitatem ontologicam cum culpa morali conflare potest si differentiae magnitudinis omittuntur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-07","text":"Pluralitas sensuum veritati, Spiritui et caritati subicitur, sed regula plena qua sensus veri inter se compatibiles discernantur non exhibetur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-01","text":"Malum mutatur ab adversario substantiali in privationem, inde in aversionem, consuetudinem et directionem temporalem boni donati.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-02","text":"Mediatio mutatur ab instrumento captationis in auctoritatem medicinalem, deinde in structuram universalem originis, reformationis et collectionis ecclesialis.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-03","text":"Veritas mutatur ex obiecto doctrinali in iudicium quod cognoscentem detegit, dum interioritas ex refugio sui in commissuram receptionis et transcendendae opacitatis transit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-04","text":"Dilatio mutatur ex patientia praeparatoria in servitutem voluntatis, dum tempus ex mensura transitus in intervallum pedagogicum fidei, actionis et institutionis amplificatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-05","text":"Clausura conversionis mutatur in principium formationis, et requies stabilizans vitae praesentis in terminum qui opera atque successionem claudit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-01","text":"Realitas est campus donationis circa centrum immutabile, in quo formae receptae possunt averti et vulnerari, sed nullam naturam centro contrariam producunt.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus porosus et diachronicus, qui influxus amoris, signorum, consuetudinis et gratiae recipit, se repetitione ligat atque confessione recollegitur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est fluvius et officina: aufert obiecta, sedimenta format, decisionem comprimit et creaturam inter originem sine successione atque requiem sine vespera extendit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-04","text":"Mediatio est rete prismaticae transmissionis, in quo voces, corpora, memoria et Scriptura absentiam in vestigium, sensum et operationem praesentem refringunt.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga a conditione creata, sed directio attentionis per dona temporalia ad communionem quae etiam mortem transit et in requie stabilitur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam quae creationem, aversionem, conversionem, memoriam, tempus, interpretationem, Ecclesiam et requiem sub lege participationis coordinat.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est coniunctio bonitatis creaturae cum dependentia radicali, mali privativi cum efficacia temporali, et responsabilitatis voluntatis cum prioritate gratiae.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B03","text":"Cohaerentia totius libri reditu spirali constat: biographia ad ontologiam amplificatur, ontologia biographiam reclassificat, et utraque ad clausuram eschatologicam dirigitur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B04","text":"Minus determinata manent concursus gratiae et libertatis, relatio affectionis animi ad tempus creatum, atque causalitas qua actus aeternus successionem producit.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt densitas affectuum, singularitas luctus, pluralitas sensuum et actus allocutionis, quia non ornamenta sed media performativae conversionis sunt.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B06","text":"Pons inter Verbum creatorium et Christum mediatorem necessarius est clausurae globali, sed analogia et praemissis revelatis plus quam deductione ex temporalitate sola sustentatur.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T142858467824Z_25d82076","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Confessions","source_sha256":"6982ab63e4b82c09b4ece5b8ea3127d618eede3243c7f4110bc59af54a792c0f","sha256":"9ecc04ed540a87b741ecb36936c0ed2b290a67e12766526ef4132131b46c734f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T14:28:58.468911+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-01","text":"The system asks how a creature alienated from its principle, opaque to itself and temporally dispersed, may acknowledge guilt and receive the unity, truth, and rest that it cannot give itself.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-02","text":"The global thesis is governed by the same asymmetry of gift in creation and reformation: the creature is good because it receives being, healable because its form has not been destroyed, and in need of grace because it does not possess within itself the principle of return.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-03","text":"The argument combines affective confession, ontological refutation, analysis of time, and exegesis, so that the history of the subject and the history of creation become dimensions of one receptive dependence.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from external dispersion through interior judgment, but transcends interiority itself toward the Word, common mediation, and an end not subject to succession.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-05","text":"The progression is at once spiral and liminal: repeated illuminations disclose opacity more deeply, conversion integrates the will pointwise, and formation continues recursively after the threshold.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-06","text":"Biographical conversion is not the closure of the prior part, but a local model of reformative creation, which the later investigation of origin, time, and the Word expands into a universal law of participation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RMD-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-07","text":"Confession constitutes the mechanism of the whole book: it places memory and Scripture under truth, redistributes causality, and reforms the subject together with the interpreting community.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AS-08","text":"The present closure remains open, because conversion and works anticipate peace but do not possess it; terminal closure is the Sabbath without evening, which does not prolong temporal recursion but terminates it.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The immutable God is the source of being, the measure of truth, and the end of rest; no opposing principle, time, or created work contains or perfects him.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Everything created, insofar as it is created, is good; but, made from nothing, it does not possess within itself the cause of being, unity, knowledge, and perseverance.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Evil lacks a substance of its own, but as a turning-away from the good, a disordered direction, and sedimented habit, it acquires real temporal efficacy.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Created goods and multiplicity retain goodness; they become dispersive when the means is usurped as the end, but fruitful when referred to the common source and end.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The subject is a receptive and diachronic node, whose identity is constituted by memory, attention, desire, habit, signs, and social relations.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Interior truth and the Word are more intimate to the subject yet transcend it, judge errors, and call the knowing capacity to form.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Knowledge is a participated and morally ordered act; certainty of principle does not remove the need for humility, correction of affect, and provisionality of application.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Time is a created mode of change, distension, and reception, which dissolves objects, sediments habits, unfolds formation, and sustains the history of salvation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Mediation through society, authority, Scripture, sacrament, and Church is necessary but ambivalent: according to the order of reception, it either captures or heals.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Grace and divine causality precede and constitute the capacity for a good act, healing the will without abolishing its consent or responsibility.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-10","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Conversion is a threshold that integrates the will, not a consummation that removes temporality; its act unfolds in discipline, communion, and continuing reformation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-11","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Confession and interpretation do not merely report truth, but performatively reform memory, affect, intellect, and community under its judgment.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-13","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] True unity is the ordering of plurality through charity, not the suppression of difference nor indiscriminate license of senses or desires.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-15","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Wisdom and rest in the immutable good direct unrest, knowledge, conversion, and temporal works, although they are not stably possessed within the present life.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] The Sabbath without evening terminally closes the succession of works, where peace and Wisdom are no longer received through traces, delay, or change.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-14","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-01","text":"Creation ex nihilo is expanded from an ontological proposition into a law of knowledge, will, interpretation, and conversion, so that every reformation retains the form of reception.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-02","text":"Dispersion, formlessness, and turning-away recur around the absence of proper unity, but do not coincide: formlessness does not yet have formed capacity, dispersion loses unity, and turning-away culpably perverts direction.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-03","text":"Interiority changes from the site of biographical examination into the joint of ontology and hermeneutics, then is transcended by the Word lest it be enclosed in reflexive autonomy.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-04","text":"Memory moves from a moral origin requiring reconstruction to the temporal mediation of absence, to which Scripture responds as the common memory of the Word in temporal speech.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RMD-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-05","text":"Attention changes from the operation of measuring succession into the direction of love, and under charity becomes a norm at once moral, hermeneutical, and ontological.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-06","text":"Conversion is transferred from biographical closure into a model of universal reformation, while the nexus of the creating and mediating Word relates the personal event to the structure of creation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RMD-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-07","text":"Mediation reverses its force: at first it distributes social and doctrinal capture; after correction it becomes medicine; and finally it constitutes the structure of origin, interpretation, and ecclesial formation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-08","text":"Rest changes from sought affective stability into terminal transformation, which does not confirm a single temporal state but excludes succession and evening itself.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-10","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RMD-09","text":"Multiplicity moves from a sign of dispersion into a point of bifurcation: without one end it divides attention; under the undivided source it renders the plurality of things and meanings fruitful.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-01","text":"Childhood, theft, mourning, taxonomies of memory, and formless matter remain local mechanisms; they ascend to the level of the whole book only as examples of reception, turning-away, trace, or formation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:CGD-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-02","text":"Interior ascents and aporias of time are not united into a regional doctrine, but jointly demonstrate that knowledge of truth is distinct from stable possession, and presence from exhaustive self-comprehension.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:CGD-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-03","text":"Anti-Manichaean, anti-Donatist, and exegetical polemics recede as local controversies, while their exclusions against substantial evil, autonomous judgment, and created measure persist globally.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:CGD-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-04","text":"Schools, associations, and individual examples are expanded into a theory of common formation, to which the community of confession adds a distribution of interpretation, charity, and works.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:CGD-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-05","text":"Christ the mediator and the creating Word transcend regional conclusions, because they bear the bridge between the gift of being, the healing of the will, and the direction of mortality toward justice.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-06","text":"The event of conversion effects a greater transition when it is placed under the law of creation and grace: the point of consent becomes a condensation of dependence governing all created action.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:CGD-07","text":"Questions concerning temporal affection, formlessness, and eternal causality are not resolved by raising the level; synthesis only coordinates their limiting and analogical function.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-01","text":"No ontological exteriority, coeternal matter, or independent time is given from which substantial opposition to God could be constituted.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-02","text":"Evil is neither created nature nor matter nor a property of body; its efficacy is attributed to a privative and temporally sedimented direction.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-03","text":"No creature can be its own cause, illuminator, repairer, or consummator, although it truly performs its own acts and bears their guilt.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-04","text":"No finite good is condemned as substance, but neither body, society, doctrine, nor any other mutable means provides the stable end of desire.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-05","text":"Knowledge, eloquence, introspection, and analysis of time effect neither salvation nor immediate ascent if the knowing capacity and the order of love remain unchanged.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-06","text":"Mediation is neither neutral nor a dispensable obstacle: voices, societies, and signs already form reception, although they cannot of themselves bestow salvation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-07","text":"Unity is not univocity, nor does multiplicity in itself effect truth or order; absolute identity and indiscriminate license are equally excluded.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:GNX-08","text":"No conversion, virtue, institution, or series of works within mortality constitutes consummate closure.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that creation and conversion are two separate economies; otherwise the healable goodness of the creature and the asymmetry of grace would lose their common foundation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the mind is the principle of its own truth or that interiority provides full transparency to itself.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:DBN-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that divine causality and created action are competitively distributed, whether grace is added after the act or coercively substitutes for the will.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:DBN-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that knowledge of the end carries with it the capacity to arrive, or that truth is produced from the subject's affect and intention.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:DBN-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that delay is an empty medium or that created succession contains the rationale of the divine will.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:DBN-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that introspection or correction of the known object suffices, because the impediment is transferred to receptive capacity and the order of love.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-06","MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that the homology among formlessness, distension, and dispersion demonstrates the ontological identity of guilt or the immediate necessity of translating creation into the Church.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-01","text":"Radically unavailable is the possibility of a creature that possesses itself wholly, regenerates itself, and produces from its own nature the principle of stability.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-02","text":"Nothing outside divine causality is given in which the creature may flee God or receive substantial opposition, independent being, or independent truth.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-03","text":"A created perspective that exhausts soul, providence, time, and Scripture simultaneously is unavailable; conversion increases knowledge without abolishing this absence.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-04","text":"No stable neutrality between turning-away and conversion, or between attention to the source and usurpation of the means, is conceded.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-05","text":"Stably possessed Wisdom and the end without evening are not moments within the present series; their unavailability gives terminal force to the entire movement.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSA-06","text":"Absolute innocence and closed merit are excluded ontologically and narratively, so that mercy, sacrifice, and intercession remain forms of ultimate acts.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-01","text":"The reformation of the subject is participated creation on a moral scale: just as being is not produced from itself, so the capacity for return is not generated by a will already divided.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01","WB_5P0L:GA-02","WB_5P0L:GA-10","WB_5P0L:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-02","text":"Privative evil makes use of ontological mutability, but is not equated with formlessness: receptivity is the condition of form, whereas turning-away is the culpable direction of an already gifted capacity.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-02","WB_5P0L:GA-02","WB_5P0L:GA-03","WB_5P0L:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-03","text":"Time joins phenomenological distension and moral causality, because the same succession divides the will, accumulates habit, and sustains correction to be gradually received.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-03","WB_5P0L:GA-05","WB_5P0L:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-04","text":"Memory and Scripture convert absence into present efficacy, while confession through the traces of life and exegesis through temporal words reorder causality under the judgment of the Word.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-04","WB_5P0L:GA-06","WB_5P0L:GA-09","WB_5P0L:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-05","text":"Pointwise conversion and spiral illumination are not rival forms: the event closes the division of the will, while renewed capacity opens new opacities and receptions.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-01","WB_5P0L:GA-07","WB_5P0L:GA-11","WB_5P0L:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-06","text":"The Church emerges as an apparatus of double formation, because it joins the governance of affects and credibility with common interpretation, memory, and the distribution of means.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-04","WB_5P0L:GA-05","WB_5P0L:GA-09","WB_5P0L:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-07","text":"The tension between grace and responsibility reiterates the entire ontology of participation: action is truly imputed to the creature, although its capacity to act well is not sufficient to itself.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-07","WB_5P0L:GA-02","WB_5P0L:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-08","text":"Medicinal authority and the creating Word effect the same circuit of capacity: a transcendent principle forms the faculty that can afterward recognize and judge its sign.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-08","WB_5P0L:GA-06","WB_5P0L:GA-09","WB_5P0L:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-09","text":"Personal unrest and the unity of creation converge upon the same attractor, but the sought Wisdom is expanded into rest that terminally closes succession.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-08","WB_5P0L:GA-13","WB_5P0L:GA-14","WB_5P0L:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:XD-10","text":"Confession recursively converts biography into the doctrine of creation and the doctrine of creation into a new reading of biography, so that the community externalizes and perpetuates the same movement.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-05","WB_5P0L:GA-05","WB_5P0L:GA-12","WB_5P0L:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-01","text":"Autobiography and cosmology mutually determine one another: the history of conversion shows what creaturely dependence does temporally, while creation retrospectively reconstitutes the origin and measure of that very history.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes created receptivity that can bind itself through its own acts, but is recollected into unity only through gift, mediation, and confessional memory.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-03","text":"Time constitutes one multiple structure: it is the effect of change, the distension of the soul, the sediment of habit, and the medium of history through which the creature is formed toward a non-temporal state.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-04","text":"Evil becomes a parasitic direction of gift, which does not destroy created goodness but encloses it within a false end and amplifies it through history, repetition, and mediation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-05","text":"Epistemology, hermeneutics, and soteriology constitute one discipline of receptivity, in which stable truth forms intellect, affect, and community as always corrigible.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-06","text":"The Church is distributed memory and attention, which externalizes personal conversion into sacrament, interpretation, song, ministry, and common expectation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-07","text":"Temporal works contain the structure of their own obsolescence: they are good as means of formation, but their consummation requires cessation rather than perpetuation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:XD-10","MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:BSD-08","text":"The form of the book changes the reader from spectator into a confessing and interpreting subject, because retracted narrative and corrigible exegesis simultaneously demand the act of reception they describe.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-01","text":"Gift constitutes the capacity to act; operation manifests defect or receptivity; manifestation opens confession; confession calls for a broader reception of grace.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-02","MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-02","text":"Hunger for the immutable good absolutizes a finite good; its insufficiency increases dispersion; dispersion invokes mercy; mercy restores the means to the source.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-03","text":"Praise generates authority, authority imitation, and imitation new praise; community converts this captive circuit into the propagation of example, confession, and gathering.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-04","text":"A perverse act generates habit, habit division, and division delay; the same succession, reordered through attention, produces a history of formation and desire for consummation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-05","text":"Truth discloses impotence, impotence generates confession, confession seeks illumination, and illumination increases the capacity that recognizes new opacity.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-06","text":"A sign refers back to interior judgment, judgment seeks the Word, the Word opens a plurality of senses, and plurality renews the examination of truth and charity.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-07","text":"Through confession an event manifests providence, narrative gives rise to common formation, and the community reinterprets the event under the broader history of creation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:RLE-08","text":"Stable Wisdom attracts temporal recursion without full presence, while the Sabbath without evening provides the terminal attractor that finally closes the circuit of works.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B01","text":"Immutable source → creation ex nihilo → received goodness → universal mutability and dependence.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B02","text":"Desire for the immutable good → absolutization of the means → captive attention → affective and ontological dispersion.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B03","text":"Dispersion → temporal repetition → habit and distension → diachronically divided and self-opaque subject.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-09","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B04","text":"Voice, authority, memory, and Scripture → interior judgment → detection of the false → mediated reception of truth.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B05","text":"Truth and the Word → refutation of the false object → revelation of intact habit → confession of receptive impotence.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:XD-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B06","text":"Prevenient grace and mediator → integrated consent → conversion → capacity for participated action and continuing reformation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-10","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B07","text":"Confession, baptism, exegesis, and community → distributed memory and attention → plurality gathered under charity.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-13","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FGS-B08","text":"Temporal works → healing begun → desire for their own consummation → Wisdom and the Sabbath without evening.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:GA-14","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:AFM-B01","text":"Gift of being → created good wrongly made finite → affective dispersion → social and doctrinal capture → interior judgment → responsibility of the will.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AFM-B02","text":"Refutation of the false → detection of impotence → integrating grace → conversion → performative confession → reinterpretation of memory under the grammar of creation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:AFM-B03","text":"Conversion → ecclesial formation → temporal works and ordered plurality → mediation of Christ → future peace → Sabbath without evening.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:BAS-08","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ASP-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-01","text":"Evil is reclassified from an opposing nature into a parasitic direction that turns gifted good away from the source and acquires efficacy through time, habit, and mediation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-02","text":"The subject changes from a soul present to itself into historical receptivity, whose unity is constituted by gifts, acts, traces, signs, and relations.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-03","text":"Time is transferred from a neutral receptacle into a multiple structure of change, measurement, moral sedimentation, and salvific institution.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-04","text":"Interiority changes from a space of autonomy into an interface where transcendent truth, traces of memory, external signs, and the judgment of community converge.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-05","text":"Authority is transferred from the opposite of freedom or possession of meaning into a ministry of medicinal formation, which remains examinable and corrigible under truth.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-06","text":"Multiplicity ceases to be pure dispersion: the unity of charity converts it into ontological, social, and hermeneutical fruitfulness without abolishing difference.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-07","text":"Conversion expands from a substitution of opinion into a local form of reformative creation, by which receptive capacity is restored to consent, action, and perseverance.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MME-08","text":"Narrative and exegesis are reclassified as apparatuses of intervention: the one reorders culpable memory, the other creation and Church; both form the reader under truth.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MME-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level presents biography, memory, temptation, conversion, time, creation, Scripture, Church, and Sabbath as distinct sites of one question concerning ordered reception.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level composes gift, turning-away, dispersion, mediation, memory, grace, and gathering into a mechanism of temporal formation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level transfers the contest from the choice of doctrines to the formation of a capacity able to love, attend, believe, interpret, and consent.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level constitutes reality as participation, the creature as mutable receptivity, and history as an ordered passage between origin without time and end without succession.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level, through confession, repetition, variation, and exegesis, transfers the reader from dispersion to intention and from false closure to corrigibility.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-01","text":"Grace constitutes the capacity for good consent and the will retains guilt, but the mode by which divine priority effects freedom without coercion remains formally incomplete.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-02","text":"Providence orders wicked acts toward good and eternal act produces successive effects, but the distinction among ordering, efficiency, and imputability is affirmed more firmly than it is explained.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-03","text":"Interior truth judges medicinal authority, while the very faculty of judging is formed by authority and community; the circuit lacks a wholly external criterion.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-04","text":"Created goods and legitimate multiplicity remain, but these same things sustain capture and dispersion; the criterion of order depends on end and charity, which are not always operationally defined.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-05","text":"Conversion requires an integral act, while mutable nature and further formation persist; terminal rest promises a stability whose mode remains fragile without the abolition of the creature.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:FL-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-06","text":"The homology of formlessness, distension, and dispersion compresses the system, but can conflate ontological capacity with moral guilt if differences of scale are omitted.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:DBN-07","MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:FL-07","text":"The plurality of senses is subjected to truth, Spirit, and charity, but no full rule is presented by which mutually compatible true senses may be discerned.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:FL-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-01","text":"Evil changes from a substantial adversary into privation, then into turning-away, habit, and the temporal direction of gifted good.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-02","text":"Mediation changes from an instrument of capture into medicinal authority, then into the universal structure of origin, reformation, and ecclesial gathering.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-03","text":"Truth changes from a doctrinal object into a judgment that discloses the knower, while interiority passes from a refuge of self into the joint of reception and opacity to be transcended.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-04","text":"Delay changes from preparatory patience into the bondage of the will, while time expands from a measure of passage into a pedagogical interval of faith, action, and institution.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MP-05","text":"The closure of conversion changes into the principle of formation, and the stabilizing rest of present life into the end that closes works and succession.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MP-05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a field of gift around an immutable center, in which received forms can turn away and be wounded, but produce no nature contrary to the center.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a porous and diachronic node, receiving the influxes of love, signs, habit, and grace, binding itself through repetition, and being recollected through confession.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-03","text":"Time is a river and a workshop: it removes objects, forms sediments, compresses decision, and extends the creature between origin without succession and rest without evening.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-04","text":"Mediation is a network of prismatic transmission, in which voices, bodies, memory, and Scripture refract absence into present trace, meaning, and operation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation is not escape from the created condition, but the direction of attention through temporal gifts toward communion that also passes through death and is stabilized in rest.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:HSI-05","MC_5P0L_L1_01:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to reconstruct the mechanism that coordinates creation, turning-away, conversion, memory, time, interpretation, Church, and rest under the law of participation.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the conjunction of the creature's goodness with radical dependence, privative evil with temporal efficacy, and responsibility of the will with the priority of grace.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B03","text":"The coherence of the whole book consists in spiral return: biography is expanded into ontology, ontology reclassifies biography, and both are directed toward eschatological closure.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B04","text":"The concurrence of grace and freedom, the relation of affection of soul to created time, and the causality by which eternal act produces succession remain less determined.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B05","text":"The density of affects, the singularity of mourning, the plurality of senses, and the act of address resist axiomatic reduction, because they are not ornaments but means of performative conversion.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_01:MRT-B05","MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_5P0L:MRT-B06","text":"The bridge between the creating Word and Christ the mediator is necessary to global closure, but is sustained more by analogy and revealed premises than by deduction from temporality alone.","refs":["MC_5P0L_L1_02:MRT-B06","MC_5P0L_L1_02:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"WB_5P0L@R002","source_variant_id":"canonical:WB_5P0L:WB_5P0L@R002","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Confessiones"}],"identity":{"layer":"WB","kicker":"Whole book","title":"Confessions","context":"","original_title":"Confessiones"},"original_title":"Confessiones","title_language_code":"en"}],"seal":"SEAL_5P0L_1E2DA1293A9384AA39CB","version_work_id":"WORK_STORE_CANON_PG33849","version_label":"Added 16 Oct 2026 · Run 16 Oct 2026 · gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-sol","version_revision":"ecd7a4d68e55f7f8dc20f08a903737b4bb7f01b604e328bf99956ba00347dbdf","original_title":"Confessiones","title_language_code":"en","title_translation":{"schema":"care-title-heading-translation/1","language_code":"en","rendition_type":"machine","originals_preserved":true}},{"id":"WORK_STORE_PERSEUS_LATIN_PHI0550_PHI001_PERSEUS_LAT1","slug":"beta-f7e0ff97940e1001","title":"On the Nature of Things","author":"Lucretius","edition":"Lucretius De Rerum Natura Lost information","year":"55 BCE","original_year":-55,"publication_year":null,"description":"","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-f7e0ff97940e1001-24de84d5b17d77d2.png","source_url":"https://github.com/PerseusDL/canonical-latinLit/blob/128a05afa9cc7179ab18f8d05f78a9f1dd50e415/data/phi0550/phi001/phi0550.phi001.perseus-lat1.xml","source_notices":{"provider":"Perseus Latin","source_url":"https://github.com/PerseusDL/canonical-latinLit/blob/128a05afa9cc7179ab18f8d05f78a9f1dd50e415/data/phi0550/phi001/phi0550.phi001.perseus-lat1.xml","licence":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","statement":"CC BY-SA 4.0 repository default; individual edition review required"},"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","sources":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP","title":"Book One","order":1,"chapter_id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP","text":"## Liber Primus\n\n  \n\nAeneadum genetrix, hominum divomque voluptas,\n\n \n\nalma Venus, caeli subter labentia signa\n\n \n\nquae mare navigerum, quae terras frugiferentis\n\n \n\nconcelebras, per te quoniam genus omne animantum\n\n \n\nconcipitur visitque exortum lumina solis:\n\n \n\nte, dea, te fugiunt venti, te nubila caeli\n\n \n\nadventumque tuum, tibi suavis daedala tellus\n\n \n\nsummittit flores, tibi rident aequora ponti\n\n \n\nplacatumque nitet diffuso lumine caelum.\n\n \n\nnam simul ac species patefactast verna diei\n\n \n\net reserata viget genitabilis aura favoni,\n\n \n\naeriae primum volucris te, diva, tuumque\n\n \n\nsignificant initum perculsae corda tua vi.\n\n \n\ninde ferae pecudes persultant pabula laeta\n\n \n\net rapidos tranant amnis: ita capta lepore\n\n \n\nte sequitur cupide quo quamque inducere pergis.\n\n \n\ndenique per maria ac montis fluviosque rapacis\n\n \n\nfrondiferasque domos avium camposque virentis\n\n \n\nomnibus incutiens blandum per pectora amorem\n\n \n\nefficis ut cupide generatim saecla propagent.\n\n \n\nquae quoniam rerum naturam sola gubernas\n\n \n\nnec sine te quicquam dias in luminis oras\n\n \n\nexoritur neque fit laetum neque amabile quicquam,\n\n \n\nte sociam studeo scribendis versibus esse,\n\n \n\nquos ego de rerum natura pangere conor\n\n \n\nMemmiadae nostro, quem tu, dea, tempore in omni\n\n \n\nomnibus ornatum voluisti excellere rebus.\n\n \n\nquo magis aeternum da dictis, diva, leporem.\n\n \n\neffice ut interea fera moenera militiai\n\n \n\nper maria ac terras omnis sopita quiescant;\n\n \n\nnam tu sola potes tranquilla pace iuvare\n\n \n\nmortalis, quoniam belli fera moenera Mavors\n\n \n\narmipotens regit, in gremium qui saepe tuum se\n\n \n\nreiicit aeterno devictus vulnere amoris,\n\n \n\natque ita suspiciens tereti cervice reposta\n\n \n\npascit amore avidos inhians in te, dea, visus\n\n \n\neque tuo pendet resupini spiritus ore.\n\n \n\nhunc tu, diva, tuo recubantem corpore sancto\n\n \n\ncircum fusa super, suavis ex ore loquellas\n\n \n\nfunde petens placidam Romanis, incluta, pacem;\n\n \n\nnam neque nos agere hoc patriai tempore iniquo\n\n \n\npossumus aequo animo nec Memmi clara propago\n\n \n\ntalibus in rebus communi desse saluti.\n\n \n\nomnis enim per se divum natura necessest\n\n \n\nimmortali aevo summa cum pace fruatur\n\n \n\nsemota ab nostris rebus seiunctaque longe;\n\n \n\nnam privata dolore omni, privata periclis,\n\n \n\nipsa suis pollens opibus, nihil indiga nostri,\n\n \n\nnec bene promeritis capitur nec tangitur ira.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, vacuas auris animumque sagacem\n\n \n\nsemotum a curis adhibe veram ad rationem,\n\n \n\nne mea dona tibi studio disposta fideli,\n\n \n\nintellecta prius quam sint, contempta relinquas.\n\n \n\nnam tibi de summa caeli ratione deumque\n\n \n\ndisserere incipiam et rerum primordia pandam,\n\n \n\nunde omnis natura creet res, auctet alatque,\n\n \n\nquove eadem rursum natura perempta resolvat,\n\n \n\nquae nos materiem et genitalia corpora rebus\n\n \n\nreddunda in ratione vocare et semina rerum\n\n \n\nappellare suemus et haec eadem usurpare\n\n \n\ncorpora prima, quod ex illis sunt omnia primis.\n\n  \n\nHumana ante oculos foede cum vita iaceret\n\n \n\nin terris oppressa gravi sub religione,\n\n \n\nquae caput a caeli regionibus ostendebat\n\n \n\nhorribili super aspectu mortalibus instans,\n\n \n\nprimum Graius homo mortalis tollere contra\n\n \n\nest oculos ausus primusque obsistere contra;\n\n \n\nquem neque fama deum nec fulmina nec minitanti\n\n \n\nmurmure compressit caelum, sed eo magis acrem\n\n \n\ninritat animi virtutem, effringere ut arta\n\n \n\nnaturae primus portarum claustra cupiret.\n\n \n\nergo vivida vis animi pervicit et extra\n\n \n\nprocessit longe flammantia moenia mundi\n\n \n\natque omne immensum peragravit mente animoque,\n\n \n\nunde refert nobis victor quid possit oriri,\n\n \n\nquid nequeat, finita potestas denique cuique\n\n \n\nqua nam sit ratione atque alte terminus haerens.\n\n \n\nquare religio pedibus subiecta vicissim\n\n \n\nopteritur, nos exaequat victoria caelo.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus vereor, ne forte rearis\n\n \n\nimpia te rationis inire elementa viamque\n\n \n\nindugredi sceleris. quod contra saepius illa\n\n \n\nreligio peperit scelerosa atque impia facta.\n\n \n\nAulide quo pacto Triviai virginis aram\n\n \n\nIphianassai turparunt sanguine foede\n\n \n\nductores Danaum delecti, prima virorum.\n\n \n\ncui simul infula virgineos circum data comptus\n\n \n\nex utraque pari malarum parte profusast,\n\n \n\net maestum simul ante aras adstare parentem\n\n \n\nsensit et hunc propter ferrum celare ministros\n\n \n\naspectuque suo lacrimas effundere civis,\n\n \n\nmuta metu terram genibus summissa petebat.\n\n \n\nnec miserae prodesse in tali tempore quibat,\n\n \n\nquod patrio princeps donarat nomine regem;\n\n \n\nnam sublata virum manibus tremibundaque ad aras\n\n \n\ndeductast, non ut sollemni more sacrorum\n\n \n\nperfecto posset claro comitari Hymenaeo,\n\n \n\nsed casta inceste nubendi tempore in ipso\n\n \n\nhostia concideret mactatu maesta parentis,\n\n \n\nexitus ut classi felix faustusque daretur.\n\n \n\ntantum religio potuit suadere malorum.\n\n  \n\nTutemet a nobis iam quovis tempore vatum\n\n \n\nterriloquis victus dictis desciscere quaeres.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim quam multa tibi iam fingere possunt\n\n \n\nsomnia, quae vitae rationes vertere possint\n\n \n\nfortunasque tuas omnis turbare timore!\n\n \n\net merito; nam si certam finem esse viderent\n\n \n\naerumnarum homines, aliqua ratione valerent\n\n \n\nreligionibus atque minis obsistere vatum.\n\n \n\nnunc ratio nulla est restandi, nulla facultas,\n\n \n\naeternas quoniam poenas in morte timendum.\n\n \n\nignoratur enim quae sit natura animai,\n\n \n\nnata sit an contra nascentibus insinuetur\n\n \n\net simul intereat nobiscum morte dirempta\n\n \n\nan tenebras Orci visat vastasque lacunas\n\n \n\nan pecudes alias divinitus insinuet se,\n\n \n\nEnnius ut noster cecinit, qui primus amoeno\n\n \n\ndetulit ex Helicone perenni fronde coronam,\n\n \n\nper gentis Italas hominum quae clara clueret;\n\n \n\netsi praeterea tamen esse Acherusia templa\n\n \n\nEnnius aeternis exponit versibus edens,\n\n \n\nquo neque permaneant animae neque corpora nostra,\n\n \n\nsed quaedam simulacra modis pallentia miris;\n\n \n\nunde sibi exortam semper florentis Homeri\n\n \n\ncommemorat speciem lacrimas effundere salsas\n\n \n\ncoepisse et rerum naturam expandere dictis.\n\n \n\nqua propter bene cum superis de rebus habenda\n\n \n\nnobis est ratio, solis lunaeque meatus\n\n \n\nqua fiant ratione, et qua vi quaeque gerantur\n\n \n\nin terris, tunc cum primis ratione sagaci\n\n \n\nunde anima atque animi constet natura videndum,\n\n \n\net quae res nobis vigilantibus obvia mentes\n\n \n\nterrificet morbo adfectis somnoque sepultis,\n\n \n\ncernere uti videamur eos audireque coram,\n\n \n\nmorte obita quorum tellus amplectitur ossa.\n\n  \n\nNec me animi fallit Graiorum obscura reperta\n\n \n\ndifficile inlustrare Latinis versibus esse,\n\n \n\nmulta novis verbis praesertim cum sit agendum\n\n \n\npropter egestatem linguae et rerum novitatem;\n\n \n\nsed tua me virtus tamen et sperata voluptas\n\n \n\nsuavis amicitiae quemvis efferre laborem\n\n \n\nsuadet et inducit noctes vigilare serenas\n\n \n\nquaerentem dictis quibus et quo carmine demum\n\n \n\nclara tuae possim praepandere lumina menti,\n\n \n\nres quibus occultas penitus convisere possis.\n\n  \n\nhunc igitur terrorem animi tenebrasque necessest\n\n \n\nnon radii solis neque lucida tela diei\n\n \n\ndiscutiant, sed naturae species ratioque.\n\n \n\nPrincipium cuius hinc nobis exordia sumet,\n\n \n\nnullam rem e nihilo gigni divinitus umquam.\n\n \n\nquippe ita formido mortalis continet omnis,\n\n \n\nquod multa in terris fieri caeloque tuentur,\n\n \n\nquorum operum causas nulla ratione videre\n\n \n\npossunt ac fieri divino numine rentur.\n\n \n\nquas ob res ubi viderimus nil posse creari\n\n \n\nde nihilo, tum quod sequimur iam rectius inde\n\n \n\nperspiciemus, et unde queat res quaeque creari\n\n \n\net quo quaeque modo fiant opera sine divom.\n\n \n\nNam si de nihilo fierent, ex omnibus rebus\n\n \n\nomne genus nasci posset, nil semine egeret.\n\n \n\ne mare primum homines, e terra posset oriri\n\n \n\nsquamigerum genus et volucres erumpere caelo;\n\n \n\narmenta atque aliae pecudes, genus omne ferarum,\n\n \n\nincerto partu culta ac deserta tenerent.\n\n \n\nnec fructus idem arboribus constare solerent,\n\n \n\nsed mutarentur, ferre omnes omnia possent.\n\n \n\nquippe ubi non essent genitalia corpora cuique,\n\n \n\nqui posset mater rebus consistere certa?\n\n \n\nat nunc seminibus quia certis quaeque creantur,\n\n \n\ninde enascitur atque oras in luminis exit,\n\n \n\nmateries ubi inest cuiusque et corpora prima;\n\n \n\natque hac re nequeunt ex omnibus omnia gigni,\n\n \n\nquod certis in rebus inest secreta facultas.\n\n \n\nPraeterea cur vere rosam, frumenta calore,\n\n \n\nvites autumno fundi suadente videmus,\n\n \n\nsi non, certa suo quia tempore semina rerum\n\n \n\ncum confluxerunt, patefit quod cumque creatur,\n\n \n\ndum tempestates adsunt et vivida tellus\n\n \n\ntuto res teneras effert in luminis oras?\n\n \n\nquod si de nihilo fierent, subito exorerentur\n\n \n\nincerto spatio atque alienis partibus anni,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi nulla forent primordia, quae genitali\n\n \n\nconcilio possent arceri tempore iniquo.\n\n  \n\nNec porro augendis rebus spatio foret usus\n\n \n\nseminis ad coitum, si e nilo crescere possent;\n\n \n\nnam fierent iuvenes subito ex infantibus parvis\n\n \n\ne terraque exorta repente arbusta salirent.\n\n \n\nquorum nil fieri manifestum est, omnia quando\n\n \n\npaulatim crescunt, ut par est semine certo,\n\n \n\ncrescentesque genus servant; ut noscere possis\n\n \n\nquicque sua de materia grandescere alique.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit uti sine certis imbribus anni\n\n \n\nlaetificos nequeat fetus submittere tellus\n\n \n\nnec porro secreta cibo natura animantum\n\n \n\npropagare genus possit vitamque tueri;\n\n \n\nut potius multis communia corpora rebus\n\n \n\nmulta putes esse, ut verbis elementa videmus,\n\n \n\nquam sine principiis ullam rem existere posse.\n\n \n\nDenique cur homines tantos natura parare\n\n \n\nnon potuit, pedibus qui pontum per vada possent\n\n \n\ntransire et magnos manibus divellere montis\n\n \n\nmultaque vivendo vitalia vincere saecla,\n\n \n\nsi non, materies quia rebus reddita certast\n\n \n\ngignundis, e qua constat quid possit oriri?\n\n  \n\nnil igitur fieri de nilo posse fatendumst,\n\n \n\nsemine quando opus est rebus, quo quaeque creatae\n\n \n\naeris in teneras possint proferrier auras.\n\n \n\nPostremo quoniam incultis praestare videmus\n\n \n\nculta loca et manibus melioris reddere fetus,\n\n \n\nesse videlicet in terris primordia rerum\n\n \n\nquae nos fecundas vertentes vomere glebas\n\n \n\nterraique solum subigentes cimus ad ortus;\n\n \n\nquod si nulla forent, nostro sine quaeque labore\n\n \n\nsponte sua multo fieri meliora videres.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit uti quicque in sua corpora rursum\n\n \n\ndissoluat natura neque ad nihilum interemat res.\n\n \n\nnam siquid mortale e cunctis partibus esset,\n\n \n\nex oculis res quaeque repente erepta periret;\n\n \n\nnulla vi foret usus enim, quae partibus eius\n\n \n\ndiscidium parere et nexus exsolvere posset.\n\n \n\nquod nunc, aeterno quia constant semine quaeque,\n\n \n\ndonec vis obiit, quae res diverberet ictu\n\n \n\naut intus penetret per inania dissoluatque,\n\n \n\nnullius exitium patitur natura videri.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quae cumque vetustate amovet aetas,\n\n \n\nsi penitus peremit consumens materiem omnem,\n\n \n\nunde animale genus generatim in lumina vitae\n\n \n\nredducit Venus, aut redductum daedala tellus\n\n \n\nunde alit atque auget generatim pabula praebens?\n\n \n\nunde mare ingenuei fontes externaque longe\n\n \n\nflumina suppeditant? unde aether sidera pascit?\n\n \n\nomnia enim debet, mortali corpore quae sunt,\n\n \n\ninfinita aetas consumpse ante acta diesque.\n\n \n\nquod si in eo spatio atque ante acta aetate fuere\n\n \n\ne quibus haec rerum consistit summa refecta,\n\n \n\ninmortali sunt natura praedita certe.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur possunt ad nilum quaeque reverti.\n\n \n\nDenique res omnis eadem vis causaque volgo\n\n \n\nconficeret, nisi materies aeterna teneret,\n\n \n\ninter se nexus minus aut magis indupedita;\n\n \n\ntactus enim leti satis esset causa profecto,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi nulla forent aeterno corpore, quorum\n\n \n\ncontextum vis deberet dissolvere quaeque.\n\n \n\nat nunc, inter se quia nexus principiorum\n\n \n\ndissimiles constant aeternaque materies est,\n\n \n\nincolumi remanent res corpore, dum satis acris\n\n \n\nvis obeat pro textura cuiusque reperta.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur redit ad nihilum res ulla, sed omnes\n\n \n\ndiscidio redeunt in corpora materiai.\n\n \n\npostremo pereunt imbres, ubi eos pater aether\n\n \n\nin gremium matris terrai praecipitavit;\n\n \n\nat nitidae surgunt fruges ramique virescunt\n\n \n\narboribus, crescunt ipsae fetuque gravantur.\n\n \n\nhinc alitur porro nostrum genus atque ferarum,\n\n \n\nhinc laetas urbes pueris florere videmus\n\n \n\nfrondiferasque novis avibus canere undique silvas,\n\n \n\nhinc fessae pecudes pinguis per pabula laeta\n\n \n\ncorpora deponunt et candens lacteus umor\n\n \n\nuberibus manat distentis, hinc nova proles\n\n \n\nartubus infirmis teneras lasciva per herbas\n\n \n\nludit lacte mero mentes perculsa novellas.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur penitus pereunt quaecumque videntur,\n\n \n\nquando alit ex alio reficit natura nec ullam\n\n \n\nrem gigni patitur nisi morte adiuta aliena.\n\n \n\nNunc age, res quoniam docui non posse creari\n\n \n\nde nihilo neque item genitas ad nil revocari,\n\n \n\nne qua forte tamen coeptes diffidere dictis,\n\n \n\nquod nequeunt oculis rerum primordia cerni,\n\n \n\naccipe praeterea quae corpora tute necessest\n\n \n\nconfiteare esse in rebus nec posse videri.\n\n \n\nPrincipio venti vis verberat incita corpus\n\n \n\ningentisque ruit navis et nubila differt,\n\n \n\ninter dum rapido percurrens turbine campos\n\n \n\narboribus magnis sternit montisque supremos\n\n \n\nsilvifragis vexat flabris: ita perfurit acri\n\n \n\ncum fremitu saevitque minaci murmure pontus.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur venti ni mirum corpora caeca,\n\n \n\nquae mare, quae terras, quae denique nubila caeli\n\n \n\nverrunt ac subito vexantia turbine raptant,\n\n \n\nnec ratione fluunt alia stragemque propagant\n\n \n\net cum mollis aquae fertur natura repente\n\n \n\nflumine abundanti, quam largis imbribus auget\n\n \n\nmontibus ex altis magnus decursus aquai\n\n \n\nfragmina coniciens silvarum arbustaque tota,\n\n \n\nnec validi possunt pontes venientis aquai\n\n \n\nvim subitam tolerare: ita magno turbidus imbri\n\n \n\nmolibus incurrit validis cum viribus amnis,\n\n \n\ndat sonitu magno stragem volvitque sub undis\n\n \n\ngrandia saxa, ruit qua quidquid fluctibus obstat.\n\n \n\nsic igitur debent venti quoque flamina ferri,\n\n \n\nquae vel uti validum cum flumen procubuere\n\n \n\nquam libet in partem, trudunt res ante ruuntque\n\n \n\nimpetibus crebris, inter dum vertice torto\n\n \n\ncorripiunt rapidique rotanti turbine portant.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam sunt venti corpora caeca,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem factis et moribus aemula magnis\n\n \n\namnibus inveniuntur, aperto corpore qui sunt.\n\n \n\nTum porro varios rerum sentimus odores\n\n \n\nnec tamen ad naris venientis cernimus umquam\n\n \n\nnec calidos aestus tuimur nec frigora quimus\n\n \n\nusurpare oculis nec voces cernere suemus;\n\n \n\nquae tamen omnia corporea constare necessest\n\n \n\nnatura, quoniam sensus inpellere possunt;\n\n \n\ntangere enim et tangi, nisi corpus, nulla potest res.\n\n \n\nDenique fluctifrago suspensae in litore vestis\n\n \n\nuvescunt, eaedem dispansae in sole serescunt.\n\n \n\nat neque quo pacto persederit umor aquai\n\n \n\nvisumst nec rursum quo pacto fugerit aestu.\n\n \n\nin parvas igitur partis dispergitur umor,\n\n \n\nquas oculi nulla possunt ratione videre.\n\n \n\nquin etiam multis solis redeuntibus annis\n\n \n\nanulus in digito subter tenuatur habendo,\n\n \n\nstilicidi casus lapidem cavat, uncus aratri\n\n \n\nferreus occulte decrescit vomer in arvis,\n\n \n\nstrataque iam volgi pedibus detrita viarum\n\n \n\nsaxea conspicimus; tum portas propter aena\n\n \n\nsigna manus dextras ostendunt adtenuari\n\n \n\nsaepe salutantum tactu praeterque meantum.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur minui, cum sint detrita, videmus.\n\n \n\nsed quae corpora decedant in tempore quoque,\n\n \n\ninvida praeclusit speciem natura videndi.\n\n \n\nPostremo quae cumque dies naturaque rebus\n\n \n\npaulatim tribuit moderatim crescere cogens,\n\n \n\nnulla potest oculorum acies contenta tueri,\n\n \n\nnec porro quae cumque aevo macieque senescunt,\n\n \n\nnec, mare quae impendent, vesco sale saxa peresa\n\n \n\nquid quoque amittant in tempore cernere possis.\n\n \n\ncorporibus caecis igitur natura gerit res.\n\n \n\nNec tamen undique corporea stipata tenentur\n\n \n\nomnia natura; namque est in rebus inane.\n\n \n\nquod tibi cognosse in multis erit utile rebus\n\n \n\nnec sinet errantem dubitare et quaerere semper\n\n \n\nde summa rerum et nostris diffidere dictis.\n\n \n\nqua propter locus est intactus inane vacansque.\n\n \n\nquod si non esset, nulla ratione moveri\n\n \n\nres possent; namque officium quod corporis exstat,\n\n \n\nofficere atque obstare, id in omni tempore adesset\n\n \n\nomnibus; haud igitur quicquam procedere posset,\n\n \n\nprincipium quoniam cedendi nulla daret res.\n\n \n\nat nunc per maria ac terras sublimaque caeli\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis varia ratione moveri\n\n \n\ncernimus ante oculos, quae, si non esset inane,\n\n \n\nnon tam sollicito motu privata carerent\n\n \n\nquam genita omnino nulla ratione fuissent,\n\n \n\nundique materies quoniam stipata quiesset.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quamvis solidae res esse putentur,\n\n \n\nhinc tamen esse licet raro cum corpore cernas.\n\n \n\nin saxis ac speluncis permanat aquarum\n\n \n\nliquidus umor et uberibus flent omnia guttis.\n\n \n\ndissipat in corpus sese cibus omne animantum;\n\n \n\ncrescunt arbusta et fetus in tempore fundunt,\n\n \n\nquod cibus in totas usque ab radicibus imis\n\n \n\nper truncos ac per ramos diffunditur omnis.\n\n \n\ninter saepta meant voces et clausa domorum\n\n \n\ntransvolitant, rigidum permanat frigus ad ossa.\n\n \n\nquod nisi inania sint, qua possent corpora quaeque\n\n \n\ntransire, haud ulla fieri ratione videres.\n\n \n\nDenique cur alias aliis praestare videmus\n\n \n\npondere res rebus nihilo maiore figura?\n\n \n\nnam si tantundemst in lanae glomere quantum\n\n \n\ncorporis in plumbo est, tantundem pendere par est,\n\n \n\ncorporis officiumst quoniam premere omnia deorsum,\n\n \n\ncontra autem natura manet sine pondere inanis.\n\n \n\nergo quod magnumst aeque leviusque videtur,\n\n \n\nni mirum plus esse sibi declarat inanis;\n\n \n\nat contra gravius plus in se corporis esse\n\n \n\ndedicat et multo vacui minus intus habere.\n\n \n\nest igitur ni mirum id quod ratione sagaci\n\n \n\nquaerimus, admixtum rebus, quod inane vocamus.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus ne te deducere vero\n\n \n\npossit, quod quidam fingunt, praecurrere cogor.\n\n \n\ncedere squamigeris latices nitentibus aiunt\n\n \n\net liquidas aperire vias, quia post loca pisces\n\n \n\nlinquant, quo possint cedentes confluere undae;\n\n \n\nsic alias quoque res inter se posse moveri\n\n \n\net mutare locum, quamvis sint omnia plena.\n\n \n\nscilicet id falsa totum ratione receptumst.\n\n \n\nnam quo squamigeri poterunt procedere tandem,\n\n \n\nni spatium dederint latices? concedere porro\n\n \n\nquo poterunt undae, cum pisces ire nequibunt?\n\n \n\naut igitur motu privandumst corpora quaeque\n\n \n\naut esse admixtum dicundumst rebus inane,\n\n \n\nunde initum primum capiat res quaeque movendi.\n\n \n\nPostremo duo de concursu corpora lata\n\n \n\nsi cita dissiliant, nempe aer omne necessest,\n\n \n\ninter corpora quod fiat, possidat inane.\n\n \n\nis porro quamvis circum celerantibus auris\n\n \n\nconfluat, haud poterit tamen uno tempore totum\n\n \n\ncompleri spatium; nam primum quemque necessest\n\n \n\noccupet ille locum, deinde omnia possideantur.\n\n \n\nquod si forte aliquis, cum corpora dissiluere,\n\n \n\ntum putat id fieri quia se condenseat aer,\n\n \n\nerrat; nam vacuum tum fit quod non fuit ante\n\n \n\net repletur item vacuum quod constitit ante,\n\n \n\nnec tali ratione potest denserier aer\n\n \n\nnec, si iam posset, sine inani posset, opinor,\n\n \n\nipse in se trahere et partis conducere in unum.\n\n \n\nQua propter, quamvis causando multa moreris,\n\n \n\nesse in rebus inane tamen fateare necessest.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea tibi possum commemorando\n\n \n\nargumenta fidem dictis conradere nostris.\n\n \n\nverum animo satis haec vestigia parva sagaci\n\n \n\nsunt, per quae possis cognoscere cetera tute.\n\n \n\nnamque canes ut montivagae persaepe ferai\n\n \n\nnaribus inveniunt intectas fronde quietes,\n\n \n\ncum semel institerunt vestigia certa viai,\n\n \n\nsic alid ex alio per te tute ipse videre\n\n \n\ntalibus in rebus poteris caecasque latebras\n\n \n\ninsinuare omnis et verum protrahere inde.\n\n \n\nquod si pigraris paulumve recesseris ab re,\n\n \n\nhoc tibi de plano possum promittere, Memmi:\n\n \n\nusque adeo largos haustus e fontibus magnis\n\n \n\nlingua meo suavis diti de pectore fundet,\n\n \n\nut verear ne tarda prius per membra senectus\n\n \n\nserpat et in nobis vitai claustra resolvat,\n\n \n\nquam tibi de quavis una re versibus omnis\n\n \n\nargumentorum sit copia missa per auris.\n\n \n\nSed nunc ut repetam coeptum pertexere dictis,\n\n \n\nomnis ut est igitur per se natura duabus\n\n \n\nconstitit in rebus; nam corpora sunt et inane,\n\n \n\nhaec in quo sita sunt et qua diversa moventur.\n\n \n\ncorpus enim per se communis dedicat esse\n\n \n\nsensus; cui nisi prima fides fundata valebit,\n\n \n\nhaut erit occultis de rebus quo referentes\n\n \n\nconfirmare animi quicquam ratione queamus.\n\n \n\ntum porro locus ac spatium, quod inane vocamus,\n\n \n\nsi nullum foret, haut usquam sita corpora possent\n\n \n\nesse neque omnino quoquam diversa meare;\n\n \n\nid quod iam supera tibi paulo ostendimus ante.\n\n \n\npraeterea nihil est quod possis dicere ab omni\n\n \n\ncorpore seiunctum secretumque esse ab inani,\n\n \n\nquod quasi tertia sit numero natura reperta.\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque erit, esse aliquid debebit id ipsum\n\n \n\naugmine vel grandi vel parvo denique, dum sit;\n\n \n\ncui si tactus erit quamvis levis exiguusque,\n\n \n\ncorporis augebit numerum summamque sequetur;\n\n \n\nsin intactile erit, nulla de parte quod ullam\n\n \n\nrem prohibere queat per se transire meantem,\n\n \n\nscilicet hoc id erit, vacuum quod inane vocamus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea per se quod cumque erit, aut faciet quid\n\n \n\naut aliis fungi debebit agentibus ipsum\n\n \n\naut erit ut possint in eo res esse gerique.\n\n \n\nat facere et fungi sine corpore nulla potest res\n\n \n\nnec praebere locum porro nisi inane vacansque.\n\n \n\nergo praeter inane et corpora tertia per se\n\n \n\nnulla potest rerum in numero natura relinqui,\n\n \n\nnec quae sub sensus cadat ullo tempore nostros\n\n \n\nnec ratione animi quam quisquam possit apisci.\n\n \n\nNam quae cumque cluent, aut his coniuncta duabus\n\n \n\nrebus ea invenies aut horum eventa videbis.\n\n \n\nconiunctum est id quod nusquam sine permitiali\n\n \n\ndiscidio potis est seiungi seque gregari,\n\n \n\npondus uti saxis, calor ignis, liquor aquai,\n\n \n\ntactus corporibus cunctis, intactus inani.\n\n \n\nservitium contra paupertas divitiaeque,\n\n \n\nlibertas bellum concordia cetera quorum\n\n \n\nadventu manet incolumis natura abituque,\n\n \n\nhaec soliti sumus, ut par est, eventa vocare.\n\n \n\ntempus item per se non est, sed rebus ab ipsis\n\n \n\nconsequitur sensus, transactum quid sit in aevo,\n\n \n\ntum quae res instet, quid porro deinde sequatur;\n\n \n\nnec per se quemquam tempus sentire fatendumst\n\n \n\nsemotum ab rerum motu placidaque quiete.\n\n \n\ndenique Tyndaridem raptam belloque subactas\n\n \n\nTroiiugenas gentis cum dicunt esse, videndumst\n\n \n\nne forte haec per se cogant nos esse fateri,\n\n \n\nquando ea saecla hominum, quorum haec eventa fuerunt,\n\n \n\ninrevocabilis abstulerit iam praeterita aetas;\n\n \n\nnamque aliud terris, aliud regionibus ipsis\n\n \n\neventum dici poterit quod cumque erit actum.\n\n \n\ndenique materies si rerum nulla fuisset\n\n \n\nnec locus ac spatium, res in quo quaeque geruntur,\n\n \n\nnumquam Tyndaridis forma conflatus amore\n\n \n\nignis Alexandri Phrygio sub pectore gliscens\n\n \n\nclara accendisset saevi certamina belli\n\n \n\nnec clam durateus Troiianis Pergama partu\n\n \n\ninflammasset equos nocturno Graiiugenarum;\n\n \n\nperspicere ut possis res gestas funditus omnis\n\n \n\nnon ita uti corpus per se constare neque esse\n\n \n\nnec ratione cluere eadem qua constet inane,\n\n \n\nsed magis ut merito possis eventa vocare\n\n \n\ncorporis atque loci, res in quo quaeque gerantur.\n\n \n\nCorpora sunt porro partim primordia rerum,\n\n \n\npartim concilio quae constant principiorum.\n\n \n\nsed quae sunt rerum primordia, nulla potest vis\n\n \n\nstinguere; nam solido vincunt ea corpore demum.\n\n \n\netsi difficile esse videtur credere quicquam\n\n \n\nin rebus solido reperiri corpore posse.\n\n \n\ntransit enim fulmen caeli per saepta domorum\n\n \n\nclamor ut ac voces, ferrum candescit in igni\n\n \n\ndissiliuntque fero ferventi saxa vapore;\n\n \n\ncum labefactatus rigor auri solvitur aestu,\n\n \n\ntum glacies aeris flamma devicta liquescit;\n\n \n\npermanat calor argentum penetraleque frigus,\n\n \n\nquando utrumque manu retinentes pocula rite\n\n \n\nsensimus infuso lympharum rore superne.\n\n \n\nusque adeo in rebus solidi nihil esse videtur.\n\n \n\nsed quia vera tamen ratio naturaque rerum\n\n \n\ncogit, ades, paucis dum versibus expediamus\n\n \n\nesse ea quae solido atque aeterno corpore constent,\n\n \n\nsemina quae rerum primordiaque esse docemus,\n\n \n\nunde omnis rerum nunc constet summa creata.\n\n \n\nPrincipio quoniam duplex natura duarum\n\n \n\ndissimilis rerum longe constare repertast,\n\n \n\ncorporis atque loci, res in quo quaeque geruntur,\n\n \n\nesse utramque sibi per se puramque necessest.\n\n \n\nnam qua cumque vacat spatium, quod inane vocamus,\n\n \n\ncorpus ea non est; qua porro cumque tenet se\n\n \n\ncorpus, ea vacuum nequaquam constat inane.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur solida ac sine inani corpora prima.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam genitis in rebus inanest,\n\n \n\nmateriem circum solidam constare necessest;\n\n \n\nnec res ulla potest vera ratione probari\n\n \n\ncorpore inane suo celare atque intus habere,\n\n \n\nsi non, quod cohibet, solidum constare relinquas.\n\n \n\nid porro nihil esse potest nisi materiai\n\n \n\nconcilium, quod inane queat rerum cohibere.\n\n \n\nmateries igitur, solido quae corpore constat,\n\n \n\nesse aeterna potest, cum cetera dissoluantur.\n\n \n\nTum porro si nil esset quod inane vocaret,\n\n \n\nomne foret solidum; nisi contra corpora certa\n\n \n\nessent quae loca complerent quae cumque tenerent\n\n \n\nomne quod est spatium, vacuum constaret inane.\n\n \n\nalternis igitur ni mirum corpus inani\n\n \n\ndistinctum, quoniam nec plenum naviter extat\n\n \n\nnec porro vacuum; sunt ergo corpora certa,\n\n \n\nquae spatium pleno possint distinguere inane.\n\n \n\nhaec neque dissolui plagis extrinsecus icta\n\n \n\npossunt nec porro penitus penetrata retexi\n\n \n\nnec ratione queunt alia temptata labare;\n\n \n\nid quod iam supra tibi paulo ostendimus ante.\n\n \n\nnam neque conlidi sine inani posse videtur\n\n \n\nquicquam nec frangi nec findi in bina secando\n\n \n\nnec capere umorem neque item manabile frigus\n\n \n\nnec penetralem ignem, quibus omnia conficiuntur.\n\n \n\net quo quaeque magis cohibet res intus inane,\n\n \n\ntam magis his rebus penitus temptata labascit.\n\n \n\nergo si solida ac sine inani corpora prima\n\n \n\nsunt ita uti docui, sint haec aeterna necessest.\n\n \n\nPraeterea nisi materies aeterna fuisset,\n\n \n\nantehac ad nihilum penitus res quaeque redissent\n\n \n\nde nihiloque renata forent quae cumque videmus.\n\n \n\nat quoniam supra docui nil posse creari\n\n \n\nde nihilo neque quod genitumst ad nil revocari,\n\n \n\nesse inmortali primordia corpore debent,\n\n \n\ndissolui quo quaeque supremo tempore possint,\n\n \n\nmateries ut subpeditet rebus reparandis.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur solida primordia simplicitate\n\n \n\nnec ratione queunt alia servata per aevom\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore res reparare.\n\n  \n\ndenique si nullam finem natura parasset\n\n \n\nfrangendis rebus, iam corpora materiai\n\n \n\nusque redacta forent aevo frangente priore,\n\n \n\nut nihil ex illis a certo tempore posset\n\n \n\nconceptum summum aetatis pervadere finem.\n\n \n\nnam quidvis citius dissolvi posse videmus\n\n \n\nquam rursus refici; qua propter longa diei\n\n \n\ninfinita aetas ante acti temporis omnis\n\n \n\nquod fregisset adhuc disturbans dissoluensque,\n\n \n\nnumquam relicuo reparari tempore posset.\n\n \n\nat nunc ni mirum frangendi reddita finis\n\n \n\ncerta manet, quoniam refici rem quamque videmus\n\n \n\net finita simul generatim tempora rebus\n\n \n\nstare, quibus possint aevi contingere florem.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit uti, solidissima materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora cum constant, possint tamen omnia reddi,\n\n \n\nmollia quae fiunt, aer aqua terra vapores,\n\n \n\nquo pacto fiant et qua vi quaeque gerantur,\n\n \n\nadmixtum quoniam semel est in rebus inane.\n\n \n\nat contra si mollia sint primordia rerum,\n\n \n\nunde queant validi silices ferrumque creari,\n\n \n\nnon poterit ratio reddi; nam funditus omnis\n\n \n\nprincipio fundamenti natura carebit.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur solida pollentia simplicitate,\n\n \n\nquorum condenso magis omnia conciliatu\n\n \n\nartari possunt validasque ostendere viris.\n\n \n\nporro si nullast frangendis reddita finis\n\n \n\ncorporibus, tamen ex aeterno tempore quaeque\n\n \n\nnunc etiam superare necessest corpora rebus,\n\n \n\nquae non dum clueant ullo temptata periclo.\n\n \n\nat quoniam fragili natura praedita constant,\n\n \n\ndiscrepat aeternum tempus potuisse manere\n\n \n\ninnumerabilibus plagis vexata per aevom.\n\n \n\nDenique iam quoniam generatim reddita finis\n\n \n\ncrescendi rebus constat vitamque tenendi,\n\n \n\net quid quaeque queant per foedera naturai,\n\n \n\nquid porro nequeant, sancitum quando quidem extat,\n\n \n\nnec commutatur quicquam, quin omnia constant\n\n \n\nusque adeo, variae volucres ut in ordine cunctae\n\n \n\nostendant maculas generalis corpore inesse,\n\n \n\ninmutabilis materiae quoque corpus habere\n\n \n\ndebent ni mirum; nam si primordia rerum\n\n \n\ncommutari aliqua possent ratione revicta,\n\n \n\nincertum quoque iam constet quid possit oriri,\n\n \n\nquid nequeat, finita potestas denique cuique\n\n \n\nqua nam sit ratione atque alte terminus haerens,\n\n \n\nnec totiens possent generatim saecla referre\n\n \n\nnaturam mores victum motusque parentum.\n\n \n\nTum porro quoniam est extremum quodque cacumen\n\n \n\ncorporis illius, quod nostri cernere sensus\n\n \n\niam nequeunt, id ni mirum sine partibus extat\n\n \n\net minima constat natura nec fuit umquam\n\n \n\nper se secretum neque post hac esse valebit,\n\n \n\nalterius quoniamst ipsum pars primaque et una,\n\n \n\ninde aliae atque aliae similes ex ordine partes\n\n \n\nagmine condenso naturam corporis explent;\n\n \n\nquae quoniam per se nequeunt constare, necessest\n\n \n\nhaerere unde queant nulla ratione revelli.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur solida primordia simplicitate,\n\n \n\nquae minimis stipata cohaerent partibus arte.\n\n \n\nnon ex illorum conventu conciliata,\n\n \n\nsed magis aeterna pollentia simplicitate,\n\n \n\nunde neque avelli quicquam neque deminui iam\n\n \n\nconcedit natura reservans semina rebus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea nisi erit minimum, parvissima quaeque\n\n \n\ncorpora constabunt ex partibus infinitis,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi dimidiae partis pars semper habebit\n\n \n\ndimidiam partem nec res praefiniet ulla.\n\n \n\nergo rerum inter summam minimamque quod escit,\n\n \n\nnil erit ut distet; nam quamvis funditus omnis\n\n \n\nsumma sit infinita, tamen, parvissima quae sunt,\n\n \n\nex infinitis constabunt partibus aeque.\n\n \n\nquod quoniam ratio reclamat vera negatque\n\n \n\ncredere posse animum, victus fateare necessest\n\n \n\nesse ea quae nullis iam praedita partibus extent\n\n \n\net minima constent natura. quae quoniam sunt,\n\n \n\nilla quoque esse tibi solida atque aeterna fatendum.\n\n \n\nDenique si minimas in partis cuncta resolvi\n\n \n\ncogere consuesset rerum natura creatrix,\n\n \n\niam nihil ex illis eadem reparare valeret\n\n \n\npropterea quia, quae nullis sunt partibus aucta,\n\n \n\nnon possunt ea quae debet genitalis habere\n\n \n\nmateries, varios conexus pondera plagas\n\n \n\nconcursus motus, per quas res quaeque geruntur.\n\n  \n\nQuapropter qui materiem rerum esse putarunt\n\n \n\nignem atque ex igni summam consistere solo,\n\n \n\nmagno opere a vera lapsi ratione videntur.\n\n \n\nHeraclitus init quorum dux proelia primus,\n\n \n\nclarus ob obscuram linguam magis inter inanis\n\n \n\nquamde gravis inter Graios, qui vera requirunt;\n\n \n\nomnia enim stolidi magis admirantur amantque,\n\n \n\ninversis quae sub verbis latitantia cernunt,\n\n \n\nveraque constituunt quae belle tangere possunt\n\n \n\nauris et lepido quae sunt fucata sonore.\n\n \n\nNam cur tam variae res possent esse, requiro,\n\n \n\nex uno si sunt igni puroque creatae?\n\n \n\nnil prodesset enim calidum denserier ignem\n\n \n\nnec rare fieri, si partes ignis eandem\n\n \n\nnaturam quam totus habet super ignis haberent.\n\n \n\nacrior ardor enim conductis partibus esset,\n\n \n\nlanguidior porro disiectis disque supatis.\n\n \n\namplius hoc fieri nihil est quod posse rearis\n\n \n\ntalibus in causis, ne dum variantia rerum\n\n \n\ntanta queat densis rarisque ex ignibus esse.\n\n \n\nId quoque: si faciant admixtum rebus inane,\n\n \n\ndenseri poterunt ignes rarique relinqui;\n\n \n\nsed quia multa sibi cernunt contraria quae sint\n\n \n\net fugitant in rebus inane relinquere purum,\n\n \n\nardua dum metuunt, amittunt vera viai\n\n \n\nnec rursum cernunt exempto rebus inane\n\n \n\nomnia denseri fierique ex omnibus unum\n\n \n\ncorpus, nil ab se quod possit mittere raptim,\n\n \n\naestifer ignis uti lumen iacit atque vaporem,\n\n \n\nut videas non e stipatis partibus esse.\n\n \n\nQuod si forte alia credunt ratione potesse\n\n \n\nignis in coetu stingui mutareque corpus,\n\n \n\nscilicet ex nulla facere id si parte reparcent,\n\n \n\noccidet ad nihilum ni mirum funditus ardor\n\n \n\nomnis et e nihilo fient quae cumque creantur;\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque suis mutatum finibus exit,\n\n \n\ncontinuo hoc mors est illius quod fuit ante.\n\n \n\nproinde aliquid superare necesse est incolume ollis,\n\n \n\nne tibi res redeant ad nilum funditus omnes\n\n \n\nde nihiloque renata vigescat copia rerum.\n\n \n\nNunc igitur quoniam certissima corpora quaedam\n\n \n\nsunt, quae conservant naturam semper eandem,\n\n \n\nquorum abitu aut aditu mutatoque ordine mutant\n\n \n\nnaturam res et convertunt corpora sese,\n\n \n\nscire licet non esse haec ignea corpora rerum.\n\n \n\nnil referret enim quaedam decedere, abire\n\n \n\natque alia adtribui mutarique ordine quaedam,\n\n \n\nsi tamen ardoris naturam cuncta tenerent;\n\n \n\nignis enim foret omnimodis quod cumque crearet.\n\n \n\nverum, ut opinor, itast: sunt quaedam corpora, quorum\n\n \n\nconcursus motus ordo positura figurae\n\n \n\nefficiunt ignis mutatoque ordine mutant\n\n \n\nnaturam neque sunt igni simulata neque ulli\n\n \n\npraeterea rei quae corpora mittere possit\n\n \n\nsensibus et nostros adiectu tangere tactus.\n\n \n\ndicere porro ignem res omnis esse neque ullam\n\n \n\nrem veram in numero rerum constare nisi ignem,\n\n \n\nquod facit hic idem, perdelirum esse videtur.\n\n \n\nnam contra sensus ab sensibus ipse repugnat\n\n \n\net labefactat eos, unde omnia credita pendent,\n\n \n\nunde hic cognitus est ipsi quem nominat ignem;\n\n \n\ncredit enim sensus ignem cognoscere vere,\n\n \n\ncetera non credit, quae nilo clara minus sunt.\n\n \n\nquod mihi cum vanum tum delirum esse videtur;\n\n \n\nquo referemus enim? quid nobis certius ipsis\n\n \n\nsensibus esse potest, qui vera ac falsa notemus?\n\n \n\nPraeterea quare quisquam magis omnia tollat\n\n \n\net velit ardoris naturam linquere solam,\n\n \n\nquam neget esse ignis, aliam tamen esse relinquat?\n\n \n\naequa videtur enim dementia dicere utrumque.\n\n \n\nQuapropter qui materiem rerum esse putarunt\n\n \n\nignem atque ex igni summam consistere posse,\n\n \n\net qui principium gignundis aera rebus\n\n \n\nconstituere aut umorem qui cumque putarunt\n\n \n\nfingere res ipsum per se terramve creare\n\n \n\nomnia et in rerum naturas vertier omnis,\n\n \n\nmagno opere a vero longe derrasse videntur.\n\n \n\nadde etiam qui conduplicant primordia rerum\n\n \n\naera iungentes igni terramque liquori,\n\n \n\net qui quattuor ex rebus posse omnia rentur\n\n \n\nex igni terra atque anima procrescere et imbri.\n\n \n\nquorum Acragantinus cum primis Empedocles est,\n\n \n\ninsula quem triquetris terrarum gessit in oris,\n\n \n\nquam fluitans circum magnis anfractibus aequor\n\n \n\nIonium glaucis aspargit virus ab undis\n\n \n\nangustoque fretu rapidum mare dividit undis\n\n \n\nAeoliae terrarum oras a finibus eius.\n\n \n\nhic est vasta Charybdis et hic Aetnaea minantur\n\n \n\nmurmura flammarum rursum se colligere iras,\n\n \n\nfaucibus eruptos iterum vis ut vomat ignis\n\n \n\nad caelumque ferat flammai fulgura rursum.\n\n \n\nquae cum magna modis multis miranda videtur\n\n \n\ngentibus humanis regio visendaque fertur\n\n \n\nrebus opima bonis, multa munita virum vi,\n\n \n\nnil tamen hoc habuisse viro praeclarius in se\n\n \n\nnec sanctum magis et mirum carumque videtur.\n\n \n\ncarmina quin etiam divini pectoris eius\n\n \n\nvociferantur et exponunt praeclara reperta,\n\n \n\nut vix humana videatur stirpe creatus.\n\n \n\nHic tamen et supra quos diximus inferiores\n\n \n\npartibus egregie multis multoque minores,\n\n \n\nquamquam multa bene ac divinitus invenientes\n\n \n\nex adyto tam quam cordis responsa dedere\n\n \n\nsanctius et multo certa ratione magis quam\n\n \n\nPythia quae tripodi a Phoebi lauroque profatur,\n\n \n\nprincipiis tamen in rerum fecere ruinas\n\n \n\net graviter magni magno cecidere ibi casu.\n\n \n\nPrimum quod motus exempto rebus inani\n\n \n\nconstituunt et res mollis rarasque relinquunt\n\n \n\naera solem ignem terras animalia frugis\n\n \n\nnec tamen admiscent in eorum corpus inane;\n\n \n\ndeinde quod omnino finem non esse secandis\n\n \n\ncorporibus facient neque pausam stare fragori\n\n \n\nnec prorsum in rebus minimum consistere quicquam,\n\n \n\ncum videamus id extremum cuiusque cacumen\n\n \n\nesse quod ad sensus nostros minimum esse videtur,\n\n \n\nconicere ut possis ex hoc, quae cernere non quis\n\n \n\nextremum quod habent, minimum consistere rerum.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit item, quoniam primordia rerum\n\n \n\nmollia constituunt, quae nos nativa videmus\n\n \n\nesse et mortali cum corpore, funditus ut qui\n\n \n\ndebeat ad nihilum iam rerum summa reverti\n\n \n\nde nihiloque renata vigescere copia rerum;\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque quid a vero iam distet habebis.\n\n \n\nDeinde inimica modis multis sunt atque veneno\n\n \n\nipsa sibi inter se; quare aut congressa peribunt\n\n \n\naut ita diffugient, ut tempestate coacta\n\n \n\nfulmina diffugere atque imbris ventosque videmus.\n\n \n\nDenique quattuor ex rebus si cuncta creantur\n\n \n\natque in eas rursum res omnia dissoluuntur,\n\n \n\nqui magis illa queunt rerum primordia dici\n\n \n\nquam contra res illorum retroque putari?\n\n \n\nalternis gignuntur enim mutantque colorem\n\n \n\net totam inter se naturam tempore ab omni.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nsin ita forte putas ignis terraeque coire\n\n \n\ncorpus et aerias auras roremque liquoris,\n\n \n\nnil in concilio naturam ut mutet eorum,\n\n \n\nnulla tibi ex illis poterit res esse creata,\n\n \n\nnon animans, non exanimo cum corpore, ut arbos;\n\n \n\nquippe suam quicque in coetu variantis acervi\n\n \n\nnaturam ostendet mixtusque videbitur aer\n\n \n\ncum terra simul et quodam cum rore manere.\n\n \n\nat primordia gignundis in rebus oportet\n\n \n\nnaturam clandestinam caecamque adhibere,\n\n \n\nemineat ne quid, quod contra pugnet et obstet\n\n \n\nquo minus esse queat proprie quodcumque creatur.\n\n \n\nQuin etiam repetunt a caelo atque ignibus eius\n\n \n\net primum faciunt ignem se vertere in auras\n\n \n\naeris, hinc imbrem gigni terramque creari\n\n \n\nex imbri retroque a terra cuncta reverti,\n\n \n\numorem primum, post aera, deinde calorem,\n\n \n\nnec cessare haec inter se mutare, meare\n\n \n\na caelo ad terram, de terra ad sidera mundi.\n\n \n\nquod facere haud ullo debent primordia pacto.\n\n \n\nimmutabile enim quiddam superare necessest,\n\n \n\nne res ad nihilum redigantur funditus omnes;\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque suis mutatum finibus exit,\n\n \n\ncontinuo hoc mors est illius quod fuit ante.\n\n \n\nquapropter quoniam quae paulo diximus ante\n\n \n\nin commutatum veniunt, constare necessest\n\n \n\nex aliis ea, quae nequeant convertier usquam,\n\n \n\nne tibi res redeant ad nilum funditus omnis;\n\n \n\nquin potius tali natura praedita quaedam\n\n \n\ncorpora constituas, ignem si forte crearint,\n\n \n\nposse eadem demptis paucis paucisque tributis,\n\n \n\nordine mutato et motu, facere aeris auras,\n\n \n\nsic alias aliis rebus mutarier omnis?\n\n \n\n'At manifesta palam res indicat' inquis 'in auras\n\n \n\naeris e terra res omnis crescere alique;\n\n \n\net nisi tempestas indulget tempore fausto\n\n \n\nimbribus, ut tabe nimborum arbusta vacillent,\n\n \n\nsolque sua pro parte fovet tribuitque calorem,\n\n \n\ncrescere non possint fruges arbusta animantis.'\n\n \n\nscilicet et nisi nos cibus aridus et tener umor\n\n \n\nadiuvet, amisso iam corpore vita quoque omnis\n\n \n\nomnibus e nervis atque ossibus exsoluatur;\n\n \n\nadiutamur enim dubio procul atque alimur nos\n\n \n\ncertis ab rebus, certis aliae atque aliae res.\n\n \n\nni mirum quia multa modis communia multis\n\n \n\nmultarum rerum in rebus primordia mixta\n\n \n\nsunt, ideo variis variae res rebus aluntur.\n\n \n\natque eadem magni refert primordia saepe\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali positura contineantur\n\n \n\net quos inter se dent motus accipiantque;\n\n \n\nnamque eadem caelum mare terras flumina solem\n\n \n\nconstituunt, eadem fruges arbusta animantis,\n\n \n\nverum aliis alioque modo commixta moventur.\n\n \n\nquin etiam passim nostris in versibus ipsis\n\n \n\nmulta elementa vides multis communia verbis,\n\n \n\ncum tamen inter se versus ac verba necessest\n\n \n\nconfiteare et re et sonitu distare sonanti.\n\n \n\ntantum elementa queunt permutato ordine solo;\n\n \n\nat rerum quae sunt primordia, plura adhibere\n\n \n\npossunt unde queant variae res quaeque creari.\n\n \n\nNunc et Anaxagorae scrutemur homoeomerian\n\n \n\nquam Grai memorant nec nostra dicere lingua\n\n \n\nconcedit nobis patrii sermonis egestas,\n\n \n\nsed tamen ipsam rem facilest exponere verbis.\n\n \n\nprincipio, rerum quam dicit homoeomerian,\n\n \n\nossa videlicet e pauxillis atque minutis\n\n \n\nossibus hic et de pauxillis atque minutis\n\n \n\nvisceribus viscus gigni sanguenque creari\n\n \n\nsanguinis inter se multis coeuntibus guttis\n\n \n\nex aurique putat micis consistere posse\n\n \n\naurum et de terris terram concrescere parvis,\n\n \n\nignibus ex ignis, umorem umoribus esse,\n\n \n\ncetera consimili fingit ratione putatque.\n\n \n\nnec tamen esse ulla de parte in rebus inane\n\n \n\nconcedit neque corporibus finem esse secandis.\n\n \n\nquare in utraque mihi pariter ratione videtur\n\n \n\nerrare atque illi, supra quos diximus ante.\n\n \n\nAdde quod inbecilla nimis primordia fingit;\n\n \n\nsi primordia sunt, simili quae praedita constant\n\n \n\nnatura atque ipsae res sunt aequeque laborant\n\n \n\net pereunt, neque ab exitio res ulla refrenat.\n\n \n\nnam quid in oppressu valido durabit eorum,\n\n \n\nut mortem effugiat, leti sub dentibus ipsis?\n\n \n\nignis an umor an aura? quid horum? sanguen an ossa?\n\n \n\nnil ut opinor, ubi ex aequo res funditus omnis\n\n \n\ntam mortalis erit quam quae manifesta videmus\n\n \n\nex oculis nostris aliqua vi victa perire.\n\n \n\nat neque reccidere ad nihilum res posse neque autem\n\n \n\ncrescere de nihilo testor res ante probatas.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam cibus auget corpus alitque,\n\n \n\nscire licet nobis venas et sanguen et ossa\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nsive cibos omnis commixto corpore dicent\n\n \n\nesse et habere in se nervorum corpora parva\n\n \n\nossaque et omnino venas partisque cruoris,\n\n \n\nfiet uti cibus omnis et aridus et liquor ipse\n\n \n\nex alienigenis rebus constare putetur,\n\n \n\nossibus et nervis sanieque et sanguine mixto.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quae cumque e terra corpora crescunt,\n\n \n\nsi sunt in terris, terram constare necessest\n\n \n\nex alienigenis, quae terris exoriuntur.\n\n \n\ntransfer item, totidem verbis utare licebit:\n\n \n\nin lignis si flamma latet fumusque cinisque,\n\n \n\nex alienigenis consistant ligna necessest,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nex alienigenis, quae lignis exoriuntur.\n\n \n\nLinquitur hic quaedam latitandi copia tenvis,\n\n \n\nid quod Anaxagoras sibi sumit, ut omnibus omnis\n\n \n\nres putet inmixtas rebus latitare, sed illud\n\n \n\napparere unum, cuius sint plurima mixta\n\n \n\net magis in promptu primaque in fronte locata.\n\n \n\nquod tamen a vera longe ratione repulsumst;\n\n \n\nconveniebat enim fruges quoque saepe, minaci\n\n \n\nrobore cum in saxi franguntur, mittere signum\n\n \n\nsanguinis aut aliquid, nostro quae corpore aluntur.\n\n \n\ncum lapidi in lapidem terimus, manare cruorem\n\n \n\nconsimili ratione herbis quoque saepe decebat,\n\n \n\net latices dulcis guttas similique sapore\n\n \n\nmittere, lanigerae quali sunt ubere lactis,\n\n \n\nscilicet et glebis terrarum saepe friatis\n\n \n\nherbarum genera et fruges frondesque videri\n\n \n\ndispertita inter terram latitare minute,\n\n \n\npostremo in lignis cinerem fumumque videri,\n\n \n\ncum praefracta forent, ignisque latere minutos.\n\n \n\nquorum nil fieri quoniam manifesta docet res,\n\n \n\nscire licet non esse in rebus res ita mixtas,\n\n \n\nverum semina multimodis inmixta latere\n\n \n\nmultarum rerum in rebus communia debent.\n\n \n\n'At saepe in magnis fit montibus' inquis 'ut altis\n\n \n\narboribus vicina cacumina summa terantur\n\n \n\ninter se validis facere id cogentibus austris,\n\n \n\ndonec flammai fulserunt flore coorto.'\n\n \n\nscilicet et non est lignis tamen insitus ignis,\n\n \n\nverum semina sunt ardoris multa, terendo\n\n \n\nquae cum confluxere, creant incendia silvis.\n\n \n\nquod si facta foret silvis abscondita flamma,\n\n \n\nnon possent ullum tempus celarier ignes,\n\n \n\nconficerent volgo silvas, arbusta cremarent.\n\n \n\niamne vides igitur, paulo quod diximus ante,\n\n \n\npermagni referre eadem primordia saepe\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali positura contineantur\n\n \n\net quos inter se dent motus accipiantque,\n\n \n\natque eadem paulo inter se mutata creare\n\n \n\nignes et lignum? quo pacto verba quoque ipsa\n\n \n\ninter se paulo mutatis sunt elementis,\n\n \n\ncum ligna atque ignes distincta voce notemus.\n\n \n\nDenique iam quae cumque in rebus cernis apertis\n\n \n\nsi fieri non posse putas, quin materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora consimili natura praedita fingas,\n\n \n\nhac ratione tibi pereunt primordia rerum:\n\n \n\nfiet uti risu tremulo concussa cachinnent\n\n \n\net lacrimis salsis umectent ora genasque.\n\n \n\nNunc age, quod super est, cognosce et clarius audi.\n\n \n\nnec me animi fallit quam sint obscura; sed acri\n\n \n\npercussit thyrso laudis spes magna meum cor\n\n \n\net simul incussit suavem mi in pectus amorem\n\n \n\nMusarum, quo nunc instinctus mente vigenti\n\n \n\navia Pieridum peragro loca nullius ante\n\n \n\ntrita solo. iuvat integros accedere fontis\n\n \n\natque haurire iuvatque novos decerpere flores\n\n \n\ninsignemque meo capiti petere inde coronam,\n\n \n\nunde prius nulli velarint tempora Musae;\n\n \n\nprimum quod magnis doceo de rebus et artis\n\n \n\nreligionum animum nodis exsolvere pergo,\n\n \n\ndeinde quod obscura de re tam lucida pango\n\n \n\ncarmina musaeo contingens cuncta lepore.\n\n \n\nid quoque enim non ab nulla ratione videtur;\n\n \n\nsed vel uti pueris absinthia taetra medentes\n\n \n\ncum dare conantur, prius oras pocula circum\n\n \n\ncontingunt mellis dulci flavoque liquore,\n\n \n\nut puerorum aetas inprovida ludificetur\n\n \n\nlabrorum tenus, interea perpotet amarum\n\n \n\nabsinthi laticem deceptaque non capiatur,\n\n \n\nsed potius tali facto recreata valescat,\n\n \n\nsic ego nunc, quoniam haec ratio plerumque videtur\n\n \n\ntristior esse quibus non est tractata, retroque\n\n \n\nvolgus abhorret ab hac, volui tibi suaviloquenti\n\n \n\ncarmine Pierio rationem exponere nostram\n\n \n\net quasi musaeo dulci contingere melle,\n\n \n\nsi tibi forte animum tali ratione tenere\n\n \n\nversibus in nostris possem, dum perspicis omnem\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum, qua constet compta figura.\n\n  \n\nSed quoniam docui solidissima materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora perpetuo volitare invicta per aevom,\n\n \n\nnunc age, summai quaedam sit finis eorum\n\n \n\nnecne sit, evolvamus; item quod inane repertumst\n\n \n\nseu locus ac spatium, res in quo quaeque gerantur,\n\n \n\npervideamus utrum finitum funditus omne\n\n \n\nconstet an immensum pateat vasteque profundum.\n\n \n\nOmne quod est igitur nulla regione viarum\n\n \n\nfinitumst; namque extremum debebat habere.\n\n \n\nextremum porro nullius posse videtur\n\n \n\nesse, nisi ultra sit quod finiat, ut videatur\n\n \n\nquo non longius haec sensus natura sequatur.\n\n \n\nnunc extra summam quoniam nihil esse fatendum,\n\n \n\nnon habet extremum, caret ergo fine modoque.\n\n \n\nnec refert quibus adsistas regionibus eius;\n\n \n\nusque adeo, quem quisque locum possedit, in omnis\n\n \n\ntantundem partis infinitum omne relinquit.\n\n \n\nPraeterea si iam finitum constituatur\n\n \n\nomne quod est spatium, si quis procurrat ad oras\n\n \n\nultimus extremas iaciatque volatile telum,\n\n \n\nid validis utrum contortum viribus ire\n\n \n\nquo fuerit missum mavis longeque volare,\n\n \n\nan prohibere aliquid censes obstareque posse?\n\n \n\nalterutrum fatearis enim sumasque necessest.\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque tibi effugium praecludit et omne\n\n \n\ncogit ut exempta concedas fine patere.\n\n \n\nnam sive est aliquid quod probeat efficiatque\n\n \n\nquo minus quo missum est veniat finique locet se,\n\n \n\nsive foras fertur, non est a fine profectum.\n\n \n\nhoc pacto sequar atque, oras ubi cumque locaris\n\n \n\nextremas, quaeram: quid telo denique fiet?\n\n \n\nfiet uti nusquam possit consistere finis\n\n \n\neffugiumque fugae prolatet copia semper.\n\n \n\nPraeterea spatium summai totius omne\n\n \n\nundique si inclusum certis consisteret oris\n\n \n\nfinitumque foret, iam copia materiai\n\n \n\nundique ponderibus solidis confluxet ad imum\n\n \n\nnec res ulla geri sub caeli tegmine posset\n\n \n\nnec foret omnino caelum neque lumina solis,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi materies omnis cumulata iaceret\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore subsidendo.\n\n \n\nat nunc ni mirum requies data principiorum\n\n \n\ncorporibus nullast, quia nil est funditus imum,\n\n \n\nquo quasi confluere et sedes ubi ponere possint.\n\n \n\nsemper in adsiduo motu res quaeque geruntur\n\n \n\npartibus in cunctis, infernaque suppeditantur\n\n \n\nex infinito cita corpora materiai.\n\n \n\nPostremo ante oculos res rem finire videtur;\n\n \n\naer dissaepit collis atque aera montes,\n\n \n\nterra mare et contra mare terras terminat omnis;\n\n \n\nomne quidem vero nihil est quod finiat extra.\n\n  \n\nest igitur natura loci spatiumque profundi,\n\n \n\nquod neque clara suo percurrere fulmina cursu\n\n \n\nperpetuo possint aevi labentia tractu\n\n \n\nnec prorsum facere ut restet minus ire meando;\n\n \n\nusque adeo passim patet ingens copia rebus\n\n \n\nfinibus exemptis in cunctas undique partis.\n\n \n\nIpsa modum porro sibi rerum summa parare\n\n \n\nne possit, natura tenet, quae corpus inane\n\n \n\net quod inane autem est finiri corpore cogit,\n\n \n\nut sic alternis infinita omnia reddat,\n\n \n\naut etiam alterutrum, nisi terminet alterum eorum,\n\n \n\nsimplice natura pateat tamen inmoderatum,\n\n \n\nnec mare nec tellus neque caeli lucida templa\n\n \n\nnec mortale genus nec divum corpora sancta\n\n \n\nexiguum possent horai sistere tempus;\n\n \n\nnam dispulsa suo de coetu materiai\n\n \n\ncopia ferretur magnum per inane soluta,\n\n \n\nsive adeo potius numquam concreta creasset\n\n \n\nullam rem, quoniam cogi disiecta nequisset.\n\n \n\nnam certe neque consilio primordia rerum\n\n \n\nordine se suo quaeque sagaci mente locarunt\n\n \n\nnec quos quaeque darent motus pepigere profecto\n\n \n\nsed quia multa modis multis mutata per omne\n\n \n\nex infinito vexantur percita plagis,\n\n \n\nomne genus motus et coetus experiundo\n\n \n\ntandem deveniunt in talis disposituras,\n\n \n\nqualibus haec rerum consistit summa creata,\n\n \n\net multos etiam magnos servata per annos\n\n \n\nut semel in motus coniectast convenientis,\n\n \n\nefficit ut largis avidum mare fluminis undis\n\n \n\nintegrent amnes et solis terra vapore\n\n \n\nfota novet fetus summissaque gens animantum\n\n \n\nfloreat et vivant labentis aetheris ignes.\n\n \n\nquod nullo facerent pacto, nisi materiai\n\n \n\nex infinito suboriri copia posset,\n\n \n\nunde amissa solent reparare in tempore quaeque.\n\n \n\nnam vel uti privata cibo natura animantum\n\n \n\ndiffluit amittens corpus, sic omnia debent\n\n \n\ndissolui simul ac defecit suppeditare\n\n \n\nmateries aliqua ratione aversa viai.\n\n \n\nnec plagae possunt extrinsecus undique summam\n\n \n\nconservare omnem, quae cumque est conciliata.\n\n \n\ncudere enim crebro possunt partemque morari,\n\n \n\ndum veniant aliae ac suppleri summa queatur;\n\n \n\ninter dum resilire tamen coguntur et una\n\n \n\nprincipiis rerum spatium tempusque fugai\n\n \n\nlargiri, ut possint a coetu libera ferri.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam suboriri multa necessest,\n\n \n\net tamen ut plagae quoque possint suppetere ipsae,\n\n \n\ninfinita opus est vis undique materiai.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus longe fuge credere, Memmi,\n\n \n\nin medium summae quod dicunt omnia niti\n\n \n\natque ideo mundi naturam stare sine ullis\n\n \n\nictibus externis neque quoquam posse resolvi\n\n \n\nsumma atque ima, quod in medium sint omnia nixa,\n\n \n\nipsum si quicquam posse in se sistere credis,\n\n \n\net quae pondera sunt sub terris omnia sursum\n\n \n\nnitier in terraque retro requiescere posta,\n\n \n\nut per aquas quae nunc rerum simulacra videmus;\n\n \n\net simili ratione animalia suppa vagari\n\n \n\ncontendunt neque posse e terris in loca caeli\n\n \n\nreccidere inferiora magis quam corpora nostra\n\n \n\nsponte sua possint in caeli templa volare;\n\n \n\nilli cum videant solem, nos sidera noctis\n\n \n\ncernere et alternis nobiscum tempora caeli\n\n \n\ndividere et noctes parilis agitare diebus.\n\n \n\nsed vanus stolidis haec * * *\n\n \n\namplexi quod habent perv * * *\n\n \n\nnam medium nihil esse potest * * *\n\n \n\ninfinita; neque omnino, si iam medium sit,\n\n \n\npossit ibi quicquam consistere * * *\n\n \n\nquam quavis alia longe ratione * * *\n\n \n\nomnis enim locus ac spatium, quod inane vocamus,\n\n \n\nper medium, per non medium, concedere debet\n\n \n\naeque ponderibus, motus qua cumque feruntur.\n\n \n\nnec quisquam locus est, quo corpora cum venerunt,\n\n \n\nponderis amissa vi possint stare in inani;\n\n \n\nnec quod inane autem est ulli subsistere debet,\n\n \n\nquin, sua quod natura petit, concedere pergat.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur possunt tali ratione teneri\n\n \n\nres in concilium medii cuppedine victae.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam non omnia corpora fingunt\n\n \n\nin medium niti, sed terrarum atque liquoris\n\n \n\numorem ponti magnasque e montibus undas,\n\n \n\net quasi terreno quae corpore contineantur,\n\n \n\nat contra tenuis exponunt aeris auras\n\n \n\net calidos simul a medio differrier ignis,\n\n \n\natque ideo totum circum tremere aethera signis\n\n \n\net solis flammam per caeli caerula pasci,\n\n \n\nquod calor a medio fugiens se ibi conligat omnis,\n\n \n\nnec prorsum arboribus summos frondescere ramos\n\n \n\nposse, nisi a terris paulatim cuique cibatum\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nne volucri ritu flammarum moenia mundi\n\n \n\ndiffugiant subito magnum per inane soluta\n\n \n\net ne cetera consimili ratione sequantur\n\n \n\nneve ruant caeli tonitralia templa superne\n\n \n\nterraque se pedibus raptim subducat et omnis\n\n \n\ninter permixtas rerum caelique ruinas\n\n \n\ncorpora solventes abeat per inane profundum,\n\n \n\ntemporis ut puncto nihil extet reliquiarum\n\n \n\ndesertum praeter spatium et primordia caeca.\n\n \n\nnam qua cumque prius de parti corpora desse\n\n \n\nconstitues, haec rebus erit pars ianua leti,\n\n \n\nhac se turba foras dabit omnis materiai.\n\n \n\nHaec sic pernosces parva perductus opella;\n\n \n\nnamque alid ex alio clarescet nec tibi caeca\n\n \n\nnox iter eripiet, quin ultima naturai\n\n \n\npervideas: ita res accendent lumina rebus.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0001","text":"## Liber Primus\n\n  \n\nAeneadum genetrix, hominum divomque voluptas,\n\n \n\nalma Venus, caeli subter labentia signa\n\n \n\nquae mare navigerum, quae terras frugiferentis\n\n \n\nconcelebras, per te quoniam genus omne animantum\n\n \n\nconcipitur visitque exortum lumina solis:\n\n \n\nte, dea, te fugiunt venti, te nubila caeli\n\n \n\nadventumque tuum, tibi suavis daedala tellus\n\n \n\nsummittit flores, tibi rident aequora ponti\n\n \n\nplacatumque nitet diffuso lumine caelum.\n\n \n\nnam simul ac species patefactast verna diei\n\n \n\net reserata viget genitabilis aura favoni,\n\n \n\naeriae primum volucris te, diva, tuumque\n\n \n\nsignificant initum perculsae corda tua vi.\n\n \n\ninde ferae pecudes persultant pabula laeta\n\n \n\net rapidos tranant amnis: ita capta lepore\n\n \n\nte sequitur cupide quo quamque inducere pergis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","text":"denique per maria ac montis fluviosque rapacis\n\n \n\nfrondiferasque domos avium camposque virentis\n\n \n\nomnibus incutiens blandum per pectora amorem\n\n \n\nefficis ut cupide generatim saecla propagent.\n\n \n\nquae quoniam rerum naturam sola gubernas\n\n \n\nnec sine te quicquam dias in luminis oras\n\n \n\nexoritur neque fit laetum neque amabile quicquam,\n\n \n\nte sociam studeo scribendis versibus esse,\n\n \n\nquos ego de rerum natura pangere conor\n\n \n\nMemmiadae nostro, quem tu, dea, tempore in omni\n\n \n\nomnibus ornatum voluisti excellere rebus.\n\n \n\nquo magis aeternum da dictis, diva, leporem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003","text":"effice ut interea fera moenera militiai\n\n \n\nper maria ac terras omnis sopita quiescant;\n\n \n\nnam tu sola potes tranquilla pace iuvare\n\n \n\nmortalis, quoniam belli fera moenera Mavors\n\n \n\narmipotens regit, in gremium qui saepe tuum se\n\n \n\nreiicit aeterno devictus vulnere amoris,\n\n \n\natque ita suspiciens tereti cervice reposta\n\n \n\npascit amore avidos inhians in te, dea, visus\n\n \n\neque tuo pendet resupini spiritus ore.\n\n \n\nhunc tu, diva, tuo recubantem corpore sancto\n\n \n\ncircum fusa super, suavis ex ore loquellas\n\n \n\nfunde petens placidam Romanis, incluta, pacem;\n\n \n\nnam neque nos agere hoc patriai tempore iniquo\n\n \n\npossumus aequo animo nec Memmi clara propago\n\n \n\ntalibus in rebus communi desse saluti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","text":"omnis enim per se divum natura necessest\n\n \n\nimmortali aevo summa cum pace fruatur\n\n \n\nsemota ab nostris rebus seiunctaque longe;\n\n \n\nnam privata dolore omni, privata periclis,\n\n \n\nipsa suis pollens opibus, nihil indiga nostri,\n\n \n\nnec bene promeritis capitur nec tangitur ira.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, vacuas auris animumque sagacem\n\n \n\nsemotum a curis adhibe veram ad rationem,\n\n \n\nne mea dona tibi studio disposta fideli,\n\n \n\nintellecta prius quam sint, contempta relinquas.\n\n \n\nnam tibi de summa caeli ratione deumque\n\n \n\ndisserere incipiam et rerum primordia pandam,\n\n \n\nunde omnis natura creet res, auctet alatque,\n\n \n\nquove eadem rursum natura perempta resolvat,\n\n \n\nquae nos materiem et genitalia corpora rebus\n\n \n\nreddunda in ratione vocare et semina rerum\n\n \n\nappellare suemus et haec eadem usurpare\n\n \n\ncorpora prima, quod ex illis sunt omnia primis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0005","text":"Humana ante oculos foede cum vita iaceret\n\n \n\nin terris oppressa gravi sub religione,\n\n \n\nquae caput a caeli regionibus ostendebat\n\n \n\nhorribili super aspectu mortalibus instans,\n\n \n\nprimum Graius homo mortalis tollere contra\n\n \n\nest oculos ausus primusque obsistere contra;\n\n \n\nquem neque fama deum nec fulmina nec minitanti\n\n \n\nmurmure compressit caelum, sed eo magis acrem\n\n \n\ninritat animi virtutem, effringere ut arta\n\n \n\nnaturae primus portarum claustra cupiret.\n\n \n\nergo vivida vis animi pervicit et extra\n\n \n\nprocessit longe flammantia moenia mundi\n\n \n\natque omne immensum peragravit mente animoque,\n\n \n\nunde refert nobis victor quid possit oriri,\n\n \n\nquid nequeat, finita potestas denique cuique\n\n \n\nqua nam sit ratione atque alte terminus haerens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006","text":"quare religio pedibus subiecta vicissim\n\n \n\nopteritur, nos exaequat victoria caelo.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus vereor, ne forte rearis\n\n \n\nimpia te rationis inire elementa viamque\n\n \n\nindugredi sceleris. quod contra saepius illa\n\n \n\nreligio peperit scelerosa atque impia facta.\n\n \n\nAulide quo pacto Triviai virginis aram\n\n \n\nIphianassai turparunt sanguine foede\n\n \n\nductores Danaum delecti, prima virorum.\n\n \n\ncui simul infula virgineos circum data comptus\n\n \n\nex utraque pari malarum parte profusast,\n\n \n\net maestum simul ante aras adstare parentem\n\n \n\nsensit et hunc propter ferrum celare ministros\n\n \n\naspectuque suo lacrimas effundere civis,\n\n \n\nmuta metu terram genibus summissa petebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","text":"nec miserae prodesse in tali tempore quibat,\n\n \n\nquod patrio princeps donarat nomine regem;\n\n \n\nnam sublata virum manibus tremibundaque ad aras\n\n \n\ndeductast, non ut sollemni more sacrorum\n\n \n\nperfecto posset claro comitari Hymenaeo,\n\n \n\nsed casta inceste nubendi tempore in ipso\n\n \n\nhostia concideret mactatu maesta parentis,\n\n \n\nexitus ut classi felix faustusque daretur.\n\n \n\ntantum religio potuit suadere malorum.\n\n  \n\nTutemet a nobis iam quovis tempore vatum\n\n \n\nterriloquis victus dictis desciscere quaeres.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim quam multa tibi iam fingere possunt\n\n \n\nsomnia, quae vitae rationes vertere possint\n\n \n\nfortunasque tuas omnis turbare timore!\n\n \n\net merito; nam si certam finem esse viderent\n\n \n\naerumnarum homines, aliqua ratione valerent\n\n \n\nreligionibus atque minis obsistere vatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","text":"nunc ratio nulla est restandi, nulla facultas,\n\n \n\naeternas quoniam poenas in morte timendum.\n\n \n\nignoratur enim quae sit natura animai,\n\n \n\nnata sit an contra nascentibus insinuetur\n\n \n\net simul intereat nobiscum morte dirempta\n\n \n\nan tenebras Orci visat vastasque lacunas\n\n \n\nan pecudes alias divinitus insinuet se,\n\n \n\nEnnius ut noster cecinit, qui primus amoeno\n\n \n\ndetulit ex Helicone perenni fronde coronam,\n\n \n\nper gentis Italas hominum quae clara clueret;\n\n \n\netsi praeterea tamen esse Acherusia templa\n\n \n\nEnnius aeternis exponit versibus edens,\n\n \n\nquo neque permaneant animae neque corpora nostra,\n\n \n\nsed quaedam simulacra modis pallentia miris;\n\n \n\nunde sibi exortam semper florentis Homeri\n\n \n\ncommemorat speciem lacrimas effundere salsas\n\n \n\ncoepisse et rerum naturam expandere dictis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0009","text":"qua propter bene cum superis de rebus habenda\n\n \n\nnobis est ratio, solis lunaeque meatus\n\n \n\nqua fiant ratione, et qua vi quaeque gerantur\n\n \n\nin terris, tunc cum primis ratione sagaci\n\n \n\nunde anima atque animi constet natura videndum,\n\n \n\net quae res nobis vigilantibus obvia mentes\n\n \n\nterrificet morbo adfectis somnoque sepultis,\n\n \n\ncernere uti videamur eos audireque coram,\n\n \n\nmorte obita quorum tellus amplectitur ossa.\n\n  \n\nNec me animi fallit Graiorum obscura reperta\n\n \n\ndifficile inlustrare Latinis versibus esse,\n\n \n\nmulta novis verbis praesertim cum sit agendum\n\n \n\npropter egestatem linguae et rerum novitatem;\n\n \n\nsed tua me virtus tamen et sperata voluptas\n\n \n\nsuavis amicitiae quemvis efferre laborem\n\n \n\nsuadet et inducit noctes vigilare serenas\n\n \n\nquaerentem dictis quibus et quo carmine demum\n\n \n\nclara tuae possim praepandere lumina menti,\n\n \n\nres quibus occultas penitus convisere possis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","text":"hunc igitur terrorem animi tenebrasque necessest\n\n \n\nnon radii solis neque lucida tela diei\n\n \n\ndiscutiant, sed naturae species ratioque.\n\n \n\nPrincipium cuius hinc nobis exordia sumet,\n\n \n\nnullam rem e nihilo gigni divinitus umquam.\n\n \n\nquippe ita formido mortalis continet omnis,\n\n \n\nquod multa in terris fieri caeloque tuentur,\n\n \n\nquorum operum causas nulla ratione videre\n\n \n\npossunt ac fieri divino numine rentur.\n\n \n\nquas ob res ubi viderimus nil posse creari\n\n \n\nde nihilo, tum quod sequimur iam rectius inde\n\n \n\nperspiciemus, et unde queat res quaeque creari\n\n \n\net quo quaeque modo fiant opera sine divom.\n\n \n\nNam si de nihilo fierent, ex omnibus rebus\n\n \n\nomne genus nasci posset, nil semine egeret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","text":"e mare primum homines, e terra posset oriri\n\n \n\nsquamigerum genus et volucres erumpere caelo;\n\n \n\narmenta atque aliae pecudes, genus omne ferarum,\n\n \n\nincerto partu culta ac deserta tenerent.\n\n \n\nnec fructus idem arboribus constare solerent,\n\n \n\nsed mutarentur, ferre omnes omnia possent.\n\n \n\nquippe ubi non essent genitalia corpora cuique,\n\n \n\nqui posset mater rebus consistere certa?\n\n \n\nat nunc seminibus quia certis quaeque creantur,\n\n \n\ninde enascitur atque oras in luminis exit,\n\n \n\nmateries ubi inest cuiusque et corpora prima;\n\n \n\natque hac re nequeunt ex omnibus omnia gigni,\n\n \n\nquod certis in rebus inest secreta facultas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0012","text":"Praeterea cur vere rosam, frumenta calore,\n\n \n\nvites autumno fundi suadente videmus,\n\n \n\nsi non, certa suo quia tempore semina rerum\n\n \n\ncum confluxerunt, patefit quod cumque creatur,\n\n \n\ndum tempestates adsunt et vivida tellus\n\n \n\ntuto res teneras effert in luminis oras?\n\n \n\nquod si de nihilo fierent, subito exorerentur\n\n \n\nincerto spatio atque alienis partibus anni,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi nulla forent primordia, quae genitali\n\n \n\nconcilio possent arceri tempore iniquo.\n\n  \n\nNec porro augendis rebus spatio foret usus\n\n \n\nseminis ad coitum, si e nilo crescere possent;\n\n \n\nnam fierent iuvenes subito ex infantibus parvis\n\n \n\ne terraque exorta repente arbusta salirent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0013","text":"quorum nil fieri manifestum est, omnia quando\n\n \n\npaulatim crescunt, ut par est semine certo,\n\n \n\ncrescentesque genus servant; ut noscere possis\n\n \n\nquicque sua de materia grandescere alique.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit uti sine certis imbribus anni\n\n \n\nlaetificos nequeat fetus submittere tellus\n\n \n\nnec porro secreta cibo natura animantum\n\n \n\npropagare genus possit vitamque tueri;\n\n \n\nut potius multis communia corpora rebus\n\n \n\nmulta putes esse, ut verbis elementa videmus,\n\n \n\nquam sine principiis ullam rem existere posse.\n\n \n\nDenique cur homines tantos natura parare\n\n \n\nnon potuit, pedibus qui pontum per vada possent\n\n \n\ntransire et magnos manibus divellere montis\n\n \n\nmultaque vivendo vitalia vincere saecla,\n\n \n\nsi non, materies quia rebus reddita certast\n\n \n\ngignundis, e qua constat quid possit oriri?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","text":"nil igitur fieri de nilo posse fatendumst,\n\n \n\nsemine quando opus est rebus, quo quaeque creatae\n\n \n\naeris in teneras possint proferrier auras.\n\n \n\nPostremo quoniam incultis praestare videmus\n\n \n\nculta loca et manibus melioris reddere fetus,\n\n \n\nesse videlicet in terris primordia rerum\n\n \n\nquae nos fecundas vertentes vomere glebas\n\n \n\nterraique solum subigentes cimus ad ortus;\n\n \n\nquod si nulla forent, nostro sine quaeque labore\n\n \n\nsponte sua multo fieri meliora videres.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit uti quicque in sua corpora rursum\n\n \n\ndissoluat natura neque ad nihilum interemat res.\n\n \n\nnam siquid mortale e cunctis partibus esset,\n\n \n\nex oculis res quaeque repente erepta periret;\n\n \n\nnulla vi foret usus enim, quae partibus eius\n\n \n\ndiscidium parere et nexus exsolvere posset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0015","text":"quod nunc, aeterno quia constant semine quaeque,\n\n \n\ndonec vis obiit, quae res diverberet ictu\n\n \n\naut intus penetret per inania dissoluatque,\n\n \n\nnullius exitium patitur natura videri.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quae cumque vetustate amovet aetas,\n\n \n\nsi penitus peremit consumens materiem omnem,\n\n \n\nunde animale genus generatim in lumina vitae\n\n \n\nredducit Venus, aut redductum daedala tellus\n\n \n\nunde alit atque auget generatim pabula praebens?\n\n \n\nunde mare ingenuei fontes externaque longe\n\n \n\nflumina suppeditant? unde aether sidera pascit?\n\n \n\nomnia enim debet, mortali corpore quae sunt,\n\n \n\ninfinita aetas consumpse ante acta diesque.\n\n \n\nquod si in eo spatio atque ante acta aetate fuere\n\n \n\ne quibus haec rerum consistit summa refecta,\n\n \n\ninmortali sunt natura praedita certe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","text":"haud igitur possunt ad nilum quaeque reverti.\n\n \n\nDenique res omnis eadem vis causaque volgo\n\n \n\nconficeret, nisi materies aeterna teneret,\n\n \n\ninter se nexus minus aut magis indupedita;\n\n \n\ntactus enim leti satis esset causa profecto,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi nulla forent aeterno corpore, quorum\n\n \n\ncontextum vis deberet dissolvere quaeque.\n\n \n\nat nunc, inter se quia nexus principiorum\n\n \n\ndissimiles constant aeternaque materies est,\n\n \n\nincolumi remanent res corpore, dum satis acris\n\n \n\nvis obeat pro textura cuiusque reperta.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur redit ad nihilum res ulla, sed omnes\n\n \n\ndiscidio redeunt in corpora materiai.\n\n \n\npostremo pereunt imbres, ubi eos pater aether\n\n \n\nin gremium matris terrai praecipitavit;\n\n \n\nat nitidae surgunt fruges ramique virescunt\n\n \n\narboribus, crescunt ipsae fetuque gravantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","text":"hinc alitur porro nostrum genus atque ferarum,\n\n \n\nhinc laetas urbes pueris florere videmus\n\n \n\nfrondiferasque novis avibus canere undique silvas,\n\n \n\nhinc fessae pecudes pinguis per pabula laeta\n\n \n\ncorpora deponunt et candens lacteus umor\n\n \n\nuberibus manat distentis, hinc nova proles\n\n \n\nartubus infirmis teneras lasciva per herbas\n\n \n\nludit lacte mero mentes perculsa novellas.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur penitus pereunt quaecumque videntur,\n\n \n\nquando alit ex alio reficit natura nec ullam\n\n \n\nrem gigni patitur nisi morte adiuta aliena.\n\n \n\nNunc age, res quoniam docui non posse creari\n\n \n\nde nihilo neque item genitas ad nil revocari,\n\n \n\nne qua forte tamen coeptes diffidere dictis,\n\n \n\nquod nequeunt oculis rerum primordia cerni,\n\n \n\naccipe praeterea quae corpora tute necessest\n\n \n\nconfiteare esse in rebus nec posse videri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","text":"Principio venti vis verberat incita corpus\n\n \n\ningentisque ruit navis et nubila differt,\n\n \n\ninter dum rapido percurrens turbine campos\n\n \n\narboribus magnis sternit montisque supremos\n\n \n\nsilvifragis vexat flabris: ita perfurit acri\n\n \n\ncum fremitu saevitque minaci murmure pontus.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur venti ni mirum corpora caeca,\n\n \n\nquae mare, quae terras, quae denique nubila caeli\n\n \n\nverrunt ac subito vexantia turbine raptant,\n\n \n\nnec ratione fluunt alia stragemque propagant\n\n \n\net cum mollis aquae fertur natura repente\n\n \n\nflumine abundanti, quam largis imbribus auget\n\n \n\nmontibus ex altis magnus decursus aquai\n\n \n\nfragmina coniciens silvarum arbustaque tota,\n\n \n\nnec validi possunt pontes venientis aquai\n\n \n\nvim subitam tolerare: ita magno turbidus imbri\n\n \n\nmolibus incurrit validis cum viribus amnis,\n\n \n\ndat sonitu magno stragem volvitque sub undis\n\n \n\ngrandia saxa, ruit qua quidquid fluctibus obstat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","text":"sic igitur debent venti quoque flamina ferri,\n\n \n\nquae vel uti validum cum flumen procubuere\n\n \n\nquam libet in partem, trudunt res ante ruuntque\n\n \n\nimpetibus crebris, inter dum vertice torto\n\n \n\ncorripiunt rapidique rotanti turbine portant.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam sunt venti corpora caeca,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem factis et moribus aemula magnis\n\n \n\namnibus inveniuntur, aperto corpore qui sunt.\n\n \n\nTum porro varios rerum sentimus odores\n\n \n\nnec tamen ad naris venientis cernimus umquam\n\n \n\nnec calidos aestus tuimur nec frigora quimus\n\n \n\nusurpare oculis nec voces cernere suemus;\n\n \n\nquae tamen omnia corporea constare necessest\n\n \n\nnatura, quoniam sensus inpellere possunt;\n\n \n\ntangere enim et tangi, nisi corpus, nulla potest res.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0020","text":"Denique fluctifrago suspensae in litore vestis\n\n \n\nuvescunt, eaedem dispansae in sole serescunt.\n\n \n\nat neque quo pacto persederit umor aquai\n\n \n\nvisumst nec rursum quo pacto fugerit aestu.\n\n \n\nin parvas igitur partis dispergitur umor,\n\n \n\nquas oculi nulla possunt ratione videre.\n\n \n\nquin etiam multis solis redeuntibus annis\n\n \n\nanulus in digito subter tenuatur habendo,\n\n \n\nstilicidi casus lapidem cavat, uncus aratri\n\n \n\nferreus occulte decrescit vomer in arvis,\n\n \n\nstrataque iam volgi pedibus detrita viarum\n\n \n\nsaxea conspicimus; tum portas propter aena\n\n \n\nsigna manus dextras ostendunt adtenuari\n\n \n\nsaepe salutantum tactu praeterque meantum.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur minui, cum sint detrita, videmus.\n\n \n\nsed quae corpora decedant in tempore quoque,\n\n \n\ninvida praeclusit speciem natura videndi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0021","text":"Postremo quae cumque dies naturaque rebus\n\n \n\npaulatim tribuit moderatim crescere cogens,\n\n \n\nnulla potest oculorum acies contenta tueri,\n\n \n\nnec porro quae cumque aevo macieque senescunt,\n\n \n\nnec, mare quae impendent, vesco sale saxa peresa\n\n \n\nquid quoque amittant in tempore cernere possis.\n\n \n\ncorporibus caecis igitur natura gerit res.\n\n \n\nNec tamen undique corporea stipata tenentur\n\n \n\nomnia natura; namque est in rebus inane.\n\n \n\nquod tibi cognosse in multis erit utile rebus\n\n \n\nnec sinet errantem dubitare et quaerere semper\n\n \n\nde summa rerum et nostris diffidere dictis.\n\n \n\nqua propter locus est intactus inane vacansque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","text":"quod si non esset, nulla ratione moveri\n\n \n\nres possent; namque officium quod corporis exstat,\n\n \n\nofficere atque obstare, id in omni tempore adesset\n\n \n\nomnibus; haud igitur quicquam procedere posset,\n\n \n\nprincipium quoniam cedendi nulla daret res.\n\n \n\nat nunc per maria ac terras sublimaque caeli\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis varia ratione moveri\n\n \n\ncernimus ante oculos, quae, si non esset inane,\n\n \n\nnon tam sollicito motu privata carerent\n\n \n\nquam genita omnino nulla ratione fuissent,\n\n \n\nundique materies quoniam stipata quiesset.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quamvis solidae res esse putentur,\n\n \n\nhinc tamen esse licet raro cum corpore cernas.\n\n \n\nin saxis ac speluncis permanat aquarum\n\n \n\nliquidus umor et uberibus flent omnia guttis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0023","text":"dissipat in corpus sese cibus omne animantum;\n\n \n\ncrescunt arbusta et fetus in tempore fundunt,\n\n \n\nquod cibus in totas usque ab radicibus imis\n\n \n\nper truncos ac per ramos diffunditur omnis.\n\n \n\ninter saepta meant voces et clausa domorum\n\n \n\ntransvolitant, rigidum permanat frigus ad ossa.\n\n \n\nquod nisi inania sint, qua possent corpora quaeque\n\n \n\ntransire, haud ulla fieri ratione videres.\n\n \n\nDenique cur alias aliis praestare videmus\n\n \n\npondere res rebus nihilo maiore figura?\n\n \n\nnam si tantundemst in lanae glomere quantum\n\n \n\ncorporis in plumbo est, tantundem pendere par est,\n\n \n\ncorporis officiumst quoniam premere omnia deorsum,\n\n \n\ncontra autem natura manet sine pondere inanis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0024","text":"ergo quod magnumst aeque leviusque videtur,\n\n \n\nni mirum plus esse sibi declarat inanis;\n\n \n\nat contra gravius plus in se corporis esse\n\n \n\ndedicat et multo vacui minus intus habere.\n\n \n\nest igitur ni mirum id quod ratione sagaci\n\n \n\nquaerimus, admixtum rebus, quod inane vocamus.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus ne te deducere vero\n\n \n\npossit, quod quidam fingunt, praecurrere cogor.\n\n \n\ncedere squamigeris latices nitentibus aiunt\n\n \n\net liquidas aperire vias, quia post loca pisces\n\n \n\nlinquant, quo possint cedentes confluere undae;\n\n \n\nsic alias quoque res inter se posse moveri\n\n \n\net mutare locum, quamvis sint omnia plena.\n\n \n\nscilicet id falsa totum ratione receptumst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0025","text":"nam quo squamigeri poterunt procedere tandem,\n\n \n\nni spatium dederint latices? concedere porro\n\n \n\nquo poterunt undae, cum pisces ire nequibunt?\n\n \n\naut igitur motu privandumst corpora quaeque\n\n \n\naut esse admixtum dicundumst rebus inane,\n\n \n\nunde initum primum capiat res quaeque movendi.\n\n \n\nPostremo duo de concursu corpora lata\n\n \n\nsi cita dissiliant, nempe aer omne necessest,\n\n \n\ninter corpora quod fiat, possidat inane.\n\n \n\nis porro quamvis circum celerantibus auris\n\n \n\nconfluat, haud poterit tamen uno tempore totum\n\n \n\ncompleri spatium; nam primum quemque necessest\n\n \n\noccupet ille locum, deinde omnia possideantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0026","text":"quod si forte aliquis, cum corpora dissiluere,\n\n \n\ntum putat id fieri quia se condenseat aer,\n\n \n\nerrat; nam vacuum tum fit quod non fuit ante\n\n \n\net repletur item vacuum quod constitit ante,\n\n \n\nnec tali ratione potest denserier aer\n\n \n\nnec, si iam posset, sine inani posset, opinor,\n\n \n\nipse in se trahere et partis conducere in unum.\n\n \n\nQua propter, quamvis causando multa moreris,\n\n \n\nesse in rebus inane tamen fateare necessest.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea tibi possum commemorando\n\n \n\nargumenta fidem dictis conradere nostris.\n\n \n\nverum animo satis haec vestigia parva sagaci\n\n \n\nsunt, per quae possis cognoscere cetera tute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0027","text":"namque canes ut montivagae persaepe ferai\n\n \n\nnaribus inveniunt intectas fronde quietes,\n\n \n\ncum semel institerunt vestigia certa viai,\n\n \n\nsic alid ex alio per te tute ipse videre\n\n \n\ntalibus in rebus poteris caecasque latebras\n\n \n\ninsinuare omnis et verum protrahere inde.\n\n \n\nquod si pigraris paulumve recesseris ab re,\n\n \n\nhoc tibi de plano possum promittere, Memmi:\n\n \n\nusque adeo largos haustus e fontibus magnis\n\n \n\nlingua meo suavis diti de pectore fundet,\n\n \n\nut verear ne tarda prius per membra senectus\n\n \n\nserpat et in nobis vitai claustra resolvat,\n\n \n\nquam tibi de quavis una re versibus omnis\n\n \n\nargumentorum sit copia missa per auris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","text":"Sed nunc ut repetam coeptum pertexere dictis,\n\n \n\nomnis ut est igitur per se natura duabus\n\n \n\nconstitit in rebus; nam corpora sunt et inane,\n\n \n\nhaec in quo sita sunt et qua diversa moventur.\n\n \n\ncorpus enim per se communis dedicat esse\n\n \n\nsensus; cui nisi prima fides fundata valebit,\n\n \n\nhaut erit occultis de rebus quo referentes\n\n \n\nconfirmare animi quicquam ratione queamus.\n\n \n\ntum porro locus ac spatium, quod inane vocamus,\n\n \n\nsi nullum foret, haut usquam sita corpora possent\n\n \n\nesse neque omnino quoquam diversa meare;\n\n \n\nid quod iam supera tibi paulo ostendimus ante.\n\n \n\npraeterea nihil est quod possis dicere ab omni\n\n \n\ncorpore seiunctum secretumque esse ab inani,\n\n \n\nquod quasi tertia sit numero natura reperta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","text":"nam quod cumque erit, esse aliquid debebit id ipsum\n\n \n\naugmine vel grandi vel parvo denique, dum sit;\n\n \n\ncui si tactus erit quamvis levis exiguusque,\n\n \n\ncorporis augebit numerum summamque sequetur;\n\n \n\nsin intactile erit, nulla de parte quod ullam\n\n \n\nrem prohibere queat per se transire meantem,\n\n \n\nscilicet hoc id erit, vacuum quod inane vocamus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea per se quod cumque erit, aut faciet quid\n\n \n\naut aliis fungi debebit agentibus ipsum\n\n \n\naut erit ut possint in eo res esse gerique.\n\n \n\nat facere et fungi sine corpore nulla potest res\n\n \n\nnec praebere locum porro nisi inane vacansque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","text":"ergo praeter inane et corpora tertia per se\n\n \n\nnulla potest rerum in numero natura relinqui,\n\n \n\nnec quae sub sensus cadat ullo tempore nostros\n\n \n\nnec ratione animi quam quisquam possit apisci.\n\n \n\nNam quae cumque cluent, aut his coniuncta duabus\n\n \n\nrebus ea invenies aut horum eventa videbis.\n\n \n\nconiunctum est id quod nusquam sine permitiali\n\n \n\ndiscidio potis est seiungi seque gregari,\n\n \n\npondus uti saxis, calor ignis, liquor aquai,\n\n \n\ntactus corporibus cunctis, intactus inani.\n\n \n\nservitium contra paupertas divitiaeque,\n\n \n\nlibertas bellum concordia cetera quorum\n\n \n\nadventu manet incolumis natura abituque,\n\n \n\nhaec soliti sumus, ut par est, eventa vocare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","text":"tempus item per se non est, sed rebus ab ipsis\n\n \n\nconsequitur sensus, transactum quid sit in aevo,\n\n \n\ntum quae res instet, quid porro deinde sequatur;\n\n \n\nnec per se quemquam tempus sentire fatendumst\n\n \n\nsemotum ab rerum motu placidaque quiete.\n\n \n\ndenique Tyndaridem raptam belloque subactas\n\n \n\nTroiiugenas gentis cum dicunt esse, videndumst\n\n \n\nne forte haec per se cogant nos esse fateri,\n\n \n\nquando ea saecla hominum, quorum haec eventa fuerunt,\n\n \n\ninrevocabilis abstulerit iam praeterita aetas;\n\n \n\nnamque aliud terris, aliud regionibus ipsis\n\n \n\neventum dici poterit quod cumque erit actum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032","text":"denique materies si rerum nulla fuisset\n\n \n\nnec locus ac spatium, res in quo quaeque geruntur,\n\n \n\nnumquam Tyndaridis forma conflatus amore\n\n \n\nignis Alexandri Phrygio sub pectore gliscens\n\n \n\nclara accendisset saevi certamina belli\n\n \n\nnec clam durateus Troiianis Pergama partu\n\n \n\ninflammasset equos nocturno Graiiugenarum;\n\n \n\nperspicere ut possis res gestas funditus omnis\n\n \n\nnon ita uti corpus per se constare neque esse\n\n \n\nnec ratione cluere eadem qua constet inane,\n\n \n\nsed magis ut merito possis eventa vocare\n\n \n\ncorporis atque loci, res in quo quaeque gerantur.\n\n \n\nCorpora sunt porro partim primordia rerum,\n\n \n\npartim concilio quae constant principiorum.\n\n \n\nsed quae sunt rerum primordia, nulla potest vis\n\n \n\nstinguere; nam solido vincunt ea corpore demum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0033","text":"etsi difficile esse videtur credere quicquam\n\n \n\nin rebus solido reperiri corpore posse.\n\n \n\ntransit enim fulmen caeli per saepta domorum\n\n \n\nclamor ut ac voces, ferrum candescit in igni\n\n \n\ndissiliuntque fero ferventi saxa vapore;\n\n \n\ncum labefactatus rigor auri solvitur aestu,\n\n \n\ntum glacies aeris flamma devicta liquescit;\n\n \n\npermanat calor argentum penetraleque frigus,\n\n \n\nquando utrumque manu retinentes pocula rite\n\n \n\nsensimus infuso lympharum rore superne.\n\n \n\nusque adeo in rebus solidi nihil esse videtur.\n\n \n\nsed quia vera tamen ratio naturaque rerum\n\n \n\ncogit, ades, paucis dum versibus expediamus\n\n \n\nesse ea quae solido atque aeterno corpore constent,\n\n \n\nsemina quae rerum primordiaque esse docemus,\n\n \n\nunde omnis rerum nunc constet summa creata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0034","text":"Principio quoniam duplex natura duarum\n\n \n\ndissimilis rerum longe constare repertast,\n\n \n\ncorporis atque loci, res in quo quaeque geruntur,\n\n \n\nesse utramque sibi per se puramque necessest.\n\n \n\nnam qua cumque vacat spatium, quod inane vocamus,\n\n \n\ncorpus ea non est; qua porro cumque tenet se\n\n \n\ncorpus, ea vacuum nequaquam constat inane.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur solida ac sine inani corpora prima.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam genitis in rebus inanest,\n\n \n\nmateriem circum solidam constare necessest;\n\n \n\nnec res ulla potest vera ratione probari\n\n \n\ncorpore inane suo celare atque intus habere,\n\n \n\nsi non, quod cohibet, solidum constare relinquas.\n\n \n\nid porro nihil esse potest nisi materiai\n\n \n\nconcilium, quod inane queat rerum cohibere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0035","text":"materies igitur, solido quae corpore constat,\n\n \n\nesse aeterna potest, cum cetera dissoluantur.\n\n \n\nTum porro si nil esset quod inane vocaret,\n\n \n\nomne foret solidum; nisi contra corpora certa\n\n \n\nessent quae loca complerent quae cumque tenerent\n\n \n\nomne quod est spatium, vacuum constaret inane.\n\n \n\nalternis igitur ni mirum corpus inani\n\n \n\ndistinctum, quoniam nec plenum naviter extat\n\n \n\nnec porro vacuum; sunt ergo corpora certa,\n\n \n\nquae spatium pleno possint distinguere inane.\n\n \n\nhaec neque dissolui plagis extrinsecus icta\n\n \n\npossunt nec porro penitus penetrata retexi\n\n \n\nnec ratione queunt alia temptata labare;\n\n \n\nid quod iam supra tibi paulo ostendimus ante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","text":"nam neque conlidi sine inani posse videtur\n\n \n\nquicquam nec frangi nec findi in bina secando\n\n \n\nnec capere umorem neque item manabile frigus\n\n \n\nnec penetralem ignem, quibus omnia conficiuntur.\n\n \n\net quo quaeque magis cohibet res intus inane,\n\n \n\ntam magis his rebus penitus temptata labascit.\n\n \n\nergo si solida ac sine inani corpora prima\n\n \n\nsunt ita uti docui, sint haec aeterna necessest.\n\n \n\nPraeterea nisi materies aeterna fuisset,\n\n \n\nantehac ad nihilum penitus res quaeque redissent\n\n \n\nde nihiloque renata forent quae cumque videmus.\n\n \n\nat quoniam supra docui nil posse creari\n\n \n\nde nihilo neque quod genitumst ad nil revocari,\n\n \n\nesse inmortali primordia corpore debent,\n\n \n\ndissolui quo quaeque supremo tempore possint,\n\n \n\nmateries ut subpeditet rebus reparandis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0037","text":"sunt igitur solida primordia simplicitate\n\n \n\nnec ratione queunt alia servata per aevom\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore res reparare.\n\n  \n\ndenique si nullam finem natura parasset\n\n \n\nfrangendis rebus, iam corpora materiai\n\n \n\nusque redacta forent aevo frangente priore,\n\n \n\nut nihil ex illis a certo tempore posset\n\n \n\nconceptum summum aetatis pervadere finem.\n\n \n\nnam quidvis citius dissolvi posse videmus\n\n \n\nquam rursus refici; qua propter longa diei\n\n \n\ninfinita aetas ante acti temporis omnis\n\n \n\nquod fregisset adhuc disturbans dissoluensque,\n\n \n\nnumquam relicuo reparari tempore posset.\n\n \n\nat nunc ni mirum frangendi reddita finis\n\n \n\ncerta manet, quoniam refici rem quamque videmus\n\n \n\net finita simul generatim tempora rebus\n\n \n\nstare, quibus possint aevi contingere florem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0038","text":"Huc accedit uti, solidissima materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora cum constant, possint tamen omnia reddi,\n\n \n\nmollia quae fiunt, aer aqua terra vapores,\n\n \n\nquo pacto fiant et qua vi quaeque gerantur,\n\n \n\nadmixtum quoniam semel est in rebus inane.\n\n \n\nat contra si mollia sint primordia rerum,\n\n \n\nunde queant validi silices ferrumque creari,\n\n \n\nnon poterit ratio reddi; nam funditus omnis\n\n \n\nprincipio fundamenti natura carebit.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur solida pollentia simplicitate,\n\n \n\nquorum condenso magis omnia conciliatu\n\n \n\nartari possunt validasque ostendere viris.\n\n \n\nporro si nullast frangendis reddita finis\n\n \n\ncorporibus, tamen ex aeterno tempore quaeque\n\n \n\nnunc etiam superare necessest corpora rebus,\n\n \n\nquae non dum clueant ullo temptata periclo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","text":"at quoniam fragili natura praedita constant,\n\n \n\ndiscrepat aeternum tempus potuisse manere\n\n \n\ninnumerabilibus plagis vexata per aevom.\n\n \n\nDenique iam quoniam generatim reddita finis\n\n \n\ncrescendi rebus constat vitamque tenendi,\n\n \n\net quid quaeque queant per foedera naturai,\n\n \n\nquid porro nequeant, sancitum quando quidem extat,\n\n \n\nnec commutatur quicquam, quin omnia constant\n\n \n\nusque adeo, variae volucres ut in ordine cunctae\n\n \n\nostendant maculas generalis corpore inesse,\n\n \n\ninmutabilis materiae quoque corpus habere\n\n \n\ndebent ni mirum; nam si primordia rerum\n\n \n\ncommutari aliqua possent ratione revicta,\n\n \n\nincertum quoque iam constet quid possit oriri,\n\n \n\nquid nequeat, finita potestas denique cuique\n\n \n\nqua nam sit ratione atque alte terminus haerens,\n\n \n\nnec totiens possent generatim saecla referre\n\n \n\nnaturam mores victum motusque parentum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0040","text":"Tum porro quoniam est extremum quodque cacumen\n\n \n\ncorporis illius, quod nostri cernere sensus\n\n \n\niam nequeunt, id ni mirum sine partibus extat\n\n \n\net minima constat natura nec fuit umquam\n\n \n\nper se secretum neque post hac esse valebit,\n\n \n\nalterius quoniamst ipsum pars primaque et una,\n\n \n\ninde aliae atque aliae similes ex ordine partes\n\n \n\nagmine condenso naturam corporis explent;\n\n \n\nquae quoniam per se nequeunt constare, necessest\n\n \n\nhaerere unde queant nulla ratione revelli.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur solida primordia simplicitate,\n\n \n\nquae minimis stipata cohaerent partibus arte.\n\n \n\nnon ex illorum conventu conciliata,\n\n \n\nsed magis aeterna pollentia simplicitate,\n\n \n\nunde neque avelli quicquam neque deminui iam\n\n \n\nconcedit natura reservans semina rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","text":"Praeterea nisi erit minimum, parvissima quaeque\n\n \n\ncorpora constabunt ex partibus infinitis,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi dimidiae partis pars semper habebit\n\n \n\ndimidiam partem nec res praefiniet ulla.\n\n \n\nergo rerum inter summam minimamque quod escit,\n\n \n\nnil erit ut distet; nam quamvis funditus omnis\n\n \n\nsumma sit infinita, tamen, parvissima quae sunt,\n\n \n\nex infinitis constabunt partibus aeque.\n\n \n\nquod quoniam ratio reclamat vera negatque\n\n \n\ncredere posse animum, victus fateare necessest\n\n \n\nesse ea quae nullis iam praedita partibus extent\n\n \n\net minima constent natura. quae quoniam sunt,\n\n \n\nilla quoque esse tibi solida atque aeterna fatendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0042","text":"Denique si minimas in partis cuncta resolvi\n\n \n\ncogere consuesset rerum natura creatrix,\n\n \n\niam nihil ex illis eadem reparare valeret\n\n \n\npropterea quia, quae nullis sunt partibus aucta,\n\n \n\nnon possunt ea quae debet genitalis habere\n\n \n\nmateries, varios conexus pondera plagas\n\n \n\nconcursus motus, per quas res quaeque geruntur.\n\n  \n\nQuapropter qui materiem rerum esse putarunt\n\n \n\nignem atque ex igni summam consistere solo,\n\n \n\nmagno opere a vera lapsi ratione videntur.\n\n \n\nHeraclitus init quorum dux proelia primus,\n\n \n\nclarus ob obscuram linguam magis inter inanis\n\n \n\nquamde gravis inter Graios, qui vera requirunt;\n\n \n\nomnia enim stolidi magis admirantur amantque,\n\n \n\ninversis quae sub verbis latitantia cernunt,\n\n \n\nveraque constituunt quae belle tangere possunt\n\n \n\nauris et lepido quae sunt fucata sonore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0043","text":"Nam cur tam variae res possent esse, requiro,\n\n \n\nex uno si sunt igni puroque creatae?\n\n \n\nnil prodesset enim calidum denserier ignem\n\n \n\nnec rare fieri, si partes ignis eandem\n\n \n\nnaturam quam totus habet super ignis haberent.\n\n \n\nacrior ardor enim conductis partibus esset,\n\n \n\nlanguidior porro disiectis disque supatis.\n\n \n\namplius hoc fieri nihil est quod posse rearis\n\n \n\ntalibus in causis, ne dum variantia rerum\n\n \n\ntanta queat densis rarisque ex ignibus esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0044","text":"Id quoque: si faciant admixtum rebus inane,\n\n \n\ndenseri poterunt ignes rarique relinqui;\n\n \n\nsed quia multa sibi cernunt contraria quae sint\n\n \n\net fugitant in rebus inane relinquere purum,\n\n \n\nardua dum metuunt, amittunt vera viai\n\n \n\nnec rursum cernunt exempto rebus inane\n\n \n\nomnia denseri fierique ex omnibus unum\n\n \n\ncorpus, nil ab se quod possit mittere raptim,\n\n \n\naestifer ignis uti lumen iacit atque vaporem,\n\n \n\nut videas non e stipatis partibus esse.\n\n \n\nQuod si forte alia credunt ratione potesse\n\n \n\nignis in coetu stingui mutareque corpus,\n\n \n\nscilicet ex nulla facere id si parte reparcent,\n\n \n\noccidet ad nihilum ni mirum funditus ardor\n\n \n\nomnis et e nihilo fient quae cumque creantur;\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque suis mutatum finibus exit,\n\n \n\ncontinuo hoc mors est illius quod fuit ante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","text":"proinde aliquid superare necesse est incolume ollis,\n\n \n\nne tibi res redeant ad nilum funditus omnes\n\n \n\nde nihiloque renata vigescat copia rerum.\n\n \n\nNunc igitur quoniam certissima corpora quaedam\n\n \n\nsunt, quae conservant naturam semper eandem,\n\n \n\nquorum abitu aut aditu mutatoque ordine mutant\n\n \n\nnaturam res et convertunt corpora sese,\n\n \n\nscire licet non esse haec ignea corpora rerum.\n\n \n\nnil referret enim quaedam decedere, abire\n\n \n\natque alia adtribui mutarique ordine quaedam,\n\n \n\nsi tamen ardoris naturam cuncta tenerent;\n\n \n\nignis enim foret omnimodis quod cumque crearet.\n\n \n\nverum, ut opinor, itast: sunt quaedam corpora, quorum\n\n \n\nconcursus motus ordo positura figurae\n\n \n\nefficiunt ignis mutatoque ordine mutant\n\n \n\nnaturam neque sunt igni simulata neque ulli\n\n \n\npraeterea rei quae corpora mittere possit\n\n \n\nsensibus et nostros adiectu tangere tactus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0046","text":"dicere porro ignem res omnis esse neque ullam\n\n \n\nrem veram in numero rerum constare nisi ignem,\n\n \n\nquod facit hic idem, perdelirum esse videtur.\n\n \n\nnam contra sensus ab sensibus ipse repugnat\n\n \n\net labefactat eos, unde omnia credita pendent,\n\n \n\nunde hic cognitus est ipsi quem nominat ignem;\n\n \n\ncredit enim sensus ignem cognoscere vere,\n\n \n\ncetera non credit, quae nilo clara minus sunt.\n\n \n\nquod mihi cum vanum tum delirum esse videtur;\n\n \n\nquo referemus enim? quid nobis certius ipsis\n\n \n\nsensibus esse potest, qui vera ac falsa notemus?\n\n \n\nPraeterea quare quisquam magis omnia tollat\n\n \n\net velit ardoris naturam linquere solam,\n\n \n\nquam neget esse ignis, aliam tamen esse relinquat?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047","text":"aequa videtur enim dementia dicere utrumque.\n\n \n\nQuapropter qui materiem rerum esse putarunt\n\n \n\nignem atque ex igni summam consistere posse,\n\n \n\net qui principium gignundis aera rebus\n\n \n\nconstituere aut umorem qui cumque putarunt\n\n \n\nfingere res ipsum per se terramve creare\n\n \n\nomnia et in rerum naturas vertier omnis,\n\n \n\nmagno opere a vero longe derrasse videntur.\n\n \n\nadde etiam qui conduplicant primordia rerum\n\n \n\naera iungentes igni terramque liquori,\n\n \n\net qui quattuor ex rebus posse omnia rentur\n\n \n\nex igni terra atque anima procrescere et imbri.\n\n \n\nquorum Acragantinus cum primis Empedocles est,\n\n \n\ninsula quem triquetris terrarum gessit in oris,\n\n \n\nquam fluitans circum magnis anfractibus aequor\n\n \n\nIonium glaucis aspargit virus ab undis\n\n \n\nangustoque fretu rapidum mare dividit undis\n\n \n\nAeoliae terrarum oras a finibus eius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0048","text":"hic est vasta Charybdis et hic Aetnaea minantur\n\n \n\nmurmura flammarum rursum se colligere iras,\n\n \n\nfaucibus eruptos iterum vis ut vomat ignis\n\n \n\nad caelumque ferat flammai fulgura rursum.\n\n \n\nquae cum magna modis multis miranda videtur\n\n \n\ngentibus humanis regio visendaque fertur\n\n \n\nrebus opima bonis, multa munita virum vi,\n\n \n\nnil tamen hoc habuisse viro praeclarius in se\n\n \n\nnec sanctum magis et mirum carumque videtur.\n\n \n\ncarmina quin etiam divini pectoris eius\n\n \n\nvociferantur et exponunt praeclara reperta,\n\n \n\nut vix humana videatur stirpe creatus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0049","text":"Hic tamen et supra quos diximus inferiores\n\n \n\npartibus egregie multis multoque minores,\n\n \n\nquamquam multa bene ac divinitus invenientes\n\n \n\nex adyto tam quam cordis responsa dedere\n\n \n\nsanctius et multo certa ratione magis quam\n\n \n\nPythia quae tripodi a Phoebi lauroque profatur,\n\n \n\nprincipiis tamen in rerum fecere ruinas\n\n \n\net graviter magni magno cecidere ibi casu.\n\n \n\nPrimum quod motus exempto rebus inani\n\n \n\nconstituunt et res mollis rarasque relinquunt\n\n \n\naera solem ignem terras animalia frugis\n\n \n\nnec tamen admiscent in eorum corpus inane;\n\n \n\ndeinde quod omnino finem non esse secandis\n\n \n\ncorporibus facient neque pausam stare fragori\n\n \n\nnec prorsum in rebus minimum consistere quicquam,\n\n \n\ncum videamus id extremum cuiusque cacumen\n\n \n\nesse quod ad sensus nostros minimum esse videtur,\n\n \n\nconicere ut possis ex hoc, quae cernere non quis\n\n \n\nextremum quod habent, minimum consistere rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050","text":"Huc accedit item, quoniam primordia rerum\n\n \n\nmollia constituunt, quae nos nativa videmus\n\n \n\nesse et mortali cum corpore, funditus ut qui\n\n \n\ndebeat ad nihilum iam rerum summa reverti\n\n \n\nde nihiloque renata vigescere copia rerum;\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque quid a vero iam distet habebis.\n\n \n\nDeinde inimica modis multis sunt atque veneno\n\n \n\nipsa sibi inter se; quare aut congressa peribunt\n\n \n\naut ita diffugient, ut tempestate coacta\n\n \n\nfulmina diffugere atque imbris ventosque videmus.\n\n \n\nDenique quattuor ex rebus si cuncta creantur\n\n \n\natque in eas rursum res omnia dissoluuntur,\n\n \n\nqui magis illa queunt rerum primordia dici\n\n \n\nquam contra res illorum retroque putari?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0051","text":"alternis gignuntur enim mutantque colorem\n\n \n\net totam inter se naturam tempore ab omni.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nsin ita forte putas ignis terraeque coire\n\n \n\ncorpus et aerias auras roremque liquoris,\n\n \n\nnil in concilio naturam ut mutet eorum,\n\n \n\nnulla tibi ex illis poterit res esse creata,\n\n \n\nnon animans, non exanimo cum corpore, ut arbos;\n\n \n\nquippe suam quicque in coetu variantis acervi\n\n \n\nnaturam ostendet mixtusque videbitur aer\n\n \n\ncum terra simul et quodam cum rore manere.\n\n \n\nat primordia gignundis in rebus oportet\n\n \n\nnaturam clandestinam caecamque adhibere,\n\n \n\nemineat ne quid, quod contra pugnet et obstet\n\n \n\nquo minus esse queat proprie quodcumque creatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0052","text":"Quin etiam repetunt a caelo atque ignibus eius\n\n \n\net primum faciunt ignem se vertere in auras\n\n \n\naeris, hinc imbrem gigni terramque creari\n\n \n\nex imbri retroque a terra cuncta reverti,\n\n \n\numorem primum, post aera, deinde calorem,\n\n \n\nnec cessare haec inter se mutare, meare\n\n \n\na caelo ad terram, de terra ad sidera mundi.\n\n \n\nquod facere haud ullo debent primordia pacto.\n\n \n\nimmutabile enim quiddam superare necessest,\n\n \n\nne res ad nihilum redigantur funditus omnes;\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque suis mutatum finibus exit,\n\n \n\ncontinuo hoc mors est illius quod fuit ante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0053","text":"quapropter quoniam quae paulo diximus ante\n\n \n\nin commutatum veniunt, constare necessest\n\n \n\nex aliis ea, quae nequeant convertier usquam,\n\n \n\nne tibi res redeant ad nilum funditus omnis;\n\n \n\nquin potius tali natura praedita quaedam\n\n \n\ncorpora constituas, ignem si forte crearint,\n\n \n\nposse eadem demptis paucis paucisque tributis,\n\n \n\nordine mutato et motu, facere aeris auras,\n\n \n\nsic alias aliis rebus mutarier omnis?\n\n \n\n'At manifesta palam res indicat' inquis 'in auras\n\n \n\naeris e terra res omnis crescere alique;\n\n \n\net nisi tempestas indulget tempore fausto\n\n \n\nimbribus, ut tabe nimborum arbusta vacillent,\n\n \n\nsolque sua pro parte fovet tribuitque calorem,\n\n \n\ncrescere non possint fruges arbusta animantis.'","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0054","text":"scilicet et nisi nos cibus aridus et tener umor\n\n \n\nadiuvet, amisso iam corpore vita quoque omnis\n\n \n\nomnibus e nervis atque ossibus exsoluatur;\n\n \n\nadiutamur enim dubio procul atque alimur nos\n\n \n\ncertis ab rebus, certis aliae atque aliae res.\n\n \n\nni mirum quia multa modis communia multis\n\n \n\nmultarum rerum in rebus primordia mixta\n\n \n\nsunt, ideo variis variae res rebus aluntur.\n\n \n\natque eadem magni refert primordia saepe\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali positura contineantur\n\n \n\net quos inter se dent motus accipiantque;\n\n \n\nnamque eadem caelum mare terras flumina solem\n\n \n\nconstituunt, eadem fruges arbusta animantis,\n\n \n\nverum aliis alioque modo commixta moventur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0055","text":"quin etiam passim nostris in versibus ipsis\n\n \n\nmulta elementa vides multis communia verbis,\n\n \n\ncum tamen inter se versus ac verba necessest\n\n \n\nconfiteare et re et sonitu distare sonanti.\n\n \n\ntantum elementa queunt permutato ordine solo;\n\n \n\nat rerum quae sunt primordia, plura adhibere\n\n \n\npossunt unde queant variae res quaeque creari.\n\n \n\nNunc et Anaxagorae scrutemur homoeomerian\n\n \n\nquam Grai memorant nec nostra dicere lingua\n\n \n\nconcedit nobis patrii sermonis egestas,\n\n \n\nsed tamen ipsam rem facilest exponere verbis.\n\n \n\nprincipio, rerum quam dicit homoeomerian,\n\n \n\nossa videlicet e pauxillis atque minutis\n\n \n\nossibus hic et de pauxillis atque minutis\n\n \n\nvisceribus viscus gigni sanguenque creari\n\n \n\nsanguinis inter se multis coeuntibus guttis\n\n \n\nex aurique putat micis consistere posse\n\n \n\naurum et de terris terram concrescere parvis,\n\n \n\nignibus ex ignis, umorem umoribus esse,\n\n \n\ncetera consimili fingit ratione putatque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","text":"nec tamen esse ulla de parte in rebus inane\n\n \n\nconcedit neque corporibus finem esse secandis.\n\n \n\nquare in utraque mihi pariter ratione videtur\n\n \n\nerrare atque illi, supra quos diximus ante.\n\n \n\nAdde quod inbecilla nimis primordia fingit;\n\n \n\nsi primordia sunt, simili quae praedita constant\n\n \n\nnatura atque ipsae res sunt aequeque laborant\n\n \n\net pereunt, neque ab exitio res ulla refrenat.\n\n \n\nnam quid in oppressu valido durabit eorum,\n\n \n\nut mortem effugiat, leti sub dentibus ipsis?\n\n \n\nignis an umor an aura? quid horum? sanguen an ossa?\n\n \n\nnil ut opinor, ubi ex aequo res funditus omnis\n\n \n\ntam mortalis erit quam quae manifesta videmus\n\n \n\nex oculis nostris aliqua vi victa perire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0057","text":"at neque reccidere ad nihilum res posse neque autem\n\n \n\ncrescere de nihilo testor res ante probatas.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam cibus auget corpus alitque,\n\n \n\nscire licet nobis venas et sanguen et ossa\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nsive cibos omnis commixto corpore dicent\n\n \n\nesse et habere in se nervorum corpora parva\n\n \n\nossaque et omnino venas partisque cruoris,\n\n \n\nfiet uti cibus omnis et aridus et liquor ipse\n\n \n\nex alienigenis rebus constare putetur,\n\n \n\nossibus et nervis sanieque et sanguine mixto.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quae cumque e terra corpora crescunt,\n\n \n\nsi sunt in terris, terram constare necessest\n\n \n\nex alienigenis, quae terris exoriuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0058","text":"transfer item, totidem verbis utare licebit:\n\n \n\nin lignis si flamma latet fumusque cinisque,\n\n \n\nex alienigenis consistant ligna necessest,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nex alienigenis, quae lignis exoriuntur.\n\n \n\nLinquitur hic quaedam latitandi copia tenvis,\n\n \n\nid quod Anaxagoras sibi sumit, ut omnibus omnis\n\n \n\nres putet inmixtas rebus latitare, sed illud\n\n \n\napparere unum, cuius sint plurima mixta\n\n \n\net magis in promptu primaque in fronte locata.\n\n \n\nquod tamen a vera longe ratione repulsumst;\n\n \n\nconveniebat enim fruges quoque saepe, minaci\n\n \n\nrobore cum in saxi franguntur, mittere signum\n\n \n\nsanguinis aut aliquid, nostro quae corpore aluntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0059","text":"cum lapidi in lapidem terimus, manare cruorem\n\n \n\nconsimili ratione herbis quoque saepe decebat,\n\n \n\net latices dulcis guttas similique sapore\n\n \n\nmittere, lanigerae quali sunt ubere lactis,\n\n \n\nscilicet et glebis terrarum saepe friatis\n\n \n\nherbarum genera et fruges frondesque videri\n\n \n\ndispertita inter terram latitare minute,\n\n \n\npostremo in lignis cinerem fumumque videri,\n\n \n\ncum praefracta forent, ignisque latere minutos.\n\n \n\nquorum nil fieri quoniam manifesta docet res,\n\n \n\nscire licet non esse in rebus res ita mixtas,\n\n \n\nverum semina multimodis inmixta latere\n\n \n\nmultarum rerum in rebus communia debent.\n\n \n\n'At saepe in magnis fit montibus' inquis 'ut altis\n\n \n\narboribus vicina cacumina summa terantur\n\n \n\ninter se validis facere id cogentibus austris,\n\n \n\ndonec flammai fulserunt flore coorto.'","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0060","text":"scilicet et non est lignis tamen insitus ignis,\n\n \n\nverum semina sunt ardoris multa, terendo\n\n \n\nquae cum confluxere, creant incendia silvis.\n\n \n\nquod si facta foret silvis abscondita flamma,\n\n \n\nnon possent ullum tempus celarier ignes,\n\n \n\nconficerent volgo silvas, arbusta cremarent.\n\n \n\niamne vides igitur, paulo quod diximus ante,\n\n \n\npermagni referre eadem primordia saepe\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali positura contineantur\n\n \n\net quos inter se dent motus accipiantque,\n\n \n\natque eadem paulo inter se mutata creare\n\n \n\nignes et lignum? quo pacto verba quoque ipsa\n\n \n\ninter se paulo mutatis sunt elementis,\n\n \n\ncum ligna atque ignes distincta voce notemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0061","text":"Denique iam quae cumque in rebus cernis apertis\n\n \n\nsi fieri non posse putas, quin materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora consimili natura praedita fingas,\n\n \n\nhac ratione tibi pereunt primordia rerum:\n\n \n\nfiet uti risu tremulo concussa cachinnent\n\n \n\net lacrimis salsis umectent ora genasque.\n\n \n\nNunc age, quod super est, cognosce et clarius audi.\n\n \n\nnec me animi fallit quam sint obscura; sed acri\n\n \n\npercussit thyrso laudis spes magna meum cor\n\n \n\net simul incussit suavem mi in pectus amorem\n\n \n\nMusarum, quo nunc instinctus mente vigenti\n\n \n\navia Pieridum peragro loca nullius ante\n\n \n\ntrita solo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062","text":"iuvat integros accedere fontis\n\n \n\natque haurire iuvatque novos decerpere flores\n\n \n\ninsignemque meo capiti petere inde coronam,\n\n \n\nunde prius nulli velarint tempora Musae;\n\n \n\nprimum quod magnis doceo de rebus et artis\n\n \n\nreligionum animum nodis exsolvere pergo,\n\n \n\ndeinde quod obscura de re tam lucida pango\n\n \n\ncarmina musaeo contingens cuncta lepore.\n\n \n\nid quoque enim non ab nulla ratione videtur;\n\n \n\nsed vel uti pueris absinthia taetra medentes\n\n \n\ncum dare conantur, prius oras pocula circum\n\n \n\ncontingunt mellis dulci flavoque liquore,\n\n \n\nut puerorum aetas inprovida ludificetur\n\n \n\nlabrorum tenus, interea perpotet amarum\n\n \n\nabsinthi laticem deceptaque non capiatur,\n\n \n\nsed potius tali facto recreata valescat,\n\n \n\nsic ego nunc, quoniam haec ratio plerumque videtur\n\n \n\ntristior esse quibus non est tractata, retroque\n\n \n\nvolgus abhorret ab hac, volui tibi suaviloquenti\n\n \n\ncarmine Pierio rationem exponere nostram\n\n \n\net quasi musaeo dulci contingere melle,\n\n \n\nsi tibi forte animum tali ratione tenere\n\n \n\nversibus in nostris possem, dum perspicis omnem\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum, qua constet compta figura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","text":"Sed quoniam docui solidissima materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora perpetuo volitare invicta per aevom,\n\n \n\nnunc age, summai quaedam sit finis eorum\n\n \n\nnecne sit, evolvamus; item quod inane repertumst\n\n \n\nseu locus ac spatium, res in quo quaeque gerantur,\n\n \n\npervideamus utrum finitum funditus omne\n\n \n\nconstet an immensum pateat vasteque profundum.\n\n \n\nOmne quod est igitur nulla regione viarum\n\n \n\nfinitumst; namque extremum debebat habere.\n\n \n\nextremum porro nullius posse videtur\n\n \n\nesse, nisi ultra sit quod finiat, ut videatur\n\n \n\nquo non longius haec sensus natura sequatur.\n\n \n\nnunc extra summam quoniam nihil esse fatendum,\n\n \n\nnon habet extremum, caret ergo fine modoque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0064","text":"nec refert quibus adsistas regionibus eius;\n\n \n\nusque adeo, quem quisque locum possedit, in omnis\n\n \n\ntantundem partis infinitum omne relinquit.\n\n \n\nPraeterea si iam finitum constituatur\n\n \n\nomne quod est spatium, si quis procurrat ad oras\n\n \n\nultimus extremas iaciatque volatile telum,\n\n \n\nid validis utrum contortum viribus ire\n\n \n\nquo fuerit missum mavis longeque volare,\n\n \n\nan prohibere aliquid censes obstareque posse?\n\n \n\nalterutrum fatearis enim sumasque necessest.\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque tibi effugium praecludit et omne\n\n \n\ncogit ut exempta concedas fine patere.\n\n \n\nnam sive est aliquid quod probeat efficiatque\n\n \n\nquo minus quo missum est veniat finique locet se,\n\n \n\nsive foras fertur, non est a fine profectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0065","text":"hoc pacto sequar atque, oras ubi cumque locaris\n\n \n\nextremas, quaeram: quid telo denique fiet?\n\n \n\nfiet uti nusquam possit consistere finis\n\n \n\neffugiumque fugae prolatet copia semper.\n\n \n\nPraeterea spatium summai totius omne\n\n \n\nundique si inclusum certis consisteret oris\n\n \n\nfinitumque foret, iam copia materiai\n\n \n\nundique ponderibus solidis confluxet ad imum\n\n \n\nnec res ulla geri sub caeli tegmine posset\n\n \n\nnec foret omnino caelum neque lumina solis,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi materies omnis cumulata iaceret\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore subsidendo.\n\n \n\nat nunc ni mirum requies data principiorum\n\n \n\ncorporibus nullast, quia nil est funditus imum,\n\n \n\nquo quasi confluere et sedes ubi ponere possint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0066","text":"semper in adsiduo motu res quaeque geruntur\n\n \n\npartibus in cunctis, infernaque suppeditantur\n\n \n\nex infinito cita corpora materiai.\n\n \n\nPostremo ante oculos res rem finire videtur;\n\n \n\naer dissaepit collis atque aera montes,\n\n \n\nterra mare et contra mare terras terminat omnis;\n\n \n\nomne quidem vero nihil est quod finiat extra.\n\n  \n\nest igitur natura loci spatiumque profundi,\n\n \n\nquod neque clara suo percurrere fulmina cursu\n\n \n\nperpetuo possint aevi labentia tractu\n\n \n\nnec prorsum facere ut restet minus ire meando;\n\n \n\nusque adeo passim patet ingens copia rebus\n\n \n\nfinibus exemptis in cunctas undique partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0067","text":"Ipsa modum porro sibi rerum summa parare\n\n \n\nne possit, natura tenet, quae corpus inane\n\n \n\net quod inane autem est finiri corpore cogit,\n\n \n\nut sic alternis infinita omnia reddat,\n\n \n\naut etiam alterutrum, nisi terminet alterum eorum,\n\n \n\nsimplice natura pateat tamen inmoderatum,\n\n \n\nnec mare nec tellus neque caeli lucida templa\n\n \n\nnec mortale genus nec divum corpora sancta\n\n \n\nexiguum possent horai sistere tempus;\n\n \n\nnam dispulsa suo de coetu materiai\n\n \n\ncopia ferretur magnum per inane soluta,\n\n \n\nsive adeo potius numquam concreta creasset\n\n \n\nullam rem, quoniam cogi disiecta nequisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","text":"nam certe neque consilio primordia rerum\n\n \n\nordine se suo quaeque sagaci mente locarunt\n\n \n\nnec quos quaeque darent motus pepigere profecto\n\n \n\nsed quia multa modis multis mutata per omne\n\n \n\nex infinito vexantur percita plagis,\n\n \n\nomne genus motus et coetus experiundo\n\n \n\ntandem deveniunt in talis disposituras,\n\n \n\nqualibus haec rerum consistit summa creata,\n\n \n\net multos etiam magnos servata per annos\n\n \n\nut semel in motus coniectast convenientis,\n\n \n\nefficit ut largis avidum mare fluminis undis\n\n \n\nintegrent amnes et solis terra vapore\n\n \n\nfota novet fetus summissaque gens animantum\n\n \n\nfloreat et vivant labentis aetheris ignes.\n\n \n\nquod nullo facerent pacto, nisi materiai\n\n \n\nex infinito suboriri copia posset,\n\n \n\nunde amissa solent reparare in tempore quaeque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0069","text":"nam vel uti privata cibo natura animantum\n\n \n\ndiffluit amittens corpus, sic omnia debent\n\n \n\ndissolui simul ac defecit suppeditare\n\n \n\nmateries aliqua ratione aversa viai.\n\n \n\nnec plagae possunt extrinsecus undique summam\n\n \n\nconservare omnem, quae cumque est conciliata.\n\n \n\ncudere enim crebro possunt partemque morari,\n\n \n\ndum veniant aliae ac suppleri summa queatur;\n\n \n\ninter dum resilire tamen coguntur et una\n\n \n\nprincipiis rerum spatium tempusque fugai\n\n \n\nlargiri, ut possint a coetu libera ferri.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam suboriri multa necessest,\n\n \n\net tamen ut plagae quoque possint suppetere ipsae,\n\n \n\ninfinita opus est vis undique materiai.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","text":"Illud in his rebus longe fuge credere, Memmi,\n\n \n\nin medium summae quod dicunt omnia niti\n\n \n\natque ideo mundi naturam stare sine ullis\n\n \n\nictibus externis neque quoquam posse resolvi\n\n \n\nsumma atque ima, quod in medium sint omnia nixa,\n\n \n\nipsum si quicquam posse in se sistere credis,\n\n \n\net quae pondera sunt sub terris omnia sursum\n\n \n\nnitier in terraque retro requiescere posta,\n\n \n\nut per aquas quae nunc rerum simulacra videmus;\n\n \n\net simili ratione animalia suppa vagari\n\n \n\ncontendunt neque posse e terris in loca caeli\n\n \n\nreccidere inferiora magis quam corpora nostra\n\n \n\nsponte sua possint in caeli templa volare;\n\n \n\nilli cum videant solem, nos sidera noctis\n\n \n\ncernere et alternis nobiscum tempora caeli\n\n \n\ndividere et noctes parilis agitare diebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071","text":"sed vanus stolidis haec * * *\n\n \n\namplexi quod habent perv * * *\n\n \n\nnam medium nihil esse potest * * *\n\n \n\ninfinita; neque omnino, si iam medium sit,\n\n \n\npossit ibi quicquam consistere * * *\n\n \n\nquam quavis alia longe ratione * * *\n\n \n\nomnis enim locus ac spatium, quod inane vocamus,\n\n \n\nper medium, per non medium, concedere debet\n\n \n\naeque ponderibus, motus qua cumque feruntur.\n\n \n\nnec quisquam locus est, quo corpora cum venerunt,\n\n \n\nponderis amissa vi possint stare in inani;\n\n \n\nnec quod inane autem est ulli subsistere debet,\n\n \n\nquin, sua quod natura petit, concedere pergat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0072","text":"haud igitur possunt tali ratione teneri\n\n \n\nres in concilium medii cuppedine victae.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam non omnia corpora fingunt\n\n \n\nin medium niti, sed terrarum atque liquoris\n\n \n\numorem ponti magnasque e montibus undas,\n\n \n\net quasi terreno quae corpore contineantur,\n\n \n\nat contra tenuis exponunt aeris auras\n\n \n\net calidos simul a medio differrier ignis,\n\n \n\natque ideo totum circum tremere aethera signis\n\n \n\net solis flammam per caeli caerula pasci,\n\n \n\nquod calor a medio fugiens se ibi conligat omnis,\n\n \n\nnec prorsum arboribus summos frondescere ramos\n\n \n\nposse, nisi a terris paulatim cuique cibatum\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0073","text":"[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nne volucri ritu flammarum moenia mundi\n\n \n\ndiffugiant subito magnum per inane soluta\n\n \n\net ne cetera consimili ratione sequantur\n\n \n\nneve ruant caeli tonitralia templa superne\n\n \n\nterraque se pedibus raptim subducat et omnis\n\n \n\ninter permixtas rerum caelique ruinas\n\n \n\ncorpora solventes abeat per inane profundum,\n\n \n\ntemporis ut puncto nihil extet reliquiarum\n\n \n\ndesertum praeter spatium et primordia caeca.\n\n \n\nnam qua cumque prius de parti corpora desse\n\n \n\nconstitues, haec rebus erit pars ianua leti,\n\n \n\nhac se turba foras dabit omnis materiai.\n\n \n\nHaec sic pernosces parva perductus opella;\n\n \n\nnamque alid ex alio clarescet nec tibi caeca\n\n \n\nnox iter eripiet, quin ultima naturai\n\n \n\npervideas: ita res accendent lumina rebus.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"27a919dbf5050992c80dde1d3f5fee0e20243398bd2f3effc348c1f4dacd4fad","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T235418318267Z_6ad7c9c1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book One","source_sha256":"27a919dbf5050992c80dde1d3f5fee0e20243398bd2f3effc348c1f4dacd4fad","sha256":"c0658a7d88e557972cd1cfe7ab0f611c09205310954cf577e8db1698390d8188","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:54:18.318277+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0001","text":"## Book One\n\nMother of the Aeneads, delight of men and gods,\n\nnourishing Venus, beneath the gliding signs of heaven,\n\nyou who haunt the ship-bearing sea, who haunt the fruit-bearing lands,\n\nsince through you every race of living things\n\nis conceived and, having arisen, sees the light of the sun:\n\nbefore you, goddess, before you the winds flee, before you the clouds of heaven\n\nand your coming; for you the artful earth\n\nputs forth sweet flowers, for you the plains of the sea smile,\n\nand the tranquil heaven shines with outpoured light.\n\nFor as soon as the vernal aspect of day has been revealed\n\nand the fecund breeze of the west wind, unbarred, flourishes,\n\nfirst the birds of the air, goddess, proclaim you and your\n\nentrance, their hearts struck by your power.\n\nThen wild beasts and herds bound over the joyful pastures\n\nand swim across swift streams: thus, seized by delight,\n\neach follows you eagerly wherever you proceed to lead it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","text":"Finally, through seas and mountains and rapacious rivers,\n\nand the leafy homes of birds and verdant fields,\n\ninstilling soothing love into the breasts of all,\n\nyou bring it about that they eagerly propagate their races, each after its kind.\n\nSince you alone govern the nature of things,\n\nand without you nothing arises into the divine shores of light,\n\nnor does anything come to be joyful or lovely,\n\nI strive to have you as a partner in writing verses,\n\nwhich I attempt to compose concerning the nature of things\n\nfor our Memmius, whom you, goddess, at every time\n\nhave wished, adorned with all things, to excel.\n\nTherefore all the more, goddess, grant everlasting charm to my words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003","text":"Cause meanwhile the savage works of war\n\nto lie lulled to rest throughout all seas and lands;\n\nfor you alone can aid mortals with tranquil peace,\n\nsince Mavors, mighty in arms, governs the savage works of war,\n\nand he often casts himself back into your lap,\n\nconquered by the everlasting wound of love,\n\nand thus, looking up, with his shapely neck thrown back,\n\nhe feeds his eager eyes with love, gazing hungrily upon you, goddess,\n\nand, as he lies on his back, his breath hangs upon your lips.\n\nAs he reclines thus, goddess, upon your sacred body,\n\npour forth, bending over and enfolding him, sweet speech from your lips,\n\nseeking, glorious one, gentle peace for the Romans;\n\nfor neither can we pursue this task with an untroubled mind in this troubled time of our country,\n\nnor can the illustrious offspring of Memmius fail\n\nthe common safety in such circumstances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","text":"For it is necessary that the whole nature of the gods\n\nenjoy immortal life with the greatest peace,\n\nremoved and separated far from our affairs;\n\nfor, free from every pain, free from dangers,\n\nherself powerful in her own resources, needing nothing of us,\n\nneither won over by good services nor touched by anger.\n\nFor what remains, lend empty ears and an acute mind,\n\nwithdrawn from cares, to true reasoning,\n\nlest my gifts, laid out for you with faithful zeal,\n\nyou should abandon in contempt before they are understood.\n\nFor I shall begin to discourse to you on the supreme ordering of heaven and of the gods,\n\nand I shall disclose the first-beginnings of things,\n\nfrom which all nature creates things, increases and nourishes them,\n\nand into which that same nature in turn resolves them when destroyed,\n\nwhich, in setting forth our account, we are accustomed to call matter and generative bodies for things,\n\nand the seeds of things,\n\nand likewise to employ these same terms\n\nas first bodies, because all things are from those first things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0005","text":"When human life lay foully before men's eyes\n\nupon the earth, crushed beneath the heavy weight of religion,\n\nwhich showed its head from the regions of heaven,\n\nlooming over mortals with a horrible aspect from above,\n\na Greek man was the first of mortals to dare to raise his eyes against it,\n\nand the first to stand against it;\n\nhim neither the renown of the gods nor thunderbolts nor heaven with threatening\n\nmurmur restrained, but all the more they stirred up the keen\n\ncourage of his mind, so that he first desired to break apart the tight\n\nbars of nature's gates.\n\nTherefore the living force of his mind prevailed and\n\nwent forth far beyond the flaming ramparts of the world\n\nand traversed the whole immeasurable expanse in thought and spirit,\n\nwhence, victorious, he reports to us what can come to be,\n\nwhat cannot, and finally by what power a limit is fixed for each thing,\n\nand by what principle there is a boundary deeply set.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006","text":"Wherefore religion, in turn, is cast beneath our feet\n\nand trampled down; victory makes us equal to heaven.\n\nIn these matters I fear this: perhaps you may think\n\nthat you are entering upon the elements of an impious doctrine and a path\n\nof crime. Yet, on the contrary, more often that\n\nreligion has brought forth criminal and impious deeds.\n\nHow at Aulis they foully defiled the altar of the maiden Trivia\n\nwith the blood of Iphianassa,\n\nthe chosen leaders of the Danaans, foremost among men.\n\nAs soon as she perceived that the fillet, encircling her maidenly tresses,\n\nhad been poured forth equally down each cheek,\n\nand that her sorrowful father stood before the altars,\n\nand beside him his attendants concealed the steel,\n\nwhile the citizens shed tears at the sight of her,\n\nspeechless with fear, she sank to the ground on bended knees.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","text":"Nor could it avail the wretched girl at such a time\n\nthat she had first bestowed upon the king a father's name;\n\nfor, lifted up by men's hands and trembling, to the altars\n\nyou led forth, not so that, in the solemn custom of the rites,\n\nwhen it was completed, she might be attended by bright Hymenaeus,\n\nbut so that, chaste though unchastely, at the very time of marriage,\n\nshe might fall as a sorrowful victim beneath her sorrowing father's sacrifice,\n\nso that a happy and favorable departure might be granted to the fleet.\n\nTo such great evils was religion able to persuade.\n\nYou yourself, at any time now, will seek, overcome by the terrifying\n\nsayings of seers, to desert us.\n\nFor indeed how many dreams can they now invent for you,\n\nwhich can overturn the principles of life\n\nand disturb all your fortunes with fear!\n\nAnd deservedly; for if men saw that there were a sure end\n\nto their miseries, they would be strong enough by some means\n\nto withstand the religions and threats of seers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","text":"Now there is no reason for resisting, no power,\n\nsince eternal punishments in death must be feared.\n\nFor it is unknown what the nature of the soul may be,\n\nwhether it is born, or, on the contrary, is implanted in beings as they are born,\n\nand at the same time perishes with us, severed by death,\n\nor visits the darkness of Orcus and the vast hollows,\n\nor by divine agency inserts itself into other beasts,\n\nas our Ennius sang, who first from lovely\n\nHelicon bore down a crown of ever-living foliage,\n\nwhich might be renowned and illustrious among the Italian tribes of men;\n\nalthough nevertheless there are Acherusian temples besides,\n\nEnnius sets forth in everlasting verses, declaring\n\nthat neither souls nor our bodies endure there,\n\nbut certain images, wan in wondrous ways;\n\nwhence he relates that there arose before him the form of ever-flourishing Homer\n\nand began to shed salt tears\n\nand to unfold the nature of things in words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0009","text":"Wherefore we must rightly hold an account also of things above,\n\nof the courses of the sun and moon,\n\nby what method they come about, and by what force each thing is done\n\non earth; then, above all, it must be seen by keen reasoning\n\nof what nature soul and mind consist,\n\nand what thing, confronting our minds while we are awake,\n\nterrifies us when we are afflicted by disease and buried in sleep,\n\nso that we seem to see them and hear them face to face,\n\nwhose bones the earth embraces after death has been undergone.\n\nNor does it escape my mind that the obscure discoveries of the Greeks\n\nare difficult to illuminate in Latin verses,\n\nespecially since many matters must be treated in new words\n\nbecause of the poverty of the language and the novelty of the things;\n\nyet your merit and the hoped-for pleasure\n\nof sweet friendship persuade me to undertake any toil\n\nand induce me to keep watch through the tranquil nights,\n\nseeking by what words and finally by what poem\n\nthat I may be able to spread clear lights before your mind,\n\nwhereby you may be able to behold things hidden deep within.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","text":"This terror of the mind, therefore, and these shadows must\n\nbe dispelled not by the sun’s rays or the bright shafts of day,\n\nbut by the aspect and reasoning of nature.\n\nThe first principle from which our account shall here take its beginning:\n\nnothing is ever begotten from nothing by divine power.\n\nFor indeed fear holds all mortals in this way,\n\nbecause they observe many things happening on earth and in heaven,\n\nthe causes of whose workings they are unable by any reasoning to see,\n\nand suppose that they come to pass by divine will.\n\nFor which reasons, when we have seen that nothing can be created\n\nfrom nothing, then from that point we shall more rightly discern what follows,\n\nboth whence each thing can be created\n\nand in what way each work comes to pass without the gods.\n\nFor if things came to be from nothing, from all things\n\nevery kind could be born; nothing would need seed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","text":"First men could arise from the sea, from the earth\n\nthe scaly race, and birds burst forth from the sky;\n\nherds and other cattle, every race of wild beasts,\n\nwould occupy cultivated and deserted places by uncertain birth.\n\nNor would the same fruits be wont to remain constant on trees,\n\nbut they would change; all could bear all things.\n\nIndeed, if there were not for each its generative bodies,\n\nhow could a fixed mother-substance subsist for things?\n\nBut now, because all things are created from fixed seeds,\n\nfrom these each is born and comes forth to the borders of light,\n\nwherein there is the matter and the first bodies of each;\n\nand for this reason not all things can be begotten from all things,\n\nbecause in fixed things there is a hidden capacity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0012","text":"Moreover, why do we see the rose in spring, grain in heat,\n\nand vines pour forth at the prompting of autumn,\n\nif not because, when the fixed seeds of things\n\nhave flowed together in their own time, whatever is created is disclosed,\n\nwhile the seasons are present and the quickened earth\n\nsafely bears tender things forth to the borders of light?\n\nBut if they came to be from nothing, they would arise suddenly\n\nat an uncertain time and in alien parts of the year,\n\nsince there would be no first beginnings which, from a generative\n\nunion, could be held back by an unfavorable season.\n\nNor, further, would there be need of an interval for things to grow,\n\nfor seeds to come together, if they could grow from nothing;\n\nfor young men would suddenly become from little infants,\n\nand shrubs, springing from the earth, would leap up all at once.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0013","text":"That none of these things happens is manifest, since all things\n\ngrow gradually, as is fitting, from a fixed seed,\n\nand in growing preserve their kind; so that you may recognize\n\nthat each thing likewise grows great from its own matter.\n\nTo this is added that without the regular rains of the year\n\nthe earth cannot send forth gladdening offspring,\n\nnor, moreover, can the nature of living creatures, set apart by food,\n\npropagate its kind and preserve life;\n\nso that you would rather suppose that bodies are common to many things,\n\nthat there are many such, as we see elements in words,\n\nthan that anything can exist without first-beginnings.\n\nFinally, why was nature unable to produce men so great\n\nthat they could cross the sea on foot through its shallows\n\nand tear apart great mountains with their hands\n\nand, by living, overcome many living ages,\n\nif not because a fixed matter has been assigned to things\n\nto be generated, from which it is established what can arise?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","text":"Therefore it must be confessed that nothing can be made from nothing,\n\nsince seed is needed for things, by which each created thing\n\nmay be brought forth into the tender breezes of air.\n\nLastly, since we see cultivated places excel uncultivated ones\n\nand, through hands, render better fruits,\n\nthere are evidently in the earth first-beginnings of things,\n\nwhich we, turning the fertile clods with the plough\n\nand subduing the soil of earth, summon forth to birth;\n\nbut if there were none, without any labour of ours\n\nyou would see each thing come about of its own accord much better.\n\nTo this is added that everything returns again into its own bodies\n\nnature dissolve and utterly destroy things into nothing.\n\nFor if anything were mortal in all its parts,\n\neach thing snatched suddenly from sight would perish;\n\nthere would be no need of force, indeed, which could from its parts\n\nproduce disruption and unbind its fastenings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0015","text":"But now, because all things consist of eternal seed,\n\nuntil a force comes which shatters things by a blow\n\nor penetrates within through the voids and dissolves them,\n\nnature suffers the destruction of nothing to be seen.\n\nMoreover, whatever time removes through age,\n\nif, consuming all its matter, it utterly destroyed it,\n\nwhence does Venus bring back into the light of life the race of living things\n\nkind by kind, or, once brought back, whence does the ingenious earth\n\nnourish and increase it, providing food kind by kind?\n\nWhence do the native springs and the rivers flowing from afar abroad\n\nsupply the sea? Whence does the ether feed the stars?\n\nFor infinite age and the days gone by ought to have consumed\n\nall things which are of mortal body.\n\nBut if, in that span and in the age gone before, there have existed\n\nthose things from which this sum of things, renewed, consists,\n\ncertainly they are endowed with an immortal nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","text":"Therefore things cannot all return to nothing.\n\nFinally, the same force and cause would commonly destroy all things,\n\nunless eternal matter held them together,\n\nlinked together among themselves more or less entangled;\n\nfor touch would surely be a sufficient cause of death,\n\nseeing that there would be no bodies of eternal substance, whose\n\ntexture some force would have to dissolve.\n\nBut now, because the linkages of the first-beginnings\n\nsubsist dissimilar among themselves and matter is eternal,\n\nthings remain with their body unharmed, until a force sufficiently keen\n\ncomes upon them, found adequate to the texture of each.\n\nTherefore no thing returns to nothing, but all\n\nreturn by dissolution into the bodies of matter.\n\nMoreover, rains perish when father Aether\n\nhas cast them down into the lap of mother Earth;\n\nbut shining crops spring up and the branches grow green\n\nupon the trees; they themselves grow and are laden with fruit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","text":"Hence in turn our race and that of the wild beasts is nourished;\n\nhence we see glad cities flourish with children\n\nand the leafy woods sing everywhere with new birds;\n\nhence weary cattle over the glad rich pastures\n\nlay down their fat bodies, and a gleaming milky fluid\n\nflows from their distended udders; hence new offspring\n\nwith feeble limbs, frolicsome through the tender grass,\n\nplays, its young minds struck by pure milk.\n\nTherefore, whatever things are seen do not perish utterly,\n\nsince nature nourishes from one thing and restores, nor does she any...\n\nallows a thing to be produced only with the aid of another's death.\n\nCome now, since I have taught that things cannot be created\n\nfrom nothing, nor likewise be recalled, once produced, to nothing,\n\nlest perhaps you should nevertheless begin to distrust my words,\n\nbecause the first-beginnings of things cannot be discerned by the eyes,\n\nreceive besides those bodies which you yourself must needs\n\nacknowledge to exist in things and yet cannot be seen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","text":"First, the impetuous force of the wind buffets the body,\n\nrushes upon huge ships and scatters clouds,\n\nmeanwhile, racing over the plains in a swift whirlwind,\n\nit lays low great trees and the loftiest mountains,\n\nit ravages forests with tree-breaking blasts: thus it rages wildly with sharp\n\nroaring, and the sea is fierce with threatening murmur.\n\nTherefore winds are, plainly, unseen bodies,\n\nwhich sweep sea, lands, and finally the clouds of heaven,\n\nand, harrying them, snatch them away in a sudden whirlwind,\n\nnor do they flow and spread destruction by any other method\n\nthan when the soft nature of water is borne onward suddenly\n\nin an overflowing stream, which a great descent of water\n\nfrom the high mountains, swollen by abundant rains,\n\nincreases, hurling down fragments of forests and whole trees;\n\nnor can strong bridges endure the sudden force of the oncoming water:\n\nthus, turbid with mighty rain, the river rushes against\n\nthe solid structures with strong force.\n\nit makes a great crashing sound and rolls destruction beneath the waves\n\nand huge rocks; wherever it rushes, it sweeps away whatever withstands the floods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","text":"Thus, therefore, the blasts of winds too must be borne along,\n\nwhich, just as when a mighty river has rushed down\n\nin whatever direction, drive things before them and rush on\n\nwith repeated assaults, and sometimes, in a twisted whirl,\n\nsnatch them up and carry them away in a swiftly spinning vortex.\n\nWherefore, again and again, winds too are unseen bodies,\n\nsince they are found to rival great\n\nrivers, which are of visible body, in deeds and ways.\n\nFurther, we perceive the various odors of things,\n\nyet we never discern them as they come to the nostrils;\n\nnor are we able to behold hot heat or cold\n\nwith the eyes, nor are we accustomed to discern voices;\n\nyet all these things must necessarily consist of bodily\n\nnature, since they can impel the senses;\n\nfor no thing can touch or be touched, except body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0020","text":"Finally, garments hung upon a wave-breaking shore\n\ngrow wet, and the same garments, spread out in the sun, grow dry.\n\nBut neither in what manner the moisture of water has settled in\n\nhas been seen, nor again in what manner it has fled through heat.\n\nTherefore the moisture is dispersed into small parts,\n\nwhich the eyes can by no means see.\n\nIndeed, even after many returning years of the sun\n\na ring on the finger is worn thin by being worn,\n\nthe fall of dripping water hollows a stone, the hook of the plough,\n\nthe iron ploughshare secretly diminishes in the fields,\n\nand we see the paved stone roads, already worn away\n\nby the feet of the common people; then beside the gates bronze\n\nstatues show their right hands becoming thinner\n\noften through the touch of those who greet them and pass by.\n\nTherefore we see that these things are diminished, since they have been worn away.\n\nBut what bodies depart at any particular time,\n\nenvious nature has shut off from view.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0021","text":"Finally, whatever days and nature\n\ngradually bestow upon things, compelling them to grow in due measure,\n\nno strain of the eyes can keep fixed upon;\n\nnor, further, can it see whatever things grow old through age and wasting,\n\nnor what the rocks that overhang the sea, eaten away by the biting salt,\n\nlikewise lose in time.\n\nNature therefore conducts things by bodies unseen.\n\nYet not all things are held packed tight on every side\n\nby body in nature; for there is void in things.\n\nFor you, to have learned this will be useful in many matters\n\nand will not allow you, wandering, always to doubt and inquire\n\nabout the sum of things and to distrust my words.\n\nFor this reason there is untouched place, empty and void.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","text":"and if this did not exist, things could in no way be moved\n\ncould [move]; for the function that belongs to body,\n\nto hinder and obstruct, would be present at all times\n\nto all things; therefore nothing could proceed,\n\nsince no thing would afford a beginning of yielding.\n\nBut as it is, through seas and lands and the heights of heaven\n\nwe discern many things moving in many ways by varied means\n\nbefore our eyes—things which, if there were no void,\n\nwould not so much lack their restless motion\n\nas would never have been generated in any way at all,\n\nsince matter would have lain packed close on every side.\n\nMoreover, however solid things may be thought to be,\n\nfrom these facts nevertheless you may see that they are of rare body.\n\nIn rocks and caverns the liquid moisture of waters\n\npercolates, and all things weep with copious drops.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0023","text":"Food disperses itself through the whole body of living creatures;\n\nshrubs grow and in due season put forth their fruit,\n\nbecause food, from the deepest roots onward, through their whole extent\n\nis diffused through trunks and through branches.\n\nVoices pass through barriers and the closed walls of houses,\n\nand stiffening cold percolates to the bones.\n\nUnless there were voids through which each body could\n\npass, you would see that this could occur by no means.\n\nFinally, why do we see some things surpass others\n\nin weight, though their form is not at all greater?\n\nfor if there is as much in a ball of wool as\n\nthere is of body in lead, it is fitting that it weigh as much,\n\nsince it is the function of body to press all things downward,\n\nwhereas the nature of void remains without weight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0024","text":"Therefore, what seems equally large yet lighter\n\nplainly declares that there is, no doubt, more void in it;\n\nbut, contrariwise, what is heavier shows that there is more body\n\nin it and that it contains much less empty space within.\n\nTherefore, no doubt, that which we seek by keen reasoning\n\nis mingled with things—the thing we call void.\n\nLest, in these matters, that which certain men imagine lead you away\n\nfrom the truth, I am compelled to anticipate it.\n\nThey say that the glittering liquids yield to scaly creatures\n\nand open up liquid paths, because behind them the fishes\n\nleave places into which the yielding waters may flow together;\n\nthus other things too can move among one another\n\nand change place, although all things are full.\n\nClearly, this whole view is accepted on false reasoning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0025","text":"For where, in the end, will the scaly creatures be able to advance,\n\nunless the liquids yield them space? Moreover, where\n\nwill the waters be able to withdraw, when the fishes cannot go?\n\nTherefore either every body must be deprived of motion,\n\nor it must be said that void is mingled with things,\n\nfrom which each thing first takes the beginning of moving.\n\nFinally, if two broad bodies, by collision,\n\nspring apart swiftly, necessarily all the air must\n\nthat comes to be between the bodies occupy the void.\n\nMoreover, although it flows together with swift currents all around,\n\nyet it will not be able in a single moment wholly\n\nto fill the space; for it must first occupy each place,\n\nand then all be possessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0026","text":"But if perhaps anyone, when bodies have sprung apart,\n\nthen thinks that this happens because the air condenses itself,\n\nhe is mistaken; for then a void comes to be which was not there before,\n\nand likewise a void is filled which existed before,\n\nnor can the air be made denser in such a manner;\n\nnor, even if it now could, could it, I think, without void,\n\ndraw itself into itself and gather its parts into one.\n\nWherefore, although you may linger long in adducing many causes,\n\nstill you must confess that there is void in things.\n\nAnd besides, by recounting them I could\n\nmarshal many arguments to win credence for my words.\n\nBut these slight traces are enough for a keen mind,\n\nthrough which you may be able to discover the rest by yourself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0027","text":"For just as dogs often find the leaf-covered lairs\n\nof mountain-ranging wild beasts by their nostrils,\n\nonce they have set upon the sure tracks of the path,\n\nso you yourself, by yourself, may see one thing from another\n\nin such matters you will be able to make your way into all the blind hiding-places\n\nand thence bring forth the truth.\n\nBut if you are sluggish or withdraw even a little from the matter,\n\nthis I can promise you plainly, Memmius:\n\nso copious draughts from great fountains\n\nwill my sweet tongue pour forth from my rich breast,\n\nthat I fear lest slow old age first creep through my limbs\n\nand loose in us the fastenings of life,\n\nbefore, concerning any one thing, all the abundance\n\nof arguments has been sent through your ears in verse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","text":"But now, that I may return to continue in words what I began,\n\ntherefore all nature, as it is in itself, consists of two things;\n\nfor there are bodies and void,\n\nin which these are situated and through which they move in different directions.\n\nFor common sensation declares that body exists in itself;\n\nunless primary trust in this prevails as a firmly grounded principle,\n\nthere will be no point of reference by which, concerning hidden things,\n\nwe can establish anything by reasoning of the mind.\n\nThen again, if place and space, which we call void,\n\ndid not exist, bodies could not be situated anywhere\n\nnor at all move in different directions anywhere;\n\nas we showed you a little earlier above.\n\nMoreover, there is nothing that you can say is separate from every\n\nbody and set apart from void,\n\nbecause a nature, as it were third in number, has been discovered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","text":"For whatever shall be, must itself be something,\n\nwhether of great or small, indeed, bulk, provided that it is;\n\nif it has touch, however light and slight,\n\nit will increase the number of body, and follow its sum;\n\nbut if it is intangible, so that in no respect it can\n\nprevent anything from passing through it of its own accord,\n\nthen clearly this will be that which we call empty void.\n\nMoreover, whatever exists by itself will either do something\n\nor itself must be acted upon by other things acting,\n\nor it will be such that things can be and be done in it.\n\nBut no thing can act or be acted upon without body,\n\nnor, further, provide a place except empty void.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","text":"Therefore, besides void and bodies, no third nature in itself\n\ncan be left among the number of things,\n\neither one that may at any time fall beneath our senses\n\nor one which anyone can attain by reason of mind.\n\nFor whatever things are spoken of as existing, you will find either joined to these two\n\nthings, or you will see them as events of these.\n\nA conjunction is that which nowhere can be separated and sundered\n\nwithout destructive dissolution,\n\nas weight to stones, heat to fire, fluidity to water,\n\ntouch to all bodies, intangibility to void.\n\nBut slavery, poverty, and riches,\n\nliberty, war, concord, and all the rest whose\n\nnature remains unharmed by their coming and departure,\n\nwe are accustomed, as is fitting, to call events.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","text":"Likewise, time does not exist through itself, but from things themselves\n\nthere follows a sense of what has been done in the past,\n\nthen of what is present, and what subsequently follows;\n\nnor must one admit that anyone feels time through itself,\n\nseparated from the motion of things and from tranquil rest.\n\nFinally, when they say that the Tyndarid was carried off and that the Trojan-born\n\nrace was subdued in war, one must take care\n\nlest perhaps they compel us to confess that these exist through themselves,\n\nsince the ages of men to whom these events belonged\n\nhave already been carried away by irrevocable bygone time;\n\nfor one thing may be called an event in relation to the lands, another in relation to the regions themselves,\n\nwhatever shall have been done.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032","text":"Finally, if there had been no matter of things\n\nnor place and space in which each thing is done,\n\nnever would the fire, kindled by love of the Tyndarid's beauty,\n\nwaxing beneath the Phrygian breast of Alexander,\n\nhave kindled the famous contests of cruel war,\n\nnor would the hidden, wooden Horse have set Pergama aflame for the Trojans by its birth,\n\nwith the nightly Greeks' offspring; \n\nso that you may perceive that all deeds done, fundamentally,\n\ndo not subsist or exist through themselves as a body does.\n\nnor to be designated by the same reasoning by which void is constituted,\n\nbut rather so that you may rightly be able to call them accidents\n\nof body and of place, in which each thing is done.\n\nBodies, moreover, are in part the first-beginnings of things,\n\nin part those which consist in the union of first principles.\n\nBut as for those which are the first-beginnings of things, no force can\n\nextinguish them; for in the end they prevail by their solid body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0033","text":"Although it seems difficult to believe that anything\n\ncan be found in things with a solid body.\n\nFor the lightning of heaven passes through the barriers of houses,\n\nas do shout and voices; iron grows white-hot in fire,\n\nand stones burst apart under fierce, burning vapour;\n\nwhen the hardened stiffness of gold, weakened, is dissolved by heat,\n\nthen the ice of bronze, conquered by flame, melts;\n\nheat and penetrating cold pass through silver,\n\nsince, holding cups in our hands as is proper,\n\nwe have felt both, when moisture from waters was poured in from above.\n\nTo such a degree does there seem to be nothing solid in things.\n\nBut since true reasoning and the nature of things nevertheless\n\ncompel it, attend, while in a few verses I set forth\n\nthat there are things which consist of solid and eternal body,\n\nwhich we teach to be the seeds and first-beginnings of things,\n\nfrom which the whole sum of things, now created, consists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0034","text":"First, since the nature of the twofold [kind] of the two...\n\nIt has been found that things—the body and the place in which each thing is done—consist far apart, dissimilar in kind;\n\nthe body and the place in which each thing is done,\n\nit is necessary that each be pure and exist by itself.\n\nFor wherever there is space empty, which we call void,\n\nthere is no body there; and, further, wherever a body maintains itself,\n\nthere the void empty is by no means found to be.\n\nTherefore the first bodies are solid and without void.\n\nMoreover, since there is void in things that have come into being,\n\nit is necessary that solid matter stand around it;\n\nnor can any thing be proved by true reasoning\n\nto conceal void in its body and hold it within,\n\nunless you grant that what contains it consists of solid.\n\nFurther, this can be nothing but a union\n\nof matter, which can contain the void of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0035","text":"Matter, therefore, which consists of solid body,\n\ncan be eternal, though other things are dissolved.\n\nThen again, if there were nothing which might be called void,\n\nall would be solid; unless, conversely, certain bodies\n\nexisted which filled whatever places they occupied,\n\nall space that exists would prove to be void and empty.\n\nTherefore, without doubt, body is distinguished from void in alternation,\n\nsince neither the full exists entirely\n\nnor, further, the void; therefore there are certain bodies\n\nwhich can distinguish void space by fullness.\n\nthese cannot be dissolved by blows struck from without,\n\nnor thereafter, penetrated through and through, be unwoven,\n\nnor can they, assailed by any other means, be made to fail;\n\na fact which I showed you a little earlier above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","text":"For nothing, it seems, can be crushed without void,\n\nnor broken nor split by cutting into two,\n\nnor admit moisture, nor likewise penetrating cold,\n\nnor inward-reaching fire, by which all things are brought to an end.\n\nAnd the more each thing contains void shut up within,\n\nthe more deeply, when assailed by these agencies, it gives way.\n\nTherefore, if first bodies are solid and without void\n\nas I have taught, they must of necessity be eternal.\n\nMoreover, unless matter had been eternal,\n\nlong ago every thing would have utterly returned to nothing,\n\nand whatever things we see would have been born again from nothing.\n\nBut since I have taught above that nothing can be created\n\nfrom nothing, nor what has been begotten be recalled to nothing,\n\nthe first beginnings must be of immortal body,\n\nso that, at the final time, each thing may be able to be dissolved,\n\nin order that matter may furnish a supply for things to be renewed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0037","text":"The first beginnings, therefore, are solid in their simplicity,\n\nnor can they by any other means, preserved through the age,\n\nrestore things from time infinite to the present.\n\nFinally, if nature had appointed no limit\n\nin the breaking of things, the bodies of matter had already\n\nbeen reduced away by the earlier time that breaks,\n\nso that nothing from them could, from a fixed time,\n\nreach through to the conceived utmost end of its age.\n\nFor we see that anything whatsoever can be dissolved more quickly\n\nthan remade; wherefore, through the long\n\ninfinite age of all time previously elapsed,\n\nwhatever had thus far broken, scattering and dissolving,\n\ncould never be repaired in the remaining time.\n\nBut as it is, evidently, a fixed limit of breaking has been assigned and remains,\n\nsince we see each thing remade,\n\nand, at the same time, periods limited according to kinds are set\n\nfor things, by which they may attain the flower of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0038","text":"To this is added that, although the bodies of matter are\n\nmost solid, nevertheless all things can be rendered,\n\nwhich become soft—air, water, earth, vapours—\n\nin what manner they come to be and by what force each is produced,\n\nbecause void has once been intermixed among things.\n\nBut conversely, if the first-beginnings of things are soft,\n\nwhence may hard flints and iron be created?\n\nNo account will be able to be given; for from the very foundation every\n\nnature will lack a principle of foundation.\n\nTherefore there are solid bodies, powerful in their simplicity,\n\nby whose denser union all things\n\ncan be compressed and display vigorous powers.\n\nFurthermore, if no limit has been assigned to the breaking\n\nof bodies, nevertheless from eternal time whatever\n\nbodies must even now survive from things,\n\nwhich have not yet become known as tried by any peril.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","text":"But since they are constituted endowed with a frail nature,\n\nit is inconsistent that they could have remained through eternal time,\n\nbuffeted by innumerable blows throughout the age.\n\nFinally, since it is now established that a limit has been assigned by kinds\n\nto things in growing and in holding fast to life,\n\nand what each can do according to the covenants of nature,\n\nand furthermore what they cannot do, since indeed it stands decreed,\n\nnor is anything changed, so that all things remain\n\nto such an extent that all the various birds in their order\n\ndisplay the marks of their kind as being in their body,\n\nthey must also have a body of unchangeable matter,\n\nunless, of course; for if the first beginnings of things\n\ncould be changed, having been overcome in some manner,\n\nit would likewise be uncertain what could arise,\n\nwhat could not, and finally what limited power belongs to each,\n\nby what principle, and what deeply fixed boundary there is;\n\nnor could generations so often reproduce by kind\n\nthe nature, habits, manner of life, and movements of their parents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0040","text":"Then further, since every extremity is the utmost summit\n\nof that body, which our senses discern\n\nare now no longer able to discern, it exists, not surprisingly, without parts\n\nand consists of a least nature, nor has it ever been\n\nseparate by itself, nor will it be able to be so hereafter,\n\nsince it is itself a part, and the first and single part, of another thing,\n\nthen other and other similar parts in succession\n\nin a close-packed host fill out the nature of the body;\n\nand since these cannot subsist by themselves, they must\n\ncling to that from which they can by no means be torn away.\n\nTherefore there are solid first-beginnings in their simplicity,\n\nwhich, closely packed with least parts, cohere tightly.\n\nnot held together by the union of those parts,\n\nbut rather powerful through everlasting simplicity,\n\nfrom which nature permits nothing now to be torn away or diminished,\n\npreserving them as seeds for things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","text":"Moreover, unless there be a least thing, every smallest\n\nbody will consist of infinite parts,\n\nsince indeed a part of a half will always have\n\na half-part, nor will any thing set a limit to this.\n\nTherefore, between the sum of things and the least thing that exists,\n\nthere will be no difference; for however infinite in its entirety the whole\n\nmay be, nevertheless the things that are smallest\n\nwill likewise consist of infinite parts.\n\nSince true reason protests and denies this\n\nthat the mind can exist, you must confess, once overcome,\n\nthat there are things which now exist endowed with no parts,\n\nand consist of a minimal nature. Since these things exist,\n\nyou must also confess that they are solid and eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0042","text":"Finally, if creative nature had been accustomed to compel all things to be dissolved\n\ninto their smallest parts,\n\nshe would now be unable to restore anything from them again,\n\nbecause things that are augmented by no parts\n\ncannot possess those things which generative\n\nmatter ought to possess: varied connections, weights, impacts,\n\ncollisions, motions, through which each thing is brought about.\n\nWherefore those who have supposed that the matter of things is\n\nfire, and that the universe consists of fire alone,\n\nappear to have fallen far from true reasoning.\n\nHeraclitus, first leader in this battle-line,\n\nfamous for his obscure language rather among the empty-headed\n\nthan weighty among the Greeks, who seek the truth;\n\nfor fools especially admire and love all things\n\nwhich they discern lurking beneath twisted words,\n\nand regard as true those things which can pleasantly strike\n\nthe ears and are painted over with charming sound.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0043","text":"For why, I ask, could things be so varied,\n\nif they were created from one pure fire?\n\nFor it would avail nothing for hot fire to become denser\n\nnor would it rarely happen, if the parts of fire had the same\n\nnature which the whole fire has above.\n\nFor the heat would be more keen when the parts were brought together,\n\nbut more languid when they were scattered and dispersed.\n\nMoreover, there is nothing that you could suppose to be possible\n\nin causes of such a kind, much less that the variation of things\n\ncould be so great from dense and rarefied fires.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0044","text":"This too: if they make void mixed in with things,\n\nthe fires will be able to become dense and to be left rarefied;\n\nbut because they discern many things that are contrary to them\n\nand shrink from leaving pure void in things,\n\nwhile they fear difficulties, they lose the true path\n\nand, again, do not discern that, with void removed from things,\n\nall things become dense and from all things one\n\nbody is made, having nothing of itself that it can swiftly send forth,\n\nas heat-bearing fire casts forth light and vapour,\n\nso that you may see that it is not from crowded parts.\n\nBut if perhaps they believe that in some other way it can be done\n\nthat fires in a mass are extinguished and change their substance,\n\nclearly, if they refrain from making this out of any part,\n\nall their heat will utterly perish into nothing, without doubt,\n\nand whatever things are created will come to be from nothing;\n\nfor whatever, changed, passes beyond its own boundaries,\n\nimmediately this is the death of that which was before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","text":"therefore something must remain over, unharmed, for them,\n\nlest all things return for you utterly to nothing\n\nand, reborn from nothing, the abundance of things grow strong.\n\nNow therefore, since there are certain most steadfast bodies\n\nwhich always preserve the same nature,\n\nby whose departure or arrival and altered order things change\n\ntheir nature and bodies transform themselves,\n\none may know that these are not fiery bodies of things.\n\nFor it would matter nothing that certain ones should withdraw, depart,\n\nand that others be added, and certain ones changed in order,\n\nif nevertheless all retained the nature of heat;\n\nfor whatever they created would in every way be fire.\n\nBut, as I think, it is thus: there are certain bodies whose\n\nmeetings, movements, order, position, and shapes\n\nproduce fires, and, when their order is changed, change\n\ntheir nature; nor are they fashioned like fire, nor like any other\n\nthing besides which can send bodies\n\nto the senses and, by their impact, touch our touch.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0046","text":"Furthermore, to say that fire is all things, and that no\n\ntrue thing in the number of things exists except fire,\n\nas this same man does, seems to be sheer madness.\n\nFor he himself contends against the senses by means of the senses\n\nand undermines them, on which all things believed depend,\n\nby which fire itself, which he names, has become known to him;\n\nfor he believes that the senses truly recognize fire;\n\nhe does not believe the other things, which are no less clear.\n\nwhich seems to me both vain and delirious;\n\nfor to what shall we refer? What can be more certain for us than the senses themselves,\n\nby which we distinguish true things and false?\n\nMoreover, why should anyone rather abolish all things\n\nand wish to leave the nature of heat alone,\n\nthan deny that fires exist, yet leave another nature to exist?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047","text":"For it seems an equal madness to say either.\n\nWherefore those who have thought that fire is the matter of things\n\nand that the whole can consist from fire,\n\nand those who have established air as the first principle for generating things,\n\nor whoever have thought that moisture\n\nfashions things by itself, or that earth creates them,\n\nall things, and is changed into every nature of things,\n\nseem to have wandered far, vastly far, from the truth.\n\nAdd also those who double the first beginnings of things,\n\njoining air with fire and earth with liquid,\n\nand those who suppose that all things can grow\n\nfrom four things—from fire, earth, and breath and rain.\n\nForemost among these is Empedocles of Acragas,\n\nwhom the island bore upon the three-cornered coasts of its lands,\n\naround which the flowing sea, in great windings,\n\nthe Ionian, sprinkles its brine from its grey-green waves.\n\nand with a narrow strait divides with its waves the rushing sea\n\nthe shores of Aeolian land from the boundaries of this land.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0048","text":"here is vast Charybdis, and here the Aetnean roarings\n\nof flames threaten anew to gather their wrath,\n\nso that the force may again vomit forth from its jaws the fires burst out,\n\nand again bear to heaven the flashes of flame.\n\nAlthough this region seems in many ways greatly wondrous\n\nto human nations and is said to be worth seeing,\n\nrich in good things, fortified by the great strength of many men,\n\nyet it seems to have possessed in itself nothing more illustrious than this man,\n\nnor anything more sacred and wondrous and dear.\n\nIndeed, the poems of his divine breast\n\ncry aloud and set forth his splendid discoveries,\n\nso that he scarcely seems to have been created of human stock.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0049","text":"Yet he, and those whom we have said above to be inferior,\n\noutstandingly inferior in many respects and much lesser,\n\nalthough discovering many things well and divinely,\n\ngave forth responses from the shrine, as it were, of the heart,\n\nmore sacredly and with a far more certain reason than\n\nthe Pythia who speaks from the tripod and the laurel of Phoebus,\n\nnevertheless brought ruin upon the first principles of things\n\nand, great men, fell there by a great fall.\n\nFirst, because they establish motion when void has been removed from things\n\nand leave things soft and rarefied\n\nbronze, sun, fire, earths, living creatures, crops\n\nand yet they do not admit void into their body;\n\nthen, because they will make absolutely no end to the cutting\n\nof bodies, nor let there be any halt to the breaking,\n\nnor, moreover, allow anything least to stand in things,\n\nsince we see that the ultimate point of each thing\n\nis what seems least to our senses,\n\nso that from this you may infer that the things which you cannot discern\n\nhave as their ultimate point a least thing, standing firm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050","text":"To this is added likewise that, since they posit the first beginnings of things\n\nas soft, which we see to be by nature\n\nand endowed with a mortal body, it follows utterly that\n\nthe sum of things ought now to return to nothing,\n\nand that, reborn from nothing, an abundance of things should flourish;\n\nhow far each of these is from the truth you will now understand.\n\nThen, in many ways they are hostile and poisonous\n\nto one another themselves; wherefore, either, having met, they will perish,\n\nor they will flee apart as, when compelled by a storm,\n\nwe see lightning, rains, and winds flee apart.\n\nFinally, if all things are created from four things\n\nand all things are in turn dissolved back into those things,\n\nwhy can those more rightly be called the first beginnings\n\nof things than, conversely, things be thought theirs in return?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0051","text":"For they are generated in alternation and change their colour\n\nand all nature among themselves from all time.\n\nbut if perhaps you think that fire and earth unite\n\nin body, and the airy breezes and the moisture of liquid,\n\nso that nothing in their assemblage changes its nature,\n\nno thing will be able to be created for you out of them,\n\nneither animate nor with an inanimate body, as a tree;\n\nfor each, in a shifting mass, will display its own\n\nnature, and air, mixed, will be seen to remain\n\ntogether with earth and with some moisture.\n\nBut in generating things the first-beginnings must\n\nemploy a hidden and blind nature,\n\nlest anything stand out that might fight against and hinder\n\nwhatever is created from being able to exist in its own proper way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0052","text":"Indeed, they also trace things back from the sky and its fires,\n\nand first they make fire turn into the breezes\n\nof air; thence rain be generated and earth be created\n\nfrom rain, and, back again, all things return from earth,\n\nfirst moisture, then air, then heat,\n\nnor do these cease to change among themselves, to pass\n\nfrom sky to earth, from earth to the stars of the world.\n\nThe first-beginnings ought by no means to do this.\n\nFor something immutable must remain over,\n\nlest all things be reduced utterly to nothing;\n\nfor whatever, changed, passes beyond its own boundaries,\n\nat once this is the death of that which was before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0053","text":"Wherefore, since the things which we said a little before\n\ncome into change, it must needs be that they consist\n\nof others which cannot be changed in any way,\n\nlest all things wholly return to nothing for you;\n\nnay rather, posit certain bodies endowed with such a nature\n\nthat, if perchance they have created fire,\n\nthe same bodies, with a few taken away and a few added,\n\ntheir order and motion changed, can make the breezes of air,\n\nand thus all things be changed into other things from others.\n\n“But,” you say, “plainly manifest fact shows that all things grow into the breezes\n\nof air from the earth; and unless the weather grants\n\nrain at a favorable season,\n\nso that the trees quiver beneath the soaking of the clouds,\n\nand the sun, for its part, cherishes them and bestows heat,\n\ncrops, trees, and living creatures cannot grow.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0054","text":"And indeed, unless dry food and tender moisture\n\naid us, once the body has been lost, all life likewise\n\nwould be released from all the sinews and bones;\n\nfor without doubt we are aided and nourished\n\nby certain things, and different things by certain other things.\n\nNo wonder, since many first-beginnings, common in many ways,\n\nof many things, are mingled in things;\n\ntherefore different things are nourished by different things in various ways.\n\nand likewise it often greatly matters what first-beginnings\n\nare held together with which others and in what arrangement,\n\nand what motions they impart to and receive from one another;\n\nfor the same ones constitute heaven, sea, lands, rivers, and sun,\n\nthe same ones crops, shrubs, and living creatures,\n\nbut, mixed with others and in another manner, they are moved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0055","text":"Indeed, even everywhere in our own verses themselves\n\nyou see many elements common to many words,\n\nalthough you must nevertheless admit that verses and words among themselves\n\ndiffer both in meaning and in their sounding sound.\n\nSo much can elements accomplish by a change of order alone;\n\nbut the first-beginnings of things that are can employ more means,\n\nwhereby each of the various things can be created.\n\nNow let us also examine Anaxagoras' homoeomeria,\n\nwhich the Greeks call thus, nor does the poverty of our native speech\n\nallow us to express it in our language,\n\nyet it is easy to set forth the thing itself in words.\n\nFirst, by the homoeomeria of things, as he calls it,\n\nhe means, plainly, that bones [are formed] from tiny and minute\n\nbones, and that flesh is produced from tiny and minute\n\npieces of flesh, and blood comes into being\n\nfrom many drops of blood coming together among themselves;\n\nand he thinks that gold can consist of particles of gold,\n\nand earth grow together from small particles of earth,\n\nfires from fires, moisture from moistures,\n\nhe fashions and supposes the rest by a similar reasoning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","text":"Yet he grants that there is no void in things in any part,\n\nnor that there is an end to bodies to be divided.\n\nWherefore, in each respect alike, it seems to me\n\nthat he too is mistaken, along with those whom we mentioned above before.\n\nAdd that he imagines first-beginnings far too feeble;\n\nif the first-beginnings are constituted as endowed with a similar\n\nnature, and are like the things themselves and likewise suffer\n\nand perish, and nothing restrains them from destruction.\n\nFor what of these will endure under a mighty crushing,\n\nso as to escape death, beneath the very teeth of destruction?\n\nFire, or moisture, or air? Which of these? Blood or bones?\n\nNone, as I think, when every thing alike utterly\n\nwill be as mortal as those things which we plainly see\n\nperishing before our eyes, overcome by some force.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0057","text":"But that things can neither fall back into nothing nor indeed\n\ngrow from nothing, I maintain has previously been proved.\n\nMoreover, since food increases and nourishes the body,\n\nwe may know that veins and blood and bones\n\n* * *\n\nor they will say that all foods, with their substance mingled,\n\nexist and contain within themselves small bodies of sinews,\n\nand bones, and in general veins and portions of blood,\n\nit will come about that all food, both dry and liquid itself,\n\nis thought to consist of things of another kind,\n\nof bones and sinews and mixed pus and blood.\n\nMoreover, whatever bodies grow from the earth,\n\nif they are in the earth, the earth must consist\n\nof things of another kind, which arise from the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0058","text":"Transfer the argument likewise: you may use the same words:\n\nif flame and smoke and ash lie hidden in woods,\n\nthe woods must consist of things of another kind,\n\nof things of another kind, which arise from woods.\n\nHere there remains a certain slender opportunity for hiding,\n\nwhich Anaxagoras takes for himself: that he thinks all things\n\nlie hidden, mingled with all things, but that that one thing\n\nappears of which the greatest number are mixed\n\nand are more at hand and placed in the foremost front.\n\nYet this is far removed from true reasoning;\n\nfor it would then be fitting that crops too, often, when shattered by the threatening\n\nstrength of a rock, should give forth a sign\n\nof blood or something nourished in our body,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0059","text":"when we grind stone against stone, that gore should flow;\n\nby like reasoning it was fitting that grasses too should often\n\nsend forth sweet liquids, drops of a similar taste,\n\nsuch as milk is from the udder of the wool-bearing beast,\n\nand indeed that often from crumbled clods of earth\n\nthat kinds of herbs, and crops and leaves, are seen\n\nto lie hidden, minutely distributed throughout the earth;\n\nfinally, that ash and smoke are seen in logs,\n\nwhen they have been broken apart, and tiny fires to lie hidden.\n\nSince manifest reality teaches that none of these things happens,\n\nyou may know that things are not so mixed in things,\n\nbut that seeds, mixed in many ways, lie hidden\n\ncommon to many things in things.\n\n“But it often happens on great mountains,” you say, “that the lofty\n\ntopmost crowns of neighboring trees are rubbed\n\nagainst one another, strong south winds compelling them to do this,\n\nuntil they have flashed forth, the blossom of flame having arisen.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0060","text":"To be sure, fire is nevertheless not implanted in the logs;\n\nrather, there are many seeds of heat which, through rubbing,\n\nwhen they have flowed together, create conflagrations in the woods.\n\nBut if flame had been hidden, already formed, in the woods,\n\nthe fires could not remain concealed for any length of time;\n\nthey would generally consume the woods, burn up the trees.\n\nDo you now see, therefore, what we said a little before,\n\nthat it matters exceedingly much that the same first-beginnings\n\nare held together with which others and in what arrangement,\n\nand what motions they give and receive among themselves,\n\nand that the same ones, slightly altered among themselves, create\n\nfires and wood? In what way also the words themselves\n\nthe elements having been slightly altered among themselves,\n\nalthough we designate woods and fires by distinct names.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0061","text":"Finally, whatever things you now discern in plain view,\n\nif you think that they cannot come to pass unless you imagine\n\nthat the bodies of matter are endowed with a like nature,\n\nby this reasoning the first-beginnings of things are lost to you:\n\nit will come about that, shaken by trembling laughter, they guffaw\n\nand moisten face and cheeks with salty tears.\n\nCome now, learn what remains, and hear more clearly.\n\nNor does it escape my mind how obscure these things are; but a great hope\n\nof praise has struck my heart with its sharp thyrsus\n\nand at the same time has driven into my breast a sweet love\n\nof the Muses, by which now, inspired and with vigorous mind,\n\nI range through the pathless places of the Pierides, trodden before\n\nby no one's foot.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062","text":"It delights me to approach untouched springs,\n\nand it delights me to draw from them and to pluck new flowers,\n\nand thence to seek a distinguished crown for my head,\n\nfrom where the Muses have veiled no one's temples before;\n\nfirst because I teach concerning great things and proceed to free the mind\n\nfrom the tight knots of religions,\n\nthen because, concerning so obscure a matter, I compose such lucid\n\nverses, touching everything with the Muses' charm.\n\nFor this too seems not without reason;\n\nbut just as physicians, when they endeavor to give foul wormwood to children,\n\nfirst touch the rims of the cup all around\n\nwith the sweet, golden liquid of honey,\n\nso that the unguarded age of children may be deceived\n\nto the very lips, while meanwhile it drinks deeply the bitter\n\nfluid of wormwood, and, deceived, is not harmed,\n\nbut rather, restored by such means, grows strong,\n\nso I now, since this doctrine for the most part seems\n\ntoo grim to those by whom it has not been handled, and the common crowd\n\nshrinks back from it, have wished to set forth to you our doctrine in sweet-speaking\n\nPierian song and, as it were, to touch it with the sweet honey\n\nof the Muses,\n\nif perhaps by such a method I might be able to hold your mind\n\nin our verses, while you discern the whole\n\nnature of things, with what formed shape it is endowed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","text":"But since I have taught that the most solid bodies of matter\n\nfly about forever, unconquered, through time,\n\ncome now, let us unfold whether there is any limit to their totality\n\nor not; likewise, whether that void which has been found,\n\nor place and space in which each thing is done,\n\nis seen to be wholly finite at its foundation\n\nor to lie open immeasurable and profoundly vast.\n\nTherefore all that exists is limited in no direction of its paths;\n\nfor it would have to possess an extremity.\n\nMoreover, an extremity seems unable to exist for anything.\n\nto be, unless there be something beyond that sets a limit, so that it may be seen\n\nbeyond which this nature of sensation does not pursue farther.\n\nNow, since it must be admitted that there is nothing outside the whole,\n\nit has no extremity; therefore it lacks limit and measure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0064","text":"Nor does it matter in what regions of it you stand;\n\nso completely does whatever place each person has occupied leave in every\n\ndirection the whole infinite equally great.\n\nMoreover, if all space that exists\n\nbe now supposed finite, if someone should run forth to its\n\nfurthest borders and, standing last, cast a flying missile,\n\ndo you prefer that it, hurled with mighty force, should go\n\nwhere it has been sent and fly far on,\n\nor do you think that something can prevent and obstruct it?\n\nFor you must confess and adopt one or the other.\n\nEither of these alternatives bars every escape for you and\n\ncompels you to grant that it extends, removed from any limit.\n\nFor whether there is something that checks and brings it about\n\nthat it does not reach where it was sent and set itself at the boundary,\n\nor it is borne outside, it did not set out from the boundary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0065","text":"In this way I shall pursue the argument, and wherever you place\n\nthe outermost borders, I shall ask: what then will become of the missile?\n\nIt will come about that a limit can be established nowhere,\n\nand the opportunity for flight will always extend the escape.\n\nMoreover, all the space of the whole sum\n\nif, enclosed on every side, it stood within fixed boundaries\n\nand were finite, by now the abundance of matter\n\nwould have flowed together from every side by its solid weights to the bottom,\n\nnor could any thing be done beneath the canopy of heaven,\n\nnor would there be heaven at all, nor the lights of the sun,\n\nsince all matter, heaped together, would lie\n\nsettling down from infinite time already.\n\nBut now, evidently, no rest has been granted to the bodies\n\nof the first-beginnings, because there is no lowest bottom at all,\n\nwhither they might, as it were, flow together and where they might establish their seats.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0066","text":"All things are ever carried on in unceasing motion\n\nin every quarter, and bodies of matter are supplied from below,\n\nswiftly, from the infinite.\n\nFinally, before our eyes one thing seems to bound another:\n\nair separates hills, and mountains the air,\n\nland bounds sea, and conversely sea bounds all lands;\n\nyet in truth there is nothing outside that sets a limit to the whole.\n\nTherefore the nature of place and the expanse of the deep is such\n\nthat not even bright thunderbolts, running their course,\n\nas they glide on through the everlasting stretch of time,\n\ncould traverse it, or bring it about that less remained to be gone over in their passage;\n\nso vastly does an immense abundance lie open everywhere for things,\n\nwith boundaries removed in every direction on all sides.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0067","text":"Moreover, the sum of things itself to set a limit for itself\n\ncannot be, nature holds, which [is] body [and] void;\n\nand that which is void compels [it] to be bounded by body,\n\nso that in this way it renders all things infinite in alternation,\n\nor else one or the other, unless the other of them bounds it,\n\nthough of simple nature, may yet lie open unbounded,\n\nnor sea nor earth nor the bright precincts of heaven\n\nnor the race of mortals nor the sacred bodies of the gods\n\ncould endure for a scant moment of time;\n\nfor, dispersed from its assemblage, the abundance of matter\n\nwould be borne, loosened, through the great void,\n\nor rather indeed would never have created any concrete thing,\n\nsince, scattered, it would not have been able to be gathered together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","text":"For certainly not by design did the first-beginnings of things\n\neach settle themselves in their place by keen intelligence and according to their own order,\n\nnor indeed did they covenant what motions each should give;\n\nbut because, changed in many ways through all time by many means,\n\nthey are driven and agitated by blows from the infinite,\n\ntrying every kind of motion and combination,\n\nat last they arrive at such arrangements,\n\nas those by which this sum of things, having been created, endures,\n\nand, preserved also through many great years,\n\nonce it has been cast into harmonious motions,\n\nbrings it about that rivers replenish the hungry sea with their abundant waves of water,\n\nand that the earth, by the sun's heat—\n\ncherished, may renew its offspring, and may the race of living things,\n\nflourish, and the fires of the gliding ether may live.\n\nwhich they could do in no way, unless a supply of matter\n\ncould arise from the infinite,\n\nwhence all things are wont in due time to repair what has been lost.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0069","text":"For just as the nature of living creatures, deprived of food,\n\nwastes away, losing its body, so all things must\n\nbe dissolved, as soon as matter has failed to furnish\n\nitself, having been turned aside by some means from its course.\n\nNor can blows from without on every side preserve the whole\n\nentirety, whatever has been brought together.\n\nFor they can often strike and delay a part,\n\nuntil others arrive and the whole can be made full;\n\nyet meanwhile they are compelled to rebound, and at the same time\n\nto grant to the first-beginnings of things space and time for flight,\n\nso that they may be borne free from the assemblage.\n\nWherefore again and again many things must arise,\n\nand yet, in order that the blows themselves also may be able to be supplied,\n\nan infinite force of matter is needed on every side.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","text":"In these matters, Memmius, flee far from believing that\n\nwhich they say: that all things lean toward the middle of the whole,\n\nand that therefore the nature of the world stands without any\n\nexternal blows and cannot anywhere be dissolved,\n\nat top or bottom, because all things have leaned toward the middle.\n\nif you believe that it itself can stand firm in itself at all,\n\nand that all weights which are beneath the earth strive\n\nupward, and rest, turned back, upon the earth,\n\nas through waters we now see images of things;\n\nand that by like reasoning animals wander upside down\n\nand cannot fall away from the earth into the regions of heaven\n\ndownward any more than our bodies\n\ncan of their own accord fly into the temples of heaven;\n\nthat, when they see the sun, we behold the stars of night,\n\nand that they divide with us in alternation the seasons of heaven\n\nand pass nights equal to our days.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071","text":"but vain [error] has * * * for fools\n\nhaving embraced what they have, through * * *\n\nfor there can be no middle * * *\n\ninfinity; nor indeed, even if there should now be a middle,\n\ncould anything stand there * * *\n\nthan by any other far-off reasoning * * *\n\nfor every place and space, which we call void,\n\nwhether through the middle or not through the middle, must yield\n\nequally to weights, in whatever direction they are borne by motion.\n\nNor is there any place where bodies, when they have arrived,\n\ncan stand in the void, having lost the force of weight;\n\nnor, moreover, ought that which is void to support anything,\n\nbut, as its own nature demands, it must continue to yield.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0072","text":"Therefore things cannot be held by such a principle\n\nconquered by a desire for the middle into a council.\n\nMoreover, since they do not suppose that all bodies\n\nstrive toward the middle, but [only] those of lands and of liquid\n\nthe moisture of the sea and the great waves from the mountains,\n\nand the things which, as it were, are held together in an earthly body;\n\nbut, on the contrary, they set forth that the thin breezes of air\n\nand likewise hot fires are borne away from the middle,\n\nand therefore that the whole ether trembles round about with constellations\n\nand that the flame of the sun is fed through the blue regions of heaven,\n\nbecause all heat, fleeing from the middle, gathers itself there,\n\nnor at all can the topmost branches grow leafy on trees\n\nunless nourishment comes gradually to each from the earth\n\n[Gap in source]\n\n[Gap in source]\n\n[Gap in source]\n\n[Gap in source]\n\n[Gap in source]","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0073","text":"[Gap in source]\n\n[Gap in source]\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nlest, in the swift manner of flames, the walls of the world\n\nsuddenly fly apart, released through the great void,\n\nand lest the rest follow by a similar principle\n\nlest the thunderous temples of heaven crash down from above,\n\nand the earth swiftly withdraw itself from beneath our feet, and all things,\n\namid the mingled ruins of things and heaven,\n\ndissolving their bodies, depart into the deep void,\n\nso that in an instant of time no remnant may remain\n\nsave deserted space and blind first-beginnings.\n\nFor from whatever part you first determine bodies to be absent,\n\nthis will be for things the gate of death,\n\nby this way the whole throng of matter will give itself forth.\n\nThus you will discern these things, led on by a slight effort;\n\nfor one thing after another will grow clear, nor will blind\n\nnight snatch away your path, but you will see through to the utmost things of nature:\n\nso will things kindle lights for things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0073"]}],"source_digest":"63f43b271c9074233f66a6c89c7a0e83cad05c13ee750e03d479f7a9a968ca94","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Liber Primus"}],"passage_basis":"revised-passages","original_title":"Liber Primus","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS","title":"Book Two","order":2,"chapter_id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS","text":"## Liber Secundus\n\n  \n\nSuave, mari magno turbantibus aequora ventis\n\n \n\ne terra magnum alterius spectare laborem;\n\n \n\nnon quia vexari quemquamst iucunda voluptas,\n\n \n\nsed quibus ipse malis careas quia cernere suavest.\n\n \n\nsuave etiam belli certamina magna tueri\n\n \n\nper campos instructa tua sine parte pericli;\n\n \n\nsed nihil dulcius est, bene quam munita tenere\n\n \n\nedita doctrina sapientum templa serena,\n\n \n\ndespicere unde queas alios passimque videre\n\n \n\nerrare atque viam palantis quaerere vitae,\n\n \n\ncertare ingenio, contendere nobilitate,\n\n \n\nnoctes atque dies niti praestante labore\n\n \n\nad summas emergere opes rerumque potiri.\n\n \n\no miseras hominum mentes, o pectora caeca!\n\n \n\nqualibus in tenebris vitae quantisque periclis\n\n \n\ndegitur hoc aevi quod cumquest! nonne videre\n\n \n\nnihil aliud sibi naturam latrare, nisi ut qui\n\n \n\ncorpore seiunctus dolor absit, mente fruatur\n\n \n\niucundo sensu cura semota metuque?\n\n \n\nergo corpoream ad naturam pauca videmus\n\n \n\nesse opus omnino: quae demant cumque dolorem,\n\n \n\ndelicias quoque uti multas substernere possint\n\n \n\ngratius inter dum, neque natura ipsa requirit,\n\n \n\nsi non aurea sunt iuvenum simulacra per aedes\n\n \n\nlampadas igniferas manibus retinentia dextris,\n\n \n\nlumina nocturnis epulis ut suppeditentur,\n\n \n\nnec domus argento fulget auroque renidet\n\n \n\nnec citharae reboant laqueata aurataque templa,\n\n \n\ncum tamen inter se prostrati in gramine molli\n\n \n\npropter aquae rivum sub ramis arboris altae\n\n \n\nnon magnis opibus iucunde corpora curant,\n\n \n\npraesertim cum tempestas adridet et anni\n\n \n\ntempora conspergunt viridantis floribus herbas.\n\n \n\nnec calidae citius decedunt corpore febres,\n\n \n\ntextilibus si in picturis ostroque rubenti\n\n \n\niacteris, quam si in plebeia veste cubandum est.\n\n \n\nquapropter quoniam nihil nostro in corpore gazae\n\n \n\nproficiunt neque nobilitas nec gloria regni,\n\n \n\nquod super est, animo quoque nil prodesse putandum;\n\n \n\nsi non forte tuas legiones per loca campi\n\n \n\nfervere cum videas belli simulacra cientis,\n\n \n\nsubsidiis magnis et opum vi constabilitas,\n\n \n\nornatas armis stlattas pariterque animatas,\n\n \n\nhis tibi tum rebus timefactae religiones\n\n \n\neffugiunt animo pavidae mortisque timores\n\n \n\ntum vacuum pectus lincunt curaque solutum.\n\n \n\nquod si ridicula haec ludibriaque esse videmus,\n\n \n\nre veraque metus hominum curaeque sequaces\n\n \n\nnec metuunt sonitus armorum nec fera tela\n\n \n\naudacterque inter reges rerumque potentis\n\n \n\nversantur neque fulgorem reverentur ab auro\n\n \n\nnec clarum vestis splendorem purpureai,\n\n \n\nquid dubitas quin omnis sit haec rationis potestas,\n\n \n\nomnis cum in tenebris praesertim vita laboret?\n\n \n\nnam vel uti pueri trepidant atque omnia caecis\n\n \n\nin tenebris metuunt, sic nos in luce timemus\n\n \n\ninter dum, nihilo quae sunt metuenda magis quam\n\n \n\nquae pueri in tenebris pavitant finguntque futura.\n\n \n\nhunc igitur terrorem animi tenebrasque necessest\n\n \n\nnon radii solis neque lucida tela diei\n\n \n\ndiscutiant, sed naturae species ratioque.\n\n \n\nNunc age, quo motu genitalia materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora res varias gignant genitasque resolvant\n\n \n\net qua vi facere id cogantur quaeque sit ollis\n\n \n\nreddita mobilitas magnum per inane meandi,\n\n \n\nexpediam: tu te dictis praebere memento.\n\n \n\nnam certe non inter se stipata cohaeret\n\n \n\nmateries, quoniam minui rem quamque videmus\n\n \n\net quasi longinquo fluere omnia cernimus aevo\n\n \n\nex oculisque vetustatem subducere nostris,\n\n \n\ncum tamen incolumis videatur summa manere\n\n \n\npropterea quia, quae decedunt corpora cuique,\n\n \n\nunde abeunt minuunt, quo venere augmine donant.\n\n \n\nilla senescere, at haec contra florescere cogunt,\n\n \n\nnec remorantur ibi. sic rerum summa novatur\n\n \n\nsemper, et inter se mortales mutua vivunt.\n\n \n\naugescunt aliae gentes, aliae minuuntur,\n\n \n\ninque brevi spatio mutantur saecla animantum\n\n \n\net quasi cursores vitai lampada tradunt.\n\n  \n\nSi cessare putas rerum primordia posse\n\n \n\ncessandoque novos rerum progignere motus,\n\n \n\navius a vera longe ratione vagaris.\n\n \n\nnam quoniam per inane vagantur, cuncta necessest\n\n \n\naut gravitate sua ferri primordia rerum\n\n \n\naut ictu forte alterius. nam cum cita saepe\n\n \n\nobvia conflixere, fit ut diversa repente\n\n \n\ndissiliant; neque enim mirum, durissima quae sint\n\n \n\nponderibus solidis neque quicquam a tergibus obstet.\n\n \n\net quo iactari magis omnia materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora pervideas, reminiscere totius imum\n\n \n\nnil esse in summa, neque habere ubi corpora prima\n\n \n\nconsistant, quoniam spatium sine fine modoquest\n\n \n\ninmensumque patere in cunctas undique partis\n\n \n\npluribus ostendi et certa ratione probatumst.\n\n \n\nquod quoniam constat, ni mirum nulla quies est\n\n \n\nreddita corporibus primis per inane profundum,\n\n \n\nsed magis adsiduo varioque exercita motu\n\n \n\npartim intervallis magnis confulta resultant,\n\n \n\npars etiam brevibus spatiis vexantur ab ictu.\n\n \n\net quae cumque magis condenso conciliatu\n\n \n\nexiguis intervallis convecta resultant,\n\n \n\nindupedita suis perplexis ipsa figuris,\n\n \n\nhaec validas saxi radices et fera ferri\n\n \n\ncorpora constituunt et cetera de genere horum.\n\n \n\npaucula quae porro magnum per inane vagantur,\n\n \n\ncetera dissiliunt longe longeque recursant\n\n \n\nin magnis intervallis; haec aera rarum\n\n \n\nsufficiunt nobis et splendida lumina solis.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea magnum per inane vagantur,\n\n \n\nconciliis rerum quae sunt reiecta nec usquam\n\n \n\nconsociare etiam motus potuere recepta.\n\n \n\nCuius, uti memoro, rei simulacrum et imago\n\n \n\nante oculos semper nobis versatur et instat.\n\n \n\ncontemplator enim, cum solis lumina cumque\n\n \n\ninserti fundunt radii per opaca domorum:\n\n \n\nmulta minuta modis multis per inane videbis\n\n \n\ncorpora misceri radiorum lumine in ipso\n\n \n\net vel ut aeterno certamine proelia pugnas\n\n \n\nedere turmatim certantia nec dare pausam,\n\n \n\nconciliis et discidiis exercita crebris;\n\n \n\nconicere ut possis ex hoc, primordia rerum\n\n \n\nquale sit in magno iactari semper inani.\n\n \n\ndum taxat, rerum magnarum parva potest res\n\n \n\nexemplare dare et vestigia notitiai.\n\n \n\nHoc etiam magis haec animum te advertere par est\n\n \n\ncorpora quae in solis radiis turbare videntur,\n\n \n\nquod tales turbae motus quoque materiai\n\n \n\nsignificant clandestinos caecosque subesse.\n\n \n\nmulta videbis enim plagis ibi percita caecis\n\n \n\ncommutare viam retroque repulsa reverti\n\n \n\nnunc huc nunc illuc in cunctas undique partis.\n\n \n\nscilicet hic a principiis est omnibus error.\n\n \n\nprima moventur enim per se primordia rerum,\n\n \n\ninde ea quae parvo sunt corpora conciliatu\n\n \n\net quasi proxima sunt ad viris principiorum,\n\n \n\nictibus illorum caecis inpulsa cientur,\n\n \n\nipsaque proporro paulo maiora lacessunt.\n\n \n\nsic a principiis ascendit motus et exit\n\n \n\npaulatim nostros ad sensus, ut moveantur\n\n \n\nilla quoque, in solis quae lumine cernere quimus\n\n \n\nnec quibus id faciant plagis apparet aperte.\n\n  \n\nNunc quae mobilitas sit reddita materiai\n\n \n\ncorporibus, paucis licet hinc cognoscere, Memmi.\n\n \n\nprimum aurora novo cum spargit lumine terras\n\n \n\net variae volucres nemora avia pervolitantes\n\n \n\naera per tenerum liquidis loca vocibus opplent,\n\n \n\nquam subito soleat sol ortus tempore tali\n\n \n\nconvestire sua perfundens omnia luce,\n\n \n\nomnibus in promptu manifestumque esse videmus.\n\n \n\nat vapor is, quem sol mittit, lumenque serenum\n\n \n\nnon per inane meat vacuum; quo tardius ire\n\n \n\ncogitur, aerias quasi dum diverberat undas;\n\n \n\nnec singillatim corpuscula quaeque vaporis\n\n \n\nsed complexa meant inter se conque globata;\n\n \n\nqua propter simul inter se retrahuntur et extra\n\n \n\nofficiuntur, uti cogantur tardius ire.\n\n \n\nat quae sunt solida primordia simplicitate,\n\n \n\ncum per inane meant vacuum nec res remoratur\n\n \n\nulla foris atque ipsa suis e partibus unum,\n\n \n\nunum, in quem coepere, locum conixa feruntur,\n\n \n\ndebent ni mirum praecellere mobilitate\n\n \n\net multo citius ferri quam lumina solis\n\n \n\nmultiplexque loci spatium transcurrere eodem\n\n \n\ntempore quo solis pervolgant fulgura caelum.\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nnec persectari primordia singula quaeque,\n\n \n\nut videant qua quicque geratur cum ratione.\n\n \n\nAt quidam contra haec, ignari materiai,\n\n \n\nnaturam non posse deum sine numine reddunt\n\n \n\ntanto opere humanis rationibus atmoderate\n\n \n\ntempora mutare annorum frugesque creare\n\n \n\net iam cetera, mortalis quae suadet adire\n\n \n\nipsaque deducit dux vitae dia voluptas\n\n \n\net res per Veneris blanditur saecla propagent,\n\n \n\nne genus occidat humanum. quorum omnia causa\n\n \n\nconstituisse deos cum fingunt, omnibus rebus\n\n \n\nmagno opere a vera lapsi ratione videntur.\n\n \n\nnam quamvis rerum ignorem primordia quae sint,\n\n \n\nhoc tamen ex ipsis caeli rationibus ausim\n\n \n\nconfirmare aliisque ex rebus reddere multis,\n\n \n\nnequaquam nobis divinitus esse creatam\n\n \n\nnaturam mundi: tanta stat praedita culpa.\n\n \n\nquae tibi posterius, Memmi, faciemus aperta;\n\n \n\nnunc id quod super est de motibus expediemus.\n\n \n\nNunc locus est, ut opinor, in his illud quoque rebus\n\n \n\nconfirmare tibi, nullam rem posse sua vi\n\n \n\ncorpoream sursum ferri sursumque meare.\n\n \n\nne tibi dent in eo flammarum corpora frudem;\n\n \n\nsursus enim versus gignuntur et augmina sumunt\n\n \n\net sursum nitidae fruges arbustaque crescunt,\n\n \n\npondera, quantum in se est, cum deorsum cuncta ferantur.\n\n \n\nnec cum subsiliunt ignes ad tecta domorum\n\n \n\net celeri flamma degustant tigna trabesque,\n\n \n\nsponte sua facere id sine vi subiecta putandum est.\n\n \n\nquod genus e nostro com missus corpore sanguis\n\n \n\nemicat exultans alte spargitque cruorem.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam quanta vi tigna trabesque\n\n \n\nrespuat umor aquae? nam quo magis ursimus altum\n\n \n\nderecta et magna vi multi pressimus aegre,\n\n \n\ntam cupide sursum removet magis atque remittit,\n\n \n\nplus ut parte foras emergant exiliantque.\n\n \n\nnec tamen haec, quantum est in se, dubitamus, opinor,\n\n \n\nquin vacuum per inane deorsum cuncta ferantur.\n\n \n\nsic igitur debent flammae quoque posse per auras\n\n \n\naeris expressae sursum succedere, quamquam\n\n \n\npondera, quantum in se est, deorsum deducere pugnent.\n\n \n\nnocturnasque faces caeli sublime volantis\n\n \n\nnonne vides longos flammarum ducere tractus\n\n \n\nin quas cumque dedit partis natura meatum?\n\n \n\nnon cadere in terras stellas et sidera cernis?\n\n \n\nsol etiam caeli de vertice dissipat omnis\n\n \n\nardorem in partis et lumine conserit arva;\n\n \n\nin terras igitur quoque solis vergitur ardor.\n\n \n\ntransversosque volare per imbris fulmina cernis,\n\n \n\nnunc hinc nunc illinc abrupti nubibus ignes\n\n \n\nconcursant; cadit in terras vis flammea volgo.\n\n \n\nIllud in his quoque te rebus cognoscere avemus,\n\n \n\ncorpora cum deorsum rectum per inane feruntur\n\n \n\nponderibus propriis, incerto tempore ferme\n\n \n\nincertisque locis spatio depellere paulum,\n\n \n\ntantum quod momen mutatum dicere possis.\n\n \n\nquod nisi declinare solerent, omnia deorsum\n\n \n\nimbris uti guttae caderent per inane profundum\n\n \n\nnec foret offensus natus nec plaga creata\n\n \n\nprincipiis; ita nihil umquam natura creasset.\n\n \n\nQuod si forte aliquis credit graviora potesse\n\n \n\ncorpora, quo citius rectum per inane feruntur,\n\n \n\nincidere ex supero levioribus atque ita plagas\n\n \n\ngignere, quae possint genitalis reddere motus,\n\n \n\navius a vera longe ratione recedit.\n\n \n\nnam per aquas quae cumque cadunt atque aera rarum,\n\n \n\nhaec pro ponderibus casus celerare necessest\n\n \n\npropterea quia corpus aquae naturaque tenvis\n\n \n\naeris haud possunt aeque rem quamque morari,\n\n \n\nsed citius cedunt gravioribus exsuperata;\n\n \n\nat contra nulli de nulla parte neque ullo\n\n \n\ntempore inane potest vacuum subsistere rei,\n\n \n\nquin, sua quod natura petit, concedere pergat;\n\n \n\nomnia qua propter debent per inane quietum\n\n \n\naeque ponderibus non aequis concita ferri.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur poterunt levioribus incidere umquam\n\n \n\nex supero graviora neque ictus gignere per se,\n\n \n\nqui varient motus, per quos natura gerat res.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam paulum inclinare necessest\n\n \n\ncorpora; nec plus quam minimum, ne fingere motus\n\n \n\nobliquos videamur et id res vera refutet.\n\n \n\nnamque hoc in promptu manifestumque esse videmus,\n\n \n\npondera, quantum in se est, non posse obliqua meare,\n\n \n\nex supero cum praecipitant, quod cernere possis;\n\n \n\nsed nihil omnino recta regione viai\n\n \n\ndeclinare quis est qui possit cernere sese?\n\n \n\nDenique si semper motu conectitur omnis\n\n \n\net vetere exoritur motus novus ordine certo\n\n \n\nnec declinando faciunt primordia motus\n\n \n\nprincipium quoddam, quod fati foedera rumpat,\n\n \n\nex infinito ne causam causa sequatur,\n\n \n\nlibera per terras unde haec animantibus exstat,\n\n \n\nunde est haec, inquam, fatis avolsa voluntas,\n\n \n\nper quam progredimur quo ducit quemque voluptas,\n\n \n\ndeclinamus item motus nec tempore certo\n\n \n\nnec regione loci certa, sed ubi ipsa tulit mens?\n\n \n\nnam dubio procul his rebus sua cuique voluntas\n\n \n\nprincipium dat et hinc motus per membra rigantur.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam patefactis tempore puncto\n\n \n\ncarceribus non posse tamen prorumpere equorum\n\n \n\nvim cupidam tam de subito quam mens avet ipsa?\n\n \n\nomnis enim totum per corpus materiai\n\n \n\ncopia conciri debet, concita per artus\n\n \n\nomnis ut studium mentis conixa sequatur;\n\n \n\nut videas initum motus a corde creari\n\n \n\nex animique voluntate id procedere primum,\n\n \n\ninde dari porro per totum corpus et artus.\n\n \n\nnec similest ut cum inpulsi procedimus ictu\n\n \n\nviribus alterius magnis magnoque coactu;\n\n \n\nnam tum materiem totius corporis omnem\n\n \n\nperspicuumst nobis invitis ire rapique,\n\n \n\ndonec eam refrenavit per membra voluntas.\n\n \n\niamne vides igitur, quamquam vis extera multos\n\n \n\npellat et invitos cogat procedere saepe\n\n \n\npraecipitesque rapi, tamen esse in pectore nostro\n\n \n\nquiddam quod contra pugnare obstareque possit?\n\n \n\ncuius ad arbitrium quoque copia materiai\n\n \n\ncogitur inter dum flecti per membra per artus\n\n \n\net proiecta refrenatur retroque residit.\n\n \n\nquare in seminibus quoque idem fateare necessest,\n\n \n\nesse aliam praeter plagas et pondera causam\n\n \n\nmotibus, unde haec est nobis innata potestas,\n\n \n\nde nihilo quoniam fieri nihil posse videmus.\n\n \n\npondus enim prohibet ne plagis omnia fiant\n\n \n\nexterna quasi vi; sed ne res ipsa necessum\n\n \n\nintestinum habeat cunctis in rebus agendis\n\n \n\net devicta quasi cogatur ferre patique,\n\n \n\nid facit exiguum clinamen principiorum\n\n \n\nnec regione loci certa nec tempore certo.\n\n \n\nNec stipata magis fuit umquam materiai\n\n \n\ncopia nec porro maioribus intervallis;\n\n \n\nnam neque adaugescit quicquam neque deperit inde.\n\n \n\nqua propter quo nunc in motu principiorum\n\n \n\ncorpora sunt, in eodem ante acta aetate fuere\n\n \n\net post haec semper simili ratione ferentur,\n\n \n\net quae consuerint gigni gignentur eadem\n\n \n\ncondicione et erunt et crescent vique valebunt,\n\n \n\nquantum cuique datum est per foedera naturai.\n\n \n\nnec rerum summam commutare ulla potest vis;\n\n \n\nnam neque quo possit genus ullum materiai\n\n \n\neffugere ex omni quicquam est extra, neque in omne\n\n \n\nunde coorta queat nova vis inrumpere et omnem\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum mutare et vertere motus.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus non est mirabile, quare,\n\n \n\nomnia cum rerum primordia sint in motu,\n\n \n\nsumma tamen summa videatur stare quiete,\n\n \n\npraeter quam siquid proprio dat corpore motus.\n\n \n\nomnis enim longe nostris ab sensibus infra\n\n \n\nprimorum natura iacet; qua propter, ubi ipsa\n\n \n\ncernere iam nequeas, motus quoque surpere debent;\n\n \n\npraesertim cum, quae possimus cernere, celent\n\n \n\nsaepe tamen motus spatio diducta locorum.\n\n \n\nnam saepe in colli tondentes pabula laeta\n\n \n\nlanigerae reptant pecudes, quo quamque vocantes\n\n \n\ninvitant herbae gemmantes rore recenti,\n\n \n\net satiati agni ludunt blandeque coruscant;\n\n \n\nomnia quae nobis longe confusa videntur\n\n \n\net velut in viridi candor consistere colli.\n\n \n\npraeterea magnae legiones cum loca cursu\n\n \n\ncamporum complent belli simulacra cientes,\n\n \n\nfulgor ubi ad caelum se tollit totaque circum\n\n \n\naere renidescit tellus supterque virum vi\n\n \n\nexcitur pedibus sonitus clamoreque montes\n\n \n\nicti reiectant voces ad sidera mundi\n\n \n\net circum volitant equites mediosque repente\n\n \n\ntramittunt valido quatientes impete campos;\n\n \n\net tamen est quidam locus altis montibus, unde\n\n \n\nstare videntur et in campis consistere fulgor.\n\n \n\nNunc age, iam deinceps cunctarum exordia rerum\n\n \n\nqualia sint et quam longe distantia formis,\n\n \n\npercipe, multigenis quam sint variata figuris;\n\n \n\nnon quo multa parum simili sint praedita forma,\n\n \n\nsed quia non volgo paria omnibus omnia constant.\n\n \n\nnec mirum; nam cum sit eorum copia tanta,\n\n \n\nut neque finis, uti docui, neque summa sit ulla,\n\n \n\ndebent ni mirum non omnibus omnia prorsum\n\n \n\nesse pari filo similique adfecta figura.\n\n \n\nPraeterea genus humanum mutaeque natantes\n\n \n\nsquamigerum pecudes et laeta armenta feraeque\n\n \n\net variae volucres, laetantia quae loca aquarum\n\n \n\nconcelebrant circum ripas fontisque lacusque,\n\n \n\net quae pervolgant nemora avia pervolitantes,\n\n \n\nquorum unum quidvis generatim sumere perge;\n\n \n\ninvenies tamen inter se differre figuris.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia proles cognoscere matrem\n\n \n\nnec mater posset prolem; quod posse videmus\n\n \n\nnec minus atque homines inter se nota cluere.\n\n \n\nnam saepe ante deum vitulus delubra decora\n\n \n\nturicremas propter mactatus concidit aras\n\n \n\nsanguinis expirans calidum de pectore flumen;\n\n \n\nat mater viridis saltus orbata peragrans\n\n \n\nnovit humi pedibus vestigia pressa bisulcis,\n\n \n\nomnia convisens oculis loca, si queat usquam\n\n \n\nconspicere amissum fetum, completque querellis\n\n \n\nfrondiferum nemus adsistens et crebra revisit\n\n \n\nad stabulum desiderio perfixa iuvenci,\n\n \n\nnec tenerae salices atque herbae rore vigentes\n\n \n\nfluminaque ulla queunt summis labentia ripis\n\n \n\noblectare animum subitamque avertere curam,\n\n \n\nnec vitulorum aliae species per pabula laeta\n\n \n\nderivare queunt animum curaque levare;\n\n \n\nusque adeo quiddam proprium notumque requirit.\n\n \n\npraeterea teneri tremulis cum vocibus haedi\n\n \n\ncornigeras norunt matres agnique petulci\n\n \n\nbalantum pecudes; ita, quod natura resposcit,\n\n \n\nad sua quisque fere decurrunt ubera lactis.\n\n \n\nPostremo quodvis frumentum non tamen omne\n\n \n\nquidque suo genere inter se simile esse videbis,\n\n \n\nquin intercurrat quaedam distantia formis.\n\n \n\nconcharumque genus parili ratione videmus\n\n \n\npingere telluris gremium, qua mollibus undis\n\n \n\nlitoris incurvi bibulam pavit aequor harenam.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam simili ratione necessest,\n\n \n\nnatura quoniam constant neque facta manu sunt\n\n \n\nunius ad certam formam primordia rerum,\n\n \n\ndissimili inter se quaedam volitare figura.\n\n \n\nPerfacile est animi ratione exsolvere nobis\n\n \n\nquare fulmineus multo penetralior ignis\n\n \n\nquam noster fluat e taedis terrestribus ortus;\n\n \n\ndicere enim possis caelestem fulminis ignem\n\n \n\nsubtilem magis e parvis constare figuris\n\n \n\natque ideo transire foramina quae nequit ignis\n\n \n\nnoster hic e lignis ortus taedaque creatus.\n\n \n\npraeterea lumen per cornum transit, at imber\n\n \n\nrespuitur. quare, nisi luminis illa minora\n\n \n\ncorpora sunt quam de quibus est liquor almus aquarum?\n\n \n\net quamvis subito per colum vina videmus\n\n \n\nperfluere, at contra tardum cunctatur olivom,\n\n \n\naut quia ni mirum maioribus est elementis\n\n \n\naut magis hamatis inter se perque plicatis,\n\n \n\natque ideo fit uti non tam diducta repente\n\n \n\ninter se possint primordia singula quaeque\n\n \n\nsingula per cuiusque foramina permanare.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit uti mellis lactisque liquores\n\n \n\niucundo sensu linguae tractentur in ore;\n\n \n\nat contra taetra absinthi natura ferique\n\n \n\ncentauri foedo pertorquent ora sapore;\n\n \n\nut facile agnoscas e levibus atque rutundis\n\n \n\nesse ea quae sensus iucunde tangere possunt,\n\n \n\nat contra quae amara atque aspera cumque videntur,\n\n \n\nhaec magis hamatis inter se nexa teneri\n\n \n\nproptereaque solere vias rescindere nostris\n\n \n\nsensibus introituque suo perrumpere corpus.\n\n \n\nomnia postremo bona sensibus et mala tactu\n\n \n\ndissimili inter se pugnant perfecta figura;\n\n \n\nne tu forte putes serrae stridentis acerbum\n\n \n\nhorrorem constare elementis levibus aeque\n\n \n\nac musaea mele, per chordas organici quae\n\n \n\nmobilibus digitis expergefacta figurant;\n\n \n\nneu simili penetrare putes primordia forma\n\n \n\nin nares hominum, cum taetra cadavera torrent,\n\n \n\net cum scena croco Cilici perfusa recens est\n\n \n\naraque Panchaeos exhalat propter odores;\n\n \n\nneve bonos rerum simili constare colores\n\n \n\nsemine constituas, oculos qui pascere possunt,\n\n \n\net qui conpungunt aciem lacrimareque cogunt\n\n \n\naut foeda specie foedi turpesque videntur.\n\n \n\nomnis enim, sensus quae mulcet cumque, tibi res\n\n \n\nhaut sine principiali aliquo levore creatast;\n\n \n\nat contra quae cumque molesta atque aspera constat,\n\n \n\nnon aliquo sine materiae squalore repertast.\n\n \n\nSunt etiam quae iam nec levia iure putantur\n\n \n\nesse neque omnino flexis mucronibus unca,\n\n \n\nsed magis angellis paulum prostantibus, ut quae\n\n \n\ntitillare magis sensus quam laedere possint,\n\n \n\nfecula iam quo de genere est inulaeque sapores.\n\n \n\nDenique iam calidos ignis gelidamque pruinam\n\n \n\ndissimili dentata modo conpungere sensus\n\n \n\ncorporis, indicio nobis est tactus uterque.\n\n \n\ntactus enim, tactus, pro divum numina sancta,\n\n \n\ncorporis est sensus, vel cum res extera sese\n\n \n\ninsinuat, vel cum laedit quae in corpore natast\n\n \n\naut iuvat egrediens genitalis per Veneris res,\n\n \n\naut ex offensu cum turbant corpore in ipso,\n\n \n\nsemina confundunt inter se concita sensum;\n\n \n\nut si forte manu quamvis iam corporis ipse\n\n \n\ntute tibi partem ferias atque experiare.\n\n \n\nqua propter longe formas distare necessest\n\n \n\nprincipiis, varios quae possint edere sensus.\n\n \n\nDenique quae nobis durata ac spissa videntur,\n\n \n\nhaec magis hamatis inter sese esse necessest\n\n \n\net quasi ramosis alte compacta teneri.\n\n \n\nin quo iam genere in primis adamantina saxa\n\n \n\nprima acie constant ictus contemnere sueta\n\n \n\net validi silices ac duri robora ferri\n\n \n\naeraque quae claustris restantia vociferantur.\n\n \n\nilla quidem debent e levibus atque rutundis\n\n \n\nesse magis, fluvido quae corpore liquida constant.\n\n \n\nnamque papaveris haustus itemst facilis quod aquarum;\n\n \n\nnec retinentur enim inter se glomeramina quaeque\n\n \n\net perculsus item proclive volubilis exstat.\n\n \n\nomnia postremo quae puncto tempore cernis\n\n \n\ndiffugere ut fumum nebulas flammasque, necessest,\n\n \n\nsi minus omnia sunt e levibus atque rotundis,\n\n \n\nat non esse tamen perplexis indupedita,\n\n \n\npungere uti possint corpus penetrareque saxa,\n\n \n\nnec tamen haerere inter se; quod cumque videmus\n\n \n\nsensibus dentatum, facile ut cognoscere possis\n\n \n\nnon e perplexis, sed acutis esse elementis.\n\n \n\nsed quod amara vides eadem quae fluvida constant,\n\n \n\nsudor uti maris est, minime mirabile debet\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nnam quod fluvidus est, e levibus atque rotundis\n\n \n\nest, sed levibus sunt hamata admixta doloris\n\n \n\ncorpora. nec tamen haec retineri hamata necessust:\n\n \n\nscilicet esse globosa tamen, cum squalida constent,\n\n \n\nprovolvi simul ut possint et laedere sensus.\n\n \n\net quo mixta putes magis aspera levibus esse\n\n \n\nprincipiis, unde est Neptuni corpus acerbum,\n\n \n\nest ratio secernendi seorsumque videndi,\n\n \n\numor dulcis ubi per terras crebrius idem\n\n \n\npercolatur, ut in foveam fluat ac mansuescat;\n\n \n\nlinquit enim supera taetri primordia viri,\n\n \n\naspera quo magis in terris haerescere possint.\n\n  \n\nQuod quoniam docui, pergam conectere rem quae\n\n \n\nex hoc apta fidem ducat, primordia rerum\n\n \n\nfinita variare figurarum ratione.\n\n \n\nquod si non ita sit, rursum iam semina quaedam\n\n \n\nesse infinito debebunt corporis auctu.\n\n \n\nnamque in eadem una cuiusvis iam brevitate\n\n \n\ncorporis inter se multum variare figurae\n\n \n\nnon possunt. fac enim minimis e partibus esse\n\n \n\ncorpora prima tribus, vel paulo pluribus auge;\n\n \n\nnempe ubi eas partis unius corporis omnis,\n\n \n\nsumma atque ima locans, transmutans dextera laevis,\n\n \n\nomnimodis expertus eris, quam quisque det ordo\n\n \n\nformai speciem totius corporis eius,\n\n \n\nquod super est, si forte voles variare figuras,\n\n \n\naddendum partis alias erit. inde sequetur,\n\n \n\nadsimili ratione alias ut postulet ordo,\n\n \n\nsi tu forte voles etiam variare figuras.\n\n \n\nergo formarum novitatem corporis augmen\n\n \n\nsubsequitur. quare non est ut credere possis\n\n \n\nesse infinitis distantia semina formis,\n\n \n\nne quaedam cogas inmani maximitate\n\n \n\nesse, supra quod iam docui non posse probari.\n\n \n\niam tibi barbaricae vestes Meliboeaque fulgens\n\n \n\npurpura Thessalico concharum tacta colore,\n\n \n\naurea pavonum ridenti imbuta lepore\n\n \n\nsaecla novo rerum superata colore iacerent\n\n \n\net contemptus odor smyrnae mellisque sapores,\n\n \n\net cycnea mele Phoebeaque daedala chordis\n\n \n\ncarmina consimili ratione oppressa silerent;\n\n \n\nnamque aliis aliud praestantius exoreretur.\n\n \n\ncedere item retro possent in deteriores\n\n \n\nomnia sic partis, ut diximus in melioris;\n\n \n\nnamque aliis aliud retro quoque taetrius esset\n\n \n\nnaribus auribus atque oculis orisque sapori.\n\n \n\nquae quoniam non sunt, sed rebus reddita certa\n\n \n\nfinis utrimque tenet summam, fateare necessest\n\n \n\nmateriem quoque finitis differe figuris.\n\n \n\ndenique ab ignibus ad gelidas hiemum usque pruinas\n\n \n\nfinitumst retroque pari ratione remensumst.\n\n \n\nomnis enim calor ac frigus mediique tepores\n\n \n\ninterutrasque iacent explentes ordine summam.\n\n \n\nergo finita distant ratione creata,\n\n \n\nancipiti quoniam mucroni utrimque notantur,\n\n \n\nhinc flammis illinc rigidis infesta pruinis.\n\n \n\nQuod quoniam docui, pergam conectere rem quae\n\n \n\nex hoc apta fidem ducat, primordia rerum,\n\n \n\ninter se simili quae sunt perfecta figura,\n\n \n\ninfinita cluere. etenim distantia cum sit\n\n \n\nformarum finita, necesse est quae similes sint\n\n \n\nesse infinitas aut summam materiai\n\n \n\nfinitam constare, id quod non esse probavi.\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nversibus ostendam corpuscula materiai\n\n \n\nex infinito summam rerum usque tenere\n\n \n\nundique protelo plagarum continuato.\n\n \n\nnam quod rara vides magis esse animalia quaedam\n\n \n\nfecundamque magis naturam cernis in illis,\n\n \n\nat regione locoque alio terrisque remotis\n\n \n\nmulta licet genere esse in eo numerumque repleri;\n\n \n\nsicut quadripedum cum primis esse videmus\n\n \n\nin genere anguimanus elephantos, India quorum\n\n \n\nmilibus e multis vallo munitur eburno,\n\n \n\nut penitus nequeat penetrari: tanta ferarum\n\n \n\nvis est, quarum nos perpauca exempla videmus.\n\n \n\nsed tamen id quoque uti concedam, quam lubet esto\n\n \n\nunica res quaedem nativo corpore sola,\n\n \n\ncui similis toto terrarum non sit, in orbi;\n\n \n\ninfinita tamen nisi erit vis materiai,\n\n \n\nunde ea progigni possit concepta, creari\n\n \n\nnon poterit neque, quod super est, procrescere alique.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim sumant alii finita per omne\n\n \n\ncorpora iactari unius genitalia rei,\n\n \n\nunde ubi qua vi et quo pacto congressa coibunt\n\n \n\nmateriae tanto in pelago turbaque aliena?\n\n \n\nnon, ut opinor, habent rationem conciliandi:\n\n \n\nsed quasi naufragiis magnis multisque coortis\n\n \n\ndisiactare solet magnum mare transtra cavernas\n\n \n\nantemnas prorem malos tonsasque natantis,\n\n \n\nper terrarum omnis oras fluitantia aplustra\n\n \n\nut videantur et indicium mortalibus edant,\n\n \n\ninfidi maris insidias virisque dolumque\n\n \n\nut vitare velint, neve ullo tempore credant,\n\n \n\nsubdola cum ridet placidi pellacia ponti,\n\n \n\nsic tibi si finita semel primordia quaedam\n\n \n\nconstitues, aevom debebunt sparsa per omnem\n\n \n\ndisiectare aestus diversi materiai,\n\n \n\nnumquam in concilium ut possint compulsa coire\n\n \n\nnec remorari in concilio nec crescere adaucta;\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque palam fieri manifesta docet res,\n\n \n\net res progigni et genitas procrescere posse.\n\n \n\nesse igitur genere in quovis primordia rerum\n\n \n\ninfinita palam est, unde omnia suppeditantur.\n\n \n\nNec superare queunt motus itaque exitiales\n\n \n\nperpetuo neque in aeternum sepelire salutem,\n\n \n\nnec porro rerum genitales auctificique\n\n \n\nmotus perpetuo possunt servare creata.\n\n \n\nsic aequo geritur certamine principiorum\n\n \n\nex infinito contractum tempore bellum.\n\n \n\nnunc hic nunc illic superant vitalia rerum\n\n \n\net superantur item. miscetur funere vagor,\n\n \n\nquem pueri tollunt visentis luminis oras;\n\n \n\nnec nox ulla diem neque noctem aurora secutast,\n\n \n\nquae non audierit mixtos vagitibus aegris\n\n \n\nploratus, mortis comites et funeris atri.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his obsignatum quoque rebus habere\n\n \n\nconvenit et memori mandatum mente tenere,\n\n \n\nnil esse, in promptu quorum natura videtur,\n\n \n\nquod genere ex uno consistat principiorum,\n\n \n\nnec quicquam quod non permixto semine constet.\n\n \n\net quod cumque magis vis multas possidet in se\n\n \n\natque potestates, ita plurima principiorum\n\n \n\nin sese genera ac varias docet esse figuras.\n\n \n\nPrincipio tellus habet in se corpora prima,\n\n \n\nunde mare inmensum volventes frigora fontes\n\n \n\nadsidue renovent, habet ignes unde oriantur;\n\n \n\nnam multis succensa locis ardent sola terrae,\n\n \n\nex imis vero furit ignibus impetus Aetnae.\n\n \n\ntum porro nitidas fruges arbustaque laeta\n\n \n\ngentibus humanis habet unde extollere possit,\n\n \n\nunde etiam fluvios frondes et pabula laeta\n\n \n\nmontivago generi possit praebere ferarum.\n\n \n\nquare magna deum mater materque ferarum\n\n \n\net nostri genetrix haec dicta est corporis una.\n\n \n\nHanc veteres Graium docti cecinere poetae\n\n \n\nsedibus in curru biiugos agitare leones,\n\n \n\naeris in spatio magnam pendere docentes\n\n \n\ntellurem neque posse in terra sistere terram.\n\n \n\nadiunxere feras, quia quamvis effera proles\n\n \n\nofficiis debet molliri victa parentum.\n\n \n\nmuralique caput summum cinxere corona,\n\n \n\neximiis munita locis quia sustinet urbes.\n\n \n\nquo nunc insigni per magnas praedita terras\n\n \n\nhorrifice fertur divinae matris imago.\n\n \n\nhanc variae gentes antiquo more sacrorum\n\n \n\nIdaeam vocitant matrem Phrygiasque catervas\n\n \n\ndant comites, quia primum ex illis finibus edunt\n\n \n\nper terrarum orbes fruges coepisse creari.\n\n \n\nGallos attribuunt, quia, numen qui violarint\n\n \n\nMatris et ingrati genitoribus inventi sint,\n\n \n\nsignificare volunt indignos esse putandos,\n\n \n\nvivam progeniem qui in oras luminis edant.\n\n \n\ntympana tenta tonant palmis et cymbala circum\n\n \n\nconcava, raucisonoque minantur cornua cantu,\n\n \n\net Phrygio stimulat numero cava tibia mentis,\n\n \n\ntelaque praeportant, violenti signa furoris,\n\n \n\ningratos animos atque impia pectora volgi\n\n \n\nconterrere metu quae possint numine divae.\n\n \n\nergo cum primum magnas invecta per urbis\n\n \n\nmunificat tacita mortalis muta salute,\n\n \n\naere atque argento sternunt iter omne viarum\n\n \n\nlargifica stipe ditantes ninguntque rosarum\n\n \n\nfloribus umbrantes matrem comitumque catervam.\n\n \n\nhic armata manus, Curetas nomine Grai\n\n \n\nquos memorant, Phrygias inter si forte catervas\n\n \n\nludunt in numerumque exultant sanguine laeti\n\n \n\nterrificas capitum quatientes numine cristas,\n\n \n\nDictaeos referunt Curetas, qui Iovis illum\n\n \n\nvagitum in Creta quondam occultasse feruntur,\n\n \n\ncum pueri circum puerum pernice chorea\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\narmati in numerum pulsarent aeribus aera,\n\n \n\nne Saturnus eum malis mandaret adeptus\n\n \n\naeternumque daret matri sub pectore volnus.\n\n \n\npropterea magnam armati matrem comitantur,\n\n \n\naut quia significant divam praedicere ut armis\n\n \n\nac virtute velint patriam defendere terram\n\n \n\npraesidioque parent decorique parentibus esse.\n\n \n\nquae bene et eximie quamvis disposta ferantur,\n\n \n\nlonge sunt tamen a vera ratione repulsa.\n\n \n\nomnis enim per se divom natura necessest\n\n \n\ninmortali aevo summa cum pace fruatur\n\n \n\nsemota ab nostris rebus seiunctaque longe;\n\n \n\nnam privata dolore omni, privata periclis,\n\n \n\nipsa suis pollens opibus, nihil indiga nostri,\n\n \n\nnec bene promeritis capitur neque tangitur ira.\n\n \n\nterra quidem vero caret omni tempore sensu,\n\n \n\net quia multarum potitur primordia rerum,\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis effert in lumina solis.\n\n \n\nhic siquis mare Neptunum Cereremque vocare\n\n \n\nconstituet fruges et Bacchi nomine abuti\n\n \n\nmavolt quam laticis proprium proferre vocamen,\n\n \n\nconcedamus ut hic terrarum dictitet orbem\n\n \n\nesse deum matrem, dum vera re tamen ipse\n\n \n\nreligione animum turpi contingere parcat.\n\n \n\nSaepe itaque ex uno tondentes gramina campo\n\n \n\nlanigerae pecudes et equorum duellica proles\n\n \n\nbuceriaeque greges eodem sub tegmine caeli\n\n \n\nex unoque sitim sedantes flumine aquai\n\n \n\ndissimili vivont specie retinentque parentum\n\n \n\nnaturam et mores generatim quaeque imitantur.\n\n \n\ntanta est in quovis genere herbae materiai\n\n \n\ndissimilis ratio, tanta est in flumine quoque.\n\n \n\nHinc porro quamvis animantem ex omnibus unam\n\n \n\nossa cruor venae calor umor viscera nervi\n\n \n\nconstituunt, quae sunt porro distantia longe,\n\n \n\ndissimili perfecta figura principiorum.\n\n \n\nTum porro quae cumque igni flammata cremantur.\n\n \n\nsi nil praeterea, tamen haec in corpore tradunt,\n\n \n\nunde ignem iacere et lumen submittere possint\n\n \n\nscintillasque agere ac late differre favillam.\n\n \n\ncetera consimili mentis ratione peragrans\n\n \n\ninvenies igitur multarum semina rerum\n\n \n\ncorpore celare et varias cohibere figuras.\n\n \n\nDenique multa vides, quibus et color et sapor una\n\n \n\nreddita sunt cum odore in primis pleraque poma.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur variis debent constare figuris;\n\n \n\nnidor enim penetrat qua fucus non it in artus,\n\n \n\nfucus item sorsum, sorsum sapor insinuatur\n\n \n\nsensibus; ut noscas primis differre figuris.\n\n \n\ndissimiles igitur formae glomeramen in unum\n\n \n\nconveniunt et res permixto semine constant.\n\n \n\nQuin etiam passim nostris in versibus ipsis\n\n \n\nmulta elementa vides multis communia verbis,\n\n \n\ncum tamen inter se versus ac verba necesse est\n\n \n\nconfiteare alia ex aliis constare elementis;\n\n \n\nnon quo multa parum communis littera currat\n\n \n\naut nulla inter se duo sint ex omnibus isdem,\n\n \n\nsed quia non volgo paria omnibus omnia constant.\n\n \n\nsic aliis in rebus item communia multa\n\n \n\nmultarum rerum cum sint, primordia rerum\n\n \n\ndissimili tamen inter se consistere summa\n\n \n\npossunt; ut merito ex aliis constare feratur\n\n \n\nhumanum genus et fruges arbustaque laeta.\n\n \n\nNec tamen omnimodis conecti posse putandum est\n\n \n\nomnia; nam volgo fieri portenta videres,\n\n \n\nsemiferas hominum species existere et altos\n\n \n\ninter dum ramos egigni corpore vivo\n\n \n\nmultaque conecti terrestria membra marinis,\n\n \n\ntum flammam taetro spirantis ore Chimaeras\n\n \n\npascere naturam per terras omniparentis.\n\n \n\nquorum nil fieri manifestum est, omnia quando\n\n \n\nseminibus certis certa genetrice creata\n\n \n\nconservare genus crescentia posse videmus.\n\n \n\nscilicet id certa fieri ratione necessust.\n\n \n\nnam sua cuique cibis ex omnibus intus in artus\n\n \n\ncorpora discedunt conexaque convenientis\n\n \n\nefficiunt motus; at contra aliena videmus\n\n \n\nreicere in terras naturam, multaque caecis\n\n \n\ncorporibus fugiunt e corpore percita plagis,\n\n \n\nquae neque conecti quoquam potuere neque intus\n\n \n\nvitalis motus consentire atque imitari.\n\n \n\nsed ne forte putes animalia sola teneri\n\n \n\nlegibus his, quaedam ratio res terminat omnis\n\n \n\nnam vel uti tota natura dissimiles sunt\n\n \n\ninter se genitae res quaeque, ita quamque necessest\n\n \n\ndissimili constare figura principiorum;\n\n \n\nnon quo multa parum simili sint praedita forma,\n\n \n\nsed quia non volgo paria omnibus omnia constant.\n\n \n\nsemina cum porro distent, differre necessust\n\n \n\nintervalla vias conexus pondera plagas\n\n \n\nconcursus motus; quae non animalia solum\n\n \n\ncorpora seiungunt, sed terras ac mare totum\n\n \n\nsecernunt caelumque a terris omne retentant.\n\n \n\nNunc age dicta meo dulci quaesita labore\n\n \n\npercipe, ne forte haec albis ex alba rearis\n\n \n\nprincipiis esse, ante oculos quae candida cernis,\n\n \n\naut ea quae nigrant nigro de semine nata;\n\n \n\nnive alium quemvis quae sunt inbuta colorem,\n\n \n\npropterea gerere hunc credas, quod materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora consimili sint eius tincta colore;\n\n \n\nnullus enim color est omnino materiai\n\n \n\ncorporibus, neque par rebus neque denique dispar.\n\n \n\nin quae corpora si nullus tibi forte videtur\n\n \n\nposse animi iniectus fieri, procul avius erras.\n\n \n\nnam cum caecigeni, solis qui lumina numquam\n\n \n\ndispexere, tamen cognoscant corpora tactu\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo nullo coniuncta colore,\n\n \n\nscire licet nostrae quoque menti corpora posse\n\n \n\nvorti in notitiam nullo circum lita fuco.\n\n \n\ndenique nos ipsi caecis quaecumque tenebris\n\n \n\ntangimus, haud ullo sentimus tincta colore.\n\n \n\nQuod quoniam vinco fieri, nunc esse docebo.\n\n \n\nomnis enim color omnino mutatur in omnis;\n\n \n\nquod facere haud ullo debent primordia pacto;\n\n \n\nimmutabile enim quiddam superare necessest,\n\n \n\nne res ad nihilum redigantur funditus omnes;\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque suis mutatum finibus exit,\n\n \n\ncontinuo hoc mors est illius quod fuit ante.\n\n \n\nproinde colore cave contingas semina rerum,\n\n \n\nne tibi res redeant ad nihilum funditus omnes.\n\n \n\nPraeterea si nulla coloris principiis est\n\n \n\nreddita natura et variis sunt praedita formis,\n\n \n\ne quibus omnigenus gignunt variantque colores,\n\n \n\npropterea magni quod refert, semina quaeque\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali positura contineantur\n\n \n\net quos inter se dent motus accipiantque,\n\n \n\nperfacile extemplo rationem reddere possis,\n\n \n\ncur ea quae nigro fuerint paulo ante colore,\n\n \n\nmarmoreo fieri possint candore repente,\n\n \n\nut mare, cum magni commorunt aequora venti,\n\n \n\nvertitur in canos candenti marmore fluctus;\n\n \n\ndicere enim possis, nigrum quod saepe videmus,\n\n \n\nmateries ubi permixta est illius et ordo\n\n \n\nprincipiis mutatus et addita demptaque quaedam,\n\n \n\ncontinuo id fieri ut candens videatur et album.\n\n \n\nquod si caeruleis constarent aequora ponti\n\n \n\nseminibus, nullo possent albescere pacto;\n\n \n\nnam quo cumque modo perturbes caerula quae sint,\n\n \n\nnumquam in marmoreum possunt migrare colorem.\n\n \n\nsin alio atque alio sunt semina tincta colore,\n\n \n\nquae maris efficiunt unum purumque nitorem,\n\n \n\nut saepe ex aliis formis variisque figuris\n\n \n\nefficitur quiddam quadratum unaque figura,\n\n \n\nconveniebat, ut in quadrato cernimus esse\n\n \n\ndissimiles formas, ita cernere in aequore ponti\n\n \n\naut alio in quovis uno puroque nitore\n\n \n\ndissimiles longe inter se variosque colores.\n\n \n\npraeterea nihil officiunt obstantque figurae\n\n \n\ndissimiles, quo quadratum minus omne sit extra;\n\n \n\nat varii rerum inpediunt prohibentque colores,\n\n \n\nquo minus esse uno possit res tota nitore.\n\n \n\nTum porro quae ducit et inlicit ut tribuamus\n\n \n\nprincipiis rerum non numquam causa colores,\n\n \n\noccidit, ex albis quoniam non alba creantur,\n\n \n\nnec quae nigra cluent de nigris, sed variis ex.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim multo proclivius exorientur\n\n \n\ncandida de nullo quam nigro nata colore\n\n \n\naut alio quovis, qui contra pugnet et obstet.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam nequeunt sine luce colores\n\n \n\nesse neque in lucem existunt primordia rerum,\n\n \n\nscire licet quam sint nullo velata colore;\n\n \n\nqualis enim caecis poterit color esse tenebris?\n\n \n\nlumine quin ipso mutatur propterea quod\n\n \n\nrecta aut obliqua percussus luce refulget;\n\n \n\npluma columbarum quo pacto in sole videtur,\n\n \n\nquae sita cervices circum collumque coronat;\n\n \n\nnamque alias fit uti claro sit rubra pyropo,\n\n \n\ninter dum quodam sensu fit uti videatur\n\n \n\ninter caeruleum viridis miscere zmaragdos.\n\n \n\ncaudaque pavonis, larga cum luce repleta est,\n\n \n\nconsimili mutat ratione obversa colores;\n\n \n\nqui quoniam quodam gignuntur luminis ictu,\n\n \n\nscire licet, sine eo fieri non posse putandum est.\n\n \n\nEt quoniam plagae quoddam genus excipit in se\n\n \n\npupula, cum sentire colorem dicitur album,\n\n \n\natque aliud porro, nigrum cum et cetera sentit,\n\n \n\nnec refert ea quae tangas quo forte colore\n\n \n\npraedita sint, verum quali magis apta figura,\n\n \n\nscire licet nihil principiis opus esse colore,\n\n \n\nsed variis formis variantes edere tactus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam non certis certa figuris\n\n \n\nest natura coloris et omnia principiorum\n\n \n\nformamenta queunt in quovis esse nitore,\n\n \n\ncur ea quae constant ex illis non pariter sunt\n\n \n\nomnigenus perfusa coloribus in genere omni?\n\n \n\nconveniebat enim corvos quoque saepe volantis\n\n \n\nex albis album pinnis iactare colorem\n\n \n\net nigros fieri nigro de semine cycnos\n\n \n\naut alio quovis uno varioque colore.\n\n \n\nQuin etiam quanto in partes res quaeque minutas\n\n \n\ndistrahitur magis, hoc magis est ut cernere possis\n\n \n\nevanescere paulatim stinguique colorem;\n\n \n\nut fit ubi in parvas partis discerpitur austrum:\n\n \n\npurpura poeniceusque color clarissimus multo,\n\n \n\nfilatim cum distractum est, disperditur omnis;\n\n \n\nnoscere ut hinc possis prius omnem efflare colorem\n\n \n\nparticulas, quam discedant ad semina rerum.\n\n \n\nPostremo quoniam non omnia corpora vocem\n\n \n\nmittere concedis neque odorem, propterea fit\n\n \n\nut non omnibus adtribuas sonitus et odores:\n\n \n\nsic oculis quoniam non omnia cernere quimus,\n\n \n\nscire licet quaedam tam constare orba colore\n\n \n\nquam sine odore ullo quaedam sonituque remota,\n\n \n\nnec minus haec animum cognoscere posse sagacem\n\n \n\nquam quae sunt aliis rebus privata notare.\n\n \n\nSed ne forte putes solo spoliata colore\n\n \n\ncorpora prima manere, etiam secreta teporis\n\n \n\nsunt ac frigoris omnino calidique vaporis,\n\n \n\net sonitu sterila et suco ieiuna feruntur,\n\n \n\nnec iaciunt ullum proprium de corpore odorem.\n\n \n\nsicut amaracini blandum stactaeque liquorem\n\n \n\net nardi florem, nectar qui naribus halat,\n\n \n\ncum facere instituas, cum primis quaerere par est,\n\n \n\nquod licet ac possis reperire, inolentis olivi\n\n \n\nnaturam, nullam quae mittat naribus auram,\n\n \n\nquam minime ut possit mixtos in corpore odores\n\n \n\nconcoctosque suo contractans perdere viro,\n\n \n\npropter eandem rem debent primordia rerum\n\n \n\nnon adhibere suum gignundis rebus odorem\n\n \n\nnec sonitum, quoniam nihil ab se mittere possunt,\n\n \n\nnec simili ratione saporem denique quemquam\n\n \n\nnec frigus neque item calidum tepidumque vaporem,\n\n \n\ncetera, quae cum ita sunt tamen ut mortalia constent,\n\n \n\nmolli lenta, fragosa putri, cava corpore raro,\n\n \n\nomnia sint a principiis seiuncta necessest,\n\n \n\ninmortalia si volumus subiungere rebus\n\n \n\nfundamenta, quibus nitatur summa salutis;\n\n \n\nne tibi res redeant ad nihilum funditus omnes.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quae sentire videmus cumque necessest\n\n \n\nex insensilibus tamen omnia confiteare\n\n \n\nprincipiis constare. neque id manufesta refutant\n\n \n\nnec contra pugnant, in promptu cognita quae sunt,\n\n \n\nsed magis ipsa manu ducunt et credere cogunt\n\n \n\nex insensilibus, quod dico, animalia gigni.\n\n \n\nquippe videre licet vivos existere vermes\n\n \n\nstercore de taetro, putorem cum sibi nacta est\n\n \n\nintempestivis ex imbribus umida tellus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea cunctas itidem res vertere sese.\n\n \n\nvertunt se fluvii in frondes et pabula laeta\n\n \n\nin pecudes, vertunt pecudes in corpora nostra\n\n \n\nnaturam, et nostro de corpore saepe ferarum\n\n \n\naugescunt vires et corpora pennipotentum.\n\n \n\nergo omnes natura cibos in corpora viva\n\n \n\nvertit et hinc sensus animantum procreat omnes,\n\n \n\nnon alia longe ratione atque arida ligna\n\n \n\nexplicat in flammas et in ignis omnia versat.\n\n \n\niamne vides igitur magni primordia rerum\n\n \n\nreferre in quali sint ordine quaeque locata\n\n \n\net commixta quibus dent motus accipiantque?\n\n \n\nTum porro, quid id est, animum quod percutit, ipsum,\n\n \n\nquod movet et varios sensus expromere cogit,\n\n \n\nex insensilibus ne credas sensile gigni?\n\n \n\nni mirum lapides et ligna et terra quod una\n\n \n\nmixta tamen nequeunt vitalem reddere sensum.\n\n \n\nillud in his igitur rebus meminisse decebit,\n\n \n\nnon ex omnibus omnino, quaecumque creant res\n\n \n\nsensilia, extemplo me gigni dicere sensus,\n\n \n\nsed magni referre ea primum quantula constent,\n\n \n\nsensile quae faciunt, et qua sint praedita forma,\n\n \n\nmotibus ordinibus posituris denique quae sint.\n\n \n\nquarum nil rerum in lignis glaebisque videmus;\n\n \n\net tamen haec, cum sunt quasi putrefacta per imbres,\n\n \n\nvermiculos pariunt, quia corpora materiai\n\n \n\nantiquis ex ordinibus permota nova re\n\n \n\nconciliantur ita ut debent animalia gigni.\n\n \n\nDeinde ex sensilibus qui sensile posse creari\n\n \n\nconstituunt, porro ex aliis sentire sueti\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nmollia cum faciunt; nam sensus iungitur omnis\n\n \n\nvisceribus nervis venis, quae cumque videmus\n\n \n\nmollia mortali consistere corpore creta.\n\n \n\nsed tamen esto iam posse haec aeterna manere;\n\n \n\nnempe tamen debent aut sensum partis habere\n\n \n\naut similis totis animalibus esse putari.\n\n \n\nat nequeant per se partes sentire necesse est:\n\n \n\nnamque animus sensus membrorum respuit omnis,\n\n \n\nnec manus a nobis potis est secreta neque ulla\n\n \n\ncorporis omnino sensum pars sola tenere.\n\n \n\nlinquitur ut totis animantibus adsimulentur,\n\n \n\nvitali ut possint consentire undique sensu.\n\n \n\nqui poterunt igitur rerum primordia dici\n\n \n\net leti vitare vias, animalia cum sint,\n\n \n\natque animalia sint mortalibus una eademque?\n\n \n\nquod tamen ut possint, at coetu concilioque\n\n \n\nnil facient praeter volgum turbamque animantum,\n\n \n\nscilicet ut nequeant homines armenta feraeque\n\n \n\ninter sese ullam rem gignere conveniundo.\n\n \n\nsic itidem quae sentimus sentire necessest.\n\n \n\nquod si forte suum dimittunt corpore sensum\n\n \n\natque alium capiunt, quid opus fuit adtribui id quod\n\n \n\ndetrahitur? tum praeterea, quod fudimus ante,\n\n \n\nquatinus in pullos animalis vertier ova\n\n \n\ncernimus alituum vermisque effervere terra,\n\n \n\nintempestivos quam putor cepit ob imbris,\n\n \n\nscire licet gigni posse ex non sensibus sensus.\n\n \n\nQuod si forte aliquis dicet, dum taxat oriri\n\n \n\nposse ex non sensu sensus mutabilitate,\n\n \n\naut aliquo tamquam partu quod proditur extra,\n\n \n\nhuic satis illud erit planum facere atque probare,\n\n \n\nnon fieri partum nisi concilio ante coacto,\n\n \n\nnec quicquam commutari sine conciliatu.\n\n \n\nPrincipio nequeunt ullius corporis esse\n\n \n\nsensus ante ipsam genitam naturam animantis,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia materies disiecta tenetur\n\n \n\naere fluminibus terris terraque creatis,\n\n \n\nnec congressa modo vitalis convenientes\n\n \n\ncontulit inter se motus, quibus omnituentes\n\n \n\naccensi sensus animantem quamque tuentur.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quamvis animantem grandior ictus,\n\n \n\nquam patitur natura, repente adfligit et omnis\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animi pergit confundere sensus.\n\n \n\ndissoluuntur enim positurae principiorum\n\n \n\net penitus motus vitales inpediuntur,\n\n \n\ndonec materies omnis concussa per artus\n\n \n\nvitalis animae nodos a corpore solvit\n\n \n\ndispersamque foras per caulas eiecit omnis;\n\n \n\nnam quid praeterea facere ictum posse reamur\n\n \n\noblatum, nisi discutere ac dissolvere quaeque?\n\n \n\nfit quoque uti soleant minus oblato acriter ictu\n\n \n\nreliqui motus vitalis vincere saepe,\n\n \n\nvincere et ingentis plagae sedare tumultus\n\n \n\ninque suos quicquid rursus revocare meatus\n\n \n\net quasi iam leti dominantem in corpore motum\n\n \n\ndiscutere ac paene amissos accendere sensus;\n\n \n\nnam qua re potius leti iam limine ab ipso\n\n \n\nad vitam possint conlecta mente reverti,\n\n \n\nquam quo decursum prope iam siet ire et abire?\n\n \n\nPraeterea, quoniam dolor est, ubi materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora vi quadam per viscera viva per artus\n\n \n\nsollicitata suis trepidant in sedibus intus,\n\n \n\ninque locum quando remigrant, fit blanda voluptas,\n\n \n\nscire licet nullo primordia posse dolore\n\n \n\ntemptari nullamque voluptatem capere ex se;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem non sunt ex ullis principiorum\n\n \n\ncorporibus, quorum motus novitate laborent\n\n \n\naut aliquem fructum capiant dulcedinis almae.\n\n \n\nhaut igitur debent esse ullo praedita sensu.\n\n \n\nDenique uti possint sentire animalia quaeque,\n\n \n\nprincipiis si iam est sensus tribuendus eorum,\n\n \n\nquid, genus humanum propritim de quibus auctumst?\n\n \n\nscilicet et risu tremulo concussa cachinnant\n\n \n\net lacrimis spargunt rorantibus ora genasque\n\n \n\nmultaque de rerum mixtura dicere callent\n\n \n\net sibi proporro quae sint primordia quaerunt;\n\n \n\nquando quidem totis mortalibus adsimulata\n\n \n\nipsa quoque ex aliis debent constare elementis,\n\n \n\ninde alia ex aliis, nusquam consistere ut ausis;\n\n \n\nquippe sequar, quod cumque loqui ridereque dices\n\n \n\net sapere, ex aliis eadem haec facientibus ut sit.\n\n \n\nquod si delira haec furiosaque cernimus esse\n\n \n\net ridere potest non ex ridentibus auctus,\n\n \n\net sapere et doctis rationem reddere dictis\n\n \n\nnon ex seminibus sapientibus atque disertis,\n\n \n\nqui minus esse queant ea quae sentire videmus\n\n \n\nseminibus permixta carentibus undique sensu?\n\n \n\nDenique caelesti sumus omnes semine oriundi;\n\n \n\nomnibus ille idem pater est, unde alma liquentis\n\n \n\numoris guttas mater cum terra recepit,\n\n \n\nfeta parit nitidas fruges arbustaque laeta\n\n \n\net genus humanum, parit omnia saecla ferarum,\n\n \n\npabula cum praebet, quibus omnes corpora pascunt\n\n \n\net dulcem ducunt vitam prolemque propagant;\n\n \n\nqua propter merito maternum nomen adepta est.\n\n \n\ncedit item retro, de terra quod fuit ante,\n\n \n\nin terras, et quod missumst ex aetheris oris,\n\n \n\nid rursum caeli rellatum templa receptant.\n\n \n\nnec sic interemit mors res ut materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora conficiat, sed coetum dissupat ollis;\n\n \n\ninde aliis aliud coniungit et efficit, omnis\n\n \n\nres ut convertant formas mutentque colores\n\n \n\net capiant sensus et puncto tempore reddant;\n\n \n\nut noscas referre earum primordia rerum\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali positura contineantur\n\n \n\net quos inter se dent motus accipiantque,\n\n \n\nneve putes aeterna penes residere potesse\n\n \n\ncorpora prima quod in summis fluitare videmus\n\n \n\nrebus et interdum nasci subitoque perire.\n\n \n\nquin etiam refert nostris in versibus ipsis\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali sint ordine quaeque locata;\n\n \n\nnamque eadem caelum mare terras flumina solem\n\n \n\nsignificant, eadem fruges arbusta animantis;\n\n \n\nsi non omnia sunt, at multo maxima pars est\n\n \n\nconsimilis; verum positura discrepitant res.\n\n \n\nsic ipsis in rebus item iam materiai\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nconcursus motus ordo positura figurae\n\n \n\ncum permutantur, mutari res quoque debent.\n\n \n\nNunc animum nobis adhibe veram ad rationem.\n\n \n\nnam tibi vehementer nova res molitur ad auris\n\n \n\naccedere et nova se species ostendere rerum.\n\n \n\nsed neque tam facilis res ulla est, quin ea primum\n\n \n\ndifficilis magis ad credendum constet, itemque\n\n \n\nnil adeo magnum neque tam mirabile quicquam,\n\n \n\nquod non paulatim minuant mirarier omnes,\n\n \n\nprincipio caeli clarum purumque colorem\n\n \n\nquaeque in se cohibet, palantia sidera passim,\n\n \n\nlunamque et solis praeclara luce nitorem;\n\n \n\nomnia quae nunc si primum mortalibus essent\n\n \n\nex improviso si sint obiecta repente,\n\n \n\nquid magis his rebus poterat mirabile dici,\n\n \n\naut minus ante quod auderent fore credere gentes?\n\n \n\nnil, ut opinor; ita haec species miranda fuisset.\n\n \n\nquam tibi iam nemo fessus satiate videndi,\n\n \n\nsuspicere in caeli dignatur lucida templa.\n\n \n\ndesine qua propter novitate exterritus ipsa\n\n \n\nexpuere ex animo rationem, sed magis acri\n\n \n\niudicio perpende, et si tibi vera videntur,\n\n \n\ndede manus, aut, si falsum est, accingere contra.\n\n \n\nquaerit enim rationem animus, cum summa loci sit\n\n \n\ninfinita foris haec extra moenia mundi,\n\n \n\nquid sit ibi porro, quo prospicere usque velit mens\n\n \n\natque animi iactus liber quo pervolet ipse.\n\n \n\nPrincipio nobis in cunctas undique partis\n\n \n\net latere ex utroque supra supterque per omne\n\n \n\nnulla est finis; uti docui, res ipsaque per se\n\n \n\nvociferatur, et elucet natura profundi.\n\n \n\nnullo iam pacto veri simile esse putandumst,\n\n \n\nundique cum vorsum spatium vacet infinitum\n\n \n\nseminaque innumero numero summaque profunda\n\n \n\nmultimodis volitent aeterno percita motu,\n\n \n\nhunc unum terrarum orbem caelumque creatum,\n\n \n\nnil agere illa foris tot corpora materiai;\n\n \n\ncum praesertim hic sit natura factus et ipsa\n\n \n\nsponte sua forte offensando semina rerum\n\n \n\nmultimodis temere in cassum frustraque coacta\n\n \n\ntandem coluerunt ea quae coniecta repente\n\n \n\nmagnarum rerum fierent exordia semper,\n\n \n\nterrai maris et caeli generisque animantum.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam talis fateare necesse est\n\n \n\nesse alios alibi congressus materiai,\n\n \n\nqualis hic est, avido complexu quem tenet aether.\n\n \n\nPraeterea cum materies est multa parata,\n\n \n\ncum locus est praesto nec res nec causa moratur\n\n \n\nulla, geri debent ni mirum et confieri res.\n\n \n\nnunc et seminibus si tanta est copia, quantam\n\n \n\nenumerare aetas animantum non queat omnis,\n\n \n\nquis eadem natura manet, quae semina rerum\n\n \n\nconicere in loca quaeque queat simili ratione\n\n \n\natque huc sunt coniecta, necesse est confiteare\n\n \n\nesse alios aliis terrarum in partibus orbis\n\n \n\net varias hominum gentis et saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit ut in summa res nulla sit una,\n\n \n\nunica quae gignatur et unica solaque crescat,\n\n \n\nquin aliquoius siet saecli permultaque eodem\n\n \n\nsint genere. in primis animalibus indice mente\n\n \n\ninvenies sic montivagum genus esse ferarum,\n\n \n\nsic hominum geminam prolem, sic denique mutas\n\n \n\nsquamigerum pecudes et corpora cuncta volantum.\n\n \n\nqua propter caelum simili ratione fatendumst\n\n \n\nterramque et solem, lunam mare cetera quae sunt,\n\n \n\nnon esse unica, sed numero magis innumerali;\n\n \n\nquando quidem vitae depactus terminus alte\n\n \n\ntam manet haec et tam nativo corpore constant\n\n \n\nquam genus omne, quod his generatimst rebus abundans.\n\n \n\nQuae bene cognita si teneas, natura videtur\n\n \n\nlibera continuo, dominis privata superbis,\n\n \n\nipsa sua per se sponte omnia dis agere expers.\n\n \n\nnam pro sancta deum tranquilla pectora pace\n\n \n\nquae placidum degunt aevom vitamque serenam,\n\n \n\nquis regere immensi summam, quis habere profundi\n\n \n\nindu manu validas potis est moderanter habenas,\n\n \n\nquis pariter caelos omnis convertere et omnis\n\n \n\nignibus aetheriis terras suffire feracis,\n\n \n\nomnibus inve locis esse omni tempore praesto,\n\n \n\nnubibus ut tenebras faciat caelique serena\n\n \n\nconcutiat sonitu, tum fulmina mittat et aedis\n\n \n\nsaepe suas disturbet et in deserta recedens\n\n \n\nsaeviat exercens telum, quod saepe nocentes\n\n \n\npraeterit exanimatque indignos inque merentes?\n\n \n\nMultaque post mundi tempus genitale diemque\n\n \n\nprimigenum maris et terrae solisque coortum\n\n \n\naddita corpora sunt extrinsecus, addita circum\n\n \n\nsemina, quae magnum iaculando contulit omne,\n\n \n\nunde mare et terrae possent augescere et unde\n\n \n\nappareret spatium caeli domus altaque tecta\n\n \n\ntolleret a terris procul et consurgeret aer.\n\n \n\nnam sua cuique, locis ex omnibus, omnia plagis\n\n \n\ncorpora distribuuntur et ad sua saecla recedunt,\n\n \n\numor ad umorem, terreno corpore terra\n\n \n\ncrescit et ignem ignes procudunt aetheraque aether,\n\n \n\ndonique ad extremum crescendi perfica finem\n\n \n\nomnia perduxit rerum natura creatrix;\n\n \n\nut fit ubi nihilo iam plus est quod datur intra\n\n \n\nvitalis venas quam quod fluit atque recedit.\n\n \n\nomnibus hic aetas debet consistere rebus,\n\n \n\nhic natura suis refrenat viribus auctum.\n\n \n\nnam quae cumque vides hilaro grandescere adauctu\n\n \n\npaulatimque gradus aetatis scandere adultae,\n\n \n\nplura sibi adsumunt quam de se corpora mittunt,\n\n \n\ndum facile in venas cibus omnis inditur et dum\n\n \n\nnon ita sunt late dispessa, ut multa remittant\n\n \n\net plus dispendi faciant quam vescitur aetas.\n\n \n\nnam certe fluere atque recedere corpora rebus\n\n \n\nmulta manus dandum est; sed plura accedere debent,\n\n \n\ndonec alescendi summum tetigere cacumen.\n\n \n\ninde minutatim vires et robur adultum\n\n \n\nfrangit et in partem peiorem liquitur aetas.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim quanto est res amplior, augmine adempto,\n\n \n\net quo latior est, in cunctas undique partis\n\n \n\nplura modo dispargit et a se corpora mittit,\n\n \n\nnec facile in venas cibus omnis diditur ei\n\n \n\nnec satis est, pro quam largos exaestuat aestus,\n\n \n\nunde queat tantum suboriri ac subpeditare.\n\n \n\niure igitur pereunt, cum rarefacta fluendo\n\n \n\nsunt et cum externis succumbunt omnia plagis,\n\n \n\nquando quidem grandi cibus aevo denique defit,\n\n \n\nnec tuditantia rem cessant extrinsecus ullam\n\n \n\ncorpora conficere et plagis infesta domare.\n\n \n\nSic igitur magni quoque circum moenia mundi\n\n \n\nexpugnata dabunt labem putrisque ruinas;\n\n \n\nomnia debet enim cibus integrare novando\n\n \n\net fulcire cibus, cibus omnia sustentare,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam nec venae perpetiuntur\n\n \n\nquod satis est, neque quantum opus est natura ministrat.\n\n \n\nIamque adeo fracta est aetas effetaque tellus\n\n \n\nvix animalia parva creat, quae cuncta creavit\n\n \n\nsaecla deditque ferarum ingentia corpora partu.\n\n \n\nhaud, ut opinor, enim mortalia saecla superne\n\n \n\naurea de caelo demisit funis in arva\n\n \n\nnec mare nec fluctus plangentis saxa crearunt,\n\n \n\nsed genuit tellus eadem quae nunc alit ex se.\n\n \n\npraeterea nitidas fruges vinetaque laeta\n\n \n\nsponte sua primum mortalibus ipsa creavit,\n\n \n\nipsa dedit dulcis fetus et pabula laeta;\n\n \n\nquae nunc vix nostro grandescunt aucta labore,\n\n \n\nconterimusque boves et viris agricolarum,\n\n \n\nconficimus ferrum vix arvis suppeditati:\n\n \n\nusque adeo parcunt fetus augentque laborem.\n\n \n\niamque caput quassans grandis suspirat arator\n\n \n\ncrebrius, in cassum magnos cecidisse labores,\n\n \n\net cum tempora temporibus praesentia confert\n\n \n\npraeteritis, laudat fortunas saepe parentis.\n\n \n\ntristis item vetulae vitis sator atque vietae\n\n \n\ntemporis incusat momen saeclumque fatigat,\n\n \n\net crepat, antiquum genus ut pietate repletum\n\n \n\nperfacile angustis tolerarit finibus aevom,\n\n \n\ncum minor esset agri multo modus ante viritim;\n\n \n\nnec tenet omnia paulatim tabescere et ire\n\n \n\nad capulum spatio aetatis defessa vetusto.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0001","text":"## Liber Secundus\n\n  \n\nSuave, mari magno turbantibus aequora ventis\n\n \n\ne terra magnum alterius spectare laborem;\n\n \n\nnon quia vexari quemquamst iucunda voluptas,\n\n \n\nsed quibus ipse malis careas quia cernere suavest.\n\n \n\nsuave etiam belli certamina magna tueri\n\n \n\nper campos instructa tua sine parte pericli;\n\n \n\nsed nihil dulcius est, bene quam munita tenere\n\n \n\nedita doctrina sapientum templa serena,\n\n \n\ndespicere unde queas alios passimque videre\n\n \n\nerrare atque viam palantis quaerere vitae,\n\n \n\ncertare ingenio, contendere nobilitate,\n\n \n\nnoctes atque dies niti praestante labore\n\n \n\nad summas emergere opes rerumque potiri.\n\n \n\no miseras hominum mentes, o pectora caeca!\n\n \n\nqualibus in tenebris vitae quantisque periclis\n\n \n\ndegitur hoc aevi quod cumquest!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0002","text":"nonne videre\n\n \n\nnihil aliud sibi naturam latrare, nisi ut qui\n\n \n\ncorpore seiunctus dolor absit, mente fruatur\n\n \n\niucundo sensu cura semota metuque?\n\n \n\nergo corpoream ad naturam pauca videmus\n\n \n\nesse opus omnino: quae demant cumque dolorem,\n\n \n\ndelicias quoque uti multas substernere possint\n\n \n\ngratius inter dum, neque natura ipsa requirit,\n\n \n\nsi non aurea sunt iuvenum simulacra per aedes\n\n \n\nlampadas igniferas manibus retinentia dextris,\n\n \n\nlumina nocturnis epulis ut suppeditentur,\n\n \n\nnec domus argento fulget auroque renidet\n\n \n\nnec citharae reboant laqueata aurataque templa,\n\n \n\ncum tamen inter se prostrati in gramine molli\n\n \n\npropter aquae rivum sub ramis arboris altae\n\n \n\nnon magnis opibus iucunde corpora curant,\n\n \n\npraesertim cum tempestas adridet et anni\n\n \n\ntempora conspergunt viridantis floribus herbas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0003","text":"nec calidae citius decedunt corpore febres,\n\n \n\ntextilibus si in picturis ostroque rubenti\n\n \n\niacteris, quam si in plebeia veste cubandum est.\n\n \n\nquapropter quoniam nihil nostro in corpore gazae\n\n \n\nproficiunt neque nobilitas nec gloria regni,\n\n \n\nquod super est, animo quoque nil prodesse putandum;\n\n \n\nsi non forte tuas legiones per loca campi\n\n \n\nfervere cum videas belli simulacra cientis,\n\n \n\nsubsidiis magnis et opum vi constabilitas,\n\n \n\nornatas armis stlattas pariterque animatas,\n\n \n\nhis tibi tum rebus timefactae religiones\n\n \n\neffugiunt animo pavidae mortisque timores\n\n \n\ntum vacuum pectus lincunt curaque solutum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","text":"quod si ridicula haec ludibriaque esse videmus,\n\n \n\nre veraque metus hominum curaeque sequaces\n\n \n\nnec metuunt sonitus armorum nec fera tela\n\n \n\naudacterque inter reges rerumque potentis\n\n \n\nversantur neque fulgorem reverentur ab auro\n\n \n\nnec clarum vestis splendorem purpureai,\n\n \n\nquid dubitas quin omnis sit haec rationis potestas,\n\n \n\nomnis cum in tenebris praesertim vita laboret?\n\n \n\nnam vel uti pueri trepidant atque omnia caecis\n\n \n\nin tenebris metuunt, sic nos in luce timemus\n\n \n\ninter dum, nihilo quae sunt metuenda magis quam\n\n \n\nquae pueri in tenebris pavitant finguntque futura.\n\n \n\nhunc igitur terrorem animi tenebrasque necessest\n\n \n\nnon radii solis neque lucida tela diei\n\n \n\ndiscutiant, sed naturae species ratioque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0005","text":"Nunc age, quo motu genitalia materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora res varias gignant genitasque resolvant\n\n \n\net qua vi facere id cogantur quaeque sit ollis\n\n \n\nreddita mobilitas magnum per inane meandi,\n\n \n\nexpediam: tu te dictis praebere memento.\n\n \n\nnam certe non inter se stipata cohaeret\n\n \n\nmateries, quoniam minui rem quamque videmus\n\n \n\net quasi longinquo fluere omnia cernimus aevo\n\n \n\nex oculisque vetustatem subducere nostris,\n\n \n\ncum tamen incolumis videatur summa manere\n\n \n\npropterea quia, quae decedunt corpora cuique,\n\n \n\nunde abeunt minuunt, quo venere augmine donant.\n\n \n\nilla senescere, at haec contra florescere cogunt,\n\n \n\nnec remorantur ibi. sic rerum summa novatur\n\n \n\nsemper, et inter se mortales mutua vivunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0006","text":"augescunt aliae gentes, aliae minuuntur,\n\n \n\ninque brevi spatio mutantur saecla animantum\n\n \n\net quasi cursores vitai lampada tradunt.\n\n  \n\nSi cessare putas rerum primordia posse\n\n \n\ncessandoque novos rerum progignere motus,\n\n \n\navius a vera longe ratione vagaris.\n\n \n\nnam quoniam per inane vagantur, cuncta necessest\n\n \n\naut gravitate sua ferri primordia rerum\n\n \n\naut ictu forte alterius. nam cum cita saepe\n\n \n\nobvia conflixere, fit ut diversa repente\n\n \n\ndissiliant; neque enim mirum, durissima quae sint\n\n \n\nponderibus solidis neque quicquam a tergibus obstet.\n\n \n\net quo iactari magis omnia materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora pervideas, reminiscere totius imum\n\n \n\nnil esse in summa, neque habere ubi corpora prima\n\n \n\nconsistant, quoniam spatium sine fine modoquest\n\n \n\ninmensumque patere in cunctas undique partis\n\n \n\npluribus ostendi et certa ratione probatumst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0007","text":"quod quoniam constat, ni mirum nulla quies est\n\n \n\nreddita corporibus primis per inane profundum,\n\n \n\nsed magis adsiduo varioque exercita motu\n\n \n\npartim intervallis magnis confulta resultant,\n\n \n\npars etiam brevibus spatiis vexantur ab ictu.\n\n \n\net quae cumque magis condenso conciliatu\n\n \n\nexiguis intervallis convecta resultant,\n\n \n\nindupedita suis perplexis ipsa figuris,\n\n \n\nhaec validas saxi radices et fera ferri\n\n \n\ncorpora constituunt et cetera de genere horum.\n\n \n\npaucula quae porro magnum per inane vagantur,\n\n \n\ncetera dissiliunt longe longeque recursant\n\n \n\nin magnis intervallis; haec aera rarum\n\n \n\nsufficiunt nobis et splendida lumina solis.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea magnum per inane vagantur,\n\n \n\nconciliis rerum quae sunt reiecta nec usquam\n\n \n\nconsociare etiam motus potuere recepta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0008","text":"Cuius, uti memoro, rei simulacrum et imago\n\n \n\nante oculos semper nobis versatur et instat.\n\n \n\ncontemplator enim, cum solis lumina cumque\n\n \n\ninserti fundunt radii per opaca domorum:\n\n \n\nmulta minuta modis multis per inane videbis\n\n \n\ncorpora misceri radiorum lumine in ipso\n\n \n\net vel ut aeterno certamine proelia pugnas\n\n \n\nedere turmatim certantia nec dare pausam,\n\n \n\nconciliis et discidiis exercita crebris;\n\n \n\nconicere ut possis ex hoc, primordia rerum\n\n \n\nquale sit in magno iactari semper inani.\n\n \n\ndum taxat, rerum magnarum parva potest res\n\n \n\nexemplare dare et vestigia notitiai.\n\n \n\nHoc etiam magis haec animum te advertere par est\n\n \n\ncorpora quae in solis radiis turbare videntur,\n\n \n\nquod tales turbae motus quoque materiai\n\n \n\nsignificant clandestinos caecosque subesse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0009","text":"multa videbis enim plagis ibi percita caecis\n\n \n\ncommutare viam retroque repulsa reverti\n\n \n\nnunc huc nunc illuc in cunctas undique partis.\n\n \n\nscilicet hic a principiis est omnibus error.\n\n \n\nprima moventur enim per se primordia rerum,\n\n \n\ninde ea quae parvo sunt corpora conciliatu\n\n \n\net quasi proxima sunt ad viris principiorum,\n\n \n\nictibus illorum caecis inpulsa cientur,\n\n \n\nipsaque proporro paulo maiora lacessunt.\n\n \n\nsic a principiis ascendit motus et exit\n\n \n\npaulatim nostros ad sensus, ut moveantur\n\n \n\nilla quoque, in solis quae lumine cernere quimus\n\n \n\nnec quibus id faciant plagis apparet aperte.\n\n  \n\nNunc quae mobilitas sit reddita materiai\n\n \n\ncorporibus, paucis licet hinc cognoscere, Memmi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0010","text":"primum aurora novo cum spargit lumine terras\n\n \n\net variae volucres nemora avia pervolitantes\n\n \n\naera per tenerum liquidis loca vocibus opplent,\n\n \n\nquam subito soleat sol ortus tempore tali\n\n \n\nconvestire sua perfundens omnia luce,\n\n \n\nomnibus in promptu manifestumque esse videmus.\n\n \n\nat vapor is, quem sol mittit, lumenque serenum\n\n \n\nnon per inane meat vacuum; quo tardius ire\n\n \n\ncogitur, aerias quasi dum diverberat undas;\n\n \n\nnec singillatim corpuscula quaeque vaporis\n\n \n\nsed complexa meant inter se conque globata;\n\n \n\nqua propter simul inter se retrahuntur et extra\n\n \n\nofficiuntur, uti cogantur tardius ire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0011","text":"at quae sunt solida primordia simplicitate,\n\n \n\ncum per inane meant vacuum nec res remoratur\n\n \n\nulla foris atque ipsa suis e partibus unum,\n\n \n\nunum, in quem coepere, locum conixa feruntur,\n\n \n\ndebent ni mirum praecellere mobilitate\n\n \n\net multo citius ferri quam lumina solis\n\n \n\nmultiplexque loci spatium transcurrere eodem\n\n \n\ntempore quo solis pervolgant fulgura caelum.\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nnec persectari primordia singula quaeque,\n\n \n\nut videant qua quicque geratur cum ratione.\n\n \n\nAt quidam contra haec, ignari materiai,\n\n \n\nnaturam non posse deum sine numine reddunt\n\n \n\ntanto opere humanis rationibus atmoderate\n\n \n\ntempora mutare annorum frugesque creare\n\n \n\net iam cetera, mortalis quae suadet adire\n\n \n\nipsaque deducit dux vitae dia voluptas\n\n \n\net res per Veneris blanditur saecla propagent,\n\n \n\nne genus occidat humanum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0012","text":"quorum omnia causa\n\n \n\nconstituisse deos cum fingunt, omnibus rebus\n\n \n\nmagno opere a vera lapsi ratione videntur.\n\n \n\nnam quamvis rerum ignorem primordia quae sint,\n\n \n\nhoc tamen ex ipsis caeli rationibus ausim\n\n \n\nconfirmare aliisque ex rebus reddere multis,\n\n \n\nnequaquam nobis divinitus esse creatam\n\n \n\nnaturam mundi: tanta stat praedita culpa.\n\n \n\nquae tibi posterius, Memmi, faciemus aperta;\n\n \n\nnunc id quod super est de motibus expediemus.\n\n \n\nNunc locus est, ut opinor, in his illud quoque rebus\n\n \n\nconfirmare tibi, nullam rem posse sua vi\n\n \n\ncorpoream sursum ferri sursumque meare.\n\n \n\nne tibi dent in eo flammarum corpora frudem;\n\n \n\nsursus enim versus gignuntur et augmina sumunt\n\n \n\net sursum nitidae fruges arbustaque crescunt,\n\n \n\npondera, quantum in se est, cum deorsum cuncta ferantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0013","text":"nec cum subsiliunt ignes ad tecta domorum\n\n \n\net celeri flamma degustant tigna trabesque,\n\n \n\nsponte sua facere id sine vi subiecta putandum est.\n\n \n\nquod genus e nostro com missus corpore sanguis\n\n \n\nemicat exultans alte spargitque cruorem.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam quanta vi tigna trabesque\n\n \n\nrespuat umor aquae? nam quo magis ursimus altum\n\n \n\nderecta et magna vi multi pressimus aegre,\n\n \n\ntam cupide sursum removet magis atque remittit,\n\n \n\nplus ut parte foras emergant exiliantque.\n\n \n\nnec tamen haec, quantum est in se, dubitamus, opinor,\n\n \n\nquin vacuum per inane deorsum cuncta ferantur.\n\n \n\nsic igitur debent flammae quoque posse per auras\n\n \n\naeris expressae sursum succedere, quamquam\n\n \n\npondera, quantum in se est, deorsum deducere pugnent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0014","text":"nocturnasque faces caeli sublime volantis\n\n \n\nnonne vides longos flammarum ducere tractus\n\n \n\nin quas cumque dedit partis natura meatum?\n\n \n\nnon cadere in terras stellas et sidera cernis?\n\n \n\nsol etiam caeli de vertice dissipat omnis\n\n \n\nardorem in partis et lumine conserit arva;\n\n \n\nin terras igitur quoque solis vergitur ardor.\n\n \n\ntransversosque volare per imbris fulmina cernis,\n\n \n\nnunc hinc nunc illinc abrupti nubibus ignes\n\n \n\nconcursant; cadit in terras vis flammea volgo.\n\n \n\nIllud in his quoque te rebus cognoscere avemus,\n\n \n\ncorpora cum deorsum rectum per inane feruntur\n\n \n\nponderibus propriis, incerto tempore ferme\n\n \n\nincertisque locis spatio depellere paulum,\n\n \n\ntantum quod momen mutatum dicere possis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0015","text":"quod nisi declinare solerent, omnia deorsum\n\n \n\nimbris uti guttae caderent per inane profundum\n\n \n\nnec foret offensus natus nec plaga creata\n\n \n\nprincipiis; ita nihil umquam natura creasset.\n\n \n\nQuod si forte aliquis credit graviora potesse\n\n \n\ncorpora, quo citius rectum per inane feruntur,\n\n \n\nincidere ex supero levioribus atque ita plagas\n\n \n\ngignere, quae possint genitalis reddere motus,\n\n \n\navius a vera longe ratione recedit.\n\n \n\nnam per aquas quae cumque cadunt atque aera rarum,\n\n \n\nhaec pro ponderibus casus celerare necessest\n\n \n\npropterea quia corpus aquae naturaque tenvis\n\n \n\naeris haud possunt aeque rem quamque morari,\n\n \n\nsed citius cedunt gravioribus exsuperata;\n\n \n\nat contra nulli de nulla parte neque ullo\n\n \n\ntempore inane potest vacuum subsistere rei,\n\n \n\nquin, sua quod natura petit, concedere pergat;\n\n \n\nomnia qua propter debent per inane quietum\n\n \n\naeque ponderibus non aequis concita ferri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0016","text":"haud igitur poterunt levioribus incidere umquam\n\n \n\nex supero graviora neque ictus gignere per se,\n\n \n\nqui varient motus, per quos natura gerat res.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam paulum inclinare necessest\n\n \n\ncorpora; nec plus quam minimum, ne fingere motus\n\n \n\nobliquos videamur et id res vera refutet.\n\n \n\nnamque hoc in promptu manifestumque esse videmus,\n\n \n\npondera, quantum in se est, non posse obliqua meare,\n\n \n\nex supero cum praecipitant, quod cernere possis;\n\n \n\nsed nihil omnino recta regione viai\n\n \n\ndeclinare quis est qui possit cernere sese?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0017","text":"Denique si semper motu conectitur omnis\n\n \n\net vetere exoritur motus novus ordine certo\n\n \n\nnec declinando faciunt primordia motus\n\n \n\nprincipium quoddam, quod fati foedera rumpat,\n\n \n\nex infinito ne causam causa sequatur,\n\n \n\nlibera per terras unde haec animantibus exstat,\n\n \n\nunde est haec, inquam, fatis avolsa voluntas,\n\n \n\nper quam progredimur quo ducit quemque voluptas,\n\n \n\ndeclinamus item motus nec tempore certo\n\n \n\nnec regione loci certa, sed ubi ipsa tulit mens?\n\n \n\nnam dubio procul his rebus sua cuique voluntas\n\n \n\nprincipium dat et hinc motus per membra rigantur.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam patefactis tempore puncto\n\n \n\ncarceribus non posse tamen prorumpere equorum\n\n \n\nvim cupidam tam de subito quam mens avet ipsa?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0018","text":"omnis enim totum per corpus materiai\n\n \n\ncopia conciri debet, concita per artus\n\n \n\nomnis ut studium mentis conixa sequatur;\n\n \n\nut videas initum motus a corde creari\n\n \n\nex animique voluntate id procedere primum,\n\n \n\ninde dari porro per totum corpus et artus.\n\n \n\nnec similest ut cum inpulsi procedimus ictu\n\n \n\nviribus alterius magnis magnoque coactu;\n\n \n\nnam tum materiem totius corporis omnem\n\n \n\nperspicuumst nobis invitis ire rapique,\n\n \n\ndonec eam refrenavit per membra voluntas.\n\n \n\niamne vides igitur, quamquam vis extera multos\n\n \n\npellat et invitos cogat procedere saepe\n\n \n\npraecipitesque rapi, tamen esse in pectore nostro\n\n \n\nquiddam quod contra pugnare obstareque possit?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0019","text":"cuius ad arbitrium quoque copia materiai\n\n \n\ncogitur inter dum flecti per membra per artus\n\n \n\net proiecta refrenatur retroque residit.\n\n \n\nquare in seminibus quoque idem fateare necessest,\n\n \n\nesse aliam praeter plagas et pondera causam\n\n \n\nmotibus, unde haec est nobis innata potestas,\n\n \n\nde nihilo quoniam fieri nihil posse videmus.\n\n \n\npondus enim prohibet ne plagis omnia fiant\n\n \n\nexterna quasi vi; sed ne res ipsa necessum\n\n \n\nintestinum habeat cunctis in rebus agendis\n\n \n\net devicta quasi cogatur ferre patique,\n\n \n\nid facit exiguum clinamen principiorum\n\n \n\nnec regione loci certa nec tempore certo.\n\n \n\nNec stipata magis fuit umquam materiai\n\n \n\ncopia nec porro maioribus intervallis;\n\n \n\nnam neque adaugescit quicquam neque deperit inde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0020","text":"qua propter quo nunc in motu principiorum\n\n \n\ncorpora sunt, in eodem ante acta aetate fuere\n\n \n\net post haec semper simili ratione ferentur,\n\n \n\net quae consuerint gigni gignentur eadem\n\n \n\ncondicione et erunt et crescent vique valebunt,\n\n \n\nquantum cuique datum est per foedera naturai.\n\n \n\nnec rerum summam commutare ulla potest vis;\n\n \n\nnam neque quo possit genus ullum materiai\n\n \n\neffugere ex omni quicquam est extra, neque in omne\n\n \n\nunde coorta queat nova vis inrumpere et omnem\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum mutare et vertere motus.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus non est mirabile, quare,\n\n \n\nomnia cum rerum primordia sint in motu,\n\n \n\nsumma tamen summa videatur stare quiete,\n\n \n\npraeter quam siquid proprio dat corpore motus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0021","text":"omnis enim longe nostris ab sensibus infra\n\n \n\nprimorum natura iacet; qua propter, ubi ipsa\n\n \n\ncernere iam nequeas, motus quoque surpere debent;\n\n \n\npraesertim cum, quae possimus cernere, celent\n\n \n\nsaepe tamen motus spatio diducta locorum.\n\n \n\nnam saepe in colli tondentes pabula laeta\n\n \n\nlanigerae reptant pecudes, quo quamque vocantes\n\n \n\ninvitant herbae gemmantes rore recenti,\n\n \n\net satiati agni ludunt blandeque coruscant;\n\n \n\nomnia quae nobis longe confusa videntur\n\n \n\net velut in viridi candor consistere colli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0022","text":"praeterea magnae legiones cum loca cursu\n\n \n\ncamporum complent belli simulacra cientes,\n\n \n\nfulgor ubi ad caelum se tollit totaque circum\n\n \n\naere renidescit tellus supterque virum vi\n\n \n\nexcitur pedibus sonitus clamoreque montes\n\n \n\nicti reiectant voces ad sidera mundi\n\n \n\net circum volitant equites mediosque repente\n\n \n\ntramittunt valido quatientes impete campos;\n\n \n\net tamen est quidam locus altis montibus, unde\n\n \n\nstare videntur et in campis consistere fulgor.\n\n \n\nNunc age, iam deinceps cunctarum exordia rerum\n\n \n\nqualia sint et quam longe distantia formis,\n\n \n\npercipe, multigenis quam sint variata figuris;\n\n \n\nnon quo multa parum simili sint praedita forma,\n\n \n\nsed quia non volgo paria omnibus omnia constant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0023","text":"nec mirum; nam cum sit eorum copia tanta,\n\n \n\nut neque finis, uti docui, neque summa sit ulla,\n\n \n\ndebent ni mirum non omnibus omnia prorsum\n\n \n\nesse pari filo similique adfecta figura.\n\n \n\nPraeterea genus humanum mutaeque natantes\n\n \n\nsquamigerum pecudes et laeta armenta feraeque\n\n \n\net variae volucres, laetantia quae loca aquarum\n\n \n\nconcelebrant circum ripas fontisque lacusque,\n\n \n\net quae pervolgant nemora avia pervolitantes,\n\n \n\nquorum unum quidvis generatim sumere perge;\n\n \n\ninvenies tamen inter se differre figuris.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia proles cognoscere matrem\n\n \n\nnec mater posset prolem; quod posse videmus\n\n \n\nnec minus atque homines inter se nota cluere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0024","text":"nam saepe ante deum vitulus delubra decora\n\n \n\nturicremas propter mactatus concidit aras\n\n \n\nsanguinis expirans calidum de pectore flumen;\n\n \n\nat mater viridis saltus orbata peragrans\n\n \n\nnovit humi pedibus vestigia pressa bisulcis,\n\n \n\nomnia convisens oculis loca, si queat usquam\n\n \n\nconspicere amissum fetum, completque querellis\n\n \n\nfrondiferum nemus adsistens et crebra revisit\n\n \n\nad stabulum desiderio perfixa iuvenci,\n\n \n\nnec tenerae salices atque herbae rore vigentes\n\n \n\nfluminaque ulla queunt summis labentia ripis\n\n \n\noblectare animum subitamque avertere curam,\n\n \n\nnec vitulorum aliae species per pabula laeta\n\n \n\nderivare queunt animum curaque levare;\n\n \n\nusque adeo quiddam proprium notumque requirit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0025","text":"praeterea teneri tremulis cum vocibus haedi\n\n \n\ncornigeras norunt matres agnique petulci\n\n \n\nbalantum pecudes; ita, quod natura resposcit,\n\n \n\nad sua quisque fere decurrunt ubera lactis.\n\n \n\nPostremo quodvis frumentum non tamen omne\n\n \n\nquidque suo genere inter se simile esse videbis,\n\n \n\nquin intercurrat quaedam distantia formis.\n\n \n\nconcharumque genus parili ratione videmus\n\n \n\npingere telluris gremium, qua mollibus undis\n\n \n\nlitoris incurvi bibulam pavit aequor harenam.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam simili ratione necessest,\n\n \n\nnatura quoniam constant neque facta manu sunt\n\n \n\nunius ad certam formam primordia rerum,\n\n \n\ndissimili inter se quaedam volitare figura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0026","text":"Perfacile est animi ratione exsolvere nobis\n\n \n\nquare fulmineus multo penetralior ignis\n\n \n\nquam noster fluat e taedis terrestribus ortus;\n\n \n\ndicere enim possis caelestem fulminis ignem\n\n \n\nsubtilem magis e parvis constare figuris\n\n \n\natque ideo transire foramina quae nequit ignis\n\n \n\nnoster hic e lignis ortus taedaque creatus.\n\n \n\npraeterea lumen per cornum transit, at imber\n\n \n\nrespuitur. quare, nisi luminis illa minora\n\n \n\ncorpora sunt quam de quibus est liquor almus aquarum?\n\n \n\net quamvis subito per colum vina videmus\n\n \n\nperfluere, at contra tardum cunctatur olivom,\n\n \n\naut quia ni mirum maioribus est elementis\n\n \n\naut magis hamatis inter se perque plicatis,\n\n \n\natque ideo fit uti non tam diducta repente\n\n \n\ninter se possint primordia singula quaeque\n\n \n\nsingula per cuiusque foramina permanare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0027","text":"Huc accedit uti mellis lactisque liquores\n\n \n\niucundo sensu linguae tractentur in ore;\n\n \n\nat contra taetra absinthi natura ferique\n\n \n\ncentauri foedo pertorquent ora sapore;\n\n \n\nut facile agnoscas e levibus atque rutundis\n\n \n\nesse ea quae sensus iucunde tangere possunt,\n\n \n\nat contra quae amara atque aspera cumque videntur,\n\n \n\nhaec magis hamatis inter se nexa teneri\n\n \n\nproptereaque solere vias rescindere nostris\n\n \n\nsensibus introituque suo perrumpere corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0028","text":"omnia postremo bona sensibus et mala tactu\n\n \n\ndissimili inter se pugnant perfecta figura;\n\n \n\nne tu forte putes serrae stridentis acerbum\n\n \n\nhorrorem constare elementis levibus aeque\n\n \n\nac musaea mele, per chordas organici quae\n\n \n\nmobilibus digitis expergefacta figurant;\n\n \n\nneu simili penetrare putes primordia forma\n\n \n\nin nares hominum, cum taetra cadavera torrent,\n\n \n\net cum scena croco Cilici perfusa recens est\n\n \n\naraque Panchaeos exhalat propter odores;\n\n \n\nneve bonos rerum simili constare colores\n\n \n\nsemine constituas, oculos qui pascere possunt,\n\n \n\net qui conpungunt aciem lacrimareque cogunt\n\n \n\naut foeda specie foedi turpesque videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0029","text":"omnis enim, sensus quae mulcet cumque, tibi res\n\n \n\nhaut sine principiali aliquo levore creatast;\n\n \n\nat contra quae cumque molesta atque aspera constat,\n\n \n\nnon aliquo sine materiae squalore repertast.\n\n \n\nSunt etiam quae iam nec levia iure putantur\n\n \n\nesse neque omnino flexis mucronibus unca,\n\n \n\nsed magis angellis paulum prostantibus, ut quae\n\n \n\ntitillare magis sensus quam laedere possint,\n\n \n\nfecula iam quo de genere est inulaeque sapores.\n\n \n\nDenique iam calidos ignis gelidamque pruinam\n\n \n\ndissimili dentata modo conpungere sensus\n\n \n\ncorporis, indicio nobis est tactus uterque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0030","text":"tactus enim, tactus, pro divum numina sancta,\n\n \n\ncorporis est sensus, vel cum res extera sese\n\n \n\ninsinuat, vel cum laedit quae in corpore natast\n\n \n\naut iuvat egrediens genitalis per Veneris res,\n\n \n\naut ex offensu cum turbant corpore in ipso,\n\n \n\nsemina confundunt inter se concita sensum;\n\n \n\nut si forte manu quamvis iam corporis ipse\n\n \n\ntute tibi partem ferias atque experiare.\n\n \n\nqua propter longe formas distare necessest\n\n \n\nprincipiis, varios quae possint edere sensus.\n\n \n\nDenique quae nobis durata ac spissa videntur,\n\n \n\nhaec magis hamatis inter sese esse necessest\n\n \n\net quasi ramosis alte compacta teneri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0031","text":"in quo iam genere in primis adamantina saxa\n\n \n\nprima acie constant ictus contemnere sueta\n\n \n\net validi silices ac duri robora ferri\n\n \n\naeraque quae claustris restantia vociferantur.\n\n \n\nilla quidem debent e levibus atque rutundis\n\n \n\nesse magis, fluvido quae corpore liquida constant.\n\n \n\nnamque papaveris haustus itemst facilis quod aquarum;\n\n \n\nnec retinentur enim inter se glomeramina quaeque\n\n \n\net perculsus item proclive volubilis exstat.\n\n \n\nomnia postremo quae puncto tempore cernis\n\n \n\ndiffugere ut fumum nebulas flammasque, necessest,\n\n \n\nsi minus omnia sunt e levibus atque rotundis,\n\n \n\nat non esse tamen perplexis indupedita,\n\n \n\npungere uti possint corpus penetrareque saxa,\n\n \n\nnec tamen haerere inter se; quod cumque videmus\n\n \n\nsensibus dentatum, facile ut cognoscere possis\n\n \n\nnon e perplexis, sed acutis esse elementis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0032","text":"sed quod amara vides eadem quae fluvida constant,\n\n \n\nsudor uti maris est, minime mirabile debet\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nnam quod fluvidus est, e levibus atque rotundis\n\n \n\nest, sed levibus sunt hamata admixta doloris\n\n \n\ncorpora. nec tamen haec retineri hamata necessust:\n\n \n\nscilicet esse globosa tamen, cum squalida constent,\n\n \n\nprovolvi simul ut possint et laedere sensus.\n\n \n\net quo mixta putes magis aspera levibus esse\n\n \n\nprincipiis, unde est Neptuni corpus acerbum,\n\n \n\nest ratio secernendi seorsumque videndi,\n\n \n\numor dulcis ubi per terras crebrius idem\n\n \n\npercolatur, ut in foveam fluat ac mansuescat;\n\n \n\nlinquit enim supera taetri primordia viri,\n\n \n\naspera quo magis in terris haerescere possint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0033","text":"Quod quoniam docui, pergam conectere rem quae\n\n \n\nex hoc apta fidem ducat, primordia rerum\n\n \n\nfinita variare figurarum ratione.\n\n \n\nquod si non ita sit, rursum iam semina quaedam\n\n \n\nesse infinito debebunt corporis auctu.\n\n \n\nnamque in eadem una cuiusvis iam brevitate\n\n \n\ncorporis inter se multum variare figurae\n\n \n\nnon possunt. fac enim minimis e partibus esse\n\n \n\ncorpora prima tribus, vel paulo pluribus auge;\n\n \n\nnempe ubi eas partis unius corporis omnis,\n\n \n\nsumma atque ima locans, transmutans dextera laevis,\n\n \n\nomnimodis expertus eris, quam quisque det ordo\n\n \n\nformai speciem totius corporis eius,\n\n \n\nquod super est, si forte voles variare figuras,\n\n \n\naddendum partis alias erit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0034","text":"inde sequetur,\n\n \n\nadsimili ratione alias ut postulet ordo,\n\n \n\nsi tu forte voles etiam variare figuras.\n\n \n\nergo formarum novitatem corporis augmen\n\n \n\nsubsequitur. quare non est ut credere possis\n\n \n\nesse infinitis distantia semina formis,\n\n \n\nne quaedam cogas inmani maximitate\n\n \n\nesse, supra quod iam docui non posse probari.\n\n \n\niam tibi barbaricae vestes Meliboeaque fulgens\n\n \n\npurpura Thessalico concharum tacta colore,\n\n \n\naurea pavonum ridenti imbuta lepore\n\n \n\nsaecla novo rerum superata colore iacerent\n\n \n\net contemptus odor smyrnae mellisque sapores,\n\n \n\net cycnea mele Phoebeaque daedala chordis\n\n \n\ncarmina consimili ratione oppressa silerent;\n\n \n\nnamque aliis aliud praestantius exoreretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0035","text":"cedere item retro possent in deteriores\n\n \n\nomnia sic partis, ut diximus in melioris;\n\n \n\nnamque aliis aliud retro quoque taetrius esset\n\n \n\nnaribus auribus atque oculis orisque sapori.\n\n \n\nquae quoniam non sunt, sed rebus reddita certa\n\n \n\nfinis utrimque tenet summam, fateare necessest\n\n \n\nmateriem quoque finitis differe figuris.\n\n \n\ndenique ab ignibus ad gelidas hiemum usque pruinas\n\n \n\nfinitumst retroque pari ratione remensumst.\n\n \n\nomnis enim calor ac frigus mediique tepores\n\n \n\ninterutrasque iacent explentes ordine summam.\n\n \n\nergo finita distant ratione creata,\n\n \n\nancipiti quoniam mucroni utrimque notantur,\n\n \n\nhinc flammis illinc rigidis infesta pruinis.\n\n \n\nQuod quoniam docui, pergam conectere rem quae\n\n \n\nex hoc apta fidem ducat, primordia rerum,\n\n \n\ninter se simili quae sunt perfecta figura,\n\n \n\ninfinita cluere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0036","text":"etenim distantia cum sit\n\n \n\nformarum finita, necesse est quae similes sint\n\n \n\nesse infinitas aut summam materiai\n\n \n\nfinitam constare, id quod non esse probavi.\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nversibus ostendam corpuscula materiai\n\n \n\nex infinito summam rerum usque tenere\n\n \n\nundique protelo plagarum continuato.\n\n \n\nnam quod rara vides magis esse animalia quaedam\n\n \n\nfecundamque magis naturam cernis in illis,\n\n \n\nat regione locoque alio terrisque remotis\n\n \n\nmulta licet genere esse in eo numerumque repleri;\n\n \n\nsicut quadripedum cum primis esse videmus\n\n \n\nin genere anguimanus elephantos, India quorum\n\n \n\nmilibus e multis vallo munitur eburno,\n\n \n\nut penitus nequeat penetrari: tanta ferarum\n\n \n\nvis est, quarum nos perpauca exempla videmus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0037","text":"sed tamen id quoque uti concedam, quam lubet esto\n\n \n\nunica res quaedem nativo corpore sola,\n\n \n\ncui similis toto terrarum non sit, in orbi;\n\n \n\ninfinita tamen nisi erit vis materiai,\n\n \n\nunde ea progigni possit concepta, creari\n\n \n\nnon poterit neque, quod super est, procrescere alique.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim sumant alii finita per omne\n\n \n\ncorpora iactari unius genitalia rei,\n\n \n\nunde ubi qua vi et quo pacto congressa coibunt\n\n \n\nmateriae tanto in pelago turbaque aliena?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0038","text":"non, ut opinor, habent rationem conciliandi:\n\n \n\nsed quasi naufragiis magnis multisque coortis\n\n \n\ndisiactare solet magnum mare transtra cavernas\n\n \n\nantemnas prorem malos tonsasque natantis,\n\n \n\nper terrarum omnis oras fluitantia aplustra\n\n \n\nut videantur et indicium mortalibus edant,\n\n \n\ninfidi maris insidias virisque dolumque\n\n \n\nut vitare velint, neve ullo tempore credant,\n\n \n\nsubdola cum ridet placidi pellacia ponti,\n\n \n\nsic tibi si finita semel primordia quaedam\n\n \n\nconstitues, aevom debebunt sparsa per omnem\n\n \n\ndisiectare aestus diversi materiai,\n\n \n\nnumquam in concilium ut possint compulsa coire\n\n \n\nnec remorari in concilio nec crescere adaucta;\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque palam fieri manifesta docet res,\n\n \n\net res progigni et genitas procrescere posse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0039","text":"esse igitur genere in quovis primordia rerum\n\n \n\ninfinita palam est, unde omnia suppeditantur.\n\n \n\nNec superare queunt motus itaque exitiales\n\n \n\nperpetuo neque in aeternum sepelire salutem,\n\n \n\nnec porro rerum genitales auctificique\n\n \n\nmotus perpetuo possunt servare creata.\n\n \n\nsic aequo geritur certamine principiorum\n\n \n\nex infinito contractum tempore bellum.\n\n \n\nnunc hic nunc illic superant vitalia rerum\n\n \n\net superantur item. miscetur funere vagor,\n\n \n\nquem pueri tollunt visentis luminis oras;\n\n \n\nnec nox ulla diem neque noctem aurora secutast,\n\n \n\nquae non audierit mixtos vagitibus aegris\n\n \n\nploratus, mortis comites et funeris atri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0040","text":"Illud in his obsignatum quoque rebus habere\n\n \n\nconvenit et memori mandatum mente tenere,\n\n \n\nnil esse, in promptu quorum natura videtur,\n\n \n\nquod genere ex uno consistat principiorum,\n\n \n\nnec quicquam quod non permixto semine constet.\n\n \n\net quod cumque magis vis multas possidet in se\n\n \n\natque potestates, ita plurima principiorum\n\n \n\nin sese genera ac varias docet esse figuras.\n\n \n\nPrincipio tellus habet in se corpora prima,\n\n \n\nunde mare inmensum volventes frigora fontes\n\n \n\nadsidue renovent, habet ignes unde oriantur;\n\n \n\nnam multis succensa locis ardent sola terrae,\n\n \n\nex imis vero furit ignibus impetus Aetnae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0041","text":"tum porro nitidas fruges arbustaque laeta\n\n \n\ngentibus humanis habet unde extollere possit,\n\n \n\nunde etiam fluvios frondes et pabula laeta\n\n \n\nmontivago generi possit praebere ferarum.\n\n \n\nquare magna deum mater materque ferarum\n\n \n\net nostri genetrix haec dicta est corporis una.\n\n \n\nHanc veteres Graium docti cecinere poetae\n\n \n\nsedibus in curru biiugos agitare leones,\n\n \n\naeris in spatio magnam pendere docentes\n\n \n\ntellurem neque posse in terra sistere terram.\n\n \n\nadiunxere feras, quia quamvis effera proles\n\n \n\nofficiis debet molliri victa parentum.\n\n \n\nmuralique caput summum cinxere corona,\n\n \n\neximiis munita locis quia sustinet urbes.\n\n \n\nquo nunc insigni per magnas praedita terras\n\n \n\nhorrifice fertur divinae matris imago.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0042","text":"hanc variae gentes antiquo more sacrorum\n\n \n\nIdaeam vocitant matrem Phrygiasque catervas\n\n \n\ndant comites, quia primum ex illis finibus edunt\n\n \n\nper terrarum orbes fruges coepisse creari.\n\n \n\nGallos attribuunt, quia, numen qui violarint\n\n \n\nMatris et ingrati genitoribus inventi sint,\n\n \n\nsignificare volunt indignos esse putandos,\n\n \n\nvivam progeniem qui in oras luminis edant.\n\n \n\ntympana tenta tonant palmis et cymbala circum\n\n \n\nconcava, raucisonoque minantur cornua cantu,\n\n \n\net Phrygio stimulat numero cava tibia mentis,\n\n \n\ntelaque praeportant, violenti signa furoris,\n\n \n\ningratos animos atque impia pectora volgi\n\n \n\nconterrere metu quae possint numine divae.\n\n \n\nergo cum primum magnas invecta per urbis\n\n \n\nmunificat tacita mortalis muta salute,\n\n \n\naere atque argento sternunt iter omne viarum\n\n \n\nlargifica stipe ditantes ninguntque rosarum\n\n \n\nfloribus umbrantes matrem comitumque catervam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0043","text":"hic armata manus, Curetas nomine Grai\n\n \n\nquos memorant, Phrygias inter si forte catervas\n\n \n\nludunt in numerumque exultant sanguine laeti\n\n \n\nterrificas capitum quatientes numine cristas,\n\n \n\nDictaeos referunt Curetas, qui Iovis illum\n\n \n\nvagitum in Creta quondam occultasse feruntur,\n\n \n\ncum pueri circum puerum pernice chorea\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\narmati in numerum pulsarent aeribus aera,\n\n \n\nne Saturnus eum malis mandaret adeptus\n\n \n\naeternumque daret matri sub pectore volnus.\n\n \n\npropterea magnam armati matrem comitantur,\n\n \n\naut quia significant divam praedicere ut armis\n\n \n\nac virtute velint patriam defendere terram\n\n \n\npraesidioque parent decorique parentibus esse.\n\n \n\nquae bene et eximie quamvis disposta ferantur,\n\n \n\nlonge sunt tamen a vera ratione repulsa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0044","text":"omnis enim per se divom natura necessest\n\n \n\ninmortali aevo summa cum pace fruatur\n\n \n\nsemota ab nostris rebus seiunctaque longe;\n\n \n\nnam privata dolore omni, privata periclis,\n\n \n\nipsa suis pollens opibus, nihil indiga nostri,\n\n \n\nnec bene promeritis capitur neque tangitur ira.\n\n \n\nterra quidem vero caret omni tempore sensu,\n\n \n\net quia multarum potitur primordia rerum,\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis effert in lumina solis.\n\n \n\nhic siquis mare Neptunum Cereremque vocare\n\n \n\nconstituet fruges et Bacchi nomine abuti\n\n \n\nmavolt quam laticis proprium proferre vocamen,\n\n \n\nconcedamus ut hic terrarum dictitet orbem\n\n \n\nesse deum matrem, dum vera re tamen ipse\n\n \n\nreligione animum turpi contingere parcat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0045","text":"Saepe itaque ex uno tondentes gramina campo\n\n \n\nlanigerae pecudes et equorum duellica proles\n\n \n\nbuceriaeque greges eodem sub tegmine caeli\n\n \n\nex unoque sitim sedantes flumine aquai\n\n \n\ndissimili vivont specie retinentque parentum\n\n \n\nnaturam et mores generatim quaeque imitantur.\n\n \n\ntanta est in quovis genere herbae materiai\n\n \n\ndissimilis ratio, tanta est in flumine quoque.\n\n \n\nHinc porro quamvis animantem ex omnibus unam\n\n \n\nossa cruor venae calor umor viscera nervi\n\n \n\nconstituunt, quae sunt porro distantia longe,\n\n \n\ndissimili perfecta figura principiorum.\n\n \n\nTum porro quae cumque igni flammata cremantur.\n\n \n\nsi nil praeterea, tamen haec in corpore tradunt,\n\n \n\nunde ignem iacere et lumen submittere possint\n\n \n\nscintillasque agere ac late differre favillam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0046","text":"cetera consimili mentis ratione peragrans\n\n \n\ninvenies igitur multarum semina rerum\n\n \n\ncorpore celare et varias cohibere figuras.\n\n \n\nDenique multa vides, quibus et color et sapor una\n\n \n\nreddita sunt cum odore in primis pleraque poma.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur variis debent constare figuris;\n\n \n\nnidor enim penetrat qua fucus non it in artus,\n\n \n\nfucus item sorsum, sorsum sapor insinuatur\n\n \n\nsensibus; ut noscas primis differre figuris.\n\n \n\ndissimiles igitur formae glomeramen in unum\n\n \n\nconveniunt et res permixto semine constant.\n\n \n\nQuin etiam passim nostris in versibus ipsis\n\n \n\nmulta elementa vides multis communia verbis,\n\n \n\ncum tamen inter se versus ac verba necesse est\n\n \n\nconfiteare alia ex aliis constare elementis;\n\n \n\nnon quo multa parum communis littera currat\n\n \n\naut nulla inter se duo sint ex omnibus isdem,\n\n \n\nsed quia non volgo paria omnibus omnia constant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047","text":"sic aliis in rebus item communia multa\n\n \n\nmultarum rerum cum sint, primordia rerum\n\n \n\ndissimili tamen inter se consistere summa\n\n \n\npossunt; ut merito ex aliis constare feratur\n\n \n\nhumanum genus et fruges arbustaque laeta.\n\n \n\nNec tamen omnimodis conecti posse putandum est\n\n \n\nomnia; nam volgo fieri portenta videres,\n\n \n\nsemiferas hominum species existere et altos\n\n \n\ninter dum ramos egigni corpore vivo\n\n \n\nmultaque conecti terrestria membra marinis,\n\n \n\ntum flammam taetro spirantis ore Chimaeras\n\n \n\npascere naturam per terras omniparentis.\n\n \n\nquorum nil fieri manifestum est, omnia quando\n\n \n\nseminibus certis certa genetrice creata\n\n \n\nconservare genus crescentia posse videmus.\n\n \n\nscilicet id certa fieri ratione necessust.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0048","text":"nam sua cuique cibis ex omnibus intus in artus\n\n \n\ncorpora discedunt conexaque convenientis\n\n \n\nefficiunt motus; at contra aliena videmus\n\n \n\nreicere in terras naturam, multaque caecis\n\n \n\ncorporibus fugiunt e corpore percita plagis,\n\n \n\nquae neque conecti quoquam potuere neque intus\n\n \n\nvitalis motus consentire atque imitari.\n\n \n\nsed ne forte putes animalia sola teneri\n\n \n\nlegibus his, quaedam ratio res terminat omnis\n\n \n\nnam vel uti tota natura dissimiles sunt\n\n \n\ninter se genitae res quaeque, ita quamque necessest\n\n \n\ndissimili constare figura principiorum;\n\n \n\nnon quo multa parum simili sint praedita forma,\n\n \n\nsed quia non volgo paria omnibus omnia constant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0049","text":"semina cum porro distent, differre necessust\n\n \n\nintervalla vias conexus pondera plagas\n\n \n\nconcursus motus; quae non animalia solum\n\n \n\ncorpora seiungunt, sed terras ac mare totum\n\n \n\nsecernunt caelumque a terris omne retentant.\n\n \n\nNunc age dicta meo dulci quaesita labore\n\n \n\npercipe, ne forte haec albis ex alba rearis\n\n \n\nprincipiis esse, ante oculos quae candida cernis,\n\n \n\naut ea quae nigrant nigro de semine nata;\n\n \n\nnive alium quemvis quae sunt inbuta colorem,\n\n \n\npropterea gerere hunc credas, quod materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora consimili sint eius tincta colore;\n\n \n\nnullus enim color est omnino materiai\n\n \n\ncorporibus, neque par rebus neque denique dispar.\n\n \n\nin quae corpora si nullus tibi forte videtur\n\n \n\nposse animi iniectus fieri, procul avius erras.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0050","text":"nam cum caecigeni, solis qui lumina numquam\n\n \n\ndispexere, tamen cognoscant corpora tactu\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo nullo coniuncta colore,\n\n \n\nscire licet nostrae quoque menti corpora posse\n\n \n\nvorti in notitiam nullo circum lita fuco.\n\n \n\ndenique nos ipsi caecis quaecumque tenebris\n\n \n\ntangimus, haud ullo sentimus tincta colore.\n\n \n\nQuod quoniam vinco fieri, nunc esse docebo.\n\n \n\nomnis enim color omnino mutatur in omnis;\n\n \n\nquod facere haud ullo debent primordia pacto;\n\n \n\nimmutabile enim quiddam superare necessest,\n\n \n\nne res ad nihilum redigantur funditus omnes;\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque suis mutatum finibus exit,\n\n \n\ncontinuo hoc mors est illius quod fuit ante.\n\n \n\nproinde colore cave contingas semina rerum,\n\n \n\nne tibi res redeant ad nihilum funditus omnes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0051","text":"Praeterea si nulla coloris principiis est\n\n \n\nreddita natura et variis sunt praedita formis,\n\n \n\ne quibus omnigenus gignunt variantque colores,\n\n \n\npropterea magni quod refert, semina quaeque\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali positura contineantur\n\n \n\net quos inter se dent motus accipiantque,\n\n \n\nperfacile extemplo rationem reddere possis,\n\n \n\ncur ea quae nigro fuerint paulo ante colore,\n\n \n\nmarmoreo fieri possint candore repente,\n\n \n\nut mare, cum magni commorunt aequora venti,\n\n \n\nvertitur in canos candenti marmore fluctus;\n\n \n\ndicere enim possis, nigrum quod saepe videmus,\n\n \n\nmateries ubi permixta est illius et ordo\n\n \n\nprincipiis mutatus et addita demptaque quaedam,\n\n \n\ncontinuo id fieri ut candens videatur et album.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0052","text":"quod si caeruleis constarent aequora ponti\n\n \n\nseminibus, nullo possent albescere pacto;\n\n \n\nnam quo cumque modo perturbes caerula quae sint,\n\n \n\nnumquam in marmoreum possunt migrare colorem.\n\n \n\nsin alio atque alio sunt semina tincta colore,\n\n \n\nquae maris efficiunt unum purumque nitorem,\n\n \n\nut saepe ex aliis formis variisque figuris\n\n \n\nefficitur quiddam quadratum unaque figura,\n\n \n\nconveniebat, ut in quadrato cernimus esse\n\n \n\ndissimiles formas, ita cernere in aequore ponti\n\n \n\naut alio in quovis uno puroque nitore\n\n \n\ndissimiles longe inter se variosque colores.\n\n \n\npraeterea nihil officiunt obstantque figurae\n\n \n\ndissimiles, quo quadratum minus omne sit extra;\n\n \n\nat varii rerum inpediunt prohibentque colores,\n\n \n\nquo minus esse uno possit res tota nitore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0053","text":"Tum porro quae ducit et inlicit ut tribuamus\n\n \n\nprincipiis rerum non numquam causa colores,\n\n \n\noccidit, ex albis quoniam non alba creantur,\n\n \n\nnec quae nigra cluent de nigris, sed variis ex.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim multo proclivius exorientur\n\n \n\ncandida de nullo quam nigro nata colore\n\n \n\naut alio quovis, qui contra pugnet et obstet.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam nequeunt sine luce colores\n\n \n\nesse neque in lucem existunt primordia rerum,\n\n \n\nscire licet quam sint nullo velata colore;\n\n \n\nqualis enim caecis poterit color esse tenebris?\n\n \n\nlumine quin ipso mutatur propterea quod\n\n \n\nrecta aut obliqua percussus luce refulget;\n\n \n\npluma columbarum quo pacto in sole videtur,\n\n \n\nquae sita cervices circum collumque coronat;\n\n \n\nnamque alias fit uti claro sit rubra pyropo,\n\n \n\ninter dum quodam sensu fit uti videatur\n\n \n\ninter caeruleum viridis miscere zmaragdos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0054","text":"caudaque pavonis, larga cum luce repleta est,\n\n \n\nconsimili mutat ratione obversa colores;\n\n \n\nqui quoniam quodam gignuntur luminis ictu,\n\n \n\nscire licet, sine eo fieri non posse putandum est.\n\n \n\nEt quoniam plagae quoddam genus excipit in se\n\n \n\npupula, cum sentire colorem dicitur album,\n\n \n\natque aliud porro, nigrum cum et cetera sentit,\n\n \n\nnec refert ea quae tangas quo forte colore\n\n \n\npraedita sint, verum quali magis apta figura,\n\n \n\nscire licet nihil principiis opus esse colore,\n\n \n\nsed variis formis variantes edere tactus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quoniam non certis certa figuris\n\n \n\nest natura coloris et omnia principiorum\n\n \n\nformamenta queunt in quovis esse nitore,\n\n \n\ncur ea quae constant ex illis non pariter sunt\n\n \n\nomnigenus perfusa coloribus in genere omni?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0055","text":"conveniebat enim corvos quoque saepe volantis\n\n \n\nex albis album pinnis iactare colorem\n\n \n\net nigros fieri nigro de semine cycnos\n\n \n\naut alio quovis uno varioque colore.\n\n \n\nQuin etiam quanto in partes res quaeque minutas\n\n \n\ndistrahitur magis, hoc magis est ut cernere possis\n\n \n\nevanescere paulatim stinguique colorem;\n\n \n\nut fit ubi in parvas partis discerpitur austrum:\n\n \n\npurpura poeniceusque color clarissimus multo,\n\n \n\nfilatim cum distractum est, disperditur omnis;\n\n \n\nnoscere ut hinc possis prius omnem efflare colorem\n\n \n\nparticulas, quam discedant ad semina rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0056","text":"Postremo quoniam non omnia corpora vocem\n\n \n\nmittere concedis neque odorem, propterea fit\n\n \n\nut non omnibus adtribuas sonitus et odores:\n\n \n\nsic oculis quoniam non omnia cernere quimus,\n\n \n\nscire licet quaedam tam constare orba colore\n\n \n\nquam sine odore ullo quaedam sonituque remota,\n\n \n\nnec minus haec animum cognoscere posse sagacem\n\n \n\nquam quae sunt aliis rebus privata notare.\n\n \n\nSed ne forte putes solo spoliata colore\n\n \n\ncorpora prima manere, etiam secreta teporis\n\n \n\nsunt ac frigoris omnino calidique vaporis,\n\n \n\net sonitu sterila et suco ieiuna feruntur,\n\n \n\nnec iaciunt ullum proprium de corpore odorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0057","text":"sicut amaracini blandum stactaeque liquorem\n\n \n\net nardi florem, nectar qui naribus halat,\n\n \n\ncum facere instituas, cum primis quaerere par est,\n\n \n\nquod licet ac possis reperire, inolentis olivi\n\n \n\nnaturam, nullam quae mittat naribus auram,\n\n \n\nquam minime ut possit mixtos in corpore odores\n\n \n\nconcoctosque suo contractans perdere viro,\n\n \n\npropter eandem rem debent primordia rerum\n\n \n\nnon adhibere suum gignundis rebus odorem\n\n \n\nnec sonitum, quoniam nihil ab se mittere possunt,\n\n \n\nnec simili ratione saporem denique quemquam\n\n \n\nnec frigus neque item calidum tepidumque vaporem,\n\n \n\ncetera, quae cum ita sunt tamen ut mortalia constent,\n\n \n\nmolli lenta, fragosa putri, cava corpore raro,\n\n \n\nomnia sint a principiis seiuncta necessest,\n\n \n\ninmortalia si volumus subiungere rebus\n\n \n\nfundamenta, quibus nitatur summa salutis;\n\n \n\nne tibi res redeant ad nihilum funditus omnes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0058","text":"Nunc ea quae sentire videmus cumque necessest\n\n \n\nex insensilibus tamen omnia confiteare\n\n \n\nprincipiis constare. neque id manufesta refutant\n\n \n\nnec contra pugnant, in promptu cognita quae sunt,\n\n \n\nsed magis ipsa manu ducunt et credere cogunt\n\n \n\nex insensilibus, quod dico, animalia gigni.\n\n \n\nquippe videre licet vivos existere vermes\n\n \n\nstercore de taetro, putorem cum sibi nacta est\n\n \n\nintempestivis ex imbribus umida tellus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea cunctas itidem res vertere sese.\n\n \n\nvertunt se fluvii in frondes et pabula laeta\n\n \n\nin pecudes, vertunt pecudes in corpora nostra\n\n \n\nnaturam, et nostro de corpore saepe ferarum\n\n \n\naugescunt vires et corpora pennipotentum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0059","text":"ergo omnes natura cibos in corpora viva\n\n \n\nvertit et hinc sensus animantum procreat omnes,\n\n \n\nnon alia longe ratione atque arida ligna\n\n \n\nexplicat in flammas et in ignis omnia versat.\n\n \n\niamne vides igitur magni primordia rerum\n\n \n\nreferre in quali sint ordine quaeque locata\n\n \n\net commixta quibus dent motus accipiantque?\n\n \n\nTum porro, quid id est, animum quod percutit, ipsum,\n\n \n\nquod movet et varios sensus expromere cogit,\n\n \n\nex insensilibus ne credas sensile gigni?\n\n \n\nni mirum lapides et ligna et terra quod una\n\n \n\nmixta tamen nequeunt vitalem reddere sensum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0060","text":"illud in his igitur rebus meminisse decebit,\n\n \n\nnon ex omnibus omnino, quaecumque creant res\n\n \n\nsensilia, extemplo me gigni dicere sensus,\n\n \n\nsed magni referre ea primum quantula constent,\n\n \n\nsensile quae faciunt, et qua sint praedita forma,\n\n \n\nmotibus ordinibus posituris denique quae sint.\n\n \n\nquarum nil rerum in lignis glaebisque videmus;\n\n \n\net tamen haec, cum sunt quasi putrefacta per imbres,\n\n \n\nvermiculos pariunt, quia corpora materiai\n\n \n\nantiquis ex ordinibus permota nova re\n\n \n\nconciliantur ita ut debent animalia gigni.\n\n \n\nDeinde ex sensilibus qui sensile posse creari\n\n \n\nconstituunt, porro ex aliis sentire sueti\n\n \n\n* * *\n\n \n\nmollia cum faciunt; nam sensus iungitur omnis\n\n \n\nvisceribus nervis venis, quae cumque videmus\n\n \n\nmollia mortali consistere corpore creta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0061","text":"sed tamen esto iam posse haec aeterna manere;\n\n \n\nnempe tamen debent aut sensum partis habere\n\n \n\naut similis totis animalibus esse putari.\n\n \n\nat nequeant per se partes sentire necesse est:\n\n \n\nnamque animus sensus membrorum respuit omnis,\n\n \n\nnec manus a nobis potis est secreta neque ulla\n\n \n\ncorporis omnino sensum pars sola tenere.\n\n \n\nlinquitur ut totis animantibus adsimulentur,\n\n \n\nvitali ut possint consentire undique sensu.\n\n \n\nqui poterunt igitur rerum primordia dici\n\n \n\net leti vitare vias, animalia cum sint,\n\n \n\natque animalia sint mortalibus una eademque?\n\n \n\nquod tamen ut possint, at coetu concilioque\n\n \n\nnil facient praeter volgum turbamque animantum,\n\n \n\nscilicet ut nequeant homines armenta feraeque\n\n \n\ninter sese ullam rem gignere conveniundo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0062","text":"sic itidem quae sentimus sentire necessest.\n\n \n\nquod si forte suum dimittunt corpore sensum\n\n \n\natque alium capiunt, quid opus fuit adtribui id quod\n\n \n\ndetrahitur? tum praeterea, quod fudimus ante,\n\n \n\nquatinus in pullos animalis vertier ova\n\n \n\ncernimus alituum vermisque effervere terra,\n\n \n\nintempestivos quam putor cepit ob imbris,\n\n \n\nscire licet gigni posse ex non sensibus sensus.\n\n \n\nQuod si forte aliquis dicet, dum taxat oriri\n\n \n\nposse ex non sensu sensus mutabilitate,\n\n \n\naut aliquo tamquam partu quod proditur extra,\n\n \n\nhuic satis illud erit planum facere atque probare,\n\n \n\nnon fieri partum nisi concilio ante coacto,\n\n \n\nnec quicquam commutari sine conciliatu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0063","text":"Principio nequeunt ullius corporis esse\n\n \n\nsensus ante ipsam genitam naturam animantis,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia materies disiecta tenetur\n\n \n\naere fluminibus terris terraque creatis,\n\n \n\nnec congressa modo vitalis convenientes\n\n \n\ncontulit inter se motus, quibus omnituentes\n\n \n\naccensi sensus animantem quamque tuentur.\n\n \n\nPraeterea quamvis animantem grandior ictus,\n\n \n\nquam patitur natura, repente adfligit et omnis\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animi pergit confundere sensus.\n\n \n\ndissoluuntur enim positurae principiorum\n\n \n\net penitus motus vitales inpediuntur,\n\n \n\ndonec materies omnis concussa per artus\n\n \n\nvitalis animae nodos a corpore solvit\n\n \n\ndispersamque foras per caulas eiecit omnis;\n\n \n\nnam quid praeterea facere ictum posse reamur\n\n \n\noblatum, nisi discutere ac dissolvere quaeque?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0064","text":"fit quoque uti soleant minus oblato acriter ictu\n\n \n\nreliqui motus vitalis vincere saepe,\n\n \n\nvincere et ingentis plagae sedare tumultus\n\n \n\ninque suos quicquid rursus revocare meatus\n\n \n\net quasi iam leti dominantem in corpore motum\n\n \n\ndiscutere ac paene amissos accendere sensus;\n\n \n\nnam qua re potius leti iam limine ab ipso\n\n \n\nad vitam possint conlecta mente reverti,\n\n \n\nquam quo decursum prope iam siet ire et abire?\n\n \n\nPraeterea, quoniam dolor est, ubi materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora vi quadam per viscera viva per artus\n\n \n\nsollicitata suis trepidant in sedibus intus,\n\n \n\ninque locum quando remigrant, fit blanda voluptas,\n\n \n\nscire licet nullo primordia posse dolore\n\n \n\ntemptari nullamque voluptatem capere ex se;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem non sunt ex ullis principiorum\n\n \n\ncorporibus, quorum motus novitate laborent\n\n \n\naut aliquem fructum capiant dulcedinis almae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0065","text":"haut igitur debent esse ullo praedita sensu.\n\n \n\nDenique uti possint sentire animalia quaeque,\n\n \n\nprincipiis si iam est sensus tribuendus eorum,\n\n \n\nquid, genus humanum propritim de quibus auctumst?\n\n \n\nscilicet et risu tremulo concussa cachinnant\n\n \n\net lacrimis spargunt rorantibus ora genasque\n\n \n\nmultaque de rerum mixtura dicere callent\n\n \n\net sibi proporro quae sint primordia quaerunt;\n\n \n\nquando quidem totis mortalibus adsimulata\n\n \n\nipsa quoque ex aliis debent constare elementis,\n\n \n\ninde alia ex aliis, nusquam consistere ut ausis;\n\n \n\nquippe sequar, quod cumque loqui ridereque dices\n\n \n\net sapere, ex aliis eadem haec facientibus ut sit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0066","text":"quod si delira haec furiosaque cernimus esse\n\n \n\net ridere potest non ex ridentibus auctus,\n\n \n\net sapere et doctis rationem reddere dictis\n\n \n\nnon ex seminibus sapientibus atque disertis,\n\n \n\nqui minus esse queant ea quae sentire videmus\n\n \n\nseminibus permixta carentibus undique sensu?\n\n \n\nDenique caelesti sumus omnes semine oriundi;\n\n \n\nomnibus ille idem pater est, unde alma liquentis\n\n \n\numoris guttas mater cum terra recepit,\n\n \n\nfeta parit nitidas fruges arbustaque laeta\n\n \n\net genus humanum, parit omnia saecla ferarum,\n\n \n\npabula cum praebet, quibus omnes corpora pascunt\n\n \n\net dulcem ducunt vitam prolemque propagant;\n\n \n\nqua propter merito maternum nomen adepta est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0067","text":"cedit item retro, de terra quod fuit ante,\n\n \n\nin terras, et quod missumst ex aetheris oris,\n\n \n\nid rursum caeli rellatum templa receptant.\n\n \n\nnec sic interemit mors res ut materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora conficiat, sed coetum dissupat ollis;\n\n \n\ninde aliis aliud coniungit et efficit, omnis\n\n \n\nres ut convertant formas mutentque colores\n\n \n\net capiant sensus et puncto tempore reddant;\n\n \n\nut noscas referre earum primordia rerum\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali positura contineantur\n\n \n\net quos inter se dent motus accipiantque,\n\n \n\nneve putes aeterna penes residere potesse\n\n \n\ncorpora prima quod in summis fluitare videmus\n\n \n\nrebus et interdum nasci subitoque perire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0068","text":"quin etiam refert nostris in versibus ipsis\n\n \n\ncum quibus et quali sint ordine quaeque locata;\n\n \n\nnamque eadem caelum mare terras flumina solem\n\n \n\nsignificant, eadem fruges arbusta animantis;\n\n \n\nsi non omnia sunt, at multo maxima pars est\n\n \n\nconsimilis; verum positura discrepitant res.\n\n \n\nsic ipsis in rebus item iam materiai\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nconcursus motus ordo positura figurae\n\n \n\ncum permutantur, mutari res quoque debent.\n\n \n\nNunc animum nobis adhibe veram ad rationem.\n\n \n\nnam tibi vehementer nova res molitur ad auris\n\n \n\naccedere et nova se species ostendere rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0069","text":"sed neque tam facilis res ulla est, quin ea primum\n\n \n\ndifficilis magis ad credendum constet, itemque\n\n \n\nnil adeo magnum neque tam mirabile quicquam,\n\n \n\nquod non paulatim minuant mirarier omnes,\n\n \n\nprincipio caeli clarum purumque colorem\n\n \n\nquaeque in se cohibet, palantia sidera passim,\n\n \n\nlunamque et solis praeclara luce nitorem;\n\n \n\nomnia quae nunc si primum mortalibus essent\n\n \n\nex improviso si sint obiecta repente,\n\n \n\nquid magis his rebus poterat mirabile dici,\n\n \n\naut minus ante quod auderent fore credere gentes?\n\n \n\nnil, ut opinor; ita haec species miranda fuisset.\n\n \n\nquam tibi iam nemo fessus satiate videndi,\n\n \n\nsuspicere in caeli dignatur lucida templa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0070","text":"desine qua propter novitate exterritus ipsa\n\n \n\nexpuere ex animo rationem, sed magis acri\n\n \n\niudicio perpende, et si tibi vera videntur,\n\n \n\ndede manus, aut, si falsum est, accingere contra.\n\n \n\nquaerit enim rationem animus, cum summa loci sit\n\n \n\ninfinita foris haec extra moenia mundi,\n\n \n\nquid sit ibi porro, quo prospicere usque velit mens\n\n \n\natque animi iactus liber quo pervolet ipse.\n\n \n\nPrincipio nobis in cunctas undique partis\n\n \n\net latere ex utroque supra supterque per omne\n\n \n\nnulla est finis; uti docui, res ipsaque per se\n\n \n\nvociferatur, et elucet natura profundi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","text":"nullo iam pacto veri simile esse putandumst,\n\n \n\nundique cum vorsum spatium vacet infinitum\n\n \n\nseminaque innumero numero summaque profunda\n\n \n\nmultimodis volitent aeterno percita motu,\n\n \n\nhunc unum terrarum orbem caelumque creatum,\n\n \n\nnil agere illa foris tot corpora materiai;\n\n \n\ncum praesertim hic sit natura factus et ipsa\n\n \n\nsponte sua forte offensando semina rerum\n\n \n\nmultimodis temere in cassum frustraque coacta\n\n \n\ntandem coluerunt ea quae coniecta repente\n\n \n\nmagnarum rerum fierent exordia semper,\n\n \n\nterrai maris et caeli generisque animantum.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam talis fateare necesse est\n\n \n\nesse alios alibi congressus materiai,\n\n \n\nqualis hic est, avido complexu quem tenet aether.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0072","text":"Praeterea cum materies est multa parata,\n\n \n\ncum locus est praesto nec res nec causa moratur\n\n \n\nulla, geri debent ni mirum et confieri res.\n\n \n\nnunc et seminibus si tanta est copia, quantam\n\n \n\nenumerare aetas animantum non queat omnis,\n\n \n\nquis eadem natura manet, quae semina rerum\n\n \n\nconicere in loca quaeque queat simili ratione\n\n \n\natque huc sunt coniecta, necesse est confiteare\n\n \n\nesse alios aliis terrarum in partibus orbis\n\n \n\net varias hominum gentis et saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit ut in summa res nulla sit una,\n\n \n\nunica quae gignatur et unica solaque crescat,\n\n \n\nquin aliquoius siet saecli permultaque eodem\n\n \n\nsint genere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","text":"in primis animalibus indice mente\n\n \n\ninvenies sic montivagum genus esse ferarum,\n\n \n\nsic hominum geminam prolem, sic denique mutas\n\n \n\nsquamigerum pecudes et corpora cuncta volantum.\n\n \n\nqua propter caelum simili ratione fatendumst\n\n \n\nterramque et solem, lunam mare cetera quae sunt,\n\n \n\nnon esse unica, sed numero magis innumerali;\n\n \n\nquando quidem vitae depactus terminus alte\n\n \n\ntam manet haec et tam nativo corpore constant\n\n \n\nquam genus omne, quod his generatimst rebus abundans.\n\n \n\nQuae bene cognita si teneas, natura videtur\n\n \n\nlibera continuo, dominis privata superbis,\n\n \n\nipsa sua per se sponte omnia dis agere expers.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0074","text":"nam pro sancta deum tranquilla pectora pace\n\n \n\nquae placidum degunt aevom vitamque serenam,\n\n \n\nquis regere immensi summam, quis habere profundi\n\n \n\nindu manu validas potis est moderanter habenas,\n\n \n\nquis pariter caelos omnis convertere et omnis\n\n \n\nignibus aetheriis terras suffire feracis,\n\n \n\nomnibus inve locis esse omni tempore praesto,\n\n \n\nnubibus ut tenebras faciat caelique serena\n\n \n\nconcutiat sonitu, tum fulmina mittat et aedis\n\n \n\nsaepe suas disturbet et in deserta recedens\n\n \n\nsaeviat exercens telum, quod saepe nocentes\n\n \n\npraeterit exanimatque indignos inque merentes?\n\n \n\nMultaque post mundi tempus genitale diemque\n\n \n\nprimigenum maris et terrae solisque coortum\n\n \n\naddita corpora sunt extrinsecus, addita circum\n\n \n\nsemina, quae magnum iaculando contulit omne,\n\n \n\nunde mare et terrae possent augescere et unde\n\n \n\nappareret spatium caeli domus altaque tecta\n\n \n\ntolleret a terris procul et consurgeret aer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0075","text":"nam sua cuique, locis ex omnibus, omnia plagis\n\n \n\ncorpora distribuuntur et ad sua saecla recedunt,\n\n \n\numor ad umorem, terreno corpore terra\n\n \n\ncrescit et ignem ignes procudunt aetheraque aether,\n\n \n\ndonique ad extremum crescendi perfica finem\n\n \n\nomnia perduxit rerum natura creatrix;\n\n \n\nut fit ubi nihilo iam plus est quod datur intra\n\n \n\nvitalis venas quam quod fluit atque recedit.\n\n \n\nomnibus hic aetas debet consistere rebus,\n\n \n\nhic natura suis refrenat viribus auctum.\n\n \n\nnam quae cumque vides hilaro grandescere adauctu\n\n \n\npaulatimque gradus aetatis scandere adultae,\n\n \n\nplura sibi adsumunt quam de se corpora mittunt,\n\n \n\ndum facile in venas cibus omnis inditur et dum\n\n \n\nnon ita sunt late dispessa, ut multa remittant\n\n \n\net plus dispendi faciant quam vescitur aetas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0076","text":"nam certe fluere atque recedere corpora rebus\n\n \n\nmulta manus dandum est; sed plura accedere debent,\n\n \n\ndonec alescendi summum tetigere cacumen.\n\n \n\ninde minutatim vires et robur adultum\n\n \n\nfrangit et in partem peiorem liquitur aetas.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim quanto est res amplior, augmine adempto,\n\n \n\net quo latior est, in cunctas undique partis\n\n \n\nplura modo dispargit et a se corpora mittit,\n\n \n\nnec facile in venas cibus omnis diditur ei\n\n \n\nnec satis est, pro quam largos exaestuat aestus,\n\n \n\nunde queat tantum suboriri ac subpeditare.\n\n \n\niure igitur pereunt, cum rarefacta fluendo\n\n \n\nsunt et cum externis succumbunt omnia plagis,\n\n \n\nquando quidem grandi cibus aevo denique defit,\n\n \n\nnec tuditantia rem cessant extrinsecus ullam\n\n \n\ncorpora conficere et plagis infesta domare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0077","text":"Sic igitur magni quoque circum moenia mundi\n\n \n\nexpugnata dabunt labem putrisque ruinas;\n\n \n\nomnia debet enim cibus integrare novando\n\n \n\net fulcire cibus, cibus omnia sustentare,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam nec venae perpetiuntur\n\n \n\nquod satis est, neque quantum opus est natura ministrat.\n\n \n\nIamque adeo fracta est aetas effetaque tellus\n\n \n\nvix animalia parva creat, quae cuncta creavit\n\n \n\nsaecla deditque ferarum ingentia corpora partu.\n\n \n\nhaud, ut opinor, enim mortalia saecla superne\n\n \n\naurea de caelo demisit funis in arva\n\n \n\nnec mare nec fluctus plangentis saxa crearunt,\n\n \n\nsed genuit tellus eadem quae nunc alit ex se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0078","text":"praeterea nitidas fruges vinetaque laeta\n\n \n\nsponte sua primum mortalibus ipsa creavit,\n\n \n\nipsa dedit dulcis fetus et pabula laeta;\n\n \n\nquae nunc vix nostro grandescunt aucta labore,\n\n \n\nconterimusque boves et viris agricolarum,\n\n \n\nconficimus ferrum vix arvis suppeditati:\n\n \n\nusque adeo parcunt fetus augentque laborem.\n\n \n\niamque caput quassans grandis suspirat arator\n\n \n\ncrebrius, in cassum magnos cecidisse labores,\n\n \n\net cum tempora temporibus praesentia confert\n\n \n\npraeteritis, laudat fortunas saepe parentis.\n\n \n\ntristis item vetulae vitis sator atque vietae\n\n \n\ntemporis incusat momen saeclumque fatigat,\n\n \n\net crepat, antiquum genus ut pietate repletum\n\n \n\nperfacile angustis tolerarit finibus aevom,\n\n \n\ncum minor esset agri multo modus ante viritim;\n\n \n\nnec tenet omnia paulatim tabescere et ire\n\n \n\nad capulum spatio aetatis defessa vetusto.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2d93118d03cf48eb08dca0b9cb027aafd7ad11aab994305117084bfa188c60b1","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T235450217779Z_819e1b1e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book Two","source_sha256":"2d93118d03cf48eb08dca0b9cb027aafd7ad11aab994305117084bfa188c60b1","sha256":"112cbeed3fc8c472d3150c0dd72253fd9874cfbff17766ae3bc434e1929f6b2c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:54:50.217819+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0001","text":"## Book Two\n\nSweet it is, when upon the great sea the winds trouble the waters,\n\nto gaze from land upon another’s great toil;\n\nnot because it is a joyous pleasure for anyone to be distressed,\n\nbut because it is sweet to perceive from what evils you yourself are free.\n\nSweet too is it to behold great contests of war\n\nset in array across the plains, with no share of danger to yourself;\n\nbut nothing is sweeter than to hold the well-fortified,\n\nlofty, serene temples built by the teaching of the wise,\n\nwhence you may look down upon others and see them everywhere\n\nwandering and straying as they seek the path of life,\n\nvying in intellect, striving in nobility,\n\nlabouring night and day with surpassing effort\n\nto rise forth to the highest riches and gain mastery of affairs.\n\nO wretched minds of men, O blind hearts!\n\nIn what darknesses of life, and amid how great dangers,\n\nis passed this span of time, whatever it is!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0002","text":"Do you not see\n\nthat nature barks for nothing else for herself, except that, when\n\npain is absent from the body, she may enjoy in the mind\n\na pleasant sensation, with care and fear removed?\n\nTherefore we see that for bodily nature few things\n\nare altogether needed: such as can remove pain,\n\nand can besides spread beneath us many delights\n\nat times more agreeably; nor does nature herself require,\n\nif there are not in the house golden statues of youths\n\nholding fiery lamps in their right hands,\n\nso that light may be supplied for nightly banquets,\n\nnor does the house gleam with silver and shine with gold,\n\nnor do lyres resound through panelled and gilded halls,\n\nwhen nevertheless, stretched out together upon soft grass\n\nbeside a stream of water, beneath the branches of a tall tree,\n\nthey tend their bodies pleasantly without great wealth,\n\nespecially when the weather smiles and the seasons of the year\n\nsprinkle the green grasses with flowers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0003","text":"Nor do burning fevers depart from the body more quickly\n\nif you toss about upon tapestries and pictures and blushing purple,\n\nthan if you must lie in a common garment.\n\nWherefore, since treasures avail nothing for our body,\n\nnor noble birth nor the glory of kingship,\n\nit must be thought that, besides, they avail the mind nothing either;\n\nunless perhaps, when you see your legions\n\nseething over the spaces of the plain, making images of war,\n\nstrong in great reserves and in the force of resources,\n\nadorned with arms, the fleets likewise manned,\n\nthen, at these things, religions, terrified for you,\n\nflee from your mind, and the fears of death, trembling,\n\nthen leave your breast empty and free from care.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","text":"But if we see that these things are ridiculous and mockeries,\n\nindeed, the fears of men and their attendant cares;\n\nnor do they fear the clash of arms or savage weapons,\n\nand boldly among kings and the powerful in affairs\n\nthey move, nor do they revere the gleam from gold\n\nor the bright splendour of purple clothing,\n\nwhy do you doubt that all this power belongs to reason,\n\nespecially since all life toils in darkness?\n\nFor just as children tremble and fear everything in blind\n\ndarkness, so we sometimes fear in the light\n\nthings that are no more to be feared than those which\n\nchildren dread in darkness and imagine will happen.\n\nTherefore this terror of the mind and these shadows must\n\nbe scattered not by the rays of the sun nor by the bright shafts of day,\n\nbut by the aspect and reason of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0005","text":"Come now, by what motion the generative bodies of matter\n\nproduce the various things and dissolve those produced,\n\nand by what force they are compelled to do this, and what mobility has been assigned to them\n\nfor moving through the great void,\n\nI shall explain: remember to offer yourself to my words.\n\nFor surely matter does not cohere, packed closely together,\n\nsince we see every thing diminished\n\nand perceive all things, as it were, flowing away through long time\n\nand withdrawing old age from before our eyes,\n\nalthough the sum total seems to remain unharmed.\n\nbecause the bodies that depart from each thing,\n\ndiminish that from which they go, and enrich with increase that to which they have come.\n\nThey compel the former to grow old, but the latter, contrariwise, to flourish,\n\nnor do they linger there. Thus the sum of things is ever renewed,\n\nand mortals live among themselves by mutual exchange.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0006","text":"Some nations grow, others diminish,\n\nand within a short span the generations of living things are changed,\n\nand, like runners, hand on the torch of life.\n\nIf you think that the first-beginnings of things can cease\n\nand, by ceasing, beget new motions of things,\n\nyou wander far astray from true reasoning.\n\nFor, since they wander through the void, the first-beginnings of things must necessarily\n\neither be carried on by their own weight\n\nor by chance impact from another. For when, often moving swiftly,\n\nthey have collided head-on, it happens that they suddenly\n\nfly apart in different directions; nor indeed is it surprising, since they are exceedingly hard\n\nwith solid weights and nothing obstructs them from behind.\n\nAnd that you may perceive how much more all the bodies of matter\n\nare tossed about, remember that there is no bottom at all\n\nin the sum of things, nor have the first bodies any place\n\nwhere they may come to rest, since space is boundless and extends\n\nimmeasurably open in every direction on all sides,\n\nas has been shown in many ways and proved by certain reasoning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0007","text":"Since this is established, assuredly there is no rest.\n\ngiven back to the first bodies through the deep void,\n\nbut are exercised rather by continual and varied motion,\n\npartly, pressed together at great intervals, they rebound,\n\nwhile another part too are harassed by blows in short spaces.\n\nAnd whatever things, by a more compact conjunction,\n\nare borne together and rebound at tiny intervals,\n\nentangled themselves by their own interwoven shapes,\n\nthese constitute the strong roots of stone and the fierce\n\nbodies of iron, and the other things of this kind.\n\nA few, moreover, wander through the great void,\n\nthe rest fly apart far and wide and rebound\n\nat great intervals; these furnish us with thin air\n\nand the bright lights of the sun.\n\nAnd many besides wander through the great void,\n\nhaving been cast off from the combinations of things, nor anywhere\n\nwhen received have they been able even to unite their motions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0008","text":"Of this matter, as I relate, a likeness and image\n\nis always turning before our eyes and pressing upon us.\n\nFor observe, whenever the light of the sun and its\n\nadmitted rays pour in through the dark places of houses:\n\nyou will see many minute bodies in many ways through the void\n\nmingling in the very light of the rays,\n\nand, as though in everlasting conflict, battles and fights\n\nwaging in squadrons, contending, and giving no pause,\n\nexercised by frequent unions and separations;\n\nso that from this you may be able to infer what sort of thing it is for the first-beginnings of things\n\nto be tossed always in the great void.\n\nprovided only that a small thing can furnish an example of great things\n\nand traces for knowledge.\n\nFor this reason it is all the more fitting that you direct your mind to these things also,\n\nthe bodies which seem to be disturbed in the sun's rays,\n\nbecause such disturbances indicate that motions too of matter\n\nlie beneath them, secret and unseen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0009","text":"For there you will see many, struck by unseen blows,\n\nchange their course and, driven back, return\n\nnow this way, now that, in every direction from all sides.\n\nEvidently, in this case all the error arises from the first-beginnings.\n\nFor first the first-beginnings of things move of themselves;\n\nthen those bodies which are composed of a small union\n\nand are, as it were, nearest to the powers of the first-beginnings,\n\nbeing impelled by their unseen blows, are set in motion,\n\nand they themselves in turn strike somewhat larger ones.\n\nThus motion rises from the first-beginnings and comes forth\n\nlittle by little to our senses, so that those things too are moved\n\nwhich we are able to discern in the light of the sun,\n\nnor is it plainly apparent by what blows they do this.\n\nNow what mobility has been assigned to the bodies of matter,\n\nMemmius, you may learn hence in a few words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0010","text":"first, when dawn sprinkles the lands with new light\n\nand the various birds, flying through the pathless groves,\n\nfill the yielding air with their liquid cries,\n\nhow suddenly the sun, rising at such a time, is wont\n\nto clothe all things, suffusing them with its own light,\n\nwe see to be plain and manifest to all.\n\nBut the heat which the sun sends forth, and its clear light,\n\ndoes not travel through empty void; wherefore it is compelled to go more slowly,\n\nwhile, as it were, it beats its way through the airy waves;\n\nnor do all the minute bodies of the heat travel singly,\n\nbut entwined among themselves and rolled together in a mass;\n\nfor which reason they are at once drawn back by one another and hindered from without,\n\nso that they are compelled to go more slowly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0011","text":"but those which are solid by the simplicity of their first-elements,\n\nwhen they move through the empty void and no thing outside delays them, and, being themselves one from their own parts,\n\none, are borne, straining, into the one place toward which they have begun,\n\nmust, plainly, excel in swiftness\n\nand be carried much more quickly than the sun’s light\n\nand traverse a manifold extent of space in the same\n\ntime in which the sun’s flashes speed through the sky.\n\n* * *\n\nnor to pursue each several first-element,\n\nso as to see by what principle each thing is done.\n\nBut certain men, on the contrary, ignorant of matter,\n\nmaintain that nature cannot, without the divinity of the gods,\n\nin so great a work, suitably to human concerns,\n\nchange the seasons of the years and create crops,\n\nand do besides all the rest which divine pleasure, the guide of life,\n\npersuades mortals to seek and itself leads them to,\n\nand, through the allurements of Venus, causes the generations to propagate,\n\nlest the human race perish.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0012","text":"When they pretend that the gods established all these things\n\nfor that cause, they seem in all matters\n\nto have fallen far away from true reasoning.\n\nFor although I do not know what the first-elements of things are,\n\nyet from the very constitution of the sky I would dare\n\nto affirm this, and to demonstrate it from many other things as well:\n\nthat the nature of the world was by no means created for us by divine power—so great a faultiness does it stand endowed with.\n\nWhat these are, Memmius, we shall make clear to you later;\n\nnow we shall set forth what remains concerning motions.\n\nNow is the place, I think, to establish for you this point also in these matters:\n\nthat no bodily thing can by its own force\n\nbe borne upward or move upward.\n\nLet not bodies of flame deceive you in this regard;\n\nfor flames arise directed upward and take their increase,\n\nand shining crops and trees grow upward,\n\nalthough all weights, so far as lies in themselves, are borne downward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0013","text":"nor when fires leap up to the roofs of houses\n\nand with swift flame taste the beams and timbers,\n\nmust it be thought that they do this of their own accord, without force applied.\n\nThus blood, discharged from our body,\n\nleaps forth, bounding high and scattering gore.\n\nDo you not see also with how great a force the water\n\nrepels beams and timbers? For the more we have forced them down deep\n\nstraight down, and with great force have pressed many of them down with difficulty,\n\nthe more eagerly it thrusts them upward and sends them back,\n\nso that more than half emerges and springs out above.\n\nAnd yet, so far as lies in themselves, we do not doubt, I think,\n\nthat all these things are borne downward through the empty void.\n\nThus, therefore, flames too must be able, pressed out through the breezes\n\nof air, to rise upward, although\n\ntheir weights, so far as lies in them, struggle to draw them downward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0014","text":"And the nocturnal torches of heaven flying on high—\n\ndo you not see them draw out long trails of flame\n\nin whatever directions nature has granted them a path?\n\nDo you not see stars and constellations fall to earth?\n\nThe sun too, from the summit of heaven, scatters all\n\nits heat in every direction and sows the fields with light;\n\ntherefore the sun's heat too is directed toward the earth.\n\nAnd you see thunderbolts fly crosswise through the rains,\n\nnow from this side, now from that, fires burst from the clouds\n\nthey clash together; fiery force falls everywhere upon the earth.\n\nThis too we desire that you should recognize in these matters:\n\nwhen bodies are borne straight downward through the void\n\nby their own weights, at times more or less uncertain\n\nand at uncertain places, they swerve a little from their course,\n\nonly so much that you could say their motion has been altered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0015","text":"For unless they were accustomed to swerve, all things downward\n\nwould fall like drops of rain through the deep void,\n\nnor would collision arise, nor a blow be produced\n\namong the first-beginnings; thus nature would never have created anything.\n\nBut if perhaps anyone believes that heavier\n\nbodies, because they are borne more swiftly straight through the void,\n\nfall from above upon lighter ones and thus generate blows\n\nwhich can yield generative motions,\n\nhe strays far away from true reasoning.\n\nFor whatever things fall through water and thin air,\n\nmust accelerate their falls in proportion to their weights,\n\nbecause the body of water and the thin nature\n\nof air cannot equally delay every thing,\n\nbut, overcome, yield more quickly to heavier things;\n\nbut, on the contrary, the void can at no point, from no quarter, nor at any\n\ntime, offer resistance to any thing,\n\nwithout continuing to give way, as its own nature demands;\n\nwherefore all things ought through the untroubled void\n\nto be driven with unequal weights equally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0016","text":"Therefore the heavier will never be able to fall upon the lighter\n\nfrom above, nor by themselves to produce blows\n\nthat vary their motions, through which nature conducts things.\n\nWherefore, again and again, it is necessary that bodies swerve a little;\n\nand no more than the least, lest we seem to invent\n\noblique motions and the true fact refute this.\n\nFor we see that this is plain and manifest:\n\nweights, so far as lies in themselves, cannot move obliquely\n\nwhen they plunge down from above, as you may discern;\n\nbut that they swerve at all from the straight course of their path—\n\nwho is there that can discern it?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0017","text":"Finally, if every motion is always connected\n\nand a new motion arises from the old in a fixed order,\n\nand if the first-beginnings do not, by swerving, make\n\nsome beginning of motion that may break the bonds of fate,\n\nso that cause does not follow cause from infinity,\n\nwhence comes this freedom in living creatures throughout the lands,\n\nwhence comes this will, I say, torn away from the fates,\n\nby which we proceed wherever pleasure leads each one,\n\nand likewise swerve in our motions neither at a fixed time\n\nnor in a fixed region of place, but where the mind itself has borne us?\n\nFor beyond doubt in these matters each person’s own will\n\ngives the beginning, and from it motions are spread through the limbs.\n\ndo you not see also, when the barriers have been opened in a point of time,\n\nthat nevertheless the eager force of the horses cannot burst forth\n\nso suddenly as the mind itself desires?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0018","text":"For the whole supply of matter throughout the entire body\n\nmust be stirred up, stirred up through all the limbs,\n\nso that it may follow, straining, the mind's impulse;\n\nso that you may see that the beginning of motion is created from the heart\n\nand proceeds first from the will of the mind,\n\nthen thereafter is imparted through the whole body and limbs.\n\nNor is it the same as when we move forward, impelled by a blow\n\nthrough the great force and great compulsion of another;\n\nfor then the whole matter of the entire body,\n\nas is clear to us, goes and is dragged on against our will,\n\nuntil the will has checked it through the limbs.\n\nDo you not now see, therefore, that although an external force may drive many men\n\nand often compel them to move forward against their will\n\nand to be swept headlong along, yet there is in our breast\n\nsomething that can fight against and resist it?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0019","text":"At whose discretion also the supply of matter\n\nis compelled sometimes to bend through the limbs and members,\n\nand, when thrown forward, is checked and settles back again.\n\nWherefore you must admit that in the seeds too there is the same thing,\n\nthat there is another cause of motions besides blows and weights,\n\nwhence this inborn power is ours,\n\nsince we see that nothing can be made from nothing.\n\nFor weight prevents everything from occurring through blows\n\nas though by an external force; but lest necessity itself\n\nhave mastery within in all things that are done,\n\nand, as though conquered, be compelled to yield and suffer,\n\nthis is effected by the slight swerve of the first-beginnings,\n\nneither at a fixed region of place nor at a fixed time.\n\nNor has the store of matter ever been more closely packed\n\nnor, again, at greater intervals;\n\nfor nothing is increased, nor does anything perish from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0020","text":"Therefore, in whatever motion the bodies of the first-beginnings\n\nnow are, in the same they were borne in ages past,\n\nand thereafter they will always be borne in like manner,\n\nand the same things which have been wont to be begotten will be begotten\n\nunder the same condition, and will be and will grow and prevail by strength,\n\nso far as is granted to each through the covenants of nature.\n\nNor can any force change the sum of things;\n\nfor there is no place to which any kind of matter\n\ncan escape from the whole, nor into the whole is there any place whence\n\na new force, having arisen, can break in and change the whole\n\nnature of things and alter its motions.\n\nIn these matters it is not wonderful why,\n\nalthough all the first-beginnings of things are in motion,\n\nthe totality nonetheless appears to stand in complete repose,\n\nexcept insofar as it imparts motion from its own body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0021","text":"For the nature of the first elements lies wholly far below\n\nour senses; wherefore, when you can no longer\n\ndiscern them themselves, their motions too must escape you;\n\nespecially since the things that we can discern conceal\n\ntheir motions nevertheless, often when separated by a space of places.\n\nFor often on a hill, cropping the luxuriant pastures,\n\nthe fleecy flocks creep along, wherever the grasses, calling each one,\n\ninvite them, budding with fresh dew,\n\nand the lambs, when sated, play and flash about gently;\n\nall of which appear to us confused from afar\n\nand as though a whiteness were standing still on the green hill.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0022","text":"Moreover, when great legions fill the places\n\nof the plains in their course, stirring up the likenesses of war,\n\nwhen their gleam rises to the sky and all around\n\nthe earth glitters with bronze, and beneath, by the force of men,\n\na sound is raised by their feet, and the mountains, struck by shouting,\n\nthrow back their voices to the stars of the world,\n\nand horsemen fly about all around and suddenly through the midst\n\ntraverse the plains, shaking them with mighty onset;\n\nand yet there is some place among the high mountains, from which\n\nthey seem to stand still and the gleam to remain fixed upon the plains.\n\nCome now, next learn what kind the beginnings of all things are\n\nand how far they differ in their shapes,\n\nperceive how varied they are in their manifold shapes;\n\nnot because many are endowed with a form too little alike,\n\nbut because not all things universally consist alike for all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0023","text":"Nor is this surprising; for since their abundance is so great\n\nthat there is neither an end, as I have taught, nor any sum,\n\nthey must, unsurprisingly, not all altogether\n\nbe affected with equal texture and similar shape.\n\nMoreover, the human race, and the dumb swimming creatures,\n\nthe scaly herds, the glad cattle, and the wild beasts,\n\nand the various birds, which the gladdening places of waters\n\nthrong around the banks, springs, and lakes,\n\nand those which, flying about, haunt the pathless groves—\n\ngo on, take any one of these kinds;\n\nyet you will find that they differ among themselves in shape.\n\nNor could offspring recognize their mother by any other means,\n\nnor a mother her offspring; yet we see that they can do this\n\nno less than humans are known among themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0024","text":"For often before the splendid shrines of the gods a calf,\n\nslain beside the incense-burning altars, falls down,\n\nbreathing out from its breast a warm stream of blood;\n\nbut its bereft mother, wandering through the green glades,\n\nknows the tracks pressed on the ground by cloven hooves,\n\nsurveying every place with her eyes, if anywhere she may be able\n\nto catch sight of her lost young, and fills [the place] with lamentations\n\nstanding by the leafy grove and repeatedly revisits it,\n\npierced by longing for the stallion's stall;\n\nnor can the tender willows and the grasses thriving with dew,\n\nnor any streams gliding down from their lofty banks,\n\ndelight her mind and turn away her sudden care,\n\nnor can other forms of calves amid the happy pastures\n\ndivert her mind and lighten her care;\n\nso greatly does she require something her own and familiar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0025","text":"Moreover, tender kids, with trembling voices,\n\nrecognize their horned mothers, and wanton lambs the sheep\n\nthat bleat; thus, as nature demands,\n\nnearly each runs down to its own udder of milk.\n\nFinally, any grain, yet not every grain\n\nof whatever kind, you will see to be like one another in its own kind,\n\nwithout some difference in forms occurring among them.\n\nAnd by a like principle we see the race of shells\n\npaint the bosom of the earth, where with soft waves\n\nthe sea has fed the thirsty sand of the curving shore.\n\nWherefore, again and again, by a similar principle it is necessary,\n\nsince they endure by nature and are not made by hand,\n\nthat the first-beginnings of things are not fashioned\n\nto one fixed form, but fly about, some unlike one another in shape.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0026","text":"It is very easy for us to resolve by the reasoning of the mind\n\nwhy lightning's fire is far more penetrating\n\nhow our [fire] flows from earthly torches;\n\nfor you might say that the heavenly fire of lightning\n\nconsists of subtler and smaller shapes,\n\nand therefore passes through openings through which the fire\n\nof ours, arisen here from logs and created from torch-pine, cannot pass.\n\nMoreover, light passes through horn, but rain\n\nis repelled. Why, unless those [bodies] of light are smaller\n\nthan those of which the nourishing liquid of waters consists?\n\nAnd although we see wines suddenly flow\n\nthrough a strainer, olive oil, on the contrary, lingers slowly,\n\neither because, doubtless, it consists of larger elements\n\nor of elements more hooked and interwoven with one another,\n\nand thus it comes about that the first-beginnings are not so suddenly drawn apart\n\nfrom one another, each individual one\n\nseeping singly through the openings of each [strainer].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0027","text":"To this is added the fact that the liquids of honey and milk\n\naffect the tongue in the mouth with a pleasant sensation;\n\nbut, on the contrary, the foul nature of wormwood and of fierce\n\ncentaury wracks the mouth with a loathsome taste;\n\nso that you may readily recognize that those things which can touch the senses\n\npleasantly are composed of smooth and round [particles],\n\nwhereas, on the contrary, whatever things seem bitter and rough,\n\nthese are held together more by hooked [particles] linked with one another\n\nand for that reason are accustomed to cut through our passages\n\nto break through the body by their senses and their entrance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0028","text":"Finally, all good things and bad things in their effect upon the senses\n\ncontend among themselves, being formed in unlike shapes;\n\nlest perhaps you should suppose that the bitter\n\nharshness of the grating saw consists equally of smooth elements\n\nand the music of the Muses, which through the strings of an instrument\n\nthey fashion, awakened by the moving fingers;\n\nnor suppose that first-beginnings of similar shape penetrate\n\ninto men's nostrils when foul corpses are burning,\n\nand when the stage is freshly drenched with Cilician saffron\n\nand the altar nearby breathes out Panchaean scents;\n\nnor should you establish that the good colours of things consist of similar\n\nseed, colours which can feed the eyes,\n\nand those which prick the sight and compel it to weep,\n\nor which appear foul and hideous in their ugly appearance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0029","text":"For every thing whatsoever that soothes the senses for you\n\nhas not been created without some primary smoothness;\n\nbut, on the contrary, whatever is constituted troublesome and rough\n\nhas not been found without some harshness of matter.\n\nThere are also things which are now rightly judged neither smooth\n\nnor at all hooked with curved points,\n\nbut rather with angles projecting slightly, so that they can\n\ntickle the senses rather than wound them;\n\nof this kind are, now, dregs and the flavours of elecampane.\n\nFinally, that hot fires and icy frost\n\nby unlike means prick the senses of the body\n\nis an indication to us of each kind of touch.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0030","text":"For touch—touch, by the holy divinities of the gods—\n\nis the body's sense, whether an external thing\n\nmakes its way in, or whether that which has arisen within the body harms it\n\nor gives pleasure as it issues forth in the generative acts of Venus,\n\nor when, from a blow, within the body itself\n\nthe stirred seeds disturb and confound the sense among themselves;\n\nas, for instance, if you yourself should happen with your hand\n\nto strike any part of your own body and test it.\n\nWherefore it must needs be that the shapes differ greatly\n\nin the first-beginnings, which can produce diverse sensations.\n\nFinally, those things which seem to us hard and dense\n\nmust needs consist more of hooked atoms interlinked among themselves\n\nand be held together, as it were, closely compacted by branching forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0031","text":"In this class, now, foremost are adamantine rocks,\n\nwhich stand firm in the first rank, accustomed to scorn blows,\n\nand strong flints and the hard strength of iron\n\nand bronzes that cry out in resistance to bolts.\n\nThose things, indeed, ought to consist more of smooth and round atoms\n\nwhich are liquid, made up of a flowing body.\n\nFor a draught of poppy-seed is likewise easy to drink as water is;\n\nfor the particles of each are not held together among themselves\n\nand, when struck, likewise stands out inclined to roll.\n\nFinally, all things which you discern in a moment of time\n\nto scatter abroad, like smoke, clouds, and flames, must,\n\nif they are not all composed of smooth and round [elements],\n\nyet not be entangled and intertwined in such a way\n\nthat they can prick bodies and penetrate rocks,\n\nand yet not cling together among themselves; whatever we see\n\nto be jagged to the senses, so that you may easily recognize\n\nthat it consists not of entangled, but of sharp, elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0032","text":"But that the same things which are fluid you see to be bitter\n\n—as is the brine of the sea—ought by no means to be wonderful;\n\n* * *\n\nfor, in so far as it is fluid, it is composed of smooth and round [elements],\n\nbut hooked bodies, productive of pain, are mixed with the smooth.\n\nYet these hooked [bodies] need not be held back:\n\nto be sure, they are nevertheless spherical, since they are composed rough,\n\nso that they may at once roll onward and injure the senses.\n\nAnd that you may think that rougher [elements] are mixed with the smooth\n\nfirst beginnings, whence comes the bitter substance of Neptune,\n\nthere is a means of separating and seeing them apart,\n\nwhere the same fresh moisture, more abundantly through the lands,\n\nis filtered, so that it flows into a pit and grows mild;\n\nfor it leaves above the first beginnings of foul savor,\n\nthe rougher to be able the more to cling in the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0033","text":"Since I have shown this, I shall go on to link a matter\n\nwhich may suitably derive credence from this: that the first-beginnings of things\n\nare limited in the variety of their shapes.\n\nBut if this were not so, then certain seeds would have to be\n\nbe of infinite increase in body.\n\nFor within the same single smallness of any body,\n\nthe shapes cannot differ much among themselves. Suppose, indeed, that the first\n\nbodies consist of three smallest parts, or increase them by a few more;\n\nsurely, when you have placed all those parts of one body,\n\nthe highest and the lowest, changing right and left,\n\nand have tried every manner, you will see what appearance of form\n\neach arrangement gives to the shape of that whole body;\n\nif thereafter by chance you wish to vary the shapes,\n\nyou will have to add other parts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0034","text":"It will then follow,\n\nby like reasoning, that the arrangement will demand others,\n\nif by chance you wish also to vary the shapes.\n\nTherefore an increase of body follows upon the novelty\n\nof shapes. Wherefore you cannot believe that seeds\n\nare distinct in infinitely many shapes,\n\nunless you compel some to be of monstrous vastness—\n\na thing which, as I have already shown, cannot be proved.\n\nAlready barbarian garments and the gleaming Meliboean\n\npurple, touched by the Thessalian colour of shells,\n\nthe golden generations of peacocks, steeped in smiling beauty,\n\nwould lie surpassed by the novel colour of things;\n\nand the scent of myrrh and the flavours of honey, held in contempt,\n\nand the swan's melodies and Phoebean songs wrought upon strings\n\nwould in like manner be overwhelmed and fall silent;\n\nfor something else, more excellent than each, would arise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0035","text":"Likewise all things could thus recede backward into worse things\n\nby parts, as we said into better;\n\nfor something else too would be more foul for nostrils,\n\nears, and eyes, and for the taste of the mouth.\n\nSince these things are not so, but a fixed limit assigned to things\n\nholds the whole on either side, you must admit that matter also\n\ndiffers in limited shapes.\n\nFinally, from fires up to the cold frosts of winter\n\nthere is a limit, and the distance back is measured by equal reasoning.\n\nFor every heat and cold and the temperate warmths between\n\nlie in order, filling out the whole between the two.\n\nTherefore created things differ by a limited ratio,\n\nsince they are marked on either side by a two-edged point,\n\nhere assailed by flames, there by stiff frosts.\n\nSince I have shown this, I shall go on to link a matter which\n\nmay suitably derive credence from this: that the first-beginnings of things,\n\nwhich are made perfect in a shape similar among themselves,\n\nare known to be infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0036","text":"For, since the difference\n\nof shapes is limited, it is necessary that those which are alike\n\nbe infinite, or that the sum of matter\n\nconsist of a finite amount—which I have proved not to be so.\n\n* * *\n\nIn verses I shall show that the particles of matter\n\nfrom infinity maintain the sum of things continually\n\non every side by an unbroken succession of blows.\n\nFor although you see certain living creatures to be more rare\n\nand discern nature to be more fertile in those,\n\nyet in another region and place, and in lands remote,\n\nthere may be many of that kind and the number be filled up;\n\njust as among quadrupeds we see, before all else,\n\nthe elephant, with snaky hands, in whose kind India\n\nis fortified by a barrier of many thousands of ivory,\n\nso that it cannot be penetrated within: so great is the host\n\nof wild beasts, of which we see exceedingly few examples.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0037","text":"But yet let me grant this also; let there be, if you please,\n\nsome single thing alone, unique in its native body,\n\nsuch that nothing like it exists in the whole circle of lands;\n\nnevertheless, unless the force of matter is infinite,\n\nfrom which it may be produced after being conceived, it will not be able\n\nto be created, nor, what remains, to grow and be nourished.\n\nFor suppose that the generative particles of one single thing, finite in number, are throughout\n\ncast about among all bodies by others,\n\nwhence, when they have come together, by what force and in what manner will they unite\n\nin so great a sea and in a strange throng of matter?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0038","text":"They do not, I think, possess a principle of bringing together;\n\nbut just as, when great and many shipwrecks have occurred,\n\nthe great sea is wont to scatter benches and hollow hulls,\n\nyardarms, prows, masts, and swimming oars,\n\nfloating decorations over all the shores of the lands,\n\nso that they may be seen and give warning to mortals\n\nof the snares, the force, and the treachery of the faithless sea,\n\nso that they may wish to avoid them and never trust it at any time,\n\nwhen the deceptive guile of the calm sea smiles;\n\nthus, if once you posit certain first beginnings as finite,\n\nthey must, scattered throughout all time,\n\nbe dispersed by the differing tides of matter,\n\nso that, driven together, they can never assemble into a union,\n\nnor remain in union, nor grow by addition;\n\nyet manifest reality plainly teaches that both occur:\n\nthat things are produced and, once produced, can increase.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0039","text":"Therefore it is plainly the case that in every kind there are\n\ninfinite first beginnings of things, whence all things are supplied.\n\nThus destructive motions cannot prevail\n\ncontinually, nor bury well-being for eternity;\n\nnor, further, can the generative and augmentative\n\nmotions of things continually preserve what has been created.\n\nThus is the contest of the first-beginnings waged on equal terms,\n\na war drawn out from infinite time.\n\nnow here, now there, the vital elements of things prevail\n\nand likewise are overcome. Wailing is mingled with the funeral cry,\n\nwhich infants raise when they behold the shores of light;\n\nnor has any night followed day, nor dawn night,\n\nthat has not heard lamentations mingled with sickly wailings,\n\ncompanions of death and of the black funeral.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0040","text":"This also is fitting to have sealed up in these matters\n\nand to keep committed to mindful memory:\n\nthat there is nothing, among things whose nature appears manifest,\n\nwhich consists of one single kind of first-beginnings,\n\nnor anything that does not consist of mingled seed.\n\nAnd whatever possesses more powers and many forces in itself,\n\nshows thereby that very many kinds of first-beginnings\n\nand various shapes exist within it.\n\nFirst, the earth has within itself the primary bodies,\n\nwhence fountains, rolling their coldness, may continually renew the vast sea;\n\nit has whence fires may arise;\n\nfor in many places the soils of the earth, set ablaze, burn,\n\nbut from its depths the force of Aetna rages with fires.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0041","text":"Then moreover it has whence it may raise up shining crops and luxuriant trees\n\nfor the peoples of humankind,\n\nwhence also rivers, foliage, and luxuriant pastures\n\nmay furnish [food] for the mountain-roaming race of wild beasts.\n\nwherefore she is called the great mother of the gods and mother of wild beasts\n\nand, she alone, the begetter of our body.\n\nHer the learned poets of the Greeks of old have sung\n\nas driving a pair of yoked lions in a chariot from her seat,\n\nteaching that the great earth hangs in the expanse of air\n\nand that earth cannot rest upon earth.\n\nThey yoked wild beasts [to her], because however wild the offspring,\n\nconquered, ought to be softened by the duties of parents.\n\nThey encircled her lofty head with a mural crown,\n\nbecause, fortified in choice places, she supports cities.\n\nNow adorned with this emblem throughout great lands,\n\nthe image of the divine mother is borne in awe-inspiring fashion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0042","text":"By the ancient rite of sacred observances, diverse peoples\n\ncall her the Idaean Mother and assign Phrygian bands\n\nas companions, because from those regions first, they say,\n\ncrops began to be produced throughout the circles of the earth.\n\nThey assign Galli [to her], because those who have violated the divinity\n\nof the Mother and have been found ungrateful to their parents,\n\nthey wish to signify, ought to be thought unworthy\n\nto bring forth living offspring into the realms of light.\n\nTaut drums thunder beneath palms, and hollow cymbals round about;\n\nhorns threaten with their hoarse-sounding song,\n\nand the hollow pipe goads minds with its Phrygian rhythm,\n\nand bear weapons before her, signs of violent frenzy,\n\nso as to terrify the ungrateful minds and impious breasts of the populace\n\nwith fear, which are able to be [terrified] by the goddess's divine power.\n\nTherefore, as soon as she has been borne into the great cities,\n\nshe bestows a mute salvation upon mortals in silentness,\n\nthey carpet every course of the roads with bronze and silver\n\nenriching [her] with lavish tribute, and shower [her] with roses,\n\novershadowing the mother and the troop of her attendants with flowers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0043","text":"Here an armed band—the Greeks call them Curetes—\n\nwhom they relate, if perhaps they are among the Phrygian companies,\n\nplay and leap in rhythm, glad in blood,\n\nshaking the terrible crests of their heads in divine frenzy;\n\nthey represent the Dictaean Curetes, who, they say,\n\nonce concealed that crying of Jupiter in Crete,\n\nwhen boys around the boy, in swift dance,\n\narmed, beat bronze against bronze in rhythm,\n\nlest Saturn, having got hold of him, should consign him to evil\n\nand give his mother an eternal wound beneath her breast.\n\nFor this reason armed men accompany the great mother,\n\nor because they signify that the goddess declares that they should wish, by arms\n\nand valour, to defend their native land\n\nand to be a protection and an honour to their parents.\n\nAlthough these things are handed down as well and excellently arranged,\n\nyet they have been driven far away from true reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0044","text":"for it is necessary that the whole nature of the gods, in itself,\n\nenjoy eternal life with the utmost peace,\n\nremoved and far separated from our affairs;\n\nfor, free from every pain, free from dangers,\n\nitself strong in its own resources, needing nothing from us,\n\nit is neither won by good services nor touched by anger.\n\nThe earth indeed is at all times devoid of sensation,\n\nand because it possesses the first-beginnings of many things,\n\nit brings many things forth into the light of the sun in many ways.\n\nHere, if anyone shall resolve to call the sea Neptune and Ceres grain,\n\nand to make use of the name of Bacchus\n\nrather than to utter the proper name of liquid,\n\nlet us concede that he may also declare this orb of lands\n\nto be the mother of the gods, provided only that he himself\n\nrefrains from tainting his mind with foul religion in truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0045","text":"Thus often, feeding on grass from one field,\n\nthe wool-bearing flocks and the warlike offspring of horses\n\nand the herds of horned cattle, beneath the same canopy of heaven\n\nand slaking their thirst from one stream of water,\n\nlive with unlike appearance and retain that of their parents,\n\nand each imitates, according to its kind, its nature and habits.\n\nSo great is the dissimilarity of matter in every kind of plant,\n\nso great is it likewise in every stream.\n\nHence furthermore, however much one living creature [be formed] from all [these].\n\nbones, blood, veins, heat, moisture, entrails, nerves\n\nconstitute—which, moreover, are far different,\n\nbeing made perfect by the dissimilar shape of their first-beginnings.\n\nThen, moreover, whatever things, kindled, burn with fire,\n\neven if nothing else, yet contain these things in their body,\n\nwhence they can cast forth fire and send out light\n\nand drive forth sparks and scatter ashes far and wide.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0046","text":"Surveying the rest by a like reasoning of the mind,\n\nyou will therefore find that they conceal in their body\n\nthe seeds of many things and hold in check various shapes.\n\nLastly, you see many things to which colour and taste alike\n\nhave been imparted together with smell—most especially many fruits.\n\nThese, therefore, must consist of various shapes;\n\nfor smell penetrates where colour does not enter into the limbs,\n\nand colour likewise separately, taste separately, makes its way\n\ninto the senses; so that you may know that they differ in their first shapes.\n\nDissimilar forms, therefore, come together into one mass,\n\nand things consist of mingled seed.\n\nIndeed, even everywhere in these verses of mine themselves\n\nyou see many elements common to many words,\n\nalthough you must nevertheless admit that verses and words among themselves\n\nconsist of different elements from one another;\n\nnot because many letters do not occur in common,\n\nor because no two things among all are the same with one another,\n\nBut because not all things are universally alike for all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047","text":"Thus likewise in other things, although many are common\n\nto many things, the first-beginnings of things\n\ncan nevertheless subsist in a sum differing among themselves; so that rightly it may be said that\n\nthe human race, and crops and flourishing trees, consist of different [elements].\n\nthe human race and crops and flourishing trees.\n\nYet it must not be thought that all things can be connected in every way;\n\nfor you would see prodigies commonly come to pass,\n\nhalf-wild races of men arise, and tall [trees]\n\nbe generated at times from living body,\n\nand many land-limbs be joined to sea-[limbs],\n\nthen Chimaeras breathing flame from their foul mouth\n\nbe fed by nature, mother of all, over the earth.\n\nThat none of these things comes to pass is manifest, since all things,\n\ncreated from fixed seeds and a fixed mother,\n\nwe see are able, as they grow, to preserve their kind.\n\nClearly, it necessarily happens by a fixed principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0048","text":"For, from all foods, bodies pass within into their own limbs for each thing,\n\nand, joined together, produce suitable motions; but, conversely, we see\n\nnature cast alien things back upon the earth, and many things, driven by blind\n\nblows, flee from bodies out of the body,\n\ndriven by blind blows, flee from bodies out of the body,\n\nwhich could neither be joined anywhere nor within\n\naccord with and imitate the vital motions.\n\nBut lest perhaps you think that living creatures alone are held\n\nby these laws, a certain principle sets bounds to all things.\n\nFor just as all things born are dissimilar\n\nfrom one another in their whole nature, so each one must necessarily\n\nconsist of a dissimilar shape of first-beginnings;\n\nnot because many things are not endowed with a rather similar form,\n\nbut because all things do not universally consist alike for all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0049","text":"Moreover, since the seeds differ, necessarily there differ\n\ntheir intervals, paths, connections, weights, blows,\n\nencounters, motions; and these not only separate living creatures\n\nin their bodies, but also distinguish lands and the whole sea\n\nand keep all the sky apart from the lands.\n\nCome now, receive what has been sought out by my sweet labor,\n\nlest perhaps you should suppose that these things which you discern white before your eyes\n\nare from white first-beginnings,\n\nor that those which are black are born from black seed;\n\nor that things imbued with any other color whatsoever\n\nbear this color for the reason that the bodies\n\nof matter are tinged with a color similar to it;\n\nfor there is no color at all in the bodies\n\nof matter, neither like to things nor, indeed, unlike.\n\nIf perhaps it seems to you that no casting of the mind can be made upon these bodies,\n\nyou wander far from the path.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0050","text":"For since those born blind, who have never [seen] the light of the sun\n\nthough they have discerned them, nevertheless recognize bodies by touch\n\nas joined from their earliest age with no color,\n\nyou may know that bodies too can come to the knowledge of our mind\n\nwithout being smeared round about with any coloring.\n\nFinally, whatever things we ourselves touch in blind\n\ndarkness, we do not perceive as dyed with any color.\n\nSince I have shown that this happens, I shall now teach that they are [colorless].\n\nFor every color is wholly changed into every [other color];\n\nand the first-beginnings ought by no means to do this;\n\nfor something unchangeable must remain over,\n\nlest all things be utterly reduced to nothing;\n\nfor whatever has changed and passes beyond its own limits,\n\nat once this is the death of that which it was before.\n\nTherefore take care not to endow the seeds of things with color,\n\nlest all things utterly return for you to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0051","text":"Moreover, if no nature of color has been assigned to the first-beginnings,\n\nand they are endowed with various shapes,\n\nfrom which they produce and vary every kind of colors,\n\nbecause it greatly matters which seeds\n\nare held together with which others and in what arrangement,\n\nand what motions they impart to and receive from one another,\n\nyou can very readily at once give an account\n\nwhy those things which a little before were of black color\n\ncan suddenly become of marble whiteness,\n\nas the sea, when the great winds have stirred its plains,\n\nis turned into grey waves with gleaming marble whiteness;\n\nfor you might say that what we often see as black,\n\nwhen its material is mixed together and its arrangement\n\namong its first-beginnings altered, with certain elements added and removed,\n\nthereupon comes to pass that it seems gleaming and white.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0052","text":"But if the sea's plains consisted of azure\n\nseeds, they could by no means grow white;\n\nfor however you might disturb things that are blue,\n\nthey could never pass over into a marble colour.\n\nBut if the seeds that produce the sea's\n\nsingle, pure sheen are dyed with one colour and another,\n\nthen, just as often from other forms and varied shapes\n\nsomething square and of a single shape is produced,\n\nit was fitting that, as we discern that in a square there are\n\nunlike shapes, so in the sea's expanse we should discern\n\nor in any other single and pure sheen\n\ncolours far unlike one another and various.\n\nMoreover, unlike shapes do nothing to hinder or obstruct\n\nthe whole from being no less square outwardly;\n\nbut the various colours of things impede and prevent\n\nthe whole thing from being able to possess a single sheen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0053","text":"Then furthermore, that which leads and allures us to ascribe\n\ncolours sometimes to the first-beginnings of things—the reason—\n\nperishes, since white things are not created from white,\n\nnor are those called black from black, but from varied things.\n\nfor indeed they will arise much more readily,\n\nwhite things born from no colour than from black colour\n\nor from any other whatsoever, which might fight and oppose.\n\nMoreover, since colours cannot exist without light\n\nnor do the first-beginnings of things come forth into the light,\n\nyou may know that they are veiled in no colour;\n\nfor what colour can there be in blind darkness?\n\nIndeed, colour itself is changed by light, because it\n\nshines when struck by direct or slanting light;\n\nas the plumage of doves is seen in the sun,\n\nwhich, placed about the nape, crowns the neck;\n\nfor at one time it happens to be red with bright ruby,\n\nat times, in some manner, it happens that it seems\n\nto mingle green emeralds amid blue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0054","text":"And the peacock's tail, when it is filled with abundant light,\n\nchanges its colours in a similar manner when turned about;\n\nand since these are produced by a certain stroke of light,\n\nyou may know that it must be thought impossible for them to occur without it.\n\nAnd since the pupil receives into itself a certain kind of blow\n\nwhen it is said to perceive a white colour,\n\nand then another when it perceives black and the rest,\n\nnor does it matter of what colour those things which you touch may happen to be\n\nare endowed, but with what sort of figure they are more fitted,\n\nyou may know that the first beginnings have no need of colour,\n\nbut, varying by diverse shapes, produce varying touches.\n\nMoreover, since the nature of colour is not fixed by fixed\n\nfigures, and all the formations of the first beginnings\n\ncan exist in any brightness,\n\nwhy are those things which consist of them not likewise\n\nsteeped in every kind of colour in every kind?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0055","text":"For it would be fitting that ravens too, often flying,\n\nshould cast forth a white colour from white feathers,\n\nand that black swans should arise from black seed,\n\nor from any other single and varied colour.\n\nIndeed, the more each thing is drawn apart into minute\n\nparts, the more you can discern\n\nthat colour gradually vanishes and is extinguished;\n\nas happens when purple cloth is torn into small parts:\n\npurple and the bright scarlet colour in great measure,\n\nwhen it has been drawn apart thread by thread, is wholly lost;\n\nso that from this you may know that the particles breathe out all their colour\n\nbefore they depart to the first beginnings of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0056","text":"Finally, since you grant that not all bodies\n\ngive forth sound or smell, it follows\n\nthat you do not attribute sounds and smells to all things:\n\nthus, since we cannot see all things with our eyes,\n\nOne may know that certain things subsist wholly without colour\n\njust as certain things are without any smell and removed from sound,\n\nnor is a keen mind any less able to perceive these\n\nthan to discern things deprived of other properties.\n\nBut lest perhaps you should think that bodies first principles remain stripped of colour alone,\n\nthey are also wholly devoid of heat,\n\nand cold and hot vapour,\n\nand are said to be barren of sound and empty of taste,\n\nnor do they emit from their body any smell of their own.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0057","text":"Just as, when you set about making the pleasant liquid of marjoram and stacte\n\nand the flower of nard, nectar that breathes fragrance into the nostrils,\n\nit is especially proper to seek out,\n\nif you can and are able to find it, the nature of scentless olive oil,\n\nwhich sends no breath at all into the nostrils,\n\nso that as little as possible it may be able to destroy the odours mixed in the compound\n\nand blended, by assailing them with its own strength;\n\nfor the same reason the first-beginnings of things ought\n\nnot to contribute their own smell to things being produced,\n\nnor sound, since they can emit nothing from themselves,\n\nnor, by like reasoning, any taste at all,\n\nnor cold, nor likewise hot or tepid vapour,\n\nnor the other things which are nevertheless such as to consist of mortal nature:\n\nsoft, pliant, brittle, rotten, hollow, and of sparse body—\n\nall these must be separate from the first principles.\n\nif we wish to assign to things immortal\n\nfoundations, upon which the sum of salvation may rest;\n\nlest all things utterly return to nothing for you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0058","text":"Now, whatever things we see to possess sensation, you must needs\n\nnevertheless admit that all consist of insentient\n\nprinciples. Nor do manifest facts refute this\n\nor contend against it—facts which are known and ready to hand—\n\nbut rather themselves lead us by the hand and compel us to believe\n\nthat living creatures are generated from insentient things, as I say.\n\nFor indeed one may see living worms come into being\n\nfrom foul dung, when the earth, having acquired rottenness for itself,\n\nhas become moist from unseasonable rains.\n\nMoreover, in like manner all things transform themselves.\n\nRivers transform themselves into foliage and luxuriant fodder,\n\ninto cattle; cattle transform their nature into our bodies,\n\nand often from our body the strength and bodies of wild beasts\n\nand of winged creatures are increased.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0059","text":"Therefore nature transforms all foods into living bodies\n\nand from these produces all the senses of living creatures,\n\nby no far different method than she\n\nunfolds dry logs into flames and transforms everything into fires.\n\nDo you not see, then, that the first-beginnings of great things\n\nmatter greatly in what order each is placed\n\nand with which things, when mixed, they impart and receive motions?\n\nThen furthermore, what is it that strikes the mind itself,\n\nthat moves it and compels it to utter varied sensations—\n\ndo you not believe that the sentient is produced from the insentient?\n\nUnless, indeed, stones and wood and earth, though mingled together,\n\nare nevertheless unable to render vital sensation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0060","text":"Therefore, in these matters it will be fitting to remember this:\n\nnot that from absolutely all things whatsoever that create\n\nsentient things I say sensations are forthwith produced,\n\nbut that it matters greatly, first, of what smallness they consist—\n\nthe things that make the sentient—and with what shape they are endowed,\n\nwhat motions, arrangements, and positions, finally, they possess.\n\nOf these things we see nothing in wood and clods of earth;\n\nand yet these, when they have been, as it were, rotted by rains,\n\nproduce little worms, because the bodies of matter,\n\nmoved from their ancient arrangements by a new circumstance,\n\nare so brought together as they ought to be for animals to be produced.\n\nNext, those who maintain that the sentient can be created from sentient things,\n\nand, further, from others accustomed to sensation\n\n* * *\n\nmake them soft; for every sensation is joined\n\nto viscera, nerves, and veins, all of which we see\n\nto be composed soft in a mortal body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0061","text":"But let it now be granted that these can remain eternal;\n\nyet surely they must either have the sensation of parts\n\nor be thought similar to whole animals.\n\nBut it is necessary that the parts should be unable to feel by themselves:\n\nfor the mind rejects every sensation of the limbs,\n\nnor can a hand, separated from us, nor any\n\npart of the body whatsoever, by itself retain sensation.\n\nIt remains that they be likened to whole living creatures,\n\nso that they may be able to share everywhere in vital sensation.\n\nHow then will they be able to be called the first-beginnings of things,\n\nand to avoid the paths of death, when they are living creatures,\n\nand living creatures of one and the same kind as mortal things?\n\nYet, though they could do this, by gathering and assembly\n\nthey will effect nothing beyond a populace and a throng of living creatures,\n\nnamely, so that neither men nor herds nor wild beasts\n\ncan produce anything among themselves by coming together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0062","text":"Thus likewise the things that we perceive must necessarily perceive.\n\nBut if perhaps they abandon their own sensation with their body\n\nand take on another, what need was there for that which\n\nis taken away to be assigned? Then besides, as we set forth before,\n\ninasmuch as we see the eggs of birds changed into living chicks\n\nand the earth teem with worms,\n\nwhen stench has seized it because of unseasonable rains,\n\none may know that sensations can be produced from things without sensation.\n\nBut if perhaps someone will say that sensation can arise only\n\nfrom non-sensation through change,\n\nor by some offspring, as it were, which is brought forth outside,\n\nfor him it will be enough to make that plain and prove,\n\nthat no offspring comes to be except after a union has first been brought together,\n\nnor is anything changed without union.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0063","text":"First, there cannot be sensations of any body\n\nbefore the very nature of a living creature has been born,\n\nunless, indeed, because matter is held dispersed\n\nin air, rivers, lands, and things created from earth,\n\nnor, having assembled in a manner suited to life,\n\nhas it brought together among themselves motions by which, surveying all things,\n\nkindled senses preserve each living creature.\n\nMoreover, a blow greater than the nature of any living creature can endure\n\nsuddenly strikes it and proceeds to confound every\n\nsensation of body and mind.\n\nFor the arrangements of the first-beginnings are dissolved,\n\nand the vital motions are thoroughly impeded,\n\nuntil all matter, shaken through the limbs,\n\nloosens the knots of the vital soul from the body\n\nand casts it, dispersed, entirely outside through all the passages;\n\nfor what else do we suppose an encountered blow can do\n\nexcept scatter and dissolve everything?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0064","text":"It also happens that, when a less severe blow is encountered,\n\nthe remaining vital motions are often wont to prevail,\n\nto prevail and to calm the great tumult of the blow.\n\nand to recall whatever back again into its own courses,\n\nand to dispel the motion of death, as it were already ruling in the body,\n\nand to kindle the senses almost lost;\n\nfor by what means rather, from the very threshold of death now,\n\ncan they return to life, their mind gathered together,\n\nthan by that toward which they have almost already run down, to go and depart?\n\nMoreover, since pain occurs when the bodies of matter,\n\nstirred by some force through the living entrails, through the limbs,\n\ntremble within in their own seats,\n\nand when they migrate back to their place, pleasant pleasure comes about,\n\none may know that the first-beginnings can be assailed by no pain\n\nand receive no pleasure from themselves;\n\nsince they are not composed of any bodies of first-beginnings,\n\nby whose novelty of motion they might suffer\n\nor gain some fruit of fostering sweetness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0065","text":"Therefore they must not be endowed with any sensation.\n\nFinally, in order that all animals may be able to feel,\n\nif sensation must now be attributed to their first-beginnings,\n\nwhat of those from which the human race in particular has been increased?\n\nSurely they too, shaken by quivering laughter, guffaw,\n\nand sprinkle face and cheeks with dripping tears,\n\nand are skilled in speaking much about the mixture of things,\n\nand moreover inquire for themselves what their first-beginnings are;\n\nsince indeed, made like all mortals,\n\nmust themselves also consist of other elements,\n\nthen others from others, never to come to a stop, if you dare?\n\nIndeed, I shall follow you, whatever you say can speak and laugh\n\nand understand, so that this same thing may arise from others doing these things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0066","text":"But if we perceive these things to be mad and frenzied,\n\nand [a thing] can laugh though not increased from laughing things,\n\nand understand and render an account in learned speech\n\nthough not from seeds that are understanding and eloquent,\n\nhow can those things which we see to have sensation be any less able\n\nto be mingled with seeds wholly lacking sensation?\n\nFinally, we all arise from heavenly seed;\n\nthat same one is father to all, from whom, when nurturing Earth has received the flowing\n\ndrops of moisture as mother,\n\npregnant, she bears bright crops and luxuriant trees\n\nand the human race; she bears every breed of wild beasts,\n\nwhen she provides the food by which all nourish their bodies\n\nand lead a sweet life and propagate offspring;\n\nwherefore she has deservedly acquired the name of mother.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0067","text":"Likewise, what was formerly from earth returns back\n\ninto the earth, and what was sent from the regions of ether,\n\nthat in turn the precincts of heaven receive back.\n\nNor does death so destroy things as to bring the bodies of matter\n\nto an end, but disperses their assemblage;\n\nthen it joins one thing to others and brings it about that all—\n\nso that they may change their shapes and alter their colours,\n\nand seize the senses and give them back in a moment of time;\n\nso that you may know with what [elements] the first-beginnings of these things are connected,\n\nand in what arrangement they are held together,\n\nand what motions they give and receive among themselves,\n\nand lest you suppose that the eternal first bodies can reside in themselves,\n\nbecause we see them floating on the surfaces of things\n\nand at times coming to birth and suddenly perishing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0068","text":"Indeed, it matters even in our verses themselves\n\nwith which [letters], and in what order, each is placed;\n\nfor the same [letters] signify heaven, sea, lands, rivers, sun;\n\nthe same [letters], crops, shrubs, living creatures;\n\nif they are not all the same, yet by far the greatest part is\n\nsimilar; but things differ by their arrangement.\n\nThus likewise in things themselves, now, the [elements] of matter—\n\ntheir collisions, motions, order, arrangement, shapes—\n\nwhen these are changed, the things too must be changed.\n\nNow apply your mind to true reasoning.\n\nFor a very new thing strives to reach your ears\n\nand a new aspect of things to reveal itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0069","text":"But no matter is so easy that at first it does not appear\n\nrather difficult to believe; and likewise\n\nthere is nothing so great nor anything so wonderful\n\nthat all do not gradually cease to marvel at it,\n\nfirst, the bright and pure color of heaven,\n\nand all that it contains within itself—the stars wandering everywhere,\n\nand the moon, and the splendor shining with the sun’s brilliant light;\n\nall these things, if now for the first time they existed for mortals,\n\nif they had suddenly been presented unexpectedly,\n\nwhat could be called more marvelous than these things,\n\nor what would nations have dared less to believe could come to pass?\n\nNothing, I think; so wondrous would this spectacle have been.\n\nYet now no one, wearied and sated with seeing,\n\ndeigns to look up at the bright temples of heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0070","text":"Therefore cease, terrified by novelty itself,\n\nto spit out the argument from your mind, but rather with keen\n\njudgment weigh it; and if it seems true to you,\n\nyield assent, or, if it is false, gird yourself against it.\n\nFor the mind seeks an account, since the totality of space is\n\ninfinite outside these walls of the world,\n\nwhat, further, is there there, whither the mind desires to look onward,\n\nand whither the free flight of the spirit itself may wing its way.\n\nFirst, for us in every direction on all sides,\n\nand on either flank, above and below, throughout the whole,\n\nthere is no end; as I have taught, and the thing itself\n\nproclaims aloud, and the nature of the deep makes clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","text":"By no means now must it be thought likely,\n\nsince empty space lies open infinitely in every direction\n\nand seeds in numberless quantity and in a deep sum\n\nfly about in manifold ways, stirred by everlasting motion,\n\nthat this one world of lands and heaven has been created,\n\nwhile all those bodies of matter do nothing outside it;\n\nespecially since this nature here has been made, and of itself\n\nby chance, through the seeds of things clashing together\n\nin manifold ways, rashly, vainly, and to no purpose, when forced together,\n\nat last those particles came together which, suddenly cast together,\n\nwould always become the beginnings of great things—\n\nof earth, sea, and heaven, and of the race of living creatures.\n\nTherefore again and again you must confess that there are\n\nother assemblages of matter elsewhere,\n\nsuch as this one which the ether holds in its greedy embrace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0072","text":"Moreover, when much matter is ready,\n\nwhen place is at hand and no thing or cause delays,\n\nthings must, assuredly, be done and come into being.\n\nNow, if there is so great an abundance of seeds as\n\nthe whole age of living creatures cannot avail to enumerate,\n\nand if the same nature remains which can cast the seeds\n\nof things into each place by a like method\n\nto that by which they have been cast hither, you must confess\n\nthat there are other worlds in other regions of the earths,\n\nand diverse races of men and generations of wild beasts.\n\nTo this is added that in the whole sum there is no single thing,\n\nsolely one that is begotten and solely one that grows,\n\nwithout belonging to some kind, and without very many being\n\nof the same kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","text":"Foremost among living creatures, with mind as guide,\n\nyou will find that thus there is a mountain-roaming race of wild beasts,\n\nthus a twofold offspring of men, thus finally the dumb\n\nflocks of scaled creatures and every body of birds.\n\nFor which reason it must be confessed by like reasoning that heaven,\n\nearth, and sun, moon, sea, and the rest that are,\n\nare not single, but rather innumerable in number;\n\nsince indeed the deeply fixed boundary of life\n\nabides for these just as much, and they consist of a body born by nature,\n\nas does every kind which abounds in these things by kinds.\n\nIf you hold these things well understood, nature is seen at once\n\nas free, deprived of haughty masters,\n\nherself doing all things of her own accord, through herself, free from the gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0074","text":"For, by the holy peace of the gods' tranquil breasts,\n\nwhich pass a placid age and a serene life,\n\nwho is able to govern the sum of the immeasurable, who to hold\n\nin his strong hand the mighty reins with control,\n\nwho at once to turn all the heavens and all\n\nto fumigate the fruitful lands with ethereal fires,\n\nand to be present in all places at every time,\n\nso that he makes darkness with clouds and shakes the clear skies\n\nwith sound, then sends thunderbolts and [overturns] temples\n\noften throws its own affairs into disorder and, withdrawing into deserted places,\n\nrages, wielding the weapon which often passes by the guilty\n\nand strikes lifeless the undeserving and the deserving?\n\nAnd long after the world's generative time and the first day\n\nof sea and earth and sun had arisen,\n\nbodies were added from without, seeds were added all around,\n\nwhich the great whole contributed by casting them about,\n\nwhence sea and lands might grow, and whence\n\nthe expanse of heaven might appear and the lofty house of the sky\n\nmight raise its roof far from the earth, and air might rise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0075","text":"For, from all places and regions, all bodies are distributed to each their own,\n\nand return to their own kinds:\n\nmoisture to moisture; with earthly body earth\n\ngrows; fires forge fire, and ether ether,\n\nuntil at last, to the final perfected limit of growth,\n\nthe creative nature of things has brought all things;\n\nas happens when there is now no more given within\n\nthe veins of life than flows away and departs.\n\nHere the age of all things must come to a standstill;\n\nhere nature checks their increase by her own powers.\n\nFor whatever things you see grow great with joyous increase\n\nand gradually climb the steps of mature age,\n\ntake to themselves more bodies than they send forth from themselves,\n\nso long as all nourishment is easily introduced into the veins and so long as\n\nare not so widely dispersed that they give off much,\n\nand cause more loss than age consumes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0076","text":"For indeed, that many bodies flow away and withdraw from things\n\nmust certainly be granted; but more must be added,\n\nuntil they have reached the highest summit of growth.\n\nThereafter, little by little, age breaks\n\nstrength and mature vigor, and melts away into a worse condition.\n\nFor indeed, the larger a thing is, once growth has been taken away,\n\nand the broader it is, in every direction on all sides\n\nthe more bodies it now scatters and sends forth from itself;\n\nnor is all nourishment easily distributed into its veins,\n\nnor, in proportion to the abundant tides it seethes forth,\n\nis there enough from which so much can arise and be supplied.\n\nRightly, therefore, they perish, when, being rarefied by flowing away,\n\nthey are, and when all things succumb to external blows,\n\nsince indeed nourishment at last fails in great age,\n\nnor do bodies striking from without cease to wear down every thing\n\nand to subdue it with hostile blows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0077","text":"Thus, therefore, the walls also of the great world all around,\n\nwhen stormed, will yield collapse and crumbling ruins;\n\nfor nourishment must renew and make whole all things,\n\nand nourishment support all things and sustain all things—\n\nto no avail, since neither do the veins endure\n\nwhat is enough, nor does nature supply as much as is needed.\n\nAnd now indeed the age is broken and the earth exhausted;\n\nit scarcely creates small animals, though it created all\n\ngenerations and gave forth the huge bodies of wild beasts in birth.\n\nFor not, I think, did a golden rope from above\n\nlower mortal generations from heaven into the fields,\n\nnor did the sea or waves breaking upon rocks create them,\n\nbut the same earth that now nourishes them from itself begot them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0078","text":"Moreover, shining crops and joyful vineyards\n\nit first itself created spontaneously for mortals;\n\nit itself gave sweet fruits and joyful fodder;\n\nwhich now scarcely grow large even when increased by our labor,\n\nand we wear out oxen and the strength of farmers,\n\nwe exhaust iron, scarcely supplied for the fields:\n\nso greatly do they stint their produce and increase toil.\n\nAnd now, shaking his head, the aged ploughman sighs\n\nmore often that his great labors have fallen in vain,\n\nand, when he compares present times with\n\ntimes past, often praises the fortunes of his father.\n\nLikewise the sad planter of an old and withered vine\n\nblames the course of time and rails at the age,\n\nand chatters that the ancient race, filled with piety,\n\nmost easily endured life within narrow bounds,\n\nwhen formerly the measure of land for each man was much smaller;\n\nnor does he grasp that all things gradually waste away and go\n\nwearied unto the grave by the length of a long lifetime.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0078"]}],"source_digest":"da2221eea3c996f09c346f482f1a6930550173c822307ef31a30249403507ccf","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Liber Secundus"}],"passage_basis":"revised-passages","original_title":"Liber Secundus","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT","title":"Book Three","order":3,"chapter_id":"CH_0T6T_LIBT","text":"## Liber Tertius\n\n  \n\nE tenebris tantis tam clarum extollere lumen\n\n \n\nqui primus potuisti inlustrans commoda vitae,\n\n \n\nte sequor, o Graiae gentis decus, inque tuis nunc\n\n \n\nficta pedum pono pressis vestigia signis,\n\n \n\nnon ita certandi cupidus quam propter amorem\n\n \n\nquod te imitari aveo; quid enim contendat hirundo\n\n \n\ncycnis, aut quid nam tremulis facere artubus haedi\n\n \n\nconsimile in cursu possint et fortis equi vis?\n\n \n\ntu, pater, es rerum inventor, tu patria nobis\n\n \n\nsuppeditas praecepta, tuisque ex, inclute, chartis,\n\n \n\nfloriferis ut apes in saltibus omnia libant,\n\n \n\nomnia nos itidem depascimur aurea dicta,\n\n \n\naurea, perpetua semper dignissima vita.\n\n \n\nnam simul ac ratio tua coepit vociferari\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum divina mente coorta\n\n \n\ndiffugiunt animi terrores, moenia mundi\n\n \n\ndiscedunt. totum video per inane geri res.\n\n \n\napparet divum numen sedesque quietae,\n\n \n\nquas neque concutiunt venti nec nubila nimbis\n\n \n\naspergunt neque nix acri concreta pruina\n\n \n\ncana cadens violat semper innubilus aether\n\n \n\nintegit et large diffuso lumine ridet:\n\n \n\nomnia suppeditat porro natura neque ulla\n\n \n\nres animi pacem delibat tempore in ullo.\n\n \n\nat contra nusquam apparent Acherusia templa,\n\n \n\nnec tellus obstat quin omnia dispiciantur,\n\n \n\nsub pedibus quae cumque infra per inane geruntur.\n\n \n\nhis ibi me rebus quaedam divina voluptas\n\n \n\npercipit atque horror, quod sic natura tua vi\n\n \n\ntam manifesta patens ex omni parte retecta est.\n\n \n\nEt quoniam docui, cunctarum exordia rerum\n\n \n\nqualia sint et quam variis distantia formis\n\n \n\nsponte sua volitent aeterno percita motu,\n\n \n\nquove modo possint res ex his quaeque creari,\n\n \n\nhasce secundum res animi natura videtur\n\n \n\natque animae claranda meis iam versibus esse\n\n \n\net metus ille foras praeceps Acheruntis agendus,\n\n \n\nfunditus humanam qui vitam turbat ab imo\n\n \n\nomnia suffundens mortis nigrore neque ullam\n\n \n\nesse voluptatem liquidam puramque relinquit.\n\n  \n\nnam quod saepe homines morbos magis esse timendos\n\n \n\ninfamemque ferunt vitam quam Tartara leti\n\n \n\net se scire animi naturam sanguinis esse,\n\n \n\naut etiam venti, si fert ita forte voluntas,\n\n \n\nnec prosum quicquam nostrae rationis egere,\n\n \n\nhinc licet advertas animum magis omnia laudis\n\n \n\niactari causa quam quod res ipsa probetur.\n\n \n\nextorres idem patria longeque fugati\n\n \n\nconspectu ex hominum, foedati crimine turpi,\n\n \n\nomnibus aerumnis adfecti denique vivunt,\n\n \n\net quo cumque tamen miseri venere parentant\n\n \n\net nigras mactant pecudes et manibus divis\n\n \n\ninferias mittunt multoque in rebus acerbis\n\n \n\n acrius advertunt animos ad religionem.\n\n \n\nquo magis in dubiis hominem spectare periclis\n\n \n\nconvenit adversisque in rebus noscere qui sit;\n\n \n\nnam verae voces tum demum pectore ab imo\n\n \n\neliciuntur  eripitur persona amanare.\n\n \n\ndenique avarities et honorum caeca cupido,\n\n \n\nquae miseros homines cogunt transcendere fines\n\n \n\niuris et inter dum socios scelerum atque ministros\n\n \n\nnoctes atque dies niti praestante labore\n\n \n\nad summas emergere opes, haec vulnera vitae\n\n \n\nnon minimam partem mortis formidine aluntur.\n\n \n\nturpis enim ferme contemptus et acris egestas\n\n \n\nsemota ab dulci vita stabilique videtur\n\n \n\net quasi iam leti portas cunctarier ante;\n\n \n\nunde homines dum se falso terrore coacti\n\n \n\neffugisse volunt longe longeque remosse,\n\n \n\nsanguine civili rem conflant divitiasque\n\n \n\nconduplicant avidi, caedem caede accumulantes,\n\n \n\ncrudeles gaudent in tristi funere fratris\n\n \n\net consanguineum mensas odere timentque.\n\n \n\nconsimili ratione ab eodem saepe timore\n\n \n\nmacerat invidia ante oculos illum esse potentem,\n\n \n\nillum aspectari, claro qui incedit honore,\n\n \n\nipsi se in tenebris volvi caenoque queruntur.\n\n \n\nintereunt partim statuarum et nominis ergo.\n\n \n\net saepe usque adeo, mortis formidine, vitae\n\n \n\npercipit humanos odium lucisque videndae,\n\n \n\nut sibi consciscant maerenti pectore letum\n\n \n\nobliti fontem curarum hunc esse timorem:\n\n \n\nhunc vexare pudorem, hunc vincula amicitiai\n\n \n\nrumpere et in summa pietate evertere suadet:\n\n \n\nnam iam saepe homines patriam carosque parentis\n\n \n\nprodiderunt vitare Acherusia templa petentes.\n\n \n\nnam vel uti pueri trepidant atque omnia caecis\n\n \n\nin tenebris metuunt, sic nos in luce timemus\n\n \n\ninter dum, nihilo quae sunt metuenda magis quam\n\n \n\nquae pueri in tenebris pavitant finguntque futura.\n\n \n\nhunc igitur terrorem animi tenebrasque necessest\n\n \n\nnon radii solis neque lucida tela diei\n\n \n\ndiscutiant, sed naturae species ratioque.\n\n  \n\nPrimum animum dico, mentem quem saepe vocamus,\n\n \n\nin quo consilium vitae regimenque locatum est,\n\n \n\nesse hominis partem nihilo minus ac manus et pes\n\n \n\natque oculei partes animantis totius extant.\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nsensum animi certa non esse in parte locatum,\n\n \n\nverum habitum quendam vitalem corporis esse,\n\n \n\nharmoniam Grai quam dicunt, quod faciat nos\n\n \n\nvivere cum sensu, nulla cum in parte siet mens;\n\n \n\nut bona saepe valetudo cum dicitur esse\n\n \n\ncorporis, et non est tamen haec pars ulla valentis,\n\n \n\nsic animi sensum non certa parte reponunt;\n\n \n\nmagno opere in quo mi diversi errare videntur.\n\n \n\nSaepe itaque, in promptu corpus quod cernitur, aegret,\n\n \n\ncum tamen ex alia laetamur parte latenti;\n\n \n\n et retro fit ubi contra sit saepe vicissim,\n\n \n\ncum miser ex animo laetatur corpore toto;\n\n \n\nnon alio pacto quam si, pes cum dolet aegri,\n\n \n\nin nullo caput interea sit forte dolore.\n\n \n\nPraeterea molli cum somno dedita membra\n\n \n\neffusumque iacet sine sensu corpus honustum,\n\n \n\nest aliud tamen in nobis quod tempore in illo\n\n \n\nmultimodis agitatur et omnis accipit in se\n\n \n\nlaetitiae motus et curas cordis inanis.\n\n \n\nNunc animam quoque ut in membris cognoscere possis\n\n \n\nesse neque harmonia corpus sentire solere,\n\n \n\nprincipio fit uti detracto corpore multo\n\n \n\nsaepe tamen nobis in membris vita moretur.\n\n \n\nAtque eadem rursum, cum corpora pauca caloris\n\n \n\ndiffugere forasque per os est editus aër,\n\n \n\ndeserit extemplo venas atque ossa relinquit;\n\n \n\nnoscere ut hinc possis non aequas omnia partis\n\n \n\ncorpora habere neque ex aequo fulcire salutem,\n\n \n\nsed magis haec, venti quae sunt calidique vaporis\n\n \n\nsemina, curare in membris ut vita moretur.\n\n \n\nest igitur calor ac ventus vitalis in ipso\n\n \n\ncorpore, qui nobis moribundos deserit artus.\n\n \n\nquapropter quoniam est animi natura reperta\n\n \n\natque animae quasi pars hominis, redde harmoniai\n\n \n\nnomen, ad organicos alto delatum Heliconi,\n\n \n\nsive aliunde ipsi porro traxere et in illam\n\n \n\ntranstulerunt, proprio quae tum res nomine egebat.\n\n \n\nquidquid  est, habeant: tu cetera percipe dicta.\n\n  \n\nNunc animum atque animam dico coniuncta teneri\n\n \n\ninter se atque unam naturam conficere ex se,\n\n \n\nsed caput esse quasi et dominari in corpore toto\n\n \n\nconsilium, quod nos animum mentemque vocamus.\n\n \n\nidque situm media regione in pectoris haeret.\n\n \n\nhic exultat enim pavor ac metus, haec loca circum\n\n \n\nlaetitiae mulcent: hic ergo mens animusquest.\n\n \n\ncetera pars animae per totum dissita corpus\n\n \n\nparet et ad numen mentis momenque movetur.\n\n \n\nidque sibi solum per se sapit et sibi gaudet,\n\n \n\ncum neque res animam neque corpus commovet una.\n\n \n\net quasi, cum caput aut oculus temptante dolore\n\n \n\nlaeditur in nobis, non omni concruciamur\n\n \n\ncorpore, sic animus nonnumquam laeditur ipse\n\n \n\nlaetitiaque viget, cum cetera pars animai\n\n \n\nper membra atque artus nulla novitate cietur;\n\n \n\nverum ubi vementi magis est commota metu mens,\n\n \n\nconsentire animam totam per membra videmus\n\n \n\nsudoresque ita palloremque existere toto\n\n \n\ncorpore et infringi linguam vocemque aboriri,\n\n \n\ncaligare oculos, sonere auris, succidere artus,\n\n \n\ndenique concidere ex animi terrore videmus\n\n \n\nsaepe homines; facile ut quivis hinc noscere possit\n\n \n\nesse animam cum animo coniunctam, quae cum animi \n\n \n\npercussa est, exim corpus propellit et icit.\n\n \n\nHaec eadem ratio naturam animi atque animai\n\n \n\ncorpoream docet esse; ubi enim propellere membra,\n\n \n\n corripere ex somno corpus mutareque vultum\n\n \n\natque hominem totum regere ac versare videtur,\n\n \n\nquorum nil fieri sine tactu posse videmus\n\n \n\nnec tactum porro sine corpore, nonne fatendumst\n\n \n\ncorporea natura animum constare animamque?\n\n \n\npraeterea pariter fungi cum corpore et una\n\n \n\nconsentire animum nobis in corpore cernis.\n\n \n\nsi minus offendit vitam vis horrida teli\n\n \n\nossibus ac nervis disclusis intus adacta,\n\n \n\nat tamen insequitur languor terraeque petitus\n\n \n\nsuavis et in terra mentis qui gignitur aestus\n\n \n\ninter dumque quasi exsurgendi incerta voluntas.\n\n \n\nergo corpoream naturam animi esse necessest,\n\n \n\ncorporeis quoniam telis ictuque laborat.\n\n \n\nIs tibi nunc animus quali sit corpore et unde\n\n \n\nconstiterit pergam rationem reddere dictis.\n\n \n\nprincipio esse aio persuptilem atque minutis\n\n \n\nperquam corporibus factum constare. id ita esse\n\n \n\nhinc licet advertas animum, ut pernoscere possis.\n\n  \n\n Nil adeo fieri celeri ratione videtur,\n\n \n\nquam si mens fieri proponit et inchoat ipsa;\n\n \n\nocius ergo animus quam res se perciet ulla,\n\n \n\nante oculos quorum in promptu natura videtur.\n\n \n\nat quod mobile tanto operest, constare rutundis\n\n \n\nperquam seminibus debet perquamque minutis,\n\n \n\nmomine uti parvo possint inpulsa moveri.\n\n \n\nnamque movetur aqua et tantillo momine flutat,\n\n \n\nquippe volubilibus parvisque creata figuris.\n\n \n\nat contra mellis constantior est natura\n\n \n\net pigri latices magis et cunctantior actus:\n\n \n\nhaeret enim inter se magis omnis materiai\n\n \n\ncopia, ni mirum quia non tam levibus extat\n\n \n\ncorporibus neque tam suptilibus atque rutundis.\n\n \n\nnamque papaveris aura potest suspensa levisque\n\n \n\ncogere ut ab summo tibi diffluat altus acervus,\n\n \n\nat contra lapidum coniectum spicarumque\n\n \n\nnoenu potest. igitur parvissima corpora pro quam\n\n \n\net levissima sunt, ita mobilitate fruuntur;\n\n \n\nat contra quae cumque magis cum pondere magno\n\n \n\nasperaque inveniuntur, eo stabilita magis sunt.\n\n \n\nnunc igitur quoniamst animi natura reperta\n\n \n\nmobilis egregie, perquam constare necessest\n\n \n\ncorporibus parvis et levibus atque rutundis.\n\n \n\nquae tibi cognita res in multis, o bone, rebus\n\n \n\nutilis invenietur et opportuna cluebit.\n\n \n\nHaec quoque res etiam naturam dedicat eius,\n\n \n\nquam tenui constet textura quamque loco se\n\n \n\ncontineat parvo, si possit conglomerari,\n\n \n\nquod simul atque hominem leti secura quies est\n\n \n\nindepta atque animi natura animaeque recessit,\n\n \n\nnil ibi libatum de toto corpore cernas\n\n \n\nad speciem, nihil ad pondus: mors omnia praestat,\n\n \n\nvitalem praeter sensum calidumque vaporem.\n\n \n\nergo animam totam perparvis esse necessest\n\n \n\nseminibus nexam per venas viscera nervos,\n\n \n\n qua tenus, omnis ubi e toto iam corpore cessit,\n\n \n\nextima membrorum circumcaesura tamen se\n\n \n\nincolumem praestat nec defit ponderis hilum.\n\n \n\nquod genus est, Bacchi cum flos evanuit aut cum\n\n \n\nspiritus unguenti suavis diffugit in auras\n\n \n\naut aliquo cum iam sucus de corpore cessit;\n\n \n\nnil oculis tamen esse minor res ipsa videtur\n\n \n\npropterea neque detractum de pondere quicquam,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia multa minutaque semina sucos\n\n \n\nefficiunt et odorem in toto corpore rerum.\n\n  \n\nquare etiam atque etiam mentis naturam animaeque\n\n \n\nscire licet perquam pauxillis esse creatam\n\n \n\nseminibus, quoniam fugiens nil ponderis aufert.\n\n \n\nNec tamen haec simplex nobis natura putanda est.\n\n \n\ntenvis enim quaedam moribundos deserit aura\n\n \n\nmixta vapore, vapor porro trahit aëra secum;\n\n \n\nnec calor est quisquam, cui non sit mixtus et aër;\n\n \n\nrara quod eius enim constat natura, necessest\n\n \n\naëris inter eum primordia multa moveri.\n\n \n\niam triplex animi est igitur natura reperta;\n\n \n\nnec tamen haec sat sunt ad sensum cuncta creandum,\n\n \n\nnil horum quoniam recipit mens posse creare\n\n \n\nsensiferos motus, quae denique mente volutat.\n\n \n\nquarta quoque his igitur quaedam natura necessest\n\n \n\nadtribuatur; east omnino nominis expers;\n\n \n\nqua neque mobilius quicquam neque tenvius extat\n\n \n\nnec magis e parvis et levibus ex elementis;\n\n \n\nsensiferos motus quae didit prima per artus.\n\n \n\nprima cietur enim, parvis perfecta figuris,\n\n \n\ninde calor motus et venti caeca potestas\n\n \n\naccipit, inde aër, inde omnia mobilitantur:\n\n \n\nconcutitur sanguis, tum viscera persentiscunt\n\n \n\nomnia, postremis datur ossibus atque medullis\n\n \n\nsive voluptas est sive est contrarius ardor.\n\n \n\nnec temere huc dolor usque potest penetrare neque acre\n\n \n\npermanare malum, quin omnia perturbentur\n\n \n\nusque adeo  vitae desit locus atque animai\n\n \n\ndiffugiant partes per caulas corporis omnis.\n\n \n\nsed plerumque fit in summo quasi corpore finis\n\n \n\nmotibus: hanc ob rem vitam retinere valemus.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quo pacto inter sese mixta quibusque\n\n \n\ncompta modis vigeant rationem reddere aventem\n\n \n\nabstrahit invitum patrii sermonis egestas;\n\n \n\nsed tamen, ut potero summatim attingere, tangam.\n\n  \n\ninter enim cursant primordia principiorum\n\n \n\nmotibus inter se, nihil ut secernier unum\n\n \n\npossit nec spatio fieri divisa potestas,\n\n \n\nsed quasi multae vis unius corporis extant.\n\n \n\nquod genus in quovis animantum viscere volgo\n\n \n\nest odor et quidam color et sapor, et tamen ex his\n\n \n\nomnibus est unum perfectum corporis augmen,\n\n \n\nsic calor atque aër et venti caeca potestas\n\n \n\nmixta creant unam naturam et mobilis illa\n\n \n\nvis, initum motus ab se quae dividit ollis,\n\n \n\nsensifer unde oritur primum per viscera motus.\n\n \n\n nam penitus prorsum latet haec natura subestque\n\n \n\nnec magis hac infra quicquam est in corpore nostro\n\n \n\natque anima est animae proporro totius ipsa.\n\n \n\nquod genus in nostris membris et corpore toto\n\n \n\nmixta latens animi vis est animaeque potestas,\n\n \n\ncorporibus quia de parvis paucisque creatast,\n\n \n\nsic tibi nominis haec expers vis, facta minutis\n\n \n\ncorporibus, latet atque animae quasi totius ipsa\n\n \n\nproporrost anima et dominatur corpore toto.\n\n \n\nconsimili ratione necessest ventus et aër\n\n \n\net calor inter se vigeant commixta per artus\n\n \n\natque aliis aliud subsit magis emineatque,\n\n \n\nut quiddam fieri videatur ab omnibus unum,\n\n \n\nni calor ac ventus seorsum seorsumque potestas\n\n \n\naëris interemant sensum diductaque solvant.\n\n  \n\nest etiam calor ille animo, quem sumit, in ira\n\n \n\ncum fervescit et ex oculis micat acrius ardor;\n\n \n\nest et frigida multa, comes formidinis, aura,\n\n \n\nquae ciet horrorem membris et concitat artus;\n\n \n\nest etiam quoque pacati status aëris ille,\n\n \n\npectore tranquillo fit qui voltuque sereno.\n\n \n\nsed calidi plus est illis quibus acria corda\n\n \n\niracundaque mens facile effervescit in ira,\n\n \n\nquo genere in primis vis est violenta leonum,\n\n \n\npectora qui fremitu rumpunt plerumque gementes\n\n \n\nnec capere irarum fluctus in pectore possunt.\n\n \n\nat ventosa magis cervorum frigida mens est\n\n \n\net gelidas citius per viscera concitat auras,\n\n \n\nquae tremulum faciunt membris existere motum.\n\n \n\nat natura boum placido magis aëre vivit\n\n \n\nnec nimis irai fax umquam subdita percit\n\n \n\nfumida, suffundens caecae caliginis umbra,\n\n \n\nnec gelidis torpet telis perfixa pavoris;\n\n \n\ninterutrasque sitast cervos saevosque leones.\n\n \n\nsic hominum genus est: quamvis doctrina politos\n\n \n\nconstituat pariter quosdam, tamen illa relinquit\n\n \n\nnaturae cuiusque animi vestigia prima.\n\n \n\nnec radicitus evelli mala posse putandumst,\n\n \n\nquin proclivius hic iras decurrat ad acris,\n\n \n\nille metu citius paulo temptetur, at ille\n\n \n\ntertius accipiat quaedam clementius aequo.\n\n \n\ninque aliis rebus multis differre necessest\n\n \n\nnaturas hominum varias moresque sequacis;\n\n \n\nquorum ego nunc nequeo caecas exponere causas\n\n \n\nnec reperire figurarum tot nomina quot sunt\n\n \n\nprincipiis, unde haec oritur variantia rerum.\n\n \n\nillud in his rebus video firmare potesse,\n\n \n\nusque adeo naturarum vestigia linqui\n\n \n\nparvola, quae nequeat ratio depellere nobis,\n\n \n\nut nihil inpediat dignam dis degere vitam.\n\n \n\nHaec igitur natura tenetur corpore ab omni\n\n \n\nipsaque corporis est custos et causa salutis;\n\n \n\nnam communibus inter se radicibus haerent\n\n \n\nnec sine pernicie divelli posse videntur.\n\n \n\nquod genus e thuris glaebis evellere odorem\n\n \n\n haud facile est, quin intereat natura quoque eius,\n\n \n\nsic animi atque animae naturam corpore toto\n\n \n\nextrahere haut facile est, quin omnia dissoluantur.\n\n \n\ninplexis ita principiis ab origine prima\n\n \n\ninter se fiunt consorti praedita vita,\n\n \n\nnec sibi quaeque sine alterius vi posse videtur\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animi seorsum sentire potestas,\n\n \n\nsed communibus inter eas conflatur utrimque\n\n \n\nmotibus accensus nobis per viscera sensus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea corpus per se nec gignitur umquam\n\n \n\nnec crescit neque post mortem durare videtur.\n\n \n\nnon enim, ut umor aquae dimittit saepe vaporem,\n\n \n\nqui datus est, neque ea causa convellitur ipse,\n\n \n\nsed manet incolumis, non, inquam, sic animai\n\n \n\ndiscidium possunt artus perferre relicti,\n\n \n\nsed penitus pereunt convulsi conque putrescunt.\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo sic corporis atque animai\n\n \n\nmutua vitalis discunt contagia motus,\n\n \n\nmaternis etiam membris alvoque reposta,\n\n \n\ndiscidium  nequeat fieri sine peste maloque;\n\n \n\nut videas, quoniam coniunctast causa salutis,\n\n \n\nconiunctam quoque naturam consistere eorum.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, siquis corpus sentire refutat\n\n \n\natque animam credit permixtam corpore toto\n\n \n\nsuscipere hunc motum quem sensum nominitamus,\n\n \n\nvel manifestas res contra verasque repugnat.\n\n \n\nquid sit enim corpus sentire quis adferet umquam,\n\n \n\nsi non ipsa palam quod res dedit ac docuit nos?\n\n \n\n'at dimissa anima corpus caret undique sensu.'\n\n \n\nperdit enim quod non proprium fuit eius in aevo\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea perdit quom expellitur aevo.\n\n \n\nDicere porro oculos nullam rem cernere posse,\n\n \n\nsed per eos animum ut foribus spectare reclusis,\n\n \n\ndifficilest, contra cum sensus ducat eorum;\n\n \n\nsensus enim trahit atque acies detrudit ad ipsas,\n\n \n\nfulgida praesertim cum cernere saepe nequimus,\n\n \n\nlumina luminibus quia nobis praepediuntur.\n\n \n\nquod foribus non fit; neque enim, qua cernimus ipsi,\n\n \n\nostia suscipiunt ullum reclusa laborem.\n\n \n\npraeterea si pro foribus sunt lumina nostra,\n\n \n\niam magis exemptis oculis debere videtur\n\n \n\ncernere res animus sublatis postibus ipsis.\n\n \n\nIllud in his rebus nequaquam sumere possis,\n\n \n\nDemocriti quod sancta viri sententia ponit,\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animi primordia singula primis\n\n \n\nadposita alternis, variare ac nectere membra.\n\n \n\nnam cum multo sunt animae elementa minora\n\n \n\nquam quibus e corpus nobis et viscera constant,\n\n \n\ntum numero quoque concedunt et rara per artus\n\n \n\ndissita sunt, dum taxat ut hoc promittere possis,\n\n \n\nquantula prima queant nobis iniecta ciere\n\n \n\ncorpora sensiferos motus in corpore, tanta\n\n \n\nintervalla tenere exordia prima animai.\n\n  \n\nnam neque pulveris inter dum sentimus adhaesum\n\n \n\ncorpore nec membris incussam sidere cretam,\n\n \n\n nec nebulam noctu neque arani tenvia fila\n\n \n\nobvia sentimus, quando obretimur euntes,\n\n \n\nnec supera caput eiusdem cecidisse vietam\n\n \n\nvestem nec plumas avium papposque volantis,\n\n \n\nqui nimia levitate cadunt plerumque gravatim,\n\n \n\nnec repentis itum cuiusvis cumque animantis\n\n \n\nsentimus nec priva pedum vestigia quaeque,\n\n \n\ncorpore quae in nostro culices et cetera ponunt.\n\n \n\nusque adeo prius est in nobis multa ciendum\n\n \n\nquam primordia sentiscant concussa animai,\n\n \n\nsemina corporibus nostris inmixta per artus,\n\n \n\net quam in his intervallis tuditantia possint\n\n \n\nconcursare coire et dissultare vicissim.\n\n \n\nEt magis est animus vitai claustra coërcens\n\n \n\net dominantior ad vitam quam vis animai.\n\n \n\nnam sine mente animoque nequit residere per artus\n\n \n\ntemporis exiguam partem pars ulla animai,\n\n \n\nsed comes insequitur facile et discedit in auras\n\n \n\net gelidos artus in leti frigore linquit.\n\n \n\nat manet in vita cui mens animusque remansit,\n\n \n\nquamvis est circum caesis lacer undique membris;\n\n \n\ntruncus adempta anima circum membrisque remota\n\n \n\nvivit et aetherias vitalis suscipit auras;\n\n \n\nsi non omnimodis, at magna parte animai\n\n \n\nprivatus, tamen in vita cunctatur et haeret;\n\n \n\nut, lacerato oculo circum si pupula mansit\n\n \n\nincolumis, stat cernundi vivata potestas,\n\n \n\ndum modo ne totum corrumpas luminis orbem\n\n \n\net circum caedas aciem solamque relinquas;\n\n \n\nid quoque enim sine pernicie non fiet eorum.\n\n \n\nat si tantula pars oculi media illa peresa est,\n\n \n\noccidit extemplo lumen tenebraeque secuntur,\n\n \n\nincolumis quamvis alioqui splendidus orbis.\n\n \n\nhoc anima atque animus vincti sunt foedere semper.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, nativos animantibus et mortalis\n\n \n\nesse animos animasque levis ut noscere possis,\n\n \n\nconquisita diu dulcique reperta labore\n\n \n\ndigna tua pergam disponere carmina vita.\n\n \n\ntu fac utrumque uno subiungas nomine eorum\n\n \n\natque animam verbi causa cum dicere pergam,\n\n \n\nmortalem esse docens, animum quoque dicere credas,\n\n \n\nqua tenus est unum inter se coniunctaque res est.\n\n \n\nPrincipio quoniam tenuem constare minutis\n\n \n\ncorporibus docui multoque minoribus esse\n\n \n\nprincipiis factam quam liquidus umor aquai\n\n \n\naut nebula aut fumus —; nam longe mobilitate\n\n \n\npraestat et a tenui causa magis icta movetur,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi imaginibus fumi nebulaeque movetur;\n\n \n\nquod genus in somnis sopiti ubi cernimus alte\n\n \n\nexhalare vaporem altaria ferreque fumum;\n\n \n\nnam procul haec dubio nobis simulacra gerunturæ\n\n \n\nnunc igitur quoniam quassatis undique vasis\n\n \n\ndiffluere umorem et laticem discedere cernis,\n\n \n\net nebula ac fumus quoniam discedit in auras,\n\n \n\ncrede animam quoque diffundi multoque perire\n\n \n\n ocius et citius dissolvi in corpora prima,\n\n \n\ncum semel ex hominis membris ablata recessit;\n\n \n\nquippe etenim corpus, quod vas quasi constitit eius,\n\n \n\ncum cohibere nequit conquassatum ex aliqua re\n\n \n\nac rarefactum detracto sanguine venis,\n\n \n\naëre qui credas posse hanc cohiberier ullo,\n\n \n\ncorpore qui nostro rarus magis incohibens sit?\n\n  \n\nPraeterea gigni pariter cum corpore et una\n\n \n\ncrescere sentimus pariterque senescere mentem.\n\n \n\nnam vel ut infirmo pueri teneroque vagantur\n\n \n\ncorpore, sic animi sequitur sententia tenvis.\n\n \n\ninde ubi robustis adolevit viribus aetas,\n\n \n\nconsilium quoque maius et auctior est animi vis.\n\n \n\npost ubi iam validis quassatum est viribus aevi\n\n \n\ncorpus et obtusis ceciderunt viribus artus,\n\n \n\nclaudicat ingenium, delirat lingua  mens,\n\n \n\nomnia deficiunt atque uno tempore desunt.\n\n \n\nergo dissolui quoque convenit omnem animai\n\n \n\nnaturam, ceu fumus, in altas aëris auras;\n\n \n\nquando quidem gigni pariter pariterque videmus\n\n \n\ncrescere et,  docui, simul aevo fessa fatisci.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit uti videamus, corpus ut ipsum\n\n \n\nsuscipere inmanis morbos durumque dolorem,\n\n \n\nsic animum curas acris luctumque metumque;\n\n \n\nquare participem leti quoque convenit esse.\n\n \n\nquin etiam morbis in corporis avius errat\n\n \n\nsaepe animus; dementit enim deliraque fatur,\n\n \n\ninter dumque gravi lethargo fertur in altum\n\n \n\naeternumque soporem oculis nutuque cadenti;\n\n \n\nunde neque exaudit voces nec noscere voltus\n\n \n\nillorum potis est, ad vitam qui revocantes\n\n \n\ncircum stant lacrimis rorantes ora genasque.\n\n \n\nquare animum quoque dissolui fateare necessest,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem penetrant in eum contagia morbi;\n\n \n\nnam dolor ac morbus leti fabricator uterquest,\n\n \n\nmultorum exitio perdocti quod sumus ante.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\ndenique cor, hominem cum vini vis penetravit\n\n \n\nacris et in venas discessit diditus ardor,\n\n \n\nconsequitur gravitas membrorum, praepediuntur\n\n \n\ncrura vacillanti, tardescit lingua, madet mens,\n\n \n\nnant oculi, clamor singultus iurgia gliscunt,\n\n \n\net iam cetera de genere hoc quae cumque secuntur,\n\n \n\ncur ea sunt, nisi quod vehemens violentia vini\n\n \n\nconturbare animam consuevit corpore in ipso?\n\n \n\nat quae cumque queunt conturbari inque pediri,\n\n \n\nsignificant, paulo si durior insinuarit\n\n \n\ncausa, fore ut pereant aevo privata futuro.\n\n  \n\nQuin etiam subito vi morbi saepe coactus\n\n \n\nante oculos aliquis nostros, ut fulminis ictu,\n\n \n\nconcidit et spumas agit, ingemit et tremit artus,\n\n \n\ndesipit, extentat nervos, torquetur, anhelat\n\n \n\ninconstanter, et in iactando membra fatigat,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia vis morbi distracta per artus\n\n \n\n turbat agens animam, spumans  in aequore salso\n\n \n\nventorum validis fervescunt viribus undae.\n\n \n\nexprimitur porro gemitus, quia membra dolore\n\n \n\nadficiuntur et omnino quod semina vocis\n\n \n\neliciuntur et ore foras glomerata feruntur\n\n \n\nqua quasi consuerunt et sunt munita viai.\n\n \n\ndesipientia fit, quia vis animi atque animai\n\n \n\nconturbatur et, ut docui, divisa seorsum\n\n \n\ndisiectatur eodem illo distracta veneno.\n\n \n\ninde ubi iam morbi reflexit causa, reditque\n\n \n\nin latebras acer corrupti corporis umor,\n\n \n\ntum quasi vaccillans primum consurgit et omnis\n\n \n\npaulatim redit in sensus animamque receptat.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur tantis ubi morbis corpore in ipso\n\n \n\niactentur miserisque modis distracta laborent,\n\n \n\ncur eadem credis sine corpore in aëre aperto\n\n \n\ncum validis ventis aetatem degere posse?\n\n \n\nEt quoniam mentem sanari corpus ut aegrum\n\n \n\ncernimus et flecti medicina posse videmus,\n\n \n\nid quoque praesagit mortalem vivere mentem.\n\n \n\naddere enim partis aut ordine traiecere aecumst\n\n \n\naut aliquid prosum de summa detrahere hilum,\n\n \n\ncommutare animum qui cumque adoritur et infit\n\n \n\naut aliam quamvis naturam flectere quaerit.\n\n \n\nat neque transferri sibi partis nec tribui vult\n\n \n\ninmortale quod est quicquam neque defluere hilum;\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque suis mutatum finibus exit,\n\n \n\ncontinuo hoc mors est illius quod fuit ante.\n\n \n\nergo animus sive aegrescit, mortalia signa\n\n \n\nmittit, uti docui, seu flectitur a medicina.\n\n \n\nusque adeo falsae rationi vera videtur\n\n \n\nres occurrere et effugium praecludere eunti\n\n \n\nancipitique refutatu convincere falsum.\n\n \n\nDenique saepe hominem paulatim cernimus ire\n\n \n\net membratim vitalem deperdere sensum;\n\n \n\nin pedibus primum digitos livescere et unguis,\n\n \n\ninde pedes et crura mori, post inde per artus\n\n \n\nire alios tractim gelidi vestigia leti.\n\n \n\nscinditur atque animae haec quoniam natura nec uno\n\n \n\ntempore sincera existit, mortalis habendast.\n\n \n\nquod si forte putas ipsam se posse per artus\n\n \n\nintrosum trahere et partis conducere in unum\n\n \n\natque ideo cunctis sensum diducere membris,\n\n \n\nat locus ille tamen, quo copia tanta animai\n\n \n\ncogitur, in sensu debet maiore videri;\n\n \n\nqui quoniam nusquamst, ni mirum, ut diximus ,\n\n \n\ndilaniata foras dispargitur, interit ergo.\n\n \n\nquin etiam si iam libeat concedere falsum\n\n \n\net dare posse animam glomerari in corpore eorum,\n\n \n\nlumina qui lincunt moribundi particulatim,\n\n \n\nmortalem tamen esse animam fateare necesse\n\n \n\nnec refert utrum pereat dispersa per auras\n\n \n\nan contracta suis e partibus obbrutescat,\n\n \n\nquando hominem totum magis ac magis undique sensus\n\n \n\ndeficit et vitae minus et minus undique restat.\n\n  \n\n Et quoniam mens est hominis pars una locoque\n\n \n\nfixa manet certo, vel ut aures atque oculi sunt\n\n \n\natque alii sensus qui vitam cumque gubernant,\n\n \n\net vel uti manus atque oculus naresve seorsum\n\n \n\nsecreta ab nobis nequeunt sentire neque esse,\n\n \n\nsed tamen in parvo lincuntur tempore tali,\n\n \n\nsic animus per se non quit sine corpore et ipso\n\n \n\nesse homine, illius quasi quod vas esse videtur,\n\n \n\nsive aliud quid vis potius coniunctius ei\n\n \n\nfingere, quandoquidem conexu corpus adhaeret.\n\n \n\nDenique corporis atque animi vivata potestas\n\n \n\ninter se coniuncta valent vitaque fruuntur;\n\n \n\nnec sine corpore enim vitalis edere motus\n\n \n\nsola potest animi per se natura nec autem\n\n \n\ncassum anima corpus durare et sensibus uti.\n\n \n\nscilicet avolsus radicibus ut nequit ullam\n\n \n\ndispicere ipse oculus rem seorsum corpore toto,\n\n \n\nsic anima atque animus per se nil posse videtur.\n\n \n\nni mirum quia  venas et viscera mixtim,\n\n \n\nper nervos atque ossa tenentur corpore ab omni\n\n \n\nnec magnis intervallis primordia possunt\n\n \n\nlibera dissultare, ideo conclusa moventur\n\n \n\nsensiferos motus, quos extra corpus in auras\n\n \n\naëris haut possunt post mortem eiecta moveri\n\n \n\npropterea quia non simili ratione tenentur;\n\n \n\ncorpus enim atque animans erit aër, si cohibere\n\n \n\nsese anima atque in eos poterit concludere motus,\n\n \n\nquos ante in nervis et in ipso corpore agebat.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam resoluto corporis omni\n\n \n\ntegmine et eiectis extra vitalibus auris\n\n \n\ndissolui sensus animi fateare necessest\n\n \n\natque animam, quoniam coniunctast causa duobus.\n\n \n\nDenique cum corpus nequeat perferre animai\n\n \n\ndiscidium, quin in taetro tabescat odore,\n\n \n\nquid dubitas quin ex imo penitusque coorta\n\n \n\nemanarit uti fumus diffusa animae vis,\n\n \n\natque ideo tanta mutatum putre ruina\n\n \n\nconciderit corpus, penitus quia mota loco sunt\n\n \n\nfundamenta foras manant animaeque per artus\n\n \n\nperque viarum omnis flexus, in corpore qui sunt,\n\n \n\natque foramina? multimodis ut noscere possis\n\n \n\ndispertitam animae naturam exisse per artus\n\n \n\net prius esse sibi distractam corpore in ipso,\n\n \n\nquam prolapsa foras enaret in aëris auras.\n\n  \n\nQuin etiam finis dum vitae vertitur intra,\n\n \n\nsaepe aliqua tamen e causa labefacta videtur\n\n \n\nire anima ac toto solui de corpore \n\n \n\net quasi supremo languescere tempore voltus\n\n \n\nmolliaque exsangui cadere omnia  membra.\n\n \n\nquod genus est, animo male factum cum perhibetur\n\n \n\naut animam liquisse; ubi iam trepidatur et omnes\n\n \n\nextremum cupiunt vitae reprehendere vinclum;\n\n \n\nconquassatur enim tum mens animaeque potestas\n\n \n\nomnis. et haec ipso cum corpore conlabefiunt,\n\n \n\nut gravior paulo possit dissolvere causa.\n\n \n\n Quid dubitas tandem quin extra prodita corpus\n\n \n\ninbecilla foras in aperto, tegmine dempto,\n\n \n\nnon modo non omnem possit durare per aevom,\n\n \n\nsed minimum quodvis nequeat consistere tempus?\n\n \n\nnec sibi enim quisquam moriens sentire videtur\n\n \n\nire foras animam incolumem de corpore toto,\n\n \n\nnec prius ad iugulum et supera succedere fauces,\n\n \n\nverum deficere in certa regione locatam;\n\n \n\nut sensus alios in parti quemque sua scit\n\n \n\ndissolui. quod si inmortalis nostra foret mens,\n\n \n\nnon tam se moriens dissolvi conquereretur,\n\n \n\nsed magis ire foras vestemque relinquere, ut anguis.\n\n \n\nDenique cur animi numquam mens consiliumque\n\n \n\ngignitur in capite aut pedibus manibusve, sed unis\n\n \n\nsedibus et certis regionibus omnibus haeret,\n\n \n\nsi non certa loca ad nascendum reddita cuique\n\n \n\nsunt, et ubi quicquid possit durare creatum\n\n \n\natque ita multimodis partitis artubus esse,\n\n \n\nmembrorum ut numquam existat praeposterus ordo?\n\n \n\nusque adeo sequitur res rem, neque flamma creari\n\n \n\nfluminibus solitast neque in igni gignier algor.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea si inmortalis natura animaist\n\n \n\net sentire potest secreta a corpore nostro,\n\n \n\nquinque, ut opinor, eam faciundum est sensibus auctam.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia nosmet proponere nobis\n\n \n\npossumus infernas animas Acherunte vagare.\n\n \n\npictores itaque et scriptorum saecla priora\n\n \n\nsic animas intro duxerunt sensibus auctas.\n\n \n\nat neque sorsum oculi neque nares nec manus ipsa\n\n \n\nesse potest animae neque sorsum lingua neque aures;\n\n \n\nhaud igitur per se possunt sentire neque esse.\n\n \n\nEt quoniam toto sentimus corpore inesse\n\n \n\nvitalem sensum et totum esse animale videmus,\n\n \n\nsi subito medium celeri praeciderit ictu\n\n \n\nvis aliqua, ut sorsum partem secernat utramque,\n\n \n\ndispertita procul dubio quoque vis animai\n\n \n\net discissa simul cum corpore dissicietur.\n\n \n\nat quod scinditur et partis discedit in ullas,\n\n \n\nscilicet aeternam sibi naturam abnuit esse.\n\n \n\nfalciferos memorant currus abscidere membra\n\n \n\nsaepe ita de subito permixta caede calentis,\n\n \n\nut tremere in terra videatur ab artubus id quod\n\n \n\ndecidit abscisum, cum mens tamen atque hominis vis\n\n \n\nmobilitate mali non quit sentire dolorem;\n\n \n\net simul in pugnae studio quod dedita mens est,\n\n \n\ncorpore relicuo pugnam caedesque petessit,\n\n \n\nnec tenet amissam laevam cum tegmine saepe\n\n \n\ninter equos abstraxe rotas falcesque rapaces,\n\n \n\nnec cecidisse alius dextram, cum scandit et instat.\n\n \n\ninde alius conatur adempto surgere crure,\n\n \n\ncum digitos agitat propter moribundus humi pes.\n\n \n\net caput abscisum calido viventeque trunco\n\n \n\nservat humi voltum vitalem oculosque patentis,\n\n \n\ndonec reliquias animai reddidit omnes.\n\n \n\nquin etiam tibi si, lingua vibrante, minanti\n\n \n\n serpentis cauda, procero corpore, utrumque\n\n \n\nsit libitum in multas partis discidere ferro,\n\n \n\nomnia iam sorsum cernes ancisa recenti\n\n \n\nvolnere tortari et terram conspargere tabo,\n\n \n\nipsam seque retro partem petere ore priorem,\n\n \n\nvolneris ardenti ut morsu premat icta dolore.\n\n \n\nomnibus esse igitur totas dicemus in illis\n\n \n\nparticulis animas? at ea ratione sequetur\n\n \n\nunam animantem animas habuisse in corpore multas.\n\n \n\nergo divisast ea quae fuit una simul cum\n\n \n\ncorpore; quapropter mortale utrumque putandumst,\n\n \n\nin multas quoniam partis disciditur aeque.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea si inmortalis natura animai\n\n \n\nconstat et in corpus nascentibus insinuatur,\n\n \n\ncur super ante actam aetatem meminisse nequimus\n\n \n\nnec vestigia gestarum rerum ulla tenemus?\n\n \n\nnam si tanto operest animi mutata potestas,\n\n \n\nomnis ut actarum exciderit retinentia rerum,\n\n \n\nnon, ut opinor, id ab leto iam longius errat;\n\n \n\nqua propter fateare necessest quae fuit ante\n\n \n\ninteriisse, et quae nunc est nunc esse creatam.\n\n \n\nPraeterea si iam perfecto corpore nobis\n\n \n\ninferri solitast animi vivata potestas\n\n \n\ntum cum gignimur et vitae cum limen inimus,\n\n \n\nhaud ita conveniebat uti cum corpore et una\n\n \n\ncum membris videatur in ipso sanguine cresse,\n\n \n\nsed vel ut in cavea per se sibi vivere solam\n\n \n\nconvenit, ut sensu corpus tamen affluat omne.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam neque originis esse putandumst\n\n \n\nexpertis animas nec leti lege solutas;\n\n \n\nnam neque tanto opere adnecti potuisse putandumst\n\n \n\ncorporibus nostris extrinsecus insinuatas,\n\n \n\nquod fieri totum contra manifesta docet res\n\n \n\nænamque ita conexa est per venas viscera nervos\n\n \n\nossaque, uti dentes quoque sensu participentur;\n\n \n\nmorbus ut indicat et gelidai stringor aquai\n\n \n\net lapis oppressus subitis e frugibus asperæ\n\n \n\nnec, tam contextae cum sint, exire videntur\n\n \n\nincolumes posse et salvas exsolvere sese\n\n \n\nomnibus e nervis atque ossibus articulisque,\n\n \n\nquod si forte putas extrinsecus insinuatam\n\n \n\npermanare animam nobis per membra solere,\n\n \n\ntanto quique magis cum corpore fusa peribit;\n\n \n\nquod permanat enim, dissolvitur, interit ergo;\n\n \n\ndispertitur enim per caulas corporis omnis.\n\n \n\nut cibus, in membra atque artus cum diditur omnis,\n\n \n\ndisperit atque aliam naturam sufficit ex se,\n\n \n\nsic anima atque animus quamvis  integra recens \n\n \n\ncorpus eunt, tamen in manando dissoluuntur,\n\n \n\ndum quasi per caulas omnis diduntur in artus\n\n \n\nparticulae quibus haec animi natura creatur,\n\n \n\nquae nunc in nostro dominatur corpore nata\n\n \n\nex illa quae tunc periit partita per artus.\n\n  \n\nquapropter neque natali privata videtur\n\n \n\n esse die natura animae nec funeris expers.\n\n \n\nSemina praeterea linquontur necne animai\n\n \n\ncorpore in exanimo? quod si lincuntur et insunt,\n\n \n\nhaut erit ut merito inmortalis possit haberi,\n\n \n\npartibus amissis quoniam libata recessit.\n\n \n\nsin ita sinceris membris ablata profugit,\n\n \n\nut nullas partis in corpore liquerit ex se,\n\n \n\nunde cadavera rancenti iam viscere vermes\n\n \n\nexpirant atque unde animantum copia tanta\n\n \n\nexos et exanguis tumidos perfluctuat artus?\n\n \n\nquod si forte animas extrinsecus insinuari?\n\n \n\nvermibus et privas in corpora posse venire\n\n \n\ncredis nec reputas cur milia multa animarum\n\n \n\nconveniant unde una recesserit, hoc tamen est ut\n\n \n\nquaerendum videatur et in discrimen agendum,\n\n \n\nutrum tandem animae venentur semina quaeque\n\n \n\nvermiculorum ipsaeque sibi fabricentur ubi sint,\n\n \n\nan quasi corporibus perfectis insinuentur.\n\n \n\nat neque cur faciant ipsae quareve laborent\n\n \n\ndicere suppeditat. neque enim, sine corpore cum sunt,\n\n \n\nsollicitae volitant morbis alguque fameque;\n\n \n\ncorpus enim magis his vitiis adfine laborat,\n\n \n\net mala multa animus contage fungitur eius.\n\n \n\nsed tamen his esto quamvis facere utile corpus,\n\n \n\ncum subeant; at qua possint via nulla videtur.\n\n \n\nhaut igitur faciunt animae sibi corpora et artus.\n\n \n\nnec tamen est ut qui  perfectis insinuentur\n\n \n\ncorporibus; neque enim poterunt suptiliter esse\n\n \n\nconexae neque consensu contagia fient.\n\n  \n\nDenique cur acris violentia triste leonum\n\n \n\nseminium sequitur, volpes dolus, et fuga cervos?\n\n \n\na patribus datur et  patrius pavor incitat artus,\n\n \n\net iam cetera de genere hoc cur omnia membris\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo generascunt ingenioque,\n\n \n\nsi non, certa suo quia semine seminioque\n\n \n\nvis animi pariter crescit cum corpore quoque?\n\n \n\nquod si inmortalis foret et mutare soleret\n\n \n\ncorpora, permixtis animantes moribus essent,\n\n \n\neffugeret canis Hyrcano de semine saepe\n\n \n\ncornigeri incursum cervi tremeretque per auras\n\n \n\naëris accipiter fugiens veniente columba,\n\n \n\ndesiperent homines, saperent fera saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\nillud enim falsa fertur ratione, quod aiunt\n\n \n\ninmortalem animam mutato corpore flecti;\n\n \n\nquod mutatur enim, dissolvitur, interit ergo;\n\n \n\ntraiciuntur enim partes atque ordine migrant;\n\n \n\nquare dissolui quoque debent posse per artus,\n\n \n\ndenique ut intereant una cum corpore cunctae.\n\n \n\nsin animas hominum dicent in corpora semper\n\n \n\nire humana, tamen quaeram cur e sapienti\n\n \n\nstulta queat fieri, nec prudens sit puer ullus,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nnec tam doctus equae pullus quam fortis equi vis.\n\n \n\nscilicet in tenero tenerascere corpore mentem\n\n \n\nconfugient. quod si iam fit, fateare necessest\n\n \n\n mortalem esse animam, quoniam mutata per artus\n\n \n\ntanto opere amittit vitam sensumque priorem.\n\n \n\nquove modo poterit pariter cum corpore quoque\n\n \n\nconfirmata cupitum aetatis tangere florem\n\n \n\nvis animi, nisi erit consors in origine prima?\n\n \n\nquidve foras sibi vult membris exire senectis?\n\n \n\nan metuit conclusa manere in corpore putri\n\n \n\net domus aetatis spatio ne fessa vetusto\n\n \n\nobruat? at non sunt immortali ulla pericla.\n\n  \n\nDenique conubia ad Veneris partusque ferarum\n\n \n\nesse animas praesto deridiculum esse videtur,\n\n \n\nexpectare immortalis mortalia membra\n\n \n\ninnumero numero certareque praeproperanter\n\n \n\ninter se quae prima potissimaque insinuetur;\n\n \n\nsi non forte ita sunt animarum foedera pacta,\n\n \n\nut quae prima volans advenerit insinuetur\n\n \n\nprima neque inter se contendant viribus hilum.\n\n \n\nDenique in aethere non arbor, non aequore in alto\n\n \n\nnubes esse queunt nec pisces vivere in arvis\n\n \n\nnec cruor in lignis neque saxis sucus inesse.\n\n \n\ncertum ac dispositumst ubi quicquid crescat et insit.\n\n \n\nsic animi natura nequit sine corpore oriri\n\n \n\nsola neque a nervis et sanguine longius esse.\n\n \n\nquod si posset enim, multo prius ipsa animi vis\n\n \n\nin capite aut umeris aut imis calcibus esse\n\n \n\nposset et innasci quavis in parte soleret,\n\n \n\ntandem in eodem homine atque in eodem vase manere.\n\n \n\nquod quoniam nostro quoque constat corpore certum\n\n \n\ndispositumque videtur ubi esse et crescere possit\n\n \n\nsorsum anima atque animus, tanto magis infitiandum\n\n \n\ntotum posse extra corpus durare genique.\n\n \n\nquare, corpus ubi interiit, periisse necessest\n\n \n\nconfiteare animam distractam in corpore toto.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim mortale aeterno iungere et una\n\n \n\nconsentire putare et fungi mutua posse\n\n \n\ndesiperest; quid enim diversius esse putandumst\n\n \n\naut magis inter se disiunctum discrepitansque,\n\n \n\nquam mortale quod est inmortali atque perenni\n\n \n\niunctum in concilio saevas tolerare procellas?\n\n \n\npraeterea quaecumque manent aeterna necessest\n\n \n\naut quia sunt solido cum corpore respuere ictus\n\n \n\nnec penetrare pati sibi quicquam quod queat artas\n\n \n\ndissociare intus partis, ut materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora sunt, quorum naturam ostendimus ante,\n\n \n\naut ideo durare aetatem posse per omnem,\n\n \n\nplagarum quia sunt expertia sicut inanest,\n\n \n\nquod manet intactum neque ab ictu fungitur hilum,\n\n \n\naut etiam quia nulla loci sit copia circum,\n\n \n\nquo quasi res possint discedere dissoluique,\n\n \n\nsicut summarum summast aeterna, neque extra\n\n \n\nquis locus est quo diffugiant neque corpora sunt quae\n\n \n\npossint incidere et valida dissolvere plaga.\n\n  \n\nQuod si forte ideo magis inmortalis habendast,\n\n \n\nquod vitalibus ab rebus munita tenetur,\n\n \n\naut quia non veniunt omnino aliena salutis,\n\n \n\n aut quia quae veniunt aliqua ratione recedunt\n\n \n\npulsa prius quam quid noceant sentire queamus,\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\npraeter enim quam quod morbis cum corporis aegret,\n\n \n\nadvenit id quod eam de rebus saepe futuris\n\n \n\nmacerat inque metu male habet curisque fatigat,\n\n \n\npraeteritisque male admissis peccata remordent.\n\n \n\nadde furorem animi proprium atque oblivia rerum,\n\n \n\nadde quod in nigras lethargi mergitur undas.\n\n  \n\nNil igitur mors est ad nos neque pertinet hilum,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem natura animi mortalis habetur.\n\n \n\net vel ut ante acto nihil tempore sensimus aegri,\n\n \n\nad confligendum venientibus undique Poenis,\n\n \n\nomnia cum belli trepido concussa tumultu\n\n \n\nhorrida contremuere sub altis aetheris auris,\n\n \n\nin dubioque fuere utrorum ad regna cadendum\n\n \n\nomnibus humanis esset terraque marique,\n\n \n\nsic, ubi non erimus, cum corporis atque animai\n\n \n\ndiscidium fuerit, quibus e sumus uniter apti,\n\n \n\nscilicet haud nobis quicquam, qui non erimus tum,\n\n \n\naccidere omnino poterit sensumque movere,\n\n \n\nnon si terra mari miscebitur et mare caelo.\n\n \n\net si iam nostro sentit de corpore postquam\n\n \n\ndistractast animi natura animaeque potestas,\n\n \n\nnil tamen est ad nos, qui comptu coniugioque\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animae consistimus uniter apti.\n\n \n\nnec, si materiem nostram collegerit aetas\n\n \n\npost obitum rursumque redegerit ut sita nunc est,\n\n \n\natque iterum nobis fuerint data lumina vitae,\n\n \n\npertineat quicquam tamen ad nos id quoque factum,\n\n \n\ninterrupta semel cum sit repetentia nostri.\n\n \n\net nunc nil ad nos de nobis attinet, ante\n\n \n\nqui fuimus,  iam de illis nos adficit angor.\n\n \n\nnam cum respicias inmensi temporis omne\n\n \n\npraeteritum spatium, tum motus materiai\n\n \n\nmultimodi quam sint, facile hoc adcredere possis,\n\n \n\nsemina saepe in eodem, ut nunc sunt, ordine posta\n\n \n\nhaec eadem, quibus e nunc nos sumus, ante fuisse.\n\n \n\nnec memori tamen id quimus reprehendere mente;\n\n \n\ninter enim iectast vitai pausa vageque\n\n \n\ndeerrarunt passim motus ab sensibus omnes.\n\n \n\ndebet enim, misere si forte aegreque futurumst;\n\n \n\nipse quoque esse in eo tum tempore, cui male possit\n\n \n\naccidere. id quoniam mors eximit, esseque prohibet\n\n \n\nillum cui possint incommoda conciliari,\n\n \n\nscire licet nobis nihil esse in morte timendum\n\n \n\nnec miserum fieri qui non est posse, neque hilum\n\n \n\ndifferre an nullo fuerit iam tempore natus,\n\n \n\nmortalem vitam mors cum inmortalis ademit.\n\n  \n\nProinde ubi se videas hominem indignarier ipsum,\n\n \n\npost mortem fore ut aut putescat corpore posto\n\n \n\naut flammis interfiat malisve ferarum,\n\n \n\nscire licet non sincerum sonere atque subesse\n\n \n\ncaecum aliquem cordi stimulum, quamvis neget ipse\n\n \n\ncredere se quemquam sibi sensum in morte futurum;\n\n \n\n non, ut opinor, enim dat quod promittit et unde\n\n \n\nnec radicitus e vita se tollit et eicit,\n\n \n\nsed facit esse sui quiddam super inscius ipse.\n\n \n\nvivus enim sibi cum proponit quisque futurum,\n\n \n\ncorpus uti volucres lacerent in morte feraeque,\n\n \n\nipse sui miseret; neque enim se dividit illim\n\n \n\nnec removet satis a proiecto corpore et illum\n\n \n\nse fingit sensuque suo contaminat astans.\n\n \n\nhinc indignatur se mortalem esse creatum\n\n \n\nnec videt in vera nullum fore morte alium se,\n\n \n\nqui possit vivus sibi se lugere peremptum\n\n \n\nstansque iacentem  lacerari urive dolere.\n\n \n\nnam si in morte malumst malis morsuque ferarum\n\n \n\ntractari, non invenio qui non sit acerbum\n\n \n\nignibus inpositum calidis torrescere flammis\n\n \n\naut in melle situm suffocari atque rigere\n\n \n\nfrigore, cum summo gelidi cubat aequore saxi,\n\n \n\nurgerive superne obrutum pondere terrae.\n\n  \n\n'Iam iam non domus accipiet te laeta neque uxor\n\n \n\noptima, nec dulces occurrent oscula nati\n\n \n\npraeripere et tacita pectus dulcedine tangent.\n\n \n\nnon poteris factis florentibus esse tuisque\n\n \n\npraesidium. misero misere' aiunt 'omnia ademit\n\n \n\nuna dies infesta tibi tot praemia vitae.'\n\n \n\nillud in his rebus non addunt 'nec tibi earum\n\n \n\niam desiderium rerum super insidet una.'\n\n \n\nquod bene si videant animo dictisque sequantur,\n\n \n\ndissoluant animi magno se angore metuque.\n\n \n\n'tu quidem ut es leto sopitus, sic eris aevi\n\n \n\nquod super est cunctis privatus doloribus aegris;\n\n \n\nat nos horrifico cinefactum te prope busto\n\n \n\ninsatiabiliter deflevimus, aeternumque\n\n \n\nnulla dies nobis maerorem e pectore demet.'\n\n \n\nillud ab hoc igitur quaerendum est, quid sit amari\n\n \n\ntanto opere, ad somnum si res redit atque quietem,\n\n \n\ncur quisquam aeterno possit tabescere luctu.\n\n \n\nHoc etiam faciunt ubi discubuere tenentque\n\n \n\npocula saepe homines et inumbrant ora coronis,\n\n \n\nex animo ut dicant: 'brevis hic est fructus homullis;\n\n \n\niam fuerit neque post umquam revocare licebit.'\n\n \n\ntam quam in morte mali cum primis hoc sit eorum,\n\n \n\nquod sitis exurat miseros atque arida torrat,\n\n \n\naut aliae cuius desiderium insideat rei.\n\n \n\nnec sibi enim quisquam tum se vitamque requiret,\n\n \n\ncum pariter mens et corpus sopita quiescunt;\n\n \n\nnam licet aeternum per nos sic esse soporem,\n\n \n\nnec desiderium nostri nos adficit ullum,\n\n \n\net tamen haud quaquam nostros tunc illa per artus\n\n \n\nlonge ab sensiferis primordia motibus errant,\n\n \n\ncum correptus homo ex somno se colligit ipse.\n\n \n\nmulto igitur mortem minus ad nos esse putandumst,\n\n \n\nsi minus esse potest quam quod nihil esse videmus;\n\n \n\nmaior enim turbae disiectus materiai\n\n \n\nconsequitur leto nec quisquam expergitus extat,\n\n \n\nfrigida quem semel est vitai pausa secuta.\n\n  \n\n Denique si vocem rerum natura repente.\n\n \n\nmittat et hoc alicui nostrum sic increpet ipsa:\n\n \n\n'quid tibi tanto operest, mortalis, quod nimis aegris\n\n \n\nluctibus indulges? quid mortem congemis ac fles?\n\n \n\nnam  grata fuit tibi vita ante acta priorque\n\n \n\net non omnia pertusum congesta quasi in vas\n\n \n\ncommoda perfluxere atque ingrata interiere;\n\n \n\ncur non ut plenus vitae conviva recedis\n\n \n\naequo animoque capis securam, stulte, quietem?\n\n \n\nsin ea quae fructus cumque es periere profusa\n\n \n\nvitaque in offensost, cur amplius addere quaeris,\n\n \n\nrursum quod pereat male et ingratum occidat omne,\n\n \n\nnon potius vitae finem facis atque laboris?\n\n \n\nnam tibi praeterea quod machiner inveniamque,\n\n \n\nquod placeat, nihil est; eadem sunt omnia semper.\n\n \n\nsi tibi non annis corpus iam marcet et artus\n\n \n\nconfecti languent, eadem tamen omnia restant,\n\n \n\nomnia si perges vivendo vincere saecla,\n\n \n\natque etiam potius, si numquam sis moriturus',\n\n \n\nquid respondemus, nisi iustam intendere litem\n\n \n\nnaturam et veram verbis exponere causam?\n\n \n\ngrandior hic vero si iam seniorque queratur\n\n \n\natque obitum lamentetur miser amplius aequo,\n\n \n\nnon merito inclamet magis et voce increpet acri:\n\n \n\n'aufer abhinc lacrimas, baratre, et compesce querellas.\n\n \n\nomnia perfunctus vitai praemia marces;\n\n \n\nsed quia semper aves quod abest, praesentia temnis,\n\n \n\ninperfecta tibi elapsast ingrataque vita,\n\n \n\net nec opinanti mors ad caput adstitit ante\n\n \n\nquam satur ac plenus possis discedere rerum.\n\n \n\nnunc aliena tua tamen aetate omnia mitte\n\n \n\naequo animoque, age dum, magnis concede necessis?'\n\n \n\niure, ut opinor, agat, iure increpet inciletque;\n\n \n\ncedit enim rerum novitate extrusa vetustas\n\n \n\nsemper, et ex aliis aliud reparare necessest.\n\n \n\nNec quisquam in barathrum nec Tartara deditur atra;\n\n \n\nmateries opus est, ut crescant postera saecla;\n\n \n\nquae tamen omnia te vita perfuncta sequentur;\n\n \n\nnec minus ergo ante haec quam tu cecidere cadentque.\n\n \n\nsic alid ex alio numquam desistet oriri\n\n \n\nvitaque mancipio nulli datur, omnibus usu.\n\n \n\nrespice item quam nil ad nos ante acta vetustas\n\n \n\ntemporis aeterni fuerit, quam nascimur ante.\n\n \n\nhoc igitur speculum nobis natura futuri\n\n \n\ntemporis exponit post mortem denique nostram.\n\n \n\nnumquid ibi horribile apparet, num triste videtur\n\n \n\nquicquam, non omni somno securius exstat?\n\n  \n\nAtque ea ni mirum quae cumque Acherunte profundo\n\n \n\nprodita sunt esse, in vita sunt omnia nobis.\n\n \n\nnec miser inpendens magnum timet aëre saxum\n\n \n\nTantalus, ut famast, cassa formidine torpens;\n\n \n\nsed magis in vita divom metus urget inanis\n\n \n\nmortalis casumque timent quem cuique ferat fors.\n\n \n\nnec Tityon volucres ineunt Acherunte iacentem\n\n \n\nnec quod sub magno scrutentur pectore quicquam\n\n \n\n perpetuam aetatem possunt reperire profecto.\n\n \n\nquam libet immani proiectu corporis exstet,\n\n \n\nqui non sola novem dispessis iugera membris\n\n \n\noptineat, sed qui terrai totius orbem,\n\n \n\nnon tamen aeternum poterit perferre dolorem\n\n \n\nnec praebere cibum proprio de corpore semper.\n\n \n\nsed Tityos nobis hic est, in amore iacentem\n\n \n\nquem volucres lacerant atque exest anxius angor\n\n \n\naut alia quavis scindunt cuppedine curae.\n\n \n\nSisyphus in vita quoque nobis ante oculos est,\n\n \n\nqui petere a populo fasces saevasque secures\n\n \n\nimbibit et semper victus tristisque recedit.\n\n \n\nnam petere imperium, quod inanest nec datur umquam,\n\n \n\natque in eo semper durum sufferre laborem,\n\n \n\nhoc est adverso nixantem trudere monte\n\n \n\nsaxum, quod tamen  summo iam vertice rusum\n\n \n\nvolvitur et plani raptim petit aequora campi.\n\n \n\ndeinde animi ingratam naturam pascere semper\n\n \n\natque explere bonis rebus satiareque numquam,\n\n \n\nquod faciunt nobis annorum tempora, circum\n\n \n\ncum redeunt fetusque ferunt variosque lepores,\n\n \n\nnec tamen explemur vitai fructibus umquam,\n\n \n\nhoc, ut opinor, id est, aevo florente puellas\n\n \n\nquod memorant laticem pertusum congerere in vas,\n\n \n\nquod tamen expleri nulla ratione potestur.\n\n \n\nCerberus et Furiae iam vero et lucis egestas,\n\n \n\nTartarus horriferos eructans faucibus aestus!\n\n \n\nqui neque sunt usquam nec possunt esse profecto;\n\n \n\nsed metus in vita poenarum pro male factis\n\n \n\nest insignibus insignis scelerisque luela,\n\n \n\ncarcer et horribilis de saxo iactus deorsum,\n\n \n\nverbera carnifices robur pix lammina taedae;\n\n \n\nquae tamen etsi absunt, at mens sibi conscia factis\n\n \n\npraemetuens adhibet stimulos torretque flagellis,\n\n \n\nnec videt interea qui terminus esse malorum\n\n \n\npossit nec quae sit poenarum denique finis,\n\n \n\natque eadem metuit magis haec ne in morte gravescant.\n\n \n\nhic Acherusia fit stultorum denique vita.\n\n  \n\nHoc etiam tibi tute interdum dicere possis.\n\n \n\n'lumina sis oculis etiam bonus Ancus reliquit,\n\n \n\nqui melior multis quam tu fuit, improbe, rebus.\n\n \n\ninde alii multi reges rerumque potentes\n\n \n\nocciderunt, magnis qui gentibus imperitarunt.\n\n \n\nille quoque ipse, viam qui quondam per mare magnum\n\n \n\nstravit iterque dedit legionibus ire per altum\n\n \n\nac pedibus salsas docuit super ire lucunas\n\n \n\net contempsit equis insultans murmura ponti,\n\n \n\nlumine adempto animam moribundo corpore fudit.\n\n \n\nScipiadas, belli fulmen, Carthaginis horror,\n\n \n\nossa dedit terrae proinde ac famul infimus esset.\n\n \n\nadde repertores doctrinarum atque leporum,\n\n \n\nadde Heliconiadum comites; quorum unus Homerus\n\n \n\nsceptra potitus eadem aliis sopitus quietest.\n\n \n\ndenique Democritum post quam matura vetustas\n\n \n\nadmonuit memores motus languescere mentis,\n\n \n\n sponte sua leto caput obvius optulit ipse.\n\n \n\nipse Epicurus obit decurso lumine vitae,\n\n \n\nqui genus humanum ingenio superavit et omnis\n\n \n\nrestinxit stellas exortus ut aetherius sol.\n\n \n\ntu vero dubitabis et indignabere obire?\n\n \n\nmortua cui vita est prope iam vivo atque videnti,\n\n \n\nqui somno partem maiorem conteris aevi,\n\n \n\net viligans stertis nec somnia cernere cessas\n\n \n\nsollicitamque geris cassa formidine mentem\n\n \n\nnec reperire potes tibi quid sit saepe mali, cum\n\n \n\nebrius urgeris multis miser undique curis\n\n \n\natque animo incerto fluitans errore vagaris.'\n\n  \n\nSi possent homines, proinde ac sentire videntur\n\n \n\npondus inesse animo, quod se gravitate fatiget,\n\n \n\ne quibus id fiat causis quoque noscere et unde\n\n \n\ntanta mali tam quam moles in pectore constet,\n\n \n\nhaut ita vitam agerent, ut nunc plerumque videmus\n\n \n\nquid sibi quisque velit nescire et quaerere semper,\n\n \n\ncommutare locum, quasi onus deponere possit.\n\n \n\nexit saepe foras magnis ex aedibus ille,\n\n \n\nesse domi quem pertaesumst, subitoque ,\n\n \n\nquippe foris nihilo melius qui sentiat esse.\n\n \n\ncurrit agens mannos ad villam praecipitanter\n\n \n\nauxilium tectis quasi ferre ardentibus instans;\n\n \n\noscitat extemplo, tetigit cum limina villae,\n\n \n\naut abit in somnum gravis atque oblivia quaerit,\n\n \n\naut etiam properans urbem petit atque revisit.\n\n \n\nhoc se quisque modo fugit, at quem scilicet, ut fit,\n\n \n\neffugere haut potis est: ingratius haeret et odit\n\n \n\npropterea, morbi quia causam non tenet aeger;\n\n \n\nquam bene si videat, iam rebus quisque relictis\n\n \n\nnaturam primum studeat cognoscere rerum,\n\n \n\ntemporis aeterni quoniam, non unius horae,\n\n \n\nambigitur status, in quo sit mortalibus omnis\n\n \n\naetas, post mortem quae restat cumque manendo.\n\n  \n\nDenique tanto opere in dubiis trepidare periclis\n\n \n\nquae mala nos subigit vitai tanta cupido?\n\n \n\ncerte equidem finis vitae mortalibus adstat\n\n \n\nnec devitari letum pote, quin obeamus.\n\n \n\npraeterea versamur ibidem atque insumus usque\n\n \n\nnec nova vivendo procuditur ulla voluptas;\n\n \n\nsed dum abest quod avemus, id exsuperare videtur\n\n \n\ncetera; post aliud, cum contigit illud, avemus\n\n \n\net sitis aequa tenet vitai semper hiantis.\n\n \n\nposteraque in dubiost fortunam quam vehat aetas,\n\n \n\nquidve ferat nobis casus quive exitus instet.\n\n \n\nnec prorsum vitam ducendo demimus hilum\n\n \n\ntempore de mortis nec delibare valemus,\n\n \n\nquo minus esse diu possimus forte perempti.\n\n \n\nproinde licet quod vis vivendo condere saecla,\n\n \n\nmors aeterna tamen nihilo minus illa manebit,\n\n \n\nnec minus ille diu iam non erit, ex hodierno\n\n \n\nlumine qui finem vitai fecit, et ille,\n\n \n\nmensibus atque annis qui multis occidit ante.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0001","text":"## Liber Tertius\n\n  \n\nE tenebris tantis tam clarum extollere lumen\n\n \n\nqui primus potuisti inlustrans commoda vitae,\n\n \n\nte sequor, o Graiae gentis decus, inque tuis nunc\n\n \n\nficta pedum pono pressis vestigia signis,\n\n \n\nnon ita certandi cupidus quam propter amorem\n\n \n\nquod te imitari aveo; quid enim contendat hirundo\n\n \n\ncycnis, aut quid nam tremulis facere artubus haedi\n\n \n\nconsimile in cursu possint et fortis equi vis?\n\n \n\ntu, pater, es rerum inventor, tu patria nobis\n\n \n\nsuppeditas praecepta, tuisque ex, inclute, chartis,\n\n \n\nfloriferis ut apes in saltibus omnia libant,\n\n \n\nomnia nos itidem depascimur aurea dicta,\n\n \n\naurea, perpetua semper dignissima vita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0002","text":"nam simul ac ratio tua coepit vociferari\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum divina mente coorta\n\n \n\ndiffugiunt animi terrores, moenia mundi\n\n \n\ndiscedunt. totum video per inane geri res.\n\n \n\napparet divum numen sedesque quietae,\n\n \n\nquas neque concutiunt venti nec nubila nimbis\n\n \n\naspergunt neque nix acri concreta pruina\n\n \n\ncana cadens violat semper innubilus aether\n\n \n\nintegit et large diffuso lumine ridet:\n\n \n\nomnia suppeditat porro natura neque ulla\n\n \n\nres animi pacem delibat tempore in ullo.\n\n \n\nat contra nusquam apparent Acherusia templa,\n\n \n\nnec tellus obstat quin omnia dispiciantur,\n\n \n\nsub pedibus quae cumque infra per inane geruntur.\n\n \n\nhis ibi me rebus quaedam divina voluptas\n\n \n\npercipit atque horror, quod sic natura tua vi\n\n \n\ntam manifesta patens ex omni parte retecta est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0003","text":"Et quoniam docui, cunctarum exordia rerum\n\n \n\nqualia sint et quam variis distantia formis\n\n \n\nsponte sua volitent aeterno percita motu,\n\n \n\nquove modo possint res ex his quaeque creari,\n\n \n\nhasce secundum res animi natura videtur\n\n \n\natque animae claranda meis iam versibus esse\n\n \n\net metus ille foras praeceps Acheruntis agendus,\n\n \n\nfunditus humanam qui vitam turbat ab imo\n\n \n\nomnia suffundens mortis nigrore neque ullam\n\n \n\nesse voluptatem liquidam puramque relinquit.\n\n  \n\nnam quod saepe homines morbos magis esse timendos\n\n \n\ninfamemque ferunt vitam quam Tartara leti\n\n \n\net se scire animi naturam sanguinis esse,\n\n \n\naut etiam venti, si fert ita forte voluntas,\n\n \n\nnec prosum quicquam nostrae rationis egere,\n\n \n\nhinc licet advertas animum magis omnia laudis\n\n \n\niactari causa quam quod res ipsa probetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0004","text":"extorres idem patria longeque fugati\n\n \n\nconspectu ex hominum, foedati crimine turpi,\n\n \n\nomnibus aerumnis adfecti denique vivunt,\n\n \n\net quo cumque tamen miseri venere parentant\n\n \n\net nigras mactant pecudes et manibus divis\n\n \n\ninferias mittunt multoque in rebus acerbis\n\n \n\n acrius advertunt animos ad religionem.\n\n \n\nquo magis in dubiis hominem spectare periclis\n\n \n\nconvenit adversisque in rebus noscere qui sit;\n\n \n\nnam verae voces tum demum pectore ab imo\n\n \n\neliciuntur  eripitur persona amanare.\n\n \n\ndenique avarities et honorum caeca cupido,\n\n \n\nquae miseros homines cogunt transcendere fines\n\n \n\niuris et inter dum socios scelerum atque ministros\n\n \n\nnoctes atque dies niti praestante labore\n\n \n\nad summas emergere opes, haec vulnera vitae\n\n \n\nnon minimam partem mortis formidine aluntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0005","text":"turpis enim ferme contemptus et acris egestas\n\n \n\nsemota ab dulci vita stabilique videtur\n\n \n\net quasi iam leti portas cunctarier ante;\n\n \n\nunde homines dum se falso terrore coacti\n\n \n\neffugisse volunt longe longeque remosse,\n\n \n\nsanguine civili rem conflant divitiasque\n\n \n\nconduplicant avidi, caedem caede accumulantes,\n\n \n\ncrudeles gaudent in tristi funere fratris\n\n \n\net consanguineum mensas odere timentque.\n\n \n\nconsimili ratione ab eodem saepe timore\n\n \n\nmacerat invidia ante oculos illum esse potentem,\n\n \n\nillum aspectari, claro qui incedit honore,\n\n \n\nipsi se in tenebris volvi caenoque queruntur.\n\n \n\nintereunt partim statuarum et nominis ergo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0006","text":"et saepe usque adeo, mortis formidine, vitae\n\n \n\npercipit humanos odium lucisque videndae,\n\n \n\nut sibi consciscant maerenti pectore letum\n\n \n\nobliti fontem curarum hunc esse timorem:\n\n \n\nhunc vexare pudorem, hunc vincula amicitiai\n\n \n\nrumpere et in summa pietate evertere suadet:\n\n \n\nnam iam saepe homines patriam carosque parentis\n\n \n\nprodiderunt vitare Acherusia templa petentes.\n\n \n\nnam vel uti pueri trepidant atque omnia caecis\n\n \n\nin tenebris metuunt, sic nos in luce timemus\n\n \n\ninter dum, nihilo quae sunt metuenda magis quam\n\n \n\nquae pueri in tenebris pavitant finguntque futura.\n\n \n\nhunc igitur terrorem animi tenebrasque necessest\n\n \n\nnon radii solis neque lucida tela diei\n\n \n\ndiscutiant, sed naturae species ratioque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0007","text":"Primum animum dico, mentem quem saepe vocamus,\n\n \n\nin quo consilium vitae regimenque locatum est,\n\n \n\nesse hominis partem nihilo minus ac manus et pes\n\n \n\natque oculei partes animantis totius extant.\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nsensum animi certa non esse in parte locatum,\n\n \n\nverum habitum quendam vitalem corporis esse,\n\n \n\nharmoniam Grai quam dicunt, quod faciat nos\n\n \n\nvivere cum sensu, nulla cum in parte siet mens;\n\n \n\nut bona saepe valetudo cum dicitur esse\n\n \n\ncorporis, et non est tamen haec pars ulla valentis,\n\n \n\nsic animi sensum non certa parte reponunt;\n\n \n\nmagno opere in quo mi diversi errare videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0008","text":"Saepe itaque, in promptu corpus quod cernitur, aegret,\n\n \n\ncum tamen ex alia laetamur parte latenti;\n\n \n\n et retro fit ubi contra sit saepe vicissim,\n\n \n\ncum miser ex animo laetatur corpore toto;\n\n \n\nnon alio pacto quam si, pes cum dolet aegri,\n\n \n\nin nullo caput interea sit forte dolore.\n\n \n\nPraeterea molli cum somno dedita membra\n\n \n\neffusumque iacet sine sensu corpus honustum,\n\n \n\nest aliud tamen in nobis quod tempore in illo\n\n \n\nmultimodis agitatur et omnis accipit in se\n\n \n\nlaetitiae motus et curas cordis inanis.\n\n \n\nNunc animam quoque ut in membris cognoscere possis\n\n \n\nesse neque harmonia corpus sentire solere,\n\n \n\nprincipio fit uti detracto corpore multo\n\n \n\nsaepe tamen nobis in membris vita moretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0009","text":"Atque eadem rursum, cum corpora pauca caloris\n\n \n\ndiffugere forasque per os est editus aër,\n\n \n\ndeserit extemplo venas atque ossa relinquit;\n\n \n\nnoscere ut hinc possis non aequas omnia partis\n\n \n\ncorpora habere neque ex aequo fulcire salutem,\n\n \n\nsed magis haec, venti quae sunt calidique vaporis\n\n \n\nsemina, curare in membris ut vita moretur.\n\n \n\nest igitur calor ac ventus vitalis in ipso\n\n \n\ncorpore, qui nobis moribundos deserit artus.\n\n \n\nquapropter quoniam est animi natura reperta\n\n \n\natque animae quasi pars hominis, redde harmoniai\n\n \n\nnomen, ad organicos alto delatum Heliconi,\n\n \n\nsive aliunde ipsi porro traxere et in illam\n\n \n\ntranstulerunt, proprio quae tum res nomine egebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0010","text":"quidquid  est, habeant: tu cetera percipe dicta.\n\n  \n\nNunc animum atque animam dico coniuncta teneri\n\n \n\ninter se atque unam naturam conficere ex se,\n\n \n\nsed caput esse quasi et dominari in corpore toto\n\n \n\nconsilium, quod nos animum mentemque vocamus.\n\n \n\nidque situm media regione in pectoris haeret.\n\n \n\nhic exultat enim pavor ac metus, haec loca circum\n\n \n\nlaetitiae mulcent: hic ergo mens animusquest.\n\n \n\ncetera pars animae per totum dissita corpus\n\n \n\nparet et ad numen mentis momenque movetur.\n\n \n\nidque sibi solum per se sapit et sibi gaudet,\n\n \n\ncum neque res animam neque corpus commovet una.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0011","text":"et quasi, cum caput aut oculus temptante dolore\n\n \n\nlaeditur in nobis, non omni concruciamur\n\n \n\ncorpore, sic animus nonnumquam laeditur ipse\n\n \n\nlaetitiaque viget, cum cetera pars animai\n\n \n\nper membra atque artus nulla novitate cietur;\n\n \n\nverum ubi vementi magis est commota metu mens,\n\n \n\nconsentire animam totam per membra videmus\n\n \n\nsudoresque ita palloremque existere toto\n\n \n\ncorpore et infringi linguam vocemque aboriri,\n\n \n\ncaligare oculos, sonere auris, succidere artus,\n\n \n\ndenique concidere ex animi terrore videmus\n\n \n\nsaepe homines; facile ut quivis hinc noscere possit\n\n \n\nesse animam cum animo coniunctam, quae cum animi \n\n \n\npercussa est, exim corpus propellit et icit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0012","text":"Haec eadem ratio naturam animi atque animai\n\n \n\ncorpoream docet esse; ubi enim propellere membra,\n\n \n\n corripere ex somno corpus mutareque vultum\n\n \n\natque hominem totum regere ac versare videtur,\n\n \n\nquorum nil fieri sine tactu posse videmus\n\n \n\nnec tactum porro sine corpore, nonne fatendumst\n\n \n\ncorporea natura animum constare animamque?\n\n \n\npraeterea pariter fungi cum corpore et una\n\n \n\nconsentire animum nobis in corpore cernis.\n\n \n\nsi minus offendit vitam vis horrida teli\n\n \n\nossibus ac nervis disclusis intus adacta,\n\n \n\nat tamen insequitur languor terraeque petitus\n\n \n\nsuavis et in terra mentis qui gignitur aestus\n\n \n\ninter dumque quasi exsurgendi incerta voluntas.\n\n \n\nergo corpoream naturam animi esse necessest,\n\n \n\ncorporeis quoniam telis ictuque laborat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0013","text":"Is tibi nunc animus quali sit corpore et unde\n\n \n\nconstiterit pergam rationem reddere dictis.\n\n \n\nprincipio esse aio persuptilem atque minutis\n\n \n\nperquam corporibus factum constare. id ita esse\n\n \n\nhinc licet advertas animum, ut pernoscere possis.\n\n  \n\n Nil adeo fieri celeri ratione videtur,\n\n \n\nquam si mens fieri proponit et inchoat ipsa;\n\n \n\nocius ergo animus quam res se perciet ulla,\n\n \n\nante oculos quorum in promptu natura videtur.\n\n \n\nat quod mobile tanto operest, constare rutundis\n\n \n\nperquam seminibus debet perquamque minutis,\n\n \n\nmomine uti parvo possint inpulsa moveri.\n\n \n\nnamque movetur aqua et tantillo momine flutat,\n\n \n\nquippe volubilibus parvisque creata figuris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0014","text":"at contra mellis constantior est natura\n\n \n\net pigri latices magis et cunctantior actus:\n\n \n\nhaeret enim inter se magis omnis materiai\n\n \n\ncopia, ni mirum quia non tam levibus extat\n\n \n\ncorporibus neque tam suptilibus atque rutundis.\n\n \n\nnamque papaveris aura potest suspensa levisque\n\n \n\ncogere ut ab summo tibi diffluat altus acervus,\n\n \n\nat contra lapidum coniectum spicarumque\n\n \n\nnoenu potest. igitur parvissima corpora pro quam\n\n \n\net levissima sunt, ita mobilitate fruuntur;\n\n \n\nat contra quae cumque magis cum pondere magno\n\n \n\nasperaque inveniuntur, eo stabilita magis sunt.\n\n \n\nnunc igitur quoniamst animi natura reperta\n\n \n\nmobilis egregie, perquam constare necessest\n\n \n\ncorporibus parvis et levibus atque rutundis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0015","text":"quae tibi cognita res in multis, o bone, rebus\n\n \n\nutilis invenietur et opportuna cluebit.\n\n \n\nHaec quoque res etiam naturam dedicat eius,\n\n \n\nquam tenui constet textura quamque loco se\n\n \n\ncontineat parvo, si possit conglomerari,\n\n \n\nquod simul atque hominem leti secura quies est\n\n \n\nindepta atque animi natura animaeque recessit,\n\n \n\nnil ibi libatum de toto corpore cernas\n\n \n\nad speciem, nihil ad pondus: mors omnia praestat,\n\n \n\nvitalem praeter sensum calidumque vaporem.\n\n \n\nergo animam totam perparvis esse necessest\n\n \n\nseminibus nexam per venas viscera nervos,\n\n \n\n qua tenus, omnis ubi e toto iam corpore cessit,\n\n \n\nextima membrorum circumcaesura tamen se\n\n \n\nincolumem praestat nec defit ponderis hilum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0016","text":"quod genus est, Bacchi cum flos evanuit aut cum\n\n \n\nspiritus unguenti suavis diffugit in auras\n\n \n\naut aliquo cum iam sucus de corpore cessit;\n\n \n\nnil oculis tamen esse minor res ipsa videtur\n\n \n\npropterea neque detractum de pondere quicquam,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia multa minutaque semina sucos\n\n \n\nefficiunt et odorem in toto corpore rerum.\n\n  \n\nquare etiam atque etiam mentis naturam animaeque\n\n \n\nscire licet perquam pauxillis esse creatam\n\n \n\nseminibus, quoniam fugiens nil ponderis aufert.\n\n \n\nNec tamen haec simplex nobis natura putanda est.\n\n \n\ntenvis enim quaedam moribundos deserit aura\n\n \n\nmixta vapore, vapor porro trahit aëra secum;\n\n \n\nnec calor est quisquam, cui non sit mixtus et aër;\n\n \n\nrara quod eius enim constat natura, necessest\n\n \n\naëris inter eum primordia multa moveri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0017","text":"iam triplex animi est igitur natura reperta;\n\n \n\nnec tamen haec sat sunt ad sensum cuncta creandum,\n\n \n\nnil horum quoniam recipit mens posse creare\n\n \n\nsensiferos motus, quae denique mente volutat.\n\n \n\nquarta quoque his igitur quaedam natura necessest\n\n \n\nadtribuatur; east omnino nominis expers;\n\n \n\nqua neque mobilius quicquam neque tenvius extat\n\n \n\nnec magis e parvis et levibus ex elementis;\n\n \n\nsensiferos motus quae didit prima per artus.\n\n \n\nprima cietur enim, parvis perfecta figuris,\n\n \n\ninde calor motus et venti caeca potestas\n\n \n\naccipit, inde aër, inde omnia mobilitantur:\n\n \n\nconcutitur sanguis, tum viscera persentiscunt\n\n \n\nomnia, postremis datur ossibus atque medullis\n\n \n\nsive voluptas est sive est contrarius ardor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0018","text":"nec temere huc dolor usque potest penetrare neque acre\n\n \n\npermanare malum, quin omnia perturbentur\n\n \n\nusque adeo  vitae desit locus atque animai\n\n \n\ndiffugiant partes per caulas corporis omnis.\n\n \n\nsed plerumque fit in summo quasi corpore finis\n\n \n\nmotibus: hanc ob rem vitam retinere valemus.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quo pacto inter sese mixta quibusque\n\n \n\ncompta modis vigeant rationem reddere aventem\n\n \n\nabstrahit invitum patrii sermonis egestas;\n\n \n\nsed tamen, ut potero summatim attingere, tangam.\n\n  \n\ninter enim cursant primordia principiorum\n\n \n\nmotibus inter se, nihil ut secernier unum\n\n \n\npossit nec spatio fieri divisa potestas,\n\n \n\nsed quasi multae vis unius corporis extant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0019","text":"quod genus in quovis animantum viscere volgo\n\n \n\nest odor et quidam color et sapor, et tamen ex his\n\n \n\nomnibus est unum perfectum corporis augmen,\n\n \n\nsic calor atque aër et venti caeca potestas\n\n \n\nmixta creant unam naturam et mobilis illa\n\n \n\nvis, initum motus ab se quae dividit ollis,\n\n \n\nsensifer unde oritur primum per viscera motus.\n\n \n\n nam penitus prorsum latet haec natura subestque\n\n \n\nnec magis hac infra quicquam est in corpore nostro\n\n \n\natque anima est animae proporro totius ipsa.\n\n \n\nquod genus in nostris membris et corpore toto\n\n \n\nmixta latens animi vis est animaeque potestas,\n\n \n\ncorporibus quia de parvis paucisque creatast,\n\n \n\nsic tibi nominis haec expers vis, facta minutis\n\n \n\ncorporibus, latet atque animae quasi totius ipsa\n\n \n\nproporrost anima et dominatur corpore toto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0020","text":"consimili ratione necessest ventus et aër\n\n \n\net calor inter se vigeant commixta per artus\n\n \n\natque aliis aliud subsit magis emineatque,\n\n \n\nut quiddam fieri videatur ab omnibus unum,\n\n \n\nni calor ac ventus seorsum seorsumque potestas\n\n \n\naëris interemant sensum diductaque solvant.\n\n  \n\nest etiam calor ille animo, quem sumit, in ira\n\n \n\ncum fervescit et ex oculis micat acrius ardor;\n\n \n\nest et frigida multa, comes formidinis, aura,\n\n \n\nquae ciet horrorem membris et concitat artus;\n\n \n\nest etiam quoque pacati status aëris ille,\n\n \n\npectore tranquillo fit qui voltuque sereno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0021","text":"sed calidi plus est illis quibus acria corda\n\n \n\niracundaque mens facile effervescit in ira,\n\n \n\nquo genere in primis vis est violenta leonum,\n\n \n\npectora qui fremitu rumpunt plerumque gementes\n\n \n\nnec capere irarum fluctus in pectore possunt.\n\n \n\nat ventosa magis cervorum frigida mens est\n\n \n\net gelidas citius per viscera concitat auras,\n\n \n\nquae tremulum faciunt membris existere motum.\n\n \n\nat natura boum placido magis aëre vivit\n\n \n\nnec nimis irai fax umquam subdita percit\n\n \n\nfumida, suffundens caecae caliginis umbra,\n\n \n\nnec gelidis torpet telis perfixa pavoris;\n\n \n\ninterutrasque sitast cervos saevosque leones.\n\n \n\nsic hominum genus est: quamvis doctrina politos\n\n \n\nconstituat pariter quosdam, tamen illa relinquit\n\n \n\nnaturae cuiusque animi vestigia prima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0022","text":"nec radicitus evelli mala posse putandumst,\n\n \n\nquin proclivius hic iras decurrat ad acris,\n\n \n\nille metu citius paulo temptetur, at ille\n\n \n\ntertius accipiat quaedam clementius aequo.\n\n \n\ninque aliis rebus multis differre necessest\n\n \n\nnaturas hominum varias moresque sequacis;\n\n \n\nquorum ego nunc nequeo caecas exponere causas\n\n \n\nnec reperire figurarum tot nomina quot sunt\n\n \n\nprincipiis, unde haec oritur variantia rerum.\n\n \n\nillud in his rebus video firmare potesse,\n\n \n\nusque adeo naturarum vestigia linqui\n\n \n\nparvola, quae nequeat ratio depellere nobis,\n\n \n\nut nihil inpediat dignam dis degere vitam.\n\n \n\nHaec igitur natura tenetur corpore ab omni\n\n \n\nipsaque corporis est custos et causa salutis;\n\n \n\nnam communibus inter se radicibus haerent\n\n \n\nnec sine pernicie divelli posse videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0023","text":"quod genus e thuris glaebis evellere odorem\n\n \n\n haud facile est, quin intereat natura quoque eius,\n\n \n\nsic animi atque animae naturam corpore toto\n\n \n\nextrahere haut facile est, quin omnia dissoluantur.\n\n \n\ninplexis ita principiis ab origine prima\n\n \n\ninter se fiunt consorti praedita vita,\n\n \n\nnec sibi quaeque sine alterius vi posse videtur\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animi seorsum sentire potestas,\n\n \n\nsed communibus inter eas conflatur utrimque\n\n \n\nmotibus accensus nobis per viscera sensus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea corpus per se nec gignitur umquam\n\n \n\nnec crescit neque post mortem durare videtur.\n\n \n\nnon enim, ut umor aquae dimittit saepe vaporem,\n\n \n\nqui datus est, neque ea causa convellitur ipse,\n\n \n\nsed manet incolumis, non, inquam, sic animai\n\n \n\ndiscidium possunt artus perferre relicti,\n\n \n\nsed penitus pereunt convulsi conque putrescunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0024","text":"ex ineunte aevo sic corporis atque animai\n\n \n\nmutua vitalis discunt contagia motus,\n\n \n\nmaternis etiam membris alvoque reposta,\n\n \n\ndiscidium  nequeat fieri sine peste maloque;\n\n \n\nut videas, quoniam coniunctast causa salutis,\n\n \n\nconiunctam quoque naturam consistere eorum.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, siquis corpus sentire refutat\n\n \n\natque animam credit permixtam corpore toto\n\n \n\nsuscipere hunc motum quem sensum nominitamus,\n\n \n\nvel manifestas res contra verasque repugnat.\n\n \n\nquid sit enim corpus sentire quis adferet umquam,\n\n \n\nsi non ipsa palam quod res dedit ac docuit nos?\n\n \n\n'at dimissa anima corpus caret undique sensu.'\n\n \n\nperdit enim quod non proprium fuit eius in aevo\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea perdit quom expellitur aevo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0025","text":"Dicere porro oculos nullam rem cernere posse,\n\n \n\nsed per eos animum ut foribus spectare reclusis,\n\n \n\ndifficilest, contra cum sensus ducat eorum;\n\n \n\nsensus enim trahit atque acies detrudit ad ipsas,\n\n \n\nfulgida praesertim cum cernere saepe nequimus,\n\n \n\nlumina luminibus quia nobis praepediuntur.\n\n \n\nquod foribus non fit; neque enim, qua cernimus ipsi,\n\n \n\nostia suscipiunt ullum reclusa laborem.\n\n \n\npraeterea si pro foribus sunt lumina nostra,\n\n \n\niam magis exemptis oculis debere videtur\n\n \n\ncernere res animus sublatis postibus ipsis.\n\n \n\nIllud in his rebus nequaquam sumere possis,\n\n \n\nDemocriti quod sancta viri sententia ponit,\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animi primordia singula primis\n\n \n\nadposita alternis, variare ac nectere membra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0026","text":"nam cum multo sunt animae elementa minora\n\n \n\nquam quibus e corpus nobis et viscera constant,\n\n \n\ntum numero quoque concedunt et rara per artus\n\n \n\ndissita sunt, dum taxat ut hoc promittere possis,\n\n \n\nquantula prima queant nobis iniecta ciere\n\n \n\ncorpora sensiferos motus in corpore, tanta\n\n \n\nintervalla tenere exordia prima animai.\n\n  \n\nnam neque pulveris inter dum sentimus adhaesum\n\n \n\ncorpore nec membris incussam sidere cretam,\n\n \n\n nec nebulam noctu neque arani tenvia fila\n\n \n\nobvia sentimus, quando obretimur euntes,\n\n \n\nnec supera caput eiusdem cecidisse vietam\n\n \n\nvestem nec plumas avium papposque volantis,\n\n \n\nqui nimia levitate cadunt plerumque gravatim,\n\n \n\nnec repentis itum cuiusvis cumque animantis\n\n \n\nsentimus nec priva pedum vestigia quaeque,\n\n \n\ncorpore quae in nostro culices et cetera ponunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0027","text":"usque adeo prius est in nobis multa ciendum\n\n \n\nquam primordia sentiscant concussa animai,\n\n \n\nsemina corporibus nostris inmixta per artus,\n\n \n\net quam in his intervallis tuditantia possint\n\n \n\nconcursare coire et dissultare vicissim.\n\n \n\nEt magis est animus vitai claustra coërcens\n\n \n\net dominantior ad vitam quam vis animai.\n\n \n\nnam sine mente animoque nequit residere per artus\n\n \n\ntemporis exiguam partem pars ulla animai,\n\n \n\nsed comes insequitur facile et discedit in auras\n\n \n\net gelidos artus in leti frigore linquit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0028","text":"at manet in vita cui mens animusque remansit,\n\n \n\nquamvis est circum caesis lacer undique membris;\n\n \n\ntruncus adempta anima circum membrisque remota\n\n \n\nvivit et aetherias vitalis suscipit auras;\n\n \n\nsi non omnimodis, at magna parte animai\n\n \n\nprivatus, tamen in vita cunctatur et haeret;\n\n \n\nut, lacerato oculo circum si pupula mansit\n\n \n\nincolumis, stat cernundi vivata potestas,\n\n \n\ndum modo ne totum corrumpas luminis orbem\n\n \n\net circum caedas aciem solamque relinquas;\n\n \n\nid quoque enim sine pernicie non fiet eorum.\n\n \n\nat si tantula pars oculi media illa peresa est,\n\n \n\noccidit extemplo lumen tenebraeque secuntur,\n\n \n\nincolumis quamvis alioqui splendidus orbis.\n\n \n\nhoc anima atque animus vincti sunt foedere semper.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0029","text":"Nunc age, nativos animantibus et mortalis\n\n \n\nesse animos animasque levis ut noscere possis,\n\n \n\nconquisita diu dulcique reperta labore\n\n \n\ndigna tua pergam disponere carmina vita.\n\n \n\ntu fac utrumque uno subiungas nomine eorum\n\n \n\natque animam verbi causa cum dicere pergam,\n\n \n\nmortalem esse docens, animum quoque dicere credas,\n\n \n\nqua tenus est unum inter se coniunctaque res est.\n\n \n\nPrincipio quoniam tenuem constare minutis\n\n \n\ncorporibus docui multoque minoribus esse\n\n \n\nprincipiis factam quam liquidus umor aquai\n\n \n\naut nebula aut fumus —; nam longe mobilitate\n\n \n\npraestat et a tenui causa magis icta movetur,\n\n \n\nquippe ubi imaginibus fumi nebulaeque movetur;\n\n \n\nquod genus in somnis sopiti ubi cernimus alte","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0030","text":"exhalare vaporem altaria ferreque fumum;\n\n \n\nnam procul haec dubio nobis simulacra gerunturæ\n\n \n\nnunc igitur quoniam quassatis undique vasis\n\n \n\ndiffluere umorem et laticem discedere cernis,\n\n \n\net nebula ac fumus quoniam discedit in auras,\n\n \n\ncrede animam quoque diffundi multoque perire\n\n \n\n ocius et citius dissolvi in corpora prima,\n\n \n\ncum semel ex hominis membris ablata recessit;\n\n \n\nquippe etenim corpus, quod vas quasi constitit eius,\n\n \n\ncum cohibere nequit conquassatum ex aliqua re\n\n \n\nac rarefactum detracto sanguine venis,\n\n \n\naëre qui credas posse hanc cohiberier ullo,\n\n \n\ncorpore qui nostro rarus magis incohibens sit?\n\n  \n\nPraeterea gigni pariter cum corpore et una\n\n \n\ncrescere sentimus pariterque senescere mentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0031","text":"nam vel ut infirmo pueri teneroque vagantur\n\n \n\ncorpore, sic animi sequitur sententia tenvis.\n\n \n\ninde ubi robustis adolevit viribus aetas,\n\n \n\nconsilium quoque maius et auctior est animi vis.\n\n \n\npost ubi iam validis quassatum est viribus aevi\n\n \n\ncorpus et obtusis ceciderunt viribus artus,\n\n \n\nclaudicat ingenium, delirat lingua  mens,\n\n \n\nomnia deficiunt atque uno tempore desunt.\n\n \n\nergo dissolui quoque convenit omnem animai\n\n \n\nnaturam, ceu fumus, in altas aëris auras;\n\n \n\nquando quidem gigni pariter pariterque videmus\n\n \n\ncrescere et,  docui, simul aevo fessa fatisci.\n\n \n\nHuc accedit uti videamus, corpus ut ipsum\n\n \n\nsuscipere inmanis morbos durumque dolorem,\n\n \n\nsic animum curas acris luctumque metumque;\n\n \n\nquare participem leti quoque convenit esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0032","text":"quin etiam morbis in corporis avius errat\n\n \n\nsaepe animus; dementit enim deliraque fatur,\n\n \n\ninter dumque gravi lethargo fertur in altum\n\n \n\naeternumque soporem oculis nutuque cadenti;\n\n \n\nunde neque exaudit voces nec noscere voltus\n\n \n\nillorum potis est, ad vitam qui revocantes\n\n \n\ncircum stant lacrimis rorantes ora genasque.\n\n \n\nquare animum quoque dissolui fateare necessest,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem penetrant in eum contagia morbi;\n\n \n\nnam dolor ac morbus leti fabricator uterquest,\n\n \n\nmultorum exitio perdocti quod sumus ante.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\ndenique cor, hominem cum vini vis penetravit\n\n \n\nacris et in venas discessit diditus ardor,\n\n \n\nconsequitur gravitas membrorum, praepediuntur\n\n \n\ncrura vacillanti, tardescit lingua, madet mens,\n\n \n\nnant oculi, clamor singultus iurgia gliscunt,\n\n \n\net iam cetera de genere hoc quae cumque secuntur,\n\n \n\ncur ea sunt, nisi quod vehemens violentia vini\n\n \n\nconturbare animam consuevit corpore in ipso?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0033","text":"at quae cumque queunt conturbari inque pediri,\n\n \n\nsignificant, paulo si durior insinuarit\n\n \n\ncausa, fore ut pereant aevo privata futuro.\n\n  \n\nQuin etiam subito vi morbi saepe coactus\n\n \n\nante oculos aliquis nostros, ut fulminis ictu,\n\n \n\nconcidit et spumas agit, ingemit et tremit artus,\n\n \n\ndesipit, extentat nervos, torquetur, anhelat\n\n \n\ninconstanter, et in iactando membra fatigat,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia vis morbi distracta per artus\n\n \n\n turbat agens animam, spumans  in aequore salso\n\n \n\nventorum validis fervescunt viribus undae.\n\n \n\nexprimitur porro gemitus, quia membra dolore\n\n \n\nadficiuntur et omnino quod semina vocis\n\n \n\neliciuntur et ore foras glomerata feruntur\n\n \n\nqua quasi consuerunt et sunt munita viai.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0034","text":"desipientia fit, quia vis animi atque animai\n\n \n\nconturbatur et, ut docui, divisa seorsum\n\n \n\ndisiectatur eodem illo distracta veneno.\n\n \n\ninde ubi iam morbi reflexit causa, reditque\n\n \n\nin latebras acer corrupti corporis umor,\n\n \n\ntum quasi vaccillans primum consurgit et omnis\n\n \n\npaulatim redit in sensus animamque receptat.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur tantis ubi morbis corpore in ipso\n\n \n\niactentur miserisque modis distracta laborent,\n\n \n\ncur eadem credis sine corpore in aëre aperto\n\n \n\ncum validis ventis aetatem degere posse?\n\n \n\nEt quoniam mentem sanari corpus ut aegrum\n\n \n\ncernimus et flecti medicina posse videmus,\n\n \n\nid quoque praesagit mortalem vivere mentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0035","text":"addere enim partis aut ordine traiecere aecumst\n\n \n\naut aliquid prosum de summa detrahere hilum,\n\n \n\ncommutare animum qui cumque adoritur et infit\n\n \n\naut aliam quamvis naturam flectere quaerit.\n\n \n\nat neque transferri sibi partis nec tribui vult\n\n \n\ninmortale quod est quicquam neque defluere hilum;\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque suis mutatum finibus exit,\n\n \n\ncontinuo hoc mors est illius quod fuit ante.\n\n \n\nergo animus sive aegrescit, mortalia signa\n\n \n\nmittit, uti docui, seu flectitur a medicina.\n\n \n\nusque adeo falsae rationi vera videtur\n\n \n\nres occurrere et effugium praecludere eunti\n\n \n\nancipitique refutatu convincere falsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0036","text":"Denique saepe hominem paulatim cernimus ire\n\n \n\net membratim vitalem deperdere sensum;\n\n \n\nin pedibus primum digitos livescere et unguis,\n\n \n\ninde pedes et crura mori, post inde per artus\n\n \n\nire alios tractim gelidi vestigia leti.\n\n \n\nscinditur atque animae haec quoniam natura nec uno\n\n \n\ntempore sincera existit, mortalis habendast.\n\n \n\nquod si forte putas ipsam se posse per artus\n\n \n\nintrosum trahere et partis conducere in unum\n\n \n\natque ideo cunctis sensum diducere membris,\n\n \n\nat locus ille tamen, quo copia tanta animai\n\n \n\ncogitur, in sensu debet maiore videri;\n\n \n\nqui quoniam nusquamst, ni mirum, ut diximus ,\n\n \n\ndilaniata foras dispargitur, interit ergo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0037","text":"quin etiam si iam libeat concedere falsum\n\n \n\net dare posse animam glomerari in corpore eorum,\n\n \n\nlumina qui lincunt moribundi particulatim,\n\n \n\nmortalem tamen esse animam fateare necesse\n\n \n\nnec refert utrum pereat dispersa per auras\n\n \n\nan contracta suis e partibus obbrutescat,\n\n \n\nquando hominem totum magis ac magis undique sensus\n\n \n\ndeficit et vitae minus et minus undique restat.\n\n  \n\n Et quoniam mens est hominis pars una locoque\n\n \n\nfixa manet certo, vel ut aures atque oculi sunt\n\n \n\natque alii sensus qui vitam cumque gubernant,\n\n \n\net vel uti manus atque oculus naresve seorsum\n\n \n\nsecreta ab nobis nequeunt sentire neque esse,\n\n \n\nsed tamen in parvo lincuntur tempore tali,\n\n \n\nsic animus per se non quit sine corpore et ipso\n\n \n\nesse homine, illius quasi quod vas esse videtur,\n\n \n\nsive aliud quid vis potius coniunctius ei\n\n \n\nfingere, quandoquidem conexu corpus adhaeret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0038","text":"Denique corporis atque animi vivata potestas\n\n \n\ninter se coniuncta valent vitaque fruuntur;\n\n \n\nnec sine corpore enim vitalis edere motus\n\n \n\nsola potest animi per se natura nec autem\n\n \n\ncassum anima corpus durare et sensibus uti.\n\n \n\nscilicet avolsus radicibus ut nequit ullam\n\n \n\ndispicere ipse oculus rem seorsum corpore toto,\n\n \n\nsic anima atque animus per se nil posse videtur.\n\n \n\nni mirum quia  venas et viscera mixtim,\n\n \n\nper nervos atque ossa tenentur corpore ab omni\n\n \n\nnec magnis intervallis primordia possunt\n\n \n\nlibera dissultare, ideo conclusa moventur\n\n \n\nsensiferos motus, quos extra corpus in auras\n\n \n\naëris haut possunt post mortem eiecta moveri\n\n \n\npropterea quia non simili ratione tenentur;\n\n \n\ncorpus enim atque animans erit aër, si cohibere\n\n \n\nsese anima atque in eos poterit concludere motus,\n\n \n\nquos ante in nervis et in ipso corpore agebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0039","text":"quare etiam atque etiam resoluto corporis omni\n\n \n\ntegmine et eiectis extra vitalibus auris\n\n \n\ndissolui sensus animi fateare necessest\n\n \n\natque animam, quoniam coniunctast causa duobus.\n\n \n\nDenique cum corpus nequeat perferre animai\n\n \n\ndiscidium, quin in taetro tabescat odore,\n\n \n\nquid dubitas quin ex imo penitusque coorta\n\n \n\nemanarit uti fumus diffusa animae vis,\n\n \n\natque ideo tanta mutatum putre ruina\n\n \n\nconciderit corpus, penitus quia mota loco sunt\n\n \n\nfundamenta foras manant animaeque per artus\n\n \n\nperque viarum omnis flexus, in corpore qui sunt,\n\n \n\natque foramina? multimodis ut noscere possis\n\n \n\ndispertitam animae naturam exisse per artus\n\n \n\net prius esse sibi distractam corpore in ipso,\n\n \n\nquam prolapsa foras enaret in aëris auras.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0040","text":"Quin etiam finis dum vitae vertitur intra,\n\n \n\nsaepe aliqua tamen e causa labefacta videtur\n\n \n\nire anima ac toto solui de corpore \n\n \n\net quasi supremo languescere tempore voltus\n\n \n\nmolliaque exsangui cadere omnia  membra.\n\n \n\nquod genus est, animo male factum cum perhibetur\n\n \n\naut animam liquisse; ubi iam trepidatur et omnes\n\n \n\nextremum cupiunt vitae reprehendere vinclum;\n\n \n\nconquassatur enim tum mens animaeque potestas\n\n \n\nomnis. et haec ipso cum corpore conlabefiunt,\n\n \n\nut gravior paulo possit dissolvere causa.\n\n \n\n Quid dubitas tandem quin extra prodita corpus\n\n \n\ninbecilla foras in aperto, tegmine dempto,\n\n \n\nnon modo non omnem possit durare per aevom,\n\n \n\nsed minimum quodvis nequeat consistere tempus?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0041","text":"nec sibi enim quisquam moriens sentire videtur\n\n \n\nire foras animam incolumem de corpore toto,\n\n \n\nnec prius ad iugulum et supera succedere fauces,\n\n \n\nverum deficere in certa regione locatam;\n\n \n\nut sensus alios in parti quemque sua scit\n\n \n\ndissolui. quod si inmortalis nostra foret mens,\n\n \n\nnon tam se moriens dissolvi conquereretur,\n\n \n\nsed magis ire foras vestemque relinquere, ut anguis.\n\n \n\nDenique cur animi numquam mens consiliumque\n\n \n\ngignitur in capite aut pedibus manibusve, sed unis\n\n \n\nsedibus et certis regionibus omnibus haeret,\n\n \n\nsi non certa loca ad nascendum reddita cuique\n\n \n\nsunt, et ubi quicquid possit durare creatum\n\n \n\natque ita multimodis partitis artubus esse,\n\n \n\nmembrorum ut numquam existat praeposterus ordo?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0042","text":"usque adeo sequitur res rem, neque flamma creari\n\n \n\nfluminibus solitast neque in igni gignier algor.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea si inmortalis natura animaist\n\n \n\net sentire potest secreta a corpore nostro,\n\n \n\nquinque, ut opinor, eam faciundum est sensibus auctam.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia nosmet proponere nobis\n\n \n\npossumus infernas animas Acherunte vagare.\n\n \n\npictores itaque et scriptorum saecla priora\n\n \n\nsic animas intro duxerunt sensibus auctas.\n\n \n\nat neque sorsum oculi neque nares nec manus ipsa\n\n \n\nesse potest animae neque sorsum lingua neque aures;\n\n \n\nhaud igitur per se possunt sentire neque esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0043","text":"Et quoniam toto sentimus corpore inesse\n\n \n\nvitalem sensum et totum esse animale videmus,\n\n \n\nsi subito medium celeri praeciderit ictu\n\n \n\nvis aliqua, ut sorsum partem secernat utramque,\n\n \n\ndispertita procul dubio quoque vis animai\n\n \n\net discissa simul cum corpore dissicietur.\n\n \n\nat quod scinditur et partis discedit in ullas,\n\n \n\nscilicet aeternam sibi naturam abnuit esse.\n\n \n\nfalciferos memorant currus abscidere membra\n\n \n\nsaepe ita de subito permixta caede calentis,\n\n \n\nut tremere in terra videatur ab artubus id quod\n\n \n\ndecidit abscisum, cum mens tamen atque hominis vis\n\n \n\nmobilitate mali non quit sentire dolorem;\n\n \n\net simul in pugnae studio quod dedita mens est,\n\n \n\ncorpore relicuo pugnam caedesque petessit,\n\n \n\nnec tenet amissam laevam cum tegmine saepe\n\n \n\ninter equos abstraxe rotas falcesque rapaces,\n\n \n\nnec cecidisse alius dextram, cum scandit et instat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0044","text":"inde alius conatur adempto surgere crure,\n\n \n\ncum digitos agitat propter moribundus humi pes.\n\n \n\net caput abscisum calido viventeque trunco\n\n \n\nservat humi voltum vitalem oculosque patentis,\n\n \n\ndonec reliquias animai reddidit omnes.\n\n \n\nquin etiam tibi si, lingua vibrante, minanti\n\n \n\n serpentis cauda, procero corpore, utrumque\n\n \n\nsit libitum in multas partis discidere ferro,\n\n \n\nomnia iam sorsum cernes ancisa recenti\n\n \n\nvolnere tortari et terram conspargere tabo,\n\n \n\nipsam seque retro partem petere ore priorem,\n\n \n\nvolneris ardenti ut morsu premat icta dolore.\n\n \n\nomnibus esse igitur totas dicemus in illis\n\n \n\nparticulis animas? at ea ratione sequetur\n\n \n\nunam animantem animas habuisse in corpore multas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0045","text":"ergo divisast ea quae fuit una simul cum\n\n \n\ncorpore; quapropter mortale utrumque putandumst,\n\n \n\nin multas quoniam partis disciditur aeque.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea si inmortalis natura animai\n\n \n\nconstat et in corpus nascentibus insinuatur,\n\n \n\ncur super ante actam aetatem meminisse nequimus\n\n \n\nnec vestigia gestarum rerum ulla tenemus?\n\n \n\nnam si tanto operest animi mutata potestas,\n\n \n\nomnis ut actarum exciderit retinentia rerum,\n\n \n\nnon, ut opinor, id ab leto iam longius errat;\n\n \n\nqua propter fateare necessest quae fuit ante\n\n \n\ninteriisse, et quae nunc est nunc esse creatam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0046","text":"Praeterea si iam perfecto corpore nobis\n\n \n\ninferri solitast animi vivata potestas\n\n \n\ntum cum gignimur et vitae cum limen inimus,\n\n \n\nhaud ita conveniebat uti cum corpore et una\n\n \n\ncum membris videatur in ipso sanguine cresse,\n\n \n\nsed vel ut in cavea per se sibi vivere solam\n\n \n\nconvenit, ut sensu corpus tamen affluat omne.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam neque originis esse putandumst\n\n \n\nexpertis animas nec leti lege solutas;\n\n \n\nnam neque tanto opere adnecti potuisse putandumst\n\n \n\ncorporibus nostris extrinsecus insinuatas,\n\n \n\nquod fieri totum contra manifesta docet res\n\n \n\nænamque ita conexa est per venas viscera nervos\n\n \n\nossaque, uti dentes quoque sensu participentur;","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0047","text":"morbus ut indicat et gelidai stringor aquai\n\n \n\net lapis oppressus subitis e frugibus asperæ\n\n \n\nnec, tam contextae cum sint, exire videntur\n\n \n\nincolumes posse et salvas exsolvere sese\n\n \n\nomnibus e nervis atque ossibus articulisque,\n\n \n\nquod si forte putas extrinsecus insinuatam\n\n \n\npermanare animam nobis per membra solere,\n\n \n\ntanto quique magis cum corpore fusa peribit;\n\n \n\nquod permanat enim, dissolvitur, interit ergo;\n\n \n\ndispertitur enim per caulas corporis omnis.\n\n \n\nut cibus, in membra atque artus cum diditur omnis,\n\n \n\ndisperit atque aliam naturam sufficit ex se,\n\n \n\nsic anima atque animus quamvis  integra recens \n\n \n\ncorpus eunt, tamen in manando dissoluuntur,\n\n \n\ndum quasi per caulas omnis diduntur in artus\n\n \n\nparticulae quibus haec animi natura creatur,\n\n \n\nquae nunc in nostro dominatur corpore nata\n\n \n\nex illa quae tunc periit partita per artus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0048","text":"quapropter neque natali privata videtur\n\n \n\n esse die natura animae nec funeris expers.\n\n \n\nSemina praeterea linquontur necne animai\n\n \n\ncorpore in exanimo? quod si lincuntur et insunt,\n\n \n\nhaut erit ut merito inmortalis possit haberi,\n\n \n\npartibus amissis quoniam libata recessit.\n\n \n\nsin ita sinceris membris ablata profugit,\n\n \n\nut nullas partis in corpore liquerit ex se,\n\n \n\nunde cadavera rancenti iam viscere vermes\n\n \n\nexpirant atque unde animantum copia tanta\n\n \n\nexos et exanguis tumidos perfluctuat artus?\n\n \n\nquod si forte animas extrinsecus insinuari?\n\n \n\nvermibus et privas in corpora posse venire\n\n \n\ncredis nec reputas cur milia multa animarum\n\n \n\nconveniant unde una recesserit, hoc tamen est ut\n\n \n\nquaerendum videatur et in discrimen agendum,\n\n \n\nutrum tandem animae venentur semina quaeque\n\n \n\nvermiculorum ipsaeque sibi fabricentur ubi sint,\n\n \n\nan quasi corporibus perfectis insinuentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0049","text":"at neque cur faciant ipsae quareve laborent\n\n \n\ndicere suppeditat. neque enim, sine corpore cum sunt,\n\n \n\nsollicitae volitant morbis alguque fameque;\n\n \n\ncorpus enim magis his vitiis adfine laborat,\n\n \n\net mala multa animus contage fungitur eius.\n\n \n\nsed tamen his esto quamvis facere utile corpus,\n\n \n\ncum subeant; at qua possint via nulla videtur.\n\n \n\nhaut igitur faciunt animae sibi corpora et artus.\n\n \n\nnec tamen est ut qui  perfectis insinuentur\n\n \n\ncorporibus; neque enim poterunt suptiliter esse\n\n \n\nconexae neque consensu contagia fient.\n\n  \n\nDenique cur acris violentia triste leonum\n\n \n\nseminium sequitur, volpes dolus, et fuga cervos?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0050","text":"a patribus datur et  patrius pavor incitat artus,\n\n \n\net iam cetera de genere hoc cur omnia membris\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo generascunt ingenioque,\n\n \n\nsi non, certa suo quia semine seminioque\n\n \n\nvis animi pariter crescit cum corpore quoque?\n\n \n\nquod si inmortalis foret et mutare soleret\n\n \n\ncorpora, permixtis animantes moribus essent,\n\n \n\neffugeret canis Hyrcano de semine saepe\n\n \n\ncornigeri incursum cervi tremeretque per auras\n\n \n\naëris accipiter fugiens veniente columba,\n\n \n\ndesiperent homines, saperent fera saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\nillud enim falsa fertur ratione, quod aiunt\n\n \n\ninmortalem animam mutato corpore flecti;\n\n \n\nquod mutatur enim, dissolvitur, interit ergo;\n\n \n\ntraiciuntur enim partes atque ordine migrant;\n\n \n\nquare dissolui quoque debent posse per artus,\n\n \n\ndenique ut intereant una cum corpore cunctae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0051","text":"sin animas hominum dicent in corpora semper\n\n \n\nire humana, tamen quaeram cur e sapienti\n\n \n\nstulta queat fieri, nec prudens sit puer ullus,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nnec tam doctus equae pullus quam fortis equi vis.\n\n \n\nscilicet in tenero tenerascere corpore mentem\n\n \n\nconfugient. quod si iam fit, fateare necessest\n\n \n\n mortalem esse animam, quoniam mutata per artus\n\n \n\ntanto opere amittit vitam sensumque priorem.\n\n \n\nquove modo poterit pariter cum corpore quoque\n\n \n\nconfirmata cupitum aetatis tangere florem\n\n \n\nvis animi, nisi erit consors in origine prima?\n\n \n\nquidve foras sibi vult membris exire senectis?\n\n \n\nan metuit conclusa manere in corpore putri\n\n \n\net domus aetatis spatio ne fessa vetusto\n\n \n\nobruat?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0052","text":"at non sunt immortali ulla pericla.\n\n  \n\nDenique conubia ad Veneris partusque ferarum\n\n \n\nesse animas praesto deridiculum esse videtur,\n\n \n\nexpectare immortalis mortalia membra\n\n \n\ninnumero numero certareque praeproperanter\n\n \n\ninter se quae prima potissimaque insinuetur;\n\n \n\nsi non forte ita sunt animarum foedera pacta,\n\n \n\nut quae prima volans advenerit insinuetur\n\n \n\nprima neque inter se contendant viribus hilum.\n\n \n\nDenique in aethere non arbor, non aequore in alto\n\n \n\nnubes esse queunt nec pisces vivere in arvis\n\n \n\nnec cruor in lignis neque saxis sucus inesse.\n\n \n\ncertum ac dispositumst ubi quicquid crescat et insit.\n\n \n\nsic animi natura nequit sine corpore oriri\n\n \n\nsola neque a nervis et sanguine longius esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0053","text":"quod si posset enim, multo prius ipsa animi vis\n\n \n\nin capite aut umeris aut imis calcibus esse\n\n \n\nposset et innasci quavis in parte soleret,\n\n \n\ntandem in eodem homine atque in eodem vase manere.\n\n \n\nquod quoniam nostro quoque constat corpore certum\n\n \n\ndispositumque videtur ubi esse et crescere possit\n\n \n\nsorsum anima atque animus, tanto magis infitiandum\n\n \n\ntotum posse extra corpus durare genique.\n\n \n\nquare, corpus ubi interiit, periisse necessest\n\n \n\nconfiteare animam distractam in corpore toto.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim mortale aeterno iungere et una\n\n \n\nconsentire putare et fungi mutua posse\n\n \n\ndesiperest; quid enim diversius esse putandumst\n\n \n\naut magis inter se disiunctum discrepitansque,\n\n \n\nquam mortale quod est inmortali atque perenni\n\n \n\niunctum in concilio saevas tolerare procellas?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0054","text":"praeterea quaecumque manent aeterna necessest\n\n \n\naut quia sunt solido cum corpore respuere ictus\n\n \n\nnec penetrare pati sibi quicquam quod queat artas\n\n \n\ndissociare intus partis, ut materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora sunt, quorum naturam ostendimus ante,\n\n \n\naut ideo durare aetatem posse per omnem,\n\n \n\nplagarum quia sunt expertia sicut inanest,\n\n \n\nquod manet intactum neque ab ictu fungitur hilum,\n\n \n\naut etiam quia nulla loci sit copia circum,\n\n \n\nquo quasi res possint discedere dissoluique,\n\n \n\nsicut summarum summast aeterna, neque extra\n\n \n\nquis locus est quo diffugiant neque corpora sunt quae\n\n \n\npossint incidere et valida dissolvere plaga.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0055","text":"Quod si forte ideo magis inmortalis habendast,\n\n \n\nquod vitalibus ab rebus munita tenetur,\n\n \n\naut quia non veniunt omnino aliena salutis,\n\n \n\n aut quia quae veniunt aliqua ratione recedunt\n\n \n\npulsa prius quam quid noceant sentire queamus,\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\npraeter enim quam quod morbis cum corporis aegret,\n\n \n\nadvenit id quod eam de rebus saepe futuris\n\n \n\nmacerat inque metu male habet curisque fatigat,\n\n \n\npraeteritisque male admissis peccata remordent.\n\n \n\nadde furorem animi proprium atque oblivia rerum,\n\n \n\nadde quod in nigras lethargi mergitur undas.\n\n  \n\nNil igitur mors est ad nos neque pertinet hilum,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem natura animi mortalis habetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0056","text":"et vel ut ante acto nihil tempore sensimus aegri,\n\n \n\nad confligendum venientibus undique Poenis,\n\n \n\nomnia cum belli trepido concussa tumultu\n\n \n\nhorrida contremuere sub altis aetheris auris,\n\n \n\nin dubioque fuere utrorum ad regna cadendum\n\n \n\nomnibus humanis esset terraque marique,\n\n \n\nsic, ubi non erimus, cum corporis atque animai\n\n \n\ndiscidium fuerit, quibus e sumus uniter apti,\n\n \n\nscilicet haud nobis quicquam, qui non erimus tum,\n\n \n\naccidere omnino poterit sensumque movere,\n\n \n\nnon si terra mari miscebitur et mare caelo.\n\n \n\net si iam nostro sentit de corpore postquam\n\n \n\ndistractast animi natura animaeque potestas,\n\n \n\nnil tamen est ad nos, qui comptu coniugioque\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animae consistimus uniter apti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0057","text":"nec, si materiem nostram collegerit aetas\n\n \n\npost obitum rursumque redegerit ut sita nunc est,\n\n \n\natque iterum nobis fuerint data lumina vitae,\n\n \n\npertineat quicquam tamen ad nos id quoque factum,\n\n \n\ninterrupta semel cum sit repetentia nostri.\n\n \n\net nunc nil ad nos de nobis attinet, ante\n\n \n\nqui fuimus,  iam de illis nos adficit angor.\n\n \n\nnam cum respicias inmensi temporis omne\n\n \n\npraeteritum spatium, tum motus materiai\n\n \n\nmultimodi quam sint, facile hoc adcredere possis,\n\n \n\nsemina saepe in eodem, ut nunc sunt, ordine posta\n\n \n\nhaec eadem, quibus e nunc nos sumus, ante fuisse.\n\n \n\nnec memori tamen id quimus reprehendere mente;\n\n \n\ninter enim iectast vitai pausa vageque\n\n \n\ndeerrarunt passim motus ab sensibus omnes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0058","text":"debet enim, misere si forte aegreque futurumst;\n\n \n\nipse quoque esse in eo tum tempore, cui male possit\n\n \n\naccidere. id quoniam mors eximit, esseque prohibet\n\n \n\nillum cui possint incommoda conciliari,\n\n \n\nscire licet nobis nihil esse in morte timendum\n\n \n\nnec miserum fieri qui non est posse, neque hilum\n\n \n\ndifferre an nullo fuerit iam tempore natus,\n\n \n\nmortalem vitam mors cum inmortalis ademit.\n\n  \n\nProinde ubi se videas hominem indignarier ipsum,\n\n \n\npost mortem fore ut aut putescat corpore posto\n\n \n\naut flammis interfiat malisve ferarum,\n\n \n\nscire licet non sincerum sonere atque subesse\n\n \n\ncaecum aliquem cordi stimulum, quamvis neget ipse\n\n \n\ncredere se quemquam sibi sensum in morte futurum;\n\n \n\n non, ut opinor, enim dat quod promittit et unde\n\n \n\nnec radicitus e vita se tollit et eicit,\n\n \n\nsed facit esse sui quiddam super inscius ipse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0059","text":"vivus enim sibi cum proponit quisque futurum,\n\n \n\ncorpus uti volucres lacerent in morte feraeque,\n\n \n\nipse sui miseret; neque enim se dividit illim\n\n \n\nnec removet satis a proiecto corpore et illum\n\n \n\nse fingit sensuque suo contaminat astans.\n\n \n\nhinc indignatur se mortalem esse creatum\n\n \n\nnec videt in vera nullum fore morte alium se,\n\n \n\nqui possit vivus sibi se lugere peremptum\n\n \n\nstansque iacentem  lacerari urive dolere.\n\n \n\nnam si in morte malumst malis morsuque ferarum\n\n \n\ntractari, non invenio qui non sit acerbum\n\n \n\nignibus inpositum calidis torrescere flammis\n\n \n\naut in melle situm suffocari atque rigere\n\n \n\nfrigore, cum summo gelidi cubat aequore saxi,\n\n \n\nurgerive superne obrutum pondere terrae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0060","text":"'Iam iam non domus accipiet te laeta neque uxor\n\n \n\noptima, nec dulces occurrent oscula nati\n\n \n\npraeripere et tacita pectus dulcedine tangent.\n\n \n\nnon poteris factis florentibus esse tuisque\n\n \n\npraesidium. misero misere' aiunt 'omnia ademit\n\n \n\nuna dies infesta tibi tot praemia vitae.'\n\n \n\nillud in his rebus non addunt 'nec tibi earum\n\n \n\niam desiderium rerum super insidet una.'\n\n \n\nquod bene si videant animo dictisque sequantur,\n\n \n\ndissoluant animi magno se angore metuque.\n\n \n\n'tu quidem ut es leto sopitus, sic eris aevi\n\n \n\nquod super est cunctis privatus doloribus aegris;\n\n \n\nat nos horrifico cinefactum te prope busto\n\n \n\ninsatiabiliter deflevimus, aeternumque\n\n \n\nnulla dies nobis maerorem e pectore demet.'","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0061","text":"illud ab hoc igitur quaerendum est, quid sit amari\n\n \n\ntanto opere, ad somnum si res redit atque quietem,\n\n \n\ncur quisquam aeterno possit tabescere luctu.\n\n \n\nHoc etiam faciunt ubi discubuere tenentque\n\n \n\npocula saepe homines et inumbrant ora coronis,\n\n \n\nex animo ut dicant: 'brevis hic est fructus homullis;\n\n \n\niam fuerit neque post umquam revocare licebit.'\n\n \n\ntam quam in morte mali cum primis hoc sit eorum,\n\n \n\nquod sitis exurat miseros atque arida torrat,\n\n \n\naut aliae cuius desiderium insideat rei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0062","text":"nec sibi enim quisquam tum se vitamque requiret,\n\n \n\ncum pariter mens et corpus sopita quiescunt;\n\n \n\nnam licet aeternum per nos sic esse soporem,\n\n \n\nnec desiderium nostri nos adficit ullum,\n\n \n\net tamen haud quaquam nostros tunc illa per artus\n\n \n\nlonge ab sensiferis primordia motibus errant,\n\n \n\ncum correptus homo ex somno se colligit ipse.\n\n \n\nmulto igitur mortem minus ad nos esse putandumst,\n\n \n\nsi minus esse potest quam quod nihil esse videmus;\n\n \n\nmaior enim turbae disiectus materiai\n\n \n\nconsequitur leto nec quisquam expergitus extat,\n\n \n\nfrigida quem semel est vitai pausa secuta.\n\n  \n\n Denique si vocem rerum natura repente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0063","text":"mittat et hoc alicui nostrum sic increpet ipsa:\n\n \n\n'quid tibi tanto operest, mortalis, quod nimis aegris\n\n \n\nluctibus indulges? quid mortem congemis ac fles?\n\n \n\nnam  grata fuit tibi vita ante acta priorque\n\n \n\net non omnia pertusum congesta quasi in vas\n\n \n\ncommoda perfluxere atque ingrata interiere;\n\n \n\ncur non ut plenus vitae conviva recedis\n\n \n\naequo animoque capis securam, stulte, quietem?\n\n \n\nsin ea quae fructus cumque es periere profusa\n\n \n\nvitaque in offensost, cur amplius addere quaeris,\n\n \n\nrursum quod pereat male et ingratum occidat omne,\n\n \n\nnon potius vitae finem facis atque laboris?\n\n \n\nnam tibi praeterea quod machiner inveniamque,\n\n \n\nquod placeat, nihil est; eadem sunt omnia semper.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0064","text":"si tibi non annis corpus iam marcet et artus\n\n \n\nconfecti languent, eadem tamen omnia restant,\n\n \n\nomnia si perges vivendo vincere saecla,\n\n \n\natque etiam potius, si numquam sis moriturus',\n\n \n\nquid respondemus, nisi iustam intendere litem\n\n \n\nnaturam et veram verbis exponere causam?\n\n \n\ngrandior hic vero si iam seniorque queratur\n\n \n\natque obitum lamentetur miser amplius aequo,\n\n \n\nnon merito inclamet magis et voce increpet acri:\n\n \n\n'aufer abhinc lacrimas, baratre, et compesce querellas.\n\n \n\nomnia perfunctus vitai praemia marces;\n\n \n\nsed quia semper aves quod abest, praesentia temnis,\n\n \n\ninperfecta tibi elapsast ingrataque vita,\n\n \n\net nec opinanti mors ad caput adstitit ante\n\n \n\nquam satur ac plenus possis discedere rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0065","text":"nunc aliena tua tamen aetate omnia mitte\n\n \n\naequo animoque, age dum, magnis concede necessis?'\n\n \n\niure, ut opinor, agat, iure increpet inciletque;\n\n \n\ncedit enim rerum novitate extrusa vetustas\n\n \n\nsemper, et ex aliis aliud reparare necessest.\n\n \n\nNec quisquam in barathrum nec Tartara deditur atra;\n\n \n\nmateries opus est, ut crescant postera saecla;\n\n \n\nquae tamen omnia te vita perfuncta sequentur;\n\n \n\nnec minus ergo ante haec quam tu cecidere cadentque.\n\n \n\nsic alid ex alio numquam desistet oriri\n\n \n\nvitaque mancipio nulli datur, omnibus usu.\n\n \n\nrespice item quam nil ad nos ante acta vetustas\n\n \n\ntemporis aeterni fuerit, quam nascimur ante.\n\n \n\nhoc igitur speculum nobis natura futuri\n\n \n\ntemporis exponit post mortem denique nostram.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0066","text":"numquid ibi horribile apparet, num triste videtur\n\n \n\nquicquam, non omni somno securius exstat?\n\n  \n\nAtque ea ni mirum quae cumque Acherunte profundo\n\n \n\nprodita sunt esse, in vita sunt omnia nobis.\n\n \n\nnec miser inpendens magnum timet aëre saxum\n\n \n\nTantalus, ut famast, cassa formidine torpens;\n\n \n\nsed magis in vita divom metus urget inanis\n\n \n\nmortalis casumque timent quem cuique ferat fors.\n\n \n\nnec Tityon volucres ineunt Acherunte iacentem\n\n \n\nnec quod sub magno scrutentur pectore quicquam\n\n \n\n perpetuam aetatem possunt reperire profecto.\n\n \n\nquam libet immani proiectu corporis exstet,\n\n \n\nqui non sola novem dispessis iugera membris\n\n \n\noptineat, sed qui terrai totius orbem,\n\n \n\nnon tamen aeternum poterit perferre dolorem\n\n \n\nnec praebere cibum proprio de corpore semper.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0067","text":"sed Tityos nobis hic est, in amore iacentem\n\n \n\nquem volucres lacerant atque exest anxius angor\n\n \n\naut alia quavis scindunt cuppedine curae.\n\n \n\nSisyphus in vita quoque nobis ante oculos est,\n\n \n\nqui petere a populo fasces saevasque secures\n\n \n\nimbibit et semper victus tristisque recedit.\n\n \n\nnam petere imperium, quod inanest nec datur umquam,\n\n \n\natque in eo semper durum sufferre laborem,\n\n \n\nhoc est adverso nixantem trudere monte\n\n \n\nsaxum, quod tamen  summo iam vertice rusum\n\n \n\nvolvitur et plani raptim petit aequora campi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0068","text":"deinde animi ingratam naturam pascere semper\n\n \n\natque explere bonis rebus satiareque numquam,\n\n \n\nquod faciunt nobis annorum tempora, circum\n\n \n\ncum redeunt fetusque ferunt variosque lepores,\n\n \n\nnec tamen explemur vitai fructibus umquam,\n\n \n\nhoc, ut opinor, id est, aevo florente puellas\n\n \n\nquod memorant laticem pertusum congerere in vas,\n\n \n\nquod tamen expleri nulla ratione potestur.\n\n \n\nCerberus et Furiae iam vero et lucis egestas,\n\n \n\nTartarus horriferos eructans faucibus aestus!\n\n \n\nqui neque sunt usquam nec possunt esse profecto;\n\n \n\nsed metus in vita poenarum pro male factis\n\n \n\nest insignibus insignis scelerisque luela,\n\n \n\ncarcer et horribilis de saxo iactus deorsum,\n\n \n\nverbera carnifices robur pix lammina taedae;\n\n \n\nquae tamen etsi absunt, at mens sibi conscia factis\n\n \n\npraemetuens adhibet stimulos torretque flagellis,\n\n \n\nnec videt interea qui terminus esse malorum\n\n \n\npossit nec quae sit poenarum denique finis,\n\n \n\natque eadem metuit magis haec ne in morte gravescant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0069","text":"hic Acherusia fit stultorum denique vita.\n\n  \n\nHoc etiam tibi tute interdum dicere possis.\n\n \n\n'lumina sis oculis etiam bonus Ancus reliquit,\n\n \n\nqui melior multis quam tu fuit, improbe, rebus.\n\n \n\ninde alii multi reges rerumque potentes\n\n \n\nocciderunt, magnis qui gentibus imperitarunt.\n\n \n\nille quoque ipse, viam qui quondam per mare magnum\n\n \n\nstravit iterque dedit legionibus ire per altum\n\n \n\nac pedibus salsas docuit super ire lucunas\n\n \n\net contempsit equis insultans murmura ponti,\n\n \n\nlumine adempto animam moribundo corpore fudit.\n\n \n\nScipiadas, belli fulmen, Carthaginis horror,\n\n \n\nossa dedit terrae proinde ac famul infimus esset.\n\n \n\nadde repertores doctrinarum atque leporum,\n\n \n\nadde Heliconiadum comites; quorum unus Homerus\n\n \n\nsceptra potitus eadem aliis sopitus quietest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0070","text":"denique Democritum post quam matura vetustas\n\n \n\nadmonuit memores motus languescere mentis,\n\n \n\n sponte sua leto caput obvius optulit ipse.\n\n \n\nipse Epicurus obit decurso lumine vitae,\n\n \n\nqui genus humanum ingenio superavit et omnis\n\n \n\nrestinxit stellas exortus ut aetherius sol.\n\n \n\ntu vero dubitabis et indignabere obire?\n\n \n\nmortua cui vita est prope iam vivo atque videnti,\n\n \n\nqui somno partem maiorem conteris aevi,\n\n \n\net viligans stertis nec somnia cernere cessas\n\n \n\nsollicitamque geris cassa formidine mentem\n\n \n\nnec reperire potes tibi quid sit saepe mali, cum\n\n \n\nebrius urgeris multis miser undique curis\n\n \n\natque animo incerto fluitans errore vagaris.'","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0071","text":"Si possent homines, proinde ac sentire videntur\n\n \n\npondus inesse animo, quod se gravitate fatiget,\n\n \n\ne quibus id fiat causis quoque noscere et unde\n\n \n\ntanta mali tam quam moles in pectore constet,\n\n \n\nhaut ita vitam agerent, ut nunc plerumque videmus\n\n \n\nquid sibi quisque velit nescire et quaerere semper,\n\n \n\ncommutare locum, quasi onus deponere possit.\n\n \n\nexit saepe foras magnis ex aedibus ille,\n\n \n\nesse domi quem pertaesumst, subitoque ,\n\n \n\nquippe foris nihilo melius qui sentiat esse.\n\n \n\ncurrit agens mannos ad villam praecipitanter\n\n \n\nauxilium tectis quasi ferre ardentibus instans;\n\n \n\noscitat extemplo, tetigit cum limina villae,\n\n \n\naut abit in somnum gravis atque oblivia quaerit,\n\n \n\naut etiam properans urbem petit atque revisit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0072","text":"hoc se quisque modo fugit, at quem scilicet, ut fit,\n\n \n\neffugere haut potis est: ingratius haeret et odit\n\n \n\npropterea, morbi quia causam non tenet aeger;\n\n \n\nquam bene si videat, iam rebus quisque relictis\n\n \n\nnaturam primum studeat cognoscere rerum,\n\n \n\ntemporis aeterni quoniam, non unius horae,\n\n \n\nambigitur status, in quo sit mortalibus omnis\n\n \n\naetas, post mortem quae restat cumque manendo.\n\n  \n\nDenique tanto opere in dubiis trepidare periclis\n\n \n\nquae mala nos subigit vitai tanta cupido?\n\n \n\ncerte equidem finis vitae mortalibus adstat\n\n \n\nnec devitari letum pote, quin obeamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0073","text":"praeterea versamur ibidem atque insumus usque\n\n \n\nnec nova vivendo procuditur ulla voluptas;\n\n \n\nsed dum abest quod avemus, id exsuperare videtur\n\n \n\ncetera; post aliud, cum contigit illud, avemus\n\n \n\net sitis aequa tenet vitai semper hiantis.\n\n \n\nposteraque in dubiost fortunam quam vehat aetas,\n\n \n\nquidve ferat nobis casus quive exitus instet.\n\n \n\nnec prorsum vitam ducendo demimus hilum\n\n \n\ntempore de mortis nec delibare valemus,\n\n \n\nquo minus esse diu possimus forte perempti.\n\n \n\nproinde licet quod vis vivendo condere saecla,\n\n \n\nmors aeterna tamen nihilo minus illa manebit,\n\n \n\nnec minus ille diu iam non erit, ex hodierno\n\n \n\nlumine qui finem vitai fecit, et ille,\n\n \n\nmensibus atque annis qui multis occidit ante.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8d90587482fa70cf91d9b70f2ffcee1a6cefbfab334c3a1417ca957ab93c5e70","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T235416178632Z_9990b318","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book Three","source_sha256":"8d90587482fa70cf91d9b70f2ffcee1a6cefbfab334c3a1417ca957ab93c5e70","sha256":"00ded5e204362cfc5cae36e36ee3231afc0ca8b9eeca766c7c6b6d072dd2f2c5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:54:16.178645+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0001","text":"## Book Three\n\nYou who were first able to raise so bright a light from such great darkness,\n\nilluminating the benefits of life,\n\nI follow you, O glory of the Greek race, and now upon your\n\nimprinted marks I set my footsteps, fashioned [after them],\n\nnot so much eager to contend as because of love\n\nby which I long to imitate you; for why should a swallow compete\n\nwith swans, or what, with trembling limbs, could kids\n\nbe able to accomplish in running like the strength of a mighty horse?\n\nYou, father, are the discoverer of things; you supply us with fatherly\n\nprecepts, and from your pages, illustrious one,\n\nas bees in flowery glades sip everything,\n\nso likewise do we feed upon all your golden sayings,\n\ngolden sayings, ever most worthy of everlasting life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0002","text":"For as soon as your reasoning began to proclaim\n\nthe nature of things, sprung from a divine mind,\n\nthe terrors of the mind flee away; the walls of the world\n\npart. I see all things happening throughout the whole void.\n\nThe divinity and the tranquil abodes of the gods appear,\n\nwhich neither winds shake nor clouds sprinkle with rain,\n\nnor does white snow, congealed by sharp frost, falling violate them;\n\nan ever-cloudless aether enwraps [them],\n\nand smiles with light poured forth abundantly:\n\nmoreover, nature supplies everything, nor does any...\n\never takes away the mind's peace.\n\nBut, on the contrary, nowhere do the Acherusian temples appear,\n\nnor does the earth obstruct all things from being discerned\n\nthat are carried on beneath our feet through the void below.\n\nThereupon a certain divine pleasure takes hold of me in these matters,\n\nand a shudder, because by your force nature is thus\n\nlaid open, manifest and uncovered on every side.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0003","text":"And since I have taught what the first-beginnings of all things\n\nare like, and how, differing in so many forms,\n\nthey fly about of their own accord, driven by everlasting motion,\n\nand in what way each thing can be created from these,\n\nnext after these matters, the nature of mind\n\nand of soul seems now to be made clear by my verses,\n\nand that headlong fear of Acheron must be driven forth,\n\nwhich from its deepest foundation troubles human life,\n\nsuffusing everything with the blackness of death and leaving no\n\npleasure clear and pure.\n\nFor when men often say that diseases are more to be feared\n\nand a life of ill repute than the Tartarus of death,\n\nand that they know the nature of the mind to be blood,\n\nor even wind, if perchance their inclination so carries them,\n\nand that they have no need at all of our doctrine,\n\nfrom this you may infer that all is proclaimed by them more for the sake of glory\n\nthan because the thing itself is approved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0004","text":"exiles likewise from their country and driven far away\n\nfrom the sight of men, defiled by a shameful charge,\n\nafflicted by every misery, at last live on,\n\nand, wherever nevertheless the wretched have come, they make offerings to the dead\n\nand sacrifice black cattle and send funeral offerings to the divine shades\n\nand amid much bitterness of circumstances\n\nturn their minds more keenly toward religion.\n\nWherefore it is the more fitting to observe a man in doubtful dangers\n\nand to learn, in adverse circumstances, what sort of man he is;\n\nfor then at last true utterances from the inmost breast\n\nare drawn forth; the mask is torn away, to flow forth.\n\nFinally, avarice and the blind desire for honours,\n\nwhich compel wretched men to overstep the bounds\n\nof right and sometimes to become accomplices and agents of crimes,\n\nto strive night and day with surpassing toil\n\nto emerge into the highest wealth—these wounds of life\n\nare nourished in no small part by fear of death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0005","text":"For shameful contempt and harsh poverty\n\nseem removed from the sweet and settled life\n\nand, as it were, already to linger before the gates of death;\n\nwhence men, while compelled by false terror\n\nto wish to escape far, far away from these things,\n\namass substance and wealth by civil bloodshed,\n\ngreedily double them, heaping slaughter upon slaughter,\n\nthe cruel rejoice at the brother’s mournful funeral\n\nand hate and fear the table of their kinsman.\n\nBy a like reasoning, often from the same fear,\n\nenvy torments them because before their eyes that man is powerful,\n\nthat man is looked upon, who walks in conspicuous honor,\n\nwhile they themselves complain that they roll about in darkness and mire.\n\nSome perish for the sake of statues and a name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0006","text":"And often, to such a degree, through fear of death, does hatred of life\n\nand of beholding the light seize human beings,\n\nthat with sorrowing breast they inflict death upon themselves,\n\nforgetting that this fear is the fountainhead of cares:\n\nit prompts them to assail shame, to break the bonds\n\nof friendship, and in sum to overthrow piety:\n\nfor now often men have betrayed their fatherland and dear parents,\n\nseeking to avoid the Acherusian temples.\n\nFor just as children tremble and fear everything in blind\n\ndarkness, so we too in the light sometimes fear\n\nthings that are no more to be feared than those which\n\nchildren in the darkness dread and imagine will come to pass.\n\nThis terror, therefore, and these darknesses of the mind must\n\nbe dispelled not by the rays of the sun nor by the bright shafts of day,\n\nbut by the aspect and reason of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0007","text":"First I say that the spirit, which we often call the mind,\n\nin which the counsel and governance of life are situated,\n\nto be a part of a human being no less than hand and foot\n\nand eyes are parts of the whole living creature.\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nthat the sensation of the mind is not situated in any fixed part,\n\nbut is a certain vital condition of the body,\n\nthe harmony, as the Greeks call it, which makes us\n\nlive with sensation, though the mind is in no part;\n\nas when good health is often said to be\n\nof the body, and yet this is no part of the healthy person,\n\nso they place the sensation of the mind in no fixed part;\n\nwherein they seem to me to err greatly in different ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0008","text":"Thus often the body, which is plainly seen, is sick,\n\nwhile nevertheless we rejoice in another hidden part;\n\nand conversely it often happens in turn that the opposite is the case,\n\nwhen one miserable in mind rejoices in his whole body;\n\nin no other way than if, when the sick man's foot aches,\n\nhis head meanwhile happens to be in no pain.\n\nMoreover, when the limbs are given over to gentle sleep\n\nand the body lies outstretched, comely, without sensation,\n\nyet there is something else within us which at that time\n\nis stirred in many ways and receives into itself every\n\nmotion of joy and the empty cares of the heart.\n\nNow, that you may also be able to recognize that the soul is in the limbs\n\nand that the body is not wont to feel by harmony,\n\nat first it comes about that, when much of the body has been removed,\n\nyet often life lingers on for us in the limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0009","text":"And that same life, again, when a few bodies of heat\n\nhave scattered and the air has been sent forth outside through the mouth,\n\nat once abandons the veins and leaves the bones;\n\nso that from this you may know that not all bodies hold equal parts\n\nnor support well-being equally,\n\nbut rather that these, which are the seeds of wind and hot vapour,\n\nensure that life lingers in the limbs.\n\nTherefore there is vital heat and wind in the body itself,\n\nwhich deserts our limbs as we die.\n\nWherefore, since the nature of mind\n\nand soul has been discovered as, so to speak, a part of man, give back the name\n\nof harmony, handed down to musicians from lofty Helicon,\n\nwhether they themselves subsequently derived it from elsewhere and transferred it to that thing\n\nwhich at that time lacked its own proper name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0010","text":"Whatever it is, let them keep it: do you take in the rest of what is said.\n\nNow I say that mind and soul are held joined\n\ntogether and of themselves make up one nature,\n\nbut that, as it were, the head and ruling power in the whole body is\n\nthe deliberative faculty, which we call mind and intellect.\n\nAnd it clings fixed in the middle region of the breast.\n\nFor here dread and fear leap up; these same places are soothed by\n\njoys: here, therefore, are mind and intellect.\n\nthe rest of the soul, scattered throughout the whole body,\n\nobeys and is moved at the will and impulse of the mind.\n\nAnd this alone has understanding through itself and rejoices in itself,\n\nwhen neither thing nor body moves the soul as a whole.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0011","text":"And just as, when the head or eye is injured by an assailing pain\n\nin us, we are not tortured throughout the whole\n\nbody, so the mind itself is sometimes afflicted\n\nand flourishes in joy, while the remaining part of the soul\n\nthrough the limbs and members is stirred by no change;\n\nbut when the mind has been more violently stirred by fear,\n\nwe see that the whole soul shares its feeling through the limbs,\n\nand that then sweats and paleness arise over the whole\n\nbody, the tongue falters and the voice is choked,\n\nthe eyes grow dim, the ears ring, the limbs give way;\n\nfinally, we often see human beings collapse from terror of the mind,\n\nso that anyone may readily infer from this\n\nthat the soul is joined with the mind, and when it has been\n\nstruck by the mind, it then drives and strikes the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0012","text":"This same reasoning teaches that the nature of mind and soul\n\nis bodily; for since it seems to drive the limbs,\n\nto rouse the body from sleep and to alter the countenance\n\nand to govern and turn about the whole human being,\n\nnone of which, we see, can occur without touch,\n\nnor, furthermore, touch without body, must it not be admitted\n\nthat the mind and soul consist of bodily nature?\n\nMoreover, you see that the mind acts in like manner with the body and\n\nagrees with it as one in the body.\n\nIf the dreadful force of a weapon does not rob one of life,\n\ndriven inward with bones and sinews parted,\n\nyet there follows languor and a pleasant sinking to the ground,\n\nand upon the ground an agitation of mind that arises,\n\nand at times, as it were, an uncertain desire to rise.\n\nTherefore the nature of the mind must be bodily,\n\nsince it suffers from the blow and bodily weapons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0013","text":"Now what kind of body this mind is of, and whence\n\nit has been constituted, I shall proceed to set forth to you in my verses.\n\nFirst, I say that it is exceedingly subtle and consists, made up of\n\nexceedingly minute bodies. That this is so\n\nyou may infer from this, so that you may fully perceive the mind.\n\nNothing seems to be done by a swifter process\n\nthan when the mind itself proposes and begins to act;\n\ntherefore the mind bestirs itself more quickly than any thing\n\nwhose nature appears manifest before the eyes.\n\nBut that which is so mobile must consist of exceedingly round\n\nand exceedingly minute seeds,\n\nso that, struck by a slight impulse, they can be moved.\n\nFor water is moved and ripples from a very slight impulse,\n\nsince indeed it is made of rolling and small shapes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0014","text":"but, on the contrary, the nature of honey is more constant,\n\nand its liquid more sluggish, and its motion more lingering:\n\nfor the whole store of matter clings more closely together,\n\nno doubt because it consists not of bodies so smooth\n\nnor so subtle and round.\n\nFor a light, suspended breath can cause a heap of poppy-seed\n\nto flow apart for you from its lofty summit,\n\nbut, on the contrary, it cannot [cause] a mass of stones and ears of grain [to do so].\n\nTherefore, in proportion as bodies are smallest\n\nand lightest, so they enjoy mobility;\n\nbut, on the contrary, whatever things are found with greater weight\n\nand roughness, are the more stable for that reason.\n\nNow therefore, since the nature of the mind has been found\n\nto be exceedingly mobile, it must needs consist\n\nof small, light, and round bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0015","text":"This fact, once known to you, will in many matters, my good man,\n\nbe found useful and will prove serviceable.\n\nThis fact also discloses its nature,\n\nof how fine a texture it consists and how small a place\n\nit occupies, if it can be compacted together,\n\nbecause, as soon as the care-free repose of death has\n\ncome upon a man and the nature of mind and soul has departed,\n\nyou can discern nothing taken away from the whole body\n\nin appearance, nothing in weight: death leaves all things intact,\n\nlife apart from sensation and warm vapor.\n\ntherefore it must be that the whole soul is bound\n\nby exceedingly small seeds throughout veins, entrails, and sinews,\n\nto such an extent that, when it has now wholly departed from the entire body,\n\nthe outer contour of the limbs nevertheless presents itself\n\nunimpaired, nor is a jot of weight lacking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0016","text":"Such is the case when the bloom of Bacchus has vanished, or when\n\nthe sweet breath of perfume has fled away into the air,\n\nor when juice has now departed from some body;\n\nyet the thing itself seems no smaller to the eyes,\n\nand for that reason nothing whatever has been subtracted from its weight,\n\nbecause, indeed, many tiny seeds produce\n\nthe juices and odor throughout the whole body of things.\n\nwherefore again and again one may know that the nature of mind and soul\n\nhas been created from exceedingly few seeds,\n\nsince in fleeing it carries away no weight.\n\nYet we must not suppose that this nature is simple.\n\nFor a certain subtle breath, mingled with vapor, leaves the dying;\n\nand vapor in turn draws air along with itself;\n\nnor is there any heat to which air too is not mingled;\n\nbecause its nature is rare, it must be that\n\nmany first-beginnings of air move among it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0017","text":"Thus a threefold nature of the spirit has now been discovered;\n\nyet all these are not sufficient to create sensation.\n\nsince the mind admits that it can create none of these things,\n\nthe sentient motions which, in short, it turns over in the mind.\n\nTherefore a certain fourth nature too must necessarily\n\nbe assigned to these; it is altogether without a name;\n\nthan which nothing exists more mobile or more subtle,\n\nnor made more of small and light elements;\n\nit first distributes sentient motions through the limbs.\n\nFor it is first set in motion, being composed of tiny shapes;\n\nthen heat receives motion, and the blind power of wind,\n\nthen air, then all things are set in motion:\n\nthe blood is shaken, then all the viscera perceive the sensation;\n\nlast of all it is given to the bones and marrow,\n\nwhether it be pleasure or a contrary burning pain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0018","text":"Nor can pain penetrate hither without cause, nor can sharp\n\nevil pervade, without all things being disturbed\n\nto such an extent that room for life is lacking and the parts of the soul\n\nscatter abroad through all the passages of the body.\n\nBut for the most part a limit to the motions occurs, as it were, on the surface of the body:\n\nfor this reason we are able to retain life.\n\nNow, desiring to give an account in what way these things, mixed among themselves and in what\n\narrangement, flourish,\n\nthe poverty of my native speech draws me back against my will;\n\nyet, as I shall be able, I shall touch upon it briefly.\n\nFor the first-beginnings of the principles run among one another\n\nin their motions among themselves, so that no one thing\n\ncan be separated, nor can a divided power come to be through space,\n\nbut they exist as though the many forces were of one body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0019","text":"Of this sort, in any living creature's flesh, commonly\n\nthere are smell and a certain color and taste, and yet from all these\n\nthere is one complete growth of body;\n\nthus heat and air and the blind power of wind\n\nmingled create one nature, and that mobile\n\nforce which, having begun motion from itself, imparts it to them,\n\nwhence feeling first arises, motion through the limbs.\n\nFor this nature lies hidden deep within and underlies them;\n\nnor is there anything in our body further beneath than this,\n\nand it is itself, moreover, the soul of the whole soul.\n\nAs in our limbs and throughout the whole body\n\nthe hidden force of mind and the power of soul are mingled,\n\nbecause they have been created from small and few bodies,\n\nso this force, devoid of a name, made from minute\n\nbodies, lies hidden and is, as it were, itself the soul\n\nof the whole soul, and rules the whole body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0020","text":"By a like reasoning it is necessary that wind and air\n\nand heat should be strong, mingled among themselves through the limbs,\n\nand that one thing should underlie another more, and stand out more,\n\nso that one something may seem to be made from all of them,\n\nlest heat and wind, and each separate power,\n\ndestroy the sensation of air and dissolve it when drawn apart.\n\nThere is also that heat in the mind which it takes on in anger\n\nwhen it grows hot and the blaze flashes more fiercely from the eyes;\n\nthere is also much cold air, companion of fear,\n\nwhich rouses shuddering in the limbs and stirs the body;\n\nthere is likewise that air of a calm condition,\n\nwhich comes about with a tranquil breast and a serene countenance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0021","text":"But there is more of the hot in those whose hearts are fierce\n\nand whose angry mind easily boils up in wrath,\n\nof which kind pre-eminently is the violent force of lions,\n\nwho for the most part burst their breasts with roaring groans\n\nand cannot contain the waves of anger in their breast.\n\nBut the cold mind of deer is more windy,\n\nand more quickly stirs icy airs through their entrails,\n\nwhich make a trembling motion arise in their limbs.\n\nBut the nature of oxen lives by a calmer air\n\nand never is too much stirred by the smoky torch of anger,\n\npouring in the shadow of blind darkness,\n\nnor does it grow numb, pierced by the icy darts of fear;\n\nit is situated between the two, deer and savage lions.\n\nSo it is with the race of men: although training may equally refine\n\nsome, nevertheless it leaves behind those first traces\n\nof the nature of each one's mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0022","text":"Nor must it be thought that evils can be plucked out by the roots,\n\nor why this man should run down more readily into sharp anger,\n\nthat one be assailed somewhat more quickly by fear, while that other\n\nthird should take certain things more mildly than is fair.\n\nAnd in many other matters it must needs be that there differ\n\nthe diverse natures of men and the habits that follow upon them;\n\nthe hidden causes of which I am now unable to set forth,\n\nnor to discover names for so many shapes as there are\n\namong the first-beginnings from which this variety of things arises.\n\nThis I see can be maintained concerning these matters:\n\nthat traces of natures are left so slight\n\nthat reason cannot drive them out of us,\n\nas to hinder nothing from living a life worthy of the gods.\n\nTherefore this nature is held fast by the whole body,\n\nand is itself the body's guardian and cause of preservation;\n\nfor they cling together by roots shared between them,\n\nnor do they seem able to be torn apart without destruction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0023","text":"Just as it is not easy to tear scent from lumps of frankincense\n\nwithout its nature also perishing,\n\nso it is not easy to draw the nature of mind and soul from the whole body\n\nwithout all things being dissolved.\n\nThus, from their first origin, with first-beginnings intertwined,\n\nthey come to be endowed with a life shared in common,\n\nnor does the power of each seem able, without the force of the other,\n\nto possess sensation separately, that of body and that of mind.\n\nbut by motions common to both, kindled on either side,\n\nsensation is kindled within our entrails.\n\nMoreover, the body is never generated by itself,\n\nnor does it grow, nor does it seem to endure after death.\n\nFor it is not as water's moisture often gives off the vapour\n\nwhich has been imparted to it, nor for that reason is itself torn apart,\n\nbut remains unharmed: not so, I say, can the parts of the soul,\n\nwhen left behind, endure separation from the limbs,\n\nbut, violently wrenched away, perish utterly and rot together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0024","text":"Thus from their earliest beginning, body and soul\n\nlearn the mutually shared contagions of vital motion,\n\neven while placed back in the mother's limbs and womb,\n\nso that their separation cannot occur without destructive bane and evil;\n\nso that you may see that, since the cause of their preservation is joined,\n\ntheir nature too is joined in its subsistence.\n\nFor the rest, if anyone denies that the body feels\n\nand believes that the soul, intermingled with the whole body,\n\nundergoes this motion which we call sensation,\n\nhe opposes manifest and true facts.\n\nFor who will ever set forth what it is for the body to feel,\n\nif not that which the thing itself openly has given and taught us?\n\n“But when the soul has departed, the body is altogether without sensation.”\n\nFor in life it loses what was not its own,\n\nand loses many other things besides when it is driven out of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0025","text":"Moreover, to say that the eyes are unable to discern anything,\n\nbut that through them the mind looks out as through opened doors,\n\nis difficult, since their sensation leads us to the contrary;\n\nfor sensation draws and thrusts the gaze forward to the things themselves,\n\nespecially since we are often unable to see bright\n\nlights, because our eyes are hindered by lights.\n\nBut this does not happen with doors; for the gateways through which we ourselves see\n\ndo not, when opened, undergo any strain.\n\nFurthermore, if our eyes are in place of doors,\n\nthen it seems that, once the eyes have been removed, the mind ought all the more\n\nto discern things when the doorposts themselves have been taken away.\n\nIn these matters you can by no means adopt\n\nwhat the holy opinion of the man Democritus maintains:\n\nthat the first-beginnings of body and soul, each set beside the first-beginnings\n\nof the other in alternation, vary and bind together the limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0026","text":"For since the elements of the soul are much smaller\n\nthan those of which our body and viscera consist,\n\nthey also yield in number and are sparsely dispersed\n\nthrough the limbs, so that at most you can assert this:\n\nhow small first bodies, when cast in, can arouse in us\n\nsentient motions in the body—so great\n\nare the intervals maintained by the first beginnings of the soul.\n\nFor we do not at times feel dust clinging\n\nto the body, nor chalk struck upon the limbs by a star,\n\nnor a cloud by night, nor the slender threads of a spider,\n\ndo we perceive when they meet us as we go, when we are caught in them,\n\nnor [do we perceive] that a vine has fallen upon the head of the same [person] from above,\n\nnor a garment, nor birds’ feathers and the flying down,\n\nwhich, through excessive lightness, generally fall slowly,\n\nnor do we perceive the course of any creeping creature whatsoever,\n\nnor each separate trace of feet\n\nwhich gnats and other things set upon our body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0027","text":"so greatly must many things first be set in motion within us\n\nbefore the first-beginnings of the soul perceive that they have been shaken,\n\nthe seeds intermixed through the limbs with our bodies,\n\nand before, in these intervals, they can, striking,\n\nrun together, unite, and in turn spring apart.\n\nAnd the mind is more potent in holding fast the barriers of life\n\nand has greater mastery over life than the force of the soul.\n\nFor without mind and soul, no part of the soul can remain\n\nthrough the limbs for even a small portion of time;\n\nbut as a companion it readily follows, departs into the air,\n\nand leaves the cold limbs in death’s chill.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0028","text":"But he remains alive in whom mind and soul have remained,\n\nalthough he is mangled all around, with his limbs cut away;\n\nthe trunk, its soul taken away all around and its limbs removed,\n\nlives and takes in the life-giving airs of heaven;\n\nif not in every respect, yet with a great part of the soul.\n\nthough impaired, yet it lingers and clings to life;\n\nas, when the eye is mangled, if the pupil remains\n\nunharmed, the living power of seeing remains standing,\n\nprovided only that you do not destroy the whole orb of light\n\nand cut away around it, leaving the pupil alone;\n\nfor this too will not happen without their destruction.\n\nBut if that tiny middle part of the eye is eaten away,\n\nthe light dies forthwith and darkness follows,\n\nhowever unharmed and bright the rest of the orb may be.\n\nBy such a bond soul and mind are always held fast.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0029","text":"Now come, that you may be able to know that minds and souls are born\n\nwith living creatures and are mortal,\n\nI shall proceed to set forth verses long sought out\n\nand discovered by sweet labor, worthy of your life.\n\nDo you make both of them subject to one name,\n\nand when, for example, I go on to speak of the soul,\n\nteaching that it is mortal, believe that I speak of the mind also,\n\ninsofar as it is one thing, joined together among itself.\n\nFirst, since I have shown that it consists, thin as it is, of tiny\n\nbodies, and is made of elements much smaller\n\nthan the liquid moisture of water,\n\nor mist or smoke—for it far surpasses them in mobility\n\nand is moved more by a slight cause,\n\nindeed, since it is moved by images of smoke and mist;\n\nsuch as, when asleep in dreams, we behold altars","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0030","text":"exhaling vapor high aloft and bearing smoke;\n\nfor beyond doubt these images are produced for us at the same time.\n\nNow therefore, since you see that, when vessels are shattered on every side,\n\nmoisture flows away and liquid departs,\n\nand since mist and smoke depart into the air,\n\nbelieve that the soul too is dispersed and perishes much\n\nmore swiftly and quickly, being dissolved into its first bodies,\n\nonce it has withdrawn, removed from a man's limbs;\n\nsince indeed the body, which stood as though its vessel,\n\nwhen shattered by some cause, cannot contain it\n\nand made porous by blood being drawn from the veins,\n\nby what air would you believe that it can be contained,\n\nwhen it is more rarefied, less capable of being contained, than our body?\n\nMoreover, we perceive that the mind is begotten equally with the body and\n\ngrows equally with it and grows old equally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0031","text":"For just as children move about with a weak and tender\n\nbody, so a slender judgment of mind follows.\n\nThen, when age has grown up in vigorous strength,\n\ncounsel too is greater, and the power of the mind increased.\n\nAfterward, when the body has been shaken by the strong powers of age\n\nand the limbs have fallen with blunted powers,\n\nthe intellect falters, the tongue raves, the mind [raves];\n\nall things fail and are wanting at one and the same time.\n\ntherefore it is fitting that the whole nature of the soul also be dissolved\n\nlike smoke, into the lofty breezes of the air;\n\nsince indeed we see it likewise come to birth and likewise\n\ngrow and, as I have shown, at the same time, worn out by age, decay.\n\nTo this is added that we see, just as the body itself\n\ntakes on dreadful diseases and harsh pain,\n\nso the mind takes on sharp cares, grief, and fear;\n\nwherefore it is fitting that it too be a sharer in death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0032","text":"Nay more, in bodily diseases the mind often wanders astray;\n\nfor it raves and speaks deliriously,\n\nand at times is borne into the deep by heavy lethargy,\n\ninto eternal sleep, with eyes and nodding head sinking;\n\nwhence it neither hears voices nor is able to recognize the faces\n\nof those who, calling it back to life,\n\nstand around, wetting cheeks and face with tears.\n\nwherefore you must confess that the mind also is dissolved,\n\nsince the infections of disease penetrate into it;\n\nfor pain and disease are each the maker of death,\n\nas we have learned before through the destruction of many.\n\nLastly, when the potent force of wine has penetrated a man\n\nand its diffused burning has spread into his veins,\n\nheaviness of the limbs follows; his legs are hampered as he staggers,\n\nhis tongue grows slow, his mind is sodden,\n\nhis eyes swim; shouting, hiccups, and quarrels grow apace,\n\nand now all the other things of this kind, whatever follow,\n\nwhy are they so, unless because the vehement violence of wine\n\nis wont to throw the mind into confusion in the very body?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0033","text":"But whatever things can be thrown into confusion and impeded\n\nindicate that, if a somewhat harsher cause has insinuated itself,\n\nthey will perish, deprived of future age.\n\nNay indeed, often someone, suddenly compelled by the force of disease,\n\nbefore our eyes, as by a thunderbolt's stroke,\n\nfalls down and foams, groans and trembles in his limbs,\n\nraves, stretches out his sinews, is twisted, gasps\n\nirregularly, and in tossing about wears out his limbs,\n\nclearly because the force of disease, dispersed through the limbs,\n\ndrives and disorders the mind, as in the salty sea\n\nthe waves boil under the mighty forces of winds.\n\nMoreover, a groan is forced out because the limbs\n\nare affected by pain, and altogether because the seeds of voice\n\nare drawn forth and, gathered together, are borne outward through the mouth\n\nby the way by which, as it were, they have grown accustomed and been provided with a passage.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0034","text":"Madness comes about because the force of mind and soul\n\nis disturbed and, as I have taught, divided apart,\n\nis scattered, torn asunder by that same poison.\n\nThen, when now the cause of the disease has recoiled and returns\n\ninto the recesses, the sharp humour of the corrupted body,\n\nthen, as though staggering, he first rises, and all\n\nlittle by little returns to the senses and recovers the soul.\n\nTherefore, when these things amid such great diseases in the body itself\n\nare tossed about and, racked in miserable ways, suffer,\n\nwhy do you believe that the same things, without the body, in the open air,\n\ncan pass their lifetime amid strong winds?\n\nAnd since we perceive that the mind is healed, like a sick body,\n\nand we see that it can be altered by medicine,\n\nthis too foretells that the mind lives mortally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0035","text":"For it is right either to add parts or to transpose them in order,\n\nor to take away from the whole some tiny jot,\n\nfor whoever sets about and begins to change the mind,\n\nor seeks to alter any other nature whatsoever.\n\nBut that which is immortal does not wish parts to be transferred to itself or bestowed upon it,\n\nnor for anything at all to flow away from it, even by a tiny jot;\n\nfor whatever, changed, passes beyond its own boundaries,\n\nis at once the death of that which it was before.\n\nTherefore, whether the mind falls sick, it gives forth mortal signs,\n\nas I have taught, or whether it is altered by medicine.\n\nSo greatly does the true thing seem to confront false reasoning\n\nand to block escape for one proceeding,\n\nand, by a two-edged refutation, to convict falsehood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0036","text":"Finally, we often see a man gradually passing away\n\nand losing life-sustaining sensation limb by limb;\n\nfirst, in his feet, the toes and nails growing blue.\n\nthen the feet and legs die, thereafter from there through the limbs\n\nthe traces of chill death pass gradually to the rest.\n\nAnd since this nature of the soul is divided and does not remain whole at one\n\ntime, it must be held to be mortal.\n\nBut if perhaps you think that it can draw itself inward through the limbs\n\nand gather its parts into one\n\nand thereby withdraw sensation from all the members,\n\nyet that place into which so great an abundance of soul\n\nis gathered ought to be seen to possess greater sensation;\n\nand since it is nowhere, assuredly, as we have said,\n\nbeing torn apart, it is scattered abroad and therefore perishes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0037","text":"Nay even if now it pleased you to concede what is false\n\nand grant that the soul can be massed together in the body of those\n\nwho, dying little by little, leave the light,\n\nnevertheless you must admit that the soul is mortal,\n\nnor does it matter whether it perishes dispersed through the air\n\nor, contracted from its own parts, grows torpid,\n\nsince sensation fails the whole man more and more on every side\n\nand less and less of life remains on every side.\n\nAnd since the mind is one part of a human being and remains fixed in a definite\n\nplace, just as the ears and eyes are\n\nand the other senses that govern life together,\n\nand just as hand and eye or nostrils, separately\n\nsevered from us, can neither feel nor exist,\n\nbut nevertheless they are left behind in so brief a time of this kind,\n\nthus the mind cannot by itself exist without the body and the man himself,\n\nwhich seems to be, as it were, its vessel,\n\nor whatever else you prefer to imagine as more closely joined to it,\n\nsince the body clings to it in connection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0038","text":"Finally, the living power of body and mind,\n\njoined together, are strong among themselves and enjoy life;\n\nfor without the body the nature of the mind alone cannot produce\n\nvital motions by itself, nor, on the other hand,\n\ncan the body, bereft of soul, endure and make use of the senses.\n\nClearly, just as an eye torn from its roots cannot\n\nitself discern any thing apart from the whole body,\n\nso soul and mind appear able to do nothing by themselves.\n\nNo doubt because, mingled through veins and viscera,\n\nthey are held through nerves and bones by the whole body,\n\nand their first-beginnings cannot, with great intervals,\n\nspring apart freely; therefore, confined, they produce\n\nsense-bearing motions, which, when cast out beyond the body into the breezes\n\nof air after death, they cannot produce,\n\nbecause they are not held together in a like manner;\n\nfor the air will be both body and living, if it can restrain\n\nitself, the soul, and confine itself into those motions\n\nwhich it formerly carried on in the nerves and in the body itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0039","text":"wherefore again and again, when the whole body has been dissolved\n\nfrom its covering and with the vital airs cast out beyond,\n\nyou must confess that the senses of the mind are dissolved\n\nand the soul, since the cause is conjoined for the two.\n\nFinally, since the body cannot endure the soul's\n\nseverance, without wasting away in a foul stench,\n\nwhy do you doubt that, having arisen from the bottom and utterly,\n\nthe force of the soul has flowed out, diffused like smoke,\n\nand that therefore, altered by so great a ruin,\n\nthe body has fallen apart, because its foundations have been moved wholly from their place\n\nand stream outward—the foundations of the soul—through the limbs\n\nand through all the bendings of the passages that are in the body,\n\nand the openings? So that in many ways you may be able to know\n\nthat the divided nature of the soul has gone forth through the limbs\n\nand has first been torn apart within the body itself,\n\nbefore, slipping forth, it swam out into the breezes of air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0040","text":"Indeed, even while the end of life is being turned within,\n\nthe soul nevertheless often seems, shaken by some cause,\n\nto depart and be dissolved from the whole body,\n\nand the face, as if at the final moment, to grow languid,\n\nand all the limbs to fall slack and bloodless.\n\nSuch is the case when one is said to have been stricken in spirit\n\nor to have left one's soul; when now there is panic, and all\n\ndesire to seize hold of life's final bond;\n\nfor then the mind and the power of the soul are shaken.\n\nwhole. And these collapse together with the body itself,\n\nso that a somewhat weightier cause can dissolve them.\n\nWhy, then, do you doubt that, brought forth outside the body,\n\nfeeble, out into the open, its covering removed,\n\nit can not only not endure through all eternity,\n\nbut cannot even stand firm for any, however brief, time?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0041","text":"For no one, as he dies, seems to himself to feel\n\nthat his soul goes forth unharmed from his whole body,\n\nnor that it first approaches the throat and the upper jaws;\n\nrather, it fails, situated in a definite region;\n\njust as he knows that each of the other senses is dissolved\n\nin its own part. But if our mind were immortal,\n\nin dying it would not complain so much that it is being dissolved,\n\nbut rather that it is going forth and leaving its garment behind, like a snake.\n\nFinally, why is the mind and counsel of the spirit never\n\nproduced in the head or feet or hands, but clings\n\nin one seat and in definite regions in all men,\n\nunless definite places have been assigned to each for being born,\n\nand places where whatever is created can endure,\n\nand thus be in limbs divided in many ways,\n\nso that the order of the members may never be inverted?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0042","text":"So strictly does thing follow thing: nor is flame accustomed to be created\n\nin rivers, nor cold to be born in fire.\n\nMoreover, if the nature of the soul is immortal\n\nand can perceive things hidden from our body,\n\nit must, I think, be made endowed with five senses.\n\nnor by any other reasoning can we set before ourselves\n\nsouls wandering in the lower world by Acheron.\n\nThus painters and the generations of earlier writers\n\nhave introduced souls endowed with senses.\n\nBut neither eyes apart, nor nostrils, nor the hand itself\n\ncan belong to the soul, nor separately tongue or ears;\n\ntherefore they cannot perceive or exist by themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0043","text":"And since we perceive that vital sensation is present throughout the whole body\n\nand see that the whole is animate,\n\nif suddenly some force should cut it through the middle with a swift blow,\n\nso as to separate each part apart,\n\nthe force of the soul too, beyond doubt divided,\n\nwill be cut apart and rent at the same time as the body.\n\nBut what is cut and departs into any parts,\n\nplainly denies that it has an eternal nature.\n\nThey tell how scythe-bearing chariots often cut off limbs\n\nso suddenly amid the mingled warm slaughter,\n\nthat what has fallen, severed from the limbs, may be seen to tremble on the ground,\n\nwhile nevertheless the mind and force of the man\n\ncannot, because of the swiftness of the injury, feel the pain;\n\nand at the same time, since the mind is given over to the ardor of battle,\n\nwith the rest of the body it seeks battle and slaughter,\n\nnor is he often aware that his left hand, together with its shield, has been lost\n\namong the horses, torn away by the wheels and ravening scythes,\n\nnor does another notice that his right hand has fallen off, as he climbs and presses on.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0044","text":"Then another tries to rise with his leg taken away,\n\nwhile nearby on the ground his dying foot moves its toes.\n\nAnd a severed head from a warm and living trunk\n\nkeeps on the ground its vital expression and its open eyes,\n\nuntil it has yielded up all the remnants of the soul.\n\nIndeed, if you should wish, while the serpent's tail threatens with vibrating tongue,\n\nto cut with steel both the tail and the tall body\n\ninto many parts,\n\nyou would see all the parts separately, cut by the fresh\n\nwound, writhing and sprinkling the ground with gore,\n\nand each part seeking backwards with its mouth the part before it,\n\nso that, struck by the burning pain of the wound, it may press it with a bite.\n\nShall we say, then, that whole souls are in all those\n\nparticles? But by that reasoning it will follow\n\nthat one living creature has had many souls in its body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0045","text":"Therefore that which was one has been divided together with\n\nthe body; wherefore each must be thought mortal,\n\nsince it is likewise cut into many parts.\n\nMoreover, if the nature of the soul is immortal\n\nand insinuates itself into bodies as they are born,\n\nwhy are we unable to remember the age lived before?\n\nnor do we retain any traces of things done?\n\nFor if the altered power of the mind is of such great force,\n\nthat all retention of things done has fallen away,\n\nthen, I think, it does not wander far from death already;\n\nwherefore you must confess that that which existed before\n\nhas perished, and that what now exists has now been created.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0046","text":"Moreover, if, when the body has already been completed, the living power of the mind is wont to be brought into us\n\nwhen the body has already been completed,\n\nthen when we are born and when we enter upon the threshold of life,\n\nit would not be fitting that it should seem to grow together with the body and at the same time\n\nwith the limbs, in the very blood,\n\nbut rather, as in a cage, to live by itself and alone;\n\nyet it is fitting that the whole body should abound in sensation.\n\nWherefore again and again souls must not be thought to be without origin\n\nnor released from the law of death;\n\nfor neither must they be thought able to have been attached so closely\n\nto our bodies, insinuated from without,\n\nsince the manifest fact teaches that this is altogether contrary to what happens:\n\nfor it is so connected through veins, entrails, and sinews,\n\nand bones, that even the teeth too partake of sensation;","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0047","text":"as disease shows, and the constriction of icy water,\n\nand the harsh stone, pressed out from sudden fruits;\n\nnor, since they are so interwoven, do they seem able to depart\n\nunharmed and to disentangle themselves safe and sound.\n\nfrom all the sinews and bones and joints,\n\nbut if perhaps you think that, insinuated from without,\n\nthe soul is wont to permeate through our limbs,\n\nby so much the more, diffused with the body, it will perish;\n\nfor what permeates is dissolved, and therefore perishes;\n\nfor it is distributed through all the passages of the body.\n\nas food, when it is distributed into all the limbs and members,\n\nperishes and from itself supplies another nature,\n\nso soul and mind, although fresh and whole,\n\nenter the body, yet in permeating they are dissolved,\n\nwhile, as it were, through all the passages they are distributed into the limbs—\n\nthe particles by which this nature of mind is created,\n\nwhich now, having arisen, rules in our body,\n\ndivided from that which then perished through the limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0048","text":"Wherefore the nature of the soul plainly seems neither to be deprived of\n\na day of birth nor exempt from death.\n\nMoreover, are seeds of the soul left or not\n\nin the lifeless body? If they are left and remain within,\n\nit will not rightly be able to be held immortal,\n\nsince, with parts lost, it has departed diminished.\n\nBut if, taken away, it has fled forth with its limbs so intact\n\nas to have left no parts of itself in the body,\n\nwhence do worms, in corpses with their flesh now putrefying,\n\ncome forth, and whence so great a multitude of living creatures\n\nor, bloodless and drained of blood, does it surge through the swollen limbs?\n\nBut if perhaps you believe that souls are insinuated from without\n\ninto worms and can come into their separate bodies,\n\nand do not reflect why many thousands of souls\n\nshould gather where one has departed, yet this would seem\n\nto require inquiry and to be brought into question:\n\nwhether at last the souls hunt out the seeds of each\n\nlittle worm and themselves fashion them where they are,\n\nor are, as it were, insinuated into bodies already complete.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0049","text":"But it is not possible to say why they themselves act, or why they toil;\n\nfor, when they are without a body, they do not\n\nflit about troubled by diseases and cold and hunger;\n\nfor the body suffers more from these defects,\n\nand the mind performs many evils through contagion with it.\n\nYet grant that, when they enter, it is however useful for them to make a body;\n\nbut no way by which they can do so is apparent.\n\nTherefore souls do not make bodies and limbs for themselves.\n\nNor yet is it possible that they are insinuated into bodies\n\nalready complete; for they will not be able to be subtly\n\nconnected, nor will contacts occur by mutual accord.\n\nFinally, why does the fierce violence of grim lions\n\nfollow their breed, cunning the fox, and flight the deer?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0050","text":"It is given from their fathers, and hereditary fear stirs their limbs,\n\nand now why besides do all other things of this kind in their limbs\n\nare generated from the beginning of life, and in disposition,\n\nif not, because by a fixed seed and seminal power of its own\n\nthe force of mind likewise grows together with the body?\n\nBut if it were immortal and accustomed to change\n\nbodies, living creatures would be of intermingled natures;\n\na dog of Hyrcanian breed would often flee\n\nthe attack of a horned stag, and would tremble through the breezes;\n\na hawk, fleeing through the air, at the approach of a dove;\n\nmen would be mad, the wild generations of beasts would be wise.\n\nFor this is advanced by false reasoning, which they say:\n\nthat an immortal soul is altered when the body is changed;\n\nfor whatever is changed is dissolved, and therefore perishes;\n\nfor its parts are shifted and migrate from their order;\n\nwherefore they must also be able to be dissolved throughout the limbs,\n\nand finally all perish together with the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0051","text":"if they say that the souls of human beings always\n\nenter into human bodies, nevertheless I shall ask why from a wise man\n\na foolish one can come to be, nor is any boy prudent,\n\nnor is the foal of a mare as learned as the strength of a vigorous horse.\n\nThey will no doubt have recourse to the notion that the mind grows tender in a tender body.\n\nBut if this happens, you must admit\n\nthat the soul is mortal, since, altered throughout the limbs\n\nby so great a change, it loses its former life and sense.\n\nOr in what way will the power of mind, likewise strengthened together with the body,\n\nbe able to attain the desired flower of age,\n\nunless it shall be a partner from the first origin?\n\nOr what does it mean by wishing to go forth from aged limbs?\n\nDoes it fear to remain enclosed in a rotting body,\n\nand lest its house, wearied by the space of old age,\n\ncollapse upon it?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0052","text":"But there are no dangers at all for an immortal being.\n\nFinally, that souls should be at hand for the unions of Venus and the births of beasts\n\nseems ridiculous:\n\nthat immortals should await mortal limbs,\n\ncontend with one another in countless number,\n\nand in excessive haste, as to which shall first and most preferably make its way in;\n\nunless perhaps the compacts of souls are so agreed,\n\nthat whichever, flying, arrives first shall enter\n\nfirst, and they shall not contend at all with one another in strength.\n\nFinally, in the sky there cannot be a tree, nor clouds in the deep sea, nor can fishes live in fields,\n\nnor blood be in wood, nor sap reside in stones.\n\nIt is fixed and ordered where each thing may grow and exist.\n\nThus the nature of mind cannot arise alone without a body,\n\nnor exist at any distance from sinews and blood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0053","text":"For if it could, the very force of mind much sooner\n\ncould be in the head or shoulders or the soles of the feet,\n\nand, being born in any part whatsoever, would be accustomed\n\nnevertheless to remain in the same man and in the same vessel.\n\nSince it is also established that our body has a definite...\n\n...and seems so arranged that there it can be and grow...\n\n...the more must it be denied that soul and mind separately...\n\n...can endure and be generated wholly outside the body.\n\nWherefore, when the body has perished, you must confess...\n\n...that the soul too has perished, scattered throughout the whole body.\n\nFor indeed, to join the mortal with the eternal and to suppose that they...\n\n...are in harmony as one and can perform mutual offices...\n\n...is madness. For what is to be thought more different...\n\n...or more sundered and discordant among themselves...\n\n...than that which is mortal, joined with the immortal and everlasting...\n\n...should endure savage storms in an alliance?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0054","text":"Moreover, whatever things necessarily remain eternal...\n\n...either, because they are things with solid body, repel blows...\n\n...and suffer nothing to penetrate them that can...\n\n...disunite their close-packed inner parts, as the bodies...\n\n...of matter do, whose nature we showed before;\n\n...or are able for this reason to endure through all time,\n\n...because they are exempt from blows, as is the void,\n\n...which remains untouched and is not affected in the least by a blow;\n\n...or else because there is no available space around them...\n\n...into which things might, as it were, depart and be dissolved,\n\n...as the sum of sums is eternal; nor is there any place outside...\n\n...to which they might flee away, nor are there bodies which...\n\nmay fall upon it and dissolve it with a mighty blow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0055","text":"But if perhaps for this reason it must the more be held immortal,\n\nbecause it is kept fortified against things vital,\n\nor because things alien to its safety do not come at all,\n\nor because those which come withdraw in some way\n\nhaving first been driven away before we can perceive what harm they do,\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nfor, besides the fact that it is sick along with the body's diseases,\n\nthere comes that which often, concerning things to come,\n\ntorments it and keeps it ill in fear and wearies it with cares,\n\nand the sins of things ill done in the past gnaw at it.\n\nAdd the madness proper to the mind and forgetfulness of things,\n\nadd that it is plunged into the black waves of lethargy.\n\nTherefore death is nothing to us and does not concern us a whit,\n\nsince the nature of the mind is held to be mortal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0056","text":"And even as in the time gone by we felt no pain,\n\nwhen the Carthaginians came from every side to contend,\n\nwhen all things, shaken by the tremulous tumult of war,\n\ntrembled horribly beneath the high regions of heaven,\n\nand it was uncertain under whose rule it must fall\n\nfor all mankind on land and sea,\n\nso, when we shall no longer be, when the separation of body and soul\n\nshall have occurred, of which we are composed in united conjunction,\n\nassuredly nothing at all will be able to happen to us, who then shall not be,\n\ncan at all occur and move sensation,\n\nnot even if earth were mingled with sea and sea with sky.\n\nAnd if it now has sensation from our body after\n\nthe nature of the mind and the power of the soul have been torn apart,\n\nyet it is nothing to us, who by the combination and union\n\nof body and soul consist, fitted together as one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0057","text":"Nor, if time should gather together our matter\n\nafter death and bring it back again as it is now placed,\n\nand the lights of life should again be given to us,\n\nwould that event too concern us at all,\n\nonce the continuity of ourselves has been interrupted.\n\nAnd now nothing concerning ourselves pertains to us, before\n\nwe were; nor does any anguish affect us concerning those former selves.\n\nFor when you look back upon the whole\n\npast expanse of immeasurable time, and upon how manifold the motions of matter are,\n\nyou may easily believe this:\n\nthat the seeds have often before been set in the same order\n\nas now, these same seeds of which we now are composed.\n\nYet we cannot grasp this again in memory;\n\nfor a pause of life has been interposed, and all the motions have wandered\n\nfar and wide, away from the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0058","text":"For, if misery and grief are perhaps to befall one,\n\nthere must also then be a person himself to whom ill can\n\nhappen. Since death removes this person and prevents him from existing\n\nhim to whom misfortunes can be joined,\n\nwe may know that there is nothing to be feared in death,\n\nand that one who does not exist cannot become miserable, nor does it differ a jot\n\nwhether he has now been born at no time,\n\nwhen immortal death has taken away mortal life.\n\nAccordingly, when you see a man himself grow indignant,\n\nthat after death he will either rot, his body having been laid aside,\n\nor be destroyed by flames or the jaws of wild beasts,\n\nyou may know that he does not ring true, and that beneath there lies\n\nsome blind sting in his heart, although he himself denies\n\nthat he believes any sensation will be his in death;\n\nfor, I think, he does not grant what he promises, nor the point from which [he speaks],\n\nnor does he utterly remove and cast himself out of life,\n\nbut unwittingly makes some part of himself remain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0059","text":"For, while alive, when each man sets before himself what will be,\n\nthat birds and wild beasts will tear his body in death,\n\nhe pities himself; for he does not separate himself from it,\n\nnor remove himself sufficiently from the cast-off body, and he\n\nimagines that body to be himself and, standing by, contaminates it with his own sensation.\n\nHence he resents that he was created mortal\n\nand does not see that in true death there will be no other self,\n\nwho, alive, can mourn himself as slain\n\nand, standing, grieve that he lying there is torn apart or burned.\n\nFor if in death it is an evil thing, the jaws and bite of wild beasts\n\nto be handled, I find no one for whom it is not bitter;\n\nto be placed upon hot fires and scorched by flames,\n\nor, placed in honey, to be suffocated and stiffen\n\nwith cold, when he lies upon the upper surface of an icy rock,\n\nor to be crushed, buried beneath the weight of earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0060","text":"“Now, now, no glad home will receive you, nor your\n\nexcellent wife, nor will your sweet children run to meet you\n\nto snatch kisses first and touch your breast with silent sweetness.\n\nYou will not be able to be a protection to your own flourishing deeds.\n\n‘Wretch, wretchedly,’ they say, ‘one hostile day has taken away\n\nfrom you all these many prizes of life.’\n\nIn these matters they do not add this: ‘nor does there now\n\nbesides sit upon you any longing for these things.’\n\nIf they clearly perceived this in their minds and followed it in their words,\n\nthey would release themselves from great anguish and fear of mind.\n\n‘You indeed, as you are sunk in death, so will you be for all\n\nthe time that remains, deprived of every painful suffering;\n\nbut we, beside the dreadful pyre, with you turned to ash,\n\nhave wept insatiably, and forever\n\nno day will remove grief from our breast.’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0061","text":"This, therefore, must be asked of him: what is so bitter\n\nif the matter returns to sleep and rest,\n\nthat anyone can waste away in eternal mourning.\n\nThis too they do when they have reclined and hold\n\nMen often crown their cups and shade their faces with garlands,\n\nso that they may say from the heart: “Brief is this enjoyment for little men;\n\nsoon it will have been, nor thereafter will it ever be permitted to recall it.”\n\njust as though this were foremost among their evils in death,\n\nthat thirst burns up the wretched and parching heat scorches them,\n\nor that desire for some other thing settles upon them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0062","text":"For then no one will seek himself and his life,\n\nwhen mind and body alike rest in sleep;\n\nfor sleep may thus be eternal for us,\n\nand yet no longing for ourselves affects us,\n\nand nevertheless at that time the first-beginnings do not at all wander through our limbs\n\nfar away from their sense-bearing motions,\n\nwhen a man, seized from sleep, gathers himself together.\n\nTherefore death must be thought to be much less to us,\n\nif anything can be less than what we see to be nothing;\n\nfor a greater scattering of the mass of matter\n\nfollows upon death, nor does anyone stand forth awakened,\n\nwhom once the cold pause of life has followed.\n\nFinally, if the nature of things should suddenly send forth a voice","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0063","text":"and herself thus rebuke some one of us:\n\n“What need is so great for you, mortal, that you indulge too much\n\nin grievous lamentations? Why do you groan and weep over death?\n\nFor if the life previously lived and gone before was pleasing to you,\n\nand not all things heaped together, as if into a perforated vessel\n\nthe advantages flowed past and perished unacknowledged;\n\nwhy do you not depart like a guest filled with life\n\nand, foolish man, take secure rest with an even mind?\n\nBut if those things which, whatever their fruits, you have squandered have perished,\n\nand life is an offense to you, why do you seek to add more,\n\nagain something that may perish badly and all die ungratefully,\n\nrather than make an end of life and toil?\n\nFor besides, what I might contrive and discover for you\n\nthat would please, there is nothing; all things are always the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0064","text":"Even if your body were not now decaying with years and your limbs\n\nworn out and languishing, yet all things remain the same,\n\neven if by living you should go on to conquer all ages,\n\nand even more so, if you should never be going to die—\n\nwhat do we reply, except to bring a just charge against\n\nnature and set forth the true case in words?\n\nBut if one greater in age and now an old man should complain\n\nand, wretched, lament his death more than is fair,\n\nwould nature not deservedly cry out all the more and rebuke him in a sharp voice:\n\n‘Away with tears from here, you wretch, and restrain your complaints.\n\nHaving gone through all the prizes of life, you wither;\n\nbut because you always crave what is absent and despise what is present,\n\nyour life has slipped away incomplete and ungrateful to you,\n\nand death stood by your head before you expected it,\n\nbefore, sated and full of things, you could depart.’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0065","text":"now cast off all that is alien to your age,\n\nwith an even mind; come now, yield to a great necessity?”\n\nrightly, I think, he would act, rightly rebuke and urge on;\n\nfor, driven out by the newness of things, old age yields\n\nalways, and one thing must be renewed from others.\n\nNor is anyone given over to the abyss or to gloomy Tartarus;\n\nthere is need of matter, that generations to come may grow;\n\nyet, when you have finished life, all these things will follow you;\n\nand no less than you, these things have fallen before and will fall.\n\nThus one thing will never cease to arise from another,\n\nand life is granted to no one as property, to all for use.\n\nConsider likewise how the antiquity of time gone by before us\n\nhas been nothing to us before we were born.\n\nTherefore nature sets forth this as a mirror for us of the future\n\ntime after at last our death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0066","text":"Does anything there appear horrible, does anything seem sad,\n\nthat does not stand out as more secure than every sleep?\n\nAnd indeed, no wonder: whatever things are said to exist in deep\n\nAcheron are all in life among us.\n\nNor does wretched Tantalus fear a huge rock hanging in the air,\n\nas the tale goes, benumbed by empty dread;\n\nbut rather in life an idle fear of the gods presses upon\n\nmortals, and they fear whatever disaster chance may bring to each.\n\nnor do birds assail Tityos as he lies in Acheron.\n\nnor because, searching with mighty breast, they can discover anything\n\nthat is of perpetual duration, assuredly.\n\nHowever vast he may stand in the projection of his body,\n\nthough he occupy not nine acres with his limbs alone, stretched apart,\n\nbut though he occupy the orb of the whole earth,\n\nhe will nevertheless not be able to endure eternal pain\n\nnor to furnish food from his own body forever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0067","text":"But Tityos for us is here: lying in love,\n\nhim birds tear apart, and anxious anguish devours;\n\nor cares rend him in some other desire whatsoever.\n\nSisyphus too is before our eyes in life,\n\nwho thirsts to seek from the people the fasces and savage axes\n\nand always, defeated and sorrowful, withdraws.\n\nFor to seek power, which is empty and is never granted,\n\nand in its pursuit always to undergo harsh toil—\n\nthis is to push, straining, a stone up a mountain against its slope,\n\nwhich nevertheless, now from the very summit,\n\nrolls back and swiftly seeks the level plains of the flat field.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0068","text":"Then always to feed the ungrateful nature of the mind\n\nand to fill it with good things and never satisfy it—\n\nas the seasons of the years do for us, when in their cycle\n\nthey return and bring forth their fruits and varied delights—\n\nand yet we are never filled with the fruits of life:\n\nthis, I suppose, is to pluck maidens in the flower of their age\n\nbecause they tell how they heap pierced liquid into a vessel,\n\nwhich nevertheless can by no means be filled.\n\nCerberus too, and the Furies, and indeed the want of light,\n\nTartarus belching forth dreadful heat from its jaws!\n\nwhich neither are anywhere nor certainly can be;\n\nbut in life there is fear of penalties for evil deeds,\n\nfor conspicuous crimes a conspicuous requital of crime,\n\nprison and the dreadful casting down from a rock,\n\nscourgings, executioners, the rack, pitch, metal plate, torches;\n\nand yet, even if these are absent, the mind conscious of its deeds,\n\nforeboding, applies goads to itself and sears itself with whips,\n\nnor meanwhile sees what limit of evils there can be,\n\nnor what finally may be the end of punishments,\n\nand fears, all the more, that these same things may become more grievous in death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0069","text":"Here, in short, the life of fools becomes Acherusian.\n\nThis too you yourself may sometimes say to yourself:\n\n‘Even good Ancus left the light to his eyes,\n\nwho was better than you, wicked man, in many respects.\n\nThen many other kings and potentates\n\ndied, who ruled over great nations.\n\nHe too himself, who once made a road across the great sea\n\nand gave his legions a passage to go over the deep\n\nand taught them to tread with their feet upon the salt pools\n\nand, riding over them on horseback, despised the murmurings of the sea,\n\nwith the light taken away, he breathed forth his soul from his dying body.\n\nThe Scipio-born, thunderbolt of war, terror of Carthage,\n\ngave his bones to earth just as though he were the lowliest slave.\n\nAdd the discoverers of doctrines and of charms,\n\nadd the companions of the Heliconian Muses; of whom Homer alone,\n\nhaving gained the same sceptre, rests in sleep with the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0070","text":"Finally, after ripe old age had\n\nwarned Democritus that the mindful motions of his mind were growing weak,\n\nhe himself of his own accord offered his head to death, confronting it.\n\nEpicurus himself dies, when the light of life has run its course,\n\nhe who surpassed the human race in intellect and all\n\neclipsed the stars, as the ethereal sun rising does.\n\nAnd will you hesitate and be indignant to die?\n\nYou, for whom life is almost dead though alive and seeing,\n\nwho waste the greater part of your age in sleep,\n\nand, awake, snore, nor cease to behold dreams,\n\nand bear a mind troubled by empty fear,\n\nnor can often discover what evil there is for you, when\n\nwretched, you are pressed, drunken, from every side by many cares\n\nand wander, drifting in the uncertain error of your mind.'","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0071","text":"If men could, just as they seem to perceive\n\nthat there is a weight in the mind which wearies it with its heaviness,\n\nalso know from what causes and whence this comes about,\n\nthat so great a mass, as it were, of evil stands in the breast,\n\nor live their lives in such a way as we now for the most part see,\n\nnot knowing what each wishes for himself and always seeking\n\nto change his place, as though he could lay down a burden.\n\nOften that man goes out from his great house,\n\nweary of being at home, and suddenly,\n\nsince he feels that outside is no better at all.\n\nHe rushes on, driving his nags headlong toward his country villa,\n\nas though urgently bringing aid to a house on fire;\n\nat once he yawns when he has touched the threshold of the villa,\n\nor, heavy with sleep, he goes off into slumber and seeks forgetfulness,\n\nor even hastens to the city and visits it again.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0072","text":"In this way each man flees himself; but him, of course, as happens,\n\nhe is unable to escape, he clings to unwillingly and hates\n\nfor this reason, because, being sick, he does not grasp the cause of his disease;\n\nif he saw it clearly, each man, abandoning everything else,\n\nwould first strive to know the nature of things,\n\nsince it is the condition of eternal time, not of a single hour,\n\nthat is at issue, the condition in which every age must be for mortals\n\nafter death, whatever remains in waiting.\n\nFinally, what great desire for life compels us to tremble so greatly amid doubtful dangers?\n\nwhat great desire for life compels us to tremble so greatly amid doubtful dangers?\n\nCertainly the end of life stands before mortals,\n\nnor can death be avoided, so that we do not meet it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0073","text":"Moreover, we are ever turning about in the same place and remain there.\n\nnor is any new pleasure hammered out by living;\n\nbut while what we desire is absent, it seems to surpass\n\nall else; afterward, when that has fallen to our lot, we desire another,\n\nand an equal thirst always holds us, gaping for life.\n\nand it is uncertain what fortune the following age will bring,\n\nor what chance may bear to us, or what end hangs over us.\n\nNor, by prolonging life, do we remove one jot\n\nfrom the time of death, nor are we able to take anything away from it,\n\nso that we may perhaps be able to exist for long when dead.\n\nAccordingly, though you may wish by living to heap up whatever ages,\n\nthat eternal death will nevertheless remain no whit the less;\n\nnor will he who already no longer exists be any less long gone, from today's\n\nlight, who has made an end of life, than he\n\nwho died many months and years before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBT:PR2291A2AD738E2FD6:P0073"]}],"source_digest":"515186e2747f8b239cafea636a1cb28a2c5fcf2ea0a5353a475e34a1208a9efd","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Liber Tertius"}],"passage_basis":"revised-passages","original_title":"Liber Tertius","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ","title":"Book Four","order":4,"chapter_id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ","text":"## Liber Quartus\n\n  \n\nAvia Pieridum peragro loca nullius ante\n\n \n\ntrita solo. iuvat integros accedere fontis\n\n \n\natque haurire, iuvatque novos decerpere flores\n\n \n\ninsignemque meo capiti petere inde coronam,\n\n \n\nunde prius nulli velarint tempora musae;\n\n \n\nprimum quod magnis doceo de rebus et artis\n\n \n\nreligionum animum nodis exsolvere pergo,\n\n \n\ndeinde quod obscura de re tam lucida pango\n\n \n\ncarmina musaeo contingens cuncta lepore.\n\n \n\nid quoque enim non ab nulla ratione videtur;\n\n \n\nnam vel uti pueris absinthia taetra medentes\n\n \n\ncum dare conantur, prius oras pocula circum\n\n \n\ncontingunt mellis dulci flavoque liquore,\n\n \n\nut puerorum aetas inprovida ludificetur\n\n \n\nlabrorum tenus, interea perpotet amarum\n\n \n\nabsinthi laticem deceptaque non capiatur,\n\n \n\nsed potius tali facto recreata valescat,\n\n \n\nsic ego nunc, quoniam haec ratio plerumque videtur\n\n \n\ntristior esse quibus non est tractata, retroque\n\n \n\nvolgus abhorret ab hac, volui tibi suaviloquenti\n\n \n\ncarmine Pierio rationem exponere nostram\n\n \n\net quasi musaeo dulci contingere melle;\n\n \n\nsi tibi forte animum tali ratione tenere\n\n \n\nversibus in nostris possem, dum percipis omnem\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum ac persentis utilitatem.\n\n  \n\nSed quoniam docui cunctarum exordia rerum\n\n \n\nqualia sint et quam variis distantia formis\n\n \n\nsponte sua volitent aeterno percita motu\n\n \n\nquoque modo possit res ex his quaeque creari,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\natque animi quoniam docui natura quid esset\n\n \n\net quibus e rebus cum corpore compta vigeret\n\n \n\nquove modo distracta rediret in ordia prima,\n\n \n\nnunc agere incipiam tibi, quod vehementer ad has res\n\n \n\nattinet esse ea quae rerum simulacra vocamus,\n\n \n\nquod speciem ac formam similem gerit eius imago,\n\n \n\ncuius cumque cluet de corpore fusa vagari;\n\n \n\nquae quasi membranae summo de corpore rerum\n\n \n\ndereptae volitant ultroque citroque per auras,\n\n \n\natque eadem nobis vigilantibus obvia mentes\n\n \n\nterrificant atque in somnis, cum saepe figuras\n\n \n\ncontuimur miras simulacraque luce carentum,\n\n \n\nquae nos horrifice languentis saepe sopore\n\n \n\nexcierunt ne forte animas Acherunte reamur\n\n \n\neffugere aut umbras inter vivos volitare\n\n \n\nneve aliquid nostri post mortem posse relinqui,\n\n \n\ncum corpus simul atque animi natura perempta\n\n \n\nin sua discessum dederint primordia quaeque.\n\n \n\ndico igitur rerum effigias tenuisque figuras\n\n \n\nmittier ab rebus summo de cortice eorum;\n\n \n\nid licet hinc quamvis hebeti cognoscere corde.\n\n  \n\n Principio quoniam mittunt in rebus apertis\n\n \n\ncorpora res multae, partim diffusa solute,\n\n \n\nrobora ceu fumum mittunt ignesque vaporem,\n\n \n\net partim contexta magis condensaque, ut olim\n\n \n\ncum teretis ponunt tunicas aestate cicadae,\n\n \n\net vituli cum membranas de corpore summo\n\n \n\nnascentes mittunt, et item cum lubrica serpens\n\n \n\nexuit in spinis vestem; nam saepe videmus\n\n \n\nillorum spoliis vepres volitantibus auctas.\n\n \n\nquae quoniam fiunt, tenuis quoque debet imago\n\n \n\nab rebus mitti summo de corpore rerum.\n\n \n\nnam cur illa cadant magis ab rebusque recedant\n\n \n\nquam quae tenvia sunt, hiscendist nulla potestas;\n\n \n\npraesertim cum sint in summis corpora rebus\n\n \n\nmulta minuta, iaci quae possint ordine eodem\n\n \n\nquo fuerint et formai servare figuram,\n\n \n\net multo citius, quanto minus indupediri\n\n \n\npauca queunt et  sunt prima fronte locata.\n\n \n\nnam certe iacere ac largiri multa videmus,\n\n \n\nnon solum ex alto penitusque, ut diximus ante,\n\n \n\nverum de summis ipsum quoque saepe colorem.\n\n \n\net volgo faciunt id lutea russaque vela\n\n \n\net ferrugina, cum magnis intenta theatris\n\n \n\nper malos volgata trabesque trementia flutant;\n\n \n\nnamque ibi consessum caveai supter et omnem\n\n \n\nscaenai speciem patrum matrumque deorsum\n\n \n\ninficiunt coguntque suo fluitare colore.\n\n \n\net quanto circum mage sunt inclusa theatri\n\n \n\nmoenia, tam magis haec intus perfusa lepore\n\n \n\nomnia conrident correpta luce diei.\n\n \n\nergo lintea de summo cum corpore fucum\n\n \n\nmittunt, effigias quoque debent mittere tenvis\n\n \n\nres quaeque, ex summo quoniam iaculantur utraque.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur iam formarum vestigia certa,\n\n \n\nquae volgo volitant subtili praedita filo\n\n \n\nnec singillatim possunt secreta videri.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea omnis odor fumus vapor atque aliae res\n\n \n\nconsimiles ideo diffusae rebus abundant,\n\n \n\nex alto quia dum veniunt extrinsecus ortae\n\n \n\nscinduntur per iter flexum, nec recta viarum\n\n \n\nostia sunt, qua contendant exire coortae.\n\n \n\nat contra tenuis summi membrana coloris\n\n \n\ncum iacitur, nihil est quod eam discerpere possit,\n\n \n\nin promptu quoniam est in prima fronte locata.\n\n \n\nPostremo speculis in aqua splendoreque in omni\n\n \n\nquae cumque apparent nobis simulacra, necessest,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem simili specie sunt praedita rerum,\n\n \n\nexin imaginibus missis consistere eorum.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nsunt igitur tenues formarum illis similesque\n\n \n\neffigiae, singillatim quas cernere nemo\n\n \n\ncum possit, tamen adsiduo crebroque repulsu\n\n \n\nreiectae reddunt speculorum ex aequore visum,\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia servari posse videntur,\n\n \n\n tanto opere ut similes reddantur cuique figurae.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quam tenui natura constet imago\n\n \n\npercipe. et in primis, quoniam primordia tantum\n\n \n\nsunt infra nostros sensus tantoque minora\n\n \n\nquam quae primum oculi coeptant non posse tueri,\n\n \n\nnunc tamen id quoque uti confirmem, exordia rerum\n\n \n\ncunctarum quam sint subtilia percipe paucis.\n\n \n\nprimum animalia sunt iam partim tantula, corum\n\n \n\ntertia pars nulla possit ratione videri.\n\n \n\nhorum intestinum quodvis quale esse putandumst!\n\n \n\nquid cordis globus aut oculi? quid membra? quid artus?\n\n \n\nquantula sunt! quid praeterea primordia quaeque,\n\n \n\nunde anima atque animi constet natura necessumst,\n\n \n\nnonne vides quam sint subtilia quamque minuta?\n\n \n\npraeterea quaecumque suo de corpore odorem\n\n \n\nexpirant acrem, panaces absinthia taetra\n\n \n\nhabrotonique graves et tristia centaurea,\n\n \n\nquorum unum quidvis leviter si forte duobus\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nquin potius noscas rerum simulacra vagari\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis, nulla vi cassaque sensu?\n\n \n\nSed ne forte putes ea demum sola vagari,\n\n \n\nquae cumque ab rebus rerum simulacra recedunt,\n\n \n\nsunt etiam quae sponte sua gignuntur et ipsa\n\n \n\nconstituuntur in hoc caelo, qui dicitur aer,\n\n \n\nquae multis formata modis sublime feruntur,\n\n \n\nut nubes facile inter dum concrescere in alto\n\n \n\ncernimus et mundi speciem violare serenam\n\n \n\naëra mulcentes motu; nam saepe Gigantum\n\n \n\nora volare videntur et umbram ducere late,\n\n \n\ninter dum magni montes avolsaque saxa\n\n \n\nmontibus ante ire et solem succedere praeter,\n\n \n\ninde alios trahere atque inducere belua nimbos.\n\n \n\nnec speciem mutare suam liquentia cessant\n\n \n\net cuiusque modi formarum vertere in oras.\n\n  \n\nNunc ea quam facili et celeri ratione genantur\n\n \n\nperpetuoque fluant ab rebus lapsaque cedant\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nsemper enim summum quicquid de rebus abundat,\n\n \n\nquod iaculentur. et hoc alias cum pervenit in res,\n\n \n\ntransit, ut in primis vestem; sed ubi aspera saxa\n\n \n\naut in materiam ligni pervenit, ibi iam\n\n \n\nscinditur, ut nullum simulacrum reddere possit.\n\n \n\nat cum splendida quae constant opposta fuerunt\n\n \n\ndensaque, ut in primis speculum est, nihil accidit horum;\n\n \n\nnam neque, uti vestem, possunt transire, neque autem\n\n \n\nscindi; quam meminit levor praestare salutem.\n\n \n\nqua propter fit ut hinc nobis simulacra redundent.\n\n \n\net quamvis subito quovis in tempore quamque\n\n \n\nrem contra speculum ponas, apparet imago;\n\n \n\nperpetuo fluere ut noscas e corpore summo\n\n \n\ntexturas rerum tenuis tenuisque figuras.\n\n \n\nergo multa brevi spatio simulacra genuntur,\n\n \n\nut merito celer his rebus dicatur origo.\n\n \n\net quasi multa brevi spatio summittere debet\n\n \n\n lumina sol, ut perpetuo sint omnia plena,\n\n \n\nsic ab rebus item simili ratione necessest\n\n \n\ntemporis in puncto rerum simulacra ferantur\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis in cunctas undique partis;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem speculum quo cumque obvertimus oris,\n\n \n\nres ibi respondent simili forma atque colore.\n\n \n\nPraeterea modo cum fuerit liquidissima caeli\n\n \n\ntempestas, perquam subito fit turbida foede,\n\n \n\nundique uti tenebras omnis Acherunta rearis\n\n \n\nliquisse et magnas caeli complesse cavernas.\n\n \n\nusque adeo taetra nimborum nocte coorta\n\n \n\ninpendent atrae Formidinis ora superne;\n\n \n\nquorum quantula pars sit imago dicere nemost\n\n \n\nqui possit neque eam rationem reddere dictis.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quam celeri motu simulacra ferantur,\n\n \n\net quae mobilitas ollis tranantibus auras\n\n \n\nreddita sit, longo spatio ut brevis hora teratur,\n\n \n\nin quem quaeque locum diverso numine tendunt,\n\n \n\nsuavidicis potius quam multis versibus edam;\n\n \n\nparvus ut est cycni melior canor, ille gruum quam\n\n \n\nclamor in aetheriis dispersus nubibus austri.\n\n \n\nPrincipio persaepe levis res atque minutis\n\n \n\ncorporibus factas celeris licet esse videre.\n\n \n\nin quo iam genere est solis lux et vapor eius,\n\n \n\npropterea quia sunt e primis facta minutis,\n\n \n\nquae quasi cuduntur perque aëris intervallum\n\n \n\nnon dubitant transire sequenti concita plaga;\n\n \n\nsuppeditatur enim confestim lumine lumen\n\n \n\net quasi protelo stimulatur fulgere fulgur.\n\n \n\nqua propter simulacra pari ratione necessest\n\n \n\ninmemorabile per spatium transcurrere posse\n\n \n\ntemporis in puncto, primum quod parvola causa\n\n \n\nest procul a tergo quae provehat atque propellat,\n\n \n\nquod super est, ubi tam volucri levitate ferantur,\n\n \n\ndeinde quod usque adeo textura praedita rara\n\n \n\nmittuntur, facile ut quasvis penetrare queant res\n\n \n\net quasi permanare per aëris intervallum.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea si quae penitus corpuscula rerum\n\n \n\nex altoque foras mittuntur, solis uti lux\n\n \n\nac vapor, haec puncto cernuntur lapsa diei\n\n \n\nper totum caeli spatium diffundere sese\n\n \n\nperque volare mare ac terras caelumque rigare.\n\n \n\nquid quae sunt igitur iam prima fronte parata,\n\n \n\ncum iaciuntur et emissum res nulla moratur?\n\n \n\nquone vides citius debere et longius ire\n\n \n\nmultiplexque loci spatium transcurrere eodem\n\n \n\ntempore quo solis pervolgant lumina caelum?\n\n \n\nHoc etiam in primis specimen verum esse videtur,\n\n \n\nquam celeri motu rerum simulacra ferantur,\n\n \n\nquod simul ac primum sub diu splendor aquai\n\n \n\nponitur, extemplo caelo stellante serena\n\n \n\nsidera respondent in aqua radiantia mundi.\n\n \n\niamne vides igitur quam puncto tempore imago\n\n \n\naetheris ex oris in terrarum accidat oras?\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam mitti fateare necessest\n\n  \n\n corpora quae feriant oculos visumque lacessant.\n\n \n\nperpetuoque fluunt certis ab rebus odores,\n\n \n\nfrigus ut a fluviis, calor ab sole, aestus ab undis\n\n \n\naequoris, exesor moerorum litora circum,\n\n \n\nnec variae cessant voces volitare per auras.\n\n \n\ndenique in os salsi venit umor saepe saporis,\n\n \n\ncum mare versamur propter, dilutaque contra\n\n \n\ncum tuimur misceri absinthia, tangit amaror.\n\n \n\nusque adeo omnibus ab rebus res quaeque fluenter\n\n \n\nfertur et in cunctas dimittitur undique partis\n\n \n\nnec mora nec requies interdatur ulla fluendi,\n\n \n\nperpetuo quoniam sentimus et omnia semper\n\n \n\ncernere odorari licet et sentire sonare.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea quoniam manibus tractata figura\n\n \n\nin tenebris quaedam cognoscitur esse eadem quae\n\n \n\ncernitur in luce et claro candore, necessest\n\n \n\nconsimili causa tactum visumque moveri.\n\n \n\nnunc igitur si quadratum temptamus et id nos\n\n \n\ncommovet in tenebris, in luci quae poterit res\n\n \n\naccidere ad speciem quadrata, nisi eius imago?\n\n \n\nesse in imaginibus qua propter causa videtur\n\n \n\ncernundi neque posse sine his res ulla videri.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quae dico rerum simulacra feruntur\n\n \n\nundique et in cunctas iaciuntur didita partis;\n\n \n\nverum nos oculis quia solis cernere quimus,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti, speciem quo vertimus, omnes\n\n \n\nres ibi eam contra feriant forma atque colore.\n\n \n\net quantum quaeque ab nobis res absit, imago\n\n \n\nefficit ut videamus et internoscere curat;\n\n \n\nnam cum mittitur, extemplo protrudit agitque\n\n \n\naëra qui inter se cumque est oculosque locatus,\n\n \n\nisque ita per nostras acies perlabitur omnis\n\n \n\net quasi perterget pupillas atque ita transit.\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti videamus quam procul absit\n\n \n\nres quaeque. et quanto plus aëris ante agitatur\n\n \n\net nostros oculos perterget longior aura,\n\n \n\ntam procul esse magis res quaeque remota videtur.\n\n \n\nscilicet haec summe celeri ratione geruntur,\n\n \n\nquale sit ut videamus, et una quam procul absit.\n\n \n\nIllud in his rebus minime mirabile habendumst,\n\n \n\ncur, ea quae feriant oculos simulacra videri\n\n \n\nsingula cum nequeant, res ipsae perspiciantur.\n\n \n\nventus enim quoque paulatim cum verberat et cum\n\n \n\nacre fluit frigus, non privam quamque solemus\n\n \n\nparticulam venti sentire et frigoris eius,\n\n \n\nsed magis unorsum, fierique perinde videmus\n\n \n\ncorpore tum plagas in nostro tam quam aliquae res\n\n \n\nverberet atque sui det sensum corporis extra.\n\n \n\npraeterea lapidem digito cum tundimus, ipsum\n\n \n\ntangimus extremum saxi summumque colorem\n\n \n\nnec sentimus eum tactu, verum magis ipsam\n\n \n\nduritiem penitus saxi sentimus in alto.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, cur ultra speculum videatur imago\n\n \n\npercipe: nam certe penitus remmota videtur.\n\n \n\nquod genus illa foris quae vere transpiciuntur,\n\n \n\n ianua cum per se transpectum praebet apertum,\n\n \n\nmulta facitque foris ex aedibus ut videantur;\n\n \n\nis quoque enim duplici geminoque fit aëre visus.\n\n \n\nprimus enim citra postes tum cernitur aër,\n\n \n\ninde fores ipsae dextra laevaque secuntur,\n\n \n\npost extraria lux oculos perterget et aër\n\n \n\nalter, et illa foris quae vere transpiciuntur.\n\n \n\nsic ubi se primum speculi proiecit imago,\n\n \n\ndum venit ad nostras acies, protrudit agitque\n\n \n\naëra qui inter se cumquest oculosque locatus,\n\n \n\net facit, ut prius hunc omnem sentire queamus\n\n \n\nquam speculum; sed ubi  speculum quoque sensimus ipsum,\n\n \n\ncontinuo a nobis in eum quae fertur imago\n\n \n\npervenit, et nostros oculos reiecta revisit\n\n \n\natque alium prae se propellens aëra volvit,\n\n \n\net facit ut prius hunc quam se videamus, eoque\n\n \n\ndistare ab speculo tantum semota videtur.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam minime mirarier est par\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nillis quae reddunt speculorum ex aequore visum,\n\n \n\naëribus binis quoniam res confit utraque.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quae nobis membrorum dextera pars est,\n\n \n\nin speculis fit ut in laeva videatur eo quod\n\n \n\nplanitiem ad speculi veniens cum offendit imago,\n\n \n\nnon convertitur incolumis, sed recta retrorsum\n\n \n\nsic eliditur, ut siquis, prius arida quam sit\n\n \n\ncretea persona, adlidat pilaeve trabive,\n\n \n\natque ea continuo rectam si fronte figuram\n\n \n\nservet et elisam retro sese exprimat ipsa.\n\n \n\nfiet ut, ante oculus fuerit qui dexter, ut idem\n\n \n\nnunc sit laevus et e laevo sit mutua dexter.\n\n  \n\nFit quoque de speculo in speculum ut tradatur imago,\n\n \n\nquinque etiam  sex ut fieri simulacra suërint.\n\n \n\nnam quae cumque retro parte interiore latebunt,\n\n \n\ninde tamen, quamvis torte penitusque remota,\n\n \n\nomnia per flexos aditus educta licebit\n\n \n\npluribus haec speculis videantur in aedibus esse.\n\n \n\nusque adeo speculo in speculum translucet imago,\n\n \n\net cum laeva data est, fit rusum ut dextera fiat,\n\n \n\ninde retro rursum redit et convertit eodem.\n\n \n\nQuin etiam quae cumque latuscula sunt speculorum\n\n \n\nadsimili lateris flexura praedita nostri,\n\n \n\ndextera ea propter nobis simulacra remittunt,\n\n \n\naut quia de speculo in speculum transfertur imago,\n\n \n\ninde ad nos elisa bis advolat, aut etiam quod\n\n \n\ncircum agitur, cum venit, imago propterea quod\n\n \n\nflexa figura docet speculi convertier ad nos.\n\n \n\nIndugredi porro pariter simulacra pedemque\n\n \n\nponere nobiscum credas gestumque imitari\n\n \n\npropterea quia, de speculi qua parte recedas,\n\n \n\ncontinuo nequeunt illinc simulacra reverti;\n\n \n\nomnia quandoquidem cogit natura referri\n\n \n\nac resilire ab rebus ad aequos reddita flexus.\n\n \n\nSplendida porro oculi fugitant vitantque tueri.\n\n \n\n sol etiam caecat, contra si tendere pergas,\n\n \n\npropterea quia vis magnast ipsius et alte\n\n \n\naëra per purum simulacra feruntur\n\n \n\net feriunt oculos turbantia composituras.\n\n \n\nPraeterea splendor qui cumque est acer adurit\n\n \n\nsaepe oculos ideo quod semina possidet ignis\n\n \n\nmulta, dolorem oculis quae gignunt insinuando.\n\n \n\nlurida praeterea fiunt quae cumque tuentur\n\n \n\narquati, quia luroris de corpore eorum\n\n \n\nsemina multa fluunt simulacris obvia rerum,\n\n \n\nmultaque sunt oculis in eorum denique mixta,\n\n \n\nquae contage sua palloribus omnia pingunt.\n\n  \n\nE tenebris autem quae sunt in luce tuemur\n\n \n\npropterea quia, cum propior caliginis aër\n\n \n\nater init oculos prior et possedit apertos,\n\n \n\ninsequitur candens confestim lucidus aër,\n\n \n\nqui quasi purgat eos ac nigras discutit umbras\n\n \n\naëris illius; nam multis partibus hic est\n\n \n\nmobilior multisque minutior et mage pollens.\n\n \n\nqui simul atque vias oculorum luce replevit\n\n \n\natque pate fecit, quas ante obsederat aër\n\n \n\nATER, continuo rerum simulacra secuntur,\n\n \n\nquae sita sunt in luce, lacessuntque ut videamus.\n\n \n\nquod contra facere in tenebris e luce nequimus\n\n \n\npropterea quia posterior caliginis aër\n\n \n\ncrassior insequitur, qui cuncta foramina complet\n\n \n\nobsiditque vias oculorum, ne simulacra\n\n \n\npossint ullarum rerum coniecta moveri.\n\n \n\nQuadratasque procul turris cum cernimus urbis,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti videantur saepe rutundae,\n\n \n\nangulus optusus quia longe cernitur omnis\n\n \n\nsive etiam potius non cernitur ac perit eius\n\n \n\nplaga nec ad nostras acies perlabitur ictus,\n\n \n\naëra per multum quia dum simulacra feruntur,\n\n \n\ncogit hebescere eum crebris offensibus aër.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi suffugit sensum simul angulus omnis.\n\n \n\nfit quasi ut ad turnum saxorum structa tuantur;\n\n \n\nnon tamen ut coram quae sunt vereque rutunda,\n\n \n\nsed quasi adumbratim paulum simulata videntur.\n\n \n\nUmbra videtur item nobis in sole moveri\n\n \n\net vestigia nostra sequi gestumque imitari,\n\n \n\naëra si credis privatum lumine posse\n\n \n\nindugredi, motus hominum gestumque sequentem;\n\n \n\nnam nihil esse potest aliud nisi lumine cassus\n\n \n\naër id quod nos umbram perhibere suëmus.\n\n \n\nni mirum, quia terra locis ex ordine certis\n\n \n\nlumine privatur solis qua cumque meantes\n\n \n\nofficimus, repletur item quod liquimus eius,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti videatur, quae fuit umbra\n\n \n\ncorporis, e regione eadem nos usque secuta.\n\n \n\nsemper enim nova se radiorum lumina fundunt\n\n \n\nprimaque dispereunt, quasi in ignem lana trahatur.\n\n \n\npropterea facile et spoliatur lumine terra\n\n \n\net repletur item nigrasque sibi abluit umbras.\n\n  \n\nNec tamen hic oculos falli concedimus hilum.\n\n \n\n nam quo cumque loco sit lux atque umbra tueri\n\n \n\nillorum est; eadem vero sint lumina necne,\n\n \n\numbraque quae fuit hic eadem nunc transeat illuc,\n\n \n\nan potius fiat paulo quod diximus ante,\n\n \n\nhoc animi demum ratio discernere debet,\n\n \n\nnec possunt oculi naturam noscere rerum.\n\n \n\nproinde animi vitium hoc oculis adfingere noli.\n\n \n\nQua vehimur navi, fertur, cum stare videtur;\n\n \n\nquae manet in statione, ea praeter creditur ire.\n\n \n\net fugere ad puppim colles campique videntur,\n\n \n\nquos agimus praeter navem velisque volamus.\n\n \n\nSidera cessare aetheriis adfixa cavernis\n\n \n\ncuncta videntur, et adsiduo sunt omnia motu,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem longos obitus exorta revisunt,\n\n \n\ncum permensa suo sunt caelum corpore claro.\n\n \n\nsolque pari ratione manere et luna videtur\n\n \n\nin statione, ea quae ferri res indicat ipsa.\n\n \n\nExstantisque procul medio de gurgite montis\n\n \n\nclassibus inter quos liber patet exitus ingens,\n\n \n\ninsula coniunctis tamen ex his una videtur.\n\n \n\natria versari et circum cursare columnae\n\n \n\nusque adeo fit uti pueris videantur, ubi ipsi\n\n \n\ndesierunt verti, vix ut iam credere possint\n\n \n\nnon supra sese ruere omnia tecta minari.\n\n \n\nIamque rubrum tremulis iubar ignibus erigere alte\n\n \n\ncum coeptat natura supraque extollere montes,\n\n \n\nquos tibi tum supra sol montis esse videtur\n\n \n\ncomminus ipse suo contingens fervidus igni,\n\n \n\nvix absunt nobis missus bis mille sagittae,\n\n \n\nvix etiam cursus quingentos saepe veruti;\n\n \n\ninter eos solemque iacent immania ponti\n\n \n\naequora substrata aetheriis ingentibus oris,\n\n \n\ninteriectaque sunt terrarum milia multa,\n\n \n\nquae variae retinent gentes et saecla ferarum.\n\n  \n\nAt coniectus aquae digitum non altior unum,\n\n \n\nqui lapides inter sistit per strata viarum,\n\n \n\ndespectum praebet sub terras inpete tanto,\n\n \n\na terris quantum caeli patet altus hiatus,\n\n \n\nnubila despicere et caelum ut videare videre,\n\n \n\ncorpora mirande sub terras abdita caelo.\n\n \n\nDenique ubi in medio nobis ecus acer obhaesit\n\n \n\nflumine et in rapidas amnis despeximus undas,\n\n \n\nstantis equi corpus transversum ferre videtur\n\n \n\nvis et in adversum flumen contrudere raptim,\n\n \n\net quo cumque oculos traiecimus omnia ferri\n\n \n\net fluere adsimili nobis ratione videntur.\n\n \n\nPorticus aequali quamvis est denique ductu\n\n \n\nstansque in perpetuum paribus suffulta columnis,\n\n \n\nlonga tamen parte ab summa cum tota videtur,\n\n \n\npaulatim trahit angusti fastigia coni,\n\n \n\ntecta solo iungens atque omnia dextera laevis\n\n \n\ndonec in obscurum coni conduxit acumen.\n\n \n\nIn pelago nautis ex undis ortus in undis\n\n \n\nsol fit uti videatur obire et condere lumen;\n\n \n\nquippe ubi nil aliud nisi aquam caelumque tuentur;\n\n \n\n ne leviter credas labefactari undique sensus.\n\n \n\nat maris ignaris in portu clauda videntur\n\n \n\nnavigia aplustris fractis obnitier undis.\n\n \n\nnam quae cumque supra rorem salis edita pars est\n\n \n\nremorum, recta est, et recta superne guberna;\n\n \n\nquae demersa liquore obeunt, refracta videntur\n\n \n\nomnia converti sursumque supina reverti\n\n \n\net reflexa prope in summo fluitare liquore.\n\n \n\nRaraque per caelum cum venti nubila portant\n\n \n\ntempore nocturno, tum splendida signa videntur\n\n \n\nlabier adversum nimbos atque ire superne\n\n \n\nlonge aliam in partem ac vera ratione feruntur\n\n  \n\nAt si forte oculo manus uni subdita supter\n\n \n\npressit eum, quodam sensu fit uti videantur\n\n \n\nomnia quae tuimur fieri tum bina tuendo,\n\n \n\nbina lucernarum florentia lumina flammis\n\n \n\nbinaque per totas aedis geminare supellex\n\n \n\net duplicis hominum facies et corpora bina.\n\n \n\nDenique cum suavi devinxit membra sopore\n\n \n\nsomnus et in summa corpus iacet omne quiete,\n\n \n\ntum vigilare tamen nobis et membra movere\n\n \n\nnostra videmur, et in noctis caligine caeca\n\n \n\ncernere censemus solem lumenque diurnum,\n\n \n\nconclusoque loco caelum mare flumina montis\n\n \n\nmutare et campos pedibus transire videmur,\n\n \n\net sonitus audire, severa silentia noctis\n\n \n\nundique cum constent, et reddere dicta tacentes.\n\n \n\nCetera de genere hoc mirande multa videmus,\n\n \n\nquae violare fidem quasi sensibus omnia quaerunt,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam pars horum maxima fallit\n\n \n\npropter opinatus animi, quos addimus ipsi,\n\n \n\npro visis ut sint quae non sunt sensibus visa;\n\n \n\nnam nihil aegrius est quam res secernere apertas\n\n \n\nab dubiis, animus quas ab se protinus addit.\n\n  \n\nDenique nil sciri siquis putat, id quoque nescit\n\n \n\nan sciri possit, quoniam nil scire fatetur.\n\n \n\nhunc igitur contra minuam contendere causam,\n\n \n\nqui capite ipse suo in statuit vestigia sese.\n\n \n\net tamen hoc quoque uti concedam scire, at id ipsum\n\n \n\nquaeram, cum in rebus veri nil viderit ante,\n\n \n\nunde sciat quid sit scire et nescire vicissim,\n\n \n\nnotitiam veri quae res falsique crearit\n\n \n\net dubium certo quae res differre probarit.\n\n \n\ninvenies primis ab sensibus esse creatam\n\n \n\nnotitiem veri neque sensus posse refelli.\n\n \n\nnam maiore fide debet reperirier illud,\n\n \n\nsponte sua veris quod possit vincere falsa.\n\n \n\nquid maiore fide porro quam sensus haberi\n\n \n\ndebet? an ab sensu falso ratio orta valebit\n\n \n\ndicere eos contra, quae tota ab sensibus orta est?\n\n \n\nqui nisi sunt veri, ratio quoque falsa fit omnis.\n\n \n\nAn poterunt oculos aures reprehendere, an aures\n\n \n\ntactus? an hunc porro tactum sapor arguet oris,\n\n \n\nan confutabunt nares oculive revincent?\n\n \n\nnon, ut opinor, ita est. nam seorsum cuique potestas\n\n \n\n divisast, sua vis cuiquest, ideoque necesse est\n\n \n\net quod molle sit et gelidum fervensve videre\n\n \n\net seorsum varios rerum sentire colores\n\n \n\net quae cumque coloribus sint coniuncta necessest.\n\n \n\nseorsus item sapor oris habet vim, seorsus odores\n\n \n\nnascuntur, seorsum sonitus. ideoque necesse est\n\n \n\nnon possint alios alii convincere sensus.\n\n \n\nnec porro poterunt ipsi reprehendere sese,\n\n \n\naequa fides quoniam debebit semper haberi.\n\n \n\nproinde quod in quoquest his visum tempore, verumst.\n\n  \n\nEt si non poterit ratio dissolvere causam,\n\n \n\ncur ea quae fuerint iuxtim quadrata, procul sint\n\n \n\nvisa rutunda, tamen praestat rationis egentem\n\n \n\nreddere mendose causas utriusque figurae,\n\n \n\nquam manibus manifesta suis emittere quoquam\n\n \n\net violare fidem primam et convellere tota\n\n \n\nfundamenta quibus nixatur vita salusque.\n\n \n\nnon modo enim ratio ruat omnis, vita quoque ipsa\n\n \n\nconcidat extemplo, nisi credere sensibus ausis\n\n \n\npraecipitisque locos vitare et cetera quae sint\n\n \n\nin genere hoc fugienda, sequi contraria quae sint.\n\n \n\nilla tibi est igitur verborum copia cassa\n\n \n\nomnis, quae contra sensus instructa paratast.\n\n \n\nDenique ut in fabrica, si pravast regula prima,\n\n \n\nnormaque si fallax rectis regionibus exit,\n\n \n\net libella aliqua si ex parti claudicat hilum,\n\n \n\nomnia mendose fieri atque obstipa necessu est\n\n \n\nprava cubantia prona supina atque absona tecta,\n\n \n\niam ruere ut quaedam videantur velle, ruantque\n\n \n\nprodita iudiciis fallacibus omnia primis,\n\n \n\nsic igitur ratio tibi rerum prava necessest\n\n \n\nfalsaque sit, falsis quae cumque ab sensibus ortast.\n\n  \n\nNunc alii sensus quo pacto quisque suam rem\n\n \n\nsentiat, haud quaquam ratio scruposa relicta est.\n\n \n\nPrincipio auditur sonus et vox omnis, in auris\n\n \n\ninsinuata suo pepulere ubi corpore sensum.\n\n \n\ncorpoream quoque enim  constare fatendumst\n\n \n\net sonitum, quoniam possunt inpellere sensus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea radit vox fauces saepe facitque\n\n \n\nasperiora foras gradiens arteria clamor,\n\n \n\nquippe per angustum turba maiore coorta\n\n \n\nire foras ubi coeperunt primordia vocum.\n\n \n\nscilicet expletis quoque ianua raditur oris.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur dubiumst quin voces verbaque constent\n\n \n\ncorporeis e principiis, ut laedere possint.\n\n \n\nnec te fallit item quid corporis auferat et quid\n\n \n\ndetrahat ex hominum nervis ac viribus ipsis\n\n \n\nperpetuus sermo nigrai noctis ad umbram\n\n \n\naurorae perductus ab exoriente nitore,\n\n \n\npraesertim si cum summost clamore profusus.\n\n \n\nergo corpoream vocem constare necessest,\n\n \n\nmulta loquens quoniam amittit de corpore partem.\n\n \n\nAsperitas autem vocis fit ab asperitate\n\n \n\nprincipiorum et item levor levore creatur;\n\n \n\nnec simili penetrant auris primordia forma,\n\n \n\n cum tuba depresso graviter sub murmure mugit\n\n \n\net reboat raucum retro cita barbita bombum,\n\n \n\net  Dauliades natae hortis ex Heliconis\n\n \n\ncum liquidam tollunt lugubri voce querellam.\n\n  \n\nHasce igitur penitus voces cum corpore nostro\n\n \n\nexprimimus rectoque foras emittimus ore,\n\n \n\nmobilis articulat nervorum daedala lingua,\n\n \n\nformaturaque labrorum pro parte figurat.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi non longum spatiumst unde illa profecta\n\n \n\nperveniat vox quaeque, necessest verba quoque ipsa\n\n \n\nplane exaudiri discernique articulatim;\n\n \n\nservat enim formaturam servatque figuram.\n\n \n\nat si inter positum spatium sit longius aequo,\n\n \n\naëra per multum confundi verba necessest\n\n \n\net conturbari vocem, dum transvolat auras.\n\n \n\nergo fit, sonitum ut possis sentire neque illam\n\n \n\ninternoscere, verborum sententia quae sit;\n\n \n\nusque adeo confusa venit vox inque pedita.\n\n \n\nPraeterea verbum saepe unum perciet auris\n\n \n\nomnibus in populo missum praeconis ab ore.\n\n \n\nin multas igitur voces vox una repente\n\n \n\ndiffugit, in privas quoniam se dividit auris\n\n \n\nobsignans formam verbis clarumque sonorem.\n\n \n\nat quae pars vocum non auris incidit ipsas,\n\n \n\npraeter lata perit frustra diffusa per auras.\n\n \n\npars solidis adlisa locis reiecta sonorem\n\n \n\nreddit et inter dum frustratur imagine verbi.\n\n  \n\nQuae bene cum videas, rationem reddere possis\n\n \n\ntute tibi atque aliis, quo pacto per loca sola\n\n \n\nsaxa paris formas verborum ex ordine reddant.\n\n \n\npalantis comites com montis inter opacos\n\n \n\nquaerimus et magna dispersos voce ciemus.\n\n \n\nsex etiam aut septem loca vidi reddere vocis,\n\n \n\nunam cum iaceres: ita colles collibus ipsi\n\n \n\nverba repulsantes iterabant dicta referri.\n\n \n\nhaec loca capripedes Satyros Nymphasque tenere\n\n \n\nfinitimi fingunt et Faunos esse locuntur,\n\n \n\nquorum noctivago strepitu ludoque iocanti\n\n \n\nadfirmant volgo taciturna silentia rumpi\n\n \n\nchordarumque sonos fieri dulcisque querellas,\n\n \n\ntibia quas fundit digitis pulsata canentum,\n\n \n\net genus agricolum late sentiscere, quom Pan\n\n \n\npinea semiferi capitis velamina quassans\n\n \n\nunco saepe labro calamos percurrit hiantis,\n\n \n\nfistula silvestrem ne cesset fundere musam.\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc monstra ac portenta loquontur,\n\n \n\nne loca deserta ab divis quoque forte putentur\n\n \n\nsola tenere. ideo iactant miracula dictis\n\n \n\naut aliqua ratione alia ducuntur, ut omne\n\n \n\nhumanum genus est avidum nimis auricularum.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, non est mirandum qua ratione,\n\n \n\nper loca quae nequeunt oculi res cernere apertas,\n\n \n\nhaec loca per voces veniant aurisque lacessant,\n\n \n\nconloquium clausis foribus quoque saepe videmus;\n\n \n\nni mirum quia vox per flexa foramina rerum\n\n \n\n incolumis transire potest, simulacra renutant;\n\n \n\nperscinduntur enim, nisi recta foramina tranant,\n\n \n\nqualia sunt vitrei, species qua travolat omnis.\n\n \n\npraeterea partis in cunctas dividitur vox,\n\n \n\nex aliis aliae quoniam gignuntur, ubi una\n\n \n\ndissuluit semel in multas exorta, quasi ignis\n\n \n\nsaepe solet scintilla suos se spargere in ignis.\n\n \n\nergo replentur loca vocibus abdita retro,\n\n \n\nomnia quae circum fervunt sonituque cientur.\n\n \n\nat simulacra viis derectis omnia tendunt,\n\n \n\nut sunt missa semel; qua propter cernere nemo\n\n \n\nsaepe supra potis est, at voces accipere extra.\n\n \n\net tamen ipsa quoque haec, dum transit clausa \n\n \n\nvox optunditur atque auris confusa penetrat\n\n \n\net sonitum potius quam verba audire videmur.\n\n  \n\nHoc, qui sentimus sucum, lingua atque palatum\n\n \n\nplusculum habent in se rationis, plus operai.\n\n \n\nprincipio sucum sentimus in ore, cibum cum\n\n \n\nmandendo exprimimus, ceu plenam spongiam aquai\n\n \n\nsiquis forte manu premere ac siccare coëpit.\n\n \n\ninde quod exprimimus per caulas omne palati\n\n \n\ndiditur et rarae per flexa foramina linguae,\n\n \n\nhoc ubi levia sunt manantis corpora suci,\n\n \n\nsuaviter attingunt et suaviter omnia tractant\n\n \n\numida linguai circum sudantia templa;\n\n \n\nat contra pungunt sensum lacerantque coorta,\n\n \n\nquanto quaeque magis sunt asperitate repleta.\n\n \n\ndeinde voluptas est e suco fine palati;\n\n \n\ncum vero deorsum per fauces praecipitavit,\n\n \n\nnulla voluptas est, dum diditur omnis in artus;\n\n \n\nnec refert quicquam quo victu corpus alatur,\n\n \n\ndum modo quod capias concoctum didere possis\n\n \n\nartubus et stomachi tumidum servare tenorem.\n\n \n\nNunc aliis alius qui sit cibus ut videamus,\n\n \n\nexpediam, quareve, aliis quod triste et amarumst,\n\n \n\nhoc tamen esse aliis possit perdulce videri,\n\n \n\ntantaque  his rebus distantia differitasque est,\n\n \n\nut quod aliis cibus est aliis fuat acre venenum;\n\n \n\nest itaque ut serpens, hominis quae tacta salivis\n\n \n\ndisperit ac sese mandendo conficit ipsa.\n\n \n\npraeterea nobis veratrum est acre venenum,\n\n \n\nat capris adipes et cocturnicibus auget.\n\n \n\nid quibus ut fiat rebus cognoscere possis,\n\n \n\nprincipio meminisse decet quae diximus ante,\n\n \n\nsemina multimodis in rebus mixta teneri.\n\n \n\nporro omnes quae cumque cibum capiunt animantes,\n\n \n\nut sunt dissimiles extrinsecus et generatim\n\n \n\nextima membrorum circumcaesura coërcet,\n\n \n\nproinde et seminibus constant variantque figura.\n\n \n\nsemina cum porro distent, differre necessest\n\n \n\nintervalla viasque, foramina quae perhibemus,\n\n \n\nomnibus in membris et in ore ipsoque palato.\n\n \n\nesse minora igitur quaedam maioraque debent,\n\n \n\nesse triquetra aliis, aliis quadrata necessest,\n\n \n\nmulta rutunda, modis multis multangula quaedam.\n\n \n\n namque figurarum ratio ut motusque reposcunt,\n\n \n\nproinde foraminibus debent differe figurae\n\n \n\net variare viae proinde ac textura coërcet.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi quod suave est aliis aliis fit amarum,\n\n \n\nilli, cui suave est, levissima corpora debent\n\n \n\ncontractabiliter caulas intrare palati,\n\n \n\nat contra quibus est eadem res intus acerba,\n\n \n\naspera ni mirum penetrant hamataque fauces.\n\n \n\nnunc facile est ex his rebus cognoscere quaeque.\n\n \n\nquippe ubi cui febris bili superante coorta est\n\n \n\naut alia ratione aliquast vis excita morbi,\n\n \n\nperturbatur ibi iam totum corpus et omnes\n\n \n\ncommutantur ibi positurae principiorum;\n\n \n\nfit prius ad sensum  quae corpora conveniebant\n\n \n\nnunc non conveniant, et cetera sint magis apta,\n\n \n\nquae penetrata queunt sensum progignere acerbum;\n\n \n\nutraque enim sunt in mellis commixta sapore;\n\n \n\nid quod iam supera tibi saepe ostendimus ante.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quo pacto naris adiectus odoris\n\n \n\ntangat agam. primum res multas esse necessest\n\n \n\nunde fluens volvat varius se fluctus odorum,\n\n \n\net fluere et mitti volgo spargique putandumst;\n\n \n\nverum aliis alius magis est animantibus aptus,\n\n \n\ndissimilis propter formas. ideoque per auras\n\n \n\nmellis apes quamvis longe ducuntur odore,\n\n \n\nvolturiique cadaveribus; tum fissa ferarum\n\n \n\nungula quo tulerit gressum promissa canum vis\n\n \n\nducit, et humanum longe praesentit odorem\n\n \n\nRomulidarum arcis servator, candidus anser.\n\n \n\nsic aliis alius nidor datus ad sua quemque\n\n \n\npabula ducit et a taetro resilire veneno\n\n \n\ncogit, eoque modo servantur saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\nHic odor ipse igitur, naris qui cumque lacessit,\n\n \n\nest alio ut possit permitti longius alter;\n\n \n\nsed tamen haud quisquam tam longe fertur eorum\n\n \n\nquam sonitus, quam vox, mitto iam dicere quam res\n\n \n\nquae feriunt oculorum acies visumque lacessunt.\n\n \n\nerrabundus enim tarde venit ac perit ante\n\n \n\npaulatim facilis distractus in aëris auras;\n\n \n\nex alto primum quia vix emittitur ex re;\n\n \n\nnam penitus fluere atque recedere rebus odores\n\n \n\nsignificat quod fracta magis redolere videntur\n\n \n\nomnia, quod contrita, quod igni conlabefacta.\n\n \n\ndeinde videre licet maioribus esse creatum\n\n \n\nprincipiis quam vox, quoniam per saxea saepta\n\n \n\nnon penetrat, qua vox volgo sonitusque feruntur.\n\n \n\nquare etiam quod olet non tam facile esse videbis\n\n \n\ninvestigare in qua sit regione locatum;\n\n \n\nrefrigescit enim cunctando plaga per auras\n\n \n\nnec calida ad sensum decurrunt nuntia rerum.\n\n \n\nerrant saepe canes itaque et vestigia quaerunt.\n\n \n\nNec tamen hoc solis in odoribus atque saporum\n\n \n\nin generest, sed item species rerum atque colores\n\n \n\nnon ita conveniunt ad sensus omnibus omnes,\n\n \n\nut non sint aliis quaedam magis acria visu.\n\n \n\n quin etiam gallum noctem explaudentibus alis\n\n \n\nauroram clara consuetum voce vocare,\n\n \n\nnoenu queunt rapidi contra constare leones\n\n \n\ninque tueri: ita continuo meminere fugai.\n\n \n\nni mirum quia sunt gallorum in corpore quaedam\n\n \n\nsemina, quae cum sunt oculis inmissa leonum,\n\n \n\npupillas interfodiunt acremque dolorem\n\n \n\npraebent, ut nequeant contra durare feroces,\n\n \n\ncum tamen haec nostras acies nil laedere possint,\n\n \n\naut quia non penetrant aut quod penetrantibus illis\n\n \n\nexitus ex oculis liber datur, in remorando\n\n \n\nlaedere ne possint ex ulla lumina parte.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quae moveant animum res accipe, et unde\n\n \n\nquae veniunt veniant in mentem percipe paucis.\n\n \n\nprincipio hoc dico, rerum simulacra vagari\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis in cunctas undique partis\n\n \n\ntenvia, quae facile inter se iunguntur in auris,\n\n \n\nobvia cum veniunt, ut aranea bratteaque auri.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim multo magis haec sunt tenvia textu\n\n \n\nquam quae percipiunt oculos visumque lacessunt,\n\n \n\ncorporis haec quoniam penetrant per rara cientque\n\n \n\ntenvem animi naturam intus sensumque lacessunt.\n\n \n\nCentauros itaque et Scyllarum membra videmus\n\n \n\nCerbereasque canum facies simulacraque eorum\n\n \n\nquorum morte obita tellus amplectitur ossa;\n\n \n\nomnigenus quoniam passim simulacra feruntur,\n\n \n\npartim sponte sua quae fiunt aëre in ipso,\n\n \n\npartim quae variis ab rebus cumque recedunt\n\n \n\net quae confiunt ex horum facta figuris.\n\n \n\nnam certe ex vivo Centauri non fit imago,\n\n \n\nnulla fuit quoniam talis natura animata;\n\n \n\nverum ubi equi atque hominis casu convenit imago,\n\n \n\nhaerescit facile extemplo, quod diximus ante,\n\n \n\npropter subtilem naturam et tenvia texta.\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc eadem ratione creantur.\n\n \n\nquae cum mobiliter summa levitate feruntur,\n\n \n\nut prius ostendi, facile uno commovet ictu\n\n \n\nquae libet una animum nobis subtilis imago;\n\n \n\ntenvis enim mens est et mire mobilis ipsa.\n\n \n\nhaec fieri ut memoro, facile hinc cognoscere possis.\n\n \n\nquatinus hoc simile est illi, quod mente videmus\n\n \n\natque oculis, simili fieri ratione necessest.\n\n \n\nNunc igitur docui quoniam me forte leonum\n\n \n\ncernere per simulacra, oculos quae cumque lacessunt,\n\n \n\nscire licet mentem simili ratione moveri\n\n \n\nper simulacra leonum  cetera quae videt aeque\n\n \n\nnec minus atque oculi, nisi quod mage tenvia cernit.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia, cum somnus membra profudit,\n\n \n\nmens animi vigilat, nisi quod simulacra lacessunt\n\n \n\nhaec eadem nostros animos quae cum vigilamus,\n\n \n\nusque adeo, certe ut videamur cernere eum quem\n\n \n\nrellicta vita iam mors et terra potitast.\n\n \n\nhoc ideo fieri cogit natura, quod omnes\n\n \n\ncorporis offecti sensus per membra quiescunt\n\n \n\nnec possunt falsum veris convincere rebus.\n\n \n\n praeterea meminisse iacet languetque sopore,\n\n \n\nnec dissentit eum mortis letique potitum\n\n \n\niam pridem, quem mens vivom se cernere credit.\n\n \n\nquod super est, non est mirum simulacra moveri\n\n \n\nbracchiaque in numerum iactare et cetera membra;\n\n \n\nnam fit ut in somnis facere hoc videatur imago.\n\n \n\nquippe, ubi prima perit alioque est altera nata\n\n \n\ninde statu, prior hic gestum mutasse videtur.\n\n \n\nscilicet id fieri celeri ratione putandumst:\n\n \n\ntanta est mobilitas et rerum copia tanta\n\n \n\ntantaque sensibili quovis est tempore in uno\n\n \n\ncopia particularum, ut possit suppeditare.\n\n  \n\nMultaque in his rebus quaeruntur multaque nobis\n\n \n\nclarandumst, plane si res exponere avemus.\n\n \n\nquaeritur in primis quare, quod cuique libido\n\n \n\nvenerit, extemplo mens cogitet eius id ipsum.\n\n \n\nanne voluntatem nostram simulacra tuentur\n\n \n\net simul ac volumus nobis occurrit imago,\n\n \n\nsi mare, si terram cordist, si denique caelum?\n\n \n\nconventus hominum, pompam, convivia, pugnas,\n\n \n\nomnia sub verbone creat natura paratque?\n\n \n\ncum praesertim aliis eadem in regione locoque\n\n \n\nlonge dissimilis animus res cogitet omnis.\n\n \n\nquid porro, in numerum procedere cum simulacra\n\n \n\ncernimus in somnis et mollia membra movere,\n\n \n\nmollia mobiliter cum alternis bracchia mittunt\n\n \n\net repetunt oculis gestum pede convenienti?\n\n \n\nscilicet arte madent simulacra et docta vagantur,\n\n \n\nnocturno facere ut possint in tempore ludos.\n\n \n\nan magis illud erit verum? quia tempore in uno,\n\n \n\ncum sentimus, id est cum vox emittitur una,\n\n \n\ntempora multa latent, ratio quae comperit esse,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti quovis in tempore quaeque\n\n \n\npraesto sint simulacra locis in quisque parata.\n\n \n\ntanta est mobilitas et rerum copia tanta.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi prima perit alioque est altera nata\n\n \n\ninde statu, prior hic gestum mutasse videtur.\n\n \n\net quia tenvia sunt, nisi quae contendit, acute\n\n \n\ncernere non potis est animus; proinde omnia quae sunt\n\n \n\npraeterea pereunt, nisi quae ex se ipse paravit.\n\n \n\nipse parat sese porro speratque futurum\n\n \n\nut videat quod consequitur rem quamque: fit ergo.\n\n \n\nnonne vides oculos etiam, cum tenvia quae sunt\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\ncernere coeperunt, contendere se atque parare,\n\n \n\nnec sine eo fieri posse ut cernamus acute?\n\n \n\net tamen in rebus quoque apertis noscere possis,\n\n \n\nsi non advertas animum, proinde esse quasi omni\n\n \n\ntempore semotum fuerit longeque remotum.\n\n \n\ncur igitur mirumst, animus si cetera perdit\n\n \n\npraeter quam quibus est in rebus deditus ipse?\n\n \n\ndeinde adopinamur de signis maxima parvis\n\n \n\nac nos in fraudem induimus frustraminis ipsi.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut inter dum non suppeditetur imago\n\n \n\neiusdem generis, sed femina quae fuit ante,\n\n \n\n in manibus vir uti factus videatur adesse,\n\n \n\naut alia ex alia facies aetasque sequatur.\n\n \n\nquod ne miremur sopor atque oblivia curant.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus vitium vehementer äinesse\n\n \n\neffugere errorem vitareque praemetuenter,\n\n \n\nlumina ne facias oculorum clara creata,\n\n \n\nprospicere ut possimus, et ut proferre queamus\n\n \n\nproceros passus, ideo fastigia posse\n\n \n\nsurarum ac feminum pedibus fundata plicari,\n\n \n\nbracchia tum porro validis ex apta lacertis\n\n \n\nesse manusque datas utraque  parte ministras,\n\n \n\nut facere ad vitam possemus quae foret usus.\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc inter quae cumque pretantur,\n\n \n\nomnia perversa praepostera sunt ratione,\n\n \n\nnil ideo quoniam natumst in corpore ut uti\n\n \n\npossemus, sed quod natumst id procreat usum.\n\n \n\nnec fuit ante videre oculorum lumina nata,\n\n \n\nnec dictis orare prius quam lingua creatast,\n\n \n\nsed potius longe linguae praecessit origo\n\n \n\nsermonem multoque creatae sunt prius aures\n\n \n\nquam sonus est auditus, et omnia denique membra\n\n \n\nante fuere, ut opinor, eorum quam foret usus;\n\n \n\nhaud igitur potuere utendi crescere causa.\n\n \n\nat contra conferre manu certamina pugnae\n\n \n\net lacerare artus foedareque membra cruore\n\n \n\nante fuit multo quam lucida tela volarent,\n\n \n\net volnus vitare prius natura coëgit\n\n \n\nquam daret obiectum parmai laeva per artem.\n\n \n\nscilicet et fessum corpus mandare quieti\n\n \n\nmulto antiquius est quam lecti mollia strata,\n\n \n\net sedare sitim prius est quam pocula natum.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur possunt utendi cognita causa\n\n \n\ncredier, ex usu quae sunt vitaque reperta.\n\n \n\nilla quidem seorsum sunt omnia, quae prius ipsa\n\n \n\nnata dedere suae post notitiam utilitatis.\n\n \n\nquo genere in primis sensus et membra videmus;\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam procul est ut credere possis\n\n \n\nutilitatis ob officium potuisse creari.\n\n  \n\nIllud item non est mirandum, corporis ipsa\n\n \n\nquod natura cibum quaerit cuiusque animantis.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim fluere atque recedere corpora rebus\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis docui, sed plurima debent\n\n \n\nex animalibus;  quia sunt exercita motu,\n\n \n\nmulta per os exhalantur, cum languida anhelant,\n\n \n\nmultaque per sudorem ex alto pressa feruntur.\n\n \n\nhis igitur rebus rarescit corpus et omnis\n\n \n\nsubruitur natura, dolor quam consequitur rem.\n\n \n\npropterea capitur cibus, ut suffulciat artus\n\n \n\net recreet vires inter datus, atque patentem\n\n \n\nper membra ac venas ut amorem opturet edendi.\n\n \n\numor item discedit in omnia quae loca cumque\n\n \n\nposcunt umorem; glomerataque multa vaporis\n\n \n\ncorpora, quae stomacho praebent incendia nostro,\n\n \n\ndissupat adveniens liquor ac restinguit ut ignem,\n\n \n\nurere ne possit calor amplius aridus artus.\n\n \n\n sic igitur tibi anhela sitis de corpore nostro\n\n \n\nabluitur, sic expletur ieiuna cupido.\n\n  \n\nNunc qui fiat uti passus proferre queamus,\n\n \n\ncum volumus, quareque datum sit membra movere\n\n \n\net quae res tantum hoc oneris protrudere nostri\n\n \n\ncorporis insuerit, dicam: tu percipe dicta.\n\n \n\ndico animo nostro primum simulacra meandi\n\n \n\naccidere atque animum pulsare, ut diximus ante.\n\n \n\ninde voluntas fit; neque enim facere incipit ullam\n\n \n\nrem quisquam,  mens providit quid velit ante.\n\n \n\nid quod providet, illius rei constat imago,\n\n \n\nergo animus cum sese ita commovet ut velit ire\n\n \n\ninque gredi, ferit extemplo quae in corpore toto\n\n \n\nper membra atque artus animai dissita vis est;\n\n \n\net facilest factu, quoniam coniuncta tenetur.\n\n \n\ninde ea proporro corpus ferit, atque ita tota\n\n \n\npaulatim moles protruditur atque movetur.\n\n \n\npraeterea tum rarescit quoque corpus et aër,\n\n \n\nscilicet ut debet qui semper mobilis extat,\n\n \n\nper patefacta venit penetratque foramina largus,\n\n \n\net dispargitur ad partis ita quasque minutas\n\n \n\ncorporis. hic igitur rebus fit utrimque duabus,\n\n \n\ncorpus ut ac navis velis ventoque feratur.\n\n \n\nnec tamen illud in his rebus mirabile constat,\n\n \n\ntantula quod tantum corpus corpuscula possunt\n\n \n\ncontorquere et onus totum convertere nostrum;\n\n \n\nquippe etenim ventus subtili corpore tenvis\n\n \n\ntrudit agens magnam magno molimine navem\n\n \n\net manus una regit quanto vis impete euntem\n\n \n\natque gubernaclum contorquet quo libet unum,\n\n \n\nmultaque per trocleas et tympana pondere magno\n\n \n\ncommovet atque levi sustollit machina nisu.\n\n  \n\nNunc quibus ille modis somnus per membra quietem\n\n \n\ninriget atque animi curas e pectore solvat,\n\n \n\nsuavidicis potius quom multis versibus edam,\n\n \n\nparvus ut est cycni melior canor, ille gruum quam\n\n \n\nclamor in aetheriis dispersus nubibus austri.\n\n \n\ntu mihi da tenuis auris animumque sagacem,\n\n \n\nne fieri negites quae dicam posse retroque\n\n \n\nvera repulsanti discedas pectore dicta,\n\n \n\ntutemet in culpa cum sis neque cernere possis.\n\n \n\nPrincipio somnus fit ubi est distracta per artus\n\n \n\nvis animae partimque foras eiecta recessit\n\n \n\net partim contrusa magis concessit in altum;\n\n \n\ndissoluuntur enim tum demum membra fluuntque.\n\n \n\nnam dubium non est, animai quin opera sit\n\n \n\nsensus hic in nobis, quem cum sopor inpedit esse,\n\n \n\ntum nobis animam perturbatam esse putandumst\n\n \n\neiectamque foras, non omnem; namque iaceret\n\n \n\naeterno corpus perfusum frigore leti.\n\n \n\nquippe ubi nulla latens animai pars remaneret\n\n \n\nin membris, cinere ut multa latet obrutus ignis,\n\n \n\nunde reconflari sensus per membra repente\n\n \n\npossit, ut ex igni caeco consurgere flamma?\n\n  \n\nSed quibus haec rebus novitas confiat et unde\n\n \n\n perturbari anima et corpus languescere possit,\n\n \n\nexpediam: tu fac ne ventis verba profundam.\n\n \n\nPrincipio externa corpus de parte necessum est,\n\n \n\naëriis quoniam vicinum tangitur auris,\n\n \n\ntundier atque eius crebro pulsarier ictu,\n\n \n\nproptereaque fere res omnes aut corio sunt\n\n \n\naut etiam conchis aut callo aut cortice tectae.\n\n \n\ninteriorem etiam partem spirantibus aër\n\n \n\nverberat hic idem, cum ducitur atque reflatur.\n\n \n\nquare utrimque secus cum corpus vapulet et cum\n\n \n\nperveniant plagae per parva foramina nobis\n\n \n\ncorporis ad primas partis elementaque prima,\n\n \n\nfit quasi paulatim nobis per membra ruina.\n\n \n\nconturbantur enim positurae principiorum\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animi. fit uti pars inde animai\n\n \n\neliciatur et introrsum pars abdita cedat,\n\n \n\npars etiam distracta per artus non queat esse\n\n \n\nconiuncta inter se neque motu mutua fungi;\n\n \n\ninter enim saepit coetus natura viasque.\n\n \n\nergo sensus abit mutatis motibus alte.\n\n \n\net quoniam non est quasi quod suffulciat artus,\n\n \n\ndebile fit corpus languescuntque omnia membra,\n\n \n\nbracchia palpebraeque cadunt poplitesque cubanti\n\n \n\nsaepe tamen summittuntur virisque resolvunt.\n\n \n\nDeinde cibum sequitur somnus, quia, quae facit aër,\n\n \n\nhaec eadem cibus, in venas dum diditur omnis,\n\n \n\nefficit. et multo sopor ille gravissimus exstat,\n\n \n\nquem satur aut lassus capias, quia plurima tum se\n\n \n\ncorpora conturbant magno contusa labore.\n\n \n\nfit ratione eadem coniectus parte animai\n\n \n\naltior atque foras eiectus largior eius,\n\n \n\net divisior inter se ac distractior intus.\n\n  \n\nEt quo quisque fere studio devinctus adhaeret\n\n \n\naut quibus in rebus multum sumus ante morati\n\n \n\natque in ea ratione fuit contenta magis mens,\n\n \n\nin somnis eadem plerumque videmur obire:\n\n \n\ncausidici causas agere et componere leges,\n\n \n\ninduperatores pugnare ac proelia obire,\n\n \n\nnautae contractum cum ventis degere bellum,\n\n \n\nnos agere hoc autem et naturam quaerere rerum\n\n \n\nsemper et inventam patriis exponere chartis.\n\n \n\ncetera sic studia atque artes plerumque videntur\n\n \n\nin somnis animos hominum frustrata tenere.\n\n \n\net qui cumque dies multos ex ordine ludis\n\n \n\nadsiduas dederunt operas, plerumque videmus,\n\n \n\ncum iam destiterunt ea sensibus usurpare,\n\n \n\nrelicuas tamen esse vias in mente patentis,\n\n \n\nqua possint eadem rerum simulacra venire;\n\n \n\nper multos itaque illa dies eadem obversantur\n\n \n\nante oculos, etiam vigilantes ut videantur\n\n \n\ncernere saltantis et mollia membra moventis\n\n \n\net citharae liquidum carmen chordasque loquentis\n\n \n\nauribus accipere et consessum cernere eundem\n\n \n\nscenaique simul varios splendere decores.\n\n \n\nusque adeo magni refert studium atque voluntas,\n\n \n\n et quibus in rebus consuerint esse operati\n\n \n\nnon homines solum sed vero animalia cuncta.\n\n \n\nquippe videbis equos fortis, cum membra iacebunt,\n\n \n\nin somnis sudare tamen spirareque semper\n\n \n\net quasi de palma summas contendere viris\n\n \n\naut quasi carceribus patefactis \n\n \n\nvenantumque canes in molli saepe quiete\n\n \n\niactant crura tamen subito vocisque repente\n\n \n\nmittunt et crebro redducunt naribus auras.\n\n \n\nut vestigia si teneant inventa ferarum,\n\n \n\nexpergefactique secuntur inania saepe\n\n \n\ncervorum simulacra, fugae quasi dedita cernant,\n\n \n\ndonec discussis redeant erroribus ad se.\n\n \n\nat consueta domi catulorum blanda propago\n\n \n\ndiscutere et corpus de terra corripere instant,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nproinde quasi ignotas facies atque ora tuantur.\n\n \n\net quo quaeque magis sunt aspera seminiorum,\n\n \n\ntam magis in somnis eadem saevire necessust.\n\n \n\nat variae fugiunt volucres pinnisque repente\n\n \n\nsollicitant divom nocturno tempore lucos,\n\n \n\naccipitres somno in leni si proelia pugnas\n\n \n\nedere sunt persectantes visaeque volantes.\n\n \n\nporro hominum mentes, magnis quae motibus edunt\n\n \n\nmagna, itidem saepe in somnis faciuntque geruntque,\n\n \n\nreges expugnant, capiuntur, proelia miscent,\n\n \n\ntollunt clamorem, quasi si iugulentur ibidem.\n\n \n\nmulti depugnant gemitusque doloribus edunt\n\n \n\net quasi pantherae morsu saevive leonis\n\n \n\nmandantur, magnis clamoribus omnia complent.\n\n \n\nmulti de magnis per somnum rebus loquuntur\n\n \n\nindicioque sui facti persaepe fuere.\n\n \n\nmulti mortem obeunt. multi, de montibus altis\n\n \n\nut quasi praecipitent ad terram corpore toto,\n\n \n\nexterruntur et ex somno quasi mentibus capti\n\n \n\nvix ad se redeunt permoti corporis aestu.\n\n \n\nflumen item sitiens aut fontem propter amoenum\n\n \n\nadsidet et totum prope faucibus occupat amnem.\n\n \n\npuri saepe lacum propter si ac dolia curta\n\n \n\nsomno devincti credunt se extollere vestem,\n\n \n\ntotius umorem saccatum corporis fundunt,\n\n \n\ncum Babylonica magnifico splendore rigantur.\n\n \n\ntum quibus aetatis freta primitus insinuatur\n\n \n\nsemen, ubi ipsa dies membris matura creavit,\n\n \n\nconveniunt simulacra foris e corpore quoque,\n\n \n\nnuntia praeclari voltus pulchrique coloris,\n\n \n\nqui ciet inritans loca turgida semine multo,\n\n \n\nut quasi transactis saepe omnibus rebus profundant\n\n \n\nfluminis ingentis fluctus vestemque cruentent.\n\n  \n\nSollicitatur id  nobis, quod diximus ante,\n\n \n\nsemen, adulta aetas cum primum roborat artus.\n\n \n\nnamque alias aliud res commovet atque lacessit;\n\n \n\n ex homine humanum semen ciet una hominis vis.\n\n \n\nquod simul atque suis eiectum sedibus exit,\n\n \n\nper membra atque artus decedit corpore toto,\n\n \n\nin loca conveniens nervorum certa cietque\n\n \n\ncontinuo partis genitalis corporis ipsas.\n\n \n\ninritata tument loca semine fitque voluntas\n\n \n\neicere id quo se contendit dira lubido,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nidque petit corpus, mens unde est saucia amore;\n\n \n\nnamque omnes plerumque cadunt in vulnus et illam\n\n \n\nemicat in partem sanguis, unde icimur ictu,\n\n \n\net si comminus est, hostem ruber occupat umor.\n\n \n\nsic igitur Veneris qui telis accipit ictus,\n\n \n\nsive puer membris muliebribus hunc iaculatur\n\n \n\nseu mulier toto iactans e corpore amorem,\n\n \n\nunde feritur, eo tendit gestitque coire\n\n \n\net iacere umorem in corpus de corpore ductum;\n\n \n\nnamque voluptatem praesagit muta cupido.\n\n  \n\nHaec Venus est nobis; hinc autemst nomen Amoris,\n\n \n\nhinc illaec primum Veneris dulcedinis in cor\n\n \n\nstillavit gutta et successit frigida cura;\n\n \n\nnam si abest quod ames, praesto simulacra tamen sunt\n\n \n\nillius et nomen dulce obversatur ad auris.\n\n \n\nsed fugitare decet simulacra et pabula amoris\n\n \n\nabsterrere sibi atque alio convertere mentem\n\n \n\net iacere umorem coniectum in corpora quaeque\n\n \n\nnec retinere semel conversum unius amore\n\n \n\net servare sibi curam certumque dolorem;\n\n \n\nulcus enim vivescit et inveterascit alendo\n\n \n\ninque dies gliscit furor atque aerumna gravescit,\n\n \n\nsi non prima novis conturbes volnera plagis\n\n \n\nvolgivagaque vagus Venere ante recentia cures\n\n \n\naut alio possis animi traducere motus.\n\n  \n\nNec Veneris fructu caret is qui vitat amorem,\n\n \n\nsed potius quae sunt sine poena commoda sumit;\n\n \n\nnam certe purast sanis magis inde voluptas\n\n \n\nquam miseris; etenim potiundi tempore in ipso\n\n \n\nfluctuat incertis erroribus ardor amantum\n\n \n\nnec constat quid primum oculis manibusque fruantur.\n\n \n\nquod petiere, premunt arte faciuntque dolorem\n\n \n\ncorporis et dentes inlidunt saepe labellis\n\n \n\nosculaque adfigunt, quia non est pura voluptas\n\n \n\net stimuli subsunt, qui instigant laedere id ipsum,\n\n \n\nquod cumque est, rabies unde illaec germina surgunt.\n\n \n\nsed leviter poenas frangit Venus inter amorem\n\n \n\nblandaque refrenat morsus admixta voluptas.\n\n \n\nnamque in eo spes est, unde est ardoris origo,\n\n \n\nrestingui quoque posse ab eodem corpore flammam.\n\n \n\nquod fieri contra totum natura repugnat;\n\n \n\nunaque res haec est, cuius quam plurima habemus,\n\n \n\ntam magis ardescit dira cuppedine pectus.\n\n \n\nnam cibus atque umor membris adsumitur intus;\n\n \n\nquae quoniam certas possunt obsidere partis,\n\n \n\nhoc facile expletur laticum frugumque cupido.\n\n \n\nex hominis vero facie pulchroque colore\n\n \n\n nil datur in corpus praeter simulacra fruendum\n\n \n\ntenvia; quae vento spes raptast saepe misella.\n\n \n\nut bibere in somnis sitiens quom quaerit et umor\n\n \n\nnon datur, ardorem qui membris stinguere possit,\n\n \n\nsed laticum simulacra petit frustraque laborat\n\n \n\nin medioque sitit torrenti flumine potans,\n\n \n\nsic in amore Venus simulacris ludit amantis,\n\n \n\nnec satiare queunt spectando corpora coram\n\n \n\nnec manibus quicquam teneris abradere membris\n\n \n\npossunt errantes incerti corpore toto.\n\n \n\ndenique cum membris conlatis flore fruuntur\n\n \n\naetatis, iam cum praesagit gaudia corpus\n\n \n\natque in eost Venus ut muliebria conserat arva,\n\n \n\nadfigunt avide corpus iunguntque salivas\n\n \n\noris et inspirant pressantes dentibus ora,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam nihil inde abradere possunt\n\n \n\nnec penetrare et abire in corpus corpore toto;\n\n \n\nnam facere inter dum velle et certare videntur.\n\n \n\nusque adeo cupide in Veneris compagibus haerent,\n\n \n\nmembra voluptatis dum vi labefacta liquescunt.\n\n \n\ntandem ubi se erupit nervis coniecta cupido,\n\n \n\nparva fit ardoris violenti pausa parumper.\n\n \n\ninde redit rabies eadem et furor ille revisit,\n\n \n\ncum sibi quod cupiant ipsi contingere quaerunt,\n\n \n\nnec reperire malum id possunt quae machina vincat.\n\n \n\nusque adeo incerti tabescunt volnere caeco.\n\n  \n\nAdde quod absumunt viris pereuntque labore,\n\n \n\nadde quod alterius sub nutu degitur aetas,\n\n \n\nlanguent officia atque aegrotat fama vacillans.\n\n \n\nlabitur interea res et Babylonia fiunt\n\n \n\nunguenta et pulchra in pedibus Sicyonia rident,\n\n \n\nscilicet et grandes viridi cum luce zmaragdi\n\n \n\nauro includuntur teriturque thalassina vestis\n\n \n\nadsidue et Veneris sudorem exercita potat.\n\n \n\net bene parta patrum fiunt anademata, mitrae,\n\n \n\ninter dum in pallam atque Alidensia Ciaque vertunt.\n\n \n\neximia veste et victu convivia, ludi,\n\n \n\npocula crebra, unguenta, coronae, serta parantur,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam medio de fonte leporum\n\n \n\nsurgit amari aliquid, quod in ipsis floribus angat,\n\n \n\naut cum conscius ipse animus se forte remordet\n\n \n\ndesidiose agere aetatem lustrisque perire,\n\n \n\naut quod in ambiguo verbum iaculata reliquit,\n\n \n\nquod cupido adfixum cordi vivescit ut ignis,\n\n \n\naut nimium iactare oculos aliumve tueri\n\n \n\nquod putat in voltuque videt vestigia risus.\n\n  \n\nAtque in amore mala haec proprio summeque secundo\n\n \n\ninveniuntur; in adverso vero atque inopi sunt,\n\n \n\nprendere quae possis oculorum lumine operto.\n\n \n\ninnumerabilia; ut melius vigilare sit ante,\n\n \n\nqua docui ratione, cavereque, ne inliciaris.\n\n \n\nnam vitare, plagas in amoris ne iaciamur,\n\n \n\nnon ita difficile est quam captum retibus ipsis\n\n \n\nexire et validos Veneris perrumpere nodos.\n\n \n\net tamen implicitus quoque possis inque peditus\n\n \n\n effugere infestum, nisi tute tibi obvius obstes\n\n \n\net praetermittas animi vitia omnia primum\n\n \n\naut quae corporis sunt eius, quam praepetis ac vis.\n\n \n\nnam faciunt homines plerumque cupidine caeci\n\n \n\net tribuunt ea quae non sunt his commoda vere.\n\n \n\nmultimodis igitur pravas turpisque videmus\n\n \n\nesse in deliciis summoque in honore vigere.\n\n \n\natque alios alii inrident Veneremque suadent\n\n \n\nut placent, quoniam foedo adflictentur amore,\n\n \n\nnec sua respiciunt miseri mala maxima saepe.\n\n \n\nnigra melichrus est, inmunda et fetida acosmos,\n\n \n\ncaesia Palladium, nervosa et lignea dorcas,\n\n \n\nparvula, pumilio, chariton mia, tota merum sal,\n\n \n\nmagna atque inmanis cataplexis plenaque honoris.\n\n \n\nbalba loqui non quit, traulizi, muta pudens est;\n\n \n\nat flagrans, odiosa, loquacula Lampadium fit.\n\n \n\nischnon eromenion tum fit, cum vivere non quit\n\n \n\nprae macie; rhadine verost iam mortua tussi.\n\n \n\nat nimia et mammosa Ceres est ipsa ab Iaccho,\n\n \n\nsimula Silena ac Saturast, labeosa philema.\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc longum est si dicere coner.\n\n \n\nsed tamen esto iam quantovis oris honore,\n\n \n\ncui Veneris membris vis omnibus exoriatur;\n\n \n\nnempe aliae quoque sunt; nempe hac sine viximus ante;\n\n \n\nnempe eadem facit et scimus facere omnia turpi\n\n \n\net miseram taetris se suffit odoribus ipsa,\n\n \n\nquam famulae longe fugitant furtimque cachinnant.\n\n \n\nat lacrimans exclusus amator limina saepe\n\n \n\nfloribus et sertis operit postisque superbos\n\n \n\nunguit amaracino et foribus miser oscula figit;\n\n \n\nquem si iam ammissum venientem offenderit aura\n\n \n\nuna modo, causas abeundi quaerat honestas\n\n \n\net meditata diu cadat alte sumpta querella\n\n \n\nstultitiaque ibi se damnet, tribuisse quod illi\n\n \n\nplus videat quam mortali concedere par est.\n\n \n\nnec Veneres nostras hoc fallit; quo magis ipsae\n\n \n\nomnia summo opere hos vitae poscaenia celant,\n\n \n\nquos retinere volunt adstrictosque esse in amore,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam tu animo tamen omnia possis\n\n \n\nprotrahere in lucem atque omnis inquirere risus\n\n \n\net, si bello animost et non odiosa, vicissim\n\n \n\npraetermittere  humanis concedere rebus.\n\n  \n\nNec mulier semper ficto suspirat amore,\n\n \n\nquae conplexa viri corpus cum corpore iungit\n\n \n\net tenet adsuctis umectans oscula labris;\n\n \n\nnam facit ex animo saepe et communia quaerens\n\n \n\ngaudia sollicitat spatium decurrere amoris.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia volucres armenta feraeque\n\n \n\net pecudes et equae maribus subsidere possent,\n\n \n\nsi non, ipsa quod illarum subat, ardet abundans\n\n \n\nnatura et Venerem salientum laeta retractat.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam quos mutua saepe voluptas\n\n \n\nvinxit, ut in vinclis communibus excrucientur,\n\n \n\nin triviis cum saepe canes discedere aventis\n\n \n\ndivorsi cupide summis ex viribus tendunt,\n\n \n\n quom interea validis Veneris compagibus haerent?\n\n \n\nquod facerent numquam, nisi mutua gaudia nossent,\n\n \n\nquae iacere in fraudem possent vinctosque tenere.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam, ut dico, est communis voluptas.\n\n  \n\nEt commiscendo quom semine forte virilem\n\n \n\nfemina vim vicit subita vi corripuitque,\n\n \n\ntum similes matrum materno semine fiunt,\n\n \n\nut patribus patrio. sed quos utriusque figurae\n\n \n\nesse vides, iuxtim miscentes vulta parentum,\n\n \n\ncorpore de patrio et materno sanguine crescunt,\n\n \n\nsemina cum Veneris stimulis excita per artus\n\n \n\nobvia conflixit conspirans mutuus ardor,\n\n \n\net neque utrum superavit eorum nec superatumst.\n\n \n\nfit quoque ut inter dum similes existere avorum\n\n \n\npossint et referant proavorum saepe figuras,\n\n \n\npropterea quia multa modis primordia multis\n\n \n\nmixta suo celant in corpore saepe parentis,\n\n \n\nquae patribus patres tradunt a stirpe profecta.\n\n \n\ninde Venus varia producit sorte figuras,\n\n \n\nmaiorumque refert voltus vocesque comasque;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem nihilo magis haec  semine certo\n\n \n\nfiunt quam facies et corpora membraque nobis.\n\n \n\net muliebre oritur patrio de semine saeclum\n\n \n\nmaternoque mares existunt corpore creti;\n\n \n\nsemper enim partus duplici de semine constat,\n\n \n\natque utri similest magis id quod cumque creatur,\n\n \n\neius habet plus parte aequa; quod cernere possis,\n\n \n\nsive virum suboles sivest muliebris origo.\n\n  \n\nNec divina satum genitalem numina cuiquam\n\n \n\nabsterrent, pater a gnatis ne dulcibus umquam\n\n \n\nappelletur et ut sterili Venere exigat aevom;\n\n \n\nquod plerumque putant et multo sanguine maesti\n\n \n\nconspergunt aras adolentque altaria donis,\n\n \n\nut gravidas reddant uxores semine largo;\n\n \n\nne quiquam divom numen sortisque fatigant;\n\n \n\nnam steriles nimium crasso sunt semine partim,\n\n \n\net liquido praeter iustum tenuique vicissim.\n\n \n\ntenve locis quia non potis est adfigere adhaesum,\n\n \n\nliquitur extemplo et revocatum cedit abortu.\n\n \n\ncrassius hinc porro quoniam concretius aequo\n\n \n\nmittitur, aut non tam prolixo provolat ictu\n\n \n\naut penetrare locos aeque nequit aut penetratum\n\n \n\naegre admiscetur muliebri semine semen.\n\n \n\nnam multum harmoniae Veneris differre videntur.\n\n \n\natque alias alii complent magis ex aliisque\n\n \n\nsuccipiunt aliae pondus magis inque gravescunt.\n\n \n\net multae steriles Hymenaeis ante fuerunt\n\n \n\npluribus et nactae post sunt tamen unde puellos\n\n \n\nsuscipere et partu possent ditescere dulci.\n\n \n\net quibus ante domi fecundae saepe nequissent\n\n \n\nuxoris parere, inventast illis quoque compar\n\n \n\nnatura, ut possent gnatis munire senectam.\n\n \n\nusque adeo magni refert, ut semina possint\n\n \n\nseminibus commisceri genitaliter apta\n\n \n\ncrassaque conveniant liquidis et liquida crassis.\n\n \n\n atque in eo refert quo victu vita colatur;\n\n \n\nnamque aliis rebus concrescunt semina membris\n\n \n\natque aliis extenvantur tabentque vicissim.\n\n \n\net quibus ipsa modis tractetur blanda voluptas.\n\n \n\nid quoque permagni refert; nam more ferarum\n\n \n\nquadrupedumque magis ritu plerumque putantur\n\n \n\nconcipere uxores, quia sic loca sumere possunt\n\n \n\npectoribus positis sublatis semina lumbis.\n\n \n\nnec molles opus sunt motus uxoribus hilum.\n\n \n\nnam mulier prohibet se concipere atque repugnat,\n\n \n\nclunibus ipsa viri Venerem si laeta retractat\n\n \n\natque exossato ciet omni pectore fluctus;\n\n \n\neicit enim sulcum recta regione viaque\n\n \n\nvomeris atque locis avertit seminis ictum.\n\n \n\nidque sua causa consuerunt scorta moveri,\n\n \n\nne complerentur crebro gravidaeque iacerent,\n\n \n\net simul ipsa viris Venus ut concinnior esset;\n\n \n\nconiugibus quod nil nostris opus esse videtur.\n\n  \n\nNec divinitus inter dum Venerisque sagittis\n\n \n\ndeteriore fit ut forma muliercula ametur;\n\n \n\nnam facit ipsa suis inter dum femina factis\n\n \n\nmorigerisque modis et munde corpore culto,\n\n \n\nut facile insuescat secum  degere vitam.\n\n \n\nquod super est, consuetudo concinnat amorem;\n\n \n\nnam leviter quamvis quod crebro tunditur ictu,\n\n \n\nvincitur in longo spatio tamen atque labascit.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam guttas in saxa cadentis\n\n \n\numoris longo in spatio pertundere saxa?","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0001","text":"## Liber Quartus\n\n  \n\nAvia Pieridum peragro loca nullius ante\n\n \n\ntrita solo. iuvat integros accedere fontis\n\n \n\natque haurire, iuvatque novos decerpere flores\n\n \n\ninsignemque meo capiti petere inde coronam,\n\n \n\nunde prius nulli velarint tempora musae;\n\n \n\nprimum quod magnis doceo de rebus et artis\n\n \n\nreligionum animum nodis exsolvere pergo,\n\n \n\ndeinde quod obscura de re tam lucida pango\n\n \n\ncarmina musaeo contingens cuncta lepore.\n\n \n\nid quoque enim non ab nulla ratione videtur;\n\n \n\nnam vel uti pueris absinthia taetra medentes\n\n \n\ncum dare conantur, prius oras pocula circum\n\n \n\ncontingunt mellis dulci flavoque liquore,\n\n \n\nut puerorum aetas inprovida ludificetur\n\n \n\nlabrorum tenus, interea perpotet amarum\n\n \n\nabsinthi laticem deceptaque non capiatur,\n\n \n\nsed potius tali facto recreata valescat,\n\n \n\nsic ego nunc, quoniam haec ratio plerumque videtur\n\n \n\ntristior esse quibus non est tractata, retroque\n\n \n\nvolgus abhorret ab hac, volui tibi suaviloquenti\n\n \n\ncarmine Pierio rationem exponere nostram\n\n \n\net quasi musaeo dulci contingere melle;\n\n \n\nsi tibi forte animum tali ratione tenere\n\n \n\nversibus in nostris possem, dum percipis omnem\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum ac persentis utilitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","text":"Sed quoniam docui cunctarum exordia rerum\n\n \n\nqualia sint et quam variis distantia formis\n\n \n\nsponte sua volitent aeterno percita motu\n\n \n\nquoque modo possit res ex his quaeque creari,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\natque animi quoniam docui natura quid esset\n\n \n\net quibus e rebus cum corpore compta vigeret\n\n \n\nquove modo distracta rediret in ordia prima,\n\n \n\nnunc agere incipiam tibi, quod vehementer ad has res\n\n \n\nattinet esse ea quae rerum simulacra vocamus,\n\n \n\nquod speciem ac formam similem gerit eius imago,\n\n \n\ncuius cumque cluet de corpore fusa vagari;\n\n \n\nquae quasi membranae summo de corpore rerum\n\n \n\ndereptae volitant ultroque citroque per auras,\n\n \n\natque eadem nobis vigilantibus obvia mentes\n\n \n\nterrificant atque in somnis, cum saepe figuras\n\n \n\ncontuimur miras simulacraque luce carentum,\n\n \n\nquae nos horrifice languentis saepe sopore\n\n \n\nexcierunt ne forte animas Acherunte reamur\n\n \n\neffugere aut umbras inter vivos volitare\n\n \n\nneve aliquid nostri post mortem posse relinqui,\n\n \n\ncum corpus simul atque animi natura perempta\n\n \n\nin sua discessum dederint primordia quaeque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0003","text":"dico igitur rerum effigias tenuisque figuras\n\n \n\nmittier ab rebus summo de cortice eorum;\n\n \n\nid licet hinc quamvis hebeti cognoscere corde.\n\n  \n\n Principio quoniam mittunt in rebus apertis\n\n \n\ncorpora res multae, partim diffusa solute,\n\n \n\nrobora ceu fumum mittunt ignesque vaporem,\n\n \n\net partim contexta magis condensaque, ut olim\n\n \n\ncum teretis ponunt tunicas aestate cicadae,\n\n \n\net vituli cum membranas de corpore summo\n\n \n\nnascentes mittunt, et item cum lubrica serpens\n\n \n\nexuit in spinis vestem; nam saepe videmus\n\n \n\nillorum spoliis vepres volitantibus auctas.\n\n \n\nquae quoniam fiunt, tenuis quoque debet imago\n\n \n\nab rebus mitti summo de corpore rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0004","text":"nam cur illa cadant magis ab rebusque recedant\n\n \n\nquam quae tenvia sunt, hiscendist nulla potestas;\n\n \n\npraesertim cum sint in summis corpora rebus\n\n \n\nmulta minuta, iaci quae possint ordine eodem\n\n \n\nquo fuerint et formai servare figuram,\n\n \n\net multo citius, quanto minus indupediri\n\n \n\npauca queunt et  sunt prima fronte locata.\n\n \n\nnam certe iacere ac largiri multa videmus,\n\n \n\nnon solum ex alto penitusque, ut diximus ante,\n\n \n\nverum de summis ipsum quoque saepe colorem.\n\n \n\net volgo faciunt id lutea russaque vela\n\n \n\net ferrugina, cum magnis intenta theatris\n\n \n\nper malos volgata trabesque trementia flutant;\n\n \n\nnamque ibi consessum caveai supter et omnem\n\n \n\nscaenai speciem patrum matrumque deorsum\n\n \n\ninficiunt coguntque suo fluitare colore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0005","text":"et quanto circum mage sunt inclusa theatri\n\n \n\nmoenia, tam magis haec intus perfusa lepore\n\n \n\nomnia conrident correpta luce diei.\n\n \n\nergo lintea de summo cum corpore fucum\n\n \n\nmittunt, effigias quoque debent mittere tenvis\n\n \n\nres quaeque, ex summo quoniam iaculantur utraque.\n\n \n\nsunt igitur iam formarum vestigia certa,\n\n \n\nquae volgo volitant subtili praedita filo\n\n \n\nnec singillatim possunt secreta videri.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea omnis odor fumus vapor atque aliae res\n\n \n\nconsimiles ideo diffusae rebus abundant,\n\n \n\nex alto quia dum veniunt extrinsecus ortae\n\n \n\nscinduntur per iter flexum, nec recta viarum\n\n \n\nostia sunt, qua contendant exire coortae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","text":"at contra tenuis summi membrana coloris\n\n \n\ncum iacitur, nihil est quod eam discerpere possit,\n\n \n\nin promptu quoniam est in prima fronte locata.\n\n \n\nPostremo speculis in aqua splendoreque in omni\n\n \n\nquae cumque apparent nobis simulacra, necessest,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem simili specie sunt praedita rerum,\n\n \n\nexin imaginibus missis consistere eorum.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nsunt igitur tenues formarum illis similesque\n\n \n\neffigiae, singillatim quas cernere nemo\n\n \n\ncum possit, tamen adsiduo crebroque repulsu\n\n \n\nreiectae reddunt speculorum ex aequore visum,\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia servari posse videntur,\n\n \n\n tanto opere ut similes reddantur cuique figurae.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quam tenui natura constet imago\n\n \n\npercipe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0007","text":"et in primis, quoniam primordia tantum\n\n \n\nsunt infra nostros sensus tantoque minora\n\n \n\nquam quae primum oculi coeptant non posse tueri,\n\n \n\nnunc tamen id quoque uti confirmem, exordia rerum\n\n \n\ncunctarum quam sint subtilia percipe paucis.\n\n \n\nprimum animalia sunt iam partim tantula, corum\n\n \n\ntertia pars nulla possit ratione videri.\n\n \n\nhorum intestinum quodvis quale esse putandumst!\n\n \n\nquid cordis globus aut oculi? quid membra? quid artus?\n\n \n\nquantula sunt! quid praeterea primordia quaeque,\n\n \n\nunde anima atque animi constet natura necessumst,\n\n \n\nnonne vides quam sint subtilia quamque minuta?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0008","text":"praeterea quaecumque suo de corpore odorem\n\n \n\nexpirant acrem, panaces absinthia taetra\n\n \n\nhabrotonique graves et tristia centaurea,\n\n \n\nquorum unum quidvis leviter si forte duobus\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nquin potius noscas rerum simulacra vagari\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis, nulla vi cassaque sensu?\n\n \n\nSed ne forte putes ea demum sola vagari,\n\n \n\nquae cumque ab rebus rerum simulacra recedunt,\n\n \n\nsunt etiam quae sponte sua gignuntur et ipsa\n\n \n\nconstituuntur in hoc caelo, qui dicitur aer,\n\n \n\nquae multis formata modis sublime feruntur,\n\n \n\nut nubes facile inter dum concrescere in alto\n\n \n\ncernimus et mundi speciem violare serenam\n\n \n\naëra mulcentes motu; nam saepe Gigantum\n\n \n\nora volare videntur et umbram ducere late,\n\n \n\ninter dum magni montes avolsaque saxa\n\n \n\nmontibus ante ire et solem succedere praeter,\n\n \n\ninde alios trahere atque inducere belua nimbos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0009","text":"nec speciem mutare suam liquentia cessant\n\n \n\net cuiusque modi formarum vertere in oras.\n\n  \n\nNunc ea quam facili et celeri ratione genantur\n\n \n\nperpetuoque fluant ab rebus lapsaque cedant\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nsemper enim summum quicquid de rebus abundat,\n\n \n\nquod iaculentur. et hoc alias cum pervenit in res,\n\n \n\ntransit, ut in primis vestem; sed ubi aspera saxa\n\n \n\naut in materiam ligni pervenit, ibi iam\n\n \n\nscinditur, ut nullum simulacrum reddere possit.\n\n \n\nat cum splendida quae constant opposta fuerunt\n\n \n\ndensaque, ut in primis speculum est, nihil accidit horum;\n\n \n\nnam neque, uti vestem, possunt transire, neque autem\n\n \n\nscindi; quam meminit levor praestare salutem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0010","text":"qua propter fit ut hinc nobis simulacra redundent.\n\n \n\net quamvis subito quovis in tempore quamque\n\n \n\nrem contra speculum ponas, apparet imago;\n\n \n\nperpetuo fluere ut noscas e corpore summo\n\n \n\ntexturas rerum tenuis tenuisque figuras.\n\n \n\nergo multa brevi spatio simulacra genuntur,\n\n \n\nut merito celer his rebus dicatur origo.\n\n \n\net quasi multa brevi spatio summittere debet\n\n \n\n lumina sol, ut perpetuo sint omnia plena,\n\n \n\nsic ab rebus item simili ratione necessest\n\n \n\ntemporis in puncto rerum simulacra ferantur\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis in cunctas undique partis;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem speculum quo cumque obvertimus oris,\n\n \n\nres ibi respondent simili forma atque colore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0011","text":"Praeterea modo cum fuerit liquidissima caeli\n\n \n\ntempestas, perquam subito fit turbida foede,\n\n \n\nundique uti tenebras omnis Acherunta rearis\n\n \n\nliquisse et magnas caeli complesse cavernas.\n\n \n\nusque adeo taetra nimborum nocte coorta\n\n \n\ninpendent atrae Formidinis ora superne;\n\n \n\nquorum quantula pars sit imago dicere nemost\n\n \n\nqui possit neque eam rationem reddere dictis.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quam celeri motu simulacra ferantur,\n\n \n\net quae mobilitas ollis tranantibus auras\n\n \n\nreddita sit, longo spatio ut brevis hora teratur,\n\n \n\nin quem quaeque locum diverso numine tendunt,\n\n \n\nsuavidicis potius quam multis versibus edam;\n\n \n\nparvus ut est cycni melior canor, ille gruum quam\n\n \n\nclamor in aetheriis dispersus nubibus austri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0012","text":"Principio persaepe levis res atque minutis\n\n \n\ncorporibus factas celeris licet esse videre.\n\n \n\nin quo iam genere est solis lux et vapor eius,\n\n \n\npropterea quia sunt e primis facta minutis,\n\n \n\nquae quasi cuduntur perque aëris intervallum\n\n \n\nnon dubitant transire sequenti concita plaga;\n\n \n\nsuppeditatur enim confestim lumine lumen\n\n \n\net quasi protelo stimulatur fulgere fulgur.\n\n \n\nqua propter simulacra pari ratione necessest\n\n \n\ninmemorabile per spatium transcurrere posse\n\n \n\ntemporis in puncto, primum quod parvola causa\n\n \n\nest procul a tergo quae provehat atque propellat,\n\n \n\nquod super est, ubi tam volucri levitate ferantur,\n\n \n\ndeinde quod usque adeo textura praedita rara\n\n \n\nmittuntur, facile ut quasvis penetrare queant res\n\n \n\net quasi permanare per aëris intervallum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0013","text":"Praeterea si quae penitus corpuscula rerum\n\n \n\nex altoque foras mittuntur, solis uti lux\n\n \n\nac vapor, haec puncto cernuntur lapsa diei\n\n \n\nper totum caeli spatium diffundere sese\n\n \n\nperque volare mare ac terras caelumque rigare.\n\n \n\nquid quae sunt igitur iam prima fronte parata,\n\n \n\ncum iaciuntur et emissum res nulla moratur?\n\n \n\nquone vides citius debere et longius ire\n\n \n\nmultiplexque loci spatium transcurrere eodem\n\n \n\ntempore quo solis pervolgant lumina caelum?\n\n \n\nHoc etiam in primis specimen verum esse videtur,\n\n \n\nquam celeri motu rerum simulacra ferantur,\n\n \n\nquod simul ac primum sub diu splendor aquai\n\n \n\nponitur, extemplo caelo stellante serena\n\n \n\nsidera respondent in aqua radiantia mundi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","text":"iamne vides igitur quam puncto tempore imago\n\n \n\naetheris ex oris in terrarum accidat oras?\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam mitti fateare necessest\n\n  \n\n corpora quae feriant oculos visumque lacessant.\n\n \n\nperpetuoque fluunt certis ab rebus odores,\n\n \n\nfrigus ut a fluviis, calor ab sole, aestus ab undis\n\n \n\naequoris, exesor moerorum litora circum,\n\n \n\nnec variae cessant voces volitare per auras.\n\n \n\ndenique in os salsi venit umor saepe saporis,\n\n \n\ncum mare versamur propter, dilutaque contra\n\n \n\ncum tuimur misceri absinthia, tangit amaror.\n\n \n\nusque adeo omnibus ab rebus res quaeque fluenter\n\n \n\nfertur et in cunctas dimittitur undique partis\n\n \n\nnec mora nec requies interdatur ulla fluendi,\n\n \n\nperpetuo quoniam sentimus et omnia semper\n\n \n\ncernere odorari licet et sentire sonare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","text":"Praeterea quoniam manibus tractata figura\n\n \n\nin tenebris quaedam cognoscitur esse eadem quae\n\n \n\ncernitur in luce et claro candore, necessest\n\n \n\nconsimili causa tactum visumque moveri.\n\n \n\nnunc igitur si quadratum temptamus et id nos\n\n \n\ncommovet in tenebris, in luci quae poterit res\n\n \n\naccidere ad speciem quadrata, nisi eius imago?\n\n \n\nesse in imaginibus qua propter causa videtur\n\n \n\ncernundi neque posse sine his res ulla videri.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quae dico rerum simulacra feruntur\n\n \n\nundique et in cunctas iaciuntur didita partis;\n\n \n\nverum nos oculis quia solis cernere quimus,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti, speciem quo vertimus, omnes\n\n \n\nres ibi eam contra feriant forma atque colore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0016","text":"et quantum quaeque ab nobis res absit, imago\n\n \n\nefficit ut videamus et internoscere curat;\n\n \n\nnam cum mittitur, extemplo protrudit agitque\n\n \n\naëra qui inter se cumque est oculosque locatus,\n\n \n\nisque ita per nostras acies perlabitur omnis\n\n \n\net quasi perterget pupillas atque ita transit.\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti videamus quam procul absit\n\n \n\nres quaeque. et quanto plus aëris ante agitatur\n\n \n\net nostros oculos perterget longior aura,\n\n \n\ntam procul esse magis res quaeque remota videtur.\n\n \n\nscilicet haec summe celeri ratione geruntur,\n\n \n\nquale sit ut videamus, et una quam procul absit.\n\n \n\nIllud in his rebus minime mirabile habendumst,\n\n \n\ncur, ea quae feriant oculos simulacra videri\n\n \n\nsingula cum nequeant, res ipsae perspiciantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0017","text":"ventus enim quoque paulatim cum verberat et cum\n\n \n\nacre fluit frigus, non privam quamque solemus\n\n \n\nparticulam venti sentire et frigoris eius,\n\n \n\nsed magis unorsum, fierique perinde videmus\n\n \n\ncorpore tum plagas in nostro tam quam aliquae res\n\n \n\nverberet atque sui det sensum corporis extra.\n\n \n\npraeterea lapidem digito cum tundimus, ipsum\n\n \n\ntangimus extremum saxi summumque colorem\n\n \n\nnec sentimus eum tactu, verum magis ipsam\n\n \n\nduritiem penitus saxi sentimus in alto.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, cur ultra speculum videatur imago\n\n \n\npercipe: nam certe penitus remmota videtur.\n\n \n\nquod genus illa foris quae vere transpiciuntur,\n\n \n\n ianua cum per se transpectum praebet apertum,\n\n \n\nmulta facitque foris ex aedibus ut videantur;\n\n \n\nis quoque enim duplici geminoque fit aëre visus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0018","text":"primus enim citra postes tum cernitur aër,\n\n \n\ninde fores ipsae dextra laevaque secuntur,\n\n \n\npost extraria lux oculos perterget et aër\n\n \n\nalter, et illa foris quae vere transpiciuntur.\n\n \n\nsic ubi se primum speculi proiecit imago,\n\n \n\ndum venit ad nostras acies, protrudit agitque\n\n \n\naëra qui inter se cumquest oculosque locatus,\n\n \n\net facit, ut prius hunc omnem sentire queamus\n\n \n\nquam speculum; sed ubi  speculum quoque sensimus ipsum,\n\n \n\ncontinuo a nobis in eum quae fertur imago\n\n \n\npervenit, et nostros oculos reiecta revisit\n\n \n\natque alium prae se propellens aëra volvit,\n\n \n\net facit ut prius hunc quam se videamus, eoque\n\n \n\ndistare ab speculo tantum semota videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0019","text":"quare etiam atque etiam minime mirarier est par\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nillis quae reddunt speculorum ex aequore visum,\n\n \n\naëribus binis quoniam res confit utraque.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quae nobis membrorum dextera pars est,\n\n \n\nin speculis fit ut in laeva videatur eo quod\n\n \n\nplanitiem ad speculi veniens cum offendit imago,\n\n \n\nnon convertitur incolumis, sed recta retrorsum\n\n \n\nsic eliditur, ut siquis, prius arida quam sit\n\n \n\ncretea persona, adlidat pilaeve trabive,\n\n \n\natque ea continuo rectam si fronte figuram\n\n \n\nservet et elisam retro sese exprimat ipsa.\n\n \n\nfiet ut, ante oculus fuerit qui dexter, ut idem\n\n \n\nnunc sit laevus et e laevo sit mutua dexter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0020","text":"Fit quoque de speculo in speculum ut tradatur imago,\n\n \n\nquinque etiam  sex ut fieri simulacra suërint.\n\n \n\nnam quae cumque retro parte interiore latebunt,\n\n \n\ninde tamen, quamvis torte penitusque remota,\n\n \n\nomnia per flexos aditus educta licebit\n\n \n\npluribus haec speculis videantur in aedibus esse.\n\n \n\nusque adeo speculo in speculum translucet imago,\n\n \n\net cum laeva data est, fit rusum ut dextera fiat,\n\n \n\ninde retro rursum redit et convertit eodem.\n\n \n\nQuin etiam quae cumque latuscula sunt speculorum\n\n \n\nadsimili lateris flexura praedita nostri,\n\n \n\ndextera ea propter nobis simulacra remittunt,\n\n \n\naut quia de speculo in speculum transfertur imago,\n\n \n\ninde ad nos elisa bis advolat, aut etiam quod\n\n \n\ncircum agitur, cum venit, imago propterea quod\n\n \n\nflexa figura docet speculi convertier ad nos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0021","text":"Indugredi porro pariter simulacra pedemque\n\n \n\nponere nobiscum credas gestumque imitari\n\n \n\npropterea quia, de speculi qua parte recedas,\n\n \n\ncontinuo nequeunt illinc simulacra reverti;\n\n \n\nomnia quandoquidem cogit natura referri\n\n \n\nac resilire ab rebus ad aequos reddita flexus.\n\n \n\nSplendida porro oculi fugitant vitantque tueri.\n\n \n\n sol etiam caecat, contra si tendere pergas,\n\n \n\npropterea quia vis magnast ipsius et alte\n\n \n\naëra per purum simulacra feruntur\n\n \n\net feriunt oculos turbantia composituras.\n\n \n\nPraeterea splendor qui cumque est acer adurit\n\n \n\nsaepe oculos ideo quod semina possidet ignis\n\n \n\nmulta, dolorem oculis quae gignunt insinuando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0022","text":"lurida praeterea fiunt quae cumque tuentur\n\n \n\narquati, quia luroris de corpore eorum\n\n \n\nsemina multa fluunt simulacris obvia rerum,\n\n \n\nmultaque sunt oculis in eorum denique mixta,\n\n \n\nquae contage sua palloribus omnia pingunt.\n\n  \n\nE tenebris autem quae sunt in luce tuemur\n\n \n\npropterea quia, cum propior caliginis aër\n\n \n\nater init oculos prior et possedit apertos,\n\n \n\ninsequitur candens confestim lucidus aër,\n\n \n\nqui quasi purgat eos ac nigras discutit umbras\n\n \n\naëris illius; nam multis partibus hic est\n\n \n\nmobilior multisque minutior et mage pollens.\n\n \n\nqui simul atque vias oculorum luce replevit\n\n \n\natque pate fecit, quas ante obsederat aër\n\n \n\nATER, continuo rerum simulacra secuntur,\n\n \n\nquae sita sunt in luce, lacessuntque ut videamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0023","text":"quod contra facere in tenebris e luce nequimus\n\n \n\npropterea quia posterior caliginis aër\n\n \n\ncrassior insequitur, qui cuncta foramina complet\n\n \n\nobsiditque vias oculorum, ne simulacra\n\n \n\npossint ullarum rerum coniecta moveri.\n\n \n\nQuadratasque procul turris cum cernimus urbis,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti videantur saepe rutundae,\n\n \n\nangulus optusus quia longe cernitur omnis\n\n \n\nsive etiam potius non cernitur ac perit eius\n\n \n\nplaga nec ad nostras acies perlabitur ictus,\n\n \n\naëra per multum quia dum simulacra feruntur,\n\n \n\ncogit hebescere eum crebris offensibus aër.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi suffugit sensum simul angulus omnis.\n\n \n\nfit quasi ut ad turnum saxorum structa tuantur;\n\n \n\nnon tamen ut coram quae sunt vereque rutunda,\n\n \n\nsed quasi adumbratim paulum simulata videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0024","text":"Umbra videtur item nobis in sole moveri\n\n \n\net vestigia nostra sequi gestumque imitari,\n\n \n\naëra si credis privatum lumine posse\n\n \n\nindugredi, motus hominum gestumque sequentem;\n\n \n\nnam nihil esse potest aliud nisi lumine cassus\n\n \n\naër id quod nos umbram perhibere suëmus.\n\n \n\nni mirum, quia terra locis ex ordine certis\n\n \n\nlumine privatur solis qua cumque meantes\n\n \n\nofficimus, repletur item quod liquimus eius,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti videatur, quae fuit umbra\n\n \n\ncorporis, e regione eadem nos usque secuta.\n\n \n\nsemper enim nova se radiorum lumina fundunt\n\n \n\nprimaque dispereunt, quasi in ignem lana trahatur.\n\n \n\npropterea facile et spoliatur lumine terra\n\n \n\net repletur item nigrasque sibi abluit umbras.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","text":"Nec tamen hic oculos falli concedimus hilum.\n\n \n\n nam quo cumque loco sit lux atque umbra tueri\n\n \n\nillorum est; eadem vero sint lumina necne,\n\n \n\numbraque quae fuit hic eadem nunc transeat illuc,\n\n \n\nan potius fiat paulo quod diximus ante,\n\n \n\nhoc animi demum ratio discernere debet,\n\n \n\nnec possunt oculi naturam noscere rerum.\n\n \n\nproinde animi vitium hoc oculis adfingere noli.\n\n \n\nQua vehimur navi, fertur, cum stare videtur;\n\n \n\nquae manet in statione, ea praeter creditur ire.\n\n \n\net fugere ad puppim colles campique videntur,\n\n \n\nquos agimus praeter navem velisque volamus.\n\n \n\nSidera cessare aetheriis adfixa cavernis\n\n \n\ncuncta videntur, et adsiduo sunt omnia motu,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem longos obitus exorta revisunt,\n\n \n\ncum permensa suo sunt caelum corpore claro.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0026","text":"solque pari ratione manere et luna videtur\n\n \n\nin statione, ea quae ferri res indicat ipsa.\n\n \n\nExstantisque procul medio de gurgite montis\n\n \n\nclassibus inter quos liber patet exitus ingens,\n\n \n\ninsula coniunctis tamen ex his una videtur.\n\n \n\natria versari et circum cursare columnae\n\n \n\nusque adeo fit uti pueris videantur, ubi ipsi\n\n \n\ndesierunt verti, vix ut iam credere possint\n\n \n\nnon supra sese ruere omnia tecta minari.\n\n \n\nIamque rubrum tremulis iubar ignibus erigere alte\n\n \n\ncum coeptat natura supraque extollere montes,\n\n \n\nquos tibi tum supra sol montis esse videtur\n\n \n\ncomminus ipse suo contingens fervidus igni,\n\n \n\nvix absunt nobis missus bis mille sagittae,\n\n \n\nvix etiam cursus quingentos saepe veruti;\n\n \n\ninter eos solemque iacent immania ponti\n\n \n\naequora substrata aetheriis ingentibus oris,\n\n \n\ninteriectaque sunt terrarum milia multa,\n\n \n\nquae variae retinent gentes et saecla ferarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0027","text":"At coniectus aquae digitum non altior unum,\n\n \n\nqui lapides inter sistit per strata viarum,\n\n \n\ndespectum praebet sub terras inpete tanto,\n\n \n\na terris quantum caeli patet altus hiatus,\n\n \n\nnubila despicere et caelum ut videare videre,\n\n \n\ncorpora mirande sub terras abdita caelo.\n\n \n\nDenique ubi in medio nobis ecus acer obhaesit\n\n \n\nflumine et in rapidas amnis despeximus undas,\n\n \n\nstantis equi corpus transversum ferre videtur\n\n \n\nvis et in adversum flumen contrudere raptim,\n\n \n\net quo cumque oculos traiecimus omnia ferri\n\n \n\net fluere adsimili nobis ratione videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0028","text":"Porticus aequali quamvis est denique ductu\n\n \n\nstansque in perpetuum paribus suffulta columnis,\n\n \n\nlonga tamen parte ab summa cum tota videtur,\n\n \n\npaulatim trahit angusti fastigia coni,\n\n \n\ntecta solo iungens atque omnia dextera laevis\n\n \n\ndonec in obscurum coni conduxit acumen.\n\n \n\nIn pelago nautis ex undis ortus in undis\n\n \n\nsol fit uti videatur obire et condere lumen;\n\n \n\nquippe ubi nil aliud nisi aquam caelumque tuentur;\n\n \n\n ne leviter credas labefactari undique sensus.\n\n \n\nat maris ignaris in portu clauda videntur\n\n \n\nnavigia aplustris fractis obnitier undis.\n\n \n\nnam quae cumque supra rorem salis edita pars est\n\n \n\nremorum, recta est, et recta superne guberna;\n\n \n\nquae demersa liquore obeunt, refracta videntur\n\n \n\nomnia converti sursumque supina reverti\n\n \n\net reflexa prope in summo fluitare liquore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0029","text":"Raraque per caelum cum venti nubila portant\n\n \n\ntempore nocturno, tum splendida signa videntur\n\n \n\nlabier adversum nimbos atque ire superne\n\n \n\nlonge aliam in partem ac vera ratione feruntur\n\n  \n\nAt si forte oculo manus uni subdita supter\n\n \n\npressit eum, quodam sensu fit uti videantur\n\n \n\nomnia quae tuimur fieri tum bina tuendo,\n\n \n\nbina lucernarum florentia lumina flammis\n\n \n\nbinaque per totas aedis geminare supellex\n\n \n\net duplicis hominum facies et corpora bina.\n\n \n\nDenique cum suavi devinxit membra sopore\n\n \n\nsomnus et in summa corpus iacet omne quiete,\n\n \n\ntum vigilare tamen nobis et membra movere\n\n \n\nnostra videmur, et in noctis caligine caeca\n\n \n\ncernere censemus solem lumenque diurnum,\n\n \n\nconclusoque loco caelum mare flumina montis\n\n \n\nmutare et campos pedibus transire videmur,\n\n \n\net sonitus audire, severa silentia noctis\n\n \n\nundique cum constent, et reddere dicta tacentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","text":"Cetera de genere hoc mirande multa videmus,\n\n \n\nquae violare fidem quasi sensibus omnia quaerunt,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam pars horum maxima fallit\n\n \n\npropter opinatus animi, quos addimus ipsi,\n\n \n\npro visis ut sint quae non sunt sensibus visa;\n\n \n\nnam nihil aegrius est quam res secernere apertas\n\n \n\nab dubiis, animus quas ab se protinus addit.\n\n  \n\nDenique nil sciri siquis putat, id quoque nescit\n\n \n\nan sciri possit, quoniam nil scire fatetur.\n\n \n\nhunc igitur contra minuam contendere causam,\n\n \n\nqui capite ipse suo in statuit vestigia sese.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","text":"et tamen hoc quoque uti concedam scire, at id ipsum\n\n \n\nquaeram, cum in rebus veri nil viderit ante,\n\n \n\nunde sciat quid sit scire et nescire vicissim,\n\n \n\nnotitiam veri quae res falsique crearit\n\n \n\net dubium certo quae res differre probarit.\n\n \n\ninvenies primis ab sensibus esse creatam\n\n \n\nnotitiem veri neque sensus posse refelli.\n\n \n\nnam maiore fide debet reperirier illud,\n\n \n\nsponte sua veris quod possit vincere falsa.\n\n \n\nquid maiore fide porro quam sensus haberi\n\n \n\ndebet? an ab sensu falso ratio orta valebit\n\n \n\ndicere eos contra, quae tota ab sensibus orta est?\n\n \n\nqui nisi sunt veri, ratio quoque falsa fit omnis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","text":"An poterunt oculos aures reprehendere, an aures\n\n \n\ntactus? an hunc porro tactum sapor arguet oris,\n\n \n\nan confutabunt nares oculive revincent?\n\n \n\nnon, ut opinor, ita est. nam seorsum cuique potestas\n\n \n\n divisast, sua vis cuiquest, ideoque necesse est\n\n \n\net quod molle sit et gelidum fervensve videre\n\n \n\net seorsum varios rerum sentire colores\n\n \n\net quae cumque coloribus sint coniuncta necessest.\n\n \n\nseorsus item sapor oris habet vim, seorsus odores\n\n \n\nnascuntur, seorsum sonitus. ideoque necesse est\n\n \n\nnon possint alios alii convincere sensus.\n\n \n\nnec porro poterunt ipsi reprehendere sese,\n\n \n\naequa fides quoniam debebit semper haberi.\n\n \n\nproinde quod in quoquest his visum tempore, verumst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0033","text":"Et si non poterit ratio dissolvere causam,\n\n \n\ncur ea quae fuerint iuxtim quadrata, procul sint\n\n \n\nvisa rutunda, tamen praestat rationis egentem\n\n \n\nreddere mendose causas utriusque figurae,\n\n \n\nquam manibus manifesta suis emittere quoquam\n\n \n\net violare fidem primam et convellere tota\n\n \n\nfundamenta quibus nixatur vita salusque.\n\n \n\nnon modo enim ratio ruat omnis, vita quoque ipsa\n\n \n\nconcidat extemplo, nisi credere sensibus ausis\n\n \n\npraecipitisque locos vitare et cetera quae sint\n\n \n\nin genere hoc fugienda, sequi contraria quae sint.\n\n \n\nilla tibi est igitur verborum copia cassa\n\n \n\nomnis, quae contra sensus instructa paratast.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","text":"Denique ut in fabrica, si pravast regula prima,\n\n \n\nnormaque si fallax rectis regionibus exit,\n\n \n\net libella aliqua si ex parti claudicat hilum,\n\n \n\nomnia mendose fieri atque obstipa necessu est\n\n \n\nprava cubantia prona supina atque absona tecta,\n\n \n\niam ruere ut quaedam videantur velle, ruantque\n\n \n\nprodita iudiciis fallacibus omnia primis,\n\n \n\nsic igitur ratio tibi rerum prava necessest\n\n \n\nfalsaque sit, falsis quae cumque ab sensibus ortast.\n\n  \n\nNunc alii sensus quo pacto quisque suam rem\n\n \n\nsentiat, haud quaquam ratio scruposa relicta est.\n\n \n\nPrincipio auditur sonus et vox omnis, in auris\n\n \n\ninsinuata suo pepulere ubi corpore sensum.\n\n \n\ncorpoream quoque enim  constare fatendumst\n\n \n\net sonitum, quoniam possunt inpellere sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0035","text":"Praeterea radit vox fauces saepe facitque\n\n \n\nasperiora foras gradiens arteria clamor,\n\n \n\nquippe per angustum turba maiore coorta\n\n \n\nire foras ubi coeperunt primordia vocum.\n\n \n\nscilicet expletis quoque ianua raditur oris.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur dubiumst quin voces verbaque constent\n\n \n\ncorporeis e principiis, ut laedere possint.\n\n \n\nnec te fallit item quid corporis auferat et quid\n\n \n\ndetrahat ex hominum nervis ac viribus ipsis\n\n \n\nperpetuus sermo nigrai noctis ad umbram\n\n \n\naurorae perductus ab exoriente nitore,\n\n \n\npraesertim si cum summost clamore profusus.\n\n \n\nergo corpoream vocem constare necessest,\n\n \n\nmulta loquens quoniam amittit de corpore partem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0036","text":"Asperitas autem vocis fit ab asperitate\n\n \n\nprincipiorum et item levor levore creatur;\n\n \n\nnec simili penetrant auris primordia forma,\n\n \n\n cum tuba depresso graviter sub murmure mugit\n\n \n\net reboat raucum retro cita barbita bombum,\n\n \n\net  Dauliades natae hortis ex Heliconis\n\n \n\ncum liquidam tollunt lugubri voce querellam.\n\n  \n\nHasce igitur penitus voces cum corpore nostro\n\n \n\nexprimimus rectoque foras emittimus ore,\n\n \n\nmobilis articulat nervorum daedala lingua,\n\n \n\nformaturaque labrorum pro parte figurat.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi non longum spatiumst unde illa profecta\n\n \n\nperveniat vox quaeque, necessest verba quoque ipsa\n\n \n\nplane exaudiri discernique articulatim;\n\n \n\nservat enim formaturam servatque figuram.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0037","text":"at si inter positum spatium sit longius aequo,\n\n \n\naëra per multum confundi verba necessest\n\n \n\net conturbari vocem, dum transvolat auras.\n\n \n\nergo fit, sonitum ut possis sentire neque illam\n\n \n\ninternoscere, verborum sententia quae sit;\n\n \n\nusque adeo confusa venit vox inque pedita.\n\n \n\nPraeterea verbum saepe unum perciet auris\n\n \n\nomnibus in populo missum praeconis ab ore.\n\n \n\nin multas igitur voces vox una repente\n\n \n\ndiffugit, in privas quoniam se dividit auris\n\n \n\nobsignans formam verbis clarumque sonorem.\n\n \n\nat quae pars vocum non auris incidit ipsas,\n\n \n\npraeter lata perit frustra diffusa per auras.\n\n \n\npars solidis adlisa locis reiecta sonorem\n\n \n\nreddit et inter dum frustratur imagine verbi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0038","text":"Quae bene cum videas, rationem reddere possis\n\n \n\ntute tibi atque aliis, quo pacto per loca sola\n\n \n\nsaxa paris formas verborum ex ordine reddant.\n\n \n\npalantis comites com montis inter opacos\n\n \n\nquaerimus et magna dispersos voce ciemus.\n\n \n\nsex etiam aut septem loca vidi reddere vocis,\n\n \n\nunam cum iaceres: ita colles collibus ipsi\n\n \n\nverba repulsantes iterabant dicta referri.\n\n \n\nhaec loca capripedes Satyros Nymphasque tenere\n\n \n\nfinitimi fingunt et Faunos esse locuntur,\n\n \n\nquorum noctivago strepitu ludoque iocanti\n\n \n\nadfirmant volgo taciturna silentia rumpi\n\n \n\nchordarumque sonos fieri dulcisque querellas,\n\n \n\ntibia quas fundit digitis pulsata canentum,\n\n \n\net genus agricolum late sentiscere, quom Pan\n\n \n\npinea semiferi capitis velamina quassans\n\n \n\nunco saepe labro calamos percurrit hiantis,\n\n \n\nfistula silvestrem ne cesset fundere musam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0039","text":"cetera de genere hoc monstra ac portenta loquontur,\n\n \n\nne loca deserta ab divis quoque forte putentur\n\n \n\nsola tenere. ideo iactant miracula dictis\n\n \n\naut aliqua ratione alia ducuntur, ut omne\n\n \n\nhumanum genus est avidum nimis auricularum.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, non est mirandum qua ratione,\n\n \n\nper loca quae nequeunt oculi res cernere apertas,\n\n \n\nhaec loca per voces veniant aurisque lacessant,\n\n \n\nconloquium clausis foribus quoque saepe videmus;\n\n \n\nni mirum quia vox per flexa foramina rerum\n\n \n\n incolumis transire potest, simulacra renutant;\n\n \n\nperscinduntur enim, nisi recta foramina tranant,\n\n \n\nqualia sunt vitrei, species qua travolat omnis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0040","text":"praeterea partis in cunctas dividitur vox,\n\n \n\nex aliis aliae quoniam gignuntur, ubi una\n\n \n\ndissuluit semel in multas exorta, quasi ignis\n\n \n\nsaepe solet scintilla suos se spargere in ignis.\n\n \n\nergo replentur loca vocibus abdita retro,\n\n \n\nomnia quae circum fervunt sonituque cientur.\n\n \n\nat simulacra viis derectis omnia tendunt,\n\n \n\nut sunt missa semel; qua propter cernere nemo\n\n \n\nsaepe supra potis est, at voces accipere extra.\n\n \n\net tamen ipsa quoque haec, dum transit clausa \n\n \n\nvox optunditur atque auris confusa penetrat\n\n \n\net sonitum potius quam verba audire videmur.\n\n  \n\nHoc, qui sentimus sucum, lingua atque palatum\n\n \n\nplusculum habent in se rationis, plus operai.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0041","text":"principio sucum sentimus in ore, cibum cum\n\n \n\nmandendo exprimimus, ceu plenam spongiam aquai\n\n \n\nsiquis forte manu premere ac siccare coëpit.\n\n \n\ninde quod exprimimus per caulas omne palati\n\n \n\ndiditur et rarae per flexa foramina linguae,\n\n \n\nhoc ubi levia sunt manantis corpora suci,\n\n \n\nsuaviter attingunt et suaviter omnia tractant\n\n \n\numida linguai circum sudantia templa;\n\n \n\nat contra pungunt sensum lacerantque coorta,\n\n \n\nquanto quaeque magis sunt asperitate repleta.\n\n \n\ndeinde voluptas est e suco fine palati;\n\n \n\ncum vero deorsum per fauces praecipitavit,\n\n \n\nnulla voluptas est, dum diditur omnis in artus;\n\n \n\nnec refert quicquam quo victu corpus alatur,\n\n \n\ndum modo quod capias concoctum didere possis\n\n \n\nartubus et stomachi tumidum servare tenorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0042","text":"Nunc aliis alius qui sit cibus ut videamus,\n\n \n\nexpediam, quareve, aliis quod triste et amarumst,\n\n \n\nhoc tamen esse aliis possit perdulce videri,\n\n \n\ntantaque  his rebus distantia differitasque est,\n\n \n\nut quod aliis cibus est aliis fuat acre venenum;\n\n \n\nest itaque ut serpens, hominis quae tacta salivis\n\n \n\ndisperit ac sese mandendo conficit ipsa.\n\n \n\npraeterea nobis veratrum est acre venenum,\n\n \n\nat capris adipes et cocturnicibus auget.\n\n \n\nid quibus ut fiat rebus cognoscere possis,\n\n \n\nprincipio meminisse decet quae diximus ante,\n\n \n\nsemina multimodis in rebus mixta teneri.\n\n \n\nporro omnes quae cumque cibum capiunt animantes,\n\n \n\nut sunt dissimiles extrinsecus et generatim\n\n \n\nextima membrorum circumcaesura coërcet,\n\n \n\nproinde et seminibus constant variantque figura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","text":"semina cum porro distent, differre necessest\n\n \n\nintervalla viasque, foramina quae perhibemus,\n\n \n\nomnibus in membris et in ore ipsoque palato.\n\n \n\nesse minora igitur quaedam maioraque debent,\n\n \n\nesse triquetra aliis, aliis quadrata necessest,\n\n \n\nmulta rutunda, modis multis multangula quaedam.\n\n \n\n namque figurarum ratio ut motusque reposcunt,\n\n \n\nproinde foraminibus debent differe figurae\n\n \n\net variare viae proinde ac textura coërcet.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi quod suave est aliis aliis fit amarum,\n\n \n\nilli, cui suave est, levissima corpora debent\n\n \n\ncontractabiliter caulas intrare palati,\n\n \n\nat contra quibus est eadem res intus acerba,\n\n \n\naspera ni mirum penetrant hamataque fauces.\n\n \n\nnunc facile est ex his rebus cognoscere quaeque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0044","text":"quippe ubi cui febris bili superante coorta est\n\n \n\naut alia ratione aliquast vis excita morbi,\n\n \n\nperturbatur ibi iam totum corpus et omnes\n\n \n\ncommutantur ibi positurae principiorum;\n\n \n\nfit prius ad sensum  quae corpora conveniebant\n\n \n\nnunc non conveniant, et cetera sint magis apta,\n\n \n\nquae penetrata queunt sensum progignere acerbum;\n\n \n\nutraque enim sunt in mellis commixta sapore;\n\n \n\nid quod iam supera tibi saepe ostendimus ante.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quo pacto naris adiectus odoris\n\n \n\ntangat agam. primum res multas esse necessest\n\n \n\nunde fluens volvat varius se fluctus odorum,\n\n \n\net fluere et mitti volgo spargique putandumst;\n\n \n\nverum aliis alius magis est animantibus aptus,\n\n \n\ndissimilis propter formas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0045","text":"ideoque per auras\n\n \n\nmellis apes quamvis longe ducuntur odore,\n\n \n\nvolturiique cadaveribus; tum fissa ferarum\n\n \n\nungula quo tulerit gressum promissa canum vis\n\n \n\nducit, et humanum longe praesentit odorem\n\n \n\nRomulidarum arcis servator, candidus anser.\n\n \n\nsic aliis alius nidor datus ad sua quemque\n\n \n\npabula ducit et a taetro resilire veneno\n\n \n\ncogit, eoque modo servantur saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\nHic odor ipse igitur, naris qui cumque lacessit,\n\n \n\nest alio ut possit permitti longius alter;\n\n \n\nsed tamen haud quisquam tam longe fertur eorum\n\n \n\nquam sonitus, quam vox, mitto iam dicere quam res\n\n \n\nquae feriunt oculorum acies visumque lacessunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0046","text":"errabundus enim tarde venit ac perit ante\n\n \n\npaulatim facilis distractus in aëris auras;\n\n \n\nex alto primum quia vix emittitur ex re;\n\n \n\nnam penitus fluere atque recedere rebus odores\n\n \n\nsignificat quod fracta magis redolere videntur\n\n \n\nomnia, quod contrita, quod igni conlabefacta.\n\n \n\ndeinde videre licet maioribus esse creatum\n\n \n\nprincipiis quam vox, quoniam per saxea saepta\n\n \n\nnon penetrat, qua vox volgo sonitusque feruntur.\n\n \n\nquare etiam quod olet non tam facile esse videbis\n\n \n\ninvestigare in qua sit regione locatum;\n\n \n\nrefrigescit enim cunctando plaga per auras\n\n \n\nnec calida ad sensum decurrunt nuntia rerum.\n\n \n\nerrant saepe canes itaque et vestigia quaerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0047","text":"Nec tamen hoc solis in odoribus atque saporum\n\n \n\nin generest, sed item species rerum atque colores\n\n \n\nnon ita conveniunt ad sensus omnibus omnes,\n\n \n\nut non sint aliis quaedam magis acria visu.\n\n \n\n quin etiam gallum noctem explaudentibus alis\n\n \n\nauroram clara consuetum voce vocare,\n\n \n\nnoenu queunt rapidi contra constare leones\n\n \n\ninque tueri: ita continuo meminere fugai.\n\n \n\nni mirum quia sunt gallorum in corpore quaedam\n\n \n\nsemina, quae cum sunt oculis inmissa leonum,\n\n \n\npupillas interfodiunt acremque dolorem\n\n \n\npraebent, ut nequeant contra durare feroces,\n\n \n\ncum tamen haec nostras acies nil laedere possint,\n\n \n\naut quia non penetrant aut quod penetrantibus illis\n\n \n\nexitus ex oculis liber datur, in remorando\n\n \n\nlaedere ne possint ex ulla lumina parte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","text":"Nunc age, quae moveant animum res accipe, et unde\n\n \n\nquae veniunt veniant in mentem percipe paucis.\n\n \n\nprincipio hoc dico, rerum simulacra vagari\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis in cunctas undique partis\n\n \n\ntenvia, quae facile inter se iunguntur in auris,\n\n \n\nobvia cum veniunt, ut aranea bratteaque auri.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim multo magis haec sunt tenvia textu\n\n \n\nquam quae percipiunt oculos visumque lacessunt,\n\n \n\ncorporis haec quoniam penetrant per rara cientque\n\n \n\ntenvem animi naturam intus sensumque lacessunt.\n\n \n\nCentauros itaque et Scyllarum membra videmus\n\n \n\nCerbereasque canum facies simulacraque eorum\n\n \n\nquorum morte obita tellus amplectitur ossa;\n\n \n\nomnigenus quoniam passim simulacra feruntur,\n\n \n\npartim sponte sua quae fiunt aëre in ipso,\n\n \n\npartim quae variis ab rebus cumque recedunt\n\n \n\net quae confiunt ex horum facta figuris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0049","text":"nam certe ex vivo Centauri non fit imago,\n\n \n\nnulla fuit quoniam talis natura animata;\n\n \n\nverum ubi equi atque hominis casu convenit imago,\n\n \n\nhaerescit facile extemplo, quod diximus ante,\n\n \n\npropter subtilem naturam et tenvia texta.\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc eadem ratione creantur.\n\n \n\nquae cum mobiliter summa levitate feruntur,\n\n \n\nut prius ostendi, facile uno commovet ictu\n\n \n\nquae libet una animum nobis subtilis imago;\n\n \n\ntenvis enim mens est et mire mobilis ipsa.\n\n \n\nhaec fieri ut memoro, facile hinc cognoscere possis.\n\n \n\nquatinus hoc simile est illi, quod mente videmus\n\n \n\natque oculis, simili fieri ratione necessest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050","text":"Nunc igitur docui quoniam me forte leonum\n\n \n\ncernere per simulacra, oculos quae cumque lacessunt,\n\n \n\nscire licet mentem simili ratione moveri\n\n \n\nper simulacra leonum  cetera quae videt aeque\n\n \n\nnec minus atque oculi, nisi quod mage tenvia cernit.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia, cum somnus membra profudit,\n\n \n\nmens animi vigilat, nisi quod simulacra lacessunt\n\n \n\nhaec eadem nostros animos quae cum vigilamus,\n\n \n\nusque adeo, certe ut videamur cernere eum quem\n\n \n\nrellicta vita iam mors et terra potitast.\n\n \n\nhoc ideo fieri cogit natura, quod omnes\n\n \n\ncorporis offecti sensus per membra quiescunt\n\n \n\nnec possunt falsum veris convincere rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051","text":"praeterea meminisse iacet languetque sopore,\n\n \n\nnec dissentit eum mortis letique potitum\n\n \n\niam pridem, quem mens vivom se cernere credit.\n\n \n\nquod super est, non est mirum simulacra moveri\n\n \n\nbracchiaque in numerum iactare et cetera membra;\n\n \n\nnam fit ut in somnis facere hoc videatur imago.\n\n \n\nquippe, ubi prima perit alioque est altera nata\n\n \n\ninde statu, prior hic gestum mutasse videtur.\n\n \n\nscilicet id fieri celeri ratione putandumst:\n\n \n\ntanta est mobilitas et rerum copia tanta\n\n \n\ntantaque sensibili quovis est tempore in uno\n\n \n\ncopia particularum, ut possit suppeditare.\n\n  \n\nMultaque in his rebus quaeruntur multaque nobis\n\n \n\nclarandumst, plane si res exponere avemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0052","text":"quaeritur in primis quare, quod cuique libido\n\n \n\nvenerit, extemplo mens cogitet eius id ipsum.\n\n \n\nanne voluntatem nostram simulacra tuentur\n\n \n\net simul ac volumus nobis occurrit imago,\n\n \n\nsi mare, si terram cordist, si denique caelum?\n\n \n\nconventus hominum, pompam, convivia, pugnas,\n\n \n\nomnia sub verbone creat natura paratque?\n\n \n\ncum praesertim aliis eadem in regione locoque\n\n \n\nlonge dissimilis animus res cogitet omnis.\n\n \n\nquid porro, in numerum procedere cum simulacra\n\n \n\ncernimus in somnis et mollia membra movere,\n\n \n\nmollia mobiliter cum alternis bracchia mittunt\n\n \n\net repetunt oculis gestum pede convenienti?\n\n \n\nscilicet arte madent simulacra et docta vagantur,\n\n \n\nnocturno facere ut possint in tempore ludos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","text":"an magis illud erit verum? quia tempore in uno,\n\n \n\ncum sentimus, id est cum vox emittitur una,\n\n \n\ntempora multa latent, ratio quae comperit esse,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti quovis in tempore quaeque\n\n \n\npraesto sint simulacra locis in quisque parata.\n\n \n\ntanta est mobilitas et rerum copia tanta.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi prima perit alioque est altera nata\n\n \n\ninde statu, prior hic gestum mutasse videtur.\n\n \n\net quia tenvia sunt, nisi quae contendit, acute\n\n \n\ncernere non potis est animus; proinde omnia quae sunt\n\n \n\npraeterea pereunt, nisi quae ex se ipse paravit.\n\n \n\nipse parat sese porro speratque futurum\n\n \n\nut videat quod consequitur rem quamque: fit ergo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0054","text":"nonne vides oculos etiam, cum tenvia quae sunt\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\ncernere coeperunt, contendere se atque parare,\n\n \n\nnec sine eo fieri posse ut cernamus acute?\n\n \n\net tamen in rebus quoque apertis noscere possis,\n\n \n\nsi non advertas animum, proinde esse quasi omni\n\n \n\ntempore semotum fuerit longeque remotum.\n\n \n\ncur igitur mirumst, animus si cetera perdit\n\n \n\npraeter quam quibus est in rebus deditus ipse?\n\n \n\ndeinde adopinamur de signis maxima parvis\n\n \n\nac nos in fraudem induimus frustraminis ipsi.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut inter dum non suppeditetur imago\n\n \n\neiusdem generis, sed femina quae fuit ante,\n\n \n\n in manibus vir uti factus videatur adesse,\n\n \n\naut alia ex alia facies aetasque sequatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","text":"quod ne miremur sopor atque oblivia curant.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus vitium vehementer äinesse\n\n \n\neffugere errorem vitareque praemetuenter,\n\n \n\nlumina ne facias oculorum clara creata,\n\n \n\nprospicere ut possimus, et ut proferre queamus\n\n \n\nproceros passus, ideo fastigia posse\n\n \n\nsurarum ac feminum pedibus fundata plicari,\n\n \n\nbracchia tum porro validis ex apta lacertis\n\n \n\nesse manusque datas utraque  parte ministras,\n\n \n\nut facere ad vitam possemus quae foret usus.\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc inter quae cumque pretantur,\n\n \n\nomnia perversa praepostera sunt ratione,\n\n \n\nnil ideo quoniam natumst in corpore ut uti\n\n \n\npossemus, sed quod natumst id procreat usum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0056","text":"nec fuit ante videre oculorum lumina nata,\n\n \n\nnec dictis orare prius quam lingua creatast,\n\n \n\nsed potius longe linguae praecessit origo\n\n \n\nsermonem multoque creatae sunt prius aures\n\n \n\nquam sonus est auditus, et omnia denique membra\n\n \n\nante fuere, ut opinor, eorum quam foret usus;\n\n \n\nhaud igitur potuere utendi crescere causa.\n\n \n\nat contra conferre manu certamina pugnae\n\n \n\net lacerare artus foedareque membra cruore\n\n \n\nante fuit multo quam lucida tela volarent,\n\n \n\net volnus vitare prius natura coëgit\n\n \n\nquam daret obiectum parmai laeva per artem.\n\n \n\nscilicet et fessum corpus mandare quieti\n\n \n\nmulto antiquius est quam lecti mollia strata,\n\n \n\net sedare sitim prius est quam pocula natum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0057","text":"haec igitur possunt utendi cognita causa\n\n \n\ncredier, ex usu quae sunt vitaque reperta.\n\n \n\nilla quidem seorsum sunt omnia, quae prius ipsa\n\n \n\nnata dedere suae post notitiam utilitatis.\n\n \n\nquo genere in primis sensus et membra videmus;\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam procul est ut credere possis\n\n \n\nutilitatis ob officium potuisse creari.\n\n  \n\nIllud item non est mirandum, corporis ipsa\n\n \n\nquod natura cibum quaerit cuiusque animantis.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim fluere atque recedere corpora rebus\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis docui, sed plurima debent\n\n \n\nex animalibus;  quia sunt exercita motu,\n\n \n\nmulta per os exhalantur, cum languida anhelant,\n\n \n\nmultaque per sudorem ex alto pressa feruntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0058","text":"his igitur rebus rarescit corpus et omnis\n\n \n\nsubruitur natura, dolor quam consequitur rem.\n\n \n\npropterea capitur cibus, ut suffulciat artus\n\n \n\net recreet vires inter datus, atque patentem\n\n \n\nper membra ac venas ut amorem opturet edendi.\n\n \n\numor item discedit in omnia quae loca cumque\n\n \n\nposcunt umorem; glomerataque multa vaporis\n\n \n\ncorpora, quae stomacho praebent incendia nostro,\n\n \n\ndissupat adveniens liquor ac restinguit ut ignem,\n\n \n\nurere ne possit calor amplius aridus artus.\n\n \n\n sic igitur tibi anhela sitis de corpore nostro\n\n \n\nabluitur, sic expletur ieiuna cupido.\n\n  \n\nNunc qui fiat uti passus proferre queamus,\n\n \n\ncum volumus, quareque datum sit membra movere\n\n \n\net quae res tantum hoc oneris protrudere nostri\n\n \n\ncorporis insuerit, dicam: tu percipe dicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","text":"dico animo nostro primum simulacra meandi\n\n \n\naccidere atque animum pulsare, ut diximus ante.\n\n \n\ninde voluntas fit; neque enim facere incipit ullam\n\n \n\nrem quisquam,  mens providit quid velit ante.\n\n \n\nid quod providet, illius rei constat imago,\n\n \n\nergo animus cum sese ita commovet ut velit ire\n\n \n\ninque gredi, ferit extemplo quae in corpore toto\n\n \n\nper membra atque artus animai dissita vis est;\n\n \n\net facilest factu, quoniam coniuncta tenetur.\n\n \n\ninde ea proporro corpus ferit, atque ita tota\n\n \n\npaulatim moles protruditur atque movetur.\n\n \n\npraeterea tum rarescit quoque corpus et aër,\n\n \n\nscilicet ut debet qui semper mobilis extat,\n\n \n\nper patefacta venit penetratque foramina largus,\n\n \n\net dispargitur ad partis ita quasque minutas\n\n \n\ncorporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0060","text":"hic igitur rebus fit utrimque duabus,\n\n \n\ncorpus ut ac navis velis ventoque feratur.\n\n \n\nnec tamen illud in his rebus mirabile constat,\n\n \n\ntantula quod tantum corpus corpuscula possunt\n\n \n\ncontorquere et onus totum convertere nostrum;\n\n \n\nquippe etenim ventus subtili corpore tenvis\n\n \n\ntrudit agens magnam magno molimine navem\n\n \n\net manus una regit quanto vis impete euntem\n\n \n\natque gubernaclum contorquet quo libet unum,\n\n \n\nmultaque per trocleas et tympana pondere magno\n\n \n\ncommovet atque levi sustollit machina nisu.\n\n  \n\nNunc quibus ille modis somnus per membra quietem\n\n \n\ninriget atque animi curas e pectore solvat,\n\n \n\nsuavidicis potius quom multis versibus edam,\n\n \n\nparvus ut est cycni melior canor, ille gruum quam\n\n \n\nclamor in aetheriis dispersus nubibus austri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061","text":"tu mihi da tenuis auris animumque sagacem,\n\n \n\nne fieri negites quae dicam posse retroque\n\n \n\nvera repulsanti discedas pectore dicta,\n\n \n\ntutemet in culpa cum sis neque cernere possis.\n\n \n\nPrincipio somnus fit ubi est distracta per artus\n\n \n\nvis animae partimque foras eiecta recessit\n\n \n\net partim contrusa magis concessit in altum;\n\n \n\ndissoluuntur enim tum demum membra fluuntque.\n\n \n\nnam dubium non est, animai quin opera sit\n\n \n\nsensus hic in nobis, quem cum sopor inpedit esse,\n\n \n\ntum nobis animam perturbatam esse putandumst\n\n \n\neiectamque foras, non omnem; namque iaceret\n\n \n\naeterno corpus perfusum frigore leti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0062","text":"quippe ubi nulla latens animai pars remaneret\n\n \n\nin membris, cinere ut multa latet obrutus ignis,\n\n \n\nunde reconflari sensus per membra repente\n\n \n\npossit, ut ex igni caeco consurgere flamma?\n\n  \n\nSed quibus haec rebus novitas confiat et unde\n\n \n\n perturbari anima et corpus languescere possit,\n\n \n\nexpediam: tu fac ne ventis verba profundam.\n\n \n\nPrincipio externa corpus de parte necessum est,\n\n \n\naëriis quoniam vicinum tangitur auris,\n\n \n\ntundier atque eius crebro pulsarier ictu,\n\n \n\nproptereaque fere res omnes aut corio sunt\n\n \n\naut etiam conchis aut callo aut cortice tectae.\n\n \n\ninteriorem etiam partem spirantibus aër\n\n \n\nverberat hic idem, cum ducitur atque reflatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0063","text":"quare utrimque secus cum corpus vapulet et cum\n\n \n\nperveniant plagae per parva foramina nobis\n\n \n\ncorporis ad primas partis elementaque prima,\n\n \n\nfit quasi paulatim nobis per membra ruina.\n\n \n\nconturbantur enim positurae principiorum\n\n \n\ncorporis atque animi. fit uti pars inde animai\n\n \n\neliciatur et introrsum pars abdita cedat,\n\n \n\npars etiam distracta per artus non queat esse\n\n \n\nconiuncta inter se neque motu mutua fungi;\n\n \n\ninter enim saepit coetus natura viasque.\n\n \n\nergo sensus abit mutatis motibus alte.\n\n \n\net quoniam non est quasi quod suffulciat artus,\n\n \n\ndebile fit corpus languescuntque omnia membra,\n\n \n\nbracchia palpebraeque cadunt poplitesque cubanti\n\n \n\nsaepe tamen summittuntur virisque resolvunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0064","text":"Deinde cibum sequitur somnus, quia, quae facit aër,\n\n \n\nhaec eadem cibus, in venas dum diditur omnis,\n\n \n\nefficit. et multo sopor ille gravissimus exstat,\n\n \n\nquem satur aut lassus capias, quia plurima tum se\n\n \n\ncorpora conturbant magno contusa labore.\n\n \n\nfit ratione eadem coniectus parte animai\n\n \n\naltior atque foras eiectus largior eius,\n\n \n\net divisior inter se ac distractior intus.\n\n  \n\nEt quo quisque fere studio devinctus adhaeret\n\n \n\naut quibus in rebus multum sumus ante morati\n\n \n\natque in ea ratione fuit contenta magis mens,\n\n \n\nin somnis eadem plerumque videmur obire:\n\n \n\ncausidici causas agere et componere leges,\n\n \n\ninduperatores pugnare ac proelia obire,\n\n \n\nnautae contractum cum ventis degere bellum,\n\n \n\nnos agere hoc autem et naturam quaerere rerum\n\n \n\nsemper et inventam patriis exponere chartis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0065","text":"cetera sic studia atque artes plerumque videntur\n\n \n\nin somnis animos hominum frustrata tenere.\n\n \n\net qui cumque dies multos ex ordine ludis\n\n \n\nadsiduas dederunt operas, plerumque videmus,\n\n \n\ncum iam destiterunt ea sensibus usurpare,\n\n \n\nrelicuas tamen esse vias in mente patentis,\n\n \n\nqua possint eadem rerum simulacra venire;\n\n \n\nper multos itaque illa dies eadem obversantur\n\n \n\nante oculos, etiam vigilantes ut videantur\n\n \n\ncernere saltantis et mollia membra moventis\n\n \n\net citharae liquidum carmen chordasque loquentis\n\n \n\nauribus accipere et consessum cernere eundem\n\n \n\nscenaique simul varios splendere decores.\n\n \n\nusque adeo magni refert studium atque voluntas,\n\n \n\n et quibus in rebus consuerint esse operati\n\n \n\nnon homines solum sed vero animalia cuncta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0066","text":"quippe videbis equos fortis, cum membra iacebunt,\n\n \n\nin somnis sudare tamen spirareque semper\n\n \n\net quasi de palma summas contendere viris\n\n \n\naut quasi carceribus patefactis \n\n \n\nvenantumque canes in molli saepe quiete\n\n \n\niactant crura tamen subito vocisque repente\n\n \n\nmittunt et crebro redducunt naribus auras.\n\n \n\nut vestigia si teneant inventa ferarum,\n\n \n\nexpergefactique secuntur inania saepe\n\n \n\ncervorum simulacra, fugae quasi dedita cernant,\n\n \n\ndonec discussis redeant erroribus ad se.\n\n \n\nat consueta domi catulorum blanda propago\n\n \n\ndiscutere et corpus de terra corripere instant,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nproinde quasi ignotas facies atque ora tuantur.\n\n \n\net quo quaeque magis sunt aspera seminiorum,\n\n \n\ntam magis in somnis eadem saevire necessust.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0067","text":"at variae fugiunt volucres pinnisque repente\n\n \n\nsollicitant divom nocturno tempore lucos,\n\n \n\naccipitres somno in leni si proelia pugnas\n\n \n\nedere sunt persectantes visaeque volantes.\n\n \n\nporro hominum mentes, magnis quae motibus edunt\n\n \n\nmagna, itidem saepe in somnis faciuntque geruntque,\n\n \n\nreges expugnant, capiuntur, proelia miscent,\n\n \n\ntollunt clamorem, quasi si iugulentur ibidem.\n\n \n\nmulti depugnant gemitusque doloribus edunt\n\n \n\net quasi pantherae morsu saevive leonis\n\n \n\nmandantur, magnis clamoribus omnia complent.\n\n \n\nmulti de magnis per somnum rebus loquuntur\n\n \n\nindicioque sui facti persaepe fuere.\n\n \n\nmulti mortem obeunt. multi, de montibus altis\n\n \n\nut quasi praecipitent ad terram corpore toto,\n\n \n\nexterruntur et ex somno quasi mentibus capti\n\n \n\nvix ad se redeunt permoti corporis aestu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068","text":"flumen item sitiens aut fontem propter amoenum\n\n \n\nadsidet et totum prope faucibus occupat amnem.\n\n \n\npuri saepe lacum propter si ac dolia curta\n\n \n\nsomno devincti credunt se extollere vestem,\n\n \n\ntotius umorem saccatum corporis fundunt,\n\n \n\ncum Babylonica magnifico splendore rigantur.\n\n \n\ntum quibus aetatis freta primitus insinuatur\n\n \n\nsemen, ubi ipsa dies membris matura creavit,\n\n \n\nconveniunt simulacra foris e corpore quoque,\n\n \n\nnuntia praeclari voltus pulchrique coloris,\n\n \n\nqui ciet inritans loca turgida semine multo,\n\n \n\nut quasi transactis saepe omnibus rebus profundant\n\n \n\nfluminis ingentis fluctus vestemque cruentent.\n\n  \n\nSollicitatur id  nobis, quod diximus ante,\n\n \n\nsemen, adulta aetas cum primum roborat artus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","text":"namque alias aliud res commovet atque lacessit;\n\n \n\n ex homine humanum semen ciet una hominis vis.\n\n \n\nquod simul atque suis eiectum sedibus exit,\n\n \n\nper membra atque artus decedit corpore toto,\n\n \n\nin loca conveniens nervorum certa cietque\n\n \n\ncontinuo partis genitalis corporis ipsas.\n\n \n\ninritata tument loca semine fitque voluntas\n\n \n\neicere id quo se contendit dira lubido,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nidque petit corpus, mens unde est saucia amore;\n\n \n\nnamque omnes plerumque cadunt in vulnus et illam\n\n \n\nemicat in partem sanguis, unde icimur ictu,\n\n \n\net si comminus est, hostem ruber occupat umor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0070","text":"sic igitur Veneris qui telis accipit ictus,\n\n \n\nsive puer membris muliebribus hunc iaculatur\n\n \n\nseu mulier toto iactans e corpore amorem,\n\n \n\nunde feritur, eo tendit gestitque coire\n\n \n\net iacere umorem in corpus de corpore ductum;\n\n \n\nnamque voluptatem praesagit muta cupido.\n\n  \n\nHaec Venus est nobis; hinc autemst nomen Amoris,\n\n \n\nhinc illaec primum Veneris dulcedinis in cor\n\n \n\nstillavit gutta et successit frigida cura;\n\n \n\nnam si abest quod ames, praesto simulacra tamen sunt\n\n \n\nillius et nomen dulce obversatur ad auris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","text":"sed fugitare decet simulacra et pabula amoris\n\n \n\nabsterrere sibi atque alio convertere mentem\n\n \n\net iacere umorem coniectum in corpora quaeque\n\n \n\nnec retinere semel conversum unius amore\n\n \n\net servare sibi curam certumque dolorem;\n\n \n\nulcus enim vivescit et inveterascit alendo\n\n \n\ninque dies gliscit furor atque aerumna gravescit,\n\n \n\nsi non prima novis conturbes volnera plagis\n\n \n\nvolgivagaque vagus Venere ante recentia cures\n\n \n\naut alio possis animi traducere motus.\n\n  \n\nNec Veneris fructu caret is qui vitat amorem,\n\n \n\nsed potius quae sunt sine poena commoda sumit;\n\n \n\nnam certe purast sanis magis inde voluptas\n\n \n\nquam miseris; etenim potiundi tempore in ipso\n\n \n\nfluctuat incertis erroribus ardor amantum\n\n \n\nnec constat quid primum oculis manibusque fruantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0072","text":"quod petiere, premunt arte faciuntque dolorem\n\n \n\ncorporis et dentes inlidunt saepe labellis\n\n \n\nosculaque adfigunt, quia non est pura voluptas\n\n \n\net stimuli subsunt, qui instigant laedere id ipsum,\n\n \n\nquod cumque est, rabies unde illaec germina surgunt.\n\n \n\nsed leviter poenas frangit Venus inter amorem\n\n \n\nblandaque refrenat morsus admixta voluptas.\n\n \n\nnamque in eo spes est, unde est ardoris origo,\n\n \n\nrestingui quoque posse ab eodem corpore flammam.\n\n \n\nquod fieri contra totum natura repugnat;\n\n \n\nunaque res haec est, cuius quam plurima habemus,\n\n \n\ntam magis ardescit dira cuppedine pectus.\n\n \n\nnam cibus atque umor membris adsumitur intus;\n\n \n\nquae quoniam certas possunt obsidere partis,\n\n \n\nhoc facile expletur laticum frugumque cupido.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0073","text":"ex hominis vero facie pulchroque colore\n\n \n\n nil datur in corpus praeter simulacra fruendum\n\n \n\ntenvia; quae vento spes raptast saepe misella.\n\n \n\nut bibere in somnis sitiens quom quaerit et umor\n\n \n\nnon datur, ardorem qui membris stinguere possit,\n\n \n\nsed laticum simulacra petit frustraque laborat\n\n \n\nin medioque sitit torrenti flumine potans,\n\n \n\nsic in amore Venus simulacris ludit amantis,\n\n \n\nnec satiare queunt spectando corpora coram\n\n \n\nnec manibus quicquam teneris abradere membris\n\n \n\npossunt errantes incerti corpore toto.\n\n \n\ndenique cum membris conlatis flore fruuntur\n\n \n\naetatis, iam cum praesagit gaudia corpus\n\n \n\natque in eost Venus ut muliebria conserat arva,\n\n \n\nadfigunt avide corpus iunguntque salivas\n\n \n\noris et inspirant pressantes dentibus ora,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam nihil inde abradere possunt\n\n \n\nnec penetrare et abire in corpus corpore toto;\n\n \n\nnam facere inter dum velle et certare videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0074","text":"usque adeo cupide in Veneris compagibus haerent,\n\n \n\nmembra voluptatis dum vi labefacta liquescunt.\n\n \n\ntandem ubi se erupit nervis coniecta cupido,\n\n \n\nparva fit ardoris violenti pausa parumper.\n\n \n\ninde redit rabies eadem et furor ille revisit,\n\n \n\ncum sibi quod cupiant ipsi contingere quaerunt,\n\n \n\nnec reperire malum id possunt quae machina vincat.\n\n \n\nusque adeo incerti tabescunt volnere caeco.\n\n  \n\nAdde quod absumunt viris pereuntque labore,\n\n \n\nadde quod alterius sub nutu degitur aetas,\n\n \n\nlanguent officia atque aegrotat fama vacillans.\n\n \n\nlabitur interea res et Babylonia fiunt\n\n \n\nunguenta et pulchra in pedibus Sicyonia rident,\n\n \n\nscilicet et grandes viridi cum luce zmaragdi\n\n \n\nauro includuntur teriturque thalassina vestis\n\n \n\nadsidue et Veneris sudorem exercita potat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0075","text":"et bene parta patrum fiunt anademata, mitrae,\n\n \n\ninter dum in pallam atque Alidensia Ciaque vertunt.\n\n \n\neximia veste et victu convivia, ludi,\n\n \n\npocula crebra, unguenta, coronae, serta parantur,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam medio de fonte leporum\n\n \n\nsurgit amari aliquid, quod in ipsis floribus angat,\n\n \n\naut cum conscius ipse animus se forte remordet\n\n \n\ndesidiose agere aetatem lustrisque perire,\n\n \n\naut quod in ambiguo verbum iaculata reliquit,\n\n \n\nquod cupido adfixum cordi vivescit ut ignis,\n\n \n\naut nimium iactare oculos aliumve tueri\n\n \n\nquod putat in voltuque videt vestigia risus.\n\n  \n\nAtque in amore mala haec proprio summeque secundo\n\n \n\ninveniuntur; in adverso vero atque inopi sunt,\n\n \n\nprendere quae possis oculorum lumine operto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","text":"innumerabilia; ut melius vigilare sit ante,\n\n \n\nqua docui ratione, cavereque, ne inliciaris.\n\n \n\nnam vitare, plagas in amoris ne iaciamur,\n\n \n\nnon ita difficile est quam captum retibus ipsis\n\n \n\nexire et validos Veneris perrumpere nodos.\n\n \n\net tamen implicitus quoque possis inque peditus\n\n \n\n effugere infestum, nisi tute tibi obvius obstes\n\n \n\net praetermittas animi vitia omnia primum\n\n \n\naut quae corporis sunt eius, quam praepetis ac vis.\n\n \n\nnam faciunt homines plerumque cupidine caeci\n\n \n\net tribuunt ea quae non sunt his commoda vere.\n\n \n\nmultimodis igitur pravas turpisque videmus\n\n \n\nesse in deliciis summoque in honore vigere.\n\n \n\natque alios alii inrident Veneremque suadent\n\n \n\nut placent, quoniam foedo adflictentur amore,\n\n \n\nnec sua respiciunt miseri mala maxima saepe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0077","text":"nigra melichrus est, inmunda et fetida acosmos,\n\n \n\ncaesia Palladium, nervosa et lignea dorcas,\n\n \n\nparvula, pumilio, chariton mia, tota merum sal,\n\n \n\nmagna atque inmanis cataplexis plenaque honoris.\n\n \n\nbalba loqui non quit, traulizi, muta pudens est;\n\n \n\nat flagrans, odiosa, loquacula Lampadium fit.\n\n \n\nischnon eromenion tum fit, cum vivere non quit\n\n \n\nprae macie; rhadine verost iam mortua tussi.\n\n \n\nat nimia et mammosa Ceres est ipsa ab Iaccho,\n\n \n\nsimula Silena ac Saturast, labeosa philema.\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc longum est si dicere coner.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0078","text":"sed tamen esto iam quantovis oris honore,\n\n \n\ncui Veneris membris vis omnibus exoriatur;\n\n \n\nnempe aliae quoque sunt; nempe hac sine viximus ante;\n\n \n\nnempe eadem facit et scimus facere omnia turpi\n\n \n\net miseram taetris se suffit odoribus ipsa,\n\n \n\nquam famulae longe fugitant furtimque cachinnant.\n\n \n\nat lacrimans exclusus amator limina saepe\n\n \n\nfloribus et sertis operit postisque superbos\n\n \n\nunguit amaracino et foribus miser oscula figit;\n\n \n\nquem si iam ammissum venientem offenderit aura\n\n \n\nuna modo, causas abeundi quaerat honestas\n\n \n\net meditata diu cadat alte sumpta querella\n\n \n\nstultitiaque ibi se damnet, tribuisse quod illi\n\n \n\nplus videat quam mortali concedere par est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0079","text":"nec Veneres nostras hoc fallit; quo magis ipsae\n\n \n\nomnia summo opere hos vitae poscaenia celant,\n\n \n\nquos retinere volunt adstrictosque esse in amore,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam tu animo tamen omnia possis\n\n \n\nprotrahere in lucem atque omnis inquirere risus\n\n \n\net, si bello animost et non odiosa, vicissim\n\n \n\npraetermittere  humanis concedere rebus.\n\n  \n\nNec mulier semper ficto suspirat amore,\n\n \n\nquae conplexa viri corpus cum corpore iungit\n\n \n\net tenet adsuctis umectans oscula labris;\n\n \n\nnam facit ex animo saepe et communia quaerens\n\n \n\ngaudia sollicitat spatium decurrere amoris.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia volucres armenta feraeque\n\n \n\net pecudes et equae maribus subsidere possent,\n\n \n\nsi non, ipsa quod illarum subat, ardet abundans\n\n \n\nnatura et Venerem salientum laeta retractat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0080","text":"nonne vides etiam quos mutua saepe voluptas\n\n \n\nvinxit, ut in vinclis communibus excrucientur,\n\n \n\nin triviis cum saepe canes discedere aventis\n\n \n\ndivorsi cupide summis ex viribus tendunt,\n\n \n\n quom interea validis Veneris compagibus haerent?\n\n \n\nquod facerent numquam, nisi mutua gaudia nossent,\n\n \n\nquae iacere in fraudem possent vinctosque tenere.\n\n \n\nquare etiam atque etiam, ut dico, est communis voluptas.\n\n  \n\nEt commiscendo quom semine forte virilem\n\n \n\nfemina vim vicit subita vi corripuitque,\n\n \n\ntum similes matrum materno semine fiunt,\n\n \n\nut patribus patrio. sed quos utriusque figurae\n\n \n\nesse vides, iuxtim miscentes vulta parentum,\n\n \n\ncorpore de patrio et materno sanguine crescunt,\n\n \n\nsemina cum Veneris stimulis excita per artus\n\n \n\nobvia conflixit conspirans mutuus ardor,\n\n \n\net neque utrum superavit eorum nec superatumst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","text":"fit quoque ut inter dum similes existere avorum\n\n \n\npossint et referant proavorum saepe figuras,\n\n \n\npropterea quia multa modis primordia multis\n\n \n\nmixta suo celant in corpore saepe parentis,\n\n \n\nquae patribus patres tradunt a stirpe profecta.\n\n \n\ninde Venus varia producit sorte figuras,\n\n \n\nmaiorumque refert voltus vocesque comasque;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem nihilo magis haec  semine certo\n\n \n\nfiunt quam facies et corpora membraque nobis.\n\n \n\net muliebre oritur patrio de semine saeclum\n\n \n\nmaternoque mares existunt corpore creti;\n\n \n\nsemper enim partus duplici de semine constat,\n\n \n\natque utri similest magis id quod cumque creatur,\n\n \n\neius habet plus parte aequa; quod cernere possis,\n\n \n\nsive virum suboles sivest muliebris origo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","text":"Nec divina satum genitalem numina cuiquam\n\n \n\nabsterrent, pater a gnatis ne dulcibus umquam\n\n \n\nappelletur et ut sterili Venere exigat aevom;\n\n \n\nquod plerumque putant et multo sanguine maesti\n\n \n\nconspergunt aras adolentque altaria donis,\n\n \n\nut gravidas reddant uxores semine largo;\n\n \n\nne quiquam divom numen sortisque fatigant;\n\n \n\nnam steriles nimium crasso sunt semine partim,\n\n \n\net liquido praeter iustum tenuique vicissim.\n\n \n\ntenve locis quia non potis est adfigere adhaesum,\n\n \n\nliquitur extemplo et revocatum cedit abortu.\n\n \n\ncrassius hinc porro quoniam concretius aequo\n\n \n\nmittitur, aut non tam prolixo provolat ictu\n\n \n\naut penetrare locos aeque nequit aut penetratum\n\n \n\naegre admiscetur muliebri semine semen.\n\n \n\nnam multum harmoniae Veneris differre videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0083","text":"atque alias alii complent magis ex aliisque\n\n \n\nsuccipiunt aliae pondus magis inque gravescunt.\n\n \n\net multae steriles Hymenaeis ante fuerunt\n\n \n\npluribus et nactae post sunt tamen unde puellos\n\n \n\nsuscipere et partu possent ditescere dulci.\n\n \n\net quibus ante domi fecundae saepe nequissent\n\n \n\nuxoris parere, inventast illis quoque compar\n\n \n\nnatura, ut possent gnatis munire senectam.\n\n \n\nusque adeo magni refert, ut semina possint\n\n \n\nseminibus commisceri genitaliter apta\n\n \n\ncrassaque conveniant liquidis et liquida crassis.\n\n \n\n atque in eo refert quo victu vita colatur;\n\n \n\nnamque aliis rebus concrescunt semina membris\n\n \n\natque aliis extenvantur tabentque vicissim.\n\n \n\net quibus ipsa modis tractetur blanda voluptas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0084","text":"id quoque permagni refert; nam more ferarum\n\n \n\nquadrupedumque magis ritu plerumque putantur\n\n \n\nconcipere uxores, quia sic loca sumere possunt\n\n \n\npectoribus positis sublatis semina lumbis.\n\n \n\nnec molles opus sunt motus uxoribus hilum.\n\n \n\nnam mulier prohibet se concipere atque repugnat,\n\n \n\nclunibus ipsa viri Venerem si laeta retractat\n\n \n\natque exossato ciet omni pectore fluctus;\n\n \n\neicit enim sulcum recta regione viaque\n\n \n\nvomeris atque locis avertit seminis ictum.\n\n \n\nidque sua causa consuerunt scorta moveri,\n\n \n\nne complerentur crebro gravidaeque iacerent,\n\n \n\net simul ipsa viris Venus ut concinnior esset;\n\n \n\nconiugibus quod nil nostris opus esse videtur.\n\n  \n\nNec divinitus inter dum Venerisque sagittis\n\n \n\ndeteriore fit ut forma muliercula ametur;\n\n \n\nnam facit ipsa suis inter dum femina factis\n\n \n\nmorigerisque modis et munde corpore culto,\n\n \n\nut facile insuescat secum  degere vitam.\n\n \n\nquod super est, consuetudo concinnat amorem;\n\n \n\nnam leviter quamvis quod crebro tunditur ictu,\n\n \n\nvincitur in longo spatio tamen atque labascit.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam guttas in saxa cadentis\n\n \n\numoris longo in spatio pertundere saxa?","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2005f785c7cb6ef46fc2dbfae33a8415bed21cad2e67047d2a8cba71a41929ff","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T235851511488Z_e095ff7a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book Four","source_sha256":"2005f785c7cb6ef46fc2dbfae33a8415bed21cad2e67047d2a8cba71a41929ff","sha256":"5c0e51ab79be4efb57ba3760553f18ab9e876b2d170e85844c4e11ce7bf1b7e2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:58:51.511503+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0001","text":"## Book Four\n\nI traverse the pathless regions of the Pierides, trodden before by no foot. It delights me to approach untouched springs and to drink, and it delights me to pluck fresh flowers and thence seek a distinguished crown for my head, whence before no Muses have veiled anyone's temples; first, because I teach of great matters and proceed to free the mind from the tight knots of religion; then, because upon so dark a subject I fashion verses so bright, touching all with the Muses' charm.\n\nFor this too is seen not to be without some reason. For just as physicians, when they try to give children foul wormwood, first touch the rim of the cup all round with the sweet yellow liquid of honey, so that the unthinking age of children may be deceived as far as the lips, while meanwhile it drinks down the bitter juice of wormwood, and, deceived, is not harmed, but rather, restored by such means, regains strength: so I now, since this doctrine is commonly thought rather harsh by those who have not handled it, and the common crowd shrinks back from it, have wished to set forth our doctrine to you in sweet-speaking Pierian song and, as it were, to touch it with the sweet honey of the Muses; if perhaps by such a method I might be able to hold your mind fast in my verses, while you apprehend all the nature of things and perceive its usefulness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","text":"But since I have taught what are the beginnings of all things, and how, differing in their diverse shapes, they fly of their own accord, driven by everlasting motion, and in what manner each thing can be created from these; and since I have taught what the nature of the mind is, and from what things, joined with the body, it flourishes in vigor, and how, when severed, it returns into its first elements: I shall now begin to deal with that which is of the greatest relevance to these matters, namely that there are what we call images of things, because an image bears a likeness and form similar to that body from which it is said to stream and wander.\n\nThese, like membranes stripped from the outermost surface of things, fly about freely to and fro through the air, and meet our minds while we are awake and terrify us also in sleep, when we often behold strange shapes and images of those bereft of light, which have often roused us dreadfully from languid slumber, lest by chance we should think that souls escape from Acheron or that shades flit among the living, or that anything of us can remain after death, when both body and the nature of mind, destroyed, have each departed into their own first elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0003","text":"I say therefore that effigies and thin shapes are sent out from things, from their outermost rind; and this one may discern from here, however dull one's mind.\n\nFirst, since in things that are open many substances are emitted—partly scattered loose, as woods send smoke and fires vapor; and partly more woven and compact, as cicadas in summer once cast off their smooth tunics, and as calves at birth cast membranes from the surface of their body, and likewise when the slippery serpent sheds its garment among thorns; for we often see brambles enlarged by their flying spoils—since these things happen, a thin image too must be sent forth from things, from the outermost surface of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0004","text":"For why those things should fall away and withdraw from objects rather than things that are thin, there is no power to distinguish; especially since upon the surfaces of things there are many minute bodies, which can be cast off in the same order in which they were and preserve the form of their shape, and do so much more swiftly, inasmuch as, being few, they can be less impeded and are situated on the foremost surface.\n\nFor certainly we see many things cast and poured forth not only from deep within, as we said before, but often color itself from the outer surfaces. And yellow, red, and dark-blue awnings commonly do this, when, stretched over great theatres and rippling, they wave upon poles and beams. For there they dye the assembly seated below in the theatre and all the appearance of the stage—fathers and mothers alike—and compel them to undulate in their own color.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0005","text":"And the more closely the walls of the theatre are enclosed all around, the more all things within, suffused with this charm, smile, caught by the light of day.\n\nTherefore, since cloths send out dye from their outermost body, every thing must likewise send out thin effigies, since both are cast from the outer surface. Thus there are indeed certain traces of forms, which commonly fly about, furnished with a subtle thread, and cannot be seen separately in isolation.\n\nMoreover, every smell, smoke, vapor, and other like things abound diffused from objects for this reason: because, as they come from deep within and arise outwardly, they are split on their winding way, nor are there straight outlets of paths by which they may press on to emerge when they have arisen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","text":"But, on the contrary, when a thin membrane of surface color is cast forth, there is nothing that can tear it apart, since it is readily available, situated upon the foremost surface.\n\nLastly, whatever images appear to us in mirrors, in water, and in every shining thing, must, since they are endowed with a likeness similar to things, be composed of images sent out from them. Therefore there are thin effigies of forms, like to those things, which no one can see separately; yet, driven back by continual and repeated impact, they render vision from the smooth surface of mirrors, nor do they seem able to be preserved in any other way, so exactly are they rendered like the figure of each thing.\n\nCome now, learn how thin is the nature of an image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0007","text":"And first, since first beginnings are so far beneath our senses and so much smaller than those things which the eyes first begin to be unable to behold, yet now, that I may establish this point also, learn in few words how subtle are the beginnings of all things.\n\nFirst, there are animals now so small that a third part of them could by no means be seen. What, then, must be supposed to be the nature of any one of their entrails? What the globe of its heart or its eyes? What its limbs? What its members? How small they are! And, further, how small must be each first beginning from which it is necessary that the nature of soul and mind be composed—do you not see how subtle and how minute they are?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0008","text":"Moreover, all things that breathe out from their own body a pungent smell—panaceas, foul wormwood, heavy southernwood, and gloomy centaury—if by chance any one of these is lightly [Gap in source]\n\nWould you not rather recognize that many images of things wander in many ways, without force and void of sensation?\n\nBut lest perhaps you think that only those alone wander which withdraw from things, the images of things, there are also those which arise of their own accord and are themselves formed in this heaven which is called air; shaped in many ways, they are borne aloft. Thus we often see clouds congeal on high and violate the clear appearance of the world, caressing the air by their motion; for often the faces of Giants seem to fly and cast broad shadow, at times great mountains and rocks torn from mountains seem to go before, and to pass by the sun; then some beast to draw and bring on other clouds of storm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0009","text":"Nor do the liquid things cease to change their own appearance and turn into the outlines of every kind of shape.\n\nNow learn by how easy and swift a process they are generated and continually flow from things and, slipping away, depart.\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nFor whatever is always abundant from the surface of things is cast forth. And when this reaches other objects, it passes through them, as especially through cloth; but when it reaches rough rocks or the substance of wood, there it is already torn apart, so that it can render no image. But when bright and dense things are set opposite, as a mirror especially is, none of these things happens; for they cannot pass through as through cloth, nor yet be torn apart, because smoothness has been mindful to provide safety.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0010","text":"For this reason it comes about that images rebound to us from there. And however suddenly, at any time, you place any object before a mirror, its image appears; so that you may know that thin textures and thin figures of things continually flow from their outermost body.\n\nTherefore many images are generated in a short space, so that swift origin is rightly attributed to these things. And just as the sun must send down many lights in a brief space, so that all things may continually be full, so likewise by a similar process it is necessary that, in a point of time, many images of things be borne in many ways to every region from every side; since, in whatever direction we turn a mirror, things answer there with similar form and color.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0011","text":"Moreover, when the weather of heaven has been most clear, it suddenly becomes foully turbid, so that you would think all darkness had left Acheron and filled the great caverns of heaven. So greatly, when the foul night of storm-clouds has arisen, do the black faces of Dread hang threatening overhead; how small a part of an image they are, there is no one who can say, nor render that account in words.\n\nCome now, I shall set forth in sweet-speaking rather than many verses how swiftly images are borne, and what mobility has been granted them as they pass through the air, so that a brief hour is spent over a long distance, as each tends toward its place by diverse impulse: as the small song of the swan is better than the cry of cranes scattered by the south wind among the clouds of heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0012","text":"First, it is often possible to see that light things and things made of minute bodies are swift. Of this kind are the sun's light and its heat, because they are made of minute first elements, which, as if driven, do not hesitate to pass through the intervals of air, impelled by the following stroke; for light is instantly supplied by light, and lightning is, as it were, urged to shine by a continued prod.\n\nFor this reason images too must by like process be able to run through immeasurable space in a point of time: first, because there is a tiny cause far behind which carries and propels them onward; next, because they are sent forth endowed with so winged a lightness and so sparse a texture that they can easily penetrate any things whatsoever and, as it were, filter through the intervals of air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0013","text":"Moreover, if any bodies of things are sent out from deep within and outward, as the sun's light and heat, these are seen, in a point of day, to spread themselves over the entire expanse of heaven and to fly through sea and lands and flood heaven.\n\nWhat then of those that are already prepared on the foremost surface, when they are cast forth and no obstacle delays their emission? Do you not see that they ought to go more swiftly and farther, and traverse a manifold extent of space in the same time in which the sun's lights fly across heaven?\n\nThis too seems to be an especially true indication of how swiftly images of things are borne: as soon as the brilliance of water is placed beneath the open sky, immediately, when the heaven is serene and starry, the radiant stars of the world answer in the water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","text":"Do you now see, then, in how punctual a time an image of heaven falls from the regions of heaven into the regions of earth? Therefore again and again you must admit that bodies are sent out which strike the eyes and assail vision.\n\nAnd smells continually flow from definite things: cold from rivers, heat from the sun, spray from the waves of the sea, the eater-away of walls around the shores; nor do diverse sounds cease to fly through the air. Finally, the liquid moisture of a salty taste often comes into the mouth when we move near the sea; and, on the other hand, when we see wormwood being mixed, bitterness touches us. To such an extent does each thing flow from all things and is sent out in every direction on all sides; nor is any delay or rest granted to this flowing, because we continually sense, and are always able to see and smell all things and hear them sound.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","text":"Moreover, since a shape handled by the hands in darkness is recognized to be the same as that which is seen in light and clear brightness, touch and sight must be moved by a similar cause. Now then, if we feel out a square and it moves us in darkness, what can in light happen to the square appearance except its image? Therefore the cause of seeing appears to lie in images, nor can any thing be seen without them.\n\nNow the images of things of which I speak are borne everywhere and cast out, scattered in every direction; but because we are able to see only with the eyes, it therefore happens that, in whatever direction we turn our sight, all things strike it there with form and color.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0016","text":"And the image causes us to see and enables us to distinguish how far each thing is from us; for when it is sent forth, it immediately thrusts and drives the air that is situated between itself and the eyes, and this air thus glides through all our visual organs and, as it were, wipes the pupils and so passes on.\n\nHence it happens that we see how far away each thing is. And the more air is driven before it and the longer the breeze that wipes our eyes, the farther away each thing seems to be. Clearly these things take place with the utmost swiftness: both what the thing is that we see and, at the same time, how far away it is.\n\nIn these matters it must not at all be considered wonderful why, although the images that strike the eyes cannot be seen separately, the things themselves are perceived.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0017","text":"For when the wind strikes little by little and keen cold flows, we are not accustomed to feel each separate particle of wind and of its cold, but rather the whole at once; and we then see blows taking place upon our body, as though some thing were striking it and giving from without the sensation of its own body.\n\nMoreover, when we strike a stone with the finger, we touch its outermost surface and the surface color of the rock, nor do we feel that color by touch; rather, deep within, we feel the very hardness of the stone.\n\nCome now, learn why an image is seen beyond a mirror: for certainly it appears far removed. This is like those things that are truly seen through outside, when a door by itself offers an open view through it and makes many things outside visible from a house; for this vision too is produced by double and twin air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0018","text":"For first the air on this side of the doorposts is seen; then the doorposts themselves follow, on right and left; afterwards the external light and a second air wipe the eyes, and those things outside which are truly seen through.\n\nSo, when the image of a mirror has first projected itself, while it comes to our visual organs, it thrusts and drives the air that lies between itself and the eyes, and causes us to be able to perceive all this air before the mirror. But when we have perceived the mirror itself as well, immediately the image which is borne from us to it arrives, and, reflected, revisits our eyes, rolling before itself and propelling another air; it makes us see this air before itself, and therefore seems to be removed from the mirror by just so much.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0019","text":"Therefore again and again there is no reason at all for wonder\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nat those things which render vision from the smooth surface of mirrors, since each result is accomplished by two airs.\n\nNow that which is the right-hand side of our limbs comes about to be seen as left in mirrors because, when an image coming to the plane of a mirror strikes it, it is not turned back intact but is dashed straight backward, just as if someone were to strike a ball against a dry mask before it is of clay, or against a beam, and if it should immediately preserve a straight figure before it and, dashed back, reproduce itself backward.\n\nThe result will be that the eye which had been the right will now be the left, and the right will correspondingly come from the left.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0020","text":"It also comes about that an image is passed on from mirror to mirror,\n\nso that five or even six likenesses may be produced at the same time.\n\nFor whatever things shall lie hidden at the back, in the inner part,\n\nfrom there nevertheless, however distorted and wholly remote,\n\nall may be brought out through winding passages,\n\nso that they are seen to be in a house by means of many mirrors.\n\nSo completely does an image shine through from mirror to mirror,\n\nand when a left hand has been presented, it comes about again that it becomes a right;\n\nthen it returns backward once more and changes back to the same.\n\nMoreover, whatever little sides of mirrors\n\nare furnished with a bending like the side of our body,\n\nfor that reason send back right-hand images to us,\n\neither because the image is transferred from mirror to mirror\n\nand then, reflected twice, flies toward us, or also because,\n\nwhen it arrives, the image is turned around, for this reason that\n\nthe bent shape of the mirror teaches it to be turned toward us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0021","text":"Furthermore, you would suppose that images walk along equally and\n\nset down a foot with us and imitate our movement,\n\nbecause, from whichever side of the mirror you withdraw,\n\nstraightway the images cannot return from there;\n\nsince nature compels all things to be reflected\n\nand to rebound from objects, returned at equal angles.\n\nFurthermore, the eyes shun and avoid looking at bright things.\n\nThe sun also blinds, if you persist in directing your gaze against it,\n\nbecause its force is great, and high up\n\nthrough the clear air images are borne\n\nand strike the eyes, disturbing their structures.\n\nMoreover, every sharp brightness often burns\n\nthe eyes because it possesses many seeds of fire,\n\nwhich, insinuating themselves, produce pain in the eyes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0022","text":"Moreover, whatever jaundiced persons look upon become pale,\n\nbecause many seeds of paleness flow from their bodies\n\nto meet the images of things,\n\nand many, finally, are mingled in their eyes,\n\nwhich by their contagion dye everything with pallors.\n\nBut we look from darkness upon things that are in light\n\nbecause, when the nearer black air of darkness\n\nfirst enters the eyes and occupies them when opened,\n\nbright, shining air immediately follows,\n\nwhich, as it were, cleanses them and scatters the black shadows\n\nof that air; for in many respects this air is\n\nmore mobile, more subtle, and more potent.\n\nAs soon as it has filled the passages of the eyes with light\n\nand opened wide those which the black air\n\nhad previously besieged, immediately the images of things\n\nwhich are situated in the light follow and stimulate us to see.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0023","text":"The reverse we cannot do, from light in darkness,\n\nbecause the thicker air of darkness follows behind,\n\nwhich fills all the openings\n\nand besieges the passages of the eyes, so that the images\n\nof any things, thrown in, cannot be moved.\n\nAnd when we behold from afar the square towers of a city,\n\nit comes about that they often appear round,\n\nbecause every angle is seen as blunt from a distance,\n\nor rather is not seen at all, and its blow\n\nperishes and does not reach the vision of our eyes,\n\nbecause, while the images are borne through much air,\n\nthe air compels it to grow dull by repeated impacts.\n\nWhen every angle has at once escaped perception,\n\nit comes about as though structures of stone were seen turned round;\n\nyet not as things which are truly and actually round when seen close at hand,\n\nbut they appear somewhat as a shadowy imitation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0024","text":"Likewise, a shadow seems to us to move in the sun\n\nand to follow our footsteps and imitate our movement,\n\nif you believe that air deprived of light can\n\nwalk along, following the movements and gestures of men;\n\nfor that which we are accustomed to call a shadow can be nothing else\n\nthan air devoid of light.\n\nNo doubt because the earth is deprived of the sun's light\n\nin definite places in succession, wherever we pass along,\n\nand likewise that which we have left is filled again,\n\ntherefore it comes about that what was the shadow\n\nof the body seems to have continually followed us from the opposite side.\n\nFor ever new lights of rays pour themselves forth,\n\nand the former perish, as though wool were drawn into fire.\n\nTherefore the earth is easily stripped of light\n\nand likewise filled again, and washes away its black shadows from itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","text":"Yet here we do not grant that the eyes are deceived by a jot.\n\nFor to see in whatever place light and shadow may be belongs to them;\n\nbut whether the lights are the same or not,\n\nand whether the shadow which was here now passes there,\n\nor rather whether what we said a little before comes to be,\n\nthis the reasoning of the mind alone ought to distinguish,\n\nnor can the eyes know the nature of things.\n\nTherefore do not fasten this fault of the mind upon the eyes.\n\nThe ship in which we are carried is borne along, though it seems to stand still;\n\nthat which remains at its station is believed to be passing by.\n\nAnd hills and plains seem to flee toward the stern,\n\nwhich we pass by in the ship and fly past under sail.\n\nAll the stars seem fixed in the ethereal vaults to stand still,\n\nand yet all are in continual motion,\n\nsince, having risen, they revisit their long settings,\n\nwhen they have measured the heaven with their bright body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0026","text":"And by like reasoning the sun and moon seem to remain\n\nin one place—things which reality itself shows to be carried along.\n\nMountains rising far off from the midst of the sea,\n\nbetween which a great clear outlet lies open for fleets,\n\nyet appear as one island joined from these.\n\nIt thus comes about that porticoes and columns seem to children\n\nto turn about and run around,\n\nwhen they themselves have ceased to turn,\n\nso that they can scarcely now believe that all the roofs above them\n\nare not threatening to fall down upon them.\n\nAnd now, when nature begins to raise the red radiance high with trembling fires\n\nand to lift it above the mountains,\n\nwhich at that moment the sun seems to you to be above,\n\nitself, burning, touching them close at hand with its own fire,\n\nscarcely two thousand arrows' flights away from us,\n\nscarcely even often five hundred casts of a javelin,\n\nbetween them and the sun lie the vast level expanses\n\nof sea, spread beneath the huge shores of heaven,\n\nand many thousands of lands intervene,\n\nwhich diverse nations and tribes of wild beasts occupy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0027","text":"But a pool of water not one finger's breadth deep,\n\nwhich stands between stones in the paved layers of roads,\n\noffers a view downward beneath the earth with such an extent\n\nas the deep chasm of heaven lies open from the earth,\n\nso that you seem to look down upon clouds and to see heaven,\n\nwith bodies wondrously hidden beneath the earth in heaven.\n\nFinally, when a spirited horse has halted for us in the middle\n\nof a river and we have looked down into the swift waters of the stream,\n\nthe force of the standing horse's body seems to be carried sideways\n\nand driven rapidly upstream against the current,\n\nand wherever we cast our eyes, all things seem to be borne\n\nand to flow in a manner similar to ourselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0028","text":"Although a portico is, finally, drawn in an even line\n\nand stands out continuously, supported by equal columns,\n\nyet, when its whole length is seen from the farthest end,\n\nit gradually draws itself into the narrow point of a cone,\n\njoining roof to floor and all the right sides to the left,\n\nuntil it has led them together into the cone's obscure apex.\n\nTo sailors at sea, the sun rising from the waves seems\n\nto set in the waves and conceal its light;\n\nsince they behold nothing but water and sky—\n\nlest you lightly believe that the senses are everywhere shaken.\n\nBut to those ignorant of the sea, in harbor ships seem\n\nto struggle with broken stern-ornaments against the waves.\n\nFor whatever part of the oars rises above the salt spray\n\nis straight, and the rudders above are straight;\n\nall that is submerged beneath the liquid seems broken,\n\nto be turned back upward and to return lying on its back,\n\nand, reflected, almost to float on the surface of the liquid.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0029","text":"And when winds bear scattered clouds through the sky\n\nin the nighttime, then the bright constellations seem\n\nto glide against the clouds and to go above\n\nfar away in another direction from that in which they are truly borne.\n\nBut if by chance a hand placed beneath one eye\n\npresses it, by a certain effect it comes about that all things\n\nwhich we behold then appear double to our sight:\n\nthe bright lights of lamps flowering with flames double,\n\nand furniture throughout the whole house doubled,\n\nand the faces of men twofold and their bodies double.\n\nFinally, when sleep has bound our limbs in sweet slumber\n\nand the whole body lies in deepest repose,\n\nthen nevertheless we seem to ourselves to be awake and to move\n\nour limbs, and in the blind darkness of night\n\nwe judge that we see the sun and daylight,\n\nand, shut in a place, we seem to change sky, sea, rivers, mountains,\n\nand to cross plains with our feet,\n\nand to hear sounds, though the stern silences of night\n\nprevail everywhere, and to utter words while silent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","text":"We see many other marvelous things of this kind,\n\nwhich all, as it were, seek to violate confidence in the senses,\n\nbut in vain, since the greatest part of these things deceives\n\nbecause of the opinions of the mind which we ourselves add,\n\nso that things not seen by the senses are taken to be seen.\n\nFor nothing is harder than to distinguish clear things\n\nfrom doubtful things, which the mind immediately adds of itself.\n\nFinally, if anyone thinks that nothing can be known, he does not know even this,\n\nwhether it can be known, since he confesses that he knows nothing.\n\nAgainst him, therefore, I shall refrain from contending the case,\n\nwho has set his own footprints upon his own head.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","text":"And yet, even if I grant that he knows this too, I would ask this very thing:\n\nsince he has never before seen anything true among things,\n\nwhence does he know what knowing and not knowing are in turn,\n\nwhat thing has created the notion of truth and falsehood,\n\nand what thing has proved the distinction of the doubtful from the certain?\n\nYou will find that the notion of truth has been created from the senses first,\n\nand that the senses cannot be refuted.\n\nFor that must be discovered with greater trust,\n\nwhich of its own accord can prevail over false things by true ones.\n\nWhat, then, ought to be held in greater trust than the senses?\n\nWill reason, arisen from false sense, be strong enough\n\nto speak against them, when it has arisen wholly from the senses?\n\nUnless they are true, all reason too becomes false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","text":"Can the eyes reprove the ears, or the ears the touch? Or again,\n\nwill taste of the mouth convict this touch, or will the nostrils refute it,\n\nor will the eyes prevail?\n\nNo, I think, it is not so. For to each separately a power\n\nhas been allotted; each has its own force, and therefore it is necessary\n\nto see separately what is soft and cold or hot,\n\nand separately to perceive the various colors of things\n\nand whatever things are necessarily joined with colors.\n\nLikewise taste in the mouth has its power separately, odors\n\narise separately, sounds separately. Therefore it is necessary\n\nthat one sense cannot convict the others.\n\nNor indeed will they be able to reprove themselves,\n\nsince equal trust must always be accorded them.\n\nTherefore whatever has appeared to each of these at any time is true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0033","text":"And if reason cannot explain the cause\n\nwhy things which have been square close by are seen round at a distance,\n\nyet it is better, for want of reasoning, to give faulty\n\ncauses for either shape,\n\nthan somewhere to let go things manifest to one's hands\n\nand violate first confidence and tear away the whole\n\nfoundations upon which life and safety rest.\n\nFor not only would all reasoning collapse, but life itself also\n\nwould immediately fall, unless you dared to trust the senses\n\nand to avoid precipitous places and other things\n\nwhich are to be shunned in this kind, and to pursue their opposites.\n\nTherefore all that abundance of words which has been prepared\n\nand arrayed against the senses is empty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","text":"Finally, just as in a building, if the first rule is crooked,\n\nand if the square proves deceptive in its straight directions,\n\nand if some level is even slightly faulty in one part,\n\nall things must be made wrongly and askew,\n\ncrooked, leaning, sloping, inverted, and discordant—the roofs,\n\nso that some seem already to wish to fall and do fall,\n\nbetrayed wholly by the deceptive first judgments—\n\nso, therefore, your reasoning about things must be crooked\n\nand false, whatever has arisen from false senses.\n\nNow by what means each of the other senses perceives its own object\n\nis by no means a difficult account left to give.\n\nFirst, every sound and voice is heard when, insinuated into the ears,\n\nthey have struck the sense with their own body.\n\nFor it must be confessed that sound too\n\nconsists of body, since it can strike the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0035","text":"Moreover, voice often scrapes the throat, and as it goes out\n\nthe cry makes the windpipe rougher,\n\nsince, when the first-beginnings of voices have arisen in a greater throng\n\nand have begun to go out through a narrow passage,\n\nof course the gateway of the mouth too is scraped when filled.\n\nThere is no doubt, therefore, that voices and words consist\n\nof bodily first-beginnings, so that they can cause injury.\n\nNor does it escape you likewise how much of body continual speech\n\ntakes away and subtracts from men's sinews and strength itself,\n\nwhen it is carried on from the shadow of black night\n\nall the way to the rising brightness of dawn,\n\nespecially if it has been poured forth with the utmost shouting.\n\nTherefore voice must consist of body,\n\nsince one who speaks much loses a part from his body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0036","text":"But roughness of voice arises from roughness\n\nof first-beginnings, and likewise smoothness is created by smoothness;\n\nnor do first-beginnings of similar shape penetrate the ears,\n\nwhen a trumpet bellows heavily with a deep murmur\n\nand the lyre, struck back, resounds with its hoarse humming,\n\nand when the daughters of Daulis in the gardens of Helicon\n\nraise their clear lament in mournful voice.\n\nTherefore, when we force these voices forth from deep within our body\n\nand send them outward through the straight mouth,\n\nthe mobile, artful tongue of sinews articulates them,\n\nand the shaping of the lips in part gives them shape.\n\nWhen the distance from which each voice has set out is not long,\n\nthe words themselves too must necessarily be heard clearly\n\nand distinguished articulation by articulation;\n\nfor it preserves its shaping and preserves its figure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0037","text":"But if the intervening distance is longer than is fitting,\n\nthe words must necessarily be confused through much air\n\nand the voice disordered while it flies through the breezes.\n\nTherefore it comes about that you can perceive the sound but cannot\n\ndiscern what the meaning of the words is;\n\nso confused and impeded does the voice arrive.\n\nMoreover, one word often strikes the ears\n\nof all the people when sent from the mouth of a herald.\n\nOne voice, therefore, suddenly scatters into many voices,\n\nsince it divides itself among individual ears,\n\nstamping upon the words their shape and clear sound.\n\nBut that part of the voices which does not strike the ears themselves\n\nperishes beyond them, scattered in vain through the broad air.\n\nA part, striking solid places, is thrown back\n\nand returns a sound, and sometimes deceives by the semblance of a word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0038","text":"When you see these things well, you can render the account\n\nto yourself and others how, in lonely places,\n\nrocks give back in succession sounds of words in like forms.\n\nWe seek companions wandering among shadowy mountains\n\nand call aloud for those scattered abroad.\n\nI have seen even six or seven places give back a voice\n\nwhen you hurled out one; thus hills themselves, repelling words against hills,\n\nrepeated the utterances as they were returned.\n\nThe neighboring people imagine that goat-footed Satyrs and Nymphs possess\n\nthese places, and say that there are Fauns,\n\nby whose night-wandering noise and jesting play\n\nthey commonly affirm that the silent silences are broken,\n\nthat sounds of strings and sweet complaints are made,\n\nsuch as the pipe pours forth when struck by the fingers of players,\n\nand that the race of farmers perceives far and wide, when Pan,\n\nshaking the pine wreaths of his half-wild head,\noften runs his hooked lip over the gaping reeds,\n\nso that the pipe does not cease to pour forth its woodland music.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0039","text":"They tell of other prodigies and portents of this kind,\n\nlest perhaps deserted places too should be thought to be occupied by gods alone.\n\nTherefore they spread miracles in their tales,\n\nor are led by some other reason, since the whole\n\nhuman race is excessively greedy for ears.\n\nFor the rest, it is not wonderful by what means,\n\nthrough places where the eyes cannot behold things openly,\n\nthings come through those places by voices and strike the ears,\n\nsince we often see conversation even through closed doors;\n\nfor voice can pass unharmed through the winding openings of things,\n\nwhile images refuse to do so;\n\nfor they are torn apart unless they pass through straight openings,\n\nsuch as those of glass, through which every image flies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0040","text":"Moreover, voice is divided into all directions,\n\nbecause some [voices] are generated from others, when one,\n\nonce dissolved, has arisen into many, just as fire\n\noften is wont to scatter its spark into fires of its own.\n\nTherefore hidden places in turn are filled with voices,\n\nall places which boil around and are stirred by sound.\n\nBut images all proceed by straight paths,\n\nas they have once been sent forth; wherefore no one is often\n\nable to see beyond, but to receive voices from outside.\n\nAnd yet even this voice itself, while it passes enclosed,\n\nis blunted and enters the ear confused,\n\nand we seem to hear a sound rather than words.\n\nThis too: the tongue and palate, by which we perceive flavour,\n\nhave in themselves somewhat more of contrivance, more labour.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0041","text":"First, we perceive flavour in the mouth, when we\n\nsqueeze out food by chewing, as if someone perhaps began\n\nto press and dry a sponge full of water with his hand.\n\nThen all that we squeeze out is distributed through the passages\n\nof the palate and through the winding pores of the sparse tongue;\n\nwhen the bodies of the flowing flavour are smooth,\n\nthey touch pleasantly and pleasantly affect all\n\nthe moist temples sweating around the tongue;\n\nbut, on the contrary, as they have arisen, they prick and tear sensation,\n\nthe more each is filled with roughness.\n\nThen pleasure from the flavour extends as far as the palate;\n\nbut when it has rushed downward through the throat,\n\nthere is no pleasure, while it is all distributed among the limbs;\n\nnor does it matter at all by what nourishment the body is fed,\n\nprovided only that you can distribute through the limbs what you take in,\n\nonce digested, and preserve the swollen condition of the stomach.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0042","text":"Now, that we may see what food is for one thing and what for another,\n\nI shall explain why what is bitter and harsh to some\n\ncan nevertheless seem exceedingly sweet to others,\n\nand why in these matters there is so great a difference and diversity,\n\nthat what is food to some may be sharp poison to others;\n\nthus it happens that a serpent, touched by human saliva,\n\nperishes and destroys itself by biting itself.\n\nMoreover, hellebore is sharp poison to us,\n\nbut it fattens goats and quails.\n\nThat you may be able to know by what causes this comes about,\n\nfirst it is fitting to remember what we said before,\n\nthat seeds are held mixed in things in many ways.\n\nFurthermore, all living creatures that take food,\n\njust as they are unlike externally and the encompassing\n\noutline of their limbs restrains each kind,\n\nso likewise consist of seeds differing in shape.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","text":"Moreover, since the seeds differ, the intervals and paths,\n\nthe pores, which we declare to exist,\n\nmust differ in all the limbs and in the mouth and palate itself.\n\nThus some must be smaller and others larger,\n\nsome necessarily three-cornered, others square,\n\nmany round, and certain many-angled in many ways.\n\nFor as the nature of their shapes and motions requires,\n\nso the shapes must differ for the pores,\n\nand the paths vary according as their texture restrains them.\n\nAccordingly, when what is sweet to some becomes bitter to others,\n\nfor him to whom it is sweet the smoothest bodies must\n\nenter flexibly into the passages of the palate;\n\nbut, on the contrary, for those to whom the same thing is bitter within,\n\nrough and, plainly, hooked [bodies] penetrate the throat.\n\nNow from these matters it is easy to know each thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0044","text":"Indeed, when fever has arisen in anyone through an excess of bile,\n\nor some force of disease has been stirred up in another way,\n\nthen the whole body is already disturbed and all\n\nthe positions of the first-beginnings are changed;\n\nit comes about that the bodies which formerly suited sensation\n\nnow do not suit it, and that others are more fitted,\n\nwhich, penetrating, can produce bitter sensation;\n\nfor both are mixed in the flavour of honey—\n\na fact which I have often shown you before above.\n\nCome now, I shall explain in what way the smell, applied to the nostrils,\n\ntouches [them]. First, there must necessarily be many things\n\nfrom which a varied flood of odours, flowing, rolls itself forth,\n\nand it must be thought that [odours] flow, are emitted, and scattered abroad;\n\nbut one [odour] is more suited to some living creatures, another to others,\n\non account of their differing shapes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0045","text":"And therefore through the air\n\nbees, however far away, are led by the smell of honey,\n\nand vultures by corpses; then the promised power of dogs\n\nleads their cloven paw whither the track of wild beasts has directed its step,\n\nand the white goose, guardian of the citadel of Romulus' sons,\n\nperceives from afar a human smell.\n\nThus one scent given to each leads it to its own\n\nfood and compels it to recoil from foul poison,\n\nand in this way the races of wild creatures are preserved.\n\nThis smell itself, then, whatever it be that strikes the nostrils,\n\ncan be transmitted farther by one [thing] than by another;\n\nbut yet none of these is borne so far\n\nas sound, as voice—not now to mention the things\n\nwhich strike the eyes' sharpness and assault vision.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0046","text":"For it comes wandering slowly and perishes first,\n\ngradually dispersed with ease into the breezes of air;\n\nfirst because it is scarcely emitted from deep within a thing;\n\nfor that odours flow and withdraw from things deep within\n\nis shown by the fact that all things seem to smell more strongly when broken,\n\nwhen crushed, when weakened by fire.\n\nThen you may see that it is produced from larger\n\nfirst-beginnings than voice, since it does not penetrate\n\nthrough stony barriers, where voice and sound are commonly borne.\n\nTherefore you will also see that what smells is not so easy\n\nto discover as to the region in which it is situated;\n\nfor its stroke cools by lingering through the air,\n\nand the messengers of things do not run warmly to sensation.\n\nThus dogs often wander and seek the tracks.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0047","text":"Nor yet is this so only in the case of odours and kinds\n\nof flavours; likewise the appearances and colours of things\n\ndo not all suit all senses so well\n\nthat some are not sharper to the sight of some [creatures].\n\nWhy, even the cock, accustomed with clear voice to call\n\nthe dawn when night is clapped away by its wings,\n\nswift lions cannot withstand in turn\n\nor look upon: so straightway they remember flight.\n\nNo doubt because there are certain seeds in the body of cocks\n\nwhich, when sent into the eyes of lions,\n\npierce the pupils and inflict sharp pain,\n\nso that the fierce creatures cannot endure them in return,\n\nthough these same [seeds] can do no harm to our eyes,\n\neither because they do not penetrate, or because, when they penetrate,\n\na free exit is granted them from the eyes, so that by lingering\n\nthey cannot harm the lights from any quarter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","text":"Come now, receive what things move the mind, and whence\n\nthe things that come into the mind come, in a few words.\n\nFirst I say this: many images of things wander\n\nin many ways, everywhere into all directions,\n\nthin [images], which easily join together in the air\n\nwhen they come in contact, like a spider's web and gold leaf.\n\nFor indeed these are much thinner in texture\n\nthan those which strike the eyes and assault vision,\n\nsince these penetrate through the body's rare spaces and stir\n\nthe thin nature of the mind within and assault sensation.\n\nThus we see Centaurs and limbs of Scyllas,\n\nand the dog-like faces of Cerberus and images of those\n\nwhose bones, after death, the earth embraces;\n\nbecause images of every kind are borne everywhere,\n\npartly those which arise spontaneously in the air itself,\n\npartly those which at any time withdraw from various things,\n\nand those which are formed from the shapes made from these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0049","text":"For certainly an image of a Centaur does not arise from a living [Centaur],\n\nsince no such animate nature has existed;\n\nbut when by chance an image of a horse and of a man comes together,\n\nit immediately adheres easily, as we said before,\n\non account of its subtle nature and thin texture.\n\nAll other things of this kind are created by the same method.\n\nAnd since they are borne about with mobile, utmost lightness,\n\nas I showed before, any one thin image, with a single stroke,\n\neasily moves the mind in us as it pleases;\n\nfor the mind is thin and itself wonderfully mobile.\n\nThat these things come about as I recount, you may easily learn from this:\n\ninsofar as what we see with the mind is like what [we see]\n\nwith the eyes, it must come about in a similar way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050","text":"Now therefore, since I have taught that, when I happen\n\nto see lions through images which assault the eyes,\n\nyou may know that the mind is moved in a similar way\n\nby images of lions and all other things which it sees equally,\n\nand no less than the eyes, except that it sees thinner [images].\n\nNor for another reason, when sleep has poured over the limbs,\n\ndoes the mind's consciousness remain awake, except because images\n\nassault our minds, these same images which [assault them] when we are awake,\n\nso much so that we certainly seem to see him whom,\n\nhis life relinquished, death and earth have already taken possession of.\n\nNature compels this to happen because all\n\nthe body's senses, disabled throughout the limbs, are at rest\n\nand cannot refute false things by true realities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051","text":"Moreover, memory lies dormant and languishes in sleep,\n\nnor does it protest that he has long since attained\n\ndeath and destruction, whom the mind believes it sees alive.\n\nAs for the rest, it is not strange that images move\n\ntheir arms and fling about their other limbs in rhythm;\n\nfor it happens that in dreams the image seems to do this.\n\nIndeed, when the first [image] perishes and another has arisen\n\nthen in another position, the former seems to have changed its gesture.\n\nClearly this must be thought to occur with rapid process:\n\nso great is the mobility and so great the abundance of things,\n\nand so great, in any one perceptible instant, is the abundance\n\nof particles, that it can furnish [them].\n\nMany questions are asked concerning these matters, and much must be\n\nmade clear by us, if we desire to set forth the subject plainly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0052","text":"First of all it is asked why, whatever desire has come to each person,\n\nthe mind straightway thinks of that very thing.\n\nDo images watch for our will,\n\nand as soon as we wish does the image meet us,\n\nif the sea is in our heart, if earth, if finally heaven?\n\nAssemblies of men, a procession, banquets, battles—\n\ndoes nature create and prepare all things at a word?\n\nEspecially since in the same region and place\n\ndifferent minds think all things far differently.\n\nWhat then? When we see images proceed in rhythm\n\nand move their supple limbs in dreams,\n\nwhen they nimbly stretch forth their supple arms alternately\n\nand repeat the gesture before the eyes with corresponding foot?\n\nClearly the images are steeped in art and wander trained,\n\nso that they may be able to perform their plays in the time of night.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","text":"Or will this rather be true? Because within a single time,\n\nwhen we perceive—that is, when a single utterance is emitted—\n\nmany times lie hidden, which reason discovers to exist,\n\ntherefore it comes about that at every moment all images\n\nare present, prepared in every place.\n\nSo great is the mobility and so great the abundance of things.\n\nWhen the first [image] perishes and another has arisen\n\nthen in another position, the former seems to have changed its gesture.\n\nAnd because they are thin, unless it strains, the mind\n\nis unable to discern sharply; accordingly all the rest\n\nperish, except those which it itself has prepared.\n\nMoreover, it prepares itself and hopes that there will come about\n\nthe sight of what follows each thing: therefore it happens.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0054","text":"Do you not see that even the eyes, when they have begun\n\nto discern thin things, strain and prepare themselves,\n\nand that without this we cannot see sharply?\n\nAnd yet even in things plainly visible you may recognize\n\nthat, if you do not direct your mind [to them], they are just as if\n\nthey had at every moment been set apart and removed far away.\n\nWhy then is it strange if the mind loses all other things\n\nbesides those matters to which it has itself applied?\n\nThen we infer great things from small signs,\n\nand ourselves enter into the deception of our own error.\n\nIt also happens sometimes that an image\n\nof the same kind is not supplied, but that a woman who was before\n\nseems to be present in the hands as a man,\n\nor that one face and age follows upon another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","text":"Sleep and forgetfulness ensure that we do not wonder at this.\n\nIn these matters there is a fault that must especially be avoided,\n\nand error must be shunned with fearful care: do not suppose that\n\nthe bright lights of the eyes were created\n\nso that we might be able to see, and that, in order that we might be able\n\nto take long strides, the joints of shins and thighs, founded on the feet,\n\ncan bend; or again that arms were fitted to strong shoulders\n\nand hands given as servants on either side,\n\nso that we might be able to do in life what was useful.\n\nAll other explanations of this kind which they interpret together with these\n\nare wholly perverse and backward in reasoning,\n\nbecause nothing was born in the body in order that we might use it,\n\nbut what has been born itself creates its use.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0056","text":"Nor were the lights of the eyes born before seeing,\n\nnor [was there] speaking with words before the tongue was created;\n\nrather, the origin of the tongue far preceded\n\nspeech, and ears were created much earlier than sound was heard,\n\nand finally all the limbs existed, as I think,\n\nbefore there was any use of them; thus they could not have grown for the sake of use.\n\nBut, on the contrary, to join battle in hand-to-hand fighting,\n\nand to tear limbs and stain the body with blood,\n\nexisted long before shining weapons flew;\n\nand nature compelled [men] to avoid a wound before\n\nthe left hand, through art, was given the protection of a shield.\n\nClearly, too, to entrust the weary body to rest\n\nis much more ancient than the soft spread coverings of a bed,\n\nand to quench thirst is prior to the birth of cups.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0057","text":"These things, therefore, which have been discovered from use and life,\n\ncan be believed to have been recognized for the sake of use.\n\nBut all those things are separate which, first born themselves,\n\nafterwards gave knowledge of their usefulness.\n\nAmong such things we see especially the senses and limbs;\n\ntherefore again and again it is far from possible for you to believe\n\nthat they could have been created for the service of usefulness.\n\nLikewise, it is not surprising in these matters\n\nthat the nature of the body itself seeks food for each living creature.\n\nFor indeed I have taught that many bodies flow and withdraw\n\nfrom things in many ways, but very many must [withdraw]\n\nfrom animals, because they are exercised in motion:\n\nmany are breathed out through the mouth when they pant languidly,\n\nand many, forced out from deep within, are carried away through sweat.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0058","text":"Thus by these things the body becomes rarefied and its whole\n\nnature is undermined, a process followed by pain.\n\nTherefore food is taken, that it may support the limbs\n\nand, distributed among them, restore their strength, and stop up\n\nthe open craving for eating through the limbs and veins.\n\nMoisture likewise goes into all whatever places\n\nrequire moisture; and many gathered bodies of heat,\n\nwhich supply fires to our stomach,\n\nthe liquid arriving disperses and extinguishes as though a fire,\n\nso that dry heat can no longer burn the limbs.\n\nThus, then, the panting thirst is washed away from our body;\n\nthus the hungry desire is filled.\n\nNow how it comes about that we can put forth strides\n\nwhen we wish, and why it has been granted to move the limbs,\n\nand what cause has accustomed [them] to thrust forward this great weight\n\nof our body, I shall say: take in my words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","text":"I say that first images of walking\n\nfall upon our mind and strike the mind, as we said before.\n\nThen will arises; for no one begins to do\n\nany action before the mind has first foreseen what it wishes.\n\nWhat it foresees consists in an image of that thing;\n\ntherefore, when the mind so moves itself that it wishes to go\n\nand step forward, it immediately strikes the force of spirit\n\ndispersed through the whole body, throughout the limbs and joints;\n\nand this is easy to do, since it is held in conjunction.\n\nThen that force in turn strikes the body, and so the whole mass\n\nis gradually thrust forward and moved.\n\nMoreover, then the body too becomes rarefied, and air—\n\nas indeed it must, since it is always mobile—\n\ncomes abundantly through the opened pores and penetrates,\n\nand is thus dispersed through all the minute parts\n\nof the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0060","text":"Here, therefore, it comes about in both cases,\n\nthat a body, like a ship, is borne by sails and wind.\n\nNor yet in these matters is it wonderful\n\nthat such tiny bodies can twist about so great a body\n\nand turn our whole weight; \n\nfor indeed wind, of subtle, thin body,\n\ndrives and impels with great force a great ship,\n\nand one hand guides it, however great its speed,\n\nand turns a single rudder wherever it pleases;\n\nand by pulleys and drums many a machine moves great weights\n\nand lifts them with a light exertion.\n\nNow by what means sleep pours rest through the limbs\n\nand releases from the breast the cares of the mind,\n\nI shall set forth in pleasing speech rather than in many verses,\n\nas the small song of the swan is better than the cry\n\nof cranes scattered through the aetherial clouds of the south wind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061","text":"Do you grant me attentive ears and a discerning mind,\n\nlest you deny that what I say can come to pass, and turn away\n\nfrom truths with a resisting breast,\n\nwhen you yourself are at fault and cannot discern it.\n\nFirst, sleep occurs when the force of the soul has been dispersed\n\nthrough the limbs, and in part has withdrawn, cast out abroad,\n\nand in part, driven farther inward, has retired into the depths;\n\nfor only then are the limbs dissolved and grow slack.\n\nFor there is no doubt that this sensation in us\n\nis the work of the soul; when sleep prevents it from existing,\n\nthen we must suppose that the soul is disturbed\n\nand cast forth—not all of it; for the body would lie\n\nsteeped in the eternal cold of death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0062","text":"Indeed, if no hidden part of the soul remained\n\nin the limbs, as fire lies hidden buried beneath much ash,\n\nwhence could sensation suddenly be rekindled\n\nthrough the limbs, as flame rises from hidden fire?\n\nBut by what causes this new condition is brought about, and whence\n\nthe soul can be disturbed and the body grow languid,\n\nI shall explain: do you see to it that I do not pour words into the winds.\n\nFirst, it is necessary that the body on its outer side,\n\nsince it is touched by the neighbouring blows of the air,\n\nbe struck and repeatedly beaten by its impact;\n\nand therefore nearly all things are covered either with hide,\n\nor even with shells, or with callus, or with bark.\n\nThis same air also beats the inner part in breathing,\n\nwhen it is drawn in and breathed back out.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0063","text":"Therefore, since the body is struck from either side, and since\n\nthe blows reach us through the small openings\n\nof the body to the primary parts and first elements,\n\nthere comes about, as it were, a gradual collapse through our limbs.\n\nFor the arrangements of the first-beginnings\n\nof body and soul are thrown into confusion. Thus part of the soul\n\nis drawn out, part hidden within recedes inward,\n\nand part, dispersed through the limbs, cannot remain\n\njoined with itself or perform reciprocal motion;\n\nfor nature blocks up its gatherings and pathways.\n\nTherefore sensation departs, the motions being profoundly changed.\n\nAnd since there is, as it were, nothing to support the limbs,\n\nthe body becomes weak and all the members languish;\n\nthe arms and eyelids droop, and the knees, in one lying down,\n\noften give way and relax their strength.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0064","text":"Then sleep follows food, because what air does,\n\nfood too, while it is distributed through all the veins,\n\nproduces. And that sleep is by far the heaviest\n\nwhich you take when sated or weary, because then very many\n\nbodies are thrown into confusion, battered by great labour.\n\nBy the same reasoning, a deeper withdrawal of part of the soul occurs,\n\nand a more abundant portion of it is cast outward,\n\nand it is more divided within itself and more dispersed.\n\nAnd whatever pursuit each person is generally bound to and clings to,\n\nor whatever matters we have long dwelt upon before,\n\nand in whatever occupation the mind has been more intent,\n\nthese same things we generally seem to pursue in dreams:\n\nadvocates to plead cases and compose laws,\n\ncommanders to fight and engage in battles,\n\nsailors to wage their contracted war with the winds,\n\nbut I to pursue this work and always to inquire into the nature of things\n\nand, once discovered, to set it forth in the writings of my fathers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0065","text":"Thus other pursuits and arts too are generally seen\n\nto hold men's minds deceived in dreams.\n\nAnd all who for many days in succession\n\nhave given continuous attention to games, we generally see,\n\nwhen they have now ceased to employ those things with their senses,\n\nstill to have open pathways remaining in the mind,\n\nby which the same images of things may come in.\n\nThus for many days these same things present themselves\n\nbefore their eyes, so that even while awake they seem\n\nto see dancers and moving supple limbs,\n\nand to receive in their ears the liquid song of the lyre and its speaking strings,\n\nand to see the same assembly and the varied splendours\n\nof the stage simultaneously shining.\n\nSo greatly do pursuit and inclination matter,\n\nand the things in which they have been accustomed to be occupied—\n\nnot human beings alone, but indeed all animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0066","text":"Indeed, you will see strong horses, while their limbs lie at rest,\n\nyet sweating and continually breathing in dreams,\n\nand as though striving with all their strength for the prize,\n\nor as though the barriers had been opened;\n\nand hunting dogs, often in gentle repose,\n\nyet suddenly toss their legs and all at once emit cries,\n\nand repeatedly draw back breath through their nostrils,\n\nas if they held the discovered tracks of wild beasts;\n\nand, awakened, they often pursue the empty images\n\nof deer, as though they saw them given over to flight,\n\nuntil, their errors shaken off, they return to themselves.\n\nBut the gentle home-bred brood of pups strive\n\nto shake themselves awake and lift their bodies from the ground,\n\njust as though they were looking upon unknown faces and visages.\n\nAnd the harsher the seeds of each kind are,\n\nthe more must they rage in dreams likewise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0067","text":"But birds of various kinds take flight and suddenly with their wings\n\ndisturb the groves of the gods in the night-time,\n\nif in gentle sleep they have seemed to engage in battles and combats,\n\npursuing and flying after hawks.\n\nMoreover, the minds of men, which accomplish great things with great motions,\n\nlikewise often act and perform in dreams:\n\nthey storm kings, are captured, mingle battles,\n\nraise a cry, as though they were being slaughtered there.\n\nMany fight desperately and utter groans of pain,\n\nand as though they were being bitten by a panther or fierce lion,\n\nthey fill everything with loud cries.\n\nMany speak in sleep of great affairs\n\nand have very often thereby betrayed their own deeds.\n\nMany meet death. Many, as though they were falling\n\nfrom high mountains to the ground with their whole body,\n\nare terrified and, as though mentally distraught, scarcely\n\nreturn to themselves from sleep, stirred by the agitation of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068","text":"Likewise, thirsty, one sits beside a river or a pleasant spring\n\nand all but takes the whole stream into his throat.\n\nOften, beside a pool or a vessel, pure boys,\n\nbound in sleep, think that they are lifting up their garment,\n\nand pour out the filtered moisture of their whole body,\n\nwhen Babylonian coverings are drenched in magnificent splendour.\n\nThen those into whose limbs the seed of age first insinuates itself,\n\nwhen the day itself has made them mature in body,\n\nare met from outside by images also, messengers\n\nof a distinguished face and beautiful colour;\n\nthese stir and irritate places swollen with much seed,\n\nso that, as though all things had often been accomplished, they pour forth\n\nthe waves of a mighty stream and stain the garment.\n\nThat seed of which we spoke before is set in motion in us\n\nwhen mature age first strengthens the limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","text":"For one thing moves and excites one thing, another another;\n\nfrom a human being, the force of a human being alone draws forth human seed.\n\nAs soon as it has been cast out from its own seats, it goes forth\n\nfrom the whole body through the limbs and members,\n\ngathering in the appointed places of the sinews and immediately exciting\n\nthe very regions of the generative part of the body.\n\nThe irritated places swell with seed, and there arises the desire\n\nto cast it forth whither fierce lust strains;\n\nand it seeks the body whence the mind is wounded by love.\n\nFor all generally fall toward the wound, and the blood leaps\n\ntoward that quarter whence we are struck by the blow;\n\nand, if he is near at hand, the red fluid occupies the foe.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0070","text":"Thus, then, one who receives a blow from Venus' weapons,\n\nwhether a boy with womanly limbs shoots him,\n\nor a woman casting love from her whole body,\n\nstrains toward the source of the wound and longs to unite\n\nand to cast moisture, drawn from body, into body;\n\nfor mute desire foresees pleasure.\n\nThis is Venus for us; hence too comes the name of Love,\n\nhence first into the heart distilled that drop of Venus' sweetness,\n\nand chilling care followed after;\n\nfor if that which you love is absent, nevertheless its images are at hand,\n\nand its sweet name comes before your ears.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","text":"But it is fitting to flee images and drive away from oneself\n\nthe food of love, to turn the mind elsewhere,\n\nand to cast the gathered moisture into whatever bodies,\n\nnot, once turned toward the love of one person, to retain it\n\nand store up for oneself care and certain pain.\n\nFor the ulcer lives again and grows inveterate by being fed,\n\nand day by day the madness increases and the misery grows heavier,\n\nunless you disturb the first wounds with new blows\n\nand, wandering, heal them while fresh by roaming Venus,\n\nor can transfer the motions of your mind elsewhere.\n\nNor is he who avoids love deprived of Venus' fruit,\n\nbut rather takes those advantages which are without penalty;\n\nfor surely the pleasure thence is purer for the healthy than for the miserable.\n\nFor even at the very moment of possession\n\nthe passion of lovers wavers in uncertain wanderings,\n\nnor is it settled what they should first enjoy with eyes and hands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0072","text":"What they have sought, they press hard; they inflict pain on the body\n\nand often drive their teeth into the lips\n\nand fasten kisses there, because the pleasure is not pure,\n\nand beneath lie stings that urge them to hurt that very thing,\n\nwhatever it is, whence those seeds of frenzy arise.\n\nBut Venus lightly breaks the penalties amid love,\n\nand mingled pleasure restrains the biting.\n\nFor therein is the hope that, from the same body\n\nwhence the origin of the ardour comes, the flame can also be quenched.\n\nThat nature wholly resists being so.\n\nAnd this is the one thing of which the more we have,\n\nthe more the breast burns with dreadful desire.\n\nFor food and moisture are taken into the limbs within;\n\nand because they can occupy fixed places,\n\nthis desire for liquids and food is easily filled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0073","text":"But from a human face and beautiful colour\n\nnothing is given to the body but thin images to enjoy;\n\noften wretched hope is snatched away by the wind.\n\nAs when, thirsty in dreams, a man seeks to drink, and no moisture\n\nis given that can extinguish the burning in his limbs,\n\nbut he seeks images of liquid and labours in vain,\n\nand, drinking in the midst of a rushing river, remains thirsty,\n\nso in love Venus toys with lovers by images,\n\nnor can they satisfy themselves by looking upon bodies face to face,\n\nnor can their wandering hands rub off anything\n\nfrom the tender limbs, uncertain over the whole body.\n\nFinally, when with joined limbs they enjoy the flower\n\nof youth, when now the body foresees joys\n\nand Venus is on the point of sowing the woman's fields,\n\nthey eagerly fasten body to body and mingle their saliva\n\nfrom their mouths, and breathe in, pressing mouths with teeth—\n\nyet to no purpose, since they cannot rub anything off from there,\n\nnor penetrate and pass with their whole body into body;\n\nfor sometimes they seem to wish and strive to do this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0074","text":"So eagerly do they cling in Venus' bonds,\n\nuntil the limbs, weakened by the force of pleasure, melt away.\n\nAt last, when the desire launched from the sinews has burst forth,\n\nthere comes for a little while a brief pause in the violent ardour.\n\nThen the same frenzy returns and that madness revisits them,\n\nwhen they seek to attain what they themselves desire,\n\nand cannot discover what device may conquer that evil.\n\nSo uncertainly do they waste away with a hidden wound.\n\nAdd that they consume their strength and perish through labour;\n\nadd that life is lived at another's nod;\n\nduties languish and reputation, sick and wavering, fails.\n\nMeanwhile substance slips away; Babylonian\n\nointments are procured, and beautiful Sicyonian slippers smile upon the feet;\n\nindeed large emeralds with green light\n\nare set in gold, and sea-purple cloth is continually worn away\n\nand, exercised, drinks in Venus' sweat.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0075","text":"And wealth well won by the fathers becomes diadems and turbans,\n\nsometimes they turn it into a mantle and Alidensian and Cean garments.\n\nBanquets with exquisite clothing and fare, games,\n\nfrequent cups, ointments, crowns, garlands are prepared—\n\nto no purpose, since from the midst of the fountain of delights\n\nsomething bitter rises that chokes even amid the flowers:\n\neither when the conscious mind itself perhaps reproaches itself\n\nfor spending life idly and perishing in brothels,\n\nor because she has left behind some word cast out ambiguously,\n\nwhich, fixed in the desirous heart, lives again like fire;\n\nor because he thinks that she casts her eyes about too freely or looks upon another,\n\nand sees in her face traces of a smile.\n\nAnd these evils are found in love at its height and greatest prosperity;\n\nbut in adverse and impoverished love there are innumerable evils,\n\nwhich you may grasp with eyes unclosed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","text":"Innumerable; so that it is better to be watchful beforehand,\n\nin the manner I have taught, and take care not to be enticed.\n\nFor to avoid being struck by the darts of love\n\nis not so difficult as, once caught in the nets themselves,\n\nto get out and break through Venus' stout knots.\n\nAnd yet, even entangled and caught in the noose, you can\n\nescape the hostile thing, unless you yourself stand in your own way\n\nand first overlook all the faults of mind,\n\nor of body, of her whom you seek and desire.\n\nFor men, commonly blinded by desire,\n\ndo this and attribute to them advantages which are not truly theirs.\n\nThus we see in many ways women who are misshapen and ugly\n\nheld dear and flourishing in the highest honour.\n\nAnd one mocks another and advises him to propitiate Venus,\n\nsince he is afflicted by foul love,\n\nwhile the wretches often do not look back upon their own greatest evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0077","text":"The black woman is “honey-coloured”; the filthy and foul-smelling one, “unadorned”;\n\nthe grey-eyed one, “Pallas”; the sinewy and wooden one, “a gazelle.”\n\nThe tiny, dwarfish girl is “one little Grace,” all sheer salt;\n\nthe huge and monstrous one is “a cataplexy,” and full of dignity.\n\nThe stammerer cannot speak: she is “lisping”; the mute woman is modest.\n\nBut the fiery, hateful, talkative woman becomes a “Lampadium.”\n\nThen she becomes a “slender darling” when she cannot live\n\nfor emaciation; and she who is already dead with a cough is truly “delicate.”\n\nBut the excessively fat and big-breasted woman is Ceres herself from Iacchus;\n\nthe snub-nosed is Silenus and Satyr, the thick-lipped woman a “kiss.”\n\nIt would take too long, if I tried to mention the rest of this kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0078","text":"But let her now be of however great distinction of face,\n\nfrom whose limbs the power of Venus rises in all its force;\n\nsurely there are other women too; surely we lived without this one before;\n\nsurely she does, and we know that she does, everything that an ugly woman does,\n\nand poor wretch fumigates herself with foul odours,\n\nwhile her maidservants flee far away and secretly laugh.\n\nBut the lover, weeping, shut out, often covers the threshold\n\nwith flowers and garlands, anoints the proud doorposts\n\nwith marjoram-oil, and wretchedly plants kisses upon the doors.\n\nIf, once admitted, he should encounter but a single breath of odour as he comes in,\n\nhe would seek honourable reasons for departing,\n\nand his complaint, long meditated and drawn from deep within, would fall;\n\nand there he would condemn himself for folly,\n\nwhen he saw that he had granted her more\n\nthan is right to concede to a mortal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0079","text":"Nor does this escape our Venuses; therefore all the more carefully\n\nthey conceal all the backstage of life from those\n\nwhom they wish to hold fast and bound in love—\n\nto no purpose, since you can nevertheless draw everything\n\nforth into the light with your mind and investigate every cause of laughter,\n\nand, if she is kindly disposed and not disagreeable, in turn\n\noverlook it and make allowance for human things.\n\nNor does a woman always sigh with feigned love,\n\nwhen, embracing, she joins her body to a man's body\n\nand holds him, moistening kisses with lips drawn in;\n\nfor she often does it from the heart and, seeking mutual\n\njoys, urges love's course to run its full length.\n\nNor by any other reason could birds, herds, wild beasts,\n\nand flocks and mares submit to males,\n\nif the abundant nature of the females did not itself grow hot\n\nand gladly receive again the Venus of those that leap upon them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0080","text":"Do you not see also how those whom mutual pleasure has often bound are tortured, as though in common chains, when at crossroads dogs, often eager to depart in different directions, strain greedily with all their utmost strength, while meanwhile they cling fast in the strong bonds of Venus? This they would never do unless they knew mutual joys which could cast them into a snare and hold them bound. Therefore, again and again, as I say, the pleasure is shared.\n\nAnd when, by the mingling of seed, the woman has by chance overcome the man's force and seized it by sudden force, then offspring become like their mothers through the maternal seed, or like their fathers through the paternal. But those whom you see to be of the form of both, blending together the features of their parents, grow from the father's body and the mother's blood, when, stirred through the limbs by the goads of Venus, the seeds have clashed in encounter, the mutual ardor breathing together; and neither of the two has prevailed nor has been prevailed over.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","text":"It also happens sometimes that they may be born resembling their grandfathers and often reproduce the forms of their great-grandfathers, because the parents often conceal in their bodies many first-beginnings mingled in many ways, which fathers, sprung from their stock, hand down from fathers. From these Venus produces forms in varied allotment, and restores the faces, voices, and hair of ancestors; since these arise from no more determinate a seed than do our faces and bodies and limbs.\n\nAnd a female offspring arises from the father's seed, and males come into being formed from the mother's body; for offspring always consists of a twofold seed, and whatever is created is more like that parent of whose share it has more than an equal part. This you may discern, whether the offspring is male or whether it has a female origin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","text":"Nor do divine powers deprive anyone of generative capacity, so that he is never called father by sweet children and passes his life in barren Venus; though people generally think this, and sadly sprinkle the altars with much blood and load the shrines with offerings, that they may render their wives pregnant with abundant seed. Yet in vain do they weary the divine power and the lots. For some are sterile through seed that is too thick, and others, conversely, through seed that is fluid and thinner than is right.\n\nBecause the thin seed cannot adhere and fasten in the proper places, it at once flows away and, recalled, yields in miscarriage. But because the thicker seed is emitted more congealed than is fitting, either it does not fly forth in so extended a stroke, or it cannot penetrate the places equally, or, having penetrated, the seed is with difficulty mingled with the woman's seed. For the harmonies of Venus seem to differ greatly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0083","text":"And some men impregnate some women more readily, and some women receive the burden more readily from some men and grow heavy. And many women were sterile in several marriages before, yet afterward found husbands by whom they could conceive children and become rich in sweet offspring.\n\nAnd for those whose fertile wives had often previously been unable to bear at home, a nature suited to them too has been found, so that they might fortify their old age with children. So greatly does it matter that seeds be able to mingle with seeds in a generatively fitting way, and that thick ones meet with fluid and fluid with thick. And in this matter it is important by what diet life is sustained; for by some things the seeds are made thick in the limbs, and by others they are thinned and wasted in turn; and it matters in what ways the delightful pleasure itself is pursued.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0084","text":"This too matters very greatly; for wives are generally thought to conceive more in the manner of beasts and four-footed creatures, because in this way they can take in the seeds, with their breasts placed down and their loins raised. Nor do wives need soft movements at all. For a woman prevents herself from conceiving and resists if, joyfully, she draws back Venus from the man with her buttocks and sets all the waves in motion from her unboned breast; for she ejects the furrow from the straight line and course of the plowshare and turns aside the blow of the seed from its places. And courtesans have accustomed themselves to move for this reason: lest they be frequently filled and lie pregnant, and at the same time that Venus itself might be more pleasing to men; which seems to be of no use to our wives.\n\nNor is it by divine intervention and Venus' arrows that sometimes a little woman of inferior beauty is loved; for sometimes the woman herself, by her own deeds and accommodating ways and by a body kept neatly groomed, causes a man easily to grow accustomed to spending his life with her. Besides, habit makes love harmonious; for whatever, however lightly, is struck by frequent blows is nevertheless overcome and gives way over a long stretch of time. Do you not see too how drops of water falling upon rocks pierce the rocks over a long stretch of time?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0084"]}],"source_digest":"67424cc98edc9856480a25b4be6a6f800fa1d0698d45ba3ff97c7fb52749f97e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Liber Quartus"}],"passage_basis":"revised-passages","original_title":"Liber Quartus","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2","title":"Book Five","order":5,"chapter_id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2","text":"## Liber Quintus\n\n  \n\nQuis potis est dignum pollenti pectore carmen\n\n \n\ncondere pro rerum maiestate hisque repertis?\n\n \n\nquisve valet verbis tantum, qui fingere laudes\n\n \n\npro meritis eius possit, qui talia nobis\n\n \n\npectore parta suo quaesitaque praemia liquit?\n\n \n\nnemo, ut opinor, erit mortali corpore cretus.\n\n \n\nnam si, ut ipsa petit maiestas cognita rerum,\n\n \n\ndicendum est, deus ille fuit, deus, inclyte Memmi,\n\n \n\nqui princeps vitae rationem invenit eam quae\n\n \n\nnunc appellatur sapientia, quique per artem\n\n \n\nfluctibus et tantis vitam tantisque tenebris\n\n \n\nin tam tranquillo et tam clara luce locavit.\n\n \n\nconfer enim divina aliorum antiqua reperta.\n\n \n\nnamque Ceres fertur fruges Liberque liquoris\n\n \n\nvitigeni laticem mortalibus instituisse;\n\n \n\ncum tamen his posset sine rebus vita manere,\n\n \n\nut fama est aliquas etiam nunc vivere gentis.\n\n \n\nat bene non poterat sine puro pectore vivi;\n\n \n\nquo magis hic merito nobis deus esse videtur,\n\n \n\nex quo nunc etiam per magnas didita gentis\n\n \n\ndulcia permulcent animos solacia vitae.\n\n \n\nHerculis antistare autem si facta putabis,\n\n \n\nlongius a vera multo ratione ferere.\n\n \n\nquid Nemeaeus enim nobis nunc magnus hiatus\n\n \n\nille leonis obesset et horrens Arcadius sus,\n\n \n\ntanto opere officerent nobis Stymphala colentes?\n\n \n\ndenique quid Cretae taurus Lernaeaque pestis\n\n \n\nhydra venenatis posset vallata colubris?\n\n \n\nquidve tripectora tergemini vis Geryonai\n\n \n\net Diomedis equi spirantes naribus ignem\n\n \n\nThracia Bistoniasque plagas atque Ismara propter\n\n \n\naureaque Hesperidum servans fulgentia mala,\n\n \n\nasper, acerba tuens, immani corpore serpens\n\n \n\narboris amplexus stirpes? quid denique obesset\n\n \n\npropter Atlanteum litus pelagique severa,\n\n \n\nquo neque noster adit quisquam nec barbarus audet?\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc quae sunt portenta perempta,\n\n \n\nsi non victa forent, quid tandem viva nocerent?\n\n \n\nnil, ut opinor: ita ad satiatem terra ferarum\n\n \n\nnunc etiam scatit et trepido terrore repleta est\n\n \n\nper nemora ac montes magnos silvasque profundas;\n\n \n\nquae loca vitandi plerumque est nostra potestas.\n\n \n\nat nisi purgatumst pectus, quae proelia nobis\n\n \n\natque pericula tumst ingratis insinuandum!\n\n \n\nquantae tum scindunt hominem cuppedinis acres\n\n \n\nsollicitum curae quantique perinde timores!\n\n \n\nquidve superbia spurcitia ac petulantia? quantas\n\n \n\nefficiunt clades! quid luxus desidiaeque?\n\n \n\nhaec igitur qui cuncta subegerit ex animoque\n\n \n\nexpulerit dictis, non armis, nonne decebit\n\n \n\nhunc hominem numero divom dignarier esse?\n\n \n\ncum bene praesertim multa ac divinitus ipsis\n\n \n\niam mortalibus e divis dare dicta suerit\n\n \n\natque omnem rerum naturam pandere dictis.\n\n  \n\nCuius ego ingressus vestigia dum rationes\n\n \n\npersequor ac doceo dictis, quo quaeque creata\n\n \n\nfoedere sint, in eo quam sit durare necessum\n\n \n\nnec validas valeant aevi rescindere leges,\n\n \n\nquo genere in primis animi natura reperta est\n\n \n\nnativo primum consistere corpore creta,\n\n \n\nnec posse incolumem magnum durare per aevum,\n\n \n\nsed simulacra solere in somnis fallere mentem,\n\n \n\ncernere cum videamur eum quem vita reliquit,\n\n \n\nquod super est, nunc huc rationis detulit ordo,\n\n \n\nut mihi mortali consistere corpore mundum\n\n \n\nnativomque simul ratio reddunda sit esse;\n\n \n\net quibus ille modis congressus materiai\n\n \n\nfundarit terram caelum mare sidera solem\n\n \n\nlunaique globum; tum quae tellure animantes\n\n \n\nextiterint, et quae nullo sint tempore natae;\n\n \n\nquove modo genus humanum variante loquella\n\n \n\ncoeperit inter se vesci per nomina rerum;\n\n \n\net quibus ille modis divom metus insinuarit\n\n \n\npectora, terrarum qui in orbi sancta tuetur\n\n \n\nfana lacus lucos aras simulacraque divom.\n\n \n\npraeterea solis cursus lunaeque meatus\n\n \n\nexpediam qua vi flectat natura gubernans;\n\n \n\nne forte haec inter caelum terramque reamur\n\n \n\nlibera sponte sua cursus lustrare perennis,\n\n \n\nmorigera ad fruges augendas atque animantis,\n\n \n\nneve aliqua divom volvi ratione putemus.\n\n \n\nnam bene qui didicere deos securum agere aevom,\n\n \n\nsi tamen interea mirantur qua ratione\n\n \n\nquaeque geri possint, praesertim rebus in illis\n\n \n\nquae supera caput aetheriis cernuntur in oris,\n\n \n\nrursus in antiquas referuntur religiones\n\n \n\net dominos acris adsciscunt, omnia posse\n\n \n\nquos miseri credunt, ignari quid queat esse,\n\n \n\nquid nequeat, finita potestas denique cuique\n\n \n\nqua nam sit ratione atque alte terminus haerens.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, ne te in promissis plura moremur,\n\n \n\nprincipio maria ac terras caelumque tuere;\n\n \n\nquorum naturam triplicem, tria corpora, Memmi,\n\n \n\ntris species tam dissimilis, tria talia texta,\n\n \n\nuna dies dabit exitio, multosque per annos\n\n \n\nsustentata ruet moles et machina mundi.\n\n \n\nnec me animi fallit quam res nova miraque menti\n\n \n\naccidat exitium caeli terraeque futurum,\n\n \n\net quam difficile id mihi sit pervincere dictis;\n\n \n\nut fit ubi insolitam rem adportes auribus ante\n\n \n\nnec tamen hanc possis oculorum subdere visu\n\n \n\nnec iacere indu manus, via qua munita fidei\n\n \n\nproxima fert humanum in pectus templaque mentis.\n\n \n\nsed tamen effabor. dictis dabit ipsa fidem res\n\n \n\nforsitan et graviter terrarum motibus ortis\n\n \n\nomnia conquassari in parvo tempore cernes.\n\n \n\nquod procul a nobis flectat fortuna gubernans,\n\n \n\net ratio potius quam res persuadeat ipsa\n\n \n\n succidere horrisono posse omnia victa fragore.\n\n  \n\nQua prius adgrediar quam de re fundere fata\n\n \n\nsanctius et multo certa ratione magis quam\n\n \n\nPythia quae tripode a Phoebi lauroque profatur,\n\n \n\nmulta tibi expediam doctis solacia dictis;\n\n \n\nreligione refrenatus ne forte rearis\n\n \n\nterras et solem et caelum, mare sidera lunam,\n\n \n\ncorpore divino debere aeterna manere,\n\n \n\nproptereaque putes ritu par esse Gigantum\n\n \n\npendere eos poenas inmani pro scelere omnis,\n\n \n\nqui ratione sua disturbent moenia mundi\n\n \n\npraeclarumque velint caeli restinguere solem\n\n \n\ninmortalia mortali sermone notantes;\n\n \n\nquae procul usque adeo divino a numine distent\n\n \n\ninque deum numero quae sint indigna videri,\n\n \n\nnotitiam potius praebere ut posse putentur\n\n \n\nquid sit vitali motu sensuque remotum.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim non est, cum quovis corpore ut esse\n\n \n\nposse animi natura putetur consiliumque.\n\n \n\nsicut in aethere non arbor, non aequore salso\n\n \n\nnubes esse queunt neque pisces vivere in arvis\n\n \n\nnec cruor in lignis neque saxis sucus inesse,\n\n \n\ncertum ac dispositumst ubi quicquid crescat et insit,\n\n \n\nsic animi natura nequit sine corpore oriri\n\n \n\nsola neque a nervis et sanguine longius esse.\n\n \n\nquod si posset enim, multo prius ipsa animi vis\n\n \n\nin capite aut umeris aut imis calcibus esse\n\n \n\nposset et innasci quavis in parte soleret,\n\n \n\ntandem in eodem homine atque in eodem vase manere.\n\n \n\nquod quoniam nostro quoque constat corpore certum\n\n \n\ndispositumque videtur ubi esse et crescere possit\n\n \n\nseorsum anima atque animus, tanto magis infitiandum\n\n \n\ntotum posse extra corpus formamque animalem\n\n \n\nputribus in glebis terrarum aut solis in igni\n\n \n\naut in aqua durare aut altis aetheris oris.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur constant divino praedita sensu,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem nequeunt vitaliter esse animata.\n\n  \n\nIllud item non est ut possis credere, sedes\n\n \n\nesse deum sanctas in mundi partibus ullis.\n\n \n\ntenvis enim natura deum longeque remota\n\n \n\nsensibus ab nostris animi vix mente videtur;\n\n \n\nquae quoniam manuum tactum suffugit et ictum,\n\n \n\ntactile nil nobis quod sit contingere debet;\n\n \n\ntangere enim non quit quod tangi non licet ipsum.\n\n \n\nquare etiam sedes quoque nostris sedibus esse\n\n \n\ndissimiles debent, tenues de corpore eorum;\n\n \n\nquae tibi posterius largo sermone probabo.\n\n \n\nDicere porro hominum causa voluisse parare\n\n \n\npraeclaram mundi naturam proptereaque\n\n \n\nadlaudabile opus divom laudare decere\n\n \n\naeternumque putare atque inmortale futurum,\n\n \n\nnec fas esse, deum quod sit ratione vetusta\n\n \n\ngentibus humanis fundatum perpetuo aevo,\n\n \n\nsollicitare suis ulla vi ex sedibus umquam\n\n \n\nnec verbis vexare et ab imo evertere summa,\n\n \n\n cetera de genere hoc adfingere et addere, Memmi,\n\n \n\ndesiperest. quid enim inmortalibus atque beatis\n\n \n\ngratia nostra queat largirier emolumenti,\n\n \n\nut nostra quicquam causa gerere adgrediantur?\n\n \n\nquidve novi potuit tanto post ante quietos\n\n \n\ninlicere ut cuperent vitam mutare priorem?\n\n \n\nnam gaudere novis rebus debere videtur\n\n \n\ncui veteres obsunt; sed cui nihil accidit aegri\n\n \n\ntempore in ante acto, cum pulchre degeret aevom,\n\n \n\nquid potuit novitatis amorem accendere tali?\n\n \n\nquidve mali fuerat nobis non esse creatis?\n\n \n\nan, credo, in tenebris vita ac maerore iacebat,\n\n \n\ndonec diluxit rerum genitalis origo?\n\n \n\nnatus enim debet qui cumque est velle manere\n\n \n\nin vita, donec retinebit blanda voluptas;\n\n \n\nqui numquam vero vitae gustavit amorem\n\n \n\nnec fuit in numero, quid obest non esse creatum?\n\n \n\nexemplum porro gignundis rebus et ipsa\n\n \n\nnotities hominum divis unde insita primum est,\n\n \n\nquid vellent facere ut scirent animoque viderent,\n\n \n\nquove modost umquam vis cognita principiorum\n\n \n\nquidque inter sese permutato ordine possent.\n\n \n\nsi non ipsa dedit speciem natura creandi?\n\n \n\nnamque ita multa modis multis primordia rerum\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore percita plagis\n\n \n\nponderibusque suis consuerunt concita ferri\n\n \n\nomnimodisque coire atque omnia pertemptare,\n\n \n\nquae cumque inter se possint congressa creare,\n\n \n\nut non sit mirum, si in talis disposituras\n\n \n\ndeciderunt quoque et in talis venere meatus,\n\n \n\nqualibus haec rerum geritur nunc summa novando.\n\n  \n\nQuod  iam rerum ignorem primordia quae sint,\n\n \n\nhoc tamen ex ipsis caeli rationibus ausim\n\n \n\nconfirmare aliisque ex rebus reddere multis,\n\n \n\nnequaquam nobis divinitus esse paratam\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum: tanta stat praedita culpa.\n\n \n\nprincipio quantum caeli tegit impetus ingens,\n\n \n\ninde avidam partem montes silvaeque ferarum\n\n \n\npossedere, tenent rupes vastaeque paludes\n\n \n\net mare, quod late terrarum distinet oras.\n\n \n\ninde duas porro prope partis fervidus ardor\n\n \n\nadsiduusque geli casus mortalibus aufert.\n\n \n\nquod super est arvi, tamen id natura sua vi\n\n \n\nsentibus obducat, ni vis humana resistat\n\n \n\nvitai causa valido consueta bidenti\n\n \n\ningemere et terram pressis proscindere aratris.\n\n \n\nsi non fecundas vertentes vomere glebas\n\n \n\nterraique solum subigentes cimus ad ortus.\n\n \n\nsponte sua nequeant liquidas existere in auras.\n\n \n\net tamen inter dum magno quaesita labore\n\n \n\ncum iam per terras frondent atque omnia florent,\n\n \n\naut nimiis torret fervoribus aetherius sol\n\n \n\naut subiti peremunt imbris gelidaeque pruinae\n\n \n\nflabraque ventorum violento turbine vexant.\n\n \n\npraeterea genus horriferum natura ferarum\n\n \n\n humanae genti infestum terraque marique\n\n \n\ncur alit atque auget? cur anni tempora morbos\n\n \n\nadportant? quare mors inmatura vagatur?\n\n \n\ntum porro puer, ut saevis proiectus ab undis\n\n \n\nnavita, nudus humi iacet infans indigus omni\n\n \n\nvitali auxilio, cum primum in luminis oras\n\n \n\nnixibus ex alvo matris natura profudit,\n\n \n\nvagituque locum lugubri complet, ut aequumst\n\n \n\ncui tantum in vita restet transire malorum.\n\n \n\nat variae crescunt pecudes armenta feraeque\n\n \n\nnec crepitacillis opus est nec cuiquam adhibendast\n\n \n\nalmae nutricis blanda atque infracta loquella\n\n \n\nnec varias quaerunt vestes pro tempore caeli,\n\n \n\ndenique non armis opus est, non moenibus altis,\n\n \n\nqui sua tutentur, quando omnibus omnia large\n\n \n\ntellus ipsa parit naturaque daedala rerum.\n\n  \n\nPrincipio quoniam terrai corpus et umor\n\n \n\naurarumque leves animae calidique vapores,\n\n \n\ne quibus haec rerum consistere summa videtur,\n\n \n\nomnia nativo ac mortali corpore constant,\n\n \n\ndebet eodem omnis mundi natura putari.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim, quorum partis et membra videmus\n\n \n\ncorpore nativo mortalibus esse figuris,\n\n \n\nhaec eadem ferme mortalia cernimus esse\n\n \n\net nativa simul. qua propter maxima mundi\n\n \n\ncum videam membra ac partis consumpta regigni,\n\n \n\nscire licet caeli quoque item terraeque fuisse\n\n \n\nprincipiale aliquod tempus clademque futuram.\n\n \n\nIllud in his rebus ne corripuisse rearis\n\n \n\nme mihi, quod terram atque ignem mortalia sumpsi\n\n \n\nesse neque umorem dubitavi aurasque perire\n\n \n\natque eadem gigni rursusque augescere dixi.\n\n \n\nprincipio pars terrai non nulla, perusta\n\n \n\nsolibus adsiduis, multa pulsata pedum vi,\n\n \n\npulveris exhalat nebulam nubesque volantis,\n\n \n\nquas validi toto dispergunt aëre venti.\n\n \n\npars etiam glebarum ad diluviem revocatur\n\n \n\nimbribus et ripas radentia flumina rodunt.\n\n \n\npraeterea pro parte sua, quod cumque alit auget,\n\n \n\nredditur; et quoniam dubio procul esse videtur\n\n \n\nomniparens eadem rerum commune sepulcrum.\n\n \n\nergo terra tibi libatur et aucta recrescit.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, umore novo mare flumina fontes\n\n \n\nsemper abundare et latices manare perennis\n\n \n\nnil opus est verbis: magnus decursus aquarum\n\n \n\nundique declarat. sed primum quicquid aquai\n\n \n\ntollitur in summaque fit ut nihil umor abundet,\n\n \n\npartim quod validi verrentes aequora venti\n\n \n\ndeminuunt radiisque retexens aetherius sol,\n\n \n\npartim quod supter per terras diditur omnis;\n\n \n\npercolatur enim virus retroque remanat\n\n \n\nmateries umoris et ad caput amnibus omnis\n\n \n\nconvenit, inde super terras fluit agmine dulci\n\n \n\nqua via secta semel liquido pede detulit undas.\n\n  \n\nAëra nunc igitur dicam, qui corpore toto\n\n \n\n innumerabiliter privas mutatur in horas.\n\n \n\nsemper enim, quod cumque fluit de rebus, id omne\n\n \n\naëris in magnum fertur mare; qui nisi contra\n\n \n\ncorpora retribuat rebus recreetque fluentis,\n\n \n\nomnia iam resoluta forent et in aëra versa.\n\n \n\nhaut igitur cessat gigni de rebus et in res\n\n \n\nreccidere, adsidue quoniam fluere omnia constat.\n\n  \n\nLargus item liquidi fons luminis, aetherius sol,\n\n \n\ninrigat adsidue caelum candore recenti\n\n \n\nsuppeditatque novo confestim lumine lumen.\n\n \n\nnam primum quicquid fulgoris disperit ei,\n\n \n\nquo cumque accidit. id licet hinc cognoscere possis,\n\n \n\nquod simul ac primum nubes succedere soli\n\n \n\ncoepere et radios inter quasi rumpere lucis,\n\n \n\nextemplo inferior pars horum disperit omnis\n\n \n\nterraque inumbratur qua nimbi cumque feruntur;\n\n \n\nut noscas splendore novo res semper egere\n\n \n\net primum iactum fulgoris quemque perire\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia res posse in sole videri,\n\n \n\nperpetuo ni suppeditet lucis caput ipsum.\n\n \n\nquin etiam nocturna tibi, terrestria quae sunt,\n\n \n\nlumina, pendentes lychni claraeque coruscis\n\n \n\nfulguribus pingues multa caligine taedae\n\n \n\nconsimili properant ratione, ardore ministro,\n\n \n\nsuppeditare novom lumen, tremere ignibus instant,\n\n \n\ninstant, nec loca lux inter quasi rupta relinquit:\n\n \n\nusque adeo properanter ab omnibus ignibus ei\n\n \n\nexitium celeri celeratur origine flammae.\n\n \n\nsic igitur solem lunam stellasque putandum\n\n \n\nex alio atque alio lucem iactare subortu\n\n \n\net primum quicquid flammarum perdere semper,\n\n \n\ninviolabilia haec ne credas forte vigere.\n\n  \n\nDenique non lapides quoque vinci cernis ab aevo,\n\n \n\nnon altas turris ruere et putrescere saxa,\n\n \n\nnon delubra deum simulacraque fessa fatisci\n\n \n\nnec sanctum numen fati protollere finis\n\n \n\nposse neque adversus naturae foedera niti?\n\n \n\ndenique non monimenta virum dilapsa videmus,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nnon ruere avolsos silices a montibus altis\n\n \n\nnec validas aevi vires perferre patique\n\n \n\nfiniti? neque enim caderent avolsa repente,\n\n \n\nex infinito quae tempore pertolerassent\n\n \n\nomnia tormenta aetatis, privata fragore.\n\n  \n\nDenique iam tuere hoc, circum supraque quod omne\n\n \n\ncontinet amplexu terram: si procreat ex se\n\n \n\nomnia, quod quidam memorant, recipitque perempta,\n\n \n\ntotum nativum mortali corpore constat.\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque alias ex se res auget alitque,\n\n \n\ndeminui debet, recreari, cum recipit res.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea si nulla fuit genitalis origo\n\n \n\nterrarum et caeli semperque aeterna fuere,\n\n \n\ncur supera bellum Thebanum et funera Troiae\n\n \n\nnon alias alii quoque res cecinere poëtae?\n\n \n\nquo tot facta virum totiens cecidere neque usquam\n\n \n\n aeternis famae monimentis insita florent?\n\n \n\nverum, ut opinor, habet novitatem summa recensque\n\n \n\nnaturast mundi neque pridem exordia cepit.\n\n \n\nquare etiam quaedam nunc artes expoliuntur,\n\n \n\nnunc etiam augescunt; nunc addita navigiis sunt\n\n \n\nmulta, modo organici melicos peperere sonores,\n\n \n\ndenique natura haec rerum ratioque repertast\n\n \n\nnuper, et hanc primus cum primis ipse repertus\n\n \n\nnunc ego sum in patrias qui possim vertere voces.\n\n \n\nQuod si forte fuisse ante hac eadem omnia credis,\n\n \n\nsed periise hominum torrenti saecla vapore,\n\n \n\naut cecidisse urbis magno vexamine mundi,\n\n \n\naut ex imbribus adsiduis exisse rapaces\n\n \n\nper terras amnes atque oppida coperuisse.\n\n \n\ntanto quique magis victus fateare necessest\n\n \n\nexitium quoque terrarum caelique futurum;\n\n \n\nnam cum res tantis morbis tantisque periclis\n\n \n\ntemptarentur, ibi si tristior incubuisset\n\n \n\ncausa, darent late cladem magnasque ruinas.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia mortales esse videmur,\n\n \n\ninter nos nisi quod morbis aegrescimus isdem\n\n \n\natque illi quos a vita natura removit.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea quae cumque manent aeterna necessust\n\n \n\naut, quia sunt solido cum corpore, respuere ictus\n\n \n\nnec penetrare pati sibi quicquam quod queat artas\n\n \n\ndissociare intus partis, ut materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora sunt, quorum naturam ostendimus ante,\n\n \n\naut ideo durare aetatem posse per omnem,\n\n \n\nplagarum quia sunt expertia, sicut inane est,\n\n \n\nquod manet intactum neque ab ictu fungitur hilum,\n\n \n\naut etiam quia nulla loci sit copia circum,\n\n \n\nquo quasi res possint discedere dissoluique,\n\n \n\nsicut summarum summa est aeterna, neque extra\n\n \n\nqui locus est quo dissiliant neque corpora sunt quae\n\n \n\npossint incidere et valida dissolvere plaga.\n\n \n\nat neque, uti docui, solido cum corpore mundi\n\n \n\nnaturast, quoniam admixtumst in rebus inane,\n\n \n\nnec tamen est ut inane, neque autem corpora desunt,\n\n \n\nex infinito quae possint forte coorta\n\n \n\ncorruere hanc rerum violento turbine summam\n\n \n\naut aliam quamvis cladem inportare pericli,\n\n \n\nnec porro natura loci spatiumque profundi\n\n \n\ndeficit, exspargi quo possint moenia mundi,\n\n \n\naut alia quavis possunt vi pulsa perire.\n\n \n\nhaut igitur leti praeclusa est ianua caelo\n\n \n\nnec soli terraeque neque altis aequoris undis,\n\n \n\nsed patet immani et vasto respectat hiatu.\n\n \n\nquare etiam nativa necessumst confiteare\n\n \n\nhaec eadem; neque enim, mortali corpore quae sunt,\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore adhuc potuissent\n\n \n\ninmensi validas aevi contemnere vires.\n\n  \n\nDenique tantopere inter se cum maxima mundi\n\n \n\npugnent membra, pio nequaquam concita bello,\n\n \n\nnonne vides aliquam longi certaminis ollis\n\n \n\nposse dari finem, vel cum sol et vapor omnis\n\n \n\n omnibus epotis umoribus exsuperarint?\n\n \n\nquod facere intendunt, neque adhuc conata patrantur;\n\n \n\ntantum suppeditant amnes ultraque minantur\n\n \n\nomnia diluviare ex alto gurgite ponti:\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam verrentes aequora venti\n\n \n\ndeminuunt radiisque retexens aetherius sol,\n\n \n\net siccare prius confidunt omnia posse\n\n \n\nquam liquor incepti possit contingere finem.\n\n \n\ntantum spirantes aequo certamine bellum\n\n \n\nmagnis  de rebus cernere certant,\n\n \n\ncum semel interea fuerit superantior ignis\n\n \n\net semel, ut fama est, umor regnarit in arvis.\n\n \n\nignis enim superavit et ambiens multa perussit,\n\n \n\navia cum Phaethonta rapax vis solis equorum\n\n \n\naethere raptavit toto terrasque per omnis.\n\n \n\nat pater omnipotens ira tum percitus acri\n\n \n\nmagnanimum Phaethonta repenti fulminis ictu\n\n \n\ndeturbavit equis in terram, Solque cadenti\n\n \n\nobvius aeternam succepit lampada mundi\n\n \n\ndisiectosque redegit equos iunxitque trementis,\n\n \n\ninde suum per iter recreavit cuncta gubernans,\n\n \n\nscilicet ut veteres Graium cecinere poëtae.\n\n \n\nquod procul a vera nimis est ratione repulsum.\n\n \n\nignis enim superare potest ubi materiai\n\n \n\nex infinito sunt corpora plura coorta;\n\n \n\ninde cadunt vires aliqua ratione revictae,\n\n \n\naut pereunt res exustae torrentibus auris.\n\n \n\numor item quondam coepit superare coortus,\n\n \n\nut fama est, hominum vitas quando obruit undis;\n\n \n\ninde ubi vis aliqua ratione aversa recessit,\n\n \n\nex infinito fuerat quae cumque coorta,\n\n \n\nconstiterunt imbres et flumina vim minuerunt.\n\n  \n\nSed quibus ille modis coniectus materiai\n\n \n\nfundarit terram et caelum pontique profunda,\n\n \n\nsolis lunai cursus, ex ordine ponam.\n\n \n\nnam certe neque consilio primordia rerum\n\n \n\nordine se suo quaeque sagaci mente locarunt\n\n \n\nnec quos quaeque darent motus pepigere profecto;\n\n \n\nsed quia multa modis multis primordia rerum\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore percita plagis\n\n \n\nponderibusque suis consuerunt concita ferri\n\n \n\nomnimodisque coire atque omnia pertemptare,\n\n \n\nquae cumque inter se possent congressa creare,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti magnum volgata per aevom\n\n \n\nomnigenus coetus et motus experiundo\n\n \n\ntandem conveniant ea quae coniecta repente\n\n \n\nmagnarum rerum fiunt exordia saepe,\n\n \n\nterrai maris et caeli generisque animantum.\n\n  \n\nHic neque tum solis rota cerni lumine largo\n\n \n\naltivolans poterat nec magni sidera mundi\n\n \n\nnec mare nec caelum nec denique terra neque aër\n\n \n\nnec similis nostris rebus res ulla videri,\n\n \n\nsed nova tempestas quaedam molesque coorta.\n\n \n\ndiffugere inde loci partes coepere paresque\n\n \n\ncum paribus iungi res et discludere mundum\n\n \n\n membraque dividere et magnas disponere partes\n\n \n\nomnigenis e principiis, discordia quorum\n\n \n\nintervalla vias conexus pondera plagas\n\n \n\nconcursus motus turbabat proelia miscens\n\n \n\npropter dissimilis formas variasque figuras,\n\n \n\nquod non omnia sic poterant coniuncta manere\n\n \n\nnec motus inter sese dare convenientis,\n\n \n\nhoc est, a terris altum secernere caelum,\n\n \n\net sorsum mare, uti secreto umore pateret,\n\n \n\nseorsus item puri secretique aetheris ignes.\n\n  \n\nQuippe etenim primum terrai corpora quaeque,\n\n \n\npropterea quod erant gravia et perplexa, coibant\n\n \n\nin medio atque imas capiebant omnia sedes;\n\n \n\nquae quanto magis inter se perplexa coibant,\n\n \n\ntam magis expressere ea quae mare sidera solem\n\n \n\nlunamque efficerent et magni moenia mundi;\n\n \n\nomnia enim magis haec e levibus atque rutundis\n\n \n\nseminibus multoque minoribus sunt elementis\n\n \n\nquam tellus. ideo per rara foramina terrae\n\n \n\npartibus erumpens primus se sustulit aether\n\n \n\nignifer et multos secum levis abstulit ignis,\n\n \n\nnon alia longe ratione ac saepe videmus,\n\n \n\naurea cum primum gemmantis rore per herbas\n\n \n\nmatutina rubent radiati lumina solis\n\n \n\nexhalantque lacus nebulam fluviique perennes\n\n \n\nipsaque ut inter dum tellus fumare videtur;\n\n \n\nomnia quae sursum cum conciliantur, in alto\n\n \n\ncorpore concreto subtexunt nubila caelum.\n\n \n\nsic igitur tum se levis ac diffusilis aether\n\n \n\ncorpore concreto circum datus undique saepsit\n\n \n\net late diffusus in omnis undique partis\n\n \n\nomnia sic avido complexu cetera saepsit.\n\n \n\nhunc exordia sunt solis lunaeque secuta,\n\n \n\ninterutrasque globi quorum vertuntur in auris;\n\n \n\nquae neque terra sibi adscivit nec maximus aether,\n\n \n\nquod neque tam fuerunt gravia ut depressa sederent,\n\n \n\nnec levia ut possent per summas labier oras,\n\n \n\net tamen interutrasque ita sunt, ut corpora viva\n\n \n\nversent et partes ut mundi totius extent;\n\n \n\nquod genus in nobis quaedam licet in statione\n\n \n\nmembra manere, tamen cum sint ea quae moveantur.\n\n \n\nhis igitur rebus retractis terra repente,\n\n \n\nmaxuma qua nunc se ponti plaga caerula tendit,\n\n \n\nsuccidit et salso suffudit gurgite fossas.\n\n \n\ninque dies quanto circum magis aetheris aestus\n\n \n\net radii solis cogebant undique terram\n\n \n\nverberibus crebris extrema ad limina fartam\n\n \n\nin medio ut propulsa suo condensa coiret,\n\n \n\ntam magis expressus salsus de corpore sudor\n\n \n\naugebat mare manando camposque natantis,\n\n \n\net tanto magis illa foras elapsa volabant\n\n \n\ncorpora multa vaporis et aëris altaque caeli\n\n \n\ndensabant procul a terris fulgentia templa.\n\n \n\nsidebant campi, crescebant montibus altis\n\n \n\nascensus; neque enim poterant subsidere saxa\n\n \n\n nec pariter tantundem omnes succumbere partis.\n\n  \n\nSic igitur terrae concreto corpore pondus\n\n \n\nconstitit atque omnis mundi quasi limus in imum\n\n \n\nconfluxit gravis et subsedit funditus ut faex;\n\n \n\ninde mare, inde aër, inde aether ignifer ipse\n\n \n\ncorporibus liquidis sunt omnia pura relicta\n\n \n\net leviora aliis alia, et liquidissimus aether\n\n \n\natque levissimus aërias super influit auras\n\n \n\nnec liquidum corpus turbantibus aëris auris\n\n \n\ncommiscet; sinit haec violentis omnia verti\n\n \n\nturbinibus, sinit incertis turbare procellis,\n\n \n\nipse suos ignis certo fert impete labens.\n\n \n\nnam modice fluere atque uno posse aethera nisu\n\n \n\nsignificat Pontos, mare certo quod fluit aestu\n\n \n\nunum labendi conservans usque tenorem.\n\n  \n\nMotibus astrorum nunc quae sit causa canamus.\n\n \n\nprincipio magnus caeli si vortitur orbis,\n\n \n\nex utraque polum parti premere aëra nobis\n\n \n\ndicendum est extraque tenere et claudere utrimque;\n\n \n\ninde alium supra fluere atque intendere eodem\n\n \n\nquo volvenda micant aeterni sidera mundi;\n\n \n\naut alium supter, contra qui subvehat orbem,\n\n \n\nut fluvios versare rotas atque austra videmus.\n\n \n\nest etiam quoque uti possit caelum omne manere\n\n \n\nin statione, tamen cum lucida signa ferantur,\n\n \n\nsive quod inclusi rapidi sunt aetheris aestus\n\n \n\nquaerentesque viam circum versantur et ignes\n\n \n\npassim per caeli volvunt summania templa,\n\n \n\nsive aliunde fluens alicunde extrinsecus aër\n\n \n\nversat agens ignis, sive ipsi serpere possunt,\n\n \n\nquo cuiusque cibus vocat atque invitat euntis,\n\n \n\nflammea per caelum pascentis corpora passim.\n\n \n\nnam quid in hoc mundo sit eorum ponere certum\n\n \n\ndifficilest; sed quid possit fiatque per omne\n\n \n\nin variis mundis varia ratione creatis,\n\n \n\nid doceo plurisque sequor disponere causas,\n\n \n\nmotibus astrorum quae possint esse per omne;\n\n \n\ne quibus una tamen sit et haec quoque causa necessest,\n\n \n\nquae vegeat motum signis; sed quae sit earum\n\n \n\npraecipere haud quaquamst pedetemptim progredientis.\n\n  \n\nTerraque ut in media mundi regione quiescat,\n\n \n\nevanescere paulatim et decrescere pondus\n\n \n\nconvenit atque aliam naturam supter habere\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo coniunctam atque uniter aptam\n\n \n\npartibus aëriis mundi, quibus insita vivit.\n\n \n\npropterea non est oneri neque deprimit auras,\n\n \n\nut sua cuique homini nullo sunt pondere membra\n\n \n\nnec caput est oneri collo nec denique totum\n\n \n\ncorporis in pedibus pondus sentimus inesse;\n\n \n\nat quae cumque foris veniunt inpostaque nobis\n\n \n\npondera sunt laedunt, permulto saepe minora.\n\n \n\nusque adeo magni refert quid quaeque queat res.\n\n \n\nsic igitur tellus non est aliena repente\n\n \n\nallata atque auris aliunde obiecta alienis,\n\n \n\nsed pariter prima concepta ab origine mundi\n\n \n\n certaque pars eius, quasi nobis membra videntur.\n\n \n\nPraeterea grandi tonitru concussa repente\n\n \n\nterra supra quae se sunt concutit omnia motu;\n\n \n\nquod facere haut ulla posset ratione, nisi esset\n\n \n\npartibus aëriis mundi caeloque revincta;\n\n \n\nnam communibus inter se radicibus haerent\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo coniuncta atque uniter aucta.\n\n \n\nNonne vides etiam quam magno pondere nobis\n\n \n\nsustineat corpus tenuissima vis animai,\n\n \n\npropterea quia tam coniuncta atque uniter apta est?\n\n \n\nDenique iam saltu pernici tollere corpus\n\n \n\nquid potis est nisi vis animae, quae membra gubernat?\n\n \n\niamne vides quantum tenuis natura valere\n\n \n\npossit, ubi est coniuncta gravi cum corpore, ut aër\n\n \n\nconiunctus terris et nobis est animi vis?\n\n  \n\nNec nimio solis maior rota nec minor ardor\n\n \n\nesse potest, nostris quam sensibus esse videtur.\n\n \n\nnam quibus e spatiis cumque ignes lumina possunt\n\n \n\nadiicere et calidum membris adflare vaporem,\n\n \n\nnil magnis intervallis de corpore libant\n\n \n\nflammarum, nihil ad speciem est contractior ignis.\n\n \n\nproinde, calor quoniam solis lumenque profusum\n\n \n\nperveniunt nostros ad sensus et loca fulgent,\n\n \n\nforma quoque hinc solis debet filumque videri,\n\n \n\nnil adeo ut possis plus aut minus addere vere.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nlunaque sive notho fertur loca lumine lustrans,\n\n \n\nsive suam proprio iactat de corpore lucem,\n\n \n\nquidquid id est, nihilo fertur maiore figura\n\n \n\nquam, nostris oculis qua cernimus, esse videtur.\n\n \n\nnam prius omnia, quae longe semota tuemur\n\n \n\naëra per multum, specie confusa videntur\n\n \n\nquam minui filum. quapropter luna necesse est,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem claram speciem certamque figuram\n\n \n\npraebet, ut est oris extremis cumque notata,\n\n \n\nquanta quoquest, tanta hinc nobis videatur in alto.\n\n \n\npostremo quos cumque vides hinc aetheris ignes,\n\n \n\nscire licet perquam pauxillo posse minores\n\n \n\nesse vel exigua maioris parte brevique.\n\n \n\nquandoquidem quos cumque in terris cernimus ,\n\n \n\ndum tremor  clarus dum cernitur ardor eorum,\n\n \n\nperparvom quiddam inter dum mutare videntur\n\n \n\nalteram utram in partem filum, quo longius absunt.\n\n  \n\nIllud item non est mirandum, qua ratione\n\n \n\ntantulus ille queat tantum sol mittere lumen,\n\n \n\nquod maria ac terras omnis caelumque rigando\n\n \n\ncompleat et calido perfundat cuncta vapore.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nnam licet hinc mundi patefactum totius unum\n\n \n\nlargifluum fontem scatere atque erumpere lumen,\n\n \n\nex omni mundo quia sic elementa vaporis\n\n \n\nundique conveniunt et sic coniectus eorum\n\n \n\nconfluit, ex uno capite hic ut profluat ardor.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam quam late parvus aquai\n\n \n\nprata riget fons inter dum campisque redundet?\n\n \n\n est etiam quoque uti non magno solis ab igni\n\n \n\naëra percipiat calidis fervoribus ardor,\n\n \n\nopportunus ita est si forte et idoneus aër,\n\n \n\nut queat accendi parvis ardoribus ictus;\n\n \n\nquod genus inter dum segetes stipulamque videmus\n\n \n\naccidere ex una scintilla incendia passim.\n\n \n\nforsitan et rosea sol alte lampade lucens\n\n \n\npossideat multum caecis fervoribus ignem\n\n \n\ncircum se, nullo qui sit fulgore notatus,\n\n \n\naestifer ut tantum radiorum exaugeat ictum.\n\n  \n\nNec ratio solis simplex  recta patescit,\n\n \n\nquo pacto aestivis e partibus aegocerotis\n\n \n\nbrumalis adeat flexus atque inde revertens\n\n \n\ncanceris ut vertat metas ad solstitialis,\n\n \n\nlunaque mensibus id spatium videatur obire,\n\n \n\nannua sol in quo consumit tempora cursu.\n\n \n\nnon, inquam, simplex his rebus reddita causast.\n\n \n\nnam fieri vel cum primis id posse videtur,\n\n \n\nDemocriti quod sancta viri sententia ponit,\n\n \n\nquanto quaeque magis sint terram sidera propter,\n\n \n\ntanto posse minus cum caeli turbine ferri;\n\n \n\nevanescere enim rapidas illius et acris\n\n \n\nimminui supter viris, ideoque relinqui\n\n \n\npaulatim solem cum posterioribus signis,\n\n \n\ninferior multo quod sit quam fervida signa.\n\n \n\net magis hoc lunam: quanto demissior eius\n\n \n\ncursus abest procul a caelo terrisque propinquat,\n\n \n\ntanto posse minus cum signis tendere cursum.\n\n \n\nflaccidiore etiam quanto iam turbine fertur\n\n \n\ninferior quam sol, tanto magis omnia signa\n\n \n\nhanc adipiscuntur circum praeterque feruntur.\n\n \n\npropterea fit ut haec ad signum quodque reverti\n\n \n\nmobilius videatur, ad hanc quia signa revisunt.\n\n \n\nfit quoque ut e mundi transversis partibus aër\n\n \n\nalternis certo fluere alter tempore possit,\n\n \n\nqui queat aestivis solem detrudere signis\n\n \n\nbrumalis usque ad flexus gelidumque rigorem,\n\n \n\net qui reiciat gelidis a frigoris umbris\n\n \n\naestiferas usque in partis et fervida signa.\n\n \n\net ratione pari lunam stellasque putandumst,\n\n \n\nquae volvunt magnos in magnis orbibus annos,\n\n \n\naëribus posse alternis e partibus ire.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam diversis nubila ventis\n\n \n\ndiversas ire in partis inferna supernis?\n\n \n\nqui minus illa queant per magnos aetheris orbis\n\n \n\naestibus inter se diversis sidera ferri?\n\n  \n\nAt nox obruit ingenti caligine terras,\n\n \n\naut ubi de longo cursu sol ultima caeli\n\n \n\nimpulit atque suos efflavit languidus ignis\n\n \n\nconcussos itere et labefactos aëre multo,\n\n \n\naut quia sub terras cursum convortere cogit\n\n \n\nvis eadem, supra quae terras pertulit orbem.\n\n \n\nTempore item certo roseam Matuta per oras\n\n \n\naetheris auroram differt et lumina pandit,\n\n \n\naut quia sol idem, sub terras ille revertens,\n\n \n\n anticipat caelum radiis accendere temptans,\n\n \n\naut quia conveniunt ignes et semina multa\n\n \n\nconfluere ardoris consuerunt tempore certo,\n\n \n\nquae faciunt solis nova semper lumina gigni;\n\n \n\nquod genus Idaeis fama est e montibus altis\n\n \n\ndispersos ignis orienti lumine cerni,\n\n \n\ninde coire globum quasi in unum et conficere orbem.\n\n \n\nnec tamen illud in his rebus mirabile debet\n\n \n\nesse, quod haec ignis tam certo tempore possint\n\n \n\nsemina confluere et solis reparare nitorem.\n\n \n\nmulta videmus enim, certo quae tempore fiunt\n\n \n\nomnibus in rebus. florescunt tempore certo\n\n \n\narbusta et certo dimittunt tempore florem.\n\n \n\nnec minus in certo dentes cadere imperat aetas\n\n \n\ntempore et inpubem molli pubescere veste\n\n \n\net pariter mollem malis demittere barbam.\n\n \n\nfulmina postremo nix imbres nubila venti\n\n \n\nnon nimis incertis fiunt in partibus anni.\n\n \n\nnamque ubi sic fuerunt causarum exordia prima\n\n \n\natque ita res mundi cecidere ab origine prima,\n\n \n\nconseque quoque iam redeunt ex ordine certo.\n\n  \n\nCrescere itemque dies licet et tabescere noctes,\n\n \n\net minui luces, cum sumant augmina noctis,\n\n \n\naut quia sol idem sub terras atque superne\n\n \n\nimparibus currens amfractibus aetheris oras\n\n \n\npartit et in partis non aequas dividit orbem,\n\n \n\net quod ab alterutra detraxit parte, reponit\n\n \n\neius in adversa tanto plus parte relatus,\n\n \n\ndonec ad id signum caeli pervenit, ubi anni\n\n \n\nnodus nocturnas exaequat lucibus umbras;\n\n \n\nnam medio cursu flatus aquilonis et austri\n\n \n\ndistinet aequato caelum discrimine metas\n\n \n\npropter signiferi posituram totius orbis,\n\n \n\nannua sol in quo concludit tempora serpens,\n\n \n\nobliquo terras et caelum lumine lustrans,\n\n \n\nut ratio declarat eorum qui loca caeli\n\n \n\nomnia dispositis signis ornata notarunt.\n\n \n\naut quia crassior est certis in partibus aër,\n\n \n\nsub terris ideo tremulum iubar haesitat ignis\n\n \n\nnec penetrare potest facile atque emergere ad ortus;\n\n \n\npropterea noctes hiberno tempore longae\n\n \n\ncessant, dum veniat radiatum insigne diei.\n\n \n\naut etiam, quia sic alternis partibus anni\n\n \n\ntardius et citius consuerunt confluere ignes,\n\n \n\nqui faciunt solem certa de surgere parte,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti videantur dicere verum.\n\n  \n\nLuna potest solis radiis percussa nitere\n\n \n\ninque dies magis  lumen convertere nobis\n\n \n\nad speciem, quantum solis secedit ab orbi,\n\n \n\ndonique eum contra pleno bene lumine fulsit\n\n \n\natque oriens obitus eius super edita vidit;\n\n \n\ninde minutatim retro quasi condere lumen\n\n \n\ndebet item, quanto propius iam solis ad ignem\n\n \n\nlabitur ex alia signorum parte per orbem;\n\n \n\nut faciunt, lunam qui fingunt esse pilai\n\n \n\n consimilem cursusque viam sub sole tenere.\n\n \n\nest etiam quare proprio cum lumine possit\n\n \n\nvolvier et varias splendoris reddere formas;\n\n \n\ncorpus enim licet esse aliud, quod fertur et una\n\n \n\nlabitur omnimodis occursans officiensque,\n\n \n\nnec potis est cerni, quia cassum lumine fertur.\n\n \n\nversarique potest, globus ut, si forte, pilai\n\n \n\ndimidia ex parti candenti lumine tinctus,\n\n \n\nversandoque globum variantis edere formas,\n\n \n\ndonique eam partem, quae cumque est ignibus aucta,\n\n \n\nad speciem vertit nobis oculosque patentis;\n\n \n\ninde minutatim retro contorquet et aufert\n\n \n\nluciferam partem glomeraminis atque pilai;\n\n \n\nut Babylonica Chaldaeum doctrina refutans\n\n \n\nastrologorum artem contra convincere tendit,\n\n \n\nproinde quasi id fieri nequeat quod pugnat uterque\n\n \n\naut minus hoc illo sit cur amplectier ausis.\n\n \n\ndenique cur nequeat semper nova luna creari\n\n \n\nordine formarum certo certisque figuris\n\n \n\ninque dies privos aborisci quaeque creata\n\n \n\natque alia illius reparari in parte locoque,\n\n \n\ndifficilest ratione docere et vincere verbis,\n\n \n\nordine cum  tam certo multa creari.\n\n \n\nit Ver et Venus et Veneris praenuntius ante\n\n \n\npennatus graditur, Zephyri vestigia propter\n\n \n\nFlora quibus mater praespargens ante viai\n\n \n\ncuncta coloribus egregiis et odoribus opplet.\n\n \n\ninde loci sequitur Calor aridus et comes una\n\n \n\npulverulenta Ceres  etesia flabra aquilonum.\n\n \n\ninde Autumnus adit, graditur simul Euhius Euan.\n\n \n\ninde aliae tempestates ventique secuntur,\n\n \n\naltitonans Volturnus et Auster fulmine pollens.\n\n \n\ntandem Bruma nives adfert pigrumque rigorem\n\n \n\nreddit. Hiemps sequitur crepitans hanc dentibus algu.\n\n \n\nquo minus est mirum, si certo tempore luna\n\n \n\ngignitur et certo deletur tempore rusus,\n\n \n\ncum fieri possint tam certo tempore multa.\n\n  \n\nSolis item quoque defectus lunaeque latebras\n\n \n\npluribus e causis fieri tibi posse putandumst.\n\n \n\nnam cur luna queat terram secludere solis\n\n \n\nlumine et a terris altum caput obstruere ei,\n\n \n\nobiciens caecum radiis ardentibus orbem,\n\n \n\ntempore eodem aliut facere id non posse putetur\n\n \n\ncorpus, quod cassum labatur lumine semper?\n\n \n\nsolque suos etiam dimittere languidus ignis\n\n \n\ntempore cur certo nequeat recreareque lumen,\n\n \n\ncum loca praeteriit flammis infesta per auras,\n\n \n\nquae faciunt ignis interstingui atque perire?\n\n \n\net cur terra queat lunam spoliare vicissim\n\n \n\nlumine et oppressum solem super ipsa tenere,\n\n \n\nmenstrua dum rigidas coni perlabitur umbras,\n\n \n\ntempore eodem aliud nequeat succurrere lunae\n\n \n\ncorpus vel supra solis perlabier orbem,\n\n \n\nquod radios inter rumpat lumenque profusum?\n\n \n\net tamen ipsa suo si fulget luna nitore,\n\n \n\n cur nequeat certa mundi languescere parte,\n\n \n\ndum loca luminibus propriis inimica per exit?\n\n \n\n.\n\n  \n\nQuod superest, quoniam magni per caerula mundi\n\n \n\nqua fieri quicquid posset ratione resolvi,\n\n \n\nsolis uti varios cursus lunaeque meatus\n\n \n\nnoscere possemus quae vis et causa cieret,\n\n \n\nquove modo  offecto lumine obire\n\n \n\net neque opinantis tenebris obducere terras,\n\n \n\ncum quasi conivent et aperto lumine rursum\n\n \n\nomnia convisunt clara loca candida luce,\n\n \n\nnunc redeo ad mundi novitatem et mollia terrae\n\n \n\narva, novo fetu quid primum in luminis oras\n\n \n\ntollere et incertis crerint committere ventis.\n\n \n\nPrincipio genus herbarum viridemque nitorem\n\n \n\nterra dedit circum collis camposque per omnis,\n\n \n\nflorida fulserunt viridanti prata colore,\n\n \n\narboribusque datumst variis exinde per auras\n\n \n\ncrescendi magnum inmissis certamen habenis.\n\n \n\nut pluma atque pili primum saetaeque creantur\n\n \n\nquadripedum membris et corpore pennipotentum,\n\n \n\nsic nova tum tellus herbas virgultaque primum\n\n \n\nsustulit, inde loci mortalia saecla creavit\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis varia ratione coorta.\n\n \n\nnam neque de caelo cecidisse animalia possunt,\n\n \n\nnec terrestria de salsis exisse lacunis.\n\n \n\nlinquitur ut merito maternum nomen adepta\n\n \n\nterra sit, e terra quoniam sunt cuncta creata.\n\n \n\nmultaque nunc etiam existunt animalia terris\n\n \n\nimbribus et calido solis concreta vapore;\n\n \n\nquo minus est mirum, si tum sunt plura coorta\n\n \n\net maiora, nova tellure atque aethere adulta.\n\n \n\nprincipio genus alituum variaeque volucres\n\n \n\nova relinquebant exclusae tempore verno,\n\n \n\nfolliculos ut nunc teretis aestate cicadae\n\n \n\nlincunt sponte sua victum vitamque petentes.\n\n \n\ntum tibi terra dedit primum mortalia saecla.\n\n \n\nmultus enim calor atque umor superabat in arvis.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi quaeque loci regio opportuna dabatur,\n\n \n\ncrescebant uteri terram radicibus apti;\n\n \n\nquos ubi tempore maturo pate fecerat aetas\n\n \n\ninfantum, fugiens umorem aurasque petessens,\n\n \n\nconvertebat ibi natura foramina terrae\n\n \n\net sucum venis cogebat fundere apertis\n\n \n\nconsimilem lactis, sicut nunc femina quaeque\n\n \n\ncum peperit, dulci repletur lacte, quod omnis\n\n \n\nimpetus in mammas convertitur ille alimenti.\n\n \n\nterra cibum pueris, vestem vapor, herba cubile\n\n \n\npraebebat multa et molli lanugine abundans.\n\n \n\nat novitas mundi nec frigora dura ciebat\n\n \n\nnec nimios aestus nec magnis viribus auras.\n\n \n\nomnia enim pariter crescunt et robora sumunt.\n\n  \n\nQuare etiam atque etiam maternum nomen adepta\n\n \n\nterra tenet merito, quoniam genus ipsa creavit\n\n \n\nhumanum atque animal prope certo tempore fudit\n\n \n\n omne quod in magnis bacchatur montibus passim,\n\n \n\naëriasque simul volucres variantibus formis.\n\n \n\nsed quia finem aliquam pariendi debet habere,\n\n \n\ndestitit, ut mulier spatio defessa vetusto.\n\n \n\nmutat enim mundi naturam totius aetas\n\n \n\nex alioque alius status excipere omnia debet\n\n \n\nnec manet ulla sui similis res: omnia migrant,\n\n \n\nomnia commutat natura et vertere cogit.\n\n \n\nnamque aliud putrescit et aevo debile languet,\n\n \n\nporro aliud crescit et  contemptibus exit.\n\n \n\nsic igitur mundi naturam totius aetas\n\n \n\nmutat, et ex alio terram status excipit alter,\n\n \n\nquod potuit nequeat, possit quod non tulit ante.\n\n \n\nMultaque tum tellus etiam portenta creare\n\n \n\nconatast mira facie membrisque coorta,\n\n \n\nandrogynem, interutras necutrumque utrimque remotum,\n\n \n\norba pedum partim, manuum viduata vicissim,\n\n \n\nmuta sine ore etiam, sine voltu caeca reperta,\n\n \n\nvinctaque membrorum per totum corpus adhaesu,\n\n \n\nnec facere ut possent quicquam nec cedere quoquam\n\n \n\nnec vitare malum nec sumere quod volet usus.\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc monstra ac portenta creabat,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam natura absterruit auctum\n\n \n\nnec potuere cupitum aetatis tangere florem\n\n \n\nnec reperire cibum nec iungi per Veneris res.\n\n \n\nmulta videmus enim rebus concurrere debere,\n\n \n\nut propagando possint procudere saecla;\n\n \n\npabula primum ut sint, genitalia deinde per artus\n\n \n\nsemina qua possint membris manare remissis,\n\n \n\nfeminaque ut maribus coniungi possit, habere,\n\n \n\nmutua qui mutent inter se gaudia uterque.\n\n  \n\nMultaque tum interiisse animantum saecla necessest\n\n \n\nnec potuisse propagando procudere prolem.\n\n \n\nnam quae cumque vides vesci vitalibus auris,\n\n \n\naut dolus aut virtus aut denique mobilitas est\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo genus id tutaTA reservans.\n\n \n\nmultaque sunt, nobis ex utilitate sua quae\n\n \n\ncommendata manent, tutelae tradita nostrae.\n\n \n\nprincipio genus acre leonum saevaque saecla\n\n \n\ntutatast virtus, volpes dolus et fuga cervos.\n\n \n\nat levisomna canum fido cum pectore corda,\n\n \n\net genus omne quod est veterino semine partum\n\n \n\nlanigeraeque simul pecudes et bucera saecla\n\n \n\nomnia sunt hominum tutelae tradita, Memmi;\n\n \n\nnam cupide fugere feras pacemque secuta\n\n \n\nsunt et larga suo sine pabula parta labore,\n\n \n\nquae damus utilitatis eorum praemia causa.\n\n \n\nat quis nil horum tribuit natura, nec ipsa\n\n \n\nsponte sua possent ut vivere nec dare nobis\n\n \n\nutilitatem aliquam, quare pateremur eorum\n\n \n\npraesidio nostro pasci genus esseque tutum,\n\n \n\nscilicet haec aliis praedae lucroque iacebant\n\n \n\nindupedita suis fatalibus omnia vinclis,\n\n \n\ndonec ad interitum genus id natura redegit.\n\n  \n\nSed neque Centauri fuerunt nec tempore in ullo\n\n \n\n esse queunt duplici natura et corpore bino\n\n \n\nex alienigenis membris compacta, potestas\n\n \n\nhinc illinc partis ut sat par esse potissit.\n\n \n\nid licet hinc quamvis hebeti cognoscere corde.\n\n \n\nprincipio circum tribus actis impiger annis\n\n \n\nfloret equus, puer haut quaquam; nam saepe etiam nunc\n\n \n\nubera mammarum in somnis lactantia quaeret.\n\n \n\npost ubi equum validae vires aetate senecta\n\n \n\nmembraque deficiunt fugienti languida vita,\n\n \n\ntum demum puerili aevo florenta iuventas\n\n \n\nofficit et molli vestit lanugine malas;\n\n \n\nne forte ex homine et veterino semine equorum\n\n \n\nconfieri credas Centauros posse neque esse,\n\n \n\naut rapidis canibus succinctas semimarinis\n\n \n\ncorporibus Scyllas et cetera de genere horum,\n\n \n\ninter se quorum discordia membra videmus;\n\n \n\nquae neque florescunt pariter nec robora sumunt\n\n \n\ncorporibus neque proiciunt aetate senecta\n\n \n\nnec simili Venere ardescunt nec moribus unis\n\n \n\nconveniunt neque sunt eadem iucunda per artus.\n\n \n\nquippe videre licet pinguescere saepe cicuta\n\n \n\nbarbigeras pecudes, homini quae est acre venenum.\n\n \n\nflamma quidem  cum corpora fulva leonum\n\n \n\ntam soleat torrere atque urere quam genus omne\n\n \n\nvisceris in terris quod cumque et sanguinis extet,\n\n \n\nqui fieri potuit, triplici cum corpore ut una,\n\n \n\nprima leo, postrema draco, media ipsa, Chimaera\n\n \n\nore foras acrem flaret de corpore flammam?\n\n \n\nquare etiam tellure nova caeloque recenti\n\n \n\ntalia qui fingit potuisse animalia gigni,\n\n \n\nnixus in hoc uno novitatis nomine inani,\n\n \n\nmulta licet simili ratione effutiat ore,\n\n \n\naurea tum dicat per terras flumina vulgo\n\n \n\nfluxisse et gemmis florere arbusta suësse\n\n \n\naut hominem tanto membrorum esse impete natum,\n\n \n\ntrans maria alta pedum nisus ut ponere posset\n\n \n\net manibus totum circum se vertere caelum.\n\n \n\nnam quod multa fuere in terris semina rerum,\n\n \n\ntempore quo primum tellus animalia fudit,\n\n \n\nnil tamen est signi mixtas potuisse creari\n\n \n\ninter se pecudes compactaque membra animantum,\n\n \n\npropterea quia quae de terris nunc quoque abundant\n\n \n\nherbarum genera ac fruges arbustaque laeta\n\n \n\nnon tamen inter se possunt complexa creari,\n\n \n\nsed res quaeque suo ritu procedit et omnes\n\n \n\nfoedere naturae certo discrimina servant.\n\n  \n\nEt genus humanum multo fuit illud in arvis\n\n \n\ndurius, ut decuit, tellus quod dura creasset,\n\n \n\net maioribus et solidis magis ossibus intus\n\n \n\nfundatum, validis aptum per viscera nervis,\n\n \n\nnec facile ex aestu nec frigore quod caperetur\n\n \n\nnec novitate cibi nec labi corporis ulla.\n\n \n\nmultaque per caelum solis volventia lustra\n\n \n\nvolgivago vitam tractabant more ferarum.\n\n \n\nnec robustus erat curvi moderator aratri\n\n \n\n quisquam, nec scibat ferro molirier arva\n\n \n\nnec nova defodere in terram virgulta neque altis\n\n \n\narboribus veteres decidere falcibus ramos.\n\n \n\nquod sol atque imbres dederant, quod terra crearat\n\n \n\nsponte sua, satis id placabat pectora donum.\n\n \n\nglandiferas inter curabant corpora quercus\n\n \n\nplerumque; et quae nunc hiberno tempore cernis\n\n \n\narbita puniceo fieri matura colore,\n\n \n\nplurima tum tellus etiam maiora ferebat.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea novitas tum florida mundi\n\n \n\npabula dura tulit, miseris mortalibus ampla.\n\n \n\nat sedare sitim fluvii fontesque vocabant,\n\n \n\nut nunc montibus e magnis decursus aquai\n\n \n\nclaricitat late sitientia saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\ndenique nota vagis silvestria templa tenebant\n\n \n\nnympharum, quibus e scibant umore fluenta\n\n \n\nlubrica proluvie larga lavere umida saxa,\n\n \n\numida saxa, super viridi stillantia musco,\n\n \n\net partim plano scatere atque erumpere campo.\n\n \n\nnecdum res igni scibant tractare neque uti\n\n \n\npellibus et spoliis corpus vestire ferarum,\n\n \n\nsed nemora atque cavos montis silvasque colebant\n\n \n\net frutices inter condebant squalida membra\n\n \n\nverbera ventorum vitare imbrisque coacti.\n\n \n\nnec commune bonum poterant spectare neque ullis\n\n \n\nmoribus inter se scibant nec legibus uti.\n\n \n\nquod cuique obtulerat praedae fortuna, ferebat\n\n \n\nsponte sua sibi quisque valere et vivere doctus.\n\n \n\net Venus in silvis iungebat corpora amantum;\n\n \n\nconciliabat enim vel mutua quamque cupido\n\n \n\nvel violenta viri vis atque inpensa libido\n\n \n\nvel pretium, glandes atque arbita vel pira lecta.\n\n \n\net manuum mira freti virtute pedumque\n\n \n\nconsectabantur silvestria saecla ferarum\n\n \n\nmissilibus saxis et magno pondere clavae.\n\n \n\nmultaque vincebant, vitabant pauca latebris;\n\n \n\nsaetigerisque pares subus silvestria membra\n\n \n\nnuda dabant terrae nocturno tempore capti,\n\n \n\ncircum se foliis ac frondibus involventes.\n\n \n\nnec plangore diem magno solemque per agros\n\n \n\nquaerebant pavidi palantes noctis in umbris,\n\n \n\nsed taciti respectabant somnoque sepulti,\n\n \n\ndum rosea face sol inferret lumina caelo.\n\n \n\na parvis quod enim consuerant cernere semper\n\n \n\nalterno tenebras et lucem tempore gigni,\n\n \n\nnon erat ut fieri posset mirarier umquam\n\n \n\nnec diffidere, ne terras aeterna teneret\n\n \n\nnox in perpetuum detracto lumine solis.\n\n \n\nsed magis illud erat curae, quod saecla ferarum\n\n \n\ninfestam miseris faciebant saepe quietem.\n\n \n\neiectique domo fugiebant saxea tecta\n\n \n\nspumigeri suis adventu validique leonis\n\n \n\natque intempesta cedebant nocte paventes\n\n \n\nhospitibus saevis instrata cubilia fronde.\n\n  \n\nNec nimio tum plus quam nunc mortalia saecla\n\n \n\n dulcia linquebant lamentis lumina vitae.\n\n \n\nunus enim tum quisque magis deprensus eorum\n\n \n\npabula viva feris praebebat, dentibus haustus,\n\n \n\net nemora ac montis gemitu silvasque replebat\n\n \n\nviva videns vivo sepeliri viscera busto.\n\n \n\nat quos effugium servarat corpore adeso,\n\n \n\nposterius tremulas super ulcera tetra tenentes\n\n \n\npalmas horriferis accibant vocibus Orcum,\n\n \n\ndonique eos vita privarant vermina saeva\n\n \n\nexpertis opis, ignaros quid volnera vellent.\n\n \n\nat non multa virum sub signis milia ducta\n\n \n\nuna dies dabat exitio nec turbida ponti\n\n \n\naequora lidebant navis ad saxa virosque.\n\n \n\nnam temere in cassum frustra mare saepe coortum\n\n \n\nsaevibat leviterque minas ponebat inanis,\n\n \n\nnec poterat quemquam placidi pellacia ponti\n\n \n\nsubdola pellicere in fraudem ridentibus undis.\n\n \n\nimproba navigii ratio tum caeca iacebat.\n\n \n\ntum penuria deinde cibi languentia leto\n\n \n\nmembra dabat, contra nunc rerum copia mersat.\n\n \n\nilli inprudentes ipsi sibi saepe venenum\n\n \n\nvergebant, nunc dant  sollertius ipsi.\n\n  \n\nInde casas postquam ac pellis ignemque pararunt\n\n \n\net mulier coniuncta viro concessit in unum\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\ncognita sunt, prolemque ex se videre creatam,\n\n \n\ntum genus humanum primum mollescere coepit.\n\n \n\nignis enim curavit, ut alsia corpora frigus\n\n \n\nnon ita iam possent caeli sub tegmine ferre,\n\n \n\net Venus inminuit viris puerique parentum\n\n \n\nblanditiis facile ingenium fregere superbum.\n\n \n\ntunc et amicitiem coeperunt iungere aventes\n\n \n\nfinitimi inter se nec laedere nec violari,\n\n \n\net pueros commendarunt muliebreque saeclum,\n\n \n\nvocibus et gestu cum balbe significarent\n\n \n\nimbecillorum esse aequum misererier omnis.\n\n \n\nnec tamen omnimodis poterat concordia gigni,\n\n \n\nsed bona magnaque pars servabat foedera caste;\n\n \n\naut genus humanum iam tum foret omne peremptum\n\n \n\nnec potuisset adhuc perducere saecla propago.\n\n  \n\nAt varios linguae sonitus natura subegit\n\n \n\nmittere et utilitas expressit nomina rerum,\n\n \n\nnon alia longe ratione atque ipsa videtur\n\n \n\nprotrahere ad gestum pueros infantia linguae,\n\n \n\ncum facit ut digito quae sint praesentia monstrent.\n\n \n\nsentit enim vim quisque suam quod possit abuti.\n\n \n\ncornua nata prius vitulo quam frontibus extent,\n\n \n\nillis iratus petit atque infestus inurget.\n\n \n\nat catuli pantherarum scymnique leonum\n\n \n\nunguibus ac pedibus iam tum morsuque repugnant,\n\n \n\nvix etiam cum sunt dentes unguesque creati.\n\n \n\nalituum porro genus alis omne videmus\n\n \n\nfidere et a pennis tremulum petere auxiliatum.\n\n \n\nproinde putare aliquem tum nomina distribuisse\n\n \n\nrebus et inde homines didicisse vocabula prima,\n\n \n\n desiperest. nam cur hic posset cuncta notare\n\n \n\nvocibus et varios sonitus emittere linguae,\n\n \n\ntempore eodem alii facere id non quisse putentur?\n\n \n\npraeterea si non alii quoque vocibus usi\n\n \n\ninter se fuerant, unde insita notities est\n\n \n\nutilitatis et unde data est huic prima potestas,\n\n \n\nquid vellet facere ut sciret animoque videret?\n\n \n\ncogere item pluris unus victosque domare\n\n \n\nnon poterat, rerum ut perdiscere nomina vellent.\n\n \n\nnec ratione docere ulla suadereque surdis,\n\n \n\nquid sit opus facto, facilest; neque enim paterentur\n\n \n\nnec ratione ulla sibi ferrent amplius auris\n\n \n\nvocis inauditos sonitus obtundere frustra.\n\n \n\npostremo quid in hac mirabile tantoperest re,\n\n \n\nsi genus humanum, cui vox et lingua vigeret,\n\n \n\npro vario sensu varia res voce notaret?\n\n \n\ncum pecudes mutae, cum denique saecla ferarum\n\n \n\ndissimilis soleant voces variasque ciere,\n\n \n\ncum metus aut dolor est et cum iam gaudia gliscunt.\n\n \n\nquippe enim licet id rebus cognoscere apertis.\n\n \n\ninritata canum cum primum magna Molossum\n\n \n\nmollia ricta fremunt duros nudantia dentes,\n\n \n\nlonge alio sonitu rabies stricta minatur,\n\n \n\net cum iam latrant et vocibus omnia complent;\n\n \n\nat catulos blande cum lingua lambere temptant\n\n \n\naut ubi eos lactant, pedibus morsuque potentes\n\n \n\nsuspensis teneros imitantur dentibus haustus,\n\n \n\nlonge alio pacto gannitu vocis adulant,\n\n \n\net cum deserti baubantur in aedibus, aut cum\n\n \n\nplorantis fugiunt summisso corpore plagas.\n\n \n\ndenique non hinnitus item differre videtur,\n\n \n\ninter equas ubi equus florenti aetate iuvencus\n\n \n\npinnigeri saevit calcaribus ictus Amoris\n\n \n\net fremitum patulis sub naribus edit ad arma,\n\n \n\net cum sic alias concussis artibus hinnit?\n\n \n\npostremo genus alituum variaeque volucres,\n\n \n\naccipitres atque ossifragae mergique marinis\n\n \n\nfluctibus in salso victum vitamque petentes,\n\n \n\nlonge alias alio iaciunt in tempore voces,\n\n \n\net quom de victu certant praedaque repugnant.\n\n \n\net partim mutant cum tempestatibus una\n\n \n\nraucisonos cantus, cornicum ut saecla vetusta\n\n \n\ncorvorumque gregis ubi aquam dicuntur et imbris\n\n \n\nposcere et inter dum ventos aurasque vocare.\n\n \n\nergo si varii sensus animalia cogunt,\n\n \n\nmuta tamen cum sint, varias emittere voces,\n\n \n\nquanto mortalis magis aequumst tum potuisse\n\n \n\ndissimilis alia atque alia res voce notare!\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus tacitus ne forte requiras,\n\n \n\nfulmen detulit in terram mortalibus ignem\n\n \n\nprimitus, inde omnis flammarum diditur ardor;\n\n \n\nmulta videmus enim caelestibus insita flammis\n\n \n\nfulgere, cum caeli donavit plaga vaporis.\n\n \n\net ramosa tamen cum ventis pulsa vacillans\n\n \n\naestuat in ramos incumbens arboris arbor,\n\n \n\n exprimitur validis extritus viribus ignis,\n\n \n\nemicat inter dum flammai fervidus ardor,\n\n \n\nmutua dum inter se rami stirpesque teruntur.\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque dedisse potest mortalibus ignem.\n\n \n\ninde cibum quoquere ac flammae mollire vapore\n\n \n\nsol docuit, quoniam mitescere multa videbant\n\n \n\nverberibus radiorum atque aestu victa per agros.\n\n  \n\nInque dies magis hi victum vitamque priorem\n\n \n\ncommutare novis monstrabant rebus et igni,\n\n \n\ningenio qui praestabant et corde vigebant.\n\n \n\ncondere coeperunt urbis arcemque locare\n\n \n\npraesidium reges ipsi sibi perfugiumque,\n\n \n\net pecudes et agros divisere atque dedere\n\n \n\npro facie cuiusque et viribus ingenioque;\n\n \n\nnam facies multum valuit viresque vigebant.\n\n \n\nposterius res inventast aurumque repertum,\n\n \n\nquod facile et validis et pulchris dempsit honorem;\n\n \n\ndivitioris enim sectam plerumque secuntur\n\n \n\nquam lubet et fortes et pulchro corpore creti.\n\n \n\nquod siquis vera vitam ratione gubernet,\n\n \n\ndivitiae grandes homini sunt vivere parce\n\n \n\naequo animo; neque enim est umquam penuria parvi.\n\n \n\nat claros homines voluerunt se atque potentes,\n\n \n\nut fundamento stabili fortuna maneret\n\n \n\net placidam possent opulenti degere vitam,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam ad summum succedere honorem\n\n \n\ncertantes iter infestum fecere viai,\n\n \n\net tamen e summo, quasi fulmen, deicit ictos\n\n \n\ninvidia inter dum contemptim in Tartara taetra;\n\n \n\ninvidia quoniam ceu fulmine summa vaporant\n\n \n\nplerumque et quae sunt aliis magis edita cumque;\n\n \n\nut satius multo iam sit parere quietum\n\n \n\nquam regere imperio res velle et regna tenere.\n\n \n\nproinde sine in cassum defessi sanguine sudent,\n\n \n\nangustum per iter luctantes ambitionis;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem sapiunt alieno ex ore petuntque\n\n \n\nres ex auditis potius quam sensibus ipsis,\n\n \n\nnec magis id nunc est neque erit mox quam fuit ante.\n\n  \n\nErgo regibus occisis subversa iacebat\n\n \n\npristina maiestas soliorum et sceptra superba,\n\n \n\net capitis summi praeclarum insigne cruentum\n\n \n\nsub pedibus vulgi magnum lugebat honorem;\n\n \n\nnam cupide conculcatur nimis ante metutum.\n\n \n\nres itaque ad summam faecem turbasque redibat,\n\n \n\nimperium sibi cum ac summatum quisque petebat.\n\n \n\ninde magistratum partim docuere creare\n\n \n\niuraque constituere, ut vellent legibus uti.\n\n \n\nnam genus humanum, defessum vi colere aevom,\n\n \n\nex inimicitiis languebat; quo magis ipsum\n\n \n\nsponte sua cecidit sub leges artaque iura.\n\n \n\nacrius ex ira quod enim se quisque parabat\n\n \n\nulcisci quam nunc concessumst legibus aequis,\n\n \n\nhanc ob rem est homines pertaesum vi colere aevom.\n\n \n\ninde metus maculat poenarum praemia vitae.\n\n \n\ncircumretit enim vis atque iniuria quemque\n\n \n\n atque unde exortast, ad eum plerumque revertit,\n\n \n\nnec facilest placidam ac pacatam degere vitam\n\n \n\nqui violat factis communia foedera pacis.\n\n \n\netsi fallit enim divom genus humanumque,\n\n \n\nperpetuo tamen id fore clam diffidere debet;\n\n \n\nquippe ubi se multi per somnia saepe loquentes\n\n \n\naut morbo delirantes protraxe ferantur\n\n \n\net celata  in medium et peccata dedisse.\n\n  \n\nNunc quae causa deum per magnas numina gentis\n\n \n\npervulgarit et ararum compleverit urbis\n\n \n\nsuscipiendaque curarit sollemnia sacra,\n\n \n\nquae nunc in magnis florent sacra rebus locisque,\n\n \n\nunde etiam nunc est mortalibus insitus horror,\n\n \n\nqui delubra deum nova toto suscitat orbi\n\n \n\nterrarum et festis cogit celebrare diebus,\n\n \n\nnon ita difficilest rationem reddere verbis.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim iam tum divom mortalia saecla\n\n \n\negregias animo facies vigilante videbant\n\n \n\net magis in somnis mirando corporis auctu.\n\n \n\nhis igitur sensum tribuebant propterea quod\n\n \n\nmembra movere videbantur vocesque superbas\n\n \n\nmittere pro facie praeclara et viribus amplis.\n\n \n\naeternamque dabant vitam, quia semper eorum\n\n \n\nsubpeditabatur facies et forma manebat,\n\n \n\net tamen omnino quod tantis viribus auctos\n\n \n\nnon temere ulla vi convinci posse putabant.\n\n \n\nfortunisque ideo longe praestare putabant,\n\n \n\nquod mortis timor haut quemquam vexaret eorum,\n\n \n\net simul in somnis quia multa et mira videbant\n\n \n\nefficere et nullum capere ipsos inde laborem.\n\n \n\npraeterea caeli rationes ordine certo\n\n \n\net varia annorum cernebant tempora verti\n\n \n\nnec poterant quibus id fieret cognoscere causis.\n\n \n\nergo perfugium sibi habebant omnia divis\n\n \n\ntradere et illorum nutu facere omnia flecti.\n\n \n\nin caeloque deum sedes et templa locarunt,\n\n \n\nper caelum volvi quia nox et luna videtur,\n\n \n\nluna dies et nox et noctis signa severa\n\n \n\nnoctivagaeque faces caeli flammaeque volantes,\n\n \n\nnubila sol imbres nix venti fulmina grando\n\n \n\net rapidi fremitus et murmura magna minarum.\n\n  \n\nO genus infelix humanum, talia divis\n\n \n\ncum tribuit facta atque iras adiunxit acerbas!\n\n \n\nquantos tum gemitus ipsi sibi, quantaque nobis\n\n \n\nvolnera, quas lacrimas peperere minoribus nostris!\n\n \n\nnec pietas ullast velatum saepe videri\n\n \n\nvertier ad lapidem atque omnis accedere ad aras\n\n \n\nnec procumbere humi prostratum et pandere palmas\n\n \n\nante deum delubra nec aras sanguine multo\n\n \n\nspargere quadrupedum nec votis nectere vota,\n\n \n\nsed mage pacata posse omnia mente tueri.\n\n \n\nnam cum suspicimus magni caelestia mundi\n\n \n\ntempla super stellisque micantibus aethera fixum,\n\n \n\net venit in mentem solis lunaeque viarum,\n\n \n\ntunc aliis oppressa malis in pectora cura\n\n \n\n illa quoque expergefactum caput erigere infit,\n\n \n\nne quae forte deum nobis inmensa potestas\n\n \n\nsit, vario motu quae candida sidera verset;\n\n \n\ntemptat enim dubiam mentem rationis egestas,\n\n \n\necquae nam fuerit mundi genitalis origo,\n\n \n\net simul ecquae sit finis, quoad moenia mundi\n\n \n\net taciti motus hunc possint ferre laborem,\n\n \n\nan divinitus aeterna donata salute\n\n \n\nperpetuo possint aevi labentia tractu\n\n \n\ninmensi validas aevi contemnere viris.\n\n \n\npraeterea cui non animus formidine divum\n\n \n\ncontrahitur, cui non correpunt membra pavore,\n\n \n\nfulminis horribili cum plaga torrida tellus\n\n \n\ncontremit et magnum percurrunt murmura caelum?\n\n \n\nnon populi gentesque tremunt, regesque superbi\n\n \n\ncorripiunt divum percussi membra timore,\n\n \n\nne quod ob admissum foede dictumve superbe\n\n \n\npoenarum grave sit solvendi tempus adauctum?\n\n \n\nsumma etiam cum vis violenti per mare venti\n\n \n\ninduperatorem classis super aequora verrit\n\n \n\ncum validis pariter legionibus atque elephantis,\n\n \n\nnon divom pacem votis adit ac prece quaesit\n\n \n\nventorum pavidus paces animasque secundas?\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam violento turbine saepe\n\n \n\ncorreptus nihilo fertur minus ad vada leti.\n\n \n\nusque adeo res humanas vis abdita quaedam\n\n \n\nopterit et pulchros fascis saevasque secures\n\n \n\nproculcare ac ludibrio sibi habere videtur.\n\n \n\ndenique sub pedibus tellus cum tota vacillat\n\n \n\nconcussaeque cadunt urbes dubiaeque minantur,\n\n \n\nquid mirum si se temnunt mortalia saecla\n\n \n\natque potestatis magnas mirasque relinquunt\n\n \n\nin rebus viris divum, quae cuncta gubernent?\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, aeque aurum ferrumque repertumst\n\n \n\net simul argenti pondus plumbique potestas,\n\n \n\nignis ubi ingentis silvas ardore cremarat\n\n \n\nmontibus in magnis, seu caelo fulmine misso,\n\n \n\nsive quod inter se bellum silvestre gerentes\n\n \n\nhostibus intulerant ignem formidinis ergo,\n\n \n\nsive quod inducti terrae bonitate volebant\n\n \n\npandere agros pinguis et pascua reddere rura,\n\n \n\nsive feras interficere et ditescere praeda;\n\n \n\nnam fovea atque igni prius est venarier ortum\n\n \n\nquam saepire plagis saltum canibusque ciere.\n\n \n\nquicquid id est, qua cumque e causa flammeus ardor\n\n \n\nhorribili sonitu silvas exederat altis\n\n \n\na radicibus et terram percoxerat igni,\n\n \n\nmanabat venis ferventibus in loca terrae\n\n \n\nconcava conveniens argenti rivus et auri,\n\n \n\naeris item et plumbi. quae cum concreta videbant\n\n \n\nposterius claro in terra splendere colore,\n\n \n\ntollebant nitido capti levique lepore,\n\n \n\net simili formata videbant esse figura\n\n \n\natque lacunarum fuerant vestigia cuique.\n\n \n\ntum penetrabat eos posse haec liquefacta calore\n\n \n\n quamlibet in formam et faciem decurrere rerum,\n\n \n\net prorsum quamvis in acuta ac tenvia posse\n\n \n\nmucronum duci fastigia procudendo,\n\n \n\nut sibi tela parent silvasque ut caedere possint\n\n \n\nmateriemque dolare et levia radere tigna\n\n \n\net terebrare etiam ac pertundere perque forare.\n\n \n\nnec minus argento facere haec auroque parabant\n\n \n\nquam validi primum violentis viribus aeris,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam cedebat victa potestas\n\n \n\nnec poterant pariter durum sufferre laborem.\n\n \n\nnam fuit in pretio magis aes aurumque iacebat\n\n \n\npropter inutilitatem hebeti mucrone retusum;\n\n \n\nnunc iacet aes, aurum in summum successit honorem.\n\n \n\nsic volvenda aetas commutat tempora rerum.\n\n \n\nquod fuit in pretio, fit nullo denique honore;\n\n \n\nporro aliud succedit et  contemptibus exit\n\n \n\ninque dies magis adpetitur floretque repertum\n\n \n\nlaudibus et miro est mortalis inter honore.\n\n  \n\nNunc tibi quo pacto ferri natura reperta\n\n \n\nsit facilest ipsi per te cognoscere, Memmi.\n\n \n\narma antiqua manus ungues dentesque fuerunt\n\n \n\net lapides et item silvarum fragmina rami\n\n \n\net flamma atque ignes, post quam sunt cognita primum.\n\n \n\nposterius ferri vis est aerisque reperta.\n\n \n\net prior aeris erat quam ferri cognitus usus,\n\n \n\nquo facilis magis est natura et copia maior.\n\n \n\naere solum terrae tractabant, aereque belli\n\n \n\nmiscebant fluctus et vulnera vasta serebant\n\n \n\net pecus atque agros adimebant; nam facile ollis\n\n \n\nomnia cedebant armatis nuda et inerma.\n\n \n\ninde minutatim processit ferreus ensis\n\n \n\nversaque in obprobrium species est falcis ahenae,\n\n \n\net ferro coepere solum proscindere terrae\n\n \n\nexaequataque sunt creperi certamina belli.\n\n \n\net prius est armatum in equi conscendere costas\n\n \n\net moderarier hunc frenis dextraque vigere\n\n \n\nquam biiugo curru belli temptare pericla.\n\n \n\net biiugo prius est quam bis coniungere binos\n\n \n\net quam falciferos armatum escendere currus.\n\n \n\ninde boves Lucas turrito corpore, tetras,\n\n \n\nanguimanus, belli docuerunt volnera Poeni\n\n \n\nsufferre et magnas Martis turbare catervas.\n\n \n\nsic alid ex alio peperit discordia tristis,\n\n \n\nhorribile humanis quod gentibus esset in armis,\n\n \n\ninque dies belli terroribus addidit augmen.\n\n  \n\nTemptarunt etiam tauros in moenere belli\n\n \n\nexpertique sues saevos sunt mittere in hostis.\n\n \n\net validos partim prae se misere leones\n\n \n\ncum doctoribus armatis saevisque magistris,\n\n \n\nqui moderarier his possent vinclisque tenere,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam permixta caede calentes\n\n \n\nturbabant saevi nullo discrimine turmas,\n\n \n\nterrificas capitum quatientis undique cristas,\n\n \n\nnec poterant equites fremitu perterrita equorum\n\n \n\npectora mulcere et frenis convertere in hostis.\n\n \n\n inritata leae iaciebant corpora saltu\n\n \n\nundique et adversum venientibus ora patebant\n\n \n\net nec opinantis a tergo deripiebant\n\n \n\ndeplexaeque dabant in terram volnere victos,\n\n \n\nmorsibus adfixae validis atque unguibus uncis.\n\n \n\niactabantque suos tauri pedibusque terebant\n\n \n\net latera ac ventres hauribant supter equorum\n\n \n\ncornibus et terram minitanti mente ruebant.\n\n \n\net validis socios caedebant dentibus apri\n\n \n\ntela infracta suo tinguentes sanguine saevi\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\npermixtasque dabant equitum peditumque ruinas.\n\n \n\nnam transversa feros exibant dentis adactus\n\n \n\niumenta aut pedibus ventos erecta petebant,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam ab nervis succisa videres\n\n \n\nconcidere atque gravi terram consternere casu.\n\n \n\nsi quos ante domi domitos satis esse putabant,\n\n \n\neffervescere cernebant in rebus agundis\n\n \n\nvolneribus clamore fuga terrore tumultu,\n\n \n\nnec poterant ullam partem redducere eorum;\n\n \n\ndiffugiebat enim varium genus omne ferarum,\n\n \n\nut nunc saepe boves Lucae ferro male mactae\n\n \n\ndiffugiunt, fera facta suis cum multa dedere.\n\n \n\nSed facere id non tam vincendi spe voluerunt;\n\n \n\nquam dare quod gemerent hostes, ipsique perire,\n\n \n\nqui numero diffidebant armisque vacabant,\n\n \n\nsi fuit ut facerent. sed vix adducor ut ante\n\n \n\nnon quierint animo praesentire atque videre,\n\n \n\nquam commune malum fieret foedumque, futurum.\n\n \n\net magis id possis factum contendere in omni\n\n \n\nin variis mundis varia ratione creatis,\n\n \n\nquam certo atque uno terrarum quolibet orbi.\n\n  \n\nNexilis ante fuit vestis quam textile tegmen.\n\n \n\ntextile post ferrumst, quia ferro tela paratur,\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia possunt tam levia gigni\n\n \n\ninsilia ac fusi, radii, scapique sonantes.\n\n \n\net facere ante viros lanam natura coëgit\n\n \n\nquam muliebre genus; nam longe praestat in arte\n\n \n\net sollertius est multo genus omne virile;\n\n \n\nagricolae donec vitio vertere severi,\n\n \n\nut muliebribus id manibus concedere vellent\n\n \n\natque ipsi pariter durum sufferre laborem\n\n \n\natque opere in duro durarent membra manusque.\n\n  \n\nAt specimen sationis et insitionis origo\n\n \n\nipsa fuit rerum primum natura creatrix,\n\n \n\narboribus quoniam bacae glandesque caducae\n\n \n\ntempestiva dabant pullorum examina supter;\n\n \n\nunde etiam libitumst stirpis committere ramis\n\n \n\net nova defodere in terram virgulta per agros.\n\n \n\ninde aliam atque aliam culturam dulcis agelli\n\n \n\ntemptabant fructusque feros mansuescere terra\n\n \n\ncernebant indulgendo blandeque colendo.\n\n \n\ninque dies magis in montem succedere silvas\n\n \n\ncogebant infraque locum concedere cultis,\n\n \n\nprata lacus rivos segetes vinetaque laeta\n\n \n\n collibus et campis ut haberent, atque olearum\n\n \n\ncaerula distinguens inter plaga currere posset\n\n \n\nper tumulos et convallis camposque profusa;\n\n \n\nut nunc esse vides vario distincta lepore\n\n \n\nomnia, quae pomis intersita dulcibus ornant\n\n \n\narbustisque tenent felicibus opsita circum.\n\n  \n\nAt liquidas avium voces imitarier ore\n\n \n\nante fuit multo quam levia carmina cantu\n\n \n\nconcelebrare homines possent aurisque iuvare.\n\n \n\net zephyri cava per calamorum sibila primum\n\n \n\nagrestis docuere cavas inflare cicutas.\n\n \n\ninde minutatim dulcis didicere querellas,\n\n \n\ntibia quas fundit digitis pulsata canentum,\n\n \n\navia per nemora ac silvas saltusque reperta,\n\n \n\nper loca pastorum deserta atque otia dia.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nhaec animos ollis mulcebant atque iuvabant\n\n \n\ncum satiate cibi; nam tum sunt omnia cordi.\n\n \n\nsaepe itaque inter se prostrati in gramine molli\n\n \n\npropter aquae rivom sub ramis arboris altae.\n\n \n\nnon magnis opibus iucunde corpora habebant,\n\n \n\npraesertim cum tempestas ridebat et anni\n\n \n\ntempora pingebant viridantis floribus herbas.\n\n \n\ntum ioca, tum sermo, tum dulces esse cachinni\n\n \n\nconsuerant; agrestis enim tum musa vigebat.\n\n \n\ntum caput atque umeros plexis redimire coronis\n\n \n\nfloribus et foliis lascivia laeta movebat,\n\n \n\natque extra numerum procedere membra moventes\n\n \n\nduriter et duro terram pede pellere matrem;\n\n \n\nunde oriebantur risus dulcesque cachinni,\n\n \n\nomnia quod nova tum magis haec et mira vigebant.\n\n \n\net vigilantibus hinc aderant solacia somno\n\n \n\nducere multimodis voces et flectere cantus\n\n \n\net supera calamos unco percurrere labro;\n\n \n\nunde etiam vigiles nunc haec accepta tuentur.\n\n \n\net numerum servare genus didicere, neque hilo\n\n \n\nmaiore interea capiunt dulcedine fructum\n\n \n\nquam silvestre genus capiebat terrigenarum.\n\n \n\nnam quod adest praesto, nisi quid cognovimus ante\n\n \n\nsuavius, in primis placet et pollere videtur,\n\n \n\nposteriorque fere melior res illa reperta\n\n \n\nperdit et immutat sensus ad pristina quaeque.\n\n  \n\nsic odium coepit glandis, sic illa relicta\n\n \n\nstrata cubilia sunt herbis et frondibus aucta.\n\n \n\npellis item cecidit vestis contempta ferina;\n\n \n\nquam reor invidia tali tunc esse repertam,\n\n \n\nut letum insidiis qui gessit primus obiret,\n\n \n\net tamen inter eos distractam sanguine multo\n\n \n\ndisperiise neque in fructum convertere quisse.\n\n \n\ntunc igitur pelles, nunc aurum et purpura curis\n\n \n\nexercent hominum vitam belloque fatigant;\n\n \n\nquo magis in nobis, ut opinor, culpa resedit.\n\n \n\nfrigus enim nudos sine pellibus excruciabat\n\n \n\nterrigenas; at nos nil laedit veste carere\n\n \n\npurpurea atque auro signisque ingentibus apta,\n\n \n\ndum plebeia tamen sit, quae defendere possit.\n\n \n\nErgo hominum genus in cassum frustraque laborat\n\n \n\nsemper et  curis consumit inanibus aevom,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia non cognovit quae sit habendi\n\n \n\nfinis et omnino quoad crescat vera voluptas;\n\n \n\nidque minutatim vitam provexit in altum\n\n \n\net belli magnos commovit funditus aestus.\n\n  \n\nat vigiles mundi magnum versatile templum\n\n \n\nsol et luna suo lustrantes lumine circum\n\n \n\nperdocuere homines annorum tempora verti\n\n \n\net certa ratione geri rem atque ordine certo.\n\n \n\nIam validis saepti degebant turribus aevom,\n\n \n\net divisa colebatur discretaque tellus,\n\n \n\ntum mare velivolis florebat navibus ponti,\n\n \n\nauxilia ac socios iam pacto foedere habebant,\n\n \n\ncarminibus cum res gestas coepere poëtae\n\n \n\ntradere; nec multo prius sunt elementa reperta.\n\n \n\npropterea quid sit prius actum respicere aetas\n\n \n\nnostra nequit, nisi qua ratio vestigia monstrat.\n\n \n\nNavigia atque agri culturas moenia leges\n\n \n\narma vias vestes  cetera de genere horum,\n\n \n\npraemia, delicias quoque vitae funditus omnis,\n\n \n\ncarmina, picturas et daedala signa polita\n\n \n\nusus et impigrae simul experientia mentis\n\n \n\npaulatim docuit pedetemptim progredientis.\n\n \n\nsic unum quicquid paulatim protrahit aetas\n\n \n\nin medium ratioque in luminis erigit oras;\n\n \n\nnamque alid ex alio clarescere corde videbant,\n\n \n\nartibus ad summum donec venere cacumen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001","text":"## Liber Quintus\n\n  \n\nQuis potis est dignum pollenti pectore carmen\n\n \n\ncondere pro rerum maiestate hisque repertis?\n\n \n\nquisve valet verbis tantum, qui fingere laudes\n\n \n\npro meritis eius possit, qui talia nobis\n\n \n\npectore parta suo quaesitaque praemia liquit?\n\n \n\nnemo, ut opinor, erit mortali corpore cretus.\n\n \n\nnam si, ut ipsa petit maiestas cognita rerum,\n\n \n\ndicendum est, deus ille fuit, deus, inclyte Memmi,\n\n \n\nqui princeps vitae rationem invenit eam quae\n\n \n\nnunc appellatur sapientia, quique per artem\n\n \n\nfluctibus et tantis vitam tantisque tenebris\n\n \n\nin tam tranquillo et tam clara luce locavit.\n\n \n\nconfer enim divina aliorum antiqua reperta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0002","text":"namque Ceres fertur fruges Liberque liquoris\n\n \n\nvitigeni laticem mortalibus instituisse;\n\n \n\ncum tamen his posset sine rebus vita manere,\n\n \n\nut fama est aliquas etiam nunc vivere gentis.\n\n \n\nat bene non poterat sine puro pectore vivi;\n\n \n\nquo magis hic merito nobis deus esse videtur,\n\n \n\nex quo nunc etiam per magnas didita gentis\n\n \n\ndulcia permulcent animos solacia vitae.\n\n \n\nHerculis antistare autem si facta putabis,\n\n \n\nlongius a vera multo ratione ferere.\n\n \n\nquid Nemeaeus enim nobis nunc magnus hiatus\n\n \n\nille leonis obesset et horrens Arcadius sus,\n\n \n\ntanto opere officerent nobis Stymphala colentes?\n\n \n\ndenique quid Cretae taurus Lernaeaque pestis\n\n \n\nhydra venenatis posset vallata colubris?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0003","text":"quidve tripectora tergemini vis Geryonai\n\n \n\net Diomedis equi spirantes naribus ignem\n\n \n\nThracia Bistoniasque plagas atque Ismara propter\n\n \n\naureaque Hesperidum servans fulgentia mala,\n\n \n\nasper, acerba tuens, immani corpore serpens\n\n \n\narboris amplexus stirpes? quid denique obesset\n\n \n\npropter Atlanteum litus pelagique severa,\n\n \n\nquo neque noster adit quisquam nec barbarus audet?\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc quae sunt portenta perempta,\n\n \n\nsi non victa forent, quid tandem viva nocerent?\n\n \n\nnil, ut opinor: ita ad satiatem terra ferarum\n\n \n\nnunc etiam scatit et trepido terrore repleta est\n\n \n\nper nemora ac montes magnos silvasque profundas;\n\n \n\nquae loca vitandi plerumque est nostra potestas.\n\n \n\nat nisi purgatumst pectus, quae proelia nobis\n\n \n\natque pericula tumst ingratis insinuandum!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","text":"quantae tum scindunt hominem cuppedinis acres\n\n \n\nsollicitum curae quantique perinde timores!\n\n \n\nquidve superbia spurcitia ac petulantia? quantas\n\n \n\nefficiunt clades! quid luxus desidiaeque?\n\n \n\nhaec igitur qui cuncta subegerit ex animoque\n\n \n\nexpulerit dictis, non armis, nonne decebit\n\n \n\nhunc hominem numero divom dignarier esse?\n\n \n\ncum bene praesertim multa ac divinitus ipsis\n\n \n\niam mortalibus e divis dare dicta suerit\n\n \n\natque omnem rerum naturam pandere dictis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","text":"Cuius ego ingressus vestigia dum rationes\n\n \n\npersequor ac doceo dictis, quo quaeque creata\n\n \n\nfoedere sint, in eo quam sit durare necessum\n\n \n\nnec validas valeant aevi rescindere leges,\n\n \n\nquo genere in primis animi natura reperta est\n\n \n\nnativo primum consistere corpore creta,\n\n \n\nnec posse incolumem magnum durare per aevum,\n\n \n\nsed simulacra solere in somnis fallere mentem,\n\n \n\ncernere cum videamur eum quem vita reliquit,\n\n \n\nquod super est, nunc huc rationis detulit ordo,\n\n \n\nut mihi mortali consistere corpore mundum\n\n \n\nnativomque simul ratio reddunda sit esse;\n\n \n\net quibus ille modis congressus materiai\n\n \n\nfundarit terram caelum mare sidera solem\n\n \n\nlunaique globum; tum quae tellure animantes\n\n \n\nextiterint, et quae nullo sint tempore natae;\n\n \n\nquove modo genus humanum variante loquella\n\n \n\ncoeperit inter se vesci per nomina rerum;\n\n \n\net quibus ille modis divom metus insinuarit\n\n \n\npectora, terrarum qui in orbi sancta tuetur\n\n \n\nfana lacus lucos aras simulacraque divom.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","text":"praeterea solis cursus lunaeque meatus\n\n \n\nexpediam qua vi flectat natura gubernans;\n\n \n\nne forte haec inter caelum terramque reamur\n\n \n\nlibera sponte sua cursus lustrare perennis,\n\n \n\nmorigera ad fruges augendas atque animantis,\n\n \n\nneve aliqua divom volvi ratione putemus.\n\n \n\nnam bene qui didicere deos securum agere aevom,\n\n \n\nsi tamen interea mirantur qua ratione\n\n \n\nquaeque geri possint, praesertim rebus in illis\n\n \n\nquae supera caput aetheriis cernuntur in oris,\n\n \n\nrursus in antiquas referuntur religiones\n\n \n\net dominos acris adsciscunt, omnia posse\n\n \n\nquos miseri credunt, ignari quid queat esse,\n\n \n\nquid nequeat, finita potestas denique cuique\n\n \n\nqua nam sit ratione atque alte terminus haerens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0007","text":"Quod super est, ne te in promissis plura moremur,\n\n \n\nprincipio maria ac terras caelumque tuere;\n\n \n\nquorum naturam triplicem, tria corpora, Memmi,\n\n \n\ntris species tam dissimilis, tria talia texta,\n\n \n\nuna dies dabit exitio, multosque per annos\n\n \n\nsustentata ruet moles et machina mundi.\n\n \n\nnec me animi fallit quam res nova miraque menti\n\n \n\naccidat exitium caeli terraeque futurum,\n\n \n\net quam difficile id mihi sit pervincere dictis;\n\n \n\nut fit ubi insolitam rem adportes auribus ante\n\n \n\nnec tamen hanc possis oculorum subdere visu\n\n \n\nnec iacere indu manus, via qua munita fidei\n\n \n\nproxima fert humanum in pectus templaque mentis.\n\n \n\nsed tamen effabor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0008","text":"dictis dabit ipsa fidem res\n\n \n\nforsitan et graviter terrarum motibus ortis\n\n \n\nomnia conquassari in parvo tempore cernes.\n\n \n\nquod procul a nobis flectat fortuna gubernans,\n\n \n\net ratio potius quam res persuadeat ipsa\n\n \n\n succidere horrisono posse omnia victa fragore.\n\n  \n\nQua prius adgrediar quam de re fundere fata\n\n \n\nsanctius et multo certa ratione magis quam\n\n \n\nPythia quae tripode a Phoebi lauroque profatur,\n\n \n\nmulta tibi expediam doctis solacia dictis;\n\n \n\nreligione refrenatus ne forte rearis\n\n \n\nterras et solem et caelum, mare sidera lunam,\n\n \n\ncorpore divino debere aeterna manere,\n\n \n\nproptereaque putes ritu par esse Gigantum\n\n \n\npendere eos poenas inmani pro scelere omnis,\n\n \n\nqui ratione sua disturbent moenia mundi\n\n \n\npraeclarumque velint caeli restinguere solem\n\n \n\ninmortalia mortali sermone notantes;\n\n \n\nquae procul usque adeo divino a numine distent\n\n \n\ninque deum numero quae sint indigna videri,\n\n \n\nnotitiam potius praebere ut posse putentur\n\n \n\nquid sit vitali motu sensuque remotum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009","text":"quippe etenim non est, cum quovis corpore ut esse\n\n \n\nposse animi natura putetur consiliumque.\n\n \n\nsicut in aethere non arbor, non aequore salso\n\n \n\nnubes esse queunt neque pisces vivere in arvis\n\n \n\nnec cruor in lignis neque saxis sucus inesse,\n\n \n\ncertum ac dispositumst ubi quicquid crescat et insit,\n\n \n\nsic animi natura nequit sine corpore oriri\n\n \n\nsola neque a nervis et sanguine longius esse.\n\n \n\nquod si posset enim, multo prius ipsa animi vis\n\n \n\nin capite aut umeris aut imis calcibus esse\n\n \n\nposset et innasci quavis in parte soleret,\n\n \n\ntandem in eodem homine atque in eodem vase manere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010","text":"quod quoniam nostro quoque constat corpore certum\n\n \n\ndispositumque videtur ubi esse et crescere possit\n\n \n\nseorsum anima atque animus, tanto magis infitiandum\n\n \n\ntotum posse extra corpus formamque animalem\n\n \n\nputribus in glebis terrarum aut solis in igni\n\n \n\naut in aqua durare aut altis aetheris oris.\n\n \n\nhaud igitur constant divino praedita sensu,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem nequeunt vitaliter esse animata.\n\n  \n\nIllud item non est ut possis credere, sedes\n\n \n\nesse deum sanctas in mundi partibus ullis.\n\n \n\ntenvis enim natura deum longeque remota\n\n \n\nsensibus ab nostris animi vix mente videtur;\n\n \n\nquae quoniam manuum tactum suffugit et ictum,\n\n \n\ntactile nil nobis quod sit contingere debet;\n\n \n\ntangere enim non quit quod tangi non licet ipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","text":"quare etiam sedes quoque nostris sedibus esse\n\n \n\ndissimiles debent, tenues de corpore eorum;\n\n \n\nquae tibi posterius largo sermone probabo.\n\n \n\nDicere porro hominum causa voluisse parare\n\n \n\npraeclaram mundi naturam proptereaque\n\n \n\nadlaudabile opus divom laudare decere\n\n \n\naeternumque putare atque inmortale futurum,\n\n \n\nnec fas esse, deum quod sit ratione vetusta\n\n \n\ngentibus humanis fundatum perpetuo aevo,\n\n \n\nsollicitare suis ulla vi ex sedibus umquam\n\n \n\nnec verbis vexare et ab imo evertere summa,\n\n \n\n cetera de genere hoc adfingere et addere, Memmi,\n\n \n\ndesiperest. quid enim inmortalibus atque beatis\n\n \n\ngratia nostra queat largirier emolumenti,\n\n \n\nut nostra quicquam causa gerere adgrediantur?\n\n \n\nquidve novi potuit tanto post ante quietos\n\n \n\ninlicere ut cuperent vitam mutare priorem?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0012","text":"nam gaudere novis rebus debere videtur\n\n \n\ncui veteres obsunt; sed cui nihil accidit aegri\n\n \n\ntempore in ante acto, cum pulchre degeret aevom,\n\n \n\nquid potuit novitatis amorem accendere tali?\n\n \n\nquidve mali fuerat nobis non esse creatis?\n\n \n\nan, credo, in tenebris vita ac maerore iacebat,\n\n \n\ndonec diluxit rerum genitalis origo?\n\n \n\nnatus enim debet qui cumque est velle manere\n\n \n\nin vita, donec retinebit blanda voluptas;\n\n \n\nqui numquam vero vitae gustavit amorem\n\n \n\nnec fuit in numero, quid obest non esse creatum?\n\n \n\nexemplum porro gignundis rebus et ipsa\n\n \n\nnotities hominum divis unde insita primum est,\n\n \n\nquid vellent facere ut scirent animoque viderent,\n\n \n\nquove modost umquam vis cognita principiorum\n\n \n\nquidque inter sese permutato ordine possent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","text":"si non ipsa dedit speciem natura creandi?\n\n \n\nnamque ita multa modis multis primordia rerum\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore percita plagis\n\n \n\nponderibusque suis consuerunt concita ferri\n\n \n\nomnimodisque coire atque omnia pertemptare,\n\n \n\nquae cumque inter se possint congressa creare,\n\n \n\nut non sit mirum, si in talis disposituras\n\n \n\ndeciderunt quoque et in talis venere meatus,\n\n \n\nqualibus haec rerum geritur nunc summa novando.\n\n  \n\nQuod  iam rerum ignorem primordia quae sint,\n\n \n\nhoc tamen ex ipsis caeli rationibus ausim\n\n \n\nconfirmare aliisque ex rebus reddere multis,\n\n \n\nnequaquam nobis divinitus esse paratam\n\n \n\nnaturam rerum: tanta stat praedita culpa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","text":"principio quantum caeli tegit impetus ingens,\n\n \n\ninde avidam partem montes silvaeque ferarum\n\n \n\npossedere, tenent rupes vastaeque paludes\n\n \n\net mare, quod late terrarum distinet oras.\n\n \n\ninde duas porro prope partis fervidus ardor\n\n \n\nadsiduusque geli casus mortalibus aufert.\n\n \n\nquod super est arvi, tamen id natura sua vi\n\n \n\nsentibus obducat, ni vis humana resistat\n\n \n\nvitai causa valido consueta bidenti\n\n \n\ningemere et terram pressis proscindere aratris.\n\n \n\nsi non fecundas vertentes vomere glebas\n\n \n\nterraique solum subigentes cimus ad ortus.\n\n \n\nsponte sua nequeant liquidas existere in auras.\n\n \n\net tamen inter dum magno quaesita labore\n\n \n\ncum iam per terras frondent atque omnia florent,\n\n \n\naut nimiis torret fervoribus aetherius sol\n\n \n\naut subiti peremunt imbris gelidaeque pruinae\n\n \n\nflabraque ventorum violento turbine vexant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","text":"praeterea genus horriferum natura ferarum\n\n \n\n humanae genti infestum terraque marique\n\n \n\ncur alit atque auget? cur anni tempora morbos\n\n \n\nadportant? quare mors inmatura vagatur?\n\n \n\ntum porro puer, ut saevis proiectus ab undis\n\n \n\nnavita, nudus humi iacet infans indigus omni\n\n \n\nvitali auxilio, cum primum in luminis oras\n\n \n\nnixibus ex alvo matris natura profudit,\n\n \n\nvagituque locum lugubri complet, ut aequumst\n\n \n\ncui tantum in vita restet transire malorum.\n\n \n\nat variae crescunt pecudes armenta feraeque\n\n \n\nnec crepitacillis opus est nec cuiquam adhibendast\n\n \n\nalmae nutricis blanda atque infracta loquella\n\n \n\nnec varias quaerunt vestes pro tempore caeli,\n\n \n\ndenique non armis opus est, non moenibus altis,\n\n \n\nqui sua tutentur, quando omnibus omnia large\n\n \n\ntellus ipsa parit naturaque daedala rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","text":"Principio quoniam terrai corpus et umor\n\n \n\naurarumque leves animae calidique vapores,\n\n \n\ne quibus haec rerum consistere summa videtur,\n\n \n\nomnia nativo ac mortali corpore constant,\n\n \n\ndebet eodem omnis mundi natura putari.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim, quorum partis et membra videmus\n\n \n\ncorpore nativo mortalibus esse figuris,\n\n \n\nhaec eadem ferme mortalia cernimus esse\n\n \n\net nativa simul. qua propter maxima mundi\n\n \n\ncum videam membra ac partis consumpta regigni,\n\n \n\nscire licet caeli quoque item terraeque fuisse\n\n \n\nprincipiale aliquod tempus clademque futuram.\n\n \n\nIllud in his rebus ne corripuisse rearis\n\n \n\nme mihi, quod terram atque ignem mortalia sumpsi\n\n \n\nesse neque umorem dubitavi aurasque perire\n\n \n\natque eadem gigni rursusque augescere dixi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0017","text":"principio pars terrai non nulla, perusta\n\n \n\nsolibus adsiduis, multa pulsata pedum vi,\n\n \n\npulveris exhalat nebulam nubesque volantis,\n\n \n\nquas validi toto dispergunt aëre venti.\n\n \n\npars etiam glebarum ad diluviem revocatur\n\n \n\nimbribus et ripas radentia flumina rodunt.\n\n \n\npraeterea pro parte sua, quod cumque alit auget,\n\n \n\nredditur; et quoniam dubio procul esse videtur\n\n \n\nomniparens eadem rerum commune sepulcrum.\n\n \n\nergo terra tibi libatur et aucta recrescit.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, umore novo mare flumina fontes\n\n \n\nsemper abundare et latices manare perennis\n\n \n\nnil opus est verbis: magnus decursus aquarum\n\n \n\nundique declarat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0018","text":"sed primum quicquid aquai\n\n \n\ntollitur in summaque fit ut nihil umor abundet,\n\n \n\npartim quod validi verrentes aequora venti\n\n \n\ndeminuunt radiisque retexens aetherius sol,\n\n \n\npartim quod supter per terras diditur omnis;\n\n \n\npercolatur enim virus retroque remanat\n\n \n\nmateries umoris et ad caput amnibus omnis\n\n \n\nconvenit, inde super terras fluit agmine dulci\n\n \n\nqua via secta semel liquido pede detulit undas.\n\n  \n\nAëra nunc igitur dicam, qui corpore toto\n\n \n\n innumerabiliter privas mutatur in horas.\n\n \n\nsemper enim, quod cumque fluit de rebus, id omne\n\n \n\naëris in magnum fertur mare; qui nisi contra\n\n \n\ncorpora retribuat rebus recreetque fluentis,\n\n \n\nomnia iam resoluta forent et in aëra versa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0019","text":"haut igitur cessat gigni de rebus et in res\n\n \n\nreccidere, adsidue quoniam fluere omnia constat.\n\n  \n\nLargus item liquidi fons luminis, aetherius sol,\n\n \n\ninrigat adsidue caelum candore recenti\n\n \n\nsuppeditatque novo confestim lumine lumen.\n\n \n\nnam primum quicquid fulgoris disperit ei,\n\n \n\nquo cumque accidit. id licet hinc cognoscere possis,\n\n \n\nquod simul ac primum nubes succedere soli\n\n \n\ncoepere et radios inter quasi rumpere lucis,\n\n \n\nextemplo inferior pars horum disperit omnis\n\n \n\nterraque inumbratur qua nimbi cumque feruntur;\n\n \n\nut noscas splendore novo res semper egere\n\n \n\net primum iactum fulgoris quemque perire\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia res posse in sole videri,\n\n \n\nperpetuo ni suppeditet lucis caput ipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0020","text":"quin etiam nocturna tibi, terrestria quae sunt,\n\n \n\nlumina, pendentes lychni claraeque coruscis\n\n \n\nfulguribus pingues multa caligine taedae\n\n \n\nconsimili properant ratione, ardore ministro,\n\n \n\nsuppeditare novom lumen, tremere ignibus instant,\n\n \n\ninstant, nec loca lux inter quasi rupta relinquit:\n\n \n\nusque adeo properanter ab omnibus ignibus ei\n\n \n\nexitium celeri celeratur origine flammae.\n\n \n\nsic igitur solem lunam stellasque putandum\n\n \n\nex alio atque alio lucem iactare subortu\n\n \n\net primum quicquid flammarum perdere semper,\n\n \n\ninviolabilia haec ne credas forte vigere.\n\n  \n\nDenique non lapides quoque vinci cernis ab aevo,\n\n \n\nnon altas turris ruere et putrescere saxa,\n\n \n\nnon delubra deum simulacraque fessa fatisci\n\n \n\nnec sanctum numen fati protollere finis\n\n \n\nposse neque adversus naturae foedera niti?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0021","text":"denique non monimenta virum dilapsa videmus,\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nnon ruere avolsos silices a montibus altis\n\n \n\nnec validas aevi vires perferre patique\n\n \n\nfiniti? neque enim caderent avolsa repente,\n\n \n\nex infinito quae tempore pertolerassent\n\n \n\nomnia tormenta aetatis, privata fragore.\n\n  \n\nDenique iam tuere hoc, circum supraque quod omne\n\n \n\ncontinet amplexu terram: si procreat ex se\n\n \n\nomnia, quod quidam memorant, recipitque perempta,\n\n \n\ntotum nativum mortali corpore constat.\n\n \n\nnam quod cumque alias ex se res auget alitque,\n\n \n\ndeminui debet, recreari, cum recipit res.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea si nulla fuit genitalis origo\n\n \n\nterrarum et caeli semperque aeterna fuere,\n\n \n\ncur supera bellum Thebanum et funera Troiae\n\n \n\nnon alias alii quoque res cecinere poëtae?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0022","text":"quo tot facta virum totiens cecidere neque usquam\n\n \n\n aeternis famae monimentis insita florent?\n\n \n\nverum, ut opinor, habet novitatem summa recensque\n\n \n\nnaturast mundi neque pridem exordia cepit.\n\n \n\nquare etiam quaedam nunc artes expoliuntur,\n\n \n\nnunc etiam augescunt; nunc addita navigiis sunt\n\n \n\nmulta, modo organici melicos peperere sonores,\n\n \n\ndenique natura haec rerum ratioque repertast\n\n \n\nnuper, et hanc primus cum primis ipse repertus\n\n \n\nnunc ego sum in patrias qui possim vertere voces.\n\n \n\nQuod si forte fuisse ante hac eadem omnia credis,\n\n \n\nsed periise hominum torrenti saecla vapore,\n\n \n\naut cecidisse urbis magno vexamine mundi,\n\n \n\naut ex imbribus adsiduis exisse rapaces\n\n \n\nper terras amnes atque oppida coperuisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0023","text":"tanto quique magis victus fateare necessest\n\n \n\nexitium quoque terrarum caelique futurum;\n\n \n\nnam cum res tantis morbis tantisque periclis\n\n \n\ntemptarentur, ibi si tristior incubuisset\n\n \n\ncausa, darent late cladem magnasque ruinas.\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia mortales esse videmur,\n\n \n\ninter nos nisi quod morbis aegrescimus isdem\n\n \n\natque illi quos a vita natura removit.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea quae cumque manent aeterna necessust\n\n \n\naut, quia sunt solido cum corpore, respuere ictus\n\n \n\nnec penetrare pati sibi quicquam quod queat artas\n\n \n\ndissociare intus partis, ut materiai\n\n \n\ncorpora sunt, quorum naturam ostendimus ante,\n\n \n\naut ideo durare aetatem posse per omnem,\n\n \n\nplagarum quia sunt expertia, sicut inane est,\n\n \n\nquod manet intactum neque ab ictu fungitur hilum,\n\n \n\naut etiam quia nulla loci sit copia circum,\n\n \n\nquo quasi res possint discedere dissoluique,\n\n \n\nsicut summarum summa est aeterna, neque extra\n\n \n\nqui locus est quo dissiliant neque corpora sunt quae\n\n \n\npossint incidere et valida dissolvere plaga.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","text":"at neque, uti docui, solido cum corpore mundi\n\n \n\nnaturast, quoniam admixtumst in rebus inane,\n\n \n\nnec tamen est ut inane, neque autem corpora desunt,\n\n \n\nex infinito quae possint forte coorta\n\n \n\ncorruere hanc rerum violento turbine summam\n\n \n\naut aliam quamvis cladem inportare pericli,\n\n \n\nnec porro natura loci spatiumque profundi\n\n \n\ndeficit, exspargi quo possint moenia mundi,\n\n \n\naut alia quavis possunt vi pulsa perire.\n\n \n\nhaut igitur leti praeclusa est ianua caelo\n\n \n\nnec soli terraeque neque altis aequoris undis,\n\n \n\nsed patet immani et vasto respectat hiatu.\n\n \n\nquare etiam nativa necessumst confiteare\n\n \n\nhaec eadem; neque enim, mortali corpore quae sunt,\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore adhuc potuissent\n\n \n\ninmensi validas aevi contemnere vires.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0025","text":"Denique tantopere inter se cum maxima mundi\n\n \n\npugnent membra, pio nequaquam concita bello,\n\n \n\nnonne vides aliquam longi certaminis ollis\n\n \n\nposse dari finem, vel cum sol et vapor omnis\n\n \n\n omnibus epotis umoribus exsuperarint?\n\n \n\nquod facere intendunt, neque adhuc conata patrantur;\n\n \n\ntantum suppeditant amnes ultraque minantur\n\n \n\nomnia diluviare ex alto gurgite ponti:\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam verrentes aequora venti\n\n \n\ndeminuunt radiisque retexens aetherius sol,\n\n \n\net siccare prius confidunt omnia posse\n\n \n\nquam liquor incepti possit contingere finem.\n\n \n\ntantum spirantes aequo certamine bellum\n\n \n\nmagnis  de rebus cernere certant,\n\n \n\ncum semel interea fuerit superantior ignis\n\n \n\net semel, ut fama est, umor regnarit in arvis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0026","text":"ignis enim superavit et ambiens multa perussit,\n\n \n\navia cum Phaethonta rapax vis solis equorum\n\n \n\naethere raptavit toto terrasque per omnis.\n\n \n\nat pater omnipotens ira tum percitus acri\n\n \n\nmagnanimum Phaethonta repenti fulminis ictu\n\n \n\ndeturbavit equis in terram, Solque cadenti\n\n \n\nobvius aeternam succepit lampada mundi\n\n \n\ndisiectosque redegit equos iunxitque trementis,\n\n \n\ninde suum per iter recreavit cuncta gubernans,\n\n \n\nscilicet ut veteres Graium cecinere poëtae.\n\n \n\nquod procul a vera nimis est ratione repulsum.\n\n \n\nignis enim superare potest ubi materiai\n\n \n\nex infinito sunt corpora plura coorta;\n\n \n\ninde cadunt vires aliqua ratione revictae,\n\n \n\naut pereunt res exustae torrentibus auris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","text":"umor item quondam coepit superare coortus,\n\n \n\nut fama est, hominum vitas quando obruit undis;\n\n \n\ninde ubi vis aliqua ratione aversa recessit,\n\n \n\nex infinito fuerat quae cumque coorta,\n\n \n\nconstiterunt imbres et flumina vim minuerunt.\n\n  \n\nSed quibus ille modis coniectus materiai\n\n \n\nfundarit terram et caelum pontique profunda,\n\n \n\nsolis lunai cursus, ex ordine ponam.\n\n \n\nnam certe neque consilio primordia rerum\n\n \n\nordine se suo quaeque sagaci mente locarunt\n\n \n\nnec quos quaeque darent motus pepigere profecto;\n\n \n\nsed quia multa modis multis primordia rerum\n\n \n\nex infinito iam tempore percita plagis\n\n \n\nponderibusque suis consuerunt concita ferri\n\n \n\nomnimodisque coire atque omnia pertemptare,\n\n \n\nquae cumque inter se possent congressa creare,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti magnum volgata per aevom\n\n \n\nomnigenus coetus et motus experiundo\n\n \n\ntandem conveniant ea quae coniecta repente\n\n \n\nmagnarum rerum fiunt exordia saepe,\n\n \n\nterrai maris et caeli generisque animantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0028","text":"Hic neque tum solis rota cerni lumine largo\n\n \n\naltivolans poterat nec magni sidera mundi\n\n \n\nnec mare nec caelum nec denique terra neque aër\n\n \n\nnec similis nostris rebus res ulla videri,\n\n \n\nsed nova tempestas quaedam molesque coorta.\n\n \n\ndiffugere inde loci partes coepere paresque\n\n \n\ncum paribus iungi res et discludere mundum\n\n \n\n membraque dividere et magnas disponere partes\n\n \n\nomnigenis e principiis, discordia quorum\n\n \n\nintervalla vias conexus pondera plagas\n\n \n\nconcursus motus turbabat proelia miscens\n\n \n\npropter dissimilis formas variasque figuras,\n\n \n\nquod non omnia sic poterant coniuncta manere\n\n \n\nnec motus inter sese dare convenientis,\n\n \n\nhoc est, a terris altum secernere caelum,\n\n \n\net sorsum mare, uti secreto umore pateret,\n\n \n\nseorsus item puri secretique aetheris ignes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0029","text":"Quippe etenim primum terrai corpora quaeque,\n\n \n\npropterea quod erant gravia et perplexa, coibant\n\n \n\nin medio atque imas capiebant omnia sedes;\n\n \n\nquae quanto magis inter se perplexa coibant,\n\n \n\ntam magis expressere ea quae mare sidera solem\n\n \n\nlunamque efficerent et magni moenia mundi;\n\n \n\nomnia enim magis haec e levibus atque rutundis\n\n \n\nseminibus multoque minoribus sunt elementis\n\n \n\nquam tellus. ideo per rara foramina terrae\n\n \n\npartibus erumpens primus se sustulit aether\n\n \n\nignifer et multos secum levis abstulit ignis,\n\n \n\nnon alia longe ratione ac saepe videmus,\n\n \n\naurea cum primum gemmantis rore per herbas\n\n \n\nmatutina rubent radiati lumina solis\n\n \n\nexhalantque lacus nebulam fluviique perennes\n\n \n\nipsaque ut inter dum tellus fumare videtur;\n\n \n\nomnia quae sursum cum conciliantur, in alto\n\n \n\ncorpore concreto subtexunt nubila caelum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0030","text":"sic igitur tum se levis ac diffusilis aether\n\n \n\ncorpore concreto circum datus undique saepsit\n\n \n\net late diffusus in omnis undique partis\n\n \n\nomnia sic avido complexu cetera saepsit.\n\n \n\nhunc exordia sunt solis lunaeque secuta,\n\n \n\ninterutrasque globi quorum vertuntur in auris;\n\n \n\nquae neque terra sibi adscivit nec maximus aether,\n\n \n\nquod neque tam fuerunt gravia ut depressa sederent,\n\n \n\nnec levia ut possent per summas labier oras,\n\n \n\net tamen interutrasque ita sunt, ut corpora viva\n\n \n\nversent et partes ut mundi totius extent;\n\n \n\nquod genus in nobis quaedam licet in statione\n\n \n\nmembra manere, tamen cum sint ea quae moveantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0031","text":"his igitur rebus retractis terra repente,\n\n \n\nmaxuma qua nunc se ponti plaga caerula tendit,\n\n \n\nsuccidit et salso suffudit gurgite fossas.\n\n \n\ninque dies quanto circum magis aetheris aestus\n\n \n\net radii solis cogebant undique terram\n\n \n\nverberibus crebris extrema ad limina fartam\n\n \n\nin medio ut propulsa suo condensa coiret,\n\n \n\ntam magis expressus salsus de corpore sudor\n\n \n\naugebat mare manando camposque natantis,\n\n \n\net tanto magis illa foras elapsa volabant\n\n \n\ncorpora multa vaporis et aëris altaque caeli\n\n \n\ndensabant procul a terris fulgentia templa.\n\n \n\nsidebant campi, crescebant montibus altis\n\n \n\nascensus; neque enim poterant subsidere saxa\n\n \n\n nec pariter tantundem omnes succumbere partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0032","text":"Sic igitur terrae concreto corpore pondus\n\n \n\nconstitit atque omnis mundi quasi limus in imum\n\n \n\nconfluxit gravis et subsedit funditus ut faex;\n\n \n\ninde mare, inde aër, inde aether ignifer ipse\n\n \n\ncorporibus liquidis sunt omnia pura relicta\n\n \n\net leviora aliis alia, et liquidissimus aether\n\n \n\natque levissimus aërias super influit auras\n\n \n\nnec liquidum corpus turbantibus aëris auris\n\n \n\ncommiscet; sinit haec violentis omnia verti\n\n \n\nturbinibus, sinit incertis turbare procellis,\n\n \n\nipse suos ignis certo fert impete labens.\n\n \n\nnam modice fluere atque uno posse aethera nisu\n\n \n\nsignificat Pontos, mare certo quod fluit aestu\n\n \n\nunum labendi conservans usque tenorem.\n\n  \n\nMotibus astrorum nunc quae sit causa canamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0033","text":"principio magnus caeli si vortitur orbis,\n\n \n\nex utraque polum parti premere aëra nobis\n\n \n\ndicendum est extraque tenere et claudere utrimque;\n\n \n\ninde alium supra fluere atque intendere eodem\n\n \n\nquo volvenda micant aeterni sidera mundi;\n\n \n\naut alium supter, contra qui subvehat orbem,\n\n \n\nut fluvios versare rotas atque austra videmus.\n\n \n\nest etiam quoque uti possit caelum omne manere\n\n \n\nin statione, tamen cum lucida signa ferantur,\n\n \n\nsive quod inclusi rapidi sunt aetheris aestus\n\n \n\nquaerentesque viam circum versantur et ignes\n\n \n\npassim per caeli volvunt summania templa,\n\n \n\nsive aliunde fluens alicunde extrinsecus aër\n\n \n\nversat agens ignis, sive ipsi serpere possunt,\n\n \n\nquo cuiusque cibus vocat atque invitat euntis,\n\n \n\nflammea per caelum pascentis corpora passim.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","text":"nam quid in hoc mundo sit eorum ponere certum\n\n \n\ndifficilest; sed quid possit fiatque per omne\n\n \n\nin variis mundis varia ratione creatis,\n\n \n\nid doceo plurisque sequor disponere causas,\n\n \n\nmotibus astrorum quae possint esse per omne;\n\n \n\ne quibus una tamen sit et haec quoque causa necessest,\n\n \n\nquae vegeat motum signis; sed quae sit earum\n\n \n\npraecipere haud quaquamst pedetemptim progredientis.\n\n  \n\nTerraque ut in media mundi regione quiescat,\n\n \n\nevanescere paulatim et decrescere pondus\n\n \n\nconvenit atque aliam naturam supter habere\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo coniunctam atque uniter aptam\n\n \n\npartibus aëriis mundi, quibus insita vivit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0035","text":"propterea non est oneri neque deprimit auras,\n\n \n\nut sua cuique homini nullo sunt pondere membra\n\n \n\nnec caput est oneri collo nec denique totum\n\n \n\ncorporis in pedibus pondus sentimus inesse;\n\n \n\nat quae cumque foris veniunt inpostaque nobis\n\n \n\npondera sunt laedunt, permulto saepe minora.\n\n \n\nusque adeo magni refert quid quaeque queat res.\n\n \n\nsic igitur tellus non est aliena repente\n\n \n\nallata atque auris aliunde obiecta alienis,\n\n \n\nsed pariter prima concepta ab origine mundi\n\n \n\n certaque pars eius, quasi nobis membra videntur.\n\n \n\nPraeterea grandi tonitru concussa repente\n\n \n\nterra supra quae se sunt concutit omnia motu;\n\n \n\nquod facere haut ulla posset ratione, nisi esset\n\n \n\npartibus aëriis mundi caeloque revincta;\n\n \n\nnam communibus inter se radicibus haerent\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo coniuncta atque uniter aucta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0036","text":"Nonne vides etiam quam magno pondere nobis\n\n \n\nsustineat corpus tenuissima vis animai,\n\n \n\npropterea quia tam coniuncta atque uniter apta est?\n\n \n\nDenique iam saltu pernici tollere corpus\n\n \n\nquid potis est nisi vis animae, quae membra gubernat?\n\n \n\niamne vides quantum tenuis natura valere\n\n \n\npossit, ubi est coniuncta gravi cum corpore, ut aër\n\n \n\nconiunctus terris et nobis est animi vis?\n\n  \n\nNec nimio solis maior rota nec minor ardor\n\n \n\nesse potest, nostris quam sensibus esse videtur.\n\n \n\nnam quibus e spatiis cumque ignes lumina possunt\n\n \n\nadiicere et calidum membris adflare vaporem,\n\n \n\nnil magnis intervallis de corpore libant\n\n \n\nflammarum, nihil ad speciem est contractior ignis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0037","text":"proinde, calor quoniam solis lumenque profusum\n\n \n\nperveniunt nostros ad sensus et loca fulgent,\n\n \n\nforma quoque hinc solis debet filumque videri,\n\n \n\nnil adeo ut possis plus aut minus addere vere.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nlunaque sive notho fertur loca lumine lustrans,\n\n \n\nsive suam proprio iactat de corpore lucem,\n\n \n\nquidquid id est, nihilo fertur maiore figura\n\n \n\nquam, nostris oculis qua cernimus, esse videtur.\n\n \n\nnam prius omnia, quae longe semota tuemur\n\n \n\naëra per multum, specie confusa videntur\n\n \n\nquam minui filum. quapropter luna necesse est,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem claram speciem certamque figuram\n\n \n\npraebet, ut est oris extremis cumque notata,\n\n \n\nquanta quoquest, tanta hinc nobis videatur in alto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0038","text":"postremo quos cumque vides hinc aetheris ignes,\n\n \n\nscire licet perquam pauxillo posse minores\n\n \n\nesse vel exigua maioris parte brevique.\n\n \n\nquandoquidem quos cumque in terris cernimus ,\n\n \n\ndum tremor  clarus dum cernitur ardor eorum,\n\n \n\nperparvom quiddam inter dum mutare videntur\n\n \n\nalteram utram in partem filum, quo longius absunt.\n\n  \n\nIllud item non est mirandum, qua ratione\n\n \n\ntantulus ille queat tantum sol mittere lumen,\n\n \n\nquod maria ac terras omnis caelumque rigando\n\n \n\ncompleat et calido perfundat cuncta vapore.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nnam licet hinc mundi patefactum totius unum\n\n \n\nlargifluum fontem scatere atque erumpere lumen,\n\n \n\nex omni mundo quia sic elementa vaporis\n\n \n\nundique conveniunt et sic coniectus eorum\n\n \n\nconfluit, ex uno capite hic ut profluat ardor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0039","text":"nonne vides etiam quam late parvus aquai\n\n \n\nprata riget fons inter dum campisque redundet?\n\n \n\n est etiam quoque uti non magno solis ab igni\n\n \n\naëra percipiat calidis fervoribus ardor,\n\n \n\nopportunus ita est si forte et idoneus aër,\n\n \n\nut queat accendi parvis ardoribus ictus;\n\n \n\nquod genus inter dum segetes stipulamque videmus\n\n \n\naccidere ex una scintilla incendia passim.\n\n \n\nforsitan et rosea sol alte lampade lucens\n\n \n\npossideat multum caecis fervoribus ignem\n\n \n\ncircum se, nullo qui sit fulgore notatus,\n\n \n\naestifer ut tantum radiorum exaugeat ictum.\n\n  \n\nNec ratio solis simplex  recta patescit,\n\n \n\nquo pacto aestivis e partibus aegocerotis\n\n \n\nbrumalis adeat flexus atque inde revertens\n\n \n\ncanceris ut vertat metas ad solstitialis,\n\n \n\nlunaque mensibus id spatium videatur obire,\n\n \n\nannua sol in quo consumit tempora cursu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0040","text":"non, inquam, simplex his rebus reddita causast.\n\n \n\nnam fieri vel cum primis id posse videtur,\n\n \n\nDemocriti quod sancta viri sententia ponit,\n\n \n\nquanto quaeque magis sint terram sidera propter,\n\n \n\ntanto posse minus cum caeli turbine ferri;\n\n \n\nevanescere enim rapidas illius et acris\n\n \n\nimminui supter viris, ideoque relinqui\n\n \n\npaulatim solem cum posterioribus signis,\n\n \n\ninferior multo quod sit quam fervida signa.\n\n \n\net magis hoc lunam: quanto demissior eius\n\n \n\ncursus abest procul a caelo terrisque propinquat,\n\n \n\ntanto posse minus cum signis tendere cursum.\n\n \n\nflaccidiore etiam quanto iam turbine fertur\n\n \n\ninferior quam sol, tanto magis omnia signa\n\n \n\nhanc adipiscuntur circum praeterque feruntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0041","text":"propterea fit ut haec ad signum quodque reverti\n\n \n\nmobilius videatur, ad hanc quia signa revisunt.\n\n \n\nfit quoque ut e mundi transversis partibus aër\n\n \n\nalternis certo fluere alter tempore possit,\n\n \n\nqui queat aestivis solem detrudere signis\n\n \n\nbrumalis usque ad flexus gelidumque rigorem,\n\n \n\net qui reiciat gelidis a frigoris umbris\n\n \n\naestiferas usque in partis et fervida signa.\n\n \n\net ratione pari lunam stellasque putandumst,\n\n \n\nquae volvunt magnos in magnis orbibus annos,\n\n \n\naëribus posse alternis e partibus ire.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam diversis nubila ventis\n\n \n\ndiversas ire in partis inferna supernis?\n\n \n\nqui minus illa queant per magnos aetheris orbis\n\n \n\naestibus inter se diversis sidera ferri?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0042","text":"At nox obruit ingenti caligine terras,\n\n \n\naut ubi de longo cursu sol ultima caeli\n\n \n\nimpulit atque suos efflavit languidus ignis\n\n \n\nconcussos itere et labefactos aëre multo,\n\n \n\naut quia sub terras cursum convortere cogit\n\n \n\nvis eadem, supra quae terras pertulit orbem.\n\n \n\nTempore item certo roseam Matuta per oras\n\n \n\naetheris auroram differt et lumina pandit,\n\n \n\naut quia sol idem, sub terras ille revertens,\n\n \n\n anticipat caelum radiis accendere temptans,\n\n \n\naut quia conveniunt ignes et semina multa\n\n \n\nconfluere ardoris consuerunt tempore certo,\n\n \n\nquae faciunt solis nova semper lumina gigni;\n\n \n\nquod genus Idaeis fama est e montibus altis\n\n \n\ndispersos ignis orienti lumine cerni,\n\n \n\ninde coire globum quasi in unum et conficere orbem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","text":"nec tamen illud in his rebus mirabile debet\n\n \n\nesse, quod haec ignis tam certo tempore possint\n\n \n\nsemina confluere et solis reparare nitorem.\n\n \n\nmulta videmus enim, certo quae tempore fiunt\n\n \n\nomnibus in rebus. florescunt tempore certo\n\n \n\narbusta et certo dimittunt tempore florem.\n\n \n\nnec minus in certo dentes cadere imperat aetas\n\n \n\ntempore et inpubem molli pubescere veste\n\n \n\net pariter mollem malis demittere barbam.\n\n \n\nfulmina postremo nix imbres nubila venti\n\n \n\nnon nimis incertis fiunt in partibus anni.\n\n \n\nnamque ubi sic fuerunt causarum exordia prima\n\n \n\natque ita res mundi cecidere ab origine prima,\n\n \n\nconseque quoque iam redeunt ex ordine certo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0044","text":"Crescere itemque dies licet et tabescere noctes,\n\n \n\net minui luces, cum sumant augmina noctis,\n\n \n\naut quia sol idem sub terras atque superne\n\n \n\nimparibus currens amfractibus aetheris oras\n\n \n\npartit et in partis non aequas dividit orbem,\n\n \n\net quod ab alterutra detraxit parte, reponit\n\n \n\neius in adversa tanto plus parte relatus,\n\n \n\ndonec ad id signum caeli pervenit, ubi anni\n\n \n\nnodus nocturnas exaequat lucibus umbras;\n\n \n\nnam medio cursu flatus aquilonis et austri\n\n \n\ndistinet aequato caelum discrimine metas\n\n \n\npropter signiferi posituram totius orbis,\n\n \n\nannua sol in quo concludit tempora serpens,\n\n \n\nobliquo terras et caelum lumine lustrans,\n\n \n\nut ratio declarat eorum qui loca caeli\n\n \n\nomnia dispositis signis ornata notarunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0045","text":"aut quia crassior est certis in partibus aër,\n\n \n\nsub terris ideo tremulum iubar haesitat ignis\n\n \n\nnec penetrare potest facile atque emergere ad ortus;\n\n \n\npropterea noctes hiberno tempore longae\n\n \n\ncessant, dum veniat radiatum insigne diei.\n\n \n\naut etiam, quia sic alternis partibus anni\n\n \n\ntardius et citius consuerunt confluere ignes,\n\n \n\nqui faciunt solem certa de surgere parte,\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti videantur dicere verum.\n\n  \n\nLuna potest solis radiis percussa nitere\n\n \n\ninque dies magis  lumen convertere nobis\n\n \n\nad speciem, quantum solis secedit ab orbi,\n\n \n\ndonique eum contra pleno bene lumine fulsit\n\n \n\natque oriens obitus eius super edita vidit;\n\n \n\ninde minutatim retro quasi condere lumen\n\n \n\ndebet item, quanto propius iam solis ad ignem\n\n \n\nlabitur ex alia signorum parte per orbem;\n\n \n\nut faciunt, lunam qui fingunt esse pilai\n\n \n\n consimilem cursusque viam sub sole tenere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0046","text":"est etiam quare proprio cum lumine possit\n\n \n\nvolvier et varias splendoris reddere formas;\n\n \n\ncorpus enim licet esse aliud, quod fertur et una\n\n \n\nlabitur omnimodis occursans officiensque,\n\n \n\nnec potis est cerni, quia cassum lumine fertur.\n\n \n\nversarique potest, globus ut, si forte, pilai\n\n \n\ndimidia ex parti candenti lumine tinctus,\n\n \n\nversandoque globum variantis edere formas,\n\n \n\ndonique eam partem, quae cumque est ignibus aucta,\n\n \n\nad speciem vertit nobis oculosque patentis;\n\n \n\ninde minutatim retro contorquet et aufert\n\n \n\nluciferam partem glomeraminis atque pilai;\n\n \n\nut Babylonica Chaldaeum doctrina refutans\n\n \n\nastrologorum artem contra convincere tendit,\n\n \n\nproinde quasi id fieri nequeat quod pugnat uterque\n\n \n\naut minus hoc illo sit cur amplectier ausis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0047","text":"denique cur nequeat semper nova luna creari\n\n \n\nordine formarum certo certisque figuris\n\n \n\ninque dies privos aborisci quaeque creata\n\n \n\natque alia illius reparari in parte locoque,\n\n \n\ndifficilest ratione docere et vincere verbis,\n\n \n\nordine cum  tam certo multa creari.\n\n \n\nit Ver et Venus et Veneris praenuntius ante\n\n \n\npennatus graditur, Zephyri vestigia propter\n\n \n\nFlora quibus mater praespargens ante viai\n\n \n\ncuncta coloribus egregiis et odoribus opplet.\n\n \n\ninde loci sequitur Calor aridus et comes una\n\n \n\npulverulenta Ceres  etesia flabra aquilonum.\n\n \n\ninde Autumnus adit, graditur simul Euhius Euan.\n\n \n\ninde aliae tempestates ventique secuntur,\n\n \n\naltitonans Volturnus et Auster fulmine pollens.\n\n \n\ntandem Bruma nives adfert pigrumque rigorem\n\n \n\nreddit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","text":"Hiemps sequitur crepitans hanc dentibus algu.\n\n \n\nquo minus est mirum, si certo tempore luna\n\n \n\ngignitur et certo deletur tempore rusus,\n\n \n\ncum fieri possint tam certo tempore multa.\n\n  \n\nSolis item quoque defectus lunaeque latebras\n\n \n\npluribus e causis fieri tibi posse putandumst.\n\n \n\nnam cur luna queat terram secludere solis\n\n \n\nlumine et a terris altum caput obstruere ei,\n\n \n\nobiciens caecum radiis ardentibus orbem,\n\n \n\ntempore eodem aliut facere id non posse putetur\n\n \n\ncorpus, quod cassum labatur lumine semper?\n\n \n\nsolque suos etiam dimittere languidus ignis\n\n \n\ntempore cur certo nequeat recreareque lumen,\n\n \n\ncum loca praeteriit flammis infesta per auras,\n\n \n\nquae faciunt ignis interstingui atque perire?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","text":"et cur terra queat lunam spoliare vicissim\n\n \n\nlumine et oppressum solem super ipsa tenere,\n\n \n\nmenstrua dum rigidas coni perlabitur umbras,\n\n \n\ntempore eodem aliud nequeat succurrere lunae\n\n \n\ncorpus vel supra solis perlabier orbem,\n\n \n\nquod radios inter rumpat lumenque profusum?\n\n \n\net tamen ipsa suo si fulget luna nitore,\n\n \n\n cur nequeat certa mundi languescere parte,\n\n \n\ndum loca luminibus propriis inimica per exit?\n\n \n\n.\n\n  \n\nQuod superest, quoniam magni per caerula mundi\n\n \n\nqua fieri quicquid posset ratione resolvi,\n\n \n\nsolis uti varios cursus lunaeque meatus\n\n \n\nnoscere possemus quae vis et causa cieret,\n\n \n\nquove modo  offecto lumine obire\n\n \n\net neque opinantis tenebris obducere terras,\n\n \n\ncum quasi conivent et aperto lumine rursum\n\n \n\nomnia convisunt clara loca candida luce,\n\n \n\nnunc redeo ad mundi novitatem et mollia terrae\n\n \n\narva, novo fetu quid primum in luminis oras\n\n \n\ntollere et incertis crerint committere ventis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0050","text":"Principio genus herbarum viridemque nitorem\n\n \n\nterra dedit circum collis camposque per omnis,\n\n \n\nflorida fulserunt viridanti prata colore,\n\n \n\narboribusque datumst variis exinde per auras\n\n \n\ncrescendi magnum inmissis certamen habenis.\n\n \n\nut pluma atque pili primum saetaeque creantur\n\n \n\nquadripedum membris et corpore pennipotentum,\n\n \n\nsic nova tum tellus herbas virgultaque primum\n\n \n\nsustulit, inde loci mortalia saecla creavit\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis varia ratione coorta.\n\n \n\nnam neque de caelo cecidisse animalia possunt,\n\n \n\nnec terrestria de salsis exisse lacunis.\n\n \n\nlinquitur ut merito maternum nomen adepta\n\n \n\nterra sit, e terra quoniam sunt cuncta creata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","text":"multaque nunc etiam existunt animalia terris\n\n \n\nimbribus et calido solis concreta vapore;\n\n \n\nquo minus est mirum, si tum sunt plura coorta\n\n \n\net maiora, nova tellure atque aethere adulta.\n\n \n\nprincipio genus alituum variaeque volucres\n\n \n\nova relinquebant exclusae tempore verno,\n\n \n\nfolliculos ut nunc teretis aestate cicadae\n\n \n\nlincunt sponte sua victum vitamque petentes.\n\n \n\ntum tibi terra dedit primum mortalia saecla.\n\n \n\nmultus enim calor atque umor superabat in arvis.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi quaeque loci regio opportuna dabatur,\n\n \n\ncrescebant uteri terram radicibus apti;\n\n \n\nquos ubi tempore maturo pate fecerat aetas\n\n \n\ninfantum, fugiens umorem aurasque petessens,\n\n \n\nconvertebat ibi natura foramina terrae\n\n \n\net sucum venis cogebat fundere apertis\n\n \n\nconsimilem lactis, sicut nunc femina quaeque\n\n \n\ncum peperit, dulci repletur lacte, quod omnis\n\n \n\nimpetus in mammas convertitur ille alimenti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","text":"terra cibum pueris, vestem vapor, herba cubile\n\n \n\npraebebat multa et molli lanugine abundans.\n\n \n\nat novitas mundi nec frigora dura ciebat\n\n \n\nnec nimios aestus nec magnis viribus auras.\n\n \n\nomnia enim pariter crescunt et robora sumunt.\n\n  \n\nQuare etiam atque etiam maternum nomen adepta\n\n \n\nterra tenet merito, quoniam genus ipsa creavit\n\n \n\nhumanum atque animal prope certo tempore fudit\n\n \n\n omne quod in magnis bacchatur montibus passim,\n\n \n\naëriasque simul volucres variantibus formis.\n\n \n\nsed quia finem aliquam pariendi debet habere,\n\n \n\ndestitit, ut mulier spatio defessa vetusto.\n\n \n\nmutat enim mundi naturam totius aetas\n\n \n\nex alioque alius status excipere omnia debet\n\n \n\nnec manet ulla sui similis res: omnia migrant,\n\n \n\nomnia commutat natura et vertere cogit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0053","text":"namque aliud putrescit et aevo debile languet,\n\n \n\nporro aliud crescit et  contemptibus exit.\n\n \n\nsic igitur mundi naturam totius aetas\n\n \n\nmutat, et ex alio terram status excipit alter,\n\n \n\nquod potuit nequeat, possit quod non tulit ante.\n\n \n\nMultaque tum tellus etiam portenta creare\n\n \n\nconatast mira facie membrisque coorta,\n\n \n\nandrogynem, interutras necutrumque utrimque remotum,\n\n \n\norba pedum partim, manuum viduata vicissim,\n\n \n\nmuta sine ore etiam, sine voltu caeca reperta,\n\n \n\nvinctaque membrorum per totum corpus adhaesu,\n\n \n\nnec facere ut possent quicquam nec cedere quoquam\n\n \n\nnec vitare malum nec sumere quod volet usus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","text":"cetera de genere hoc monstra ac portenta creabat,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam natura absterruit auctum\n\n \n\nnec potuere cupitum aetatis tangere florem\n\n \n\nnec reperire cibum nec iungi per Veneris res.\n\n \n\nmulta videmus enim rebus concurrere debere,\n\n \n\nut propagando possint procudere saecla;\n\n \n\npabula primum ut sint, genitalia deinde per artus\n\n \n\nsemina qua possint membris manare remissis,\n\n \n\nfeminaque ut maribus coniungi possit, habere,\n\n \n\nmutua qui mutent inter se gaudia uterque.\n\n  \n\nMultaque tum interiisse animantum saecla necessest\n\n \n\nnec potuisse propagando procudere prolem.\n\n \n\nnam quae cumque vides vesci vitalibus auris,\n\n \n\naut dolus aut virtus aut denique mobilitas est\n\n \n\nex ineunte aevo genus id tutaTA reservans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","text":"multaque sunt, nobis ex utilitate sua quae\n\n \n\ncommendata manent, tutelae tradita nostrae.\n\n \n\nprincipio genus acre leonum saevaque saecla\n\n \n\ntutatast virtus, volpes dolus et fuga cervos.\n\n \n\nat levisomna canum fido cum pectore corda,\n\n \n\net genus omne quod est veterino semine partum\n\n \n\nlanigeraeque simul pecudes et bucera saecla\n\n \n\nomnia sunt hominum tutelae tradita, Memmi;\n\n \n\nnam cupide fugere feras pacemque secuta\n\n \n\nsunt et larga suo sine pabula parta labore,\n\n \n\nquae damus utilitatis eorum praemia causa.\n\n \n\nat quis nil horum tribuit natura, nec ipsa\n\n \n\nsponte sua possent ut vivere nec dare nobis\n\n \n\nutilitatem aliquam, quare pateremur eorum\n\n \n\npraesidio nostro pasci genus esseque tutum,\n\n \n\nscilicet haec aliis praedae lucroque iacebant\n\n \n\nindupedita suis fatalibus omnia vinclis,\n\n \n\ndonec ad interitum genus id natura redegit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056","text":"Sed neque Centauri fuerunt nec tempore in ullo\n\n \n\n esse queunt duplici natura et corpore bino\n\n \n\nex alienigenis membris compacta, potestas\n\n \n\nhinc illinc partis ut sat par esse potissit.\n\n \n\nid licet hinc quamvis hebeti cognoscere corde.\n\n \n\nprincipio circum tribus actis impiger annis\n\n \n\nfloret equus, puer haut quaquam; nam saepe etiam nunc\n\n \n\nubera mammarum in somnis lactantia quaeret.\n\n \n\npost ubi equum validae vires aetate senecta\n\n \n\nmembraque deficiunt fugienti languida vita,\n\n \n\ntum demum puerili aevo florenta iuventas\n\n \n\nofficit et molli vestit lanugine malas;\n\n \n\nne forte ex homine et veterino semine equorum\n\n \n\nconfieri credas Centauros posse neque esse,\n\n \n\naut rapidis canibus succinctas semimarinis\n\n \n\ncorporibus Scyllas et cetera de genere horum,\n\n \n\ninter se quorum discordia membra videmus;\n\n \n\nquae neque florescunt pariter nec robora sumunt\n\n \n\ncorporibus neque proiciunt aetate senecta\n\n \n\nnec simili Venere ardescunt nec moribus unis\n\n \n\nconveniunt neque sunt eadem iucunda per artus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0057","text":"quippe videre licet pinguescere saepe cicuta\n\n \n\nbarbigeras pecudes, homini quae est acre venenum.\n\n \n\nflamma quidem  cum corpora fulva leonum\n\n \n\ntam soleat torrere atque urere quam genus omne\n\n \n\nvisceris in terris quod cumque et sanguinis extet,\n\n \n\nqui fieri potuit, triplici cum corpore ut una,\n\n \n\nprima leo, postrema draco, media ipsa, Chimaera\n\n \n\nore foras acrem flaret de corpore flammam?\n\n \n\nquare etiam tellure nova caeloque recenti\n\n \n\ntalia qui fingit potuisse animalia gigni,\n\n \n\nnixus in hoc uno novitatis nomine inani,\n\n \n\nmulta licet simili ratione effutiat ore,\n\n \n\naurea tum dicat per terras flumina vulgo\n\n \n\nfluxisse et gemmis florere arbusta suësse\n\n \n\naut hominem tanto membrorum esse impete natum,\n\n \n\ntrans maria alta pedum nisus ut ponere posset\n\n \n\net manibus totum circum se vertere caelum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0058","text":"nam quod multa fuere in terris semina rerum,\n\n \n\ntempore quo primum tellus animalia fudit,\n\n \n\nnil tamen est signi mixtas potuisse creari\n\n \n\ninter se pecudes compactaque membra animantum,\n\n \n\npropterea quia quae de terris nunc quoque abundant\n\n \n\nherbarum genera ac fruges arbustaque laeta\n\n \n\nnon tamen inter se possunt complexa creari,\n\n \n\nsed res quaeque suo ritu procedit et omnes\n\n \n\nfoedere naturae certo discrimina servant.\n\n  \n\nEt genus humanum multo fuit illud in arvis\n\n \n\ndurius, ut decuit, tellus quod dura creasset,\n\n \n\net maioribus et solidis magis ossibus intus\n\n \n\nfundatum, validis aptum per viscera nervis,\n\n \n\nnec facile ex aestu nec frigore quod caperetur\n\n \n\nnec novitate cibi nec labi corporis ulla.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0059","text":"multaque per caelum solis volventia lustra\n\n \n\nvolgivago vitam tractabant more ferarum.\n\n \n\nnec robustus erat curvi moderator aratri\n\n \n\n quisquam, nec scibat ferro molirier arva\n\n \n\nnec nova defodere in terram virgulta neque altis\n\n \n\narboribus veteres decidere falcibus ramos.\n\n \n\nquod sol atque imbres dederant, quod terra crearat\n\n \n\nsponte sua, satis id placabat pectora donum.\n\n \n\nglandiferas inter curabant corpora quercus\n\n \n\nplerumque; et quae nunc hiberno tempore cernis\n\n \n\narbita puniceo fieri matura colore,\n\n \n\nplurima tum tellus etiam maiora ferebat.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea novitas tum florida mundi\n\n \n\npabula dura tulit, miseris mortalibus ampla.\n\n \n\nat sedare sitim fluvii fontesque vocabant,\n\n \n\nut nunc montibus e magnis decursus aquai\n\n \n\nclaricitat late sitientia saecla ferarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0060","text":"denique nota vagis silvestria templa tenebant\n\n \n\nnympharum, quibus e scibant umore fluenta\n\n \n\nlubrica proluvie larga lavere umida saxa,\n\n \n\numida saxa, super viridi stillantia musco,\n\n \n\net partim plano scatere atque erumpere campo.\n\n \n\nnecdum res igni scibant tractare neque uti\n\n \n\npellibus et spoliis corpus vestire ferarum,\n\n \n\nsed nemora atque cavos montis silvasque colebant\n\n \n\net frutices inter condebant squalida membra\n\n \n\nverbera ventorum vitare imbrisque coacti.\n\n \n\nnec commune bonum poterant spectare neque ullis\n\n \n\nmoribus inter se scibant nec legibus uti.\n\n \n\nquod cuique obtulerat praedae fortuna, ferebat\n\n \n\nsponte sua sibi quisque valere et vivere doctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0061","text":"et Venus in silvis iungebat corpora amantum;\n\n \n\nconciliabat enim vel mutua quamque cupido\n\n \n\nvel violenta viri vis atque inpensa libido\n\n \n\nvel pretium, glandes atque arbita vel pira lecta.\n\n \n\net manuum mira freti virtute pedumque\n\n \n\nconsectabantur silvestria saecla ferarum\n\n \n\nmissilibus saxis et magno pondere clavae.\n\n \n\nmultaque vincebant, vitabant pauca latebris;\n\n \n\nsaetigerisque pares subus silvestria membra\n\n \n\nnuda dabant terrae nocturno tempore capti,\n\n \n\ncircum se foliis ac frondibus involventes.\n\n \n\nnec plangore diem magno solemque per agros\n\n \n\nquaerebant pavidi palantes noctis in umbris,\n\n \n\nsed taciti respectabant somnoque sepulti,\n\n \n\ndum rosea face sol inferret lumina caelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0062","text":"a parvis quod enim consuerant cernere semper\n\n \n\nalterno tenebras et lucem tempore gigni,\n\n \n\nnon erat ut fieri posset mirarier umquam\n\n \n\nnec diffidere, ne terras aeterna teneret\n\n \n\nnox in perpetuum detracto lumine solis.\n\n \n\nsed magis illud erat curae, quod saecla ferarum\n\n \n\ninfestam miseris faciebant saepe quietem.\n\n \n\neiectique domo fugiebant saxea tecta\n\n \n\nspumigeri suis adventu validique leonis\n\n \n\natque intempesta cedebant nocte paventes\n\n \n\nhospitibus saevis instrata cubilia fronde.\n\n  \n\nNec nimio tum plus quam nunc mortalia saecla\n\n \n\n dulcia linquebant lamentis lumina vitae.\n\n \n\nunus enim tum quisque magis deprensus eorum\n\n \n\npabula viva feris praebebat, dentibus haustus,\n\n \n\net nemora ac montis gemitu silvasque replebat\n\n \n\nviva videns vivo sepeliri viscera busto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0063","text":"at quos effugium servarat corpore adeso,\n\n \n\nposterius tremulas super ulcera tetra tenentes\n\n \n\npalmas horriferis accibant vocibus Orcum,\n\n \n\ndonique eos vita privarant vermina saeva\n\n \n\nexpertis opis, ignaros quid volnera vellent.\n\n \n\nat non multa virum sub signis milia ducta\n\n \n\nuna dies dabat exitio nec turbida ponti\n\n \n\naequora lidebant navis ad saxa virosque.\n\n \n\nnam temere in cassum frustra mare saepe coortum\n\n \n\nsaevibat leviterque minas ponebat inanis,\n\n \n\nnec poterat quemquam placidi pellacia ponti\n\n \n\nsubdola pellicere in fraudem ridentibus undis.\n\n \n\nimproba navigii ratio tum caeca iacebat.\n\n \n\ntum penuria deinde cibi languentia leto\n\n \n\nmembra dabat, contra nunc rerum copia mersat.\n\n \n\nilli inprudentes ipsi sibi saepe venenum\n\n \n\nvergebant, nunc dant  sollertius ipsi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064","text":"Inde casas postquam ac pellis ignemque pararunt\n\n \n\net mulier coniuncta viro concessit in unum\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\ncognita sunt, prolemque ex se videre creatam,\n\n \n\ntum genus humanum primum mollescere coepit.\n\n \n\nignis enim curavit, ut alsia corpora frigus\n\n \n\nnon ita iam possent caeli sub tegmine ferre,\n\n \n\net Venus inminuit viris puerique parentum\n\n \n\nblanditiis facile ingenium fregere superbum.\n\n \n\ntunc et amicitiem coeperunt iungere aventes\n\n \n\nfinitimi inter se nec laedere nec violari,\n\n \n\net pueros commendarunt muliebreque saeclum,\n\n \n\nvocibus et gestu cum balbe significarent\n\n \n\nimbecillorum esse aequum misererier omnis.\n\n \n\nnec tamen omnimodis poterat concordia gigni,\n\n \n\nsed bona magnaque pars servabat foedera caste;\n\n \n\naut genus humanum iam tum foret omne peremptum\n\n \n\nnec potuisset adhuc perducere saecla propago.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","text":"At varios linguae sonitus natura subegit\n\n \n\nmittere et utilitas expressit nomina rerum,\n\n \n\nnon alia longe ratione atque ipsa videtur\n\n \n\nprotrahere ad gestum pueros infantia linguae,\n\n \n\ncum facit ut digito quae sint praesentia monstrent.\n\n \n\nsentit enim vim quisque suam quod possit abuti.\n\n \n\ncornua nata prius vitulo quam frontibus extent,\n\n \n\nillis iratus petit atque infestus inurget.\n\n \n\nat catuli pantherarum scymnique leonum\n\n \n\nunguibus ac pedibus iam tum morsuque repugnant,\n\n \n\nvix etiam cum sunt dentes unguesque creati.\n\n \n\nalituum porro genus alis omne videmus\n\n \n\nfidere et a pennis tremulum petere auxiliatum.\n\n \n\nproinde putare aliquem tum nomina distribuisse\n\n \n\nrebus et inde homines didicisse vocabula prima,\n\n \n\n desiperest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0066","text":"nam cur hic posset cuncta notare\n\n \n\nvocibus et varios sonitus emittere linguae,\n\n \n\ntempore eodem alii facere id non quisse putentur?\n\n \n\npraeterea si non alii quoque vocibus usi\n\n \n\ninter se fuerant, unde insita notities est\n\n \n\nutilitatis et unde data est huic prima potestas,\n\n \n\nquid vellet facere ut sciret animoque videret?\n\n \n\ncogere item pluris unus victosque domare\n\n \n\nnon poterat, rerum ut perdiscere nomina vellent.\n\n \n\nnec ratione docere ulla suadereque surdis,\n\n \n\nquid sit opus facto, facilest; neque enim paterentur\n\n \n\nnec ratione ulla sibi ferrent amplius auris\n\n \n\nvocis inauditos sonitus obtundere frustra.\n\n \n\npostremo quid in hac mirabile tantoperest re,\n\n \n\nsi genus humanum, cui vox et lingua vigeret,\n\n \n\npro vario sensu varia res voce notaret?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0067","text":"cum pecudes mutae, cum denique saecla ferarum\n\n \n\ndissimilis soleant voces variasque ciere,\n\n \n\ncum metus aut dolor est et cum iam gaudia gliscunt.\n\n \n\nquippe enim licet id rebus cognoscere apertis.\n\n \n\ninritata canum cum primum magna Molossum\n\n \n\nmollia ricta fremunt duros nudantia dentes,\n\n \n\nlonge alio sonitu rabies stricta minatur,\n\n \n\net cum iam latrant et vocibus omnia complent;\n\n \n\nat catulos blande cum lingua lambere temptant\n\n \n\naut ubi eos lactant, pedibus morsuque potentes\n\n \n\nsuspensis teneros imitantur dentibus haustus,\n\n \n\nlonge alio pacto gannitu vocis adulant,\n\n \n\net cum deserti baubantur in aedibus, aut cum\n\n \n\nplorantis fugiunt summisso corpore plagas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0068","text":"denique non hinnitus item differre videtur,\n\n \n\ninter equas ubi equus florenti aetate iuvencus\n\n \n\npinnigeri saevit calcaribus ictus Amoris\n\n \n\net fremitum patulis sub naribus edit ad arma,\n\n \n\net cum sic alias concussis artibus hinnit?\n\n \n\npostremo genus alituum variaeque volucres,\n\n \n\naccipitres atque ossifragae mergique marinis\n\n \n\nfluctibus in salso victum vitamque petentes,\n\n \n\nlonge alias alio iaciunt in tempore voces,\n\n \n\net quom de victu certant praedaque repugnant.\n\n \n\net partim mutant cum tempestatibus una\n\n \n\nraucisonos cantus, cornicum ut saecla vetusta\n\n \n\ncorvorumque gregis ubi aquam dicuntur et imbris\n\n \n\nposcere et inter dum ventos aurasque vocare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0069","text":"ergo si varii sensus animalia cogunt,\n\n \n\nmuta tamen cum sint, varias emittere voces,\n\n \n\nquanto mortalis magis aequumst tum potuisse\n\n \n\ndissimilis alia atque alia res voce notare!\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus tacitus ne forte requiras,\n\n \n\nfulmen detulit in terram mortalibus ignem\n\n \n\nprimitus, inde omnis flammarum diditur ardor;\n\n \n\nmulta videmus enim caelestibus insita flammis\n\n \n\nfulgere, cum caeli donavit plaga vaporis.\n\n \n\net ramosa tamen cum ventis pulsa vacillans\n\n \n\naestuat in ramos incumbens arboris arbor,\n\n \n\n exprimitur validis extritus viribus ignis,\n\n \n\nemicat inter dum flammai fervidus ardor,\n\n \n\nmutua dum inter se rami stirpesque teruntur.\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque dedisse potest mortalibus ignem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","text":"inde cibum quoquere ac flammae mollire vapore\n\n \n\nsol docuit, quoniam mitescere multa videbant\n\n \n\nverberibus radiorum atque aestu victa per agros.\n\n  \n\nInque dies magis hi victum vitamque priorem\n\n \n\ncommutare novis monstrabant rebus et igni,\n\n \n\ningenio qui praestabant et corde vigebant.\n\n \n\ncondere coeperunt urbis arcemque locare\n\n \n\npraesidium reges ipsi sibi perfugiumque,\n\n \n\net pecudes et agros divisere atque dedere\n\n \n\npro facie cuiusque et viribus ingenioque;\n\n \n\nnam facies multum valuit viresque vigebant.\n\n \n\nposterius res inventast aurumque repertum,\n\n \n\nquod facile et validis et pulchris dempsit honorem;\n\n \n\ndivitioris enim sectam plerumque secuntur\n\n \n\nquam lubet et fortes et pulchro corpore creti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0071","text":"quod siquis vera vitam ratione gubernet,\n\n \n\ndivitiae grandes homini sunt vivere parce\n\n \n\naequo animo; neque enim est umquam penuria parvi.\n\n \n\nat claros homines voluerunt se atque potentes,\n\n \n\nut fundamento stabili fortuna maneret\n\n \n\net placidam possent opulenti degere vitam,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam ad summum succedere honorem\n\n \n\ncertantes iter infestum fecere viai,\n\n \n\net tamen e summo, quasi fulmen, deicit ictos\n\n \n\ninvidia inter dum contemptim in Tartara taetra;\n\n \n\ninvidia quoniam ceu fulmine summa vaporant\n\n \n\nplerumque et quae sunt aliis magis edita cumque;\n\n \n\nut satius multo iam sit parere quietum\n\n \n\nquam regere imperio res velle et regna tenere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0072","text":"proinde sine in cassum defessi sanguine sudent,\n\n \n\nangustum per iter luctantes ambitionis;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem sapiunt alieno ex ore petuntque\n\n \n\nres ex auditis potius quam sensibus ipsis,\n\n \n\nnec magis id nunc est neque erit mox quam fuit ante.\n\n  \n\nErgo regibus occisis subversa iacebat\n\n \n\npristina maiestas soliorum et sceptra superba,\n\n \n\net capitis summi praeclarum insigne cruentum\n\n \n\nsub pedibus vulgi magnum lugebat honorem;\n\n \n\nnam cupide conculcatur nimis ante metutum.\n\n \n\nres itaque ad summam faecem turbasque redibat,\n\n \n\nimperium sibi cum ac summatum quisque petebat.\n\n \n\ninde magistratum partim docuere creare\n\n \n\niuraque constituere, ut vellent legibus uti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","text":"nam genus humanum, defessum vi colere aevom,\n\n \n\nex inimicitiis languebat; quo magis ipsum\n\n \n\nsponte sua cecidit sub leges artaque iura.\n\n \n\nacrius ex ira quod enim se quisque parabat\n\n \n\nulcisci quam nunc concessumst legibus aequis,\n\n \n\nhanc ob rem est homines pertaesum vi colere aevom.\n\n \n\ninde metus maculat poenarum praemia vitae.\n\n \n\ncircumretit enim vis atque iniuria quemque\n\n \n\n atque unde exortast, ad eum plerumque revertit,\n\n \n\nnec facilest placidam ac pacatam degere vitam\n\n \n\nqui violat factis communia foedera pacis.\n\n \n\netsi fallit enim divom genus humanumque,\n\n \n\nperpetuo tamen id fore clam diffidere debet;\n\n \n\nquippe ubi se multi per somnia saepe loquentes\n\n \n\naut morbo delirantes protraxe ferantur\n\n \n\net celata  in medium et peccata dedisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","text":"Nunc quae causa deum per magnas numina gentis\n\n \n\npervulgarit et ararum compleverit urbis\n\n \n\nsuscipiendaque curarit sollemnia sacra,\n\n \n\nquae nunc in magnis florent sacra rebus locisque,\n\n \n\nunde etiam nunc est mortalibus insitus horror,\n\n \n\nqui delubra deum nova toto suscitat orbi\n\n \n\nterrarum et festis cogit celebrare diebus,\n\n \n\nnon ita difficilest rationem reddere verbis.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim iam tum divom mortalia saecla\n\n \n\negregias animo facies vigilante videbant\n\n \n\net magis in somnis mirando corporis auctu.\n\n \n\nhis igitur sensum tribuebant propterea quod\n\n \n\nmembra movere videbantur vocesque superbas\n\n \n\nmittere pro facie praeclara et viribus amplis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","text":"aeternamque dabant vitam, quia semper eorum\n\n \n\nsubpeditabatur facies et forma manebat,\n\n \n\net tamen omnino quod tantis viribus auctos\n\n \n\nnon temere ulla vi convinci posse putabant.\n\n \n\nfortunisque ideo longe praestare putabant,\n\n \n\nquod mortis timor haut quemquam vexaret eorum,\n\n \n\net simul in somnis quia multa et mira videbant\n\n \n\nefficere et nullum capere ipsos inde laborem.\n\n \n\npraeterea caeli rationes ordine certo\n\n \n\net varia annorum cernebant tempora verti\n\n \n\nnec poterant quibus id fieret cognoscere causis.\n\n \n\nergo perfugium sibi habebant omnia divis\n\n \n\ntradere et illorum nutu facere omnia flecti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","text":"in caeloque deum sedes et templa locarunt,\n\n \n\nper caelum volvi quia nox et luna videtur,\n\n \n\nluna dies et nox et noctis signa severa\n\n \n\nnoctivagaeque faces caeli flammaeque volantes,\n\n \n\nnubila sol imbres nix venti fulmina grando\n\n \n\net rapidi fremitus et murmura magna minarum.\n\n  \n\nO genus infelix humanum, talia divis\n\n \n\ncum tribuit facta atque iras adiunxit acerbas!\n\n \n\nquantos tum gemitus ipsi sibi, quantaque nobis\n\n \n\nvolnera, quas lacrimas peperere minoribus nostris!\n\n \n\nnec pietas ullast velatum saepe videri\n\n \n\nvertier ad lapidem atque omnis accedere ad aras\n\n \n\nnec procumbere humi prostratum et pandere palmas\n\n \n\nante deum delubra nec aras sanguine multo\n\n \n\nspargere quadrupedum nec votis nectere vota,\n\n \n\nsed mage pacata posse omnia mente tueri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0077","text":"nam cum suspicimus magni caelestia mundi\n\n \n\ntempla super stellisque micantibus aethera fixum,\n\n \n\net venit in mentem solis lunaeque viarum,\n\n \n\ntunc aliis oppressa malis in pectora cura\n\n \n\n illa quoque expergefactum caput erigere infit,\n\n \n\nne quae forte deum nobis inmensa potestas\n\n \n\nsit, vario motu quae candida sidera verset;\n\n \n\ntemptat enim dubiam mentem rationis egestas,\n\n \n\necquae nam fuerit mundi genitalis origo,\n\n \n\net simul ecquae sit finis, quoad moenia mundi\n\n \n\net taciti motus hunc possint ferre laborem,\n\n \n\nan divinitus aeterna donata salute\n\n \n\nperpetuo possint aevi labentia tractu\n\n \n\ninmensi validas aevi contemnere viris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0078","text":"praeterea cui non animus formidine divum\n\n \n\ncontrahitur, cui non correpunt membra pavore,\n\n \n\nfulminis horribili cum plaga torrida tellus\n\n \n\ncontremit et magnum percurrunt murmura caelum?\n\n \n\nnon populi gentesque tremunt, regesque superbi\n\n \n\ncorripiunt divum percussi membra timore,\n\n \n\nne quod ob admissum foede dictumve superbe\n\n \n\npoenarum grave sit solvendi tempus adauctum?\n\n \n\nsumma etiam cum vis violenti per mare venti\n\n \n\ninduperatorem classis super aequora verrit\n\n \n\ncum validis pariter legionibus atque elephantis,\n\n \n\nnon divom pacem votis adit ac prece quaesit\n\n \n\nventorum pavidus paces animasque secundas?\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam violento turbine saepe\n\n \n\ncorreptus nihilo fertur minus ad vada leti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0079","text":"usque adeo res humanas vis abdita quaedam\n\n \n\nopterit et pulchros fascis saevasque secures\n\n \n\nproculcare ac ludibrio sibi habere videtur.\n\n \n\ndenique sub pedibus tellus cum tota vacillat\n\n \n\nconcussaeque cadunt urbes dubiaeque minantur,\n\n \n\nquid mirum si se temnunt mortalia saecla\n\n \n\natque potestatis magnas mirasque relinquunt\n\n \n\nin rebus viris divum, quae cuncta gubernent?\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, aeque aurum ferrumque repertumst\n\n \n\net simul argenti pondus plumbique potestas,\n\n \n\nignis ubi ingentis silvas ardore cremarat\n\n \n\nmontibus in magnis, seu caelo fulmine misso,\n\n \n\nsive quod inter se bellum silvestre gerentes\n\n \n\nhostibus intulerant ignem formidinis ergo,\n\n \n\nsive quod inducti terrae bonitate volebant\n\n \n\npandere agros pinguis et pascua reddere rura,\n\n \n\nsive feras interficere et ditescere praeda;\n\n \n\nnam fovea atque igni prius est venarier ortum\n\n \n\nquam saepire plagis saltum canibusque ciere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0080","text":"quicquid id est, qua cumque e causa flammeus ardor\n\n \n\nhorribili sonitu silvas exederat altis\n\n \n\na radicibus et terram percoxerat igni,\n\n \n\nmanabat venis ferventibus in loca terrae\n\n \n\nconcava conveniens argenti rivus et auri,\n\n \n\naeris item et plumbi. quae cum concreta videbant\n\n \n\nposterius claro in terra splendere colore,\n\n \n\ntollebant nitido capti levique lepore,\n\n \n\net simili formata videbant esse figura\n\n \n\natque lacunarum fuerant vestigia cuique.\n\n \n\ntum penetrabat eos posse haec liquefacta calore\n\n \n\n quamlibet in formam et faciem decurrere rerum,\n\n \n\net prorsum quamvis in acuta ac tenvia posse\n\n \n\nmucronum duci fastigia procudendo,\n\n \n\nut sibi tela parent silvasque ut caedere possint\n\n \n\nmateriemque dolare et levia radere tigna\n\n \n\net terebrare etiam ac pertundere perque forare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0081","text":"nec minus argento facere haec auroque parabant\n\n \n\nquam validi primum violentis viribus aeris,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam cedebat victa potestas\n\n \n\nnec poterant pariter durum sufferre laborem.\n\n \n\nnam fuit in pretio magis aes aurumque iacebat\n\n \n\npropter inutilitatem hebeti mucrone retusum;\n\n \n\nnunc iacet aes, aurum in summum successit honorem.\n\n \n\nsic volvenda aetas commutat tempora rerum.\n\n \n\nquod fuit in pretio, fit nullo denique honore;\n\n \n\nporro aliud succedit et  contemptibus exit\n\n \n\ninque dies magis adpetitur floretque repertum\n\n \n\nlaudibus et miro est mortalis inter honore.\n\n  \n\nNunc tibi quo pacto ferri natura reperta\n\n \n\nsit facilest ipsi per te cognoscere, Memmi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0082","text":"arma antiqua manus ungues dentesque fuerunt\n\n \n\net lapides et item silvarum fragmina rami\n\n \n\net flamma atque ignes, post quam sunt cognita primum.\n\n \n\nposterius ferri vis est aerisque reperta.\n\n \n\net prior aeris erat quam ferri cognitus usus,\n\n \n\nquo facilis magis est natura et copia maior.\n\n \n\naere solum terrae tractabant, aereque belli\n\n \n\nmiscebant fluctus et vulnera vasta serebant\n\n \n\net pecus atque agros adimebant; nam facile ollis\n\n \n\nomnia cedebant armatis nuda et inerma.\n\n \n\ninde minutatim processit ferreus ensis\n\n \n\nversaque in obprobrium species est falcis ahenae,\n\n \n\net ferro coepere solum proscindere terrae\n\n \n\nexaequataque sunt creperi certamina belli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0083","text":"et prius est armatum in equi conscendere costas\n\n \n\net moderarier hunc frenis dextraque vigere\n\n \n\nquam biiugo curru belli temptare pericla.\n\n \n\net biiugo prius est quam bis coniungere binos\n\n \n\net quam falciferos armatum escendere currus.\n\n \n\ninde boves Lucas turrito corpore, tetras,\n\n \n\nanguimanus, belli docuerunt volnera Poeni\n\n \n\nsufferre et magnas Martis turbare catervas.\n\n \n\nsic alid ex alio peperit discordia tristis,\n\n \n\nhorribile humanis quod gentibus esset in armis,\n\n \n\ninque dies belli terroribus addidit augmen.\n\n  \n\nTemptarunt etiam tauros in moenere belli\n\n \n\nexpertique sues saevos sunt mittere in hostis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0084","text":"et validos partim prae se misere leones\n\n \n\ncum doctoribus armatis saevisque magistris,\n\n \n\nqui moderarier his possent vinclisque tenere,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam permixta caede calentes\n\n \n\nturbabant saevi nullo discrimine turmas,\n\n \n\nterrificas capitum quatientis undique cristas,\n\n \n\nnec poterant equites fremitu perterrita equorum\n\n \n\npectora mulcere et frenis convertere in hostis.\n\n \n\n inritata leae iaciebant corpora saltu\n\n \n\nundique et adversum venientibus ora patebant\n\n \n\net nec opinantis a tergo deripiebant\n\n \n\ndeplexaeque dabant in terram volnere victos,\n\n \n\nmorsibus adfixae validis atque unguibus uncis.\n\n \n\niactabantque suos tauri pedibusque terebant\n\n \n\net latera ac ventres hauribant supter equorum\n\n \n\ncornibus et terram minitanti mente ruebant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0085","text":"et validis socios caedebant dentibus apri\n\n \n\ntela infracta suo tinguentes sanguine saevi\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\npermixtasque dabant equitum peditumque ruinas.\n\n \n\nnam transversa feros exibant dentis adactus\n\n \n\niumenta aut pedibus ventos erecta petebant,\n\n \n\nne quiquam, quoniam ab nervis succisa videres\n\n \n\nconcidere atque gravi terram consternere casu.\n\n \n\nsi quos ante domi domitos satis esse putabant,\n\n \n\neffervescere cernebant in rebus agundis\n\n \n\nvolneribus clamore fuga terrore tumultu,\n\n \n\nnec poterant ullam partem redducere eorum;\n\n \n\ndiffugiebat enim varium genus omne ferarum,\n\n \n\nut nunc saepe boves Lucae ferro male mactae\n\n \n\ndiffugiunt, fera facta suis cum multa dedere.\n\n \n\nSed facere id non tam vincendi spe voluerunt;\n\n \n\nquam dare quod gemerent hostes, ipsique perire,\n\n \n\nqui numero diffidebant armisque vacabant,\n\n \n\nsi fuit ut facerent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0086","text":"sed vix adducor ut ante\n\n \n\nnon quierint animo praesentire atque videre,\n\n \n\nquam commune malum fieret foedumque, futurum.\n\n \n\net magis id possis factum contendere in omni\n\n \n\nin variis mundis varia ratione creatis,\n\n \n\nquam certo atque uno terrarum quolibet orbi.\n\n  \n\nNexilis ante fuit vestis quam textile tegmen.\n\n \n\ntextile post ferrumst, quia ferro tela paratur,\n\n \n\nnec ratione alia possunt tam levia gigni\n\n \n\ninsilia ac fusi, radii, scapique sonantes.\n\n \n\net facere ante viros lanam natura coëgit\n\n \n\nquam muliebre genus; nam longe praestat in arte\n\n \n\net sollertius est multo genus omne virile;\n\n \n\nagricolae donec vitio vertere severi,\n\n \n\nut muliebribus id manibus concedere vellent\n\n \n\natque ipsi pariter durum sufferre laborem\n\n \n\natque opere in duro durarent membra manusque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0087","text":"At specimen sationis et insitionis origo\n\n \n\nipsa fuit rerum primum natura creatrix,\n\n \n\narboribus quoniam bacae glandesque caducae\n\n \n\ntempestiva dabant pullorum examina supter;\n\n \n\nunde etiam libitumst stirpis committere ramis\n\n \n\net nova defodere in terram virgulta per agros.\n\n \n\ninde aliam atque aliam culturam dulcis agelli\n\n \n\ntemptabant fructusque feros mansuescere terra\n\n \n\ncernebant indulgendo blandeque colendo.\n\n \n\ninque dies magis in montem succedere silvas\n\n \n\ncogebant infraque locum concedere cultis,\n\n \n\nprata lacus rivos segetes vinetaque laeta\n\n \n\n collibus et campis ut haberent, atque olearum\n\n \n\ncaerula distinguens inter plaga currere posset\n\n \n\nper tumulos et convallis camposque profusa;\n\n \n\nut nunc esse vides vario distincta lepore\n\n \n\nomnia, quae pomis intersita dulcibus ornant\n\n \n\narbustisque tenent felicibus opsita circum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0088","text":"At liquidas avium voces imitarier ore\n\n \n\nante fuit multo quam levia carmina cantu\n\n \n\nconcelebrare homines possent aurisque iuvare.\n\n \n\net zephyri cava per calamorum sibila primum\n\n \n\nagrestis docuere cavas inflare cicutas.\n\n \n\ninde minutatim dulcis didicere querellas,\n\n \n\ntibia quas fundit digitis pulsata canentum,\n\n \n\navia per nemora ac silvas saltusque reperta,\n\n \n\nper loca pastorum deserta atque otia dia.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nhaec animos ollis mulcebant atque iuvabant\n\n \n\ncum satiate cibi; nam tum sunt omnia cordi.\n\n \n\nsaepe itaque inter se prostrati in gramine molli\n\n \n\npropter aquae rivom sub ramis arboris altae.\n\n \n\nnon magnis opibus iucunde corpora habebant,\n\n \n\npraesertim cum tempestas ridebat et anni\n\n \n\ntempora pingebant viridantis floribus herbas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0089","text":"tum ioca, tum sermo, tum dulces esse cachinni\n\n \n\nconsuerant; agrestis enim tum musa vigebat.\n\n \n\ntum caput atque umeros plexis redimire coronis\n\n \n\nfloribus et foliis lascivia laeta movebat,\n\n \n\natque extra numerum procedere membra moventes\n\n \n\nduriter et duro terram pede pellere matrem;\n\n \n\nunde oriebantur risus dulcesque cachinni,\n\n \n\nomnia quod nova tum magis haec et mira vigebant.\n\n \n\net vigilantibus hinc aderant solacia somno\n\n \n\nducere multimodis voces et flectere cantus\n\n \n\net supera calamos unco percurrere labro;\n\n \n\nunde etiam vigiles nunc haec accepta tuentur.\n\n \n\net numerum servare genus didicere, neque hilo\n\n \n\nmaiore interea capiunt dulcedine fructum\n\n \n\nquam silvestre genus capiebat terrigenarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090","text":"nam quod adest praesto, nisi quid cognovimus ante\n\n \n\nsuavius, in primis placet et pollere videtur,\n\n \n\nposteriorque fere melior res illa reperta\n\n \n\nperdit et immutat sensus ad pristina quaeque.\n\n  \n\nsic odium coepit glandis, sic illa relicta\n\n \n\nstrata cubilia sunt herbis et frondibus aucta.\n\n \n\npellis item cecidit vestis contempta ferina;\n\n \n\nquam reor invidia tali tunc esse repertam,\n\n \n\nut letum insidiis qui gessit primus obiret,\n\n \n\net tamen inter eos distractam sanguine multo\n\n \n\ndisperiise neque in fructum convertere quisse.\n\n \n\ntunc igitur pelles, nunc aurum et purpura curis\n\n \n\nexercent hominum vitam belloque fatigant;\n\n \n\nquo magis in nobis, ut opinor, culpa resedit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","text":"frigus enim nudos sine pellibus excruciabat\n\n \n\nterrigenas; at nos nil laedit veste carere\n\n \n\npurpurea atque auro signisque ingentibus apta,\n\n \n\ndum plebeia tamen sit, quae defendere possit.\n\n \n\nErgo hominum genus in cassum frustraque laborat\n\n \n\nsemper et  curis consumit inanibus aevom,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia non cognovit quae sit habendi\n\n \n\nfinis et omnino quoad crescat vera voluptas;\n\n \n\nidque minutatim vitam provexit in altum\n\n \n\net belli magnos commovit funditus aestus.\n\n  \n\nat vigiles mundi magnum versatile templum\n\n \n\nsol et luna suo lustrantes lumine circum\n\n \n\nperdocuere homines annorum tempora verti\n\n \n\net certa ratione geri rem atque ordine certo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","text":"Iam validis saepti degebant turribus aevom,\n\n \n\net divisa colebatur discretaque tellus,\n\n \n\ntum mare velivolis florebat navibus ponti,\n\n \n\nauxilia ac socios iam pacto foedere habebant,\n\n \n\ncarminibus cum res gestas coepere poëtae\n\n \n\ntradere; nec multo prius sunt elementa reperta.\n\n \n\npropterea quid sit prius actum respicere aetas\n\n \n\nnostra nequit, nisi qua ratio vestigia monstrat.\n\n \n\nNavigia atque agri culturas moenia leges\n\n \n\narma vias vestes  cetera de genere horum,\n\n \n\npraemia, delicias quoque vitae funditus omnis,\n\n \n\ncarmina, picturas et daedala signa polita\n\n \n\nusus et impigrae simul experientia mentis\n\n \n\npaulatim docuit pedetemptim progredientis.\n\n \n\nsic unum quicquid paulatim protrahit aetas\n\n \n\nin medium ratioque in luminis erigit oras;\n\n \n\nnamque alid ex alio clarescere corde videbant,\n\n \n\nartibus ad summum donec venere cacumen.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"42029b6c05fce66b81015523757e0e8511146266f63bb3c3e89c687668904bc0","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T235937846680Z_7f762176","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book Five","source_sha256":"42029b6c05fce66b81015523757e0e8511146266f63bb3c3e89c687668904bc0","sha256":"ed47f51bd45ee70e0d6f89714964660ac6de89e97ec1337933cfd400083b3312","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:59:37.846706+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001","text":"## Book Five\n\nWho has the power to fashion a poem worthy of the majesty of things\n\nwith a mighty breast, and of these discoveries?\n\nOr who is so strong in words that he could shape praises\n\nin proportion to the merits of him who has left us\n\nsuch prizes, won and sought out by his own breast?\n\nNo one, I think, will be born in a mortal body.\n\nFor if, as the known majesty of things itself demands,\n\nit must be said, that man was a god, a god, illustrious Memmius,\n\nwho first discovered that rule of life which\n\nis now called wisdom, and who by his art\n\nset life, amid such great waves and such great darkness,\n\nin so tranquil and so clear a light.\n\nFor compare the ancient divine discoveries of others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0002","text":"For Ceres is said to have established grain, and Liber the liquid\n\nmoisture of the vine, for mortals;\n\nthough life could remain without these things,\n\nas report has it that even now certain nations live.\n\nYet one could not live well without a pure breast;\n\nwherefore this man seems to us more deservedly a god,\n\nsince even now, disseminated through great nations,\n\nthe sweet consolations of life soothe men's minds.\n\nBut if you should think that the deeds of Hercules surpass these,\n\nyou will be carried much farther from true reason.\n\nFor what harm to us now would that great gaping maw\n\nof the Nemean lion do, or the bristling Arcadian boar,\n\nor to what great extent would the Stymphalian dwellers obstruct us?\n\nFinally, what could the bull of Crete and the Lernaean pest,\n\nthe hydra fenced about with venomous serpents, do?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0003","text":"Or what the three-breasted force of triple Geryon,\n\nor the horses of Diomedes, breathing fire from their nostrils,\n\nbeside Thrace and the Bistonian regions and Ismara;\n\nor the fierce serpent, bitterly watching over\n\nthe gleaming golden apples of the Hesperides,\n\nwith its huge body embracing the roots of the tree? What, finally, would harm us\n\nby the Atlantic shore and the harsh places of the sea,\n\nwhere neither any one of our people goes nor any barbarian dares?\n\nThe other marvels of this kind which have been slain—\n\nif they had not been conquered, what harm, pray, would they do while alive?\n\nNone, I think: so even now the earth teems to satiety\n\nwith wild beasts and is filled with trembling terror\n\nthrough groves and great mountains and deep woods;\n\nplaces which for the most part it is in our power to avoid.\n\nBut unless the breast is cleansed, what battles\n\nand dangers must then make their way into us against our will!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","text":"How great, then, are the sharp desires that rend the anxious man\n\nwith care, and likewise how great the fears!\n\nAnd what of pride, foulness, and wantonness? How great\n\nthe disasters they bring about! What of luxury and sloth?\n\nTherefore, he who has subdued all these things and driven them\n\nfrom the mind by words, not by arms—will it not be fitting\n\nthat this man be deemed worthy of the number of the gods?\n\nEspecially since he was accustomed to give many things well and divinely\n\nin speech to mortals themselves, as if from the gods,\n\nand to disclose in speech the whole nature of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","text":"While I follow in his footsteps, pursuing his reasonings,\n\nand teach in words by what covenant each thing created is bound,\n\nhow it is necessary for it to endure therein\n\nand how it cannot undo the strong laws of age;\n\nand in what fashion especially the nature of the mind has been found\n\nfirst to subsist, created in a native body,\n\nand not to be able to endure unharmed through a great age,\n\nbut how images are wont in dreams to deceive the mind\n\nwhen we seem to see one whom life has left:\n\nfor what remains, the order of reasoning has now brought me here,\n\nso that I must set forth that the world consists in a mortal body\n\nand likewise is born;\n\nand by what modes that concourse of matter\n\nhas founded earth, heaven, sea, stars, the sun,\n\nand the globe of the moon; then what living creatures\n\nhave arisen from the earth, and which have at no time been born;\n\nand in what way the human race, with speech varying,\n\nbegan among themselves to make use of the names of things;\n\nand by what modes that fear of the gods entered\n\ninto hearts—the fear which throughout the world preserves sacred\n\nshrines, pools, groves, altars, and images of the gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","text":"Moreover, I shall explain the courses of the sun and the paths of the moon,\n\nby what force governing nature turns them;\n\nlest perhaps we should think that between heaven and earth\n\nthey traverse their everlasting courses by free will,\n\nobedient to the increase of crops and living creatures,\n\nor suppose that they are rolled on by some plan of the gods.\n\nFor those who have well learned that the gods pass a life free from care,\n\nif nevertheless meanwhile they wonder by what reasoning\n\neach thing can come about, especially in those matters\n\nwhich are seen above their heads in the ethereal regions,\n\nthey are carried back again to the old religions\n\nand take on harsh masters, whom in their misery they believe\n\nto be able to do all things, ignorant of what can be,\n\nwhat cannot be, and, finally, by what reasoning a bounded power\n\nis assigned to each thing and a limit deeply fixed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0007","text":"For what remains, lest I delay you longer in promises,\n\nfirst look upon the seas, lands, and heaven;\n\nwhose threefold nature, three bodies, Memmius,\n\nthree forms so unlike, three such textures,\n\none day will give over to destruction, and the mass and fabric of the world,\n\nsustained for many years, will fall.\n\nNor does it escape my mind how novel and marvelous to the mind\n\nthe coming destruction of heaven and earth may seem,\n\nand how difficult it is for me to prevail upon this in words;\n\nas happens when you bring before the ears something unfamiliar,\n\nand yet cannot set it beneath the sight of the eyes\n\nor place it into the hands—the way by which conviction,\n\nmost fortified, passes nearest into the human breast and the temples of the mind.\n\nYet I shall speak.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0008","text":"The thing itself perhaps will give credence to my words, and, when great\n\nearthquakes arise, you will see all things shaken to pieces in a short time.\n\nMay governing fortune turn this far away from us,\n\nand may reason rather than the thing itself persuade us\n\nthat all things, overcome, can collapse with a horrifying crash.\n\nBefore I begin to utter pronouncements on this matter,\n\nholier and by much more certain reasoning than those\n\nwhich the Pythia speaks from the tripod and Phoebus' laurel,\n\nI shall unfold for you many consolations in learned words;\n\nlest, restrained by religion, you should perhaps think\n\nthat earth and sun and heaven, sea, stars, and moon,\n\nbeing of divine body, ought to remain eternal,\n\nand therefore judge it right that all those should pay penalties\n\nafter the manner of the Giants, for monstrous crime,\n\nwho by their reasoning disturb the walls of the world\n\nand would extinguish the splendid sun of heaven,\n\ncalling immortal things mortal in speech;\n\nthough these things are so far from the divine nature\n\nand so unworthy of being seen in the number of gods,\n\nthat they may rather be thought to furnish an indication\n\nof what is removed from vital motion and sensation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009","text":"For indeed it is not the case that the nature and counsel of mind\n\ncan be thought able to exist with just any body.\n\nJust as in the aether there can be no tree, nor clouds in the salt sea,\n\nnor fish live in fields,\n\nnor blood in wood, nor moisture be present in stones—\n\nit is fixed and ordered where each thing may grow and abide—\n\nso the nature of mind cannot arise without a body\n\nby itself, nor exist farther apart from nerves and blood.\n\nFor if it could, the force of mind itself could much sooner\n\nbe in the head or shoulders or the lowest heels,\n\nand would be wont to be born in any part whatever,\n\nand finally remain in the same man and in the same vessel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010","text":"Since it is also established as fixed and ordered in our body\n\nwhere soul and mind can separately be and grow,\n\nall the more must it be denied that they can endure wholly outside\n\nthe body and living form,\n\nin the decaying clods of earth, or in the fire of the sun,\n\nor in water, or in the lofty regions of aether.\n\nTherefore they do not consist endowed with divine sensation,\n\nsince they cannot be animated in a vital manner.\n\nLikewise, you cannot believe that the holy seats\n\nof the gods are in any parts of the world.\n\nFor the nature of the gods is subtle and far removed\n\nfrom our senses, scarcely seen by the mind's intelligence;\n\nand since it escapes the touch and blow of hands,\n\nit ought not to touch anything that is tangible to us;\n\nfor that which itself cannot be touched cannot touch.\n\n","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","text":"Wherefore their seats also must be unlike our seats,\n\nsubtle in accordance with their body;\n\nthis I shall prove to you later by ample discourse.\n\nMoreover, to say that they wished to prepare the splendid\n\nnature of the world for the sake of men, and therefore that it is fitting\n\nto praise the praiseworthy work of the gods,\n\nand to think it eternal and destined to be immortal,\n\nand that it is not right ever by any force to shake from its seats,\n\nor vex with words and overthrow from its foundation,\n\nthat which has been established by the ancient reasoning of the gods\n\nfor human races throughout everlasting time—\n\nto invent and add other things of this kind, Memmius,\n\nis madness. For what advantage can our gratitude\n\nbestow upon the immortals and blessed,\n\nthat they should undertake to do anything for our sake?\n\nOr what new thing could, after so long a time of previous repose,\n\nhave enticed them to desire to change their former life?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0012","text":"For one to whom old things are harmful seems bound to take pleasure\n\nin new things; but for one to whom nothing painful happened\n\nin the time previously elapsed, while he passed life pleasantly,\n\nwhat could kindle a love of novelty in such a one?\n\nOr what evil would it have been for us not to have been created?\n\nDid life, I suppose, lie in darkness and grief\n\nuntil the generative origin of things dawned?\n\nFor whoever has been born ought to wish to remain\n\nin life, so long as soothing pleasure holds him fast;\n\nbut he who has never tasted the love of life\n\nand has not been in the number, what harm is it not to have been created?\n\nFurther, whence was first implanted in the gods\n\nthe model for creating things and the very notion of men,\n\nso that they should know what they wished to do and see it in their mind?\n\nAnd how was the force of first-beginnings ever known,\n\nand what they could do among themselves by changing their order?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","text":"Unless nature herself gave the appearance of creating?\n\nFor in this way the many first-beginnings of things, driven for infinite time\n\nby many modes through blows\n\nand their own weights, have become accustomed to be borne along in motion\n\nand to come together in every fashion and to try all things,\n\nwhatever they can create by meeting among themselves;\n\nso that it is not surprising if they too fell into such arrangements\n\nand came into such courses,\n\nby which this totality of things is now conducted in renewal.\n\nThough I should not know what the first-beginnings of things are,\n\nyet from the very arrangements of heaven I would dare\n\nto affirm this, and establish it from many other things besides:\n\nthat the nature of things was by no means prepared for us by divine power—\n\nso great is the fault with which it stands endowed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","text":"First, of however much the mighty expanse of heaven covers,\n\nfrom this mountains and woods of wild beasts possess\n\na greedy part; cliffs and vast marshes hold it,\n\nand the sea, which widely sunders the shores of lands.\n\nThen besides, burning heat and the constant onset\n\nof frost take almost two parts away from mortals.\n\nWhat remains of the fields, nevertheless nature by its own force\n\nwould cover with brambles, unless human force resisted,\n\naccustomed for the sake of life to groan over the sturdy mattock\n\nand to cleave the earth with pressed ploughs.\n\nUnless, turning the fruitful clods with the ploughshare\n\nand subduing the soil of earth, we incite them to rise,\n\nthey could not of their own accord emerge into the clear air.\n\nAnd yet sometimes, when won by great labor\n\nthey already put forth leaves through the lands and all things flower,\n\neither the ethereal sun scorches them with excessive heats,\n\nor sudden rains and chilling frosts destroy them,\n\nand blasts of winds harass them with violent whirlwind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","text":"Moreover, why does nature nourish and increase the terrible race of wild beasts,\n\nhostile to the human race by land and sea? Why do\n\nthe seasons of the year bring diseases? Why does untimely death roam abroad?\n\nThen, moreover, a child, like a sailor cast out by cruel waves,\n\nlies naked upon the ground, an infant needing every\n\nvital aid, as soon as nature, with straining, has poured him forth\n\nfrom his mother's womb into the regions of light;\n\nand he fills the place with doleful wailing, as is fitting\n\nfor one to whom so many evils remain to pass through in life.\n\nBut the various herds, flocks, and wild beasts grow up;\n\nthey have no need of rattles, nor must there be applied to any\n\nthe fond and broken speech of a kindly nurse;\n\nnor do they seek varied garments according to the season of heaven;\n\nfinally, there is no need of arms or lofty walls\n\nto protect their own, since the earth itself and the ingenious nature of things\n\nproduce all things for all in abundance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","text":"First, since the body of earth, and moisture,\n\nand the light breaths of air and hot vapors,\n\nfrom which this totality of things seems to consist,\n\nall consist in a born and mortal body,\n\nthe whole nature of the world too must be thought the same.\n\nFor indeed, those things whose parts and members we see\n\nto be of a born body and mortal forms,\n\nwe see to be themselves likewise, for the most part, mortal\n\nand at the same time born. Wherefore, since I see that the greatest\n\nmembers and parts of the world are consumed and born again,\n\nyou may know that heaven and earth likewise had\n\nsome originating time and will have disaster to come.\n\nIn these matters, do not think that I have seized something for myself,\n\nbecause I have assumed that earth and fire are mortal,\n\nnor doubted that moisture and air perish,\n\nand have said that these same things are born and grow again.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0017","text":"First, no small part of earth, scorched\n\nby unceasing suns and beaten by the many feet,\n\nbreathes out a cloud and flying mists of dust,\n\nwhich strong winds scatter through the whole air.\n\nA part of the clods too is recalled to flood\n\nby rains, and rivers grazing their banks gnaw them away.\n\nMoreover, whatever nourishes and increases anything, in its own share,\n\nis given back; and since beyond doubt earth seems to be\n\nthe all-bearing mother and the common tomb of things,\n\nthe earth is therefore diminished for you and, increased, grows again.\n\nFor what remains, that sea, rivers, and springs always abound\n\nwith fresh moisture, and that perennial streams flow,\n\nrequires no words: the great downward flow of waters\n\nfrom every side makes this clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0018","text":"But first, whatever water\n\nis taken away—and thus it comes about that moisture does not abound—\n\nis partly diminished by strong winds sweeping the plains of sea\n\nand by the ethereal sun unweaving it with its rays,\n\npartly it is distributed beneath through all the lands;\n\nfor the liquid is filtered, and the substance of moisture flows back\n\nand all gathers at the head of the rivers; thence it flows above\n\nthe lands in a sweet current, where a channel once cut\n\nhas carried down the waters with liquid foot.\n\nNow therefore I shall speak of air, which in its whole body\n\nis changed countless times in successive hours.\n\nFor whatever flows away from things, all of it\n\nis carried into the great sea of air; unless air in return\n\nrestored bodies to things and renewed their flowing,\n\nall things would by now have been dissolved and turned into air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0019","text":"Therefore it does not cease to be generated from things and to fall back\n\ninto things, since it is established that all things flow continually.\n\nLikewise, the bountiful source of liquid light, the ethereal sun,\n\ncontinually floods heaven with fresh brilliance\n\nand at once supplies light with new light.\n\nFor whatever brightness first perishes from it,\n\nperishes wherever it happens. You may be able to know this from the fact\n\nthat, as soon as clouds first begin to come beneath the sun\n\nand as it were break apart the rays of light between them,\n\nat once all their lower part disappears,\n\nand the earth is shadowed wherever the clouds are borne;\n\nso that you may know that things always need fresh splendor,\n\nand that every first emission of brightness perishes,\n\nand that things cannot be seen in the sun by any other reasoning,\n\nunless the very source of light continually supplies it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0020","text":"nay more, the nightly lights that are upon earth,\n\nthe hanging lamps and the rich pine-torches, bright with flashing\n\nblazes amid much soot, hasten by a like process, with heat as their servant,\n\nto supply fresh light, and persist in trembling with their fires,\n\npersist, nor does the light leave places, as it were, broken off:\n\nso exceedingly swiftly, from all the fires, is its destruction\n\nhastened by the rapid birth of flame.\n\nThus therefore must sun, moon, and stars be thought\n\nto cast forth their light from one fresh rising after another,\n\nand always to lose whatever flames first [arise],\n\nlest you perhaps believe that these flourish inviolate.\n\nFinally, do you not also see stones conquered by time,\n\ndo you not see lofty towers fall and rocks decay,\n\ndo shrines of gods and images, worn out, not crumble away,\n\nnor can the holy divinity of the gods prolong the bounds of fate\n\nor strive against the covenants of nature?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0021","text":"Finally, do we not see memorials of men falling apart,\n\ndo we not see rocks torn away from high mountains fall,\n\nand unable to endure and suffer the mighty powers\n\nof limited time? For they would not suddenly fall away,\n\nif they had borne all the assaults of age\n\nfrom infinite time, exempt from crashing ruin.\n\nFinally, now behold this which all around and above\n\ncontains the earth in its embrace: if it produces from itself\n\nall things, as some tell, and receives them back when destroyed,\n\nthe whole consists of a body born and mortal.\n\nFor whatever else increases and nourishes things from itself\n\nmust be diminished, and replenished when it receives things back.\n\nMoreover, if there was no generative origin\n\nof earth and heaven, and they have always been eternal,\n\nwhy, before the Theban war and the funeral of Troy,\n\ndid not other poets too sing of other deeds?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0022","text":"Why have so many deeds of men fallen so often away, and nowhere\n\ndo they flourish, implanted in eternal memorials of fame?\n\nBut, as I suppose, the whole has novelty, and the nature of the world\n\nis fresh, nor did it take its beginning long ago.\n\nTherefore certain arts too are now being polished,\n\nare now also increasing; many things have now been added\n\nto ships; only lately have musicians produced melodious sounds;\n\nfinally, this nature and system of things has been discovered\n\nlately, and I myself have now been found first among the first\n\nwho can turn it into ancestral speech.\n\nBut if perhaps you believe that all these same things existed before,\n\nbut that generations of men perished in burning heat,\n\nor that cities fell in a great convulsion of the world,\n\nor that from incessant rains devouring rivers\n\nwent forth over the lands and covered towns.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0023","text":"So much the more must you confess, vanquished,\n\nthat destruction too awaits earth and heaven;\n\nfor when things were assailed by such great maladies and such great dangers,\n\nthen, if a more grievous cause had fallen upon them,\n\nthey would have dealt devastation far and wide and mighty ruin.\n\nNor do we seem to be mortal by any other reasoning,\n\nexcept because we too fall ill with the same diseases\n\nas those whom nature has removed from life.\n\nMoreover, whatever things must remain eternal\n\nmust either, because they are of solid body, repel blows\n\nand not allow anything to penetrate them that could sunder\n\ntheir close-packed inner parts, as are the bodies\n\nof matter, whose nature we showed before;\n\nor be able to endure through all time\n\nbecause they are proof against blows, as void is,\n\nwhich remains untouched and is not affected a whit by a blow;\n\nor else because there is no supply of place around them\n\ninto which things might, as it were, withdraw and be dissolved,\n\nas the sum of sums is eternal: nor is there outside it\n\na place into which they may burst apart, nor are there bodies that\n\ncould strike upon it and dissolve it by a mighty blow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","text":"But neither, as I have taught, is the nature of the world\n\nof solid body, since void is mingled in things;\n\nnor yet is it like void, nor are bodies lacking\n\nwhich, having arisen by chance from infinity,\n\nmay overthrow this sum of things in a violent whirlwind\n\nor bring upon it some other ruinous disaster;\n\nnor again does the nature of place and the expanse of the deep\n\nfail, into which the walls of the world may be scattered abroad,\n\nor they may perish, struck by any other force whatsoever.\n\nTherefore the gate of death is not barred against heaven,\n\nnor against sun and earth, nor the high waves of the sea,\n\nbut stands open and looks out with huge and gaping maw.\n\nWherefore you must also confess that these same things are born;\n\nfor things that are of mortal body could not, from infinite\n\ntime until now, have been able to scorn the strong powers\n\nof immeasurable age.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0025","text":"Finally, since the greatest members of the world\n\nstruggle so fiercely among themselves, stirred by no pious war,\n\ndo you not see that some end may be given them\n\nin their long contention—either when the sun and every heat,\n\nhaving drunk up all the moisture, shall prevail?\n\nThis they strive to do, nor yet are their attempts accomplished;\n\nso greatly do the rivers supply [moisture] and threaten besides\n\nto flood all things from the deep gulf of the sea:\n\nbut in vain, since the winds sweeping the waters\n\ndiminish it, and the ethereal sun, unweaving [it] by his rays,\n\ntrusts that he can dry all things sooner\n\nthan the liquid can attain the end of its undertaking.\n\nBreathing out war in equal combat, they strive to contend\n\nconcerning great matters,\n\nalthough once fire has been the stronger,\n\nand once, as the story goes, moisture reigned over the fields.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0026","text":"For fire prevailed and burned up many things round about,\n\nwhen the ravening force of the sun's horses carried Phaethon,\n\ngone astray, through the whole heaven and over every land.\n\nBut then the omnipotent father, stirred by sharp wrath,\n\nstruck down high-hearted Phaethon from his horses to earth\n\nwith the sudden blow of a thunderbolt; and the Sun, meeting him\n\nas he fell, received the eternal lamp of the world,\n\ngathered back the scattered horses and yoked them trembling;\n\nthen, guiding all things, he restored them to their own course—\n\nof course as the ancient poets of the Greeks sang.\n\nThis is far too remote from true reasoning to be accepted.\n\nFor fire can prevail when more bodies of matter\n\nhave arisen from infinity;\n\nthen its powers, overcome by some means, fail,\n\nor things perish, burned up by scorching air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","text":"Likewise moisture once began to prevail when it had arisen,\n\nas the story goes, when it overwhelmed the lives of men with waves;\n\nthen, when its force, which had arisen from infinity,\n\nhad withdrawn and receded by some means,\n\nthe rains came to a standstill and the rivers lessened their force.\n\nBut by what means that gathering of matter\n\nlaid the foundations of earth and heaven and the sea's depths,\n\nand the courses of sun and moon, I shall set forth in order.\n\nFor certainly the first-beginnings of things did not,\n\nby design, each settle themselves in their own order with shrewd mind,\n\nnor indeed did they agree what motions each should give;\n\nbut because many first-beginnings of things, in many ways,\n\nhave from infinite time, driven by blows\n\nand their own weights, been accustomed to be carried in motion\n\nand to come together in every manner and try all things\n\nthat, striking upon one another, they could create,\n\ntherefore it comes about that, spread abroad through great time,\n\nby experiencing every kind of union and motion,\n\nthey at last come together, such that, suddenly cast together,\n\nthey often become the beginnings of great things—\n\nof earth, sea, and heaven, and the race of living things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0028","text":"Here neither then could the wheel of the sun be seen flying high\n\nwith its broad light, nor the stars of the great world,\n\nnor sea nor heaven nor, finally, earth nor air,\n\nnor could any thing be seen resembling our things,\n\nbut a certain new tempest and mass arisen.\n\nThen the parts of things began to disperse from one another, and like things\n\nto join with like, to separate the world,\n\ndivide its members, and dispose its great parts\n\nfrom first-beginnings of every kind—their discord\n\nconfusing intervals, ways, connexions, weights, blows,\n\ncollisions, motions, and battles,\n\nbecause of their unlike forms and varied shapes,\n\nsince not all could thus remain joined together\n\nor give mutually harmonious motions:\n\nthat is, to separate high heaven from the earth,\n\nand the sea apart, so that it might lie open with its moisture set apart,\n\nand likewise apart the fires of pure and separate aether.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0029","text":"For first all the bodies of earth,\n\nbecause they were heavy and entangled, came together\n\nin the middle and all took the lowest places;\n\nand the more entangled they came together among themselves,\n\nthe more they squeezed out those things that would make sea, stars, sun,\n\nand moon, and the walls of the great world.\n\nFor all these are composed of lighter and rounder\n\nseeds, and of elements much smaller,\n\nthan earth. Therefore, bursting out through the porous openings\n\nof earth, aether, bearing fire, first raised itself up\n\nand, being light, bore off with itself many fires—\n\nnot by a principle far different from that by which we often see,\n\nwhen first the radiant lights of the morning sun\n\nredden over grass jeweled with golden dew,\n\nand lakes and perennial rivers exhale mist,\n\nand even the earth itself seems at times to smoke:\n\nwhen all these are gathered upward, they weave beneath them, aloft,\n\na cloudy heaven of condensed body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0030","text":"Thus then light and diffusive aether, condensed in body,\n\nencircled itself round about on every side,\n\nand, spread broadly in every direction on every side,\n\nthus enclosed all the rest in a greedy embrace.\n\nThe beginnings of sun and moon followed it,\n\nbetween either of which their globes revolve in the airs.\n\nNeither earth nor greatest aether appropriated them to itself,\n\nbecause they were neither so heavy as to settle down depressed,\n\nnor so light as to glide along the highest borders;\n\nand yet they are so placed between the two that they turn\n\nas living bodies and stand as parts of the whole world:\n\njust as in us some members may remain at rest,\n\nwhile there are nevertheless others that are moved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0031","text":"When these things had been drawn back, earth suddenly,\n\nwhere now the vast blue expanse of the sea stretches forth,\n\nsank down and flooded the hollows with its salt gulf.\n\nAnd day by day, the more the heat of aether around it\n\nand the sun's rays compelled earth from every side,\n\npacked tight at its outermost borders by frequent blows,\n\nto gather, driven and condensed, into its own middle,\n\nthe more the salty sweat squeezed from its body\n\nincreased the sea by flowing forth, and the swimming fields;\n\nand so much the more did many bodies of vapour and air, slipping forth,\n\nfly upward and, far from earth, condense the shining regions\n\nof high heaven. The plains sank; the ascents of high mountains\n\ngrew; for rocks could not sink down,\n\nnor could all parts yield equally by the same amount.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0032","text":"Thus then earth's weight, its body having condensed,\n\ncame to rest, and all the world's, as it were, slime\n\nflowed together, heavy, to the bottom and settled there like lees;\n\nthen sea, then air, then fire-bearing aether itself,\n\nall were left pure, with liquid bodies,\n\nand some lighter than others; and the most liquid and lightest aether\n\nflows above the airy airs,\n\nnor does it mingle its liquid body with the turbulent airs of air:\n\nit permits these to be turned all about by violent\n\nwhirlwinds, permits them to be disturbed by uncertain storms;\n\nitself, gliding, bears its own fires in a fixed impetus.\n\nFor that aether can flow steadily and with a single striving\n\nis indicated by Pontus, the sea, which flows with fixed tide,\n\npreserving throughout one unbroken tenor of gliding.\n\nNow let us sing what is the cause of the motions of the stars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0033","text":"First, if the great sphere of heaven turns,\n\nwe must say that air presses upon the pole from either side,\n\noutside us, and holds and encloses it on both sides;\n\nthen that another [air] flows above and strains in the same direction\n\nin which the eternal stars of the world shine as they are rolled around;\n\nor another beneath, which bears the sphere upward contrarywise,\n\nas we see rivers turn wheels and south winds.\n\nIt is also possible that the whole heaven may remain\n\nat rest, while nevertheless the bright signs are borne along,\n\neither because swift currents of aether, shut in,\n\nturn about seeking a way, and roll their fires\n\nhere and there through the highest regions of heaven;\n\nor because air, flowing from elsewhere and from some point outside,\n\nturns them, driving the fires; or because they themselves can creep\n\nwhere the food of each calls and invites them as they go,\n\nfeeding their fiery bodies everywhere through heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","text":"For it is difficult to lay down with certainty what is the case among these\n\nin this world; but what can be and comes to pass throughout\n\nvarious worlds created in various ways,\n\nthat I teach, and I proceed to set forth several causes\n\nwhich may account for the motions of the stars throughout [the worlds];\n\none of which, nevertheless, must also be the cause\n\nwhich gives motion to the signs; but to prescribe which of them it is\n\nis by no means the task of one advancing step by step.\n\nAnd in order that earth may rest in the middle region of the world,\n\nits weight must gradually vanish and diminish,\n\nand it must have another nature beneath it, from its beginning,\n\njoined and fitted in unity\n\nto the airy parts of the world, in which it is implanted and lives.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0035","text":"Therefore it is no burden and does not weigh down the airs,\n\njust as a man's limbs are no burden to each man\n\nand the head is no burden to the neck, nor, finally, do we feel\n\nthat the whole weight of the body lies in the feet;\n\nbut whatever weights come from outside and are laid upon us\n\nharm us, often though much smaller.\n\nSo greatly does it matter what each thing can do.\n\nThus earth was not suddenly brought from elsewhere,\n\nan alien thing cast upon alien airs,\n\nbut was conceived equally from the first origin of the world,\n\nand is a fixed part of it, just as our limbs appear [to be].\n\nMoreover, when earth is suddenly shaken by a great thunderclap,\n\nit shakes by its motion all things that are above it;\n\nwhich it could not do by any means unless it were bound fast\n\nto the airy parts of the world and to heaven;\n\nfor they cling together by common roots,\n\njoined and grown in unity from their beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0036","text":"Do you not see also how the exceedingly subtle force of spirit\n\nsupports our body with so great a weight,\n\nbecause it is so joined and fitted in unity [with it]?\n\nFinally, what is able to lift the body in swift leap\n\nexcept the force of spirit, which governs the limbs?\n\nDo you now see how much a subtle nature can avail,\n\nwhen it is joined with a heavy body, as air\n\nis joined to earth and the force of mind is to us?\n\nNor can the sun's wheel be much greater, nor its heat less,\n\nthan it appears to be to our senses.\n\nFor from whatever distances fires can cast their light\n\nand breathe warm heat upon our limbs,\n\nthey lose nothing of the body of their flames through the great intervals;\n\nthe fire is not at all diminished to sight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0037","text":"Accordingly, since the sun's heat and poured-out light\n\nreach our senses and make places shine,\n\nthe sun's form also and size must be seen from here\n\nsuch that you can truly add to it nothing more or less.\n\nAnd whether the moon is borne along, surveying places with borrowed light,\n\nor casts forth light from its own body,\n\nwhatever the case, it is borne along with no larger a figure\n\nthan it appears to have when we see it with our eyes.\n\nFor all things that we view far removed\n\nthrough much air seem confused in appearance\n\nbefore they seem diminished in size. Therefore the moon must,\n\nsince it presents a bright appearance and a definite shape,\n\nbe seen by us from here on high as great as it is,\n\nmarked out as it is in every outline.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0038","text":"Finally, whatever fires of aether you see from here,\n\nyou may know can be smaller by a very little,\n\nor larger by a slight and tiny part.\n\nSince any fires whatever that we see upon earth,\n\nwhile their bright flickering, while their heat is seen,\n\nsometimes seem to alter their size by something very small,\n\ntoward one side or the other, the farther away they are.\n\nLikewise it is not to be wondered by what means\n\nthat little sun can send forth so great a light,\n\nwhich, watering seas and all lands and heaven,\n\nfills them and bathes all things in warm vapour.\n\nFor it may be that from here one open fountain of the whole world,\n\nbountifully flowing, wells up and bursts forth in light,\n\nbecause thus elements of vapour from the whole world\n\ncome together from every side, and thus their gathering\n\nflows together, so that this heat streams forth from one source.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0039","text":"Do you not see also how widely a small spring of water\n\nwaters the meadows at times and overflows the fields?\n\nThere is also this: that from no great fire of the sun\n\nthe air may receive heat with warm burnings,\n\nif the air be by chance suitable and apt,\n\nso that, struck by small burnings, it can be kindled;\n\nas at times we see fires arise everywhere among crops\n\nand stubble from a single spark.\n\nPerhaps too the sun, shining with rosy-bright lamp,\n\npossesses much fire about itself in unseen burnings,\n\nwhich is marked by no radiance,\n\nso that it may only increase the heat of the rays' stroke.\n\nNor does a single straightforward account of the sun stand revealed,\n\nhow from the summer regions of Capricorn\n\nit approaches the wintry turning and then, returning,\n\nturns its course toward the solstitial limits of Cancer,\n\nand how the moon seems to traverse that space in months\n\nwhich the sun consumes in its yearly course.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0040","text":"No, I say, a single cause has not been rendered for these matters.\n\nFor this seems especially able to happen,\n\nwhich the revered opinion of Democritus lays down:\n\nthat the nearer each of the stars is to the earth,\n\nthe less it can be carried along with the revolution of heaven;\n\nfor the swift and keen force of that revolution\n\nwanes and is diminished below, and therefore\n\nthe sun is gradually left behind with the signs that follow,\n\nbecause it is far lower than the fiery signs.\n\nAnd this is still more so for the moon: the more its\n\ncourse, descending, lies far from heaven and draws near to earth,\n\nthe less it can strain onward with the signs.\n\nAnd the more slackly it is now borne by the revolution,\n\nbeing lower than the sun, the more all the signs\n\novertake it in their circuit and are borne past it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0041","text":"Therefore it comes about that it seems to return\n\nmore quickly to each sign, because the signs return to it.\n\nIt also comes about that from the transverse parts of the world air\n\ncan flow alternately at a fixed time,\n\nwhich can drive the sun from its summer signs\n\nall the way to its wintry turnings and icy cold,\n\nand which can cast it back from the cold shadows of frost\n\nall the way into the summer regions and fiery signs.\n\nAnd by like reasoning one must think that the moon and stars,\n\nwhich roll their great years in great orbits,\n\ncan move by alternating airs from different regions.\n\nDo you not see too clouds, driven by different winds,\n\ngo in different directions, the lower from the upper?\n\nWhy should those stars be less able to be borne among themselves\n\nby differing tides through the great orbits of ether?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0042","text":"But night overwhelms the lands in mighty darkness,\n\neither when the sun, from its long course, has reached the farthest part\n\nof heaven and, languid, has breathed out its fires,\n\nshaken by their journey and weakened by much air,\n\nor because the same force which bore its orb above the lands\n\ncompels it to turn its course beneath the earth.\n\nLikewise at a fixed time rosy Matuta spreads dawn\n\nthrough the regions of ether and unveils the light,\n\neither because that same sun, returning beneath the earth,\n\nanticipates the sky, essaying to kindle it with rays,\n\nor because fires gather and many seeds\n\nof heat have been accustomed to flow together at a fixed time,\n\nwhich cause ever-new lights of the sun to be born;\n\nas report has it that from the high Idaean mountains\n\nscattered fires are seen in the eastern light,\n\nthen gather, as it were, into one globe and make up an orb.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","text":"Yet in these matters it ought not to be thought wonderful\n\nthat these seeds of fire can so exactly at a fixed time\n\ncome together and restore the sun's brightness.\n\nFor we see many things that happen at a fixed time\n\nin all matters. At a fixed time trees bloom,\n\nand at a fixed time they shed their blossom.\n\nNor less does age command teeth to fall at a fixed\n\ntime, and the beardless youth to grow hairy in soft down\n\nand likewise let a soft beard descend from his cheeks.\n\nLastly lightning, snow, rains, clouds, winds,\n\narise in not too uncertain seasons of the year.\n\nFor since the first beginnings of causes were so,\n\nand the things of the world thus fell out from their first origin,\n\nin consequence they now also return in fixed order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0044","text":"Likewise, the days may grow and the nights waste away,\n\nand the daylight diminish, as the nights take their increase,\n\neither because that same sun, running beneath the earth and above it\n\nthrough unequal windings, divides the regions of ether\n\nand apportions its orb into unequal parts;\n\nand what it has taken away from one of the two regions, it restores,\n\nhaving travelled, by so much more in the opposite region,\n\nuntil it reaches that sign of heaven where the knot of the year\n\nmakes the night-shadows equal to the daylight;\n\nfor midway in its course, the sky holds its boundaries\n\nat equal division between the breath of the north wind and south wind,\n\non account of the position of the whole zodiacal circle,\n\nin which the sun, gliding, completes the yearly seasons,\n\nsurveying lands and heaven with slanting light,\n\nas the reasoning of those declares who have marked all\n\nthe regions of heaven adorned with arranged signs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0045","text":"Or because the air is thicker in certain regions,\n\ntherefore beneath the earth the trembling radiance of fire lingers\n\nand cannot easily penetrate and emerge at its rising;\n\ntherefore, in winter time, long nights\n\ndelay until the radiant badge of day arrives.\n\nOr again, because in the alternating regions of the year\n\nfires have been accustomed to gather more slowly and more swiftly,\n\nwhich cause the sun to rise from a certain region,\n\ntherefore it comes about that they seem to speak truth.\n\nThe moon may shine when struck by the sun's rays\n\nand turn toward us day by day more light\n\nin appearance, by as much as it withdraws from the sun's orb,\n\nuntil opposite it it shines brightly in full light\n\nand, rising, has seen its setting from above;\n\nthen little by little it must in like manner hide its light backward,\n\nby as much as it now glides nearer to the sun's fire\n\nthrough another region of the circle of signs;\n\nas those do who imagine the moon to be like a ball\n\nand to hold its course beneath the sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0046","text":"There is also a reason why it may roll with its own light\n\nand display varying forms of brightness;\n\nfor there may be another body, which is borne along and at the same time\n\nglides in every way, confronting and obstructing it,\n\nand it cannot be seen, because it is borne devoid of light.\n\nAnd it can revolve, as, for example, a globe like a ball,\n\ntinged on one half with shining light,\n\nand, by revolving the globe, produce changing forms,\n\nuntil it turns whatever part is increased by fires\n\ntoward our sight and open eyes;\n\nthen little by little it twists back and takes away\n\nthe light-bearing part of the rounded mass and ball;\n\nas the Babylonian doctrine of the Chaldaeans, refuting\n\nthe art of the astrologers, strives to establish against it,\n\nas though what each side contends could not happen,\n\nor as though this had less cause to be embraced than that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0047","text":"Finally, why a new moon cannot always be created\n\nin a fixed order of forms and fixed figures,\n\nand each thing created vanish into its private days,\n\nwhile another be restored in that part and place of it,\n\nis difficult to demonstrate by reasoning and establish by words,\n\nwhen so many things are created in so fixed an order.\n\nSpring goes, and Venus, and before Venus her winged herald\n\nsteps forth; and in the tracks of Zephyr,\n\nmother Flora, scattering before them along the way,\n\nfills all things with splendid colours and scents.\n\nThen follows parched Heat, and with it dusty Ceres\n\nand the Etesian blasts of the north winds.\n\nThen Autumn approaches, and at the same time Euhius Euan advances.\n\nThen other storms and winds follow,\n\nloud-thundering Volturnus and the South Wind, mighty with lightning.\n\nAt last Winter brings snows and restores\n\nsluggish cold.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","text":"Winter follows, clattering with cold in its teeth.\n\nThus it is less wonderful if at a fixed time the moon\n\nis born and at a fixed time is again destroyed,\n\nwhen so many things can happen at so fixed a time.\n\nLikewise you must think that eclipses of the sun and obscurations of the moon\n\ncan occur from several causes.\n\nFor why, when the moon can shut out the earth from the sun's\n\nlight and bar from the earth its lofty head,\n\ncasting its dark orb before the burning rays,\n\nshould it be thought that at the same time another body cannot do this,\n\nwhich always glides devoid of light?\n\nAnd why can the sun not, languid, send forth its fires\n\nat a fixed time and renew its light,\n\nwhen it has passed through regions hostile to flames in the air,\n\nwhich cause fires to be quenched and perish?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","text":"And why can the earth in turn strip the moon\n\nof light and keep the sun oppressed above itself,\n\nwhile the moon passes through the rigid shadows of the cone,\n\nbut at the same time another body cannot come to the moon's aid,\n\nor glide above the sun's orb,\n\nso as to break off its rays and outpoured light?\n\nAnd yet, if the moon itself shines by its own radiance,\n\nwhy can it not grow faint in a certain region of the world,\n\nwhile it passes through places hostile to its own lights?\n\n.\n\nFor the rest, since through the blue of the great world\n\nI have explained by what reasoning whatever might happen,\n\nso that we might know what force and cause stirred\n\nthe sun's various courses and the moon's wanderings,\n\nand in what way, their light being extinguished, they are eclipsed\n\nand shroud the unsuspecting lands in darkness,\n\nwhen as though they wink, and again with open light\n\nbehold all clear places with bright radiance,\n\nnow I return to the newness of the world and the soft fields\n\nof earth: what they first resolved, in new birth, to raise\n\ninto the regions of light and entrust to uncertain winds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0050","text":"First the earth gave forth the race of grasses and green brightness\n\nall around the hills and across all the plains;\n\nthe flowery meadows gleamed with verdant colour,\n\nand thereafter to the diverse trees there was given through the air\n\na great contest of growing, with reins let loose.\n\nAs down and hair and bristles are first created\n\non the limbs of four-footed creatures and the bodies of winged ones,\n\nso then the new earth first brought forth grasses and shrubs;\n\nthen in turn it created many mortal races,\n\narisen in many ways by diverse means.\n\nFor animals cannot have fallen from the sky,\n\nnor can terrestrial creatures have come forth from the salt pools.\n\nIt remains that earth has deservedly gained\n\nthe name of mother, since all things have been created from earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","text":"And even now many animals arise from the earth,\n\nformed by rains and the warm vapour of the sun;\n\nso it is less wonderful that then more and greater ones arose,\n\nnourished by the new earth and ether.\n\nFirst the race of birds and varied flying creatures\n\nleft eggs, hatched in the season of spring,\n\nas now in summer cicadas of rounded form\n\nof their own accord leave their skins, seeking food and life.\n\nThen the earth first gave you mortal races.\n\nFor much heat and moisture abounded in the fields.\n\nWhenever, therefore, any region of ground offered a suitable place,\n\nwombs grew, fastened to the earth by roots;\n\nwhen at the ripe time age had opened these,\n\nfor the infants, fleeing moisture and seeking air,\n\nnature there turned the pores of the earth,\n\nand compelled it to pour out from open veins a juice\n\nlike milk, just as now every woman,\n\nafter she has borne, is filled with sweet milk, because all\n\nthat current of nourishment is turned toward the breasts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","text":"The earth provided food for the children, heat clothing, and grass a bed,\n\nabounding in much soft down.\n\nBut the newness of the world brought forth neither harsh colds\n\nnor excessive heats nor winds of great strength.\n\nFor all things grow and gain strength alike.\n\nWherefore, again and again, the earth deservedly retains\n\nthe name of mother, since it itself created the human race\n\nand at almost a fixed time poured forth every animal\n\nwhich ranges wildly everywhere upon great mountains,\n\nand likewise aerial birds of varying forms.\n\nBut because it must have some end of bearing,\n\nit ceased, like a woman wearied by the long passage of time.\n\nFor age changes the nature of the whole world,\n\nand one state must take up all things from another;\n\nno thing remains like itself: all things migrate,\n\nnature changes all things and compels them to be transformed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0053","text":"For one thing decays and, weakened by age, languishes;\n\nthen another grows and emerges from contempt.\n\nThus age changes the nature of the whole world,\n\nand one state of the earth takes up another,\n\nso that what could do is unable, and what could not bear before is able.\n\nThen too the earth tried to create many monsters,\n\narisen with wondrous face and limbs:\n\nthe androgyne, between the two sexes and removed from either;\n\nsome bereft of feet, in turn deprived of hands,\n\nalso mute without a mouth, found blind without a face,\n\nand bound through the whole body by limbs adhering together,\n\nso that they could neither do anything nor move anywhere\n\nnor avoid harm nor take what need would desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","text":"It created other monsters and prodigies of this kind,\n\nin vain, because nature barred their growth,\n\nand they could not attain the desired flower of age,\n\nnor find food, nor unite in the works of Venus.\n\nFor we see that many things must concur in creatures,\n\nso that they can forge forth races by propagation:\n\nfirst, that food may exist; then, that generative seeds\n\ncan flow through the limbs from relaxed members;\n\nand that the female can be joined with the male, and that each may possess\n\nmutual pleasures which they exchange between themselves.\n\nAnd many races of living creatures must then have perished\n\nand been unable by propagation to forge forth offspring.\n\nFor every kind that you see feeding on the vital breezes,\n\neither craft or strength or finally swiftness\n\nhas preserved that race from its earliest age.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","text":"And there are many kinds which, commended to us by their usefulness,\n\nremain entrusted to our protection.\n\nFirst, strength has protected the fierce race of lions and savage breeds,\n\ncraft foxes, and flight deer.\n\nBut the lightly sleeping hearts of dogs with faithful breast,\n\nand every race sprung from the seed of beasts of burden,\n\nas well as wool-bearing flocks and horned herds,\n\nall have been entrusted to the protection of men, Memmius;\n\nfor eagerly they fled wild beasts and followed peace,\n\nand sought abundant food procured without their own labour,\n\nwhich we give as rewards for their usefulness.\n\nBut those to which nature granted none of these things—neither\n\nthe power to live of their own accord, nor to render us\n\nsome usefulness for which we should allow their race\n\nto feed and remain safe beneath our protection—\n\nthese plainly lay as prey and gain for others,\n\nall entangled in their own fateful bonds,\n\nuntil nature led that race to destruction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056","text":"But Centaurs neither existed nor can at any time\n\nthere exist a being of double nature and twofold body,\n\ncompacted from limbs of alien kinds, such that its power\n\nfrom this side and that might be sufficiently equal.\n\nThis you may understand from this, however dull your mind.\n\nFirst, when three years have passed in active course, the horse\n\nis in its prime—not at all the boy; for even now he will often\n\nseek in dreams the milk-filled breasts.\n\nLater, when in old age the horse's strong powers\n\nand limbs fail, with life languidly fleeing,\n\nonly then does flowering youth come upon boyish age\n\nand clothe the cheeks with soft down;\n\nlest by chance you should believe that Centaurs can be made and exist\n\nfrom a man and the seed of a beast of burden, horses,\n\nor Scyllas, girded with the bodies of swift half-marine dogs,\n\nand the rest of this kind, whose limbs we see discordant\n\namong themselves:\n\nfor they neither flower together nor gain strength\n\nin their bodies nor waste away in old age,\n\nnor burn with like love nor agree in one character,\n\nnor are the same things pleasing through their limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0057","text":"for one may often see bearded flocks grow fat on hemlock,\n\nwhich is a sharp poison to man.\n\nIndeed, since flame is wont to scorch and burn the tawny bodies of lions\n\njust as much as every kind\n\nof creature and blood that exists upon the earth,\n\nhow could it have come about that, with a triple body, one\n\nfirst part a lion, last a dragon, and the middle part itself, the Chimera,\n\nshould breathe forth from its body fierce flame out of its mouth?\n\nTherefore he also who imagines that in the new-made earth and fresh heaven\n\nsuch animals could have been produced,\n\nrelying upon this one empty name of newness,\n\nmay with like reasoning utter many things from his mouth:\n\nhe may say that then streams of gold flowed everywhere over the earth\n\nand that trees were wont to bloom with gems,\n\nor that man was born with such mighty force of limbs\n\nas to be able to plant his steps across the deep seas\n\nand to turn the whole heaven about himself with his hands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0058","text":"For although there were many seeds of things in the earth\n\nat the time when the earth first brought forth animals,\n\nyet there is no indication that mixed creatures could have been created\n\namong themselves, and the limbs of living beings compounded together,\n\nbecause the kinds of herbs and crops and luxuriant trees\n\nwhich even now abound from the earth\n\ncannot nevertheless be created interwoven with one another,\n\nbut each thing proceeds according to its own manner, and all\n\npreserve their distinctions by a fixed covenant of nature.\n\nAnd that race of men in the fields was much\n\nharder, as was fitting, since the hard earth had created it,\n\nfounded inwardly on larger and more solid bones,\n\nsuited with strong sinews throughout its flesh,\n\nnot easily overcome by heat or cold,\n\nor by novelty of food or any failing of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0059","text":"And through many revolutions of the sun rolling through the sky\n\nthey led their lives after the wandering fashion of wild beasts.\n\nNor was anyone a sturdy guide of the curved plough,\n\nnor did anyone know how to work the fields with iron\n\nor to sink new saplings into the earth or cut down with high\n\nsickles the old branches from tall trees.\n\nWhat the sun and rains had given, what the earth had created\n\nof its own accord, that gift was enough to delight their hearts.\n\nAmong acorn-bearing oaks they commonly tended their bodies;\n\nand the arbutus fruits which now in winter time you see\n\nripen into a ruddy color,\n\nthe earth then bore in greater abundance, and larger too.\n\nAnd besides, the flowering newness of the world then produced\n\nmany hard foods, ample for wretched mortals.\n\nBut rivers and springs summoned them to quench their thirst,\n\nas now the downward rush of water from great mountains\n\ncries aloud far and wide to the thirsty tribes of wild beasts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0060","text":"Finally, wanderers occupied the woodland sanctuaries of nymphs, well known to them,\n\nfrom which they knew that streams of water washed the slippery rocks\n\nwith abundant flowing flood—damp rocks,\n\ndamp rocks dripping above with green moss—\n\nand in part bubbled up and burst forth upon the level plain.\n\nNot yet did they know how to handle things with fire, nor to use\n\nskins and spoils of beasts to clothe their bodies,\n\nbut they inhabited groves and hollow mountains and woods,\n\nand hid their squalid limbs amid the thickets,\n\nforced to avoid the blows of winds and rains.\n\nNor could they look to the common good, nor did they know how to use\n\nany customs or laws among themselves.\n\nWhatever prey fortune had offered to each, he carried off;\n\neach one taught by himself to be strong and live for himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0061","text":"And Venus joined the bodies of lovers in the woods;\n\nfor each union was brought about either by mutual desire,\n\nor by the man's violent force and vehement lust,\n\nor by a price—acorns and arbutus fruits or picked pears.\n\nAnd, trusting in the wondrous strength of hands and feet,\n\nthey pursued the woodland tribes of wild beasts\n\nwith hurled stones and the heavy weight of a club.\n\nThey conquered many, avoided a few by hiding places;\n\nand, like bristle-bearing swine, they gave their woodland bodies\n\nnaked to the earth when overtaken by night,\n\nwrapping themselves round with leaves and boughs.\n\nNor did they seek the day and sun through the fields\n\nwith loud lament, wandering fearful in the shadows of night;\n\nbut silently they waited, buried in sleep,\n\nuntil the sun should bring light into heaven with his rosy torch.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0062","text":"For since from childhood they had always been accustomed to see\n\ndarkness and light arise in alternating time,\n\nthere was no way it could ever happen that they should wonder,\n\nor despair lest everlasting night should hold the lands\n\nforever, the sun's light having been withdrawn.\n\nBut this was more their concern: that tribes of wild beasts\n\noften made repose hostile to the wretched.\n\nDriven from their homes, they fled their rocky shelters\n\nat the coming of a foam-flecked boar or mighty lion,\n\nand in the dead of night, frightened, yielded up\n\ntheir leaf-strewn beds to savage guests.\n\nNor then did mortal races leave behind the sweet light of life\n\nwith much more lamentation than they do now.\n\nFor then each one, more often seized among them,\n\nprovided living food for wild beasts, snatched by teeth,\n\nand filled groves and mountains and woods with groaning,\n\nseeing his living flesh buried in a living tomb.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0063","text":"But those whom flight had saved, though their bodies were devoured,\n\nafterward, holding trembling palms over foul wounds,\n\ncalled upon Orcus with dreadful cries,\n\nuntil savage worms deprived them of life,\n\nbereft of help, ignorant what their wounds required.\n\nBut not many thousands of men, led beneath standards,\n\ndid one day then give to destruction, nor did the troubled\n\nwaters of the sea dash ships and men against the rocks.\n\nFor the sea, having often risen rashly, vainly, to no purpose,\n\nraged and lightly laid aside its empty threats;\n\nnor could the treacherous blandishment of the calm sea\n\nentice anyone into disaster with its smiling waves.\n\nThe wicked craft of navigation then lay neglected.\n\nThen want of food gave their languishing limbs over to death;\n\nwhereas now abundance of things overwhelms them.\n\nThey themselves, unwitting, often poured out poison for themselves;\n\nnow they give it themselves more cunningly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064","text":"Thereafter, when they had prepared huts and skins and fire,\n\nand woman joined to man had yielded herself into one\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nwere known, and they saw offspring created from themselves,\n\nthen the human race first began to grow soft.\n\nFor fire brought it about that bodies chilled by cold\n\ncould no longer endure it so well beneath the canopy of heaven,\n\nand Venus diminished their strength, and children easily broke\n\nthe proud temper of their parents by caresses.\n\nThen neighboring men, desiring it, began to join friendship among themselves,\n\nneither to harm nor be harmed,\n\nand they commended children and the female sex,\n\nsignifying with voices and gesture, though inarticulately,\n\nthat it was right for all to pity the weak.\n\nYet concord could not arise in every respect,\n\nbut a great and good part faithfully kept the covenants;\n\notherwise the whole human race would already then have perished,\n\nnor could propagation have prolonged the generations until now.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","text":"But nature compelled them to utter the various sounds of speech,\n\nand use brought forth the names of things,\n\nby no very different reasoning than infancy itself seems\n\nto lead children on to gesture with the tongue,\n\nwhen it makes them point with the finger to things present.\n\nFor each one perceives what use he can make of his own power.\n\nBefore horns born upon a calf stand out from its forehead,\n\nangered, it attacks and presses with them in hostility.\n\nBut the cubs of panthers and the whelps of lions\n\nalready fight back with claws and feet and bite,\n\nwhen teeth and claws have scarcely even been created.\n\nMoreover, we see every race of birds\n\ntrusting to wings and seeking trembling aid from feathers.\n\nAccordingly, to suppose that some one person then distributed names\n\nto things, and that men thereafter learned their first words,\n\nis madness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0066","text":"For why should it be thought that this man could mark all things\n\nwith sounds and emit the various noises of speech,\n\nwhile others at the same time could not do this?\n\nBesides, if others too had not used speech\n\namong themselves, whence was implanted in him knowledge\n\nof its usefulness, and whence was first given to him the power\n\nto know and see in his mind what he wished to do?\n\nNor could one man compel many and subdue them when conquered,\n\nso that they would wish to learn thoroughly the names of things.\n\nNor is it easy by any reasoning to teach and persuade the deaf\n\nwhat must be done; for they would not endure it,\n\nnor would they any longer suffer their ears to be battered in vain\n\nby unheard sounds of voice, by any reasoning.\n\nFinally, what is so wondrous in this matter,\n\nif the human race, in which voice and tongue were strong,\n\nmarked different things with different sound according to its various feeling?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0067","text":"When dumb cattle, when indeed the tribes of wild beasts,\n\nare wont to utter unlike and various cries,\n\nwhen there is fear or pain and when joys now increase.\n\nFor indeed one may learn this from clear instances.\n\nWhen first the great soft muzzles of angered Molossian dogs growl,\n\nbaring their hard teeth,\n\ntheir restrained rage threatens with a far different sound,\n\nthan when they are already barking and filling everything with cries.\n\nBut when they try gently to lick their pups with their tongues,\n\nor when they suckle them, or, mighty in feet and bite,\n\nimitate tender bites with suspended teeth,\n\nthey fawn with a far different fashion of voice and whimpering;\n\nand so too when, deserted, they bay in a house, or when\n\nthey flee whimpering blows with crouching body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0068","text":"Finally, does not neighing likewise seem to differ,\n\nwhen among mares a youthful horse in flowering age,\n\nstruck by the spurs of winged Love, rages,\n\nand gives forth a snort for battle from his open nostrils,\n\nand when he neighs otherwise with limbs thus shaken?\n\nLastly, the race of birds and the various fowl,\n\nhawks and sea-eagles and gulls seeking food and life\n\nin the salty sea-waves,\n\nsend forth far different cries at one time than at another,\n\nand when they contend over food and struggle over prey.\n\nAnd some alter their hoarse-sounding songs along with the weather,\n\nas the ancient tribes of crows\n\nand the flock of ravens, when they are said to ask for water and rains,\n\nand sometimes to call forth winds and breezes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0069","text":"Therefore, if various feelings compel animals,\n\nthough they are dumb, to emit various sounds,\n\nhow much more fitting was it that mortals then should be able\n\nto mark different things with one sound and another!\n\nLest perhaps in these matters you silently ask this:\n\nlightning first brought fire down to earth for mortals,\n\nand from it all the heat of flames spread abroad;\n\nfor we see many things flashing, kindled by heavenly flames,\n\nwhen the blow of heaven has bestowed heat.\n\nAnd yet when a branching tree, shaken by winds and swaying,\n\nsurges, leaning its boughs upon the boughs of another tree,\n\nfire is squeezed out, worn away by mighty force,\n\nand at times the hot heat of flame darts forth,\n\nwhile branches and trunks rub against one another.\n\nEither of these may have given fire to mortals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","text":"Thereafter the sun taught them to cook food and soften it with the vapor of flame,\n\nbecause they saw many things grow mild,\n\nconquered in the fields by blows of rays and heat.\n\nAnd day by day those who excelled in talent and were strong in mind\n\nshowed them how to change their former sustenance and life\n\nby new things and fire.\n\nKings themselves began to found cities and establish a citadel,\n\na defense and refuge for themselves,\n\nand divided and assigned cattle and fields\n\naccording to each man's appearance and strength and talent;\n\nfor appearance counted greatly, and strength was vigorous.\n\nLater wealth was discovered and gold found,\n\nwhich easily stripped even the strong and beautiful of honor;\n\nfor men generally prefer to follow the party of the richer man\n\nrather than of the strong and those endowed with a handsome body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0071","text":"But if anyone should govern life by true reasoning,\n\ngreat riches for a man are to live sparingly\n\nwith an even mind; for there is never want in little.\n\nBut men wished themselves to be famous and powerful,\n\nso that their fortune might remain on a firm foundation\n\nand that, rich, they might be able to pass a tranquil life—\n\nin vain, since in striving to attain the highest honor\n\nthey made the path of life a dangerous road,\n\nand yet envy, like lightning, sometimes hurls them down from the height,\n\nscornfully, into grim Tartarus.\n\nFor envy, as if with lightning, usually scorches the highest things\n\nand whatever things stand out more than others;\n\nso that it is now far better to obey in peace\n\nthan to wish to rule things by command and hold kingdoms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0072","text":"Therefore let them, worn out, sweat blood in vain,\n\nstruggling along the narrow path of ambition;\n\nsince they take their wisdom from another's mouth and seek\n\nthings from hearsay rather than from their own senses,\n\nand this is no more so now, nor will it be hereafter, than it was before.\n\nTherefore, when kings had been slain, there lay overthrown\n\nthe former majesty of thrones and proud scepters,\n\nand the splendid, blood-stained emblem of the highest head\n\nbeneath the feet of the mob mourned its great honor;\n\nfor what was too much feared before is eagerly trampled down.\n\nThus things returned to the utter dregs and confusion,\n\nwhen each man sought dominion and sovereignty for himself.\n\nThen some taught the creation of magistracies\n\nand the establishment of laws, that they might wish to use laws.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","text":"For the human race, wearied with living an age by force,\n\nlanguished from hostilities; and therefore more readily it itself\n\nof its own accord fell under laws and binding statutes.\n\nFor each man made himself ready to avenge himself more fiercely in anger\n\nthan is now permitted by equitable laws;\n\nfor this reason men grew utterly weary of living an age by force.\n\nThereafter fear of punishments stains the prizes of life.\n\nFor violence and wrong entangle each man in a net,\n\nand for the most part return to him from whom they arose;\n\nnor is it easy to lead a calm and peaceful life\n\nfor one who violates by deeds the common covenants of peace.\n\nFor even if he deceives the race of gods and humankind,\n\nyet he must distrust that this will remain hidden forever;\n\nsince many are often reported to have betrayed themselves while speaking in dreams\n\nor raving in sickness,\n\nand to have brought hidden things and sins into the open.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","text":"Now what cause has spread abroad the divinities of the gods among great nations\n\nand filled cities with altars,\n\nand brought it about that solemn sacred rites should be undertaken,\n\nrites which now flourish in great affairs and places,\n\nwhence even now a terror is implanted in mortals,\n\nwhich raises new shrines of gods throughout the whole circle\n\nof lands and compels their celebration on festal days—\n\nit is not so difficult to render an account of this in words.\n\nFor even then mortal races saw the gods' outstanding forms\n\nin their waking mind,\n\nand still more in sleep, of marvelous increase of body.\n\nThey attributed feeling to these, therefore, because\n\nthey seemed to move their limbs and utter proud voices,\n\nin accordance with their splendid appearance and vast strength.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","text":"And they gave them eternal life, because their appearance\n\nwas always at hand and their form remained,\n\nand moreover because they thought that those endowed with such great powers\n\ncould not lightly be overcome by any force.\n\nAnd they thought them far superior in fortunes for this reason,\n\nbecause fear of death troubled none of them,\n\nand at the same time because in dreams they saw them accomplish\n\nmany marvelous things and suffer no toil thereby.\n\nBesides, they perceived that the courses of heaven in fixed order\n\nand the various seasons of the years revolved,\n\nand could not discover by what causes this came about.\n\nTherefore they had this refuge: to hand over all things to the gods\n\nand make all things bend by their nod.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","text":"And they placed the dwellings and temples of the gods in heaven,\n\nbecause through heaven night and moon are seen to roll,\n\nthe moon, day and night, and the stern signs of night,\n\nnight-wandering torches and the flying fires of heaven,\n\nclouds, sun, rains, snow, winds, lightning, hail,\nand swift rumblings and mighty murmurs of threats.\n\nO unhappy race of men, when it attributed such deeds to the gods\n\nand joined to them bitter angers!\n\nWhat groans did they then beget for themselves, what wounds for us,\n\nwhat tears for our descendants!\n\nNor is piety at all to be seen often veiled, turning toward a stone,\n\nand approaching every altar,\n\nnor to fall prostrate upon the ground and spread one's palms\n\nbefore the shrines of the gods, nor to sprinkle altars\n\nwith much blood of four-footed beasts, nor to link vow upon vow,\n\nbut rather to be able to behold all things with a mind at peace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0077","text":"For when we behold the great\n\ntemple-structures of the heavenly world above, and the aether fixed with gleaming stars,\n\nand there comes to mind the courses of sun and moon,\n\nthen, oppressed by other evils, that care too\n\nbegins to raise its awakened head in our breasts:\n\nwhether perhaps there is for us some immeasurable power of the gods\n\nwhich turns the bright stars in their various motion.\n\nFor poverty of reasoning assails the doubting mind,\n\nboth what was the generative origin of the world,\n\nand likewise what its end may be, how long the walls of the world\n\ncan endure this strain of restless motion,\n\nor whether, endowed by the gods with everlasting preservation,\n\nthey can, rolling on through the perpetual course of time,\n\ndespise the mighty forces of immeasurable age.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0078","text":"Moreover, whose mind is not contracted by fear of the gods,\n\nwhose limbs do not shudder with dread,\n\nwhen the parched earth trembles beneath the dreadful blow\n\nof lightning and great rumblings run through the sky?\n\nDo not peoples and nations tremble, and proud kings\n\nshrink in body, smitten by fear of the gods,\n\nlest, because of some foul deed done or arrogant word spoken,\n\nthe heavy time for paying penalties has been increased?\n\nAgain, when the utmost force of a violent wind over the sea\n\nsweeps the commander of a fleet across the waters\n\ntogether with mighty legions and elephants,\n\ndoes he not seek the peace of the gods by vows and prayers,\n\nfearfully begging peaceful winds and favorable breaths?\n\nYet in vain, since, often caught in the violent whirlwind,\n\nhe is borne no less onward to the shoals of death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0079","text":"So utterly does some hidden force trample upon human affairs\n\nand seem to tread underfoot the splendid fasces and cruel axes\n\nand hold them in mockery for itself.\n\nLastly, when the whole earth shakes beneath our feet\n\nand shaken cities fall and others, tottering, threaten to fall,\n\nwhat wonder if mortal races despise themselves\n\nand leave to the gods great and marvelous powers\n\nand forces in things, to govern the whole?\n\nWhat remains: gold and iron alike were discovered,\n\nand at the same time the weight of silver and the power of lead,\n\nwhere the heat of a great fire had burned forests\n\non great mountains, whether by lightning sent from heaven,\n\nor because, waging woodland warfare among themselves,\n\nthey had brought fire against their enemies for the sake of terror,\n\nor because, induced by the goodness of the soil, they wished\n\nto open rich fields and render the countryside pastureland,\n\nor to kill wild beasts and grow rich by plunder.\n\nFor hunting by pit and fire arose earlier\n\nthan enclosing a woodland glade with nets and stirring it with dogs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0080","text":"Whatever it was, from whatever cause the flaming heat\n\nhad consumed the woods with dreadful noise from their deep\n\nroots and baked the earth with fire,\n\na stream of silver and gold, gathering from heated veins,\n\nflowed down into hollow places of the ground,\n\nand likewise of bronze and lead. When afterward they saw these\n\ncongealed and shining in the earth with bright color,\n\nthey lifted them up, captivated by their smooth and gleaming beauty,\n\nand saw that they were fashioned in a shape\n\nsimilar to the outlines of the hollows belonging to each.\n\nThen it occurred to them that these, melted by heat,\n\ncould flow into any form and shape of things whatever,\n\nand that, by hammering, they could be drawn out altogether into\n\nthe sharp and slender points of blades,\n\nso that they might prepare weapons for themselves and be able to cut down woods,\n\nhew timber and smooth light beams,\n\nand even bore, pierce, and perforate through and through.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0081","text":"And they prepared to do these things with silver and gold\n\nno less than at first with the strong might of violent bronze—\n\nin vain, since their defeated strength gave way,\n\nand they could not equally endure the hard labor.\n\nFor bronze was more highly valued, and gold lay neglected\n\nbecause of its uselessness, blunted with its dull edge;\n\nnow bronze lies neglected, gold has risen to highest honor.\n\nThus the rolling age changes the seasons of things.\n\nWhat was valued comes at last into no honor;\n\nthen another thing succeeds and emerges from contempt,\n\nis sought more and more from day to day, and, once discovered, flourishes\n\nin praise and is in marvelous honor among mortals.\n\nNow, Memmius, in what manner the nature of iron\n\nwas discovered is easy for you yourself to learn by yourself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0082","text":"The ancient weapons were hands, nails, and teeth,\n\nand stones, and likewise broken branches from the woods,\n\nand flame and fire, after these were first known.\n\nLater the force of iron and bronze was discovered.\n\nAnd the use of bronze was known before that of iron,\n\nbecause its nature is more manageable and its abundance greater.\n\nWith bronze they worked the soil of the earth, and with bronze\n\nthey stirred up the waves of war and sowed vast wounds,\n\nand took away flocks and fields; for all things unarmed and naked\n\neasily yielded to those armed with it.\n\nThereupon the iron sword advanced little by little,\n\nand the form of the bronze sickle was turned into reproach;\n\nand with iron they began to cleave the soil of the earth,\n\nand the doubtful contests of war were made equal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0083","text":"And earlier was it to mount upon the armed back of a horse\n\nand govern him with reins and display strength with the right hand,\n\nthan to try the perils of war in a two-horse chariot.\n\nAnd earlier was the two-horse chariot than to yoke together two pairs,\n\nand than to mount armed in scythe-bearing chariots.\n\nThereupon the Carthaginians taught Lucan oxen, with towered bodies,\n\ndark, snake-handed, to endure the wounds of war\n\nand to throw into confusion the great battalions of Mars.\n\nThus grim discord produced one thing from another,\n\nsomething dreadful in arms for the races of men,\n\nand day by day added increase to the terrors of war.\n\nThey also tried bulls in the business of war,\n\nand experimented with sending savage boars against the enemy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0084","text":"And some sent powerful lions before them,\n\nwith armed trainers and savage masters,\n\nwho might be able to govern them and hold them in bonds—\n\nin vain, since, heated by indiscriminate slaughter,\n\nthe savage beasts threw the ranks into confusion without distinction,\n\nshaking everywhere the dreadful crests of their heads;\n\nnor could the horsemen soothe the breasts of their horses, terrified by the roaring,\n\nand turn them with the reins against the enemy.\n\nThe enraged lionesses hurled their bodies in leaps\n\non every side and opened their mouths against those coming toward them,\n\nand seized from behind those who did not expect them,\n\nand, having thrown them down, gave them to the ground, conquered by wounds,\n\nfastened upon them with strong bites and hooked claws.\n\nAnd bulls tossed their own men and pierced them with their feet,\n\nand tore open the flanks and bellies of horses from beneath\n\nwith their horns, and rushed upon the ground with threatening rage.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0085","text":"And boars savagely struck down allies with their strong teeth,\n\nsteeping in their own blood the weapons they had broken,\n\nand spread mingled ruin among horsemen and foot-soldiers.\n\nFor the beasts, urged aside by the crosswise stroke of teeth,\n\nwould flee, or, rearing up, would strike at the winds with their feet—\n\nin vain, since you would see them hamstrung\n\nfall and cover the earth with a heavy crash.\n\nIf they thought some sufficiently tamed at home before,\n\nthey saw them grow wild in the business being done,\n\namid wounds, shouting, flight, terror, and tumult,\n\nnor could they bring back any part of them;\n\nfor every varied kind of wild beast scattered in flight,\n\nas now Lucan oxen, badly butchered by iron, often scatter,\n\nafter they have inflicted many savage deeds upon their own men.\n\nBut they wished to do this not so much in hope of victory\n\nas to give the enemy cause to groan, while they themselves perished—\n\nthey who distrusted their numbers and lacked weapons—\n\nif indeed it was so that they did this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0086","text":"But I am scarcely persuaded that beforehand\n\nthey were unable in mind to foresee and see\n\nwhat a common and foul evil would come to be.\n\nAnd you could more plausibly maintain that this occurred in the whole\n\nof the various worlds created in various ways,\n\nthan in any one fixed and single world of earth.\n\nA sewn garment existed before a woven covering.\n\nThe woven covering is after iron, because the loom is prepared by iron,\n\nnor can such light things otherwise be produced:\n\nheddles and spindles, beams and sounding shuttles.\n\nAnd nature compelled men to make wool before women;\n\nfor the whole male sex is far superior in skill\n\nand much more ingenious—until stern farmers changed this by fault,\n\nso that they wished to concede it to women's hands\n\nand themselves equally to endure hard labor\n\nand harden their limbs and hands in harsh work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0087","text":"But the first model of sowing and grafting\n\nwas nature herself, the creatress of things;\n\nfor berries and fallen acorns beneath the trees\n\nin their season gave forth swarms of young shoots.\n\nHence too came the desire to entrust grafts to branches\n\nand to bury new slips in the earth through the fields.\n\nThen they tried one cultivation after another of the pleasant little field,\n\nand saw that wild fruits grew tame through yielding to the earth\n\nand cultivating it gently.\n\nAnd day by day they compelled the woods to retreat farther up the mountain\n\nand below to yield their place to cultivation,\n\nso that they might have meadows, pools, streams, grainfields, and joyful vineyards\n\non hills and plains, and so that a bluish band of olive trees,\n\ndistinguishing the space between, might run spread out\n\nacross mounds and valleys and plains—\n\nas now you see all things distinguished by varied beauty,\n\nwhich adorn them, interspersed with sweet fruits,\n\nand hold them clothed round about with flourishing trees.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0088","text":"But to imitate with the mouth the clear voices of birds\n\nwas much earlier than men could celebrate smooth songs in singing\n\nand delight the ears.\n\nAnd the hollow whistles of the west wind through reeds first\n\ntaught countryfolk to blow into hollow hemlock pipes.\n\nThen little by little they learned sweet plaints,\n\nwhich the pipe, struck by the fingers of players, pours forth,\n\ndiscovered through pathless groves and woods and glades,\n\nthrough the deserted places of shepherds and their divine leisure.\n\nThese things soothed and delighted their minds\n\nwhen they had their fill of food; for then all things are pleasing.\n\nThus often, stretched out together on soft grass\n\nbeside a stream of water, beneath the branches of a tall tree,\n\nthey disposed their bodies pleasantly, without great wealth,\n\nespecially when the weather smiled and the seasons of the year\n\npainted the green grasses with flowers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0089","text":"Then jests, then talk, then sweet laughter\n\nhad become customary; for then the rustic Muse was vigorous.\n\nThen joyous wantonness moved them to crown head and shoulders\n\nwith intertwined garlands of flowers and leaves,\n\nand to proceed moving their limbs out of measure\n\nawkwardly and beat mother earth with hard foot;\n\nwhence arose laughter and sweet peals of laughter,\n\nbecause all these things, then new and strange, flourished more greatly.\n\nAnd for those awake there were henceforth consolations: to draw out sleep\n\nwith manifold sounds and bend songs,\n\nand run over the reeds above with curved lip;\n\nwhence even now the wakeful preserve these practices received from them.\n\nAnd they learned to keep measure, yet in the meantime they reap\n\nnot a whit greater fruit of sweetness\n\nthan the woodland race of earth-born men reaped.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090","text":"For what is present and at hand, unless we have known something sweeter before,\n\npleases above all and seems to prevail;\n\nand the later thing discovered, generally better,\n\ndestroys and changes our feeling toward every former thing.\n\nThus hatred of acorns began; thus were abandoned those beds\n\nspread with grass and heaped with leaves.\n\nLikewise the garment of animal skin fell into contempt;\n\nand I think it was then discovered with such envy\n\nthat he who first wore it perished by treachery,\n\nand yet that, torn apart among them with much blood,\n\nit perished and could not be turned to use.\n\nThen skins, now gold and purple,\n\ntorment the life of men with cares and weary it with war;\n\nin which matter, I think, the greater fault rests with us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","text":"For cold tortured the earth-born naked without skins;\n\nbut nothing harms us in lacking a garment\n\nof purple, fitted with gold and great emblems,\n\nprovided there is nevertheless a common one able to protect us.\n\nTherefore the race of men labors in vain and fruitlessly\n\nalways, and consumes its life in empty cares,\n\nevidently because it has not known what is the limit\n\nof possessing, or altogether how far true pleasure increases;\n\nand this little by little drove life onward into the deep\n\nand stirred up from its foundations the great billows of war.\n\nBut the watchmen of the world, the sun and moon,\n\nsurveying round about the great revolving temple with their own light,\n\nthoroughly taught men that the seasons of the years turn\n\nand that events are conducted by fixed method and fixed order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","text":"Now, enclosed by strong towers, they were passing their life,\n\nand the earth was cultivated, divided and apportioned;\n\nthen the sea was flourishing with sail-bearing ships upon the deep,\n\nand they already had auxiliaries and allies by treaty compact,\n\nwhen poets began to hand down deeds accomplished in song;\n\nnor were letters discovered much earlier.\n\nTherefore our age cannot look back to see what was done before,\n\nexcept insofar as reason points out traces.\n\nShips and the cultivation of fields, walls, laws,\n\narms, roads, garments, and all else of this kind,\n\nrewards, luxuries too, and every delight of life,\n\nsongs, paintings, and cunningly wrought polished statues—\n\nuse and the experience together of the vigorous mind,\n\nadvancing step by step, taught these things little by little.\n\nThus time gradually brings each thing whatsoever out into the open,\n\nand reason raises it up to the borders of light;\n\nfor they saw one thing grow clear from another in the mind,\n\nuntil they reached the highest summit by the arts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092"]}],"source_digest":"5df6b92df08ea7a77dbbe86fdb6a4b9bd40efc092769d99d5a99ab75868f49bd","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Liber Quintus"}],"passage_basis":"revised-passages","original_title":"Liber Quintus","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3","title":"Book Six","order":6,"chapter_id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3","text":"## Liber Sextus\n\n  \n\nPrimae frugiparos fetus mortalibus aegris\n\n \n\ndididerunt quondam praeclaro nomine Athenae\n\n \n\net recreaverunt vitam legesque rogarunt\n\n \n\net primae dederunt solacia dulcia vitae,\n\n \n\ncum genuere virum tali cum corde repertum,\n\n \n\nomnia veridico qui quondam ex ore profudit;\n\n \n\ncuius et extincti propter divina reperta\n\n \n\ndivolgata vetus iam ad caelum gloria fertur.\n\n \n\nnam cum vidit hic ad victum quae flagitat usus\n\n \n\nomnia iam ferme mortalibus esse parata\n\n \n\net, pro quam possent, vitam consistere tutam,\n\n \n\ndivitiis homines et honore et laude potentis\n\n \n\naffluere atque bona gnatorum excellere fama,\n\n \n\nnec minus esse domi cuiquam tamen anxia cordi,\n\n \n\natque animi ingratis vitam vexare sine ulla\n\n \n\npausa atque infestis cogi saevire querellis,\n\n \n\nintellegit ibi vitium vas efficere ipsum\n\n \n\nomniaque illius vitio corrumpier intus,\n\n \n\nquae conlata foris et commoda cumque venirent;\n\n \n\npartim quod fluxum pertusumque esse videbat,\n\n \n\nut nulla posset ratione explerier umquam,\n\n \n\npartim quod taetro quasi conspurcare sapore\n\n \n\nomnia cernebat, quae cumque receperat, intus.\n\n \n\nveridicis igitur purgavit pectora dictis\n\n \n\net finem statuit cuppedinis atque timoris\n\n \n\nexposuitque bonum summum, quo tendimus omnes,\n\n \n\nquid foret, atque viam monstravit, tramite parvo\n\n \n\nqua possemus ad id recto contendere cursu,\n\n \n\nquidve mali foret in rebus mortalibus passim,\n\n \n\nquod fieret naturali varieque volaret\n\n \n\nseu casu seu vi, quod sic natura parasset,\n\n \n\net quibus e portis occurri cuique deceret,\n\n \n\net genus humanum frustra plerumque probavit\n\n \n\nvolvere curarum tristis in pectore fluctus.\n\n \n\nnam vel uti pueri trepidant atque omnia caecis\n\n \n\nin tenebris metuunt, sic nos in luce timemus\n\n \n\ninter dum, nihilo quae sunt metuenda magis quam\n\n \n\nquae pueri in tenebris pavitant finguntque futura.\n\n \n\nhunc igitur terrorem animi tenebrasque necessest\n\n \n\nnon radii solis nec lucida tela diei\n\n \n\ndiscutiant, sed naturae species ratioque.\n\n \n\nquo magis inceptum pergam pertexere dictis.\n\n  \n\nEt quoniam docui mundi mortalia templa\n\n \n\nesse  nativo consistere corpore caelum,\n\n \n\net quae cumque in eo fiunt fierique necessest\n\n \n\npleraque dissolui, qui restant percipe porro,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem semel insignem conscendere currum\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\ntu mihi supremae praescripta ad candida callis\n\n \n\ncurrenti spatium praemonstra, callida musa\n\n \n\nCalliope, requies hominum divomque voluptas,\n\n \n\nte duce ut insigni capiam cum laude coronam.\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n ventorum existant, placentur  omnia rursum\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nquae fuerint, sint placato conversa furore.\n\n \n\ncetera quae fieri in terris caeloque tuentur\n\n \n\nmortales, pavidis cum pendent mentibus saepe\n\n \n\net faciunt animos humilis formidine divom\n\n \n\ndepressosque premunt ad terram propterea quod\n\n \n\nignorantia causarum conferre deorum\n\n \n\ncogit ad imperium res et concedere regnum.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nnam bene qui didicere deos securum agere aevom,\n\n \n\nsi tamen interea mirantur qua ratione\n\n \n\nquaeque geri possint, praesertim rebus in illis\n\n \n\nquae supera caput aetheriis cernuntur in oris,\n\n \n\nrursus in antiquas referuntur religionis\n\n \n\net dominos acris adsciscunt, omnia posse\n\n \n\nquos miseri credunt, ignari quid queat esse,\n\n \n\nquid nequeat, finita potestas denique cuique\n\n \n\nqua nam sit ratione atque alte terminus haerens;\n\n \n\nquo magis errantes caeca ratione feruntur.\n\n \n\nquae nisi respuis ex animo longeque remittis\n\n \n\ndis indigna putare alienaque pacis eorum,\n\n \n\ndelibata deum per te tibi numina sancta\n\n \n\nsaepe oberunt; non quo violari summa deum vis\n\n \n\npossit, ut ex ira poenas petere inbibat acris,\n\n \n\nsed quia tute tibi placida cum pace quietos\n\n \n\nconstitues magnos irarum volvere fluctus,\n\n \n\nnec delubra deum placido cum pectore adibis,\n\n \n\nnec de corpore quae sancto simulacra feruntur\n\n \n\nin mentes hominum divinae nuntia formae,\n\n \n\nsuscipere haec animi tranquilla pace valebis.\n\n \n\ninde videre licet qualis iam vita sequatur.\n\n \n\nquam quidem ut a nobis ratio verissima longe\n\n \n\nreiciat, quamquam sunt a me multa profecta,\n\n \n\nmulta tamen restant et sunt ornanda politis\n\n \n\nversibus; est ratio caeliQUE ignisque tenenda,\n\n \n\nsunt tempestates et fulmina clara canenda,\n\n \n\nquid faciant et qua de causa cumque ferantur;\n\n \n\nne trepides caeli divisis partibus amens,\n\n \n\nunde volans ignis pervenerit aut in utram se\n\n \n\nverterit hinc partim, quo pacto per loca saepta\n\n \n\ninsinuarit, et hinc dominatus ut extulerit se.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n  \n\nPrincipio tonitru quatiuntur caerula caeli\n\n \n\npropterea quia concurrunt sublime volantes\n\n \n\naetheriae nubes contra pugnantibus ventis.\n\n \n\nnec fit enim sonitus caeli de parte serena,\n\n \n\nverum ubi cumque magis denso sunt agmine nubes,\n\n \n\ntam magis hinc magno fremitus fit murmure saepe.\n\n \n\npraeterea neque tam condenso corpore nubes\n\n \n\nesse queunt quam sunt lapides ac ligna, neque autem\n\n \n\ntam tenues quam sunt nebulae fumique volantes;\n\n \n\nnam cadere aut bruto deberent pondere pressae\n\n \n\n ut lapides, aut ut fumus constare nequirent\n\n \n\nnec cohibere nives gelidas et grandinis imbris.\n\n \n\nDant etiam sonitum patuli super aequora mundi,\n\n \n\ncarbasus ut quondam magnis intenta theatris\n\n \n\ndat crepitum malos inter iactata trabesque,\n\n \n\ninter dum perscissa furit petulantibus auris\n\n \n\net fragilis  chartarum commeditatur;\n\n \n\nid quoque enim genus in tonitru cognoscere possis,\n\n \n\naut ubi suspensam vestem chartasque volantis\n\n \n\nverberibus venti versant planguntque per auras.\n\n \n\nfit quoque enim inter dum  non tam concurrere nubes\n\n \n\nfrontibus adversis possint quam de latere ire\n\n \n\ndiverso motu radentes corpora tractim,\n\n \n\naridus unde auris terget sonus ille diuque\n\n \n\nducitur, exierunt donec regionibus artis.\n\n  \n\nHoc etiam pacto tonitru concussa videntur\n\n \n\nomnia saepe gravi tremere et divolsa repente\n\n \n\nmaxima dissiluisse capacis moenia mundi,\n\n \n\ncum subito validi venti conlecta procella\n\n \n\nnubibus intorsit sese conclusaque ibidem\n\n \n\nturbine versanti magis ac magis undique nubem\n\n \n\ncogit uti fiat spisso cava corpore circum,\n\n \n\npost ubi conminuit vis eius et impetus acer,\n\n \n\ntum perterricrepo sonitu dat scissa fragorem.\n\n \n\nnec mirum, cum plena animae vensicula parva\n\n \n\nsaepe haud dat parvum sonitum displosa repente.\n\n \n\nEst etiam ratio, cum venti nubila perflant,\n\n \n\nut sonitus faciant; etenim ramosa videmus\n\n \n\nnubila saepe modis multis atque aspera ferri;\n\n \n\nscilicet ut, crebram silvam cum flamina cauri\n\n \n\nperflant, dant sonitum frondes ramique fragorem.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut inter dum validi vis incita venti\n\n \n\nperscindat nubem perfringens impete recto;\n\n \n\nnam quid possit ibi flatus manifesta docet res,\n\n \n\nhic, ubi lenior est, in terra cum tamen alta\n\n \n\narbusta evolvens radicibus haurit ab imis.\n\n \n\nsunt etiam fluctus per nubila, qui quasi murmur\n\n \n\ndant in frangendo graviter; quod item fit in altis\n\n \n\nfluminibus magnoque mari, cum frangitur aestus.\n\n \n\nFit quoque, ubi e nubi in nubem vis incidit ardens\n\n \n\nfulminis; haec multo si forte umore recepit\n\n \n\nignem, continuo magno clamore trucidat;\n\n \n\nut calidis candens ferrum e fornacibus olim\n\n \n\nstridit, ubi in gelidum propter demersimus imbrem.\n\n \n\nAridior porro si nubes accipit ignem,\n\n \n\nuritur ingenti sonitu succensa repente,\n\n \n\nlauricomos ut si per montis flamma vagetur\n\n \n\nturbine ventorum comburens impete magno;\n\n \n\nnec res ulla magis quam Phoebi Delphica laurus\n\n \n\nterribili sonitu flamma crepitante crematur.\n\n \n\nDenique saepe geli multus fragor atque ruina\n\n \n\ngrandinis in magnis sonitum dat nubibus alte;\n\n \n\nventus enim cum confercit, franguntur in artum\n\n \n\nconcreti montes nimborum et grandine mixti.\n\n  \n\nFulgit item, nubes ignis cum semina multa\n\n \n\n excussere suo concursu, ceu lapidem si\n\n \n\npercutiat lapis aut ferrum; nam tum quoque lumen\n\n \n\nexilit et claras scintillas dissipat ignis.\n\n \n\nsed tonitrum fit uti post auribus accipiamus,\n\n \n\nfulgere quam cernant oculi, quia semper ad auris\n\n \n\ntardius adveniunt quam visum quae moveant res.\n\n \n\nid licet hinc etiam cognoscere: caedere si quem\n\n \n\nancipiti videas ferro procul arboris auctum,\n\n \n\nante fit ut cernas ictum quam plaga per auris\n\n \n\ndet sonitum; sic fulgorem quoque cernimus ante\n\n \n\nquam tonitrum accipimus, pariter qui mittitur igni\n\n \n\ne simili causa, concursu natus eodem.\n\n  \n\nHoc etiam pacto volucri loca lumine tingunt\n\n \n\nnubes et tremulo tempestas impete fulgit.\n\n \n\nventus ubi invasit nubem et versatus ibidem\n\n \n\nfecit ut ante cavam docui spissescere nubem,\n\n \n\nmobilitate sua fervescit; ut omnia motu\n\n \n\npercalefacta vides ardescere, plumbea vero\n\n \n\nglans etiam longo cursu volvenda liquescit.\n\n \n\nergo fervidus hic nubem cum perscidit atram,\n\n \n\ndissipat ardoris quasi per vim expressa repente\n\n \n\nsemina, quae faciunt nictantia fulgura flammae;\n\n \n\ninde sonus sequitur, qui tardius adlicit auris\n\n \n\nquam quae perveniunt oculorum ad lumina nostra.\n\n \n\nscilicet hoc densis fit nubibus et simul alte\n\n \n\nextructis aliis alias super impete miro.\n\n \n\nne tibi sit frudi quod nos inferne videmus\n\n \n\nquam sint lata magis quam sursum extructa quid extent.\n\n \n\ncontemplator enim, cum montibus adsimulata\n\n \n\nnubila portabunt venti transversa per auras,\n\n \n\naut ubi per magnos montis cumulata videbis\n\n \n\ninsuper esse aliis alia atque urguere superna\n\n \n\nin statione locata sepultis undique ventis;\n\n \n\ntum poteris magnas moles cognoscere eorum\n\n \n\nspeluncasque vel ut saxis pendentibus structas\n\n \n\ncernere, quas venti cum tempestate coorta\n\n \n\nconplerunt, magno indignantur murmure clausi\n\n \n\nnubibus in caveisque ferarum more minantur,\n\n \n\nnunc hinc nunc illinc fremitus per nubila mittunt,\n\n \n\nquaerentesque viam circum versantur et ignis\n\n \n\nsemina convolvunt  nubibus atque ita cogunt\n\n \n\nmulta rotantque cavis flammam fornacibus intus,\n\n \n\ndonec divolsa fulserunt nube corusci.\n\n  \n\nHac etiam fit uti de causa mobilis ille\n\n \n\ndevolet in terram liquidi color aureus ignis,\n\n \n\nsemina quod nubes ipsas permulta necessust\n\n \n\nignis habere; etenim cum sunt umore sine ullo,\n\n \n\nflammeus  plerumque colos et splendidus ollis.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim solis de lumine multa necessest\n\n \n\nconcipere, ut merito rubeant ignesque profundant.\n\n \n\nhasce igitur cum ventus agens contrusit in unum\n\n \n\ncompressitque locum cogens, expressa profundunt\n\n \n\nsemina, quae faciunt flammae fulgere colores.\n\n \n\nFulgit item, cum rarescunt quoque nubila caeli;\n\n \n\nnam cum ventus eas leviter diducit euntis\n\n \n\n dissoluitque, cadant ingratius illa necessest\n\n \n\nsemina quae faciunt fulgorem. tum sine taetro\n\n \n\nterrore atque sonis fulgit nulloque tumultu.\n\n  \n\nQuod superest,  natura praedita constent\n\n \n\nfulmina, declarant ictus et inusta vaporis\n\n \n\nsigna notaeque gravis halantis sulpuris auras;\n\n \n\nignis enim sunt haec non venti signa neque imbris.\n\n \n\npraeterea saepe accendunt quoque tecta domorum\n\n \n\net celeri flamma dominantur in aedibus ipsis.\n\n \n\nhunc tibi subtilem cum primis ignibus ignem\n\n \n\nconstituit natura minutis mobilibusque\n\n \n\ncorporibus, cui nil omnino obsistere possit.\n\n \n\ntransit enim validum fulmen per saepta domorum\n\n \n\nclamor ut ac voces, transit per saxa, per aera\n\n \n\net liquidum puncto facit aes in tempore et aurum.\n\n \n\ncurat item vasis integris vina repente\n\n \n\ndiffugiant, quia ni mirum facile omnia circum\n\n \n\nconlaxat rareque facit lateramina vasis\n\n \n\nadveniens calor eius et insinuatus in ipsum\n\n \n\nmobiliter soluens differt primordia vini.\n\n \n\nquod solis vapor aetatem non posse videtur\n\n \n\nefficere usque adeo pollens fervore corusco.\n\n \n\ntanto mobilior vis et dominantior haec est.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quo pacto gignantur et impete tanto\n\n \n\nfiant ut possint ictu discludere turris,\n\n \n\ndisturbare domos, avellere tigna trabesque\n\n \n\net monimenta virum commoliri atque ciere,\n\n \n\nexanimare homines, pecudes prosternere passim,\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc qua vi facere omnia possint,\n\n \n\nexpediam neque  in promissis plura morabor.\n\n  \n\nFulmina gignier e crassis alteque putandumst\n\n \n\nnubibus extructis; nam caelo nulla sereno\n\n \n\nnec leviter densis mittuntur nubibus umquam.\n\n \n\nnam dubio procul hoc fieri manifesta docet res;\n\n \n\nquod tunc per totum concrescunt aeëra nubes,\n\n \n\nundique uti tenebras omnis Acherunta reamur\n\n \n\nliquisse et magnas caeli complesse cavernas,\n\n \n\næusque adeo tetra nimborum nocte coorta\n\n \n\ninpendent atrae formidinis ora superne,æ\n\n \n\ncum commoliri tempestas fulmina coeptat.\n\n \n\npraeterea persaepe niger quoque per mare nimbus,\n\n \n\nut picis e caelo demissum flumen, in undas\n\n \n\nsic cadit effertus tenebris procul et trahit atram\n\n \n\nfulminibus gravidam tempestatem atque procellis,\n\n \n\nignibus ac ventis cum primis ipse repletus,\n\n \n\nin terra quoque ut horrescant ac tecta requirant.\n\n \n\nsic igitur supera nostrum caput esse putandumst\n\n \n\ntempestatem altam; neque enim caligine tanta\n\n \n\nobruerent terras, nisi inaedificata superne\n\n \n\nmulta forent multis exempto nubila sole;\n\n \n\nnec tanto possent venientes opprimere imbri,\n\n \n\nflumina abundare ut facerent camposque natare,\n\n \n\nsi non extructis foret alte nubibus aether.\n\n \n\nhic igitur ventis atque ignibus omnia plena\n\n \n\nsunt; ideo passim fremitus et fulgura fiunt.\n\n \n\n quippe etenim supra docui permulta vaporis\n\n \n\nsemina habere cavas nubes et multa necessest\n\n \n\nconcipere ex solis radiis ardoreque eorum.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi ventus eas idem qui cogit in unum\n\n \n\nforte locum quemvis, expressit multa vaporis\n\n \n\nsemina seque simul cum eo commiscuit igni,\n\n \n\ninsinuatus ibi vortex versatur in arto\n\n \n\net calidis acuit fulmen fornacibus intus;\n\n \n\nnam duplici ratione accenditur: ipse sua cum\n\n \n\nmobilitate calescit et e contagibus ignis.\n\n \n\ninde ubi percaluit venti vis  gravis ignis\n\n \n\nimpetus incessit, maturum tum quasi fulmen\n\n \n\nperscindit subito nubem ferturque coruscis\n\n \n\nomnia luminibus lustrans loca percitus ardor.\n\n \n\nquem gravis insequitur sonitus, displosa repente\n\n \n\nopprimere ut caeli videantur templa superne.\n\n \n\ninde tremor terras graviter pertemptat et altum\n\n \n\nmurmura percurrunt caelum; nam tota fere tum\n\n \n\ntempestas concussa tremit fremitusque moventur.\n\n \n\nquo de concussu sequitur gravis imber et uber,\n\n \n\nomnis uti videatur in imbrem vertier aether\n\n \n\natque ita praecipitans ad diluviem revocare;\n\n \n\ntantus discidio nubis ventique procella\n\n \n\nmittitur, ardenti sonitus cum provolat ictu.\n\n  \n\nEst etiam cum vis extrinsecus incita venti\n\n \n\nincidit in validam maturo culmine nubem;\n\n \n\nquam cum perscidit, extemplo cadit igneus ille\n\n \n\nvertex, quem patrio vocitamus nomine fulmen.\n\n \n\nhoc fit idem in partis alias, quo cumque tulit vis.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut inter dum venti vis missa sine igni\n\n \n\nigniscat tamen in spatio longoque meatu,\n\n \n\ndum venit amittens in cursu corpora quaedam\n\n \n\ngrandia, quae nequeunt pariter penetrare per auras,\n\n \n\natque alia ex ipso conradens aeëre portat\n\n \n\nparvola, quae faciunt ignem commixta volando;\n\n \n\nnon alia longe ratione ac plumbea saepe\n\n \n\nfervida fit glans in cursu, cum multa rigoris\n\n \n\ncorpora dimittens ignem concepit in auris.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut ipsius plagae vis excitet ignem,\n\n \n\nfrigida cum venti pepulit vis missa sine igni,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia, cum vehementi perculit ictu,\n\n \n\nconfluere ex ipso possunt elementa vaporis\n\n \n\net simul ex illa quae tum res excipit ictum;\n\n \n\nut, lapidem ferro cum caedimus, evolat ignis,\n\n \n\nnec, quod frigida vis ferrist, hoc setius illi\n\n \n\nsemina concurrunt calidi fulgoris ad ictum.\n\n \n\nsic igitur quoque res accendi fulmine debet,\n\n \n\nopportuna fuit si forte et idonea flammis.\n\n \n\nnec temere omnino plane vis frigida venti\n\n \n\nesse potest, ea quae tanta vi missa supernest,\n\n \n\nquin, prius in cursu si non accenditur igni,\n\n \n\nat tepefacta tamen veniat commixta calore.\n\n  \n\nMobilitas autem fit fulminis et gravis ictus\n\n \n\net celeri ferme percurrunt fulmina lapsu,\n\n \n\nnubibus ipsa quod omnino prius incita se vis\n\n \n\n colligit et magnum conamen sumit eundi,\n\n \n\ninde ubi non potuit nubes capere inpetis auctum,\n\n \n\nexprimitur vis atque ideo volat impete miro,\n\n \n\nut validis quae de tormentis missa feruntur.\n\n \n\nAdde quod e parvis et levibus est elementis,\n\n \n\nnec facilest tali naturae obsistere quicquam;\n\n \n\ninter enim fugit ac penetrat per rara viarum,\n\n \n\nnon igitur multis offensibus in remorando\n\n \n\nhaesitat, hanc ob rem celeri volat impete labens.\n\n \n\nDeinde, quod omnino natura pondera deorsum\n\n \n\nomnia nituntur, cum plagast addita vero,\n\n \n\nmobilitas duplicatur et impetus ille gravescit,\n\n \n\nut vehementius et citius quae cumque morantur\n\n \n\nobvia discutiat plagis itinerque sequatur.\n\n \n\nDenique quod longo venit impete, sumere debet\n\n \n\nmobilitatem etiam atque etiam, quae crescit eundo\n\n \n\net validas auget viris et roborat ictum;\n\n \n\nnam facit ut quae sint illius semina cumque\n\n \n\ne regione locum quasi in unum cuncta ferantur,\n\n \n\nomnia coniciens in eum volventia cursum.\n\n \n\nForsitan ex ipso veniens trahat aeëre quaedam\n\n \n\ncorpora, quae plagis incendunt mobilitatem.\n\n \n\nincolumisque venit per res atque integra transit\n\n \n\nmulta, foraminibus liquidus quia transviat ignis.\n\n \n\nmultaque perfringit, cum corpora fulminis ipsa\n\n \n\ncorporibus rerum inciderunt, qua texta tenentur.\n\n \n\ndissoluit porro facile aes aurumque repente\n\n \n\nconferve facit, e parvis quia facta minute\n\n \n\ncorporibus vis est et levibus ex elementis,\n\n \n\nquae facile insinuantur et insinuata repente\n\n \n\ndissoluont nodos omnis et vincla relaxant.\n\n  \n\nAutumnoque magis stellis fulgentibus alta\n\n \n\nconcutitur caeli domus undique totaque tellus,\n\n \n\net cum tempora se veris florentia pandunt.\n\n \n\nfrigore enim desunt ignes ventique calore\n\n \n\ndeficiunt neque sunt tam denso corpore nubes.\n\n \n\ninterutrasque igitur cum caeli tempora constant,\n\n \n\ntum variae causae concurrunt fulminis omnes.\n\n \n\nnam fretus ipse anni permiscet frigus  aestum.\n\n \n\nquorum utrumque opus est fabricanda ad fulmina nubi,\n\n \n\nut discordia  rerum magnoque tumultu\n\n \n\nignibus et ventis furibundus fluctuet aeër.\n\n \n\nprima caloris enim pars est postrema rigoris;\n\n \n\ntempus id est vernum; quare pugnare necessest\n\n \n\ndissimilis  inter se turbareque mixtas.\n\n \n\net calor extremus primo cum frigore mixtus\n\n \n\nvolvitur, autumni quod fertur nomine tempus,\n\n \n\nhic quoque confligunt hiemes aestatibus acres.\n\n \n\npropterea  sunt haec anni nominitanda,\n\n \n\nnec mirumst, in eo si tempore plurima fiunt\n\n \n\nfulmina tempestasque cietur turbida caelo,\n\n \n\n ancipiti quoniam bello turbatur utrimque,\n\n \n\nhinc flammis, illinc ventis umoreque mixto.\n\n  \n\nHoc est igniferi naturam fulminis ipsam\n\n \n\nperspicere et qua vi faciat rem quamque videre,\n\n \n\nnon Tyrrhena retro volventem carmina frustra\n\n \n\nindicia occultae divum perquirere mentis,\n\n \n\nunde volans ignis pervenerit aut in utram se\n\n \n\nverterit hinc partim, quo pacto per loca saepta\n\n \n\ninsinuarit, et hinc dominatus ut extulerit se,\n\n \n\nquidve nocere queat de caelo fulminis ictus.\n\n \n\nquod si Iuppiter atque alii fulgentia divi\n\n \n\nterrifico quatiunt sonitu caelestia templa\n\n \n\net iaciunt ignem quo cuiquest cumque voluntas,\n\n \n\ncur quibus incautum scelus aversabile cumquest\n\n \n\nnon faciunt icti flammas ut fulguris halent\n\n \n\npectore perfixo, documen mortalibus acre,\n\n \n\net potius nulla sibi turpi conscius in re\n\n \n\nvolvitur in flammis innoxius inque peditur\n\n \n\nturbine caelesti subito correptus et igni?\n\n \n\ncur etiam loca sola petunt frustraque laborant?\n\n \n\nan tum bracchia consuescunt firmantque lacertos?\n\n \n\nin terraque patris cur telum perpetiuntur\n\n \n\noptundi? cur ipse sinit neque parcit in hostis?\n\n \n\ndenique cur numquam caelo iacit undique puro\n\n \n\nIuppiter in terras fulmen sonitusque profundit?\n\n \n\nan simul ac nubes successere, ipse in eas tum\n\n \n\ndescendit, prope ut hinc teli determinet ictus?\n\n \n\nin mare qua porro mittit ratione? quid undas\n\n \n\narguit et liquidam molem camposque natantis?\n\n \n\npraeterea si vult caveamus fulminis ictum,\n\n \n\ncur dubitat facere ut possimus cernere missum?\n\n \n\nsi nec opinantis autem volt opprimere igni,\n\n \n\ncur tonat ex illa parte, ut vitare queamus,\n\n \n\ncur tenebras ante et fremitus et murmura concit?\n\n \n\net simul in multas partis qui credere possis\n\n \n\nmittere? an hoc ausis numquam contendere factum,\n\n \n\nut fierent ictus uno sub tempore plures?\n\n \n\nat saepest numero factum fierique necessest,\n\n \n\nut pluere in multis regionibus et cadere imbris,\n\n \n\nfulmina sic uno fieri sub tempore multa.\n\n \n\npostremo cur sancta deum delubra suasque\n\n \n\ndiscutit infesto praeclaras fulmine sedes\n\n \n\net bene facta deum frangit simulacra suisque\n\n \n\ndemit imaginibus violento volnere honorem?\n\n \n\naltaque cur plerumque petit loca plurimaque eius\n\n \n\nmontibus in summis vestigia cernimus ignis?\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, facilest ex his cognoscere rebus,\n\n \n\npresteras Graii quos ab re nominitarunt,\n\n \n\nin mare qua missi veniant ratione superne.\n\n \n\nnam fit ut inter dum tam quam demissa columna\n\n \n\nin mare de caelo descendat, quam freta circum\n\n \n\nfervescunt graviter spirantibus incita flabris,\n\n \n\net quae cumque in eo tum sint deprensa tumultu\n\n \n\nnavigia in summum veniant vexata periclum.\n\n \n\nhoc fit ubi inter dum non quit vis incita venti\n\n \n\n rumpere quam coepit nubem, sed deprimit, ut sit\n\n \n\nin mare de caelo tam quam demissa columna,\n\n \n\npaulatim, quasi quid pugno bracchique superne\n\n \n\nconiectu trudatur et extendatur in undas;\n\n \n\nquam cum discidit, hinc prorumpitur in mare venti\n\n \n\nvis et fervorem mirum concinnat in undis;\n\n \n\nversabundus enim turbo descendit et illam\n\n \n\ndeducit pariter lento cum corpore nubem;\n\n \n\nquam simul ac gravidam detrusit ad aequora ponti,\n\n \n\nille in aquam subito totum se inmittit et omne\n\n \n\nexcitat ingenti sonitu mare fervere cogens.\n\n  \n\nFit quoque ut involvat venti se nubibus ipse\n\n \n\nvertex conradens ex aeëre semina nubis\n\n \n\net quasi demissum caelo prestera imitetur;\n\n \n\nhic ubi se in terras demisit dissoluitque,\n\n \n\nturbinis immanem vim provomit atque procellae.\n\n \n\nsed quia fit raro omnino montisque necessest\n\n \n\nofficere in terris, apparet crebrius idem\n\n \n\nprospectu maris in magno caeloque patenti.\n\n \n\nNubila concrescunt, ubi corpora multa volando\n\n \n\nhoc super in caeli spatio coiere repente\n\n \n\nasperiora, modis quae possint indupedita\n\n \n\nexiguis tamen inter se compressa teneri.\n\n \n\nhaec faciunt primum parvas consistere nubes;\n\n \n\ninde ea comprendunt inter se conque gregantur\n\n \n\net coniungendo crescunt ventisque feruntur\n\n \n\nusque adeo donec tempestas saeva coortast.\n\n \n\nFit quoque uti montis vicina cacumina caelo\n\n \n\nquam sint quoque magis, tanto magis edita fument\n\n \n\nadsidue fulvae nubis caligine crassa\n\n \n\npropterea quia, cum consistunt nubila primum,\n\n \n\nante videre oculi quam possint tenvia, venti\n\n \n\nportantes cogunt ad summa cacumina montis;\n\n \n\nhic demum fit uti turba maiore coorta\n\n \n\net condensa queant apparere et simul ipso\n\n \n\nvertice de montis videantur surgere in aethram.\n\n \n\nnam loca declarat sursum ventosa patere\n\n \n\nres ipsa et sensus, montis cum ascendimus altos.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea permulta mari quoque tollere toto\n\n \n\ncorpora naturam declarant litore vestis\n\n \n\nsuspensae, cum concipiunt umoris adhaesum.\n\n \n\nquo magis ad nubis augendas multa videntur\n\n \n\nposse quoque e salso consurgere momine ponti;\n\n \n\nnam ratio consanguineast umoribus omnis.\n\n \n\nPraeterea fluviis ex omnibus et simul ipsa\n\n \n\nsurgere de terra nebulas aestumque videmus,\n\n \n\nquae vel ut halitus hinc ita sursum expressa feruntur\n\n \n\nsuffunduntque sua caelum caligine et altas\n\n \n\nsufficiunt nubis paulatim conveniundo;\n\n \n\nurget enim quoque signiferi super aetheris aestus\n\n \n\net quasi densendo subtexit caerula nimbis.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut hunc veniant in caelum extrinsecus illa\n\n \n\ncorpora quae faciunt nubis nimbosque volantis;\n\n \n\ninnumerabilem enim numerum summamque profundi\n\n \n\nesse infinitam docui, quantaque volarent\n\n \n\n corpora mobilitate ostendi quamque repente\n\n \n\nimmemorabile  spatium transire solerent.\n\n \n\nhaut igitur mirumst, si parvo tempore saepe\n\n \n\ntam magnis ventis tempestas atque tenebrae\n\n \n\ncoperiant maria ac terras inpensa superne,\n\n \n\nundique quandoquidem per caulas aetheris omnis\n\n \n\net quasi per magni circum spiracula mundi\n\n \n\nexitus introitusque elementis redditus extat.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quo pacto pluvius concrescat in altis\n\n \n\nnubibus umor et in terras demissus ut imber\n\n \n\ndecidat, expediam. primum iam semina aquai\n\n \n\nmulta simul vincam consurgere nubibus ipsis\n\n \n\nomnibus ex rebus pariterque ita crescere utrumque\n\n \n\net nubis et aquam, quae cumque in nubibus extat,\n\n \n\nut pariter nobis corpus cum sanguine crescit,\n\n \n\nsudor item atque umor qui cumque est denique membris.\n\n \n\nconcipiunt etiam multum quoque saepe marinum\n\n \n\numorem, vel uti pendentia vellera lanae,\n\n \n\ncum supera magnum mare venti nubila portant.\n\n \n\nconsimili ratione ex omnibus amnibus umor\n\n \n\ntollitur in nubis. quo cum bene semina aquarum\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis convenere undique adaucta,\n\n \n\nconfertae nubes umorem mittere certant\n\n \n\ndupliciter; nam vis venti contrudit et ipsa\n\n \n\ncopia nimborum turba maiore coacta\n\n \n\nurget et e supero premit ac facit effluere imbris.\n\n \n\npraeterea cum rarescunt quoque nubila ventis\n\n \n\naut dissolvuntur solis super icta calore,\n\n \n\nmittunt umorem pluvium stillantque, quasi igni\n\n \n\ncera super calido tabescens multa liquescat.\n\n \n\nsed vehemens imber fit, ubi vehementer utraque\n\n \n\nnubila vi cumulata premuntur et impete venti.\n\n \n\nat retinere diu pluviae longumque morari\n\n \n\nconsuerunt, ubi multa cientur semina aquarum\n\n \n\natque aliis aliae nubes nimbique rigantes\n\n \n\ninsuper atque omni vulgo de parte feruntur,\n\n \n\nterraque cum fumans umorem tota redhalat.\n\n \n\nhic ubi sol radiis tempestatem inter opacam\n\n \n\nadversa fulsit nimborum aspargine contra,\n\n \n\ntum color in nigris existit nubibus arqui.\n\n \n\nCetera quae sursum crescunt sursumque creantur,\n\n \n\net quae concrescunt in nubibus, omnia, prorsum\n\n \n\nomnia, nix venti grando gelidaeque pruinae\n\n \n\net vis magna geli, magnum duramen aquarum,\n\n \n\net mora quae fluvios passim refrenat aventis,\n\n \n\nperfacilest tamen haec reperire animoque videre,\n\n \n\nomnia quo pacto fiant quareve creentur,\n\n \n\ncum bene cognoris elementis reddita quae sint.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quae ratio terrai motibus extet\n\n \n\npercipe. et in primis terram fac ut esse rearis\n\n \n\nsupter item ut supera ventosis undique plenam\n\n \n\nspeluncis multosque lacus multasque lucunas\n\n \n\nin gremio gerere et rupes deruptaque saxa;\n\n \n\nmultaque sub tergo terrai flumina tecta\n\n \n\nvolvere vi fluctus summersos ca putandumst;\n\n \n\n undique enim similem esse sui res postulat ipsa.\n\n \n\nhis igitur rebus subiunctis suppositisque\n\n \n\nterra superne tremit magnis concussa ruinis,\n\n \n\nsubter ubi ingentis speluncas subruit aetas;\n\n \n\nquippe cadunt toti montes magnoque repente\n\n \n\nconcussu late disserpunt inde tremores.\n\n \n\net merito, quoniam plaustris concussa tremescunt\n\n \n\ntecta viam propter non magno pondere tota,\n\n \n\nnec minus exultant, si quidvis cumque viai\n\n \n\nferratos utrimque rotarum succutit orbes.\n\n \n\nFit quoque, ubi in magnas aquae vastasque lucunas\n\n \n\ngleba vetustate e terra provolvitur ingens,\n\n \n\nut iactetur aquae fluctu quoque terra vacillans;\n\n \n\nut vas inter  non quit constare, nisi umor\n\n \n\ndestitit in dubio fluctu iactarier intus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea ventus cum per loca subcava terrae\n\n \n\ncollectus parte ex una procumbit et urget\n\n \n\nobnixus magnis speluncas viribus altas,\n\n \n\nincumbit tellus quo venti prona premit vis.\n\n \n\ntum supera terram quae sunt extructa domorum\n\n \n\nad caelumque magis quanto sunt edita quaeque,\n\n \n\ninclinata minent in eandem prodita partem\n\n \n\nprotractaeque trabes inpendent ire paratae.\n\n \n\net metuunt magni naturam credere mundi\n\n \n\nexitiale aliquod tempus clademque manere,\n\n \n\ncum videant tantam terrarum incumbere molem!\n\n \n\nquod nisi respirent venti,  nulla refrenet\n\n \n\nres neque ab exitio possit reprehendere euntis;\n\n \n\nnunc quia respirant alternis inque gravescunt\n\n \n\net quasi collecti redeunt ceduntque repulsi,\n\n \n\nsaepius hanc ob rem minitatur terra ruinas\n\n \n\nquam facit; inclinatur enim retroque recellit\n\n \n\net recipit prolapsa suas in pondere sedes.\n\n \n\nhac igitur ratione vacillant omnia tecta,\n\n \n\nsumma magis mediis, media imis, ima perhilum.\n\n  \n\nEst haec eiusdem quoque magni causa tremoris.\n\n \n\nventus ubi atque animae subito vis maxima quaedam\n\n \n\naut extrinsecus aut ipsa tellure coorta\n\n \n\nin loca se cava terrai coniecit ibique\n\n \n\nspeluncas inter magnas fremit ante tumultu\n\n \n\nversabundaQUE portatur, post incita cum vis\n\n \n\nexagitata foras erumpitur et simul altam\n\n \n\ndiffindens terram magnum concinnat hiatum.\n\n \n\nin Syria Sidone quod accidit et fuit Aegi\n\n \n\nin Peloponneso, quas exitus hic animai\n\n \n\ndisturbat urbes et terrae motus obortus.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea ceciderunt moenia magnis\n\n \n\nmotibus in terris et multae per mare pessum\n\n \n\nsubsedere suis pariter cum civibus urbes.\n\n \n\nquod nisi prorumpit, tamen impetus ipse animai\n\n \n\net fera vis venti per crebra foramina terrae\n\n \n\ndispertitur ut horror et incutit inde tremorem;\n\n \n\nfrigus uti nostros penitus cum venit in artus,\n\n \n\nconcutit invitos cogens tremere atque movere.\n\n \n\nancipiti trepidant igitur terrore per urbis,\n\n \n\n tecta superne timent, metuunt inferne cavernas\n\n \n\nterrai ne dissoluat natura repente,\n\n \n\nneu distracta suum late dispandat hiatum\n\n \n\nidque suis confusa velit complere ruinis.\n\n \n\nproinde licet quamvis caelum terramque reantur\n\n \n\nincorrupta fore aeternae mandata saluti:\n\n \n\net tamen inter dum praesens vis ipsa pericli\n\n \n\nsubdit et hunc stimulum quadam de parte timoris,\n\n \n\nne pedibus raptim tellus subtracta feratur\n\n \n\nin barathrum rerumque sequatur prodita summa\n\n \n\nfunditus et fiat mundi confusa ruina.\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n  \n\nPrincipio mare mirantur non reddere maius\n\n \n\nnaturam, quo sit tantus decursus aquarum,\n\n \n\nomnia quo veniant ex omni flumina parte.\n\n \n\nadde vagos imbris tempestatesque volantes,\n\n \n\nomnia quae maria ac terras sparguntque rigantque;\n\n \n\nadde suos fontis; tamen ad maris omnia summam\n\n \n\nguttai vix instar erunt unius adaugmen;\n\n \n\nquo minus est mirum mare non augescere magnum.\n\n \n\nPraeterea magnam sol partem detrahit aestu.\n\n \n\nquippe videmus enim vestis umore madentis\n\n \n\nexsiccare suis radiis ardentibus solem;\n\n \n\nat pelage multa et late substrata videmus.\n\n \n\nproinde licet quamvis ex uno quoque loco sol\n\n \n\numoris parvam delibet ab aequore partem,\n\n \n\nlargiter in tanto spatio tamen auferet undis.\n\n \n\nTum porro venti quoque magnam tollere partem\n\n \n\numoris possunt verrentes aequora, ventis\n\n \n\nuna nocte vias quoniam persaepe videmus\n\n \n\nsiccari mollisque luti concrescere crustas.\n\n \n\nPraeterea docui multum quoque tollere nubes\n\n \n\numorem magno conceptum ex aequore ponti\n\n \n\net passim toto terrarum spargere in orbi,\n\n \n\ncum pluit in terris et venti nubila portant.\n\n \n\nPostremo quoniam raro cum corpore tellus\n\n \n\nest et coniunctast oras maris undique cingens,\n\n \n\ndebet, ut in mare de terris venit umor aquai,\n\n \n\nin terras itidem manare ex aequore salso;\n\n \n\npercolatur enim virus retroque remanat\n\n \n\nmateries umoris et ad caput amnibus omnis\n\n \n\nconfluit, inde super terras redit agmine dulci\n\n \n\nqua via secta semel liquido pede detulit undas.\n\n  \n\nNunc ratio quae sit, per fauces montis ut Aetnae\n\n \n\nexpirent ignes inter dum turbine tanto,\n\n \n\nexpediam; neque enim mediocri clade coorta\n\n \n\nflammae tempestas Siculum dominata per agros\n\n \n\nfinitimis ad se convertit gentibus ora,\n\n \n\nfumida cum caeli scintillare omnia templa\n\n \n\ncernentes pavida complebant pectora cura,\n\n \n\nquid moliretur rerum natura novarum.\n\n \n\nHisce tibi in rebus latest alteque videndum\n\n \n\net longe cunctas in partis dispiciendum,\n\n \n\nut reminiscaris summam rerum esse profundam\n\n \n\net videas caelum summai totius unum\n\n \n\n quam sit parvula pars et quam multesima constet\n\n \n\nnec tota pars, homo terrai quota totius unus.\n\n \n\nquod bene propositum si plane contueare\n\n \n\nac videas plane, mirari multa relinquas.\n\n \n\nnumquis enim nostrum miratur, siquis in artus\n\n \n\naccepit calido febrim fervore coortam\n\n \n\naut alium quemvis morbi per membra dolorem?\n\n \n\nopturgescit enim subito pes, arripit acer\n\n \n\nsaepe dolor dentes, oculos invadit in ipsos,\n\n \n\nexistit sacer ignis et urit corpore serpens\n\n \n\nquam cumque arripuit partem repitque per artus,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia sunt multarum semina rerum\n\n \n\net satis haec tellus morbi caelumque mali fert,\n\n \n\nunde queat vis immensi procrescere morbi.\n\n \n\nsic igitur toti caelo terraeque putandumst\n\n \n\nex infinito satis omnia suppeditare,\n\n \n\nunde repente queat tellus concussa moveri\n\n \n\nperque mare ac terras rapidus percurrere turbo,\n\n \n\nignis abundare Aetnaeus, flammescere caelum;\n\n \n\nid quoque enim fit et ardescunt caelestia templa\n\n \n\net tempestates pluviae graviore coortu\n\n \n\nsunt, ubi forte ita se tetulerunt semina aquarum.\n\n \n\n'at nimis est ingens incendi turbidus ardor.'\n\n \n\nscilicet et fluvius qui visus maximus ei,\n\n \n\nqui non ante aliquem maiorem vidit, et ingens\n\n \n\narbor homoque videtur et omnia de genere omni\n\n \n\nmaxima quae vidit quisque, haec ingentia fingit,\n\n \n\ncum tamen omnia cum caelo terraque marique\n\n \n\nnil sint ad summam summai totius omnem.\n\n  \n\nNunc tamen illa modis quibus inritata repente\n\n \n\nflamma foras vastis Aetnae fornacibus efflet,\n\n \n\nexpediam. primum totius subcava montis\n\n \n\nest natura fere silicum suffulta cavernis.\n\n \n\nomnibus est porro in speluncis ventus et aeër.\n\n \n\nventus enim fit, ubi est agitando percitus aeër.\n\n \n\nhic ubi percaluit cale fecitque omnia circum\n\n \n\nsaxa furens, qua contingit, terramque et ab ollis\n\n \n\nexcussit calidum flammis velocibus ignem,\n\n \n\ntollit se ac rectis ita faucibus eicit alte.\n\n \n\nfert itaque ardorem longe longeque favillam\n\n \n\ndiffert et crassa volvit caligine fumum\n\n \n\nextruditque simul mirando pondere saxa;\n\n \n\nne dubites quin haec animai turbida sit vis.\n\n \n\npraeterea magna ex parti mare montis ad eius\n\n \n\nradices frangit fluctus aestumque resolvit.\n\n \n\nex hoc usque mari speluncae montis ad altas\n\n \n\nperveniunt subter fauces. hac ire fatendumst\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\net penetrare mari penitus res cogit aperto\n\n \n\natque efflare foras ideoque extollere flammam\n\n \n\nsaxaque subiectare et arenae tollere nimbos.\n\n \n\nin summo sunt vertice enim crateres, ut ipsi\n\n \n\nnominitant, nos quod fauces perhibemus et ora.\n\n  \n\nSunt aliquot quoque res quarum unam dicere causam\n\n \n\nnon satis est, verum pluris, unde una tamen sit;\n\n \n\n corpus ut exanimum siquod procul ipse iacere\n\n \n\nconspicias hominis, fit ut omnis dicere causas\n\n \n\nconveniat leti, dicatur ut illius una;\n\n \n\nnam que eum ferro nec frigore vincere possis\n\n \n\ninteriisse neque a morbo neque forte veneno,\n\n \n\nverum aliquid genere esse ex hoc quod contigit ei\n\n \n\nscimus. item in multis hoc rebus dicere habemus.\n\n \n\nNilus in aestatem crescit campisque redundat\n\n \n\nunicus in terris, Aegypti totius amnis.\n\n \n\nis rigat Aegyptum medium per saepe calorem,\n\n \n\naut quia sunt aestate aquilones ostia contra,\n\n \n\nanni tempore eo, qui etesiae esse feruntur,\n\n \n\net contra fluvium flantes remorantur et undas\n\n \n\ncogentes sursus replent coguntque manere.\n\n \n\nnam dubio procul haec adverso flabra feruntur\n\n \n\nflumine, quae gelidis ab stellis axis aguntur;\n\n \n\nille ex aestifera parti venit amnis ab austro\n\n \n\ninter nigra virum percocto saecla colore\n\n \n\nexoriens penitus media ab regione diei.\n\n \n\nest quoque uti possit magnus congestus harenae\n\n \n\nfluctibus adversis oppilare ostia contra,\n\n \n\ncum mare permotum ventis ruit intus harenam;\n\n \n\nquo fit uti pacto liber minus exitus amnis\n\n \n\net proclivis item fiat minus impetus undis.\n\n \n\nfit quoque uti pluviae forsan magis ad caput ei\n\n \n\ntempore eo fiant, quo etesia flabra aquilonum\n\n \n\nnubila coniciunt in eas tunc omnia partis.\n\n \n\nscilicet, ad mediam regionem eiecta diei\n\n \n\ncum convenerunt, ibi ad altos denique montis\n\n \n\ncontrusae nubes coguntur vique premuntur.\n\n \n\nforsitan Aethiopum penitus de montibus altis\n\n \n\ncrescat, ubi in campos albas descendere ningues\n\n \n\ntabificis subigit radiis sol omnia lustrans.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, Averna tibi quae sint loca cumque lacusque,\n\n \n\nexpediam, quali natura praedita constent.\n\n \n\nprincipio, quod Averna vocantur nomine, id ab re\n\n \n\ninpositumst, quia sunt avibus contraria cunctis,\n\n \n\ne regione ea quod loca cum venere volantes,\n\n \n\nremigii oblitae pennarum vela remittunt\n\n \n\npraecipitesque cadunt molli cervice profusae\n\n \n\nin terram, si forte ita fert natura locorum,\n\n \n\naut in aquam, si forte lacus substratus Averni.\n\n \n\nis locus est Cumas aput, acri sulpure montis\n\n \n\noppleti calidis ubi fumant fontibus aucti.\n\n \n\nest et Athenaeis in moenibus, arcis in ipso\n\n \n\nvertice, Palladis ad templum Tritonidis almae,\n\n \n\nquo numquam pennis appellunt corpora raucae\n\n \n\ncornices, non cum fumant altaria donis;\n\n \n\nusque adeo fugitant non iras Palladis acris\n\n \n\npervigili causa, Graium ut cecinere poeëtae,\n\n \n\nsed natura loci opus efficit ipsa suapte.\n\n \n\nin Syria quoque fertur item locus esse videri,\n\n \n\nquadripedes quoque quo simul ac vestigia primum\n\n \n\nintulerint, graviter vis cogat concidere ipsa,\n\n \n\nmanibus ut si sint divis mactata repente.\n\n \n\n omnia quae naturali ratione geruntur,\n\n \n\net quibus e fiant causis apparet origo;\n\n \n\nianua ne pote eis Orci regionibus esse\n\n \n\ncredatur, post hinc animas Acheruntis in oras\n\n \n\nducere forte deos manis inferne reamur,\n\n \n\nnaribus alipedes ut cervi saepe putantur\n\n \n\nducere de latebris serpentia saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\nquod procul a vera quam sit ratione repulsum\n\n \n\npercipe; nam de re nunc ipsa dicere conor.\n\n  \n\nPrincipio hoc dico, quod dixi saepe quoque ante,\n\n \n\nin terra cuiusque modi rerum esse figuras;\n\n \n\nmulta, cibo quae sunt, vitalia multaque, morbos\n\n \n\nincutere et mortem quae possint adcelerare.\n\n \n\net magis esse aliis alias animantibus aptas\n\n \n\nres ad vitai rationem ostendimus ante\n\n \n\npropter dissimilem naturam dissimilisque\n\n \n\ntexturas inter sese primasque figuras.\n\n \n\nmulta meant inimica per auris, multa per ipsas\n\n \n\ninsinuant naris infesta atque aspera tactu,\n\n \n\nnec sunt multa parum tactu vitanda neque autem\n\n \n\naspectu fugienda saporeque tristia quae sint.\n\n \n\nDeinde videre licet quam multae sint homini res\n\n \n\nacriter infesto sensu spurcaeque gravisque;\n\n \n\narboribus primum certis gravis umbra tributa\n\n \n\nusque adeo, capitis faciant ut saepe dolores,\n\n \n\nsiquis eas subter iacuit prostratus in herbis.\n\n \n\nest etiam magnis Heliconis montibus arbos\n\n \n\nfloris odore hominem taetro consueta necare.\n\n \n\nscilicet haec ideo terris ex omnia surgunt,\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis multarum semina rerum\n\n \n\nquod permixta gerit tellus discretaque tradit.\n\n \n\nnocturnumque recens extinctum lumen ubi acri\n\n \n\nnidore offendit nares, consopit ibidem,\n\n \n\nconcidere et spumas qui morbo mittere suevit.\n\n \n\ncastoreoque gravi mulier sopita recumbit,\n\n \n\net manibus nitidum teneris opus effluit ei,\n\n \n\ntempore eo si odoratast quo menstrua solvit.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea languentia membra per artus\n\n \n\nsolvunt atque animam labefactant sedibus intus.\n\n \n\ndenique si calidis etiam cunctere lavabris\n\n \n\nplenior et lueris, solio ferventis aquai\n\n \n\nquam facile in medio fit uti des saepe ruinas!\n\n \n\ncarbonumque gravis vis atque odor insinuatur\n\n \n\nquam facile in cerebrum, nisi aqua praecepimus ante!\n\n \n\nat cum membra domans percepit fervida febris,\n\n \n\ntum fit odor vini plagae mactabilis instar.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam terra quoque sulpur in ipsa\n\n \n\ngignier et taetro concrescere odore bitumen,\n\n \n\ndenique ubi argenti venas aurique secuntur,\n\n \n\nterrai penitus scrutantes abdita ferro,\n\n \n\nqualis expiret Scaptensula subter odores?\n\n \n\nquidve mali fit ut exalent aurata metalla!\n\n \n\nquas hominum reddunt facies qualisque colores!\n\n \n\nnonne vides audisve perire in tempore parvo\n\n \n\nquam soleant et quam vitai copia desit,\n\n \n\n quos opere in tali cohibet vis magna necessis?\n\n \n\nhos igitur tellus omnis exaestuat aestus\n\n \n\nexpiratque foras in apertum promptaque caeli.\n\n  \n\nSic et Averna loca alitibus summittere debent\n\n \n\nmortiferam vim. de terra quae surgit in auras,\n\n \n\nut spatium caeli quadam de parte venenet;\n\n \n\nquo simul ac primum pennis delata sit ales,\n\n \n\nimpediatur ibi caeco correpta veneno,\n\n \n\nut cadat e regione loci, qua derigit aestus.\n\n \n\nquo cum conruit, hic eadem vis illius aestus\n\n \n\nreliquias vitae membris ex omnibus aufert.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim primo quasi quendam conciet aestum;\n\n \n\nposterius fit uti. cum iam cecidere veneni\n\n \n\nin fontis ipsos, ibi sit quoque vita vomenda,\n\n \n\npropterea quod magna mali fit copia circum.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut inter dum vis haec atque aestus Averni\n\n \n\naeëra, qui inter avis cumquest terramque locatus.\n\n \n\ndiscutiat, prope uti locus hic linquatur inanis.\n\n \n\ncuius ubi e regione loci venere volantis,\n\n \n\nclaudicat extemplo pinnarum nisus inanis\n\n \n\net conamen utrimque alarum proditur omne.\n\n \n\nhic ubi nixari nequeunt insistereque alis,\n\n \n\nscilicet in terram delabi pondere cogit\n\n \n\nnatura, et vacuum prope iam per inane iacentes\n\n \n\ndispergunt animas per caulas corporis omnis.\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n  \n\nfrigidior porro in puteis aestate fit umor,\n\n \n\narescit quia terra calore et semina si qua\n\n \n\nforte vaporis habet proprie, dimittit in auras.\n\n \n\nquo magis est igitur tellus effeta calore,\n\n \n\nfit quoque frigidior qui in terrast abditus umor.\n\n \n\nfrigore cum premitur porro omnis terra coitque\n\n \n\net quasi concrescit, fit scilicet ut coeundo\n\n \n\nexprimat in puteos si quem gerit ipsa calorem.\n\n \n\nEsse apud Hammonis fanum fons luce diurna\n\n \n\nfrigidus et calidus nocturno tempore fertur.\n\n \n\nhunc homines fontem nimis admirantur et acri\n\n \n\nsole putant subter terras fervescere partim,\n\n \n\nnox ubi terribili terras caligine texit.\n\n \n\nquod nimis a verast longe ratione remotum.\n\n \n\nquippe ubi sol nudum contractans corpus aquai\n\n \n\nnon quierit calidum supera de reddere parte,\n\n \n\ncum superum lumen tanto fervore fruatur,\n\n \n\nqui queat hic supter tam crasso corpore terram\n\n \n\nperquoquere umorem et calido focilare vapore?\n\n \n\npraesertim cum vix possit per saepta domorum\n\n \n\ninsinuare suum radiis ardentibus aestum.\n\n \n\nquae ratiost igitur? ni mirum terra magis quod\n\n \n\nrara tenet circum fontem quam cetera tellus\n\n \n\nmultaque sunt ignis prope semina corpus aquai.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi roriferis terram nox obruit undis,\n\n \n\nextemplo penitus frigescit terra coitque.\n\n \n\nhac ratione fit ut, tam quam compressa manu sit,\n\n \n\nexprimat in fontem quae semina cumque habet ignis,\n\n \n\nquae calidum faciunt laticis tactum atque vaporem.\n\n \n\n inde ubi sol radiis terram dimovit obortus\n\n \n\net rare fecit calido miscente vapore,\n\n \n\nrursus in antiquas redeunt primordia sedes\n\n \n\nignis et in terram cedit calor omnis aquai.\n\n \n\nfrigidus hanc ob rem fit fons in luce diurna.\n\n \n\npraeterea solis radiis iactatur aquai\n\n \n\numor et in lucem tremulo rarescit ab aestu;\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti quae semina cumque habet ignis\n\n \n\ndimittat; quasi saepe gelum, quod continet in se,\n\n \n\nmittit et exsolvit glaciem nodosque relaxat.\n\n  \n\nFrigidus est etiam fons, supra quem sita saepe\n\n \n\nstuppa iacit flammam concepto protinus igni,\n\n \n\ntaedaque consimili ratione accensa per undas\n\n \n\nconlucet, quo cumque natans impellitur auris.\n\n \n\nni mirum quia sunt in aqua permulta vaporis\n\n \n\nsemina de terraque necessest funditus ipsa\n\n \n\nignis corpora per totum consurgere fontem\n\n \n\net simul exspirare foras exireque in auras,\n\n \n\nnon ita multa tamen, calidus queat ut fieri fons;\n\n \n\npraeterea dispersa foras erumpere cogit\n\n \n\nvis per aquam subito sursumque ea conciliari.\n\n \n\nquod genus endo marist Aradi fons, dulcis aquai\n\n \n\nqui scatit et salsas circum se dimovet undas;\n\n \n\net multis aliis praebet regionibus aequor\n\n \n\nutilitatem opportunam sitientibus nautis,\n\n \n\nquod dulcis inter salsas intervomit undas.\n\n \n\nsic igitur per eum possunt erumpere fontem\n\n \n\net scatere illa foras; in stuppam semina quae cum\n\n \n\nconveniunt aut in taedai corpore adhaerent,\n\n \n\nardescunt facile extemplo, quia multa quoque in se\n\n \n\nsemina habent ignis stuppae taedaeque tenentes.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam, nocturna ad lumina linum\n\n \n\nnuper ubi extinctum admoveas, accendier ante\n\n \n\nquam tetigit flammam, taedamque pari ratione?\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea prius ipso tacta vapore\n\n \n\neminus ardescunt quam comminus imbuat ignis.\n\n \n\nhoc igitur fieri quoque in illo fonte putandumst.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, agere incipiam quo foedere fiat\n\n \n\nnaturae, lapis hic ut ferrum ducere possit,\n\n \n\nquem Magneta vocant patrio de nomine Grai,\n\n \n\nMagnetum quia sit patriis in finibus ortus.\n\n \n\nhunc homines lapidem mirantur; quippe catenam\n\n \n\nsaepe ex anellis reddit pendentibus ex se.\n\n \n\nquinque etenim licet inter dum pluresque videre\n\n \n\nordine demisso levibus iactarier auris,\n\n \n\nunus ubi ex uno dependet supter adhaerens\n\n \n\nex alioque alius lapidis vim vinclaque noscit;\n\n \n\nusque adeo permananter vis pervalet eius.\n\n \n\nHoc genus in rebus firmandumst multa prius quam\n\n \n\nipsius rei rationem reddere possis,\n\n \n\net nimium longis ambagibus est adeundum;\n\n \n\nquo magis attentas auris animumque reposco.\n\n \n\nPrincipio omnibus ab rebus, quas cumque videmus,\n\n \n\nperpetuo fluere ac mitti spargique necessest\n\n \n\ncorpora quae feriant oculos visumque lacessant.\n\n \n\n perpetuoque fluunt certis ab rebus odores;\n\n \n\nfrigus ut  fluviis, calor a sole, aestus ab undis\n\n \n\naequoris, exesor moerorum, litora propter;\n\n \n\nnec varii cessant sonitus manare per auras;\n\n \n\ndenique in os salsi venit umor saepe saporis,\n\n \n\ncum mare versamur propter, dilutaque contra\n\n \n\ncum tuimur misceri absinthia, tangit amaror.\n\n \n\nusque adeo omnibus ab rebus res quaeque fluenter\n\n \n\nfertur et in cunctas dimittitur undique partis\n\n \n\nnec mora nec requies interdatur ulla fluendi,\n\n \n\nperpetuo quoniam sentimus et omnia semper\n\n \n\ncernere odorari licet et sentire sonare.\n\n  \n\nNunc omnis repetam quam raro corpore sint res\n\n \n\ncommemorare; quod in primo quoque carmine claret.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim, quamquam multas hoc pertinet ad res\n\n \n\nnoscere, cum primis hanc ad rem protinus ipsam,\n\n \n\nqua de disserere adgredior, firmare necessest\n\n \n\nnil esse in promptu nisi mixtum corpus inani.\n\n \n\nprincipio fit ut in speluncis saxa superna\n\n \n\nsudent umore et guttis manantibus stillent.\n\n \n\nmanat item nobis e toto corpore sudor,\n\n \n\ncrescit barba pilique per omnia membra, per artus.\n\n \n\ndiditur in venas cibus omnis, auget alitque\n\n \n\ncorporis extremas quoque partis unguiculosque.\n\n \n\nfrigus item transire per aes calidumque vaporem\n\n \n\nsentimus, sentimus item transire per aurum\n\n \n\natque per argentum, cum pocula plena tenemus.\n\n \n\ndenique per dissaepta domorum saxea voces\n\n \n\npervolitant, permanat odor frigusque vaposque\n\n \n\nignis, qui ferri quoque vim penetrare sueëvit,\n\n \n\ndenique qua circum caeli lorica coeërcet,\n\n \n\nmorbida visque simul, cum extrinsecus insinuatur;\n\n \n\net tempestate in terra caeloque coorta\n\n \n\nin caelum terrasque remotae iure facessunt;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem nihil est nisi raro corpore nexum.\n\n  \n\nHuc accedit uti non omnia, quae iaciuntur\n\n \n\ncorpora cumque ab rebus, eodem praedita sensu\n\n \n\natque eodem pacto rebus sint omnibus apta.\n\n \n\nprincipio terram sol excoquit et facit are,\n\n \n\nat glaciem dissolvit et altis montibus altas\n\n \n\nextructas nives radiis tabescere cogit;\n\n \n\ndenique cera lique fit in eius posta vapore.\n\n \n\nignis item liquidum facit aes aurumque resolvit,\n\n \n\nat coria et carnem trahit et conducit in unum.\n\n \n\numor aquae porro ferrum condurat ab igni,\n\n \n\nat coria et carnem mollit durata calore.\n\n \n\nbarbigeras oleaster eo iuvat usque capellas,\n\n \n\neffluat ambrosias quasi vero et nectare tinctus;\n\n \n\nqua nihil est homini quod amarius fronde ac extet.\n\n \n\ndenique amaracinum fugitat sus et timet omne\n\n \n\nunguentum; nam saetigeris subus acre venenumst;\n\n \n\nquod nos inter dum tam quam recreare videtur.\n\n \n\nat contra nobis caenum taeterrima cum sit\n\n \n\nspurcities, eadem subus haec iucunda videtur,\n\n \n\ninsatiabiliter toti ut volvantur ibidem.\n\n  \n\n Hoc etiam super est, ipsa quam dicere de re\n\n \n\nadgredior, quod dicendum prius esse videtur.\n\n \n\nmulta foramina cum variis sint reddita rebus,\n\n \n\ndissimili inter se natura praedita debent\n\n \n\nesse et habere suam naturam quaeque viasque.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim varii sensus animantibus insunt,\n\n \n\nquorum quisque suam proprie rem percipit in se;\n\n \n\nnam penetrare alio sonitus alioque saporem\n\n \n\ncernimus e sucis, alio nidoris odores.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\npraeterea manare aliud per saxa videtur,\n\n \n\natque aliud lignis, aliud transire per aurum,\n\n \n\nargentoque foras aliud vitroque meare;\n\n \n\nnam fluere hac species, illac calor ire videtur,\n\n \n\natque aliis aliud citius transmittere eadem.\n\n \n\nscilicet id fieri cogit natura viarum\n\n \n\nmultimodis varians, ut paulo ostendimus ante,\n\n \n\npropter dissimilem naturam textaque rerum.\n\n \n\nQua propter, bene ubi haec confirmata atque locata\n\n \n\nomnia constiterint nobis praeposta parata,\n\n \n\nquod super est, facile hinc ratio reddetur et omnis\n\n \n\ncausa pate fiet, quae ferri pelliciat vim.\n\n  \n\nPrincipio fluere e lapide hoc permulta necessest\n\n \n\nsemina sive aestum, qui discutit aeëra plagis,\n\n \n\ninter qui lapidem ferrumque est cumque locatus.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi inanitur spatium multusque vace fit\n\n \n\nin medio locus, extemplo primordia ferri\n\n \n\nin vacuum prolapsa cadunt coniuncta, fit utque\n\n \n\nanulus ipse sequatur eatque ita corpore toto.\n\n \n\nnec res ulla magis primoribus ex elementis\n\n \n\nindupedita suis arte conexa cohaeret\n\n \n\nquam validi ferri natura et frigidus horror.\n\n \n\nquo minus est mirum, quod dicitur esse alienum,\n\n \n\ncorpora si nequeunt e ferro plura coorta\n\n \n\nin vacuum ferri, quin anulus ipse sequatur;\n\n \n\nquod facit et sequitur, donec pervenit ad ipsum\n\n \n\niam lapidem caecisque in eo compagibus haesit.\n\n \n\nhoc fit idem cunctas in partis; unde vace fit\n\n \n\ncumque locus, sive e transverso sive superne,\n\n \n\ncorpora continuo in vacuum vicina feruntur;\n\n \n\nquippe agitantur enim plagis aliunde nec ipsa\n\n \n\nsponte sua sursum possunt consurgere in auras.\n\n \n\nhuc accedit item, quare queat id magis esse,\n\n \n\nhaec quoque res adiumento motuque iuvatur,\n\n \n\nquod, simul a fronte est anelli rarior aeër\n\n \n\nfactus inanitusque locus magis ac vacuatus,\n\n \n\ncontinuo fit uti qui post est cumque locatus\n\n \n\naeër a tergo quasi provehat atque propellat.\n\n \n\nsemper enim circum positus res verberat aeër;\n\n \n\nsed tali fit uti propellat tempore ferrum,\n\n \n\nparte quod ex una spatium vacat et capit in se.\n\n \n\nhic, tibi quem memoro, per crebra foramina ferri\n\n \n\nparvas ad partis subtiliter insinuatus\n\n \n\ntrudit et inpellit, quasi navem velaque ventus.\n\n \n\n denique res omnes debent in corpore habere\n\n \n\naeëra, quandoquidem raro sunt corpore et aeër\n\n \n\nomnibus est rebus circum datus adpositusque.\n\n \n\nhic igitur, penitus qui in ferrost abditus aeër,\n\n \n\nsollicito motu semper iactatur eoque\n\n \n\nverberat anellum dubio procul et ciet intus,\n\n \n\nscilicet illo eodem fertur, quo praecipitavit\n\n \n\niam semel et partem in vacuam conamina sumpsit.\n\n  \n\nFit quoque ut a lapide hoc ferri natura recedat\n\n \n\ninter dum, fugere atque sequi consueta vicissim.\n\n \n\nexultare etiam Samothracia ferrea vidi\n\n \n\net ramenta simul ferri furere intus ahenis\n\n \n\nin scaphiis, lapis hic Magnes cum subditus esset;\n\n \n\nusque adeo fugere a saxo gestire videtur.\n\n \n\naere interposito discordia tanta creatur\n\n \n\npropterea quia ni mirum prius aestus ubi aeris\n\n \n\npraecepit ferrique vias possedit apertas,\n\n \n\nposterior lapidis venit aestus et omnia plena\n\n \n\ninvenit in ferro neque habet qua tranet ut ante;\n\n \n\ncogitur offensare igitur pulsareque fluctu\n\n \n\nferrea texta suo; quo pacto respuit ab se\n\n \n\natque per aes agitat, sine eo quod saepe resorbet.\n\n \n\nIllud in his rebus mirari mitte, quod aestus\n\n \n\nnon valet e lapide hoc alias impellere item res.\n\n \n\npondere enim fretae partim stant, quod genus aurum;\n\n \n\nat partim raro quia sunt cum corpore, ut aestus\n\n \n\npervolet intactus, nequeunt inpellier usquam,\n\n \n\nlignea materies in quo genere esse videtur.\n\n \n\ninterutrasque igitur ferri natura locata\n\n \n\naeris ubi accepit quaedam corpuscula, tum fit,\n\n \n\ninpellant ut eo Magnesia flumine saxa.\n\n  \n\nnec tamen haec ita sunt aliarum rerum aliena,\n\n \n\nut mihi multa parum genere ex hoc suppeditentur,\n\n \n\nquae memorare queam inter se singlariter apta.\n\n \n\nsaxa vides primum sola colescere calce.\n\n \n\nglutine materies taurino iungitur una,\n\n \n\nut vitio venae tabularum saepius hiscant\n\n \n\nquam laxare queant compages taurea vincla.\n\n \n\nvitigeni latices aquai fontibus audent\n\n \n\nmisceri, cum pix nequeat gravis et leve olivom.\n\n \n\npurpureusque colos conchyli iungitur uno\n\n \n\ncorpore cum lanae, dirimi qui non queat usquam,\n\n \n\nnon si Neptuni fluctu renovare operam des,\n\n \n\nnon mare si totum velit eluere omnibus undis.\n\n \n\ndenique res auro non aurum copulat una,\n\n \n\naerique  plumbo fit uti iungatur ab albo?\n\n \n\ncetera iam quam multa licet reperire! quid ergo?\n\n \n\nnec tibi tam longis opus est ambagibus usquam\n\n \n\nnec me tam multam hic operam consumere par est,\n\n \n\nsed breviter paucis praestat comprendere multa.\n\n \n\nquorum ita texturae ceciderunt mutua contra,\n\n \n\nut cava conveniant plenis haec illius illa\n\n \n\nhuiusque inter se, iunctura haec optima constat.\n\n \n\nest etiam, quasi ut anellis hamisque plicata\n\n \n\ninter se quaedam possint coplata teneri;\n\n \n\n quod magis in lapide hoc fieri ferroque videtur.\n\n  \n\nNunc ratio quae sit morbis aut unde repente\n\n \n\nmortiferam possit cladem conflare coorta\n\n \n\nmorbida vis hominum generi pecudumque catervis,\n\n \n\nexpediam, primum multarum semina rerum\n\n \n\nesse supra docui quae sint vitalia nobis,\n\n \n\net contra quae sint morbo mortique necessest\n\n \n\nmulta volare; ea cum casu sunt forte coorta\n\n \n\net perturbarunt caelum, fit morbidus aeër.\n\n \n\natque ea vis omnis morborum pestilitasque\n\n \n\naut extrinsecus ut nubes nebulaeque superne\n\n \n\nper caelum veniunt aut ipsa saepe coorta\n\n \n\nde terra surgunt, ubi putorem umida nactast\n\n \n\nintempestivis pluviisque et solibus icta.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam caeli novitate et aquarum\n\n \n\ntemptari procul a patria qui cumque domoque\n\n \n\nadveniunt ideo quia longe discrepitant res?\n\n \n\nnam quid Brittannis caelum differre putamus,\n\n \n\net quod in Aegypto est, qua mundi claudicat axis,\n\n \n\nquidve quod in Ponto est differre et Gadibus atque\n\n \n\nusque ad nigra virum percocto saecla colore?\n\n \n\nquae cum quattuor inter se diversa videmus\n\n \n\nquattuor a ventis et caeli partibus esse,\n\n \n\ntum color et facies hominum distare videntur\n\n \n\nlargiter et morbi generatim saecla tenere.\n\n \n\nest elephas morbus qui propter flumina Nili\n\n \n\ngignitur Aegypto in media neque praeterea usquam.\n\n \n\nAtthide temptantur gressus oculique in Achaeis\n\n \n\nfinibus. inde aliis alius locus est inimicus\n\n \n\npartibus ac membris; varius concinnat id aeër.\n\n \n\nproinde ubi se caelum, quod nobis forte alienum,\n\n \n\ncommovet atque aeër inimicus serpere coepit,\n\n \n\nut nebula ac nubes paulatim repit et omne\n\n \n\nqua graditur conturbat et immutare coactat,\n\n \n\nfit quoque ut, in nostrum cum venit denique caelum,\n\n \n\ncorrumpat reddatque sui simile atque alienum.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur subito clades nova pestilitasque\n\n \n\naut in aquas cadit aut fruges persidit in ipsas\n\n \n\naut alios hominum pastus pecudumque cibatus,\n\n \n\naut etiam suspensa manet vis aeëre in ipso\n\n \n\net, cum spirantes mixtas hinc ducimus auras,\n\n \n\nilla quoque in corpus pariter sorbere necessest.\n\n \n\nconsimili ratione venit bubus quoque saepe\n\n \n\npestilitas et iam pigris balantibus aegror.\n\n \n\nnec refert utrum nos in loca deveniamus\n\n \n\nnobis adversa et caeli mutemus amictum,\n\n \n\nan caelum nobis ultro natura corumptum\n\n \n\ndeferat aut aliquid quo non consuevimus uti,\n\n \n\nquod nos adventu possit temptare recenti.\n\n  \n\nHaec ratio quondam morborum et mortifer aestus\n\n \n\nfinibus in Cecropis funestos reddidit agros\n\n \n\nvastavitque vias, exhausit civibus urbem.\n\n \n\nnam penitus veniens Aegypti finibus ortus,\n\n \n\naeëra permensus multum camposque natantis,\n\n \n\nincubuit tandem populo Pandionis omni.\n\n \n\n inde catervatim morbo mortique dabantur.\n\n \n\nprincipio caput incensum fervore gerebant\n\n \n\net duplicis oculos suffusa luce rubentes.\n\n \n\nsudabant etiam fauces intrinsecus atrae\n\n \n\nsanguine et ulceribus vocis via saepta coibat\n\n \n\natque animi interpres manabat lingua cruore\n\n \n\ndebilitata malis, motu gravis, aspera tactu.\n\n \n\ninde ubi per fauces pectus complerat et ipsum\n\n \n\nmorbida vis in cor maestum confluxerat aegris,\n\n \n\nomnia tum vero vitai claustra lababant.\n\n \n\nspiritus ore foras taetrum volvebat odorem,\n\n \n\nrancida quo perolent proiecta cadavera ritu.\n\n \n\natque animi prorsum  vires totius, omne\n\n \n\nlanguebat corpus leti iam limine in ipso.\n\n \n\nintolerabilibusque malis erat anxius angor\n\n \n\nadsidue comes et gemitu commixta querella,\n\n \n\nsingultusque frequens noctem per saepe diemque\n\n \n\ncorripere adsidue nervos et membra coactans\n\n \n\ndissoluebat eos, defessos ante, fatigans.\n\n \n\nnec nimio cuiquam posses ardore tueri\n\n \n\ncorporis in summo summam fervescere partem,\n\n \n\nsed potius tepidum manibus proponere tactum\n\n \n\net simul ulceribus quasi inustis omne rubere\n\n \n\ncorpus, ut est per membra sacer dum diditur ignis.\n\n \n\nintima pars hominum vero flagrabat ad ossa,\n\n \n\nflagrabat stomacho flamma ut fornacibus intus.\n\n \n\nnil adeo posses cuiquam leve tenveque membris\n\n \n\nvertere in utilitatem, at ventum et frigora semper.\n\n \n\nin fluvios partim gelidos ardentia morbo\n\n \n\nmembra dabant nudum iacientes corpus in undas.\n\n  \n\nmulti praecipites nymphis putealibus alte\n\n \n\ninciderunt ipso venientes ore patente:\n\n \n\ninsedabiliter sitis arida corpora mersans\n\n \n\naequabat multum parvis umoribus imbrem.\n\n \n\nnec requies erat ulla mali: defessa iacebant\n\n \n\ncorpora. mussabat tacito medicina timore,\n\n \n\nquippe patentia cum totiens ardentia morbis\n\n \n\nlumina versarent oculorum expertia somno.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea mortis tum signa dabantur:\n\n \n\nperturbata animi mens in maerore metuque,\n\n \n\ntriste supercilium, furiosus voltus et acer,\n\n \n\nsollicitae porro plenaeque sonoribus aures,\n\n \n\ncreber spiritus aut ingens raroque coortus,\n\n \n\nsudorisque madens per collum splendidus umor,\n\n \n\ntenvia sputa minuta, croci contacta colore\n\n \n\nsalsaque per fauces rauca vix edita tussi.\n\n \n\nin manibus vero nervi trahere et tremere artus\n\n \n\na pedibusque minutatim succedere frigus\n\n \n\nnon dubitabat. item ad supremum denique tempus\n\n \n\nconpressae nares, nasi primoris acumen\n\n \n\ntenve, cavati oculi, cava tempora, frigida pellis\n\n \n\nduraque in ore, iacens rictu, frons tenta manebat.\n\n \n\nnec nimio rigida post artus morte iacebant.\n\n \n\noctavoque fere candenti lumine solis\n\n \n\naut etiam nona reddebant lampade vitam.\n\n \n\n quorum siquis, ut est, vitarat funera leti,\n\n \n\nulceribus taetris et nigra proluvie alvi\n\n \n\nposterius tamen hunc tabes letumque manebat,\n\n \n\naut etiam multus capitis cum saepe dolore\n\n \n\ncorruptus sanguis expletis naribus ibat.\n\n \n\nhuc hominis totae vires corpusque fluebat.\n\n  \n\nprofluvium porro qui taetri sanguinis acre\n\n \n\nexierat, tamen in nervos huic morbus et artus\n\n \n\nibat et in partis genitalis corporis ipsas.\n\n \n\net graviter partim metuentes limina leti\n\n \n\nvivebant ferro privati parte virili,\n\n \n\net manibus sine non nulli pedibusque manebant\n\n \n\nin vita tamen et perdebant lumina partim.\n\n \n\nusque adeo mortis metus iis incesserat acer.\n\n \n\natque etiam quosdam cepere oblivia rerum\n\n \n\ncunctarum, neque se possent cognoscere ut ipsi.\n\n \n\nmultaque humi cum inhumata iacerent corpora supra\n\n \n\ncorporibus, tamen alituum genus atque ferarum\n\n \n\naut procul absiliebat, ut acrem exiret odorem,\n\n \n\naut, ubi gustarat, languebat morte propinqua.\n\n \n\nnec tamen omnino temere illis solibus ulla\n\n \n\ncomparebat avis, nec tristia saecla ferarum\n\n \n\nexibant silvis. languebant pleraque morbo\n\n \n\net moriebantur. cum primis fida canum vis\n\n \n\nstrata viis animam ponebat in omnibus aegre;\n\n \n\nextorquebat enim vitam vis morbida membris.\n\n \n\nincomitata rapi certabant funera vasta\n\n \n\nnec ratio remedii communis certa dabatur;\n\n \n\nnam quod ali dederat vitalis aeëris auras\n\n \n\nvolvere in ore licere et caeli templa tueri,\n\n \n\nhoc aliis erat exitio letumque parabat.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus miserandum magnopere unum\n\n \n\naerumnabile erat, quod ubi se quisque videbat\n\n \n\nimplicitum morbo, morti damnatus ut esset,\n\n \n\ndeficiens animo maesto cum corde iacebat,\n\n \n\nfunera respectans animam amittebat ibidem.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim nullo cessabant tempore apisci\n\n \n\nex aliis alios avidi contagia morbi,\n\n \n\nlanigeras tam quam pecudes et bucera saecla,\n\n \n\nidque vel in primis cumulabat funere funus\n\n \n\nnam qui cumque suos fugitabant visere ad aegros,\n\n \n\nvitai nimium cupidos mortisque timentis\n\n \n\npoenibat paulo post turpi morte malaque,\n\n \n\ndesertos, opis expertis, incuria mactans.\n\n \n\nqui fuerant autem praesto, contagibus ibant\n\n \n\natque labore, pudor quem tum cogebat obire\n\n \n\nblandaque lassorum vox mixta voce querellae.\n\n \n\noptimus hoc leti genus ergo quisque subibat.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea iam pastor et armentarius omnis\n\n \n\net robustus item curvi moderator aratri\n\n \n\nlanguebat, penitusque casa contrusa iacebant\n\n \n\ncorpora paupertate et morbo dedita morti.\n\n \n\nexanimis pueris super exanimata parentum\n\n \n\ncorpora non numquam posses retroque videre\n\n \n\nmatribus et patribus natos super edere vitam.\n\n \n\n nec minimam partem ex agris maeror is in urbem\n\n \n\nconfluxit, languens quem contulit agricolarum\n\n \n\ncopia conveniens ex omni morbida parte.\n\n \n\nomnia conplebant loca tectaque quo magis aestu,\n\n \n\nconfertos ita acervatim mors accumulabat.\n\n \n\nmulta siti prostrata viam per proque voluta\n\n \n\ncorpora silanos ad aquarum strata iacebant\n\n \n\ninterclusa anima nimia ab dulcedine aquarum,\n\n \n\nmultaque per populi passim loca prompta viasque\n\n \n\nlanguida semanimo cum corpore membra videres\n\n \n\nhorrida paedore et pannis cooperta perire,\n\n \n\ncorporis inluvie, pelli super ossibus una,\n\n \n\nulceribus taetris prope iam sordeque sepulta.\n\n \n\nomnia denique sancta deum delubra replerat\n\n \n\ncorporibus mors exanimis onerataque passim\n\n \n\ncuncta cadaveribus caelestum templa manebant,\n\n \n\nhospitibus loca quae complerant aedituentes.\n\n \n\nnec iam religio divom nec numina magni\n\n \n\npendebantur enim: praesens dolor exsuperabat.\n\n \n\nnec mos ille sepulturae remanebat in urbe,\n\n \n\nquo prius hic populus semper consuerat humari;\n\n \n\nperturbatus enim totus trepidabat et unus\n\n \n\nquisque suum pro re  maestus humabat.\n\n \n\nmultaque  subita et paupertas horrida suasit;\n\n \n\nnamque suos consanguineos aliena rogorum\n\n \n\ninsuper extructa ingenti clamore locabant\n\n \n\nsubdebantque faces, multo cum sanguine saepe\n\n \n\nrixantes, potius quam corpora desererentur,\n\n \n\ninque aliis alium populum sepelire suorum\n\n \n\ncertantes; lacrimis lassi luctuque redibant;\n\n \n\ninde bonam partem in lectum maerore dabantur;\n\n \n\nnec poterat quisquam reperiri, quem neque morbus\n\n \n\nnec mors nec luctus temptaret tempore tali.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","text":"## Liber Sextus\n\n  \n\nPrimae frugiparos fetus mortalibus aegris\n\n \n\ndididerunt quondam praeclaro nomine Athenae\n\n \n\net recreaverunt vitam legesque rogarunt\n\n \n\net primae dederunt solacia dulcia vitae,\n\n \n\ncum genuere virum tali cum corde repertum,\n\n \n\nomnia veridico qui quondam ex ore profudit;\n\n \n\ncuius et extincti propter divina reperta\n\n \n\ndivolgata vetus iam ad caelum gloria fertur.\n\n \n\nnam cum vidit hic ad victum quae flagitat usus\n\n \n\nomnia iam ferme mortalibus esse parata\n\n \n\net, pro quam possent, vitam consistere tutam,\n\n \n\ndivitiis homines et honore et laude potentis\n\n \n\naffluere atque bona gnatorum excellere fama,\n\n \n\nnec minus esse domi cuiquam tamen anxia cordi,\n\n \n\natque animi ingratis vitam vexare sine ulla\n\n \n\npausa atque infestis cogi saevire querellis,\n\n \n\nintellegit ibi vitium vas efficere ipsum\n\n \n\nomniaque illius vitio corrumpier intus,\n\n \n\nquae conlata foris et commoda cumque venirent;\n\n \n\npartim quod fluxum pertusumque esse videbat,\n\n \n\nut nulla posset ratione explerier umquam,\n\n \n\npartim quod taetro quasi conspurcare sapore\n\n \n\nomnia cernebat, quae cumque receperat, intus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","text":"veridicis igitur purgavit pectora dictis\n\n \n\net finem statuit cuppedinis atque timoris\n\n \n\nexposuitque bonum summum, quo tendimus omnes,\n\n \n\nquid foret, atque viam monstravit, tramite parvo\n\n \n\nqua possemus ad id recto contendere cursu,\n\n \n\nquidve mali foret in rebus mortalibus passim,\n\n \n\nquod fieret naturali varieque volaret\n\n \n\nseu casu seu vi, quod sic natura parasset,\n\n \n\net quibus e portis occurri cuique deceret,\n\n \n\net genus humanum frustra plerumque probavit\n\n \n\nvolvere curarum tristis in pectore fluctus.\n\n \n\nnam vel uti pueri trepidant atque omnia caecis\n\n \n\nin tenebris metuunt, sic nos in luce timemus\n\n \n\ninter dum, nihilo quae sunt metuenda magis quam\n\n \n\nquae pueri in tenebris pavitant finguntque futura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","text":"hunc igitur terrorem animi tenebrasque necessest\n\n \n\nnon radii solis nec lucida tela diei\n\n \n\ndiscutiant, sed naturae species ratioque.\n\n \n\nquo magis inceptum pergam pertexere dictis.\n\n  \n\nEt quoniam docui mundi mortalia templa\n\n \n\nesse  nativo consistere corpore caelum,\n\n \n\net quae cumque in eo fiunt fierique necessest\n\n \n\npleraque dissolui, qui restant percipe porro,\n\n \n\nquandoquidem semel insignem conscendere currum\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\ntu mihi supremae praescripta ad candida callis\n\n \n\ncurrenti spatium praemonstra, callida musa\n\n \n\nCalliope, requies hominum divomque voluptas,\n\n \n\nte duce ut insigni capiam cum laude coronam.\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\n ventorum existant, placentur  omnia rursum\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\nquae fuerint, sint placato conversa furore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","text":"cetera quae fieri in terris caeloque tuentur\n\n \n\nmortales, pavidis cum pendent mentibus saepe\n\n \n\net faciunt animos humilis formidine divom\n\n \n\ndepressosque premunt ad terram propterea quod\n\n \n\nignorantia causarum conferre deorum\n\n \n\ncogit ad imperium res et concedere regnum.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nnam bene qui didicere deos securum agere aevom,\n\n \n\nsi tamen interea mirantur qua ratione\n\n \n\nquaeque geri possint, praesertim rebus in illis\n\n \n\nquae supera caput aetheriis cernuntur in oris,\n\n \n\nrursus in antiquas referuntur religionis\n\n \n\net dominos acris adsciscunt, omnia posse\n\n \n\nquos miseri credunt, ignari quid queat esse,\n\n \n\nquid nequeat, finita potestas denique cuique\n\n \n\nqua nam sit ratione atque alte terminus haerens;\n\n \n\nquo magis errantes caeca ratione feruntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","text":"quae nisi respuis ex animo longeque remittis\n\n \n\ndis indigna putare alienaque pacis eorum,\n\n \n\ndelibata deum per te tibi numina sancta\n\n \n\nsaepe oberunt; non quo violari summa deum vis\n\n \n\npossit, ut ex ira poenas petere inbibat acris,\n\n \n\nsed quia tute tibi placida cum pace quietos\n\n \n\nconstitues magnos irarum volvere fluctus,\n\n \n\nnec delubra deum placido cum pectore adibis,\n\n \n\nnec de corpore quae sancto simulacra feruntur\n\n \n\nin mentes hominum divinae nuntia formae,\n\n \n\nsuscipere haec animi tranquilla pace valebis.\n\n \n\ninde videre licet qualis iam vita sequatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0006","text":"quam quidem ut a nobis ratio verissima longe\n\n \n\nreiciat, quamquam sunt a me multa profecta,\n\n \n\nmulta tamen restant et sunt ornanda politis\n\n \n\nversibus; est ratio caeliQUE ignisque tenenda,\n\n \n\nsunt tempestates et fulmina clara canenda,\n\n \n\nquid faciant et qua de causa cumque ferantur;\n\n \n\nne trepides caeli divisis partibus amens,\n\n \n\nunde volans ignis pervenerit aut in utram se\n\n \n\nverterit hinc partim, quo pacto per loca saepta\n\n \n\ninsinuarit, et hinc dominatus ut extulerit se.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n  \n\nPrincipio tonitru quatiuntur caerula caeli\n\n \n\npropterea quia concurrunt sublime volantes\n\n \n\naetheriae nubes contra pugnantibus ventis.\n\n \n\nnec fit enim sonitus caeli de parte serena,\n\n \n\nverum ubi cumque magis denso sunt agmine nubes,\n\n \n\ntam magis hinc magno fremitus fit murmure saepe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","text":"praeterea neque tam condenso corpore nubes\n\n \n\nesse queunt quam sunt lapides ac ligna, neque autem\n\n \n\ntam tenues quam sunt nebulae fumique volantes;\n\n \n\nnam cadere aut bruto deberent pondere pressae\n\n \n\n ut lapides, aut ut fumus constare nequirent\n\n \n\nnec cohibere nives gelidas et grandinis imbris.\n\n \n\nDant etiam sonitum patuli super aequora mundi,\n\n \n\ncarbasus ut quondam magnis intenta theatris\n\n \n\ndat crepitum malos inter iactata trabesque,\n\n \n\ninter dum perscissa furit petulantibus auris\n\n \n\net fragilis  chartarum commeditatur;\n\n \n\nid quoque enim genus in tonitru cognoscere possis,\n\n \n\naut ubi suspensam vestem chartasque volantis\n\n \n\nverberibus venti versant planguntque per auras.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0008","text":"fit quoque enim inter dum  non tam concurrere nubes\n\n \n\nfrontibus adversis possint quam de latere ire\n\n \n\ndiverso motu radentes corpora tractim,\n\n \n\naridus unde auris terget sonus ille diuque\n\n \n\nducitur, exierunt donec regionibus artis.\n\n  \n\nHoc etiam pacto tonitru concussa videntur\n\n \n\nomnia saepe gravi tremere et divolsa repente\n\n \n\nmaxima dissiluisse capacis moenia mundi,\n\n \n\ncum subito validi venti conlecta procella\n\n \n\nnubibus intorsit sese conclusaque ibidem\n\n \n\nturbine versanti magis ac magis undique nubem\n\n \n\ncogit uti fiat spisso cava corpore circum,\n\n \n\npost ubi conminuit vis eius et impetus acer,\n\n \n\ntum perterricrepo sonitu dat scissa fragorem.\n\n \n\nnec mirum, cum plena animae vensicula parva\n\n \n\nsaepe haud dat parvum sonitum displosa repente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0009","text":"Est etiam ratio, cum venti nubila perflant,\n\n \n\nut sonitus faciant; etenim ramosa videmus\n\n \n\nnubila saepe modis multis atque aspera ferri;\n\n \n\nscilicet ut, crebram silvam cum flamina cauri\n\n \n\nperflant, dant sonitum frondes ramique fragorem.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut inter dum validi vis incita venti\n\n \n\nperscindat nubem perfringens impete recto;\n\n \n\nnam quid possit ibi flatus manifesta docet res,\n\n \n\nhic, ubi lenior est, in terra cum tamen alta\n\n \n\narbusta evolvens radicibus haurit ab imis.\n\n \n\nsunt etiam fluctus per nubila, qui quasi murmur\n\n \n\ndant in frangendo graviter; quod item fit in altis\n\n \n\nfluminibus magnoque mari, cum frangitur aestus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0010","text":"Fit quoque, ubi e nubi in nubem vis incidit ardens\n\n \n\nfulminis; haec multo si forte umore recepit\n\n \n\nignem, continuo magno clamore trucidat;\n\n \n\nut calidis candens ferrum e fornacibus olim\n\n \n\nstridit, ubi in gelidum propter demersimus imbrem.\n\n \n\nAridior porro si nubes accipit ignem,\n\n \n\nuritur ingenti sonitu succensa repente,\n\n \n\nlauricomos ut si per montis flamma vagetur\n\n \n\nturbine ventorum comburens impete magno;\n\n \n\nnec res ulla magis quam Phoebi Delphica laurus\n\n \n\nterribili sonitu flamma crepitante crematur.\n\n \n\nDenique saepe geli multus fragor atque ruina\n\n \n\ngrandinis in magnis sonitum dat nubibus alte;\n\n \n\nventus enim cum confercit, franguntur in artum\n\n \n\nconcreti montes nimborum et grandine mixti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0011","text":"Fulgit item, nubes ignis cum semina multa\n\n \n\n excussere suo concursu, ceu lapidem si\n\n \n\npercutiat lapis aut ferrum; nam tum quoque lumen\n\n \n\nexilit et claras scintillas dissipat ignis.\n\n \n\nsed tonitrum fit uti post auribus accipiamus,\n\n \n\nfulgere quam cernant oculi, quia semper ad auris\n\n \n\ntardius adveniunt quam visum quae moveant res.\n\n \n\nid licet hinc etiam cognoscere: caedere si quem\n\n \n\nancipiti videas ferro procul arboris auctum,\n\n \n\nante fit ut cernas ictum quam plaga per auris\n\n \n\ndet sonitum; sic fulgorem quoque cernimus ante\n\n \n\nquam tonitrum accipimus, pariter qui mittitur igni\n\n \n\ne simili causa, concursu natus eodem.\n\n  \n\nHoc etiam pacto volucri loca lumine tingunt\n\n \n\nnubes et tremulo tempestas impete fulgit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0012","text":"ventus ubi invasit nubem et versatus ibidem\n\n \n\nfecit ut ante cavam docui spissescere nubem,\n\n \n\nmobilitate sua fervescit; ut omnia motu\n\n \n\npercalefacta vides ardescere, plumbea vero\n\n \n\nglans etiam longo cursu volvenda liquescit.\n\n \n\nergo fervidus hic nubem cum perscidit atram,\n\n \n\ndissipat ardoris quasi per vim expressa repente\n\n \n\nsemina, quae faciunt nictantia fulgura flammae;\n\n \n\ninde sonus sequitur, qui tardius adlicit auris\n\n \n\nquam quae perveniunt oculorum ad lumina nostra.\n\n \n\nscilicet hoc densis fit nubibus et simul alte\n\n \n\nextructis aliis alias super impete miro.\n\n \n\nne tibi sit frudi quod nos inferne videmus\n\n \n\nquam sint lata magis quam sursum extructa quid extent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0013","text":"contemplator enim, cum montibus adsimulata\n\n \n\nnubila portabunt venti transversa per auras,\n\n \n\naut ubi per magnos montis cumulata videbis\n\n \n\ninsuper esse aliis alia atque urguere superna\n\n \n\nin statione locata sepultis undique ventis;\n\n \n\ntum poteris magnas moles cognoscere eorum\n\n \n\nspeluncasque vel ut saxis pendentibus structas\n\n \n\ncernere, quas venti cum tempestate coorta\n\n \n\nconplerunt, magno indignantur murmure clausi\n\n \n\nnubibus in caveisque ferarum more minantur,\n\n \n\nnunc hinc nunc illinc fremitus per nubila mittunt,\n\n \n\nquaerentesque viam circum versantur et ignis\n\n \n\nsemina convolvunt  nubibus atque ita cogunt\n\n \n\nmulta rotantque cavis flammam fornacibus intus,\n\n \n\ndonec divolsa fulserunt nube corusci.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0014","text":"Hac etiam fit uti de causa mobilis ille\n\n \n\ndevolet in terram liquidi color aureus ignis,\n\n \n\nsemina quod nubes ipsas permulta necessust\n\n \n\nignis habere; etenim cum sunt umore sine ullo,\n\n \n\nflammeus  plerumque colos et splendidus ollis.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim solis de lumine multa necessest\n\n \n\nconcipere, ut merito rubeant ignesque profundant.\n\n \n\nhasce igitur cum ventus agens contrusit in unum\n\n \n\ncompressitque locum cogens, expressa profundunt\n\n \n\nsemina, quae faciunt flammae fulgere colores.\n\n \n\nFulgit item, cum rarescunt quoque nubila caeli;\n\n \n\nnam cum ventus eas leviter diducit euntis\n\n \n\n dissoluitque, cadant ingratius illa necessest\n\n \n\nsemina quae faciunt fulgorem. tum sine taetro\n\n \n\nterrore atque sonis fulgit nulloque tumultu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0015","text":"Quod superest,  natura praedita constent\n\n \n\nfulmina, declarant ictus et inusta vaporis\n\n \n\nsigna notaeque gravis halantis sulpuris auras;\n\n \n\nignis enim sunt haec non venti signa neque imbris.\n\n \n\npraeterea saepe accendunt quoque tecta domorum\n\n \n\net celeri flamma dominantur in aedibus ipsis.\n\n \n\nhunc tibi subtilem cum primis ignibus ignem\n\n \n\nconstituit natura minutis mobilibusque\n\n \n\ncorporibus, cui nil omnino obsistere possit.\n\n \n\ntransit enim validum fulmen per saepta domorum\n\n \n\nclamor ut ac voces, transit per saxa, per aera\n\n \n\net liquidum puncto facit aes in tempore et aurum.\n\n \n\ncurat item vasis integris vina repente\n\n \n\ndiffugiant, quia ni mirum facile omnia circum\n\n \n\nconlaxat rareque facit lateramina vasis\n\n \n\nadveniens calor eius et insinuatus in ipsum\n\n \n\nmobiliter soluens differt primordia vini.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0016","text":"quod solis vapor aetatem non posse videtur\n\n \n\nefficere usque adeo pollens fervore corusco.\n\n \n\ntanto mobilior vis et dominantior haec est.\n\n \n\nNunc ea quo pacto gignantur et impete tanto\n\n \n\nfiant ut possint ictu discludere turris,\n\n \n\ndisturbare domos, avellere tigna trabesque\n\n \n\net monimenta virum commoliri atque ciere,\n\n \n\nexanimare homines, pecudes prosternere passim,\n\n \n\ncetera de genere hoc qua vi facere omnia possint,\n\n \n\nexpediam neque  in promissis plura morabor.\n\n  \n\nFulmina gignier e crassis alteque putandumst\n\n \n\nnubibus extructis; nam caelo nulla sereno\n\n \n\nnec leviter densis mittuntur nubibus umquam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0017","text":"nam dubio procul hoc fieri manifesta docet res;\n\n \n\nquod tunc per totum concrescunt aeëra nubes,\n\n \n\nundique uti tenebras omnis Acherunta reamur\n\n \n\nliquisse et magnas caeli complesse cavernas,\n\n \n\næusque adeo tetra nimborum nocte coorta\n\n \n\ninpendent atrae formidinis ora superne,æ\n\n \n\ncum commoliri tempestas fulmina coeptat.\n\n \n\npraeterea persaepe niger quoque per mare nimbus,\n\n \n\nut picis e caelo demissum flumen, in undas\n\n \n\nsic cadit effertus tenebris procul et trahit atram\n\n \n\nfulminibus gravidam tempestatem atque procellis,\n\n \n\nignibus ac ventis cum primis ipse repletus,\n\n \n\nin terra quoque ut horrescant ac tecta requirant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0018","text":"sic igitur supera nostrum caput esse putandumst\n\n \n\ntempestatem altam; neque enim caligine tanta\n\n \n\nobruerent terras, nisi inaedificata superne\n\n \n\nmulta forent multis exempto nubila sole;\n\n \n\nnec tanto possent venientes opprimere imbri,\n\n \n\nflumina abundare ut facerent camposque natare,\n\n \n\nsi non extructis foret alte nubibus aether.\n\n \n\nhic igitur ventis atque ignibus omnia plena\n\n \n\nsunt; ideo passim fremitus et fulgura fiunt.\n\n \n\n quippe etenim supra docui permulta vaporis\n\n \n\nsemina habere cavas nubes et multa necessest\n\n \n\nconcipere ex solis radiis ardoreque eorum.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi ventus eas idem qui cogit in unum\n\n \n\nforte locum quemvis, expressit multa vaporis\n\n \n\nsemina seque simul cum eo commiscuit igni,\n\n \n\ninsinuatus ibi vortex versatur in arto\n\n \n\net calidis acuit fulmen fornacibus intus;\n\n \n\nnam duplici ratione accenditur: ipse sua cum\n\n \n\nmobilitate calescit et e contagibus ignis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0019","text":"inde ubi percaluit venti vis  gravis ignis\n\n \n\nimpetus incessit, maturum tum quasi fulmen\n\n \n\nperscindit subito nubem ferturque coruscis\n\n \n\nomnia luminibus lustrans loca percitus ardor.\n\n \n\nquem gravis insequitur sonitus, displosa repente\n\n \n\nopprimere ut caeli videantur templa superne.\n\n \n\ninde tremor terras graviter pertemptat et altum\n\n \n\nmurmura percurrunt caelum; nam tota fere tum\n\n \n\ntempestas concussa tremit fremitusque moventur.\n\n \n\nquo de concussu sequitur gravis imber et uber,\n\n \n\nomnis uti videatur in imbrem vertier aether\n\n \n\natque ita praecipitans ad diluviem revocare;\n\n \n\ntantus discidio nubis ventique procella\n\n \n\nmittitur, ardenti sonitus cum provolat ictu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0020","text":"Est etiam cum vis extrinsecus incita venti\n\n \n\nincidit in validam maturo culmine nubem;\n\n \n\nquam cum perscidit, extemplo cadit igneus ille\n\n \n\nvertex, quem patrio vocitamus nomine fulmen.\n\n \n\nhoc fit idem in partis alias, quo cumque tulit vis.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut inter dum venti vis missa sine igni\n\n \n\nigniscat tamen in spatio longoque meatu,\n\n \n\ndum venit amittens in cursu corpora quaedam\n\n \n\ngrandia, quae nequeunt pariter penetrare per auras,\n\n \n\natque alia ex ipso conradens aeëre portat\n\n \n\nparvola, quae faciunt ignem commixta volando;\n\n \n\nnon alia longe ratione ac plumbea saepe\n\n \n\nfervida fit glans in cursu, cum multa rigoris\n\n \n\ncorpora dimittens ignem concepit in auris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0021","text":"Fit quoque ut ipsius plagae vis excitet ignem,\n\n \n\nfrigida cum venti pepulit vis missa sine igni,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia, cum vehementi perculit ictu,\n\n \n\nconfluere ex ipso possunt elementa vaporis\n\n \n\net simul ex illa quae tum res excipit ictum;\n\n \n\nut, lapidem ferro cum caedimus, evolat ignis,\n\n \n\nnec, quod frigida vis ferrist, hoc setius illi\n\n \n\nsemina concurrunt calidi fulgoris ad ictum.\n\n \n\nsic igitur quoque res accendi fulmine debet,\n\n \n\nopportuna fuit si forte et idonea flammis.\n\n \n\nnec temere omnino plane vis frigida venti\n\n \n\nesse potest, ea quae tanta vi missa supernest,\n\n \n\nquin, prius in cursu si non accenditur igni,\n\n \n\nat tepefacta tamen veniat commixta calore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0022","text":"Mobilitas autem fit fulminis et gravis ictus\n\n \n\net celeri ferme percurrunt fulmina lapsu,\n\n \n\nnubibus ipsa quod omnino prius incita se vis\n\n \n\n colligit et magnum conamen sumit eundi,\n\n \n\ninde ubi non potuit nubes capere inpetis auctum,\n\n \n\nexprimitur vis atque ideo volat impete miro,\n\n \n\nut validis quae de tormentis missa feruntur.\n\n \n\nAdde quod e parvis et levibus est elementis,\n\n \n\nnec facilest tali naturae obsistere quicquam;\n\n \n\ninter enim fugit ac penetrat per rara viarum,\n\n \n\nnon igitur multis offensibus in remorando\n\n \n\nhaesitat, hanc ob rem celeri volat impete labens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0023","text":"Deinde, quod omnino natura pondera deorsum\n\n \n\nomnia nituntur, cum plagast addita vero,\n\n \n\nmobilitas duplicatur et impetus ille gravescit,\n\n \n\nut vehementius et citius quae cumque morantur\n\n \n\nobvia discutiat plagis itinerque sequatur.\n\n \n\nDenique quod longo venit impete, sumere debet\n\n \n\nmobilitatem etiam atque etiam, quae crescit eundo\n\n \n\net validas auget viris et roborat ictum;\n\n \n\nnam facit ut quae sint illius semina cumque\n\n \n\ne regione locum quasi in unum cuncta ferantur,\n\n \n\nomnia coniciens in eum volventia cursum.\n\n \n\nForsitan ex ipso veniens trahat aeëre quaedam\n\n \n\ncorpora, quae plagis incendunt mobilitatem.\n\n \n\nincolumisque venit per res atque integra transit\n\n \n\nmulta, foraminibus liquidus quia transviat ignis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0024","text":"multaque perfringit, cum corpora fulminis ipsa\n\n \n\ncorporibus rerum inciderunt, qua texta tenentur.\n\n \n\ndissoluit porro facile aes aurumque repente\n\n \n\nconferve facit, e parvis quia facta minute\n\n \n\ncorporibus vis est et levibus ex elementis,\n\n \n\nquae facile insinuantur et insinuata repente\n\n \n\ndissoluont nodos omnis et vincla relaxant.\n\n  \n\nAutumnoque magis stellis fulgentibus alta\n\n \n\nconcutitur caeli domus undique totaque tellus,\n\n \n\net cum tempora se veris florentia pandunt.\n\n \n\nfrigore enim desunt ignes ventique calore\n\n \n\ndeficiunt neque sunt tam denso corpore nubes.\n\n \n\ninterutrasque igitur cum caeli tempora constant,\n\n \n\ntum variae causae concurrunt fulminis omnes.\n\n \n\nnam fretus ipse anni permiscet frigus  aestum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0025","text":"quorum utrumque opus est fabricanda ad fulmina nubi,\n\n \n\nut discordia  rerum magnoque tumultu\n\n \n\nignibus et ventis furibundus fluctuet aeër.\n\n \n\nprima caloris enim pars est postrema rigoris;\n\n \n\ntempus id est vernum; quare pugnare necessest\n\n \n\ndissimilis  inter se turbareque mixtas.\n\n \n\net calor extremus primo cum frigore mixtus\n\n \n\nvolvitur, autumni quod fertur nomine tempus,\n\n \n\nhic quoque confligunt hiemes aestatibus acres.\n\n \n\npropterea  sunt haec anni nominitanda,\n\n \n\nnec mirumst, in eo si tempore plurima fiunt\n\n \n\nfulmina tempestasque cietur turbida caelo,\n\n \n\n ancipiti quoniam bello turbatur utrimque,\n\n \n\nhinc flammis, illinc ventis umoreque mixto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","text":"Hoc est igniferi naturam fulminis ipsam\n\n \n\nperspicere et qua vi faciat rem quamque videre,\n\n \n\nnon Tyrrhena retro volventem carmina frustra\n\n \n\nindicia occultae divum perquirere mentis,\n\n \n\nunde volans ignis pervenerit aut in utram se\n\n \n\nverterit hinc partim, quo pacto per loca saepta\n\n \n\ninsinuarit, et hinc dominatus ut extulerit se,\n\n \n\nquidve nocere queat de caelo fulminis ictus.\n\n \n\nquod si Iuppiter atque alii fulgentia divi\n\n \n\nterrifico quatiunt sonitu caelestia templa\n\n \n\net iaciunt ignem quo cuiquest cumque voluntas,\n\n \n\ncur quibus incautum scelus aversabile cumquest\n\n \n\nnon faciunt icti flammas ut fulguris halent\n\n \n\npectore perfixo, documen mortalibus acre,\n\n \n\net potius nulla sibi turpi conscius in re\n\n \n\nvolvitur in flammis innoxius inque peditur\n\n \n\nturbine caelesti subito correptus et igni?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0027","text":"cur etiam loca sola petunt frustraque laborant?\n\n \n\nan tum bracchia consuescunt firmantque lacertos?\n\n \n\nin terraque patris cur telum perpetiuntur\n\n \n\noptundi? cur ipse sinit neque parcit in hostis?\n\n \n\ndenique cur numquam caelo iacit undique puro\n\n \n\nIuppiter in terras fulmen sonitusque profundit?\n\n \n\nan simul ac nubes successere, ipse in eas tum\n\n \n\ndescendit, prope ut hinc teli determinet ictus?\n\n \n\nin mare qua porro mittit ratione? quid undas\n\n \n\narguit et liquidam molem camposque natantis?\n\n \n\npraeterea si vult caveamus fulminis ictum,\n\n \n\ncur dubitat facere ut possimus cernere missum?\n\n \n\nsi nec opinantis autem volt opprimere igni,\n\n \n\ncur tonat ex illa parte, ut vitare queamus,\n\n \n\ncur tenebras ante et fremitus et murmura concit?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","text":"et simul in multas partis qui credere possis\n\n \n\nmittere? an hoc ausis numquam contendere factum,\n\n \n\nut fierent ictus uno sub tempore plures?\n\n \n\nat saepest numero factum fierique necessest,\n\n \n\nut pluere in multis regionibus et cadere imbris,\n\n \n\nfulmina sic uno fieri sub tempore multa.\n\n \n\npostremo cur sancta deum delubra suasque\n\n \n\ndiscutit infesto praeclaras fulmine sedes\n\n \n\net bene facta deum frangit simulacra suisque\n\n \n\ndemit imaginibus violento volnere honorem?\n\n \n\naltaque cur plerumque petit loca plurimaque eius\n\n \n\nmontibus in summis vestigia cernimus ignis?\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, facilest ex his cognoscere rebus,\n\n \n\npresteras Graii quos ab re nominitarunt,\n\n \n\nin mare qua missi veniant ratione superne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0029","text":"nam fit ut inter dum tam quam demissa columna\n\n \n\nin mare de caelo descendat, quam freta circum\n\n \n\nfervescunt graviter spirantibus incita flabris,\n\n \n\net quae cumque in eo tum sint deprensa tumultu\n\n \n\nnavigia in summum veniant vexata periclum.\n\n \n\nhoc fit ubi inter dum non quit vis incita venti\n\n \n\n rumpere quam coepit nubem, sed deprimit, ut sit\n\n \n\nin mare de caelo tam quam demissa columna,\n\n \n\npaulatim, quasi quid pugno bracchique superne\n\n \n\nconiectu trudatur et extendatur in undas;\n\n \n\nquam cum discidit, hinc prorumpitur in mare venti\n\n \n\nvis et fervorem mirum concinnat in undis;\n\n \n\nversabundus enim turbo descendit et illam\n\n \n\ndeducit pariter lento cum corpore nubem;\n\n \n\nquam simul ac gravidam detrusit ad aequora ponti,\n\n \n\nille in aquam subito totum se inmittit et omne\n\n \n\nexcitat ingenti sonitu mare fervere cogens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0030","text":"Fit quoque ut involvat venti se nubibus ipse\n\n \n\nvertex conradens ex aeëre semina nubis\n\n \n\net quasi demissum caelo prestera imitetur;\n\n \n\nhic ubi se in terras demisit dissoluitque,\n\n \n\nturbinis immanem vim provomit atque procellae.\n\n \n\nsed quia fit raro omnino montisque necessest\n\n \n\nofficere in terris, apparet crebrius idem\n\n \n\nprospectu maris in magno caeloque patenti.\n\n \n\nNubila concrescunt, ubi corpora multa volando\n\n \n\nhoc super in caeli spatio coiere repente\n\n \n\nasperiora, modis quae possint indupedita\n\n \n\nexiguis tamen inter se compressa teneri.\n\n \n\nhaec faciunt primum parvas consistere nubes;\n\n \n\ninde ea comprendunt inter se conque gregantur\n\n \n\net coniungendo crescunt ventisque feruntur\n\n \n\nusque adeo donec tempestas saeva coortast.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0031","text":"Fit quoque uti montis vicina cacumina caelo\n\n \n\nquam sint quoque magis, tanto magis edita fument\n\n \n\nadsidue fulvae nubis caligine crassa\n\n \n\npropterea quia, cum consistunt nubila primum,\n\n \n\nante videre oculi quam possint tenvia, venti\n\n \n\nportantes cogunt ad summa cacumina montis;\n\n \n\nhic demum fit uti turba maiore coorta\n\n \n\net condensa queant apparere et simul ipso\n\n \n\nvertice de montis videantur surgere in aethram.\n\n \n\nnam loca declarat sursum ventosa patere\n\n \n\nres ipsa et sensus, montis cum ascendimus altos.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea permulta mari quoque tollere toto\n\n \n\ncorpora naturam declarant litore vestis\n\n \n\nsuspensae, cum concipiunt umoris adhaesum.\n\n \n\nquo magis ad nubis augendas multa videntur\n\n \n\nposse quoque e salso consurgere momine ponti;\n\n \n\nnam ratio consanguineast umoribus omnis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0032","text":"Praeterea fluviis ex omnibus et simul ipsa\n\n \n\nsurgere de terra nebulas aestumque videmus,\n\n \n\nquae vel ut halitus hinc ita sursum expressa feruntur\n\n \n\nsuffunduntque sua caelum caligine et altas\n\n \n\nsufficiunt nubis paulatim conveniundo;\n\n \n\nurget enim quoque signiferi super aetheris aestus\n\n \n\net quasi densendo subtexit caerula nimbis.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut hunc veniant in caelum extrinsecus illa\n\n \n\ncorpora quae faciunt nubis nimbosque volantis;\n\n \n\ninnumerabilem enim numerum summamque profundi\n\n \n\nesse infinitam docui, quantaque volarent\n\n \n\n corpora mobilitate ostendi quamque repente\n\n \n\nimmemorabile  spatium transire solerent.\n\n \n\nhaut igitur mirumst, si parvo tempore saepe\n\n \n\ntam magnis ventis tempestas atque tenebrae\n\n \n\ncoperiant maria ac terras inpensa superne,\n\n \n\nundique quandoquidem per caulas aetheris omnis\n\n \n\net quasi per magni circum spiracula mundi\n\n \n\nexitus introitusque elementis redditus extat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0033","text":"Nunc age, quo pacto pluvius concrescat in altis\n\n \n\nnubibus umor et in terras demissus ut imber\n\n \n\ndecidat, expediam. primum iam semina aquai\n\n \n\nmulta simul vincam consurgere nubibus ipsis\n\n \n\nomnibus ex rebus pariterque ita crescere utrumque\n\n \n\net nubis et aquam, quae cumque in nubibus extat,\n\n \n\nut pariter nobis corpus cum sanguine crescit,\n\n \n\nsudor item atque umor qui cumque est denique membris.\n\n \n\nconcipiunt etiam multum quoque saepe marinum\n\n \n\numorem, vel uti pendentia vellera lanae,\n\n \n\ncum supera magnum mare venti nubila portant.\n\n \n\nconsimili ratione ex omnibus amnibus umor\n\n \n\ntollitur in nubis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0034","text":"quo cum bene semina aquarum\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis convenere undique adaucta,\n\n \n\nconfertae nubes umorem mittere certant\n\n \n\ndupliciter; nam vis venti contrudit et ipsa\n\n \n\ncopia nimborum turba maiore coacta\n\n \n\nurget et e supero premit ac facit effluere imbris.\n\n \n\npraeterea cum rarescunt quoque nubila ventis\n\n \n\naut dissolvuntur solis super icta calore,\n\n \n\nmittunt umorem pluvium stillantque, quasi igni\n\n \n\ncera super calido tabescens multa liquescat.\n\n \n\nsed vehemens imber fit, ubi vehementer utraque\n\n \n\nnubila vi cumulata premuntur et impete venti.\n\n \n\nat retinere diu pluviae longumque morari\n\n \n\nconsuerunt, ubi multa cientur semina aquarum\n\n \n\natque aliis aliae nubes nimbique rigantes\n\n \n\ninsuper atque omni vulgo de parte feruntur,\n\n \n\nterraque cum fumans umorem tota redhalat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0035","text":"hic ubi sol radiis tempestatem inter opacam\n\n \n\nadversa fulsit nimborum aspargine contra,\n\n \n\ntum color in nigris existit nubibus arqui.\n\n \n\nCetera quae sursum crescunt sursumque creantur,\n\n \n\net quae concrescunt in nubibus, omnia, prorsum\n\n \n\nomnia, nix venti grando gelidaeque pruinae\n\n \n\net vis magna geli, magnum duramen aquarum,\n\n \n\net mora quae fluvios passim refrenat aventis,\n\n \n\nperfacilest tamen haec reperire animoque videre,\n\n \n\nomnia quo pacto fiant quareve creentur,\n\n \n\ncum bene cognoris elementis reddita quae sint.\n\n  \n\nNunc age, quae ratio terrai motibus extet\n\n \n\npercipe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036","text":"et in primis terram fac ut esse rearis\n\n \n\nsupter item ut supera ventosis undique plenam\n\n \n\nspeluncis multosque lacus multasque lucunas\n\n \n\nin gremio gerere et rupes deruptaque saxa;\n\n \n\nmultaque sub tergo terrai flumina tecta\n\n \n\nvolvere vi fluctus summersos ca putandumst;\n\n \n\n undique enim similem esse sui res postulat ipsa.\n\n \n\nhis igitur rebus subiunctis suppositisque\n\n \n\nterra superne tremit magnis concussa ruinis,\n\n \n\nsubter ubi ingentis speluncas subruit aetas;\n\n \n\nquippe cadunt toti montes magnoque repente\n\n \n\nconcussu late disserpunt inde tremores.\n\n \n\net merito, quoniam plaustris concussa tremescunt\n\n \n\ntecta viam propter non magno pondere tota,\n\n \n\nnec minus exultant, si quidvis cumque viai\n\n \n\nferratos utrimque rotarum succutit orbes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0037","text":"Fit quoque, ubi in magnas aquae vastasque lucunas\n\n \n\ngleba vetustate e terra provolvitur ingens,\n\n \n\nut iactetur aquae fluctu quoque terra vacillans;\n\n \n\nut vas inter  non quit constare, nisi umor\n\n \n\ndestitit in dubio fluctu iactarier intus.\n\n \n\nPraeterea ventus cum per loca subcava terrae\n\n \n\ncollectus parte ex una procumbit et urget\n\n \n\nobnixus magnis speluncas viribus altas,\n\n \n\nincumbit tellus quo venti prona premit vis.\n\n \n\ntum supera terram quae sunt extructa domorum\n\n \n\nad caelumque magis quanto sunt edita quaeque,\n\n \n\ninclinata minent in eandem prodita partem\n\n \n\nprotractaeque trabes inpendent ire paratae.\n\n \n\net metuunt magni naturam credere mundi\n\n \n\nexitiale aliquod tempus clademque manere,\n\n \n\ncum videant tantam terrarum incumbere molem!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0038","text":"quod nisi respirent venti,  nulla refrenet\n\n \n\nres neque ab exitio possit reprehendere euntis;\n\n \n\nnunc quia respirant alternis inque gravescunt\n\n \n\net quasi collecti redeunt ceduntque repulsi,\n\n \n\nsaepius hanc ob rem minitatur terra ruinas\n\n \n\nquam facit; inclinatur enim retroque recellit\n\n \n\net recipit prolapsa suas in pondere sedes.\n\n \n\nhac igitur ratione vacillant omnia tecta,\n\n \n\nsumma magis mediis, media imis, ima perhilum.\n\n  \n\nEst haec eiusdem quoque magni causa tremoris.\n\n \n\nventus ubi atque animae subito vis maxima quaedam\n\n \n\naut extrinsecus aut ipsa tellure coorta\n\n \n\nin loca se cava terrai coniecit ibique\n\n \n\nspeluncas inter magnas fremit ante tumultu\n\n \n\nversabundaQUE portatur, post incita cum vis\n\n \n\nexagitata foras erumpitur et simul altam\n\n \n\ndiffindens terram magnum concinnat hiatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0039","text":"in Syria Sidone quod accidit et fuit Aegi\n\n \n\nin Peloponneso, quas exitus hic animai\n\n \n\ndisturbat urbes et terrae motus obortus.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea ceciderunt moenia magnis\n\n \n\nmotibus in terris et multae per mare pessum\n\n \n\nsubsedere suis pariter cum civibus urbes.\n\n \n\nquod nisi prorumpit, tamen impetus ipse animai\n\n \n\net fera vis venti per crebra foramina terrae\n\n \n\ndispertitur ut horror et incutit inde tremorem;\n\n \n\nfrigus uti nostros penitus cum venit in artus,\n\n \n\nconcutit invitos cogens tremere atque movere.\n\n \n\nancipiti trepidant igitur terrore per urbis,\n\n \n\n tecta superne timent, metuunt inferne cavernas\n\n \n\nterrai ne dissoluat natura repente,\n\n \n\nneu distracta suum late dispandat hiatum\n\n \n\nidque suis confusa velit complere ruinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040","text":"proinde licet quamvis caelum terramque reantur\n\n \n\nincorrupta fore aeternae mandata saluti:\n\n \n\net tamen inter dum praesens vis ipsa pericli\n\n \n\nsubdit et hunc stimulum quadam de parte timoris,\n\n \n\nne pedibus raptim tellus subtracta feratur\n\n \n\nin barathrum rerumque sequatur prodita summa\n\n \n\nfunditus et fiat mundi confusa ruina.\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n  \n\nPrincipio mare mirantur non reddere maius\n\n \n\nnaturam, quo sit tantus decursus aquarum,\n\n \n\nomnia quo veniant ex omni flumina parte.\n\n \n\nadde vagos imbris tempestatesque volantes,\n\n \n\nomnia quae maria ac terras sparguntque rigantque;\n\n \n\nadde suos fontis; tamen ad maris omnia summam\n\n \n\nguttai vix instar erunt unius adaugmen;\n\n \n\nquo minus est mirum mare non augescere magnum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0041","text":"Praeterea magnam sol partem detrahit aestu.\n\n \n\nquippe videmus enim vestis umore madentis\n\n \n\nexsiccare suis radiis ardentibus solem;\n\n \n\nat pelage multa et late substrata videmus.\n\n \n\nproinde licet quamvis ex uno quoque loco sol\n\n \n\numoris parvam delibet ab aequore partem,\n\n \n\nlargiter in tanto spatio tamen auferet undis.\n\n \n\nTum porro venti quoque magnam tollere partem\n\n \n\numoris possunt verrentes aequora, ventis\n\n \n\nuna nocte vias quoniam persaepe videmus\n\n \n\nsiccari mollisque luti concrescere crustas.\n\n \n\nPraeterea docui multum quoque tollere nubes\n\n \n\numorem magno conceptum ex aequore ponti\n\n \n\net passim toto terrarum spargere in orbi,\n\n \n\ncum pluit in terris et venti nubila portant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0042","text":"Postremo quoniam raro cum corpore tellus\n\n \n\nest et coniunctast oras maris undique cingens,\n\n \n\ndebet, ut in mare de terris venit umor aquai,\n\n \n\nin terras itidem manare ex aequore salso;\n\n \n\npercolatur enim virus retroque remanat\n\n \n\nmateries umoris et ad caput amnibus omnis\n\n \n\nconfluit, inde super terras redit agmine dulci\n\n \n\nqua via secta semel liquido pede detulit undas.\n\n  \n\nNunc ratio quae sit, per fauces montis ut Aetnae\n\n \n\nexpirent ignes inter dum turbine tanto,\n\n \n\nexpediam; neque enim mediocri clade coorta\n\n \n\nflammae tempestas Siculum dominata per agros\n\n \n\nfinitimis ad se convertit gentibus ora,\n\n \n\nfumida cum caeli scintillare omnia templa\n\n \n\ncernentes pavida complebant pectora cura,\n\n \n\nquid moliretur rerum natura novarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","text":"Hisce tibi in rebus latest alteque videndum\n\n \n\net longe cunctas in partis dispiciendum,\n\n \n\nut reminiscaris summam rerum esse profundam\n\n \n\net videas caelum summai totius unum\n\n \n\n quam sit parvula pars et quam multesima constet\n\n \n\nnec tota pars, homo terrai quota totius unus.\n\n \n\nquod bene propositum si plane contueare\n\n \n\nac videas plane, mirari multa relinquas.\n\n \n\nnumquis enim nostrum miratur, siquis in artus\n\n \n\naccepit calido febrim fervore coortam\n\n \n\naut alium quemvis morbi per membra dolorem?\n\n \n\nopturgescit enim subito pes, arripit acer\n\n \n\nsaepe dolor dentes, oculos invadit in ipsos,\n\n \n\nexistit sacer ignis et urit corpore serpens\n\n \n\nquam cumque arripuit partem repitque per artus,\n\n \n\nni mirum quia sunt multarum semina rerum\n\n \n\net satis haec tellus morbi caelumque mali fert,\n\n \n\nunde queat vis immensi procrescere morbi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","text":"sic igitur toti caelo terraeque putandumst\n\n \n\nex infinito satis omnia suppeditare,\n\n \n\nunde repente queat tellus concussa moveri\n\n \n\nperque mare ac terras rapidus percurrere turbo,\n\n \n\nignis abundare Aetnaeus, flammescere caelum;\n\n \n\nid quoque enim fit et ardescunt caelestia templa\n\n \n\net tempestates pluviae graviore coortu\n\n \n\nsunt, ubi forte ita se tetulerunt semina aquarum.\n\n \n\n'at nimis est ingens incendi turbidus ardor.'\n\n \n\nscilicet et fluvius qui visus maximus ei,\n\n \n\nqui non ante aliquem maiorem vidit, et ingens\n\n \n\narbor homoque videtur et omnia de genere omni\n\n \n\nmaxima quae vidit quisque, haec ingentia fingit,\n\n \n\ncum tamen omnia cum caelo terraque marique\n\n \n\nnil sint ad summam summai totius omnem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0045","text":"Nunc tamen illa modis quibus inritata repente\n\n \n\nflamma foras vastis Aetnae fornacibus efflet,\n\n \n\nexpediam. primum totius subcava montis\n\n \n\nest natura fere silicum suffulta cavernis.\n\n \n\nomnibus est porro in speluncis ventus et aeër.\n\n \n\nventus enim fit, ubi est agitando percitus aeër.\n\n \n\nhic ubi percaluit cale fecitque omnia circum\n\n \n\nsaxa furens, qua contingit, terramque et ab ollis\n\n \n\nexcussit calidum flammis velocibus ignem,\n\n \n\ntollit se ac rectis ita faucibus eicit alte.\n\n \n\nfert itaque ardorem longe longeque favillam\n\n \n\ndiffert et crassa volvit caligine fumum\n\n \n\nextruditque simul mirando pondere saxa;\n\n \n\nne dubites quin haec animai turbida sit vis.\n\n \n\npraeterea magna ex parti mare montis ad eius\n\n \n\nradices frangit fluctus aestumque resolvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","text":"ex hoc usque mari speluncae montis ad altas\n\n \n\nperveniunt subter fauces. hac ire fatendumst\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n \n\net penetrare mari penitus res cogit aperto\n\n \n\natque efflare foras ideoque extollere flammam\n\n \n\nsaxaque subiectare et arenae tollere nimbos.\n\n \n\nin summo sunt vertice enim crateres, ut ipsi\n\n \n\nnominitant, nos quod fauces perhibemus et ora.\n\n  \n\nSunt aliquot quoque res quarum unam dicere causam\n\n \n\nnon satis est, verum pluris, unde una tamen sit;\n\n \n\n corpus ut exanimum siquod procul ipse iacere\n\n \n\nconspicias hominis, fit ut omnis dicere causas\n\n \n\nconveniat leti, dicatur ut illius una;\n\n \n\nnam que eum ferro nec frigore vincere possis\n\n \n\ninteriisse neque a morbo neque forte veneno,\n\n \n\nverum aliquid genere esse ex hoc quod contigit ei\n\n \n\nscimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0047","text":"item in multis hoc rebus dicere habemus.\n\n \n\nNilus in aestatem crescit campisque redundat\n\n \n\nunicus in terris, Aegypti totius amnis.\n\n \n\nis rigat Aegyptum medium per saepe calorem,\n\n \n\naut quia sunt aestate aquilones ostia contra,\n\n \n\nanni tempore eo, qui etesiae esse feruntur,\n\n \n\net contra fluvium flantes remorantur et undas\n\n \n\ncogentes sursus replent coguntque manere.\n\n \n\nnam dubio procul haec adverso flabra feruntur\n\n \n\nflumine, quae gelidis ab stellis axis aguntur;\n\n \n\nille ex aestifera parti venit amnis ab austro\n\n \n\ninter nigra virum percocto saecla colore\n\n \n\nexoriens penitus media ab regione diei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0048","text":"est quoque uti possit magnus congestus harenae\n\n \n\nfluctibus adversis oppilare ostia contra,\n\n \n\ncum mare permotum ventis ruit intus harenam;\n\n \n\nquo fit uti pacto liber minus exitus amnis\n\n \n\net proclivis item fiat minus impetus undis.\n\n \n\nfit quoque uti pluviae forsan magis ad caput ei\n\n \n\ntempore eo fiant, quo etesia flabra aquilonum\n\n \n\nnubila coniciunt in eas tunc omnia partis.\n\n \n\nscilicet, ad mediam regionem eiecta diei\n\n \n\ncum convenerunt, ibi ad altos denique montis\n\n \n\ncontrusae nubes coguntur vique premuntur.\n\n \n\nforsitan Aethiopum penitus de montibus altis\n\n \n\ncrescat, ubi in campos albas descendere ningues\n\n \n\ntabificis subigit radiis sol omnia lustrans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0049","text":"Nunc age, Averna tibi quae sint loca cumque lacusque,\n\n \n\nexpediam, quali natura praedita constent.\n\n \n\nprincipio, quod Averna vocantur nomine, id ab re\n\n \n\ninpositumst, quia sunt avibus contraria cunctis,\n\n \n\ne regione ea quod loca cum venere volantes,\n\n \n\nremigii oblitae pennarum vela remittunt\n\n \n\npraecipitesque cadunt molli cervice profusae\n\n \n\nin terram, si forte ita fert natura locorum,\n\n \n\naut in aquam, si forte lacus substratus Averni.\n\n \n\nis locus est Cumas aput, acri sulpure montis\n\n \n\noppleti calidis ubi fumant fontibus aucti.\n\n \n\nest et Athenaeis in moenibus, arcis in ipso\n\n \n\nvertice, Palladis ad templum Tritonidis almae,\n\n \n\nquo numquam pennis appellunt corpora raucae\n\n \n\ncornices, non cum fumant altaria donis;\n\n \n\nusque adeo fugitant non iras Palladis acris\n\n \n\npervigili causa, Graium ut cecinere poeëtae,\n\n \n\nsed natura loci opus efficit ipsa suapte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","text":"in Syria quoque fertur item locus esse videri,\n\n \n\nquadripedes quoque quo simul ac vestigia primum\n\n \n\nintulerint, graviter vis cogat concidere ipsa,\n\n \n\nmanibus ut si sint divis mactata repente.\n\n \n\n omnia quae naturali ratione geruntur,\n\n \n\net quibus e fiant causis apparet origo;\n\n \n\nianua ne pote eis Orci regionibus esse\n\n \n\ncredatur, post hinc animas Acheruntis in oras\n\n \n\nducere forte deos manis inferne reamur,\n\n \n\nnaribus alipedes ut cervi saepe putantur\n\n \n\nducere de latebris serpentia saecla ferarum.\n\n \n\nquod procul a vera quam sit ratione repulsum\n\n \n\npercipe; nam de re nunc ipsa dicere conor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0051","text":"Principio hoc dico, quod dixi saepe quoque ante,\n\n \n\nin terra cuiusque modi rerum esse figuras;\n\n \n\nmulta, cibo quae sunt, vitalia multaque, morbos\n\n \n\nincutere et mortem quae possint adcelerare.\n\n \n\net magis esse aliis alias animantibus aptas\n\n \n\nres ad vitai rationem ostendimus ante\n\n \n\npropter dissimilem naturam dissimilisque\n\n \n\ntexturas inter sese primasque figuras.\n\n \n\nmulta meant inimica per auris, multa per ipsas\n\n \n\ninsinuant naris infesta atque aspera tactu,\n\n \n\nnec sunt multa parum tactu vitanda neque autem\n\n \n\naspectu fugienda saporeque tristia quae sint.\n\n \n\nDeinde videre licet quam multae sint homini res\n\n \n\nacriter infesto sensu spurcaeque gravisque;\n\n \n\narboribus primum certis gravis umbra tributa\n\n \n\nusque adeo, capitis faciant ut saepe dolores,\n\n \n\nsiquis eas subter iacuit prostratus in herbis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0052","text":"est etiam magnis Heliconis montibus arbos\n\n \n\nfloris odore hominem taetro consueta necare.\n\n \n\nscilicet haec ideo terris ex omnia surgunt,\n\n \n\nmulta modis multis multarum semina rerum\n\n \n\nquod permixta gerit tellus discretaque tradit.\n\n \n\nnocturnumque recens extinctum lumen ubi acri\n\n \n\nnidore offendit nares, consopit ibidem,\n\n \n\nconcidere et spumas qui morbo mittere suevit.\n\n \n\ncastoreoque gravi mulier sopita recumbit,\n\n \n\net manibus nitidum teneris opus effluit ei,\n\n \n\ntempore eo si odoratast quo menstrua solvit.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea languentia membra per artus\n\n \n\nsolvunt atque animam labefactant sedibus intus.\n\n \n\ndenique si calidis etiam cunctere lavabris\n\n \n\nplenior et lueris, solio ferventis aquai\n\n \n\nquam facile in medio fit uti des saepe ruinas!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0053","text":"carbonumque gravis vis atque odor insinuatur\n\n \n\nquam facile in cerebrum, nisi aqua praecepimus ante!\n\n \n\nat cum membra domans percepit fervida febris,\n\n \n\ntum fit odor vini plagae mactabilis instar.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam terra quoque sulpur in ipsa\n\n \n\ngignier et taetro concrescere odore bitumen,\n\n \n\ndenique ubi argenti venas aurique secuntur,\n\n \n\nterrai penitus scrutantes abdita ferro,\n\n \n\nqualis expiret Scaptensula subter odores?\n\n \n\nquidve mali fit ut exalent aurata metalla!\n\n \n\nquas hominum reddunt facies qualisque colores!\n\n \n\nnonne vides audisve perire in tempore parvo\n\n \n\nquam soleant et quam vitai copia desit,\n\n \n\n quos opere in tali cohibet vis magna necessis?\n\n \n\nhos igitur tellus omnis exaestuat aestus\n\n \n\nexpiratque foras in apertum promptaque caeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0054","text":"Sic et Averna loca alitibus summittere debent\n\n \n\nmortiferam vim. de terra quae surgit in auras,\n\n \n\nut spatium caeli quadam de parte venenet;\n\n \n\nquo simul ac primum pennis delata sit ales,\n\n \n\nimpediatur ibi caeco correpta veneno,\n\n \n\nut cadat e regione loci, qua derigit aestus.\n\n \n\nquo cum conruit, hic eadem vis illius aestus\n\n \n\nreliquias vitae membris ex omnibus aufert.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim primo quasi quendam conciet aestum;\n\n \n\nposterius fit uti. cum iam cecidere veneni\n\n \n\nin fontis ipsos, ibi sit quoque vita vomenda,\n\n \n\npropterea quod magna mali fit copia circum.\n\n \n\nFit quoque ut inter dum vis haec atque aestus Averni\n\n \n\naeëra, qui inter avis cumquest terramque locatus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0055","text":"discutiat, prope uti locus hic linquatur inanis.\n\n \n\ncuius ubi e regione loci venere volantis,\n\n \n\nclaudicat extemplo pinnarum nisus inanis\n\n \n\net conamen utrimque alarum proditur omne.\n\n \n\nhic ubi nixari nequeunt insistereque alis,\n\n \n\nscilicet in terram delabi pondere cogit\n\n \n\nnatura, et vacuum prope iam per inane iacentes\n\n \n\ndispergunt animas per caulas corporis omnis.\n\n \n\n[Gap in source]\n\n  \n\nfrigidior porro in puteis aestate fit umor,\n\n \n\narescit quia terra calore et semina si qua\n\n \n\nforte vaporis habet proprie, dimittit in auras.\n\n \n\nquo magis est igitur tellus effeta calore,\n\n \n\nfit quoque frigidior qui in terrast abditus umor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0056","text":"frigore cum premitur porro omnis terra coitque\n\n \n\net quasi concrescit, fit scilicet ut coeundo\n\n \n\nexprimat in puteos si quem gerit ipsa calorem.\n\n \n\nEsse apud Hammonis fanum fons luce diurna\n\n \n\nfrigidus et calidus nocturno tempore fertur.\n\n \n\nhunc homines fontem nimis admirantur et acri\n\n \n\nsole putant subter terras fervescere partim,\n\n \n\nnox ubi terribili terras caligine texit.\n\n \n\nquod nimis a verast longe ratione remotum.\n\n \n\nquippe ubi sol nudum contractans corpus aquai\n\n \n\nnon quierit calidum supera de reddere parte,\n\n \n\ncum superum lumen tanto fervore fruatur,\n\n \n\nqui queat hic supter tam crasso corpore terram\n\n \n\nperquoquere umorem et calido focilare vapore?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0057","text":"praesertim cum vix possit per saepta domorum\n\n \n\ninsinuare suum radiis ardentibus aestum.\n\n \n\nquae ratiost igitur? ni mirum terra magis quod\n\n \n\nrara tenet circum fontem quam cetera tellus\n\n \n\nmultaque sunt ignis prope semina corpus aquai.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi roriferis terram nox obruit undis,\n\n \n\nextemplo penitus frigescit terra coitque.\n\n \n\nhac ratione fit ut, tam quam compressa manu sit,\n\n \n\nexprimat in fontem quae semina cumque habet ignis,\n\n \n\nquae calidum faciunt laticis tactum atque vaporem.\n\n \n\n inde ubi sol radiis terram dimovit obortus\n\n \n\net rare fecit calido miscente vapore,\n\n \n\nrursus in antiquas redeunt primordia sedes\n\n \n\nignis et in terram cedit calor omnis aquai.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0058","text":"frigidus hanc ob rem fit fons in luce diurna.\n\n \n\npraeterea solis radiis iactatur aquai\n\n \n\numor et in lucem tremulo rarescit ab aestu;\n\n \n\npropterea fit uti quae semina cumque habet ignis\n\n \n\ndimittat; quasi saepe gelum, quod continet in se,\n\n \n\nmittit et exsolvit glaciem nodosque relaxat.\n\n  \n\nFrigidus est etiam fons, supra quem sita saepe\n\n \n\nstuppa iacit flammam concepto protinus igni,\n\n \n\ntaedaque consimili ratione accensa per undas\n\n \n\nconlucet, quo cumque natans impellitur auris.\n\n \n\nni mirum quia sunt in aqua permulta vaporis\n\n \n\nsemina de terraque necessest funditus ipsa\n\n \n\nignis corpora per totum consurgere fontem\n\n \n\net simul exspirare foras exireque in auras,\n\n \n\nnon ita multa tamen, calidus queat ut fieri fons;\n\n \n\npraeterea dispersa foras erumpere cogit\n\n \n\nvis per aquam subito sursumque ea conciliari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0059","text":"quod genus endo marist Aradi fons, dulcis aquai\n\n \n\nqui scatit et salsas circum se dimovet undas;\n\n \n\net multis aliis praebet regionibus aequor\n\n \n\nutilitatem opportunam sitientibus nautis,\n\n \n\nquod dulcis inter salsas intervomit undas.\n\n \n\nsic igitur per eum possunt erumpere fontem\n\n \n\net scatere illa foras; in stuppam semina quae cum\n\n \n\nconveniunt aut in taedai corpore adhaerent,\n\n \n\nardescunt facile extemplo, quia multa quoque in se\n\n \n\nsemina habent ignis stuppae taedaeque tenentes.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam, nocturna ad lumina linum\n\n \n\nnuper ubi extinctum admoveas, accendier ante\n\n \n\nquam tetigit flammam, taedamque pari ratione?\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea prius ipso tacta vapore\n\n \n\neminus ardescunt quam comminus imbuat ignis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0060","text":"hoc igitur fieri quoque in illo fonte putandumst.\n\n  \n\nQuod super est, agere incipiam quo foedere fiat\n\n \n\nnaturae, lapis hic ut ferrum ducere possit,\n\n \n\nquem Magneta vocant patrio de nomine Grai,\n\n \n\nMagnetum quia sit patriis in finibus ortus.\n\n \n\nhunc homines lapidem mirantur; quippe catenam\n\n \n\nsaepe ex anellis reddit pendentibus ex se.\n\n \n\nquinque etenim licet inter dum pluresque videre\n\n \n\nordine demisso levibus iactarier auris,\n\n \n\nunus ubi ex uno dependet supter adhaerens\n\n \n\nex alioque alius lapidis vim vinclaque noscit;\n\n \n\nusque adeo permananter vis pervalet eius.\n\n \n\nHoc genus in rebus firmandumst multa prius quam\n\n \n\nipsius rei rationem reddere possis,\n\n \n\net nimium longis ambagibus est adeundum;\n\n \n\nquo magis attentas auris animumque reposco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","text":"Principio omnibus ab rebus, quas cumque videmus,\n\n \n\nperpetuo fluere ac mitti spargique necessest\n\n \n\ncorpora quae feriant oculos visumque lacessant.\n\n \n\n perpetuoque fluunt certis ab rebus odores;\n\n \n\nfrigus ut  fluviis, calor a sole, aestus ab undis\n\n \n\naequoris, exesor moerorum, litora propter;\n\n \n\nnec varii cessant sonitus manare per auras;\n\n \n\ndenique in os salsi venit umor saepe saporis,\n\n \n\ncum mare versamur propter, dilutaque contra\n\n \n\ncum tuimur misceri absinthia, tangit amaror.\n\n \n\nusque adeo omnibus ab rebus res quaeque fluenter\n\n \n\nfertur et in cunctas dimittitur undique partis\n\n \n\nnec mora nec requies interdatur ulla fluendi,\n\n \n\nperpetuo quoniam sentimus et omnia semper\n\n \n\ncernere odorari licet et sentire sonare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","text":"Nunc omnis repetam quam raro corpore sint res\n\n \n\ncommemorare; quod in primo quoque carmine claret.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim, quamquam multas hoc pertinet ad res\n\n \n\nnoscere, cum primis hanc ad rem protinus ipsam,\n\n \n\nqua de disserere adgredior, firmare necessest\n\n \n\nnil esse in promptu nisi mixtum corpus inani.\n\n \n\nprincipio fit ut in speluncis saxa superna\n\n \n\nsudent umore et guttis manantibus stillent.\n\n \n\nmanat item nobis e toto corpore sudor,\n\n \n\ncrescit barba pilique per omnia membra, per artus.\n\n \n\ndiditur in venas cibus omnis, auget alitque\n\n \n\ncorporis extremas quoque partis unguiculosque.\n\n \n\nfrigus item transire per aes calidumque vaporem\n\n \n\nsentimus, sentimus item transire per aurum\n\n \n\natque per argentum, cum pocula plena tenemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0063","text":"denique per dissaepta domorum saxea voces\n\n \n\npervolitant, permanat odor frigusque vaposque\n\n \n\nignis, qui ferri quoque vim penetrare sueëvit,\n\n \n\ndenique qua circum caeli lorica coeërcet,\n\n \n\nmorbida visque simul, cum extrinsecus insinuatur;\n\n \n\net tempestate in terra caeloque coorta\n\n \n\nin caelum terrasque remotae iure facessunt;\n\n \n\nquandoquidem nihil est nisi raro corpore nexum.\n\n  \n\nHuc accedit uti non omnia, quae iaciuntur\n\n \n\ncorpora cumque ab rebus, eodem praedita sensu\n\n \n\natque eodem pacto rebus sint omnibus apta.\n\n \n\nprincipio terram sol excoquit et facit are,\n\n \n\nat glaciem dissolvit et altis montibus altas\n\n \n\nextructas nives radiis tabescere cogit;\n\n \n\ndenique cera lique fit in eius posta vapore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0064","text":"ignis item liquidum facit aes aurumque resolvit,\n\n \n\nat coria et carnem trahit et conducit in unum.\n\n \n\numor aquae porro ferrum condurat ab igni,\n\n \n\nat coria et carnem mollit durata calore.\n\n \n\nbarbigeras oleaster eo iuvat usque capellas,\n\n \n\neffluat ambrosias quasi vero et nectare tinctus;\n\n \n\nqua nihil est homini quod amarius fronde ac extet.\n\n \n\ndenique amaracinum fugitat sus et timet omne\n\n \n\nunguentum; nam saetigeris subus acre venenumst;\n\n \n\nquod nos inter dum tam quam recreare videtur.\n\n \n\nat contra nobis caenum taeterrima cum sit\n\n \n\nspurcities, eadem subus haec iucunda videtur,\n\n \n\ninsatiabiliter toti ut volvantur ibidem.\n\n  \n\n Hoc etiam super est, ipsa quam dicere de re\n\n \n\nadgredior, quod dicendum prius esse videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","text":"multa foramina cum variis sint reddita rebus,\n\n \n\ndissimili inter se natura praedita debent\n\n \n\nesse et habere suam naturam quaeque viasque.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim varii sensus animantibus insunt,\n\n \n\nquorum quisque suam proprie rem percipit in se;\n\n \n\nnam penetrare alio sonitus alioque saporem\n\n \n\ncernimus e sucis, alio nidoris odores.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\npraeterea manare aliud per saxa videtur,\n\n \n\natque aliud lignis, aliud transire per aurum,\n\n \n\nargentoque foras aliud vitroque meare;\n\n \n\nnam fluere hac species, illac calor ire videtur,\n\n \n\natque aliis aliud citius transmittere eadem.\n\n \n\nscilicet id fieri cogit natura viarum\n\n \n\nmultimodis varians, ut paulo ostendimus ante,\n\n \n\npropter dissimilem naturam textaque rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066","text":"Qua propter, bene ubi haec confirmata atque locata\n\n \n\nomnia constiterint nobis praeposta parata,\n\n \n\nquod super est, facile hinc ratio reddetur et omnis\n\n \n\ncausa pate fiet, quae ferri pelliciat vim.\n\n  \n\nPrincipio fluere e lapide hoc permulta necessest\n\n \n\nsemina sive aestum, qui discutit aeëra plagis,\n\n \n\ninter qui lapidem ferrumque est cumque locatus.\n\n \n\nhoc ubi inanitur spatium multusque vace fit\n\n \n\nin medio locus, extemplo primordia ferri\n\n \n\nin vacuum prolapsa cadunt coniuncta, fit utque\n\n \n\nanulus ipse sequatur eatque ita corpore toto.\n\n \n\nnec res ulla magis primoribus ex elementis\n\n \n\nindupedita suis arte conexa cohaeret\n\n \n\nquam validi ferri natura et frigidus horror.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0067","text":"quo minus est mirum, quod dicitur esse alienum,\n\n \n\ncorpora si nequeunt e ferro plura coorta\n\n \n\nin vacuum ferri, quin anulus ipse sequatur;\n\n \n\nquod facit et sequitur, donec pervenit ad ipsum\n\n \n\niam lapidem caecisque in eo compagibus haesit.\n\n \n\nhoc fit idem cunctas in partis; unde vace fit\n\n \n\ncumque locus, sive e transverso sive superne,\n\n \n\ncorpora continuo in vacuum vicina feruntur;\n\n \n\nquippe agitantur enim plagis aliunde nec ipsa\n\n \n\nsponte sua sursum possunt consurgere in auras.\n\n \n\nhuc accedit item, quare queat id magis esse,\n\n \n\nhaec quoque res adiumento motuque iuvatur,\n\n \n\nquod, simul a fronte est anelli rarior aeër\n\n \n\nfactus inanitusque locus magis ac vacuatus,\n\n \n\ncontinuo fit uti qui post est cumque locatus\n\n \n\naeër a tergo quasi provehat atque propellat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0068","text":"semper enim circum positus res verberat aeër;\n\n \n\nsed tali fit uti propellat tempore ferrum,\n\n \n\nparte quod ex una spatium vacat et capit in se.\n\n \n\nhic, tibi quem memoro, per crebra foramina ferri\n\n \n\nparvas ad partis subtiliter insinuatus\n\n \n\ntrudit et inpellit, quasi navem velaque ventus.\n\n \n\n denique res omnes debent in corpore habere\n\n \n\naeëra, quandoquidem raro sunt corpore et aeër\n\n \n\nomnibus est rebus circum datus adpositusque.\n\n \n\nhic igitur, penitus qui in ferrost abditus aeër,\n\n \n\nsollicito motu semper iactatur eoque\n\n \n\nverberat anellum dubio procul et ciet intus,\n\n \n\nscilicet illo eodem fertur, quo praecipitavit\n\n \n\niam semel et partem in vacuam conamina sumpsit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0069","text":"Fit quoque ut a lapide hoc ferri natura recedat\n\n \n\ninter dum, fugere atque sequi consueta vicissim.\n\n \n\nexultare etiam Samothracia ferrea vidi\n\n \n\net ramenta simul ferri furere intus ahenis\n\n \n\nin scaphiis, lapis hic Magnes cum subditus esset;\n\n \n\nusque adeo fugere a saxo gestire videtur.\n\n \n\naere interposito discordia tanta creatur\n\n \n\npropterea quia ni mirum prius aestus ubi aeris\n\n \n\npraecepit ferrique vias possedit apertas,\n\n \n\nposterior lapidis venit aestus et omnia plena\n\n \n\ninvenit in ferro neque habet qua tranet ut ante;\n\n \n\ncogitur offensare igitur pulsareque fluctu\n\n \n\nferrea texta suo; quo pacto respuit ab se\n\n \n\natque per aes agitat, sine eo quod saepe resorbet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0070","text":"Illud in his rebus mirari mitte, quod aestus\n\n \n\nnon valet e lapide hoc alias impellere item res.\n\n \n\npondere enim fretae partim stant, quod genus aurum;\n\n \n\nat partim raro quia sunt cum corpore, ut aestus\n\n \n\npervolet intactus, nequeunt inpellier usquam,\n\n \n\nlignea materies in quo genere esse videtur.\n\n \n\ninterutrasque igitur ferri natura locata\n\n \n\naeris ubi accepit quaedam corpuscula, tum fit,\n\n \n\ninpellant ut eo Magnesia flumine saxa.\n\n  \n\nnec tamen haec ita sunt aliarum rerum aliena,\n\n \n\nut mihi multa parum genere ex hoc suppeditentur,\n\n \n\nquae memorare queam inter se singlariter apta.\n\n \n\nsaxa vides primum sola colescere calce.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0071","text":"glutine materies taurino iungitur una,\n\n \n\nut vitio venae tabularum saepius hiscant\n\n \n\nquam laxare queant compages taurea vincla.\n\n \n\nvitigeni latices aquai fontibus audent\n\n \n\nmisceri, cum pix nequeat gravis et leve olivom.\n\n \n\npurpureusque colos conchyli iungitur uno\n\n \n\ncorpore cum lanae, dirimi qui non queat usquam,\n\n \n\nnon si Neptuni fluctu renovare operam des,\n\n \n\nnon mare si totum velit eluere omnibus undis.\n\n \n\ndenique res auro non aurum copulat una,\n\n \n\naerique  plumbo fit uti iungatur ab albo?\n\n \n\ncetera iam quam multa licet reperire! quid ergo?\n\n \n\nnec tibi tam longis opus est ambagibus usquam\n\n \n\nnec me tam multam hic operam consumere par est,\n\n \n\nsed breviter paucis praestat comprendere multa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","text":"quorum ita texturae ceciderunt mutua contra,\n\n \n\nut cava conveniant plenis haec illius illa\n\n \n\nhuiusque inter se, iunctura haec optima constat.\n\n \n\nest etiam, quasi ut anellis hamisque plicata\n\n \n\ninter se quaedam possint coplata teneri;\n\n \n\n quod magis in lapide hoc fieri ferroque videtur.\n\n  \n\nNunc ratio quae sit morbis aut unde repente\n\n \n\nmortiferam possit cladem conflare coorta\n\n \n\nmorbida vis hominum generi pecudumque catervis,\n\n \n\nexpediam, primum multarum semina rerum\n\n \n\nesse supra docui quae sint vitalia nobis,\n\n \n\net contra quae sint morbo mortique necessest\n\n \n\nmulta volare; ea cum casu sunt forte coorta\n\n \n\net perturbarunt caelum, fit morbidus aeër.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","text":"atque ea vis omnis morborum pestilitasque\n\n \n\naut extrinsecus ut nubes nebulaeque superne\n\n \n\nper caelum veniunt aut ipsa saepe coorta\n\n \n\nde terra surgunt, ubi putorem umida nactast\n\n \n\nintempestivis pluviisque et solibus icta.\n\n \n\nnonne vides etiam caeli novitate et aquarum\n\n \n\ntemptari procul a patria qui cumque domoque\n\n \n\nadveniunt ideo quia longe discrepitant res?\n\n \n\nnam quid Brittannis caelum differre putamus,\n\n \n\net quod in Aegypto est, qua mundi claudicat axis,\n\n \n\nquidve quod in Ponto est differre et Gadibus atque\n\n \n\nusque ad nigra virum percocto saecla colore?\n\n \n\nquae cum quattuor inter se diversa videmus\n\n \n\nquattuor a ventis et caeli partibus esse,\n\n \n\ntum color et facies hominum distare videntur\n\n \n\nlargiter et morbi generatim saecla tenere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","text":"est elephas morbus qui propter flumina Nili\n\n \n\ngignitur Aegypto in media neque praeterea usquam.\n\n \n\nAtthide temptantur gressus oculique in Achaeis\n\n \n\nfinibus. inde aliis alius locus est inimicus\n\n \n\npartibus ac membris; varius concinnat id aeër.\n\n \n\nproinde ubi se caelum, quod nobis forte alienum,\n\n \n\ncommovet atque aeër inimicus serpere coepit,\n\n \n\nut nebula ac nubes paulatim repit et omne\n\n \n\nqua graditur conturbat et immutare coactat,\n\n \n\nfit quoque ut, in nostrum cum venit denique caelum,\n\n \n\ncorrumpat reddatque sui simile atque alienum.\n\n \n\nhaec igitur subito clades nova pestilitasque\n\n \n\naut in aquas cadit aut fruges persidit in ipsas\n\n \n\naut alios hominum pastus pecudumque cibatus,\n\n \n\naut etiam suspensa manet vis aeëre in ipso\n\n \n\net, cum spirantes mixtas hinc ducimus auras,\n\n \n\nilla quoque in corpus pariter sorbere necessest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0075","text":"consimili ratione venit bubus quoque saepe\n\n \n\npestilitas et iam pigris balantibus aegror.\n\n \n\nnec refert utrum nos in loca deveniamus\n\n \n\nnobis adversa et caeli mutemus amictum,\n\n \n\nan caelum nobis ultro natura corumptum\n\n \n\ndeferat aut aliquid quo non consuevimus uti,\n\n \n\nquod nos adventu possit temptare recenti.\n\n  \n\nHaec ratio quondam morborum et mortifer aestus\n\n \n\nfinibus in Cecropis funestos reddidit agros\n\n \n\nvastavitque vias, exhausit civibus urbem.\n\n \n\nnam penitus veniens Aegypti finibus ortus,\n\n \n\naeëra permensus multum camposque natantis,\n\n \n\nincubuit tandem populo Pandionis omni.\n\n \n\n inde catervatim morbo mortique dabantur.\n\n \n\nprincipio caput incensum fervore gerebant\n\n \n\net duplicis oculos suffusa luce rubentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0076","text":"sudabant etiam fauces intrinsecus atrae\n\n \n\nsanguine et ulceribus vocis via saepta coibat\n\n \n\natque animi interpres manabat lingua cruore\n\n \n\ndebilitata malis, motu gravis, aspera tactu.\n\n \n\ninde ubi per fauces pectus complerat et ipsum\n\n \n\nmorbida vis in cor maestum confluxerat aegris,\n\n \n\nomnia tum vero vitai claustra lababant.\n\n \n\nspiritus ore foras taetrum volvebat odorem,\n\n \n\nrancida quo perolent proiecta cadavera ritu.\n\n \n\natque animi prorsum  vires totius, omne\n\n \n\nlanguebat corpus leti iam limine in ipso.\n\n \n\nintolerabilibusque malis erat anxius angor\n\n \n\nadsidue comes et gemitu commixta querella,\n\n \n\nsingultusque frequens noctem per saepe diemque\n\n \n\ncorripere adsidue nervos et membra coactans\n\n \n\ndissoluebat eos, defessos ante, fatigans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0077","text":"nec nimio cuiquam posses ardore tueri\n\n \n\ncorporis in summo summam fervescere partem,\n\n \n\nsed potius tepidum manibus proponere tactum\n\n \n\net simul ulceribus quasi inustis omne rubere\n\n \n\ncorpus, ut est per membra sacer dum diditur ignis.\n\n \n\nintima pars hominum vero flagrabat ad ossa,\n\n \n\nflagrabat stomacho flamma ut fornacibus intus.\n\n \n\nnil adeo posses cuiquam leve tenveque membris\n\n \n\nvertere in utilitatem, at ventum et frigora semper.\n\n \n\nin fluvios partim gelidos ardentia morbo\n\n \n\nmembra dabant nudum iacientes corpus in undas.\n\n  \n\nmulti praecipites nymphis putealibus alte\n\n \n\ninciderunt ipso venientes ore patente:\n\n \n\ninsedabiliter sitis arida corpora mersans\n\n \n\naequabat multum parvis umoribus imbrem.\n\n \n\nnec requies erat ulla mali: defessa iacebant\n\n \n\ncorpora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0078","text":"mussabat tacito medicina timore,\n\n \n\nquippe patentia cum totiens ardentia morbis\n\n \n\nlumina versarent oculorum expertia somno.\n\n \n\nmultaque praeterea mortis tum signa dabantur:\n\n \n\nperturbata animi mens in maerore metuque,\n\n \n\ntriste supercilium, furiosus voltus et acer,\n\n \n\nsollicitae porro plenaeque sonoribus aures,\n\n \n\ncreber spiritus aut ingens raroque coortus,\n\n \n\nsudorisque madens per collum splendidus umor,\n\n \n\ntenvia sputa minuta, croci contacta colore\n\n \n\nsalsaque per fauces rauca vix edita tussi.\n\n \n\nin manibus vero nervi trahere et tremere artus\n\n \n\na pedibusque minutatim succedere frigus\n\n \n\nnon dubitabat. item ad supremum denique tempus\n\n \n\nconpressae nares, nasi primoris acumen\n\n \n\ntenve, cavati oculi, cava tempora, frigida pellis\n\n \n\nduraque in ore, iacens rictu, frons tenta manebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0079","text":"nec nimio rigida post artus morte iacebant.\n\n \n\noctavoque fere candenti lumine solis\n\n \n\naut etiam nona reddebant lampade vitam.\n\n \n\n quorum siquis, ut est, vitarat funera leti,\n\n \n\nulceribus taetris et nigra proluvie alvi\n\n \n\nposterius tamen hunc tabes letumque manebat,\n\n \n\naut etiam multus capitis cum saepe dolore\n\n \n\ncorruptus sanguis expletis naribus ibat.\n\n \n\nhuc hominis totae vires corpusque fluebat.\n\n  \n\nprofluvium porro qui taetri sanguinis acre\n\n \n\nexierat, tamen in nervos huic morbus et artus\n\n \n\nibat et in partis genitalis corporis ipsas.\n\n \n\net graviter partim metuentes limina leti\n\n \n\nvivebant ferro privati parte virili,\n\n \n\net manibus sine non nulli pedibusque manebant\n\n \n\nin vita tamen et perdebant lumina partim.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0080","text":"usque adeo mortis metus iis incesserat acer.\n\n \n\natque etiam quosdam cepere oblivia rerum\n\n \n\ncunctarum, neque se possent cognoscere ut ipsi.\n\n \n\nmultaque humi cum inhumata iacerent corpora supra\n\n \n\ncorporibus, tamen alituum genus atque ferarum\n\n \n\naut procul absiliebat, ut acrem exiret odorem,\n\n \n\naut, ubi gustarat, languebat morte propinqua.\n\n \n\nnec tamen omnino temere illis solibus ulla\n\n \n\ncomparebat avis, nec tristia saecla ferarum\n\n \n\nexibant silvis. languebant pleraque morbo\n\n \n\net moriebantur. cum primis fida canum vis\n\n \n\nstrata viis animam ponebat in omnibus aegre;\n\n \n\nextorquebat enim vitam vis morbida membris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","text":"incomitata rapi certabant funera vasta\n\n \n\nnec ratio remedii communis certa dabatur;\n\n \n\nnam quod ali dederat vitalis aeëris auras\n\n \n\nvolvere in ore licere et caeli templa tueri,\n\n \n\nhoc aliis erat exitio letumque parabat.\n\n  \n\nIllud in his rebus miserandum magnopere unum\n\n \n\naerumnabile erat, quod ubi se quisque videbat\n\n \n\nimplicitum morbo, morti damnatus ut esset,\n\n \n\ndeficiens animo maesto cum corde iacebat,\n\n \n\nfunera respectans animam amittebat ibidem.\n\n \n\nquippe etenim nullo cessabant tempore apisci\n\n \n\nex aliis alios avidi contagia morbi,\n\n \n\nlanigeras tam quam pecudes et bucera saecla,\n\n \n\nidque vel in primis cumulabat funere funus\n\n \n\nnam qui cumque suos fugitabant visere ad aegros,\n\n \n\nvitai nimium cupidos mortisque timentis\n\n \n\npoenibat paulo post turpi morte malaque,\n\n \n\ndesertos, opis expertis, incuria mactans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0082","text":"qui fuerant autem praesto, contagibus ibant\n\n \n\natque labore, pudor quem tum cogebat obire\n\n \n\nblandaque lassorum vox mixta voce querellae.\n\n \n\noptimus hoc leti genus ergo quisque subibat.\n\n  \n\nPraeterea iam pastor et armentarius omnis\n\n \n\net robustus item curvi moderator aratri\n\n \n\nlanguebat, penitusque casa contrusa iacebant\n\n \n\ncorpora paupertate et morbo dedita morti.\n\n \n\nexanimis pueris super exanimata parentum\n\n \n\ncorpora non numquam posses retroque videre\n\n \n\nmatribus et patribus natos super edere vitam.\n\n \n\n nec minimam partem ex agris maeror is in urbem\n\n \n\nconfluxit, languens quem contulit agricolarum\n\n \n\ncopia conveniens ex omni morbida parte.\n\n \n\nomnia conplebant loca tectaque quo magis aestu,\n\n \n\nconfertos ita acervatim mors accumulabat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","text":"multa siti prostrata viam per proque voluta\n\n \n\ncorpora silanos ad aquarum strata iacebant\n\n \n\ninterclusa anima nimia ab dulcedine aquarum,\n\n \n\nmultaque per populi passim loca prompta viasque\n\n \n\nlanguida semanimo cum corpore membra videres\n\n \n\nhorrida paedore et pannis cooperta perire,\n\n \n\ncorporis inluvie, pelli super ossibus una,\n\n \n\nulceribus taetris prope iam sordeque sepulta.\n\n \n\nomnia denique sancta deum delubra replerat\n\n \n\ncorporibus mors exanimis onerataque passim\n\n \n\ncuncta cadaveribus caelestum templa manebant,\n\n \n\nhospitibus loca quae complerant aedituentes.\n\n \n\nnec iam religio divom nec numina magni\n\n \n\npendebantur enim: praesens dolor exsuperabat.\n\n \n\nnec mos ille sepulturae remanebat in urbe,\n\n \n\nquo prius hic populus semper consuerat humari;\n\n \n\nperturbatus enim totus trepidabat et unus\n\n \n\nquisque suum pro re  maestus humabat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0084","text":"multaque  subita et paupertas horrida suasit;\n\n \n\nnamque suos consanguineos aliena rogorum\n\n \n\ninsuper extructa ingenti clamore locabant\n\n \n\nsubdebantque faces, multo cum sanguine saepe\n\n \n\nrixantes, potius quam corpora desererentur,\n\n \n\ninque aliis alium populum sepelire suorum\n\n \n\ncertantes; lacrimis lassi luctuque redibant;\n\n \n\ninde bonam partem in lectum maerore dabantur;\n\n \n\nnec poterat quisquam reperiri, quem neque morbus\n\n \n\nnec mors nec luctus temptaret tempore tali.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8201c8637d0568b38859e1f3f0fa138349deacda8776dd129a51a0cb1483b032","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T235937339882Z_845352c4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book Six","source_sha256":"8201c8637d0568b38859e1f3f0fa138349deacda8776dd129a51a0cb1483b032","sha256":"802472fbcfa74cb0a36555be3f33f2b4c214c2b03a2443fb5f6db957cd970caa","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:59:37.339907+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","text":"## Book Six\n\nFirst fruit-bearing crops for sickly mortals\n\nonce distributed renowned Athens by her illustrious name,\n\nand renewed life and proposed laws,\n\nand first gave sweet consolations to life,\n\nwhen she bore the man found with such a heart,\n\nwho once poured forth all things from a truthful mouth;\n\nand because of whose discoveries, divine and spread abroad,\n\nthe ancient glory even now is borne to heaven, though he is dead.\n\nFor when this man saw that all things which need demands for sustenance\n\nwere now almost prepared for mortals,\n\nand that, so far as they could, life was established in safety,\n\nthat men abounded in wealth and honour and power of praise,\n\nand excelled in the good repute of their children,\n\nand yet that no one was any the less anxious at heart at home,\n\nand that they tormented life with an ungrateful mind without\n\nany respite and were compelled to rage with hostile complaints,\n\nhe understood then that the vessel itself produced the fault,\n\nand that through its fault all things were corrupted within\n\nwhich, brought together from without, came in however advantageous;\n\npartly because he saw it was leaky and pierced through,\n\nso that by no means could it ever be filled,\n\npartly because he perceived that it, as it were, befouled with a foul taste\n\nall things whatsoever it had received within.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","text":"Therefore he purified hearts with truthful sayings,\n\nand set a limit to desire and fear,\n\nand expounded what the highest good was, at which we all aim,\n\nand showed the road by which, along a narrow path,\n\nwe might strive straight towards it in a direct course,\n\nand what evil there might be everywhere in mortal affairs,\n\nwhich came to pass by nature and flew about in various ways,\n\nwhether by chance or by force, as nature had so ordained,\n\nand through what gates it was fitting to meet each thing;\n\nand he proved that the human race for the most part rolls in vain\n\nthe sad waves of cares within its breast.\n\nFor just as children tremble and fear all things\n\nin blind darkness, so we sometimes fear in the light\n\nthings no more to be feared than those which children in darkness\n\ndread and imagine to be about to happen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","text":"This terror of the mind, therefore, and these shadows must\n\nbe dispelled not by the rays of the sun nor the bright shafts of day,\n\nbut by the aspect and reason of nature.\n\nAll the more shall I proceed to weave out my undertaking in words.\n\nAnd since I have taught that the mortal structures of the world—heaven—\n\nconsist of a native body,\n\nand that whatever things occur in it and must occur\n\nfor the most part are dissolved, next receive what remains,\n\nsince once I have mounted the distinguished chariot\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nyou, for me running, point out beforehand the space to the bright paths prescribed at the uttermost end,\n\ncunning Muse Calliope, rest of men and delight of the gods,\n\nthat under your guidance I may win the crown with distinguished praise.\n\n[Gap in source]\n\n the winds arise, all things are again appeased\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nwhich had been, are changed by fury being appeased.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","text":"The other things which mortals behold coming to pass in earth and heaven,\n\nwhen they often hang in fearful minds,\n\nmake their spirits lowly through fear of the gods\n\nand press them down to earth, because\n\nignorance of causes compels them to refer things\n\nto the dominion of gods and concede sovereignty.\n\nFor those who have well learned that the gods pass an untroubled age,\n\nif nevertheless meanwhile they wonder by what means\n\neach thing can be done, especially in those matters\n\nwhich are seen above their heads in the ethereal regions,\n\nthey are carried back again into the old religions\n\nand take unto themselves harsh masters, whom, miserable as they are, they believe\n\nto be able to do all things, ignorant what can be,\n\nwhat cannot be, and finally what limited power belongs to each,\n\nby what principle and with what deeply fixed boundary;\n\nall the more are they borne astray by blind reasoning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","text":"Unless you reject these things from your mind and cast far away\n\nthe thought that they are unworthy of the gods and alien to their peace,\n\nthe sacred divinities of the gods, wronged by you, will often harm you—\n\nnot because the supreme power of the gods can be violated,\n\nso that it may drink in cruel penalties from anger,\n\nbut because you yourself will establish that the great gods, tranquil in peaceful repose,\n\nroll the waves of angers;\n\nnor will you approach the shrines of the gods with a peaceful breast,\n\nnor will you be able, in the tranquil peace of your mind,\n\nto receive those images which are borne from their sacred bodies\n\ninto the minds of men, messengers of divine form.\n\nFrom this one may see what manner of life now follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0006","text":"Although the truest reasoning casts that life far away from us,\n\nalthough many things have been set forth by me,\n\nmany nevertheless remain and must be adorned in polished\n\nverse; the nature of heaven AND fire must be grasped,\n\nstorms and bright thunderbolts must be sung,\n\nwhat they do and for what cause they are borne in every case,\n\nlest, mad with fear at the divided quarters of heaven,\n\nyou tremble whence flying fire has come or towards which quarter\n\nit has turned itself, how it has slipped through enclosed places,\n\nand how, having emerged from them, it has raised itself to mastery.\n\nFirst, the blue regions of heaven are shaken by thunder\n\nbecause ethereal clouds, flying on high, clash together\n\nwith winds fighting against one another.\n\nFor no sound is produced from the clear part of heaven;\n\nbut wherever clouds are in a denser mass,\n\nthere from this cause a rumbling often arises with great roar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","text":"Moreover, clouds cannot consist of a body so dense\n\nas stones and wood, nor indeed so thin\n\nas mists and flying smokes;\n\nfor they would either have to fall, pressed down by their dull weight,\n\nlike stones, or, like smoke, could not hold together\n\nnor retain chill snows and showers of hail.\n\nThey also give a sound over the plains of the open world,\n\nas canvas once stretched in great theatres\n\ngives a crackling, tossed among masts and beams,\n\nand sometimes, torn through, rages under wanton winds\n\nand imitates the brittle rustling of sheets;\n\nfor you may recognize this kind too in thunder,\n\nor when suspended cloth and flying sheets\n\nthe blows of winds toss and beat through the air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0008","text":"It also happens sometimes that clouds are able not so much to clash\n\nwith opposing fronts as to move sideways\n\nwith divergent motion, grazing their bodies continuously,\n\nwhence the dry sound rubs the air for a long time\n\nand is prolonged until they have left the narrow regions.\n\nIn this fashion too all things often seem, shaken by thunder,\n\nto tremble with a heavy motion, and suddenly the mighty walls\n\nof the vast world to have been burst apart,\n\nwhen the gathered storm of strong winds has hurled itself\n\ninto the clouds and, enclosed there,\n\nwith a whirling eddy compels the cloud more and more on every side\n\nto become hollow around with a thick body;\n\nafterward, when its force and fierce onset have worn it thin,\n\nthen, split apart, it gives forth a crash with terrifying sound.\n\nNor is it surprising, since a little bladder full of air\n\noften, suddenly burst, makes no little sound.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0009","text":"There is also a means by which, when winds blow through clouds,\n\nthey make sounds; for we often see\n\nbranching and rough clouds borne along in many ways;\n\njust as, plainly, when the blasts of the northwesterly wind blow\n\nthrough a thick forest, the leaves give a sound and the branches a crash.\n\nIt also happens that sometimes the impetuous force of a strong wind\n\ntears a cloud apart, breaking it with a direct onset;\n\nfor what a blast can do there the manifest fact teaches,\n\nhere, where it is gentler, when nevertheless on the deep earth\n\nit overturns trees and draws them up from their lowest roots.\n\nThere are also waves through the clouds which give, as it were, a murmur\n\nas they break heavily; and the same happens in deep\n\nrivers and in the great sea, when the surge is broken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0010","text":"It also happens when the burning force of thunderbolt falls\n\nfrom cloud into cloud; if the latter by chance has received much\n\nmoisture, it immediately kills the fire with a great cry,\n\nas glowing iron from hot furnaces once\n\nhisses when we plunge it near at hand into cold water.\n\nFurther, if a drier cloud receives fire,\n\nit is burned, suddenly kindled, with a huge sound,\n\nas if flame should wander through laurel-covered mountains\n\nin a whirlwind of winds, burning with great onset;\n\nnor is anything burned with a more terrible sound than\n\nthe Delphic laurel of Phoebus, with crackling flame.\n\nFinally, often the great crash and fall of ice\n\nmakes a sound high in great clouds;\n\nfor when wind packs them tightly, the compacted mountains\n\nof storm-clouds, mingled with hail, are broken into a narrow space.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0011","text":"Likewise it flashes when clouds have shaken out many seeds of fire\n\nby their collision, as if one stone\n\nshould strike another stone or iron; for then too light\n\nleaps out and fire scatters bright sparks.\n\nBut thunder comes to be received by our ears after\n\nthe eyes perceive the flashing, because things which move sight\n\nalways arrive more slowly at the ears.\n\nThis too you may learn from the following: if from afar you see someone\n\ncutting down a tall tree with a two-edged axe,\n\nit comes about that you see the stroke before the blow\n\ngives its sound to the ears; so too we see lightning before\n\nwe receive thunder, which is sent forth together with fire,\n\nborn from the same cause, from the same collision.\n\nIn this way too clouds dye places with swift light,\n\nand the storm flashes with trembling onset.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0012","text":"When wind has entered a cloud and, whirling there,\n\nhas caused the cloud, as I taught before, to grow dense and hollow,\n\nit grows hot by its own motion; just as you see all things\n\nheated thoroughly by motion grow incandescent, while even a leaden\n\nbullet, rolled by a long course, melts.\n\nTherefore, when this hot wind has torn through the black cloud,\n\nit scatters suddenly, as though forced out by violence, seeds\n\nof heat, which make the flashing gleams of flame;\n\nthen sound follows, which reaches the ears more slowly\n\nthan those things which reach the light of our eyes.\n\nPlainly this happens in dense clouds, and when at the same time they are piled\n\nhigh, one above another, with marvellous force.\n\nLet it not deceive you that we see from below\n\nhow much broader they are than how far upward they extend.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0013","text":"For observe, when winds carry clouds, made like mountains,\n\nsideways through the air,\n\nor when you see them heaped over great mountains,\n\none above another and pressing down from above,\n\nset in their stations, with winds buried on every side;\n\nthen you will be able to recognize their great masses\n\nand to discern their caverns, as it were, built of hanging rocks,\n\nwhich, when a storm has arisen, winds\n\nfill and, shut in, rage with a great murmur\n\nwithin the clouds and, in the manner of wild beasts, threaten in their caves;\n\nnow from this side, now from that, they send roarings through the clouds,\n\nand, seeking a way, wheel about and roll up seeds\n\nof fire within the clouds, and so compel many together\n\nand whirl flame inside the hollow furnaces,\n\nuntil, with the cloud burst apart, they have flashed forth brightly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0014","text":"For this cause also that mobile golden colour\n\nof liquid fire flies down to earth,\n\nbecause it is necessary that the clouds themselves have very many\n\nseeds of fire; indeed, when they are without any moisture,\n\ntheir colour for the most part is fiery and bright.\n\nFor it is necessary that they receive many things from the sun's light,\n\nso that deservedly they grow red and pour forth fires.\n\nTherefore when driving wind has thrust these together into one place\n\nand, forcing them, compressed them into one spot, they pour forth the squeezed-out\n\nseeds which make the colours of flame flash.\n\nIt flashes likewise when the clouds of heaven also grow thin;\n\nfor when wind lightly draws them apart as they move\n\nand dissolves them, those seeds which make the lightning must necessarily\n\nfall out without welcome. Then it flashes without foul\n\nterror and sounds, and with no tumult.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0015","text":"As for the rest, that thunderbolts consist of endowed nature\n\ntheir strokes declare, and the burnt marks of heat\n\nand traces of heavy-smelling sulphur breathing out fumes;\n\nfor these are signs of fire, not of wind nor rain.\n\nMoreover, they often set the roofs of houses ablaze,\n\nand with swift flame hold sway in the very buildings.\n\nNature has composed for you this subtle fire, with the first fires,\n\nof minute and mobile\n\nbodies, to which nothing at all can offer resistance.\n\nFor the strong thunderbolt passes through the enclosures of houses,\n\nlike a cry and voices; it passes through stones, through bronze,\n\nand in an instant makes bronze and gold liquid.\n\nIt also causes wine suddenly to escape from unbroken vessels,\n\nbecause, plainly, its heat coming upon them easily\n\nloosens all the surroundings and makes the sides of the vessel porous,\n\nand, slipping into the wine itself,\n\nby its motion loosens and disperses the first-beginnings of the wine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0016","text":"This the heat of the sun does not seem able to accomplish,\n\nalthough it is so powerful in brilliant heat.\n\nSo much more mobile and masterful is this force.\n\nNow in what way these things are produced, and by so great an onset\n\ncome to be such that they can split open towers with a blow,\n\ndestroy houses, tear away timbers and beams,\n\nbreak down and overthrow monuments of men,\n\nkill men, lay cattle low everywhere,\n\nand by what force they can do all the other things of this kind,\n\nI shall explain, nor shall I linger longer over my promises.\n\nThunderbolts must be thought to be produced from thick clouds,\n\nbuilt up high; for they are never sent from a clear sky\n\nnor from clouds lightly dense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0017","text":"For manifest fact teaches beyond doubt that this happens:\n\nbecause then clouds thicken throughout the whole air,\n\nso that we may think that all darkness has left Acheron\n\nand filled the great caverns of heaven,\n\næto such a degree, when the foul night of storm-clouds has arisen,\n\nthe faces of black dread hang over us from above,æ\n\nwhen the storm begins to forge thunderbolts.\n\nMoreover, very often a black cloud too over the sea,\n\nlike a river of pitch sent down from heaven, thus falls,\n\nladen with darkness, upon the waves from afar and draws with it a black\n\nstorm, heavy with thunderbolts and squalls,\n\nitself filled especially with fires and winds,\n\nso that on land too men shudder and seek shelter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0018","text":"Thus, therefore, we must think that a high storm is above\n\nour head; for the lands would not be overwhelmed by so great\n\ndarkness, unless many clouds had been built above,\n\nwith the sun shut out by many others;\n\nnor could they, as they came, oppress us with such rain\n\nas to make rivers overflow and plains swim,\n\nunless the air were built high with clouds.\n\nHere, therefore, all things are full of winds and fires;\n\nfor this reason roars and flashes happen everywhere.\n\nFor indeed I have taught above that hollow clouds have very many\n\nseeds of heat, and that they must necessarily\n\ntake in many things from the rays of the sun and their heat.\n\nWhen the same wind which drives them into one place,\n\nwhatever place it may be, has squeezed out many\n\nseeds of heat and mingled itself at the same time with that fire,\n\nthe vortex, having slipped in there, whirls in a narrow space\n\nand sharpens the thunderbolt within hot furnaces;\n\nfor it is kindled in two ways: it grows hot both through its own\n\nmobility and through contacts with fire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0019","text":"Then, when the force of the wind has grown hot, the heavy onset\n\nof fire begins; then, as though ripe, the thunderbolt\n\nsuddenly tears through the cloud and, a flashing heat, is borne on,\n\nilluminating every place with brilliant lights.\n\nA heavy sound follows it, so that the structures of heaven above\n\nseem to be suddenly burst apart and to crush us.\n\nThen a trembling violently assails the lands, and murmurs run through\n\nthe deep heaven; for then almost the whole\n\nstorm, shaken, trembles and roarings are set in motion.\n\nFrom this concussion follows a heavy and abundant rain,\n\nso that all the air seems to be turned into rain\n\nand, rushing downward, to return to a deluge;\n\nso great is the shower sent by the bursting cloud and storm of wind,\n\nwhen the sound flies forth with the burning stroke.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0020","text":"There is also a time when the impetuous force of wind from without\n\nfalls upon a strong cloud at its ripened height;\n\nwhen it tears this apart, immediately that fiery\n\nwhirl falls, which by our ancestral name we call thunderbolt.\n\nThe same occurs in other directions, wherever the force has borne it.\n\nIt also happens sometimes that a force of wind sent without fire\n\nnevertheless catches fire in its course and long passage,\n\nwhile it comes, shedding in its flight certain large bodies\n\nwhich cannot penetrate through the air equally,\n\nand carrying along other small ones scraped from the air itself,\n\nwhich, mingled in flight, make fire;\n\nby a reason not at all different from that by which a leaden\n\nbullet often becomes hot in its course, when, giving off many\n\nbodies of coldness, it has caught fire in the air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0021","text":"It also comes about that the force of the very blow kindles fire,\n\nwhen the cold force of wind, sent without fire, has struck;\n\nunless perhaps because, when it has struck with a violent blow,\n\nelements of heat can flow together from the thing itself\n\nand at the same time from that which then receives the blow;\n\nas, when we strike a stone with iron, fire flies out,\n\nnor, because the force of iron is cold, do for that reason\n\nthe seeds of hot brightness fail to meet at the blow.\n\nThus therefore a thing too must be set alight by lightning,\n\nif by chance it has been suitable and fit for flames.\n\nNor indeed can the force of wind altogether plainly be cold,\n\nthat force which is sent with so great violence,\n\nwithout either being first kindled by fire on its course,\n\nor at least arriving warmed and mingled with heat.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0022","text":"Moreover, the swiftness of lightning comes about, and its heavy blows,\n\nand lightning for the most part runs through with rapid fall,\n\nbecause its force first gathers itself wholly in the clouds\n\nand takes up a great effort of going;\n\nthen, when the cloud has been unable to contain the increase of its onset,\n\nthe force is squeezed out, and therefore flies with wondrous impetus,\n\nlike things discharged from powerful engines of war.\n\nAdd that it consists of small and light elements,\n\nnor is anything easily able to resist such a nature;\n\nfor it flees and penetrates through the sparse spaces of paths,\n\ntherefore it does not linger through many obstacles in delaying,\n\nand for this reason flies, falling with swift impetus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0023","text":"Then, because by their whole nature all weights strive downward,\n\nwhen a blow is added, moreover,\n\nits swiftness is doubled and that impetus becomes more weighty,\n\nso that it more violently and swiftly scatters by its blows\n\nwhatever obstacles delay it, and follows its course.\n\nFinally, because it comes with a long-continued impetus, it must\n\ntake on swiftness again and again, which increases as it goes\n\nand augments its strong powers and strengthens its blow;\n\nfor it causes whatever seeds belong to it\n\nto be borne, as it were, all together into one place from the opposite direction,\n\ncasting all things into that rolling course.\n\nPerhaps, coming from itself, it draws along some bodies\n\nfrom the air, which kindle its swiftness by blows.\n\nAnd it comes unharmed through things and passes intact through many,\n\nbecause liquid fire makes its way through their openings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0024","text":"And it breaks through many things, when the bodies of lightning itself\n\nfall upon the bodies of things by which they are woven together.\n\nMoreover, it easily dissolves bronze and suddenly makes gold\n\nboil, because its force is made of small, minute\n\nbodies and of light elements,\n\nwhich easily insinuate themselves and, once insinuated, suddenly\n\ndissolve every knot and loosen the bonds.\n\nAnd more in autumn, when the stars shine, the high\n\nabode of heaven is shaken on every side, and the whole earth,\n\nand when the flowering seasons of spring unfold themselves.\n\nFor fires are lacking in cold, and winds fail in heat,\n\nnor are clouds of such dense body.\n\nTherefore, when the intermediate seasons of heaven prevail,\n\nthen all the diverse causes of lightning meet together.\n\nFor the very turning of the year mingles cold and heat.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0025","text":"Both of these are needed for a cloud that must fashion lightnings,\n\nso that, in the discord of things and in great tumult,\n\nthe air may surge madly with fires and winds.\n\nFor the first part of heat is the last of cold:\n\nthat is the season of spring; wherefore it is necessary that things unlike\n\none another should fight and disturb the mixture.\n\nAnd the last heat, mingled with the first cold,\n\nrolls on in the season which bears the name of autumn;\n\nthere too fierce winters contend with summers.\n\nTherefore these are to be called the seasons of the year,\n\nnor is it surprising if at that time very many\n\nlightnings occur and a troubled storm is stirred in the sky,\n\nbecause it is disturbed by a double war on either side,\n\non this side by flames, on that by winds mingled with moisture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","text":"This is to discern the nature itself of fire-bearing lightning\n\nand to see by what force it does each thing,\n\nnot uselessly to unroll backward Tyrrhenian verses,\n\nseeking indications of the hidden mind of the gods—\n\nwhence the flying fire has come or toward which quarter\n\nit has turned itself from here, in what way through enclosed places\n\nit has insinuated itself, and how, having mastered them, it has emerged from them,\n\nor what harm the stroke of lightning can do from heaven.\n\nBut if Jupiter and the other shining gods\n\nshake the heavenly halls with terrifying sound\n\nand hurl fire wherever each has the will,\n\nwhy do they not cause those whose abominable crime is unguarded\n\nto breathe out flames of lightning, pierced through the breast,\n\na keen warning for mortals,\n\nand why rather does one conscious to himself of no shameful deed\n\nroll in flames, innocent, and is caught in the fiery\n\nwhirlwind of heaven, suddenly?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0027","text":"Why too do they strike deserted places and labour in vain?\n\nOr do they then accustom their arms and strengthen their muscles?\n\nAnd why do they allow their weapon to be blunted upon the earth of their father?\n\nWhy does he himself permit it and not spare his enemies?\n\nFinally, why does Jupiter never, from a sky wholly clear,\n\nhurl lightning down to earth and send forth its sound?\n\nOr, as soon as clouds have come up, does he then descend into them,\n\nso as from there nearby to direct the blows of his weapon?\n\nMoreover, for what reason does he cast it into the sea? What does he accuse—\n\nthe waves and the liquid mass and the swimming plains?\n\nFurthermore, if he wishes us to beware the stroke of lightning,\n\nwhy does he hesitate to make it possible for us to see it when launched?\n\nBut if he wishes to crush us with fire while we expect nothing,\n\nwhy does he thunder from that quarter, so that we can avoid it?\n\nWhy does he first stir up darkness, rumblings, and murmurs?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","text":"And how can you believe that he sends it at once into many quarters?\n\nOr would you never dare to maintain that this has happened,\n\nthat several strokes should be made at one time?\n\nYet it has often happened many times, and must happen,\n\nthat, just as rain falls in many regions and showers descend,\n\nso many lightnings should occur at one time.\n\nFinally, why does he shatter the holy shrines of the gods and their\n\nsplendid abodes with hostile lightning,\n\nand break the well-made images of the gods and by a violent wound\n\ntake away honour from their own figures?\n\nAnd why does he generally seek high places, and why do we see very many traces\n\nof his fire upon mountain summits?\n\nWhat remains is easy to learn from these things:\n\nby what means the Greeks’ presters, so named from the thing,\n\nwhen sent down from above, come into the sea.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0029","text":"For it happens that sometimes, like a column let down\n\nfrom heaven into the sea, there descends something around which the straits\n\nboil violently, stirred by blowing gusts,\n\nand whatever ships are then caught in that tumult\n\ncome, harried, into the utmost danger.\n\nThis happens when sometimes the aroused force of wind cannot\n\nbreak the cloud which it has begun upon, but presses it down, so that,\n\nlike a column let down from heaven, it reaches the sea,\n\ngradually, as though something were pushed down from above\n\nby the thrust of fist and arm and extended into the waters;\n\nwhen it splits this cloud, the force of wind bursts out from it into the sea\n\nand creates a wondrous boiling in the waves;\n\nfor the whirling vortex descends and at the same time\n\ndraws down that cloud with a slow body;\n\nas soon as it has thrust it, heavy, down to the levels of the sea,\n\nit suddenly casts itself wholly into the water and compels\n\nthe entire sea to boil, stirred with a mighty sound.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0030","text":"It also happens that the vortex of wind itself wraps itself in clouds,\n\nscraping together from the air the seeds of cloud,\n\nand as it were imitates a prester let down from heaven;\n\nwhen it has cast itself down upon the lands and dissolved,\n\nit vomits forth the immense force of whirlwind and storm.\n\nBut because this happens altogether rarely and mountains must\n\nstand in the way on land, the same thing appears more often\n\nin the wide prospect of the sea and in the open sky.\n\nClouds gather when many bodies, flying,\n\nhave suddenly come together above in this region of heaven—\n\nrather rough bodies, which can, though impeded in slight ways,\n\nbe held compressed among themselves.\n\nThese first cause small clouds to stand together;\n\nthen they grasp one another and flock together,\n\nand by joining grow and are carried by winds,\n\nuntil a savage storm has arisen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0031","text":"It also happens that mountain summits, the nearer they are to heaven,\n\nso much the more continually smoke, rising aloft,\n\nwith the thick darkness of a tawny cloud,\n\nbecause, when clouds first gather,\n\nbefore the eyes can see them while they are thin, winds\n\ncarrying them compel them to the highest mountain tops;\n\nthere at last it comes about that, when a greater throng has arisen\n\nand become dense, they can appear and at the same time be seen\n\nto rise into the upper air from the very mountain peak.\n\nFor the fact itself and sense declare that windy places lie open above,\n\nwhen we climb high mountains.\n\nMoreover, suspended garments, when they take on clinging moisture,\n\ndeclare that nature raises very many bodies also from the whole sea-shore.\n\nWherefore many things seem able also to rise\n\nfrom the salty movement of the sea for increasing clouds;\n\nfor every nature is akin to moistures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0032","text":"Moreover, from all rivers and from the earth itself\n\nwe see mists and vapour rise,\n\nwhich, like breaths, pressed out from here, are borne upward\n\nand cover the sky with their darkness and gradually,\n\nby coming together, furnish up high clouds.\n\nFor the heat too of the upper star-bearing ether presses upon them\n\nand, as though by thickening, weaves the blue sky beneath the rain-clouds.\n\nIt also happens that there come into this heaven from outside those\n\nbodies which make clouds and flying rain-clouds;\n\nfor I have shown that their number is innumerable and the sum of the deep\n\nis infinite, and I have shown with what swiftness\n\nbodies fly and how suddenly they are accustomed to cross\n\nunutterable space.\n\nTherefore it is not surprising if, in a short time, storms and darkness\n\nso often cover seas and lands from above with such great winds,\n\nsince through all the passages of ether\n\nand, as it were, through the breathing-holes round about the great world,\n\nan outlet and entrance has been granted to the elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0033","text":"Come now, I shall explain in what manner rainy moisture gathers\n\nin the high clouds and, sent down, falls upon the lands as rain. First,\n\nI shall establish that many seeds of water rise into the clouds themselves\n\nat once from all things, and that both grow alike—\n\nboth the clouds and whatever water is in the clouds—\n\njust as body grows in us together with blood,\n\nand likewise sweat and whatever moisture there is finally in the limbs.\n\nThey also often take up much sea moisture,\n\nas hanging fleeces of wool do,\n\nwhen winds carry clouds above the great sea.\n\nBy a similar principle moisture is raised into clouds from all rivers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0034","text":"When many seeds of waters,\n\ngrown in many ways, have well come together there from every side,\n\nthe dense clouds strive to send out moisture in two ways: for the force\n\nof wind drives it onward, and the very abundance of rain-clouds, gathered in a greater throng,\n\npresses and weighs down from above and makes the rains flow out.\n\nFurthermore, when clouds too are made thin by winds\n\nor are dissolved, struck from above by the sun’s heat,\n\nthey send out rainy moisture and drip, as wax,\n\nmelting upon a hot fire, liquefies abundantly.\n\nBut heavy rain comes when clouds are violently pressed together\n\nby both force and the onset of wind.\n\nYet rains are accustomed to continue long and linger for a long time,\n\nwhen many seeds of water are stirred,\n\nand clouds and watering rain-clouds, one above another,\n\nare borne from every quarter in abundance,\n\nwhile the whole steaming earth exhales moisture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0035","text":"Here, when the sun has shone with its rays amid the dark storm,\n\nopposite the sprinkled rain-clouds,\n\nthen the colour of the rainbow appears in the black clouds.\n\nAll the other things which grow and are produced above,\n\nand which gather in the clouds—all things, absolutely\n\nall things: snow, winds, hail, cold hoar-frost,\n\nand the great force of freezing, the great hardening of waters,\n\nand the delay which everywhere restrains the eager rivers—\n\nit is nevertheless very easy to discover and see in the mind\n\nin what way all these things happen and why they are produced,\n\nwhen you have well learned what has been assigned to the elements.\n\nCome now, learn what principle exists for the motions of the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036","text":"And first of all, make yourself believe that the earth, below\n\njust as above, is everywhere full of windy\n\ncaverns and bears in its breast many lakes and many pools,\n\nand rocks and sheer stones;\n\nand you must suppose that many hidden rivers beneath the earth’s back\n\nroll along submerged waves by force;\n\nfor the nature of the thing itself requires it to be like itself everywhere.\n\nWith these things then joined beneath and laid below,\n\nthe earth above trembles, shaken by great collapses,\n\nwhen time has caused huge caverns beneath to fall in;\n\nfor whole mountains collapse, and suddenly great tremors\n\nspread widely from there by the concussion.\n\nAnd rightly so, since houses tremble along the road, shaken by carts\n\nof no great weight at all,\n\nnor do they leap any less if anything whatever on the road\n\njolts the iron-shod rims of wheels on both sides.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0037","text":"It also happens, when a great mass of earth through age rolls down from land\n\ninto great and vast pools of water,\n\nthat the earth too, wavering, is shaken by the wave of water;\n\njust as a vessel cannot stand firm, unless the moisture\n\nhas ceased to be tossed about in uncertain movement within.\n\nMoreover, when wind gathered through the hollow places beneath the earth\n\nleans from one side and, straining with great powers,\n\npresses upon the deep caverns,\n\nthe earth inclines where the force of wind presses downward.\n\nThen all structures standing upon the earth above,\n\nand each one in proportion as it rises more high toward heaven,\n\nthreaten to incline, thrust forth in the same direction,\n\nand their projecting beams hang, ready to fall.\n\nAnd men fear to believe that some deadly time and disaster\n\nawait the nature of the great world,\n\nwhen they see so great a mass of lands leaning over!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0038","text":"Unless the winds breathed out, no thing could check them\n\nor restrain them from going to destruction;\n\nbut now, because they breathe out in turns, grow heavy,\n\nand return as though gathered together, then yield back when repelled,\n\nfor this reason the earth more often threatens ruin\n\nthan brings it about; for it inclines and then draws back\n\nand, having slipped down by its weight, receives back its own position.\n\nFor this reason all buildings sway,\n\nthe uppermost more than the middle, the middle than the lowest, the lowest very little.\n\nThis also is a cause of that same great earthquake.\n\nWhen a certain very great force of wind and breath,\n\narising suddenly either from outside or from the earth itself,\n\nhas cast itself into the hollow places of the earth, and there\n\nroars amid great caverns, borne about in tumult,\n\nthen, after the agitated force has burst forth, driven on,\n\nand at once, splitting the deep earth, creates a great chasm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0039","text":"This happened at Sidon in Syria and at Aegium\n\nin the Peloponnese: this issuing forth of breath\n\ndestroyed these cities, together with the earthquake that arose.\n\nMoreover, many walls have fallen in lands through great\n\nmovements, and many cities together with their inhabitants\n\nhave sunk beneath the sea.\n\nBut if it does not break forth, still the very impetus of breath\n\nand the wild force of wind is distributed like a shiver\n\nthrough the frequent openings of the earth and from there causes trembling;\n\njust as when cold comes deep into our limbs,\n\nit shakes us against our will, compelling us to tremble and move.\n\nTherefore men quake in cities with double terror:\n\nabove, they fear their houses; below, they fear the caverns\n\nof earth, lest nature suddenly dissolve them,\n\nor, torn apart, spread wide its own chasm\n\nand, in confusion, wish to fill it with its own ruins.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040","text":"Accordingly, though they may think heaven and earth\n\nincorruptible, consigned to eternal safety,\n\nyet sometimes the present force of danger itself\n\ninstils this spur of fear in some degree:\n\nthat the earth, suddenly withdrawn from beneath their feet, may be carried\n\ninto the abyss, and the exposed sum of things may follow\n\nutterly, and there may come to be the world’s confused ruin.\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nAt the outset they wonder that the nature of the sea does not become greater,\n\ninto which there is so great a flow of waters,\n\ninto which all rivers come from every quarter.\n\nAdd wandering rains and flying storms,\n\nall of which scatter and water seas and lands;\n\nadd its own springs: yet all this, compared with the whole sea,\n\nwill scarcely be the increase of a single drop;\n\nwherefore it is less surprising that the great sea does not increase.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0041","text":"Moreover, the sun withdraws a great part of the moisture by its heat.\n\nFor we see the sun dry garments wet with moisture by its burning rays;\n\nbut we see the sea spread out wide and vast.\n\nTherefore, although from each single place the sun may sip a small portion of moisture from the surface of the sea,\n\nyet over so great a space it will take much away from the waves.\n\nThen besides, winds too can carry off a great part of the moisture as they sweep over the sea; for we very often see in a single night paths dried by winds and the crusts of soft mud harden.\n\nMoreover, I have shown that clouds also take up much moisture, drawn from the great surface of the sea, and scatter it everywhere throughout the whole circle of lands, when it rains upon the earth and winds bear the clouds along.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0042","text":"Lastly, since the earth is porous in body and, joined to the shores of the sea, surrounds it on every side, moisture ought, just as it comes into the sea from the lands, likewise to seep from the salt sea into the lands;\n\nfor the saltness is strained away, and the substance of the moisture flows back and gathers at the heads of all rivers; thence it returns over the lands in a sweet stream, wherever the channel once cut has carried down its waters with liquid foot.\n\nNow I shall explain by what process fires sometimes breathe forth through the jaws of Mount Etna with so great a whirlwind; for no moderate disaster having arisen, the tempest of flame, reigning over the Sicilian fields, turned toward itself the faces of neighbouring peoples,\n\nwhen, seeing all the smoky temples of heaven glittering, they filled their fearful breasts with anxiety as to what new thing the nature of things might be devising.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","text":"In these matters you must look deeply and far, and survey all directions from afar, so that you may remember that the sum of things is profound, and may see how very small a part of the whole sum is the heaven, and what a minute fraction it constitutes—not even a whole part—of the entire earth, one man.\n\nIf you contemplate this proposition well and see it clearly, you will cease to wonder at many things.\n\nFor does any one of us wonder if someone has taken into his limbs a fever arising with hot burning, or any other pain of disease through his members?\n\nFor suddenly a foot swells, sharp pain often seizes the teeth, attacks the very eyes; sacred fire arises and, creeping over the body, burns whatever part it has seized and makes its way through the limbs—no wonder, since there are seeds of many things, and this earth and heaven bear enough of evil disease from which the force of an immense malady may grow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","text":"Thus, then, we must suppose that from the infinite all things are supplied in sufficient abundance to the entire heaven and earth, whence the earth, suddenly shaken, may be moved, and a swift whirlwind may run through sea and lands, Etnean fire may abound, and heaven blaze;\n\nfor this too happens, and the heavenly regions burn, and storms of rain arise with a more violent outbreak, when by chance the seeds of waters have so borne themselves.\n\n“But the troubled burning heat is exceedingly great.”\n\nTo be sure, any river that seems greatest to one who has not before seen a greater is great; and a tree and a man seem great, and everything of every kind which each person has seen greatest he imagines immense, though all things together with heaven, earth, and sea are nothing in comparison with the entire sum of the whole.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0045","text":"Yet now I shall explain in what ways the flame, suddenly stirred, blows forth from the vast furnaces of Etna. First, the nature of the whole mountain is for the most part hollow beneath, supported by caverns of rock.\n\nMoreover, in all caverns there is wind and air.\n\nFor wind comes into being when air is set in motion and stirred up.\n\nWhen this has grown thoroughly hot and, raging, has heated all the rocks and earth around it wherever it touches them, and has struck hot fire from them with swift flames,\n\nit lifts itself and thus casts itself high up through the straight jaws.\n\nThus it carries its heat far, scatters ashes far and wide, rolls forth smoke in thick darkness, and at the same time drives out rocks of wondrous weight; so that you need not doubt that this is the stormy force of air.\n\nMoreover, for a great part of its extent the sea breaks its waves at the mountain’s roots and loosens the surge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","text":"From this sea caverns reach beneath to the high jaws of the mountain. It must be admitted that by this way\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nthings are compelled to go and penetrate deeply from the open sea, and therefore to blow forth outside and raise up flame, hurl up rocks, and lift clouds of sand.\n\nFor on the highest summit are craters, as they themselves name them, which we call jaws and mouths.\n\nThere are also some things of which it is not enough to state one cause, but several, though one alone may be the actual cause; as if you yourself should see from afar the lifeless body of a man lying there, it would be fitting to state all causes of death, so that one might be named as his;\n\nfor you could not establish that he had died by the sword, or by cold, or by disease, or perhaps by poison; but we know that something of this kind has befallen him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0047","text":"Likewise, in many matters we must speak in this way.\n\nThe Nile rises in summer and overflows its fields, the single river throughout the earth of all Egypt.\n\nIt waters Egypt often in the midst of heat, either because in summer the north winds blow against its mouths at that season of the year which they say is the time of the Etesian winds, and, blowing against the river, delay it and, forcing its waters upstream, fill it and make them remain.\n\nFor without doubt these blasts are borne against the stream, driven from the icy stars of the pole;\n\nthat river comes from the southward, from the heat-bearing region, rising deep among the black races of men, scorched in complexion, from the middle region of day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0048","text":"It may also be that a great accumulation of sand blocks the mouths from the opposing waves, when the sea, stirred by winds, rushes sand inward;\n\nby which means the river has a less free outlet, and likewise the downward rush of its waters becomes less.\n\nIt may also be that perhaps rains occur more abundantly at its source at the season when the Etesian blasts of the north cast all clouds toward those regions.\n\nFor surely, when they have gathered, cast toward the middle region of day, the clouds, driven together there, are at last forced and pressed upon the high mountains.\n\nPerhaps it rises deep in the high mountains of the Ethiopians, where the sun that surveys all things compels white snows to descend into the plains by his melting rays.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0049","text":"Come now, I shall explain to you what manner of places and lakes Avernian places are, and with what nature they are endowed.\n\nFirst, that they are called by the name of Avernian is due to this: they are hostile to all birds, because, when flying creatures have come opposite those places, forgetting the oarage of their wings they slacken the sails and fall headlong, their necks drooping, to the ground, if by chance the nature of the places so brings it about, or into water, if a lake of Avernus lies beneath.\n\nSuch a place is by Cumae, filled with the sharp sulphur of a mountain, where it smokes, increased by hot springs.\n\nThere is one also within the walls of Athens, on the very summit of the citadel, beside the temple of kindly Tritonian Pallas, where hoarse crows never set down their winged bodies, not even when the altars smoke with offerings;\n\nso greatly do they flee—not because of the keen wrath of Pallas, on account of the watchful cause, as the poets of the Greeks have sung—but the nature of the place itself accomplishes the work by its own power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","text":"In Syria too, likewise, there is said to be a place to be seen where, as soon as four-footed beasts have set their first steps, a force itself compels them to fall heavily, as though they had suddenly been sacrificed to the divine powers.\n\nAll these things occur by natural process, and the origin from which they arise is evident;\n\nlest it be believed that this is a gate into the regions of Orcus, and lest afterwards we suppose that the gods of the shades draw souls hence to the shores of Acheron below, just as winged stags are often thought to draw the creeping tribes of wild beasts from their lairs with their nostrils.\n\nLearn how far this has been driven from true reason; for I now endeavour to speak of the matter itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0051","text":"First I say this, which I have also said often before: that in the earth there are figures of things of every kind;\n\nmany which are vital for food, and many which can inflict diseases and hasten death.\n\nAnd we have shown before that different things are more suited to the mode of life of different living creatures, because of their dissimilar nature, their different textures among themselves, and their primary figures.\n\nMany hostile things pass through the ears; many things, noxious and harsh to the touch, make their way in through the nostrils themselves; nor are there few things which must be avoided by touch, or shunned by sight, or are bitter in taste.\n\nNext, you may see how many things are foul and grievous to a human being by their sharply hostile sensation; first, a heavy shade has been allotted to certain trees, so much so that they often cause headaches if anyone lies stretched out beneath them on the grass.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0052","text":"There is also a tree in the great mountains of Helicon, accustomed to kill a man by the foul smell of its blossom.\n\nSurely all these things arise from the earth because the earth bears, mixed together and yet separately distributed, many seeds of many things in many ways.\n\nAnd when the keen smell of a newly extinguished night-lamp strikes the nostrils, it immediately puts to sleep one who is accustomed to collapse and foam at the mouth from disease.\n\nAnd a woman, overcome by the heavy smell of castor, lies back; and bright work slips from her tender hands at the time when she has smelt it while discharging her menses.\n\nAnd many other things relax the languishing limbs throughout the body and unsettle the soul in its inner seats.\n\nFinally, if you linger even in hot baths when full and wash yourself in a basin of burning water, how easily does it happen that you often fall down in the midst of it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0053","text":"And how easily the heavy force and smell of charcoal make their way into the brain, unless we have first taken water!\n\nBut when a burning fever has taken hold, subduing the limbs, then the smell of wine becomes like a blow fit for slaughter.\n\nDo you not see, too, that sulphur is generated in the earth itself and bitumen congeals with a foul smell; and finally, where men follow veins of silver and gold, searching out with iron the hidden recesses of the earth, what odours does Scaptensula breathe out below?\n\nOr what evil smell do gilded metals exhale!\n\nWhat faces and what complexions do they give men!\n\nDo you not see or hear how in a short time they are wont to perish, and how greatly they lack the means of life, whom the mighty force of necessity confines in such work?\n\nThus the whole earth seethes with these heats and breathes them forth outside into the open and ready space of heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0054","text":"Thus Avernian places too must send up from the earth into the air a death-dealing force for birds, so that it poisons some portion of the space of heaven.\n\nAs soon as a bird has first been borne into it on wings, it is hampered there, seized by unseen poison, so that it falls straight down in the direction toward which the vapour is directed.\n\nWhen it has fallen, this same force of the vapour takes from all its limbs the remaining remnants of life.\n\nFor indeed at first it stirs up, as it were, a certain disturbance; afterwards it happens that, when the things of poison have already fallen into the springs themselves, life too must there be vomited forth, because a great abundance of evil arises around it.\n\nIt also happens sometimes that this force and vapour of Avernus disperses the air which lies between the bird and the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0055","text":"Thus this place is left nearly empty.\n\nWhen flying creatures have come opposite this place, the vain effort of their wings immediately fails, and every exertion of both wings is betrayed.\n\nWhen they cannot struggle or keep themselves aloft on their wings, nature, plainly, compels them to glide down to earth by their weight; and, lying now almost in a void through the empty space, they scatter their souls through all the channels of the body.\n\n[Gap in source]\n\nMoreover, water in wells becomes colder in summer, because the earth dries up with heat and, if it happens to contain seeds of vapour of its own, sends them forth into the air.\n\nTherefore, the more exhausted the earth is by heat, the colder becomes the moisture hidden in the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0056","text":"Moreover, when all the earth is pressed by cold, it contracts and, as it were, congeals; and in contracting it naturally presses out into wells whatever heat it itself contains.\n\nBy the shrine of Hammon there is said to be a spring cold in daylight and hot in the time of night.\n\nMen marvel excessively at this spring and suppose that, when night covers the earth in dreadful darkness, it is heated beneath the ground in part by the keen sun.\n\nBut this is far removed from true reason.\n\nFor when the sun, touching the bare body of the water, cannot render it hot from above, though the light above enjoys so great a heat, how can it here beneath, through so thick a body of earth, boil the moisture through and warm it with hot vapour?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0057","text":"Especially since it can scarcely make its heat penetrate with burning rays through the walls of houses.\n\nWhat, then, is the explanation? Surely it is because the earth about the spring is more porous than the rest of the earth, and there are many seeds of fire close to the body of the water.\n\nWhen night has overwhelmed the earth with dew-bearing waters, the earth immediately grows cold deep within and contracts.\n\nBy this process it happens that, as though compressed by a hand, it presses into the spring whatever seeds of fire it has, which make the touch and vapour of the water hot.\n\nThen, when the risen sun has disturbed the earth with his rays and made it porous by mingling hot vapour, the first-beginnings of fire return again to their former seats, and all the heat of the water retreats into the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0058","text":"For this reason the spring becomes cold in daylight.\n\nMoreover, the moisture of the water is agitated by the sun’s rays and grows porous in the light under the trembling heat; therefore it happens that it releases whatever seeds of fire it has—just as frost often sends forth what it contains within itself, melts the ice, and loosens its knots.\n\nThere is also a cold spring above which tow often at once catches fire when flame has been taken up, and a torch, kindled in a like manner, shines over the waters wherever, floating, it is driven by the breeze.\n\nSurely this is because there are very many seeds of heat in the water, and it is necessary that bodies of fire rise from the earth itself throughout the entire spring and at the same time breathe out and pass forth into the air—not, however, so many that the spring can become hot;\n\nmoreover, a force compels them suddenly to burst forth dispersed through the water and to be gathered upward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0059","text":"Of this kind is the spring in the sea near Aradus, which bubbles with sweet water and drives away the salt waves around itself;\n\nand in many other regions the sea affords timely aid to thirsty sailors, because it pours forth sweet water amid the salt waves.\n\nThus those seeds can burst out and bubble forth through that spring; when they gather in tow or cling to the body of a torch, they immediately catch fire easily, because tow and torches too contain within themselves many seeds of fire.\n\nDo you not see also that, when you bring newly extinguished flax to a night-lamp, it catches fire before it has touched the flame, and a torch in like manner?\n\nAnd many other things, touched beforehand by the very vapour, catch fire from a distance before fire wets them at close range.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0060","text":"Thus we must suppose that this too occurs in that spring.\n\nFor what remains, I shall begin to explain by what covenant of nature it comes about that this stone can draw iron, which the Greeks call Magnet from its native name, because it arose in the native territory of the Magnetes.\n\nMen marvel at this stone; for it often makes a chain of rings hanging from itself.\n\nIndeed, one may sometimes see five or more suspended in descending order, tossed by light breezes, when one hangs beneath another, clinging to it, and each from another learns the force and bonds of the stone;\n\nso unfailingly does its force prevail.\n\nIn matters of this kind many points must first be established before you can give an account of the thing itself, and one must proceed by exceedingly long windings; therefore I ask for the more attentive ears and mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","text":"First, from all things whatsoever that we see,\n\nbodies must continually flow and be sent forth and scattered, \n\nwhich strike the eyes and provoke sight.\n\nAnd continually odours flow from certain things;\n\nas cold from rivers, heat from the sun, spray from the waves\n\nof the sea, the eater-away of walls, beside the shores;\n\nnor do varied sounds cease to stream through the air;\n\nfinally, moisture of a salty taste often comes into the mouth,\n\nwhen we wander beside the sea; and, conversely,\n\nwhen we see wormwood being diluted, bitterness touches us.\n\nSo greatly from all things each thing flows\n\nand is borne and emitted everywhere into all directions,\n\nnor is any delay or rest granted to its flowing,\n\nsince we continually perceive, and are always able\n\nto see, to smell, and to hear sounds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","text":"Now I shall set forth how sparse in body things are,\n\nas I mentioned; this is clear in each first poem.\n\nFor indeed, although it pertains to many matters to know this,\n\nabove all it is necessary for this very matter at once,\n\nconcerning which I undertake to discourse, to establish\n\nthat nothing is manifest except body mixed with void.\n\nFirst, it comes about that in caverns the rocks above\n\nsweat with moisture and drip with falling drops.\n\nLikewise sweat streams from our whole body,\n\nthe beard and hairs grow over all the limbs, over the members.\n\nAll food is distributed into the veins, and increases and nourishes\n\neven the furthest parts of the body and the nails.\n\nLikewise we perceive cold passing through bronze and hot vapour;\n\nwe perceive it likewise passing through gold\n\nand through silver, when we hold full cups.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0063","text":"Finally, through the stone partitions of houses voices\n\nfly through, odour and cold and the vapours\n\nof fire penetrate, which is accustomed even to penetrate the force of iron;\n\nfinally, wherever the armour of heaven constrains things round about,\n\ndisease and force alike, when they steal in from outside,\n\nand, when a storm has arisen on earth and in heaven,\n\nthey duly depart, carried off to heaven and earth;\n\nsince nothing exists save what is bound together with sparse body.\n\nTo this is added that not all bodies which are thrown off\n\nfrom whatever things are endowed with the same sensation\n\nand in the same manner are suited to all things.\n\nFirst, the sun bakes the earth and makes it dry,\n\nbut melts ice and compels the deep snows\n\npiled high on lofty mountains to waste away beneath its rays;\n\nfinally, wax becomes liquid placed near its vapour.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0064","text":"Likewise fire makes bronze liquid and melts gold,\n\nbut draws together and congeals hides and flesh into one.\n\nMoreover, the moisture of water hardens iron from fire,\n\nbut softens hides and flesh hardened by heat.\n\nThe wild olive delights the bearded goats so greatly,\n\nas though it flowed with ambrosia and were steeped in nectar;\n\nwhile nothing stands out more bitter to man than its leaf.\n\nFinally, the sow shuns marjoram and fears every\n\nointment; for to bristly swine it is sharp poison;\n\nthough to us at times it seems, as it were, to refresh us.\n\nBut, conversely, though mud is the foulest filth to us,\n\nthe same seems pleasant to swine,\n\nso that they roll there wholly, insatiably.\n\nThis also remains, concerning the very matter which I undertake to discuss,\n\nwhich seems necessary to be said first.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","text":"Since many openings have been allotted to diverse things,\n\nthey must be endowed with natures unlike one another\n\nand each have its own nature and paths.\n\nFor indeed various senses dwell in living creatures,\n\nand each of them properly perceives its own object within itself;\n\nfor we see sound penetrate by one way, taste from juices by another,\n\nand odours of scent by another.\n\nMoreover, one thing seems to stream through rocks,\n\nanother through wood, another to pass through gold,\n\nanother to make its way out through silver and glass;\n\nfor form seems to flow by this route, heat to go by that,\n\nand the same things to pass more swiftly through some than through others.\n\nClearly the nature of paths, varying in many ways,\n\ncompels this to occur, as we showed a little before,\n\nbecause of the unlike nature and textures of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066","text":"Therefore, when all these things have been well established and set in place,\n\nand have stood before us prepared and ready,\n\nwhat remains will readily from this point have its account rendered, and every\n\ncause will become clear which draws the force of iron.\n\nFirst, it is necessary that from this stone very many\n\nseeds, or an effluence, flow which by its blows disperses the air\n\nthat is situated between the stone and the iron.\n\nWhen this space is emptied and a large void\n\nis made in the middle, at once the first-beginnings of iron,\n\nfalling together into the void, drop down; and it comes about\n\nthat the ring itself follows and thus goes with its whole body.\n\nNor does any thing cohere more, entangled from its first elements\n\nby a closely connected structure,\n\nthan the nature and cold horror of strong iron.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0067","text":"Wherefore it is less strange, what is said to be alien,\n\nif more bodies born from the iron cannot be borne\n\ninto the void without the ring itself following;\n\nand it does follow, until it reaches\n\nthe stone itself and clings there in unseen fastenings.\n\nThe same occurs in every direction: wherever a void place\n\ncomes to be, whether sideways or above,\n\nthe neighbouring bodies are immediately carried into the void;\n\nfor they are driven by blows from elsewhere, nor can they themselves\n\nof their own accord rise upward into the air.\n\nTo this is likewise added, whereby it may be still more able to occur:\n\nthis matter too is assisted and aided by motion,\n\nbecause, as soon as the air before the ring has been made thinner,\n\nand the place more emptied and evacuated,\n\nit immediately comes about that whatever air is situated behind\n\nas it were drives and pushes it forward from the rear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0068","text":"For air placed all around always beats upon things;\n\nbut it comes about that at such a time it drives the iron,\n\nbecause space is vacant on one side and receives it into itself.\n\nThis air, of which I speak to you, having subtly crept\n\nthrough the close openings of the iron\n\ninto its small parts, thrusts and impels it, as wind does\n\na ship and sails.\n\nFinally, all things must have air within their body,\n\nsince they are of sparse body, and air\n\nis given and placed around all things.\n\nTherefore this air, which is hidden deep within the iron,\n\nis always tossed by restless motion, and thereby\n\nwithout doubt beats and stirs the ring within;\n\nclearly it is borne in that same direction whither it once rushed headlong\n\nand took up its endeavour toward the vacant part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0069","text":"It also happens that the nature of iron withdraws from this stone\n\nat times, accustomed alternately to flee and to follow.\n\nI have also seen Samothracian iron leap\n\nand iron filings at the same time rage inside bronze\n\nbowls, when this magnet-stone had been placed beneath;\n\nso greatly does it seem eager to flee from the stone.\n\nSuch great discord is produced when bronze is interposed,\n\nbecause, naturally, when first the effluence of bronze\n\nhas rushed in and occupied the open paths of the iron,\n\nthe later effluence from the stone comes and finds all things full\n\nin the iron, nor has it a way to pass through as before;\n\ntherefore it is forced to dash against and beat with its wave\n\nthe iron textures by its own force; in this way it repels them from itself\n\nand drives them through the bronze, though without it it often draws them back.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0070","text":"In these matters cease to wonder that the effluence\n\nfrom this stone cannot in the same way impel other things.\n\nFor some stand supported by weight, as gold does;\n\nothers, because they are of sparse body, so that the effluence\n\nflies through untouched, cannot be impelled anywhere,\n\namong which kinds woody material appears to be.\n\nThus, the nature of iron being placed between the two,\n\nwhen it has received certain corpuscles of bronze,\n\nthen it comes about that Magnesian stones impel it by that stream.\n\nYet these things are not so alien from other things\n\nthat many examples of this kind, severally suited among themselves,\n\nare not abundantly supplied to me,\n\nwhich I might recount. First you see stones alone adhere to lime.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0071","text":"Wood is joined together by bull's glue,\n\nso that the veins of planks more often gape because of it\n\nthan the bull's bonds of their structure can be loosened.\n\nWine-born liquids dare to mingle with springs of water,\n\nwhere heavy pitch cannot, nor light olive oil.\n\nAnd the purple dye of the shellfish is joined in one\n\nbody with wool, and cannot be separated anywhere,\n\nnot if you should renew the task with Neptune's wave,\n\nnor if the whole sea should wish to wash it out with all its waters.\n\nFinally, does not one thing join gold to gold,\n\nand cause bronze to be joined with white lead?\n\nHow many other things may now be found! What then?\n\nYou have no need anywhere of such long digressions,\n\nnor is it fitting for me to spend so much labour here,\n\nbut it is better briefly to comprehend many things in a few words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","text":"Those whose textures have thus fallen mutually opposite,\n\nso that these hollows fit the solids of that thing and those\n\nof this thing among themselves—this is the best junction.\n\nThere are also some things which, as though folded with rings and hooks,\n\ncan be held joined together;\n\nwhich seems to occur especially in this stone and in iron.\n\nNow I shall explain what is the account of diseases, or whence a baneful force,\n\narising suddenly, can produce\n\na deadly destruction for the race of men and herds of beasts.\n\nFirst, I have shown above that there are seeds of many things\n\nwhich are vital to us,\n\nand, conversely, that many must fly about which are necessary\n\nfor disease and death; when these by chance have arisen\n\nand disturbed the sky, the air becomes diseased.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","text":"And all this force of diseases and pestilence\n\neither comes from outside through the sky above, like clouds and mists,\n\nor often, arising from the earth itself, rises up\n\nwhere it has gained putrefaction, being moist\n\nand struck by untimely rains and suns.\n\nDo you not see also that those who come from far from country and home\n\nare affected by a change of sky and waters,\n\nbecause things differ widely?\n\nFor how different do we think the sky is in Britain,\n\nand that which is in Egypt, where the axis of the world slopes,\n\nor that in Pontus and at Gades, and onward\n\nto the black races of men, their colour baked through?\n\nSince we see these four things to be different among themselves\n\naccording to the four winds and regions of the sky,\n\nthen the colour and appearance of men seem to differ greatly,\n\nand diseases to hold races according to their kinds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","text":"There is an elephant disease that arises beside the river Nile\n\nin mid Egypt, and nowhere else.\n\nIn Attica the feet are affected, and the eyes in the Achaean\n\nlands. Hence one place is hostile to some parts,\n\nanother to others and to the limbs; the varying air causes this.\n\nAccordingly, when a sky foreign to us by chance\n\nmoves itself, and hostile air begins to creep along,\n\nas mist and cloud gradually creep and disturb everything\n\nwherever they proceed and compel it to change,\n\nit likewise comes about that, when it finally enters our sky,\n\nit corrupts it and renders it like itself and foreign.\n\nThus this sudden disaster and new pestilence\n\neither falls into the waters, or settles upon the crops themselves,\n\nor upon other fodder of men and food of cattle,\n\nor else its force remains suspended in the very air,\n\nand, when breathing we draw in the mingled airs from there,\n\nit is necessary that we also at the same time absorb it into the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0075","text":"By a like account pestilence often comes also to cattle,\n\nand sickness now to sluggish bleating creatures.\n\nNor does it matter whether we arrive in places\n\nhostile to us and change the covering of the sky,\n\nor whether nature itself brings to us a corrupted sky,\n\nor something that we are unaccustomed to use,\n\nwhich by its new arrival can assail us.\n\nThis account of diseases and deadly effluence once\n\nmade the fields within Cecrops' borders deadly\n\nand laid waste the roads, emptied the city of citizens.\n\nFor, having come deep from the borders of Egypt,\n\nhaving traversed much air and floating plains,\n\nit settled at last upon all the people of Pandion.\n\nThen they were given over in crowds to disease and death.\n\nFirst they bore a head inflamed with heat\n\nand glowing eyes suffused with double light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0076","text":"Their black throats too sweated within with blood, and the passage of voice,\n\nblocked by ulcers, closed up;\n\nand the tongue, interpreter of the mind, streamed with gore,\n\nweakened by evils, heavy in motion, rough to the touch.\n\nThen, when through the throat the diseased force had filled the chest\n\nand had flowed together into the very sorrowful heart of the sick,\n\nall the barriers of life then indeed gave way.\n\nThe breath from the mouth rolled forth a foul odour,\n\nas corpses cast out reek with their rancid fashion.\n\nAnd utterly the powers of the whole mind, the whole\n\nbody, languished, already on death's very threshold.\n\nAnd anxious anguish was a constant companion to intolerable evils,\n\nwith complaint mingled with groaning,\n\nand a frequent hiccup by night and often by day,\n\ncontinually compelling the sinews and limbs to contract,\n\nbroke them down, exhausting those already wearied.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0077","text":"Nor could you observe in anyone, by excessive heat,\n\nthe outermost part of the body burning exceedingly,\n\nbut rather a lukewarm touch presented to the hands,\n\nwhile the whole body at once reddened with ulcers as though burned,\n\nas when the sacred fire spreads through the limbs.\n\nBut the innermost part of men burned to the very bones,\n\nthere burned a flame within the stomach as in furnaces.\n\nNothing so light or thin could you put upon anyone's limbs\n\nto advantage, but always wind and cold.\n\nSome gave their limbs burning with disease to chill streams,\n\ncasting their naked body into the waters.\n\nMany headlong fell deep into the water-nymphs of wells,\n\ncoming upon them with mouth itself agape:\n\nan unquenchable dry thirst, plunging their bodies,\n\nmade much water equal to scant moisture.\n\nNor was there any rest from the evil: the bodies lay exhausted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0078","text":"Medicine muttered in silent fear,\n\nas their burning eyes, so often open in sickness,\n\nrolled about, deprived of sleep.\n\nAnd besides, many signs of death then appeared:\n\nthe mind's disturbed thought in sorrow and fear,\n\na grim brow, a frantic and fierce expression,\n\nears moreover troubled and full of sounds,\n\nbreathing frequent or great and arising at rare intervals,\n\nand bright moisture of sweat wetting the neck,\n\nthin scant spittle, touched with saffron colour,\n\nand salt, scarcely emitted through the throat by a hoarse cough.\n\nIndeed in the hands the sinews began to contract and the limbs to tremble,\n\nand cold did not hesitate gradually to advance from the feet.\n\nLikewise, finally at the last time,\n\nthe nostrils were compressed, the tip of the nose thin,\n\nthe eyes sunken, the temples hollow, the skin cold,\n\nand hard upon the face; it lay with the mouth agape, the brow stretched, and remained so.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0079","text":"Nor after death did their limbs lie excessively stiff.\n\nAnd at about the eighth bright light of the sun,\n\nor even the ninth torch, they gave up life.\n\nIf any one of them, as sometimes happened, had escaped the funeral rites of death,\n\nyet afterward wasting and death awaited him,\n\nwith foul ulcers and black flux of the belly;\n\nor else, often with great pain of the head,\n\ncorrupted blood flowed from his full nostrils.\n\nInto this all the powers and body of the man flowed away.\n\nMoreover, when the sharp flux of foul blood had gone out,\n\nthe disease nonetheless passed into the sinews and limbs\n\nand into the very genital parts of the body.\n\nAnd some, grievously fearing the thresholds of death,\n\nlived deprived by the knife of their manly part;\n\nand some remained in life without hands and feet,\n\nand some lost their eyes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0080","text":"So keenly had the fear of death assailed them.\n\nAnd some were even seized by forgetfulness of all things,\n\nso that they could not recognize themselves.\n\nAnd though many bodies lay unburied on the ground, piled over\n\nother bodies, yet the race of birds and beasts\n\neither leapt away far off, that the sharp smell might escape them,\n\nor, when it had tasted, languished in death nearby.\n\nNor indeed did any bird at all readily appear in those days,\n\nnor did the gloomy tribes of beasts come forth from the woods. Most languished\n\nwith disease and died. Above all the faithful force of dogs,\n\nstrewn in every road, laid down its life in distress;\n\nfor the diseased force wrenched life from their limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","text":"Unattended vast funerals strove to be carried off,\n\nnor was any sure common plan of remedy given;\n\nfor what had granted to one to draw the vital breezes\n\nof air in his mouth and to behold the regions of heaven,\n\nwas destruction to others and prepared death.\n\nOne thing in these circumstances was especially pitiable\n\nand grievous: when each man saw himself\n\nentangled in disease, as though condemned to death,\n\nfailing in spirit, he lay with a sorrowful heart,\n\nlooking upon funerals and thereupon gave up his life.\n\nFor indeed, at no time did the greedy contagions of disease cease\n\nto seize one from another,\n\nas wool-bearing flocks and horned herds;\n\nand this, above all, heaped funeral upon funeral.\n\nFor all who avoided visiting their sick relations,\n\ntoo desirous of life and fearful of death,\n\nsoon afterward paid the penalty by a shameful and evil death,\n\ndeserted and bereft of aid, slaughtered by neglect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0082","text":"But those who had been present went to their deaths through contagion\n\nand labor, which shame then compelled them to undertake,\n\nand through the winning voice of the exhausted, mingled with the voice of complaint.\n\nThus each best man underwent this kind of death.\n\nMoreover, now every shepherd and herdsman,\n\nand likewise the sturdy driver of the curved plough,\n\ngrew weak; and bodies, huddled deep within their huts,\n\nlay delivered over to death by poverty and disease.\n\nOver the lifeless bodies of parents, lifeless children,\n\nand again, over mothers and fathers, their offspring,\n\nyou might sometimes see them yield up life.\n\nNor did a smallest part of that affliction flow from the fields into the city,\n\nbrought there by the gathering multitude of sickly farmers\n\nconverging from every quarter.\n\nThey filled all places and dwellings; the more densely packed they were in the heat,\n\nthe more death piled them up in heaps.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","text":"Many bodies, struck down by thirst and rolled along the road and everywhere,\n\nlay stretched beside the water-basins,\n\ntheir breath cut off by an excessive sweetness of the waters;\n\nand in many public places and open streets, here and there,\n\nyou could see limbs with languid, half-living bodies\n\nperishing, hideous with filth and covered with rags,\n\nwith the body's uncleanness, skin alone over bones,\n\nalmost already buried beneath loathsome ulcers and dirt.\n\nFinally death had filled all the sacred shrines of the gods\n\nwith lifeless bodies, and everywhere\n\nthe temples of the heavenly gods remained burdened with corpses—\n\nplaces which the temple-keepers had filled with guests.\n\nNor now were the religion of the gods or their divinities\n\nheld in great account: present suffering prevailed.\n\nNor did that custom of burial remain in the city\n\nby which this people had formerly always been accustomed to be buried;\n\nfor, utterly disordered, it was all in a state of panic, and each man,\n\nsorrowful according to his means, buried his own.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0084","text":"And many sudden acts did dreadful poverty prompt;\n\nfor upon alien funeral-pyres, built up above their own kinsmen,\n\nthey placed them with a great outcry\n\nand put torches beneath them, often fighting\n\nwith much bloodshed rather than leaving the bodies behind;\n\nand, striving to bury one man upon another's pyres belonging to their own people,\n\nthey returned wearied by tears and mourning;\n\nthen in large part they gave themselves over to bed in grief.\n\nNor could anyone be found whom neither disease\n\nnor death nor mourning assailed at such a time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0084"]}],"source_digest":"3286085d4118fd83f6431b6b1e99817e482f8a2dafc2362aff5b50efc9728a59","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Liber Sextus"}],"passage_basis":"revised-passages","original_title":"Liber Sextus","title_language_code":"en"}],"units":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP","layer":"CH","title":"Book One","order":1,"parents":["RAW_0T6T_LIBP"],"revision":"CH_0T6T_LIBP@R001","sha256":"ee2f4cdcf2c30b36280924f6079d88c0d9f95870ecfe692eb0c95ba73bcc01a5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS1","text":"Quaeritur quibus principiis rerum natura constet et quomodo eorum cognitio terrorem religiosum dissolvat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS2","text":"Natura ex corporibus primis et inani constat; generationes, mutationes et interitus sunt compositiones aut dissolutiones materiae permanentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS3","text":"Argumentatio diagnostica, demonstrativa, polemica et reconstructiva est: causam terroris detegit, principia naturae probat, exemplaria aemula excludit et campum cosmologicum restituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS4","text":"Motus diagnosticus ab ignorantia causarum et animi ad timorem, a timore ad religionis dominationem, inde ad necessitatem rationis naturalis procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS5","text":"Motus probativus ab effectibus sensibilibus ad corpora invisibilia, ab existentia motus ad inane, ab utroque ad ontologiam bipartitam procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS6","text":"Motus reconstructivus a conservatione materiae ad primordia solida, a stabilitate generum ad immutabilitatem principiorum, inde ad variationem per ordinem, figuram et motum transit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS7","text":"Motus cosmologicus exemplaria finita, centralia et providentialia removet atque ordinem mundanum ex infinitis concursibus sine consilio producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","text":"Natura deorum a rebus humanis semota est neque meritis aut ira movetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","text":"Terror religiosus ex ignorantia causarum naturalium atque naturae animi nascitur et naturae ratione dissolvitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","text":"Sensus prima fides est qua ratiocinatio de rebus occultis confirmatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","text":"Nulla res e nihilo gignitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","text":"Nulla res ad nihilum interit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","text":"Genera ex seminibus certis intra finitas potestates naturae oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","text":"Omnis natura per se aut corpus aut inane est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A8","text":"Corpus tangendo agit et obstat, inane autem corporibus locum motuique cessionem praebet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9","text":"Qualitates, coniunctiones, eventa et tempus non sunt naturae per se, sed a corporibus, loco et motu dependent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A10","text":"Res genitae sunt concilia principiorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A11","text":"Primordia sunt solida et inanis expertia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A12","text":"Primordia sunt aeterna.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","text":"Primordia naturam immutabilem servant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A14","text":"Divisio corporis terminatur in minimis inseparabilibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15","text":"Diversitas rerum ex concursu, motu, ordine, positura et figura primordiorum efficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","text":"Summa corporis et inanis infinita est neque terminum externum aut medium universale habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","text":"Primordia perpetuo moventur et plagis inter se concurrunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A18","text":"Dispositiones stabiles ex innumeris concursibus per infinitum tempus sine consilio proveniunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX1","text":"Via rationis naturalis non est via impietatis aut sceleris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX2","text":"Religio non est necessarium pietatis praesidium, quia ipsa scelerosa facta suadere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX3","text":"Terror animi non radiis solis neque luce diei dissolvitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX4","text":"Generatio non est productio ex nihilo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX5","text":"Interitus non est reductio ad nihilum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX6","text":"Inobservabilitas corporis non probat eius inexist­entiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX7","text":"Natura non est plenum continuum sine intervallis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX8","text":"Tertia natura per se praeter corpus et inane non datur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX9","text":"Tempus non est substantia separata a motu et quiete rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX10","text":"Ignis, aer, aqua aut terra sensibilis non sunt soli neque sufficientes rerum principia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX11","text":"Primordia non sunt particulae homoeomericae eiusdem naturae ac res mortales quas constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX12","text":"Divisio corporum non procedit in infinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX13","text":"Summa rerum non continetur finibus externis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX14","text":"Medium universale ad quod omnia corpora naturaliter nitantur non datur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX15","text":"Primordia neque consilio ordinem elegerunt neque motus inter se pepigerunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D1","text":"Regularitas originum, temporum et incrementorum requirit materiam generativam determinatam.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D2","text":"Mors et corruptio nexus corporum solvunt, sed materiam constituentem non abolent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D3","text":"Effectus tactiles et mutationes sensibiles corpora caeca inferre permittunt, quamvis ipsa corpora oculis non cernantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D4","text":"Motus corporum impossibilis esset nisi inane corporibus cederet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D5","text":"Quidquid per se agit aut tangitur corpus est; quidquid transitum sine obstaculo praebet inane est; reliqua dependentia sunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D6","text":"Praeteritum, praesens et futurum ex ordine eventuum sentiuntur neque sine rerum motu subsistunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D7","text":"Dissolutio compositorum requirit principia quae dissolutioni supersint et materiam novis compositionibus suppeditent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D8","text":"Minima inseparabilia infinitam divisionem impediunt atque simplicitatem primordiorum conservant.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D9","text":"Stabilitas generum et finita potestas cuiusque rei immutabile substratum compositivum requirunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D10","text":"Mutatio rei non est conversio principiorum in alias substantias, sed aditus, abitus aut ordinatio diversa principiorum permanentium.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D11","text":"Exemplaria ex elementis sensibilibus aut partibus homoeomericis composita motum, conservationem, diversitatem et stabilitatem generum simul explicare nequeunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D12","text":"Quicumque terminus summae ponitur aliquid ultra se requirit; ideo terminus ultimus constitui nequit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D13","text":"Mundus ordinatus est dispositio localis et temporarie stabilis intra infinitam copiam materiae et inanis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D14","text":"Divinae minae auctoritatem coercitivam amittunt ubi eventus naturales ex causis naturalibus et dii ex humana administratione semoti intelleguntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD1","text":"Natura causaliter clauditur: defectus cognitionis causae non constituit locum interventionis divinae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","text":"Generatio et interitus constituunt circuitum conservationis in quo materia ex aliis formis solvitur et alias formas alit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:D1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D2","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","text":"Qualitates macroscopiae ex relationibus principiorum immutabilium oriuntur neque in singulis principiis eiusdem formae inesse debent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","text":"Cognitio naturae est empirica origine sed inferentialis extensione: sensus effectus praebet, ratio structuram invisibilem restituit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD5","text":"Possibilitas naturalis lege finitur: quid oriri possit ex natura principiorum et eorum compositionibus, non ex arbitrio externo, determinatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD6","text":"Ordo mundanus neque necessitatem consilii neque privilegium centri requirit, sed ex selectione dispositionum durabilium intra infinitos concursus emergit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD7","text":"Liberatio a religione non est mera negatio cultus, sed effectus epistemicus translationis terroris in quaestionem de natura.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX1","text":"Eventus cuius causa nondum cognoscitur non potest propter solam ignorantiam pro signo numinis haberi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX2","text":"Causa efficiens incorporea intra naturam excluditur, quia actio et passio tactum corporeum requirunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX3","text":"Qualitates sensibiles non possunt simul esse principia ultima et formae mortales compositorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX4","text":"Conversio absoluta unius naturae in aliam excluditur, quia sine substrato invarianti creationem ex nihilo et annihilationem restitueret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX5","text":"Apparentia sensibilis non est inventarium completum realitatis, quamvis sensus fundamentum probationis maneat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX6","text":"Homo, terra et mundus visibilis nullum privilegium centri cosmici possident.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX7","text":"Stabilitas ordinis non requirit intentionem, finem praestitutum aut consensum principiorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX1","text":"Initium absolutum et interitus absolutus e campo naturae excluduntur; solae compositiones et dissolutiones admittuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX2","text":"Providentia divina mundum administrans tacite removetur, quia dii remoti sunt et natura per leges materiales sufficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX3","text":"Poenae aeternae post mortem non possunt manere fundamentum rationalis actionis, cum earum vis ab ignorantia animi, non a natura demonstrata, pendeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX4","text":"Creator aut terminator extra summam rerum locari nequit, quia nihil extra infinitam summam conceditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX5","text":"Gubernatio Veneris ad litteram accepta cum remotione deorum componi nequit; vis invocationis tacite ad generationem naturalem, voluptatem poeticam et pacis desiderium transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS1","text":"Conditio enuntiandi sic constituitur: vis generatrix Veneris et pax postulata → animus ad doctrinam dispositus → investigatio naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS2","text":"Mechanismus expositionis sic operatur: doctrina obscura et ingrata → carmen suaviloquens → attentio retenta → ratio percepta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS3","text":"Catena diagnostica est: ignorantia causarum et animi → timor indefinitus → minae vatum et religio → subiectio animi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS4","text":"Catena epistemica est: effectus sensibilis → vestigium causae invisibilis → ratiocinatio → corpus caecum confirmatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS5","text":"Catena conservationis est: nihil ex nihilo et nihil ad nihilum → substratum permanens → primordia aeterna → dissolutio sine annihilatione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS6","text":"Catena ontologica est: tactus et actio → corpus; cessio et locus → inane; reliqua → coniuncta aut eventa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS7","text":"Catena compositionis est: primordia solida et immutabilia → ordines, figurae et motus diversi → qualitates et genera diversa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS8","text":"Examinatio exemplarium procedit per incompatibilitatem cum motu, conservatione, minimis et diversitate atque monismum, elementarismum et homoeomeriam excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS9","text":"Catena cosmologica est: nulla ora ultima → infinitum spatium et materia infinita → concursus perpetui → dispositiones stabiles sine consilio → mundus contingens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AF1","text":"Venus et pax → dispositio lectoris → diagnosis terroris religiosi → constitutio rationis naturalis → principia conservationis → corpora et inane → primordia immutabilia → infinitas summae → cosmogenesis sine consilio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AF2","text":"Regularitas sensibilis → semina certa → corpora invisibilia → inane necessarium → ontologia bipartita → compositio configurativa → exclusio principiorum sensibilium → naturalis causalitas clausa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME1","text":"Quod antea tamquam indicium numinis apparebat denuo describitur ut effectus naturalis cuius causa adhuc quaerenda est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME2","text":"Oppositio inter visibile et reale corruit; corpora invisibilia per effectus suos cognoscibilia fiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME3","text":"Ager ontologicus in corpus, inane et dependentias eorum reordinatur, tertia natura exclusa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME4","text":"Generatio et mors a creatione et abolitione ad compositionem et dissolutionem materiae transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME5","text":"Qualitates, historia et tempus a substantiis ad eventa corporum et loci reclas­sificantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME6","text":"Genera naturalia definiuntur per semina, compositiones et fines potestatum, non per conversionem arbitrariam elementorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME7","text":"Elementa sensibilia ex principiis ultimorum graduum ad effectus compositorum demoventur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME8","text":"Mundus visibilis ex totalitate finita et centrata in dispositionem localem intra infinitam summam convertitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME9","text":"Oppositio inter carmen et rationem dissolvitur: carmen fit instrumentum transmissionis, ratio autem manet norma probationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Exponuntur principia rerum ut lector a religione et metu mortis ad cognitionem naturae traducatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Generatio, motus, mutatio et interitus per corpora, inane, primordia et leges compositionis explicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Auctoritas religionis debilitatur, sensus cum ratione componitur et voluptas poetica ad doctrinam difficilem recipiendam adhibetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas primaria est corpus in inani; qualitates et tempus dependent, materia manet, summa caret termino, ordo autem ex processu infinito nascitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam catenam centralem a conservatione materiae ad corpora prima, inane, compositionem et infinitatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT2","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter impossibilitatem creationis aut annihilationis et necessitatem substrati aeterni.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT3","text":"Probatio inanis et corporum invisibilium cohaeret quatenus effectus sensibiles ad causas corporeas inferendas admittuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT4","text":"Natura animi, status post mortem et plena dissolutio terroris adhuc programmatice proponuntur, non hoc libro perfecte demonstrantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT5","text":"Causa prima motus, genera atomorum et mechanica plena concursuum nondum satis determinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT6","text":"Commissura epistemica manet inter fidem sensuum et necessitatem rationis quae apparentiam corrigit; regula correctionis non omnino formalizatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT7","text":"Notio minimi inseparabilis atomum partibus constare concedit, sed eandem atomum simplicem et indivisibilem retinet; relatio inter partem et divisionem tensionem servat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT8","text":"Invocatio Veneris cum deorum remotione cohaeret tantum si vis deae non tamquam interventio physica, sed tamquam figura generationis et pacis accipitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT9","text":"Lacunae finales plenam restitutionem argumenti contra medium cosmicum impediunt, sed motum ab infinitate ad cosmologiam non centralem non evertunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T230838757470Z_57ab5954","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book One","source_sha256":"ee2f4cdcf2c30b36280924f6079d88c0d9f95870ecfe692eb0c95ba73bcc01a5","sha256":"a8e97b3a07bd2388a34c9c0ca3d5919f1083e0a585ba711edc73ad0d522f7401","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:08:42.637941+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS1","text":"It is asked by what principles the nature of things consists and how knowledge of them dissolves religious terror.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS2","text":"Nature consists of primary bodies and void; generations, changes, and destructions are compositions or dissolutions of permanent matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS3","text":"The argument is diagnostic, demonstrative, polemical, and reconstructive: it uncovers the cause of terror, proves the principles of nature, excludes rival models, and restores the cosmological field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS4","text":"The diagnostic movement proceeds from ignorance of causes and of the mind's nature to fear, from fear to the domination of religion, and thence to the necessity of natural reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS5","text":"The probative movement proceeds from sensible effects to invisible bodies, from the existence of motion to void, and from both to a bipartite ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS6","text":"The reconstructive movement passes from the conservation of matter to solid first-beginnings, from the stability of kinds to the immutability of principles, and thence to variation through order, shape, and motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS7","text":"The cosmological movement removes finite, central, and providential models and produces cosmic order from infinite collisions without design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","text":"The nature of the gods is removed from human affairs and is moved neither by merits nor by anger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","text":"Religious terror arises from ignorance of natural causes and of the nature of the mind, and is dissolved by the account of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","text":"Sensation is the first trust by which reasoning about hidden things is confirmed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","text":"Nothing is generated from nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","text":"Nothing perishes into nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","text":"Kinds arise from determinate seeds within the finite powers of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","text":"Every nature is, in itself, either body or void.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A8","text":"Body acts and resists by touching, whereas void affords bodies room and yielding for motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9","text":"Qualities, conjunctions, events, and time are not natures in themselves, but depend on bodies, place, and motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A10","text":"Generated things are combinations of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A11","text":"First-beginnings are solid and devoid of void.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A12","text":"First-beginnings are eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","text":"First-beginnings preserve an immutable nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A14","text":"The division of body terminates in inseparable minima.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15","text":"The diversity of things is brought about by the collision, motion, order, position, and shape of first-beginnings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","text":"The totality of body and void is infinite and has neither an external boundary nor a universal center.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","text":"First-beginnings move perpetually and collide with one another by blows.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:A18","text":"Stable arrangements arise from innumerable collisions over infinite time without design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX1","text":"The way of natural reason is not a way of impiety or crime.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX2","text":"Religion is not a necessary safeguard of piety, because it itself can recommend criminal deeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX3","text":"Terror of the mind is not dissolved by the rays of the sun or the light of day.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX4","text":"Generation is not production from nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX5","text":"Destruction is not reduction to nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX6","text":"The unobservability of a body does not prove its nonexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX7","text":"Nature is not a full continuum without intervals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX8","text":"No third nature in itself besides body and void is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX9","text":"Time is not a substance separate from the motion and rest of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX10","text":"Perceptible fire, air, water, or earth are neither the sole nor sufficient principles of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX11","text":"First-beginnings are not homoeomeric particles of the same nature as the mortal things they constitute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX12","text":"The division of bodies does not proceed to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX13","text":"The totality of things is not contained by external boundaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX14","text":"No universal center toward which all bodies naturally strive is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX15","text":"First-beginnings neither chose order by design nor made a compact concerning motions with one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D1","text":"The regularity of origins, seasons, and growths requires determinate generative matter.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D2","text":"Death and decay dissolve the connections of bodies, but do not abolish the constituting matter.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D3","text":"Tactile effects and perceptible changes permit the inference of unseen bodies, although the bodies themselves are not discerned by the eyes.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D4","text":"The motion of bodies would be impossible unless void yielded to bodies.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D5","text":"Whatever acts or is touched in itself is body; whatever affords passage without obstacle is void; the rest are dependencies.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D6","text":"Past, present, and future are perceived from the order of events and do not subsist without the motion of things.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D7","text":"The dissolution of composites requires principles that survive dissolution and furnish matter for new compositions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D8","text":"Inseparable minima prevent infinite division and preserve the simplicity of first-beginnings.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D9","text":"The stability of kinds and the finite power of each thing require an immutable compositional substrate.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D10","text":"The change of a thing is not the conversion of principles into other substances, but the accession, departure, or different arrangement of permanent principles.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D11","text":"Models composed of perceptible elements or homoeomeric parts cannot simultaneously explain motion, conservation, diversity, and the stability of kinds.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D12","text":"Any boundary posited for the totality requires something beyond itself; therefore an ultimate boundary cannot be established.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D13","text":"An ordered world is a local and temporarily stable arrangement within an infinite abundance of matter and void.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:D14","text":"Divine threats lose coercive authority when natural events are understood from natural causes and the gods as removed from human administration.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD1","text":"Nature is causally closed: a deficiency in knowledge of a cause does not establish a place for divine intervention.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","text":"Generation and destruction constitute a circuit of conservation in which matter is dissolved from some forms and nourishes other forms.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:D1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D2","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","text":"Macroscopic qualities arise from relations among immutable principles and need not inhere in the individual principles in the same form.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","text":"Knowledge of nature is empirical in origin but inferential in extension: sensation provides effects, reason reconstructs invisible structure.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD5","text":"Natural possibility is bounded by law: what can arise from the nature of principles and their compositions, not from external will, is determined.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD6","text":"Cosmic order requires neither the necessity of design nor the privilege of a center, but emerges from the selection of durable arrangements amid infinite collisions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD7","text":"Liberation from religion is not a mere negation of worship, but an epistemic effect of translating terror into a question concerning nature.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX1","text":"An event whose cause is not yet known cannot, solely on account of ignorance, be considered a sign of divinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX2","text":"An incorporeal efficient cause within nature is excluded, because action and passion require bodily touch.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX3","text":"Perceptible qualities cannot simultaneously be ultimate principles and the mortal forms of composites.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX4","text":"The absolute conversion of one nature into another is excluded, because without an invariant substrate it would restore creation from nothing and annihilation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX5","text":"Perceptible appearance is not a complete inventory of reality, although sensation remains the foundation of proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX6","text":"Human being, earth, and the visible world possess no privilege of a cosmic center.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX7","text":"The stability of order requires neither intention, a predetermined end, nor the agreement of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX1","text":"Absolute beginning and absolute destruction are excluded from the domain of nature; only compositions and dissolutions are admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX2","text":"Divine providence governing the world is tacitly removed, because the gods are remote and nature suffices through material laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX3","text":"Eternal punishments after death cannot remain the foundation of rational action, since their force depends on ignorance of the mind, not on demonstrated nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX4","text":"A creator or terminator cannot be situated outside the totality of things, because nothing outside the infinite totality is granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX5","text":"The governance of Venus taken literally cannot be reconciled with the removal of the gods; the force of the invocation is tacitly transferred to natural generation, poetic pleasure, and the desire for peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS1","text":"The condition of enunciation is constituted thus: Venus's generative force and the peace sought → a mind disposed toward doctrine → investigation of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS2","text":"The mechanism of exposition operates thus: obscure and ungrateful doctrine → sweet-speaking poem → attention retained → reason apprehended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS3","text":"The diagnostic chain is: ignorance of causes and of the mind → indefinite fear → threats of seers and religion → subjection of the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS4","text":"The epistemic chain is: perceptible effect → trace of invisible cause → reasoning → unseen body confirmed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS5","text":"The conservation chain is: nothing from nothing and nothing into nothing → permanent substrate → eternal first-beginnings → dissolution without annihilation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS6","text":"The ontological chain is: touch and action → body; yielding and place → void; the rest → conjunctions or events.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS7","text":"The composition chain is: solid and immutable first-beginnings → different orders, shapes, and motions → different qualities and kinds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS8","text":"The examination of models proceeds through incompatibility with motion, conservation, minima, and diversity, and excludes monism, elementarism, and homoeomery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS9","text":"The cosmological chain is: no ultimate shore → infinite space and infinite matter → perpetual collisions → stable arrangements without design → contingent world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AF1","text":"Venus and peace → disposition of the reader → diagnosis of religious terror → establishment of natural reason → principles of conservation → bodies and void → immutable first-beginnings → infinity of the totality → cosmogenesis without design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:AF2","text":"Perceptible regularity → determinate seeds → invisible bodies → necessary void → bipartite ontology → configurative composition → exclusion of perceptible principles → closed natural causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME1","text":"What previously appeared as an indication of divinity is redescribed as a natural effect whose cause is still to be sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME2","text":"The opposition between visible and real collapses; invisible bodies become knowable through their effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME3","text":"The ontological field is reordered into body, void, and their dependencies, with a third nature excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME4","text":"Generation and death are transferred from creation and abolition to the composition and dissolution of matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME5","text":"Qualities, history, and time are reclassified from substances to events of bodies and place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME6","text":"Natural kinds are defined by seeds, compositions, and limits of powers, not by the arbitrary conversion of elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME7","text":"Perceptible elements are demoted from principles of ultimate rank to effects of composites.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME8","text":"The visible world is converted from a finite and centered totality into a local arrangement within an infinite totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME9","text":"The opposition between poem and reason is dissolved: poem becomes an instrument of transmission, while reason remains the norm of proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The principles of things are set forth so that the reader may be led from religion and fear of death to knowledge of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Generation, motion, change, and destruction are explained through bodies, void, first-beginnings, and laws of composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The authority of religion is weakened, sensation is combined with reason, and poetic pleasure is employed for receiving difficult doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Primary reality is body in void; qualities and time are dependent, matter remains, the totality lacks a boundary, and order arises from infinite process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central chain from the conservation of matter to primary bodies, void, composition, and infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT2","text":"The most secure structure is the connection between the impossibility of creation or annihilation and the necessity of an eternal substrate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT3","text":"The proof of void and invisible bodies coheres insofar as perceptible effects are admitted for inferring bodily causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT4","text":"The nature of the mind, the state after death, and the full dissolution of terror are still proposed programmatically, not perfectly demonstrated in this book.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT5","text":"The first cause of motion, the kinds of atoms, and the full mechanics of collisions are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT6","text":"An epistemic juncture remains between trust in the senses and the necessity of reason that corrects appearance; the rule of correction is not fully formalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT7","text":"The notion of the inseparable minimum allows the atom to consist of parts, yet retains that same atom as simple and indivisible; the relation between part and division preserves a tension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0041","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT8","text":"The invocation of Venus coheres with the removal of the gods only if the goddess's force is understood not as physical intervention, but as a figure of generation and peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT9","text":"Final gaps hinder a full reconstruction of the argument against a cosmic center, but do not overturn the movement from infinity to non-central cosmology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBP:PR75B5F8005E4E867B:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_0T6T_LIBP@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_0T6T_LIBP:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"Book One","context":"Chapter 01","original_title":"Liber Primus","original_context":"Chapter 01"},"original_title":"Liber Primus","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS","layer":"CH","title":"Book Two","order":2,"parents":["RAW_0T6T_LIBS"],"revision":"CH_0T6T_LIBS@R001","sha256":"657706ad03b9e80cb7d124947e76eb00b9b9666713ce1222ed3bf6877d0292fe","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quomodo motus, differentiae et concilia principiorum qualitates, animantia, mundos atque mutationes mundanas producant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS2","text":"Rerum natura ex corporibus primis aeternis in infinito inani motis constat, quorum figurae, ordines, positurae, concursus et motus omnia composita sine administratione divina efficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS3","text":"Argumentatio simul reconstructiva, diagnostica, antiteleologica et therapeutica est: ex effectibus conspicuis causas occultas restituit atque cognitionem earum contra metum adhibet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS4","text":"Motus progreditur ab insufficientia bonorum externorum ad mechanicam principiorum, inde ad formas et mixturas, tum ad emergentiam sensus, pluralitatem mundorum et mortalitatem mundi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS5","text":"Probatio alternat inter necessitatem deductam, analogiam ex rebus conspicuis, exclusionem exemplarium aemulorum et reductionem qualitatum ad dispositiones materiales.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS6","text":"Finis strategicus est naturam a providentia et fato clauso liberare, ut metus religiosus, mortis timor et falsa bonorum aestimatio ratione naturali dissolvantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1","text":"Rerum summa corporibus primis et inani infinito constat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A2","text":"Corpora prima solida, simplicia, magnitudine finita, aeterna atque immutabilia sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A3","text":"Summa materiae neque augetur neque deperit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A4","text":"Corpora prima per inane numquam quiescunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A5","text":"Pondus, plagae et minimum clinamen causas motuum principiorum constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A6","text":"Corpora in vacuo, quamvis ponderibus imparia, aequa celeritate feruntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","text":"Clinamen loco temporeque incertum seriem causarum necessariam rumpit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A8","text":"Figurae principiorum numero finitae sunt, sed principia cuiusque figurae numero infinita sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9","text":"Proprietates compositorum pendent a figuris, ordinibus, posituris, concursibus et motibus principiorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A10","text":"Omnis res conspicua pluribus generibus principiorum permixtis constat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11","text":"Non omnia principia omnibus modis inter se conecti possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A12","text":"Principia colore, odore, sapore, calore, frigore, sono et sensu carent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13","text":"Vita et sensus ex apto concilio principiorum insensilium oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14","text":"Mors coetum principiorum dissolvit, non principia ipsa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A15","text":"Materiae corpora numero infinito per spatium infinitum distribuuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A16","text":"Dei in pace a rebus humanis semoti naturam mundi neque propter homines creant neque administrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A17","text":"Res mundanae crescunt dum acceptio materiae iacturam superat, consistunt dum aequantur, atque decrescunt dum iactura superat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18","text":"Metus animi ratione naturae, non divitiis, nobilitate, imperio aut luce sensibili, depellitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX1","text":"Suavitas spectandi laborem alienum non ex aliena vexatione, sed ex conscientia propriae immunitatis nascitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX2","text":"Natura corporis aurum, purpuram, splendorem aedium et copias magnas non requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX3","text":"Divitiae, nobilitas, gloria et vis militaris curas, religionem et mortis timorem non expellunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX4","text":"Tenebrae animi radiis solis aut luce diei non dissolvuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX5","text":"Principia rerum cessando novos motus producere non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX6","text":"Motus flammarum sursum non est motus spontaneus contra pondus, sed effectus vis subiectae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX7","text":"Corpora graviora in vacuo leviora non assequuntur neque hoc modo plagas genitales efficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX8","text":"Clinamen non est motus obliquus sensibilis, sed declinatio minima.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX9","text":"Mundus propter homines divinitus creatus non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX10","text":"Terra neque dea sentiens neque mater divina proprie est; appellatio mythica tantum conceditur, si vera causalitas retinetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX11","text":"Quies rerum conspicuarum quietem principiorum non probat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX12","text":"Omnia principia promiscue omnibus rebus conecti non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX13","text":"Colores rerum ex principiis coloratis non oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX14","text":"Sensus animantium ex principiis iam sentientibus non componitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX15","text":"Hic mundus neque unicus neque cosmicum exemplar privilegio singulari praeditum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX16","text":"Defectus fecunditatis et labor crescente aetate non ex sola corruptione morali aut pietatis diminutione explicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D1","text":"Generatio est accessio et ordinatio principiorum, interitus autem eorum discessio et redistributio.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D2","text":"Aequalis casus per inane congressus non produceret, nisi plagae et minimum clinamen parallelismum motuum interrumperent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D3","text":"Clinamen principium motus non fataliter praefixi praebet, quo voluntas animantium intra naturam materialem collocatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D4","text":"Durities, fluiditas, penetrabilitas et effectus tactiles ex figuris atomorum et modis conexionis oriuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D5","text":"Color mutari potest sine mutatione principiorum, quia ex ordine, positura, motu et lumine resultat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D6","text":"Sensus, dolor et voluptas sunt effectus motuum vitalium in composito, non affectiones principiorum singulorum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A12","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D7","text":"Infinitas principiorum similium reproductionem generum sustinet, dum leges conexionis eorum discrimina conservant.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D8","text":"In infinito spatio eadem vis materialis alios congressus mundo nostro similes atque alios mundos efficere debet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D9","text":"Ordo naturalis ex motibus et limitibus materiae constat neque rectorem divinum requirit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10","text":"Mundus, quia compositum materiale est, generationi, incremento, maturitati et ruinae subiacet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D11","text":"Motus principiorum sub limine sensuum positus quietis speciem in summa conspicua producere potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:D12","text":"Cognitio causarum materialium religionem timidam et aestimationem falsam bonorum externorum infirmat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1","text":"Ontologia compositorum relationalis est: proprietas rei non in semine singulari, sed in modo compositionis residet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A12","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD2","text":"Systema plures ordines temporis distinguit: principia immutabilia, summam materiae sempiternam, concilia temporaria et mundos mortales.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","text":"Natura neque fato perfecte clauso neque arbitrio extra naturam posito regitur; necessitas conexionum et clinamen indeterminatum simul operantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD4","text":"Mundus noster est concilium locale intra summam infinitam, non terminus aut centrum rerum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD5","text":"Vita et mors in gradu materiae non sunt potestates absolutae, sed vicissitudines compositionis et dispersionis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD6","text":"Regularitas generum ex compatibilitate selectiva principiorum nascitur, non ex exemplari divino aut materia homogenea.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","text":"Therapia animi a cosmologia pendet, quia securitas efficitur mutando tabulam causalitatis, non augendo potestatem externam.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse oportet qualitates conspicuas essentiam intrinsecam principiorum immediate manifestare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem motum novo motui ordine antecedenti necessario necti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordinem rerum consilium, usum humanum aut administratorem divinum praesupponere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse oportet singularitatem mundi aut hominis ex ipsa natura inferri.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse oportet summam rerum terminum externum, fundum aut receptaculum extra se habere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse oportet identitatem rei ex materia uniformi constitui; identitas ex mixtura ordinata et selectiva pendet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX7","text":"Falsum esse oportet effectum sensilem tantum ex causis eiusdem qualitatis produci posse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX8","text":"Falsum esse oportet mutationem naturalem aut senescentiam cosmicam ad historiam moralem hominum reduci.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX9","text":"Falsum esse oportet quietem, colorem, sensum aut stabilitatem in gradu conspicuo observata ad principia eodem modo pertinere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX1","text":"Summa rerum circa absentiam omnis exterioris ordinatur: nihil est extra quod materiam augeat, minuat aut cursum universum convertat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX2","text":"Creatio absoluta et annihilatio absoluta excluduntur; novitas tantum ex novo concilio immutabilium oritur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX3","text":"Natura caret centro, fine providentiae et destinatario privilegiato.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBS:PRB1C6EDC6412FDF59:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX4","text":"Fundamentum rerum per privationes constituitur: principia stabilia sunt quatenus qualitatibus mortalibus, sensu 