type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AF4","text":"Physiologia seminis → cupiditas Venerea → diagnosis amoris captivi → regimen attentionis → generatio et consuetudo materialiter explicatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME1","text":"Perceptio ex receptione passiva speciei incorporeae in commercium materiale emittere, transire et ferire reordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME2","text":"Oppositio simplicis apparentiae et veritatis solvitur per distinctionem inter affectionem sensus et opinionem animi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME3","text":"Diversi sensus tamquam canales distincti unius causalitatis corporeae, non tamquam iudices mutuo substituendi, disponuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME4","text":"Somnia, monstra, umbrae mortuorum et voces locorum ex ordine supernaturali in ordinem imaginum compositarum et repercussarum transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME5","text":"Utilitas membrorum ex causa originis in effectum posterioris usus convertitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME6","text":"Actio voluntaria ex imperio immateriali in seriem materialem imaginationis, voluntatis, animae et corporis transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME7","text":"Amor a valore absoluto obiecti separatur et tamquam captatio per simulacra, opinionem, habitum et attentionem describitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME8","text":"Fecunditas, hereditas et differentia prolis ex dominio divino in agrum compatibilitatis materialis seminum reponuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT1","text":"Surface — Imagines speculares, errores perspectivi, voces, sapores, odores, somnia, motus, cupiditates et generationes sunt obiecta immediata explicanda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT2","text":"Structural — Omnia obiecta per emissionem corporum, vias mediorum, congruentiam texturarum et ictus organorum conectuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Textus certamen a repudiatione sensuum ad separationem affectionis ab opinione transfert atque superstitionem, scepticismum et finalitatem eodem motu debilitare conatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas est compositio corporum in fluxu, subiectum est nexus corporeus mobilis, et continuitas ex successione celerrima effectuum nascitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT1","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad motum principalem a simulacris ad sensus, mentem, physiologiam, amorem et generationem restituendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT2","text":"Lacunae fontis demonstrationes de subtilitate imaginum, earum generatione, speculis et quibusdam somniorum exemplis incompletas relinquunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT3","text":"Cohaerentia firmissima est ubi unum exemplar emissionis, itineris et ictus plures sensus et processus corporales explicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT4","text":"Defensio sensuum stabilitur per distinctionem inter id quod sensus patitur et id quod animus de causa affectionis conicit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT5","text":"Probatio simulacrorum invisibilium analogiis emissionum visibilium et exclusione causarum aemularum nititur, non inspectione directa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT6","text":"Assertio omne sensibile apparens esse verum cum apparentibus distortionibus conciliatur tantum si veritas ad affectionem, non ad omne iudicium de obiecto, restringitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT7","text":"Ratio tota a sensibus pendere dicitur, quamvis primordia, simulacra singula et tempora minima sensum excedant; transitus inferentialis non plene formalizatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT8","text":"Origo imaginum sponte in aere factarum et exacta selectio simulacrorum ab attentione causata minus determinate explicantur quam transmissio sensibilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0054","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT9","text":"Pars erotica a mechanismo materiali ad praeceptum evitandi vel avertendi amorem per aestimationem poenae transit, non per deductionem physicam omnino necessariam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT10","text":"Restitutio verum textus motum servat: a fluxu corporeo ad cognitionem, ab errore ad opinionem, a functione ad emergentiam, atque a cupiditate ad captationem et regimen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T230931416840Z_9a6d6488","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book Four","source_sha256":"aa8b66df6ee726125bd9404c1e7becd8fd69f15d798891c3c3dadecfa20c5ddf","sha256":"4c9e2bfb0bb2c553b399d9875a4a91027f6cd16224c7b28e831eaad09b269b44","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:09:50.411324+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS1","text":"It is asked how perception, thought, sleep, action, desire, and generation can be explained from bodily principles without immortal souls, providence, or final causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS2","text":"All these operations arise from the emission, transmission, congruence, and impact of the most subtle bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS3","text":"The argument is materially reconstructive, polemical against superstition, scepticism, and finality, and strategically adapted for conveying a harsh doctrine with poetic charm.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS4","text":"From the doctrine of first beginnings one proceeds to the necessity of simulacra, thence to the mechanism of vision and the dissolution of apparent errors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS5","text":"The defence of the senses transfers error from sensible affection to the opinions of the mind and establishes the senses as the foundation of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS6","text":"The same grammar of bodily contact is extended to hearing, taste, smell, thought, sleep, will, and motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS7","text":"Finally, the analysis is transferred to the use of limbs, Venusian desire, the captivity of love, and generation, so that natural causality may order both knowledge and the governance of the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A1","text":"All things, including mind and soul, are composed of bodily first beginnings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","text":"First beginnings differ in shape, size, motion, order, and texture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","text":"Bodies continually flow from things and are emitted in all directions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A4","text":"Simulacra are the most subtle textures which, emitted from the surface of things, preserve the form and color of their source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A5","text":"Every sense is moved by the bodily impact of its proper messenger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A6","text":"Each sense has a power proper to itself and distinct from the other senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","text":"Sensible quality arises from the congruence of impacting shapes with the passages and texture of the recipient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8","text":"Subtlety, medium, space, obstacles, and straightness of path determine the transmission of bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A9","text":"Simulacra can be formed in the air or coalesce from images of different things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A10","text":"The mind is struck by simulacra subtler than those which move the eyes and which penetrate the body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11","text":"From an abundance of images, the mind chiefly receives those for which it has been prepared by attention, habit, or bodily state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A12","text":"What appears to each sense at its own time is true insofar as it is an affection of that sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","text":"Falsity arises from the opinions which the mind adds to things given by sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A14","text":"Knowledge of truth takes its beginning from the senses, and reason depends upon the trustworthiness of the senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15","text":"The structure of limbs precedes use, and what has been born afterwards produces the capacity for use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A16","text":"Animal life depends upon the distribution, conjunction, and motions of the bodily soul through the limbs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A17","text":"The will to act arises after the mind has conceived an image of the future action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A18","text":"Venusian desire is a material disturbance of seed excited by a congruent simulacrum or body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A19","text":"Offspring consists in the encounter and congruence of maternal and paternal seeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX1","text":"Simulacra of the dead do not prove that souls return from Acheron or that anything personal survives after the dissolution of body and mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX2","text":"Simulacra cannot be seen individually, although their frequent concurrence produces a visible thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX3","text":"Perspective illusions do not demonstrate that the eyes themselves lie.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX4","text":"The proposition that nothing can be known cannot affirm itself without contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX5","text":"Reason arising from the senses cannot refute the senses as universally false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX6","text":"One sense cannot judge or convict the proper power of another sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX7","text":"Centaurs, Scyllas, and images of the dead do not require corresponding animate natures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX8","text":"Echoes and reflected sounds do not require the presence of Satyrs, Nymphs, Fauns, or Pan.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX9","text":"Simulacra do not discern human will, nor do they artfully compose the motions of dreams.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX10","text":"Eyes, tongue, ears, and limbs were not created for future uses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX11","text":"Sleep is not the complete expulsion of the soul from the body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX12","text":"Sterility and fertility are not distributed by divine powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX13","text":"Venusian pleasure does not require exclusive love, nor is love sated by possession of the beloved body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D1","text":"A sudden mirror image arises from the continual flow and reflection of simulacra.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D2","text":"By reason of their subtlety, lightness, and superficial position, simulacra preserve their shape and move most swiftly.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D3","text":"Vision occurs when a simulacrum strikes the eyes, while distance is perceived from the air set in motion before the image.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D4","text":"The placement, inversion, and multiplication of mirror images arise from the arrangement of airs, reflections, and bends of the surface.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D5","text":"Distant shapes, lights, shadows, and refracted bodies appear otherwise when transmission changes through impacts, intervals, or media.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D6","text":"Sensible appearance correctly indicates the present affection, while error arises from judgment about the cause, identity, or motion of the thing.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A12","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D7","text":"Voice is a body emitted by the speaker which strikes the ears, is confused through space, divided into many parts, and reflected by solids.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D8","text":"Tastes and odors produce different or contrary effects according to the shape of particles, the size of passages, and the texture of the animal.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D9","text":"Imagined monsters are composed from the concurrence of images of real parts, not from the existence of corresponding monsters.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D10","text":"In sleep, simulacra move the mind while the external senses rest and memory does not supply correction from the present thing.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D11","text":"Voluntary thought does not create images at will, but selects from the abundance already present images congruent with attention.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D12","text":"Voluntary motion proceeds from the image of motion to will, from will to the soul diffused through the limbs, and thence to the body aided by air.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D13","text":"Sleep arises when external and internal impacts disturb the disposition of the soul, expel one part, and drive another part inward.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D14","text":"Hunger and thirst are effects of the loss of bodies, the rarefaction of limbs, and the necessity of restoring congruent matter.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D15","text":"The utility of limbs is a subsequent effect of an already-born structure, not the cause of its origin.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D16","text":"Venusian pleasure can be common to both sexes because the body of each is moved by a congruent impact and mutual ardor.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D17","text":"Exclusive love grows when simulacra, memory, and opinion continually repeat the same object, although the body cannot be sated by images.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D18","text":"Resemblance, hereditary variation, and fertility arise from the proportion, mixture, and compatibility of the double seed.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","text":"Vision, hearing, smell, taste, thought, and desire vary a single material grammar of emission, passage, impact, and congruence.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A8","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD2","text":"Sensible truth and judicial falsity belong to different levels, whence appearance can be preserved while interpretation is corrected.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A12","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD3","text":"Material explanation reclassifies apparitions of the dead, monsters, gods of places, and dream motions from signs of another world into effects of transmission.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D7","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D9","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD4","text":"The human agent is not a principle of motion separate from matter, but a nexus of images, attention, will, soul, and body.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD5","text":"Biological capacities and uses emerge historically from structures, congruences, and habits already established.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D14","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D15","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","text":"Love is a recursive circuit in which image excites desire, desire increases opinion, opinion fixes attention, and attention perpetuates image.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD7","text":"The continuity of an apparent thing or motion is constructed from the most swift succession of perishing and substituted forms.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D1","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD8","text":"The correction of affects occurs not by repudiating the senses, but by retracting opinions, redirecting attention, and interrupting the fodder of images.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D6","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX1","text":"Sensible qualities are not absolute values enclosed in the object, but relations between impacting shapes and the structure of the recipient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX2","text":"No perception occurs through incorporeal access, or without a medium, to the essence of the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX3","text":"The stability of an image does not require a permanent form, because the succession of discrete forms supplies the effect of continuity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX4","text":"Will does not begin from nothing, but depends on a prior image and material disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX5","text":"A future use cannot be the temporal cause of an organ already constituted before use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX6","text":"Beloved beauty does not necessarily contain the perfections which desire attributes to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX7","text":"The mind does not stand outside nature so as absolutely to govern the flow of images or judge the testimony of the senses from an extrinsic foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX8","text":"A divine cause must not be admitted where the difference of shapes, motions, and congruences suffices to produce the effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX1","text":"The system requires no providential organizer to direct senses, limbs, desires, or generation toward pre-established ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX2","text":"No tribunal of knowledge external to and independent of the senses is available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3","text":"No enduring personal subject survives intact the dissolution of body and mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX4","text":"Total possession of the beloved body is impossible, because only simulacra, not the very body of the other, are incorporated by the lover.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX5","text":"The immobile presence of things is excluded, since perception and life consist in flow, impact, substitution, and recomposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS1","text":"Shaped first beginnings → composition of things → bodily efflux → transmission → impact upon recipient → sensible affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS2","text":"Surface of a thing → simulacrum retaining form → motion through air → eye struck → appearance of the thing → distance from air driven forth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS3","text":"True sensible affection → added opinion of the mind → false attribution of causality or identity → reason grounded in the senses → distinction of the given from the added.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS4","text":"Difference of shapes and passages → different penetration or reflection → distinction of sight, voice, smell, and taste → variation according to recipient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS5","text":"Abundance of simulacra → composition or succession → preparation of attention or habit → mental image → thought, dream, or apparition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS6","text":"Impact or loss of bodies → change in the disposition of soul and body → sleep, hunger, thirst, or desire → restoration or compensatory motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS7","text":"Born structure → possible action → repetition → recognized use → art or habit; future use does not constitute the cause of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS8","text":"Simulacrum of the beloved → disturbance of seed → desire → idealizing opinion → fixed attention → insatiability → pain; redirection of attention breaks the circuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:FGS9","text":"Double seed → encounter → proportion and congruence → conception → mixture of the first beginnings of parents and ancestors → form of offspring.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AF1","text":"Atomism already established → question concerning apparitions → posit of simulacra → proof through emissions and mirrors → explanation of vision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AF2","text":"Variety of appearances → analysis of media and impacts → distinction of sense from opinion → refutation of scepticism → senses as foundation of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AF3","text":"Common bodily causality → hearing, taste, and smell → mental images and dreams → will, motion, and sleep → rejection of finality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AF4","text":"Physiology of seed → Venusian desire → diagnosis of captive love → governance of attention → generation and habit materially explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME1","text":"Perception is reordered from the passive reception of an incorporeal species into a material commerce of emitting, passing, and striking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME2","text":"The opposition of simple appearance and truth is dissolved through the distinction between the affection of sense and the opinion of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME3","text":"The different senses are arranged as distinct channels of one bodily causality, not as judges to be mutually substituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME4","text":"Dreams, monsters, shades of the dead, and voices of places are transferred from a supernatural order into an order of composite and reflected images.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME5","text":"The utility of limbs is converted from a cause of origin into an effect of subsequent use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME6","text":"Voluntary action is transferred from an immaterial command into a material series of imagination, will, soul, and body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME7","text":"Love is separated from the absolute value of the object and described as captivation through simulacra, opinion, habit, and attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME8","text":"Fertility, inheritance, and difference of offspring are relocated from divine dominion into the field of the material compatibility of seeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT1","text":"Surface — Mirror images, perspective errors, voices, tastes, odors, dreams, motions, desires, and generations are the immediate objects to be explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT2","text":"Structural — All objects are connected through the emission of bodies, pathways of media, congruence of textures, and impacts upon organs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text transfers the contest from repudiation of the senses to the separation of affection from opinion and seeks to weaken superstition, scepticism, and finality in the same movement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reality is a composition of bodies in flux, the subject is a mobile bodily nexus, and continuity arises from the most swift succession of effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to restore the principal movement from simulacra to the senses, mind, physiology, love, and generation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT2","text":"Gaps in the source leave incomplete the demonstrations concerning the subtlety of images, their generation, mirrors, and certain examples of dreams.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT3","text":"The strongest coherence is where one model of emission, passage, and impact explains multiple senses and bodily processes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT4","text":"The defence of the senses is stabilized by the distinction between what the sense undergoes and what the mind conjectures about the cause of the affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT5","text":"The proof of invisible simulacra relies on analogies with visible emissions and the exclusion of rival causes, not on direct inspection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT6","text":"The assertion that every sensible appearance is true is reconciled with apparent distortions only if truth is restricted to affection, not to every judgment about the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT7","text":"The whole of reason is said to depend upon the senses, although first beginnings, individual simulacra, and minimal times exceed sense; the inferential transition is not fully formalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0031","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT8","text":"The origin of images spontaneously formed in the air and the exact selection of simulacra caused by attention are explained less determinately than sensible transmission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0054","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT9","text":"The erotic part passes from material mechanism to the precept of avoiding or turning away from love through the estimation of punishment, not through an entirely necessary physical deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0076","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT10","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text: from bodily flux to cognition, from error to opinion, from function to emergence, and from desire to captivation and governance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LIBQ:PR6FEF796CE4799255:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_0T6T_LIBQ@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_0T6T_LIBQ:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 04","title":"Book Four","context":"Chapter 04","original_title":"Liber Quartus","original_context":"Chapter 04"},"original_title":"Liber Quartus","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2","layer":"CH","title":"Book Five","order":5,"parents":["RAW_0T6T_LI_2"],"revision":"CH_0T6T_LI_2@R001","sha256":"0f155ebaaa0b23e8b896b051b1c9f040ce210c6fd4d24b2d2af19892c8161206","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS1","text":"Quaestio est quomodo mundus, sidera, vita et instituta humana sine providentia divina oriri, ordinari et interire possint, ita ut religiosus timor tollatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS2","text":"Mundus est compositum nativum et mortale, ex primordiorum concursibus sine consilio ortum, cuius ordines naturales vitam, societatem et religionem generant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est simul reconstructiva, polemica, genealogica et therapeutica: causas naturales restituit, exemplaria providentialia excludit et animum a metu purgat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS4","text":"Demonstratio ab universis naturae finibus, mortalitate partium, defectibus mundi, analogiis sensibilibus et causarum naturalium possibilitate procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS5","text":"Motus argumenti transit a sapientia purgatrice ad mortalitatem mundi, inde ad cosmogoniam, astronomiam, biogenesim, historiam humanam et originem religionis atque artium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","text":"Cuique naturae potestas finita terminusque operationis certus inest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A2","text":"Animus et sensus tantum in corpore animali apte disposito consistere possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","text":"Natura deorum tenuis, beata et a rebus humanis remota neque utilitate neque novitatis desiderio ad mundum administrandum movetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","text":"Primordia rerum sine consilio sagaci ponderibus, plagis et motibus propriis feruntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","text":"Concursus primordiorum per tempus infinitum innumeras compositiones pertemptat, donec configurationes stabiles emergant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","text":"Mundi membra sunt nativa, mortalia et per generationem consumptionemque mutabilia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","text":"Totum ex partibus nativis mortalibusque constitutum eandem generationis et dissolutionis condicionem participat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","text":"Mundus neque solidus neque clausus est, sed inane continet, ictibus externis patet et spatium habet in quod partes dissolutae discedant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A9","text":"Natura mundi necessitatibus humanis non est apte ordinata, sed regiones inhabitabiles, labores, morbos, feras et mortem immaturam continet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","text":"Ordines recurrentes ex primis condicionibus causalibus et stabilita rerum consecutione, non ex gubernatione divina, oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","text":"Ubi effectus pluribus modis naturalibus fieri potest, plures causae physice possibiles disponendae sunt, quamquam una tantum actu viget.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A12","text":"Terra recens, calore et umore abundans, formas viventes naturaliter producere potuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","text":"Propagatio vitae cibum, organa congruentia, semina genitalia et mutuam corporum compatibilitatem requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A14","text":"Genera viventium permanent si dolo, virtute, mobilitate aut utilitate hominibus praestita tutantur; deficientia pereunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","text":"Facultates, vocabula, pacta, instituta et artes humanae ex natura, utilitate, necessitate et experientia paulatim oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","text":"Religio ex imaginibus deorum, ignoratione causarum caelestium et timore virium naturalium generatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17","text":"Ignoratio finis habendi et verae voluptatis curas inanes, ambitionem, invidiam atque bellum generat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","text":"Cognitio causarum naturalium timorem divinum et perturbationes animi dissolvere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX1","text":"Mundus neque aeternus neque divino sensu praeditus est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX2","text":"Natura rerum hominum causa non est creata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX3","text":"Sol, luna et sidera neque libera voluntate neque divino imperio cursus suos agunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX4","text":"Animus neque sine corpore neque in quolibet corpore consistere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX5","text":"Sedes deorum intra mundum non sunt, nec cultus humanus deos ad gubernationem rerum movet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX6","text":"Ordo mundi non est dispositio primordiorum a mente sagaci instituta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX7","text":"Pietas non consistit in ritibus, victimis, votis aut corporis humiliatione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX8","text":"Summum beneficium humanum neque frugibus neque vino neque armata monstrorum victoria continetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX9","text":"Monstra ex membris naturarum incompatibilium compacta neque fuerunt neque generari possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX10","text":"Vocabula rerum ab uno nominum auctore ceteris hominibus imposita non sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX11","text":"Regna, proprietas, magistratus et leges neque prima neque immutabilia societatis humanae fundamenta sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX12","text":"Incrementum opum et artium non constituit necessarium incrementum felicitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX13","text":"Phenomenis caelestibus causa singularis certo definita non semper assignari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D1","text":"Terra, mare, caelum et sidera sensu divino carent, quia dispositionem corporis animalis non habent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D2","text":"Defectus et hostilitas mundi cum natura deorum beata componi non possunt si mundus opus providentiale hominum causa factum ponitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D3","text":"Mundus, quia ex partibus mortalibus constat et dissolutioni patet, ortum habuit et exitium habiturus est.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D4","text":"Mundus mortalis ab infinito tempore integer permanere non potuit; mortalitas eius nativitatem implicat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D5","text":"Cosmogonia non requirit exemplar antecedens, quia innumeri concursus primordiorum configurationes mundi experiendo producunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D6","text":"Discretio terrae, maris, aeris et aetheris ex differentiis ponderum, figurarum, nexuum et motuum, non ex consilio, oritur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D7","text":"Regularitas motuum caelestium cum causalitate naturali congruit neque voluntatem siderum aut imperium deorum probat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D8","text":"Expositio plurium causarum naturalium sufficientium reditum ab admiratione caelesti ad religionem impedit, etiam si causa actualis nondum discernitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D9","text":"Terra formas multas producit, sed tantum genera condicionibus victus, propagationis et tutelae congruentia permanent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D10","text":"Formae hybridae mythicae excluduntur quia membra discordantia neque eodem tempore crescunt neque iisdem victus et generationis legibus parent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D11","text":"Lingua ex facultate vocis, sensuum varietate et utilitate communi emergit, non ex institutione unius legislatoris.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D12","text":"Familia, amicitia, pacta et leges ex vulnerabilitate mutua atque taedio violentiae paulatim constituuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D13","text":"Ordo caelestis, ubi causae ignorantur, in imaginem potestatis divinae convertitur et timorem religiosum nutrit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14","text":"Progressus technicus per se ad quietem non convergit, quia novas opes, distinctiones, arma et certamina desiderii producit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D15","text":"Doctrina quae cupiditates et timores expellit utilior est armis quae pericula externa tantum removent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D16","text":"Pietas vera est rerum naturam mente pacata intueri, non potestates invisibiles ritibus placare.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD1","text":"Cosmogonia, biologia et historia humana unam continuitatem materialem constituunt: eadem natura compositiones cosmicas, formas viventes et instituta socialia sine ruptura supernaturali generat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:D5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D9","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2","text":"Ordo est stabilitas temporaria configurationum inter innumeras coniunctiones, non finis antecedenter praescriptus.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3","text":"Mortalitas non est casus mundo extrinsecus accidens, sed effectus necessarius compositionis, permeabilitatis et conflictus partium.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD4","text":"Ad providentiam excludendam sufficit ostendere effectum naturaliter possibilem esse, quamquam ad historiam physicam completam causa actualis discernenda manet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD5","text":"Civilizatio pericula naturae minuit, sed per opes, dominationem et artem bellicam pericula collectiva maiora generare potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6","text":"Religio circuitum sui conservationis efficit: ignorantia causae timorem parit, timor attributionem divinam, attributio divina maiorem observationis anxietatem.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D8","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD7","text":"Dignitas divina a creatione mundi ad liberationem animi transfertur: creator providentialis excluditur, inventor autem sapientiae propter vim purgatricem honorifice deus appellatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD8","text":"Tempus naturalis historiae est successio statuum irreversibilium, in qua quod ante potuit postea nequit et novae facultates ex condicionibus mutatis oriuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX1","text":"Ignorantia causae naturalis testimonium causae divinae esse non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX2","text":"Regularitas effectuum intentionem ordinatricem tacite non implicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX3","text":"Homo neque finis cosmici processus neque mensura aptitudinis mundi est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX4","text":"Stabilitas foederum naturalium aeternitatem mundorum, specierum aut institutionum non implicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX5","text":"Lingua, lex, proprietas et valor auctoritatem transcendentem ad suam validitatem non requirunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX6","text":"Potentia politica, divitiae et ars technica securitatem stabilem praestare non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX7","text":"Permanentia generis perfectionem non probat, sed tantum sufficientem congruentiam cum condicionibus conservationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX8","text":"Fama poetica et figura mythica limites physicae possibilitatis superare non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX9","text":"Indeterminatio causae particularis eventum neque supernaturalem neque omnino inexplicabilem reddit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX10","text":"Concursus fortuitus primordiorum ab foederibus naturae solutus non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX1","text":"Systema nullum custodem cosmicum, iudicem providentem aut rectorem historiae admittit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX2","text":"Nulla mundi aeternitas humanis operibus, civitatibus aut speciei permanentiam spondet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX3","text":"Historia humana neque ad perfectionem necessariam neque ad ultimum artium et imperii statum ordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX4","text":"Nulla dignitas politica, oeconomica aut technica naturali ordine semel constituto immobilis manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX5","text":"Salus animi neque a cultu divino neque ab abundantia materiali neque ab externa victoria provenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX6","text":"Certitudo singularis de omni causa caelesti indisponibilis esse potest neque tamen naturalem explicationem impedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS1","text":"Machina principalis ita procedit: foedera finita naturae → exclusio animi cosmici et providentiae → generatio mundi ex concursibus → mortalitas compositi → naturalis genealogia vitae et societatis → purgatio timoris.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS2","text":"Circuitus cosmologicus est: primordia mota → concursus innumeri → coetus stabilis → discretio elementorum → fluxus et conflictus membrorum → dissolutio mundi.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS3","text":"Exemplar biologicum est: terra recens → multiplicitas formarum → probatio per victum et generationem → tutela aut defectus → propagatio aut interitus.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS4","text":"Genealogia humana est: vita dispersa → ignis, habitatio et coniunctio familiaris → pacta et voces → proprietas et regnum → ambitio et eversio → leges → artes, opes et bellum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS5","text":"Circuitus religiosus et eius solutio sunt: spectaculum caeleste → egestas rationis → attributio divina → timor et ritus → causae naturales possibiles → mens pacata.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AF1","text":"Motus textualis transit a divinizatione sapientiae purgatricis ad propositum doctrinae, inde ad mortalitatem mundi, cosmogoniam, causas astrorum, ortum viventium, historiam societatis, religionis, metallorum, belli et artium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AF2","text":"Motus inferentialis recurrens transit ab effectu observato ad fines naturae, a finibus ad causam naturalem possibilem, a causa possibili ad exclusionem gubernationis divinae, atque ab exclusione ad tranquillitatem animi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME1","text":"Sapientia denuo describitur ut ars liberationis interioris, cuius utilitas dona materialia et victoriam armatam superat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME2","text":"Mundus ex sede vel corpore divino in compositum materiale nativum et dissolubile transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME3","text":"Ordo naturalis a consilio antecedente separatur et ut effectus stabilitatis post innumeros concursus intelligitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME4","text":"Astronomia ab interpretatione religiosorum signorum ad agrum causarum naturalium plurium transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME5","text":"Genera viventium non ut formae perfecte datae, sed ut effectus productionis, congruentiae, tutelae et extinctionis ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME6","text":"Religio ex veritate de rebus divinis in effectum imaginum, ignorantiae causalis et timoris reclassificatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME7","text":"Lingua, lex, regnum et ars ex institutionibus originariis in eventus genealogicos utilitatis et experientiae mutantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME8","text":"Progressus technicus ab incremento necessario boni separatur et ut amplificator simul commodorum, desideriorum et belli ponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME9","text":"Pietas ab obsequio rituali ad contemplationem naturae mente pacata transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — Mundus mortalis esse demonstratur, motus caelestes naturaliter explicantur, atque origines vitae et cultus humani exponuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Concursus primordiorum, mortalitas partium, selectio formarum viabilium, utilitas et conflictus mechanismos cosmicos, biologicos et sociales constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Explanatio naturalis quaestionem ab imperio deorum ad limites possibilitatis materialis transfert et religionem in genealogiam timoris convertit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas ex compositis temporalibus, potestatibus finitis et statuum successione constat; permanentia localis sine aeternitate vel fine providentiali emergit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad mortalitatem mundi, generationem naturalem ordinis, genealogiam vitae et societatis atque finem therapeuticum restituenda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT2","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi causalitas naturalis, limites potestatum et mortalitas compositionis contra providentiam coniunguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT3","text":"Systema cohaeret si foedera naturae stabilia a configurationibus et statibus temporaliter mutabilibus distinguuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT4","text":"Pluralitas causarum astronomicarum supernaturalem necessitatem infirmat, sed causam actualem saepe indeterminatam relinquit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT5","text":"Transitus a mortalitate partium ad mortalitatem totius vi regulae compositionis nititur, cuius ambitus potius affirmatur quam separatim demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT6","text":"Argumentum a recentia monumentorum et artium ad recentiam mundi fragile manet, quia priora excidia admitti possunt; ea tamen mortalitatem mundi confirmant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT7","text":"Generatio viventium e terra recenti analogiis praesentibus et congruentiae principiis nititur, non observatione directa originis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT8","text":"Lacuna fontis transitum inter coniunctionem domesticam et primam mollitiem socialem incompletum relinquit, quamquam posterior pactarum relationum series servatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT9","text":"Appellatio inventoris sapientiae ut dei et remotio deorum ontologicorum a rebus humanis distinctionem honorificam requirunt quam textus non plene formalizat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT10","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat: a purificatione animi per cosmologiam naturalem ad genealogiam vitae, religionis et civilis ambivalentiae procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T231232102834Z_39befb82","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book Five","source_sha256":"0f155ebaaa0b23e8b896b051b1c9f040ce210c6fd4d24b2d2af19892c8161206","sha256":"c126156702d5228a78279f6e088d13812a11937bd74c82e26d8dda98f5ec825c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:12:47.375470+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS1","text":"The question is how the world, stars, life, and human institutions can arise, be ordered, and perish without divine providence, such that religious fear is removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS2","text":"The world is a native and mortal composite, arisen from collisions of first-beginnings without design, whose natural orders generate life, society, and religion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS3","text":"The argumentation is at once reconstructive, polemical, genealogical, and therapeutic: it restores natural causes, excludes providential models, and purges the mind of fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS4","text":"The demonstration proceeds from the universal limits of nature, the mortality of parts, the defects of the world, sensible analogies, and the possibility of natural causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS5","text":"The movement of the argument passes from purgative wisdom to the mortality of the world, thence to cosmogony, astronomy, biogenesis, human history, and the origin of religion and the arts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","text":"In every nature there inheres a finite power and a determinate limit of operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A2","text":"Mind and sensation can subsist only in an animal body suitably arranged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","text":"The nature of the gods, subtle, blessed, and removed from human affairs, is moved to administer the world neither by utility nor by desire for novelty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","text":"The first-beginnings of things are borne without intelligent design by their own weights, blows, and motions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","text":"The collisions of first-beginnings, through infinite time, try innumerable compositions until stable configurations emerge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","text":"The members of the world are native, mortal, and mutable through generation and consumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","text":"A whole constituted from native and mortal parts shares the same condition of generation and dissolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","text":"The world is neither solid nor closed, but contains void, lies open to external blows, and has space into which its dissolved parts may depart.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A9","text":"The nature of the world is not suitably ordered for human needs, but contains uninhabitable regions, labors, diseases, wild beasts, and premature death.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","text":"Recurrent orders arise from initial causal conditions and the established succession of things, not from divine governance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","text":"Where an effect can occur in several natural ways, several physically possible causes must be set forth, although only one is actually operative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A12","text":"The young earth, abounding in heat and moisture, could naturally produce living forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","text":"The propagation of life requires food, suitable organs, generative seeds, and the mutual compatibility of bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A14","text":"Kinds of living beings persist if they are protected by cunning, strength, mobility, or utility afforded to human beings; through deficiency they perish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","text":"Human faculties, words, compacts, institutions, and arts gradually arise from nature, utility, necessity, and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","text":"Religion is generated from images of gods, ignorance of celestial causes, and fear of natural forces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17","text":"Ignorance of the end to be attained and of true pleasure generates vain anxieties, ambition, envy, and war.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","text":"Knowledge of natural causes can dissolve divine fear and disturbances of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX1","text":"The world is neither eternal nor endowed with divine sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX2","text":"The nature of things was not created for the sake of human beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX3","text":"The sun, moon, and stars do not pursue their courses either by free will or by divine command.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX4","text":"Mind can subsist neither without a body nor in any body whatsoever.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX5","text":"The abodes of the gods are not within the world, nor does human worship move the gods to govern affairs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX6","text":"The order of the world is not an arrangement of first-beginnings established by an intelligent mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX7","text":"Piety does not consist in rites, sacrifices, vows, or humiliation of the body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX8","text":"The highest human benefit is contained neither in crops, nor wine, nor the armed defeat of monsters.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX9","text":"Monsters compounded from the members of incompatible natures neither existed nor can be generated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX10","text":"The names of things were not imposed upon other human beings by a single originator of names.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX11","text":"Kingdoms, property, magistracies, and laws are neither the first nor immutable foundations of human society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX12","text":"The increase of wealth and arts does not constitute a necessary increase in happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX13","text":"A singular, certainly determined cause cannot always be assigned to celestial phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D1","text":"Earth, sea, heaven, and stars lack divine sensation because they do not possess the arrangement of an animal body.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D2","text":"The defects and hostility of the world cannot be reconciled with the blessed nature of the gods if the world is posited as a providential work made for the sake of human beings.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D3","text":"The world, because it consists of mortal parts and is open to dissolution, had an origin and will have destruction.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D4","text":"The mortal world could not have remained whole from infinite time; its mortality entails its birth.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D5","text":"Cosmogony does not require a prior model, because innumerable collisions of first-beginnings produce configurations of the world by trial.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D6","text":"The differentiation of earth, sea, air, and ether arises from differences of weights, shapes, connections, and motions, not from design.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D7","text":"The regularity of celestial motions accords with natural causality and proves neither the will of the stars nor the command of the gods.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D8","text":"The exposition of several sufficient natural causes prevents a return from celestial wonder to religion, even if the actual cause is not yet discerned.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D9","text":"The earth produces many forms, but only kinds suited to the conditions of sustenance, propagation, and protection endure.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D10","text":"Mythical hybrid forms are excluded because discordant members neither grow at the same time nor obey the same laws of sustenance and generation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D11","text":"Language emerges from the faculty of voice, the variety of perceptions, and common utility, not from the institution of one lawgiver.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D12","text":"Family, friendship, compacts, and laws are gradually constituted from mutual vulnerability and weariness of violence.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D13","text":"Celestial order, where causes are unknown, is transformed into an image of divine power and nourishes religious fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14","text":"Technical progress does not of itself tend toward tranquility, because it produces new resources, distinctions, weapons, and contests of desire.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D15","text":"A doctrine that expels desires and fears is more useful than arms that remove only external dangers.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:D16","text":"True piety is to behold the nature of things with a tranquil mind, not to propitiate invisible powers with rites.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD1","text":"Cosmogony, biology, and human history constitute one material continuity: the same nature generates cosmic compositions, living forms, and social institutions without supernatural rupture.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:D5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D9","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2","text":"Order is the temporary stability of configurations among innumerable conjunctions, not an end prescribed in advance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3","text":"Mortality is not an accident befalling the world from outside, but a necessary effect of the composition, permeability, and conflict of its parts.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD4","text":"To exclude providence, it is sufficient to show that an effect is naturally possible, although for a complete physical history the actual cause remains to be discerned.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD5","text":"Civilization diminishes the dangers of nature, but through wealth, domination, and the art of war it can generate greater collective dangers.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6","text":"Religion produces a circuit of self-preservation: ignorance of a cause begets fear, fear divine attribution, divine attribution greater anxiety of observation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D8","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD7","text":"Divine dignity is transferred from the creation of the world to the liberation of the mind: the providential creator is excluded, but the discoverer of wisdom is honorifically called a god on account of his purgative power.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD8","text":"The time of natural history is a succession of irreversible states, in which what could be before cannot be afterward, and new faculties arise from altered conditions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX1","text":"Ignorance of a natural cause cannot be evidence of a divine cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX2","text":"The regularity of effects does not tacitly entail an ordering intention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX3","text":"The human being is neither the end of the cosmic process nor the measure of the world's fitness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX4","text":"The stability of natural compacts does not entail the eternity of worlds, species, or institutions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX5","text":"Language, law, property, and value do not require transcendent authority for their validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX6","text":"Political power, riches, and technical art cannot provide stable security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX7","text":"The persistence of a kind does not prove perfection, but only sufficient suitability to conditions of preservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX8","text":"Poetic fame and mythical form cannot surpass the limits of physical possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX9","text":"The indeterminacy of a particular cause does not render an event either supernatural or wholly inexplicable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX10","text":"The fortuitous collision of first-beginnings is not free from the compacts of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX1","text":"The system admits no cosmic guardian, provident judge, or ruler of history.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX2","text":"No eternity of the world guarantees permanence to human works, cities, or the species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX3","text":"Human history is ordered neither toward necessary perfection nor toward an ultimate state of the arts and empire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX4","text":"No political, economic, or technical dignity remains immovable once established by natural order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX5","text":"The salvation of the mind proceeds neither from divine worship, nor material abundance, nor external victory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX6","text":"Singular certainty concerning every celestial cause may be unavailable and yet does not impede natural explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS1","text":"The principal mechanism proceeds thus: finite compacts of nature → exclusion of cosmic mind and providence → generation of the world from collisions → mortality of the composite → natural genealogy of life and society → purgation of fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS2","text":"The cosmological circuit is: moving first-beginnings → innumerable collisions → stable aggregate → differentiation of elements → flux and conflict of members → dissolution of the world.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS3","text":"The biological model is: young earth → multiplicity of forms → testing through sustenance and generation → protection or deficiency → propagation or destruction.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS4","text":"The human genealogy is: dispersed life → fire, habitation, and family union → compacts and voices → property and kingship → ambition and overthrow → laws → arts, wealth, and war.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:FGS5","text":"The religious circuit and its dissolution are: celestial spectacle → poverty of reason → divine attribution → fear and rites → possible natural causes → tranquil mind.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AF1","text":"The textual movement passes from the divinization of purgative wisdom to the purpose of doctrine, thence to the mortality of the world, cosmogony, the causes of the stars, the origin of living beings, the history of society, religion, metals, war, and the arts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:AF2","text":"The recurrent inferential movement passes from an observed effect to the limits of nature, from limits to a possible natural cause, from possible cause to the exclusion of divine governance, and from exclusion to tranquility of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME1","text":"Wisdom is again described as an art of inner liberation, whose utility surpasses material gifts and armed victory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME2","text":"The world is transferred from a divine abode or body into a native and dissoluble material composite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME3","text":"Natural order is separated from prior design and understood as the effect of stability after innumerable collisions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME4","text":"Astronomy is transferred from the interpretation of religious signs to the field of multiple natural causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME5","text":"Kinds of living beings are ordered not as perfectly given forms, but as effects of production, suitability, protection, and extinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME6","text":"Religion is reclassified from truth concerning divine things into an effect of images, causal ignorance, and fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME7","text":"Language, law, kingship, and art are transformed from original institutions into genealogical events of utility and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME8","text":"Technical progress is separated from the necessary increase of good and posited as an amplifier at once of conveniences, desires, and war.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME9","text":"Piety is transferred from ritual obedience to the contemplation of nature with a tranquil mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The world is demonstrated to be mortal, celestial motions are naturally explained, and the origins of life and human culture are set forth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Collisions of first-beginnings, mortality of parts, selection of viable forms, utility, and conflict constitute cosmic, biological, and social mechanisms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Natural explanation transfers the question from the rule of the gods to the limits of material possibility and converts religion into a genealogy of fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reality consists of temporal composites, finite powers, and a succession of states; local permanence emerges without eternity or providential end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice for reconstructing the mortality of the world, the natural generation of order, the genealogy of life and society, and the therapeutic end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT2","text":"The structure is strongest where natural causality, limits of powers, and mortality of composition are joined against providence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT3","text":"The system coheres if stable compacts of nature are distinguished from temporally mutable configurations and states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT4","text":"The plurality of astronomical causes weakens supernatural necessity, but often leaves the actual cause indeterminate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT5","text":"The transition from the mortality of parts to the mortality of the whole rests on the force of the rule of composition, whose scope is asserted rather than separately demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT6","text":"The argument from the recentness of monuments and arts to the recentness of the world remains fragile, because earlier destructions can be admitted; nevertheless, these confirm the mortality of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT7","text":"The generation of living beings from the young earth rests on present analogies and principles of suitability, not on direct observation of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT8","text":"A gap in the source leaves incomplete the transition between domestic union and the first social softening, although the subsequent series of contractual relations is preserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT9","text":"The appellation of the discoverer of wisdom as a god and the removal of ontological gods from human affairs require an honorific distinction that the text does not fully formalize.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT10","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: it proceeds from purification of the mind through natural cosmology to the genealogy of life, religion, and civil ambivalence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_2:PR9959F1492883EB80:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_0T6T_LI_2@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_0T6T_LI_2:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 05","title":"Book Five","context":"Chapter 05","original_title":"Liber Quintus","original_context":"Chapter 05"},"original_title":"Liber Quintus","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3","layer":"CH","title":"Book Six","order":6,"parents":["RAW_0T6T_LI_3"],"revision":"CH_0T6T_LI_3@R001","sha256":"6d87d1205a9455243b7db1dd69679ea833de1a66f5bdf8517b73708a6ef56ef4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS1","text":"Quaeritur quomodo metus caelestium, terrestrium morborumque tolli possit, cum commoda externa anxietatem animi non tollant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS2","text":"Ratio naturae animum purgat, quia eventus intra fines potestatum materialium explicat et regimen divinum a causalitate mundi excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS3","text":"Argumentatio diagnostica, polemica, reconstructiva atque therapeutica est: timorem ad ignorantiam, ignorantiam ad falsam religionem, utramque ad cognitionem causarum refert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS4","text":"Motus argumenti ab animi receptaculo vitioso ad corpora, inane, motus et texturas tamquam grammaticam communem eventuum progreditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","text":"Unumquodque phaenomenon ex signis sensibilibus ad causas naturales possibiles reducitur, dum explicationes divinae per limites potestatum et distributionem effectuum refutantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6","text":"Pestilentia argumentum a physicis causis ad mortalitatem biologicam et dissolutionem socialem producit, ostendens intellegentiam naturae non idem esse ac remedium vel imperium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A1","text":"Exteriorum bonorum copia anxietatem non tollit, si animi receptaculum fluxum, inexplebile vel corruptivum manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2","text":"Animi sanatio fines cupidinis ac timoris et rationem summi boni determinari postulat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3","text":"Terror animi non lumine sensibili, sed conspectu naturae et ratione dissolvitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","text":"Ignorantia causarum eventus naturales ad deorum imperium refert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","text":"Dei aevum securum et placidum agunt nec eventus mundi ira aut arbitrio administrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","text":"Mundus caelumque corporea, nata, mortalia et dissolubilia sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","text":"Nullum corpus manifestum omnino plenum est; omne ex corpore et inani contextum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","text":"Potestas cuiusque naturae finita est et certo termino continetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","text":"Ab omnibus rebus corpora perpetuo fluunt et in circumiecta spatia sparguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","text":"Texturae primordiorum et formae viarum determinant quae corpora inter se recipiant, penetrent, iungantur vel repellantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","text":"Motus materialis per concursum, ictum, pressionem, pondus et loci inanitionem mutationes naturales efficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","text":"Causae occultae ex effectibus manifestis et analogiis rerum sensibilium inferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","text":"Quorundam effectuum causa singularis determinari nequit; plures causae naturales possibiles enumerandae sunt, quarum una reapse operatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","text":"Summa rerum infinita et mobilitas primordiorum materiam sufficientem eventibus localibus suppeditant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15","text":"Regiones propter diversam corporum compositionem diversos effectus in animantes producunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16","text":"Semina morbida ex externo caelo vel terra corrupta coire, aerem, aquas cibosque inficere, corpora intrare et ab aliis ad alios propagari possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17","text":"In clade contagiosa propinquitas ad aegros contagionem auget, absentia autem eos auxilio privat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX1","text":"Divitiae, honores, laus et fama domesticam anxietatem per se non sanant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX2","text":"Radii solis et lux diei tenebras animi non discutiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX3","text":"Tonitrua, fulmina, terrae motus, eruptiones et pestilentiae non sunt actus irae vel arbitrii divini.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX4","text":"Falsa opinio de deis deos non violat, sed tranquillitatem opinantis corrumpit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX5","text":"Fulmen poenam moralem non distribuit, quia innocentes, loca deserta, montes, templa et simulacra indiscrete ferit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX6","text":"Carmina divinatoria occultam deorum mentem non aperiunt nec naturam fulminis explicant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX7","text":"Loca Averna non sunt ianuae Orci nec casus animantium transitum animarum ad inferos probat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX8","text":"Fons noctu calidus non a sole per crassam terram penetrante calefit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX9","text":"Magnitudo insolita eventus supernaturalitatem eius non probat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX10","text":"Unica causa certa non est condicio necessaria omnis explicationis naturalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX11","text":"Mundus incorruptae et aeternae saluti non est mandatus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12","text":"In pestilentia certa communisque ratio remedii non necessario praesto est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX13","text":"Nec religio nec mos sepulturae sub vi praesentis doloris inviolabilis manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1","text":"Therapia ad formam animi corrigendam, non ad infinitam bonorum externorum accumulationem dirigitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","text":"Etiam cognita deorum tranquillitate, admiratio sine cognitione causarum ad veterem religionem relabitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D3","text":"Eventus caelestes et terrestres eadem grammatica corporum, inanis et motuum explicantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D4","text":"Penetratio, attractio et actio inter corpora distantia per effluvia, foramina et displacementem medii effici possunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D5","text":"Diversitas effectuum ex aptitudine selectiva texturarum et viarum, non ex qualitate occulta indeterminata, deducitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6","text":"Pluralitas causarum legitima manet, si unaquaeque causa effectui, signis et finibus naturae congruit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","text":"Exemplar gubernationis divinae deficit, quia eventuum distributio nec voluntatem constantem nec discrimen morale exhibet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D8","text":"Eventus localiter ingens pars exigua summae rerum esse potest atque ideo ordinem naturalem non excedit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D9","text":"Morborum regionalium diversitas ex compositione locali caeli, aquarum, terrae et corporum explicatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10","text":"Organismus ambitu suo materialiter continuus est, quia corpora externa per aerem, cibum, aquam et foramina corporis recipit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","text":"Contagio officium curandi in structuram duplicis periculi convertit, qua praesentia curantem, fuga aegrotum perdit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0082","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","text":"Materialis pestilentia, ubi remedium commune deest, ordines curae, religionis et sepulturae dissolvere potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD1","text":"Physica munus ethicum gerit, quia emendatio opinionum causalium fines timori cupidinique imponit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2","text":"Eadem ontologia materialis caelum, terram, corpora animantia et pestilentiam complectitur, quamvis mechanismi particulares varientur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3","text":"Pluralismus causalis non in relativismum abit, quia possibilia per signa, texturas et potestatum fines restringuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4","text":"Recta pietas deos a gubernatione mundi removet, quoniam pax divina cum administratione irata rerum componi nequit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5","text":"Meritium, innocentia et sanctitas nullam vim causaliter determinantem in distributione calamitatum habent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6","text":"Subiectum humanum non est unitas clausa, sed nodus permeabilis intra circulationem corporum ambientalium.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD7","text":"Contingentia concursus intra necessitates potestatum materialium operatur; casus eventum eligit, non rationem naturae tollit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8","text":"Intelligibilitas naturalis neque remedium certum neque imperium practicum neque conservationem ordinis socialis praestat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX1","text":"Quod causa nondum cognita est, inde gubernator divinus inferri non potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX2","text":"Admiratio et magnitudo percepta auctoritatem epistemicam per se non habent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX3","text":"Ordo physicus non continet legem qua eventus secundum meritum morale distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX4","text":"Explicatio naturalis non semper cognitionem unius causae actualis requirit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX5","text":"Mensura humana magnitudinis non est mensura summae rerum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX6","text":"Terminus inter corpus et ambitum neque clausus neque causaliter absolutus est.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX7","text":"Regularitas naturae providentiam, propositum morale vel ordinationem ad usum humanum non implicat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX8","text":"Naturalitas causae neque benignitatem effectus neque facultatem eius coercendi implicat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX1","text":"Providentia divina ex systemate tamquam principium supervacuum et cum pace deorum incompatibile excluditur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX2","text":"Humanitas neque centrum summae rerum neque mensura magnitudinis aut finalitatis cosmicae est.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3","text":"Nulla immunitas ontologica hominibus, innocentibus, templis aut ipsi mundo conceditur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX4","text":"Clausura epistemica totalis et dominium practicum naturae indisponibilia manent, quamvis fines causarum cognoscantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5","text":"Calamitas causam materialem habet, sed nullum destinatarium moralem vel auctorem iratum requirit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX6","text":"Geographia naturae nullam aperturam ad regnum poenale post mortem admittere cogitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS1","text":"Machina therapeutica hunc ordinem sequitur: copia externa sine receptaculo sano → inexpletio et corruptio → cupiditas ac timor → definitio finium → tranquillitas.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS2","text":"Machina diagnostica hunc ordinem sequitur: phaenomenon terribile → ignorantia causae → attributio divina → timor religiosus → inquisitio materialis → remotio gubernationis divinae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS3","text":"Machina explicativa hunc ordinem sequitur: effectus sensibilis → signa et analogiae → fines potestatum → mechanismi possibiles → selectio causae vel pluralitas conclusa.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS4","text":"Machina interactionis hunc ordinem sequitur: corpus cum inani → emanatio corporum → viae selectivae → pressio, penetratio, coniunctio vel repulsio → effectus manifestus.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS5","text":"Machina pestilentiae hunc ordinem sequitur: semina morbida → corruptio aeris vel alimentorum → ingressus in corpora → contagio → dilemmata curae → dissolutio remedii, ritus et ordinis socialis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS6","text":"Clausura systematis hunc ordinem sequitur: cognitio causarum → cognitio finium → imminutio admirationis → remotio timoris divini → pax animi et rectior deorum conceptio.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AF1","text":"Inventio therapeutica → diagnosis animi vitiosi → necessitas rationis naturae → mortalitas mundi → explicatio caelestium → explicatio terrestrium → interactiones singulares → pestilentia → dissolutio socialis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AF2","text":"In singulis tractationibus motus recurrit: admiratio vel terror → effectus observatus → mechanismus materialis unus vel multiplex → refutatio causae divinae → reductio timoris.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME1","text":"Securitas ab possessione rerum externarum ad capacitatem animi eas recipiendi transfertur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME2","text":"Religio terrifica ex cognitione deorum in effectum ignorantiae causarum reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter pietatem et naturalismum corruit, quia pax deorum per exclusionem interventus divini servatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME4","text":"Pluralitas causarum ex defectu rationis in formam disciplinae causaliter restrictae convertitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME5","text":"Caelum, terra, corpus et morbus in unum continuum mechanismorum materialium rediguntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME6","text":"Miraculum ex genere separato rerum in effectum magnitudinis, raritatis et ignorantiae transfertur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME7","text":"Corpus animatum ex unitate clausa in structuram permeabilem et ambientaliter dependentem reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME8","text":"Calamitas ex iudicio morali in processum materialem cum effectibus biologicis, socialibus et ritualibus reordinatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9","text":"Cognitio naturae a promissione remedii, tutelae vel imperii practici distinguitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT1","text":"In gradu superficiei explicantur eventus caelestes, terrestres, magnetici, morbidi et pestilentiales qui admirationem vel timorem movent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT2","text":"In gradu structurali eventus ex corporibus, inani, effluviis, texturis, viis, motibus et compositionibus localibus generantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT3","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen a placandis potestatibus divinis ad corrigendas opiniones et definiendos fines causalium potentiarum transfertur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT4","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas ex corporibus mortalibus, compositione, dissolutione, contingentibus concursibus et summa infinita constat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT1","text":"Corpus axiomatum sufficit ad motum ab anxietate animi per naturalem causalitatem ad pestilentiae consequentias restituendum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT2","text":"Summi boni materia et plena doctrina cupidinis ac timoris praesupponuntur potius quam hoc libro determinate explicantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT3","text":"Multorum eventuum mechanismus actualis indeterminatus manet, quia textus saepe possibilitatem naturalem demonstrat sine causa singulari eligenda.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT4","text":"Systema interne cohaeret, quia pluralitatem mechanismorum cum unitate ontologiae et finibus potestatum componit; contradictio directa non emergit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT5","text":"Firmissima commissura est nexus inter ignorantiam causarum, attributionem divinam, timorem et disciplinam finium naturalium.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT6","text":"Fragilitas principalis in analogiis consistit, quae mechanismi possibilitatem firmant sed eius actualitatem non semper probant.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT7","text":"Affirmatio rationis verissimae cum frequenti pluralitate causarum tensionem gignit inter certitudinem therapeuticam et modestiam epistemicam.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT8","text":"Promissio purificationis animi a pestilentiae clausura comprimitur, quia cognitio naturalis nec remedium commune nec stabilitatem socialem praestat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT9","text":"Dei tamquam entia pacata servantur, sed eorum status ontologicus et relatio ad simulacra divina intra hanc machinam non plene derivantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT10","text":"Lacunae fontis nexus quosdam locales relinquunt, sed restitutio servat motum principalem a therapia animi ad physicam causarum et inde ad vulnerabilitatem mortalem.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T231328950159Z_f2f1c7bb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book Six","source_sha256":"6d87d1205a9455243b7db1dd69679ea833de1a66f5bdf8517b73708a6ef56ef4","sha256":"f69e0f9599b384301d98b8f206c36a8b7941fbb8e96b4745b562d055a7c2fc05","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:13:39.584618+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS1","text":"It is asked how fear of celestial, terrestrial, and diseased things may be removed, since external advantages do not remove anxiety of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS2","text":"The account of nature purifies the mind, because it explains events within the limits of material powers and excludes divine governance from the causality of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS3","text":"The argumentation is diagnostic, polemical, reconstructive, and therapeutic: it refers fear to ignorance, ignorance to false religion, and both to knowledge of causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS4","text":"The movement of the argument proceeds from the defective receptacle of the mind to bodies, void, motions, and textures as the common grammar of events.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","text":"Each phenomenon is reduced from sensible signs to possible natural causes, while divine explanations are refuted through the limits of powers and the distribution of effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6","text":"Plague carries the argument from physical causes to biological mortality and social dissolution, showing that understanding nature is not the same as remedy or control.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A1","text":"An abundance of external goods does not remove anxiety if the receptacle of the mind remains fluid, insatiable, or corruptive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2","text":"The healing of the mind requires the limits of desire and fear, and the account of the highest good, to be determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3","text":"Terror of mind is dissolved not by sensible light, but by the sight of nature and by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","text":"Ignorance of causes refers natural events to the rule of the gods.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","text":"The gods live a secure and tranquil eternity and do not administer the events of the world by anger or will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","text":"The world and heaven are bodily, born, mortal, and dissoluble.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","text":"No manifest body is wholly full; every one is woven together from body and void.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","text":"The power of every nature is finite and contained by a fixed limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","text":"From all things bodies continually flow and are scattered into the surrounding spaces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","text":"The textures of first-beginnings and the forms of passages determine which bodies receive, penetrate, join with, or repel one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","text":"Material motion produces natural changes through collision, impact, pressure, weight, and the evacuation of place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","text":"Hidden causes are inferred from manifest effects and analogies of sensible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","text":"The single cause of certain effects cannot be determined; several possible natural causes must be enumerated, one of which actually operates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","text":"The infinite totality of things and the mobility of first-beginnings supply sufficient matter for local events.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15","text":"Regions, on account of the diverse composition of bodies, produce diverse effects in living beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16","text":"Disease-bearing seeds may gather from an external heaven or corrupted earth, infect air, waters, and foods, enter bodies, and spread from some to others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17","text":"In a contagious calamity, proximity to the sick increases contagion, whereas absence deprives them of aid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX1","text":"Riches, honors, praise, and fame do not of themselves heal domestic anxiety.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX2","text":"The rays of the sun and the light of day do not dispel the darkness of the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX3","text":"Thunder, lightning, earthquakes, eruptions, and plagues are not acts of divine anger or will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX4","text":"A false opinion about the gods does not violate the gods, but corrupts the tranquility of the one holding the opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX5","text":"Lightning does not distribute moral punishment, because it strikes indiscriminately the innocent, deserted places, mountains, temples, and images.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX6","text":"Divinatory songs do not disclose the hidden mind of the gods, nor do they explain the nature of lightning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX7","text":"Avernian places are not gates of Orcus, nor does the falling of living beings prove the passage of souls to the underworld.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX8","text":"A spring warm by night is not heated by the sun penetrating through thick earth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX9","text":"The unusual magnitude of an event does not prove its supernatural character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX10","text":"One certain cause is not a necessary condition of every natural explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX11","text":"The world has not been entrusted with incorruptible and eternal preservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12","text":"In plague, a certain and common means of remedy is not necessarily at hand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX13","text":"Neither religion nor the custom of burial remains inviolate under the force of present suffering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1","text":"Therapy is directed toward correcting the form of the mind, not toward the infinite accumulation of external goods.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","text":"Even when the tranquility of the gods is known, wonder without knowledge of causes falls back into the old religion.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D3","text":"Celestial and terrestrial events are explained by the same grammar of bodies, void, and motions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D4","text":"Penetration, attraction, and action between distant bodies may be effected through effluvia, openings, and displacement of the medium.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D5","text":"The diversity of effects is deduced from the selective suitability of textures and passages, not from an indeterminate hidden quality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6","text":"A plurality of causes remains legitimate if each cause accords with the effect, the signs, and the limits of nature.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","text":"The model of divine governance fails, because the distribution of events displays neither constant will nor moral discrimination.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D8","text":"An event immense locally may be a small part of the totality of things and therefore does not exceed natural order.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D9","text":"The diversity of regional diseases is explained by the local composition of the sky, waters, earth, and bodies.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10","text":"The organism is materially continuous with its environment, because it receives external bodies through air, food, water, and bodily openings.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","text":"Contagion transforms the duty of care into a structure of double danger, in which presence destroys the caregiver and flight destroys the sick person.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0082","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","text":"Material plague, where a common remedy is lacking, can dissolve the orders of care, religion, and burial.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD1","text":"Physics performs an ethical function, because the correction of causal opinions imposes limits upon fear and desire.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2","text":"The same material ontology encompasses heaven, earth, living bodies, and plague, although particular mechanisms vary.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3","text":"Causal pluralism does not lapse into relativism, because possibilities are constrained by signs, textures, and limits of powers.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4","text":"Right piety removes the gods from governance of the world, since divine peace cannot be reconciled with an angry administration of things.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5","text":"Merit, innocence, and sanctity have no causally determining force in the distribution of calamities.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6","text":"The human subject is not a closed unity, but a permeable node within the circulation of environmental bodies.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD7","text":"The contingency of encounters operates within the necessities of material powers; chance selects the event, but does not remove the account of nature.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8","text":"Natural intelligibility guarantees neither a certain remedy, nor practical control, nor preservation of social order.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX1","text":"From the fact that a cause is not yet known, a divine governor cannot be inferred.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX2","text":"Wonder and perceived magnitude have no epistemic authority in themselves.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX3","text":"Physical order does not contain a law by which events are distributed according to moral merit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX4","text":"Natural explanation does not always require knowledge of one actual cause.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX5","text":"The human measure of magnitude is not the measure of the totality of things.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX6","text":"The boundary between body and environment is neither closed nor causally absolute.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX7","text":"The regularity of nature does not imply providence, moral purpose, or an ordering for human use.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX8","text":"The naturalness of a cause implies neither the beneficence of its effect nor the capacity to restrain it.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX1","text":"Divine providence is excluded from the system as a superfluous principle and one incompatible with the peace of the gods.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX2","text":"Humanity is neither the center of the totality of things nor the measure of cosmic magnitude or finality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3","text":"No ontological immunity is granted to human beings, the innocent, temples, or the world itself.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX4","text":"Total epistemic closure and practical mastery of nature remain unavailable, even though the limits of causes are known.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5","text":"Calamity has a material cause, but requires no moral recipient or angry author.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX6","text":"The geography of nature is not compelled to admit any opening to a punitive kingdom after death.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS1","text":"The therapeutic machine follows this order: external abundance without a healthy receptacle → insatiability and corruption → desire and fear → definition of limits → tranquility.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS2","text":"The diagnostic machine follows this order: terrible phenomenon → ignorance of cause → divine attribution → religious fear → material inquiry → removal of divine governance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS3","text":"The explanatory machine follows this order: sensible effect → signs and analogies → limits of powers → possible mechanisms → selection of a cause or constrained plurality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS4","text":"The interaction machine follows this order: body with void → emanation of bodies → selective passages → pressure, penetration, conjunction, or repulsion → manifest effect.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS5","text":"The plague machine follows this order: disease-bearing seeds → corruption of air or food → entry into bodies → contagion → dilemmas of care → dissolution of remedy, rite, and social order.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS6","text":"The closure of the system follows this order: knowledge of causes → knowledge of limits → diminution of wonder → removal of divine fear → peace of mind and a more correct conception of the gods.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AF1","text":"Therapeutic discovery → diagnosis of the defective mind → necessity of the account of nature → mortality of the world → explanation of celestial things → explanation of terrestrial things → particular interactions → plague → social dissolution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:AF2","text":"In individual treatments the movement recurs: wonder or terror → observed effect → one or multiple material mechanisms → refutation of divine cause → reduction of fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME1","text":"Security is transferred from possession of external things to the capacity of the mind to receive them.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME2","text":"Terrifying religion is reclassified from knowledge of the gods into an effect of ignorance of causes.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME3","text":"The opposition between piety and naturalism collapses, because the peace of the gods is preserved through the exclusion of divine intervention.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME4","text":"A plurality of causes is transformed from a defect of reasoning into a form of causally constrained discipline.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME5","text":"Heaven, earth, body, and disease are reduced to one continuum of material mechanisms.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME6","text":"Miracle is transferred from a separate class of things into an effect of magnitude, rarity, and ignorance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME7","text":"The living body is reclassified from a closed unity into a permeable and environmentally dependent structure.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME8","text":"Calamity is reordered from moral judgment into a material process with biological, social, and ritual effects.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9","text":"Knowledge of nature is distinguished from the promise of remedy, protection, or practical control.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, celestial, terrestrial, magnetic, diseased, and pestilential events that provoke wonder or fear are explained.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, events are generated from bodies, void, effluvia, textures, passages, motions, and local compositions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, the contest is transferred from propitiating divine powers to correcting opinions and defining the limits of causal powers.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality consists of mortal bodies, composition, dissolution, contingent encounters, and an infinite totality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT1","text":"The body of axioms suffices to reconstruct the movement from anxiety of mind through natural causality to the consequences of plague.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT2","text":"The matter of the highest good and the full doctrine of desire and fear are presupposed rather than determinately explained in this book.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT3","text":"The actual mechanism of many events remains indeterminate, because the text often demonstrates natural possibility without selecting a single cause.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT4","text":"The system coheres internally, because it combines a plurality of mechanisms with unity of ontology and limits of powers; no direct contradiction emerges.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT5","text":"The firmest joint is the connection among ignorance of causes, divine attribution, fear, and the discipline of natural limits.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT6","text":"The principal fragility consists in analogies, which establish the possibility of a mechanism but do not always prove its actuality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT7","text":"The affirmation of the truest account generates tension with the frequent plurality of causes between therapeutic certainty and epistemic modesty.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT8","text":"The promise of purification of mind is constrained by the closure on plague, because natural knowledge guarantees neither a common remedy nor social stability.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0081","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT9","text":"The gods are preserved as peaceful beings, but their ontological status and relation to divine images are not fully derived within this machine.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]},{"id":"CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT10","text":"Gaps in the source leave certain local connections, but the reconstruction preserves the principal movement from therapy of the mind to the physics of causes and thence to mortal vulnerability.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"},{"id":"RAW_0T6T_LI_3:PRC2807FB1E4DCBC44:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"revised-passages"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_0T6T_LI_3@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_0T6T_LI_3:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 06","title":"Book Six","context":"Chapter 06","original_title":"Liber Sextus","original_context":"Chapter 06"},"original_title":"Liber Sextus","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 01–03","order":1,"parents":["CH_0T6T_LIBP","CH_0T6T_LIBS","CH_0T6T_LIBT"],"revision":"CL_0T6T_01@R001","sha256":"62c170de9bb0facfe4ee3db382eb357356542a395557744e5b05b64ff4ea63dc","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quibus principiis materia, motus, compositio et vita constent, ut cognitio eorum terrorem religiosum et metum mortis dissolvat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis naturam constituit ut campum corporum primorum in inani motorum, ex quorum coniunctionibus res, qualitates, mundi et personae temporariae emergunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-03","text":"Terror non ut responsio ad potestatem transcendentem, sed ut effectus ignorantiae causalitatis, falsa bonorum aestimatio et anticipatio subiecti post mortem denuo constituitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-04","text":"Argumentatio diagnostica, reconstructiva, polemica et therapeutica fit, quia errorem affectivum detegit, structuram invisibilem restituit, exemplaria aemula excludit atque vitam praesentem reordinat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-05","text":"Motus manifestus ab effectibus sensibilibus ad principia occulta, ab his ad compositiones emergentes, deinde ad mortalitatem mundi et personae procedit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-06","text":"Motus verus est recursivus et cumulativus: sensus indicia praebet, ratio causas invisibiles construit, consequentiae ad apparentias redeunt, probationes autem multiplicatae statum affectivum mutant.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-07","text":"Directio systematis a concursibus sine consilio ad ordinem materialem, ab ordine ad vitam sensibilem, a vita finita ad therapiam sine providentia fertur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D9","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-08","text":"Clausura est causaliter naturalis et therapeutice terminalis: exterior divinus excluditur, sed series naturalis non omnino fatalis manet; dissolutio personae aufert ipsum subiectum mali posthumi.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Ratio naturae terrorem dissolvit non consolatione extrinseca, sed translatione affectus in quaestionem causalitatis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Omnis processus naturalis in corporibus primis, inani et motibus eorum fundatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Materia nec ex nihilo producitur nec ad nihilum reducitur, sed inter formas successive distribuitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Primordia solida, immutabilia et perpetuo mobilia permanent, dum concilia eorum nascuntur et dissolvuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Configuratio principiorum primum diversitatem rerum, deinde qualitates emergentes, postremo identitatem personae constituit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Infinitas materiae promiscuitatem non infert, quia semina certa et compatibilitates selectivae fines generationis servant.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Infinitas summae mundum a centro removet, pluralitatem mundorum generat et quemque mundum in concilium locale atque mortale mutat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D8","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Clinamen seriem causarum fataliter clausam interrumpit sine causa supernaturali introducenda.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Sensus est simul fundamentum cognitionis et effectus emergens communis ordinationis corporis, animi atque animae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Dei causaliter semoti neque naturam administrant neque ordinem mundanum ad usum humanum dirigunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Persona est ordinatio temporaria corporis, animi, animae et motuum sensiferorum, non substantia simplex transmigrans.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Mors dissolvit concilium personae; quia idem subiectum sentiens non superest, mors ei malum fieri non potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Divitiae, gloria, potentia et violentia sunt circuitus compensatorii quibus metus mortis frustra tegitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Vita est usus finitus materiae, cui sufficientia non ex duratione indefinita sed ex terminatione cupiditatis provenit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-01","text":"Distinctio corporis et inanis identitatem ontologicam retinet atque fundamentum tam cosmologiae quam physicae animi manet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-02","text":"Concilium redit functione amplificata: primo rem genitam, deinde proprietatem relationalem, tandem personam temporalem significat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-03","text":"Qualitas a dependentia corporum ad emergentiam dispositionis migrat; quod initio eventum non substantialem designat, postea differentiam inter principia insensilia et effectus sensibiles ordinat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A12","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-04","text":"Sensus a norma epistemica in effectum corporeum mutatur, deinde in condicionem continuitatis personalis absorbetur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-05","text":"Infinitas ab amplitudine summae ad pluralitatem mundorum amplificatur atque postremo omnem exceptionem cosmicam vel individualem excludit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-06","text":"Mors invertitur: ex momento destructionis fit redistributio materiae, ex vicissitudine compositionis fit absentia subiecti patientis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-07","text":"Mythologia a causalitate naturali removetur, sed non simpliciter evanescit: nomina divina et inferna in figuras generationis, affectuum et conscientiae transferuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-08","text":"Invocatio divina literalitatem amittit, dum vis poetica eius in instrumentum dispositionis et transmissionis rationis absorbetur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-09","text":"Clausura causalis functionem mutat: exclusio interventionis divinae persistit, sed necessitas absoluta per clinamen limitatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-01","text":"Probationes particulares inanis et minimorum inferiores manent; hoc gradu valent ut condiciones iam constitutae motus, compositionis et dissolutionis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:D4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-02","text":"Mechanica clinaminis et figurarum atomicarum localiter motum explicat, sed sursum amplificatur in theoriam differentiae emergentis et agentiae non fatalis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:D3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-03","text":"Distributio anatomica animi et animae mechanismus localis manet; eius effectus praesentis gradus est definitio personae ut organizationis materialis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-04","text":"Ontologia corporis et inanis omnes unitates coniungit, quia eadem fundamenta a rebus externis ad animam et identitatem transferuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-05","text":"Mortalitas mundi et mortalitas animi efficiunt transitionem maiorem qua lex compositorum a cosmologia ad anthropologiam descendit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-06","text":"Appellationes mythicae locales aliter codificantur: causalitatem amittunt, sed in schemata generationis naturalis vel perturbationis praesentis servantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-07","text":"Refutationes elementorum sensibilium, medii cosmici et dispositionis Democriteae munere polemico perfecto non fiunt axiomata globalia, quamquam fines exemplarium admissibilium muniunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-01","text":"Generatio et interitus neque creatio absoluta neque annihilatio absoluta sunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-02","text":"Nulla tertia natura incorporea intra ordinem causalitatis admittitur, ideo anima separabilis causaliter agens excluditur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX8","CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-03","text":"Ordo mundanus neque consilium principiorum neque creationem ad hominis utilitatem neque administrationem divinam requirit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX15","CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-04","text":"Medium universale, mundus unicus et exceptio individualis sunt formae eiusdem privilegii cosmici repudiati.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX14","CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX15","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-05","text":"Principia ultima non gerunt qualitates sensibiles aut formas mortales compositorum quae efficiunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX11","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-06","text":"Lux sensibilis, opes, honores et mutatio loci causam perturbationis non attingunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-07","text":"Anima extra corpus integra non persistit, inferi posthumi non subsistunt, mors autem non est malum a mortuo experiendum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX9","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-01","text":"Ut systema constet, ignorantia causae non potest vim probativam pro numine habere.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-02","text":"Apparentia sensibilis non potest esse inventarium completum realitatis neque qualitates conspicuae essentiam principiorum immediate manifestare.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-03","text":"Stabilitas ordinis non potest intentionem praesupponere, sed neque omnis motus potest serie antecedentium perfecte necessaria claudi.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-04","text":"Identitas non potest in materia uniformi aut mera eiusdem materiae restitutione consistere; ordinatio selectiva et continuitas sensus requiruntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-05","text":"Subiectum propriae non-exsistentiae testis esse non potest; sine eo neque malum neque poena posthuma locum habet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:DNX2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-06","text":"Securitas non potest per accumulationem rerum exteriorum aut per fugam spatialem acquiri, quia utraque causam perturbationis intactam relinquit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:DNX6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-01","text":"Systema circa absentiam omnis exterioris ordinatur: nullus creator, terminator, receptaculum aut supplementum extra summam rerum poni potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-02","text":"Nulla potestas transcendens personam dissolutam reservat neque prospectum personalem trans mortis terminum continuat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-03","text":"Initium absolutum, interitus absolutus et status finalis universalis sunt possibilitates inadmissibiles; permanent tantum redistributiones sine clausura ultima.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-04","text":"Nullus locus, mundus, homo aut destinatarius privilegiatus potest ordinem infinitum ad se referre.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-05","text":"Possibilitas maxime exclusa est mors ut experientia personae: mors ipsum campum experientiae aufert.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-01","text":"Eadem lex compositionis et dissolutionis res particulares, mundos et personas regit; differentia inter eos est magnitudinis et ordinationis, non generis ontologici.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-02","text":"Qualitas, sensus et identitas personalis sunt proprietates relationum materialium quae in primordiis separatim inesse non debent.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","CL_0T6T_01:GA-09","CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-03","text":"Clausura naturalis excludit arbitrium divinum, non autem omnem contingentiam; clinamen facit ut immanentia materialis a fatalismo distinguatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","CL_0T6T_01:GA-08","CL_0T6T_01:GA-10","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-04","text":"Mundus localis et persona localis sunt stabilizationes temporariae intra campum materialem maiorem, unde neuter singularitatem aut immunitatem a dissolutione possidet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-07","CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D13","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-05","text":"Religio timida, appetitus bonorum exteriorum et ambitio politica sunt varia remedia falsa eiusdem ignorantiae de natura et mortalitate.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","CL_0T6T_01:GA-13","CL_0T6T_01:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-06","text":"Methodus quae ex effectibus sensibilibus causas invisibiles infert eodem actu corpora prima, motus sub limine sensus et corporeitatem animi restituit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","CL_0T6T_01:GA-09","CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D11","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-07","text":"Sublata providentia mundana et subiecto posthumo, ordo moralis ad effectus praesentes actionum, affectuum et conscientiae reponitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-10","CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ME8","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-01","text":"Realitas exhibet ordines inclusos stabilizationis: concilia rerum intra mundos locales et personae intra concilia organica, omnibus dissolutioni obnoxiis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:D13","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-02","text":"Tempus stratificatur in motum aeternum principiorum, durationes finitas mundorum et vitae, atque absentiam cuiuslibet temporis experti post dissolutionem personae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-03","text":"Agentia naturalis neque libertas a natura neque mera necessitas est, sed apertura localis intra leges materiales et compatibilitates determinatas.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-04","text":"Veritas physica criterium therapeuticum accipit: tabula causalitatis probatur etiam per facultatem suam terrores fictos et fugas exteriores dissolvendi.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-05","text":"Ambitio et violentia politica non sunt principia autonoma, sed amplificationes sociales metus mortis intra cosmologiam sine privilegio humano.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-06","text":"Bonum vitae finitae non pendet a perpetuitate personae, sed a limitatione cupiditatis et usu temporario materiae communis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-01","text":"Ignorantia causarum → terror indefinitus → religio et metus mortis → conatus compensatorii → maior perturbatio → renovata necessitas rationis naturalis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-02","text":"Effectus sensibilis → causa invisibilis restituta → apparentia reclassificata → novum indicium sensibile constituit circuitum epistemicum systematis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-03","text":"Compositio → proprietas emergens → dissolutio → redistributio materiae → nova compositio est circuitus conservationis qui generationem sine initio absoluto sustinet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-04","text":"Concursus innumeri → dispositiones stabiles → mundi locales → incrementum et senectus → dissolutio → novi concursus efficiunt spiralem cosmologicam.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D8","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-05","text":"Metus mortis → ambitio et violentia → productio mortis → metus amplificatus est via catastrophica qua fuga a morte obiectum suum multiplicat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:D2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-06","text":"Attractor occultus est configuratio: diversitas rerum, proprietates compositorum et persona ad modos ordinationis potius quam ad substantias separatas convergunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B01","text":"Ignorantia causalitatis → terror religiosus et mortis → perturbatio affectiva → quaestio de natura.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B02","text":"Corpora prima + inane → campus ontologicus sufficiens sine tertia natura aut interventu externo.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B03","text":"Motus perpetuus + pondus + plagae + clinamen → concursus non providentiales neque fataliter exhausti.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B04","text":"Figurae et ordines varii + compatibilitas selectiva → genera stabilia sine exemplaribus transcendentibus.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B05","text":"Principia qualitatibus carentia + ordinatio relationum → qualitates, vita et sensus emergentes.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B06","text":"Stabilizatio localis → res composita / mundus / persona → incrementum, alteratio et mortalitas.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:D13","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B07","text":"Dissolutio conciliorum → materia conservata → successio formarum sine annihilatione aut identitate personali restituta.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B08","text":"Reclassificatio causalitatis → remotio providentiae et subiecti posthumi → imminutio cupiditatis compensatoriae → tranquillitas vitae finitae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AFM-B01","text":"Effectus sensibilis → causa corporea invisibilis → corpus et inane → motus principiorum → compositio selectiva → proprietates emergentes → mundus et persona mortales → redistributio materiae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorantia → terror → religio, cupiditas et ambitio → investigatio naturae → mortalitas animi demonstrata → nullum subiectum post mortem → sufficientia vitae finitae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-01","text":"Oppositio inter visibile et reale corruit: invisibilitas non removet rem e cognitione, sed methodum inferentialem requirit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME2","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-02","text":"Qualitas et sensus ex proprietatibus simplicibus substantiarum in effectus relationum et motuum communium reclassificantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:ME2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-03","text":"Mundus et mens ex entibus privilegiatis in organizationes locales, multiplicabiles et mortales transferuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:ME7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-04","text":"Ager causalitatis non iam inter providentiam et fatum dividitur; immanentia materialis contingentiam limitatam continere potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ME8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-05","text":"Mors et vitia politica in eadem tabula disponuntur: absentia subiecti posthumi verum mortis statum constituit, dum imaginatio contraria cupiditatem et violentiam parit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-06","text":"Oppositio inter doctrinam et therapiam solvitur: forma poetica rationem transmittit, ratio autem campum affectivum et practicum reordinat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME9","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei principia rerum, metus humanos, qualitates conspicuas, naturam animi et quaestionem mortis coniungit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ALT1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis corpora prima, inane, motus, compositiones, leges conexionis et organizationem sensiferam continet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ALT2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus apparentias in vestigia causarum convertit atque certamen contra deos, fatum et mortem in correctionem tabulae causalitatis mutat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ALT3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis materiam aeternam, concilia mortalia, mundos plurales, identitatem continuam et mortem sine experientia distinguit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ALT4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus obscuritatem doctrinae per carmen, analogiam et probationem cumulativam tractat, ita ut forma expositionis transitum ab affectu ad rationem efficiat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-01","text":"Sensus fundamentum probationis manet, quamquam ratio inventarium apparentiae corrigere debet; regula huius correctionis non plene formalizatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-02","text":"Leges certas compositionis et compatibilitatis clinamen loco temporeque incertum interrumpit, sine clara mensura relationis inter necessitatem et contingentiam.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-03","text":"Sensus ex principiis insensilibus emergere debet, sed transitus a figura et motu ad qualitatem sentiendi mechanice incompletus manet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-04","text":"Immortalitas deorum cum lege dissolutionis compositorum nondum componitur, quia dei remoti potius postulantur quam materialiter restituti.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-05","text":"Invocatio Veneris vim agendi poeticam retinet, quamquam interventio divina physice negatur; distinctio figurae et causalitatis hanc commissuram sustinet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-06","text":"Unitas animi et animae cum hierarchia inter animum regentem, animam consentientem et quartam vim sensiferam imperfecte cohaeret.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MP-01","text":"Qualitas mutatur ex accidente dependenti in proprietatem relationalem emergentem quae gradus ontologicos distinguit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MP-02","text":"Clinamen causalitatem clausam mutat in immanentiam apertam, sed nexus inter declinationem atomicam et voluntatem conscientem fragilis manet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MP-03","text":"Mors ab eventu dissolutionis ad mutationem logicam transit qua, subiecto sublato, ipsa relatio nocendi impossibilis fit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MP-04","text":"Identitas a similitudine materiae ad continuitatem organizationis, sensus et memoriae transfertur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:D10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est campus infinitus corporum mobilium qui sine centro concilia localia, mundos et ruinas eorum generat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus corporeus temporarius in quo animus, anima, organa et motus sensiferi identitatem finitam efficiunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est distributio ordinum heterochronorum: principia manent, concilia durant et intereunt, persona autem propriam absentiam numquam experitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga e natura aut e se ipso, sed cognitio causarum intra naturam non fataliter clausam.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam catenam a materia conservata et motu ad emergentiam, mundos mortales, personam dissolubilem et therapiam metus.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est coniunctio conservationis materiae, explicationis configurationalis et probationis cumulativae mortalitatis organicae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinati manent nexus a clinamine ad voluntatem et a motibus primordiorum insensilium ad sensum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B04","text":"Systema hoc gradu cohaeret quia motum ab effectu affectivo ad physicam, ab ontologia ad cosmologiam, deinde ad conversionem vitae servat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B05","text":"Commissurae fragiliores analogiis qualitatum, principio mutationis substantialis et correctione rationali apparentiarum nituntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT6","CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistit transitus performativus quo doctrina obscura per formam poeticam et cognitionem causalitatis in acceptationem affectivam vitae finitae convertitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T231443724088Z_c00c6c2f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 01–03","source_sha256":"62c170de9bb0facfe4ee3db382eb357356542a395557744e5b05b64ff4ea63dc","sha256":"c80882ecd795c1ca4e4f485c1e7b4d0dcd74aa9acec5ada674be9547b0362726","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:14:43.725619+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-01","text":"The system asks by what principles matter, motion, composition, and life consist, so that knowledge of them may dissolve religious terror and fear of death.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes nature as a field of first bodies moving in void, from whose conjunctions things, qualities, worlds, and temporary persons emerge.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-03","text":"Terror is reconstituted not as a response to transcendent power, but as an effect of ignorance of causality, false valuation of goods, and anticipation of the subject after death.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-04","text":"The argument becomes diagnostic, reconstructive, polemical, and therapeutic, because it detects affective error, restores invisible structure, excludes rival models, and reorders present life.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-05","text":"The manifest movement proceeds from sensible effects to hidden principles, from these to emergent compositions, and then to the mortality of the world and the person.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-06","text":"The true movement is recursive and cumulative: sense supplies indications, reason constructs invisible causes, consequences return to appearances, while multiplied proofs alter the affective state.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-07","text":"The direction of the system leads from chance encounters without design to material order, from order to sentient life, from finite life to therapy without providence.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D9","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AS-08","text":"The closure is causally natural and therapeutically terminal: a divine exterior is excluded, yet the natural series does not remain wholly fatal; the dissolution of the person removes the very subject of subsequent evil.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] The account of nature dissolves terror not by extrinsic consolation, but by translating affect into a question of causality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every natural process is founded in first bodies, void, and their motions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Matter is neither produced from nothing nor reduced to nothing, but is successively distributed among forms.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The first principles remain solid, immutable, and perpetually mobile, while their compounds arise and dissolve.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The configuration of principles first constitutes the diversity of things, then emergent qualities, and finally personal identity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Infinity does not entail the indiscriminate mixture of matter, because determinate seeds and selective compatibilities preserve the limits of generation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] The infinity of the whole removes the world from the center, generates a plurality of worlds, and transforms each world into a local and mortal compound.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D8","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Clinamen interrupts a fatally closed sequence of causes without introducing a supernatural cause.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Sense is at once the foundation of knowledge and an emergent effect of the common ordering of body, animus, and anima.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Causally removed gods neither govern nature nor direct worldly order to human use.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] The person is a temporary ordering of body, animus, anima, and sentient motions, not a simple transmigrating substance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Death dissolves the compound of the person; because the same sentient subject does not survive, death cannot become an evil for it.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Wealth, glory, power, and violence are compensatory circuits by which fear of death is vainly covered over.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Life is a finite use of matter, whose sufficiency proceeds not from indefinite duration but from the termination of desire.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-01","text":"The distinction between body and void retains its ontological identity and remains the foundation of both cosmology and the physics of the animus.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-02","text":"Compound recurs with an expanded function: first it signifies the generated thing, then relational property, finally the temporal person.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-03","text":"Quality migrates from dependence on bodies to the emergence of disposition; what initially designates a non-substantial event later orders the difference between insentient principles and sensible effects.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A12","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-04","text":"Sense changes from an epistemic norm into a bodily effect, then is absorbed into the condition of personal continuity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-05","text":"Infinity is amplified from the magnitude of the whole to the plurality of worlds and finally excludes every cosmic or individual exception.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A16","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-06","text":"Death is inverted: from a moment of destruction it becomes redistribution of matter, from a change of composition it becomes the absence of a suffering subject.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-07","text":"Mythology is removed from natural causality, but does not simply vanish: divine and infernal names are transferred into figures of generation, affects, and consciousness.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-08","text":"Divine invocation loses literalness, while its poetic force is absorbed into an instrument of disposition and transmission of reason.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RMD-09","text":"Causal closure changes function: the exclusion of divine intervention persists, but absolute necessity is limited through clinamen.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-01","text":"The particular proofs of void and minima remain lower-level; at this level they are valid as already established conditions of motion, composition, and dissolution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:D4","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-02","text":"The mechanics of clinamen and atomic shapes explains motion locally, but is amplified upward into a theory of emergent difference and non-fatal agency.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:D3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-03","text":"The anatomical distribution of animus and anima remains a local mechanism; its present-level effect is the definition of the person as material organization.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-04","text":"The ontology of body and void unites all units, because the same foundations are carried from external things to the anima and identity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-05","text":"The mortality of the world and the mortality of the animus effect the major transition by which the law of composites descends from cosmology to anthropology.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-06","text":"Local mythical appellations are encoded differently: they lose causality, but are retained in schemas of natural generation or present disturbance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:CGD-07","text":"Having fulfilled their polemical function, refutations of sensible elements, the cosmic medium, and Democritean arrangement do not become global axioms, although they secure the boundaries of admissible models.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-01","text":"Generation and destruction are neither absolute creation nor absolute annihilation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-02","text":"No third incorporeal nature is admitted within the order of causality; therefore a separable causally acting anima is excluded.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX8","CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-03","text":"Worldly order requires neither a design of principles, nor creation for human benefit, nor divine administration.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX15","CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX9","CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-04","text":"A universal medium, a unique world, and individual exception are forms of the same rejected cosmic privilege.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX14","CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX15","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-05","text":"Ultimate principles do not bear sensible qualities or the mortal forms of the composites they produce.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX11","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-06","text":"Sensible light, wealth, honors, and change of place do not reach the cause of disturbance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:NX3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:NX3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:GNX-07","text":"The anima does not persist whole outside the body, posthumous infernal regions do not subsist, and death is not an evil to be experienced by the dead.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX9","CH_0T6T_LIBT:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-01","text":"For the system to stand, ignorance of a cause cannot have probative force for divinity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-02","text":"Sensible appearance cannot be a complete inventory of reality, nor can conspicuous qualities immediately reveal the essence of principles.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-03","text":"The stability of order cannot presuppose intention, but neither can every motion be enclosed by a perfectly necessary series of antecedents.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-04","text":"Identity cannot consist in uniform matter or in the mere restoration of the same matter; selective ordering and continuity of sense are required.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:DNX6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-05","text":"A subject cannot be witness to its own non-existence; without it, neither evil nor subsequent punishment has a place.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:DNX2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:DBN-06","text":"Security cannot be acquired through accumulation of external things or through spatial flight, because each leaves the cause of disturbance untouched.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:DNX6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-01","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of every exterior: no creator, terminator, receptacle, or supplement can be posited outside the sum of things.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-02","text":"No transcendent power preserves the dissolved person or continues a personal perspective beyond the boundary of death.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-03","text":"An absolute beginning, absolute destruction, and a universal final state are inadmissible possibilities; only redistributions without ultimate closure remain.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-04","text":"No privileged place, world, human being, or recipient can refer the infinite order to itself.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSA-05","text":"The possibility most excluded is death as the experience of a person: death removes the very field of experience.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-01","text":"The same law of composition and dissolution governs particular things, worlds, and persons; the difference among them is one of magnitude and ordering, not ontological kind.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-02","text":"Quality, sense, and personal identity are properties of material relations that need not inhere separately in the first principles.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","CL_0T6T_01:GA-09","CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-03","text":"Natural closure excludes divine arbitrariness, but not every contingency; clinamen ensures that material immanence is distinguished from fatalism.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","CL_0T6T_01:GA-08","CL_0T6T_01:GA-10","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-04","text":"The local world and the local person are temporary stabilizations within a greater material field, whence neither possesses singularity or immunity from dissolution.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-07","CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","CH_0T6T_LIBP:D13","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-05","text":"Fearful religion, desire for external goods, and political ambition are various false remedies for the same ignorance concerning nature and mortality.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","CL_0T6T_01:GA-13","CL_0T6T_01:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-06","text":"The method that infers invisible causes from sensible effects restores in the same act first bodies, motions beneath the threshold of sense, and the corporeality of the animus.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","CL_0T6T_01:GA-09","CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D11","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:XD-07","text":"With worldly providence and the subsequent subject removed, moral order is relocated to the present effects of actions, affects, and consciousness.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-10","CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ME8","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-01","text":"Reality exhibits nested orders of stabilization: compounds of things within local worlds and persons within organic compounds, all subject to dissolution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:D13","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-02","text":"Time is stratified into the eternal motion of principles, the finite durations of worlds and lives, and the absence of any experienced time after the dissolution of the person.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-03","text":"Natural agency is neither freedom from nature nor mere necessity, but local openness within material laws and determinate compatibilities.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-04","text":"Physical truth acquires a therapeutic criterion: the causal map is proved also by its capacity to dissolve fictitious terrors and external flights.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-05","text":"Political ambition and violence are not autonomous principles, but social amplifications of fear of death within a cosmology without human privilege.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:DNX6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:BSD-06","text":"The good of finite life does not depend on the perpetuity of the person, but on the limitation of desire and the temporary use of common matter.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-01","text":"Ignorance of causes → indefinite terror → religion and fear of death → compensatory efforts → greater disturbance → renewed need for natural reason.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-02","text":"Sensible effect → restored invisible cause → reclassified appearance → new sensible indication constitutes the epistemic circuit of the system.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-03","text":"Composition → emergent property → dissolution → redistribution of matter → new composition is the circuit of conservation that sustains generation without an absolute beginning.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-04","text":"Innumerable encounters → stable dispositions → local worlds → growth and old age → dissolution → new encounters produce a cosmological spiral.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D8","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-05","text":"Fear of death → ambition and violence → production of death → amplified fear is the catastrophic path by which flight from death multiplies its object.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:D2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:RLE-06","text":"The hidden attractor is configuration: the diversity of things, the properties of composites, and the person converge upon modes of ordering rather than upon separate substances.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B01","text":"Ignorance of causality → religious terror and death → affective disturbance → inquiry into nature.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B02","text":"First bodies + void → an ontological field sufficient without a third nature or external intervention.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B03","text":"Perpetual motion + weight + blows + clinamen → encounters neither providential nor fatally exhausted.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A17","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B04","text":"Various shapes and orders + selective compatibility → stable kinds without transcendent models.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B05","text":"Principles lacking qualities + ordering of relations → emergent qualities, life, and sense.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B06","text":"Local stabilization → composite thing / world / person → growth, alteration, and mortality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:D13","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B07","text":"Dissolution of compounds → conserved matter → succession of forms without annihilation or restored personal identity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FGS-B08","text":"Reclassification of causality → removal of providence and the subsequent subject → diminution of compensatory desire → tranquillity of finite life.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AFM-B01","text":"Sensible effect → invisible bodily cause → body and void → motion of principles → selective composition → emergent properties → mortal world and person → redistribution of matter.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:AS5","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorance → terror → religion, desire, and ambition → investigation of nature → mortality of the animus demonstrated → no subject after death → sufficiency of finite life.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A18","CH_0T6T_LIBT:AS6","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-01","text":"The opposition between visible and real collapses: invisibility does not remove a thing from knowledge, but requires an inferential method.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME2","CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-02","text":"Quality and sense are reclassified from simple properties of substances into effects of relations and common motions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:ME2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-03","text":"World and mind are transferred from privileged entities into local, multipliable, and mortal organizations.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:ME7","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-04","text":"The field of causality is no longer divided between providence and fate; material immanence can contain limited contingency.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ME8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-05","text":"Death and political vices are arranged on the same map: the absence of a subsequent subject constitutes the true state of death, while contrary imagination produces desire and violence.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MME-06","text":"The opposition between doctrine and therapy is dissolved: poetic form transmits reason, while reason reorders the affective and practical field.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ME9","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level joins the principles of things, human fears, conspicuous qualities, the nature of the animus, and the question of death.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ALT1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level contains first bodies, void, motion, compositions, laws of connection, and sentient organization.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ALT2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts appearances into traces of causes and transforms the struggle against gods, fate, and death into correction of the causal map.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ALT3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level distinguishes eternal matter, mortal compounds, plural worlds, continuous identity, and death without experience.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:ALT4","CH_0T6T_LIBS:ALT4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level treats the obscurity of doctrine through poetry, analogy, and cumulative proof, so that the form of exposition effects the transition from affect to reason.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-01","text":"Sense remains the foundation of proof, although reason must correct the inventory of appearance; the rule of this correction is not fully formalized.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-02","text":"Clinamen, uncertain in place and time, interrupts determinate laws of composition and compatibility, without a clear measure of the relation between necessity and contingency.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-03","text":"Sense must emerge from insentient principles, but the transition from shape and motion to the quality of sensing remains mechanically incomplete.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A13","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-04","text":"The immortality of the gods is not yet reconciled with the law of dissolution of composites, because the remote gods are postulated rather than materially reconstructed.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-05","text":"The invocation of Venus retains poetic agency, although divine intervention is physically denied; the distinction between figure and causality sustains this joint.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SNX5","CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:FL-06","text":"The unity of animus and anima coheres imperfectly with the hierarchy between the governing animus, the consenting anima, and the fourth sentient force.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MP-01","text":"Quality changes from a dependent accident into an emergent relational property that distinguishes ontological levels.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MP-02","text":"Clinamen transforms closed causality into open immanence, but the link between atomic deviation and conscious will remains fragile.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:A7","CH_0T6T_LIBS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MP-03","text":"Death passes from an event of dissolution to a logical change whereby, once the subject is removed, the very relation of harming becomes impossible.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD5","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MP-04","text":"Identity is transferred from likeness of matter to continuity of organization, sense, and memory.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:D10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly an infinite field of moving bodies that, without a center, generates local compounds, worlds, and their ruins.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:AS2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a temporary bodily node in which animus, anima, organs, and sentient motions produce finite identity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBT:A15","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is the distribution of heterochronous orders: principles remain, compounds endure and perish, but the person never experiences its own absence.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from nature or from oneself, but knowledge of causes within nature not fatally closed.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:SD3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to reconstruct the chain from conserved matter and motion to emergence, mortal worlds, the dissoluble person, and therapy of fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT1","CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT1","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the conjunction of conservation of matter, configurational explanation, and cumulative proof of organic mortality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT2","CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B03","text":"The links from clinamen to will and from the insentient motions of first principles to sense remain less determinate.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT3","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres at this level because it preserves the movement from affective effect to physics, from ontology to cosmology, and then to the transformation of life.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT10","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B05","text":"The more fragile joints rest on analogies of qualities, the principle of substantial change, and the rational correction of appearances.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBS:MRT4","CH_0T6T_LIBT:MRT6","CH_0T6T_LIBP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","text":"The axiomatic reduction resists the performative transition whereby obscure doctrine is converted, through poetic form and knowledge of causality, into affective acceptance of finite life.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBP:FGS2","CH_0T6T_LIBT:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_0T6T_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_0T6T_01:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 01–03"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · De Rerum Natura","title":"Chapters 01–03","context":"Book One · Book Two · Book Three","original_title":"Chapters 01–03","original_context":"Liber Primus · Liber Secundus · Liber Tertius"},"original_title":"Chapters 01–03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 04–06","order":2,"parents":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ","CH_0T6T_LI_2","CH_0T6T_LI_3"],"revision":"CL_0T6T_02@R001","sha256":"639414bfdc2780f1bc875fcb1c5dc2cdb1c0fdb646bea8703d4f74cd27a05aa8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo perceptio, affectus, mundus, vita, historia et calamitas ex principiis corporeis explicentur, providentia et animorum immortalitate exclusis, ut metus religiosus tollatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis est unam causalitatem materialem, per fluxum, concursum, congruentiam et dissolutionem variatam, operationes sensibiles, ordines cosmicos, formas viventes et eventus morbidos sine ruptura supernaturali generare.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio coniungit restitutionem materialem, polemicam adversus providentiam, genealogiam ordinum emergentium et therapiam opinionum timorem producentium.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-04","text":"Motus progreditur a transmissionibus minimis et perceptione ad cosmogoniam atque historiam, deinde ad calamitatem biologicam quae ordinem socialem dissolvere potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-05","text":"Cognitio a sensibus incipit, causas occultas ex effectibus manifestis infert, atque incertitudinem causae singularis per pluralitatem possibilitatum materialiter finitarum administrat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-06","text":"Motus verus est recursivus et amplificativus: ignorantia causae attributionem divinam et timorem gignit, inquisitio materialis autem circuitum rescindit et ad tranquillitatem redit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-07","text":"Ordo non est exemplar antecedens sed stabilitas temporaria inter concursus, cuius regularitas intentionem ordinatricem non probat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-08","text":"Clausura fit catastrophica potius quam triumphalis: pestilentia confirmat causalitatem materialem, sed simul ostendit intelligibilitatem neque remedium certum neque dominationem practicam praestare.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-09","text":"Clausura ultima est apophatico-therapeutica: pax animi ex cognitione finium et remotione gubernationis divinae petitur, nulla tamen immunitate ontologica hominibus promissa.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnis res operans, animus inclusus, est compositio corporea cuius facultates ex motibus et interactionibus primordiorum oriuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Potestas cuiusque naturae finita est, atque effectus possibilis terminis texturae, motus et compositionis continetur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Effectus neque in agente neque in recipiente solitario residet, sed ex congruentia selectiva corporum, viarum et texturarum emergit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Ordo est stabilizatio temporaria configurationum inter concursus innumeros, non forma finalis antecedenter imposita.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Omne compositum est permeabile, mutabile et mortale; eadem conditio corpus, mundum et ordines ex corporibus constitutos attingit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Affectio sensibilis verum eventum relationis corporeae refert, falsitas autem nascitur cum opinio causam, identitatem vel finem affectioni addit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A12","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Causae occultae ex effectibus sensibilibus inferuntur, sed unitas ontologiae cum pluralitate mechanismorum particularium compatitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Ignorantia causalis imagines, admirationem et eventus naturales in signa potestatis divinae convertit, inde timorem religiosum generans.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Finalitas providentialis excluditur: usus, aptitudo et regularitas sunt effectus structurarum et stabilitatum, non causae originis earum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Subiectum est nexus materialis permeabilis, non principium separatum: imaginibus, ambiente, anima corporea et institutis mediantibus agit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Facultates, usus, vocabula et instituta post structuram per consuetudinem, utilitatem et experientiam emergunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Therapia affectuum opiniones retractat, attentionem convertit et cupidinis timorisque fines definit, potius quam sensus vel corpora repudiat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Intelligibilitas naturalis a dominio practico separatur: causa materialis cognosci potest etiam ubi remedium, tutela vel imperium deest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Tranquillitas ex conspectu naturae et cognitione finium causalium emergit, non ex favore divino aut accumulatione bonorum externorum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Calamitas sine destinatario morali corpora, officia, ritus et ordines sociales materialiter dissolvere potest.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-01","text":"Effluxus corporeus identitatem causalitatis servat, sed a productione simulacrorum ad circulationem ambientalem et morbidam amplificatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-02","text":"Congruentia a qualitate sensibili ad generationem, conservationem biologicam et susceptibilitatem regionalem migrat; functio eius ex perceptione in viabilitatem extenditur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-03","text":"Imago a nuntio sensibili in instrumentum captationis affectivae et attributionis religiosae mutatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-04","text":"Opinio addita affectioni, primo fons erroris perceptivi, in scala sociali fit machina qua ignorantia eventus naturales ad deos refert.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-05","text":"Pluralitas causarum ex cautela astronomica in disciplinam epistemicam generalem amplificatur, qua naturalitas sine falsa certitudine singulari defenditur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-06","text":"Utilitas, postquam ex causa originis organorum in effectum posterioris usus conversa est, in genealogiam institutionum transit et tandem per progressum bellicum invertitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-07","text":"Mortalitas a dissolutione personae ad dissolutionem mundi et ordinum curae atque ritus amplificatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-08","text":"Divinitas inversionem patitur: a causa providentiali removetur, deinde vel nomen honorificum sapientiae vel exemplar pacis non intervenientis fit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-09","text":"Continuitas ex successione imaginum pereuntium ad historiam statuum irreversibilium transfertur; tempus apparens fit tempus generationis et amissionis facultatum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-10","text":"Circuitus amoris, ubi imago attentionem et cupiditatem alit, in circuitu religionis absorbetur, ubi ignorantia, timor et attributio divina se mutuo confirmant.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-01","text":"Repercussiones speculorum, divisiones vocis et singulae transmissiones sensuum machinae locales manent; hoc gradu tantum exemplar commune medii, itineris et ictus sustinent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-02","text":"Causae particulares motuum caelestium localiter indeterminatae manent neque in propositionem globalem de causa singulari convertuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX13","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-03","text":"Physiologia Venerea localis est, sed circuitus imaginis, opinionis et attentionis ad theoriam generalem captationis affectivae amplificatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-04","text":"Semina morbida et contagio sunt mechanismi locales; permeabilitas subiecti et dissolubilitas ordinis socialis sunt consequentiae huius gradus.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-05","text":"Hoc gradu visio, cosmogonia et pestilentia non ut argumenta separata aggregantur, sed ut variationes unius ontologiae materialis per diversas magnitudines coniunguntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-06","text":"Centauri, monstra et alia exemplaria fabulosa opere exclusorio peracto evanescunt; persistit regula incompatibilitatis materialis, non catalogus fabularum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-07","text":"Status ontologicus deorum et simulacrorum divinorum minus determinatus manet; synthesis praesentis gradus tantum eorum gubernationem causaliter excludit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-08","text":"Facultates biologicae, stabilitates cosmicae et nexus subiecti in structuram superiorem absorbentur, cuius termini sunt emergentia, permeabilitas et dissolutio.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat animam personalem incorpoream dissolutionem corporis integram superare.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX1","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-02","text":"Providentia et finalitas excluduntur tamquam causae sensuum, membrorum, mundi et historiae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-03","text":"Scepticismus universalis et tribunal cognitionis sensibus extrinsecum simul reiciuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-04","text":"Imago composita, raritas aut magnitudo eventus correspondentem naturam supernaturalem non requirit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-05","text":"Distributio calamitatum meritum, innocentiam et sanctitatem causaliter non sequitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-06","text":"Homo neque finis processus cosmici neque mensura magnitudinis et aptitudinis naturae est.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-07","text":"Stabilitas foederum, specierum et institutionum aeternitatem vel immutabilitatem non implicat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-08","text":"Divitiae, honores et progressus technicus incrementum necessarium felicitatis non constituunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-09","text":"Cognitio naturalis promissionem remedii, tutelae aut imperii universalis non continet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem regularitatem intentionem ordinatricem vel propositum humanum implicare.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet continuitatem apparentem vel stabilitatem localem substantiam permanentem et aeternam requirere.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet ignorantiam causae testimonium positivum gubernatoris divini praebere.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet mentem vel corpus terminum clausum extra circulationem naturalem constituere.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet futuram utilitatem formam organi vel institutionis originarie causare.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet naturalem explicationem certitudine unius causae actualis in omni casu niti.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse oportet causam naturalem effectum benignum, coercibilem aut remedio communi tractabilem reddere.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-08","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordinem physicum ordinem moralem exprimere vel calamitatem destinatario merenti assignare.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-01","text":"Indisponibilis est locus cognitionis extra sensus, corpus et naturam ex quo totum systema sine mediatione iudicetur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis est custodia providentialis quae mundum regat aut hominibus immunitatem ontologicam concedat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-03","text":"Indisponibilis est possessio plena quae cupiditatem semel satiat, quia relatio semper imaginibus, corporibus et opinionibus mediatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-04","text":"Indisponibilis est finis cosmicus vel historicus qui mundos, civitates et artes ad perfectionem necessariam conducat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-05","text":"Calamitas circum absentiam iudicis moralis ordinatur: effectus habet causas et victimas, sed nullum destinatarium metaphysicum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-06","text":"Clausura epistemica totalis et dominium practicum naturae vetantur, etiamsi fines potestatum ac genera causarum cognosci possunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-01","text":"Circulatio quae imagines ad sensus defert eadem structura amplificata corpora ambientalia et semina morbida diffundit; realitas igitur systemice ut commercium permeabile constituitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-02","text":"Veritas affectionis et indeterminatio mechanismi actualis simul efficiunt realismum asymmetricalem: eventus relationis certior esse potest quam causa singularis eius.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-07","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-03","text":"Cum structura usum antecedat et ordo ex concursibus stabilitur, functio fit effectus retrospectivus conservationis, non consilium originarium.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-04","text":"Captatio erotica et religio eandem formam recursive variant: repraesentatio repetita attentionem figit, affectum auget et fontem commotionis falso obiecto attribuit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-08","CL_0T6T_02:GA-12","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-05","text":"Quia persona, mundus et instituta sunt composita, dissolutio socialis pestilentiae non est casus ontologiae extrinsecus, sed amplificatio mortalitatis communis.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-06","text":"Recta pietas per negationem constituitur: pax deorum servatur tantum si administratio providentialis et ira causaliter operans removentur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-07","text":"Congruentia est operator transversalis qui qualitatem sensibilem, generationem, conservationem specierum et differentiam morborum ex relationibus, non essentiis absolutis, producit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-08","text":"Ars et divitiae facultates augent, sed tranquillitatem aut immunitatem non augent necessario; mediatio technica potest etiam desiderium, bellum et vulnerabilitatem amplificare.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-09","text":"Actio et institutio originem sovereignam non habent: utraque per imagines, motus corporeos, vulnerabilitatem mutuam et consuetudinem distributim constituitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-10","CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-10","text":"Efficacia cognitionis est imprimis modalis et affectiva: causas divinas excludit et timorem minuit, sed eventum naturalem neque necessario sanat neque coercet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-12","CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-01","text":"Causalitas materialis invarians magnitudine est: eadem syntaxis differentiarum, fluxuum, viarum et congruentiarum a perceptione ad cosmologiam et pestilentiam operatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:GA-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-03","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-02","text":"Ontologia huius gregis est ontologia compositorum apertorum, in quibus identitas temporaria ex permutatione materiae et finibus interactionum persistit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-03","text":"Tempus componit successionem celerrimam, emergentiam irreversibilem et dissolutionem mortalem; permanentia est effectus localis intra has tres operationes.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-04","text":"Epistemologia resultans est realismus finitus: affectiones, signa et limites potestatum cognitionem sustinent, dum causa actualis interdum indeterminata manet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-07","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-05","text":"Libertas practica non est exemptio a causalitate, sed facultas circuitus attentionis, opinionis et timoris per cognitionem atque redirectionem materialem interrumpendi.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-10","CL_0T6T_02:GA-12","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-06","text":"Ordo politicus est compositum contingens ex vulnerabilitate et utilitate ortum, ideo eadem dissolutione materiali ac corpora constituentia affici potest.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-07","text":"Religio terrifica est compressio causalis quae ignorantiam in intentionem divinam vertit; pietas naturalis hanc compressionem solvit sine deis ipsis necessario tollendis.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-08","CL_0T6T_02:GA-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-08","text":"Pestilentia promissionem liberationis non negat, sed eius ambitum terminaliter restringit: mens a falso terrore liberari potest, corpus et societas a calamitate non immunia fiunt.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-01","text":"Effluxus → receptio selectiva → affectio → nova dispositio recipientis constituit circuitum basalium interactionum, ex quo sensationes, actiones et contagiones variantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-02","text":"Imago amati → cupiditas → opinio idealizans → attentio fixa → maior copia imaginum efficit escalationem affectivam quae se ipsa pascit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-03","text":"Ignorantia → timor → attributio divina → observatio anxia → renovata ignorantia constituit attractorem religiosum, ad quem admiratio sine causa cognita relabitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-04","text":"Concursus innumeri → configuratio stabilis → ordo localis → conflictus partium → dissolutio → materia novis concursibus reddita constituit circuitum cosmicum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-05","text":"Ars → opes → distinctio → ambitio → arma → periculum collectivum est via amplificationis qua progressus commodorum in augmentum conflictus invertitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-06","text":"Cura aegroti → propinquitas → contagio, sed fuga → desertio → interitus aegroti constituit duplicem clausuram tragicam officii.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-07","text":"Effectus sensibilis → analogiae → fines potestatum → causae naturales restrictae → remotio gubernationis divinae constituit circuitum inquisitivum qui timorem minuit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-08","text":"Attractor communis est cognitio potentiarum finitarum: ad eum perceptionis correctio, cosmogonia naturalis et explicatio calamitatum diversis viis convergunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B01","text":"Differentiae primordiorum + inane → composita corporea varia et permeabilia.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B02","text":"Compositum permeabile → effluxus continuus + motus per medium → commercium cum aliis compositis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B03","text":"Commercium → selectio per vias et texturas → congruentia, repulsio vel penetratio → affectio determinata.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B04","text":"Concursus repetiti → configurationes stabiles → usus, species, ordines cosmici et instituta → dissolutio ubi congruentia deficit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B05","text":"Affectio → opinio addita → falsa identitas, finalitas vel intentio → captatio affectiva et religio terrifica.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B06","text":"Structura nata → usus repetitus → consuetudo et utilitas → facultas vel institutum emergens sine auctore originario.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B07","text":"Effectus manifestus → inferentia causae materialis intra fines potestatum → exclusio causae divinae → correctio opinionis et imminutio timoris.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B08","text":"Calamitas materialis → vulnerabilitas corporis → defectus remedii communis → dissolutio ordinis → distinctio terminalis inter intelligibilitatem et dominium.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B01","text":"Differentia primordiorum → effluxus → receptio congruens → sensus → opinio addita → error corrigibilis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B02","text":"Concursus sine consilio → stabilitas cosmica → vita viabilis → instituta emergentia → progressus ambivalens → mortalitas ordinum.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B03","text":"Admiratio → ignorantia causae → attributio divina → timor → causalitas naturalis restricta → pietas sine gubernatione divina.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B04","text":"Permeabilitas ambientalium corporum → contagio → dilemma curae → ruptura ritus et ordinis → clausura sine remedio aut iudicio morali.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B05","text":"Cognitio naturae → imminutio timoris → tranquillitas possibilis → persistentia mortalitatis → recusatio immunitatis et dominii totalis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-01","text":"Oppositio apparentiae et veritatis reordinatur in distinctionem inter affectionem materialiter veram et opinionem causaliter corrigibilem.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-02","text":"Ordo a consilio antecedente separatur et ut effectus stabilitatis post concursus reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-03","text":"Subiectum ex unitate clausa in nodum permeabilem circulationis corporum, imaginum et affectuum mutatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-04","text":"Oppositio pietatis et naturalismi corruit, quia tranquillitas divina per exclusionem interventus servatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-05","text":"Incertitudo causae singularis ex defectu explicationis in disciplinam pluralitatis materialiter restrictae convertitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-06","text":"Lingua, lex et potestas ex fundamentis originariis in eventus genealogicos utilitatis, conflictus et consuetudinis transferuntur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-07","text":"Progressus technicus ex incremento necessario boni in amplificatorem simultaneum commodorum, desideriorum et belli mutatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-08","text":"Cognitio naturae ab imperio practico separatur; eius effectus proprius est reordinatio timoris et interpretationis, non tutela universalis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei apparent imagines, errores sensuum, mundus et sidera, origines vitae, eventus terrestres, morbi et pestilentia tamquam obiecta explicanda.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali emissiones, concursus, viae, texturas, congruentiae, utilitates et conflictus operationes diversas in unam causalitatem materialem distribuunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen a repudiatione sensuum et placatione deorum ad correctionem opinionum, definitionem finium et interruptionem timoris transfertur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est nexus compositorum finitorum, subiectum est nodus corporeus, et tempus coniungit successionem, emergentiam irreversibilem atque dissolutionem.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo textus per reditum eiusdem grammaticae in campos maiores id quod affirmat efficit: asperam doctrinam causaliter reconstructivam in experientiam repetitionis cum variatione convertit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-01","text":"Ratio sensibus innititur, quamvis primordia et simulacra singula sensum excedant; transitus ab effectu visibili ad causam invisibilem analogia potius quam inspectione directa firmatur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-02","text":"Certitudo therapeutica rationis cum indeterminatione mechanismorum actualium tensionem servat, quia exclusio divini firmior est quam electio unius causae naturalis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-03","text":"Promissio purificationis animi a clausura pestilentiali comprimitur: cognitio timorem corrigit, sed remedium commune vel conservationem societatis non praestat.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-04","text":"Dei causaliter remoti servantur, sed eorum status ontologicus et relatio ad simulacra divina non plene ex machina materiali derivantur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-05","text":"Concursus fortuiti cum foederibus stabilibus componendi sunt; casus eventum eligit, sed limites naturae infringere non debet.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-06","text":"Veritas affectionis sensus cum distortionibus apparentibus cohaeret tantum si veritas ad eventum recipientis, non ad omne iudicium de obiecto, restringitur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT6","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-01","text":"Simulacrum mutatur a nuntio qui formam originis servat in operatorem recursivum cupiditatis, memoriae et attentionis.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-02","text":"Congruentia mutatur a principio differentiae sensibilium in regulam generationis, viabilitatis et susceptibilitatis morbidae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-03","text":"Ordo mutatur a consecutione recurrente in configurationem temporaliter stabilem, cui dissolutio iam intrinseca est.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-04","text":"Pietas mutatur ab obsequio rituali in contemplationem naturae et conservationem pacis divinae per non-interventionem.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-05","text":"Mortalitas mutatur a fine personae in principium cosmologicum et tandem in potestatem dissolvendi instituta socialia.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-06","text":"Therapia mutatur a fiducia in cognitionem causarum ad formam sobriam liberationis quae timorem tollit sine promissione dominii.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina porosa compositorum, per quam corpora fluunt, formae substituuntur, configurationes stabilizantur et partes tandem discedunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum intra hanc machinam est receptor selectivus et nodus motuum distributorum, non centrum immateriale neque unitas clausa.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est simul velocitas substitutionis formarum et successio irreversibilis statuum in quibus facultates oriuntur atque pereunt.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-04","text":"Certamen principale fit inter circuitus captationis, qui imaginem vel ignorantiam se ipsam alere cogunt, et cognitionem quae attentionem atque attributionem causaliter convertit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis est inferentia finita intra naturam: mechanismi restringuntur sine clausura absoluta, timor minuitur sine immunitate, et actio exercetur sine dominio totali.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad machinam principalem a perceptione corporea per emergentiam cosmologicam et historicam ad vulnerabilitatem pestilentialem restituendam.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est communis grammatica causalitatis materialis coniuncta cum finibus potestatum, mortalitate compositorum et correctione attributionis divinae.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent origo quarundam imaginum, causa actualis plurium phaenomenorum et exacti mechanismi quorum possibilitas analogiis tantum demonstratur.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT8","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia huius gradus pendet ex distinctione foederum stabiliter finitorum a configurationibus temporalibus, contingentibus et dissolubilibus.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat: a fluxu corporeo ad cognitionem, a concursu ad ordinem emergentem, ab ignorantia ad timorem, atque ab explicatione ad limites imperii.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B06","text":"Tensionem terminalem eliminare non licet, quia discrimen inter liberationem animi et persistentem vulnerabilitatem corporis structuram, non defectum accidentalem, constituit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae partim resistit vis performativa formae poeticae, quae doctrinae asperitatem tractabilem reddit et repetitionem causalitatis in actum persuasionis convertit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T231552192870Z_c4cf2d7a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 04–06","source_sha256":"639414bfdc2780f1bc875fcb1c5dc2cdb1c0fdb646bea8703d4f74cd27a05aa8","sha256":"f33b25e7079c50bc102f3ec760bc7ecfa2f0be0b24b8fc97ecd4e17fc0d5784d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:15:52.194435+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-01","text":"The system asks how perception, affect, world, life, history, and calamity may be explained from bodily principles, with providence and the immortality of souls excluded, so that religious fear may be removed.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-02","text":"The global thesis is that a single material causality, through varied flux, encounter, congruence, and dissolution, generates sensible operations, cosmic orders, living forms, and morbid events without supernatural rupture.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-03","text":"The argumentation joins material reconstruction, polemic against providence, a genealogy of emergent orders, and a therapy of opinions producing fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from minimal transmissions and perception to cosmogony and history, then to biological calamity capable of dissolving social order.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-05","text":"Knowledge begins from the senses, infers hidden causes from manifest effects, and manages uncertainty concerning a singular cause through a plurality of materially bounded possibilities.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-06","text":"The true movement is recursive and amplifying: ignorance of the cause generates divine attribution and fear, whereas material inquiry breaks the circuit and returns to tranquillity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-07","text":"Order is not a prior model but temporary stability among encounters, whose regularity does not prove an ordering intention.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-08","text":"The closure is catastrophic rather than triumphant: plague confirms material causality, but also shows that intelligibility provides neither certain remedy nor practical mastery.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AS-09","text":"The final closure is apophatic-therapeutic: peace of mind is sought from knowledge of limits and the removal of divine governance, though no ontological immunity is promised to human beings.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every acting thing, including the mind, is a bodily composite whose capacities arise from the movements and interactions of primordial elements.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The power of each nature is finite, and every possible effect is contained by the limits of texture, motion, and composition.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] An effect resides neither in the agent nor in the recipient taken alone, but emerges from the selective congruence of bodies, pathways, and textures.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Order is the temporary stabilization of configurations among innumerable encounters, not a final form imposed in advance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Every composite is permeable, mutable, and mortal; the same condition affects body, world, and orders constituted from bodies.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Sensible affection reports a true event of bodily relation, whereas falsity arises when opinion adds cause, identity, or end to the affection.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A12","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Hidden causes are inferred from sensible effects, but ontological unity is compatible with a plurality of particular mechanisms.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Causal ignorance transforms images, wonder, and natural events into signs of divine power, thereby generating religious fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Providential finality is excluded: use, fitness, and regularity are effects of structures and stabilities, not causes of their origin.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The subject is a permeable material nexus, not a separate principle: it acts through images, environment, bodily soul, and institutions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Capacities, uses, terms, and institutions emerge after structure through custom, utility, and experience.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] The therapy of affects revises opinions, redirects attention, and defines the limits of desire and fear, rather than repudiating senses or bodies.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Natural intelligibility is separated from practical mastery: a material cause may be known even where remedy, protection, or control is absent.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Tranquillity emerges from contemplation of nature and knowledge of causal limits, not from divine favor or the accumulation of external goods.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Calamity without a moral addressee can materially dissolve bodies, duties, rites, and social orders.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-01","text":"Bodily efflux preserves the identity of causality, but is amplified from the production of simulacra to environmental and morbid circulation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-02","text":"Congruence migrates from sensible quality to generation, biological preservation, and regional susceptibility; its function extends from perception to viability.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-03","text":"The image changes from a sensible message into an instrument of affective capture and religious attribution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-04","text":"Opinion added to affection, initially a source of perceptual error, becomes on the social scale the machine by which ignorance refers natural events to the gods.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-05","text":"The plurality of causes is amplified from astronomical caution into a general epistemic discipline, by which naturalness is defended without false singular certainty.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-06","text":"Utility, after being converted from the cause of the origin of organs into an effect of later use, passes into the genealogy of institutions and is finally inverted through military progress.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-07","text":"Mortality is amplified from the dissolution of the person to the dissolution of the world and of orders of care and rite.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-08","text":"Divinity undergoes an inversion: it is removed from providential causation, then becomes either an honorific name for wisdom or a model of non-intervening peace.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-09","text":"Continuity is transferred from the succession of perishing images to the history of irreversible states; apparent time becomes the time of the generation and loss of capacities.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RMD-10","text":"The circuit of love, in which the image feeds attention and desire, is absorbed into the circuit of religion, in which ignorance, fear, and divine attribution mutually confirm one another.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-01","text":"Mirror reflections, divisions of sound, and individual sensory transmissions remain local mechanisms; at this level they sustain only the common pattern of medium, path, and impact.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-02","text":"Particular causes of celestial motions remain locally indeterminate and are not converted into a global proposition concerning a singular cause.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX13","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-03","text":"Venereal physiology is local, but the circuit of image, opinion, and attention is amplified into a general theory of affective capture.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-04","text":"Morbid seeds and contagion are local mechanisms; the permeability of the subject and the dissolubility of social order are consequences at this level.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-05","text":"At this level, vision, cosmogony, and plague are not aggregated as separate arguments, but are joined as variations of one material ontology across diverse magnitudes.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-06","text":"Centaurs, monsters, and other fabulous exemplars vanish once their exclusionary work is done; what persists is the rule of material incompatibility, not a catalogue of fables.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-07","text":"The ontological status of gods and divine simulacra remains less determinate; the synthesis at the present level excludes only their governance causally.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:CGD-08","text":"Biological capacities, cosmic stabilities, and nexuses of the subject are absorbed into a higher structure, whose terms are emergence, permeability, and dissolution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that an incorporeal personal soul survives the dissolution of the body intact.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX1","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-02","text":"Providence and finality are excluded as causes of the senses, limbs, world, and history.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-03","text":"Universal skepticism and a tribunal of knowledge extrinsic to the senses are rejected together.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-04","text":"A composite image, rarity, or the magnitude of an event does not require a corresponding supernatural nature.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-05","text":"The distribution of calamities does not causally follow merit, innocence, or sanctity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-06","text":"The human being is neither the end of the cosmic process nor the measure of nature's magnitude and fitness.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-07","text":"The stability of compacts, species, and institutions does not imply eternity or immutability.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-08","text":"Wealth, honors, and technical progress do not constitute a necessary increase in happiness.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:GNX-09","text":"Natural knowledge contains no promise of universal remedy, protection, or control.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that every regularity implies an ordering intention or human purpose.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that apparent continuity or local stability requires a permanent and eternal substance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that ignorance of a cause supplies positive testimony of a divine governor.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that mind or body constitutes a closed boundary outside natural circulation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that future utility originally causes the form of an organ or institution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX5","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that natural explanation depends in every case upon certainty of one actual cause.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that a natural cause renders an effect benign, controllable, or manageable by a common remedy.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:DBN-08","text":"It must be false that physical order expresses moral order or assigns calamity to a deserving addressee.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:DNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-01","text":"Unavailable is a site of knowledge outside the senses, body, and nature from which the whole system may be judged without mediation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-02","text":"Unavailable is a providential guardianship that governs the world or grants human beings ontological immunity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-03","text":"Unavailable is a complete possession that satisfies desire once and for all, because relation is always mediated by images, bodies, and opinions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-04","text":"Unavailable is a cosmic or historical end that leads worlds, cities, and arts toward necessary perfection.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-05","text":"Calamity is ordered around the absence of a moral judge: it has causes and victims, but no metaphysical addressee.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSA-06","text":"Total epistemic closure and practical mastery of nature are prohibited, even if the limits of powers and kinds of causes may be known.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-01","text":"The circulation that conveys images to the senses, in the same structure amplified, diffuses environmental bodies and morbid seeds; reality is therefore constituted systemically as permeable exchange.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-02","text":"The truth of affection and the indeterminacy of the actual mechanism together produce an asymmetrical realism: the event of relation may be more certain than its singular cause.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-07","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-03","text":"When structure precedes use and order is stabilized from encounters, function becomes a retrospective effect of preservation, not an original design.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-04","text":"Erotic capture and religion recursively vary the same form: repeated representation fixes attention, increases affect, and falsely attributes the source of disturbance to an object.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-08","CL_0T6T_02:GA-12","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-05","text":"Because person, world, and institutions are composites, the social dissolution of plague is not extrinsic to ontology, but an amplification of common mortality.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-06","text":"Right piety is constituted through negation: the peace of the gods is preserved only if providential administration and causally operating anger are removed.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-07","text":"Congruence is a transversal operator that produces sensible quality, generation, preservation of species, and difference in diseases from relations, not absolute essences.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-08","text":"Art and wealth increase capacities, but do not necessarily increase tranquillity or immunity; technical mediation can also amplify desire, war, and vulnerability.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-09","text":"Action and institution have no sovereign origin: each is constituted distributively through images, bodily motions, mutual vulnerability, and custom.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-10","CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:XD-10","text":"The efficacy of knowledge is primarily modal and affective: it excludes divine causes and diminishes fear, but neither necessarily heals nor controls a natural event.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-12","CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-01","text":"Material causality is invariant across magnitude: the same syntax of differences, fluxes, pathways, and congruences operates from perception to cosmology and plague.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:GA-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-03","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-02","text":"The ontology of this whole is an ontology of open composites, in which temporary identity persists through the exchange of matter and the limits of interactions.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-03","text":"Time composes most rapid succession, irreversible emergence, and mortal dissolution; permanence is a local effect within these three operations.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-04","text":"The resulting epistemology is a finite realism: affections, signs, and the limits of powers sustain knowledge, while the actual cause sometimes remains indeterminate.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-07","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-05","text":"Practical freedom is not exemption from causality, but the capacity to interrupt circuits of attention, opinion, and fear through knowledge and material redirection.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-10","CL_0T6T_02:GA-12","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-06","text":"Political order is a contingent composite arising from vulnerability and utility, and may therefore be affected by the same material dissolution as its constituent bodies.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-05","CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-07","text":"Terrifying religion is a causal compression that turns ignorance into divine intention; natural piety dissolves this compression without necessarily abolishing the gods themselves.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-08","CL_0T6T_02:GA-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:BSD-08","text":"Plague does not negate the promise of liberation, but terminally restricts its scope: the mind can be freed from false terror, but body and society do not become immune to calamity.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-01","text":"Efflux → selective reception → affection → new disposition of the recipient constitutes the circuit of basic interactions, from which sensations, actions, and contagions vary.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-02","text":"Image of the beloved → desire → idealizing opinion → fixed attention → a greater abundance of images produces a self-feeding affective escalation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-03","text":"Ignorance → fear → divine attribution → anxious observation → renewed ignorance constitutes a religious attractor, to which wonder without a known cause relapses.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-04","text":"Innumerable encounters → stable configuration → local order → conflict of parts → dissolution → matter returned to new encounters constitutes a cosmic circuit.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-05","text":"Art → wealth → distinction → ambition → weapons → collective danger is a path of amplification by which progress in advantages is inverted into an increase of conflict.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:D14","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-06","text":"Care for the sick → proximity → contagion, but flight → abandonment → death of the sick constitutes the double tragic closure of duty.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-07","text":"Sensible effect → analogies → limits of powers → restricted natural causes → removal of divine governance constitutes an inquiring circuit that diminishes fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D6","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:RLE-08","text":"The common attractor is knowledge of finite powers: correction of perception, natural cosmogony, and explanation of calamities converge upon it by different routes.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B01","text":"Differences of primordial elements + void → varied and permeable bodily composites.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B02","text":"Permeable composite → continuous efflux + motion through a medium → exchange with other composites.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B03","text":"Exchange → selection through pathways and textures → congruence, repulsion, or penetration → determinate affection.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B04","text":"Repeated encounters → stable configurations → uses, species, cosmic orders, and institutions → dissolution where congruence fails.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B05","text":"Affection → added opinion → false identity, finality, or intention → affective capture and terrifying religion.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B06","text":"Structure arisen → repeated use → custom and utility → capacity or institution emerging without an original author.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A15","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B07","text":"Manifest effect → inference of material cause within the limits of powers → exclusion of divine cause → correction of opinion and diminution of fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A12","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B08","text":"Material calamity → bodily vulnerability → lack of common remedy → dissolution of order → terminal distinction between intelligibility and mastery.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B01","text":"Difference of primordial elements → efflux → congruent reception → sensation → added opinion → corrigible error.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B02","text":"Encounter without design → cosmic stability → viable life → emergent institutions → ambivalent progress → mortality of orders.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B03","text":"Wonder → ignorance of cause → divine attribution → fear → restricted natural causality → piety without divine governance.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:A4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B04","text":"Permeability of environmental bodies → contagion → dilemma of care → rupture of rite and order → closure without remedy or moral judgment.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:D10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D11","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:AFM-B05","text":"Knowledge of nature → diminution of fear → possible tranquillity → persistence of mortality → refusal of immunity and total mastery.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-01","text":"The opposition of appearance and truth is reordered into the distinction between materially true affection and causally corrigible opinion.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD2","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-02","text":"Order is separated from antecedent design and reclassified as an effect of stability after encounters.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-03","text":"The subject changes from a closed unity into a permeable node of the circulation of bodies, images, and affects.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-04","text":"The opposition of piety and naturalism collapses, because divine tranquillity is preserved through the exclusion of intervention.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-05","text":"Uncertainty concerning a singular cause is converted from a deficiency of explanation into a discipline of materially restricted plurality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-06","text":"Language, law, and power are transferred from original foundations into genealogical events of utility, conflict, and custom.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-07","text":"Technical progress changes from a necessary increase of good into a simultaneous amplifier of advantages, desires, and war.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:ME8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MME-08","text":"Knowledge of nature is separated from practical control; its proper effect is the reordering of fear and interpretation, not universal protection.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface appear images, errors of the senses, the world and stars, origins of life, terrestrial events, diseases, and plague as objects to be explained.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, emissions, encounters, pathways, textures, congruences, utilities, and conflicts distribute diverse operations into one material causality.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the struggle shifts from repudiation of the senses and propitiation of the gods to correction of opinions, definition of limits, and interruption of fear.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is a nexus of finite composites, the subject is a bodily node, and time joins succession, irreversible emergence, and dissolution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:ALT4","CH_0T6T_LI_2:ALT4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text accomplishes what it affirms through the return of the same grammar in larger fields: it turns a harsh causally reconstructive doctrine into an experience of repetition with variation.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:AS3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-01","text":"Reason relies upon the senses, although primordial elements and individual simulacra exceed sensation; the transition from visible effect to invisible cause is secured by analogy rather than direct inspection.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A14","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-02","text":"The therapeutic certainty of reason sustains a tension with the indeterminacy of actual mechanisms, because exclusion of the divine is firmer than selection of one natural cause.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT7","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-03","text":"The promise of purification of the mind is compressed by the pestilential closure: knowledge corrects fear, but does not provide a common remedy or preservation of society.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-04","text":"The gods are causally removed yet preserved, but their ontological status and relation to divine simulacra are not fully derived from the material mechanism.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT9","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-05","text":"Fortuitous encounters must be reconciled with stable compacts; chance selects the event, but must not violate the limits of nature.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:DNX10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:FL-06","text":"The truth of sensible affection coheres with apparent distortions only if truth is restricted to the event in the recipient, not to every judgment concerning the object.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT6","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-01","text":"The simulacrum changes from a message preserving the form of its origin into a recursive operator of desire, memory, and attention.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A4","CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-02","text":"Congruence changes from a principle of the difference of sensible things into a rule of generation, viability, and morbid susceptibility.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:A7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A13","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-03","text":"Order changes from recurrent succession into a temporally stable configuration, to which dissolution is already intrinsic.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-04","text":"Piety changes from ritual obedience into contemplation of nature and preservation of divine peace through non-intervention.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:NX7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-05","text":"Mortality changes from the end of the person into a cosmological principle and finally into a power to dissolve social institutions.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MP-06","text":"Therapy changes from confidence in knowledge of causes into a sober form of liberation that removes fear without promising mastery.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:A18","CH_0T6T_LI_3:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a porous machine of composites, through which bodies flow, forms are replaced, configurations stabilize, and parts finally depart.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SNX5","CH_0T6T_LI_2:A8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-02","text":"Within this machine, the subject is a selective receiver and node of distributed motions, not an immaterial center or closed unity.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is at once the speed of the substitution of forms and the irreversible succession of states in which capacities arise and perish.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD7","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-04","text":"The principal struggle occurs between circuits of capture, which compel image or ignorance to feed itself, and knowledge, which causally redirects attention and attribution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:SD6","CH_0T6T_LI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:HSI-05","text":"Navigation is possible as finite inference within nature: mechanisms are restricted without absolute closure, fear is diminished without immunity, and action is exercised without total mastery.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:SD3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to reconstruct the principal mechanism from bodily perception through cosmological and historical emergence to pestilential vulnerability.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT1","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT1","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the common grammar of material causality conjoined with the limits of powers, mortality of composites, and correction of divine attribution.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT3","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT2","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B03","text":"Less determinate remain the origin of certain images, the actual cause of many phenomena, and exact mechanisms whose possibility is demonstrated only through analogies.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT8","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT4","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B04","text":"The coherence of this level depends on the distinction between stably finite compacts and temporal, contingent, and dissoluble configurations.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT3","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: from bodily flux to knowledge, from encounter to emergent order, from ignorance to fear, and from explanation to the limits of mastery.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:MRT10","CH_0T6T_LI_2:MRT10","CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B06","text":"The terminal tension must not be eliminated, because the distinction between liberation of the mind and persistent bodily vulnerability constitutes a structure, not an accidental defect.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LI_3:MRT8","CH_0T6T_LI_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07","text":"The performative force of poetic form partly resists axiomatic reduction; it renders the harshness of doctrine manageable and turns the repetition of causality into an act of persuasion.","refs":["CH_0T6T_LIBQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_0T6T_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_0T6T_02:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 04–06"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · De Rerum Natura","title":"Chapters 04–06","context":"Book Four · Book Five · Book Six","original_title":"Chapters 04–06","original_context":"Liber Quartus · Liber Quintus · Liber Sextus"},"original_title":"Chapters 04–06","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"WB_0T6T","layer":"WB","title":"On the Nature of Things","order":1,"parents":["CL_0T6T_01","CL_0T6T_02"],"revision":"WB_0T6T@R002","sha256":"abd3933f5cd41beb3ef2a001c894d4b6d41927305ef5810cc4b6bbec5f904475","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo materia, perceptio, vita, historia et calamitas una causalitate naturali comprehendantur, ut terror religiosus et metus mortis sine consolatione transcendente dissolvantur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-01","CL_0T6T_02:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis realitatem constituit ut campum corporum primorum in inani, quorum motus, concursus, congruentiae et dissolutiones omnes ordines sensibiles atque sociales producunt.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-02","CL_0T6T_02:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio simul diagnostica, reconstructiva, polemica, genealogica et therapeutica fit, quia falsas attributiones detegit, causas occultas restituit, ordines sine consilio explicat atque affectus reordinat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-04","CL_0T6T_02:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-04","text":"Motus manifestus ab effectibus sensibilibus ad principia invisibilia, deinde a compositione ad personam, mundum, historiam et calamitatem biologicam amplificatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-05","CL_0T6T_02:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-05","text":"Motus verus est recursivus: explicatio ad apparentias et affectus redit, dum eadem grammatica materialis per magnitudines maiores cum variatione repetitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-06","CL_0T6T_02:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-06","text":"Directio procedit a concursibus sine consilio ad stabilitates temporarias, inde ad formas viventes, instituta, vulnerabilitatem et dissolutionem.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-07","CL_0T6T_02:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-07","text":"Clausura duplicatur: mors personae subiectum mali posthumi aufert, pestilentia autem ostendit cognitionem naturalem neque remedium certum neque conservationem ordinis praestare.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-08","CL_0T6T_02:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-08","text":"Liberatio terminalis immunitatem non promittit; removet interpretationem providentialem et timorem falsum, sed persistentem vulnerabilitatem corporis et societatis servat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:AS-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-09","text":"Forma poetica transitum a doctrina difficili ad conversionem affectivam efficit, quia repetitionem causalitatis in actum persuasionis recursivum mutat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnis operatio naturalis, mens et sensus inclusis, in corporibus primis, inani atque motibus corporeis fundatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Materia neque producitur ex nihilo neque annihilatur, sed composita quae format permeabilia, mutabilia et mortalia sunt.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Potentiae naturales finitae sunt, quia texturae, semina et compatibilitates determinant quid effici, recipi aut conservari possit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Configuratio principiorum a causa diversitatis et qualitatum in regulam identitatis, congruentiae et actionis relationalis amplificatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","CL_0T6T_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Ordo est stabilizatio temporaria inter concursus innumeros, non exemplar antecedens aut dispositio providentialis.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-07","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Res, mundi, personae et instituta diversae magnitudinis sunt compositiones locales eiusdem grammaticae ontologicae.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:XD-01","CL_0T6T_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Sensus est fundamentum cognitionis et eventus corporeus verus, dum falsitas ex opinione identitatem, causam aut finem affectioni addente nascitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Causae invisibiles ex effectibus sensibilibus inferuntur, quamquam unitas ontologiae pluralitatem mechanismorum particularium admittit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:XD-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Dei neque naturam administrant neque usus, aptitudines aut regularitates rerum originarie disponunt.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-10","CL_0T6T_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Ignorantia causalitatis admirationem in attributionem divinam vertit, ratio autem terrorem dissolvit causas intra naturam restituendo.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Persona ex ordinatione temporaria corporis, animi et animae in nodum materialem permeabilem imaginibus, ambiente et institutis mutatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Facultates, functiones et instituta structuras non antecedunt, sed ex usu, consuetudine et conservatione retrospectiva emergunt.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-13","text":"[Mutating] Immanentia materialis seriem fataliter clausam interrumpit et ignorantiam causae actualis per possibilitates naturaliter finitas administrat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-08","CL_0T6T_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Mors personam dissolvit, dum calamitas corpora viventia et ordines sociales sine destinatario morali destruere potest.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Intelligibilitas naturae a dominio practico separatur: cognitio timorem corrigit, sed immunitatem ontologicam non confert.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","CL_0T6T_02:AS-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-16","text":"[Attractor] Sufficientia vitae finitae et tranquillitas ex terminatione cupiditatis atque cognitione finium, non ex favore divino, oriuntur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-14","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-01","text":"Concilium identitatem functionalem servat, sed a re genita ad personam, mundum et configurationem temporaliter stabilem amplificatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-02","text":"Qualitas a dependentia corporum in operatorum congruentiam migrat, qui perceptionem, generationem, conservationem et susceptibilitatem morbidam regit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-03","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-03","text":"Sensus a norma epistemica in eventum relationis corporeae mutatur, cui opinio corrigibilis, non ipsa affectio, falsitatem imponit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-04","text":"Imago a nuntio sensibili in instrumentum dispositionis, attentionis et captationis affectivae transit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-08","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-05","text":"Divinitas a causa naturae removetur, sed nomina divina in figuras affectuum aut exempla pacis non intervenientis transferuntur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-07","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-06","text":"Mortalitas ex dissolutione personae in legem mundorum et demum in facultatem dissolvendi ordines curae, ritus et societatis amplificatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-06","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-07","text":"Utilitas ex falsa causa originis in effectum usus et genealogiam institutionum mutatur, deinde per arma ac vulnerabilitatem socialem invertitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:RMD-06","CL_0T6T_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-08","text":"Apertura causalis primo necessitatem absolutam per clinamen limitat, deinde in disciplinam epistemicam pluralitatis causarum migrat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-09","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-09","text":"Vis therapeutica veritatis persistit, sed a fiducia in explicationem ad liberationem sobriam sine promissione dominii mutatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-04","CL_0T6T_02:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-10","text":"Forma poetica rationem non ornat tantum, sed repetitionem cum variatione in mechanismum transmissionis et conversionis affectivae absorbet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-06","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-01","text":"Probationes particulares inanis, minimorum et motuum inferiori gradu manent; hic tantum condiciones compositionis, transmissionis et dissolutionis praebent.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:CGD-01","CL_0T6T_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-02","text":"Clinamen et figurae atomicorum localiter motum distinguunt, sed hoc gradu in theoriam differentiae emergentis et agentiae finitae amplificantur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:CGD-02","CL_0T6T_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-03","text":"Anatomia animi et semina morbida machinae locales sunt; definitio personae ut organizationis permeabilis atque dissolubilis ad gradum praesentem pertinet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-04","text":"Causae particulares motuum caelestium indeterminatae manent, sed earum pluralitas in disciplinam generalem explicationis naturalis convertitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:CGD-02","CL_0T6T_02:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-05","text":"Catalogi fabularum et refutationes exemplorum evanescunt opere exclusorio peracto; persistunt fines compatibilitatis et formarum admissibilium.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:CGD-07","CL_0T6T_02:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-06","text":"Contagio inferiori gradu mechanismum morbi explicat; dissolutio institutionum et mortalitas ordinis socialis sunt effectus totius libri.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:CGD-04","CL_0T6T_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-07","text":"Status materialis deorum non plene restituitur; praesentis gradus structura eorum gubernationem tantum excludit atque immortalitatem cum lege compositorum in tensione relinquit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:CGD-07","CL_0T6T_01:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-08","text":"Ars poetica localiter doctrinam tractabilem reddit, sed hoc gradu fit modus performativus quo causalitas repetita persuasionem producit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-09","text":"Transitus a cosmologia ad anthropologiam et a perceptione ad pestilentiam in unam ontologiam materialem magnitudine variantem coniungitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:CGD-05","CL_0T6T_02:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-01","text":"Systema neque creationem absolutam neque annihilationem neque animam incorpoream dissolutioni corporis superstitem admittit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-01","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-02","text":"Providentia, finalitas et administratio mundi ad usum humanum tamquam causae originis excluduntur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-03","text":"Principia ultima qualitates sensibiles non continent, sicut futura utilitas formam organi vel institutionis originarie causare non potest.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-05","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-04","text":"Apparentia sensibilis inventarium completum realitatis non est, sed nullum tribunal cognitionis sensibus et naturae extrinsecum conceditur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-02","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-05","text":"Mundus, locus et homo privilegium cosmicum non possident neque finis processus naturalis constituuntur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-04","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-06","text":"Opes, honores, mutatio loci et progressus technicus causam perturbationis non tollunt neque felicitatem necessario augent.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-06","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-07","text":"Distributio calamitatum neque meritum sequitur neque ordinem physicum in iudicium morale convertit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GNX-05","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-08","text":"Explicatio naturalis promissionem remedii, tutelae, immunitatis aut dominii universalis non continet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GNX-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-09","text":"Inferi posthumi et mors tamquam experientia personae excluduntur, quia dissolutio ipsum campum experientiae aufert.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-07","CL_0T6T_01:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-01","text":"Ignorantia causae testimonium numinis esse non potest; aliter ipsa absentia explicationis in probationem gubernationis converteretur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-01","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-02","text":"Regularitas neque intentionem ordinatricem neque necessitatem causaliter clausam probare potest.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-03","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-03","text":"Identitas et stabilitas localis substantiam permanentem non requirunt, sed ordinationem selectivam et continuitatem operationum.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-04","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-04","text":"Naturalitas explicationis a certitudine unius causae actualis separanda est, quia eventus relationis certior quam mechanismus singularis esse potest.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:DBN-06","CL_0T6T_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-05","text":"Securitas per fugam, accumulationem aut possessionem plenam acquiri non potest, quia cupiditas et perturbatio mediatione recursiva renovantur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-06","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-06","text":"Causa naturalis effectum benignum, coercibilem aut remedio tractabilem facere non debet; aliter cognitio in promissionem immunitatis mutaretur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-07","text":"Ordo physicus iudicium morale exprimere non potest, quia calamitas causas et victimas habet sine destinatario metaphysico.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:DBN-08","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-08","text":"Vis poetica invocationis divinae testimonium interventionis esse non potest, quamquam status deorum extra machinam materialem imperfecte determinatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-05","CL_0T6T_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-01","text":"Indisponibilis est omnis exterior: nullus creator, receptaculum aut locus cognitionis extra naturam totum campum ordinat vel iudicat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-01","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis est destinatarius privilegiatus cui mundus referatur aut cui providentia immunitatem ontologicam concedat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-04","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-03","text":"Indisponibilis est finis universalis cosmicus vel historicus; permanent redistributiones et successiones sine perfectione necessaria.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-03","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-04","text":"Indisponibilis est prospectus personalis qui dissolutionem suam superet atque propriam non-exsistentiam observet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-02","CL_0T6T_01:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-05","text":"Indisponibiles sunt clausura epistemica totalis et dominium practicum, etiam ubi causalitas materialis firmiter constituitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:BSA-06","CL_0T6T_02:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-06","text":"Totum inter duas absentias terminales ordinatur: mors patientem posthumum non habet, pestilentia iudicem moralem non habet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-05","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-01","text":"Cum eadem grammatica res, mundos et personas constituat atque composita porosa sint, identitas fit stabilitas commutationis terminatae, non possessio materiae immutabilis.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-02","WB_0T6T:GA-06","WB_0T6T:GA-11","CL_0T6T_01:XD-01","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-02","text":"Conservatio materiae consolationem personalem non praebet, quia eadem redistributio quae materiam servat personas, mundos et ordines irreversibiliter dissolvit.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-02","WB_0T6T:GA-14","CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-03","text":"Clinamen et pluralitas causarum efficiunt immanentiam simul ontologice apertam et epistemice finitam, sine recursu ad voluntatem supernaturalem.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-08","WB_0T6T:GA-13","CL_0T6T_01:XD-03","CL_0T6T_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-04","text":"Affectus est eventus materialis verus, sed captatio nascitur cum opinio fontem commotionis falso obiecto tribuit atque attentionem recursive figit.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-07","WB_0T6T:GA-10","WB_0T6T:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-05","text":"Cum ordo post concursus stabilitur et functio structuram sequitur, apparentia consilii fit memoria retrospectiva conservationis, non causa originis.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-05","WB_0T6T:GA-12","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CL_0T6T_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-06","text":"Pestilentia ontologiae non opponitur, sed legem mortalitatis compositorum a corpore ad officia, ritus et societatem amplificat.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-06","WB_0T6T:GA-14","CL_0T6T_01:BSD-01","CL_0T6T_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-07","text":"Distinctio inter mortem et calamitatem ethicam therapiam terminat: non-experientia posthuma timorem falsum aufert, sed vulnerabilitas praesentis corporis malum reale relinquit.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-14","WB_0T6T:GA-15","CL_0T6T_01:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-08","text":"Religio terrifica ex actione deorum non oritur, sed ex imaginibus et nominibus divinis quae attentionem, timorem et attributionem causaliter errantem stabilizant.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-09","WB_0T6T:GA-10","WB_0T6T:GA-11","CL_0T6T_01:RMD-07","CL_0T6T_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-09","text":"Efficacia cognitionis est imprimis discriminativa: causas naturales restringit, attributionem divinam excludit et affectum reordinat, quamquam eventum neque praedicit perfecte neque coercet.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-08","WB_0T6T:GA-15","CL_0T6T_01:XD-06","CL_0T6T_02:XD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-10","text":"Finitae potentiae naturae fundamentum ethicum fiunt, quia sufficientia non ex possessione infinita sed ex cognitione terminorum desiderii et actionis provenit.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-03","WB_0T6T:GA-16","CL_0T6T_01:BSD-06","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-11","text":"Repetitio poetica structuram ipsius naturae imitatur: eadem grammatica per campos varios transit, differentias servat et conversionem affectivam per iterationem producit.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-08","WB_0T6T:GA-10","CL_0T6T_01:MME-06","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-01","text":"Realitas est hierarchia sine centro stabilizationum inclusarum, sed syntaxis differentiae, fluxus, congruentiae et dissolutionis invarians magnitudine manet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-01","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-02","text":"Tempus distribuitur inter motum aeternum principiorum, durationem finitam compositorum, successionem irreversibilem historiae et absentiam temporis personaliter experti post mortem.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-02","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum est nodus corporeus temporarius et permeabilis, cuius identitas ex limite commutationum, memoria et ordinatione sensifera, non ex clausura substantiali, provenit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-02","CL_0T6T_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-04","text":"Epistemologia totius est realismus asymmetricalis: genera causarum et fines potestatum cognosci possunt, dum mechanismus actualis singularis interdum indeterminatus manet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:XD-02","CL_0T6T_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-05","text":"Potestas politica et instituta non habent originem sovereignam, sed sunt amplificationes sociales metus, utilitatis, consuetudinis et vulnerabilitatis compositorum.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-05","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-06","text":"Libertas neque exemptio a natura neque necessitas mera est, sed facultas localis circuitus attentionis, opinionis et timoris intra causalitatem materialem interrumpendi.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-03","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-07","text":"Ethica totius distinguit inter malum imaginarium mortis posthumae et mala praesentia corporis vulnerabilis; primum cognitione tollitur, altera ontologice permanent.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-08","text":"Clausura pestilentialis doctrinam materialem confirmat recusando iudicium providentiale, sed simul promissionem liberationis ad correctionem timoris restringit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:AS-08","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-09","text":"Methodus totius est performativa: doctrina causalitatis non tantum exponitur, sed per formam poeticam, recursum et variationem in habitum interpretandi convertitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-01","text":"Ignorantia causae → attributio intentionis → terror → observatio anxia → renovata ignorantia constituit circuitum religiosum auto-confirmantem.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-03","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-02","text":"Effectus sensibilis → causa invisibilis restricta → apparentia reclassificata → novum indicium constituit circuitum inquisitivum qui cognitionem cumulativam generat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:XD-06","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-03","text":"Compositio → stabilizatio → proprietas emergens → dissolutio → materia novis concursibus reddita constituit circuitum conservationis sine identitate formarum.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-04","text":"Imago → attentio → cupiditas vel timor → opinio idealizans → plures imagines constituit circuitum communem captationis eroticae et religiosae.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:RMD-10","CL_0T6T_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-05","text":"Structura utilis → consuetudo → institutum → opes → ambitio → arma → vulnerabilitas collectivam escalationem progressus ambivalentis producit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-06","text":"Metus mortis → ambitio → potentia → violentia → productio mortis → metus amplificatus est via catastrophica compensationis.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-13","CL_0T6T_01:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-07","text":"Ratio naturae → cognitio potentiarum finitarum → remotio causae divinae → imminutio timoris → attentio renovata ad naturam constituit attractorem therapeuticum.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-08","text":"Cura → propinquitas → contagio et fuga → desertio → mors efficiunt duplicem clausuram tragicam in qua officium nullo itinere immunitatem praestat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:RLE-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-09","text":"Attractor terminalis est finitas potentiarum: inquisitio mechanismos restringit, actio intra naturam manet et timor sine dominio totali minuitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-02","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B01","text":"Ignorantia causalitatis → falsa aestimatio → attributio divina → terror religiosus et mortis → quaestio de natura.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B02","text":"Corpora prima + inane + motus → campus ontologicus sufficiens omnibus rebus operantibus sine tertia natura.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B03","text":"Pondus + plagae + clinamen + potentiae finitae → causalitas immanens neque providentialis neque fataliter exhausta.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-08","CL_0T6T_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B04","text":"Semina certa + differentiae texturarum + congruentia selectiva → generationes finitae et commercia determinata.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B05","text":"Concursus innumeri + configuratio → stabilitas temporaria → qualitates, vita, mundi et identitates emergentes.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B06","text":"Compositum permeabile + effluxus + receptio selectiva → sensus, memoria, actio et vulnerabilitas ambientaliter mediata.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B07","text":"Affectio + opinio addita + attentio repetita → falsa identitas causae → cupiditas, religio et circuitus compensatorii.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:XD-05","CL_0T6T_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B08","text":"Structura nata + usus + consuetudo + conflictus → facultates, vocabula, leges et instituta sine auctore originario.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B09","text":"Dissolutio configurationis → mors personae / mundi / institutionis → materia conservata + forma irreversibiliter amissa.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","CL_0T6T_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B10","text":"Reconstructio causalis → exclusio providentiae → correctio opinionis → imminutio timoris, sed non remedium universale aut dominium naturae.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:AFM-B01","text":"Effectus sensibilis → principia corporea invisibilia → motus et concursus → configuratio selectiva → proprietates emergentes → composita permeabilia → dissolutio per omnes magnitudines.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-05","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorantia causae → religio et metus mortis → inquisitio naturalis → remotio providentiae → mortalitas personae → correctio affectus → tranquillitas finita.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:XD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:AFM-B03","text":"Stabilitas sine consilio → vita et utilitas → consuetudo atque instituta → opes et arma → vulnerabilitas communis → pestilentia → distinctio terminalis inter intelligibilitatem et dominium.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:RMD-06","CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-01","text":"Oppositio inter visibile et reale reordinatur: invisibilitas causae methodum inferentialem requirit, non exclusionem a cognitione.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-01","CL_0T6T_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-02","text":"Qualitates, sensus et identitates ex proprietatibus simplicibus substantiarum in effectus relationum, texturarum et configurationum transferuntur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-02","CL_0T6T_02:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-03","text":"Ordo et functio a consilio antecedente separantur atque in stabilitatem post concursus et conservationem retrospectivam reclassificantur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MME-02","CL_0T6T_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-04","text":"Subiectum ex centro immateriali in nodum permeabilem circulationis corporum, imaginum, affectuum et institutionum mutatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-02","CL_0T6T_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-05","text":"Oppositio inter providentiam et fatum corruit, quia immanentia materialis contingentiam limitatam et pluralitatem causaliter restrictam continet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-04","CL_0T6T_02:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-06","text":"Progressus technicus ex incremento necessario boni in amplificatorem commodorum, desideriorum, belli et vulnerabilitatis convertitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:XD-08","CL_0T6T_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-07","text":"Cognitio naturae ab imperio separatur, quia eius effectus proprius est reordinatio interpretationis et timoris, non tutela universalis.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MME-08","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-08","text":"Mors et calamitas in eadem ontologia sed non eadem ethica locantur: mors subiectum tollit, calamitas subiecta viventia vulnerat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-09","text":"Pietas et naturalismus non iam opponuntur, quia pax divina per exclusionem gubernationis et interventionis causaliter servatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:XD-06","CL_0T6T_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei principia rerum, sensus, metus, imagines, mundi, origines vitae, historia, morbi et pestilentia ut obiecta continuae explicationis apparent.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B01","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali corpora, inane, motus, effluxus, texturas, congruentiae et dissolutiones diversas operationes in unam causalitatem materialem distribuunt.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B02","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen contra deos, fatum et mortem in correctionem opinionum, definitionem finium et interruptionem circuituum affectivorum convertitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B03","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali materia manet, composita durant et pereunt, subiecta nodi finiti sunt, historia autem status irreversibiles accumulat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B04","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo carmen, analogia et probatio cumulativa eandem grammaticam per variationes repetunt atque affectum in cognitionem causaliter ordinatam traducunt.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B05","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-01","text":"Sensus fundamentum probationis manet, quamquam causae ultimae inspectionem directam excedunt et transitus ad eas analogia firmatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-01","CL_0T6T_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-02","text":"Leges compatibilitatis stabilitatem generant, sed clinamen et casus eventum aperiunt; mensura inter necessitatem et contingentiam incompleta manet.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-02","CL_0T6T_02:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-03","text":"Sensus ex principiis insensilibus emergere debet, sed transitus a figura et motu ad veritatem affectionis mechanice non absolvitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-03","CL_0T6T_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-04","text":"Immortalitas deorum cum lege dissolutionis compositorum non plene cohaeret, quia dei remoti in machina materiali imperfecte derivantur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-04","CL_0T6T_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-05","text":"Invocatio poetica Veneris vim agendi retinet, dum pietas doctrinalis pacem divinam per non-interventionem conservat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-05","CL_0T6T_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-06","text":"Exclusio causae divinae firmior est quam electio mechanismi naturalis singularis, unde certitudo therapeutica indeterminationem explicativam superat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:FL-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-07","text":"Promissio purificationis animi cum persistentia pestilentiali comprimitur: cognitio timorem corrigit, sed conservationem corporis aut societatis non praestat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:FL-03","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-08","text":"Conservatio principiorum cum irreversibilitate formarum componitur, sed haec distinctio materiam aeternam ab historia amissionum acute dividit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-02","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-01","text":"Qualitas ex accidente dependenti in congruentiam relationalem mutatur, quae generationem, viabilitatem et susceptibilitatem ordinat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MP-01","CL_0T6T_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-02","text":"Identitas ex continuitate organizationis in nodum permeabilem commutationum corporearum et socialium amplificatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MP-04","CL_0T6T_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-03","text":"Simulacrum a nuntio formae in operatorem recursivum cupiditatis, memoriae, attentionis et religionis mutatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MP-01","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-04","text":"Ordo ex consecutione recurrente in configurationem temporaliter stabilem mutatur, cui dissolutio iam intrinseca est.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MP-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-05","text":"Mortalitas ab eventu logico qui subiectum nocendi tollit in potestatem cosmologicam et socialem dissolvendi ordines amplificatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MP-03","CL_0T6T_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-06","text":"Pietas ab obsequio rituali in conservationem pacis divinae per exclusionem interventionis mutatur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MP-04","CL_0T6T_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-07","text":"Therapia a fiducia in explicationem plenam ad liberationem finitam mutatur, quae timorem tollit sine promissione dominii.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-04","CL_0T6T_02:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est campus infinitus et porosus corporum mobilium, qui nodos locales format, per eos fluit et eos tandem dissolvit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-01","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus corporeus temporarius, simul receptor selectivus et distributio motuum, non centrum immateriale aut clausum.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-02","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est permanentia principiorum sub successione irreversibili formarum; composita durationem habent, persona autem propriam absentiam non experitur.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-03","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-04","text":"Certamen principale opponit circuitus captationis, qui imaginem et ignorantiam se ipsas alunt, cognitioni quae causas, attentionem et attributionem convertit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-04","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis est motus intra naturam sine exteriori, quo timor minuitur sine immunitate et actio exercetur sine dominio totali.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:HSI-05","CL_0T6T_01:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam a materia conservata et motu per perceptionem, emergentiam et historiam ad mortalitatem personalem atque pestilentiam.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B01","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est coniunctio conservationis materiae, causalitatis invarianti magnitudine, mortalitatis compositorum et correctionis attributionis divinae.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B02","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinati manent nexus a clinamine ad voluntatem, a principiis insensilibus ad sensum et a possibilitate analogica ad mechanismum actualem.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B03","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret quia motum ab affectu ad physicam, a concursu ad ordinem, ab ignorantia ad timorem et ab explicatione ad limites imperii servat.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B04","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B05","text":"Cohaerentia pendet ex distinctione principiorum permanentium a configurationibus temporalibus atque ex analogiis quae hiatus inter magnitudines transponunt.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B05","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B06","text":"Tensionem inter liberationem animi et vulnerabilitatem corporis eliminare non licet, quia ipsa extensionem therapiam a promissione metaphysica distinguit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:AS-08","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B07","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistit vis performativa carminis, quae doctrinam obscuram per repetitionem causalitatis in conversionem affectivam traducit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T231657432230Z_a8a05438","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Nature of Things","source_sha256":"abd3933f5cd41beb3ef2a001c894d4b6d41927305ef5810cc4b6bbec5f904475","sha256":"213fb9c9f45d1188fa38d82d7081ceb2733be8a6ed7e71b0158328d0a3ebed32","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T23:16:57.435233+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how matter, perception, life, history, and calamity may be comprehended within a single natural causality, so that religious terror and fear of death may be dissolved without transcendent consolation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-01","CL_0T6T_02:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis constitutes reality as a field of primary bodies in the void, whose motions, collisions, congruities, and dissolutions produce all sensible and social orders.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-02","CL_0T6T_02:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-03","text":"The argument becomes simultaneously diagnostic, reconstructive, polemical, genealogical, and therapeutic, because it detects false attributions, restores hidden causes, explains orders without design, and reorders affects.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-04","CL_0T6T_02:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-04","text":"The manifest movement extends from sensible effects to invisible principles, then from composition to the person, the world, history, and biological calamity.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-05","CL_0T6T_02:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-05","text":"The real movement is recursive: explanation returns to appearances and affects, while the same material grammar is repeated with variation across larger scales.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-06","CL_0T6T_02:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-06","text":"The direction proceeds from undesigned collisions to temporary stabilities, thence to living forms, institutions, vulnerability, and dissolution.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-07","CL_0T6T_02:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-07","text":"The closure is doubled: the death of the person removes the subject of posthumous evil, whereas plague shows that natural knowledge provides neither a certain remedy nor preservation of order.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-08","CL_0T6T_02:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-08","text":"Terminal liberation does not promise immunity; it removes providential interpretation and false fear, but retains the persistent vulnerability of body and society.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:AS-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BAS-09","text":"Poetic form effects the passage from difficult doctrine to affective conversion, because it turns the repetition of causality into a recursive act of persuasion.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every natural operation, including mind and sensation, is founded on primary bodies, void, and bodily motions.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Matter is neither produced from nothing nor annihilated, but composes entities whose forms are permeable, mutable, and mortal.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Natural powers are finite, because textures, seeds, and compatibilities determine what can be effected, received, or preserved.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The configuration of principles is extended from a cause of diversity and qualities into a rule of identity, congruity, and relational action.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","CL_0T6T_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Order is a temporary stabilization among innumerable collisions, not an antecedent model or providential arrangement.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-07","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Things, worlds, persons, and institutions of diverse scales are local compositions of the same ontological grammar.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:XD-01","CL_0T6T_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Sensation is the foundation of knowledge and a true bodily event, while falsity arises from opinion adding identity, cause, or end to an affection.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Invisible causes are inferred from sensible effects, although the unity of ontology admits a plurality of particular mechanisms.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:XD-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] The gods neither govern nature nor originally arrange the uses, capacities, or regularities of things.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-10","CL_0T6T_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Ignorance of causality turns wonder into divine attribution, whereas reason dissolves terror by restoring causes within nature.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The person is transformed from a temporary ordering of body, mind, and soul into a material node permeable to images, environment, and institutions.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Faculties, functions, and institutions do not precede structures, but emerge from use, custom, and retrospective preservation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-13","text":"[Mutating] Material immanence interrupts a fatally closed series and manages ignorance of the actual cause through naturally finite possibilities.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-08","CL_0T6T_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Death dissolves the person, while calamity can destroy living bodies and social orders without a moral addressee.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] The intelligibility of nature is separated from practical mastery: knowledge corrects fear, but does not confer ontological immunity.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-13","CL_0T6T_02:AS-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GA-16","text":"[Attractor] The sufficiency of finite life and tranquillity arise from the termination of desire and knowledge of limits, not from divine favor.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-14","CL_0T6T_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-01","text":"Design retains functional identity, but is extended from a generated thing to the person, the world, and a temporally stable configuration.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-02","text":"Quality migrates from the dependence of bodies into the congruity of operators, which governs perception, generation, preservation, and susceptibility to disease.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-03","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-03","text":"Sensation changes from an epistemic norm into an event of bodily relation, upon which corrigible opinion, not the affection itself, imposes falsity.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-04","text":"The image passes from a sensible message into an instrument of disposition, attention, and affective captivation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-08","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-05","text":"Divinity is removed as a cause of nature, but divine names are transferred into figures of affects or examples of non-intervening peace.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-07","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-06","text":"Mortality is extended from the dissolution of the person into a law of worlds and finally into a capacity to dissolve the orders of care, ritual, and society.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-06","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-07","text":"Utility changes from a false cause of origin into an effect of use and a genealogy of institutions, then is inverted through arms and social vulnerability.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:RMD-06","CL_0T6T_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-08","text":"Causal openness first limits absolute necessity through clinamen, then migrates into an epistemic discipline of a plurality of causes.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-09","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-09","text":"The therapeutic force of truth persists, but changes from confidence in explanation to sober liberation without a promise of mastery.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-04","CL_0T6T_02:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RMD-10","text":"Poetic form does not merely adorn reason, but absorbs repetition with variation into a mechanism of transmission and affective conversion.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-06","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-01","text":"Particular proofs of void, minima, and motions remain at the lower level; here they provide only conditions of composition, transmission, and dissolution.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:CGD-01","CL_0T6T_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-02","text":"Clinamen and the figures of atoms locally distinguish motion, but at this level are extended into a theory of emergent difference and finite agency.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:CGD-02","CL_0T6T_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-03","text":"The anatomy of mind and seeds of disease are local mechanisms; the definition of the person as permeable and dissoluble organization belongs to the present level.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-04","text":"The particular causes of celestial motions remain indeterminate, but their plurality is converted into a general discipline of natural explanation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:CGD-02","CL_0T6T_02:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-05","text":"Catalogues of fables and refutations of examples vanish once their exclusionary work has been completed; limits of compatibility and admissible forms persist.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:CGD-07","CL_0T6T_02:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-06","text":"Contagion explains the mechanism of disease at the lower level; the dissolution of institutions and mortality of social order are effects of the whole book.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:CGD-04","CL_0T6T_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-07","text":"The material status of the gods is not fully restored; the structure of the present level only excludes their governance and leaves immortality in tension with the law of composites.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:CGD-07","CL_0T6T_01:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-08","text":"Poetic art locally renders doctrine tractable, but at this level becomes a performative mode by which repeated causality produces persuasion.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:CGD-09","text":"The passage from cosmology to anthropology and from perception to plague is joined in a single material ontology varying by scale.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:CGD-05","CL_0T6T_02:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-01","text":"The system admits neither absolute creation nor annihilation nor an incorporeal soul surviving the dissolution of the body.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-01","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-02","text":"Providence, finality, and governance of the world for human use are excluded as causes of origin.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-03","text":"Ultimate principles do not contain sensible qualities, just as future utility cannot originally cause the form of an organ or institution.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-05","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-04","text":"Sensible appearance is not a complete inventory of reality, but no tribunal of knowledge extrinsic to the senses and nature is granted.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-02","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-05","text":"The world, place, and human being possess no cosmic privilege, nor are they constituted as the end of natural process.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-04","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-06","text":"Wealth, honors, change of place, and technical progress neither remove the cause of disturbance nor necessarily increase happiness.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-06","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-07","text":"The distribution of calamities neither follows merit nor converts physical order into moral judgment.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GNX-05","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-08","text":"Natural explanation contains no promise of remedy, protection, immunity, or universal mastery.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GNX-09","CL_0T6T_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:GNX-09","text":"Posthumous underworlds and death as an experience of the person are excluded, because dissolution removes the very field of experience.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GNX-07","CL_0T6T_01:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-01","text":"Ignorance of a cause cannot be evidence of divinity; otherwise the very absence of explanation would be converted into proof of governance.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-01","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-02","text":"Regularity can prove neither ordering intention nor causally closed necessity.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-03","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-03","text":"Local identity and stability do not require a permanent substance, but selective ordering and continuity of operations.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-04","CL_0T6T_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-04","text":"The naturalness of explanation must be separated from certainty about a single actual cause, because an event of relation can be more certain than a singular mechanism.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:DBN-06","CL_0T6T_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-05","text":"Security cannot be acquired through flight, accumulation, or full possession, because desire and disturbance are renewed through recursive mediation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-06","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-06","text":"A natural cause need not make its effect benign, controllable, or treatable by remedy; otherwise knowledge would be changed into a promise of immunity.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-07","text":"Physical order cannot express moral judgment, because calamity has causes and victims without a metaphysical addressee.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:DBN-08","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:DBN-08","text":"The poetic force of divine invocation cannot be evidence of intervention, although the status of the gods outside the material machine is imperfectly determined.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-05","CL_0T6T_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-01","text":"Every exterior is unavailable: no creator, receptacle, or site of knowledge outside nature orders or judges the whole field.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-01","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-02","text":"Unavailable is a privileged addressee to whom the world is referred or to whom providence grants ontological immunity.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-04","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-03","text":"Unavailable is a universal cosmic or historical end; redistributions and successions persist without necessary perfection.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-03","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-04","text":"Unavailable is a personal perspective that surpasses its own dissolution and observes its own non-existence.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-02","CL_0T6T_01:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-05","text":"Total epistemic closure and practical mastery are unavailable, even where material causality is firmly established.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:BSA-06","CL_0T6T_02:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSA-06","text":"The whole is ordered between two terminal absences: death has no posthumous sufferer; plague has no moral judge.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSA-05","CL_0T6T_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-01","text":"Since the same grammar constitutes things, worlds, and persons, and composites are porous, identity becomes the stability of bounded exchange, not possession of immutable matter.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-02","WB_0T6T:GA-06","WB_0T6T:GA-11","CL_0T6T_01:XD-01","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-02","text":"The conservation of matter provides no personal consolation, because the same redistribution that preserves matter irreversibly dissolves persons, worlds, and orders.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-02","WB_0T6T:GA-14","CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-03","text":"Clinamen and the plurality of causes make immanence simultaneously ontologically open and epistemically finite, without recourse to supernatural will.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-08","WB_0T6T:GA-13","CL_0T6T_01:XD-03","CL_0T6T_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-04","text":"Affect is a true material event, but captivation arises when opinion falsely attributes the source of disturbance to an object and recursively fixes attention.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-07","WB_0T6T:GA-10","WB_0T6T:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-05","text":"When order stabilizes after collisions and function follows structure, the appearance of design becomes a retrospective memory of preservation, not a cause of origin.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-05","WB_0T6T:GA-12","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04","CL_0T6T_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-06","text":"Plague does not oppose ontology, but extends the law of mortality of composites from the body to offices, rites, and society.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-06","WB_0T6T:GA-14","CL_0T6T_01:BSD-01","CL_0T6T_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-07","text":"The distinction between death and calamity terminates ethical therapy: posthumous non-experience removes false fear, but the vulnerability of the present body leaves real evil.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-14","WB_0T6T:GA-15","CL_0T6T_01:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-08","text":"Terrifying religion does not arise from the action of gods, but from divine images and names that stabilize attention, fear, and causally erroneous attribution.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-09","WB_0T6T:GA-10","WB_0T6T:GA-11","CL_0T6T_01:RMD-07","CL_0T6T_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-09","text":"The efficacy of knowledge is primarily discriminative: it restricts natural causes, excludes divine attribution, and reorders affect, although it neither perfectly predicts nor controls the event.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-08","WB_0T6T:GA-15","CL_0T6T_01:XD-06","CL_0T6T_02:XD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-10","text":"The finite powers of nature become an ethical foundation, because sufficiency arises not from infinite possession but from knowledge of the limits of desire and action.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-03","WB_0T6T:GA-16","CL_0T6T_01:BSD-06","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:XD-11","text":"Poetic repetition imitates the structure of nature itself: the same grammar passes through diverse fields, preserves differences, and produces affective conversion through iteration.","refs":["WB_0T6T:GA-08","WB_0T6T:GA-10","CL_0T6T_01:MME-06","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-01","text":"Reality is a centerless hierarchy of nested stabilizations, but the syntax of difference, flux, congruity, and dissolution remains invariant across scale.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-01","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-02","text":"Time is distributed among the eternal motion of principles, the finite duration of composites, the irreversible succession of history, and the absence of personally experienced time after death.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-02","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-03","text":"The subject is a temporary and permeable bodily node, whose identity arises from the limit of exchanges, memory, and sensory ordering, not from substantial closure.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-02","CL_0T6T_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-04","text":"The epistemology of the whole is asymmetrical realism: kinds of causes and limits of powers can be known, while the singular actual mechanism sometimes remains indeterminate.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:XD-02","CL_0T6T_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-05","text":"Political power and institutions have no sovereign origin, but are social amplifications of fear, utility, custom, and the vulnerability of composites.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-05","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-06","text":"Freedom is neither exemption from nature nor mere necessity, but a local capacity to interrupt circuits of attention, opinion, and fear within material causality.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-03","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-07","text":"The ethics of the whole distinguishes between the imaginary evil of posthumous death and the present evils of the vulnerable body; the former is removed by knowledge, the latter persist ontologically.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-08","text":"The pestilential closure confirms material doctrine by rejecting providential judgment, but simultaneously restricts the promise of liberation to the correction of fear.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:AS-08","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:BSD-09","text":"The method of the whole is performative: the doctrine of causality is not merely expounded, but transformed through poetic form, recurrence, and variation into a habit of interpreting.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-01","text":"Ignorance of cause → attribution of intention → terror → anxious observation → renewed ignorance constitutes a self-confirming religious circuit.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-03","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-02","text":"Sensible effect → restricted invisible cause → reclassified appearance → new indication constitutes an investigative circuit that generates cumulative knowledge.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:XD-06","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-03","text":"Composition → stabilization → emergent property → dissolution → matter returned to new collisions constitutes a circuit of conservation without identity of forms.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-03","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-04","text":"Image → attention → desire or fear → idealizing opinion → more images constitutes a common circuit of erotic and religious captivation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:RMD-10","CL_0T6T_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-05","text":"Useful structure → custom → institution → wealth → ambition → arms → vulnerability produces a collective escalation of ambivalent progress.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-06","text":"Fear of death → ambition → power → violence → production of death → amplified fear is a catastrophic path of compensation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-13","CL_0T6T_01:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-07","text":"Reason concerning nature → knowledge of finite powers → removal of divine cause → diminution of fear → renewed attention to nature constitutes a therapeutic attractor.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-08","text":"Care → proximity → contagion and flight → abandonment → death effect a double tragic closure in which duty provides immunity by no path.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:RLE-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:RLE-09","text":"The terminal attractor is the finitude of powers: inquiry restricts mechanisms, action remains within nature, and fear diminishes without total mastery.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-02","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B01","text":"Ignorance of causality → false estimation → divine attribution → religious and deathly terror → inquiry into nature.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B02","text":"Primary bodies + void + motion → an ontological field sufficient for all operating things without a third nature.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B03","text":"Weight + impacts + clinamen + finite powers → immanent causality neither providential nor fatally exhausted.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-08","CL_0T6T_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B04","text":"Determinate seeds + differences of textures + selective congruity → finite generations and determinate interactions.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-06","CL_0T6T_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B05","text":"Innumerable collisions + configuration → temporary stability → emergent qualities, life, worlds, and identities.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-05","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B06","text":"Permeable composite + efflux + selective reception → sensation, memory, action, and environmentally mediated vulnerability.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:RMD-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B07","text":"Affection + added opinion + repeated attention → false identity of cause → desire, religion, and compensatory circuits.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:XD-05","CL_0T6T_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B08","text":"Born structure + use + custom + conflict → faculties, vocabularies, laws, and institutions without an original author.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:GA-11","CL_0T6T_02:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B09","text":"Dissolution of configuration → death of person / world / institution → matter conserved + form irreversibly lost.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-12","CL_0T6T_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FGS-B10","text":"Causal reconstruction → exclusion of providence → correction of opinion → diminution of fear, but not universal remedy or mastery of nature.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-04","CL_0T6T_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:AFM-B01","text":"Sensible effect → invisible bodily principles → motions and collisions → selective configuration → emergent properties → permeable composites → dissolution across all scales.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:AS-05","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorance of cause → religion and fear of death → natural inquiry → removal of providence → mortality of the person → correction of affect → finite tranquillity.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:GA-01","CL_0T6T_02:XD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:AFM-B03","text":"Stability without design → life and utility → custom and institutions → wealth and arms → common vulnerability → plague → terminal distinction between intelligibility and mastery.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:RMD-06","CL_0T6T_02:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-01","text":"The opposition between visible and real is reordered: the invisibility of cause requires an inferential method, not exclusion from knowledge.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-01","CL_0T6T_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-02","text":"Qualities, sensations, and identities are transferred from simple properties of substances into effects of relations, textures, and configurations.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-02","CL_0T6T_02:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-03","text":"Order and function are separated from antecedent design and reclassified into post-collision stability and retrospective preservation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MME-02","CL_0T6T_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-04","text":"The subject changes from an immaterial center into a permeable node of the circulation of bodies, images, affects, and institutions.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-02","CL_0T6T_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-05","text":"The opposition between providence and fate collapses, because material immanence contains limited contingency and causally restricted plurality.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-04","CL_0T6T_02:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-06","text":"Technical progress is converted from a necessary increase of good into an amplifier of conveniences, desires, war, and vulnerability.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:XD-08","CL_0T6T_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-07","text":"Knowledge of nature is separated from rule, because its proper effect is the reordering of interpretation and fear, not universal protection.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MME-08","CL_0T6T_02:GNX-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-08","text":"Death and calamity are placed in the same ontology but not the same ethics: death removes the subject, calamity wounds living subjects.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MME-09","text":"Piety and naturalism no longer stand opposed, because divine peace is causally preserved through the exclusion of governance and intervention.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:XD-06","CL_0T6T_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the principles of things, sensation, fear, images, worlds, the origins of life, history, diseases, and plague appear as objects of continuous explanation.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B01","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, bodies, void, motion, effluxes, textures, congruities, and dissolutions distribute diverse operations within a single material causality.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B02","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the struggle against gods, fate, and death is converted into correction of opinions, definition of limits, and interruption of affective circuits.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B03","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, matter remains, composites endure and perish, subjects are finite nodes, whereas history accumulates irreversible states.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B04","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, poem, analogy, and cumulative proof repeat the same grammar through variations and carry affect into causally ordered knowledge.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:ALT-B05","CL_0T6T_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-01","text":"Sensation remains the foundation of proof, although ultimate causes exceed direct inspection and the transition to them is supported by analogy.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-01","CL_0T6T_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-02","text":"Laws of compatibility generate stability, but clinamen and chance open events; the measure between necessity and contingency remains incomplete.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-02","CL_0T6T_02:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-03","text":"Sensation must emerge from insensible principles, but the transition from figure and motion to the truth of affection is not mechanically resolved.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-03","CL_0T6T_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-04","text":"The immortality of the gods does not fully cohere with the law of dissolution of composites, because remote gods are imperfectly derived within the material machine.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-04","CL_0T6T_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-05","text":"The poetic invocation of Venus retains agency, while doctrinal piety preserves divine peace through non-intervention.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:FL-05","CL_0T6T_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-06","text":"The exclusion of divine cause is stronger than the selection of a singular natural mechanism, whence therapeutic certainty surpasses explanatory indetermination.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:FL-02","CL_0T6T_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-07","text":"The promise of purification of the mind is compressed by pestilential persistence: knowledge corrects fear, but does not provide preservation of body or society.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:FL-03","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:FL-08","text":"The conservation of principles is joined with the irreversibility of forms, but this distinction sharply divides eternal matter from the history of losses.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-02","CL_0T6T_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-01","text":"Quality changes from a dependent accident into relational congruity, which orders generation, viability, and susceptibility.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MP-01","CL_0T6T_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-02","text":"Identity is extended from continuity of organization into a permeable node of bodily and social exchanges.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MP-04","CL_0T6T_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-03","text":"The simulacrum changes from a message of form into a recursive operator of desire, memory, attention, and religion.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MP-01","CL_0T6T_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-04","text":"Order changes from recurrent sequence into a temporally stable configuration, to which dissolution is already intrinsic.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MP-03","CL_0T6T_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-05","text":"Mortality is extended from a logical event that removes the subject of harm into a cosmological and social power to dissolve orders.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MP-03","CL_0T6T_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-06","text":"Piety changes from ritual obedience into the preservation of divine peace through the exclusion of intervention.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:MP-04","CL_0T6T_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MP-07","text":"Therapy changes from confidence in full explanation into finite liberation, which removes fear without a promise of mastery.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:BSD-04","CL_0T6T_02:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly an infinite and porous field of moving bodies, which forms local nodes, flows through them, and finally dissolves them.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-01","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a temporary bodily node, at once a selective receiver and a distribution of motions, not an immaterial or closed center.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-02","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-03","text":"Time is the permanence of principles under the irreversible succession of forms; composites have duration, whereas the person does not experience its own absence.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-03","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-04","text":"The principal struggle opposes circuits of captivation, in which image and ignorance nourish themselves, to knowledge that converts causes, attention, and attribution.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:HSI-04","CL_0T6T_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation is motion within nature without an exterior, by which fear is diminished without immunity and action is exercised without total mastery.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:HSI-05","CL_0T6T_01:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to reconstruct the machine from conserved matter and motion, through perception, emergence, and history, to personal mortality and plague.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B01","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the conjunction of conservation of matter, causality invariant across scale, mortality of composites, and correction of divine attribution.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B02","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain the links from clinamen to will, from insensible principles to sensation, and from analogical possibility to actual mechanism.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B03","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres because it retains the movement from affect to physics, from collision to order, from ignorance to fear, and from explanation to the limits of rule.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B04","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B05","text":"Coherence depends on the distinction of permanent principles from temporal configurations and on analogies that bridge gaps between scales.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B05","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B06","text":"The tension between liberation of the mind and vulnerability of the body must not be eliminated, because it itself distinguishes therapy's extension from a metaphysical promise.","refs":["CL_0T6T_02:AS-08","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_0T6T:MRT-B07","text":"The performative force of the poem resists axiomatic reduction, conveying obscure doctrine through repetition of causality into affective conversion.","refs":["CL_0T6T_01:MRT-B06","CL_0T6T_02:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"WB_0T6T@R002","source_variant_id":"canonical:WB_0T6T:WB_0T6T@R002","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"WB","kicker":"Whole book","title":"On the Nature of Things","context":"","original_title":"De Rerum Natura"},"original_title":"De Rerum Natura","title_language_code":"en"}],"seal":"SEAL_0T6T_040E40D9C902F37B75B4","original_title":"De Rerum Natura","title_language_code":"en","title_translation":{"schema":"care-title-heading-translation/1","language_code":"en","rendition_type":"machine","originals_preserved":true},"version_work_id":"WORK_STORE_PERSEUS_LATIN_PHI0550_PHI001_PERSEUS_LAT1","version_label":"Added 16 Oct 2026 · Run 16 Oct 2026 · gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-sol","version_revision":"140b71f97eba2a98d0ef9b58ec9560fc31a549d4bdbcc24c13e466630c7fbf74"},{"id":"WORK_STORE_THOMISTICUM_SUMMA_PRIMA","slug":"beta-ef18241e546d391b","title":"Summa Theologiae · First Part","author":"Thomas Aquinas","edition":"Leonine Latin text, Rome 1888–1889; Corpus Thomisticum transcription","year":"1274","original_year":1274,"publication_year":1888,"description":"","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-ef18241e546d391b-d0a1f05565a51515.png","source_url":"https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth0000.html","source_notices":{"provider":"Corpus Thomisticum","source_url":"https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth0000.html","statement":"Historical Latin work; Corpus Thomisticum electronic edition rights reserved"},"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","sources":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0","title":"First Part · Prologue","order":1,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[Summa theologiae, pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth0000.html#28231)\n\nQuia Catholicae veritatis doctor non solum provectos debet instruere, sed ad eum pertinet etiam incipientes erudire, secundum illud apostoli I ad Corinth. III, *tanquam parvulis in Christo, lac vobis potum dedi, non escam*; propositum nostrae intentionis in hoc opere est, ea quae ad Christianam religionem pertinent, eo modo tradere, secundum quod congruit ad eruditionem incipientium. Consideravimus namque huius doctrinae novitios, in his quae a diversis conscripta sunt, plurimum impediri, partim quidem propter multiplicationem inutilium quaestionum, articulorum et argumentorum; partim etiam quia ea quae sunt necessaria talibus ad sciendum, non traduntur secundum ordinem disciplinae, sed secundum quod requirebat librorum expositio, vel secundum quod se praebebat occasio disputandi; partim quidem quia eorundem frequens repetitio et fastidium et confusionem generabat in animis auditorum. Haec igitur et alia huiusmodi evitare studentes, tentabimus, cum confidentia divini auxilii, ea quae ad sacram doctrinam pertinent, breviter ac dilucide prosequi, secundum quod materia patietur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0002","text":"[Summa theologiae, pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth0000.html#28231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","text":"Quia Catholicae veritatis doctor non solum provectos debet instruere, sed ad eum pertinet etiam incipientes erudire, secundum illud apostoli I ad Corinth. III, *tanquam parvulis in Christo, lac vobis potum dedi, non escam*; propositum nostrae intentionis in hoc opere est, ea quae ad Christianam religionem pertinent, eo modo tradere, secundum quod congruit ad eruditionem incipientium. Consideravimus namque huius doctrinae novitios, in his quae a diversis conscripta sunt, plurimum impediri, partim quidem propter multiplicationem inutilium quaestionum, articulorum et argumentorum; partim etiam quia ea quae sunt necessaria talibus ad sciendum, non traduntur secundum ordinem disciplinae, sed secundum quod requirebat librorum expositio, vel secundum quod se praebebat occasio disputandi; partim quidem quia eorundem frequens repetitio et fastidium et confusionem generabat in animis auditorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","text":"Haec igitur et alia huiusmodi evitare studentes, tentabimus, cum confidentia divini auxilii, ea quae ad sacram doctrinam pertinent, breviter ac dilucide prosequi, secundum quod materia patietur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8536722edf55dd39638dd71039d6832f5026e6cc368fbce55ea08c1379a382e7","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T133223930806Z_3ea316c0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Prologue","source_sha256":"8536722edf55dd39638dd71039d6832f5026e6cc368fbce55ea08c1379a382e7","sha256":"23f31fc202d3dc7738c6f0fbd27a37770f583ad45944cf2186bd8f65c388f214","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:32:23.930820+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0002","text":"[Summa Theologiae, prologue.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth0000.html#28231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","text":"Because a teacher of Catholic truth ought not only to instruct the advanced, but also has the task of educating beginners, according to that saying of the Apostle, I Corinthians III, *As unto little ones in Christ, I gave you milk to drink, not food*; the purpose of our intention in this work is to set forth those things that pertain to the Christian religion in a manner suited to the instruction of beginners. For we have observed that novices in this teaching are greatly hindered by the things written by different authors: partly indeed because of the multiplication of useless questions, articles, and arguments; partly also because the things necessary for such persons to know are not presented according to the order of the discipline, but according as the exposition of books required, or as an occasion for disputation presented itself; and partly because the frequent repetition of the same things generated both weariness and confusion in the minds of the hearers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","text":"Therefore, endeavoring to avoid these and other such things, we shall attempt, with confidence in divine assistance, to treat briefly and clearly those matters that pertain to sacred doctrine, insofar as the subject matter will allow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"6b92ac83cb46baef7060fd00a34122ef3e585e011c21a160b712bdcdb8e37045","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Prooemium"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Prooemium","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1","title":"First Part · Question 1","order":2,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 1 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28232)\n\nEt ut intentio nostra sub aliquibus certis limitibus comprehendatur, necessarium est primo investigare de ipsa sacra doctrina, qualis sit, et ad quae se extendat. Circa quae quaerenda sunt decem. Primo, de necessitate huius doctrinae. Secundo, utrum sit scientia. Tertio, utrum sit una vel plures. Quarto, utrum sit speculativa vel practica. Quinto, de comparatione eius ad alias scientias. Sexto, utrum sit sapientia. Septimo, quid sit subiectum eius. Octavo, utrum sit argumentativa. Nono, utrum uti debeat metaphoricis vel symbolicis locutionibus. Decimo, utrum Scriptura sacra huius doctrinae sit secundum plures sensus exponenda.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 1 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28233)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit necessarium, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, aliam doctrinam haberi. Ad ea enim quae supra rationem sunt, homo non debet conari, secundum illud Eccli. III, *altiora te ne quaesieris*. Sed ea quae rationi subduntur, sufficienter traduntur in philosophicis disciplinis. Superfluum igitur videtur, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, aliam doctrinam haberi.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28234)\n\nPraeterea, doctrina non potest esse nisi de ente, nihil enim scitur nisi verum, quod cum ente convertitur. Sed de omnibus entibus tractatur in philosophicis disciplinis, et etiam de Deo, unde quaedam pars philosophiae dicitur theologia, sive scientia divina, ut patet per philosophum in VI Metaphys. Non fuit igitur necessarium, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, aliam doctrinam haberi.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28235)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur II ad Tim. III, *omnis Scriptura divinitus inspirata utilis est ad docendum, ad arguendum, ad corripiendum, ad erudiendum ad iustitiam*. Scriptura autem divinitus inspirata non pertinet ad philosophicas disciplinas, quae sunt secundum rationem humanam inventae. Utile igitur est, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, esse aliam scientiam divinitus inspiratam.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28236)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necessarium fuit ad humanam salutem, esse doctrinam quandam secundum revelationem divinam, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, quae ratione humana investigantur. Primo quidem, quia homo ordinatur ad Deum sicut ad quendam finem qui comprehensionem rationis excedit, secundum illud Isaiae LXIV, *oculus non vidit Deus absque te, quae praeparasti diligentibus te*. Finem autem oportet esse praecognitum hominibus, qui suas intentiones et actiones debent ordinare in finem. Unde necessarium fuit homini ad salutem, quod ei nota fierent quaedam per revelationem divinam, quae rationem humanam excedunt. Ad ea etiam quae de Deo ratione humana investigari possunt, necessarium fuit hominem instrui revelatione divina. Quia veritas de Deo, per rationem investigata, a paucis, et per longum tempus, et cum admixtione multorum errorum, homini proveniret, a cuius tamen veritatis cognitione dependet tota hominis salus, quae in Deo est. Ut igitur salus hominibus et convenientius et certius proveniat, necessarium fuit quod de divinis per divinam revelationem instruantur. Necessarium igitur fuit, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, quae per rationem investigantur, sacram doctrinam per revelationem haberi.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28237)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet ea quae sunt altiora hominis cognitione, non sint ab homine per rationem inquirenda, sunt tamen, a Deo revelata, suscipienda per fidem. Unde et ibidem subditur, *plurima supra sensum hominum ostensa sunt tibi*. Et in huiusmodi sacra doctrina consistit.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28238)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod diversa ratio cognoscibilis diversitatem scientiarum inducit. Eandem enim conclusionem demonstrat astrologus et naturalis, puta quod terra est rotunda, sed astrologus per medium mathematicum, idest a materia abstractum; naturalis autem per medium circa materiam consideratum. Unde nihil prohibet de eisdem rebus, de quibus philosophicae disciplinae tractant secundum quod sunt cognoscibilia lumine naturalis rationis, et aliam scientiam tractare secundum quod cognoscuntur lumine divinae revelationis. Unde theologia quae ad sacram doctrinam pertinet, differt secundum genus ab illa theologia quae pars philosophiae ponitur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 1 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28239)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra doctrina non sit scientia. Omnis enim scientia procedit ex principiis per se notis. Sed sacra doctrina procedit ex articulis fidei, qui non sunt per se noti, cum non ab omnibus concedantur, *non enim omnium est fides*, ut dicitur II Thessalon. III. Non igitur sacra doctrina est scientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28240)\n\nPraeterea, scientia non est singularium. Sed sacra doctrina tractat de singularibus, puta de gestis Abrahae, Isaac et Iacob, et similibus. Ergo sacra doctrina non est scientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28241)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trinitate, *huic scientiae attribuitur illud tantummodo quo fides saluberrima gignitur, nutritur, defenditur, roboratur*. Hoc autem ad nullam scientiam pertinet nisi ad sacram doctrinam. Ergo sacra doctrina est scientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28242)\n\nRespondeo dicendum sacram doctrinam esse scientiam. Sed sciendum est quod duplex est scientiarum genus. Quaedam enim sunt, quae procedunt ex principiis notis lumine naturali intellectus, sicut arithmetica, geometria, et huiusmodi. Quaedam vero sunt, quae procedunt ex principiis notis lumine superioris scientiae, sicut perspectiva procedit ex principiis notificatis per geometriam, et musica ex principiis per arithmeticam notis. Et hoc modo sacra doctrina est scientia, quia procedit ex principiis notis lumine superioris scientiae, quae scilicet est scientia Dei et beatorum. Unde sicut musica credit principia tradita sibi ab arithmetico, ita doctrina sacra credit principia revelata sibi a Deo.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28243)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod principia cuiuslibet scientiae vel sunt nota per se, vel reducuntur ad notitiam superioris scientiae. Et talia sunt principia sacrae doctrinae, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28244)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod singularia traduntur in sacra doctrina, non quia de eis principaliter tractetur, sed introducuntur tum in exemplum vitae, sicut in scientiis moralibus; tum etiam ad declarandum auctoritatem virorum per quos ad nos revelatio divina processit, super quam fundatur sacra Scriptura seu doctrina.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 1 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28245)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra doctrina non sit una scientia. Quia secundum philosophum in I Poster., *una scientia est quae est unius generis subiecti*. Creator autem et creatura, de quibus in sacra doctrina tractatur, non continentur sub uno genere subiecti. Ergo sacra doctrina non est una scientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28246)\n\nPraeterea, in sacra doctrina tractatur de Angelis, de creaturis corporalibus, de moribus hominum. Huiusmodi autem ad diversas scientias philosophicas pertinent. Igitur sacra doctrina non est una scientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28247)\n\nSed contra est quod sacra Scriptura de ea loquitur sicut de una scientia, dicitur enim Sap. X, *dedit illi scientiam sanctorum*.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28248)\n\nRespondeo dicendum sacram doctrinam unam scientiam esse. Est enim unitas potentiae et habitus consideranda secundum obiectum, non quidem materialiter, sed secundum rationem formalem obiecti, puta homo, asinus et lapis conveniunt in una formali ratione colorati, quod est obiectum visus. Quia igitur sacra Scriptura considerat aliqua secundum quod sunt divinitus revelata, secundum quod dictum est, omnia quaecumque sunt divinitus revelabilia, communicant in una ratione formali obiecti huius scientiae. Et ideo comprehenduntur sub sacra doctrina sicut sub scientia una.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28249)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sacra doctrina non determinat de Deo et de creaturis ex aequo, sed de Deo principaliter, et de creaturis secundum quod referuntur ad Deum, ut ad principium vel finem. Unde unitas scientiae non impeditur.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28250)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nihil prohibet inferiores potentias vel habitus diversificari circa illas materias, quae communiter cadunt sub una potentia vel habitu superiori, quia superior potentia vel habitus respicit obiectum sub universaliori ratione formali. Sicut obiectum sensus communis est sensibile, quod comprehendit sub se visibile et audibile, unde sensus communis, cum sit una potentia, extendit se ad omnia obiecta quinque sensuum. Et similiter ea quae in diversis scientiis philosophicis tractantur, potest sacra doctrina, una existens, considerare sub una ratione, inquantum scilicet sunt divinitus revelabilia, ut sic sacra doctrina sit velut quaedam impressio divinae scientiae, quae est una et simplex omnium.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 1 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28251)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra doctrina sit scientia practica. Finis enim practicae est operatio, secundum philosophum in II Metaphys. Sacra autem doctrina ad operationem ordinatur, secundum illud Iac. I, *estote factores verbi, et non auditores tantum*. Ergo sacra doctrina est practica scientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28252)\n\nPraeterea, sacra doctrina dividitur per legem veterem et novam. Lex autem pertinet ad scientiam moralem, quae est scientia practica. Ergo sacra doctrina est scientia practica.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28253)\n\nSed contra, omnis scientia practica est de rebus operabilibus ab homine; ut moralis de actibus hominum, et aedificativa de aedificiis. Sacra autem doctrina est principaliter de Deo, cuius magis homines sunt opera. Non ergo est scientia practica, sed magis speculativa.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28254)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod sacra doctrina, ut dictum est, una existens, se extendit ad ea quae pertinent ad diversas scientias philosophicas, propter rationem formalem quam in diversis attendit, scilicet prout sunt divino lumine cognoscibilia. Unde licet in scientiis philosophicis alia sit speculativa et alia practica, sacra tamen doctrina comprehendit sub se utramque; sicut et Deus eadem scientia se cognoscit, et ea quae facit. Magis tamen est speculativa quam practica, quia principalius agit de rebus divinis quam de actibus humanis; de quibus agit secundum quod per eos ordinatur homo ad perfectam Dei cognitionem, in qua aeterna beatitudo consistit.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28255)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 1 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28256)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra doctrina non sit dignior aliis scientiis. Certitudo enim pertinet ad dignitatem scientiae. Sed aliae scientiae, de quarum principiis dubitari non potest, videntur esse certiores sacra doctrina, cuius principia, scilicet articuli fidei, dubitationem recipiunt. Aliae igitur scientiae videntur ista digniores.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28257)\n\nPraeterea, inferioris scientiae est a superiori accipere, sicut musicus ab arithmetico. Sed sacra doctrina accipit aliquid a philosophicis disciplinis, dicit enim Hieronymus in epistola ad magnum oratorem urbis Romae, quod *doctores antiqui intantum philosophorum doctrinis atque sententiis suos resperserunt libros, ut nescias quid in illis prius admirari debeas, eruditionem saeculi, an scientiam Scripturarum*. Ergo sacra doctrina est inferior aliis scientiis.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28258)\n\nSed contra est quod aliae scientiae dicuntur ancillae huius, Prov. IX, *misit ancillas suas vocare ad arcem*.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28259)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum ista scientia quantum ad aliquid sit speculativa, et quantum ad aliquid sit practica, omnes alias transcendit tam speculativas quam practicas. Speculativarum enim scientiarum una altera dignior dicitur, tum propter certitudinem, tum propter dignitatem materiae. Et quantum ad utrumque, haec scientia alias speculativas scientias excedit. Secundum certitudinem quidem, quia aliae scientiae certitudinem habent ex naturali lumine rationis humanae, quae potest errare, haec autem certitudinem habet ex lumine divinae scientiae, quae decipi non potest. Secundum dignitatem vero materiae, quia ista scientia est principaliter de his quae sua altitudine rationem transcendunt, aliae vero scientiae considerant ea tantum quae rationi subduntur. Practicarum vero scientiarum illa dignior est, quae ad ulteriorem finem ordinatur, sicut civilis militari, nam bonum exercitus ad bonum civitatis ordinatur. Finis autem huius doctrinae inquantum est practica, est beatitudo aeterna, ad quam sicut ad ultimum finem ordinantur omnes alii fines scientiarum practicarum. Unde manifestum est, secundum omnem modum, eam digniorem esse aliis.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28260)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod nihil prohibet id quod est certius secundum naturam, esse quoad nos minus certum, propter debilitatem intellectus nostri, qui se habet ad manifestissima naturae, sicut oculus noctuae ad lumen solis, sicut dicitur in II Metaphys. Unde dubitatio quae accidit in aliquibus circa articulos fidei, non est propter incertitudinem rei, sed propter debilitatem intellectus humani. Et tamen minimum quod potest haberi de cognitione rerum altissimarum, desiderabilius est quam certissima cognitio quae habetur de minimis rebus, ut dicitur in XI de animalibus.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28261)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod haec scientia accipere potest aliquid a philosophicis disciplinis, non quod ex necessitate eis indigeat, sed ad maiorem manifestationem eorum quae in hac scientia traduntur. Non enim accipit sua principia ab aliis scientiis, sed immediate a Deo per revelationem. Et ideo non accipit ab aliis scientiis tanquam a superioribus, sed utitur eis tanquam inferioribus et ancillis; sicut architectonicae utuntur subministrantibus, ut civilis militari. Et hoc ipsum quod sic utitur eis, non est propter defectum vel insufficientiam eius, sed propter defectum intellectus nostri; qui ex his quae per naturalem rationem (ex qua procedunt aliae scientiae) cognoscuntur, facilius manuducitur in ea quae sunt supra rationem, quae in hac scientia traduntur.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28262)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod haec doctrina non sit sapientia. Nulla enim doctrina quae supponit sua principia aliunde, digna est nomine sapientiae, *quia sapientis est ordinare, et non ordinari* (I Metaphys.). Sed haec doctrina supponit principia sua aliunde, ut ex dictis patet. Ergo haec doctrina non est sapientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28263)\n\nPraeterea, ad sapientiam pertinet probare principia aliarum scientiarum, unde ut caput dicitur scientiarum, ut VI Ethic. patet. Sed haec doctrina non probat principia aliarum scientiarum. Ergo non est sapientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28264)\n\nPraeterea, haec doctrina per studium acquiritur. Sapientia autem per infusionem habetur, unde inter septem dona spiritus sancti connumeratur, ut patet Isaiae XI. Ergo haec doctrina non est sapientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28265)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Deut. IV, in principio legis, *haec est nostra sapientia et intellectus coram populis*.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28266)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod haec doctrina maxime sapientia est inter omnes sapientias humanas, non quidem in aliquo genere tantum, sed simpliciter. Cum enim sapientis sit ordinare et iudicare, iudicium autem per altiorem causam de inferioribus habeatur; ille sapiens dicitur in unoquoque genere, qui considerat causam altissimam illius generis. Ut in genere aedificii, artifex qui disponit formam domus, dicitur sapiens et architector, respectu inferiorum artificum, qui dolant ligna vel parant lapides, unde dicitur I Cor. III, *ut sapiens architector fundamentum posui*. Et rursus, in genere totius humanae vitae, prudens sapiens dicitur, inquantum ordinat humanos actus ad debitum finem, unde dicitur Prov. X, *sapientia est viro prudentia*. Ille igitur qui considerat simpliciter altissimam causam totius universi, quae Deus est, maxime sapiens dicitur, unde et sapientia dicitur esse divinorum cognitio, ut patet per Augustinum, XII de Trinitate. Sacra autem doctrina propriissime determinat de Deo secundum quod est altissima causa, quia non solum quantum ad illud quod est per creaturas cognoscibile (quod philosophi cognoverunt, ut dicitur Rom. I, *quod notum est Dei, manifestum est illis*); sed etiam quantum ad id quod notum est sibi soli de seipso, et aliis per revelationem communicatum. Unde sacra doctrina maxime dicitur sapientia.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28267)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sacra doctrina non supponit sua principia ab aliqua scientia humana, sed a scientia divina, a qua, sicut a summa sapientia, omnis nostra cognitio ordinatur.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28268)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliarum scientiarum principia vel sunt per se nota, et probari non possunt, vel per aliquam rationem naturalem probantur in aliqua alia scientia. Propria autem huius scientiae cognitio est, quae est per revelationem, non autem quae est per naturalem rationem. Et ideo non pertinet ad eam probare principia aliarum scientiarum, sed solum iudicare de eis, quidquid enim in aliis scientiis invenitur veritati huius scientiae repugnans, totum condemnatur ut falsum, unde dicitur II Cor. X, *consilia destruentes, et omnem altitudinem extollentem se adversus scientiam Dei*.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28269)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum iudicium ad sapientem pertineat, secundum duplicem modum iudicandi, dupliciter sapientia accipitur. Contingit enim aliquem iudicare, uno modo per modum inclinationis, sicut qui habet habitum virtutis, recte iudicat de his quae sunt secundum virtutem agenda, inquantum ad illa inclinatur, unde et in X Ethic. dicitur quod virtuosus est mensura et regula actuum humanorum. Alio modo, per modum cognitionis, sicut aliquis instructus in scientia morali, posset iudicare de actibus virtutis, etiam si virtutem non haberet. Primus igitur modus iudicandi de rebus divinis, pertinet ad sapientiam quae ponitur donum spiritus sancti secundum illud I Cor. II, *spiritualis homo iudicat omnia,* etc., et Dionysius dicit, II cap. de divinis nominibus, *Hierotheus doctus est non solum discens, sed et patiens divina*. Secundus autem modus iudicandi pertinet ad hanc doctrinam, secundum quod per studium habetur; licet eius principia ex revelatione habeantur.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 1 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28270)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit subiectum huius scientiae. In qualibet enim scientia oportet supponere de subiecto quid est, secundum philosophum in I Poster. Sed haec scientia non supponit de Deo quid est, dicit enim Damascenus, *in Deo quid est, dicere impossibile est*. Ergo Deus non est subiectum huius scientiae.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28271)\n\nPraeterea, omnia quae determinantur in aliqua scientia, comprehenduntur sub subiecto illius scientiae. Sed in sacra Scriptura determinatur de multis aliis quam de Deo, puta de creaturis, et de moribus hominum. Ergo Deus non est subiectum huius scientiae.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28272)\n\nSed contra, illud est subiectum scientiae, de quo est sermo in scientia. Sed in hac scientia fit sermo de Deo, dicitur enim theologia, quasi sermo de Deo. Ergo Deus est subiectum huius scientiae.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28273)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus est subiectum huius scientiae. Sic enim se habet subiectum ad scientiam, sicut obiectum ad potentiam vel habitum. Proprie autem illud assignatur obiectum alicuius potentiae vel habitus, sub cuius ratione omnia referuntur ad potentiam vel habitum, sicut homo et lapis referuntur ad visum inquantum sunt colorata, unde coloratum est proprium obiectum visus. Omnia autem pertractantur in sacra doctrina sub ratione Dei, vel quia sunt ipse Deus; vel quia habent ordinem ad Deum, ut ad principium et finem. Unde sequitur quod Deus vere sit subiectum huius scientiae. Quod etiam manifestum fit ex principiis huius scientiae, quae sunt articuli fidei, quae est de Deo, idem autem est subiectum principiorum et totius scientiae, cum tota scientia virtute contineatur in principiis. Quidam vero, attendentes ad ea quae in ista scientia tractantur, et non ad rationem secundum quam considerantur, assignaverunt aliter subiectum huius scientiae, vel res et signa; vel opera reparationis; vel totum Christum, idest caput et membra. De omnibus enim istis tractatur in ista scientia, sed secundum ordinem ad Deum.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28274)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet de Deo non possimus scire quid est, utimur tamen eius effectu, in hac doctrina, vel naturae vel gratiae, loco definitionis, ad ea quae de Deo in hac doctrina considerantur, sicut et in aliquibus scientiis philosophicis demonstratur aliquid de causa per effectum, accipiendo effectum loco definitionis causae.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28275)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod omnia alia quae determinantur in sacra doctrina, comprehenduntur sub Deo, non ut partes vel species vel accidentia, sed ut ordinata aliqualiter ad ipsum.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 1 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28276)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod haec doctrina non sit argumentativa. Dicit enim Ambrosius in libro I de fide Catholica, *tolle argumenta, ubi fides quaeritur*. Sed in hac doctrina praecipue fides quaeritur, unde dicitur Ioan. XX, *haec scripta sunt ut credatis*. Ergo sacra doctrina non est argumentativa.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28277)\n\nPraeterea, si sit argumentativa, aut argumentatur ex auctoritate, aut ex ratione. Si ex auctoritate, non videtur hoc congruere eius dignitati, nam locus ab auctoritate est infirmissimus, secundum Boetium. Si etiam ex ratione, hoc non congruit eius fini, quia secundum Gregorium in homilia, *fides non habet meritum, ubi humana ratio praebet experimentum*. Ergo sacra doctrina non est argumentativa.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28278)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur ad Titum I, de episcopo, *amplectentem eum qui secundum doctrinam est, fidelem sermonem, ut potens sit exhortari in doctrina sana, et eos qui contradicunt arguere*.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28279)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut aliae scientiae non argumentantur ad sua principia probanda, sed ex principiis argumentantur ad ostendendum alia in ipsis scientiis; ita haec doctrina non argumentatur ad sua principia probanda, quae sunt articuli fidei; sed ex eis procedit ad aliquid aliud ostendendum; sicut apostolus, I ad Cor. XV, ex resurrectione Christi argumentatur ad resurrectionem communem probandam. Sed tamen considerandum est in scientiis philosophicis, quod inferiores scientiae nec probant sua principia, nec contra negantem principia disputant, sed hoc relinquunt superiori scientiae, suprema vero inter eas, scilicet metaphysica, disputat contra negantem sua principia, si adversarius aliquid concedit, si autem nihil concedit, non potest cum eo disputare, potest tamen solvere rationes ipsius. Unde sacra Scriptura, cum non habeat superiorem, disputat cum negante sua principia, argumentando quidem, si adversarius aliquid concedat eorum quae per divinam revelationem habentur; sicut per auctoritates sacrae doctrinae disputamus contra haereticos, et per unum articulum contra negantes alium. Si vero adversarius nihil credat eorum quae divinitus revelantur, non remanet amplius via ad probandum articulos fidei per rationes, sed ad solvendum rationes, si quas inducit, contra fidem. Cum enim fides infallibili veritati innitatur, impossibile autem sit de vero demonstrari contrarium, manifestum est probationes quae contra fidem inducuntur, non esse demonstrationes, sed solubilia argumenta.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28280)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet argumenta rationis humanae non habeant locum ad probandum quae fidei sunt, tamen ex articulis fidei haec doctrina ad alia argumentatur, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28281)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod argumentari ex auctoritate est maxime proprium huius doctrinae, eo quod principia huius doctrinae per revelationem habentur, et sic oportet quod credatur auctoritati eorum quibus revelatio facta est. Nec hoc derogat dignitati huius doctrinae, nam licet locus ab auctoritate quae fundatur super ratione humana, sit infirmissimus; locus tamen ab auctoritate quae fundatur super revelatione divina, est efficacissimus. Utitur tamen sacra doctrina etiam ratione humana, non quidem ad probandum fidem, quia per hoc tolleretur meritum fidei; sed ad manifestandum aliqua alia quae traduntur in hac doctrina. Cum enim gratia non tollat naturam, sed perficiat, oportet quod naturalis ratio subserviat fidei; sicut et naturalis inclinatio voluntatis obsequitur caritati. Unde et apostolus dicit, II ad Cor. X, *in captivitatem redigentes omnem intellectum in obsequium Christi*. Et inde est quod etiam auctoritatibus philosophorum sacra doctrina utitur, ubi per rationem naturalem veritatem cognoscere potuerunt; sicut Paulus, actuum XVII, inducit verbum Arati, dicens, *sicut et quidam poetarum vestrorum dixerunt, genus Dei sumus*. Sed tamen sacra doctrina huiusmodi auctoritatibus utitur quasi extraneis argumentis, et probabilibus. Auctoritatibus autem canonicae Scripturae utitur proprie, ex necessitate argumentando. Auctoritatibus autem aliorum doctorum Ecclesiae, quasi arguendo ex propriis, sed probabiliter. Innititur enim fides nostra revelationi apostolis et prophetis factae, qui canonicos libros scripserunt, non autem revelationi, si qua fuit aliis doctoribus facta. Unde dicit Augustinus, in epistola ad Hieronymum, *solis eis Scripturarum libris qui canonici appellantur, didici hunc honorem deferre, ut nullum auctorem eorum in scribendo errasse aliquid firmissime credam. Alios autem ita lego, ut, quantalibet sanctitate doctrinaque praepolleant, non ideo verum putem, quod ipsi ita senserunt vel scripserunt*.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28282)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra Scriptura non debeat uti metaphoris. Illud enim quod est proprium infimae doctrinae, non videtur competere huic scientiae, quae inter alias tenet locum supremum, ut iam dictum est. Procedere autem per similitudines varias et repraesentationes, est proprium poeticae, quae est infima inter omnes doctrinas. Ergo huiusmodi similitudinibus uti, non est conveniens huic scientiae.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28283)\n\nPraeterea, haec doctrina videtur esse ordinata ad veritatis manifestationem, unde et manifestatoribus eius praemium promittitur, Eccli. XXIV, *qui elucidant me, vitam aeternam habebunt*. Sed per huiusmodi similitudines veritas occultatur. Non ergo competit huic doctrinae divina tradere sub similitudine corporalium rerum.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28284)\n\nPraeterea, quanto aliquae creaturae sunt sublimiores, tanto magis ad divinam similitudinem accedunt. Si igitur aliquae ex creaturis transumerentur ad Deum, tunc oporteret talem transumptionem maxime fieri ex sublimioribus creaturis, et non ex infimis. Quod tamen in Scripturis frequenter invenitur.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28285)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Osee XII, *ego visionem multiplicavi eis, et in manibus prophetarum assimilatus sum*. Tradere autem aliquid sub similitudine, est metaphoricum. Ergo ad sacram doctrinam pertinet uti metaphoris.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28286)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod conveniens est sacrae Scripturae divina et spiritualia sub similitudine corporalium tradere. Deus enim omnibus providet secundum quod competit eorum naturae. Est autem naturale homini ut per sensibilia ad intelligibilia veniat, quia omnis nostra cognitio a sensu initium habet. Unde convenienter in sacra Scriptura traduntur nobis spiritualia sub metaphoris corporalium. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, I cap. caelestis hierarchiae, *impossibile est nobis aliter lucere divinum radium, nisi varietate sacrorum velaminum circumvelatum*. Convenit etiam sacrae Scripturae, quae communiter omnibus proponitur (secundum illud ad Rom. I, *sapientibus et insipientibus debitor sum*), ut spiritualia sub similitudinibus corporalium proponantur; ut saltem vel sic rudes eam capiant, qui ad intelligibilia secundum se capienda non sunt idonei.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28287)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod poeta utitur metaphoris propter repraesentationem, repraesentatio enim naturaliter homini delectabilis est. Sed sacra doctrina utitur metaphoris propter necessitatem et utilitatem, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28288)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod radius divinae revelationis non destruitur propter figuras sensibiles quibus circumvelatur, ut dicit Dionysius, sed remanet in sua veritate; ut mentes quibus fit revelatio, non permittat in similitudinibus permanere, sed elevet eas ad cognitionem intelligibilium; et per eos quibus revelatio facta est, alii etiam circa haec instruantur. Unde ea quae in uno loco Scripturae traduntur sub metaphoris, in aliis locis expressius exponuntur. Et ipsa etiam occultatio figurarum utilis est, ad exercitium studiosorum, et contra irrisiones infidelium, de quibus dicitur, Matth. VII, *nolite sanctum dare canibus*.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28289)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut docet Dionysius, cap. II Cael. Hier., magis est conveniens quod divina in Scripturis tradantur sub figuris vilium corporum, quam corporum nobilium. Et hoc propter tria. Primo, quia per hoc magis liberatur humanus animus ab errore. Manifestum enim apparet quod haec secundum proprietatem non dicuntur de divinis, quod posset esse dubium, si sub figuris nobilium corporum describerentur divina; maxime apud illos qui nihil aliud a corporibus nobilius excogitare noverunt. Secundo, quia hic modus convenientior est cognitioni quam de Deo habemus in hac vita. Magis enim manifestatur nobis de ipso quid non est, quam quid est, et ideo similitudines illarum rerum quae magis elongantur a Deo, veriorem nobis faciunt aestimationem quod sit supra illud quod de Deo dicimus vel cogitamus. Tertio, quia per huiusmodi, divina magis occultantur indignis.\n\n## Articulus 10\n\n[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28290)\n\nAd decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra Scriptura sub una littera non habeat plures sensus, qui sunt historicus vel litteralis, allegoricus, tropologicus sive moralis, et anagogicus. Multiplicitas enim sensuum in una Scriptura parit confusionem et deceptionem, et tollit arguendi firmitatem, unde ex multiplicibus propositionibus non procedit argumentatio, sed secundum hoc aliquae fallaciae assignantur. Sacra autem Scriptura debet esse efficax ad ostendendam veritatem absque omni fallacia. Ergo non debent in ea sub una littera plures sensus tradi.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28291)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit in libro de utilitate credendi, quod *Scriptura quae testamentum vetus vocatur, quadrifariam traditur, scilicet, secundum historiam, secundum aetiologiam, secundum analogiam, secundum allegoriam*. Quae quidem quatuor a quatuor praedictis videntur esse aliena omnino. Non igitur conveniens videtur quod eadem littera sacrae Scripturae secundum quatuor sensus praedictos exponatur.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28292)\n\nPraeterea, praeter praedictos sensus, invenitur sensus parabolicus, qui inter illos sensus quatuor non continetur.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28293)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Gregorius, XX Moralium, *sacra Scriptura omnes scientias ipso locutionis suae more transcendit, quia uno eodemque sermone, dum narrat gestum, prodit mysterium*.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28294)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod auctor sacrae Scripturae est Deus, in cuius potestate est ut non solum voces ad significandum accommodet (quod etiam homo facere potest), sed etiam res ipsas. Et ideo, cum in omnibus scientiis voces significent, hoc habet proprium ista scientia, quod ipsae res significatae per voces, etiam significant aliquid. Illa ergo prima significatio, qua voces significant res, pertinet ad primum sensum, qui est sensus historicus vel litteralis. Illa vero significatio qua res significatae per voces, iterum res alias significant, dicitur sensus spiritualis; qui super litteralem fundatur, et eum supponit. Hic autem sensus spiritualis trifariam dividitur. Sicut enim dicit apostolus, ad Hebr. VII, lex vetus figura est novae legis, et ipsa nova lex, ut dicit Dionysius in ecclesiastica hierarchia, est figura futurae gloriae, in nova etiam lege, ea quae in capite sunt gesta, sunt signa eorum quae nos agere debemus. Secundum ergo quod ea quae sunt veteris legis, significant ea quae sunt novae legis, est sensus allegoricus, secundum vero quod ea quae in Christo sunt facta, vel in his quae Christum significant, sunt signa eorum quae nos agere debemus, est sensus moralis, prout vero significant ea quae sunt in aeterna gloria, est sensus anagogicus. Quia vero sensus litteralis est, quem auctor intendit, auctor autem sacrae Scripturae Deus est, qui omnia simul suo intellectu comprehendit, non est inconveniens, ut dicit Augustinus XII confessionum, si etiam secundum litteralem sensum in una littera Scripturae plures sint sensus.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28295)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod multiplicitas horum sensuum non facit aequivocationem, aut aliam speciem multiplicitatis, quia, sicut iam dictum est, sensus isti non multiplicantur propter hoc quod una vox multa significet; sed quia ipsae res significatae per voces, aliarum rerum possunt esse signa. Et ita etiam nulla confusio sequitur in sacra Scriptura, cum omnes sensus fundentur super unum, scilicet litteralem; ex quo solo potest trahi argumentum, non autem ex his quae secundum allegoriam dicuntur, ut dicit Augustinus in epistola contra Vincentium Donatistam. Non tamen ex hoc aliquid deperit sacrae Scripturae, quia nihil sub spirituali sensu continetur fidei necessarium, quod Scriptura per litteralem sensum alicubi manifeste non tradat.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28296)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illa tria, historia, aetiologia, analogia, ad unum litteralem sensum pertinent. Nam historia est, ut ipse Augustinus exponit, cum simpliciter aliquid proponitur, aetiologia vero, cum causa dicti assignatur, sicut cum dominus assignavit causam quare Moyses permisit licentiam repudiandi uxores, scilicet propter duritiam cordis ipsorum, Matt. XIX, analogia vero est, cum veritas unius Scripturae ostenditur veritati alterius non repugnare. Sola autem allegoria, inter illa quatuor, pro tribus spiritualibus sensibus ponitur. Sicut et Hugo de sancto Victore sub sensu allegorico etiam anagogicum comprehendit, ponens in tertio suarum sententiarum solum tres sensus, scilicet historicum, allegoricum et tropologicum.\n\n[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28297)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod sensus parabolicus sub litterali continetur, nam per voces significatur aliquid proprie, et aliquid figurative; nec est litteralis sensus ipsa figura, sed id quod est figuratum. Non enim cum Scriptura nominat Dei brachium, est litteralis sensus quod in Deo sit membrum huiusmodi corporale, sed id quod per hoc membrum significatur, scilicet virtus operativa. In quo patet quod sensui litterali sacrae Scripturae nunquam potest subesse falsum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0002","text":"[I q. 1 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003","text":"Et ut intentio nostra sub aliquibus certis limitibus comprehendatur, necessarium est primo investigare de ipsa sacra doctrina, qualis sit, et ad quae se extendat. Circa quae quaerenda sunt decem. Primo, de necessitate huius doctrinae. Secundo, utrum sit scientia. Tertio, utrum sit una vel plures. Quarto, utrum sit speculativa vel practica. Quinto, de comparatione eius ad alias scientias. Sexto, utrum sit sapientia. Septimo, quid sit subiectum eius. Octavo, utrum sit argumentativa. Nono, utrum uti debeat metaphoricis vel symbolicis locutionibus. Decimo, utrum Scriptura sacra huius doctrinae sit secundum plures sensus exponenda.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0005","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit necessarium, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, aliam doctrinam haberi. Ad ea enim quae supra rationem sunt, homo non debet conari, secundum illud Eccli. III, *altiora te ne quaesieris*. Sed ea quae rationi subduntur, sufficienter traduntur in philosophicis disciplinis. Superfluum igitur videtur, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, aliam doctrinam haberi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0007","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0008","text":"Praeterea, doctrina non potest esse nisi de ente, nihil enim scitur nisi verum, quod cum ente convertitur. Sed de omnibus entibus tractatur in philosophicis disciplinis, et etiam de Deo, unde quaedam pars philosophiae dicitur theologia, sive scientia divina, ut patet per philosophum in VI Metaphys. Non fuit igitur necessarium, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, aliam doctrinam haberi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0009","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0010","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur II ad Tim. III, *omnis Scriptura divinitus inspirata utilis est ad docendum, ad arguendum, ad corripiendum, ad erudiendum ad iustitiam*. Scriptura autem divinitus inspirata non pertinet ad philosophicas disciplinas, quae sunt secundum rationem humanam inventae. Utile igitur est, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, esse aliam scientiam divinitus inspiratam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0011","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necessarium fuit ad humanam salutem, esse doctrinam quandam secundum revelationem divinam, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, quae ratione humana investigantur. Primo quidem, quia homo ordinatur ad Deum sicut ad quendam finem qui comprehensionem rationis excedit, secundum illud Isaiae LXIV, *oculus non vidit Deus absque te, quae praeparasti diligentibus te*. Finem autem oportet esse praecognitum hominibus, qui suas intentiones et actiones debent ordinare in finem. Unde necessarium fuit homini ad salutem, quod ei nota fierent quaedam per revelationem divinam, quae rationem humanam excedunt. Ad ea etiam quae de Deo ratione humana investigari possunt, necessarium fuit hominem instrui revelatione divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","text":"Quia veritas de Deo, per rationem investigata, a paucis, et per longum tempus, et cum admixtione multorum errorum, homini proveniret, a cuius tamen veritatis cognitione dependet tota hominis salus, quae in Deo est. Ut igitur salus hominibus et convenientius et certius proveniat, necessarium fuit quod de divinis per divinam revelationem instruantur. Necessarium igitur fuit, praeter philosophicas disciplinas, quae per rationem investigantur, sacram doctrinam per revelationem haberi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0014","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0015","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet ea quae sunt altiora hominis cognitione, non sint ab homine per rationem inquirenda, sunt tamen, a Deo revelata, suscipienda per fidem. Unde et ibidem subditur, *plurima supra sensum hominum ostensa sunt tibi*. Et in huiusmodi sacra doctrina consistit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0016","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod diversa ratio cognoscibilis diversitatem scientiarum inducit. Eandem enim conclusionem demonstrat astrologus et naturalis, puta quod terra est rotunda, sed astrologus per medium mathematicum, idest a materia abstractum; naturalis autem per medium circa materiam consideratum. Unde nihil prohibet de eisdem rebus, de quibus philosophicae disciplinae tractant secundum quod sunt cognoscibilia lumine naturalis rationis, et aliam scientiam tractare secundum quod cognoscuntur lumine divinae revelationis. Unde theologia quae ad sacram doctrinam pertinet, differt secundum genus ab illa theologia quae pars philosophiae ponitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0018","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0019","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0020","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra doctrina non sit scientia. Omnis enim scientia procedit ex principiis per se notis. Sed sacra doctrina procedit ex articulis fidei, qui non sunt per se noti, cum non ab omnibus concedantur, *non enim omnium est fides*, ut dicitur II Thessalon. III. Non igitur sacra doctrina est scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0021","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0022","text":"Praeterea, scientia non est singularium. Sed sacra doctrina tractat de singularibus, puta de gestis Abrahae, Isaac et Iacob, et similibus. Ergo sacra doctrina non est scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0023","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0024","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trinitate, *huic scientiae attribuitur illud tantummodo quo fides saluberrima gignitur, nutritur, defenditur, roboratur*. Hoc autem ad nullam scientiam pertinet nisi ad sacram doctrinam. Ergo sacra doctrina est scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0025","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","text":"Respondeo dicendum sacram doctrinam esse scientiam. Sed sciendum est quod duplex est scientiarum genus. Quaedam enim sunt, quae procedunt ex principiis notis lumine naturali intellectus, sicut arithmetica, geometria, et huiusmodi. Quaedam vero sunt, quae procedunt ex principiis notis lumine superioris scientiae, sicut perspectiva procedit ex principiis notificatis per geometriam, et musica ex principiis per arithmeticam notis. Et hoc modo sacra doctrina est scientia, quia procedit ex principiis notis lumine superioris scientiae, quae scilicet est scientia Dei et beatorum. Unde sicut musica credit principia tradita sibi ab arithmetico, ita doctrina sacra credit principia revelata sibi a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0027","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod principia cuiuslibet scientiae vel sunt nota per se, vel reducuntur ad notitiam superioris scientiae. Et talia sunt principia sacrae doctrinae, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0029","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0030","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod singularia traduntur in sacra doctrina, non quia de eis principaliter tractetur, sed introducuntur tum in exemplum vitae, sicut in scientiis moralibus; tum etiam ad declarandum auctoritatem virorum per quos ad nos revelatio divina processit, super quam fundatur sacra Scriptura seu doctrina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0031","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0032","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0033","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra doctrina non sit una scientia. Quia secundum philosophum in I Poster., *una scientia est quae est unius generis subiecti*. Creator autem et creatura, de quibus in sacra doctrina tractatur, non continentur sub uno genere subiecti. Ergo sacra doctrina non est una scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0034","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0035","text":"Praeterea, in sacra doctrina tractatur de Angelis, de creaturis corporalibus, de moribus hominum. Huiusmodi autem ad diversas scientias philosophicas pertinent. Igitur sacra doctrina non est una scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0036","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0037","text":"Sed contra est quod sacra Scriptura de ea loquitur sicut de una scientia, dicitur enim Sap. X, *dedit illi scientiam sanctorum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0038","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","text":"Respondeo dicendum sacram doctrinam unam scientiam esse. Est enim unitas potentiae et habitus consideranda secundum obiectum, non quidem materialiter, sed secundum rationem formalem obiecti, puta homo, asinus et lapis conveniunt in una formali ratione colorati, quod est obiectum visus. Quia igitur sacra Scriptura considerat aliqua secundum quod sunt divinitus revelata, secundum quod dictum est, omnia quaecumque sunt divinitus revelabilia, communicant in una ratione formali obiecti huius scientiae. Et ideo comprehenduntur sub sacra doctrina sicut sub scientia una.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0040","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sacra doctrina non determinat de Deo et de creaturis ex aequo, sed de Deo principaliter, et de creaturis secundum quod referuntur ad Deum, ut ad principium vel finem. Unde unitas scientiae non impeditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0042","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0043","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nihil prohibet inferiores potentias vel habitus diversificari circa illas materias, quae communiter cadunt sub una potentia vel habitu superiori, quia superior potentia vel habitus respicit obiectum sub universaliori ratione formali. Sicut obiectum sensus communis est sensibile, quod comprehendit sub se visibile et audibile, unde sensus communis, cum sit una potentia, extendit se ad omnia obiecta quinque sensuum. Et similiter ea quae in diversis scientiis philosophicis tractantur, potest sacra doctrina, una existens, considerare sub una ratione, inquantum scilicet sunt divinitus revelabilia, ut sic sacra doctrina sit velut quaedam impressio divinae scientiae, quae est una et simplex omnium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0044","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0045","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0046","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra doctrina sit scientia practica. Finis enim practicae est operatio, secundum philosophum in II Metaphys. Sacra autem doctrina ad operationem ordinatur, secundum illud Iac. I, *estote factores verbi, et non auditores tantum*. Ergo sacra doctrina est practica scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0047","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0048","text":"Praeterea, sacra doctrina dividitur per legem veterem et novam. Lex autem pertinet ad scientiam moralem, quae est scientia practica. Ergo sacra doctrina est scientia practica.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0049","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0050","text":"Sed contra, omnis scientia practica est de rebus operabilibus ab homine; ut moralis de actibus hominum, et aedificativa de aedificiis. Sacra autem doctrina est principaliter de Deo, cuius magis homines sunt opera. Non ergo est scientia practica, sed magis speculativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0051","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod sacra doctrina, ut dictum est, una existens, se extendit ad ea quae pertinent ad diversas scientias philosophicas, propter rationem formalem quam in diversis attendit, scilicet prout sunt divino lumine cognoscibilia. Unde licet in scientiis philosophicis alia sit speculativa et alia practica, sacra tamen doctrina comprehendit sub se utramque; sicut et Deus eadem scientia se cognoscit, et ea quae facit. Magis tamen est speculativa quam practica, quia principalius agit de rebus divinis quam de actibus humanis; de quibus agit secundum quod per eos ordinatur homo ad perfectam Dei cognitionem, in qua aeterna beatitudo consistit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0053","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0054","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0055","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0056","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0057","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra doctrina non sit dignior aliis scientiis. Certitudo enim pertinet ad dignitatem scientiae. Sed aliae scientiae, de quarum principiis dubitari non potest, videntur esse certiores sacra doctrina, cuius principia, scilicet articuli fidei, dubitationem recipiunt. Aliae igitur scientiae videntur ista digniores.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0058","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0059","text":"Praeterea, inferioris scientiae est a superiori accipere, sicut musicus ab arithmetico. Sed sacra doctrina accipit aliquid a philosophicis disciplinis, dicit enim Hieronymus in epistola ad magnum oratorem urbis Romae, quod *doctores antiqui intantum philosophorum doctrinis atque sententiis suos resperserunt libros, ut nescias quid in illis prius admirari debeas, eruditionem saeculi, an scientiam Scripturarum*. Ergo sacra doctrina est inferior aliis scientiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0060","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0061","text":"Sed contra est quod aliae scientiae dicuntur ancillae huius, Prov. IX, *misit ancillas suas vocare ad arcem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0062","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum ista scientia quantum ad aliquid sit speculativa, et quantum ad aliquid sit practica, omnes alias transcendit tam speculativas quam practicas. Speculativarum enim scientiarum una altera dignior dicitur, tum propter certitudinem, tum propter dignitatem materiae. Et quantum ad utrumque, haec scientia alias speculativas scientias excedit. Secundum certitudinem quidem, quia aliae scientiae certitudinem habent ex naturali lumine rationis humanae, quae potest errare, haec autem certitudinem habet ex lumine divinae scientiae, quae decipi non potest. Secundum dignitatem vero materiae, quia ista scientia est principaliter de his quae sua altitudine rationem transcendunt, aliae vero scientiae considerant ea tantum quae rationi subduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","text":"Practicarum vero scientiarum illa dignior est, quae ad ulteriorem finem ordinatur, sicut civilis militari, nam bonum exercitus ad bonum civitatis ordinatur. Finis autem huius doctrinae inquantum est practica, est beatitudo aeterna, ad quam sicut ad ultimum finem ordinantur omnes alii fines scientiarum practicarum. Unde manifestum est, secundum omnem modum, eam digniorem esse aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0065","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod nihil prohibet id quod est certius secundum naturam, esse quoad nos minus certum, propter debilitatem intellectus nostri, qui se habet ad manifestissima naturae, sicut oculus noctuae ad lumen solis, sicut dicitur in II Metaphys. Unde dubitatio quae accidit in aliquibus circa articulos fidei, non est propter incertitudinem rei, sed propter debilitatem intellectus humani. Et tamen minimum quod potest haberi de cognitione rerum altissimarum, desiderabilius est quam certissima cognitio quae habetur de minimis rebus, ut dicitur in XI de animalibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0067","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod haec scientia accipere potest aliquid a philosophicis disciplinis, non quod ex necessitate eis indigeat, sed ad maiorem manifestationem eorum quae in hac scientia traduntur. Non enim accipit sua principia ab aliis scientiis, sed immediate a Deo per revelationem. Et ideo non accipit ab aliis scientiis tanquam a superioribus, sed utitur eis tanquam inferioribus et ancillis; sicut architectonicae utuntur subministrantibus, ut civilis militari. Et hoc ipsum quod sic utitur eis, non est propter defectum vel insufficientiam eius, sed propter defectum intellectus nostri; qui ex his quae per naturalem rationem (ex qua procedunt aliae scientiae) cognoscuntur, facilius manuducitur in ea quae sunt supra rationem, quae in hac scientia traduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0069","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0070","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0071","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod haec doctrina non sit sapientia. Nulla enim doctrina quae supponit sua principia aliunde, digna est nomine sapientiae, *quia sapientis est ordinare, et non ordinari* (I Metaphys.). Sed haec doctrina supponit principia sua aliunde, ut ex dictis patet. Ergo haec doctrina non est sapientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0072","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0073","text":"Praeterea, ad sapientiam pertinet probare principia aliarum scientiarum, unde ut caput dicitur scientiarum, ut VI Ethic. patet. Sed haec doctrina non probat principia aliarum scientiarum. Ergo non est sapientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0074","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0075","text":"Praeterea, haec doctrina per studium acquiritur. Sapientia autem per infusionem habetur, unde inter septem dona spiritus sancti connumeratur, ut patet Isaiae XI. Ergo haec doctrina non est sapientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0076","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0077","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Deut. IV, in principio legis, *haec est nostra sapientia et intellectus coram populis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0078","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0079","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod haec doctrina maxime sapientia est inter omnes sapientias humanas, non quidem in aliquo genere tantum, sed simpliciter. Cum enim sapientis sit ordinare et iudicare, iudicium autem per altiorem causam de inferioribus habeatur; ille sapiens dicitur in unoquoque genere, qui considerat causam altissimam illius generis. Ut in genere aedificii, artifex qui disponit formam domus, dicitur sapiens et architector, respectu inferiorum artificum, qui dolant ligna vel parant lapides, unde dicitur I Cor. III, *ut sapiens architector fundamentum posui*. Et rursus, in genere totius humanae vitae, prudens sapiens dicitur, inquantum ordinat humanos actus ad debitum finem, unde dicitur Prov. X, *sapientia est viro prudentia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","text":"Ille igitur qui considerat simpliciter altissimam causam totius universi, quae Deus est, maxime sapiens dicitur, unde et sapientia dicitur esse divinorum cognitio, ut patet per Augustinum, XII de Trinitate. Sacra autem doctrina propriissime determinat de Deo secundum quod est altissima causa, quia non solum quantum ad illud quod est per creaturas cognoscibile (quod philosophi cognoverunt, ut dicitur Rom. I, *quod notum est Dei, manifestum est illis*); sed etiam quantum ad id quod notum est sibi soli de seipso, et aliis per revelationem communicatum. Unde sacra doctrina maxime dicitur sapientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0081","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0082","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sacra doctrina non supponit sua principia ab aliqua scientia humana, sed a scientia divina, a qua, sicut a summa sapientia, omnis nostra cognitio ordinatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0083","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliarum scientiarum principia vel sunt per se nota, et probari non possunt, vel per aliquam rationem naturalem probantur in aliqua alia scientia. Propria autem huius scientiae cognitio est, quae est per revelationem, non autem quae est per naturalem rationem. Et ideo non pertinet ad eam probare principia aliarum scientiarum, sed solum iudicare de eis, quidquid enim in aliis scientiis invenitur veritati huius scientiae repugnans, totum condemnatur ut falsum, unde dicitur II Cor. X, *consilia destruentes, et omnem altitudinem extollentem se adversus scientiam Dei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0085","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0086","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum iudicium ad sapientem pertineat, secundum duplicem modum iudicandi, dupliciter sapientia accipitur. Contingit enim aliquem iudicare, uno modo per modum inclinationis, sicut qui habet habitum virtutis, recte iudicat de his quae sunt secundum virtutem agenda, inquantum ad illa inclinatur, unde et in X Ethic. dicitur quod virtuosus est mensura et regula actuum humanorum. Alio modo, per modum cognitionis, sicut aliquis instructus in scientia morali, posset iudicare de actibus virtutis, etiam si virtutem non haberet. Primus igitur modus iudicandi de rebus divinis, pertinet ad sapientiam quae ponitur donum spiritus sancti secundum illud I Cor. II, *spiritualis homo iudicat omnia,* etc., et Dionysius dicit, II cap. de divinis nominibus, *Hierotheus doctus est non solum discens, sed et patiens divina*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0087","text":"Secundus autem modus iudicandi pertinet ad hanc doctrinam, secundum quod per studium habetur; licet eius principia ex revelatione habeantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0088","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0089","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0090","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit subiectum huius scientiae. In qualibet enim scientia oportet supponere de subiecto quid est, secundum philosophum in I Poster. Sed haec scientia non supponit de Deo quid est, dicit enim Damascenus, *in Deo quid est, dicere impossibile est*. Ergo Deus non est subiectum huius scientiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0091","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0092","text":"Praeterea, omnia quae determinantur in aliqua scientia, comprehenduntur sub subiecto illius scientiae. Sed in sacra Scriptura determinatur de multis aliis quam de Deo, puta de creaturis, et de moribus hominum. Ergo Deus non est subiectum huius scientiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0093","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0094","text":"Sed contra, illud est subiectum scientiae, de quo est sermo in scientia. Sed in hac scientia fit sermo de Deo, dicitur enim theologia, quasi sermo de Deo. Ergo Deus est subiectum huius scientiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0095","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus est subiectum huius scientiae. Sic enim se habet subiectum ad scientiam, sicut obiectum ad potentiam vel habitum. Proprie autem illud assignatur obiectum alicuius potentiae vel habitus, sub cuius ratione omnia referuntur ad potentiam vel habitum, sicut homo et lapis referuntur ad visum inquantum sunt colorata, unde coloratum est proprium obiectum visus. Omnia autem pertractantur in sacra doctrina sub ratione Dei, vel quia sunt ipse Deus; vel quia habent ordinem ad Deum, ut ad principium et finem. Unde sequitur quod Deus vere sit subiectum huius scientiae. Quod etiam manifestum fit ex principiis huius scientiae, quae sunt articuli fidei, quae est de Deo, idem autem est subiectum principiorum et totius scientiae, cum tota scientia virtute contineatur in principiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0097","text":"Quidam vero, attendentes ad ea quae in ista scientia tractantur, et non ad rationem secundum quam considerantur, assignaverunt aliter subiectum huius scientiae, vel res et signa; vel opera reparationis; vel totum Christum, idest caput et membra. De omnibus enim istis tractatur in ista scientia, sed secundum ordinem ad Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0098","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet de Deo non possimus scire quid est, utimur tamen eius effectu, in hac doctrina, vel naturae vel gratiae, loco definitionis, ad ea quae de Deo in hac doctrina considerantur, sicut et in aliquibus scientiis philosophicis demonstratur aliquid de causa per effectum, accipiendo effectum loco definitionis causae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0100","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0101","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod omnia alia quae determinantur in sacra doctrina, comprehenduntur sub Deo, non ut partes vel species vel accidentia, sed ut ordinata aliqualiter ad ipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0102","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0103","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0104","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod haec doctrina non sit argumentativa. Dicit enim Ambrosius in libro I de fide Catholica, *tolle argumenta, ubi fides quaeritur*. Sed in hac doctrina praecipue fides quaeritur, unde dicitur Ioan. XX, *haec scripta sunt ut credatis*. Ergo sacra doctrina non est argumentativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0105","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0106","text":"Praeterea, si sit argumentativa, aut argumentatur ex auctoritate, aut ex ratione. Si ex auctoritate, non videtur hoc congruere eius dignitati, nam locus ab auctoritate est infirmissimus, secundum Boetium. Si etiam ex ratione, hoc non congruit eius fini, quia secundum Gregorium in homilia, *fides non habet meritum, ubi humana ratio praebet experimentum*. Ergo sacra doctrina non est argumentativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0107","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0108","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur ad Titum I, de episcopo, *amplectentem eum qui secundum doctrinam est, fidelem sermonem, ut potens sit exhortari in doctrina sana, et eos qui contradicunt arguere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0109","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut aliae scientiae non argumentantur ad sua principia probanda, sed ex principiis argumentantur ad ostendendum alia in ipsis scientiis; ita haec doctrina non argumentatur ad sua principia probanda, quae sunt articuli fidei; sed ex eis procedit ad aliquid aliud ostendendum; sicut apostolus, I ad Cor. XV, ex resurrectione Christi argumentatur ad resurrectionem communem probandam. Sed tamen considerandum est in scientiis philosophicis, quod inferiores scientiae nec probant sua principia, nec contra negantem principia disputant, sed hoc relinquunt superiori scientiae, suprema vero inter eas, scilicet metaphysica, disputat contra negantem sua principia, si adversarius aliquid concedit, si autem nihil concedit, non potest cum eo disputare, potest tamen solvere rationes ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","text":"Unde sacra Scriptura, cum non habeat superiorem, disputat cum negante sua principia, argumentando quidem, si adversarius aliquid concedat eorum quae per divinam revelationem habentur; sicut per auctoritates sacrae doctrinae disputamus contra haereticos, et per unum articulum contra negantes alium. Si vero adversarius nihil credat eorum quae divinitus revelantur, non remanet amplius via ad probandum articulos fidei per rationes, sed ad solvendum rationes, si quas inducit, contra fidem. Cum enim fides infallibili veritati innitatur, impossibile autem sit de vero demonstrari contrarium, manifestum est probationes quae contra fidem inducuntur, non esse demonstrationes, sed solubilia argumenta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0112","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet argumenta rationis humanae non habeant locum ad probandum quae fidei sunt, tamen ex articulis fidei haec doctrina ad alia argumentatur, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0114","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod argumentari ex auctoritate est maxime proprium huius doctrinae, eo quod principia huius doctrinae per revelationem habentur, et sic oportet quod credatur auctoritati eorum quibus revelatio facta est. Nec hoc derogat dignitati huius doctrinae, nam licet locus ab auctoritate quae fundatur super ratione humana, sit infirmissimus; locus tamen ab auctoritate quae fundatur super revelatione divina, est efficacissimus. Utitur tamen sacra doctrina etiam ratione humana, non quidem ad probandum fidem, quia per hoc tolleretur meritum fidei; sed ad manifestandum aliqua alia quae traduntur in hac doctrina. Cum enim gratia non tollat naturam, sed perficiat, oportet quod naturalis ratio subserviat fidei; sicut et naturalis inclinatio voluntatis obsequitur caritati. Unde et apostolus dicit, II ad Cor. X, *in captivitatem redigentes omnem intellectum in obsequium Christi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","text":"Et inde est quod etiam auctoritatibus philosophorum sacra doctrina utitur, ubi per rationem naturalem veritatem cognoscere potuerunt; sicut Paulus, actuum XVII, inducit verbum Arati, dicens, *sicut et quidam poetarum vestrorum dixerunt, genus Dei sumus*. Sed tamen sacra doctrina huiusmodi auctoritatibus utitur quasi extraneis argumentis, et probabilibus. Auctoritatibus autem canonicae Scripturae utitur proprie, ex necessitate argumentando. Auctoritatibus autem aliorum doctorum Ecclesiae, quasi arguendo ex propriis, sed probabiliter. Innititur enim fides nostra revelationi apostolis et prophetis factae, qui canonicos libros scripserunt, non autem revelationi, si qua fuit aliis doctoribus facta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0117","text":"Unde dicit Augustinus, in epistola ad Hieronymum, *solis eis Scripturarum libris qui canonici appellantur, didici hunc honorem deferre, ut nullum auctorem eorum in scribendo errasse aliquid firmissime credam. Alios autem ita lego, ut, quantalibet sanctitate doctrinaque praepolleant, non ideo verum putem, quod ipsi ita senserunt vel scripserunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0118","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0119","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0120","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra Scriptura non debeat uti metaphoris. Illud enim quod est proprium infimae doctrinae, non videtur competere huic scientiae, quae inter alias tenet locum supremum, ut iam dictum est. Procedere autem per similitudines varias et repraesentationes, est proprium poeticae, quae est infima inter omnes doctrinas. Ergo huiusmodi similitudinibus uti, non est conveniens huic scientiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0121","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0122","text":"Praeterea, haec doctrina videtur esse ordinata ad veritatis manifestationem, unde et manifestatoribus eius praemium promittitur, Eccli. XXIV, *qui elucidant me, vitam aeternam habebunt*. Sed per huiusmodi similitudines veritas occultatur. Non ergo competit huic doctrinae divina tradere sub similitudine corporalium rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0123","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0124","text":"Praeterea, quanto aliquae creaturae sunt sublimiores, tanto magis ad divinam similitudinem accedunt. Si igitur aliquae ex creaturis transumerentur ad Deum, tunc oporteret talem transumptionem maxime fieri ex sublimioribus creaturis, et non ex infimis. Quod tamen in Scripturis frequenter invenitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0125","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0126","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Osee XII, *ego visionem multiplicavi eis, et in manibus prophetarum assimilatus sum*. Tradere autem aliquid sub similitudine, est metaphoricum. Ergo ad sacram doctrinam pertinet uti metaphoris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0127","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod conveniens est sacrae Scripturae divina et spiritualia sub similitudine corporalium tradere. Deus enim omnibus providet secundum quod competit eorum naturae. Est autem naturale homini ut per sensibilia ad intelligibilia veniat, quia omnis nostra cognitio a sensu initium habet. Unde convenienter in sacra Scriptura traduntur nobis spiritualia sub metaphoris corporalium. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, I cap. caelestis hierarchiae, *impossibile est nobis aliter lucere divinum radium, nisi varietate sacrorum velaminum circumvelatum*. Convenit etiam sacrae Scripturae, quae communiter omnibus proponitur (secundum illud ad Rom. I, *sapientibus et insipientibus debitor sum*), ut spiritualia sub similitudinibus corporalium proponantur; ut saltem vel sic rudes eam capiant, qui ad intelligibilia secundum se capienda non sunt idonei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0129","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0130","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod poeta utitur metaphoris propter repraesentationem, repraesentatio enim naturaliter homini delectabilis est. Sed sacra doctrina utitur metaphoris propter necessitatem et utilitatem, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0131","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod radius divinae revelationis non destruitur propter figuras sensibiles quibus circumvelatur, ut dicit Dionysius, sed remanet in sua veritate; ut mentes quibus fit revelatio, non permittat in similitudinibus permanere, sed elevet eas ad cognitionem intelligibilium; et per eos quibus revelatio facta est, alii etiam circa haec instruantur. Unde ea quae in uno loco Scripturae traduntur sub metaphoris, in aliis locis expressius exponuntur. Et ipsa etiam occultatio figurarum utilis est, ad exercitium studiosorum, et contra irrisiones infidelium, de quibus dicitur, Matth. VII, *nolite sanctum dare canibus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0133","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut docet Dionysius, cap. II Cael. Hier., magis est conveniens quod divina in Scripturis tradantur sub figuris vilium corporum, quam corporum nobilium. Et hoc propter tria. Primo, quia per hoc magis liberatur humanus animus ab errore. Manifestum enim apparet quod haec secundum proprietatem non dicuntur de divinis, quod posset esse dubium, si sub figuris nobilium corporum describerentur divina; maxime apud illos qui nihil aliud a corporibus nobilius excogitare noverunt. Secundo, quia hic modus convenientior est cognitioni quam de Deo habemus in hac vita. Magis enim manifestatur nobis de ipso quid non est, quam quid est, et ideo similitudines illarum rerum quae magis elongantur a Deo, veriorem nobis faciunt aestimationem quod sit supra illud quod de Deo dicimus vel cogitamus. Tertio, quia per huiusmodi, divina magis occultantur indignis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0135","text":"## Articulus 10","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0136","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0137","text":"Ad decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sacra Scriptura sub una littera non habeat plures sensus, qui sunt historicus vel litteralis, allegoricus, tropologicus sive moralis, et anagogicus. Multiplicitas enim sensuum in una Scriptura parit confusionem et deceptionem, et tollit arguendi firmitatem, unde ex multiplicibus propositionibus non procedit argumentatio, sed secundum hoc aliquae fallaciae assignantur. Sacra autem Scriptura debet esse efficax ad ostendendam veritatem absque omni fallacia. Ergo non debent in ea sub una littera plures sensus tradi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0138","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0139","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit in libro de utilitate credendi, quod *Scriptura quae testamentum vetus vocatur, quadrifariam traditur, scilicet, secundum historiam, secundum aetiologiam, secundum analogiam, secundum allegoriam*. Quae quidem quatuor a quatuor praedictis videntur esse aliena omnino. Non igitur conveniens videtur quod eadem littera sacrae Scripturae secundum quatuor sensus praedictos exponatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0140","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0141","text":"Praeterea, praeter praedictos sensus, invenitur sensus parabolicus, qui inter illos sensus quatuor non continetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0142","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0143","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Gregorius, XX Moralium, *sacra Scriptura omnes scientias ipso locutionis suae more transcendit, quia uno eodemque sermone, dum narrat gestum, prodit mysterium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0144","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod auctor sacrae Scripturae est Deus, in cuius potestate est ut non solum voces ad significandum accommodet (quod etiam homo facere potest), sed etiam res ipsas. Et ideo, cum in omnibus scientiis voces significent, hoc habet proprium ista scientia, quod ipsae res significatae per voces, etiam significant aliquid. Illa ergo prima significatio, qua voces significant res, pertinet ad primum sensum, qui est sensus historicus vel litteralis. Illa vero significatio qua res significatae per voces, iterum res alias significant, dicitur sensus spiritualis; qui super litteralem fundatur, et eum supponit. Hic autem sensus spiritualis trifariam dividitur. Sicut enim dicit apostolus, ad Hebr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","text":"VII, lex vetus figura est novae legis, et ipsa nova lex, ut dicit Dionysius in ecclesiastica hierarchia, est figura futurae gloriae, in nova etiam lege, ea quae in capite sunt gesta, sunt signa eorum quae nos agere debemus. Secundum ergo quod ea quae sunt veteris legis, significant ea quae sunt novae legis, est sensus allegoricus, secundum vero quod ea quae in Christo sunt facta, vel in his quae Christum significant, sunt signa eorum quae nos agere debemus, est sensus moralis, prout vero significant ea quae sunt in aeterna gloria, est sensus anagogicus. Quia vero sensus litteralis est, quem auctor intendit, auctor autem sacrae Scripturae Deus est, qui omnia simul suo intellectu comprehendit, non est inconveniens, ut dicit Augustinus XII confessionum, si etiam secundum litteralem sensum in una littera Scripturae plures sint sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0147","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod multiplicitas horum sensuum non facit aequivocationem, aut aliam speciem multiplicitatis, quia, sicut iam dictum est, sensus isti non multiplicantur propter hoc quod una vox multa significet; sed quia ipsae res significatae per voces, aliarum rerum possunt esse signa. Et ita etiam nulla confusio sequitur in sacra Scriptura, cum omnes sensus fundentur super unum, scilicet litteralem; ex quo solo potest trahi argumentum, non autem ex his quae secundum allegoriam dicuntur, ut dicit Augustinus in epistola contra Vincentium Donatistam. Non tamen ex hoc aliquid deperit sacrae Scripturae, quia nihil sub spirituali sensu continetur fidei necessarium, quod Scriptura per litteralem sensum alicubi manifeste non tradat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0149","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0150","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illa tria, historia, aetiologia, analogia, ad unum litteralem sensum pertinent. Nam historia est, ut ipse Augustinus exponit, cum simpliciter aliquid proponitur, aetiologia vero, cum causa dicti assignatur, sicut cum dominus assignavit causam quare Moyses permisit licentiam repudiandi uxores, scilicet propter duritiam cordis ipsorum, Matt. XIX, analogia vero est, cum veritas unius Scripturae ostenditur veritati alterius non repugnare. Sola autem allegoria, inter illa quatuor, pro tribus spiritualibus sensibus ponitur. Sicut et Hugo de sancto Victore sub sensu allegorico etiam anagogicum comprehendit, ponens in tertio suarum sententiarum solum tres sensus, scilicet historicum, allegoricum et tropologicum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0151","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod sensus parabolicus sub litterali continetur, nam per voces significatur aliquid proprie, et aliquid figurative; nec est litteralis sensus ipsa figura, sed id quod est figuratum. Non enim cum Scriptura nominat Dei brachium, est litteralis sensus quod in Deo sit membrum huiusmodi corporale, sed id quod per hoc membrum significatur, scilicet virtus operativa. In quo patet quod sensui litterali sacrae Scripturae nunquam potest subesse falsum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9fd52799f342f5257d4e72135c68b65cb15254ab8e5cb729a517ef485238c6e6","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T133409496593Z_ee138deb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 1","source_sha256":"9fd52799f342f5257d4e72135c68b65cb15254ab8e5cb729a517ef485238c6e6","sha256":"3a2421f5a976c4cef4f61146145aa97626ee7843cfe866dded536c5e7dc3c22d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:34:09.496651+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0002","text":"[I q. 1 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003","text":"And so that our intention may be comprehended within certain definite limits, it is necessary first to inquire concerning sacred doctrine itself: what sort of thing it is, and to what things it extends. Concerning these, ten questions must be asked. First, concerning the necessity of this doctrine. Second, whether it is a science. Third, whether it is one or many. Fourth, whether it is speculative or practical. Fifth, concerning its comparison with the other sciences. Sixth, whether it is wisdom. Seventh, what its subject is. Eighth, whether it is argumentative. Ninth, whether it ought to employ metaphorical or symbolic expressions. Tenth, whether the sacred Scripture of this doctrine ought to be expounded according to several senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0005","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that, besides the philosophical disciplines, it is not necessary to have another doctrine. For man ought not to endeavor after those things which are above reason, according to that saying of Ecclesiasticus III, *Seek not things higher than thyself*. But those things which are subject to reason are sufficiently handed down in the philosophical disciplines. Therefore it seems superfluous, besides the philosophical disciplines, to have another doctrine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0007","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0008","text":"Moreover, doctrine can be only about being, for nothing is known except the true, which is convertible with being. But all beings are treated of in the philosophical disciplines, and likewise God; hence a certain part of philosophy is called theology, or divine science, as is clear from the Philosopher in VI *Metaphysics*. Therefore it was not necessary, besides the philosophical disciplines, to have another doctrine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0009","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0010","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in II Timothy III: *All Scripture divinely inspired is useful for teaching, for arguing, for correcting, for instructing in righteousness*. But Scripture divinely inspired does not belong to the philosophical disciplines, which have been discovered according to human reason. Therefore it is useful that, besides the philosophical disciplines, there should be another science divinely inspired.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0011","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","text":"I answer that it must be said that, for human salvation, it was necessary that there be a certain doctrine according to divine revelation, besides the philosophical disciplines, which are investigated by human reason. First indeed, because man is ordered to God as to an end that exceeds the grasp of reason, according to that saying of Isaiah LXIV: *The eye hath not seen, O God, besides thee, the things which thou hast prepared for those who love thee*. But the end must be pre-known by men, who ought to order their intentions and actions toward the end. Hence it was necessary for man’s salvation that certain things exceeding human reason be made known to him through divine revelation. Even concerning those things which can be investigated about God by human reason, it was necessary for man to be instructed by divine revelation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","text":"For truth concerning God, investigated by reason, would come to man through few persons, after a long time, and with an admixture of many errors; yet upon the knowledge of this truth depends the whole salvation of man, which is in God. Therefore, in order that salvation might come to men both more suitably and more certainly, it was necessary that they be instructed concerning divine things by divine revelation. Therefore it was necessary that, besides the philosophical disciplines, which are investigated by reason, sacred doctrine be had through revelation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0014","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0015","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although those things which are higher than man’s knowledge are not to be inquired into by man through reason, nevertheless, once revealed by God, they are to be received through faith. Hence there is added in the same place: *Many things above the sense of men have been shown to thee*. And sacred doctrine consists in things of this sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0016","text":"[I q. 1 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","text":"To the second, it must be said that a different aspect of knowability brings about a diversity of sciences. For the astronomer and the natural philosopher demonstrate the same conclusion—for example, that the earth is round—but the astronomer through a mathematical middle term, that is, one abstracted from matter; whereas the natural philosopher through a middle term considered in relation to matter. Hence nothing prevents another science from treating of the same things of which the philosophical disciplines treat insofar as they are knowable by the light of natural reason, insofar as they are known by the light of divine revelation. Hence the theology that belongs to sacred doctrine differs in genus from that theology which is posited as a part of philosophy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0018","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0019","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0020","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that sacred doctrine is not a science. For every science proceeds from principles known through themselves. But sacred doctrine proceeds from the articles of faith, which are not known through themselves, since they are not conceded by everyone: *for faith is not of all*, as is said in II Thessalonians III. Therefore sacred doctrine is not a science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0021","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0022","text":"Moreover, science is not concerned with singulars. But sacred doctrine treats of singulars, for example, of the deeds of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and the like. Therefore sacred doctrine is not a science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0023","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0024","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in XIV *On the Trinity*: *To this science is attributed only that by which most salutary faith is begotten, nourished, defended, and strengthened*. But this belongs to no science except sacred doctrine. Therefore sacred doctrine is a science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0025","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","text":"I answer that sacred doctrine is a science. But it should be known that there are two kinds of sciences. For some proceed from principles known by the natural light of the intellect, such as arithmetic, geometry, and the like. But some proceed from principles known by the light of a higher science, as perspective proceeds from principles made known through geometry, and music from principles known through arithmetic. And in this way sacred doctrine is a science, because it proceeds from principles known by the light of a higher science, namely, the science of God and of the blessed. Hence, just as music believes the principles handed down to it by the arithmetician, so sacred doctrine believes the principles revealed to it by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0027","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the principles of any science are either self-evident or are reduced to the knowledge of a higher science. And such are the principles of sacred doctrine, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0029","text":"[I q. 1 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0030","text":"To the second it must be said that singulars are treated in sacred doctrine, not because they are dealt with principally, but because they are introduced both as examples of life, as in the moral sciences, and also for declaring the authority of the men through whom divine revelation came down to us, upon which sacred Scripture or doctrine is founded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0031","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0032","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0033","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that sacred doctrine is not one science. For according to the Philosopher in I Posterior Analytics, *one science is that which is of one genus of subject*. But Creator and creature, which are treated of in sacred doctrine, are not contained under one genus of subject. Therefore sacred doctrine is not one science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0034","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0035","text":"Moreover, sacred doctrine treats of angels, bodily creatures, and human morals. But such matters pertain to diverse philosophical sciences. Therefore sacred doctrine is not one science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0036","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0037","text":"But on the contrary, sacred Scripture speaks of it as one science; for it is said in Wisdom X, *he gave him the science of the saints*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0038","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","text":"I answer that sacred doctrine is one science. For the unity of a power and of a habit is to be considered according to the object, not indeed materially, but according to the formal account of the object; thus a human being, an ass, and a stone agree in one formal account of being colored, which is the object of sight. Therefore, since sacred Scripture considers certain things insofar as they are divinely revealed, as has been said, whatever things are divinely revealable share in one formal account of the object of this science. And therefore they are comprehended under sacred doctrine as under one science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0040","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that sacred doctrine does not determine concerning God and creatures equally, but concerning God principally, and concerning creatures insofar as they are referred to God as to a principle or end. Hence the unity of the science is not impeded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0042","text":"[I q. 1 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0043","text":"To the second it must be said that nothing prevents lower powers or habits from being diversified with respect to those matters which commonly fall under one higher power or habit, because a higher power or habit regards its object under a more universal formal account. Thus the object of common sense is the sensible, which comprehends under itself the visible and the audible; hence common sense, since it is one power, extends itself to all the objects of the five senses. And likewise, the things treated in diverse philosophical sciences can be considered by sacred doctrine, though it is one, under one account, namely insofar as they are divinely revealable; so that sacred doctrine may be as it were a certain imprint of the divine science, which is one and simple concerning all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0044","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0045","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0046","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that sacred doctrine is a practical science. For the end of the practical is operation, according to the Philosopher in II Metaphysics. But sacred doctrine is ordered to operation, according to that saying of James I, *be doers of the word, and not hearers only*. Therefore sacred doctrine is a practical science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0047","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0048","text":"Moreover, sacred doctrine is divided by the old and the new law. But law pertains to moral science, which is a practical science. Therefore sacred doctrine is a practical science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0049","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0050","text":"On the contrary, every practical science concerns things workable by man; thus moral science concerns human acts, and building science buildings. But sacred doctrine is principally about God, of whom men are rather the works. Therefore it is not a practical science, but rather a speculative one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0051","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","text":"I answer that sacred doctrine, as has been said, while being one, extends to those things that pertain to the various philosophical sciences, on account of the formal aspect which it considers in diverse things, namely, insofar as they are knowable by the divine light. Hence, although among the philosophical sciences one is speculative and another practical, sacred doctrine nevertheless comprehends both under itself; just as God knows both himself and the things that he makes by the same knowledge. Yet it is more speculative than practical, because it treats more principally of divine things than of human acts; and it treats of the latter insofar as through them man is ordered to the perfect knowledge of God, in which eternal beatitude consists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0053","text":"[I q. 1 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0054","text":"And through this the answer to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0055","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0056","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0057","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that sacred doctrine is not more worthy than the other sciences. For certainty pertains to the worthiness of a science. But the other sciences, whose principles cannot be doubted, seem to be more certain than sacred doctrine, whose principles, namely the articles of faith, admit doubt. Therefore the other sciences seem more worthy than this one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0058","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0059","text":"Further, it belongs to an inferior science to receive from a superior one, as the musician receives from the arithmetician. But sacred doctrine receives something from the philosophical disciplines; for Jerome says in his letter to Magnus, orator of the city of Rome, that *the ancient doctors sprinkled their books with the teachings and opinions of philosophers to such an extent that you would not know what you ought first to admire in them: the learning of the age or the knowledge of the Scriptures*. Therefore sacred doctrine is inferior to the other sciences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0060","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0061","text":"On the contrary, the other sciences are called the handmaids of this one: Proverbs IX, *she sent her handmaids to call to the citadel*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0062","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","text":"I answer that, since this science is speculative in one respect and practical in another, it transcends all the others, both speculative and practical. For among speculative sciences one is said to be more worthy than another, both on account of certainty and on account of the dignity of the subject matter. And in both respects this science surpasses the other speculative sciences. As to certainty, indeed, the other sciences have certainty from the natural light of human reason, which can err; but this one has certainty from the light of divine knowledge, which cannot be deceived. As to the dignity of the subject matter, however, this science is principally about those things which, by their loftiness, transcend reason, whereas the other sciences consider only those things subject to reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","text":"Among practical sciences, moreover, that one is more worthy which is ordered to a further end, as civil science is to military science; for the good of the army is ordered to the good of the state. But the end of this doctrine, insofar as it is practical, is eternal beatitude, to which, as to their ultimate end, all the other ends of the practical sciences are ordered. Hence it is evident that, in every respect, it is more worthy than the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0065","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that nothing prevents what is more certain by nature from being less certain to us, on account of the weakness of our intellect, which stands in relation to the most evident things of nature as the eye of an owl to the light of the sun, as is said in Metaphysics II. Hence the doubt that occurs in some persons concerning the articles of faith is not due to uncertainty in the thing, but to the weakness of the human intellect. And yet the least that can be had of the knowledge of the highest things is more desirable than the most certain knowledge that is had of the least things, as is said in On Animals XI.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0067","text":"[I q. 1 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","text":"To the second, it must be said that this science can receive something from the philosophical disciplines, not because it needs them of necessity, but for the greater manifestation of those things transmitted in this science. For it does not receive its principles from other sciences, but immediately from God through revelation. And therefore it does not receive from the other sciences as from superiors, but uses them as inferiors and handmaids; just as architectonic sciences use subordinate ones, as civil science uses military science. And its very use of them in this way is not on account of any defect or insufficiency in it, but on account of the defect of our intellect; which is more easily led by the hand, from those things known through natural reason (from which the other sciences proceed), to those things that are above reason, which are transmitted in this science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0069","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0070","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0071","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that this doctrine is not wisdom. For no doctrine that presupposes its principles from elsewhere is worthy of the name of wisdom, *because it belongs to the wise man to order, and not to be ordered* (Metaphysics I). But this doctrine presupposes its principles from elsewhere, as is clear from what has been said. Therefore this doctrine is not wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0072","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0073","text":"Moreover, it belongs to wisdom to prove the principles of the other sciences; hence it is called the head of the sciences, as is clear in Ethics VI. But this doctrine does not prove the principles of the other sciences. Therefore it is not wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0074","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0075","text":"Moreover, this doctrine is acquired by study. But wisdom is had by infusion; hence it is numbered among the seven gifts of the Holy Spirit, as is clear from Isaiah XI. Therefore this doctrine is not wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0076","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0077","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Deuteronomy IV, at the beginning of the Law, *this is our wisdom and understanding before the peoples*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0078","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0079","text":"I answer that it must be said that this doctrine is wisdom in the highest degree among all human wisdoms, not indeed in some particular genus only, but simply. For since it belongs to the wise person to order and to judge, and judgment concerning lower things is obtained through a higher cause, that person is called wise in each genus who considers the highest cause of that genus. Thus, in the genus of building, the craftsman who disposes the form of a house is called wise and architect, in relation to the lower craftsmen who hew wood or prepare stones; hence it is said in I Corinthians III, *as a wise architect I laid the foundation*. And again, in the genus of the whole of human life, the prudent person is called wise insofar as he orders human acts to their due end; hence it is said in Proverbs X, *wisdom is prudence to a man*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","text":"Therefore, he who considers simply the highest cause of the whole universe, which is God, is called most wise; hence wisdom too is said to be the knowledge of divine things, as is clear from Augustine, On the Trinity XII. But sacred doctrine treats most properly of God insofar as He is the highest cause: because it treats not only of that which is knowable through creatures (which the philosophers knew, as is said in Romans I, *what is known of God is manifest to them*), but also of that which is known to Himself alone about Himself, and communicated to others through revelation. Hence sacred doctrine is called wisdom in the highest degree.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0081","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0082","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that sacred doctrine does not presuppose its principles from any human science, but from divine knowledge, by which, as by the highest wisdom, all our knowledge is ordered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0083","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","text":"To the second it must be said that the principles of the other sciences are either self-evident and cannot be proved, or are proved by some natural reason in some other science. But the proper knowledge of this science is that which is through revelation, not that which is through natural reason. And therefore it does not belong to it to prove the principles of the other sciences, but only to judge concerning them: for whatever is found in the other sciences contrary to the truth of this science is altogether condemned as false; hence it is said in II Corinthians X, *destroying counsels, and every height that exalts itself against the knowledge of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0085","text":"[I q. 1 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0086","text":"To the third it must be said that, since judgment belongs to the wise person, wisdom is understood in two ways according to a twofold mode of judging. For it happens that someone judges in one way by way of inclination, as one who has the habit of virtue judges rightly concerning those things that are to be done according to virtue, insofar as he is inclined toward them; hence it is also said in Ethics X that the virtuous person is the measure and rule of human acts. In another way, by way of knowledge, as someone instructed in moral science could judge concerning acts of virtue even if he did not possess virtue. Therefore, the first mode of judging concerning divine things belongs to the wisdom that is placed among the gifts of the Holy Spirit, according to that of I Corinthians II, *the spiritual man judges all things*, etc.; and Dionysius says, in chapter II of On the Divine Names, *Hierotheus was taught not only by learning, but also by suffering divine things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0087","text":"But the second mode of judging belongs to this doctrine, insofar as it is had through study, although its principles are had from revelation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0088","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0089","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0090","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that God is not the subject of this science. For in every science one must presuppose concerning the subject what it is, according to the Philosopher in Posterior Analytics I. But this science does not presuppose concerning God what He is, for Damascene says, *in God, to say what He is is impossible*. Therefore God is not the subject of this science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0091","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0092","text":"Moreover, all the things that are treated in some science are comprehended under the subject of that science. But in sacred Scripture there is treatment of many things other than God, namely, of creatures and of human morals. Therefore God is not the subject of this science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0093","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0094","text":"On the contrary, that is the subject of a science concerning which discourse is made in the science. But in this science discourse is made concerning God, for it is called theology, as though discourse concerning God. Therefore God is the subject of this science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0095","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","text":"I answer that it must be said that God is the subject of this science. For the relation of subject to science is like that of object to power or habit. But that is properly assigned as the object of a power or habit under the account of which all things are referred to the power or habit; just as man and stone are referred to sight insofar as they are colored, whence the colored is the proper object of sight. But all things are treated in sacred doctrine under the account of God, either because they are God Himself, or because they have an order to God as to their principle and end. Hence it follows that God is truly the subject of this science. This is also made manifest from the principles of this science, which are the articles of faith, and faith is concerning God. But the subject of the principles and of the whole science is the same, since the whole science is contained virtually in its principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0097","text":"But some, attending to the things treated in this science and not to the mode according to which they are considered, have assigned the subject of this science otherwise: either things and signs; or the works of restoration; or the whole Christ, that is, head and members. For all these are treated in this science, but according to their order to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0098","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although we cannot know of God what He is, nevertheless in this doctrine we use His effect, whether of nature or of grace, in place of a definition, for those matters which are considered concerning God in this doctrine; just as in certain philosophical sciences something concerning a cause is demonstrated through an effect, taking the effect in place of the definition of the cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0100","text":"[I q. 1 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0101","text":"To the second, it must be said that all the other things determined in sacred doctrine are comprehended under God, not as parts or species or accidents, but as ordered in some way to Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0102","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0103","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0104","text":"The eighth is approached thus. It seems that this doctrine is not argumentative. For Ambrose says in Book I *On the Catholic Faith*, *remove arguments where faith is sought*. But in this doctrine faith is sought especially; hence it is said in John 20, *these things are written that you may believe*. Therefore sacred doctrine is not argumentative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0105","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0106","text":"Moreover, if it is argumentative, it argues either from authority or from reason. If from authority, this does not seem to befit its dignity, for an argument from authority is the weakest, according to Boethius. And if from reason, this does not befit its end, because according to Gregory in a homily, *faith has no merit where human reason furnishes proof*. Therefore sacred doctrine is not argumentative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0107","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0108","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Titus 1 concerning a bishop, *embracing the faithful word which is according to doctrine, that he may be able to exhort in sound doctrine and to refute those who contradict*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0109","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","text":"I answer that, just as the other sciences do not argue in order to prove their principles, but argue from principles in order to show other things in those sciences, so this doctrine does not argue in order to prove its principles, which are the articles of faith; but proceeds from them to show something else, as the Apostle, in 1 Corinthians 15, argues from Christ's resurrection to prove the common resurrection. Yet it must be considered concerning the philosophical sciences that the lower sciences neither prove their principles nor dispute against one denying the principles, but leave this to a higher science; whereas the highest among them, namely metaphysics, disputes against one denying its principles, if the adversary grants something; but if he grants nothing, it cannot dispute with him, though it can resolve his arguments.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","text":"Hence sacred Scripture, since it has no superior, disputes with one denying its principles, indeed by arguing, if the adversary grants something of those things held through divine revelation; just as through the authorities of sacred doctrine we dispute against heretics, and through one article against those denying another. But if the adversary believes nothing of those things divinely revealed, there remains no further way to prove the articles of faith by reasons, but only to resolve whatever reasons he may adduce against the faith. For since faith rests upon infallible truth, and since it is impossible for the contrary of the true to be demonstrated, it is evident that the proofs adduced against faith are not demonstrations, but arguments capable of being resolved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0112","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although arguments of human reason have no place in proving matters of faith, nevertheless this doctrine argues from the articles of faith to other things, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0114","text":"[I q. 1 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","text":"To the second, it must be said that to argue from authority is most proper to this doctrine, because the principles of this doctrine are held through revelation, and thus it is necessary to believe the authority of those to whom revelation was made. Nor does this detract from the dignity of this doctrine; for although an argument from authority founded upon human reason is the weakest, an argument from authority founded upon divine revelation is the most efficacious. Yet sacred doctrine also uses human reason, not indeed to prove faith, because by this the merit of faith would be taken away, but to make manifest certain other things handed down in this doctrine. For since grace does not destroy nature but perfects it, natural reason must be subordinate to faith, just as the natural inclination of the will obeys charity. Hence the Apostle also says, in 2 Corinthians 10, *bringing every understanding into captivity unto the obedience of Christ*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","text":"And from this it is that sacred doctrine also uses the authorities of philosophers, where they could know the truth through natural reason; as Paul, in Acts 17, adduces a saying of Aratus, saying, *as certain also of your poets have said, we are the offspring of God*. Yet sacred doctrine uses such authorities as extraneous and probable arguments. But it uses the authorities of canonical Scripture properly, arguing from them of necessity. It uses the authorities of the other doctors of the Church as arguing from proper authorities, but probabilistically. For our faith rests upon the revelation made to the apostles and prophets, who wrote the canonical books, but not upon a revelation, if any was made, to other doctors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0117","text":"Hence Augustine says, in his letter to Jerome, *I have learned to render this honor to those books of Scripture alone which are called canonical: that I most firmly believe that none of their authors erred in writing anything. But I read others in such a way that, however much they may excel in holiness and learning, I do not therefore think something true because they themselves have so thought or written*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0118","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0119","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0120","text":"The ninth is approached thus. It seems that sacred Scripture ought not to use metaphors. For that which is proper to the lowest doctrine does not seem to befit this science, which holds the highest place among the others, as has already been said. But to proceed through various likenesses and representations is proper to poetry, which is the lowest among all doctrines. Therefore, to use such likenesses is not fitting for this science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0121","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0122","text":"Moreover, this doctrine seems to be ordered toward the manifestation of truth; hence a reward is promised also to its elucidators: Sirach 24, *those who make me clear will have eternal life*. But through such likenesses truth is concealed. Therefore it does not befit this doctrine to convey divine things under a likeness of bodily things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0123","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0124","text":"Moreover, the more sublime certain creatures are, the more they approach the divine likeness. Therefore, if any things from creatures were transferred to God, such a transfer ought especially to be made from the more sublime creatures, and not from the lowest. Yet the contrary is frequently found in the Scriptures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0125","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0126","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Hosea 12: *I have multiplied vision for them, and in the hands of the prophets I have been made like*. But to convey something under a likeness is metaphorical. Therefore it belongs to sacred doctrine to use metaphors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0127","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","text":"I answer that it is fitting for sacred Scripture to convey divine and spiritual things under the likeness of bodily things. For God provides for all things according as befits their nature. Now it is natural to man to come to intelligible things through sensible things, because all our knowledge takes its beginning from sense. Hence, fittingly, in sacred Scripture spiritual things are conveyed to us under metaphors of bodily things. And this is what Dionysius says, in chapter 1 of *The Celestial Hierarchy*: *it is impossible for the divine ray to shine upon us otherwise than enveloped in the variety of sacred veils*. It also befits sacred Scripture, which is set forth commonly to all (according to that in Romans 1, *I am debtor both to the wise and to the unwise*), that spiritual things be set forth under likenesses of bodily things, so that at least in this way the unlearned may grasp it, who are not suited to grasp intelligible things in themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0129","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0130","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the poet uses metaphors for the sake of representation, for representation is naturally pleasing to man. But sacred doctrine uses metaphors because of necessity and usefulness, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0131","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132","text":"To the second, it must be said that the ray of divine revelation is not destroyed on account of the sensible figures by which it is enveloped, as Dionysius says; rather, it remains in its truth, so that it does not permit the minds to whom revelation is made to remain in the likenesses, but raises them to knowledge of intelligible things; and so that, through those to whom revelation has been made, others also may be instructed concerning these matters. Hence those things which are conveyed under metaphors in one place of Scripture are explained more expressly in other places. And the very concealment by figures is also useful, for the exercise of students and against the mockery of unbelievers, concerning whom it is said, Matthew 7: *do not give what is holy to dogs*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0133","text":"[I q. 1 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as Dionysius teaches in chapter 2 of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, it is more fitting that divine things be conveyed in the Scriptures under figures of lowly bodies than of noble bodies. And this is for three reasons. First, because through this the human mind is more freed from error. For it is plainly apparent that these things are not said of divine things according to their proper meaning, whereas this could be doubtful if divine things were described under figures of noble bodies—especially among those who know how to conceive of nothing nobler than bodies. Second, because this mode is more fitting to the knowledge that we have of God in this life. For what he is not is made more manifest to us than what he is; and therefore likenesses of those things that are more distant from God make us judge more truly that he is above that which we say or think of God. Third, because through such things divine matters are more concealed from the unworthy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0135","text":"## Article 10","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0136","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0137","text":"We proceed thus to the tenth article. It seems that sacred Scripture does not have, under one letter, several senses: namely, the historical or literal, the allegorical, the tropological or moral, and the anagogical. For a multiplicity of senses in one Scripture gives rise to confusion and deception, and removes the firmness of argumentation; hence argumentation does not proceed from multiple propositions, but rather certain fallacies are assigned on this account. But sacred Scripture ought to be efficacious for showing the truth without any fallacy. Therefore, several senses ought not to be conveyed in it under one letter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0138","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0139","text":"Moreover, Augustine says in the book *On the Usefulness of Believing* that *the Scripture called the Old Testament is handed down in four ways: namely, according to history, according to aetiology, according to analogy, and according to allegory*. These four seem to be wholly different from the four previously mentioned. Therefore, it does not seem fitting that the same letter of sacred Scripture should be expounded according to the four aforesaid senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0140","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0141","text":"Moreover, besides the aforesaid senses, a parabolic sense is found, which is not included among those four senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0142","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0143","text":"On the contrary, Gregory says in Book 20 of the *Moralia*: *sacred Scripture transcends all sciences by the very manner of its speech, because, while in one and the same discourse it narrates a deed, it discloses a mystery*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0144","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","text":"I answer that it must be said that the author of Sacred Scripture is God, in whose power it is not only to adapt words for signifying (which man also can do), but also the things themselves. And therefore, whereas in all sciences words signify, this science has the special property that the things signified by words themselves also signify something. Therefore that first signification, by which words signify things, belongs to the first sense, which is the historical or literal sense. But that signification by which the things signified by words in turn signify other things is called the spiritual sense; which is founded upon and presupposes the literal sense. Now this spiritual sense is divided threefold. For as the Apostle says, in Heb.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","text":"VII, the old law is a figure of the new law; and the new law itself, as Dionysius says in the Ecclesiastical Hierarchy, is a figure of future glory; moreover, in the new law, those things that were done in the head are signs of those things which we ought to do. Therefore, insofar as those things which belong to the old law signify those which belong to the new law, there is the allegorical sense; but insofar as those things which were done in Christ, or in those things which signify Christ, are signs of those things which we ought to do, there is the moral sense; whereas insofar as they signify those things which are in eternal glory, there is the anagogical sense. But since the literal sense is that which the author intends, and the author of Sacred Scripture is God, who comprehends all things simultaneously by his intellect, it is not unfitting, as Augustine says in Book XII of the Confessions, that even according to the literal sense there should be several senses in one letter of Scripture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0147","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the multiplicity of these senses does not produce equivocation, or another kind of multiplicity, because, as has already been said, these senses are not multiplied because one word signifies many things, but because the things themselves signified by words can be signs of other things. And thus no confusion follows in Sacred Scripture either, since all the senses are founded upon one, namely, the literal sense; from which alone an argument can be drawn, but not from those things which are said according to allegory, as Augustine says in the letter against Vincent the Donatist. Yet nothing is thereby lost to Sacred Scripture, because nothing necessary to faith is contained under the spiritual sense which Scripture does not somewhere manifestly hand down through the literal sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0149","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0150","text":"To the second it must be said that those three—history, aetiology, analogy—belong to the one literal sense. For history is, as Augustine himself explains, when something is simply set forth; aetiology, however, when the cause of what is said is assigned, as when the Lord assigned the reason why Moses permitted the license of dismissing wives, namely, because of the hardness of their hearts, Matt. XIX; and analogy is when the truth of one Scripture is shown not to be repugnant to the truth of another. But allegory alone, among those four, is posited in place of the three spiritual senses. Thus Hugh of Saint Victor also includes the anagogical under the allegorical sense, positing in the third book of his Sentences only three senses, namely, the historical, allegorical, and tropological.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0151","text":"[I q. 1 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html#28297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","text":"To the third it must be said that the parabolic sense is contained under the literal, for by words something is signified properly and something figuratively; nor is the literal sense the figure itself, but that which is figured. For when Scripture names God's arm, the literal sense is not that there is in God a bodily member of this kind, but that which is signified by this member, namely, operative power. From this it is clear that no falsehood can ever underlie the literal sense of Sacred Scripture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152"]}],"source_digest":"05360be468f0d2b52180b4b606729d9e357554bd131798a12d0009984268bf3d","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 1"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2","title":"First Part · Question 2","order":3,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 2 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28298)\n\nQuia igitur principalis intentio huius sacrae doctrinae est Dei cognitionem tradere, et non solum secundum quod in se est, sed etiam secundum quod est principium rerum et finis earum, et specialiter rationalis creaturae, ut ex dictis est manifestum; ad huius doctrinae expositionem intendentes, primo tractabimus de Deo; secundo, de motu rationalis creaturae in Deum; tertio, de Christo, qui, secundum quod homo, via est nobis tendendi in Deum. Consideratio autem de Deo tripartita erit. Primo namque considerabimus ea quae ad essentiam divinam pertinent; secundo, ea quae pertinent ad distinctionem personarum; tertio, ea quae pertinent ad processum creaturarum ab ipso. Circa essentiam vero divinam, primo considerandum est an Deus sit; secundo, quomodo sit, vel potius quomodo non sit; tertio considerandum erit de his quae ad operationem ipsius pertinent, scilicet de scientia et de voluntate et potentia. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Deum esse sit per se notum. Secundo, utrum sit demonstrabile. Tertio, an Deus sit.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28299)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deum esse sit per se notum. Illa enim nobis dicuntur per se nota, quorum cognitio nobis naturaliter inest, sicut patet de primis principiis. Sed, sicut dicit Damascenus in principio libri sui, *omnibus cognitio existendi Deum naturaliter est inserta*. Ergo Deum esse est per se notum.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28300)\n\nPraeterea, illa dicuntur esse per se nota, quae statim, cognitis terminis, cognoscuntur, quod philosophus attribuit primis demonstrationis principiis, in I Poster., scito enim quid est totum et quid pars, statim scitur quod omne totum maius est sua parte. Sed intellecto quid significet hoc nomen Deus, statim habetur quod Deus est. Significatur enim hoc nomine id quo maius significari non potest, maius autem est quod est in re et intellectu, quam quod est in intellectu tantum, unde cum, intellecto hoc nomine Deus, statim sit in intellectu, sequitur etiam quod sit in re. Ergo Deum esse est per se notum.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28301)\n\nPraeterea, veritatem esse est per se notum, quia qui negat veritatem esse, concedit veritatem esse, si enim veritas non est, verum est veritatem non esse. Si autem est aliquid verum, oportet quod veritas sit. Deus autem est ipsa veritas, Ioann. XIV, *ego sum via, veritas et vita*. Ergo Deum esse est per se notum.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28302)\n\nSed contra, nullus potest cogitare oppositum eius quod est per se notum ut patet per philosophum, in IV Metaphys. et I Poster., circa prima demonstrationis principia. Cogitari autem potest oppositum eius quod est Deum esse, secundum illud Psalmi LII, *dixit insipiens in corde suo, non est Deus*. Ergo Deum esse non est per se notum.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28303)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod contingit aliquid esse per se notum dupliciter, uno modo, secundum se et non quoad nos; alio modo, secundum se et quoad nos. Ex hoc enim aliqua propositio est per se nota, quod praedicatum includitur in ratione subiecti, ut homo est animal, nam animal est de ratione hominis. Si igitur notum sit omnibus de praedicato et de subiecto quid sit, propositio illa erit omnibus per se nota, sicut patet in primis demonstrationum principiis, quorum termini sunt quaedam communia quae nullus ignorat, ut ens et non ens, totum et pars, et similia. Si autem apud aliquos notum non sit de praedicato et subiecto quid sit, propositio quidem quantum in se est, erit per se nota, non tamen apud illos qui praedicatum et subiectum propositionis ignorant. Et ideo contingit, ut dicit Boetius in libro de hebdomadibus, quod quaedam sunt communes animi conceptiones et per se notae, apud sapientes tantum, ut incorporalia in loco non esse. Dico ergo quod haec propositio, Deus est, quantum in se est, per se nota est, quia praedicatum est idem cum subiecto; Deus enim est suum esse, ut infra patebit. Sed quia nos non scimus de Deo quid est, non est nobis per se nota, sed indiget demonstrari per ea quae sunt magis nota quoad nos, et minus nota quoad naturam, scilicet per effectus.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28304)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod cognoscere Deum esse in aliquo communi, sub quadam confusione, est nobis naturaliter insertum, inquantum scilicet Deus est hominis beatitudo, homo enim naturaliter desiderat beatitudinem, et quod naturaliter desideratur ab homine, naturaliter cognoscitur ab eodem. Sed hoc non est simpliciter cognoscere Deum esse; sicut cognoscere venientem, non est cognoscere Petrum, quamvis sit Petrus veniens, multi enim perfectum hominis bonum, quod est beatitudo, existimant divitias; quidam vero voluptates; quidam autem aliquid aliud.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28305)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod forte ille qui audit hoc nomen Deus, non intelligit significari aliquid quo maius cogitari non possit, cum quidam crediderint Deum esse corpus. Dato etiam quod quilibet intelligat hoc nomine Deus significari hoc quod dicitur, scilicet illud quo maius cogitari non potest; non tamen propter hoc sequitur quod intelligat id quod significatur per nomen, esse in rerum natura; sed in apprehensione intellectus tantum. Nec potest argui quod sit in re, nisi daretur quod sit in re aliquid quo maius cogitari non potest, quod non est datum a ponentibus Deum non esse.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28306)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod veritatem esse in communi, est per se notum, sed primam veritatem esse, hoc non est per se notum quoad nos.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28307)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deum esse non sit demonstrabile. Deum enim esse est articulus fidei. Sed ea quae sunt fidei, non sunt demonstrabilia, quia demonstratio facit scire, fides autem de non apparentibus est, ut patet per apostolum, ad Hebr. XI. Ergo Deum esse non est demonstrabile.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28308)\n\nPraeterea, medium demonstrationis est quod quid est. Sed de Deo non possumus scire quid est, sed solum quid non est, ut dicit Damascenus. Ergo non possumus demonstrare Deum esse.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28309)\n\nPraeterea, si demonstraretur Deum esse, hoc non esset nisi ex effectibus eius. Sed effectus eius non sunt proportionati ei, cum ipse sit infinitus, et effectus finiti; finiti autem ad infinitum non est proportio. Cum ergo causa non possit demonstrari per effectum sibi non proportionatum, videtur quod Deum esse non possit demonstrari.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28310)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit, ad Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*. Sed hoc non esset, nisi per ea quae facta sunt, posset demonstrari Deum esse, primum enim quod oportet intelligi de aliquo, est an sit.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28311)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod duplex est demonstratio. Una quae est per causam, et dicitur propter quid, et haec est per priora simpliciter. Alia est per effectum, et dicitur demonstratio quia, et haec est per ea quae sunt priora quoad nos, cum enim effectus aliquis nobis est manifestior quam sua causa, per effectum procedimus ad cognitionem causae. Ex quolibet autem effectu potest demonstrari propriam causam eius esse (si tamen eius effectus sint magis noti quoad nos), quia, cum effectus dependeant a causa, posito effectu necesse est causam praeexistere. Unde Deum esse, secundum quod non est per se notum quoad nos, demonstrabile est per effectus nobis notos.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28312)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deum esse, et alia huiusmodi quae per rationem naturalem nota possunt esse de Deo, ut dicitur Rom. I non sunt articuli fidei, sed praeambula ad articulos, sic enim fides praesupponit cognitionem naturalem, sicut gratia naturam, et ut perfectio perfectibile. Nihil tamen prohibet illud quod secundum se demonstrabile est et scibile, ab aliquo accipi ut credibile, qui demonstrationem non capit.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28313)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod cum demonstratur causa per effectum, necesse est uti effectu loco definitionis causae, ad probandum causam esse, et hoc maxime contingit in Deo. Quia ad probandum aliquid esse, necesse est accipere pro medio quid significet nomen non autem quod quid est, quia quaestio quid est, sequitur ad quaestionem an est. Nomina autem Dei imponuntur ab effectibus, ut postea ostendetur, unde, demonstrando Deum esse per effectum, accipere possumus pro medio quid significet hoc nomen Deus.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28314)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod per effectus non proportionatos causae, non potest perfecta cognitio de causa haberi, sed tamen ex quocumque effectu potest manifeste nobis demonstrari causam esse, ut dictum est. Et sic ex effectibus Dei potest demonstrari Deum esse, licet per eos non perfecte possimus eum cognoscere secundum suam essentiam.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 2 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28315)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit. Quia si unum contrariorum fuerit infinitum, totaliter destruetur aliud. Sed hoc intelligitur in hoc nomine Deus, scilicet quod sit quoddam bonum infinitum. Si ergo Deus esset, nullum malum inveniretur. Invenitur autem malum in mundo. Ergo Deus non est.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28316)\n\nPraeterea, quod potest compleri per pauciora principia, non fit per plura. Sed videtur quod omnia quae apparent in mundo, possunt compleri per alia principia, supposito quod Deus non sit, quia ea quae sunt naturalia, reducuntur in principium quod est natura; ea vero quae sunt a proposito, reducuntur in principium quod est ratio humana vel voluntas. Nulla igitur necessitas est ponere Deum esse.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28317)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Exodi III, ex persona Dei, *ego sum qui sum*.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28318)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deum esse quinque viis probari potest. Prima autem et manifestior via est, quae sumitur ex parte motus. Certum est enim, et sensu constat, aliqua moveri in hoc mundo. Omne autem quod movetur, ab alio movetur. Nihil enim movetur, nisi secundum quod est in potentia ad illud ad quod movetur, movet autem aliquid secundum quod est actu. Movere enim nihil aliud est quam educere aliquid de potentia in actum, de potentia autem non potest aliquid reduci in actum, nisi per aliquod ens in actu, sicut calidum in actu, ut ignis, facit lignum, quod est calidum in potentia, esse actu calidum, et per hoc movet et alterat ipsum. Non autem est possibile ut idem sit simul in actu et potentia secundum idem, sed solum secundum diversa, quod enim est calidum in actu, non potest simul esse calidum in potentia, sed est simul frigidum in potentia. Impossibile est ergo quod, secundum idem et eodem modo, aliquid sit movens et motum, vel quod moveat seipsum. Omne ergo quod movetur, oportet ab alio moveri. Si ergo id a quo movetur, moveatur, oportet et ipsum ab alio moveri et illud ab alio. Hic autem non est procedere in infinitum, quia sic non esset aliquod primum movens; et per consequens nec aliquod aliud movens, quia moventia secunda non movent nisi per hoc quod sunt mota a primo movente, sicut baculus non movet nisi per hoc quod est motus a manu. Ergo necesse est devenire ad aliquod primum movens, quod a nullo movetur, et hoc omnes intelligunt Deum. Secunda via est ex ratione causae efficientis. Invenimus enim in istis sensibilibus esse ordinem causarum efficientium, nec tamen invenitur, nec est possibile, quod aliquid sit causa efficiens sui ipsius; quia sic esset prius seipso, quod est impossibile. Non autem est possibile quod in causis efficientibus procedatur in infinitum. Quia in omnibus causis efficientibus ordinatis, primum est causa medii, et medium est causa ultimi, sive media sint plura sive unum tantum, remota autem causa, removetur effectus, ergo, si non fuerit primum in causis efficientibus, non erit ultimum nec medium. Sed si procedatur in infinitum in causis efficientibus, non erit prima causa efficiens, et sic non erit nec effectus ultimus, nec causae efficientes mediae, quod patet esse falsum. Ergo est necesse ponere aliquam causam efficientem primam, quam omnes Deum nominant. Tertia via est sumpta ex possibili et necessario, quae talis est. Invenimus enim in rebus quaedam quae sunt possibilia esse et non esse, cum quaedam inveniantur generari et corrumpi, et per consequens possibilia esse et non esse. Impossibile est autem omnia quae sunt, talia esse, quia quod possibile est non esse, quandoque non est. Si igitur omnia sunt possibilia non esse, aliquando nihil fuit in rebus. Sed si hoc est verum, etiam nunc nihil esset, quia quod non est, non incipit esse nisi per aliquid quod est; si igitur nihil fuit ens, impossibile fuit quod aliquid inciperet esse, et sic modo nihil esset, quod patet esse falsum. Non ergo omnia entia sunt possibilia, sed oportet aliquid esse necessarium in rebus. Omne autem necessarium vel habet causam suae necessitatis aliunde, vel non habet. Non est autem possibile quod procedatur in infinitum in necessariis quae habent causam suae necessitatis, sicut nec in causis efficientibus, ut probatum est. Ergo necesse est ponere aliquid quod sit per se necessarium, non habens causam necessitatis aliunde, sed quod est causa necessitatis aliis, quod omnes dicunt Deum. Quarta via sumitur ex gradibus qui in rebus inveniuntur. Invenitur enim in rebus aliquid magis et minus bonum, et verum, et nobile, et sic de aliis huiusmodi. Sed magis et minus dicuntur de diversis secundum quod appropinquant diversimode ad aliquid quod maxime est, sicut magis calidum est, quod magis appropinquat maxime calido. Est igitur aliquid quod est verissimum, et optimum, et nobilissimum, et per consequens maxime ens, nam quae sunt maxime vera, sunt maxime entia, ut dicitur II Metaphys. Quod autem dicitur maxime tale in aliquo genere, est causa omnium quae sunt illius generis, sicut ignis, qui est maxime calidus, est causa omnium calidorum, ut in eodem libro dicitur. Ergo est aliquid quod omnibus entibus est causa esse, et bonitatis, et cuiuslibet perfectionis, et hoc dicimus Deum. Quinta via sumitur ex gubernatione rerum. Videmus enim quod aliqua quae cognitione carent, scilicet corpora naturalia, operantur propter finem, quod apparet ex hoc quod semper aut frequentius eodem modo operantur, ut consequantur id quod est optimum; unde patet quod non a casu, sed ex intentione perveniunt ad finem. Ea autem quae non habent cognitionem, non tendunt in finem nisi directa ab aliquo cognoscente et intelligente, sicut sagitta a sagittante. Ergo est aliquid intelligens, a quo omnes res naturales ordinantur ad finem, et hoc dicimus Deum.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28319)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus in Enchiridio, *Deus, cum sit summe bonus, nullo modo sineret aliquid mali esse in operibus suis, nisi esset adeo omnipotens et bonus, ut bene faceret etiam de malo*. Hoc ergo ad infinitam Dei bonitatem pertinet, ut esse permittat mala, et ex eis eliciat bona.\n\n[I q. 2 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28320)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum natura propter determinatum finem operetur ex directione alicuius superioris agentis, necesse est ea quae a natura fiunt, etiam in Deum reducere, sicut in primam causam. Similiter etiam quae ex proposito fiunt, oportet reducere in aliquam altiorem causam, quae non sit ratio et voluntas humana, quia haec mutabilia sunt et defectibilia; oportet autem omnia mobilia et deficere possibilia reduci in aliquod primum principium immobile et per se necessarium, sicut ostensum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0002","text":"[I q. 2 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0003","text":"Quia igitur principalis intentio huius sacrae doctrinae est Dei cognitionem tradere, et non solum secundum quod in se est, sed etiam secundum quod est principium rerum et finis earum, et specialiter rationalis creaturae, ut ex dictis est manifestum; ad huius doctrinae expositionem intendentes, primo tractabimus de Deo; secundo, de motu rationalis creaturae in Deum; tertio, de Christo, qui, secundum quod homo, via est nobis tendendi in Deum. Consideratio autem de Deo tripartita erit. Primo namque considerabimus ea quae ad essentiam divinam pertinent; secundo, ea quae pertinent ad distinctionem personarum; tertio, ea quae pertinent ad processum creaturarum ab ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0004","text":"Circa essentiam vero divinam, primo considerandum est an Deus sit; secundo, quomodo sit, vel potius quomodo non sit; tertio considerandum erit de his quae ad operationem ipsius pertinent, scilicet de scientia et de voluntate et potentia. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Deum esse sit per se notum. Secundo, utrum sit demonstrabile. Tertio, an Deus sit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0006","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deum esse sit per se notum. Illa enim nobis dicuntur per se nota, quorum cognitio nobis naturaliter inest, sicut patet de primis principiis. Sed, sicut dicit Damascenus in principio libri sui, *omnibus cognitio existendi Deum naturaliter est inserta*. Ergo Deum esse est per se notum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0008","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0009","text":"Praeterea, illa dicuntur esse per se nota, quae statim, cognitis terminis, cognoscuntur, quod philosophus attribuit primis demonstrationis principiis, in I Poster., scito enim quid est totum et quid pars, statim scitur quod omne totum maius est sua parte. Sed intellecto quid significet hoc nomen Deus, statim habetur quod Deus est. Significatur enim hoc nomine id quo maius significari non potest, maius autem est quod est in re et intellectu, quam quod est in intellectu tantum, unde cum, intellecto hoc nomine Deus, statim sit in intellectu, sequitur etiam quod sit in re. Ergo Deum esse est per se notum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0010","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0011","text":"Praeterea, veritatem esse est per se notum, quia qui negat veritatem esse, concedit veritatem esse, si enim veritas non est, verum est veritatem non esse. Si autem est aliquid verum, oportet quod veritas sit. Deus autem est ipsa veritas, Ioann. XIV, *ego sum via, veritas et vita*. Ergo Deum esse est per se notum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0012","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0013","text":"Sed contra, nullus potest cogitare oppositum eius quod est per se notum ut patet per philosophum, in IV Metaphys. et I Poster., circa prima demonstrationis principia. Cogitari autem potest oppositum eius quod est Deum esse, secundum illud Psalmi LII, *dixit insipiens in corde suo, non est Deus*. Ergo Deum esse non est per se notum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0014","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod contingit aliquid esse per se notum dupliciter, uno modo, secundum se et non quoad nos; alio modo, secundum se et quoad nos. Ex hoc enim aliqua propositio est per se nota, quod praedicatum includitur in ratione subiecti, ut homo est animal, nam animal est de ratione hominis. Si igitur notum sit omnibus de praedicato et de subiecto quid sit, propositio illa erit omnibus per se nota, sicut patet in primis demonstrationum principiis, quorum termini sunt quaedam communia quae nullus ignorat, ut ens et non ens, totum et pars, et similia. Si autem apud aliquos notum non sit de praedicato et subiecto quid sit, propositio quidem quantum in se est, erit per se nota, non tamen apud illos qui praedicatum et subiectum propositionis ignorant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","text":"Et ideo contingit, ut dicit Boetius in libro de hebdomadibus, quod quaedam sunt communes animi conceptiones et per se notae, apud sapientes tantum, ut incorporalia in loco non esse. Dico ergo quod haec propositio, Deus est, quantum in se est, per se nota est, quia praedicatum est idem cum subiecto; Deus enim est suum esse, ut infra patebit. Sed quia nos non scimus de Deo quid est, non est nobis per se nota, sed indiget demonstrari per ea quae sunt magis nota quoad nos, et minus nota quoad naturam, scilicet per effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0017","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod cognoscere Deum esse in aliquo communi, sub quadam confusione, est nobis naturaliter insertum, inquantum scilicet Deus est hominis beatitudo, homo enim naturaliter desiderat beatitudinem, et quod naturaliter desideratur ab homine, naturaliter cognoscitur ab eodem. Sed hoc non est simpliciter cognoscere Deum esse; sicut cognoscere venientem, non est cognoscere Petrum, quamvis sit Petrus veniens, multi enim perfectum hominis bonum, quod est beatitudo, existimant divitias; quidam vero voluptates; quidam autem aliquid aliud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0019","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod forte ille qui audit hoc nomen Deus, non intelligit significari aliquid quo maius cogitari non possit, cum quidam crediderint Deum esse corpus. Dato etiam quod quilibet intelligat hoc nomine Deus significari hoc quod dicitur, scilicet illud quo maius cogitari non potest; non tamen propter hoc sequitur quod intelligat id quod significatur per nomen, esse in rerum natura; sed in apprehensione intellectus tantum. Nec potest argui quod sit in re, nisi daretur quod sit in re aliquid quo maius cogitari non potest, quod non est datum a ponentibus Deum non esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0021","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod veritatem esse in communi, est per se notum, sed primam veritatem esse, hoc non est per se notum quoad nos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0024","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deum esse non sit demonstrabile. Deum enim esse est articulus fidei. Sed ea quae sunt fidei, non sunt demonstrabilia, quia demonstratio facit scire, fides autem de non apparentibus est, ut patet per apostolum, ad Hebr. XI. Ergo Deum esse non est demonstrabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0026","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027","text":"Praeterea, medium demonstrationis est quod quid est. Sed de Deo non possumus scire quid est, sed solum quid non est, ut dicit Damascenus. Ergo non possumus demonstrare Deum esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0028","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029","text":"Praeterea, si demonstraretur Deum esse, hoc non esset nisi ex effectibus eius. Sed effectus eius non sunt proportionati ei, cum ipse sit infinitus, et effectus finiti; finiti autem ad infinitum non est proportio. Cum ergo causa non possit demonstrari per effectum sibi non proportionatum, videtur quod Deum esse non possit demonstrari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0030","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit, ad Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*. Sed hoc non esset, nisi per ea quae facta sunt, posset demonstrari Deum esse, primum enim quod oportet intelligi de aliquo, est an sit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0032","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod duplex est demonstratio. Una quae est per causam, et dicitur propter quid, et haec est per priora simpliciter. Alia est per effectum, et dicitur demonstratio quia, et haec est per ea quae sunt priora quoad nos, cum enim effectus aliquis nobis est manifestior quam sua causa, per effectum procedimus ad cognitionem causae. Ex quolibet autem effectu potest demonstrari propriam causam eius esse (si tamen eius effectus sint magis noti quoad nos), quia, cum effectus dependeant a causa, posito effectu necesse est causam praeexistere. Unde Deum esse, secundum quod non est per se notum quoad nos, demonstrabile est per effectus nobis notos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0034","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deum esse, et alia huiusmodi quae per rationem naturalem nota possunt esse de Deo, ut dicitur Rom. I non sunt articuli fidei, sed praeambula ad articulos, sic enim fides praesupponit cognitionem naturalem, sicut gratia naturam, et ut perfectio perfectibile. Nihil tamen prohibet illud quod secundum se demonstrabile est et scibile, ab aliquo accipi ut credibile, qui demonstrationem non capit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0036","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod cum demonstratur causa per effectum, necesse est uti effectu loco definitionis causae, ad probandum causam esse, et hoc maxime contingit in Deo. Quia ad probandum aliquid esse, necesse est accipere pro medio quid significet nomen non autem quod quid est, quia quaestio quid est, sequitur ad quaestionem an est. Nomina autem Dei imponuntur ab effectibus, ut postea ostendetur, unde, demonstrando Deum esse per effectum, accipere possumus pro medio quid significet hoc nomen Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0038","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod per effectus non proportionatos causae, non potest perfecta cognitio de causa haberi, sed tamen ex quocumque effectu potest manifeste nobis demonstrari causam esse, ut dictum est. Et sic ex effectibus Dei potest demonstrari Deum esse, licet per eos non perfecte possimus eum cognoscere secundum suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0041","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit. Quia si unum contrariorum fuerit infinitum, totaliter destruetur aliud. Sed hoc intelligitur in hoc nomine Deus, scilicet quod sit quoddam bonum infinitum. Si ergo Deus esset, nullum malum inveniretur. Invenitur autem malum in mundo. Ergo Deus non est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0043","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","text":"Praeterea, quod potest compleri per pauciora principia, non fit per plura. Sed videtur quod omnia quae apparent in mundo, possunt compleri per alia principia, supposito quod Deus non sit, quia ea quae sunt naturalia, reducuntur in principium quod est natura; ea vero quae sunt a proposito, reducuntur in principium quod est ratio humana vel voluntas. Nulla igitur necessitas est ponere Deum esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0045","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0046","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Exodi III, ex persona Dei, *ego sum qui sum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0047","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deum esse quinque viis probari potest. Prima autem et manifestior via est, quae sumitur ex parte motus. Certum est enim, et sensu constat, aliqua moveri in hoc mundo. Omne autem quod movetur, ab alio movetur. Nihil enim movetur, nisi secundum quod est in potentia ad illud ad quod movetur, movet autem aliquid secundum quod est actu. Movere enim nihil aliud est quam educere aliquid de potentia in actum, de potentia autem non potest aliquid reduci in actum, nisi per aliquod ens in actu, sicut calidum in actu, ut ignis, facit lignum, quod est calidum in potentia, esse actu calidum, et per hoc movet et alterat ipsum. Non autem est possibile ut idem sit simul in actu et potentia secundum idem, sed solum secundum diversa, quod enim est calidum in actu, non potest simul esse calidum in potentia, sed est simul frigidum in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","text":"Impossibile est ergo quod, secundum idem et eodem modo, aliquid sit movens et motum, vel quod moveat seipsum. Omne ergo quod movetur, oportet ab alio moveri. Si ergo id a quo movetur, moveatur, oportet et ipsum ab alio moveri et illud ab alio. Hic autem non est procedere in infinitum, quia sic non esset aliquod primum movens; et per consequens nec aliquod aliud movens, quia moventia secunda non movent nisi per hoc quod sunt mota a primo movente, sicut baculus non movet nisi per hoc quod est motus a manu. Ergo necesse est devenire ad aliquod primum movens, quod a nullo movetur, et hoc omnes intelligunt Deum. Secunda via est ex ratione causae efficientis. Invenimus enim in istis sensibilibus esse ordinem causarum efficientium, nec tamen invenitur, nec est possibile, quod aliquid sit causa efficiens sui ipsius; quia sic esset prius seipso, quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","text":"Non autem est possibile quod in causis efficientibus procedatur in infinitum. Quia in omnibus causis efficientibus ordinatis, primum est causa medii, et medium est causa ultimi, sive media sint plura sive unum tantum, remota autem causa, removetur effectus, ergo, si non fuerit primum in causis efficientibus, non erit ultimum nec medium. Sed si procedatur in infinitum in causis efficientibus, non erit prima causa efficiens, et sic non erit nec effectus ultimus, nec causae efficientes mediae, quod patet esse falsum. Ergo est necesse ponere aliquam causam efficientem primam, quam omnes Deum nominant. Tertia via est sumpta ex possibili et necessario, quae talis est. Invenimus enim in rebus quaedam quae sunt possibilia esse et non esse, cum quaedam inveniantur generari et corrumpi, et per consequens possibilia esse et non esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","text":"Impossibile est autem omnia quae sunt, talia esse, quia quod possibile est non esse, quandoque non est. Si igitur omnia sunt possibilia non esse, aliquando nihil fuit in rebus. Sed si hoc est verum, etiam nunc nihil esset, quia quod non est, non incipit esse nisi per aliquid quod est; si igitur nihil fuit ens, impossibile fuit quod aliquid inciperet esse, et sic modo nihil esset, quod patet esse falsum. Non ergo omnia entia sunt possibilia, sed oportet aliquid esse necessarium in rebus. Omne autem necessarium vel habet causam suae necessitatis aliunde, vel non habet. Non est autem possibile quod procedatur in infinitum in necessariis quae habent causam suae necessitatis, sicut nec in causis efficientibus, ut probatum est. Ergo necesse est ponere aliquid quod sit per se necessarium, non habens causam necessitatis aliunde, sed quod est causa necessitatis aliis, quod omnes dicunt Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","text":"Quarta via sumitur ex gradibus qui in rebus inveniuntur. Invenitur enim in rebus aliquid magis et minus bonum, et verum, et nobile, et sic de aliis huiusmodi. Sed magis et minus dicuntur de diversis secundum quod appropinquant diversimode ad aliquid quod maxime est, sicut magis calidum est, quod magis appropinquat maxime calido. Est igitur aliquid quod est verissimum, et optimum, et nobilissimum, et per consequens maxime ens, nam quae sunt maxime vera, sunt maxime entia, ut dicitur II Metaphys. Quod autem dicitur maxime tale in aliquo genere, est causa omnium quae sunt illius generis, sicut ignis, qui est maxime calidus, est causa omnium calidorum, ut in eodem libro dicitur. Ergo est aliquid quod omnibus entibus est causa esse, et bonitatis, et cuiuslibet perfectionis, et hoc dicimus Deum. Quinta via sumitur ex gubernatione rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","text":"Videmus enim quod aliqua quae cognitione carent, scilicet corpora naturalia, operantur propter finem, quod apparet ex hoc quod semper aut frequentius eodem modo operantur, ut consequantur id quod est optimum; unde patet quod non a casu, sed ex intentione perveniunt ad finem. Ea autem quae non habent cognitionem, non tendunt in finem nisi directa ab aliquo cognoscente et intelligente, sicut sagitta a sagittante. Ergo est aliquid intelligens, a quo omnes res naturales ordinantur ad finem, et hoc dicimus Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0054","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus in Enchiridio, *Deus, cum sit summe bonus, nullo modo sineret aliquid mali esse in operibus suis, nisi esset adeo omnipotens et bonus, ut bene faceret etiam de malo*. Hoc ergo ad infinitam Dei bonitatem pertinet, ut esse permittat mala, et ex eis eliciat bona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0056","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum natura propter determinatum finem operetur ex directione alicuius superioris agentis, necesse est ea quae a natura fiunt, etiam in Deum reducere, sicut in primam causam. Similiter etiam quae ex proposito fiunt, oportet reducere in aliquam altiorem causam, quae non sit ratio et voluntas humana, quia haec mutabilia sunt et defectibilia; oportet autem omnia mobilia et deficere possibilia reduci in aliquod primum principium immobile et per se necessarium, sicut ostensum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"98f9b066da001a381b547accaf8a1b70b77e4a419bdae7e2cc113d7fc8855d37","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T133454930996Z_b0a58ca2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 2","source_sha256":"98f9b066da001a381b547accaf8a1b70b77e4a419bdae7e2cc113d7fc8855d37","sha256":"cca2d9c844580e04b4e814d8c6bacb685a47ce26c318558d203309cb0f6b5c46","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:34:54.931008+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0002","text":"[I q. 2 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0003","text":"Therefore, since the principal intention of this sacred doctrine is to convey knowledge of God, not only according to what He is in Himself, but also according to His being the principle and end of things, and especially of the rational creature, as is clear from what has been said; in undertaking the exposition of this doctrine, we shall first treat of God; secondly, of the movement of the rational creature toward God; thirdly, of Christ, who, insofar as He is man, is for us the way of tending toward God. Now the consideration of God will be threefold. For first we shall consider those things that pertain to the divine essence; secondly, those that pertain to the distinction of persons; thirdly, those that pertain to the procession of creatures from Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0004","text":"Concerning the divine essence, however, first it must be considered whether God exists; secondly, how He exists, or rather how He does not exist; thirdly, there will be consideration of those things that pertain to His operation, namely, knowledge, will, and power. Concerning the first, three questions are asked. First, whether that God exists is self-evident. Secondly, whether it is demonstrable. Thirdly, whether God exists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0006","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0007","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that God's existence is self-evident. For those things are said to be self-evident to us, knowledge of which is naturally within us, as is clear concerning first principles. But, as Damascene says at the beginning of his book, *knowledge that God exists is naturally implanted in all*. Therefore, God's existence is self-evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0008","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0009","text":"Further, those things are said to be self-evident which, once the terms are known, are immediately known; and the Philosopher attributes this to the first principles of demonstration, in *Posterior Analytics* I: for, once it is known what a whole is and what a part is, it is immediately known that every whole is greater than its part. But once it is understood what this name God signifies, it is immediately grasped that God exists. For by this name is signified that than which nothing greater can be signified; but that which exists both in reality and in the intellect is greater than that which exists in the intellect alone. Hence, since, once this name God is understood, it is immediately in the intellect, it follows also that it is in reality. Therefore, God's existence is self-evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0010","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0011","text":"Further, that truth exists is self-evident, because whoever denies that truth exists concedes that truth exists: for if truth does not exist, it is true that truth does not exist. But if there is something true, truth must exist. Now God is truth itself, John XIV: *I am the way, the truth, and the life*. Therefore, God's existence is self-evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0012","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0013","text":"On the contrary, no one can think the opposite of that which is self-evident, as is clear from the Philosopher, in *Metaphysics* IV and *Posterior Analytics* I, concerning the first principles of demonstration. But the opposite of the proposition that God exists can be thought, according to that saying of Psalm LII: *The fool said in his heart, There is no God*. Therefore, God's existence is not self-evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0014","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","text":"I answer that it must be said that something can be self-evident in two ways: in one way, in itself and not with respect to us; in another way, in itself and with respect to us. For a proposition is self-evident from the fact that the predicate is included in the account of the subject, as “man is an animal,” for animal belongs to the account of man. Therefore, if what the predicate and subject are is known to everyone, that proposition will be self-evident to everyone, as is clear in the first principles of demonstrations, whose terms are certain common things that no one is ignorant of, such as being and non-being, whole and part, and the like. But if, among some people, what the predicate and subject are is not known, then the proposition, indeed, insofar as it is in itself, will be self-evident, though not among those who are ignorant of the predicate and subject of the proposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","text":"And therefore it happens, as Boethius says in the book *On the Hebdomads*, that some things are common conceptions of the mind and self-evident only among the wise, such as that incorporeal things are not in a place. Therefore I say that this proposition, “God exists,” insofar as it is in itself, is self-evident, because the predicate is the same as the subject; for God is His own existence, as will become clear below. But because we do not know what God is, it is not self-evident to us, but needs to be demonstrated through those things that are more known with respect to us and less known with respect to nature, namely, through effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0017","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to know in some general way, under a certain confusion, that God exists is naturally implanted in us, insofar as God is man's beatitude. For man naturally desires beatitude, and that which is naturally desired by man is naturally known by him. But this is not simply to know that God exists; just as to know one approaching is not to know Peter, although it is Peter who is approaching. For many judge the perfect good of man, which is beatitude, to be riches; others, pleasures; and still others, something else.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0019","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that perhaps one who hears this name God does not understand that something than which nothing greater can be thought is signified, since some have believed God to be a body. Even granted that everyone understands this name God to signify what is said, namely, that than which nothing greater can be thought, nevertheless it does not follow from this that he understands what is signified by the name to exist in the nature of things, but only in the apprehension of the intellect. Nor can it be argued that it exists in reality, unless it were granted that in reality there is something than which nothing greater can be thought; which is not granted by those who posit that God does not exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0021","text":"[I q. 2 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that the existence of truth in general is self-evident, but that the first truth exists is not self-evident with respect to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0024","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second [question]. It seems that the existence of God is not demonstrable. For God's existence is an article of faith. But those things that are of faith are not demonstrable, because demonstration produces knowledge, whereas faith is concerning things not apparent, as is clear through the Apostle, in Hebrews XI. Therefore, the existence of God is not demonstrable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0026","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027","text":"Moreover, the middle term of demonstration is what a thing is. But concerning God we cannot know what He is, but only what He is not, as Damascene says. Therefore, we cannot demonstrate that God exists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0028","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029","text":"Moreover, if the existence of God were demonstrated, this would be only from His effects. But His effects are not proportionate to Him, since He Himself is infinite and the effects finite; but there is no proportion of the finite to the infinite. Therefore, since a cause cannot be demonstrated through an effect not proportionate to it, it seems that the existence of God cannot be demonstrated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0030","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0031","text":"On the contrary, the Apostle says, in Romans I, *the invisible things of God are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made*. But this would not be so unless the existence of God could be demonstrated through the things that are made; for the first thing that must be understood concerning anything is whether it exists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0032","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","text":"I answer that it must be said that there are two kinds of demonstration. One is through a cause, and is called demonstration *propter quid* [of the reason why], and this proceeds through things prior simply. The other is through an effect, and is called demonstration *quia* [that], and this proceeds through things prior relative to us. For when an effect is more manifest to us than its cause, we proceed through the effect to knowledge of the cause. Now from any effect it can be demonstrated that its proper cause exists (provided that its effects are better known relative to us), because, since effects depend upon a cause, once the effect is posited the cause must pre-exist. Hence, the existence of God, insofar as it is not self-evident relative to us, is demonstrable through effects known to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0034","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the existence of God, and other such things that can be known concerning God by natural reason, as is said in Romans I, are not articles of faith but preambles to the articles. For faith presupposes natural knowledge, just as grace presupposes nature, and perfection what is perfectible. Nevertheless, nothing prevents that which is in itself demonstrable and knowable from being accepted as an object of belief by someone who does not grasp the demonstration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0036","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","text":"To the second, it must be said that when a cause is demonstrated through an effect, it is necessary to use the effect in place of the definition of the cause, in order to prove that the cause exists; and this occurs especially in the case of God. For in order to prove that something exists, it is necessary to take as the middle term what the name signifies, but not what it is; because the question what it is follows upon the question whether it is. Now the names of God are imposed from effects, as will be shown later. Hence, in demonstrating the existence of God through an effect, we can take as the middle term what this name God signifies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0038","text":"[I q. 2 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","text":"To the third, it must be said that through effects not proportionate to the cause, perfect knowledge of the cause cannot be had; nevertheless, from any effect it can be manifestly demonstrated to us that the cause exists, as has been said. And thus from the effects of God it can be demonstrated that God exists, although through them we cannot know Him perfectly according to His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0041","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","text":"We proceed thus to the third [question]. It seems that God does not exist. For if one of two contraries were infinite, the other would be totally destroyed. But this is understood in the name God, namely, that He is a certain infinite good. Therefore, if God existed, no evil would be found. But evil is found in the world. Therefore, God does not exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0043","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","text":"Moreover, what can be accomplished through fewer principles is not done through more. But it seems that all the things appearing in the world can be accomplished through other principles, supposing that God does not exist: for natural things are reduced to a principle which is nature; whereas things that are from purpose are reduced to a principle which is human reason or will. Therefore, there is no necessity to posit that God exists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0045","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0046","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Exodus III, from the person of God, *I am who am*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0047","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","text":"I answer that it must be said that the existence of God can be proved in five ways. The first and more manifest way is that which is taken from the side of motion. For it is certain, and evident to sense, that some things are moved in this world. But everything that is moved is moved by another. For nothing is moved except insofar as it is in potency toward that to which it is moved, whereas something moves insofar as it is in act. For to move is nothing other than to bring something from potency into act; but nothing can be brought from potency into act except through some being in act. Thus what is actually hot, as fire, makes wood, which is potentially hot, to be actually hot, and thereby moves and alters it. But it is not possible for the same thing to be simultaneously in act and in potency in the same respect, but only in different respects: for what is actually hot cannot at the same time be potentially hot, but is at the same time potentially cold.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","text":"Therefore it is impossible that, with respect to the same thing and in the same way, something be both mover and moved, or that it move itself. Therefore, whatever is moved must be moved by another. If, then, that by which it is moved is itself moved, it too must be moved by another, and that by another. But here one cannot proceed to infinity, because in that case there would be no first mover; and consequently no other mover either, because secondary movers move only insofar as they are moved by the first mover, just as a staff moves only insofar as it is moved by the hand. Therefore it is necessary to arrive at some first mover, which is moved by nothing, and this everyone understands to be God. The second way is from the notion of efficient cause. For we find among these sensible things an order of efficient causes; yet neither is it found, nor is it possible, that anything be the efficient cause of itself, for then it would be prior to itself, which is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","text":"But it is not possible to proceed to infinity in efficient causes. For in all ordered efficient causes, the first is the cause of the intermediate, and the intermediate is the cause of the last, whether the intermediates be many or only one; but when the cause is removed, the effect is removed. Therefore, if there were no first among efficient causes, there would be neither last nor intermediate. But if one proceeded to infinity in efficient causes, there would be no first efficient cause, and thus there would be neither an ultimate effect nor intermediate efficient causes, which is plainly false. Therefore it is necessary to posit some first efficient cause, which everyone calls God. The third way is taken from the possible and the necessary, and is as follows. For we find among things certain things that can be and not be, since certain things are found to be generated and corrupted, and consequently can be and not be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","text":"But it is impossible that all things that are be such, because that which can not be is at some time not. If, therefore, all things can not be, then at some time there was nothing among things. But if this is true, even now there would be nothing, because what is not does not begin to be except through something that is; if, therefore, there was no being, it was impossible for anything to begin to be, and thus now there would be nothing, which is plainly false. Therefore not all beings are possible, but there must be something necessary among things. But every necessary thing either has the cause of its necessity from elsewhere, or does not have it. Now it is not possible to proceed to infinity in necessary things that have the cause of their necessity, just as neither in efficient causes, as has been proved. Therefore it is necessary to posit something that is necessary through itself, having no cause of necessity from elsewhere, but which is the cause of necessity for others, which everyone calls God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","text":"The fourth way is taken from the degrees that are found in things. For among things something is found to be more and less good, and true, and noble, and likewise with other such things. But more and less are said of diverse things according as they approach in diverse ways something that is most so; just as that is hotter which approaches most closely to what is hottest. Therefore there is something that is truest, and best, and noblest, and consequently most being; for those things that are most true are most beings, as is said in Metaphysics II. But what is said to be most such in some genus is the cause of all things that are of that genus, just as fire, which is hottest, is the cause of all hot things, as is said in the same book. Therefore there is something that is the cause for all beings of being, and of goodness, and of every perfection, and this we call God. The fifth way is taken from the governance of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","text":"For we see that certain things lacking cognition, namely natural bodies, act for an end; this appears from the fact that they always, or for the most part, act in the same way, so as to attain what is best. Hence it is evident that they arrive at their end not by chance, but by intention. But things that do not have cognition do not tend toward an end unless directed by something cognizing and understanding, as an arrow by an archer. Therefore there is something intelligent, by which all natural things are ordered to an end, and this we call God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0054","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Augustine says in the Enchiridion, *God, since He is supremely good, would in no way permit any evil to exist in His works, unless He were so omnipotent and good as to bring about good even from evil*. Therefore it pertains to the infinite goodness of God that He permits evils to be, and draws goods from them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0056","text":"[I q. 2 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1002.html#28320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","text":"To the second, it must be said that, since nature acts for a determinate end under the direction of some higher agent, it is necessary also to trace back to God, as to the first cause, those things that are done by nature. Likewise, those things that are done from purpose must be traced back to some higher cause, which is not human reason and will, because these are changeable and liable to fail; but all things movable and capable of failing must be traced back to some first principle, immovable and necessary through itself, as has been shown.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057"]}],"source_digest":"28b6204453959dad191e004c14ecfb28493736eb9e7ffeffa07dc1444d849903","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 2"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3","title":"First Part · Question 3","order":4,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 3 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28321)\n\nCognito de aliquo an sit, inquirendum restat quomodo sit, ut sciatur de eo quid sit. Sed quia de Deo scire non possumus quid sit, sed quid non sit, non possumus considerare de Deo quomodo sit, sed potius quomodo non sit. Primo ergo considerandum est quomodo non sit; secundo, quomodo a nobis cognoscatur; tertio, quomodo nominetur. Potest autem ostendi de Deo quomodo non sit, removendo ab eo ea quae ei non conveniunt, utpote compositionem, motum, et alia huiusmodi. Primo ergo inquiratur de simplicitate ipsius, per quam removetur ab eo compositio. Et quia simplicia in rebus corporalibus sunt imperfecta et partes, secundo inquiretur de perfectione ipsius; tertio, de infinitate eius; quarto, de immutabilitate; quinto, de unitate. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum Deus sit corpus. Secundo, utrum sit in eo compositio formae et materiae. Tertio, utrum sit in eo compositio quidditatis, sive essentiae, vel naturae, et subiecti. Quarto, utrum sit in eo compositio quae est ex essentia et esse. Quinto, utrum sit in eo compositio generis et differentiae. Sexto, utrum sit in eo compositio subiecti et accidentis. Septimo, utrum sit quocumque modo compositus, vel totaliter simplex. Octavo, utrum veniat in compositionem cum aliis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28322)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus sit corpus. Corpus enim est quod habet trinam dimensionem. Sed sacra Scriptura attribuit Deo trinam dimensionem, dicitur enim Iob XI, *excelsior caelo est, et quid facies? Profundior Inferno, et unde cognosces? Longior terra mensura eius, et latior mari*. Ergo Deus est corpus.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28323)\n\nPraeterea, omne figuratum est corpus, cum figura sit qualitas circa quantitatem. Sed Deus videtur esse figuratus, cum scriptum sit Gen. I, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*, figura enim imago dicitur, secundum illud Hebr. I, *cum sit splendor gloriae, et figura substantiae eius*, idest imago. Ergo Deus est corpus.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28324)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod habet partes corporeas, est corpus. Sed Scriptura attribuit Deo partes corporeas, dicitur enim Iob XL, *si habes brachium ut Deus*; et in Psalmo, *oculi domini super iustos; et, dextera domini fecit virtutem*. Ergo Deus est corpus.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28325)\n\nPraeterea, situs non convenit nisi corpori. Sed ea quae ad situm pertinent, in Scripturis dicuntur de Deo, dicitur enim Isaiae VI, *vidi dominum sedentem*; et Isaiae III, *stat ad iudicandum dominus*. Ergo Deus est corpus.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28326)\n\nPraeterea, nihil potest esse terminus localis a quo vel ad quem, nisi sit corpus vel aliquod corporeum. Sed Deus in Scriptura dicitur esse terminus localis ut ad quem, secundum illud Psalmi, *accedite ad eum, et illuminamini*; et ut a quo, secundum illud Hierem. XVII, recedentes a te in terra scribentur. Ergo Deus est corpus.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28327)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. IV, *spiritus est Deus*.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28328)\n\nRespondeo dicendum absolute Deum non esse corpus. Quod tripliciter ostendi potest. Primo quidem, quia nullum corpus movet non motum, ut patet inducendo per singula. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus est primum movens immobile. Unde manifestum est quod Deus non est corpus. Secundo, quia necesse est id quod est primum ens, esse in actu, et nullo modo in potentia. Licet enim in uno et eodem quod exit de potentia in actum, prius sit potentia quam actus tempore, simpliciter tamen actus prior est potentia, quia quod est in potentia, non reducitur in actum nisi per ens actu. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus est primum ens. Impossibile est igitur quod in Deo sit aliquid in potentia. Omne autem corpus est in potentia, quia continuum, inquantum huiusmodi, divisibile est in infinitum. Impossibile est igitur Deum esse corpus. Tertio, quia Deus est id quod est nobilissimum in entibus, ut ex dictis patet. Impossibile est autem aliquod corpus esse nobilissimum in entibus. Quia corpus aut est vivum, aut non vivum. Corpus autem vivum, manifestum est quod est nobilius corpore non vivo. Corpus autem vivum non vivit inquantum corpus, quia sic omne corpus viveret, oportet igitur quod vivat per aliquid aliud, sicut corpus nostrum vivit per animam. Illud autem per quod vivit corpus, est nobilius quam corpus. Impossibile est igitur Deum esse corpus.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28329)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, sacra Scriptura tradit nobis spiritualia et divina sub similitudinibus corporalium. Unde, cum trinam dimensionem Deo attribuit, sub similitudine quantitatis corporeae, quantitatem virtualem ipsius designat, utpote per profunditatem, virtutem ad cognoscendum occulta; per altitudinem, excellentiam virtutis super omnia; per longitudinem, durationem sui esse; per latitudinem, affectum dilectionis ad omnia. Vel, ut dicit Dionysius, cap. IX de Div. Nom., per profunditatem Dei intelligitur incomprehensibilitas ipsius essentiae; per longitudinem, processus virtutis eius, omnia penetrantis; per latitudinem vero, superextensio eius ad omnia, inquantum scilicet sub eius protectione omnia continentur.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28330)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod homo dicitur esse ad imaginem Dei, non secundum corpus, sed secundum id quo homo excellit alia animalia, unde, Gen. I, postquam dictum est, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*, subditur, *ut praesit piscibus maris,* et cetera. Excellit autem homo omnia animalia quantum ad rationem et intellectum. Unde secundum intellectum et rationem, quae sunt incorporea, homo est ad imaginem Dei.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28331)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod partes corporeae attribuuntur Deo in Scripturis ratione suorum actuum, secundum quandam similitudinem. Sicut actus oculi est videre, unde oculus de Deo dictus, significat virtutem eius ad videndum modo intelligibili, non sensibili. Et simile est de aliis partibus.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28332)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod etiam ea quae ad situm pertinent, non attribuuntur Deo nisi secundum quandam similitudinem, sicut dicitur sedens, propter suam immobilitatem et auctoritatem; et stans, propter suam fortitudinem ad debellandum omne quod adversatur.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28333)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod ad Deum non acceditur passibus corporalibus, cum ubique sit, sed affectibus mentis, et eodem modo ab eo receditur. Et sic accessus et recessus, sub similitudine localis motus, designant spiritualem affectum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28334)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo sit compositio formae et materiae. Omne enim quod habet animam, est compositum ex materia et forma, quia anima est forma corporis. Sed Scriptura attribuit animam Deo, introducitur enim ad Hebr. X, ex persona Dei, *iustus autem meus ex fide vivit; quod si subtraxerit se, non placebit animae meae*. Ergo Deus est compositus ex materia et forma.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28335)\n\nPraeterea, ira, gaudium, et huiusmodi, sunt passiones coniuncti, ut dicitur I de anima. Sed huiusmodi attribuuntur Deo in Scriptura dicitur enim in Psalmo, *iratus est furore dominus in populum suum*. Ergo Deus ex materia et forma est compositus.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28336)\n\nPraeterea, materia est principium individuationis. Sed Deus videtur esse individuum, non enim de multis praedicatur. Ergo est compositus ex materia et forma.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28337)\n\nSed contra, omne compositum ex materia et forma est corpus, quantitas enim dimensiva est quae primo inhaeret materiae. Sed Deus non est corpus, ut ostensum est. Ergo Deus non est compositus ex materia et forma.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28338)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est in Deo esse materiam. Primo quidem, quia materia est id quod est in potentia. Ostensum est autem quod Deus est purus actus, non habens aliquid de potentialitate. Unde impossibile est quod Deus sit compositus ex materia et forma. Secundo, quia omne compositum ex materia et forma est perfectum et bonum per suam formam, unde oportet quod sit bonum per participationem, secundum quod materia participat formam. Primum autem quod est bonum et optimum, quod Deus est, non est bonum per participationem, quia bonum per essentiam, prius est bono per participationem. Unde impossibile est quod Deus sit compositus ex materia et forma. Tertio, quia unumquodque agens agit per suam formam, unde secundum quod aliquid se habet ad suam formam, sic se habet ad hoc quod sit agens. Quod igitur primum est et per se agens, oportet quod sit primo et per se forma. Deus autem est primum agens, cum sit prima causa efficiens, ut ostensum est. Est igitur per essentiam suam forma; et non compositus ex materia et forma.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28339)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod anima attribuitur Deo per similitudinem actus. Quod enim volumus aliquid nobis, ex anima nostra est, unde illud dicitur esse placitum animae Dei, quod est placitum voluntati ipsius.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28340)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ira et huiusmodi attribuuntur Deo secundum similitudinem effectus, quia enim proprium est irati punire, ira eius punitio metaphorice vocatur.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28341)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod formae quae sunt receptibiles in materia individuantur per materiam, quae non potest esse in alio, cum sit primum subiectum substans, forma vero, quantum est de se, nisi aliquid aliud impediat, recipi potest a pluribus. Sed illa forma quae non est receptibilis in materia, sed est per se subsistens, ex hoc ipso individuatur, quod non potest recipi in alio, et huiusmodi forma est Deus. Unde non sequitur quod habeat materiam.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 3 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28342)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit idem Deus quod sua essentia vel natura. Nihil enim est in seipso. Sed essentia vel natura Dei, quae est deitas, dicitur esse in Deo. Ergo videtur quod Deus non sit idem quod sua essentia vel natura.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28343)\n\nPraeterea, effectus assimilatur suae causae, quia omne agens agit sibi simile. Sed in rebus creatis non est idem suppositum quod sua natura, non enim idem est homo quod sua humanitas. Ergo nec Deus est idem quod sua deitas.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28344)\n\nContra, de Deo dicitur quod est vita, et non solum quod est vivens, ut patet Ioan. XIV, *ego sum via, veritas et vita*. Sicut autem se habet vita ad viventem, ita deitas ad Deum. Ergo Deus est ipsa deitas.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28345)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus est idem quod sua essentia vel natura. Ad cuius intellectum sciendum est, quod in rebus compositis ex materia et forma, necesse est quod differant natura vel essentia et suppositum. Quia essentia vel natura comprehendit in se illa tantum quae cadunt in definitione speciei, sicut humanitas comprehendit in se ea quae cadunt in definitione hominis, his enim homo est homo, et hoc significat humanitas, hoc scilicet quo homo est homo. Sed materia individualis, cum accidentibus omnibus individuantibus ipsam, non cadit in definitione speciei, non enim cadunt in definitione hominis hae carnes et haec ossa, aut albedo vel nigredo, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Unde hae carnes et haec ossa, et accidentia designantia hanc materiam, non concluduntur in humanitate. Et tamen in eo quod est homo, includuntur, unde id quod est homo, habet in se aliquid quod non habet humanitas. Et propter hoc non est totaliter idem homo et humanitas, sed humanitas significatur ut pars formalis hominis; quia principia definientia habent se formaliter, respectu materiae individuantis. In his igitur quae non sunt composita ex materia et forma, in quibus individuatio non est per materiam individualem, idest per hanc materiam, sed ipsae formae per se individuantur, oportet quod ipsae formae sint supposita subsistentia. Unde in eis non differt suppositum et natura. Et sic, cum Deus non sit compositus ex materia et forma, ut ostensum est, oportet quod Deus sit sua deitas, sua vita, et quidquid aliud sic de Deo praedicatur.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28346)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod de rebus simplicibus loqui non possumus, nisi per modum compositorum, a quibus cognitionem accipimus. Et ideo, de Deo loquentes, utimur nominibus concretis, ut significemus eius subsistentiam, quia apud nos non subsistunt nisi composita, et utimur nominibus abstractis, ut significemus eius simplicitatem. Quod ergo dicitur deitas vel vita, vel aliquid huiusmodi, esse in Deo, referendum est ad diversitatem quae est in acceptione intellectus nostri; et non ad aliquam diversitatem rei.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28347)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod effectus Dei imitantur ipsum, non perfecte, sed secundum quod possunt. Et hoc ad defectum imitationis pertinet, quod id quod est simplex et unum, non potest repraesentari nisi per multa, et sic accidit in eis compositio, ex qua provenit quod in eis non est idem suppositum quod natura.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 3 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28348)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit idem essentia et esse. Si enim hoc sit, tunc ad esse divinum nihil additur. Sed esse cui nulla fit additio, est esse commune quod de omnibus praedicatur, sequitur ergo quod Deus sit ens commune praedicabile de omnibus. Hoc autem est falsum, secundum illud Sap. XIV, *incommunicabile nomen lignis et lapidibus imposuerunt*. Ergo esse Dei non est eius essentia.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28349)\n\nPraeterea, de Deo scire possumus an sit, ut supra dictum est. Non autem possumus scire quid sit. Ergo non est idem esse Dei, et quod quid est eius, sive quidditas vel natura.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28350)\n\nSed contra est quod Hilarius dicit in VII de Trin., *esse non est accidens in Deo, sed subsistens veritas*. Id ergo quod subsistit in Deo, est suum esse.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28351)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus non solum est sua essentia, ut ostensum est, sed etiam suum esse. Quod quidem multipliciter ostendi potest. Primo quidem, quia quidquid est in aliquo quod est praeter essentiam eius, oportet esse causatum vel a principiis essentiae, sicut accidentia propria consequentia speciem, ut risibile consequitur hominem et causatur ex principiis essentialibus speciei; vel ab aliquo exteriori, sicut calor in aqua causatur ab igne. Si igitur ipsum esse rei sit aliud ab eius essentia, necesse est quod esse illius rei vel sit causatum ab aliquo exteriori, vel a principiis essentialibus eiusdem rei. Impossibile est autem quod esse sit causatum tantum ex principiis essentialibus rei, quia nulla res sufficit quod sit sibi causa essendi, si habeat esse causatum. Oportet ergo quod illud cuius esse est aliud ab essentia sua, habeat esse causatum ab alio. Hoc autem non potest dici de Deo, quia Deum dicimus esse primam causam efficientem. Impossibile est ergo quod in Deo sit aliud esse, et aliud eius essentia. Secundo, quia esse est actualitas omnis formae vel naturae, non enim bonitas vel humanitas significatur in actu, nisi prout significamus eam esse. Oportet igitur quod ipsum esse comparetur ad essentiam quae est aliud ab ipso, sicut actus ad potentiam. Cum igitur in Deo nihil sit potentiale, ut ostensum est supra, sequitur quod non sit aliud in eo essentia quam suum esse. Sua igitur essentia est suum esse. Tertio, quia sicut illud quod habet ignem et non est ignis, est ignitum per participationem, ita illud quod habet esse et non est esse, est ens per participationem. Deus autem est sua essentia, ut ostensum est. Si igitur non sit suum esse, erit ens per participationem, et non per essentiam. Non ergo erit primum ens, quod absurdum est dicere. Est igitur Deus suum esse, et non solum sua essentia.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28352)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aliquid cui non fit additio potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, ut de ratione eius sit quod non fiat ei additio; sicut de ratione animalis irrationalis est, ut sit sine ratione. Alio modo intelligitur aliquid cui non fit additio, quia non est de ratione eius quod sibi fiat additio, sicut animal commune est sine ratione, quia non est de ratione animalis communis ut habeat rationem; sed nec de ratione eius est ut careat ratione. Primo igitur modo, esse sine additione, est esse divinum, secundo modo, esse sine additione, est esse commune.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28353)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod esse dupliciter dicitur, uno modo, significat actum essendi; alio modo, significat compositionem propositionis, quam anima adinvenit coniungens praedicatum subiecto. Primo igitur modo accipiendo esse, non possumus scire esse Dei, sicut nec eius essentiam, sed solum secundo modo. Scimus enim quod haec propositio quam formamus de Deo, cum dicimus Deus est, vera est. Et hoc scimus ex eius effectibus, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 3 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28354)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus sit in genere aliquo. Substantia enim est ens per se subsistens. Hoc autem maxime convenit Deo. Ergo Deus est in genere substantiae.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28355)\n\nPraeterea, unumquodque mensuratur per aliquid sui generis; sicut longitudines per longitudinem, et numeri per numerum. Sed Deus est mensura omnium substantiarum, ut patet per Commentatorem, X Metaphys. Ergo Deus est in genere substantiae.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28356)\n\nSed contra, genus est prius, secundum intellectum, eo quod in genere continetur. Sed nihil est prius Deo, nec secundum rem, nec secundum intellectum. Ergo Deus non est in aliquo genere.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28357)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliquid est in genere dupliciter. Uno modo simpliciter et proprie; sicut species, quae sub genere continentur. Alio modo, per reductionem, sicut principia et privationes, sicut punctus et unitas reducuntur ad genus quantitatis, sicut principia; caecitas autem, et omnis privatio, reducitur ad genus sui habitus. Neutro autem modo Deus est in genere. Quod enim non possit esse species alicuius generis, tripliciter ostendi potest. Primo quidem, quia species constituitur ex genere et differentia. Semper autem id a quo sumitur differentia constituens speciem, se habet ad illud unde sumitur genus, sicut actus ad potentiam. Animal enim sumitur a natura sensitiva per modum concretionis; hoc enim dicitur animal, quod naturam sensitivam habet, rationale vero sumitur a natura intellectiva, quia rationale est quod naturam intellectivam habet, intellectivum autem comparatur ad sensitivum, sicut actus ad potentiam. Et similiter manifestum est in aliis. Unde, cum in Deo non adiungatur potentia actui, impossibile est quod sit in genere tanquam species. Secundo, quia, cum esse Dei sit eius essentia, ut ostensum est, si Deus esset in aliquo genere, oporteret quod genus eius esset ens, nam genus significat essentiam rei, cum praedicetur in eo quod quid est. Ostendit autem philosophus in III Metaphys., quod ens non potest esse genus alicuius, omne enim genus habet differentias quae sunt extra essentiam generis; nulla autem differentia posset inveniri, quae esset extra ens; quia non ens non potest esse differentia. Unde relinquitur quod Deus non sit in genere. Tertio, quia omnia quae sunt in genere uno, communicant in quidditate vel essentia generis, quod praedicatur de eis in eo quod quid est. Differunt autem secundum esse, non enim idem est esse hominis et equi, nec huius hominis et illius hominis. Et sic oportet quod quaecumque sunt in genere, differant in eis esse et quod quid est, idest essentia. In Deo autem non differt, ut ostensum est. Unde manifestum est quod Deus non est in genere sicut species. Et ex hoc patet quod non habet genus, neque differentias; neque est definitio ipsius; neque demonstratio, nisi per effectum, quia definitio est ex genere et differentia, demonstrationis autem medium est definitio. Quod autem Deus non sit in genere per reductionem ut principium, manifestum est ex eo quod principium quod reducitur in aliquod genus, non se extendit ultra genus illud, sicut punctum non est principium nisi quantitatis continuae, et unitas quantitatis discretae. Deus autem est principium totius esse, ut infra ostendetur. Unde non continetur in aliquo genere sicut principium.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28358)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod substantiae nomen non significat hoc solum quod est per se esse, quia hoc quod est esse, non potest per se esse genus, ut ostensum est. Sed significat essentiam cui competit sic esse, idest per se esse, quod tamen esse non est ipsa eius essentia. Et sic patet quod Deus non est in genere substantiae.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28359)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de mensura proportionata, hanc enim oportet esse homogeneam mensurato. Deus autem non est mensura proportionata alicui. Dicitur tamen mensura omnium, ex eo quod unumquodque tantum habet de esse, quantum ei appropinquat.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 3 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28360)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo sint aliqua accidentia. Substantia enim nulli est accidens, ut dicitur in I Physic. Quod ergo in uno est accidens, non potest in alio esse substantia, sicut probatur quod calor non sit forma substantialis ignis, quia in aliis est accidens. Sed sapientia, virtus, et huiusmodi, quae in nobis sunt accidentia, Deo attribuuntur. Ergo et in Deo sunt accidentia.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28361)\n\nPraeterea, in quolibet genere est unum primum. Multa autem sunt genera accidentium. Si igitur prima illorum generum non sunt in Deo, erunt multa prima extra Deum, quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28362)\n\nSed contra, omne accidens in subiecto est. Deus autem non potest esse subiectum, quia *forma simplex non potest esse subiectum*, ut dicit Boetius in Lib. de Trin. Ergo in Deo non potest esse accidens.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28363)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum praemissa, manifeste apparet quod in Deo accidens esse non potest. Primo quidem, quia subiectum comparatur ad accidens, sicut potentia ad actum, subiectum enim secundum accidens est aliquo modo in actu. Esse autem in potentia, omnino removetur a Deo, ut ex praedictis patet. Secundo, quia Deus est suum esse, et, ut Boetius dicit in Lib. de Hebdomad., *licet id quod est, aliquid aliud possit habere adiunctum, tamen ipsum esse nihil aliud adiunctum habere potest*, sicut quod est calidum, potest habere aliquid extraneum quam calidum, ut albedinem; sed ipse calor nihil habet praeter calorem. Tertio, quia omne quod est per se, prius est eo quod est per accidens. Unde, cum Deus sit simpliciter primum ens, in eo non potest esse aliquid per accidens. Sed nec accidentia per se in eo esse possunt, sicut risibile est per se accidens hominis. Quia huiusmodi accidentia causantur ex principiis subiecti, in Deo autem nihil potest esse causatum, cum sit causa prima. Unde relinquitur quod in Deo nullum sit accidens.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28364)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod virtus et sapientia non univoce dicuntur de Deo et de nobis, ut infra patebit. Unde non sequitur quod accidentia sint in Deo, sicut in nobis.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28365)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum substantia sit prior accidentibus, principia accidentium reducuntur in principia substantiae sicut in priora. Quamvis Deus non sit primum contentum in genere substantiae, sed primum extra omne genus, respectu totius esse.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 3 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28366)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit omnino simplex. Ea enim quae sunt a Deo, imitantur ipsum, unde a primo ente sunt omnia entia, et a primo bono sunt omnia bona. Sed in rebus quae sunt a Deo, nihil est omnino simplex. Ergo Deus non est omnino simplex.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28367)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est melius, Deo attribuendum est. Sed, apud nos, composita sunt meliora simplicibus, sicut corpora mixta elementis, et elementa suis partibus. Ergo non est dicendum quod Deus sit omnino simplex.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28368)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod Deus vere et summe simplex est.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28369)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deum omnino esse simplicem, multipliciter potest esse manifestum. Primo quidem per supradicta. Cum enim in Deo non sit compositio, neque quantitativarum partium, quia corpus non est; neque compositio formae et materiae, neque in eo sit aliud natura et suppositum; neque aliud essentia et esse, neque in eo sit compositio generis et differentiae; neque subiecti et accidentis, manifestum est quod Deus nullo modo compositus est, sed est omnino simplex. Secundo, quia omne compositum est posterius suis componentibus, et dependens ex eis. Deus autem est primum ens, ut supra ostensum est. Tertio, quia omne compositum causam habet, quae enim secundum se diversa sunt, non conveniunt in aliquod unum nisi per aliquam causam adunantem ipsa. Deus autem non habet causam, ut supra ostensum est, cum sit prima causa efficiens. Quarto, quia in omni composito oportet esse potentiam et actum, quod in Deo non est, quia vel una partium est actus respectu alterius; vel saltem omnes partes sunt sicut in potentia respectu totius. Quinto, quia omne compositum est aliquid quod non convenit alicui suarum partium. Et quidem in totis dissimilium partium, manifestum est, nulla enim partium hominis est homo, neque aliqua partium pedis est pes. In totis vero similium partium, licet aliquid quod dicitur de toto, dicatur de parte, sicut pars aeris est aer, et aquae aqua; aliquid tamen dicitur de toto, quod non convenit alicui partium, non enim si tota aqua est bicubita, et pars eius. Sic igitur in omni composito est aliquid quod non est ipsum. Hoc autem etsi possit dici de habente formam, quod scilicet habeat aliquid quod non est ipsum (puta in albo est aliquid quod non pertinet ad rationem albi), tamen in ipsa forma nihil est alienum. Unde, cum Deus sit ipsa forma, vel potius ipsum esse, nullo modo compositus esse potest. Et hanc rationem tangit Hilarius, VII de Trin., dicens, *Deus, qui virtus est, ex infirmis non continetur, neque qui lux est, ex obscuris coaptatur*.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28370)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ea quae sunt a Deo, imitantur Deum sicut causata primam causam. Est autem hoc de ratione causati, quod sit aliquo modo compositum, quia ad minus esse eius est aliud quam quod quid est, ut infra patebit.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28371)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod apud nos composita sunt meliora simplicibus, quia perfectio bonitatis creaturae non invenitur in uno simplici, sed in multis. Sed perfectio divinae bonitatis invenitur in uno simplici, ut infra ostendetur.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28372)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus in compositionem aliorum veniat. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV cap. Cael. Hier., *esse omnium est, quae super esse est deitas*. Sed esse omnium intrat compositionem uniuscuiusque. Ergo Deus in compositionem aliorum venit.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28373)\n\nPraeterea, Deus est forma, dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de verbis domini, quod verbum Dei (quod est Deus) est forma quaedam non formata. Sed forma est pars compositi. Ergo Deus est pars alicuius compositi.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28374)\n\nPraeterea, quaecumque sunt et nullo modo differunt, sunt idem. Sed Deus et materia prima sunt, et nullo modo differunt. Ergo penitus sunt idem. Sed materia prima intrat compositionem rerum. Ergo et Deus. Probatio mediae, quaecumque differunt, aliquibus differentiis differunt, et ita oportet ea esse composita; sed Deus et materia prima sunt omnino simplicia; ergo nullo modo differunt.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28375)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, II cap. de Div. Nom., quod neque tactus est eius (scilicet Dei), *neque alia quaedam ad partes commiscendi communio*.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28376)\n\nPraeterea, dicitur in libro de causis, quod *causa prima regit omnes res, praeterquam commisceatur eis*.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28377)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc fuerunt tres errores. Quidam enim posuerunt quod Deus esset anima mundi, ut patet per Augustinum in Lib. VII de civitate Dei, et ad hoc etiam reducitur, quod quidam dixerunt Deum esse animam primi caeli. Alii autem dixerunt Deum esse principium formale omnium rerum. Et haec dicitur fuisse opinio Almarianorum. Sed tertius error fuit David de Dinando, qui stultissime posuit Deum esse materiam primam. Omnia enim haec manifestam continent falsitatem, neque est possibile Deum aliquo modo in compositionem alicuius venire, nec sicut principium formale, nec sicut principium materiale. Primo quidem, quia supra diximus Deum esse primam causam efficientem. Causa autem efficiens cum forma rei factae non incidit in idem numero, sed solum in idem specie, homo enim generat hominem. Materia vero cum causa efficiente non incidit in idem numero, nec in idem specie, quia hoc est in potentia, illud vero in actu. Secundo, quia cum Deus sit prima causa efficiens, eius est primo et per se agere. Quod autem venit in compositionem alicuius, non est primo et per se agens, sed magis compositum, non enim manus agit, sed homo per manum; et ignis calefacit per calorem. Unde Deus non potest esse pars alicuius compositi. Tertio, quia nulla pars compositi potest esse simpliciter prima in entibus; neque etiam materia et forma, quae sunt primae partes compositorum. Nam materia est in potentia, potentia autem est posterior actu simpliciter, ut ex dictis patet. Forma autem quae est pars compositi, est forma participata, sicut autem participans est posterius eo quod est per essentiam, ita et ipsum participatum; sicut ignis in ignitis est posterior eo quod est per essentiam. Ostensum est autem quod Deus est primum ens simpliciter.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28378)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod deitas dicitur esse omnium effective et exemplariter, non autem per essentiam.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28379)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod verbum est forma exemplaris, non autem forma quae est pars compositi.\n\n[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28380)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod simplicia non differunt aliquibus aliis differentiis, hoc enim compositorum est. Homo enim et equus differunt rationali et irrationali differentiis, quae quidem differentiae non differunt amplius ab invicem aliis differentiis. Unde, si fiat vis in verbo, non proprie dicuntur differre, sed diversa esse, nam, secundum philosophum X Metaphys., diversum absolute dicitur, sed omne differens aliquo differt. Unde, si fiat vis in verbo, materia prima et Deus non differunt, sed sunt diversa seipsis. Unde non sequitur quod sint idem.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0002","text":"[I q. 3 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","text":"Cognito de aliquo an sit, inquirendum restat quomodo sit, ut sciatur de eo quid sit. Sed quia de Deo scire non possumus quid sit, sed quid non sit, non possumus considerare de Deo quomodo sit, sed potius quomodo non sit. Primo ergo considerandum est quomodo non sit; secundo, quomodo a nobis cognoscatur; tertio, quomodo nominetur. Potest autem ostendi de Deo quomodo non sit, removendo ab eo ea quae ei non conveniunt, utpote compositionem, motum, et alia huiusmodi. Primo ergo inquiratur de simplicitate ipsius, per quam removetur ab eo compositio. Et quia simplicia in rebus corporalibus sunt imperfecta et partes, secundo inquiretur de perfectione ipsius; tertio, de infinitate eius; quarto, de immutabilitate; quinto, de unitate. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum Deus sit corpus. Secundo, utrum sit in eo compositio formae et materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004","text":"Tertio, utrum sit in eo compositio quidditatis, sive essentiae, vel naturae, et subiecti. Quarto, utrum sit in eo compositio quae est ex essentia et esse. Quinto, utrum sit in eo compositio generis et differentiae. Sexto, utrum sit in eo compositio subiecti et accidentis. Septimo, utrum sit quocumque modo compositus, vel totaliter simplex. Octavo, utrum veniat in compositionem cum aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0006","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus sit corpus. Corpus enim est quod habet trinam dimensionem. Sed sacra Scriptura attribuit Deo trinam dimensionem, dicitur enim Iob XI, *excelsior caelo est, et quid facies? Profundior Inferno, et unde cognosces? Longior terra mensura eius, et latior mari*. Ergo Deus est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0008","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0009","text":"Praeterea, omne figuratum est corpus, cum figura sit qualitas circa quantitatem. Sed Deus videtur esse figuratus, cum scriptum sit Gen. I, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*, figura enim imago dicitur, secundum illud Hebr. I, *cum sit splendor gloriae, et figura substantiae eius*, idest imago. Ergo Deus est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0010","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0011","text":"Praeterea, omne quod habet partes corporeas, est corpus. Sed Scriptura attribuit Deo partes corporeas, dicitur enim Iob XL, *si habes brachium ut Deus*; et in Psalmo, *oculi domini super iustos; et, dextera domini fecit virtutem*. Ergo Deus est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0012","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0013","text":"Praeterea, situs non convenit nisi corpori. Sed ea quae ad situm pertinent, in Scripturis dicuntur de Deo, dicitur enim Isaiae VI, *vidi dominum sedentem*; et Isaiae III, *stat ad iudicandum dominus*. Ergo Deus est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0014","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0015","text":"Praeterea, nihil potest esse terminus localis a quo vel ad quem, nisi sit corpus vel aliquod corporeum. Sed Deus in Scriptura dicitur esse terminus localis ut ad quem, secundum illud Psalmi, *accedite ad eum, et illuminamini*; et ut a quo, secundum illud Hierem. XVII, recedentes a te in terra scribentur. Ergo Deus est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0016","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0017","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. IV, *spiritus est Deus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0018","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","text":"Respondeo dicendum absolute Deum non esse corpus. Quod tripliciter ostendi potest. Primo quidem, quia nullum corpus movet non motum, ut patet inducendo per singula. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus est primum movens immobile. Unde manifestum est quod Deus non est corpus. Secundo, quia necesse est id quod est primum ens, esse in actu, et nullo modo in potentia. Licet enim in uno et eodem quod exit de potentia in actum, prius sit potentia quam actus tempore, simpliciter tamen actus prior est potentia, quia quod est in potentia, non reducitur in actum nisi per ens actu. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus est primum ens. Impossibile est igitur quod in Deo sit aliquid in potentia. Omne autem corpus est in potentia, quia continuum, inquantum huiusmodi, divisibile est in infinitum. Impossibile est igitur Deum esse corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0020","text":"Tertio, quia Deus est id quod est nobilissimum in entibus, ut ex dictis patet. Impossibile est autem aliquod corpus esse nobilissimum in entibus. Quia corpus aut est vivum, aut non vivum. Corpus autem vivum, manifestum est quod est nobilius corpore non vivo. Corpus autem vivum non vivit inquantum corpus, quia sic omne corpus viveret, oportet igitur quod vivat per aliquid aliud, sicut corpus nostrum vivit per animam. Illud autem per quod vivit corpus, est nobilius quam corpus. Impossibile est igitur Deum esse corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0021","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, sacra Scriptura tradit nobis spiritualia et divina sub similitudinibus corporalium. Unde, cum trinam dimensionem Deo attribuit, sub similitudine quantitatis corporeae, quantitatem virtualem ipsius designat, utpote per profunditatem, virtutem ad cognoscendum occulta; per altitudinem, excellentiam virtutis super omnia; per longitudinem, durationem sui esse; per latitudinem, affectum dilectionis ad omnia. Vel, ut dicit Dionysius, cap. IX de Div. Nom., per profunditatem Dei intelligitur incomprehensibilitas ipsius essentiae; per longitudinem, processus virtutis eius, omnia penetrantis; per latitudinem vero, superextensio eius ad omnia, inquantum scilicet sub eius protectione omnia continentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0023","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0024","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod homo dicitur esse ad imaginem Dei, non secundum corpus, sed secundum id quo homo excellit alia animalia, unde, Gen. I, postquam dictum est, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*, subditur, *ut praesit piscibus maris,* et cetera. Excellit autem homo omnia animalia quantum ad rationem et intellectum. Unde secundum intellectum et rationem, quae sunt incorporea, homo est ad imaginem Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0025","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod partes corporeae attribuuntur Deo in Scripturis ratione suorum actuum, secundum quandam similitudinem. Sicut actus oculi est videre, unde oculus de Deo dictus, significat virtutem eius ad videndum modo intelligibili, non sensibili. Et simile est de aliis partibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0027","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod etiam ea quae ad situm pertinent, non attribuuntur Deo nisi secundum quandam similitudinem, sicut dicitur sedens, propter suam immobilitatem et auctoritatem; et stans, propter suam fortitudinem ad debellandum omne quod adversatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0029","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod ad Deum non acceditur passibus corporalibus, cum ubique sit, sed affectibus mentis, et eodem modo ab eo receditur. Et sic accessus et recessus, sub similitudine localis motus, designant spiritualem affectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0031","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0032","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0033","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo sit compositio formae et materiae. Omne enim quod habet animam, est compositum ex materia et forma, quia anima est forma corporis. Sed Scriptura attribuit animam Deo, introducitur enim ad Hebr. X, ex persona Dei, *iustus autem meus ex fide vivit; quod si subtraxerit se, non placebit animae meae*. Ergo Deus est compositus ex materia et forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0034","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0035","text":"Praeterea, ira, gaudium, et huiusmodi, sunt passiones coniuncti, ut dicitur I de anima. Sed huiusmodi attribuuntur Deo in Scriptura dicitur enim in Psalmo, *iratus est furore dominus in populum suum*. Ergo Deus ex materia et forma est compositus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0036","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0037","text":"Praeterea, materia est principium individuationis. Sed Deus videtur esse individuum, non enim de multis praedicatur. Ergo est compositus ex materia et forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0038","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0039","text":"Sed contra, omne compositum ex materia et forma est corpus, quantitas enim dimensiva est quae primo inhaeret materiae. Sed Deus non est corpus, ut ostensum est. Ergo Deus non est compositus ex materia et forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0040","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est in Deo esse materiam. Primo quidem, quia materia est id quod est in potentia. Ostensum est autem quod Deus est purus actus, non habens aliquid de potentialitate. Unde impossibile est quod Deus sit compositus ex materia et forma. Secundo, quia omne compositum ex materia et forma est perfectum et bonum per suam formam, unde oportet quod sit bonum per participationem, secundum quod materia participat formam. Primum autem quod est bonum et optimum, quod Deus est, non est bonum per participationem, quia bonum per essentiam, prius est bono per participationem. Unde impossibile est quod Deus sit compositus ex materia et forma. Tertio, quia unumquodque agens agit per suam formam, unde secundum quod aliquid se habet ad suam formam, sic se habet ad hoc quod sit agens. Quod igitur primum est et per se agens, oportet quod sit primo et per se forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","text":"Deus autem est primum agens, cum sit prima causa efficiens, ut ostensum est. Est igitur per essentiam suam forma; et non compositus ex materia et forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0043","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod anima attribuitur Deo per similitudinem actus. Quod enim volumus aliquid nobis, ex anima nostra est, unde illud dicitur esse placitum animae Dei, quod est placitum voluntati ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0045","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ira et huiusmodi attribuuntur Deo secundum similitudinem effectus, quia enim proprium est irati punire, ira eius punitio metaphorice vocatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0047","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0048","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod formae quae sunt receptibiles in materia individuantur per materiam, quae non potest esse in alio, cum sit primum subiectum substans, forma vero, quantum est de se, nisi aliquid aliud impediat, recipi potest a pluribus. Sed illa forma quae non est receptibilis in materia, sed est per se subsistens, ex hoc ipso individuatur, quod non potest recipi in alio, et huiusmodi forma est Deus. Unde non sequitur quod habeat materiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0049","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0050","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0051","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit idem Deus quod sua essentia vel natura. Nihil enim est in seipso. Sed essentia vel natura Dei, quae est deitas, dicitur esse in Deo. Ergo videtur quod Deus non sit idem quod sua essentia vel natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0052","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0053","text":"Praeterea, effectus assimilatur suae causae, quia omne agens agit sibi simile. Sed in rebus creatis non est idem suppositum quod sua natura, non enim idem est homo quod sua humanitas. Ergo nec Deus est idem quod sua deitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0054","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0055","text":"Contra, de Deo dicitur quod est vita, et non solum quod est vivens, ut patet Ioan. XIV, *ego sum via, veritas et vita*. Sicut autem se habet vita ad viventem, ita deitas ad Deum. Ergo Deus est ipsa deitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0056","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0057","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus est idem quod sua essentia vel natura. Ad cuius intellectum sciendum est, quod in rebus compositis ex materia et forma, necesse est quod differant natura vel essentia et suppositum. Quia essentia vel natura comprehendit in se illa tantum quae cadunt in definitione speciei, sicut humanitas comprehendit in se ea quae cadunt in definitione hominis, his enim homo est homo, et hoc significat humanitas, hoc scilicet quo homo est homo. Sed materia individualis, cum accidentibus omnibus individuantibus ipsam, non cadit in definitione speciei, non enim cadunt in definitione hominis hae carnes et haec ossa, aut albedo vel nigredo, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Unde hae carnes et haec ossa, et accidentia designantia hanc materiam, non concluduntur in humanitate. Et tamen in eo quod est homo, includuntur, unde id quod est homo, habet in se aliquid quod non habet humanitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","text":"Et propter hoc non est totaliter idem homo et humanitas, sed humanitas significatur ut pars formalis hominis; quia principia definientia habent se formaliter, respectu materiae individuantis. In his igitur quae non sunt composita ex materia et forma, in quibus individuatio non est per materiam individualem, idest per hanc materiam, sed ipsae formae per se individuantur, oportet quod ipsae formae sint supposita subsistentia. Unde in eis non differt suppositum et natura. Et sic, cum Deus non sit compositus ex materia et forma, ut ostensum est, oportet quod Deus sit sua deitas, sua vita, et quidquid aliud sic de Deo praedicatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0059","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod de rebus simplicibus loqui non possumus, nisi per modum compositorum, a quibus cognitionem accipimus. Et ideo, de Deo loquentes, utimur nominibus concretis, ut significemus eius subsistentiam, quia apud nos non subsistunt nisi composita, et utimur nominibus abstractis, ut significemus eius simplicitatem. Quod ergo dicitur deitas vel vita, vel aliquid huiusmodi, esse in Deo, referendum est ad diversitatem quae est in acceptione intellectus nostri; et non ad aliquam diversitatem rei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0061","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0062","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod effectus Dei imitantur ipsum, non perfecte, sed secundum quod possunt. Et hoc ad defectum imitationis pertinet, quod id quod est simplex et unum, non potest repraesentari nisi per multa, et sic accidit in eis compositio, ex qua provenit quod in eis non est idem suppositum quod natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0063","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0064","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0065","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit idem essentia et esse. Si enim hoc sit, tunc ad esse divinum nihil additur. Sed esse cui nulla fit additio, est esse commune quod de omnibus praedicatur, sequitur ergo quod Deus sit ens commune praedicabile de omnibus. Hoc autem est falsum, secundum illud Sap. XIV, *incommunicabile nomen lignis et lapidibus imposuerunt*. Ergo esse Dei non est eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0066","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0067","text":"Praeterea, de Deo scire possumus an sit, ut supra dictum est. Non autem possumus scire quid sit. Ergo non est idem esse Dei, et quod quid est eius, sive quidditas vel natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0068","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0069","text":"Sed contra est quod Hilarius dicit in VII de Trin., *esse non est accidens in Deo, sed subsistens veritas*. Id ergo quod subsistit in Deo, est suum esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0070","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus non solum est sua essentia, ut ostensum est, sed etiam suum esse. Quod quidem multipliciter ostendi potest. Primo quidem, quia quidquid est in aliquo quod est praeter essentiam eius, oportet esse causatum vel a principiis essentiae, sicut accidentia propria consequentia speciem, ut risibile consequitur hominem et causatur ex principiis essentialibus speciei; vel ab aliquo exteriori, sicut calor in aqua causatur ab igne. Si igitur ipsum esse rei sit aliud ab eius essentia, necesse est quod esse illius rei vel sit causatum ab aliquo exteriori, vel a principiis essentialibus eiusdem rei. Impossibile est autem quod esse sit causatum tantum ex principiis essentialibus rei, quia nulla res sufficit quod sit sibi causa essendi, si habeat esse causatum. Oportet ergo quod illud cuius esse est aliud ab essentia sua, habeat esse causatum ab alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","text":"Hoc autem non potest dici de Deo, quia Deum dicimus esse primam causam efficientem. Impossibile est ergo quod in Deo sit aliud esse, et aliud eius essentia. Secundo, quia esse est actualitas omnis formae vel naturae, non enim bonitas vel humanitas significatur in actu, nisi prout significamus eam esse. Oportet igitur quod ipsum esse comparetur ad essentiam quae est aliud ab ipso, sicut actus ad potentiam. Cum igitur in Deo nihil sit potentiale, ut ostensum est supra, sequitur quod non sit aliud in eo essentia quam suum esse. Sua igitur essentia est suum esse. Tertio, quia sicut illud quod habet ignem et non est ignis, est ignitum per participationem, ita illud quod habet esse et non est esse, est ens per participationem. Deus autem est sua essentia, ut ostensum est. Si igitur non sit suum esse, erit ens per participationem, et non per essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073","text":"Non ergo erit primum ens, quod absurdum est dicere. Est igitur Deus suum esse, et non solum sua essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0074","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aliquid cui non fit additio potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, ut de ratione eius sit quod non fiat ei additio; sicut de ratione animalis irrationalis est, ut sit sine ratione. Alio modo intelligitur aliquid cui non fit additio, quia non est de ratione eius quod sibi fiat additio, sicut animal commune est sine ratione, quia non est de ratione animalis communis ut habeat rationem; sed nec de ratione eius est ut careat ratione. Primo igitur modo, esse sine additione, est esse divinum, secundo modo, esse sine additione, est esse commune.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0076","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod esse dupliciter dicitur, uno modo, significat actum essendi; alio modo, significat compositionem propositionis, quam anima adinvenit coniungens praedicatum subiecto. Primo igitur modo accipiendo esse, non possumus scire esse Dei, sicut nec eius essentiam, sed solum secundo modo. Scimus enim quod haec propositio quam formamus de Deo, cum dicimus Deus est, vera est. Et hoc scimus ex eius effectibus, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0078","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0079","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0080","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus sit in genere aliquo. Substantia enim est ens per se subsistens. Hoc autem maxime convenit Deo. Ergo Deus est in genere substantiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0081","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0082","text":"Praeterea, unumquodque mensuratur per aliquid sui generis; sicut longitudines per longitudinem, et numeri per numerum. Sed Deus est mensura omnium substantiarum, ut patet per Commentatorem, X Metaphys. Ergo Deus est in genere substantiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0083","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0084","text":"Sed contra, genus est prius, secundum intellectum, eo quod in genere continetur. Sed nihil est prius Deo, nec secundum rem, nec secundum intellectum. Ergo Deus non est in aliquo genere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0085","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0086","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliquid est in genere dupliciter. Uno modo simpliciter et proprie; sicut species, quae sub genere continentur. Alio modo, per reductionem, sicut principia et privationes, sicut punctus et unitas reducuntur ad genus quantitatis, sicut principia; caecitas autem, et omnis privatio, reducitur ad genus sui habitus. Neutro autem modo Deus est in genere. Quod enim non possit esse species alicuius generis, tripliciter ostendi potest. Primo quidem, quia species constituitur ex genere et differentia. Semper autem id a quo sumitur differentia constituens speciem, se habet ad illud unde sumitur genus, sicut actus ad potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0087","text":"Animal enim sumitur a natura sensitiva per modum concretionis; hoc enim dicitur animal, quod naturam sensitivam habet, rationale vero sumitur a natura intellectiva, quia rationale est quod naturam intellectivam habet, intellectivum autem comparatur ad sensitivum, sicut actus ad potentiam. Et similiter manifestum est in aliis. Unde, cum in Deo non adiungatur potentia actui, impossibile est quod sit in genere tanquam species. Secundo, quia, cum esse Dei sit eius essentia, ut ostensum est, si Deus esset in aliquo genere, oporteret quod genus eius esset ens, nam genus significat essentiam rei, cum praedicetur in eo quod quid est. Ostendit autem philosophus in III Metaphys., quod ens non potest esse genus alicuius, omne enim genus habet differentias quae sunt extra essentiam generis; nulla autem differentia posset inveniri, quae esset extra ens; quia non ens non potest esse differentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","text":"Unde relinquitur quod Deus non sit in genere. Tertio, quia omnia quae sunt in genere uno, communicant in quidditate vel essentia generis, quod praedicatur de eis in eo quod quid est. Differunt autem secundum esse, non enim idem est esse hominis et equi, nec huius hominis et illius hominis. Et sic oportet quod quaecumque sunt in genere, differant in eis esse et quod quid est, idest essentia. In Deo autem non differt, ut ostensum est. Unde manifestum est quod Deus non est in genere sicut species. Et ex hoc patet quod non habet genus, neque differentias; neque est definitio ipsius; neque demonstratio, nisi per effectum, quia definitio est ex genere et differentia, demonstrationis autem medium est definitio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","text":"Quod autem Deus non sit in genere per reductionem ut principium, manifestum est ex eo quod principium quod reducitur in aliquod genus, non se extendit ultra genus illud, sicut punctum non est principium nisi quantitatis continuae, et unitas quantitatis discretae. Deus autem est principium totius esse, ut infra ostendetur. Unde non continetur in aliquo genere sicut principium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0090","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0091","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod substantiae nomen non significat hoc solum quod est per se esse, quia hoc quod est esse, non potest per se esse genus, ut ostensum est. Sed significat essentiam cui competit sic esse, idest per se esse, quod tamen esse non est ipsa eius essentia. Et sic patet quod Deus non est in genere substantiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0092","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0093","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de mensura proportionata, hanc enim oportet esse homogeneam mensurato. Deus autem non est mensura proportionata alicui. Dicitur tamen mensura omnium, ex eo quod unumquodque tantum habet de esse, quantum ei appropinquat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0094","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0095","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0096","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo sint aliqua accidentia. Substantia enim nulli est accidens, ut dicitur in I Physic. Quod ergo in uno est accidens, non potest in alio esse substantia, sicut probatur quod calor non sit forma substantialis ignis, quia in aliis est accidens. Sed sapientia, virtus, et huiusmodi, quae in nobis sunt accidentia, Deo attribuuntur. Ergo et in Deo sunt accidentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0097","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0098","text":"Praeterea, in quolibet genere est unum primum. Multa autem sunt genera accidentium. Si igitur prima illorum generum non sunt in Deo, erunt multa prima extra Deum, quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0099","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0100","text":"Sed contra, omne accidens in subiecto est. Deus autem non potest esse subiectum, quia *forma simplex non potest esse subiectum*, ut dicit Boetius in Lib. de Trin. Ergo in Deo non potest esse accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0101","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum praemissa, manifeste apparet quod in Deo accidens esse non potest. Primo quidem, quia subiectum comparatur ad accidens, sicut potentia ad actum, subiectum enim secundum accidens est aliquo modo in actu. Esse autem in potentia, omnino removetur a Deo, ut ex praedictis patet. Secundo, quia Deus est suum esse, et, ut Boetius dicit in Lib. de Hebdomad., *licet id quod est, aliquid aliud possit habere adiunctum, tamen ipsum esse nihil aliud adiunctum habere potest*, sicut quod est calidum, potest habere aliquid extraneum quam calidum, ut albedinem; sed ipse calor nihil habet praeter calorem. Tertio, quia omne quod est per se, prius est eo quod est per accidens. Unde, cum Deus sit simpliciter primum ens, in eo non potest esse aliquid per accidens. Sed nec accidentia per se in eo esse possunt, sicut risibile est per se accidens hominis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103","text":"Quia huiusmodi accidentia causantur ex principiis subiecti, in Deo autem nihil potest esse causatum, cum sit causa prima. Unde relinquitur quod in Deo nullum sit accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0104","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod virtus et sapientia non univoce dicuntur de Deo et de nobis, ut infra patebit. Unde non sequitur quod accidentia sint in Deo, sicut in nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0106","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0107","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum substantia sit prior accidentibus, principia accidentium reducuntur in principia substantiae sicut in priora. Quamvis Deus non sit primum contentum in genere substantiae, sed primum extra omne genus, respectu totius esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0108","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0109","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0110","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit omnino simplex. Ea enim quae sunt a Deo, imitantur ipsum, unde a primo ente sunt omnia entia, et a primo bono sunt omnia bona. Sed in rebus quae sunt a Deo, nihil est omnino simplex. Ergo Deus non est omnino simplex.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0111","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0112","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est melius, Deo attribuendum est. Sed, apud nos, composita sunt meliora simplicibus, sicut corpora mixta elementis, et elementa suis partibus. Ergo non est dicendum quod Deus sit omnino simplex.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0113","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0114","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod Deus vere et summe simplex est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0115","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deum omnino esse simplicem, multipliciter potest esse manifestum. Primo quidem per supradicta. Cum enim in Deo non sit compositio, neque quantitativarum partium, quia corpus non est; neque compositio formae et materiae, neque in eo sit aliud natura et suppositum; neque aliud essentia et esse, neque in eo sit compositio generis et differentiae; neque subiecti et accidentis, manifestum est quod Deus nullo modo compositus est, sed est omnino simplex. Secundo, quia omne compositum est posterius suis componentibus, et dependens ex eis. Deus autem est primum ens, ut supra ostensum est. Tertio, quia omne compositum causam habet, quae enim secundum se diversa sunt, non conveniunt in aliquod unum nisi per aliquam causam adunantem ipsa. Deus autem non habet causam, ut supra ostensum est, cum sit prima causa efficiens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0117","text":"Quarto, quia in omni composito oportet esse potentiam et actum, quod in Deo non est, quia vel una partium est actus respectu alterius; vel saltem omnes partes sunt sicut in potentia respectu totius. Quinto, quia omne compositum est aliquid quod non convenit alicui suarum partium. Et quidem in totis dissimilium partium, manifestum est, nulla enim partium hominis est homo, neque aliqua partium pedis est pes. In totis vero similium partium, licet aliquid quod dicitur de toto, dicatur de parte, sicut pars aeris est aer, et aquae aqua; aliquid tamen dicitur de toto, quod non convenit alicui partium, non enim si tota aqua est bicubita, et pars eius. Sic igitur in omni composito est aliquid quod non est ipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0118","text":"Hoc autem etsi possit dici de habente formam, quod scilicet habeat aliquid quod non est ipsum (puta in albo est aliquid quod non pertinet ad rationem albi), tamen in ipsa forma nihil est alienum. Unde, cum Deus sit ipsa forma, vel potius ipsum esse, nullo modo compositus esse potest. Et hanc rationem tangit Hilarius, VII de Trin., dicens, *Deus, qui virtus est, ex infirmis non continetur, neque qui lux est, ex obscuris coaptatur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0119","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0120","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ea quae sunt a Deo, imitantur Deum sicut causata primam causam. Est autem hoc de ratione causati, quod sit aliquo modo compositum, quia ad minus esse eius est aliud quam quod quid est, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0121","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod apud nos composita sunt meliora simplicibus, quia perfectio bonitatis creaturae non invenitur in uno simplici, sed in multis. Sed perfectio divinae bonitatis invenitur in uno simplici, ut infra ostendetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0123","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0124","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0125","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus in compositionem aliorum veniat. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV cap. Cael. Hier., *esse omnium est, quae super esse est deitas*. Sed esse omnium intrat compositionem uniuscuiusque. Ergo Deus in compositionem aliorum venit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0126","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0127","text":"Praeterea, Deus est forma, dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de verbis domini, quod verbum Dei (quod est Deus) est forma quaedam non formata. Sed forma est pars compositi. Ergo Deus est pars alicuius compositi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0128","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0129","text":"Praeterea, quaecumque sunt et nullo modo differunt, sunt idem. Sed Deus et materia prima sunt, et nullo modo differunt. Ergo penitus sunt idem. Sed materia prima intrat compositionem rerum. Ergo et Deus. Probatio mediae, quaecumque differunt, aliquibus differentiis differunt, et ita oportet ea esse composita; sed Deus et materia prima sunt omnino simplicia; ergo nullo modo differunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0130","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0131","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, II cap. de Div. Nom., quod neque tactus est eius (scilicet Dei), *neque alia quaedam ad partes commiscendi communio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0132","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0133","text":"Praeterea, dicitur in libro de causis, quod *causa prima regit omnes res, praeterquam commisceatur eis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0134","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc fuerunt tres errores. Quidam enim posuerunt quod Deus esset anima mundi, ut patet per Augustinum in Lib. VII de civitate Dei, et ad hoc etiam reducitur, quod quidam dixerunt Deum esse animam primi caeli. Alii autem dixerunt Deum esse principium formale omnium rerum. Et haec dicitur fuisse opinio Almarianorum. Sed tertius error fuit David de Dinando, qui stultissime posuit Deum esse materiam primam. Omnia enim haec manifestam continent falsitatem, neque est possibile Deum aliquo modo in compositionem alicuius venire, nec sicut principium formale, nec sicut principium materiale. Primo quidem, quia supra diximus Deum esse primam causam efficientem. Causa autem efficiens cum forma rei factae non incidit in idem numero, sed solum in idem specie, homo enim generat hominem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","text":"Materia vero cum causa efficiente non incidit in idem numero, nec in idem specie, quia hoc est in potentia, illud vero in actu. Secundo, quia cum Deus sit prima causa efficiens, eius est primo et per se agere. Quod autem venit in compositionem alicuius, non est primo et per se agens, sed magis compositum, non enim manus agit, sed homo per manum; et ignis calefacit per calorem. Unde Deus non potest esse pars alicuius compositi. Tertio, quia nulla pars compositi potest esse simpliciter prima in entibus; neque etiam materia et forma, quae sunt primae partes compositorum. Nam materia est in potentia, potentia autem est posterior actu simpliciter, ut ex dictis patet. Forma autem quae est pars compositi, est forma participata, sicut autem participans est posterius eo quod est per essentiam, ita et ipsum participatum; sicut ignis in ignitis est posterior eo quod est per essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0137","text":"Ostensum est autem quod Deus est primum ens simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0138","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod deitas dicitur esse omnium effective et exemplariter, non autem per essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0140","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0141","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod verbum est forma exemplaris, non autem forma quae est pars compositi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0142","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0143","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod simplicia non differunt aliquibus aliis differentiis, hoc enim compositorum est. Homo enim et equus differunt rationali et irrationali differentiis, quae quidem differentiae non differunt amplius ab invicem aliis differentiis. Unde, si fiat vis in verbo, non proprie dicuntur differre, sed diversa esse, nam, secundum philosophum X Metaphys., diversum absolute dicitur, sed omne differens aliquo differt. Unde, si fiat vis in verbo, materia prima et Deus non differunt, sed sunt diversa seipsis. Unde non sequitur quod sint idem.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ad3f7377fe48cd25de338c119d62e048c85ab26d4c4c45f8fbeaf7fe5ca0c624","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T133624374438Z_890cea58","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 3","source_sha256":"ad3f7377fe48cd25de338c119d62e048c85ab26d4c4c45f8fbeaf7fe5ca0c624","sha256":"64d281867a347d1df45cd201b73c8053b349a30a96178630a603b1d7493787f1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:36:24.374451+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0002","text":"[I q. 3 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","text":"Once it has been known of something whether it is, it remains to inquire how it is, so that it may be known what it is. But because concerning God we cannot know what He is, but [only] what He is not, we cannot consider concerning God how He is, but rather how He is not. Therefore, first it must be considered how He is not; second, how He is known by us; third, how He is named. Now it can be shown concerning God how He is not by removing from Him those things that do not befit Him, such as composition, motion, and other things of this kind. Therefore, first let inquiry be made concerning His simplicity, through which composition is removed from Him. And because simple things among corporeal things are imperfect and parts, second, inquiry will be made concerning His perfection; third, concerning His infinity; fourth, concerning His immutability; fifth, concerning His unity. Concerning the first, eight questions are asked. First, whether God is a body. Second, whether there is in Him a composition of form and matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004","text":"Third, whether there is in Him a composition of quiddity, or essence, or nature, and subject. Fourth, whether there is in Him a composition that is from essence and being. Fifth, whether there is in Him a composition of genus and difference. Sixth, whether there is in Him a composition of subject and accident. Seventh, whether He is composite in any way, or altogether simple. Eighth, whether He enters into composition with other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0006","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0007","text":"Concerning the first, the procedure is as follows. It seems that God is a body. For a body is that which has threefold dimension. But sacred Scripture attributes threefold dimension to God, for it is said in Job XI: *He is higher than heaven, and what will you do? Deeper than Hell, and how will you know? His measure is longer than the earth and broader than the sea.* Therefore God is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0008","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0009","text":"Further, everything figured is a body, since figure is a quality concerning quantity. But God seems to be figured, since it is written in Gen. I, *Let us make man in our image and likeness*; for a figure is called an image, according to Heb. I, *being the splendor of glory, and the figure of His substance*, that is, image. Therefore God is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0010","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0011","text":"Further, everything that has bodily parts is a body. But Scripture attributes bodily parts to God, for it is said in Job XL, *If you have an arm like God*; and in the Psalm, *The eyes of the Lord are upon the just*; and, *The right hand of the Lord has wrought power*. Therefore God is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0012","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0013","text":"Further, position belongs only to a body. But things pertaining to position are said of God in the Scriptures, for it is said in Isaiah VI, *I saw the Lord sitting*; and in Isaiah III, *The Lord stands to judge*. Therefore God is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0014","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0015","text":"Further, nothing can be a local terminus from which or to which unless it is a body or something bodily. But in Scripture God is said to be a local terminus as that to which, according to the Psalm, *Draw near to Him, and be enlightened*; and as that from which, according to Jer. XVII, *Those departing from You shall be written in the earth*. Therefore God is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0016","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0017","text":"On the contrary, it is said in John IV, *God is spirit*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0018","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","text":"I answer that it must be said that God absolutely is not a body. This can be shown in three ways. First, indeed, because no body moves without being moved, as is clear by induction through individual cases. But it was shown above that God is the first unmoved mover. Hence it is manifest that God is not a body. Second, because it is necessary that that which is the first being be in act, and in no way in potency. For although, in one and the same thing which passes from potency into act, potency is prior to act in time, nevertheless, absolutely speaking, act is prior to potency, because what is in potency is not reduced to act except by a being in act. But it was shown above that God is the first being. Therefore it is impossible that there be anything in God in potency. But every body is in potency, because a continuum, as such, is divisible to infinity. Therefore it is impossible for God to be a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0020","text":"Third, because God is that which is most noble among beings, as is clear from what has been said. But it is impossible that any body be most noble among beings. For a body either is living or non-living. Now it is manifest that a living body is more noble than a non-living body. But a living body does not live insofar as it is a body, because then every body would live; therefore it must live through something else, just as our body lives through the soul. But that through which a body lives is more noble than the body. Therefore it is impossible for God to be a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0021","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as was said above, sacred Scripture conveys spiritual and divine things to us under likenesses of bodily things. Hence, when it attributes threefold dimension to God, under the likeness of bodily quantity it signifies His virtual quantity: namely, by depth, His power for knowing hidden things; by height, the excellence of His power above all things; by length, the duration of His being; by breadth, the affection of love toward all things. Or, as Dionysius says, ch. IX of *On the Divine Names*, by God's depth is understood the incomprehensibility of His essence; by length, the procession of His power, penetrating all things; but by breadth, His superextension toward all things, insofar as all things are contained under His protection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0023","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0024","text":"To the second it must be said that man is said to be in the image of God, not according to the body, but according to that whereby man excels the other animals; whence, in Gen. I, after it has been said, *Let us make man in our image and likeness*, there is added, *that he may rule over the fish of the sea,* and so forth. Now man excels all animals with respect to reason and intellect. Hence according to intellect and reason, which are incorporeal, man is in the image of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0025","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","text":"To the third, it must be said that bodily parts are attributed to God in the Scriptures by reason of their acts, according to a certain likeness. For example, the act of the eye is to see; hence, when an eye is spoken of God, it signifies His power to see in an intelligible, not a sensible, manner. And it is similar regarding the other parts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0027","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that even those things which pertain to position are not attributed to God except according to a certain likeness: thus He is said to be sitting, on account of His immovability and authority; and standing, on account of His strength to overcome everything that opposes Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0029","text":"[I q. 3 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that one does not approach God by bodily steps, since He is everywhere, but by the affections of the mind; and in the same way one withdraws from Him. And thus approach and withdrawal, under the likeness of local motion, denote a spiritual affection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0031","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0032","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0033","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there is a composition of form and matter in God. For everything that has a soul is composed of matter and form, because the soul is the form of the body. But Scripture attributes a soul to God; for it is introduced in Hebrews X, in the person of God: *but my just one lives by faith; but if he withdraw himself, my soul shall not be pleased*. Therefore God is composed of matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0034","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0035","text":"Further, anger, joy, and the like are passions of the composite, as is said in I *On the Soul*. But things of this sort are attributed to God in Scripture; for it is said in the Psalm, *the Lord was angered in wrath against His people*. Therefore God is composed of matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0036","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0037","text":"Further, matter is the principle of individuation. But God seems to be an individual, for He is not predicated of many. Therefore He is composed of matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0038","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0039","text":"On the contrary, everything composed of matter and form is a body, for dimensional quantity is that which first inheres in matter. But God is not a body, as has been shown. Therefore God is not composed of matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0040","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible for there to be matter in God. First, indeed, because matter is that which is in potency. But it has been shown that God is pure act, having nothing of potentiality. Hence it is impossible that God should be composed of matter and form. Second, because everything composed of matter and form is perfect and good through its form; whence it must be good by participation, insofar as matter participates in form. But that which is the first good and the highest good, which God is, is not good by participation, because that which is good by essence is prior to that which is good by participation. Hence it is impossible that God should be composed of matter and form. Third, because every agent acts through its form; hence, according as something is related to its form, so is it related to being an agent. Therefore, that which is first and acts through itself must be form first and through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","text":"But God is the first agent, since He is the first efficient cause, as has been shown. Therefore He is form through His essence; and not composed of matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0043","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a soul is attributed to God by a likeness of act. For it is from our soul that we will something for ourselves; hence that is said to be pleasing to the soul of God which is pleasing to His will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0045","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","text":"To the second, it must be said that anger and the like are attributed to God according to a likeness of effect, for since it is proper to one who is angry to punish, His punishment is metaphorically called anger.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0047","text":"[I q. 3 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0048","text":"To the third, it must be said that forms which are receivable in matter are individuated through matter, which cannot be in another, since it is the first subsisting subject; whereas form, so far as it is in itself, unless something else prevents it, can be received by many. But that form which is not receivable in matter, but is subsistent through itself, is individuated by this very fact, that it cannot be received in another; and God is a form of this kind. Hence it does not follow that He has matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0049","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0050","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0051","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that God is not the same as his essence or nature. For nothing is in itself. But the essence or nature of God, which is deity, is said to be in God. Therefore it seems that God is not the same as his essence or nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0052","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0053","text":"Further, an effect is assimilated to its cause, because every agent acts producing something like itself. But in created things the supposit is not the same as its nature; for man is not the same as his humanity. Therefore neither is God the same as his deity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0054","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0055","text":"On the contrary, it is said of God that he is life, and not merely that he is living, as is clear in John XIV: *I am the way, the truth, and the life*. Now deity is related to God as life is related to the living one. Therefore God is deity itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0056","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0057","text":"I answer that it must be said that God is the same as his essence or nature. For the understanding of this, it should be known that in things composed of matter and form, nature or essence and supposit must differ. For essence or nature includes in itself only those things which fall under the definition of the species; just as humanity includes in itself those things which fall under the definition of man, for by these man is man, and this is what humanity signifies, namely, that whereby man is man. But individual matter, together with all the accidents individuating it, does not fall under the definition of the species; for these flesh and these bones, or whiteness or blackness, or anything of this sort, do not fall under the definition of man. Hence these flesh and these bones, and the accidents designating this matter, are not included in humanity. Yet they are included in that which is man; hence that which is man has in itself something which humanity does not have.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","text":"And for this reason man and humanity are not altogether the same, but humanity is signified as the formal part of man; because the defining principles are related formally with respect to individuating matter. Therefore, in those things which are not composed of matter and form, in which individuation is not through individual matter, that is, through this matter, but the forms themselves are individuated through themselves, the forms themselves must be subsisting supposits. Hence in them supposit and nature do not differ. And thus, since God is not composed of matter and form, as has been shown, God must be his deity, his life, and whatever else is thus predicated of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0059","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that we cannot speak of simple things except in the manner of composite things, from which we receive our knowledge. And therefore, in speaking of God, we use concrete names, in order to signify his subsistence, because among us only composite things subsist; and we use abstract names, in order to signify his simplicity. Therefore, when deity or life, or anything of this sort, is said to be in God, this must be referred to the diversity that exists in the apprehension of our intellect, and not to any diversity of the thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0061","text":"[I q. 3 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0062","text":"To the second, it must be said that God's effects imitate him, not perfectly, but insofar as they can. And it pertains to the defect of the imitation that what is simple and one cannot be represented except by many; and thus composition occurs in them, from which it follows that in them the supposit is not the same as the nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0063","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0064","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0065","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that in God essence and being are not the same. For if this were so, then nothing would be added to divine being. But being to which no addition is made is common being, which is predicated of all things; therefore it follows that God is common being, predicable of all things. But this is false, according to Wisdom XIV: *they bestowed the incommunicable name upon wood and stones*. Therefore God's being is not his essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0066","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0067","text":"Further, we can know of God whether he is, as was said above. But we cannot know what he is. Therefore God's being is not the same as what he is, or his quiddity or nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0068","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0069","text":"But on the contrary, Hilary says in *On the Trinity*, VII: *being is not an accident in God, but subsisting truth*. Therefore that which subsists in God is his being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0070","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","text":"I answer that it must be said that God is not only his essence, as has been shown, but also his being. This can be shown in many ways. First, because whatever is in something besides its essence must be caused either by the principles of the essence, as proper accidents consequent upon the species—for example, risibility follows man and is caused by the essential principles of the species—or by something exterior, as heat in water is caused by fire. Therefore, if the very being of a thing is other than its essence, the being of that thing must be caused either by something exterior or by the essential principles of that same thing. But it is impossible for being to be caused solely by the essential principles of a thing, because no thing is sufficient to be the cause of its own being, if it has caused being. Therefore that whose being is other than its essence must have being caused by another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","text":"But this cannot be said of God, because we say that God is the first efficient cause. Therefore it is impossible that in God being should be one thing and his essence another. Second, because being is the actuality of every form or nature; for goodness or humanity is not signified as actual except insofar as we signify it to be. Therefore being itself must be related to an essence which is other than it as act to potency. Therefore, since there is nothing potential in God, as was shown above, it follows that his essence is not other than his being. Therefore his essence is his being. Third, because just as that which has fire and is not fire is fiery by participation, so that which has being and is not being is a being by participation. But God is his essence, as has been shown. Therefore, if he is not his being, he will be a being by participation and not by essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073","text":"Therefore there will not be a first being, which is absurd to say. Therefore God is His own being, and not merely His own essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0074","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that something to which no addition is made can be understood in two ways. In one way, such that it belongs to its intelligible account that no addition be made to it; as it belongs to the intelligible account of an irrational animal that it be without reason. In another way, something to which no addition is made is understood because it does not belong to its intelligible account that addition be made to it, just as the common animal is without reason, because it does not belong to the intelligible account of the common animal to have reason; but neither does it belong to its intelligible account to lack reason. Therefore, in the first way, being without addition is divine being; in the second way, being without addition is common being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0076","text":"[I q. 3 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","text":"To the second, it must be said that being is spoken of in two ways: in one way, it signifies the act of being; in another way, it signifies the composition of a proposition, which the soul devises by joining the predicate to the subject. Therefore, taking being in the first way, we cannot know God's being, just as neither can we know His essence, but only in the second way. For we know that this proposition which we form concerning God, when we say “God is,” is true. And we know this from His effects, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0078","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0079","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0080","text":"Thus one proceeds to the fifth. It seems that God is in some genus. For substance is a being subsisting through itself. But this belongs most of all to God. Therefore God is in the genus of substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0081","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0082","text":"Moreover, each thing is measured by something of its own genus; as lengths by length, and numbers by number. But God is the measure of all substances, as is clear from the Commentator, Metaphysics X. Therefore God is in the genus of substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0083","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0084","text":"But on the contrary, a genus is prior, according to the intellect, to that which is contained in the genus. But nothing is prior to God, neither in reality nor according to the intellect. Therefore God is not in any genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0085","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0086","text":"I answer that it must be said that something is in a genus in two ways. In one way, simply and properly, as species, which are contained under a genus. In another way, by reduction, as principles and privations: thus a point and unity are reduced to the genus of quantity as principles; but blindness, and every privation, is reduced to the genus of its habit. But God is in a genus in neither way. For that He cannot be a species of any genus can be shown in three ways. First, indeed, because a species is constituted from genus and difference. But that from which the difference constituting a species is taken always stands in relation to that from which the genus is taken as act to potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0087","text":"For animal is taken from sensitive nature by way of concretion; for that which has sensitive nature is called an animal. But rational is taken from intellectual nature, because rational is that which has intellectual nature; and intellectual is related to sensitive as act to potency. And similarly it is evident in other cases. Hence, since potency is not joined to act in God, it is impossible that He be in a genus as a species. Second, because, since the being of God is His essence, as has been shown, if God were in some genus, it would be necessary that His genus be being, for genus signifies the essence of a thing, since it is predicated in answer to the question what it is. But the Philosopher shows in Metaphysics III that being cannot be the genus of anything, for every genus has differences that are outside the essence of the genus; but no difference could be found that was outside being, because non-being cannot be a difference.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","text":"Hence it remains that God is not in a genus. Third, because all things that are in one genus share in the quiddity or essence of the genus, which is predicated of them in answer to the question what it is. But they differ according to being; for the being of a man and of a horse is not the same, nor that of this man and that man. And thus it is necessary that whatever things are in a genus differ in them in being and what-it-is, that is, essence. But in God these do not differ, as has been shown. Hence it is manifest that God is not in a genus as a species. And from this it is clear that He has neither genus nor differences; nor is there a definition of Him; nor a demonstration, except through an effect, because a definition is from genus and difference, while the middle term of demonstration is a definition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","text":"But that God is not in a genus by reduction as a principle is evident from the fact that a principle which is reduced to some genus does not extend beyond that genus, just as a point is a principle only of continuous quantity, and unity of discrete quantity. But God is the principle of all being, as will be shown below. Hence He is not contained in any genus as a principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0090","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0091","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the name substance does not signify merely that which is to be through itself, because that which is being cannot itself be a genus, as has been shown. But it signifies an essence to which it belongs to be thus, that is, to be through itself, although that being is not its very essence. And thus it is clear that God is not in the genus of substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0092","text":"[I q. 3 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0093","text":"To the second, it must be said that that objection proceeds from a proportionate measure, for this must be homogeneous with what is measured. But God is not a proportionate measure of anything. Nevertheless, He is called the measure of all things, from the fact that each thing has being to the extent that it approaches Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0094","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0095","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0096","text":"Thus one proceeds to the sixth. It seems that there are some accidents in God. For substance is an accident of nothing, as is said in Physics I. Therefore, that which is an accident in one thing cannot be a substance in another, just as it is proved that heat is not the substantial form of fire because in other things it is an accident. But wisdom, virtue, and the like, which are accidents in us, are attributed to God. Therefore there are accidents in God as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0097","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0098","text":"Moreover, in every genus there is one first. But there are many genera of accidents. Therefore, if the first things of those genera are not in God, there will be many first things outside God, which is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0099","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0100","text":"But on the contrary, every accident is in a subject. But God cannot be a subject, because *a simple form cannot be a subject*, as Boethius says in the Book on the Trinity. Therefore, there cannot be an accident in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0101","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","text":"I answer that it must be said that, according to what has been set forth, it is manifest that there cannot be an accident in God. First, indeed, because a subject is related to an accident as potency to act; for a subject, insofar as regards an accident, is in act in some way. But to be in potency is altogether excluded from God, as is clear from what has been said. Second, because God is His own being, and, as Boethius says in the Book on the Hebdomads, *although that which is can have something else joined to it, being itself can have nothing else joined to it*; just as that which is hot can have something extraneous to hotness, such as whiteness, but heat itself has nothing besides heat. Third, because everything which is through itself is prior to that which is through accident. Hence, since God is being simply first, there cannot be anything in Him through accident. But neither can per se accidents be in Him, as the capable of laughter is a per se accident of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103","text":"For accidents of this kind are caused from the principles of the subject; but nothing can be caused in God, since He is the first cause. Hence it remains that there is no accident in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0104","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that power and wisdom are not said univocally of God and of us, as will become clear below. Hence it does not follow that accidents are in God as they are in us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0106","text":"[I q. 3 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0107","text":"To the second it must be said that, since substance is prior to accidents, the principles of accidents are reduced to the principles of substance as to prior principles. Although God is not the first contained in the genus of substance, but is first outside every genus, with respect to all being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0108","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0109","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0110","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that God is not altogether simple. For those things which are from God imitate Him; hence from the first being are all beings, and from the first good are all goods. But among the things which are from God, nothing is altogether simple. Therefore God is not altogether simple.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0111","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0112","text":"Moreover, everything that is better is to be attributed to God. But among us, composite things are better than simple things, as mixed bodies are better than elements, and elements than their parts. Therefore it should not be said that God is altogether simple.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0113","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0114","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in VI On the Trinity, that God is truly and supremely simple.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0115","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","text":"I answer that it must be said that it can be made manifest in many ways that God is altogether simple. First, indeed, through the things said above. For since there is no composition in God—neither of quantitative parts, because He is not a body; nor composition of form and matter; nor is nature and supposit distinct in Him; nor essence and being; nor is there composition of genus and difference in Him; nor of subject and accident—it is manifest that God is in no way composite, but is altogether simple. Second, because every composite is posterior to and dependent upon its components. But God is the first being, as was shown above. Third, because every composite has a cause: for things which are diverse in themselves do not come together into some one thing except through some cause uniting them. But God has no cause, as was shown above, since He is the first efficient cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0117","text":"Fourth, because in every composite there must be potency and act, which is not the case in God: for either one of the parts is act with respect to the other; or at least all the parts are as potency with respect to the whole. Fifth, because every composite is something that does not belong to any one of its parts. And indeed, in wholes of dissimilar parts, this is manifest: for no part of a man is a man, nor is any part of a foot a foot. But in wholes of similar parts, although something which is said of the whole is said of the part, as a part of air is air and a part of water water, nevertheless something is said of the whole which does not belong to any of the parts: for if the whole water is two cubits long, so is not its part. Thus, therefore, in every composite there is something which is not itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0118","text":"But although this can be said of that which has a form, namely, that it has something which is not itself (for example, in a white thing there is something that does not pertain to the account of white), nevertheless in the form itself there is nothing alien. Hence, since God is the form itself, or rather being itself, He can in no way be composite. And Hilary touches upon this reason, in VII On the Trinity, saying, *God, who is power, is not contained by weak things; nor is He who is light fitted together out of dark things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0119","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0120","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that those things which are from God imitate God as caused things imitate the first cause. But it belongs to the notion of a caused thing that it be composite in some way, because at least its being is other than what it is, as will become clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0121","text":"[I q. 3 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122","text":"To the second, it must be said that among us composite things are better than simple things, because the perfection of a creature’s goodness is not found in one simple thing, but in many. But the perfection of divine goodness is found in one simple thing, as will be shown below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0123","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0124","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0125","text":"The eighth is approached thus. It seems that God enters into the composition of other things. For Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, “the divinity which is above being is the being of all things.” But the being of all things enters into the composition of each thing. Therefore God enters into the composition of other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0126","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0127","text":"Further, God is form; for Augustine says, in the book *On the Words of the Lord*, that the Word of God (which is God) is a certain unformed form. But form is a part of a composite. Therefore God is part of some composite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0128","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0129","text":"Further, whatever things exist and in no way differ are the same. But God and prime matter exist, and in no way differ. Therefore they are altogether the same. But prime matter enters into the composition of things. Therefore so too does God. Proof of the middle proposition: whatever things differ, differ by some differences, and thus they must be composite; but God and prime matter are altogether simple; therefore they in no way differ.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0130","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0131","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter II of *On the Divine Names*, that there is neither contact with Him, namely God, “nor any other communion of mingling into parts.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0132","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0133","text":"Further, it is said in the *Book of Causes* that “the first cause governs all things without being mingled with them.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0134","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","text":"I answer that it must be said that there were three errors concerning this matter. For some held that God was the soul of the world, as is clear from Augustine in Book VII of *The City of God*; and reducible to this also is the claim of some that God is the soul of the first heaven. Others, however, said that God is the formal principle of all things. And this is said to have been the opinion of the Amalricians. But the third error was that of David of Dinant, who most foolishly held that God is prime matter. For all these contain manifest falsehood, nor is it possible for God in any way to enter into the composition of anything, either as a formal principle or as a material principle. First indeed, because we have said above that God is the first efficient cause. But the efficient cause and the form of the thing made do not coincide in the same individual, but only in the same species; for man generates man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","text":"But matter and the efficient cause do not coincide in the same individual, nor in the same species, because the former is in potency, whereas the latter is in act. Second, because, since God is the first efficient cause, it belongs to Him to act first and through Himself. But that which enters into the composition of something is not what acts first and through itself; rather, it is the composite that does so. For the hand does not act, but the man acts through the hand; and fire heats through heat. Hence God cannot be part of any composite. Third, because no part of a composite can be simply first among beings; not even matter and form, which are the first parts of composites. For matter is in potency, and potency is simply posterior to act, as is clear from what has been said. But the form which is part of a composite is a participated form; and just as that which participates is posterior to that which is through essence, so too is the participated itself; just as fire in fiery things is posterior to that which is fire through essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0137","text":"But it has been shown that God is simply the first being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0138","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that divinity is said to be the being of all things effectively and exemplarily, but not through essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0140","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0141","text":"To the second, it must be said that the Word is an exemplar form, but not a form which is a part of a composite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0142","text":"[I q. 3 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0143","text":"To the third, it must be said that simple things do not differ by any other differences, for this belongs to composite things. For man and horse differ by the differences rational and irrational; and these differences themselves do not further differ from one another by other differences. Hence, if strict attention is paid to the word, they are not properly said to differ, but to be diverse; for, according to the Philosopher in *Metaphysics* X, “diverse” is said absolutely, whereas everything that differs differs by something. Hence, if strict attention is paid to the word, prime matter and God do not differ, but are diverse through themselves. Hence it does not follow that they are the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0143"]}],"source_digest":"68d23782c27870eaca493ee44f9f4bf100f620d2999eb2d62c4558a34c0941d9","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 3"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4","title":"First Part · Question 4","order":5,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 4 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28381)\n\nPost considerationem divinae simplicitatis, de perfectione ipsius Dei dicendum est. Et quia unumquodque, secundum quod perfectum est, sic dicitur bonum, primo agendum est de perfectione divina; secundo de eius bonitate. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Deus sit perfectus. Secundo, utrum Deus sit universaliter perfectus omnium in se perfectiones habens. Tertio, utrum creaturae similes Deo dici possint.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28382)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse perfectum non conveniat Deo. Perfectum enim dicitur quasi totaliter factum. Sed Deo non convenit esse factum. Ergo nec esse perfectum.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28383)\n\nPraeterea, Deus est primum rerum principium. Sed principia rerum videntur esse imperfecta, semen enim est principium animalium et plantarum. Ergo Deus est imperfectus.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28384)\n\nPraeterea, ostensum est supra quod essentia Dei est ipsum esse. Sed ipsum esse videtur esse imperfectissimum, cum sit communissimum, et recipiens omnium additiones. Ergo Deus est imperfectus.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28385)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Matt. V, *estote perfecti, sicut et pater vester caelestis perfectus est*.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28386)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut philosophus narrat in XII Metaphys., quidam antiqui philosophi, scilicet Pythagorici et Speusippus, non attribuerunt optimum et perfectissimum primo principio. Cuius ratio est, quia philosophi antiqui consideraverunt principium materiale tantum, primum autem principium materiale imperfectissimum est. Cum enim materia, inquantum huiusmodi, sit in potentia, oportet quod primum principium materiale sit maxime in potentia; et ita maxime imperfectum. Deus autem ponitur primum principium, non materiale, sed in genere causae efficientis, et hoc oportet esse perfectissimum. Sicut enim materia, inquantum huiusmodi, est in potentia; ita agens, inquantum huiusmodi, est in actu. Unde primum principium activum oportet maxime esse in actu, et per consequens maxime esse perfectum. Secundum hoc enim dicitur aliquid esse perfectum, secundum quod est actu, nam perfectum dicitur, cui nihil deest secundum modum suae perfectionis.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28387)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Gregorius, *balbutiendo ut possumus, excelsa Dei resonamus, quod enim factum non est, perfectum proprie dici non potest*. Sed quia in his quae fiunt, tunc dicitur esse aliquid perfectum, cum de potentia educitur in actum; transumitur hoc nomen perfectum ad significandum omne illud cui non deest esse in actu, sive hoc habeat per modum factionis, sive non.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28388)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod principium materiale, quod apud nos imperfectum invenitur, non potest esse simpliciter primum, sed praeceditur ab alio perfecto. Nam semen, licet sit principium animalis generati ex semine, tamen habet ante se animal vel plantam unde deciditur. Oportet enim ante id quod est in potentia, esse aliquid actu, cum ens in potentia non reducatur in actum, nisi per aliquod ens in actu.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28389)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ipsum esse est perfectissimum omnium, comparatur enim ad omnia ut actus. Nihil enim habet actualitatem, nisi inquantum est, unde ipsum esse est actualitas omnium rerum, et etiam ipsarum formarum. Unde non comparatur ad alia sicut recipiens ad receptum, sed magis sicut receptum ad recipiens. Cum enim dico esse hominis, vel equi, vel cuiuscumque alterius, ipsum esse consideratur ut formale et receptum, non autem ut illud cui competit esse.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28390)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sint perfectiones omnium rerum. Deus enim simplex est, ut ostensum est. Sed perfectiones rerum sunt multae et diversae. Ergo in Deo non sunt omnes perfectiones rerum.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28391)\n\nPraeterea, opposita non possunt esse in eodem. Sed perfectiones rerum sunt oppositae, unaquaeque enim species perficitur per suam differentiam specificam; differentiae autem quibus dividitur genus et constituuntur species, sunt oppositae. Cum ergo opposita non possint simul esse in eodem, videtur quod non omnes rerum perfectiones sint in Deo.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28392)\n\nPraeterea, vivens est perfectius quam ens, et sapiens quam vivens, ergo et vivere est perfectius quam esse, et sapere quam vivere. Sed essentia Dei est ipsum esse. Ergo non habet in se perfectionem vitae et sapientiae, et alias huiusmodi perfectiones.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28393)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, cap. V de Div. Nom., quod Deus in uno existentia omnia praehabet.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28394)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in Deo sunt perfectiones omnium rerum. Unde et dicitur universaliter perfectus, quia non deest ei aliqua nobilitas quae inveniatur in aliquo genere, ut dicit Commentator in V Metaphys. Et hoc quidem ex duobus considerari potest. Primo quidem, per hoc quod quidquid perfectionis est in effectu, oportet inveniri in causa effectiva, vel secundum eandem rationem, si sit agens univocum, ut homo generat hominem; vel eminentiori modo, si sit, agens aequivocum, sicut in sole est similitudo eorum quae generantur per virtutem solis. Manifestum est enim quod effectus praeexistit virtute in causa agente, praeexistere autem in virtute causae agentis, non est praeexistere imperfectiori modo, sed perfectiori; licet praeexistere in potentia causae materialis, sit praeexistere imperfectiori modo, eo quod materia, inquantum huiusmodi, est imperfecta; agens vero, inquantum huiusmodi, est perfectum. Cum ergo Deus sit prima causa effectiva rerum, oportet omnium rerum perfectiones praeexistere in Deo secundum eminentiorem modum. Et hanc rationem tangit Dionysius, cap. V de Div. Nom., dicens de Deo quod *non hoc quidem est, hoc autem non est, sed omnia est, ut omnium causa*. Secundo vero, ex hoc quod supra ostensum est, quod Deus est ipsum esse per se subsistens, ex quo oportet quod totam perfectionem essendi in se contineat. Manifestum est enim quod, si aliquod calidum non habeat totam perfectionem calidi, hoc ideo est, quia calor non participatur secundum perfectam rationem, sed si calor esset per se subsistens, non posset ei aliquid deesse de virtute caloris. Unde, cum Deus sit ipsum esse subsistens, nihil de perfectione essendi potest ei deesse. Omnium autem perfectiones pertinent ad perfectionem essendi, secundum hoc enim aliqua perfecta sunt, quod aliquo modo esse habent. Unde sequitur quod nullius rei perfectio Deo desit. Et hanc etiam rationem tangit Dionysius, cap. V de Div. Nom., dicens quod *Deus non quodammodo est existens, sed simpliciter et incircumscripte totum in seipso uniformiter esse praeaccipit*, et postea subdit quod *ipse est esse subsistentibus*.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28395)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, *sicut sol*, ut dicit Dionysius, cap. V de Div. Nom., *sensibilium substantias et qualitates multas et differentes, ipse unus existens et uniformiter lucendo, in seipso uniformiter praeaccipit; ita multo magis in causa omnium necesse est praeexistere omnia secundum naturalem unionem*. Et sic, quae sunt diversa et opposita in seipsis, in Deo praeexistunt ut unum, absque detrimento simplicitatis ipsius.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28396)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad secundum.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28397)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut in eodem capite idem Dionysius dicit, licet ipsum esse sit perfectius quam vita, et ipsa vita quam ipsa sapientia, si considerentur secundum quod distinguuntur ratione, tamen vivens est perfectius quam ens tantum, quia vivens etiam est ens; et sapiens est ens et vivens. Licet igitur ens non includat in se vivens et sapiens, quia non oportet quod illud quod participat esse, participet ipsum secundum omnem modum essendi, tamen ipsum esse Dei includit in se vitam et sapientiam; quia nulla de perfectionibus essendi potest deesse ei quod est ipsum esse subsistens.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28398)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nulla creatura possit esse similis Deo. Dicitur enim in Psalmo, *non est similis tui in diis, domine*. Sed inter omnes creaturas, excellentiores sunt quae dicuntur dii participative. Multo ergo minus aliae creaturae possunt dici Deo similes.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28399)\n\nPraeterea, similitudo est comparatio quaedam. Non est autem comparatio eorum quae sunt diversorum generum; ergo nec similitudo, non enim dicimus quod dulcedo sit similis albedini. Sed nulla creatura est eiusdem generis cum Deo, cum Deus non sit in genere, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo nulla creatura est similis Deo.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28400)\n\nPraeterea, similia dicuntur quae conveniunt in forma. Sed nihil convenit cum Deo in forma, nullius enim rei essentia est ipsum esse, nisi solius Dei. Ergo nulla creatura potest esse similis Deo.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28401)\n\nPraeterea, in similibus est mutua similitudo, nam simile est simili simile. Si igitur aliqua creatura est similis Deo, et Deus erit similis alicui creaturae. Quod est contra id quod dicitur Isaiae XL, *cui similem fecistis Deum?*\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28402)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*; et I Ioann. III, *cum apparuerit, similes ei erimus*.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28403)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum similitudo attendatur secundum convenientiam vel communicationem in forma, multiplex est similitudo, secundum multos modos communicandi in forma. Quaedam enim dicuntur similia, quae communicant in eadem forma secundum eandem rationem, et secundum eundem modum, et haec non solum dicuntur similia, sed aequalia in sua similitudine; sicut duo aequaliter alba, dicuntur similia in albedine. Et haec est perfectissima similitudo. Alio modo dicuntur similia, quae communicant in forma secundum eandem rationem, et non secundum eundem modum, sed secundum magis et minus; ut minus album dicitur simile magis albo. Et haec est similitudo imperfecta. Tertio modo dicuntur aliqua similia, quae communicant in eadem forma, sed non secundum eandem rationem; ut patet in agentibus non univocis. Cum enim omne agens agat sibi simile inquantum est agens, agit autem unumquodque secundum suam formam, necesse est quod in effectu sit similitudo formae agentis. Si ergo agens sit contentum in eadem specie cum suo effectu, erit similitudo inter faciens et factum in forma, secundum eandem rationem speciei; sicut homo generat hominem. Si autem agens non sit contentum in eadem specie, erit similitudo, sed non secundum eandem rationem speciei, sicut ea quae generantur ex virtute solis, accedunt quidem ad aliquam similitudinem solis, non tamen ut recipiant formam solis secundum similitudinem speciei, sed secundum similitudinem generis. Si igitur sit aliquod agens, quod non in genere contineatur, effectus eius adhuc magis accedent remote ad similitudinem formae agentis, non tamen ita quod participent similitudinem formae agentis secundum eandem rationem speciei aut generis, sed secundum aliqualem analogiam, sicut ipsum esse est commune omnibus. Et hoc modo illa quae sunt a Deo, assimilantur ei inquantum sunt entia, ut primo et universali principio totius esse.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28404)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Dionysius cap. IX de Div. Nom., cum sacra Scriptura dicit aliquid non esse simile Deo, *non est contrarium assimilationi ad ipsum. Eadem enim sunt similia Deo, et dissimilia, similia quidem secundum quod imitantur ipsum, prout contingit eum imitari qui non perfecte imitabilis est dissimilia vero, secundum quod deficiunt a sua causa*; non solum secundum intensionem et remissionem, sicut minus album deficit a magis albo; sed quia non est convenientia nec secundum speciem nec secundum genus.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28405)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus non se habet ad creaturas sicut res diversorum generum, sed sicut id quod est extra omne genus, et principium omnium generum.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28406)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non dicitur esse similitudo creaturae ad Deum propter communicantiam in forma secundum eandem rationem generis et speciei, sed secundum analogiam tantum; prout scilicet Deus est ens per essentiam, et alia per participationem.\n\n[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28407)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, licet aliquo modo concedatur quod creatura sit similis Deo, nullo tamen modo concedendum est quod Deus sit similis creaturae, quia, ut dicit Dionysius cap. IX de Div. Nom., *in his quae unius ordinis sunt, recipitur mutua similitudo, non autem in causa et causato*, dicimus enim quod imago sit similis homini, et non e converso. Et similiter dici potest aliquo modo quod creatura sit similis Deo, non tamen quod Deus sit similis creaturae.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0002","text":"[I q. 4 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0003","text":"Post considerationem divinae simplicitatis, de perfectione ipsius Dei dicendum est. Et quia unumquodque, secundum quod perfectum est, sic dicitur bonum, primo agendum est de perfectione divina; secundo de eius bonitate. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Deus sit perfectus. Secundo, utrum Deus sit universaliter perfectus omnium in se perfectiones habens. Tertio, utrum creaturae similes Deo dici possint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0005","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse perfectum non conveniat Deo. Perfectum enim dicitur quasi totaliter factum. Sed Deo non convenit esse factum. Ergo nec esse perfectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0007","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Deus est primum rerum principium. Sed principia rerum videntur esse imperfecta, semen enim est principium animalium et plantarum. Ergo Deus est imperfectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0009","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0010","text":"Praeterea, ostensum est supra quod essentia Dei est ipsum esse. Sed ipsum esse videtur esse imperfectissimum, cum sit communissimum, et recipiens omnium additiones. Ergo Deus est imperfectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0011","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Matt. V, *estote perfecti, sicut et pater vester caelestis perfectus est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0013","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut philosophus narrat in XII Metaphys., quidam antiqui philosophi, scilicet Pythagorici et Speusippus, non attribuerunt optimum et perfectissimum primo principio. Cuius ratio est, quia philosophi antiqui consideraverunt principium materiale tantum, primum autem principium materiale imperfectissimum est. Cum enim materia, inquantum huiusmodi, sit in potentia, oportet quod primum principium materiale sit maxime in potentia; et ita maxime imperfectum. Deus autem ponitur primum principium, non materiale, sed in genere causae efficientis, et hoc oportet esse perfectissimum. Sicut enim materia, inquantum huiusmodi, est in potentia; ita agens, inquantum huiusmodi, est in actu. Unde primum principium activum oportet maxime esse in actu, et per consequens maxime esse perfectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","text":"Secundum hoc enim dicitur aliquid esse perfectum, secundum quod est actu, nam perfectum dicitur, cui nihil deest secundum modum suae perfectionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0016","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Gregorius, *balbutiendo ut possumus, excelsa Dei resonamus, quod enim factum non est, perfectum proprie dici non potest*. Sed quia in his quae fiunt, tunc dicitur esse aliquid perfectum, cum de potentia educitur in actum; transumitur hoc nomen perfectum ad significandum omne illud cui non deest esse in actu, sive hoc habeat per modum factionis, sive non.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0018","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod principium materiale, quod apud nos imperfectum invenitur, non potest esse simpliciter primum, sed praeceditur ab alio perfecto. Nam semen, licet sit principium animalis generati ex semine, tamen habet ante se animal vel plantam unde deciditur. Oportet enim ante id quod est in potentia, esse aliquid actu, cum ens in potentia non reducatur in actum, nisi per aliquod ens in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0020","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ipsum esse est perfectissimum omnium, comparatur enim ad omnia ut actus. Nihil enim habet actualitatem, nisi inquantum est, unde ipsum esse est actualitas omnium rerum, et etiam ipsarum formarum. Unde non comparatur ad alia sicut recipiens ad receptum, sed magis sicut receptum ad recipiens. Cum enim dico esse hominis, vel equi, vel cuiuscumque alterius, ipsum esse consideratur ut formale et receptum, non autem ut illud cui competit esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0023","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sint perfectiones omnium rerum. Deus enim simplex est, ut ostensum est. Sed perfectiones rerum sunt multae et diversae. Ergo in Deo non sunt omnes perfectiones rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0025","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0026","text":"Praeterea, opposita non possunt esse in eodem. Sed perfectiones rerum sunt oppositae, unaquaeque enim species perficitur per suam differentiam specificam; differentiae autem quibus dividitur genus et constituuntur species, sunt oppositae. Cum ergo opposita non possint simul esse in eodem, videtur quod non omnes rerum perfectiones sint in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0027","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0028","text":"Praeterea, vivens est perfectius quam ens, et sapiens quam vivens, ergo et vivere est perfectius quam esse, et sapere quam vivere. Sed essentia Dei est ipsum esse. Ergo non habet in se perfectionem vitae et sapientiae, et alias huiusmodi perfectiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0029","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, cap. V de Div. Nom., quod Deus in uno existentia omnia praehabet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0031","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in Deo sunt perfectiones omnium rerum. Unde et dicitur universaliter perfectus, quia non deest ei aliqua nobilitas quae inveniatur in aliquo genere, ut dicit Commentator in V Metaphys. Et hoc quidem ex duobus considerari potest. Primo quidem, per hoc quod quidquid perfectionis est in effectu, oportet inveniri in causa effectiva, vel secundum eandem rationem, si sit agens univocum, ut homo generat hominem; vel eminentiori modo, si sit, agens aequivocum, sicut in sole est similitudo eorum quae generantur per virtutem solis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","text":"Manifestum est enim quod effectus praeexistit virtute in causa agente, praeexistere autem in virtute causae agentis, non est praeexistere imperfectiori modo, sed perfectiori; licet praeexistere in potentia causae materialis, sit praeexistere imperfectiori modo, eo quod materia, inquantum huiusmodi, est imperfecta; agens vero, inquantum huiusmodi, est perfectum. Cum ergo Deus sit prima causa effectiva rerum, oportet omnium rerum perfectiones praeexistere in Deo secundum eminentiorem modum. Et hanc rationem tangit Dionysius, cap. V de Div. Nom., dicens de Deo quod *non hoc quidem est, hoc autem non est, sed omnia est, ut omnium causa*. Secundo vero, ex hoc quod supra ostensum est, quod Deus est ipsum esse per se subsistens, ex quo oportet quod totam perfectionem essendi in se contineat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","text":"Manifestum est enim quod, si aliquod calidum non habeat totam perfectionem calidi, hoc ideo est, quia calor non participatur secundum perfectam rationem, sed si calor esset per se subsistens, non posset ei aliquid deesse de virtute caloris. Unde, cum Deus sit ipsum esse subsistens, nihil de perfectione essendi potest ei deesse. Omnium autem perfectiones pertinent ad perfectionem essendi, secundum hoc enim aliqua perfecta sunt, quod aliquo modo esse habent. Unde sequitur quod nullius rei perfectio Deo desit. Et hanc etiam rationem tangit Dionysius, cap. V de Div. Nom., dicens quod *Deus non quodammodo est existens, sed simpliciter et incircumscripte totum in seipso uniformiter esse praeaccipit*, et postea subdit quod *ipse est esse subsistentibus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0035","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, *sicut sol*, ut dicit Dionysius, cap. V de Div. Nom., *sensibilium substantias et qualitates multas et differentes, ipse unus existens et uniformiter lucendo, in seipso uniformiter praeaccipit; ita multo magis in causa omnium necesse est praeexistere omnia secundum naturalem unionem*. Et sic, quae sunt diversa et opposita in seipsis, in Deo praeexistunt ut unum, absque detrimento simplicitatis ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0037","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0038","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad secundum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0039","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut in eodem capite idem Dionysius dicit, licet ipsum esse sit perfectius quam vita, et ipsa vita quam ipsa sapientia, si considerentur secundum quod distinguuntur ratione, tamen vivens est perfectius quam ens tantum, quia vivens etiam est ens; et sapiens est ens et vivens. Licet igitur ens non includat in se vivens et sapiens, quia non oportet quod illud quod participat esse, participet ipsum secundum omnem modum essendi, tamen ipsum esse Dei includit in se vitam et sapientiam; quia nulla de perfectionibus essendi potest deesse ei quod est ipsum esse subsistens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0042","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nulla creatura possit esse similis Deo. Dicitur enim in Psalmo, *non est similis tui in diis, domine*. Sed inter omnes creaturas, excellentiores sunt quae dicuntur dii participative. Multo ergo minus aliae creaturae possunt dici Deo similes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0044","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0045","text":"Praeterea, similitudo est comparatio quaedam. Non est autem comparatio eorum quae sunt diversorum generum; ergo nec similitudo, non enim dicimus quod dulcedo sit similis albedini. Sed nulla creatura est eiusdem generis cum Deo, cum Deus non sit in genere, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo nulla creatura est similis Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0046","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0047","text":"Praeterea, similia dicuntur quae conveniunt in forma. Sed nihil convenit cum Deo in forma, nullius enim rei essentia est ipsum esse, nisi solius Dei. Ergo nulla creatura potest esse similis Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0048","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0049","text":"Praeterea, in similibus est mutua similitudo, nam simile est simili simile. Si igitur aliqua creatura est similis Deo, et Deus erit similis alicui creaturae. Quod est contra id quod dicitur Isaiae XL, *cui similem fecistis Deum?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0050","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0051","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*; et I Ioann. III, *cum apparuerit, similes ei erimus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0052","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum similitudo attendatur secundum convenientiam vel communicationem in forma, multiplex est similitudo, secundum multos modos communicandi in forma. Quaedam enim dicuntur similia, quae communicant in eadem forma secundum eandem rationem, et secundum eundem modum, et haec non solum dicuntur similia, sed aequalia in sua similitudine; sicut duo aequaliter alba, dicuntur similia in albedine. Et haec est perfectissima similitudo. Alio modo dicuntur similia, quae communicant in forma secundum eandem rationem, et non secundum eundem modum, sed secundum magis et minus; ut minus album dicitur simile magis albo. Et haec est similitudo imperfecta. Tertio modo dicuntur aliqua similia, quae communicant in eadem forma, sed non secundum eandem rationem; ut patet in agentibus non univocis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0054","text":"Cum enim omne agens agat sibi simile inquantum est agens, agit autem unumquodque secundum suam formam, necesse est quod in effectu sit similitudo formae agentis. Si ergo agens sit contentum in eadem specie cum suo effectu, erit similitudo inter faciens et factum in forma, secundum eandem rationem speciei; sicut homo generat hominem. Si autem agens non sit contentum in eadem specie, erit similitudo, sed non secundum eandem rationem speciei, sicut ea quae generantur ex virtute solis, accedunt quidem ad aliquam similitudinem solis, non tamen ut recipiant formam solis secundum similitudinem speciei, sed secundum similitudinem generis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","text":"Si igitur sit aliquod agens, quod non in genere contineatur, effectus eius adhuc magis accedent remote ad similitudinem formae agentis, non tamen ita quod participent similitudinem formae agentis secundum eandem rationem speciei aut generis, sed secundum aliqualem analogiam, sicut ipsum esse est commune omnibus. Et hoc modo illa quae sunt a Deo, assimilantur ei inquantum sunt entia, ut primo et universali principio totius esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0056","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Dionysius cap. IX de Div. Nom., cum sacra Scriptura dicit aliquid non esse simile Deo, *non est contrarium assimilationi ad ipsum. Eadem enim sunt similia Deo, et dissimilia, similia quidem secundum quod imitantur ipsum, prout contingit eum imitari qui non perfecte imitabilis est dissimilia vero, secundum quod deficiunt a sua causa*; non solum secundum intensionem et remissionem, sicut minus album deficit a magis albo; sed quia non est convenientia nec secundum speciem nec secundum genus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0058","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus non se habet ad creaturas sicut res diversorum generum, sed sicut id quod est extra omne genus, et principium omnium generum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0060","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non dicitur esse similitudo creaturae ad Deum propter communicantiam in forma secundum eandem rationem generis et speciei, sed secundum analogiam tantum; prout scilicet Deus est ens per essentiam, et alia per participationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0062","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, licet aliquo modo concedatur quod creatura sit similis Deo, nullo tamen modo concedendum est quod Deus sit similis creaturae, quia, ut dicit Dionysius cap. IX de Div. Nom., *in his quae unius ordinis sunt, recipitur mutua similitudo, non autem in causa et causato*, dicimus enim quod imago sit similis homini, et non e converso. Et similiter dici potest aliquo modo quod creatura sit similis Deo, non tamen quod Deus sit similis creaturae.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4bda1639efbd458c8a75c9559f29834ee99a4fa5669b20c0a1315680cc901e15","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T133703196343Z_71724e9a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 4","source_sha256":"4bda1639efbd458c8a75c9559f29834ee99a4fa5669b20c0a1315680cc901e15","sha256":"77ba71250c541ec7131bc42fced2bfd8945ef15681d66f5d3707c7c8ed26705f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:37:03.196360+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0002","text":"[I q. 4 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0003","text":"After the consideration of the divine simplicity, we must speak of the perfection of God himself. And because each thing, insofar as it is perfect, is thereby called good, we must first treat of divine perfection; second, of his goodness. Concerning the first, three questions are asked. First, whether God is perfect. Second, whether God is universally perfect, having in himself the perfections of all things. Third, whether creatures can be said to be like God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0005","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that being perfect does not belong to God. For “perfect” is said as though “completely made.” But being made does not belong to God. Therefore neither does being perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0007","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0008","text":"Moreover, God is the first principle of things. But the principles of things seem to be imperfect; for seed is the principle of animals and plants. Therefore God is imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0009","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0010","text":"Moreover, it has been shown above that the essence of God is being itself. But being itself seems to be most imperfect, since it is most common and receives all additions. Therefore God is imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0011","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Matt. V: *Be perfect, as your heavenly Father also is perfect*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0013","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","text":"I answer that, as the Philosopher relates in Metaphysics XII, certain ancient philosophers, namely the Pythagoreans and Speusippus, did not attribute what is best and most perfect to the first principle. The reason for this is that the ancient philosophers considered only a material principle; but the first material principle is most imperfect. For since matter, as such, is in potency, the first material principle must be most in potency, and thus most imperfect. But God is posited as the first principle, not material, but in the genus of efficient cause, and this must be most perfect. For as matter, as such, is in potency, so an agent, as such, is in act. Hence the first active principle must be maximally in act, and consequently maximally perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","text":"For according to this, something is said to be perfect insofar as it is in act; for that is called perfect to which nothing is lacking according to the mode of its perfection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0016","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Gregory says, *stammering as we can, we resound the sublime things of God; for what has not been made cannot properly be called perfect*. But because among things that are made, something is then said to be perfect when it is led from potency into act, this name “perfect” is transferred to signify everything that does not lack being in act, whether it has this by way of being made or not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0018","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that the material principle, which is found imperfect among us, cannot be simply first, but is preceded by something perfect. For seed, although it is the principle of an animal generated from seed, nevertheless has before it the animal or plant from which it falls. For before that which is in potency there must be something in act, since a being in potency is not reduced to act except by some being in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0020","text":"[I q. 4 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that being itself is the most perfect of all things, for it is compared to all things as act. For nothing has actuality except insofar as it is; hence being itself is the actuality of all things, and even of the forms themselves. Hence it is not compared to other things as receiver to received, but rather as received to receiver. For when I say the being of a man, or of a horse, or of anything else, being itself is considered as formal and received, but not as that to which being belongs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0023","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the perfections of all things are not in God. For God is simple, as has been shown. But the perfections of things are many and diverse. Therefore not all the perfections of things are in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0025","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0026","text":"Moreover, opposites cannot be in the same thing. But the perfections of things are opposites, for each species is perfected through its own specific difference; and the differences by which a genus is divided and species are constituted are opposites. Therefore, since opposites cannot be simultaneously in the same thing, it seems that not all the perfections of things are in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0027","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0028","text":"Moreover, a living being is more perfect than a being, and a wise being than a living being; therefore living too is more perfect than being, and understanding than living. But the essence of God is being itself. Therefore, he does not have in himself the perfection of life and wisdom, and other perfections of this kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0029","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter V of *On the Divine Names*, that God, existing as one, precontains all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0031","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","text":"I answer that, the perfections of all things are in God. Hence he is also said to be universally perfect, because no excellence found in any genus is lacking to him, as the Commentator says in V *Metaphysics*. This can be considered from two points. First, because whatever perfection is in an effect must be found in its efficient cause, either according to the same account, if it is a univocal agent, as man generates man; or in a more eminent way, if it is an equivocal agent, as in the sun there is a likeness of those things that are generated by the power of the sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","text":"For it is manifest that an effect pre-exists by power in its acting cause; but to pre-exist in the power of an acting cause is not to pre-exist in a more imperfect way, but in a more perfect way. Yet to pre-exist in the potentiality of a material cause is to pre-exist in a more imperfect way, because matter, as such, is imperfect; whereas an agent, as such, is perfect. Therefore, since God is the first efficient cause of things, the perfections of all things must pre-exist in God in a more eminent manner. And Dionysius touches upon this argument in chapter V of *On the Divine Names*, saying of God that *he is not this indeed and not that, but is all things, as the cause of all things*. Secondly, from what was shown above: that God is subsistent being itself, from which it follows that he contains in himself the whole perfection of being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","text":"For it is manifest that, if something hot does not possess the whole perfection of hotness, this is because heat is not participated according to its complete account; but if heat were subsistent through itself, nothing could be lacking to it from the power of heat. Hence, since God is subsistent being itself, nothing of the perfection of being can be lacking to him. But the perfections of all things pertain to the perfection of being, for things are perfect insofar as they have being in some way. Hence it follows that the perfection of no thing is lacking to God. Dionysius also touches upon this argument in chapter V of *On the Divine Names*, saying that *God is not existing in some qualified way, but simply and without limitation precontains in himself uniformly the whole of being*, and afterward adds that *he himself is being for subsisting things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0035","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, *just as the sun*, as Dionysius says in chapter V of *On the Divine Names*, *being itself one and shining uniformly, precontains in itself uniformly the many and different substances and qualities of sensible things; so much more must all things pre-exist according to natural unity in the cause of all things*. And thus, those things which are diverse and opposed in themselves pre-exist in God as one, without detriment to his simplicity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0037","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0038","text":"And through this the solution to the second is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0039","text":"[I q. 4 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as the same Dionysius says in the same chapter, although being itself is more perfect than life, and life itself than wisdom itself, if they are considered insofar as they are distinguished in account, nevertheless a living being is more perfect than a being merely, because a living being is also a being; and a wise being is a being and living. Therefore, although a being does not include in itself a living and wise being, because it is not necessary that that which participates being should participate it according to every mode of being, nevertheless God’s very being includes in itself life and wisdom, because none of the perfections of being can be lacking to that which is subsistent being itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0042","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0043","text":"The third is approached thus. It seems that no creature can be like God. For it is said in the Psalm, *there is none like you among the gods, O Lord*. But among all creatures, the more excellent are those which are called gods by participation. Therefore, much less can other creatures be called like God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0044","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0045","text":"Moreover, likeness is a certain comparison. But there is no comparison between things that are of different genera; therefore neither is there likeness, for we do not say that sweetness is like whiteness. But no creature is in the same genus as God, since God is not in a genus, as was shown above. Therefore, no creature is like God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0046","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0047","text":"Moreover, things are called like which agree in form. But nothing agrees with God in form, for the essence of no thing is being itself except that of God alone. Therefore, no creature can be like God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0048","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0049","text":"Moreover, among similar things there is mutual likeness, for like is like to like. If, therefore, some creature is like God, then God will be like some creature. This is contrary to what is said in Isaiah 40: *To whom have you made God like?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0050","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0051","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. 1: *Let us make man in our image and likeness*; and in 1 John 3: *When he shall appear, we shall be like him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0052","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053","text":"I answer that, since likeness is considered according to agreement or communication in form, likeness is manifold, according to the many modes of communicating in form. For some things are called similar which communicate in the same form according to the same account and according to the same mode; and these are not only called similar, but equal in their likeness, as two things equally white are called similar in whiteness. And this is the most perfect likeness. In another way, things are called similar which communicate in form according to the same account, but not according to the same mode, rather according to more and less; as that which is less white is called like that which is more white. And this is imperfect likeness. In a third way, some things are called similar which communicate in the same form, but not according to the same account; as is evident in non-univocal agents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0054","text":"For since every agent produces what is like itself insofar as it is an agent, and each thing acts according to its form, it is necessary that in the effect there be a likeness of the agent's form. If, therefore, the agent is contained in the same species as its effect, there will be a likeness between maker and thing made in form, according to the same account of species; as man generates man. But if the agent is not contained in the same species, there will be likeness, but not according to the same account of species; just as things generated by the power of the sun do indeed approach some likeness of the sun, yet not so as to receive the form of the sun according to likeness of species, but according to likeness of genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","text":"If, therefore, there is some agent which is not contained in a genus, its effects will approach still more remotely to a likeness of the agent's form, yet not so that they participate in the likeness of the agent's form according to the same account of species or genus, but according to a certain analogy, just as being itself is common to all things. And in this way those things which are from God are assimilated to him insofar as they are beings, to the first and universal principle of all being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0056","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Dionysius says in chapter 9 of *On the Divine Names*, when Sacred Scripture says that something is not like God, *this is not contrary to assimilation to him. For the same things are like God and unlike him: like, indeed, insofar as they imitate him, as far as it is possible to imitate him who is not perfectly imitable; but unlike, insofar as they fall short of their cause*—not only according to greater or lesser intensity, as what is less white falls short of what is more white, but because there is no agreement either according to species or according to genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0058","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059","text":"To the second, it must be said that God is not related to creatures as things of diverse genera are, but as that which is outside every genus and the principle of all genera.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0060","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","text":"To the third, it must be said that the likeness of a creature to God is not said on account of communication in form according to the same account of genus and species, but according to analogy alone; namely, insofar as God is being through essence, and other things through participation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0062","text":"[I q. 4 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, although in some way it may be granted that a creature is like God, nevertheless it must in no way be granted that God is like a creature, because, as Dionysius says in chapter 9 of *On the Divine Names*, *among things which are of one order mutual likeness is admitted, but not in cause and caused*. For we say that an image is like a man, and not conversely. And similarly, in some way it can be said that a creature is like God, but not that God is like a creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063"]}],"source_digest":"731f5280f384fbe3f812d6f2b38f2b2c22d60baddc039d8fb567db06833d96fe","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 4"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5","title":"First Part · Question 5","order":6,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 5 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28408)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de bono, et primo de bono in communi; secundo de bonitate Dei. Circa primum quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum bonum et ens sint idem secundum rem. Secundo, supposito quod differant ratione tantum, quid sit prius secundum rationem, utrum bonum vel ens. Tertio, supposito quod ens sit prius, utrum omne ens sit bonum. Quarto, ad quam causam ratio boni reducatur. Quinto, utrum ratio boni consistat in modo, specie et ordine. Sexto, quomodo dividatur bonum in honestum, utile et delectabile.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28409)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum differat secundum rem ab ente. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Hebdom., *intueor in rebus aliud esse quod sunt bona, et aliud esse quod sunt*. Ergo bonum et ens differunt secundum rem.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28410)\n\nPraeterea, nihil informatur seipso. Sed bonum dicitur per informationem entis, ut habetur in commento libri de causis. Ergo bonum differt secundum rem ab ente.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28411)\n\nPraeterea, bonum suscipit magis et minus. Esse autem non suscipit magis et minus. Ergo bonum differt secundum rem ab ente.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28412)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de doctrina Christiana, quod *inquantum sumus, boni sumus*.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28413)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod bonum et ens sunt idem secundum rem, sed differunt secundum rationem tantum. Quod sic patet. Ratio enim boni in hoc consistit, quod aliquid sit appetibile, unde philosophus, in I Ethic., dicit quod *bonum est quod omnia appetunt*. Manifestum est autem quod unumquodque est appetibile secundum quod est perfectum, nam omnia appetunt suam perfectionem. Intantum est autem perfectum unumquodque, inquantum est actu, unde manifestum est quod intantum est aliquid bonum, inquantum est ens, esse enim est actualitas omnis rei, ut ex superioribus patet. Unde manifestum est quod bonum et ens sunt idem secundum rem, sed bonum dicit rationem appetibilis, quam non dicit ens.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28414)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet bonum et ens sint idem secundum rem, quia tamen differunt secundum rationem, non eodem modo dicitur aliquid ens simpliciter, et bonum simpliciter. Nam cum ens dicat aliquid proprie esse in actu; actus autem proprie ordinem habeat ad potentiam; secundum hoc simpliciter aliquid dicitur ens, secundum quod primo discernitur ab eo quod est in potentia tantum. Hoc autem est esse substantiale rei uniuscuiusque; unde per suum esse substantiale dicitur unumquodque ens simpliciter. Per actus autem superadditos, dicitur aliquid esse secundum quid, sicut esse album significat esse secundum quid, non enim esse album aufert esse in potentia simpliciter, cum adveniat rei iam praeexistenti in actu. Sed bonum dicit rationem perfecti, quod est appetibile, et per consequens dicit rationem ultimi. Unde id quod est ultimo perfectum, dicitur bonum simpliciter. Quod autem non habet ultimam perfectionem quam debet habere, quamvis habeat aliquam perfectionem inquantum est actu, non tamen dicitur perfectum simpliciter, nec bonum simpliciter, sed secundum quid. Sic ergo secundum primum esse, quod est substantiale, dicitur aliquid ens simpliciter et bonum secundum quid, idest inquantum est ens, secundum vero ultimum actum dicitur aliquid ens secundum quid, et bonum simpliciter. Sic ergo quod dicit Boetius, quod *in rebus aliud est quod sunt bona, et aliud quod sunt*, referendum est ad esse bonum et ad esse simpliciter, quia secundum primum actum est aliquid ens simpliciter; et secundum ultimum, bonum simpliciter. Et tamen secundum primum actum est quodammodo bonum, et secundum ultimum actum est quodammodo ens.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28415)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bonum dicitur per informationem, prout accipitur bonum simpliciter, secundum ultimum actum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28416)\n\nEt similiter dicendum ad tertium, quod bonum dicitur secundum magis et minus, secundum actum supervenientem; puta secundum scientiam vel virtutem.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28417)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum secundum rationem sit prius quam ens. Ordo enim nominum est secundum ordinem rerum significatarum per nomina. Sed Dionysius, inter alia nomina Dei, prius ponit bonum quam ens, ut patet, in III cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo bonum secundum rationem est prius quam ens.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28418)\n\nPraeterea, illud est prius secundum rationem, quod ad plura se extendit. Sed bonum ad plura se extendit quam ens, quia, ut dicit Dionysius, V cap. de Div. Nom., *bonum se extendit ad existentia et non existentia, ens vero ad existentia tantum*. Ergo bonum est prius secundum rationem quam ens.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28419)\n\nPraeterea, quod est universalius, est prius secundum rationem. Sed bonum videtur universalius esse quam ens, quia bonum habet rationem appetibilis; quibusdam autem appetibile est ipsum non esse; dicitur enim, Matth. XXVI, de Iuda, *bonum erat ei, si natus non fuisset* et cetera. Ergo bonum est prius quam ens, secundum rationem.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28420)\n\nPraeterea, non solum esse est appetibile, sed et vita et sapientia, et multa huiusmodi, et sic videtur quod esse sit quoddam particulare appetibile, et bonum, universale. Bonum ergo simpliciter est prius secundum rationem quam ens.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28421)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro de causis, quod *prima rerum creatarum est esse*.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28422)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ens secundum rationem est prius quam bonum. Ratio enim significata per nomen, est id quod concipit intellectus de re, et significat illud per vocem, illud ergo est prius secundum rationem, quod prius cadit in conceptione intellectus. Primo autem in conceptione intellectus cadit ens, quia secundum hoc unumquodque cognoscibile est, inquantum est actu, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Unde ens est proprium obiectum intellectus, et sic est primum intelligibile, sicut sonus est primum audibile. Ita ergo secundum rationem prius est ens quam bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28423)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius determinat de divinis nominibus secundum quod important circa Deum habitudinem causae, nominamus enim Deum, ut ipse dicit, ex creaturis, sicut causam ex effectibus. Bonum autem, cum habeat rationem appetibilis, importat habitudinem causae finalis, cuius causalitas prima est, quia agens non agit nisi propter finem, et ab agente materia movetur ad formam, unde dicitur quod finis est causa causarum. Et sic, in causando, bonum est prius quam ens, sicut finis quam forma, et hac ratione, inter nomina significantia causalitatem divinam, prius ponitur bonum quam ens. Et iterum quia, secundum Platonicos, qui, materiam a privatione non distinguentes, dicebant materiam esse non ens, ad plura se extendit participatio boni quam participatio entis. Nam materia prima participat bonum, cum appetat ipsum (nihil autem appetit nisi simile sibi), non autem participat ens, cum ponatur non ens. Et ideo dicit Dionysius quod *bonum extenditur ad non existentia*.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28424)\n\nUnde patet solutio ad secundum. Vel dicendum quod bonum extenditur ad existentia et non existentia, non secundum praedicationem, sed secundum causalitatem, ut per non existentia intelligamus, non ea simpliciter quae penitus non sunt, sed ea quae sunt in potentia et non in actu, quia bonum habet rationem finis, in quo non solum quiescunt quae sunt in actu, sed ad ipsum etiam ea moventur quae in actu non sunt sed in potentia tantum. Ens autem non importat habitudinem causae nisi formalis tantum, vel inhaerentis vel exemplaris, cuius causalitas non se extendit nisi ad ea quae sunt in actu.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28425)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non esse secundum se non est appetibile, sed per accidens, inquantum scilicet ablatio alicuius mali est appetibilis, quod malum quidem aufertur per non esse. Ablatio vero mali non est appetibilis, nisi inquantum per malum privatur quodam esse. Illud igitur quod per se est appetibile, est esse, non esse vero per accidens tantum, inquantum scilicet quoddam esse appetitur, quo homo non sustinet privari. Et sic etiam per accidens non esse dicitur bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28426)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod vita et scientia, et alia huiusmodi, sic appetuntur ut sunt in actu, unde in omnibus appetitur quoddam esse. Et sic nihil est appetibile nisi ens, et per consequens nihil est bonum nisi ens.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28427)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omne ens sit bonum. Bonum enim addit supra ens, ut ex dictis patet. Ea vero quae addunt aliquid supra ens, contrahunt ipsum, sicut substantia, quantitas, qualitas, et alia huiusmodi. Ergo bonum contrahit ens. Non igitur omne ens est bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28428)\n\nPraeterea, nullum malum est bonum, Isaiae V, *vae qui dicitis malum bonum, et bonum malum*. Sed aliquod ens dicitur malum. Ergo non omne ens est bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28429)\n\nPraeterea, bonum habet rationem appetibilis. Sed materia prima non habet rationem appetibilis, sed appetentis tantum. Ergo materia prima non habet rationem boni. Non igitur omne ens est bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28430)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in III Metaphys., quod in mathematicis non est bonum. Sed mathematica sunt quaedam entia, alioquin de eis non esset scientia. Ergo non omne ens est bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28431)\n\nSed contra, omne ens quod non est Deus, est Dei creatura. Sed omnis creatura Dei est bona, ut dicitur I ad Tim., IV cap., Deus vero est maxime bonus. Ergo omne ens est bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28432)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod omne ens, inquantum est ens, est bonum. Omne enim ens, inquantum est ens, est in actu, et quodammodo perfectum, quia omnis actus perfectio quaedam est. Perfectum vero habet rationem appetibilis et boni, ut ex dictis patet. Unde sequitur omne ens, inquantum huiusmodi, bonum esse.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28433)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod substantia, quantitas et qualitas, et ea quae sub eis continentur, contrahunt ens applicando ens ad aliquam quidditatem seu naturam. Sic autem non addit aliquid bonum super ens, sed rationem tantum appetibilis et perfectionis, quod convenit ipsi esse in quacumque natura sit. Unde bonum non contrahit ens.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28434)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nullum ens dicitur malum inquantum est ens, sed inquantum caret quodam esse, sicut homo dicitur malus inquantum caret esse virtutis, et oculus dicitur malus inquantum caret acumine visus.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28435)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod materia prima, sicut non est ens nisi in potentia, ita nec bonum nisi in potentia. Licet, secundum Platonicos, dici possit quod materia prima est non ens, propter privationem adiunctam. Sed tamen participat aliquid de bono, scilicet ipsum ordinem vel aptitudinem ad bonum. Et ideo non convenit sibi quod sit appetibile, sed quod appetat.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28436)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod mathematica non subsistunt separata secundum esse, quia si subsisterent, esset in eis bonum, scilicet ipsum esse ipsorum. Sunt autem mathematica separata secundum rationem tantum, prout abstrahuntur a motu et a materia, et sic abstrahuntur a ratione finis, qui habet rationem moventis. Non est autem inconveniens quod in aliquo ente secundum rationem non sit bonum vel ratio boni, cum ratio entis sit prior quam ratio boni, sicut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28437)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum non habeat rationem causae finalis, sed magis aliarum. Ut enim dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *bonum laudatur ut pulchrum*. Sed pulchrum importat rationem causae formalis. Ergo bonum habet rationem causae formalis.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28438)\n\nPraeterea, bonum est diffusivum sui esse, ut ex verbis Dionysii accipitur, quibus dicit quod *bonum est ex quo omnia subsistunt et sunt*. Sed esse diffusivum importat rationem causae efficientis. Ergo bonum habet rationem causae efficientis.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28439)\n\nPraeterea, dicit Augustinus in I de Doctr. Christ., quod *quia Deus bonus est, nos sumus*. Sed ex Deo sumus sicut ex causa efficiente. Ergo bonum importat rationem causae efficientis.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28440)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II Physic., quod *illud cuius causa est, est sicut finis et bonum aliorum*. Bonum ergo habet rationem causae finalis.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28441)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum bonum sit quod omnia appetunt, hoc autem habet rationem finis; manifestum est quod bonum rationem finis importat. Sed tamen ratio boni praesupponit rationem causae efficientis, et rationem causae formalis. Videmus enim quod id quod est primum in causando, ultimum est in causato, ignis enim primo calefacit quam formam ignis inducat, cum tamen calor in igne consequatur formam substantialem. In causando autem, primum invenitur bonum et finis, qui movet efficientem; secundo, actio efficientis, movens ad formam; tertio advenit forma. Unde e converso esse oportet in causato, quod primum sit ipsa forma, per quam est ens; secundo consideratur in ea virtus effectiva, secundum quod est perfectum in esse (quia unumquodque tunc perfectum est, quando potest sibi simile facere, ut dicit philosophus in IV Meteor.); tertio consequitur ratio boni, per quam in ente perfectio fundatur.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28442)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod pulchrum et bonum in subiecto quidem sunt idem, quia super eandem rem fundantur, scilicet super formam, et propter hoc, bonum laudatur ut pulchrum. Sed ratione differunt. Nam bonum proprie respicit appetitum, est enim bonum quod omnia appetunt. Et ideo habet rationem finis, nam appetitus est quasi quidam motus ad rem. Pulchrum autem respicit vim cognoscitivam, pulchra enim dicuntur quae visa placent. Unde pulchrum in debita proportione consistit, quia sensus delectatur in rebus debite proportionatis, sicut in sibi similibus; nam et sensus ratio quaedam est, et omnis virtus cognoscitiva. Et quia cognitio fit per assimilationem, similitudo autem respicit formam, pulchrum proprie pertinet ad rationem causae formalis.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28443)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bonum dicitur diffusivum sui esse, eo modo quo finis dicitur movere.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28444)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quilibet habens voluntatem, dicitur bonus inquantum habet bonam voluntatem, quia per voluntatem utimur omnibus quae in nobis sunt. Unde non dicitur bonus homo, qui habet bonum intellectum, sed qui habet bonam voluntatem. Voluntas autem respicit finem ut obiectum proprium, et sic, quod dicitur, quia Deus est bonus, sumus, refertur ad causam finalem.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28445)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ratio boni non consistat in modo, specie et ordine. Bonum enim et ens ratione differunt, ut supra dictum est. Sed modus, species et ordo pertinere ad rationem entis videntur, quia, sicut dicitur Sap. XI, *omnia in numero, pondere et mensura disposuisti*, ad quae tria reducuntur species, modus et ordo, quia, ut dicit Augustinus, IV super Gen. ad litteram, *mensura omni rei modum praefigit, et numerus omni rei speciem praebet, et pondus omnem rem ad quietem et stabilitatem trahit*. Ergo ratio boni non consistit in modo, specie et ordine.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28446)\n\nPraeterea, ipse modus, species et ordo bona quaedam sunt. Si ergo ratio boni consistit in modo, specie et ordine, oportet etiam quod modus habeat modum, speciem et ordinem, et similiter species et ordo. Ergo procederetur in infinitum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28447)\n\nPraeterea, malum est privatio modi et speciei et ordinis. Sed malum non tollit totaliter bonum. Ergo ratio boni non consistit in modo, specie et ordine.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28448)\n\nPraeterea, illud in quo consistit ratio boni, non potest dici malum. Sed dicitur malus modus, mala species, malus ordo. Ergo ratio boni non consistit in modo, specie et ordine.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28449)\n\nPraeterea, modus, species et ordo ex pondere, numero et mensura causantur, ut ex auctoritate Augustini inducta patet. Non autem omnia bona habent pondus, numerum et mensuram, dicit enim Ambrosius, in Hexaemeron, quod *lucis natura est, ut non in numero, non in pondere, non in mensura creata sit*. Non ergo ratio boni consistit in modo, specie et ordine.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28450)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de natura boni, *haec tria, modus, species et ordo, tanquam generalia bona sunt in rebus a Deo factis, et ita, haec tria ubi magna sunt, magna bona sunt; ubi parva, parva bona sunt; ubi nulla, nullum bonum est*. Quod non esset, nisi ratio boni in eis consisteret. Ergo ratio boni consistit in modo, specie et ordine.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28451)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod unumquodque dicitur bonum, inquantum est perfectum, sic enim est appetibile, ut supra dictum est. Perfectum autem dicitur, cui nihil deest secundum modum suae perfectionis. Cum autem unumquodque sit id quod est, per suam formam; forma autem praesupponit quaedam, et quaedam ad ipsam ex necessitate consequuntur; ad hoc quod aliquid sit perfectum et bonum, necesse est quod formam habeat, et ea quae praeexiguntur ad eam, et ea quae consequuntur ad ipsam. Praeexigitur autem ad formam determinatio sive commensuratio principiorum, seu materialium, seu efficientium ipsam, et hoc significatur per modum, unde dicitur quod mensura modum praefigit. Ipsa autem forma significatur per speciem, quia per formam unumquodque in specie constituitur. Et propter hoc dicitur quod numerus speciem praebet, quia definitiones significantes speciem sunt sicut numeri, secundum philosophum in VIII Metaphys.; sicut enim unitas addita vel subtracta variat speciem numeri, ita in definitionibus differentia apposita vel subtracta. Ad formam autem consequitur inclinatio ad finem, aut ad actionem, aut ad aliquid huiusmodi, quia unumquodque, inquantum est actu, agit, et tendit in id quod sibi convenit secundum suam formam. Et hoc pertinet ad pondus et ordinem. Unde ratio boni, secundum quod consistit in perfectione, consistit etiam in modo, specie et ordine.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28452)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ista tria non consequuntur ens, nisi inquantum est perfectum, et secundum hoc est bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28453)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod modus, species et ordo eo modo dicuntur bona, sicut et entia, non quia ipsa sint quasi subsistentia, sed quia eis alia sunt et entia et bona. Unde non oportet quod ipsa habeant aliqua alia, quibus sint bona. Non enim sic dicuntur bona, quasi formaliter aliis sint bona; sed quia ipsis formaliter aliqua sunt bona; sicut albedo non dicitur ens quia ipsa aliquo sit, sed quia ipsa aliquid est secundum quid, scilicet album.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28454)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quodlibet esse est secundum formam aliquam, unde secundum quodlibet esse rei, consequuntur ipsam modus, species et ordo, sicut homo habet speciem, modum et ordinem, inquantum est homo; et similiter inquantum est albus, habet similiter modum, speciem et ordinem; et inquantum est virtuosus, et inquantum est sciens, et secundum omnia quae de ipso dicuntur. Malum autem privat quodam esse, sicut caecitas privat esse visus, unde non tollit omnem modum, speciem et ordinem; sed solum modum, speciem et ordinem quae consequuntur esse visus.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28455)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus in libro de natura boni, *omnis modus, inquantum modus, bonus est* (et sic potest dici de specie et ordine), *sed malus modus, vel mala species, vel malus ordo, aut ideo dicuntur quia minora sunt quam esse debuerunt; aut quia non his rebus accommodantur, quibus accommodanda sunt; ut ideo dicantur mala, quia sunt aliena et incongrua*.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28456)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod natura lucis dicitur esse sine numero et pondere et mensura, non simpliciter, sed per comparationem ad corporalia, quia virtus lucis ad omnia corporalia se extendit, inquantum est qualitas activa primi corporis alterantis, scilicet caeli.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28457)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non convenienter dividatur bonum per honestum, utile et delectabile. Bonum enim, sicut dicit philosophus in I Ethic., dividitur per decem praedicamenta. Honestum autem, utile et delectabile inveniri possunt in uno praedicamento. Ergo non convenienter per haec dividitur bonum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28458)\n\nPraeterea, omnis divisio fit per opposita. Sed haec tria non videntur esse opposita, nam honesta sunt delectabilia, nullumque inhonestum est utile (quod tamen oportet, si divisio fieret per opposita, ut opponerentur honestum et utile), ut etiam dicit Tullius, in libro de officiis. Ergo praedicta divisio non est conveniens.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28459)\n\nPraeterea, ubi unum propter alterum, ibi unum tantum est. Sed utile non est bonum nisi propter delectabile vel honestum. Ergo non debet utile dividi contra delectabile et honestum.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28460)\n\nSed contra est quod Ambrosius, in libro de officiis, utitur ista divisione boni.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28461)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod haec divisio proprie videtur esse boni humani. Si tamen altius et communius rationem boni consideremus, invenitur haec divisio proprie competere bono, secundum quod bonum est. Nam bonum est aliquid, inquantum est appetibile, et terminus motus appetitus. Cuius quidem motus terminatio considerari potest ex consideratione motus corporis naturalis. Terminatur autem motus corporis naturalis, simpliciter quidem ad ultimum; secundum quid autem etiam ad medium, per quod itur ad ultimum quod terminat motum, et dicitur aliquis terminus motus, inquantum aliquam partem motus terminat. Id autem quod est ultimus terminus motus, potest accipi dupliciter, vel ipsa res in quam tenditur, utpote locus vel forma; vel quies in re illa. Sic ergo in motu appetitus, id quod est appetibile terminans motum appetitus secundum quid, ut medium per quod tenditur in aliud, vocatur utile. Id autem quod appetitur ut ultimum, terminans totaliter motum appetitus, sicut quaedam res in quam per se appetitus tendit, vocatur honestum, quia honestum dicitur quod per se desideratur. Id autem quod terminat motum appetitus ut quies in re desiderata, est delectatio.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28462)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod bonum, inquantum est idem subiecto cum ente, dividitur per decem praedicamenta, sed secundum propriam rationem, competit sibi ista divisio.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28463)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod haec divisio non est per oppositas res, sed per oppositas rationes. Dicuntur tamen illa proprie delectabilia, quae nullam habent aliam rationem appetibilitatis nisi delectationem, cum aliquando sint et noxia et inhonesta. Utilia vero dicuntur, quae non habent in se unde desiderentur; sed desiderantur solum ut sunt ducentia in alterum, sicut sumptio medicinae amarae. Honesta vero dicuntur, quae in seipsis habent unde desiderentur.\n\n[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28464)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod bonum non dividitur in ista tria sicut univocum aequaliter de his praedicatum, sed sicut analogum, quod praedicatur secundum prius et posterius. Per prius enim praedicatur de honesto; et secundario de delectabili; tertio de utili.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0002","text":"[I q. 5 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de bono, et primo de bono in communi; secundo de bonitate Dei. Circa primum quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum bonum et ens sint idem secundum rem. Secundo, supposito quod differant ratione tantum, quid sit prius secundum rationem, utrum bonum vel ens. Tertio, supposito quod ens sit prius, utrum omne ens sit bonum. Quarto, ad quam causam ratio boni reducatur. Quinto, utrum ratio boni consistat in modo, specie et ordine. Sexto, quomodo dividatur bonum in honestum, utile et delectabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0005","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum differat secundum rem ab ente. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Hebdom., *intueor in rebus aliud esse quod sunt bona, et aliud esse quod sunt*. Ergo bonum et ens differunt secundum rem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0007","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0008","text":"Praeterea, nihil informatur seipso. Sed bonum dicitur per informationem entis, ut habetur in commento libri de causis. Ergo bonum differt secundum rem ab ente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0009","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0010","text":"Praeterea, bonum suscipit magis et minus. Esse autem non suscipit magis et minus. Ergo bonum differt secundum rem ab ente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0011","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de doctrina Christiana, quod *inquantum sumus, boni sumus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0013","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod bonum et ens sunt idem secundum rem, sed differunt secundum rationem tantum. Quod sic patet. Ratio enim boni in hoc consistit, quod aliquid sit appetibile, unde philosophus, in I Ethic., dicit quod *bonum est quod omnia appetunt*. Manifestum est autem quod unumquodque est appetibile secundum quod est perfectum, nam omnia appetunt suam perfectionem. Intantum est autem perfectum unumquodque, inquantum est actu, unde manifestum est quod intantum est aliquid bonum, inquantum est ens, esse enim est actualitas omnis rei, ut ex superioribus patet. Unde manifestum est quod bonum et ens sunt idem secundum rem, sed bonum dicit rationem appetibilis, quam non dicit ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0015","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet bonum et ens sint idem secundum rem, quia tamen differunt secundum rationem, non eodem modo dicitur aliquid ens simpliciter, et bonum simpliciter. Nam cum ens dicat aliquid proprie esse in actu; actus autem proprie ordinem habeat ad potentiam; secundum hoc simpliciter aliquid dicitur ens, secundum quod primo discernitur ab eo quod est in potentia tantum. Hoc autem est esse substantiale rei uniuscuiusque; unde per suum esse substantiale dicitur unumquodque ens simpliciter. Per actus autem superadditos, dicitur aliquid esse secundum quid, sicut esse album significat esse secundum quid, non enim esse album aufert esse in potentia simpliciter, cum adveniat rei iam praeexistenti in actu. Sed bonum dicit rationem perfecti, quod est appetibile, et per consequens dicit rationem ultimi. Unde id quod est ultimo perfectum, dicitur bonum simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","text":"Quod autem non habet ultimam perfectionem quam debet habere, quamvis habeat aliquam perfectionem inquantum est actu, non tamen dicitur perfectum simpliciter, nec bonum simpliciter, sed secundum quid. Sic ergo secundum primum esse, quod est substantiale, dicitur aliquid ens simpliciter et bonum secundum quid, idest inquantum est ens, secundum vero ultimum actum dicitur aliquid ens secundum quid, et bonum simpliciter. Sic ergo quod dicit Boetius, quod *in rebus aliud est quod sunt bona, et aliud quod sunt*, referendum est ad esse bonum et ad esse simpliciter, quia secundum primum actum est aliquid ens simpliciter; et secundum ultimum, bonum simpliciter. Et tamen secundum primum actum est quodammodo bonum, et secundum ultimum actum est quodammodo ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0018","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bonum dicitur per informationem, prout accipitur bonum simpliciter, secundum ultimum actum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0020","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0021","text":"Et similiter dicendum ad tertium, quod bonum dicitur secundum magis et minus, secundum actum supervenientem; puta secundum scientiam vel virtutem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0023","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum secundum rationem sit prius quam ens. Ordo enim nominum est secundum ordinem rerum significatarum per nomina. Sed Dionysius, inter alia nomina Dei, prius ponit bonum quam ens, ut patet, in III cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo bonum secundum rationem est prius quam ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0025","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0026","text":"Praeterea, illud est prius secundum rationem, quod ad plura se extendit. Sed bonum ad plura se extendit quam ens, quia, ut dicit Dionysius, V cap. de Div. Nom., *bonum se extendit ad existentia et non existentia, ens vero ad existentia tantum*. Ergo bonum est prius secundum rationem quam ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0027","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0028","text":"Praeterea, quod est universalius, est prius secundum rationem. Sed bonum videtur universalius esse quam ens, quia bonum habet rationem appetibilis; quibusdam autem appetibile est ipsum non esse; dicitur enim, Matth. XXVI, de Iuda, *bonum erat ei, si natus non fuisset* et cetera. Ergo bonum est prius quam ens, secundum rationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0029","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0030","text":"Praeterea, non solum esse est appetibile, sed et vita et sapientia, et multa huiusmodi, et sic videtur quod esse sit quoddam particulare appetibile, et bonum, universale. Bonum ergo simpliciter est prius secundum rationem quam ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0031","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro de causis, quod *prima rerum creatarum est esse*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0033","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ens secundum rationem est prius quam bonum. Ratio enim significata per nomen, est id quod concipit intellectus de re, et significat illud per vocem, illud ergo est prius secundum rationem, quod prius cadit in conceptione intellectus. Primo autem in conceptione intellectus cadit ens, quia secundum hoc unumquodque cognoscibile est, inquantum est actu, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Unde ens est proprium obiectum intellectus, et sic est primum intelligibile, sicut sonus est primum audibile. Ita ergo secundum rationem prius est ens quam bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0035","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius determinat de divinis nominibus secundum quod important circa Deum habitudinem causae, nominamus enim Deum, ut ipse dicit, ex creaturis, sicut causam ex effectibus. Bonum autem, cum habeat rationem appetibilis, importat habitudinem causae finalis, cuius causalitas prima est, quia agens non agit nisi propter finem, et ab agente materia movetur ad formam, unde dicitur quod finis est causa causarum. Et sic, in causando, bonum est prius quam ens, sicut finis quam forma, et hac ratione, inter nomina significantia causalitatem divinam, prius ponitur bonum quam ens. Et iterum quia, secundum Platonicos, qui, materiam a privatione non distinguentes, dicebant materiam esse non ens, ad plura se extendit participatio boni quam participatio entis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0037","text":"Nam materia prima participat bonum, cum appetat ipsum (nihil autem appetit nisi simile sibi), non autem participat ens, cum ponatur non ens. Et ideo dicit Dionysius quod *bonum extenditur ad non existentia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0038","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","text":"Unde patet solutio ad secundum. Vel dicendum quod bonum extenditur ad existentia et non existentia, non secundum praedicationem, sed secundum causalitatem, ut per non existentia intelligamus, non ea simpliciter quae penitus non sunt, sed ea quae sunt in potentia et non in actu, quia bonum habet rationem finis, in quo non solum quiescunt quae sunt in actu, sed ad ipsum etiam ea moventur quae in actu non sunt sed in potentia tantum. Ens autem non importat habitudinem causae nisi formalis tantum, vel inhaerentis vel exemplaris, cuius causalitas non se extendit nisi ad ea quae sunt in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0040","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non esse secundum se non est appetibile, sed per accidens, inquantum scilicet ablatio alicuius mali est appetibilis, quod malum quidem aufertur per non esse. Ablatio vero mali non est appetibilis, nisi inquantum per malum privatur quodam esse. Illud igitur quod per se est appetibile, est esse, non esse vero per accidens tantum, inquantum scilicet quoddam esse appetitur, quo homo non sustinet privari. Et sic etiam per accidens non esse dicitur bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0042","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0043","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod vita et scientia, et alia huiusmodi, sic appetuntur ut sunt in actu, unde in omnibus appetitur quoddam esse. Et sic nihil est appetibile nisi ens, et per consequens nihil est bonum nisi ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0045","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omne ens sit bonum. Bonum enim addit supra ens, ut ex dictis patet. Ea vero quae addunt aliquid supra ens, contrahunt ipsum, sicut substantia, quantitas, qualitas, et alia huiusmodi. Ergo bonum contrahit ens. Non igitur omne ens est bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0047","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0048","text":"Praeterea, nullum malum est bonum, Isaiae V, *vae qui dicitis malum bonum, et bonum malum*. Sed aliquod ens dicitur malum. Ergo non omne ens est bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0049","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0050","text":"Praeterea, bonum habet rationem appetibilis. Sed materia prima non habet rationem appetibilis, sed appetentis tantum. Ergo materia prima non habet rationem boni. Non igitur omne ens est bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0051","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0052","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in III Metaphys., quod in mathematicis non est bonum. Sed mathematica sunt quaedam entia, alioquin de eis non esset scientia. Ergo non omne ens est bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0053","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0054","text":"Sed contra, omne ens quod non est Deus, est Dei creatura. Sed omnis creatura Dei est bona, ut dicitur I ad Tim., IV cap., Deus vero est maxime bonus. Ergo omne ens est bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0055","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod omne ens, inquantum est ens, est bonum. Omne enim ens, inquantum est ens, est in actu, et quodammodo perfectum, quia omnis actus perfectio quaedam est. Perfectum vero habet rationem appetibilis et boni, ut ex dictis patet. Unde sequitur omne ens, inquantum huiusmodi, bonum esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0057","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod substantia, quantitas et qualitas, et ea quae sub eis continentur, contrahunt ens applicando ens ad aliquam quidditatem seu naturam. Sic autem non addit aliquid bonum super ens, sed rationem tantum appetibilis et perfectionis, quod convenit ipsi esse in quacumque natura sit. Unde bonum non contrahit ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0059","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nullum ens dicitur malum inquantum est ens, sed inquantum caret quodam esse, sicut homo dicitur malus inquantum caret esse virtutis, et oculus dicitur malus inquantum caret acumine visus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0061","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod materia prima, sicut non est ens nisi in potentia, ita nec bonum nisi in potentia. Licet, secundum Platonicos, dici possit quod materia prima est non ens, propter privationem adiunctam. Sed tamen participat aliquid de bono, scilicet ipsum ordinem vel aptitudinem ad bonum. Et ideo non convenit sibi quod sit appetibile, sed quod appetat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0063","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod mathematica non subsistunt separata secundum esse, quia si subsisterent, esset in eis bonum, scilicet ipsum esse ipsorum. Sunt autem mathematica separata secundum rationem tantum, prout abstrahuntur a motu et a materia, et sic abstrahuntur a ratione finis, qui habet rationem moventis. Non est autem inconveniens quod in aliquo ente secundum rationem non sit bonum vel ratio boni, cum ratio entis sit prior quam ratio boni, sicut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0065","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0066","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0067","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum non habeat rationem causae finalis, sed magis aliarum. Ut enim dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *bonum laudatur ut pulchrum*. Sed pulchrum importat rationem causae formalis. Ergo bonum habet rationem causae formalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0068","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0069","text":"Praeterea, bonum est diffusivum sui esse, ut ex verbis Dionysii accipitur, quibus dicit quod *bonum est ex quo omnia subsistunt et sunt*. Sed esse diffusivum importat rationem causae efficientis. Ergo bonum habet rationem causae efficientis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0070","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0071","text":"Praeterea, dicit Augustinus in I de Doctr. Christ., quod *quia Deus bonus est, nos sumus*. Sed ex Deo sumus sicut ex causa efficiente. Ergo bonum importat rationem causae efficientis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0072","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II Physic., quod *illud cuius causa est, est sicut finis et bonum aliorum*. Bonum ergo habet rationem causae finalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0074","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum bonum sit quod omnia appetunt, hoc autem habet rationem finis; manifestum est quod bonum rationem finis importat. Sed tamen ratio boni praesupponit rationem causae efficientis, et rationem causae formalis. Videmus enim quod id quod est primum in causando, ultimum est in causato, ignis enim primo calefacit quam formam ignis inducat, cum tamen calor in igne consequatur formam substantialem. In causando autem, primum invenitur bonum et finis, qui movet efficientem; secundo, actio efficientis, movens ad formam; tertio advenit forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","text":"Unde e converso esse oportet in causato, quod primum sit ipsa forma, per quam est ens; secundo consideratur in ea virtus effectiva, secundum quod est perfectum in esse (quia unumquodque tunc perfectum est, quando potest sibi simile facere, ut dicit philosophus in IV Meteor.); tertio consequitur ratio boni, per quam in ente perfectio fundatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0077","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0078","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod pulchrum et bonum in subiecto quidem sunt idem, quia super eandem rem fundantur, scilicet super formam, et propter hoc, bonum laudatur ut pulchrum. Sed ratione differunt. Nam bonum proprie respicit appetitum, est enim bonum quod omnia appetunt. Et ideo habet rationem finis, nam appetitus est quasi quidam motus ad rem. Pulchrum autem respicit vim cognoscitivam, pulchra enim dicuntur quae visa placent. Unde pulchrum in debita proportione consistit, quia sensus delectatur in rebus debite proportionatis, sicut in sibi similibus; nam et sensus ratio quaedam est, et omnis virtus cognoscitiva. Et quia cognitio fit per assimilationem, similitudo autem respicit formam, pulchrum proprie pertinet ad rationem causae formalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0079","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0080","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bonum dicitur diffusivum sui esse, eo modo quo finis dicitur movere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0081","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0082","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quilibet habens voluntatem, dicitur bonus inquantum habet bonam voluntatem, quia per voluntatem utimur omnibus quae in nobis sunt. Unde non dicitur bonus homo, qui habet bonum intellectum, sed qui habet bonam voluntatem. Voluntas autem respicit finem ut obiectum proprium, et sic, quod dicitur, quia Deus est bonus, sumus, refertur ad causam finalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0083","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0084","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0085","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ratio boni non consistat in modo, specie et ordine. Bonum enim et ens ratione differunt, ut supra dictum est. Sed modus, species et ordo pertinere ad rationem entis videntur, quia, sicut dicitur Sap. XI, *omnia in numero, pondere et mensura disposuisti*, ad quae tria reducuntur species, modus et ordo, quia, ut dicit Augustinus, IV super Gen. ad litteram, *mensura omni rei modum praefigit, et numerus omni rei speciem praebet, et pondus omnem rem ad quietem et stabilitatem trahit*. Ergo ratio boni non consistit in modo, specie et ordine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0086","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0087","text":"Praeterea, ipse modus, species et ordo bona quaedam sunt. Si ergo ratio boni consistit in modo, specie et ordine, oportet etiam quod modus habeat modum, speciem et ordinem, et similiter species et ordo. Ergo procederetur in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0088","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0089","text":"Praeterea, malum est privatio modi et speciei et ordinis. Sed malum non tollit totaliter bonum. Ergo ratio boni non consistit in modo, specie et ordine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0090","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0091","text":"Praeterea, illud in quo consistit ratio boni, non potest dici malum. Sed dicitur malus modus, mala species, malus ordo. Ergo ratio boni non consistit in modo, specie et ordine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0092","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0093","text":"Praeterea, modus, species et ordo ex pondere, numero et mensura causantur, ut ex auctoritate Augustini inducta patet. Non autem omnia bona habent pondus, numerum et mensuram, dicit enim Ambrosius, in Hexaemeron, quod *lucis natura est, ut non in numero, non in pondere, non in mensura creata sit*. Non ergo ratio boni consistit in modo, specie et ordine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0094","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0095","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de natura boni, *haec tria, modus, species et ordo, tanquam generalia bona sunt in rebus a Deo factis, et ita, haec tria ubi magna sunt, magna bona sunt; ubi parva, parva bona sunt; ubi nulla, nullum bonum est*. Quod non esset, nisi ratio boni in eis consisteret. Ergo ratio boni consistit in modo, specie et ordine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0096","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod unumquodque dicitur bonum, inquantum est perfectum, sic enim est appetibile, ut supra dictum est. Perfectum autem dicitur, cui nihil deest secundum modum suae perfectionis. Cum autem unumquodque sit id quod est, per suam formam; forma autem praesupponit quaedam, et quaedam ad ipsam ex necessitate consequuntur; ad hoc quod aliquid sit perfectum et bonum, necesse est quod formam habeat, et ea quae praeexiguntur ad eam, et ea quae consequuntur ad ipsam. Praeexigitur autem ad formam determinatio sive commensuratio principiorum, seu materialium, seu efficientium ipsam, et hoc significatur per modum, unde dicitur quod mensura modum praefigit. Ipsa autem forma significatur per speciem, quia per formam unumquodque in specie constituitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","text":"Et propter hoc dicitur quod numerus speciem praebet, quia definitiones significantes speciem sunt sicut numeri, secundum philosophum in VIII Metaphys.; sicut enim unitas addita vel subtracta variat speciem numeri, ita in definitionibus differentia apposita vel subtracta. Ad formam autem consequitur inclinatio ad finem, aut ad actionem, aut ad aliquid huiusmodi, quia unumquodque, inquantum est actu, agit, et tendit in id quod sibi convenit secundum suam formam. Et hoc pertinet ad pondus et ordinem. Unde ratio boni, secundum quod consistit in perfectione, consistit etiam in modo, specie et ordine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0099","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0100","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ista tria non consequuntur ens, nisi inquantum est perfectum, et secundum hoc est bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0101","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0102","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod modus, species et ordo eo modo dicuntur bona, sicut et entia, non quia ipsa sint quasi subsistentia, sed quia eis alia sunt et entia et bona. Unde non oportet quod ipsa habeant aliqua alia, quibus sint bona. Non enim sic dicuntur bona, quasi formaliter aliis sint bona; sed quia ipsis formaliter aliqua sunt bona; sicut albedo non dicitur ens quia ipsa aliquo sit, sed quia ipsa aliquid est secundum quid, scilicet album.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0103","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quodlibet esse est secundum formam aliquam, unde secundum quodlibet esse rei, consequuntur ipsam modus, species et ordo, sicut homo habet speciem, modum et ordinem, inquantum est homo; et similiter inquantum est albus, habet similiter modum, speciem et ordinem; et inquantum est virtuosus, et inquantum est sciens, et secundum omnia quae de ipso dicuntur. Malum autem privat quodam esse, sicut caecitas privat esse visus, unde non tollit omnem modum, speciem et ordinem; sed solum modum, speciem et ordinem quae consequuntur esse visus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0105","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0106","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus in libro de natura boni, *omnis modus, inquantum modus, bonus est* (et sic potest dici de specie et ordine), *sed malus modus, vel mala species, vel malus ordo, aut ideo dicuntur quia minora sunt quam esse debuerunt; aut quia non his rebus accommodantur, quibus accommodanda sunt; ut ideo dicantur mala, quia sunt aliena et incongrua*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0107","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0108","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod natura lucis dicitur esse sine numero et pondere et mensura, non simpliciter, sed per comparationem ad corporalia, quia virtus lucis ad omnia corporalia se extendit, inquantum est qualitas activa primi corporis alterantis, scilicet caeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0109","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0110","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0111","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non convenienter dividatur bonum per honestum, utile et delectabile. Bonum enim, sicut dicit philosophus in I Ethic., dividitur per decem praedicamenta. Honestum autem, utile et delectabile inveniri possunt in uno praedicamento. Ergo non convenienter per haec dividitur bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0112","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0113","text":"Praeterea, omnis divisio fit per opposita. Sed haec tria non videntur esse opposita, nam honesta sunt delectabilia, nullumque inhonestum est utile (quod tamen oportet, si divisio fieret per opposita, ut opponerentur honestum et utile), ut etiam dicit Tullius, in libro de officiis. Ergo praedicta divisio non est conveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0114","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0115","text":"Praeterea, ubi unum propter alterum, ibi unum tantum est. Sed utile non est bonum nisi propter delectabile vel honestum. Ergo non debet utile dividi contra delectabile et honestum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0116","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0117","text":"Sed contra est quod Ambrosius, in libro de officiis, utitur ista divisione boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0118","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod haec divisio proprie videtur esse boni humani. Si tamen altius et communius rationem boni consideremus, invenitur haec divisio proprie competere bono, secundum quod bonum est. Nam bonum est aliquid, inquantum est appetibile, et terminus motus appetitus. Cuius quidem motus terminatio considerari potest ex consideratione motus corporis naturalis. Terminatur autem motus corporis naturalis, simpliciter quidem ad ultimum; secundum quid autem etiam ad medium, per quod itur ad ultimum quod terminat motum, et dicitur aliquis terminus motus, inquantum aliquam partem motus terminat. Id autem quod est ultimus terminus motus, potest accipi dupliciter, vel ipsa res in quam tenditur, utpote locus vel forma; vel quies in re illa. Sic ergo in motu appetitus, id quod est appetibile terminans motum appetitus secundum quid, ut medium per quod tenditur in aliud, vocatur utile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","text":"Id autem quod appetitur ut ultimum, terminans totaliter motum appetitus, sicut quaedam res in quam per se appetitus tendit, vocatur honestum, quia honestum dicitur quod per se desideratur. Id autem quod terminat motum appetitus ut quies in re desiderata, est delectatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0121","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0122","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod bonum, inquantum est idem subiecto cum ente, dividitur per decem praedicamenta, sed secundum propriam rationem, competit sibi ista divisio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0123","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod haec divisio non est per oppositas res, sed per oppositas rationes. Dicuntur tamen illa proprie delectabilia, quae nullam habent aliam rationem appetibilitatis nisi delectationem, cum aliquando sint et noxia et inhonesta. Utilia vero dicuntur, quae non habent in se unde desiderentur; sed desiderantur solum ut sunt ducentia in alterum, sicut sumptio medicinae amarae. Honesta vero dicuntur, quae in seipsis habent unde desiderentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0125","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod bonum non dividitur in ista tria sicut univocum aequaliter de his praedicatum, sed sicut analogum, quod praedicatur secundum prius et posterius. Per prius enim praedicatur de honesto; et secundario de delectabili; tertio de utili.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"5e8ec6cbaf46bda83097de6d73949cb92b4cbae8c23fb9089ae10caec401aaf3","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T133817322531Z_c27a5c97","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 5","source_sha256":"5e8ec6cbaf46bda83097de6d73949cb92b4cbae8c23fb9089ae10caec401aaf3","sha256":"b78e5d89adb16c0bdd539cc2fa12ff3a0f266bdb2db602b1b35a48ffe2d2e03a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:38:17.322543+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0002","text":"[I q. 5 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","text":"Next, inquiry is made concerning the good: first, concerning the good in general; second, concerning the goodness of God. With regard to the first, six questions are asked. First, whether the good and being are the same in reality. Second, granted that they differ only in notion, which is prior in notion, the good or being. Third, granted that being is prior, whether every being is good. Fourth, to which cause the notion of good is reduced. Fifth, whether the notion of good consists in mode, species, and order. Sixth, how the good is divided into the honorable, the useful, and the delightful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0005","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the good differs in reality from being. For Boethius says, in the book *De Hebdomadibus*, “I perceive in things that it is one thing that they are good, and another thing that they are.” Therefore the good and being differ in reality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0007","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0008","text":"Moreover, nothing is informed by itself. But the good is called so through the informing of being, as is stated in the commentary on the *Book of Causes*. Therefore the good differs in reality from being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0009","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0010","text":"Moreover, the good admits of more and less. But being does not admit of more and less. Therefore the good differs in reality from being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0011","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *On Christian Doctrine*, that “insofar as we are, we are good.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0013","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","text":"I answer that the good and being are the same in reality, but differ only in notion. This is evident as follows. For the notion of good consists in this, that something is desirable; hence the Philosopher says, in *Ethics* I, that “the good is what all things desire.” Now it is manifest that each thing is desirable insofar as it is perfect, for all things desire their perfection. But each thing is perfect insofar as it is in act; hence it is manifest that something is good insofar as it is a being, for to be is the actuality of every thing, as is clear from what has been said above. Hence it is manifest that the good and being are the same in reality, but the good expresses the notion of the desirable, which being does not express.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0015","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although the good and being are the same in reality, nevertheless, since they differ in notion, something is not called being simply and good simply in the same way. For since being properly signifies something to be in act, while act properly has an order to potency, something is called a being simply according as it is first distinguished from that which is only in potency. Now this is the substantial being of each thing; hence each thing is called a being simply through its substantial being. But through superadded acts, something is said to be in a certain respect; thus, to be white signifies being in a certain respect. For being white does not remove being in potency simply, since it comes to a thing already pre-existing in act. But the good expresses the notion of the perfect, which is desirable, and consequently expresses the notion of the ultimate. Hence that which is ultimately perfect is called good simply.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","text":"But that which does not have the ultimate perfection that it ought to have, although it has some perfection insofar as it is in act, is nevertheless not called perfect simply, nor good simply, but in a certain respect. Thus, according to the first being, which is substantial, something is called a being simply and good in a certain respect, that is, insofar as it is a being; whereas according to the ultimate act, something is called a being in a certain respect and good simply. Thus, then, what Boethius says—that “in things it is one thing that they are good, and another thing that they are”—must be referred to being good and to being simply, because according to the first act something is a being simply, and according to the ultimate act, good simply. Yet according to the first act it is in some way good, and according to the ultimate act it is in some way a being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0018","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that the good is called so through informing, insofar as the good is taken simply, according to the ultimate act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0020","text":"[I q. 5 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0021","text":"And the same must be said to the third: that the good is spoken of according to more and less, according to a supervening act—for example, according to knowledge or virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0023","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the good is prior in notion to being. For the order of names follows the order of the things signified by names. But Dionysius, among the other names of God, places the good before being, as is clear in chapter III of *On the Divine Names*. Therefore the good is prior in notion to being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0025","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0026","text":"Moreover, that which extends to more things is prior in notion. But the good extends to more things than being, because, as Dionysius says in chapter V of *On the Divine Names*, *the good extends to things existing and things not existing, whereas being extends only to things existing*. Therefore the good is prior in notion to being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0027","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0028","text":"Moreover, what is more universal is prior in notion. But the good seems to be more universal than being, because the good has the character of the appetible; but for some persons non-being itself is appetible. For it is said, in Matthew XXVI, concerning Judas, *it would have been good for him if he had not been born*, and so forth. Therefore the good is prior in notion to being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0029","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0030","text":"Moreover, not only being is appetible, but also life and wisdom, and many such things; and thus it seems that being is a certain particular appetible, and the good universal. Therefore the good simply is prior in notion to being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0031","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0032","text":"On the contrary, it is said in the *Book of Causes* that *the first of created things is being*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0033","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","text":"I answer that being is prior in notion to the good. For the notion signified by a name is that which the intellect conceives of a thing and signifies by a vocal sound. Therefore, that is prior in notion which falls first within the conception of the intellect. Now being falls first within the conception of the intellect, because each thing is knowable insofar as it is in act, as is said in *Metaphysics* IX. Hence being is the proper object of the intellect, and thus it is the first intelligible, just as sound is the first audible. Therefore being is prior in notion to the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0035","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Dionysius treats of the divine names according as they imply a relation of cause with respect to God; for, as he himself says, we name God from creatures, as a cause from its effects. But the good, since it has the character of the appetible, implies the relation of final cause, whose causality is first, because an agent does not act except for the sake of an end, and matter is moved by the agent toward form; hence it is said that the end is the cause of causes. And thus, in causing, the good is prior to being, as the end is prior to the form; and for this reason, among the names signifying divine causality, the good is set before being. And again, because according to the Platonists, who, not distinguishing matter from privation, said that matter is non-being, participation in the good extends to more things than participation in being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0037","text":"For prime matter participates in the good, since it desires it (but nothing desires anything except what is like itself); yet it does not participate in being, since it is posited as non-being. And therefore Dionysius says that *the good extends to things not existing*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0038","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","text":"Hence the solution to the second is clear. Or it must be said that the good extends to things existing and things not existing, not according to predication, but according to causality, so that by things not existing we understand not those things simply which are altogether not, but those which are in potency and not in act; because the good has the character of an end, in which not only those things which are in act rest, but toward which also those things are moved which are not in act but only in potency. Being, however, does not imply a relation of cause except only of formal cause, whether inherent or exemplary, the causality of which does not extend except to those things which are in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0040","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","text":"To the third it must be said that non-being is not appetible in itself, but accidentally, namely insofar as the removal of some evil is appetible; and this evil is removed through non-being. But the removal of evil is not appetible except insofar as through evil some being is deprived. Therefore that which is appetible through itself is being; non-being, however, is so only accidentally, namely insofar as some being is desired, of which a human being does not endure being deprived. And thus non-being too is called good accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0042","text":"[I q. 5 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0043","text":"To the fourth it must be said that life and knowledge, and other such things, are desired insofar as they are in act; hence in all things some being is desired. And thus nothing is appetible except being, and consequently nothing is good except being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0045","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0046","text":"We proceed to the third as follows. It seems that not every being is good. For the good adds something over and above being, as is clear from what has been said. But things that add something over and above being contract it, as substance, quantity, quality, and other such things do. Therefore the good contracts being. Therefore not every being is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0047","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0048","text":"Moreover, no evil is good: Isaiah V, *woe to those who call evil good, and good evil*. But some being is called evil. Therefore not every being is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0049","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0050","text":"Moreover, the good has the character of what is appetible. But prime matter does not have the character of what is appetible, but only of what appetizes. Therefore prime matter does not have the character of the good. Therefore not every being is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0051","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0052","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in Metaphysics III, that there is no good in mathematics. But mathematical things are certain beings; otherwise there would be no science concerning them. Therefore not every being is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0053","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0054","text":"On the contrary, every being that is not God is a creature of God. But every creature of God is good, as is said in 1 Timothy, chapter 4; God, however, is supremely good. Therefore every being is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0055","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","text":"I answer that it must be said that every being, insofar as it is a being, is good. For every being, insofar as it is a being, is in act and is in some way perfect, because every act is a certain perfection. But the perfect has the character of what is appetible and good, as is clear from what has been said. Hence it follows that every being, insofar as it is such, is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0057","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that substance, quantity, and quality, and the things contained under them, contract being by applying being to some quiddity or nature. But in this way good does not add anything to being, but only the character of appetibility and perfection, which belongs to being itself in whatever nature it may be. Hence good does not contract being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0059","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","text":"To the second, it must be said that no being is called evil insofar as it is a being, but insofar as it lacks a certain being; just as a man is called evil insofar as he lacks the being of virtue, and an eye is called evil insofar as it lacks keenness of sight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0061","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","text":"To the third, it must be said that prime matter, just as it is a being only in potency, so too it is good only in potency. Although, according to the Platonists, it can be said that prime matter is non-being, on account of the privation conjoined to it. Nevertheless, it participates in something of the good, namely, the very order or aptitude toward the good. And therefore it does not belong to it to be appetible, but to appetize.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0063","text":"[I q. 5 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that mathematical things do not subsist separately according to being, because if they subsisted, there would be good in them, namely, their very being. But mathematical things are separate only according to reason, insofar as they are abstracted from motion and matter; and thus they are abstracted from the character of an end, which has the character of a mover. But it is not unfitting that in some being according to reason there should be no good or character of the good, since the character of being is prior to the character of the good, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0065","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0066","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0067","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that the good does not have the character of a final cause, but rather that of the other causes. For, as Dionysius says in chapter 4 of On the Divine Names, *the good is praised as beautiful*. But the beautiful implies the character of a formal cause. Therefore the good has the character of a formal cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0068","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0069","text":"Moreover, the good is diffusive of its being, as is gathered from the words of Dionysius, in which he says that *the good is that from which all things subsist and are*. But to be diffusive implies the character of an efficient cause. Therefore the good has the character of an efficient cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0070","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0071","text":"Moreover, Augustine says in On Christian Doctrine I that *because God is good, we are*. But we are from God as from an efficient cause. Therefore good implies the character of an efficient cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0072","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0073","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *Physics* II, that *that for the sake of which [something is], is as the end and good of the others*. Therefore, the good has the character of a final cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0074","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since the good is what all things desire, and this has the character of an end, it is manifest that the good implies the character of an end. Yet the character of the good presupposes the character of an efficient cause and the character of a formal cause. For we see that what is first in causing is last in the thing caused: for fire first heats before it induces the form of fire, although heat in fire follows upon the substantial form. But in causing, the good and the end, which moves the efficient cause, is found first; second, the action of the efficient cause, moving toward the form; third, the form arrives.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","text":"Hence, conversely, it must be in the thing caused that first there is the form itself, through which it is a being; second, the effective power in it is considered, insofar as it is perfect in being (because each thing is then perfect when it can make something like itself, as the Philosopher says in *Meteorology* IV); third, the character of the good follows, through which perfection is founded in being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0077","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0078","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the beautiful and the good are indeed the same in subject, because they are founded upon the same thing, namely, upon form; and on account of this, the good is praised as beautiful. But they differ in character. For the good properly regards appetite, since the good is what all things desire. And therefore it has the character of an end, for appetite is as it were a certain movement toward a thing. But the beautiful regards the cognitive power, for those things are called beautiful which please when seen. Hence the beautiful consists in due proportion, because sense takes delight in things duly proportioned, as in things like itself; for sense too is a certain ratio, as is every cognitive power. And because cognition occurs through assimilation, while likeness regards form, the beautiful properly pertains to the character of a formal cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0079","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0080","text":"To the second it must be said that the good is called diffusive of its being in the way in which an end is said to move.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0081","text":"[I q. 5 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0082","text":"To the third it must be said that anyone who has a will is called good insofar as he has a good will, because through the will we use all the things that are in us. Hence a man is not called good who has a good intellect, but one who has a good will. But the will regards the end as its proper object, and thus the saying, “because God is good, we are,” is referred to the final cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0083","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0084","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0085","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth [article]. It seems that the character of the good does not consist in mode, species, and order. For the good and being differ in character, as was said above. But mode, species, and order seem to pertain to the character of being, because, as is said in Wisdom XI, *you have ordered all things in number, weight, and measure*; to which three species, mode, and order are reduced, because, as Augustine says in *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis* IV, *measure sets a mode to every thing, number gives species to every thing, and weight draws every thing toward rest and stability*. Therefore, the character of the good does not consist in mode, species, and order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0086","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0087","text":"Further, mode, species, and order themselves are certain goods. Therefore, if the character of the good consists in mode, species, and order, it is also necessary that mode have mode, species, and order, and likewise species and order. Therefore, one would proceed to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0088","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0089","text":"Further, evil is the privation of mode, species, and order. But evil does not totally remove the good. Therefore, the character of the good does not consist in mode, species, and order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0090","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0091","text":"Further, that in which the character of the good consists cannot be called evil. But an evil mode, an evil species, and an evil order are spoken of. Therefore, the character of the good does not consist in mode, species, and order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0092","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0093","text":"Further, mode, species, and order are caused by weight, number, and measure, as is clear from the authority of Augustine cited. But not all goods have weight, number, and measure; for Ambrose says, in the *Hexaemeron*, that *it is the nature of light that it was created not in number, not in weight, not in measure*. Therefore, the character of the good does not consist in mode, species, and order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0094","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0095","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says in the book *On the Nature of the Good*, *these three—mode, species, and order—are, as it were, general goods in things made by God; and thus, where these three are great, the goods are great; where [they are] small, the goods are small; where [they are] none, there is no good*. This would not be so unless the character of the good consisted in them. Therefore, the character of the good consists in mode, species, and order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0096","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","text":"I answer that, each thing is called good insofar as it is perfect, for thus it is desirable, as was said above. Now that is called perfect to which nothing is lacking according to the mode of its perfection. But since each thing is what it is through its form, and form presupposes certain things, while certain things follow upon it of necessity, in order that something be perfect and good, it must have its form, and those things that are pre-required for it, and those that follow upon it. Now, the determination or commensuration of the principles, whether material or efficient, of the form is pre-required; and this is signified by mode, whence it is said that measure sets a mode. But the form itself is signified by species, because each thing is constituted in a species through form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","text":"And for this reason it is said that number provides species, because definitions signifying species are like numbers, according to the Philosopher in Metaphysics VIII; for just as a unit added or subtracted varies the species of a number, so in definitions a difference added or subtracted does so. But an inclination toward an end, or toward an action, or toward something of this kind, follows upon form, because each thing, insofar as it is in act, acts and tends toward that which is suitable to it according to its form. And this pertains to weight and order. Hence the character of the good, insofar as it consists in perfection, also consists in mode, species, and order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0099","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0100","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that these three do not follow upon being except insofar as it is perfect, and according to this it is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0101","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0102","text":"To the second, it must be said that mode, species, and order are called good in the same way as they are called beings: not because they themselves are as though subsistent, but because through them other things are both beings and good. Hence it is not necessary that they themselves have certain other things by which they are good. For they are not called good as though they were formally good for other things; but because by them other things are formally good; just as whiteness is not called a being because it itself is by something, but because by it something is a being in a certain respect, namely, white.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0103","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","text":"To the third, it must be said that every being is according to some form; hence, according to every being of a thing, mode, species, and order follow upon it. Thus a man has species, mode, and order insofar as he is a man; and likewise insofar as he is white, he likewise has mode, species, and order; and insofar as he is virtuous, and insofar as he is knowing, and according to all the things that are said of him. But evil deprives a certain being, just as blindness deprives the being of sight; hence it does not remove every mode, species, and order, but only the mode, species, and order that follow upon the being of sight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0105","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0106","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, as Augustine says in the book On the Nature of the Good, *every mode, insofar as it is a mode, is good* (and the same can be said of species and order), *but a bad mode, or a bad species, or a bad order, are so called either because they are less than they ought to have been; or because they are not suited to those things to which they ought to be suited; so that they are called bad because they are alien and incongruous*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0107","text":"[I q. 5 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0108","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the nature of light is said to be without number, weight, and measure, not simply, but in comparison with bodily things, because the power of light extends to all bodily things, insofar as it is an active quality of the first altering body, namely, the heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0109","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0110","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0111","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth article. It seems that good is not suitably divided into the honorable, the useful, and the pleasant. For good, as the Philosopher says in Ethics I, is divided through the ten categories. But the honorable, the useful, and the pleasant can be found in one category. Therefore good is not suitably divided by these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0112","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0113","text":"Further, every division is made through opposites. But these three do not seem to be opposites, for honorable things are pleasant, and nothing dishonorable is useful (which nevertheless would have to be so, if the division were made through opposites, so that the honorable and the useful would be opposed), as Tully also says in the book On Duties. Therefore the aforesaid division is not suitable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0114","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0115","text":"Further, where one thing is for the sake of another, there is only one thing. But the useful is good only for the sake of the pleasant or the honorable. Therefore the useful ought not to be divided against the pleasant and the honorable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0116","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0117","text":"On the contrary, Ambrose, in the book On Duties, uses this division of the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0118","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","text":"I answer that, this division seems properly to be of human good. Yet if we consider the character of good more deeply and more commonly, this division is found properly to belong to good insofar as it is good. For good is something insofar as it is desirable, and is the terminus of the movement of appetite. The termination of this movement can indeed be considered from consideration of the movement of a natural body. Now the movement of a natural body is terminated simply in the ultimate, but in a certain respect also in the intermediate, through which one goes to the ultimate that terminates the movement; and something is called a terminus of movement insofar as it terminates some part of the movement. But that which is the ultimate terminus of movement can be understood in two ways: either as the thing itself toward which there is a tendency, such as place or form; or as rest in that thing. Thus, therefore, in the movement of appetite, that which is desirable and terminates the movement of appetite in a certain respect, as a means through which one tends toward another thing, is called the useful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","text":"But that which is desired as ultimate, wholly terminating the movement of appetite, as a certain thing toward which appetite tends through itself, is called the honorable, because the honorable is said to be that which is desired through itself. But that which terminates the movement of appetite as rest in the thing desired is pleasure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0121","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0122","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the good, insofar as it is the same in subject as being, is divided by the ten categories; but according to its proper notion, this division belongs to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0123","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","text":"To the second, it must be said that this division is not by opposed things, but by opposed notions. Yet those things are properly called pleasurable which have no other notion of desirability than pleasure, although they are sometimes harmful and dishonorable. Useful things, however, are called those which do not have in themselves that whereby they are desired, but are desired only insofar as they lead to something else, as the taking of bitter medicine. Honorable things, however, are called those which have in themselves that whereby they are desired.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0125","text":"[I q. 5 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","text":"To the third, it must be said that the good is not divided into these three as something univocal predicated equally of them, but as something analogous, which is predicated according to prior and posterior. For it is predicated primarily of the honorable; secondarily of the pleasurable; thirdly of the useful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126"]}],"source_digest":"37acc4643d368a7f221b627c7c3413c20c0205703b5682223569e00de4d129d5","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 5"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6","title":"First Part · Question 6","order":7,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 6 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28465)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de bonitate Dei. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum esse bonum conveniat Deo. Secundo, utrum Deus sit summum bonum. Tertio, utrum ipse solus sit bonus per suam essentiam. Quarto, utrum omnia sint bona bonitate divina.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 6 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28466)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse bonum non conveniat Deo. Ratio enim boni consistit in modo, specie et ordine. Haec autem non videntur Deo convenire, cum Deus immensus sit, et ad aliquid non ordinetur. Ergo esse bonum non convenit Deo.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28467)\n\nPraeterea, bonum est quod omnia appetunt. Sed Deum non omnia appetunt, quia non omnia cognoscunt ipsum, nihil autem appetitur nisi notum. Ergo esse bonum non convenit Deo.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28468)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Thren. III, *bonus est dominus sperantibus in eum, animae quaerenti illum*.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28469)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod bonum esse praecipue Deo convenit. Bonum enim aliquid est, secundum quod est appetibile. Unumquodque autem appetit suam perfectionem. Perfectio autem et forma effectus est quaedam similitudo agentis, cum omne agens agat sibi simile. Unde ipsum agens est appetibile, et habet rationem boni, hoc enim est quod de ipso appetitur, ut eius similitudo participetur. Cum ergo Deus sit prima causa effectiva omnium, manifestum est quod sibi competit ratio boni et appetibilis. Unde Dionysius, in libro de Div. Nom., attribuit bonum Deo sicut primae causae efficienti, dicens quod bonus dicitur Deus, *sicut ex quo omnia subsistunt*.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28470)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod habere modum, speciem et ordinem, pertinet ad rationem boni causati. Sed bonum in Deo est sicut in causa, unde ad eum pertinet imponere aliis modum, speciem et ordinem. Unde ista tria sunt in Deo sicut in causa.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28471)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod omnia, appetendo proprias perfectiones, appetunt ipsum Deum, inquantum perfectiones omnium rerum sunt quaedam similitudines divini esse, ut ex dictis patet. Et sic eorum quae Deum appetunt, quaedam cognoscunt ipsum secundum seipsum, quod est proprium creaturae rationalis. Quaedam vero cognoscunt aliquas participationes suae bonitatis, quod etiam extenditur usque ad cognitionem sensibilem. Quaedam vero appetitum naturalem habent absque cognitione, utpote inclinata ad suos fines ab alio superiori cognoscente.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28472)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit summum bonum. Summum enim bonum addit aliquid supra bonum, alioquin omni bono conveniret. Sed omne quod se habet ex additione ad aliquid, est compositum. Ergo summum bonum est compositum. Sed Deus est summe simplex, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo Deus non est summum bonum.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28473)\n\n*Praeterea, bonum est quod omnia appetunt*, ut dicit philosophus. Sed nihil aliud est quod omnia appetunt, nisi solus Deus, qui est finis omnium. Ergo nihil aliud est bonum nisi Deus. Quod etiam videtur per id quod dicitur Matth. XIX, *nemo bonus nisi solus Deus*. Sed summum dicitur in comparatione aliorum; sicut summum calidum in comparatione ad omnia calida. Ergo Deus non potest dici summum bonum.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28474)\n\nPraeterea, summum comparationem importat. Sed quae non sunt unius generis, non sunt comparabilia; sicut dulcedo inconvenienter dicitur maior vel minor quam linea. Cum igitur Deus non sit in eodem genere cum aliis bonis, ut ex superioribus patet, videtur quod Deus non possit dici summum bonum respectu eorum.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28475)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, I de Trin., quod *Trinitas divinarum personarum est summum bonum, quod purgatissimis mentibus cernitur*.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28476)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus est summum bonum simpliciter, et non solum in aliquo genere vel ordine rerum. Sic enim bonum Deo attribuitur, ut dictum est, inquantum omnes perfectiones desideratae effluunt ab eo, sicut a prima causa. Non autem effluunt ab eo sicut ab agente univoco, ut ex superioribus patet, sed sicut ab agente quod non convenit cum suis effectibus, neque in ratione speciei, nec in ratione generis. Similitudo autem effectus in causa quidem univoca invenitur uniformiter, in causa autem aequivoca invenitur excellentius, sicut calor excellentiori modo est in sole quam in igne. Sic ergo oportet quod cum bonum sit in Deo sicut in prima causa omnium non univoca, quod sit in eo excellentissimo modo. Et propter hoc dicitur summum bonum.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28477)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod summum bonum addit super bonum, non rem aliquam absolutam, sed relationem tantum. Relatio autem qua aliquid de Deo dicitur relative ad creaturas, non est realiter in Deo, sed in creatura; in Deo vero secundum rationem; sicut scibile relative dicitur ad scientiam, non quia ad ipsam referatur, sed quia scientia refertur ad ipsum. Et sic non oportet quod in summo bono sit aliqua compositio, sed solum quod alia deficiant ab ipso.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28478)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur *bonum est quod omnia appetunt*, non sic intelligitur quasi unumquodque bonum ab omnibus appetatur, sed quia quidquid appetitur, rationem boni habet. Quod autem dicitur, *nemo bonus nisi solus Deus*, intelligitur de bono per essentiam, ut post dicetur.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28479)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ea quae non sunt in eodem genere, si quidem sint in diversis generibus contenta, nullo modo comparabilia sunt. De Deo autem negatur esse in eodem genere cum aliis bonis, non quod ipse sit in quodam alio genere; sed quia ipse est extra genus, et principium omnis generis. Et sic comparatur ad alia per excessum. Et huiusmodi comparationem importat summum bonum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28480)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse bonum per essentiam non sit proprium Dei. Sicut enim unum convertitur cum ente, ita et bonum, ut supra habitum est. Sed omne ens est unum per suam essentiam, ut patet per philosophum in IV Metaphys. Ergo omne ens est bonum per suam essentiam.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28481)\n\nPraeterea, si bonum est quod omnia appetunt, cum ipsum esse sit desideratum ab omnibus, ipsum esse cuiuslibet rei est eius bonum. Sed quaelibet res est ens per suam essentiam. Ergo quaelibet res est bona per suam essentiam.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28482)\n\nPraeterea, omnis res per suam bonitatem est bona. Si igitur aliqua res est quae non sit bona per suam essentiam, oportebit quod eius bonitas non sit sua essentia. Illa ergo bonitas, cum sit ens quoddam, oportet quod sit bona, et si quidem alia bonitate, iterum de illa bonitate quaeretur. Aut ergo erit procedere in infinitum, aut venire ad aliquam bonitatem quae non erit bona per aliam bonitatem. Eadem ergo ratione standum est in primo. Res igitur quaelibet est bona per suam essentiam.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28483)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Boetius, in libro de Hebdomad., quod alia omnia a Deo sunt bona per participationem. Non igitur per essentiam.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28484)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod solus Deus est bonus per suam essentiam. Unumquodque enim dicitur bonum, secundum quod est perfectum. Perfectio autem alicuius rei triplex est. Prima quidem, secundum quod in suo esse constituitur. Secunda vero, prout ei aliqua accidentia superadduntur, ad suam perfectam operationem necessaria. Tertia vero perfectio alicuius est per hoc, quod aliquid aliud attingit sicut finem. Utpote prima perfectio ignis consistit in esse, quod habet per suam formam substantialem, secunda vero eius perfectio consistit in caliditate, levitate et siccitate, et huiusmodi, tertia vero perfectio eius est secundum quod in loco suo quiescit. Haec autem triplex perfectio nulli creato competit secundum suam essentiam, sed soli Deo, cuius solius essentia est suum esse; et cui non adveniunt aliqua accidentia; sed quae de aliis dicuntur accidentaliter, sibi conveniunt essentialiter, ut esse potentem, sapientem, et huiusmodi, sicut ex dictis patet. Ipse etiam ad nihil aliud ordinatur sicut ad finem, sed ipse est ultimus finis omnium rerum. Unde manifestum est quod solus Deus habet omnimodam perfectionem secundum suam essentiam. Et ideo ipse solus est bonus per suam essentiam.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28485)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod unum non importat rationem perfectionis, sed indivisionis tantum, quae unicuique rei competit secundum suam essentiam. Simplicium autem essentiae sunt indivisae et actu et potentia, compositorum vero essentiae sunt indivisae secundum actum tantum. Et ideo oportet quod quaelibet res sit una per suam essentiam, non autem bona, ut ostensum est.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28486)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet unumquodque sit bonum inquantum habet esse, tamen essentia rei creatae non est ipsum esse, et ideo non sequitur quod res creata sit bona per suam essentiam.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28487)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod bonitas rei creatae non est ipsa eius essentia, sed aliquid superadditum; vel ipsum esse eius, vel aliqua perfectio superaddita, vel ordo ad finem. Ipsa tamen bonitas sic superaddita dicitur bona sicut et ens, hac autem ratione dicitur ens, quia ea est aliquid, non quia ipsa aliquo alio sit. Unde hac ratione dicitur bona, quia ea est aliquid bonum, non quia ipsa habeat aliquam aliam bonitatem, qua sit bona.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 6 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28488)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnia sint bona bonitate divina. Dicit enim Augustinus, VII de Trin., *bonum hoc et bonum illud, tolle hoc et tolle illud, et vide ipsum bonum, si potes, ita Deum videbis, non alio bono bonum, sed bonum omnis boni*. Sed unumquodque est bonum suo bono. Ergo unumquodque est bonum ipso bono quod est Deus.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28489)\n\nPraeterea, sicut dicit Boetius, in libro de Hebdomad., omnia dicuntur bona inquantum ordinantur ad Deum, et hoc ratione bonitatis divinae. Ergo omnia sunt bona bonitate divina.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28490)\n\nSed contra est quod omnia sunt bona inquantum sunt. Sed non dicuntur omnia entia per esse divinum, sed per esse proprium. Ergo non omnia sunt bona bonitate divina, sed bonitate propria.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28491)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nihil prohibet in his quae relationem important, aliquid ab extrinseco denominari; sicut aliquid denominatur locatum a loco, et mensuratum a mensura. Circa vero ea quae absolute dicuntur, diversa fuit opinio. Plato enim posuit omnium rerum species separatas; et quod ab eis individua denominantur, quasi species separatas participando; ut puta quod Socrates dicitur homo secundum ideam hominis separatam. Et sicut ponebat ideam hominis et equi separatam, quam vocabat per se hominem et per se equum, ita ponebat ideam entis et ideam unius separatam, quam dicebat per se ens et per se unum, et eius participatione unumquodque dicitur ens vel unum. Hoc autem quod est per se bonum et per se unum, ponebat esse summum Deum, a quo omnia dicuntur bona per modum participationis. Et quamvis haec opinio irrationabilis videatur quantum ad hoc, quod ponebat species rerum naturalium separatas per se subsistentes, ut Aristoteles multipliciter probat; tamen hoc absolute verum est, quod est aliquod unum per essentiam suam bonum, quod dicimus Deum, ut ex superioribus patet. Huic etiam sententiae concordat Aristoteles. A primo igitur per suam essentiam ente et bono, unumquodque potest dici bonum et ens, inquantum participat ipsum per modum cuiusdam assimilationis, licet remote et deficienter, ut ex superioribus patet. Sic ergo unumquodque dicitur bonum bonitate divina, sicut primo principio exemplari, effectivo et finali totius bonitatis. Nihilominus tamen unumquodque dicitur bonum similitudine divinae bonitatis sibi inhaerente, quae est formaliter sua bonitas denominans ipsum. Et sic est bonitas una omnium; et etiam multae bonitates.\n\n[I q. 6 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28492)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0002","text":"[I q. 6 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de bonitate Dei. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum esse bonum conveniat Deo. Secundo, utrum Deus sit summum bonum. Tertio, utrum ipse solus sit bonus per suam essentiam. Quarto, utrum omnia sint bona bonitate divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0005","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse bonum non conveniat Deo. Ratio enim boni consistit in modo, specie et ordine. Haec autem non videntur Deo convenire, cum Deus immensus sit, et ad aliquid non ordinetur. Ergo esse bonum non convenit Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0007","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0008","text":"Praeterea, bonum est quod omnia appetunt. Sed Deum non omnia appetunt, quia non omnia cognoscunt ipsum, nihil autem appetitur nisi notum. Ergo esse bonum non convenit Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0009","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0010","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Thren. III, *bonus est dominus sperantibus in eum, animae quaerenti illum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0011","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod bonum esse praecipue Deo convenit. Bonum enim aliquid est, secundum quod est appetibile. Unumquodque autem appetit suam perfectionem. Perfectio autem et forma effectus est quaedam similitudo agentis, cum omne agens agat sibi simile. Unde ipsum agens est appetibile, et habet rationem boni, hoc enim est quod de ipso appetitur, ut eius similitudo participetur. Cum ergo Deus sit prima causa effectiva omnium, manifestum est quod sibi competit ratio boni et appetibilis. Unde Dionysius, in libro de Div. Nom., attribuit bonum Deo sicut primae causae efficienti, dicens quod bonus dicitur Deus, *sicut ex quo omnia subsistunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0013","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod habere modum, speciem et ordinem, pertinet ad rationem boni causati. Sed bonum in Deo est sicut in causa, unde ad eum pertinet imponere aliis modum, speciem et ordinem. Unde ista tria sunt in Deo sicut in causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0015","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod omnia, appetendo proprias perfectiones, appetunt ipsum Deum, inquantum perfectiones omnium rerum sunt quaedam similitudines divini esse, ut ex dictis patet. Et sic eorum quae Deum appetunt, quaedam cognoscunt ipsum secundum seipsum, quod est proprium creaturae rationalis. Quaedam vero cognoscunt aliquas participationes suae bonitatis, quod etiam extenditur usque ad cognitionem sensibilem. Quaedam vero appetitum naturalem habent absque cognitione, utpote inclinata ad suos fines ab alio superiori cognoscente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0017","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0018","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0019","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit summum bonum. Summum enim bonum addit aliquid supra bonum, alioquin omni bono conveniret. Sed omne quod se habet ex additione ad aliquid, est compositum. Ergo summum bonum est compositum. Sed Deus est summe simplex, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo Deus non est summum bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0020","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0021","text":"*Praeterea, bonum est quod omnia appetunt*, ut dicit philosophus. Sed nihil aliud est quod omnia appetunt, nisi solus Deus, qui est finis omnium. Ergo nihil aliud est bonum nisi Deus. Quod etiam videtur per id quod dicitur Matth. XIX, *nemo bonus nisi solus Deus*. Sed summum dicitur in comparatione aliorum; sicut summum calidum in comparatione ad omnia calida. Ergo Deus non potest dici summum bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0022","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0023","text":"Praeterea, summum comparationem importat. Sed quae non sunt unius generis, non sunt comparabilia; sicut dulcedo inconvenienter dicitur maior vel minor quam linea. Cum igitur Deus non sit in eodem genere cum aliis bonis, ut ex superioribus patet, videtur quod Deus non possit dici summum bonum respectu eorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0024","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0025","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, I de Trin., quod *Trinitas divinarum personarum est summum bonum, quod purgatissimis mentibus cernitur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0026","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus est summum bonum simpliciter, et non solum in aliquo genere vel ordine rerum. Sic enim bonum Deo attribuitur, ut dictum est, inquantum omnes perfectiones desideratae effluunt ab eo, sicut a prima causa. Non autem effluunt ab eo sicut ab agente univoco, ut ex superioribus patet, sed sicut ab agente quod non convenit cum suis effectibus, neque in ratione speciei, nec in ratione generis. Similitudo autem effectus in causa quidem univoca invenitur uniformiter, in causa autem aequivoca invenitur excellentius, sicut calor excellentiori modo est in sole quam in igne. Sic ergo oportet quod cum bonum sit in Deo sicut in prima causa omnium non univoca, quod sit in eo excellentissimo modo. Et propter hoc dicitur summum bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0028","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod summum bonum addit super bonum, non rem aliquam absolutam, sed relationem tantum. Relatio autem qua aliquid de Deo dicitur relative ad creaturas, non est realiter in Deo, sed in creatura; in Deo vero secundum rationem; sicut scibile relative dicitur ad scientiam, non quia ad ipsam referatur, sed quia scientia refertur ad ipsum. Et sic non oportet quod in summo bono sit aliqua compositio, sed solum quod alia deficiant ab ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0030","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0031","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur *bonum est quod omnia appetunt*, non sic intelligitur quasi unumquodque bonum ab omnibus appetatur, sed quia quidquid appetitur, rationem boni habet. Quod autem dicitur, *nemo bonus nisi solus Deus*, intelligitur de bono per essentiam, ut post dicetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0032","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ea quae non sunt in eodem genere, si quidem sint in diversis generibus contenta, nullo modo comparabilia sunt. De Deo autem negatur esse in eodem genere cum aliis bonis, non quod ipse sit in quodam alio genere; sed quia ipse est extra genus, et principium omnis generis. Et sic comparatur ad alia per excessum. Et huiusmodi comparationem importat summum bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0034","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0035","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0036","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse bonum per essentiam non sit proprium Dei. Sicut enim unum convertitur cum ente, ita et bonum, ut supra habitum est. Sed omne ens est unum per suam essentiam, ut patet per philosophum in IV Metaphys. Ergo omne ens est bonum per suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0037","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0038","text":"Praeterea, si bonum est quod omnia appetunt, cum ipsum esse sit desideratum ab omnibus, ipsum esse cuiuslibet rei est eius bonum. Sed quaelibet res est ens per suam essentiam. Ergo quaelibet res est bona per suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0039","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0040","text":"Praeterea, omnis res per suam bonitatem est bona. Si igitur aliqua res est quae non sit bona per suam essentiam, oportebit quod eius bonitas non sit sua essentia. Illa ergo bonitas, cum sit ens quoddam, oportet quod sit bona, et si quidem alia bonitate, iterum de illa bonitate quaeretur. Aut ergo erit procedere in infinitum, aut venire ad aliquam bonitatem quae non erit bona per aliam bonitatem. Eadem ergo ratione standum est in primo. Res igitur quaelibet est bona per suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0041","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0042","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Boetius, in libro de Hebdomad., quod alia omnia a Deo sunt bona per participationem. Non igitur per essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0043","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod solus Deus est bonus per suam essentiam. Unumquodque enim dicitur bonum, secundum quod est perfectum. Perfectio autem alicuius rei triplex est. Prima quidem, secundum quod in suo esse constituitur. Secunda vero, prout ei aliqua accidentia superadduntur, ad suam perfectam operationem necessaria. Tertia vero perfectio alicuius est per hoc, quod aliquid aliud attingit sicut finem. Utpote prima perfectio ignis consistit in esse, quod habet per suam formam substantialem, secunda vero eius perfectio consistit in caliditate, levitate et siccitate, et huiusmodi, tertia vero perfectio eius est secundum quod in loco suo quiescit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","text":"Haec autem triplex perfectio nulli creato competit secundum suam essentiam, sed soli Deo, cuius solius essentia est suum esse; et cui non adveniunt aliqua accidentia; sed quae de aliis dicuntur accidentaliter, sibi conveniunt essentialiter, ut esse potentem, sapientem, et huiusmodi, sicut ex dictis patet. Ipse etiam ad nihil aliud ordinatur sicut ad finem, sed ipse est ultimus finis omnium rerum. Unde manifestum est quod solus Deus habet omnimodam perfectionem secundum suam essentiam. Et ideo ipse solus est bonus per suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0046","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0047","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod unum non importat rationem perfectionis, sed indivisionis tantum, quae unicuique rei competit secundum suam essentiam. Simplicium autem essentiae sunt indivisae et actu et potentia, compositorum vero essentiae sunt indivisae secundum actum tantum. Et ideo oportet quod quaelibet res sit una per suam essentiam, non autem bona, ut ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0048","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet unumquodque sit bonum inquantum habet esse, tamen essentia rei creatae non est ipsum esse, et ideo non sequitur quod res creata sit bona per suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0050","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod bonitas rei creatae non est ipsa eius essentia, sed aliquid superadditum; vel ipsum esse eius, vel aliqua perfectio superaddita, vel ordo ad finem. Ipsa tamen bonitas sic superaddita dicitur bona sicut et ens, hac autem ratione dicitur ens, quia ea est aliquid, non quia ipsa aliquo alio sit. Unde hac ratione dicitur bona, quia ea est aliquid bonum, non quia ipsa habeat aliquam aliam bonitatem, qua sit bona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0052","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0053","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0054","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnia sint bona bonitate divina. Dicit enim Augustinus, VII de Trin., *bonum hoc et bonum illud, tolle hoc et tolle illud, et vide ipsum bonum, si potes, ita Deum videbis, non alio bono bonum, sed bonum omnis boni*. Sed unumquodque est bonum suo bono. Ergo unumquodque est bonum ipso bono quod est Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0055","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0056","text":"Praeterea, sicut dicit Boetius, in libro de Hebdomad., omnia dicuntur bona inquantum ordinantur ad Deum, et hoc ratione bonitatis divinae. Ergo omnia sunt bona bonitate divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0057","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0058","text":"Sed contra est quod omnia sunt bona inquantum sunt. Sed non dicuntur omnia entia per esse divinum, sed per esse proprium. Ergo non omnia sunt bona bonitate divina, sed bonitate propria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0059","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0060","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nihil prohibet in his quae relationem important, aliquid ab extrinseco denominari; sicut aliquid denominatur locatum a loco, et mensuratum a mensura. Circa vero ea quae absolute dicuntur, diversa fuit opinio. Plato enim posuit omnium rerum species separatas; et quod ab eis individua denominantur, quasi species separatas participando; ut puta quod Socrates dicitur homo secundum ideam hominis separatam. Et sicut ponebat ideam hominis et equi separatam, quam vocabat per se hominem et per se equum, ita ponebat ideam entis et ideam unius separatam, quam dicebat per se ens et per se unum, et eius participatione unumquodque dicitur ens vel unum. Hoc autem quod est per se bonum et per se unum, ponebat esse summum Deum, a quo omnia dicuntur bona per modum participationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","text":"Et quamvis haec opinio irrationabilis videatur quantum ad hoc, quod ponebat species rerum naturalium separatas per se subsistentes, ut Aristoteles multipliciter probat; tamen hoc absolute verum est, quod est aliquod unum per essentiam suam bonum, quod dicimus Deum, ut ex superioribus patet. Huic etiam sententiae concordat Aristoteles. A primo igitur per suam essentiam ente et bono, unumquodque potest dici bonum et ens, inquantum participat ipsum per modum cuiusdam assimilationis, licet remote et deficienter, ut ex superioribus patet. Sic ergo unumquodque dicitur bonum bonitate divina, sicut primo principio exemplari, effectivo et finali totius bonitatis. Nihilominus tamen unumquodque dicitur bonum similitudine divinae bonitatis sibi inhaerente, quae est formaliter sua bonitas denominans ipsum. Et sic est bonitas una omnium; et etiam multae bonitates.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0062","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0063","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6ee8eec6b5cf036e6618de0383f30e0081d90b01ae1f022284675d4e64bde738","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T133854485241Z_7edf2a36","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 6","source_sha256":"6ee8eec6b5cf036e6618de0383f30e0081d90b01ae1f022284675d4e64bde738","sha256":"39d4c0105f1349d3e31f464b58dcef4ca9569e92d4ad643d47dd7631e7475512","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:38:54.485308+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0002","text":"[I q. 6 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0003","text":"Next, the goodness of God is considered. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether to be good befits God. Second, whether God is the highest good. Third, whether He alone is good through His essence. Fourth, whether all things are good by divine goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0005","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that being good does not befit God. For the account of the good consists in mode, species, and order. But these do not seem to befit God, since God is immeasurable and is not ordered to anything. Therefore, being good does not befit God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0007","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0008","text":"Moreover, the good is that which all things desire. But not all things desire God, because not all things know Him; yet nothing is desired except what is known. Therefore, being good does not befit God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0009","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0010","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Lamentations III: *the Lord is good to those who hope in Him, to the soul that seeks Him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0011","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","text":"I answer that it must be said that being good especially befits God. For something is good insofar as it is desirable. But each thing desires its own perfection. Now the perfection and form of an effect is a certain likeness of the agent, since every agent acts so as to produce something like itself. Hence the agent itself is desirable, and has the character of the good; for this is what is desired of it, that its likeness may be participated. Therefore, since God is the first efficient cause of all things, it is manifest that the character of the good and of the desirable belongs to Him. Hence Dionysius, in the book *On the Divine Names*, attributes the good to God as to the first efficient cause, saying that God is called good, *as that from which all things subsist*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0013","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to have mode, species, and order belongs to the account of caused good. But good is in God as in a cause; hence it belongs to Him to impose mode, species, and order upon others. Therefore, these three are in God as in a cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0015","text":"[I q. 6 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","text":"To the second, it must be said that all things, by desiring their own perfections, desire God Himself, insofar as the perfections of all things are certain likenesses of divine being, as is clear from what has been said. And thus, among those things that desire God, some know Him in Himself, which is proper to the rational creature. But some know certain participations of His goodness, and this extends even to sensible knowledge. But some have a natural appetite without knowledge, as being inclined toward their ends by another, higher knower.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0017","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0018","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0019","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God is not the highest good. For the highest good adds something above good; otherwise, it would belong to every good. But everything that stands by addition in relation to something is composite. Therefore, the highest good is composite. But God is supremely simple, as was shown above. Therefore, God is not the highest good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0020","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0021","text":"*Moreover, the good is that which all things desire*, as the Philosopher says. But that which all things desire is nothing other than God alone, who is the end of all things. Therefore, nothing other than God is good. This also seems evident from what is said in Matthew XIX: *none is good except God alone*. But the highest is said in comparison with others, as the hottest in comparison with all hot things. Therefore, God cannot be called the highest good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0022","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0023","text":"Moreover, the highest implies comparison. But things that are not of one genus are not comparable; just as sweetness is improperly said to be greater or less than a line. Therefore, since God is not in the same genus as other goods, as is clear from the foregoing, it seems that God cannot be called the highest good with respect to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0024","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0025","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, I, that *the Trinity of the divine persons is the supreme good, which is beheld by minds most purified*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0026","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","text":"I answer that it must be said that God is the supreme good simply, and not only in some genus or order of things. For good is attributed to God, as has been said, insofar as all desirable perfections flow forth from Him, as from the first cause. Yet they do not flow forth from Him as from a univocal agent, as is clear from what has been said above, but as from an agent that does not agree with its effects, neither in the account of species nor in the account of genus. Now the likeness of the effect is found uniformly in a univocal cause, but in an equivocal cause it is found more excellently, just as heat is in a more excellent manner in the sun than in fire. Therefore, since good is in God as in the first non-univocal cause of all things, it must be in Him in the most excellent manner. And for this reason He is called the supreme good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0028","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the supreme good adds to good not some absolute thing, but only a relation. But a relation by which something is said of God relative to creatures is not really in God, but in the creature; whereas in God it is according to reason; just as the knowable is said relatively to knowledge, not because it is referred to knowledge, but because knowledge is referred to it. And thus it is not necessary that there be any composition in the supreme good, but only that other things fall short of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0030","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0031","text":"To the second it must be said that, when it is said, *good is that which all things desire*, this is not understood as though each good were desired by all things, but because whatever is desired has the account of good. But what is said, *no one is good except God alone*, is understood of good through essence, as will be said below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0032","text":"[I q. 6 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","text":"To the third it must be said that things which are not in the same genus, if indeed they are contained in diverse genera, are in no way comparable. But God is denied to be in the same genus as other goods, not because He is in some other genus, but because He is outside genus and is the principle of every genus. And thus He is compared to others by excess. And the supreme good implies a comparison of this sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0034","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0035","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0036","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that to be good through essence is not proper to God. For just as one is convertible with being, so too is good, as was maintained above. But every being is one through its essence, as is clear through the Philosopher in *Metaphysics* IV. Therefore every being is good through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0037","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0038","text":"Further, if good is that which all things desire, since being itself is desired by all things, the very being of any thing is its good. But each thing is a being through its essence. Therefore each thing is good through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0039","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0040","text":"Further, every thing is good through its goodness. Therefore, if there is some thing that is not good through its essence, its goodness will have to be not its essence. That goodness, then, since it is a certain being, must be good; and if indeed through another goodness, the question will again be asked concerning that goodness. Therefore, either there will be a proceeding to infinity, or one will arrive at some goodness which will not be good through another goodness. By the same reasoning, then, one must stop at the first. Therefore every thing is good through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0041","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0042","text":"On the contrary, Boethius says, in the book *On the Hebdomads*, that all other things besides God are good by participation. Therefore, not through essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0043","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","text":"I answer that it must be said that God alone is good through His essence. For each thing is called good insofar as it is perfect. Now the perfection of a thing is threefold. The first is according as it is constituted in its being. The second, indeed, insofar as certain accidents necessary for its perfect operation are added to it. The third perfection of a thing is through this: that it attains something else as an end. Thus the first perfection of fire consists in being, which it has through its substantial form; its second perfection consists in heat, lightness, and dryness, and such things; but its third perfection is insofar as it rests in its own place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","text":"But this threefold perfection belongs to no creature according to its essence, but to God alone, whose essence alone is His being; and to whom no accidents accrue. Rather, things which are said of others accidentally belong to Him essentially, such as being powerful, wise, and the like, as is clear from what has been said. He Himself is also not ordered to anything else as to an end, but He is the ultimate end of all things. Hence it is manifest that God alone has every kind of perfection according to His essence. And therefore He alone is good through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0046","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0047","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that one does not convey the account of perfection, but only of indivision, which belongs to each thing according to its essence. The essences of simple things, however, are undivided both in act and in potency, whereas the essences of composite things are undivided according to act only. And therefore every thing must be one through its essence, but not good, as has been shown.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0048","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although each thing is good insofar as it has being, nevertheless the essence of a created thing is not its very being; and therefore it does not follow that a created thing is good through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0050","text":"[I q. 6 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051","text":"To the third, it must be said that the goodness of a created thing is not its very essence, but something added over and above: either its very being, or some perfection added over and above, or an order to an end. Yet the goodness itself thus added is called good just as being is called being; but it is called being for this reason, because by it something is, not because it itself is by something else. Hence it is called good for this reason, because by it something is good, not because it itself has some other goodness by which it is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0052","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0053","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0054","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that all things are good by the divine goodness. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, VII: *This good and that good—take away this and take away that, and see good itself, if you can; thus you will see God, good not by another good, but the good of every good.* But each thing is good by its own good. Therefore each thing is good by the good itself which is God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0055","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0056","text":"Further, as Boethius says in the book *On the Hebdomads*, all things are called good insofar as they are ordered to God, and this by reason of the divine goodness. Therefore all things are good by the divine goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0057","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0058","text":"On the contrary, all things are good insofar as they are. But not all things are called beings through divine being, but through their own being. Therefore not all things are good by the divine goodness, but by their own goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0059","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0060","text":"I answer that it must be said that nothing prevents, in those things which imply a relation, something from being denominated from without; just as something is denominated located from place, and measured from measure. But concerning those things which are said absolutely, there was a diversity of opinion. For Plato posited separate species of all things; and that individuals are denominated from them, as though by participating in the separate species; for instance, that Socrates is called a man according to the separate idea of man. And just as he posited a separate idea of man and of horse, which he called man-in-itself and horse-in-itself, so he posited a separate idea of being and a separate idea of one, which he called being-in-itself and one-in-itself; and by participation in it each thing is called a being or one. But that which is good-in-itself and one-in-itself, he posited to be the highest God, from whom all things are called good by way of participation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","text":"And although this opinion seems unreasonable insofar as it posited the species of natural things as separate, subsisting through themselves, as Aristotle proves in many ways; nevertheless, this is absolutely true: that there is something which is one and good through its essence, which we call God, as is clear from what has been said above. Aristotle also agrees with this position. Therefore, from the first being and good through its essence, each thing can be called good and being insofar as it participates in it by way of a certain assimilation, although remotely and deficiently, as is clear from what has been said above. Thus, therefore, each thing is called good by the divine goodness, as by the first exemplary, efficient, and final principle of all goodness. Nevertheless, each thing is called good by a likeness of the divine goodness inhering in it, which is formally its goodness, denominating it. And thus goodness is one for all things; and also there are many goodnesses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0062","text":"[I q. 6 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0063","text":"And through this the response to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0063"]}],"source_digest":"313edaa874b0d53a48db2d99945b8dd03355719a625bfaf455471e6974fb80c0","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 6"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 6","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7","title":"First Part · Question 7","order":8,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 7 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28493)\n\nPost considerationem divinae perfectionis, considerandum est de eius infinitate, et de existentia eius in rebus, attribuitur enim Deo quod sit ubique et in omnibus rebus, inquantum est incircumscriptibilis et infinitus. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Deus sit infinitus. Secundo, utrum aliquid praeter ipsum sit infinitum secundum essentiam. Tertio, utrum aliquid possit esse infinitum secundum magnitudinem. Quarto, utrum possit esse infinitum in rebus secundum multitudinem.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28494)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit infinitus. Omne enim infinitum est imperfectum, quia habet rationem partis et materiae, ut dicitur in III Physic. Sed Deus est perfectissimus. Ergo non est infinitus.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28495)\n\nPraeterea, secundum philosophum in I Physic., finitum et infinitum conveniunt quantitati. Sed in Deo non est quantitas, cum non sit corpus, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo non competit sibi esse infinitum.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28496)\n\nPraeterea, quod ita est hic quod non alibi, est finitum secundum locum, ergo quod ita est hoc quod non est aliud, est finitum secundum substantiam. Sed Deus est hoc, et non est aliud, non enim est lapis nec lignum. Ergo Deus non est infinitus secundum substantiam.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28497)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Damascenus, quod *Deus est infinitus et aeternus et incircumscriptibilis*.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28498)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod omnes antiqui philosophi attribuunt infinitum primo principio, ut dicitur in III Physic., et hoc rationabiliter, considerantes res effluere a primo principio in infinitum. Sed quia quidam erraverunt circa naturam primi principii, consequens fuit ut errarent circa infinitatem ipsius. Quia enim ponebant primum principium materiam, consequenter attribuerunt primo principio infinitatem materialem; dicentes aliquod corpus infinitum esse primum principium rerum. Considerandum est igitur quod infinitum dicitur aliquid ex eo quod non est finitum. Finitur autem quodammodo et materia per formam, et forma per materiam. Materia quidem per formam, inquantum materia, antequam recipiat formam, est in potentia ad multas formas, sed cum recipit unam, terminatur per illam. Forma vero finitur per materiam, inquantum forma, in se considerata, communis est ad multa, sed per hoc quod recipitur in materia, fit forma determinate huius rei. Materia autem perficitur per formam per quam finitur, et ideo infinitum secundum quod attribuitur materiae, habet rationem imperfecti; est enim quasi materia non habens formam. Forma autem non perficitur per materiam, sed magis per eam eius amplitudo contrahitur, unde infinitum secundum quod se tenet ex parte formae non determinatae per materiam, habet rationem perfecti. Illud autem quod est maxime formale omnium, est ipsum esse, ut ex superioribus patet. Cum igitur esse divinum non sit esse receptum in aliquo, sed ipse sit suum esse subsistens, ut supra ostensum est; manifestum est quod ipse Deus sit infinitus et perfectus.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28499)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad primum.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28500)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod terminus quantitatis est sicut forma ipsius, cuius signum est, quod figura, quae consistit in terminatione quantitatis, est quaedam forma circa quantitatem. Unde infinitum quod competit quantitati, est infinitum quod se tenet ex parte materiae, et tale infinitum non attribuitur Deo, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28501)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, ex hoc ipso quod esse Dei est per se subsistens non receptum in aliquo, prout dicitur infinitum, distinguitur ab omnibus aliis, et alia removentur ab eo, sicut, si esset albedo subsistens, ex hoc ipso quod non esset in alio, differret ab omni albedine existente in subiecto.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28502)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquid aliud quam Deus possit esse infinitum per essentiam. Virtus enim rei proportionatur essentiae eius. Si igitur essentia Dei est infinita, oportet quod eius virtus sit infinita. Ergo potest producere effectum infinitum, cum quantitas virtutis per effectum cognoscatur.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28503)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid habet virtutem infinitam, habet essentiam infinitam. Sed intellectus creatus habet virtutem infinitam, apprehendit enim universale, quod se potest extendere ad infinita singularia. Ergo omnis substantia intellectualis creata est infinita.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28504)\n\nPraeterea, materia prima aliud est a Deo, ut supra ostensum est. Sed materia prima est infinita. Ergo aliquid aliud praeter Deum potest esse infinitum.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28505)\n\nSed contra est quod infinitum non potest esse ex principio aliquo, ut dicitur in III Physic. Omne autem quod est praeter Deum, est ex Deo sicut ex primo principio. Ergo nihil quod est praeter Deum, potest esse infinitum.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28506)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliquid praeter Deum potest esse infinitum secundum quid, sed non simpliciter. Si enim loquamur de infinito secundum quod competit materiae, manifestum est quod omne existens in actu, habet aliquam formam, et sic materia eius est terminata per formam. Sed quia materia, secundum quod est sub una forma substantiali, remanet in potentia ad multas formas accidentales; quod est finitum simpliciter, potest esse infinitum secundum quid, utpote lignum est finitum secundum suam formam, sed tamen est infinitum secundum quid, inquantum est in potentia ad figuras infinitas. Si autem loquamur de infinito secundum quod convenit formae, sic manifestum est quod illa quorum formae sunt in materia, sunt simpliciter finita, et nullo modo infinita. Si autem sint aliquae formae creatae non receptae in materia, sed per se subsistentes, ut quidam de Angelis opinantur, erunt quidem infinitae secundum quid, inquantum huiusmodi formae non terminantur neque contrahuntur per aliquam materiam, sed quia forma creata sic subsistens habet esse, et non est suum esse, necesse est quod ipsum eius esse sit receptum et contractum ad determinatam naturam. Unde non potest esse infinitum simpliciter.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28507)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc est contra rationem facti, quod essentia rei sit ipsum esse eius, quia esse subsistens non est esse creatum, unde contra rationem facti est, quod sit simpliciter infinitum. Sicut ergo Deus, licet habeat potentiam infinitam, non tamen potest facere aliquid non factum (hoc enim esset contradictoria esse simul); ita non potest facere aliquid infinitum simpliciter.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28508)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod hoc ipsum quod virtus intellectus extendit se quodammodo ad infinita, procedit ex hoc quod intellectus est forma non in materia; sed vel totaliter separata, sicut sunt substantiae Angelorum; vel ad minus potentia intellectiva, quae non est actus alicuius organi, in anima intellectiva corpori coniuncta.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28509)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod materia prima non existit in rerum natura per seipsam, cum non sit ens in actu, sed potentia tantum, unde magis est aliquid concreatum, quam creatum. Nihilominus tamen materia prima, etiam secundum potentiam, non est infinita simpliciter, sed secundum quid, quia eius potentia non se extendit nisi ad formas naturales.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28510)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod possit esse aliquid infinitum actu secundum magnitudinem. In scientiis enim mathematicis non invenitur falsum, *quia abstrahentium non est mendacium*, ut dicitur in II Physic. Sed scientiae mathematicae utuntur infinito secundum magnitudinem, dicit enim geometra in suis demonstrationibus, sit linea talis infinita. Ergo non est impossibile aliquid esse infinitum secundum magnitudinem.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28511)\n\nPraeterea, id quod non est contra rationem alicuius, non est impossibile convenire sibi. Sed esse infinitum non est contra rationem magnitudinis, sed magis finitum et infinitum videntur esse passiones quantitatis. Ergo non est impossibile aliquam magnitudinem esse infinitam.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28512)\n\nPraeterea, magnitudo divisibilis est in infinitum, sic enim definitur continuum, quod est in infinitum divisibile, ut patet in III Physic. Sed contraria nata sunt fieri circa idem. Cum ergo divisioni opponatur additio, et diminutioni augmentum, videtur quod magnitudo possit crescere in infinitum. Ergo possibile est esse magnitudinem infinitam.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28513)\n\nPraeterea, motus et tempus habent quantitatem et continuitatem a magnitudine super quam transit motus, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Sed non est contra rationem temporis et motus quod sint infinita, cum unumquodque indivisibile signatum in tempore et motu circulari, sit principium et finis. Ergo nec contra rationem magnitudinis erit quod sit infinita.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28514)\n\nSed contra, omne corpus superficiem habet. Sed omne corpus superficiem habens est finitum, quia superficies est terminus corporis finiti. Ergo omne corpus est finitum. Et similiter potest dici de superficie et linea. Nihil est ergo infinitum secundum magnitudinem.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28515)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliud est esse infinitum secundum suam essentiam, et secundum magnitudinem. Dato enim quod esset aliquod corpus infinitum secundum magnitudinem, utpote ignis vel aer, non tamen esset infinitum secundum essentiam, quia essentia sua esset terminata ad aliquam speciem per formam, et ad aliquod individuum per materiam. Et ideo, habito ex praemissis quod nulla creatura est infinita secundum essentiam, adhuc restat inquirere utrum aliquid creatum sit infinitum secundum magnitudinem. Sciendum est igitur quod corpus, quod est magnitudo completa, dupliciter sumitur, scilicet mathematice, secundum quod consideratur in eo sola quantitas; et naturaliter, secundum quod consideratur in eo materia et forma. Et de corpore quidem naturali, quod non possit esse infinitum in actu, manifestum est. Nam omne corpus naturale aliquam formam substantialem habet determinatam, cum igitur ad formam substantialem consequantur accidentia, necesse est quod ad determinatam formam consequantur determinata accidentia; inter quae est quantitas. Unde omne corpus naturale habet determinatam quantitatem et in maius et in minus. Unde impossibile est aliquod corpus naturale infinitum esse. Hoc etiam ex motu patet. Quia omne corpus naturale habet aliquem motum naturalem. Corpus autem infinitum non posset habere aliquem motum naturalem, nec rectum, quia nihil movetur naturaliter motu recto, nisi cum est extra suum locum, quod corpori infinito accidere non posset; occuparet enim omnia loca, et sic indifferenter quilibet locus esset locus eius. Et similiter etiam neque secundum motum circularem. Quia in motu circulari oportet quod una pars corporis transferatur ad locum in quo fuit alia pars; quod in corpore circulari, si ponatur infinitum, esse non posset, quia duae lineae protractae a centro, quanto longius protrahuntur a centro, tanto longius distant ab invicem; si ergo corpus esset infinitum, in infinitum lineae distarent ab invicem, et sic una nunquam posset pervenire ad locum alterius. De corpore etiam mathematico eadem ratio est. Quia si imaginemur corpus mathematicum existens actu, oportet quod imaginemur ipsum sub aliqua forma, quia nihil est actu nisi per suam formam. Unde, cum forma quanti, inquantum huiusmodi, sit figura, oportebit quod habeat aliquam figuram. Et sic erit finitum, est enim figura, quae termino vel terminis comprehenditur.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28516)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod geometer non indiget sumere aliquam lineam esse infinitam actu, sed indiget accipere aliquam lineam finitam actu, a qua possit subtrahi quantum necesse est, et hanc nominat lineam infinitam.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28517)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet infinitum non sit contra rationem magnitudinis in communi, est tamen contra rationem cuiuslibet speciei eius, scilicet contra rationem magnitudinis bicubitae vel tricubitae, sive circularis vel triangularis, et similium. Non autem est possibile in genere esse quod in nulla specie est. Unde non est possibile esse aliquam magnitudinem infinitam, cum nulla species magnitudinis sit infinita.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28518)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod infinitum quod convenit quantitati, ut dictum est, se tenet ex parte materiae. Per divisionem autem totius acceditur ad materiam, nam partes se habent in ratione materiae, per additionem autem acceditur ad totum, quod se habet in ratione formae. Et ideo non invenitur infinitum in additione magnitudinis, sed in divisione tantum.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28519)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod motus et tempus non sunt secundum totum in actu, sed successive, unde habent potentiam permixtam actui. Sed magnitudo est tota in actu. Et ideo infinitum quod convenit quantitati, et se tenet ex parte materiae, repugnat totalitati magnitudinis, non autem totalitati temporis vel motus, esse enim in potentia convenit materiae.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28520)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod possibile sit esse multitudinem infinitam secundum actum. Non enim est impossibile id quod est in potentia reduci ad actum. Sed numerus est in infinitum multiplicabilis. Ergo non est impossibile esse multitudinem infinitam in actu.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28521)\n\nPraeterea, cuiuslibet speciei possibile est esse aliquod individuum in actu. Sed species figurae sunt infinitae. Ergo possibile est esse infinitas figuras in actu.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28522)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae non opponuntur ad invicem, non impediunt se invicem. Sed, posita aliqua multitudine rerum, adhuc possunt fieri alia multa quae eis non opponuntur, ergo non est impossibile aliqua iterum simul esse cum eis, et sic in infinitum. Ergo possibile est esse infinita in actu.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28523)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XI, *omnia in pondere, numero et mensura disposuisti*.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28524)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc fuit duplex opinio. Quidam enim, sicut Avicenna et Algazel, dixerunt quod impossibile est esse multitudinem actu infinitam per se, sed infinitam per accidens multitudinem esse, non est impossibile. Dicitur enim multitudo esse infinita per se, quando requiritur ad aliquid ut multitudo infinita sit. Et hoc est impossibile esse, quia sic oporteret quod aliquid dependeret ex infinitis; unde eius generatio nunquam compleretur, cum non sit infinita pertransire. Per accidens autem dicitur multitudo infinita, quando non requiritur ad aliquid infinitas multitudinis, sed accidit ita esse. Et hoc sic manifestari potest in operatione fabri, ad quam quaedam multitudo requiritur per se, scilicet quod sit ars in anima, et manus movens, et martellus. Et si haec in infinitum multiplicarentur, nunquam opus fabrile compleretur, quia dependeret ex infinitis causis. Sed multitudo martellorum quae accidit ex hoc quod unum frangitur et accipitur aliud, est multitudo per accidens, accidit enim quod multis martellis operetur; et nihil differt utrum uno vel duobus vel pluribus operetur, vel infinitis, si infinito tempore operaretur. Per hunc igitur modum, posuerunt quod possibile est esse actu multitudinem infinitam per accidens. Sed hoc est impossibile. Quia omnem multitudinem oportet esse in aliqua specie multitudinis. Species autem multitudinis sunt secundum species numerorum. Nulla autem species numeri est infinita, quia quilibet numerus est multitudo mensurata per unum. Unde impossibile est esse multitudinem infinitam actu, sive per se, sive per accidens. Item, multitudo in rerum natura existens est creata, et omne creatum sub aliqua certa intentione creantis comprehenditur, non enim in vanum agens aliquod operatur. Unde necesse est quod sub certo numero omnia creata comprehendantur. Impossibile est ergo esse multitudinem infinitam in actu, etiam per accidens. Sed esse multitudinem infinitam in potentia, possibile est. Quia augmentum multitudinis consequitur divisionem magnitudinis, quanto enim aliquid plus dividitur, tanto plura secundum numerum resultant. Unde, sicut infinitum invenitur in potentia in divisione continui, quia proceditur ad materiam, ut supra ostensum est; eadem ratione etiam infinitum invenitur in potentia in additione multitudinis.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28525)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod unumquodque quod est in potentia, reducitur in actum secundum modum sui esse, dies enim non reducitur in actum ut sit tota simul, sed successive. Et similiter infinitum multitudinis non reducitur in actum ut sit totum simul, sed successive, quia post quamlibet multitudinem, potest sumi alia multitudo in infinitum.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28526)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod species figurarum habent infinitatem ex infinitate numeri, sunt enim species figurarum, trilaterum, quadrilaterum, et sic inde. Unde, sicut multitudo infinita numerabilis non reducitur in actum quod sit tota simul, ita nec multitudo figurarum.\n\n[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28527)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet, quibusdam positis, alia poni non sit eis oppositum; tamen infinita poni opponitur cuilibet speciei multitudinis. Unde non est possibile esse aliquam multitudinem actu infinitam.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0002","text":"[I q. 7 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003","text":"Post considerationem divinae perfectionis, considerandum est de eius infinitate, et de existentia eius in rebus, attribuitur enim Deo quod sit ubique et in omnibus rebus, inquantum est incircumscriptibilis et infinitus. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Deus sit infinitus. Secundo, utrum aliquid praeter ipsum sit infinitum secundum essentiam. Tertio, utrum aliquid possit esse infinitum secundum magnitudinem. Quarto, utrum possit esse infinitum in rebus secundum multitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0005","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit infinitus. Omne enim infinitum est imperfectum, quia habet rationem partis et materiae, ut dicitur in III Physic. Sed Deus est perfectissimus. Ergo non est infinitus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0007","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0008","text":"Praeterea, secundum philosophum in I Physic., finitum et infinitum conveniunt quantitati. Sed in Deo non est quantitas, cum non sit corpus, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo non competit sibi esse infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0009","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quod ita est hic quod non alibi, est finitum secundum locum, ergo quod ita est hoc quod non est aliud, est finitum secundum substantiam. Sed Deus est hoc, et non est aliud, non enim est lapis nec lignum. Ergo Deus non est infinitus secundum substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0011","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Damascenus, quod *Deus est infinitus et aeternus et incircumscriptibilis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0013","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod omnes antiqui philosophi attribuunt infinitum primo principio, ut dicitur in III Physic., et hoc rationabiliter, considerantes res effluere a primo principio in infinitum. Sed quia quidam erraverunt circa naturam primi principii, consequens fuit ut errarent circa infinitatem ipsius. Quia enim ponebant primum principium materiam, consequenter attribuerunt primo principio infinitatem materialem; dicentes aliquod corpus infinitum esse primum principium rerum. Considerandum est igitur quod infinitum dicitur aliquid ex eo quod non est finitum. Finitur autem quodammodo et materia per formam, et forma per materiam. Materia quidem per formam, inquantum materia, antequam recipiat formam, est in potentia ad multas formas, sed cum recipit unam, terminatur per illam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","text":"Forma vero finitur per materiam, inquantum forma, in se considerata, communis est ad multa, sed per hoc quod recipitur in materia, fit forma determinate huius rei. Materia autem perficitur per formam per quam finitur, et ideo infinitum secundum quod attribuitur materiae, habet rationem imperfecti; est enim quasi materia non habens formam. Forma autem non perficitur per materiam, sed magis per eam eius amplitudo contrahitur, unde infinitum secundum quod se tenet ex parte formae non determinatae per materiam, habet rationem perfecti. Illud autem quod est maxime formale omnium, est ipsum esse, ut ex superioribus patet. Cum igitur esse divinum non sit esse receptum in aliquo, sed ipse sit suum esse subsistens, ut supra ostensum est; manifestum est quod ipse Deus sit infinitus et perfectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0016","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0017","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0018","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod terminus quantitatis est sicut forma ipsius, cuius signum est, quod figura, quae consistit in terminatione quantitatis, est quaedam forma circa quantitatem. Unde infinitum quod competit quantitati, est infinitum quod se tenet ex parte materiae, et tale infinitum non attribuitur Deo, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0020","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, ex hoc ipso quod esse Dei est per se subsistens non receptum in aliquo, prout dicitur infinitum, distinguitur ab omnibus aliis, et alia removentur ab eo, sicut, si esset albedo subsistens, ex hoc ipso quod non esset in alio, differret ab omni albedine existente in subiecto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0023","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquid aliud quam Deus possit esse infinitum per essentiam. Virtus enim rei proportionatur essentiae eius. Si igitur essentia Dei est infinita, oportet quod eius virtus sit infinita. Ergo potest producere effectum infinitum, cum quantitas virtutis per effectum cognoscatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0025","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0026","text":"Praeterea, quidquid habet virtutem infinitam, habet essentiam infinitam. Sed intellectus creatus habet virtutem infinitam, apprehendit enim universale, quod se potest extendere ad infinita singularia. Ergo omnis substantia intellectualis creata est infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0027","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0028","text":"Praeterea, materia prima aliud est a Deo, ut supra ostensum est. Sed materia prima est infinita. Ergo aliquid aliud praeter Deum potest esse infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0029","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod infinitum non potest esse ex principio aliquo, ut dicitur in III Physic. Omne autem quod est praeter Deum, est ex Deo sicut ex primo principio. Ergo nihil quod est praeter Deum, potest esse infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0031","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliquid praeter Deum potest esse infinitum secundum quid, sed non simpliciter. Si enim loquamur de infinito secundum quod competit materiae, manifestum est quod omne existens in actu, habet aliquam formam, et sic materia eius est terminata per formam. Sed quia materia, secundum quod est sub una forma substantiali, remanet in potentia ad multas formas accidentales; quod est finitum simpliciter, potest esse infinitum secundum quid, utpote lignum est finitum secundum suam formam, sed tamen est infinitum secundum quid, inquantum est in potentia ad figuras infinitas. Si autem loquamur de infinito secundum quod convenit formae, sic manifestum est quod illa quorum formae sunt in materia, sunt simpliciter finita, et nullo modo infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","text":"Si autem sint aliquae formae creatae non receptae in materia, sed per se subsistentes, ut quidam de Angelis opinantur, erunt quidem infinitae secundum quid, inquantum huiusmodi formae non terminantur neque contrahuntur per aliquam materiam, sed quia forma creata sic subsistens habet esse, et non est suum esse, necesse est quod ipsum eius esse sit receptum et contractum ad determinatam naturam. Unde non potest esse infinitum simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0034","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc est contra rationem facti, quod essentia rei sit ipsum esse eius, quia esse subsistens non est esse creatum, unde contra rationem facti est, quod sit simpliciter infinitum. Sicut ergo Deus, licet habeat potentiam infinitam, non tamen potest facere aliquid non factum (hoc enim esset contradictoria esse simul); ita non potest facere aliquid infinitum simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0036","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod hoc ipsum quod virtus intellectus extendit se quodammodo ad infinita, procedit ex hoc quod intellectus est forma non in materia; sed vel totaliter separata, sicut sunt substantiae Angelorum; vel ad minus potentia intellectiva, quae non est actus alicuius organi, in anima intellectiva corpori coniuncta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0038","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod materia prima non existit in rerum natura per seipsam, cum non sit ens in actu, sed potentia tantum, unde magis est aliquid concreatum, quam creatum. Nihilominus tamen materia prima, etiam secundum potentiam, non est infinita simpliciter, sed secundum quid, quia eius potentia non se extendit nisi ad formas naturales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0041","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod possit esse aliquid infinitum actu secundum magnitudinem. In scientiis enim mathematicis non invenitur falsum, *quia abstrahentium non est mendacium*, ut dicitur in II Physic. Sed scientiae mathematicae utuntur infinito secundum magnitudinem, dicit enim geometra in suis demonstrationibus, sit linea talis infinita. Ergo non est impossibile aliquid esse infinitum secundum magnitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0043","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0044","text":"Praeterea, id quod non est contra rationem alicuius, non est impossibile convenire sibi. Sed esse infinitum non est contra rationem magnitudinis, sed magis finitum et infinitum videntur esse passiones quantitatis. Ergo non est impossibile aliquam magnitudinem esse infinitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0045","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0046","text":"Praeterea, magnitudo divisibilis est in infinitum, sic enim definitur continuum, quod est in infinitum divisibile, ut patet in III Physic. Sed contraria nata sunt fieri circa idem. Cum ergo divisioni opponatur additio, et diminutioni augmentum, videtur quod magnitudo possit crescere in infinitum. Ergo possibile est esse magnitudinem infinitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0047","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0048","text":"Praeterea, motus et tempus habent quantitatem et continuitatem a magnitudine super quam transit motus, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Sed non est contra rationem temporis et motus quod sint infinita, cum unumquodque indivisibile signatum in tempore et motu circulari, sit principium et finis. Ergo nec contra rationem magnitudinis erit quod sit infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0049","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0050","text":"Sed contra, omne corpus superficiem habet. Sed omne corpus superficiem habens est finitum, quia superficies est terminus corporis finiti. Ergo omne corpus est finitum. Et similiter potest dici de superficie et linea. Nihil est ergo infinitum secundum magnitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0051","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0052","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliud est esse infinitum secundum suam essentiam, et secundum magnitudinem. Dato enim quod esset aliquod corpus infinitum secundum magnitudinem, utpote ignis vel aer, non tamen esset infinitum secundum essentiam, quia essentia sua esset terminata ad aliquam speciem per formam, et ad aliquod individuum per materiam. Et ideo, habito ex praemissis quod nulla creatura est infinita secundum essentiam, adhuc restat inquirere utrum aliquid creatum sit infinitum secundum magnitudinem. Sciendum est igitur quod corpus, quod est magnitudo completa, dupliciter sumitur, scilicet mathematice, secundum quod consideratur in eo sola quantitas; et naturaliter, secundum quod consideratur in eo materia et forma. Et de corpore quidem naturali, quod non possit esse infinitum in actu, manifestum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0053","text":"Nam omne corpus naturale aliquam formam substantialem habet determinatam, cum igitur ad formam substantialem consequantur accidentia, necesse est quod ad determinatam formam consequantur determinata accidentia; inter quae est quantitas. Unde omne corpus naturale habet determinatam quantitatem et in maius et in minus. Unde impossibile est aliquod corpus naturale infinitum esse. Hoc etiam ex motu patet. Quia omne corpus naturale habet aliquem motum naturalem. Corpus autem infinitum non posset habere aliquem motum naturalem, nec rectum, quia nihil movetur naturaliter motu recto, nisi cum est extra suum locum, quod corpori infinito accidere non posset; occuparet enim omnia loca, et sic indifferenter quilibet locus esset locus eius. Et similiter etiam neque secundum motum circularem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","text":"Quia in motu circulari oportet quod una pars corporis transferatur ad locum in quo fuit alia pars; quod in corpore circulari, si ponatur infinitum, esse non posset, quia duae lineae protractae a centro, quanto longius protrahuntur a centro, tanto longius distant ab invicem; si ergo corpus esset infinitum, in infinitum lineae distarent ab invicem, et sic una nunquam posset pervenire ad locum alterius. De corpore etiam mathematico eadem ratio est. Quia si imaginemur corpus mathematicum existens actu, oportet quod imaginemur ipsum sub aliqua forma, quia nihil est actu nisi per suam formam. Unde, cum forma quanti, inquantum huiusmodi, sit figura, oportebit quod habeat aliquam figuram. Et sic erit finitum, est enim figura, quae termino vel terminis comprehenditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0055","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod geometer non indiget sumere aliquam lineam esse infinitam actu, sed indiget accipere aliquam lineam finitam actu, a qua possit subtrahi quantum necesse est, et hanc nominat lineam infinitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0057","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0058","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet infinitum non sit contra rationem magnitudinis in communi, est tamen contra rationem cuiuslibet speciei eius, scilicet contra rationem magnitudinis bicubitae vel tricubitae, sive circularis vel triangularis, et similium. Non autem est possibile in genere esse quod in nulla specie est. Unde non est possibile esse aliquam magnitudinem infinitam, cum nulla species magnitudinis sit infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0059","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod infinitum quod convenit quantitati, ut dictum est, se tenet ex parte materiae. Per divisionem autem totius acceditur ad materiam, nam partes se habent in ratione materiae, per additionem autem acceditur ad totum, quod se habet in ratione formae. Et ideo non invenitur infinitum in additione magnitudinis, sed in divisione tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0061","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod motus et tempus non sunt secundum totum in actu, sed successive, unde habent potentiam permixtam actui. Sed magnitudo est tota in actu. Et ideo infinitum quod convenit quantitati, et se tenet ex parte materiae, repugnat totalitati magnitudinis, non autem totalitati temporis vel motus, esse enim in potentia convenit materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0063","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0064","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0065","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod possibile sit esse multitudinem infinitam secundum actum. Non enim est impossibile id quod est in potentia reduci ad actum. Sed numerus est in infinitum multiplicabilis. Ergo non est impossibile esse multitudinem infinitam in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0066","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0067","text":"Praeterea, cuiuslibet speciei possibile est esse aliquod individuum in actu. Sed species figurae sunt infinitae. Ergo possibile est esse infinitas figuras in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0068","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0069","text":"Praeterea, ea quae non opponuntur ad invicem, non impediunt se invicem. Sed, posita aliqua multitudine rerum, adhuc possunt fieri alia multa quae eis non opponuntur, ergo non est impossibile aliqua iterum simul esse cum eis, et sic in infinitum. Ergo possibile est esse infinita in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0070","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0071","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XI, *omnia in pondere, numero et mensura disposuisti*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0072","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0073","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc fuit duplex opinio. Quidam enim, sicut Avicenna et Algazel, dixerunt quod impossibile est esse multitudinem actu infinitam per se, sed infinitam per accidens multitudinem esse, non est impossibile. Dicitur enim multitudo esse infinita per se, quando requiritur ad aliquid ut multitudo infinita sit. Et hoc est impossibile esse, quia sic oporteret quod aliquid dependeret ex infinitis; unde eius generatio nunquam compleretur, cum non sit infinita pertransire. Per accidens autem dicitur multitudo infinita, quando non requiritur ad aliquid infinitas multitudinis, sed accidit ita esse. Et hoc sic manifestari potest in operatione fabri, ad quam quaedam multitudo requiritur per se, scilicet quod sit ars in anima, et manus movens, et martellus. Et si haec in infinitum multiplicarentur, nunquam opus fabrile compleretur, quia dependeret ex infinitis causis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","text":"Sed multitudo martellorum quae accidit ex hoc quod unum frangitur et accipitur aliud, est multitudo per accidens, accidit enim quod multis martellis operetur; et nihil differt utrum uno vel duobus vel pluribus operetur, vel infinitis, si infinito tempore operaretur. Per hunc igitur modum, posuerunt quod possibile est esse actu multitudinem infinitam per accidens. Sed hoc est impossibile. Quia omnem multitudinem oportet esse in aliqua specie multitudinis. Species autem multitudinis sunt secundum species numerorum. Nulla autem species numeri est infinita, quia quilibet numerus est multitudo mensurata per unum. Unde impossibile est esse multitudinem infinitam actu, sive per se, sive per accidens. Item, multitudo in rerum natura existens est creata, et omne creatum sub aliqua certa intentione creantis comprehenditur, non enim in vanum agens aliquod operatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","text":"Unde necesse est quod sub certo numero omnia creata comprehendantur. Impossibile est ergo esse multitudinem infinitam in actu, etiam per accidens. Sed esse multitudinem infinitam in potentia, possibile est. Quia augmentum multitudinis consequitur divisionem magnitudinis, quanto enim aliquid plus dividitur, tanto plura secundum numerum resultant. Unde, sicut infinitum invenitur in potentia in divisione continui, quia proceditur ad materiam, ut supra ostensum est; eadem ratione etiam infinitum invenitur in potentia in additione multitudinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0076","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod unumquodque quod est in potentia, reducitur in actum secundum modum sui esse, dies enim non reducitur in actum ut sit tota simul, sed successive. Et similiter infinitum multitudinis non reducitur in actum ut sit totum simul, sed successive, quia post quamlibet multitudinem, potest sumi alia multitudo in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0078","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0079","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod species figurarum habent infinitatem ex infinitate numeri, sunt enim species figurarum, trilaterum, quadrilaterum, et sic inde. Unde, sicut multitudo infinita numerabilis non reducitur in actum quod sit tota simul, ita nec multitudo figurarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0080","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0081","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet, quibusdam positis, alia poni non sit eis oppositum; tamen infinita poni opponitur cuilibet speciei multitudinis. Unde non est possibile esse aliquam multitudinem actu infinitam.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"961d463ae5c2e4eee604b246f3401c7cf6eea4d4b434946c28d0201aeb8d27ff","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T133944024488Z_721a77ca","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 7","source_sha256":"961d463ae5c2e4eee604b246f3401c7cf6eea4d4b434946c28d0201aeb8d27ff","sha256":"f881fa42d375fea8665616fc7d9986a8934a0aeca682dd818c9460201e2c7755","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:39:44.024501+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0002","text":"[I q. 7 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003","text":"After the consideration of the divine perfection, there remains to be considered His infinity, and His existence in things; for it is attributed to God that He is everywhere and in all things, insofar as He is uncircumscribable and infinite. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether God is infinite. Second, whether anything besides Him is infinite according to essence. Third, whether anything can be infinite according to magnitude. Fourth, whether there can be an infinite in things according to multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0005","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0006","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that God is not infinite. For everything infinite is imperfect, because it has the character of part and matter, as is said in Physics III. But God is most perfect. Therefore He is not infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0007","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0008","text":"Further, according to the Philosopher in Physics I, finite and infinite belong to quantity. But there is no quantity in God, since He is not a body, as was shown above. Therefore to be infinite does not befit Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0009","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0010","text":"Further, that which is here in such a way that it is not elsewhere is finite according to place; therefore that which is this in such a way that it is not another is finite according to substance. But God is this, and is not another; for He is neither a stone nor wood. Therefore God is not infinite according to substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0011","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, Damascene says that *God is infinite and eternal and uncircumscribable*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0013","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that all the ancient philosophers attribute infinity to the first principle, as is said in Physics III, and this reasonably, considering that things flow forth from the first principle unto infinity. But because some erred concerning the nature of the first principle, it followed that they erred concerning its infinity. For, since they posited matter as the first principle, they consequently attributed material infinity to the first principle, saying that some infinite body is the first principle of things. Therefore it must be considered that something is called infinite from the fact that it is not finite. Now matter is in some way made finite by form, and form by matter. Matter indeed by form, insofar as matter, before it receives form, is in potency to many forms, but when it receives one, it is terminated by that form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","text":"But form is made finite by matter, insofar as form, considered in itself, is common to many, but by being received in matter it becomes the form determinately of this thing. Now matter is perfected by form, by which it is made finite; and therefore the infinite, according as it is attributed to matter, has the character of the imperfect, for it is as it were matter not having form. But form is not perfected by matter; rather, its amplitude is contracted by it. Hence the infinite, according as it is on the side of form not determined by matter, has the character of the perfect. But that which is most formal of all is being itself, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore, since the divine being is not being received in something, but He Himself is His own subsisting being, as was shown above, it is manifest that God Himself is infinite and perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0016","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0017","text":"And through this the reply to the first is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0018","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that the limit of quantity is as its form; a sign of this is that figure, which consists in the termination of quantity, is a certain form concerning quantity. Hence the infinite which belongs to quantity is an infinite which is on the side of matter, and such an infinite is not attributed to God, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0020","text":"[I q. 7 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that, from the very fact that the being of God is per se subsisting, not received in anything, insofar as it is called infinite, He is distinguished from all other things, and other things are removed from Him; just as, if there were subsisting whiteness, from the very fact that it was not in another, it would differ from every whiteness existing in a subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0023","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0024","text":"The second is approached thus. It seems that something other than God can be infinite through essence. For the power of a thing is proportioned to its essence. Therefore, if the essence of God is infinite, His power must be infinite. Therefore He can produce an infinite effect, since the quantity of a power is known through its effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0025","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0026","text":"Moreover, whatever has infinite power has infinite essence. But the created intellect has infinite power, for it apprehends the universal, which can extend itself to infinitely many singulars. Therefore every created intellectual substance is infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0027","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0028","text":"Moreover, prime matter is other than God, as was shown above. But prime matter is infinite. Therefore something other than God can be infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0029","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0030","text":"On the contrary, the infinite cannot be from any principle, as is said in Physics III. But everything that is other than God is from God as from a first principle. Therefore nothing that is other than God can be infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0031","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","text":"I answer that something other than God can be infinite in a certain respect, but not simply. For if we speak of the infinite insofar as it belongs to matter, it is clear that everything existing in act has some form, and thus its matter is terminated by form. But because matter, insofar as it is under one substantial form, remains in potency toward many accidental forms, that which is finite simply can be infinite in a certain respect; for example, wood is finite according to its form, but is nevertheless infinite in a certain respect, insofar as it is in potency toward infinite shapes. But if we speak of the infinite insofar as it belongs to form, then it is clear that those things whose forms are in matter are simply finite, and in no way infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","text":"But if there are any created forms not received in matter, but subsisting through themselves, as some hold concerning the Angels, they will indeed be infinite in a certain respect, insofar as forms of this kind are not terminated or contracted by any matter; but because a created form thus subsisting has being, and is not its own being, it is necessary that its very being be received and contracted to a determinate nature. Hence it cannot be infinite simply.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0034","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that it is contrary to the notion of a thing made for the essence of a thing to be its very being, because subsistent being is not created being; hence it is contrary to the notion of a thing made that it be infinite simply. Therefore, just as God, although He has infinite power, nevertheless cannot make something not made (for this would be for contradictories to be at the same time), so He cannot make something infinite simply.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0036","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0037","text":"To the second it must be said that the very fact that the power of the intellect extends itself in some way to infinite things proceeds from this, that the intellect is a form not in matter; but either wholly separate, as are the substances of the Angels, or at least the intellective power, which is not the act of any organ, in the intellective soul conjoined to a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0038","text":"[I q. 7 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0039","text":"To the third it must be said that prime matter does not exist in the nature of things through itself, since it is not a being in act but only in potency; hence it is more something concreated than created. Nevertheless, prime matter, even according to potency, is not infinite simply, but in a certain respect, because its potency extends only to natural forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0041","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0042","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that there can be something actually infinite in magnitude. For no falsehood is found in the mathematical sciences, because “there is no falsehood in abstractions,” as is said in Physics II. But the mathematical sciences make use of the infinite in magnitude, for the geometer says in his demonstrations, let such a line be infinite. Therefore it is not impossible for something to be infinite in magnitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0043","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0044","text":"Moreover, that which is not contrary to the notion of something is not impossible for it to belong to it. But to be infinite is not contrary to the notion of magnitude; rather, the finite and the infinite seem to be properties of quantity. Therefore it is not impossible for some magnitude to be infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0045","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0046","text":"Moreover, magnitude is divisible to infinity; for the continuous is thus defined, namely, as what is divisible to infinity, as is clear in Physics III. But contraries are naturally apt to occur concerning the same thing. Therefore, since addition is opposed to division, and increase to diminution, it seems that magnitude can increase to infinity. Therefore it is possible for there to be an infinite magnitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0047","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0048","text":"Moreover, motion and time have quantity and continuity from the magnitude over which motion passes, as is said in Physics IV. But it is not contrary to the notion of time and motion that they be infinite, since every designated indivisible in circular time and motion is a beginning and an end. Therefore neither will it be contrary to the notion of magnitude that it be infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0049","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0050","text":"On the contrary, every body has a surface. But every body having a surface is finite, because a surface is the boundary of a finite body. Therefore every body is finite. And the same can likewise be said of surface and line. Therefore nothing is infinite in magnitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0051","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0052","text":"I answer that, it must be said that it is one thing to be infinite according to its essence, and another according to magnitude. For granted that there were some body infinite in magnitude, such as fire or air, it would nevertheless not be infinite in essence, because its essence would be limited to some species through form, and to some individual through matter. And therefore, having established from the foregoing that no creature is infinite according to essence, it still remains to inquire whether anything created is infinite according to magnitude. Therefore it should be known that body, which is complete magnitude, is taken in two ways: namely, mathematically, according as quantity alone is considered in it; and naturally, according as matter and form are considered in it. And concerning natural body, that it cannot be actually infinite is manifest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0053","text":"For every natural body has some determinate substantial form; therefore, since accidents follow upon substantial form, it is necessary that determinate accidents follow upon a determinate form, among which is quantity. Hence every natural body has a determinate quantity, both as to greater and as to less. Hence it is impossible for any natural body to be infinite. This is also clear from motion. For every natural body has some natural motion. But an infinite body could not have any natural motion, not rectilinear motion, because nothing is naturally moved with rectilinear motion except when it is outside its place, which could not happen to an infinite body; for it would occupy all places, and thus every place would indifferently be its place. And likewise neither could it have circular motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","text":"For in circular motion it is necessary that one part of the body be transferred to the place in which another part was; which could not be in a circular body, if it were posited as infinite, because two lines drawn from the center, the farther they are extended from the center, the farther they are distant from each other. Therefore, if the body were infinite, the lines would be infinitely distant from one another, and thus one could never arrive at the place of the other. The same reasoning also applies to mathematical body. For if we imagine a mathematical body actually existing, we must imagine it under some form, because nothing is actual except through its form. Hence, since the form of a quantified thing, as such, is figure, it will have to have some figure. And thus it will be finite; for figure is that which is comprehended by a boundary or boundaries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0055","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the geometer does not need to suppose some line to be actually infinite, but needs to take some line actually finite, from which as much as is necessary may be subtracted, and he calls this an infinite line.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0057","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0058","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although the infinite is not contrary to the notion of magnitude in general, it is nevertheless contrary to the notion of each of its species, namely, contrary to the notion of a magnitude of two cubits or three cubits, or of a circular or triangular magnitude, and the like. But it is not possible for there to be in a genus what is in no species. Hence it is not possible for any infinite magnitude to exist, since no species of magnitude is infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0059","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060","text":"To the third, it must be said that infinity belonging to quantity, as has been said, is on the side of matter. But by division of the whole one approaches matter, for parts stand in the character of matter; whereas by addition one approaches the whole, which stands in the character of form. And therefore infinity is not found in the addition of magnitude, but only in division.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0061","text":"[I q. 7 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that motion and time are not actual as a whole, but successively; hence they have potency mingled with act. But magnitude is whole in act. And therefore infinity, which belongs to quantity and is on the side of matter, is incompatible with the totality of magnitude, but not with the totality of time or motion; for being in potency belongs to matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0063","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0064","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0065","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that it is possible for an actually infinite multitude to exist. For it is not impossible that what is in potency be reduced to act. But number is capable of being multiplied to infinity. Therefore it is not impossible for an infinite multitude to exist in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0066","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0067","text":"Moreover, for every species it is possible that some individual exist in act. But the species of figure are infinite. Therefore it is possible for infinitely many figures to exist in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0068","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0069","text":"Moreover, things that are not opposed to one another do not impede one another. But, once some multitude of things has been posited, still many other things that are not opposed to them can come to be; therefore it is not impossible for some other things to exist simultaneously with them, and thus to infinity. Therefore it is possible for infinitely many things to exist in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0070","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0071","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Wisdom 11: *You have ordered all things in weight, number, and measure*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0072","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0073","text":"I answer that, on this matter there was a twofold opinion. For some, such as Avicenna and Algazel, said that it is impossible for there to be a multitude actually infinite per se, but that an infinite multitude per accidens is not impossible. For a multitude is said to be infinite per se when it is required for something that the multitude be infinite. And this is impossible, because in that case something would have to depend upon infinitely many things; hence its generation would never be completed, since it is not possible to traverse the infinite. But a multitude is said to be infinite per accidens when the infinity of the multitude is not required for something, but happens to be so. And this can be made manifest in the operation of a craftsman, for which a certain multitude is required per se, namely, that there be art in the soul, and a moving hand, and a hammer. And if these were multiplied to infinity, the work of the craftsman would never be completed, because it would depend upon infinite causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","text":"But the multitude of hammers that occurs from the fact that one is broken and another is taken is a multitude per accidens; for it happens that he works with many hammers. And it makes no difference whether he works with one, or two, or several, or infinitely many, if he were to work for an infinite time. Therefore, in this way they held that it is possible for there to be an actually infinite multitude per accidens. But this is impossible. For every multitude must be in some species of multitude. But the species of multitude are according to the species of numbers. Now no species of number is infinite, because every number is a multitude measured by one. Hence it is impossible for there to be an actually infinite multitude, whether per se or per accidens. Likewise, the multitude existing in the nature of things is created, and every created thing is comprehended under some definite intention of the creator; for no agent acts in vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","text":"Hence it is necessary that all created things be comprehended under a definite number. Therefore it is impossible for there to be an actually infinite multitude, even per accidens. But for there to be an infinite multitude in potency is possible. For the increase of multitude follows the division of magnitude; for the more something is divided, the more things result according to number. Hence, just as the infinite is found in potency in the division of the continuum, because one proceeds to matter, as was shown above, so for the same reason the infinite is also found in potency in the addition of multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0076","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that everything which is in potency is reduced to act according to the mode of its being; for a day is not reduced to act so as to be whole all at once, but successively. And similarly, the infinite of multitude is not reduced to act so as to be whole all at once, but successively, because after any multitude another multitude can be taken to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0078","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0079","text":"To the second, it must be said that the species of figures have infinity from the infinity of number; for there are species of figures, the three-sided, the four-sided, and so on. Hence, just as an infinite numerable multitude is not reduced to act so as to be whole all at once, so neither is the multitude of figures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0080","text":"[I q. 7 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0081","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although, upon positing certain things, the positing of others is not opposed to them, nevertheless the positing of infinite things is opposed to every species of multitude. Hence it is not possible for there to be any actually infinite multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0081"]}],"source_digest":"1085bc0e3796243b8efe7e4d89097bfbc4a77bfde37d4b987dbfb488e5a65d95","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 7"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 7","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8","title":"First Part · Question 8","order":9,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 8 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28528)\n\nQuia vero infinito convenire videtur quod ubique et in omnibus sit, considerandum est utrum hoc Deo conveniat. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Deus sit in omnibus rebus. Secundo, utrum Deus sit ubique. Tertio, utrum Deus sit ubique per essentiam et potentiam et praesentiam. Quarto, utrum esse ubique sit proprium Dei.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28529)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit in omnibus rebus. Quod enim est supra omnia, non est in omnibus rebus. Sed Deus est supra omnia, secundum illud Psalmi, *excelsus super omnes gentes dominus,* et cetera. Ergo Deus non est in omnibus rebus.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28530)\n\nPraeterea, quod est in aliquo, continetur ab eo. Sed Deus non continetur a rebus, sed magis continet res. Ergo Deus non est in rebus, sed magis res sunt in eo. Unde Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., dicit quod *in ipso potius sunt omnia, quam ipse alicubi*.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28531)\n\nPraeterea, quanto aliquod agens est virtuosius, tanto ad magis distans eius actio procedit. Sed Deus est virtuosissimum agens. Ergo eius actio pertingere potest ad ea etiam quae ab ipso distant, nec oportet quod sit in omnibus.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28532)\n\nPraeterea, Daemones res aliquae sunt. Nec tamen Deus est in Daemonibus, non enim est conventio lucis ad tenebras, ut dicitur II ad Cor. VI. Ergo Deus non est in omnibus rebus.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28533)\n\nSed contra, ubicumque operatur aliquid, ibi est. Sed Deus operatur in omnibus, secundum illud Isaiae XXVI, *omnia opera nostra operatus es in nobis, domine*. Ergo Deus est in omnibus rebus.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28534)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus est in omnibus rebus, non quidem sicut pars essentiae, vel sicut accidens, sed sicut agens adest ei in quod agit. Oportet enim omne agens coniungi ei in quod immediate agit, et sua virtute illud contingere, unde in VII Physic. probatur quod motum et movens oportet esse simul. Cum autem Deus sit ipsum esse per suam essentiam, oportet quod esse creatum sit proprius effectus eius; sicut ignire est proprius effectus ignis. Hunc autem effectum causat Deus in rebus, non solum quando primo esse incipiunt, sed quandiu in esse conservantur; sicut lumen causatur in aere a sole quandiu aer illuminatus manet. Quandiu igitur res habet esse, tandiu oportet quod Deus adsit ei, secundum modum quo esse habet. Esse autem est illud quod est magis intimum cuilibet, et quod profundius omnibus inest, cum sit formale respectu omnium quae in re sunt, ut ex supra dictis patet. Unde oportet quod Deus sit in omnibus rebus, et intime.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28535)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus est supra omnia per excellentiam suae naturae, et tamen est in omnibus rebus, ut causans omnium esse, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28536)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet corporalia dicantur esse in aliquo sicut in continente, tamen spiritualia continent ea in quibus sunt, sicut anima continet corpus. Unde et Deus est in rebus sicut continens res. Tamen, per quandam similitudinem corporalium, dicuntur omnia esse in Deo, inquantum continentur ab ipso.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28537)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nullius agentis, quantumcumque virtuosi, actio procedit ad aliquid distans, nisi inquantum in illud per media agit. Hoc autem ad maximam virtutem Dei pertinet, quod immediate in omnibus agit. Unde nihil est distans ab eo, quasi in se illud Deum non habeat. Dicuntur tamen res distare a Deo per dissimilitudinem naturae vel gratiae, sicut et ipse est super omnia per excellentiam suae naturae.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28538)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in Daemonibus intelligitur et natura, quae est a Deo, et deformitas culpae, quae non est ab ipso. Et ideo non est absolute concedendum quod Deus sit in Daemonibus, sed cum hac additione, inquantum sunt res quaedam. In rebus autem quae nominant naturam non deformatam, absolute dicendum est Deum esse.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28539)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit ubique. Esse enim ubique significat esse in omni loco. Sed esse in omni loco non convenit Deo, cui non convenit esse in loco, nam incorporalia, ut dicit Boetius, in libro de Hebdomad., non sunt in loco. Ergo Deus non est ubique.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28540)\n\nPraeterea, sicut se habet tempus ad successiva, ita se habet locus ad permanentia. Sed unum indivisibile actionis vel motus, non potest esse in diversis temporibus. Ergo nec unum indivisibile in genere rerum permanentium, potest esse in omnibus locis. Esse autem divinum non est successivum, sed permanens. Ergo Deus non est in pluribus locis. Et ita non est ubique.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28541)\n\nPraeterea, quod est totum alicubi, nihil eius est extra locum illum. Sed Deus, si est in aliquo loco, totus est ibi, non enim habet partes. Ergo nihil eius est extra locum illum. Ergo Deus non est ubique.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28542)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ierem. XXIII, *caelum et terram ego impleo*.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28543)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum locus sit res quaedam, esse aliquid in loco potest intelligi dupliciter, vel per modum aliarum rerum, idest sicut dicitur aliquid esse in aliis rebus quocumque modo, sicut accidentia loci sunt in loco; vel per modum proprium loci, sicut locata sunt in loco. Utroque autem modo, secundum aliquid, Deus est in omni loco, quod est esse ubique. Primo quidem, sicut est in omnibus rebus, ut dans eis esse et virtutem et operationem, sic enim est in omni loco, ut dans ei esse et virtutem locativam. Item, locata sunt in loco inquantum replent locum, et Deus omnem locum replet. Non sicut corpus, corpus enim dicitur replere locum, inquantum non compatitur secum aliud corpus; sed per hoc quod Deus est in aliquo loco, non excluditur quin alia sint ibi, imo per hoc replet omnia loca, quod dat esse omnibus locatis, quae replent omnia loca.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28544)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod incorporalia non sunt in loco per contactum quantitatis dimensivae, sicut corpora, sed per contactum virtutis.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28545)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod indivisibile est duplex. Unum quod est terminus continui, ut punctus in permanentibus, et momentum in successivis. Et huiusmodi indivisibile, in permanentibus, quia habet determinatum situm, non potest esse in pluribus partibus loci, vel in pluribus locis, et similiter indivisibile actionis vel motus, quia habet determinatum ordinem in motu vel actione, non potest esse in pluribus partibus temporis. Aliud autem indivisibile est, quod est extra totum genus continui, et hoc modo substantiae incorporeae, ut Deus, Angelus et anima, dicuntur esse indivisibiles. Tale igitur indivisibile non applicatur ad continuum sicut aliquid eius, sed inquantum contingit illud sua virtute. Unde secundum quod virtus sua se potest extendere ad unum vel multa, ad parvum vel magnum, secundum hoc est in uno vel pluribus locis, et in loco parvo vel magno.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28546)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod totum dicitur respectu partium. Est autem duplex pars, scilicet pars essentiae, ut forma et materia dicuntur partes compositi, et genus et differentia partes speciei; et etiam pars quantitatis, in quam scilicet dividitur aliqua quantitas. Quod ergo est totum in aliquo loco totalitate quantitatis, non potest esse extra locum illum, quia quantitas locati commensuratur quantitati loci, unde non est totalitas quantitatis, si non sit totalitas loci. Sed totalitas essentiae non commensuratur totalitati loci. Unde non oportet quod illud quod est totum totalitate essentiae in aliquo, nullo modo sit extra illud. Sicut apparet etiam in formis accidentalibus, quae secundum accidens quantitatem habent, albedo enim est tota in qualibet parte superficiei, si accipiatur totalitas essentiae, quia secundum perfectam rationem suae speciei invenitur in qualibet parte superficiei, si autem accipiatur totalitas secundum quantitatem, quam habet per accidens, sic non est tota in qualibet parte superficiei. In substantiis autem incorporeis non est totalitas, nec per se nec per accidens, nisi secundum perfectam rationem essentiae. Et ideo, sicut anima est tota in qualibet parte corporis, ita Deus totus est in omnibus et singulis.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28547)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod male assignentur modi existendi Deum in rebus, cum dicitur quod Deus est in omnibus rebus per essentiam, potentiam et praesentiam. Id enim per essentiam est in aliquo, quod essentialiter est in eo. Deus autem non est essentialiter in rebus, non enim est de essentia alicuius rei. Ergo non debet dici quod Deus sit in rebus per essentiam, praesentiam et potentiam.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28548)\n\nPraeterea, hoc est esse praesentem alicui rei, scilicet non deesse illi. Sed hoc est Deum esse per essentiam in rebus, scilicet non deesse alicui rei. Ergo idem est esse Deum in omnibus per essentiam et praesentiam. Superfluum ergo fuit dicere quod Deus sit in rebus per essentiam, praesentiam et potentiam.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28549)\n\nPraeterea, sicut Deus est principium omnium rerum per suam potentiam, ita per scientiam et voluntatem. Sed non dicitur Deus esse in rebus per scientiam et voluntatem. Ergo nec per potentiam.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28550)\n\nPraeterea, sicut gratia est quaedam perfectio superaddita substantiae rei, ita multae sunt aliae perfectiones superadditae. Si ergo Deus dicitur esse speciali modo in quibusdam per gratiam, videtur quod secundum quamlibet perfectionem debeat accipi specialis modus essendi Deum in rebus.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28551)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, super Cant. Cantic., quod *Deus communi modo est in omnibus rebus praesentia, potentia et substantia, tamen familiari modo dicitur esse in aliquibus per gratiam*.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28552)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus dicitur esse in re aliqua dupliciter. Uno modo, per modum causae agentis, et sic est in omnibus rebus creatis ab ipso. Alio modo, sicut obiectum operationis est in operante, quod proprium est in operationibus animae, secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente, et desideratum in desiderante. Hoc igitur secundo modo, Deus specialiter est in rationali creatura, quae cognoscit et diligit illum actu vel habitu. Et quia hoc habet rationalis creatura per gratiam, ut infra patebit, dicitur esse hoc modo in sanctis per gratiam. In rebus vero aliis ab ipso creatis quomodo sit, considerandum est ex his quae in rebus humanis esse dicuntur. Rex enim dicitur esse in toto regno suo per suam potentiam, licet non sit ubique praesens. Per praesentiam vero suam, dicitur aliquid esse in omnibus quae in prospectu ipsius sunt; sicut omnia quae sunt in aliqua domo, dicuntur esse praesentia alicui, qui tamen non est secundum substantiam suam in qualibet parte domus. Secundum vero substantiam vel essentiam, dicitur aliquid esse in loco in quo eius substantia habetur. Fuerunt ergo aliqui, scilicet Manichaei, qui dixerunt divinae potestati subiecta spiritualia esse et incorporalia, visibilia vero et corporalia subiecta esse dicebant potestati principii contrarii. Contra hos ergo oportet dicere quod Deus sit in omnibus per potentiam suam. Fuerunt vero alii, qui licet crederent omnia esse subiecta divinae potentiae, tamen providentiam divinam usque ad haec inferiora corpora non extendebant, ex quorum persona dicitur Iob XXII, *circa cardines caeli perambulat, nec nostra considerat*. Et contra hos oportuit dicere quod sit in omnibus per suam praesentiam. Fuerunt vero alii, qui licet dicerent omnia ad Dei providentiam pertinere, tamen posuerunt omnia non immediate esse a Deo creata, sed quod immediate creavit primas creaturas, et illae creaverunt alias. Et contra hos oportet dicere quod sit in omnibus per essentiam. Sic ergo est in omnibus per potentiam, inquantum omnia eius potestati subduntur. Est per praesentiam in omnibus, inquantum omnia nuda sunt et aperta oculis eius. Est in omnibus per essentiam, inquantum adest omnibus ut causa essendi, sicut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28553)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus dicitur esse in omnibus per essentiam, non quidem rerum, quasi sit de essentia earum, sed per essentiam suam, quia substantia sua adest omnibus ut causa essendi, sicut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28554)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliquid potest dici praesens alicui, inquantum subiacet eius conspectui, quod tamen distat ab eo secundum suam substantiam, ut dictum est. Et ideo oportuit duos modos poni, scilicet per essentiam, et praesentiam.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28555)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod de ratione scientiae et voluntatis est, quod scitum sit in sciente, et volitum in volente, unde secundum scientiam et voluntatem, magis res sunt in Deo, quam Deus in rebus. Sed de ratione potentiae est, quod sit principium agendi in aliud, unde secundum potentiam agens comparatur et applicatur rei exteriori. Et sic per potentiam potest dici agens esse in altero.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28556)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod nulla alia perfectio superaddita substantiae, facit Deum esse in aliquo sicut obiectum cognitum et amatum, nisi gratia, et ideo sola gratia facit singularem modum essendi Deum in rebus. Est autem alius singularis modus essendi Deum in homine per unionem, de quo modo suo loco agetur.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28557)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse ubique non sit proprium Dei. Universale enim, secundum philosophum, est ubique et semper, materia etiam prima, cum sit in omnibus corporibus, est ubique. Neutrum autem horum est Deus, ut ex praemissis patet. Ergo esse ubique non est proprium Dei.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28558)\n\nPraeterea, numerus est in numeratis. Sed totum universum est constitutum in numero, ut patet Sap. XI. Ergo aliquis numerus est, qui est in toto universo, et ita ubique.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28559)\n\nPraeterea, totum universum est quoddam totum corpus perfectum, ut dicitur in I caeli et mundi. Sed totum universum est ubique, quia extra ipsum nullus locus est. Non ergo solus Deus est ubique.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28560)\n\nPraeterea, si aliquod corpus esset infinitum, nullus locus esset extra ipsum. Ergo esset ubique. Et sic, esse ubique non videtur proprium Dei.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28561)\n\n*Praeterea, anima*, ut dicit Augustinus, in VI de Trin., *est tota in toto corpore, et tota in qualibet eius parte*. Si ergo non esset in mundo nisi unum solum animal, anima eius esset ubique. Et sic, esse ubique non est proprium Dei.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28562)\n\nPraeterea, ut Augustinus dicit in epistola ad Volusianum, *anima ubi videt, ibi sentit; et ubi sentit, ibi vivit; et ubi vivit, ibi est*. Sed anima videt quasi ubique, quia successive videt etiam totum caelum. Ergo anima est ubique.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28563)\n\nSed contra est quod Ambrosius dicit, in libro de spiritu sancto, *quis audeat creaturam dicere spiritum sanctum, qui in omnibus et ubique et semper est; quod utique divinitatis est proprium?*\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28564)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod esse ubique primo et per se, est proprium Dei. Dico autem esse ubique primo, quod secundum se totum est ubique. Si quid enim esset ubique, secundum diversas partes in diversis locis existens, non esset primo ubique, quia quod convenit alicui ratione partis suae, non convenit ei primo; sicut si homo est albus dente, albedo non convenit primo homini, sed denti. Esse autem ubique per se dico id cui non convenit esse ubique per accidens, propter aliquam suppositionem factam, quia sic granum milii esset ubique, supposito quod nullum aliud corpus esset. Per se igitur convenit esse ubique alicui, quando tale est quod, qualibet positione facta, sequitur illud esse ubique. Et hoc proprie convenit Deo. Quia quotcumque loca ponantur, etiam si ponerentur infinita praeter ista quae sunt, oporteret in omnibus esse Deum, quia nihil potest esse nisi per ipsum. Sic igitur esse ubique primo et per se convenit Deo, et est proprium eius, quia quotcumque loca ponantur, oportet quod in quolibet sit Deus, non secundum partem, sed secundum seipsum.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28565)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod universale et materia prima sunt quidem ubique, sed non secundum idem esse.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28566)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod numerus, cum sit accidens, non est per se sed per accidens, in loco. Nec est totus in quolibet numeratorum, sed secundum partem. Et sic non sequitur quod sit primo et per se ubique.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28567)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod totum corpus universi est ubique, sed non primo, quia non totum est in quolibet loco, sed secundum suas partes. Nec iterum per se, quia si ponerentur aliqua alia loca, non esset in eis.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28568)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, si esset corpus infinitum, esset ubique; sed secundum suas partes.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28569)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, si esset unum solum animal, anima eius esset ubique primo quidem, sed per accidens.\n\n[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28570)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod, cum dicitur anima alicubi videre, potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod hoc adverbium alicubi determinat actum videndi ex parte obiecti. Et sic verum est quod, dum caelum videt, in caelo videt, et eadem ratione in caelo sentit. Non tamen sequitur quod in caelo vivat vel sit, quia vivere et esse non important actum transeuntem in exterius obiectum. Alio modo potest intelligi secundum quod adverbium determinat actum videntis, secundum quod exit a vidente. Et sic verum est quod anima ubi sentit et videt, ibi est et vivit, secundum istum modum loquendi. Et ita non sequitur quod sit ubique.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0002","text":"[I q. 8 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0003","text":"Quia vero infinito convenire videtur quod ubique et in omnibus sit, considerandum est utrum hoc Deo conveniat. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Deus sit in omnibus rebus. Secundo, utrum Deus sit ubique. Tertio, utrum Deus sit ubique per essentiam et potentiam et praesentiam. Quarto, utrum esse ubique sit proprium Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0005","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit in omnibus rebus. Quod enim est supra omnia, non est in omnibus rebus. Sed Deus est supra omnia, secundum illud Psalmi, *excelsus super omnes gentes dominus,* et cetera. Ergo Deus non est in omnibus rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0007","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quod est in aliquo, continetur ab eo. Sed Deus non continetur a rebus, sed magis continet res. Ergo Deus non est in rebus, sed magis res sunt in eo. Unde Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., dicit quod *in ipso potius sunt omnia, quam ipse alicubi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0009","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quanto aliquod agens est virtuosius, tanto ad magis distans eius actio procedit. Sed Deus est virtuosissimum agens. Ergo eius actio pertingere potest ad ea etiam quae ab ipso distant, nec oportet quod sit in omnibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0011","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0012","text":"Praeterea, Daemones res aliquae sunt. Nec tamen Deus est in Daemonibus, non enim est conventio lucis ad tenebras, ut dicitur II ad Cor. VI. Ergo Deus non est in omnibus rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0013","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0014","text":"Sed contra, ubicumque operatur aliquid, ibi est. Sed Deus operatur in omnibus, secundum illud Isaiae XXVI, *omnia opera nostra operatus es in nobis, domine*. Ergo Deus est in omnibus rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0015","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus est in omnibus rebus, non quidem sicut pars essentiae, vel sicut accidens, sed sicut agens adest ei in quod agit. Oportet enim omne agens coniungi ei in quod immediate agit, et sua virtute illud contingere, unde in VII Physic. probatur quod motum et movens oportet esse simul. Cum autem Deus sit ipsum esse per suam essentiam, oportet quod esse creatum sit proprius effectus eius; sicut ignire est proprius effectus ignis. Hunc autem effectum causat Deus in rebus, non solum quando primo esse incipiunt, sed quandiu in esse conservantur; sicut lumen causatur in aere a sole quandiu aer illuminatus manet. Quandiu igitur res habet esse, tandiu oportet quod Deus adsit ei, secundum modum quo esse habet. Esse autem est illud quod est magis intimum cuilibet, et quod profundius omnibus inest, cum sit formale respectu omnium quae in re sunt, ut ex supra dictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","text":"Unde oportet quod Deus sit in omnibus rebus, et intime.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0018","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus est supra omnia per excellentiam suae naturae, et tamen est in omnibus rebus, ut causans omnium esse, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0020","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet corporalia dicantur esse in aliquo sicut in continente, tamen spiritualia continent ea in quibus sunt, sicut anima continet corpus. Unde et Deus est in rebus sicut continens res. Tamen, per quandam similitudinem corporalium, dicuntur omnia esse in Deo, inquantum continentur ab ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0022","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nullius agentis, quantumcumque virtuosi, actio procedit ad aliquid distans, nisi inquantum in illud per media agit. Hoc autem ad maximam virtutem Dei pertinet, quod immediate in omnibus agit. Unde nihil est distans ab eo, quasi in se illud Deum non habeat. Dicuntur tamen res distare a Deo per dissimilitudinem naturae vel gratiae, sicut et ipse est super omnia per excellentiam suae naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0024","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in Daemonibus intelligitur et natura, quae est a Deo, et deformitas culpae, quae non est ab ipso. Et ideo non est absolute concedendum quod Deus sit in Daemonibus, sed cum hac additione, inquantum sunt res quaedam. In rebus autem quae nominant naturam non deformatam, absolute dicendum est Deum esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0027","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit ubique. Esse enim ubique significat esse in omni loco. Sed esse in omni loco non convenit Deo, cui non convenit esse in loco, nam incorporalia, ut dicit Boetius, in libro de Hebdomad., non sunt in loco. Ergo Deus non est ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0029","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0030","text":"Praeterea, sicut se habet tempus ad successiva, ita se habet locus ad permanentia. Sed unum indivisibile actionis vel motus, non potest esse in diversis temporibus. Ergo nec unum indivisibile in genere rerum permanentium, potest esse in omnibus locis. Esse autem divinum non est successivum, sed permanens. Ergo Deus non est in pluribus locis. Et ita non est ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0031","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0032","text":"Praeterea, quod est totum alicubi, nihil eius est extra locum illum. Sed Deus, si est in aliquo loco, totus est ibi, non enim habet partes. Ergo nihil eius est extra locum illum. Ergo Deus non est ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0033","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0034","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ierem. XXIII, *caelum et terram ego impleo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0035","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum locus sit res quaedam, esse aliquid in loco potest intelligi dupliciter, vel per modum aliarum rerum, idest sicut dicitur aliquid esse in aliis rebus quocumque modo, sicut accidentia loci sunt in loco; vel per modum proprium loci, sicut locata sunt in loco. Utroque autem modo, secundum aliquid, Deus est in omni loco, quod est esse ubique. Primo quidem, sicut est in omnibus rebus, ut dans eis esse et virtutem et operationem, sic enim est in omni loco, ut dans ei esse et virtutem locativam. Item, locata sunt in loco inquantum replent locum, et Deus omnem locum replet. Non sicut corpus, corpus enim dicitur replere locum, inquantum non compatitur secum aliud corpus; sed per hoc quod Deus est in aliquo loco, non excluditur quin alia sint ibi, imo per hoc replet omnia loca, quod dat esse omnibus locatis, quae replent omnia loca.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0037","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod incorporalia non sunt in loco per contactum quantitatis dimensivae, sicut corpora, sed per contactum virtutis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0039","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod indivisibile est duplex. Unum quod est terminus continui, ut punctus in permanentibus, et momentum in successivis. Et huiusmodi indivisibile, in permanentibus, quia habet determinatum situm, non potest esse in pluribus partibus loci, vel in pluribus locis, et similiter indivisibile actionis vel motus, quia habet determinatum ordinem in motu vel actione, non potest esse in pluribus partibus temporis. Aliud autem indivisibile est, quod est extra totum genus continui, et hoc modo substantiae incorporeae, ut Deus, Angelus et anima, dicuntur esse indivisibiles. Tale igitur indivisibile non applicatur ad continuum sicut aliquid eius, sed inquantum contingit illud sua virtute. Unde secundum quod virtus sua se potest extendere ad unum vel multa, ad parvum vel magnum, secundum hoc est in uno vel pluribus locis, et in loco parvo vel magno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0041","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod totum dicitur respectu partium. Est autem duplex pars, scilicet pars essentiae, ut forma et materia dicuntur partes compositi, et genus et differentia partes speciei; et etiam pars quantitatis, in quam scilicet dividitur aliqua quantitas. Quod ergo est totum in aliquo loco totalitate quantitatis, non potest esse extra locum illum, quia quantitas locati commensuratur quantitati loci, unde non est totalitas quantitatis, si non sit totalitas loci. Sed totalitas essentiae non commensuratur totalitati loci. Unde non oportet quod illud quod est totum totalitate essentiae in aliquo, nullo modo sit extra illud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","text":"Sicut apparet etiam in formis accidentalibus, quae secundum accidens quantitatem habent, albedo enim est tota in qualibet parte superficiei, si accipiatur totalitas essentiae, quia secundum perfectam rationem suae speciei invenitur in qualibet parte superficiei, si autem accipiatur totalitas secundum quantitatem, quam habet per accidens, sic non est tota in qualibet parte superficiei. In substantiis autem incorporeis non est totalitas, nec per se nec per accidens, nisi secundum perfectam rationem essentiae. Et ideo, sicut anima est tota in qualibet parte corporis, ita Deus totus est in omnibus et singulis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0045","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod male assignentur modi existendi Deum in rebus, cum dicitur quod Deus est in omnibus rebus per essentiam, potentiam et praesentiam. Id enim per essentiam est in aliquo, quod essentialiter est in eo. Deus autem non est essentialiter in rebus, non enim est de essentia alicuius rei. Ergo non debet dici quod Deus sit in rebus per essentiam, praesentiam et potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0047","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0048","text":"Praeterea, hoc est esse praesentem alicui rei, scilicet non deesse illi. Sed hoc est Deum esse per essentiam in rebus, scilicet non deesse alicui rei. Ergo idem est esse Deum in omnibus per essentiam et praesentiam. Superfluum ergo fuit dicere quod Deus sit in rebus per essentiam, praesentiam et potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0049","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0050","text":"Praeterea, sicut Deus est principium omnium rerum per suam potentiam, ita per scientiam et voluntatem. Sed non dicitur Deus esse in rebus per scientiam et voluntatem. Ergo nec per potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0051","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0052","text":"Praeterea, sicut gratia est quaedam perfectio superaddita substantiae rei, ita multae sunt aliae perfectiones superadditae. Si ergo Deus dicitur esse speciali modo in quibusdam per gratiam, videtur quod secundum quamlibet perfectionem debeat accipi specialis modus essendi Deum in rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0053","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0054","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, super Cant. Cantic., quod *Deus communi modo est in omnibus rebus praesentia, potentia et substantia, tamen familiari modo dicitur esse in aliquibus per gratiam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0055","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus dicitur esse in re aliqua dupliciter. Uno modo, per modum causae agentis, et sic est in omnibus rebus creatis ab ipso. Alio modo, sicut obiectum operationis est in operante, quod proprium est in operationibus animae, secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente, et desideratum in desiderante. Hoc igitur secundo modo, Deus specialiter est in rationali creatura, quae cognoscit et diligit illum actu vel habitu. Et quia hoc habet rationalis creatura per gratiam, ut infra patebit, dicitur esse hoc modo in sanctis per gratiam. In rebus vero aliis ab ipso creatis quomodo sit, considerandum est ex his quae in rebus humanis esse dicuntur. Rex enim dicitur esse in toto regno suo per suam potentiam, licet non sit ubique praesens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","text":"Per praesentiam vero suam, dicitur aliquid esse in omnibus quae in prospectu ipsius sunt; sicut omnia quae sunt in aliqua domo, dicuntur esse praesentia alicui, qui tamen non est secundum substantiam suam in qualibet parte domus. Secundum vero substantiam vel essentiam, dicitur aliquid esse in loco in quo eius substantia habetur. Fuerunt ergo aliqui, scilicet Manichaei, qui dixerunt divinae potestati subiecta spiritualia esse et incorporalia, visibilia vero et corporalia subiecta esse dicebant potestati principii contrarii. Contra hos ergo oportet dicere quod Deus sit in omnibus per potentiam suam. Fuerunt vero alii, qui licet crederent omnia esse subiecta divinae potentiae, tamen providentiam divinam usque ad haec inferiora corpora non extendebant, ex quorum persona dicitur Iob XXII, *circa cardines caeli perambulat, nec nostra considerat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","text":"Et contra hos oportuit dicere quod sit in omnibus per suam praesentiam. Fuerunt vero alii, qui licet dicerent omnia ad Dei providentiam pertinere, tamen posuerunt omnia non immediate esse a Deo creata, sed quod immediate creavit primas creaturas, et illae creaverunt alias. Et contra hos oportet dicere quod sit in omnibus per essentiam. Sic ergo est in omnibus per potentiam, inquantum omnia eius potestati subduntur. Est per praesentiam in omnibus, inquantum omnia nuda sunt et aperta oculis eius. Est in omnibus per essentiam, inquantum adest omnibus ut causa essendi, sicut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0059","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0060","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus dicitur esse in omnibus per essentiam, non quidem rerum, quasi sit de essentia earum, sed per essentiam suam, quia substantia sua adest omnibus ut causa essendi, sicut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0061","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0062","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliquid potest dici praesens alicui, inquantum subiacet eius conspectui, quod tamen distat ab eo secundum suam substantiam, ut dictum est. Et ideo oportuit duos modos poni, scilicet per essentiam, et praesentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0063","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod de ratione scientiae et voluntatis est, quod scitum sit in sciente, et volitum in volente, unde secundum scientiam et voluntatem, magis res sunt in Deo, quam Deus in rebus. Sed de ratione potentiae est, quod sit principium agendi in aliud, unde secundum potentiam agens comparatur et applicatur rei exteriori. Et sic per potentiam potest dici agens esse in altero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0065","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod nulla alia perfectio superaddita substantiae, facit Deum esse in aliquo sicut obiectum cognitum et amatum, nisi gratia, et ideo sola gratia facit singularem modum essendi Deum in rebus. Est autem alius singularis modus essendi Deum in homine per unionem, de quo modo suo loco agetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0067","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0068","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0069","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse ubique non sit proprium Dei. Universale enim, secundum philosophum, est ubique et semper, materia etiam prima, cum sit in omnibus corporibus, est ubique. Neutrum autem horum est Deus, ut ex praemissis patet. Ergo esse ubique non est proprium Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0070","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0071","text":"Praeterea, numerus est in numeratis. Sed totum universum est constitutum in numero, ut patet Sap. XI. Ergo aliquis numerus est, qui est in toto universo, et ita ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0072","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0073","text":"Praeterea, totum universum est quoddam totum corpus perfectum, ut dicitur in I caeli et mundi. Sed totum universum est ubique, quia extra ipsum nullus locus est. Non ergo solus Deus est ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0074","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0075","text":"Praeterea, si aliquod corpus esset infinitum, nullus locus esset extra ipsum. Ergo esset ubique. Et sic, esse ubique non videtur proprium Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0076","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0077","text":"*Praeterea, anima*, ut dicit Augustinus, in VI de Trin., *est tota in toto corpore, et tota in qualibet eius parte*. Si ergo non esset in mundo nisi unum solum animal, anima eius esset ubique. Et sic, esse ubique non est proprium Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0078","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0079","text":"Praeterea, ut Augustinus dicit in epistola ad Volusianum, *anima ubi videt, ibi sentit; et ubi sentit, ibi vivit; et ubi vivit, ibi est*. Sed anima videt quasi ubique, quia successive videt etiam totum caelum. Ergo anima est ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0080","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0081","text":"Sed contra est quod Ambrosius dicit, in libro de spiritu sancto, *quis audeat creaturam dicere spiritum sanctum, qui in omnibus et ubique et semper est; quod utique divinitatis est proprium?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0082","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod esse ubique primo et per se, est proprium Dei. Dico autem esse ubique primo, quod secundum se totum est ubique. Si quid enim esset ubique, secundum diversas partes in diversis locis existens, non esset primo ubique, quia quod convenit alicui ratione partis suae, non convenit ei primo; sicut si homo est albus dente, albedo non convenit primo homini, sed denti. Esse autem ubique per se dico id cui non convenit esse ubique per accidens, propter aliquam suppositionem factam, quia sic granum milii esset ubique, supposito quod nullum aliud corpus esset. Per se igitur convenit esse ubique alicui, quando tale est quod, qualibet positione facta, sequitur illud esse ubique. Et hoc proprie convenit Deo. Quia quotcumque loca ponantur, etiam si ponerentur infinita praeter ista quae sunt, oporteret in omnibus esse Deum, quia nihil potest esse nisi per ipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","text":"Sic igitur esse ubique primo et per se convenit Deo, et est proprium eius, quia quotcumque loca ponantur, oportet quod in quolibet sit Deus, non secundum partem, sed secundum seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0085","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0086","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod universale et materia prima sunt quidem ubique, sed non secundum idem esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0087","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0088","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod numerus, cum sit accidens, non est per se sed per accidens, in loco. Nec est totus in quolibet numeratorum, sed secundum partem. Et sic non sequitur quod sit primo et per se ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0089","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0090","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod totum corpus universi est ubique, sed non primo, quia non totum est in quolibet loco, sed secundum suas partes. Nec iterum per se, quia si ponerentur aliqua alia loca, non esset in eis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0091","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0092","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, si esset corpus infinitum, esset ubique; sed secundum suas partes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0093","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0094","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, si esset unum solum animal, anima eius esset ubique primo quidem, sed per accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0095","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0096","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod, cum dicitur anima alicubi videre, potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod hoc adverbium alicubi determinat actum videndi ex parte obiecti. Et sic verum est quod, dum caelum videt, in caelo videt, et eadem ratione in caelo sentit. Non tamen sequitur quod in caelo vivat vel sit, quia vivere et esse non important actum transeuntem in exterius obiectum. Alio modo potest intelligi secundum quod adverbium determinat actum videntis, secundum quod exit a vidente. Et sic verum est quod anima ubi sentit et videt, ibi est et vivit, secundum istum modum loquendi. Et ita non sequitur quod sit ubique.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"1505a9de376c9f55dab791f452ca32ba22c73c25d66188e6168076516d1a1671","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T134045291409Z_68fca309","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 8","source_sha256":"1505a9de376c9f55dab791f452ca32ba22c73c25d66188e6168076516d1a1671","sha256":"55d9eae77d55fa935f5731c3b0f90ef5122574ec8e3cc1ab1a6af60cc00c5e00","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:40:45.291476+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0002","text":"[I q. 8 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0003","text":"But since it seems to belong to the infinite that it be everywhere and in all things, we must consider whether this belongs to God. And concerning this four questions are asked. First, whether God is in all things. Second, whether God is everywhere. Third, whether God is everywhere by essence and power and presence. Fourth, whether to be everywhere is proper to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0005","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that God is not in all things. For what is above all things is not in all things. But God is above all things, according to that saying of the Psalm, *the Lord is exalted above all nations,* and so forth. Therefore God is not in all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0007","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0008","text":"Further, what is in something is contained by it. But God is not contained by things; rather, he contains things. Therefore God is not in things, but rather things are in him. Hence Augustine, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, says that *all things are rather in him than he is anywhere*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0009","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0010","text":"Further, the more powerful an agent is, the farther its action extends. But God is a most powerful agent. Therefore his action can reach even those things that are distant from him, and it is not necessary that he be in all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0011","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0012","text":"Further, demons are certain things. Yet God is not in demons, for there is no fellowship of light with darkness, as is said in II Corinthians VI. Therefore God is not in all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0013","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0014","text":"On the contrary, wherever something operates, there it is. But God operates in all things, according to that saying of Isaiah XXVI, *you have wrought all our works in us, O Lord*. Therefore God is in all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0015","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that God is in all things, not indeed as part of their essence, or as an accident, but as an agent is present to that upon which it acts. For every agent must be joined to that upon which it acts immediately, and must touch it by its power; hence it is proved in Physics VII that the moved and the mover must be together. But since God is subsistent being itself through his essence, created being must be his proper effect, just as making fire is the proper effect of fire. Now God causes this effect in things not only when they first begin to be, but as long as they are preserved in being; just as light is caused in the air by the sun as long as the air remains illuminated. Therefore, as long as a thing has being, God must be present to it, according to the mode in which it has being. But being is that which is most intimate to each thing, and which is most deeply present in all things, since it is formal with respect to everything that is in a thing, as is clear from what has been said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","text":"Hence God must be in all things, and intimately so.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0018","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0019","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that God is above all things by the excellence of his nature, and yet is in all things, as causing the being of all things, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0020","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","text":"To the second it must be said that, although corporeal things are said to be in something as in a container, spiritual things contain those things in which they are, just as the soul contains the body. Hence God too is in things as containing things. Nevertheless, by a certain likeness to corporeal things, all things are said to be in God, insofar as they are contained by him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0022","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0023","text":"To the third it must be said that the action of no agent, however powerful, extends to something distant except insofar as it acts upon it through intermediaries. But this belongs to the greatest power of God: that he acts immediately in all things. Hence nothing is distant from him, as though it did not have God in itself. Nevertheless, things are said to be distant from God by dissimilarity of nature or of grace, just as he himself is above all things by the excellence of his nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0024","text":"[I q. 8 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that in demons both the nature, which is from God, and the deformity of fault, which is not from Him, are understood. And therefore it must not be granted without qualification that God is in demons, but with this addition: insofar as they are certain things. But in things that name nature not deformed, it must be said without qualification that God is present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0027","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0028","text":"The second is approached thus. It seems that God is not everywhere. For to be everywhere signifies to be in every place. But to be in every place does not befit God, to whom being in a place does not befit; for incorporeal things, as Boethius says in the book *De Hebdomadibus*, are not in a place. Therefore God is not everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0029","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0030","text":"Further, as time is related to successive things, so place is related to permanent things. But one indivisible of action or motion cannot be in different times. Therefore neither can one indivisible in the genus of permanent things be in all places. But the divine being is not successive, but permanent. Therefore God is not in several places. And thus He is not everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0031","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0032","text":"Further, of what is wholly somewhere, none of it is outside that place. But God, if He is in some place, is wholly there, for He has no parts. Therefore none of Him is outside that place. Therefore God is not everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0033","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0034","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Jeremiah XXIII: *I fill heaven and earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0035","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","text":"I answer that, since place is a certain thing, for something to be in a place can be understood in two ways: either in the manner of other things, that is, as something is said to be in other things in whatever way, as the accidents of place are in place; or in the manner proper to place, as things located are in a place. Now in both ways, in a certain respect, God is in every place, which is to be everywhere. First, indeed, just as He is in all things, as giving them being, power, and operation, so He is in every place, as giving it being and locative power. Likewise, things located are in a place insofar as they fill the place, and God fills every place. Not as a body does; for a body is said to fill a place insofar as it does not admit another body along with itself. But by the fact that God is in some place, it is not excluded that other things are there; indeed, He fills all places precisely by giving being to all things located, which fill all places.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0037","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that incorporeal things are not in a place by contact of dimensive quantity, as bodies are, but by contact of power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0039","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0040","text":"To the second, it must be said that the indivisible is twofold. One kind is the terminus of a continuum, as a point in permanent things and an instant in successive things. And such an indivisible, in permanent things, because it has a determinate position, cannot be in several parts of a place or in several places; and likewise an indivisible of action or motion, because it has a determinate order in motion or action, cannot be in several parts of time. But another indivisible is that which is outside the whole genus of the continuous; and in this way incorporeal substances, such as God, an angel, and the soul, are called indivisible. Such an indivisible, therefore, is not applied to the continuum as something belonging to it, but insofar as it reaches it by its power. Hence, according as its power can extend to one or many, to small or great, so it is in one or several places, and in a small or great place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0041","text":"[I q. 8 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042","text":"To the third, it must be said that “whole” is said with respect to parts. But there are two kinds of part: namely, a part of essence, as form and matter are called parts of a composite, and genus and difference, parts of a species; and also a part of quantity, into which, namely, some quantity is divided. Therefore, what is whole in some place by totality of quantity cannot be outside that place, because the quantity of the located thing is commensurate with the quantity of the place; hence there is not totality of quantity unless there is totality of place. But totality of essence is not commensurate with totality of place. Hence it need not be that what is whole by totality of essence in something is in no way outside it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","text":"This is also apparent in accidental forms, which have quantity accidentally. For whiteness is whole in every part of a surface, if totality of essence is taken, because according to the complete intelligible account of its species it is found in every part of the surface. But if totality according to quantity, which it has accidentally, is taken, then it is not whole in every part of the surface. But in incorporeal substances there is no totality, either per se or accidentally, except according to the complete intelligible account of essence. And therefore, just as the soul is whole in every part of the body, so God is whole in all things and each individual thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0045","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0046","text":"The third is approached thus. It seems that the modes of God’s existing in things are assigned incorrectly when it is said that God is in all things through essence, power, and presence. For that is in something through essence which is essentially in it. But God is not essentially in things, for He is not of the essence of any thing. Therefore it should not be said that God is in things through essence, presence, and power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0047","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0048","text":"Further, to be present to some thing is, namely, not to be absent from it. But this is for God to be in things through essence, namely, not to be absent from any thing. Therefore for God to be in all things through essence and through presence is the same. Therefore it was superfluous to say that God is in things through essence, presence, and power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0049","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0050","text":"Further, just as God is the principle of all things through His power, so too through knowledge and will. But God is not said to be in things through knowledge and will. Therefore neither is He in them through power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0051","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0052","text":"Further, just as grace is a certain perfection added to the substance of a thing, so there are many other perfections added [to it]. Therefore, if God is said to be in some things in a special way through grace, it seems that, according to every perfection whatever, a special mode of God's being in things ought to be recognized.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0053","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0054","text":"But on the contrary, Gregory says, on the Song of Songs, that *God is present in all things in a common way by presence, power, and substance; yet He is said to be in some in a familiar way through grace*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0055","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","text":"I answer that it must be said that God is said to be in a thing in two ways. In one way, by the mode of an efficient cause; and thus He is in all things created by Him. In another way, as the object of an operation is in the operator, which is proper to the operations of the soul, insofar as the known is in the knower, and the desired in the one desiring. Therefore, in this second way, God is specially in the rational creature, which knows and loves Him actually or habitually. And because the rational creature has this through grace, as will become clear below, He is said to be in the saints in this way through grace. But how He is in the other things created by Him must be considered from those things which are said to exist in human affairs. For a king is said to be in his whole kingdom through his power, although he is not present everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","text":"But through his presence, someone is said to be in all those things which are within his view; just as all things that are in some house are said to be present to someone who nevertheless is not, according to his substance, in every part of the house. But according to substance or essence, something is said to be in a place in which its substance is had. There were therefore some, namely the Manichaeans, who said that spiritual and incorporeal things are subject to the divine power, while they said that visible and corporeal things are subject to the power of a contrary principle. Against these, therefore, one must say that God is in all things through His power. But there were others who, although they believed that all things are subject to the divine power, nevertheless did not extend divine providence down to these lower bodies, on whose behalf Job 22 says, *He walks about the hinges of heaven, and does not consider us*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","text":"And against these it was necessary to say that He is in all things through His presence. But there were others who, although they said that all things pertain to God's providence, nevertheless held that not all things were created immediately by God, but that He immediately created the first creatures, and they created the others. And against these one must say that He is in all things through essence. Thus, therefore, He is in all things through power, insofar as all things are subject to His power. He is in all things through presence, insofar as all things are naked and open to His eyes. He is in all things through essence, insofar as He is present to all things as the cause of being, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0059","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0060","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God is said to be in all things through essence, not indeed through the essence of things, as though He were of their essence, but through His own essence, because His substance is present to all things as the cause of being, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0061","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0062","text":"To the second it must be said that something can be called present to another insofar as it lies under its sight, while nevertheless being distant from it according to its substance, as has been said. And therefore it was necessary to posit two modes, namely, through essence and presence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0063","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","text":"To the third it must be said that it belongs to the nature of knowledge and will that the known be in the knower and the willed in the one willing; hence, according to knowledge and will, things are more in God than God is in things. But it belongs to the nature of power that it be a principle of acting upon another; hence according to power the agent is related and applied to the exterior thing. And thus through power an agent can be said to be in another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0065","text":"[I q. 8 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","text":"To the fourth it must be said that no other perfection added to substance makes God to be in something as a known and loved object, except grace; and therefore grace alone produces a singular mode of God's being in things. But there is another singular mode of God's being in man through union, concerning which mode it will be treated in its proper place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0067","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0068","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0069","text":"We proceed to the fourth thus. It seems that to be everywhere is not proper to God. For the universal, according to the Philosopher, is everywhere and always; and first matter also, since it is in all bodies, is everywhere. But neither of these is God, as is clear from what has been set forth above. Therefore, to be everywhere is not proper to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0070","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0071","text":"Further, number is in numbered things. But the whole universe is constituted in number, as is clear from Wisdom 11. Therefore there is some number which is in the whole universe, and thus everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0072","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0073","text":"Moreover, the whole universe is a certain perfect whole body, as is said in *On the Heavens and the World*, I. But the whole universe is everywhere, because there is no place outside it. Therefore God alone is not everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0074","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0075","text":"Moreover, if some body were infinite, there would be no place outside it. Therefore it would be everywhere. And thus, to be everywhere does not seem proper to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0076","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0077","text":"*Moreover, the soul*, as Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, VI, *is whole in the whole body, and whole in each of its parts*. Therefore, if there were only one single animal in the world, its soul would be everywhere. And thus, to be everywhere is not proper to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0078","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0079","text":"Moreover, as Augustine says in the letter to Volusianus, *where the soul sees, there it senses; and where it senses, there it lives; and where it lives, there it is*. But the soul sees as if everywhere, because successively it sees even the whole heaven. Therefore the soul is everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0080","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0081","text":"On the contrary, Ambrose says, in the book *On the Holy Spirit*: *who would dare call the Holy Spirit a creature, who is in all things and everywhere and always; which indeed is proper to divinity?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0082","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","text":"I answer that it must be said that to be everywhere primarily and through itself is proper to God. Now, I call that to be everywhere primarily which is everywhere according to its whole self. For if something were everywhere, existing in different places according to different parts, it would not be everywhere primarily, because what belongs to something by reason of one of its parts does not belong to it primarily; just as, if a man is white by his tooth, whiteness does not belong primarily to the man, but to the tooth. But I call to be everywhere through itself that to which being everywhere does not belong accidentally, on account of some supposition made; for thus a grain of millet would be everywhere, if it were supposed that no other body existed. Therefore, being everywhere belongs through itself to something when it is such that, whatever position is made, it follows that it is everywhere. And this properly belongs to God. For however many places may be posited, even if infinite places besides those which exist were posited, God would have to be in all of them, because nothing can be except through him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","text":"Thus, therefore, to be everywhere primarily and through itself belongs to God, and is proper to him, because however many places may be posited, God must be in each of them, not according to a part, but according to himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0085","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0086","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the universal and prime matter are indeed everywhere, but not according to the same being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0087","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0088","text":"To the second it must be said that number, since it is an accident, is in a place not through itself but accidentally. Nor is it whole in each of the things numbered, but according to a part. And thus it does not follow that it is everywhere primarily and through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0089","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0090","text":"To the third it must be said that the whole body of the universe is everywhere, but not primarily, because it is not whole in each place, but according to its parts. Nor again is it so through itself, because if certain other places were posited, it would not be in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0091","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0092","text":"To the fourth it must be said that, if there were an infinite body, it would be everywhere, but according to its parts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0093","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0094","text":"To the fifth it must be said that, if there were only one animal, its soul would indeed be everywhere primarily, but accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0095","text":"[I q. 8 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0096","text":"To the sixth it must be said that, when the soul is said to see somewhere, this can be understood in two ways. In one way, insofar as this adverb “somewhere” determines the act of seeing on the part of the object. And thus it is true that, while it sees the heaven, it sees in the heaven, and for the same reason senses in the heaven. Yet it does not follow that it lives or is in the heaven, because to live and to be do not imply an act passing over into an external object. In another way, it can be understood insofar as the adverb determines the act of the one seeing, insofar as it proceeds from the one seeing. And thus it is true that where the soul senses and sees, there it is and lives, according to this manner of speaking. And thus it does not follow that it is everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0096"]}],"source_digest":"cb554c5a854e8f4d716bca4fbe0c47eaf9e02d5d40a17498066d5e6b00560a44","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 8"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 8","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9","title":"First Part · Question 9","order":10,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 9 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28571)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de immutabilitate et aeternitate divina, quae immutabilitatem consequitur. Circa immutabilitatem vero quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum Deus sit omnino immutabilis. Secundo, utrum esse immutabile sit proprium Dei.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28572)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit omnino immutabilis. Quidquid enim movet seipsum, est aliquo modo mutabile. Sed, sicut dicit Augustinus, VIII super Genesim ad litteram, *spiritus creator movet se nec per tempus nec per locum*. Ergo Deus est aliquo modo mutabilis.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28573)\n\nPraeterea, Sap. VII dicitur de sapientia quod est *mobilior omnibus mobilibus*. Sed Deus est ipsa sapientia. Ergo Deus est mobilis.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28574)\n\nPraeterea, appropinquari et elongari motum significant. Huiusmodi autem dicuntur de Deo in Scriptura, Iac. IV, *appropinquate Deo, et appropinquabit vobis*. Ergo Deus est mutabilis.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28575)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Malach. III, *ego Deus, et non mutor*.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28576)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ex praemissis ostenditur Deum esse omnino immutabilem. Primo quidem, quia supra ostensum est esse aliquod primum ens, quod Deum dicimus, et quod huiusmodi primum ens oportet esse purum actum absque permixtione alicuius potentiae, eo quod potentia simpliciter est posterior actu. Omne autem quod quocumque modo mutatur, est aliquo modo in potentia. Ex quo patet quod impossibile est Deum aliquo modo mutari. Secundo, quia omne quod movetur, quantum ad aliquid manet, et quantum ad aliquid transit, sicut quod movetur de albedine in nigredinem, manet secundum substantiam. Et sic in omni eo quod movetur, attenditur aliqua compositio. Ostensum est autem supra quod in Deo nulla est compositio, sed est omnino simplex. Unde manifestum est quod Deus moveri non potest. Tertio, quia omne quod movetur, motu suo aliquid acquirit, et pertingit ad illud ad quod prius non pertingebat. Deus autem, cum sit infinitus, comprehendens in se omnem plenitudinem perfectionis totius esse, non potest aliquid acquirere, nec extendere se in aliquid ad quod prius non pertingebat. Unde nullo modo sibi competit motus. Et inde est quod quidam antiquorum, quasi ab ipsa veritate coacti, posuerunt primum principium esse immobile.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28577)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus ibi loquitur secundum modum quo Plato dicebat primum movens movere seipsum, omnem operationem nominans motum; secundum quod etiam ipsum intelligere et velle et amare motus quidam dicuntur. Quia ergo Deus intelligit et amat seipsum, secundum hoc dixerunt quod Deus movet seipsum, non autem secundum quod motus et mutatio est existentis in potentia, ut nunc loquimur de mutatione et motu.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28578)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sapientia dicitur mobilis esse similitudinarie, secundum quod suam similitudinem diffundit usque ad ultima rerum. Nihil enim esse potest, quod non procedat a divina sapientia per quandam imitationem, sicut a primo principio effectivo et formali; prout etiam artificiata procedunt a sapientia artificis. Sic igitur inquantum similitudo divinae sapientiae gradatim procedit a supremis, quae magis participant de eius similitudine, usque ad infima rerum, quae minus participant dicitur esse quidam processus et motus divinae sapientiae in res, sicut si dicamus solem procedere usque ad terram, inquantum radius luminis eius usque ad terram pertingit. Et hoc modo exponit Dionysius, cap. I Cael. Hier., dicens quod *omnis processus divinae manifestationis venit ad nos a patre luminum moto*.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28579)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod huiusmodi dicuntur de Deo in Scripturis metaphorice. Sicut enim dicitur sol intrare domum vel exire, inquantum radius eius pertingit ad domum; sic dicitur Deus appropinquare ad nos vel recedere a nobis, inquantum percipimus influentiam bonitatis ipsius, vel ab eo deficimus.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28580)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse immutabile non sit proprium Dei. Dicit enim philosophus, in II Metaphys., quod materia est in omni eo quod movetur. Sed substantiae quaedam creatae, sicut Angeli et animae, non habent materiam, ut quibusdam videtur. Ergo esse immutabile non est proprium Dei.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28581)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod movetur, movetur propter aliquem finem, quod ergo iam pervenit ad ultimum finem, non movetur. Sed quaedam creaturae iam pervenerunt ad ultimum finem, sicut omnes beati. Ergo aliquae creaturae sunt immobiles.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28582)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est mutabile, est variabile. Sed formae sunt invariabiles, dicitur enim in libro sex principiorum, quod *forma est simplici et invariabili essentia consistens*. Ergo non est solius Dei proprium esse immutabile.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28583)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de natura boni, *solus Deus immutabilis est; quae autem fecit, quia ex nihilo sunt, mutabilia sunt*.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28584)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod solus Deus est omnino immutabilis, omnis autem creatura aliquo modo est mutabilis. Sciendum est enim quod mutabile potest aliquid dici dupliciter, uno modo, per potentiam quae in ipso est; alio modo, per potentiam quae in altero est. Omnes enim creaturae, antequam essent, non erant possibiles esse per aliquam potentiam creatam, cum nullum creatum sit aeternum, sed per solam potentiam divinam, inquantum Deus poterat eas in esse producere. Sicut autem ex voluntate Dei dependet quod res in esse producit, ita ex voluntate eius dependet quod res in esse conservat, non enim aliter eas in esse conservat, quam semper eis esse dando; unde si suam actionem eis subtraheret, omnia in nihilum redigerentur, ut patet per Augustinum, IV super Gen. ad Litt. Sicut igitur in potentia creatoris fuit ut res essent, antequam essent in seipsis, ita in potentia creatoris est, postquam sunt in seipsis, ut non sint. Sic igitur per potentiam quae est in altero, scilicet in Deo, sunt mutabiles, inquantum ab ipso ex nihilo potuerunt produci in esse, et de esse possunt reduci in non esse. Si autem dicatur aliquid mutabile per potentiam in ipso existentem, sic etiam aliquo modo omnis creatura est mutabilis. Est enim in creatura duplex potentia, scilicet activa et passiva. Dico autem potentiam passivam, secundum quam aliquid assequi potest suam perfectionem, vel in essendo vel in consequendo finem. Si igitur attendatur mutabilitas rei secundum potentiam ad esse, sic non in omnibus creaturis est mutabilitas, sed in illis solum in quibus illud quod est possibile in eis, potest stare cum non esse. Unde in corporibus inferioribus est mutabilitas et secundum esse substantiale, quia materia eorum potest esse cum privatione formae substantialis ipsorum, et quantum ad esse accidentale, si subiectum compatiatur secum privationem accidentis; sicut hoc subiectum, homo, compatitur secum non album, et ideo potest mutari de albo in non album. Si vero sit tale accidens quod consequatur principia essentialia subiecti, privatio illius accidentis non potest stare cum subiecto, unde subiectum non potest mutari secundum illud accidens, sicut nix non potest fieri nigra. In corporibus vero caelestibus, materia non compatitur secum privationem formae, quia forma perficit totam potentialitatem materiae, et ideo non sunt mutabilia secundum esse substantiale; sed secundum esse locale, quia subiectum compatitur secum privationem huius loci vel illius. Substantiae vero incorporeae, quia sunt ipsae formae subsistentes, quae tamen se habent ad esse ipsarum sicut potentia ad actum, non compatiuntur secum privationem huius actus, quia esse consequitur formam, et nihil corrumpitur nisi per hoc quod amittit formam. Unde in ipsa forma non est potentia ad non esse, et ideo huiusmodi substantiae sunt immutabiles et invariabiles secundum esse. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *substantiae intellectuales creatae mundae sunt a generatione et ab omni variatione, sicut incorporales et immateriales*. Sed tamen remanet in eis duplex mutabilitas. Una secundum quod sunt in potentia ad finem, et sic est in eis mutabilitas secundum electionem de bono in malum, ut Damascenus dicit. Alia secundum locum, inquantum virtute sua finita possunt attingere quaedam loca quae prius non attingebant, quod de Deo dici non potest, qui sua infinitate omnia loca replet, ut supra dictum est. Sic igitur in omni creatura est potentia ad mutationem, vel secundum esse substantiale, sicut corpora corruptibilia; vel secundum esse locale tantum, sicut corpora caelestia, vel secundum ordinem ad finem et applicationem virtutis ad diversa, sicut in Angelis. Et universaliter omnes creaturae communiter sunt mutabiles secundum potentiam creantis, in cuius potestate est esse et non esse earum. Unde, cum Deus nullo istorum modorum sit mutabilis, proprium eius est omnino immutabilem esse.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28585)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de eo quod est mutabile secundum esse substantiale vel accidentale, de tali enim motu philosophi tractaverunt.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28586)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angeli boni, supra immutabilitatem essendi, quae competit eis secundum naturam, habent immutabilitatem electionis ex divina virtute, tamen remanet in eis mutabilitas secundum locum.\n\n[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28587)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod formae dicuntur invariabiles, quia non possunt esse subiectum variationis, subiiciuntur tamen variationi, inquantum subiectum secundum eas variatur. Unde patet quod secundum quod sunt, sic variantur, non enim dicuntur entia quasi sint subiectum essendi, sed quia eis aliquid est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0002","text":"[I q. 9 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de immutabilitate et aeternitate divina, quae immutabilitatem consequitur. Circa immutabilitatem vero quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum Deus sit omnino immutabilis. Secundo, utrum esse immutabile sit proprium Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0005","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit omnino immutabilis. Quidquid enim movet seipsum, est aliquo modo mutabile. Sed, sicut dicit Augustinus, VIII super Genesim ad litteram, *spiritus creator movet se nec per tempus nec per locum*. Ergo Deus est aliquo modo mutabilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0007","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Sap. VII dicitur de sapientia quod est *mobilior omnibus mobilibus*. Sed Deus est ipsa sapientia. Ergo Deus est mobilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0009","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0010","text":"Praeterea, appropinquari et elongari motum significant. Huiusmodi autem dicuntur de Deo in Scriptura, Iac. IV, *appropinquate Deo, et appropinquabit vobis*. Ergo Deus est mutabilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0011","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Malach. III, *ego Deus, et non mutor*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0013","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ex praemissis ostenditur Deum esse omnino immutabilem. Primo quidem, quia supra ostensum est esse aliquod primum ens, quod Deum dicimus, et quod huiusmodi primum ens oportet esse purum actum absque permixtione alicuius potentiae, eo quod potentia simpliciter est posterior actu. Omne autem quod quocumque modo mutatur, est aliquo modo in potentia. Ex quo patet quod impossibile est Deum aliquo modo mutari. Secundo, quia omne quod movetur, quantum ad aliquid manet, et quantum ad aliquid transit, sicut quod movetur de albedine in nigredinem, manet secundum substantiam. Et sic in omni eo quod movetur, attenditur aliqua compositio. Ostensum est autem supra quod in Deo nulla est compositio, sed est omnino simplex. Unde manifestum est quod Deus moveri non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","text":"Tertio, quia omne quod movetur, motu suo aliquid acquirit, et pertingit ad illud ad quod prius non pertingebat. Deus autem, cum sit infinitus, comprehendens in se omnem plenitudinem perfectionis totius esse, non potest aliquid acquirere, nec extendere se in aliquid ad quod prius non pertingebat. Unde nullo modo sibi competit motus. Et inde est quod quidam antiquorum, quasi ab ipsa veritate coacti, posuerunt primum principium esse immobile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0016","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus ibi loquitur secundum modum quo Plato dicebat primum movens movere seipsum, omnem operationem nominans motum; secundum quod etiam ipsum intelligere et velle et amare motus quidam dicuntur. Quia ergo Deus intelligit et amat seipsum, secundum hoc dixerunt quod Deus movet seipsum, non autem secundum quod motus et mutatio est existentis in potentia, ut nunc loquimur de mutatione et motu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0018","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sapientia dicitur mobilis esse similitudinarie, secundum quod suam similitudinem diffundit usque ad ultima rerum. Nihil enim esse potest, quod non procedat a divina sapientia per quandam imitationem, sicut a primo principio effectivo et formali; prout etiam artificiata procedunt a sapientia artificis. Sic igitur inquantum similitudo divinae sapientiae gradatim procedit a supremis, quae magis participant de eius similitudine, usque ad infima rerum, quae minus participant dicitur esse quidam processus et motus divinae sapientiae in res, sicut si dicamus solem procedere usque ad terram, inquantum radius luminis eius usque ad terram pertingit. Et hoc modo exponit Dionysius, cap. I Cael. Hier., dicens quod *omnis processus divinae manifestationis venit ad nos a patre luminum moto*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0020","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod huiusmodi dicuntur de Deo in Scripturis metaphorice. Sicut enim dicitur sol intrare domum vel exire, inquantum radius eius pertingit ad domum; sic dicitur Deus appropinquare ad nos vel recedere a nobis, inquantum percipimus influentiam bonitatis ipsius, vel ab eo deficimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0023","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse immutabile non sit proprium Dei. Dicit enim philosophus, in II Metaphys., quod materia est in omni eo quod movetur. Sed substantiae quaedam creatae, sicut Angeli et animae, non habent materiam, ut quibusdam videtur. Ergo esse immutabile non est proprium Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0025","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0026","text":"Praeterea, omne quod movetur, movetur propter aliquem finem, quod ergo iam pervenit ad ultimum finem, non movetur. Sed quaedam creaturae iam pervenerunt ad ultimum finem, sicut omnes beati. Ergo aliquae creaturae sunt immobiles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0027","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0028","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est mutabile, est variabile. Sed formae sunt invariabiles, dicitur enim in libro sex principiorum, quod *forma est simplici et invariabili essentia consistens*. Ergo non est solius Dei proprium esse immutabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0029","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de natura boni, *solus Deus immutabilis est; quae autem fecit, quia ex nihilo sunt, mutabilia sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0031","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod solus Deus est omnino immutabilis, omnis autem creatura aliquo modo est mutabilis. Sciendum est enim quod mutabile potest aliquid dici dupliciter, uno modo, per potentiam quae in ipso est; alio modo, per potentiam quae in altero est. Omnes enim creaturae, antequam essent, non erant possibiles esse per aliquam potentiam creatam, cum nullum creatum sit aeternum, sed per solam potentiam divinam, inquantum Deus poterat eas in esse producere. Sicut autem ex voluntate Dei dependet quod res in esse producit, ita ex voluntate eius dependet quod res in esse conservat, non enim aliter eas in esse conservat, quam semper eis esse dando; unde si suam actionem eis subtraheret, omnia in nihilum redigerentur, ut patet per Augustinum, IV super Gen. ad Litt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","text":"Sicut igitur in potentia creatoris fuit ut res essent, antequam essent in seipsis, ita in potentia creatoris est, postquam sunt in seipsis, ut non sint. Sic igitur per potentiam quae est in altero, scilicet in Deo, sunt mutabiles, inquantum ab ipso ex nihilo potuerunt produci in esse, et de esse possunt reduci in non esse. Si autem dicatur aliquid mutabile per potentiam in ipso existentem, sic etiam aliquo modo omnis creatura est mutabilis. Est enim in creatura duplex potentia, scilicet activa et passiva. Dico autem potentiam passivam, secundum quam aliquid assequi potest suam perfectionem, vel in essendo vel in consequendo finem. Si igitur attendatur mutabilitas rei secundum potentiam ad esse, sic non in omnibus creaturis est mutabilitas, sed in illis solum in quibus illud quod est possibile in eis, potest stare cum non esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","text":"Unde in corporibus inferioribus est mutabilitas et secundum esse substantiale, quia materia eorum potest esse cum privatione formae substantialis ipsorum, et quantum ad esse accidentale, si subiectum compatiatur secum privationem accidentis; sicut hoc subiectum, homo, compatitur secum non album, et ideo potest mutari de albo in non album. Si vero sit tale accidens quod consequatur principia essentialia subiecti, privatio illius accidentis non potest stare cum subiecto, unde subiectum non potest mutari secundum illud accidens, sicut nix non potest fieri nigra. In corporibus vero caelestibus, materia non compatitur secum privationem formae, quia forma perficit totam potentialitatem materiae, et ideo non sunt mutabilia secundum esse substantiale; sed secundum esse locale, quia subiectum compatitur secum privationem huius loci vel illius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","text":"Substantiae vero incorporeae, quia sunt ipsae formae subsistentes, quae tamen se habent ad esse ipsarum sicut potentia ad actum, non compatiuntur secum privationem huius actus, quia esse consequitur formam, et nihil corrumpitur nisi per hoc quod amittit formam. Unde in ipsa forma non est potentia ad non esse, et ideo huiusmodi substantiae sunt immutabiles et invariabiles secundum esse. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *substantiae intellectuales creatae mundae sunt a generatione et ab omni variatione, sicut incorporales et immateriales*. Sed tamen remanet in eis duplex mutabilitas. Una secundum quod sunt in potentia ad finem, et sic est in eis mutabilitas secundum electionem de bono in malum, ut Damascenus dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","text":"Alia secundum locum, inquantum virtute sua finita possunt attingere quaedam loca quae prius non attingebant, quod de Deo dici non potest, qui sua infinitate omnia loca replet, ut supra dictum est. Sic igitur in omni creatura est potentia ad mutationem, vel secundum esse substantiale, sicut corpora corruptibilia; vel secundum esse locale tantum, sicut corpora caelestia, vel secundum ordinem ad finem et applicationem virtutis ad diversa, sicut in Angelis. Et universaliter omnes creaturae communiter sunt mutabiles secundum potentiam creantis, in cuius potestate est esse et non esse earum. Unde, cum Deus nullo istorum modorum sit mutabilis, proprium eius est omnino immutabilem esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0037","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de eo quod est mutabile secundum esse substantiale vel accidentale, de tali enim motu philosophi tractaverunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0039","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angeli boni, supra immutabilitatem essendi, quae competit eis secundum naturam, habent immutabilitatem electionis ex divina virtute, tamen remanet in eis mutabilitas secundum locum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0041","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod formae dicuntur invariabiles, quia non possunt esse subiectum variationis, subiiciuntur tamen variationi, inquantum subiectum secundum eas variatur. Unde patet quod secundum quod sunt, sic variantur, non enim dicuntur entia quasi sint subiectum essendi, sed quia eis aliquid est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"70b0a990eb098c514cbc14e3e3c5c5633288218400b335d0edb877196529cc4e","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T134113236742Z_517d3b22","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 9","source_sha256":"70b0a990eb098c514cbc14e3e3c5c5633288218400b335d0edb877196529cc4e","sha256":"a55ff0baf781805dfc9384a7bbdedc5280cf3efb61058297808909e16404738c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:41:13.236814+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0002","text":"[I q. 9 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0003","text":"Consequently, we must consider the divine immutability and eternity, the latter of which follows upon immutability. Concerning immutability, however, two questions are asked. First, whether God is altogether immutable. Second, whether to be immutable is proper to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0005","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that God is not altogether immutable. For whatever moves itself is mutable in some way. But, as Augustine says, in Book VIII of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, *the creator Spirit moves Himself neither through time nor through place*. Therefore God is mutable in some way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0007","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0008","text":"Further, it is said of wisdom in Wisdom VII that it is *more mobile than all movable things*. But God is wisdom itself. Therefore God is mobile.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0009","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0010","text":"Further, to draw near and to withdraw signify motion. But things of this sort are said of God in Scripture, James IV: *Draw near to God, and He will draw near to you*. Therefore God is mutable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0011","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0012","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Malachi III: *I am God, and I do not change*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0013","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that, from what has been set forth above, God is shown to be altogether immutable. First, indeed, because it was shown above that there is a certain first being, which we call God, and that such a first being must be pure act without any admixture of potency, since potency is simply subsequent to act. But everything that is changed in any way is in potency in some way. From this it is clear that it is impossible for God to be changed in any way. Second, because everything that is moved, in one respect remains and in another passes away, as that which is moved from whiteness to blackness remains according to substance. And thus, in everything that is moved, some composition is observed. But it was shown above that in God there is no composition, but He is altogether simple. Hence it is manifest that God cannot be moved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","text":"Third, because everything that is moved acquires something by its motion and reaches that to which it had not previously reached. But God, since He is infinite, comprehending in Himself all the fullness of the perfection of all being, cannot acquire anything, nor extend Himself to anything to which He had not previously reached. Hence motion is in no way fitting to Him. And from this it is that certain of the ancients, as though compelled by truth itself, held the first principle to be immovable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0016","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine there speaks according to the manner in which Plato said that the first mover moves itself, calling every operation motion; according to which even understanding itself, willing, and loving are called certain motions. Therefore, because God understands and loves Himself, according to this they said that God moves Himself, but not according as motion and change belong to that which exists in potency, as we are now speaking of change and motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0018","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that wisdom is called mobile by way of likeness, insofar as it diffuses its likeness even to the lowest things. For nothing can exist that does not proceed from divine wisdom through a certain imitation, as from a first effective and formal principle; just as artefacts proceed from the wisdom of the artisan. Thus, therefore, insofar as the likeness of divine wisdom proceeds by degrees from the highest things, which participate more in its likeness, down to the lowest things, which participate less, there is said to be a certain procession and motion of divine wisdom into things, as if we were to say that the sun proceeds as far as the earth, insofar as its ray of light reaches as far as the earth. And Dionysius explains it in this way, in chapter I of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, saying that *every procession of divine manifestation comes to us from the Father of lights moved*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0020","text":"[I q. 9 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that things of this sort are said of God metaphorically in the Scriptures. For just as the sun is said to enter a house or depart from it insofar as its ray reaches the house, so God is said to draw near to us or withdraw from us insofar as we perceive the influence of His goodness or fall away from Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0023","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that to be immutable is not proper to God. For the Philosopher says, in Book II of the *Metaphysics*, that matter is in everything that is moved. But certain created substances, such as angels and souls, do not have matter, as seems to some. Therefore to be immutable is not proper to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0025","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0026","text":"Moreover, everything that is moved is moved for the sake of some end; therefore, what has already arrived at its ultimate end is not moved. But some creatures have already arrived at their ultimate end, as all the blessed have. Therefore, some creatures are immovable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0027","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0028","text":"Moreover, everything that is mutable is variable. But forms are invariable; for it is said in the Book of Six Principles that *form consists in a simple and invariable essence*. Therefore, it is not proper to God alone to be immutable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0029","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0030","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says in the book On the Nature of the Good: *God alone is immutable; but the things that He made, because they are from nothing, are mutable*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0031","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that God alone is altogether immutable, whereas every creature is mutable in some respect. For it must be known that something can be called mutable in two ways: in one way, through a potency that is in itself; in another way, through a potency that is in another. For all creatures, before they existed, were not able to exist through any created potency, since no created thing is eternal, but through divine potency alone, insofar as God was able to bring them forth into being. Now just as it depends upon God's will that He brings things forth into being, so it depends upon His will that He preserves things in being; for He preserves them in being in no other way than by always giving them being. Hence, if He were to withdraw His action from them, all things would be reduced to nothing, as is clear from Augustine, IV, On Genesis according to the Letter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","text":"Therefore, just as it was in the power of the Creator that things should exist before they existed in themselves, so, after they exist in themselves, it is in the power of the Creator that they should not exist. Thus, through the potency that is in another, namely in God, they are mutable, insofar as they could be produced by Him from nothing into being, and can be led back from being into non-being. But if something is called mutable through a potency existing in itself, then in this way also every creature is mutable in some respect. For there is a twofold potency in a creature, namely active and passive. By passive potency I mean that according to which something can attain its perfection, either in being or in attaining its end. If, therefore, the mutability of a thing is considered according to potency for being, then mutability is not in all creatures, but only in those in which that which is possible in them can coexist with non-being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","text":"Hence, in lower bodies there is mutability both with respect to substantial being, because their matter can coexist with the privation of their substantial form, and with respect to accidental being, if the subject can coexist with the privation of the accident; just as this subject, man, can coexist with not-white, and therefore can be changed from white to not-white. But if there is such an accident as follows upon the essential principles of the subject, the privation of that accident cannot coexist with the subject; hence the subject cannot be changed with respect to that accident, just as snow cannot become black. But in the heavenly bodies, matter cannot coexist with the privation of form, because form perfects the entire potentiality of matter; and therefore they are not mutable with respect to substantial being, but with respect to local being, because the subject can coexist with the privation of this place or that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","text":"But incorporeal substances, because they are themselves subsisting forms, which nevertheless stand in relation to their being as potency to act, cannot coexist with the privation of this act, because being follows upon form, and nothing is corrupted except through losing form. Hence, in form itself there is no potency toward non-being, and therefore substances of this kind are immutable and invariable with respect to being. And this is what Dionysius says, chapter IV of On the Divine Names: that *created intellectual substances are free from generation and from every variation, as incorporeal and immaterial*. Yet a twofold mutability remains in them. One is according as they are in potency toward an end; and thus there is in them mutability according to choice from good to evil, as Damascene says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","text":"The other is according to place, insofar as by their finite power they can reach certain places which they did not previously reach; this cannot be said of God, who by His infinity fills all places, as was said above. Thus, therefore, in every creature there is a potency for change: either according to substantial being, as in corruptible bodies; or according to local being only, as in heavenly bodies; or according to order to an end and the application of power to diverse things, as in angels. And universally, all creatures are commonly mutable according to the power of the Creator, in whose power are their being and non-being. Hence, since God is mutable in none of these ways, it is proper to Him to be altogether immutable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0037","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that objection proceeds concerning what is mutable according to substantial or accidental being; for the philosophers treated of motion of that sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0039","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","text":"To the second it must be said that the good angels, beyond the immutability of being which belongs to them according to nature, have immutability of choice from divine power; nevertheless, mutability according to place remains in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0041","text":"[I q. 9 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042","text":"To the third it must be said that forms are called invariable because they cannot be the subject of variation; yet they are subjected to variation insofar as the subject is varied according to them. Hence it is clear that they are varied according as they are; for they are not called beings as though they were the subject of being, but because something is by them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042"]}],"source_digest":"4b3576174a6bf4bebffe0028beeffac1043577b5757cdc6295e9d9db1126cbda","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 9"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 9","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2","title":"First Part · Question 10","order":11,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 10 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28588)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de aeternitate. Et circa hoc quaeruntur sex. Primo, quid sit aeternitas. Secundo, utrum Deus sit aeternus. Tertio, utrum esse aeternum sit proprium Dei. Quarto, utrum aeternitas differat a tempore. Quinto, de differentia aevi et temporis. Sexto, utrum sit unum aevum tantum, sicut est unum tempus et una aeternitas.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28589)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit conveniens definitio aeternitatis, quam Boetius ponit V de consolatione, dicens quod *aeternitas est interminabilis vitae tota simul et perfecta possessio*. Interminabile enim negative dicitur. Sed negatio non est de ratione nisi eorum quae sunt deficientia, quod aeternitati non competit. Ergo in definitione aeternitatis non debet poni interminabile.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28590)\n\nPraeterea, aeternitas durationem quandam significat. Duratio autem magis respicit esse quam vitam. Ergo non debuit poni in definitione aeternitatis vita, sed magis esse.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28591)\n\nPraeterea, totum dicitur quod habet partes. Hoc autem aeternitati non convenit, cum sit simplex. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur tota.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28592)\n\nPraeterea, plures dies non possunt esse simul, nec plura tempora. Sed in aeternitate pluraliter dicuntur dies et tempora, dicitur enim Micheae V, *egressus eius ab initio, a diebus aeternitatis*; et ad Rom., XVI cap., *secundum revelationem mysterii temporibus aeternis taciti*. Ergo aeternitas non est tota simul.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28593)\n\nPraeterea, totum et perfectum sunt idem. Posito igitur quod sit tota, superflue additur quod sit perfecta.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28594)\n\nPraeterea, possessio ad durationem non pertinet. Aeternitas autem quaedam duratio est. Ergo aeternitas non est possessio.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28595)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut in cognitionem simplicium oportet nos venire per composita, ita in cognitionem aeternitatis oportet nos venire per tempus; quod nihil aliud est quam numerus motus secundum prius et posterius. Cum enim in quolibet motu sit successio, et una pars post alteram, ex hoc quod numeramus prius et posterius in motu, apprehendimus tempus; quod nihil aliud est quam numerus prioris et posterioris in motu. In eo autem quod caret motu, et semper eodem modo se habet, non est accipere prius et posterius. Sicut igitur ratio temporis consistit in numeratione prioris et posterioris in motu, ita in apprehensione uniformitatis eius quod est omnino extra motum, consistit ratio aeternitatis. Item, ea dicuntur tempore mensurari, quae principium et finem habent in tempore, ut dicitur in IV Physic., et hoc ideo, quia in omni eo quod movetur est accipere aliquod principium et aliquem finem. Quod vero est omnino immutabile, sicut nec successionem, ita nec principium aut finem habere potest. Sic ergo ex duobus notificatur aeternitas. Primo, ex hoc quod id quod est in aeternitate, est interminabile, idest principio et fine carens (ut terminus ad utrumque referatur). Secundo, per hoc quod ipsa aeternitas successione caret, tota simul existens.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28596)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod simplicia consueverunt per negationem definiri, sicut punctus est cuius pars non est. Quod non ideo est, quod negatio sit de essentia eorum, sed quia intellectus noster, qui primo apprehendit composita, in cognitionem simplicium pervenire non potest, nisi per remotionem compositionis.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28597)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illud quod est vere aeternum, non solum est ens, sed vivens, et ipsum vivere se extendit quodammodo ad operationem, non autem esse. Protensio autem durationis videtur attendi secundum operationem, magis quam secundum esse, unde et tempus est numerus motus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28598)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aeternitas dicitur tota, non quia habet partes, sed inquantum nihil ei deest.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28599)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut Deus, cum sit incorporeus, nominibus rerum corporalium metaphorice in Scripturis nominatur, sic aeternitas, tota simul existens, nominibus temporalibus successivis.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28600)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod in tempore est duo considerare, scilicet ipsum tempus, quod est successivum; et nunc temporis, quod est imperfectum. Dicit ergo tota simul, ad removendum tempus, et perfecta, ad excludendum nunc temporis.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28601)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod illud quod possidetur, firmiter et quiete habetur. Ad designandam ergo immutabilitatem et indeficientiam aeternitatis, usus est nomine possessionis.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28602)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit aeternus. Nihil enim factum potest dici de Deo. Sed aeternitas est aliquid factum, dicit enim Boetius quod *nunc fluens facit tempus, nunc stans facit aeternitatem*; et Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod Deus est auctor aeternitatis. Ergo Deus non est aeternus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28603)\n\nPraeterea, quod est ante aeternitatem et post aeternitatem, non mensuratur aeternitate. Sed Deus est ante aeternitatem, ut dicitur in libro de causis, et post aeternitatem; dicitur enim Exod. XV, quod *dominus regnabit in aeternum et ultra*. Ergo esse aeternum non convenit Deo.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28604)\n\nPraeterea, aeternitas mensura quaedam est. Sed Deo non convenit esse mensuratum. Ergo non competit ei esse aeternum.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28605)\n\nPraeterea, in aeternitate non est praesens, praeteritum vel futurum cum sit tota simul, ut dictum est. Sed de Deo dicuntur in Scripturis verba praesentis temporis, praeteriti vel futuri. Ergo Deus non est aeternus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28606)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Athanasius, *aeternus pater, aeternus filius, aeternus spiritus sanctus*.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28607)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ratio aeternitatis consequitur immutabilitatem, sicut ratio temporis consequitur motum, ut ex dictis patet. Unde, cum Deus sit maxime immutabilis, sibi maxime competit esse aeternum. Nec solum est aeternus, sed est sua aeternitas, cum tamen nulla alia res sit sua duratio, quia non est suum esse. Deus autem est suum esse uniforme, unde, sicut est sua essentia, ita est sua aeternitas.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28608)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod nunc stans dicitur facere aeternitatem, secundum nostram apprehensionem. Sicut enim causatur in nobis apprehensio temporis, eo quod apprehendimus fluxum ipsius nunc, ita causatur in nobis apprehensio aeternitatis, inquantum apprehendimus nunc stans. Quod autem dicit Augustinus, quod *Deus est auctor aeternitatis*, intelligitur de aeternitate participata, eo enim modo communicat Deus suam aeternitatem aliquibus, quo et suam immutabilitatem.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28609)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad secundum. Nam Deus dicitur esse ante aeternitatem, prout participatur a substantiis immaterialibus. Unde et ibidem dicitur, quod *intelligentia parificatur aeternitati*. Quod autem dicitur in Exodo, *dominus regnabit in aeternum et ultra* sciendum quod aeternum accipitur ibi pro saeculo, sicut habet alia translatio. Sic igitur dicitur quod regnabit ultra aeternum, quia durat ultra quodcumque saeculum, idest ultra quamcumque durationem datam, nihil est enim aliud saeculum quam periodus cuiuslibet rei, ut dicitur in libro I de caelo. Vel dicitur etiam ultra aeternum regnare, quia, si etiam aliquid aliud semper esset (ut motus caeli secundum quosdam philosophos), tamen Deus ultra regnat, inquantum eius regnum est totum simul.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28610)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aeternitas non est aliud quam ipse Deus. Unde non dicitur Deus aeternus, quasi sit aliquo modo mensuratus, sed accipitur ibi ratio mensurae secundum apprehensionem nostram tantum.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28611)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod verba diversorum temporum attribuuntur Deo, inquantum eius aeternitas omnia tempora includit, non quod ipse varietur per praesens, praeteritum et futurum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28612)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse aeternum non sit soli Deo proprium. Dicitur enim Danielis XII, quod *qui ad iustitiam erudiunt plurimos, erunt quasi stellae in perpetuas aeternitates*. Non autem essent plures aeternitates, si solus Deus esset aeternus. Non igitur solus Deus est aeternus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28613)\n\nPraeterea, Matth. XXV dicitur, *ite, maledicti, in ignem aeternum*. Non igitur solus Deus est aeternus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28614)\n\nPraeterea, omne necessarium est aeternum. Sed multa sunt necessaria; sicut omnia principia demonstrationis, et omnes propositiones demonstrativae. Ergo non solus Deus est aeternus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28615)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Hieronymus, ad Marcellam, *Deus solus est qui exordium non habet*. Quidquid autem exordium habet, non est aeternum. Solus ergo Deus est aeternus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28616)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aeternitas vere et proprie in solo Deo est. Quia aeternitas immutabilitatem consequitur, ut ex dictis patet. Solus autem Deus est omnino immutabilis, ut est superius ostensum. Secundum tamen quod aliqua ab ipso immutabilitatem percipiunt, secundum hoc aliqua eius aeternitatem participant. Quaedam ergo quantum ad hoc immutabilitatem sortiuntur a Deo, quod nunquam esse desinunt, et secundum hoc dicitur Eccle. I de terra, quod in aeternum stat. Quaedam etiam aeterna in Scripturis dicuntur propter diuturnitatem durationis, licet corruptibilia sint, sicut in Psalmo dicuntur montes aeterni; et Deuter. XXXIII etiam dicitur, de pomis collium aeternorum. Quaedam autem amplius participant de ratione aeternitatis, inquantum habent intransmutabilitatem vel secundum esse, vel ulterius secundum operationem, sicut Angeli et beati, qui verbo fruuntur, quia quantum ad illam visionem verbi, non sunt in sanctis volubiles cogitationes, ut dicit Augustinus, XV de Trin. Unde et videntes Deum dicuntur habere vitam aeternam, secundum illud Ioann. XVII, *haec est vita aeterna, ut cognoscant* et cetera.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28617)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dicuntur multae aeternitates, secundum quod sunt multi participantes aeternitatem ex ipsa Dei contemplatione.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28618)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ignis Inferni dicitur aeternus propter interminabilitatem tantum. Est tamen in poenis eorum transmutatio, secundum illud Iob XXIV, *ad nimium calorem transibunt ab aquis nivium*. Unde in Inferno non est vera aeternitas, sed magis tempus; secundum illud Psalmi, *erit tempus eorum in saecula*.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28619)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod necessarium significat quendam modum veritatis. Verum autem, secundum philosophum, VI Metaphys., est in intellectu. Secundum hoc igitur vera et necessaria sunt aeterna, quia sunt in intellectu aeterno, qui est intellectus divinus solus. Unde non sequitur quod aliquid extra Deum sit aeternum.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28620)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aeternitas non sit aliud a tempore. Impossibile est enim duas esse mensuras durationis simul, nisi una sit pars alterius, non enim sunt simul duo dies vel duae horae; sed dies et hora sunt simul, quia hora est pars diei. Sed aeternitas et tempus sunt simul, quorum utrumque mensuram quandam durationis importat. Cum igitur aeternitas non sit pars temporis, quia aeternitas excedit tempus et includit ipsum; videtur quod tempus sit pars aeternitatis, et non aliud ab aeternitate.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28621)\n\nPraeterea, secundum philosophum in IV Physic., nunc temporis manet idem in toto tempore. Sed hoc videtur constituere rationem aeternitatis, quod sit idem indivisibiliter se habens in toto decursu temporis. Ergo aeternitas est nunc temporis. Sed nunc temporis non est aliud secundum substantiam a tempore. Ergo aeternitas non est aliud secundum substantiam a tempore.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28622)\n\nPraeterea, sicut mensura primi motus est mensura omnium motuum, ut dicitur in IV Physic., ita videtur quod mensura primi esse sit mensura omnis esse. Sed aeternitas est mensura primi esse, quod est esse divinum. Ergo aeternitas est mensura omnis esse. Sed esse rerum corruptibilium mensuratur tempore. Ergo tempus vel est aeternitas, vel aliquid aeternitatis.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28623)\n\nSed contra est quod aeternitas est tota simul, in tempore autem est prius et posterius. Ergo tempus et aeternitas non sunt idem.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28624)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod manifestum est tempus et aeternitatem non esse idem. Sed huius diversitatis rationem quidam assignaverunt ex hoc quod aeternitas caret principio et fine, tempus autem habet principium et finem. Sed haec est differentia per accidens, et non per se. Quia dato quod tempus semper fuerit et semper futurum sit, secundum positionem eorum qui motum caeli ponunt sempiternum, adhuc remanebit differentia inter aeternitatem et tempus, ut dicit Boetius in libro de Consolat., ex hoc quod aeternitas est tota simul, quod tempori non convenit, quia aeternitas est mensura esse permanentis, tempus vero est mensura motus. Si tamen praedicta differentia attendatur quantum ad mensurata, et non quantum ad mensuras, sic habet aliquam rationem, quia solum illud mensuratur tempore, quod habet principium et finem in tempore, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Unde si motus caeli semper duraret, tempus non mensuraret ipsum secundum suam totam durationem, cum infinitum non sit mensurabile; sed mensuraret quamlibet circulationem, quae habet principium et finem in tempore. Potest tamen et aliam rationem habere ex parte istarum mensurarum, si accipiatur finis et principium in potentia. Quia etiam dato quod tempus semper duret, tamen possibile est signare in tempore et principium et finem, accipiendo aliquas partes ipsius, sicut dicimus principium et finem diei vel anni, quod non contingit in aeternitate. Sed tamen istae differentiae consequuntur eam quae est per se et primo, differentiam, per hoc quod aeternitas est tota simul, non autem tempus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28625)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si tempus et aeternitas essent mensurae unius generis, quod patet esse falsum, ex his quorum est tempus et aeternitas mensura.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28626)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nunc temporis est idem subiecto in toto tempore, sed differens ratione, eo quod, sicut tempus respondet motui, ita nunc temporis respondet mobili; mobile autem est idem subiecto in toto decursu temporis, sed differens ratione, inquantum est hic et ibi. Et ista alternatio est motus. Similiter fluxus ipsius nunc, secundum quod alternatur ratione, est tempus. Aeternitas autem manet eadem et subiecto et ratione. Unde aeternitas non est idem quod nunc temporis.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28627)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut aeternitas est propria mensura ipsius esse, ita tempus est propria mensura motus. Unde secundum quod aliquod esse recedit a permanentia essendi et subditur transmutationi, secundum hoc recedit ab aeternitate et subditur tempori. Esse ergo rerum corruptibilium, quia est transmutabile, non mensuratur aeternitate, sed tempore. Tempus enim mensurat non solum quae transmutantur in actu, sed quae sunt transmutabilia. Unde non solum mensurat motum, sed etiam quietem; quae est eius quod natum est moveri, et non movetur.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28628)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aevum non sit aliud a tempore. Dicit enim Augustinus, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Deus movet creaturam spiritualem per tempus*. Sed aevum dicitur esse mensura spiritualium substantiarum. Ergo tempus non differt ab aevo.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28629)\n\nPraeterea, de ratione temporis est quod habeat prius et posterius, de ratione vero aeternitatis est quod sit tota simul, ut dictum est. Sed aevum non est aeternitas, dicitur enim Eccli. I, quod sapientia aeterna est ante aevum. Ergo non est totum simul, sed habet prius et posterius, et ita est tempus.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28630)\n\nPraeterea, si in aevo non est prius et posterius, sequitur quod in aeviternis non differat esse vel fuisse vel futurum esse. Cum igitur sit impossibile aeviterna non fuisse, sequitur quod impossibile sit ea non futura esse. Quod falsum est, cum Deus possit ea reducere in nihilum.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28631)\n\nPraeterea, cum duratio aeviternorum sit infinita ex parte post, si aevum sit totum simul, sequitur quod aliquod creatum sit infinitum in actu, quod est impossibile. Non igitur aevum differt a tempore.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28632)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Boetius, *qui tempus ab aevo ire iubes*.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28633)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aevum differt a tempore et ab aeternitate, sicut medium existens inter illa. Sed horum differentiam aliqui sic assignant, dicentes quod aeternitas principio et fine caret; aevum habet principium, sed non finem; tempus autem habet principium et finem. Sed haec differentia est per accidens, sicut supra dictum est, quia si etiam semper aeviterna fuissent et semper futura essent, ut aliqui ponunt; vel etiam si quandoque deficerent, quod Deo possibile esset, adhuc aevum distingueretur ab aeternitate et tempore. Alii vero assignant differentiam inter haec tria, per hoc quod aeternitas non habet prius et posterius; tempus autem habet prius et posterius cum innovatione et veteratione; aevum habet prius et posterius sine innovatione et veteratione. Sed haec positio implicat contradictoria. Quod quidem manifeste apparet, si innovatio et veteratio referantur ad ipsam mensuram. Cum enim prius et posterius durationis non possint esse simul, si aevum habet prius et posterius, oportet quod, priore parte aevi recedente, posterior de novo adveniat, et sic erit innovatio in ipso aevo, sicut in tempore. Si vero referantur ad mensurata, adhuc sequitur inconveniens. Ex hoc enim res temporalis inveteratur tempore, quod habet esse transmutabile, et ex transmutabilitate mensurati, est prius et posterius in mensura, ut patet ex IV Physic. Si igitur ipsum aeviternum non sit inveterabile nec innovabile, hoc erit quia esse eius est intransmutabile. Mensura ergo eius non habebit prius et posterius. Est ergo dicendum quod, cum aeternitas sit mensura esse permanentis, secundum quod aliquid recedit a permanentia essendi, secundum hoc recedit ab aeternitate. Quaedam autem sic recedunt a permanentia essendi, quod esse eorum est subiectum transmutationis, vel in transmutatione consistit, et huiusmodi mensurantur tempore; sicut omnis motus, et etiam esse omnium corruptibilium. Quaedam vero recedunt minus a permanentia essendi, quia esse eorum nec in transmutatione consistit, nec est subiectum transmutationis, tamen habent transmutationem adiunctam, vel in actu vel in potentia. Sicut patet in corporibus caelestibus, quorum esse substantiale est intransmutabile; tamen esse intransmutabile habent cum transmutabilitate secundum locum. Et similiter patet de Angelis, quod habent esse intransmutabile cum transmutabilitate secundum electionem, quantum ad eorum naturam pertinet; et cum transmutabilitate intelligentiarum et affectionum, et locorum suo modo. Et ideo huiusmodi mensurantur aevo, quod est medium inter aeternitatem et tempus. Esse autem quod mensurat aeternitas, nec est mutabile, nec mutabilitati adiunctum. Sic ergo tempus habet prius et posterius, aevum autem non habet in se prius et posterius, sed ei coniungi possunt, aeternitas autem non habet prius neque posterius, neque ea compatitur.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28634)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod creaturae spirituales, quantum ad affectiones et intelligentias, in quibus est successio, mensurantur tempore. Unde et Augustinus ibidem dicit quod per tempus moveri, est per affectiones moveri. Quantum vero ad eorum esse naturale, mensurantur aevo. Sed quantum ad visionem gloriae, participant aeternitatem.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28635)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aevum est totum simul, non tamen est aeternitas, quia compatitur secum prius et posterius.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28636)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in ipso esse Angeli in se considerato, non est differentia praeteriti et futuri, sed solum secundum adiunctas mutationes. Sed quod dicimus Angelum esse vel fuisse vel futurum esse, differt secundum acceptionem intellectus nostri, qui accipit esse Angeli per comparationem ad diversas partes temporis. Et cum dicit Angelum esse vel fuisse, supponit aliquid cum quo eius oppositum non subditur divinae potentiae, cum vero dicit futurum esse, nondum supponit aliquid. Unde, cum esse et non esse Angeli subsit divinae potentiae, absolute considerando, potest Deus facere quod esse Angeli non sit futurum, tamen non potest facere quod non sit dum est, vel quod non fuerit postquam fuit.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28637)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod duratio aevi est infinita, quia non finitur tempore. Sic autem esse aliquod creatum infinitum, quod non finiatur quodam alio, non est inconveniens.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28638)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit tantum unum aevum. Dicitur enim in apocryphis Esdrae, *maiestas et potestas aevorum est apud te, domine*.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28639)\n\nPraeterea, diversorum generum diversae sunt mensurae. Sed quaedam aeviterna sunt in genere corporalium, scilicet corpora caelestia, quaedam vero sunt spirituales substantiae, scilicet Angeli. Non ergo est unum aevum tantum.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28640)\n\nPraeterea, cum aevum sit nomen durationis, quorum est unum aevum, est una duratio. Sed non omnium aeviternorum est una duratio, quia quaedam post alia esse incipiunt, ut maxime patet in animabus humanis. Non est ergo unum aevum tantum.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28641)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae non dependent ab invicem, non videntur habere unam mensuram durationis, propter hoc enim omnium temporalium videtur esse unum tempus, quia omnium motuum quodammodo causa est primus motus, qui prius tempore mensuratur. Sed aeviterna non dependent ab invicem, quia unus Angelus non est causa alterius. Non ergo est unum aevum tantum.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28642)\n\nSed contra, aevum est simplicius tempore, et propinquius se habens ad aeternitatem. Sed tempus est unum tantum. Ergo multo magis aevum.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28643)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc est duplex opinio, quidam enim dicunt quod est unum aevum tantum; quidam quod multa. Quid autem horum verius sit, oportet considerare ex causa unitatis temporis, in cognitionem enim spiritualium per corporalia devenimus. Dicunt autem quidam esse unum tempus omnium temporalium, propter hoc quod est unus numerus omnium numeratorum, cum tempus sit numerus, secundum philosophum. Sed hoc non sufficit, quia tempus non est numerus ut abstractus extra numeratum, sed ut in numerato existens, alioquin non esset continuus; quia decem ulnae panni continuitatem habent, non ex numero, sed ex numerato. Numerus autem in numerato existens non est idem omnium, sed diversus diversorum. Unde alii assignant causam unitatis temporis ex unitate aeternitatis, quae est principium omnis durationis. Et sic, omnes durationes sunt unum, si consideretur earum principium, sunt vero multae, si consideretur diversitas eorum quae recipiunt durationem ex influxu primi principii. Alii vero assignant causam unitatis temporis ex parte materiae primae, quae est primum subiectum motus, cuius mensura est tempus. Sed neutra assignatio sufficiens videtur, quia ea quae sunt unum principio vel subiecto, et maxime remoto, non sunt unum simpliciter sed secundum quid. Est ergo ratio unitatis temporis, unitas primi motus, secundum quem, cum sit simplicissimus, omnes alii mensurantur, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sic ergo tempus ad illum motum comparatur non solum ut mensura ad mensuratum, sed etiam ut accidens ad subiectum; et sic ab eo recipit unitatem. Ad alios autem motus comparatur solum ut mensura ad mensuratum. Unde secundum eorum multitudinem non multiplicatur, quia una mensura separata multa mensurari possunt. Hoc igitur habito, sciendum quod de substantiis spiritualibus duplex fuit opinio. Quidam enim dixerunt quod omnes processerunt a Deo in quadam aequalitate, ut Origenes dixit; vel etiam multae earum, ut quidam posuerunt. Alii vero dixerunt quod omnes substantiae spirituales processerunt a Deo quodam gradu et ordine et hoc videtur sentire Dionysius, qui dicit, cap. X Cael. Hier., quod inter substantias spirituales sunt primae, mediae et ultimae, etiam in uno ordine Angelorum. Secundum igitur primam opinionem, necesse est dicere quod sunt plura aeva, secundum quod sunt plura aeviterna prima aequalia. Secundum autem secundam opinionem, oportet dicere quod sit unum aevum tantum, quia, cum unumquodque mensuretur simplicissimo sui generis, ut dicitur in X Metaphys., oportet quod esse omnium aeviternorum mensuretur esse primi aeviterni, quod tanto est simplicius, quanto prius. Et quia secunda opinio verior est, ut infra ostendetur, concedimus ad praesens unum esse aevum tantum.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28644)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aevum aliquando accipitur pro saeculo, quod est periodus durationis alicuius rei, et sic dicuntur multa aeva, sicut multa saecula.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28645)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet corpora caelestia et spiritualia differant in genere naturae, tamen conveniunt in hoc, quod habent esse intransmutabile. Et sic mensurantur aevo.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28646)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nec omnia temporalia simul incipiunt, et tamen omnium est unum tempus, propter primum quod mensuratur tempore. Et sic omnia aeviterna habent unum aevum propter primum, etiam si non omnia simul incipiant.\n\n[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28647)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod ad hoc quod aliqua mensurentur per aliquod unum, non requiritur quod illud unum sit causa omnium eorum; sed quod sit simplicius.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0002","text":"[I q. 10 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de aeternitate. Et circa hoc quaeruntur sex. Primo, quid sit aeternitas. Secundo, utrum Deus sit aeternus. Tertio, utrum esse aeternum sit proprium Dei. Quarto, utrum aeternitas differat a tempore. Quinto, de differentia aevi et temporis. Sexto, utrum sit unum aevum tantum, sicut est unum tempus et una aeternitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0005","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit conveniens definitio aeternitatis, quam Boetius ponit V de consolatione, dicens quod *aeternitas est interminabilis vitae tota simul et perfecta possessio*. Interminabile enim negative dicitur. Sed negatio non est de ratione nisi eorum quae sunt deficientia, quod aeternitati non competit. Ergo in definitione aeternitatis non debet poni interminabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0007","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0008","text":"Praeterea, aeternitas durationem quandam significat. Duratio autem magis respicit esse quam vitam. Ergo non debuit poni in definitione aeternitatis vita, sed magis esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0009","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0010","text":"Praeterea, totum dicitur quod habet partes. Hoc autem aeternitati non convenit, cum sit simplex. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur tota.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0011","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0012","text":"Praeterea, plures dies non possunt esse simul, nec plura tempora. Sed in aeternitate pluraliter dicuntur dies et tempora, dicitur enim Micheae V, *egressus eius ab initio, a diebus aeternitatis*; et ad Rom., XVI cap., *secundum revelationem mysterii temporibus aeternis taciti*. Ergo aeternitas non est tota simul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0013","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0014","text":"Praeterea, totum et perfectum sunt idem. Posito igitur quod sit tota, superflue additur quod sit perfecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0015","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0016","text":"Praeterea, possessio ad durationem non pertinet. Aeternitas autem quaedam duratio est. Ergo aeternitas non est possessio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0017","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut in cognitionem simplicium oportet nos venire per composita, ita in cognitionem aeternitatis oportet nos venire per tempus; quod nihil aliud est quam numerus motus secundum prius et posterius. Cum enim in quolibet motu sit successio, et una pars post alteram, ex hoc quod numeramus prius et posterius in motu, apprehendimus tempus; quod nihil aliud est quam numerus prioris et posterioris in motu. In eo autem quod caret motu, et semper eodem modo se habet, non est accipere prius et posterius. Sicut igitur ratio temporis consistit in numeratione prioris et posterioris in motu, ita in apprehensione uniformitatis eius quod est omnino extra motum, consistit ratio aeternitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","text":"Item, ea dicuntur tempore mensurari, quae principium et finem habent in tempore, ut dicitur in IV Physic., et hoc ideo, quia in omni eo quod movetur est accipere aliquod principium et aliquem finem. Quod vero est omnino immutabile, sicut nec successionem, ita nec principium aut finem habere potest. Sic ergo ex duobus notificatur aeternitas. Primo, ex hoc quod id quod est in aeternitate, est interminabile, idest principio et fine carens (ut terminus ad utrumque referatur). Secundo, per hoc quod ipsa aeternitas successione caret, tota simul existens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0020","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod simplicia consueverunt per negationem definiri, sicut punctus est cuius pars non est. Quod non ideo est, quod negatio sit de essentia eorum, sed quia intellectus noster, qui primo apprehendit composita, in cognitionem simplicium pervenire non potest, nisi per remotionem compositionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0022","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0023","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illud quod est vere aeternum, non solum est ens, sed vivens, et ipsum vivere se extendit quodammodo ad operationem, non autem esse. Protensio autem durationis videtur attendi secundum operationem, magis quam secundum esse, unde et tempus est numerus motus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0024","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0025","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aeternitas dicitur tota, non quia habet partes, sed inquantum nihil ei deest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0026","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut Deus, cum sit incorporeus, nominibus rerum corporalium metaphorice in Scripturis nominatur, sic aeternitas, tota simul existens, nominibus temporalibus successivis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0028","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0029","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod in tempore est duo considerare, scilicet ipsum tempus, quod est successivum; et nunc temporis, quod est imperfectum. Dicit ergo tota simul, ad removendum tempus, et perfecta, ad excludendum nunc temporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0030","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0031","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod illud quod possidetur, firmiter et quiete habetur. Ad designandam ergo immutabilitatem et indeficientiam aeternitatis, usus est nomine possessionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0032","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0033","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0034","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit aeternus. Nihil enim factum potest dici de Deo. Sed aeternitas est aliquid factum, dicit enim Boetius quod *nunc fluens facit tempus, nunc stans facit aeternitatem*; et Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod Deus est auctor aeternitatis. Ergo Deus non est aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0035","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0036","text":"Praeterea, quod est ante aeternitatem et post aeternitatem, non mensuratur aeternitate. Sed Deus est ante aeternitatem, ut dicitur in libro de causis, et post aeternitatem; dicitur enim Exod. XV, quod *dominus regnabit in aeternum et ultra*. Ergo esse aeternum non convenit Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0037","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0038","text":"Praeterea, aeternitas mensura quaedam est. Sed Deo non convenit esse mensuratum. Ergo non competit ei esse aeternum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0039","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0040","text":"Praeterea, in aeternitate non est praesens, praeteritum vel futurum cum sit tota simul, ut dictum est. Sed de Deo dicuntur in Scripturis verba praesentis temporis, praeteriti vel futuri. Ergo Deus non est aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0041","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0042","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Athanasius, *aeternus pater, aeternus filius, aeternus spiritus sanctus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0043","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ratio aeternitatis consequitur immutabilitatem, sicut ratio temporis consequitur motum, ut ex dictis patet. Unde, cum Deus sit maxime immutabilis, sibi maxime competit esse aeternum. Nec solum est aeternus, sed est sua aeternitas, cum tamen nulla alia res sit sua duratio, quia non est suum esse. Deus autem est suum esse uniforme, unde, sicut est sua essentia, ita est sua aeternitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0045","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0046","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod nunc stans dicitur facere aeternitatem, secundum nostram apprehensionem. Sicut enim causatur in nobis apprehensio temporis, eo quod apprehendimus fluxum ipsius nunc, ita causatur in nobis apprehensio aeternitatis, inquantum apprehendimus nunc stans. Quod autem dicit Augustinus, quod *Deus est auctor aeternitatis*, intelligitur de aeternitate participata, eo enim modo communicat Deus suam aeternitatem aliquibus, quo et suam immutabilitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0047","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0048","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad secundum. Nam Deus dicitur esse ante aeternitatem, prout participatur a substantiis immaterialibus. Unde et ibidem dicitur, quod *intelligentia parificatur aeternitati*. Quod autem dicitur in Exodo, *dominus regnabit in aeternum et ultra* sciendum quod aeternum accipitur ibi pro saeculo, sicut habet alia translatio. Sic igitur dicitur quod regnabit ultra aeternum, quia durat ultra quodcumque saeculum, idest ultra quamcumque durationem datam, nihil est enim aliud saeculum quam periodus cuiuslibet rei, ut dicitur in libro I de caelo. Vel dicitur etiam ultra aeternum regnare, quia, si etiam aliquid aliud semper esset (ut motus caeli secundum quosdam philosophos), tamen Deus ultra regnat, inquantum eius regnum est totum simul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0049","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aeternitas non est aliud quam ipse Deus. Unde non dicitur Deus aeternus, quasi sit aliquo modo mensuratus, sed accipitur ibi ratio mensurae secundum apprehensionem nostram tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0051","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod verba diversorum temporum attribuuntur Deo, inquantum eius aeternitas omnia tempora includit, non quod ipse varietur per praesens, praeteritum et futurum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0053","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0054","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0055","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse aeternum non sit soli Deo proprium. Dicitur enim Danielis XII, quod *qui ad iustitiam erudiunt plurimos, erunt quasi stellae in perpetuas aeternitates*. Non autem essent plures aeternitates, si solus Deus esset aeternus. Non igitur solus Deus est aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0056","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0057","text":"Praeterea, Matth. XXV dicitur, *ite, maledicti, in ignem aeternum*. Non igitur solus Deus est aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0058","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0059","text":"Praeterea, omne necessarium est aeternum. Sed multa sunt necessaria; sicut omnia principia demonstrationis, et omnes propositiones demonstrativae. Ergo non solus Deus est aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0060","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0061","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Hieronymus, ad Marcellam, *Deus solus est qui exordium non habet*. Quidquid autem exordium habet, non est aeternum. Solus ergo Deus est aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0062","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aeternitas vere et proprie in solo Deo est. Quia aeternitas immutabilitatem consequitur, ut ex dictis patet. Solus autem Deus est omnino immutabilis, ut est superius ostensum. Secundum tamen quod aliqua ab ipso immutabilitatem percipiunt, secundum hoc aliqua eius aeternitatem participant. Quaedam ergo quantum ad hoc immutabilitatem sortiuntur a Deo, quod nunquam esse desinunt, et secundum hoc dicitur Eccle. I de terra, quod in aeternum stat. Quaedam etiam aeterna in Scripturis dicuntur propter diuturnitatem durationis, licet corruptibilia sint, sicut in Psalmo dicuntur montes aeterni; et Deuter. XXXIII etiam dicitur, de pomis collium aeternorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","text":"Quaedam autem amplius participant de ratione aeternitatis, inquantum habent intransmutabilitatem vel secundum esse, vel ulterius secundum operationem, sicut Angeli et beati, qui verbo fruuntur, quia quantum ad illam visionem verbi, non sunt in sanctis volubiles cogitationes, ut dicit Augustinus, XV de Trin. Unde et videntes Deum dicuntur habere vitam aeternam, secundum illud Ioann. XVII, *haec est vita aeterna, ut cognoscant* et cetera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0065","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0066","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dicuntur multae aeternitates, secundum quod sunt multi participantes aeternitatem ex ipsa Dei contemplatione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0067","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ignis Inferni dicitur aeternus propter interminabilitatem tantum. Est tamen in poenis eorum transmutatio, secundum illud Iob XXIV, *ad nimium calorem transibunt ab aquis nivium*. Unde in Inferno non est vera aeternitas, sed magis tempus; secundum illud Psalmi, *erit tempus eorum in saecula*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0069","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod necessarium significat quendam modum veritatis. Verum autem, secundum philosophum, VI Metaphys., est in intellectu. Secundum hoc igitur vera et necessaria sunt aeterna, quia sunt in intellectu aeterno, qui est intellectus divinus solus. Unde non sequitur quod aliquid extra Deum sit aeternum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0071","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0072","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0073","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aeternitas non sit aliud a tempore. Impossibile est enim duas esse mensuras durationis simul, nisi una sit pars alterius, non enim sunt simul duo dies vel duae horae; sed dies et hora sunt simul, quia hora est pars diei. Sed aeternitas et tempus sunt simul, quorum utrumque mensuram quandam durationis importat. Cum igitur aeternitas non sit pars temporis, quia aeternitas excedit tempus et includit ipsum; videtur quod tempus sit pars aeternitatis, et non aliud ab aeternitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0074","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0075","text":"Praeterea, secundum philosophum in IV Physic., nunc temporis manet idem in toto tempore. Sed hoc videtur constituere rationem aeternitatis, quod sit idem indivisibiliter se habens in toto decursu temporis. Ergo aeternitas est nunc temporis. Sed nunc temporis non est aliud secundum substantiam a tempore. Ergo aeternitas non est aliud secundum substantiam a tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0076","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0077","text":"Praeterea, sicut mensura primi motus est mensura omnium motuum, ut dicitur in IV Physic., ita videtur quod mensura primi esse sit mensura omnis esse. Sed aeternitas est mensura primi esse, quod est esse divinum. Ergo aeternitas est mensura omnis esse. Sed esse rerum corruptibilium mensuratur tempore. Ergo tempus vel est aeternitas, vel aliquid aeternitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0078","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0079","text":"Sed contra est quod aeternitas est tota simul, in tempore autem est prius et posterius. Ergo tempus et aeternitas non sunt idem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0080","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod manifestum est tempus et aeternitatem non esse idem. Sed huius diversitatis rationem quidam assignaverunt ex hoc quod aeternitas caret principio et fine, tempus autem habet principium et finem. Sed haec est differentia per accidens, et non per se. Quia dato quod tempus semper fuerit et semper futurum sit, secundum positionem eorum qui motum caeli ponunt sempiternum, adhuc remanebit differentia inter aeternitatem et tempus, ut dicit Boetius in libro de Consolat., ex hoc quod aeternitas est tota simul, quod tempori non convenit, quia aeternitas est mensura esse permanentis, tempus vero est mensura motus. Si tamen praedicta differentia attendatur quantum ad mensurata, et non quantum ad mensuras, sic habet aliquam rationem, quia solum illud mensuratur tempore, quod habet principium et finem in tempore, ut dicitur in IV Physic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0082","text":"Unde si motus caeli semper duraret, tempus non mensuraret ipsum secundum suam totam durationem, cum infinitum non sit mensurabile; sed mensuraret quamlibet circulationem, quae habet principium et finem in tempore. Potest tamen et aliam rationem habere ex parte istarum mensurarum, si accipiatur finis et principium in potentia. Quia etiam dato quod tempus semper duret, tamen possibile est signare in tempore et principium et finem, accipiendo aliquas partes ipsius, sicut dicimus principium et finem diei vel anni, quod non contingit in aeternitate. Sed tamen istae differentiae consequuntur eam quae est per se et primo, differentiam, per hoc quod aeternitas est tota simul, non autem tempus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0083","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si tempus et aeternitas essent mensurae unius generis, quod patet esse falsum, ex his quorum est tempus et aeternitas mensura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0085","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0086","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nunc temporis est idem subiecto in toto tempore, sed differens ratione, eo quod, sicut tempus respondet motui, ita nunc temporis respondet mobili; mobile autem est idem subiecto in toto decursu temporis, sed differens ratione, inquantum est hic et ibi. Et ista alternatio est motus. Similiter fluxus ipsius nunc, secundum quod alternatur ratione, est tempus. Aeternitas autem manet eadem et subiecto et ratione. Unde aeternitas non est idem quod nunc temporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0087","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut aeternitas est propria mensura ipsius esse, ita tempus est propria mensura motus. Unde secundum quod aliquod esse recedit a permanentia essendi et subditur transmutationi, secundum hoc recedit ab aeternitate et subditur tempori. Esse ergo rerum corruptibilium, quia est transmutabile, non mensuratur aeternitate, sed tempore. Tempus enim mensurat non solum quae transmutantur in actu, sed quae sunt transmutabilia. Unde non solum mensurat motum, sed etiam quietem; quae est eius quod natum est moveri, et non movetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0089","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0090","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0091","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aevum non sit aliud a tempore. Dicit enim Augustinus, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Deus movet creaturam spiritualem per tempus*. Sed aevum dicitur esse mensura spiritualium substantiarum. Ergo tempus non differt ab aevo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0092","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0093","text":"Praeterea, de ratione temporis est quod habeat prius et posterius, de ratione vero aeternitatis est quod sit tota simul, ut dictum est. Sed aevum non est aeternitas, dicitur enim Eccli. I, quod sapientia aeterna est ante aevum. Ergo non est totum simul, sed habet prius et posterius, et ita est tempus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0094","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0095","text":"Praeterea, si in aevo non est prius et posterius, sequitur quod in aeviternis non differat esse vel fuisse vel futurum esse. Cum igitur sit impossibile aeviterna non fuisse, sequitur quod impossibile sit ea non futura esse. Quod falsum est, cum Deus possit ea reducere in nihilum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0096","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0097","text":"Praeterea, cum duratio aeviternorum sit infinita ex parte post, si aevum sit totum simul, sequitur quod aliquod creatum sit infinitum in actu, quod est impossibile. Non igitur aevum differt a tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0098","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0099","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Boetius, *qui tempus ab aevo ire iubes*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0100","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aevum differt a tempore et ab aeternitate, sicut medium existens inter illa. Sed horum differentiam aliqui sic assignant, dicentes quod aeternitas principio et fine caret; aevum habet principium, sed non finem; tempus autem habet principium et finem. Sed haec differentia est per accidens, sicut supra dictum est, quia si etiam semper aeviterna fuissent et semper futura essent, ut aliqui ponunt; vel etiam si quandoque deficerent, quod Deo possibile esset, adhuc aevum distingueretur ab aeternitate et tempore. Alii vero assignant differentiam inter haec tria, per hoc quod aeternitas non habet prius et posterius; tempus autem habet prius et posterius cum innovatione et veteratione; aevum habet prius et posterius sine innovatione et veteratione. Sed haec positio implicat contradictoria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102","text":"Quod quidem manifeste apparet, si innovatio et veteratio referantur ad ipsam mensuram. Cum enim prius et posterius durationis non possint esse simul, si aevum habet prius et posterius, oportet quod, priore parte aevi recedente, posterior de novo adveniat, et sic erit innovatio in ipso aevo, sicut in tempore. Si vero referantur ad mensurata, adhuc sequitur inconveniens. Ex hoc enim res temporalis inveteratur tempore, quod habet esse transmutabile, et ex transmutabilitate mensurati, est prius et posterius in mensura, ut patet ex IV Physic. Si igitur ipsum aeviternum non sit inveterabile nec innovabile, hoc erit quia esse eius est intransmutabile. Mensura ergo eius non habebit prius et posterius. Est ergo dicendum quod, cum aeternitas sit mensura esse permanentis, secundum quod aliquid recedit a permanentia essendi, secundum hoc recedit ab aeternitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","text":"Quaedam autem sic recedunt a permanentia essendi, quod esse eorum est subiectum transmutationis, vel in transmutatione consistit, et huiusmodi mensurantur tempore; sicut omnis motus, et etiam esse omnium corruptibilium. Quaedam vero recedunt minus a permanentia essendi, quia esse eorum nec in transmutatione consistit, nec est subiectum transmutationis, tamen habent transmutationem adiunctam, vel in actu vel in potentia. Sicut patet in corporibus caelestibus, quorum esse substantiale est intransmutabile; tamen esse intransmutabile habent cum transmutabilitate secundum locum. Et similiter patet de Angelis, quod habent esse intransmutabile cum transmutabilitate secundum electionem, quantum ad eorum naturam pertinet; et cum transmutabilitate intelligentiarum et affectionum, et locorum suo modo. Et ideo huiusmodi mensurantur aevo, quod est medium inter aeternitatem et tempus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","text":"Esse autem quod mensurat aeternitas, nec est mutabile, nec mutabilitati adiunctum. Sic ergo tempus habet prius et posterius, aevum autem non habet in se prius et posterius, sed ei coniungi possunt, aeternitas autem non habet prius neque posterius, neque ea compatitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0105","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod creaturae spirituales, quantum ad affectiones et intelligentias, in quibus est successio, mensurantur tempore. Unde et Augustinus ibidem dicit quod per tempus moveri, est per affectiones moveri. Quantum vero ad eorum esse naturale, mensurantur aevo. Sed quantum ad visionem gloriae, participant aeternitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0107","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0108","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aevum est totum simul, non tamen est aeternitas, quia compatitur secum prius et posterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0109","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0110","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in ipso esse Angeli in se considerato, non est differentia praeteriti et futuri, sed solum secundum adiunctas mutationes. Sed quod dicimus Angelum esse vel fuisse vel futurum esse, differt secundum acceptionem intellectus nostri, qui accipit esse Angeli per comparationem ad diversas partes temporis. Et cum dicit Angelum esse vel fuisse, supponit aliquid cum quo eius oppositum non subditur divinae potentiae, cum vero dicit futurum esse, nondum supponit aliquid. Unde, cum esse et non esse Angeli subsit divinae potentiae, absolute considerando, potest Deus facere quod esse Angeli non sit futurum, tamen non potest facere quod non sit dum est, vel quod non fuerit postquam fuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0111","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0112","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod duratio aevi est infinita, quia non finitur tempore. Sic autem esse aliquod creatum infinitum, quod non finiatur quodam alio, non est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0113","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0114","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0115","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit tantum unum aevum. Dicitur enim in apocryphis Esdrae, *maiestas et potestas aevorum est apud te, domine*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0116","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0117","text":"Praeterea, diversorum generum diversae sunt mensurae. Sed quaedam aeviterna sunt in genere corporalium, scilicet corpora caelestia, quaedam vero sunt spirituales substantiae, scilicet Angeli. Non ergo est unum aevum tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0118","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0119","text":"Praeterea, cum aevum sit nomen durationis, quorum est unum aevum, est una duratio. Sed non omnium aeviternorum est una duratio, quia quaedam post alia esse incipiunt, ut maxime patet in animabus humanis. Non est ergo unum aevum tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0120","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0121","text":"Praeterea, ea quae non dependent ab invicem, non videntur habere unam mensuram durationis, propter hoc enim omnium temporalium videtur esse unum tempus, quia omnium motuum quodammodo causa est primus motus, qui prius tempore mensuratur. Sed aeviterna non dependent ab invicem, quia unus Angelus non est causa alterius. Non ergo est unum aevum tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0122","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0123","text":"Sed contra, aevum est simplicius tempore, et propinquius se habens ad aeternitatem. Sed tempus est unum tantum. Ergo multo magis aevum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0124","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0125","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc est duplex opinio, quidam enim dicunt quod est unum aevum tantum; quidam quod multa. Quid autem horum verius sit, oportet considerare ex causa unitatis temporis, in cognitionem enim spiritualium per corporalia devenimus. Dicunt autem quidam esse unum tempus omnium temporalium, propter hoc quod est unus numerus omnium numeratorum, cum tempus sit numerus, secundum philosophum. Sed hoc non sufficit, quia tempus non est numerus ut abstractus extra numeratum, sed ut in numerato existens, alioquin non esset continuus; quia decem ulnae panni continuitatem habent, non ex numero, sed ex numerato. Numerus autem in numerato existens non est idem omnium, sed diversus diversorum. Unde alii assignant causam unitatis temporis ex unitate aeternitatis, quae est principium omnis durationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","text":"Et sic, omnes durationes sunt unum, si consideretur earum principium, sunt vero multae, si consideretur diversitas eorum quae recipiunt durationem ex influxu primi principii. Alii vero assignant causam unitatis temporis ex parte materiae primae, quae est primum subiectum motus, cuius mensura est tempus. Sed neutra assignatio sufficiens videtur, quia ea quae sunt unum principio vel subiecto, et maxime remoto, non sunt unum simpliciter sed secundum quid. Est ergo ratio unitatis temporis, unitas primi motus, secundum quem, cum sit simplicissimus, omnes alii mensurantur, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sic ergo tempus ad illum motum comparatur non solum ut mensura ad mensuratum, sed etiam ut accidens ad subiectum; et sic ab eo recipit unitatem. Ad alios autem motus comparatur solum ut mensura ad mensuratum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","text":"Unde secundum eorum multitudinem non multiplicatur, quia una mensura separata multa mensurari possunt. Hoc igitur habito, sciendum quod de substantiis spiritualibus duplex fuit opinio. Quidam enim dixerunt quod omnes processerunt a Deo in quadam aequalitate, ut Origenes dixit; vel etiam multae earum, ut quidam posuerunt. Alii vero dixerunt quod omnes substantiae spirituales processerunt a Deo quodam gradu et ordine et hoc videtur sentire Dionysius, qui dicit, cap. X Cael. Hier., quod inter substantias spirituales sunt primae, mediae et ultimae, etiam in uno ordine Angelorum. Secundum igitur primam opinionem, necesse est dicere quod sunt plura aeva, secundum quod sunt plura aeviterna prima aequalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","text":"Secundum autem secundam opinionem, oportet dicere quod sit unum aevum tantum, quia, cum unumquodque mensuretur simplicissimo sui generis, ut dicitur in X Metaphys., oportet quod esse omnium aeviternorum mensuretur esse primi aeviterni, quod tanto est simplicius, quanto prius. Et quia secunda opinio verior est, ut infra ostendetur, concedimus ad praesens unum esse aevum tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0129","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0130","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aevum aliquando accipitur pro saeculo, quod est periodus durationis alicuius rei, et sic dicuntur multa aeva, sicut multa saecula.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0131","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0132","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet corpora caelestia et spiritualia differant in genere naturae, tamen conveniunt in hoc, quod habent esse intransmutabile. Et sic mensurantur aevo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0133","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0134","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nec omnia temporalia simul incipiunt, et tamen omnium est unum tempus, propter primum quod mensuratur tempore. Et sic omnia aeviterna habent unum aevum propter primum, etiam si non omnia simul incipiant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0135","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod ad hoc quod aliqua mensurentur per aliquod unum, non requiritur quod illud unum sit causa omnium eorum; sed quod sit simplicius.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"62048f05b7964d1378553f05e5adcc88c091e8e092bd7c564591a6871e21efc3","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T134235758297Z_ddceac8f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 10","source_sha256":"62048f05b7964d1378553f05e5adcc88c091e8e092bd7c564591a6871e21efc3","sha256":"3fa520d55397413780c5d1b3de8ebb8423caee9c1bbd76d8436a4def3e2f07de","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:42:35.758337+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0002","text":"[I q. 10 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003","text":"Next there is inquiry concerning eternity. And concerning this, six questions are asked. First, what eternity is. Second, whether God is eternal. Third, whether being eternal is proper to God. Fourth, whether eternity differs from time. Fifth, concerning the difference between aevum and time. Sixth, whether there is only one aevum, just as there is one time and one eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0005","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the definition of eternity which Boethius gives in *The Consolation of Philosophy*, V, is unsuitable, when he says that *eternity is the whole, simultaneous, and perfect possession of interminable life*. For “interminable” is said negatively. But negation belongs to the notion only of things that are deficient, which does not befit eternity. Therefore “interminable” ought not to be placed in the definition of eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0007","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0008","text":"Moreover, eternity signifies a certain duration. But duration regards being more than life. Therefore life ought not to have been placed in the definition of eternity, but rather being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0009","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0010","text":"Moreover, that which has parts is called a whole. But this does not befit eternity, since it is simple. Therefore it is inappropriately called whole.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0011","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0012","text":"Moreover, several days cannot exist simultaneously, nor can several times. But in eternity days and times are spoken of in the plural; for it is said in Micah V, *his going forth is from the beginning, from the days of eternity*; and in Romans, chapter XVI, *according to the revelation of the mystery kept silent through eternal times*. Therefore eternity is not all at once.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0013","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0014","text":"Moreover, whole and perfect are the same. Therefore, granted that it is whole, it is superfluously added that it is perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0015","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0016","text":"Moreover, possession does not pertain to duration. But eternity is a certain duration. Therefore eternity is not possession.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0017","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","text":"I answer that, just as we must come to the knowledge of simple things through composite things, so we must come to the knowledge of eternity through time; which is nothing other than the number of motion according to before and after. For since in every motion there is succession, and one part comes after another, from the fact that we number before and after in motion, we apprehend time; which is nothing other than the number of before and after in motion. But in that which lacks motion and is always in the same way, there is no before and after to be taken. Therefore, just as the notion of time consists in the numbering of before and after in motion, so the notion of eternity consists in the apprehension of the uniformity of that which is altogether outside motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","text":"Likewise, those things are said to be measured by time which have a beginning and an end in time, as is said in *Physics* IV; and this is because in everything that is moved a certain beginning and a certain end can be taken. But that which is altogether immutable can have neither succession nor beginning or end. Thus eternity is made known from two things. First, from the fact that that which is in eternity is interminable, that is, lacking beginning and end (so that “term” is referred to both). Second, through the fact that eternity itself lacks succession, existing all at once.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0020","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that simple things are customarily defined through negation, just as a point is that whose part does not exist. This is not because negation belongs to their essence, but because our intellect, which first apprehends composite things, cannot arrive at the knowledge of simple things except through the removal of composition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0022","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0023","text":"To the second, it must be said that that which is truly eternal is not only a being, but living; and living itself extends in a certain way to operation, whereas being does not. But the extension of duration seems to be reckoned according to operation rather than according to being; hence time too is the number of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0024","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0025","text":"To the third, it must be said that eternity is called whole, not because it has parts, but insofar as nothing is lacking to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0026","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, just as God, since He is incorporeal, is metaphorically named in Scripture by names of corporeal things, so eternity, existing all at once, is [named] by successive temporal names.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0028","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0029","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that two things are to be considered in time: namely, time itself, which is successive; and the now of time, which is imperfect. Therefore, he says “all at once” in order to remove time, and “perfect” in order to exclude the now of time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0030","text":"[I q. 10 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0031","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that what is possessed is held firmly and quietly. Therefore, to designate the immutability and unfailingness of eternity, he used the name of possession.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0032","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0033","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0034","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God is not eternal. For nothing made can be said of God. But eternity is something made, for Boethius says that *the flowing now makes time, the standing now makes eternity*; and Augustine says, in the book of Eighty-Three Questions, that God is the author of eternity. Therefore God is not eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0035","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0036","text":"Further, what is before eternity and after eternity is not measured by eternity. But God is before eternity, as is said in the Book of Causes, and after eternity; for it is said in Exodus XV that *the Lord shall reign for ever and beyond*. Therefore to be eternal does not belong to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0037","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0038","text":"Further, eternity is a certain measure. But it does not belong to God to be measured. Therefore it is not fitting for Him to be eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0039","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0040","text":"Further, in eternity there is no present, past, or future, since it is all at once, as has been said. But in Scripture verbs of the present, past, or future tense are said of God. Therefore God is not eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0041","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0042","text":"On the contrary, Athanasius says: *the Father eternal, the Son eternal, the Holy Spirit eternal*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0043","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","text":"I answer that the notion of eternity follows upon immutability, just as the notion of time follows upon motion, as is clear from what has been said. Hence, since God is supremely immutable, it belongs to Him most of all to be eternal. Nor is He merely eternal, but He is His own eternity, whereas no other thing is its own duration, because it is not its own being. But God is His own uniform being; hence, just as He is His own essence, so He is His own eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0045","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0046","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the standing now is said to make eternity according to our apprehension. For just as the apprehension of time is caused in us because we apprehend the flowing of the now itself, so the apprehension of eternity is caused in us insofar as we apprehend the standing now. But what Augustine says, that *God is the author of eternity*, is understood of participated eternity; for God communicates His eternity to certain things in the same way as He communicates His immutability.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0047","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0048","text":"And through this the solution to the second is clear. For God is said to be before eternity insofar as it is participated by immaterial substances. Hence it is also said there that *intelligence is equated with eternity*. But as to what is said in Exodus, *the Lord shall reign for ever and beyond*, it should be known that “eternal” is taken there for an age, as another translation has it. Thus, therefore, He is said to reign beyond eternity, because He endures beyond every age, that is, beyond any given duration; for an age is nothing other than the period of any thing, as is said in Book I of On the Heavens. Or He is also said to reign beyond eternity because, even if something else were always to exist (as the motion of the heaven according to certain philosophers), nevertheless God reigns beyond it, insofar as His reign is all at once.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0049","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","text":"To the third, it must be said that eternity is nothing other than God himself. Hence God is not called eternal as though he were in some way measured, but the notion of measure is taken there only according to our apprehension.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0051","text":"[I q. 10 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that words of different tenses are attributed to God insofar as his eternity includes all times, not because he himself is changed through present, past, and future.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0053","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0054","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0055","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that to be eternal is not proper to God alone. For it is said in Daniel XII that *those who instruct many unto justice shall be as stars unto perpetual eternities*. But there would not be several eternities if God alone were eternal. Therefore God alone is not eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0056","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0057","text":"Further, in Matthew XXV it is said, *Depart, ye cursed, into eternal fire*. Therefore God alone is not eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0058","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0059","text":"Further, everything necessary is eternal. But many things are necessary, such as all the principles of demonstration and all demonstrative propositions. Therefore God alone is not eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0060","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0061","text":"On the contrary, Jerome says, to Marcella, *God alone is he who has no beginning*. But whatever has a beginning is not eternal. Therefore God alone is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0062","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","text":"I answer that eternity is truly and properly in God alone. For eternity follows immutability, as is clear from what has been said. But God alone is altogether immutable, as was shown above. Yet insofar as some things receive immutability from him, in that respect they participate in his eternity. Therefore some things obtain from God immutability in this respect, that they never cease to be; and in this respect it is said in Ecclesiastes I of the earth that it stands for ever. Some things are also called eternal in the Scriptures because of the long duration of their enduring, although they are corruptible, as in the Psalm the mountains are called eternal; and in Deuteronomy XXXIII also mention is made of the fruits of the eternal hills.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","text":"But some participate more fully in the notion of eternity, insofar as they have unchangeableness either according to being, or further according to operation, as the Angels and the blessed, who enjoy the Word; because with respect to that vision of the Word, there are no changeable thoughts in the saints, as Augustine says, *On the Trinity*, XV. Hence also those who see God are said to have eternal life, according to John XVII, *this is eternal life, that they may know*, and so forth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0065","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0066","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that many eternities are spoken of insofar as there are many who participate in eternity from the very contemplation of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0067","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068","text":"To the second, it must be said that the fire of Hell is called eternal only on account of its interminability. Yet there is change in their punishments, according to Job XXIV, *from the waters of snow they shall pass to excessive heat*. Hence in Hell there is not true eternity, but rather time, according to the Psalm, *their time shall be unto ages*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0069","text":"[I q. 10 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070","text":"To the third, it must be said that “necessary” signifies a certain mode of truth. But truth, according to the Philosopher, *Metaphysics* VI, is in the intellect. Accordingly, true and necessary things are eternal because they are in the eternal intellect, which is the divine intellect alone. Hence it does not follow that anything outside God is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0071","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0072","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0073","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that eternity is not other than time. For it is impossible for there to be two measures of duration simultaneously, unless one is a part of the other; for there are not simultaneously two days or two hours, but a day and an hour are simultaneous, because an hour is part of a day. But eternity and time are simultaneous, each of which signifies a certain measure of duration. Therefore, since eternity is not a part of time, because eternity exceeds time and includes it, it seems that time is a part of eternity, and not other than eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0074","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0075","text":"Moreover, according to the Philosopher in Physics IV, the now of time remains the same throughout the whole of time. But this seems to constitute the notion of eternity: that it is the same, having itself indivisibly throughout the whole course of time. Therefore eternity is the now of time. But the now of time is not other in substance than time. Therefore eternity is not other in substance than time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0076","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0077","text":"Moreover, just as the measure of the first motion is the measure of all motions, as is said in Physics IV, so it seems that the measure of the first being is the measure of every being. But eternity is the measure of the first being, which is divine being. Therefore eternity is the measure of every being. But the being of corruptible things is measured by time. Therefore time either is eternity, or something of eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0078","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0079","text":"But on the contrary, eternity is whole simultaneously, whereas in time there is before and after. Therefore time and eternity are not the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0080","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","text":"I answer that it is clear that time and eternity are not the same. But some have assigned the reason for this difference from the fact that eternity lacks beginning and end, whereas time has a beginning and an end. But this is a difference accidentally, and not per se. For granted that time always has been and always will be, according to the position of those who hold the motion of the heavens to be everlasting, there will still remain a difference between eternity and time, as Boethius says in the book On Consolation, from the fact that eternity is whole simultaneously, which does not belong to time; for eternity is the measure of permanent being, whereas time is the measure of motion. Yet if the aforesaid difference is considered with respect to the things measured, and not with respect to the measures, then it has some basis, because only that is measured by time which has a beginning and an end in time, as is said in Physics IV.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0082","text":"Hence, if the motion of the heavens were to endure always, time would not measure it according to its whole duration, since the infinite is not measurable; but it would measure each revolution, which has a beginning and an end in time. Yet it can also have another basis on the part of these measures, if end and beginning are taken potentially. For even granted that time endures always, it is nevertheless possible to designate in time both a beginning and an end, by taking some parts of it, as we speak of the beginning and end of a day or a year; which does not occur in eternity. But these differences nevertheless follow upon that difference which is per se and primary, namely, that eternity is whole simultaneously, but time is not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0083","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that argument would proceed if time and eternity were measures of one genus, which is plainly false from the things of which time and eternity are measures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0085","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0086","text":"To the second, it must be said that the now of time is the same in subject throughout the whole of time, but different in notion, because, just as time corresponds to motion, so the now of time corresponds to the movable; but the movable is the same in subject throughout the whole course of time, but different in notion, insofar as it is here and there. And this alternation is motion. Similarly, the flow of the now itself, insofar as it alternates in notion, is time. But eternity remains the same both in subject and in notion. Hence eternity is not the same as the now of time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0087","text":"[I q. 10 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","text":"To the third, it must be said that, just as eternity is the proper measure of being itself, so time is the proper measure of motion. Hence, insofar as some being recedes from the permanence of being and is subjected to change, so far does it recede from eternity and become subject to time. Therefore the being of corruptible things, because it is changeable, is measured not by eternity but by time. For time measures not only things that are actually changed, but also things that are changeable. Hence it measures not only motion, but also rest, which belongs to that which is naturally apt to be moved and is not moved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0089","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0090","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0091","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that aevum is not other than time. For Augustine says, in Book VIII of On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, that *God moves the spiritual creature through time*. But aevum is said to be the measure of spiritual substances. Therefore time does not differ from aevum.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0092","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0093","text":"Moreover, it belongs to the notion of time that it have before and after, whereas it belongs to the notion of eternity that it be whole simultaneously, as has been said. But aevum is not eternity, for it is said in Ecclesiasticus 1 that eternal wisdom is before aevum. Therefore it is not whole simultaneously, but has before and after, and thus it is time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0094","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0095","text":"Moreover, if there is no before and after in aevum, it follows that in things aeviternal, to be, to have been, or to be going to be do not differ. Therefore, since it is impossible for aeviternal things not to have been, it follows that it is impossible for them not to be going to be. This is false, since God can reduce them to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0096","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0097","text":"Moreover, since the duration of aeviternal things is infinite on the side of what follows, if aevum is all at once, it follows that some created thing is actually infinite, which is impossible. Therefore aevum does not differ from time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0098","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0099","text":"But on the contrary is what Boethius says: *who commandest time to proceed from aevum*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0100","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","text":"I answer that aevum differs from time and from eternity, as a mean existing between them. But some assign their difference in this way, saying that eternity lacks beginning and end; aevum has a beginning but no end; while time has a beginning and an end. But this difference is accidental, as was said above, because even if aeviternal things had always existed and were always going to exist, as some maintain; or even if they were at some time to fail, which would be possible to God, aevum would still be distinguished from eternity and time. Others, however, assign a difference among these three on the ground that eternity does not have before and after; whereas time has before and after together with renewal and aging; and aevum has before and after without renewal and aging. But this position entails contradictions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102","text":"This is clearly apparent if renewal and aging are referred to the measure itself. For since the before and after of duration cannot exist simultaneously, if aevum has before and after, then, as the prior part of the aevum passes away, the subsequent part must newly arrive; and thus there will be renewal in aevum itself, as in time. But if they are referred to the things measured, an incongruity still follows. For a temporal thing grows old through time because it has changeable being; and from the changeability of the thing measured there is before and after in the measure, as is clear from Physics IV. Therefore, if that which is itself aeviternal is neither capable of aging nor of renewal, this will be because its being is unchangeable. Therefore its measure will not have before and after. It must therefore be said that, since eternity is the measure of permanent being, according as something recedes from permanence of being, so it recedes from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","text":"But some things so recede from permanence of being that their being is subject to change, or consists in change; and things of this kind are measured by time, such as every motion, and also the being of all corruptible things. But some recede less from permanence of being, because their being neither consists in change nor is subject to change; yet they have change conjoined to them, either actually or potentially. This is evident in the heavenly bodies, whose substantial being is unchangeable; yet they have unchangeable being together with changeability according to place. And likewise it is evident concerning angels that they have unchangeable being together with changeability according to choice, insofar as pertains to their nature; and together with changeability of understandings and affections, and, in their own manner, of places. And therefore things of this kind are measured by aevum, which is intermediate between eternity and time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","text":"But the being which eternity measures is neither changeable nor conjoined with changeability. Thus, therefore, time has before and after; aevum, however, does not have before and after in itself, though these can be conjoined to it; while eternity has neither before nor after, nor does it admit them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0105","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that spiritual creatures, as regards affections and understandings, in which there is succession, are measured by time. Hence Augustine also says in the same place that to be moved through time is to be moved through affections. But as regards their natural being, they are measured by aevum. And as regards the vision of glory, they participate in eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0107","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0108","text":"To the second, it must be said that aevum is all at once; yet it is not eternity, because it admits before and after together with itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0109","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0110","text":"To the third, it must be said that in the angel's very being, considered in itself, there is no distinction of past and future, but only according to changes conjoined to it. But when we say that an angel is, or was, or will be, this differs according to the apprehension of our intellect, which apprehends the angel's being in relation to different parts of time. And when it says that the angel is or was, it presupposes something with respect to which its opposite is not subject to divine power; but when it says that it will be, it does not yet presuppose something. Hence, since the being and non-being of an angel are subject to divine power, considered absolutely, God can make it the case that the angel's being will not be; yet He cannot make it the case that it is not while it is, or that it has not been after it was.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0111","text":"[I q. 10 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0112","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the duration of aevum is infinite because it is not terminated by time. But for some created being to be infinite in this way, namely, not to be terminated by something else, is not unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0113","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0114","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0115","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that there is not only one aevum. For it is said in the apocryphal books of Esdras: *the majesty and power of the ages are with thee, O Lord*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0116","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0117","text":"Moreover, different genera have different measures. But some aeviternal things are in the genus of bodily things, namely the heavenly bodies, while others are spiritual substances, namely angels. Therefore there is not only one aevum.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0118","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0119","text":"Moreover, since aevum is the name of a duration, those which have one aevum have one duration. But not all aeviternal things have one duration, because some begin to be after others, as is especially evident in human souls. Therefore there is not only one aevum.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0120","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0121","text":"Moreover, those things which do not depend upon one another do not seem to have one measure of duration; for this reason there seems to be one time for all temporal things, because the first motion, which is measured by time priorly, is in some way the cause of all motions. But everlasting things do not depend upon one another, because one Angel is not the cause of another. Therefore there is not only one aevum.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0122","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0123","text":"On the contrary, aevum is simpler than time, and bears a closer relation to eternity. But there is only one time. Therefore, much more so, one aevum.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0124","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0125","text":"I answer that it must be said that there are two opinions concerning this matter. For some say that there is only one aevum; others, that there are many. But which of these is truer must be considered from the cause of the unity of time, for through corporeal things we arrive at the knowledge of spiritual things. Now some say that there is one time for all temporal things, on the ground that there is one number of all things numbered, since time is a number, according to the Philosopher. But this does not suffice, because time is not a number as something abstract outside the numbered thing, but as existing in the numbered thing; otherwise it would not be continuous. For ten ells of cloth have continuity, not from the number, but from the numbered thing. But number existing in the numbered thing is not the same for all things, but is diverse for diverse things. Hence others assign the cause of the unity of time from the unity of eternity, which is the principle of all duration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","text":"And thus all durations are one, if their principle is considered; but they are many, if the diversity of those things which receive duration from the influx of the first principle is considered. Others, however, assign the cause of the unity of time on the part of prime matter, which is the first subject of motion, the measure of which is time. But neither assignment seems sufficient, because those things which are one in principle or subject, especially a remote one, are not one simply, but in a certain respect. Therefore the reason for the unity of time is the unity of the first motion, according to which, since it is the most simple, all other motions are measured, as is said in Metaphysics X. Thus time is compared to that motion not only as measure to measured, but also as accident to subject; and thus it receives unity from it. But to other motions it is compared only as measure to measured.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","text":"Hence it is not multiplied according to their multitude, because many things can be measured by one separate measure. This having been established, it should be known that there were two opinions concerning spiritual substances. For some said that all proceeded from God in a certain equality, as Origen said; or at least many of them, as some maintained. Others, however, said that all spiritual substances proceeded from God according to a certain grade and order; and Dionysius seems to hold this, who says, in chapter X of the Celestial Hierarchy, that among spiritual substances there are first, middle, and last, even within one order of Angels. Therefore, according to the first opinion, it is necessary to say that there are several aeva, according as there are several equal first everlasting things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","text":"But according to the second opinion, it is necessary to say that there is only one aevum, because, since each thing is measured by what is most simple in its genus, as is said in Metaphysics X, the being of all everlasting things must be measured by the being of the first everlasting thing, which is simpler insofar as it is prior. And because the second opinion is truer, as will be shown below, we grant for the present that there is only one aevum.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0129","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0130","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that aevum is sometimes taken for an age, which is a period of the duration of some thing; and thus many aeva are spoken of, just as many ages.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0131","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0132","text":"To the second it must be said that, although celestial and spiritual bodies differ in the genus of nature, nevertheless they agree in this, that they have unchangeable being. And thus they are measured by aevum.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0133","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0134","text":"To the third it must be said that not all temporal things begin at the same time, and yet there is one time for all of them, on account of the first thing that is measured by time. And thus all everlasting things have one aevum on account of the first, even if they do not all begin at the same time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0135","text":"[I q. 10 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136","text":"To the fourth it must be said that, for certain things to be measured by some one thing, it is not required that that one thing be the cause of all of them, but that it be simpler.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136"]}],"source_digest":"6f7e303be76d097168c194c9ba3085300cc248371a0b411541acc57f64fd7e79","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 10"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 10","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3","title":"First Part · Question 11","order":12,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 11 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28648)\n\nPost praemissa, considerandum est de divina unitate. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum unum addat aliquid supra ens. Secundo, utrum opponantur unum et multa. Tertio, utrum Deus sit unus. Quarto, utrum sit maxime unus.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28649)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unum addat aliquid supra ens. Omne enim quod est in aliquo genere determinato, se habet ex additione ad ens, quod circuit omnia genera. Sed unum est in genere determinato, est enim principium numeri, qui est species quantitatis. Ergo unum addit aliquid supra ens.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28650)\n\nPraeterea, quod dividit aliquod commune, se habet ex additione ad illud. Sed ens dividitur per unum et multa. Ergo unum addit aliquid supra ens.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28651)\n\nPraeterea, si unum non addit supra ens, idem esset dicere unum et ens. Sed nugatorie dicitur ens ens. Ergo nugatio esset dicere ens unum, quod falsum est. Addit igitur unum supra ens.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28652)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, ult. cap. de Div. Nom., *nihil est existentium non participans uno*, quod non esset, si unum adderet supra ens quod contraheret ipsum. Ergo unum non habet se ex additione ad ens.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28653)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod unum non addit supra ens rem aliquam, sed tantum negationem divisionis, unum enim nihil aliud significat quam ens indivisum. Et ex hoc ipso apparet quod unum convertitur cum ente. Nam omne ens aut est simplex, aut compositum. Quod autem est simplex, est indivisum et actu et potentia. Quod autem est compositum, non habet esse quandiu partes eius sunt divisae, sed postquam constituunt et componunt ipsum compositum. Unde manifestum est quod esse cuiuslibet rei consistit in indivisione. Et inde est quod unumquodque, sicut custodit suum esse, ita custodit suam unitatem.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28654)\n\nAd primum igitur dicendum quod quidam, putantes idem esse unum quod convertitur cum ente, et quod est principium numeri, divisi sunt in contrarias positiones. Pythagoras enim et Plato, videntes quod unum quod convertitur cum ente, non addit aliquam rem supra ens, sed significat substantiam entis prout est indivisa, existimaverunt sic se habere de uno quod est principium numeri. Et quia numerus componitur ex unitatibus, crediderunt quod numeri essent substantiae omnium rerum. E contrario autem Avicenna, considerans quod unum quod est principium numeri, addit aliquam rem supra substantiam entis (alias numerus ex unitatibus compositus non esset species quantitatis), credidit quod unum quod convertitur cum ente, addat rem aliquam supra substantiam entis, sicut album supra hominem. Sed hoc manifeste falsum est, quia quaelibet res est una per suam substantiam. Si enim per aliquid aliud esset una quaelibet res, cum illud iterum sit unum, si esset iterum unum per aliquid aliud, esset abire in infinitum. Unde standum est in primo. Sic igitur dicendum est quod unum quod convertitur cum ente, non addit aliquam rem supra ens, sed unum quod est principium numeri, addit aliquid supra ens, ad genus quantitatis pertinens.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28655)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nihil prohibet id quod est uno modo divisum, esse alio modo indivisum; sicut quod est divisum numero, est indivisum secundum speciem, et sic contingit aliquid esse uno modo unum, alio modo multa. Sed tamen si sit indivisum simpliciter; vel quia est indivisum secundum id quod pertinet ad essentiam rei, licet sit divisum quantum ad ea quae sunt extra essentiam rei, sicut quod est unum subiecto et multa secundum accidentia; vel quia est indivisum in actu, et divisum in potentia, sicut quod est unum toto et multa secundum partes, huiusmodi erit unum simpliciter, et multa secundum quid. Si vero aliquid e converso sit indivisum secundum quid, et divisum simpliciter; utpote quia est divisum secundum essentiam, et indivisum secundum rationem, vel secundum principium sive causam, erit multa simpliciter, et unum secundum quid; ut quae sunt multa numero et unum specie, vel unum principio. Sic igitur ens dividitur per unum et multa, quasi per unum simpliciter, et multa secundum quid. Nam et ipsa multitudo non contineretur sub ente, nisi contineretur aliquo modo sub uno. Dicit enim Dionysius, ult. cap. de Div. Nom., quod *non est multitudo non participans uno, sed quae sunt multa partibus, sunt unum toto; et quae sunt multa accidentibus, sunt unum subiecto; et quae sunt multa numero, sunt unum specie; et quae sunt speciebus multa, sunt unum genere; et quae sunt multa processibus, sunt unum principio*.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28656)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ideo non est nugatio cum dicitur ens unum, quia unum addit aliquid secundum rationem supra ens.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28657)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unum et multa non opponantur. Nullum enim oppositum praedicatur de suo opposito. Sed omnis multitudo est quodammodo unum, ut ex praedictis patet. Ergo unum non opponitur multitudini.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28658)\n\nPraeterea, nullum oppositum constituitur ex suo opposito. Sed unum constituit multitudinem. Ergo non opponitur multitudini.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28659)\n\nPraeterea, unum uni est oppositum. Sed multo opponitur paucum. Ergo non opponitur ei unum.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28660)\n\nPraeterea, si unum opponitur multitudini, opponitur ei sicut indivisum diviso, et sic opponetur ei ut privatio habitui. Hoc autem videtur inconveniens, quia sequeretur quod unum sit posterius multitudine, et definiatur per eam; cum tamen multitudo definiatur per unum. Unde erit circulus in definitione, quod est inconveniens. Non ergo unum et multa sunt opposita.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28661)\n\nSed contra, quorum rationes sunt oppositae, ipsa sunt opposita. Sed ratio unius consistit in indivisibilitate, ratio vero multitudinis divisionem continet. Ergo unum et multa sunt opposita.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28662)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod unum opponitur multis, sed diversimode. Nam unum quod est principium numeri, opponitur multitudini quae est numerus, ut mensura mensurato, unum enim habet rationem primae mensurae, et numerus est multitudo mensurata per unum, ut patet ex X Metaphys. Unum vero quod convertitur cum ente, opponitur multitudini per modum privationis, ut indivisum diviso.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28663)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod nulla privatio tollit totaliter esse, quia privatio est negatio in subiecto, secundum philosophum. Sed tamen omnis privatio tollit aliquod esse. Et ideo in ente, ratione suae communitatis, accidit quod privatio entis fundatur in ente, quod non accidit in privationibus formarum specialium, ut visus vel albedinis, vel alicuius huiusmodi. Et sicut est de ente, ita est de uno et bono, quae convertuntur cum ente, nam privatio boni fundatur in aliquo bono, et similiter remotio unitatis fundatur in aliquo uno. Et exinde contingit quod multitudo est quoddam unum, et malum est quoddam bonum, et non ens est quoddam ens. Non tamen oppositum praedicatur de opposito, quia alterum horum est simpliciter, et alterum secundum quid. Quod enim secundum quid est ens, ut in potentia, est non ens simpliciter, idest actu, vel quod est ens simpliciter in genere substantiae, est non ens secundum quid, quantum ad aliquod esse accidentale. Similiter ergo quod est bonum secundum quid, est malum simpliciter; vel e converso. Et similiter quod est unum simpliciter, est multa secundum quid; et e converso.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28664)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod duplex est totum, quoddam homogeneum, quod componitur ex similibus partibus; quoddam vero heterogeneum, quod componitur ex dissimilibus partibus. In quolibet autem toto homogeneo, totum constituitur ex partibus habentibus formam totius, sicut quaelibet pars aquae est aqua, et talis est constitutio continui ex suis partibus. In quolibet autem toto heterogeneo, quaelibet pars caret forma totius, nulla enim pars domus est domus, nec aliqua pars hominis est homo. Et tale totum est multitudo. Inquantum ergo pars eius non habet formam multitudinis, componitur multitudo ex unitatibus, sicut domus ex non domibus, non quod unitates constituant multitudinem secundum id quod habent de ratione indivisionis, prout opponuntur multitudini; sed secundum hoc quod habent de entitate, sicut et partes domus constituunt domum per hoc quod sunt quaedam corpora, non per hoc quod sunt non domus.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28665)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod multum accipitur dupliciter. Uno modo, absolute, et sic opponitur uni. Alio modo, secundum quod importat excessum quendam, et sic opponitur pauco. Unde primo modo duo sunt multa; non autem secundo.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28666)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod unum opponitur privative multis, inquantum in ratione multorum est quod sint divisa. Unde oportet quod divisio sit prius unitate, non simpliciter, sed secundum rationem nostrae apprehensionis. Apprehendimus enim simplicia per composita, unde definimus punctum, cuius pars non est, vel principium lineae. Sed multitudo, etiam secundum rationem, consequenter se habet ad unum, quia divisa non intelligimus habere rationem multitudinis, nisi per hoc quod utrique divisorum attribuimus unitatem. Unde unum ponitur in definitione multitudinis, non autem multitudo in definitione unius. Sed divisio cadit in intellectu ex ipsa negatione entis. Ita quod primo cadit in intellectu ens; secundo, quod hoc ens non est illud ens, et sic secundo apprehendimus divisionem; tertio, unum; quarto, multitudinem.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 11 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28667)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit unus. Dicitur enim I ad Cor. VIII, *siquidem sunt dii multi et domini multi*.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28668)\n\nPraeterea, unum quod est principium numeri, non potest praedicari de Deo, cum nulla quantitas de Deo praedicetur. Similiter nec unum quod convertitur cum ente, quia importat privationem, et omnis privatio imperfectio est, quae Deo non competit. Non est igitur dicendum quod Deus sit unus.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28669)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Deut. VI, *audi, Israel, dominus Deus tuus unus est*.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28670)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deum esse unum, ex tribus demonstratur. Primo quidem ex eius simplicitate. Manifestum est enim quod illud unde aliquod singulare est hoc aliquid, nullo modo est multis communicabile. Illud enim unde Socrates est homo, multis communicari potest, sed id unde est hic homo, non potest communicari nisi uni tantum. Si ergo Socrates per id esset homo, per quod est hic homo, sicut non possunt esse plures Socrates, ita non possent esse plures homines. Hoc autem convenit Deo, nam ipse Deus est sua natura, ut supra ostensum est. Secundum igitur idem est Deus, et hic Deus. Impossibile est igitur esse plures deos. Secundo vero, ex infinitate eius perfectionis. Ostensum est enim supra quod Deus comprehendit in se totam perfectionem essendi. Si ergo essent plures dii, oporteret eos differre. Aliquid ergo conveniret uni, quod non alteri. Et si hoc esset privatio, non esset simpliciter perfectus, si autem hoc esset perfectio, alteri eorum deesset. Impossibile est ergo esse plures deos. Unde et antiqui philosophi, quasi ab ipsa coacti veritate, ponentes principium infinitum, posuerunt unum tantum principium. Tertio, ab unitate mundi. Omnia enim quae sunt, inveniuntur esse ordinata ad invicem, dum quaedam quibusdam deserviunt. Quae autem diversa sunt, in unum ordinem non convenirent, nisi ab aliquo uno ordinarentur. Melius enim multa reducuntur in unum ordinem per unum, quam per multa, quia per se unius unum est causa, et multa non sunt causa unius nisi per accidens, inquantum scilicet sunt aliquo modo unum. Cum igitur illud quod est primum, sit perfectissimum et per se, non per accidens, oportet quod primum reducens omnia in unum ordinem, sit unum tantum. Et hoc est Deus.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28671)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dicuntur dii multi secundum errorem quorundam qui multos deos colebant, existimantes planetas et alias stellas esse deos, vel etiam singulas partes mundi. Unde subdit, *nobis autem unus Deus,* et cetera.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28672)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod unum secundum quod est principium numeri, non praedicatur de Deo; sed solum de his quae habent esse in materia. Unum enim quod est principium numeri, est de genere mathematicorum; quae habent esse in materia, sed sunt secundum rationem a materia abstracta. Unum vero quod convertitur cum ente, est quoddam metaphysicum, quod secundum esse non dependet a materia. Et licet in Deo non sit aliqua privatio, tamen, secundum modum apprehensionis nostrae, non cognoscitur a nobis nisi per modum privationis et remotionis. Et sic nihil prohibet aliqua privative dicta de Deo praedicari; sicut quod est incorporeus, infinitus. Et similiter de Deo dicitur quod sit unus.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28673)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit maxime unus. Unum enim dicitur secundum privationem divisionis. Sed privatio non recipit magis et minus. Ergo Deus non dicitur magis unus quam alia quae sunt unum.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28674)\n\nPraeterea, nihil videtur esse magis indivisibile quam id quod est indivisibile actu et potentia, cuiusmodi est punctus et unitas. Sed intantum dicitur aliquid magis unum, inquantum est indivisibile. Ergo Deus non est magis unum quam unitas et punctus.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28675)\n\nPraeterea, quod est per essentiam bonum, est maxime bonum, ergo quod est per essentiam suam unum, est maxime unum. Sed omne ens est unum per suam essentiam, ut patet per philosophum in IV Metaphys. Ergo omne ens est maxime unum. Deus igitur non est magis unum quam alia entia.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28676)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Bernardus, quod *inter omnia quae unum dicuntur, arcem tenet unitas divinae Trinitatis*.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28677)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum unum sit ens indivisum, ad hoc quod aliquid sit maxime unum, oportet quod sit et maxime ens et maxime indivisum. Utrumque autem competit Deo. Est enim maxime ens, inquantum est non habens aliquod esse determinatum per aliquam naturam cui adveniat, sed est ipsum esse subsistens, omnibus modis indeterminatum. Est autem maxime indivisum, inquantum neque dividitur actu neque potentia, secundum quemcunque modum divisionis, cum sit omnibus modis simplex, ut supra ostensum est. Unde manifestum est quod Deus est maxime unus.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28678)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet privatio secundum se non recipiat magis et minus, tamen secundum quod eius oppositum recipit magis et minus, etiam ipsa privativa dicuntur secundum magis et minus. Secundum igitur quod aliquid est magis divisum vel divisibile, vel minus, vel nullo modo, secundum hoc aliquid dicitur magis et minus vel maxime unum.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28679)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod punctus et unitas quae est principium numeri, non sunt maxime entia, cum non habeant esse nisi in subiecto aliquo. Unde neutrum eorum est maxime unum. Sicut enim subiectum non est maxime unum, propter diversitatem accidentis et subiecti, ita nec accidens.\n\n[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28680)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet omne ens sit unum per suam substantiam, non tamen se habet aequaliter substantia cuiuslibet ad causandam unitatem, quia substantia quorundam est ex multis composita, quorundam vero non.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0002","text":"[I q. 11 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0003","text":"Post praemissa, considerandum est de divina unitate. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum unum addat aliquid supra ens. Secundo, utrum opponantur unum et multa. Tertio, utrum Deus sit unus. Quarto, utrum sit maxime unus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0005","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unum addat aliquid supra ens. Omne enim quod est in aliquo genere determinato, se habet ex additione ad ens, quod circuit omnia genera. Sed unum est in genere determinato, est enim principium numeri, qui est species quantitatis. Ergo unum addit aliquid supra ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0007","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quod dividit aliquod commune, se habet ex additione ad illud. Sed ens dividitur per unum et multa. Ergo unum addit aliquid supra ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0009","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0010","text":"Praeterea, si unum non addit supra ens, idem esset dicere unum et ens. Sed nugatorie dicitur ens ens. Ergo nugatio esset dicere ens unum, quod falsum est. Addit igitur unum supra ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0011","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, ult. cap. de Div. Nom., *nihil est existentium non participans uno*, quod non esset, si unum adderet supra ens quod contraheret ipsum. Ergo unum non habet se ex additione ad ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0013","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod unum non addit supra ens rem aliquam, sed tantum negationem divisionis, unum enim nihil aliud significat quam ens indivisum. Et ex hoc ipso apparet quod unum convertitur cum ente. Nam omne ens aut est simplex, aut compositum. Quod autem est simplex, est indivisum et actu et potentia. Quod autem est compositum, non habet esse quandiu partes eius sunt divisae, sed postquam constituunt et componunt ipsum compositum. Unde manifestum est quod esse cuiuslibet rei consistit in indivisione. Et inde est quod unumquodque, sicut custodit suum esse, ita custodit suam unitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0015","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016","text":"Ad primum igitur dicendum quod quidam, putantes idem esse unum quod convertitur cum ente, et quod est principium numeri, divisi sunt in contrarias positiones. Pythagoras enim et Plato, videntes quod unum quod convertitur cum ente, non addit aliquam rem supra ens, sed significat substantiam entis prout est indivisa, existimaverunt sic se habere de uno quod est principium numeri. Et quia numerus componitur ex unitatibus, crediderunt quod numeri essent substantiae omnium rerum. E contrario autem Avicenna, considerans quod unum quod est principium numeri, addit aliquam rem supra substantiam entis (alias numerus ex unitatibus compositus non esset species quantitatis), credidit quod unum quod convertitur cum ente, addat rem aliquam supra substantiam entis, sicut album supra hominem. Sed hoc manifeste falsum est, quia quaelibet res est una per suam substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","text":"Si enim per aliquid aliud esset una quaelibet res, cum illud iterum sit unum, si esset iterum unum per aliquid aliud, esset abire in infinitum. Unde standum est in primo. Sic igitur dicendum est quod unum quod convertitur cum ente, non addit aliquam rem supra ens, sed unum quod est principium numeri, addit aliquid supra ens, ad genus quantitatis pertinens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0018","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nihil prohibet id quod est uno modo divisum, esse alio modo indivisum; sicut quod est divisum numero, est indivisum secundum speciem, et sic contingit aliquid esse uno modo unum, alio modo multa. Sed tamen si sit indivisum simpliciter; vel quia est indivisum secundum id quod pertinet ad essentiam rei, licet sit divisum quantum ad ea quae sunt extra essentiam rei, sicut quod est unum subiecto et multa secundum accidentia; vel quia est indivisum in actu, et divisum in potentia, sicut quod est unum toto et multa secundum partes, huiusmodi erit unum simpliciter, et multa secundum quid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","text":"Si vero aliquid e converso sit indivisum secundum quid, et divisum simpliciter; utpote quia est divisum secundum essentiam, et indivisum secundum rationem, vel secundum principium sive causam, erit multa simpliciter, et unum secundum quid; ut quae sunt multa numero et unum specie, vel unum principio. Sic igitur ens dividitur per unum et multa, quasi per unum simpliciter, et multa secundum quid. Nam et ipsa multitudo non contineretur sub ente, nisi contineretur aliquo modo sub uno. Dicit enim Dionysius, ult. cap. de Div. Nom., quod *non est multitudo non participans uno, sed quae sunt multa partibus, sunt unum toto; et quae sunt multa accidentibus, sunt unum subiecto; et quae sunt multa numero, sunt unum specie; et quae sunt speciebus multa, sunt unum genere; et quae sunt multa processibus, sunt unum principio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0021","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ideo non est nugatio cum dicitur ens unum, quia unum addit aliquid secundum rationem supra ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0024","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unum et multa non opponantur. Nullum enim oppositum praedicatur de suo opposito. Sed omnis multitudo est quodammodo unum, ut ex praedictis patet. Ergo unum non opponitur multitudini.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0026","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0027","text":"Praeterea, nullum oppositum constituitur ex suo opposito. Sed unum constituit multitudinem. Ergo non opponitur multitudini.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0028","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0029","text":"Praeterea, unum uni est oppositum. Sed multo opponitur paucum. Ergo non opponitur ei unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0030","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0031","text":"Praeterea, si unum opponitur multitudini, opponitur ei sicut indivisum diviso, et sic opponetur ei ut privatio habitui. Hoc autem videtur inconveniens, quia sequeretur quod unum sit posterius multitudine, et definiatur per eam; cum tamen multitudo definiatur per unum. Unde erit circulus in definitione, quod est inconveniens. Non ergo unum et multa sunt opposita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0032","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0033","text":"Sed contra, quorum rationes sunt oppositae, ipsa sunt opposita. Sed ratio unius consistit in indivisibilitate, ratio vero multitudinis divisionem continet. Ergo unum et multa sunt opposita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0034","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod unum opponitur multis, sed diversimode. Nam unum quod est principium numeri, opponitur multitudini quae est numerus, ut mensura mensurato, unum enim habet rationem primae mensurae, et numerus est multitudo mensurata per unum, ut patet ex X Metaphys. Unum vero quod convertitur cum ente, opponitur multitudini per modum privationis, ut indivisum diviso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0036","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod nulla privatio tollit totaliter esse, quia privatio est negatio in subiecto, secundum philosophum. Sed tamen omnis privatio tollit aliquod esse. Et ideo in ente, ratione suae communitatis, accidit quod privatio entis fundatur in ente, quod non accidit in privationibus formarum specialium, ut visus vel albedinis, vel alicuius huiusmodi. Et sicut est de ente, ita est de uno et bono, quae convertuntur cum ente, nam privatio boni fundatur in aliquo bono, et similiter remotio unitatis fundatur in aliquo uno. Et exinde contingit quod multitudo est quoddam unum, et malum est quoddam bonum, et non ens est quoddam ens. Non tamen oppositum praedicatur de opposito, quia alterum horum est simpliciter, et alterum secundum quid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0038","text":"Quod enim secundum quid est ens, ut in potentia, est non ens simpliciter, idest actu, vel quod est ens simpliciter in genere substantiae, est non ens secundum quid, quantum ad aliquod esse accidentale. Similiter ergo quod est bonum secundum quid, est malum simpliciter; vel e converso. Et similiter quod est unum simpliciter, est multa secundum quid; et e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0039","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod duplex est totum, quoddam homogeneum, quod componitur ex similibus partibus; quoddam vero heterogeneum, quod componitur ex dissimilibus partibus. In quolibet autem toto homogeneo, totum constituitur ex partibus habentibus formam totius, sicut quaelibet pars aquae est aqua, et talis est constitutio continui ex suis partibus. In quolibet autem toto heterogeneo, quaelibet pars caret forma totius, nulla enim pars domus est domus, nec aliqua pars hominis est homo. Et tale totum est multitudo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","text":"Inquantum ergo pars eius non habet formam multitudinis, componitur multitudo ex unitatibus, sicut domus ex non domibus, non quod unitates constituant multitudinem secundum id quod habent de ratione indivisionis, prout opponuntur multitudini; sed secundum hoc quod habent de entitate, sicut et partes domus constituunt domum per hoc quod sunt quaedam corpora, non per hoc quod sunt non domus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0042","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0043","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod multum accipitur dupliciter. Uno modo, absolute, et sic opponitur uni. Alio modo, secundum quod importat excessum quendam, et sic opponitur pauco. Unde primo modo duo sunt multa; non autem secundo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0044","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod unum opponitur privative multis, inquantum in ratione multorum est quod sint divisa. Unde oportet quod divisio sit prius unitate, non simpliciter, sed secundum rationem nostrae apprehensionis. Apprehendimus enim simplicia per composita, unde definimus punctum, cuius pars non est, vel principium lineae. Sed multitudo, etiam secundum rationem, consequenter se habet ad unum, quia divisa non intelligimus habere rationem multitudinis, nisi per hoc quod utrique divisorum attribuimus unitatem. Unde unum ponitur in definitione multitudinis, non autem multitudo in definitione unius. Sed divisio cadit in intellectu ex ipsa negatione entis. Ita quod primo cadit in intellectu ens; secundo, quod hoc ens non est illud ens, et sic secundo apprehendimus divisionem; tertio, unum; quarto, multitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0046","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0047","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0048","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit unus. Dicitur enim I ad Cor. VIII, *siquidem sunt dii multi et domini multi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0049","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0050","text":"Praeterea, unum quod est principium numeri, non potest praedicari de Deo, cum nulla quantitas de Deo praedicetur. Similiter nec unum quod convertitur cum ente, quia importat privationem, et omnis privatio imperfectio est, quae Deo non competit. Non est igitur dicendum quod Deus sit unus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0051","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0052","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Deut. VI, *audi, Israel, dominus Deus tuus unus est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0053","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deum esse unum, ex tribus demonstratur. Primo quidem ex eius simplicitate. Manifestum est enim quod illud unde aliquod singulare est hoc aliquid, nullo modo est multis communicabile. Illud enim unde Socrates est homo, multis communicari potest, sed id unde est hic homo, non potest communicari nisi uni tantum. Si ergo Socrates per id esset homo, per quod est hic homo, sicut non possunt esse plures Socrates, ita non possent esse plures homines. Hoc autem convenit Deo, nam ipse Deus est sua natura, ut supra ostensum est. Secundum igitur idem est Deus, et hic Deus. Impossibile est igitur esse plures deos. Secundo vero, ex infinitate eius perfectionis. Ostensum est enim supra quod Deus comprehendit in se totam perfectionem essendi. Si ergo essent plures dii, oporteret eos differre. Aliquid ergo conveniret uni, quod non alteri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","text":"Et si hoc esset privatio, non esset simpliciter perfectus, si autem hoc esset perfectio, alteri eorum deesset. Impossibile est ergo esse plures deos. Unde et antiqui philosophi, quasi ab ipsa coacti veritate, ponentes principium infinitum, posuerunt unum tantum principium. Tertio, ab unitate mundi. Omnia enim quae sunt, inveniuntur esse ordinata ad invicem, dum quaedam quibusdam deserviunt. Quae autem diversa sunt, in unum ordinem non convenirent, nisi ab aliquo uno ordinarentur. Melius enim multa reducuntur in unum ordinem per unum, quam per multa, quia per se unius unum est causa, et multa non sunt causa unius nisi per accidens, inquantum scilicet sunt aliquo modo unum. Cum igitur illud quod est primum, sit perfectissimum et per se, non per accidens, oportet quod primum reducens omnia in unum ordinem, sit unum tantum. Et hoc est Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0056","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0057","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dicuntur dii multi secundum errorem quorundam qui multos deos colebant, existimantes planetas et alias stellas esse deos, vel etiam singulas partes mundi. Unde subdit, *nobis autem unus Deus,* et cetera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0058","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod unum secundum quod est principium numeri, non praedicatur de Deo; sed solum de his quae habent esse in materia. Unum enim quod est principium numeri, est de genere mathematicorum; quae habent esse in materia, sed sunt secundum rationem a materia abstracta. Unum vero quod convertitur cum ente, est quoddam metaphysicum, quod secundum esse non dependet a materia. Et licet in Deo non sit aliqua privatio, tamen, secundum modum apprehensionis nostrae, non cognoscitur a nobis nisi per modum privationis et remotionis. Et sic nihil prohibet aliqua privative dicta de Deo praedicari; sicut quod est incorporeus, infinitus. Et similiter de Deo dicitur quod sit unus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0060","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0061","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0062","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit maxime unus. Unum enim dicitur secundum privationem divisionis. Sed privatio non recipit magis et minus. Ergo Deus non dicitur magis unus quam alia quae sunt unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0063","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0064","text":"Praeterea, nihil videtur esse magis indivisibile quam id quod est indivisibile actu et potentia, cuiusmodi est punctus et unitas. Sed intantum dicitur aliquid magis unum, inquantum est indivisibile. Ergo Deus non est magis unum quam unitas et punctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0065","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0066","text":"Praeterea, quod est per essentiam bonum, est maxime bonum, ergo quod est per essentiam suam unum, est maxime unum. Sed omne ens est unum per suam essentiam, ut patet per philosophum in IV Metaphys. Ergo omne ens est maxime unum. Deus igitur non est magis unum quam alia entia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0067","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0068","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Bernardus, quod *inter omnia quae unum dicuntur, arcem tenet unitas divinae Trinitatis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0069","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum unum sit ens indivisum, ad hoc quod aliquid sit maxime unum, oportet quod sit et maxime ens et maxime indivisum. Utrumque autem competit Deo. Est enim maxime ens, inquantum est non habens aliquod esse determinatum per aliquam naturam cui adveniat, sed est ipsum esse subsistens, omnibus modis indeterminatum. Est autem maxime indivisum, inquantum neque dividitur actu neque potentia, secundum quemcunque modum divisionis, cum sit omnibus modis simplex, ut supra ostensum est. Unde manifestum est quod Deus est maxime unus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0071","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet privatio secundum se non recipiat magis et minus, tamen secundum quod eius oppositum recipit magis et minus, etiam ipsa privativa dicuntur secundum magis et minus. Secundum igitur quod aliquid est magis divisum vel divisibile, vel minus, vel nullo modo, secundum hoc aliquid dicitur magis et minus vel maxime unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0073","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0074","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod punctus et unitas quae est principium numeri, non sunt maxime entia, cum non habeant esse nisi in subiecto aliquo. Unde neutrum eorum est maxime unum. Sicut enim subiectum non est maxime unum, propter diversitatem accidentis et subiecti, ita nec accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0075","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet omne ens sit unum per suam substantiam, non tamen se habet aequaliter substantia cuiuslibet ad causandam unitatem, quia substantia quorundam est ex multis composita, quorundam vero non.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"37af44c5e02650ad6f0e16dc691777ba605132a0a032fb58bb9055f90b8f553c","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T134326874039Z_14403d9c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 11","source_sha256":"37af44c5e02650ad6f0e16dc691777ba605132a0a032fb58bb9055f90b8f553c","sha256":"9c7231f15e193139a1cca54457d9f947009045f2c2789d58692984f0ebb7e9e8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:43:26.874051+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0002","text":"[I q. 11 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0003","text":"After the foregoing, we must consider the divine unity. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether one adds something over and above being. Second, whether one and many are opposed. Third, whether God is one. Fourth, whether He is supremely one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0005","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that one adds something over and above being. For everything that is in some determinate genus is related to being, which encompasses all genera, by addition. But one is in a determinate genus, for it is the principle of number, which is a species of quantity. Therefore one adds something over and above being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0007","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0008","text":"Moreover, what divides some common thing is related to it by addition. But being is divided by one and many. Therefore one adds something over and above being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0009","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0010","text":"Moreover, if one does not add over and above being, to say one and being would be the same. But “being being” is said tautologously. Therefore it would be tautological to say “being one,” which is false. Therefore one adds over and above being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0011","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in the last chapter of *On the Divine Names*, *there is nothing among beings that does not participate in one*; which would not be so if one added something over and above being that contracted it. Therefore one is not related to being by addition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0013","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that one does not add any thing over and above being, but only the negation of division; for one signifies nothing other than undivided being. And from this itself it is apparent that one is convertible with being. For every being is either simple or composite. But what is simple is undivided both actually and potentially. But what is composite does not have being so long as its parts are divided, but only after they constitute and compose the composite itself. Hence it is manifest that the being of each thing consists in indivision. And thus it is that each thing, just as it preserves its own being, so preserves its own unity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0015","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that certain persons, thinking that the one which is convertible with being is the same as that which is the principle of number, were divided into contrary positions. For Pythagoras and Plato, seeing that the one which is convertible with being adds no thing over and above being, but signifies the substance of being insofar as it is undivided, judged that the one which is the principle of number was likewise so constituted. And because number is composed of unities, they believed that numbers were the substances of all things. Avicenna, on the contrary, considering that the one which is the principle of number adds some thing over and above the substance of being (otherwise number, composed of unities, would not be a species of quantity), believed that the one which is convertible with being adds some thing over and above the substance of being, as white does over and above man. But this is plainly false, because every thing is one through its own substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","text":"For if each thing were one through something else, since that again is one, if it were again one through something else, one would proceed to infinity. Hence one must stop at the first. Thus, therefore, it must be said that the one which is convertible with being adds no thing over and above being; but the one which is the principle of number adds something over and above being, belonging to the genus of quantity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0018","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that nothing prevents that which is divided in one way from being undivided in another way; just as what is divided in number is undivided according to species, and thus something may be one in one way and many in another. Yet if it is undivided simply—either because it is undivided according to what pertains to the essence of the thing, although it is divided with respect to those things that are outside the essence of the thing, as what is one in subject and many according to accidents; or because it is undivided in act and divided in potency, as what is one in whole and many according to parts—it will be one simply and many in a certain respect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","text":"But if, conversely, something is undivided in a certain respect and divided simply—for instance, because it is divided according to essence and undivided according to notion, or according to principle or cause—it will be many simply and one in a certain respect; as things that are many in number and one in species, or one in principle. Thus, therefore, being is divided by one and many, as it were by one simply and many in a certain respect. For even multitude itself would not be contained under being unless it were in some way contained under one. For Dionysius says, in the last chapter of *On the Divine Names*, that *there is no multitude not participating in one; but things that are many in parts are one in whole; and things that are many in accidents are one in subject; and things that are many in number are one in species; and things that are many in species are one in genus; and things that are many in processions are one in principle*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0021","text":"[I q. 11 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that it is not tautology when “being one” is said, because one adds something in notion over and above being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0024","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second point. It seems that the one and the many are not opposed. For no opposite is predicated of its opposite. But every multitude is in some way one, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore the one is not opposed to multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0026","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0027","text":"Further, no opposite is constituted from its opposite. But the one constitutes multitude. Therefore it is not opposed to multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0028","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0029","text":"Further, one is opposed to one. But few is opposed to many. Therefore one is not opposed to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0030","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0031","text":"Further, if the one is opposed to multitude, it is opposed to it as the undivided to the divided, and thus it will be opposed to it as privation to habit. But this seems unsuitable, because it would follow that the one is subsequent to multitude and is defined through it; whereas multitude is defined through the one. Hence there will be a circle in the definition, which is unsuitable. Therefore the one and the many are not opposites.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0032","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0033","text":"On the contrary, those whose accounts are opposed are themselves opposed. But the account of the one consists in indivisibility, whereas the account of multitude includes division. Therefore the one and the many are opposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0034","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","text":"I answer that it must be said that the one is opposed to the many, but in diverse ways. For the one which is the principle of number is opposed to the multitude which is number, as measure to what is measured; for the one has the character of the first measure, and number is a multitude measured by one, as is clear from Metaphysics X. But the one which is convertible with being is opposed to multitude by way of privation, as the undivided to the divided.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0036","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that no privation wholly removes being, because privation is negation in a subject, according to the Philosopher. Yet every privation removes some being. And therefore, in being, by reason of its commonness, it happens that the privation of being is founded in being, which does not happen in the privations of special forms, such as sight or whiteness, or anything of this kind. And as it is with being, so it is with one and good, which are convertible with being; for the privation of good is founded in some good, and likewise the removal of unity is founded in some one. Hence it happens that multitude is a certain one, and evil is a certain good, and non-being is a certain being. Nevertheless, an opposite is not predicated of its opposite, because one of these is simply, and the other in a certain respect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0038","text":"For what is being in a certain respect, as in potency, is non-being simply, that is, in act; or what is being simply in the genus of substance is non-being in a certain respect, with respect to some accidental being. Similarly, therefore, what is good in a certain respect is evil simply; or conversely. And likewise, what is one simply is many in a certain respect; and conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0039","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0040","text":"To the second it must be said that there are two kinds of whole: one homogeneous, which is composed of similar parts; the other heterogeneous, which is composed of dissimilar parts. Now in every homogeneous whole, the whole is constituted from parts having the form of the whole, just as every part of water is water; and such is the constitution of a continuum from its parts. But in every heterogeneous whole, each part lacks the form of the whole; for no part of a house is a house, nor is any part of a man a man. And such a whole is multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","text":"Therefore, insofar as its part does not have the form of multitude, multitude is composed of unities, just as a house is composed of non-houses—not because unities constitute multitude according to what they have of the character of indivision, insofar as they are opposed to multitude, but according to what they have of being, just as the parts of a house constitute a house through being certain bodies, not through being non-houses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0042","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0043","text":"To the third it must be said that “many” is taken in two ways. In one way, absolutely, and thus it is opposed to one. In another way, insofar as it conveys a certain excess, and thus it is opposed to few. Hence, in the first way, two are many; but not in the second.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0044","text":"[I q. 11 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the one is opposed privatively to the many, insofar as it belongs to the account of the many that they be divided. Hence division must be prior to unity, not simply, but according to the order of our apprehension. For we apprehend simple things through composite things; hence we define a point, whose part does not exist, or the principle of a line. But multitude, even according to its account, is subsequent to the one, because we do not understand divided things to have the character of multitude except through the fact that we attribute unity to each of the divided things. Hence the one is placed in the definition of multitude, but multitude is not placed in the definition of the one. But division falls into the intellect from the very negation of being. Thus being first falls into the intellect; second, that this being is not that being, and thus second we apprehend division; third, one; fourth, multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0046","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0047","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0048","text":"We proceed thus to the third point. It seems that God is not one. For it is said in I Corinthians VIII, *indeed there are many gods and many lords*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0049","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0050","text":"Moreover, the one which is the principle of number cannot be predicated of God, since no quantity is predicated of God. Likewise, neither can the one which is convertible with being, because it implies privation, and every privation is an imperfection, which does not belong to God. Therefore it must not be said that God is one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0051","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0052","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Deut. VI, *Hear, O Israel, the Lord thy God is one*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0053","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","text":"I answer that it must be said that God's being one is demonstrated in three ways. First, indeed, from His simplicity. For it is clear that that whereby some singular thing is this something can in no way be communicated to many. For that whereby Socrates is a man can be communicated to many, but that whereby he is this man cannot be communicated except to one alone. Therefore, if Socrates were a man through that whereby he is this man, then, just as there cannot be several Socrateses, so neither could there be several men. Now this belongs to God, for God Himself is His nature, as was shown above. Therefore God and this God are the same according to the same thing. It is therefore impossible for there to be several gods. Secondly, from the infinity of His perfection. For it was shown above that God comprehends within Himself the whole perfection of being. Therefore, if there were several gods, they would have to differ. Something, therefore, would belong to one which did not belong to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","text":"And if this were a privation, he would not be simply perfect; but if it were a perfection, it would be lacking to the other of them. Therefore it is impossible for there to be several gods. Hence also the ancient philosophers, as though compelled by truth itself, in positing an infinite principle, posited only one principle. Thirdly, from the unity of the world. For all things that exist are found to be ordered to one another, inasmuch as some serve others. But things that are diverse would not concur in one order unless they were ordered by some one thing. For many are reduced to one order better by one than by many, because one is per se the cause of one, and many are the cause of one only per accidens, namely, insofar as they are in some way one. Therefore, since that which is first is most perfect and per se, not per accidens, the first thing reducing all things to one order must be only one. And this is God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0056","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0057","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that many gods are spoken of according to the error of certain persons who worshipped many gods, thinking the planets and other stars to be gods, or even the individual parts of the world. Hence he adds, *but for us there is one God,* and so forth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0058","text":"[I q. 11 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","text":"To the second, it must be said that the one insofar as it is the principle of number is not predicated of God, but only of those things which have being in matter. For the one which is the principle of number belongs to the genus of mathematical things, which have being in matter, but are abstracted from matter according to reason. But the one which is convertible with being is something metaphysical, which does not depend on matter according to being. And although there is no privation in God, nevertheless, according to our mode of apprehension, He is known by us only through the mode of privation and removal. And thus nothing prevents things said privatively from being predicated of God, such as that He is incorporeal and infinite. And similarly, God is said to be one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0060","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0061","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0062","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that God is not maximally one. For one is said according to the privation of division. But privation does not admit of more and less. Therefore God is not said to be more one than other things which are one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0063","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0064","text":"Moreover, nothing seems to be more indivisible than that which is indivisible in act and potency, such as a point and unity. But a thing is said to be more one to the extent that it is indivisible. Therefore God is not more one than unity and a point.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0065","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0066","text":"Moreover, that which is good through its essence is maximally good; therefore that which is one through its essence is maximally one. But every being is one through its essence, as is clear from the Philosopher in *Metaphysics* IV. Therefore every being is maximally one. God, therefore, is not more one than other beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0067","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0068","text":"But on the contrary, Bernard says that *among all things which are called one, the unity of the divine Trinity holds the summit*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0069","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since one is undivided being, for something to be maximally one it must be both maximally being and maximally undivided. Both belong to God. For He is maximally being, insofar as He does not have some being determined by some nature that accrues to it, but is subsisting being itself, indeterminate in every way. And He is maximally undivided, insofar as He is divided neither in act nor in potency, according to any mode whatsoever of division, since He is simple in every way, as was shown above. Hence it is clear that God is maximally one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0071","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although privation in itself does not admit of more and less, nevertheless, insofar as its opposite admits of more and less, privative terms themselves are also said according to more and less. Therefore, according as something is more divided or divisible, or less so, or in no way, according to this something is said to be more, less, or maximally one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0073","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0074","text":"To the second, it must be said that a point and the unit which is the principle of number are not beings in the highest degree, since they have being only in some subject. Hence neither of them is one in the highest degree. For just as a subject is not one in the highest degree, on account of the diversity of accident and subject, so neither is an accident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0075","text":"[I q. 11 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0076","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although every being is one through its substance, nevertheless the substance of each does not stand in an equal relation to causing unity, because the substance of some is composed of many things, whereas that of others is not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0076"]}],"source_digest":"6ef0d48feda0a2f7317768e617541f3ae21833166778d8615a9280597b167247","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 11"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 11","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4","title":"First Part · Question 12","order":13,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 12 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28681)\n\nQuia in superioribus consideravimus qualiter Deus sit secundum seipsum, restat considerandum qualiter sit in cognitione nostra, idest quomodo cognoscatur a creaturis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tredecim. Primo, utrum aliquis intellectus creatus possit videre essentiam Dei. Secundo, utrum Dei essentia videatur ab intellectu per aliquam speciem creatam. Tertio, utrum oculo corporeo Dei essentia possit videri. Quarto, utrum aliqua substantia intellectualis creata ex suis naturalibus sufficiens sit videre Dei essentiam. Quinto, utrum intellectus creatus ad videndam Dei essentiam indigeat aliquo lumine creato. Sexto, utrum videntium essentiam Dei unus alio perfectius videat. Septimo, utrum aliquis intellectus creatus possit comprehendere Dei essentiam. Octavo, utrum intellectus creatus videns Dei essentiam, omnia in ipsa cognoscat. Nono, utrum ea quae ibi cognoscit, per aliquas similitudines cognoscat. Decimo, utrum simul cognoscat omnia quae in Deo videt. Undecimo, utrum in statu huius vitae possit aliquis homo essentiam Dei videre. Duodecimo, utrum per rationem naturalem Deum in hac vita possimus cognoscere. Tertiodecimo, utrum, supra cognitionem naturalis rationis, sit in praesenti vita aliqua cognitio Dei per gratiam.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28682)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullus intellectus creatus possit Deum per essentiam videre. Chrysostomus enim, super Ioannem, exponens illud quod dicitur Ioan. I, *Deum nemo vidit unquam*, sic dicit, *ipsum quod est Deus, non solum prophetae, sed nec Angeli viderunt nec Archangeli, quod enim creabilis est naturae, qualiter videre poterit quod increabile est?* Dionysius etiam, I cap. de Div. Nom., loquens de Deo, dicit, *neque sensus est eius, neque phantasia, neque opinio, nec ratio, nec scientia*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28683)\n\nPraeterea, omne infinitum, inquantum huiusmodi, est ignotum. Sed Deus est infinitus, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo secundum se est ignotus.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28684)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus creatus non est cognoscitivus nisi existentium, primum enim quod cadit in apprehensione intellectus, est ens. Sed Deus non est existens, sed supra existentia. Ut dicit Dionysius. Ergo non est intelligibilis; sed est supra omnem intellectum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28685)\n\nPraeterea, cognoscentis ad cognitum oportet esse aliquam proportionem, cum cognitum sit perfectio cognoscentis. Sed nulla est proportio intellectus creati ad Deum, quia in infinitum distant. Ergo intellectus creatus non potest videre essentiam Dei.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28686)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur I Ioan. III, *videbimus eum sicuti est*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28687)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum unumquodque sit cognoscibile secundum quod est in actu, Deus, qui est actus purus absque omni permixtione potentiae, quantum in se est, maxime cognoscibilis est. Sed quod est maxime cognoscibile in se, alicui intellectui cognoscibile non est, propter excessum intelligibilis supra intellectum, sicut sol, qui est maxime visibilis, videri non potest a vespertilione, propter excessum luminis. Hoc igitur attendentes, quidam posuerunt quod nullus intellectus creatus essentiam Dei videre potest. Sed hoc inconvenienter dicitur. Cum enim ultima hominis beatitudo in altissima eius operatione consistat, quae est operatio intellectus, si nunquam essentiam Dei videre potest intellectus creatus, vel nunquam beatitudinem obtinebit, vel in alio eius beatitudo consistet quam in Deo. Quod est alienum a fide. In ipso enim est ultima perfectio rationalis creaturae, quia est ei principium essendi, intantum enim unumquodque perfectum est, inquantum ad suum principium attingit. Similiter etiam est praeter rationem. Inest enim homini naturale desiderium cognoscendi causam, cum intuetur effectum; et ex hoc admiratio in hominibus consurgit. Si igitur intellectus rationalis creaturae pertingere non possit ad primam causam rerum, remanebit inane desiderium naturae. Unde simpliciter concedendum est quod beati Dei essentiam videant.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28688)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod utraque auctoritas loquitur de visione comprehensionis. Unde praemittit Dionysius immediate ante verba proposita, dicens, *omnibus ipse est universaliter incomprehensibilis, et nec sensus est*, et cetera. Et Chrysostomus parum post verba praedicta subdit, *visionem hic dicit certissimam patris considerationem et comprehensionem, tantam quantam pater habet de filio*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28689)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod infinitum quod se tenet ex parte materiae non perfectae per formam, ignotum est secundum se, quia omnis cognitio est per formam. Sed infinitum quod se tenet ex parte formae non limitatae per materiam, est secundum se maxime notum. Sic autem Deus est infinitus, et non primo modo, ut ex superioribus patet.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28690)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Deus non sic dicitur non existens, quasi nullo modo sit existens, sed quia est supra omne existens, inquantum est suum esse. Unde ex hoc non sequitur quod nullo modo possit cognosci, sed quod omnem cognitionem excedat, quod est ipsum non comprehendi.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28691)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod proportio dicitur dupliciter. Uno modo, certa habitudo unius quantitatis ad alteram; secundum quod duplum, triplum et aequale sunt species proportionis. Alio modo, quaelibet habitudo unius ad alterum proportio dicitur. Et sic potest esse proportio creaturae ad Deum, inquantum se habet ad ipsum ut effectus ad causam, et ut potentia ad actum. Et secundum hoc, intellectus creatus proportionatus esse potest ad cognoscendum Deum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28692)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod essentia Dei ab intellectu creato per aliquam similitudinem videatur. Dicitur enim I Ioan. III, *scimus quoniam, cum apparuerit, similes ei erimus, et videbimus eum sicuti est*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28693)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, IX de Trin., *cum Deum novimus, fit aliqua Dei similitudo in nobis*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28694)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus in actu est intelligibile in actu, sicut sensus in actu est sensibile in actu. Hoc autem non est nisi inquantum informatur sensus similitudine rei sensibilis, et intellectus similitudine rei intellectae. Ergo, si Deus ab intellectu creato videtur in actu, oportet quod per aliquam similitudinem videatur.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28695)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XV de Trin., quod cum apostolus dicit *videmus nunc per speculum et in aenigmate, speculi et aenigmatis nomine, quaecumque similitudines ab ipso significatae intelligi possunt, quae accommodatae sunt ad intelligendum Deum*. Sed videre Deum per essentiam non est visio aenigmatica vel specularis, sed contra eam dividitur. Ergo divina essentia non videtur per similitudines.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28696)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ad visionem, tam sensibilem quam intellectualem, duo requiruntur, scilicet virtus visiva, et unio rei visae cum visu, non enim fit visio in actu, nisi per hoc quod res visa quodammodo est in vidente. Et in rebus quidem corporalibus, apparet quod res visa non potest esse in vidente per suam essentiam, sed solum per suam similitudinem, sicut similitudo lapidis est in oculo, per quam fit visio in actu, non autem ipsa substantia lapidis. Si autem esset una et eadem res, quae esset principium visivae virtutis, et quae esset res visa, oporteret videntem ab illa re et virtutem visivam habere, et formam per quam videret. Manifestum est autem quod Deus et est auctor intellectivae virtutis, et ab intellectu videri potest. Et cum ipsa intellectiva virtus creaturae non sit Dei essentia, relinquitur quod sit aliqua participata similitudo ipsius, qui est primus intellectus. Unde et virtus intellectualis creaturae lumen quoddam intelligibile dicitur, quasi a prima luce derivatum, sive hoc intelligatur de virtute naturali, sive de aliqua perfectione superaddita gratiae vel gloriae. Requiritur ergo ad videndum Deum aliqua Dei similitudo ex parte visivae potentiae, qua scilicet intellectus sit efficax ad videndum Deum. Sed ex parte visae rei, quam necesse est aliquo modo uniri videnti per nullam similitudinem creatam Dei essentia videri potest. Primo quidem, quia, sicut dicit Dionysius, I cap. de Div. Nom., per similitudines inferioris ordinis rerum nullo modo superiora possunt cognosci, sicut per speciem corporis non potest cognosci essentia rei incorporeae. Multo igitur minus per speciem creatam quamcumque potest essentia Dei videri. Secundo, quia essentia Dei est ipsum esse eius, ut supra ostensum est, quod nulli formae creatae competere potest. Non potest igitur aliqua forma creata esse similitudo repraesentans videnti Dei essentiam. Tertio, quia divina essentia est aliquod incircumscriptum, continens in se supereminenter quidquid potest significari vel intelligi ab intellectu creato. Et hoc nullo modo per aliquam speciem creatam repraesentari potest, quia omnis forma creata est determinata secundum aliquam rationem vel sapientiae, vel virtutis, vel ipsius esse, vel alicuius huiusmodi. Unde dicere Deum per similitudinem videri, est dicere divinam essentiam non videri, quod est erroneum. Dicendum ergo quod ad videndum Dei essentiam requiritur aliqua similitudo ex parte visivae potentiae, scilicet lumen gloriae, confortans intellectum ad videndum Deum, de quo dicitur in Psalmo, *in lumine tuo videbimus lumen*. Non autem per aliquam similitudinem creatam Dei essentia videri potest, quae ipsam divinam essentiam repraesentet ut in se est.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28697)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod auctoritas illa loquitur de similitudine quae est per participationem luminis gloriae.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28698)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Augustinus ibi loquitur de cognitione Dei quae habetur in via.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28699)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod divina essentia est ipsum esse. Unde, sicut aliae formae intelligibiles quae non sunt suum esse, uniuntur intellectui secundum aliquod esse quo informant ipsum intellectum et faciunt ipsum in actu; ita divina essentia unitur intellectui creato ut intellectum in actu, per seipsam faciens intellectum in actu.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28700)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod essentia Dei videri possit oculo corporali. Dicitur enim Iob XIX, *in carne mea videbo Deum,* etc.; et XLII, *auditu auris audivi te, nunc autem oculus meus videt te*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28701)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, ultimo de civitate Dei, cap. XXIX, *vis itaque praepollentior oculorum erit illorum* (scilicet glorificatorum), *non ut acutius videant quam quidam perhibentur videre serpentes vel aquilae (quantalibet enim acrimonia cernendi eadem animalia vigeant, nihil aliud possunt videre quam corpora), sed ut videant et incorporalia*. Quicumque autem potest videre incorporalia, potest elevari ad videndum Deum. Ergo oculus glorificatus potest videre Deum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28702)\n\nPraeterea, Deus potest videri ab homine visione imaginaria, dicitur enim Isaiae VI, *vidi dominum sedentem super solium,* et cetera. Sed visio imaginaria a sensu originem habet, *phantasia enim est motus factus a sensu secundum actum*, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo Deus sensibili visione videri potest.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28703)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de videndo Deum ad Paulinam, *Deum nemo vidit unquam, vel in hac vita, sicut ipse est; vel in Angelorum vita, sicut visibilia ista quae corporali visione cernuntur*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28704)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est Deum videri sensu visus, vel quocumque alio sensu aut potentia sensitivae partis. Omnis enim potentia huiusmodi est actus corporalis organi, ut infra dicetur. Actus autem proportionatur ei cuius est actus. Unde nulla huiusmodi potentia potest se extendere ultra corporalia. Deus autem incorporeus est, ut supra ostensum est. Unde nec sensu nec imaginatione videri potest, sed solo intellectu.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28705)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur *in carne mea videbo Deum, salvatorem meum*, non intelligitur quod oculo carnis sit Deum visurus, sed quod in carne existens, post resurrectionem, visurus sit Deum. Similiter quod dicitur, *nunc oculus meus videt te*, intelligitur de oculo mentis, sicut Ephes. I dicit apostolus, *det vobis spiritum sapientiae in agnitione eius, illuminatos oculos cordis vestri*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28706)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur inquirendo in verbis illis, et sub conditione. Quod patet ex hoc quod praemittitur, longe itaque potentiae alterius erunt (scilicet oculi glorificati), si per eos videbitur incorporea illa natura. Sed postmodum hoc determinat, dicens, *valde credibile est sic nos visuros mundana tunc corpora caeli novi et terrae novae, ut Deum ubique praesentem, et universa etiam corporalia gubernantem, clarissima perspicuitate videamus; non sicut nunc invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt intellecta conspiciuntur; sed sicut homines, inter quos viventes motusque vitales exerentes vivimus, mox ut aspicimus, non credimus vivere, sed videmus*. Ex quo patet quod hoc modo intelligit oculos glorificatos Deum visuros, sicut nunc oculi nostri vident alicuius vitam. Vita autem non videtur oculo corporali, sicut per se visibile, sed sicut sensibile per accidens, quod quidem a sensu non cognoscitur, sed statim cum sensu ab aliqua alia virtute cognoscitiva. Quod autem statim, visis corporibus, divina praesentia ex eis cognoscatur per intellectum, ex duobus contingit, scilicet ex perspicacitate intellectus; et ex refulgentia divinae claritatis in corporibus innovatis.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28707)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in visione imaginaria non videtur Dei essentia, sed aliqua forma in imaginatione formatur, repraesentans Deum secundum aliquem modum similitudinis, prout in Scripturis divinis divina per res sensibiles metaphorice describuntur.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28708)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquis intellectus creatus per sua naturalia divinam essentiam videre possit. Dicit enim Dionysius, cap. IV de Div. Nom., quod Angelus est speculum purum, clarissimum, *suscipiens totam, si fas est dicere, pulchritudinem Dei*. Sed unumquodque videtur dum videtur eius speculum. Cum igitur Angelus per sua naturalia intelligat seipsum, videtur quod etiam per sua naturalia intelligat divinam essentiam.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28709)\n\nPraeterea, illud quod est maxime visibile, fit minus visibile nobis propter defectum nostri visus, vel corporalis vel intellectualis. Sed intellectus Angeli non patitur aliquem defectum. Cum ergo Deus secundum se sit maxime intelligibilis, videtur quod ab Angelo sit maxime intelligibilis. Si igitur alia intelligibilia per sua naturalia intelligere potest, multo magis Deum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28710)\n\nPraeterea, sensus corporeus non potest elevari ad intelligendam substantiam incorpoream, quia est supra eius naturam. Si igitur videre Deum per essentiam sit supra naturam cuiuslibet intellectus creati, videtur quod nullus intellectus creatus ad videndum Dei essentiam pertingere possit, quod est erroneum, ut ex supradictis patet. Videtur ergo quod intellectui creato sit naturale divinam essentiam videre.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28711)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Rom. VI, *gratia Dei vita aeterna*. Sed vita aeterna consistit in visione divinae essentiae, secundum illud Ioan. XVII, *haec est vita aeterna, ut cognoscant te solum verum Deum,* et cetera. Ergo videre Dei essentiam convenit intellectui creato per gratiam, et non per naturam.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28712)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est quod aliquis intellectus creatus per sua naturalia essentiam Dei videat. Cognitio enim contingit secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente. Cognitum autem est in cognoscente secundum modum cognoscentis. Unde cuiuslibet cognoscentis cognitio est secundum modum suae naturae. Si igitur modus essendi alicuius rei cognitae excedat modum naturae cognoscentis, oportet quod cognitio illius rei sit supra naturam illius cognoscentis. Est autem multiplex modus essendi rerum. Quaedam enim sunt, quorum natura non habet esse nisi in hac materia individuali, et huiusmodi sunt omnia corporalia. Quaedam vero sunt, quorum naturae sunt per se subsistentes, non in materia aliqua, quae tamen non sunt suum esse, sed sunt esse habentes, et huiusmodi sunt substantiae incorporeae, quas Angelos dicimus. Solius autem Dei proprius modus essendi est, ut sit suum esse subsistens. Ea igitur quae non habent esse nisi in materia individuali, cognoscere est nobis connaturale, eo quod anima nostra, per quam cognoscimus, est forma alicuius materiae. Quae tamen habet duas virtutes cognoscitivas. Unam, quae est actus alicuius corporei organi. Et huic connaturale est cognoscere res secundum quod sunt in materia individuali, unde sensus non cognoscit nisi singularia. Alia vero virtus cognoscitiva eius est intellectus, qui non est actus alicuius organi corporalis. Unde per intellectum connaturale est nobis cognoscere naturas, quae quidem non habent esse nisi in materia individuali; non tamen secundum quod sunt in materia individuali, sed secundum quod abstrahuntur ab ea per considerationem intellectus. Unde secundum intellectum possumus cognoscere huiusmodi res in universali, quod est supra facultatem sensus. Intellectui autem angelico connaturale est cognoscere naturas non in materia existentes. Quod est supra naturalem facultatem intellectus animae humanae, secundum statum praesentis vitae, quo corpori unitur. Relinquitur ergo quod cognoscere ipsum esse subsistens, sit connaturale soli intellectui divino, et quod sit supra facultatem naturalem cuiuslibet intellectus creati, quia nulla creatura est suum esse, sed habet esse participatum. Non igitur potest intellectus creatus Deum per essentiam videre, nisi inquantum Deus per suam gratiam se intellectui creato coniungit, ut intelligibile ab ipso.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28713)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod iste modus cognoscendi Deum, est Angelo connaturalis, ut scilicet cognoscat eum per similitudinem eius in ipso Angelo refulgentem. Sed cognoscere Deum per aliquam similitudinem creatam, non est cognoscere essentiam Dei, ut supra ostensum est. Unde non sequitur quod Angelus per sua naturalia possit cognoscere essentiam Dei.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28714)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus Angeli non habet defectum, si defectus accipiatur privative, ut scilicet careat eo quod habere debet. Si vero accipiatur negative, sic quaelibet creatura invenitur deficiens, Deo comparata, dum non habet illam excellentiam quae invenitur in Deo.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28715)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod sensus visus, quia omnino materialis est, nullo modo elevari potest ad aliquid immateriale. Sed intellectus noster vel angelicus, quia secundum naturam a materia aliqualiter elevatus est, potest ultra suam naturam per gratiam ad aliquid altius elevari. Et huius signum est, quia visus nullo modo potest in abstractione cognoscere id quod in concretione cognoscit, nullo enim modo potest percipere naturam, nisi ut hanc. Sed intellectus noster potest in abstractione considerare quod in concretione cognoscit. Etsi enim cognoscat res habentes formam in materia, tamen resolvit compositum in utrumque, et considerat ipsam formam per se. Et similiter intellectus Angeli, licet connaturale sit ei cognoscere esse concretum in aliqua natura, tamen potest ipsum esse secernere per intellectum, dum cognoscit quod aliud est ipse, et aliud est suum esse. Et ideo, cum intellectus creatus per suam naturam natus sit apprehendere formam concretam et esse concretum in abstractione, per modum resolutionis cuiusdam, potest per gratiam elevari ut cognoscat substantiam separatam subsistentem, et esse separatum subsistens.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28716)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus creatus ad videndum essentiam Dei aliquo lumine creato non indigeat. Illud enim quod est per se lucidum in rebus sensibilibus, alio lumine non indiget ut videatur, ergo nec in intelligibilibus. Sed Deus est lux intelligibilis. Ergo non videtur per aliquod lumen creatum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28717)\n\nPraeterea, cum Deus videtur per medium, non videtur per suam essentiam. Sed cum videtur per aliquod lumen creatum, videtur per medium. Ergo non videtur per suam essentiam.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28718)\n\nPraeterea, illud quod est creatum, nihil prohibet alicui creaturae esse naturale. Si ergo per aliquod lumen creatum Dei essentia videtur, poterit illud lumen esse naturale alicui creaturae. Et ita illa creatura non indigebit aliquo alio lumine ad videndum Deum, quod est impossibile. Non est ergo necessarium quod omnis creatura ad videndum Dei essentiam lumen superadditum requirat.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28719)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo, *in lumine tuo videbimus lumen*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28720)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod omne quod elevatur ad aliquid quod excedit suam naturam, oportet quod disponatur aliqua dispositione quae sit supra suam naturam, sicut, si aer debeat accipere formam ignis, oportet quod disponatur aliqua dispositione ad talem formam. Cum autem aliquis intellectus creatus videt Deum per essentiam, ipsa essentia Dei fit forma intelligibilis intellectus. Unde oportet quod aliqua dispositio supernaturalis ei superaddatur, ad hoc quod elevetur in tantam sublimitatem. Cum igitur virtus naturalis intellectus creati non sufficiat ad Dei essentiam videndam, ut ostensum est, oportet quod ex divina gratia superaccrescat ei virtus intelligendi. Et hoc augmentum virtutis intellectivae illuminationem intellectus vocamus; sicut et ipsum intelligibile vocatur lumen vel lux. Et istud est lumen de quo dicitur Apoc. XXI, *quod claritas Dei illuminabit eam*, scilicet societatem beatorum Deum videntium. Et secundum hoc lumen efficiuntur deiformes, idest Deo similes; secundum illud I Ioan. III, *cum apparuerit, similes ei erimus, et videbimus eum sicuti est*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28721)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod lumen creatum est necessarium ad videndum Dei essentiam, non quod per hoc lumen Dei essentia intelligibilis fiat, quae secundum se intelligibilis est, sed ad hoc quod intellectus fiat potens ad intelligendum, per modum quo potentia fit potentior ad operandum per habitum, sicut etiam et lumen corporale necessarium est in visu exteriori, inquantum facit medium transparens in actu, ut possit moveri a colore.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28722)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod lumen istud non requiritur ad videndum Dei essentiam quasi similitudo in qua Deus videatur, sed quasi perfectio quaedam intellectus, confortans ipsum ad videndum Deum. Et ideo potest dici quod non est medium in quo Deus videatur, sed sub quo videtur. Et hoc non tollit immediatam visionem Dei.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28723)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod dispositio ad formam ignis non potest esse naturalis nisi habenti formam ignis. Unde lumen gloriae non potest esse naturale creaturae, nisi creatura esset naturae divinae, quod est impossibile. Per hoc enim lumen fit creatura rationalis deiformis, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28724)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod videntium essentiam Dei unus alio perfectius non videat. Dicitur enim I Ioan. III, *videbimus eum sicuti est*. Sed ipse uno modo est. Ergo uno modo videbitur ab omnibus. Non ergo perfectius et minus perfecte.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28725)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium qq., quod unam rem non potest unus alio plus intelligere. Sed omnes videntes Deum per essentiam, intelligunt Dei essentiam, intellectu enim videtur Deus, non sensu, ut supra habitum est. Ergo videntium divinam essentiam unus alio non clarius videt.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28726)\n\nPraeterea, quod aliquid altero perfectius videatur, ex duobus contingere potest, vel ex parte obiecti visibilis; vel ex parte potentiae visivae videntis. Ex parte autem obiecti, per hoc quod obiectum perfectius in vidente recipitur, scilicet secundum perfectiorem similitudinem. Quod in proposito locum non habet, Deus enim non per aliquam similitudinem, sed per eius essentiam praesens est intellectui essentiam eius videnti. Relinquitur ergo quod, si unus alio perfectius eum videat, quod hoc sit secundum differentiam potentiae intellectivae. Et ita sequitur quod cuius potentia intellectiva naturaliter est sublimior, clarius eum videat. Quod est inconveniens, cum hominibus promittatur in beatitudine aequalitas Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28727)\n\nSed contra est quod vita aeterna in visione Dei consistit, secundum illud Ioan. XVII, *haec est vita aeterna,* et cetera. Ergo, si omnes aequaliter Dei essentiam vident, in vita aeterna omnes erunt aequales. Cuius contrarium dicit apostolus, I Cor. XV, *stella differt a stella in claritate*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28728)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod videntium Deum per essentiam unus alio perfectius eum videbit. Quod quidem non erit per aliquam Dei similitudinem perfectiorem in uno quam in alio, cum illa visio non sit futura per aliquam similitudinem, ut ostensum est. Sed hoc erit per hoc, quod intellectus unius habebit maiorem virtutem seu facultatem ad videndum Deum, quam alterius. Facultas autem videndi Deum non competit intellectui creato secundum suam naturam, sed per lumen gloriae, quod intellectum in quadam deiformitate constituit, ut ex superioribus patet. Unde intellectus plus participans de lumine gloriae, perfectius Deum videbit. Plus autem participabit de lumine gloriae, qui plus habet de caritate, quia ubi est maior caritas, ibi est maius desiderium; et desiderium quodammodo facit desiderantem aptum et paratum ad susceptionem desiderati. Unde qui plus habebit de caritate, perfectius Deum videbit, et beatior erit.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28729)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur videbimus eum sicuti est, hoc adverbium sicuti determinat modum visionis ex parte rei visae ut sit sensus, videbimus eum ita esse sicuti est, quia ipsum esse eius videbimus, quod est eius essentia. Non autem determinat modum visionis ex parte videntis, ut sit sensus, quod ita erit perfectus modus videndi, sicut est in Deo perfectus modus essendi.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28730)\n\nEt per hoc etiam patet solutio ad secundum. Cum enim dicitur quod rem unam unus alio melius non intelligit, hoc habet veritatem si referatur ad modum rei intellectae, quia quicumque intelligit rem esse aliter quam sit, non vere intelligit. Non autem si referatur ad modum intelligendi, quia intelligere unius est perfectius quam intelligere alterius.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28731)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod diversitas videndi non erit ex parte obiecti, quia idem obiectum omnibus praesentabitur, scilicet Dei essentia, nec ex diversa participatione obiecti per differentes similitudines, sed erit per diversam facultatem intellectus, non quidem naturalem, sed gloriosam, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28732)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod videntes Deum per essentiam ipsum comprehendant. Dicit enim apostolus, Philipp. III, *sequor autem si quo modo comprehendam*. Non autem frustra sequebatur, dicit enim ipse, I Cor. IX, *sic curro, non quasi in incertum*. Ergo ipse comprehendit, et eadem ratione alii, quos ad hoc invitat, dicens, *sic currite, ut comprehendatis*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28733)\n\nPraeterea, ut dicit Augustinus in libro de videndo Deum ad Paulinam, *illud comprehenditur, quod ita totum videtur, ut nihil eius lateat videntem*. Sed si Deus per essentiam videtur, totus videtur, et nihil eius latet videntem; cum Deus sit simplex. Ergo a quocumque videtur per essentiam, comprehenditur.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28734)\n\nSi dicatur quod videtur totus, sed non totaliter, contra, totaliter vel dicit modum videntis, vel modum rei visae. Sed ille qui videt Deum per essentiam, videt eum totaliter, si significetur modus rei visae, quia videt eum sicuti est, ut dictum est. Similiter videt eum totaliter, si significetur modus videntis, quia tota virtute sua intellectus Dei essentiam videbit. Quilibet ergo videns Deum per essentiam, totaliter eum videbit. Ergo eum comprehendet.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28735)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ierem. XXXII, *fortissime, magne, potens, dominus exercituum nomen tibi; magnus consilio, et incomprehensibilis cogitatu*. Ergo comprehendi non potest.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28736)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod comprehendere Deum impossibile est cuicumque intellectui creato, *attingere vero mente Deum qualitercumque, magna est beatitudo*, ut dicit Augustinus. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod illud comprehenditur, quod perfecte cognoscitur. Perfecte autem cognoscitur, quod tantum cognoscitur, quantum est cognoscibile. Unde si id quod est cognoscibile per scientiam demonstrativam, opinione teneatur ex aliqua ratione probabili concepta, non comprehenditur. Puta, si hoc quod est triangulum habere tres angulos aequales duobus rectis, aliquis sciat per demonstrationem, comprehendit illud, si vero aliquis eius opinionem accipiat probabiliter, per hoc quod a sapientibus vel pluribus ita dicitur, non comprehendet ipsum, quia non pertingit ad illum perfectum modum cognitionis, quo cognoscibilis est. Nullus autem intellectus creatus pertingere potest ad illum perfectum modum cognitionis divinae essentiae, quo cognoscibilis est. Quod sic patet. Unumquodque enim sic cognoscibile est, secundum quod est ens actu. Deus igitur, cuius esse est infinitum, ut supra ostensum est, infinite cognoscibilis est. Nullus autem intellectus creatus potest Deum infinite cognoscere. Intantum enim intellectus creatus divinam essentiam perfectius vel minus perfecte cognoscit, inquantum maiori vel minori lumine gloriae perfunditur. Cum igitur lumen gloriae creatum, in quocumque intellectu creato receptum, non possit esse infinitum, impossibile est quod aliquis intellectus creatus Deum infinite cognoscat. Unde impossibile est quod Deum comprehendat.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28737)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod comprehensio dicitur dupliciter. Uno modo, stricte et proprie, secundum quod aliquid includitur in comprehendente. Et sic nullo modo Deus comprehenditur, nec intellectu nec aliquo alio, quia, cum sit infinitus, nullo finito includi potest, ut aliquid finitum eum infinite capiat, sicut ipse infinite est. Et sic de comprehensione nunc quaeritur. Alio modo comprehensio largius sumitur, secundum quod comprehensio insecutioni opponitur. Qui enim attingit aliquem, quando iam tenet ipsum, comprehendere eum dicitur. Et sic Deus comprehenditur a beatis, secundum illud Cant. III, *tenui eum, nec dimittam*. Et sic intelliguntur auctoritates apostoli de comprehensione. Et hoc modo comprehensio est una de tribus dotibus animae, quae respondet spei; sicut visio fidei, et fruitio caritati. Non enim, apud nos, omne quod videtur, iam tenetur vel habetur, quia videntur interdum distantia, vel quae non sunt in potestate nostra. Neque iterum omnibus quae habemus, fruimur, vel quia non delectamur in eis; vel quia non sunt ultimus finis desiderii nostri, ut desiderium nostrum impleant et quietent. Sed haec tria habent beati in Deo, quia et vident ipsum; et videndo, tenent sibi praesentem, in potestate habentes semper eum videre; et tenentes, fruuntur sicut ultimo fine desiderium implente.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28738)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non propter hoc Deus incomprehensibilis dicitur, quasi aliquid eius sit quod non videatur, sed quia non ita perfecte videtur, sicut visibilis est. Sicut cum aliqua demonstrabilis propositio per aliquam probabilem rationem cognoscitur, non est aliquid eius quod non cognoscatur, nec subiectum, nec praedicatum, nec compositio, sed tota non ita perfecte cognoscitur, sicut cognoscibilis est. Unde Augustinus, definiendo comprehensionem, dicit quod *totum comprehenditur videndo, quod ita videtur, ut nihil eius lateat videntem; aut cuius fines circumspici possunt*, tunc enim fines alicuius circumspiciuntur, quando ad finem in modo cognoscendi illam rem pervenitur.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28739)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod totaliter dicit modum obiecti, non quidem ita quod totus modus obiecti non cadat sub cognitione; sed quia modus obiecti non est modus cognoscentis. Qui igitur videt Deum per essentiam, videt hoc in eo, quod infinite existit, et infinite cognoscibilis est, sed hic infinitus modus non competit ei, ut scilicet ipse infinite cognoscat, sicut aliquis probabiliter scire potest aliquam propositionem esse demonstrabilem, licet ipse eam demonstrative non cognoscat.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28740)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod videntes Deum per essentiam omnia in Deo videant. Dicit enim Gregorius, in IV Dialog. *quid est quod non videant, qui videntem omnia vident?* Sed Deus est videns omnia. Ergo qui vident Deum, omnia vident.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28741)\n\nItem, quicumque videt speculum, videt ea quae in speculo resplendent. Sed omnia quaecumque fiunt vel fieri possunt, in Deo resplendent sicut in quodam speculo, ipse enim omnia in seipso cognoscit. Ergo quicumque videt Deum, videt omnia quae sunt et quae fieri possunt.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28742)\n\nPraeterea, qui intelligit id quod est maius, potest intelligere minima, ut dicitur III de anima. Sed omnia quae Deus facit vel facere potest, sunt minus quam eius essentia. Ergo quicumque intelligit Deum, potest intelligere omnia quae Deus facit vel facere potest.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28743)\n\nPraeterea, rationalis creatura omnia naturaliter scire desiderat. Si igitur videndo Deum non omnia sciat, non quietatur eius naturale desiderium, et ita, videndo Deum non erit beata. Quod est inconveniens. Videndo igitur Deum, omnia scit.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28744)\n\nSed contra est quod Angeli vident Deum per essentiam, et tamen non omnia sciunt. Inferiores enim Angeli purgantur a superioribus a nescientia, ut dicit Dionysius, VII cap. Cael. Hier. Ipsi etiam nesciunt futura contingentia et cogitationes cordium, hoc enim solius Dei est. Non ergo quicumque vident Dei essentiam, vident omnia.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28745)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod intellectus creatus, videndo divinam essentiam, non videt in ipsa omnia quae facit Deus vel facere potest. Manifestum est enim quod sic aliqua videntur in Deo, secundum quod sunt in ipso. Omnia autem alia sunt in Deo, sicut effectus sunt virtute in sua causa. Sic igitur videntur alia in Deo, sicut effectus in sua causa. Sed manifestum est quod quanto aliqua causa perfectius videtur, tanto plures eius effectus in ipsa videri possunt. Qui enim habet intellectum elevatum, statim uno principio demonstrativo proposito, ex ipso multarum conclusionum cognitionem accipit, quod non convenit ei qui debilioris intellectus est, sed oportet quod ei singula explanentur. Ille igitur intellectus potest in causa cognoscere omnes causae effectus, et omnes rationes effectuum, qui causam totaliter comprehendit. Nullus autem intellectus creatus totaliter Deum comprehendere potest, ut ostensum est. Nullus igitur intellectus creatus, videndo Deum, potest cognoscere omnia quae Deus facit vel potest facere, hoc enim esset comprehendere eius virtutem. Sed horum quae Deus facit vel facere potest, tanto aliquis intellectus plura cognoscit, quanto perfectius Deum videt.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28746)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Gregorius loquitur quantum ad sufficientiam obiecti, scilicet Dei, quod, quantum in se est, sufficienter continet omnia et demonstrat. Non tamen sequitur quod unusquisque videns Deum omnia cognoscat, quia non perfecte comprehendit ipsum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28747)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod videns speculum, non est necessarium quod omnia in speculo videat, nisi speculum visu suo comprehendat.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28748)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet maius sit videre Deum quam omnia alia, tamen maius est videre sic Deum quod omnia in eo cognoscantur, quam videre sic ipsum quod non omnia, sed pauciora vel plura cognoscantur in eo. Iam enim ostensum est quod multitudo cognitorum in Deo, consequitur modum videndi ipsum vel magis perfectum vel minus perfectum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28749)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod naturale desiderium rationalis creaturae est ad sciendum omnia illa quae pertinent ad perfectionem intellectus; et haec sunt species et genera rerum, et rationes earum, quae in Deo videbit quilibet videns essentiam divinam. Cognoscere autem alia singularia, et cogitata et facta eorum, non est de perfectione intellectus creati, nec ad hoc eius naturale desiderium tendit, nec iterum cognoscere illa quae nondum sunt, sed fieri a Deo possunt. Si tamen solus Deus videretur, qui est fons et principium totius esse et veritatis, ita repleret naturale desiderium sciendi, quod nihil aliud quaereretur, et beatus esset. Unde dicit Augustinus, V Confess., infelix homo qui scit omnia illa (scilicet creaturas), *te autem nescit, beatus autem qui te scit, etiam si illa nesciat. Qui vero te et illa novit, non propter illa beatior est, sed propter te solum beatus*.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 12 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28750)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ea quae videntur in Deo, a videntibus divinam essentiam per aliquas similitudines videantur. Omnis enim cognitio est per assimilationem cognoscentis ad cognitum, sic enim intellectus in actu fit intellectum in actu, et sensus in actu sensibile in actu, inquantum eius similitudine informatur, ut pupilla similitudine coloris. Si igitur intellectus videntis Deum per essentiam intelligat in Deo aliquas creaturas, oportet quod earum similitudinibus informetur.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28751)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae prius vidimus, memoriter tenemus. Sed Paulus, videns in raptu essentiam Dei, ut dicit Augustinus XII super Gen. ad litteram, postquam desiit essentiam Dei videre, recordatus est multorum quae in illo raptu viderat, unde ipse dicit quod *audivit arcana verba, quae non licet homini loqui*, II Cor. XII. Ergo oportet dicere quod aliquae similitudines eorum quae recordatus est, in eius intellectu remanserint. Et eadem ratione, quando praesentialiter videbat Dei essentiam, eorum quae in ipsa videbat, aliquas similitudines vel species habebat.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28752)\n\nSed contra est quod per unam speciem videtur speculum, et ea quae in speculo apparent. Sed omnia sic videntur in Deo sicut in quodam speculo intelligibili. Ergo, si ipse Deus non videtur per aliquam similitudinem, sed per suam essentiam; nec ea quae in ipso videntur, per aliquas similitudines sive species videntur.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28753)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod videntes Deum per essentiam, ea quae in ipsa essentia Dei vident, non vident per aliquas species, sed per ipsam essentiam divinam intellectui eorum unitam. Sic enim cognoscitur unumquodque, secundum quod similitudo eius est in cognoscente. Sed hoc contingit dupliciter. Cum enim quaecumque uni et eidem sunt similia, sibi invicem sint similia, virtus cognoscitiva dupliciter assimilari potest alicui cognoscibili. Uno modo, secundum se, quando directe eius similitudine informatur, et tunc cognoscitur illud secundum se. Alio modo, secundum quod informatur specie alicuius quod est ei simile, et tunc non dicitur res cognosci in seipsa, sed in suo simili. Alia enim est cognitio qua cognoscitur aliquis homo in seipso, et alia qua cognoscitur in sua imagine. Sic ergo, cognoscere res per earum similitudines in cognoscente existentes, est cognoscere eas in seipsis, seu in propriis naturis, sed cognoscere eas prout earum similitudines praeexistunt in Deo, est videre eas in Deo. Et hae duae cognitiones differunt. Unde secundum illam cognitionem qua res cognoscuntur a videntibus Deum per essentiam in ipso Deo, non videntur per aliquas similitudines alias; sed per solam essentiam divinam intellectui praesentem, per quam et Deus videtur.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28754)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus videntis Deum assimilatur rebus quae videntur in Deo, inquantum unitur essentiae divinae, in qua rerum omnium similitudines praeexistunt.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28755)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliquae potentiae cognoscitivae sunt, quae ex speciebus primo conceptis alias formare possunt. Sicut imaginatio ex praeconceptis speciebus montis et auri, format speciem montis aurei, et intellectus ex praeconceptis speciebus generis et differentiae, format rationem speciei. Et similiter ex similitudine imaginis formare possumus in nobis similitudinem eius cuius est imago. Et sic Paulus, vel quicumque alius videns Deum, ex ipsa visione essentiae divinae potest formare in se similitudines rerum quae in essentia divina videntur, quae remanserunt in Paulo etiam postquam desiit Dei essentiam videre. Ista tamen visio, qua videntur res per huiusmodi species sic conceptas, est alia a visione qua videntur res in Deo.\n\n## Articulus 10\n\n[I q. 12 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28756)\n\nAd decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod videntes Deum per essentiam non simul videant omnia quae in ipso vident. Quia, secundum philosophum, contingit multa scire, intelligere vero unum. Sed ea quae videntur in Deo, intelliguntur, intellectu enim videtur Deus. Ergo non contingit a videntibus Deum simul multa videri in Deo.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28757)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, VIII super Gen. ad litteram, quod *Deus movet creaturam spiritualem per tempus*, hoc est per intelligentiam et affectionem. Sed creatura spiritualis est Angelus, qui Deum videt. Ergo videntes Deum, successive intelligunt et afficiuntur, tempus enim successionem importat.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28758)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, ultimo de Trin., *non erunt volubiles nostrae cogitationes, ab aliis in alia euntes atque redeuntes; sed omnem scientiam nostram uno simul conspectu videbimus*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28759)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ea quae videntur in verbo, non successive, sed simul videntur. Ad cuius evidentiam considerandum est, quod ideo nos simul non possumus multa intelligere, quia multa per diversas species intelligimus; diversis autem speciebus non potest intellectus unus simul actu informari ad intelligendum per eas, sicut nec unum corpus potest simul diversis figuris figurari. Unde contingit quod, quando aliqua multa una specie intelligi possunt, simul intelliguntur, sicut diversae partes alicuius totius, si singulae propriis speciebus intelligantur, successive intelliguntur, et non simul; si autem omnes intelligantur una specie totius, simul intelliguntur. Ostensum est autem quod ea quae videntur in Deo, non videntur singula per suas similitudines, sed omnia per unam essentiam Dei. Unde simul, et non successive videntur.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28760)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sic unum tantum intelligimus, inquantum una specie intelligimus. Sed multa una specie intellecta simul intelliguntur, sicut in specie hominis intelligimus animal et rationale, et in specie domus parietem et tectum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28761)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angeli, quantum ad cognitionem naturalem qua cognoscunt res per species diversas eis inditas, non simul omnia cognoscunt, et sic moventur, secundum intelligentiam, per tempus. Sed secundum quod vident res in Deo, simul eas vident.\n\n## Articulus 11\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28762)\n\nAd undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquis in hac vita possit Deum per essentiam videre. Dicit enim Iacob, Gen. XXXII, *vidi Deum facie ad faciem*. Sed videre facie ad faciem, est videre per essentiam, ut patet per illud quod dicitur I Cor. XIII, *videmus nunc per speculum et in aenigmate, tunc autem facie ad faciem*. Ergo Deus in hac vita per essentiam videri potest.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28763)\n\nPraeterea, Num. XII dicit dominus de Moyse, *ore ad os loquor ei, et palam, et non per aenigmata et figuras, videt Deum*. Sed hoc est videre Deum per essentiam. Ergo aliquis in statu huius vitae potest Deum per essentiam videre.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28764)\n\nPraeterea, illud in quo alia omnia cognoscimus, et per quod de aliis iudicamus, est nobis secundum se notum. Sed omnia etiam nunc in Deo cognoscimus. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII Conf., *si ambo videmus verum esse quod dicis, et ambo videmus verum esse quod dico, ubi quaeso illud videmus? Nec ego in te, nec tu in me, sed ambo in ipsa quae supra mentes nostras est, incommutabili veritate*. Idem etiam, in libro de vera religione, dicit quod secundum veritatem divinam de omnibus iudicamus. Et XII de Trin. dicit quod *rationis est iudicare de istis corporalibus secundum rationes incorporales et sempiternas, quae nisi supra mentem essent, incommutabiles profecto non essent*. Ergo et in hac vita ipsum Deum videmus.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28765)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Augustinum, XII super Gen. ad Litt., visione intellectuali videntur ea quae sunt in anima per suam essentiam. Sed visio intellectualis est de rebus intelligibilibus, non per aliquas similitudines, sed per suas essentias, ut ipse ibidem dicit. Ergo, cum Deus sit per essentiam suam in anima nostra, per essentiam suam videtur a nobis.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28766)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Exod. XXXIII, *non videbit me homo et vivet*. Glossa, *quandiu hic mortaliter vivitur, videri per quasdam imagines Deus potest; sed per ipsam naturae suae speciem non potest*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28767)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ab homine puro Deus videri per essentiam non potest, nisi ab hac vita mortali separetur. Cuius ratio est quia, sicut supra dictum est, modus cognitionis sequitur modum naturae rei cognoscentis. Anima autem nostra, quandiu in hac vita vivimus, habet esse in materia corporali, unde naturaliter non cognoscit aliqua nisi quae habent formam in materia, vel quae per huiusmodi cognosci possunt. Manifestum est autem quod per naturas rerum materialium divina essentia cognosci non potest. Ostensum est enim supra quod cognitio Dei per quamcumque similitudinem creatam, non est visio essentiae ipsius. Unde impossibile est animae hominis secundum hanc vitam viventis, essentiam Dei videre. Et huius signum est, quod anima nostra, quanto magis a corporalibus abstrahitur, tanto intelligibilium abstractorum fit capacior. Unde in somniis et alienationibus a sensibus corporis, magis divinae revelationes percipiuntur, et praevisiones futurorum. Quod ergo anima elevetur usque ad supremum intelligibilium, quod est essentia divina, esse non potest quandiu hac mortali vita utitur.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28768)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Dionysium, IV cap. Cael. Hier., sic in Scripturis dicitur aliquis Deum vidisse, inquantum formatae sunt aliquae figurae, vel sensibiles vel imaginariae, secundum aliquam similitudinem aliquod divinum repraesentantes. Quod ergo dicit Iacob, vidi Deum facie ad faciem, referendum est, non ad ipsam divinam essentiam, sed ad figuram in qua repraesentabatur Deus. Et hoc ipsum ad quandam prophetiae eminentiam pertinet, ut videatur persona Dei loquentis, licet imaginaria visione, ut infra patebit, cum de gradibus prophetiae loquemur. Vel hoc dicit Iacob ad designandam quandam eminentiam intelligibilis contemplationis, supra communem statum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28769)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Deus miraculose aliquid supernaturaliter in rebus corporeis operatur, ita etiam et supernaturaliter, et praeter communem ordinem, mentes aliquorum in hac carne viventium, sed non sensibus carnis utentium, usque ad visionem suae essentiae elevavit; ut dicit Augustinus, XII super Genes. ad Litt., et in libro de videndo Deum de Moyse, qui fuit magister Iudaeorum, et Paulo, qui fuit magister gentium. Et de hoc plenius tractabitur, cum de raptu agemus.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28770)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnia dicimur in Deo videre, et secundum ipsum de omnibus iudicare, inquantum per participationem sui luminis omnia cognoscimus et diiudicamus, nam et ipsum lumen naturale rationis participatio quaedam est divini luminis; sicut etiam omnia sensibilia dicimus videre et iudicare in sole, idest per lumen solis. Unde dicit Augustinus, I Soliloquiorum, *disciplinarum spectamina videri non possunt, nisi aliquo velut suo sole illustrentur, videlicet Deo*. Sicut ergo ad videndum aliquid sensibiliter, non est necesse quod videatur substantia solis, ita ad videndum aliquid intelligibiliter, non est necessarium quod videatur essentia Dei.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28771)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod visio intellectualis est eorum quae sunt in anima per suam essentiam sicut intelligibilia in intellectu. Sic autem Deus est in anima beatorum, non autem in anima nostra; sed per praesentiam, essentiam, et potentiam.\n\n## Articulus 12\n\n[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28772)\n\nAd duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod per naturalem rationem Deum in hac vita cognoscere non possimus. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Consol., quod *ratio non capit simplicem formam*. Deus autem maxime est simplex forma, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo ad eius cognitionem ratio naturalis pervenire non potest.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28773)\n\nPraeterea, ratione naturali sine phantasmate nihil intelligit anima, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed Dei, cum sit incorporeus, phantasma in nobis esse non potest. Ergo cognosci non potest a nobis cognitione naturali.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28774)\n\nPraeterea, cognitio quae est per rationem naturalem, communis est bonis et malis, sicut natura eis communis est. Sed cognitio Dei competit tantum bonis, dicit enim Augustinus, I de Trin., quod *mentis humanae acies in tam excellenti luce non figitur, nisi per iustitiam fidei emundetur*. Ergo Deus per rationem naturalem cognosci non potest.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28775)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Rom. I, *quod notum est Dei, manifestum est in illis*, idest, quod cognoscibile est de Deo per rationem naturalem.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28776)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod naturalis nostra cognitio a sensu principium sumit, unde tantum se nostra naturalis cognitio extendere potest, inquantum manuduci potest per sensibilia. Ex sensibilibus autem non potest usque ad hoc intellectus noster pertingere, quod divinam essentiam videat, quia creaturae sensibiles sunt effectus Dei virtutem causae non adaequantes. Unde ex sensibilium cognitione non potest tota Dei virtus cognosci, et per consequens nec eius essentia videri. Sed quia sunt eius effectus a causa dependentes, ex eis in hoc perduci possumus, ut cognoscamus de Deo an est; et ut cognoscamus de ipso ea quae necesse est ei convenire secundum quod est prima omnium causa, excedens omnia sua causata. Unde cognoscimus de ipso habitudinem ipsius ad creaturas, quod scilicet omnium est causa; et differentiam creaturarum ab ipso, quod scilicet ipse non est aliquid eorum quae ab eo causantur; et quod haec non removentur ab eo propter eius defectum, sed quia superexcedit.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28777)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio ad formam simplicem pertingere non potest, ut sciat de ea quid est, potest tamen de ea cognoscere, ut sciat an est.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28778)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus naturali cognitione cognoscitur per phantasmata effectus sui.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28779)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod cognitio Dei per essentiam, cum sit per gratiam, non competit nisi bonis, sed cognitio eius quae est per rationem naturalem, potest competere bonis et malis. Unde dicit Augustinus, in libro Retractationum, *non approbo quod in oratione dixi, Deus, qui non nisi mundos verum scire voluisti, responderi enim potest, multos etiam non mundos multa scire vera*, scilicet per rationem naturalem.\n\n## Articulus 13\n\n[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28780)\n\nAd decimumtertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod per gratiam non habeatur altior cognitio Dei, quam ea quae habetur per naturalem rationem. Dicit enim Dionysius, in libro de mystica theologia, quod ille qui melius unitur Deo in hac vita, unitur ei sicut omnino ignoto, quod etiam de Moyse dicit, qui tamen excellentiam quandam obtinuit in gratiae cognitione. Sed coniungi Deo ignorando de eo quid est, hoc contingit etiam per rationem naturalem. Ergo per gratiam non plenius cognoscitur a nobis Deus, quam per rationem naturalem.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28781)\n\nPraeterea, per rationem naturalem in cognitionem divinorum pervenire non possumus, nisi per phantasmata, sic etiam nec secundum cognitionem gratiae. Dicit enim Dionysius, I cap. de Cael. Hier., quod *impossibile est nobis aliter lucere divinum radium, nisi varietate sacrorum velaminum circumvelatum*. Ergo per gratiam non plenius cognoscimus Deum, quam per rationem naturalem.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28782)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus noster per gratiam fidei Deo adhaeret. Fides autem non videtur esse cognitio, dicit enim Gregorius, in Homil., quod ea quae non videntur fidem habent, et non agnitionem. Ergo per gratiam non additur nobis aliqua excellentior cognitio de Deo.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28783)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit apostolus, I Cor. II, *nobis revelavit Deus per spiritum suum*, illa scilicet quae nemo principum huius saeculi novit, idest philosophorum, ut exponit Glossa.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28784)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod per gratiam perfectior cognitio de Deo habetur a nobis, quam per rationem naturalem. Quod sic patet. Cognitio enim quam per naturalem rationem habemus, duo requirit, scilicet, phantasmata ex sensibilibus accepta, et lumen naturale intelligibile, cuius virtute intelligibiles conceptiones ab eis abstrahimus. Et quantum ad utrumque, iuvatur humana cognitio per revelationem gratiae. Nam et lumen naturale intellectus confortatur per infusionem luminis gratuiti. Et interdum etiam phantasmata in imaginatione hominis formantur divinitus, magis exprimentia res divinas, quam ea quae naturaliter a sensibilibus accipimus; sicut apparet in visionibus prophetalibus. Et interdum etiam aliquae res sensibiles formantur divinitus, aut etiam voces, ad aliquid divinum exprimendum; sicut in Baptismo visus est spiritus sanctus in specie columbae, et vox patris audita est, *hic est filius meus dilectus*.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28785)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet per revelationem gratiae in hac vita non cognoscamus de Deo quid est, et sic ei quasi ignoto coniungamur; tamen plenius ipsum cognoscimus, inquantum plures et excellentiores effectus eius nobis demonstrantur; et inquantum ei aliqua attribuimus ex revelatione divina, ad quae ratio naturalis non pertingit, ut Deum esse trinum et unum.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28786)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ex phantasmatibus, vel a sensu acceptis secundum naturalem ordinem, vel divinitus in imaginatione formatis, tanto excellentior cognitio intellectualis habetur, quanto lumen intelligibile in homine fortius fuerit. Et sic per revelationem ex phantasmatibus plenior cognitio accipitur, ex infusione divini luminis.\n\n[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28787)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod fides cognitio quaedam est, inquantum intellectus determinatur per fidem ad aliquod cognoscibile. Sed haec determinatio ad unum non procedit ex visione credentis, sed a visione eius cui creditur. Et sic, inquantum deest visio, deficit a ratione cognitionis quae est in scientia, nam scientia determinat intellectum ad unum per visionem et intellectum primorum principiorum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0002","text":"[I q. 12 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","text":"Quia in superioribus consideravimus qualiter Deus sit secundum seipsum, restat considerandum qualiter sit in cognitione nostra, idest quomodo cognoscatur a creaturis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tredecim. Primo, utrum aliquis intellectus creatus possit videre essentiam Dei. Secundo, utrum Dei essentia videatur ab intellectu per aliquam speciem creatam. Tertio, utrum oculo corporeo Dei essentia possit videri. Quarto, utrum aliqua substantia intellectualis creata ex suis naturalibus sufficiens sit videre Dei essentiam. Quinto, utrum intellectus creatus ad videndam Dei essentiam indigeat aliquo lumine creato. Sexto, utrum videntium essentiam Dei unus alio perfectius videat. Septimo, utrum aliquis intellectus creatus possit comprehendere Dei essentiam. Octavo, utrum intellectus creatus videns Dei essentiam, omnia in ipsa cognoscat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","text":"Nono, utrum ea quae ibi cognoscit, per aliquas similitudines cognoscat. Decimo, utrum simul cognoscat omnia quae in Deo videt. Undecimo, utrum in statu huius vitae possit aliquis homo essentiam Dei videre. Duodecimo, utrum per rationem naturalem Deum in hac vita possimus cognoscere. Tertiodecimo, utrum, supra cognitionem naturalis rationis, sit in praesenti vita aliqua cognitio Dei per gratiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0006","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullus intellectus creatus possit Deum per essentiam videre. Chrysostomus enim, super Ioannem, exponens illud quod dicitur Ioan. I, *Deum nemo vidit unquam*, sic dicit, *ipsum quod est Deus, non solum prophetae, sed nec Angeli viderunt nec Archangeli, quod enim creabilis est naturae, qualiter videre poterit quod increabile est?* Dionysius etiam, I cap. de Div. Nom., loquens de Deo, dicit, *neque sensus est eius, neque phantasia, neque opinio, nec ratio, nec scientia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0008","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0009","text":"Praeterea, omne infinitum, inquantum huiusmodi, est ignotum. Sed Deus est infinitus, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo secundum se est ignotus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0010","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0011","text":"Praeterea, intellectus creatus non est cognoscitivus nisi existentium, primum enim quod cadit in apprehensione intellectus, est ens. Sed Deus non est existens, sed supra existentia. Ut dicit Dionysius. Ergo non est intelligibilis; sed est supra omnem intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0012","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0013","text":"Praeterea, cognoscentis ad cognitum oportet esse aliquam proportionem, cum cognitum sit perfectio cognoscentis. Sed nulla est proportio intellectus creati ad Deum, quia in infinitum distant. Ergo intellectus creatus non potest videre essentiam Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0014","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0015","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur I Ioan. III, *videbimus eum sicuti est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0016","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum unumquodque sit cognoscibile secundum quod est in actu, Deus, qui est actus purus absque omni permixtione potentiae, quantum in se est, maxime cognoscibilis est. Sed quod est maxime cognoscibile in se, alicui intellectui cognoscibile non est, propter excessum intelligibilis supra intellectum, sicut sol, qui est maxime visibilis, videri non potest a vespertilione, propter excessum luminis. Hoc igitur attendentes, quidam posuerunt quod nullus intellectus creatus essentiam Dei videre potest. Sed hoc inconvenienter dicitur. Cum enim ultima hominis beatitudo in altissima eius operatione consistat, quae est operatio intellectus, si nunquam essentiam Dei videre potest intellectus creatus, vel nunquam beatitudinem obtinebit, vel in alio eius beatitudo consistet quam in Deo. Quod est alienum a fide.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","text":"In ipso enim est ultima perfectio rationalis creaturae, quia est ei principium essendi, intantum enim unumquodque perfectum est, inquantum ad suum principium attingit. Similiter etiam est praeter rationem. Inest enim homini naturale desiderium cognoscendi causam, cum intuetur effectum; et ex hoc admiratio in hominibus consurgit. Si igitur intellectus rationalis creaturae pertingere non possit ad primam causam rerum, remanebit inane desiderium naturae. Unde simpliciter concedendum est quod beati Dei essentiam videant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0019","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod utraque auctoritas loquitur de visione comprehensionis. Unde praemittit Dionysius immediate ante verba proposita, dicens, *omnibus ipse est universaliter incomprehensibilis, et nec sensus est*, et cetera. Et Chrysostomus parum post verba praedicta subdit, *visionem hic dicit certissimam patris considerationem et comprehensionem, tantam quantam pater habet de filio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0021","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0022","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod infinitum quod se tenet ex parte materiae non perfectae per formam, ignotum est secundum se, quia omnis cognitio est per formam. Sed infinitum quod se tenet ex parte formae non limitatae per materiam, est secundum se maxime notum. Sic autem Deus est infinitus, et non primo modo, ut ex superioribus patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0023","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0024","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Deus non sic dicitur non existens, quasi nullo modo sit existens, sed quia est supra omne existens, inquantum est suum esse. Unde ex hoc non sequitur quod nullo modo possit cognosci, sed quod omnem cognitionem excedat, quod est ipsum non comprehendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0025","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0026","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod proportio dicitur dupliciter. Uno modo, certa habitudo unius quantitatis ad alteram; secundum quod duplum, triplum et aequale sunt species proportionis. Alio modo, quaelibet habitudo unius ad alterum proportio dicitur. Et sic potest esse proportio creaturae ad Deum, inquantum se habet ad ipsum ut effectus ad causam, et ut potentia ad actum. Et secundum hoc, intellectus creatus proportionatus esse potest ad cognoscendum Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0027","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0028","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0029","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod essentia Dei ab intellectu creato per aliquam similitudinem videatur. Dicitur enim I Ioan. III, *scimus quoniam, cum apparuerit, similes ei erimus, et videbimus eum sicuti est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0030","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0031","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, IX de Trin., *cum Deum novimus, fit aliqua Dei similitudo in nobis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0032","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0033","text":"Praeterea, intellectus in actu est intelligibile in actu, sicut sensus in actu est sensibile in actu. Hoc autem non est nisi inquantum informatur sensus similitudine rei sensibilis, et intellectus similitudine rei intellectae. Ergo, si Deus ab intellectu creato videtur in actu, oportet quod per aliquam similitudinem videatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0034","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0035","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XV de Trin., quod cum apostolus dicit *videmus nunc per speculum et in aenigmate, speculi et aenigmatis nomine, quaecumque similitudines ab ipso significatae intelligi possunt, quae accommodatae sunt ad intelligendum Deum*. Sed videre Deum per essentiam non est visio aenigmatica vel specularis, sed contra eam dividitur. Ergo divina essentia non videtur per similitudines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0036","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ad visionem, tam sensibilem quam intellectualem, duo requiruntur, scilicet virtus visiva, et unio rei visae cum visu, non enim fit visio in actu, nisi per hoc quod res visa quodammodo est in vidente. Et in rebus quidem corporalibus, apparet quod res visa non potest esse in vidente per suam essentiam, sed solum per suam similitudinem, sicut similitudo lapidis est in oculo, per quam fit visio in actu, non autem ipsa substantia lapidis. Si autem esset una et eadem res, quae esset principium visivae virtutis, et quae esset res visa, oporteret videntem ab illa re et virtutem visivam habere, et formam per quam videret. Manifestum est autem quod Deus et est auctor intellectivae virtutis, et ab intellectu videri potest. Et cum ipsa intellectiva virtus creaturae non sit Dei essentia, relinquitur quod sit aliqua participata similitudo ipsius, qui est primus intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","text":"Unde et virtus intellectualis creaturae lumen quoddam intelligibile dicitur, quasi a prima luce derivatum, sive hoc intelligatur de virtute naturali, sive de aliqua perfectione superaddita gratiae vel gloriae. Requiritur ergo ad videndum Deum aliqua Dei similitudo ex parte visivae potentiae, qua scilicet intellectus sit efficax ad videndum Deum. Sed ex parte visae rei, quam necesse est aliquo modo uniri videnti per nullam similitudinem creatam Dei essentia videri potest. Primo quidem, quia, sicut dicit Dionysius, I cap. de Div. Nom., per similitudines inferioris ordinis rerum nullo modo superiora possunt cognosci, sicut per speciem corporis non potest cognosci essentia rei incorporeae. Multo igitur minus per speciem creatam quamcumque potest essentia Dei videri. Secundo, quia essentia Dei est ipsum esse eius, ut supra ostensum est, quod nulli formae creatae competere potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","text":"Non potest igitur aliqua forma creata esse similitudo repraesentans videnti Dei essentiam. Tertio, quia divina essentia est aliquod incircumscriptum, continens in se supereminenter quidquid potest significari vel intelligi ab intellectu creato. Et hoc nullo modo per aliquam speciem creatam repraesentari potest, quia omnis forma creata est determinata secundum aliquam rationem vel sapientiae, vel virtutis, vel ipsius esse, vel alicuius huiusmodi. Unde dicere Deum per similitudinem videri, est dicere divinam essentiam non videri, quod est erroneum. Dicendum ergo quod ad videndum Dei essentiam requiritur aliqua similitudo ex parte visivae potentiae, scilicet lumen gloriae, confortans intellectum ad videndum Deum, de quo dicitur in Psalmo, *in lumine tuo videbimus lumen*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0040","text":"Non autem per aliquam similitudinem creatam Dei essentia videri potest, quae ipsam divinam essentiam repraesentet ut in se est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0041","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0042","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod auctoritas illa loquitur de similitudine quae est per participationem luminis gloriae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0043","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0044","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Augustinus ibi loquitur de cognitione Dei quae habetur in via.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0045","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod divina essentia est ipsum esse. Unde, sicut aliae formae intelligibiles quae non sunt suum esse, uniuntur intellectui secundum aliquod esse quo informant ipsum intellectum et faciunt ipsum in actu; ita divina essentia unitur intellectui creato ut intellectum in actu, per seipsam faciens intellectum in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0047","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0048","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0049","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod essentia Dei videri possit oculo corporali. Dicitur enim Iob XIX, *in carne mea videbo Deum,* etc.; et XLII, *auditu auris audivi te, nunc autem oculus meus videt te*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0050","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0051","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, ultimo de civitate Dei, cap. XXIX, *vis itaque praepollentior oculorum erit illorum* (scilicet glorificatorum), *non ut acutius videant quam quidam perhibentur videre serpentes vel aquilae (quantalibet enim acrimonia cernendi eadem animalia vigeant, nihil aliud possunt videre quam corpora), sed ut videant et incorporalia*. Quicumque autem potest videre incorporalia, potest elevari ad videndum Deum. Ergo oculus glorificatus potest videre Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0052","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0053","text":"Praeterea, Deus potest videri ab homine visione imaginaria, dicitur enim Isaiae VI, *vidi dominum sedentem super solium,* et cetera. Sed visio imaginaria a sensu originem habet, *phantasia enim est motus factus a sensu secundum actum*, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo Deus sensibili visione videri potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0054","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0055","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de videndo Deum ad Paulinam, *Deum nemo vidit unquam, vel in hac vita, sicut ipse est; vel in Angelorum vita, sicut visibilia ista quae corporali visione cernuntur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0056","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est Deum videri sensu visus, vel quocumque alio sensu aut potentia sensitivae partis. Omnis enim potentia huiusmodi est actus corporalis organi, ut infra dicetur. Actus autem proportionatur ei cuius est actus. Unde nulla huiusmodi potentia potest se extendere ultra corporalia. Deus autem incorporeus est, ut supra ostensum est. Unde nec sensu nec imaginatione videri potest, sed solo intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0058","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0059","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur *in carne mea videbo Deum, salvatorem meum*, non intelligitur quod oculo carnis sit Deum visurus, sed quod in carne existens, post resurrectionem, visurus sit Deum. Similiter quod dicitur, *nunc oculus meus videt te*, intelligitur de oculo mentis, sicut Ephes. I dicit apostolus, *det vobis spiritum sapientiae in agnitione eius, illuminatos oculos cordis vestri*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0060","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0061","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur inquirendo in verbis illis, et sub conditione. Quod patet ex hoc quod praemittitur, longe itaque potentiae alterius erunt (scilicet oculi glorificati), si per eos videbitur incorporea illa natura. Sed postmodum hoc determinat, dicens, *valde credibile est sic nos visuros mundana tunc corpora caeli novi et terrae novae, ut Deum ubique praesentem, et universa etiam corporalia gubernantem, clarissima perspicuitate videamus; non sicut nunc invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt intellecta conspiciuntur; sed sicut homines, inter quos viventes motusque vitales exerentes vivimus, mox ut aspicimus, non credimus vivere, sed videmus*. Ex quo patet quod hoc modo intelligit oculos glorificatos Deum visuros, sicut nunc oculi nostri vident alicuius vitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0062","text":"Vita autem non videtur oculo corporali, sicut per se visibile, sed sicut sensibile per accidens, quod quidem a sensu non cognoscitur, sed statim cum sensu ab aliqua alia virtute cognoscitiva. Quod autem statim, visis corporibus, divina praesentia ex eis cognoscatur per intellectum, ex duobus contingit, scilicet ex perspicacitate intellectus; et ex refulgentia divinae claritatis in corporibus innovatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0063","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0064","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in visione imaginaria non videtur Dei essentia, sed aliqua forma in imaginatione formatur, repraesentans Deum secundum aliquem modum similitudinis, prout in Scripturis divinis divina per res sensibiles metaphorice describuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0065","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0066","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0067","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquis intellectus creatus per sua naturalia divinam essentiam videre possit. Dicit enim Dionysius, cap. IV de Div. Nom., quod Angelus est speculum purum, clarissimum, *suscipiens totam, si fas est dicere, pulchritudinem Dei*. Sed unumquodque videtur dum videtur eius speculum. Cum igitur Angelus per sua naturalia intelligat seipsum, videtur quod etiam per sua naturalia intelligat divinam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0068","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0069","text":"Praeterea, illud quod est maxime visibile, fit minus visibile nobis propter defectum nostri visus, vel corporalis vel intellectualis. Sed intellectus Angeli non patitur aliquem defectum. Cum ergo Deus secundum se sit maxime intelligibilis, videtur quod ab Angelo sit maxime intelligibilis. Si igitur alia intelligibilia per sua naturalia intelligere potest, multo magis Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0070","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0071","text":"Praeterea, sensus corporeus non potest elevari ad intelligendam substantiam incorpoream, quia est supra eius naturam. Si igitur videre Deum per essentiam sit supra naturam cuiuslibet intellectus creati, videtur quod nullus intellectus creatus ad videndum Dei essentiam pertingere possit, quod est erroneum, ut ex supradictis patet. Videtur ergo quod intellectui creato sit naturale divinam essentiam videre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0072","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Rom. VI, *gratia Dei vita aeterna*. Sed vita aeterna consistit in visione divinae essentiae, secundum illud Ioan. XVII, *haec est vita aeterna, ut cognoscant te solum verum Deum,* et cetera. Ergo videre Dei essentiam convenit intellectui creato per gratiam, et non per naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0074","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est quod aliquis intellectus creatus per sua naturalia essentiam Dei videat. Cognitio enim contingit secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente. Cognitum autem est in cognoscente secundum modum cognoscentis. Unde cuiuslibet cognoscentis cognitio est secundum modum suae naturae. Si igitur modus essendi alicuius rei cognitae excedat modum naturae cognoscentis, oportet quod cognitio illius rei sit supra naturam illius cognoscentis. Est autem multiplex modus essendi rerum. Quaedam enim sunt, quorum natura non habet esse nisi in hac materia individuali, et huiusmodi sunt omnia corporalia. Quaedam vero sunt, quorum naturae sunt per se subsistentes, non in materia aliqua, quae tamen non sunt suum esse, sed sunt esse habentes, et huiusmodi sunt substantiae incorporeae, quas Angelos dicimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0076","text":"Solius autem Dei proprius modus essendi est, ut sit suum esse subsistens. Ea igitur quae non habent esse nisi in materia individuali, cognoscere est nobis connaturale, eo quod anima nostra, per quam cognoscimus, est forma alicuius materiae. Quae tamen habet duas virtutes cognoscitivas. Unam, quae est actus alicuius corporei organi. Et huic connaturale est cognoscere res secundum quod sunt in materia individuali, unde sensus non cognoscit nisi singularia. Alia vero virtus cognoscitiva eius est intellectus, qui non est actus alicuius organi corporalis. Unde per intellectum connaturale est nobis cognoscere naturas, quae quidem non habent esse nisi in materia individuali; non tamen secundum quod sunt in materia individuali, sed secundum quod abstrahuntur ab ea per considerationem intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","text":"Unde secundum intellectum possumus cognoscere huiusmodi res in universali, quod est supra facultatem sensus. Intellectui autem angelico connaturale est cognoscere naturas non in materia existentes. Quod est supra naturalem facultatem intellectus animae humanae, secundum statum praesentis vitae, quo corpori unitur. Relinquitur ergo quod cognoscere ipsum esse subsistens, sit connaturale soli intellectui divino, et quod sit supra facultatem naturalem cuiuslibet intellectus creati, quia nulla creatura est suum esse, sed habet esse participatum. Non igitur potest intellectus creatus Deum per essentiam videre, nisi inquantum Deus per suam gratiam se intellectui creato coniungit, ut intelligibile ab ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0078","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0079","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod iste modus cognoscendi Deum, est Angelo connaturalis, ut scilicet cognoscat eum per similitudinem eius in ipso Angelo refulgentem. Sed cognoscere Deum per aliquam similitudinem creatam, non est cognoscere essentiam Dei, ut supra ostensum est. Unde non sequitur quod Angelus per sua naturalia possit cognoscere essentiam Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0080","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0081","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus Angeli non habet defectum, si defectus accipiatur privative, ut scilicet careat eo quod habere debet. Si vero accipiatur negative, sic quaelibet creatura invenitur deficiens, Deo comparata, dum non habet illam excellentiam quae invenitur in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0082","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0083","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod sensus visus, quia omnino materialis est, nullo modo elevari potest ad aliquid immateriale. Sed intellectus noster vel angelicus, quia secundum naturam a materia aliqualiter elevatus est, potest ultra suam naturam per gratiam ad aliquid altius elevari. Et huius signum est, quia visus nullo modo potest in abstractione cognoscere id quod in concretione cognoscit, nullo enim modo potest percipere naturam, nisi ut hanc. Sed intellectus noster potest in abstractione considerare quod in concretione cognoscit. Etsi enim cognoscat res habentes formam in materia, tamen resolvit compositum in utrumque, et considerat ipsam formam per se. Et similiter intellectus Angeli, licet connaturale sit ei cognoscere esse concretum in aliqua natura, tamen potest ipsum esse secernere per intellectum, dum cognoscit quod aliud est ipse, et aliud est suum esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0084","text":"Et ideo, cum intellectus creatus per suam naturam natus sit apprehendere formam concretam et esse concretum in abstractione, per modum resolutionis cuiusdam, potest per gratiam elevari ut cognoscat substantiam separatam subsistentem, et esse separatum subsistens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0085","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0086","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0087","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus creatus ad videndum essentiam Dei aliquo lumine creato non indigeat. Illud enim quod est per se lucidum in rebus sensibilibus, alio lumine non indiget ut videatur, ergo nec in intelligibilibus. Sed Deus est lux intelligibilis. Ergo non videtur per aliquod lumen creatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0088","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0089","text":"Praeterea, cum Deus videtur per medium, non videtur per suam essentiam. Sed cum videtur per aliquod lumen creatum, videtur per medium. Ergo non videtur per suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0090","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0091","text":"Praeterea, illud quod est creatum, nihil prohibet alicui creaturae esse naturale. Si ergo per aliquod lumen creatum Dei essentia videtur, poterit illud lumen esse naturale alicui creaturae. Et ita illa creatura non indigebit aliquo alio lumine ad videndum Deum, quod est impossibile. Non est ergo necessarium quod omnis creatura ad videndum Dei essentiam lumen superadditum requirat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0092","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0093","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo, *in lumine tuo videbimus lumen*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0094","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod omne quod elevatur ad aliquid quod excedit suam naturam, oportet quod disponatur aliqua dispositione quae sit supra suam naturam, sicut, si aer debeat accipere formam ignis, oportet quod disponatur aliqua dispositione ad talem formam. Cum autem aliquis intellectus creatus videt Deum per essentiam, ipsa essentia Dei fit forma intelligibilis intellectus. Unde oportet quod aliqua dispositio supernaturalis ei superaddatur, ad hoc quod elevetur in tantam sublimitatem. Cum igitur virtus naturalis intellectus creati non sufficiat ad Dei essentiam videndam, ut ostensum est, oportet quod ex divina gratia superaccrescat ei virtus intelligendi. Et hoc augmentum virtutis intellectivae illuminationem intellectus vocamus; sicut et ipsum intelligibile vocatur lumen vel lux. Et istud est lumen de quo dicitur Apoc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0096","text":"XXI, *quod claritas Dei illuminabit eam*, scilicet societatem beatorum Deum videntium. Et secundum hoc lumen efficiuntur deiformes, idest Deo similes; secundum illud I Ioan. III, *cum apparuerit, similes ei erimus, et videbimus eum sicuti est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0097","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0098","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod lumen creatum est necessarium ad videndum Dei essentiam, non quod per hoc lumen Dei essentia intelligibilis fiat, quae secundum se intelligibilis est, sed ad hoc quod intellectus fiat potens ad intelligendum, per modum quo potentia fit potentior ad operandum per habitum, sicut etiam et lumen corporale necessarium est in visu exteriori, inquantum facit medium transparens in actu, ut possit moveri a colore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0099","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod lumen istud non requiritur ad videndum Dei essentiam quasi similitudo in qua Deus videatur, sed quasi perfectio quaedam intellectus, confortans ipsum ad videndum Deum. Et ideo potest dici quod non est medium in quo Deus videatur, sed sub quo videtur. Et hoc non tollit immediatam visionem Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0101","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod dispositio ad formam ignis non potest esse naturalis nisi habenti formam ignis. Unde lumen gloriae non potest esse naturale creaturae, nisi creatura esset naturae divinae, quod est impossibile. Per hoc enim lumen fit creatura rationalis deiformis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0103","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0104","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0105","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod videntium essentiam Dei unus alio perfectius non videat. Dicitur enim I Ioan. III, *videbimus eum sicuti est*. Sed ipse uno modo est. Ergo uno modo videbitur ab omnibus. Non ergo perfectius et minus perfecte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0106","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0107","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium qq., quod unam rem non potest unus alio plus intelligere. Sed omnes videntes Deum per essentiam, intelligunt Dei essentiam, intellectu enim videtur Deus, non sensu, ut supra habitum est. Ergo videntium divinam essentiam unus alio non clarius videt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0108","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0109","text":"Praeterea, quod aliquid altero perfectius videatur, ex duobus contingere potest, vel ex parte obiecti visibilis; vel ex parte potentiae visivae videntis. Ex parte autem obiecti, per hoc quod obiectum perfectius in vidente recipitur, scilicet secundum perfectiorem similitudinem. Quod in proposito locum non habet, Deus enim non per aliquam similitudinem, sed per eius essentiam praesens est intellectui essentiam eius videnti. Relinquitur ergo quod, si unus alio perfectius eum videat, quod hoc sit secundum differentiam potentiae intellectivae. Et ita sequitur quod cuius potentia intellectiva naturaliter est sublimior, clarius eum videat. Quod est inconveniens, cum hominibus promittatur in beatitudine aequalitas Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0110","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0111","text":"Sed contra est quod vita aeterna in visione Dei consistit, secundum illud Ioan. XVII, *haec est vita aeterna,* et cetera. Ergo, si omnes aequaliter Dei essentiam vident, in vita aeterna omnes erunt aequales. Cuius contrarium dicit apostolus, I Cor. XV, *stella differt a stella in claritate*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0112","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod videntium Deum per essentiam unus alio perfectius eum videbit. Quod quidem non erit per aliquam Dei similitudinem perfectiorem in uno quam in alio, cum illa visio non sit futura per aliquam similitudinem, ut ostensum est. Sed hoc erit per hoc, quod intellectus unius habebit maiorem virtutem seu facultatem ad videndum Deum, quam alterius. Facultas autem videndi Deum non competit intellectui creato secundum suam naturam, sed per lumen gloriae, quod intellectum in quadam deiformitate constituit, ut ex superioribus patet. Unde intellectus plus participans de lumine gloriae, perfectius Deum videbit. Plus autem participabit de lumine gloriae, qui plus habet de caritate, quia ubi est maior caritas, ibi est maius desiderium; et desiderium quodammodo facit desiderantem aptum et paratum ad susceptionem desiderati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0114","text":"Unde qui plus habebit de caritate, perfectius Deum videbit, et beatior erit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0115","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0116","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur videbimus eum sicuti est, hoc adverbium sicuti determinat modum visionis ex parte rei visae ut sit sensus, videbimus eum ita esse sicuti est, quia ipsum esse eius videbimus, quod est eius essentia. Non autem determinat modum visionis ex parte videntis, ut sit sensus, quod ita erit perfectus modus videndi, sicut est in Deo perfectus modus essendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0117","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0118","text":"Et per hoc etiam patet solutio ad secundum. Cum enim dicitur quod rem unam unus alio melius non intelligit, hoc habet veritatem si referatur ad modum rei intellectae, quia quicumque intelligit rem esse aliter quam sit, non vere intelligit. Non autem si referatur ad modum intelligendi, quia intelligere unius est perfectius quam intelligere alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0119","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod diversitas videndi non erit ex parte obiecti, quia idem obiectum omnibus praesentabitur, scilicet Dei essentia, nec ex diversa participatione obiecti per differentes similitudines, sed erit per diversam facultatem intellectus, non quidem naturalem, sed gloriosam, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0121","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0122","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0123","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod videntes Deum per essentiam ipsum comprehendant. Dicit enim apostolus, Philipp. III, *sequor autem si quo modo comprehendam*. Non autem frustra sequebatur, dicit enim ipse, I Cor. IX, *sic curro, non quasi in incertum*. Ergo ipse comprehendit, et eadem ratione alii, quos ad hoc invitat, dicens, *sic currite, ut comprehendatis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0124","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0125","text":"Praeterea, ut dicit Augustinus in libro de videndo Deum ad Paulinam, *illud comprehenditur, quod ita totum videtur, ut nihil eius lateat videntem*. Sed si Deus per essentiam videtur, totus videtur, et nihil eius latet videntem; cum Deus sit simplex. Ergo a quocumque videtur per essentiam, comprehenditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0126","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0127","text":"Si dicatur quod videtur totus, sed non totaliter, contra, totaliter vel dicit modum videntis, vel modum rei visae. Sed ille qui videt Deum per essentiam, videt eum totaliter, si significetur modus rei visae, quia videt eum sicuti est, ut dictum est. Similiter videt eum totaliter, si significetur modus videntis, quia tota virtute sua intellectus Dei essentiam videbit. Quilibet ergo videns Deum per essentiam, totaliter eum videbit. Ergo eum comprehendet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0128","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0129","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ierem. XXXII, *fortissime, magne, potens, dominus exercituum nomen tibi; magnus consilio, et incomprehensibilis cogitatu*. Ergo comprehendi non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0130","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod comprehendere Deum impossibile est cuicumque intellectui creato, *attingere vero mente Deum qualitercumque, magna est beatitudo*, ut dicit Augustinus. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod illud comprehenditur, quod perfecte cognoscitur. Perfecte autem cognoscitur, quod tantum cognoscitur, quantum est cognoscibile. Unde si id quod est cognoscibile per scientiam demonstrativam, opinione teneatur ex aliqua ratione probabili concepta, non comprehenditur. Puta, si hoc quod est triangulum habere tres angulos aequales duobus rectis, aliquis sciat per demonstrationem, comprehendit illud, si vero aliquis eius opinionem accipiat probabiliter, per hoc quod a sapientibus vel pluribus ita dicitur, non comprehendet ipsum, quia non pertingit ad illum perfectum modum cognitionis, quo cognoscibilis est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","text":"Nullus autem intellectus creatus pertingere potest ad illum perfectum modum cognitionis divinae essentiae, quo cognoscibilis est. Quod sic patet. Unumquodque enim sic cognoscibile est, secundum quod est ens actu. Deus igitur, cuius esse est infinitum, ut supra ostensum est, infinite cognoscibilis est. Nullus autem intellectus creatus potest Deum infinite cognoscere. Intantum enim intellectus creatus divinam essentiam perfectius vel minus perfecte cognoscit, inquantum maiori vel minori lumine gloriae perfunditur. Cum igitur lumen gloriae creatum, in quocumque intellectu creato receptum, non possit esse infinitum, impossibile est quod aliquis intellectus creatus Deum infinite cognoscat. Unde impossibile est quod Deum comprehendat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0133","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0134","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod comprehensio dicitur dupliciter. Uno modo, stricte et proprie, secundum quod aliquid includitur in comprehendente. Et sic nullo modo Deus comprehenditur, nec intellectu nec aliquo alio, quia, cum sit infinitus, nullo finito includi potest, ut aliquid finitum eum infinite capiat, sicut ipse infinite est. Et sic de comprehensione nunc quaeritur. Alio modo comprehensio largius sumitur, secundum quod comprehensio insecutioni opponitur. Qui enim attingit aliquem, quando iam tenet ipsum, comprehendere eum dicitur. Et sic Deus comprehenditur a beatis, secundum illud Cant. III, *tenui eum, nec dimittam*. Et sic intelliguntur auctoritates apostoli de comprehensione. Et hoc modo comprehensio est una de tribus dotibus animae, quae respondet spei; sicut visio fidei, et fruitio caritati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135","text":"Non enim, apud nos, omne quod videtur, iam tenetur vel habetur, quia videntur interdum distantia, vel quae non sunt in potestate nostra. Neque iterum omnibus quae habemus, fruimur, vel quia non delectamur in eis; vel quia non sunt ultimus finis desiderii nostri, ut desiderium nostrum impleant et quietent. Sed haec tria habent beati in Deo, quia et vident ipsum; et videndo, tenent sibi praesentem, in potestate habentes semper eum videre; et tenentes, fruuntur sicut ultimo fine desiderium implente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0136","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non propter hoc Deus incomprehensibilis dicitur, quasi aliquid eius sit quod non videatur, sed quia non ita perfecte videtur, sicut visibilis est. Sicut cum aliqua demonstrabilis propositio per aliquam probabilem rationem cognoscitur, non est aliquid eius quod non cognoscatur, nec subiectum, nec praedicatum, nec compositio, sed tota non ita perfecte cognoscitur, sicut cognoscibilis est. Unde Augustinus, definiendo comprehensionem, dicit quod *totum comprehenditur videndo, quod ita videtur, ut nihil eius lateat videntem; aut cuius fines circumspici possunt*, tunc enim fines alicuius circumspiciuntur, quando ad finem in modo cognoscendi illam rem pervenitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0138","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod totaliter dicit modum obiecti, non quidem ita quod totus modus obiecti non cadat sub cognitione; sed quia modus obiecti non est modus cognoscentis. Qui igitur videt Deum per essentiam, videt hoc in eo, quod infinite existit, et infinite cognoscibilis est, sed hic infinitus modus non competit ei, ut scilicet ipse infinite cognoscat, sicut aliquis probabiliter scire potest aliquam propositionem esse demonstrabilem, licet ipse eam demonstrative non cognoscat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0140","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0141","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0142","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod videntes Deum per essentiam omnia in Deo videant. Dicit enim Gregorius, in IV Dialog. *quid est quod non videant, qui videntem omnia vident?* Sed Deus est videns omnia. Ergo qui vident Deum, omnia vident.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0143","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0144","text":"Item, quicumque videt speculum, videt ea quae in speculo resplendent. Sed omnia quaecumque fiunt vel fieri possunt, in Deo resplendent sicut in quodam speculo, ipse enim omnia in seipso cognoscit. Ergo quicumque videt Deum, videt omnia quae sunt et quae fieri possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0145","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0146","text":"Praeterea, qui intelligit id quod est maius, potest intelligere minima, ut dicitur III de anima. Sed omnia quae Deus facit vel facere potest, sunt minus quam eius essentia. Ergo quicumque intelligit Deum, potest intelligere omnia quae Deus facit vel facere potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0147","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0148","text":"Praeterea, rationalis creatura omnia naturaliter scire desiderat. Si igitur videndo Deum non omnia sciat, non quietatur eius naturale desiderium, et ita, videndo Deum non erit beata. Quod est inconveniens. Videndo igitur Deum, omnia scit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0149","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0150","text":"Sed contra est quod Angeli vident Deum per essentiam, et tamen non omnia sciunt. Inferiores enim Angeli purgantur a superioribus a nescientia, ut dicit Dionysius, VII cap. Cael. Hier. Ipsi etiam nesciunt futura contingentia et cogitationes cordium, hoc enim solius Dei est. Non ergo quicumque vident Dei essentiam, vident omnia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0151","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod intellectus creatus, videndo divinam essentiam, non videt in ipsa omnia quae facit Deus vel facere potest. Manifestum est enim quod sic aliqua videntur in Deo, secundum quod sunt in ipso. Omnia autem alia sunt in Deo, sicut effectus sunt virtute in sua causa. Sic igitur videntur alia in Deo, sicut effectus in sua causa. Sed manifestum est quod quanto aliqua causa perfectius videtur, tanto plures eius effectus in ipsa videri possunt. Qui enim habet intellectum elevatum, statim uno principio demonstrativo proposito, ex ipso multarum conclusionum cognitionem accipit, quod non convenit ei qui debilioris intellectus est, sed oportet quod ei singula explanentur. Ille igitur intellectus potest in causa cognoscere omnes causae effectus, et omnes rationes effectuum, qui causam totaliter comprehendit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","text":"Nullus autem intellectus creatus totaliter Deum comprehendere potest, ut ostensum est. Nullus igitur intellectus creatus, videndo Deum, potest cognoscere omnia quae Deus facit vel potest facere, hoc enim esset comprehendere eius virtutem. Sed horum quae Deus facit vel facere potest, tanto aliquis intellectus plura cognoscit, quanto perfectius Deum videt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0154","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0155","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Gregorius loquitur quantum ad sufficientiam obiecti, scilicet Dei, quod, quantum in se est, sufficienter continet omnia et demonstrat. Non tamen sequitur quod unusquisque videns Deum omnia cognoscat, quia non perfecte comprehendit ipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0156","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0157","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod videns speculum, non est necessarium quod omnia in speculo videat, nisi speculum visu suo comprehendat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0158","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0159","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet maius sit videre Deum quam omnia alia, tamen maius est videre sic Deum quod omnia in eo cognoscantur, quam videre sic ipsum quod non omnia, sed pauciora vel plura cognoscantur in eo. Iam enim ostensum est quod multitudo cognitorum in Deo, consequitur modum videndi ipsum vel magis perfectum vel minus perfectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0160","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod naturale desiderium rationalis creaturae est ad sciendum omnia illa quae pertinent ad perfectionem intellectus; et haec sunt species et genera rerum, et rationes earum, quae in Deo videbit quilibet videns essentiam divinam. Cognoscere autem alia singularia, et cogitata et facta eorum, non est de perfectione intellectus creati, nec ad hoc eius naturale desiderium tendit, nec iterum cognoscere illa quae nondum sunt, sed fieri a Deo possunt. Si tamen solus Deus videretur, qui est fons et principium totius esse et veritatis, ita repleret naturale desiderium sciendi, quod nihil aliud quaereretur, et beatus esset. Unde dicit Augustinus, V Confess., infelix homo qui scit omnia illa (scilicet creaturas), *te autem nescit, beatus autem qui te scit, etiam si illa nesciat. Qui vero te et illa novit, non propter illa beatior est, sed propter te solum beatus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0162","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0163","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0164","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ea quae videntur in Deo, a videntibus divinam essentiam per aliquas similitudines videantur. Omnis enim cognitio est per assimilationem cognoscentis ad cognitum, sic enim intellectus in actu fit intellectum in actu, et sensus in actu sensibile in actu, inquantum eius similitudine informatur, ut pupilla similitudine coloris. Si igitur intellectus videntis Deum per essentiam intelligat in Deo aliquas creaturas, oportet quod earum similitudinibus informetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0165","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0166","text":"Praeterea, ea quae prius vidimus, memoriter tenemus. Sed Paulus, videns in raptu essentiam Dei, ut dicit Augustinus XII super Gen. ad litteram, postquam desiit essentiam Dei videre, recordatus est multorum quae in illo raptu viderat, unde ipse dicit quod *audivit arcana verba, quae non licet homini loqui*, II Cor. XII. Ergo oportet dicere quod aliquae similitudines eorum quae recordatus est, in eius intellectu remanserint. Et eadem ratione, quando praesentialiter videbat Dei essentiam, eorum quae in ipsa videbat, aliquas similitudines vel species habebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0167","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0168","text":"Sed contra est quod per unam speciem videtur speculum, et ea quae in speculo apparent. Sed omnia sic videntur in Deo sicut in quodam speculo intelligibili. Ergo, si ipse Deus non videtur per aliquam similitudinem, sed per suam essentiam; nec ea quae in ipso videntur, per aliquas similitudines sive species videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0169","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0170","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod videntes Deum per essentiam, ea quae in ipsa essentia Dei vident, non vident per aliquas species, sed per ipsam essentiam divinam intellectui eorum unitam. Sic enim cognoscitur unumquodque, secundum quod similitudo eius est in cognoscente. Sed hoc contingit dupliciter. Cum enim quaecumque uni et eidem sunt similia, sibi invicem sint similia, virtus cognoscitiva dupliciter assimilari potest alicui cognoscibili. Uno modo, secundum se, quando directe eius similitudine informatur, et tunc cognoscitur illud secundum se. Alio modo, secundum quod informatur specie alicuius quod est ei simile, et tunc non dicitur res cognosci in seipsa, sed in suo simili. Alia enim est cognitio qua cognoscitur aliquis homo in seipso, et alia qua cognoscitur in sua imagine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171","text":"Sic ergo, cognoscere res per earum similitudines in cognoscente existentes, est cognoscere eas in seipsis, seu in propriis naturis, sed cognoscere eas prout earum similitudines praeexistunt in Deo, est videre eas in Deo. Et hae duae cognitiones differunt. Unde secundum illam cognitionem qua res cognoscuntur a videntibus Deum per essentiam in ipso Deo, non videntur per aliquas similitudines alias; sed per solam essentiam divinam intellectui praesentem, per quam et Deus videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0172","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0173","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus videntis Deum assimilatur rebus quae videntur in Deo, inquantum unitur essentiae divinae, in qua rerum omnium similitudines praeexistunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0174","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0175","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliquae potentiae cognoscitivae sunt, quae ex speciebus primo conceptis alias formare possunt. Sicut imaginatio ex praeconceptis speciebus montis et auri, format speciem montis aurei, et intellectus ex praeconceptis speciebus generis et differentiae, format rationem speciei. Et similiter ex similitudine imaginis formare possumus in nobis similitudinem eius cuius est imago. Et sic Paulus, vel quicumque alius videns Deum, ex ipsa visione essentiae divinae potest formare in se similitudines rerum quae in essentia divina videntur, quae remanserunt in Paulo etiam postquam desiit Dei essentiam videre. Ista tamen visio, qua videntur res per huiusmodi species sic conceptas, est alia a visione qua videntur res in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0176","text":"## Articulus 10","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0177","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0178","text":"Ad decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod videntes Deum per essentiam non simul videant omnia quae in ipso vident. Quia, secundum philosophum, contingit multa scire, intelligere vero unum. Sed ea quae videntur in Deo, intelliguntur, intellectu enim videtur Deus. Ergo non contingit a videntibus Deum simul multa videri in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0179","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0180","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, VIII super Gen. ad litteram, quod *Deus movet creaturam spiritualem per tempus*, hoc est per intelligentiam et affectionem. Sed creatura spiritualis est Angelus, qui Deum videt. Ergo videntes Deum, successive intelligunt et afficiuntur, tempus enim successionem importat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0181","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0182","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, ultimo de Trin., *non erunt volubiles nostrae cogitationes, ab aliis in alia euntes atque redeuntes; sed omnem scientiam nostram uno simul conspectu videbimus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0183","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ea quae videntur in verbo, non successive, sed simul videntur. Ad cuius evidentiam considerandum est, quod ideo nos simul non possumus multa intelligere, quia multa per diversas species intelligimus; diversis autem speciebus non potest intellectus unus simul actu informari ad intelligendum per eas, sicut nec unum corpus potest simul diversis figuris figurari. Unde contingit quod, quando aliqua multa una specie intelligi possunt, simul intelliguntur, sicut diversae partes alicuius totius, si singulae propriis speciebus intelligantur, successive intelliguntur, et non simul; si autem omnes intelligantur una specie totius, simul intelliguntur. Ostensum est autem quod ea quae videntur in Deo, non videntur singula per suas similitudines, sed omnia per unam essentiam Dei. Unde simul, et non successive videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0185","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0186","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sic unum tantum intelligimus, inquantum una specie intelligimus. Sed multa una specie intellecta simul intelliguntur, sicut in specie hominis intelligimus animal et rationale, et in specie domus parietem et tectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0187","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angeli, quantum ad cognitionem naturalem qua cognoscunt res per species diversas eis inditas, non simul omnia cognoscunt, et sic moventur, secundum intelligentiam, per tempus. Sed secundum quod vident res in Deo, simul eas vident.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0189","text":"## Articulus 11","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0190","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0191","text":"Ad undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquis in hac vita possit Deum per essentiam videre. Dicit enim Iacob, Gen. XXXII, *vidi Deum facie ad faciem*. Sed videre facie ad faciem, est videre per essentiam, ut patet per illud quod dicitur I Cor. XIII, *videmus nunc per speculum et in aenigmate, tunc autem facie ad faciem*. Ergo Deus in hac vita per essentiam videri potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0192","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0193","text":"Praeterea, Num. XII dicit dominus de Moyse, *ore ad os loquor ei, et palam, et non per aenigmata et figuras, videt Deum*. Sed hoc est videre Deum per essentiam. Ergo aliquis in statu huius vitae potest Deum per essentiam videre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0194","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0195","text":"Praeterea, illud in quo alia omnia cognoscimus, et per quod de aliis iudicamus, est nobis secundum se notum. Sed omnia etiam nunc in Deo cognoscimus. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII Conf., *si ambo videmus verum esse quod dicis, et ambo videmus verum esse quod dico, ubi quaeso illud videmus? Nec ego in te, nec tu in me, sed ambo in ipsa quae supra mentes nostras est, incommutabili veritate*. Idem etiam, in libro de vera religione, dicit quod secundum veritatem divinam de omnibus iudicamus. Et XII de Trin. dicit quod *rationis est iudicare de istis corporalibus secundum rationes incorporales et sempiternas, quae nisi supra mentem essent, incommutabiles profecto non essent*. Ergo et in hac vita ipsum Deum videmus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0196","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0197","text":"Praeterea, secundum Augustinum, XII super Gen. ad Litt., visione intellectuali videntur ea quae sunt in anima per suam essentiam. Sed visio intellectualis est de rebus intelligibilibus, non per aliquas similitudines, sed per suas essentias, ut ipse ibidem dicit. Ergo, cum Deus sit per essentiam suam in anima nostra, per essentiam suam videtur a nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0198","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0199","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Exod. XXXIII, *non videbit me homo et vivet*. Glossa, *quandiu hic mortaliter vivitur, videri per quasdam imagines Deus potest; sed per ipsam naturae suae speciem non potest*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0200","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ab homine puro Deus videri per essentiam non potest, nisi ab hac vita mortali separetur. Cuius ratio est quia, sicut supra dictum est, modus cognitionis sequitur modum naturae rei cognoscentis. Anima autem nostra, quandiu in hac vita vivimus, habet esse in materia corporali, unde naturaliter non cognoscit aliqua nisi quae habent formam in materia, vel quae per huiusmodi cognosci possunt. Manifestum est autem quod per naturas rerum materialium divina essentia cognosci non potest. Ostensum est enim supra quod cognitio Dei per quamcumque similitudinem creatam, non est visio essentiae ipsius. Unde impossibile est animae hominis secundum hanc vitam viventis, essentiam Dei videre. Et huius signum est, quod anima nostra, quanto magis a corporalibus abstrahitur, tanto intelligibilium abstractorum fit capacior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0202","text":"Unde in somniis et alienationibus a sensibus corporis, magis divinae revelationes percipiuntur, et praevisiones futurorum. Quod ergo anima elevetur usque ad supremum intelligibilium, quod est essentia divina, esse non potest quandiu hac mortali vita utitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0203","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0204","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Dionysium, IV cap. Cael. Hier., sic in Scripturis dicitur aliquis Deum vidisse, inquantum formatae sunt aliquae figurae, vel sensibiles vel imaginariae, secundum aliquam similitudinem aliquod divinum repraesentantes. Quod ergo dicit Iacob, vidi Deum facie ad faciem, referendum est, non ad ipsam divinam essentiam, sed ad figuram in qua repraesentabatur Deus. Et hoc ipsum ad quandam prophetiae eminentiam pertinet, ut videatur persona Dei loquentis, licet imaginaria visione, ut infra patebit, cum de gradibus prophetiae loquemur. Vel hoc dicit Iacob ad designandam quandam eminentiam intelligibilis contemplationis, supra communem statum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0205","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0206","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Deus miraculose aliquid supernaturaliter in rebus corporeis operatur, ita etiam et supernaturaliter, et praeter communem ordinem, mentes aliquorum in hac carne viventium, sed non sensibus carnis utentium, usque ad visionem suae essentiae elevavit; ut dicit Augustinus, XII super Genes. ad Litt., et in libro de videndo Deum de Moyse, qui fuit magister Iudaeorum, et Paulo, qui fuit magister gentium. Et de hoc plenius tractabitur, cum de raptu agemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0207","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0208","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnia dicimur in Deo videre, et secundum ipsum de omnibus iudicare, inquantum per participationem sui luminis omnia cognoscimus et diiudicamus, nam et ipsum lumen naturale rationis participatio quaedam est divini luminis; sicut etiam omnia sensibilia dicimus videre et iudicare in sole, idest per lumen solis. Unde dicit Augustinus, I Soliloquiorum, *disciplinarum spectamina videri non possunt, nisi aliquo velut suo sole illustrentur, videlicet Deo*. Sicut ergo ad videndum aliquid sensibiliter, non est necesse quod videatur substantia solis, ita ad videndum aliquid intelligibiliter, non est necessarium quod videatur essentia Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0209","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0210","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod visio intellectualis est eorum quae sunt in anima per suam essentiam sicut intelligibilia in intellectu. Sic autem Deus est in anima beatorum, non autem in anima nostra; sed per praesentiam, essentiam, et potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0211","text":"## Articulus 12","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0212","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0213","text":"Ad duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod per naturalem rationem Deum in hac vita cognoscere non possimus. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Consol., quod *ratio non capit simplicem formam*. Deus autem maxime est simplex forma, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo ad eius cognitionem ratio naturalis pervenire non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0214","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0215","text":"Praeterea, ratione naturali sine phantasmate nihil intelligit anima, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed Dei, cum sit incorporeus, phantasma in nobis esse non potest. Ergo cognosci non potest a nobis cognitione naturali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0216","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0217","text":"Praeterea, cognitio quae est per rationem naturalem, communis est bonis et malis, sicut natura eis communis est. Sed cognitio Dei competit tantum bonis, dicit enim Augustinus, I de Trin., quod *mentis humanae acies in tam excellenti luce non figitur, nisi per iustitiam fidei emundetur*. Ergo Deus per rationem naturalem cognosci non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0218","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0219","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Rom. I, *quod notum est Dei, manifestum est in illis*, idest, quod cognoscibile est de Deo per rationem naturalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0220","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod naturalis nostra cognitio a sensu principium sumit, unde tantum se nostra naturalis cognitio extendere potest, inquantum manuduci potest per sensibilia. Ex sensibilibus autem non potest usque ad hoc intellectus noster pertingere, quod divinam essentiam videat, quia creaturae sensibiles sunt effectus Dei virtutem causae non adaequantes. Unde ex sensibilium cognitione non potest tota Dei virtus cognosci, et per consequens nec eius essentia videri. Sed quia sunt eius effectus a causa dependentes, ex eis in hoc perduci possumus, ut cognoscamus de Deo an est; et ut cognoscamus de ipso ea quae necesse est ei convenire secundum quod est prima omnium causa, excedens omnia sua causata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0222","text":"Unde cognoscimus de ipso habitudinem ipsius ad creaturas, quod scilicet omnium est causa; et differentiam creaturarum ab ipso, quod scilicet ipse non est aliquid eorum quae ab eo causantur; et quod haec non removentur ab eo propter eius defectum, sed quia superexcedit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0223","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio ad formam simplicem pertingere non potest, ut sciat de ea quid est, potest tamen de ea cognoscere, ut sciat an est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0225","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0226","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus naturali cognitione cognoscitur per phantasmata effectus sui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0227","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0228","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod cognitio Dei per essentiam, cum sit per gratiam, non competit nisi bonis, sed cognitio eius quae est per rationem naturalem, potest competere bonis et malis. Unde dicit Augustinus, in libro Retractationum, *non approbo quod in oratione dixi, Deus, qui non nisi mundos verum scire voluisti, responderi enim potest, multos etiam non mundos multa scire vera*, scilicet per rationem naturalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0229","text":"## Articulus 13","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0230","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0231","text":"Ad decimumtertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod per gratiam non habeatur altior cognitio Dei, quam ea quae habetur per naturalem rationem. Dicit enim Dionysius, in libro de mystica theologia, quod ille qui melius unitur Deo in hac vita, unitur ei sicut omnino ignoto, quod etiam de Moyse dicit, qui tamen excellentiam quandam obtinuit in gratiae cognitione. Sed coniungi Deo ignorando de eo quid est, hoc contingit etiam per rationem naturalem. Ergo per gratiam non plenius cognoscitur a nobis Deus, quam per rationem naturalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0232","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0233","text":"Praeterea, per rationem naturalem in cognitionem divinorum pervenire non possumus, nisi per phantasmata, sic etiam nec secundum cognitionem gratiae. Dicit enim Dionysius, I cap. de Cael. Hier., quod *impossibile est nobis aliter lucere divinum radium, nisi varietate sacrorum velaminum circumvelatum*. Ergo per gratiam non plenius cognoscimus Deum, quam per rationem naturalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0234","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0235","text":"Praeterea, intellectus noster per gratiam fidei Deo adhaeret. Fides autem non videtur esse cognitio, dicit enim Gregorius, in Homil., quod ea quae non videntur fidem habent, et non agnitionem. Ergo per gratiam non additur nobis aliqua excellentior cognitio de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0236","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0237","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit apostolus, I Cor. II, *nobis revelavit Deus per spiritum suum*, illa scilicet quae nemo principum huius saeculi novit, idest philosophorum, ut exponit Glossa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0238","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod per gratiam perfectior cognitio de Deo habetur a nobis, quam per rationem naturalem. Quod sic patet. Cognitio enim quam per naturalem rationem habemus, duo requirit, scilicet, phantasmata ex sensibilibus accepta, et lumen naturale intelligibile, cuius virtute intelligibiles conceptiones ab eis abstrahimus. Et quantum ad utrumque, iuvatur humana cognitio per revelationem gratiae. Nam et lumen naturale intellectus confortatur per infusionem luminis gratuiti. Et interdum etiam phantasmata in imaginatione hominis formantur divinitus, magis exprimentia res divinas, quam ea quae naturaliter a sensibilibus accipimus; sicut apparet in visionibus prophetalibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0240","text":"Et interdum etiam aliquae res sensibiles formantur divinitus, aut etiam voces, ad aliquid divinum exprimendum; sicut in Baptismo visus est spiritus sanctus in specie columbae, et vox patris audita est, *hic est filius meus dilectus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0241","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet per revelationem gratiae in hac vita non cognoscamus de Deo quid est, et sic ei quasi ignoto coniungamur; tamen plenius ipsum cognoscimus, inquantum plures et excellentiores effectus eius nobis demonstrantur; et inquantum ei aliqua attribuimus ex revelatione divina, ad quae ratio naturalis non pertingit, ut Deum esse trinum et unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0243","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0244","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ex phantasmatibus, vel a sensu acceptis secundum naturalem ordinem, vel divinitus in imaginatione formatis, tanto excellentior cognitio intellectualis habetur, quanto lumen intelligibile in homine fortius fuerit. Et sic per revelationem ex phantasmatibus plenior cognitio accipitur, ex infusione divini luminis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0245","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0246","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod fides cognitio quaedam est, inquantum intellectus determinatur per fidem ad aliquod cognoscibile. Sed haec determinatio ad unum non procedit ex visione credentis, sed a visione eius cui creditur. Et sic, inquantum deest visio, deficit a ratione cognitionis quae est in scientia, nam scientia determinat intellectum ad unum per visionem et intellectum primorum principiorum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"854076f59e3407cf4fd1f12dbd3914c0b01eca55ef51455009c6ea77b51e91c9","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T134622624626Z_c26026b3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 12","source_sha256":"854076f59e3407cf4fd1f12dbd3914c0b01eca55ef51455009c6ea77b51e91c9","sha256":"916f7fc1b19b33cfedfcec98ecb2dfbbc70dec24556b8c81df7740f36b1ec24b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:46:22.624639+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0002","text":"[I q. 12 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","text":"Because in the foregoing we have considered how God is according to Himself, it remains to consider how He is in our cognition, that is, how He is known by creatures. And concerning this thirteen questions are asked. First, whether any created intellect can see the essence of God. Second, whether the essence of God is seen by the intellect through any created species. Third, whether the essence of God can be seen by a bodily eye. Fourth, whether any created intellectual substance is sufficient, from its natural powers, to see the essence of God. Fifth, whether the created intellect needs some created light in order to see the essence of God. Sixth, whether one of those seeing the essence of God sees more perfectly than another. Seventh, whether any created intellect can comprehend the essence of God. Eighth, whether the created intellect seeing the essence of God knows all things in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","text":"Ninth, whether it knows the things which it knows there through any likenesses. Tenth, whether it knows simultaneously all the things which it sees in God. Eleventh, whether in the state of this life any human being can see the essence of God. Twelfth, whether by natural reason we can know God in this life. Thirteenth, whether, above the cognition of natural reason, there is in the present life any cognition of God through grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0006","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0007","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that no created intellect can see God through His essence. For Chrysostom, commenting on John, expounding what is said in John 1, *No one has ever seen God*, says thus: *Not only have the prophets not seen that which God Himself is, but neither have the Angels nor the Archangels; for how will that which is of a creatable nature be able to see that which is uncreatable?* Dionysius also, in chapter 1 of *On the Divine Names*, speaking of God, says: *He is neither sense, nor imagination, nor opinion, nor reason, nor knowledge*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0008","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0009","text":"Further, every infinite thing, insofar as it is such, is unknown. But God is infinite, as was shown above. Therefore He is unknown in Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0010","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0011","text":"Further, the created intellect is cognizant only of beings; for the first thing that falls under the apprehension of the intellect is being. But God is not an existing being, but is above beings, as Dionysius says. Therefore He is not intelligible, but is above every intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0012","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0013","text":"Further, there must be some proportion between the knower and the known, since the known is the perfection of the knower. But there is no proportion between a created intellect and God, because they are infinitely distant. Therefore a created intellect cannot see the essence of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0014","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0015","text":"But to the contrary, it is said in 1 John 3: *We shall see Him as He is*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0016","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","text":"I answer that, since each thing is knowable insofar as it is in act, God, who is pure act without any admixture of potency, is, as far as He is concerned, supremely knowable. But what is supremely knowable in itself is not knowable to some intellect because of the excess of the intelligible over the intellect, just as the sun, which is supremely visible, cannot be seen by a bat because of the excess of light. Taking note of this, therefore, some have maintained that no created intellect can see the essence of God. But this is said inappropriately. For since the ultimate beatitude of man consists in his highest operation, which is the operation of the intellect, if the created intellect can never see the essence of God, it will either never attain beatitude, or its beatitude will consist in something other than God. This is alien to the faith.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","text":"For in Him is the ultimate perfection of the rational creature, because He is for it the principle of being; for each thing is perfect insofar as it attains to its principle. Likewise, it is also contrary to reason. For there is in man a natural desire to know the cause when he beholds an effect; and from this wonder arises in human beings. If, therefore, the intellect of a rational creature could not reach the first cause of things, the desire of nature would remain in vain. Hence it must simply be granted that the blessed see the essence of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0019","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0020","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that both authorities speak of the vision of comprehension. Hence Dionysius immediately before the words cited prefaces, saying: *He is universally incomprehensible to all, and is neither sense*, and so forth. And Chrysostom, a little after the words aforesaid, adds: *Here by vision he means the most certain contemplation and comprehension of the Father, as great as that which the Father has of the Son*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0021","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0022","text":"To the second it must be said that the infinite which is on the side of matter not perfected by form is unknown in itself, because all cognition is through form. But the infinite which is on the side of form not limited by matter is in itself supremely known. Now God is infinite in this way, and not in the first way, as is clear from the foregoing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0023","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0024","text":"To the third it must be said that God is not said to be non-existing as though He were in no way existing, but because He is above every existing being, insofar as He is His own being. Hence it does not follow from this that He cannot in any way be known, but that He exceeds every cognition, which is precisely not to be comprehended.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0025","text":"[I q. 12 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0026","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that proportion is spoken of in two ways. In one way, as a determinate relation of one quantity to another; according to which double, triple, and equal are species of proportion. In another way, any relation of one thing to another is called proportion. And in this way there can be a proportion of a creature to God, insofar as it is related to him as an effect to a cause, and as potency to act. And according to this, a created intellect can be proportioned to knowing God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0027","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0028","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0029","text":"We proceed thus to the second article. It seems that the essence of God is seen by the created intellect through some likeness. For it is said in 1 John 3, *we know that, when he shall appear, we shall be like him, and we shall see him as he is*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0030","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0031","text":"Further, Augustine says, in IX *On the Trinity*, *when we know God, some likeness of God comes to be in us*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0032","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0033","text":"Further, the intellect in act is the intelligible in act, just as sense in act is the sensible in act. But this is not so except insofar as sense is informed by the likeness of the sensible thing, and intellect by the likeness of the thing understood. Therefore, if God is seen in act by a created intellect, he must be seen through some likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0034","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0035","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in XV *On the Trinity*, that when the Apostle says, *we now see through a mirror and in an enigma*, by the name of mirror and enigma, whatever likenesses signified by him can be understood which are suited to understanding God. But to see God through essence is not an enigmatic or specular vision, but is divided off against it. Therefore the divine essence is not seen through likenesses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0036","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","text":"I answer that it must be said that for vision, both sensible and intellectual, two things are required: namely, the power of seeing, and the union of the thing seen with the sight. For vision does not come to be in act except through the fact that the thing seen is in some way in the seer. And in bodily things, indeed, it is apparent that the thing seen cannot be in the seer through its essence, but only through its likeness; just as the likeness of a stone is in the eye, through which vision comes to be in act, but not the very substance of the stone. But if there were one and the same thing which was the principle of the power of seeing and which was the thing seen, the seer would have to have from that thing both the power of seeing and the form through which it would see. Now it is manifest that God is both the author of the intellectual power and can be seen by the intellect. And since the very intellectual power of a creature is not God's essence, it remains that it is some participated likeness of him who is the first intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","text":"Hence the intellectual power of a creature is also called a certain intelligible light, as though derived from the first light, whether this is understood of a natural power or of some superadded perfection of grace or glory. Therefore, for seeing God, some likeness of God is required on the part of the power of seeing, namely, that by which the intellect is made efficacious for seeing God. But on the part of the thing seen, which must in some way be united to the seer, God's essence can be seen through no created likeness. First indeed, because, as Dionysius says in chapter 1 of *On the Divine Names*, through likenesses of things of a lower order higher things can in no way be known; just as through the species of a body the essence of an incorporeal thing cannot be known. Much less, therefore, can the essence of God be seen through any created species. Second, because the essence of God is his very being, as was shown above, which cannot belong to any created form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","text":"Therefore no created form can be a likeness representing God's essence to the seer. Third, because the divine essence is something uncircumscribed, containing within itself supereminently whatever can be signified or understood by a created intellect. And this can in no way be represented through any created species, because every created form is determinate according to some account of wisdom, or power, or being itself, or something of this sort. Hence, to say that God is seen through a likeness is to say that the divine essence is not seen, which is erroneous. Therefore it must be said that for seeing God's essence some likeness is required on the part of the power of seeing, namely, the light of glory, strengthening the intellect to see God, of which it is said in the Psalm, *in your light we shall see light*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0040","text":"But God's essence cannot be seen through any created likeness that represents the divine essence itself as it is in itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0041","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0042","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that authority speaks of the likeness which is through participation in the light of glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0043","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0044","text":"To the second, it must be said that Augustine there speaks of the knowledge of God which is had on the way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0045","text":"[I q. 12 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","text":"To the third, it must be said that the divine essence is being itself. Hence, just as other intelligible forms, which are not their own being, are united to the intellect according to some being by which they inform the intellect itself and make it to be in act, so the divine essence is united to the created intellect as an intelligible in act, through itself making the intellect to be in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0047","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0048","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0049","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that the essence of God can be seen by the bodily eye. For it is said in Job 19, *in my flesh I shall see God,* etc.; and in 42, *by the hearing of the ear I have heard thee, but now my eye sees thee*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0050","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0051","text":"Further, Augustine says, in the last book of *The City of God*, ch. 29, *therefore the power of the eyes of those* (namely, the glorified) *will be more excellent, not so that they may see more keenly than certain serpents or eagles are said to see (for however great the sharpness of sight in which these animals excel, they can see nothing other than bodies), but so that they may see incorporeal things*. But whoever can see incorporeal things can be elevated to see God. Therefore the glorified eye can see God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0052","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0053","text":"Further, God can be seen by a vision of the imagination, for it is said in Isaiah 6, *I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne,* and so forth. But an imaginary vision takes its origin from sense, *for phantasy is a movement produced by sense in act*, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. Therefore God can be seen by sensible vision.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0054","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0055","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *On Seeing God*, addressed to Paulina: *No one has ever seen God, either in this life, as He is Himself; or in the life of the angels, as these visible things that are discerned by bodily vision*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0056","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible for God to be seen by the sense of sight, or by any other sense or power of the sensitive part. For every power of this sort is the act of a bodily organ, as will be said below. But an act is proportioned to that of which it is the act. Hence no power of this sort can extend beyond bodily things. But God is incorporeal, as was shown above. Hence He can be seen neither by sense nor by imagination, but by intellect alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0058","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0059","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that when it is said, *in my flesh I shall see God, my savior*, it is not understood that he will see God with the eye of the flesh, but that, existing in the flesh after the resurrection, he will see God. Likewise, what is said, *now my eye sees thee*, is understood of the eye of the mind, as the Apostle says in Ephesians 1, *may He give you the spirit of wisdom in the knowledge of Him, the eyes of your heart having been enlightened*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0060","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0061","text":"To the second, it must be said that Augustine speaks in those words by way of inquiry, and conditionally. This is clear from what is prefaced: therefore the powers of the other sort will be far different (namely, those of the glorified eye), if through them that incorporeal nature will be seen. But afterward he determines this, saying: *it is very credible that we shall then so see the worldly bodies of the new heaven and the new earth that, with most clear perspicuity, we shall see God as present everywhere and governing all bodily things also; not as now the invisible things of God are beheld, understood through the things that have been made; but as, when we look upon human beings among whom we live as they live and exercise vital motions, we do not believe that they live, but see it*. From this it is clear that he understands the glorified eyes to see God in the way that our eyes now see someone's life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0062","text":"But life is not seen by the bodily eye as visible per se, but as sensible per accidens; this is not indeed known by sense, but is known immediately together with sense by some other cognitive power. But that, as soon as bodies are seen, the divine presence may be known from them by the intellect, arises from two things: namely, from the perspicacity of the intellect and from the radiance of the divine brightness in renewed bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0063","text":"[I q. 12 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0064","text":"To the third, it must be said that in an imaginary vision the essence of God is not seen, but some form is formed in the imagination, representing God according to some mode of likeness, insofar as in the divine Scriptures divine things are described metaphorically through sensible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0065","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0066","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0067","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that some created intellect can see the divine essence through its natural powers. For Dionysius says, ch. 4 of *The Divine Names*, that an angel is a pure and most luminous mirror, *receiving the whole, if it is permissible to say so, beauty of God*. But each thing is seen when its mirror is seen. Therefore, since an angel understands himself through his natural powers, it seems that he also understands the divine essence through his natural powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0068","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0069","text":"Further, that which is most visible becomes less visible to us on account of a defect of our vision, whether bodily or intellectual. But the intellect of an angel suffers no defect. Therefore, since God in Himself is most intelligible, it seems that He is most intelligible to an angel. Therefore, if the angel can understand other intelligible things through its natural powers, much more so God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0070","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0071","text":"Further, bodily sense cannot be elevated to understand an incorporeal substance, because this is above its nature. Therefore, if to see God through His essence is above the nature of every created intellect, it seems that no created intellect can attain to seeing God's essence, which is erroneous, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore it seems natural to a created intellect to see the divine essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0072","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0073","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Rom. VI, *the grace of God is eternal life*. But eternal life consists in the vision of the divine essence, according to that of John XVII, *this is eternal life, that they may know thee, the only true God,* and so forth. Therefore, to see God's essence belongs to the created intellect through grace, and not through nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0074","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible for any created intellect to see the essence of God by its natural powers. For cognition occurs insofar as the thing known is in the knower. But the thing known is in the knower according to the mode of the knower. Hence the cognition of every knower is according to the mode of its nature. If, therefore, the mode of being of some known thing exceeds the mode of the nature of the knower, the cognition of that thing must be above the nature of that knower. Now there is a manifold mode of being of things. For some things are such that their nature has being only in this individual matter, and all corporeal things are of this kind. But some are such that their natures subsist through themselves, not in any matter, which nevertheless are not their own being, but have being; and of this kind are the incorporeal substances, which we call Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0076","text":"But the proper mode of being of God alone is that He is His own subsisting being. Therefore, to know those things which have being only in individual matter is connatural to us, because our soul, by which we know, is the form of some matter. Yet it has two cognitive powers. One is the act of a corporeal organ. And it is connatural to this to know things according as they are in individual matter; whence sense knows only singulars. But its other cognitive power is the intellect, which is not the act of any corporeal organ. Hence through the intellect it is connatural to us to know natures which indeed have being only in individual matter, yet not according as they are in individual matter, but according as they are abstracted from it by the consideration of the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","text":"Hence by the intellect we can know such things universally, which is above the capacity of sense. But it is connatural to the angelic intellect to know natures not existing in matter. This is above the natural capacity of the intellect of the human soul, according to the state of the present life, in which it is united to the body. It remains, therefore, that to know subsisting being itself is connatural to the divine intellect alone, and that it is above the natural capacity of every created intellect, because no creature is its own being, but has participated being. Therefore a created intellect cannot see God through His essence, except insofar as God by His grace unites Himself to the created intellect as intelligible by it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0078","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0079","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that this mode of knowing God is connatural to an Angel, namely, that it knows Him through His likeness shining forth in the Angel itself. But to know God through some created likeness is not to know the essence of God, as was shown above. Hence it does not follow that an Angel can know the essence of God by its natural powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0080","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0081","text":"To the second it must be said that the intellect of an Angel does not have defect, if defect is taken privatively, namely, as lacking that which it ought to have. But if it is taken negatively, then every creature is found to be deficient in comparison with God, since it does not have that excellence which is found in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0082","text":"[I q. 12 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0083","text":"To the third it must be said that the sense of sight, because it is altogether material, can in no way be elevated to anything immaterial. But our intellect, or the angelic intellect, because by nature it is in some measure elevated above matter, can be elevated beyond its nature by grace to something higher. And a sign of this is that sight can in no way know in abstraction what it knows in concretion; for it can in no way perceive a nature except as this one. But our intellect can consider in abstraction what it knows in concretion. For although it knows things having form in matter, nevertheless it resolves the composite into both, and considers the form itself through itself. And similarly, although it is connatural to the intellect of an Angel to know being concrete in some nature, nevertheless it can separate being itself by intellect, while it knows that it itself is one thing and its being another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0084","text":"And therefore, since the created intellect is by its nature apt to apprehend concrete form and concrete being in abstraction, by way of a certain resolution, it can by grace be elevated so as to know a separate subsisting substance and separate subsisting being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0085","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0086","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0087","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that the created intellect does not require any created light in order to see the essence of God. For that which is through itself luminous among sensible things does not require another light in order to be seen; therefore neither does it among intelligible things. But God is intelligible light. Therefore He is not seen through any created light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0088","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0089","text":"Moreover, when God is seen through a medium, He is not seen through His essence. But when He is seen through some created light, He is seen through a medium. Therefore He is not seen through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0090","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0091","text":"Moreover, nothing prevents that which is created from being natural to some creature. Therefore, if God's essence is seen through some created light, that light can be natural to some creature. And thus that creature will not require any other light in order to see God, which is impossible. Therefore it is not necessary that every creature require a superadded light in order to see the essence of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0092","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0093","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in the Psalm, *in thy light we shall see light*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0094","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","text":"I answer that it must be said that everything which is elevated to something exceeding its nature must be disposed by some disposition that is above its nature; just as, if air is to receive the form of fire, it must be disposed by some disposition for such a form. But when some created intellect sees God through His essence, the very essence of God becomes the intelligible form of the intellect. Hence some supernatural disposition must be superadded to it, so that it may be elevated to such sublimity. Therefore, since the natural power of the created intellect is not sufficient for seeing the essence of God, as was shown, a power of understanding must accrue to it from divine grace. And we call this increase of intellectual power the illumination of the intellect; just as the intelligible itself is called light or brightness. And this is the light of which it is said in Apoc.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0096","text":"XXI, *that the glory of God will illuminate it*, namely, the society of the blessed who see God. And according to this light they are made deiform, that is, like God; according to that of I John III, *when He shall appear, we shall be like Him, and we shall see Him as He is*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0097","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0098","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that a created light is necessary for seeing the essence of God, not in order that through this light God's essence should become intelligible, for it is intelligible in itself, but in order that the intellect may become capable of understanding, in the manner in which a power becomes more powerful for acting through a habit; just as corporeal light too is necessary in external sight, insofar as it makes the transparent medium actual, so that it can be moved by color.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0099","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","text":"To the second it must be said that this light is not required for seeing the essence of God as a likeness in which God is seen, but as a certain perfection of the intellect, strengthening it to see God. And therefore it can be said that it is not a medium in which God is seen, but under which he is seen. And this does not remove the immediate vision of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0101","text":"[I q. 12 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102","text":"To the third it must be said that a disposition toward the form of fire cannot be natural except to one possessing the form of fire. Hence the light of glory cannot be natural to a creature, unless the creature were of the divine nature, which is impossible. For through this light the rational creature is made godlike, as was said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0103","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0104","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0105","text":"We proceed to the sixth as follows. It seems that, among those seeing the essence of God, one does not see more perfectly than another. For it is said in 1 John 3, *we shall see him as he is*. But he himself is in one way. Therefore, he will be seen in one way by all. Therefore, not more perfectly and less perfectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0106","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0107","text":"Further, Augustine says, in the book of Eighty-Three Questions, that one person cannot understand one thing more than another. But all who see God through his essence understand God's essence; for God is seen by intellect, not by sense, as was held above. Therefore, among those seeing the divine essence, one does not see more clearly than another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0108","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0109","text":"Further, that something be seen more perfectly by one than by another can happen in two ways: either on the part of the visible object, or on the part of the seeing power of the one seeing. But on the part of the object, this would be by the object being received more perfectly in the seer, namely according to a more perfect likeness. This has no place in the matter at hand, for God is present to the intellect seeing his essence not through any likeness, but through his essence. It remains, therefore, that if one sees him more perfectly than another, this is according to a difference of intellectual power. And thus it follows that he whose intellectual power is naturally more sublime sees him more clearly. This is unfitting, since equality with the angels is promised to human beings in beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0110","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0111","text":"But on the contrary, eternal life consists in the vision of God, according to that of John 17, *this is eternal life*, and so forth. Therefore, if all see the essence of God equally, all will be equal in eternal life. The contrary of this is said by the Apostle, in 1 Corinthians 15: *star differs from star in glory*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0112","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","text":"I answer that it must be said that, among those seeing God through his essence, one will see him more perfectly than another. This indeed will not be through any more perfect likeness of God in one than in another, since that vision will not occur through any likeness, as has been shown. But it will be because the intellect of one will have greater power or capacity for seeing God than that of another. Now the capacity for seeing God does not belong to the created intellect according to its nature, but through the light of glory, which establishes the intellect in a certain godlikeness, as is clear from what has been said above. Hence the intellect participating more in the light of glory will see God more perfectly. But he will participate more in the light of glory who has more charity, because where there is greater charity, there is greater desire; and desire in a certain way makes the one desiring apt and ready for the reception of what is desired.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0114","text":"Hence he who has more charity will see God more perfectly and will be more blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0115","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0116","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, when it is said, *we shall see him as he is*, this adverb *as* determines the mode of vision on the part of the thing seen, so that the sense is: we shall see him to be as he is, because we shall see his very being, which is his essence. But it does not determine the mode of vision on the part of the seer, so that the sense would be that the mode of seeing will be as perfect as the mode of being is perfect in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0117","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0118","text":"And through this the solution to the second is also clear. For when it is said that one person does not understand one thing better than another, this is true if it is referred to the mode of the thing understood, because whoever understands a thing to be otherwise than it is does not understand truly. But it is not true if it is referred to the mode of understanding, because one person's understanding is more perfect than another's.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0119","text":"[I q. 12 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120","text":"To the third it must be said that the diversity of seeing will not be on the part of the object, because the same object will be presented to all, namely the essence of God; nor will it be from a diverse participation in the object through different likenesses, but it will be through a diverse capacity of intellect, not indeed natural, but glorious, as was said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0121","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0122","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0123","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh article. It seems that those seeing God through His essence comprehend Him. For the Apostle says, Phil. III, *but I pursue, if by any means I may comprehend*. But he did not pursue in vain, for he himself says, I Cor. IX, *thus I run, not as though uncertainly*. Therefore he comprehends, and for the same reason so do others, whom he exhorts to this, saying, *so run that you may comprehend*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0124","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0125","text":"Further, as Augustine says in the book *On Seeing God*, to Paulina, *that is comprehended which is seen so wholly that nothing of it escapes the one seeing*. But if God is seen through His essence, He is seen wholly, and nothing of Him escapes the one seeing; since God is simple. Therefore, by whomever He is seen through His essence, He is comprehended.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0126","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0127","text":"If it be said that He is seen wholly, but not totally, on the contrary, “totally” either signifies the mode of the one seeing or the mode of the thing seen. But one who sees God through His essence sees Him totally, if the mode of the thing seen is signified, because he sees Him as He is, as has been said. Likewise, he sees Him totally, if the mode of the one seeing is signified, because the intellect will see God's essence with all its power. Therefore, whoever sees God through His essence will see Him totally. Therefore he will comprehend Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0128","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0129","text":"On the contrary, it is said, Jer. XXXII, *most mighty, great, powerful, Lord of hosts is Your name; great in counsel, and incomprehensible in thought*. Therefore He cannot be comprehended.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0130","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131","text":"I answer that it must be said that for any created intellect to comprehend God is impossible; yet, as Augustine says, *to attain God in the mind in any manner whatever is great blessedness*. For the clarification of this, it should be known that that is comprehended which is perfectly known. But that is perfectly known which is known as much as it is knowable. Hence, if that which is knowable through demonstrative science is held by opinion conceived from some probable reason, it is not comprehended. For example, if someone knows through demonstration that a triangle has three angles equal to two right angles, he comprehends this; but if someone accepts an opinion of it as probable because the wise, or the many, say so, he will not comprehend it, because he does not attain to that perfect mode of cognition by which it is knowable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","text":"But no created intellect can attain to that perfect mode of cognition of the divine essence by which it is knowable. This is evident as follows. For each thing is knowable insofar as it is being in act. God, therefore, whose being is infinite, as was shown above, is infinitely knowable. But no created intellect can know God infinitely. For a created intellect knows the divine essence more perfectly or less perfectly insofar as it is suffused with a greater or lesser light of glory. Therefore, since the created light of glory, received in whatever created intellect, cannot be infinite, it is impossible for any created intellect to know God infinitely. Hence, it is impossible that it should comprehend God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0133","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0134","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that comprehension is spoken of in two ways. In one way, strictly and properly, according as something is included in that which comprehends. And in this way God is in no manner comprehended, neither by intellect nor by anything else, because, since He is infinite, He cannot be enclosed by anything finite, so that something finite might contain Him infinitely, as He Himself is infinite. And it is concerning comprehension in this sense that the present question is asked. In another way, comprehension is taken more broadly, according as comprehension is opposed to pursuit. For one who attains someone, when he already holds him, is said to comprehend him. And in this way God is comprehended by the blessed, according to Cant. III, *I held him, and I will not let him go*. And the Apostle's authorities concerning comprehension are understood in this way. And in this mode comprehension is one of the three endowments of the soul, corresponding to hope; just as vision corresponds to faith, and enjoyment to charity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135","text":"For among us not everything that is seen is already held or possessed, because things at a distance, or things not in our power, are sometimes seen. Nor again do we enjoy everything that we possess, either because we do not delight in it, or because it is not the ultimate end of our desire, so as to fulfill and bring our desire to rest. But the blessed have these three things in God, because they see Him; and, in seeing, hold Him present to themselves, having it in their power always to see Him; and, holding Him, enjoy Him as the ultimate end fulfilling their desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0136","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","text":"To the second it must be said that God is called incomprehensible not because there is something of Him that is not seen, but because He is not seen as perfectly as He is visible. Just as when some demonstrable proposition is known through some probable reason, there is nothing of it that is not known—neither the subject, nor the predicate, nor the composition—but the whole is not known as perfectly as it is knowable. Hence Augustine, in defining comprehension, says that *the whole is comprehended by seeing which is seen in such a way that nothing of it escapes the one seeing; or whose limits can be surveyed*; for then the limits of something are surveyed when one arrives at the end in the mode of knowing that thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0138","text":"[I q. 12 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","text":"To the third it must be said that “totally” indicates the mode of the object, not indeed such that the whole mode of the object does not fall under cognition, but because the mode of the object is not the mode of the knower. Therefore, one who sees God through His essence sees in Him that He exists infinitely and is infinitely knowable; but this infinite mode does not belong to him, namely, that he himself should know infinitely, just as someone can know probably that a proposition is demonstrable, although he does not himself know it demonstratively.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0140","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0141","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0142","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth article. It seems that those seeing God through His essence see all things in God. For Gregory says, in *Dialogues* IV: *what is it that those do not see who see Him who sees all things?* But God sees all things. Therefore, those who see God see all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0143","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0144","text":"Likewise, whoever sees a mirror sees those things that shine forth in the mirror. But all things whatsoever that occur or can occur shine forth in God as in a certain mirror, for He Himself knows all things in Himself. Therefore, whoever sees God sees all things that are and that can come to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0145","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0146","text":"Moreover, whoever understands that which is greater can understand lesser things, as is said in *On the Soul* III. But all things that God makes or can make are less than His essence. Therefore, whoever understands God can understand all things that God makes or can make.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0147","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0148","text":"Moreover, a rational creature naturally desires to know all things. Therefore, if, in seeing God, it does not know all things, its natural desire is not at rest; and thus, in seeing God, it will not be blessed. This is unfitting. Therefore, in seeing God, it knows all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0149","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0150","text":"But on the contrary, the angels see God through His essence, and yet they do not know all things. For the lower angels are purified of ignorance by the higher, as Dionysius says, *Celestial Hierarchy*, chap. VII. They likewise do not know future contingents and the thoughts of hearts, for this belongs to God alone. Therefore, not all who see God's essence see all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0151","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","text":"I answer that it must be said that a created intellect, in seeing the divine essence, does not see in it all that God does or can do. For it is manifest that things are seen in God insofar as they are in Him. But all other things are in God as effects are in their cause by virtue. Thus, then, other things are seen in God as effects are in their cause. But it is manifest that the more perfectly a cause is seen, the more of its effects can be seen in it. For one who has an elevated intellect, once a single demonstrative principle has been proposed, immediately derives from it knowledge of many conclusions; this does not befit one whose intellect is weaker, but it is necessary that individual matters be explained to him. Therefore, that intellect can know in the cause all the effects of the cause, and all the intelligible accounts of the effects, which comprehends the cause totally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","text":"But no created intellect can totally comprehend God, as has been shown. Therefore, no created intellect, in seeing God, can know all that God does or can do; for this would be to comprehend His power. But among those things which God does or can do, an intellect knows more insofar as it sees God more perfectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0154","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0155","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Gregory speaks as to the sufficiency of the object, namely God, which, as far as concerns itself, sufficiently contains and manifests all things. Yet it does not follow that everyone seeing God knows all things, because he does not perfectly comprehend Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0156","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0157","text":"To the second it must be said that one who sees a mirror need not see everything in the mirror, unless he comprehends the mirror by his sight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0158","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0159","text":"To the third it must be said that, although seeing God is greater than seeing all other things, nevertheless it is greater to see God in such a way that all things are known in Him than to see Him in such a way that not all things, but fewer or more, are known in Him. For it has already been shown that the multitude of things known in God follows upon the mode of seeing Him, whether more perfectly or less perfectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0160","text":"[I q. 12 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the natural desire of a rational creature is to know all those things that pertain to the perfection of the intellect; and these are the species and genera of things, and their intelligible accounts, which everyone who sees the divine essence will see in God. But to know other singular things, and their thoughts and deeds, does not belong to the perfection of the created intellect; nor does its natural desire tend toward this, nor again toward knowing those things which do not yet exist but can be made by God. Yet if God alone were seen, who is the fount and principle of all being and truth, He would so fill the natural desire for knowing that nothing else would be sought, and one would be blessed. Hence Augustine says, *Confessions* V: “Unhappy is the man who knows all those things (namely, creatures), *but does not know You; whereas blessed is he who knows You, even if he does not know those things. But he who knows both You and those things is not more blessed on account of those things, but is blessed on account of You alone*.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0162","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0163","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0164","text":"The ninth point is approached thus. It seems that those things which are seen in God are seen by those seeing the divine essence through certain likenesses. For every cognition is through an assimilation of the knower to the known; for in this way the intellect in act becomes the thing understood in act, and the sense in act the sensible thing in act, insofar as it is informed by its likeness, as the pupil is by the likeness of color. Therefore, if the intellect of one seeing God through His essence understands certain creatures in God, it must be informed by their likenesses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0165","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0166","text":"Moreover, we retain in memory those things which we have previously seen. But Paul, seeing in rapture the essence of God, as Augustine says in *On Genesis according to the Letter* XII, after he ceased to see the essence of God, remembered many things which he had seen in that rapture; hence he himself says that *he heard secret words, which it is not permitted for a man to speak*, II Cor. XII. Therefore, it must be said that certain likenesses of those things which he remembered remained in his intellect. And for the same reason, when he was presently seeing the essence of God, he had certain likenesses or species of those things which he saw in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0167","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0168","text":"But on the contrary, by one species a mirror and the things appearing in the mirror are seen. But all things are thus seen in God as in a certain intelligible mirror. Therefore, if God Himself is not seen through any likeness, but through His essence, then neither are those things which are seen in Him seen through any likenesses or species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0169","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0170","text":"I answer that those who see God through His essence do not see the things that they see in the very essence of God through any species, but through the divine essence itself united to their intellect. For each thing is known according as its likeness is in the knower. But this occurs in two ways. For, since whatever things are similar to one and the same thing are similar to one another, a cognitive power can be assimilated to a knowable thing in two ways. In one way, in itself, when it is directly informed by its likeness; and then that thing is known in itself. In another way, insofar as it is informed by the species of something similar to it; and then the thing is said to be known not in itself, but in its similar. For the cognition by which a man is known in himself is one thing, and that by which he is known in his image is another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171","text":"Thus, therefore, to know things through their likenesses existing in the knower is to know them in themselves, or in their proper natures; but to know them insofar as their likenesses pre-exist in God is to see them in God. And these two cognitions differ. Hence, according to that cognition by which things are known by those seeing God through His essence in God Himself, they are not seen through any other likenesses, but through the divine essence alone present to the intellect, through which God too is seen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0172","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0173","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the intellect of one seeing God is assimilated to the things that are seen in God, insofar as it is united to the divine essence, in which the likenesses of all things pre-exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0174","text":"[I q. 12 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0175","text":"To the second it must be said that there are certain cognitive powers which can form other species from species first conceived. Thus imagination, from pre-conceived species of a mountain and of gold, forms the species of a golden mountain; and the intellect, from pre-conceived species of genus and difference, forms the notion of a species. Likewise, from the likeness of an image we can form within ourselves the likeness of that of which it is the image. And thus Paul, or any other person seeing God, from the very vision of the divine essence can form within himself likenesses of the things that are seen in the divine essence, which remained in Paul even after he ceased to see the essence of God. Yet this vision, by which things are seen through species thus conceived, is other than the vision by which things are seen in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0176","text":"## Article 10","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0177","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0178","text":"We proceed thus to the tenth article. It seems that those who see God through His essence do not see simultaneously all the things that they see in Him. For, according to the Philosopher, it is possible to know many things, but to understand one thing. But the things that are seen in God are understood, for God is seen by the intellect. Therefore, it is not possible for those seeing God to see many things in God simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0179","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0180","text":"Further, Augustine says, in Book VIII of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that *God moves the spiritual creature through time*, that is, through understanding and affection. But the spiritual creature is the angel, who sees God. Therefore, those seeing God understand and are affected successively, for time implies succession.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0181","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0182","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in the last book of *On the Trinity*, *our thoughts will not be shifting, going and returning from some things to others; but we shall see all our knowledge in one simultaneous view*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0183","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","text":"I answer that the things which are seen in the Word are seen not successively but simultaneously. To make this evident, it must be considered that the reason why we cannot understand many things simultaneously is that we understand many things through diverse species; but one intellect cannot be simultaneously informed in act by diverse species so as to understand through them, just as one body cannot at the same time be shaped by diverse figures. Hence it happens that, when many things can be understood by one species, they are understood simultaneously; as the diverse parts of some whole, if the individual parts are understood through their proper species, are understood successively and not simultaneously; but if they are all understood through the one species of the whole, they are understood simultaneously. Now it has been shown that the things seen in God are not each seen through their own likenesses, but all through the one essence of God. Hence they are seen simultaneously, and not successively.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0185","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0186","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that we understand only one thing in this way, insofar as we understand through one species. But many things understood through one species are understood simultaneously, as in the species of man we understand animal and rational, and in the species of house, wall and roof.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0187","text":"[I q. 12 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","text":"To the second it must be said that angels, with respect to natural cognition by which they know things through diverse species infused into them, do not know all things simultaneously, and thus they are moved through time according to understanding. But insofar as they see things in God, they see them simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0189","text":"## Article 11","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0190","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0191","text":"We proceed thus to the eleventh article. It seems that someone can see God through His essence in this life. For Jacob says, in Genesis XXXII, *I saw God face to face*. But to see face to face is to see through essence, as is clear from what is said in I Corinthians XIII, *we now see through a mirror and in an enigma, but then face to face*. Therefore, God can be seen through His essence in this life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0192","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0193","text":"Moreover, in Num. XII the Lord says of Moses, *I speak to him mouth to mouth, and openly, and not through riddles and figures; he sees God*. But this is to see God through His essence. Therefore, someone in the state of this life can see God through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0194","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0195","text":"Moreover, that in which we know all other things, and through which we judge concerning other things, is known to us in itself. But even now we know all things in God. For Augustine says, in XII Conf., *if we both see that what you say is true, and we both see that what I say is true, where, I ask, do we see that? Neither I in you nor you in me, but both of us in that immutable truth which is above our minds*. He also says, in the book *On True Religion*, that we judge concerning all things according to divine truth. And in XII *On the Trinity* he says that *it belongs to reason to judge concerning these corporeal things according to incorporeal and everlasting reasons, which, unless they were above the mind, would certainly not be immutable*. Therefore, even in this life we see God Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0196","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0197","text":"Moreover, according to Augustine, XII *On Genesis, according to the Letter*, by intellectual vision those things which are in the soul are seen through their essence. But intellectual vision is of intelligible things, not through any likenesses, but through their essences, as he himself says in the same place. Therefore, since God is in our soul through His essence, He is seen by us through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0198","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0199","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Exod. XXXIII, *man shall not see me and live*. The Gloss: *so long as one lives here mortally, God can be seen through certain images; but He cannot be seen through the very form of His nature*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0200","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","text":"I answer that it must be said that God cannot be seen through His essence by a mere man unless he is separated from this mortal life. The reason for this is that, as was said above, the mode of knowing follows the mode of the nature of the thing knowing. Now our soul, so long as we live in this life, has being in corporeal matter; hence it naturally knows only those things which have form in matter, or those which can be known through such things. But it is manifest that the divine essence cannot be known through the natures of material things. For it was shown above that knowledge of God through any created likeness is not the vision of His essence. Hence it is impossible for the soul of a man living according to this life to see the essence of God. And a sign of this is that our soul, the more it is abstracted from corporeal things, the more capable it becomes of abstract intelligible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0202","text":"Hence, in dreams and withdrawals from the senses of the body, divine revelations and foreknowledge of future things are perceived more fully. Therefore, that the soul should be raised up to the supreme of intelligible things, which is the divine essence, cannot occur so long as it makes use of this mortal life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0203","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0204","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, according to Dionysius, chapter IV of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, someone is said in the Scriptures to have seen God insofar as certain figures, whether sensible or imaginary, were formed which represented something divine according to some likeness. Therefore, what Jacob says, *I saw God face to face*, must be referred not to the divine essence itself, but to a figure in which God was represented. And this itself belongs to a certain eminence of prophecy: that the person of God speaking should be seen, even if by an imaginary vision, as will be clear below when we speak of the degrees of prophecy. Or Jacob says this to designate a certain eminence of intelligible contemplation, above the common state.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0205","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0206","text":"To the second it must be said that, just as God miraculously works something supernaturally in corporeal things, so too, supernaturally and beyond the common order, He raised the minds of certain persons living in this flesh, yet not using the senses of the flesh, up to the vision of His essence; as Augustine says, XII *On Genesis, according to the Letter*, and in the book *On Seeing God*, concerning Moses, who was the teacher of the Jews, and Paul, who was the teacher of the Gentiles. And this will be treated more fully when we discuss rapture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0207","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0208","text":"To the third it must be said that we are said to see all things in God, and to judge concerning all things according to Him, insofar as through participation in His light we know and judge all things; for the natural light of reason itself is a certain participation in the divine light. Thus we also say that we see and judge all sensible things in the sun, that is, through the light of the sun. Hence Augustine says, in I *Soliloquies*, *the spectacles of the disciplines cannot be seen unless they are illuminated by something like their own sun, namely God*. Therefore, just as in order to see something sensibly it is not necessary that the substance of the sun be seen, so in order to see something intelligibly it is not necessary that the essence of God be seen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0209","text":"[I q. 12 a. 11 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0210","text":"To the fourth it must be said that intellectual vision is of those things which are in the soul through their essence, as intelligible things are in the intellect. But God is in this way in the soul of the blessed, not, however, in our soul; rather, He is there by presence, essence, and power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0211","text":"## Article 12","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0212","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0213","text":"We proceed thus to the twelfth article. It seems that we cannot know God in this life through natural reason. For Boethius says, in the book *On Consolation*, that *reason does not grasp a simple form*. But God is most of all simple form, as was shown above. Therefore natural reason cannot attain to the knowledge of Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0213"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0214","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0214"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0215","text":"Moreover, by natural reason the soul understands nothing without a phantasm, as is said in III *On the Soul*. But, since God is incorporeal, there can be no phantasm of God in us. Therefore He cannot be known by us through natural knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0215"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0216","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0216"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0217","text":"Moreover, knowledge that is through natural reason is common to the good and the evil, just as nature is common to them. But knowledge of God belongs only to the good; for Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, I, that *the keenness of the human mind is not fixed upon so excellent a light unless it is cleansed by the justice of faith*. Therefore God cannot be known through natural reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0217"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0218","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0218"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0219","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Rom. I, *what is known of God is manifest in them*, that is, what is knowable of God through natural reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0219"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0220","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0220"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","text":"I answer that it must be said that our natural knowledge takes its beginning from sense; hence our natural knowledge can extend only so far as it can be led by sensible things. But from sensible things our intellect cannot attain to seeing the divine essence, because sensible creatures are effects of God that do not equal the power of the cause. Hence, from knowledge of sensible things, the whole power of God cannot be known, and consequently neither can his essence be seen. But because they are his effects dependent upon their cause, from them we can be led so far as to know concerning God whether he is; and to know concerning him those things that must belong to him insofar as he is the first cause of all things, exceeding all his caused things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0222","text":"Hence we know concerning him his relation to creatures, namely, that he is the cause of all; and the difference of creatures from him, namely, that he is not anything of those things that are caused by him; and that these are not removed from him on account of any defect in him, but because he surpasses them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0222"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0223","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0223"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that reason cannot attain to a simple form so as to know of it what it is; nevertheless, it can know of it so as to know whether it is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0225","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0225"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0226","text":"To the second it must be said that God is known by natural knowledge through phantasms of his effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0226"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0227","text":"[I q. 12 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0227"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0228","text":"To the third it must be said that knowledge of God through his essence, since it is through grace, belongs only to the good; but knowledge of him that is through natural reason can belong to both the good and the evil. Hence Augustine says, in the book of *Retractions*, *I do not approve what I said in prayer, O God, who willed that none but the pure should know truth; for it can be answered that many who are not pure also know many true things*, namely, through natural reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0228"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0229","text":"## Article 13","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0229"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0230","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0230"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0231","text":"We proceed thus to the thirteenth article. It seems that through grace no higher knowledge of God is possessed than that which is possessed through natural reason. For Dionysius says, in the book *On Mystical Theology*, that he who is more perfectly united to God in this life is united to him as wholly unknown; which he also says of Moses, who nevertheless attained a certain excellence in the knowledge of grace. But being united to God while not knowing what he is also occurs through natural reason. Therefore through grace God is not known by us more fully than through natural reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0231"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0232","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0232"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0233","text":"Moreover, through natural reason we cannot arrive at knowledge of divine things except through phantasms; and neither can we do so according to the knowledge of grace. For Dionysius says, chapter I of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, that *it is impossible for the divine ray to shine upon us otherwise than veiled by a variety of sacred coverings*. Therefore through grace we do not know God more fully than through natural reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0233"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0234","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0234"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0235","text":"Moreover, our intellect adheres to God through the grace of faith. But faith does not seem to be knowledge; for Gregory says, in the *Homilies*, that those things which are not seen have faith and not recognition. Therefore through grace no more excellent knowledge of God is added to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0235"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0236","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0236"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0237","text":"On the contrary, the Apostle says, I Cor. II, *God has revealed to us through his Spirit*, namely, those things which none of the rulers of this age knew, that is, the philosophers, as the Gloss expounds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0237"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0238","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0238"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","text":"I answer that it must be said that through grace we possess a more perfect knowledge of God than through natural reason. This is clear as follows. For the knowledge that we have through natural reason requires two things: namely, phantasms received from sensible things, and the natural intelligible light by whose power we abstract intelligible conceptions from them. And with respect to both, human knowledge is aided through the revelation of grace. For the natural light of the intellect is strengthened by the infusion of gratuitous light. And sometimes also phantasms are formed divinely in a human being's imagination, expressing divine things more distinctly than those which we naturally receive from sensible things; as is apparent in prophetic visions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0240","text":"And sometimes also certain sensible things, or even voices, are formed divinely in order to express something divine; as in Baptism the Holy Spirit was seen in the form of a dove, and the voice of the Father was heard: *this is my beloved Son*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0240"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0241","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0241"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","text":"Therefore, to the first objection it must be said that, although through the revelation of grace in this life we do not know of God what He is, and thus are united to Him as though to one unknown; nevertheless, we know Him more fully, insofar as more numerous and more excellent effects of His are shown to us, and insofar as we attribute to Him certain things by divine revelation to which natural reason does not attain, as that God is three and one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0243","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0243"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0244","text":"To the second, it must be said that from phantasms, whether received from sense according to the natural order or divinely formed in the imagination, intellectual knowledge is had in proportion as the intelligible light in a human being is stronger. And thus, through revelation, fuller knowledge is received from phantasms by the infusion of divine light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0244"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0245","text":"[I q. 12 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0245"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0246","text":"To the third, it must be said that faith is a certain kind of knowledge, insofar as the intellect is determined by faith to something knowable. But this determination to one thing does not proceed from the vision of the believer, but from the vision of the one to whom belief is given. And thus, insofar as vision is lacking, it falls short of the character of knowledge that is present in science; for science determines the intellect to one thing through vision and the understanding of first principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0246"]}],"source_digest":"55a3f38ad42ca3a91597dc5d33f63458fc1c94fa687f2af1a62e2cebeedeb73a","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 12"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5","title":"First Part · Question 13","order":14,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 13 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28788)\n\nConsideratis his quae ad divinam cognitionem pertinent, procedendum est ad considerationem divinorum nominum, unumquodque enim nominatur a nobis, secundum quod ipsum cognoscimus. Circa hoc ergo quaeruntur duodecim. Primo, utrum Deus sit nominabilis a nobis. Secundo, utrum aliqua nomina dicta de Deo, praedicentur de ipso substantialiter. Tertio, utrum aliqua nomina dicta de Deo, proprie dicantur de ipso; an omnia attribuantur ei metaphorice. Quarto, utrum multa nomina dicta de Deo, sint synonyma. Quinto, utrum nomina aliqua dicantur de Deo et creaturis univoce, vel aequivoce. Sexto, supposito quod dicantur analogice, utrum dicantur de Deo per prius, vel de creaturis. Septimo, utrum aliqua nomina dicantur de Deo ex tempore. Octavo, utrum hoc nomen Deus sit nomen naturae, vel operationis. Nono, utrum hoc nomen Deus sit nomen communicabile. Decimo, utrum accipiatur univoce vel aequivoce, secundum quod significat Deum per naturam, et per participationem, et secundum opinionem. Undecimo, utrum hoc nomen qui est sit maxime proprium nomen Dei. Duodecimo, utrum propositiones affirmativae possint formari de Deo.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28789)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullum nomen Deo conveniat. Dicit enim Dionysius, I cap. de Div. Nom., quod neque nomen eius est, neque opinio. Et Prov. XXX dicitur, *quod nomen eius, et quod nomen filii eius, si nosti?*\n\n[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28790)\n\nPraeterea, omne nomen aut dicitur in abstracto, aut in concreto. Sed nomina significantia in concreto, non competunt Deo, cum simplex sit, neque nomina significantia in abstracto, quia non significant aliquid perfectum subsistens. Ergo nullum nomen potest dici de Deo.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28791)\n\nPraeterea, nomina significant substantiam cum qualitate; verba autem et participia significant cum tempore; pronomina autem cum demonstratione vel relatione. Quorum nihil competit Deo, quia sine qualitate est et sine omni accidente, et sine tempore; et sentiri non potest, ut demonstrari possit; nec relative significari, cum relativa sint aliquorum antedictorum recordativa, vel nominum, vel participiorum, vel pronominum demonstrativorum. Ergo Deus nullo modo potest nominari a nobis.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28792)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Exod. XV, *dominus quasi vir pugnator, omnipotens nomen eius*.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28793)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, voces sunt signa intellectuum, et intellectus sunt rerum similitudines. Et sic patet quod voces referuntur ad res significandas, mediante conceptione intellectus. Secundum igitur quod aliquid a nobis intellectu cognosci potest, sic a nobis potest nominari. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus in hac vita non potest a nobis videri per suam essentiam; sed cognoscitur a nobis ex creaturis, secundum habitudinem principii, et per modum excellentiae et remotionis. Sic igitur potest nominari a nobis ex creaturis, non tamen ita quod nomen significans ipsum, exprimat divinam essentiam secundum quod est, sicut hoc nomen homo exprimit sua significatione essentiam hominis secundum quod est, significat enim eius definitionem, declarantem eius essentiam; ratio enim quam significat nomen, est definitio.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28794)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ea ratione dicitur Deus non habere nomen, vel esse supra nominationem, quia essentia eius est supra id quod de Deo intelligimus et voce significamus.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28795)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quia ex creaturis in Dei cognitionem venimus, et ex ipsis eum nominamus, nomina quae Deo attribuimus, hoc modo significant, secundum quod competit creaturis materialibus, quarum cognitio est nobis connaturalis, ut supra dictum est. Et quia in huiusmodi creaturis, ea quae sunt perfecta et subsistentia sunt composita; forma autem in eis non est aliquid completum subsistens, sed magis quo aliquid est, inde est quod omnia nomina a nobis imposita ad significandum aliquid completum subsistens, significant in concretione, prout competit compositis; quae autem imponuntur ad significandas formas simplices, significant aliquid non ut subsistens, sed ut quo aliquid est, sicut albedo significat ut quo aliquid est album. Quia igitur et Deus simplex est, et subsistens est, attribuimus ei et nomina abstracta, ad significandam simplicitatem eius; et nomina concreta, ad significandum subsistentiam et perfectionem ipsius, quamvis utraque nomina deficiant a modo ipsius, sicut intellectus noster non cognoscit eum ut est, secundum hanc vitam.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28796)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod significare substantiam cum qualitate, est significare suppositum cum natura vel forma determinata in qua subsistit. Unde, sicut de Deo dicuntur aliqua in concretione, ad significandum subsistentiam et perfectionem ipsius, sicut iam dictum est, ita dicuntur de Deo nomina significantia substantiam cum qualitate. Verba vero et participia consignificantia tempus dicuntur de ipso, ex eo quod aeternitas includit omne tempus, sicut enim simplicia subsistentia non possumus apprehendere et significare nisi per modum compositorum, ita simplicem aeternitatem non possumus intelligere vel voce exprimere, nisi per modum temporalium rerum; et hoc propter connaturalitatem intellectus nostri ad res compositas et temporales. Pronomina vero demonstrativa dicuntur de Deo, secundum quod faciunt demonstrationem ad id quod intelligitur, non ad id quod sentitur, secundum enim quod a nobis intelligitur, secundum hoc sub demonstratione cadit. Et sic, secundum illum modum quo nomina et participia et pronomina demonstrativa de Deo dicuntur, secundum hoc et pronominibus relativis significari potest.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28797)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullum nomen dicatur de Deo substantialiter. Dicit enim Damascenus, *oportet singulum eorum quae de Deo dicuntur, non quid est secundum substantiam significare, sed quid non est ostendere, aut habitudinem quandam, aut aliquid eorum quae assequuntur naturam vel operationem*.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28798)\n\nPraeterea, dicit Dionysius, I cap. de Div. Nom., *omnem sanctorum theologorum hymnum invenies, ad bonos thearchiae processus, manifestative et laudative Dei nominationes dividentem*, et est sensus, quod nomina quae in divinam laudem sancti doctores assumunt, secundum processus ipsius Dei distinguuntur. Sed quod significat processum alicuius rei, nihil significat ad eius essentiam pertinens. Ergo nomina dicta de Deo, non dicuntur de ipso substantialiter.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28799)\n\nPraeterea, secundum hoc nominatur aliquid a nobis, secundum quod intelligitur. Sed non intelligitur Deus a nobis in hac vita secundum suam substantiam. Ergo nec aliquod nomen impositum a nobis, dicitur de Deo secundum suam substantiam.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28800)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, VI de Trin., *Deo hoc est esse, quod fortem esse vel sapientem esse, et si quid de illa simplicitate dixeris, quo eius substantia significatur*. Ergo omnia nomina huiusmodi significant divinam substantiam.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28801)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod de nominibus quae de Deo dicuntur negative, vel quae relationem ipsius ad creaturam significant, manifestum est quod substantiam eius nullo modo significant; sed remotionem alicuius ab ipso, vel relationem eius ad alium, vel potius alicuius ad ipsum. Sed de nominibus quae absolute et affirmative de Deo dicuntur, sicut bonus, sapiens, et huiusmodi, multipliciter aliqui sunt opinati. Quidam enim dixerunt quod haec omnia nomina, licet affirmative de Deo dicantur, tamen magis inventa sunt ad aliquid removendum a Deo, quam ad aliquid ponendum in ipso. Unde dicunt quod, cum dicimus Deum esse viventem, significamus quod Deus non hoc modo est, sicut res inanimatae, et similiter accipiendum est in aliis. Et hoc posuit Rabbi Moyses. Alii vero dicunt quod haec nomina imposita sunt ad significandum habitudinem eius ad creata, ut, cum dicimus Deus est bonus, sit sensus, Deus est causa bonitatis in rebus. Et eadem ratio est in aliis. Sed utrumque istorum videtur esse inconveniens, propter tria. Primo quidem, quia secundum neutram harum positionum posset assignari ratio quare quaedam nomina magis de Deo dicerentur quam alia. Sic enim est causa corporum, sicut est causa bonorum, unde, si nihil aliud significatur, cum dicitur Deus est bonus, nisi Deus est causa bonorum, poterit similiter dici quod Deus est corpus, quia est causa corporum. Item, per hoc quod dicitur quod est corpus, removetur quod non sit ens in potentia tantum, sicut materia prima. Secundo, quia sequeretur quod omnia nomina dicta de Deo, per posterius dicerentur de ipso, sicut sanum per posterius dicitur de medicina, eo quod significat hoc tantum quod sit causa sanitatis in animali, quod per prius dicitur sanum. Tertio, quia hoc est contra intentionem loquentium de Deo. Aliud enim intendunt dicere, cum dicunt Deum viventem, quam quod sit causa vitae nostrae, vel quod differat a corporibus inanimatis. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod huiusmodi quidem nomina significant substantiam divinam, et praedicantur de Deo substantialiter, sed deficiunt a repraesentatione ipsius. Quod sic patet. Significant enim sic nomina Deum, secundum quod intellectus noster cognoscit ipsum. Intellectus autem noster, cum cognoscat Deum ex creaturis, sic cognoscit ipsum, secundum quod creaturae ipsum repraesentant. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus in se praehabet omnes perfectiones creaturarum, quasi simpliciter et universaliter perfectus. Unde quaelibet creatura intantum eum repraesentat, et est ei similis, inquantum perfectionem aliquam habet, non tamen ita quod repraesentet eum sicut aliquid eiusdem speciei vel generis, sed sicut excellens principium, a cuius forma effectus deficiunt, cuius tamen aliqualem similitudinem effectus consequuntur; sicut formae corporum inferiorum repraesentant virtutem solarem. Et hoc supra expositum est, cum de perfectione divina agebatur. Sic igitur praedicta nomina divinam substantiam significant, imperfecte tamen, sicut et creaturae imperfecte eam repraesentant. Cum igitur dicitur Deus est bonus, non est sensus, Deus est causa bonitatis, vel Deus non est malus, sed est sensus, id quod bonitatem dicimus in creaturis, praeexistit in Deo, et hoc quidem secundum modum altiorem. Unde ex hoc non sequitur quod Deo competat esse bonum inquantum causat bonitatem, sed potius e converso, quia est bonus, bonitatem rebus diffundit, secundum illud Augustini, de Doct. Christ., *inquantum bonus est, sumus*.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28802)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Damascenus ideo dicit quod haec nomina non significant quid est Deus, quia a nullo istorum nominum exprimitur quid est Deus perfecte, sed unumquodque imperfecte eum significat, sicut et creaturae imperfecte eum repraesentant.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28803)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in significatione nominum, aliud est quandoque a quo imponitur nomen ad significandum, et id ad quod significandum nomen imponitur, sicut hoc nomen lapis imponitur ab eo quod laedit pedem, non tamen imponitur ad hoc significandum quod significet laedens pedem, sed ad significandam quandam speciem corporum; alioquin omne laedens pedem esset lapis. Sic igitur dicendum est quod huiusmodi divina nomina imponuntur quidem a processibus deitatis, sicut enim secundum diversos processus perfectionum, creaturae Deum repraesentant, licet imperfecte; ita intellectus noster, secundum unumquemque processum, Deum cognoscit et nominat. Sed tamen haec nomina non imponit ad significandum ipsos processus, ut, cum dicitur Deus est vivens, sit sensus, ab eo procedit vita, sed ad significandum ipsum rerum principium, prout in eo praeexistit vita, licet eminentiori modo quam intelligatur vel significetur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28804)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod essentiam Dei in hac vita cognoscere non possumus secundum quod in se est, sed cognoscimus eam secundum quod repraesentatur in perfectionibus creaturarum. Et sic nomina a nobis imposita eam significant.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28805)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullum nomen dicatur de Deo proprie. Omnia enim nomina quae de Deo dicimus, sunt a creaturis accepta, ut dictum est. Sed nomina creaturarum metaphorice dicuntur de Deo, sicut cum dicitur Deus est lapis, vel leo, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Ergo omnia nomina dicta de Deo, dicuntur metaphorice.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28806)\n\nPraeterea, nullum nomen proprie dicitur de aliquo, a quo verius removetur quam de eo praedicetur. Sed omnia huiusmodi nomina, bonus sapiens, et similia, verius removentur a Deo quam de eo praedicentur, ut patet per Dionysium, II cap. Cael. Hier. Ergo nullum istorum nominum proprie dicitur de Deo.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28807)\n\nPraeterea, nomina corporum non dicuntur de Deo nisi metaphorice, cum sit incorporeus. Sed omnia huiusmodi nomina implicant quasdam corporales conditiones, significant enim cum tempore, et cum compositione, et cum aliis huiusmodi, quae sunt conditiones corporum. Ergo omnia huiusmodi nomina dicuntur de Deo metaphorice.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28808)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Ambrosius, in Lib. II de fide, *sunt quaedam nomina, quae evidenter proprietatem divinitatis ostendunt; et quaedam quae perspicuam divinae maiestatis exprimunt veritatem; alia vero sunt, quae translative per similitudinem de Deo dicuntur*. Non igitur omnia nomina dicuntur de Deo metaphorice, sed aliqua dicuntur proprie.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28809)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, Deum cognoscimus ex perfectionibus procedentibus in creaturas ab ipso; quae quidem perfectiones in Deo sunt secundum eminentiorem modum quam in creaturis. Intellectus autem noster eo modo apprehendit eas, secundum quod sunt in creaturis, et secundum quod apprehendit, ita significat per nomina. In nominibus igitur quae Deo attribuimus, est duo considerare, scilicet, perfectiones ipsas significatas, ut bonitatem, vitam, et huiusmodi; et modum significandi. Quantum igitur ad id quod significant huiusmodi nomina, proprie competunt Deo, et magis proprie quam ipsis creaturis, et per prius dicuntur de eo. Quantum vero ad modum significandi, non proprie dicuntur de Deo, habent enim modum significandi qui creaturis competit.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28810)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod quaedam nomina significant huiusmodi perfectiones a Deo procedentes in res creatas, hoc modo quod ipse modus imperfectus quo a creatura participatur divina perfectio, in ipso nominis significato includitur, sicut lapis significat aliquid materialiter ens, et huiusmodi nomina non possunt attribui Deo nisi metaphorice. Quaedam vero nomina significant ipsas perfectiones absolute, absque hoc quod aliquis modus participandi claudatur in eorum significatione, ut ens, bonum vivens, et huiusmodi, et talia proprie dicuntur de Deo.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28811)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ideo huiusmodi nomina dicit Dionysius negari a Deo, quia id quod significatur per nomen, non convenit eo modo ei, quo nomen significat, sed excellentiori modo. Unde ibidem dicit Dionysius quod Deus est super omnem substantiam et vitam.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28812)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ista nomina quae proprie dicuntur de Deo important conditiones corporales, non in ipso significato nominis, sed quantum ad modum significandi. Ea vero quae metaphorice de Deo dicuntur, important conditionem corporalem in ipso suo significato.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28813)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ista nomina dicta de Deo, sint nomina synonyma. Synonyma enim nomina dicuntur, quae omnino idem significant. Sed ista nomina dicta de Deo, omnino idem significant in Deo, quia bonitas Dei est eius essentia, et similiter sapientia. Ergo ista nomina sunt omnino synonyma.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28814)\n\nSi dicatur quod ista nomina significant idem secundum rem, sed secundum rationes diversas, contra, ratio cui non respondet aliquid in re, est vana; si ergo istae rationes sunt multae, et res est una, videtur quod rationes istae sint vanae.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28815)\n\nPraeterea, magis est unum quod est unum re et ratione, quam quod est unum re et multiplex ratione. Sed Deus est maxime unus. Ergo videtur quod non sit unus re et multiplex ratione. Et sic nomina dicta de Deo non significant rationes diversas, et ita sunt synonyma.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28816)\n\nSed contra, omnia synonyma, sibi invicem adiuncta, nugationem adducunt, sicut si dicatur vestis indumentum. Si igitur omnia nomina dicta de Deo sunt synonyma, non posset convenienter dici Deus bonus, vel aliquid huiusmodi; cum tamen scriptum sit Ierem. XXXII, *fortissime, magne, potens, dominus exercituum nomen tibi*.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28817)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod huiusmodi nomina dicta de Deo, non sunt synonyma. Quod quidem facile esset videre, si diceremus quod huiusmodi nomina sunt inducta ad removendum, vel ad designandum habitudinem causae respectu creaturarum, sic enim essent diversae rationes horum nominum secundum diversa negata, vel secundum diversos effectus connotatos. Sed secundum quod dictum est huiusmodi nomina substantiam divinam significare, licet imperfecte, etiam plane apparet, secundum praemissa, quod habent rationes diversas. Ratio enim quam significat nomen, est conceptio intellectus de re significata per nomen. Intellectus autem noster, cum cognoscat Deum ex creaturis, format ad intelligendum Deum conceptiones proportionatas perfectionibus procedentibus a Deo in creaturas. Quae quidem perfectiones in Deo praeexistunt unite et simpliciter, in creaturis vero recipiuntur divise et multipliciter. Sicut igitur diversis perfectionibus creaturarum respondet unum simplex principium, repraesentatum per diversas perfectiones creaturarum varie et multipliciter; ita variis et multiplicibus conceptibus intellectus nostri respondet unum omnino simplex, secundum huiusmodi conceptiones imperfecte intellectum. Et ideo nomina Deo attributa, licet significent unam rem, tamen, quia significant eam sub rationibus multis et diversis, non sunt synonyma.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28818)\n\nEt sic patet solutio ad primum, quia nomina synonyma dicuntur, quae significant unum secundum unam rationem. Quae enim significant rationes diversas unius rei, non primo et per se unum significant, quia nomen non significat rem, nisi mediante conceptione intellectus, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28819)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod rationes plures horum nominum non sunt cassae et vanae, quia omnibus eis respondet unum quid simplex, per omnia huiusmodi multipliciter et imperfecte repraesentatum.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28820)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod hoc ipsum ad perfectam Dei unitatem pertinet, quod ea quae sunt multipliciter et divisim in aliis, in ipso sunt simpliciter et unite. Et ex hoc contingit quod est unus re et plures secundum rationem, quia intellectus noster ita multipliciter apprehendit eum, sicut res multipliciter ipsum repraesentant.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28821)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ea quae dicuntur de Deo et creaturis, univoce de ipsis dicantur. Omne enim aequivocum reducitur ad univocum, sicut multa ad unum. Nam si hoc nomen canis aequivoce dicitur de latrabili et marino, oportet quod de aliquibus univoce dicatur, scilicet de omnibus latrabilibus, aliter enim esset procedere in infinitum. Inveniuntur autem quaedam agentia univoca, quae conveniunt cum suis effectibus in nomine et definitione, ut homo generat hominem; quaedam vero agentia aequivoca, sicut sol causat calidum, cum tamen ipse non sit calidus nisi aequivoce. Videtur igitur quod primum agens, ad quod omnia agentia reducuntur, sit agens univocum. Et ita, quae de Deo et creaturis dicuntur, univoce praedicantur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28822)\n\nPraeterea, secundum aequivoca non attenditur aliqua similitudo. Cum igitur creaturae ad Deum sit aliqua similitudo, secundum illud Genes. I, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*, videtur quod aliquid univoce de Deo et creaturis dicatur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28823)\n\nPraeterea, mensura est homogenea mensurato, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sed Deus est prima mensura omnium entium, ut ibidem dicitur. Ergo Deus est homogeneus creaturis. Et ita aliquid univoce de Deo et creaturis dici potest.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28824)\n\nSed contra, quidquid praedicatur de aliquibus secundum idem nomen et non secundum eandem rationem, praedicatur de eis aequivoce. Sed nullum nomen convenit Deo secundum illam rationem, secundum quam dicitur de creatura, nam sapientia in creaturis est qualitas, non autem in Deo; genus autem variatum mutat rationem, cum sit pars definitionis. Et eadem ratio est in aliis. Quidquid ergo de Deo et creaturis dicitur, aequivoce dicitur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28825)\n\nPraeterea, Deus plus distat a creaturis, quam quaecumque creaturae ab invicem. Sed propter distantiam quarundam creaturarum, contingit quod nihil univoce de eis praedicari potest; sicut de his quae non conveniunt in aliquo genere. Ergo multo minus de Deo et creaturis aliquid univoce praedicatur, sed omnia praedicantur aequivoce.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28826)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est aliquid praedicari de Deo et creaturis univoce. Quia omnis effectus non adaequans virtutem causae agentis, recipit similitudinem agentis non secundum eandem rationem, sed deficienter, ita ut quod divisim et multipliciter est in effectibus, in causa est simpliciter et eodem modo; sicut sol secundum unam virtutem, multiformes et varias formas in istis inferioribus producit. Eodem modo, ut supra dictum est, omnes rerum perfectiones, quae sunt in rebus creatis divisim et multipliciter, in Deo praeexistunt unite. Sic igitur, cum aliquod nomen ad perfectionem pertinens de creatura dicitur, significat illam perfectionem ut distinctam secundum rationem definitionis ab aliis, puta cum hoc nomen sapiens de homine dicitur, significamus aliquam perfectionem distinctam ab essentia hominis, et a potentia et ab esse ipsius, et ab omnibus huiusmodi. Sed cum hoc nomen de Deo dicimus, non intendimus significare aliquid distinctum ab essentia vel potentia vel esse ipsius. Et sic, cum hoc nomen sapiens de homine dicitur, quodammodo circumscribit et comprehendit rem significatam, non autem cum dicitur de Deo, sed relinquit rem significatam ut incomprehensam, et excedentem nominis significationem. Unde patet quod non secundum eandem rationem hoc nomen sapiens de Deo et de homine dicitur. Et eadem ratio est de aliis. Unde nullum nomen univoce de Deo et creaturis praedicatur. Sed nec etiam pure aequivoce, ut aliqui dixerunt. Quia secundum hoc, ex creaturis nihil posset cognosci de Deo, nec demonstrari; sed semper incideret fallacia aequivocationis. Et hoc est tam contra philosophos, qui multa demonstrative de Deo probant, quam etiam contra apostolum dicentem, Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*. Dicendum est igitur quod huiusmodi nomina dicuntur de Deo et creaturis secundum analogiam, idest proportionem. Quod quidem dupliciter contingit in nominibus, vel quia multa habent proportionem ad unum, sicut sanum dicitur de medicina et urina, inquantum utrumque habet ordinem et proportionem ad sanitatem animalis, cuius hoc quidem signum est, illud vero causa; vel ex eo quod unum habet proportionem ad alterum, sicut sanum dicitur de medicina et animali, inquantum medicina est causa sanitatis quae est in animali. Et hoc modo aliqua dicuntur de Deo et creaturis analogice, et non aequivoce pure, neque univoce. Non enim possumus nominare Deum nisi ex creaturis, ut supra dictum est. Et sic, quidquid dicitur de Deo et creaturis, dicitur secundum quod est aliquis ordo creaturae ad Deum, ut ad principium et causam, in qua praeexistunt excellenter omnes rerum perfectiones. Et iste modus communitatis medius est inter puram aequivocationem et simplicem univocationem. Neque enim in his quae analogice dicuntur, est una ratio, sicut est in univocis; nec totaliter diversa, sicut in aequivocis; sed nomen quod sic multipliciter dicitur, significat diversas proportiones ad aliquid unum; sicut sanum, de urina dictum, significat signum sanitatis animalis, de medicina vero dictum, significat causam eiusdem sanitatis.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28827)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet in praedicationibus oporteat aequivoca ad univoca reduci, tamen in actionibus agens non univocum ex necessitate praecedit agens univocum. Agens enim non univocum est causa universalis totius speciei, ut sol est causa generationis omnium hominum. Agens vero univocum non est causa agens universalis totius speciei (alioquin esset causa sui ipsius, cum sub specie contineatur), sed est causa particularis respectu huius individui, quod in participatione speciei constituit. Causa igitur universalis totius speciei non est agens univocum. Causa autem universalis est prior particulari. Hoc autem agens universale, licet non sit univocum, non tamen est omnino aequivocum, quia sic non faceret sibi simile; sed potest dici agens analogicum, sicut in praedicationibus omnia univoca reducuntur ad unum primum, non univocum, sed analogicum, quod est ens.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28828)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod similitudo creaturae ad Deum est imperfecta, quia etiam nec idem secundum genus repraesentat, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28829)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Deus non est mensura proportionata mensuratis. Unde non oportet quod Deus et creaturae sub uno genere contineantur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28830)\n\nEa vero quae sunt in contrarium, concludunt quod non univoce huiusmodi nomina de Deo et creaturis praedicentur, non autem quod aequivoce.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28831)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina per prius dicantur de creaturis quam de Deo. Secundum enim quod cognoscimus aliquid, secundum hoc illud nominamus; cum nomina, secundum philosophum, sint signa intellectuum. Sed per prius cognoscimus creaturam quam Deum. Ergo nomina a nobis imposita, per prius conveniunt creaturis quam Deo.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28832)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Dionysium, in libro de Div. Nom., Deum ex creaturis nominamus. Sed nomina a creaturis translata in Deum, per prius dicuntur de creaturis quam de Deo; sicut leo, lapis, et huiusmodi. Ergo omnia nomina quae de Deo et de creaturis dicuntur, per prius de creaturis quam de Deo dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28833)\n\nPraeterea, omnia nomina quae communiter de Deo et creaturis dicuntur, dicuntur de Deo sicut de causa omnium, ut dicit Dionysius. Sed quod dicitur de aliquo per causam, per posterius de illo dicitur, per prius enim dicitur animal sanum quam medicina, quae est causa sanitatis. Ergo huiusmodi nomina per prius dicuntur de creaturis quam de Deo.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28834)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ephes. III, *flecto genua mea ad patrem domini nostri Iesu, ex quo omnis paternitas in caelo et in terra nominatur*. Et eadem ratio videtur de nominibus aliis quae de Deo et creaturis dicuntur. Ergo huiusmodi nomina per prius de Deo quam de creaturis dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28835)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in omnibus nominibus quae de pluribus analogice dicuntur, necesse est quod omnia dicantur per respectum ad unum, et ideo illud unum oportet quod ponatur in definitione omnium. Et quia ratio quam significat nomen, est definitio, ut dicitur in IV Metaphys., necesse est quod illud nomen per prius dicatur de eo quod ponitur in definitione aliorum, et per posterius de aliis, secundum ordinem quo appropinquant ad illud primum vel magis vel minus, sicut sanum quod dicitur de animali, cadit in definitione sani quod dicitur de medicina, quae dicitur sana inquantum causat sanitatem in animali; et in definitione sani quod dicitur de urina, quae dicitur sana inquantum est signum sanitatis animalis. Sic ergo omnia nomina quae metaphorice de Deo dicuntur, per prius de creaturis dicuntur quam de Deo, quia dicta de Deo, nihil aliud significant quam similitudines ad tales creaturas. Sicut enim ridere, dictum de prato, nihil aliud significat quam quod pratum similiter se habet in decore cum floret, sicut homo cum ridet, secundum similitudinem proportionis; sic nomen leonis, dictum de Deo, nihil aliud significat quam quod Deus similiter se habet ut fortiter operetur in suis operibus, sicut leo in suis. Et sic patet quod, secundum quod dicuntur de Deo, eorum significatio definiri non potest, nisi per illud quod de creaturis dicitur. De aliis autem nominibus, quae non metaphorice dicuntur de Deo, esset etiam eadem ratio, si dicerentur de Deo causaliter tantum, ut quidam posuerunt. Sic enim. Cum dicitur Deus est bonus, nihil aliud esset quam Deus est causa bonitatis creaturae, et sic hoc nomen bonum, dictum de Deo, clauderet in suo intellectu bonitatem creaturae. Unde bonum per prius diceretur de creatura quam de Deo. Sed supra ostensum est quod huiusmodi nomina non solum dicuntur de Deo causaliter, sed etiam essentialiter. Cum enim dicitur Deus est bonus, vel sapiens, non solum significatur quod ipse sit causa sapientiae vel bonitatis, sed quod haec in eo eminentius praeexistunt. Unde, secundum hoc, dicendum est quod, quantum ad rem significatam per nomen, per prius dicuntur de Deo quam de creaturis, quia a Deo huiusmodi perfectiones in creaturas manant. Sed quantum ad impositionem nominis, per prius a nobis imponuntur creaturis, quas prius cognoscimus. Unde et modum significandi habent qui competit creaturis, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28836)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit quantum ad impositionem nominis.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28837)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non est eadem ratio de nominibus quae metaphorice de Deo dicuntur, et de aliis, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28838)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod obiectio illa procederet, si huiusmodi nomina solum de Deo causaliter dicerentur et non essentialiter, sicut sanum de medicina.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28839)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina quae important relationem ad creaturas, non dicantur de Deo ex tempore. Omnia enim huiusmodi nomina significant divinam substantiam, ut communiter dicitur. Unde et Ambrosius dicit quod hoc nomen dominus est nomen potestatis, quae est divina substantia, et creator significat Dei actionem, quae est eius essentia. Sed divina substantia non est temporalis, sed aeterna. Ergo huiusmodi nomina non dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, sed ab aeterno.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28840)\n\nPraeterea, cuicumque convenit aliquid ex tempore, potest dici factum, quod enim ex tempore est album, fit album. Sed Deo non convenit esse factum. Ergo de Deo nihil praedicatur ex tempore.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28841)\n\nPraeterea, si aliqua nomina dicuntur de Deo ex tempore propter hoc quod important relationem ad creaturas, eadem ratio videtur de omnibus quae relationem ad creaturas important. Sed quaedam nomina importantia relationem ad creaturas, dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno, ab aeterno enim scivit creaturam et dilexit, secundum illud Ierem. XXXI, *in caritate perpetua dilexi te*. Ergo et alia nomina quae important relationem ad creaturas, ut dominus et creator, dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28842)\n\nPraeterea, huiusmodi nomina relationem significant. Oportet igitur quod relatio illa vel sit aliquid in Deo, vel in creatura tantum. Sed non potest esse quod sit in creatura tantum, quia sic Deus denominaretur dominus a relatione opposita, quae est in creaturis; nihil autem denominatur a suo opposito. Relinquitur ergo quod relatio est etiam aliquid in Deo. Sed in Deo nihil potest esse ex tempore, cum ipse sit supra tempus. Ergo videtur quod huiusmodi nomina non dicantur de Deo ex tempore.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28843)\n\nPraeterea, secundum relationem dicitur aliquid relative, puta secundum dominium dominus, sicut secundum albedinem albus. Si igitur relatio dominii non est in Deo secundum rem, sed solum secundum rationem, sequitur quod Deus non sit realiter dominus, quod patet esse falsum.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28844)\n\nPraeterea, in relativis quae non sunt simul natura, unum potest esse, altero non existente, sicut scibile existit, non existente scientia, ut dicitur in praedicamentis. Sed relativa quae dicuntur de Deo et creaturis, non sunt simul natura. Ergo potest aliquid dici relative de Deo ad creaturam, etiam creatura non existente. Et sic huiusmodi nomina, dominus et creator, dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno, et non ex tempore.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28845)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, V de Trin., quod haec relativa appellatio dominus Deo convenit ex tempore.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28846)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quaedam nomina importantia relationem ad creaturam, ex tempore de Deo dicuntur, et non ab aeterno. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod quidam posuerunt relationem non esse rem naturae, sed rationis tantum. Quod quidem apparet esse falsum, ex hoc quod ipsae res naturalem ordinem et habitudinem habent ad invicem. Veruntamen sciendum est quod, cum relatio requirat duo extrema, tripliciter se habere potest ad hoc quod sit res naturae et rationis. Quandoque enim ex utraque parte est res rationis tantum, quando scilicet ordo vel habitudo non potest esse inter aliqua, nisi secundum apprehensionem rationis tantum, utpote cum dicimus idem eidem idem. Nam secundum quod ratio apprehendit bis aliquod unum, statuit illud ut duo; et sic apprehendit quandam habitudinem ipsius ad seipsum. Et similiter est de omnibus relationibus quae sunt inter ens et non ens; quas format ratio, inquantum apprehendit non ens ut quoddam extremum. Et idem est de omnibus relationibus quae consequuntur actum rationis, ut genus et species, et huiusmodi. Quaedam vero relationes sunt, quantum ad utrumque extremum, res naturae, quando scilicet est habitudo inter aliqua duo secundum aliquid realiter conveniens utrique. Sicut patet de omnibus relationibus quae consequuntur quantitatem, ut magnum et parvum, duplum et dimidium, et huiusmodi, nam quantitas est in utroque extremorum. Et simile est de relationibus quae consequuntur actionem et passionem, ut motivum et mobile, pater et filius, et similia. Quandoque vero relatio in uno extremorum est res naturae, et in altero est res rationis tantum. Et hoc contingit quandocumque duo extrema non sunt unius ordinis. Sicut sensus et scientia referuntur ad sensibile et scibile, quae quidem, inquantum sunt res quaedam in esse naturali existentes, sunt extra ordinem esse sensibilis et intelligibilis, et ideo in scientia quidem et sensu est relatio realis, secundum quod ordinantur ad sciendum vel sentiendum res; sed res ipsae in se consideratae, sunt extra ordinem huiusmodi. Unde in eis non est aliqua relatio realiter ad scientiam et sensum; sed secundum rationem tantum, inquantum intellectus apprehendit ea ut terminos relationum scientiae et sensus. Unde philosophus dicit, in V Metaphys., quod non dicuntur relative eo quod ipsa referantur ad alia, sed quia alia referuntur ad ipsa. Et similiter dextrum non dicitur de columna, nisi inquantum ponitur animali ad dextram, unde huiusmodi relatio non est realiter in columna, sed in animali. Cum igitur Deus sit extra totum ordinem creaturae, et omnes creaturae ordinentur ad ipsum, et non e converso, manifestum est quod creaturae realiter referuntur ad ipsum Deum; sed in Deo non est aliqua realis relatio eius ad creaturas, sed secundum rationem tantum, inquantum creaturae referuntur ad ipsum. Et sic nihil prohibet huiusmodi nomina importantia relationem ad creaturam, praedicari de Deo ex tempore, non propter aliquam mutationem ipsius, sed propter creaturae mutationem; sicut columna fit dextera animali, nulla mutatione circa ipsam existente, sed animali translato.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28847)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod relativa quaedam sunt imposita ad significandum ipsas habitudines relativas, ut dominus, servus, pater et filius, et huiusmodi, et haec dicuntur relativa secundum esse. Quaedam vero sunt imposita ad significandas res quas consequuntur quaedam habitudines, sicut movens et motum, caput et capitatum, et alia huiusmodi, quae dicuntur relativa secundum dici. Sic igitur et circa nomina divina haec differentia est consideranda. Nam quaedam significant ipsam habitudinem ad creaturam, ut dominus. Et huiusmodi non significant substantiam divinam directe, sed indirecte, inquantum praesupponunt ipsam, sicut dominium praesupponit potestatem, quae est divina substantia. Quaedam vero significant directe essentiam divinam, et ex consequenti important habitudinem; sicut salvator, creator, et huiusmodi, significant actionem Dei, quae est eius essentia. Utraque tamen nomina ex tempore de Deo dicuntur quantum ad habitudinem quam important, vel principaliter vel consequenter, non autem quantum ad hoc quod significant essentiam, vel directe vel indirecte.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28848)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut relationes quae de Deo dicuntur ex tempore, non sunt in Deo nisi secundum rationem, ita nec fieri nec factum esse dicitur de Deo, nisi secundum rationem, nulla mutatione circa ipsum existente, sicut est id, *domine refugium factus es nobis*.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28849)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod operatio intellectus et voluntatis est in operante, et ideo nomina quae significant relationes consequentes actionem intellectus vel voluntatis, dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno. Quae vero consequuntur actiones procedentes, secundum modum intelligendi, ad exteriores effectus, dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, ut salvator, creator, et huiusmodi.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28850)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod relationes significatae per huiusmodi nomina quae dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, sunt in Deo secundum rationem tantum, oppositae autem relationes in creaturis sunt secundum rem. Nec est inconveniens quod a relationibus realiter existentibus in re, Deus denominetur, tamen secundum quod cointelliguntur per intellectum nostrum oppositae relationes in Deo. Ut sic Deus dicatur relative ad creaturam, quia creatura refertur ad ipsum, sicut philosophus dicit, in V Metaphys., quod scibile dicitur relative, quia scientia refertur ad ipsum.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28851)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, cum ea ratione referatur Deus ad creaturam, qua creatura refertur ad ipsum; cum relatio subiectionis realiter sit in creatura, sequitur quod Deus non secundum rationem tantum, sed realiter sit dominus. Eo enim modo dicitur dominus, quo creatura ei subiecta est.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28852)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod, ad cognoscendum utrum relativa sint simul natura vel non, non oportet considerare ordinem rerum de quibus relativa dicuntur, sed significationes ipsorum relativorum. Si enim unum in sui intellectu claudat aliud et e converso, tunc sunt simul natura, sicut duplum et dimidium, pater et filius, et similia. Si autem unum in sui intellectu claudat aliud, et non e converso, tunc non sunt simul natura. Et hoc modo se habent scientia et scibile. Nam scibile dicitur secundum potentiam, scientia autem secundum habitum, vel secundum actum. Unde scibile, secundum modum suae significationis, praeexistit scientiae. Sed si accipiatur scibile secundum actum, tunc est simul cum scientia secundum actum, nam scitum non est aliquid nisi sit eius scientia. Licet igitur Deus sit prior creaturis, quia tamen in significatione domini clauditur quod habeat servum, et e converso, ista duo relativa, dominus et servus, sunt simul natura. Unde Deus non fuit dominus, antequam haberet creaturam sibi subiectam.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 13 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28853)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen Deus non sit nomen naturae. Dicit enim Damascenus, in I libro, quod *Deus dicitur a theein*, quod est currere, et fovere universa; *vel ab aethein, idest ardere (Deus enim noster ignis consumens est omnem malitiam); vel a theasthai*, quod est considerare, omnia. Haec autem omnia ad operationem pertinent. Ergo hoc nomen Deus operationem significat, et non naturam.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28854)\n\nPraeterea, secundum hoc aliquid nominatur a nobis, secundum quod cognoscitur. Sed divina natura est nobis ignota. Ergo hoc nomen Deus non significat naturam divinam.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28855)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Ambrosius, in libro I de fide, quod Deus est nomen naturae.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28856)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod non est semper idem id a quo imponitur nomen ad significandum, et id ad quod significandum nomen imponitur. Sicut enim substantiam rei ex proprietatibus vel operationibus eius cognoscimus, ita substantiam rei denominamus quandoque ab aliqua eius operatione vel proprietate, sicut substantiam lapidis denominamus ab aliqua actione eius, quia laedit pedem; non tamen hoc nomen impositum est ad significandum hanc actionem, sed substantiam lapidis. Si qua vero sunt quae secundum se sunt nota nobis, ut calor, frigus, albedo, et huiusmodi, non ab aliis denominantur. Unde in talibus idem est quod nomen significat, et id a quo imponitur nomen ad significandum. Quia igitur Deus non est notus nobis in sui natura, sed innotescit nobis ex operationibus vel effectibus eius, ex his possumus eum nominare, ut supra dictum est. Unde hoc nomen Deus est nomen operationis, quantum ad id a quo imponitur ad significandum. Imponitur enim hoc nomen ab universali rerum providentia, omnes enim loquentes de Deo, hoc intendunt nominare Deum, quod habet providentiam universalem de rebus. Unde dicit Dionysius, XII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *deitas est quae omnia videt providentia et bonitate perfecta*. Ex hac autem operatione hoc nomen Deus assumptum, impositum est ad significandum divinam naturam.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28857)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod omnia quae posuit Damascenus, pertinent ad providentiam, a qua imponitur hoc nomen Deus ad significandum.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28858)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, secundum quod naturam alicuius rei ex eius proprietatibus et effectibus cognoscere possumus, sic eam nomine possumus significare. Unde, quia substantiam lapidis ex eius proprietate possumus cognoscere secundum seipsam, sciendo quid est lapis, hoc nomen lapis ipsam lapidis naturam, secundum quod in se est, significat, significat enim definitionem lapidis, per quam scimus quid est lapis. Ratio enim quam significat nomen, est definitio, ut dicitur in IV Metaphys. Sed ex effectibus divinis divinam naturam non possumus cognoscere secundum quod in se est, ut sciamus de ea quid est; sed per modum eminentiae et causalitatis et negationis, ut supra dictum est. Et sic hoc nomen Deus significat naturam divinam. Impositum est enim nomen hoc ad aliquid significandum supra omnia existens, quod est principium omnium, et remotum ab omnibus. Hoc enim intendunt significare nominantes Deum.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28859)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen Deus sit communicabile. Cuicumque enim communicatur res significata per nomen, communicatur et nomen ipsum. Sed hoc nomen Deus, ut dictum est, significat divinam naturam, quae est communicabilis aliis, secundum illud II Pet. I, *magna et pretiosa promissa nobis donavit, ut per hoc efficiamur divinae consortes naturae*. Ergo hoc nomen Deus est communicabile.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28860)\n\nPraeterea, sola nomina propria non sunt communicabilia. Sed hoc nomen Deus non est nomen proprium, sed appellativum, quod patet ex hoc quod habet plurale, secundum illud Psalmi LXXXI, *ego dixi, dii estis*. Ergo hoc nomen Deus est communicabile.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28861)\n\nPraeterea, hoc nomen Deus imponitur ab operatione, ut dictum est. Sed alia nomina quae imponuntur Deo ab operationibus, sive ab effectibus, sunt communicabilia, ut bonus, sapiens et huiusmodi. Ergo et hoc nomen Deus est communicabile.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28862)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XIV, *incommunicabile nomen lignis et lapidibus imposuerunt*; et loquitur de nomine deitatis. Ergo hoc nomen Deus est nomen incommunicabile.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28863)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliquod nomen potest esse communicabile dupliciter, uno modo, proprie; alio modo, per similitudinem. Proprie quidem communicabile est, quod secundum totam significationem nominis, est communicabile multis. Per similitudinem autem communicabile est, quod est communicabile secundum aliquid eorum quae includuntur in nominis significatione. Hoc enim nomen leo proprie communicatur omnibus illis in quibus invenitur natura quam significat hoc nomen leo, per similitudinem vero communicabile est illis qui participant aliquid leoninum, ut puta audaciam vel fortitudinem, qui metaphorice leones dicuntur. Ad sciendum autem quae nomina proprie sunt communicabilia, considerandum est quod omnis forma in supposito singulari existens, per quod individuatur, communis est multis, vel secundum rem vel secundum rationem saltem, sicut natura humana communis est multis secundum rem et rationem, natura autem solis non est communis multis secundum rem, sed secundum rationem tantum; potest enim natura solis intelligi ut in pluribus suppositis existens. Et hoc ideo, quia intellectus intelligit naturam cuiuslibet speciei per abstractionem a singulari, unde esse in uno supposito singulari vel in pluribus, est praeter intellectum naturae speciei, unde, servato intellectu naturae speciei, potest intelligi ut in pluribus existens. Sed singulare, ex hoc ipso quod est singulare, est divisum ab omnibus aliis. Unde omne nomen impositum ad significandum aliquod singulare, est incommunicabile et re et ratione, non enim potest nec in apprehensione cadere pluralitas huius individui. Unde nullum nomen significans aliquod individuum, est communicabile multis proprie, sed solum secundum similitudinem; sicut aliquis metaphorice potest dici Achilles, inquantum habet aliquid de proprietatibus Achillis, scilicet fortitudinem. Formae vero quae non individuantur per aliquod suppositum, sed per seipsas (quia scilicet sunt formae subsistentes), si intelligerentur secundum quod sunt in seipsis, non possent communicari nec re neque ratione; sed forte per similitudinem, sicut dictum est de individuis. Sed quia formas simplices per se subsistentes non possumus intelligere secundum quod sunt, sed intelligimus eas ad modum rerum compositarum habentium formas in materia; ideo, ut dictum est, imponimus eis nomina concreta significantia naturam in aliquo supposito. Unde, quantum pertinet ad rationem nominum, eadem ratio est de nominibus quae a nobis imponuntur ad significandum naturas rerum compositarum, et de nominibus quae a nobis imponuntur ad significandum naturas simplices subsistentes. Unde, cum hoc nomen Deus impositum sit ad significandum naturam divinam, ut dictum est; natura autem divina multiplicabilis non est, ut supra ostensum est, sequitur quod hoc nomen Deus incommunicabile quidem sit secundum rem, sed communicabile sit secundum opinionem, quemadmodum hoc nomen sol esset communicabile secundum opinionem ponentium multos soles. Et secundum hoc dicitur Gal. IV, *his qui natura non sunt dii, serviebatis*; Glossa, *non sunt dii natura, sed opinione hominum*. Est nihilominus communicabile hoc nomen Deus, non secundum suam totam significationem, sed secundum aliquid eius, per quandam similitudinem, ut dii dicantur, qui participant aliquid divinum per similitudinem, secundum illud, *ego dixi, dii estis*. Si vero esset aliquod nomen impositum ad significandum Deum non ex parte naturae, sed ex parte suppositi, secundum quod consideratur ut hoc aliquid, illud nomen esset omnibus modis incommunicabile, sicut forte est nomen tetragrammaton apud Hebraeos. Et est simile si quis imponeret nomen soli designans hoc individuum.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28864)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod natura divina non est communicabilis nisi secundum similitudinis participationem.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28865)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod hoc nomen Deus est nomen appellativum, et non proprium, quia significat naturam divinam ut in habente; licet ipse Deus, secundum rem, non sit nec universalis nec particularis. Nomina enim non sequuntur modum essendi qui est in rebus, sed modum essendi secundum quod in cognitione nostra est. Et tamen, secundum rei veritatem, est incommunicabile, secundum quod dictum est de hoc nomine sol.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28866)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod haec nomina bonus, sapiens, et similia, imposita quidem sunt a perfectionibus procedentibus a Deo in creaturas, non tamen sunt imposita ad significandum divinam naturam, sed ad significandum ipsas perfectiones absolute. Et ideo etiam secundum rei veritatem sunt communicabilia multis. Sed hoc nomen Deus impositum est ab operatione propria Deo, quam experimur continue, ad significandum divinam naturam.\n\n## Articulus 10\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28867)\n\nAd decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen Deus univoce dicatur de Deo per naturam, et per participationem, et secundum opinionem. Ubi enim est diversa significatio, non est contradictio affirmantis et negantis, aequivocatio enim impedit contradictionem sed Catholicus dicens idolum non est Deus, contradicit Pagano dicenti idolum est Deus. Ergo Deus utrobique sumptum univoce dicitur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28868)\n\nPraeterea, sicut idolum est Deus secundum opinionem et non secundum veritatem, ita fruitio carnalium delectationum dicitur felicitas secundum opinionem, et non secundum veritatem. Sed hoc nomen beatitudo univoce dicitur de hac beatitudine opinata, et de hac beatitudine vera. Ergo et hoc nomen Deus univoce dicitur de Deo secundum veritatem, et de Deo secundum opinionem.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28869)\n\nPraeterea, univoca dicuntur quorum est ratio una. Sed Catholicus, cum dicit unum esse Deum, intelligit nomine Dei rem omnipotentem, et super omnia venerandam, et hoc idem intelligit gentilis, cum dicit idolum esse Deum. Ergo hoc nomen Deus univoce dicitur utrobique.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28870)\n\nSed contra, illud quod est in intellectu, est similitudo eius quod est in re, ut dicitur in I Periherm. Sed animal, dictum de animali vero et de animali picto, aequivoce dicitur. Ergo hoc nomen Deus, dictum de Deo vero et de Deo secundum opinionem, aequivoce dicitur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28871)\n\nPraeterea, nullus potest significare id quod non cognoscit, sed gentilis non cognoscit naturam divinam, ergo, cum dicit idolum est Deus, non significat veram deitatem. Hanc autem significat Catholicus dicens unum esse Deum. Ergo hoc nomen Deus non dicitur univoce, sed aequivoce, de Deo vero, et de Deo secundum opinionem.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28872)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod hoc nomen Deus, in praemissis tribus significationibus, non accipitur neque univoce neque aequivoce, sed analogice. Quod ex hoc patet. Quia univocorum est omnino eadem ratio, aequivocorum est omnino ratio diversa, in analogicis vero, oportet quod nomen secundum unam significationem acceptum, ponatur in definitione eiusdem nominis secundum alias significationes accepti. Sicut ens de substantia dictum, ponitur in definitione entis secundum quod de accidente dicitur; et sanum dictum de animali, ponitur in definitione sani secundum quod dicitur de urina et de medicina; huius enim sani quod est in animali, urina est significativa, et medicina factiva. Sic accidit in proposito. Nam hoc nomen Deus, secundum quod pro Deo vero sumitur, in ratione Dei sumitur secundum quod dicitur Deus secundum opinionem vel participationem. Cum enim aliquem nominamus Deum secundum participationem, intelligimus nomine Dei aliquid habens similitudinem veri Dei. Similiter cum idolum nominamus Deum, hoc nomine Deus intelligimus significari aliquid, de quo homines opinantur quod sit Deus. Et sic manifestum est quod alia et alia est significatio nominis, sed una illarum significationum clauditur in significationibus aliis. Unde manifestum est quod analogice dicitur.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28873)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod nominum multiplicitas non attenditur secundum nominis praedicationem, sed secundum significationem, hoc enim nomen homo, de quocumque praedicetur, sive vere sive false, dicitur uno modo. Sed tunc multipliciter diceretur, si per hoc nomen homo intenderemus significare diversa, puta, si unus intenderet significare per hoc nomen homo id quod vere est homo, et alius intenderet significare eodem nomine lapidem, vel aliquid aliud. Unde patet quod Catholicus dicens idolum non esse Deum, contradicit Pagano hoc asserenti, quia uterque utitur hoc nomine Deus ad significandum verum Deum. Cum enim Paganus dicit idolum esse Deum, non utitur hoc nomine secundum quod significat Deum opinabilem, sic enim verum diceret, cum etiam Catholici interdum in tali significatione hoc nomine utantur, ut cum dicitur, *omnes dii gentium Daemonia*.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28874)\n\nEt similiter dicendum ad secundum et tertium. Nam illae rationes procedunt secundum diversitatem praedicationis nominis, et non secundum diversam significationem.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28875)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod animal dictum de animali vero et de picto, non dicitur pure aequivoce; sed philosophus largo modo accipit aequivoca, secundum quod includunt in se analoga. Quia et ens, quod analogice dicitur, aliquando dicitur aequivoce praedicari de diversis praedicamentis.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28876)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod ipsam naturam Dei prout in se est, neque Catholicus neque Paganus cognoscit, sed uterque cognoscit eam secundum aliquam rationem causalitatis vel excellentiae vel remotionis, ut supra dictum est. Et secundum hoc, in eadem significatione accipere potest gentilis hoc nomen Deus, cum dicit idolum est Deus, in qua accipit ipsum Catholicus dicens idolum non est Deus. Si vero aliquis esset qui secundum nullam rationem Deum cognosceret, nec ipsum nominaret, nisi forte sicut proferimus nomina quorum significationem ignoramus.\n\n## Articulus 11\n\n[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28877)\n\nAd undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen qui est non sit maxime proprium nomen Dei. Hoc enim nomen Deus est nomen incommunicabile, ut dictum est. Sed hoc nomen qui est non est nomen incommunicabile. Ergo hoc nomen qui est non est maxime proprium nomen Dei.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28878)\n\nPraeterea, Dionysius dicit, III cap. de Div. Nom., quod *boni nominatio est manifestativa omnium Dei processionum*. Sed hoc maxime Deo convenit, quod sit universale rerum principium. Ergo hoc nomen bonum est maxime proprium Dei, et non hoc nomen qui est.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28879)\n\nPraeterea, omne nomen divinum videtur importare relationem ad creaturas, cum Deus non cognoscatur a nobis nisi per creaturas. Sed hoc nomen qui est nullam importat habitudinem ad creaturas. Ergo hoc nomen qui est non est maxime proprium nomen Dei.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28880)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Exod. III, quod Moysi quaerenti, *si dixerint mihi, quod est nomen eius? Quid dicam eis?* Et respondit ei dominus, *sic dices eis, qui est misit me ad vos*. Ergo hoc nomen qui est est maxime proprium nomen Dei.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28881)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod hoc nomen qui est triplici ratione est maxime proprium nomen Dei. Primo quidem, propter sui significationem. Non enim significat formam aliquam, sed ipsum esse. Unde, cum esse Dei sit ipsa eius essentia, et hoc nulli alii conveniat, ut supra ostensum est, manifestum est quod inter alia nomina hoc maxime proprie nominat Deum, unumquodque enim denominatur a sua forma. Secundo, propter eius universalitatem. Omnia enim alia nomina vel sunt minus communia; vel, si convertantur cum ipso, tamen addunt aliqua supra ipsum secundum rationem; unde quodammodo informant et determinant ipsum. Intellectus autem noster non potest ipsam Dei essentiam cognoscere in statu viae, secundum quod in se est, sed quemcumque modum determinet circa id quod de Deo intelligit, deficit a modo quo Deus in se est. Et ideo, quanto aliqua nomina sunt minus determinata, et magis communia et absoluta, tanto magis proprie dicuntur de Deo a nobis. Unde et Damascenus dicit quod *principalius omnibus quae de Deo dicuntur nominibus, est qui est, totum enim in seipso comprehendens, habet ipsum esse velut quoddam pelagus substantiae infinitum et indeterminatum*. Quolibet enim alio nomine determinatur aliquis modus substantiae rei, sed hoc nomen qui est nullum modum essendi determinat, sed se habet indeterminate ad omnes; et ideo nominat ipsum *pelagus substantiae infinitum*. Tertio vero, ex eius consignificatione. Significat enim esse in praesenti, et hoc maxime proprie de Deo dicitur, cuius esse non novit praeteritum vel futurum, ut dicit Augustinus in V de Trin.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28882)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc nomen qui est est magis proprium nomen Dei quam hoc nomen Deus, quantum ad id a quo imponitur, scilicet ab esse, et quantum ad modum significandi et consignificandi, ut dictum est. Sed quantum ad id ad quod imponitur nomen ad significandum, est magis proprium hoc nomen Deus, quod imponitur ad significandum naturam divinam. Et adhuc magis proprium nomen est tetragrammaton, quod est impositum ad significandam ipsam Dei substantiam incommunicabilem, et, ut sic liceat loqui, singularem.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28883)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod hoc nomen bonum est principale nomen Dei inquantum est causa, non tamen simpliciter, nam esse absolute praeintelligitur causae.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28884)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non est necessarium quod omnia nomina divina importent habitudinem ad creaturas; sed sufficit quod imponantur ab aliquibus perfectionibus procedentibus a Deo in creaturas. Inter quas prima est ipsum esse, a qua sumitur hoc nomen qui est.\n\n## Articulus 12\n\n[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28885)\n\nAd duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod propositiones affirmativae non possunt formari de Deo. Dicit enim Dionysius, II cap. Cael. Hier., quod *negationes de Deo sunt verae, affirmationes autem incompactae*.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28886)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin., quod *forma simplex subiectum esse non potest*. Sed Deus maxime est forma simplex, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo non potest esse subiectum. Sed omne illud de quo propositio affirmativa formatur, accipitur ut subiectum. Ergo de Deo propositio affirmativa formari non potest.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28887)\n\nPraeterea, omnis intellectus intelligens rem aliter quam sit, est falsus. Sed Deus habet esse absque omni compositione, ut supra probatum est. Cum igitur omnis intellectus affirmativus intelligat aliquid cum compositione, videtur quod propositio affirmativa vere de Deo formari non possit.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28888)\n\nSed contra est quod fidei non subest falsum. Sed propositiones quaedam affirmativae subduntur fidei, utpote quod Deus est trinus et unus, et quod est omnipotens. Ergo propositiones affirmativae possunt vere formari de Deo.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28889)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod propositiones affirmativae possunt vere formari de Deo. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod in qualibet propositione affirmativa vera, oportet quod praedicatum et subiectum significent idem secundum rem aliquo modo, et diversum secundum rationem. Et hoc patet tam in propositionibus quae sunt de praedicato accidentali, quam in illis quae sunt de praedicato substantiali. Manifestum est enim quod homo et albus sunt idem subiecto, et differunt ratione, alia enim est ratio hominis, et alia ratio albi. Et similiter cum dico homo est animal, illud enim ipsum quod est homo, vere animal est; in eodem enim supposito est et natura sensibilis, a qua dicitur animal, et rationalis, a qua dicitur homo. Unde hic etiam praedicatum et subiectum sunt idem supposito, sed diversa ratione. Sed et in propositionibus in quibus idem praedicatur de seipso, hoc aliquo modo invenitur; inquantum intellectus id quod ponit ex parte subiecti, trahit ad partem suppositi, quod vero ponit ex parte praedicati, trahit ad naturam formae in supposito existentis, secundum quod dicitur quod praedicata tenentur formaliter, et subiecta materialiter. Huic vero diversitati quae est secundum rationem, respondet pluralitas praedicati et subiecti, identitatem vero rei significat intellectus per ipsam compositionem. Deus autem, in se consideratus, est omnino unus et simplex, sed tamen intellectus noster secundum diversas conceptiones ipsum cognoscit, eo quod non potest ipsum ut in seipso est, videre. Sed tamen, quamvis intelligat ipsum sub diversis conceptionibus, cognoscit tamen quod omnibus suis conceptionibus respondet una et eadem res simpliciter. Hanc ergo pluralitatem quae est secundum rationem, repraesentat per pluralitatem praedicati et subiecti, unitatem vero repraesentat intellectus per compositionem.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28890)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius dicit affirmationes de Deo esse incompactas, vel inconvenientes secundum aliam translationem, inquantum nullum nomen Deo competit secundum modum significandi, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28891)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus noster non potest formas simplices subsistentes secundum quod in seipsis sunt, apprehendere, sed apprehendit eas secundum modum compositorum, in quibus est aliquid quod subiicitur, et est aliquid quod inest. Et ideo apprehendit formam simplicem in ratione subiecti, et attribuit ei aliquid.\n\n[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28892)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod haec propositio, intellectus intelligens rem aliter quam sit, est falsus, est duplex, ex eo quod hoc adverbium aliter potest determinare hoc verbum intelligit ex parte intellecti, vel ex parte intelligentis. Si ex parte intellecti, sic propositio vera est, et est sensus, quicumque intellectus intelligit rem esse aliter quam sit, falsus est. Sed hoc non habet locum in proposito, quia intellectus noster, formans propositionem de Deo, non dicit eum esse compositum, sed simplicem. Si vero ex parte intelligentis, sic propositio falsa est. Alius est enim modus intellectus in intelligendo, quam rei in essendo. Manifestum est enim quod intellectus noster res materiales infra se existentes intelligit immaterialiter; non quod intelligat eas esse immateriales, sed habet modum immaterialem in intelligendo. Et similiter, cum intelligit simplicia quae sunt supra se, intelligit ea secundum modum suum, scilicet composite, non tamen ita quod intelligat ea esse composita. Et sic intellectus noster non est falsus, formans compositionem de Deo.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0002","text":"[I q. 13 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003","text":"Consideratis his quae ad divinam cognitionem pertinent, procedendum est ad considerationem divinorum nominum, unumquodque enim nominatur a nobis, secundum quod ipsum cognoscimus. Circa hoc ergo quaeruntur duodecim. Primo, utrum Deus sit nominabilis a nobis. Secundo, utrum aliqua nomina dicta de Deo, praedicentur de ipso substantialiter. Tertio, utrum aliqua nomina dicta de Deo, proprie dicantur de ipso; an omnia attribuantur ei metaphorice. Quarto, utrum multa nomina dicta de Deo, sint synonyma. Quinto, utrum nomina aliqua dicantur de Deo et creaturis univoce, vel aequivoce. Sexto, supposito quod dicantur analogice, utrum dicantur de Deo per prius, vel de creaturis. Septimo, utrum aliqua nomina dicantur de Deo ex tempore. Octavo, utrum hoc nomen Deus sit nomen naturae, vel operationis. Nono, utrum hoc nomen Deus sit nomen communicabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004","text":"Decimo, utrum accipiatur univoce vel aequivoce, secundum quod significat Deum per naturam, et per participationem, et secundum opinionem. Undecimo, utrum hoc nomen qui est sit maxime proprium nomen Dei. Duodecimo, utrum propositiones affirmativae possint formari de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0006","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullum nomen Deo conveniat. Dicit enim Dionysius, I cap. de Div. Nom., quod neque nomen eius est, neque opinio. Et Prov. XXX dicitur, *quod nomen eius, et quod nomen filii eius, si nosti?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0008","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0009","text":"Praeterea, omne nomen aut dicitur in abstracto, aut in concreto. Sed nomina significantia in concreto, non competunt Deo, cum simplex sit, neque nomina significantia in abstracto, quia non significant aliquid perfectum subsistens. Ergo nullum nomen potest dici de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0010","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0011","text":"Praeterea, nomina significant substantiam cum qualitate; verba autem et participia significant cum tempore; pronomina autem cum demonstratione vel relatione. Quorum nihil competit Deo, quia sine qualitate est et sine omni accidente, et sine tempore; et sentiri non potest, ut demonstrari possit; nec relative significari, cum relativa sint aliquorum antedictorum recordativa, vel nominum, vel participiorum, vel pronominum demonstrativorum. Ergo Deus nullo modo potest nominari a nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0012","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0013","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Exod. XV, *dominus quasi vir pugnator, omnipotens nomen eius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0014","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, voces sunt signa intellectuum, et intellectus sunt rerum similitudines. Et sic patet quod voces referuntur ad res significandas, mediante conceptione intellectus. Secundum igitur quod aliquid a nobis intellectu cognosci potest, sic a nobis potest nominari. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus in hac vita non potest a nobis videri per suam essentiam; sed cognoscitur a nobis ex creaturis, secundum habitudinem principii, et per modum excellentiae et remotionis. Sic igitur potest nominari a nobis ex creaturis, non tamen ita quod nomen significans ipsum, exprimat divinam essentiam secundum quod est, sicut hoc nomen homo exprimit sua significatione essentiam hominis secundum quod est, significat enim eius definitionem, declarantem eius essentiam; ratio enim quam significat nomen, est definitio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0016","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ea ratione dicitur Deus non habere nomen, vel esse supra nominationem, quia essentia eius est supra id quod de Deo intelligimus et voce significamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0018","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quia ex creaturis in Dei cognitionem venimus, et ex ipsis eum nominamus, nomina quae Deo attribuimus, hoc modo significant, secundum quod competit creaturis materialibus, quarum cognitio est nobis connaturalis, ut supra dictum est. Et quia in huiusmodi creaturis, ea quae sunt perfecta et subsistentia sunt composita; forma autem in eis non est aliquid completum subsistens, sed magis quo aliquid est, inde est quod omnia nomina a nobis imposita ad significandum aliquid completum subsistens, significant in concretione, prout competit compositis; quae autem imponuntur ad significandas formas simplices, significant aliquid non ut subsistens, sed ut quo aliquid est, sicut albedo significat ut quo aliquid est album.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0020","text":"Quia igitur et Deus simplex est, et subsistens est, attribuimus ei et nomina abstracta, ad significandam simplicitatem eius; et nomina concreta, ad significandum subsistentiam et perfectionem ipsius, quamvis utraque nomina deficiant a modo ipsius, sicut intellectus noster non cognoscit eum ut est, secundum hanc vitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0021","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod significare substantiam cum qualitate, est significare suppositum cum natura vel forma determinata in qua subsistit. Unde, sicut de Deo dicuntur aliqua in concretione, ad significandum subsistentiam et perfectionem ipsius, sicut iam dictum est, ita dicuntur de Deo nomina significantia substantiam cum qualitate. Verba vero et participia consignificantia tempus dicuntur de ipso, ex eo quod aeternitas includit omne tempus, sicut enim simplicia subsistentia non possumus apprehendere et significare nisi per modum compositorum, ita simplicem aeternitatem non possumus intelligere vel voce exprimere, nisi per modum temporalium rerum; et hoc propter connaturalitatem intellectus nostri ad res compositas et temporales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0023","text":"Pronomina vero demonstrativa dicuntur de Deo, secundum quod faciunt demonstrationem ad id quod intelligitur, non ad id quod sentitur, secundum enim quod a nobis intelligitur, secundum hoc sub demonstratione cadit. Et sic, secundum illum modum quo nomina et participia et pronomina demonstrativa de Deo dicuntur, secundum hoc et pronominibus relativis significari potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0025","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullum nomen dicatur de Deo substantialiter. Dicit enim Damascenus, *oportet singulum eorum quae de Deo dicuntur, non quid est secundum substantiam significare, sed quid non est ostendere, aut habitudinem quandam, aut aliquid eorum quae assequuntur naturam vel operationem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0027","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0028","text":"Praeterea, dicit Dionysius, I cap. de Div. Nom., *omnem sanctorum theologorum hymnum invenies, ad bonos thearchiae processus, manifestative et laudative Dei nominationes dividentem*, et est sensus, quod nomina quae in divinam laudem sancti doctores assumunt, secundum processus ipsius Dei distinguuntur. Sed quod significat processum alicuius rei, nihil significat ad eius essentiam pertinens. Ergo nomina dicta de Deo, non dicuntur de ipso substantialiter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0029","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0030","text":"Praeterea, secundum hoc nominatur aliquid a nobis, secundum quod intelligitur. Sed non intelligitur Deus a nobis in hac vita secundum suam substantiam. Ergo nec aliquod nomen impositum a nobis, dicitur de Deo secundum suam substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0031","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, VI de Trin., *Deo hoc est esse, quod fortem esse vel sapientem esse, et si quid de illa simplicitate dixeris, quo eius substantia significatur*. Ergo omnia nomina huiusmodi significant divinam substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0033","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod de nominibus quae de Deo dicuntur negative, vel quae relationem ipsius ad creaturam significant, manifestum est quod substantiam eius nullo modo significant; sed remotionem alicuius ab ipso, vel relationem eius ad alium, vel potius alicuius ad ipsum. Sed de nominibus quae absolute et affirmative de Deo dicuntur, sicut bonus, sapiens, et huiusmodi, multipliciter aliqui sunt opinati. Quidam enim dixerunt quod haec omnia nomina, licet affirmative de Deo dicantur, tamen magis inventa sunt ad aliquid removendum a Deo, quam ad aliquid ponendum in ipso. Unde dicunt quod, cum dicimus Deum esse viventem, significamus quod Deus non hoc modo est, sicut res inanimatae, et similiter accipiendum est in aliis. Et hoc posuit Rabbi Moyses.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0035","text":"Alii vero dicunt quod haec nomina imposita sunt ad significandum habitudinem eius ad creata, ut, cum dicimus Deus est bonus, sit sensus, Deus est causa bonitatis in rebus. Et eadem ratio est in aliis. Sed utrumque istorum videtur esse inconveniens, propter tria. Primo quidem, quia secundum neutram harum positionum posset assignari ratio quare quaedam nomina magis de Deo dicerentur quam alia. Sic enim est causa corporum, sicut est causa bonorum, unde, si nihil aliud significatur, cum dicitur Deus est bonus, nisi Deus est causa bonorum, poterit similiter dici quod Deus est corpus, quia est causa corporum. Item, per hoc quod dicitur quod est corpus, removetur quod non sit ens in potentia tantum, sicut materia prima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","text":"Secundo, quia sequeretur quod omnia nomina dicta de Deo, per posterius dicerentur de ipso, sicut sanum per posterius dicitur de medicina, eo quod significat hoc tantum quod sit causa sanitatis in animali, quod per prius dicitur sanum. Tertio, quia hoc est contra intentionem loquentium de Deo. Aliud enim intendunt dicere, cum dicunt Deum viventem, quam quod sit causa vitae nostrae, vel quod differat a corporibus inanimatis. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod huiusmodi quidem nomina significant substantiam divinam, et praedicantur de Deo substantialiter, sed deficiunt a repraesentatione ipsius. Quod sic patet. Significant enim sic nomina Deum, secundum quod intellectus noster cognoscit ipsum. Intellectus autem noster, cum cognoscat Deum ex creaturis, sic cognoscit ipsum, secundum quod creaturae ipsum repraesentant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","text":"Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus in se praehabet omnes perfectiones creaturarum, quasi simpliciter et universaliter perfectus. Unde quaelibet creatura intantum eum repraesentat, et est ei similis, inquantum perfectionem aliquam habet, non tamen ita quod repraesentet eum sicut aliquid eiusdem speciei vel generis, sed sicut excellens principium, a cuius forma effectus deficiunt, cuius tamen aliqualem similitudinem effectus consequuntur; sicut formae corporum inferiorum repraesentant virtutem solarem. Et hoc supra expositum est, cum de perfectione divina agebatur. Sic igitur praedicta nomina divinam substantiam significant, imperfecte tamen, sicut et creaturae imperfecte eam repraesentant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0038","text":"Cum igitur dicitur Deus est bonus, non est sensus, Deus est causa bonitatis, vel Deus non est malus, sed est sensus, id quod bonitatem dicimus in creaturis, praeexistit in Deo, et hoc quidem secundum modum altiorem. Unde ex hoc non sequitur quod Deo competat esse bonum inquantum causat bonitatem, sed potius e converso, quia est bonus, bonitatem rebus diffundit, secundum illud Augustini, de Doct. Christ., *inquantum bonus est, sumus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0039","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Damascenus ideo dicit quod haec nomina non significant quid est Deus, quia a nullo istorum nominum exprimitur quid est Deus perfecte, sed unumquodque imperfecte eum significat, sicut et creaturae imperfecte eum repraesentant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0041","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in significatione nominum, aliud est quandoque a quo imponitur nomen ad significandum, et id ad quod significandum nomen imponitur, sicut hoc nomen lapis imponitur ab eo quod laedit pedem, non tamen imponitur ad hoc significandum quod significet laedens pedem, sed ad significandam quandam speciem corporum; alioquin omne laedens pedem esset lapis. Sic igitur dicendum est quod huiusmodi divina nomina imponuntur quidem a processibus deitatis, sicut enim secundum diversos processus perfectionum, creaturae Deum repraesentant, licet imperfecte; ita intellectus noster, secundum unumquemque processum, Deum cognoscit et nominat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0043","text":"Sed tamen haec nomina non imponit ad significandum ipsos processus, ut, cum dicitur Deus est vivens, sit sensus, ab eo procedit vita, sed ad significandum ipsum rerum principium, prout in eo praeexistit vita, licet eminentiori modo quam intelligatur vel significetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0044","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0045","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod essentiam Dei in hac vita cognoscere non possumus secundum quod in se est, sed cognoscimus eam secundum quod repraesentatur in perfectionibus creaturarum. Et sic nomina a nobis imposita eam significant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0046","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0047","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0048","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullum nomen dicatur de Deo proprie. Omnia enim nomina quae de Deo dicimus, sunt a creaturis accepta, ut dictum est. Sed nomina creaturarum metaphorice dicuntur de Deo, sicut cum dicitur Deus est lapis, vel leo, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Ergo omnia nomina dicta de Deo, dicuntur metaphorice.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0049","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0050","text":"Praeterea, nullum nomen proprie dicitur de aliquo, a quo verius removetur quam de eo praedicetur. Sed omnia huiusmodi nomina, bonus sapiens, et similia, verius removentur a Deo quam de eo praedicentur, ut patet per Dionysium, II cap. Cael. Hier. Ergo nullum istorum nominum proprie dicitur de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0051","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0052","text":"Praeterea, nomina corporum non dicuntur de Deo nisi metaphorice, cum sit incorporeus. Sed omnia huiusmodi nomina implicant quasdam corporales conditiones, significant enim cum tempore, et cum compositione, et cum aliis huiusmodi, quae sunt conditiones corporum. Ergo omnia huiusmodi nomina dicuntur de Deo metaphorice.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0053","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0054","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Ambrosius, in Lib. II de fide, *sunt quaedam nomina, quae evidenter proprietatem divinitatis ostendunt; et quaedam quae perspicuam divinae maiestatis exprimunt veritatem; alia vero sunt, quae translative per similitudinem de Deo dicuntur*. Non igitur omnia nomina dicuntur de Deo metaphorice, sed aliqua dicuntur proprie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0055","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, Deum cognoscimus ex perfectionibus procedentibus in creaturas ab ipso; quae quidem perfectiones in Deo sunt secundum eminentiorem modum quam in creaturis. Intellectus autem noster eo modo apprehendit eas, secundum quod sunt in creaturis, et secundum quod apprehendit, ita significat per nomina. In nominibus igitur quae Deo attribuimus, est duo considerare, scilicet, perfectiones ipsas significatas, ut bonitatem, vitam, et huiusmodi; et modum significandi. Quantum igitur ad id quod significant huiusmodi nomina, proprie competunt Deo, et magis proprie quam ipsis creaturis, et per prius dicuntur de eo. Quantum vero ad modum significandi, non proprie dicuntur de Deo, habent enim modum significandi qui creaturis competit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0057","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod quaedam nomina significant huiusmodi perfectiones a Deo procedentes in res creatas, hoc modo quod ipse modus imperfectus quo a creatura participatur divina perfectio, in ipso nominis significato includitur, sicut lapis significat aliquid materialiter ens, et huiusmodi nomina non possunt attribui Deo nisi metaphorice. Quaedam vero nomina significant ipsas perfectiones absolute, absque hoc quod aliquis modus participandi claudatur in eorum significatione, ut ens, bonum vivens, et huiusmodi, et talia proprie dicuntur de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0059","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ideo huiusmodi nomina dicit Dionysius negari a Deo, quia id quod significatur per nomen, non convenit eo modo ei, quo nomen significat, sed excellentiori modo. Unde ibidem dicit Dionysius quod Deus est super omnem substantiam et vitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0061","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ista nomina quae proprie dicuntur de Deo important conditiones corporales, non in ipso significato nominis, sed quantum ad modum significandi. Ea vero quae metaphorice de Deo dicuntur, important conditionem corporalem in ipso suo significato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0063","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0064","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0065","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ista nomina dicta de Deo, sint nomina synonyma. Synonyma enim nomina dicuntur, quae omnino idem significant. Sed ista nomina dicta de Deo, omnino idem significant in Deo, quia bonitas Dei est eius essentia, et similiter sapientia. Ergo ista nomina sunt omnino synonyma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0066","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0067","text":"Si dicatur quod ista nomina significant idem secundum rem, sed secundum rationes diversas, contra, ratio cui non respondet aliquid in re, est vana; si ergo istae rationes sunt multae, et res est una, videtur quod rationes istae sint vanae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0068","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0069","text":"Praeterea, magis est unum quod est unum re et ratione, quam quod est unum re et multiplex ratione. Sed Deus est maxime unus. Ergo videtur quod non sit unus re et multiplex ratione. Et sic nomina dicta de Deo non significant rationes diversas, et ita sunt synonyma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0070","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0071","text":"Sed contra, omnia synonyma, sibi invicem adiuncta, nugationem adducunt, sicut si dicatur vestis indumentum. Si igitur omnia nomina dicta de Deo sunt synonyma, non posset convenienter dici Deus bonus, vel aliquid huiusmodi; cum tamen scriptum sit Ierem. XXXII, *fortissime, magne, potens, dominus exercituum nomen tibi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0072","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod huiusmodi nomina dicta de Deo, non sunt synonyma. Quod quidem facile esset videre, si diceremus quod huiusmodi nomina sunt inducta ad removendum, vel ad designandum habitudinem causae respectu creaturarum, sic enim essent diversae rationes horum nominum secundum diversa negata, vel secundum diversos effectus connotatos. Sed secundum quod dictum est huiusmodi nomina substantiam divinam significare, licet imperfecte, etiam plane apparet, secundum praemissa, quod habent rationes diversas. Ratio enim quam significat nomen, est conceptio intellectus de re significata per nomen. Intellectus autem noster, cum cognoscat Deum ex creaturis, format ad intelligendum Deum conceptiones proportionatas perfectionibus procedentibus a Deo in creaturas. Quae quidem perfectiones in Deo praeexistunt unite et simpliciter, in creaturis vero recipiuntur divise et multipliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","text":"Sicut igitur diversis perfectionibus creaturarum respondet unum simplex principium, repraesentatum per diversas perfectiones creaturarum varie et multipliciter; ita variis et multiplicibus conceptibus intellectus nostri respondet unum omnino simplex, secundum huiusmodi conceptiones imperfecte intellectum. Et ideo nomina Deo attributa, licet significent unam rem, tamen, quia significant eam sub rationibus multis et diversis, non sunt synonyma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0075","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0076","text":"Et sic patet solutio ad primum, quia nomina synonyma dicuntur, quae significant unum secundum unam rationem. Quae enim significant rationes diversas unius rei, non primo et per se unum significant, quia nomen non significat rem, nisi mediante conceptione intellectus, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0077","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0078","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod rationes plures horum nominum non sunt cassae et vanae, quia omnibus eis respondet unum quid simplex, per omnia huiusmodi multipliciter et imperfecte repraesentatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0079","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0080","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod hoc ipsum ad perfectam Dei unitatem pertinet, quod ea quae sunt multipliciter et divisim in aliis, in ipso sunt simpliciter et unite. Et ex hoc contingit quod est unus re et plures secundum rationem, quia intellectus noster ita multipliciter apprehendit eum, sicut res multipliciter ipsum repraesentant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0081","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0082","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0083","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ea quae dicuntur de Deo et creaturis, univoce de ipsis dicantur. Omne enim aequivocum reducitur ad univocum, sicut multa ad unum. Nam si hoc nomen canis aequivoce dicitur de latrabili et marino, oportet quod de aliquibus univoce dicatur, scilicet de omnibus latrabilibus, aliter enim esset procedere in infinitum. Inveniuntur autem quaedam agentia univoca, quae conveniunt cum suis effectibus in nomine et definitione, ut homo generat hominem; quaedam vero agentia aequivoca, sicut sol causat calidum, cum tamen ipse non sit calidus nisi aequivoce. Videtur igitur quod primum agens, ad quod omnia agentia reducuntur, sit agens univocum. Et ita, quae de Deo et creaturis dicuntur, univoce praedicantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0084","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0085","text":"Praeterea, secundum aequivoca non attenditur aliqua similitudo. Cum igitur creaturae ad Deum sit aliqua similitudo, secundum illud Genes. I, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*, videtur quod aliquid univoce de Deo et creaturis dicatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0086","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0087","text":"Praeterea, mensura est homogenea mensurato, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sed Deus est prima mensura omnium entium, ut ibidem dicitur. Ergo Deus est homogeneus creaturis. Et ita aliquid univoce de Deo et creaturis dici potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0088","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0089","text":"Sed contra, quidquid praedicatur de aliquibus secundum idem nomen et non secundum eandem rationem, praedicatur de eis aequivoce. Sed nullum nomen convenit Deo secundum illam rationem, secundum quam dicitur de creatura, nam sapientia in creaturis est qualitas, non autem in Deo; genus autem variatum mutat rationem, cum sit pars definitionis. Et eadem ratio est in aliis. Quidquid ergo de Deo et creaturis dicitur, aequivoce dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0090","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0091","text":"Praeterea, Deus plus distat a creaturis, quam quaecumque creaturae ab invicem. Sed propter distantiam quarundam creaturarum, contingit quod nihil univoce de eis praedicari potest; sicut de his quae non conveniunt in aliquo genere. Ergo multo minus de Deo et creaturis aliquid univoce praedicatur, sed omnia praedicantur aequivoce.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0092","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est aliquid praedicari de Deo et creaturis univoce. Quia omnis effectus non adaequans virtutem causae agentis, recipit similitudinem agentis non secundum eandem rationem, sed deficienter, ita ut quod divisim et multipliciter est in effectibus, in causa est simpliciter et eodem modo; sicut sol secundum unam virtutem, multiformes et varias formas in istis inferioribus producit. Eodem modo, ut supra dictum est, omnes rerum perfectiones, quae sunt in rebus creatis divisim et multipliciter, in Deo praeexistunt unite. Sic igitur, cum aliquod nomen ad perfectionem pertinens de creatura dicitur, significat illam perfectionem ut distinctam secundum rationem definitionis ab aliis, puta cum hoc nomen sapiens de homine dicitur, significamus aliquam perfectionem distinctam ab essentia hominis, et a potentia et ab esse ipsius, et ab omnibus huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","text":"Sed cum hoc nomen de Deo dicimus, non intendimus significare aliquid distinctum ab essentia vel potentia vel esse ipsius. Et sic, cum hoc nomen sapiens de homine dicitur, quodammodo circumscribit et comprehendit rem significatam, non autem cum dicitur de Deo, sed relinquit rem significatam ut incomprehensam, et excedentem nominis significationem. Unde patet quod non secundum eandem rationem hoc nomen sapiens de Deo et de homine dicitur. Et eadem ratio est de aliis. Unde nullum nomen univoce de Deo et creaturis praedicatur. Sed nec etiam pure aequivoce, ut aliqui dixerunt. Quia secundum hoc, ex creaturis nihil posset cognosci de Deo, nec demonstrari; sed semper incideret fallacia aequivocationis. Et hoc est tam contra philosophos, qui multa demonstrative de Deo probant, quam etiam contra apostolum dicentem, Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","text":"Dicendum est igitur quod huiusmodi nomina dicuntur de Deo et creaturis secundum analogiam, idest proportionem. Quod quidem dupliciter contingit in nominibus, vel quia multa habent proportionem ad unum, sicut sanum dicitur de medicina et urina, inquantum utrumque habet ordinem et proportionem ad sanitatem animalis, cuius hoc quidem signum est, illud vero causa; vel ex eo quod unum habet proportionem ad alterum, sicut sanum dicitur de medicina et animali, inquantum medicina est causa sanitatis quae est in animali. Et hoc modo aliqua dicuntur de Deo et creaturis analogice, et non aequivoce pure, neque univoce. Non enim possumus nominare Deum nisi ex creaturis, ut supra dictum est. Et sic, quidquid dicitur de Deo et creaturis, dicitur secundum quod est aliquis ordo creaturae ad Deum, ut ad principium et causam, in qua praeexistunt excellenter omnes rerum perfectiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","text":"Et iste modus communitatis medius est inter puram aequivocationem et simplicem univocationem. Neque enim in his quae analogice dicuntur, est una ratio, sicut est in univocis; nec totaliter diversa, sicut in aequivocis; sed nomen quod sic multipliciter dicitur, significat diversas proportiones ad aliquid unum; sicut sanum, de urina dictum, significat signum sanitatis animalis, de medicina vero dictum, significat causam eiusdem sanitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0097","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0098","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet in praedicationibus oporteat aequivoca ad univoca reduci, tamen in actionibus agens non univocum ex necessitate praecedit agens univocum. Agens enim non univocum est causa universalis totius speciei, ut sol est causa generationis omnium hominum. Agens vero univocum non est causa agens universalis totius speciei (alioquin esset causa sui ipsius, cum sub specie contineatur), sed est causa particularis respectu huius individui, quod in participatione speciei constituit. Causa igitur universalis totius speciei non est agens univocum. Causa autem universalis est prior particulari. Hoc autem agens universale, licet non sit univocum, non tamen est omnino aequivocum, quia sic non faceret sibi simile; sed potest dici agens analogicum, sicut in praedicationibus omnia univoca reducuntur ad unum primum, non univocum, sed analogicum, quod est ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0099","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0100","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod similitudo creaturae ad Deum est imperfecta, quia etiam nec idem secundum genus repraesentat, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0101","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Deus non est mensura proportionata mensuratis. Unde non oportet quod Deus et creaturae sub uno genere contineantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0103","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0104","text":"Ea vero quae sunt in contrarium, concludunt quod non univoce huiusmodi nomina de Deo et creaturis praedicentur, non autem quod aequivoce.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0105","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0106","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0107","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina per prius dicantur de creaturis quam de Deo. Secundum enim quod cognoscimus aliquid, secundum hoc illud nominamus; cum nomina, secundum philosophum, sint signa intellectuum. Sed per prius cognoscimus creaturam quam Deum. Ergo nomina a nobis imposita, per prius conveniunt creaturis quam Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0108","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0109","text":"Praeterea, secundum Dionysium, in libro de Div. Nom., Deum ex creaturis nominamus. Sed nomina a creaturis translata in Deum, per prius dicuntur de creaturis quam de Deo; sicut leo, lapis, et huiusmodi. Ergo omnia nomina quae de Deo et de creaturis dicuntur, per prius de creaturis quam de Deo dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0110","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0111","text":"Praeterea, omnia nomina quae communiter de Deo et creaturis dicuntur, dicuntur de Deo sicut de causa omnium, ut dicit Dionysius. Sed quod dicitur de aliquo per causam, per posterius de illo dicitur, per prius enim dicitur animal sanum quam medicina, quae est causa sanitatis. Ergo huiusmodi nomina per prius dicuntur de creaturis quam de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0112","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0113","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ephes. III, *flecto genua mea ad patrem domini nostri Iesu, ex quo omnis paternitas in caelo et in terra nominatur*. Et eadem ratio videtur de nominibus aliis quae de Deo et creaturis dicuntur. Ergo huiusmodi nomina per prius de Deo quam de creaturis dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0114","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0115","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in omnibus nominibus quae de pluribus analogice dicuntur, necesse est quod omnia dicantur per respectum ad unum, et ideo illud unum oportet quod ponatur in definitione omnium. Et quia ratio quam significat nomen, est definitio, ut dicitur in IV Metaphys., necesse est quod illud nomen per prius dicatur de eo quod ponitur in definitione aliorum, et per posterius de aliis, secundum ordinem quo appropinquant ad illud primum vel magis vel minus, sicut sanum quod dicitur de animali, cadit in definitione sani quod dicitur de medicina, quae dicitur sana inquantum causat sanitatem in animali; et in definitione sani quod dicitur de urina, quae dicitur sana inquantum est signum sanitatis animalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0116","text":"Sic ergo omnia nomina quae metaphorice de Deo dicuntur, per prius de creaturis dicuntur quam de Deo, quia dicta de Deo, nihil aliud significant quam similitudines ad tales creaturas. Sicut enim ridere, dictum de prato, nihil aliud significat quam quod pratum similiter se habet in decore cum floret, sicut homo cum ridet, secundum similitudinem proportionis; sic nomen leonis, dictum de Deo, nihil aliud significat quam quod Deus similiter se habet ut fortiter operetur in suis operibus, sicut leo in suis. Et sic patet quod, secundum quod dicuntur de Deo, eorum significatio definiri non potest, nisi per illud quod de creaturis dicitur. De aliis autem nominibus, quae non metaphorice dicuntur de Deo, esset etiam eadem ratio, si dicerentur de Deo causaliter tantum, ut quidam posuerunt. Sic enim.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","text":"Cum dicitur Deus est bonus, nihil aliud esset quam Deus est causa bonitatis creaturae, et sic hoc nomen bonum, dictum de Deo, clauderet in suo intellectu bonitatem creaturae. Unde bonum per prius diceretur de creatura quam de Deo. Sed supra ostensum est quod huiusmodi nomina non solum dicuntur de Deo causaliter, sed etiam essentialiter. Cum enim dicitur Deus est bonus, vel sapiens, non solum significatur quod ipse sit causa sapientiae vel bonitatis, sed quod haec in eo eminentius praeexistunt. Unde, secundum hoc, dicendum est quod, quantum ad rem significatam per nomen, per prius dicuntur de Deo quam de creaturis, quia a Deo huiusmodi perfectiones in creaturas manant. Sed quantum ad impositionem nominis, per prius a nobis imponuntur creaturis, quas prius cognoscimus. Unde et modum significandi habent qui competit creaturis, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0118","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0119","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit quantum ad impositionem nominis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0120","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0121","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non est eadem ratio de nominibus quae metaphorice de Deo dicuntur, et de aliis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0122","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0123","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod obiectio illa procederet, si huiusmodi nomina solum de Deo causaliter dicerentur et non essentialiter, sicut sanum de medicina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0124","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0125","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0126","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina quae important relationem ad creaturas, non dicantur de Deo ex tempore. Omnia enim huiusmodi nomina significant divinam substantiam, ut communiter dicitur. Unde et Ambrosius dicit quod hoc nomen dominus est nomen potestatis, quae est divina substantia, et creator significat Dei actionem, quae est eius essentia. Sed divina substantia non est temporalis, sed aeterna. Ergo huiusmodi nomina non dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, sed ab aeterno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0127","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0128","text":"Praeterea, cuicumque convenit aliquid ex tempore, potest dici factum, quod enim ex tempore est album, fit album. Sed Deo non convenit esse factum. Ergo de Deo nihil praedicatur ex tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0129","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0130","text":"Praeterea, si aliqua nomina dicuntur de Deo ex tempore propter hoc quod important relationem ad creaturas, eadem ratio videtur de omnibus quae relationem ad creaturas important. Sed quaedam nomina importantia relationem ad creaturas, dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno, ab aeterno enim scivit creaturam et dilexit, secundum illud Ierem. XXXI, *in caritate perpetua dilexi te*. Ergo et alia nomina quae important relationem ad creaturas, ut dominus et creator, dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0131","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0132","text":"Praeterea, huiusmodi nomina relationem significant. Oportet igitur quod relatio illa vel sit aliquid in Deo, vel in creatura tantum. Sed non potest esse quod sit in creatura tantum, quia sic Deus denominaretur dominus a relatione opposita, quae est in creaturis; nihil autem denominatur a suo opposito. Relinquitur ergo quod relatio est etiam aliquid in Deo. Sed in Deo nihil potest esse ex tempore, cum ipse sit supra tempus. Ergo videtur quod huiusmodi nomina non dicantur de Deo ex tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0133","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0134","text":"Praeterea, secundum relationem dicitur aliquid relative, puta secundum dominium dominus, sicut secundum albedinem albus. Si igitur relatio dominii non est in Deo secundum rem, sed solum secundum rationem, sequitur quod Deus non sit realiter dominus, quod patet esse falsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0135","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0136","text":"Praeterea, in relativis quae non sunt simul natura, unum potest esse, altero non existente, sicut scibile existit, non existente scientia, ut dicitur in praedicamentis. Sed relativa quae dicuntur de Deo et creaturis, non sunt simul natura. Ergo potest aliquid dici relative de Deo ad creaturam, etiam creatura non existente. Et sic huiusmodi nomina, dominus et creator, dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno, et non ex tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0137","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0138","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, V de Trin., quod haec relativa appellatio dominus Deo convenit ex tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0139","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0140","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quaedam nomina importantia relationem ad creaturam, ex tempore de Deo dicuntur, et non ab aeterno. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod quidam posuerunt relationem non esse rem naturae, sed rationis tantum. Quod quidem apparet esse falsum, ex hoc quod ipsae res naturalem ordinem et habitudinem habent ad invicem. Veruntamen sciendum est quod, cum relatio requirat duo extrema, tripliciter se habere potest ad hoc quod sit res naturae et rationis. Quandoque enim ex utraque parte est res rationis tantum, quando scilicet ordo vel habitudo non potest esse inter aliqua, nisi secundum apprehensionem rationis tantum, utpote cum dicimus idem eidem idem. Nam secundum quod ratio apprehendit bis aliquod unum, statuit illud ut duo; et sic apprehendit quandam habitudinem ipsius ad seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0141","text":"Et similiter est de omnibus relationibus quae sunt inter ens et non ens; quas format ratio, inquantum apprehendit non ens ut quoddam extremum. Et idem est de omnibus relationibus quae consequuntur actum rationis, ut genus et species, et huiusmodi. Quaedam vero relationes sunt, quantum ad utrumque extremum, res naturae, quando scilicet est habitudo inter aliqua duo secundum aliquid realiter conveniens utrique. Sicut patet de omnibus relationibus quae consequuntur quantitatem, ut magnum et parvum, duplum et dimidium, et huiusmodi, nam quantitas est in utroque extremorum. Et simile est de relationibus quae consequuntur actionem et passionem, ut motivum et mobile, pater et filius, et similia. Quandoque vero relatio in uno extremorum est res naturae, et in altero est res rationis tantum. Et hoc contingit quandocumque duo extrema non sunt unius ordinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0142","text":"Sicut sensus et scientia referuntur ad sensibile et scibile, quae quidem, inquantum sunt res quaedam in esse naturali existentes, sunt extra ordinem esse sensibilis et intelligibilis, et ideo in scientia quidem et sensu est relatio realis, secundum quod ordinantur ad sciendum vel sentiendum res; sed res ipsae in se consideratae, sunt extra ordinem huiusmodi. Unde in eis non est aliqua relatio realiter ad scientiam et sensum; sed secundum rationem tantum, inquantum intellectus apprehendit ea ut terminos relationum scientiae et sensus. Unde philosophus dicit, in V Metaphys., quod non dicuntur relative eo quod ipsa referantur ad alia, sed quia alia referuntur ad ipsa. Et similiter dextrum non dicitur de columna, nisi inquantum ponitur animali ad dextram, unde huiusmodi relatio non est realiter in columna, sed in animali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","text":"Cum igitur Deus sit extra totum ordinem creaturae, et omnes creaturae ordinentur ad ipsum, et non e converso, manifestum est quod creaturae realiter referuntur ad ipsum Deum; sed in Deo non est aliqua realis relatio eius ad creaturas, sed secundum rationem tantum, inquantum creaturae referuntur ad ipsum. Et sic nihil prohibet huiusmodi nomina importantia relationem ad creaturam, praedicari de Deo ex tempore, non propter aliquam mutationem ipsius, sed propter creaturae mutationem; sicut columna fit dextera animali, nulla mutatione circa ipsam existente, sed animali translato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0144","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0145","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod relativa quaedam sunt imposita ad significandum ipsas habitudines relativas, ut dominus, servus, pater et filius, et huiusmodi, et haec dicuntur relativa secundum esse. Quaedam vero sunt imposita ad significandas res quas consequuntur quaedam habitudines, sicut movens et motum, caput et capitatum, et alia huiusmodi, quae dicuntur relativa secundum dici. Sic igitur et circa nomina divina haec differentia est consideranda. Nam quaedam significant ipsam habitudinem ad creaturam, ut dominus. Et huiusmodi non significant substantiam divinam directe, sed indirecte, inquantum praesupponunt ipsam, sicut dominium praesupponit potestatem, quae est divina substantia. Quaedam vero significant directe essentiam divinam, et ex consequenti important habitudinem; sicut salvator, creator, et huiusmodi, significant actionem Dei, quae est eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0146","text":"Utraque tamen nomina ex tempore de Deo dicuntur quantum ad habitudinem quam important, vel principaliter vel consequenter, non autem quantum ad hoc quod significant essentiam, vel directe vel indirecte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0147","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0148","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut relationes quae de Deo dicuntur ex tempore, non sunt in Deo nisi secundum rationem, ita nec fieri nec factum esse dicitur de Deo, nisi secundum rationem, nulla mutatione circa ipsum existente, sicut est id, *domine refugium factus es nobis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0149","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0150","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod operatio intellectus et voluntatis est in operante, et ideo nomina quae significant relationes consequentes actionem intellectus vel voluntatis, dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno. Quae vero consequuntur actiones procedentes, secundum modum intelligendi, ad exteriores effectus, dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, ut salvator, creator, et huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0151","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0152","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod relationes significatae per huiusmodi nomina quae dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, sunt in Deo secundum rationem tantum, oppositae autem relationes in creaturis sunt secundum rem. Nec est inconveniens quod a relationibus realiter existentibus in re, Deus denominetur, tamen secundum quod cointelliguntur per intellectum nostrum oppositae relationes in Deo. Ut sic Deus dicatur relative ad creaturam, quia creatura refertur ad ipsum, sicut philosophus dicit, in V Metaphys., quod scibile dicitur relative, quia scientia refertur ad ipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0153","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0154","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, cum ea ratione referatur Deus ad creaturam, qua creatura refertur ad ipsum; cum relatio subiectionis realiter sit in creatura, sequitur quod Deus non secundum rationem tantum, sed realiter sit dominus. Eo enim modo dicitur dominus, quo creatura ei subiecta est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0155","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0156","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod, ad cognoscendum utrum relativa sint simul natura vel non, non oportet considerare ordinem rerum de quibus relativa dicuntur, sed significationes ipsorum relativorum. Si enim unum in sui intellectu claudat aliud et e converso, tunc sunt simul natura, sicut duplum et dimidium, pater et filius, et similia. Si autem unum in sui intellectu claudat aliud, et non e converso, tunc non sunt simul natura. Et hoc modo se habent scientia et scibile. Nam scibile dicitur secundum potentiam, scientia autem secundum habitum, vel secundum actum. Unde scibile, secundum modum suae significationis, praeexistit scientiae. Sed si accipiatur scibile secundum actum, tunc est simul cum scientia secundum actum, nam scitum non est aliquid nisi sit eius scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0157","text":"Licet igitur Deus sit prior creaturis, quia tamen in significatione domini clauditur quod habeat servum, et e converso, ista duo relativa, dominus et servus, sunt simul natura. Unde Deus non fuit dominus, antequam haberet creaturam sibi subiectam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0158","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0159","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0160","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen Deus non sit nomen naturae. Dicit enim Damascenus, in I libro, quod *Deus dicitur a theein*, quod est currere, et fovere universa; *vel ab aethein, idest ardere (Deus enim noster ignis consumens est omnem malitiam); vel a theasthai*, quod est considerare, omnia. Haec autem omnia ad operationem pertinent. Ergo hoc nomen Deus operationem significat, et non naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0161","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0162","text":"Praeterea, secundum hoc aliquid nominatur a nobis, secundum quod cognoscitur. Sed divina natura est nobis ignota. Ergo hoc nomen Deus non significat naturam divinam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0163","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0164","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Ambrosius, in libro I de fide, quod Deus est nomen naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0165","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0166","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod non est semper idem id a quo imponitur nomen ad significandum, et id ad quod significandum nomen imponitur. Sicut enim substantiam rei ex proprietatibus vel operationibus eius cognoscimus, ita substantiam rei denominamus quandoque ab aliqua eius operatione vel proprietate, sicut substantiam lapidis denominamus ab aliqua actione eius, quia laedit pedem; non tamen hoc nomen impositum est ad significandum hanc actionem, sed substantiam lapidis. Si qua vero sunt quae secundum se sunt nota nobis, ut calor, frigus, albedo, et huiusmodi, non ab aliis denominantur. Unde in talibus idem est quod nomen significat, et id a quo imponitur nomen ad significandum. Quia igitur Deus non est notus nobis in sui natura, sed innotescit nobis ex operationibus vel effectibus eius, ex his possumus eum nominare, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","text":"Unde hoc nomen Deus est nomen operationis, quantum ad id a quo imponitur ad significandum. Imponitur enim hoc nomen ab universali rerum providentia, omnes enim loquentes de Deo, hoc intendunt nominare Deum, quod habet providentiam universalem de rebus. Unde dicit Dionysius, XII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *deitas est quae omnia videt providentia et bonitate perfecta*. Ex hac autem operatione hoc nomen Deus assumptum, impositum est ad significandum divinam naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0168","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0169","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod omnia quae posuit Damascenus, pertinent ad providentiam, a qua imponitur hoc nomen Deus ad significandum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0170","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0171","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, secundum quod naturam alicuius rei ex eius proprietatibus et effectibus cognoscere possumus, sic eam nomine possumus significare. Unde, quia substantiam lapidis ex eius proprietate possumus cognoscere secundum seipsam, sciendo quid est lapis, hoc nomen lapis ipsam lapidis naturam, secundum quod in se est, significat, significat enim definitionem lapidis, per quam scimus quid est lapis. Ratio enim quam significat nomen, est definitio, ut dicitur in IV Metaphys. Sed ex effectibus divinis divinam naturam non possumus cognoscere secundum quod in se est, ut sciamus de ea quid est; sed per modum eminentiae et causalitatis et negationis, ut supra dictum est. Et sic hoc nomen Deus significat naturam divinam. Impositum est enim nomen hoc ad aliquid significandum supra omnia existens, quod est principium omnium, et remotum ab omnibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0172","text":"Hoc enim intendunt significare nominantes Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0173","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0174","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0175","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen Deus sit communicabile. Cuicumque enim communicatur res significata per nomen, communicatur et nomen ipsum. Sed hoc nomen Deus, ut dictum est, significat divinam naturam, quae est communicabilis aliis, secundum illud II Pet. I, *magna et pretiosa promissa nobis donavit, ut per hoc efficiamur divinae consortes naturae*. Ergo hoc nomen Deus est communicabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0176","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0177","text":"Praeterea, sola nomina propria non sunt communicabilia. Sed hoc nomen Deus non est nomen proprium, sed appellativum, quod patet ex hoc quod habet plurale, secundum illud Psalmi LXXXI, *ego dixi, dii estis*. Ergo hoc nomen Deus est communicabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0178","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0179","text":"Praeterea, hoc nomen Deus imponitur ab operatione, ut dictum est. Sed alia nomina quae imponuntur Deo ab operationibus, sive ab effectibus, sunt communicabilia, ut bonus, sapiens et huiusmodi. Ergo et hoc nomen Deus est communicabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0180","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0181","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XIV, *incommunicabile nomen lignis et lapidibus imposuerunt*; et loquitur de nomine deitatis. Ergo hoc nomen Deus est nomen incommunicabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0182","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliquod nomen potest esse communicabile dupliciter, uno modo, proprie; alio modo, per similitudinem. Proprie quidem communicabile est, quod secundum totam significationem nominis, est communicabile multis. Per similitudinem autem communicabile est, quod est communicabile secundum aliquid eorum quae includuntur in nominis significatione. Hoc enim nomen leo proprie communicatur omnibus illis in quibus invenitur natura quam significat hoc nomen leo, per similitudinem vero communicabile est illis qui participant aliquid leoninum, ut puta audaciam vel fortitudinem, qui metaphorice leones dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0184","text":"Ad sciendum autem quae nomina proprie sunt communicabilia, considerandum est quod omnis forma in supposito singulari existens, per quod individuatur, communis est multis, vel secundum rem vel secundum rationem saltem, sicut natura humana communis est multis secundum rem et rationem, natura autem solis non est communis multis secundum rem, sed secundum rationem tantum; potest enim natura solis intelligi ut in pluribus suppositis existens. Et hoc ideo, quia intellectus intelligit naturam cuiuslibet speciei per abstractionem a singulari, unde esse in uno supposito singulari vel in pluribus, est praeter intellectum naturae speciei, unde, servato intellectu naturae speciei, potest intelligi ut in pluribus existens. Sed singulare, ex hoc ipso quod est singulare, est divisum ab omnibus aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0185","text":"Unde omne nomen impositum ad significandum aliquod singulare, est incommunicabile et re et ratione, non enim potest nec in apprehensione cadere pluralitas huius individui. Unde nullum nomen significans aliquod individuum, est communicabile multis proprie, sed solum secundum similitudinem; sicut aliquis metaphorice potest dici Achilles, inquantum habet aliquid de proprietatibus Achillis, scilicet fortitudinem. Formae vero quae non individuantur per aliquod suppositum, sed per seipsas (quia scilicet sunt formae subsistentes), si intelligerentur secundum quod sunt in seipsis, non possent communicari nec re neque ratione; sed forte per similitudinem, sicut dictum est de individuis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","text":"Sed quia formas simplices per se subsistentes non possumus intelligere secundum quod sunt, sed intelligimus eas ad modum rerum compositarum habentium formas in materia; ideo, ut dictum est, imponimus eis nomina concreta significantia naturam in aliquo supposito. Unde, quantum pertinet ad rationem nominum, eadem ratio est de nominibus quae a nobis imponuntur ad significandum naturas rerum compositarum, et de nominibus quae a nobis imponuntur ad significandum naturas simplices subsistentes. Unde, cum hoc nomen Deus impositum sit ad significandum naturam divinam, ut dictum est; natura autem divina multiplicabilis non est, ut supra ostensum est, sequitur quod hoc nomen Deus incommunicabile quidem sit secundum rem, sed communicabile sit secundum opinionem, quemadmodum hoc nomen sol esset communicabile secundum opinionem ponentium multos soles. Et secundum hoc dicitur Gal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187","text":"IV, *his qui natura non sunt dii, serviebatis*; Glossa, *non sunt dii natura, sed opinione hominum*. Est nihilominus communicabile hoc nomen Deus, non secundum suam totam significationem, sed secundum aliquid eius, per quandam similitudinem, ut dii dicantur, qui participant aliquid divinum per similitudinem, secundum illud, *ego dixi, dii estis*. Si vero esset aliquod nomen impositum ad significandum Deum non ex parte naturae, sed ex parte suppositi, secundum quod consideratur ut hoc aliquid, illud nomen esset omnibus modis incommunicabile, sicut forte est nomen tetragrammaton apud Hebraeos. Et est simile si quis imponeret nomen soli designans hoc individuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0188","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0189","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod natura divina non est communicabilis nisi secundum similitudinis participationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0190","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0191","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod hoc nomen Deus est nomen appellativum, et non proprium, quia significat naturam divinam ut in habente; licet ipse Deus, secundum rem, non sit nec universalis nec particularis. Nomina enim non sequuntur modum essendi qui est in rebus, sed modum essendi secundum quod in cognitione nostra est. Et tamen, secundum rei veritatem, est incommunicabile, secundum quod dictum est de hoc nomine sol.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0192","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0193","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod haec nomina bonus, sapiens, et similia, imposita quidem sunt a perfectionibus procedentibus a Deo in creaturas, non tamen sunt imposita ad significandum divinam naturam, sed ad significandum ipsas perfectiones absolute. Et ideo etiam secundum rei veritatem sunt communicabilia multis. Sed hoc nomen Deus impositum est ab operatione propria Deo, quam experimur continue, ad significandum divinam naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0194","text":"## Articulus 10","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0195","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0196","text":"Ad decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen Deus univoce dicatur de Deo per naturam, et per participationem, et secundum opinionem. Ubi enim est diversa significatio, non est contradictio affirmantis et negantis, aequivocatio enim impedit contradictionem sed Catholicus dicens idolum non est Deus, contradicit Pagano dicenti idolum est Deus. Ergo Deus utrobique sumptum univoce dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0197","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0198","text":"Praeterea, sicut idolum est Deus secundum opinionem et non secundum veritatem, ita fruitio carnalium delectationum dicitur felicitas secundum opinionem, et non secundum veritatem. Sed hoc nomen beatitudo univoce dicitur de hac beatitudine opinata, et de hac beatitudine vera. Ergo et hoc nomen Deus univoce dicitur de Deo secundum veritatem, et de Deo secundum opinionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0199","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0200","text":"Praeterea, univoca dicuntur quorum est ratio una. Sed Catholicus, cum dicit unum esse Deum, intelligit nomine Dei rem omnipotentem, et super omnia venerandam, et hoc idem intelligit gentilis, cum dicit idolum esse Deum. Ergo hoc nomen Deus univoce dicitur utrobique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0201","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0202","text":"Sed contra, illud quod est in intellectu, est similitudo eius quod est in re, ut dicitur in I Periherm. Sed animal, dictum de animali vero et de animali picto, aequivoce dicitur. Ergo hoc nomen Deus, dictum de Deo vero et de Deo secundum opinionem, aequivoce dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0203","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0204","text":"Praeterea, nullus potest significare id quod non cognoscit, sed gentilis non cognoscit naturam divinam, ergo, cum dicit idolum est Deus, non significat veram deitatem. Hanc autem significat Catholicus dicens unum esse Deum. Ergo hoc nomen Deus non dicitur univoce, sed aequivoce, de Deo vero, et de Deo secundum opinionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0205","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod hoc nomen Deus, in praemissis tribus significationibus, non accipitur neque univoce neque aequivoce, sed analogice. Quod ex hoc patet. Quia univocorum est omnino eadem ratio, aequivocorum est omnino ratio diversa, in analogicis vero, oportet quod nomen secundum unam significationem acceptum, ponatur in definitione eiusdem nominis secundum alias significationes accepti. Sicut ens de substantia dictum, ponitur in definitione entis secundum quod de accidente dicitur; et sanum dictum de animali, ponitur in definitione sani secundum quod dicitur de urina et de medicina; huius enim sani quod est in animali, urina est significativa, et medicina factiva. Sic accidit in proposito. Nam hoc nomen Deus, secundum quod pro Deo vero sumitur, in ratione Dei sumitur secundum quod dicitur Deus secundum opinionem vel participationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0207","text":"Cum enim aliquem nominamus Deum secundum participationem, intelligimus nomine Dei aliquid habens similitudinem veri Dei. Similiter cum idolum nominamus Deum, hoc nomine Deus intelligimus significari aliquid, de quo homines opinantur quod sit Deus. Et sic manifestum est quod alia et alia est significatio nominis, sed una illarum significationum clauditur in significationibus aliis. Unde manifestum est quod analogice dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0208","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0209","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod nominum multiplicitas non attenditur secundum nominis praedicationem, sed secundum significationem, hoc enim nomen homo, de quocumque praedicetur, sive vere sive false, dicitur uno modo. Sed tunc multipliciter diceretur, si per hoc nomen homo intenderemus significare diversa, puta, si unus intenderet significare per hoc nomen homo id quod vere est homo, et alius intenderet significare eodem nomine lapidem, vel aliquid aliud. Unde patet quod Catholicus dicens idolum non esse Deum, contradicit Pagano hoc asserenti, quia uterque utitur hoc nomine Deus ad significandum verum Deum. Cum enim Paganus dicit idolum esse Deum, non utitur hoc nomine secundum quod significat Deum opinabilem, sic enim verum diceret, cum etiam Catholici interdum in tali significatione hoc nomine utantur, ut cum dicitur, *omnes dii gentium Daemonia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0210","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0211","text":"Et similiter dicendum ad secundum et tertium. Nam illae rationes procedunt secundum diversitatem praedicationis nominis, et non secundum diversam significationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0212","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0213","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod animal dictum de animali vero et de picto, non dicitur pure aequivoce; sed philosophus largo modo accipit aequivoca, secundum quod includunt in se analoga. Quia et ens, quod analogice dicitur, aliquando dicitur aequivoce praedicari de diversis praedicamentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0214","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0215","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod ipsam naturam Dei prout in se est, neque Catholicus neque Paganus cognoscit, sed uterque cognoscit eam secundum aliquam rationem causalitatis vel excellentiae vel remotionis, ut supra dictum est. Et secundum hoc, in eadem significatione accipere potest gentilis hoc nomen Deus, cum dicit idolum est Deus, in qua accipit ipsum Catholicus dicens idolum non est Deus. Si vero aliquis esset qui secundum nullam rationem Deum cognosceret, nec ipsum nominaret, nisi forte sicut proferimus nomina quorum significationem ignoramus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0216","text":"## Articulus 11","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0217","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0218","text":"Ad undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen qui est non sit maxime proprium nomen Dei. Hoc enim nomen Deus est nomen incommunicabile, ut dictum est. Sed hoc nomen qui est non est nomen incommunicabile. Ergo hoc nomen qui est non est maxime proprium nomen Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0219","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0220","text":"Praeterea, Dionysius dicit, III cap. de Div. Nom., quod *boni nominatio est manifestativa omnium Dei processionum*. Sed hoc maxime Deo convenit, quod sit universale rerum principium. Ergo hoc nomen bonum est maxime proprium Dei, et non hoc nomen qui est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0221","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0222","text":"Praeterea, omne nomen divinum videtur importare relationem ad creaturas, cum Deus non cognoscatur a nobis nisi per creaturas. Sed hoc nomen qui est nullam importat habitudinem ad creaturas. Ergo hoc nomen qui est non est maxime proprium nomen Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0223","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0224","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Exod. III, quod Moysi quaerenti, *si dixerint mihi, quod est nomen eius? Quid dicam eis?* Et respondit ei dominus, *sic dices eis, qui est misit me ad vos*. Ergo hoc nomen qui est est maxime proprium nomen Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0225","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod hoc nomen qui est triplici ratione est maxime proprium nomen Dei. Primo quidem, propter sui significationem. Non enim significat formam aliquam, sed ipsum esse. Unde, cum esse Dei sit ipsa eius essentia, et hoc nulli alii conveniat, ut supra ostensum est, manifestum est quod inter alia nomina hoc maxime proprie nominat Deum, unumquodque enim denominatur a sua forma. Secundo, propter eius universalitatem. Omnia enim alia nomina vel sunt minus communia; vel, si convertantur cum ipso, tamen addunt aliqua supra ipsum secundum rationem; unde quodammodo informant et determinant ipsum. Intellectus autem noster non potest ipsam Dei essentiam cognoscere in statu viae, secundum quod in se est, sed quemcumque modum determinet circa id quod de Deo intelligit, deficit a modo quo Deus in se est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","text":"Et ideo, quanto aliqua nomina sunt minus determinata, et magis communia et absoluta, tanto magis proprie dicuntur de Deo a nobis. Unde et Damascenus dicit quod *principalius omnibus quae de Deo dicuntur nominibus, est qui est, totum enim in seipso comprehendens, habet ipsum esse velut quoddam pelagus substantiae infinitum et indeterminatum*. Quolibet enim alio nomine determinatur aliquis modus substantiae rei, sed hoc nomen qui est nullum modum essendi determinat, sed se habet indeterminate ad omnes; et ideo nominat ipsum *pelagus substantiae infinitum*. Tertio vero, ex eius consignificatione. Significat enim esse in praesenti, et hoc maxime proprie de Deo dicitur, cuius esse non novit praeteritum vel futurum, ut dicit Augustinus in V de Trin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0228","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0229","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc nomen qui est est magis proprium nomen Dei quam hoc nomen Deus, quantum ad id a quo imponitur, scilicet ab esse, et quantum ad modum significandi et consignificandi, ut dictum est. Sed quantum ad id ad quod imponitur nomen ad significandum, est magis proprium hoc nomen Deus, quod imponitur ad significandum naturam divinam. Et adhuc magis proprium nomen est tetragrammaton, quod est impositum ad significandam ipsam Dei substantiam incommunicabilem, et, ut sic liceat loqui, singularem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0230","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0231","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod hoc nomen bonum est principale nomen Dei inquantum est causa, non tamen simpliciter, nam esse absolute praeintelligitur causae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0232","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0233","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non est necessarium quod omnia nomina divina importent habitudinem ad creaturas; sed sufficit quod imponantur ab aliquibus perfectionibus procedentibus a Deo in creaturas. Inter quas prima est ipsum esse, a qua sumitur hoc nomen qui est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0234","text":"## Articulus 12","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0235","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0236","text":"Ad duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod propositiones affirmativae non possunt formari de Deo. Dicit enim Dionysius, II cap. Cael. Hier., quod *negationes de Deo sunt verae, affirmationes autem incompactae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0237","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0238","text":"Praeterea, Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin., quod *forma simplex subiectum esse non potest*. Sed Deus maxime est forma simplex, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo non potest esse subiectum. Sed omne illud de quo propositio affirmativa formatur, accipitur ut subiectum. Ergo de Deo propositio affirmativa formari non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0239","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0240","text":"Praeterea, omnis intellectus intelligens rem aliter quam sit, est falsus. Sed Deus habet esse absque omni compositione, ut supra probatum est. Cum igitur omnis intellectus affirmativus intelligat aliquid cum compositione, videtur quod propositio affirmativa vere de Deo formari non possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0241","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0242","text":"Sed contra est quod fidei non subest falsum. Sed propositiones quaedam affirmativae subduntur fidei, utpote quod Deus est trinus et unus, et quod est omnipotens. Ergo propositiones affirmativae possunt vere formari de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0243","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod propositiones affirmativae possunt vere formari de Deo. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod in qualibet propositione affirmativa vera, oportet quod praedicatum et subiectum significent idem secundum rem aliquo modo, et diversum secundum rationem. Et hoc patet tam in propositionibus quae sunt de praedicato accidentali, quam in illis quae sunt de praedicato substantiali. Manifestum est enim quod homo et albus sunt idem subiecto, et differunt ratione, alia enim est ratio hominis, et alia ratio albi. Et similiter cum dico homo est animal, illud enim ipsum quod est homo, vere animal est; in eodem enim supposito est et natura sensibilis, a qua dicitur animal, et rationalis, a qua dicitur homo. Unde hic etiam praedicatum et subiectum sunt idem supposito, sed diversa ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","text":"Sed et in propositionibus in quibus idem praedicatur de seipso, hoc aliquo modo invenitur; inquantum intellectus id quod ponit ex parte subiecti, trahit ad partem suppositi, quod vero ponit ex parte praedicati, trahit ad naturam formae in supposito existentis, secundum quod dicitur quod praedicata tenentur formaliter, et subiecta materialiter. Huic vero diversitati quae est secundum rationem, respondet pluralitas praedicati et subiecti, identitatem vero rei significat intellectus per ipsam compositionem. Deus autem, in se consideratus, est omnino unus et simplex, sed tamen intellectus noster secundum diversas conceptiones ipsum cognoscit, eo quod non potest ipsum ut in seipso est, videre. Sed tamen, quamvis intelligat ipsum sub diversis conceptionibus, cognoscit tamen quod omnibus suis conceptionibus respondet una et eadem res simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","text":"Hanc ergo pluralitatem quae est secundum rationem, repraesentat per pluralitatem praedicati et subiecti, unitatem vero repraesentat intellectus per compositionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0247","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0248","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius dicit affirmationes de Deo esse incompactas, vel inconvenientes secundum aliam translationem, inquantum nullum nomen Deo competit secundum modum significandi, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0249","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0250","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus noster non potest formas simplices subsistentes secundum quod in seipsis sunt, apprehendere, sed apprehendit eas secundum modum compositorum, in quibus est aliquid quod subiicitur, et est aliquid quod inest. Et ideo apprehendit formam simplicem in ratione subiecti, et attribuit ei aliquid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0251","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0252","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod haec propositio, intellectus intelligens rem aliter quam sit, est falsus, est duplex, ex eo quod hoc adverbium aliter potest determinare hoc verbum intelligit ex parte intellecti, vel ex parte intelligentis. Si ex parte intellecti, sic propositio vera est, et est sensus, quicumque intellectus intelligit rem esse aliter quam sit, falsus est. Sed hoc non habet locum in proposito, quia intellectus noster, formans propositionem de Deo, non dicit eum esse compositum, sed simplicem. Si vero ex parte intelligentis, sic propositio falsa est. Alius est enim modus intellectus in intelligendo, quam rei in essendo. Manifestum est enim quod intellectus noster res materiales infra se existentes intelligit immaterialiter; non quod intelligat eas esse immateriales, sed habet modum immaterialem in intelligendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","text":"Et similiter, cum intelligit simplicia quae sunt supra se, intelligit ea secundum modum suum, scilicet composite, non tamen ita quod intelligat ea esse composita. Et sic intellectus noster non est falsus, formans compositionem de Deo.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bc9a282cbb4000f9851da33a9f735c6e30c1e8927324449934b268f7cb4f1843","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T134927778198Z_71e676b6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 13","source_sha256":"bc9a282cbb4000f9851da33a9f735c6e30c1e8927324449934b268f7cb4f1843","sha256":"45a086399ca75cac8f450846afa45debc2f93bbcefae0d9f4ca9d6454dd1a1ab","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:49:27.778216+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0002","text":"[I q. 13 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003","text":"Having considered those matters that pertain to divine knowledge, we must proceed to the consideration of the divine names; for each thing is named by us according as we know it. Concerning this, then, twelve questions are asked. First, whether God can be named by us. Second, whether any names said of God are predicated of Him substantially. Third, whether any names said of God are properly said of Him, or whether all are attributed to Him metaphorically. Fourth, whether the many names said of God are synonymous. Fifth, whether any names are said of God and creatures univocally or equivocally. Sixth, granted that they are said analogically, whether they are said primarily of God or of creatures. Seventh, whether any names are said of God temporally. Eighth, whether this name God is a name of nature or of operation. Ninth, whether this name God is a communicable name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004","text":"Tenth, whether it is taken univocally or equivocally, according as it signifies God by nature, by participation, and according to opinion. Eleventh, whether this name, *He Who Is*, is the most proper name of God. Twelfth, whether affirmative propositions can be formed concerning God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0006","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0007","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that no name befits God. For Dionysius says, in chapter 1 of *On the Divine Names*, that neither is there a name of Him nor an opinion. And Proverbs 30 says: *What is His name, and what is the name of His Son, if thou knowest?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0008","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0009","text":"Further, every name is said either in the abstract or in the concrete. But names signifying in the concrete do not befit God, since He is simple; nor do names signifying in the abstract, because they do not signify something perfect and subsisting. Therefore no name can be said of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0010","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0011","text":"Further, names signify substance together with quality; but verbs and participles signify together with time; while pronouns signify together with demonstration or relation. None of these befits God, because He is without quality and without every accident, and without time; and He cannot be sensed, so as to be able to be demonstrated; nor can He be signified relatively, since relatives are recollective of certain things previously said—either names, participles, or demonstrative pronouns. Therefore God can in no way be named by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0012","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0013","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Exodus 15: *The Lord [is] as a man of war; Almighty [is] His name.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0014","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","text":"I answer that, according to the Philosopher, spoken sounds are signs of concepts, and concepts are likenesses of things. Thus it is clear that spoken sounds are referred to the things signified through the mediation of the conception of the intellect. Therefore, according as something can be known by us intellectually, so it can be named by us. Now it was shown above that God cannot be seen by us in this life through His essence; but He is known by us from creatures, according to the relation of principle, and by way of excellence and removal. Thus, therefore, He can be named by us from creatures, yet not such that the name signifying Him expresses the divine essence according to what it is, as this name *man* expresses by its signification the essence of man according to what it is; for it signifies his definition, which declares his essence, since the account signified by the name is the definition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0016","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God is said for this reason not to have a name, or to be above naming, because His essence is above that which we understand and signify in speech concerning God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0018","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0019","text":"To the second it must be said that, because we come to the knowledge of God from creatures, and name Him from them, the names that we attribute to God signify in this manner, according as befits material creatures, knowledge of which is connatural to us, as was said above. And because in such creatures those things that are perfect and subsisting are composite, whereas form in them is not something complete and subsisting, but rather that whereby something is, it follows that all names imposed by us to signify something complete and subsisting signify concretely, as befits composites; but those imposed to signify simple forms signify something not as subsisting, but as that whereby something is, just as whiteness signifies that whereby something is white.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0020","text":"Since, therefore, God is both simple and subsisting, we attribute to Him abstract names, to signify His simplicity, and concrete names, to signify His subsistence and perfection; although both kinds of names fall short of His mode, just as our intellect does not know Him as He is, in this life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0021","text":"[I q. 13 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0022","text":"To the third it must be said that to signify substance together with quality is to signify a supposit together with a determinate nature or form in which it subsists. Hence, just as certain things are said of God concretely, to signify His subsistence and perfection, as has already been said, so also names signifying substance together with quality are said of God. But verbs and participles connoting time are said of Him on account of the fact that eternity includes all time; for just as we cannot apprehend and signify simple subsistents except in the manner of composites, so we cannot understand or express in speech simple eternity except in the manner of temporal things; and this is because of the connaturality of our intellect with composite and temporal things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0023","text":"But demonstrative pronouns are said of God according as they make a demonstration of that which is understood, not of that which is sensed; for according as He is understood by us, according to this He falls under demonstration. And thus, according to that mode in which names, participles, and demonstrative pronouns are said of God, according to this He can also be signified by relative pronouns.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0025","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second point. It seems that no name is said of God substantially. For Damascene says, *each of those things that are said of God must signify not what He is according to substance, but show what He is not, or some relation, or something among those things that follow upon nature or operation*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0027","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0028","text":"Moreover, Dionysius says, in chapter 1 of *On the Divine Names*, *you will find every hymn of the holy theologians dividing the names of God, manifestatively and laudatively, according to the beneficent processions of the Godhead*; and the sense is that the names which holy doctors employ in divine praise are distinguished according to the processions of God Himself. But what signifies the procession of a thing signifies nothing pertaining to its essence. Therefore names said of God are not said of Him substantially.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0029","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0030","text":"Moreover, a thing is named by us according as it is understood. But in this life God is not understood by us according to His substance. Therefore neither is any name imposed by us said of God according to His substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0031","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0032","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in VI *On the Trinity*, *for God, to be is to be strong or to be wise, and whatever you may say of that simplicity by which His substance is signified*. Therefore all names of this sort signify the divine substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0033","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0034","text":"I answer that, concerning names that are said of God negatively, or that signify His relation to a creature, it is evident that they in no way signify His substance, but the removal of something from Him, or His relation to another, or rather another's relation to Him. But concerning names that are said of God absolutely and affirmatively, such as good, wise, and the like, some have held various opinions. For some have said that all these names, although they are said affirmatively of God, were nevertheless devised more in order to remove something from God than to posit something in Him. Hence they say that, when we say that God is living, we signify that God is not in the manner in which inanimate things are; and the same is to be understood in other cases. And Rabbi Moses held this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0035","text":"Others, however, say that these names were imposed to signify His relation to creatures; so that, when we say God is good, the sense is: God is the cause of goodness in things. And the same reasoning applies to the others. But each of these views seems unsuitable, for three reasons. First, because according to neither of these positions could a reason be assigned why certain names should be said of God more than others. For He is as much the cause of bodies as He is the cause of good things. Hence, if nothing else is signified when it is said that God is good except that God is the cause of good things, it could likewise be said that God is a body, because He is the cause of bodies. Likewise, by saying that He is a body, it is removed that He should be being only in potency, as prime matter is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","text":"Second, because it would follow that all names said of God would be said of Him secondarily, just as healthy is said secondarily of medicine, because it signifies only that it is the cause of health in the animal, which is called healthy primarily. Third, because this is contrary to the intention of those speaking of God. For when they call God living, they intend to say something other than that He is the cause of our life, or that He differs from inanimate bodies. And therefore it must be said otherwise: namely, that names of this kind signify the divine substance and are predicated of God substantially, but fall short of representing Him. This is evident as follows. For names thus signify God according as our intellect knows Him. But since our intellect knows God from creatures, it knows Him according as creatures represent Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","text":"Now it was shown above that God prepossesses in Himself all the perfections of creatures, as being perfect simply and universally. Hence every creature represents Him and is like Him insofar as it has some perfection; yet not in such a way that it represents Him as something of the same species or genus, but as an excelling principle, from whose form effects fall short, though effects attain some likeness of it; just as the forms of lower bodies represent the power of the sun. And this was explained above, when divine perfection was under discussion. Thus, therefore, the aforesaid names signify the divine substance, though imperfectly, just as creatures also represent it imperfectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0038","text":"Therefore, when it is said that God is good, the sense is not: God is the cause of goodness, or God is not evil; but the sense is: that which we call goodness in creatures pre-exists in God, and this according to a higher mode. Hence it does not follow from this that it belongs to God to be good insofar as He causes goodness; but rather conversely, because He is good, He diffuses goodness to things, according to Augustine's saying in *On Christian Doctrine*: *insofar as He is good, we are*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0039","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Damascene says that these names do not signify what God is because none of these names expresses what God is perfectly, but each signifies Him imperfectly, just as creatures also represent Him imperfectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0041","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","text":"To the second, it must be said that in the signification of names, that from which a name is imposed for signifying is sometimes distinct from that for signifying which the name is imposed; as this name, stone, is imposed from that which injures the foot, yet it is not imposed in order to signify that which injures the foot, but to signify a certain species of bodies. Otherwise, everything that injures the foot would be a stone. Thus, therefore, it must be said that divine names of this kind are indeed imposed from the processions of the Godhead. For just as, according to the diverse processions of perfections, creatures represent God, though imperfectly, so our intellect, according to each procession, knows and names God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0043","text":"Yet it does not impose these names in order to signify the processions themselves, so that, when it is said that God is living, the sense should be: life proceeds from Him; but in order to signify the very principle of things, insofar as life pre-exists in Him, although in a more eminent manner than is understood or signified.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0044","text":"[I q. 13 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0045","text":"To the third, it must be said that we cannot know the essence of God in this life according as it is in itself, but we know it according as it is represented in the perfections of creatures. And thus the names imposed by us signify it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0046","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0047","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0048","text":"We proceed thus to the third point. It seems that no name is said of God properly. For all the names that we say of God are taken from creatures, as has been said. But names of creatures are said of God metaphorically, as when it is said that God is a stone, or a lion, or something of this sort. Therefore all names said of God are said metaphorically.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0049","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0050","text":"Moreover, no name is properly said of something from which it is more truly removed than predicated of it. But all such names—good, wise, and the like—are more truly removed from God than predicated of Him, as is clear from Dionysius, *On the Celestial Hierarchy*, ch. 2. Therefore none of these names is properly said of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0051","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0052","text":"Moreover, names of bodies are not said of God except metaphorically, since He is incorporeal. But all such names imply certain bodily conditions, for they signify with time, and with composition, and with other such things, which are conditions of bodies. Therefore all such names are said of God metaphorically.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0053","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0054","text":"On the contrary, Ambrose says, in Book II *On the Faith*: *there are certain names which evidently show the propriety of divinity; and certain others which express the manifest truth of the divine majesty; while others are said of God figuratively through likeness*. Therefore not all names are said of God metaphorically, but some are said properly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0055","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","text":"I answer that, as has been said, we know God from the perfections proceeding from Him into creatures; and these perfections are in God according to a more eminent mode than in creatures. But our intellect apprehends them according as they are in creatures, and, according as it apprehends them, so it signifies them by names. Therefore, in the names which we attribute to God, two things are to be considered: namely, the perfections themselves signified, such as goodness, life, and the like; and the mode of signifying. Accordingly, as regards that which such names signify, they properly belong to God, and more properly than to creatures themselves, and are said of Him primarily. But as regards the mode of signifying, they are not properly said of God, for they have a mode of signifying that belongs to creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0057","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0058","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that certain names signify such perfections proceeding from God into created things in such a way that the imperfect mode by which the divine perfection is participated by a creature is included in the signification of the name itself; as “stone” signifies something existing materially. Such names cannot be attributed to God except metaphorically. But certain names signify the perfections themselves absolutely, without any mode of participation being enclosed in their signification, such as “being,” “good,” “living,” and the like; and such names are properly said of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0059","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0060","text":"To the second, it must be said that Dionysius says that such names are denied of God because that which is signified by the name does not belong to Him in the mode in which the name signifies it, but in a more excellent mode. Hence Dionysius says in the same place that God is above every substance and life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0061","text":"[I q. 13 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0062","text":"To the third, it must be said that those names which are properly said of God imply bodily conditions, not in the thing signified by the name itself, but as regards the mode of signifying. But those which are said of God metaphorically imply a bodily condition in their very signification.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0063","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0064","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0065","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that these names said of God are synonymous names. For synonymous names are said to be those which signify altogether the same thing. But these names said of God signify altogether the same thing in God, because the goodness of God is His essence, and likewise His wisdom. Therefore these names are altogether synonymous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0066","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0067","text":"If it be said that these names signify the same thing in reality, but according to diverse notions, on the contrary: a notion to which nothing in reality corresponds is vain. Therefore, if these notions are many and the thing is one, it seems that these notions are vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0068","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0069","text":"Moreover, that which is one in reality and notion is more one than that which is one in reality and multiple in notion. But God is supremely one. Therefore it seems that He is not one in reality and multiple in notion. And thus the names said of God do not signify diverse notions, and so are synonymous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0070","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0071","text":"On the contrary, all synonyms, when joined to one another, produce tautology, as when one says “garment clothing.” Therefore, if all names said of God are synonymous, it could not fittingly be said, “God is good,” or anything of this sort; although it is written in Jeremiah 32: *Most strong, great, mighty, Lord of hosts is thy name*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0072","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","text":"I answer that it must be said that names of this kind, said of God, are not synonymous. This indeed would be easy to see if we were to say that names of this kind were introduced in order to remove [something], or to designate the relation of a cause with respect to creatures; for thus the accounts of these names would be diverse according to the diverse things negated, or according to the diverse effects connoted. But according as the aforesaid names signify the divine substance, albeit imperfectly, it is also plainly apparent, according to what has been set forth, that they have diverse accounts. For the account which a name signifies is the conception of the intellect concerning the thing signified by the name. But since our intellect knows God from creatures, it forms, in order to understand God, conceptions proportioned to the perfections proceeding from God into creatures. These perfections indeed pre-exist in God unitedly and simply, whereas in creatures they are received dividedly and multiply.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","text":"Therefore, just as one simple principle corresponds to the diverse perfections of creatures, represented variously and multiply through the diverse perfections of creatures, so one altogether simple [thing], imperfectly understood according to such conceptions, corresponds to the various and multiple conceptions of our intellect. And therefore the names attributed to God, although they signify one thing, nevertheless, because they signify it under many and diverse accounts, are not synonymous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0075","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0076","text":"And thus the solution to the first is clear, because synonymous names are said to be those which signify one thing according to one account. For those which signify diverse accounts of one thing do not signify one thing primarily and per se, because a name does not signify a thing except through the mediation of a conception of the intellect, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0077","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0078","text":"To the second, it must be said that the many accounts of these names are not empty and vain, because one simple something corresponds to them all, represented through all such [accounts] multiply and imperfectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0079","text":"[I q. 13 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0080","text":"To the third, it must be said that this very point belongs to the perfect unity of God: that those things which are multiply and dividedly in others are in Him simply and unitedly. And from this it follows that He is one in reality and many according to account, because our intellect apprehends Him as multiply as things represent Him multiply.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0081","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0082","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0083","text":"We proceed to the fifth thus. It seems that those things which are said of God and creatures are said of them univocally. For every equivocal [term] is reduced to a univocal [term], as many [things] to one. For if this name “dog” is said equivocally of the barking [dog] and the sea-[dog], it must be said univocally of certain things, namely, of all barking [dogs]; otherwise there would be an infinite regress. Now some univocal agents are found, which agree with their effects in name and definition, as man generates man; but certain agents are equivocal, as the sun causes something hot, although it itself is not hot except equivocally. Therefore it seems that the first agent, to which all agents are reduced, is a univocal agent. And thus, those things which are said of God and creatures are predicated univocally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0084","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0085","text":"Moreover, no likeness is considered according to equivocal things. Therefore, since there is some likeness of creatures to God, according to that in Genesis 1, *let us make man in our image and likeness*, it seems that something is said univocally of God and creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0086","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0087","text":"Moreover, a measure is homogeneous with what is measured, as is said in Metaphysics X. But God is the first measure of all beings, as is said in the same place. Therefore God is homogeneous with creatures. And thus something can be said univocally of God and creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0088","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0089","text":"But on the contrary, whatever is predicated of certain things according to the same name and not according to the same account is predicated of them equivocally. But no name befits God according to that account according to which it is said of a creature; for wisdom in creatures is a quality, but not in God; moreover, a varied genus changes the account, since it is a part of the definition. And the same reasoning applies in other cases. Therefore whatever is said of God and creatures is said equivocally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0090","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0091","text":"Moreover, God is more distant from creatures than any creatures are from one another. But owing to the distance of certain creatures, it happens that nothing can be predicated of them univocally; as with those which do not agree in any genus. Therefore much less is anything predicated univocally of God and creatures, but all things are predicated equivocally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0092","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible for anything to be predicated univocally of God and creatures. For every effect not equaling the power of the agent-cause receives the likeness of the agent not according to the same account, but deficiently; so that what is dividedly and multiply in effects is in the cause simply and in the same manner. Thus the sun, by one power, produces manifold and diverse forms in these lower things. In the same way, as was said above, all the perfections of things which are in created things dividedly and multiply pre-exist in God unitedly. Thus, when some name pertaining to perfection is said of a creature, it signifies that perfection as distinct, according to the account of its definition, from others; for example, when this name “wise” is said of a man, we signify a certain perfection distinct from the essence of man, and from his power and from his being, and from all such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","text":"But when we say this name of God, we do not intend to signify something distinct from His essence or power or being. And thus, when this name “wise” is said of a man, it in a certain manner circumscribes and comprehends the thing signified; but not when it is said of God, rather it leaves the thing signified as incomprehended and exceeding the signification of the name. Hence it is clear that this name “wise” is not said according to the same account of God and of man. And the same account holds concerning the others. Hence no name is predicated univocally of God and creatures. But neither [is it predicated] purely equivocally, as some have said. For according to this, nothing could be known or demonstrated about God from creatures; but the fallacy of equivocation would always arise. And this is both contrary to the philosophers, who demonstrate many things about God, and to the Apostle saying, Romans 1, *the invisible things of God, understood through those things which have been made, are clearly seen*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","text":"Therefore it must be said that names of this kind are said of God and creatures according to analogy, that is, proportion. This occurs in two ways in names: either because many things have a proportion to one thing, as “healthy” is said of medicine and urine, insofar as each has an order and proportion to the health of the animal—of which the latter is a sign, but the former a cause; or because one has a proportion to another, as “healthy” is said of medicine and animal, insofar as medicine is the cause of the health that is in the animal. And in this way certain things are said analogically of God and creatures, and not purely equivocally, nor univocally. For we can name God only from creatures, as was said above. And thus, whatever is said of God and creatures is said according as there is some order of the creature to God, as to a principle and cause, in which all the perfections of things pre-exist excellently.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","text":"And this mode of community is intermediate between pure equivocation and simple univocation. For in those things which are said analogically, there is not one account, as there is in univocal things; nor [are the accounts] wholly diverse, as in equivocal things; rather, a name which is so said in many ways signifies diverse proportions to one thing. Thus “healthy,” said of urine, signifies a sign of the health of the animal, whereas, said of medicine, it signifies the cause of that same health.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0097","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0098","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although in predications equivocal terms must be reduced to univocal ones, nevertheless in actions a non-univocal agent necessarily precedes a univocal agent. For a non-univocal agent is the universal cause of the whole species, as the sun is the cause of the generation of all human beings. But a univocal agent is not the universal efficient cause of the whole species (otherwise it would be the cause of itself, since it is contained under the species), but is a particular cause with respect to this individual, which it constitutes in participation in the species. Therefore, the universal cause of the whole species is not a univocal agent. But a universal cause is prior to a particular one. Now this universal agent, although it is not univocal, is nevertheless not wholly equivocal, because in that case it would not produce something like itself; but it can be called an analogical agent, just as in predications all univocals are reduced to one first, not univocal, but analogical, namely being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0099","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0100","text":"To the second, it must be said that the creature's likeness to God is imperfect, because it does not even represent the same thing according to genus, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0101","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102","text":"To the third, it must be said that God is not a measure proportioned to the things measured. Hence it is not necessary that God and creatures be contained under one genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0103","text":"[I q. 13 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0104","text":"But those things which are to the contrary conclude that names of this sort are not predicated univocally of God and creatures, but not that they are predicated equivocally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0105","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0106","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0107","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that names are said primarily of creatures rather than of God. For according as we know something, so we name it; since names, according to the Philosopher, are signs of concepts. But we know a creature before God. Therefore, names imposed by us belong primarily to creatures rather than to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0108","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0109","text":"Further, according to Dionysius, in the book *On the Divine Names*, we name God from creatures. But names transferred from creatures to God are said primarily of creatures rather than of God; as lion, stone, and the like. Therefore all names which are said of God and creatures are said primarily of creatures rather than of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0110","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0111","text":"Further, all names which are said commonly of God and creatures are said of God as of the cause of all things, as Dionysius says. But what is said of something through a cause is said of it secondarily; for healthy is said primarily of an animal rather than of medicine, which is the cause of health. Therefore names of this sort are said primarily of creatures rather than of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0112","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0113","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Eph. III, *I bend my knees to the Father of our Lord Jesus, from whom all fatherhood in heaven and on earth is named*. And the same reasoning seems to apply to the other names which are said of God and creatures. Therefore names of this sort are said primarily of God rather than of creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0114","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0115","text":"I answer that it must be said that in all names which are said analogically of several things, it is necessary that they all be said in reference to one thing, and therefore that one thing must be placed in the definition of all. And because the account which a name signifies is a definition, as is said in *Metaphysics* IV, it is necessary that that name be said primarily of that which is placed in the definition of the others, and secondarily of the others, according to the order in which they approach that first more or less. Thus healthy, which is said of an animal, falls into the definition of healthy which is said of medicine, which is called healthy insofar as it causes health in an animal; and into the definition of healthy which is said of urine, which is called healthy insofar as it is a sign of an animal's health.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0116","text":"Thus, therefore, all names which are said metaphorically of God are said primarily of creatures rather than of God, because, when said of God, they signify nothing other than likenesses to such creatures. For just as laughing, said of a meadow, signifies nothing other than that the meadow is similarly disposed in beauty when it flowers, as a human being is when he laughs, according to a likeness of proportion; so the name lion, said of God, signifies nothing other than that God is similarly disposed to act mightily in his works, as a lion is in its own. And thus it is clear that, insofar as they are said of God, their signification cannot be defined except through that which is said of creatures. But concerning the other names, which are not said metaphorically of God, the same reasoning would also hold if they were said of God only causally, as some have held. Thus indeed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","text":"When it is said, God is good, it would signify nothing other than that God is the cause of the goodness of the creature, and thus this name good, said of God, would include in its intelligibility the goodness of the creature. Hence good would be said primarily of the creature rather than of God. But it was shown above that names of this sort are not said of God only causally, but also essentially. For when it is said that God is good, or wise, not only is it signified that he is the cause of wisdom or goodness, but that these pre-exist in him more eminently. Hence, according to this, it must be said that, as to the thing signified by the name, they are said primarily of God rather than of creatures, because such perfections flow from God into creatures. But as to the imposition of the name, they are imposed by us primarily on creatures, which we know first. Hence they also have a mode of signifying that befits creatures, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0118","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0119","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that objection proceeds as regards the imposition of the name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0120","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0121","text":"To the second, it must be said that the account is not the same concerning names that are said of God metaphorically, and concerning the others, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0122","text":"[I q. 13 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0123","text":"To the third, it must be said that that objection would proceed if names of this sort were said of God causally only and not essentially, as “healthy” is said of medicine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0124","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0125","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0126","text":"The seventh is approached thus. It seems that names which imply a relation to creatures are not said of God in time. For all names of this kind signify the divine substance, as is commonly said. Hence Ambrose also says that this name “Lord” is a name of power, which is the divine substance; and “Creator” signifies God's action, which is His essence. But the divine substance is not temporal, but eternal. Therefore names of this kind are not said of God in time, but from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0127","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0128","text":"Further, whatever belongs to something in time can be said to have been made; for what is white in time becomes white. But being made does not belong to God. Therefore nothing is predicated of God in time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0129","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0130","text":"Further, if certain names are said of God in time because they imply a relation to creatures, the same account seems to hold for all things that imply a relation to creatures. But certain names implying a relation to creatures are said of God from eternity; for from eternity He knew and loved the creature, according to that in Jer. XXXI, *I have loved you with everlasting charity*. Therefore the other names too which imply a relation to creatures, such as “Lord” and “Creator,” are said of God from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0131","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0132","text":"Further, names of this kind signify a relation. Therefore that relation must either be something in God, or in the creature alone. But it cannot be that it is in the creature alone, because then God would be denominated “Lord” from the opposite relation, which is in creatures; but nothing is denominated from its opposite. It remains, therefore, that the relation is also something in God. But nothing can be in God in time, since He is above time. Therefore it seems that names of this kind are not said of God in time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0133","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0134","text":"Further, something is called relative according to a relation, for example, “lord” according to lordship, just as “white” according to whiteness. Therefore, if the relation of lordship is not in God in reality but only in reason, it follows that God is not really Lord, which is evidently false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0135","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0136","text":"Further, among relatives that are not simultaneous by nature, one can exist while the other does not exist; thus the knowable exists when knowledge does not exist, as is said in the Categories. But the relatives that are said of God and creatures are not simultaneous by nature. Therefore something can be said relatively of God in relation to a creature even when the creature does not exist. And thus names of this kind, “Lord” and “Creator,” are said of God from eternity, and not in time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0137","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0138","text":"But on the contrary is what Augustine says, in V On the Trinity, that this relative appellation “Lord” belongs to God in time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0139","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0140","text":"I answer that it must be said that certain names implying a relation to a creature are said of God in time, and not from eternity. For the clarification of this, it must be known that some have held that relation is not a thing of nature, but of reason only. This indeed appears to be false from the fact that natural things themselves have an order and disposition toward one another. Nevertheless, it must be known that, since relation requires two termini, it can stand in a threefold way with respect to its being a thing of nature and of reason. For sometimes it is a thing of reason only on both sides, namely, when an order or disposition cannot exist among certain things except according to the apprehension of reason alone, as when we say that the same is the same as the same. For insofar as reason apprehends one thing twice, it sets it down as two; and thus it apprehends a certain disposition of it toward itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0141","text":"And it is similar concerning all relations that are between being and non-being; which reason forms insofar as it apprehends non-being as a certain terminus. And the same holds for all relations that follow the act of reason, such as genus and species, and the like. But certain relations are things of nature with respect to both termini, namely, when there is a disposition between two things according to something really belonging to each. This is clear in all relations that follow quantity, such as large and small, double and half, and the like; for quantity is in both termini. And it is similar concerning relations that follow action and passion, such as mover and movable, father and son, and the like. But sometimes relation in one of the termini is a thing of nature, and in the other is a thing of reason only. And this occurs whenever the two termini are not of one order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0142","text":"Thus sense and knowledge are related to the sensible and the knowable, which, insofar as they are certain things existing in natural being, are outside the order of sensible and intelligible being; and therefore in knowledge and sense there is a real relation, insofar as they are ordered to knowing or sensing things. But the things themselves, considered in themselves, are outside the order of this kind. Hence in them there is no relation really to knowledge and sense, but only according to reason, insofar as the intellect apprehends them as termini of the relations of knowledge and sense. Hence the Philosopher says, in V Metaphysics, that they are not called relative because they themselves are referred to other things, but because other things are referred to them. And similarly, “right” is not said of a column except insofar as it is placed to the right of an animal; hence such a relation is not really in the column, but in the animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","text":"Therefore, since God is outside the whole order of creatures, and all creatures are ordered to Him, and not conversely, it is manifest that creatures are really referred to God Himself; but in God there is no real relation of Himself to creatures, but only according to reason, insofar as creatures are referred to Him. And thus nothing prevents names of this kind, implying a relation to a creature, from being predicated of God in time, not on account of any change in Him, but on account of a change in the creature; just as a column becomes right-hand to an animal, with no change occurring in it, but the animal having been moved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0144","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0145","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that certain relatives are imposed to signify the relative relations themselves, as master, servant, father, and son, and the like; and these are called relatives according to being. But some are imposed to signify things which certain relations follow, such as mover and moved, head and headed, and other such things, which are called relatives according to being said. Thus, therefore, this difference must also be considered concerning the divine names. For some signify the relation itself to a creature, as master. And things of this sort do not signify the divine substance directly, but indirectly, insofar as they presuppose it, just as lordship presupposes power, which is the divine substance. But others directly signify the divine essence, and consequently convey a relation; as savior, creator, and the like, signify God's action, which is His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0146","text":"Nevertheless, both kinds of names are said of God temporally with respect to the relation which they convey, whether principally or consequently, but not with respect to the fact that they signify the essence, whether directly or indirectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0147","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0148","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as relations which are said of God temporally are in God only according to reason, so neither becoming nor having been made is said of God except according to reason, with no change occurring in Him; as in this: *Lord, you have become a refuge for us*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0149","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0150","text":"To the third, it must be said that the operation of intellect and will is in the operator; and therefore names which signify relations consequent upon the action of intellect or will are said of God from eternity. But those which follow actions proceeding, according to the mode of understanding, to external effects, are said of God temporally, as savior, creator, and the like.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0151","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0152","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the relations signified by names of this sort which are said of God temporally are in God according to reason alone; but the opposite relations in creatures are according to reality. Nor is it unfitting that God be denominated from relations really existing in the thing, yet insofar as opposite relations in God are co-understood by our intellect. Thus God is said relatively to the creature because the creature is referred to Him, just as the Philosopher says in Metaphysics V that the knowable is called relative because knowledge is referred to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0153","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0154","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, since God is related to the creature by that very account by which the creature is related to Him, since the relation of subjection is really in the creature, it follows that God is lord not merely according to reason, but really. For He is called lord in the manner in which the creature is subject to Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0155","text":"[I q. 13 a. 7 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0156","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that, in order to know whether relatives are simultaneous by nature or not, one ought not to consider the order of the things of which relatives are said, but the significations of the relatives themselves. For if one includes the other in its intelligibility, and conversely, then they are simultaneous by nature, as double and half, father and son, and the like. But if one includes the other in its intelligibility, and not conversely, then they are not simultaneous by nature. And knowledge and the knowable are related in this way. For the knowable is said according to potency, but knowledge according to habit or according to act. Hence the knowable, according to the mode of its signification, pre-exists knowledge. But if the knowable is taken according to act, then it is simultaneous with knowledge according to act; for something known is not anything unless there is knowledge of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0157","text":"Therefore, although God is prior to creatures, nevertheless, because the signification of lord includes that He have a servant, and conversely, these two relatives, lord and servant, are simultaneous by nature. Hence God was not lord before He had a creature subject to Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0158","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0159","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0160","text":"The eighth is approached thus. It seems that this name God is not a name of nature. For Damascene says, in Book I, that *God is called from theein*, which is to run, and to cherish all things; *or from aethein, that is, to burn (for our God is a consuming fire of all malice); or from theasthai*, which is to behold, all things. But all these pertain to operation. Therefore this name God signifies operation, and not nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0161","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0162","text":"Further, a thing is named by us according as it is known. But the divine nature is unknown to us. Therefore this name God does not signify the divine nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0163","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0164","text":"But on the contrary, Ambrose says, in Book I On Faith, that God is a name of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0165","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0166","text":"I answer that it must be said that that from which a name is imposed for signifying is not always the same as that which the name is imposed to signify. For just as we know the substance of a thing through its properties or operations, so we sometimes denominate the substance of a thing from some operation or property of it; just as we denominate the substance of a stone from some action of it, because it injures the foot. Yet this name is not imposed to signify this action, but the substance of the stone. But if there are things which are known to us in themselves, such as heat, cold, whiteness, and the like, they are not denominated from other things. Hence, in such things, what the name signifies and that from which the name is imposed for signifying are the same. Therefore, because God is not known to us in His nature, but becomes known to us through His operations or effects, we can name Him from these, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","text":"Hence this name God is a name of operation, with respect to that from which it is imposed for signifying. For this name is imposed from the universal providence of things; since all who speak of God intend by this to name God as that which has universal providence over things. Hence Dionysius says, in chapter XII of The Divine Names, that *deity is that which sees all things by perfect providence and goodness*. But this name God, taken from this operation, was imposed to signify the divine nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0168","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0169","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that all the things Damascene set forth pertain to providence, from which this name God is imposed for signifying.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0170","text":"[I q. 13 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0171","text":"To the second it must be said that, insofar as we can know the nature of a thing from its properties and effects, so can we signify it by a name. Hence, because we can know the substance of a stone from its property in itself, knowing what a stone is, this name stone signifies the very nature of the stone, according as it is in itself; for it signifies the definition of a stone, through which we know what a stone is. For the account signified by a name is a definition, as is said in Metaphysics IV. But from divine effects we cannot know the divine nature according as it is in itself, so as to know what it is; but [we know it] by the mode of eminence and causality and negation, as was said above. And thus this name God signifies the divine nature. For this name was imposed to signify something existing above all things, which is the principle of all things and removed from all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0172","text":"For those naming God intend to signify this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0173","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0174","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0175","text":"We proceed thus to the ninth. It seems that this name God is communicable. For to whomever the thing signified by a name is communicated, the name itself is also communicated. But this name God, as has been said, signifies the divine nature, which is communicable to others, according to II Pet. I: *he gave us great and precious promises, that through these we might be made partakers of the divine nature*. Therefore this name God is communicable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0176","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0177","text":"Further, only proper names are not communicable. But this name God is not a proper name, but an appellative, as is clear from the fact that it has a plural, according to Psalm LXXXI: *I said, you are gods*. Therefore this name God is communicable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0178","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0179","text":"Further, this name God is imposed from operation, as has been said. But other names imposed on God from operations or effects are communicable, such as good, wise, and the like. Therefore this name God too is communicable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0180","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0181","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Wisdom XIV: *they imposed the incommunicable name upon wood and stones*; and it speaks of the name of deity. Therefore this name God is an incommunicable name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0182","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183","text":"I answer that it must be said that a name can be communicable in two ways: in one way, properly; in another, by likeness. That is properly communicable which, according to the whole signification of the name, is communicable to many. But that is communicable by likeness which is communicable according to something of those things included in the signification of the name. For this name lion is properly communicated to all those in whom the nature signified by this name lion is found; but it is communicable by likeness to those who share something leonine, for example boldness or strength, and who are metaphorically called lions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0184","text":"But in order to know which names are properly communicable, it must be considered that every form existing in a singular supposit, by which it is individuated, is common to many, either in reality or at least in account, just as human nature is common to many in reality and in account; whereas the nature of the sun is not common to many in reality, but only in account. For the nature of the sun can be understood as existing in several supposits. And this is because the intellect understands the nature of any species by abstraction from the singular; hence, existing in one singular supposit or in several is outside the understanding of the nature of the species. Therefore, while the understanding of the nature of the species is preserved, it can be understood as existing in several. But the singular, by the very fact that it is singular, is divided from all others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0185","text":"Hence every name imposed to signify some singular is incommunicable both in reality and in account; for a plurality of this individual cannot even fall under apprehension. Hence no name signifying an individual is properly communicable to many, but only according to likeness; just as someone can metaphorically be called Achilles, insofar as he has something of the properties of Achilles, namely strength. But forms which are not individuated by some supposit, but through themselves (namely, because they are subsistent forms), if they were understood according as they are in themselves, could not be communicated either in reality or in account, but perhaps by likeness, as was said concerning individuals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","text":"But because we cannot understand simple forms subsisting through themselves according as they are, but understand them after the mode of composite things having forms in matter, therefore, as has been said, we impose upon them concrete names signifying a nature in some supposit. Hence, as regards the account of names, the same account holds concerning names imposed by us to signify the natures of composite things and concerning names imposed by us to signify simple subsistent natures. Hence, since this name God was imposed to signify the divine nature, as has been said; and since the divine nature is not capable of multiplication, as was shown above; it follows that this name God is indeed incommunicable in reality, but communicable according to opinion, just as this name sun would be communicable according to the opinion of those positing many suns. And according to this it is said in Gal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187","text":"IV: *you served those who by nature are not gods*; the Gloss: *they are not gods by nature, but by human opinion*. Nevertheless, this name God is communicable, not according to its whole signification, but according to something of it, through a certain likeness, so that those are called gods who participate in something divine by likeness, according to: *I said, you are gods*. But if there were some name imposed to signify God not on the part of nature but on the part of the supposit, according as he is considered as this something, that name would be incommunicable in every way, as perhaps the name tetragrammaton is among the Hebrews. And it would be similar if someone imposed a name on the sun designating this individual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0188","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0189","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the divine nature is communicable only according to participation by likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0190","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0191","text":"To the second it must be said that this name God is an appellative name, and not a proper name, because it signifies the divine nature as in one having it; although God himself, in reality, is neither universal nor particular. For names do not follow the mode of being which is in things, but the mode of being according as it is in our cognition. And yet, according to the truth of the thing, it is incommunicable, according to what was said concerning this name sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0192","text":"[I q. 13 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0193","text":"To the third, it must be said that these names, good, wise, and the like, are indeed imposed from the perfections proceeding from God into creatures; nevertheless, they are not imposed to signify the divine nature, but to signify the perfections themselves absolutely. And therefore, even according to the truth of the thing, they are communicable to many. But this name God is imposed from an operation proper to God, which we continually experience, to signify the divine nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0194","text":"## Article 10","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0195","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0196","text":"We proceed thus to the tenth. It seems that this name God is predicated univocally of God by nature, and by participation, and according to opinion. For where there is a diverse signification, there is no contradiction between one affirming and one denying; for equivocation prevents contradiction. But a Catholic saying that an idol is not God contradicts a pagan saying that an idol is God. Therefore, God, taken in both places, is predicated univocally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0197","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0198","text":"Further, just as an idol is God according to opinion and not according to truth, so the enjoyment of carnal pleasures is called happiness according to opinion and not according to truth. But this name happiness is predicated univocally of this supposed happiness and of this true happiness. Therefore this name God also is predicated univocally of God according to truth and of God according to opinion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0199","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0200","text":"Further, things whose notion is one are called univocal. But when a Catholic says that God is one, he understands by the name God an omnipotent thing, to be venerated above all things; and a gentile understands the same when he says that an idol is God. Therefore this name God is predicated univocally in both cases.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0201","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0202","text":"On the contrary, what is in the intellect is a likeness of that which is in the thing, as is said in I *Periherm.* But animal, said of a true animal and of a painted animal, is said equivocally. Therefore this name God, said of the true God and of God according to opinion, is said equivocally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0203","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0204","text":"Further, no one can signify what he does not know; but a gentile does not know the divine nature. Therefore, when he says that an idol is God, he does not signify true deity. But a Catholic signifies this when he says that God is one. Therefore this name God is not predicated univocally, but equivocally, of the true God and of God according to opinion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0205","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","text":"I answer that it must be said that this name God, in the three aforesaid significations, is taken neither univocally nor equivocally, but analogically. This is clear from the following. For univocal things have altogether the same notion; equivocal things have altogether a diverse notion; but in analogical things, it is necessary that the name, taken according to one signification, be placed in the definition of the same name taken according to its other significations. Thus being, said of substance, is placed in the definition of being insofar as it is said of accident; and healthy, said of an animal, is placed in the definition of healthy insofar as it is said of urine and of medicine. For urine is indicative, and medicine productive, of this health which is in the animal. So it occurs in the present case. For this name God, insofar as it is taken for the true God, is taken in the notion of God insofar as God is said according to opinion or participation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0207","text":"For when we name someone God according to participation, we understand by the name God something having a likeness to the true God. Similarly, when we name an idol God, by this name God we understand something to be signified concerning which men think that it is God. And thus it is manifest that the signification of the name is different in each case, but one of those significations is enclosed in the other significations. Hence it is manifest that it is said analogically.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0208","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0209","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the multiplicity of names is not considered according to the predication of the name, but according to its signification. For this name man, of whatever it is predicated, whether truly or falsely, is said in one way. But it would then be said in many ways if by this name man we intended to signify different things—for example, if one intended by this name man to signify that which is truly a man, and another intended by the same name to signify a stone or something else. Hence it is clear that a Catholic saying that an idol is not God contradicts a pagan asserting this, because both use this name God to signify the true God. For when the pagan says that an idol is God, he does not use this name insofar as it signifies a God according to opinion; for in that case he would speak truly, since even Catholics sometimes use this name in such a signification, as when it is said, *all the gods of the gentiles are demons*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0210","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0211","text":"And the same must be said in reply to the second and third. For those arguments proceed according to the diversity of predication of the name, and not according to a diverse signification.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0212","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0213","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that animal, said of a true animal and of a painted one, is not said purely equivocally; but the Philosopher takes equivocal in a broad manner, insofar as it includes analogical things within itself. For being also, which is said analogically, is sometimes said to be predicated equivocally of the diverse categories.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0213"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0214","text":"[I q. 13 a. 10 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0214"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0215","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that neither the Catholic nor the pagan knows the very nature of God as it is in itself; but each knows it according to some notion of causality, or excellence, or removal, as was said above. And according to this, the gentile can take this name God, when he says that an idol is God, in the same signification in which the Catholic takes it when saying that an idol is not God. But if there were someone who knew God according to no notion at all, neither would he name Him, except perhaps as we utter names whose signification we do not know.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0215"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0216","text":"## Article 11","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0216"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0217","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0217"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0218","text":"We proceed thus to the eleventh. It seems that this name, “He who is,” is not the most proper name of God. For this name “God” is an incommunicable name, as has been said. But this name, “He who is,” is not an incommunicable name. Therefore this name, “He who is,” is not the most proper name of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0218"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0219","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0219"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0220","text":"Further, Dionysius says, in chapter III of *On the Divine Names*, that *the naming of the good is manifestative of all God’s processions*. But it belongs most of all to God that He be the universal principle of things. Therefore this name “good” is most proper to God, and not this name, “He who is.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0220"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0221","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0221"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0222","text":"Further, every divine name seems to imply a relation to creatures, since God is known by us only through creatures. But this name, “He who is,” implies no relation to creatures. Therefore this name, “He who is,” is not the most proper name of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0222"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0223","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0223"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0224","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Exodus III that, when Moses asked, *if they should say to me, What is His name? What shall I say to them?* the Lord answered him, *Thus shall you say to them: He who is has sent me to you.* Therefore this name, “He who is,” is the most proper name of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0224"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0225","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0225"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","text":"I answer that this name, “He who is,” is the most proper name of God for three reasons. First, on account of its signification. For it does not signify any form, but being itself. Hence, since God’s being is His very essence, and this befits no other, as was shown above, it is manifest that among the other names this one names God most properly; for each thing is denominated from its form. Second, on account of its universality. For all other names either are less common; or, if they are convertible with it, nevertheless they add something over and above it according to reason; hence they in some way inform and determine it. But our intellect cannot know God’s very essence in the state of wayfarers, according as it is in itself; rather, whatever mode it determines concerning that which it understands of God falls short of the mode in which God is in Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","text":"And therefore, the less determined and the more common and absolute certain names are, the more properly they are said of God by us. Hence Damascene also says that *the chiefest of all the names said of God is “He who is,” for, comprehending all things in Himself, He has being itself as a certain infinite and indeterminate sea of substance*. For by any other name some mode of a thing’s substance is determined, but this name, “He who is,” determines no mode of being, but stands indeterminately in relation to all; and therefore it names that very *infinite sea of substance*. Third, from its consignification. For it signifies being in the present, and this is said most properly of God, whose being knows neither past nor future, as Augustine says in V *On the Trinity*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0228","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0228"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0229","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that this name, “He who is,” is a more proper name of God than this name “God,” as regards that from which it is imposed, namely, from being, and as regards the mode of signifying and consignifying, as has been said. But as regards that to which the name is imposed in order to signify, this name “God,” which is imposed to signify the divine nature, is more proper. And a still more proper name is the tetragrammaton, which is imposed to signify God’s very incommunicable and, if it is permitted so to speak, singular substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0229"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0230","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0230"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0231","text":"To the second, it must be said that this name “good” is the principal name of God insofar as He is a cause, but not simply speaking; for being is absolutely presupposed to cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0231"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0232","text":"[I q. 13 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0232"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0233","text":"To the third, it must be said that it is not necessary that all divine names imply a relation to creatures; rather, it suffices that they be imposed from certain perfections proceeding from God into creatures. Among these, the first is being itself, from which this name, “He who is,” is taken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0233"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0234","text":"## Article 12","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0234"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0235","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0235"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0236","text":"We proceed thus to the twelfth. It seems that affirmative propositions cannot be formed about God. For Dionysius says, in chapter II of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, that *negations concerning God are true, whereas affirmations are unfitting*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0236"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0237","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0237"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0238","text":"Further, Boethius says, in the book *On the Trinity*, that *a simple form cannot be a subject*. But God is supremely a simple form, as was shown above. Therefore He cannot be a subject. But everything of which an affirmative proposition is formed is taken as a subject. Therefore an affirmative proposition cannot be formed about God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0238"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0239","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0239"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0240","text":"Further, every intellect understanding a thing otherwise than it is, is false. But God has being without all composition, as was proved above. Therefore, since every affirmative intellect understands something with composition, it seems that an affirmative proposition cannot truly be formed about God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0240"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0241","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0241"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0242","text":"On the contrary, falsehood is not subject to faith. But certain affirmative propositions are subject to faith, such as that God is three and one, and that He is omnipotent. Therefore affirmative propositions can truly be formed about God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0242"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0243","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0243"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","text":"I answer that affirmative propositions can truly be formed about God. For the evidence of this, it must be known that in every true affirmative proposition, the predicate and subject must in some way signify the same thing in reality, and something diverse in notion. This is clear both in propositions concerning an accidental predicate and in those concerning a substantial predicate. For it is manifest that man and white are the same in subject, and differ in notion; for the notion of man is one thing, and the notion of white another. Likewise, when I say, “Man is an animal,” that very thing which is man is truly animal; for in the same supposit there are both the sensitive nature, from which it is called animal, and the rational nature, from which it is called man. Hence here too predicate and subject are the same in supposit, but diverse in notion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","text":"But this is found in some way even in propositions in which the same thing is predicated of itself: insofar as the intellect draws what it posits on the side of the subject to the side of the supposit, whereas what it posits on the side of the predicate it draws to the nature of the form existing in the supposit; according as it is said that predicates are taken formally, and subjects materially. To this diversity which is according to notion corresponds the plurality of predicate and subject, while the intellect signifies the identity of the thing through composition itself. But God, considered in Himself, is altogether one and simple; nevertheless, our intellect knows Him according to diverse conceptions, because it cannot see Him as He is in Himself. Yet, although it understands Him under diverse conceptions, it nevertheless knows that one and the same simple reality corresponds to all its conceptions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","text":"Therefore it represents this plurality which is according to notion by the plurality of predicate and subject, but represents unity by composition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0247","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0247"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0248","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Dionysius says that affirmations about God are incompact, or inappropriate according to another translation, insofar as no name belongs to God according to its mode of signifying, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0248"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0249","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0249"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0250","text":"To the second, it must be said that our intellect cannot apprehend simple subsisting forms according as they are in themselves, but apprehends them according to the mode of composites, in which there is something that is subjected and something that inheres. And therefore it apprehends a simple form under the notion of a subject, and attributes something to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0250"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0251","text":"[I q. 13 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0251"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0252","text":"To the third, it must be said that this proposition, “An intellect understanding a thing otherwise than it is, is false,” is ambiguous, because this adverb “otherwise” can determine the verb “understands” on the part of the thing understood, or on the part of the one understanding. If on the part of the thing understood, then the proposition is true, and its sense is: whatever intellect understands a thing to be otherwise than it is, is false. But this has no place in the present matter, because our intellect, in forming a proposition about God, does not say that He is composite, but simple. But if it is on the part of the one understanding, then the proposition is false. For the mode of the intellect in understanding is other than that of the thing in being. For it is manifest that our intellect understands material things existing beneath it immaterially; not because it understands them to be immaterial, but because it has an immaterial mode in understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0252"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","text":"And likewise, when it understands simple things which are above it, it understands them according to its own mode, namely, compositely; yet not in such a way that it understands them to be composite. And thus our intellect is not false in forming a composition about God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253"]}],"source_digest":"cb2af2b1e9923d13b994b4f924dc10137cb9018b0d2989336493e376f0809947","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 13"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 13","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6","title":"First Part · Question 14","order":15,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 14 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28893)\n\nPost considerationem eorum quae ad divinam substantiam pertinent, restat considerandum de his quae pertinent ad operationem ipsius. Et quia operatio quaedam est quae manet in operante, quaedam vero quae procedit in exteriorem effectum, primo agemus de scientia et voluntate (nam intelligere in intelligente est, et velle in volente); et postmodum de potentia Dei, quae consideratur ut principium operationis divinae in effectum exteriorem procedentis. Quia vero intelligere quoddam vivere est, post considerationem divinae scientiae, considerandum erit de vita divina. Et quia scientia verorum est, erit etiam considerandum de veritate et falsitate. Rursum, quia omne cognitum in cognoscente est, rationes autem rerum secundum quod sunt in Deo cognoscente, ideae vocantur, cum consideratione scientiae erit etiam adiungenda consideratio de ideis. Circa scientiam vero quaeruntur sexdecim. Primo, utrum in Deo sit scientia. Secundo, utrum Deus intelligat seipsum. Tertio, utrum comprehendat se. Quarto, utrum suum intelligere sit sua substantia. Quinto, utrum intelligat alia a se. Sexto, utrum habeat de eis propriam cognitionem. Septimo, utrum scientia Dei sit discursiva. Octavo, utrum scientia Dei sit causa rerum. Nono, utrum scientia Dei sit eorum quae non sunt. Decimo, utrum sit malorum. Undecimo, utrum sit singularium. Duodecimo, utrum sit infinitorum. Decimotertio, utrum sit contingentium futurorum. Decimoquarto, utrum sit enuntiabilium. Decimoquinto, utrum scientia Dei sit variabilis. Decimosexto, utrum Deus de rebus habeat speculativam scientiam vel practicam.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28894)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit scientia. Scientia enim habitus est, qui Deo non competit, cum sit medius inter potentiam et actum. Ergo scientia non est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28895)\n\nPraeterea, scientia, cum sit conclusionum, est quaedam cognitio ab alio causata, scilicet ex cognitione principiorum. Sed nihil causatum est in Deo. Ergo scientia non est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28896)\n\nPraeterea, omnis scientia vel universalis vel particularis est. Sed in Deo non est universale et particulare, ut ex superioribus patet; ergo in Deo non est scientia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28897)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit Rom. XI, *o altitudo divitiarum sapientiae et scientiae Dei*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28898)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in Deo perfectissime est scientia. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod cognoscentia a non cognoscentibus in hoc distinguuntur, quia non cognoscentia nihil habent nisi formam suam tantum; sed cognoscens natum est habere formam etiam rei alterius, nam species cogniti est in cognoscente. Unde manifestum est quod natura rei non cognoscentis est magis coarctata et limitata, natura autem rerum cognoscentium habet maiorem amplitudinem et extensionem. Propter quod dicit philosophus, III de anima, quod *anima est quodammodo omnia*. Coarctatio autem formae est per materiam. Unde et supra diximus quod formae, secundum quod sunt magis immateriales, secundum hoc magis accedunt ad quandam infinitatem. Patet igitur quod immaterialitas alicuius rei est ratio quod sit cognoscitiva; et secundum modum immaterialitatis est modus cognitionis. Unde in II de anima dicitur quod plantae non cognoscunt, propter suam materialitatem. Sensus autem cognoscitivus est, quia receptivus est specierum sine materia, et intellectus adhuc magis cognoscitivus, quia magis separatus est a materia et immixtus, ut dicitur in III de anima. Unde, cum Deus sit in summo immaterialitatis, ut ex superioribus patet, sequitur quod ipse sit in summo cognitionis.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28899)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, quia perfectiones procedentes a Deo in creaturas, altiori modo sunt in Deo, ut supra dictum est, oportet quod, quandocumque aliquod nomen sumptum a quacumque perfectione creaturae Deo attribuitur, secludatur ab eius significatione omne illud quod pertinet ad imperfectum modum qui competit creaturae. Unde scientia non est qualitas in Deo vel habitus, sed substantia et actus purus.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28900)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ea quae sunt divisim et multipliciter in creaturis, in Deo sunt simpliciter et unite, ut supra dictum est. Homo autem, secundum diversa cognita, habet diversas cognitiones, nam secundum quod cognoscit principia, dicitur habere intelligentiam; scientiam vero, secundum quod cognoscit conclusiones; sapientiam, secundum quod cognoscit causam altissimam; consilium vel prudentiam, secundum quod cognoscit agibilia. Sed haec omnia Deus una et simplici cognitione cognoscit, ut infra patebit. Unde simplex Dei cognitio omnibus istis nominibus nominari potest, ita tamen quod ab unoquoque eorum, secundum quod in divinam praedicationem venit, secludatur quidquid imperfectionis est, et retineatur quidquid perfectionis est. Et secundum hoc dicitur Iob XII, *apud ipsum est sapientia et fortitudo; ipse habet consilium et intelligentiam*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28901)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod scientia est secundum modum cognoscentis, scitum enim est in sciente secundum modum scientis. Et ideo, cum modus divinae essentiae sit altior quam modus quo creaturae sunt, scientia divina non habet modum creatae scientiae, ut scilicet sit universalis vel particularis, vel in habitu vel in potentia, vel secundum aliquem talem modum disposita.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28902)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non intelligat se. Dicitur enim in libro de causis, quod *omnis sciens qui scit suam essentiam, est rediens ad essentiam suam reditione completa*. Sed Deus non exit extra essentiam suam, nec aliquo modo movetur, et sic non competit sibi redire ad essentiam suam. Ergo ipse non est sciens essentiam suam.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28903)\n\nPraeterea, intelligere est quoddam pati et moveri, ut dicitur in III de anima, scientia etiam est assimilatio ad rem scitam, et scitum etiam est perfectio scientis. Sed nihil movetur, vel patitur, vel perficitur a seipso; neque similitudo sibi est, ut Hilarius dicit. Ergo Deus non est sciens seipsum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28904)\n\nPraeterea, praecipue Deo sumus similes secundum intellectum, quia secundum mentem sumus ad imaginem Dei, ut dicit Augustinus. Sed intellectus noster non intelligit se, nisi sicut intelligit alia, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo nec Deus intelligit se, nisi forte intelligendo alia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28905)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur I ad Cor. II, *quae sunt Dei, nemo novit nisi spiritus Dei*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28906)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus se per seipsum intelligit. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod, licet in operationibus quae transeunt in exteriorem effectum, obiectum operationis, quod significatur ut terminus, sit aliquid extra operantem; tamen in operationibus quae sunt in operante, obiectum quod significatur ut terminus operationis, est in ipso operante; et secundum quod est in eo, sic est operatio in actu. Unde dicitur in libro de anima, quod sensibile in actu est sensus in actu, et intelligibile in actu est intellectus in actu. Ex hoc enim aliquid in actu sentimus vel intelligimus, quod intellectus noster vel sensus informatur in actu per speciem sensibilis vel intelligibilis. Et secundum hoc tantum sensus vel intellectus aliud est a sensibili vel intelligibili, quia utrumque est in potentia. Cum igitur Deus nihil potentialitatis habeat, sed sit actus purus, oportet quod in eo intellectus et intellectum sint idem omnibus modis, ita scilicet, ut neque careat specie intelligibili, sicut intellectus noster cum intelligit in potentia; neque species intelligibilis sit aliud a substantia intellectus divini, sicut accidit in intellectu nostro, cum est actu intelligens; sed ipsa species intelligibilis est ipse intellectus divinus. Et sic seipsum per seipsum intelligit.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28907)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod redire ad essentiam suam nihil aliud est quam rem subsistere in seipsa. Forma enim, inquantum perficit materiam dando ei esse, quodammodo supra ipsam effunditur, inquantum vero in seipsa habet esse, in seipsam redit. Virtutes igitur cognoscitivae quae non sunt subsistentes, sed actus aliquorum organorum, non cognoscunt seipsas; sicut patet in singulis sensibus. Sed virtutes cognoscitivae per se subsistentes, cognoscunt seipsas. Et propter hoc dicitur in libro de causis, quod *sciens essentiam suam, redit ad essentiam suam*. Per se autem subsistere maxime convenit Deo. Unde secundum hunc modum loquendi, ipse est maxime rediens ad essentiam suam, et cognoscens seipsum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28908)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod moveri et pati sumuntur aequivoce secundum quod intelligere dicitur esse quoddam moveri vel pati, ut dicitur in III de anima. Non enim intelligere est motus qui est actus imperfecti, qui est ab alio in aliud, sed actus perfecti, existens in ipso agente. Similiter etiam quod intellectus perficiatur ab intelligibili vel assimiletur ei, hoc convenit intellectui qui quandoque est in potentia, quia per hoc quod est in potentia, differt ab intelligibili, et assimilatur ei per speciem intelligibilem, quae est similitudo rei intellectae; et perficitur per ipsam, sicut potentia per actum. Sed intellectus divinus, qui nullo modo est in potentia, non perficitur per intelligibile, neque assimilatur ei, sed est sua perfectio et suum intelligibile.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28909)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod esse naturale non est materiae primae, quae est in potentia, nisi secundum quod est reducta in actum per formam. Intellectus autem noster possibilis se habet in ordine intelligibilium, sicut materia prima in ordine rerum naturalium, eo quod est in potentia ad intelligibilia, sicut materia prima ad naturalia. Unde intellectus noster possibilis non potest habere intelligibilem operationem, nisi inquantum perficitur per speciem intelligibilem alicuius. Et sic intelligit seipsum per speciem intelligibilem, sicut et alia, manifestum est enim quod ex eo quod cognoscit intelligibile, intelligit ipsum suum intelligere, et per actum cognoscit potentiam intellectivam. Deus autem est sicut actus purus tam in ordine existentium, quam in ordine intelligibilium, et ideo per seipsum, seipsum intelligit.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 14 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28910)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non comprehendat seipsum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod id quod comprehendit se, finitum est sibi. Sed Deus est omnibus modis infinitus. Ergo non comprehendit se.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28911)\n\nSi dicatur quod Deus infinitus est nobis, sed sibi finitus, contra, verius est unumquodque secundum quod est apud Deum, quam secundum quod est apud nos. Si igitur Deus sibi ipsi est finitus, nobis autem infinitus, verius est Deum esse finitum, quam infinitum. Quod est contra prius determinata. Non ergo Deus comprehendit seipsum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28912)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit ibidem, *omne quod intelligit se, comprehendit se*. Sed Deus intelligit se. Ergo comprehendit se.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28913)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus perfecte comprehendit seipsum. Quod sic patet. Tunc enim dicitur aliquid comprehendi, quando pervenitur ad finem cognitionis ipsius, et hoc est quando res cognoscitur ita perfecte, sicut cognoscibilis est. Sicut propositio demonstrabilis comprehenditur, quando scitur per demonstrationem, non autem quando cognoscitur per aliquam rationem probabilem. Manifestum est autem quod Deus ita perfecte cognoscit seipsum, sicut perfecte cognoscibilis est. Est enim unumquodque cognoscibile secundum modum sui actus, non enim cognoscitur aliquid secundum quod in potentia est, sed secundum quod est in actu, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Tanta est autem virtus Dei in cognoscendo, quanta est actualitas eius in existendo, quia per hoc quod actu est, et ab omni materia et potentia separatus, Deus cognoscitivus est, ut ostensum est. Unde manifestum est quod tantum seipsum cognoscit, quantum cognoscibilis est. Et propter hoc seipsum perfecte comprehendit.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28914)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod comprehendere, si proprie accipiatur, significat aliquid habens et includens alterum. Et sic oportet quod omne comprehensum sit finitum, sicut omne inclusum. Non sic autem comprehendi dicitur Deus a seipso, ut intellectus suus sit aliud quam ipse, et capiat ipsum et includat. Sed huiusmodi locutiones per negationem sunt exponendae. Sicut enim Deus dicitur esse in seipso, quia a nullo exteriori continetur; ita dicitur comprehendi a seipso, quia nihil est sui quod lateat ipsum dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de videndo Deum, quod *totum comprehenditur videndo, quod ita videtur, ut nihil eius lateat videntem*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28915)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur Deus finitus est sibi, intelligendum est secundum quandam similitudinem proportionis; quia sic se habet in non excedendo intellectum suum, sicut se habet aliquod finitum in non excedendo intellectum finitum. Non autem sic dicitur Deus sibi finitus, quod ipse intelligat se esse aliquid finitum.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28916)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ipsum intelligere Dei non sit eius substantia. Intelligere enim est quaedam operatio. Operatio autem aliquid significat procedens ab operante. Ergo ipsum intelligere Dei non est ipsa Dei substantia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28917)\n\nPraeterea, cum aliquis intelligit se intelligere, hoc non est intelligere aliquid magnum vel principale intellectum, sed intelligere quoddam secundarium et accessorium. Si igitur Deus sit ipsum intelligere, intelligere Deum erit sicut cum intelligimus intelligere. Et sic non erit aliquid magnum intelligere Deum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28918)\n\nPraeterea, omne intelligere est aliquid intelligere. Cum ergo Deus intelligit se, si ipsemet non est aliud quam suum intelligere, intelligit se intelligere, et intelligere se intelligere se, et sic in infinitum. Non ergo ipsum intelligere Dei est eius substantia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28919)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, Lib. VII de Trin., *Deo hoc est esse, quod sapientem esse*. Hoc autem est sapientem esse, quod intelligere. Ergo Deo hoc est esse, quod intelligere. Sed esse Dei est eius substantia, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo intelligere Dei est eius substantia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28920)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod est necesse dicere quod intelligere Dei est eius substantia. Nam si intelligere Dei sit aliud quam eius substantia, oporteret, ut dicit philosophus in XII Metaphys., quod aliquid aliud esset actus et perfectio substantiae divinae, ad quod se haberet substantia divina sicut potentia ad actum (quod est omnino impossibile), nam intelligere est perfectio et actus intelligentis. Hoc autem qualiter sit, considerandum est. Sicut enim supra dictum est, intelligere non est actio progrediens ad aliquid extrinsecum, sed manet in operante sicut actus et perfectio eius, prout esse est perfectio existentis, sicut enim esse consequitur formam, ita intelligere sequitur speciem intelligibilem. In Deo autem non est forma quae sit aliud quam suum esse, ut supra ostensum est. Unde, cum ipsa sua essentia sit etiam species intelligibilis, ut dictum est, ex necessitate sequitur quod ipsum eius intelligere sit eius essentia et eius esse. Et sic patet ex omnibus praemissis quod in Deo intellectus, et id quod intelligitur, et species intelligibilis, et ipsum intelligere, sunt omnino unum et idem. Unde patet quod per hoc quod Deus dicitur intelligens, nulla multiplicitas ponitur in eius substantia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28921)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intelligere non est operatio exiens ab ipso operante, sed manens in ipso.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28922)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum intelligitur illud intelligere quod non est subsistens, non intelligitur aliquid magnum; sicut cum intelligimus intelligere nostrum. Et ideo non est simile de ipso intelligere divino, quod est subsistens.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28923)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad tertium. Nam intelligere divinum, quod est in seipso subsistens, est sui ipsius; et non alicuius alterius, ut sic oporteat procedere in infinitum.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28924)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat alia a se. Quaecumque enim sunt alia a Deo, sunt extra ipsum. Sed Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium qu., quod *neque quidquam Deus extra seipsum intuetur*. Ergo non cognoscit alia a se.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28925)\n\nPraeterea, intellectum est perfectio intelligentis. Si ergo Deus intelligat alia a se, aliquid aliud erit perfectio Dei, et nobilius ipso. Quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28926)\n\nPraeterea, ipsum intelligere speciem habet ab intelligibili, sicut et omnis alius actus a suo obiecto, unde et ipsum intelligere tanto est nobilius, quanto etiam nobilius est ipsum quod intelligitur. Sed Deus est ipsum suum intelligere, ut ex dictis patet. Si igitur Deus intelligit aliquid aliud a se, ipse Deus specificatur per aliquid aliud a se, quod est impossibile. Non igitur intelligit alia a se.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28927)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Hebr. IV, *omnia nuda et aperta sunt oculis eius*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28928)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est Deum cognoscere alia a se. Manifestum est enim quod seipsum perfecte intelligit, alioquin suum esse non esset perfectum, cum suum esse sit suum intelligere. Si autem perfecte aliquid cognoscitur, necesse est quod virtus eius perfecte cognoscatur. Virtus autem alicuius rei perfecte cognosci non potest, nisi cognoscantur ea ad quae virtus se extendit. Unde, cum virtus divina se extendat ad alia, eo quod ipsa est prima causa effectiva omnium entium, ut ex supradictis patet; necesse est quod Deus alia a se cognoscat. Et hoc etiam evidentius fit, si adiungatur quod ipsum esse causae agentis primae, scilicet Dei, est eius intelligere. Unde quicumque effectus praeexistunt in Deo sicut in causa prima, necesse est quod sint in ipso eius intelligere; et quod omnia in eo sint secundum modum intelligibilem, nam omne quod est in altero, est in eo secundum modum eius in quo est. Ad sciendum autem qualiter alia a se cognoscat, considerandum est quod dupliciter aliquid cognoscitur, uno modo, in seipso; alio modo, in altero. In seipso quidem cognoscitur aliquid, quando cognoscitur per speciem propriam adaequatam ipsi cognoscibili, sicut cum oculus videt hominem per speciem hominis. In alio autem videtur id quod videtur per speciem continentis, sicut cum pars videtur in toto per speciem totius, vel cum homo videtur in speculo per speciem speculi, vel quocumque alio modo contingat aliquid in alio videri. Sic igitur dicendum est quod Deus seipsum videt in seipso, quia seipsum videt per essentiam suam. Alia autem a se videt non in ipsis, sed in seipso, inquantum essentia sua continet similitudinem aliorum ab ipso.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28929)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Augustini dicentis quod Deus nihil extra se intuetur, non est sic intelligendum, quasi nihil quod sit extra se intueatur, sed quia id quod est extra seipsum, non intuetur nisi in seipso, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28930)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectum est perfectio intelligentis non quidem secundum suam substantiam, sed secundum suam speciem, secundum quam est in intellectu, ut forma et perfectio eius, *lapis enim non est in anima, sed species eius*, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ea vero quae sunt alia a Deo, intelliguntur a Deo inquantum essentia Dei continet species eorum, ut dictum est. Unde non sequitur quod aliquid aliud sit perfectio divini intellectus, quam ipsa essentia Dei.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28931)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ipsum intelligere non specificatur per id quod in alio intelligitur, sed per principale intellectum, in quo alia intelliguntur. Intantum enim ipsum intelligere specificatur per obiectum suum, inquantum forma intelligibilis est principium intellectualis operationis, nam omnis operatio specificatur per formam quae est principium operationis, sicut calefactio per calorem. Unde per illam formam intelligibilem specificatur intellectualis operatio, quae facit intellectum in actu. Et haec est species principalis intellecti, quae in Deo nihil est aliud quam essentia sua, in qua omnes species rerum comprehenduntur. Unde non oportet quod ipsum intelligere divinum, vel potius ipse Deus, specificetur per aliud quam per essentiam divinam.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28932)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat alia a se propria cognitione. Sic enim cognoscit alia a se, ut dictum est, secundum quod alia ab ipso in eo sunt. Sed alia ab eo sunt in ipso sicut in prima causa communi et universali. Ergo et alia cognoscuntur a Deo, sicut in causa prima et universali. Hoc autem est cognoscere in universali, et non secundum propriam cognitionem. Ergo Deus cognoscit alia a se in universali, et non secundum propriam cognitionem.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28933)\n\nPraeterea, quantum distat essentia creaturae ab essentia divina, tantum distat essentia divina ab essentia creaturae. Sed per essentiam creaturae non potest cognosci essentia divina, ut supra dictum est. Ergo nec per essentiam divinam potest cognosci essentia creaturae. Et sic, cum Deus nihil cognoscat nisi per essentiam suam, sequitur quod non cognoscat creaturam secundum eius essentiam, ut cognoscat de ea quid est, quod est propriam cognitionem de re habere.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28934)\n\nPraeterea, propria cognitio non habetur de re, nisi per propriam eius rationem. Sed cum Deus cognoscat omnia per essentiam suam, non videtur quod unumquodque per propriam rationem cognoscat, idem enim non potest esse propria ratio multorum et diversorum. Non ergo habet propriam cognitionem Deus de rebus, sed communem, nam cognoscere res non secundum propriam rationem, est cognoscere res solum in communi.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28935)\n\nSed contra, habere propriam cognitionem de rebus, est cognoscere res non solum in communi, sed secundum quod sunt ab invicem distinctae. Sic autem Deus cognoscit res. Unde dicitur Heb. IV, quod *pertingit usque ad divisionem spiritus et animae, compagum quoque et medullarum; et discretor cogitationum et intentionum cordis; et non est ulla creatura invisibilis in conspectu eius*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28936)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc quidam erraverunt, dicentes quod Deus alia a se non cognoscit nisi in communi, scilicet inquantum sunt entia. Sicut enim ignis, si cognosceret seipsum ut est principium caloris, cognosceret naturam caloris, et omnia alia inquantum sunt calida; ita Deus, inquantum cognoscit se ut principium essendi, cognoscit naturam entis, et omnia alia inquantum sunt entia. Sed hoc non potest esse. Nam intelligere aliquid in communi, et non in speciali, est imperfecte aliquid cognoscere. Unde intellectus noster, dum de potentia in actum reducitur, pertingit prius ad cognitionem universalem et confusam de rebus, quam ad propriam rerum cognitionem, sicut de imperfecto ad perfectum procedens, ut patet in I Physic. Si igitur cognitio Dei de rebus aliis a se, esset in universali tantum, et non in speciali, sequeretur quod eius intelligere non esset omnibus modis perfectum, et per consequens nec eius esse, quod est contra ea quae superius ostensa sunt. Oportet igitur dicere quod alia a se cognoscat propria cognitione; non solum secundum quod communicant in ratione entis, sed secundum quod unum ab alio distinguitur. Et ad huius evidentiam, considerandum est quod quidam, volentes ostendere quod Deus per unum cognoscit multa, utuntur quibusdam exemplis, ut puta quod, si centrum cognosceret seipsum, cognosceret omnes lineas progredientes a centro; vel lux, si cognosceret seipsam, cognosceret omnes colores. Sed haec exempla, licet quantum ad aliquid similia sint, scilicet quantum ad universalem causalitatem; tamen deficiunt quantum ad hoc, quod multitudo et diversitas non causantur ab illo uno principio universali, quantum ad id quod principium distinctionis est, sed solum quantum ad id in quo communicant. Non enim diversitas colorum causatur ex luce solum, sed ex diversa dispositione diaphani recipientis, et similiter diversitas linearum ex diverso situ. Et inde est quod huiusmodi diversitas et multitudo non potest cognosci in suo principio secundum propriam cognitionem, sed solum in communi. Sed in Deo non sic est. Supra enim ostensum est quod quidquid perfectionis est in quacumque creatura, totum praeexistit et continetur in Deo secundum modum excellentem. Non solum autem id in quo creaturae communicant, scilicet ipsum esse, ad perfectionem pertinet; sed etiam ea per quae creaturae ad invicem distinguuntur, sicut vivere, et intelligere, et huiusmodi, quibus viventia a non viventibus, et intelligentia a non intelligentibus distinguuntur. Et omnis forma, per quam quaelibet res in propria specie constituitur, perfectio quaedam est. Et sic omnia in Deo praeexistunt, non solum quantum ad id quod commune est omnibus, sed etiam quantum ad ea secundum quae res distinguuntur. Et sic, cum Deus in se omnes perfectiones contineat, comparatur Dei essentia ad omnes rerum essentias, non sicut commune ad propria, ut unitas ad numeros, vel centrum ad lineas; sed sicut perfectus actus ad imperfectos, ut si dicerem, homo ad animal, vel senarius, qui est numerus perfectus, ad numeros imperfectos sub ipso contentos. Manifestum est autem quod per actum perfectum cognosci possunt actus imperfecti, non solum in communi, sed etiam propria cognitione. Sicut qui cognoscit hominem, cognoscit animal propria cognitione, et qui cognoscit senarium, cognoscit trinarium propria cognitione. Sic igitur, cum essentia Dei habeat in se quidquid perfectionis habet essentia cuiuscumque rei alterius, et adhuc amplius, Deus in seipso potest omnia propria cognitione cognoscere. Propria enim natura uniuscuiusque consistit, secundum quod per aliquem modum divinam perfectionem participat. Non autem Deus perfecte seipsum cognosceret, nisi cognosceret quomodocumque participabilis est ab aliis sua perfectio, nec etiam ipsam naturam essendi perfecte sciret, nisi cognosceret omnes modos essendi. Unde manifestum est quod Deus cognoscit omnes res propria cognitione, secundum quod ab aliis distinguuntur.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28937)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sic cognoscere aliquid sicut in cognoscente est, potest dupliciter intelligi. Uno modo, secundum quod hoc adverbium sic importat modum cognitionis ex parte rei cognitae. Et sic falsum est. Non enim semper cognoscens cognoscit cognitum secundum illud esse quod habet in cognoscente, oculus enim non cognoscit lapidem secundum esse quod habet in oculo; sed per speciem lapidis quam habet in se, cognoscit lapidem secundum esse quod habet extra oculum. Et si aliquis cognoscens cognoscat cognitum secundum esse quod habet in cognoscente, nihilominus cognoscit ipsum secundum esse quod habet extra cognoscentem, sicut intellectus cognoscit lapidem secundum esse intelligibile quod habet in intellectu, inquantum cognoscit se intelligere; sed nihilominus cognoscit esse lapidis in propria natura. Si vero intelligatur secundum quod hoc adverbium sic importat modum ex parte cognoscentis, verum est quod sic solum cognoscens cognoscit cognitum, secundum quod est in cognoscente, quia quanto perfectius est cognitum in cognoscente, tanto perfectior est modus cognitionis. Sic igitur dicendum est quod Deus non solum cognoscit res esse in seipso; sed per id quod in seipso continet res, cognoscit eas in propria natura; et tanto perfectius, quanto perfectius est unumquodque in ipso.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28938)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod essentia creaturae comparatur ad essentiam Dei, ut actus imperfectus ad perfectum. Et ideo essentia creaturae non sufficienter ducit in cognitionem essentiae divinae, sed e converso.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28939)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod idem non potest accipi ut ratio diversorum per modum adaequationis. Sed divina essentia est aliquid excedens omnes creaturas. Unde potest accipi ut propria ratio uniuscuiusque, secundum quod diversimode est participabilis vel imitabilis a diversis creaturis.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28940)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod scientia Dei sit discursiva. Scientia enim Dei non est secundum scire in habitu, sed secundum intelligere in actu. Sed secundum philosophum, in II Topic., scire in habitu contingit multa simul, sed intelligere actu unum tantum. Cum ergo Deus multa cognoscat, quia et se et alia, ut ostensum est, videtur quod non simul omnia intelligat, sed de uno in aliud discurrat.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28941)\n\nPraeterea, cognoscere effectum per causam est scire discurrentis. Sed Deus cognoscit alia per seipsum, sicut effectum per causam. Ergo cognitio sua est discursiva.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28942)\n\nPraeterea, perfectius Deus scit unamquamque creaturam quam nos sciamus. Sed nos in causis creatis cognoscimus earum effectus, et sic de causis ad causata discurrimus. Ergo videtur similiter esse in Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28943)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in XV de Trin., quod *Deus non particulatim vel singillatim omnia videt, velut alternante conspectu hinc illuc, et inde huc; sed omnia videt simul*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28944)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in scientia divina nullus est discursus. Quod sic patet. In scientia enim nostra duplex est discursus. Unus secundum successionem tantum, sicut cum, postquam intelligimus aliquid in actu, convertimus nos ad intelligendum aliud. Alius discursus est secundum causalitatem, sicut cum per principia pervenimus in cognitionem conclusionum. Primus autem discursus Deo convenire non potest. Multa enim, quae successive intelligimus si unumquodque eorum in seipso consideretur, omnia simul intelligimus si in aliquo uno ea intelligamus, puta si partes intelligamus in toto, vel si diversas res videamus in speculo. Deus autem omnia videt in uno, quod est ipse, ut habitum est. Unde simul, et non successive omnia videt. Similiter etiam et secundus discursus Deo competere non potest. Primo quidem, quia secundus discursus praesupponit primum, procedentes enim a principiis ad conclusiones, non simul utrumque considerant. Deinde, quia discursus talis est procedentis de noto ad ignotum. Unde manifestum est quod, quando cognoscitur primum, adhuc ignoratur secundum. Et sic secundum non cognoscitur in primo, sed ex primo. Terminus vero discursus est, quando secundum videtur in primo, resolutis effectibus in causas, et tunc cessat discursus. Unde, cum Deus effectus suos in seipso videat sicut in causa, eius cognitio non est discursiva.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28945)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet sit unum tantum intelligere in seipso, tamen contingit multa intelligere in aliquo uno, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28946)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus non cognoscit per causam quasi prius cognitam, effectus incognitos, sed eos cognoscit in causa. Unde eius cognitio est sine discursu, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28947)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod effectus causarum creatarum videt quidem Deus in ipsis causis, multo melius quam nos, non tamen ita quod cognitio effectuum causetur in ipso ex cognitione causarum creatarum, sicut in nobis. Unde eius scientia non est discursiva.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28948)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod scientia Dei non sit causa rerum. Dicit enim Origenes, super epistolam ad Rom., *non propterea aliquid erit, quia id scit Deus futurum; sed quia futurum est, ideo scitur a Deo antequam fiat*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28949)\n\nPraeterea, posita causa ponitur effectus. Sed scientia Dei est aeterna. Si ergo scientia Dei est causa rerum creatarum, videtur quod creaturae sint ab aeterno.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28950)\n\nPraeterea, scibile est prius scientia, et mensura eius, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sed id quod est posterius et mensuratum, non potest esse causa. Ergo scientia Dei non est causa rerum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28951)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XV de Trin., *universas creaturas, et spirituales et corporales, non quia sunt, ideo novit Deus; sed ideo sunt, quia novit*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28952)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod scientia Dei est causa rerum. Sic enim scientia Dei se habet ad omnes res creatas, sicut scientia artificis se habet ad artificiata. Scientia autem artificis est causa artificiatorum, eo quod artifex operatur per suum intellectum, unde oportet quod forma intellectus sit principium operationis, sicut calor est principium calefactionis. Sed considerandum est quod forma naturalis, inquantum est forma manens in eo cui dat esse, non nominat principium actionis; sed secundum quod habet inclinationem ad effectum. Et similiter forma intelligibilis non nominat principium actionis secundum quod est tantum in intelligente, nisi adiungatur ei inclinatio ad effectum, quae est per voluntatem. Cum enim forma intelligibilis ad opposita se habeat (cum sit eadem scientia oppositorum), non produceret determinatum effectum, nisi determinaretur ad unum per appetitum, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Manifestum est autem quod Deus per intellectum suum causat res, cum suum esse sit suum intelligere. Unde necesse est quod sua scientia sit causa rerum, secundum quod habet voluntatem coniunctam. Unde scientia Dei, secundum quod est causa rerum, consuevit nominari scientia approbationis.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28953)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Origenes locutus est attendens rationem scientiae, cui non competit ratio causalitatis, nisi adiuncta voluntate, ut dictum est. Sed quod dicit ideo praescire Deum aliqua, quia sunt futura, intelligendum est secundum causam consequentiae, non secundum causam essendi. Sequitur enim, si aliqua sunt futura, quod Deus ea praescierit, non tamen res futurae sunt causa quod Deus sciat.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28954)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod scientia Dei est causa rerum, secundum quod res sunt in scientia. Non fuit autem in scientia Dei, quod res essent ab aeterno. Unde, quamvis scientia Dei sit aeterna, non sequitur tamen quod creaturae sint ab aeterno.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28955)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod res naturales sunt mediae inter scientiam Dei et scientiam nostram, nos enim scientiam accipimus a rebus naturalibus, quarum Deus per suam scientiam causa est. Unde, sicut scibilia naturalia sunt priora quam scientia nostra, et mensura eius, ita scientia Dei est prior quam res naturales, et mensura ipsarum. Sicut aliqua domus est media inter scientiam artificis qui eam fecit, et scientiam illius qui eius cognitionem ex ipsa iam facta capit.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28956)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non habeat scientiam non entium. Scientia enim Dei non est nisi verorum. Sed verum et ens convertuntur. Ergo scientia Dei non est non entium.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28957)\n\nPraeterea, scientia requirit similitudinem inter scientem et scitum. Sed ea quae non sunt, non possunt habere aliquam similitudinem ad Deum, qui est ipsum esse. Ergo ea quae non sunt, non possunt sciri a Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28958)\n\nPraeterea, scientia Dei est causa scitorum ab ipso. Sed non est causa non entium, quia non ens non habet causam. Ergo Deus non habet scientiam de non entibus.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28959)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit apostolus ad Rom. IV, *qui vocat ea quae non sunt, tanquam ea quae sunt*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28960)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus scit omnia quaecumque sunt quocumque modo. Nihil autem prohibet ea quae non sunt simpliciter, aliquo modo esse. Simpliciter enim sunt, quae actu sunt. Ea vero quae non sunt actu, sunt in potentia vel ipsius Dei, vel creaturae; sive in potentia activa, sive in passiva, sive in potentia opinandi, vel imaginandi, vel quocumque modo significandi. Quaecumque igitur possunt per creaturam fieri vel cogitari vel dici, et etiam quaecumque ipse facere potest, omnia cognoscit Deus, etiam si actu non sint. Et pro tanto dici potest quod habet etiam non entium scientiam. Sed horum quae actu non sunt, est attendenda quaedam diversitas. Quaedam enim, licet non sint nunc in actu, tamen vel fuerunt vel erunt, et omnia ista dicitur Deus scire scientia visionis. Quia, cum intelligere Dei, quod est eius esse, aeternitate mensuretur, quae sine successione existens totum tempus comprehendit, praesens intuitus Dei fertur in totum tempus, et in omnia quae sunt in quocumque tempore, sicut in subiecta sibi praesentialiter. Quaedam vero sunt, quae sunt in potentia Dei vel creaturae, quae tamen nec sunt nec erunt neque fuerunt. Et respectu horum non dicitur habere scientiam visionis, sed simplicis intelligentiae. Quod ideo dicitur, quia ea quae videntur apud nos, habent esse distinctum extra videntem.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28961)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum quod sunt in potentia, sic habent veritatem ea quae non sunt actu, verum est enim ea esse in potentia. Et sic sciuntur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28962)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum Deus sit ipsum esse, intantum unumquodque est, inquantum participat de Dei similitudine, sicut unumquodque intantum est calidum, inquantum participat calorem. Sic et ea quae sunt in potentia, etiam si non sunt in actu, cognoscuntur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28963)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Dei scientia est causa rerum, voluntate adiuncta. Unde non oportet quod quaecumque scit Deus, sint vel fuerint vel futura sint, sed solum ea quae vult esse, vel permittit esse. Et iterum, non est in scientia Dei ut illa sint, sed quod esse possint.\n\n## Articulus 10\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28964)\n\nAd decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat mala. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod intellectus qui non est in potentia, non cognoscit privationem. Sed malum est privatio boni, ut dicit Augustinus. Igitur, cum intellectus Dei nunquam sit in potentia, sed semper actu, ut ex dictis patet, videtur quod Deus non cognoscat mala.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28965)\n\nPraeterea, omnis scientia vel est causa sciti, vel causatur ab eo. Sed scientia Dei non est causa mali, nec causatur a malo. Ergo scientia Dei non est malorum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28966)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod cognoscitur, cognoscitur per suam similitudinem, vel per suum oppositum. Quidquid autem cognoscit Deus, cognoscit per suam essentiam, ut ex dictis patet. Divina autem essentia neque est similitudo mali, neque ei malum opponitur, divinae enim essentiae nihil est contrarium, ut dicit Augustinus, XII de Civ. Dei. Ergo Deus non cognoscit mala.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28967)\n\nPraeterea, quod cognoscitur non per seipsum, sed per aliud, imperfecte cognoscitur. Sed malum non cognoscitur a Deo per seipsum, quia sic oporteret quod malum esset in Deo; oportet enim cognitum esse in cognoscente. Si ergo cognoscitur per aliud, scilicet per bonum, imperfecte cognoscetur ab ipso, quod est impossibile, quia nulla cognitio Dei est imperfecta. Ergo scientia Dei non est malorum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28968)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Proverb. XV, *Infernus et perditio coram Deo*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28969)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quicumque perfecte cognoscit aliquid, oportet quod cognoscat omnia quae possunt illi accidere. Sunt autem quaedam bona, quibus accidere potest ut per mala corrumpantur. Unde Deus non perfecte cognosceret bona, nisi etiam cognosceret mala. Sic autem est cognoscibile unumquodque, secundum quod est. Unde, cum hoc sit esse mali, quod est privatio boni, per hoc ipsum quod Deus cognoscit bona, cognoscit etiam mala; sicut per lucem cognoscuntur tenebrae. Unde dicit Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Deus per semetipsum tenebrarum accipit visionem, non aliunde videns tenebras quam a lumine*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28970)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum philosophi est sic intelligendum, quod intellectus qui non est in potentia, non cognoscit privationem per privationem in ipso existentem. Et hoc congruit cum eo quod supra dixerat, quod punctum et omne indivisibile per privationem divisionis cognoscitur. Quod contingit ex hoc, quia formae simplices et indivisibiles non sunt actu in intellectu nostro, sed in potentia tantum, nam si essent actu in intellectu nostro, non per privationem cognoscerentur. Et sic cognoscuntur simplicia a substantiis separatis. Deus igitur non cognoscit malum per privationem in se existentem, sed per bonum oppositum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28971)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod scientia Dei non est causa mali, sed est causa boni, per quod cognoscitur malum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28972)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet malum non opponatur essentiae divinae, quae non est corruptibilis per malum, opponitur tamen effectibus Dei; quos per essentiam suam cognoscit, et eos cognoscens, mala opposita cognoscit.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28973)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod cognoscere aliquid per aliud tantum, est imperfectae cognitionis, si illud sit cognoscibile per se. Sed malum non est per se cognoscibile, quia de ratione mali est, quod sit privatio boni. Et sic neque definiri, neque cognosci potest, nisi per bonum.\n\n## Articulus 11\n\n[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28974)\n\nAd undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat singularia. Intellectus enim divinus immaterialior est quam intellectus humanus. Sed intellectus humanus, propter suam immaterialitatem, non cognoscit singularia, sed, sicut dicitur in II de anima, *ratio est universalium, sensus vero singularium*. Ergo Deus non cognoscit singularia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28975)\n\nPraeterea, illae solae virtutes in nobis sunt singularium cognoscitivae, quae recipiunt species non abstractas a materialibus conditionibus. Sed res in Deo sunt maxime abstractae ab omni materialitate. Ergo Deus non cognoscit singularia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28976)\n\nPraeterea, omnis cognitio est per aliquam similitudinem. Sed similitudo singularium, inquantum sunt singularia, non videtur esse in Deo, quia principium singularitatis est materia, quae, cum sit ens in potentia tantum, omnino est dissimilis Deo, qui est actus purus. Non ergo Deus potest cognoscere singularia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28977)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur, Proverb. XVI, *omnes viae hominum patent oculis eius*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28978)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus cognoscit singularia. Omnes enim perfectiones in creaturis inventae, in Deo praeexistunt secundum altiorem modum, ut ex dictis patet. Cognoscere autem singularia pertinet ad perfectionem nostram. Unde necesse est quod Deus singularia cognoscat. Nam et philosophus pro inconvenienti habet, quod aliquid cognoscatur a nobis, quod non cognoscatur a Deo. Unde contra Empedoclem arguit, in I de anima et in III Metaphys., quod accideret Deum esse insipientissimum, si discordiam ignoraret. Sed perfectiones quae in inferioribus dividuntur, in Deo simpliciter et unite existunt. Unde, licet nos per aliam potentiam cognoscamus universalia et immaterialia, et per aliam singularia et materialia; Deus tamen per suum simplicem intellectum utraque cognoscit. Sed qualiter hoc esse possit, quidam manifestare volentes, dixerunt quod Deus cognoscit singularia per causas universales, nam nihil est in aliquo singularium, quod non ex aliqua causa oriatur universali. Et ponunt exemplum, sicut si aliquis astrologus cognosceret omnes motus universales caeli, posset praenuntiare omnes eclipses futuras. Sed istud non sufficit. Quia singularia ex causis universalibus sortiuntur quasdam formas et virtutes, quae, quantumcumque ad invicem coniungantur, non individuantur nisi per materiam individualem. Unde qui cognosceret Socratem per hoc quod est albus vel Sophronisci filius, vel quidquid aliud sic dicatur, non cognosceret ipsum inquantum est hic homo. Unde secundum modum praedictum, Deus non cognosceret singularia in sua singularitate. Alii vero dixerunt quod Deus cognoscit singularia, applicando causas universales ad particulares effectus. Sed hoc nihil est. Quia nullus potest applicare aliquid ad alterum, nisi illud praecognoscat, unde dicta applicatio non potest esse ratio cognoscendi particularia, sed cognitionem singularium praesupponit. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod, cum Deus sit causa rerum per suam scientiam, ut dictum est, intantum se extendit scientia Dei, inquantum se extendit eius causalitas. Unde, cum virtus activa Dei se extendat non solum ad formas, a quibus accipitur ratio universalis, sed etiam usque ad materiam, ut infra ostendetur; necesse est quod scientia Dei usque ad singularia se extendat, quae per materiam individuantur. Cum enim sciat alia a se per essentiam suam, inquantum est similitudo rerum velut principium activum earum, necesse est quod essentia sua sit principium sufficiens cognoscendi omnia quae per ipsum fiunt, non solum in universali, sed etiam in singulari. Et esset simile de scientia artificis, si esset productiva totius rei, et non formae tantum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28979)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus noster speciem intelligibilem abstrahit a principiis individuantibus, unde species intelligibilis nostri intellectus non potest esse similitudo principiorum individualium. Et propter hoc, intellectus noster singularia non cognoscit. Sed species intelligibilis divini intellectus, quae est Dei essentia, non est immaterialis per abstractionem, sed per seipsam, principium existens omnium principiorum quae intrant rei compositionem, sive sint principia speciei, sive principia individui. Unde per eam Deus cognoscit non solum universalia, sed etiam singularia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28980)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quamvis species intellectus divini secundum esse suum non habeat conditiones materiales, sicut species receptae in imaginatione et sensu; tamen virtute se extendit ad immaterialia et materialia, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28981)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod materia, licet recedat a Dei similitudine secundum suam potentialitatem, tamen inquantum vel sic esse habet, similitudinem quandam retinet divini esse.\n\n## Articulus 12\n\n[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28982)\n\nAd duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit cognoscere infinita. Infinitum enim, secundum quod est infinitum, est ignotum, quia infinitum est cuius quantitatem accipientibus semper est aliquid extra assumere, ut dicitur in III Physic. Augustinus etiam dicit, XII de Civ. Dei, quod *quidquid scientia comprehenditur, scientis comprehensione finitur*. Sed infinita non possunt finiri. Ergo non possunt scientia Dei comprehendi.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28983)\n\nSi dicatur quod ea quae in se sunt infinita, scientiae Dei finita sunt, contra, ratio infiniti est quod sit impertransibile; et finiti quod sit pertransibile, ut dicitur in III Physic. Sed infinitum non potest transiri nec a finito, nec ab infinito, ut probatur in VI Physic. Ergo infinitum non potest esse finitum finito, neque etiam infinito. Et ita infinita non sunt finita scientiae Dei, quae est infinita.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28984)\n\nPraeterea, scientia Dei est mensura scitorum. Sed contra rationem infiniti est, quod sit mensuratum. Ergo infinita non possunt sciri a Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28985)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XII de Civ. Dei, *quamvis infinitorum numerorum nullus sit numerus, non est tamen incomprehensibilis ei, cuius scientiae non est numerus*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28986)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum Deus sciat non solum ea quae sunt actu, sed etiam ea quae sunt in potentia vel sua vel creaturae, ut ostensum est; haec autem constat esse infinita; necesse est dicere quod Deus sciat infinita. Et licet scientia visionis, quae est tantum eorum quae sunt vel erunt vel fuerunt, non sit infinitorum, ut quidam dicunt, cum non ponamus mundum ab aeterno fuisse, nec generationem et motum in aeternum mansura, ut individua in infinitum multiplicentur, tamen, si diligentius consideretur, necesse est dicere quod Deus etiam scientia visionis sciat infinita. Quia Deus scit etiam cogitationes et affectiones cordium, quae in infinitum multiplicabuntur, creaturis rationalibus permanentibus absque fine. Hoc autem ideo est, quia cognitio cuiuslibet cognoscentis se extendit secundum modum formae quae est principium cognitionis. Species enim sensibilis, quae est in sensu, est similitudo solum unius individui, unde per eam solum unum individuum cognosci potest. Species autem intelligibilis intellectus nostri est similitudo rei quantum ad naturam speciei, quae est participabilis a particularibus infinitis, unde intellectus noster per speciem intelligibilem hominis, cognoscit quodammodo homines infinitos. Sed tamen non inquantum distinguuntur ab invicem, sed secundum quod communicant in natura speciei; propter hoc quod species intelligibilis intellectus nostri non est similitudo hominum quantum ad principia individualia, sed solum quantum ad principia speciei. Essentia autem divina, per quam intellectus divinus intelligit, est similitudo sufficiens omnium quae sunt vel esse possunt, non solum quantum ad principia communia, sed etiam quantum ad principia propria uniuscuiusque, ut ostensum est. Unde sequitur quod scientia Dei se extendat ad infinita, etiam secundum quod sunt ab invicem distincta.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28987)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod *infiniti ratio congruit quantitati*, secundum philosophum in I Physic. De ratione autem quantitatis est ordo partium. Cognoscere ergo infinitum secundum modum infiniti, est cognoscere partem post partem. Et sic nullo modo contingit cognosci infinitum, quia quantacumque quantitas partium accipiatur, semper remanet aliquid extra accipientem. Deus autem non sic cognoscit infinitum vel infinita, quasi enumerando partem post partem; cum cognoscat omnia simul, non successive, ut supra dictum est. Unde nihil prohibet ipsum cognoscere infinita.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28988)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod transitio importat quandam successionem in partibus, et inde est quod infinitum transiri non potest, neque a finito neque ab infinito. Sed ad rationem comprehensionis sufficit adaequatio, quia id comprehendi dicitur, cuius nihil est extra comprehendentem. Unde non est contra rationem infiniti, quod comprehendatur ab infinito. Et sic, quod in se est infinitum, potest dici finitum scientiae Dei, tanquam comprehensum, non tamen tanquam pertransibile.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28989)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod scientia Dei est mensura rerum, non quantitativa, qua quidem mensura carent infinita; sed quia mensurat essentiam et veritatem rei. Unumquodque enim intantum habet de veritate suae naturae, inquantum imitatur Dei scientiam; sicut artificiatum inquantum concordat arti. Dato autem quod essent aliqua infinita actu secundum numerum, puta infiniti homines; vel secundum quantitatem continuam, ut si esset aer infinitus, ut quidam antiqui dixerunt, tamen manifestum est quod haberent esse determinatum et finitum, quia esse eorum esset limitatum ad aliquas determinatas naturas. Unde mensurabilia essent secundum scientiam Dei.\n\n## Articulus 13\n\n[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28990)\n\nAd decimumtertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod scientia Dei non sit futurorum contingentium. A causa enim necessaria procedit effectus necessarius. Sed scientia Dei est causa scitorum, ut supra dictum est. Cum ergo ipsa sit necessaria, sequitur scita eius esse necessaria. Non ergo scientia Dei est contingentium.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28991)\n\nPraeterea, omnis conditionalis cuius antecedens est necessarium absolute, consequens est necessarium absolute. Sic enim se habet antecedens ad consequens, sicut principia ad conclusionem, ex principiis autem necessariis non sequitur conclusio nisi necessaria, ut in I Poster. probatur. Sed haec est quaedam conditionalis vera, si Deus scivit hoc futurum esse, hoc erit, quia scientia Dei non est nisi verorum. Huius autem conditionalis antecedens est necessarium absolute, tum quia est aeternum; tum quia significatur ut praeteritum. Ergo et consequens est necessarium absolute. Igitur quidquid scitur a Deo, est necessarium. Et sic scientia Dei non est contingentium.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28992)\n\nPraeterea, omne scitum a Deo necesse est esse, quia etiam omne scitum a nobis necesse est esse, cum tamen scientia Dei certior sit quam scientia nostra. Sed nullum contingens futurum necesse est esse. Ergo nullum contingens futurum est scitum a Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28993)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XXXII, *qui finxit singillatim corda eorum, qui intelligit omnia opera eorum*, scilicet hominum. Sed opera hominum sunt contingentia, utpote libero arbitrio subiecta. Ergo Deus scit futura contingentia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28994)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum supra ostensum sit quod Deus sciat omnia non solum quae actu sunt, sed etiam quae sunt in potentia sua vel creaturae; horum autem quaedam sunt contingentia nobis futura; sequitur quod Deus contingentia futura cognoscat. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod contingens aliquod dupliciter potest considerari. Uno modo, in seipso, secundum quod iam actu est. Et sic non consideratur ut futurum, sed ut praesens, neque ut ad utrumlibet contingens, sed ut determinatum ad unum. Et propter hoc, sic infallibiliter subdi potest certae cognitioni, utpote sensui visus, sicut cum video Socratem sedere. Alio modo potest considerari contingens, ut est in sua causa. Et sic consideratur ut futurum, et ut contingens nondum determinatum ad unum, quia causa contingens se habet ad opposita. Et sic contingens non subditur per certitudinem alicui cognitioni. Unde quicumque cognoscit effectum contingentem in causa sua tantum, non habet de eo nisi coniecturalem cognitionem. Deus autem cognoscit omnia contingentia, non solum prout sunt in suis causis, sed etiam prout unumquodque eorum est actu in seipso. Et licet contingentia fiant in actu successive, non tamen Deus successive cognoscit contingentia, prout sunt in suo esse, sicut nos, sed simul. Quia sua cognitio mensuratur aeternitate, sicut etiam suum esse, aeternitas autem, tota simul existens, ambit totum tempus, ut supra dictum est. Unde omnia quae sunt in tempore, sunt Deo ab aeterno praesentia, non solum ea ratione qua habet rationes rerum apud se praesentes, ut quidam dicunt, sed quia eius intuitus fertur ab aeterno super omnia, prout sunt in sua praesentialitate. Unde manifestum est quod contingentia et infallibiliter a Deo cognoscuntur, inquantum subduntur divino conspectui secundum suam praesentialitatem, et tamen sunt futura contingentia, suis causis comparata.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28995)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet causa suprema sit necessaria, tamen effectus potest esse contingens, propter causam proximam contingentem, sicut germinatio plantae est contingens propter causam proximam contingentem, licet motus solis, qui est causa prima, sit necessarius. Et similiter scita a Deo sunt contingentia propter causas proximas, licet scientia Dei, quae est causa prima, sit necessaria.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28996)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod hoc antecedens, Deus scivit hoc contingens futurum, non est necessarium, sed contingens, quia, licet sit praeteritum, tamen importat respectum ad futurum. Sed hoc non tollit ei necessitatem, quia id quod habuit respectum ad futurum, necesse est habuisse, licet etiam futurum non sequatur quandoque. Alii vero dicunt hoc antecedens esse contingens, quia est compositum ex necessario et contingenti; sicut istud dictum est contingens, Socratem esse hominem album. Sed hoc etiam nihil est. Quia cum dicitur, Deus scivit esse futurum hoc contingens, contingens non ponitur ibi nisi ut materia verbi, et non sicut principalis pars propositionis, unde contingentia eius vel necessitas nihil refert ad hoc quod propositio sit necessaria vel contingens, vera vel falsa. Ita enim potest esse verum me dixisse hominem esse asinum, sicut me dixisse Socratem currere, vel Deum esse, et eadem ratio est de necessario et contingenti. Unde dicendum est quod hoc antecedens est necessarium absolute. Nec tamen sequitur, ut quidam dicunt, quod consequens sit necessarium absolute, quia antecedens est causa remota consequentis, quod propter causam proximam contingens est. Sed hoc nihil est. Esset enim conditionalis falsa, cuius antecedens esset causa remota necessaria, et consequens effectus contingens, ut puta si dicerem, si sol movetur, herba germinabit. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod quando in antecedente ponitur aliquid pertinens ad actum animae, consequens est accipiendum non secundum quod in se est, sed secundum quod est in anima, aliud enim est esse rei in seipsa, et esse rei in anima. Ut puta, si dicam, si anima intelligit aliquid, illud est immateriale, intelligendum est quod illud est immateriale secundum quod est in intellectu, non secundum quod est in seipso. Et similiter si dicam, si Deus scivit aliquid, illud erit, consequens intelligendum est prout subest divinae scientiae, scilicet prout est in sua praesentialitate. Et sic necessarium est, sicut et antecedens, *quia omne quod est, dum est, necesse est esse*, ut dicitur in I Periherm.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28997)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ea quae temporaliter in actum reducuntur, a nobis successive cognoscuntur in tempore, sed a Deo in aeternitate, quae est supra tempus. Unde nobis, quia cognoscimus futura contingentia inquantum talia sunt, certa esse non possunt, sed soli Deo, cuius intelligere est in aeternitate supra tempus. Sicut ille qui vadit per viam, non videt illos qui post eum veniunt, sed ille qui ab aliqua altitudine totam viam intuetur, simul videt omnes transeuntes per viam. Et ideo illud quod scitur a nobis, oportet esse necessarium etiam secundum quod in se est, quia ea quae in se sunt contingentia futura, a nobis sciri non possunt. Sed ea quae sunt scita a Deo, oportet esse necessaria secundum modum quo subsunt divinae scientiae, ut dictum est, non autem absolute, secundum quod in propriis causis considerantur. Unde et haec propositio, omne scitum a Deo necessarium est esse, consuevit distingui. Quia potest esse de re, vel de dicto. Si intelligatur de re, est divisa et falsa, et est sensus, omnis res quam Deus scit, est necessaria. Vel potest intelligi de dicto, et sic est composita et vera; et est sensus, hoc dictum, scitum a Deo esse, est necessarium. Sed obstant quidam, dicentes quod ista distinctio habet locum in formis separabilibus a subiecto; ut si dicam, album possibile est esse nigrum. Quae quidem de dicto est falsa, et de re est vera, res enim quae est alba, potest esse nigra; sed hoc dictum, album esse nigrum, nunquam potest esse verum. In formis autem inseparabilibus a subiecto, non habet locum praedicta distinctio; ut si dicam, corvum nigrum possibile est esse album, quia in utroque sensu est falsa. Esse autem scitum a Deo, est inseparabile a re, quia quod est scitum a Deo, non potest esse non scitum. Haec autem instantia locum haberet, si hoc quod dico scitum, importaret aliquam dispositionem subiecto inhaerentem. Sed cum importet actum scientis, ipsi rei scitae, licet semper sciatur, potest aliquid attribui secundum se, quod non attribuitur ei inquantum stat sub actu sciendi, sicut esse materiale attribuitur lapidi secundum se, quod non attribuitur ei secundum quod est intelligibile.\n\n## Articulus 14\n\n[I q. 14 a. 14 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28998)\n\nAd decimumquartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat enuntiabilia. Cognoscere enim enuntiabilia convenit intellectui nostro, secundum quod componit et dividit. Sed in intellectu divino nulla est compositio. Ergo Deus non cognoscit enuntiabilia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 14 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28999)\n\nPraeterea, omnis cognitio fit per aliquam similitudinem. Sed in Deo nulla est similitudo enuntiabilium, cum sit omnino simplex. Ergo Deus non cognoscit enuntiabilia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 14 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29000)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XCIII, *dominus scit cogitationes hominum*. Sed enuntiabilia continentur in cogitationibus hominum. Ergo Deus cognoscit enuntiabilia.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 14 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29001)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum formare enuntiabilia sit in potestate intellectus nostri; Deus autem scit quidquid est in potentia sua vel creaturae, ut supra dictum est; necesse est quod Deus sciat omnia enuntiabilia quae formari possunt. Sed, sicut scit materialia immaterialiter, et composita simpliciter, ita scit enuntiabilia non per modum enuntiabilium, quasi scilicet in intellectu eius sit compositio vel divisio enuntiabilium; sed unumquodque cognoscit per simplicem intelligentiam, intelligendo essentiam uniuscuiusque. Sicut si nos in hoc ipso quod intelligimus quid est homo, intelligeremus omnia quae de homine praedicari possunt. Quod quidem in intellectu nostro non contingit, qui de uno in aliud discurrit, propter hoc quod species intelligibilis sic repraesentat unum, quod non repraesentat aliud. Unde, intelligendo quid est homo, non ex hoc ipso alia quae ei insunt, intelligimus; sed divisim, secundum quandam successionem. Et propter hoc, ea quae seorsum intelligimus, oportet nos in unum redigere per modum compositionis vel divisionis, enuntiationem formando. Sed species intellectus divini, scilicet eius essentia, sufficit ad demonstrandum omnia. Unde, intelligendo essentiam suam, cognoscit essentias omnium, et quaecumque eis accidere possunt.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 14 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29002)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si Deus cognosceret enuntiabilia per modum enuntiabilium.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 14 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29003)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod compositio enuntiabilis significat aliquod esse rei, et sic Deus per suum esse, quod est eius essentia, est similitudo omnium eorum quae per enuntiabilia significantur.\n\n## Articulus 15\n\n[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29004)\n\nAd decimumquintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod scientia Dei sit variabilis. Scientia enim relative dicitur ad scibile. Sed ea quae important relationem ad creaturam, dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, et variantur secundum variationem creaturarum. Ergo scientia Dei est variabilis, secundum variationem creaturarum.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29005)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid potest Deus facere, potest scire. Sed Deus potest plura facere quam faciat. Ergo potest plura scire quam sciat. Et sic scientia sua potest variari secundum augmentum et diminutionem.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29006)\n\nPraeterea, Deus scivit Christum nasciturum. Nunc autem nescit Christum nasciturum, quia Christus nasciturus non est. Ergo non quidquid Deus scivit, scit. Et ita scientia Dei videtur esse variabilis.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 15 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29007)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Iac. I, quod *apud Deum non est transmutatio, neque vicissitudinis obumbratio*.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 15 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29008)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum scientia Dei sit eius substantia, ut ex dictis patet; sicut substantia eius est omnino immutabilis, ut supra ostensum est, ita oportet scientiam eius omnino invariabilem esse.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29009)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dominus et creator, et huiusmodi, important relationes ad creaturas secundum quod in seipsis sunt. Sed scientia Dei importat relationem ad creaturas secundum quod sunt in Deo, quia secundum hoc est unumquodque intellectum in actu quod est in intelligente. Res autem creatae sunt in Deo invariabiliter, in seipsis autem variabiliter. Vel aliter dicendum est, quod dominus et creator, et huiusmodi, important relationes quae consequuntur actus qui intelliguntur terminari ad ipsas creaturas secundum quod in seipsis sunt, et ideo huiusmodi relationes varie de Deo dicuntur, secundum variationem creaturarum. Sed scientia et amor, et huiusmodi, important relationes quae consequuntur actus qui intelliguntur in Deo esse, et ideo invariabiliter praedicantur de Deo.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29010)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus scit etiam ea quae potest facere et non facit. Unde ex hoc quod potest plura facere quam facit, non sequitur quod possit plura scire quam sciat, nisi hoc referatur ad scientiam visionis, secundum quam dicitur scire ea quae sunt in actu secundum aliquod tempus. Ex hoc tamen quod scit quod aliqua possunt esse quae non sunt, vel non esse quae sunt, non sequitur quod scientia sua sit variabilis, sed quod cognoscat rerum variabilitatem. Si tamen aliquid esset quod prius Deus nescivisset et postea sciret, esset eius scientia variabilis. Sed hoc esse non potest, quia quidquid est vel potest esse secundum aliquod tempus, Deus in aeterno suo scit. Et ideo ex hoc ipso quod ponitur aliquid esse secundum quodcumque tempus, oportet poni quod ab aeterno sit scitum a Deo. Et ideo non debet concedi quod Deus possit plura scire quam sciat, quia haec propositio implicat quod ante nesciverit et postea sciat.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29011)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod antiqui nominales dixerunt idem esse enuntiabile, Christum nasci, et esse nasciturum, et esse natum, quia eadem res significatur per haec tria, scilicet nativitas Christi. Et secundum hoc sequitur quod Deus quidquid scivit, sciat, quia modo scit Christum natum, quod significat idem ei quod est Christum esse nasciturum. Sed haec opinio falsa est. Tum quia diversitas partium orationis diversitatem enuntiabilium causat. Tum etiam quia sequeretur quod propositio quae semel est vera, esset semper vera, quod est contra philosophum, qui dicit quod haec oratio, Socrates sedet, vera est eo sedente, et eadem falsa est, eo surgente. Et ideo concedendum est quod haec non est vera, quidquid Deus scivit, scit, si ad enuntiabilia referatur. Sed ex hoc non sequitur quod scientia Dei sit variabilis. Sicut enim absque variatione divinae scientiae est, quod sciat unam et eandem rem quandoque esse et quandoque non esse; ita absque variatione divinae scientiae est, quod scit aliquod enuntiabile quandoque esse verum, et quandoque esse falsum. Esset autem ex hoc scientia Dei variabilis, si enuntiabilia cognosceret per modum enuntiabilium, componendo et dividendo, sicut accidit in intellectu nostro. Unde cognitio nostra variatur, vel secundum veritatem et falsitatem, puta si, mutata re, eandem opinionem de re illa retineamus, vel secundum diversas opiniones, ut si primo opinemur aliquem sedere, et postea opinemur eum non sedere. Quorum neutrum potest esse in Deo.\n\n## Articulus 16\n\n[I q. 14 a. 16 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29012)\n\nAd decimumsextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus de rebus non habeat scientiam speculativam. Scientia enim Dei est causa rerum, ut supra ostensum est. Sed scientia speculativa non est causa rerum scitarum. Ergo scientia Dei non est speculativa.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 16 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29013)\n\nPraeterea. Scientia speculativa est per abstractionem a rebus, quod divinae scientiae non competit. Ergo scientia Dei non est speculativa.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 16 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29014)\n\nSed contra, omne quod est nobilius, Deo est attribuendum. Sed scientia speculativa est nobilior quam practica, ut patet per philosophum, in principio Metaphys. Ergo Deus habet de rebus scientiam speculativam.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 16 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29015)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliqua scientia est speculativa tantum, aliqua practica tantum, aliqua vero secundum aliquid speculativa et secundum aliquid practica. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod aliqua scientia potest dici speculativa tripliciter. Primo, ex parte rerum scitarum, quae non sunt operabiles a sciente, sicut est scientia hominis de rebus naturalibus vel divinis. Secundo, quantum ad modum sciendi, ut puta si aedificator consideret domum definiendo et dividendo et considerando universalia praedicata ipsius. Hoc siquidem est operabilia modo speculativo considerare, et non secundum quod operabilia sunt, operabile enim est aliquid per applicationem formae ad materiam, non per resolutionem compositi in principia universalia formalia. Tertio, quantum ad finem, *nam intellectus practicus differt fine a speculativo*, sicut dicitur in III de anima. Intellectus enim practicus ordinatur ad finem operationis, finis autem intellectus speculativi est consideratio veritatis. Unde, si quis aedificator consideret qualiter posset fieri aliqua domus, non ordinans ad finem operationis, sed ad cognoscendum tantum, erit, quantum ad finem, speculativa consideratio, tamen de re operabili. Scientia igitur quae est speculativa ratione ipsius rei scitae, est speculativa tantum. Quae vero speculativa est vel secundum modum vel secundum finem, est secundum quid speculativa et secundum quid practica. Cum vero ordinatur ad finem operationis, est simpliciter practica. Secundum hoc ergo, dicendum est quod Deus de seipso habet scientiam speculativam tantum, ipse enim operabilis non est. De omnibus vero aliis habet scientiam et speculativam et practicam. Speculativam quidem, quantum ad modum, quidquid enim in rebus nos speculative cognoscimus definiendo et dividendo, hoc totum Deus multo perfectius novit. Sed de his quae potest quidem facere, sed secundum nullum tempus facit, non habet practicam scientiam, secundum quod practica scientia dicitur a fine. Sic autem habet practicam scientiam de his quae secundum aliquod tempus facit. Mala vero, licet ab eo non sint operabilia, tamen sub cognitione practica ipsius cadunt, sicut et bona, inquantum permittit vel impedit vel ordinat ea, sicut et aegritudines cadunt sub practica scientia medici, inquantum per artem suam curat eas.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29016)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod scientia Dei est causa, non quidem sui ipsius, sed aliorum, quorundam quidem actu, scilicet eorum quae secundum aliquod tempus fiunt; quorundam vero virtute, scilicet eorum quae potest facere, et tamen nunquam fiunt.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29017)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod scientiam esse acceptam a rebus scitis, non per se convenit scientiae speculativae, sed per accidens, inquantum est humana.\n\n[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29018)\n\nAd id vero quod in contrarium obiicitur, dicendum quod de operabilibus perfecta scientia non habetur, nisi sciantur inquantum operabilia sunt. Et ideo, cum scientia Dei sit omnibus modis perfecta, oportet quod sciat ea quae sunt a se operabilia, inquantum huiusmodi, et non solum secundum quod sunt speculabilia. Sed tamen non receditur a nobilitate speculativae scientiae, quia omnia alia a se videt in seipso, seipsum autem speculative cognoscit; et sic in speculativa sui ipsius scientia, habet cognitionem et speculativam et practicam omnium aliorum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0002","text":"[I q. 14 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0003","text":"Post considerationem eorum quae ad divinam substantiam pertinent, restat considerandum de his quae pertinent ad operationem ipsius. Et quia operatio quaedam est quae manet in operante, quaedam vero quae procedit in exteriorem effectum, primo agemus de scientia et voluntate (nam intelligere in intelligente est, et velle in volente); et postmodum de potentia Dei, quae consideratur ut principium operationis divinae in effectum exteriorem procedentis. Quia vero intelligere quoddam vivere est, post considerationem divinae scientiae, considerandum erit de vita divina. Et quia scientia verorum est, erit etiam considerandum de veritate et falsitate. Rursum, quia omne cognitum in cognoscente est, rationes autem rerum secundum quod sunt in Deo cognoscente, ideae vocantur, cum consideratione scientiae erit etiam adiungenda consideratio de ideis. Circa scientiam vero quaeruntur sexdecim.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004","text":"Primo, utrum in Deo sit scientia. Secundo, utrum Deus intelligat seipsum. Tertio, utrum comprehendat se. Quarto, utrum suum intelligere sit sua substantia. Quinto, utrum intelligat alia a se. Sexto, utrum habeat de eis propriam cognitionem. Septimo, utrum scientia Dei sit discursiva. Octavo, utrum scientia Dei sit causa rerum. Nono, utrum scientia Dei sit eorum quae non sunt. Decimo, utrum sit malorum. Undecimo, utrum sit singularium. Duodecimo, utrum sit infinitorum. Decimotertio, utrum sit contingentium futurorum. Decimoquarto, utrum sit enuntiabilium. Decimoquinto, utrum scientia Dei sit variabilis. Decimosexto, utrum Deus de rebus habeat speculativam scientiam vel practicam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0006","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit scientia. Scientia enim habitus est, qui Deo non competit, cum sit medius inter potentiam et actum. Ergo scientia non est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0008","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0009","text":"Praeterea, scientia, cum sit conclusionum, est quaedam cognitio ab alio causata, scilicet ex cognitione principiorum. Sed nihil causatum est in Deo. Ergo scientia non est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0010","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0011","text":"Praeterea, omnis scientia vel universalis vel particularis est. Sed in Deo non est universale et particulare, ut ex superioribus patet; ergo in Deo non est scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0012","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0013","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit Rom. XI, *o altitudo divitiarum sapientiae et scientiae Dei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0014","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in Deo perfectissime est scientia. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod cognoscentia a non cognoscentibus in hoc distinguuntur, quia non cognoscentia nihil habent nisi formam suam tantum; sed cognoscens natum est habere formam etiam rei alterius, nam species cogniti est in cognoscente. Unde manifestum est quod natura rei non cognoscentis est magis coarctata et limitata, natura autem rerum cognoscentium habet maiorem amplitudinem et extensionem. Propter quod dicit philosophus, III de anima, quod *anima est quodammodo omnia*. Coarctatio autem formae est per materiam. Unde et supra diximus quod formae, secundum quod sunt magis immateriales, secundum hoc magis accedunt ad quandam infinitatem. Patet igitur quod immaterialitas alicuius rei est ratio quod sit cognoscitiva; et secundum modum immaterialitatis est modus cognitionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","text":"Unde in II de anima dicitur quod plantae non cognoscunt, propter suam materialitatem. Sensus autem cognoscitivus est, quia receptivus est specierum sine materia, et intellectus adhuc magis cognoscitivus, quia magis separatus est a materia et immixtus, ut dicitur in III de anima. Unde, cum Deus sit in summo immaterialitatis, ut ex superioribus patet, sequitur quod ipse sit in summo cognitionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0017","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, quia perfectiones procedentes a Deo in creaturas, altiori modo sunt in Deo, ut supra dictum est, oportet quod, quandocumque aliquod nomen sumptum a quacumque perfectione creaturae Deo attribuitur, secludatur ab eius significatione omne illud quod pertinet ad imperfectum modum qui competit creaturae. Unde scientia non est qualitas in Deo vel habitus, sed substantia et actus purus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0019","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ea quae sunt divisim et multipliciter in creaturis, in Deo sunt simpliciter et unite, ut supra dictum est. Homo autem, secundum diversa cognita, habet diversas cognitiones, nam secundum quod cognoscit principia, dicitur habere intelligentiam; scientiam vero, secundum quod cognoscit conclusiones; sapientiam, secundum quod cognoscit causam altissimam; consilium vel prudentiam, secundum quod cognoscit agibilia. Sed haec omnia Deus una et simplici cognitione cognoscit, ut infra patebit. Unde simplex Dei cognitio omnibus istis nominibus nominari potest, ita tamen quod ab unoquoque eorum, secundum quod in divinam praedicationem venit, secludatur quidquid imperfectionis est, et retineatur quidquid perfectionis est. Et secundum hoc dicitur Iob XII, *apud ipsum est sapientia et fortitudo; ipse habet consilium et intelligentiam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0021","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod scientia est secundum modum cognoscentis, scitum enim est in sciente secundum modum scientis. Et ideo, cum modus divinae essentiae sit altior quam modus quo creaturae sunt, scientia divina non habet modum creatae scientiae, ut scilicet sit universalis vel particularis, vel in habitu vel in potentia, vel secundum aliquem talem modum disposita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0024","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non intelligat se. Dicitur enim in libro de causis, quod *omnis sciens qui scit suam essentiam, est rediens ad essentiam suam reditione completa*. Sed Deus non exit extra essentiam suam, nec aliquo modo movetur, et sic non competit sibi redire ad essentiam suam. Ergo ipse non est sciens essentiam suam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0026","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0027","text":"Praeterea, intelligere est quoddam pati et moveri, ut dicitur in III de anima, scientia etiam est assimilatio ad rem scitam, et scitum etiam est perfectio scientis. Sed nihil movetur, vel patitur, vel perficitur a seipso; neque similitudo sibi est, ut Hilarius dicit. Ergo Deus non est sciens seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0028","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0029","text":"Praeterea, praecipue Deo sumus similes secundum intellectum, quia secundum mentem sumus ad imaginem Dei, ut dicit Augustinus. Sed intellectus noster non intelligit se, nisi sicut intelligit alia, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo nec Deus intelligit se, nisi forte intelligendo alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0030","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur I ad Cor. II, *quae sunt Dei, nemo novit nisi spiritus Dei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0032","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus se per seipsum intelligit. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod, licet in operationibus quae transeunt in exteriorem effectum, obiectum operationis, quod significatur ut terminus, sit aliquid extra operantem; tamen in operationibus quae sunt in operante, obiectum quod significatur ut terminus operationis, est in ipso operante; et secundum quod est in eo, sic est operatio in actu. Unde dicitur in libro de anima, quod sensibile in actu est sensus in actu, et intelligibile in actu est intellectus in actu. Ex hoc enim aliquid in actu sentimus vel intelligimus, quod intellectus noster vel sensus informatur in actu per speciem sensibilis vel intelligibilis. Et secundum hoc tantum sensus vel intellectus aliud est a sensibili vel intelligibili, quia utrumque est in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","text":"Cum igitur Deus nihil potentialitatis habeat, sed sit actus purus, oportet quod in eo intellectus et intellectum sint idem omnibus modis, ita scilicet, ut neque careat specie intelligibili, sicut intellectus noster cum intelligit in potentia; neque species intelligibilis sit aliud a substantia intellectus divini, sicut accidit in intellectu nostro, cum est actu intelligens; sed ipsa species intelligibilis est ipse intellectus divinus. Et sic seipsum per seipsum intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0035","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod redire ad essentiam suam nihil aliud est quam rem subsistere in seipsa. Forma enim, inquantum perficit materiam dando ei esse, quodammodo supra ipsam effunditur, inquantum vero in seipsa habet esse, in seipsam redit. Virtutes igitur cognoscitivae quae non sunt subsistentes, sed actus aliquorum organorum, non cognoscunt seipsas; sicut patet in singulis sensibus. Sed virtutes cognoscitivae per se subsistentes, cognoscunt seipsas. Et propter hoc dicitur in libro de causis, quod *sciens essentiam suam, redit ad essentiam suam*. Per se autem subsistere maxime convenit Deo. Unde secundum hunc modum loquendi, ipse est maxime rediens ad essentiam suam, et cognoscens seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0037","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod moveri et pati sumuntur aequivoce secundum quod intelligere dicitur esse quoddam moveri vel pati, ut dicitur in III de anima. Non enim intelligere est motus qui est actus imperfecti, qui est ab alio in aliud, sed actus perfecti, existens in ipso agente. Similiter etiam quod intellectus perficiatur ab intelligibili vel assimiletur ei, hoc convenit intellectui qui quandoque est in potentia, quia per hoc quod est in potentia, differt ab intelligibili, et assimilatur ei per speciem intelligibilem, quae est similitudo rei intellectae; et perficitur per ipsam, sicut potentia per actum. Sed intellectus divinus, qui nullo modo est in potentia, non perficitur per intelligibile, neque assimilatur ei, sed est sua perfectio et suum intelligibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0039","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod esse naturale non est materiae primae, quae est in potentia, nisi secundum quod est reducta in actum per formam. Intellectus autem noster possibilis se habet in ordine intelligibilium, sicut materia prima in ordine rerum naturalium, eo quod est in potentia ad intelligibilia, sicut materia prima ad naturalia. Unde intellectus noster possibilis non potest habere intelligibilem operationem, nisi inquantum perficitur per speciem intelligibilem alicuius. Et sic intelligit seipsum per speciem intelligibilem, sicut et alia, manifestum est enim quod ex eo quod cognoscit intelligibile, intelligit ipsum suum intelligere, et per actum cognoscit potentiam intellectivam. Deus autem est sicut actus purus tam in ordine existentium, quam in ordine intelligibilium, et ideo per seipsum, seipsum intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0042","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non comprehendat seipsum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod id quod comprehendit se, finitum est sibi. Sed Deus est omnibus modis infinitus. Ergo non comprehendit se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0044","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0045","text":"Si dicatur quod Deus infinitus est nobis, sed sibi finitus, contra, verius est unumquodque secundum quod est apud Deum, quam secundum quod est apud nos. Si igitur Deus sibi ipsi est finitus, nobis autem infinitus, verius est Deum esse finitum, quam infinitum. Quod est contra prius determinata. Non ergo Deus comprehendit seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0046","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0047","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit ibidem, *omne quod intelligit se, comprehendit se*. Sed Deus intelligit se. Ergo comprehendit se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0048","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus perfecte comprehendit seipsum. Quod sic patet. Tunc enim dicitur aliquid comprehendi, quando pervenitur ad finem cognitionis ipsius, et hoc est quando res cognoscitur ita perfecte, sicut cognoscibilis est. Sicut propositio demonstrabilis comprehenditur, quando scitur per demonstrationem, non autem quando cognoscitur per aliquam rationem probabilem. Manifestum est autem quod Deus ita perfecte cognoscit seipsum, sicut perfecte cognoscibilis est. Est enim unumquodque cognoscibile secundum modum sui actus, non enim cognoscitur aliquid secundum quod in potentia est, sed secundum quod est in actu, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Tanta est autem virtus Dei in cognoscendo, quanta est actualitas eius in existendo, quia per hoc quod actu est, et ab omni materia et potentia separatus, Deus cognoscitivus est, ut ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0050","text":"Unde manifestum est quod tantum seipsum cognoscit, quantum cognoscibilis est. Et propter hoc seipsum perfecte comprehendit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0051","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0052","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod comprehendere, si proprie accipiatur, significat aliquid habens et includens alterum. Et sic oportet quod omne comprehensum sit finitum, sicut omne inclusum. Non sic autem comprehendi dicitur Deus a seipso, ut intellectus suus sit aliud quam ipse, et capiat ipsum et includat. Sed huiusmodi locutiones per negationem sunt exponendae. Sicut enim Deus dicitur esse in seipso, quia a nullo exteriori continetur; ita dicitur comprehendi a seipso, quia nihil est sui quod lateat ipsum dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de videndo Deum, quod *totum comprehenditur videndo, quod ita videtur, ut nihil eius lateat videntem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0053","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0054","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur Deus finitus est sibi, intelligendum est secundum quandam similitudinem proportionis; quia sic se habet in non excedendo intellectum suum, sicut se habet aliquod finitum in non excedendo intellectum finitum. Non autem sic dicitur Deus sibi finitus, quod ipse intelligat se esse aliquid finitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0055","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0056","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0057","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ipsum intelligere Dei non sit eius substantia. Intelligere enim est quaedam operatio. Operatio autem aliquid significat procedens ab operante. Ergo ipsum intelligere Dei non est ipsa Dei substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0058","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0059","text":"Praeterea, cum aliquis intelligit se intelligere, hoc non est intelligere aliquid magnum vel principale intellectum, sed intelligere quoddam secundarium et accessorium. Si igitur Deus sit ipsum intelligere, intelligere Deum erit sicut cum intelligimus intelligere. Et sic non erit aliquid magnum intelligere Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0060","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0061","text":"Praeterea, omne intelligere est aliquid intelligere. Cum ergo Deus intelligit se, si ipsemet non est aliud quam suum intelligere, intelligit se intelligere, et intelligere se intelligere se, et sic in infinitum. Non ergo ipsum intelligere Dei est eius substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0062","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0063","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, Lib. VII de Trin., *Deo hoc est esse, quod sapientem esse*. Hoc autem est sapientem esse, quod intelligere. Ergo Deo hoc est esse, quod intelligere. Sed esse Dei est eius substantia, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo intelligere Dei est eius substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0064","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0065","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod est necesse dicere quod intelligere Dei est eius substantia. Nam si intelligere Dei sit aliud quam eius substantia, oporteret, ut dicit philosophus in XII Metaphys., quod aliquid aliud esset actus et perfectio substantiae divinae, ad quod se haberet substantia divina sicut potentia ad actum (quod est omnino impossibile), nam intelligere est perfectio et actus intelligentis. Hoc autem qualiter sit, considerandum est. Sicut enim supra dictum est, intelligere non est actio progrediens ad aliquid extrinsecum, sed manet in operante sicut actus et perfectio eius, prout esse est perfectio existentis, sicut enim esse consequitur formam, ita intelligere sequitur speciem intelligibilem. In Deo autem non est forma quae sit aliud quam suum esse, ut supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","text":"Unde, cum ipsa sua essentia sit etiam species intelligibilis, ut dictum est, ex necessitate sequitur quod ipsum eius intelligere sit eius essentia et eius esse. Et sic patet ex omnibus praemissis quod in Deo intellectus, et id quod intelligitur, et species intelligibilis, et ipsum intelligere, sunt omnino unum et idem. Unde patet quod per hoc quod Deus dicitur intelligens, nulla multiplicitas ponitur in eius substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0067","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0068","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intelligere non est operatio exiens ab ipso operante, sed manens in ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0069","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0070","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum intelligitur illud intelligere quod non est subsistens, non intelligitur aliquid magnum; sicut cum intelligimus intelligere nostrum. Et ideo non est simile de ipso intelligere divino, quod est subsistens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0071","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0072","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad tertium. Nam intelligere divinum, quod est in seipso subsistens, est sui ipsius; et non alicuius alterius, ut sic oporteat procedere in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0073","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0074","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0075","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat alia a se. Quaecumque enim sunt alia a Deo, sunt extra ipsum. Sed Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium qu., quod *neque quidquam Deus extra seipsum intuetur*. Ergo non cognoscit alia a se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0076","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0077","text":"Praeterea, intellectum est perfectio intelligentis. Si ergo Deus intelligat alia a se, aliquid aliud erit perfectio Dei, et nobilius ipso. Quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0078","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0079","text":"Praeterea, ipsum intelligere speciem habet ab intelligibili, sicut et omnis alius actus a suo obiecto, unde et ipsum intelligere tanto est nobilius, quanto etiam nobilius est ipsum quod intelligitur. Sed Deus est ipsum suum intelligere, ut ex dictis patet. Si igitur Deus intelligit aliquid aliud a se, ipse Deus specificatur per aliquid aliud a se, quod est impossibile. Non igitur intelligit alia a se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0080","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0081","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Hebr. IV, *omnia nuda et aperta sunt oculis eius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0082","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est Deum cognoscere alia a se. Manifestum est enim quod seipsum perfecte intelligit, alioquin suum esse non esset perfectum, cum suum esse sit suum intelligere. Si autem perfecte aliquid cognoscitur, necesse est quod virtus eius perfecte cognoscatur. Virtus autem alicuius rei perfecte cognosci non potest, nisi cognoscantur ea ad quae virtus se extendit. Unde, cum virtus divina se extendat ad alia, eo quod ipsa est prima causa effectiva omnium entium, ut ex supradictis patet; necesse est quod Deus alia a se cognoscat. Et hoc etiam evidentius fit, si adiungatur quod ipsum esse causae agentis primae, scilicet Dei, est eius intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0084","text":"Unde quicumque effectus praeexistunt in Deo sicut in causa prima, necesse est quod sint in ipso eius intelligere; et quod omnia in eo sint secundum modum intelligibilem, nam omne quod est in altero, est in eo secundum modum eius in quo est. Ad sciendum autem qualiter alia a se cognoscat, considerandum est quod dupliciter aliquid cognoscitur, uno modo, in seipso; alio modo, in altero. In seipso quidem cognoscitur aliquid, quando cognoscitur per speciem propriam adaequatam ipsi cognoscibili, sicut cum oculus videt hominem per speciem hominis. In alio autem videtur id quod videtur per speciem continentis, sicut cum pars videtur in toto per speciem totius, vel cum homo videtur in speculo per speciem speculi, vel quocumque alio modo contingat aliquid in alio videri. Sic igitur dicendum est quod Deus seipsum videt in seipso, quia seipsum videt per essentiam suam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","text":"Alia autem a se videt non in ipsis, sed in seipso, inquantum essentia sua continet similitudinem aliorum ab ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0086","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0087","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Augustini dicentis quod Deus nihil extra se intuetur, non est sic intelligendum, quasi nihil quod sit extra se intueatur, sed quia id quod est extra seipsum, non intuetur nisi in seipso, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0088","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectum est perfectio intelligentis non quidem secundum suam substantiam, sed secundum suam speciem, secundum quam est in intellectu, ut forma et perfectio eius, *lapis enim non est in anima, sed species eius*, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ea vero quae sunt alia a Deo, intelliguntur a Deo inquantum essentia Dei continet species eorum, ut dictum est. Unde non sequitur quod aliquid aliud sit perfectio divini intellectus, quam ipsa essentia Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0090","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ipsum intelligere non specificatur per id quod in alio intelligitur, sed per principale intellectum, in quo alia intelliguntur. Intantum enim ipsum intelligere specificatur per obiectum suum, inquantum forma intelligibilis est principium intellectualis operationis, nam omnis operatio specificatur per formam quae est principium operationis, sicut calefactio per calorem. Unde per illam formam intelligibilem specificatur intellectualis operatio, quae facit intellectum in actu. Et haec est species principalis intellecti, quae in Deo nihil est aliud quam essentia sua, in qua omnes species rerum comprehenduntur. Unde non oportet quod ipsum intelligere divinum, vel potius ipse Deus, specificetur per aliud quam per essentiam divinam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0092","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0093","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0094","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat alia a se propria cognitione. Sic enim cognoscit alia a se, ut dictum est, secundum quod alia ab ipso in eo sunt. Sed alia ab eo sunt in ipso sicut in prima causa communi et universali. Ergo et alia cognoscuntur a Deo, sicut in causa prima et universali. Hoc autem est cognoscere in universali, et non secundum propriam cognitionem. Ergo Deus cognoscit alia a se in universali, et non secundum propriam cognitionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0095","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0096","text":"Praeterea, quantum distat essentia creaturae ab essentia divina, tantum distat essentia divina ab essentia creaturae. Sed per essentiam creaturae non potest cognosci essentia divina, ut supra dictum est. Ergo nec per essentiam divinam potest cognosci essentia creaturae. Et sic, cum Deus nihil cognoscat nisi per essentiam suam, sequitur quod non cognoscat creaturam secundum eius essentiam, ut cognoscat de ea quid est, quod est propriam cognitionem de re habere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0097","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0098","text":"Praeterea, propria cognitio non habetur de re, nisi per propriam eius rationem. Sed cum Deus cognoscat omnia per essentiam suam, non videtur quod unumquodque per propriam rationem cognoscat, idem enim non potest esse propria ratio multorum et diversorum. Non ergo habet propriam cognitionem Deus de rebus, sed communem, nam cognoscere res non secundum propriam rationem, est cognoscere res solum in communi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0099","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0100","text":"Sed contra, habere propriam cognitionem de rebus, est cognoscere res non solum in communi, sed secundum quod sunt ab invicem distinctae. Sic autem Deus cognoscit res. Unde dicitur Heb. IV, quod *pertingit usque ad divisionem spiritus et animae, compagum quoque et medullarum; et discretor cogitationum et intentionum cordis; et non est ulla creatura invisibilis in conspectu eius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0101","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0102","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc quidam erraverunt, dicentes quod Deus alia a se non cognoscit nisi in communi, scilicet inquantum sunt entia. Sicut enim ignis, si cognosceret seipsum ut est principium caloris, cognosceret naturam caloris, et omnia alia inquantum sunt calida; ita Deus, inquantum cognoscit se ut principium essendi, cognoscit naturam entis, et omnia alia inquantum sunt entia. Sed hoc non potest esse. Nam intelligere aliquid in communi, et non in speciali, est imperfecte aliquid cognoscere. Unde intellectus noster, dum de potentia in actum reducitur, pertingit prius ad cognitionem universalem et confusam de rebus, quam ad propriam rerum cognitionem, sicut de imperfecto ad perfectum procedens, ut patet in I Physic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0103","text":"Si igitur cognitio Dei de rebus aliis a se, esset in universali tantum, et non in speciali, sequeretur quod eius intelligere non esset omnibus modis perfectum, et per consequens nec eius esse, quod est contra ea quae superius ostensa sunt. Oportet igitur dicere quod alia a se cognoscat propria cognitione; non solum secundum quod communicant in ratione entis, sed secundum quod unum ab alio distinguitur. Et ad huius evidentiam, considerandum est quod quidam, volentes ostendere quod Deus per unum cognoscit multa, utuntur quibusdam exemplis, ut puta quod, si centrum cognosceret seipsum, cognosceret omnes lineas progredientes a centro; vel lux, si cognosceret seipsam, cognosceret omnes colores.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0104","text":"Sed haec exempla, licet quantum ad aliquid similia sint, scilicet quantum ad universalem causalitatem; tamen deficiunt quantum ad hoc, quod multitudo et diversitas non causantur ab illo uno principio universali, quantum ad id quod principium distinctionis est, sed solum quantum ad id in quo communicant. Non enim diversitas colorum causatur ex luce solum, sed ex diversa dispositione diaphani recipientis, et similiter diversitas linearum ex diverso situ. Et inde est quod huiusmodi diversitas et multitudo non potest cognosci in suo principio secundum propriam cognitionem, sed solum in communi. Sed in Deo non sic est. Supra enim ostensum est quod quidquid perfectionis est in quacumque creatura, totum praeexistit et continetur in Deo secundum modum excellentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0105","text":"Non solum autem id in quo creaturae communicant, scilicet ipsum esse, ad perfectionem pertinet; sed etiam ea per quae creaturae ad invicem distinguuntur, sicut vivere, et intelligere, et huiusmodi, quibus viventia a non viventibus, et intelligentia a non intelligentibus distinguuntur. Et omnis forma, per quam quaelibet res in propria specie constituitur, perfectio quaedam est. Et sic omnia in Deo praeexistunt, non solum quantum ad id quod commune est omnibus, sed etiam quantum ad ea secundum quae res distinguuntur. Et sic, cum Deus in se omnes perfectiones contineat, comparatur Dei essentia ad omnes rerum essentias, non sicut commune ad propria, ut unitas ad numeros, vel centrum ad lineas; sed sicut perfectus actus ad imperfectos, ut si dicerem, homo ad animal, vel senarius, qui est numerus perfectus, ad numeros imperfectos sub ipso contentos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","text":"Manifestum est autem quod per actum perfectum cognosci possunt actus imperfecti, non solum in communi, sed etiam propria cognitione. Sicut qui cognoscit hominem, cognoscit animal propria cognitione, et qui cognoscit senarium, cognoscit trinarium propria cognitione. Sic igitur, cum essentia Dei habeat in se quidquid perfectionis habet essentia cuiuscumque rei alterius, et adhuc amplius, Deus in seipso potest omnia propria cognitione cognoscere. Propria enim natura uniuscuiusque consistit, secundum quod per aliquem modum divinam perfectionem participat. Non autem Deus perfecte seipsum cognosceret, nisi cognosceret quomodocumque participabilis est ab aliis sua perfectio, nec etiam ipsam naturam essendi perfecte sciret, nisi cognosceret omnes modos essendi. Unde manifestum est quod Deus cognoscit omnes res propria cognitione, secundum quod ab aliis distinguuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0107","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0108","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sic cognoscere aliquid sicut in cognoscente est, potest dupliciter intelligi. Uno modo, secundum quod hoc adverbium sic importat modum cognitionis ex parte rei cognitae. Et sic falsum est. Non enim semper cognoscens cognoscit cognitum secundum illud esse quod habet in cognoscente, oculus enim non cognoscit lapidem secundum esse quod habet in oculo; sed per speciem lapidis quam habet in se, cognoscit lapidem secundum esse quod habet extra oculum. Et si aliquis cognoscens cognoscat cognitum secundum esse quod habet in cognoscente, nihilominus cognoscit ipsum secundum esse quod habet extra cognoscentem, sicut intellectus cognoscit lapidem secundum esse intelligibile quod habet in intellectu, inquantum cognoscit se intelligere; sed nihilominus cognoscit esse lapidis in propria natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0109","text":"Si vero intelligatur secundum quod hoc adverbium sic importat modum ex parte cognoscentis, verum est quod sic solum cognoscens cognoscit cognitum, secundum quod est in cognoscente, quia quanto perfectius est cognitum in cognoscente, tanto perfectior est modus cognitionis. Sic igitur dicendum est quod Deus non solum cognoscit res esse in seipso; sed per id quod in seipso continet res, cognoscit eas in propria natura; et tanto perfectius, quanto perfectius est unumquodque in ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0110","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0111","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod essentia creaturae comparatur ad essentiam Dei, ut actus imperfectus ad perfectum. Et ideo essentia creaturae non sufficienter ducit in cognitionem essentiae divinae, sed e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0112","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod idem non potest accipi ut ratio diversorum per modum adaequationis. Sed divina essentia est aliquid excedens omnes creaturas. Unde potest accipi ut propria ratio uniuscuiusque, secundum quod diversimode est participabilis vel imitabilis a diversis creaturis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0114","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0115","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0116","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod scientia Dei sit discursiva. Scientia enim Dei non est secundum scire in habitu, sed secundum intelligere in actu. Sed secundum philosophum, in II Topic., scire in habitu contingit multa simul, sed intelligere actu unum tantum. Cum ergo Deus multa cognoscat, quia et se et alia, ut ostensum est, videtur quod non simul omnia intelligat, sed de uno in aliud discurrat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0117","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0118","text":"Praeterea, cognoscere effectum per causam est scire discurrentis. Sed Deus cognoscit alia per seipsum, sicut effectum per causam. Ergo cognitio sua est discursiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0119","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0120","text":"Praeterea, perfectius Deus scit unamquamque creaturam quam nos sciamus. Sed nos in causis creatis cognoscimus earum effectus, et sic de causis ad causata discurrimus. Ergo videtur similiter esse in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0121","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0122","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in XV de Trin., quod *Deus non particulatim vel singillatim omnia videt, velut alternante conspectu hinc illuc, et inde huc; sed omnia videt simul*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0123","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in scientia divina nullus est discursus. Quod sic patet. In scientia enim nostra duplex est discursus. Unus secundum successionem tantum, sicut cum, postquam intelligimus aliquid in actu, convertimus nos ad intelligendum aliud. Alius discursus est secundum causalitatem, sicut cum per principia pervenimus in cognitionem conclusionum. Primus autem discursus Deo convenire non potest. Multa enim, quae successive intelligimus si unumquodque eorum in seipso consideretur, omnia simul intelligimus si in aliquo uno ea intelligamus, puta si partes intelligamus in toto, vel si diversas res videamus in speculo. Deus autem omnia videt in uno, quod est ipse, ut habitum est. Unde simul, et non successive omnia videt. Similiter etiam et secundus discursus Deo competere non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125","text":"Primo quidem, quia secundus discursus praesupponit primum, procedentes enim a principiis ad conclusiones, non simul utrumque considerant. Deinde, quia discursus talis est procedentis de noto ad ignotum. Unde manifestum est quod, quando cognoscitur primum, adhuc ignoratur secundum. Et sic secundum non cognoscitur in primo, sed ex primo. Terminus vero discursus est, quando secundum videtur in primo, resolutis effectibus in causas, et tunc cessat discursus. Unde, cum Deus effectus suos in seipso videat sicut in causa, eius cognitio non est discursiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0126","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet sit unum tantum intelligere in seipso, tamen contingit multa intelligere in aliquo uno, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0128","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0129","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus non cognoscit per causam quasi prius cognitam, effectus incognitos, sed eos cognoscit in causa. Unde eius cognitio est sine discursu, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0130","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0131","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod effectus causarum creatarum videt quidem Deus in ipsis causis, multo melius quam nos, non tamen ita quod cognitio effectuum causetur in ipso ex cognitione causarum creatarum, sicut in nobis. Unde eius scientia non est discursiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0132","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0133","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0134","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod scientia Dei non sit causa rerum. Dicit enim Origenes, super epistolam ad Rom., *non propterea aliquid erit, quia id scit Deus futurum; sed quia futurum est, ideo scitur a Deo antequam fiat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0135","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0136","text":"Praeterea, posita causa ponitur effectus. Sed scientia Dei est aeterna. Si ergo scientia Dei est causa rerum creatarum, videtur quod creaturae sint ab aeterno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0137","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0138","text":"Praeterea, scibile est prius scientia, et mensura eius, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sed id quod est posterius et mensuratum, non potest esse causa. Ergo scientia Dei non est causa rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0139","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0140","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XV de Trin., *universas creaturas, et spirituales et corporales, non quia sunt, ideo novit Deus; sed ideo sunt, quia novit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0141","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod scientia Dei est causa rerum. Sic enim scientia Dei se habet ad omnes res creatas, sicut scientia artificis se habet ad artificiata. Scientia autem artificis est causa artificiatorum, eo quod artifex operatur per suum intellectum, unde oportet quod forma intellectus sit principium operationis, sicut calor est principium calefactionis. Sed considerandum est quod forma naturalis, inquantum est forma manens in eo cui dat esse, non nominat principium actionis; sed secundum quod habet inclinationem ad effectum. Et similiter forma intelligibilis non nominat principium actionis secundum quod est tantum in intelligente, nisi adiungatur ei inclinatio ad effectum, quae est per voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","text":"Cum enim forma intelligibilis ad opposita se habeat (cum sit eadem scientia oppositorum), non produceret determinatum effectum, nisi determinaretur ad unum per appetitum, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Manifestum est autem quod Deus per intellectum suum causat res, cum suum esse sit suum intelligere. Unde necesse est quod sua scientia sit causa rerum, secundum quod habet voluntatem coniunctam. Unde scientia Dei, secundum quod est causa rerum, consuevit nominari scientia approbationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0144","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0145","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Origenes locutus est attendens rationem scientiae, cui non competit ratio causalitatis, nisi adiuncta voluntate, ut dictum est. Sed quod dicit ideo praescire Deum aliqua, quia sunt futura, intelligendum est secundum causam consequentiae, non secundum causam essendi. Sequitur enim, si aliqua sunt futura, quod Deus ea praescierit, non tamen res futurae sunt causa quod Deus sciat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0146","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0147","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod scientia Dei est causa rerum, secundum quod res sunt in scientia. Non fuit autem in scientia Dei, quod res essent ab aeterno. Unde, quamvis scientia Dei sit aeterna, non sequitur tamen quod creaturae sint ab aeterno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0148","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod res naturales sunt mediae inter scientiam Dei et scientiam nostram, nos enim scientiam accipimus a rebus naturalibus, quarum Deus per suam scientiam causa est. Unde, sicut scibilia naturalia sunt priora quam scientia nostra, et mensura eius, ita scientia Dei est prior quam res naturales, et mensura ipsarum. Sicut aliqua domus est media inter scientiam artificis qui eam fecit, et scientiam illius qui eius cognitionem ex ipsa iam facta capit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0150","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0151","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0152","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non habeat scientiam non entium. Scientia enim Dei non est nisi verorum. Sed verum et ens convertuntur. Ergo scientia Dei non est non entium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0153","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0154","text":"Praeterea, scientia requirit similitudinem inter scientem et scitum. Sed ea quae non sunt, non possunt habere aliquam similitudinem ad Deum, qui est ipsum esse. Ergo ea quae non sunt, non possunt sciri a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0155","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0156","text":"Praeterea, scientia Dei est causa scitorum ab ipso. Sed non est causa non entium, quia non ens non habet causam. Ergo Deus non habet scientiam de non entibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0157","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0158","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit apostolus ad Rom. IV, *qui vocat ea quae non sunt, tanquam ea quae sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0159","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus scit omnia quaecumque sunt quocumque modo. Nihil autem prohibet ea quae non sunt simpliciter, aliquo modo esse. Simpliciter enim sunt, quae actu sunt. Ea vero quae non sunt actu, sunt in potentia vel ipsius Dei, vel creaturae; sive in potentia activa, sive in passiva, sive in potentia opinandi, vel imaginandi, vel quocumque modo significandi. Quaecumque igitur possunt per creaturam fieri vel cogitari vel dici, et etiam quaecumque ipse facere potest, omnia cognoscit Deus, etiam si actu non sint. Et pro tanto dici potest quod habet etiam non entium scientiam. Sed horum quae actu non sunt, est attendenda quaedam diversitas. Quaedam enim, licet non sint nunc in actu, tamen vel fuerunt vel erunt, et omnia ista dicitur Deus scire scientia visionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","text":"Quia, cum intelligere Dei, quod est eius esse, aeternitate mensuretur, quae sine successione existens totum tempus comprehendit, praesens intuitus Dei fertur in totum tempus, et in omnia quae sunt in quocumque tempore, sicut in subiecta sibi praesentialiter. Quaedam vero sunt, quae sunt in potentia Dei vel creaturae, quae tamen nec sunt nec erunt neque fuerunt. Et respectu horum non dicitur habere scientiam visionis, sed simplicis intelligentiae. Quod ideo dicitur, quia ea quae videntur apud nos, habent esse distinctum extra videntem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0162","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0163","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum quod sunt in potentia, sic habent veritatem ea quae non sunt actu, verum est enim ea esse in potentia. Et sic sciuntur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0164","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0165","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum Deus sit ipsum esse, intantum unumquodque est, inquantum participat de Dei similitudine, sicut unumquodque intantum est calidum, inquantum participat calorem. Sic et ea quae sunt in potentia, etiam si non sunt in actu, cognoscuntur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0166","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Dei scientia est causa rerum, voluntate adiuncta. Unde non oportet quod quaecumque scit Deus, sint vel fuerint vel futura sint, sed solum ea quae vult esse, vel permittit esse. Et iterum, non est in scientia Dei ut illa sint, sed quod esse possint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0168","text":"## Articulus 10","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0169","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0170","text":"Ad decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat mala. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod intellectus qui non est in potentia, non cognoscit privationem. Sed malum est privatio boni, ut dicit Augustinus. Igitur, cum intellectus Dei nunquam sit in potentia, sed semper actu, ut ex dictis patet, videtur quod Deus non cognoscat mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0171","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0172","text":"Praeterea, omnis scientia vel est causa sciti, vel causatur ab eo. Sed scientia Dei non est causa mali, nec causatur a malo. Ergo scientia Dei non est malorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0173","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0174","text":"Praeterea, omne quod cognoscitur, cognoscitur per suam similitudinem, vel per suum oppositum. Quidquid autem cognoscit Deus, cognoscit per suam essentiam, ut ex dictis patet. Divina autem essentia neque est similitudo mali, neque ei malum opponitur, divinae enim essentiae nihil est contrarium, ut dicit Augustinus, XII de Civ. Dei. Ergo Deus non cognoscit mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0175","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0176","text":"Praeterea, quod cognoscitur non per seipsum, sed per aliud, imperfecte cognoscitur. Sed malum non cognoscitur a Deo per seipsum, quia sic oporteret quod malum esset in Deo; oportet enim cognitum esse in cognoscente. Si ergo cognoscitur per aliud, scilicet per bonum, imperfecte cognoscetur ab ipso, quod est impossibile, quia nulla cognitio Dei est imperfecta. Ergo scientia Dei non est malorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0177","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0178","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Proverb. XV, *Infernus et perditio coram Deo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0179","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quicumque perfecte cognoscit aliquid, oportet quod cognoscat omnia quae possunt illi accidere. Sunt autem quaedam bona, quibus accidere potest ut per mala corrumpantur. Unde Deus non perfecte cognosceret bona, nisi etiam cognosceret mala. Sic autem est cognoscibile unumquodque, secundum quod est. Unde, cum hoc sit esse mali, quod est privatio boni, per hoc ipsum quod Deus cognoscit bona, cognoscit etiam mala; sicut per lucem cognoscuntur tenebrae. Unde dicit Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Deus per semetipsum tenebrarum accipit visionem, non aliunde videns tenebras quam a lumine*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0181","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0182","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum philosophi est sic intelligendum, quod intellectus qui non est in potentia, non cognoscit privationem per privationem in ipso existentem. Et hoc congruit cum eo quod supra dixerat, quod punctum et omne indivisibile per privationem divisionis cognoscitur. Quod contingit ex hoc, quia formae simplices et indivisibiles non sunt actu in intellectu nostro, sed in potentia tantum, nam si essent actu in intellectu nostro, non per privationem cognoscerentur. Et sic cognoscuntur simplicia a substantiis separatis. Deus igitur non cognoscit malum per privationem in se existentem, sed per bonum oppositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0183","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0184","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod scientia Dei non est causa mali, sed est causa boni, per quod cognoscitur malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0185","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0186","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet malum non opponatur essentiae divinae, quae non est corruptibilis per malum, opponitur tamen effectibus Dei; quos per essentiam suam cognoscit, et eos cognoscens, mala opposita cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0187","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod cognoscere aliquid per aliud tantum, est imperfectae cognitionis, si illud sit cognoscibile per se. Sed malum non est per se cognoscibile, quia de ratione mali est, quod sit privatio boni. Et sic neque definiri, neque cognosci potest, nisi per bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0189","text":"## Articulus 11","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0190","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0191","text":"Ad undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat singularia. Intellectus enim divinus immaterialior est quam intellectus humanus. Sed intellectus humanus, propter suam immaterialitatem, non cognoscit singularia, sed, sicut dicitur in II de anima, *ratio est universalium, sensus vero singularium*. Ergo Deus non cognoscit singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0192","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0193","text":"Praeterea, illae solae virtutes in nobis sunt singularium cognoscitivae, quae recipiunt species non abstractas a materialibus conditionibus. Sed res in Deo sunt maxime abstractae ab omni materialitate. Ergo Deus non cognoscit singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0194","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0195","text":"Praeterea, omnis cognitio est per aliquam similitudinem. Sed similitudo singularium, inquantum sunt singularia, non videtur esse in Deo, quia principium singularitatis est materia, quae, cum sit ens in potentia tantum, omnino est dissimilis Deo, qui est actus purus. Non ergo Deus potest cognoscere singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0196","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0197","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur, Proverb. XVI, *omnes viae hominum patent oculis eius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0198","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0199","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus cognoscit singularia. Omnes enim perfectiones in creaturis inventae, in Deo praeexistunt secundum altiorem modum, ut ex dictis patet. Cognoscere autem singularia pertinet ad perfectionem nostram. Unde necesse est quod Deus singularia cognoscat. Nam et philosophus pro inconvenienti habet, quod aliquid cognoscatur a nobis, quod non cognoscatur a Deo. Unde contra Empedoclem arguit, in I de anima et in III Metaphys., quod accideret Deum esse insipientissimum, si discordiam ignoraret. Sed perfectiones quae in inferioribus dividuntur, in Deo simpliciter et unite existunt. Unde, licet nos per aliam potentiam cognoscamus universalia et immaterialia, et per aliam singularia et materialia; Deus tamen per suum simplicem intellectum utraque cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0200","text":"Sed qualiter hoc esse possit, quidam manifestare volentes, dixerunt quod Deus cognoscit singularia per causas universales, nam nihil est in aliquo singularium, quod non ex aliqua causa oriatur universali. Et ponunt exemplum, sicut si aliquis astrologus cognosceret omnes motus universales caeli, posset praenuntiare omnes eclipses futuras. Sed istud non sufficit. Quia singularia ex causis universalibus sortiuntur quasdam formas et virtutes, quae, quantumcumque ad invicem coniungantur, non individuantur nisi per materiam individualem. Unde qui cognosceret Socratem per hoc quod est albus vel Sophronisci filius, vel quidquid aliud sic dicatur, non cognosceret ipsum inquantum est hic homo. Unde secundum modum praedictum, Deus non cognosceret singularia in sua singularitate. Alii vero dixerunt quod Deus cognoscit singularia, applicando causas universales ad particulares effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","text":"Sed hoc nihil est. Quia nullus potest applicare aliquid ad alterum, nisi illud praecognoscat, unde dicta applicatio non potest esse ratio cognoscendi particularia, sed cognitionem singularium praesupponit. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod, cum Deus sit causa rerum per suam scientiam, ut dictum est, intantum se extendit scientia Dei, inquantum se extendit eius causalitas. Unde, cum virtus activa Dei se extendat non solum ad formas, a quibus accipitur ratio universalis, sed etiam usque ad materiam, ut infra ostendetur; necesse est quod scientia Dei usque ad singularia se extendat, quae per materiam individuantur. Cum enim sciat alia a se per essentiam suam, inquantum est similitudo rerum velut principium activum earum, necesse est quod essentia sua sit principium sufficiens cognoscendi omnia quae per ipsum fiunt, non solum in universali, sed etiam in singulari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0202","text":"Et esset simile de scientia artificis, si esset productiva totius rei, et non formae tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0203","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus noster speciem intelligibilem abstrahit a principiis individuantibus, unde species intelligibilis nostri intellectus non potest esse similitudo principiorum individualium. Et propter hoc, intellectus noster singularia non cognoscit. Sed species intelligibilis divini intellectus, quae est Dei essentia, non est immaterialis per abstractionem, sed per seipsam, principium existens omnium principiorum quae intrant rei compositionem, sive sint principia speciei, sive principia individui. Unde per eam Deus cognoscit non solum universalia, sed etiam singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0205","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0206","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quamvis species intellectus divini secundum esse suum non habeat conditiones materiales, sicut species receptae in imaginatione et sensu; tamen virtute se extendit ad immaterialia et materialia, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0207","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0208","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod materia, licet recedat a Dei similitudine secundum suam potentialitatem, tamen inquantum vel sic esse habet, similitudinem quandam retinet divini esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0209","text":"## Articulus 12","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0210","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0211","text":"Ad duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit cognoscere infinita. Infinitum enim, secundum quod est infinitum, est ignotum, quia infinitum est cuius quantitatem accipientibus semper est aliquid extra assumere, ut dicitur in III Physic. Augustinus etiam dicit, XII de Civ. Dei, quod *quidquid scientia comprehenditur, scientis comprehensione finitur*. Sed infinita non possunt finiri. Ergo non possunt scientia Dei comprehendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0212","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0213","text":"Si dicatur quod ea quae in se sunt infinita, scientiae Dei finita sunt, contra, ratio infiniti est quod sit impertransibile; et finiti quod sit pertransibile, ut dicitur in III Physic. Sed infinitum non potest transiri nec a finito, nec ab infinito, ut probatur in VI Physic. Ergo infinitum non potest esse finitum finito, neque etiam infinito. Et ita infinita non sunt finita scientiae Dei, quae est infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0214","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0215","text":"Praeterea, scientia Dei est mensura scitorum. Sed contra rationem infiniti est, quod sit mensuratum. Ergo infinita non possunt sciri a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0216","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0217","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XII de Civ. Dei, *quamvis infinitorum numerorum nullus sit numerus, non est tamen incomprehensibilis ei, cuius scientiae non est numerus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0218","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum Deus sciat non solum ea quae sunt actu, sed etiam ea quae sunt in potentia vel sua vel creaturae, ut ostensum est; haec autem constat esse infinita; necesse est dicere quod Deus sciat infinita. Et licet scientia visionis, quae est tantum eorum quae sunt vel erunt vel fuerunt, non sit infinitorum, ut quidam dicunt, cum non ponamus mundum ab aeterno fuisse, nec generationem et motum in aeternum mansura, ut individua in infinitum multiplicentur, tamen, si diligentius consideretur, necesse est dicere quod Deus etiam scientia visionis sciat infinita. Quia Deus scit etiam cogitationes et affectiones cordium, quae in infinitum multiplicabuntur, creaturis rationalibus permanentibus absque fine. Hoc autem ideo est, quia cognitio cuiuslibet cognoscentis se extendit secundum modum formae quae est principium cognitionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0220","text":"Species enim sensibilis, quae est in sensu, est similitudo solum unius individui, unde per eam solum unum individuum cognosci potest. Species autem intelligibilis intellectus nostri est similitudo rei quantum ad naturam speciei, quae est participabilis a particularibus infinitis, unde intellectus noster per speciem intelligibilem hominis, cognoscit quodammodo homines infinitos. Sed tamen non inquantum distinguuntur ab invicem, sed secundum quod communicant in natura speciei; propter hoc quod species intelligibilis intellectus nostri non est similitudo hominum quantum ad principia individualia, sed solum quantum ad principia speciei. Essentia autem divina, per quam intellectus divinus intelligit, est similitudo sufficiens omnium quae sunt vel esse possunt, non solum quantum ad principia communia, sed etiam quantum ad principia propria uniuscuiusque, ut ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0221","text":"Unde sequitur quod scientia Dei se extendat ad infinita, etiam secundum quod sunt ab invicem distincta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0222","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0223","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod *infiniti ratio congruit quantitati*, secundum philosophum in I Physic. De ratione autem quantitatis est ordo partium. Cognoscere ergo infinitum secundum modum infiniti, est cognoscere partem post partem. Et sic nullo modo contingit cognosci infinitum, quia quantacumque quantitas partium accipiatur, semper remanet aliquid extra accipientem. Deus autem non sic cognoscit infinitum vel infinita, quasi enumerando partem post partem; cum cognoscat omnia simul, non successive, ut supra dictum est. Unde nihil prohibet ipsum cognoscere infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0224","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0225","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod transitio importat quandam successionem in partibus, et inde est quod infinitum transiri non potest, neque a finito neque ab infinito. Sed ad rationem comprehensionis sufficit adaequatio, quia id comprehendi dicitur, cuius nihil est extra comprehendentem. Unde non est contra rationem infiniti, quod comprehendatur ab infinito. Et sic, quod in se est infinitum, potest dici finitum scientiae Dei, tanquam comprehensum, non tamen tanquam pertransibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0226","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0227","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod scientia Dei est mensura rerum, non quantitativa, qua quidem mensura carent infinita; sed quia mensurat essentiam et veritatem rei. Unumquodque enim intantum habet de veritate suae naturae, inquantum imitatur Dei scientiam; sicut artificiatum inquantum concordat arti. Dato autem quod essent aliqua infinita actu secundum numerum, puta infiniti homines; vel secundum quantitatem continuam, ut si esset aer infinitus, ut quidam antiqui dixerunt, tamen manifestum est quod haberent esse determinatum et finitum, quia esse eorum esset limitatum ad aliquas determinatas naturas. Unde mensurabilia essent secundum scientiam Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0228","text":"## Articulus 13","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0229","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0230","text":"Ad decimumtertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod scientia Dei non sit futurorum contingentium. A causa enim necessaria procedit effectus necessarius. Sed scientia Dei est causa scitorum, ut supra dictum est. Cum ergo ipsa sit necessaria, sequitur scita eius esse necessaria. Non ergo scientia Dei est contingentium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0231","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0232","text":"Praeterea, omnis conditionalis cuius antecedens est necessarium absolute, consequens est necessarium absolute. Sic enim se habet antecedens ad consequens, sicut principia ad conclusionem, ex principiis autem necessariis non sequitur conclusio nisi necessaria, ut in I Poster. probatur. Sed haec est quaedam conditionalis vera, si Deus scivit hoc futurum esse, hoc erit, quia scientia Dei non est nisi verorum. Huius autem conditionalis antecedens est necessarium absolute, tum quia est aeternum; tum quia significatur ut praeteritum. Ergo et consequens est necessarium absolute. Igitur quidquid scitur a Deo, est necessarium. Et sic scientia Dei non est contingentium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0233","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0234","text":"Praeterea, omne scitum a Deo necesse est esse, quia etiam omne scitum a nobis necesse est esse, cum tamen scientia Dei certior sit quam scientia nostra. Sed nullum contingens futurum necesse est esse. Ergo nullum contingens futurum est scitum a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0235","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0236","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XXXII, *qui finxit singillatim corda eorum, qui intelligit omnia opera eorum*, scilicet hominum. Sed opera hominum sunt contingentia, utpote libero arbitrio subiecta. Ergo Deus scit futura contingentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0237","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum supra ostensum sit quod Deus sciat omnia non solum quae actu sunt, sed etiam quae sunt in potentia sua vel creaturae; horum autem quaedam sunt contingentia nobis futura; sequitur quod Deus contingentia futura cognoscat. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod contingens aliquod dupliciter potest considerari. Uno modo, in seipso, secundum quod iam actu est. Et sic non consideratur ut futurum, sed ut praesens, neque ut ad utrumlibet contingens, sed ut determinatum ad unum. Et propter hoc, sic infallibiliter subdi potest certae cognitioni, utpote sensui visus, sicut cum video Socratem sedere. Alio modo potest considerari contingens, ut est in sua causa. Et sic consideratur ut futurum, et ut contingens nondum determinatum ad unum, quia causa contingens se habet ad opposita. Et sic contingens non subditur per certitudinem alicui cognitioni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","text":"Unde quicumque cognoscit effectum contingentem in causa sua tantum, non habet de eo nisi coniecturalem cognitionem. Deus autem cognoscit omnia contingentia, non solum prout sunt in suis causis, sed etiam prout unumquodque eorum est actu in seipso. Et licet contingentia fiant in actu successive, non tamen Deus successive cognoscit contingentia, prout sunt in suo esse, sicut nos, sed simul. Quia sua cognitio mensuratur aeternitate, sicut etiam suum esse, aeternitas autem, tota simul existens, ambit totum tempus, ut supra dictum est. Unde omnia quae sunt in tempore, sunt Deo ab aeterno praesentia, non solum ea ratione qua habet rationes rerum apud se praesentes, ut quidam dicunt, sed quia eius intuitus fertur ab aeterno super omnia, prout sunt in sua praesentialitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","text":"Unde manifestum est quod contingentia et infallibiliter a Deo cognoscuntur, inquantum subduntur divino conspectui secundum suam praesentialitatem, et tamen sunt futura contingentia, suis causis comparata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0241","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet causa suprema sit necessaria, tamen effectus potest esse contingens, propter causam proximam contingentem, sicut germinatio plantae est contingens propter causam proximam contingentem, licet motus solis, qui est causa prima, sit necessarius. Et similiter scita a Deo sunt contingentia propter causas proximas, licet scientia Dei, quae est causa prima, sit necessaria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0243","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0244","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod hoc antecedens, Deus scivit hoc contingens futurum, non est necessarium, sed contingens, quia, licet sit praeteritum, tamen importat respectum ad futurum. Sed hoc non tollit ei necessitatem, quia id quod habuit respectum ad futurum, necesse est habuisse, licet etiam futurum non sequatur quandoque. Alii vero dicunt hoc antecedens esse contingens, quia est compositum ex necessario et contingenti; sicut istud dictum est contingens, Socratem esse hominem album. Sed hoc etiam nihil est. Quia cum dicitur, Deus scivit esse futurum hoc contingens, contingens non ponitur ibi nisi ut materia verbi, et non sicut principalis pars propositionis, unde contingentia eius vel necessitas nihil refert ad hoc quod propositio sit necessaria vel contingens, vera vel falsa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0245","text":"Ita enim potest esse verum me dixisse hominem esse asinum, sicut me dixisse Socratem currere, vel Deum esse, et eadem ratio est de necessario et contingenti. Unde dicendum est quod hoc antecedens est necessarium absolute. Nec tamen sequitur, ut quidam dicunt, quod consequens sit necessarium absolute, quia antecedens est causa remota consequentis, quod propter causam proximam contingens est. Sed hoc nihil est. Esset enim conditionalis falsa, cuius antecedens esset causa remota necessaria, et consequens effectus contingens, ut puta si dicerem, si sol movetur, herba germinabit. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod quando in antecedente ponitur aliquid pertinens ad actum animae, consequens est accipiendum non secundum quod in se est, sed secundum quod est in anima, aliud enim est esse rei in seipsa, et esse rei in anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0246","text":"Ut puta, si dicam, si anima intelligit aliquid, illud est immateriale, intelligendum est quod illud est immateriale secundum quod est in intellectu, non secundum quod est in seipso. Et similiter si dicam, si Deus scivit aliquid, illud erit, consequens intelligendum est prout subest divinae scientiae, scilicet prout est in sua praesentialitate. Et sic necessarium est, sicut et antecedens, *quia omne quod est, dum est, necesse est esse*, ut dicitur in I Periherm.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0247","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ea quae temporaliter in actum reducuntur, a nobis successive cognoscuntur in tempore, sed a Deo in aeternitate, quae est supra tempus. Unde nobis, quia cognoscimus futura contingentia inquantum talia sunt, certa esse non possunt, sed soli Deo, cuius intelligere est in aeternitate supra tempus. Sicut ille qui vadit per viam, non videt illos qui post eum veniunt, sed ille qui ab aliqua altitudine totam viam intuetur, simul videt omnes transeuntes per viam. Et ideo illud quod scitur a nobis, oportet esse necessarium etiam secundum quod in se est, quia ea quae in se sunt contingentia futura, a nobis sciri non possunt. Sed ea quae sunt scita a Deo, oportet esse necessaria secundum modum quo subsunt divinae scientiae, ut dictum est, non autem absolute, secundum quod in propriis causis considerantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0249","text":"Unde et haec propositio, omne scitum a Deo necessarium est esse, consuevit distingui. Quia potest esse de re, vel de dicto. Si intelligatur de re, est divisa et falsa, et est sensus, omnis res quam Deus scit, est necessaria. Vel potest intelligi de dicto, et sic est composita et vera; et est sensus, hoc dictum, scitum a Deo esse, est necessarium. Sed obstant quidam, dicentes quod ista distinctio habet locum in formis separabilibus a subiecto; ut si dicam, album possibile est esse nigrum. Quae quidem de dicto est falsa, et de re est vera, res enim quae est alba, potest esse nigra; sed hoc dictum, album esse nigrum, nunquam potest esse verum. In formis autem inseparabilibus a subiecto, non habet locum praedicta distinctio; ut si dicam, corvum nigrum possibile est esse album, quia in utroque sensu est falsa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0250","text":"Esse autem scitum a Deo, est inseparabile a re, quia quod est scitum a Deo, non potest esse non scitum. Haec autem instantia locum haberet, si hoc quod dico scitum, importaret aliquam dispositionem subiecto inhaerentem. Sed cum importet actum scientis, ipsi rei scitae, licet semper sciatur, potest aliquid attribui secundum se, quod non attribuitur ei inquantum stat sub actu sciendi, sicut esse materiale attribuitur lapidi secundum se, quod non attribuitur ei secundum quod est intelligibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0251","text":"## Articulus 14","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0252","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0253","text":"Ad decimumquartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non cognoscat enuntiabilia. Cognoscere enim enuntiabilia convenit intellectui nostro, secundum quod componit et dividit. Sed in intellectu divino nulla est compositio. Ergo Deus non cognoscit enuntiabilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0254","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0255","text":"Praeterea, omnis cognitio fit per aliquam similitudinem. Sed in Deo nulla est similitudo enuntiabilium, cum sit omnino simplex. Ergo Deus non cognoscit enuntiabilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0256","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0257","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XCIII, *dominus scit cogitationes hominum*. Sed enuntiabilia continentur in cogitationibus hominum. Ergo Deus cognoscit enuntiabilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0258","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum formare enuntiabilia sit in potestate intellectus nostri; Deus autem scit quidquid est in potentia sua vel creaturae, ut supra dictum est; necesse est quod Deus sciat omnia enuntiabilia quae formari possunt. Sed, sicut scit materialia immaterialiter, et composita simpliciter, ita scit enuntiabilia non per modum enuntiabilium, quasi scilicet in intellectu eius sit compositio vel divisio enuntiabilium; sed unumquodque cognoscit per simplicem intelligentiam, intelligendo essentiam uniuscuiusque. Sicut si nos in hoc ipso quod intelligimus quid est homo, intelligeremus omnia quae de homine praedicari possunt. Quod quidem in intellectu nostro non contingit, qui de uno in aliud discurrit, propter hoc quod species intelligibilis sic repraesentat unum, quod non repraesentat aliud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0260","text":"Unde, intelligendo quid est homo, non ex hoc ipso alia quae ei insunt, intelligimus; sed divisim, secundum quandam successionem. Et propter hoc, ea quae seorsum intelligimus, oportet nos in unum redigere per modum compositionis vel divisionis, enuntiationem formando. Sed species intellectus divini, scilicet eius essentia, sufficit ad demonstrandum omnia. Unde, intelligendo essentiam suam, cognoscit essentias omnium, et quaecumque eis accidere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0261","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0262","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si Deus cognosceret enuntiabilia per modum enuntiabilium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0263","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0264","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod compositio enuntiabilis significat aliquod esse rei, et sic Deus per suum esse, quod est eius essentia, est similitudo omnium eorum quae per enuntiabilia significantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0265","text":"## Articulus 15","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0266","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0267","text":"Ad decimumquintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod scientia Dei sit variabilis. Scientia enim relative dicitur ad scibile. Sed ea quae important relationem ad creaturam, dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, et variantur secundum variationem creaturarum. Ergo scientia Dei est variabilis, secundum variationem creaturarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0268","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0269","text":"Praeterea, quidquid potest Deus facere, potest scire. Sed Deus potest plura facere quam faciat. Ergo potest plura scire quam sciat. Et sic scientia sua potest variari secundum augmentum et diminutionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0270","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0271","text":"Praeterea, Deus scivit Christum nasciturum. Nunc autem nescit Christum nasciturum, quia Christus nasciturus non est. Ergo non quidquid Deus scivit, scit. Et ita scientia Dei videtur esse variabilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0272","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0273","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Iac. I, quod *apud Deum non est transmutatio, neque vicissitudinis obumbratio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0274","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum scientia Dei sit eius substantia, ut ex dictis patet; sicut substantia eius est omnino immutabilis, ut supra ostensum est, ita oportet scientiam eius omnino invariabilem esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0276","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0277","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dominus et creator, et huiusmodi, important relationes ad creaturas secundum quod in seipsis sunt. Sed scientia Dei importat relationem ad creaturas secundum quod sunt in Deo, quia secundum hoc est unumquodque intellectum in actu quod est in intelligente. Res autem creatae sunt in Deo invariabiliter, in seipsis autem variabiliter. Vel aliter dicendum est, quod dominus et creator, et huiusmodi, important relationes quae consequuntur actus qui intelliguntur terminari ad ipsas creaturas secundum quod in seipsis sunt, et ideo huiusmodi relationes varie de Deo dicuntur, secundum variationem creaturarum. Sed scientia et amor, et huiusmodi, important relationes quae consequuntur actus qui intelliguntur in Deo esse, et ideo invariabiliter praedicantur de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0278","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0279","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus scit etiam ea quae potest facere et non facit. Unde ex hoc quod potest plura facere quam facit, non sequitur quod possit plura scire quam sciat, nisi hoc referatur ad scientiam visionis, secundum quam dicitur scire ea quae sunt in actu secundum aliquod tempus. Ex hoc tamen quod scit quod aliqua possunt esse quae non sunt, vel non esse quae sunt, non sequitur quod scientia sua sit variabilis, sed quod cognoscat rerum variabilitatem. Si tamen aliquid esset quod prius Deus nescivisset et postea sciret, esset eius scientia variabilis. Sed hoc esse non potest, quia quidquid est vel potest esse secundum aliquod tempus, Deus in aeterno suo scit. Et ideo ex hoc ipso quod ponitur aliquid esse secundum quodcumque tempus, oportet poni quod ab aeterno sit scitum a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0280","text":"Et ideo non debet concedi quod Deus possit plura scire quam sciat, quia haec propositio implicat quod ante nesciverit et postea sciat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0281","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0282","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod antiqui nominales dixerunt idem esse enuntiabile, Christum nasci, et esse nasciturum, et esse natum, quia eadem res significatur per haec tria, scilicet nativitas Christi. Et secundum hoc sequitur quod Deus quidquid scivit, sciat, quia modo scit Christum natum, quod significat idem ei quod est Christum esse nasciturum. Sed haec opinio falsa est. Tum quia diversitas partium orationis diversitatem enuntiabilium causat. Tum etiam quia sequeretur quod propositio quae semel est vera, esset semper vera, quod est contra philosophum, qui dicit quod haec oratio, Socrates sedet, vera est eo sedente, et eadem falsa est, eo surgente. Et ideo concedendum est quod haec non est vera, quidquid Deus scivit, scit, si ad enuntiabilia referatur. Sed ex hoc non sequitur quod scientia Dei sit variabilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","text":"Sicut enim absque variatione divinae scientiae est, quod sciat unam et eandem rem quandoque esse et quandoque non esse; ita absque variatione divinae scientiae est, quod scit aliquod enuntiabile quandoque esse verum, et quandoque esse falsum. Esset autem ex hoc scientia Dei variabilis, si enuntiabilia cognosceret per modum enuntiabilium, componendo et dividendo, sicut accidit in intellectu nostro. Unde cognitio nostra variatur, vel secundum veritatem et falsitatem, puta si, mutata re, eandem opinionem de re illa retineamus, vel secundum diversas opiniones, ut si primo opinemur aliquem sedere, et postea opinemur eum non sedere. Quorum neutrum potest esse in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0284","text":"## Articulus 16","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0285","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0286","text":"Ad decimumsextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus de rebus non habeat scientiam speculativam. Scientia enim Dei est causa rerum, ut supra ostensum est. Sed scientia speculativa non est causa rerum scitarum. Ergo scientia Dei non est speculativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0287","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0288","text":"Praeterea. Scientia speculativa est per abstractionem a rebus, quod divinae scientiae non competit. Ergo scientia Dei non est speculativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0289","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0290","text":"Sed contra, omne quod est nobilius, Deo est attribuendum. Sed scientia speculativa est nobilior quam practica, ut patet per philosophum, in principio Metaphys. Ergo Deus habet de rebus scientiam speculativam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0291","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliqua scientia est speculativa tantum, aliqua practica tantum, aliqua vero secundum aliquid speculativa et secundum aliquid practica. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod aliqua scientia potest dici speculativa tripliciter. Primo, ex parte rerum scitarum, quae non sunt operabiles a sciente, sicut est scientia hominis de rebus naturalibus vel divinis. Secundo, quantum ad modum sciendi, ut puta si aedificator consideret domum definiendo et dividendo et considerando universalia praedicata ipsius. Hoc siquidem est operabilia modo speculativo considerare, et non secundum quod operabilia sunt, operabile enim est aliquid per applicationem formae ad materiam, non per resolutionem compositi in principia universalia formalia. Tertio, quantum ad finem, *nam intellectus practicus differt fine a speculativo*, sicut dicitur in III de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","text":"Intellectus enim practicus ordinatur ad finem operationis, finis autem intellectus speculativi est consideratio veritatis. Unde, si quis aedificator consideret qualiter posset fieri aliqua domus, non ordinans ad finem operationis, sed ad cognoscendum tantum, erit, quantum ad finem, speculativa consideratio, tamen de re operabili. Scientia igitur quae est speculativa ratione ipsius rei scitae, est speculativa tantum. Quae vero speculativa est vel secundum modum vel secundum finem, est secundum quid speculativa et secundum quid practica. Cum vero ordinatur ad finem operationis, est simpliciter practica. Secundum hoc ergo, dicendum est quod Deus de seipso habet scientiam speculativam tantum, ipse enim operabilis non est. De omnibus vero aliis habet scientiam et speculativam et practicam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","text":"Speculativam quidem, quantum ad modum, quidquid enim in rebus nos speculative cognoscimus definiendo et dividendo, hoc totum Deus multo perfectius novit. Sed de his quae potest quidem facere, sed secundum nullum tempus facit, non habet practicam scientiam, secundum quod practica scientia dicitur a fine. Sic autem habet practicam scientiam de his quae secundum aliquod tempus facit. Mala vero, licet ab eo non sint operabilia, tamen sub cognitione practica ipsius cadunt, sicut et bona, inquantum permittit vel impedit vel ordinat ea, sicut et aegritudines cadunt sub practica scientia medici, inquantum per artem suam curat eas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0295","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0296","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod scientia Dei est causa, non quidem sui ipsius, sed aliorum, quorundam quidem actu, scilicet eorum quae secundum aliquod tempus fiunt; quorundam vero virtute, scilicet eorum quae potest facere, et tamen nunquam fiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0297","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0298","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod scientiam esse acceptam a rebus scitis, non per se convenit scientiae speculativae, sed per accidens, inquantum est humana.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0299","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0300","text":"Ad id vero quod in contrarium obiicitur, dicendum quod de operabilibus perfecta scientia non habetur, nisi sciantur inquantum operabilia sunt. Et ideo, cum scientia Dei sit omnibus modis perfecta, oportet quod sciat ea quae sunt a se operabilia, inquantum huiusmodi, et non solum secundum quod sunt speculabilia. Sed tamen non receditur a nobilitate speculativae scientiae, quia omnia alia a se videt in seipso, seipsum autem speculative cognoscit; et sic in speculativa sui ipsius scientia, habet cognitionem et speculativam et practicam omnium aliorum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4fa1603ca52361037c5f70485dbc838e8d0a384d8a346d94d17856196475ebd4","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T135250382452Z_455662a9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 14","source_sha256":"4fa1603ca52361037c5f70485dbc838e8d0a384d8a346d94d17856196475ebd4","sha256":"f54698e420888112af11a021953262d993247ffeaf1a8a8f1a70364f5245c083","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:52:50.382486+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0002","text":"[I q. 14 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0003","text":"After consideration of those things that pertain to the divine substance, there remains consideration of those things that pertain to its operation. And because there is one operation that remains in the operator, and another that proceeds into an exterior effect, we shall first treat of knowledge and will (for understanding is in the one understanding, and willing in the one willing); and afterwards of the power of God, which is considered as the principle of the divine operation proceeding into an exterior effect. But because understanding is a certain kind of living, after consideration of divine knowledge there will be need to consider divine life. And because knowledge is of true things, truth and falsity must also be considered. Again, because every thing known is in the knower, whereas the intelligible accounts of things, insofar as they are in God knowing, are called ideas, consideration of ideas must likewise be joined to consideration of knowledge. Concerning knowledge, however, sixteen questions are asked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004","text":"First, whether there is knowledge in God. Second, whether God understands Himself. Third, whether He comprehends Himself. Fourth, whether His understanding is His substance. Fifth, whether He understands things other than Himself. Sixth, whether He has proper knowledge of them. Seventh, whether God's knowledge is discursive. Eighth, whether God's knowledge is the cause of things. Ninth, whether it is of things that are not. Tenth, whether it is of evils. Eleventh, whether it is of singulars. Twelfth, whether it is of infinites. Thirteenth, whether it is of future contingents. Fourteenth, whether it is of enunciables. Fifteenth, whether God's knowledge is variable. Sixteenth, whether God has speculative or practical knowledge of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0006","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0007","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that there is no knowledge in God. For knowledge is a habit, which does not befit God, since it is intermediate between potency and act. Therefore knowledge is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0008","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0009","text":"Moreover, knowledge, since it is of conclusions, is a kind of cognition caused by another, namely from cognition of principles. But nothing caused is in God. Therefore knowledge is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0010","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0011","text":"Moreover, every knowledge is either universal or particular. But there is no universal or particular in God, as is clear from what has gone before; therefore there is no knowledge in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0012","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0013","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, Rom. XI, *O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0014","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","text":"I answer that it must be said that knowledge is in God most perfectly. For the evidence of this, it must be considered that knowing things are distinguished from non-knowing things in this: that non-knowing things have nothing but their own form alone; but a knower is naturally apt to have also the form of another thing, for the species of the thing known is in the knower. Hence it is manifest that the nature of a non-knowing thing is more restricted and limited, whereas the nature of knowing things has greater breadth and extension. For this reason the Philosopher says, in III *On the Soul*, that *the soul is in a certain way all things*. But the restriction of form is through matter. Hence we also said above that forms, insofar as they are more immaterial, accordingly approach more nearly to a certain infinity. It is clear, therefore, that the immateriality of a thing is the reason why it is cognitive; and the mode of cognition is according to the mode of immateriality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","text":"Hence it is said in II *On the Soul* that plants do not know, on account of their materiality. But sense is cognitive, because it is receptive of species without matter; and intellect is still more cognitive, because it is more separate from matter and unmixed, as is said in III *On the Soul*. Hence, since God is at the summit of immateriality, as is clear from what has gone before, it follows that He is at the summit of cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0017","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, because perfections proceeding from God into creatures are in God in a higher mode, as was said above, it is necessary that, whenever some name taken from any perfection of a creature is attributed to God, everything that pertains to the imperfect mode befitting the creature be excluded from its signification. Hence knowledge is not a quality or habit in God, but substance and pure act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0019","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0020","text":"To the second it must be said that those things which are separately and multiply in creatures are in God simply and unitedly, as was said above. But a human being, according to diverse things known, has diverse cognitions: for insofar as he knows principles, he is said to have understanding; knowledge, insofar as he knows conclusions; wisdom, insofar as he knows the highest cause; counsel or prudence, insofar as he knows things to be done. But God knows all these by one simple cognition, as will become clear below. Hence God's simple cognition can be named by all these names, provided nevertheless that from each of them, insofar as it enters into divine predication, whatever is imperfect is excluded, and whatever is perfect is retained. And according to this it is said in Job XII, *with Him are wisdom and strength; He has counsel and understanding*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0021","text":"[I q. 14 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","text":"To the third it must be said that knowledge is according to the mode of the knower, for the thing known is in the knower according to the mode of the knower. And therefore, since the mode of the divine essence is higher than the mode by which creatures exist, divine knowledge does not have the mode of created knowledge, namely, so as to be universal or particular, or in habit or in potency, or disposed according to any such mode.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0024","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God does not understand Himself. For it is said in the Book of Causes that *every knower who knows his own essence returns to his own essence by a complete return*. But God does not go outside His essence, nor is He moved in any way, and thus it does not belong to Him to return to His essence. Therefore, He is not one knowing His own essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0026","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0027","text":"Moreover, understanding is a kind of being acted upon and being moved, as is said in *On the Soul*, III; knowledge too is an assimilation to the thing known, and the known is also the perfection of the knower. But nothing is moved, acted upon, or perfected by itself; nor is there likeness to itself, as Hilary says. Therefore God is not one knowing Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0028","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0029","text":"Moreover, we are especially like God in respect of intellect, because according to the mind we are in the image of God, as Augustine says. But our intellect does not understand itself except as it understands other things, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. Therefore neither does God understand Himself, unless perhaps by understanding other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0030","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0031","text":"On the contrary, it is said in I Corinthians II: *no one knows the things of God except the Spirit of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0032","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0033","text":"I answer that God understands Himself through Himself. To make this evident, it must be known that, although in operations which pass over into an external effect, the object of the operation, which is designated as its terminus, is something outside the operator; nevertheless, in operations which are in the operator, the object which is designated as the terminus of the operation is in the operator himself; and according as it is in him, so is the operation actual. Hence it is said in the book *On the Soul* that the sensible in act is sense in act, and the intelligible in act is intellect in act. For we actually sense or understand something from the fact that our intellect or sense is actually informed by the species of the sensible or intelligible. And in this respect alone sense or intellect is other than the sensible or intelligible: because each is in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","text":"Therefore, since God has no potentiality, but is pure act, it must be that in Him intellect and the understood are the same in every way: namely, so that He neither lacks an intelligible species, as our intellect does when it understands potentially; nor is the intelligible species other than the substance of the divine intellect, as occurs in our intellect when it is actually understanding; but the intelligible species itself is the divine intellect itself. And thus He understands Himself through Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0035","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to return to one's own essence is nothing other than for a thing to subsist in itself. For form, insofar as it perfects matter by giving it being, is in a certain way poured out above it; but insofar as it has being in itself, it returns into itself. Therefore cognitive powers which are not subsistent in themselves, but are acts of certain organs, do not know themselves, as is evident in the individual senses. But cognitive powers subsisting through themselves know themselves. And on this account it is said in the Book of Causes that *one knowing his own essence returns to his own essence*. But to subsist through oneself belongs most of all to God. Hence, according to this manner of speaking, He is most of all returning to His own essence and knowing Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0037","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0038","text":"To the second, it must be said that to be moved and to be acted upon are taken equivocally when understanding is said to be a kind of being moved or being acted upon, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. For understanding is not a motion which is the act of the imperfect, passing from another into another, but an act of the perfect, existing in the agent itself. Likewise, that the intellect is perfected by the intelligible or assimilated to it belongs to an intellect which is sometimes in potency, because through its being in potency it differs from the intelligible and is assimilated to it through the intelligible species, which is a likeness of the thing understood; and it is perfected through this species, as potency is by act. But the divine intellect, which is in no way in potency, is not perfected by the intelligible, nor assimilated to it, but is its own perfection and its own intelligible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0039","text":"[I q. 14 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that natural being does not belong to prime matter, which is in potency, except insofar as it is brought into act through form. But our possible intellect is related in the order of intelligibles as prime matter is in the order of natural things, because it is in potency toward intelligibles, as prime matter is toward natural things. Hence our possible intellect cannot have an intelligible operation except insofar as it is perfected through the intelligible species of some thing. And thus it understands itself through an intelligible species, just as it does other things; for it is evident that from the fact that it knows an intelligible, it understands its own understanding, and through the act it knows the intellective power. But God is as pure act both in the order of beings and in the order of intelligibles, and therefore through Himself He understands Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0042","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that God does not comprehend Himself. For Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, that that which comprehends itself is finite to itself. But God is infinite in every way. Therefore He does not comprehend Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0044","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0045","text":"If it be said that God is infinite to us but finite to Himself, on the contrary, each thing is more truly according as it is with God than according as it is with us. Therefore, if God is finite to Himself but infinite to us, it is truer that God is finite than infinite. This is contrary to what was previously determined. Therefore God does not comprehend Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0046","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0047","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says in the same place: *everything that understands itself comprehends itself*. But God understands Himself. Therefore He comprehends Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0048","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049","text":"I answer that it must be said that God comprehends Himself perfectly. This is clear as follows. For then is something said to be comprehended when the end of its cognition is attained; and this is when a thing is known as perfectly as it is knowable. Just as a demonstrable proposition is comprehended when it is known through demonstration, but not when it is known through some probable argument. Now it is manifest that God knows Himself as perfectly as He is perfectly knowable. For each thing is knowable according to the mode of its act; for something is not known insofar as it is in potency, but insofar as it is in act, as is said in Metaphysics IX. But God's power in knowing is as great as His actuality in existing, because through the fact that He is in act and separated from all matter and potency, God is cognitive, as has been shown.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0050","text":"Whence it is manifest that He knows Himself as much as He is knowable. And on this account He comprehends Himself perfectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0051","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0052","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to comprehend, if taken properly, signifies something having and enclosing another. And thus every comprehended thing must be finite, as is every enclosed thing. But God is not said to be comprehended by Himself in this way, as though His intellect were other than Himself and grasped and enclosed Him. Rather, expressions of this kind must be explained by negation. For just as God is said to be in Himself because He is contained by nothing exterior, so He is said to be comprehended by Himself because there is nothing of Himself that is hidden from Him; for Augustine says, in the book On Seeing God, that *the whole is comprehended by seeing which is so seen that nothing of it is hidden from the one seeing*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0053","text":"[I q. 14 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0054","text":"To the second it must be said that, when it is said that God is finite to Himself, this must be understood according to a certain likeness of proportion; because He is related to not exceeding His own intellect as some finite thing is related to not exceeding a finite intellect. But God is not said to be finite to Himself in such a way that He understands Himself to be something finite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0055","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0056","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0057","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that God's act of understanding is not His substance. For understanding is a certain operation. But operation signifies something proceeding from the operator. Therefore, God's act of understanding is not God's very substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0058","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0059","text":"Moreover, when someone understands that he understands, this is not to understand something great or principal as understood, but to understand something secondary and accessory. Therefore, if God is the very act of understanding, to understand God will be as when we understand understanding. And thus to understand God will not be something great.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0060","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0061","text":"Moreover, every act of understanding is understanding something. Therefore, since God understands Himself, if He Himself is nothing other than His understanding, He understands Himself to understand, and understands Himself to understand Himself to understand, and so on to infinity. Therefore, God's very act of understanding is not His substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0062","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0063","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book VII of On the Trinity, *for God, to be is to be wise*. But to be wise is to understand. Therefore, for God, to be is to understand. But God's being is His substance, as was shown above. Therefore, God's understanding is His substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0064","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0065","text":"I answer that it must be said that God's act of understanding is His substance. For if God's understanding were other than His substance, then, as the Philosopher says in Metaphysics XII, something else would have to be the act and perfection of the divine substance, to which the divine substance would be related as potency to act—which is altogether impossible—for understanding is the perfection and act of the one understanding. But how this is so must be considered. For, as was said above, understanding is not an action proceeding toward something extrinsic, but remains in the operator as its act and perfection, just as being is the perfection of that which exists; for as being follows form, so understanding follows the intelligible species. But in God there is no form that is other than His being, as was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","text":"Hence, since His very essence is also the intelligible species, as was said, it follows of necessity that His very act of understanding is His essence and His being. And thus it is evident from all the foregoing that in God the intellect, and that which is understood, and the intelligible species, and the very act of understanding, are altogether one and the same. Hence it is clear that, by the fact that God is called understanding, no multiplicity is posited in His substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0067","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0068","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that understanding is not an operation going forth from the operator himself, but remaining in him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0069","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0070","text":"To the second it must be said that, when that act of understanding which is not subsistent is understood, nothing great is understood; as when we understand our own understanding. And therefore the case is not similar with the divine act of understanding itself, which is subsistent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0071","text":"[I q. 14 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0072","text":"And through this the answer to the third is clear. For the divine act of understanding, which subsists in itself, is of itself; and not of some other, so that it would be necessary to proceed to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0073","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0074","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0075","text":"The fifth point is approached thus. It seems that God does not know things other than Himself. For whatever things are other than God are outside Him. But Augustine says, in the book of Eighty-Three Questions, that *God beholds nothing outside Himself*. Therefore, He does not know things other than Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0076","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0077","text":"Moreover, the thing understood is the perfection of the one understanding. Therefore, if God understands things other than Himself, something other will be God's perfection, and nobler than He. This is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0078","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0079","text":"Moreover, understanding itself receives its species from the intelligible, just as every other act does from its object; whence understanding itself is so much the nobler as that which is understood is nobler. But God is His own understanding itself, as is clear from what has been said. Therefore, if God understands something other than Himself, God Himself is specified by something other than Himself, which is impossible. Therefore, He does not understand things other than Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0080","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0081","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Heb. IV: *all things are naked and open to His eyes*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0082","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","text":"I answer that it must be said that God necessarily knows things other than Himself. For it is evident that He understands Himself perfectly; otherwise His being would not be perfect, since His being is His understanding. But if something is known perfectly, it is necessary that its power be known perfectly. Now the power of some thing cannot be known perfectly unless those things to which the power extends are known. Hence, since the divine power extends to things other than Himself, because it is itself the first efficient cause of all beings, as is clear from the foregoing; it is necessary that God know things other than Himself. And this becomes still more evident if it be added that the very being of the first acting cause, namely God, is His understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0084","text":"Hence whatever effects pre-exist in God as in the first cause must be in His understanding itself; and all things must be in Him according to an intelligible mode, for everything that is in another is in it according to the mode of that in which it is. But in order to know how He knows things other than Himself, it must be considered that something is known in two ways: in one way, in itself; in another way, in another. A thing is known in itself when it is known through a proper species adequate to the knowable thing itself, as when the eye sees a man through the species of man. But in another, that is seen which is seen through the species of the containing thing, as when a part is seen in the whole through the species of the whole, or when a man is seen in a mirror through the species of the mirror, or in whatever other way something happens to be seen in another. Thus, then, it must be said that God sees Himself in Himself, because He sees Himself through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","text":"But things other than Himself He sees not in themselves, but in Himself, insofar as His essence contains the likeness of things other than Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0086","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0087","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine's statement that God beholds nothing outside Himself is not to be understood as though He beholds nothing that is outside Himself, but because that which is outside Himself He does not behold except in Himself, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0088","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089","text":"To the second it must be said that the thing understood is the perfection of the one understanding, not indeed according to its substance, but according to its species, according to which it is in the intellect as its form and perfection; for, as is said in III *On the Soul*, *the stone is not in the soul, but its species is*. But things that are other than God are understood by God insofar as the essence of God contains their species, as has been said. Hence it does not follow that anything other than the very essence of God is the perfection of the divine intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0090","text":"[I q. 14 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091","text":"To the third it must be said that understanding itself is not specified by that which is understood in another, but by the principal thing understood, in which other things are understood. For understanding itself is specified by its object insofar as the intelligible form is the principle of the intellectual operation; for every operation is specified by the form which is the principle of the operation, as heating by heat. Hence the intellectual operation is specified by that intelligible form which makes the intellect actual. And this is the species of the principal thing understood, which in God is nothing other than His essence, in which all the species of things are comprehended. Hence it is not necessary that the divine understanding itself, or rather God Himself, be specified by anything other than the divine essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0092","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0093","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0094","text":"The sixth point is approached thus. It seems that God does not know things other than Himself by proper knowledge. For, as has been said, He knows things other than Himself according as things other than Himself are in Him. But things other than Him are in Him as in the first common and universal cause. Therefore, things other than Himself are also known by God as in the first and universal cause. But this is to know universally, and not according to proper knowledge. Therefore, God knows things other than Himself universally, and not according to proper knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0095","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0096","text":"Moreover, as far as the essence of a creature is distant from the divine essence, so far is the divine essence distant from the essence of a creature. But through the essence of a creature the divine essence cannot be known, as has been said above. Therefore, neither can the essence of a creature be known through the divine essence. And thus, since God knows nothing except through His essence, it follows that He does not know the creature according to its essence, so as to know what it is—which is to have proper knowledge of a thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0097","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0098","text":"Moreover, proper knowledge of a thing is not had except through its proper intelligible account. But since God knows all things through his essence, it does not seem that he knows each thing through its proper intelligible account; for the same thing cannot be the proper intelligible account of many and diverse things. Therefore God does not have proper knowledge of things, but common knowledge; for to know things not according to their proper intelligible account is to know things only in common.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0099","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0100","text":"But on the contrary, to have proper knowledge of things is to know things not only in common, but according as they are distinct from one another. But God knows things in this way. Hence it is said in Heb. IV that *it reaches even to the dividing of spirit and soul, of joints also and marrow; and is a discerner of the thoughts and intentions of the heart; and there is no creature invisible in his sight*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0101","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0102","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have erred concerning this, saying that God knows things other than himself only in common, namely, insofar as they are beings. For just as fire, if it knew itself as the principle of heat, would know the nature of heat and all other things insofar as they are hot; so God, insofar as he knows himself as the principle of being, knows the nature of being and all other things insofar as they are beings. But this cannot be. For to understand something in common and not in particular is to know it imperfectly. Hence our intellect, while it is brought from potency into act, arrives first at universal and confused knowledge of things, and then at proper knowledge of things, proceeding as from the imperfect to the perfect, as is clear in I Physics.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0103","text":"Therefore, if God's knowledge of things other than himself were only universal and not particular, it would follow that his understanding was not perfect in every way, and consequently neither would his being be, which is contrary to what has been shown above. Therefore it must be said that he knows things other than himself with proper knowledge: not only according as they share in the intelligible account of being, but according as one is distinguished from another. And for the evidence of this, it should be considered that some, wishing to show that God knows many things through one, employ certain examples: for instance, that if a center knew itself, it would know all the lines proceeding from the center; or that light, if it knew itself, would know all colors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0104","text":"But these examples, although they are similar in some respect, namely with respect to universal causality, nevertheless fall short with respect to this: that multitude and diversity are not caused by that one universal principle as to that which is the principle of distinction, but only as to that in which they share. For the diversity of colors is not caused by light alone, but by the diverse disposition of the transparent recipient; and similarly the diversity of lines by diverse position. And hence such diversity and multitude cannot be known in their principle with proper knowledge, but only in common. But in God it is not so. For it has been shown above that whatever perfection is in any creature pre-exists wholly and is contained in God in an eminent mode.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0105","text":"Moreover, not only that in which creatures share, namely being itself, pertains to perfection; but also those things through which creatures are distinguished from one another, such as living and understanding and the like, by which living things are distinguished from non-living things and intelligent things from non-intelligent things. And every form through which any thing is constituted in its proper species is a certain perfection. And thus all things pre-exist in God, not only as to that which is common to all, but also as to those things according to which things are distinguished. And thus, since God contains in himself all perfections, the essence of God is compared to all the essences of things not as a common thing to proper things, as unity to numbers or a center to lines, but as perfect act to imperfect acts: as if I were to say, man to animal, or six, which is a perfect number, to the imperfect numbers contained under it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","text":"Now it is manifest that imperfect acts can be known through a perfect act, not only in common but also with proper knowledge. Thus one who knows man knows animal with proper knowledge, and one who knows six knows three with proper knowledge. Therefore, since the essence of God has in itself whatever perfection the essence of any other thing has, and still more, God can know all things in himself with proper knowledge. For the proper nature of each thing consists in its participating, in some mode, in the divine perfection. But God would not know himself perfectly unless he knew in whatever way his perfection is capable of being participated in by others; nor would he know the very nature of being perfectly unless he knew all modes of being. Hence it is manifest that God knows all things with proper knowledge, according as they are distinguished from others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0107","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0108","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to know something as it is in the knower can be understood in two ways. In one way, according as this adverb “as” conveys the mode of knowledge on the part of the thing known. And thus it is false. For the knower does not always know the known thing according to that being which it has in the knower; for the eye does not know the stone according to the being which it has in the eye, but through the species of the stone which it has in itself, it knows the stone according to the being which it has outside the eye. And if some knower knows the known thing according to the being which it has in the knower, nevertheless it knows it according to the being which it has outside the knower; just as the intellect knows a stone according to the intelligible being which it has in the intellect, insofar as it knows itself to understand, but nevertheless it knows the being of the stone in its proper nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0109","text":"But if it is understood according as this adverb “as” conveys the mode on the part of the knower, it is true that the knower knows the known thing only thus, according as it is in the knower, because the more perfectly the known thing is in the knower, the more perfect is the mode of knowledge. Therefore it must be said that God not only knows things to be in himself, but through that by which he contains things in himself, he knows them in their proper nature; and the more perfectly, the more perfectly each thing is in him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0110","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0111","text":"To the second it must be said that the essence of a creature is compared to the essence of God as imperfect act to perfect act. And therefore the essence of a creature does not sufficiently lead to knowledge of the divine essence, but conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0112","text":"[I q. 14 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113","text":"To the third it must be said that the same thing cannot be taken as the intelligible account of diverse things by way of adequation. But the divine essence is something exceeding all creatures. Hence it can be taken as the proper intelligible account of each thing, according as it is diversely capable of being participated in or imitated by diverse creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0114","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0115","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0116","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh article. It seems that God's knowledge is discursive. For God's knowledge is not according to knowing in habit, but according to understanding in act. But according to the Philosopher, in II Topics, knowing in habit can concern many things at once, whereas understanding in act concerns only one. Therefore, since God knows many things, both himself and others, as has been shown, it seems that he does not understand all things simultaneously, but discourses from one thing to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0117","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0118","text":"Moreover, to know an effect through its cause is the knowledge of one who discourses. But God knows other things through himself, as an effect through its cause. Therefore his knowledge is discursive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0119","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0120","text":"Moreover, God knows each creature more perfectly than we know it. But in created causes we know their effects, and thus we discourse from causes to things caused. Therefore it seems to be similar in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0121","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0122","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XV, that *God does not see all things piecemeal or singly, as though with a gaze alternating from here to there and from there to here; rather, He sees all things simultaneously*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0123","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","text":"I answer that it must be said that there is no discursive process in divine knowledge. This is clear as follows. For in our knowledge there is a twofold discursive process. One is according to succession alone, as when, after we understand something actually, we turn ourselves to understanding something else. Another discursive process is according to causality, as when through principles we arrive at knowledge of conclusions. But the first discursive process cannot belong to God. For many things which we understand successively, if each of them is considered in itself, we understand all simultaneously if we understand them in some one thing—for example, if we understand the parts in the whole, or see diverse things in a mirror. But God sees all things in one, which is Himself, as has been established. Hence He sees all things simultaneously, and not successively. Likewise, the second discursive process also cannot belong to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125","text":"First, indeed, because the second discursive process presupposes the first; for those proceeding from principles to conclusions do not consider both at once. Then, because such a discursive process is of one proceeding from what is known to what is unknown. Hence it is evident that, when the first is known, the second is still unknown. And thus the second is not known in the first, but from the first. But the terminus of the discursive process is when the second is seen in the first, effects having been resolved into causes, and then the discursive process ceases. Hence, since God sees His effects in Himself as in a cause, His knowledge is not discursive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0126","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although there is only one act of understanding in itself, nevertheless it is possible to understand many things in some one thing, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0128","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0129","text":"To the second, it must be said that God does not know effects unknown to Him through a cause previously known, but knows them in the cause. Hence His knowledge is without discursive process, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0130","text":"[I q. 14 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0131","text":"To the third, it must be said that God indeed sees the effects of created causes in those causes themselves, much better than we do; yet not in such a way that knowledge of the effects is caused in Him by knowledge of the created causes, as it is in us. Hence His knowledge is not discursive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0132","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0133","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0134","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth article. It seems that God's knowledge is not the cause of things. For Origen says, in his commentary on the Epistle to the Romans, *something will not be because God knows it will be; rather, because it will be, it is therefore known by God before it comes to be*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0135","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0136","text":"Further, once the cause is posited, the effect is posited. But God's knowledge is eternal. Therefore, if God's knowledge is the cause of created things, it seems that creatures are from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0137","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0138","text":"Further, the knowable is prior to knowledge, and is its measure, as is said in *Metaphysics* X. But that which is posterior and measured cannot be a cause. Therefore God's knowledge is not the cause of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0139","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0140","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XV, *God knows all creatures, both spiritual and bodily, not because they are, but they are because He knows*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0141","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","text":"I answer that it must be said that God's knowledge is the cause of things. For God's knowledge stands to all created things as the knowledge of an artisan stands to artifacts. Now the knowledge of the artisan is the cause of artifacts, because the artisan works through his intellect; hence the form in the intellect must be the principle of operation, just as heat is the principle of heating. But it should be considered that a natural form, insofar as it is a form remaining in that to which it gives being, does not designate a principle of action, but insofar as it has an inclination toward an effect. And similarly, an intelligible form does not designate a principle of action insofar as it is only in the one understanding, unless there is joined to it an inclination toward an effect, which is through the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","text":"For since an intelligible form is related to opposites (since the knowledge of opposites is the same), it would not produce a determinate effect unless it were determined to one thing by appetite, as is said in *Metaphysics* IX. But it is manifest that God causes things through His intellect, since His being is His understanding. Hence it is necessary that His knowledge be the cause of things, insofar as it has will conjoined to it. Hence God's knowledge, insofar as it is the cause of things, is customarily called knowledge of approval.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0144","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0145","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Origen spoke with attention to the character of knowledge, to which the character of causality does not belong, unless will is conjoined to it, as has been said. But when he says that God foreknows certain things because they are future, this must be understood according to the cause of consequence, not according to the cause of being. For it follows that, if certain things are future, God has foreknown them; nevertheless, future things are not the cause that God knows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0146","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0147","text":"To the second, it must be said that the knowledge of God is the cause of things, insofar as things are in knowledge. But it was not in God's knowledge that things should exist from eternity. Hence, although God's knowledge is eternal, it nevertheless does not follow that creatures are from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0148","text":"[I q. 14 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149","text":"To the third, it must be said that natural things are intermediate between God's knowledge and our knowledge; for we receive knowledge from natural things, of which God is the cause through His knowledge. Hence, just as natural knowable things are prior to our knowledge and are its measure, so God's knowledge is prior to natural things and is their measure. Just as a certain house is intermediate between the knowledge of the craftsman who made it and the knowledge of the one who derives knowledge of it from the house itself now made.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0150","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0151","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0152","text":"The ninth point is approached thus. It seems that God does not have knowledge of non-beings. For God's knowledge is only of truths. But truth and being are convertible. Therefore, God's knowledge is not of non-beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0153","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0154","text":"Further, knowledge requires a likeness between the knower and the known. But things that are not cannot have any likeness to God, who is being itself. Therefore, things that are not cannot be known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0155","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0156","text":"Further, God's knowledge is the cause of the things known by Him. But He is not the cause of non-beings, because a non-being does not have a cause. Therefore, God does not have knowledge of non-beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0157","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0158","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says in Romans IV, *who calls those things which are not, as those which are*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0159","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","text":"I answer that God knows all things whatsoever that are in whatever way. But nothing prevents things that simply are not from being in some way. For those things simply are which are in act. But those things which are not in act are in the potency either of God Himself or of a creature: whether in active potency or passive potency, or in the potency of opining, or imagining, or signifying in whatever way. Therefore, whatever things can be made, thought, or said by a creature, and also whatever things He Himself can make, God knows them all, even if they are not in act. And in this respect it can be said that He has knowledge even of non-beings. But among those things which are not in act, a certain distinction must be noted. For some things, although they are not now in act, nevertheless either have been or will be; and God is said to know all these by the knowledge of vision.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","text":"For since God's understanding, which is His being, is measured by eternity, which, existing without succession, comprehends all time, the present gaze of God extends over all time and over all things that are in whatever time, as though they were present to Him. But there are certain things which are in the potency of God or of a creature, yet neither are, nor will be, nor have been. And with respect to these He is not said to have knowledge of vision, but of simple intelligence. This is so called because the things seen by us have a distinct being outside the one seeing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0162","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0163","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, insofar as they are in potency, things which are not in act have truth; for it is true that they are in potency. And thus they are known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0164","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0165","text":"To the second, it must be said that, since God is being itself, each thing is to the extent that it participates in the likeness of God, just as each thing is hot to the extent that it participates in heat. Thus also things that are in potency, even if they are not in act, are known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0166","text":"[I q. 14 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","text":"To the third, it must be said that God's knowledge is the cause of things, with will conjoined. Hence it is not necessary that whatever God knows either is, or has been, or will be, but only those things that He wills to be or permits to be. And again, it is not in God's knowledge that those things are, but that they can be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0168","text":"## Article 10","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0169","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0170","text":"We proceed thus to the tenth. It seems that God does not know evils. For the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that an intellect which is not in potency does not know privation. But evil is the privation of good, as Augustine says. Therefore, since God's intellect is never in potency, but always in act, as is clear from what has been said, it seems that God does not know evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0171","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0172","text":"Moreover, every knowledge either is the cause of what is known, or is caused by it. But God's knowledge is not the cause of evil, nor is it caused by evil. Therefore God's knowledge is not of evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0173","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0174","text":"Moreover, everything that is known is known through its likeness, or through its opposite. But whatever God knows, He knows through His essence, as is clear from what has been said. But the divine essence is neither a likeness of evil, nor is evil opposed to it; for nothing is contrary to the divine essence, as Augustine says, in *The City of God* XII. Therefore God does not know evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0175","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0176","text":"Moreover, what is known not through itself, but through another, is known imperfectly. But evil is not known by God through itself, because in that way evil would have to be in God; for what is known must be in the knower. Therefore, if it is known through another, namely through good, it will be known by Him imperfectly, which is impossible, because no knowledge of God is imperfect. Therefore God's knowledge is not of evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0177","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0178","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Proverbs XV: *Hell and destruction are before God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0179","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","text":"I answer that it must be said that whoever knows something perfectly must know all that can happen to it. Now there are certain goods to which it can happen that they are corrupted through evils. Hence God would not know goods perfectly unless He also knew evils. But each thing is knowable insofar as it is. Therefore, since the being of evil consists in being a privation of good, by the very fact that God knows goods, He also knows evils; just as darkness is known through light. Hence Dionysius says, in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*, that *God receives the vision of darkness through Himself, seeing darkness from no other source than from light*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0181","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0182","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher's statement is to be understood thus: an intellect that is not in potency does not know privation through a privation existing in itself. And this agrees with what he had said above, namely, that a point and every indivisible are known through the privation of division. This occurs because simple and indivisible forms are not in act in our intellect, but only in potency; for if they were in act in our intellect, they would not be known through privation. And thus simple things are known by separate substances. Therefore God does not know evil through a privation existing in Himself, but through the opposite good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0183","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0184","text":"To the second, it must be said that God's knowledge is not the cause of evil, but is the cause of the good through which evil is known.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0185","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0186","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although evil is not opposed to the divine essence, which cannot be corrupted by evil, it is nevertheless opposed to the effects of God, which He knows through His essence; and in knowing them, He knows the evils opposed to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0187","text":"[I q. 14 a. 10 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that to know something only through another belongs to imperfect knowledge, if that thing is knowable through itself. But evil is not knowable through itself, because it belongs to the nature of evil that it be a privation of good. And thus it can neither be defined nor known except through good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0189","text":"## Article 11","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0190","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0191","text":"We proceed thus to the eleventh. It seems that God does not know singulars. For the divine intellect is more immaterial than the human intellect. But the human intellect, on account of its immateriality, does not know singulars; rather, as is said in *On the Soul* II, *reason is concerned with universals, but sense with singulars*. Therefore God does not know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0192","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0193","text":"Moreover, those powers in us alone are cognizant of singulars which receive species not abstracted from material conditions. But things in God are most abstract from all materiality. Therefore God does not know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0194","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0195","text":"Moreover, all knowledge is through some likeness. But the likeness of singulars, insofar as they are singular, does not seem to be in God, because the principle of singularity is matter, which, since it is being only in potency, is altogether unlike God, who is pure act. Therefore God cannot know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0196","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0197","text":"But on the contrary, it is said, Prov. XVI, *all the ways of men lie open to his eyes*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0198","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0199","text":"I answer that it must be said that God knows singulars. For all perfections found in creatures pre-exist in God in a higher mode, as is clear from what has been said. But knowing singulars pertains to our perfection. Hence it is necessary that God know singulars. For the Philosopher also considers it unfitting that something be known by us which is not known by God. Hence, against Empedocles, he argues in I *On the Soul* and in III *Metaphysics* that God would be most ignorant if he were unaware of discord. But perfections which are divided in lower things exist in God simply and unitedly. Hence, although we know universals and immaterial things by one power, and singular and material things by another, nevertheless God knows both by his simple intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0200","text":"But in seeking to show how this can be, some have said that God knows singulars through universal causes, for there is nothing in any singulars that does not arise from some universal cause. And they offer an example: just as, if an astronomer knew all the universal motions of heaven, he could foretell all future eclipses. But this does not suffice. For singulars derive from universal causes certain forms and powers which, however much they may be joined together, are not individuated except through individual matter. Hence, one who knew Socrates by the fact that he is white, or the son of Sophroniscus, or by whatever else might be said in this way, would not know him insofar as he is this man. Hence, according to the aforesaid mode, God would not know singulars in their singularity. Others, however, have said that God knows singulars by applying universal causes to particular effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","text":"But this is nothing. For no one can apply one thing to another unless he has prior knowledge of it; hence the said application cannot be the means of knowing particulars, but presupposes knowledge of singulars. And therefore it must be said otherwise: since God is the cause of things through his knowledge, as has been said, God's knowledge extends as far as his causality extends. Hence, since God's active power extends not only to forms, from which the notion of the universal is taken, but also even to matter, as will be shown below, it is necessary that God's knowledge extend even to singulars, which are individuated by matter. For since he knows things other than himself through his essence, insofar as it is the likeness of things as their active principle, it is necessary that his essence be a sufficient principle for knowing all things made through him, not only universally, but also singularly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0202","text":"And it would be similar in the case of an artisan's knowledge, if it were productive of the whole thing and not of the form alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0203","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that our intellect abstracts the intelligible species from individuating principles; hence the intelligible species of our intellect cannot be a likeness of individual principles. And on this account our intellect does not know singulars. But the intelligible species of the divine intellect, which is God's essence, is not immaterial through abstraction, but is through itself the existing principle of all the principles that enter into the composition of a thing, whether they are principles of the species or principles of the individual. Hence through it God knows not only universals but also singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0205","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0206","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although the species of the divine intellect does not, according to its being, have material conditions, as do species received in imagination and sense, nevertheless by its power it extends to immaterial and material things, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0207","text":"[I q. 14 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0208","text":"To the third, it must be said that matter, although it departs from God's likeness according to its potentiality, nevertheless, insofar as it has being even in this way, retains a certain likeness of divine being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0209","text":"## Article 12","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0210","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0211","text":"We proceed thus to the twelfth article. It seems that God cannot know infinite things. For the infinite, insofar as it is infinite, is unknown, because the infinite is that whose quantity, when portions are taken, always has something outside to be taken, as is said in III *Physics*. Augustine also says, in XII *City of God*, that *whatever is comprehended by knowledge is bounded by the comprehension of the knower*. But infinite things cannot be bounded. Therefore they cannot be comprehended by God's knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0212","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0213","text":"If it be said that those things which are infinite in themselves are finite to God's knowledge, on the contrary: the nature of the infinite is that it is untraversable, and of the finite that it is traversable, as is said in III *Physics*. But the infinite cannot be traversed either by the finite or by the infinite, as is proved in VI *Physics*. Therefore the infinite cannot be finite to the finite, nor even to the infinite. And thus infinite things are not finite to God's knowledge, which is infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0213"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0214","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0214"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0215","text":"Moreover, God's knowledge is the measure of things known. But it is contrary to the nature of the infinite that it be measured. Therefore infinite things cannot be known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0215"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0216","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0216"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0217","text":"But on the contrary is what Augustine says, *City of God*, XII: “although there is no number of infinite numbers, yet they are not incomprehensible to Him whose knowledge has no number.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0217"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0218","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0218"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since God knows not only those things which are in act, but also those which are in potentiality, whether His own or that of the creature, as has been shown; and since these are evidently infinite; it is necessary to say that God knows infinite things. And although the knowledge of vision, which is only of those things which are, or will be, or have been, is not of infinite things, as some say, since we do not posit that the world has existed from eternity, nor that generation and motion will remain unto eternity, so that individuals may be multiplied to infinity; nevertheless, if the matter is considered more carefully, it is necessary to say that God, even by knowledge of vision, knows infinite things. For God knows also the thoughts and affections of hearts, which will be multiplied to infinity, rational creatures enduring without end. This is so because the cognition of any knower extends according to the mode of the form which is the principle of cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0220","text":"For the sensible species which is in the sense is a likeness only of one individual; hence through it only one individual can be known. But the intelligible species of our intellect is a likeness of a thing as regards the nature of the species, which can be participated in by infinite particulars; hence our intellect, through the intelligible species of man, knows in some manner infinite men. Yet it does not know them insofar as they are distinguished from one another, but insofar as they share in the nature of the species; because the intelligible species of our intellect is not a likeness of men as regards individual principles, but only as regards the principles of the species. But the divine essence, through which the divine intellect understands, is a sufficient likeness of all things that are or can be, not only as regards common principles, but also as regards the proper principles of each one, as has been shown.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0220"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0221","text":"Hence it follows that God's knowledge extends to infinite things, even insofar as they are distinct from one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0221"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0222","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0222"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0223","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that “the notion of the infinite belongs to quantity,” according to the Philosopher in *Physics* I. But the notion of quantity includes the order of parts. Therefore, to know the infinite according to the mode of the infinite is to know part after part. And in this way the infinite can in no manner be known, because however great a quantity of parts may be taken, something always remains outside the one taking them. But God does not know the infinite or infinite things in this way, as though by enumerating part after part; since He knows all things simultaneously, not successively, as was said above. Hence nothing prevents Him from knowing infinite things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0223"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0224","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0224"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0225","text":"To the second, it must be said that traversal implies a certain succession in the parts, and for this reason the infinite cannot be traversed, neither by the finite nor by the infinite. But for the notion of comprehension, adequation suffices, because that is said to be comprehended outside of which there is nothing for the comprehender. Hence it is not contrary to the notion of the infinite that it be comprehended by the infinite. And thus, what is in itself infinite can be called finite to the knowledge of God, as comprehended, though not as traversable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0225"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0226","text":"[I q. 14 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0226"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0227","text":"To the third, it must be said that God's knowledge is the measure of things, not a quantitative measure, which infinite things indeed lack; but because it measures the essence and truth of a thing. For each thing has so much truth of its nature insofar as it imitates God's knowledge, just as an artifact insofar as it accords with art. But even if there were some things actually infinite in number—for example, infinite men—or in continuous quantity, as if there were infinite air, as some ancients said, nevertheless it is evident that they would have determinate and finite being, because their being would be limited to certain determinate natures. Hence they would be measurable according to God's knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0227"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0228","text":"## Article 13","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0228"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0229","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0229"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0230","text":"We proceed thus to the thirteenth. It seems that God's knowledge is not of future contingents. For from a necessary cause there proceeds a necessary effect. But God's knowledge is the cause of the things known, as was said above. Therefore, since it is necessary, it follows that the things known by it are necessary. Therefore God's knowledge is not of contingent things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0230"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0231","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0231"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0232","text":"Moreover, every conditional whose antecedent is necessary absolutely has a consequent necessary absolutely. For antecedent and consequent are related as principles and conclusion; but from necessary principles there follows only a necessary conclusion, as is proved in *Posterior Analytics* I. But this is a true conditional: if God knew that this would be, this will be; because God's knowledge is only of truths. Now the antecedent of this conditional is necessary absolutely, both because it is eternal and because it is signified as past. Therefore the consequent also is necessary absolutely. Therefore whatever is known by God is necessary. And thus God's knowledge is not of contingent things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0232"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0233","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0233"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0234","text":"Moreover, everything known by God must be, because even everything known by us must be, although God's knowledge is more certain than our knowledge. But no future contingent must be. Therefore no future contingent is known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0234"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0235","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0235"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0236","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm XXXII: “He who fashioned their hearts individually, who understands all their works,” namely, the works of men. But the works of men are contingent, being subject to free choice. Therefore God knows future contingents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0236"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0237","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0237"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since it has been shown above that God knows all things, not only those which are actually, but also those which are in His own potentiality or that of the creature; and since some of these are future contingents for us; it follows that God knows future contingents. For the clarification of this, it must be considered that a contingent thing can be considered in two ways. In one way, in itself, according as it is already in act. And thus it is considered not as future, but as present, nor as contingent toward either of two alternatives, but as determined to one. And for this reason it can thus be subjected infallibly to certain cognition, such as the sense of sight, as when I see Socrates sitting. In another way, a contingent can be considered as it is in its cause. And thus it is considered as future and as contingent, not yet determined to one, because a contingent cause stands in relation to opposites. And thus a contingent does not fall under any cognition with certainty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","text":"Hence whoever knows a contingent effect only in its cause has concerning it only conjectural cognition. But God knows all contingents, not only insofar as they are in their causes, but also insofar as each of them is actually in itself. And although contingents come to be in act successively, nevertheless God does not know contingents successively, insofar as they are in their being, as we do, but simultaneously. For His cognition is measured by eternity, just as His being is; but eternity, existing all at once, embraces the whole of time, as was said above. Hence all things which are in time are present to God from eternity, not only by reason of His having the notions of things present within Himself, as some say, but because His gaze is borne from eternity over all things, insofar as they are in their presence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","text":"Hence it is manifest that contingents are known infallibly by God, insofar as they are subject to the divine gaze according to their presence, and yet they are future contingents in comparison with their causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0241","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0241"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, although the supreme cause is necessary, nevertheless the effect can be contingent, on account of the contingent proximate cause; just as the germination of a plant is contingent on account of its contingent proximate cause, although the motion of the sun, which is the first cause, is necessary. And similarly, the things known by God are contingent on account of proximate causes, although the knowledge of God, which is the first cause, is necessary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0243","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0243"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0244","text":"To the second it must be said that some say that this antecedent, God knew that this contingent thing would be future, is not necessary but contingent, because, although it is past, it nevertheless imports a relation to the future. But this does not remove its necessity, because that which had a relation to the future necessarily had it, although the future does not sometimes follow. Others, however, say that this antecedent is contingent because it is composed of the necessary and the contingent; just as this statement is contingent, Socrates is a white man. But this too is nothing. For when it is said, God knew that this contingent thing would be future, “contingent” is put there only as the matter of the verb, and not as the principal part of the proposition; hence its contingency or necessity makes no difference as to whether the proposition is necessary or contingent, true or false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0244"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0245","text":"For it can be just as true that I said that a man is an ass as that I said that Socrates runs, or that God exists; and the same reasoning applies to the necessary and the contingent. Hence it must be said that this antecedent is necessary absolutely. Yet it does not follow, as some say, that the consequent is necessary absolutely, because the antecedent is a remote cause of the consequent, which is contingent on account of its proximate cause. But this is nothing. For the conditional would be false whose antecedent was a necessary remote cause and whose consequent was a contingent effect, as if I were to say, if the sun moves, grass will germinate. And therefore it must be said otherwise: when something pertaining to an act of the soul is placed in the antecedent, the consequent must be taken not according as it is in itself, but according as it is in the soul; for the being of a thing in itself is one thing, and the being of a thing in the soul another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0245"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0246","text":"For example, if I say, if the soul understands something, it is immaterial, it must be understood that it is immaterial according as it is in the intellect, not according as it is in itself. And similarly, if I say, if God knew something, it will be, the consequent must be understood as subject to the divine knowledge, namely, according as it is in its presentness. And thus it is necessary, as is also the antecedent, *because everything which is, while it is, necessarily is*, as is said in I *Periherm.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0246"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0247","text":"[I q. 14 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0247"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248","text":"To the third it must be said that those things which are brought into act temporally are known by us successively in time, but by God in eternity, which is above time. Hence for us, because we know future contingents insofar as they are such, they cannot be certain, but only for God, whose understanding is in eternity above time. Just as one who walks along a road does not see those who come after him, whereas one who views the whole road from some height sees at once all who pass along the road. And therefore that which is known by us must be necessary also according as it is in itself, because things which are in themselves future contingents cannot be known by us. But those things which are known by God must be necessary according to the mode in which they are subject to divine knowledge, as has been said, but not absolutely, according as they are considered in their proper causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0249","text":"Hence this proposition also, everything known by God necessarily is, is customarily distinguished. For it can be about the thing, or about the statement. If it is understood about the thing, it is divided and false, and its sense is: every thing that God knows is necessary. Or it can be understood about the statement, and thus it is composite and true; and its sense is: this statement, that it is known by God, is necessary. But some object, saying that this distinction has a place in forms separable from the subject; as if I were to say, a white thing can be black. This, indeed, understood about the statement is false, and understood about the thing is true; for a thing which is white can be black, but this statement, a white thing is black, can never be true. But in forms inseparable from the subject the aforesaid distinction has no place; as if I were to say, a black raven can be white, because in both senses it is false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0249"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0250","text":"But being known by God is inseparable from the thing, because that which is known by God cannot be not known. This objection, however, would have force if what I call “known” imported some disposition inhering in the subject. But since it imports an act of the knower, something can be attributed to the known thing, although it is always known, according to itself, which is not attributed to it insofar as it stands under the act of knowing; just as material being is attributed to a stone according to itself, which is not attributed to it insofar as it is intelligible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0250"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0251","text":"## Article 14","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0251"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0252","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0252"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0253","text":"We proceed thus to the fourteenth article. It seems that God does not know enunciables. For knowing enunciables belongs to our intellect insofar as it composes and divides. But there is no composition in the divine intellect. Therefore God does not know enunciables.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0253"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0254","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#28999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0254"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0255","text":"Further, every cognition takes place through some likeness. But in God there is no likeness of enunciables, since He is altogether simple. Therefore God does not know enunciables.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0255"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0256","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0256"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0257","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm XCIII, *the Lord knows the thoughts of men*. But enunciables are contained in the thoughts of men. Therefore God knows enunciables.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0257"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0258","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0258"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since the forming of enunciables is within the power of our intellect, while God knows whatever is in His own power or that of the creature, as was said above, God must know all enunciables that can be formed. But, just as He knows material things immaterially and composite things simply, so He knows enunciables not in the mode of enunciables—as though there were composition or division of enunciables in His intellect—but He knows each thing by simple understanding, by understanding the essence of each thing. It would be as though, in the very fact that we understand what man is, we understood everything that can be predicated of man. This indeed does not occur in our intellect, which proceeds discursively from one thing to another, because the intelligible species represents one thing in such a way that it does not represent another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0260","text":"Hence, by understanding what man is, we do not by that very fact understand the other things that are in him, but separately, according to a certain succession. And on this account, the things which we understand separately, we must bring together into one by way of composition or division, forming an enunciation. But the species of the divine intellect, namely His essence, suffices to make all things manifest. Hence, by understanding His essence, He knows the essences of all things and whatever can happen to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0260"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0261","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0261"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0262","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that that argument would proceed if God knew enunciables in the mode of enunciables.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0262"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0263","text":"[I q. 14 a. 14 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0263"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0264","text":"To the second it must be said that the composition of an enunciable signifies some being of a thing, and thus God, through His being, which is His essence, is the likeness of all those things that are signified through enunciables.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0264"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0265","text":"## Article 15","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0265"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0266","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0266"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0267","text":"We proceed thus to the fifteenth. It seems that God's knowledge is changeable. For knowledge is spoken of relatively to the knowable. But those things which imply a relation to a creature are said of God in time, and vary according to the variation of creatures. Therefore God's knowledge is changeable, according to the variation of creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0267"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0268","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0268"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0269","text":"Further, whatever God can do, He can know. But God can do more things than He does. Therefore He can know more things than He knows. And thus His knowledge can vary according to increase and diminution.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0269"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0270","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0270"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0271","text":"Further, God knew that Christ would be born. But now He does not know that Christ will be born, because Christ is not going to be born. Therefore not everything that God knew does He know. And thus the knowledge of God seems to be changeable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0271"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0272","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0272"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0273","text":"On the contrary, it is said in James 1 that *with God there is no change, nor shadow of vicissitude*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0273"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0274","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0274"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since God's knowledge is His substance, as is clear from what has been said, just as His substance is altogether immutable, as was shown above, so His knowledge must be altogether invariable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0276","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0276"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0277","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that lord and creator, and the like, imply relations to creatures insofar as they are in themselves. But God's knowledge implies a relation to creatures insofar as they are in God, because in this respect each thing that is in one knowing is understood in act. Created things, however, are in God in an invariable manner, but in themselves in a variable manner. Or it must be said otherwise, that lord and creator, and the like, imply relations consequent upon acts which are understood to be terminated at the creatures themselves insofar as they are in themselves, and therefore relations of this sort are variously said of God according to the variation of creatures. But knowledge and love, and the like, imply relations consequent upon acts which are understood to be in God, and therefore are predicated of God in an invariable manner.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0277"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0278","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0278"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0279","text":"To the second it must be said that God knows also those things which He can do and does not do. Hence, from the fact that He can do more things than He does, it does not follow that He can know more things than He knows, unless this be referred to the knowledge of vision, according to which He is said to know those things which are in act at some time. Yet from the fact that He knows that certain things can be which are not, or not be which are, it does not follow that His knowledge is changeable, but that He knows the variability of things. If, however, there were something that God did not know before and knew afterward, His knowledge would be changeable. But this cannot be, because whatever is or can be at any time, God knows in His eternity. And therefore, from the very fact that something is posited as being at whatever time, it must be posited that it has been known by God from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0279"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0280","text":"And therefore it should not be conceded that God can know more things than He knows, because this proposition implies that He did not know before and knows afterward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0280"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0281","text":"[I q. 14 a. 15 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0281"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0282","text":"To the third it must be said that the ancient nominalists said that it is the same enuntiable, that Christ is being born, and that He will be born, and that He has been born, because the same thing is signified by these three, namely, the birth of Christ. And according to this it follows that whatever God knew, He knows, because now He knows that Christ has been born, which signifies for Him the same as that Christ will be born. But this opinion is false. First, because a diversity of parts of speech causes a diversity of enuntiables. Second, because it would follow that a proposition which is once true would always be true, which is contrary to the Philosopher, who says that this statement, Socrates is sitting, is true while he is sitting, and the same is false when he rises. And therefore it must be conceded that this is not true, whatever God knew, He knows, if it be referred to enuntiables. But it does not follow from this that God's knowledge is changeable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0282"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","text":"For just as, without variation of the divine knowledge, He knows one and the same thing at one time to be and at another time not to be, so, without variation of the divine knowledge, He knows some enuntiable at one time to be true and at another time to be false. But God's knowledge would be changeable on this account if He knew enuntiables in the manner of enuntiables, by composing and dividing, as occurs in our intellect. Hence our knowledge varies either according to truth and falsity, for example, if, the thing having changed, we retain the same opinion about that thing; or according to different opinions, as if we first think that someone is sitting, and afterward think that he is not sitting. Neither of these can be in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0284","text":"## Article 16","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0284"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0285","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0285"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0286","text":"We proceed thus to the sixteenth. It seems that God does not have speculative knowledge of things. For God's knowledge is the cause of things, as was shown above. But speculative knowledge is not the cause of the things known. Therefore God's knowledge is not speculative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0286"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0287","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0287"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0288","text":"Further. Speculative knowledge is through abstraction from things, which does not belong to divine knowledge. Therefore God's knowledge is not speculative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0288"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0289","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0289"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0290","text":"On the contrary, whatever is more noble is to be attributed to God. But speculative knowledge is more noble than practical knowledge, as is clear from the Philosopher, at the beginning of the *Metaphysics*. Therefore God has speculative knowledge of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0290"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0291","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0291"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","text":"I answer that it must be said that some knowledge is speculative only, some practical only, and some speculative in one respect and practical in another respect. For the elucidation of this, it must be known that knowledge can be called speculative in three ways. First, on the part of the things known, which are not capable of being made by the knower, as is a man's knowledge of natural or divine things. Second, as to the mode of knowing; for instance, if a builder considers a house by defining and dividing and considering its universal predicates. For this is to consider things capable of being made in a speculative mode, and not according as they are capable of being made; for something is capable of being made through the application of form to matter, not through the resolution of a composite into universal formal principles. Third, as to the end, *for the practical intellect differs from the speculative by its end*, as is said in *On the Soul* III.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","text":"For the practical intellect is ordered to the end of operation, whereas the end of the speculative intellect is the consideration of truth. Hence, if some builder considers how a certain house could be made, ordering this not to the end of operation but only to knowing, the consideration will be speculative as to its end, though concerning a thing capable of being made. Therefore, knowledge which is speculative by reason of the very thing known is speculative only. But that which is speculative either according to its mode or according to its end is in some respect speculative and in some respect practical. But when it is ordered to the end of operation, it is simply practical. Accordingly, therefore, it must be said that God has knowledge of Himself that is speculative only, for He Himself is not capable of being made. But of all other things He has both speculative and practical knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","text":"Speculative indeed as to the mode, for whatever we know speculatively in things by defining and dividing, God knows all this much more perfectly. But of those things which He can indeed make, but does not make at any time, He does not have practical knowledge, insofar as practical knowledge is denominated from its end. But He has practical knowledge in this way of those things which He makes at some time. Evils, however, although they are not capable of being made by Him, nevertheless fall under His practical cognition, just as goods do, insofar as He permits or impedes or orders them; just as illnesses fall under the practical knowledge of a physician, insofar as through his art he cures them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0295","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0295"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0296","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God's knowledge is the cause, not indeed of itself, but of other things: of some actually, namely, those which come to be at some time; but of others virtually, namely, those which He can make and yet never come to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0296"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0297","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0297"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0298","text":"To the second, it must be said that for knowledge to be received from the things known does not belong to speculative knowledge in itself, but accidentally, insofar as it is human.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0298"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0299","text":"[I q. 14 a. 16 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html#29018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0299"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0300","text":"But to that which is objected to the contrary, it must be said that perfect knowledge of things capable of being made is not had unless they are known insofar as they are capable of being made. And therefore, since God's knowledge is perfect in every way, it is necessary that He know those things which can be made by Him, as such, and not only according as they are capable of speculative consideration. Yet there is no departure from the nobility of speculative knowledge, because He sees all other things than Himself in Himself, while He knows Himself speculatively; and thus, in His speculative knowledge of Himself, He has both speculative and practical cognition of all other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0300"]}],"source_digest":"3dd9be820557f79f1a22bd998f55f253c9224ed94673375b5820578615db694f","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 14"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 14","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7","title":"First Part · Question 15","order":16,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 15 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29019)\n\nPost considerationem de scientia Dei, restat considerare de ideis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, an sint ideae. Secundo, utrum sint plures, vel una tantum. Tertio, utrum sint omnium quae cognoscuntur a Deo.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29020)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ideae non sint. Dicit enim Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod Deus non cognoscit res secundum ideam. Sed ideae non ponuntur ad aliud, nisi ut per eas cognoscantur res. Ergo ideae non sunt.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29021)\n\nPraeterea, Deus in seipso cognoscit omnia, ut supra dictum est. Sed seipsum non cognoscit per ideam. Ergo nec alia.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29022)\n\nPraeterea, idea ponitur ut principium cognoscendi et operandi. Sed essentia divina est sufficiens principium cognoscendi et operandi omnia. Non ergo necesse est ponere ideas.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29023)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *tanta vis in ideis constituitur, ut, nisi his intellectis, sapiens esse nemo possit*.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29024)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere in mente divina ideas. Idea enim Graece, Latine forma dicitur, unde per ideas intelliguntur formae aliarum rerum, praeter ipsas res existentes. Forma autem alicuius rei praeter ipsam existens, ad duo esse potest, vel ut sit exemplar eius cuius dicitur forma; vel ut sit principium cognitionis ipsius, secundum quod formae cognoscibilium dicuntur esse in cognoscente. Et quantum ad utrumque est necesse ponere ideas. Quod sic patet. In omnibus enim quae non a casu generantur, necesse est formam esse finem generationis cuiuscumque. Agens autem non ageret propter formam, nisi inquantum similitudo formae est in ipso. Quod quidem contingit dupliciter. In quibusdam enim agentibus praeexistit forma rei fiendae secundum esse naturale, sicut in his quae agunt per naturam; sicut homo generat hominem, et ignis ignem. In quibusdam vero secundum esse intelligibile, ut in his quae agunt per intellectum; sicut similitudo domus praeexistit in mente aedificatoris. Et haec potest dici idea domus, quia artifex intendit domum assimilare formae quam mente concepit. Quia igitur mundus non est casu factus, sed est factus a Deo per intellectum agente, ut infra patebit, necesse est quod in mente divina sit forma, ad similitudinem cuius mundus est factus. Et in hoc consistit ratio ideae.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29025)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus non intelligit res secundum ideam extra se existentem. Et sic etiam Aristoteles improbat opinionem Platonis de ideis, secundum quod ponebat eas per se existentes, non in intellectu.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29026)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet Deus per essentiam suam se et alia cognoscat, tamen essentia sua est principium operativum aliorum, non autem sui ipsius, et ideo habet rationem ideae secundum quod ad alia comparatur, non autem secundum quod comparatur ad ipsum Deum.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29027)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Deus secundum essentiam suam est similitudo omnium rerum. Unde idea in Deo nihil est aliud quam Dei essentia.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29028)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint plures ideae. Idea enim in Deo est eius essentia. Sed essentia Dei est una tantum. Ergo et idea est una.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29029)\n\nPraeterea, sicut idea est principium cognoscendi et operandi, ita ars et sapientia. Sed in Deo non sunt plures artes et sapientiae. Ergo nec plures ideae.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29030)\n\nSi dicatur quod ideae multiplicantur secundum respectus ad diversas creaturas, contra, pluralitas idearum est ab aeterno. Si ergo ideae sunt plures, creaturae autem sunt temporales, ergo temporale erit causa aeterni.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29031)\n\nPraeterea, respectus isti aut sunt secundum rem in creaturis tantum, aut etiam in Deo. Si in creaturis tantum, cum creaturae non sint ab aeterno, pluralitas idearum non erit ab aeterno, si multiplicentur solum secundum huiusmodi respectus. Si autem realiter sunt in Deo, sequitur quod alia pluralitas realis sit in Deo quam pluralitas personarum, quod est contra Damascenum, dicentem quod in divinis omnia unum sunt, praeter ingenerationem, generationem et processionem. Sic igitur non sunt plures ideae.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29032)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *ideae sunt principales quaedam formae vel rationes rerum stabiles atque incommutabiles, quia ipsae formatae non sunt, ac per hoc aeternae ac semper eodem modo se habentes, quae divina intelligentia continentur. Sed cum ipsae neque oriantur neque intereant, secundum eas tamen formari dicitur omne quod oriri et interire potest, et omne quod oritur et interit*.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29033)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere plures ideas. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod in quolibet effectu illud quod est ultimus finis, proprie est intentum a principali agente; sicut ordo exercitus a duce. Illud autem quod est optimum in rebus existens, est bonum ordinis universi, ut patet per philosophum in XII Metaphys. Ordo igitur universi est proprie a Deo intentus, et non per accidens proveniens secundum successionem agentium, prout quidam dixerunt quod Deus creavit primum creatum tantum, quod creatum creavit secundum creatum, et sic inde quousque producta est tanta rerum multitudo, secundum quam opinionem, Deus non haberet nisi ideam primi creati. Sed si ipse ordo universi est per se creatus ab eo, et intentus ab ipso, necesse est quod habeat ideam ordinis universi. Ratio autem alicuius totius haberi non potest, nisi habeantur propriae rationes eorum ex quibus totum constituitur, sicut aedificator speciem domus concipere non posset, nisi apud ipsum esset propria ratio cuiuslibet partium eius. Sic igitur oportet quod in mente divina sint propriae rationes omnium rerum. Unde dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *singula propriis rationibus a Deo creata sunt*. Unde sequitur quod in mente divina sint plures ideae. Hoc autem quomodo divinae simplicitati non repugnet, facile est videre, si quis consideret ideam operati esse in mente operantis sicut quod intelligitur; non autem sicut species qua intelligitur, quae est forma faciens intellectum in actu. Forma enim domus in mente aedificatoris est aliquid ab eo intellectum, ad cuius similitudinem domum in materia format. Non est autem contra simplicitatem divini intellectus, quod multa intelligat, sed contra simplicitatem eius esset, si per plures species eius intellectus formaretur. Unde plures ideae sunt in mente divina ut intellectae ab ipso. Quod hoc modo potest videri. Ipse enim essentiam suam perfecte cognoscit, unde cognoscit eam secundum omnem modum quo cognoscibilis est. Potest autem cognosci non solum secundum quod in se est, sed secundum quod est participabilis secundum aliquem modum similitudinis a creaturis. Unaquaeque autem creatura habet propriam speciem, secundum quod aliquo modo participat divinae essentiae similitudinem. Sic igitur inquantum Deus cognoscit suam essentiam ut sic imitabilem a tali creatura, cognoscit eam ut propriam rationem et ideam huius creaturae. Et similiter de aliis. Et sic patet quod Deus intelligit plures rationes proprias plurium rerum; quae sunt plures ideae.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29034)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod idea non nominat divinam essentiam inquantum est essentia, sed inquantum est similitudo vel ratio huius vel illius rei. Unde secundum quod sunt plures rationes intellectae ex una essentia, secundum hoc dicuntur plures ideae.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29035)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sapientia et ars significantur ut quo Deus intelligit, sed idea ut quod Deus intelligit. Deus autem uno intelligit multa; et non solum secundum quod in seipsis sunt, sed etiam secundum quod intellecta sunt; quod est intelligere plures rationes rerum. Sicut artifex, dum intelligit formam domus in materia, dicitur intelligere domum, dum autem intelligit formam domus ut a se speculatam, ex eo quod intelligit se intelligere eam, intelligit ideam vel rationem domus. Deus autem non solum intelligit multas res per essentiam suam, sed etiam intelligit se intelligere multa per essentiam suam. Sed hoc est intelligere plures rationes rerum; vel, plures ideas esse in intellectu eius ut intellectas.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29036)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod huiusmodi respectus, quibus multiplicantur ideae, non causantur a rebus, sed ab intellectu divino, comparante essentiam suam ad res.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29037)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod respectus multiplicantes ideas, non sunt in rebus creatis, sed in Deo. Non tamen sunt reales respectus, sicut illi quibus distinguuntur personae, sed respectus intellecti a Deo.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29038)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnium quae cognoscit Deus, sint ideae in ipso. Mali enim idea non est in Deo, quia sequeretur malum esse in Deo. Sed mala cognoscuntur a Deo. Ergo non omnium quae cognoscuntur a Deo, sunt ideae.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29039)\n\nPraeterea, Deus cognoscit ea quae nec sunt nec erunt nec fuerunt, ut supra dictum est. Sed horum non sunt ideae, quia dicit Dionysius, V cap. de Div. Nom., quod *exemplaria sunt divinae voluntates, determinativae et effectivae rerum*. Ergo non omnium quae a Deo cognoscuntur, sunt ideae in ipso.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29040)\n\nPraeterea, Deus cognoscit materiam primam, quae non potest habere ideam, cum nullam habeat formam. Ergo idem quod prius.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29041)\n\nPraeterea, constat quod Deus scit non solum species, sed etiam genera et singularia et accidentia. Sed horum non sunt ideae, secundum positionem Platonis, qui primus ideas introduxit, ut dicit Augustinus. Non ergo omnium cognitorum a Deo sunt ideae in ipso.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29042)\n\nSed contra, ideae sunt rationes in mente divina existentes, ut per Augustinum patet. Sed omnium quae cognoscit, Deus habet proprias rationes. Ergo omnium quae cognoscit, habet ideam.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29043)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum ideae a Platone ponerentur principia cognitionis rerum et generationis ipsarum, ad utrumque se habet idea, prout in mente divina ponitur. Et secundum quod est principium factionis rerum, exemplar dici potest, et ad practicam cognitionem pertinet. Secundum autem quod principium cognoscitivum est, proprie dicitur ratio; et potest etiam ad scientiam speculativam pertinere. Secundum ergo quod exemplar est, secundum hoc se habet ad omnia quae a Deo fiunt secundum aliquod tempus. Secundum vero quod principium cognoscitivum est, se habet ad omnia quae cognoscuntur a Deo, etiam si nullo tempore fiant; et ad omnia quae a Deo cognoscuntur secundum propriam rationem, et secundum quod cognoscuntur ab ipso per modum speculationis.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29044)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod malum cognoscitur a Deo non per propriam rationem, sed per rationem boni. Et ideo malum non habet in Deo ideam, neque secundum quod idea est exemplar, neque secundum quod est ratio.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29045)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod eorum quae neque sunt neque erunt neque fuerunt, Deus non habet practicam cognitionem, nisi virtute tantum. Unde respectu eorum non est idea in Deo, secundum quod idea significat exemplar, sed solum secundum quod significat rationem.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29046)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Plato, secundum quosdam, posuit materiam non creatam, et ideo non posuit ideam esse materiae, sed materiae concausam. Sed quia nos ponimus materiam creatam a Deo, non tamen sine forma, habet quidem materia ideam in Deo, non tamen aliam ab idea compositi. Nam materia secundum se neque esse habet, neque cognoscibilis est.\n\n[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29047)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod genera non possunt habere ideam aliam ab idea speciei, secundum quod idea significat exemplar, quia nunquam genus fit nisi in aliqua specie. Similiter etiam est de accidentibus quae inseparabiliter concomitantur subiectum, quia haec simul fiunt cum subiecto. Accidentia autem quae superveniunt subiecto, specialem ideam habent. Artifex enim per formam domus facit omnia accidentia quae a principio concomitantur domum, sed ea quae superveniunt domui iam factae, ut picturae vel aliquid aliud, facit per aliquam aliam formam. Individua vero, secundum Platonem, non habebant aliam ideam quam ideam speciei, tum quia singularia individuantur per materiam, quam ponebat esse increatam, ut quidam dicunt, et concausam ideae; tum quia intentio naturae consistit in speciebus, nec particularia producit, nisi ut in eis species salventur. Sed providentia divina non solum se extendit ad species, sed ad singularia, ut infra dicetur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0002","text":"[I q. 15 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0003","text":"Post considerationem de scientia Dei, restat considerare de ideis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, an sint ideae. Secundo, utrum sint plures, vel una tantum. Tertio, utrum sint omnium quae cognoscuntur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0005","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ideae non sint. Dicit enim Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod Deus non cognoscit res secundum ideam. Sed ideae non ponuntur ad aliud, nisi ut per eas cognoscantur res. Ergo ideae non sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0007","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Deus in seipso cognoscit omnia, ut supra dictum est. Sed seipsum non cognoscit per ideam. Ergo nec alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0009","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0010","text":"Praeterea, idea ponitur ut principium cognoscendi et operandi. Sed essentia divina est sufficiens principium cognoscendi et operandi omnia. Non ergo necesse est ponere ideas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0011","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *tanta vis in ideis constituitur, ut, nisi his intellectis, sapiens esse nemo possit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0013","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere in mente divina ideas. Idea enim Graece, Latine forma dicitur, unde per ideas intelliguntur formae aliarum rerum, praeter ipsas res existentes. Forma autem alicuius rei praeter ipsam existens, ad duo esse potest, vel ut sit exemplar eius cuius dicitur forma; vel ut sit principium cognitionis ipsius, secundum quod formae cognoscibilium dicuntur esse in cognoscente. Et quantum ad utrumque est necesse ponere ideas. Quod sic patet. In omnibus enim quae non a casu generantur, necesse est formam esse finem generationis cuiuscumque. Agens autem non ageret propter formam, nisi inquantum similitudo formae est in ipso. Quod quidem contingit dupliciter. In quibusdam enim agentibus praeexistit forma rei fiendae secundum esse naturale, sicut in his quae agunt per naturam; sicut homo generat hominem, et ignis ignem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","text":"In quibusdam vero secundum esse intelligibile, ut in his quae agunt per intellectum; sicut similitudo domus praeexistit in mente aedificatoris. Et haec potest dici idea domus, quia artifex intendit domum assimilare formae quam mente concepit. Quia igitur mundus non est casu factus, sed est factus a Deo per intellectum agente, ut infra patebit, necesse est quod in mente divina sit forma, ad similitudinem cuius mundus est factus. Et in hoc consistit ratio ideae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0016","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus non intelligit res secundum ideam extra se existentem. Et sic etiam Aristoteles improbat opinionem Platonis de ideis, secundum quod ponebat eas per se existentes, non in intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0018","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet Deus per essentiam suam se et alia cognoscat, tamen essentia sua est principium operativum aliorum, non autem sui ipsius, et ideo habet rationem ideae secundum quod ad alia comparatur, non autem secundum quod comparatur ad ipsum Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0020","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Deus secundum essentiam suam est similitudo omnium rerum. Unde idea in Deo nihil est aliud quam Dei essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0023","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint plures ideae. Idea enim in Deo est eius essentia. Sed essentia Dei est una tantum. Ergo et idea est una.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0025","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0026","text":"Praeterea, sicut idea est principium cognoscendi et operandi, ita ars et sapientia. Sed in Deo non sunt plures artes et sapientiae. Ergo nec plures ideae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0027","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0028","text":"Si dicatur quod ideae multiplicantur secundum respectus ad diversas creaturas, contra, pluralitas idearum est ab aeterno. Si ergo ideae sunt plures, creaturae autem sunt temporales, ergo temporale erit causa aeterni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0029","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0030","text":"Praeterea, respectus isti aut sunt secundum rem in creaturis tantum, aut etiam in Deo. Si in creaturis tantum, cum creaturae non sint ab aeterno, pluralitas idearum non erit ab aeterno, si multiplicentur solum secundum huiusmodi respectus. Si autem realiter sunt in Deo, sequitur quod alia pluralitas realis sit in Deo quam pluralitas personarum, quod est contra Damascenum, dicentem quod in divinis omnia unum sunt, praeter ingenerationem, generationem et processionem. Sic igitur non sunt plures ideae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0031","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *ideae sunt principales quaedam formae vel rationes rerum stabiles atque incommutabiles, quia ipsae formatae non sunt, ac per hoc aeternae ac semper eodem modo se habentes, quae divina intelligentia continentur. Sed cum ipsae neque oriantur neque intereant, secundum eas tamen formari dicitur omne quod oriri et interire potest, et omne quod oritur et interit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0033","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere plures ideas. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod in quolibet effectu illud quod est ultimus finis, proprie est intentum a principali agente; sicut ordo exercitus a duce. Illud autem quod est optimum in rebus existens, est bonum ordinis universi, ut patet per philosophum in XII Metaphys. Ordo igitur universi est proprie a Deo intentus, et non per accidens proveniens secundum successionem agentium, prout quidam dixerunt quod Deus creavit primum creatum tantum, quod creatum creavit secundum creatum, et sic inde quousque producta est tanta rerum multitudo, secundum quam opinionem, Deus non haberet nisi ideam primi creati. Sed si ipse ordo universi est per se creatus ab eo, et intentus ab ipso, necesse est quod habeat ideam ordinis universi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","text":"Ratio autem alicuius totius haberi non potest, nisi habeantur propriae rationes eorum ex quibus totum constituitur, sicut aedificator speciem domus concipere non posset, nisi apud ipsum esset propria ratio cuiuslibet partium eius. Sic igitur oportet quod in mente divina sint propriae rationes omnium rerum. Unde dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *singula propriis rationibus a Deo creata sunt*. Unde sequitur quod in mente divina sint plures ideae. Hoc autem quomodo divinae simplicitati non repugnet, facile est videre, si quis consideret ideam operati esse in mente operantis sicut quod intelligitur; non autem sicut species qua intelligitur, quae est forma faciens intellectum in actu. Forma enim domus in mente aedificatoris est aliquid ab eo intellectum, ad cuius similitudinem domum in materia format.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","text":"Non est autem contra simplicitatem divini intellectus, quod multa intelligat, sed contra simplicitatem eius esset, si per plures species eius intellectus formaretur. Unde plures ideae sunt in mente divina ut intellectae ab ipso. Quod hoc modo potest videri. Ipse enim essentiam suam perfecte cognoscit, unde cognoscit eam secundum omnem modum quo cognoscibilis est. Potest autem cognosci non solum secundum quod in se est, sed secundum quod est participabilis secundum aliquem modum similitudinis a creaturis. Unaquaeque autem creatura habet propriam speciem, secundum quod aliquo modo participat divinae essentiae similitudinem. Sic igitur inquantum Deus cognoscit suam essentiam ut sic imitabilem a tali creatura, cognoscit eam ut propriam rationem et ideam huius creaturae. Et similiter de aliis. Et sic patet quod Deus intelligit plures rationes proprias plurium rerum; quae sunt plures ideae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0037","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod idea non nominat divinam essentiam inquantum est essentia, sed inquantum est similitudo vel ratio huius vel illius rei. Unde secundum quod sunt plures rationes intellectae ex una essentia, secundum hoc dicuntur plures ideae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0039","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sapientia et ars significantur ut quo Deus intelligit, sed idea ut quod Deus intelligit. Deus autem uno intelligit multa; et non solum secundum quod in seipsis sunt, sed etiam secundum quod intellecta sunt; quod est intelligere plures rationes rerum. Sicut artifex, dum intelligit formam domus in materia, dicitur intelligere domum, dum autem intelligit formam domus ut a se speculatam, ex eo quod intelligit se intelligere eam, intelligit ideam vel rationem domus. Deus autem non solum intelligit multas res per essentiam suam, sed etiam intelligit se intelligere multa per essentiam suam. Sed hoc est intelligere plures rationes rerum; vel, plures ideas esse in intellectu eius ut intellectas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0041","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod huiusmodi respectus, quibus multiplicantur ideae, non causantur a rebus, sed ab intellectu divino, comparante essentiam suam ad res.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0043","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod respectus multiplicantes ideas, non sunt in rebus creatis, sed in Deo. Non tamen sunt reales respectus, sicut illi quibus distinguuntur personae, sed respectus intellecti a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0045","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0046","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0047","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnium quae cognoscit Deus, sint ideae in ipso. Mali enim idea non est in Deo, quia sequeretur malum esse in Deo. Sed mala cognoscuntur a Deo. Ergo non omnium quae cognoscuntur a Deo, sunt ideae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0048","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0049","text":"Praeterea, Deus cognoscit ea quae nec sunt nec erunt nec fuerunt, ut supra dictum est. Sed horum non sunt ideae, quia dicit Dionysius, V cap. de Div. Nom., quod *exemplaria sunt divinae voluntates, determinativae et effectivae rerum*. Ergo non omnium quae a Deo cognoscuntur, sunt ideae in ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0050","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0051","text":"Praeterea, Deus cognoscit materiam primam, quae non potest habere ideam, cum nullam habeat formam. Ergo idem quod prius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0052","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0053","text":"Praeterea, constat quod Deus scit non solum species, sed etiam genera et singularia et accidentia. Sed horum non sunt ideae, secundum positionem Platonis, qui primus ideas introduxit, ut dicit Augustinus. Non ergo omnium cognitorum a Deo sunt ideae in ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0054","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0055","text":"Sed contra, ideae sunt rationes in mente divina existentes, ut per Augustinum patet. Sed omnium quae cognoscit, Deus habet proprias rationes. Ergo omnium quae cognoscit, habet ideam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0056","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum ideae a Platone ponerentur principia cognitionis rerum et generationis ipsarum, ad utrumque se habet idea, prout in mente divina ponitur. Et secundum quod est principium factionis rerum, exemplar dici potest, et ad practicam cognitionem pertinet. Secundum autem quod principium cognoscitivum est, proprie dicitur ratio; et potest etiam ad scientiam speculativam pertinere. Secundum ergo quod exemplar est, secundum hoc se habet ad omnia quae a Deo fiunt secundum aliquod tempus. Secundum vero quod principium cognoscitivum est, se habet ad omnia quae cognoscuntur a Deo, etiam si nullo tempore fiant; et ad omnia quae a Deo cognoscuntur secundum propriam rationem, et secundum quod cognoscuntur ab ipso per modum speculationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0058","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod malum cognoscitur a Deo non per propriam rationem, sed per rationem boni. Et ideo malum non habet in Deo ideam, neque secundum quod idea est exemplar, neque secundum quod est ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0060","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod eorum quae neque sunt neque erunt neque fuerunt, Deus non habet practicam cognitionem, nisi virtute tantum. Unde respectu eorum non est idea in Deo, secundum quod idea significat exemplar, sed solum secundum quod significat rationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0062","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Plato, secundum quosdam, posuit materiam non creatam, et ideo non posuit ideam esse materiae, sed materiae concausam. Sed quia nos ponimus materiam creatam a Deo, non tamen sine forma, habet quidem materia ideam in Deo, non tamen aliam ab idea compositi. Nam materia secundum se neque esse habet, neque cognoscibilis est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0064","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod genera non possunt habere ideam aliam ab idea speciei, secundum quod idea significat exemplar, quia nunquam genus fit nisi in aliqua specie. Similiter etiam est de accidentibus quae inseparabiliter concomitantur subiectum, quia haec simul fiunt cum subiecto. Accidentia autem quae superveniunt subiecto, specialem ideam habent. Artifex enim per formam domus facit omnia accidentia quae a principio concomitantur domum, sed ea quae superveniunt domui iam factae, ut picturae vel aliquid aliud, facit per aliquam aliam formam. Individua vero, secundum Platonem, non habebant aliam ideam quam ideam speciei, tum quia singularia individuantur per materiam, quam ponebat esse increatam, ut quidam dicunt, et concausam ideae; tum quia intentio naturae consistit in speciebus, nec particularia producit, nisi ut in eis species salventur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0066","text":"Sed providentia divina non solum se extendit ad species, sed ad singularia, ut infra dicetur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"50d4a6427b543643a5dff1803d3164c4aed1288edabae9461cf7810f235e8540","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T135331798373Z_bbd9495f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 15","source_sha256":"50d4a6427b543643a5dff1803d3164c4aed1288edabae9461cf7810f235e8540","sha256":"e6b378abf8c607344fb22215422e7d0cd7f1bc3bc2581eb65d92f98cffc70e58","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:53:31.798411+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0002","text":"[I q. 15 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0003","text":"After the consideration of God's knowledge, it remains to consider ideas. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, whether ideas exist. Second, whether they are many, or only one. Third, whether they are of all things known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0005","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0006","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that ideas do not exist. For Dionysius says, in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*, that God does not know things according to an idea. But ideas are posited for no other purpose than that things may be known through them. Therefore, ideas do not exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0007","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0008","text":"Further, God knows all things in Himself, as was said above. But He does not know Himself through an idea. Therefore, neither does He know other things thus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0009","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0010","text":"Further, an idea is posited as a principle of knowing and of operating. But the divine essence is a sufficient principle for knowing and operating all things. Therefore, it is not necessary to posit ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0011","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *such great power is ascribed to ideas that no one can be wise unless he understands them*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0013","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is necessary to posit ideas in the divine mind. For idea in Greek is called *forma* in Latin; whence by ideas are understood the forms of other things, existing apart from the things themselves. Now a form of some thing existing apart from it can serve two purposes: either that it may be the exemplar of that of which it is said to be the form; or that it may be a principle of knowing it, insofar as the forms of knowable things are said to be in the knower. And with respect to both, it is necessary to posit ideas. This is clear as follows. For in all things that are generated not by chance, the form must be the end of every generation. But an agent would not act for the sake of a form except insofar as a likeness of the form is in it. This indeed occurs in two ways. For in some agents, the form of the thing to be made pre-exists according to natural being, as in those that act through nature; thus a man generates a man, and fire fire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","text":"But in some, it pre-exists according to intelligible being, as in those that act through intellect; thus the likeness of a house pre-exists in the mind of the builder. And this can be called the idea of the house, because the craftsman intends to assimilate the house to the form that he has conceived in his mind. Therefore, since the world was not made by chance, but was made by God acting through intellect, as will be clear below, it is necessary that there be in the divine mind a form to the likeness of which the world was made. And in this consists the notion of an idea.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0016","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God does not understand things according to an idea existing outside Himself. And thus Aristotle also refutes Plato's opinion concerning ideas, insofar as he posited them as existing through themselves, not in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0018","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","text":"To the second it must be said that, although God knows Himself and other things through His essence, nevertheless His essence is an operative principle of other things, but not of Himself; and therefore it has the character of an idea insofar as it is related to other things, but not insofar as it is related to God Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0020","text":"[I q. 15 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","text":"To the third it must be said that God, according to His essence, is the likeness of all things. Hence, an idea in God is nothing other than God's essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0023","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0024","text":"The second is approached thus. It seems that there are not several ideas. For an idea in God is His essence. But God's essence is only one. Therefore, the idea too is one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0025","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0026","text":"Further, just as an idea is a principle of knowing and acting, so too are art and wisdom. But in God there are not several arts and wisdoms. Therefore neither are there several ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0027","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0028","text":"If it be said that ideas are multiplied according to relations to diverse creatures, on the contrary, the plurality of ideas is from eternity. Therefore, if the ideas are several, but creatures are temporal, then the temporal will be the cause of the eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0029","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0030","text":"Further, these relations are either according to reality in creatures alone, or also in God. If in creatures alone, since creatures are not from eternity, the plurality of ideas will not be from eternity, if they are multiplied only according to relations of this kind. But if they are really in God, it follows that there is in God another real plurality than the plurality of persons, which is contrary to Damascene, who says that in divine things all are one, except ingenerateness, generation, and procession. Therefore there are not several ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0031","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0032","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *ideas are certain principal forms or stable and unchangeable rationes of things, because they themselves are not formed, and on this account are eternal and always disposed in the same way; and they are contained in the divine intelligence. But although they themselves neither arise nor perish, nevertheless according to them everything that can arise and perish, and everything that does arise and perish, is said to be formed*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0033","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","text":"I answer that it must be held that there are several ideas. For the evidence of this, it should be considered that in every effect that which is the ultimate end is properly intended by the principal agent; just as the order of an army is intended by its commander. But that which is best among existing things is the good of the order of the universe, as is clear from the Philosopher in *Metaphysics* XII. Therefore the order of the universe is properly intended by God, and does not come about accidentally according to the succession of agents, as some have said: that God created only the first creature, which creature created a second creature, and so on until such a multitude of things was produced; according to which opinion, God would have only the idea of the first creature. But if the very order of the universe is created by Him through itself and intended by Him, it is necessary that He have the idea of the order of the universe.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","text":"But the ratio of some whole cannot be possessed unless the proper rationes of those things from which the whole is constituted are possessed; just as a builder could not conceive the species of a house unless he had within himself the proper ratio of each of its parts. Thus, therefore, there must be in the divine mind the proper rationes of all things. Hence Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, that *individual things were created by God according to their proper rationes*. Hence it follows that there are several ideas in the divine mind. But how this does not conflict with divine simplicity is easy to see, if one considers that the idea of the thing made is in the mind of the maker as that which is understood, and not as the species by which it is understood, which is the form making the intellect actual. For the form of a house in the mind of a builder is something understood by him, according to the likeness of which he forms the house in matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","text":"Now it is not contrary to the simplicity of the divine intellect that it understands many things; but it would be contrary to its simplicity if its intellect were informed by several species. Hence several ideas are in the divine mind as understood by Him. This can be seen in the following way. For He knows His essence perfectly; hence He knows it according to every mode in which it is knowable. But it can be known not only according as it is in itself, but also according as it is participable by creatures according to some mode of likeness. Now each creature has its proper species, according as it participates in some way in the likeness of the divine essence. Thus, therefore, insofar as God knows His essence as thus imitable by such a creature, He knows it as the proper ratio and idea of this creature. And similarly concerning the others. And thus it is clear that God understands several proper rationes of several things, which are several ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0037","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that idea does not signify the divine essence insofar as it is essence, but insofar as it is the likeness or ratio of this or that thing. Hence, according as several rationes are understood from one essence, so several ideas are spoken of.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0039","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0040","text":"To the second it must be said that wisdom and art are signified as that by which God understands, but idea as that which God understands. Now God understands many things by one thing; and not only according as they are in themselves, but also according as they are understood, which is to understand several rationes of things. Just as an artisan, while understanding the form of a house in matter, is said to understand the house; but while understanding the form of the house as contemplated by himself, from the fact that he understands that he understands it, he understands the idea or ratio of the house. But God not only understands many things through His essence, but also understands that He understands many things through His essence. But this is to understand several rationes of things; or, that several ideas are in His intellect as understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0041","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","text":"To the third it must be said that relations of this kind, by which ideas are multiplied, are caused not by things, but by the divine intellect, comparing His essence to things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0043","text":"[I q. 15 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the relations multiplying ideas are not in created things, but in God. Yet they are not real relations, like those by which the persons are distinguished, but relations understood by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0045","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0046","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0047","text":"We proceed to the third as follows. It seems that there are not ideas in God of all things that God knows. For there is no idea of evil in God, because it would follow that evil is in God. But evils are known by God. Therefore there are not ideas of all things that are known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0048","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0049","text":"Moreover, God knows those things which neither are nor will be nor have been, as was said above. But there are no ideas of these, because Dionysius says, in chapter V of *On the Divine Names*, that *exemplars are divine wills, determinative and effective of things*. Therefore, there are not in God ideas of all things known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0050","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0051","text":"Moreover, God knows prime matter, which cannot have an idea, since it has no form. Therefore, the same as before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0052","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0053","text":"Moreover, it is clear that God knows not only species, but also genera and singulars and accidents. But of these there are no ideas, according to Plato's position—who first introduced ideas, as Augustine says. Therefore, there are not in God ideas of all things known by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0054","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0055","text":"On the contrary, ideas are reasons existing in the divine mind, as is evident through Augustine. But God has proper reasons of all things that He knows. Therefore, He has an idea of all things that He knows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0056","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","text":"I answer that, since ideas were posited by Plato as principles both of the knowledge of things and of their generation, an idea, insofar as it is posited in the divine mind, bears upon both. And insofar as it is a principle of the making of things, it can be called an exemplar, and pertains to practical knowledge. But insofar as it is a cognitive principle, it is properly called a reason; and it can also pertain to speculative knowledge. Therefore, insofar as it is an exemplar, it bears upon all things that are made by God at some time. But insofar as it is a cognitive principle, it bears upon all things known by God, even if they are made at no time; and upon all things known by God according to their proper reason, and insofar as they are known by Him in the mode of speculation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0058","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that evil is known by God not through its proper reason, but through the reason of good. And therefore evil does not have an idea in God, neither insofar as an idea is an exemplar nor insofar as it is a reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0060","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","text":"To the second, it must be said that of those things which neither are nor will be nor have been, God does not have practical knowledge except by power alone. Hence, with respect to these, there is no idea in God insofar as idea signifies an exemplar, but only insofar as it signifies a reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0062","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","text":"To the third, it must be said that Plato, according to some, posited matter as uncreated, and therefore did not posit an idea of matter, but [posited] matter as a co-cause of the idea. But because we posit matter as created by God, though not without form, matter indeed has an idea in God, though not one other than the idea of the composite. For matter, considered in itself, neither has being nor is knowable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0064","text":"[I q. 15 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that genera cannot have an idea other than the idea of the species, insofar as idea signifies an exemplar, because a genus is never made except in some species. The same is likewise true of accidents that inseparably accompany a subject, because these come to be simultaneously with the subject. But accidents that supervene upon a subject have a special idea. For an artisan, through the form of a house, makes all the accidents that accompany the house from the beginning; but those that supervene upon a house already made, such as paintings or something else, he makes through some other form. But individuals, according to Plato, did not have an idea other than the idea of the species: both because singulars are individuated through matter, which he posited to be uncreated, as some say, and a co-cause of the idea; and because the intention of nature consists in species, nor does it produce particulars except so that the species may be preserved in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0066","text":"But divine providence extends itself not only to species, but also to singulars, as will be said below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0066"]}],"source_digest":"adcd4e670b24cdaa3508c32956494027e1b17cc669b6609e800654b2d6b4e54d","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 15"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8","title":"First Part · Question 16","order":17,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 16 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29048)\n\nQuoniam autem scientia verorum est, post considerationem scientiae Dei, de veritate inquirendum est. Circa quam quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum veritas sit in re, vel tantum in intellectu. Secundo, utrum sit tantum in intellectu componente et dividente. Tertio, de comparatione veri ad ens. Quarto, de comparatione veri ad bonum. Quinto, utrum Deus sit veritas. Sexto, utrum omnia sint vera veritate una, vel pluribus. Septimo, de aeternitate veritatis. Octavo, de incommutabilitate ipsius.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29049)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod veritas non sit tantum in intellectu, sed magis in rebus. Augustinus enim, in libro Soliloq., reprobat hanc notificationem veri, verum est id quod videtur, quia secundum hoc, lapides qui sunt in abditissimo terrae sinu, non essent veri lapides, quia non videntur. Reprobat etiam istam, verum est quod ita se habet ut videtur cognitori, si velit et possit cognoscere, quia secundum hoc sequeretur quod nihil esset verum, si nullus posset cognoscere. Et definit sic verum, verum est id quod est. Et sic videtur quod veritas sit in rebus, et non in intellectu.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29050)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid est verum, veritate verum est. Si igitur veritas est in intellectu solo, nihil erit verum nisi secundum quod intelligitur, quod est error antiquorum philosophorum, qui dicebant omne quod videtur, esse verum. Ad quod sequitur contradictoria simul esse vera, cum contradictoria simul a diversis vera esse videantur.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29051)\n\n*Praeterea, propter quod unumquodque, et illud magis*, ut patet I Poster. *Sed ex eo quod res est vel non est, est opinio vel oratio vera vel falsa*, secundum philosophum in praedicamentis. Ergo veritas magis est in rebus quam in intellectu.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29052)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, VI Metaphys., quod *verum et falsum non sunt in rebus, sed in intellectu*.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29053)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut bonum nominat id in quod tendit appetitus, ita verum nominat id in quod tendit intellectus. Hoc autem distat inter appetitum et intellectum, sive quamcumque cognitionem, quia cognitio est secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente, appetitus autem est secundum quod appetens inclinatur in ipsam rem appetitam. Et sic terminus appetitus, quod est bonum, est in re appetibili, sed terminus cognitionis, quod est verum, est in ipso intellectu. Sicut autem bonum est in re, inquantum habet ordinem ad appetitum; et propter hoc ratio bonitatis derivatur a re appetibili in appetitum, secundum quod appetitus dicitur bonus, prout est boni, ita, cum verum sit in intellectu secundum quod conformatur rei intellectae, necesse est quod ratio veri ab intellectu ad rem intellectam derivetur, ut res etiam intellecta vera dicatur, secundum quod habet aliquem ordinem ad intellectum. Res autem intellecta ad intellectum aliquem potest habere ordinem vel per se, vel per accidens. Per se quidem habet ordinem ad intellectum a quo dependet secundum suum esse, per accidens autem ad intellectum a quo cognoscibilis est. Sicut si dicamus quod domus comparatur ad intellectum artificis per se, per accidens autem comparatur ad intellectum a quo non dependet. Iudicium autem de re non sumitur secundum id quod inest ei per accidens, sed secundum id quod inest ei per se. Unde unaquaeque res dicitur vera absolute, secundum ordinem ad intellectum a quo dependet. Et inde est quod res artificiales dicuntur verae per ordinem ad intellectum nostrum, dicitur enim domus vera, quae assequitur similitudinem formae quae est in mente artificis; et dicitur oratio vera, inquantum est signum intellectus veri. Et similiter res naturales dicuntur esse verae, secundum quod assequuntur similitudinem specierum quae sunt in mente divina, dicitur enim verus lapis, qui assequitur propriam lapidis naturam, secundum praeconceptionem intellectus divini. Sic ergo veritas principaliter est in intellectu; secundario vero in rebus, secundum quod comparantur ad intellectum ut ad principium. Et secundum hoc, veritas diversimode notificatur. Nam Augustinus, in libro de vera Relig., dicit quod *veritas est, qua ostenditur id quod est*. Et Hilarius dicit quod *verum est declarativum aut manifestativum esse*. Et hoc pertinet ad veritatem secundum quod est in intellectu. Ad veritatem autem rei secundum ordinem ad intellectum, pertinet definitio Augustini in libro de vera Relig., *veritas est summa similitudo principii, quae sine ulla dissimilitudine est*. Et quaedam definitio Anselmi, *veritas est rectitudo sola mente perceptibilis*; nam rectum est, quod principio concordat. Et quaedam definitio Avicennae, *veritas uniuscuiusque rei est proprietas sui esse quod stabilitum est ei*. Quod autem dicitur quod veritas est adaequatio rei et intellectus potest ad utrumque pertinere.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29054)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de veritate rei; et excludit a ratione huius veritatis, comparationem ad intellectum nostrum. Nam id quod est per accidens, ab unaquaque definitione excluditur.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29055)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod antiqui philosophi species rerum naturalium non dicebant procedere ab aliquo intellectu, sed eas provenire a casu, et quia considerabant quod verum importat comparationem ad intellectum, cogebantur veritatem rerum constituere in ordine ad intellectum nostrum. Ex quo inconvenientia sequebantur quae philosophus prosequitur in IV Metaphys. Quae quidem inconvenientia non accidunt, si ponamus veritatem rerum consistere in comparatione ad intellectum divinum.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29056)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet veritas intellectus nostri a re causetur, non tamen oportet quod in re per prius inveniatur ratio veritatis, sicut neque in medicina per prius invenitur ratio sanitatis quam in animali; virtus enim medicinae, non sanitas eius, causat sanitatem, cum non sit agens univocum. Et similiter esse rei, non veritas eius, causat veritatem intellectus. Unde philosophus dicit quod opinio et oratio vera est *ex eo quod res est, non ex eo quod res vera est*.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 16 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29057)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod veritas non sit solum in intellectu componente et dividente. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod sicut sensus propriorum sensibilium semper veri sunt, ita et intellectus eius quod quid est. Sed compositio et divisio non est neque in sensu, neque in intellectu cognoscente quod quid est. Ergo veritas non solum est in compositione et divisione intellectus.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29058)\n\nPraeterea, Isaac dicit, in libro de definitionibus, quod veritas est adaequatio rei et intellectus. Sed sicut intellectus complexorum potest adaequari rebus, ita intellectus incomplexorum, et etiam sensus sentiens rem ut est. Ergo veritas non est solum in compositione et divisione intellectus.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29059)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in VI Metaphys., quod circa simplicia et quod quid est non est veritas, nec in intellectu neque in rebus.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29060)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod verum, sicut dictum est, secundum sui primam rationem est in intellectu. Cum autem omnis res sit vera secundum quod habet propriam formam naturae suae, necesse est quod intellectus, inquantum est cognoscens, sit verus inquantum habet similitudinem rei cognitae, quae est forma eius inquantum est cognoscens. Et propter hoc per conformitatem intellectus et rei veritas definitur. Unde conformitatem istam cognoscere, est cognoscere veritatem. Hanc autem nullo modo sensus cognoscit, licet enim visus habeat similitudinem visibilis, non tamen cognoscit comparationem quae est inter rem visam et id quod ipse apprehendit de ea. Intellectus autem conformitatem sui ad rem intelligibilem cognoscere potest, sed tamen non apprehendit eam secundum quod cognoscit de aliquo quod quid est; sed quando iudicat rem ita se habere sicut est forma quam de re apprehendit, tunc primo cognoscit et dicit verum. Et hoc facit componendo et dividendo, nam in omni propositione aliquam formam significatam per praedicatum, vel applicat alicui rei significatae per subiectum, vel removet ab ea. Et ideo bene invenitur quod sensus est verus de aliqua re, vel intellectus cognoscendo quod quid est, sed non quod cognoscat aut dicat verum. Et similiter est de vocibus complexis aut incomplexis. Veritas quidem igitur potest esse in sensu, vel in intellectu cognoscente quod quid est, ut in quadam re vera, non autem ut cognitum in cognoscente, quod importat nomen veri; perfectio enim intellectus est verum ut cognitum. Et ideo, proprie loquendo, veritas est in intellectu componente et dividente, non autem in sensu, neque in intellectu cognoscente quod quid est.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 2 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29061)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29062)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod verum et ens non convertantur. Verum enim est proprie in intellectu, ut dictum est. Ens autem proprie est in rebus. Ergo non convertuntur.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29063)\n\nPraeterea, id quod se extendit ad ens et non ens, non convertitur cum ente. Sed verum se extendit ad ens et non ens, nam verum est quod est esse, et quod non est non esse. Ergo verum et ens non convertuntur.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29064)\n\nPraeterea, quae se habent secundum prius et posterius, non videntur converti. Sed verum videtur prius esse quam ens, nam ens non intelligitur nisi sub ratione veri. Ergo videtur quod non sint convertibilia.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29065)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit philosophus, II Metaphys., quod eadem est dispositio rerum in esse et veritate.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29066)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut bonum habet rationem appetibilis, ita verum habet ordinem ad cognitionem. Unumquodque autem inquantum habet de esse, intantum est cognoscibile. Et propter hoc dicitur in III de anima, quod anima est quodammodo omnia secundum sensum et intellectum. Et ideo, sicut bonum convertitur cum ente, ita et verum. Sed tamen, sicut bonum addit rationem appetibilis supra ens, ita et verum comparationem ad intellectum.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29067)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verum est in rebus et in intellectu, ut dictum est. Verum autem quod est in rebus, convertitur cum ente secundum substantiam. Sed verum quod est in intellectu, convertitur cum ente, ut manifestativum cum manifestato. Hoc enim est de ratione veri, ut dictum est. Quamvis posset dici quod etiam ens est in rebus et in intellectu, sicut et verum; licet verum principaliter in intellectu, ens vero principaliter in rebus. Et hoc accidit propter hoc, quod verum et ens differunt ratione.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29068)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non ens non habet in se unde cognoscatur, sed cognoscitur inquantum intellectus facit illud cognoscibile. Unde verum fundatur in ente, inquantum non ens est quoddam ens rationis, apprehensum scilicet a ratione.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29069)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur quod ens non potest apprehendi sine ratione veri, hoc potest dupliciter intelligi. Uno modo, ita quod non apprehendatur ens, nisi ratio veri assequatur apprehensionem entis. Et sic locutio habet veritatem. Alio modo posset sic intelligi, quod ens non posset apprehendi, nisi apprehenderetur ratio veri. Et hoc falsum est. Sed verum non potest apprehendi, nisi apprehendatur ratio entis, quia ens cadit in ratione veri. Et est simile sicut si comparemus intelligibile ad ens. Non enim potest intelligi ens, quin ens sit intelligibile, sed tamen potest intelligi ens, ita quod non intelligatur eius intelligibilitas. Et similiter ens intellectum est verum, non tamen intelligendo ens, intelligitur verum.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29070)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum secundum rationem sit prius quam verum. Quod enim est universalius, secundum rationem prius est, ut patet ex I Physic. Sed bonum est universalius quam verum, nam verum est quoddam bonum, scilicet intellectus. Ergo bonum prius est secundum rationem quam verum.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29071)\n\nPraeterea, bonum est in rebus, verum autem in compositione et divisione intellectus, ut dictum est. Sed ea quae sunt in re, sunt priora his quae sunt in intellectu. Ergo prius est secundum rationem bonum quam verum.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29072)\n\nPraeterea, veritas est quaedam species virtutis, ut patet in IV Ethic. Sed virtus continetur sub bono, est enim bona qualitas mentis, ut dicit Augustinus. Ergo bonum est prius quam verum.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29073)\n\nSed contra, quod est in pluribus, est prius secundum rationem. Sed verum est in quibusdam in quibus non est bonum, scilicet in mathematicis. Ergo verum est prius quam bonum.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29074)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, licet bonum et verum supposito convertantur cum ente, tamen ratione differunt. Et secundum hoc verum, absolute loquendo, prius est quam bonum. Quod ex duobus apparet. Primo quidem ex hoc, quod verum propinquius se habet ad ens, quod est prius, quam bonum. Nam verum respicit ipsum esse simpliciter et immediate, ratio autem boni consequitur esse, secundum quod est aliquo modo perfectum; sic enim appetibile est. Secundo apparet ex hoc, quod cognitio naturaliter praecedit appetitum. Unde, cum verum respiciat cognitionem, bonum autem appetitum, prius erit verum quam bonum secundum rationem.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29075)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod voluntas et intellectus mutuo se includunt, nam intellectus intelligit voluntatem, et voluntas vult intellectum intelligere. Sic ergo inter illa quae ordinantur ad obiectum voluntatis, continentur etiam ea quae sunt intellectus; et e converso. Unde in ordine appetibilium, bonum se habet ut universale, et verum ut particulare, in ordine autem intelligibilium est e converso. Ex hoc ergo quod verum est quoddam bonum, sequitur quod bonum sit prius in ordine appetibilium, non autem quod sit prius simpliciter.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29076)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod secundum hoc est aliquid prius ratione, quod prius cadit in intellectu. Intellectus autem per prius apprehendit ipsum ens; et secundario apprehendit se intelligere ens; et tertio apprehendit se appetere ens. Unde primo est ratio entis, secundo ratio veri, tertio ratio boni, licet bonum sit in rebus.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29077)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod virtus quae dicitur veritas, non est veritas communis, sed quaedam veritas secundum quam homo in dictis et factis ostendit se ut est. Veritas autem vitae dicitur particulariter, secundum quod homo in vita sua implet illud ad quod ordinatur per intellectum divinum, sicut etiam dictum est veritatem esse in ceteris rebus. Veritas autem iustitiae est secundum quod homo servat id quod debet alteri secundum ordinem legum. Unde ex his particularibus veritatibus non est procedendum ad veritatem communem.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29078)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit veritas. Veritas enim consistit in compositione et divisione intellectus. Sed in Deo non est compositio et divisio. Ergo non est ibi veritas.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29079)\n\nPraeterea, veritas, secundum Augustinum, in libro de vera Relig., est similitudo principii. Sed Dei non est similitudo ad principium. Ergo in Deo non est veritas.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29080)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid dicitur de Deo, dicitur de eo ut de prima causa omnium, sicut esse Dei est causa omnis esse, et bonitas eius est causa omnis boni. Si ergo in Deo sit veritas, ergo omne verum erit ab ipso. Sed aliquem peccare est verum. Ergo hoc erit a Deo. Quod patet esse falsum.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29081)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit dominus, Ioan. XIV, *ego sum via, veritas et vita*.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29082)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, veritas invenitur in intellectu secundum quod apprehendit rem ut est, et in re secundum quod habet esse conformabile intellectui. Hoc autem maxime invenitur in Deo. Nam esse suum non solum est conforme suo intellectui, sed etiam est ipsum suum intelligere; et suum intelligere est mensura et causa omnis alterius esse, et omnis alterius intellectus; et ipse est suum esse et intelligere. Unde sequitur quod non solum in ipso sit veritas, sed quod ipse sit ipsa summa et prima veritas.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29083)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet in intellectu divino non sit compositio et divisio, tamen secundum suam simplicem intelligentiam iudicat de omnibus, et cognoscit omnia complexa. Et sic in intellectu eius est veritas.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29084)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod verum intellectus nostri est secundum quod conformatur suo principio, scilicet rebus, a quibus cognitionem accipit. Veritas etiam rerum est secundum quod conformantur suo principio, scilicet intellectui divino. Sed hoc, proprie loquendo, non potest dici in veritate divina, nisi forte secundum quod veritas appropriatur filio, qui habet principium. Sed si de veritate essentialiter dicta loquamur, non potest intelligi, nisi resolvatur affirmativa in negativam, sicut cum dicitur, pater est a se, quia non est ab alio. Et similiter dici potest similitudo principii veritas divina, inquantum esse suum non est suo intellectui dissimile.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29085)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non ens et privationes non habent ex seipsis veritatem, sed solum ex apprehensione intellectus. Omnis autem apprehensio intellectus a Deo est, unde quidquid est veritatis in hoc quod dico, istum fornicari est verum, totum est a Deo. Sed si arguatur, ergo istum fornicari est a Deo, est fallacia accidentis.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 16 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29086)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod una sola sit veritas, secundum quam omnia sunt vera. Quia, secundum Augustinum, nihil est maius mente humana, nisi Deus. Sed veritas est maior mente humana, alioquin mens iudicaret de veritate; nunc autem omnia iudicat secundum veritatem, et non secundum seipsam. Ergo solus Deus est veritas. Ergo non est alia veritas quam Deus.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29087)\n\nPraeterea, Anselmus dicit, in libro de veritate, quod sicut tempus se habet ad temporalia, ita veritas ad res veras. Sed unum est tempus omnium temporalium. Ergo una est veritas, qua omnia vera sunt.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29088)\n\nSed contra est quod in Psalmo XI dicitur, *diminutae sunt veritates a filiis hominum*.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29089)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quodammodo una est veritas, qua omnia sunt vera, et quodammodo non. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod, quando aliquid praedicatur univoce de multis, illud in quolibet eorum secundum propriam rationem invenitur, sicut animal in qualibet specie animalis. Sed quando aliquid dicitur analogice de multis, illud invenitur secundum propriam rationem in uno eorum tantum, a quo alia denominantur. Sicut sanum dicitur de animali et urina et medicina, non quod sanitas sit nisi in animali tantum, sed a sanitate animalis denominatur medicina sana, inquantum est illius sanitatis effectiva, et urina, inquantum est illius sanitatis significativa. Et quamvis sanitas non sit in medicina neque in urina, tamen in utroque est aliquid per quod hoc quidem facit, illud autem significat sanitatem. Dictum est autem quod veritas per prius est in intellectu, et per posterius in rebus, secundum quod ordinantur ad intellectum divinum. Si ergo loquamur de veritate prout existit in intellectu, secundum propriam rationem, sic in multis intellectibus creatis sunt multae veritates; etiam in uno et eodem intellectu, secundum plura cognita. Unde dicit Glossa super illud Psalmi XI, *diminutae sunt veritates a filiis hominum* etc., quod sicut ab una facie hominis resultant plures similitudines in speculo, sic ab una veritate divina resultant plures veritates. Si vero loquamur de veritate secundum quod est in rebus, sic omnes sunt verae una prima veritate, cui unumquodque assimilatur secundum suam entitatem. Et sic, licet plures sint essentiae vel formae rerum, tamen una est veritas divini intellectus, secundum quam omnes res denominantur verae.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29090)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod anima non secundum quamcumque veritatem iudicat de rebus omnibus; sed secundum veritatem primam, inquantum resultat in ea sicut in speculo, secundum prima intelligibilia. Unde sequitur quod veritas prima sit maior anima. Et tamen etiam veritas creata, quae est in intellectu nostro, est maior anima, non simpliciter, sed secundum quid, inquantum est perfectio eius; sicut etiam scientia posset dici maior anima. Sed verum est quod nihil subsistens est maius mente rationali, nisi Deus.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29091)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod dictum Anselmi veritatem habet, secundum quod res dicuntur verae per comparationem ad intellectum divinum.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29092)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod veritas creata sit aeterna. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de libero arbitrio, quod nihil est magis aeternum quam ratio circuli, et duo et tria esse quinque. Sed horum veritas est veritas creata. Ergo veritas creata est aeterna.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29093)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est semper, est aeternum. Sed universalia sunt ubique et semper. Ergo sunt aeterna. Ergo et verum, quod est maxime universale.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29094)\n\nPraeterea, id quod est verum in praesenti, semper fuit verum esse futurum. Sed sicut veritas propositionis de praesenti est veritas creata, ita veritas propositionis de futuro. Ergo aliqua veritas creata est aeterna.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29095)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod caret principio et fine, est aeternum. Sed veritas enuntiabilium caret principio et fine. Quia, si veritas incoepit cum ante non esset, verum erat veritatem non esse, et utique aliqua veritate verum erat, et sic veritas erat antequam inciperet. Et similiter si ponatur veritatem habere finem, sequitur quod sit postquam desierit, verum enim erit veritatem non esse. Ergo veritas est aeterna.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29096)\n\nSed contra est quod solus Deus est aeternus, ut supra habitum est.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29097)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod veritas enuntiabilium non est aliud quam veritas intellectus. Enuntiabile enim et est in intellectu, et est in voce. Secundum autem quod est in intellectu, habet per se veritatem. Sed secundum quod est in voce, dicitur verum enuntiabile, secundum quod significat aliquam veritatem intellectus; non propter aliquam veritatem in enuntiabili existentem sicut in subiecto. Sicut urina dicitur sana, non a sanitate quae in ipsa sit, sed a sanitate animalis, quam significat. Similiter etiam supra dictum est quod res denominantur verae a veritate intellectus. Unde si nullus intellectus esset aeternus, nulla veritas esset aeterna. Sed quia solus intellectus divinus est aeternus, in ipso solo veritas aeternitatem habet. Nec propter hoc sequitur quod aliquid aliud sit aeternum quam Deus, quia veritas intellectus divini est ipse Deus, ut supra ostensum est.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29098)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio circuli, et duo et tria esse quinque, habent aeternitatem in mente divina.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29099)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliquid esse semper et ubique, potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, quia habet in se unde se extendat ad omne tempus et ad omnem locum, sicut Deo competit esse ubique et semper. Alio modo, quia non habet in se quo determinetur ad aliquem locum vel tempus, sicut materia prima dicitur esse una, non quia habet unam formam, sicut homo est unus ab unitate unius formae, sed per remotionem omnium formarum distinguentium. Et per hunc modum, quodlibet universale dicitur esse ubique et semper, inquantum universalia abstrahunt ab hic et nunc. Sed ex hoc non sequitur ea esse aeterna, nisi in intellectu, si quis sit aeternus.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29100)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod illud quod nunc est, ex eo futurum fuit antequam esset, quia in causa sua erat ut fieret. Unde, sublata causa, non esset futurum illud fieri. Sola autem causa prima est aeterna. Unde ex hoc non sequitur quod ea quae sunt, semper fuerit verum ea esse futura, nisi quatenus in causa sempiterna fuit ut essent futura. Quae quidem causa solus Deus est.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29101)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, quia intellectus noster non est aeternus, nec veritas enuntiabilium quae a nobis formantur, est aeterna, sed quandoque incoepit. Et antequam huiusmodi veritas esset, non erat verum dicere veritatem talem non esse, nisi ab intellectu divino, in quo solum veritas est aeterna. Sed nunc verum est dicere veritatem tunc non fuisse. Quod quidem non est verum nisi veritate quae nunc est in intellectu nostro, non autem per aliquam veritatem ex parte rei. Quia ista est veritas de non ente; non ens autem non habet ex se ut sit verum, sed solummodo ex intellectu apprehendente ipsum. Unde intantum est verum dicere veritatem non fuisse, inquantum apprehendimus non esse ipsius ut praecedens esse eius.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29102)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod veritas sit immutabilis. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro II de libero arbitrio, quod veritas non est aequalis menti, quia esset mutabilis, sicut et mens.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29103)\n\nPraeterea, id quod remanet post omnem mutationem, est immutabile, sicut prima materia est ingenita et incorruptibilis, quia remanet post omnem generationem et corruptionem. Sed veritas remanet post omnem mutationem, quia post omnem mutationem verum est dicere esse vel non esse. Ergo veritas est immutabilis.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29104)\n\nPraeterea, si veritas enuntiationis mutatur, maxime mutatur ad mutationem rei. Sed sic non mutatur. Veritas enim, secundum Anselmum, est rectitudo quaedam, inquantum aliquid implet id quod est de ipso in mente divina. Haec autem propositio, Socrates sedet, accipit a mente divina ut significet Socratem sedere, quod significat etiam eo non sedente. Ergo veritas propositionis nullo modo mutatur.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29105)\n\nPraeterea, ubi est eadem causa, et idem effectus. Sed eadem res est causa veritatis harum trium propositionum Socrates sedet, sedebit, et sedit. Ergo eadem est harum veritas. Sed oportet quod alterum horum sit verum. Ergo veritas harum propositionum immutabiliter manet. Et eadem ratione cuiuslibet alterius propositionis.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29106)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XI, *diminutae sunt veritates a filiis hominum*.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29107)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, veritas proprie est in solo intellectu, res autem dicuntur verae a veritate quae est in aliquo intellectu. Unde mutabilitas veritatis consideranda est circa intellectum. Cuius quidem veritas in hoc consistit, quod habeat conformitatem ad res intellectas. Quae quidem conformitas variari potest dupliciter, sicut et quaelibet alia similitudo, ex mutatione alterius extremi. Unde uno modo variatur veritas ex parte intellectus, ex eo quod de re eodem modo se habente aliquis aliam opinionem accipit, alio modo si, opinione eadem manente, res mutetur. Et utroque modo fit mutatio de vero in falsum. Si ergo sit aliquis intellectus in quo non possit esse alternatio opinionum, vel cuius acceptionem non potest subterfugere res aliqua, in eo est immutabilis veritas. Talis autem est intellectus divinus, ut ex superioribus patet. Unde veritas divini intellectus est immutabilis. Veritas autem intellectus nostri mutabilis est. Non quod ipsa sit subiectum mutationis, sed inquantum intellectus noster mutatur de veritate in falsitatem; sic enim formae mutabiles dici possunt. Veritas autem intellectus divini est secundum quam res naturales dicuntur verae, quae est omnino immutabilis.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29108)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de veritate divina.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29109)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod verum et ens sunt convertibilia. Unde, sicut ens non generatur neque corrumpitur per se, sed per accidens, inquantum hoc vel illud ens corrumpitur vel generatur, ut dicitur in I Physic.; ita veritas mutatur, non quod nulla veritas remaneat, sed quia non remanet illa veritas quae prius erat.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29110)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod propositio non solum habet veritatem sicut res aliae veritatem habere dicuntur, inquantum implent id quod de eis est ordinatum ab intellectu divino; sed dicitur habere veritatem quodam speciali modo, inquantum significat veritatem intellectus. Quae quidem consistit in conformitate intellectus et rei. Qua quidem subtracta, mutatur veritas opinionis, et per consequens veritas propositionis. Sic igitur haec propositio, Socrates sedet, eo sedente vera est et veritate rei, inquantum est quaedam vox significativa; et veritate significationis, inquantum significat opinionem veram. Socrate vero surgente, remanet prima veritas, sed mutatur secunda.\n\n[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29111)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod sessio Socratis, quae est causa veritatis huius propositionis, Socrates sedet, non eodem modo se habet dum Socrates sedet, et postquam sederit, et antequam sederet. Unde et veritas ab hoc causata, diversimode se habet; et diversimode significatur propositionibus de praesenti, praeterito et futuro. Unde non sequitur quod, licet altera trium propositionum sit vera, quod eadem veritas invariabilis maneat.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0002","text":"[I q. 16 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","text":"Quoniam autem scientia verorum est, post considerationem scientiae Dei, de veritate inquirendum est. Circa quam quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum veritas sit in re, vel tantum in intellectu. Secundo, utrum sit tantum in intellectu componente et dividente. Tertio, de comparatione veri ad ens. Quarto, de comparatione veri ad bonum. Quinto, utrum Deus sit veritas. Sexto, utrum omnia sint vera veritate una, vel pluribus. Septimo, de aeternitate veritatis. Octavo, de incommutabilitate ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0005","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod veritas non sit tantum in intellectu, sed magis in rebus. Augustinus enim, in libro Soliloq., reprobat hanc notificationem veri, verum est id quod videtur, quia secundum hoc, lapides qui sunt in abditissimo terrae sinu, non essent veri lapides, quia non videntur. Reprobat etiam istam, verum est quod ita se habet ut videtur cognitori, si velit et possit cognoscere, quia secundum hoc sequeretur quod nihil esset verum, si nullus posset cognoscere. Et definit sic verum, verum est id quod est. Et sic videtur quod veritas sit in rebus, et non in intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0007","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quidquid est verum, veritate verum est. Si igitur veritas est in intellectu solo, nihil erit verum nisi secundum quod intelligitur, quod est error antiquorum philosophorum, qui dicebant omne quod videtur, esse verum. Ad quod sequitur contradictoria simul esse vera, cum contradictoria simul a diversis vera esse videantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0009","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0010","text":"*Praeterea, propter quod unumquodque, et illud magis*, ut patet I Poster. *Sed ex eo quod res est vel non est, est opinio vel oratio vera vel falsa*, secundum philosophum in praedicamentis. Ergo veritas magis est in rebus quam in intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0011","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, VI Metaphys., quod *verum et falsum non sunt in rebus, sed in intellectu*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0013","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut bonum nominat id in quod tendit appetitus, ita verum nominat id in quod tendit intellectus. Hoc autem distat inter appetitum et intellectum, sive quamcumque cognitionem, quia cognitio est secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente, appetitus autem est secundum quod appetens inclinatur in ipsam rem appetitam. Et sic terminus appetitus, quod est bonum, est in re appetibili, sed terminus cognitionis, quod est verum, est in ipso intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","text":"Sicut autem bonum est in re, inquantum habet ordinem ad appetitum; et propter hoc ratio bonitatis derivatur a re appetibili in appetitum, secundum quod appetitus dicitur bonus, prout est boni, ita, cum verum sit in intellectu secundum quod conformatur rei intellectae, necesse est quod ratio veri ab intellectu ad rem intellectam derivetur, ut res etiam intellecta vera dicatur, secundum quod habet aliquem ordinem ad intellectum. Res autem intellecta ad intellectum aliquem potest habere ordinem vel per se, vel per accidens. Per se quidem habet ordinem ad intellectum a quo dependet secundum suum esse, per accidens autem ad intellectum a quo cognoscibilis est. Sicut si dicamus quod domus comparatur ad intellectum artificis per se, per accidens autem comparatur ad intellectum a quo non dependet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","text":"Iudicium autem de re non sumitur secundum id quod inest ei per accidens, sed secundum id quod inest ei per se. Unde unaquaeque res dicitur vera absolute, secundum ordinem ad intellectum a quo dependet. Et inde est quod res artificiales dicuntur verae per ordinem ad intellectum nostrum, dicitur enim domus vera, quae assequitur similitudinem formae quae est in mente artificis; et dicitur oratio vera, inquantum est signum intellectus veri. Et similiter res naturales dicuntur esse verae, secundum quod assequuntur similitudinem specierum quae sunt in mente divina, dicitur enim verus lapis, qui assequitur propriam lapidis naturam, secundum praeconceptionem intellectus divini. Sic ergo veritas principaliter est in intellectu; secundario vero in rebus, secundum quod comparantur ad intellectum ut ad principium. Et secundum hoc, veritas diversimode notificatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0017","text":"Nam Augustinus, in libro de vera Relig., dicit quod *veritas est, qua ostenditur id quod est*. Et Hilarius dicit quod *verum est declarativum aut manifestativum esse*. Et hoc pertinet ad veritatem secundum quod est in intellectu. Ad veritatem autem rei secundum ordinem ad intellectum, pertinet definitio Augustini in libro de vera Relig., *veritas est summa similitudo principii, quae sine ulla dissimilitudine est*. Et quaedam definitio Anselmi, *veritas est rectitudo sola mente perceptibilis*; nam rectum est, quod principio concordat. Et quaedam definitio Avicennae, *veritas uniuscuiusque rei est proprietas sui esse quod stabilitum est ei*. Quod autem dicitur quod veritas est adaequatio rei et intellectus potest ad utrumque pertinere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0018","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de veritate rei; et excludit a ratione huius veritatis, comparationem ad intellectum nostrum. Nam id quod est per accidens, ab unaquaque definitione excluditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0020","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod antiqui philosophi species rerum naturalium non dicebant procedere ab aliquo intellectu, sed eas provenire a casu, et quia considerabant quod verum importat comparationem ad intellectum, cogebantur veritatem rerum constituere in ordine ad intellectum nostrum. Ex quo inconvenientia sequebantur quae philosophus prosequitur in IV Metaphys. Quae quidem inconvenientia non accidunt, si ponamus veritatem rerum consistere in comparatione ad intellectum divinum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0022","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet veritas intellectus nostri a re causetur, non tamen oportet quod in re per prius inveniatur ratio veritatis, sicut neque in medicina per prius invenitur ratio sanitatis quam in animali; virtus enim medicinae, non sanitas eius, causat sanitatem, cum non sit agens univocum. Et similiter esse rei, non veritas eius, causat veritatem intellectus. Unde philosophus dicit quod opinio et oratio vera est *ex eo quod res est, non ex eo quod res vera est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0025","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod veritas non sit solum in intellectu componente et dividente. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod sicut sensus propriorum sensibilium semper veri sunt, ita et intellectus eius quod quid est. Sed compositio et divisio non est neque in sensu, neque in intellectu cognoscente quod quid est. Ergo veritas non solum est in compositione et divisione intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0027","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0028","text":"Praeterea, Isaac dicit, in libro de definitionibus, quod veritas est adaequatio rei et intellectus. Sed sicut intellectus complexorum potest adaequari rebus, ita intellectus incomplexorum, et etiam sensus sentiens rem ut est. Ergo veritas non est solum in compositione et divisione intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0029","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in VI Metaphys., quod circa simplicia et quod quid est non est veritas, nec in intellectu neque in rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0031","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod verum, sicut dictum est, secundum sui primam rationem est in intellectu. Cum autem omnis res sit vera secundum quod habet propriam formam naturae suae, necesse est quod intellectus, inquantum est cognoscens, sit verus inquantum habet similitudinem rei cognitae, quae est forma eius inquantum est cognoscens. Et propter hoc per conformitatem intellectus et rei veritas definitur. Unde conformitatem istam cognoscere, est cognoscere veritatem. Hanc autem nullo modo sensus cognoscit, licet enim visus habeat similitudinem visibilis, non tamen cognoscit comparationem quae est inter rem visam et id quod ipse apprehendit de ea.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","text":"Intellectus autem conformitatem sui ad rem intelligibilem cognoscere potest, sed tamen non apprehendit eam secundum quod cognoscit de aliquo quod quid est; sed quando iudicat rem ita se habere sicut est forma quam de re apprehendit, tunc primo cognoscit et dicit verum. Et hoc facit componendo et dividendo, nam in omni propositione aliquam formam significatam per praedicatum, vel applicat alicui rei significatae per subiectum, vel removet ab ea. Et ideo bene invenitur quod sensus est verus de aliqua re, vel intellectus cognoscendo quod quid est, sed non quod cognoscat aut dicat verum. Et similiter est de vocibus complexis aut incomplexis. Veritas quidem igitur potest esse in sensu, vel in intellectu cognoscente quod quid est, ut in quadam re vera, non autem ut cognitum in cognoscente, quod importat nomen veri; perfectio enim intellectus est verum ut cognitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0034","text":"Et ideo, proprie loquendo, veritas est in intellectu componente et dividente, non autem in sensu, neque in intellectu cognoscente quod quid est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0035","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0036","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0037","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0038","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0039","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod verum et ens non convertantur. Verum enim est proprie in intellectu, ut dictum est. Ens autem proprie est in rebus. Ergo non convertuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0040","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0041","text":"Praeterea, id quod se extendit ad ens et non ens, non convertitur cum ente. Sed verum se extendit ad ens et non ens, nam verum est quod est esse, et quod non est non esse. Ergo verum et ens non convertuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0042","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0043","text":"Praeterea, quae se habent secundum prius et posterius, non videntur converti. Sed verum videtur prius esse quam ens, nam ens non intelligitur nisi sub ratione veri. Ergo videtur quod non sint convertibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0044","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0045","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit philosophus, II Metaphys., quod eadem est dispositio rerum in esse et veritate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0046","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut bonum habet rationem appetibilis, ita verum habet ordinem ad cognitionem. Unumquodque autem inquantum habet de esse, intantum est cognoscibile. Et propter hoc dicitur in III de anima, quod anima est quodammodo omnia secundum sensum et intellectum. Et ideo, sicut bonum convertitur cum ente, ita et verum. Sed tamen, sicut bonum addit rationem appetibilis supra ens, ita et verum comparationem ad intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0048","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verum est in rebus et in intellectu, ut dictum est. Verum autem quod est in rebus, convertitur cum ente secundum substantiam. Sed verum quod est in intellectu, convertitur cum ente, ut manifestativum cum manifestato. Hoc enim est de ratione veri, ut dictum est. Quamvis posset dici quod etiam ens est in rebus et in intellectu, sicut et verum; licet verum principaliter in intellectu, ens vero principaliter in rebus. Et hoc accidit propter hoc, quod verum et ens differunt ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0050","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non ens non habet in se unde cognoscatur, sed cognoscitur inquantum intellectus facit illud cognoscibile. Unde verum fundatur in ente, inquantum non ens est quoddam ens rationis, apprehensum scilicet a ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0052","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0053","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur quod ens non potest apprehendi sine ratione veri, hoc potest dupliciter intelligi. Uno modo, ita quod non apprehendatur ens, nisi ratio veri assequatur apprehensionem entis. Et sic locutio habet veritatem. Alio modo posset sic intelligi, quod ens non posset apprehendi, nisi apprehenderetur ratio veri. Et hoc falsum est. Sed verum non potest apprehendi, nisi apprehendatur ratio entis, quia ens cadit in ratione veri. Et est simile sicut si comparemus intelligibile ad ens. Non enim potest intelligi ens, quin ens sit intelligibile, sed tamen potest intelligi ens, ita quod non intelligatur eius intelligibilitas. Et similiter ens intellectum est verum, non tamen intelligendo ens, intelligitur verum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0054","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0055","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0056","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum secundum rationem sit prius quam verum. Quod enim est universalius, secundum rationem prius est, ut patet ex I Physic. Sed bonum est universalius quam verum, nam verum est quoddam bonum, scilicet intellectus. Ergo bonum prius est secundum rationem quam verum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0057","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0058","text":"Praeterea, bonum est in rebus, verum autem in compositione et divisione intellectus, ut dictum est. Sed ea quae sunt in re, sunt priora his quae sunt in intellectu. Ergo prius est secundum rationem bonum quam verum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0059","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0060","text":"Praeterea, veritas est quaedam species virtutis, ut patet in IV Ethic. Sed virtus continetur sub bono, est enim bona qualitas mentis, ut dicit Augustinus. Ergo bonum est prius quam verum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0061","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0062","text":"Sed contra, quod est in pluribus, est prius secundum rationem. Sed verum est in quibusdam in quibus non est bonum, scilicet in mathematicis. Ergo verum est prius quam bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0063","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, licet bonum et verum supposito convertantur cum ente, tamen ratione differunt. Et secundum hoc verum, absolute loquendo, prius est quam bonum. Quod ex duobus apparet. Primo quidem ex hoc, quod verum propinquius se habet ad ens, quod est prius, quam bonum. Nam verum respicit ipsum esse simpliciter et immediate, ratio autem boni consequitur esse, secundum quod est aliquo modo perfectum; sic enim appetibile est. Secundo apparet ex hoc, quod cognitio naturaliter praecedit appetitum. Unde, cum verum respiciat cognitionem, bonum autem appetitum, prius erit verum quam bonum secundum rationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0065","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0066","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod voluntas et intellectus mutuo se includunt, nam intellectus intelligit voluntatem, et voluntas vult intellectum intelligere. Sic ergo inter illa quae ordinantur ad obiectum voluntatis, continentur etiam ea quae sunt intellectus; et e converso. Unde in ordine appetibilium, bonum se habet ut universale, et verum ut particulare, in ordine autem intelligibilium est e converso. Ex hoc ergo quod verum est quoddam bonum, sequitur quod bonum sit prius in ordine appetibilium, non autem quod sit prius simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0067","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod secundum hoc est aliquid prius ratione, quod prius cadit in intellectu. Intellectus autem per prius apprehendit ipsum ens; et secundario apprehendit se intelligere ens; et tertio apprehendit se appetere ens. Unde primo est ratio entis, secundo ratio veri, tertio ratio boni, licet bonum sit in rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0069","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0070","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod virtus quae dicitur veritas, non est veritas communis, sed quaedam veritas secundum quam homo in dictis et factis ostendit se ut est. Veritas autem vitae dicitur particulariter, secundum quod homo in vita sua implet illud ad quod ordinatur per intellectum divinum, sicut etiam dictum est veritatem esse in ceteris rebus. Veritas autem iustitiae est secundum quod homo servat id quod debet alteri secundum ordinem legum. Unde ex his particularibus veritatibus non est procedendum ad veritatem communem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0071","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0072","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0073","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit veritas. Veritas enim consistit in compositione et divisione intellectus. Sed in Deo non est compositio et divisio. Ergo non est ibi veritas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0074","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0075","text":"Praeterea, veritas, secundum Augustinum, in libro de vera Relig., est similitudo principii. Sed Dei non est similitudo ad principium. Ergo in Deo non est veritas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0076","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0077","text":"Praeterea, quidquid dicitur de Deo, dicitur de eo ut de prima causa omnium, sicut esse Dei est causa omnis esse, et bonitas eius est causa omnis boni. Si ergo in Deo sit veritas, ergo omne verum erit ab ipso. Sed aliquem peccare est verum. Ergo hoc erit a Deo. Quod patet esse falsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0078","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0079","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit dominus, Ioan. XIV, *ego sum via, veritas et vita*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0080","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, veritas invenitur in intellectu secundum quod apprehendit rem ut est, et in re secundum quod habet esse conformabile intellectui. Hoc autem maxime invenitur in Deo. Nam esse suum non solum est conforme suo intellectui, sed etiam est ipsum suum intelligere; et suum intelligere est mensura et causa omnis alterius esse, et omnis alterius intellectus; et ipse est suum esse et intelligere. Unde sequitur quod non solum in ipso sit veritas, sed quod ipse sit ipsa summa et prima veritas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0082","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0083","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet in intellectu divino non sit compositio et divisio, tamen secundum suam simplicem intelligentiam iudicat de omnibus, et cognoscit omnia complexa. Et sic in intellectu eius est veritas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0084","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod verum intellectus nostri est secundum quod conformatur suo principio, scilicet rebus, a quibus cognitionem accipit. Veritas etiam rerum est secundum quod conformantur suo principio, scilicet intellectui divino. Sed hoc, proprie loquendo, non potest dici in veritate divina, nisi forte secundum quod veritas appropriatur filio, qui habet principium. Sed si de veritate essentialiter dicta loquamur, non potest intelligi, nisi resolvatur affirmativa in negativam, sicut cum dicitur, pater est a se, quia non est ab alio. Et similiter dici potest similitudo principii veritas divina, inquantum esse suum non est suo intellectui dissimile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0086","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non ens et privationes non habent ex seipsis veritatem, sed solum ex apprehensione intellectus. Omnis autem apprehensio intellectus a Deo est, unde quidquid est veritatis in hoc quod dico, istum fornicari est verum, totum est a Deo. Sed si arguatur, ergo istum fornicari est a Deo, est fallacia accidentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0088","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0089","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0090","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod una sola sit veritas, secundum quam omnia sunt vera. Quia, secundum Augustinum, nihil est maius mente humana, nisi Deus. Sed veritas est maior mente humana, alioquin mens iudicaret de veritate; nunc autem omnia iudicat secundum veritatem, et non secundum seipsam. Ergo solus Deus est veritas. Ergo non est alia veritas quam Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0091","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0092","text":"Praeterea, Anselmus dicit, in libro de veritate, quod sicut tempus se habet ad temporalia, ita veritas ad res veras. Sed unum est tempus omnium temporalium. Ergo una est veritas, qua omnia vera sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0093","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0094","text":"Sed contra est quod in Psalmo XI dicitur, *diminutae sunt veritates a filiis hominum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0095","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quodammodo una est veritas, qua omnia sunt vera, et quodammodo non. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod, quando aliquid praedicatur univoce de multis, illud in quolibet eorum secundum propriam rationem invenitur, sicut animal in qualibet specie animalis. Sed quando aliquid dicitur analogice de multis, illud invenitur secundum propriam rationem in uno eorum tantum, a quo alia denominantur. Sicut sanum dicitur de animali et urina et medicina, non quod sanitas sit nisi in animali tantum, sed a sanitate animalis denominatur medicina sana, inquantum est illius sanitatis effectiva, et urina, inquantum est illius sanitatis significativa. Et quamvis sanitas non sit in medicina neque in urina, tamen in utroque est aliquid per quod hoc quidem facit, illud autem significat sanitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","text":"Dictum est autem quod veritas per prius est in intellectu, et per posterius in rebus, secundum quod ordinantur ad intellectum divinum. Si ergo loquamur de veritate prout existit in intellectu, secundum propriam rationem, sic in multis intellectibus creatis sunt multae veritates; etiam in uno et eodem intellectu, secundum plura cognita. Unde dicit Glossa super illud Psalmi XI, *diminutae sunt veritates a filiis hominum* etc., quod sicut ab una facie hominis resultant plures similitudines in speculo, sic ab una veritate divina resultant plures veritates. Si vero loquamur de veritate secundum quod est in rebus, sic omnes sunt verae una prima veritate, cui unumquodque assimilatur secundum suam entitatem. Et sic, licet plures sint essentiae vel formae rerum, tamen una est veritas divini intellectus, secundum quam omnes res denominantur verae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0098","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0099","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod anima non secundum quamcumque veritatem iudicat de rebus omnibus; sed secundum veritatem primam, inquantum resultat in ea sicut in speculo, secundum prima intelligibilia. Unde sequitur quod veritas prima sit maior anima. Et tamen etiam veritas creata, quae est in intellectu nostro, est maior anima, non simpliciter, sed secundum quid, inquantum est perfectio eius; sicut etiam scientia posset dici maior anima. Sed verum est quod nihil subsistens est maius mente rationali, nisi Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0100","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0101","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod dictum Anselmi veritatem habet, secundum quod res dicuntur verae per comparationem ad intellectum divinum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0102","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0103","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0104","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod veritas creata sit aeterna. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de libero arbitrio, quod nihil est magis aeternum quam ratio circuli, et duo et tria esse quinque. Sed horum veritas est veritas creata. Ergo veritas creata est aeterna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0105","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0106","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est semper, est aeternum. Sed universalia sunt ubique et semper. Ergo sunt aeterna. Ergo et verum, quod est maxime universale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0107","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0108","text":"Praeterea, id quod est verum in praesenti, semper fuit verum esse futurum. Sed sicut veritas propositionis de praesenti est veritas creata, ita veritas propositionis de futuro. Ergo aliqua veritas creata est aeterna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0109","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0110","text":"Praeterea, omne quod caret principio et fine, est aeternum. Sed veritas enuntiabilium caret principio et fine. Quia, si veritas incoepit cum ante non esset, verum erat veritatem non esse, et utique aliqua veritate verum erat, et sic veritas erat antequam inciperet. Et similiter si ponatur veritatem habere finem, sequitur quod sit postquam desierit, verum enim erit veritatem non esse. Ergo veritas est aeterna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0111","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0112","text":"Sed contra est quod solus Deus est aeternus, ut supra habitum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0113","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod veritas enuntiabilium non est aliud quam veritas intellectus. Enuntiabile enim et est in intellectu, et est in voce. Secundum autem quod est in intellectu, habet per se veritatem. Sed secundum quod est in voce, dicitur verum enuntiabile, secundum quod significat aliquam veritatem intellectus; non propter aliquam veritatem in enuntiabili existentem sicut in subiecto. Sicut urina dicitur sana, non a sanitate quae in ipsa sit, sed a sanitate animalis, quam significat. Similiter etiam supra dictum est quod res denominantur verae a veritate intellectus. Unde si nullus intellectus esset aeternus, nulla veritas esset aeterna. Sed quia solus intellectus divinus est aeternus, in ipso solo veritas aeternitatem habet. Nec propter hoc sequitur quod aliquid aliud sit aeternum quam Deus, quia veritas intellectus divini est ipse Deus, ut supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0115","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0116","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio circuli, et duo et tria esse quinque, habent aeternitatem in mente divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0117","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliquid esse semper et ubique, potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, quia habet in se unde se extendat ad omne tempus et ad omnem locum, sicut Deo competit esse ubique et semper. Alio modo, quia non habet in se quo determinetur ad aliquem locum vel tempus, sicut materia prima dicitur esse una, non quia habet unam formam, sicut homo est unus ab unitate unius formae, sed per remotionem omnium formarum distinguentium. Et per hunc modum, quodlibet universale dicitur esse ubique et semper, inquantum universalia abstrahunt ab hic et nunc. Sed ex hoc non sequitur ea esse aeterna, nisi in intellectu, si quis sit aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0119","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0120","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod illud quod nunc est, ex eo futurum fuit antequam esset, quia in causa sua erat ut fieret. Unde, sublata causa, non esset futurum illud fieri. Sola autem causa prima est aeterna. Unde ex hoc non sequitur quod ea quae sunt, semper fuerit verum ea esse futura, nisi quatenus in causa sempiterna fuit ut essent futura. Quae quidem causa solus Deus est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0121","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0122","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, quia intellectus noster non est aeternus, nec veritas enuntiabilium quae a nobis formantur, est aeterna, sed quandoque incoepit. Et antequam huiusmodi veritas esset, non erat verum dicere veritatem talem non esse, nisi ab intellectu divino, in quo solum veritas est aeterna. Sed nunc verum est dicere veritatem tunc non fuisse. Quod quidem non est verum nisi veritate quae nunc est in intellectu nostro, non autem per aliquam veritatem ex parte rei. Quia ista est veritas de non ente; non ens autem non habet ex se ut sit verum, sed solummodo ex intellectu apprehendente ipsum. Unde intantum est verum dicere veritatem non fuisse, inquantum apprehendimus non esse ipsius ut praecedens esse eius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0123","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0124","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0125","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod veritas sit immutabilis. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro II de libero arbitrio, quod veritas non est aequalis menti, quia esset mutabilis, sicut et mens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0126","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0127","text":"Praeterea, id quod remanet post omnem mutationem, est immutabile, sicut prima materia est ingenita et incorruptibilis, quia remanet post omnem generationem et corruptionem. Sed veritas remanet post omnem mutationem, quia post omnem mutationem verum est dicere esse vel non esse. Ergo veritas est immutabilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0128","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0129","text":"Praeterea, si veritas enuntiationis mutatur, maxime mutatur ad mutationem rei. Sed sic non mutatur. Veritas enim, secundum Anselmum, est rectitudo quaedam, inquantum aliquid implet id quod est de ipso in mente divina. Haec autem propositio, Socrates sedet, accipit a mente divina ut significet Socratem sedere, quod significat etiam eo non sedente. Ergo veritas propositionis nullo modo mutatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0130","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0131","text":"Praeterea, ubi est eadem causa, et idem effectus. Sed eadem res est causa veritatis harum trium propositionum Socrates sedet, sedebit, et sedit. Ergo eadem est harum veritas. Sed oportet quod alterum horum sit verum. Ergo veritas harum propositionum immutabiliter manet. Et eadem ratione cuiuslibet alterius propositionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0132","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0133","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XI, *diminutae sunt veritates a filiis hominum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0134","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, veritas proprie est in solo intellectu, res autem dicuntur verae a veritate quae est in aliquo intellectu. Unde mutabilitas veritatis consideranda est circa intellectum. Cuius quidem veritas in hoc consistit, quod habeat conformitatem ad res intellectas. Quae quidem conformitas variari potest dupliciter, sicut et quaelibet alia similitudo, ex mutatione alterius extremi. Unde uno modo variatur veritas ex parte intellectus, ex eo quod de re eodem modo se habente aliquis aliam opinionem accipit, alio modo si, opinione eadem manente, res mutetur. Et utroque modo fit mutatio de vero in falsum. Si ergo sit aliquis intellectus in quo non possit esse alternatio opinionum, vel cuius acceptionem non potest subterfugere res aliqua, in eo est immutabilis veritas. Talis autem est intellectus divinus, ut ex superioribus patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","text":"Unde veritas divini intellectus est immutabilis. Veritas autem intellectus nostri mutabilis est. Non quod ipsa sit subiectum mutationis, sed inquantum intellectus noster mutatur de veritate in falsitatem; sic enim formae mutabiles dici possunt. Veritas autem intellectus divini est secundum quam res naturales dicuntur verae, quae est omnino immutabilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0137","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0138","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de veritate divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0139","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0140","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod verum et ens sunt convertibilia. Unde, sicut ens non generatur neque corrumpitur per se, sed per accidens, inquantum hoc vel illud ens corrumpitur vel generatur, ut dicitur in I Physic.; ita veritas mutatur, non quod nulla veritas remaneat, sed quia non remanet illa veritas quae prius erat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0141","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod propositio non solum habet veritatem sicut res aliae veritatem habere dicuntur, inquantum implent id quod de eis est ordinatum ab intellectu divino; sed dicitur habere veritatem quodam speciali modo, inquantum significat veritatem intellectus. Quae quidem consistit in conformitate intellectus et rei. Qua quidem subtracta, mutatur veritas opinionis, et per consequens veritas propositionis. Sic igitur haec propositio, Socrates sedet, eo sedente vera est et veritate rei, inquantum est quaedam vox significativa; et veritate significationis, inquantum significat opinionem veram. Socrate vero surgente, remanet prima veritas, sed mutatur secunda.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0143","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0144","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod sessio Socratis, quae est causa veritatis huius propositionis, Socrates sedet, non eodem modo se habet dum Socrates sedet, et postquam sederit, et antequam sederet. Unde et veritas ab hoc causata, diversimode se habet; et diversimode significatur propositionibus de praesenti, praeterito et futuro. Unde non sequitur quod, licet altera trium propositionum sit vera, quod eadem veritas invariabilis maneat.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"dd545e64c247652b0f7cbe3636cd028240914fa7bb776223ad4dc5a02968c192","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T135505363668Z_02f718d9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 16","source_sha256":"dd545e64c247652b0f7cbe3636cd028240914fa7bb776223ad4dc5a02968c192","sha256":"03ffb930423a7bd8a359e08488683acfc116395b35dd68a24e3848bf53601ccf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:55:05.363685+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0002","text":"[I q. 16 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","text":"But since knowledge is of true things, after considering God's knowledge, we must inquire into truth. Concerning this, eight questions are asked. First, whether truth is in things, or only in the intellect. Second, whether it is only in the intellect that composes and divides. Third, concerning the comparison of the true with being. Fourth, concerning the comparison of the true with the good. Fifth, whether God is truth. Sixth, whether all things are true by one truth, or by several. Seventh, concerning the eternity of truth. Eighth, concerning its immutability.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0005","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that truth is not only in the intellect, but rather in things. For Augustine, in the book Soliloquies, rejects this account of the true: the true is that which appears, because according to this, stones that are in the most hidden recess of the earth would not be true stones, because they are not seen. He also rejects this: the true is that which is as it appears to a knower, if he wills and is able to know; because according to this it would follow that nothing would be true if no one could know. And he defines the true thus: the true is that which is. And thus it seems that truth is in things, and not in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0007","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0008","text":"Further, whatever is true is true by truth. If, therefore, truth is in the intellect alone, nothing will be true except insofar as it is understood, which is the error of the ancient philosophers, who said that everything which appears is true. From this it follows that contradictories are true at the same time, since contradictories seem to be true at the same time to different persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0009","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0010","text":"*Further, that because of which each thing is such is itself more so*, as is clear in Posterior Analytics I. *But it is because a thing is or is not that an opinion or statement is true or false*, according to the Philosopher in the Categories. Therefore, truth is more in things than in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0011","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in Metaphysics VI, that *the true and the false are not in things, but in the intellect*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0013","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that, just as the good names that toward which appetite tends, so the true names that toward which the intellect tends. But there is this difference between appetite and intellect, or any cognition whatsoever: cognition is according as the thing known is in the knower, whereas appetite is according as the one who desires is inclined toward the thing desired itself. And thus the terminus of appetite, which is the good, is in the desirable thing; but the terminus of cognition, which is the true, is in the intellect itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","text":"But just as the good is in a thing insofar as it has an order to appetite, and on this account the notion of goodness is derived from the desirable thing to appetite, according as appetite is called good insofar as it is of the good; so, since the true is in the intellect insofar as it is conformed to the thing understood, it is necessary that the notion of the true be derived from the intellect to the thing understood, so that the thing understood too is called true, insofar as it has some order to the intellect. Now a thing understood can have an order to an intellect either per se or accidentally. It has an order per se to the intellect on which it depends in its being, but accidentally to an intellect by which it is knowable. Thus, if we say that a house is related to the intellect of the craftsman per se, but is related accidentally to an intellect on which it does not depend.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","text":"But judgment about a thing is not taken according to what belongs to it accidentally, but according to what belongs to it per se. Hence each thing is called true absolutely according to its order to the intellect on which it depends. And from this it is that artificial things are called true by their order to our intellect; for a house is called true which attains the likeness of the form that is in the mind of the craftsman, and a statement is called true insofar as it is a sign of a true intellect. Likewise, natural things are called true according as they attain the likeness of the species that are in the divine mind; for a stone is called true which attains its proper nature of stone, according to the preconception of the divine intellect. Thus, therefore, truth is principally in the intellect, but secondarily in things, according as they are compared to the intellect as to a principle. And according to this, truth is described in diverse ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0017","text":"For Augustine, in the book On True Religion, says that *truth is that by which that which is is shown*. And Hilary says that *the true is declarative or manifestative of being*. And this pertains to truth insofar as it is in the intellect. But to the truth of a thing according to its order to the intellect pertains Augustine's definition in the book On True Religion: *truth is the highest likeness of the principle, which is without any dissimilarity*. And a certain definition of Anselm: *truth is rectitude perceptible by the mind alone*; for that is straight which accords with the principle. And a certain definition of Avicenna: *the truth of each thing is the property of its being which has been established for it*. But the statement that truth is the adequation of thing and intellect can pertain to both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0018","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine speaks of the truth of a thing; and he excludes from the account of this truth comparison to our intellect. For that which is accidental is excluded from every definition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0020","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that the ancient philosophers did not say that the species of natural things proceed from some intellect, but that they come about by chance; and because they considered that the true imports a comparison to the intellect, they were compelled to establish the truth of things in relation to our intellect. From this followed the inconveniences which the Philosopher pursues in Metaphysics IV. These inconveniences, however, do not arise if we hold that the truth of things consists in comparison to the divine intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0022","text":"[I q. 16 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although the truth of our intellect is caused by a thing, it does not follow that the notion of truth is found first in the thing, just as the notion of health is not found first in medicine rather than in the animal; for the power of medicine, not its health, causes health, since it is not a univocal agent. And similarly, the being of a thing, not its truth, causes the truth of the intellect. Hence the Philosopher says that an opinion and a statement are true *because a thing is, not because a thing is true*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0025","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that truth is not only in the intellect composing and dividing. For the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that just as the senses of proper sensibles are always true, so too is the intellect of what a thing is. But composition and division are neither in sense nor in the intellect cognizing what a thing is. Therefore truth is not only in the composition and division of the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0027","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0028","text":"Moreover, Isaac says, in the book *On Definitions*, that truth is the adequation of thing and intellect. But just as the intellect of complexes can be adequate to things, so can the intellect of incomplexes, and also sense sensing a thing as it is. Therefore truth is not only in the composition and division of the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0029","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *Metaphysics* VI, that concerning simple things and what a thing is, there is no truth, neither in the intellect nor in things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0031","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that the true, as has been said, according to its primary notion is in the intellect. But since every thing is true according as it has the proper form of its nature, it is necessary that the intellect, insofar as it is cognizing, be true insofar as it has a likeness of the thing cognized, which is its form insofar as it is cognizing. And for this reason truth is defined through the conformity of intellect and thing. Hence, to cognize this conformity is to cognize truth. But sense in no way cognizes this; for although sight has a likeness of the visible, it nevertheless does not cognize the comparison that exists between the thing seen and that which it apprehends of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","text":"But the intellect can cognize its conformity to the intelligible thing; nevertheless, it does not apprehend it when it cognizes of something what a thing is. Rather, when it judges that a thing is so as is the form which it apprehends of the thing, then for the first time it cognizes and declares the true. And it does this by composing and dividing, for in every proposition it either applies some form signified by the predicate to some thing signified by the subject, or removes it from that thing. And therefore it is rightly found that sense is true of some thing, or the intellect is true in cognizing what a thing is, but not that it cognizes or declares the true. And it is similar with complex or incomplex utterances. Truth, therefore, can indeed be in sense, or in the intellect cognizing what a thing is, as in a certain true thing, but not as the known in a knower, which the name ‘true’ implies; for the perfection of the intellect is the true as known.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0034","text":"And therefore, properly speaking, truth is in the intellect composing and dividing, but not in sense, nor in the intellect cognizing what a thing is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0035","text":"[I q. 16 a. 2 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0036","text":"And through this the solution to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0037","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0038","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0039","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the true and being are not convertible. For the true is properly in the intellect, as has been said. But being is properly in things. Therefore they are not convertible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0040","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0041","text":"Moreover, that which extends to being and non-being is not convertible with being. But the true extends to being and non-being, for it is true that what is, is, and that what is not, is not. Therefore the true and being are not convertible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0042","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0043","text":"Moreover, things that stand as prior and posterior do not seem to be convertible. But the true seems to be prior to being, for being is not understood except under the notion of the true. Therefore it seems that they are not convertible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0044","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0045","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *Metaphysics* II, that the disposition of things in being and in truth is the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0046","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","text":"I answer that it must be said that, just as the good has the character of the appetible, so the true has an order to cognition. But each thing, insofar as it has being, is cognoscible to that extent. And for this reason it is said in *On the Soul* III that the soul is in a certain way all things according to sense and intellect. And therefore, just as the good is convertible with being, so also is the true. Yet, just as the good adds the notion of the appetible over and above being, so the true adds a relation to the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0048","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that truth is in things and in the intellect, as has been said. But truth which is in things is convertible with being according to substance. Whereas truth which is in the intellect is convertible with being as the manifestative with that which is manifested. For this belongs to the notion of truth, as has been said. Although it could be said that being also is in things and in the intellect, as is truth; although truth principally [is] in the intellect, whereas being principally [is] in things. And this occurs because truth and being differ in notion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0050","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","text":"To the second, it must be said that non-being does not have in itself that by which it may be known, but it is known insofar as the intellect makes it knowable. Hence truth is founded on being, insofar as non-being is a certain being of reason, namely, apprehended by reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0052","text":"[I q. 16 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0053","text":"To the third, it must be said that, when it is said that being cannot be apprehended without the notion of truth, this can be understood in two ways. In one way, such that being is not apprehended unless the notion of truth follows upon the apprehension of being. And thus the statement is true. In another way, it could be understood thus: that being could not be apprehended unless the notion of truth were apprehended. And this is false. But truth cannot be apprehended unless the notion of being is apprehended, because being falls within the notion of truth. And it is similar if we compare the intelligible to being. For being cannot be understood without being being intelligible; nevertheless, being can be understood in such a way that its intelligibility is not understood. And similarly, being understood is true; nevertheless, in understanding being, truth is not understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0054","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0055","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0056","text":"We proceed to the fourth as follows. It seems that the good is prior in notion to the true. For what is more universal is prior in notion, as is clear from Physics I. But the good is more universal than the true, for the true is a certain good, namely, of the intellect. Therefore the good is prior in notion to the true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0057","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0058","text":"Further, the good is in things, whereas the true is in the composition and division of the intellect, as has been said. But those things that are in reality are prior to those that are in the intellect. Therefore the good is prior in notion to the true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0059","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0060","text":"Further, truth is a certain species of virtue, as is clear in Ethics IV. But virtue is contained under the good, for it is a good quality of the mind, as Augustine says. Therefore the good is prior to the true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0061","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0062","text":"On the contrary, what is in more things is prior in notion. But the true is in certain things in which the good is not, namely, in mathematics. Therefore the true is prior to the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0063","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","text":"I answer that, although the good and the true, in supposit, are convertible with being, nevertheless they differ in notion. And according to this, the true, absolutely speaking, is prior to the good. This is apparent from two considerations. First, indeed, from the fact that the true is more closely related to being, which is prior, than the good is. For the true regards being itself simply and immediately, whereas the notion of the good follows upon being insofar as it is in some way perfect; for thus it is appetible. Second, this is apparent from the fact that cognition naturally precedes appetite. Hence, since the true regards cognition, whereas the good regards appetite, the true will be prior in notion to the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0065","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0066","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the will and the intellect mutually include one another, for the intellect understands the will, and the will wills the intellect to understand. Thus, therefore, among those things that are ordered to the object of the will are also contained those which belong to the intellect; and conversely. Hence, in the order of appetibles, the good is as the universal and the true as the particular; but in the order of intelligibles it is conversely. Therefore, from the fact that the true is a certain good, it follows that the good is prior in the order of appetibles, but not that it is prior simply.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0067","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","text":"To the second, it must be said that something is prior in notion according as it falls first within the intellect. But the intellect first apprehends being itself; second, it apprehends itself understanding being; and third, it apprehends itself desiring being. Hence first is the notion of being, second the notion of the true, third the notion of the good, although the good is in things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0069","text":"[I q. 16 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0070","text":"To the third, it must be said that the virtue called truth is not truth in common, but a certain truth according to which a man shows himself in words and deeds as he is. But truth of life is spoken of particularly, according as a man in his life fulfills that to which he is ordered by the divine intellect, just as truth has also been said to be in other things. But truth of justice is according as a man observes what he owes to another according to the order of laws. Hence from these particular truths one must not proceed to truth in common.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0071","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0072","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0073","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth point. It seems that God is not truth. For truth consists in the composition and division of the intellect. But in God there is no composition and division. Therefore truth is not in Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0074","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0075","text":"Moreover, truth, according to Augustine in the book *On True Religion*, is a likeness of the principle. But God has no likeness to a principle. Therefore there is no truth in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0076","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0077","text":"Moreover, whatever is said of God is said of Him as of the first cause of all things, just as the being of God is the cause of all being, and His goodness is the cause of all good. If, therefore, truth is in God, then everything true will be from Him. But that someone sins is true. Therefore this will be from God. Which is evidently false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0078","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0079","text":"On the contrary, the Lord says, John XIV, *I am the way, the truth, and the life*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0080","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","text":"I answer that, as has been said, truth is found in the intellect insofar as it apprehends a thing as it is, and in a thing insofar as it has being conformable to the intellect. Now this is found most of all in God. For His being is not only conformable to His intellect, but is also His very act of understanding; and His act of understanding is the measure and cause of every other being and of every other intellect; and He Himself is His being and His understanding. Hence it follows that not only is truth in Him, but that He Himself is the highest and first truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0082","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0083","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although there is no composition and division in the divine intellect, nevertheless by its simple understanding it judges of all things, and knows all complex things. And thus truth is in His intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0084","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085","text":"To the second it must be said that the truth of our intellect consists in its being conformed to its principle, namely, the things from which it receives cognition. The truth of things likewise consists in their being conformed to their principle, namely, the divine intellect. But this, properly speaking, cannot be said of divine truth, except perhaps insofar as truth is appropriated to the Son, who has a principle. But if we speak of truth said essentially, it cannot be understood unless an affirmative proposition is resolved into a negative one, as when it is said, the Father is from Himself, because He is not from another. And similarly, divine truth can be called a likeness of the principle, insofar as His being is not dissimilar to His intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0086","text":"[I q. 16 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","text":"To the third it must be said that non-being and privations do not have truth from themselves, but only from the apprehension of the intellect. But every apprehension of the intellect is from God; whence whatever truth there is in what I say, “this man commits fornication is true,” is wholly from God. But if it is argued, therefore this man’s committing fornication is from God, it is the fallacy of accident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0088","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0089","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0090","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth point. It seems that there is only one truth according to which all things are true. For, according to Augustine, nothing is greater than the human mind except God. But truth is greater than the human mind; otherwise the mind would judge concerning truth, whereas it now judges all things according to truth, and not according to itself. Therefore God alone is truth. Therefore there is no truth other than God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0091","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0092","text":"Moreover, Anselm says, in the book *On Truth*, that as time is related to temporal things, so truth is related to true things. But there is one time for all temporal things. Therefore there is one truth by which all things are true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0093","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0094","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Psalm XI, *truths have been diminished from the children of men*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0095","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","text":"I answer that in one respect there is one truth by which all things are true, and in another respect there is not. For the clarification of this, it should be known that, when something is predicated univocally of many things, it is found in each of them according to its proper account, as animal in every species of animal. But when something is said analogically of many things, it is found according to its proper account in one of them only, from which the others are denominated. Thus healthy is said of an animal, urine, and medicine, not because health is in anything but the animal alone, but medicine is called healthy from the health of the animal, insofar as it is productive of that health, and urine insofar as it is indicative of that health. And although health is not in medicine or in urine, nevertheless in each there is something by which the one produces and the other signifies health.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","text":"It has been said, moreover, that truth is primarily in the intellect, and secondarily in things, insofar as they are ordered to the divine intellect. Therefore, if we speak of truth as it exists in the intellect, according to its proper account, then there are many truths in many created intellects; indeed, even in one and the same intellect, according to the several things known. Hence the Gloss says on that passage of Psalm XI, *truths have been diminished from the sons of men*, etc., that just as from one human face several likenesses result in a mirror, so from one divine truth several truths result. But if we speak of truth insofar as it is in things, then all things are true by one first truth, to which each thing is likened according to its entity. And thus, although there are several essences or forms of things, nevertheless there is one truth of the divine intellect, according to which all things are denominated true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0098","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0099","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the soul does not judge of all things according to just any truth, but according to the first truth, insofar as it is reflected in it as in a mirror, according to the first intelligibles. Hence it follows that the first truth is greater than the soul. And yet created truth also, which is in our intellect, is greater than the soul, not simply, but in a certain respect, insofar as it is its perfection; just as knowledge too could be called greater than the soul. But it is true that nothing subsisting is greater than the rational mind, except God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0100","text":"[I q. 16 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0101","text":"To the second it must be said that Anselm's statement has truth insofar as things are called true by comparison with the divine intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0102","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0103","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0104","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh article. It seems that created truth is eternal. For Augustine says, in the book *On Free Choice*, that nothing is more eternal than the nature of a circle, and than two and three being five. But the truth of these is created truth. Therefore created truth is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0105","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0106","text":"Further, everything that is always is eternal. But universals are everywhere and always. Therefore they are eternal. Therefore so too is the true, which is most universal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0107","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0108","text":"Further, what is true in the present has always been true to be future. But just as the truth of a proposition concerning the present is created truth, so also is the truth of a proposition concerning the future. Therefore some created truth is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0109","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0110","text":"Further, everything that lacks a beginning and an end is eternal. But the truth of enuntiables lacks a beginning and an end. For if truth began when previously it was not, it was true that truth was not; and undoubtedly it was true by some truth, and thus truth existed before it began. And similarly, if truth be posited to have an end, it follows that it exists after it has ceased; for it will be true that truth is not. Therefore truth is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0111","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0112","text":"But on the contrary, God alone is eternal, as was established above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0113","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","text":"I answer that it must be said that the truth of enuntiables is nothing other than the truth of the intellect. For an enuntiable both exists in the intellect and exists in speech. Now insofar as it is in the intellect, it has truth through itself. But insofar as it is in speech, an enuntiable is called true insofar as it signifies some truth of the intellect, not on account of any truth existing in the enuntiable as in a subject. Just as urine is called healthy, not from health that is in it, but from the health of the animal which it signifies. Likewise, too, it was said above that things are denominated true from the truth of the intellect. Hence, if no intellect were eternal, no truth would be eternal. But because the divine intellect alone is eternal, truth has eternity in it alone. Nor does it follow from this that anything other than God is eternal, because the truth of the divine intellect is God himself, as was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0115","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0116","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the nature of a circle, and two and three being five, have eternity in the divine mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0117","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118","text":"To the second it must be said that for something to be always and everywhere can be understood in two ways. In one way, because it has in itself that by which it extends itself to every time and every place, as it belongs to God to be everywhere and always. In another way, because it does not have in itself that by which it is determined to some place or time, just as prime matter is said to be one, not because it has one form, as man is one from the unity of one form, but by the removal of all distinguishing forms. And in this way every universal is said to be everywhere and always, insofar as universals abstract from here and now. But it does not follow from this that they are eternal, except in an intellect, if there is one that is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0119","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0120","text":"To the third it must be said that what now is was future before it existed, because it was in its cause that it would come to be. Hence, if the cause were removed, that thing's coming to be would not be future. But the first cause alone is eternal. Hence it does not follow from this that it has always been true of the things that are that they would be future, except insofar as it was in the everlasting cause that they would be future. And that cause is God alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0121","text":"[I q. 16 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0122","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, because our intellect is not eternal, neither is the truth of the enunciables formed by us eternal, but it began at some time. And before such truth existed, it was not true to say that such truth did not exist, except by the divine intellect, in which alone truth is eternal. But now it is true to say that truth then did not exist. This indeed is not true except by the truth which now exists in our intellect, and not through any truth on the part of the thing. For this is a truth concerning non-being; but non-being does not have of itself that it be true, but only from an intellect apprehending it. Hence it is true to say that truth did not exist insofar as we apprehend its non-being as preceding its being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0123","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0124","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0125","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth. It seems that truth is immutable. For Augustine says, in Book II of *On Free Choice*, that truth is not equal to the mind, because it would be mutable, just as the mind is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0126","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0127","text":"Further, that which remains after every change is immutable, just as prime matter is ungenerated and incorruptible, because it remains after every generation and corruption. But truth remains after every change, because after every change it is true to say that something is or is not. Therefore truth is immutable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0128","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0129","text":"Further, if the truth of an enunciation changes, it changes most of all upon a change in the thing. But it does not change in this way. For truth, according to Anselm, is a certain rectitude, insofar as something fulfills that which is concerning it in the divine mind. But this proposition, “Socrates sits,” receives from the divine mind that it signifies Socrates sitting, which it signifies even when he is not sitting. Therefore the truth of the proposition in no way changes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0130","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0131","text":"Further, where the cause is the same, the effect is also the same. But the same thing is the cause of the truth of these three propositions: “Socrates sits,” “Socrates will sit,” and “Socrates sat.” Therefore their truth is the same. But one of these must be true. Therefore the truth of these propositions remains immutably. And by the same reasoning, that of any other proposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0132","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0133","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm XI, *truths have been diminished among the children of men*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0134","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was said above, truth properly exists in the intellect alone, while things are called true by the truth which is in some intellect. Hence the mutability of truth must be considered with regard to the intellect. Its truth consists in this, that it has conformity with the things understood. This conformity can be varied in two ways, as can any other likeness, through a change in either extreme. Hence, in one way, truth is varied on the part of the intellect, from the fact that someone takes a different opinion concerning a thing remaining in the same manner; in another way, if the opinion remains the same, the thing changes. And in both ways there occurs a change from truth to falsity. Therefore, if there is some intellect in which there cannot be an alternation of opinions, or whose apprehension no thing can evade, in it truth is immutable. But such is the divine intellect, as is clear from what has been said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","text":"Hence the truth of the divine intellect is immutable. But the truth of our intellect is mutable—not because it itself is the subject of change, but insofar as our intellect changes from truth to falsity; for thus forms can be called mutable. But the truth of the divine intellect is that according to which natural things are called true, and it is altogether immutable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0137","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0138","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine speaks of divine truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0139","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0140","text":"To the second, it must be said that true and being are convertible. Hence, just as being is neither generated nor corrupted through itself, but accidentally, insofar as this or that being is corrupted or generated, as is said in *Physics* I; so truth changes, not because no truth remains, but because that truth which existed before does not remain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0141","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142","text":"To the third, it must be said that a proposition not only has truth in the way that other things are said to have truth, insofar as they fulfill that which is ordered concerning them by the divine intellect; but it is said to have truth in a certain special way, insofar as it signifies the truth of the intellect. This consists in the conformity of intellect and thing. When this is removed, the truth of the opinion changes, and consequently the truth of the proposition. Thus, therefore, this proposition, “Socrates sits,” while he is sitting, is true both with the truth of the thing, insofar as it is a certain significant utterance, and with the truth of signification, insofar as it signifies a true opinion. But when Socrates rises, the first truth remains, but the second changes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0143","text":"[I q. 16 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0144","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Socrates’ sitting, which is the cause of the truth of this proposition, “Socrates sits,” is not disposed in the same way while Socrates sits, and after he has sat, and before he sat. Hence the truth caused by this also is disposed differently; and it is signified differently by propositions concerning the present, past, and future. Hence it does not follow that, although one of the three propositions is true, the same unchangeable truth remains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0144"]}],"source_digest":"fbf44fb883b009b92316645196ceaa54e4d5648416d4a9362b452d5f2f09e500","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 16"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 16","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9","title":"First Part · Question 17","order":18,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 17 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29112)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de falsitate. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum falsitas sit in rebus. Secundo, utrum sit in sensu. Tertio, utrum sit in intellectu. Quarto, de oppositione veri et falsi.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29113)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod falsitas non sit in rebus. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro Soliloq., *si verum est id quod est, falsum non esse uspiam concludetur, quovis repugnante*.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29114)\n\nPraeterea, falsum dicitur a fallendo. Sed res non fallunt, ut dicit Augustinus in libro de vera Relig., *quia non ostendunt aliud quam suam speciem*. Ergo falsum in rebus non invenitur.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29115)\n\nPraeterea, verum dicitur in rebus per comparationem ad intellectum divinum, ut supra dictum est. Sed quaelibet res, inquantum est, imitatur Deum. Ergo quaelibet res vera est, absque falsitate. Et sic nulla res est falsa.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29116)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de vera Relig., quod *omne corpus est verum corpus et falsa unitas*; quia imitatur unitatem, et non est unitas. Sed quaelibet res imitatur divinam bonitatem, et ab ea deficit. Ergo in omnibus rebus est falsitas.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29117)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum verum et falsum opponantur; opposita autem sunt circa idem; necesse est ut ibi prius quaeratur falsitas, ubi primo veritas invenitur, hoc est in intellectu. In rebus autem neque veritas neque falsitas est, nisi per ordinem ad intellectum. Et quia unumquodque secundum id quod convenit ei per se, simpliciter nominatur; secundum autem id quod convenit ei per accidens, non nominatur nisi secundum quid; res quidem simpliciter falsa dici posset per comparationem ad intellectum a quo dependet, cui comparatur per se; in ordine autem ad alium intellectum, cui comparatur per accidens, non posset dici falsa nisi secundum quid. Dependent autem ab intellectu divino res naturales, sicut ab intellectu humano res artificiales. Dicuntur igitur res artificiales falsae simpliciter et secundum se, inquantum deficiunt a forma artis, unde dicitur aliquis artifex opus falsum facere, quando deficit ab operatione artis. Sic autem in rebus dependentibus a Deo, falsitas inveniri non potest per comparationem ad intellectum divinum, cum quidquid in rebus accidit, ex ordinatione divini intellectus procedat, nisi forte in voluntariis agentibus tantum, in quorum potestate est subducere se ab ordinatione divini intellectus; in quo malum culpae consistit, secundum quod ipsa peccata falsitates et mendacia dicuntur in Scripturis, secundum illud Psalmi IV, *ut quid diligitis vanitatem et quaeritis mendacium?* Sicut per oppositum operatio virtuosa veritas vitae nominatur, inquantum subditur ordini divini intellectus; sicut dicitur Ioan. III, *qui facit veritatem, venit ad lucem*. Sed per ordinem ad intellectum nostrum, ad quem comparantur res naturales per accidens, possunt dici falsae, non simpliciter, sed secundum quid. Et hoc dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum rationem significati, ut dicatur illud esse falsum in rebus, quod significatur vel repraesentatur oratione vel intellectu falso. Secundum quem modum quaelibet res potest dici esse falsa, quantum ad id quod ei non inest, sicut si dicamus diametrum esse falsum commensurabile, ut dicit philosophus in V Metaphys.; et sicut dicit Augustinus, in libro Soliloq., quod *tragoedus est falsus Hector*. Sicut e contrario potest unumquodque dici verum, secundum id quod competit ei. Alio modo, per modum causae. Et sic dicitur res esse falsa, quae nata est facere de se opinionem falsam. Et quia innatum est nobis per ea quae exterius apparent de rebus iudicare, eo quod nostra cognitio a sensu ortum habet, qui primo et per se est exteriorum accidentium; ideo ea quae in exterioribus accidentibus habent similitudinem aliarum rerum, dicuntur esse falsa secundum illas res; sicut fel est falsum mel, et stannum est falsum argentum. Et secundum hoc dicit Augustinus, in libro Soliloq., quod *eas res falsas nominamus, quae verisimilia apprehendimus*. Et philosophus dicit, in V Metaphys., quod *falsa dicuntur quaecumque apta nata sunt apparere aut qualia non sunt, aut quae non sunt*. Et per hunc modum etiam dicitur homo falsus, inquantum est amativus falsarum opinionum vel locutionum. Non autem ex hoc quod potest eas confingere, quia sic etiam sapientes et scientes falsi dicerentur, ut dicitur in V Metaphys.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29118)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod res comparata ad intellectum, secundum id quod est, dicitur vera, secundum id quod non est, dicitur falsa. *Unde verus tragoedus est falsus Hector*, ut dicitur in II Soliloq. Sicut igitur in his quae sunt, invenitur quoddam non esse; ita in his quae sunt, invenitur quaedam ratio falsitatis.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29119)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod res per se non fallunt, sed per accidens. Dant enim occasionem falsitatis, eo quod similitudinem eorum gerunt, quorum non habent existentiam.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29120)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod per comparationem ad intellectum divinum non dicuntur res falsae, quod esset eas esse falsas simpliciter, sed per comparationem ad intellectum nostrum, quod est eas esse falsas secundum quid.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29121)\n\nAd quartum, quod in oppositum obiicitur, dicendum quod similitudo vel repraesentatio deficiens non inducit rationem falsitatis, nisi inquantum praestat occasionem falsae opinionis. Unde non ubicumque est similitudo, dicitur res falsa, sed ubicumque est talis similitudo, quae nata est facere opinionem falsam, non cuicumque, sed ut in pluribus.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29122)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in sensu non sit falsitas. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de vera Relig., *si omnes corporis sensus ita nuntiant ut afficiuntur, quid ab eis amplius exigere debemus, ignoro*. Et sic videtur quod ex sensibus non fallamur. Et sic falsitas in sensu non est.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29123)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in IV Metaphys., quod *falsitas non est propria sensui, sed phantasiae*.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29124)\n\nPraeterea, in incomplexis non est verum nec falsum, sed solum in complexis. Sed componere et dividere non pertinet ad sensum. Ergo in sensu non est falsitas.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29125)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro Soliloq., *apparet nos in omnibus sensibus similitudine lenocinante falli*.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29126)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod falsitas non est quaerenda in sensu, nisi sicut ibi est veritas. Veritas autem non sic est in sensu, ut sensus cognoscat veritatem; sed inquantum veram apprehensionem habet de sensibilibus, ut supra dictum est. Quod quidem contingit eo quod apprehendit res ut sunt. Unde contingit falsitatem esse in sensu, ex hoc quod apprehendit vel iudicat res aliter quam sint. Sic autem se habet ad cognoscendum res, inquantum similitudo rerum est in sensu. Similitudo autem alicuius rei est in sensu tripliciter. Uno modo, primo et per se; sicut in visu est similitudo colorum et aliorum propriorum sensibilium. Alio modo, per se, sed non primo; sicut in visu est similitudo figurae vel magnitudinis, et aliorum communium sensibilium. Tertio modo, nec primo nec per se, sed per accidens; sicut in visu est similitudo hominis, non inquantum est homo, sed inquantum huic colorato accidit esse hominem. Et circa propria sensibilia sensus non habet falsam cognitionem, nisi per accidens, et ut in paucioribus, ex eo scilicet quod, propter indispositionem organi, non convenienter recipit formam sensibilem, sicut et alia passiva, propter suam indispositionem, deficienter recipiunt impressionem agentium. Et inde est quod, propter corruptionem linguae, infirmis dulcia amara esse videntur. De sensibilibus vero communibus et per accidens, potest esse falsum iudicium etiam in sensu recte disposito, quia sensus non directe refertur ad illa, sed per accidens, vel ex consequenti, inquantum refertur ad alia.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29127)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sensum affici, est ipsum eius sentire. Unde per hoc quod sensus ita nuntiant sicut afficiuntur, sequitur quod non decipiamur in iudicio quo iudicamus nos sentire aliquid. Sed ex eo quod sensus aliter afficitur interdum quam res sit, sequitur quod nuntiet nobis aliquando rem aliter quam sit. Et ex hoc fallimur per sensum circa rem, non circa ipsum sentire.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29128)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod falsitas dicitur non esse propria sensui, quia non decipitur circa proprium obiectum. Unde in alia translatione planius dicitur, quod *sensus proprii sensibilis falsus non est*. Phantasiae autem attribuitur falsitas, quia repraesentat similitudinem rei etiam absentis; unde quando aliquis convertitur ad similitudinem rei tanquam ad rem ipsam, provenit ex tali apprehensione falsitas. Unde etiam philosophus, in V Metaphys., dicit quod umbrae et picturae et somnia dicuntur falsa, inquantum non subsunt res quarum habent similitudinem.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29129)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa procedit, quod falsitas non sit in sensu sicut in cognoscente verum et falsum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 17 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29130)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod falsitas non sit in intellectu. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *omnis qui fallitur, id in quo fallitur, non intelligit*. Sed falsum dicitur esse in aliqua cognitione, secundum quod per eam fallimur. Ergo in intellectu non est falsitas.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29131)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod intellectus semper est rectus. Non ergo in intellectu est falsitas.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29132)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod *ubi compositio intellectuum est, ibi verum et falsum est*. Sed compositio intellectuum est in intellectu. Ergo verum et falsum est in intellectu.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29133)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut res habet esse per propriam formam, ita virtus cognoscitiva habet cognoscere per similitudinem rei cognitae. Unde, sicut res naturalis non deficit ab esse quod sibi competit secundum suam formam, potest autem deficere ab aliquibus accidentalibus vel consequentibus; sicut homo ab hoc quod est habere duos pedes, non autem ab hoc quod est esse hominem, ita virtus cognoscitiva non deficit in cognoscendo respectu illius rei cuius similitudine informatur; potest autem deficere circa aliquid consequens ad ipsam, vel accidens ei. Sicut est dictum quod visus non decipitur circa sensibile proprium, sed circa sensibilia communia, quae consequenter se habent ad illud, et circa sensibilia per accidens. Sicut autem sensus informatur directe similitudine propriorum sensibilium, ita intellectus informatur similitudine quidditatis rei. Unde circa quod quid est intellectus non decipitur, sicut neque sensus circa sensibilia propria. In componendo vero vel dividendo potest decipi, dum attribuit rei cuius quidditatem intelligit, aliquid quod eam non consequitur, vel quod ei opponitur. Sic enim se habet intellectus ad iudicandum de huiusmodi, sicut sensus ad iudicandum de sensibilibus communibus vel per accidens. Hac tamen differentia servata, quae supra circa veritatem dicta est, quod falsitas in intellectu esse potest, non solum quia cognitio intellectus falsa est, sed quia intellectus eam cognoscit, sicut et veritatem, in sensu autem falsitas non est ut cognita, ut dictum est. Quia vero falsitas intellectus per se solum circa compositionem intellectus est, per accidens etiam in operatione intellectus qua cognoscit quod quid est, potest esse falsitas, inquantum ibi compositio intellectus admiscetur. Quod potest esse dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod intellectus definitionem unius attribuit alteri; ut si definitionem circuli attribuat homini. Unde definitio unius rei est falsa de altera. Alio modo, secundum quod partes definitionis componit ad invicem, quae simul sociari non possunt, sic enim definitio non est solum falsa respectu alicuius rei, sed est falsa in se. Ut si formet talem definitionem, animal rationale quadrupes, falsus est intellectus sic definiendo, propterea quod falsus est in formando hanc compositionem, aliquod animal rationale est quadrupes. Et propter hoc, in cognoscendo quidditates simplices non potest esse intellectus falsus, sed vel est verus, vel totaliter nihil intelligit.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29134)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, quia quidditas rei est proprium obiectum intellectus, propter hoc tunc proprie dicimur aliquid intelligere, quando, reducentes illud in quod quid est, sic de eo iudicamus, sicut accidit in demonstrationibus, in quibus non est falsitas. Et hoc modo intelligitur verbum Augustini, quod omnis qui fallitur, non intelligit id in quo fallitur, non autem ita, quod in nulla operatione intellectus aliquis fallatur.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29135)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus semper est rectus, secundum quod intellectus est principiorum, circa quae non decipitur, ex eadem causa qua non decipitur circa quod quid est. Nam principia per se nota sunt illa quae statim, intellectis terminis, cognoscuntur, ex eo quod praedicatum ponitur in definitione subiecti.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29136)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod verum et falsum non sint contraria. Verum enim et falsum opponuntur sicut quod est et quod non est, *nam verum est id quod est*, ut dicit Augustinus. Sed quod est et quod non est, non opponuntur ut contraria. Ergo verum et falsum non sunt contraria.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29137)\n\nPraeterea, unum contrariorum non est in alio. Sed falsum est in vero, quia, sicut dicit Augustinus in libro Soliloq., *tragoedus non esset falsus Hector, si non esset verus tragoedus*. Ergo verum et falsum non sunt contraria.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29138)\n\nPraeterea, in Deo non est contrarietas aliqua, nihil enim divinae substantiae est contrarium, ut dicit Augustinus, XII de Civit. Dei. Sed Deo opponitur falsitas, nam idolum in Scriptura mendacium nominatur, Ierem. VIII, *apprehenderunt mendacium*; Glossa, *idest idola*. Ergo verum et falsum non sunt contraria.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29139)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in II Periherm., ponit enim falsam opinionem verae contrariam.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29140)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod verum et falsum opponuntur ut contraria, et non sicut affirmatio et negatio, ut quidam dixerunt. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod negatio neque ponit aliquid, neque determinat sibi aliquod subiectum. Et propter hoc, potest dici tam de ente quam de non ente; sicut non videns, et non sedens. Privatio autem non ponit aliquid, sed determinat sibi subiectum. Est enim negatio in subiecto, ut dicitur IV Metaphys., caecum enim non dicitur nisi de eo quod est natum videre. Contrarium vero et aliquid ponit, et subiectum determinat, nigrum enim est aliqua species coloris. Falsum autem aliquid ponit. Est enim falsum, ut dicit philosophus, IV Metaphys., ex eo quod dicitur vel videtur aliquid esse quod non est, vel non esse quod est. Sicut enim verum ponit acceptionem adaequatam rei, ita falsum acceptionem rei non adaequatam. Unde manifestum est quod verum et falsum sunt contraria.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29141)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod id quod est in rebus, est veritas rei sed id quod est ut apprehensum, est verum intellectus, in quo primo est veritas. Unde et falsum est id quod non est ut apprehensum. Apprehendere autem esse et non esse, contrarietatem habet, sicut probat philosophus, in II Periherm., quod huic opinioni, bonum est bonum, contraria est, bonum non est bonum.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29142)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod falsum non fundatur in vero sibi contrario, sicut nec malum in bono sibi contrario; sed in eo quod sibi subiicitur. Et hoc ideo in utroque accidit, quia verum et bonum communia sunt, et convertuntur cum ente, unde, sicut omnis privatio fundatur in subiecto quod est ens, ita omne malum fundatur in aliquo bono, et omne falsum in aliquo vero.\n\n[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29143)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quia contraria et opposita privative nata sunt fieri circa idem, ideo Deo, prout in se consideratur, non est aliquid contrarium, neque ratione suae bonitatis, neque ratione suae veritatis, quia in intellectu eius non potest esse falsitas aliqua. Sed in apprehensione nostra habet aliquid contrarium, nam verae opinioni de ipso contrariatur falsa opinio. Et sic idola mendacia dicuntur opposita veritati divinae, inquantum falsa opinio de idolis contrariatur verae opinioni de unitate Dei.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0002","text":"[I q. 17 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de falsitate. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum falsitas sit in rebus. Secundo, utrum sit in sensu. Tertio, utrum sit in intellectu. Quarto, de oppositione veri et falsi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0005","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod falsitas non sit in rebus. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro Soliloq., *si verum est id quod est, falsum non esse uspiam concludetur, quovis repugnante*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0007","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0008","text":"Praeterea, falsum dicitur a fallendo. Sed res non fallunt, ut dicit Augustinus in libro de vera Relig., *quia non ostendunt aliud quam suam speciem*. Ergo falsum in rebus non invenitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0009","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0010","text":"Praeterea, verum dicitur in rebus per comparationem ad intellectum divinum, ut supra dictum est. Sed quaelibet res, inquantum est, imitatur Deum. Ergo quaelibet res vera est, absque falsitate. Et sic nulla res est falsa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0011","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de vera Relig., quod *omne corpus est verum corpus et falsa unitas*; quia imitatur unitatem, et non est unitas. Sed quaelibet res imitatur divinam bonitatem, et ab ea deficit. Ergo in omnibus rebus est falsitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0013","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum verum et falsum opponantur; opposita autem sunt circa idem; necesse est ut ibi prius quaeratur falsitas, ubi primo veritas invenitur, hoc est in intellectu. In rebus autem neque veritas neque falsitas est, nisi per ordinem ad intellectum. Et quia unumquodque secundum id quod convenit ei per se, simpliciter nominatur; secundum autem id quod convenit ei per accidens, non nominatur nisi secundum quid; res quidem simpliciter falsa dici posset per comparationem ad intellectum a quo dependet, cui comparatur per se; in ordine autem ad alium intellectum, cui comparatur per accidens, non posset dici falsa nisi secundum quid. Dependent autem ab intellectu divino res naturales, sicut ab intellectu humano res artificiales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","text":"Dicuntur igitur res artificiales falsae simpliciter et secundum se, inquantum deficiunt a forma artis, unde dicitur aliquis artifex opus falsum facere, quando deficit ab operatione artis. Sic autem in rebus dependentibus a Deo, falsitas inveniri non potest per comparationem ad intellectum divinum, cum quidquid in rebus accidit, ex ordinatione divini intellectus procedat, nisi forte in voluntariis agentibus tantum, in quorum potestate est subducere se ab ordinatione divini intellectus; in quo malum culpae consistit, secundum quod ipsa peccata falsitates et mendacia dicuntur in Scripturis, secundum illud Psalmi IV, *ut quid diligitis vanitatem et quaeritis mendacium?* Sicut per oppositum operatio virtuosa veritas vitae nominatur, inquantum subditur ordini divini intellectus; sicut dicitur Ioan. III, *qui facit veritatem, venit ad lucem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","text":"Sed per ordinem ad intellectum nostrum, ad quem comparantur res naturales per accidens, possunt dici falsae, non simpliciter, sed secundum quid. Et hoc dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum rationem significati, ut dicatur illud esse falsum in rebus, quod significatur vel repraesentatur oratione vel intellectu falso. Secundum quem modum quaelibet res potest dici esse falsa, quantum ad id quod ei non inest, sicut si dicamus diametrum esse falsum commensurabile, ut dicit philosophus in V Metaphys.; et sicut dicit Augustinus, in libro Soliloq., quod *tragoedus est falsus Hector*. Sicut e contrario potest unumquodque dici verum, secundum id quod competit ei. Alio modo, per modum causae. Et sic dicitur res esse falsa, quae nata est facere de se opinionem falsam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0017","text":"Et quia innatum est nobis per ea quae exterius apparent de rebus iudicare, eo quod nostra cognitio a sensu ortum habet, qui primo et per se est exteriorum accidentium; ideo ea quae in exterioribus accidentibus habent similitudinem aliarum rerum, dicuntur esse falsa secundum illas res; sicut fel est falsum mel, et stannum est falsum argentum. Et secundum hoc dicit Augustinus, in libro Soliloq., quod *eas res falsas nominamus, quae verisimilia apprehendimus*. Et philosophus dicit, in V Metaphys., quod *falsa dicuntur quaecumque apta nata sunt apparere aut qualia non sunt, aut quae non sunt*. Et per hunc modum etiam dicitur homo falsus, inquantum est amativus falsarum opinionum vel locutionum. Non autem ex hoc quod potest eas confingere, quia sic etiam sapientes et scientes falsi dicerentur, ut dicitur in V Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0018","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod res comparata ad intellectum, secundum id quod est, dicitur vera, secundum id quod non est, dicitur falsa. *Unde verus tragoedus est falsus Hector*, ut dicitur in II Soliloq. Sicut igitur in his quae sunt, invenitur quoddam non esse; ita in his quae sunt, invenitur quaedam ratio falsitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0020","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod res per se non fallunt, sed per accidens. Dant enim occasionem falsitatis, eo quod similitudinem eorum gerunt, quorum non habent existentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0022","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod per comparationem ad intellectum divinum non dicuntur res falsae, quod esset eas esse falsas simpliciter, sed per comparationem ad intellectum nostrum, quod est eas esse falsas secundum quid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0024","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025","text":"Ad quartum, quod in oppositum obiicitur, dicendum quod similitudo vel repraesentatio deficiens non inducit rationem falsitatis, nisi inquantum praestat occasionem falsae opinionis. Unde non ubicumque est similitudo, dicitur res falsa, sed ubicumque est talis similitudo, quae nata est facere opinionem falsam, non cuicumque, sed ut in pluribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0027","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in sensu non sit falsitas. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de vera Relig., *si omnes corporis sensus ita nuntiant ut afficiuntur, quid ab eis amplius exigere debemus, ignoro*. Et sic videtur quod ex sensibus non fallamur. Et sic falsitas in sensu non est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0029","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0030","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in IV Metaphys., quod *falsitas non est propria sensui, sed phantasiae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0031","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0032","text":"Praeterea, in incomplexis non est verum nec falsum, sed solum in complexis. Sed componere et dividere non pertinet ad sensum. Ergo in sensu non est falsitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0033","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0034","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro Soliloq., *apparet nos in omnibus sensibus similitudine lenocinante falli*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0035","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod falsitas non est quaerenda in sensu, nisi sicut ibi est veritas. Veritas autem non sic est in sensu, ut sensus cognoscat veritatem; sed inquantum veram apprehensionem habet de sensibilibus, ut supra dictum est. Quod quidem contingit eo quod apprehendit res ut sunt. Unde contingit falsitatem esse in sensu, ex hoc quod apprehendit vel iudicat res aliter quam sint. Sic autem se habet ad cognoscendum res, inquantum similitudo rerum est in sensu. Similitudo autem alicuius rei est in sensu tripliciter. Uno modo, primo et per se; sicut in visu est similitudo colorum et aliorum propriorum sensibilium. Alio modo, per se, sed non primo; sicut in visu est similitudo figurae vel magnitudinis, et aliorum communium sensibilium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","text":"Tertio modo, nec primo nec per se, sed per accidens; sicut in visu est similitudo hominis, non inquantum est homo, sed inquantum huic colorato accidit esse hominem. Et circa propria sensibilia sensus non habet falsam cognitionem, nisi per accidens, et ut in paucioribus, ex eo scilicet quod, propter indispositionem organi, non convenienter recipit formam sensibilem, sicut et alia passiva, propter suam indispositionem, deficienter recipiunt impressionem agentium. Et inde est quod, propter corruptionem linguae, infirmis dulcia amara esse videntur. De sensibilibus vero communibus et per accidens, potest esse falsum iudicium etiam in sensu recte disposito, quia sensus non directe refertur ad illa, sed per accidens, vel ex consequenti, inquantum refertur ad alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0038","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0039","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sensum affici, est ipsum eius sentire. Unde per hoc quod sensus ita nuntiant sicut afficiuntur, sequitur quod non decipiamur in iudicio quo iudicamus nos sentire aliquid. Sed ex eo quod sensus aliter afficitur interdum quam res sit, sequitur quod nuntiet nobis aliquando rem aliter quam sit. Et ex hoc fallimur per sensum circa rem, non circa ipsum sentire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0040","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod falsitas dicitur non esse propria sensui, quia non decipitur circa proprium obiectum. Unde in alia translatione planius dicitur, quod *sensus proprii sensibilis falsus non est*. Phantasiae autem attribuitur falsitas, quia repraesentat similitudinem rei etiam absentis; unde quando aliquis convertitur ad similitudinem rei tanquam ad rem ipsam, provenit ex tali apprehensione falsitas. Unde etiam philosophus, in V Metaphys., dicit quod umbrae et picturae et somnia dicuntur falsa, inquantum non subsunt res quarum habent similitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0042","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0043","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa procedit, quod falsitas non sit in sensu sicut in cognoscente verum et falsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0045","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod falsitas non sit in intellectu. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *omnis qui fallitur, id in quo fallitur, non intelligit*. Sed falsum dicitur esse in aliqua cognitione, secundum quod per eam fallimur. Ergo in intellectu non est falsitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0047","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0048","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod intellectus semper est rectus. Non ergo in intellectu est falsitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0049","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0050","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod *ubi compositio intellectuum est, ibi verum et falsum est*. Sed compositio intellectuum est in intellectu. Ergo verum et falsum est in intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0051","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut res habet esse per propriam formam, ita virtus cognoscitiva habet cognoscere per similitudinem rei cognitae. Unde, sicut res naturalis non deficit ab esse quod sibi competit secundum suam formam, potest autem deficere ab aliquibus accidentalibus vel consequentibus; sicut homo ab hoc quod est habere duos pedes, non autem ab hoc quod est esse hominem, ita virtus cognoscitiva non deficit in cognoscendo respectu illius rei cuius similitudine informatur; potest autem deficere circa aliquid consequens ad ipsam, vel accidens ei. Sicut est dictum quod visus non decipitur circa sensibile proprium, sed circa sensibilia communia, quae consequenter se habent ad illud, et circa sensibilia per accidens. Sicut autem sensus informatur directe similitudine propriorum sensibilium, ita intellectus informatur similitudine quidditatis rei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","text":"Unde circa quod quid est intellectus non decipitur, sicut neque sensus circa sensibilia propria. In componendo vero vel dividendo potest decipi, dum attribuit rei cuius quidditatem intelligit, aliquid quod eam non consequitur, vel quod ei opponitur. Sic enim se habet intellectus ad iudicandum de huiusmodi, sicut sensus ad iudicandum de sensibilibus communibus vel per accidens. Hac tamen differentia servata, quae supra circa veritatem dicta est, quod falsitas in intellectu esse potest, non solum quia cognitio intellectus falsa est, sed quia intellectus eam cognoscit, sicut et veritatem, in sensu autem falsitas non est ut cognita, ut dictum est. Quia vero falsitas intellectus per se solum circa compositionem intellectus est, per accidens etiam in operatione intellectus qua cognoscit quod quid est, potest esse falsitas, inquantum ibi compositio intellectus admiscetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","text":"Quod potest esse dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod intellectus definitionem unius attribuit alteri; ut si definitionem circuli attribuat homini. Unde definitio unius rei est falsa de altera. Alio modo, secundum quod partes definitionis componit ad invicem, quae simul sociari non possunt, sic enim definitio non est solum falsa respectu alicuius rei, sed est falsa in se. Ut si formet talem definitionem, animal rationale quadrupes, falsus est intellectus sic definiendo, propterea quod falsus est in formando hanc compositionem, aliquod animal rationale est quadrupes. Et propter hoc, in cognoscendo quidditates simplices non potest esse intellectus falsus, sed vel est verus, vel totaliter nihil intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0055","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0056","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, quia quidditas rei est proprium obiectum intellectus, propter hoc tunc proprie dicimur aliquid intelligere, quando, reducentes illud in quod quid est, sic de eo iudicamus, sicut accidit in demonstrationibus, in quibus non est falsitas. Et hoc modo intelligitur verbum Augustini, quod omnis qui fallitur, non intelligit id in quo fallitur, non autem ita, quod in nulla operatione intellectus aliquis fallatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0057","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0058","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus semper est rectus, secundum quod intellectus est principiorum, circa quae non decipitur, ex eadem causa qua non decipitur circa quod quid est. Nam principia per se nota sunt illa quae statim, intellectis terminis, cognoscuntur, ex eo quod praedicatum ponitur in definitione subiecti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0059","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0060","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0061","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod verum et falsum non sint contraria. Verum enim et falsum opponuntur sicut quod est et quod non est, *nam verum est id quod est*, ut dicit Augustinus. Sed quod est et quod non est, non opponuntur ut contraria. Ergo verum et falsum non sunt contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0062","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0063","text":"Praeterea, unum contrariorum non est in alio. Sed falsum est in vero, quia, sicut dicit Augustinus in libro Soliloq., *tragoedus non esset falsus Hector, si non esset verus tragoedus*. Ergo verum et falsum non sunt contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0064","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0065","text":"Praeterea, in Deo non est contrarietas aliqua, nihil enim divinae substantiae est contrarium, ut dicit Augustinus, XII de Civit. Dei. Sed Deo opponitur falsitas, nam idolum in Scriptura mendacium nominatur, Ierem. VIII, *apprehenderunt mendacium*; Glossa, *idest idola*. Ergo verum et falsum non sunt contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0066","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0067","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in II Periherm., ponit enim falsam opinionem verae contrariam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0068","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod verum et falsum opponuntur ut contraria, et non sicut affirmatio et negatio, ut quidam dixerunt. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod negatio neque ponit aliquid, neque determinat sibi aliquod subiectum. Et propter hoc, potest dici tam de ente quam de non ente; sicut non videns, et non sedens. Privatio autem non ponit aliquid, sed determinat sibi subiectum. Est enim negatio in subiecto, ut dicitur IV Metaphys., caecum enim non dicitur nisi de eo quod est natum videre. Contrarium vero et aliquid ponit, et subiectum determinat, nigrum enim est aliqua species coloris. Falsum autem aliquid ponit. Est enim falsum, ut dicit philosophus, IV Metaphys., ex eo quod dicitur vel videtur aliquid esse quod non est, vel non esse quod est. Sicut enim verum ponit acceptionem adaequatam rei, ita falsum acceptionem rei non adaequatam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0070","text":"Unde manifestum est quod verum et falsum sunt contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0071","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0072","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod id quod est in rebus, est veritas rei sed id quod est ut apprehensum, est verum intellectus, in quo primo est veritas. Unde et falsum est id quod non est ut apprehensum. Apprehendere autem esse et non esse, contrarietatem habet, sicut probat philosophus, in II Periherm., quod huic opinioni, bonum est bonum, contraria est, bonum non est bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0073","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod falsum non fundatur in vero sibi contrario, sicut nec malum in bono sibi contrario; sed in eo quod sibi subiicitur. Et hoc ideo in utroque accidit, quia verum et bonum communia sunt, et convertuntur cum ente, unde, sicut omnis privatio fundatur in subiecto quod est ens, ita omne malum fundatur in aliquo bono, et omne falsum in aliquo vero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0075","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quia contraria et opposita privative nata sunt fieri circa idem, ideo Deo, prout in se consideratur, non est aliquid contrarium, neque ratione suae bonitatis, neque ratione suae veritatis, quia in intellectu eius non potest esse falsitas aliqua. Sed in apprehensione nostra habet aliquid contrarium, nam verae opinioni de ipso contrariatur falsa opinio. Et sic idola mendacia dicuntur opposita veritati divinae, inquantum falsa opinio de idolis contrariatur verae opinioni de unitate Dei.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"47d5f5be446b06331baaeb99a784344d41b5bc8d6937a7d26947722be5d3710b","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T135610145485Z_6da9fcdf","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 17","source_sha256":"47d5f5be446b06331baaeb99a784344d41b5bc8d6937a7d26947722be5d3710b","sha256":"f0044c3900356290c33556a960d7349a5ce9e0be7c9fc3a3b1997832e1e8380a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:56:10.145498+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0002","text":"[I q. 17 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003","text":"Next, there is inquiry concerning falsity. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether falsity is in things. Second, whether it is in sense. Third, whether it is in the intellect. Fourth, concerning the opposition of the true and the false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0005","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0006","text":"We proceed to the first as follows. It seems that falsity is not in things. For Augustine says, in the book *Soliloquies*, “if the true is that which is, it will be concluded, whatever may oppose it, that the false is nowhere.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0007","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0008","text":"Further, the false is so called from deceiving. But things do not deceive, as Augustine says in the book *On True Religion*, “because they display nothing other than their own appearance.” Therefore the false is not found in things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0009","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0010","text":"Further, the true is said to be in things by comparison with the divine intellect, as was said above. But every thing, insofar as it is, imitates God. Therefore every thing is true, without falsity. And thus no thing is false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0011","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *On True Religion*, that “every body is a true body and a false unity,” because it imitates unity and is not unity. But every thing imitates the divine goodness and falls short of it. Therefore falsity is in all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0013","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","text":"I answer that, since the true and the false are opposed, and opposites concern the same thing, falsity must first be sought where truth is first found, that is, in the intellect. But in things neither truth nor falsity is present except through an ordering to an intellect. And because each thing is named simply according to that which belongs to it through itself, whereas according to that which belongs to it accidentally it is named only in a certain respect, a thing could indeed be called false simply through comparison with the intellect upon which it depends, to which it is compared through itself; but in relation to another intellect, to which it is compared accidentally, it could not be called false except in a certain respect. Now natural things depend upon the divine intellect, just as artificial things depend upon the human intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","text":"Therefore artificial things are called false simply and in themselves insofar as they fall short of the form of the art; whence an artisan is said to make a false work when he falls short of the operation of the art. But in things dependent on God, falsity cannot be found by comparison with the divine intellect, since whatever occurs in things proceeds from the ordering of the divine intellect—except perhaps in voluntary agents alone, in whose power it is to withdraw themselves from the ordering of the divine intellect; and in this the evil of fault consists, insofar as sins themselves are called falsities and lies in the Scriptures, according to that saying of Psalm IV: “Why do you love vanity and seek a lie?” Just as, conversely, a virtuous operation is called the truth of life, insofar as it is subject to the order of the divine intellect; as is said in John III: “he who does the truth comes to the light.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","text":"But through an ordering to our intellect, to which natural things are compared accidentally, they can be called false, not simply, but in a certain respect. And this in two ways. In one way, according to the character of what is signified, so that that is said to be false in things which is signified or represented by a false statement or intellect. In this way, any thing can be said to be false with respect to that which is not in it; as when we say that the diameter is falsely commensurable, as the Philosopher says in *Metaphysics* V; and as Augustine says in the book *Soliloquies*, that “an actor is a false Hector.” Just as, conversely, each thing can be called true according to that which belongs to it. In another way, by way of cause. And thus that thing is said to be false which is naturally apt to cause a false opinion of itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0017","text":"And because it is innate in us to judge concerning things from those things which appear externally, since our knowledge takes its origin from sense, which is first and through itself of external accidents, therefore those things which in their external accidents have a likeness to other things are said to be false according to those things; just as gall is false honey, and tin is false silver. And according to this Augustine says, in the book *Soliloquies*, that “we call those things false which we apprehend as likely to be true.” And the Philosopher says, in *Metaphysics* V, that “false things are said to be whatever are naturally apt to appear either as things which they are not, or as things which are not.” And in this way a man too is called false insofar as he is a lover of false opinions or utterances. But he is not called false from being able to invent them, for in that way even the wise and the knowing would be called false, as is said in *Metaphysics* V.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0018","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a thing compared to an intellect is called true according to that which it is, and false according to that which it is not. “Hence a true actor is a false Hector,” as is said in *Soliloquies* II. Therefore, just as in those things which are there is found a certain non-being, so in those things which are there is found a certain character of falsity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0020","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that things do not deceive through themselves, but accidentally. For they provide an occasion of falsity because they bear the likeness of those things whose existence they do not have.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0022","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that things are not called false by comparison with the divine intellect, which would be for them to be false simply, but by comparison with our intellect, which is for them to be false in a certain respect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0024","text":"[I q. 17 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025","text":"To the fourth, which is objected to the contrary, it must be said that a deficient likeness or representation does not bring about the character of falsehood, except insofar as it affords an occasion for a false opinion. Hence, not wherever there is a likeness is a thing called false, but wherever there is such a likeness as is naturally apt to produce a false opinion, not in anyone whatsoever, but for the most part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0027","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0028","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there is no falsehood in sense. For Augustine says, in the book *On True Religion*, *if all the senses of the body report just as they are affected, I do not know what more we ought to demand of them*. And thus it seems that we are not deceived by the senses. And thus there is no falsehood in sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0029","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0030","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in IV *Metaphysics*, that *falsehood does not belong properly to sense, but to imagination*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0031","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0032","text":"Moreover, in incomposite things there is neither truth nor falsehood, but only in composite things. But to combine and divide does not belong to sense. Therefore there is no falsehood in sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0033","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0034","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says in the book *Soliloquies*, *it appears that in all the senses we are deceived by the seductive influence of likeness*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0035","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","text":"I answer that it must be said that falsehood is not to be sought in sense except as truth is there. But truth is not in sense in such a way that sense knows truth; rather, insofar as it has a true apprehension of sensibles, as was said above. This indeed happens because it apprehends things as they are. Hence falsehood occurs in sense from the fact that it apprehends or judges things otherwise than they are. Now it is related to knowing things insofar as a likeness of things is in sense. But the likeness of some thing is in sense in three ways. In one way, primarily and through itself; as in sight there is a likeness of colors and of the other proper sensibles. In another way, through itself, but not primarily; as in sight there is a likeness of shape or magnitude, and of the other common sensibles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","text":"In a third way, neither primarily nor through itself, but accidentally; as in sight there is a likeness of a human being, not insofar as he is a human being, but insofar as being a human being happens to belong to this colored thing. And concerning proper sensibles, sense does not have false cognition except accidentally, and in fewer cases, namely because, owing to an indisposition of the organ, it does not receive the sensible form suitably; just as other passive things too, owing to their indisposition, receive the impression of agents deficiently. Thus, because of a corruption of the tongue, sweet things seem bitter to the sick. But concerning common sensibles and accidental sensibles, there can be a false judgment even in sense rightly disposed, because sense is not directly referred to them, but accidentally or consequently, insofar as it is referred to other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0038","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0039","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that for sense to be affected is its very sensing. Hence, from the fact that the senses report just as they are affected, it follows that we are not deceived in the judgment by which we judge that we sense something. But from the fact that sense is sometimes affected otherwise than the thing is, it follows that it sometimes reports to us the thing otherwise than it is. And from this we are deceived through sense concerning the thing, not concerning the sensing itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0040","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041","text":"To the second, it must be said that falsehood is said not to belong properly to sense because it is not deceived concerning its proper object. Hence, in another translation it is said more plainly that *sense is not false with respect to the proper sensible*. But falsehood is attributed to imagination because it represents the likeness of a thing even when it is absent; hence, when someone turns to the likeness of a thing as though to the thing itself, falsehood results from such an apprehension. Hence the Philosopher also says, in V *Metaphysics*, that shadows, paintings, and dreams are called false insofar as the things whose likeness they have are not present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0042","text":"[I q. 17 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0043","text":"To the third, it must be said that that argument proves that falsehood is not in sense as in one cognizing true and false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0045","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0046","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that falsehood is not in the intellect. For Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *everyone who is deceived does not understand that in which he is deceived*. But the false is said to be in some cognition insofar as we are deceived through it. Therefore there is no falsehood in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0047","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0048","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in III *On the Soul*, that the intellect is always right. Therefore there is no falsehood in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0049","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0050","text":"On the contrary, it is said in *On the Soul* III that *where there is composition of understandings, there are truth and falsity*. But composition of understandings is in the intellect. Therefore truth and falsity are in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0051","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","text":"I answer that, as a thing has being through its proper form, so a cognitive power has cognition through the likeness of the thing known. Hence, just as a natural thing does not fail in the being that belongs to it according to its form, though it can fail in certain accidental or consequent features—for example, a human being in having two feet, but not in being a human being—so a cognitive power does not fail in knowing with respect to the thing by whose likeness it is informed; but it can fail concerning something consequent upon it or accidental to it. Thus it has been said that sight is not deceived concerning its proper sensible, but concerning common sensibles, which stand consequently in relation to it, and concerning sensibles by accident. Now, just as sense is informed directly by the likeness of proper sensibles, so intellect is informed by the likeness of the quiddity of a thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","text":"Hence the intellect is not deceived concerning what a thing is, just as neither is sense concerning proper sensibles. But in composing or dividing it can be deceived, when it attributes to the thing whose quiddity it understands something that does not follow upon it, or that is opposed to it. For in judging concerning such things, the intellect stands as sense does in judging concerning common sensibles or sensibles by accident. Yet this difference must be maintained, which was stated above concerning truth: that falsity can be in the intellect not only because the cognition of the intellect is false, but because the intellect knows it, just as it knows truth; whereas falsity is not in sense as known, as was said. But because falsity of the intellect is per se only concerning the composition of the intellect, falsity can also be present per accidens in the operation of the intellect by which it knows what a thing is, insofar as composition of the intellect is mingled in there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","text":"This can occur in two ways. In one way, insofar as the intellect attributes the definition of one thing to another; as if it were to attribute the definition of a circle to a human being. Hence the definition of one thing is false of another. In another way, insofar as it composes with one another the parts of a definition that cannot be joined together at the same time; for then the definition is not only false in respect of some thing, but is false in itself. For example, if it forms such a definition, “a rational four-footed animal,” the intellect is false in thus defining, because it is false in forming this composition, “some rational animal is four-footed.” And for this reason, in knowing simple quiddities the intellect cannot be false, but either it is true or understands nothing at all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0055","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0056","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, because the quiddity of a thing is the proper object of the intellect, we are properly said to understand something when, reducing it to what a thing is, we judge concerning it in this way, as happens in demonstrations, in which there is no falsity. And this is the sense in which Augustine's statement is understood, that everyone who is deceived does not understand that in which he is deceived; but not so that someone is deceived in no operation of the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0057","text":"[I q. 17 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0058","text":"To the second, it must be said that the intellect is always correct insofar as it is the intellect of principles, concerning which it is not deceived, for the same reason that it is not deceived concerning what a thing is. For self-evident principles are those which, once the terms have been understood, are known immediately, from the fact that the predicate is set down in the definition of the subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0059","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0060","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0061","text":"The fourth point is approached thus. It seems that truth and falsity are not contraries. For truth and falsity are opposed as that which is and that which is not, since, as Augustine says, *the true is that which is*. But that which is and that which is not are not opposed as contraries. Therefore truth and falsity are not contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0062","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0063","text":"Further, one of two contraries is not in the other. But the false is in the true, because, as Augustine says in the *Soliloquies*, *the actor would not be a false Hector if he were not a true actor*. Therefore truth and falsity are not contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0064","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0065","text":"Further, there is no contrariety in God, for nothing is contrary to the divine substance, as Augustine says, *City of God* XII. But falsity is opposed to God, for an idol is called a lie in Scripture, Jeremiah VIII: *they have laid hold of a lie*; the Gloss: *that is, idols*. Therefore truth and falsity are not contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0066","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0067","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says in *On Interpretation* II, for he holds a false opinion to be contrary to a true one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0068","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","text":"I answer that truth and falsity are opposed as contraries, and not as affirmation and negation, as some have said. To make this evident, it should be known that negation neither posits anything nor determines a subject for itself. For this reason, it can be said both of being and of non-being; for example, “not seeing” and “not sitting.” Privation, however, does not posit anything, but determines a subject for itself. For it is negation in a subject, as is said in *Metaphysics* IV; for “blind” is said only of that which is naturally apt to see. A contrary, however, both posits something and determines a subject, for black is a species of color. But the false posits something. For the false, as the Philosopher says in *Metaphysics* IV, arises from something being said or appearing to be which is not, or not to be which is. For just as the true posits an apprehension adequate to the thing, so the false posits an apprehension of the thing not adequate to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0070","text":"Hence it is manifest that truth and falsity are contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0071","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0072","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that what is in things is the truth of the thing; but what is as apprehended is the truth of the intellect, in which truth is first. Hence the false too is what is not as apprehended. But to apprehend being and non-being has contrariety, as the Philosopher proves in *On Interpretation* II: contrary to this opinion, “good is good,” is “good is not good.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0073","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","text":"To the second, it must be said that the false is not founded upon the true contrary to it, just as evil is not founded upon the good contrary to it; but upon that which is subject to it. And this occurs in both cases because the true and the good are common, and are convertible with being; whence, just as every privation is founded in a subject which is a being, so every evil is founded in some good, and every falsehood in some truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0075","text":"[I q. 17 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076","text":"To the third, it must be said that, because contraries and privative opposites are naturally apt to occur concerning the same thing, therefore nothing is contrary to God, insofar as He is considered in Himself, neither by reason of His goodness nor by reason of His truth, because no falsity can be in His intellect. But in our apprehension something is contrary to Him, for a false opinion about Him is contrary to a true opinion. And thus lying idols are said to be opposed to the divine truth, insofar as a false opinion about idols is contrary to the true opinion of the unity of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076"]}],"source_digest":"1daee6d2a87104c49cfcae965700b95de22eda014186393b38066a86facff5a5","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 17"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 17","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10","title":"First Part · Question 18","order":19,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_10","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 18 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29144)\n\nQuoniam autem intelligere viventium est, post considerationem de scientia et intellectu divino, considerandum est de vita ipsius. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, quorum sit vivere. Secundo, quid sit vita. Tertio, utrum vita Deo conveniat. Quarto, utrum omnia in Deo sint vita.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29145)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnium rerum naturalium sit vivere. Dicit enim philosophus, in VIII Physic., quod motus est ut vita quaedam natura existentibus omnibus. Sed omnes res naturales participant motum. Ergo omnes res naturales participant vitam.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29146)\n\nPraeterea, plantae dicuntur vivere, inquantum habent in seipsis principium motus augmenti et decrementi. Sed motus localis est perfectior et prior secundum naturam quam motus augmenti et decrementi, ut probatur in VIII Physic. Cum igitur omnia corpora naturalia habeant aliquod principium motus localis, videtur quod omnia corpora naturalia vivant.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29147)\n\nPraeterea, inter corpora naturalia imperfectiora sunt elementa. Sed eis attribuitur vita, dicuntur enim aquae vivae. Ergo multo magis alia corpora naturalia vitam habent.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29148)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, VI cap. de Div. Nom., quod *plantae secundum ultimam resonantiam vitae habent vivere*, ex quo potest accipi quod ultimum gradum vitae obtinent plantae. Sed corpora inanimata sunt infra plantas. Ergo eorum non est vivere.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29149)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ex his quae manifeste vivunt, accipere possumus quorum sit vivere, et quorum non sit vivere. Vivere autem manifeste animalibus convenit, dicitur enim in libro de vegetabilibus, quod *vita in animalibus manifesta est*. Unde secundum illud oportet distinguere viventia a non viventibus secundum quod animalia dicuntur vivere. Hoc autem est in quo primo manifestatur vita, et in quo ultimo remanet. Primo autem dicimus animal vivere, quando incipit ex se motum habere; et tandiu iudicatur animal vivere, quandiu talis motus in eo apparet; quando vero iam ex se non habet aliquem motum, sed movetur tantum ab alio tunc dicitur animal mortuum, per defectum vitae. Ex quo patet quod illa proprie sunt viventia, quae seipsa secundum aliquam speciem motus movent; sive accipiatur motus proprie, sicut motus dicitur actus imperfecti, idest existentis in potentia; sive motus accipiatur communiter, prout motus dicitur actus perfecti, prout intelligere et sentire dicitur moveri, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ut sic viventia dicantur quaecumque se agunt ad motum vel operationem aliquam, ea vero in quorum natura non est ut se agant ad aliquem motum vel operationem, viventia dici non possunt, nisi per aliquam similitudinem.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29150)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum illud philosophi potest intelligi vel de motu primo, scilicet corporum caelestium; vel de motu communiter. Et utroque modo motus dicitur quasi vita corporum naturalium, per similitudinem; et non per proprietatem. Nam motus caeli est in universo corporalium naturarum, sicut motus cordis in animali, quo conservatur vita. Similiter etiam quicumque motus naturalis hoc modo se habet ad res naturales, ut quaedam similitudo vitalis operationis. Unde, si totum universum corporale esset unum animal, ita quod iste motus esset a movente intrinseco, ut quidam posuerunt, sequeretur quod motus esset vita omnium naturalium corporum.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29151)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod corporibus gravibus et levibus non competit moveri, nisi secundum quod sunt extra dispositionem suae naturae, utpote cum sunt extra locum proprium, cum enim sunt in loco proprio et naturali, quiescunt. Sed plantae et aliae res viventes moventur motu vitali, secundum hoc quod sunt in sua dispositione naturali, non autem in accedendo ad eam vel in recedendo ab ea, imo secundum quod recedunt a tali motu, recedunt a naturali dispositione. Et praeterea, corpora gravia et levia moventur a motore extrinseco, vel generante, qui dat formam, vel removente prohibens, ut dicitur in VIII Physic., et ita non movent seipsa, sicut corpora viventia.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29152)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aquae vivae dicuntur, quae habent continuum fluxum, aquae enim stantes, quae non continuantur ad principium continue fluens, dicuntur mortuae, ut aquae cisternarum et lacunarum. Et hoc dicitur per similitudinem, inquantum enim videntur se movere, habent similitudinem vitae. Sed tamen non est in eis vera ratio vitae, quia hunc motum non habent a seipsis, sed a causa generante eas; sicut accidit circa motum aliorum gravium et levium.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29153)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod vita sit quaedam operatio. Nihil enim dividitur nisi per ea quae sunt sui generis. Sed vivere dividitur per operationes quasdam, ut patet per philosophum in II libro de anima, qui distinguit vivere per quatuor, scilicet alimento uti, sentire, moveri secundum locum, et intelligere. Ergo vita est operatio quaedam.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29154)\n\nPraeterea, vita activa dicitur alia esse a contemplativa. Sed contemplativi ab activis non diversificantur nisi secundum operationes quasdam. Ergo vita est quaedam operatio.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29155)\n\nPraeterea, cognoscere Deum est operatio quaedam. Haec autem est vita, ut patet per illud Ioan. XVII, *haec est autem vita aeterna, ut cognoscant te solum verum Deum*. Ergo vita est operatio.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29156)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in II de anima, *vivere viventibus est esse*.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29157)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex dictis patet, intellectus noster, qui proprie est cognoscitivus quidditatis rei ut proprii obiecti, accipit a sensu, cuius propria obiecta sunt accidentia exteriora. Et inde est quod ex his quae exterius apparent de re, devenimus ad cognoscendam essentiam rei. Et quia sic nominamus aliquid sicut cognoscimus illud, ut ex supradictis patet, inde est quod plerumque a proprietatibus exterioribus imponuntur nomina ad significandas essentias rerum. Unde huiusmodi nomina quandoque accipiuntur proprie pro ipsis essentiis rerum, ad quas significandas principaliter sunt imposita, aliquando autem sumuntur pro proprietatibus a quibus imponuntur, et hoc minus proprie. Sicut patet quod hoc nomen corpus impositum est ad significandum quoddam genus substantiarum, ex eo quod in eis inveniuntur tres dimensiones, et ideo aliquando ponitur hoc nomen corpus ad significandas tres dimensiones, secundum quod corpus ponitur species quantitatis. Sic ergo dicendum est et de vita. Nam vitae nomen sumitur ex quodam exterius apparenti circa rem, quod est movere seipsum, non tamen est impositum hoc nomen ad hoc significandum, sed ad significandam substantiam cui convenit secundum suam naturam movere seipsam, vel agere se quocumque modo ad operationem. Et secundum hoc, vivere nihil aliud est quam esse in tali natura, et vita significat hoc ipsum, sed in abstracto; sicut hoc nomen cursus significat ipsum currere in abstracto. Unde vivum non est praedicatum accidentale, sed substantiale. Quandoque tamen vita sumitur minus proprie pro operationibus vitae, a quibus nomen vitae assumitur; sicut dicit philosophus, IX Ethic., quod *vivere principaliter est sentire vel intelligere*.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29158)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus ibi accipit vivere pro operatione vitae. Vel dicendum est melius, quod sentire et intelligere, et huiusmodi, quandoque sumuntur pro quibusdam operationibus; quandoque autem pro ipso esse sic operantium. Dicitur enim IX Ethic., quod *esse est sentire vel intelligere*, idest habere naturam ad sentiendum vel intelligendum. Et hoc modo distinguit philosophus vivere per illa quatuor. Nam in istis inferioribus quatuor sunt genera viventium. Quorum quaedam habent naturam solum ad utendum alimento, et ad consequentia, quae sunt augmentum et generatio; quaedam ulterius ad sentiendum, ut patet in animalibus immobilibus, sicut sunt ostrea; quaedam vero, cum his, ulterius ad movendum se secundum locum, sicut animalia perfecta, ut quadrupedia et volatilia et huiusmodi; quaedam vero ulterius ad intelligendum, sicut homines.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29159)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod opera vitae dicuntur, quorum principia sunt in operantibus, ut seipsos inducant in tales operationes. Contingit autem aliquorum operum inesse hominibus non solum principia naturalia, ut sunt potentiae naturales; sed etiam quaedam superaddita, ut sunt habitus inclinantes ad quaedam operationum genera quasi per modum naturae, et facientes illas operationes esse delectabiles. Et ex hoc dicitur, quasi per quandam similitudinem, quod illa operatio quae est homini delectabilis, et ad quam inclinatur, et in qua conversatur, et ordinat vitam suam ad ipsam, dicitur vita hominis, unde quidam dicuntur agere vitam luxuriosam, quidam vitam honestam. Et per hunc modum vita contemplativa ab activa distinguitur. Et per hunc etiam modum cognoscere Deum dicitur vita aeterna.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29160)\n\nUnde patet solutio ad tertium.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29161)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deo non conveniat vita. Vivere enim dicuntur aliqua secundum quod movent seipsa, ut dictum est. Sed Deo non competit moveri. Ergo neque vivere.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29162)\n\nPraeterea, in omnibus quae vivunt, est accipere aliquod vivendi principium, unde dicitur in II de anima, quod *anima est viventis corporis causa et principium*. Sed Deus non habet aliquod principium. Ergo sibi non competit vivere.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29163)\n\nPraeterea, principium vitae in rebus viventibus quae apud nos sunt, est anima vegetabilis, quae non est nisi in rebus corporalibus. Ergo rebus incorporalibus non competit vivere.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29164)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo LXXXIII, *cor meum et caro mea exultaverunt in Deum vivum*.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29165)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod vita maxime proprie in Deo est. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum vivere dicantur aliqua secundum quod operantur ex seipsis, et non quasi ab aliis mota; quanto perfectius competit hoc alicui, tanto perfectius in eo invenitur vita. In moventibus autem et motis tria per ordinem inveniuntur. Nam primo, finis movet agentem; agens vero principale est quod per suam formam agit; et hoc interdum agit per aliquod instrumentum, quod non agit ex virtute suae formae, sed ex virtute principalis agentis; cui instrumento competit sola executio actionis. Inveniuntur igitur quaedam, quae movent seipsa, non habito respectu ad formam vel finem, quae inest eis a natura, sed solum quantum ad executionem motus, sed forma per quam agunt, et finis propter quem agunt, determinantur eis a natura. Et huiusmodi sunt plantae, quae secundum formam inditam eis a natura, movent seipsas secundum augmentum et decrementum. Quaedam vero ulterius movent seipsa, non solum habito respectu ad executionem motus, sed etiam quantum ad formam quae est principium motus, quam per se acquirunt. Et huiusmodi sunt animalia, quorum motus principium est forma non a natura indita, sed per sensum accepta. Unde quanto perfectiorem sensum habent, tanto perfectius movent seipsa. Nam ea quae non habent nisi sensum tactus, movent solum seipsa motu dilatationis et constrictionis, ut ostrea, parum excedentia motum plantae. Quae vero habent virtutem sensitivam perfectam, non solum ad cognoscendum coniuncta et tangentia, sed etiam ad cognoscendum distantia, movent seipsa in remotum motu processivo. Sed quamvis huiusmodi animalia formam quae est principium motus, per sensum accipiant, non tamen per seipsa praestituunt sibi finem suae operationis, vel sui motus; sed est eis inditus a natura, cuius instinctu ad aliquid agendum moventur per formam sensu apprehensam. Unde supra talia animalia sunt illa quae movent seipsa, etiam habito respectu ad finem, quem sibi praestituunt. Quod quidem non fit nisi per rationem et intellectum, cuius est cognoscere proportionem finis et eius quod est ad finem, et unum ordinare in alterum. Unde perfectior modus vivendi est eorum quae habent intellectum, haec enim perfectius movent seipsa. Et huius est signum, quod in uno et eodem homine virtus intellectiva movet potentias sensitivas; et potentiae sensitivae per suum imperium movent organa, quae exequuntur motum. Sicut etiam in artibus, videmus quod ars ad quam pertinet usus navis, scilicet ars gubernatoria, praecipit ei quae inducit formam navis, et haec praecipit illi quae habet executionem tantum, in disponendo materiam. Sed quamvis intellectus noster ad aliqua se agat, tamen aliqua sunt ei praestituta a natura; sicut sunt prima principia, circa quae non potest aliter se habere, et ultimus finis, quem non potest non velle. Unde, licet quantum ad aliquid moveat se, tamen oportet quod quantum ad aliqua ab alio moveatur. Illud igitur cuius sua natura est ipsum eius intelligere, et cui id quod naturaliter habet, non determinatur ab alio, hoc est quod obtinet summum gradum vitae. Tale autem est Deus. Unde in Deo maxime est vita. Unde philosophus, in XII Metaphys., ostenso quod Deus sit intelligens, concludit quod habeat vitam perfectissimam et sempiternam, quia intellectus eius est perfectissimus, et semper in actu.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29166)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicitur in IX Metaphys., duplex est actio, una, quae transit in exteriorem materiam, ut calefacere et secare; alia, quae manet in agente, ut intelligere, sentire et velle. Quarum haec est differentia, quia prima actio non est perfectio agentis quod movet, sed ipsius moti; secunda autem actio est perfectio agentis. Unde, quia motus est actus mobilis, secunda actio, inquantum est actus operantis, dicitur motus eius; ex hac similitudine, quod, sicut motus est actus mobilis, ita huiusmodi actio est actus agentis; licet motus sit actus imperfecti, scilicet existentis in potentia, huiusmodi autem actio est actus perfecti, idest existentis in actu, ut dicitur in III de anima. Hoc igitur modo quo intelligere est motus, id quod se intelligit, dicitur se movere. Et per hunc modum etiam Plato posuit quod Deus movet seipsum, non eo modo quo motus est actus imperfecti.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29167)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Deus est ipsum suum esse et suum intelligere, ita est suum vivere. Et propter hoc, sic vivit, quod non habet vivendi principium.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29168)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod vita in istis inferioribus recipitur in natura corruptibili, quae indiget et generatione ad conservationem speciei, et alimento ad conservationem individui. Et propter hoc, in istis inferioribus non invenitur vita sine anima vegetabili. Sed hoc non habet locum in rebus incorruptibilibus.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29169)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnia sint vita in Deo. Dicitur enim Act. XVII, *in ipso vivimus, movemur et sumus*. Sed non omnia in Deo sunt motus. Ergo non omnia in ipso sunt vita.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29170)\n\nPraeterea, omnia sunt in Deo sicut in primo exemplari. Sed exemplata debent conformari exemplari. Cum igitur non omnia vivant in seipsis, videtur quod non omnia in Deo sint vita.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29171)\n\nPraeterea, sicut Augustinus dicit in libro de vera Relig., substantia vivens est melior qualibet substantia non vivente. Si igitur ea quae in seipsis non vivunt, in Deo sunt vita, videtur quod verius sint res in Deo quam in seipsis. Quod tamen videtur esse falsum, cum in seipsis sint in actu, in Deo vero in potentia.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29172)\n\nPraeterea, sicut sciuntur a Deo bona, et ea quae fiunt secundum aliquod tempus; ita mala, et ea quae Deus potest facere, sed nunquam fiunt. Si ergo omnia sunt vita in Deo, inquantum sunt scita ab ipso, videtur quod etiam mala, et quae nunquam fiunt, sunt vita in Deo, inquantum sunt scita ab eo. Quod videtur inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29173)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. I, *quod factum est, in ipso vita erat*. Sed omnia praeter Deum facta sunt. Ergo omnia in Deo sunt vita.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29174)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, vivere Dei est eius intelligere. In Deo autem est idem intellectus et quod intelligitur, et ipsum intelligere eius. Unde quidquid est in Deo ut intellectum, est ipsum vivere vel vita eius. Unde, cum omnia quae facta sunt a Deo, sint in ipso ut intellecta, sequitur quod omnia in ipso sunt ipsa vita divina.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29175)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod creaturae in Deo esse dicuntur dupliciter. Uno modo, inquantum continentur et conservantur virtute divina, sicut dicimus ea esse in nobis, quae sunt in nostra potestate. Et sic creaturae dicuntur esse in Deo, etiam prout sunt in propriis naturis. Et hoc modo intelligendum est verbum apostoli dicentis, *in ipso vivimus, movemur et sumus*, quia et nostrum vivere, et nostrum esse, et nostrum moveri causantur a Deo. Alio modo dicuntur res esse in Deo sicut in cognoscente. Et sic sunt in Deo per proprias rationes, quae non sunt aliud in Deo ab essentia divina. Unde res, prout sic in Deo sunt, sunt essentia divina. Et quia essentia divina est vita, non autem motus, inde est quod res, hoc modo loquendi, in Deo non sunt motus, sed vita.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29176)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod exemplata oportet conformari exemplari secundum rationem formae, non autem secundum modum essendi. Nam alterius modi esse habet quandoque forma in exemplari et in exemplato, sicut forma domus in mente artificis habet esse immateriale et intelligibile, in domo autem quae est extra animam, habet esse materiale et sensibile. Unde et rationes rerum quae in seipsis non vivunt, in mente divina sunt vita, quia in mente divina habent esse divinum.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29177)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si de ratione rerum naturalium non esset materia, sed tantum forma, omnibus modis veriori modo essent res naturales in mente divina per suas ideas, quam in seipsis. Propter quod et Plato posuit quod homo separatus erat verus homo, homo autem materialis est homo per participationem. Sed quia de ratione rerum naturalium est materia, dicendum quod res naturales verius esse habent simpliciter in mente divina, quam in seipsis, quia in mente divina habent esse increatum, in seipsis autem esse creatum. Sed esse hoc, utpote homo vel equus, verius habent in propria natura quam in mente divina, quia ad veritatem hominis pertinet esse materiale, quod non habent in mente divina. Sicut domus nobilius esse habet in mente artificis, quam in materia, sed tamen verius dicitur domus quae est in materia, quam quae est in mente; quia haec est domus in actu, illa autem domus in potentia.\n\n[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29178)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, licet mala sint in Dei scientia, inquantum sub Dei scientia comprehenduntur, non tamen sunt in Deo sicut creata a Deo vel conservata ab ipso, neque sicut habentia rationem in Deo, cognoscuntur enim a Deo per rationes bonorum. Unde non potest dici quod mala sint vita in Deo. Ea vero quae secundum nullum tempus sunt, possunt dici esse vita in Deo, secundum quod vivere nominat intelligere tantum, inquantum intelliguntur a Deo, non autem secundum quod vivere importat principium operationis.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0002","text":"[I q. 18 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0003","text":"Quoniam autem intelligere viventium est, post considerationem de scientia et intellectu divino, considerandum est de vita ipsius. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, quorum sit vivere. Secundo, quid sit vita. Tertio, utrum vita Deo conveniat. Quarto, utrum omnia in Deo sint vita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0005","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnium rerum naturalium sit vivere. Dicit enim philosophus, in VIII Physic., quod motus est ut vita quaedam natura existentibus omnibus. Sed omnes res naturales participant motum. Ergo omnes res naturales participant vitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0007","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0008","text":"Praeterea, plantae dicuntur vivere, inquantum habent in seipsis principium motus augmenti et decrementi. Sed motus localis est perfectior et prior secundum naturam quam motus augmenti et decrementi, ut probatur in VIII Physic. Cum igitur omnia corpora naturalia habeant aliquod principium motus localis, videtur quod omnia corpora naturalia vivant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0009","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0010","text":"Praeterea, inter corpora naturalia imperfectiora sunt elementa. Sed eis attribuitur vita, dicuntur enim aquae vivae. Ergo multo magis alia corpora naturalia vitam habent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0011","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, VI cap. de Div. Nom., quod *plantae secundum ultimam resonantiam vitae habent vivere*, ex quo potest accipi quod ultimum gradum vitae obtinent plantae. Sed corpora inanimata sunt infra plantas. Ergo eorum non est vivere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0013","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ex his quae manifeste vivunt, accipere possumus quorum sit vivere, et quorum non sit vivere. Vivere autem manifeste animalibus convenit, dicitur enim in libro de vegetabilibus, quod *vita in animalibus manifesta est*. Unde secundum illud oportet distinguere viventia a non viventibus secundum quod animalia dicuntur vivere. Hoc autem est in quo primo manifestatur vita, et in quo ultimo remanet. Primo autem dicimus animal vivere, quando incipit ex se motum habere; et tandiu iudicatur animal vivere, quandiu talis motus in eo apparet; quando vero iam ex se non habet aliquem motum, sed movetur tantum ab alio tunc dicitur animal mortuum, per defectum vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","text":"Ex quo patet quod illa proprie sunt viventia, quae seipsa secundum aliquam speciem motus movent; sive accipiatur motus proprie, sicut motus dicitur actus imperfecti, idest existentis in potentia; sive motus accipiatur communiter, prout motus dicitur actus perfecti, prout intelligere et sentire dicitur moveri, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ut sic viventia dicantur quaecumque se agunt ad motum vel operationem aliquam, ea vero in quorum natura non est ut se agant ad aliquem motum vel operationem, viventia dici non possunt, nisi per aliquam similitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0016","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum illud philosophi potest intelligi vel de motu primo, scilicet corporum caelestium; vel de motu communiter. Et utroque modo motus dicitur quasi vita corporum naturalium, per similitudinem; et non per proprietatem. Nam motus caeli est in universo corporalium naturarum, sicut motus cordis in animali, quo conservatur vita. Similiter etiam quicumque motus naturalis hoc modo se habet ad res naturales, ut quaedam similitudo vitalis operationis. Unde, si totum universum corporale esset unum animal, ita quod iste motus esset a movente intrinseco, ut quidam posuerunt, sequeretur quod motus esset vita omnium naturalium corporum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0018","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod corporibus gravibus et levibus non competit moveri, nisi secundum quod sunt extra dispositionem suae naturae, utpote cum sunt extra locum proprium, cum enim sunt in loco proprio et naturali, quiescunt. Sed plantae et aliae res viventes moventur motu vitali, secundum hoc quod sunt in sua dispositione naturali, non autem in accedendo ad eam vel in recedendo ab ea, imo secundum quod recedunt a tali motu, recedunt a naturali dispositione. Et praeterea, corpora gravia et levia moventur a motore extrinseco, vel generante, qui dat formam, vel removente prohibens, ut dicitur in VIII Physic., et ita non movent seipsa, sicut corpora viventia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0020","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aquae vivae dicuntur, quae habent continuum fluxum, aquae enim stantes, quae non continuantur ad principium continue fluens, dicuntur mortuae, ut aquae cisternarum et lacunarum. Et hoc dicitur per similitudinem, inquantum enim videntur se movere, habent similitudinem vitae. Sed tamen non est in eis vera ratio vitae, quia hunc motum non habent a seipsis, sed a causa generante eas; sicut accidit circa motum aliorum gravium et levium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0023","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod vita sit quaedam operatio. Nihil enim dividitur nisi per ea quae sunt sui generis. Sed vivere dividitur per operationes quasdam, ut patet per philosophum in II libro de anima, qui distinguit vivere per quatuor, scilicet alimento uti, sentire, moveri secundum locum, et intelligere. Ergo vita est operatio quaedam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0025","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0026","text":"Praeterea, vita activa dicitur alia esse a contemplativa. Sed contemplativi ab activis non diversificantur nisi secundum operationes quasdam. Ergo vita est quaedam operatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0027","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0028","text":"Praeterea, cognoscere Deum est operatio quaedam. Haec autem est vita, ut patet per illud Ioan. XVII, *haec est autem vita aeterna, ut cognoscant te solum verum Deum*. Ergo vita est operatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0029","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in II de anima, *vivere viventibus est esse*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0031","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex dictis patet, intellectus noster, qui proprie est cognoscitivus quidditatis rei ut proprii obiecti, accipit a sensu, cuius propria obiecta sunt accidentia exteriora. Et inde est quod ex his quae exterius apparent de re, devenimus ad cognoscendam essentiam rei. Et quia sic nominamus aliquid sicut cognoscimus illud, ut ex supradictis patet, inde est quod plerumque a proprietatibus exterioribus imponuntur nomina ad significandas essentias rerum. Unde huiusmodi nomina quandoque accipiuntur proprie pro ipsis essentiis rerum, ad quas significandas principaliter sunt imposita, aliquando autem sumuntur pro proprietatibus a quibus imponuntur, et hoc minus proprie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","text":"Sicut patet quod hoc nomen corpus impositum est ad significandum quoddam genus substantiarum, ex eo quod in eis inveniuntur tres dimensiones, et ideo aliquando ponitur hoc nomen corpus ad significandas tres dimensiones, secundum quod corpus ponitur species quantitatis. Sic ergo dicendum est et de vita. Nam vitae nomen sumitur ex quodam exterius apparenti circa rem, quod est movere seipsum, non tamen est impositum hoc nomen ad hoc significandum, sed ad significandam substantiam cui convenit secundum suam naturam movere seipsam, vel agere se quocumque modo ad operationem. Et secundum hoc, vivere nihil aliud est quam esse in tali natura, et vita significat hoc ipsum, sed in abstracto; sicut hoc nomen cursus significat ipsum currere in abstracto. Unde vivum non est praedicatum accidentale, sed substantiale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034","text":"Quandoque tamen vita sumitur minus proprie pro operationibus vitae, a quibus nomen vitae assumitur; sicut dicit philosophus, IX Ethic., quod *vivere principaliter est sentire vel intelligere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0035","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus ibi accipit vivere pro operatione vitae. Vel dicendum est melius, quod sentire et intelligere, et huiusmodi, quandoque sumuntur pro quibusdam operationibus; quandoque autem pro ipso esse sic operantium. Dicitur enim IX Ethic., quod *esse est sentire vel intelligere*, idest habere naturam ad sentiendum vel intelligendum. Et hoc modo distinguit philosophus vivere per illa quatuor. Nam in istis inferioribus quatuor sunt genera viventium. Quorum quaedam habent naturam solum ad utendum alimento, et ad consequentia, quae sunt augmentum et generatio; quaedam ulterius ad sentiendum, ut patet in animalibus immobilibus, sicut sunt ostrea; quaedam vero, cum his, ulterius ad movendum se secundum locum, sicut animalia perfecta, ut quadrupedia et volatilia et huiusmodi; quaedam vero ulterius ad intelligendum, sicut homines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0037","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod opera vitae dicuntur, quorum principia sunt in operantibus, ut seipsos inducant in tales operationes. Contingit autem aliquorum operum inesse hominibus non solum principia naturalia, ut sunt potentiae naturales; sed etiam quaedam superaddita, ut sunt habitus inclinantes ad quaedam operationum genera quasi per modum naturae, et facientes illas operationes esse delectabiles. Et ex hoc dicitur, quasi per quandam similitudinem, quod illa operatio quae est homini delectabilis, et ad quam inclinatur, et in qua conversatur, et ordinat vitam suam ad ipsam, dicitur vita hominis, unde quidam dicuntur agere vitam luxuriosam, quidam vitam honestam. Et per hunc modum vita contemplativa ab activa distinguitur. Et per hunc etiam modum cognoscere Deum dicitur vita aeterna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0039","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0040","text":"Unde patet solutio ad tertium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0042","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deo non conveniat vita. Vivere enim dicuntur aliqua secundum quod movent seipsa, ut dictum est. Sed Deo non competit moveri. Ergo neque vivere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0044","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0045","text":"Praeterea, in omnibus quae vivunt, est accipere aliquod vivendi principium, unde dicitur in II de anima, quod *anima est viventis corporis causa et principium*. Sed Deus non habet aliquod principium. Ergo sibi non competit vivere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0046","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0047","text":"Praeterea, principium vitae in rebus viventibus quae apud nos sunt, est anima vegetabilis, quae non est nisi in rebus corporalibus. Ergo rebus incorporalibus non competit vivere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0048","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo LXXXIII, *cor meum et caro mea exultaverunt in Deum vivum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0050","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod vita maxime proprie in Deo est. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum vivere dicantur aliqua secundum quod operantur ex seipsis, et non quasi ab aliis mota; quanto perfectius competit hoc alicui, tanto perfectius in eo invenitur vita. In moventibus autem et motis tria per ordinem inveniuntur. Nam primo, finis movet agentem; agens vero principale est quod per suam formam agit; et hoc interdum agit per aliquod instrumentum, quod non agit ex virtute suae formae, sed ex virtute principalis agentis; cui instrumento competit sola executio actionis. Inveniuntur igitur quaedam, quae movent seipsa, non habito respectu ad formam vel finem, quae inest eis a natura, sed solum quantum ad executionem motus, sed forma per quam agunt, et finis propter quem agunt, determinantur eis a natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","text":"Et huiusmodi sunt plantae, quae secundum formam inditam eis a natura, movent seipsas secundum augmentum et decrementum. Quaedam vero ulterius movent seipsa, non solum habito respectu ad executionem motus, sed etiam quantum ad formam quae est principium motus, quam per se acquirunt. Et huiusmodi sunt animalia, quorum motus principium est forma non a natura indita, sed per sensum accepta. Unde quanto perfectiorem sensum habent, tanto perfectius movent seipsa. Nam ea quae non habent nisi sensum tactus, movent solum seipsa motu dilatationis et constrictionis, ut ostrea, parum excedentia motum plantae. Quae vero habent virtutem sensitivam perfectam, non solum ad cognoscendum coniuncta et tangentia, sed etiam ad cognoscendum distantia, movent seipsa in remotum motu processivo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","text":"Sed quamvis huiusmodi animalia formam quae est principium motus, per sensum accipiant, non tamen per seipsa praestituunt sibi finem suae operationis, vel sui motus; sed est eis inditus a natura, cuius instinctu ad aliquid agendum moventur per formam sensu apprehensam. Unde supra talia animalia sunt illa quae movent seipsa, etiam habito respectu ad finem, quem sibi praestituunt. Quod quidem non fit nisi per rationem et intellectum, cuius est cognoscere proportionem finis et eius quod est ad finem, et unum ordinare in alterum. Unde perfectior modus vivendi est eorum quae habent intellectum, haec enim perfectius movent seipsa. Et huius est signum, quod in uno et eodem homine virtus intellectiva movet potentias sensitivas; et potentiae sensitivae per suum imperium movent organa, quae exequuntur motum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","text":"Sicut etiam in artibus, videmus quod ars ad quam pertinet usus navis, scilicet ars gubernatoria, praecipit ei quae inducit formam navis, et haec praecipit illi quae habet executionem tantum, in disponendo materiam. Sed quamvis intellectus noster ad aliqua se agat, tamen aliqua sunt ei praestituta a natura; sicut sunt prima principia, circa quae non potest aliter se habere, et ultimus finis, quem non potest non velle. Unde, licet quantum ad aliquid moveat se, tamen oportet quod quantum ad aliqua ab alio moveatur. Illud igitur cuius sua natura est ipsum eius intelligere, et cui id quod naturaliter habet, non determinatur ab alio, hoc est quod obtinet summum gradum vitae. Tale autem est Deus. Unde in Deo maxime est vita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","text":"Unde philosophus, in XII Metaphys., ostenso quod Deus sit intelligens, concludit quod habeat vitam perfectissimam et sempiternam, quia intellectus eius est perfectissimus, et semper in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0056","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicitur in IX Metaphys., duplex est actio, una, quae transit in exteriorem materiam, ut calefacere et secare; alia, quae manet in agente, ut intelligere, sentire et velle. Quarum haec est differentia, quia prima actio non est perfectio agentis quod movet, sed ipsius moti; secunda autem actio est perfectio agentis. Unde, quia motus est actus mobilis, secunda actio, inquantum est actus operantis, dicitur motus eius; ex hac similitudine, quod, sicut motus est actus mobilis, ita huiusmodi actio est actus agentis; licet motus sit actus imperfecti, scilicet existentis in potentia, huiusmodi autem actio est actus perfecti, idest existentis in actu, ut dicitur in III de anima. Hoc igitur modo quo intelligere est motus, id quod se intelligit, dicitur se movere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0058","text":"Et per hunc modum etiam Plato posuit quod Deus movet seipsum, non eo modo quo motus est actus imperfecti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0059","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Deus est ipsum suum esse et suum intelligere, ita est suum vivere. Et propter hoc, sic vivit, quod non habet vivendi principium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0061","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod vita in istis inferioribus recipitur in natura corruptibili, quae indiget et generatione ad conservationem speciei, et alimento ad conservationem individui. Et propter hoc, in istis inferioribus non invenitur vita sine anima vegetabili. Sed hoc non habet locum in rebus incorruptibilibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0063","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0064","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0065","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnia sint vita in Deo. Dicitur enim Act. XVII, *in ipso vivimus, movemur et sumus*. Sed non omnia in Deo sunt motus. Ergo non omnia in ipso sunt vita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0066","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0067","text":"Praeterea, omnia sunt in Deo sicut in primo exemplari. Sed exemplata debent conformari exemplari. Cum igitur non omnia vivant in seipsis, videtur quod non omnia in Deo sint vita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0068","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0069","text":"Praeterea, sicut Augustinus dicit in libro de vera Relig., substantia vivens est melior qualibet substantia non vivente. Si igitur ea quae in seipsis non vivunt, in Deo sunt vita, videtur quod verius sint res in Deo quam in seipsis. Quod tamen videtur esse falsum, cum in seipsis sint in actu, in Deo vero in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0070","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0071","text":"Praeterea, sicut sciuntur a Deo bona, et ea quae fiunt secundum aliquod tempus; ita mala, et ea quae Deus potest facere, sed nunquam fiunt. Si ergo omnia sunt vita in Deo, inquantum sunt scita ab ipso, videtur quod etiam mala, et quae nunquam fiunt, sunt vita in Deo, inquantum sunt scita ab eo. Quod videtur inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0072","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. I, *quod factum est, in ipso vita erat*. Sed omnia praeter Deum facta sunt. Ergo omnia in Deo sunt vita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0074","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, vivere Dei est eius intelligere. In Deo autem est idem intellectus et quod intelligitur, et ipsum intelligere eius. Unde quidquid est in Deo ut intellectum, est ipsum vivere vel vita eius. Unde, cum omnia quae facta sunt a Deo, sint in ipso ut intellecta, sequitur quod omnia in ipso sunt ipsa vita divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0076","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod creaturae in Deo esse dicuntur dupliciter. Uno modo, inquantum continentur et conservantur virtute divina, sicut dicimus ea esse in nobis, quae sunt in nostra potestate. Et sic creaturae dicuntur esse in Deo, etiam prout sunt in propriis naturis. Et hoc modo intelligendum est verbum apostoli dicentis, *in ipso vivimus, movemur et sumus*, quia et nostrum vivere, et nostrum esse, et nostrum moveri causantur a Deo. Alio modo dicuntur res esse in Deo sicut in cognoscente. Et sic sunt in Deo per proprias rationes, quae non sunt aliud in Deo ab essentia divina. Unde res, prout sic in Deo sunt, sunt essentia divina. Et quia essentia divina est vita, non autem motus, inde est quod res, hoc modo loquendi, in Deo non sunt motus, sed vita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0078","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod exemplata oportet conformari exemplari secundum rationem formae, non autem secundum modum essendi. Nam alterius modi esse habet quandoque forma in exemplari et in exemplato, sicut forma domus in mente artificis habet esse immateriale et intelligibile, in domo autem quae est extra animam, habet esse materiale et sensibile. Unde et rationes rerum quae in seipsis non vivunt, in mente divina sunt vita, quia in mente divina habent esse divinum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0080","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si de ratione rerum naturalium non esset materia, sed tantum forma, omnibus modis veriori modo essent res naturales in mente divina per suas ideas, quam in seipsis. Propter quod et Plato posuit quod homo separatus erat verus homo, homo autem materialis est homo per participationem. Sed quia de ratione rerum naturalium est materia, dicendum quod res naturales verius esse habent simpliciter in mente divina, quam in seipsis, quia in mente divina habent esse increatum, in seipsis autem esse creatum. Sed esse hoc, utpote homo vel equus, verius habent in propria natura quam in mente divina, quia ad veritatem hominis pertinet esse materiale, quod non habent in mente divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0082","text":"Sicut domus nobilius esse habet in mente artificis, quam in materia, sed tamen verius dicitur domus quae est in materia, quam quae est in mente; quia haec est domus in actu, illa autem domus in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0083","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, licet mala sint in Dei scientia, inquantum sub Dei scientia comprehenduntur, non tamen sunt in Deo sicut creata a Deo vel conservata ab ipso, neque sicut habentia rationem in Deo, cognoscuntur enim a Deo per rationes bonorum. Unde non potest dici quod mala sint vita in Deo. Ea vero quae secundum nullum tempus sunt, possunt dici esse vita in Deo, secundum quod vivere nominat intelligere tantum, inquantum intelliguntur a Deo, non autem secundum quod vivere importat principium operationis.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"26d5e4ab77c0cb5a3f8498a32a2273139a332ea537635e334ef377fb155fd519","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T135711090191Z_0f9638b6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 18","source_sha256":"26d5e4ab77c0cb5a3f8498a32a2273139a332ea537635e334ef377fb155fd519","sha256":"86abc6540b80555e9576f7fca0967cb6cfcbc5efb420f4875f5cb2f1be5bc13d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:57:11.090234+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0002","text":"[I q. 18 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0003","text":"Now since to understand belongs to living things, after the consideration of the divine knowledge and intellect, we must consider His life. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, to what things does living belong? Second, what is life? Third, whether life befits God. Fourth, whether all things in God are life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0005","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that living belongs to all natural things. For the Philosopher says, in Physics VIII, that motion is, as it were, a certain life for all things existing by nature. But all natural things participate in motion. Therefore all natural things participate in life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0007","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0008","text":"Moreover, plants are said to live insofar as they have in themselves a principle of the motion of growth and decrease. But local motion is more perfect and naturally prior to the motion of growth and decrease, as is proved in Physics VIII. Therefore, since all natural bodies have some principle of local motion, it seems that all natural bodies live.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0009","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0010","text":"Moreover, among natural bodies the elements are more imperfect. But life is attributed to them, for waters are called living. Therefore, much more do the other natural bodies have life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0011","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter 6 of *The Divine Names*, that *plants have life according to the lowest resonance of life*, from which it may be taken that plants attain the lowest grade of life. But inanimate bodies are below plants. Therefore living does not belong to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0013","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","text":"I answer that from those things which manifestly live, we can gather to what things living belongs, and to what things it does not belong. Now living manifestly befits animals; for it is said in the book *On Plants* that *life is manifest in animals*. Hence, according to this, living things must be distinguished from non-living things in accordance with the way animals are said to live. Now this is that in which life is first manifested and in which it remains last. But we first say that an animal lives when it begins to have motion from itself; and an animal is judged to live as long as such motion appears in it. But when it no longer has any motion from itself, but is moved only by another, then the animal is said to be dead, through defect of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","text":"From this it is clear that those things properly are living which move themselves according to some kind of motion: whether motion be taken properly, as motion is called the act of the imperfect, that is, of that which exists in potency; or whether motion be taken commonly, insofar as motion is called the act of the perfect, inasmuch as understanding and sensing are said to be moved, as is said in *On the Soul* III. Thus living things are said to be whatever things move themselves toward some motion or operation; but those in whose nature it is not to move themselves toward any motion or operation cannot be called living, except by some likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0016","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that statement of the Philosopher can be understood either of the first motion, namely, that of the heavenly bodies; or of motion taken commonly. And in both ways motion is called, as it were, the life of natural bodies, by likeness and not properly. For the motion of the heavens is among the universe of corporeal natures as the motion of the heart is in an animal, by which life is preserved. Likewise, every natural motion is related in this way to natural things, as a certain likeness of vital operation. Hence, if the whole corporeal universe were one animal, so that this motion were from an intrinsic mover, as some have posited, it would follow that motion was the life of all natural bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0018","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that it belongs to heavy and light bodies to be moved only insofar as they are outside the disposition of their nature, as when they are outside their proper place; for when they are in their proper and natural place, they are at rest. But plants and other living things are moved by vital motion insofar as they are in their natural disposition, and not in approaching it or withdrawing from it; indeed, insofar as they withdraw from such motion, they withdraw from their natural disposition. Moreover, heavy and light bodies are moved by an extrinsic mover, either by that which generates, which gives form, or by that which removes an impediment, as is said in Physics VIII; and thus they do not move themselves, as living bodies do.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0020","text":"[I q. 18 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that living waters are called those which have a continuous flow; for standing waters, which are not connected with a continuously flowing source, are called dead, as the waters of cisterns and pools. And this is said by likeness: for insofar as they seem to move themselves, they have a likeness of life. Yet the true notion of life is not in them, because they do not have this motion from themselves, but from the cause generating them; as happens in regard to the motion of other heavy and light things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0023","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that life is a certain operation. For nothing is divided except through things which are of its genus. But living is divided through certain operations, as is clear from the Philosopher in book II of *On the Soul*, who distinguishes living by four things, namely, using nourishment, sensing, moving according to place, and understanding. Therefore life is a certain operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0025","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0026","text":"Moreover, the active life is said to be other than the contemplative life. But contemplatives are differentiated from those who are active only according to certain operations. Therefore, life is an operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0027","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0028","text":"Moreover, to know God is a certain operation. But this is life, as is evident from John XVII: *Now this is eternal life, that they may know thee, the only true God*. Therefore, life is an operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0029","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0030","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* II, *for living things, to live is to be*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0031","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","text":"I answer that, as is clear from what has been said, our intellect, which is properly cognizant of the quiddity of a thing as its proper object, receives from sense, whose proper objects are external accidents. And hence it is that from those things which appear externally concerning a thing, we arrive at knowing the essence of the thing. And because we name something as we know it, as is clear from what was said above, it follows that names are for the most part imposed from exterior properties in order to signify the essences of things. Hence such names are sometimes taken properly for the very essences of things, for signifying which they were principally imposed; but sometimes they are taken for the properties from which they are imposed, and this less properly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","text":"As is clear that this name “body” was imposed to signify a certain genus of substances, from the fact that three dimensions are found in them; and therefore this name “body” is sometimes used to signify the three dimensions, according as body is posited as a species of quantity. Thus, therefore, it must be said also of life. For the name of life is taken from something appearing externally about a thing, namely, that it moves itself; nevertheless, this name was not imposed in order to signify this, but in order to signify a substance to which it belongs according to its nature to move itself, or in whatever manner to act itself toward operation. And according to this, to live is nothing other than to be in such a nature, and life signifies this very thing, but abstractly; just as the name “running” signifies running itself in the abstract. Hence “living” is not an accidental predicate, but a substantial one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034","text":"Yet sometimes life is taken less properly for the operations of life, from which the name of life is assumed; as the Philosopher says in *Ethics* IX, that *to live is principally to sense or to understand*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0035","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher there takes living for an operation of life. Or, better, it must be said that sensing and understanding, and things of this sort, are sometimes taken for certain operations, but sometimes for the very being of those so operating. For it is said in *Ethics* IX that *to be is to sense or to understand*, that is, to have a nature for sensing or understanding. And in this way the Philosopher distinguishes living by those four. For among these lower things there are four genera of living things. Some of these have a nature only for using nourishment and for the consequences thereof, which are growth and generation; some, further, for sensing, as is evident in immobile animals, such as oysters; some, with these, further for moving themselves according to place, such as perfect animals, as quadrupeds and birds and the like; and some, further, for understanding, such as human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0037","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0038","text":"To the second it must be said that works are called works of life whose principles are in those operating, so that they bring themselves into such operations. Now it happens that for certain works there are in human beings not only natural principles, such as natural powers, but also certain superadded principles, such as habits inclining toward certain kinds of operations as though by way of nature, and making those operations delightful. And from this it is said, as it were by a certain likeness, that that operation which is delightful to a human being, and toward which he is inclined, and in which he is occupied, and to which he orders his life, is called the life of that human being; whence some are said to lead a luxurious life, others an honorable life. And in this way the contemplative life is distinguished from the active life. And in this way also knowing God is called eternal life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0039","text":"[I q. 18 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0040","text":"Hence the solution to the third is evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0042","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that life does not belong to God. For certain things are said to live insofar as they move themselves, as has been said. But to be moved does not belong to God. Therefore neither does to live.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0044","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0045","text":"Moreover, in all things that live, some principle of living is to be found; whence it is said in *On the Soul* II that *the soul is the cause and principle of a living body*. But God has no principle. Therefore, to live does not belong to him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0046","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0047","text":"Moreover, the principle of life in living things that are among us is the vegetative soul, which exists only in corporeal things. Therefore, to live does not belong to incorporeal things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0048","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0049","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm LXXXIII, *my heart and my flesh have exulted in the living God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0050","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","text":"I answer that it must be said that life is most properly in God. For the elucidation of this, it must be considered that, since certain things are said to live insofar as they operate of themselves, and not as though moved by others, the more perfectly this belongs to something, the more perfectly life is found in it. Now, in movers and things moved, three things are found in order. For first, the end moves the agent; but the principal agent is that which acts through its own form; and this sometimes acts through some instrument, which acts not by the power of its own form, but by the power of the principal agent; to which instrument belongs only the execution of the action. Therefore, certain things are found which move themselves, not with respect to the form or end that is in them by nature, but only as to the execution of motion; whereas the form through which they act, and the end for which they act, are determined for them by nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","text":"And of this sort are plants, which, according to the form implanted in them by nature, move themselves in respect of growth and diminution. But certain things further move themselves, not only with respect to the execution of motion, but also as to the form which is the principle of motion, which they acquire through themselves. And of this sort are animals, whose principle of motion is a form not implanted by nature, but received through sense. Hence, the more perfect a sense they have, the more perfectly they move themselves. For those which have only the sense of touch move themselves only by a motion of expansion and contraction, as oysters do, scarcely exceeding the motion of a plant. But those which have a perfect sensitive power, not only for knowing things joined and touching, but also for knowing things at a distance, move themselves to a distance by progressive motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","text":"But although animals of this sort receive through sense the form which is the principle of motion, nevertheless they do not of themselves set before themselves the end of their operation or of their motion; rather, it is implanted in them by nature, by whose instinct they are moved to do something through the form apprehended by sense. Hence above such animals are those which move themselves even with respect to an end, which they set before themselves. This indeed does not occur except through reason and intellect, whose role it is to know the proportion of the end and that which is for the end, and to order one to the other. Hence the more perfect mode of living belongs to those that have intellect, for these move themselves more perfectly. And a sign of this is that, in one and the same human being, the intellective power moves the sensitive powers; and the sensitive powers, by their command, move the organs, which execute the motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","text":"Just as also in the arts, we see that the art to which the use of a ship belongs—namely, the art of navigation—commands the art which introduces the form of the ship, and this commands that which has execution alone, in disposing the material. But although our intellect acts upon some things, nevertheless some things are set before it by nature, such as the first principles, with respect to which it cannot be otherwise disposed, and the ultimate end, which it cannot but will. Hence, although it moves itself in some respect, nevertheless it must be moved by another in some respects. Therefore, that whose nature is its very understanding, and to which what it naturally possesses is not determined by another, is that which holds the highest grade of life. But such is God. Hence life is most fully in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","text":"Hence the Philosopher, in XII *Metaphysics*, having shown that God is intelligent, concludes that He has most perfect and everlasting life, because His intellect is most perfect and always in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0056","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as is said in IX *Metaphysics*, there is a twofold action: one which passes over into external matter, such as heating and cutting; another which remains in the agent, such as understanding, sensing, and willing. The difference between these is that the first action is not the perfection of the acting mover, but of the thing moved itself; whereas the second action is the perfection of the agent. Hence, because motion is the act of the movable thing, the second action, insofar as it is the act of one operating, is called its motion, by this likeness: that just as motion is the act of the movable thing, so an action of this sort is the act of the agent. Although motion is the act of something imperfect, namely, existing in potency, such an action is the act of something perfect, that is, existing in act, as is said in III *On the Soul*. Therefore, in the mode in which understanding is motion, that which understands itself is said to move itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0058","text":"And in this manner Plato also held that God moves Himself, not in the manner in which motion is the act of something imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0059","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as God is His own being and His own understanding, so He is His own living. And on this account, He so lives that He does not have a principle of living.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0061","text":"[I q. 18 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0062","text":"To the third, it must be said that life in these lower things is received in a corruptible nature, which needs both generation for the preservation of the species and nourishment for the preservation of the individual. And on this account, in these lower things life is not found without a vegetative soul. But this has no place in incorruptible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0063","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0064","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0065","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that not all things are life in God. For it is said in Acts XVII, *in Him we live, move, and are*. But not all things in God are motion. Therefore, not all things in Him are life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0066","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0067","text":"Further, all things are in God as in their first exemplar. But things exemplified ought to be conformed to the exemplar. Therefore, since not all things live in themselves, it seems that not all things in God are life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0068","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0069","text":"Further, as Augustine says in the book *On True Religion*, a living substance is better than any non-living substance. Therefore, if those things which do not live in themselves are life in God, it seems that they are more truly things in God than in themselves. Yet this seems to be false, since in themselves they are in act, whereas in God they are in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0070","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0071","text":"Further, just as good things, and those things which come to be at some time, are known by God, so too are evils, and those things which God can do but are never done. Therefore, if all things are life in God insofar as they are known by Him, it seems that evils too, and things which are never done, are life in God insofar as they are known by Him. This seems unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0072","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0073","text":"On the contrary, it is said in John 1, *what was made, in him was life*. But all things besides God were made. Therefore all things are life in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0074","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","text":"I answer that, as has been said, God's living is his understanding. Now in God, the intellect, that which is understood, and his very understanding are the same. Hence whatever is in God as understood is his very living or life. Therefore, since all things that were made by God are in him as understood, it follows that all things in him are the divine life itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0076","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that creatures are said to be in God in two ways. In one way, insofar as they are contained and preserved by divine power, just as we say that those things are in us which are in our power. And thus creatures are said to be in God, even insofar as they are in their own natures. And in this way the word of the Apostle saying, *in him we live, and move, and are*, must be understood, because both our living, our being, and our moving are caused by God. In another way, things are said to be in God as in one knowing. And thus they are in God through their proper intelligible accounts, which are not other in God than the divine essence. Hence things, insofar as they are thus in God, are the divine essence. And because the divine essence is life, but not motion, it follows that things, in this manner of speaking, are not motion in God, but life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0078","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","text":"To the second, it must be said that things exemplified must be conformed to the exemplar according to the account of form, but not according to the mode of being. For a form sometimes has a different mode of being in the exemplar and in the thing exemplified, just as the form of a house in the mind of the craftsman has immaterial and intelligible being, whereas in the house which is outside the soul it has material and sensible being. Hence also the intelligible accounts of things which do not live in themselves are life in the divine mind, because in the divine mind they have divine being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0080","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if matter did not belong to the account of natural things, but form alone, natural things would in every way exist more truly in the divine mind through their ideas than in themselves. For this reason Plato also held that separated man was the true man, while material man is man by participation. But because matter belongs to the account of natural things, it must be said that natural things have being more truly simply speaking in the divine mind than in themselves, because in the divine mind they have uncreated being, whereas in themselves they have created being. But this being, as man or horse, they have more truly in their proper nature than in the divine mind, because material being belongs to the truth of man, which they do not have in the divine mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0082","text":"Just as a house has being more nobly in the mind of the craftsman than in matter, yet the house which is in matter is said more truly to be a house than that which is in the mind; because the former is a house in act, whereas the latter is a house in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0083","text":"[I q. 18 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, although evils are in God's knowledge, insofar as they are comprehended under God's knowledge, nevertheless they are not in God as created by God or preserved by him, nor as having an intelligible account in God; for they are known by God through the intelligible accounts of goods. Hence it cannot be said that evils are life in God. But those things which exist at no time can be said to be life in God, insofar as living signifies understanding alone, insofar as they are understood by God, but not insofar as living implies a principle of operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084"]}],"source_digest":"38b917895b0bd080fbc8dc99e136f46a8d8d44dfaf5ab47dc3afa3f0ae5c6617","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 18"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11","title":"First Part · Question 19","order":20,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_11","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 19 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29179)\n\nPost considerationem eorum quae ad divinam scientiam pertinent, considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad voluntatem divinam, ut sit prima consideratio de ipsa Dei voluntate; secunda, de his quae ad voluntatem absolute pertinent; tertia, de his quae ad intellectum in ordine ad voluntatem pertinent. Circa ipsam autem voluntatem quaeruntur duodecim. Primo, utrum in Deo sit voluntas. Secundo, utrum Deus velit alia a se. Tertio, utrum quidquid Deus vult, ex necessitate velit. Quarto, utrum voluntas Dei sit causa rerum. Quinto, utrum voluntatis divinae sit assignare aliquam causam. Sexto, utrum voluntas divina semper impleatur. Septimo, utrum voluntas Dei sit mutabilis. Octavo, utrum voluntas Dei necessitatem rebus volitis imponat. Nono, utrum in Deo sit voluntas malorum. Decimo, utrum Deus habeat liberum arbitrium. Undecimo, utrum sit distinguenda in Deo voluntas signi. Duodecimo, utrum convenienter circa divinam voluntatem ponantur quinque signa.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29180)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit voluntas. Obiectum enim voluntatis est finis et bonum. Sed Dei non est assignare aliquem finem. Ergo voluntas non est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29181)\n\nPraeterea, voluntas est appetitus quidam. Appetitus autem, cum sit rei non habitae, imperfectionem designat, quae Deo non competit. Ergo voluntas non est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29182)\n\nPraeterea, secundum philosophum, in III de anima, voluntas est movens motum. Sed Deus est primum movens immobile. Ut probatur VIII Physic. Ergo in Deo non est voluntas.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29183)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit apostolus, Rom. XII, *ut probetis quae sit voluntas Dei*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29184)\n\nRespondeo dicendum in Deo voluntatem esse, sicut et in eo est intellectus, voluntas enim intellectum consequitur. Sicut enim res naturalis habet esse in actu per suam formam, ita intellectus intelligens actu per suam formam intelligibilem. Quaelibet autem res ad suam formam naturalem hanc habet habitudinem, ut quando non habet ipsam, tendat in eam; et quando habet ipsam, quiescat in ea. Et idem est de qualibet perfectione naturali, quod est bonum naturae. Et haec habitudo ad bonum, in rebus carentibus cognitione, vocatur appetitus naturalis. Unde et natura intellectualis ad bonum apprehensum per formam intelligibilem, similem habitudinem habet, ut scilicet, cum habet ipsum, quiescat in illo; cum vero non habet, quaerat ipsum. Et utrumque pertinet ad voluntatem. Unde in quolibet habente intellectum, est voluntas; sicut in quolibet habente sensum, est appetitus animalis. Et sic oportet in Deo esse voluntatem, cum sit in eo intellectus. Et sicut suum intelligere est suum esse, ita suum velle.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29185)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet nihil aliud a Deo sit finis Dei, tamen ipsemet est finis respectu omnium quae ab eo fiunt. Et hoc per suam essentiam, cum per suam essentiam sit bonus, ut supra ostensum est, finis enim habet rationem boni.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29186)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod voluntas in nobis pertinet ad appetitivam partem, quae licet ab appetendo nominetur, non tamen hunc solum habet actum, ut appetat quae non habet; sed etiam ut amet quod habet, et delectetur in illo. Et quantum ad hoc voluntas in Deo ponitur; quae semper habet bonum quod est eius obiectum, cum sit indifferens ab eo secundum essentiam, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29187)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod voluntas cuius obiectum principale est bonum quod est extra voluntatem, oportet quod sit mota ab aliquo. Sed obiectum divinae voluntatis est bonitas sua, quae est eius essentia. Unde, cum voluntas Dei sit eius essentia, non movetur ab alio a se, sed a se tantum, eo modo loquendi quo intelligere et velle dicitur motus. Et secundum hoc Plato dixit quod primum movens movet seipsum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29188)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non velit alia a se. Velle enim divinum est eius esse. Sed Deus non est aliud a se. Ergo non vult aliud a se.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29189)\n\nPraeterea, volitum movet voluntatem, sicut appetibile appetitum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Si igitur Deus velit aliquid aliud a se, movebitur eius voluntas ab aliquo alio, quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29190)\n\nPraeterea, cuicumque voluntati sufficit aliquod volitum, nihil quaerit extra illud. Sed Deo sufficit sua bonitas, et voluntas eius ex ea satiatur. Ergo Deus non vult aliquid aliud a se.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29191)\n\nPraeterea, actus voluntatis multiplicatur secundum volita. Si igitur Deus velit se et alia a se, sequitur quod actus voluntatis eius sit multiplex, et per consequens eius esse, quod est eius velle. Hoc autem est impossibile. Non ergo vult alia a se.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29192)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit, I Thess. IV, *haec est voluntas Dei, sanctificatio vestra*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29193)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus non solum se vult, sed etiam alia a se. Quod apparet a simili prius introducto. Res enim naturalis non solum habet naturalem inclinationem respectu proprii boni, ut acquirat ipsum cum non habet, vel ut quiescat in illo cum habet; sed etiam ut proprium bonum in alia diffundat, secundum quod possibile est. Unde videmus quod omne agens, inquantum est actu et perfectum, facit sibi simile. Unde et hoc pertinet ad rationem voluntatis, ut bonum quod quis habet, aliis communicet, secundum quod possibile est. Et hoc praecipue pertinet ad voluntatem divinam, a qua, per quandam similitudinem, derivatur omnis perfectio. Unde, si res naturales, inquantum perfectae sunt, suum bonum aliis communicant, multo magis pertinet ad voluntatem divinam, ut bonum suum aliis per similitudinem communicet, secundum quod possibile est. Sic igitur vult et se esse, et alia. Sed se ut finem, alia vero ut ad finem, inquantum condecet divinam bonitatem etiam alia ipsam participare.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29194)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet divinum velle sit eius esse secundum rem, tamen differt ratione, secundum diversum modum intelligendi et significandi, ut ex superioribus patet. In hoc enim quod dico Deum esse, non importatur habitudo ad aliquid, sicut in hoc quod dico Deum velle. Et ideo, licet non sit aliquid aliud a se, vult tamen aliquid aliud a se.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29195)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in his quae volumus propter finem, tota ratio movendi est finis, et hoc est quod movet voluntatem. Et hoc maxime apparet in his quae volumus tantum propter finem. Qui enim vult sumere potionem amaram, nihil in ea vult nisi sanitatem, et hoc solum est quod movet eius voluntatem. Secus autem est in eo qui sumit potionem dulcem, quam non solum propter sanitatem, sed etiam propter se aliquis velle potest. Unde, cum Deus alia a se non velit nisi propter finem qui est sua bonitas, ut dictum est, non sequitur quod aliquid aliud moveat voluntatem eius nisi bonitas sua. Et sic, sicut alia a se intelligit intelligendo essentiam suam, ita alia a se vult, volendo bonitatem suam.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29196)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ex hoc quod voluntati divinae sufficit sua bonitas, non sequitur quod nihil aliud velit, sed quod nihil aliud vult nisi ratione suae bonitatis. Sicut etiam intellectus divinus, licet sit perfectus ex hoc ipso quod essentiam divinam cognoscit, tamen in ea cognoscit alia.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29197)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut intelligere divinum est unum, quia multa non videt nisi in uno; ita velle divinum est unum et simplex, quia multa non vult nisi per unum, quod est bonitas sua.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29198)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod quidquid Deus vult, ex necessitate velit. Omne enim aeternum est necessarium. Sed quidquid Deus vult, ab aeterno vult, alias, voluntas eius esset mutabilis. Ergo quidquid vult, ex necessitate vult.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29199)\n\nPraeterea, Deus vult alia a se, inquantum vult bonitatem suam. Sed Deus bonitatem suam ex necessitate vult. Ergo alia a se ex necessitate vult.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29200)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid est Deo naturale, est necessarium, quia Deus est per se necesse esse, et principium omnis necessitatis, ut supra ostensum est. Sed naturale est ei velle quidquid vult, quia in Deo nihil potest esse praeter naturam, ut dicitur in V Metaphys. Ergo quidquid vult, ex necessitate vult.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29201)\n\nPraeterea, non necesse esse, et possibile non esse, aequipollent. Si igitur non necesse est Deum velle aliquid eorum quae vult, possibile est eum non velle illud; et possibile est eum velle illud quod non vult. Ergo voluntas divina est contingens ad utrumlibet. Et sic imperfecta, quia omne contingens est imperfectum et mutabile.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29202)\n\nPraeterea, ab eo quod est ad utrumlibet, non sequitur aliqua actio, nisi ab aliquo alio inclinetur ad unum, ut dicit Commentator, in II Physic. Si ergo voluntas Dei in aliquibus se habet ad utrumlibet, sequitur quod ab aliquo alio determinetur ad effectum. Et sic habet aliquam causam priorem.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29203)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid Deus scit, ex necessitate scit. Sed sicut scientia divina est eius essentia, ita voluntas divina. Ergo quidquid Deus vult, ex necessitate vult.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29204)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit apostolus, Ephes. I, *qui operatur omnia secundum consilium voluntatis suae*. Quod autem operamur ex consilio voluntatis, non ex necessitate volumus. Non ergo quidquid Deus vult, ex necessitate vult.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29205)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necessarium dicitur aliquid dupliciter, scilicet absolute, et ex suppositione. Necessarium absolute iudicatur aliquid ex habitudine terminorum, utpote quia praedicatum est in definitione subiecti, sicut necessarium est hominem esse animal; vel quia subiectum est de ratione praedicati, sicut hoc est necessarium, numerum esse parem vel imparem. Sic autem non est necessarium Socratem sedere. Unde non est necessarium absolute, sed potest dici necessarium ex suppositione, supposito enim quod sedeat, necesse est eum sedere dum sedet. Circa divina igitur volita hoc considerandum est, quod aliquid Deum velle est necessarium absolute, non tamen hoc est verum de omnibus quae vult. Voluntas enim divina necessariam habitudinem habet ad bonitatem suam, quae est proprium eius obiectum. Unde bonitatem suam esse Deus ex necessitate vult; sicut et voluntas nostra ex necessitate vult beatitudinem. Sicut et quaelibet alia potentia necessariam habitudinem habet ad proprium et principale obiectum, ut visus ad colorem; quia de sui ratione est, ut in illud tendat. Alia autem a se Deus vult, inquantum ordinantur ad suam bonitatem ut in finem. Ea autem quae sunt ad finem, non ex necessitate volumus volentes finem, nisi sint talia, sine quibus finis esse non potest, sicut volumus cibum, volentes conservationem vitae; et navem, volentes transfretare. Non sic autem ex necessitate volumus ea sine quibus finis esse potest, sicut equum ad ambulandum, quia sine hoc possumus ire; et eadem ratio est in aliis. Unde, cum bonitas Dei sit perfecta, et esse possit sine aliis, cum nihil ei perfectionis ex aliis accrescat; sequitur quod alia a se eum velle, non sit necessarium absolute. Et tamen necessarium est ex suppositione, supposito enim quod velit, non potest non velle, quia non potest voluntas eius mutari.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29206)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ex hoc quod Deus ab aeterno vult aliquid, non sequitur quod necesse est eum illud velle, nisi ex suppositione.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29207)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet Deus ex necessitate velit bonitatem suam, non tamen ex necessitate vult ea quae vult propter bonitatem suam, quia bonitas eius potest esse sine aliis.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29208)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non est naturale Deo velle aliquid aliorum, quae non ex necessitate vult. Neque tamen innaturale, aut contra naturam, sed est voluntarium.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29209)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod aliquando aliqua causa necessaria habet non necessariam habitudinem ad aliquem effectum, quod est propter defectum effectus, et non propter defectum causae. Sicut virtus solis habet non necessariam habitudinem ad aliquid eorum quae contingenter hic eveniunt, non propter defectum virtutis solaris, sed propter defectum effectus non necessario ex causa provenientis. Et similiter, quod Deus non ex necessitate velit aliquid eorum quae vult, non accidit ex defectu voluntatis divinae, sed ex defectu qui competit volito secundum suam rationem, quia scilicet est tale, ut sine eo esse possit perfecta bonitas Dei. Qui quidem defectus consequitur omne bonum creatum.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29210)\n\nAd quintum ergo dicendum quod causa quae est ex se contingens, oportet quod determinetur ab aliquo exteriori ad effectum. Sed voluntas divina, quae ex se necessitatem habet, determinat seipsam ad volitum, ad quod habet habitudinem non necessariam.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29211)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod, sicut divinum esse in se est necessarium, ita et divinum velle et divinum scire, sed divinum scire habet necessariam habitudinem ad scita, non autem divinum velle ad volita. Quod ideo est, quia scientia habetur de rebus, secundum quod sunt in sciente, voluntas autem comparatur ad res, secundum quod sunt in seipsis. Quia igitur omnia alia habent necessarium esse secundum quod sunt in Deo; non autem secundum quod sunt in seipsis, habent necessitatem absolutam ita quod sint per seipsa necessaria; propter hoc Deus quaecumque scit, ex necessitate scit, non autem quaecumque vult, ex necessitate vult.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29212)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei non sit causa rerum. Dicit enim Dionysius, cap. IV de Div. Nom., *sicut noster sol, non ratiocinans aut praeeligens, sed per ipsum esse illuminat omnia participare lumen ipsius valentia; ita et bonum divinum per ipsam essentiam omnibus existentibus immittit bonitatis suae radios*. Sed omne quod agit per voluntatem, agit ut ratiocinans et praeeligens. Ergo Deus non agit per voluntatem. Ergo voluntas Dei non est causa rerum.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29213)\n\nPraeterea, id quod est per essentiam, est primum in quolibet ordine sicut in ordine ignitorum est primum, quod est ignis per essentiam sed Deus est primum agens. Ergo est agens per essentiam suam, quae est natura eius. Agit igitur per naturam, et non per voluntatem. Voluntas igitur divina non est causa rerum.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29214)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid est causa alicuius per hoc quod est tale, est causa per naturam, et non per voluntatem, ignis enim causa est calefactionis, quia est calidus; sed artifex est causa domus, quia vult eam facere. Sed Augustinus dicit, in I de Doct. Christ., quod *quia Deus bonus est, sumus*. Ergo Deus per suam naturam est causa rerum, et non per voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29215)\n\nPraeterea, unius rei una est causa. Sed rerum creatarum est causa scientia Dei, ut supra dictum est. Ergo voluntas Dei non debet poni causa rerum.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29216)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XI, *quomodo posset aliquid permanere, nisi tu voluisses?*\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29217)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere voluntatem Dei esse causam rerum, et Deum agere per voluntatem, non per necessitatem naturae, ut quidam existimaverunt. Quod quidem apparere potest tripliciter. Primo quidem, ex ipso ordine causarum agentium. Cum enim propter finem agat et intellectus et natura, ut probatur in II Physic., necesse est ut agenti per naturam praedeterminetur finis, et media necessaria ad finem, ab aliquo superiori intellectu; sicut sagittae praedeterminatur finis et certus modus a sagittante. Unde necesse est quod agens per intellectum et voluntatem, sit prius agente per naturam. Unde, cum primum in ordine agentium sit Deus, necesse est quod per intellectum et voluntatem agat. Secundo, ex ratione naturalis agentis, ad quod pertinet ut unum effectum producat, quia natura uno et eodem modo operatur, nisi impediatur. Et hoc ideo, quia secundum quod est tale, agit, unde, quandiu est tale, non facit nisi tale. Omne enim agens per naturam, habet esse determinatum. Cum igitur esse divinum non sit determinatum, sed contineat in se totam perfectionem essendi, non potest esse quod agat per necessitatem naturae, nisi forte causaret aliquid indeterminatum et infinitum in essendo; quod est impossibile, ut ex superioribus patet. Non igitur agit per necessitatem naturae sed effectus determinati ab infinita ipsius perfectione procedunt secundum determinationem voluntatis et intellectus ipsius. Tertio, ex habitudine effectuum ad causam. Secundum hoc enim effectus procedunt a causa agente, secundum quod praeexistunt in ea, quia omne agens agit sibi simile. Praeexistunt autem effectus in causa secundum modum causae. Unde, cum esse divinum sit ipsum eius intelligere, praeexistunt in eo effectus eius secundum modum intelligibilem. Unde et per modum intelligibilem procedunt ab eo. Et sic, per consequens, per modum voluntatis, nam inclinatio eius ad agendum quod intellectu conceptum est, pertinet ad voluntatem. Voluntas igitur Dei est causa rerum.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29218)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius per verba illa non intendit excludere electionem a Deo simpliciter, sed secundum quid, inquantum scilicet, non quibusdam solum bonitatem suam communicat, sed omnibus, prout scilicet electio discretionem quandam importat.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29219)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quia essentia Dei est eius intelligere et velle, ex hoc ipso quod per essentiam suam agit, sequitur quod agat per modum intellectus et voluntatis.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29220)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod bonum est obiectum voluntatis. Pro tanto ergo dicitur, quia Deus bonus est, sumus, inquantum sua bonitas est ei ratio volendi omnia alia, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29221)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod unius et eiusdem effectus, etiam in nobis, est causa scientia ut dirigens, qua concipitur forma operis, et voluntas ut imperans, quia forma, ut est in intellectu tantum, non determinatur ad hoc quod sit vel non sit in effectu, nisi per voluntatem. Unde intellectus speculativus nihil dicit de operando. Sed potentia est causa ut exequens, quia nominat immediatum principium operationis. Sed haec omnia in Deo unum sunt.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29222)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntatis divinae sit assignare aliquam causam. Dicit enim Augustinus, libro octoginta trium quaest., *quis audeat dicere Deum irrationabiliter omnia condidisse?* Sed agenti voluntario, quod est ratio operandi, est etiam causa volendi. Ergo voluntas Dei habet aliquam causam.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29223)\n\nPraeterea, in his quae fiunt a volente qui propter nullam causam aliquid vult, non oportet aliam causam assignare nisi voluntatem volentis. Sed voluntas Dei est causa omnium rerum, ut ostensum est. Si igitur voluntatis eius non sit aliqua causa, non oportebit in omnibus rebus naturalibus aliam causam quaerere, nisi solam voluntatem divinam. Et sic omnes scientiae essent supervacuae, quae causas aliquorum effectuum assignare nituntur, quod videtur inconveniens. Est igitur assignare aliquam causam voluntatis divinae.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29224)\n\nPraeterea, quod fit a volente non propter aliquam causam, dependet ex simplici voluntate eius. Si igitur voluntas Dei non habeat aliquam causam, sequitur quod omnia quae fiunt, dependeant ex simplici eius voluntate, et non habeant aliquam aliam causam. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29225)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *omnis causa efficiens maior est eo quod efficitur; nihil tamen maius est voluntate Dei; non ergo causa eius quaerenda est*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29226)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nullo modo voluntas Dei causam habet. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum voluntas sequatur intellectum, eodem modo contingit esse causam alicuius volentis ut velit, et alicuius intelligentis ut intelligat. In intellectu autem sic est quod, si seorsum intelligat principium, et seorsum conclusionem, intelligentia principii est causa scientiae conclusionis. Sed si intellectus in ipso principio inspiceret conclusionem, uno intuitu apprehendens utrumque, in eo scientia conclusionis non causaretur ab intellectu principiorum, quia idem non est causa sui ipsius. Sed tamen intelligeret principia esse causas conclusionis. Similiter est ex parte voluntatis, circa quam sic se habet finis ad ea quae sunt ad finem, sicut in intellectu principia ad conclusiones. Unde, si aliquis uno actu velit finem, et alio actu ea quae sunt ad finem, velle finem erit ei causa volendi ea quae sunt ad finem. Sed si uno actu velit finem et ea quae sunt ad finem, hoc esse non poterit, quia idem non est causa sui ipsius. Et tamen erit verum dicere quod velit ordinare ea quae sunt ad finem, in finem. Deus autem, sicut uno actu omnia in essentia sua intelligit, ita uno actu vult omnia in sua bonitate. Unde, sicut in Deo intelligere causam non est causa intelligendi effectus, sed ipse intelligit effectus in causa; ita velle finem non est ei causa volendi ea quae sunt ad finem, sed tamen vult ea quae sunt ad finem, ordinari in finem. Vult ergo hoc esse propter hoc, sed non propter hoc vult hoc.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29227)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod voluntas Dei rationabilis est, non quod aliquid sit Deo causa volendi, sed inquantum vult unum esse propter aliud.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29228)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum velit Deus effectus sic esse, ut ex causis certis proveniant, ad hoc quod servetur ordo in rebus; non est supervacuum, etiam cum voluntate Dei, alias causas quaerere. Esset tamen supervacuum, si aliae causae quaererentur ut primae, et non dependentes a divina voluntate. Et sic loquitur Augustinus in III de Trin., *placuit vanitati philosophorum etiam aliis causis effectus contingentes tribuere, cum omnino videre non possent superiorem ceteris omnibus causam, idest voluntatem Dei*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29229)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum Deus velit effectus esse propter causas, quicumque effectus praesupponunt aliquem alium effectum, non dependent ex sola Dei voluntate, sed ex aliquo alio. Sed primi effectus ex sola divina voluntate dependent. Utpote si dicamus quod Deus voluit hominem habere manus, ut deservirent intellectui, operando diversa opera, et voluit eum habere intellectum, ad hoc quod esset homo, et voluit eum esse hominem, ut frueretur ipso, vel ad complementum universi. Quae quidem non est reducere ad alios fines creatos ulteriores. Unde huiusmodi dependent ex simplici voluntate Dei, alia vero ex ordine etiam aliarum causarum.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29230)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei non semper impleatur. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Tim. II, quod *Deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri, et ad agnitionem veritatis venire*. Sed hoc non ita evenit. Ergo voluntas Dei non semper impletur.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29231)\n\nPraeterea, sicut se habet scientia ad verum, ita voluntas ad bonum. Sed Deus scit omne verum. Ergo vult omne bonum. Sed non omne bonum fit, multa enim bona possunt fieri, quae non fiunt. Non ergo voluntas Dei semper impletur.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29232)\n\nPraeterea, voluntas Dei, cum sit causa prima, non excludit causas medias, ut dictum est. Sed effectus causae primae potest impediri per defectum causae secundae, sicut effectus virtutis motivae impeditur propter debilitatem tibiae. Ergo et effectus divinae voluntatis potest impediri propter defectum secundarum causarum. Non ergo voluntas Dei semper impletur.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29233)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CXIII, *omnia quaecumque voluit Deus, fecit*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29234)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est voluntatem Dei semper impleri. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum effectus conformetur agenti secundum suam formam, eadem ratio est in causis agentibus, quae est in causis formalibus. In formis autem sic est quod, licet aliquid possit deficere ab aliqua forma particulari, tamen a forma universali nihil deficere potest, potest enim esse aliquid quod non est homo vel vivum, non autem potest esse aliquid quod non sit ens. Unde et hoc idem in causis agentibus contingere oportet. Potest enim aliquid fieri extra ordinem alicuius causae particularis agentis, non autem extra ordinem alicuius causae universalis, sub qua omnes causae particulares comprehenduntur. Quia, si aliqua causa particularis deficiat a suo effectu, hoc est propter aliquam aliam causam particularem impedientem, quae continetur sub ordine causae universalis, unde effectus ordinem causae universalis nullo modo potest exire. Et hoc etiam patet in corporalibus. Potest enim impediri quod aliqua stella non inducat suum effectum, sed tamen quicumque effectus ex causa corporea impediente in rebus corporalibus consequatur, oportet quod reducatur per aliquas causas medias in universalem virtutem primi caeli. Cum igitur voluntas Dei sit universalis causa omnium rerum, impossibile est quod divina voluntas suum effectum non consequatur. Unde quod recedere videtur a divina voluntate secundum unum ordinem, relabitur in ipsam secundum alium, sicut peccator, qui, quantum est in se, recedit a divina voluntate peccando, incidit in ordinem divinae voluntatis, dum per eius iustitiam punitur.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29235)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod illud verbum apostoli, quod Deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri etc., potest tripliciter intelligi. Uno modo, ut sit accommoda distributio, secundum hunc sensum, Deus vult salvos fieri omnes homines qui salvantur, non quia nullus homo sit quem salvum fieri non velit, sed quia nullus salvus fit, quem non velit salvum fieri, ut dicit Augustinus secundo potest intelligi, ut fiat distributio pro generibus singulorum, et non pro singulis generum, secundum hunc sensum, Deus vult de quolibet statu hominum salvos fieri, mares et feminas, Iudaeos et gentiles, parvos et magnos; non tamen omnes de singulis statibus. Tertio, secundum Damascenum, intelligitur de voluntate antecedente, non de voluntate consequente. Quae quidem distinctio non accipitur ex parte ipsius voluntatis divinae, in qua nihil est prius vel posterius; sed ex parte volitorum. Ad cuius intellectum, considerandum est quod unumquodque, secundum quod bonum est, sic est volitum a Deo. Aliquid autem potest esse in prima sui consideratione, secundum quod absolute consideratur, bonum vel malum, quod tamen, prout cum aliquo adiuncto consideratur, quae est consequens consideratio eius, e contrario se habet. Sicut hominem vivere est bonum, et hominem occidi est malum, secundum absolutam considerationem, sed si addatur circa aliquem hominem, quod sit homicida, vel vivens in periculum multitudinis, sic bonum est eum occidi, et malum est eum vivere. Unde potest dici quod iudex iustus antecedenter vult omnem hominem vivere; sed consequenter vult homicidam suspendi. Similiter Deus antecedenter vult omnem hominem salvari; sed consequenter vult quosdam damnari, secundum exigentiam suae iustitiae. Neque tamen id quod antecedenter volumus, simpliciter volumus, sed secundum quid. Quia voluntas comparatur ad res, secundum quod in seipsis sunt, in seipsis autem sunt in particulari, unde simpliciter volumus aliquid, secundum quod volumus illud consideratis omnibus circumstantiis particularibus, quod est consequenter velle. Unde potest dici quod iudex iustus simpliciter vult homicidam suspendi, sed secundum quid vellet eum vivere, scilicet inquantum est homo. Unde magis potest dici velleitas, quam absoluta voluntas. Et sic patet quod quidquid Deus simpliciter vult, fit; licet illud quod antecedenter vult, non fiat.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29236)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod actus cognoscitivae virtutis est secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente, actus autem virtutis appetitivae est ordinatus ad res, secundum quod in seipsis sunt. Quidquid autem potest habere rationem entis et veri, totum est virtualiter in Deo; sed non totum existit in rebus creatis. Et ideo Deus cognoscit omne verum, non tamen vult omne bonum, nisi inquantum vult se, in quo virtualiter omne bonum existit.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29237)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod causa prima tunc potest impediri a suo effectu per defectum causae secundae, quando non est universaliter prima, sub se omnes causas comprehendens, quia sic effectus nullo modo posset suum ordinem evadere. Et sic est de voluntate Dei, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29238)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei sit mutabilis. Dicit enim dominus Genes. VI, *poenitet me fecisse hominem*. Sed quemcumque poenitet de eo quod fecit, habet mutabilem voluntatem. Ergo Deus habet mutabilem voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29239)\n\nPraeterea, Ierem. XVIII, ex persona domini dicitur, *loquar adversus gentem et adversus regnum, ut eradicem et destruam et disperdam illud; sed si poenitentiam egerit gens illa a malo suo, agam et ego poenitentiam super malo quod cogitavi ut facerem ei*. Ergo Deus habet mutabilem voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29240)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid Deus facit, voluntarie facit. Sed Deus non semper eadem facit, nam quandoque praecepit legalia observari, quandoque prohibuit. Ergo habet mutabilem voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29241)\n\nPraeterea, Deus non ex necessitate vult quod vult, ut supra dictum est. Ergo potest velle et non velle idem. Sed omne quod habet potentiam ad opposita, est mutabile, sicut quod potest esse et non esse, est mutabile secundum substantiam; et quod potest esse hic et non esse hic, est mutabile secundum locum. Ergo Deus est mutabilis secundum voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29242)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Num. XXIII, *non est Deus, quasi homo, ut mentiatur; neque ut filius hominis, ut mutetur*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29243)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod voluntas Dei est omnino immutabilis. Sed circa hoc considerandum est, quod aliud est mutare voluntatem; et aliud est velle aliquarum rerum mutationem. Potest enim aliquis, eadem voluntate immobiliter permanente, velle quod nunc fiat hoc, et postea fiat contrarium. Sed tunc voluntas mutaretur, si aliquis inciperet velle quod prius non voluit, vel desineret velle quod voluit. Quod quidem accidere non potest, nisi praesupposita mutatione vel ex parte cognitionis, vel circa dispositionem substantiae ipsius volentis. Cum enim voluntas sit boni, aliquis de novo dupliciter potest incipere aliquid velle. Uno modo sic, quod de novo incipiat sibi illud esse bonum. Quod non est absque mutatione eius, sicut adveniente frigore, incipit esse bonum sedere ad ignem, quod prius non erat. Alio modo sic, quod de novo cognoscat illud esse sibi bonum, cum prius hoc ignorasset, ad hoc enim consiliamur, ut sciamus quid nobis sit bonum. Ostensum est autem supra quod tam substantia Dei quam eius scientia est omnino immutabilis. Unde oportet voluntatem eius omnino esse immutabilem.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29244)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod illud verbum domini metaphorice intelligendum est, secundum similitudinem nostram, cum enim nos poenitet, destruimus quod fecimus. Quamvis hoc esse possit absque mutatione voluntatis, cum etiam aliquis homo, absque mutatione voluntatis, interdum velit aliquid facere, simul intendens postea illud destruere. Sic igitur Deus poenituisse dicitur, secundum similitudinem operationis, inquantum hominem quem fecerat, per diluvium a facie terrae delevit.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29245)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod voluntas Dei, cum sit causa prima et universalis, non excludit causas medias, in quarum virtute est ut aliqui effectus producantur. Sed quia omnes causae mediae non adaequant virtutem causae primae, multa sunt in virtute et scientia et voluntate divina, quae non continentur sub ordine causarum inferiorum; sicut resuscitatio Lazari. Unde aliquis respiciens ad causas inferiores, dicere poterat, Lazarus non resurget, respiciens vero ad causam primam divinam, poterat dicere, Lazarus resurget. Et utrumque horum Deus vult, scilicet quod aliquid quandoque sit futurum secundum causam inferiorem, quod tamen futurum non sit secundum causam superiorem; vel e converso. Sic ergo dicendum est quod Deus aliquando pronuntiat aliquid futurum, secundum quod continetur in ordine causarum inferiorum, ut puta secundum dispositionem naturae vel meritorum; quod tamen non fit, quia aliter est in causa superiori divina. Sicut cum praedixit Ezechiae, *dispone domui tuae, quia morieris et non vives*, ut habetur Isaiae XXXVIII; neque tamen ita evenit, quia ab aeterno aliter fuit in scientia et voluntate divina, quae immutabilis est. Propter quod dicit Gregorius, quod *Deus immutat sententiam, non tamen mutat consilium*, scilicet voluntatis suae. Quod ergo dicit, poenitentiam agam ego, intelligitur metaphorice dictum, nam homines quando non implent quod comminati sunt, poenitere videntur.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29246)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ex ratione illa non potest concludi quod Deus habeat mutabilem voluntatem; sed quod mutationem velit.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29247)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, licet Deum velle aliquid non sit necessarium absolute, tamen necessarium est ex suppositione, propter immutabilitatem divinae voluntatis, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29248)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei rebus volitis necessitatem imponat. Dicit enim Augustinus, in Enchirid., *nullus fit salvus, nisi quem Deus voluerit salvari. Et ideo rogandus est ut velit, quia necesse est fieri, si voluerit*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29249)\n\nPraeterea, omnis causa quae non potest impediri, ex necessitate suum effectum producit, quia et natura semper idem operatur, nisi aliquid impediat, ut dicitur in II Physic. Sed voluntas Dei non potest impediri, dicit enim apostolus, ad Rom. IX, *voluntati enim eius quis resistit?* Ergo voluntas Dei imponit rebus volitis necessitatem.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29250)\n\nPraeterea, illud quod habet necessitatem ex priori, est necessarium absolute, sicut animal mori est necessarium, quia est ex contrariis compositum. Sed res creatae a Deo, comparantur ad voluntatem divinam sicut ad aliquid prius, a quo habent necessitatem, cum haec conditionalis sit vera, si aliquid Deus vult, illud est; omnis autem conditionalis vera est necessaria. Sequitur ergo quod omne quod Deus vult, sit necessarium absolute.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29251)\n\nSed contra, omnia bona quae fiunt, Deus vult fieri. Si igitur eius voluntas imponat rebus volitis necessitatem, sequitur quod omnia bona ex necessitate eveniunt. Et sic perit liberum arbitrium et consilium, et omnia huiusmodi.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29252)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod divina voluntas quibusdam volitis necessitatem imponit, non autem omnibus. Cuius quidem rationem aliqui assignare voluerunt ex causis mediis quia ea quae producit per causas necessarias, sunt necessaria; ea vero quae producit per causas contingentes, sunt contingentia. Sed hoc non videtur sufficienter dictum, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia effectus alicuius primae causae est contingens propter causam secundam, ex eo quod impeditur effectus causae primae per defectum causae secundae; sicut virtus solis per defectum plantae impeditur. Nullus autem defectus causae secundae impedire potest quin voluntas Dei effectum suum producat. Secundo, quia, si distinctio contingentium a necessariis referatur solum in causas secundas, sequitur hoc esse praeter intentionem et voluntatem divinam, quod est inconveniens. Et ideo melius dicendum est, quod hoc contingit propter efficaciam divinae voluntatis. Cum enim aliqua causa efficax fuerit ad agendum, effectus consequitur causam non tantum secundum id quod fit, sed etiam secundum modum fiendi vel essendi, ex debilitate enim virtutis activae in semine, contingit quod filius nascitur dissimilis patri in accidentibus, quae pertinent ad modum essendi. Cum igitur voluntas divina sit efficacissima, non solum sequitur quod fiant ea quae Deus vult fieri; sed quod eo modo fiant, quo Deus ea fieri vult. Vult autem quaedam fieri Deus necessario, et quaedam contingenter, ut sit ordo in rebus, ad complementum universi. Et ideo quibusdam effectibus aptavit causas necessarias, quae deficere non possunt, ex quibus effectus de necessitate proveniunt, quibusdam autem aptavit causas contingentes defectibiles, ex quibus effectus contingenter eveniunt. Non igitur propterea effectus voliti a Deo, eveniunt contingenter, quia causae proximae sunt contingentes, sed propterea quia Deus voluit eos contingenter evenire, contingentes causas ad eos praeparavit.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29253)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod per illud verbum Augustini intelligenda est necessitas in rebus volitis a Deo, non absoluta, sed conditionalis, necesse est enim hanc conditionalem veram esse, si Deus hoc vult, necesse est hoc esse.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29254)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, ex hoc ipso quod nihil voluntati divinae resistit, sequitur quod non solum fiant ea quae Deus vult fieri; sed quod fiant contingenter vel necessario, quae sic fieri vult.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29255)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod posteriora habent necessitatem a prioribus, secundum modum priorum. Unde et ea quae fiunt a voluntate divina, talem necessitatem habent, qualem Deus vult ea habere, scilicet, vel absolutam, vel conditionalem tantum. Et sic, non omnia sunt necessaria absolute.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29256)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei sit malorum. Omne enim bonum quod fit, Deus vult. Sed mala fieri bonum est, dicit enim Augustinus, in Enchirid., *quamvis ea quae mala sunt, inquantum mala sunt, non sint bona; tamen, ut non solum bona, sed etiam ut sint mala, bonum est*. Ergo Deus vult mala.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29257)\n\nPraeterea, dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., erit malum ad omnis (idest universi) perfectionem conferens. Et Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., *ex omnibus consistit universitatis admirabilis pulchritudo; in qua etiam illud quod malum dicitur, bene ordinatum, et loco suo positum, eminentius commendat bona; ut magis placeant, et laudabiliora sint, dum comparantur malis*. Sed Deus vult omne illud quod pertinet ad perfectionem et decorem universi, quia hoc est quod Deus maxime vult in creaturis. Ergo Deus vult mala.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29258)\n\nPraeterea, mala fieri, et non fieri, sunt contradictorie opposita. Sed Deus non vult mala non fieri, quia, cum mala quaedam fiant, non semper voluntas Dei impleretur. Ergo Deus vult mala fieri.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29259)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *nullo sapiente homine auctore, fit homo deterior; est autem Deus omni sapiente homine praestantior; multo igitur minus, Deo auctore, fit aliquis deterior. Illo autem auctore cum dicitur, illo volente dicitur*. Non ergo volente Deo, fit homo deterior. Constat autem quod quolibet malo fit aliquid deterius. Ergo Deus non vult mala.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29260)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum ratio boni sit ratio appetibilis, ut supra dictum est, malum autem opponatur bono; impossibile est quod aliquod malum, inquantum huiusmodi, appetatur, neque appetitu naturali, neque animali, neque intellectuali, qui est voluntas. Sed aliquod malum appetitur per accidens, inquantum consequitur ad aliquod bonum. Et hoc apparet in quolibet appetitu. Non enim agens naturale intendit privationem vel corruptionem; sed formam, cui coniungitur privatio alterius formae; et generationem unius, quae est corruptio alterius. Leo etiam, occidens cervum, intendit cibum, cui coniungitur occisio animalis. Similiter fornicator intendit delectationem, cui coniungitur deformitas culpae. Malum autem quod coniungitur alicui bono, est privatio alterius boni. Nunquam igitur appeteretur malum, nec per accidens, nisi bonum cui coniungitur malum, magis appeteretur quam bonum quod privatur per malum. Nullum autem bonum Deus magis vult quam suam bonitatem, vult tamen aliquod bonum magis quam aliud quoddam bonum. Unde malum culpae, quod privat ordinem ad bonum divinum, Deus nullo modo vult. Sed malum naturalis defectus, vel malum poenae vult, volendo aliquod bonum, cui coniungitur tale malum, sicut, volendo iustitiam, vult poenam; et volendo ordinem naturae servari, vult quaedam naturaliter corrumpi.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29261)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod, licet Deus non velit mala, vult tamen mala esse vel fieri, quia, licet mala non sint bona, bonum tamen est mala esse vel fieri. Quod ideo dicebant, quia ea quae in se mala sunt, ordinantur ad aliquod bonum, quem quidem ordinem importari credebant in hoc quod dicitur, mala esse vel fieri. Sed hoc non recte dicitur. Quia malum non ordinatur ad bonum per se, sed per accidens. Praeter intentionem enim peccantis est, quod ex hoc sequatur aliquod bonum; sicut praeter intentionem tyrannorum fuit, quod ex eorum persecutionibus claresceret patientia martyrum. Et ideo non potest dici quod talis ordo ab bonum importetur per hoc quod dicitur, quod malum esse vel fieri sit bonum, quia nihil iudicatur secundum illud quod competit ei per accidens, sed secundum illud quod competit ei per se.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29262)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod malum non operatur ad perfectionem et decorem universi nisi per accidens, ut dictum est. Unde et hoc quod dicit Dionysius, quod malum est ad universi perfectionem conferens, concludit inducendo quasi ad inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29263)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet mala fieri, et mala non fieri, contradictorie opponantur; tamen velle mala fieri, et velle mala non fieri, non opponuntur contradictorie, cum utrumque sit affirmativum. Deus igitur neque vult mala fieri, neque vult mala non fieri, sed vult permittere mala fieri. Et hoc est bonum.\n\n## Articulus 10\n\n[I q. 19 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29264)\n\nAd decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non habeat liberum arbitrium. Dicit enim Hieronymus, in homilia de filio prodigo, *solus Deus est, in quem peccatum non cadit, nec cadere potest; cetera, cum sint liberi arbitrii, in utramque partem flecti possunt*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29265)\n\nPraeterea, liberum arbitrium est facultas rationis et voluntatis, qua bonum et malum eligitur. Sed Deus non vult malum, ut dictum est. Ergo liberum arbitrium non est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29266)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Ambrosius, in libro de fide, *spiritus sanctus dividit singulis prout vult, idest pro liberae voluntatis arbitrio, non necessitatis obsequio*.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29267)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod liberum arbitrium habemus respectu eorum quae non necessario volumus, vel naturali instinctu. Non enim ad liberum arbitrium pertinet quod volumus esse felices, sed ad naturalem instinctum. Unde et alia animalia, quae naturali instinctu moventur ad aliquid, non dicuntur libero arbitrio moveri. Cum igitur Deus ex necessitate suam bonitatem velit, alia vero non ex necessitate, ut supra ostensum est; respectu illorum quae non ex necessitate vult, liberum arbitrium habet.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29268)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Hieronymus videtur excludere a Deo liberum arbitrium, non simpliciter, sed solum quantum ad hoc quod est deflecti in peccatum.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29269)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum malum culpae dicatur per aversionem a bonitate divina, per quam Deus omnia vult, ut supra ostensum est, manifestum est quod impossibile est eum malum culpae velle. Et tamen ad opposita se habet, inquantum velle potest hoc esse vel non esse. Sicut et nos, non peccando, possumus velle sedere, et non velle sedere.\n\n## Articulus 11\n\n[I q. 19 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29270)\n\nAd undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit distinguenda in Deo voluntas signi. Sicut enim voluntas Dei est causa rerum, ita et scientia. Sed non assignantur aliqua signa ex parte divinae scientiae. Ergo neque debent assignari aliqua signa ex parte divinae voluntatis.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29271)\n\nPraeterea, omne signum quod non concordat ei cuius est signum, est falsum. Si igitur signa quae assignantur circa voluntatem divinam, non concordant divinae voluntati, sunt falsa, si autem concordant, superflue assignantur. Non igitur sunt aliqua signa circa voluntatem divinam assignanda.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29272)\n\nSed contra est quod voluntas Dei est una, cum ipsa sit Dei essentia. Quandoque autem pluraliter significatur, ut cum dicitur, *magna opera domini, exquisita in omnes voluntates eius*. Ergo oportet quod aliquando signum voluntatis pro voluntate accipiatur.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29273)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in Deo quaedam dicuntur proprie, et quaedam secundum metaphoram, ut ex supradictis patet. Cum autem aliquae passiones humanae in divinam praedicationem metaphorice assumuntur, hoc fit secundum similitudinem effectus, unde illud quod est signum talis passionis in nobis, in Deo nomine illius passionis metaphorice significatur. Sicut, apud nos, irati punire consueverunt, unde ipsa punitio est signum irae, et propter hoc, ipsa punitio nomine irae significatur, cum Deo attribuitur. Similiter id quod solet esse in nobis signum voluntatis, quandoque metaphorice in Deo voluntas dicitur. Sicut, cum aliquis praecipit aliquid, signum est quod velit illud fieri, unde praeceptum divinum quandoque metaphorice voluntas Dei dicitur, secundum illud Matth. VI, *fiat voluntas tua, sicut in caelo et in terra*. Sed hoc distat inter voluntatem et iram, quia ira de Deo nunquam proprie dicitur, cum in suo principali intellectu includat passionem, voluntas autem proprie de Deo dicitur. Et ideo in Deo distinguitur voluntas proprie, et metaphorice dicta. Voluntas enim proprie dicta, vocatur voluntas beneplaciti, voluntas autem metaphorice dicta, est voluntas signi, eo quod ipsum signum voluntatis voluntas dicitur.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29274)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod scientia non est causa eorum quae fiunt, nisi per voluntatem, non enim quae scimus facimus, nisi velimus. Et ideo signum non attribuitur scientiae, sicut attribuitur voluntati.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29275)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod signa voluntatis dicuntur voluntates divinae, non quia sint signa quod Deus velit, sed quia ea quae in nobis solent esse signa volendi, in Deo divinae voluntates dicuntur. Sicut punitio non est signum quod in Deo sit ira, sed punitio, ex eo ipso quod in nobis est signum irae, in Deo dicitur ira.\n\n## Articulus 12\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29276)\n\nAd duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter circa divinam voluntatem ponantur quinque signa, scilicet, prohibitio, praeceptum, consilium, operatio et permissio. Nam eadem quae nobis praecipit Deus vel consulit, in nobis quandoque operatur, et eadem quae prohibet, quandoque permittit. Ergo non debent ex opposito dividi.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29277)\n\nPraeterea, nihil Deus operatur, nisi volens, ut dicitur Sap. XI. Sed voluntas signi distinguitur a voluntate beneplaciti. Ergo operatio sub voluntate signi comprehendi non debet.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29278)\n\nPraeterea, operatio et permissio communiter ad omnes creaturas pertinent, quia in omnibus Deus operatur, et in omnibus aliquid fieri permittit. Sed praeceptum, consilium et prohibitio pertinent ad solam rationalem creaturam. Ergo non veniunt convenienter in unam divisionem, cum non sint unius ordinis.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29279)\n\nPraeterea, malum pluribus modis contingit quam bonum, quia bonum contingit uno modo, sed malum omnifariam, ut patet per philosophum in II Ethic., et per Dionysium in IV cap. de Div. Nom. Inconvenienter igitur respectu mali assignatur unum signum tantum, scilicet prohibitio; respectu vero boni, duo signa, scilicet consilium et praeceptum.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29280)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod huiusmodi signa voluntatis dicuntur ea, quibus consuevimus demonstrare nos aliquid velle. Potest autem aliquis declarare se velle aliquid, vel per seipsum, vel per alium. Per seipsum quidem, inquantum facit aliquid, vel directe, vel indirecte et per accidens. Directe quidem, cum per se aliquid operatur, et quantum ad hoc, dicitur esse signum operatio. Indirecte autem, inquantum non impedit operationem, nam removens prohibens dicitur movens per accidens, ut dicitur in VIII Physic. Et quantum ad hoc, dicitur signum permissio. Per alium autem declarat se aliquid velle, inquantum ordinat alium ad aliquid faciendum; vel necessaria inductione, quod fit praecipiendo quod quis vult, et prohibendo contrarium; vel aliqua persuasoria inductione, quod pertinet ad consilium. Quia igitur his modis declaratur aliquem velle aliquid, propter hoc ista quinque nominantur interdum nomine voluntatis divinae, tanquam signa voluntatis. Quod enim praeceptum, consilium et prohibitio dicantur Dei voluntas, patet per id quod dicitur Matth. VI *fiat voluntas tua, sicut in caelo et in terra*. Quod autem permissio vel operatio dicantur Dei voluntas patet per Augustinum, qui dicit in Enchirid., *nihil fit, nisi omnipotens fieri velit, vel sinendo ut fiat, vel faciendo*. Vel potest dici quod permissio et operatio referuntur ad praesens permissio quidem ad malum, operatio vero ad bonum. Ad futurum vero, prohibitio, respectu mali; respectu vero boni necessarii, praeceptum; respectu vero superabundantis boni, consilium.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29281)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod nihil prohibet, circa eandem rem, aliquem diversimode declarare se aliquid velle, sicut inveniuntur multa nomina idem significantia. Unde nihil prohibet idem subiacere praecepto et consilio et operationi, et prohibitioni vel permissioni.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29282)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Deus potest significari metaphorice velle id quod non vult voluntate proprie accepta, ita potest metaphorice significari velle id quod proprie vult. Unde nihil prohibet de eodem esse voluntatem beneplaciti, et voluntatem signi. Sed operatio semper est eadem cum voluntate beneplaciti, non autem praeceptum vel consilium, tum quia haec est de praesenti, illud de futuro; tum quia haec per se est effectus voluntatis, illud autem per alium, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29283)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod creatura rationalis est domina sui actus, et ideo circa ipsam specialia quaedam signa divinae voluntatis assignantur, inquantum rationalem creaturam Deus ordinat ad agendum voluntarie et per se. Sed aliae creaturae non agunt nisi motae ex operatione divina, et ideo circa alias non habent locum nisi operatio et permissio.\n\n[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29284)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod omne malum culpae, licet multipliciter contingat, tamen in hoc convenit, quod discordat a voluntate divina et ideo unum signum respectu malorum assignatur, scilicet prohibitio. Sed diversimode bona se habent ad bonitatem divinam. Quia quaedam sunt, sine quibus fruitionem divinae bonitatis consequi non possumus, et respectu horum est praeceptum. Quaedam vero sunt, quibus perfectius consequimur, et respectu horum est consilium. Vel dicendum quod consilium est non solum de melioribus bonis assequendis, sed etiam de minoribus malis vitandis.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0002","text":"[I q. 19 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","text":"Post considerationem eorum quae ad divinam scientiam pertinent, considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad voluntatem divinam, ut sit prima consideratio de ipsa Dei voluntate; secunda, de his quae ad voluntatem absolute pertinent; tertia, de his quae ad intellectum in ordine ad voluntatem pertinent. Circa ipsam autem voluntatem quaeruntur duodecim. Primo, utrum in Deo sit voluntas. Secundo, utrum Deus velit alia a se. Tertio, utrum quidquid Deus vult, ex necessitate velit. Quarto, utrum voluntas Dei sit causa rerum. Quinto, utrum voluntatis divinae sit assignare aliquam causam. Sexto, utrum voluntas divina semper impleatur. Septimo, utrum voluntas Dei sit mutabilis. Octavo, utrum voluntas Dei necessitatem rebus volitis imponat. Nono, utrum in Deo sit voluntas malorum. Decimo, utrum Deus habeat liberum arbitrium. Undecimo, utrum sit distinguenda in Deo voluntas signi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","text":"Duodecimo, utrum convenienter circa divinam voluntatem ponantur quinque signa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0006","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit voluntas. Obiectum enim voluntatis est finis et bonum. Sed Dei non est assignare aliquem finem. Ergo voluntas non est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0008","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0009","text":"Praeterea, voluntas est appetitus quidam. Appetitus autem, cum sit rei non habitae, imperfectionem designat, quae Deo non competit. Ergo voluntas non est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0010","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0011","text":"Praeterea, secundum philosophum, in III de anima, voluntas est movens motum. Sed Deus est primum movens immobile. Ut probatur VIII Physic. Ergo in Deo non est voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0012","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0013","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit apostolus, Rom. XII, *ut probetis quae sit voluntas Dei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0014","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","text":"Respondeo dicendum in Deo voluntatem esse, sicut et in eo est intellectus, voluntas enim intellectum consequitur. Sicut enim res naturalis habet esse in actu per suam formam, ita intellectus intelligens actu per suam formam intelligibilem. Quaelibet autem res ad suam formam naturalem hanc habet habitudinem, ut quando non habet ipsam, tendat in eam; et quando habet ipsam, quiescat in ea. Et idem est de qualibet perfectione naturali, quod est bonum naturae. Et haec habitudo ad bonum, in rebus carentibus cognitione, vocatur appetitus naturalis. Unde et natura intellectualis ad bonum apprehensum per formam intelligibilem, similem habitudinem habet, ut scilicet, cum habet ipsum, quiescat in illo; cum vero non habet, quaerat ipsum. Et utrumque pertinet ad voluntatem. Unde in quolibet habente intellectum, est voluntas; sicut in quolibet habente sensum, est appetitus animalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","text":"Et sic oportet in Deo esse voluntatem, cum sit in eo intellectus. Et sicut suum intelligere est suum esse, ita suum velle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0017","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet nihil aliud a Deo sit finis Dei, tamen ipsemet est finis respectu omnium quae ab eo fiunt. Et hoc per suam essentiam, cum per suam essentiam sit bonus, ut supra ostensum est, finis enim habet rationem boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0019","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod voluntas in nobis pertinet ad appetitivam partem, quae licet ab appetendo nominetur, non tamen hunc solum habet actum, ut appetat quae non habet; sed etiam ut amet quod habet, et delectetur in illo. Et quantum ad hoc voluntas in Deo ponitur; quae semper habet bonum quod est eius obiectum, cum sit indifferens ab eo secundum essentiam, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0021","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod voluntas cuius obiectum principale est bonum quod est extra voluntatem, oportet quod sit mota ab aliquo. Sed obiectum divinae voluntatis est bonitas sua, quae est eius essentia. Unde, cum voluntas Dei sit eius essentia, non movetur ab alio a se, sed a se tantum, eo modo loquendi quo intelligere et velle dicitur motus. Et secundum hoc Plato dixit quod primum movens movet seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0024","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non velit alia a se. Velle enim divinum est eius esse. Sed Deus non est aliud a se. Ergo non vult aliud a se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0026","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0027","text":"Praeterea, volitum movet voluntatem, sicut appetibile appetitum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Si igitur Deus velit aliquid aliud a se, movebitur eius voluntas ab aliquo alio, quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0028","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0029","text":"Praeterea, cuicumque voluntati sufficit aliquod volitum, nihil quaerit extra illud. Sed Deo sufficit sua bonitas, et voluntas eius ex ea satiatur. Ergo Deus non vult aliquid aliud a se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0030","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0031","text":"Praeterea, actus voluntatis multiplicatur secundum volita. Si igitur Deus velit se et alia a se, sequitur quod actus voluntatis eius sit multiplex, et per consequens eius esse, quod est eius velle. Hoc autem est impossibile. Non ergo vult alia a se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0032","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0033","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit, I Thess. IV, *haec est voluntas Dei, sanctificatio vestra*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0034","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus non solum se vult, sed etiam alia a se. Quod apparet a simili prius introducto. Res enim naturalis non solum habet naturalem inclinationem respectu proprii boni, ut acquirat ipsum cum non habet, vel ut quiescat in illo cum habet; sed etiam ut proprium bonum in alia diffundat, secundum quod possibile est. Unde videmus quod omne agens, inquantum est actu et perfectum, facit sibi simile. Unde et hoc pertinet ad rationem voluntatis, ut bonum quod quis habet, aliis communicet, secundum quod possibile est. Et hoc praecipue pertinet ad voluntatem divinam, a qua, per quandam similitudinem, derivatur omnis perfectio. Unde, si res naturales, inquantum perfectae sunt, suum bonum aliis communicant, multo magis pertinet ad voluntatem divinam, ut bonum suum aliis per similitudinem communicet, secundum quod possibile est. Sic igitur vult et se esse, et alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","text":"Sed se ut finem, alia vero ut ad finem, inquantum condecet divinam bonitatem etiam alia ipsam participare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0037","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet divinum velle sit eius esse secundum rem, tamen differt ratione, secundum diversum modum intelligendi et significandi, ut ex superioribus patet. In hoc enim quod dico Deum esse, non importatur habitudo ad aliquid, sicut in hoc quod dico Deum velle. Et ideo, licet non sit aliquid aliud a se, vult tamen aliquid aliud a se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0039","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in his quae volumus propter finem, tota ratio movendi est finis, et hoc est quod movet voluntatem. Et hoc maxime apparet in his quae volumus tantum propter finem. Qui enim vult sumere potionem amaram, nihil in ea vult nisi sanitatem, et hoc solum est quod movet eius voluntatem. Secus autem est in eo qui sumit potionem dulcem, quam non solum propter sanitatem, sed etiam propter se aliquis velle potest. Unde, cum Deus alia a se non velit nisi propter finem qui est sua bonitas, ut dictum est, non sequitur quod aliquid aliud moveat voluntatem eius nisi bonitas sua. Et sic, sicut alia a se intelligit intelligendo essentiam suam, ita alia a se vult, volendo bonitatem suam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0041","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ex hoc quod voluntati divinae sufficit sua bonitas, non sequitur quod nihil aliud velit, sed quod nihil aliud vult nisi ratione suae bonitatis. Sicut etiam intellectus divinus, licet sit perfectus ex hoc ipso quod essentiam divinam cognoscit, tamen in ea cognoscit alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0043","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut intelligere divinum est unum, quia multa non videt nisi in uno; ita velle divinum est unum et simplex, quia multa non vult nisi per unum, quod est bonitas sua.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0045","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0046","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0047","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod quidquid Deus vult, ex necessitate velit. Omne enim aeternum est necessarium. Sed quidquid Deus vult, ab aeterno vult, alias, voluntas eius esset mutabilis. Ergo quidquid vult, ex necessitate vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0048","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0049","text":"Praeterea, Deus vult alia a se, inquantum vult bonitatem suam. Sed Deus bonitatem suam ex necessitate vult. Ergo alia a se ex necessitate vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0050","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0051","text":"Praeterea, quidquid est Deo naturale, est necessarium, quia Deus est per se necesse esse, et principium omnis necessitatis, ut supra ostensum est. Sed naturale est ei velle quidquid vult, quia in Deo nihil potest esse praeter naturam, ut dicitur in V Metaphys. Ergo quidquid vult, ex necessitate vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0052","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0053","text":"Praeterea, non necesse esse, et possibile non esse, aequipollent. Si igitur non necesse est Deum velle aliquid eorum quae vult, possibile est eum non velle illud; et possibile est eum velle illud quod non vult. Ergo voluntas divina est contingens ad utrumlibet. Et sic imperfecta, quia omne contingens est imperfectum et mutabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0054","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0055","text":"Praeterea, ab eo quod est ad utrumlibet, non sequitur aliqua actio, nisi ab aliquo alio inclinetur ad unum, ut dicit Commentator, in II Physic. Si ergo voluntas Dei in aliquibus se habet ad utrumlibet, sequitur quod ab aliquo alio determinetur ad effectum. Et sic habet aliquam causam priorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0056","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0057","text":"Praeterea, quidquid Deus scit, ex necessitate scit. Sed sicut scientia divina est eius essentia, ita voluntas divina. Ergo quidquid Deus vult, ex necessitate vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0058","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0059","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit apostolus, Ephes. I, *qui operatur omnia secundum consilium voluntatis suae*. Quod autem operamur ex consilio voluntatis, non ex necessitate volumus. Non ergo quidquid Deus vult, ex necessitate vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0060","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necessarium dicitur aliquid dupliciter, scilicet absolute, et ex suppositione. Necessarium absolute iudicatur aliquid ex habitudine terminorum, utpote quia praedicatum est in definitione subiecti, sicut necessarium est hominem esse animal; vel quia subiectum est de ratione praedicati, sicut hoc est necessarium, numerum esse parem vel imparem. Sic autem non est necessarium Socratem sedere. Unde non est necessarium absolute, sed potest dici necessarium ex suppositione, supposito enim quod sedeat, necesse est eum sedere dum sedet. Circa divina igitur volita hoc considerandum est, quod aliquid Deum velle est necessarium absolute, non tamen hoc est verum de omnibus quae vult. Voluntas enim divina necessariam habitudinem habet ad bonitatem suam, quae est proprium eius obiectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","text":"Unde bonitatem suam esse Deus ex necessitate vult; sicut et voluntas nostra ex necessitate vult beatitudinem. Sicut et quaelibet alia potentia necessariam habitudinem habet ad proprium et principale obiectum, ut visus ad colorem; quia de sui ratione est, ut in illud tendat. Alia autem a se Deus vult, inquantum ordinantur ad suam bonitatem ut in finem. Ea autem quae sunt ad finem, non ex necessitate volumus volentes finem, nisi sint talia, sine quibus finis esse non potest, sicut volumus cibum, volentes conservationem vitae; et navem, volentes transfretare. Non sic autem ex necessitate volumus ea sine quibus finis esse potest, sicut equum ad ambulandum, quia sine hoc possumus ire; et eadem ratio est in aliis. Unde, cum bonitas Dei sit perfecta, et esse possit sine aliis, cum nihil ei perfectionis ex aliis accrescat; sequitur quod alia a se eum velle, non sit necessarium absolute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","text":"Et tamen necessarium est ex suppositione, supposito enim quod velit, non potest non velle, quia non potest voluntas eius mutari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0064","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ex hoc quod Deus ab aeterno vult aliquid, non sequitur quod necesse est eum illud velle, nisi ex suppositione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0066","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0067","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet Deus ex necessitate velit bonitatem suam, non tamen ex necessitate vult ea quae vult propter bonitatem suam, quia bonitas eius potest esse sine aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0068","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0069","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non est naturale Deo velle aliquid aliorum, quae non ex necessitate vult. Neque tamen innaturale, aut contra naturam, sed est voluntarium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0070","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0071","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod aliquando aliqua causa necessaria habet non necessariam habitudinem ad aliquem effectum, quod est propter defectum effectus, et non propter defectum causae. Sicut virtus solis habet non necessariam habitudinem ad aliquid eorum quae contingenter hic eveniunt, non propter defectum virtutis solaris, sed propter defectum effectus non necessario ex causa provenientis. Et similiter, quod Deus non ex necessitate velit aliquid eorum quae vult, non accidit ex defectu voluntatis divinae, sed ex defectu qui competit volito secundum suam rationem, quia scilicet est tale, ut sine eo esse possit perfecta bonitas Dei. Qui quidem defectus consequitur omne bonum creatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0072","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073","text":"Ad quintum ergo dicendum quod causa quae est ex se contingens, oportet quod determinetur ab aliquo exteriori ad effectum. Sed voluntas divina, quae ex se necessitatem habet, determinat seipsam ad volitum, ad quod habet habitudinem non necessariam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0074","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod, sicut divinum esse in se est necessarium, ita et divinum velle et divinum scire, sed divinum scire habet necessariam habitudinem ad scita, non autem divinum velle ad volita. Quod ideo est, quia scientia habetur de rebus, secundum quod sunt in sciente, voluntas autem comparatur ad res, secundum quod sunt in seipsis. Quia igitur omnia alia habent necessarium esse secundum quod sunt in Deo; non autem secundum quod sunt in seipsis, habent necessitatem absolutam ita quod sint per seipsa necessaria; propter hoc Deus quaecumque scit, ex necessitate scit, non autem quaecumque vult, ex necessitate vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0076","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0077","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0078","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei non sit causa rerum. Dicit enim Dionysius, cap. IV de Div. Nom., *sicut noster sol, non ratiocinans aut praeeligens, sed per ipsum esse illuminat omnia participare lumen ipsius valentia; ita et bonum divinum per ipsam essentiam omnibus existentibus immittit bonitatis suae radios*. Sed omne quod agit per voluntatem, agit ut ratiocinans et praeeligens. Ergo Deus non agit per voluntatem. Ergo voluntas Dei non est causa rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0079","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0080","text":"Praeterea, id quod est per essentiam, est primum in quolibet ordine sicut in ordine ignitorum est primum, quod est ignis per essentiam sed Deus est primum agens. Ergo est agens per essentiam suam, quae est natura eius. Agit igitur per naturam, et non per voluntatem. Voluntas igitur divina non est causa rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0081","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0082","text":"Praeterea, quidquid est causa alicuius per hoc quod est tale, est causa per naturam, et non per voluntatem, ignis enim causa est calefactionis, quia est calidus; sed artifex est causa domus, quia vult eam facere. Sed Augustinus dicit, in I de Doct. Christ., quod *quia Deus bonus est, sumus*. Ergo Deus per suam naturam est causa rerum, et non per voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0083","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0084","text":"Praeterea, unius rei una est causa. Sed rerum creatarum est causa scientia Dei, ut supra dictum est. Ergo voluntas Dei non debet poni causa rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0085","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0086","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XI, *quomodo posset aliquid permanere, nisi tu voluisses?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0087","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere voluntatem Dei esse causam rerum, et Deum agere per voluntatem, non per necessitatem naturae, ut quidam existimaverunt. Quod quidem apparere potest tripliciter. Primo quidem, ex ipso ordine causarum agentium. Cum enim propter finem agat et intellectus et natura, ut probatur in II Physic., necesse est ut agenti per naturam praedeterminetur finis, et media necessaria ad finem, ab aliquo superiori intellectu; sicut sagittae praedeterminatur finis et certus modus a sagittante. Unde necesse est quod agens per intellectum et voluntatem, sit prius agente per naturam. Unde, cum primum in ordine agentium sit Deus, necesse est quod per intellectum et voluntatem agat. Secundo, ex ratione naturalis agentis, ad quod pertinet ut unum effectum producat, quia natura uno et eodem modo operatur, nisi impediatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0089","text":"Et hoc ideo, quia secundum quod est tale, agit, unde, quandiu est tale, non facit nisi tale. Omne enim agens per naturam, habet esse determinatum. Cum igitur esse divinum non sit determinatum, sed contineat in se totam perfectionem essendi, non potest esse quod agat per necessitatem naturae, nisi forte causaret aliquid indeterminatum et infinitum in essendo; quod est impossibile, ut ex superioribus patet. Non igitur agit per necessitatem naturae sed effectus determinati ab infinita ipsius perfectione procedunt secundum determinationem voluntatis et intellectus ipsius. Tertio, ex habitudine effectuum ad causam. Secundum hoc enim effectus procedunt a causa agente, secundum quod praeexistunt in ea, quia omne agens agit sibi simile. Praeexistunt autem effectus in causa secundum modum causae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090","text":"Unde, cum esse divinum sit ipsum eius intelligere, praeexistunt in eo effectus eius secundum modum intelligibilem. Unde et per modum intelligibilem procedunt ab eo. Et sic, per consequens, per modum voluntatis, nam inclinatio eius ad agendum quod intellectu conceptum est, pertinet ad voluntatem. Voluntas igitur Dei est causa rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0091","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0092","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius per verba illa non intendit excludere electionem a Deo simpliciter, sed secundum quid, inquantum scilicet, non quibusdam solum bonitatem suam communicat, sed omnibus, prout scilicet electio discretionem quandam importat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0093","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0094","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quia essentia Dei est eius intelligere et velle, ex hoc ipso quod per essentiam suam agit, sequitur quod agat per modum intellectus et voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0095","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0096","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod bonum est obiectum voluntatis. Pro tanto ergo dicitur, quia Deus bonus est, sumus, inquantum sua bonitas est ei ratio volendi omnia alia, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0097","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0098","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod unius et eiusdem effectus, etiam in nobis, est causa scientia ut dirigens, qua concipitur forma operis, et voluntas ut imperans, quia forma, ut est in intellectu tantum, non determinatur ad hoc quod sit vel non sit in effectu, nisi per voluntatem. Unde intellectus speculativus nihil dicit de operando. Sed potentia est causa ut exequens, quia nominat immediatum principium operationis. Sed haec omnia in Deo unum sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0099","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0100","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0101","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntatis divinae sit assignare aliquam causam. Dicit enim Augustinus, libro octoginta trium quaest., *quis audeat dicere Deum irrationabiliter omnia condidisse?* Sed agenti voluntario, quod est ratio operandi, est etiam causa volendi. Ergo voluntas Dei habet aliquam causam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0102","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0103","text":"Praeterea, in his quae fiunt a volente qui propter nullam causam aliquid vult, non oportet aliam causam assignare nisi voluntatem volentis. Sed voluntas Dei est causa omnium rerum, ut ostensum est. Si igitur voluntatis eius non sit aliqua causa, non oportebit in omnibus rebus naturalibus aliam causam quaerere, nisi solam voluntatem divinam. Et sic omnes scientiae essent supervacuae, quae causas aliquorum effectuum assignare nituntur, quod videtur inconveniens. Est igitur assignare aliquam causam voluntatis divinae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0104","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0105","text":"Praeterea, quod fit a volente non propter aliquam causam, dependet ex simplici voluntate eius. Si igitur voluntas Dei non habeat aliquam causam, sequitur quod omnia quae fiunt, dependeant ex simplici eius voluntate, et non habeant aliquam aliam causam. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0106","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0107","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *omnis causa efficiens maior est eo quod efficitur; nihil tamen maius est voluntate Dei; non ergo causa eius quaerenda est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0108","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0109","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nullo modo voluntas Dei causam habet. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum voluntas sequatur intellectum, eodem modo contingit esse causam alicuius volentis ut velit, et alicuius intelligentis ut intelligat. In intellectu autem sic est quod, si seorsum intelligat principium, et seorsum conclusionem, intelligentia principii est causa scientiae conclusionis. Sed si intellectus in ipso principio inspiceret conclusionem, uno intuitu apprehendens utrumque, in eo scientia conclusionis non causaretur ab intellectu principiorum, quia idem non est causa sui ipsius. Sed tamen intelligeret principia esse causas conclusionis. Similiter est ex parte voluntatis, circa quam sic se habet finis ad ea quae sunt ad finem, sicut in intellectu principia ad conclusiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","text":"Unde, si aliquis uno actu velit finem, et alio actu ea quae sunt ad finem, velle finem erit ei causa volendi ea quae sunt ad finem. Sed si uno actu velit finem et ea quae sunt ad finem, hoc esse non poterit, quia idem non est causa sui ipsius. Et tamen erit verum dicere quod velit ordinare ea quae sunt ad finem, in finem. Deus autem, sicut uno actu omnia in essentia sua intelligit, ita uno actu vult omnia in sua bonitate. Unde, sicut in Deo intelligere causam non est causa intelligendi effectus, sed ipse intelligit effectus in causa; ita velle finem non est ei causa volendi ea quae sunt ad finem, sed tamen vult ea quae sunt ad finem, ordinari in finem. Vult ergo hoc esse propter hoc, sed non propter hoc vult hoc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0111","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod voluntas Dei rationabilis est, non quod aliquid sit Deo causa volendi, sed inquantum vult unum esse propter aliud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0113","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum velit Deus effectus sic esse, ut ex causis certis proveniant, ad hoc quod servetur ordo in rebus; non est supervacuum, etiam cum voluntate Dei, alias causas quaerere. Esset tamen supervacuum, si aliae causae quaererentur ut primae, et non dependentes a divina voluntate. Et sic loquitur Augustinus in III de Trin., *placuit vanitati philosophorum etiam aliis causis effectus contingentes tribuere, cum omnino videre non possent superiorem ceteris omnibus causam, idest voluntatem Dei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0115","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0116","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum Deus velit effectus esse propter causas, quicumque effectus praesupponunt aliquem alium effectum, non dependent ex sola Dei voluntate, sed ex aliquo alio. Sed primi effectus ex sola divina voluntate dependent. Utpote si dicamus quod Deus voluit hominem habere manus, ut deservirent intellectui, operando diversa opera, et voluit eum habere intellectum, ad hoc quod esset homo, et voluit eum esse hominem, ut frueretur ipso, vel ad complementum universi. Quae quidem non est reducere ad alios fines creatos ulteriores. Unde huiusmodi dependent ex simplici voluntate Dei, alia vero ex ordine etiam aliarum causarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0117","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0118","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0119","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei non semper impleatur. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Tim. II, quod *Deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri, et ad agnitionem veritatis venire*. Sed hoc non ita evenit. Ergo voluntas Dei non semper impletur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0120","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0121","text":"Praeterea, sicut se habet scientia ad verum, ita voluntas ad bonum. Sed Deus scit omne verum. Ergo vult omne bonum. Sed non omne bonum fit, multa enim bona possunt fieri, quae non fiunt. Non ergo voluntas Dei semper impletur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0122","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0123","text":"Praeterea, voluntas Dei, cum sit causa prima, non excludit causas medias, ut dictum est. Sed effectus causae primae potest impediri per defectum causae secundae, sicut effectus virtutis motivae impeditur propter debilitatem tibiae. Ergo et effectus divinae voluntatis potest impediri propter defectum secundarum causarum. Non ergo voluntas Dei semper impletur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0124","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0125","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CXIII, *omnia quaecumque voluit Deus, fecit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0126","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0127","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est voluntatem Dei semper impleri. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum effectus conformetur agenti secundum suam formam, eadem ratio est in causis agentibus, quae est in causis formalibus. In formis autem sic est quod, licet aliquid possit deficere ab aliqua forma particulari, tamen a forma universali nihil deficere potest, potest enim esse aliquid quod non est homo vel vivum, non autem potest esse aliquid quod non sit ens. Unde et hoc idem in causis agentibus contingere oportet. Potest enim aliquid fieri extra ordinem alicuius causae particularis agentis, non autem extra ordinem alicuius causae universalis, sub qua omnes causae particulares comprehenduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","text":"Quia, si aliqua causa particularis deficiat a suo effectu, hoc est propter aliquam aliam causam particularem impedientem, quae continetur sub ordine causae universalis, unde effectus ordinem causae universalis nullo modo potest exire. Et hoc etiam patet in corporalibus. Potest enim impediri quod aliqua stella non inducat suum effectum, sed tamen quicumque effectus ex causa corporea impediente in rebus corporalibus consequatur, oportet quod reducatur per aliquas causas medias in universalem virtutem primi caeli. Cum igitur voluntas Dei sit universalis causa omnium rerum, impossibile est quod divina voluntas suum effectum non consequatur. Unde quod recedere videtur a divina voluntate secundum unum ordinem, relabitur in ipsam secundum alium, sicut peccator, qui, quantum est in se, recedit a divina voluntate peccando, incidit in ordinem divinae voluntatis, dum per eius iustitiam punitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0129","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0130","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod illud verbum apostoli, quod Deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri etc., potest tripliciter intelligi. Uno modo, ut sit accommoda distributio, secundum hunc sensum, Deus vult salvos fieri omnes homines qui salvantur, non quia nullus homo sit quem salvum fieri non velit, sed quia nullus salvus fit, quem non velit salvum fieri, ut dicit Augustinus secundo potest intelligi, ut fiat distributio pro generibus singulorum, et non pro singulis generum, secundum hunc sensum, Deus vult de quolibet statu hominum salvos fieri, mares et feminas, Iudaeos et gentiles, parvos et magnos; non tamen omnes de singulis statibus. Tertio, secundum Damascenum, intelligitur de voluntate antecedente, non de voluntate consequente. Quae quidem distinctio non accipitur ex parte ipsius voluntatis divinae, in qua nihil est prius vel posterius; sed ex parte volitorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0131","text":"Ad cuius intellectum, considerandum est quod unumquodque, secundum quod bonum est, sic est volitum a Deo. Aliquid autem potest esse in prima sui consideratione, secundum quod absolute consideratur, bonum vel malum, quod tamen, prout cum aliquo adiuncto consideratur, quae est consequens consideratio eius, e contrario se habet. Sicut hominem vivere est bonum, et hominem occidi est malum, secundum absolutam considerationem, sed si addatur circa aliquem hominem, quod sit homicida, vel vivens in periculum multitudinis, sic bonum est eum occidi, et malum est eum vivere. Unde potest dici quod iudex iustus antecedenter vult omnem hominem vivere; sed consequenter vult homicidam suspendi. Similiter Deus antecedenter vult omnem hominem salvari; sed consequenter vult quosdam damnari, secundum exigentiam suae iustitiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","text":"Neque tamen id quod antecedenter volumus, simpliciter volumus, sed secundum quid. Quia voluntas comparatur ad res, secundum quod in seipsis sunt, in seipsis autem sunt in particulari, unde simpliciter volumus aliquid, secundum quod volumus illud consideratis omnibus circumstantiis particularibus, quod est consequenter velle. Unde potest dici quod iudex iustus simpliciter vult homicidam suspendi, sed secundum quid vellet eum vivere, scilicet inquantum est homo. Unde magis potest dici velleitas, quam absoluta voluntas. Et sic patet quod quidquid Deus simpliciter vult, fit; licet illud quod antecedenter vult, non fiat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0133","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0134","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod actus cognoscitivae virtutis est secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente, actus autem virtutis appetitivae est ordinatus ad res, secundum quod in seipsis sunt. Quidquid autem potest habere rationem entis et veri, totum est virtualiter in Deo; sed non totum existit in rebus creatis. Et ideo Deus cognoscit omne verum, non tamen vult omne bonum, nisi inquantum vult se, in quo virtualiter omne bonum existit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0135","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0136","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod causa prima tunc potest impediri a suo effectu per defectum causae secundae, quando non est universaliter prima, sub se omnes causas comprehendens, quia sic effectus nullo modo posset suum ordinem evadere. Et sic est de voluntate Dei, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0137","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0138","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0139","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei sit mutabilis. Dicit enim dominus Genes. VI, *poenitet me fecisse hominem*. Sed quemcumque poenitet de eo quod fecit, habet mutabilem voluntatem. Ergo Deus habet mutabilem voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0140","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0141","text":"Praeterea, Ierem. XVIII, ex persona domini dicitur, *loquar adversus gentem et adversus regnum, ut eradicem et destruam et disperdam illud; sed si poenitentiam egerit gens illa a malo suo, agam et ego poenitentiam super malo quod cogitavi ut facerem ei*. Ergo Deus habet mutabilem voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0142","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0143","text":"Praeterea, quidquid Deus facit, voluntarie facit. Sed Deus non semper eadem facit, nam quandoque praecepit legalia observari, quandoque prohibuit. Ergo habet mutabilem voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0144","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0145","text":"Praeterea, Deus non ex necessitate vult quod vult, ut supra dictum est. Ergo potest velle et non velle idem. Sed omne quod habet potentiam ad opposita, est mutabile, sicut quod potest esse et non esse, est mutabile secundum substantiam; et quod potest esse hic et non esse hic, est mutabile secundum locum. Ergo Deus est mutabilis secundum voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0146","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0147","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Num. XXIII, *non est Deus, quasi homo, ut mentiatur; neque ut filius hominis, ut mutetur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0148","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod voluntas Dei est omnino immutabilis. Sed circa hoc considerandum est, quod aliud est mutare voluntatem; et aliud est velle aliquarum rerum mutationem. Potest enim aliquis, eadem voluntate immobiliter permanente, velle quod nunc fiat hoc, et postea fiat contrarium. Sed tunc voluntas mutaretur, si aliquis inciperet velle quod prius non voluit, vel desineret velle quod voluit. Quod quidem accidere non potest, nisi praesupposita mutatione vel ex parte cognitionis, vel circa dispositionem substantiae ipsius volentis. Cum enim voluntas sit boni, aliquis de novo dupliciter potest incipere aliquid velle. Uno modo sic, quod de novo incipiat sibi illud esse bonum. Quod non est absque mutatione eius, sicut adveniente frigore, incipit esse bonum sedere ad ignem, quod prius non erat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0150","text":"Alio modo sic, quod de novo cognoscat illud esse sibi bonum, cum prius hoc ignorasset, ad hoc enim consiliamur, ut sciamus quid nobis sit bonum. Ostensum est autem supra quod tam substantia Dei quam eius scientia est omnino immutabilis. Unde oportet voluntatem eius omnino esse immutabilem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0151","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0152","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod illud verbum domini metaphorice intelligendum est, secundum similitudinem nostram, cum enim nos poenitet, destruimus quod fecimus. Quamvis hoc esse possit absque mutatione voluntatis, cum etiam aliquis homo, absque mutatione voluntatis, interdum velit aliquid facere, simul intendens postea illud destruere. Sic igitur Deus poenituisse dicitur, secundum similitudinem operationis, inquantum hominem quem fecerat, per diluvium a facie terrae delevit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0153","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0154","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod voluntas Dei, cum sit causa prima et universalis, non excludit causas medias, in quarum virtute est ut aliqui effectus producantur. Sed quia omnes causae mediae non adaequant virtutem causae primae, multa sunt in virtute et scientia et voluntate divina, quae non continentur sub ordine causarum inferiorum; sicut resuscitatio Lazari. Unde aliquis respiciens ad causas inferiores, dicere poterat, Lazarus non resurget, respiciens vero ad causam primam divinam, poterat dicere, Lazarus resurget. Et utrumque horum Deus vult, scilicet quod aliquid quandoque sit futurum secundum causam inferiorem, quod tamen futurum non sit secundum causam superiorem; vel e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0155","text":"Sic ergo dicendum est quod Deus aliquando pronuntiat aliquid futurum, secundum quod continetur in ordine causarum inferiorum, ut puta secundum dispositionem naturae vel meritorum; quod tamen non fit, quia aliter est in causa superiori divina. Sicut cum praedixit Ezechiae, *dispone domui tuae, quia morieris et non vives*, ut habetur Isaiae XXXVIII; neque tamen ita evenit, quia ab aeterno aliter fuit in scientia et voluntate divina, quae immutabilis est. Propter quod dicit Gregorius, quod *Deus immutat sententiam, non tamen mutat consilium*, scilicet voluntatis suae. Quod ergo dicit, poenitentiam agam ego, intelligitur metaphorice dictum, nam homines quando non implent quod comminati sunt, poenitere videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0156","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0157","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ex ratione illa non potest concludi quod Deus habeat mutabilem voluntatem; sed quod mutationem velit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0158","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0159","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, licet Deum velle aliquid non sit necessarium absolute, tamen necessarium est ex suppositione, propter immutabilitatem divinae voluntatis, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0160","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0161","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0162","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei rebus volitis necessitatem imponat. Dicit enim Augustinus, in Enchirid., *nullus fit salvus, nisi quem Deus voluerit salvari. Et ideo rogandus est ut velit, quia necesse est fieri, si voluerit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0163","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0164","text":"Praeterea, omnis causa quae non potest impediri, ex necessitate suum effectum producit, quia et natura semper idem operatur, nisi aliquid impediat, ut dicitur in II Physic. Sed voluntas Dei non potest impediri, dicit enim apostolus, ad Rom. IX, *voluntati enim eius quis resistit?* Ergo voluntas Dei imponit rebus volitis necessitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0165","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0166","text":"Praeterea, illud quod habet necessitatem ex priori, est necessarium absolute, sicut animal mori est necessarium, quia est ex contrariis compositum. Sed res creatae a Deo, comparantur ad voluntatem divinam sicut ad aliquid prius, a quo habent necessitatem, cum haec conditionalis sit vera, si aliquid Deus vult, illud est; omnis autem conditionalis vera est necessaria. Sequitur ergo quod omne quod Deus vult, sit necessarium absolute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0167","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0168","text":"Sed contra, omnia bona quae fiunt, Deus vult fieri. Si igitur eius voluntas imponat rebus volitis necessitatem, sequitur quod omnia bona ex necessitate eveniunt. Et sic perit liberum arbitrium et consilium, et omnia huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0169","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0170","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod divina voluntas quibusdam volitis necessitatem imponit, non autem omnibus. Cuius quidem rationem aliqui assignare voluerunt ex causis mediis quia ea quae producit per causas necessarias, sunt necessaria; ea vero quae producit per causas contingentes, sunt contingentia. Sed hoc non videtur sufficienter dictum, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia effectus alicuius primae causae est contingens propter causam secundam, ex eo quod impeditur effectus causae primae per defectum causae secundae; sicut virtus solis per defectum plantae impeditur. Nullus autem defectus causae secundae impedire potest quin voluntas Dei effectum suum producat. Secundo, quia, si distinctio contingentium a necessariis referatur solum in causas secundas, sequitur hoc esse praeter intentionem et voluntatem divinam, quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","text":"Et ideo melius dicendum est, quod hoc contingit propter efficaciam divinae voluntatis. Cum enim aliqua causa efficax fuerit ad agendum, effectus consequitur causam non tantum secundum id quod fit, sed etiam secundum modum fiendi vel essendi, ex debilitate enim virtutis activae in semine, contingit quod filius nascitur dissimilis patri in accidentibus, quae pertinent ad modum essendi. Cum igitur voluntas divina sit efficacissima, non solum sequitur quod fiant ea quae Deus vult fieri; sed quod eo modo fiant, quo Deus ea fieri vult. Vult autem quaedam fieri Deus necessario, et quaedam contingenter, ut sit ordo in rebus, ad complementum universi. Et ideo quibusdam effectibus aptavit causas necessarias, quae deficere non possunt, ex quibus effectus de necessitate proveniunt, quibusdam autem aptavit causas contingentes defectibiles, ex quibus effectus contingenter eveniunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172","text":"Non igitur propterea effectus voliti a Deo, eveniunt contingenter, quia causae proximae sunt contingentes, sed propterea quia Deus voluit eos contingenter evenire, contingentes causas ad eos praeparavit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0173","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0174","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod per illud verbum Augustini intelligenda est necessitas in rebus volitis a Deo, non absoluta, sed conditionalis, necesse est enim hanc conditionalem veram esse, si Deus hoc vult, necesse est hoc esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0175","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0176","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, ex hoc ipso quod nihil voluntati divinae resistit, sequitur quod non solum fiant ea quae Deus vult fieri; sed quod fiant contingenter vel necessario, quae sic fieri vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0177","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0178","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod posteriora habent necessitatem a prioribus, secundum modum priorum. Unde et ea quae fiunt a voluntate divina, talem necessitatem habent, qualem Deus vult ea habere, scilicet, vel absolutam, vel conditionalem tantum. Et sic, non omnia sunt necessaria absolute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0179","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0180","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0181","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Dei sit malorum. Omne enim bonum quod fit, Deus vult. Sed mala fieri bonum est, dicit enim Augustinus, in Enchirid., *quamvis ea quae mala sunt, inquantum mala sunt, non sint bona; tamen, ut non solum bona, sed etiam ut sint mala, bonum est*. Ergo Deus vult mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0182","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0183","text":"Praeterea, dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., erit malum ad omnis (idest universi) perfectionem conferens. Et Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., *ex omnibus consistit universitatis admirabilis pulchritudo; in qua etiam illud quod malum dicitur, bene ordinatum, et loco suo positum, eminentius commendat bona; ut magis placeant, et laudabiliora sint, dum comparantur malis*. Sed Deus vult omne illud quod pertinet ad perfectionem et decorem universi, quia hoc est quod Deus maxime vult in creaturis. Ergo Deus vult mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0184","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0185","text":"Praeterea, mala fieri, et non fieri, sunt contradictorie opposita. Sed Deus non vult mala non fieri, quia, cum mala quaedam fiant, non semper voluntas Dei impleretur. Ergo Deus vult mala fieri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0186","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0187","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *nullo sapiente homine auctore, fit homo deterior; est autem Deus omni sapiente homine praestantior; multo igitur minus, Deo auctore, fit aliquis deterior. Illo autem auctore cum dicitur, illo volente dicitur*. Non ergo volente Deo, fit homo deterior. Constat autem quod quolibet malo fit aliquid deterius. Ergo Deus non vult mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0188","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum ratio boni sit ratio appetibilis, ut supra dictum est, malum autem opponatur bono; impossibile est quod aliquod malum, inquantum huiusmodi, appetatur, neque appetitu naturali, neque animali, neque intellectuali, qui est voluntas. Sed aliquod malum appetitur per accidens, inquantum consequitur ad aliquod bonum. Et hoc apparet in quolibet appetitu. Non enim agens naturale intendit privationem vel corruptionem; sed formam, cui coniungitur privatio alterius formae; et generationem unius, quae est corruptio alterius. Leo etiam, occidens cervum, intendit cibum, cui coniungitur occisio animalis. Similiter fornicator intendit delectationem, cui coniungitur deformitas culpae. Malum autem quod coniungitur alicui bono, est privatio alterius boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","text":"Nunquam igitur appeteretur malum, nec per accidens, nisi bonum cui coniungitur malum, magis appeteretur quam bonum quod privatur per malum. Nullum autem bonum Deus magis vult quam suam bonitatem, vult tamen aliquod bonum magis quam aliud quoddam bonum. Unde malum culpae, quod privat ordinem ad bonum divinum, Deus nullo modo vult. Sed malum naturalis defectus, vel malum poenae vult, volendo aliquod bonum, cui coniungitur tale malum, sicut, volendo iustitiam, vult poenam; et volendo ordinem naturae servari, vult quaedam naturaliter corrumpi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0191","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0192","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod, licet Deus non velit mala, vult tamen mala esse vel fieri, quia, licet mala non sint bona, bonum tamen est mala esse vel fieri. Quod ideo dicebant, quia ea quae in se mala sunt, ordinantur ad aliquod bonum, quem quidem ordinem importari credebant in hoc quod dicitur, mala esse vel fieri. Sed hoc non recte dicitur. Quia malum non ordinatur ad bonum per se, sed per accidens. Praeter intentionem enim peccantis est, quod ex hoc sequatur aliquod bonum; sicut praeter intentionem tyrannorum fuit, quod ex eorum persecutionibus claresceret patientia martyrum. Et ideo non potest dici quod talis ordo ab bonum importetur per hoc quod dicitur, quod malum esse vel fieri sit bonum, quia nihil iudicatur secundum illud quod competit ei per accidens, sed secundum illud quod competit ei per se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0193","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0194","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod malum non operatur ad perfectionem et decorem universi nisi per accidens, ut dictum est. Unde et hoc quod dicit Dionysius, quod malum est ad universi perfectionem conferens, concludit inducendo quasi ad inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0195","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet mala fieri, et mala non fieri, contradictorie opponantur; tamen velle mala fieri, et velle mala non fieri, non opponuntur contradictorie, cum utrumque sit affirmativum. Deus igitur neque vult mala fieri, neque vult mala non fieri, sed vult permittere mala fieri. Et hoc est bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0197","text":"## Articulus 10","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0198","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0199","text":"Ad decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non habeat liberum arbitrium. Dicit enim Hieronymus, in homilia de filio prodigo, *solus Deus est, in quem peccatum non cadit, nec cadere potest; cetera, cum sint liberi arbitrii, in utramque partem flecti possunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0200","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0201","text":"Praeterea, liberum arbitrium est facultas rationis et voluntatis, qua bonum et malum eligitur. Sed Deus non vult malum, ut dictum est. Ergo liberum arbitrium non est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0202","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0203","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Ambrosius, in libro de fide, *spiritus sanctus dividit singulis prout vult, idest pro liberae voluntatis arbitrio, non necessitatis obsequio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0204","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod liberum arbitrium habemus respectu eorum quae non necessario volumus, vel naturali instinctu. Non enim ad liberum arbitrium pertinet quod volumus esse felices, sed ad naturalem instinctum. Unde et alia animalia, quae naturali instinctu moventur ad aliquid, non dicuntur libero arbitrio moveri. Cum igitur Deus ex necessitate suam bonitatem velit, alia vero non ex necessitate, ut supra ostensum est; respectu illorum quae non ex necessitate vult, liberum arbitrium habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0206","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Hieronymus videtur excludere a Deo liberum arbitrium, non simpliciter, sed solum quantum ad hoc quod est deflecti in peccatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0208","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0209","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum malum culpae dicatur per aversionem a bonitate divina, per quam Deus omnia vult, ut supra ostensum est, manifestum est quod impossibile est eum malum culpae velle. Et tamen ad opposita se habet, inquantum velle potest hoc esse vel non esse. Sicut et nos, non peccando, possumus velle sedere, et non velle sedere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0210","text":"## Articulus 11","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0211","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0212","text":"Ad undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit distinguenda in Deo voluntas signi. Sicut enim voluntas Dei est causa rerum, ita et scientia. Sed non assignantur aliqua signa ex parte divinae scientiae. Ergo neque debent assignari aliqua signa ex parte divinae voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0213","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0214","text":"Praeterea, omne signum quod non concordat ei cuius est signum, est falsum. Si igitur signa quae assignantur circa voluntatem divinam, non concordant divinae voluntati, sunt falsa, si autem concordant, superflue assignantur. Non igitur sunt aliqua signa circa voluntatem divinam assignanda.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0215","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0216","text":"Sed contra est quod voluntas Dei est una, cum ipsa sit Dei essentia. Quandoque autem pluraliter significatur, ut cum dicitur, *magna opera domini, exquisita in omnes voluntates eius*. Ergo oportet quod aliquando signum voluntatis pro voluntate accipiatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0217","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in Deo quaedam dicuntur proprie, et quaedam secundum metaphoram, ut ex supradictis patet. Cum autem aliquae passiones humanae in divinam praedicationem metaphorice assumuntur, hoc fit secundum similitudinem effectus, unde illud quod est signum talis passionis in nobis, in Deo nomine illius passionis metaphorice significatur. Sicut, apud nos, irati punire consueverunt, unde ipsa punitio est signum irae, et propter hoc, ipsa punitio nomine irae significatur, cum Deo attribuitur. Similiter id quod solet esse in nobis signum voluntatis, quandoque metaphorice in Deo voluntas dicitur. Sicut, cum aliquis praecipit aliquid, signum est quod velit illud fieri, unde praeceptum divinum quandoque metaphorice voluntas Dei dicitur, secundum illud Matth. VI, *fiat voluntas tua, sicut in caelo et in terra*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","text":"Sed hoc distat inter voluntatem et iram, quia ira de Deo nunquam proprie dicitur, cum in suo principali intellectu includat passionem, voluntas autem proprie de Deo dicitur. Et ideo in Deo distinguitur voluntas proprie, et metaphorice dicta. Voluntas enim proprie dicta, vocatur voluntas beneplaciti, voluntas autem metaphorice dicta, est voluntas signi, eo quod ipsum signum voluntatis voluntas dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0220","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0221","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod scientia non est causa eorum quae fiunt, nisi per voluntatem, non enim quae scimus facimus, nisi velimus. Et ideo signum non attribuitur scientiae, sicut attribuitur voluntati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0222","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod signa voluntatis dicuntur voluntates divinae, non quia sint signa quod Deus velit, sed quia ea quae in nobis solent esse signa volendi, in Deo divinae voluntates dicuntur. Sicut punitio non est signum quod in Deo sit ira, sed punitio, ex eo ipso quod in nobis est signum irae, in Deo dicitur ira.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0224","text":"## Articulus 12","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0225","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0226","text":"Ad duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter circa divinam voluntatem ponantur quinque signa, scilicet, prohibitio, praeceptum, consilium, operatio et permissio. Nam eadem quae nobis praecipit Deus vel consulit, in nobis quandoque operatur, et eadem quae prohibet, quandoque permittit. Ergo non debent ex opposito dividi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0227","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0228","text":"Praeterea, nihil Deus operatur, nisi volens, ut dicitur Sap. XI. Sed voluntas signi distinguitur a voluntate beneplaciti. Ergo operatio sub voluntate signi comprehendi non debet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0229","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0230","text":"Praeterea, operatio et permissio communiter ad omnes creaturas pertinent, quia in omnibus Deus operatur, et in omnibus aliquid fieri permittit. Sed praeceptum, consilium et prohibitio pertinent ad solam rationalem creaturam. Ergo non veniunt convenienter in unam divisionem, cum non sint unius ordinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0231","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0232","text":"Praeterea, malum pluribus modis contingit quam bonum, quia bonum contingit uno modo, sed malum omnifariam, ut patet per philosophum in II Ethic., et per Dionysium in IV cap. de Div. Nom. Inconvenienter igitur respectu mali assignatur unum signum tantum, scilicet prohibitio; respectu vero boni, duo signa, scilicet consilium et praeceptum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0233","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod huiusmodi signa voluntatis dicuntur ea, quibus consuevimus demonstrare nos aliquid velle. Potest autem aliquis declarare se velle aliquid, vel per seipsum, vel per alium. Per seipsum quidem, inquantum facit aliquid, vel directe, vel indirecte et per accidens. Directe quidem, cum per se aliquid operatur, et quantum ad hoc, dicitur esse signum operatio. Indirecte autem, inquantum non impedit operationem, nam removens prohibens dicitur movens per accidens, ut dicitur in VIII Physic. Et quantum ad hoc, dicitur signum permissio. Per alium autem declarat se aliquid velle, inquantum ordinat alium ad aliquid faciendum; vel necessaria inductione, quod fit praecipiendo quod quis vult, et prohibendo contrarium; vel aliqua persuasoria inductione, quod pertinet ad consilium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0235","text":"Quia igitur his modis declaratur aliquem velle aliquid, propter hoc ista quinque nominantur interdum nomine voluntatis divinae, tanquam signa voluntatis. Quod enim praeceptum, consilium et prohibitio dicantur Dei voluntas, patet per id quod dicitur Matth. VI *fiat voluntas tua, sicut in caelo et in terra*. Quod autem permissio vel operatio dicantur Dei voluntas patet per Augustinum, qui dicit in Enchirid., *nihil fit, nisi omnipotens fieri velit, vel sinendo ut fiat, vel faciendo*. Vel potest dici quod permissio et operatio referuntur ad praesens permissio quidem ad malum, operatio vero ad bonum. Ad futurum vero, prohibitio, respectu mali; respectu vero boni necessarii, praeceptum; respectu vero superabundantis boni, consilium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0236","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0237","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod nihil prohibet, circa eandem rem, aliquem diversimode declarare se aliquid velle, sicut inveniuntur multa nomina idem significantia. Unde nihil prohibet idem subiacere praecepto et consilio et operationi, et prohibitioni vel permissioni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0238","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Deus potest significari metaphorice velle id quod non vult voluntate proprie accepta, ita potest metaphorice significari velle id quod proprie vult. Unde nihil prohibet de eodem esse voluntatem beneplaciti, et voluntatem signi. Sed operatio semper est eadem cum voluntate beneplaciti, non autem praeceptum vel consilium, tum quia haec est de praesenti, illud de futuro; tum quia haec per se est effectus voluntatis, illud autem per alium, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0240","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0241","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod creatura rationalis est domina sui actus, et ideo circa ipsam specialia quaedam signa divinae voluntatis assignantur, inquantum rationalem creaturam Deus ordinat ad agendum voluntarie et per se. Sed aliae creaturae non agunt nisi motae ex operatione divina, et ideo circa alias non habent locum nisi operatio et permissio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0242","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0243","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod omne malum culpae, licet multipliciter contingat, tamen in hoc convenit, quod discordat a voluntate divina et ideo unum signum respectu malorum assignatur, scilicet prohibitio. Sed diversimode bona se habent ad bonitatem divinam. Quia quaedam sunt, sine quibus fruitionem divinae bonitatis consequi non possumus, et respectu horum est praeceptum. Quaedam vero sunt, quibus perfectius consequimur, et respectu horum est consilium. Vel dicendum quod consilium est non solum de melioribus bonis assequendis, sed etiam de minoribus malis vitandis.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"80ff5b48813876345b85b1c94ffbc85b6975674b517577a9d0b6b3ed6e8dfbc2","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T135956103069Z_fe6d023a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 19","source_sha256":"80ff5b48813876345b85b1c94ffbc85b6975674b517577a9d0b6b3ed6e8dfbc2","sha256":"f03222098aed35c76b2f7679903f04b2b54df8f0b5936a4d5d6ff63ecf1bf2c8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T13:59:56.103083+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0002","text":"[I q. 19 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","text":"After the consideration of those things that pertain to divine knowledge, consideration must be given to those that pertain to the divine will: so that the first consideration may be of God's will itself; the second, of those things that pertain to the will absolutely; the third, of those things that pertain to the intellect in relation to the will. Concerning the will itself, moreover, twelve questions are asked. First, whether there is will in God. Second, whether God wills things other than himself. Third, whether God wills, of necessity, whatever he wills. Fourth, whether God's will is the cause of things. Fifth, whether any cause is to be assigned for the divine will. Sixth, whether the divine will is always fulfilled. Seventh, whether God's will is mutable. Eighth, whether God's will imposes necessity upon the things willed. Ninth, whether there is will of evils in God. Tenth, whether God has free choice. Eleventh, whether a will of sign is to be distinguished in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","text":"Twelfth, whether five signs are suitably posited concerning the divine will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0006","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0007","text":"We proceed to the first as follows. It seems that there is no will in God. For the object of the will is an end and a good. But no end is to be assigned to God. Therefore, will is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0008","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0009","text":"Further, will is a certain appetite. But appetite, since it is for a thing not possessed, signifies imperfection, which does not befit God. Therefore, will is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0010","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0011","text":"Further, according to the Philosopher, in *On the Soul* III, will is a moved mover. But God is the first unmoved mover, as is proved in *Physics* VIII. Therefore, there is no will in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0012","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0013","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, Rom. XII, *that you may prove what is the will of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0014","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","text":"I answer that it must be said that there is will in God, just as there is intellect in him; for will follows intellect. For just as a natural thing has actual being through its form, so the intellect understands actually through its intelligible form. Now every thing has this relation to its natural form: that when it does not have it, it tends toward it; and when it has it, it rests in it. And the same holds concerning every natural perfection, which is a good of nature. This relation to the good, in things lacking cognition, is called natural appetite. Hence an intellectual nature likewise has a similar relation to the good apprehended through an intelligible form: namely, that when it has it, it rests in it; but when it does not have it, it seeks it. And both belong to will. Hence in everything having intellect, there is will; just as in everything having sense, there is animal appetite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","text":"And thus there must be will in God, since there is intellect in him. And just as his understanding is his being, so too is his willing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0017","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although nothing other than God is God's end, nevertheless he himself is the end in relation to all things that are made by him. And this through his essence, since through his essence he is good, as was shown above; for an end has the character of good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0019","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","text":"To the second it must be said that will in us belongs to the appetitive part, which, although it is named from appetizing, nevertheless does not have only this act, namely, to seek what it does not have; but also to love what it has and to delight in it. And with respect to this, will is posited in God, which always has the good that is its object, since it is not distinct from him in essence, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0021","text":"[I q. 19 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022","text":"To the third it must be said that a will whose principal object is a good external to the will must be moved by something. But the object of the divine will is his goodness, which is his essence. Hence, since God's will is his essence, it is not moved by another distinct from itself, but only by itself, in that manner of speaking by which understanding and willing are called motion. And according to this Plato said that the first mover moves itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0024","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God does not will things other than himself. For the divine willing is his being. But God is not other than himself. Therefore, he does not will something other than himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0026","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0027","text":"Moreover, the thing willed moves the will, just as the appetible moves the appetite, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. Therefore, if God wills something other than himself, his will will be moved by something else, which is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0028","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0029","text":"Moreover, a will for which some willed thing suffices seeks nothing beyond it. But God's goodness suffices for God, and his will is satisfied by it. Therefore, God does not will something other than himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0030","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0031","text":"Moreover, the act of the will is multiplied according to the things willed. Therefore, if God wills himself and things other than himself, it follows that the act of his will is multiple, and consequently his being, which is his willing. But this is impossible. Therefore, he does not will things other than himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0032","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0033","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, 1 Thess. IV, *this is the will of God, your sanctification*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0034","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","text":"I answer that it must be said that God wills not only himself, but also things other than himself. This is apparent from the likeness introduced previously. For a natural thing has not only a natural inclination with respect to its own good, so as to acquire it when it does not have it, or to rest in it when it does have it; but also to diffuse its own good into others, insofar as this is possible. Hence we see that every agent, insofar as it is actual and perfect, makes something like itself. Thus this also belongs to the character of will: that one communicate to others the good one has, insofar as this is possible. And this belongs especially to the divine will, from which every perfection is derived by a certain likeness. Therefore, if natural things, insofar as they are perfect, communicate their good to others, much more does it belong to the divine will to communicate its good to others by likeness, insofar as this is possible. Thus, therefore, he wills both himself to be and other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","text":"But he wills himself as an end, and other things as ordered to the end, insofar as it befits the divine goodness that other things too partake of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0037","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although divine willing is his being in reality, nevertheless it differs in account, according to a different mode of understanding and signifying, as is clear from what was said above. For in saying that God is, no relation to something is implied, as it is in saying that God wills. And therefore, although he is not something other than himself, he nevertheless wills something other than himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0039","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0040","text":"To the second, it must be said that in those things which we will for the sake of an end, the entire reason for moving is the end, and this is what moves the will. This is especially apparent in those things which we will only for the sake of an end. For one who wishes to take a bitter potion wills nothing in it except health, and this alone moves his will. But it is otherwise with one who takes a sweet potion, which one can will not only for the sake of health, but also for its own sake. Hence, since God wills things other than himself only for the sake of the end which is his goodness, as was said, it does not follow that anything other than his goodness moves his will. And thus, just as he understands things other than himself by understanding his own essence, so he wills things other than himself by willing his own goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0041","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0042","text":"To the third, it must be said that from the fact that his own goodness suffices for the divine will, it does not follow that he wills nothing else, but that he wills nothing else except by reason of his goodness. Just as the divine intellect, although it is perfect precisely because it knows the divine essence, nevertheless knows other things in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0043","text":"[I q. 19 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, just as divine understanding is one, because it does not see many things except in one; so divine willing is one and simple, because it does not will many things except through one, which is its goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0045","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0046","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0047","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that whatever God wills, he wills of necessity. For everything eternal is necessary. But whatever God wills, he wills from eternity; otherwise, his will would be changeable. Therefore, whatever he wills, he wills of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0048","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0049","text":"Moreover, God wills things other than Himself insofar as He wills His goodness. But God wills His goodness of necessity. Therefore, He wills things other than Himself of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0050","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0051","text":"Moreover, whatever is natural to God is necessary, because God is through Himself necessary being, and the principle of all necessity, as was shown above. But it is natural to Him to will whatever He wills, because in God nothing can be besides nature, as is said in Metaphysics V. Therefore, whatever He wills, He wills of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0052","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0053","text":"Moreover, not to be necessary and possibly not to be are equivalent. Therefore, if it is not necessary that God will something of those things which He wills, it is possible for Him not to will it; and it is possible for Him to will that which He does not will. Therefore, the divine will is contingent toward either alternative. And thus imperfect, because every contingent thing is imperfect and mutable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0054","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0055","text":"Moreover, from that which is toward either alternative no action follows, unless it is inclined to one by something else, as the Commentator says in Physics II. Therefore, if the will of God is toward either alternative in some things, it follows that it is determined to an effect by something else. And thus it has some prior cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0056","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0057","text":"Moreover, whatever God knows, He knows of necessity. But just as divine knowledge is His essence, so too is the divine will. Therefore, whatever God wills, He wills of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0058","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0059","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, Eph. I, *who works all things according to the counsel of His will*. But what we do by the counsel of will, we do not will by necessity. Therefore, God does not will whatever He wills by necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0060","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","text":"I answer that it must be said that something is called necessary in two ways: namely, absolutely and by supposition. Something is judged necessary absolutely from the relation of its terms, for example because the predicate is in the definition of the subject, as it is necessary that man be an animal; or because the subject belongs to the account of the predicate, as this is necessary: that a number be even or odd. But that Socrates sits is not necessary in this way. Hence it is not necessary absolutely, but can be called necessary by supposition; for, supposing that he sits, it is necessary that he sit while he sits. Therefore, concerning things willed by God, this must be considered: that God's willing something is necessary absolutely; yet this is not true of all that He wills. For the divine will has a necessary relation to His goodness, which is its proper object.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","text":"Hence God necessarily wills that His goodness be; just as our will necessarily wills happiness. So too every other power has a necessary relation to its proper and principal object, as sight to color; because it belongs to its very account that it tend toward it. But God wills things other than Himself insofar as they are ordered to His goodness as to an end. Yet we do not necessarily will things that are for an end when we will the end, unless they are such things without which the end cannot be, as we will food when willing the preservation of life, and a ship when willing to cross the sea. But we do not will of necessity things without which the end can be, such as a horse for walking, because without this we can go; and the same account holds in other cases. Hence, since the goodness of God is perfect and can be without other things, since no perfection accrues to it from other things, it follows that His willing things other than Himself is not necessary absolutely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","text":"And yet it is necessary by supposition; for, supposing that He wills, He cannot not will, because His will cannot be changed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0064","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that from the fact that God eternally wills something, it does not follow that it is necessary that He will it, except by supposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0066","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0067","text":"To the second it must be said that, although God necessarily wills His goodness, He does not nevertheless necessarily will those things which He wills on account of His goodness, because His goodness can be without other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0068","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0069","text":"To the third it must be said that it is not natural to God to will any of the other things which He does not will of necessity. Yet neither is it unnatural, or contrary to nature, but voluntary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0070","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0071","text":"To the fourth it must be said that sometimes a necessary cause has a non-necessary relation to some effect, and this is because of a defect of the effect, not because of a defect of the cause. Thus the power of the sun has a non-necessary relation to some of those things that happen here contingently, not because of a defect of solar power, but because of a defect of the effect, which does not proceed necessarily from the cause. And similarly, that God does not will of necessity something of those things which He wills does not occur from a defect of the divine will, but from a defect that belongs to the thing willed according to its account: namely, because it is such that the perfect goodness of God can be without it. This defect indeed follows every created good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0072","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073","text":"To the fifth, therefore, it must be said that a cause which is contingent in itself must be determined by something external to an effect. But the divine will, which has necessity in itself, determines itself to the thing willed, toward which it has a non-necessary relation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0074","text":"[I q. 19 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075","text":"To the sixth it must be said that, just as the divine being is necessary in itself, so too are the divine willing and the divine knowing; but divine knowing has a necessary relation to the things known, whereas divine willing does not have such a relation to the things willed. This is so because knowledge is had of things according as they are in the knower, whereas will is related to things according as they are in themselves. Therefore, because all other things have necessary being according as they are in God, but not according as they are in themselves, so as to have absolute necessity such that they are necessary through themselves, for this reason God knows necessarily whatever He knows, but does not will necessarily whatever He wills.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0076","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0077","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0078","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that the will of God is not the cause of things. For Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, “just as our sun, not reasoning or choosing beforehand, but by its very being, illumines all things capable of participating in its light, so too the divine good, through its very essence, sends forth the rays of its goodness into all existing things.” But everything that acts through will acts as reasoning and choosing beforehand. Therefore God does not act through will. Therefore the will of God is not the cause of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0079","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0080","text":"Furthermore, that which is through essence is first in any order, just as in the order of fiery things that which is fire through essence is first. But God is the first agent. Therefore He is an agent through His essence, which is His nature. Therefore He acts through nature and not through will. Therefore the divine will is not the cause of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0081","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0082","text":"Furthermore, whatever is the cause of something by the fact that it is such is a cause through nature and not through will; for fire is the cause of heating because it is hot, whereas an artisan is the cause of a house because he wills to make it. But Augustine says, in I *On Christian Doctrine*, that “because God is good, we are.” Therefore God is the cause of things through His nature and not through will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0083","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0084","text":"Furthermore, one thing has one cause. But the knowledge of God is the cause of created things, as was said above. Therefore the will of God ought not to be posited as the cause of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0085","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0086","text":"But to the contrary is what is said in Wisdom XI: “How could anything endure unless you had willed it?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0087","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088","text":"I answer that it must be said that the will of God is the cause of things, and that God acts through will, not through necessity of nature, as some have thought. This can be made clear in three ways. First, from the very order of agent causes. For since both intellect and nature act for an end, as is proved in Physics II, it is necessary that for an agent acting through nature, the end and the means necessary for the end be predetermined by some higher intellect; just as the end and a definite manner are predetermined for an arrow by the archer. Hence it is necessary that an agent through intellect and will be prior to an agent through nature. Hence, since God is first in the order of agents, it is necessary that He act through intellect and will. Second, from the nature of a natural agent, to which it belongs to produce one effect, because nature operates in one and the same way unless it is impeded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0089","text":"And this is because it acts according as it is such; hence, so long as it is such, it makes only such a thing. For every agent through nature has determinate being. Therefore, since the divine being is not determinate, but contains in itself the whole perfection of being, it cannot be that it acts through necessity of nature, unless perhaps it were to cause something indeterminate and infinite in being; which is impossible, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore it does not act through necessity of nature, but determinate effects proceed from its infinite perfection according to the determination of its will and intellect. Third, from the relation of effects to their cause. For effects proceed from an agent cause according as they pre-exist in it, because every agent acts something like itself. But effects pre-exist in a cause according to the mode of the cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090","text":"Hence, since the divine being is His very understanding, His effects pre-exist in Him according to an intelligible mode. Hence they also proceed from Him in an intelligible mode. And thus, consequently, in the mode of will, for His inclination to act what has been conceived by intellect belongs to will. Therefore the will of God is the cause of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0091","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0092","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Dionysius does not intend by those words to exclude choice from God simply, but in a certain respect, namely insofar as He communicates His goodness not only to some but to all, insofar, that is, as choice implies a certain discrimination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0093","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0094","text":"To the second it must be said that, because the essence of God is His understanding and willing, it follows from the very fact that He acts through His essence that He acts in the mode of intellect and will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0095","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0096","text":"To the third it must be said that the good is the object of the will. Therefore it is said, “because God is good, we are,” insofar as His goodness is for Him the reason for willing all other things, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0097","text":"[I q. 19 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0098","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, even in us, knowledge as directing, by which the form of the work is conceived, and the will as commanding, are causes of one and the same effect; because the form, insofar as it is in the intellect alone, is not determined to be or not to be in the effect except through the will. Hence the speculative intellect says nothing about action. But power is a cause as executing, because it names the immediate principle of operation. But all these are one in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0099","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0100","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0101","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that some cause is to be assigned to the divine will. For Augustine says, in the book of Eighty-Three Questions, *who would dare say that God created all things irrationally?* But for a voluntary agent, that which is the reason for acting is also the cause of willing. Therefore God's will has some cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0102","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0103","text":"Moreover, in those things that are done by one who wills something for no cause, no other cause need be assigned save the will of the one willing. But the will of God is the cause of all things, as has been shown. Therefore, if there is no cause of His will, there will be no need to seek any other cause in all natural things besides the divine will alone. And thus all the sciences, which strive to assign causes of certain effects, would be superfluous, which seems unfitting. Therefore some cause of the divine will is to be assigned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0104","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0105","text":"Moreover, what is done by one willing not on account of any cause depends on his simple will. Therefore, if the will of God has no cause, it follows that all things which come to be depend on His simple will and have no other cause. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0106","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0107","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in the book of Eighty-Three Questions, *every efficient cause is greater than that which is effected; yet nothing is greater than the will of God; therefore its cause is not to be sought*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0108","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0109","text":"I answer that it must be said that the will of God has a cause in no way. To make this evident, one must consider that, since the will follows the intellect, it is possible for there to be a cause of someone's willing in the same way as a cause of someone's understanding. Now in the intellect it is so that, if it understands the principle separately and the conclusion separately, understanding of the principle is the cause of knowledge of the conclusion. But if the intellect were to behold the conclusion in the principle itself, apprehending both in one intuition, in it knowledge of the conclusion would not be caused by understanding the principles, because the same thing is not the cause of itself. Yet it would understand the principles to be causes of the conclusion. It is similar on the part of the will, with respect to which the end is related to the things that are for the end as principles are related to conclusions in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","text":"Hence, if someone wills the end in one act and the things that are for the end in another act, his willing the end will be the cause of his willing the things that are for the end. But if he wills the end and the things that are for the end in one act, this cannot be, because the same thing is not the cause of itself. And yet it will be true to say that he wills the things that are for the end to be ordered to the end. But God, just as He understands all things in His essence in one act, so wills all things in His goodness in one act. Hence, just as in God understanding the cause is not the cause of understanding the effects, but He understands the effects in the cause; so willing the end is not for Him the cause of willing the things that are for the end, yet He wills the things that are for the end to be ordered to the end. Therefore He wills this to be on account of that, but He does not will this on account of this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0111","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the will of God is rational, not because something is a cause for God of willing, but insofar as He wills one thing to be on account of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0113","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","text":"To the second, it must be said that, since God wills effects to be such that they proceed from determinate causes, in order that order may be preserved in things, it is not superfluous, even given the will of God, to seek other causes. It would nevertheless be superfluous if other causes were sought as first causes and not as dependent upon the divine will. And thus Augustine speaks in On the Trinity III: *it pleased the vanity of philosophers also to attribute contingent effects to other causes, since they were altogether unable to see the cause higher than all other causes, that is, the will of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0115","text":"[I q. 19 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0116","text":"To the third, it must be said that, since God wills effects to be on account of causes, whatever effects presuppose some other effect do not depend upon God's will alone, but upon something else. But first effects depend upon the divine will alone. For example, if we say that God willed man to have hands, so that they might serve the intellect by performing various works; and willed him to have intellect, so that he might be a man; and willed him to be a man, so that he might enjoy Himself, or for the completion of the universe. These indeed are not reduced to further created ends. Hence things of this kind depend upon the simple will of God, whereas other things depend also upon the order of other causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0117","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0118","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0119","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that the will of God is not always fulfilled. For the Apostle says, in 1 Timothy 2, that *God wills all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth*. But this does not happen thus. Therefore the will of God is not always fulfilled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0120","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0121","text":"Moreover, as knowledge is related to the true, so the will is related to the good. But God knows every truth. Therefore, He wills every good. But not every good comes to be; for many goods can come to be which do not come to be. Therefore, God's will is not always fulfilled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0122","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0123","text":"Moreover, since God's will is the first cause, it does not exclude intermediate causes, as has been said. But the effect of the first cause can be impeded through a defect of the second cause, just as the effect of the motive power is impeded on account of weakness in the shin. Therefore the effect of the divine will also can be impeded on account of a defect in secondary causes. Therefore God's will is not always fulfilled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0124","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0125","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm CXIII, *God has done all things whatsoever He willed*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0126","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0127","text":"I answer that it must be said that God's will is always fulfilled. To make this evident, one must consider that, since an effect is conformed to an agent according to its form, the same account holds in efficient causes as in formal causes. Now in forms it is thus: although something may fall short of some particular form, nevertheless nothing can fall short of the universal form. For there can be something that is not a man or not living, but there cannot be something that is not a being. Hence, the same must occur in efficient causes. For something can come to be outside the order of some particular efficient cause, but not outside the order of some universal cause, under which all particular causes are comprehended.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","text":"For, if some particular cause falls short of its effect, this is on account of some other particular impeding cause, which is contained under the order of the universal cause; hence the effect can in no way depart from the order of the universal cause. This is also evident in corporeal things. For it can be impeded that some star should not produce its effect; nevertheless, whatever effect follows in corporeal things from an impeding corporeal cause must be reduced, through certain intermediate causes, to the universal power of the first heaven. Therefore, since the will of God is the universal cause of all things, it is impossible that the divine will should not attain its effect. Hence, what seems to depart from the divine will according to one order falls back into it according to another; as the sinner, who, so far as lies in himself, departs from the divine will by sinning, falls under the order of the divine will when, by His justice, he is punished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0129","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0130","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the saying of the Apostle, that God wills all men to be saved, etc., can be understood in three ways. In one way, so that it is an accommodated distribution, in this sense: God wills all those men who are saved to be saved—not because there is no man whom He does not will to be saved, but because no one is saved whom He does not will to be saved, as Augustine says. Secondly, it can be understood so that the distribution is made according to the kinds of individuals, and not according to the individuals of the kinds, in this sense: God wills that persons from every state of men be saved—males and females, Jews and Gentiles, small and great—but not all from each state. Thirdly, according to Damascene, it is understood concerning the antecedent will, not concerning the consequent will. This distinction, indeed, is not taken on the part of the divine will itself, in which there is nothing prior or posterior, but on the part of the things willed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0131","text":"For the understanding of this, one must consider that each thing, insofar as it is good, is thus willed by God. But something can be good or bad in its first consideration, insofar as it is considered absolutely, which nevertheless, insofar as it is considered with something added—which is its consequent consideration—stands otherwise to the contrary. Thus, for a man to live is good, and for a man to be killed is evil, according to absolute consideration; but if it be added concerning some man that he is a murderer, or living to the peril of the multitude, then it is good for him to be killed, and evil for him to live. Hence it can be said that a just judge antecedently wills every man to live, but consequently wills a murderer to be hanged. Similarly, God antecedently wills every man to be saved, but consequently wills some to be damned, according to the requirement of His justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","text":"Yet what we will antecedently, we do not will simply, but in a certain respect. For the will is related to things according as they are in themselves; but they are in themselves in the particular. Hence we will something simply insofar as we will it with all the particular circumstances considered, which is to will consequently. Hence it can be said that a just judge simply wills a murderer to be hanged, but in a certain respect he would will him to live, namely, insofar as he is a man. Hence it can more properly be called a velleity than an absolute will. And thus it is clear that whatever God simply wills comes to be, although that which He wills antecedently does not come to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0133","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0134","text":"To the second it must be said that the act of the cognitive power is according as the thing known is in the knower, whereas the act of the appetitive power is ordered to things according as they are in themselves. But whatever can have the character of being and truth is wholly virtually in God; yet not everything exists in created things. And therefore God knows every truth, yet does not will every good, except insofar as He wills Himself, in whom every good exists virtually.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0135","text":"[I q. 19 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0136","text":"To the third it must be said that the first cause can then be impeded from its effect through a defect of the second cause when it is not universally first, comprehending under itself all causes; for thus the effect could in no way escape its order. And this is the case with God's will, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0137","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0138","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0139","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh article. It seems that God's will is mutable. For the Lord says in Genesis VI, *It repents me that I made man*. But whoever repents of what he has done has a mutable will. Therefore God has a mutable will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0140","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0141","text":"Moreover, in Jeremiah XVIII it is said in the person of the Lord: *I will speak against a nation and against a kingdom, that I may root it up and destroy it and scatter it; but if that nation should do penance from its evil, I too will repent of the evil which I thought to do to it*. Therefore God has a mutable will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0142","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0143","text":"Moreover, whatever God does, He does voluntarily. But God does not always do the same things; for sometimes He commanded that the legal observances be observed, at other times He prohibited them. Therefore He has a mutable will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0144","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0145","text":"Moreover, God does not will what He wills by necessity, as was said above. Therefore, He can will and not will the same thing. But everything that has a potency toward opposites is mutable, just as what can be and not be is mutable with respect to substance, and what can be here and not be here is mutable with respect to place. Therefore God is mutable with respect to will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0146","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0147","text":"But to the contrary, it is said in Num. XXIII, *God is not as a man, that He should lie; nor as the son of man, that He should be changed*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0148","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","text":"I answer that it must be said that the will of God is altogether immutable. But concerning this it must be considered that it is one thing to change one's will, and another to will a change in certain things. For someone can, with the same will remaining immovably fixed, will that this be done now, and that the contrary be done afterwards. But then the will would be changed if someone began to will what he had not previously willed, or ceased to will what he had willed. This cannot occur except on the presupposition of a change, either on the part of cognition or concerning the disposition of the substance of the one willing. For, since will is of the good, someone can newly begin to will something in two ways. In one way, insofar as it newly begins to be good for him. This is not without a change in him, just as, when cold arrives, it begins to be good to sit by a fire, whereas it was not so before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0150","text":"In another way, insofar as he newly recognizes it to be good for him, whereas he had previously been ignorant of this; for we deliberate in order that we may know what is good for us. But it was shown above that both the substance of God and His knowledge are altogether immutable. Hence His will must be altogether immutable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0151","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0152","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that word of the Lord is to be understood metaphorically, according to a likeness to us: for when we repent, we destroy what we have made. Although this can occur without a change of will, since even a man, without a change of will, may sometimes will to make something, while at the same time intending afterwards to destroy it. Thus, therefore, God is said to have repented, according to a likeness of operation, insofar as He wiped from the face of the earth by the flood the man whom He had made.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0153","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0154","text":"To the second it must be said that the will of God, since it is the first and universal cause, does not exclude intermediate causes, in whose power it is that certain effects are produced. But because all intermediate causes do not equal the power of the first cause, there are many things in the divine power and knowledge and will that are not contained under the order of lower causes, such as the resurrection of Lazarus. Hence someone looking to the lower causes could say, “Lazarus will not rise again”; but looking to the first divine cause, he could say, “Lazarus will rise again.” And God wills both of these, namely, that something at times will be future according to a lower cause which nevertheless will not be future according to a higher cause; or conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0155","text":"Thus, therefore, it must be said that God sometimes pronounces something to be future according as it is contained in the order of lower causes—for instance, according to the disposition of nature or of merits—which nevertheless does not occur, because it is otherwise in the higher divine cause. Thus, when He foretold to Hezekiah, *set your house in order, for you shall die and not live*, as is found in Isaiah XXXVIII; and yet it did not happen thus, because from eternity it was otherwise in the divine knowledge and will, which is immutable. On this account Gregory says that *God changes the sentence, yet does not change the counsel*, namely, that of His will. Therefore, when He says, “I will repent,” this is understood as said metaphorically; for men, when they do not carry out what they have threatened, seem to repent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0156","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0157","text":"To the third it must be said that from that argument it cannot be concluded that God has a mutable will, but that He wills change.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0158","text":"[I q. 19 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0159","text":"To the fourth it must be said that, although God's willing something is not necessary absolutely, nevertheless it is necessary on the supposition, on account of the immutability of the divine will, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0160","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0161","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0162","text":"The eighth point is approached thus. It seems that the will of God imposes necessity upon the things willed. For Augustine says, in the *Enchiridion*, *no one is saved unless God wills him to be saved. And therefore He must be prayed to that He may will it, because it must happen if He wills it*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0163","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0164","text":"Moreover, every cause that cannot be impeded produces its effect of necessity, because nature too always operates in the same way unless something impedes it, as is said in *Physics* II. But the will of God cannot be impeded, for the Apostle says, at Rom. IX, *for who resists His will?* Therefore the will of God imposes necessity upon the things willed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0165","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0166","text":"Moreover, that which has necessity from something prior is necessary absolutely, just as it is necessary for an animal to die, because it is composed of contraries. But things created by God are related to the divine will as to something prior, from which they have necessity, since this conditional is true: if God wills something, it is; but every true conditional is necessary. Therefore it follows that everything God wills is necessary absolutely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0167","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0168","text":"But to the contrary, God wills all good things that come to pass to come to pass. Therefore, if His will imposes necessity upon the things willed, it follows that all good things happen of necessity. And thus free choice and deliberation, and all such things, perish.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0169","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0170","text":"I answer that it must be said that the divine will imposes necessity on certain things willed, but not on all. Some have wished to assign the reason for this from intermediate causes: because those things which it produces through necessary causes are necessary, whereas those which it produces through contingent causes are contingent. But this does not seem to have been stated sufficiently, for two reasons. First, because the effect of a first cause is contingent on account of a second cause, insofar as the effect of the first cause is impeded through a defect of the second cause; just as the power of the sun is impeded through a defect in the plant. But no defect of a second cause can prevent God's will from producing its effect. Second, because, if the distinction of contingent things from necessary things is referred only to secondary causes, it follows that this is apart from the divine intention and will—which is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","text":"And therefore it must be said more fittingly that this occurs because of the efficacy of the divine will. For when an efficient cause has power to act, the effect follows the cause not only as to what comes to be, but also as to the mode of coming to be or of being. For from the weakness of the active power in the seed it happens that the son is born unlike the father in accidents, which pertain to the mode of being. Therefore, since the divine will is most efficacious, it follows not only that those things which God wills to be done are done, but that they are done in the manner in which God wills them to be done. Now God wills some things to be done necessarily and some contingently, so that there may be order in things, for the completion of the universe. And therefore He has adapted necessary causes, which cannot fail, to certain effects, from which effects proceed of necessity; but to others He has adapted contingent, defectible causes, from which effects come about contingently.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172","text":"Therefore, effects willed by God do not come about contingently because their proximate causes are contingent, but because God willed them to come about contingently and prepared contingent causes for them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0173","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0174","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that by that statement of Augustine necessity in things willed by God is to be understood, not absolute necessity, but conditional necessity; for this conditional is necessarily true: if God wills this, this must be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0175","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0176","text":"To the second it must be said that, from the very fact that nothing resists the divine will, it follows not only that those things which God wills to be done are done, but that those which He wills to be done thus are done contingently or necessarily.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0177","text":"[I q. 19 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0178","text":"To the third it must be said that later things have necessity from prior things, according to the mode of the prior things. Hence even those things which are done by the divine will have such necessity as God wills them to have, namely, either absolute necessity or only conditional necessity. And thus not all things are necessary absolutely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0179","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0180","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0181","text":"We proceed thus to the ninth article. It seems that God's will is of evils. For God wills every good that comes to be. But it is good that evils come to be, for Augustine says in the *Enchiridion*: *although those things which are evil, insofar as they are evil, are not good; nevertheless, it is good not only that goods exist, but also that evils exist*. Therefore God wills evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0182","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0183","text":"Further, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that evil will contribute to the perfection of the whole (that is, of the universe). And Augustine says, in the *Enchiridion*: *the wondrous beauty of the universe consists of all things; in it even that which is called evil, when well ordered and placed in its own position, commends goods more eminently, so that they may be more pleasing and more praiseworthy when compared with evils*. But God wills everything that pertains to the perfection and beauty of the universe, because this is what God chiefly wills in creatures. Therefore God wills evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0184","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0185","text":"Further, for evils to come to be and not to come to be are contradictorily opposed. But God does not will evils not to come to be, because, since certain evils do come to be, God's will would not always be fulfilled. Therefore God wills evils to come to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0186","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0187","text":"But against this is what Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*: *With no wise man as author does a man become worse; but God is more excellent than every wise man; much less, therefore, with God as author does anyone become worse. But when He is said to be the author, He is said to will it.* Therefore, with God willing, a man does not become worse. But it is clear that something becomes worse by every evil. Therefore God does not will evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0188","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since the nature of the good is the nature of the appetible, as was said above, and evil is opposed to good, it is impossible that any evil, insofar as it is such, be desired, whether by natural appetite, animal appetite, or intellectual appetite, which is the will. But some evil is desired accidentally, insofar as it follows upon some good. And this is apparent in every appetite. For a natural agent does not intend privation or corruption, but a form to which is joined the privation of another form, and the generation of one thing, which is the corruption of another. Likewise, a lion, in killing a stag, intends food, to which the killing of the animal is joined. Similarly, a fornicator intends pleasure, to which the deformity of guilt is joined. But the evil joined to some good is the privation of another good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","text":"Therefore evil would never be desired, not even accidentally, unless the good to which the evil is joined were desired more than the good which is deprived through the evil. But God wills no good more than His own goodness; nevertheless, He wills one good more than another certain good. Hence the evil of fault, which deprives of order to the divine good, God in no way wills. But the evil of natural defect, or the evil of punishment, He wills by willing some good to which such evil is joined; as, by willing justice, He wills punishment, and by willing the order of nature to be preserved, He wills certain things to be naturally corrupted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0191","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0192","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that some have said that, although God does not will evils, He nevertheless wills evils to exist or to come to be, because, although evils are not good, it is nevertheless good for evils to exist or to come to be. They said this because those things which are evil in themselves are ordered to some good; and they believed that this order was conveyed by the statement that it is good for evils to exist or to come to be. But this is not rightly said. For evil is ordered to good not through itself, but accidentally. For it is beyond the intention of the sinner that some good should follow from this; just as it was beyond the intention of tyrants that, from their persecutions, the patience of the martyrs should shine forth. And therefore it cannot be said that such an order to good is conveyed by the statement that it is good for evil to exist or to come to be, because nothing is judged according to what belongs to it accidentally, but according to what belongs to it through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0193","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0194","text":"To the second, it must be said that evil contributes to the perfection and beauty of the universe only accidentally, as has been said. Hence Dionysius too, when he says that evil contributes to the perfection of the universe, concludes by adducing it as though to an absurdity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0195","text":"[I q. 19 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although for evils to occur and for evils not to occur are contradictorily opposed, nevertheless to will evils to occur and to will evils not to occur are not contradictorily opposed, since both are affirmative. Therefore God neither wills evils to occur, nor wills evils not to occur, but wills to permit evils to occur. And this is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0197","text":"## Article 10","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0198","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0199","text":"We proceed thus to the tenth. It seems that God does not have free choice. For Jerome says, in the homily on the prodigal son: *God alone is one in whom sin does not occur, nor can occur; all others, since they possess free choice, can be bent in either direction*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0200","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0201","text":"Moreover, free choice is a faculty of reason and will by which good and evil are chosen. But God does not will evil, as has been said. Therefore free choice is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0202","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0203","text":"But on the contrary, Ambrose says, in the book *On Faith*: *The Holy Spirit distributes to each one as He wills, that is, according to the judgment of free will, not according to the service of necessity*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0204","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","text":"I answer that it must be said that we have free choice with respect to those things that we do not will necessarily or by natural instinct. For that we will to be happy does not pertain to free choice, but to natural instinct. Hence other animals also, which are moved toward something by natural instinct, are not said to be moved by free choice. Therefore, since God necessarily wills His own goodness, but does not necessarily will other things, as was shown above, He has free choice with respect to those things which He does not will necessarily.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0206","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Jerome seems to exclude free choice from God, not simply, but only as regards being turned aside into sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0208","text":"[I q. 19 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0209","text":"To the second, it must be said that, since the evil of fault is spoken of through turning away from the divine goodness, through which God wills all things, as was shown above, it is clear that it is impossible for Him to will the evil of fault. And yet He is related to opposites, insofar as He can will this to be or not to be. Just as we too, without sinning, can will to sit and not will to sit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0210","text":"## Article 11","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0211","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0212","text":"We proceed thus to the eleventh. It seems that a will of sign should not be distinguished in God. For just as the will of God is the cause of things, so also is His knowledge. But no signs are assigned on the part of the divine knowledge. Therefore neither ought any signs to be assigned on the part of the divine will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0213","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0213"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0214","text":"Moreover, every sign that does not accord with that of which it is a sign is false. Therefore, if the signs assigned concerning the divine will do not accord with the divine will, they are false; but if they do accord, they are assigned superfluously. Therefore no signs concerning the divine will ought to be assigned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0214"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0215","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0215"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0216","text":"But on the contrary, the will of God is one, since it is God's very essence. Yet it is sometimes signified in the plural, as when it is said: *Great are the works of the Lord, sought out according to all His wills*. Therefore it is necessary that sometimes a sign of the will be taken for the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0216"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0217","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0217"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","text":"I answer that it must be said that certain things are said of God properly, and certain things metaphorically, as is clear from what has been said above. Now, when certain human passions are metaphorically taken up into divine predication, this occurs according to a likeness of effect; hence, that which is a sign of such a passion in us is metaphorically signified in God by the name of that passion. Thus, among us, the angry are accustomed to punish; hence punishment itself is a sign of anger, and on this account punishment itself, when attributed to God, is signified by the name of anger. Similarly, that which is customarily in us a sign of will is sometimes metaphorically called will in God. Thus, when someone commands something, it is a sign that he wills it to be done; hence the divine command is sometimes metaphorically called the will of God, according to Matthew VI, *Thy will be done, as in heaven and on earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","text":"But there is this difference between will and anger: anger is never said of God properly, since in its principal meaning it includes passion, whereas will is said of God properly. Therefore, in God a distinction is made between will properly so called and will metaphorically so called. For will properly so called is called the will of good pleasure; but will metaphorically so called is the will of sign, because the very sign of will is called will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0220","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0220"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0221","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that knowledge is not the cause of those things that come to be except through will; for we do not do the things that we know unless we will them. Therefore, a sign is not attributed to knowledge as it is attributed to will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0221"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0222","text":"[I q. 19 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0222"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","text":"To the second it must be said that signs of will are called divine wills, not because they are signs that God wills, but because those things which in us are customarily signs of willing are called divine wills in God. Thus punishment is not a sign that anger is in God; but punishment, from the very fact that in us it is a sign of anger, is called anger in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0224","text":"## Article 12","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0224"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0225","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0225"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0226","text":"We proceed thus to the twelfth article. It seems that five signs are improperly posited concerning the divine will, namely, prohibition, command, counsel, operation, and permission. For the same things that God commands or counsels us, He sometimes works in us; and the same things that He prohibits, He sometimes permits. Therefore, they ought not to be divided in opposition to one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0226"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0227","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0227"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0228","text":"Further, God works nothing unless willing, as is said in Wisdom XI. But the will of sign is distinguished from the will of good pleasure. Therefore, operation ought not to be included under the will of sign.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0228"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0229","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0229"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0230","text":"Further, operation and permission pertain commonly to all creatures, because God works in all things and permits something to be done in all things. But command, counsel, and prohibition pertain only to the rational creature. Therefore, they do not fittingly fall under one division, since they are not of one order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0230"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0231","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0231"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0232","text":"Further, evil occurs in more ways than good, because good occurs in one way, but evil in every way, as is clear through the Philosopher in Ethics II, and through Dionysius in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*. Therefore, with respect to evil, only one sign is unfittingly assigned, namely prohibition; whereas with respect to good, two signs are assigned, namely counsel and command.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0232"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0233","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0233"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","text":"I answer that it must be said that signs of will of this kind are called those by which we are accustomed to show that we will something. Now someone can declare that he wills something either through himself or through another. Through himself, indeed, insofar as he does something either directly or indirectly and accidentally. Directly, indeed, when he works something through himself; and with respect to this, operation is said to be a sign. Indirectly, however, insofar as he does not impede an operation; for one who removes an impediment is said to move accidentally, as is said in Physics VIII. And with respect to this, permission is said to be a sign. But through another, he declares that he wills something insofar as he orders another to do something: either by a necessary inducement, which occurs by commanding what he wills and prohibiting the contrary; or by some persuasive inducement, which pertains to counsel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0235","text":"Therefore, because by these ways someone is declared to will something, on this account these five are sometimes designated by the name of the divine will, as signs of will. That command, counsel, and prohibition are called God's will is clear from what is said in Matthew VI: *Thy will be done, as in heaven and on earth*. But that permission or operation are called God's will is clear from Augustine, who says in the *Enchiridion*: *Nothing happens unless the Almighty wills it to happen, either by permitting it to happen or by doing it*. Or it may be said that permission and operation are referred to the present: permission, indeed, to evil, but operation to good. But with respect to the future: prohibition with respect to evil; command with respect to necessary good; and counsel with respect to superabundant good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0235"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0236","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0236"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0237","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that nothing prevents someone, concerning the same thing, from declaring in diverse ways that he wills something, just as many names are found signifying the same thing. Hence nothing prevents the same thing from being subject to command and counsel and operation, and to prohibition or permission.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0237"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0238","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0238"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","text":"To the second it must be said that, just as God can be metaphorically signified as willing that which He does not will by will properly taken, so He can be metaphorically signified as willing that which He properly wills. Hence nothing prevents there being, concerning the same thing, a will of good pleasure and a will of sign. But operation is always the same as the will of good pleasure, whereas command or counsel are not: both because this is concerning the present, while that is concerning the future; and because this is through itself an effect of will, whereas that is through another, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0240","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0240"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0241","text":"To the third, it must be said that the rational creature is mistress of its own act, and therefore certain special signs of the divine will are assigned concerning it, insofar as God orders the rational creature to act voluntarily and through itself. But other creatures do not act except when moved by divine operation, and therefore, with regard to the others, there is room only for operation and permission.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0241"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0242","text":"[I q. 19 a. 12 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0242"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0243","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that every evil of fault, although it occurs in many ways, nevertheless agrees in this: that it is discordant with the divine will; and therefore one sign is assigned with respect to evils, namely, prohibition. But goods are related to divine goodness in different ways. For some are such that, without them, we cannot attain the enjoyment of divine goodness, and with respect to these there is a precept. But some are such that through them we attain it more perfectly, and with respect to these there is counsel. Or it must be said that counsel concerns not only the attaining of better goods, but also the avoiding of lesser evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0243"]}],"source_digest":"d716d1e1b0ad132fd32108666e29cecb52288b6c6a1895837b087f952790906a","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 19"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 19","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12","title":"First Part · Question 20","order":21,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_12","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 20 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29285)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de his quae absolute ad voluntatem Dei pertinent. In parte autem appetitiva inveniuntur in nobis et passiones animae, ut gaudium, amor, et huiusmodi; et habitus moralium virtutum, ut iustitia, fortitudo, et huiusmodi. Unde primo considerabimus de amore Dei; secundo, de iustitia Dei, et misericordia eius. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in Deo sit amor. Secundo, utrum amet omnia. Tertio, utrum magis amet unum quam aliud. Quarto, utrum meliora magis amet.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29286)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod amor non sit in Deo. Nulla enim passio est in Deo. Amor est passio. Ergo amor non est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29287)\n\nPraeterea, amor, ira, tristitia, et huiusmodi, contra se dividuntur. Sed tristitia et ira non dicuntur de Deo nisi metaphorice. Ergo nec amor.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29288)\n\nPraeterea, Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *amor est vis unitiva et concretiva*. Hoc autem in Deo locum habere non potest, cum sit simplex. Ergo in Deo non est amor.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29289)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur I Ioan. IV, *Deus caritas est*.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29290)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere amorem in Deo. Primus enim motus voluntatis, et cuiuslibet appetitivae virtutis, est amor. Cum enim actus voluntatis, et cuiuslibet appetitivae virtutis tendat in bonum et malum, sicut in propria obiecta; bonum autem principalius et per se est obiectum voluntatis et appetitus, malum autem secundario et per aliud, inquantum scilicet opponitur bono, oportet naturaliter esse priores actus voluntatis et appetitus qui respiciunt bonum, his qui respiciunt malum; ut gaudium quam tristitia, et amor quam odium. Semper enim quod est per se, prius est eo quod est per aliud. Rursus, quod est communius, naturaliter est prius, unde et intellectus per prius habet ordinem ad verum commune, quam ad particularia quaedam vera. Sunt autem quidam actus voluntatis et appetitus, respicientes bonum sub aliqua speciali conditione, sicut gaudium et delectatio est de bono praesenti et habito; desiderium autem et spes, de bono nondum adepto. Amor autem respicit bonum in communi, sive sit habitum, sive non habitum. Unde amor naturaliter est primus actus voluntatis et appetitus. Et propter hoc, omnes alii motus appetitivi praesupponunt amorem, quasi primam radicem. Nullus enim desiderat aliquid, nisi bonum amatum, neque aliquis gaudet, nisi de bono amato. Odium etiam non est nisi de eo quod contrariatur rei amatae. Et similiter tristitiam, et cetera huiusmodi, manifestum est in amorem referri, sicut in primum principium. Unde in quocumque est voluntas vel appetitus, oportet esse amorem, remoto enim primo, removentur alia. Ostensum est autem in Deo esse voluntatem. Unde necesse est in eo ponere amorem.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29291)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod vis cognitiva non movet, nisi mediante appetitiva. Et sicut in nobis ratio universalis movet mediante ratione particulari, ut dicitur in III de anima; ita appetitus intellectivus, qui dicitur voluntas, movet in nobis mediante appetitu sensitivo. Unde proximum motivum corporis in nobis est appetitus sensitivus. Unde semper actum appetitus sensitivi concomitatur aliqua transmutatio corporis; et maxime circa cor, quod est primum principium motus in animali. Sic igitur actus appetitus sensitivi, inquantum habent transmutationem corporalem annexam, passiones dicuntur, non autem actus voluntatis. Amor igitur et gaudium et delectatio, secundum quod significant actus appetitus sensitivi, passiones sunt, non autem secundum quod significant actus appetitus intellectivi. Et sic ponuntur in Deo. Unde dicit philosophus, in VII Ethic., quod *Deus una et simplici operatione gaudet*. Et eadem ratione, sine passione amat.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29292)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in passionibus sensitivi appetitus, est considerare aliquid quasi materiale, scilicet corporalem transmutationem; et aliquid quasi formale, quod est ex parte appetitus. Sicut in ira, ut dicitur in I de anima, materiale est accensio sanguinis circa cor, vel aliquid huiusmodi; formale vero, appetitus vindictae. Sed rursus, ex parte eius quod est formale, in quibusdam horum designatur aliqua imperfectio; sicut in desiderio, quod est boni non habiti; et in tristitia, quae est mali habiti. Et eadem ratio est de ira, quae tristitiam supponit. Quaedam vero nullam imperfectionem designant, ut amor et gaudium. Cum igitur nihil horum Deo conveniat secundum illud quod est materiale in eis, ut dictum est; illa quae imperfectionem important etiam formaliter, Deo convenire non possunt nisi metaphorice, propter similitudinem effectus, ut supra dictum est. Quae autem imperfectionem non important, de Deo proprie dicuntur, ut amor et gaudium, tamen sine passione, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29293)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod actus amoris semper tendit in duo, scilicet in bonum quod quis vult alicui; et in eum cui vult bonum. Hoc enim est proprie amare aliquem, velle ei bonum. Unde in eo quod aliquis amat se, vult bonum sibi. Et sic illud bonum quaerit sibi unire, inquantum potest. Et pro tanto dicitur amor vis unitiva, etiam in Deo, sed absque compositione, quia illud bonum quod vult sibi, non est aliud quam ipse, qui est per suam essentiam bonus, ut supra ostensum est. In hoc vero quod aliquis amat alium, vult bonum illi. Et sic utitur eo tanquam seipso, referens bonum ad illum, sicut ad seipsum. Et pro tanto dicitur amor vis concretiva, quia alium aggregat sibi habens se ad eum sicut ad seipsum. Et sic etiam amor divinus est vis concretiva, absque compositione quae sit in Deo, inquantum aliis bona vult.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29294)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non omnia amet. Quia, secundum Dionysium, IV cap. de Div. Nom., amor amantem extra se ponit, et eum quodammodo in amatum transfert. Inconveniens autem est dicere quod Deus, extra se positus, in alia transferatur. Ergo inconveniens est dicere quod Deus alia a se amet.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29295)\n\nPraeterea, amor Dei aeternus est. Sed ea quae sunt alia a Deo, non sunt ab aeterno nisi in Deo. Ergo Deus non amat ea nisi in seipso. Sed secundum quod sunt in eo, non sunt aliud ab eo. Ergo Deus non amat alia a seipso.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29296)\n\nPraeterea, duplex est amor, scilicet concupiscentiae, et amicitiae. Sed Deus creaturas irrationales non amat amore concupiscentiae, quia nullius extra se eget, nec etiam amore amicitiae, quia non potest ad res irrationales haberi, ut patet per philosophum, in VIII Ethic. Ergo Deus non omnia amat.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29297)\n\nPraeterea, in Psalmo dicitur, *odisti omnes qui operantur iniquitatem*. Nihil autem simul odio habetur et amatur. Ergo Deus non omnia amat.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29298)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XI, *diligis omnia quae sunt, et nihil odisti eorum quae fecisti*.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29299)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus omnia existentia amat. Nam omnia existentia, inquantum sunt, bona sunt, ipsum enim esse cuiuslibet rei quoddam bonum est, et similiter quaelibet perfectio ipsius. Ostensum est autem supra quod voluntas Dei est causa omnium rerum et sic oportet quod intantum habeat aliquid esse, aut quodcumque bonum, inquantum est volitum a Deo. Cuilibet igitur existenti Deus vult aliquod bonum. Unde, cum amare nil aliud sit quam velle bonum alicui, manifestum est quod Deus omnia quae sunt, amat. Non tamen eo modo sicut nos. Quia enim voluntas nostra non est causa bonitatis rerum, sed ab ea movetur sicut ab obiecto, amor noster, quo bonum alicui volumus, non est causa bonitatis ipsius, sed e converso bonitas eius, vel vera vel aestimata, provocat amorem, quo ei volumus et bonum conservari quod habet, et addi quod non habet, et ad hoc operamur. Sed amor Dei est infundens et creans bonitatem in rebus.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29300)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod amans sic fit extra se in amatum translatus, inquantum vult amato bonum, et operatur per suam providentiam, sicut et sibi. Unde et Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *audendum est autem et hoc pro veritate dicere, quod et ipse omnium causa, per abundantiam amativae bonitatis, extra seipsum fit ad omnia existentia providentiis*.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29301)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet creaturae ab aeterno non fuerint nisi in Deo, tamen per hoc quod ab aeterno in Deo fuerunt, ab aeterno Deus cognovit res in propriis naturis, et eadem ratione amavit. Sicut et nos per similitudines rerum, quae in nobis sunt, cognoscimus res in seipsis existentes.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29302)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod amicitia non potest haberi nisi ad rationales creaturas, in quibus contingit esse redamationem, et communicationem in operibus vitae, et quibus contingit bene evenire vel male, secundum fortunam et felicitatem, sicut et ad eas proprie benevolentia est. Creaturae autem irrationales non possunt pertingere ad amandum Deum, neque ad communicationem intellectualis et beatae vitae, qua Deus vivit. Sic igitur Deus, proprie loquendo, non amat creaturas irrationales amore amicitiae, sed amore quasi concupiscentiae; inquantum ordinat eas ad rationales creaturas, et etiam ad seipsum; non quasi eis indigeat, sed propter suam bonitatem et nostram utilitatem. Concupiscimus enim aliquid et nobis et aliis.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29303)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod nihil prohibet unum et idem secundum aliquid amari, et secundum aliquid odio haberi. Deus autem peccatores, inquantum sunt naturae quaedam, amat, sic enim et sunt, et ab ipso sunt. Inquantum vero peccatores sunt, non sunt, sed ab esse deficiunt, et hoc in eis a Deo non est. Unde secundum hoc ab ipso odio habentur.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29304)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus aequaliter diligat omnia. Dicitur enim Sap. VI, *aequaliter est ei cura de omnibus*. Sed providentia Dei, quam habet de rebus, est ex amore quo amat res. Ergo aequaliter amat omnia.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29305)\n\nPraeterea, amor Dei est eius essentia. Sed essentia Dei magis et minus non recipit. Ergo nec amor eius. Non igitur quaedam aliis magis amat.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29306)\n\nPraeterea, sicut amor Dei se extendit ad res creatas, ita et scientia et voluntas. Sed Deus non dicitur scire quaedam magis quam alia, neque magis velle. Ergo nec magis quaedam aliis diligit.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29307)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, super Ioann., *omnia diligit Deus quae fecit; et inter ea magis diligit creaturas rationales; et de illis eas amplius, quae sunt membra unigeniti sui; et multo magis ipsum unigenitum suum*.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29308)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum amare sit velle bonum alicui, duplici ratione potest aliquid magis vel minus amari. Uno modo, ex parte ipsius actus voluntatis, qui est magis vel minus intensus. Et sic Deus non magis quaedam aliis amat, quia omnia amat uno et simplici actu voluntatis, et semper eodem modo se habente. Alio modo, ex parte ipsius boni quod aliquis vult amato. Et sic dicimur aliquem magis alio amare, cui volumus maius bonum; quamvis non magis intensa voluntate. Et hoc modo necesse est dicere quod Deus quaedam aliis magis amat. Cum enim amor Dei sit causa bonitatis rerum, ut dictum est, non esset aliquid alio melius, si Deus non vellet uni maius bonum quam alteri.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29309)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dicitur Deo aequaliter esse cura de omnibus, non quia aequalia bona sua cura omnibus dispenset; sed quia ex aequali sapientia et bonitate omnia administrat.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29310)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de intensione amoris ex parte actus voluntatis, qui est divina essentia. Bonum autem quod Deus creaturae vult, non est divina essentia. Unde nihil prohibet illud intendi vel remitti.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29311)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod intelligere et velle significant solum actus, non autem in sua significatione includunt aliqua obiecta, ex quorum diversitate possit dici Deus magis vel minus scire aut velle; sicut circa amorem dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29312)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non semper magis diligat meliora. Manifestum est enim quod Christus est melior toto genere humano, cum sit Deus et homo. Sed Deus magis dilexit genus humanum quam Christum, quia dicitur Rom. VIII, *proprio filio suo non pepercit, sed pro nobis omnibus tradidit illum*. Ergo Deus non semper magis diligit meliora.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29313)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus est melior homine, unde in Psalmo VIII dicitur de homine, *minuisti eum paulo minus ab Angelis*. Sed Deus plus dilexit hominem quam Angelum, dicitur enim Hebr. II, *nusquam Angelos apprehendit, sed semen Abrahae apprehendit*. Ergo Deus non semper magis diligit meliora.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29314)\n\nPraeterea, Petrus fuit melior Ioanne, quia plus Christum diligebat. Unde dominus, sciens hoc esse verum, interrogavit Petrum, dicens, *Simon Ioannis, diligis me plus his?* Sed tamen Christus plus dilexit Ioannem quam Petrum, ut enim dicit Augustinus, super illud Ioan. XXI, *Simon Ioannis diligis me? Hoc ipso signo Ioannes a ceteris discipulis discernitur; non quod solum eum, sed quod plus eum ceteris diligebat*. Non ergo semper magis diligit meliora.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29315)\n\nPraeterea, melior est innocens poenitente; *cum poenitentia sit secunda tabula post naufragium*, ut dicit Hieronymus. Sed Deus plus diligit poenitentem quam innocentem, quia plus de eo gaudet, dicitur enim Luc. XV, *dico vobis quod maius gaudium erit in caelo super uno peccatore poenitentiam agente, quam super nonaginta novem iustis, qui non indigent poenitentia*. Ergo Deus non semper magis diligit meliora.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29316)\n\nPraeterea, melior est iustus praescitus, quam peccator praedestinatus. Sed Deus plus diligit peccatorem praedestinatum, quia vult ei maius bonum, scilicet vitam aeternam. Ergo Deus non semper magis diligit meliora.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29317)\n\nSed contra, unumquodque diligit sibi simile; ut patet per illud quod habetur Eccli. XIII, *omne animal diligit sibi simile*. Sed intantum aliquid est melius, inquantum est Deo similius. Ergo meliora magis diliguntur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29318)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere, secundum praedicta, quod Deus magis diligat meliora. Dictum est enim quod Deum diligere magis aliquid, nihil aliud est quam ei maius bonum velle, voluntas enim Dei est causa bonitatis in rebus. Et sic, ex hoc sunt aliqua meliora, quod Deus eis maius bonum vult. Unde sequitur quod meliora plus amet.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29319)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus Christum diligit, non solum plus quam totum humanum genus, sed etiam magis quam totam universitatem creaturarum, quia scilicet ei maius bonum voluit, quia dedit ei nomen, quod est super omne nomen, ut verus Deus esset. Nec eius excellentiae deperiit ex hoc quod Deus dedit eum in mortem pro salute humani generis, quinimo ex hoc factus est victor gloriosus; *factus enim est principatus super humerum eius*, ut dicitur Isaiae IX.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29320)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod naturam humanam assumptam a Dei verbo in persona Christi, secundum praedicta, Deus plus amat quam omnes Angelos, et melior est, maxime ratione unionis. Sed loquendo de humana natura communiter, eam angelicae comparando, secundum ordinem ad gratiam et gloriam, aequalitas invenitur; cum eadem sit mensura hominis et Angeli, ut dicitur Apoc. XXI; ita tamen quod quidam Angeli quibusdam hominibus, et quidam homines quibusdam Angelis, quantum ad hoc, potiores inveniuntur. Sed quantum ad conditionem naturae, Angelus est melior homine. Nec ideo naturam humanam assumpsit Deus, quia hominem absolute plus diligeret, sed quia plus indigebat. Sicut bonus paterfamilias aliquid pretiosius dat servo aegrotanti, quod non dat filio sano.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29321)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod haec dubitatio de Petro et Ioanne multipliciter solvitur. Augustinus namque refert hoc ad mysterium, dicens quod vita activa, quae significatur per Petrum, plus diligit Deum quam vita contemplativa, quae significatur per Ioannem, quia magis sentit praesentis vitae angustias, et aestuantius ab eis liberari desiderat, et ad Deum ire. Contemplativam vero vitam Deus plus diligit, quia magis eam conservat; non enim finitur simul cum vita corporis, sicut vita activa. Quidam vero dicunt quod Petrus plus dilexit Christum in membris; et sic etiam a Christo plus fuit dilectus; unde ei Ecclesiam commendavit. Ioannes vero plus dilexit Christum in seipso; et sic etiam plus ab eo fuit dilectus; unde ei commendavit matrem. Alii vero dicunt quod incertum est quis horum plus Christum dilexerit amore caritatis, et similiter quem Deus plus dilexerit in ordine ad maiorem gloriam vitae aeternae. Sed Petrus dicitur plus dilexisse, quantum ad quandam promptitudinem vel fervorem, Ioannes vero plus dilectus, quantum ad quaedam familiaritatis indicia, quae Christus ei magis demonstrabat, propter eius iuventutem et puritatem. Alii vero dicunt quod Christus plus dilexit Petrum, quantum ad excellentius donum caritatis, Ioannem vero plus, quantum ad donum intellectus. Unde simpliciter Petrus fuit melior, et magis dilectus, sed Ioannes secundum quid. Praesumptuosum tamen videtur hoc diiudicare, quia, ut dicitur Prov. XVI, *spirituum ponderator est dominus*, et non alius.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29322)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod poenitentes et innocentes se habent sicut excedentia et excessa. Nam sive sint innocentes, sive poenitentes, illi sunt meliores et magis dilecti, qui plus habent de gratia. Ceteris tamen paribus, innocentia dignior est et magis dilecta. Dicitur tamen Deus plus gaudere de poenitente quam de innocente, quia plerumque poenitentes cautiores, humiliores et ferventiores resurgunt. Unde Gregorius dicit ibidem, quod *dux in praelio eum militem plus diligit, qui post fugam conversus, fortiter hostem premit, quam qui nunquam fugit, nec unquam fortiter fecit*. Vel, alia ratione, quia aequale donum gratiae plus est, comparatum poenitenti, qui meruit poenam, quam innocenti, qui non meruit. Sicut centum marcae maius donum est, si dentur pauperi, quam si dentur regi.\n\n[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29323)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, cum voluntas Dei sit causa bonitatis in rebus, secundum illud tempus pensanda est bonitas eius qui amatur a Deo, secundum quod dandum est ei ex bonitate divina aliquod bonum. Secundum ergo illud tempus quo praedestinato peccatori dandum est ex divina voluntate maius bonum, melior est; licet secundum aliquod aliud tempus, sit peior; quia et secundum aliquod tempus, non est nec bonus neque malus.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0002","text":"[I q. 20 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de his quae absolute ad voluntatem Dei pertinent. In parte autem appetitiva inveniuntur in nobis et passiones animae, ut gaudium, amor, et huiusmodi; et habitus moralium virtutum, ut iustitia, fortitudo, et huiusmodi. Unde primo considerabimus de amore Dei; secundo, de iustitia Dei, et misericordia eius. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in Deo sit amor. Secundo, utrum amet omnia. Tertio, utrum magis amet unum quam aliud. Quarto, utrum meliora magis amet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0005","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod amor non sit in Deo. Nulla enim passio est in Deo. Amor est passio. Ergo amor non est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0007","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0008","text":"Praeterea, amor, ira, tristitia, et huiusmodi, contra se dividuntur. Sed tristitia et ira non dicuntur de Deo nisi metaphorice. Ergo nec amor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0009","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0010","text":"Praeterea, Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *amor est vis unitiva et concretiva*. Hoc autem in Deo locum habere non potest, cum sit simplex. Ergo in Deo non est amor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0011","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur I Ioan. IV, *Deus caritas est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0013","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere amorem in Deo. Primus enim motus voluntatis, et cuiuslibet appetitivae virtutis, est amor. Cum enim actus voluntatis, et cuiuslibet appetitivae virtutis tendat in bonum et malum, sicut in propria obiecta; bonum autem principalius et per se est obiectum voluntatis et appetitus, malum autem secundario et per aliud, inquantum scilicet opponitur bono, oportet naturaliter esse priores actus voluntatis et appetitus qui respiciunt bonum, his qui respiciunt malum; ut gaudium quam tristitia, et amor quam odium. Semper enim quod est per se, prius est eo quod est per aliud. Rursus, quod est communius, naturaliter est prius, unde et intellectus per prius habet ordinem ad verum commune, quam ad particularia quaedam vera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","text":"Sunt autem quidam actus voluntatis et appetitus, respicientes bonum sub aliqua speciali conditione, sicut gaudium et delectatio est de bono praesenti et habito; desiderium autem et spes, de bono nondum adepto. Amor autem respicit bonum in communi, sive sit habitum, sive non habitum. Unde amor naturaliter est primus actus voluntatis et appetitus. Et propter hoc, omnes alii motus appetitivi praesupponunt amorem, quasi primam radicem. Nullus enim desiderat aliquid, nisi bonum amatum, neque aliquis gaudet, nisi de bono amato. Odium etiam non est nisi de eo quod contrariatur rei amatae. Et similiter tristitiam, et cetera huiusmodi, manifestum est in amorem referri, sicut in primum principium. Unde in quocumque est voluntas vel appetitus, oportet esse amorem, remoto enim primo, removentur alia. Ostensum est autem in Deo esse voluntatem. Unde necesse est in eo ponere amorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0016","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod vis cognitiva non movet, nisi mediante appetitiva. Et sicut in nobis ratio universalis movet mediante ratione particulari, ut dicitur in III de anima; ita appetitus intellectivus, qui dicitur voluntas, movet in nobis mediante appetitu sensitivo. Unde proximum motivum corporis in nobis est appetitus sensitivus. Unde semper actum appetitus sensitivi concomitatur aliqua transmutatio corporis; et maxime circa cor, quod est primum principium motus in animali. Sic igitur actus appetitus sensitivi, inquantum habent transmutationem corporalem annexam, passiones dicuntur, non autem actus voluntatis. Amor igitur et gaudium et delectatio, secundum quod significant actus appetitus sensitivi, passiones sunt, non autem secundum quod significant actus appetitus intellectivi. Et sic ponuntur in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","text":"Unde dicit philosophus, in VII Ethic., quod *Deus una et simplici operatione gaudet*. Et eadem ratione, sine passione amat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0019","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in passionibus sensitivi appetitus, est considerare aliquid quasi materiale, scilicet corporalem transmutationem; et aliquid quasi formale, quod est ex parte appetitus. Sicut in ira, ut dicitur in I de anima, materiale est accensio sanguinis circa cor, vel aliquid huiusmodi; formale vero, appetitus vindictae. Sed rursus, ex parte eius quod est formale, in quibusdam horum designatur aliqua imperfectio; sicut in desiderio, quod est boni non habiti; et in tristitia, quae est mali habiti. Et eadem ratio est de ira, quae tristitiam supponit. Quaedam vero nullam imperfectionem designant, ut amor et gaudium. Cum igitur nihil horum Deo conveniat secundum illud quod est materiale in eis, ut dictum est; illa quae imperfectionem important etiam formaliter, Deo convenire non possunt nisi metaphorice, propter similitudinem effectus, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","text":"Quae autem imperfectionem non important, de Deo proprie dicuntur, ut amor et gaudium, tamen sine passione, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0022","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod actus amoris semper tendit in duo, scilicet in bonum quod quis vult alicui; et in eum cui vult bonum. Hoc enim est proprie amare aliquem, velle ei bonum. Unde in eo quod aliquis amat se, vult bonum sibi. Et sic illud bonum quaerit sibi unire, inquantum potest. Et pro tanto dicitur amor vis unitiva, etiam in Deo, sed absque compositione, quia illud bonum quod vult sibi, non est aliud quam ipse, qui est per suam essentiam bonus, ut supra ostensum est. In hoc vero quod aliquis amat alium, vult bonum illi. Et sic utitur eo tanquam seipso, referens bonum ad illum, sicut ad seipsum. Et pro tanto dicitur amor vis concretiva, quia alium aggregat sibi habens se ad eum sicut ad seipsum. Et sic etiam amor divinus est vis concretiva, absque compositione quae sit in Deo, inquantum aliis bona vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0025","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non omnia amet. Quia, secundum Dionysium, IV cap. de Div. Nom., amor amantem extra se ponit, et eum quodammodo in amatum transfert. Inconveniens autem est dicere quod Deus, extra se positus, in alia transferatur. Ergo inconveniens est dicere quod Deus alia a se amet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0027","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0028","text":"Praeterea, amor Dei aeternus est. Sed ea quae sunt alia a Deo, non sunt ab aeterno nisi in Deo. Ergo Deus non amat ea nisi in seipso. Sed secundum quod sunt in eo, non sunt aliud ab eo. Ergo Deus non amat alia a seipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0029","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0030","text":"Praeterea, duplex est amor, scilicet concupiscentiae, et amicitiae. Sed Deus creaturas irrationales non amat amore concupiscentiae, quia nullius extra se eget, nec etiam amore amicitiae, quia non potest ad res irrationales haberi, ut patet per philosophum, in VIII Ethic. Ergo Deus non omnia amat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0031","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0032","text":"Praeterea, in Psalmo dicitur, *odisti omnes qui operantur iniquitatem*. Nihil autem simul odio habetur et amatur. Ergo Deus non omnia amat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0033","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0034","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XI, *diligis omnia quae sunt, et nihil odisti eorum quae fecisti*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0035","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus omnia existentia amat. Nam omnia existentia, inquantum sunt, bona sunt, ipsum enim esse cuiuslibet rei quoddam bonum est, et similiter quaelibet perfectio ipsius. Ostensum est autem supra quod voluntas Dei est causa omnium rerum et sic oportet quod intantum habeat aliquid esse, aut quodcumque bonum, inquantum est volitum a Deo. Cuilibet igitur existenti Deus vult aliquod bonum. Unde, cum amare nil aliud sit quam velle bonum alicui, manifestum est quod Deus omnia quae sunt, amat. Non tamen eo modo sicut nos. Quia enim voluntas nostra non est causa bonitatis rerum, sed ab ea movetur sicut ab obiecto, amor noster, quo bonum alicui volumus, non est causa bonitatis ipsius, sed e converso bonitas eius, vel vera vel aestimata, provocat amorem, quo ei volumus et bonum conservari quod habet, et addi quod non habet, et ad hoc operamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","text":"Sed amor Dei est infundens et creans bonitatem in rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0038","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod amans sic fit extra se in amatum translatus, inquantum vult amato bonum, et operatur per suam providentiam, sicut et sibi. Unde et Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *audendum est autem et hoc pro veritate dicere, quod et ipse omnium causa, per abundantiam amativae bonitatis, extra seipsum fit ad omnia existentia providentiis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0040","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0041","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet creaturae ab aeterno non fuerint nisi in Deo, tamen per hoc quod ab aeterno in Deo fuerunt, ab aeterno Deus cognovit res in propriis naturis, et eadem ratione amavit. Sicut et nos per similitudines rerum, quae in nobis sunt, cognoscimus res in seipsis existentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0042","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod amicitia non potest haberi nisi ad rationales creaturas, in quibus contingit esse redamationem, et communicationem in operibus vitae, et quibus contingit bene evenire vel male, secundum fortunam et felicitatem, sicut et ad eas proprie benevolentia est. Creaturae autem irrationales non possunt pertingere ad amandum Deum, neque ad communicationem intellectualis et beatae vitae, qua Deus vivit. Sic igitur Deus, proprie loquendo, non amat creaturas irrationales amore amicitiae, sed amore quasi concupiscentiae; inquantum ordinat eas ad rationales creaturas, et etiam ad seipsum; non quasi eis indigeat, sed propter suam bonitatem et nostram utilitatem. Concupiscimus enim aliquid et nobis et aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0044","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod nihil prohibet unum et idem secundum aliquid amari, et secundum aliquid odio haberi. Deus autem peccatores, inquantum sunt naturae quaedam, amat, sic enim et sunt, et ab ipso sunt. Inquantum vero peccatores sunt, non sunt, sed ab esse deficiunt, et hoc in eis a Deo non est. Unde secundum hoc ab ipso odio habentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0046","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0047","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0048","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus aequaliter diligat omnia. Dicitur enim Sap. VI, *aequaliter est ei cura de omnibus*. Sed providentia Dei, quam habet de rebus, est ex amore quo amat res. Ergo aequaliter amat omnia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0049","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0050","text":"Praeterea, amor Dei est eius essentia. Sed essentia Dei magis et minus non recipit. Ergo nec amor eius. Non igitur quaedam aliis magis amat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0051","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0052","text":"Praeterea, sicut amor Dei se extendit ad res creatas, ita et scientia et voluntas. Sed Deus non dicitur scire quaedam magis quam alia, neque magis velle. Ergo nec magis quaedam aliis diligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0053","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0054","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, super Ioann., *omnia diligit Deus quae fecit; et inter ea magis diligit creaturas rationales; et de illis eas amplius, quae sunt membra unigeniti sui; et multo magis ipsum unigenitum suum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0055","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum amare sit velle bonum alicui, duplici ratione potest aliquid magis vel minus amari. Uno modo, ex parte ipsius actus voluntatis, qui est magis vel minus intensus. Et sic Deus non magis quaedam aliis amat, quia omnia amat uno et simplici actu voluntatis, et semper eodem modo se habente. Alio modo, ex parte ipsius boni quod aliquis vult amato. Et sic dicimur aliquem magis alio amare, cui volumus maius bonum; quamvis non magis intensa voluntate. Et hoc modo necesse est dicere quod Deus quaedam aliis magis amat. Cum enim amor Dei sit causa bonitatis rerum, ut dictum est, non esset aliquid alio melius, si Deus non vellet uni maius bonum quam alteri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0057","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dicitur Deo aequaliter esse cura de omnibus, non quia aequalia bona sua cura omnibus dispenset; sed quia ex aequali sapientia et bonitate omnia administrat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0059","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de intensione amoris ex parte actus voluntatis, qui est divina essentia. Bonum autem quod Deus creaturae vult, non est divina essentia. Unde nihil prohibet illud intendi vel remitti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0061","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod intelligere et velle significant solum actus, non autem in sua significatione includunt aliqua obiecta, ex quorum diversitate possit dici Deus magis vel minus scire aut velle; sicut circa amorem dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0063","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0064","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0065","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non semper magis diligat meliora. Manifestum est enim quod Christus est melior toto genere humano, cum sit Deus et homo. Sed Deus magis dilexit genus humanum quam Christum, quia dicitur Rom. VIII, *proprio filio suo non pepercit, sed pro nobis omnibus tradidit illum*. Ergo Deus non semper magis diligit meliora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0066","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0067","text":"Praeterea, Angelus est melior homine, unde in Psalmo VIII dicitur de homine, *minuisti eum paulo minus ab Angelis*. Sed Deus plus dilexit hominem quam Angelum, dicitur enim Hebr. II, *nusquam Angelos apprehendit, sed semen Abrahae apprehendit*. Ergo Deus non semper magis diligit meliora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0068","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0069","text":"Praeterea, Petrus fuit melior Ioanne, quia plus Christum diligebat. Unde dominus, sciens hoc esse verum, interrogavit Petrum, dicens, *Simon Ioannis, diligis me plus his?* Sed tamen Christus plus dilexit Ioannem quam Petrum, ut enim dicit Augustinus, super illud Ioan. XXI, *Simon Ioannis diligis me? Hoc ipso signo Ioannes a ceteris discipulis discernitur; non quod solum eum, sed quod plus eum ceteris diligebat*. Non ergo semper magis diligit meliora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0070","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0071","text":"Praeterea, melior est innocens poenitente; *cum poenitentia sit secunda tabula post naufragium*, ut dicit Hieronymus. Sed Deus plus diligit poenitentem quam innocentem, quia plus de eo gaudet, dicitur enim Luc. XV, *dico vobis quod maius gaudium erit in caelo super uno peccatore poenitentiam agente, quam super nonaginta novem iustis, qui non indigent poenitentia*. Ergo Deus non semper magis diligit meliora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0072","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0073","text":"Praeterea, melior est iustus praescitus, quam peccator praedestinatus. Sed Deus plus diligit peccatorem praedestinatum, quia vult ei maius bonum, scilicet vitam aeternam. Ergo Deus non semper magis diligit meliora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0074","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0075","text":"Sed contra, unumquodque diligit sibi simile; ut patet per illud quod habetur Eccli. XIII, *omne animal diligit sibi simile*. Sed intantum aliquid est melius, inquantum est Deo similius. Ergo meliora magis diliguntur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0076","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere, secundum praedicta, quod Deus magis diligat meliora. Dictum est enim quod Deum diligere magis aliquid, nihil aliud est quam ei maius bonum velle, voluntas enim Dei est causa bonitatis in rebus. Et sic, ex hoc sunt aliqua meliora, quod Deus eis maius bonum vult. Unde sequitur quod meliora plus amet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0078","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0079","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus Christum diligit, non solum plus quam totum humanum genus, sed etiam magis quam totam universitatem creaturarum, quia scilicet ei maius bonum voluit, quia dedit ei nomen, quod est super omne nomen, ut verus Deus esset. Nec eius excellentiae deperiit ex hoc quod Deus dedit eum in mortem pro salute humani generis, quinimo ex hoc factus est victor gloriosus; *factus enim est principatus super humerum eius*, ut dicitur Isaiae IX.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0080","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod naturam humanam assumptam a Dei verbo in persona Christi, secundum praedicta, Deus plus amat quam omnes Angelos, et melior est, maxime ratione unionis. Sed loquendo de humana natura communiter, eam angelicae comparando, secundum ordinem ad gratiam et gloriam, aequalitas invenitur; cum eadem sit mensura hominis et Angeli, ut dicitur Apoc. XXI; ita tamen quod quidam Angeli quibusdam hominibus, et quidam homines quibusdam Angelis, quantum ad hoc, potiores inveniuntur. Sed quantum ad conditionem naturae, Angelus est melior homine. Nec ideo naturam humanam assumpsit Deus, quia hominem absolute plus diligeret, sed quia plus indigebat. Sicut bonus paterfamilias aliquid pretiosius dat servo aegrotanti, quod non dat filio sano.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0082","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod haec dubitatio de Petro et Ioanne multipliciter solvitur. Augustinus namque refert hoc ad mysterium, dicens quod vita activa, quae significatur per Petrum, plus diligit Deum quam vita contemplativa, quae significatur per Ioannem, quia magis sentit praesentis vitae angustias, et aestuantius ab eis liberari desiderat, et ad Deum ire. Contemplativam vero vitam Deus plus diligit, quia magis eam conservat; non enim finitur simul cum vita corporis, sicut vita activa. Quidam vero dicunt quod Petrus plus dilexit Christum in membris; et sic etiam a Christo plus fuit dilectus; unde ei Ecclesiam commendavit. Ioannes vero plus dilexit Christum in seipso; et sic etiam plus ab eo fuit dilectus; unde ei commendavit matrem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","text":"Alii vero dicunt quod incertum est quis horum plus Christum dilexerit amore caritatis, et similiter quem Deus plus dilexerit in ordine ad maiorem gloriam vitae aeternae. Sed Petrus dicitur plus dilexisse, quantum ad quandam promptitudinem vel fervorem, Ioannes vero plus dilectus, quantum ad quaedam familiaritatis indicia, quae Christus ei magis demonstrabat, propter eius iuventutem et puritatem. Alii vero dicunt quod Christus plus dilexit Petrum, quantum ad excellentius donum caritatis, Ioannem vero plus, quantum ad donum intellectus. Unde simpliciter Petrus fuit melior, et magis dilectus, sed Ioannes secundum quid. Praesumptuosum tamen videtur hoc diiudicare, quia, ut dicitur Prov. XVI, *spirituum ponderator est dominus*, et non alius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0085","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod poenitentes et innocentes se habent sicut excedentia et excessa. Nam sive sint innocentes, sive poenitentes, illi sunt meliores et magis dilecti, qui plus habent de gratia. Ceteris tamen paribus, innocentia dignior est et magis dilecta. Dicitur tamen Deus plus gaudere de poenitente quam de innocente, quia plerumque poenitentes cautiores, humiliores et ferventiores resurgunt. Unde Gregorius dicit ibidem, quod *dux in praelio eum militem plus diligit, qui post fugam conversus, fortiter hostem premit, quam qui nunquam fugit, nec unquam fortiter fecit*. Vel, alia ratione, quia aequale donum gratiae plus est, comparatum poenitenti, qui meruit poenam, quam innocenti, qui non meruit. Sicut centum marcae maius donum est, si dentur pauperi, quam si dentur regi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0087","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, cum voluntas Dei sit causa bonitatis in rebus, secundum illud tempus pensanda est bonitas eius qui amatur a Deo, secundum quod dandum est ei ex bonitate divina aliquod bonum. Secundum ergo illud tempus quo praedestinato peccatori dandum est ex divina voluntate maius bonum, melior est; licet secundum aliquod aliud tempus, sit peior; quia et secundum aliquod tempus, non est nec bonus neque malus.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2975c7b604c691a9cdb0e7eee75ea0134977885c5d0faa157ed2e5f10fdfdb56","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140058828029Z_9f8ef0ad","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 20","source_sha256":"2975c7b604c691a9cdb0e7eee75ea0134977885c5d0faa157ed2e5f10fdfdb56","sha256":"099f254fb11cb31bc3492a1aa2759c5a856ec23d8cfd7ed03667ab949f720c83","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:00:58.828075+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0002","text":"[I q. 20 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider those things that pertain absolutely to the will of God. Now, in the appetitive part, there are found in us both passions of the soul, such as joy, love, and the like; and habits of the moral virtues, such as justice, fortitude, and the like. Hence, first we shall consider the love of God; second, the justice of God and His mercy. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether there is love in God. Second, whether He loves all things. Third, whether He loves one thing more than another. Fourth, whether He loves better things more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0005","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that love is not in God. For no passion is in God. Love is a passion. Therefore love is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0007","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0008","text":"Further, love, anger, sorrow, and the like are divided against one another. But sorrow and anger are said of God only metaphorically. Therefore neither is love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0009","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0010","text":"Further, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, *love is a unitive and binding force*. But this cannot have a place in God, since He is simple. Therefore there is no love in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0011","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in I John IV, *God is charity*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0013","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be held that love is in God. For the first movement of the will, and of every appetitive power, is love. For since the act of the will, and of every appetitive power, tends toward good and evil as toward its proper objects; while good is principally and through itself the object of will and appetite, but evil secondarily and through another, namely insofar as it is opposed to good, the acts of will and appetite that regard good must naturally be prior to those that regard evil; as joy is prior to sorrow, and love to hatred. For what is through itself is always prior to what is through another. Again, what is more common is naturally prior; hence the intellect also has a prior order toward common truth than toward certain particular truths.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","text":"But there are certain acts of will and appetite that regard good under some special condition, as joy and delight concern a good that is present and possessed; whereas desire and hope concern a good not yet obtained. Love, however, regards good in common, whether possessed or not possessed. Hence love is naturally the first act of will and appetite. And on this account, all other appetitive movements presuppose love as their first root. For no one desires anything except a loved good, nor does anyone rejoice except in a loved good. Hatred also concerns only that which is contrary to the thing loved. And similarly sorrow, and other things of this kind, are manifestly referred to love as to their first principle. Hence, wherever there is will or appetite, there must be love; for when the first is removed, the others are removed. But it has been shown that there is will in God. Hence love must be posited in Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0016","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the cognitive power does not move except through the appetitive power. And just as in us universal reason moves through particular reason, as is said in *On the Soul* III, so the intellective appetite, which is called the will, moves in us through the sensitive appetite. Hence the proximate mover of the body in us is the sensitive appetite. Therefore, the act of the sensitive appetite is always accompanied by some bodily change, especially about the heart, which is the first principle of movement in an animal. Thus, then, the acts of the sensitive appetite, insofar as they have a bodily change attached to them, are called passions; but not the acts of the will. Therefore love and joy and delight, insofar as they signify acts of the sensitive appetite, are passions; but not insofar as they signify acts of the intellective appetite. And thus they are posited in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","text":"Hence the Philosopher says, in *Ethics* VII, that *God rejoices by one simple operation*. And by the same reasoning, He loves without passion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0019","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that in the passions of the sensitive appetite, one must consider something as material, namely bodily change; and something as formal, which is on the side of the appetite. Thus in anger, as is said in *On the Soul* I, the material element is the heating of the blood around the heart, or something of this sort; but the formal element is the appetite for vengeance. But again, on the side of what is formal, in some of these a certain imperfection is designated; as in desire, which is for a good not possessed; and in sorrow, which is for an evil possessed. And the same reasoning applies to anger, which presupposes sorrow. But some designate no imperfection, such as love and joy. Therefore, since none of these belongs to God according to what is material in them, as has been said, those which imply imperfection cannot belong to God even formally except metaphorically, on account of a likeness of effect, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","text":"But those which do not imply imperfection are properly said of God, such as love and joy, yet without passion, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0022","text":"[I q. 20 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that the act of love always tends toward two things: namely, toward the good that one wills for someone, and toward the one for whom one wills good. For this is properly to love someone: to will good for him. Hence, in that one loves oneself, one wills good for oneself. And thus one seeks to unite that good to oneself, insofar as one can. And to this extent love is called a unitive force, even in God, but without composition, because that good which He wills for Himself is not other than Himself, who is good through His essence, as was shown above. But in that one loves another, one wills good for him. And thus one uses him as oneself, referring good to him as to oneself. And to this extent love is called a binding force, because it joins another to oneself, having oneself toward him as toward oneself. And thus divine love too is a binding force, without composition in God, insofar as He wills goods for others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0025","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God does not love all things. For, according to Dionysius, chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, love places the lover outside himself and, in a certain way, transfers him into the beloved. But it is unfitting to say that God, placed outside Himself, is transferred into other things. Therefore, it is unfitting to say that God loves things other than Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0027","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0028","text":"Moreover, the love of God is eternal. But things that are other than God are not from eternity except in God. Therefore, God does not love them except in Himself. But insofar as they are in Him, they are not other than Him. Therefore, God does not love things other than Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0029","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0030","text":"Moreover, there is a twofold love, namely, that of concupiscence and that of friendship. But God does not love irrational creatures with the love of concupiscence, since He needs nothing outside Himself; nor with the love of friendship, since friendship cannot be had toward irrational things, as is clear from the Philosopher in *Ethics* VIII. Therefore, God does not love all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0031","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0032","text":"Moreover, it is said in the Psalm, *You hate all who work iniquity*. But nothing is at the same time hated and loved. Therefore, God does not love all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0033","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0034","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Wisdom XI, *you love all things that are, and hate none of the things that you have made*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0035","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","text":"I answer that it must be said that God loves all existing things. For all existing things, insofar as they are, are good; for the very being of each thing is a certain good, and likewise every perfection of it. But it was shown above that the will of God is the cause of all things, and thus it is necessary that a thing have being, or whatever good, insofar as it is willed by God. Therefore, God wills some good for every existing thing. Hence, since to love is nothing other than to will good to someone, it is manifest that God loves all things that are. Yet not in the way that we do. For since our will is not the cause of the goodness of things, but is moved by it as by an object, our love, by which we will good to someone, is not the cause of that one's goodness; but conversely, his goodness, whether true or estimated, provokes the love by which we will both that the good he has be preserved and that what he does not have be added, and we work toward this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","text":"But God's love infuses and creates goodness in things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0038","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the lover is thus made outside himself, transferred into the beloved, insofar as he wills good to the beloved and provides for him by his providence, as also for himself. Hence Dionysius too says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*: *But this also must be dared for the truth: that He Himself, the cause of all things, through the abundance of loving goodness, goes outside Himself unto all existing things by His providences*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0040","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0041","text":"To the second it must be said that, although creatures were not from eternity except in God, nevertheless, by the fact that they were from eternity in God, God from eternity knew things in their proper natures, and for the same reason loved them. Just as we too, through the likenesses of things that are in us, know things existing in themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0042","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","text":"To the third it must be said that friendship can be had only toward rational creatures, in whom there can be a return of love, and a sharing in the works of life, and to whom it can happen to fare well or badly, according to fortune and happiness; just as benevolence properly belongs to them. But irrational creatures cannot attain to loving God, nor to a sharing in the intellectual and blessed life by which God lives. Thus, therefore, properly speaking, God does not love irrational creatures with the love of friendship, but with a love as it were of concupiscence, insofar as He orders them to rational creatures, and also to Himself; not as though He needed them, but on account of His goodness and our usefulness. For we desire something both for ourselves and for others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0044","text":"[I q. 20 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","text":"To the fourth it must be said that nothing prevents one and the same thing from being loved in one respect and hated in another respect. But God loves sinners insofar as they are certain natures; for thus they are, and are from Him. But insofar as they are sinners, they are not, but fall short of being, and this in them is not from God. Hence, in this respect, they are hated by Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0046","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0047","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0048","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that God loves all things equally. For it is said in Wisdom VI, *He has equal care for all things*. But God's providence, which He has for things, proceeds from the love by which He loves things. Therefore, He loves all things equally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0049","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0050","text":"Further, God's love is His essence. But God's essence does not admit of more and less. Therefore neither does His love. Therefore He does not love some things more than others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0051","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0052","text":"Further, just as God's love extends to created things, so too do His knowledge and will. But God is not said to know some things more than others, nor to will more. Therefore neither does He love some things more than others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0053","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0054","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, commenting on John: *God loves all things that He has made; and among them He loves rational creatures more; and among those, more abundantly those which are members of His only-begotten Son; and much more His only-begotten Son Himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0055","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","text":"I answer that, since to love is to will good to someone, something can be loved more or less in two ways. In one way, on the part of the very act of will, which is more or less intense. And thus God does not love some things more than others, because He loves all things by one simple act of will, always disposed in the same way. In another way, on the part of the good itself which someone wills for the beloved. And thus we are said to love one person more than another when we will a greater good for him, although not with a more intense will. And in this way it must be said that God loves some things more than others. For since the love of God is the cause of the goodness of things, as has been said, one thing would not be better than another if God did not will a greater good for one than for the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0057","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God is said to have equal care for all, not because He dispenses equal goods to all by His care, but because He governs all things from equal wisdom and goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0059","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060","text":"To the second it must be said that this argument proceeds concerning the intensity of love on the part of the act of will, which is the divine essence. But the good which God wills for the creature is not the divine essence. Hence nothing prevents it from being increased or diminished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0061","text":"[I q. 20 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062","text":"To the third it must be said that to understand and to will signify acts only, but do not include in their signification any objects from the diversity of which God might be said to know or will more or less, as was said concerning love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0063","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0064","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0065","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that God does not always love better things more. For it is manifest that Christ is better than the whole human race, since He is God and man. But God loved the human race more than Christ, because it is said in Romans VIII: *He did not spare His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all*. Therefore God does not always love better things more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0066","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0067","text":"Further, an angel is better than a human being; hence it is said of man in Psalm VIII: *You have made him a little lower than the angels*. But God loved man more than the angel, for it is said in Hebrews II: *Nowhere does He take hold of angels, but He takes hold of the seed of Abraham*. Therefore God does not always love better things more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0068","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0069","text":"Further, Peter was better than John because he loved Christ more. Hence the Lord, knowing this to be true, questioned Peter, saying: *Simon, son of John, do you love me more than these?* Yet Christ loved John more than Peter; for as Augustine says, commenting on John XXI, *Simon, son of John, do you love me? By this very sign John is distinguished from the other disciples: not because He alone loved him, but because He loved him more than the others*. Therefore He does not always love better things more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0070","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0071","text":"Further, the innocent person is better than the penitent; *since repentance is the second plank after shipwreck*, as Jerome says. But God loves the penitent more than the innocent, because He rejoices more over him; for it is said in Luke XV: *I say to you that there shall be greater joy in heaven over one sinner doing penance than over ninety-nine just persons who have no need of repentance*. Therefore God does not always love better things more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0072","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0073","text":"Moreover, the foreknown just man is better than the predestined sinner. But God loves the predestined sinner more, because He wills for him a greater good, namely eternal life. Therefore God does not always love better things more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0074","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0075","text":"On the contrary, each thing loves that which is like itself; as is clear from what is found in Ecclesiasticus XIII: *every animal loves that which is like itself*. But something is better insofar as it is more like God. Therefore, better things are loved more by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0076","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","text":"I answer that it must be said, according to what has been stated above, that God loves better things more. For it has been said that for God to love something more is nothing other than to will for it a greater good, since the will of God is the cause of goodness in things. And thus, things are better because God wills for them a greater good. Hence it follows that He loves better things more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0078","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0079","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God loves Christ not only more than the whole human race, but also more than the whole universe of creatures, because He willed for Him a greater good, since He gave Him a name which is above every name, that He might be true God. Nor was anything lost from His excellence by the fact that God gave Him over to death for the salvation of the human race; indeed, by this He was made a glorious victor: *for dominion was made upon His shoulder*, as is said in Isaiah IX.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0080","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","text":"To the second it must be said that God loves the human nature assumed by the Word of God in the person of Christ more than all the Angels, according to what has been said above, and it is better, especially by reason of the union. But speaking of human nature in general, comparing it with the angelic according to the order to grace and glory, an equality is found; since the measure of man and of Angel is the same, as is said in Apocalypse XXI. Yet some Angels are found superior in this respect to certain men, and certain men to certain Angels. But as regards the condition of nature, an Angel is better than a human being. Nor did God assume human nature because He loved man more absolutely, but because he was more in need. Just as a good householder gives something more precious to a sick servant, which he does not give to a healthy son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0082","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","text":"To the third it must be said that this question concerning Peter and John is resolved in many ways. For Augustine refers it to a mystery, saying that the active life, which is signified by Peter, loves God more than the contemplative life, which is signified by John, because it feels more keenly the straits of the present life, and more ardently desires to be freed from them and to go to God. But God loves the contemplative life more, because He preserves it more; for it does not end together with the life of the body, as the active life does. Some, however, say that Peter loved Christ more in His members; and thus he too was more loved by Christ; hence He entrusted the Church to him. But John loved Christ more in Himself; and thus he too was more loved by Him; hence He entrusted His mother to him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","text":"Others, however, say that it is uncertain which of these loved Christ more with the love of charity, and likewise whom God loved more in the order to the greater glory of eternal life. But Peter is said to have loved more with respect to a certain readiness or fervor, whereas John was loved more with respect to certain signs of familiarity, which Christ showed him more, on account of his youth and purity. Others, however, say that Christ loved Peter more as to the more excellent gift of charity, but John more as to the gift of understanding. Hence Peter was better and more loved simply speaking, but John in a certain respect. Yet it seems presumptuous to judge this, because, as is said in Proverbs XVI, *the Lord is the weigher of spirits*, and no one else.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0085","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","text":"To the fourth it must be said that penitents and the innocent stand as those exceeding and those exceeded. For whether they are innocent or penitent, those are better and more loved who have more grace. Other things being equal, however, innocence is worthier and more loved. Yet God is said to rejoice more over a penitent than over an innocent, because penitents for the most part rise again more cautious, more humble, and more fervent. Hence Gregory says in the same place that *a leader in battle loves more that soldier who, after fleeing, having turned back, presses the enemy bravely, than one who never fled and never acted bravely*. Or for another reason: because an equal gift of grace is greater in comparison with a penitent, who deserved punishment, than with an innocent person, who did not deserve it. Just as one hundred marks are a greater gift if they are given to a poor man than if they are given to a king.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0087","text":"[I q. 20 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","text":"To the fifth it must be said that, since the will of God is the cause of goodness in things, the goodness of him who is loved by God must be assessed according to that time at which some good is to be given him from divine goodness. Therefore, at the time at which a greater good is to be given to the predestined sinner from the divine will, he is better; although at some other time he is worse, because at some time he is neither good nor bad.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088"]}],"source_digest":"560579c72b0ef30fc22116118467e789fab5f901a786221941c28a176ae6bb83","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 20"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 20","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13","title":"First Part · Question 21","order":22,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_13","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 21 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29324)\n\nPost considerationem divini amoris, de iustitia et misericordia eius agendum est. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in Deo sit iustitia. Secundo, utrum iustitia eius veritas dici possit. Tertio, utrum in Deo sit misericordia. Quarto, utrum in omni opere Dei sit iustitia et misericordia.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29325)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit iustitia. Iustitia enim contra temperantiam dividitur. Temperantia autem non est in Deo. Ergo nec iustitia.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29326)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque facit omnia pro libito suae voluntatis, non secundum iustitiam operatur. Sed, sicut dicit apostolus, ad Ephes. I, Deus *operatur omnia secundum consilium suae voluntatis*. Non ergo ei iustitia debet attribui.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29327)\n\nPraeterea, actus iustitiae est reddere debitum. Sed Deus nulli est debitor. Ergo Deo non competit iustitia.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29328)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid est in Deo, est eius essentia. Sed hoc non competit iustitiae, dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Hebdomad., quod *bonum essentiam, iustum vero actum respicit*. Ergo iustitia non competit Deo.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29329)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo X, *iustus dominus, et iustitias dilexit*.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29330)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod duplex est species iustitiae. Una, quae consistit in mutua datione et acceptione, ut puta quae consistit in emptione et venditione, et aliis huiusmodi communicationibus vel commutationibus. Et haec dicitur a philosopho, in V Ethic., iustitia commutativa, vel directiva commutationum sive communicationum. Et haec non competit Deo, quia, ut dicit apostolus, Rom. XI, *quis prior dedit illi, et retribuetur ei?* Alia, quae consistit in distribuendo, et dicitur distributiva iustitia, secundum quam aliquis gubernator vel dispensator dat unicuique secundum suam dignitatem. Sicut igitur ordo congruus familiae, vel cuiuscumque multitudinis gubernatae, demonstrat huiusmodi iustitiam in gubernante; ita ordo universi, qui apparet tam in rebus naturalibus quam in rebus voluntariis, demonstrat Dei iustitiam. Unde dicit Dionysius, VIII cap. de Div. Nom., *oportet videre in hoc veram Dei esse iustitiam, quod omnibus tribuit propria, secundum uniuscuiusque existentium dignitatem; et uniuscuiusque naturam in proprio salvat ordine et virtute*.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29331)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod virtutum moralium quaedam sunt circa passiones; sicut temperantia circa concupiscentias, fortitudo circa timores et audacias, mansuetudo circa iram. Et huiusmodi virtutes Deo attribui non possunt, nisi secundum metaphoram, quia in Deo neque passiones sunt, ut supra dictum est; neque appetitus sensitivus, in quo sunt huiusmodi virtutes sicut in subiecto, ut dicit philosophus in III Ethic. Quaedam vero virtutes morales sunt circa operationes; ut puta circa dationes et sumptus, ut iustitia et liberalitas et magnificentia; quae etiam non sunt in parte sensitiva, sed in voluntate. Unde nihil prohibet huiusmodi virtutes in Deo ponere, non tamen circa actiones civiles sed circa actiones Deo convenientes. Ridiculum est enim secundum virtutes politicas Deum laudare, ut dicit philosophus in X Ethic.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29332)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum bonum intellectum sit obiectum voluntatis, impossibile est Deum velle nisi quod ratio suae sapientiae habet. Quae quidem est sicut lex iustitiae, secundum quam eius voluntas recta et iusta est. Unde quod secundum suam voluntatem facit, iuste facit, sicut et nos quod secundum legem facimus, iuste facimus. Sed nos quidem secundum legem alicuius superioris, Deus autem sibi ipsi est lex.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29333)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod unicuique debetur quod suum est. Dicitur autem esse suum alicuius, quod ad ipsum ordinatur; sicut servus est domini, et non e converso; nam liberum est quod sui causa est. In nomine ergo debiti, importatur quidam ordo exigentiae vel necessitatis alicuius ad quod ordinatur. Est autem duplex ordo considerandus in rebus. Unus, quo aliquid creatum ordinatur ad aliud creatum, sicut partes ordinantur ad totum, et accidentia ad substantias, et unaquaeque res ad suum finem. Alius ordo, quo omnia creata ordinantur in Deum. Sic igitur et debitum attendi potest dupliciter in operatione divina, aut secundum quod aliquid debetur Deo; aut secundum quod aliquid debetur rei creatae. Et utroque modo Deus debitum reddit. Debitum enim est Deo, ut impleatur in rebus id quod eius sapientia et voluntas habet, et quod suam bonitatem manifestat, et secundum hoc iustitia Dei respicit decentiam ipsius, secundum quam reddit sibi quod sibi debetur. Debitum etiam est alicui rei creatae, quod habeat id quod ad ipsam ordinatur, sicut homini, quod habeat manus, et quod ei alia animalia serviant. Et sic etiam Deus operatur iustitiam, quando dat unicuique quod ei debetur secundum rationem suae naturae et conditionis. Sed hoc debitum dependet ex primo, quia hoc unicuique debetur, quod est ordinatum ad ipsum secundum ordinem divinae sapientiae. Et licet Deus hoc modo debitum alicui det, non tamen ipse est debitor, quia ipse ad alia non ordinatur, sed potius alia in ipsum. Et ideo iustitia quandoque dicitur in Deo condecentia suae bonitatis; quandoque vero retributio pro meritis. Et utrumque modum tangit Anselmus, dicens, *cum punis malos, iustum est, quia illorum meritis convenit; cum vero parcis malis, iustum est, quia bonitati tuae condecens est*.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29334)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, licet iustitia respiciat actum, non tamen per hoc excluditur quin sit essentia Dei, quia etiam id quod est de essentia rei, potest esse principium actionis. Sed bonum non semper respicit actum, quia aliquid dicitur esse bonum, non solum secundum quod agit, sed etiam secundum quod in sua essentia perfectum est. Et propter hoc ibidem dicitur quod bonum comparatur ad iustum, sicut generale ad speciale.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 21 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29335)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod iustitia Dei non sit veritas. Iustitia enim est in voluntate, est enim rectitudo voluntatis, ut dicit Anselmus. Veritas autem est in intellectu, secundum philosophum in VI Metaphys. et in VI Ethic. Ergo iustitia non pertinet ad veritatem.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29336)\n\nPraeterea, veritas, secundum philosophum in IV Ethic., est quaedam alia virtus a iustitia. Non ergo veritas pertinet ad rationem iustitiae.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29337)\n\nSed contra est quod in Psalmo LXXXIV dicitur, *misericordia et veritas obviaverunt sibi*; et ponitur ibi veritas pro iustitia.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29338)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod veritas consistit in adaequatione intellectus et rei, sicut supra dictum est. Intellectus autem qui est causa rei, comparatur ad ipsam sicut regula et mensura, e converso autem est de intellectu qui accipit scientiam a rebus. Quando igitur res sunt mensura et regula intellectus, veritas consistit in hoc, quod intellectus adaequatur rei, ut in nobis accidit, ex eo enim quod res est vel non est, opinio nostra et oratio vera vel falsa est. Sed quando intellectus est regula vel mensura rerum, veritas consistit in hoc, quod res adaequantur intellectui, sicut dicitur artifex facere verum opus, quando concordat arti. Sicut autem se habent artificiata ad artem, ita se habent opera iusta ad legem cui concordant. Iustitia igitur Dei, quae constituit ordinem in rebus conformem rationi sapientiae suae, quae est lex eius, convenienter veritas nominatur. Et sic etiam dicitur in nobis veritas iustitiae.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29339)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod iustitia, quantum ad legem regulantem, est in ratione vel intellectu, sed quantum ad imperium, quo opera regulantur secundum legem, est in voluntate.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29340)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod veritas illa de qua loquitur philosophus ibi, est quaedam virtus per quam aliquis demonstrat se talem in dictis vel factis, qualis est. Et sic consistit in conformitate signi ad significatum, non autem in conformitate effectus ad causam et regulam, sicut de veritate iustitiae dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 21 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29341)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod misericordia Deo non competat. Misericordia enim est species tristitiae, ut dicit Damascenus. Sed tristitia non est in Deo. Ergo nec misericordia.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29342)\n\nPraeterea, misericordia est relaxatio iustitiae. Sed Deus non potest praetermittere id quod ad iustitiam suam pertinet. Dicitur enim II ad Tim. II, *si non credimus, ille fidelis permanet, seipsum negare non potest*, negaret autem seipsum, ut dicit Glossa ibidem, si dicta sua negaret. Ergo misericordia Deo non competit.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29343)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CX, *miserator et misericors dominus*.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29344)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod misericordia est Deo maxime attribuenda, tamen secundum effectum, non secundum passionis affectum. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod misericors dicitur aliquis quasi habens miserum cor, quia scilicet afficitur ex miseria alterius per tristitiam, ac si esset eius propria miseria. Et ex hoc sequitur quod operetur ad depellendam miseriam alterius, sicut miseriam propriam, et hic est misericordiae effectus. Tristari ergo de miseria alterius non competit Deo, sed repellere miseriam alterius, hoc maxime ei competit, ut per miseriam quemcumque defectum intelligamus. Defectus autem non tolluntur, nisi per alicuius bonitatis perfectionem, prima autem origo bonitatis Deus est, ut supra ostensum est. Sed considerandum est quod elargiri perfectiones rebus, pertinet quidem et ad bonitatem divinam, et ad iustitiam, et ad liberalitatem, et misericordiam, tamen secundum aliam et aliam rationem. Communicatio enim perfectionum, absolute considerata, pertinet ad bonitatem, ut supra ostensum est. Sed inquantum perfectiones rebus a Deo dantur secundum earum proportionem, pertinet ad iustitiam, ut dictum est supra. Inquantum vero non attribuit rebus perfectiones propter utilitatem suam, sed solum propter suam bonitatem, pertinet ad liberalitatem. Inquantum vero perfectiones datae rebus a Deo, omnem defectum expellunt, pertinet ad misericordiam.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29345)\n\nAd primum igitur dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de misericordia, quantum ad passionis affectum.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29346)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus misericorditer agit, non quidem contra iustitiam suam faciendo, sed aliquid supra iustitiam operando, sicut si alicui cui debentur centum denarii, aliquis ducentos det de suo, tamen non contra iustitiam facit, sed liberaliter vel misericorditer operatur. Et similiter si aliquis offensam in se commissam remittat. Qui enim aliquid remittit, quodammodo donat illud, unde apostolus remissionem donationem vocat, Ephes. V, *donate invicem, sicut et Christus vobis donavit*. Ex quo patet quod misericordia non tollit iustitiam, sed est quaedam iustitiae plenitudo. Unde dicitur Iac. II, quod *misericordia superexaltat iudicium*.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29347)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non in omnibus Dei operibus sit misericordia et iustitia. Quaedam enim opera Dei attribuuntur misericordiae, ut iustificatio impii, quaedam vero iustitiae, ut damnatio impiorum. Unde dicitur Iac. II, *iudicium sine misericordia fiet ei qui non fecerit misericordiam*. Non ergo in omni opere Dei apparet misericordia et iustitia.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29348)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus, ad Rom. XV, conversionem Iudaeorum attribuit iustitiae et veritati; conversionem autem gentium, misericordiae. Ergo non in quolibet opere Dei est iustitia et misericordia.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29349)\n\nPraeterea, multi iusti in hoc mundo affliguntur. Hoc autem est iniustum. Non ergo in omni opere Dei est iustitia et misericordia.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29350)\n\nPraeterea, iustitiae est reddere debitum, misericordiae autem sublevare miseriam, et sic tam iustitia quam misericordia aliquid praesupponit in suo opere. Sed creatio nihil praesupponit. Ergo in creatione neque misericordia est, neque iustitia.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29351)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XXIV, *omnes viae domini misericordia et veritas*.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29352)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est quod in quolibet opere Dei misericordia et veritas inveniantur; si tamen misericordia pro remotione cuiuscumque defectus accipiatur; quamvis non omnis defectus proprie possit dici miseria, sed solum defectus rationalis naturae, quam contingit esse felicem; nam miseria felicitati opponitur. Huius autem necessitatis ratio est, quia, cum debitum quod ex divina iustitia redditur, sit vel debitum Deo, vel debitum alicui creaturae, neutrum potest in aliquo opere Dei praetermitti. Non enim potest facere aliquid Deus, quod non sit conveniens sapientiae et bonitati ipsius; secundum quem modum diximus aliquid esse debitum Deo. Similiter etiam quidquid in rebus creatis facit, secundum convenientem ordinem et proportionem facit; in quo consistit ratio iustitiae. Et sic oportet in omni opere Dei esse iustitiam. Opus autem divinae iustitiae semper praesupponit opus misericordiae, et in eo fundatur. Creaturae enim non debetur aliquid, nisi propter aliquid in eo praeexistens, vel praeconsideratum, et rursus, si illud creaturae debetur, hoc erit propter aliquid prius. Et cum non sit procedere in infinitum, oportet devenire ad aliquid quod ex sola bonitate divinae voluntatis dependeat, quae est ultimus finis. Utpote si dicamus quod habere manus debitum est homini propter animam rationalem; animam vero rationalem habere, ad hoc quod sit homo; hominem vero esse, propter divinam bonitatem. Et sic in quolibet opere Dei apparet misericordia, quantum ad primam radicem eius. Cuius virtus salvatur in omnibus consequentibus; et etiam vehementius in eis operatur, sicut causa primaria vehementius influit quam causa secunda. Et propter hoc etiam ea quae alicui creaturae debentur, Deus, ex abundantia suae bonitatis, largius dispensat quam exigat proportio rei. Minus enim est quod sufficeret ad conservandum ordinem iustitiae, quam quod divina bonitas confert, quae omnem proportionem creaturae excedit.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29353)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod quaedam opera attribuuntur iustitiae et quaedam misericordiae, quia in quibusdam vehementius apparet iustitia, in quibusdam misericordia. Et tamen in damnatione reproborum apparet misericordia, non quidem totaliter relaxans, sed aliqualiter allevians, dum punit citra condignum. Et in iustificatione impii apparet iustitia, dum culpas relaxat propter dilectionem, quam tamen ipse misericorditer infundit, sicut de Magdalena legitur, Luc. VII, *dimissa sunt ei peccata multa, quoniam dilexit multum*.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29354)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod iustitia et misericordia Dei apparet in conversione Iudaeorum et gentium, sed aliqua ratio iustitiae apparet in conversione Iudaeorum, quae non apparet in conversione gentium, sicut quod salvati sunt propter promissiones patribus factas.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29355)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in hoc etiam quod iusti puniuntur in hoc mundo, apparet iustitia et misericordia; inquantum per huiusmodi afflictiones aliqua levia in eis purgantur, et ab affectu terrenorum in Deum magis eriguntur; secundum illud Gregorii, *mala quae in hoc mundo nos premunt, ad Deum nos ire compellunt*.\n\n[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29356)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, licet creationi non praesupponatur aliquid in rerum natura, praesupponitur tamen aliquid in Dei cognitione. Et secundum hoc etiam salvatur ibi ratio iustitiae, inquantum res in esse producitur, secundum quod convenit divinae sapientiae et bonitati. Et salvatur quodammodo ratio misericordiae, inquantum res de non esse in esse mutatur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0002","text":"[I q. 21 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0003","text":"Post considerationem divini amoris, de iustitia et misericordia eius agendum est. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in Deo sit iustitia. Secundo, utrum iustitia eius veritas dici possit. Tertio, utrum in Deo sit misericordia. Quarto, utrum in omni opere Dei sit iustitia et misericordia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0005","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit iustitia. Iustitia enim contra temperantiam dividitur. Temperantia autem non est in Deo. Ergo nec iustitia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0007","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quicumque facit omnia pro libito suae voluntatis, non secundum iustitiam operatur. Sed, sicut dicit apostolus, ad Ephes. I, Deus *operatur omnia secundum consilium suae voluntatis*. Non ergo ei iustitia debet attribui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0009","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0010","text":"Praeterea, actus iustitiae est reddere debitum. Sed Deus nulli est debitor. Ergo Deo non competit iustitia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0011","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0012","text":"Praeterea, quidquid est in Deo, est eius essentia. Sed hoc non competit iustitiae, dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Hebdomad., quod *bonum essentiam, iustum vero actum respicit*. Ergo iustitia non competit Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0013","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo X, *iustus dominus, et iustitias dilexit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0015","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod duplex est species iustitiae. Una, quae consistit in mutua datione et acceptione, ut puta quae consistit in emptione et venditione, et aliis huiusmodi communicationibus vel commutationibus. Et haec dicitur a philosopho, in V Ethic., iustitia commutativa, vel directiva commutationum sive communicationum. Et haec non competit Deo, quia, ut dicit apostolus, Rom. XI, *quis prior dedit illi, et retribuetur ei?* Alia, quae consistit in distribuendo, et dicitur distributiva iustitia, secundum quam aliquis gubernator vel dispensator dat unicuique secundum suam dignitatem. Sicut igitur ordo congruus familiae, vel cuiuscumque multitudinis gubernatae, demonstrat huiusmodi iustitiam in gubernante; ita ordo universi, qui apparet tam in rebus naturalibus quam in rebus voluntariis, demonstrat Dei iustitiam. Unde dicit Dionysius, VIII cap. de Div.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0017","text":"Nom., *oportet videre in hoc veram Dei esse iustitiam, quod omnibus tribuit propria, secundum uniuscuiusque existentium dignitatem; et uniuscuiusque naturam in proprio salvat ordine et virtute*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0018","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod virtutum moralium quaedam sunt circa passiones; sicut temperantia circa concupiscentias, fortitudo circa timores et audacias, mansuetudo circa iram. Et huiusmodi virtutes Deo attribui non possunt, nisi secundum metaphoram, quia in Deo neque passiones sunt, ut supra dictum est; neque appetitus sensitivus, in quo sunt huiusmodi virtutes sicut in subiecto, ut dicit philosophus in III Ethic. Quaedam vero virtutes morales sunt circa operationes; ut puta circa dationes et sumptus, ut iustitia et liberalitas et magnificentia; quae etiam non sunt in parte sensitiva, sed in voluntate. Unde nihil prohibet huiusmodi virtutes in Deo ponere, non tamen circa actiones civiles sed circa actiones Deo convenientes. Ridiculum est enim secundum virtutes politicas Deum laudare, ut dicit philosophus in X Ethic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0020","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum bonum intellectum sit obiectum voluntatis, impossibile est Deum velle nisi quod ratio suae sapientiae habet. Quae quidem est sicut lex iustitiae, secundum quam eius voluntas recta et iusta est. Unde quod secundum suam voluntatem facit, iuste facit, sicut et nos quod secundum legem facimus, iuste facimus. Sed nos quidem secundum legem alicuius superioris, Deus autem sibi ipsi est lex.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0022","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod unicuique debetur quod suum est. Dicitur autem esse suum alicuius, quod ad ipsum ordinatur; sicut servus est domini, et non e converso; nam liberum est quod sui causa est. In nomine ergo debiti, importatur quidam ordo exigentiae vel necessitatis alicuius ad quod ordinatur. Est autem duplex ordo considerandus in rebus. Unus, quo aliquid creatum ordinatur ad aliud creatum, sicut partes ordinantur ad totum, et accidentia ad substantias, et unaquaeque res ad suum finem. Alius ordo, quo omnia creata ordinantur in Deum. Sic igitur et debitum attendi potest dupliciter in operatione divina, aut secundum quod aliquid debetur Deo; aut secundum quod aliquid debetur rei creatae. Et utroque modo Deus debitum reddit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","text":"Debitum enim est Deo, ut impleatur in rebus id quod eius sapientia et voluntas habet, et quod suam bonitatem manifestat, et secundum hoc iustitia Dei respicit decentiam ipsius, secundum quam reddit sibi quod sibi debetur. Debitum etiam est alicui rei creatae, quod habeat id quod ad ipsam ordinatur, sicut homini, quod habeat manus, et quod ei alia animalia serviant. Et sic etiam Deus operatur iustitiam, quando dat unicuique quod ei debetur secundum rationem suae naturae et conditionis. Sed hoc debitum dependet ex primo, quia hoc unicuique debetur, quod est ordinatum ad ipsum secundum ordinem divinae sapientiae. Et licet Deus hoc modo debitum alicui det, non tamen ipse est debitor, quia ipse ad alia non ordinatur, sed potius alia in ipsum. Et ideo iustitia quandoque dicitur in Deo condecentia suae bonitatis; quandoque vero retributio pro meritis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0025","text":"Et utrumque modum tangit Anselmus, dicens, *cum punis malos, iustum est, quia illorum meritis convenit; cum vero parcis malis, iustum est, quia bonitati tuae condecens est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0026","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0027","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, licet iustitia respiciat actum, non tamen per hoc excluditur quin sit essentia Dei, quia etiam id quod est de essentia rei, potest esse principium actionis. Sed bonum non semper respicit actum, quia aliquid dicitur esse bonum, non solum secundum quod agit, sed etiam secundum quod in sua essentia perfectum est. Et propter hoc ibidem dicitur quod bonum comparatur ad iustum, sicut generale ad speciale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0028","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0029","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0030","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod iustitia Dei non sit veritas. Iustitia enim est in voluntate, est enim rectitudo voluntatis, ut dicit Anselmus. Veritas autem est in intellectu, secundum philosophum in VI Metaphys. et in VI Ethic. Ergo iustitia non pertinet ad veritatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0031","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0032","text":"Praeterea, veritas, secundum philosophum in IV Ethic., est quaedam alia virtus a iustitia. Non ergo veritas pertinet ad rationem iustitiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0033","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0034","text":"Sed contra est quod in Psalmo LXXXIV dicitur, *misericordia et veritas obviaverunt sibi*; et ponitur ibi veritas pro iustitia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0035","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod veritas consistit in adaequatione intellectus et rei, sicut supra dictum est. Intellectus autem qui est causa rei, comparatur ad ipsam sicut regula et mensura, e converso autem est de intellectu qui accipit scientiam a rebus. Quando igitur res sunt mensura et regula intellectus, veritas consistit in hoc, quod intellectus adaequatur rei, ut in nobis accidit, ex eo enim quod res est vel non est, opinio nostra et oratio vera vel falsa est. Sed quando intellectus est regula vel mensura rerum, veritas consistit in hoc, quod res adaequantur intellectui, sicut dicitur artifex facere verum opus, quando concordat arti. Sicut autem se habent artificiata ad artem, ita se habent opera iusta ad legem cui concordant. Iustitia igitur Dei, quae constituit ordinem in rebus conformem rationi sapientiae suae, quae est lex eius, convenienter veritas nominatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0037","text":"Et sic etiam dicitur in nobis veritas iustitiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0038","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0039","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod iustitia, quantum ad legem regulantem, est in ratione vel intellectu, sed quantum ad imperium, quo opera regulantur secundum legem, est in voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0040","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0041","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod veritas illa de qua loquitur philosophus ibi, est quaedam virtus per quam aliquis demonstrat se talem in dictis vel factis, qualis est. Et sic consistit in conformitate signi ad significatum, non autem in conformitate effectus ad causam et regulam, sicut de veritate iustitiae dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0042","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0043","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0044","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod misericordia Deo non competat. Misericordia enim est species tristitiae, ut dicit Damascenus. Sed tristitia non est in Deo. Ergo nec misericordia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0045","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0046","text":"Praeterea, misericordia est relaxatio iustitiae. Sed Deus non potest praetermittere id quod ad iustitiam suam pertinet. Dicitur enim II ad Tim. II, *si non credimus, ille fidelis permanet, seipsum negare non potest*, negaret autem seipsum, ut dicit Glossa ibidem, si dicta sua negaret. Ergo misericordia Deo non competit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0047","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0048","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CX, *miserator et misericors dominus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0049","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod misericordia est Deo maxime attribuenda, tamen secundum effectum, non secundum passionis affectum. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod misericors dicitur aliquis quasi habens miserum cor, quia scilicet afficitur ex miseria alterius per tristitiam, ac si esset eius propria miseria. Et ex hoc sequitur quod operetur ad depellendam miseriam alterius, sicut miseriam propriam, et hic est misericordiae effectus. Tristari ergo de miseria alterius non competit Deo, sed repellere miseriam alterius, hoc maxime ei competit, ut per miseriam quemcumque defectum intelligamus. Defectus autem non tolluntur, nisi per alicuius bonitatis perfectionem, prima autem origo bonitatis Deus est, ut supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","text":"Sed considerandum est quod elargiri perfectiones rebus, pertinet quidem et ad bonitatem divinam, et ad iustitiam, et ad liberalitatem, et misericordiam, tamen secundum aliam et aliam rationem. Communicatio enim perfectionum, absolute considerata, pertinet ad bonitatem, ut supra ostensum est. Sed inquantum perfectiones rebus a Deo dantur secundum earum proportionem, pertinet ad iustitiam, ut dictum est supra. Inquantum vero non attribuit rebus perfectiones propter utilitatem suam, sed solum propter suam bonitatem, pertinet ad liberalitatem. Inquantum vero perfectiones datae rebus a Deo, omnem defectum expellunt, pertinet ad misericordiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0052","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0053","text":"Ad primum igitur dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de misericordia, quantum ad passionis affectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0054","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus misericorditer agit, non quidem contra iustitiam suam faciendo, sed aliquid supra iustitiam operando, sicut si alicui cui debentur centum denarii, aliquis ducentos det de suo, tamen non contra iustitiam facit, sed liberaliter vel misericorditer operatur. Et similiter si aliquis offensam in se commissam remittat. Qui enim aliquid remittit, quodammodo donat illud, unde apostolus remissionem donationem vocat, Ephes. V, *donate invicem, sicut et Christus vobis donavit*. Ex quo patet quod misericordia non tollit iustitiam, sed est quaedam iustitiae plenitudo. Unde dicitur Iac. II, quod *misericordia superexaltat iudicium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0056","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0057","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0058","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non in omnibus Dei operibus sit misericordia et iustitia. Quaedam enim opera Dei attribuuntur misericordiae, ut iustificatio impii, quaedam vero iustitiae, ut damnatio impiorum. Unde dicitur Iac. II, *iudicium sine misericordia fiet ei qui non fecerit misericordiam*. Non ergo in omni opere Dei apparet misericordia et iustitia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0059","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0060","text":"Praeterea, apostolus, ad Rom. XV, conversionem Iudaeorum attribuit iustitiae et veritati; conversionem autem gentium, misericordiae. Ergo non in quolibet opere Dei est iustitia et misericordia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0061","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0062","text":"Praeterea, multi iusti in hoc mundo affliguntur. Hoc autem est iniustum. Non ergo in omni opere Dei est iustitia et misericordia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0063","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0064","text":"Praeterea, iustitiae est reddere debitum, misericordiae autem sublevare miseriam, et sic tam iustitia quam misericordia aliquid praesupponit in suo opere. Sed creatio nihil praesupponit. Ergo in creatione neque misericordia est, neque iustitia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0065","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0066","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XXIV, *omnes viae domini misericordia et veritas*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0067","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est quod in quolibet opere Dei misericordia et veritas inveniantur; si tamen misericordia pro remotione cuiuscumque defectus accipiatur; quamvis non omnis defectus proprie possit dici miseria, sed solum defectus rationalis naturae, quam contingit esse felicem; nam miseria felicitati opponitur. Huius autem necessitatis ratio est, quia, cum debitum quod ex divina iustitia redditur, sit vel debitum Deo, vel debitum alicui creaturae, neutrum potest in aliquo opere Dei praetermitti. Non enim potest facere aliquid Deus, quod non sit conveniens sapientiae et bonitati ipsius; secundum quem modum diximus aliquid esse debitum Deo. Similiter etiam quidquid in rebus creatis facit, secundum convenientem ordinem et proportionem facit; in quo consistit ratio iustitiae. Et sic oportet in omni opere Dei esse iustitiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","text":"Opus autem divinae iustitiae semper praesupponit opus misericordiae, et in eo fundatur. Creaturae enim non debetur aliquid, nisi propter aliquid in eo praeexistens, vel praeconsideratum, et rursus, si illud creaturae debetur, hoc erit propter aliquid prius. Et cum non sit procedere in infinitum, oportet devenire ad aliquid quod ex sola bonitate divinae voluntatis dependeat, quae est ultimus finis. Utpote si dicamus quod habere manus debitum est homini propter animam rationalem; animam vero rationalem habere, ad hoc quod sit homo; hominem vero esse, propter divinam bonitatem. Et sic in quolibet opere Dei apparet misericordia, quantum ad primam radicem eius. Cuius virtus salvatur in omnibus consequentibus; et etiam vehementius in eis operatur, sicut causa primaria vehementius influit quam causa secunda.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070","text":"Et propter hoc etiam ea quae alicui creaturae debentur, Deus, ex abundantia suae bonitatis, largius dispensat quam exigat proportio rei. Minus enim est quod sufficeret ad conservandum ordinem iustitiae, quam quod divina bonitas confert, quae omnem proportionem creaturae excedit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0071","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod quaedam opera attribuuntur iustitiae et quaedam misericordiae, quia in quibusdam vehementius apparet iustitia, in quibusdam misericordia. Et tamen in damnatione reproborum apparet misericordia, non quidem totaliter relaxans, sed aliqualiter allevians, dum punit citra condignum. Et in iustificatione impii apparet iustitia, dum culpas relaxat propter dilectionem, quam tamen ipse misericorditer infundit, sicut de Magdalena legitur, Luc. VII, *dimissa sunt ei peccata multa, quoniam dilexit multum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0073","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0074","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod iustitia et misericordia Dei apparet in conversione Iudaeorum et gentium, sed aliqua ratio iustitiae apparet in conversione Iudaeorum, quae non apparet in conversione gentium, sicut quod salvati sunt propter promissiones patribus factas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0075","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in hoc etiam quod iusti puniuntur in hoc mundo, apparet iustitia et misericordia; inquantum per huiusmodi afflictiones aliqua levia in eis purgantur, et ab affectu terrenorum in Deum magis eriguntur; secundum illud Gregorii, *mala quae in hoc mundo nos premunt, ad Deum nos ire compellunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0077","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, licet creationi non praesupponatur aliquid in rerum natura, praesupponitur tamen aliquid in Dei cognitione. Et secundum hoc etiam salvatur ibi ratio iustitiae, inquantum res in esse producitur, secundum quod convenit divinae sapientiae et bonitati. Et salvatur quodammodo ratio misericordiae, inquantum res de non esse in esse mutatur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a9f79a8ee380b57e428bfd1055539610184a9ceddd774a55f249396b2851c3c0","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140152373941Z_5dd6e5dc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 21","source_sha256":"a9f79a8ee380b57e428bfd1055539610184a9ceddd774a55f249396b2851c3c0","sha256":"3dd7d069e99c15ddefffb51b36a37040b1ebdb952e7cc5a30c652202bc614359","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:01:52.373975+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0002","text":"[I q. 21 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0003","text":"After the consideration of the divine love, we must treat of His justice and mercy. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether justice is in God. Second, whether His justice can be called truth. Third, whether mercy is in God. Fourth, whether justice and mercy are in every work of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0005","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that justice is not in God. For justice is divided against temperance. But temperance is not in God. Therefore neither is justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0007","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0008","text":"Further, whoever does all things according to the pleasure of his will does not act according to justice. But, as the Apostle says, Eph. I, God *works all things according to the counsel of His will*. Therefore justice ought not to be attributed to Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0009","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0010","text":"Further, the act of justice is to render what is due. But God is debtor to no one. Therefore justice does not belong to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0011","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0012","text":"Further, whatever is in God is His essence. But this does not belong to justice, for Boethius says, in the book *De Hebdomadibus*, that *the good regards essence, whereas the just regards act*. Therefore justice does not belong to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0013","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0014","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Psalm X, *the Lord is just, and He has loved just acts*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0015","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that there are two species of justice. One consists in mutual giving and receiving, as, for example, that which consists in buying and selling and other communications or exchanges of this sort. And this is called by the Philosopher, in *Ethics* V, commutative justice, or directive of exchanges or communications. And this does not belong to God, because, as the Apostle says, Rom. XI, *who has first given to Him, and recompense shall be made to him?* The other consists in distributing and is called distributive justice, according to which a governor or dispenser gives to each according to his dignity. Therefore, just as the fitting order of a household, or of any governed multitude, manifests justice of this kind in the governor, so the order of the universe, which appears both in natural things and in voluntary things, manifests the justice of God. Hence Dionysius says, ch. VIII of *The Divine*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0017","text":"*Names*, *one must see in this the true justice of God: that He assigns to all their proper things, according to the dignity of each of the beings; and preserves the nature of each in its proper order and power*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0018","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that some of the moral virtues concern passions, as temperance concerns desires, fortitude fears and boldnesses, and meekness anger. And virtues of this kind cannot be attributed to God except metaphorically, because in God there are neither passions, as was said above, nor a sensitive appetite, in which virtues of this kind are as in their subject, as the Philosopher says in *Ethics* III. But certain moral virtues concern operations, for example, giving and expenditures, as justice, liberality, and magnificence; these too are not in the sensitive part, but in the will. Hence nothing prevents us from positing virtues of this kind in God, though not concerning civil actions but concerning actions befitting God. For it is ridiculous to praise God according to political virtues, as the Philosopher says in *Ethics* X.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0020","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that, since the understood good is the object of the will, it is impossible for God to will anything except what the account of His wisdom contains. This indeed is as the law of justice, according to which His will is right and just. Hence what He does according to His will, He does justly, just as we too do justly what we do according to the law. But we act according to the law of some superior; God, however, is law unto Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0022","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that to each is due what is its own. Now something is said to be one's own when it is ordered to that person; thus a servant belongs to his master, and not conversely, for what is free is that which is for its own sake. Therefore, in the name of what is due, a certain order of exigency or necessity of something to that to which it is ordered is implied. But there is a twofold order to be considered in things. One is that by which one created thing is ordered to another created thing, as parts are ordered to a whole, accidents to substances, and each thing to its end. The other order is that by which all created things are ordered to God. Thus, therefore, what is due can also be considered in two ways in the divine operation: either according as something is due to God, or according as something is due to a created thing. And in both ways God renders what is due.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","text":"For it is due to God that there be fulfilled in things that which His wisdom and will contain, and which manifests His goodness; and in this respect the justice of God regards His own fittingness, according to which He renders to Himself what is due to Himself. It is also due to a created thing that it have that which is ordered to it, as that a man have hands, and that other animals serve him. And thus God also works justice when He gives to each what is due to it according to the account of its nature and condition. But this due depends upon the first, because that is due to each which is ordered to it according to the order of divine wisdom. And although God gives what is due to something in this way, nevertheless He Himself is not a debtor, because He is not ordered to other things, but rather other things to Him. And therefore justice is sometimes called in God the befittingness of His goodness; but at other times, recompense for merits.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0025","text":"And Anselm touches upon both modes, saying, *when you punish the wicked, it is just, because it accords with their merits; but when you spare the wicked, it is just, because it is fitting to your goodness*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0026","text":"[I q. 21 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0027","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, although justice regards an act, it is not thereby excluded from being the essence of God, because even that which belongs to the essence of a thing can be a principle of action. But the good does not always regard an act, because something is said to be good not only insofar as it acts, but also insofar as it is perfect in its essence. And for this reason it is said in the same place that the good is related to the just as the general to the special.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0028","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0029","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0030","text":"We proceed to the second thus. It seems that God's justice is not truth. For justice is in the will, since it is rectitude of the will, as Anselm says. But truth is in the intellect, according to the Philosopher in Metaphysics VI and Ethics VI. Therefore justice does not belong to truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0031","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0032","text":"Further, according to the Philosopher in Ethics IV, truth is a virtue other than justice. Therefore truth does not belong to the notion of justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0033","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0034","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm LXXXIV, *mercy and truth have met one another*; and truth is there used for justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0035","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","text":"I answer that it must be said that truth consists in the adequation of intellect and thing, as was said above. Now an intellect that is the cause of a thing is related to it as rule and measure; but the converse is the case with an intellect that receives knowledge from things. Therefore, when things are the measure and rule of the intellect, truth consists in this: that the intellect is adequate to the thing, as happens in us; for from the fact that a thing is or is not, our opinion and speech are true or false. But when the intellect is the rule or measure of things, truth consists in this: that things are adequate to the intellect, as an artisan is said to make a true work when it agrees with the art. Now as artifacts are related to art, so just works are related to the law with which they agree. Therefore the justice of God, which establishes an order in things conformed to the ratio of his wisdom, which is his law, is fittingly called truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0037","text":"And thus in us too the truth of justice is spoken of.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0038","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0039","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that justice, as regards the law that regulates, is in reason or intellect; but as regards the command by which works are regulated according to the law, it is in the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0040","text":"[I q. 21 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0041","text":"To the second, it must be said that the truth of which the Philosopher speaks there is a certain virtue through which someone shows himself in words or deeds to be such as he is. And thus it consists in the conformity of the sign to the thing signified, but not in the conformity of the effect to its cause and rule, as was said concerning the truth of justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0042","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0043","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0044","text":"We proceed to the third thus. It seems that mercy is not fitting to God. For mercy is a species of sadness, as Damascene says. But sadness is not in God. Therefore neither is mercy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0045","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0046","text":"Further, mercy is a relaxation of justice. But God cannot pass over that which pertains to his justice. For it is said in 2 Timothy 2, *if we do not believe, he remains faithful; he cannot deny himself*; but he would deny himself, as the Gloss says in the same place, if he were to deny his own words. Therefore mercy is not fitting to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0047","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0048","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm CX, *the Lord is compassionate and merciful*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0049","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","text":"I answer that mercy is to be attributed to God most especially, yet as to its effect, not as to the affection of passion. To make this evident, it must be considered that someone is called merciful as though having a pitiable heart, because, namely, he is affected with sadness by another's misery as though it were his own misery. From this it follows that he acts to drive away another's misery as he would his own misery, and this is the effect of mercy. Therefore, to be saddened by another's misery does not belong to God; but to repel another's misery belongs to Him most especially, if by misery we understand any defect whatsoever. Defects, however, are not removed except through the perfection of some goodness; but God is the first origin of goodness, as was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","text":"But it must be considered that bestowing perfections upon things pertains indeed to divine goodness, justice, liberality, and mercy, though under different aspects. For the communication of perfections, considered absolutely, pertains to goodness, as was shown above. But insofar as perfections are given by God to things according to their proportion, it pertains to justice, as was said above. Insofar, however, as He attributes perfections to things not for His own usefulness but solely on account of His goodness, it pertains to liberality. But insofar as the perfections given by God to things expel every defect, it pertains to mercy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0052","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that objection proceeds concerning mercy with respect to the affection of passion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0054","text":"[I q. 21 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","text":"To the second, it must be said that God acts mercifully, not indeed by doing something contrary to His justice, but by effecting something beyond justice: as if someone, to whom one hundred denarii are owed, were to be given two hundred out of another's own resources; he does not thereby act against justice, but acts liberally or mercifully. Likewise, if someone forgives an offense committed against himself. For one who remits something, in some way gives it; hence the Apostle calls remission a gift, Eph. V: *forgive one another, as Christ also has forgiven you*. From this it is clear that mercy does not remove justice, but is a certain fullness of justice. Hence it is said in James II that *mercy exalts itself over judgment*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0056","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0057","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0058","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that mercy and justice are not in all God's works. For certain works of God are attributed to mercy, such as the justification of the ungodly; while others are attributed to justice, such as the damnation of the ungodly. Hence it is said in James II: *judgment without mercy shall befall him who has not shown mercy*. Therefore mercy and justice do not appear in every work of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0059","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0060","text":"Further, the Apostle, in Romans XV, attributes the conversion of the Jews to justice and truth; but the conversion of the Gentiles to mercy. Therefore justice and mercy are not in every work of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0061","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0062","text":"Further, many just persons are afflicted in this world. But this is unjust. Therefore justice and mercy are not in every work of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0063","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0064","text":"Further, it belongs to justice to render what is due, but to mercy to relieve misery; and thus both justice and mercy presuppose something in their work. But creation presupposes nothing. Therefore in creation there is neither mercy nor justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0065","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0066","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm XXIV: *all the ways of the Lord are mercy and truth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0067","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","text":"I answer that it is necessary that mercy and truth be found in every work of God, provided, however, that mercy is understood as the removal of any defect whatsoever; although not every defect can properly be called misery, but only the defect of a rational nature, which can be happy; for misery is opposed to happiness. The reason for this necessity is that, since the due which is rendered by divine justice is either due to God or due to some creature, neither can be omitted in any work of God. For God cannot make anything that is not fitting to His wisdom and goodness; in this way we said that something is due to God. Likewise, whatever He does in created things, He does according to a fitting order and proportion; and in this consists the nature of justice. Thus justice must be in every work of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","text":"But the work of divine justice always presupposes a work of mercy and is founded upon it. For nothing is due to a creature except on account of something pre-existing or preconsidered in it; and again, if that is due to the creature, this will be on account of something prior. And since one cannot proceed to infinity, one must arrive at something that depends solely upon the goodness of the divine will, which is the ultimate end. For example, if we say that it is due to a human being to have hands on account of the rational soul; but to have a rational soul, in order that he may be a human being; and to be a human being, on account of divine goodness. And thus, in every work of God mercy appears with respect to its first root. Its power is preserved in all consequent things, and indeed operates in them more forcefully, just as a primary cause has a more forceful influence than a secondary cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070","text":"And for this reason also God, from the abundance of His goodness, dispenses more generously than the proportion of the thing requires those things that are due to some creature. For less would suffice to preserve the order of justice than what divine goodness bestows, which exceeds every proportion of the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0071","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that certain works are attributed to justice and others to mercy because in some justice appears more forcefully, and in others mercy. Yet even in the damnation of the reprobate mercy appears, not indeed relaxing punishment altogether, but alleviating it in some measure, while He punishes short of what is deserved. And in the justification of the ungodly justice appears, while He remits sins on account of love, which nevertheless He Himself mercifully infuses, as is read concerning Magdalene, Luke VII: *many sins are forgiven her, because she loved much*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0073","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0074","text":"To the second, it must be said that the justice and mercy of God appear in the conversion of the Jews and the Gentiles; but some aspect of justice appears in the conversion of the Jews which does not appear in the conversion of the Gentiles, namely, that they were saved on account of the promises made to the fathers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0075","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0076","text":"To the third, it must be said that even in the fact that the just are punished in this world, justice and mercy appear: insofar as through afflictions of this kind certain slight things in them are purged, and they are raised more from attachment to earthly things toward God; according to that saying of Gregory, *the evils that oppress us in this world compel us to go to God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0077","text":"[I q. 21 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, although nothing in the nature of things is presupposed to creation, something is nevertheless presupposed in God's knowledge. And accordingly the character of justice is also preserved there, insofar as a thing is brought forth into being according as befits the divine wisdom and goodness. And the character of mercy is preserved in a certain way, insofar as a thing is changed from non-being into being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078"]}],"source_digest":"8f09670aa3c3b8b6a0f99c2c5b9ca973c0a29d276d41e86f6d5e41586b2fdf49","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 21"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 21","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14","title":"First Part · Question 22","order":23,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_14","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 22 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29357)\n\nConsideratis autem his quae ad voluntatem absolute pertinent, procedendum est ad ea quae respiciunt simul intellectum et voluntatem. Huiusmodi autem est providentia quidem respectu omnium; praedestinatio vero et reprobatio, et quae ad haec consequuntur, respectu hominum specialiter, in ordine ad aeternam salutem. Nam et post morales virtutes, in scientia morali, consideratur de prudentia, ad quam providentia pertinere videtur. Circa providentiam autem Dei quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Deo conveniat providentia. Secundo, utrum omnia divinae providentiae subsint. Tertio, utrum divina providentia immediate sit de omnibus. Quarto, utrum providentia divina imponat necessitatem rebus provisis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29358)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod providentia Deo non conveniat. Providentia enim, secundum Tullium, est pars prudentiae. Prudentia autem, cum sit bene consiliativa, secundum philosophum in VI Ethic., Deo competere non potest, qui nullum dubium habet, unde eum consiliari oporteat. Ergo providentia Deo non competit.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29359)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid est in Deo, est aeternum. Sed providentia non est aliquid aeternum, est enim circa existentia, quae non sunt aeterna, secundum Damascenum. Ergo providentia non est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29360)\n\nPraeterea, nullum compositum est in Deo. Sed providentia videtur esse aliquid compositum, quia includit in se voluntatem et intellectum. Ergo providentia non est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29361)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XIV, *tu autem, pater, gubernas omnia providentia*.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29362)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere providentiam in Deo. Omne enim bonum quod est in rebus, a Deo creatum est, ut supra ostensum est. In rebus autem invenitur bonum, non solum quantum ad substantiam rerum, sed etiam quantum ad ordinem earum in finem, et praecipue in finem ultimum, qui est bonitas divina, ut supra habitum est. Hoc igitur bonum ordinis in rebus creatis existens, a Deo creatum est. Cum autem Deus sit causa rerum per suum intellectum, et sic cuiuslibet sui effectus oportet rationem in ipso praeexistere, ut ex superioribus patet; necesse est quod ratio ordinis rerum in finem in mente divina praeexistat. Ratio autem ordinandorum in finem, proprie providentia est. Est enim principalis pars prudentiae, ad quam aliae duae partes ordinantur, scilicet memoria praeteritorum, et intelligentia praesentium; prout ex praeteritis memoratis, et praesentibus intellectis, coniectamus de futuris providendis. Prudentiae autem proprium est, secundum philosophum in VI Ethic., ordinare alia in finem; sive respectu sui ipsius, sicut dicitur homo prudens, qui bene ordinat actus suos ad finem vitae suae; sive respectu aliorum sibi subiectorum in familia vel civitate vel regno, secundum quem modum dicitur Matt. XXIV, *fidelis servus et prudens, quem constituit dominus super familiam suam*. Secundum quem modum prudentia vel providentia Deo convenire potest, nam in ipso Deo nihil est in finem ordinabile, cum ipse sit finis ultimus. Ipsa igitur ratio ordinis rerum in finem, providentia in Deo nominatur. Unde Boetius, IV de Consol., dicit quod *providentia est ipsa divina ratio in summo omnium principe constituta, quae cuncta disponit*. Dispositio autem potest dici tam ratio ordinis rerum in finem, quam ratio ordinis partium in toto.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29363)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum in VI Ethic., prudentia proprie est praeceptiva eorum, de quibus eubulia recte consiliatur, et synesis recte iudicat. Unde, licet consiliari non competat Deo, secundum quod consilium est inquisitio de rebus dubiis; tamen praecipere de ordinandis in finem, quorum rectam rationem habet, competit Deo, secundum illud Psalmi, *praeceptum posuit, et non praeteribit*. Et secundum hoc competit Deo ratio prudentiae et providentiae. Quamvis etiam dici possit, quod ipsa ratio rerum agendarum consilium in Deo dicitur; non propter inquisitionem, sed propter certitudinem cognitionis, ad quam consiliantes inquirendo perveniunt. Unde dicitur Ephes. I, *qui operatur omnia secundum consilium voluntatis suae*.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29364)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ad curam duo pertinent, scilicet ratio ordinis, quae dicitur providentia et dispositio; et executio ordinis, quae dicitur gubernatio. Quorum primum est aeternum, secundum temporale.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29365)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod providentia est in intellectu, sed praesupponit voluntatem finis, nullus enim praecipit de agendis propter finem, nisi velit finem. Unde et prudentia praesupponit virtutes morales, per quas appetitus se habet ad bonum, ut dicitur in VI Ethic. Et tamen si providentia ex aequali respiceret voluntatem et intellectum divinum, hoc esset absque detrimento divinae simplicitatis; cum voluntas et intellectus in Deo sint idem, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29366)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnia sint subiecta divinae providentiae. Nullum enim provisum est fortuitum. Si ergo omnia sunt provisa a Deo, nihil erit fortuitum, et sic perit casus et fortuna. Quod est contra communem opinionem.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29367)\n\nPraeterea, omnis sapiens provisor excludit defectum et malum, quantum potest, ab his quorum curam gerit. Videmus autem multa mala in rebus esse. Aut igitur Deus non potest ea impedire, et sic non est omnipotens, aut non de omnibus curam habet.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29368)\n\nPraeterea, quae ex necessitate eveniunt, providentiam seu prudentiam non requirunt, unde, secundum philosophum in VI Ethic., prudentia est recta ratio contingentium, de quibus est consilium et electio. Cum igitur multa in rebus ex necessitate eveniant, non omnia providentiae subduntur.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29369)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque dimittitur sibi, non subest providentiae alicuius gubernantis. Sed homines sibi ipsis dimittuntur a Deo, secundum illud Eccli. XV, *Deus ab initio constituit hominem, et reliquit eum in manu consilii sui*; et specialiter mali, secundum illud, *dimisit illos secundum desideria cordis eorum*. Non igitur omnia divinae providentiae subsunt.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29370)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus, I Cor. IX, dicit quod *non est Deo cura de bobus*, et eadem ratione, de aliis creaturis irrationalibus. Non igitur omnia subsunt divinae providentiae.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29371)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Sap. VIII, de divina sapientia, quod *attingit a fine usque ad finem fortiter, et disponit omnia suaviter*.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29372)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam totaliter providentiam negaverunt, sicut Democritus et Epicurei, ponentes mundum factum esse casu. Quidam vero posuerunt incorruptibilia tantum providentiae subiacere; corruptibilia vero, non secundum individua, sed secundum species; sic enim incorruptibilia sunt. Ex quorum persona dicitur Iob XXII, *nubes latibulum eius, et circa cardines caeli perambulat, neque nostra considerat*. A corruptibilium autem generalitate excepit Rabbi Moyses homines, propter splendorem intellectus, quem participant, in aliis autem individuis corruptibilibus, aliorum opinionem est secutus. Sed necesse est dicere omnia divinae providentiae subiacere, non in universali tantum, sed etiam in singulari. Quod sic patet. Cum enim omne agens agat propter finem, tantum se extendit ordinatio effectuum in finem, quantum se extendit causalitas primi agentis. Ex hoc enim contingit in operibus alicuius agentis aliquid provenire non ad finem ordinatum, quia effectus ille consequitur ex aliqua alia causa, praeter intentionem agentis. Causalitas autem Dei, qui est primum agens, se extendit usque ad omnia entia, non solum quantum ad principia speciei, sed etiam quantum ad individualia principia, non solum incorruptibilium, sed etiam corruptibilium. Unde necesse est omnia quae habent quocumque modo esse, ordinata esse a Deo in finem, secundum illud apostoli, ad Rom. XIII, *quae a Deo sunt, ordinata sunt*. Cum ergo nihil aliud sit Dei providentia quam ratio ordinis rerum in finem, ut dictum est, necesse est omnia, inquantum participant esse, intantum subdi divinae providentiae. Similiter etiam supra ostensum est quod Deus omnia cognoscit, et universalia et particularia. Et cum cognitio eius comparetur ad res sicut cognitio artis ad artificiata, ut supra dictum est, necesse est quod omnia supponantur suo ordini, sicut omnia artificiata subduntur ordini artis.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29373)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aliter est de causa universali, et de causa particulari. Ordinem enim causae particularis aliquid potest exire, non autem ordinem causae universalis. Non enim subducitur aliquid ab ordine causae particularis, nisi per aliquam aliam causam particularem impedientem, sicut lignum impeditur a combustione per actionem aquae. Unde, cum omnes causae particulares concludantur sub universali causa, impossibile est aliquem effectum ordinem causae universalis effugere. Inquantum igitur aliquis effectus ordinem alicuius causae particularis effugit, dicitur esse casuale vel fortuitum, respectu causae particularis, sed respectu causae universalis, a cuius ordine subtrahi non potest, dicitur esse provisum. Sicut et concursus duorum servorum, licet sit casualis quantum ad eos, est tamen provisus a domino, qui eos scienter sic ad unum locum mittit, ut unus de alio nesciat.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29374)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliter de eo est qui habet curam alicuius particularis, et de provisore universali. Quia provisor particularis excludit defectum ab eo quod eius curae subditur, quantum potest, sed provisor universalis permittit aliquem defectum in aliquo particulari accidere, ne impediatur bonum totius. Unde corruptiones et defectus in rebus naturalibus, dicuntur esse contra naturam particularem; sed tamen sunt de intentione naturae universalis, inquantum defectus unius cedit in bonum alterius, vel etiam totius universi; nam corruptio unius est generatio alterius, per quam species conservatur. Cum igitur Deus sit universalis provisor totius entis, ad ipsius providentiam pertinet ut permittat quosdam defectus esse in aliquibus particularibus rebus, ne impediatur bonum universi perfectum. Si enim omnia mala impedirentur, multa bona deessent universo, non enim esset vita leonis, si non esset occisio animalium; nec esset patientia martyrum, si non esset persecutio tyrannorum. Unde dicit Augustinus in Enchirid. *Deus omnipotens nullo modo sineret malum aliquod esse in operibus suis, nisi usque adeo esset omnipotens et bonus, ut bene faceret etiam de malo*. Ex his autem duabus rationibus quas nunc solvimus, videntur moti fuisse, qui divinae providentiae subtraxerunt corruptibilia, in quibus inveniuntur casualia et mala.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29375)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod homo non est institutor naturae, sed utitur in operibus artis et virtutis, ad suum usum, rebus naturalibus. Unde providentia humana non se extendit ad necessaria, quae ex natura proveniunt. Ad quae tamen se extendit providentia Dei, qui est auctor naturae. Et ex hac ratione videntur moti fuisse, qui cursum rerum naturalium subtraxerunt divinae providentiae, attribuentes ipsum necessitati materiae; ut Democritus, et alii naturales antiqui.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29376)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in hoc quod dicitur Deum hominem sibi reliquisse, non excluditur homo a divina providentia, sed ostenditur quod non praefigitur ei virtus operativa determinata ad unum, sicut rebus naturalibus; quae aguntur tantum, quasi ab altero directae in finem, non autem seipsa agunt, quasi se dirigentia in finem, ut creaturae rationales per liberum arbitrium, quo consiliantur et eligunt. Unde signanter dicit, *in manu consilii sui*. Sed quia ipse actus liberi arbitrii reducitur in Deum sicut in causam, necesse est ut ea quae ex libero arbitrio fiunt, divinae providentiae subdantur, providentia enim hominis continetur sub providentia Dei, sicut causa particularis sub causa universali. Hominum autem iustorum quodam excellentiori modo Deus habet providentiam quam impiorum, inquantum non permittit contra eos evenire aliquid, quod finaliter impediat salutem eorum, nam *diligentibus Deum omnia cooperantur in bonum*, ut dicitur Rom. VIII. Sed ex hoc ipso quod impios non retrahit a malo culpae, dicitur eos dimittere. Non tamen ita, quod totaliter ab eius providentia excludantur, alioquin in nihilum deciderent, nisi per eius providentiam conservarentur. Et ex hac ratione videtur motus fuisse Tullius, qui res humanas, de quibus consiliamur, divinae providentiae subtraxit.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29377)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, quia creatura rationalis habet per liberum arbitrium dominium sui actus, ut dictum est, speciali quodam modo subditur divinae providentiae; ut scilicet ei imputetur aliquid ad culpam vel ad meritum, et reddatur ei aliquid ut poena vel praemium. Et quantum ad hoc curam Dei apostolus a bobus removet. Non tamen ita quod individua irrationalium creaturarum ad Dei providentiam non pertineant, ut Rabbi Moyses existimavit.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29378)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non immediate omnibus provideat. Quidquid enim est dignitatis, Deo est attribuendum. Sed ad dignitatem alicuius regis pertinet, quod habeat ministros, quibus mediantibus subditis provideat. Ergo multo magis Deus non immediate omnibus providet.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29379)\n\nPraeterea, ad providentiam pertinet res in finem ordinare. Finis autem cuiuslibet rei est eius perfectio et bonum. Ad quamlibet autem causam pertinet effectum suum perducere ad bonum. Quaelibet igitur causa agens est causa effectus providentiae. Si igitur Deus omnibus immediate providet, subtrahuntur omnes causae secundae.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29380)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., quod *melius est quaedam nescire quam scire, ut vilia*, et idem dicit philosophus, in XII Metaphys. Sed omne quod est melius, Deo est attribuendum. Ergo Deus non habet immediate providentiam quorundam vilium et malorum.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29381)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Iob XXXIV, *quem constituit alium super terram? Aut quem posuit super orbem quem fabricatus est?* Super quo dicit Gregorius, *mundum per seipsum regit, quem per seipsum condidit*.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29382)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ad providentiam duo pertinent, scilicet ratio ordinis rerum provisarum in finem; et executio huius ordinis, quae gubernatio dicitur. Quantum igitur ad primum horum, Deus immediate omnibus providet. Quia in suo intellectu habet rationem omnium, etiam minimorum, et quascumque causas aliquibus effectibus praefecit, dedit eis virtutem ad illos effectus producendos. Unde oportet quod ordinem illorum effectuum in sua ratione praehabuerit. Quantum autem ad secundum, sunt aliqua media divinae providentiae. Quia inferiora gubernat per superiora; non propter defectum suae virtutis, sed propter abundantiam suae bonitatis, ut dignitatem causalitatis etiam creaturis communicet. Et secundum hoc excluditur opinio Platonis, quam narrat Gregorius Nyssenus, triplicem providentiam ponentis. Quarum prima est summi Dei, qui primo et principaliter providet rebus spiritualibus; et consequenter toti mundo, quantum ad genera, species et causas universales. Secunda vero providentia est, qua providetur singularibus generabilium et corruptibilium, et hanc attribuit diis qui circumeunt caelos, idest substantiis separatis, quae movent corpora caelestia circulariter. Tertia vero providentia est rerum humanarum, quam attribuebat Daemonibus, quos Platonici ponebant medios inter nos et deos, ut narrat Augustinus IX de Civ. Dei.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29383)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod habere ministros executores suae providentiae, pertinet ad dignitatem regis, sed quod non habeat rationem eorum quae per eos agenda sunt, est ex defectu ipsius. Omnis enim scientia operativa tanto perfectior est, quanto magis particularia considerat, in quibus est actus.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29384)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod per hoc quod Deus habet immediate providentiam de rebus omnibus, non excluduntur causae secundae, quae sunt executrices huius ordinis, ut ex supra dictis patet.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29385)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nobis melius est non cognoscere mala et vilia, inquantum per ea impedimur a consideratione meliorum, quia non possumus simul multa intelligere, et inquantum cogitatio malorum pervertit interdum voluntatem in malum. Sed hoc non habet locum in Deo, qui simul omnia uno intuitu videt, et cuius voluntas ad malum flecti non potest.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29386)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod divina providentia necessitatem rebus provisis imponat. Omnis enim effectus qui habet aliquam causam per se, quae iam est vel fuit, ad quam de necessitate sequitur, provenit ex necessitate, ut philosophus probat in VI Metaphys. Sed providentia Dei, cum sit aeterna, praeexistit; et ad eam sequitur effectus de necessitate; non enim potest divina providentia frustrari. Ergo providentia divina necessitatem rebus provisis imponit.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29387)\n\nPraeterea, unusquisque provisor stabilit opus suum quantum potest, ne deficiat. Sed Deus est summe potens. Ergo necessitatis firmitatem rebus a se provisis tribuit.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29388)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius dicit, IV de Consol., quod fatum, *ab immobilis providentiae proficiscens exordiis, actus fortunasque hominum indissolubili causarum connexione constringit*. Videtur ergo quod providentia necessitatem rebus provisis imponat.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29389)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *corrumpere naturam non est providentiae*. Hoc autem habet quarundam rerum natura, quod sint contingentia. Non igitur divina providentia necessitatem rebus imponit, contingentiam excludens.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29390)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod providentia divina quibusdam rebus necessitatem imponit, non autem omnibus, ut quidam crediderunt. Ad providentiam enim pertinet ordinare res in finem. Post bonitatem autem divinam, quae est finis a rebus separatus, principale bonum in ipsis rebus existens, est perfectio universi, quae quidem non esset, si non omnes gradus essendi invenirentur in rebus. Unde ad divinam providentiam pertinet omnes gradus entium producere. Et ideo quibusdam effectibus praeparavit causas necessarias, ut necessario evenirent; quibusdam vero causas contingentes, ut evenirent contingenter, secundum conditionem proximarum causarum.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29391)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod effectus divinae providentiae non solum est aliquid evenire quocumque modo; sed aliquid evenire vel contingenter vel necessario. Et ideo evenit infallibiliter et necessario, quod divina providentia disponit evenire infallibiliter et necessario, et evenit contingenter, quod divinae providentiae ratio habet ut contingenter eveniat.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29392)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in hoc est immobilis et certus divinae providentiae ordo, quod ea quae ab ipso providentur, cuncta eveniunt eo modo quo ipse providet, sive necessario sive contingenter.\n\n[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29393)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod indissolubilitas illa et immutabilitas quam Boetius tangit, pertinet ad certitudinem providentiae, quae non deficit a suo effectu, neque a modo eveniendi quem providit, non autem pertinet ad necessitatem effectuum. Et considerandum est quod necessarium et contingens proprie consequuntur ens, inquantum huiusmodi. Unde modus contingentiae et necessitatis cadit sub provisione Dei, qui est universalis provisor totius entis, non autem sub provisione aliquorum particularium provisorum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0002","text":"[I q. 22 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0003","text":"Consideratis autem his quae ad voluntatem absolute pertinent, procedendum est ad ea quae respiciunt simul intellectum et voluntatem. Huiusmodi autem est providentia quidem respectu omnium; praedestinatio vero et reprobatio, et quae ad haec consequuntur, respectu hominum specialiter, in ordine ad aeternam salutem. Nam et post morales virtutes, in scientia morali, consideratur de prudentia, ad quam providentia pertinere videtur. Circa providentiam autem Dei quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Deo conveniat providentia. Secundo, utrum omnia divinae providentiae subsint. Tertio, utrum divina providentia immediate sit de omnibus. Quarto, utrum providentia divina imponat necessitatem rebus provisis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0005","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod providentia Deo non conveniat. Providentia enim, secundum Tullium, est pars prudentiae. Prudentia autem, cum sit bene consiliativa, secundum philosophum in VI Ethic., Deo competere non potest, qui nullum dubium habet, unde eum consiliari oporteat. Ergo providentia Deo non competit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0007","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quidquid est in Deo, est aeternum. Sed providentia non est aliquid aeternum, est enim circa existentia, quae non sunt aeterna, secundum Damascenum. Ergo providentia non est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0009","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0010","text":"Praeterea, nullum compositum est in Deo. Sed providentia videtur esse aliquid compositum, quia includit in se voluntatem et intellectum. Ergo providentia non est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0011","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XIV, *tu autem, pater, gubernas omnia providentia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0013","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere providentiam in Deo. Omne enim bonum quod est in rebus, a Deo creatum est, ut supra ostensum est. In rebus autem invenitur bonum, non solum quantum ad substantiam rerum, sed etiam quantum ad ordinem earum in finem, et praecipue in finem ultimum, qui est bonitas divina, ut supra habitum est. Hoc igitur bonum ordinis in rebus creatis existens, a Deo creatum est. Cum autem Deus sit causa rerum per suum intellectum, et sic cuiuslibet sui effectus oportet rationem in ipso praeexistere, ut ex superioribus patet; necesse est quod ratio ordinis rerum in finem in mente divina praeexistat. Ratio autem ordinandorum in finem, proprie providentia est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0015","text":"Est enim principalis pars prudentiae, ad quam aliae duae partes ordinantur, scilicet memoria praeteritorum, et intelligentia praesentium; prout ex praeteritis memoratis, et praesentibus intellectis, coniectamus de futuris providendis. Prudentiae autem proprium est, secundum philosophum in VI Ethic., ordinare alia in finem; sive respectu sui ipsius, sicut dicitur homo prudens, qui bene ordinat actus suos ad finem vitae suae; sive respectu aliorum sibi subiectorum in familia vel civitate vel regno, secundum quem modum dicitur Matt. XXIV, *fidelis servus et prudens, quem constituit dominus super familiam suam*. Secundum quem modum prudentia vel providentia Deo convenire potest, nam in ipso Deo nihil est in finem ordinabile, cum ipse sit finis ultimus. Ipsa igitur ratio ordinis rerum in finem, providentia in Deo nominatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0016","text":"Unde Boetius, IV de Consol., dicit quod *providentia est ipsa divina ratio in summo omnium principe constituta, quae cuncta disponit*. Dispositio autem potest dici tam ratio ordinis rerum in finem, quam ratio ordinis partium in toto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0017","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum in VI Ethic., prudentia proprie est praeceptiva eorum, de quibus eubulia recte consiliatur, et synesis recte iudicat. Unde, licet consiliari non competat Deo, secundum quod consilium est inquisitio de rebus dubiis; tamen praecipere de ordinandis in finem, quorum rectam rationem habet, competit Deo, secundum illud Psalmi, *praeceptum posuit, et non praeteribit*. Et secundum hoc competit Deo ratio prudentiae et providentiae. Quamvis etiam dici possit, quod ipsa ratio rerum agendarum consilium in Deo dicitur; non propter inquisitionem, sed propter certitudinem cognitionis, ad quam consiliantes inquirendo perveniunt. Unde dicitur Ephes. I, *qui operatur omnia secundum consilium voluntatis suae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0019","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ad curam duo pertinent, scilicet ratio ordinis, quae dicitur providentia et dispositio; et executio ordinis, quae dicitur gubernatio. Quorum primum est aeternum, secundum temporale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0021","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod providentia est in intellectu, sed praesupponit voluntatem finis, nullus enim praecipit de agendis propter finem, nisi velit finem. Unde et prudentia praesupponit virtutes morales, per quas appetitus se habet ad bonum, ut dicitur in VI Ethic. Et tamen si providentia ex aequali respiceret voluntatem et intellectum divinum, hoc esset absque detrimento divinae simplicitatis; cum voluntas et intellectus in Deo sint idem, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0024","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnia sint subiecta divinae providentiae. Nullum enim provisum est fortuitum. Si ergo omnia sunt provisa a Deo, nihil erit fortuitum, et sic perit casus et fortuna. Quod est contra communem opinionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0026","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0027","text":"Praeterea, omnis sapiens provisor excludit defectum et malum, quantum potest, ab his quorum curam gerit. Videmus autem multa mala in rebus esse. Aut igitur Deus non potest ea impedire, et sic non est omnipotens, aut non de omnibus curam habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0028","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0029","text":"Praeterea, quae ex necessitate eveniunt, providentiam seu prudentiam non requirunt, unde, secundum philosophum in VI Ethic., prudentia est recta ratio contingentium, de quibus est consilium et electio. Cum igitur multa in rebus ex necessitate eveniant, non omnia providentiae subduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0030","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0031","text":"Praeterea, quicumque dimittitur sibi, non subest providentiae alicuius gubernantis. Sed homines sibi ipsis dimittuntur a Deo, secundum illud Eccli. XV, *Deus ab initio constituit hominem, et reliquit eum in manu consilii sui*; et specialiter mali, secundum illud, *dimisit illos secundum desideria cordis eorum*. Non igitur omnia divinae providentiae subsunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0032","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0033","text":"Praeterea, apostolus, I Cor. IX, dicit quod *non est Deo cura de bobus*, et eadem ratione, de aliis creaturis irrationalibus. Non igitur omnia subsunt divinae providentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0034","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0035","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Sap. VIII, de divina sapientia, quod *attingit a fine usque ad finem fortiter, et disponit omnia suaviter*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0036","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam totaliter providentiam negaverunt, sicut Democritus et Epicurei, ponentes mundum factum esse casu. Quidam vero posuerunt incorruptibilia tantum providentiae subiacere; corruptibilia vero, non secundum individua, sed secundum species; sic enim incorruptibilia sunt. Ex quorum persona dicitur Iob XXII, *nubes latibulum eius, et circa cardines caeli perambulat, neque nostra considerat*. A corruptibilium autem generalitate excepit Rabbi Moyses homines, propter splendorem intellectus, quem participant, in aliis autem individuis corruptibilibus, aliorum opinionem est secutus. Sed necesse est dicere omnia divinae providentiae subiacere, non in universali tantum, sed etiam in singulari. Quod sic patet. Cum enim omne agens agat propter finem, tantum se extendit ordinatio effectuum in finem, quantum se extendit causalitas primi agentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","text":"Ex hoc enim contingit in operibus alicuius agentis aliquid provenire non ad finem ordinatum, quia effectus ille consequitur ex aliqua alia causa, praeter intentionem agentis. Causalitas autem Dei, qui est primum agens, se extendit usque ad omnia entia, non solum quantum ad principia speciei, sed etiam quantum ad individualia principia, non solum incorruptibilium, sed etiam corruptibilium. Unde necesse est omnia quae habent quocumque modo esse, ordinata esse a Deo in finem, secundum illud apostoli, ad Rom. XIII, *quae a Deo sunt, ordinata sunt*. Cum ergo nihil aliud sit Dei providentia quam ratio ordinis rerum in finem, ut dictum est, necesse est omnia, inquantum participant esse, intantum subdi divinae providentiae. Similiter etiam supra ostensum est quod Deus omnia cognoscit, et universalia et particularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0039","text":"Et cum cognitio eius comparetur ad res sicut cognitio artis ad artificiata, ut supra dictum est, necesse est quod omnia supponantur suo ordini, sicut omnia artificiata subduntur ordini artis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0040","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aliter est de causa universali, et de causa particulari. Ordinem enim causae particularis aliquid potest exire, non autem ordinem causae universalis. Non enim subducitur aliquid ab ordine causae particularis, nisi per aliquam aliam causam particularem impedientem, sicut lignum impeditur a combustione per actionem aquae. Unde, cum omnes causae particulares concludantur sub universali causa, impossibile est aliquem effectum ordinem causae universalis effugere. Inquantum igitur aliquis effectus ordinem alicuius causae particularis effugit, dicitur esse casuale vel fortuitum, respectu causae particularis, sed respectu causae universalis, a cuius ordine subtrahi non potest, dicitur esse provisum. Sicut et concursus duorum servorum, licet sit casualis quantum ad eos, est tamen provisus a domino, qui eos scienter sic ad unum locum mittit, ut unus de alio nesciat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0042","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliter de eo est qui habet curam alicuius particularis, et de provisore universali. Quia provisor particularis excludit defectum ab eo quod eius curae subditur, quantum potest, sed provisor universalis permittit aliquem defectum in aliquo particulari accidere, ne impediatur bonum totius. Unde corruptiones et defectus in rebus naturalibus, dicuntur esse contra naturam particularem; sed tamen sunt de intentione naturae universalis, inquantum defectus unius cedit in bonum alterius, vel etiam totius universi; nam corruptio unius est generatio alterius, per quam species conservatur. Cum igitur Deus sit universalis provisor totius entis, ad ipsius providentiam pertinet ut permittat quosdam defectus esse in aliquibus particularibus rebus, ne impediatur bonum universi perfectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","text":"Si enim omnia mala impedirentur, multa bona deessent universo, non enim esset vita leonis, si non esset occisio animalium; nec esset patientia martyrum, si non esset persecutio tyrannorum. Unde dicit Augustinus in Enchirid. *Deus omnipotens nullo modo sineret malum aliquod esse in operibus suis, nisi usque adeo esset omnipotens et bonus, ut bene faceret etiam de malo*. Ex his autem duabus rationibus quas nunc solvimus, videntur moti fuisse, qui divinae providentiae subtraxerunt corruptibilia, in quibus inveniuntur casualia et mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0045","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0046","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod homo non est institutor naturae, sed utitur in operibus artis et virtutis, ad suum usum, rebus naturalibus. Unde providentia humana non se extendit ad necessaria, quae ex natura proveniunt. Ad quae tamen se extendit providentia Dei, qui est auctor naturae. Et ex hac ratione videntur moti fuisse, qui cursum rerum naturalium subtraxerunt divinae providentiae, attribuentes ipsum necessitati materiae; ut Democritus, et alii naturales antiqui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0047","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in hoc quod dicitur Deum hominem sibi reliquisse, non excluditur homo a divina providentia, sed ostenditur quod non praefigitur ei virtus operativa determinata ad unum, sicut rebus naturalibus; quae aguntur tantum, quasi ab altero directae in finem, non autem seipsa agunt, quasi se dirigentia in finem, ut creaturae rationales per liberum arbitrium, quo consiliantur et eligunt. Unde signanter dicit, *in manu consilii sui*. Sed quia ipse actus liberi arbitrii reducitur in Deum sicut in causam, necesse est ut ea quae ex libero arbitrio fiunt, divinae providentiae subdantur, providentia enim hominis continetur sub providentia Dei, sicut causa particularis sub causa universali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0049","text":"Hominum autem iustorum quodam excellentiori modo Deus habet providentiam quam impiorum, inquantum non permittit contra eos evenire aliquid, quod finaliter impediat salutem eorum, nam *diligentibus Deum omnia cooperantur in bonum*, ut dicitur Rom. VIII. Sed ex hoc ipso quod impios non retrahit a malo culpae, dicitur eos dimittere. Non tamen ita, quod totaliter ab eius providentia excludantur, alioquin in nihilum deciderent, nisi per eius providentiam conservarentur. Et ex hac ratione videtur motus fuisse Tullius, qui res humanas, de quibus consiliamur, divinae providentiae subtraxit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0050","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, quia creatura rationalis habet per liberum arbitrium dominium sui actus, ut dictum est, speciali quodam modo subditur divinae providentiae; ut scilicet ei imputetur aliquid ad culpam vel ad meritum, et reddatur ei aliquid ut poena vel praemium. Et quantum ad hoc curam Dei apostolus a bobus removet. Non tamen ita quod individua irrationalium creaturarum ad Dei providentiam non pertineant, ut Rabbi Moyses existimavit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0052","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0053","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0054","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non immediate omnibus provideat. Quidquid enim est dignitatis, Deo est attribuendum. Sed ad dignitatem alicuius regis pertinet, quod habeat ministros, quibus mediantibus subditis provideat. Ergo multo magis Deus non immediate omnibus providet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0055","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0056","text":"Praeterea, ad providentiam pertinet res in finem ordinare. Finis autem cuiuslibet rei est eius perfectio et bonum. Ad quamlibet autem causam pertinet effectum suum perducere ad bonum. Quaelibet igitur causa agens est causa effectus providentiae. Si igitur Deus omnibus immediate providet, subtrahuntur omnes causae secundae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0057","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0058","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., quod *melius est quaedam nescire quam scire, ut vilia*, et idem dicit philosophus, in XII Metaphys. Sed omne quod est melius, Deo est attribuendum. Ergo Deus non habet immediate providentiam quorundam vilium et malorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0059","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0060","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Iob XXXIV, *quem constituit alium super terram? Aut quem posuit super orbem quem fabricatus est?* Super quo dicit Gregorius, *mundum per seipsum regit, quem per seipsum condidit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0061","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ad providentiam duo pertinent, scilicet ratio ordinis rerum provisarum in finem; et executio huius ordinis, quae gubernatio dicitur. Quantum igitur ad primum horum, Deus immediate omnibus providet. Quia in suo intellectu habet rationem omnium, etiam minimorum, et quascumque causas aliquibus effectibus praefecit, dedit eis virtutem ad illos effectus producendos. Unde oportet quod ordinem illorum effectuum in sua ratione praehabuerit. Quantum autem ad secundum, sunt aliqua media divinae providentiae. Quia inferiora gubernat per superiora; non propter defectum suae virtutis, sed propter abundantiam suae bonitatis, ut dignitatem causalitatis etiam creaturis communicet. Et secundum hoc excluditur opinio Platonis, quam narrat Gregorius Nyssenus, triplicem providentiam ponentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0063","text":"Quarum prima est summi Dei, qui primo et principaliter providet rebus spiritualibus; et consequenter toti mundo, quantum ad genera, species et causas universales. Secunda vero providentia est, qua providetur singularibus generabilium et corruptibilium, et hanc attribuit diis qui circumeunt caelos, idest substantiis separatis, quae movent corpora caelestia circulariter. Tertia vero providentia est rerum humanarum, quam attribuebat Daemonibus, quos Platonici ponebant medios inter nos et deos, ut narrat Augustinus IX de Civ. Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0064","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0065","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod habere ministros executores suae providentiae, pertinet ad dignitatem regis, sed quod non habeat rationem eorum quae per eos agenda sunt, est ex defectu ipsius. Omnis enim scientia operativa tanto perfectior est, quanto magis particularia considerat, in quibus est actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0066","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod per hoc quod Deus habet immediate providentiam de rebus omnibus, non excluduntur causae secundae, quae sunt executrices huius ordinis, ut ex supra dictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0068","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0069","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nobis melius est non cognoscere mala et vilia, inquantum per ea impedimur a consideratione meliorum, quia non possumus simul multa intelligere, et inquantum cogitatio malorum pervertit interdum voluntatem in malum. Sed hoc non habet locum in Deo, qui simul omnia uno intuitu videt, et cuius voluntas ad malum flecti non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0070","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0071","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0072","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod divina providentia necessitatem rebus provisis imponat. Omnis enim effectus qui habet aliquam causam per se, quae iam est vel fuit, ad quam de necessitate sequitur, provenit ex necessitate, ut philosophus probat in VI Metaphys. Sed providentia Dei, cum sit aeterna, praeexistit; et ad eam sequitur effectus de necessitate; non enim potest divina providentia frustrari. Ergo providentia divina necessitatem rebus provisis imponit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0073","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0074","text":"Praeterea, unusquisque provisor stabilit opus suum quantum potest, ne deficiat. Sed Deus est summe potens. Ergo necessitatis firmitatem rebus a se provisis tribuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0075","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0076","text":"Praeterea, Boetius dicit, IV de Consol., quod fatum, *ab immobilis providentiae proficiscens exordiis, actus fortunasque hominum indissolubili causarum connexione constringit*. Videtur ergo quod providentia necessitatem rebus provisis imponat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0077","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0078","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *corrumpere naturam non est providentiae*. Hoc autem habet quarundam rerum natura, quod sint contingentia. Non igitur divina providentia necessitatem rebus imponit, contingentiam excludens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0079","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod providentia divina quibusdam rebus necessitatem imponit, non autem omnibus, ut quidam crediderunt. Ad providentiam enim pertinet ordinare res in finem. Post bonitatem autem divinam, quae est finis a rebus separatus, principale bonum in ipsis rebus existens, est perfectio universi, quae quidem non esset, si non omnes gradus essendi invenirentur in rebus. Unde ad divinam providentiam pertinet omnes gradus entium producere. Et ideo quibusdam effectibus praeparavit causas necessarias, ut necessario evenirent; quibusdam vero causas contingentes, ut evenirent contingenter, secundum conditionem proximarum causarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0081","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod effectus divinae providentiae non solum est aliquid evenire quocumque modo; sed aliquid evenire vel contingenter vel necessario. Et ideo evenit infallibiliter et necessario, quod divina providentia disponit evenire infallibiliter et necessario, et evenit contingenter, quod divinae providentiae ratio habet ut contingenter eveniat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0083","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in hoc est immobilis et certus divinae providentiae ordo, quod ea quae ab ipso providentur, cuncta eveniunt eo modo quo ipse providet, sive necessario sive contingenter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0085","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod indissolubilitas illa et immutabilitas quam Boetius tangit, pertinet ad certitudinem providentiae, quae non deficit a suo effectu, neque a modo eveniendi quem providit, non autem pertinet ad necessitatem effectuum. Et considerandum est quod necessarium et contingens proprie consequuntur ens, inquantum huiusmodi. Unde modus contingentiae et necessitatis cadit sub provisione Dei, qui est universalis provisor totius entis, non autem sub provisione aliquorum particularium provisorum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"87567b7eeeae5150a918535a6f44165269a21aed66f8721d19c5f6cd138080dd","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140253926546Z_3c83576e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 22","source_sha256":"87567b7eeeae5150a918535a6f44165269a21aed66f8721d19c5f6cd138080dd","sha256":"4f0ef576c507fb80dc3df08c3e0b7395338a352b64948c0834ad4caa0e7f2c85","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:02:53.926564+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0002","text":"[I q. 22 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0003","text":"Having considered, however, those matters that pertain to the will absolutely, we must proceed to those that regard intellect and will together. Of this sort is providence with respect to all things; but predestination and reprobation, and those things consequent upon them, with respect to human beings particularly, in relation to eternal salvation. For after the moral virtues, moral science also considers prudence, to which providence seems to belong. Concerning God's providence, however, four questions are asked. First, whether providence befits God. Second, whether all things are subject to divine providence. Third, whether divine providence is immediately concerned with all things. Fourth, whether divine providence imposes necessity upon the things provided for.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0005","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that providence does not befit God. For providence, according to Tully, is a part of prudence. But prudence, since it is good at deliberating, according to the Philosopher in Ethics VI, cannot belong to God, who has no doubt from which He would need to deliberate. Therefore providence does not belong to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0007","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0008","text":"Further, whatever is in God is eternal. But providence is not something eternal, for it concerns existing things, which are not eternal, according to Damascene. Therefore providence is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0009","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0010","text":"Further, nothing composite is in God. But providence seems to be something composite, because it includes will and intellect within itself. Therefore providence is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0011","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Wisdom XIV, *but Thou, Father, dost govern all things by providence*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0013","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be held that there is providence in God. For every good that is in things has been created by God, as was shown above. Now in things good is found not only as regards the substance of things, but also as regards their ordering to an end, and especially to the ultimate end, which is the divine goodness, as was established above. Therefore this good of order existing in created things has been created by God. But since God is the cause of things through His intellect, and thus the intelligible account of each of His effects must pre-exist in Him, as is clear from what has preceded, it is necessary that the intelligible account of the ordering of things to an end pre-exist in the divine mind. Now the intelligible account of things to be ordered to an end is, properly speaking, providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0015","text":"For it is the principal part of prudence, to which the other two parts are ordered, namely, memory of things past and understanding of things present; insofar as, from past things remembered and present things understood, we conjecture concerning future things to be provided for. But it belongs to prudence, according to the Philosopher in Ethics VI, to order other things to an end: whether with respect to oneself, as a person is called prudent who orders his acts well to the end of his life; or with respect to others subject to him in a household or city or kingdom, according to which manner Matthew XXIV says, *the faithful and prudent servant, whom the lord has appointed over his household*. In this manner prudence or providence can befit God, for in God Himself there is nothing capable of being ordered to an end, since He Himself is the ultimate end. Therefore the very intelligible account of the ordering of things to an end is called providence in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0016","text":"Hence Boethius, in Consolation IV, says that *providence is the divine intelligible account itself, established in the supreme ruler of all things, which disposes all things*. But disposition can be called both the intelligible account of the ordering of things to an end and the intelligible account of the ordering of parts in a whole.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0017","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, according to the Philosopher in Ethics VI, prudence is properly directive concerning those things about which euboulia deliberates rightly and synesis judges rightly. Hence, although to deliberate does not belong to God, insofar as deliberation is inquiry concerning doubtful matters, nevertheless to command concerning things to be ordered to an end, of which He has the right intelligible account, belongs to God, according to the Psalm: *He has set a command, and it shall not pass away*. And in this respect the account of prudence and providence belongs to God. Although it may also be said that the very intelligible account of things to be done is called counsel in God, not because of inquiry, but because of the certainty of knowledge at which those who deliberate arrive by inquiry. Hence Ephesians I says: *who works all things according to the counsel of His will*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0019","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that two things pertain to care: namely, the intelligible account of order, which is called providence and disposition; and the execution of order, which is called governance. Of these, the first is eternal, the second temporal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0021","text":"[I q. 22 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that providence is in the intellect, but presupposes the will of the end; for no one commands concerning things to be done for the sake of an end unless he wills the end. Hence prudence too presupposes the moral virtues, through which the appetite is disposed toward the good, as is said in Ethics VI. And yet, if providence regarded the divine will and intellect equally, this would be without detriment to divine simplicity, since will and intellect in God are the same, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0024","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second point. It seems that not all things are subject to divine providence. For nothing that is provided for is fortuitous. Therefore, if all things are provided for by God, nothing will be fortuitous, and thus chance and fortune perish. This is contrary to common opinion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0026","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0027","text":"Further, every wise provider excludes defect and evil, insofar as he can, from those things whose care he bears. But we see that there are many evils in things. Therefore, either God cannot prevent them, and thus is not omnipotent, or He does not have care of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0028","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0029","text":"Further, things that happen of necessity do not require providence or prudence; hence, according to the Philosopher in Ethics VI, prudence is right reason concerning contingent things, concerning which there are counsel and choice. Therefore, since many things happen in things of necessity, not all things are subject to providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0030","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0031","text":"Further, whoever is left to himself is not subject to the providence of any governor. But human beings are left to themselves by God, according to that saying of Sirach 15, *God from the beginning constituted man, and left him in the hand of his own counsel*; and especially the wicked, according to the saying, *he left them according to the desires of their heart*. Therefore not all things are subject to divine providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0032","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0033","text":"Further, the Apostle says, in 1 Corinthians 9, that *God does not have care for oxen*, and by the same reasoning, for other irrational creatures. Therefore not all things are subject to divine providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0034","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0035","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Wisdom 8, concerning divine wisdom, that *it reaches from end to end mightily, and disposes all things sweetly*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0036","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have altogether denied providence, such as Democritus and the Epicureans, holding that the world was made by chance. But some held that only incorruptible things are subject to providence; corruptible things, however, not according to individuals but according to species, for in this way they are incorruptible. In their person Job 22 says, *the clouds are his hiding place, and he walks about the hinges of heaven, and does not consider our things*. From the generality of corruptible things Rabbi Moses excepted human beings, on account of the splendor of intellect which they share; but with respect to other corruptible individuals, he followed the opinion of the others. But it is necessary to say that all things are subject to divine providence, not only universally but also singularly. This is evident as follows. For since every agent acts for an end, the ordering of effects to an end extends as far as the causality of the first agent extends.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","text":"For it comes about in the works of some agent that something does not attain the ordered end because that effect follows from some other cause, beyond the agent's intention. But the causality of God, who is the first agent, extends to all beings, not only as regards the principles of the species, but also as regards individual principles, not only of incorruptible but also of corruptible things. Hence it is necessary that all things which in whatever way have being be ordered by God to an end, according to the saying of the Apostle, in Romans 13, *those things which are from God are ordered*. Therefore, since God's providence is nothing other than the plan of the order of things to an end, as was said, it is necessary that all things, insofar as they participate in being, be subject to divine providence to that extent. Likewise, it was also shown above that God knows all things, both universals and particulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0039","text":"And since His knowledge is related to things as the knowledge of an art is related to things made by art, as was said above, it is necessary that all things be subject to His order, just as all products of art are subject to the order of the art.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0040","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that it is otherwise with a universal cause and with a particular cause. For something can fall outside the order of a particular cause, but not outside the order of a universal cause. For nothing is withdrawn from the order of a particular cause except through some other particular cause that impedes it, just as wood is prevented from burning through the action of water. Hence, since all particular causes are encompassed under a universal cause, it is impossible for any effect to escape the order of the universal cause. Therefore, insofar as an effect escapes the order of some particular cause, it is called chance or fortuitous with respect to the particular cause; but with respect to the universal cause, from whose order it cannot be withdrawn, it is called provided for. Thus also the meeting of two servants, although it is chance as regards them, is nevertheless provided for by their master, who knowingly sends them thus to one place, so that the one does not know of the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0042","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","text":"To the second, it must be said that it is otherwise concerning one who has care of something particular and a universal provider. For a particular provider excludes defect from that which is subject to his care, insofar as he can; but a universal provider permits some defect to occur in some particular thing, lest the good of the whole be impeded. Hence corruptions and defects in natural things are said to be against particular nature; yet they are according to the intention of universal nature, insofar as the defect of one contributes to the good of another, or even of the whole universe. For the corruption of one is the generation of another, through which the species is preserved. Therefore, since God is the universal provider of all being, it pertains to His providence to permit certain defects to exist in certain particular things, lest the perfect good of the universe be impeded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","text":"For if all evils were prevented, many goods would be lacking to the universe: for there would not be the life of a lion if there were not the killing of animals; nor would there be the patience of martyrs if there were not the persecution of tyrants. Hence Augustine says in the Enchiridion: *God Almighty would in no way permit any evil to exist in His works, unless He were so omnipotent and good as to bring about good even from evil.* But those who withdrew corruptible things from divine providence, in which chance occurrences and evils are found, seem to have been moved by these two arguments which we have now answered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0045","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0046","text":"To the third, it must be said that man is not the institutor of nature, but in works of art and virtue he uses natural things for his own use. Hence human providence does not extend to necessary things, which proceed from nature. Yet the providence of God, who is the author of nature, extends to these. And those who withdrew the course of natural things from divine providence, attributing it to the necessity of matter, seem to have been moved by this argument; as Democritus and the other ancient natural philosophers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0047","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, in saying that God left man to himself, man is not excluded from divine providence, but it is shown that no determinate operative power toward one thing is preassigned to him, as it is to natural things; which are only acted upon, as though directed to an end by another, and do not act themselves, as directing themselves to an end, as rational creatures do through free choice, by which they take counsel and choose. Hence it significantly says, *in the hand of his own counsel*. But because the very act of free choice is reduced to God as to a cause, it is necessary that those things which are done from free choice be subject to divine providence; for the providence of man is contained under the providence of God, as a particular cause under a universal cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0049","text":"But God has providence over just human beings in a certain more excellent manner than over the wicked, insofar as He does not permit anything to happen against them that would ultimately impede their salvation; for *all things work together unto good for those who love God*, as is said in Romans VIII. But from the very fact that He does not draw the wicked back from the evil of fault, He is said to abandon them. Yet not in such a way that they are totally excluded from His providence; otherwise they would fall into nothingness, unless they were preserved by His providence. And for this reason Tully seems to have been moved to withdraw human affairs, concerning which we deliberate, from divine providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0050","text":"[I q. 22 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","text":"To the fifth it must be said that, because the rational creature has dominion over its act through free choice, as was said, it is subject to divine providence in a certain special way: namely, so that something is imputed to it as fault or as merit, and something is rendered to it as punishment or reward. And in this respect the Apostle removes God's care from oxen. Yet not in such a way that the individuals of irrational creatures do not pertain to God's providence, as Rabbi Moses supposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0052","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0053","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0054","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that God does not provide immediately for all things. For whatever pertains to dignity ought to be attributed to God. But it pertains to the dignity of a king that he should have ministers through whom he provides for his subjects. Therefore, much more does God not provide immediately for all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0055","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0056","text":"Further, it belongs to providence to order things to an end. But the end of any thing is its perfection and good. Now it belongs to every cause to bring its effect to good. Therefore every acting cause is a cause of the effect of providence. If, therefore, God provides immediately for all things, all secondary causes are removed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0057","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0058","text":"Further, Augustine says, in the *Enchiridion*, that *it is better not to know certain things than to know them, such as base things*; and the Philosopher says the same in Metaphysics XII. But everything that is better ought to be attributed to God. Therefore God does not have immediate providence over certain base and evil things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0059","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0060","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Job XXXIV: *Whom has He appointed over the earth? Or whom has He placed over the world which He fashioned?* On this Gregory says: *He rules by Himself the world which He created by Himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0061","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","text":"I answer that it must be said that two things pertain to providence: namely, the plan of the order of the things provided for to an end; and the execution of this order, which is called governance. Therefore, as to the first of these, God immediately provides for all things. For in His intellect He has the plan of all things, even the least; and whatever causes He has set over certain effects, He has given them the power to produce those effects. Hence He must have precontained in His plan the order of those effects. But as to the second, there are certain intermediaries of divine providence. For He governs lower things through higher things—not because of any defect in His power, but because of the abundance of His goodness, so that He may communicate to creatures also the dignity of causality. And accordingly the opinion of Plato is excluded, which Gregory of Nyssa recounts, positing a threefold providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0063","text":"The first of these is that of the highest God, who provides first and principally for spiritual things, and consequently for the whole world, as regards genera, species, and universal causes. The second providence, however, is that by which provision is made for the singulars of generable and corruptible things; and he attributed this to the gods who go about the heavens, that is, to the separate substances that move the celestial bodies in a circle. The third providence is that of human affairs, which he attributed to demons, whom the Platonists posited as intermediaries between us and the gods, as Augustine relates in *The City of God*, IX.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0064","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0065","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to have ministers who execute his providence pertains to the dignity of a king; but not to have the plan of those things which are to be done through them arises from a defect in him. For every practical knowledge is so much the more perfect as it considers more particular things, in which action consists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0066","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","text":"To the second it must be said that, from the fact that God has immediate providence over all things, secondary causes are not excluded, which execute this order, as is clear from what has been said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0068","text":"[I q. 22 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0069","text":"To the third it must be said that it is better for us not to know evil and base things, insofar as through them we are impeded from the consideration of better things, because we cannot understand many things at the same time; and insofar as the thought of evils sometimes perverts the will toward evil. But this has no place in God, who sees all things simultaneously in one intuition, and whose will cannot be bent toward evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0070","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0071","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0072","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that divine providence imposes necessity upon the things provided for. For every effect that has some per se cause which already is or has been, upon which it follows of necessity, comes about necessarily, as the Philosopher proves in Metaphysics VI. But God's providence, since it is eternal, pre-exists; and the effect follows upon it of necessity, for divine providence cannot be frustrated. Therefore divine providence imposes necessity upon the things provided for.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0073","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0074","text":"Moreover, every provider establishes his work as much as he can, lest it fail. But God is supremely powerful. Therefore He bestows the firmness of necessity upon the things provided by Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0075","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0076","text":"Moreover, Boethius says, in Book IV of the Consolation, that fate, *proceeding from the beginnings of immovable providence, binds the acts and fortunes of men by an indissoluble connection of causes*. Therefore it seems that providence imposes necessity upon the things provided for.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0077","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0078","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of the Divine Names, that *to corrupt nature does not belong to providence*. But it belongs to the nature of certain things that they be contingent. Therefore divine providence does not impose necessity upon things, excluding contingency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0079","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","text":"I answer that it must be said that divine providence imposes necessity on certain things, but not on all, as some have believed. For it belongs to providence to order things to an end. Now after the divine goodness, which is an end separate from things, the principal good existing in things themselves is the perfection of the universe; and this would not exist if all the grades of being were not found in things. Hence it belongs to divine providence to produce all the grades of beings. And therefore, for certain effects it has prepared necessary causes, so that they might occur necessarily; but for others contingent causes, so that they might occur contingently, according to the condition of proximate causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0081","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the effect of divine providence is not only that something should occur in whatever manner, but that something should occur either contingently or necessarily. And therefore, that which divine providence disposes to occur infallibly and necessarily occurs infallibly and necessarily; and that occurs contingently which the plan of divine providence has to occur contingently.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0083","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","text":"To the second, it must be said that in this consists the immovable and certain order of divine providence: that all those things which are provided for by it occur in the manner in which it provides, whether necessarily or contingently.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0085","text":"[I q. 22 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","text":"To the third, it must be said that that indissolubility and immutability which Boethius touches upon pertains to the certainty of providence, which does not fail of its effect, nor of the mode of occurrence which it has provided, but does not pertain to the necessity of effects. And it should be considered that the necessary and the contingent properly follow being, insofar as it is being. Hence the mode of contingency and necessity falls under the providence of God, who is the universal provider of all being, but not under the providence of any particular providers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086"]}],"source_digest":"584afb2585276b6d004ce94dba18fabe66ea9b8ad0608c112f8f2550bf68714e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 22"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 22","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15","title":"First Part · Question 23","order":24,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_15","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 23 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29394)\n\nPost considerationem divinae providentiae, agendum est de praedestinatione, et de libro vitae. Et circa praedestinationem quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum Deo conveniat praedestinatio. Secundo, quid sit praedestinatio; et utrum ponat aliquid in praedestinato. Tertio, utrum Deo competat reprobatio aliquorum hominum. Quarto, de comparatione praedestinationis ad electionem; utrum scilicet praedestinati eligantur. Quinto, utrum merita sint causa vel ratio praedestinationis, vel reprobationis, aut electionis. Sexto, de certitudine praedestinationis; utrum scilicet praedestinati infallibiliter salventur. Septimo, utrum numerus praedestinatorum sit certus. Octavo, utrum praedestinatio possit iuvari precibus sanctorum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29395)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non praedestinentur a Deo. Dicit enim Damascenus, in II libro, *oportet cognoscere quod omnia quidem praecognoscit Deus, non autem omnia praedeterminat. Praecognoscit enim ea quae in nobis sunt; non autem praedeterminat ea*. Sed merita et demerita humana sunt in nobis, inquantum sumus nostrorum actuum domini per liberum arbitrium. Ea ergo quae pertinent ad meritum vel demeritum, non praedestinantur a Deo. Et sic hominum praedestinatio tollitur.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29396)\n\nPraeterea, omnes creaturae ordinantur ad suos fines per divinam providentiam, ut supra dictum est. Sed aliae creaturae non dicuntur praedestinari a Deo. Ergo nec homines.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29397)\n\nPraeterea, Angeli sunt capaces beatitudinis, sicut et homines. Sed Angelis non competit praedestinari, ut videtur, cum in eis nunquam fuerit miseria; praedestinatio autem est propositum miserendi, ut dicit Augustinus. Ergo homines non praedestinantur.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29398)\n\nPraeterea, beneficia hominibus a Deo collata, per spiritum sanctum viris sanctis revelantur, secundum illud apostoli, I Cor. II, *nos autem non spiritum huius mundi accepimus, sed spiritum qui ex Deo est, ut sciamus quae a Deo donata sunt nobis*. Si ergo homines praedestinarentur a Deo, cum praedestinatio sit Dei beneficium, esset praedestinatis nota sua praedestinatio. Quod patet esse falsum.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29399)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Rom. VIII, *quos praedestinavit, hos et vocavit*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29400)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deo conveniens est homines praedestinare. Omnia enim divinae providentiae subiacent, ut supra ostensum est. Ad providentiam autem pertinet res in finem ordinare, ut dictum est. Finis autem ad quem res creatae ordinantur a Deo, est duplex. Unus, qui excedit proportionem naturae creatae et facultatem, et hic finis est vita aeterna, quae in divina visione consistit, quae est supra naturam cuiuslibet creaturae, ut supra habitum est. Alius autem finis est naturae creatae proportionatus, quem scilicet res creata potest attingere secundum virtutem suae naturae. Ad illud autem ad quod non potest aliquid virtute suae naturae pervenire, oportet quod ab alio transmittatur; sicut sagitta a sagittante mittitur ad signum. Unde, proprie loquendo, rationalis creatura, quae est capax vitae aeternae, perducitur in ipsam quasi a Deo transmissa. Cuius quidem transmissionis ratio in Deo praeexistit; sicut et in eo est ratio ordinis omnium in finem, quam diximus esse providentiam. Ratio autem alicuius fiendi in mente actoris existens, est quaedam praeexistentia rei fiendae in eo. Unde ratio praedictae transmissionis creaturae rationalis in finem vitae aeternae, praedestinatio nominatur, nam destinare est mittere. Et sic patet quod praedestinatio, quantum ad obiecta, est quaedam pars providentiae.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29401)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Damascenus nominat praedeterminationem impositionem necessitatis; sicut est in rebus naturalibus, quae sunt praedeterminatae ad unum. Quod patet ex eo quod subdit, *non enim vult malitiam, neque compellit virtutem*. Unde praedestinatio non excluditur.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29402)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod creaturae irrationales non sunt capaces illius finis qui facultatem humanae naturae excedit. Unde non proprie dicuntur praedestinari, etsi aliquando abusive praedestinatio nominetur respectu cuiuscumque alterius finis.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29403)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod praedestinari convenit Angelis, sicut et hominibus, licet nunquam fuerint miseri. Nam motus non accipit speciem a termino a quo, sed a termino ad quem, nihil enim refert, quantum ad rationem dealbationis, utrum ille qui dealbatur, fuerit niger aut pallidus vel rubeus. Et similiter nihil refert ad rationem praedestinationis, utrum aliquis praedestinetur in vitam aeternam a statu miseriae, vel non. Quamvis dici possit quod omnis collatio boni supra debitum eius cui confertur, ad misericordiam pertineat, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29404)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, etiam si aliquibus ex speciali privilegio sua praedestinatio reveletur, non tamen convenit ut reveletur omnibus, quia sic illi qui non sunt praedestinati, desperarent; et securitas in praedestinatis negligentiam pareret.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29405)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praedestinatio ponat aliquid in praedestinato. Omnis enim actio ex se passionem infert. Si ergo praedestinatio actio est in Deo, oportet quod praedestinatio passio sit in praedestinatis.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29406)\n\nPraeterea, Origenes dicit, super illud Rom. I, *qui praedestinatus est etc., praedestinatio est eius qui non est, sed destinatio eius est qui est*. Sed Augustinus dicit, in libro de praedestinatione sanctorum, *quid est praedestinatio, nisi destinatio alicuius?* Ergo praedestinatio non est nisi alicuius existentis. Et ita ponit aliquid in praedestinato.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29407)\n\nPraeterea, praeparatio est aliquid in praeparato. Sed praedestinatio est praeparatio beneficiorum Dei, ut dicit Augustinus, in libro de Praedest. Sanct. Ergo praedestinatio est aliquid in praedestinatis.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29408)\n\nPraeterea, temporale non ponitur in definitione aeterni. Sed gratia, quae est aliquid temporale, ponitur in definitione praedestinationis, nam praedestinatio dicitur esse *praeparatio gratiae in praesenti, et gloriae in futuro*. Ergo praedestinatio non est aliquid aeternum. Et ita oportet quod non sit in Deo, sed in praedestinatis, nam quidquid est in Deo, est aeternum.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29409)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, quod praedestinatio est praescientia beneficiorum Dei. Sed praescientia non est in praescitis, sed in praesciente. Ergo nec praedestinatio est in praedestinatis, sed in praedestinante.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29410)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod praedestinatio non est aliquid in praedestinatis, sed in praedestinante tantum. Dictum est enim quod praedestinatio est quaedam pars providentiae providentia autem non est in rebus provisis; sed est quaedam ratio in intellectu provisoris, ut supra dictum est. Sed executio providentiae, quae gubernatio dicitur, passive quidem est in gubernatis; active autem est in gubernante. Unde manifestum est quod praedestinatio est quaedam ratio ordinis aliquorum in salutem aeternam, in mente divina existens. Executio autem huius ordinis est passive quidem in praedestinatis; active autem est in Deo. Est autem executio praedestinationis vocatio et magnificatio, secundum illud apostoli, ad Rom. VIII, *quos praedestinavit, hos et vocavit; et quos vocavit, hos et magnificavit*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29411)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod actiones in exteriorem materiam transeuntes, inferunt ex se passionem, ut calefactio et secatio, non autem actiones in agente manentes, ut sunt intelligere et velle, ut supra dictum est. Et talis actio est praedestinatio. Unde praedestinatio non ponit aliquid in praedestinato. Sed executio eius, quae transit in exteriores res, ponit in eis aliquem effectum.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29412)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod destinatio aliquando sumitur pro reali missione alicuius ad aliquem terminum, et sic destinatio non est nisi eius quod est. Alio modo sumitur destinatio pro missione quam aliquis mente concipit, secundum quod dicimur destinare, quod mente firmiter proponimus, et hoc secundo modo dicitur II Machab. cap. VI, *Eleazarus destinavit non admittere illicita propter vitae amorem*. Et sic destinatio potest esse eius quod non est. Tamen praedestinatio, ratione antecessionis quam importat, potest esse eius quod non est, qualitercumque destinatio sumatur.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29413)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod duplex est praeparatio. Quaedam patientis, ut patiatur, et haec praeparatio est in praeparato. Quaedam alia est agentis, ut agat, et haec est in agente. Et talis praeparatio est praedestinatio; prout aliquod agens per intellectum dicitur se praeparare ad agendum, inquantum praeconcipit rationem operis fiendi. Et sic Deus ab aeterno praeparavit praedestinando, concipiens rationem ordinis aliquorum in salutem.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29414)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod gratia non ponitur in definitione praedestinationis, quasi aliquid existens de essentia eius, sed inquantum praedestinatio importat respectum ad gratiam, ut causae ad effectum, et actus ad obiectum. Unde non sequitur quod praedestinatio sit aliquid temporale.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29415)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus nullum hominem reprobet. Nullus enim reprobat quem diligit. Sed Deus omnem hominem diligit, secundum illud Sap. XI, *diligis omnia quae sunt, et nihil odisti eorum quae fecisti*. Ergo Deus nullum hominem reprobat.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29416)\n\nPraeterea, si Deus aliquem hominem reprobat, oportet quod sic se habeat reprobatio ad reprobatos, sicut praedestinatio ad praedestinatos. Sed praedestinatio est causa salutis praedestinatorum. Ergo reprobatio erit causa perditionis reproborum. Hoc autem est falsum, dicitur enim Osee XIII, *perditio tua, Israel, ex te est; tantummodo ex me auxilium tuum*. Non ergo Deus aliquem reprobat.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29417)\n\nPraeterea, nulli debet imputari quod vitare non potest. Sed si Deus aliquem reprobat, non potest vitare quin ipse pereat, dicitur enim Eccle. VII, *considera opera Dei, quod nemo possit corrigere quem ipse despexerit*. Ergo non esset hominibus imputandum quod pereunt. Hoc autem est falsum. Non ergo Deus aliquem reprobat.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29418)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Malach. I, *Iacob dilexi, Esau autem odio habui*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29419)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus aliquos reprobat. Dictum enim est supra quod praedestinatio est pars providentiae. Ad providentiam autem pertinet permittere aliquem defectum in rebus quae providentiae subduntur, ut supra dictum est. Unde, cum per divinam providentiam homines in vitam aeternam ordinentur, pertinet etiam ad divinam providentiam, ut permittat aliquos ab isto fine deficere. Et hoc dicitur reprobare. Sic igitur, sicut praedestinatio est pars providentiae respectu eorum qui divinitus ordinantur in aeternam salutem; ita reprobatio est pars providentiae respectu illorum qui ab hoc fine decidunt. Unde reprobatio non nominat praescientiam tantum, sed aliquid addit secundum rationem, sicut et providentia, ut supra dictum est. Sicut enim praedestinatio includit voluntatem conferendi gratiam et gloriam, ita reprobatio includit voluntatem permittendi aliquem cadere in culpam, et inferendi damnationis poenam pro culpa.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29420)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus omnes homines diligit, et etiam omnes creaturas, inquantum omnibus vult aliquod bonum, non tamen quodcumque bonum vult omnibus. Inquantum igitur quibusdam non vult hoc bonum quod est vita aeterna, dicitur eos habere odio, vel reprobare.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29421)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliter se habet reprobatio in causando, quam praedestinatio. Nam praedestinatio est causa et eius quod expectatur in futura vita a praedestinatis, scilicet gloriae; et eius quod percipitur in praesenti, scilicet gratiae. Reprobatio vero non est causa eius quod est in praesenti, scilicet culpae; sed est causa derelictionis a Deo. Est tamen causa eius quod redditur in futuro, scilicet poenae aeternae. Sed culpa provenit ex libero arbitrio eius qui reprobatur et a gratia deseritur. Et secundum hoc verificatur dictum prophetae, scilicet, *perditio tua, Israel, ex te*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29422)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod reprobatio Dei non subtrahit aliquid de potentia reprobati. Unde, cum dicitur quod reprobatus non potest gratiam adipisci, non est hoc intelligendum secundum impossibilitatem absolutam, sed secundum impossibilitatem conditionatam, sicut supra dictum est quod praedestinatum necesse est salvari, necessitate conditionata, quae non tollit libertatem arbitrii. Unde, licet aliquis non possit gratiam adipisci qui reprobatur a Deo, tamen quod in hoc peccatum vel illud labatur, ex eius libero arbitrio contingit. Unde et merito sibi imputatur in culpam.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29423)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praedestinati non eligantur a Deo. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod, sicut sol corporeus non eligendo omnibus corporibus lumen immittit, ita et Deus suam bonitatem. Sed bonitas divina communicatur praecipue aliquibus secundum participationem gratiae et gloriae. Ergo Deus absque electione gratiam et gloriam communicat. Quod ad praedestinationem pertinet.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29424)\n\nPraeterea, electio est eorum quae sunt. Sed praedestinatio ab aeterno est etiam eorum quae non sunt. Ergo praedestinantur aliqui absque electione.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29425)\n\nPraeterea, electio quandam discretionem importat. *Sed Deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri*, ut dicitur I Tim. II. Ergo praedestinatio, quae praeordinat homines in salutem, est absque electione.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29426)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ephes. I, *elegit nos in ipso ante mundi constitutionem*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29427)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod praedestinatio, secundum rationem, praesupponit electionem; et electio dilectionem. Cuius ratio est, quia praedestinatio, ut dictum est, est pars providentiae. Providentia autem, sicut et prudentia, est ratio in intellectu existens, praeceptiva ordinationis aliquorum in finem, ut supra dictum est. Non autem praecipitur aliquid ordinandum in finem, nisi praeexistente voluntate finis. Unde praedestinatio aliquorum in salutem aeternam, praesupponit, secundum rationem, quod Deus illorum velit salutem. Ad quod pertinet electio et dilectio. Dilectio quidem, inquantum vult eis hoc bonum salutis aeternae, nam diligere est velle alicui bonum, ut supra dictum est. Electio autem, inquantum hoc bonum aliquibus prae aliis vult, cum quosdam reprobet, ut supra dictum est. Electio tamen et dilectio aliter ordinantur in nobis et in Deo, eo quod in nobis voluntas diligendo non causat bonum; sed ex bono praeexistente incitamur ad diligendum. Et ideo eligimus aliquem, quem diligamus, et sic electio dilectionem praecedit in nobis. In Deo autem est e converso. Nam voluntas eius, qua vult bonum alicui diligendo, est causa quod illud bonum ab eo prae aliis habeatur. Et sic patet quod dilectio praesupponitur electioni, secundum rationem; et electio praedestinationi. Unde omnes praedestinati sunt electi et dilecti.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29428)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, si consideretur communicatio bonitatis divinae in communi, absque electione bonitatem suam communicat; inquantum scilicet nihil est, quod non participet aliquid de bonitate eius, ut supra dictum est. Sed si consideretur communicatio istius vel illius boni, non absque electione tribuit, quia quaedam bona dat aliquibus, quae non dat aliis. Et sic in collatione gratiae et gloriae attenditur electio.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29429)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quando voluntas eligentis provocatur ad eligendum a bono in re praeexistente, tunc oportet quod electio sit eorum quae sunt; sicut accidit in electione nostra. Sed in Deo est aliter, ut dictum est. Et ideo, sicut dicit Augustinus, *eliguntur a Deo qui non sunt, neque tamen errat qui eligit*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29430)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, Deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri antecedenter, quod non est simpliciter velle, sed secundum quid, non autem consequenter, quod est simpliciter velle.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29431)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praescientia meritorum sit causa praedestinationis. Dicit enim apostolus, Rom. VIII, *quos praescivit, hos et praedestinavit*. Et Glossa Ambrosii, super illud Rom. IX, *miserebor cui miserebor* etc., dicit, *misericordiam illi dabo, quem praescio toto corde reversurum ad me*. Ergo videtur quod praescientia meritorum sit causa praedestinationis.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29432)\n\nPraeterea, praedestinatio divina includit divinam voluntatem, quae irrationabilis esse non potest, cum praedestinatio sit propositum miserendi, ut Augustinus dicit. Sed nulla alia ratio potest esse praedestinationis nisi praescientia meritorum. Ergo praescientia meritorum est causa vel ratio praedestinationis.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29433)\n\nPraeterea, *non est iniquitas apud Deum*, ut dicitur Rom. IX. Iniquum autem esse videtur, ut aequalibus inaequalia dentur. Omnes autem homines sunt aequales et secundum naturam, et secundum peccatum originale, attenditur autem in eis inaequalitas secundum merita vel demerita propriorum actuum. Non igitur inaequalia praeparat Deus hominibus, praedestinando et reprobando, nisi propter differentium meritorum praescientiam.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29434)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit apostolus, ad Tit. III, *non ex operibus iustitiae, quae fecimus nos, sed secundum suam misericordiam salvos nos fecit*. Sicut autem salvos nos fecit, ita et praedestinavit nos salvos fieri. Non ergo praescientia meritorum est causa vel ratio praedestinationis.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29435)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum praedestinatio includat voluntatem, ut supra dictum est, sic inquirenda est ratio praedestinationis, sicut inquiritur ratio divinae voluntatis. Dictum est autem supra quod non est assignare causam divinae voluntatis ex parte actus volendi; sed potest assignari ratio ex parte volitorum, inquantum scilicet Deus vult esse aliquid propter aliud. Nullus ergo fuit ita insanae mentis, qui diceret merita esse causam divinae praedestinationis, ex parte actus praedestinantis. Sed hoc sub quaestione vertitur, utrum ex parte effectus, praedestinatio habeat aliquam causam. Et hoc est quaerere, utrum Deus praeordinaverit se daturum effectum praedestinationis alicui, propter merita aliqua. Fuerunt igitur quidam, qui dixerunt quod effectus praedestinationis praeordinatur alicui propter merita praeexistentia in alia vita. Et haec fuit positio Origenis, qui posuit animas humanas ab initio creatas, et secundum diversitatem suorum operum, diversos status eas sortiri in hoc mundo corporibus unitas. Sed hanc opinionem excludit apostolus, Rom. IX, dicens, *cum nondum nati fuissent, aut aliquid egissent boni vel mali, non ex operibus, sed ex vocante dictum est, quia maior serviet minori*. Fuerunt ergo alii, qui dixerunt quod merita praeexistentia in hac vita sunt ratio et causa effectus praedestinationis. Posuerunt enim Pelagiani quod initium benefaciendi sit ex nobis, consummatio autem a Deo. Et sic, ex hoc contingit quod alicui datur praedestinationis effectus, et non alteri, quia unus initium dedit se praeparando, et non alius. Sed contra hoc est quod dicit apostolus, II Cor. III, quod *non sumus sufficientes cogitare aliquid a nobis, quasi ex nobis*. Nullum autem anterius principium inveniri potest quam cogitatio. Unde non potest dici quod aliquod in nobis initium existat, quod sit ratio effectus praedestinationis. Unde fuerunt alii, qui dixerunt quod merita sequentia praedestinationis effectum, sunt ratio praedestinationis, ut intelligatur quod ideo Deus dat gratiam alicui, et praeordinavit se ei daturum, quia praescivit eum bene usurum gratia; sicut si rex det alicui militi equum, quem scit eo bene usurum. Sed isti videntur distinxisse inter id quod est ex gratia, et id quod est ex libero arbitrio, quasi non possit esse idem ex utroque. Manifestum est autem quod id quod est gratiae, est praedestinationis effectus, et hoc non potest poni ut ratio praedestinationis, cum hoc sub praedestinatione concludatur. Si igitur aliquid aliud ex parte nostra sit ratio praedestinationis, hoc erit praeter effectum praedestinationis. Non est autem distinctum quod est ex libero arbitrio, et ex praedestinatione; sicut nec est distinctum quod est ex causa secunda, et causa prima, divina enim providentia producit effectus per operationes causarum secundarum, ut supra dictum est. Unde et id quod est per liberum arbitrium, est ex praedestinatione. Dicendum est ergo quod effectum praedestinationis considerare possumus dupliciter. Uno modo, in particulari. Et sic nihil prohibet aliquem effectum praedestinationis esse causam et rationem alterius, posteriorem quidem prioris, secundum rationem causae finalis; priorem vero posterioris, secundum rationem causae meritoriae, quae reducitur ad dispositionem materiae. Sicut si dicamus quod Deus praeordinavit se daturum alicui gloriam ex meritis; et quod praeordinavit se daturum alicui gratiam, ut mereretur gloriam. Alio modo potest considerari praedestinationis effectus in communi. Et sic impossibile est quod totus praedestinationis effectus in communi habeat aliquam causam ex parte nostra. Quia quidquid est in homine ordinans ipsum in salutem, comprehenditur totum sub effectu praedestinationis, etiam ipsa praeparatio ad gratiam, neque enim hoc fit nisi per auxilium divinum, secundum illud Thren. ultimi, *converte nos, domine, ad te, et convertemur*. Habet tamen hoc modo praedestinatio, ex parte effectus, pro ratione divinam bonitatem; ad quam totus effectus praedestinationis ordinatur ut in finem, et ex qua procedit sicut ex principio primo movente.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29436)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod usus gratiae praescitus, non est ratio collationis gratiae, nisi secundum rationem causae finalis, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29437)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod praedestinatio habet rationem ex parte effectus, in communi, ipsam divinam bonitatem. In particulari autem, unus effectus est ratio alterius, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29438)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ex ipsa bonitate divina ratio sumi potest praedestinationis aliquorum, et reprobationis aliorum. Sic enim Deus dicitur omnia propter suam bonitatem fecisse, ut in rebus divina bonitas repraesentetur. Necesse est autem quod divina bonitas, quae in se est una et simplex, multiformiter repraesentetur in rebus; propter hoc quod res creatae ad simplicitatem divinam attingere non possunt. Et inde est quod ad completionem universi requiruntur diversi gradus rerum, quarum quaedam altum, et quaedam infimum locum teneant in universo. Et ut multiformitas graduum conservetur in rebus, Deus permittit aliqua mala fieri, ne multa bona impediantur, ut supra dictum est. Sic igitur consideremus totum genus humanum, sicut totam rerum universitatem. Voluit igitur Deus in hominibus, quantum ad aliquos, quos praedestinat, suam repraesentare bonitatem per modum misericordiae, parcendo; et quantum ad aliquos, quos reprobat, per modum iustitiae, puniendo. Et haec est ratio quare Deus quosdam eligit, et quosdam reprobat. Et hanc causam assignat apostolus, ad Rom. IX, dicens, volens Deus ostendere iram (idest vindictam iustitiae), et notam facere potentiam suam, sustinuit (idest permisit) *in multa patientia, vasa irae apta in interitum, ut ostenderet divitias gloriae suae in vasa misericordiae, quae praeparavit in gloriam*. Et II Tim. II dicit, *in magna autem domo non solum sunt vasa aurea et argentea, sed etiam lignea et fictilia; et quaedam quidem in honorem, quaedam in contumeliam*. Sed quare hos elegit in gloriam, et illos reprobavit, non habet rationem nisi divinam voluntatem. Unde Augustinus dicit, super Ioannem, *quare hunc trahat, et illum non trahat, noli velle diiudicare, si non vis errare*. Sicut etiam in rebus naturalibus potest assignari ratio, cum prima materia tota sit in se uniformis, quare una pars eius est sub forma ignis, et alia sub forma terrae, a Deo in principio condita, ut scilicet sit diversitas specierum in rebus naturalibus. Sed quare haec pars materiae est sub ista forma, et illa sub alia, dependet ex simplici divina voluntate. Sicut ex simplici voluntate artificis dependet, quod ille lapis est in ista parte parietis, et ille in alia, quamvis ratio artis habeat quod aliqui sint in hac, et aliqui sint in illa. Neque tamen propter hoc est iniquitas apud Deum, si inaequalia non inaequalibus praeparat. Hoc enim esset contra iustitiae rationem, si praedestinationis effectus ex debito redderetur, et non daretur ex gratia. In his enim quae ex gratia dantur, potest aliquis pro libito suo dare cui vult, plus vel minus, dummodo nulli subtrahat debitum, absque praeiudicio iustitiae. Et hoc est quod dicit paterfamilias, Matt. XX, *tolle quod tuum est, et vade. An non licet mihi quod volo, facere?*\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29439)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praedestinatio non sit certa. Quia super illud Apoc. III, *tene quod habes, ne alius accipiat coronam tuam*, dicit Augustinus, quod *alius non est accepturus, nisi iste perdiderit*. Potest ergo et acquiri et perdi corona, quae est praedestinationis effectus. Non est igitur praedestinatio certa.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29440)\n\nPraeterea, posito possibili, nullum sequitur impossibile. Possibile est autem aliquem praedestinatum, ut Petrum, peccare, et tunc occidi. Hoc autem posito, sequitur praedestinationis effectum frustrari. Hoc igitur non est impossibile. Non ergo est praedestinatio certa.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29441)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid Deus potuit, potest. Sed potuit non praedestinare quem praedestinavit. Ergo nunc potest non praedestinare. Ergo praedestinatio non est certa.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29442)\n\nSed contra est quod super illud Rom. VIII, *quos praescivit, et praedestinavit* etc., dicit Glossa, *praedestinatio est praescientia et praeparatio beneficiorum Dei, qua certissime liberantur quicumque liberantur*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29443)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod praedestinatio certissime et infallibiliter consequitur suum effectum, nec tamen imponit necessitatem, ut scilicet effectus eius ex necessitate proveniat. Dictum est enim supra quod praedestinatio est pars providentiae. Sed non omnia quae providentiae subduntur, necessaria sunt, sed quaedam contingenter eveniunt, secundum conditionem causarum proximarum, quas ad tales effectus divina providentia ordinavit. Et tamen providentiae ordo est infallibilis, ut supra ostensum est. Sic igitur et ordo praedestinationis est certus; et tamen libertas arbitrii non tollitur, ex qua contingenter provenit praedestinationis effectus. Ad hoc etiam consideranda sunt quae supra dicta sunt de divina scientia et de divina voluntate, quae contingentiam a rebus non tollunt, licet certissima et infallibilia sint.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29444)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod corona dicitur esse alicuius, dupliciter. Uno modo, ex praedestinatione divina, et sic nullus coronam suam amittit. Alio modo, ex merito gratiae, quod enim meremur, quodammodo nostrum est. Et sic suam coronam aliquis amittere potest per peccatum mortale sequens. Alius autem illam coronam amissam accipit, inquantum loco eius subrogatur. Non enim permittit Deus aliquos cadere, quin alios erigat, secundum illud Iob XXXIV, *conteret multos et innumerabiles, et stare faciet alios pro eis*. Sic enim in locum Angelorum cadentium substituti sunt homines; et in locum Iudaeorum, gentiles. Substitutus autem in statum gratiae, etiam quantum ad hoc coronam cadentis accipit, quod de bonis quae alius fecit, in aeterna vita gaudebit, in qua unusquisque gaudebit de bonis tam a se quam ab aliis factis.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29445)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet sit possibile eum qui est praedestinatus, mori in peccato mortali, secundum se consideratum; tamen hoc est impossibile, posito (prout scilicet ponitur) eum esse praedestinatum. Unde non sequitur quod praedestinatio falli possit.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29446)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum praedestinatio includat divinam voluntatem, sicut supra dictum est quod Deum velle aliquid creatum est necessarium ex suppositione, propter immutabilitatem divinae voluntatis, non tamen absolute; ita dicendum est hic de praedestinatione. Unde non oportet dicere quod Deus possit non praedestinare quem praedestinavit, in sensu composito accipiendo; licet, absolute considerando, Deus possit praedestinare vel non praedestinare. Sed ex hoc non tollitur praedestinationis certitudo.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29447)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod numerus praedestinatorum non sit certus. Numerus enim cui potest fieri additio, non est certus. Sed numero praedestinatorum potest fieri additio, ut videtur, dicitur enim Deut. I, *dominus Deus noster addat ad hunc numerum multa millia*; Glossa, *idest definitum apud Deum, qui novit qui sunt eius*. Ergo numerus praedestinatorum non est certus.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29448)\n\nPraeterea, non potest assignari ratio quare magis in hoc numero quam in alio, Deus homines praeordinet ad salutem. Sed nihil a Deo sine ratione disponitur. Ergo non est certus numerus salvandorum praeordinatus a Deo.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29449)\n\nPraeterea, operatio Dei est perfectior quam operatio naturae. Sed in operibus naturae bonum invenitur ut in pluribus, defectus autem et malum ut in paucioribus. Si igitur a Deo institueretur numerus salvandorum, plures essent salvandi quam damnandi. Cuius contrarium ostenditur Matt. VII, ubi dicitur, *lata et spatiosa est via quae ducit ad perditionem, et multi sunt qui intrant per eam, angusta est porta, et arcta via, quae ducit ad vitam, et pauci sunt qui inveniunt eam*. Non ergo est praeordinatus a Deo numerus salvandorum.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29450)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de correptione et gratia, *certus est praedestinatorum numerus, qui neque augeri potest, neque minui*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29451)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod numerus praedestinatorum est certus. Sed quidam dixerunt eum esse certum formaliter, sed non materialiter, ut puta si diceremus certum esse quod centum vel mille salventur, non autem quod hi vel illi. Sed hoc tollit certitudinem praedestinationis, de qua iam diximus. Et ideo oportet dicere quod numerus praedestinatorum sit certus Deo non solum formaliter, sed etiam materialiter. Sed advertendum est quod numerus praedestinatorum certus Deo dicitur, non solum ratione cognitionis, quia scilicet scit quot sunt salvandi (sic enim Deo certus est etiam numerus guttarum pluviae, et arenae maris); sed ratione electionis et definitionis cuiusdam. Ad cuius evidentiam, est sciendum quod omne agens intendit facere aliquid finitum, ut ex supradictis de infinito apparet. Quicumque autem intendit aliquam determinatam mensuram in suo effectu, excogitat aliquem numerum in partibus essentialibus eius, quae per se requiruntur ad perfectionem totius. Non enim per se eligit aliquem numerum in his quae non principaliter requiruntur, sed solum propter aliud, sed in tanto numero accipit huiusmodi, inquantum sunt necessaria propter aliud. Sicut aedificator excogitat determinatam mensuram domus, et etiam determinatum numerum mansionum quas vult facere in domo, et determinatum numerum mensurarum parietis vel tecti, non autem eligit determinatum numerum lapidum, sed accipit tot, quot sufficiunt ad explendam tantam mensuram parietis. Sic igitur considerandum est in Deo, respectu totius universitatis quae est eius effectus. Praeordinavit enim in qua mensura deberet esse totum universum, et quis numerus esset conveniens essentialibus partibus universi, quae scilicet habent aliquo modo ordinem ad perpetuitatem; quot scilicet sphaerae, quot stellae, quot elementa, quot species rerum. Individua vero corruptibilia non ordinantur ad bonum universi quasi principaliter, sed quasi secundario, inquantum in eis salvatur bonum speciei. Unde, licet Deus sciat numerum omnium individuorum, non tamen numerus vel boum vel culicum, vel aliorum huiusmodi, est per se praeordinatus a Deo, sed tot ex huiusmodi divina providentia produxit, quot sufficiunt ad specierum conservationem. Inter omnes autem creaturas, principalius ordinantur ad bonum universi creaturae rationales, quae, inquantum huiusmodi, incorruptibiles sunt; et potissime illae quae beatitudinem consequuntur, quae immediatius attingunt ultimum finem. Unde certus est Deo numerus praedestinatorum, non solum per modum cognitionis, sed etiam per modum cuiusdam principalis praefinitionis. Non sic autem omnino est de numero reproborum; qui videntur esse praeordinati a Deo in bonum electorum, quibus omnia cooperantur in bonum. De numero autem omnium praedestinatorum hominum, quis sit, dicunt quidam quod tot ex hominibus salvabuntur, quot Angeli ceciderunt. Quidam vero, quod tot salvabuntur, quot Angeli remanserunt. Quidam vero, quod tot ex hominibus salvabuntur, quot Angeli ceciderunt, et insuper tot, quot fuerunt Angeli creati. Sed melius dicitur quod *soli Deo est cognitus numerus electorum in superna felicitate locandus*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29452)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum illud Deuteronomii est intelligendum de illis qui sunt praenotati a Deo respectu praesentis iustitiae. Horum enim numerus et augetur et minuitur, et non numerus praedestinatorum.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29453)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio quantitatis alicuius partis, accipienda est ex proportione illius partis ad totum. Sic enim est apud Deum ratio quare tot stellas fecerit, vel tot rerum species, et quare tot praedestinavit, ex proportione partium principalium ad bonum universi.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29454)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod bonum proportionatum communi statui naturae, accidit ut in pluribus; et defectus ab hoc bono, ut in paucioribus. Sed bonum quod excedit communem statum naturae, invenitur ut in paucioribus; et defectus ab hoc bono, ut in pluribus. Sicut patet quod plures homines sunt qui habent sufficientem scientiam ad regimen vitae suae, pauciores autem qui hac scientia carent, qui moriones vel stulti dicuntur, sed paucissimi sunt, respectu aliorum, qui attingunt ad habendam profundam scientiam intelligibilium rerum. Cum igitur beatitudo aeterna, in visione Dei consistens, excedat communem statum naturae, et praecipue secundum quod est gratia destituta per corruptionem originalis peccati, pauciores sunt qui salvantur. Et in hoc etiam maxime misericordia Dei apparet, quod aliquos in illam salutem erigit, a qua plurimi deficiunt secundum communem cursum et inclinationem naturae.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29455)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praedestinatio non possit iuvari precibus sanctorum. Nullum enim aeternum praeceditur ab aliquo temporali, et per consequens non potest temporale iuvare ad hoc quod aliquod aeternum sit. Sed praedestinatio est aeterna. Cum igitur preces sanctorum sint temporales, non possunt iuvare ad hoc quod aliquis praedestinetur. Non ergo praedestinatio iuvatur precibus sanctorum.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29456)\n\nPraeterea, sicut nihil indiget consilio nisi propter defectum cognitionis, ita nihil indiget auxilio nisi propter defectum virtutis. Sed neutrum horum competit Deo praedestinanti, unde dicitur Rom. XI, *quis adiuvit spiritum domini? Aut quis consiliarius eius fuit?* Ergo praedestinatio non iuvatur precibus sanctorum.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29457)\n\nPraeterea, eiusdem est adiuvari et impediri. Sed praedestinatio non potest aliquo impediri. Ergo non potest aliquo iuvari.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29458)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Genes. XXV, quod *Isaac rogavit Deum pro Rebecca uxore sua, et dedit conceptum Rebeccae*. Ex illo autem conceptu natus est Iacob, qui praedestinatus fuit. Non autem fuisset impleta praedestinatio, si natus non fuisset. Ergo praedestinatio iuvatur precibus sanctorum.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29459)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hanc quaestionem diversi errores fuerunt. Quidam enim, attendentes certitudinem divinae praedestinationis, dixerunt superfluas esse orationes, vel quidquid aliud fiat ad salutem aeternam consequendam, quia his factis vel non factis, praedestinati consequuntur, reprobati non consequuntur. Sed contra hoc sunt omnes admonitiones sacrae Scripturae, exhortantes ad orationem, et ad alia bona opera. Alii vero dixerunt quod per orationes mutatur divina praedestinatio. Et haec dicitur fuisse opinio Aegyptiorum, qui ponebant ordinationem divinam, quam fatum appellabant, aliquibus sacrificiis et orationibus impediri posse. Sed contra hoc etiam est auctoritas sacrae Scripturae. Dicitur enim I Reg. XV, porro *triumphator in Israel non parcet, neque poenitudine flectetur*. Et Rom. XI dicitur quod *sine poenitentia sunt dona Dei et vocatio*. Et ideo aliter dicendum, quod in praedestinatione duo sunt consideranda, scilicet ipsa praeordinatio divina, et effectus eius. Quantum igitur ad primum, nullo modo praedestinatio iuvatur precibus sanctorum, non enim precibus sanctorum fit, quod aliquis praedestinetur a Deo. Quantum vero ad secundum, dicitur praedestinatio iuvari precibus sanctorum, et aliis bonis operibus, quia providentia, cuius praedestinatio est pars, non subtrahit causas secundas, sed sic providet effectus, ut etiam ordo causarum secundarum subiaceat providentiae. Sicut igitur sic providentur naturales effectus, ut etiam causae naturales ad illos naturales effectus ordinentur, sine quibus illi effectus non provenirent; ita praedestinatur a Deo salus alicuius, ut etiam sub ordine praedestinationis cadat quidquid hominem promovet in salutem, vel orationes propriae, vel aliorum, vel alia bona, vel quidquid huiusmodi, sine quibus aliquis salutem non consequitur. Unde praedestinatis conandum est ad bene operandum et orandum, quia per huiusmodi praedestinationis effectus certitudinaliter impletur. Propter quod dicitur II Petr. I, *satagite, ut per bona opera certam vestram vocationem et electionem faciatis*.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29460)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa ostendit quod praedestinatio non iuvatur precibus sanctorum, quantum ad ipsam praeordinationem.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29461)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliquis dicitur adiuvari per alium, dupliciter. Uno modo, inquantum ab eo accipit virtutem, et sic adiuvari infirmi est, unde Deo non competit. Et sic intelligitur illud, *quis adiuvit spiritum domini?* Alio modo dicitur quis adiuvari per aliquem, per quem exequitur suam operationem, sicut dominus per ministrum. Et hoc modo Deus adiuvatur per nos, inquantum exequimur suam ordinationem, secundum illud I ad Cor. III, *Dei enim adiutores sumus*. Neque hoc est propter defectum divinae virtutis, sed quia utitur causis mediis, ut ordinis pulchritudo servetur in rebus, et ut etiam creaturis dignitatem causalitatis communicet.\n\n[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29462)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod secundae causae non possunt egredi ordinem causae primae universalis, ut supra dictum est; sed ipsum exequuntur. Et ideo praedestinatio per creaturas potest adiuvari, sed non impediri.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0002","text":"[I q. 23 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","text":"Post considerationem divinae providentiae, agendum est de praedestinatione, et de libro vitae. Et circa praedestinationem quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum Deo conveniat praedestinatio. Secundo, quid sit praedestinatio; et utrum ponat aliquid in praedestinato. Tertio, utrum Deo competat reprobatio aliquorum hominum. Quarto, de comparatione praedestinationis ad electionem; utrum scilicet praedestinati eligantur. Quinto, utrum merita sint causa vel ratio praedestinationis, vel reprobationis, aut electionis. Sexto, de certitudine praedestinationis; utrum scilicet praedestinati infallibiliter salventur. Septimo, utrum numerus praedestinatorum sit certus. Octavo, utrum praedestinatio possit iuvari precibus sanctorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0005","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non praedestinentur a Deo. Dicit enim Damascenus, in II libro, *oportet cognoscere quod omnia quidem praecognoscit Deus, non autem omnia praedeterminat. Praecognoscit enim ea quae in nobis sunt; non autem praedeterminat ea*. Sed merita et demerita humana sunt in nobis, inquantum sumus nostrorum actuum domini per liberum arbitrium. Ea ergo quae pertinent ad meritum vel demeritum, non praedestinantur a Deo. Et sic hominum praedestinatio tollitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0007","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0008","text":"Praeterea, omnes creaturae ordinantur ad suos fines per divinam providentiam, ut supra dictum est. Sed aliae creaturae non dicuntur praedestinari a Deo. Ergo nec homines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0009","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0010","text":"Praeterea, Angeli sunt capaces beatitudinis, sicut et homines. Sed Angelis non competit praedestinari, ut videtur, cum in eis nunquam fuerit miseria; praedestinatio autem est propositum miserendi, ut dicit Augustinus. Ergo homines non praedestinantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0011","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0012","text":"Praeterea, beneficia hominibus a Deo collata, per spiritum sanctum viris sanctis revelantur, secundum illud apostoli, I Cor. II, *nos autem non spiritum huius mundi accepimus, sed spiritum qui ex Deo est, ut sciamus quae a Deo donata sunt nobis*. Si ergo homines praedestinarentur a Deo, cum praedestinatio sit Dei beneficium, esset praedestinatis nota sua praedestinatio. Quod patet esse falsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0013","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Rom. VIII, *quos praedestinavit, hos et vocavit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0015","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deo conveniens est homines praedestinare. Omnia enim divinae providentiae subiacent, ut supra ostensum est. Ad providentiam autem pertinet res in finem ordinare, ut dictum est. Finis autem ad quem res creatae ordinantur a Deo, est duplex. Unus, qui excedit proportionem naturae creatae et facultatem, et hic finis est vita aeterna, quae in divina visione consistit, quae est supra naturam cuiuslibet creaturae, ut supra habitum est. Alius autem finis est naturae creatae proportionatus, quem scilicet res creata potest attingere secundum virtutem suae naturae. Ad illud autem ad quod non potest aliquid virtute suae naturae pervenire, oportet quod ab alio transmittatur; sicut sagitta a sagittante mittitur ad signum. Unde, proprie loquendo, rationalis creatura, quae est capax vitae aeternae, perducitur in ipsam quasi a Deo transmissa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","text":"Cuius quidem transmissionis ratio in Deo praeexistit; sicut et in eo est ratio ordinis omnium in finem, quam diximus esse providentiam. Ratio autem alicuius fiendi in mente actoris existens, est quaedam praeexistentia rei fiendae in eo. Unde ratio praedictae transmissionis creaturae rationalis in finem vitae aeternae, praedestinatio nominatur, nam destinare est mittere. Et sic patet quod praedestinatio, quantum ad obiecta, est quaedam pars providentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0018","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Damascenus nominat praedeterminationem impositionem necessitatis; sicut est in rebus naturalibus, quae sunt praedeterminatae ad unum. Quod patet ex eo quod subdit, *non enim vult malitiam, neque compellit virtutem*. Unde praedestinatio non excluditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0020","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod creaturae irrationales non sunt capaces illius finis qui facultatem humanae naturae excedit. Unde non proprie dicuntur praedestinari, etsi aliquando abusive praedestinatio nominetur respectu cuiuscumque alterius finis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0022","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod praedestinari convenit Angelis, sicut et hominibus, licet nunquam fuerint miseri. Nam motus non accipit speciem a termino a quo, sed a termino ad quem, nihil enim refert, quantum ad rationem dealbationis, utrum ille qui dealbatur, fuerit niger aut pallidus vel rubeus. Et similiter nihil refert ad rationem praedestinationis, utrum aliquis praedestinetur in vitam aeternam a statu miseriae, vel non. Quamvis dici possit quod omnis collatio boni supra debitum eius cui confertur, ad misericordiam pertineat, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0024","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, etiam si aliquibus ex speciali privilegio sua praedestinatio reveletur, non tamen convenit ut reveletur omnibus, quia sic illi qui non sunt praedestinati, desperarent; et securitas in praedestinatis negligentiam pareret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0027","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praedestinatio ponat aliquid in praedestinato. Omnis enim actio ex se passionem infert. Si ergo praedestinatio actio est in Deo, oportet quod praedestinatio passio sit in praedestinatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0029","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0030","text":"Praeterea, Origenes dicit, super illud Rom. I, *qui praedestinatus est etc., praedestinatio est eius qui non est, sed destinatio eius est qui est*. Sed Augustinus dicit, in libro de praedestinatione sanctorum, *quid est praedestinatio, nisi destinatio alicuius?* Ergo praedestinatio non est nisi alicuius existentis. Et ita ponit aliquid in praedestinato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0031","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0032","text":"Praeterea, praeparatio est aliquid in praeparato. Sed praedestinatio est praeparatio beneficiorum Dei, ut dicit Augustinus, in libro de Praedest. Sanct. Ergo praedestinatio est aliquid in praedestinatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0033","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0034","text":"Praeterea, temporale non ponitur in definitione aeterni. Sed gratia, quae est aliquid temporale, ponitur in definitione praedestinationis, nam praedestinatio dicitur esse *praeparatio gratiae in praesenti, et gloriae in futuro*. Ergo praedestinatio non est aliquid aeternum. Et ita oportet quod non sit in Deo, sed in praedestinatis, nam quidquid est in Deo, est aeternum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0035","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0036","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, quod praedestinatio est praescientia beneficiorum Dei. Sed praescientia non est in praescitis, sed in praesciente. Ergo nec praedestinatio est in praedestinatis, sed in praedestinante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0037","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod praedestinatio non est aliquid in praedestinatis, sed in praedestinante tantum. Dictum est enim quod praedestinatio est quaedam pars providentiae providentia autem non est in rebus provisis; sed est quaedam ratio in intellectu provisoris, ut supra dictum est. Sed executio providentiae, quae gubernatio dicitur, passive quidem est in gubernatis; active autem est in gubernante. Unde manifestum est quod praedestinatio est quaedam ratio ordinis aliquorum in salutem aeternam, in mente divina existens. Executio autem huius ordinis est passive quidem in praedestinatis; active autem est in Deo. Est autem executio praedestinationis vocatio et magnificatio, secundum illud apostoli, ad Rom. VIII, *quos praedestinavit, hos et vocavit; et quos vocavit, hos et magnificavit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0039","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod actiones in exteriorem materiam transeuntes, inferunt ex se passionem, ut calefactio et secatio, non autem actiones in agente manentes, ut sunt intelligere et velle, ut supra dictum est. Et talis actio est praedestinatio. Unde praedestinatio non ponit aliquid in praedestinato. Sed executio eius, quae transit in exteriores res, ponit in eis aliquem effectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0041","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod destinatio aliquando sumitur pro reali missione alicuius ad aliquem terminum, et sic destinatio non est nisi eius quod est. Alio modo sumitur destinatio pro missione quam aliquis mente concipit, secundum quod dicimur destinare, quod mente firmiter proponimus, et hoc secundo modo dicitur II Machab. cap. VI, *Eleazarus destinavit non admittere illicita propter vitae amorem*. Et sic destinatio potest esse eius quod non est. Tamen praedestinatio, ratione antecessionis quam importat, potest esse eius quod non est, qualitercumque destinatio sumatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0043","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0044","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod duplex est praeparatio. Quaedam patientis, ut patiatur, et haec praeparatio est in praeparato. Quaedam alia est agentis, ut agat, et haec est in agente. Et talis praeparatio est praedestinatio; prout aliquod agens per intellectum dicitur se praeparare ad agendum, inquantum praeconcipit rationem operis fiendi. Et sic Deus ab aeterno praeparavit praedestinando, concipiens rationem ordinis aliquorum in salutem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0045","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0046","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod gratia non ponitur in definitione praedestinationis, quasi aliquid existens de essentia eius, sed inquantum praedestinatio importat respectum ad gratiam, ut causae ad effectum, et actus ad obiectum. Unde non sequitur quod praedestinatio sit aliquid temporale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0047","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0048","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0049","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus nullum hominem reprobet. Nullus enim reprobat quem diligit. Sed Deus omnem hominem diligit, secundum illud Sap. XI, *diligis omnia quae sunt, et nihil odisti eorum quae fecisti*. Ergo Deus nullum hominem reprobat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0050","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0051","text":"Praeterea, si Deus aliquem hominem reprobat, oportet quod sic se habeat reprobatio ad reprobatos, sicut praedestinatio ad praedestinatos. Sed praedestinatio est causa salutis praedestinatorum. Ergo reprobatio erit causa perditionis reproborum. Hoc autem est falsum, dicitur enim Osee XIII, *perditio tua, Israel, ex te est; tantummodo ex me auxilium tuum*. Non ergo Deus aliquem reprobat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0052","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0053","text":"Praeterea, nulli debet imputari quod vitare non potest. Sed si Deus aliquem reprobat, non potest vitare quin ipse pereat, dicitur enim Eccle. VII, *considera opera Dei, quod nemo possit corrigere quem ipse despexerit*. Ergo non esset hominibus imputandum quod pereunt. Hoc autem est falsum. Non ergo Deus aliquem reprobat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0054","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0055","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Malach. I, *Iacob dilexi, Esau autem odio habui*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0056","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus aliquos reprobat. Dictum enim est supra quod praedestinatio est pars providentiae. Ad providentiam autem pertinet permittere aliquem defectum in rebus quae providentiae subduntur, ut supra dictum est. Unde, cum per divinam providentiam homines in vitam aeternam ordinentur, pertinet etiam ad divinam providentiam, ut permittat aliquos ab isto fine deficere. Et hoc dicitur reprobare. Sic igitur, sicut praedestinatio est pars providentiae respectu eorum qui divinitus ordinantur in aeternam salutem; ita reprobatio est pars providentiae respectu illorum qui ab hoc fine decidunt. Unde reprobatio non nominat praescientiam tantum, sed aliquid addit secundum rationem, sicut et providentia, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","text":"Sicut enim praedestinatio includit voluntatem conferendi gratiam et gloriam, ita reprobatio includit voluntatem permittendi aliquem cadere in culpam, et inferendi damnationis poenam pro culpa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0059","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0060","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus omnes homines diligit, et etiam omnes creaturas, inquantum omnibus vult aliquod bonum, non tamen quodcumque bonum vult omnibus. Inquantum igitur quibusdam non vult hoc bonum quod est vita aeterna, dicitur eos habere odio, vel reprobare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0061","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliter se habet reprobatio in causando, quam praedestinatio. Nam praedestinatio est causa et eius quod expectatur in futura vita a praedestinatis, scilicet gloriae; et eius quod percipitur in praesenti, scilicet gratiae. Reprobatio vero non est causa eius quod est in praesenti, scilicet culpae; sed est causa derelictionis a Deo. Est tamen causa eius quod redditur in futuro, scilicet poenae aeternae. Sed culpa provenit ex libero arbitrio eius qui reprobatur et a gratia deseritur. Et secundum hoc verificatur dictum prophetae, scilicet, *perditio tua, Israel, ex te*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0063","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0064","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod reprobatio Dei non subtrahit aliquid de potentia reprobati. Unde, cum dicitur quod reprobatus non potest gratiam adipisci, non est hoc intelligendum secundum impossibilitatem absolutam, sed secundum impossibilitatem conditionatam, sicut supra dictum est quod praedestinatum necesse est salvari, necessitate conditionata, quae non tollit libertatem arbitrii. Unde, licet aliquis non possit gratiam adipisci qui reprobatur a Deo, tamen quod in hoc peccatum vel illud labatur, ex eius libero arbitrio contingit. Unde et merito sibi imputatur in culpam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0065","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0066","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0067","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praedestinati non eligantur a Deo. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod, sicut sol corporeus non eligendo omnibus corporibus lumen immittit, ita et Deus suam bonitatem. Sed bonitas divina communicatur praecipue aliquibus secundum participationem gratiae et gloriae. Ergo Deus absque electione gratiam et gloriam communicat. Quod ad praedestinationem pertinet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0068","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0069","text":"Praeterea, electio est eorum quae sunt. Sed praedestinatio ab aeterno est etiam eorum quae non sunt. Ergo praedestinantur aliqui absque electione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0070","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0071","text":"Praeterea, electio quandam discretionem importat. *Sed Deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri*, ut dicitur I Tim. II. Ergo praedestinatio, quae praeordinat homines in salutem, est absque electione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0072","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ephes. I, *elegit nos in ipso ante mundi constitutionem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0074","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod praedestinatio, secundum rationem, praesupponit electionem; et electio dilectionem. Cuius ratio est, quia praedestinatio, ut dictum est, est pars providentiae. Providentia autem, sicut et prudentia, est ratio in intellectu existens, praeceptiva ordinationis aliquorum in finem, ut supra dictum est. Non autem praecipitur aliquid ordinandum in finem, nisi praeexistente voluntate finis. Unde praedestinatio aliquorum in salutem aeternam, praesupponit, secundum rationem, quod Deus illorum velit salutem. Ad quod pertinet electio et dilectio. Dilectio quidem, inquantum vult eis hoc bonum salutis aeternae, nam diligere est velle alicui bonum, ut supra dictum est. Electio autem, inquantum hoc bonum aliquibus prae aliis vult, cum quosdam reprobet, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","text":"Electio tamen et dilectio aliter ordinantur in nobis et in Deo, eo quod in nobis voluntas diligendo non causat bonum; sed ex bono praeexistente incitamur ad diligendum. Et ideo eligimus aliquem, quem diligamus, et sic electio dilectionem praecedit in nobis. In Deo autem est e converso. Nam voluntas eius, qua vult bonum alicui diligendo, est causa quod illud bonum ab eo prae aliis habeatur. Et sic patet quod dilectio praesupponitur electioni, secundum rationem; et electio praedestinationi. Unde omnes praedestinati sunt electi et dilecti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0077","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0078","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, si consideretur communicatio bonitatis divinae in communi, absque electione bonitatem suam communicat; inquantum scilicet nihil est, quod non participet aliquid de bonitate eius, ut supra dictum est. Sed si consideretur communicatio istius vel illius boni, non absque electione tribuit, quia quaedam bona dat aliquibus, quae non dat aliis. Et sic in collatione gratiae et gloriae attenditur electio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0079","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0080","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quando voluntas eligentis provocatur ad eligendum a bono in re praeexistente, tunc oportet quod electio sit eorum quae sunt; sicut accidit in electione nostra. Sed in Deo est aliter, ut dictum est. Et ideo, sicut dicit Augustinus, *eliguntur a Deo qui non sunt, neque tamen errat qui eligit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0081","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0082","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, Deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri antecedenter, quod non est simpliciter velle, sed secundum quid, non autem consequenter, quod est simpliciter velle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0083","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0084","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0085","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praescientia meritorum sit causa praedestinationis. Dicit enim apostolus, Rom. VIII, *quos praescivit, hos et praedestinavit*. Et Glossa Ambrosii, super illud Rom. IX, *miserebor cui miserebor* etc., dicit, *misericordiam illi dabo, quem praescio toto corde reversurum ad me*. Ergo videtur quod praescientia meritorum sit causa praedestinationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0086","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0087","text":"Praeterea, praedestinatio divina includit divinam voluntatem, quae irrationabilis esse non potest, cum praedestinatio sit propositum miserendi, ut Augustinus dicit. Sed nulla alia ratio potest esse praedestinationis nisi praescientia meritorum. Ergo praescientia meritorum est causa vel ratio praedestinationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0088","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0089","text":"Praeterea, *non est iniquitas apud Deum*, ut dicitur Rom. IX. Iniquum autem esse videtur, ut aequalibus inaequalia dentur. Omnes autem homines sunt aequales et secundum naturam, et secundum peccatum originale, attenditur autem in eis inaequalitas secundum merita vel demerita propriorum actuum. Non igitur inaequalia praeparat Deus hominibus, praedestinando et reprobando, nisi propter differentium meritorum praescientiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0090","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0091","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit apostolus, ad Tit. III, *non ex operibus iustitiae, quae fecimus nos, sed secundum suam misericordiam salvos nos fecit*. Sicut autem salvos nos fecit, ita et praedestinavit nos salvos fieri. Non ergo praescientia meritorum est causa vel ratio praedestinationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0092","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0093","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum praedestinatio includat voluntatem, ut supra dictum est, sic inquirenda est ratio praedestinationis, sicut inquiritur ratio divinae voluntatis. Dictum est autem supra quod non est assignare causam divinae voluntatis ex parte actus volendi; sed potest assignari ratio ex parte volitorum, inquantum scilicet Deus vult esse aliquid propter aliud. Nullus ergo fuit ita insanae mentis, qui diceret merita esse causam divinae praedestinationis, ex parte actus praedestinantis. Sed hoc sub quaestione vertitur, utrum ex parte effectus, praedestinatio habeat aliquam causam. Et hoc est quaerere, utrum Deus praeordinaverit se daturum effectum praedestinationis alicui, propter merita aliqua. Fuerunt igitur quidam, qui dixerunt quod effectus praedestinationis praeordinatur alicui propter merita praeexistentia in alia vita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0094","text":"Et haec fuit positio Origenis, qui posuit animas humanas ab initio creatas, et secundum diversitatem suorum operum, diversos status eas sortiri in hoc mundo corporibus unitas. Sed hanc opinionem excludit apostolus, Rom. IX, dicens, *cum nondum nati fuissent, aut aliquid egissent boni vel mali, non ex operibus, sed ex vocante dictum est, quia maior serviet minori*. Fuerunt ergo alii, qui dixerunt quod merita praeexistentia in hac vita sunt ratio et causa effectus praedestinationis. Posuerunt enim Pelagiani quod initium benefaciendi sit ex nobis, consummatio autem a Deo. Et sic, ex hoc contingit quod alicui datur praedestinationis effectus, et non alteri, quia unus initium dedit se praeparando, et non alius. Sed contra hoc est quod dicit apostolus, II Cor. III, quod *non sumus sufficientes cogitare aliquid a nobis, quasi ex nobis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0095","text":"Nullum autem anterius principium inveniri potest quam cogitatio. Unde non potest dici quod aliquod in nobis initium existat, quod sit ratio effectus praedestinationis. Unde fuerunt alii, qui dixerunt quod merita sequentia praedestinationis effectum, sunt ratio praedestinationis, ut intelligatur quod ideo Deus dat gratiam alicui, et praeordinavit se ei daturum, quia praescivit eum bene usurum gratia; sicut si rex det alicui militi equum, quem scit eo bene usurum. Sed isti videntur distinxisse inter id quod est ex gratia, et id quod est ex libero arbitrio, quasi non possit esse idem ex utroque. Manifestum est autem quod id quod est gratiae, est praedestinationis effectus, et hoc non potest poni ut ratio praedestinationis, cum hoc sub praedestinatione concludatur. Si igitur aliquid aliud ex parte nostra sit ratio praedestinationis, hoc erit praeter effectum praedestinationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","text":"Non est autem distinctum quod est ex libero arbitrio, et ex praedestinatione; sicut nec est distinctum quod est ex causa secunda, et causa prima, divina enim providentia producit effectus per operationes causarum secundarum, ut supra dictum est. Unde et id quod est per liberum arbitrium, est ex praedestinatione. Dicendum est ergo quod effectum praedestinationis considerare possumus dupliciter. Uno modo, in particulari. Et sic nihil prohibet aliquem effectum praedestinationis esse causam et rationem alterius, posteriorem quidem prioris, secundum rationem causae finalis; priorem vero posterioris, secundum rationem causae meritoriae, quae reducitur ad dispositionem materiae. Sicut si dicamus quod Deus praeordinavit se daturum alicui gloriam ex meritis; et quod praeordinavit se daturum alicui gratiam, ut mereretur gloriam. Alio modo potest considerari praedestinationis effectus in communi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","text":"Et sic impossibile est quod totus praedestinationis effectus in communi habeat aliquam causam ex parte nostra. Quia quidquid est in homine ordinans ipsum in salutem, comprehenditur totum sub effectu praedestinationis, etiam ipsa praeparatio ad gratiam, neque enim hoc fit nisi per auxilium divinum, secundum illud Thren. ultimi, *converte nos, domine, ad te, et convertemur*. Habet tamen hoc modo praedestinatio, ex parte effectus, pro ratione divinam bonitatem; ad quam totus effectus praedestinationis ordinatur ut in finem, et ex qua procedit sicut ex principio primo movente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0098","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0099","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod usus gratiae praescitus, non est ratio collationis gratiae, nisi secundum rationem causae finalis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0100","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0101","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod praedestinatio habet rationem ex parte effectus, in communi, ipsam divinam bonitatem. In particulari autem, unus effectus est ratio alterius, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0102","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0103","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ex ipsa bonitate divina ratio sumi potest praedestinationis aliquorum, et reprobationis aliorum. Sic enim Deus dicitur omnia propter suam bonitatem fecisse, ut in rebus divina bonitas repraesentetur. Necesse est autem quod divina bonitas, quae in se est una et simplex, multiformiter repraesentetur in rebus; propter hoc quod res creatae ad simplicitatem divinam attingere non possunt. Et inde est quod ad completionem universi requiruntur diversi gradus rerum, quarum quaedam altum, et quaedam infimum locum teneant in universo. Et ut multiformitas graduum conservetur in rebus, Deus permittit aliqua mala fieri, ne multa bona impediantur, ut supra dictum est. Sic igitur consideremus totum genus humanum, sicut totam rerum universitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","text":"Voluit igitur Deus in hominibus, quantum ad aliquos, quos praedestinat, suam repraesentare bonitatem per modum misericordiae, parcendo; et quantum ad aliquos, quos reprobat, per modum iustitiae, puniendo. Et haec est ratio quare Deus quosdam eligit, et quosdam reprobat. Et hanc causam assignat apostolus, ad Rom. IX, dicens, volens Deus ostendere iram (idest vindictam iustitiae), et notam facere potentiam suam, sustinuit (idest permisit) *in multa patientia, vasa irae apta in interitum, ut ostenderet divitias gloriae suae in vasa misericordiae, quae praeparavit in gloriam*. Et II Tim. II dicit, *in magna autem domo non solum sunt vasa aurea et argentea, sed etiam lignea et fictilia; et quaedam quidem in honorem, quaedam in contumeliam*. Sed quare hos elegit in gloriam, et illos reprobavit, non habet rationem nisi divinam voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","text":"Unde Augustinus dicit, super Ioannem, *quare hunc trahat, et illum non trahat, noli velle diiudicare, si non vis errare*. Sicut etiam in rebus naturalibus potest assignari ratio, cum prima materia tota sit in se uniformis, quare una pars eius est sub forma ignis, et alia sub forma terrae, a Deo in principio condita, ut scilicet sit diversitas specierum in rebus naturalibus. Sed quare haec pars materiae est sub ista forma, et illa sub alia, dependet ex simplici divina voluntate. Sicut ex simplici voluntate artificis dependet, quod ille lapis est in ista parte parietis, et ille in alia, quamvis ratio artis habeat quod aliqui sint in hac, et aliqui sint in illa. Neque tamen propter hoc est iniquitas apud Deum, si inaequalia non inaequalibus praeparat. Hoc enim esset contra iustitiae rationem, si praedestinationis effectus ex debito redderetur, et non daretur ex gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","text":"In his enim quae ex gratia dantur, potest aliquis pro libito suo dare cui vult, plus vel minus, dummodo nulli subtrahat debitum, absque praeiudicio iustitiae. Et hoc est quod dicit paterfamilias, Matt. XX, *tolle quod tuum est, et vade. An non licet mihi quod volo, facere?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0107","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0108","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0109","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praedestinatio non sit certa. Quia super illud Apoc. III, *tene quod habes, ne alius accipiat coronam tuam*, dicit Augustinus, quod *alius non est accepturus, nisi iste perdiderit*. Potest ergo et acquiri et perdi corona, quae est praedestinationis effectus. Non est igitur praedestinatio certa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0110","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0111","text":"Praeterea, posito possibili, nullum sequitur impossibile. Possibile est autem aliquem praedestinatum, ut Petrum, peccare, et tunc occidi. Hoc autem posito, sequitur praedestinationis effectum frustrari. Hoc igitur non est impossibile. Non ergo est praedestinatio certa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0112","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0113","text":"Praeterea, quidquid Deus potuit, potest. Sed potuit non praedestinare quem praedestinavit. Ergo nunc potest non praedestinare. Ergo praedestinatio non est certa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0114","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0115","text":"Sed contra est quod super illud Rom. VIII, *quos praescivit, et praedestinavit* etc., dicit Glossa, *praedestinatio est praescientia et praeparatio beneficiorum Dei, qua certissime liberantur quicumque liberantur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0116","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod praedestinatio certissime et infallibiliter consequitur suum effectum, nec tamen imponit necessitatem, ut scilicet effectus eius ex necessitate proveniat. Dictum est enim supra quod praedestinatio est pars providentiae. Sed non omnia quae providentiae subduntur, necessaria sunt, sed quaedam contingenter eveniunt, secundum conditionem causarum proximarum, quas ad tales effectus divina providentia ordinavit. Et tamen providentiae ordo est infallibilis, ut supra ostensum est. Sic igitur et ordo praedestinationis est certus; et tamen libertas arbitrii non tollitur, ex qua contingenter provenit praedestinationis effectus. Ad hoc etiam consideranda sunt quae supra dicta sunt de divina scientia et de divina voluntate, quae contingentiam a rebus non tollunt, licet certissima et infallibilia sint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0118","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0119","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod corona dicitur esse alicuius, dupliciter. Uno modo, ex praedestinatione divina, et sic nullus coronam suam amittit. Alio modo, ex merito gratiae, quod enim meremur, quodammodo nostrum est. Et sic suam coronam aliquis amittere potest per peccatum mortale sequens. Alius autem illam coronam amissam accipit, inquantum loco eius subrogatur. Non enim permittit Deus aliquos cadere, quin alios erigat, secundum illud Iob XXXIV, *conteret multos et innumerabiles, et stare faciet alios pro eis*. Sic enim in locum Angelorum cadentium substituti sunt homines; et in locum Iudaeorum, gentiles. Substitutus autem in statum gratiae, etiam quantum ad hoc coronam cadentis accipit, quod de bonis quae alius fecit, in aeterna vita gaudebit, in qua unusquisque gaudebit de bonis tam a se quam ab aliis factis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0120","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0121","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet sit possibile eum qui est praedestinatus, mori in peccato mortali, secundum se consideratum; tamen hoc est impossibile, posito (prout scilicet ponitur) eum esse praedestinatum. Unde non sequitur quod praedestinatio falli possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0122","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum praedestinatio includat divinam voluntatem, sicut supra dictum est quod Deum velle aliquid creatum est necessarium ex suppositione, propter immutabilitatem divinae voluntatis, non tamen absolute; ita dicendum est hic de praedestinatione. Unde non oportet dicere quod Deus possit non praedestinare quem praedestinavit, in sensu composito accipiendo; licet, absolute considerando, Deus possit praedestinare vel non praedestinare. Sed ex hoc non tollitur praedestinationis certitudo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0124","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0125","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0126","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod numerus praedestinatorum non sit certus. Numerus enim cui potest fieri additio, non est certus. Sed numero praedestinatorum potest fieri additio, ut videtur, dicitur enim Deut. I, *dominus Deus noster addat ad hunc numerum multa millia*; Glossa, *idest definitum apud Deum, qui novit qui sunt eius*. Ergo numerus praedestinatorum non est certus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0127","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0128","text":"Praeterea, non potest assignari ratio quare magis in hoc numero quam in alio, Deus homines praeordinet ad salutem. Sed nihil a Deo sine ratione disponitur. Ergo non est certus numerus salvandorum praeordinatus a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0129","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0130","text":"Praeterea, operatio Dei est perfectior quam operatio naturae. Sed in operibus naturae bonum invenitur ut in pluribus, defectus autem et malum ut in paucioribus. Si igitur a Deo institueretur numerus salvandorum, plures essent salvandi quam damnandi. Cuius contrarium ostenditur Matt. VII, ubi dicitur, *lata et spatiosa est via quae ducit ad perditionem, et multi sunt qui intrant per eam, angusta est porta, et arcta via, quae ducit ad vitam, et pauci sunt qui inveniunt eam*. Non ergo est praeordinatus a Deo numerus salvandorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0131","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0132","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de correptione et gratia, *certus est praedestinatorum numerus, qui neque augeri potest, neque minui*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0133","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod numerus praedestinatorum est certus. Sed quidam dixerunt eum esse certum formaliter, sed non materialiter, ut puta si diceremus certum esse quod centum vel mille salventur, non autem quod hi vel illi. Sed hoc tollit certitudinem praedestinationis, de qua iam diximus. Et ideo oportet dicere quod numerus praedestinatorum sit certus Deo non solum formaliter, sed etiam materialiter. Sed advertendum est quod numerus praedestinatorum certus Deo dicitur, non solum ratione cognitionis, quia scilicet scit quot sunt salvandi (sic enim Deo certus est etiam numerus guttarum pluviae, et arenae maris); sed ratione electionis et definitionis cuiusdam. Ad cuius evidentiam, est sciendum quod omne agens intendit facere aliquid finitum, ut ex supradictis de infinito apparet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0135","text":"Quicumque autem intendit aliquam determinatam mensuram in suo effectu, excogitat aliquem numerum in partibus essentialibus eius, quae per se requiruntur ad perfectionem totius. Non enim per se eligit aliquem numerum in his quae non principaliter requiruntur, sed solum propter aliud, sed in tanto numero accipit huiusmodi, inquantum sunt necessaria propter aliud. Sicut aedificator excogitat determinatam mensuram domus, et etiam determinatum numerum mansionum quas vult facere in domo, et determinatum numerum mensurarum parietis vel tecti, non autem eligit determinatum numerum lapidum, sed accipit tot, quot sufficiunt ad explendam tantam mensuram parietis. Sic igitur considerandum est in Deo, respectu totius universitatis quae est eius effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0136","text":"Praeordinavit enim in qua mensura deberet esse totum universum, et quis numerus esset conveniens essentialibus partibus universi, quae scilicet habent aliquo modo ordinem ad perpetuitatem; quot scilicet sphaerae, quot stellae, quot elementa, quot species rerum. Individua vero corruptibilia non ordinantur ad bonum universi quasi principaliter, sed quasi secundario, inquantum in eis salvatur bonum speciei. Unde, licet Deus sciat numerum omnium individuorum, non tamen numerus vel boum vel culicum, vel aliorum huiusmodi, est per se praeordinatus a Deo, sed tot ex huiusmodi divina providentia produxit, quot sufficiunt ad specierum conservationem. Inter omnes autem creaturas, principalius ordinantur ad bonum universi creaturae rationales, quae, inquantum huiusmodi, incorruptibiles sunt; et potissime illae quae beatitudinem consequuntur, quae immediatius attingunt ultimum finem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137","text":"Unde certus est Deo numerus praedestinatorum, non solum per modum cognitionis, sed etiam per modum cuiusdam principalis praefinitionis. Non sic autem omnino est de numero reproborum; qui videntur esse praeordinati a Deo in bonum electorum, quibus omnia cooperantur in bonum. De numero autem omnium praedestinatorum hominum, quis sit, dicunt quidam quod tot ex hominibus salvabuntur, quot Angeli ceciderunt. Quidam vero, quod tot salvabuntur, quot Angeli remanserunt. Quidam vero, quod tot ex hominibus salvabuntur, quot Angeli ceciderunt, et insuper tot, quot fuerunt Angeli creati. Sed melius dicitur quod *soli Deo est cognitus numerus electorum in superna felicitate locandus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0138","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0139","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum illud Deuteronomii est intelligendum de illis qui sunt praenotati a Deo respectu praesentis iustitiae. Horum enim numerus et augetur et minuitur, et non numerus praedestinatorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0140","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0141","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio quantitatis alicuius partis, accipienda est ex proportione illius partis ad totum. Sic enim est apud Deum ratio quare tot stellas fecerit, vel tot rerum species, et quare tot praedestinavit, ex proportione partium principalium ad bonum universi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0142","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0143","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod bonum proportionatum communi statui naturae, accidit ut in pluribus; et defectus ab hoc bono, ut in paucioribus. Sed bonum quod excedit communem statum naturae, invenitur ut in paucioribus; et defectus ab hoc bono, ut in pluribus. Sicut patet quod plures homines sunt qui habent sufficientem scientiam ad regimen vitae suae, pauciores autem qui hac scientia carent, qui moriones vel stulti dicuntur, sed paucissimi sunt, respectu aliorum, qui attingunt ad habendam profundam scientiam intelligibilium rerum. Cum igitur beatitudo aeterna, in visione Dei consistens, excedat communem statum naturae, et praecipue secundum quod est gratia destituta per corruptionem originalis peccati, pauciores sunt qui salvantur. Et in hoc etiam maxime misericordia Dei apparet, quod aliquos in illam salutem erigit, a qua plurimi deficiunt secundum communem cursum et inclinationem naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0144","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0145","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0146","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod praedestinatio non possit iuvari precibus sanctorum. Nullum enim aeternum praeceditur ab aliquo temporali, et per consequens non potest temporale iuvare ad hoc quod aliquod aeternum sit. Sed praedestinatio est aeterna. Cum igitur preces sanctorum sint temporales, non possunt iuvare ad hoc quod aliquis praedestinetur. Non ergo praedestinatio iuvatur precibus sanctorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0147","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0148","text":"Praeterea, sicut nihil indiget consilio nisi propter defectum cognitionis, ita nihil indiget auxilio nisi propter defectum virtutis. Sed neutrum horum competit Deo praedestinanti, unde dicitur Rom. XI, *quis adiuvit spiritum domini? Aut quis consiliarius eius fuit?* Ergo praedestinatio non iuvatur precibus sanctorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0149","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0150","text":"Praeterea, eiusdem est adiuvari et impediri. Sed praedestinatio non potest aliquo impediri. Ergo non potest aliquo iuvari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0151","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0152","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Genes. XXV, quod *Isaac rogavit Deum pro Rebecca uxore sua, et dedit conceptum Rebeccae*. Ex illo autem conceptu natus est Iacob, qui praedestinatus fuit. Non autem fuisset impleta praedestinatio, si natus non fuisset. Ergo praedestinatio iuvatur precibus sanctorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0153","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hanc quaestionem diversi errores fuerunt. Quidam enim, attendentes certitudinem divinae praedestinationis, dixerunt superfluas esse orationes, vel quidquid aliud fiat ad salutem aeternam consequendam, quia his factis vel non factis, praedestinati consequuntur, reprobati non consequuntur. Sed contra hoc sunt omnes admonitiones sacrae Scripturae, exhortantes ad orationem, et ad alia bona opera. Alii vero dixerunt quod per orationes mutatur divina praedestinatio. Et haec dicitur fuisse opinio Aegyptiorum, qui ponebant ordinationem divinam, quam fatum appellabant, aliquibus sacrificiis et orationibus impediri posse. Sed contra hoc etiam est auctoritas sacrae Scripturae. Dicitur enim I Reg. XV, porro *triumphator in Israel non parcet, neque poenitudine flectetur*. Et Rom. XI dicitur quod *sine poenitentia sunt dona Dei et vocatio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","text":"Et ideo aliter dicendum, quod in praedestinatione duo sunt consideranda, scilicet ipsa praeordinatio divina, et effectus eius. Quantum igitur ad primum, nullo modo praedestinatio iuvatur precibus sanctorum, non enim precibus sanctorum fit, quod aliquis praedestinetur a Deo. Quantum vero ad secundum, dicitur praedestinatio iuvari precibus sanctorum, et aliis bonis operibus, quia providentia, cuius praedestinatio est pars, non subtrahit causas secundas, sed sic providet effectus, ut etiam ordo causarum secundarum subiaceat providentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","text":"Sicut igitur sic providentur naturales effectus, ut etiam causae naturales ad illos naturales effectus ordinentur, sine quibus illi effectus non provenirent; ita praedestinatur a Deo salus alicuius, ut etiam sub ordine praedestinationis cadat quidquid hominem promovet in salutem, vel orationes propriae, vel aliorum, vel alia bona, vel quidquid huiusmodi, sine quibus aliquis salutem non consequitur. Unde praedestinatis conandum est ad bene operandum et orandum, quia per huiusmodi praedestinationis effectus certitudinaliter impletur. Propter quod dicitur II Petr. I, *satagite, ut per bona opera certam vestram vocationem et electionem faciatis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0157","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0158","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa ostendit quod praedestinatio non iuvatur precibus sanctorum, quantum ad ipsam praeordinationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0159","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0160","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliquis dicitur adiuvari per alium, dupliciter. Uno modo, inquantum ab eo accipit virtutem, et sic adiuvari infirmi est, unde Deo non competit. Et sic intelligitur illud, *quis adiuvit spiritum domini?* Alio modo dicitur quis adiuvari per aliquem, per quem exequitur suam operationem, sicut dominus per ministrum. Et hoc modo Deus adiuvatur per nos, inquantum exequimur suam ordinationem, secundum illud I ad Cor. III, *Dei enim adiutores sumus*. Neque hoc est propter defectum divinae virtutis, sed quia utitur causis mediis, ut ordinis pulchritudo servetur in rebus, et ut etiam creaturis dignitatem causalitatis communicet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0161","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0162","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod secundae causae non possunt egredi ordinem causae primae universalis, ut supra dictum est; sed ipsum exequuntur. Et ideo praedestinatio per creaturas potest adiuvari, sed non impediri.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3f0d64edf4999deb6f7f12f8c7b91d385fc3b3297c5d5c33dad35e1acade2903","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140500192031Z_a60b3fe9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 23","source_sha256":"3f0d64edf4999deb6f7f12f8c7b91d385fc3b3297c5d5c33dad35e1acade2903","sha256":"9a7b44b08537e41fd8c88e04b679fd43e39016892cc0a3977c1753da3ca01e39","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:05:00.192056+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0002","text":"[I q. 23 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","text":"After the consideration of divine providence, we must treat of predestination and of the book of life. And concerning predestination, eight questions are asked. First, whether predestination befits God. Second, what predestination is, and whether it posits something in the predestined. Third, whether the reprobation of some human beings belongs to God. Fourth, concerning the comparison of predestination to election, namely, whether the predestined are chosen. Fifth, whether merits are a cause or reason of predestination, or reprobation, or election. Sixth, concerning the certainty of predestination, namely, whether the predestined are infallibly saved. Seventh, whether the number of the predestined is certain. Eighth, whether predestination can be aided by the prayers of the saints.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0005","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that human beings are not predestined by God. For Damascene says, in Book II, *one must know that God indeed foreknows all things, but does not predetermine all things. For He foreknows the things that are in our power; but He does not predetermine them.* But human merits and demerits are in our power, insofar as we are masters of our acts through free choice. Therefore, those things pertaining to merit or demerit are not predestined by God. And thus human predestination is removed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0007","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0008","text":"Moreover, all creatures are ordered to their ends by divine providence, as was said above. But other creatures are not said to be predestined by God. Therefore neither are human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0009","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0010","text":"Moreover, angels are capable of beatitude, just as human beings are. But it does not seem fitting for angels to be predestined, since there was never misery in them; whereas predestination is a purpose of showing mercy, as Augustine says. Therefore human beings are not predestined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0011","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0012","text":"Moreover, benefits conferred by God upon human beings are revealed, through the Holy Spirit, to holy men, according to that saying of the Apostle, I Cor. II: *But we have received not the spirit of this world, but the Spirit who is from God, that we may know the things given to us by God.* Therefore, if human beings were predestined by God, since predestination is a benefit of God, their own predestination would be known to the predestined. But this is plainly false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0013","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0014","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Rom. VIII: *Those whom He predestined, these He also called.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0015","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","text":"I answer that it is fitting for God to predestine human beings. For all things are subject to divine providence, as was shown above. Now it belongs to providence to order things to an end, as was said. But the end to which created things are ordered by God is twofold. One exceeds the proportion and capacity of created nature, and this end is eternal life, which consists in the divine vision, which is above the nature of every creature, as was held above. But the other end is proportionate to created nature, namely, that which a created thing can attain according to the power of its nature. Now that to which something cannot arrive by the power of its nature must be conveyed to it by another, just as an arrow is sent by the archer to the target. Hence, properly speaking, the rational creature, which is capable of eternal life, is led into it as though sent by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","text":"The intelligible account of this sending indeed pre-exists in God; just as there is in Him the intelligible account of the ordering of all things to an end, which we called providence. But the intelligible account of something to be made, existing in the mind of the agent, is a certain pre-existence in him of the thing to be made. Hence the intelligible account of the aforesaid sending of the rational creature to the end of eternal life is called predestination, for to destine is to send. And thus it is clear that predestination, as regards its objects, is a certain part of providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0018","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Damascene calls predetermination the imposition of necessity, as is found in natural things, which are predetermined to one thing. This is clear from what he adds: *for He neither wills wickedness nor compels virtue.* Hence predestination is not excluded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0020","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that irrational creatures are not capable of that end which exceeds the capacity of human nature. Hence they are not properly said to be predestined, although predestination is sometimes improperly named with respect to any other end whatsoever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0022","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that to be predestined belongs to angels just as to human beings, although they were never miserable. For motion does not receive its species from the terminus from which, but from the terminus to which; for, as regards the intelligible account of whitening, it makes no difference whether the one whitened was black, pale, or red. And similarly, as regards the intelligible account of predestination, it makes no difference whether someone is predestined to eternal life from a state of misery or not. Although it may be said that every bestowal of a good exceeding what is due to the one upon whom it is bestowed pertains to mercy, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0024","text":"[I q. 23 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, even if to some their predestination is revealed by a special privilege, it is nevertheless not fitting that it be revealed to all, because thus those who are not predestined would despair; and security in the predestined would produce negligence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0027","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0028","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that predestination posits something in the predestined. For every action of itself entails a passion. Therefore, if predestination is an action in God, predestination must be a passion in the predestined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0029","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0030","text":"Moreover, Origen says, on the words of Rom. I, *who was predestined, etc., predestination is of that which is not, but destination is of that which is*. But Augustine says, in the book On the Predestination of the Saints, *what is predestination but the destination of someone?* Therefore predestination is only of something existing. And thus it posits something in the predestined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0031","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0032","text":"Moreover, preparation is something in that which is prepared. But predestination is the preparation of God's benefits, as Augustine says in the book On the Predestination of the Saints. Therefore predestination is something in the predestined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0033","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0034","text":"Moreover, the temporal is not placed in the definition of the eternal. But grace, which is something temporal, is placed in the definition of predestination, for predestination is said to be *the preparation of grace in the present, and of glory in the future*. Therefore predestination is not something eternal. And thus it must not be in God, but in the predestined, for whatever is in God is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0035","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0036","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says that predestination is the foreknowledge of God's benefits. But foreknowledge is not in the things foreknown, but in the one who foreknows. Therefore neither is predestination in the predestined, but in the one predestining.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0037","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","text":"I answer that it must be said that predestination is not something in the predestined, but only in the one predestining. For it has been said that predestination is a certain part of providence; but providence is not in the things provided for, but is a certain plan in the intellect of the provider, as was said above. But the execution of providence, which is called governance, is passively in the governed, but actively in the governor. Hence it is manifest that predestination is a certain plan of the ordering of some persons to eternal salvation, existing in the divine mind. But the execution of this order is passively in the predestined, but actively in God. Now the execution of predestination is calling and glorification, according to that saying of the Apostle, Rom. VIII: *those whom he predestined, these he also called; and those whom he called, these he also glorified*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0039","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that actions passing into external matter of themselves entail a passion, as heating and cutting do; but not actions remaining in the agent, such as understanding and willing, as was said above. And predestination is such an action. Hence predestination does not posit anything in the predestined. But its execution, which passes into external things, posits some effect in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0041","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0042","text":"To the second, it must be said that destination is sometimes taken for the actual sending of something to some terminus, and thus destination is only of that which exists. In another way, destination is taken for a sending that someone conceives in the mind, insofar as we are said to destine what we firmly propose in the mind; and in this second way it is said in II Maccabees, chap. VI: *Eleazar resolved not to admit unlawful things for love of life*. And thus destination can be of that which is not. Nevertheless, predestination, by reason of the precedence that it implies, can be of that which is not, however destination may be taken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0043","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0044","text":"To the third, it must be said that preparation is twofold. One is of the patient, so that it may undergo, and this preparation is in that which is prepared. Another is of the agent, so that it may act, and this is in the agent. And predestination is such a preparation, insofar as an agent through intellect is said to prepare itself for acting, inasmuch as it preconceives the plan of the work to be done. And thus God from eternity prepared by predestining, conceiving the plan of the ordering of some persons to salvation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0045","text":"[I q. 23 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0046","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that grace is not placed in the definition of predestination as though it were something existing of its essence, but insofar as predestination implies a relation to grace, as a cause to an effect, and an act to an object. Hence it does not follow that predestination is something temporal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0047","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0048","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0049","text":"We proceed thus to the third point. It seems that God reproves no man. For no one reproves one whom he loves. But God loves every man, according to that saying in Wisdom XI: *you love all things that are, and hate none of those things which you have made*. Therefore God reproves no man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0050","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0051","text":"Moreover, if God reproves any man, reprobation must stand in the same relation to the reprobate as predestination does to the predestined. But predestination is the cause of the salvation of the predestined. Therefore reprobation will be the cause of the perdition of the reprobate. But this is false, for it is said in Hosea XIII: *your perdition, O Israel, is from yourself; your help is from me alone*. Therefore God reproves no man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0052","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0053","text":"Moreover, no one ought to be held accountable for what he cannot avoid. But if God reproves anyone, he cannot avoid perishing, for it is said in Ecclesiastes VII: *consider the works of God, that no one can correct him whom he has despised*. Therefore men would not be held accountable for perishing. But this is false. Therefore God reproves no man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0054","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0055","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Malachi I: *Jacob I loved, but Esau I hated*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0056","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","text":"I answer that it must be said that God reproves some. For it was said above that predestination is a part of providence. Now it belongs to providence to permit some defect in things subject to providence, as was said above. Hence, since through divine providence men are ordered to eternal life, it also belongs to divine providence to permit some to fall short of this end. And this is called reprobation. Thus, therefore, just as predestination is a part of providence with respect to those who are divinely ordered to eternal salvation, so reprobation is a part of providence with respect to those who fall away from this end. Hence reprobation does not signify foreknowledge alone, but adds something according to its intelligible account, as does providence also, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","text":"For just as predestination includes the will to confer grace and glory, so reprobation includes the will to permit someone to fall into fault, and to inflict the punishment of damnation on account of fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0059","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0060","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God loves all men, and indeed all creatures, insofar as he wills some good to all; yet he does not will every good to all. Therefore, insofar as he does not will for some that good which is eternal life, he is said to hate them, or to reprove them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0061","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","text":"To the second it must be said that reprobation and predestination stand differently in causing. For predestination is the cause both of that which is awaited in the future life by the predestined, namely glory, and of that which is received in the present, namely grace. Reprobation, however, is not the cause of that which is in the present, namely fault; but it is the cause of abandonment by God. Yet it is the cause of that which is rendered in the future, namely eternal punishment. But fault proceeds from the free choice of him who is reprobated and abandoned by grace. And in this respect the saying of the prophet is verified, namely: *your perdition, O Israel, is from yourself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0063","text":"[I q. 23 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0064","text":"To the third it must be said that God's reprobation takes nothing away from the power of the reprobate. Hence, when it is said that one who is reprobated cannot attain grace, this must not be understood according to absolute impossibility, but according to conditional impossibility, just as it was said above that the predestined person must be saved by a conditional necessity, which does not take away freedom of choice. Hence, although one who is reprobated by God cannot attain grace, nevertheless his falling into this sin or that occurs by his free choice. Hence it is deservedly imputed to him as fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0065","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0066","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0067","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth point. It seems that the predestined are not chosen by God. For Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that, just as the corporeal sun, without choosing, pours light into all bodies, so too does God pour forth his goodness. But the divine goodness is communicated especially to some through participation in grace and glory. Therefore God communicates grace and glory without choice. But this pertains to predestination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0068","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0069","text":"Moreover, choice concerns things that exist. But predestination is from eternity, even of things that do not exist. Therefore some are predestined without choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0070","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0071","text":"Moreover, choice implies a certain distinction. *But God wills all men to be saved*, as is said in I Timothy II. Therefore predestination, which preordains men to salvation, is without choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0072","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0073","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Eph. I, *he chose us in him before the foundation of the world*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0074","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0075","text":"I answer that it must be said that predestination, according to its intelligible account, presupposes election; and election, love. The reason for this is that predestination, as has been said, is a part of providence. But providence, like prudence, is a principle existing in the intellect, commanding the ordering of certain things to an end, as was said above. Now nothing is commanded to be ordered to an end unless there pre-exists a will of the end. Hence the predestination of some to eternal salvation presupposes, according to its intelligible account, that God wills their salvation. To this pertain election and love. Love, indeed, insofar as he wills for them this good of eternal salvation, for to love is to will some good for someone, as was said above. Election, however, insofar as he wills this good for some in preference to others, since he reprobates some, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","text":"Yet election and love are ordered differently in us and in God, because in us the will, by loving, does not cause goodness; rather, from pre-existing goodness we are stirred to love. And therefore we choose someone whom we may love, and thus election precedes love in us. But in God it is the reverse. For his will, by which in loving he wills good to someone, is the cause why that good is had by him in preference to others. And thus it is clear that love is presupposed to election, according to intelligible account; and election to predestination. Hence all the predestined are chosen and loved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0077","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0078","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, if the communication of divine goodness is considered in general, he communicates his goodness without election, namely insofar as there is nothing that does not participate in something of his goodness, as was said above. But if the communication of this or that good is considered, he does not bestow it without election, because he gives certain goods to some which he does not give to others. And thus election is to be considered in the bestowal of grace and glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0079","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0080","text":"To the second it must be said that, when the will of one choosing is prompted to choose by a good pre-existing in the thing, then it is necessary that election concern things that are; as occurs in our election. But in God it is otherwise, as has been said. And therefore, as Augustine says, *those who are not are chosen by God, yet he who chooses does not err*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0081","text":"[I q. 23 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0082","text":"To the third it must be said that, as was said above, God wills all men to be saved antecedently, which is not to will simply, but in a certain respect; but not consequently, which is to will simply.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0083","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0084","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0085","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that foreknowledge of merits is the cause of predestination. For the Apostle says, Rom. VIII, *those whom he foreknew, he also predestined*. And the Gloss of Ambrose, on that passage of Rom. IX, *I will have mercy on whom I will have mercy*, etc., says, *I shall give mercy to him whom I foreknow will return to me with his whole heart*. Therefore it seems that foreknowledge of merits is the cause of predestination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0086","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0087","text":"Further, divine predestination includes the divine will, which cannot be irrational, since predestination is a purpose of showing mercy, as Augustine says. But there can be no other reason for predestination than foreknowledge of merits. Therefore foreknowledge of merits is the cause or reason of predestination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0088","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0089","text":"Further, *there is no injustice with God*, as is said in Rom. IX. But it seems unjust that unequal things should be given to equals. Now all human beings are equal both according to nature and according to original sin; but inequality among them is considered according to the merits or demerits of their own acts. Therefore God does not prepare unequal things for human beings, by predestining and reprobating, except on account of foreknowledge of differing merits.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0090","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0091","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, in Titus III, *not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us*. But just as he saved us, so too he predestined us to be saved. Therefore foreknowledge of merits is not the cause or reason of predestination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0092","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0093","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since predestination includes will, as was said above, the reason for predestination is to be sought in the same way as the reason for the divine will is sought. Now it was said above that no cause of the divine will can be assigned on the part of the act of willing; but a reason can be assigned on the part of the things willed, namely insofar as God wills one thing to be for the sake of another. Therefore no one has been of such unsound mind as to say that merits are the cause of divine predestination on the part of the act of predestining. But the question turns on this: whether, on the part of the effect, predestination has any cause. And this is to ask whether God preordained himself to give the effect of predestination to someone on account of some merits. There were therefore some who said that the effect of predestination is preordained to someone on account of merits pre-existing in another life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0094","text":"And this was the position of Origen, who held that human souls were created from the beginning and, according to the diversity of their works, obtained diverse states in this world when united to bodies. But the Apostle excludes this opinion, Rom. IX, saying, *when they had not yet been born, nor had done anything good or evil, not by works, but by him who calls, it was said that the elder shall serve the younger*. There were therefore others who said that merits pre-existing in this life are the reason and cause of the effect of predestination. For the Pelagians held that the beginning of doing good is from us, but its completion from God. And thus it comes about that the effect of predestination is given to one person and not to another, because one made a beginning by preparing himself, and not the other. But against this is what the Apostle says, II Cor. III, that *we are not sufficient to think anything from ourselves, as though from ourselves*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0095","text":"But no prior principle can be found than thought. Hence it cannot be said that there exists in us any beginning that is the reason for the effect of predestination. Hence there were others who said that merits following the effect of predestination are the reason for predestination, so that it may be understood that God therefore gives grace to someone, and preordained himself to give it to him, because he foreknew that he would use grace well; just as if a king were to give a horse to a soldier whom he knows will use it well. But these seem to have distinguished between what is from grace and what is from free choice, as though the same thing could not be from both. But it is manifest that what is of grace is an effect of predestination, and this cannot be posited as the reason for predestination, since it is included under predestination. If, therefore, anything else on our part is the reason for predestination, this will be outside the effect of predestination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","text":"But what is from free choice is not distinct from what is from predestination; just as what is from a secondary cause is not distinct from what is from the first cause, for divine providence produces effects through the operations of secondary causes, as was said above. Hence also what is through free choice is from predestination. Therefore it must be said that we can consider the effect of predestination in two ways. In one way, particularly. And thus nothing prevents one effect of predestination from being the cause and reason of another: the later, indeed, of the earlier according to the account of final causality; but the earlier of the later according to the account of meritorious causality, which is reduced to the disposition of matter. As if we were to say that God preordained himself to give someone glory on account of merits; and that he preordained himself to give someone grace, in order that he might merit glory. In another way, the effect of predestination can be considered in general.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","text":"And thus it is impossible that the whole effect of predestination in general should have any cause on our part. For whatever is in man ordering him toward salvation is wholly included under the effect of predestination, even the very preparation for grace; for this does not come about except through divine assistance, according to that saying in the last chapter of Lamentations, *Turn us, O Lord, to thee, and we shall be turned*. Yet predestination has, in this manner, on the part of its effect, as its reason, the divine goodness, to which the whole effect of predestination is ordered as to an end, and from which it proceeds as from a first moving principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0098","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0099","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the foreknown use of grace is not the reason for the bestowal of grace, except under the aspect of final cause, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0100","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0101","text":"To the second it must be said that predestination has as its reason, on the part of the effect in general, the divine goodness itself. But in particular, one effect is the reason for another, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0102","text":"[I q. 23 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0103","text":"To the third it must be said that from the divine goodness itself a reason can be taken for the predestination of some and the reprobation of others. For God is said to have made all things for the sake of his goodness, so that the divine goodness may be represented in things. But it is necessary that the divine goodness, which in itself is one and simple, be represented in things in manifold ways, because created things cannot attain to the divine simplicity. And hence, for the completion of the universe, diverse grades of things are required, some of which hold a high and some a lowest place in the universe. And in order that the manifoldness of grades may be preserved among things, God permits certain evils to occur, lest many goods be impeded, as was said above. Thus, therefore, let us consider the whole human race as the whole universe of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","text":"Therefore God willed, among men, with respect to some whom he predestines, to represent his goodness according to the mode of mercy, by sparing them; and with respect to some whom he reproves, according to the mode of justice, by punishing them. And this is the reason why God chooses some and reproves others. And the Apostle assigns this cause, in Romans IX, saying: God, willing to show wrath (that is, the vengeance of justice), and to make known his power, endured (that is, permitted) *with much patience vessels of wrath fitted for destruction, that he might show the riches of his glory upon vessels of mercy, which he prepared unto glory*. And in II Timothy II he says, *but in a great house there are not only vessels of gold and silver, but also wooden and earthenware ones; and some indeed for honor, others for dishonor*. But why he chose these for glory and reproved those has no reason except the divine will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","text":"Hence Augustine says, commenting on John, *do not wish to judge why he draws this one and does not draw that one, if you do not wish to err*. Likewise, in natural things a reason can be assigned, since primary matter is wholly uniform in itself, why one part of it is under the form of fire and another under the form of earth, created by God in the beginning: namely, that there may be diversity of species in natural things. But why this part of matter is under this form and that under another depends upon the simple divine will. Just as it depends upon the simple will of the artisan that one stone is in this part of the wall and another in another part, although the rationale of the art requires that some be in this place and others in that. Yet there is not on this account injustice with God if he prepares unequal things for those who are not unequal. For this would be contrary to the character of justice if the effect of predestination were rendered as due and were not given by grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","text":"For in matters that are given by grace, someone can, according to his own will, give to whom he wills, more or less, provided that he deprives no one of what is due, without prejudice to justice. And this is what the master of the house says in Matthew XX: *Take what is yours and go. Is it not lawful for me to do what I will?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0107","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0108","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0109","text":"The sixth point is approached thus. It seems that predestination is not certain. For on that passage of Revelation III, *Hold fast what you have, lest another receive your crown*, Augustine says that *another will not receive it unless this one loses it*. Therefore a crown, which is an effect of predestination, can both be acquired and lost. Therefore predestination is not certain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0110","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0111","text":"Further, from a possibility having been posited, no impossibility follows. But it is possible for someone predestined, such as Peter, to sin and then be killed. But if this is posited, it follows that the effect of predestination is frustrated. Therefore this is not impossible. Therefore predestination is not certain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0112","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0113","text":"Further, whatever God was able to do, he is able to do. But he was able not to predestine the one whom he predestined. Therefore he can now not predestine him. Therefore predestination is not certain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0114","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0115","text":"But on the contrary, on that passage of Romans VIII, *whom he foreknew, he also predestined*, etc., the Gloss says: *Predestination is the foreknowledge and preparation of God's benefits, by which, most certainly, all who are freed are freed.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0116","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","text":"I answer that it must be said that predestination most certainly and infallibly attains its effect; yet it does not impose necessity, such that its effect should arise of necessity. For it was said above that predestination is a part of providence. But not all things subject to providence are necessary; rather, some happen contingently, according to the condition of the proximate causes which divine providence has ordered to such effects. And yet the order of providence is infallible, as was shown above. Thus, therefore, the order of predestination also is certain; and yet freedom of choice is not taken away, from which the effect of predestination proceeds contingently. For this purpose, too, those things must be considered which were said above concerning divine knowledge and divine will, which do not remove contingency from things, although they are most certain and infallible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0118","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0119","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that a crown is said to belong to someone in two ways. In one way, by divine predestination, and thus no one loses his own crown. In another way, by the merit of grace; for what we merit is in some way ours. And thus someone can lose his own crown through a subsequent mortal sin. But another receives that lost crown, insofar as he is substituted in that person's place. For God does not permit some to fall without raising up others, according to that saying of Job XXXIV: *He shall crush many and innumerable persons, and shall make others stand in their place.* Thus men were substituted in the place of the fallen angels, and Gentiles in the place of the Jews. But the one substituted into the state of grace also receives, in this respect, the crown of the one who falls: that he will rejoice in eternal life over the good deeds which another performed, in which each person will rejoice over the good deeds done both by himself and by others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0120","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0121","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although it is possible for one who is predestined to die in mortal sin, considered in himself, nevertheless this is impossible, granted (namely, as it is granted) that he is predestined. Hence it does not follow that predestination can fail.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0122","text":"[I q. 23 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","text":"To the third, it must be said that, since predestination includes the divine will, just as it was said above that for God to will something created is necessary on a supposition, on account of the immutability of the divine will, but not absolutely; so too must it be said here concerning predestination. Hence one need not say that God can fail to predestine one whom He has predestined, taking it in the composite sense; although, considered absolutely, God can predestine or not predestine. But the certainty of predestination is not thereby removed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0124","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0125","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0126","text":"The seventh is approached thus. It seems that the number of the predestined is not certain. For a number to which an addition can be made is not certain. But an addition can be made to the number of the predestined, as appears, for it is said in Deut. I: *may the Lord our God add many thousands to this number*; the Gloss says: *that is, [the number] determined with God, who knows who are His*. Therefore the number of the predestined is not certain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0127","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0128","text":"Moreover, no reason can be assigned why God should preordain men to salvation in this number rather than another. But nothing is disposed by God without reason. Therefore there is no certain number of those to be saved preordained by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0129","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0130","text":"Moreover, God's operation is more perfect than nature's operation. But in the works of nature, good is found in the greater number, whereas defect and evil are found in the lesser number. Therefore, if the number of those to be saved were established by God, more would be saved than damned. The contrary of this is shown in Matt. VII, where it is said: *broad and spacious is the way that leads to destruction, and many are those who enter through it; narrow is the gate and confined the way that leads to life, and few are those who find it*. Therefore the number of those to be saved is not preordained by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0131","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0132","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *On Correction and Grace*: *the number of the predestined is certain, which can neither be increased nor diminished*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0133","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","text":"I answer that it must be said that the number of the predestined is certain. But some have said that it is certain formally, but not materially; as if we were to say that it is certain that a hundred or a thousand will be saved, but not that these or those [will be saved]. But this removes the certainty of predestination, concerning which we have already spoken. And therefore it must be said that the number of the predestined is certain to God not only formally but also materially. But it should be noted that the number of the predestined is called certain to God not only by reason of knowledge, namely because He knows how many are to be saved (for in this way even the number of raindrops and of the sand of the sea is certain to God); but by reason of election and a certain determination. For the evidence of this, it must be known that every agent intends to make something finite, as is clear from what was said above concerning the infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0135","text":"Now whoever intends some determinate measure in his effect devises some number among its essential parts, which are required through themselves for the perfection of the whole. For he does not through himself choose some number among those things which are not principally required, but only on account of something else; rather, he takes such things in as great a number as is necessary on account of that other thing. Thus a builder devises a determinate measure of a house, and also a determinate number of rooms that he wishes to make in the house, and a determinate number of measures of wall or roof; but he does not choose a determinate number of stones, but takes as many as suffice to complete so great a measure of wall. Thus, therefore, one must consider [the matter] in God with respect to the whole universe, which is His effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0136","text":"For He preordained in what measure the whole universe ought to exist, and what number would be fitting for the essential parts of the universe, namely those which have in some way an ordering toward perpetuity: namely, how many spheres, how many stars, how many elements, how many species of things. But corruptible individuals are not ordered to the good of the universe as though principally, but as though secondarily, insofar as in them the good of the species is preserved. Hence, although God knows the number of all individuals, nevertheless the number either of oxen or gnats, or of other such things, is not through itself preordained by God; rather, divine providence has produced as many of such things as suffice for the preservation of the species. But among all creatures, rational creatures are ordered more principally to the good of the universe, since, as such, they are incorruptible; and most especially those which attain beatitude, which more immediately reach the ultimate end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137","text":"Hence the number of the predestined is certain to God, not only in the mode of knowledge, but also in the mode of a certain principal predefinition. But it is not altogether so concerning the number of the reprobate, who seem to be preordained by God for the good of the elect, for whom all things work together unto good. But concerning the number of all predestined human beings, some say that as many from among human beings will be saved as angels fell. Others, however, that as many will be saved as angels remained. Others, however, that as many from among human beings will be saved as angels fell, and in addition as many as there were angels created. But it is better said that *the number of the elect to be placed in heavenly happiness is known to God alone*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0138","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0139","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that word of Deuteronomy must be understood of those who are foreknown by God with respect to present justice. For their number both increases and decreases, but not the number of the predestined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0140","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0141","text":"To the second, it must be said that the reason for the quantity of any part must be taken from the proportion of that part to the whole. Thus there is in God a reason why He made so many stars, or so many species of things, and why He predestined so many, from the proportion of the principal parts to the good of the universe.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0142","text":"[I q. 23 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0143","text":"To the third, it must be said that the good proportioned to the common state of nature occurs in the greater number; and the defect from this good, in the lesser number. But the good which exceeds the common state of nature is found in the lesser number; and the defect from this good, in the greater number. Thus it is clear that more men have sufficient knowledge for the governance of their own life, while fewer lack this knowledge, who are called fools or idiots; but very few, in comparison with the others, attain to having profound knowledge of intelligible things. Therefore, since eternal beatitude, consisting in the vision of God, exceeds the common state of nature, and especially insofar as it is deprived of grace through the corruption of original sin, fewer are saved. And in this too the mercy of God appears most greatly: that He raises some to that salvation from which very many fall short according to the common course and inclination of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0144","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0145","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0146","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth article. It seems that predestination cannot be helped by the prayers of the saints. For nothing eternal is preceded by anything temporal, and consequently a temporal thing cannot help toward the existence of something eternal. But predestination is eternal. Therefore, since the prayers of the saints are temporal, they cannot help toward someone's being predestined. Therefore predestination is not helped by the prayers of the saints.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0147","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0148","text":"Further, just as nothing needs counsel except on account of a defect of knowledge, so nothing needs help except on account of a defect of power. But neither of these befits God who predestines; hence it is said in Rom. XI, *who has helped the Spirit of the Lord? Or who has been his counsellor?* Therefore predestination is not helped by the prayers of the saints.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0149","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0150","text":"Further, it belongs to the same thing to be helped and to be impeded. But predestination cannot be impeded by anything. Therefore it cannot be helped by anything.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0151","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0152","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. XXV that *Isaac prayed to God for Rebecca his wife, and Rebecca conceived*. But from that conception Jacob was born, who was predestined. Yet predestination would not have been fulfilled if he had not been born. Therefore predestination is helped by the prayers of the saints.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0153","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","text":"I answer that it must be said that there have been diverse errors concerning this question. For some, considering the certainty of divine predestination, said that prayers, or anything else done for the attainment of eternal salvation, are superfluous, because, whether these things are done or not done, the predestined attain it and the reprobate do not attain it. But against this are all the admonitions of Sacred Scripture, exhorting to prayer and to other good works. Others, however, said that divine predestination is changed through prayers. And this is said to have been the opinion of the Egyptians, who held that the divine ordering, which they called fate, could be impeded by certain sacrifices and prayers. But against this too is the authority of Sacred Scripture. For it is said in I Kings XV: indeed, *the triumpher in Israel will not spare, nor will he be bent by repentance*. And in Rom. XI it is said that *the gifts and calling of God are without repentance*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","text":"And therefore it must be said otherwise: that in predestination two things are to be considered, namely, the divine preordination itself and its effect. As regards the first, predestination is in no way helped by the prayers of the saints; for it is not through the prayers of the saints that someone is predestined by God. But as regards the second, predestination is said to be helped by the prayers of the saints and by other good works, because providence, of which predestination is a part, does not exclude secondary causes, but so provides effects that even the order of secondary causes is subject to providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","text":"Therefore, just as natural effects are so provided that natural causes also are ordered to those natural effects, without which those effects would not ensue; so the salvation of someone is predestined by God, such that whatever promotes a human being toward salvation also falls under the order of predestination: either one's own prayers, or those of others, or other goods, or whatever is of this sort, without which someone does not attain salvation. Hence those predestined ought to strive to act well and to pray, because through such things the effect of predestination is fulfilled with certainty. On account of this it is said in II Peter I: *be diligent, that through good works you may make your calling and election certain*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0157","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0158","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that argument shows that predestination is not helped by the prayers of the saints with regard to the preordination itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0159","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0160","text":"To the second it must be said that someone is said to be helped by another in two ways. In one way, insofar as he receives power from him, and to be helped in this way belongs to the weak; hence it does not befit God. And this is the meaning of the statement, *who has helped the Spirit of the Lord?* In another way, someone is said to be helped by someone through whom he carries out his operation, as a master through a minister. And in this way God is helped by us, insofar as we carry out his ordering, according to I Cor. III: *for we are God's helpers*. Nor is this on account of a defect in the divine power, but because he uses intermediate causes, so that the beauty of order may be preserved in things, and also so that he may communicate to creatures the dignity of causality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0161","text":"[I q. 23 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0162","text":"To the third it must be said that secondary causes cannot go beyond the order of the universal first cause, as was said above; but they carry it out. And therefore predestination can be helped through creatures, but not impeded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0162"]}],"source_digest":"466b340841b8d96615db3111ce2c6099b810aa80a6537a2278886022990e8171","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 23"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 23","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16","title":"First Part · Question 24","order":25,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_16","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 24 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29463)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de libro vitae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, quid sit liber vitae. Secundo, cuius vitae sit liber. Tertio, utrum aliquis possit deleri de libro vitae.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29464)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod liber vitae non sit idem quod praedestinatio. Dicitur enim Eccli. XXIV, *haec omnia liber vitae*; Glossa, *idest novum et vetus testamentum*. Hoc autem non est praedestinatio. Ergo liber vitae non est idem quod praedestinatio.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29465)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus, in libro XX de Civ. Dei, ait quod liber vitae est quaedam vis divina, qua fiet *ut cuique opera sua bona vel mala in memoriam reducantur*. Sed vis divina non videtur pertinere ad praedestinationem, sed magis ad attributum potentiae. Ergo liber vitae non est idem quod praedestinatio.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29466)\n\nPraeterea, praedestinationi opponitur reprobatio. Si igitur liber vitae esset praedestinatio, inveniretur liber mortis, sicut liber vitae.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29467)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Glossa, super illud Psalmi LXVIII, *deleantur de libro viventium, liber iste est notitia Dei, qua praedestinavit ad vitam, quos praescivit*.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29468)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod liber vitae in Deo dicitur metaphorice, secundum similitudinem a rebus humanis acceptam. Est enim consuetum apud homines, quod illi qui ad aliquid eliguntur, conscribuntur in libro; utpote milites vel consiliarii, qui olim dicebantur patres conscripti. Patet autem ex praemissis quod omnes praedestinati eliguntur a Deo ad habendum vitam aeternam. Ipsa ergo praedestinatorum conscriptio dicitur liber vitae. Dicitur autem metaphorice aliquid conscriptum in intellectu alicuius, quod firmiter in memoria tenet, secundum illud Prov. III, *ne obliviscaris legis meae, et praecepta mea cor tuum custodiat*; et post pauca sequitur, *describe illa in tabulis cordis tui*. Nam et in libris materialibus aliquid conscribitur ad succurrendum memoriae. Unde ipsa Dei notitia, qua firmiter retinet se aliquos praedestinasse ad vitam aeternam, dicitur liber vitae. Nam sicut Scriptura libri est signum eorum quae fienda sunt ita Dei notitia est quoddam signum apud ipsum, eorum qui sunt perducendi ad vitam aeternam; secundum illud II Tim. II, *firmum fundamentum Dei stat, habens signaculum hoc, novit dominus qui sunt eius*.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29469)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod liber vitae potest dici dupliciter. Uno modo, conscriptio eorum qui sunt electi ad vitam, et sic loquimur nunc de libro vitae. Alio modo potest dici liber vitae, conscriptio eorum quae ducunt in vitam. Et hoc dupliciter. Vel sicut agendorum, et sic novum et vetus testamentum dicitur liber vitae. Vel sicut iam factorum, et sic illa vis divina, qua fiet ut cuilibet in memoriam reducantur facta sua, dicitur liber vitae. Sicut etiam liber militiae potest dici, vel in quo scribuntur electi ad militiam, vel in quo traditur ars militaris, vel in quo recitantur facta militum.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29470)\n\nUnde patet solutio ad secundum.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29471)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non est consuetum conscribi eos qui repudiantur, sed eos qui eliguntur. Unde reprobationi non respondet liber mortis, sicut praedestinationi liber vitae.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29472)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod secundum rationem differt liber vitae a praedestinatione. Importat enim notitiam praedestinationis, sicut etiam ex Glossa inducta apparet.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29473)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod liber vitae non sit solum respectu vitae gloriae praedestinatorum. Liber enim vitae est notitia vitae. Sed Deus per vitam suam cognoscit omnem aliam vitam. Ergo liber vitae praecipue dicitur respectu vitae divinae; et non solum respectu vitae praedestinatorum.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29474)\n\nPraeterea, sicut vita gloriae est a Deo, ita vita naturae. Si igitur notitia vitae gloriae dicitur liber vitae, etiam notitia vitae naturae dicetur liber vitae.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29475)\n\nPraeterea, aliqui eliguntur ad gratiam, qui non eliguntur ad vitam gloriae; ut patet per id quod dicitur Ioan. VI, *nonne duodecim vos elegi, et unus ex vobis Diabolus est?* Sed liber vitae est conscriptio electionis divinae, ut dictum est. Ergo etiam est respectu vitae gratiae.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29476)\n\nSed contra est quod liber vitae est notitia praedestinationis, ut dictum est. Sed praedestinatio non respicit vitam gratiae, nisi secundum quod ordinatur ad gloriam, non enim sunt praedestinati, qui habent gratiam et deficiunt a gloria. Liber igitur vitae non dicitur nisi respectu gloriae.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29477)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod liber vitae, ut dictum est, importat conscriptionem quandam sive notitiam electorum ad vitam. Eligitur autem aliquis ad id quod non competit sibi secundum suam naturam. Et iterum, id ad quod eligitur aliquis, habet rationem finis, non enim miles eligitur aut conscribitur ad hoc quod armetur, sed ad hoc quod pugnet; hoc enim est proprium officium ad quod militia ordinatur. Finis autem supra naturam existens, est vita gloriae, ut supra dictum est. Unde proprie liber vitae respicit vitam gloriae.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29478)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod vita divina, etiam prout est vita gloriosa, est Deo naturalis. Unde respectu eius non est electio, et per consequens neque liber vitae. Non enim dicimus quod aliquis homo eligatur ad habendum sensum, vel aliquid eorum quae consequuntur naturam.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29479)\n\nUnde per hoc etiam patet solutio ad secundum. Respectu enim vitae naturalis non est electio, neque liber vitae.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29480)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod vita gratiae non habet rationem finis, sed rationem eius quod est ad finem. Unde ad vitam gratiae non dicitur aliquis eligi, nisi inquantum vita gratiae ordinatur ad gloriam. Et propter hoc, illi qui habent gratiam et excidunt a gloria, non dicuntur esse electi simpliciter, sed secundum quid. Et similiter non dicuntur esse scripti simpliciter in libro vitae sed secundum quid; prout scilicet de eis in ordinatione et notitia divina existit, quod sint habituri aliquem ordinem ad vitam aeternam, secundum participationem gratiae.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29481)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullus deleatur de libro vitae. Dicit enim Augustinus, in XX de Civ. Dei, quod *praescientia Dei, quae non potest falli, liber vitae est*. Sed a praescientia Dei non potest aliquid subtrahi, similiter neque a praedestinatione. Ergo nec de libro vitae potest aliquis deleri.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29482)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid est in aliquo, est in eo per modum eius in quo est. Sed liber vitae est quid aeternum et immutabile. Ergo quidquid est in eo, est ibi non temporaliter, sed immobiliter et indelebiliter.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29483)\n\nPraeterea, deletio Scripturae opponitur. Sed aliquis non potest de novo scribi in libro vitae. Ergo neque inde deleri potest.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29484)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo LXVIII, *deleantur de libro viventium*.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29485)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod de libro vitae nullus potest deleri secundum rei veritatem, potest tamen aliquis deleri secundum opinionem hominum. Est enim consuetum in Scripturis ut aliquid dicatur fieri, quando innotescit. Et secundum hoc, aliqui dicuntur esse scripti in libro vitae, inquantum homines opinantur eos ibi scriptos, propter praesentem iustitiam quam in eis vident. Sed quando apparet, vel in hoc seculo vel in futuro, quod ab hac iustitia exciderunt, dicuntur inde deleri. Et sic etiam exponitur in Glossa deletio talis, super illud Psalmi LXVIII, *deleantur de libro viventium*. Sed quia non deleri de libro vitae ponitur inter praemia iustorum, secundum illud Apoc. III, *qui vicerit, sic vestietur vestimentis albis, et non delebo nomen eius de libro vitae*; quod autem sanctis repromittitur, non est solum in hominum opinione; potest dici quod deleri vel non deleri de libro vitae, non solum ad opinionem hominum referendum est, sed etiam quantum ad rem. Est enim liber vitae conscriptio ordinatorum in vitam aeternam. Ad quam ordinatur aliquis ex duobus, scilicet ex praedestinatione divina, et haec ordinatio nunquam deficit; et ex gratia. Quicumque enim gratiam habet, ex hoc ipso est dignus vita aeterna. Et haec ordinatio deficit interdum, quia aliqui ordinati sunt ex gratia habita ad habendum vitam aeternam, a qua tamen deficiunt per peccatum mortale. Illi igitur qui sunt ordinati ad habendum vitam aeternam ex praedestinatione divina, sunt simpliciter scripti in libro vitae, quia sunt ibi scripti ut habituri vitam aeternam in seipsa. Et isti nunquam delentur de libro vitae. Sed illi qui sunt ordinati ad habendum vitam aeternam, non ex praedestinatione divina, sed solum ex gratia, dicuntur esse scripti in libro vitae, non simpliciter, sed secundum quid, quia sunt ibi scripti ut habituri vitam aeternam, non in seipsa, sed in sua causa. Et tales possunt deleri de libro vitae, ut deletio non referatur ad notitiam Dei, quasi Deus aliquid praesciat, et postea nesciat; sed ad rem scitam, quia scilicet Deus scit aliquem prius ordinari in vitam aeternam, et postea non ordinari, cum deficit a gratia.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29486)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod deletio, ut dictum est, non refertur ad librum vitae ex parte praescientiae, quasi in Deo sit aliqua mutabilitas, sed ex parte praescitorum, quae sunt mutabilia.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29487)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet res in Deo sint immutabiliter, tamen in seipsis mutabiles sunt. Et ad hoc pertinet deletio libri vitae.\n\n[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29488)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod eo modo quo aliquis dicitur deleri de libro vitae, potest dici quod ibi scribatur de novo; vel secundum opinionem hominum, vel secundum quod de novo incipit habere ordinem ad vitam aeternam per gratiam. Quod etiam sub divina notitia comprehenditur, licet non de novo.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0002","text":"[I q. 24 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de libro vitae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, quid sit liber vitae. Secundo, cuius vitae sit liber. Tertio, utrum aliquis possit deleri de libro vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0005","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod liber vitae non sit idem quod praedestinatio. Dicitur enim Eccli. XXIV, *haec omnia liber vitae*; Glossa, *idest novum et vetus testamentum*. Hoc autem non est praedestinatio. Ergo liber vitae non est idem quod praedestinatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0007","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus, in libro XX de Civ. Dei, ait quod liber vitae est quaedam vis divina, qua fiet *ut cuique opera sua bona vel mala in memoriam reducantur*. Sed vis divina non videtur pertinere ad praedestinationem, sed magis ad attributum potentiae. Ergo liber vitae non est idem quod praedestinatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0009","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0010","text":"Praeterea, praedestinationi opponitur reprobatio. Si igitur liber vitae esset praedestinatio, inveniretur liber mortis, sicut liber vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0011","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Glossa, super illud Psalmi LXVIII, *deleantur de libro viventium, liber iste est notitia Dei, qua praedestinavit ad vitam, quos praescivit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0013","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod liber vitae in Deo dicitur metaphorice, secundum similitudinem a rebus humanis acceptam. Est enim consuetum apud homines, quod illi qui ad aliquid eliguntur, conscribuntur in libro; utpote milites vel consiliarii, qui olim dicebantur patres conscripti. Patet autem ex praemissis quod omnes praedestinati eliguntur a Deo ad habendum vitam aeternam. Ipsa ergo praedestinatorum conscriptio dicitur liber vitae. Dicitur autem metaphorice aliquid conscriptum in intellectu alicuius, quod firmiter in memoria tenet, secundum illud Prov. III, *ne obliviscaris legis meae, et praecepta mea cor tuum custodiat*; et post pauca sequitur, *describe illa in tabulis cordis tui*. Nam et in libris materialibus aliquid conscribitur ad succurrendum memoriae. Unde ipsa Dei notitia, qua firmiter retinet se aliquos praedestinasse ad vitam aeternam, dicitur liber vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0015","text":"Nam sicut Scriptura libri est signum eorum quae fienda sunt ita Dei notitia est quoddam signum apud ipsum, eorum qui sunt perducendi ad vitam aeternam; secundum illud II Tim. II, *firmum fundamentum Dei stat, habens signaculum hoc, novit dominus qui sunt eius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0016","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod liber vitae potest dici dupliciter. Uno modo, conscriptio eorum qui sunt electi ad vitam, et sic loquimur nunc de libro vitae. Alio modo potest dici liber vitae, conscriptio eorum quae ducunt in vitam. Et hoc dupliciter. Vel sicut agendorum, et sic novum et vetus testamentum dicitur liber vitae. Vel sicut iam factorum, et sic illa vis divina, qua fiet ut cuilibet in memoriam reducantur facta sua, dicitur liber vitae. Sicut etiam liber militiae potest dici, vel in quo scribuntur electi ad militiam, vel in quo traditur ars militaris, vel in quo recitantur facta militum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0018","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0019","text":"Unde patet solutio ad secundum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0020","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non est consuetum conscribi eos qui repudiantur, sed eos qui eliguntur. Unde reprobationi non respondet liber mortis, sicut praedestinationi liber vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0022","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod secundum rationem differt liber vitae a praedestinatione. Importat enim notitiam praedestinationis, sicut etiam ex Glossa inducta apparet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0025","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod liber vitae non sit solum respectu vitae gloriae praedestinatorum. Liber enim vitae est notitia vitae. Sed Deus per vitam suam cognoscit omnem aliam vitam. Ergo liber vitae praecipue dicitur respectu vitae divinae; et non solum respectu vitae praedestinatorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0027","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0028","text":"Praeterea, sicut vita gloriae est a Deo, ita vita naturae. Si igitur notitia vitae gloriae dicitur liber vitae, etiam notitia vitae naturae dicetur liber vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0029","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0030","text":"Praeterea, aliqui eliguntur ad gratiam, qui non eliguntur ad vitam gloriae; ut patet per id quod dicitur Ioan. VI, *nonne duodecim vos elegi, et unus ex vobis Diabolus est?* Sed liber vitae est conscriptio electionis divinae, ut dictum est. Ergo etiam est respectu vitae gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0031","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod liber vitae est notitia praedestinationis, ut dictum est. Sed praedestinatio non respicit vitam gratiae, nisi secundum quod ordinatur ad gloriam, non enim sunt praedestinati, qui habent gratiam et deficiunt a gloria. Liber igitur vitae non dicitur nisi respectu gloriae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0033","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod liber vitae, ut dictum est, importat conscriptionem quandam sive notitiam electorum ad vitam. Eligitur autem aliquis ad id quod non competit sibi secundum suam naturam. Et iterum, id ad quod eligitur aliquis, habet rationem finis, non enim miles eligitur aut conscribitur ad hoc quod armetur, sed ad hoc quod pugnet; hoc enim est proprium officium ad quod militia ordinatur. Finis autem supra naturam existens, est vita gloriae, ut supra dictum est. Unde proprie liber vitae respicit vitam gloriae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0035","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod vita divina, etiam prout est vita gloriosa, est Deo naturalis. Unde respectu eius non est electio, et per consequens neque liber vitae. Non enim dicimus quod aliquis homo eligatur ad habendum sensum, vel aliquid eorum quae consequuntur naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0037","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038","text":"Unde per hoc etiam patet solutio ad secundum. Respectu enim vitae naturalis non est electio, neque liber vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0039","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod vita gratiae non habet rationem finis, sed rationem eius quod est ad finem. Unde ad vitam gratiae non dicitur aliquis eligi, nisi inquantum vita gratiae ordinatur ad gloriam. Et propter hoc, illi qui habent gratiam et excidunt a gloria, non dicuntur esse electi simpliciter, sed secundum quid. Et similiter non dicuntur esse scripti simpliciter in libro vitae sed secundum quid; prout scilicet de eis in ordinatione et notitia divina existit, quod sint habituri aliquem ordinem ad vitam aeternam, secundum participationem gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0042","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullus deleatur de libro vitae. Dicit enim Augustinus, in XX de Civ. Dei, quod *praescientia Dei, quae non potest falli, liber vitae est*. Sed a praescientia Dei non potest aliquid subtrahi, similiter neque a praedestinatione. Ergo nec de libro vitae potest aliquis deleri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0044","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0045","text":"Praeterea, quidquid est in aliquo, est in eo per modum eius in quo est. Sed liber vitae est quid aeternum et immutabile. Ergo quidquid est in eo, est ibi non temporaliter, sed immobiliter et indelebiliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0046","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0047","text":"Praeterea, deletio Scripturae opponitur. Sed aliquis non potest de novo scribi in libro vitae. Ergo neque inde deleri potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0048","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo LXVIII, *deleantur de libro viventium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0050","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod de libro vitae nullus potest deleri secundum rei veritatem, potest tamen aliquis deleri secundum opinionem hominum. Est enim consuetum in Scripturis ut aliquid dicatur fieri, quando innotescit. Et secundum hoc, aliqui dicuntur esse scripti in libro vitae, inquantum homines opinantur eos ibi scriptos, propter praesentem iustitiam quam in eis vident. Sed quando apparet, vel in hoc seculo vel in futuro, quod ab hac iustitia exciderunt, dicuntur inde deleri. Et sic etiam exponitur in Glossa deletio talis, super illud Psalmi LXVIII, *deleantur de libro viventium*. Sed quia non deleri de libro vitae ponitur inter praemia iustorum, secundum illud Apoc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","text":"III, *qui vicerit, sic vestietur vestimentis albis, et non delebo nomen eius de libro vitae*; quod autem sanctis repromittitur, non est solum in hominum opinione; potest dici quod deleri vel non deleri de libro vitae, non solum ad opinionem hominum referendum est, sed etiam quantum ad rem. Est enim liber vitae conscriptio ordinatorum in vitam aeternam. Ad quam ordinatur aliquis ex duobus, scilicet ex praedestinatione divina, et haec ordinatio nunquam deficit; et ex gratia. Quicumque enim gratiam habet, ex hoc ipso est dignus vita aeterna. Et haec ordinatio deficit interdum, quia aliqui ordinati sunt ex gratia habita ad habendum vitam aeternam, a qua tamen deficiunt per peccatum mortale. Illi igitur qui sunt ordinati ad habendum vitam aeternam ex praedestinatione divina, sunt simpliciter scripti in libro vitae, quia sunt ibi scripti ut habituri vitam aeternam in seipsa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","text":"Et isti nunquam delentur de libro vitae. Sed illi qui sunt ordinati ad habendum vitam aeternam, non ex praedestinatione divina, sed solum ex gratia, dicuntur esse scripti in libro vitae, non simpliciter, sed secundum quid, quia sunt ibi scripti ut habituri vitam aeternam, non in seipsa, sed in sua causa. Et tales possunt deleri de libro vitae, ut deletio non referatur ad notitiam Dei, quasi Deus aliquid praesciat, et postea nesciat; sed ad rem scitam, quia scilicet Deus scit aliquem prius ordinari in vitam aeternam, et postea non ordinari, cum deficit a gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0054","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod deletio, ut dictum est, non refertur ad librum vitae ex parte praescientiae, quasi in Deo sit aliqua mutabilitas, sed ex parte praescitorum, quae sunt mutabilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0056","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet res in Deo sint immutabiliter, tamen in seipsis mutabiles sunt. Et ad hoc pertinet deletio libri vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0058","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0059","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod eo modo quo aliquis dicitur deleri de libro vitae, potest dici quod ibi scribatur de novo; vel secundum opinionem hominum, vel secundum quod de novo incipit habere ordinem ad vitam aeternam per gratiam. Quod etiam sub divina notitia comprehenditur, licet non de novo.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"345452f07b56f5114b278d1251044744e3920f546fbd998eb1123540894c078c","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140535195950Z_6404da2c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 24","source_sha256":"345452f07b56f5114b278d1251044744e3920f546fbd998eb1123540894c078c","sha256":"4bfb19b5f579b0b05a46d99106dcfeb09b32556635e48e6f1f173c333fdd5fff","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:05:35.195976+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0002","text":"[I q. 24 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0003","text":"Next, consideration must be given to the book of life. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, what the book of life is. Second, of whose life it is the book. Third, whether anyone can be erased from the book of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0005","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the book of life is not the same as predestination. For it is said in Ecclesiasticus XXIV, *all these things are the book of life*; the Gloss says, *that is, the New and Old Testament*. But this is not predestination. Therefore, the book of life is not the same as predestination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0007","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0008","text":"Moreover, Augustine, in Book XX of the City of God, says that the book of life is a certain divine power, through which it will come about *that each person's good or evil works are recalled to memory*. But divine power does not seem to pertain to predestination, but rather to the attribute of power. Therefore, the book of life is not the same as predestination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0009","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0010","text":"Moreover, reprobation is opposed to predestination. Therefore, if the book of life were predestination, there would be a book of death, just as there is a book of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0011","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the Gloss says, on that verse of Psalm LXVIII, *let them be erased from the book of the living*: “This book is the knowledge of God, by which He predestined to life those whom He foreknew.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0013","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that the book of life is spoken of in God metaphorically, according to a likeness taken from human things. For it is customary among men that those who are chosen for something are enrolled in a book, as soldiers or councillors, who of old were called enrolled fathers. Now it is clear from what has been said above that all the predestined are chosen by God to have eternal life. Therefore, the very enrollment of the predestined is called the book of life. Moreover, something is said metaphorically to be written in someone's intellect when he holds it firmly in memory, according to Proverbs III: *do not forget my law, and let your heart keep my commandments*; and a little later it follows: *write them on the tablets of your heart*. For something is also written in material books in order to aid memory. Hence God's very knowledge, by which He firmly retains that He has predestined some to eternal life, is called the book of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0015","text":"For just as the writing of a book is a sign of things to be done, so God's knowledge is a certain sign in Himself of those who are to be brought to eternal life; according to II Timothy II: *the firm foundation of God stands, having this seal: the Lord knows those who are his*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0016","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the book of life may be understood in two ways. In one way, as the enrollment of those who are chosen for life, and thus we are now speaking of the book of life. In another way, the book of life may be called the enrollment of those things that lead to life. And this in two ways. Either as of things to be done, and thus the New and Old Testament is called the book of life. Or as of things already done, and thus that divine power, by which it will come about that each person's deeds are recalled to memory, is called the book of life. Just as a book of warfare may also be called either that in which those chosen for military service are written, or that in which military art is handed down, or that in which the deeds of soldiers are recounted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0018","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0019","text":"Hence the solution to the second is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0020","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","text":"To the third it must be said that it is not customary for those who are rejected to be enrolled, but for those who are chosen. Hence a book of death does not correspond to reprobation as the book of life does to predestination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0022","text":"[I q. 24 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the book of life differs from predestination in account. For it signifies the knowledge of predestination, as is also clear from the Gloss cited.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0025","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the book of life is not only with respect to the life of glory of the predestined. For the book of life is knowledge of life. But God knows every other life through His own life. Therefore the book of life is said chiefly with respect to the divine life, and not only with respect to the life of the predestined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0027","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0028","text":"Further, just as the life of glory is from God, so too is the life of nature. Therefore, if knowledge of the life of glory is called the book of life, knowledge of the life of nature will likewise be called the book of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0029","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0030","text":"Further, some are chosen unto grace who are not chosen unto the life of glory, as is clear from what is said in John 6: *Have I not chosen you twelve, and one of you is a devil?* But the book of life is the enrolling of divine election, as has been said. Therefore it is also with respect to the life of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0031","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0032","text":"On the contrary, the book of life is knowledge of predestination, as has been said. But predestination does not regard the life of grace except insofar as it is ordered to glory; for those who have grace and fail of glory are not predestined. Therefore the book of life is spoken of only with respect to glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0033","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","text":"I answer that it must be said that the book of life, as has been said, signifies a certain enrolling or knowledge of those chosen unto life. Now someone is chosen for that which does not belong to him according to his nature. And again, that for which someone is chosen has the character of an end; for a soldier is not chosen or enrolled in order that he be armed, but in order that he fight, for this is the proper office to which military service is ordered. But the end that exists above nature is the life of glory, as was said above. Hence, properly speaking, the book of life concerns the life of glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0035","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the divine life, even insofar as it is glorious life, is natural to God. Hence, with respect to it there is no election, and consequently no book of life either. For we do not say that a man is chosen to have sensation, or any of those things that follow upon nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0037","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038","text":"Hence, from this the solution to the second is also clear. For with respect to natural life there is no election, nor a book of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0039","text":"[I q. 24 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that the life of grace does not have the character of an end, but the character of that which is for the end. Hence someone is not said to be chosen unto the life of grace except insofar as the life of grace is ordered to glory. And on this account, those who have grace and fall away from glory are not said to be chosen simply, but in a certain respect. Likewise, they are not said to be written simply in the book of life, but in a certain respect: namely, insofar as there exists concerning them in the divine ordering and knowledge that they are to have some ordering toward eternal life, according to participation in grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0042","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that no one is blotted out from the book of life. For Augustine says, in *The City of God*, XX, that *the foreknowledge of God, which cannot be deceived, is the book of life*. But nothing can be removed from God's foreknowledge, nor likewise from predestination. Therefore neither can anyone be blotted out from the book of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0044","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0045","text":"Further, whatever is in something is in it according to the mode of that in which it is. But the book of life is something eternal and immutable. Therefore whatever is in it is there not temporally, but immovably and indelibly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0046","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0047","text":"Further, erasure is opposed to writing. But someone cannot be newly written in the book of life. Therefore neither can he be blotted out from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0048","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0049","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Psalm LXVIII, *let them be blotted out of the book of the living*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0050","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","text":"I answer that it must be said that some say that no one can be blotted out of the book of life according to the truth of the matter, but someone can be blotted out according to the opinion of men. For it is customary in the Scriptures for something to be said to come to pass when it becomes known. And according to this, some are said to be written in the book of life insofar as men suppose them to be written there, on account of the present justice which they see in them. But when it appears, either in this age or in the future, that they have fallen away from this justice, they are said to be blotted out from it. And such a blotting out is also expounded in the Gloss, on that passage of Psalm LXVIII, *let them be blotted out of the book of the living*. But because not being blotted out of the book of life is placed among the rewards of the just, according to that passage of the Apocalypse,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","text":"III, *he who conquers shall thus be clothed in white garments, and I will not blot out his name from the book of life*; and what is promised to the saints is not only in the opinion of men; it can be said that being blotted out or not being blotted out of the book of life is to be referred not only to the opinion of men, but also to the reality. For the book of life is the inscription of those ordered to eternal life. Now someone is ordered to this in two ways: namely, by divine predestination, and this ordering never fails; and by grace. For whoever has grace is thereby worthy of eternal life. And this ordering sometimes fails, because some are ordered by the grace they possess to have eternal life, from which nevertheless they fall away through mortal sin. Therefore, those who are ordered to have eternal life by divine predestination are written simply in the book of life, because they are written there as those who will have eternal life in itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","text":"And these are never blotted out of the book of life. But those who are ordered to have eternal life, not by divine predestination but only by grace, are said to be written in the book of life, not simply, but in a certain respect, because they are written there as those who will have eternal life, not in itself, but in its cause. And such persons can be blotted out of the book of life, so that the blotting out is referred not to God's knowledge, as though God foreknew something and afterwards did not know it; but to the thing known, namely, because God knows someone first to be ordered to eternal life, and afterwards not to be so ordered, when he falls away from grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0054","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that blotting out, as has been stated, is not referred to the book of life on the part of foreknowledge, as though there were any changeability in God, but on the part of the things foreknown, which are changeable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0056","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although things are immutable in God, nevertheless they are changeable in themselves. And blotting out from the book of life pertains to this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0058","text":"[I q. 24 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0059","text":"To the third, it must be said that in the manner in which someone is said to be blotted out of the book of life, he can be said to be written there anew: either according to the opinion of men, or insofar as he begins anew to have an ordering to eternal life through grace. This too is comprehended under divine knowledge, though not anew.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0059"]}],"source_digest":"648d1b82a01f417afa82082a2e932f121f4657e081e59874440e82c7cf8be369","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 24"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 24","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17","title":"First Part · Question 25","order":26,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_17","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 25 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29489)\n\nPost considerationem divinae scientiae et voluntatis, et eorum quae ad hoc pertinent, restat considerandum de divina potentia. Et circa hoc quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum in Deo sit potentia. Secundo, utrum eius potentia sit infinita. Tertio, utrum sit omnipotens. Quarto, utrum possit facere quod ea quae sunt praeterita, non fuerint. Quinto, utrum Deus possit facere quae non facit, vel praetermittere quae facit. Sexto, utrum quae facit, possit facere meliora.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29490)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit potentia. Sicut enim prima materia se habet ad potentiam, ita Deus, qui est agens primum, se habet ad actum. Sed prima materia, secundum se considerata, est absque omni actu. Ergo agens primum, quod est Deus, est absque potentia.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29491)\n\nPraeterea, secundum philosophum, in IX Metaphys., qualibet potentia melior est eius actus, nam forma est melior quam materia, et actio quam potentia activa; est enim finis eius. Sed nihil est melius eo quod est in Deo, quia quidquid est in Deo, est Deus, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo nulla potentia est in Deo.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29492)\n\nPraeterea, potentia est principium operationis. Sed operatio divina est eius essentia, cum in Deo nullum sit accidens. Essentiae autem divinae non est aliquod principium. Ergo ratio potentiae Deo non convenit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29493)\n\nPraeterea, supra ostensum est quod scientia Dei et voluntas eius sunt causa rerum. Causa autem et principium idem sunt. Ergo non oportet in Deo assignare potentiam, sed solum scientiam et voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29494)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo LXXXVIII, *potens es, domine, et veritas tua in circuitu tuo*.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29495)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod duplex est potentia, scilicet passiva, quae nullo modo est in Deo; et activa, quam oportet in Deo summe ponere. Manifestum est enim quod unumquodque, secundum quod est actu et perfectum, secundum hoc est principium activum alicuius, patitur autem unumquodque, secundum quod est deficiens et imperfectum. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus est purus actus, et simpliciter et universaliter perfectus; neque in eo aliqua imperfectio locum habet. Unde sibi maxime competit esse principium activum, et nullo modo pati. Ratio autem activi principii convenit potentiae activae. Nam potentia activa est principium agendi in aliud, potentia vero passiva est principium patiendi ab alio, ut philosophus dicit, V Metaphys. Relinquitur ergo quod in Deo maxime sit potentia activa.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29496)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod potentia activa non dividitur contra actum, sed fundatur in eo, nam unumquodque agit secundum quod est actu. Potentia vero passiva dividitur contra actum, nam unumquodque patitur secundum quod est in potentia. Unde haec potentia excluditur a Deo, non autem activa.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29497)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quandocumque actus est aliud a potentia, oportet quod actus sit nobilior potentia. Sed actio Dei non est aliud ab eius potentia, sed utrumque est essentia divina, quia nec esse eius est aliud ab eius essentia. Unde non oportet quod aliquid sit nobilius quam potentia Dei.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29498)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod potentia in rebus creatis non solum est principium actionis, sed etiam effectus. Sic igitur in Deo salvatur ratio potentiae quantum ad hoc, quod est principium effectus, non autem quantum ad hoc, quod est principium actionis, quae est divina essentia. Nisi forte secundum modum intelligendi, prout divina essentia, quae in se simpliciter praehabet quidquid perfectionis est in rebus creatis, potest intelligi et sub ratione actionis, et sub ratione potentiae; sicut etiam intelligitur et sub ratione suppositi habentis naturam, et sub ratione naturae.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29499)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod potentia non ponitur in Deo ut aliquid differens a scientia et voluntate secundum rem, sed solum secundum rationem; inquantum scilicet potentia importat rationem principii exequentis id quod voluntas imperat, et ad quod scientia dirigit; quae tria Deo secundum idem conveniunt. Vel dicendum quod ipsa scientia vel voluntas divina, secundum quod est principium effectivum, habet rationem potentiae. Unde consideratio scientiae et voluntatis praecedit in Deo considerationem potentiae, sicut causa praecedit operationem et effectum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29500)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod potentia Dei non sit infinita. Omne enim infinitum est imperfectum, secundum philosophum, in III Physic. Sed potentia Dei non est imperfecta. Ergo non est infinita.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29501)\n\nPraeterea, omnis potentia manifestatur per effectum, alias frustra esset. Si igitur potentia Dei esset infinita, posset facere effectum infinitum. Quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29502)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus probat in VIII Physic., quod si potentia alicuius corporis esset infinita, moveret in instanti. Deus autem non movet in instanti, sed movet creaturam spiritualem *per tempus, creaturam vero corporalem per locum et tempus*, secundum Augustinum, VIII super Genesim ad litteram. Non ergo est eius potentia infinita.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29503)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Hilarius, VIII de Trin., quod Deus est immensae virtutis, vivens, potens. Omne autem immensum est infinitum. Ergo virtus divina est infinita.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29504)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, secundum hoc potentia activa invenitur in Deo, secundum quod ipse actu est. Esse autem eius est infinitum, inquantum non est limitatum per aliquid recipiens; ut patet per ea quae supra dicta sunt, cum de infinitate divinae essentiae ageretur. Unde necesse est quod activa potentia Dei sit infinita. In omnibus enim agentibus hoc invenitur, quod quanto aliquod agens perfectius habet formam qua agit, tanto est maior eius potentia in agendo. Sicut quanto est aliquid magis calidum, tanto habet maiorem potentiam ad calefaciendum, et haberet utique potentiam infinitam ad calefaciendum, si eius calor esset infinitus. Unde, cum ipsa essentia divina, per quam Deus agit, sit infinita, sicut supra ostensum est, sequitur quod eius potentia sit infinita.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29505)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de infinito quod est ex parte materiae non terminatae per formam; cuiusmodi est infinitum quod congruit quantitati. Sic autem non est infinita divina essentia, ut supra ostensum est; et per consequens nec eius potentia. Unde non sequitur quod sit imperfecta.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29506)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod potentia agentis univoci tota manifestatur in suo effectu, potentia enim generativa hominis nihil potest plus quam generare hominem. Sed potentia agentis non univoci non tota manifestatur in sui effectus productione, sicut potentia solis non tota manifestatur in productione alicuius animalis ex putrefactione generati. Manifestum est autem quod Deus non est agens univocum, nihil enim aliud potest cum eo convenire neque in specie, neque in genere, ut supra ostensum est. Unde relinquitur quod effectus eius semper est minor quam potentia eius. Non ergo oportet quod manifestetur infinita potentia Dei in hoc, quod producat effectum infinitum. Et tamen, etiam si nullum effectum produceret, non esset Dei potentia frustra. Quia frustra est quod ordinatur ad finem, quem non attingit, potentia autem Dei non ordinatur ad effectum sicut ad finem, sed magis ipsa est finis sui effectus.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29507)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod philosophus in VIII Physic., probat, quod si aliquod corpus haberet potentiam infinitam, quod moveret in non tempore. Et tamen ostendit, quod potentia motoris caeli est infinita, quia movere potest tempore infinito. Relinquitur ergo secundum eius intentionem, quod potentia infinita corporis si esset, moveret in non tempore, non autem potentia incorporei motoris. Cuius ratio est, quia corpus movens aliud corpus, est agens univocum. Unde oportet quod tota potentia agentis manifestetur in motu. Quia igitur quanto moventis corporis potentia est maior, tanto velocius movet, necesse est quod si fuerit infinita, moveat improportionabiliter citius, quod est movere in non tempore. Sed movens incorporeum est agens non univocum. Unde non oportet, quod tota virtus eius manifestetur in motu ita, quod moveat in non tempore. Et praesertim, quia movet secundum dispositionem suae voluntatis.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29508)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit omnipotens. Moveri enim et pati aliquid omnium est. Sed hoc Deus non potest, est enim immobilis, ut supra dictum est. Non igitur est omnipotens.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29509)\n\nPraeterea, peccare aliquid agere est. Sed Deus non potest peccare, neque seipsum negare, ut dicitur II Tim. II. Ergo Deus non est omnipotens.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29510)\n\nPraeterea, de Deo dicitur quod *omnipotentiam suam parcendo maxime et miserando manifestat*. Ultimum igitur quod potest divina potentia, est parcere et misereri. Aliquid autem est multo maius quam parcere et misereri; sicut creare alium mundum, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Ergo Deus non est omnipotens.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29511)\n\nPraeterea, super illud I Cor. I, *stultam fecit Deus sapientiam huius mundi*, dicit Glossa, *sapientiam huius mundi fecit Deus stultam, ostendendo possibile, quod illa impossibile iudicabat*. Unde videtur quod non sit aliquid iudicandum possibile vel impossibile secundum inferiores causas, prout sapientia huius mundi iudicat; sed secundum potentiam divinam. Si igitur Deus sit omnipotens, omnia erunt possibilia. Nihil ergo impossibile. Sublato autem impossibili, tollitur necessarium, nam quod necesse est esse, impossibile est non esse. Nihil ergo erit necessarium in rebus, si Deus est omnipotens. Hoc autem est impossibile. Ergo Deus non est omnipotens.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29512)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Luc. I, *non erit impossibile apud Deum omne verbum*.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29513)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod communiter confitentur omnes Deum esse omnipotentem. Sed rationem omnipotentiae assignare videtur difficile. Dubium enim potest esse quid comprehendatur sub ista distributione, cum dicitur omnia posse Deum. Sed si quis recte consideret, cum potentia dicatur ad possibilia, cum Deus omnia posse dicitur, nihil rectius intelligitur quam quod possit omnia possibilia, et ob hoc omnipotens dicatur. Possibile autem dicitur dupliciter, secundum philosophum, in V Metaphys. Uno modo, per respectum ad aliquam potentiam, sicut quod subditur humanae potentiae, dicitur esse possibile homini. Non autem potest dici quod Deus dicatur omnipotens, quia potest omnia quae sunt possibilia naturae creatae, quia divina potentia in plura extenditur. Si autem dicatur quod Deus sit omnipotens, quia potest omnia quae sunt possibilia suae potentiae, erit circulatio in manifestatione omnipotentiae, hoc enim non erit aliud quam dicere quod Deus est omnipotens, quia potest omnia quae potest. Relinquitur igitur quod Deus dicatur omnipotens, quia potest omnia possibilia absolute, quod est alter modus dicendi possibile. Dicitur autem aliquid possibile vel impossibile absolute, ex habitudine terminorum, possibile quidem, quia praedicatum non repugnat subiecto, ut Socratem sedere; impossibile vero absolute, quia praedicatum repugnat subiecto, ut hominem esse asinum. Est autem considerandum quod, cum unumquodque agens agat sibi simile, unicuique potentiae activae correspondet possibile ut obiectum proprium, secundum rationem illius actus in quo fundatur potentia activa, sicut potentia calefactiva refertur, ut ad proprium obiectum, ad esse calefactibile. Esse autem divinum, super quod ratio divinae potentiae fundatur, est esse infinitum, non limitatum ad aliquod genus entis, sed praehabens in se totius esse perfectionem. Unde quidquid potest habere rationem entis, continetur sub possibilibus absolutis, respectu quorum Deus dicitur omnipotens. Nihil autem opponitur rationi entis, nisi non ens. Hoc igitur repugnat rationi possibilis absoluti, quod subditur divinae omnipotentiae, quod implicat in se esse et non esse simul. Hoc enim omnipotentiae non subditur, non propter defectum divinae potentiae; sed quia non potest habere rationem factibilis neque possibilis. Quaecumque igitur contradictionem non implicant, sub illis possibilibus continentur, respectu quorum dicitur Deus omnipotens. Ea vero quae contradictionem implicant, sub divina omnipotentia non continentur, quia non possunt habere possibilium rationem. Unde convenientius dicitur quod non possunt fieri, quam quod Deus non potest ea facere. Neque hoc est contra verbum Angeli dicentis, *non erit impossibile apud Deum omne verbum*. Id enim quod contradictionem implicat, verbum esse non potest, quia nullus intellectus potest illud concipere.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29514)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus dicitur omnipotens secundum potentiam activam, non secundum potentiam passivam, ut dictum est. Unde, quod non potest moveri et pati, non repugnat omnipotentiae.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29515)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod peccare est deficere a perfecta actione, unde posse peccare est posse deficere in agendo, quod repugnat omnipotentiae. Et propter hoc, Deus peccare non potest, qui est omnipotens. Quamvis philosophus dicat, in IV Topic., quod potest Deus et studiosus prava agere. Sed hoc intelligitur vel sub conditione cuius antecedens sit impossibile, ut puta si dicamus quod potest Deus prava agere si velit, nihil enim prohibet conditionalem esse veram, cuius antecedens et consequens est impossibile; sicut si dicatur, si homo est asinus, habet quatuor pedes. Vel ut intelligatur quod Deus potest aliqua agere, quae nunc prava videntur; quae tamen si ageret, bona essent. Vel loquitur secundum communem opinionem gentilium, qui homines dicebant transferri in deos, ut Iovem vel Mercurium.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29516)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Dei omnipotentia ostenditur maxime in parcendo et miserando, quia per hoc ostenditur Deum habere summam potestatem, quod libere peccata dimittit, eius enim qui superioris legi astringitur, non est libere peccata condonare. Vel quia, parcendo hominibus et miserando, perducit eos ad participationem infiniti boni, qui est ultimus effectus divinae virtutis. Vel quia, ut supra dictum est, effectus divinae misericordiae est fundamentum omnium divinorum operum, nihil enim debetur alicui nisi propter id quod est datum ei a Deo non debitum. In hoc autem maxime divina omnipotentia manifestatur, quod ad ipsam pertinet prima institutio omnium bonorum.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29517)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod possibile absolutum non dicitur neque secundum causas superiores, neque secundum causas inferiores sed secundum seipsum. Possibile vero quod dicitur secundum aliquam potentiam, nominatur possibile secundum proximam causam. Unde ea quae immediate nata sunt fieri a Deo solo, ut creare, iustificare, et huiusmodi, dicuntur possibilia secundum causam superiorem, quae autem nata sunt fieri a causis inferioribus, dicuntur possibilia secundum causas inferiores. Nam secundum conditionem causae proximae, effectus habet contingentiam vel necessitatem, ut supra dictum est. In hoc autem reputatur stulta mundi sapientia, quod ea quae sunt impossibilia naturae, etiam Deo impossibilia iudicabat. Et sic patet quod omnipotentia Dei impossibilitatem et necessitatem a rebus non excludit.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29518)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus possit facere quod praeterita non fuerint. Quod enim est impossibile per se, magis est impossibile quam quod est impossibile per accidens. Sed Deus potest facere id quod est impossibile per se, ut caecum illuminare, vel mortuum resuscitare. Ergo multo magis potest Deus facere illud quod est impossibile per accidens. Sed praeterita non fuisse, est impossibile per accidens, accidit enim Socratem non currere esse impossibile, ex hoc quod praeteriit. Ergo Deus potest facere quod praeterita non fuerint.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29519)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid Deus facere potuit, potest, cum eius potentia non minuatur. Sed Deus potuit facere, antequam Socrates curreret, quod non curreret. Ergo, postquam cucurrit, potest Deus facere quod non cucurrerit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29520)\n\nPraeterea, caritas est maior virtus quam virginitas. Sed Deus potest reparare caritatem amissam. Ergo et virginitatem. Ergo potest facere quod illa quae corrupta fuit, non fuerit corrupta.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29521)\n\nSed contra est quod Hieronymus dicit, *cum Deus omnia possit, non potest de corrupta facere incorruptam*. Ergo eadem ratione non potest facere de quocumque alio praeterito quod non fuerit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29522)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, sub omnipotentia Dei non cadit aliquid quod contradictionem implicat. Praeterita autem non fuisse, contradictionem implicat. Sicut enim contradictionem implicat dicere quod Socrates sedet et non sedet, ita, quod sederit et non sederit. Dicere autem quod sederit, est dicere quod sit praeteritum, dicere autem quod non sederit, est dicere quod non fuerit. Unde praeterita non fuisse, non subiacet divinae potentiae. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit, contra Faustum, *quisquis ita dicit, si Deus omnipotens est, faciat ut quae facta sunt, facta non fuerint, non videt hoc se dicere, si Deus omnipotens est, faciat ut ea quae vera sunt, eo ipso quod vera sunt, falsa sint*. Et philosophus dicit, in VI Ethic., quod *hoc solo privatur Deus, ingenita facere quae sunt facta*.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29523)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet praeterita non fuisse sit impossibile per accidens, si consideretur id quod est praeteritum, idest cursus Socratis; tamen, si consideretur praeteritum sub ratione praeteriti, ipsum non fuisse est impossibile non solum per se, sed absolute, contradictionem implicans. Et sic est magis impossibile quam mortuum resurgere, quod non implicat contradictionem, quod dicitur impossibile secundum aliquam potentiam, scilicet naturalem. Talia enim impossibilia divinae potentiae subduntur.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29524)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sicut Deus, quantum est ad perfectionem divinae potentiae, omnia potest, sed quaedam non subiacent eius potentiae, quia deficiunt a ratione possibilium; ita, si attendatur immutabilitas divinae potentiae, quidquid Deus potuit, potest; aliqua tamen olim habuerunt rationem possibilium, dum erant fienda, quae iam deficiunt a ratione possibilium, dum sunt facta. Et sic dicitur Deus ea non posse, quia ea non possunt fieri.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29525)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnem corruptionem mentis et corporis Deus auferre potest a muliere corrupta, hoc tamen ab ea removeri non poterit, quod corrupta non fuerit. Sicut etiam ab aliquo peccatore auferre non potest quod non peccaverit, et quod caritatem non amiserit.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29526)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit facere nisi ea quae facit. Deus enim non potest facere quae non praescivit et praeordinavit se facturum. Sed non praescivit neque praeordinavit se facturum, nisi ea quae facit. Ergo non potest facere nisi ea quae facit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29527)\n\nPraeterea, Deus non potest facere nisi quod debet, et quod iustum est fieri. Sed Deus non debet facere quae non facit, nec iustum est ut faciat quae non facit. Ergo Deus non potest facere nisi quae facit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29528)\n\nPraeterea, Deus non potest facere nisi quod bonum est, et conveniens rebus factis. Sed rebus factis a Deo non est bonum nec conveniens aliter esse quam sint. Ergo Deus non potest facere nisi quae facit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29529)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Matt. XXVI, *an non possum rogare patrem meum, et exhibebit mihi modo plus quam duodecim legiones Angelorum?* Neque autem ipse rogabat, neque pater exhibebat ad repugnandum Iudaeis. Ergo Deus potest facere quod non facit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29530)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc quidam dupliciter erraverunt. Quidam enim posuerunt Deum agere quasi ex necessitate naturae; ut sicut ex actione rerum naturalium non possunt alia provenire nisi quae eveniunt, utpote ex semine hominis homo, ex semine olivae oliva; ita ex operatione divina non possint aliae res, vel alius ordo rerum effluere, nisi sicut nunc est. Sed supra ostendimus Deum non agere quasi ex necessitate naturae, sed voluntatem eius esse omnium rerum causam; neque etiam ipsam voluntatem naturaliter et ex necessitate determinari ad has res. Unde nullo modo iste cursus rerum sic ex necessitate a Deo provenit, quod alia provenire non possent. Alii vero dixerunt quod potentia divina determinatur ad hunc cursum rerum, propter ordinem sapientiae et iustitiae divinae, sine quo Deus nihil operatur. Cum autem potentia Dei, quae est eius essentia, non sit aliud quam Dei sapientia, convenienter quidem dici potest quod nihil sit in Dei potentia, quod non sit in ordine divinae sapientiae, nam divina sapientia totum posse potentiae comprehendit. Sed tamen ordo a divina sapientia rebus inditus, in quo ratio iustitiae consistit, ut supra dictum est, non adaequat divinam sapientiam, sic ut divina sapientia limitetur ad hunc ordinem. Manifestum est enim quod tota ratio ordinis, quam sapiens rebus a se factis imponit, a fine sumitur. Quando igitur finis est proportionatus rebus propter finem factis, sapientia facientis limitatur ad aliquem determinatum ordinem. Sed divina bonitas est finis improportionabiliter excedens res creatas. Unde divina sapientia non determinatur ad aliquem certum ordinem rerum, ut non possit alius cursus rerum ab ipsa effluere. Unde dicendum est simpliciter quod Deus potest alia facere quam quae facit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29531)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in nobis, in quibus est aliud potentia et essentia a voluntate et intellectu, et iterum intellectus aliud a sapientia, et voluntas aliud a iustitia, potest esse aliquid in potentia, quod non potest esse in voluntate iusta, vel in intellectu sapiente. Sed in Deo est idem potentia et essentia et voluntas et intellectus et sapientia et iustitia. Unde nihil potest esse in potentia divina, quod non possit esse in voluntate iusta ipsius, et in intellectu sapiente eius. Tamen, quia voluntas non determinatur ex necessitate ad haec vel illa, nisi forte ex suppositione, ut supra dictum est; neque sapientia Dei et iustitia determinantur ad hunc ordinem, ut supra dictum est; nihil prohibet esse aliquid in potentia Dei, quod non vult, et quod non continetur sub ordine quem statuit rebus. Et quia potentia intelligitur ut exequens, voluntas autem ut imperans, et intellectus et sapientia ut dirigens, quod attribuitur potentiae secundum se consideratae, dicitur Deus posse secundum potentiam absolutam. Et huiusmodi est omne illud in quo potest salvari ratio entis, ut supra dictum est. Quod autem attribuitur potentiae divinae secundum quod exequitur imperium voluntatis iustae, hoc dicitur Deus posse facere de potentia ordinata. Secundum hoc ergo, dicendum est quod Deus potest alia facere, de potentia absoluta, quam quae praescivit et praeordinavit se facturum, non tamen potest esse quod aliqua faciat, quae non praesciverit et praeordinaverit se facturum. Quia ipsum facere subiacet praescientiae et praeordinationi, non autem ipsum posse, quod est naturale. Ideo enim Deus aliquid facit, quia vult, non tamen ideo potest, quia vult, sed quia talis est in sua natura.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29532)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus non debet aliquid alicui nisi sibi. Unde, cum dicitur quod Deus non potest facere nisi quod debet nihil aliud significatur nisi quod Deus non potest facere nisi quod ei est conveniens et iustum. Sed hoc quod dico conveniens et iustum, potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, sic quod hoc quod dico conveniens et iustum, prius intelligatur coniungi cum hoc verbo est, ita quod restringatur ad standum pro praesentibus; et sic referatur ad potentiam. Et sic falsum est quod dicitur, est enim sensus, Deus non potest facere nisi quod modo conveniens est et iustum. Si vero prius coniungatur cum hoc verbo potest, quod habet vim ampliandi, et postmodum cum hoc verbo est, significabitur quoddam praesens confusum, et erit locutio vera, sub hoc sensu, Deus non potest facere nisi id quod, si faceret, esset conveniens et iustum.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29533)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet iste cursus rerum sit determinatus istis rebus quae nunc sunt, non tamen ad hunc cursum limitatur divina sapientia et potestas. Unde, licet istis rebus quae nunc sunt, nullus alius cursus esset bonus et conveniens, tamen Deus posset alias res facere, et alium eis imponere ordinem.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29534)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit meliora facere ea quae facit. Quidquid enim Deus facit, potentissime et sapientissime facit. Sed tanto fit aliquid melius, quanto fit potentius et sapientius. Ergo Deus non potest aliquid facere melius quam facit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29535)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus, contra Maximinum, sic argumentatur, *si Deus potuit, et noluit, gignere filium sibi aequalem, invidus fuit*. Eadem ratione, si Deus potuit res meliores facere quam fecerit, et noluit, invidus fuit. Sed invidia est omnino relegata a Deo. Ergo Deus unumquodque fecit optimum. Non ergo Deus potest aliquid facere melius quam fecit.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29536)\n\nPraeterea, id quod est maxime et valde bonum, non potest melius fieri, quia maximo nihil est maius. Sed, sicut Augustinus dicit in Enchirid., *bona sunt singula quae Deus fecit, sed simul universa valde bona, quia ex omnibus consistit universitatis admirabilis pulchritudo*. Ergo bonum universi non potest melius fieri a Deo.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29537)\n\nPraeterea, homo Christus est plenus gratia et veritate, et spiritum habet non ad mensuram, et sic non potest esse melior. Beatitudo etiam creata dicitur esse summum bonum, et sic non potest esse melius. Beata etiam virgo Maria est super omnes choros Angelorum exaltata, et sic non potest esse melior. Non igitur omnia quae fecit Deus, potest facere meliora.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29538)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur ad Ephes. III, quod *Deus potens est omnia facere abundantius quam petimus aut intelligimus*.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29539)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod bonitas alicuius rei est duplex. Una quidem, quae est de essentia rei; sicut esse rationale est de essentia hominis. Et quantum ad hoc bonum, Deus non potest facere aliquam rem meliorem quam ipsa sit, licet possit facere aliquam aliam ea meliorem. Sicut etiam non potest facere quaternarium maiorem, quia, si esset maior, iam non esset quaternarius, sed alius numerus. Sic enim se habet additio differentiae substantialis in definitionibus, sicut additio unitatis in numeris, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. Alia bonitas est, quae est extra essentiam rei; sicut bonum hominis est esse virtuosum vel sapientem. Et secundum tale bonum, potest Deus res a se factas facere meliores. Simpliciter autem loquendo, qualibet re a se facta potest Deus facere aliam meliorem.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29540)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur Deum posse aliquid facere melius quam facit, si ly melius sit nomen, verum est, qualibet enim re potest facere aliam meliorem. Eandem vero potest facere meliorem quodammodo, et quodammodo non, sicut dictum est. Si vero ly melius sit adverbium, et importet modum ex parte facientis, sic Deus non potest facere melius quam sicut facit, quia non potest facere ex maiori sapientia et bonitate. Si autem importet modum ex parte facti, sic potest facere melius, quia potest dare rebus a se factis meliorem modum essendi quantum ad accidentalia, licet non quantum ad essentialia.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29541)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod de ratione filii est quod aequetur patri, cum ad perfectum venerit, non est autem de ratione creaturae alicuius, quod sit melior quam a Deo facta est. Unde non est similis ratio.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29542)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod universum, suppositis istis rebus, non potest esse melius; propter decentissimum ordinem his rebus attributum a Deo, in quo bonum universi consistit. Quorum si unum aliquod esset melius, corrumperetur proportio ordinis, sicut, si una chorda plus debito intenderetur, corrumperetur citharae melodia. Posset tamen Deus alias res facere, vel alias addere istis rebus factis, et sic esset illud universum melius.\n\n[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29543)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod humanitas Christi ex hoc quod est unita Deo, et beatitudo creata ex hoc quod est fruitio Dei, et beata virgo ex hoc quod est mater Dei, habent quandam dignitatem infinitam, ex bono infinito quod est Deus. Et ex hac parte non potest aliquid fieri melius eis, sicut non potest aliquid melius esse Deo.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0002","text":"[I q. 25 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003","text":"Post considerationem divinae scientiae et voluntatis, et eorum quae ad hoc pertinent, restat considerandum de divina potentia. Et circa hoc quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum in Deo sit potentia. Secundo, utrum eius potentia sit infinita. Tertio, utrum sit omnipotens. Quarto, utrum possit facere quod ea quae sunt praeterita, non fuerint. Quinto, utrum Deus possit facere quae non facit, vel praetermittere quae facit. Sexto, utrum quae facit, possit facere meliora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0005","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sit potentia. Sicut enim prima materia se habet ad potentiam, ita Deus, qui est agens primum, se habet ad actum. Sed prima materia, secundum se considerata, est absque omni actu. Ergo agens primum, quod est Deus, est absque potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0007","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0008","text":"Praeterea, secundum philosophum, in IX Metaphys., qualibet potentia melior est eius actus, nam forma est melior quam materia, et actio quam potentia activa; est enim finis eius. Sed nihil est melius eo quod est in Deo, quia quidquid est in Deo, est Deus, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo nulla potentia est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0009","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0010","text":"Praeterea, potentia est principium operationis. Sed operatio divina est eius essentia, cum in Deo nullum sit accidens. Essentiae autem divinae non est aliquod principium. Ergo ratio potentiae Deo non convenit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0011","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0012","text":"Praeterea, supra ostensum est quod scientia Dei et voluntas eius sunt causa rerum. Causa autem et principium idem sunt. Ergo non oportet in Deo assignare potentiam, sed solum scientiam et voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0013","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo LXXXVIII, *potens es, domine, et veritas tua in circuitu tuo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0015","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod duplex est potentia, scilicet passiva, quae nullo modo est in Deo; et activa, quam oportet in Deo summe ponere. Manifestum est enim quod unumquodque, secundum quod est actu et perfectum, secundum hoc est principium activum alicuius, patitur autem unumquodque, secundum quod est deficiens et imperfectum. Ostensum est autem supra quod Deus est purus actus, et simpliciter et universaliter perfectus; neque in eo aliqua imperfectio locum habet. Unde sibi maxime competit esse principium activum, et nullo modo pati. Ratio autem activi principii convenit potentiae activae. Nam potentia activa est principium agendi in aliud, potentia vero passiva est principium patiendi ab alio, ut philosophus dicit, V Metaphys. Relinquitur ergo quod in Deo maxime sit potentia activa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0017","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod potentia activa non dividitur contra actum, sed fundatur in eo, nam unumquodque agit secundum quod est actu. Potentia vero passiva dividitur contra actum, nam unumquodque patitur secundum quod est in potentia. Unde haec potentia excluditur a Deo, non autem activa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0019","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quandocumque actus est aliud a potentia, oportet quod actus sit nobilior potentia. Sed actio Dei non est aliud ab eius potentia, sed utrumque est essentia divina, quia nec esse eius est aliud ab eius essentia. Unde non oportet quod aliquid sit nobilius quam potentia Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0021","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod potentia in rebus creatis non solum est principium actionis, sed etiam effectus. Sic igitur in Deo salvatur ratio potentiae quantum ad hoc, quod est principium effectus, non autem quantum ad hoc, quod est principium actionis, quae est divina essentia. Nisi forte secundum modum intelligendi, prout divina essentia, quae in se simpliciter praehabet quidquid perfectionis est in rebus creatis, potest intelligi et sub ratione actionis, et sub ratione potentiae; sicut etiam intelligitur et sub ratione suppositi habentis naturam, et sub ratione naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0023","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod potentia non ponitur in Deo ut aliquid differens a scientia et voluntate secundum rem, sed solum secundum rationem; inquantum scilicet potentia importat rationem principii exequentis id quod voluntas imperat, et ad quod scientia dirigit; quae tria Deo secundum idem conveniunt. Vel dicendum quod ipsa scientia vel voluntas divina, secundum quod est principium effectivum, habet rationem potentiae. Unde consideratio scientiae et voluntatis praecedit in Deo considerationem potentiae, sicut causa praecedit operationem et effectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0025","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0026","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0027","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod potentia Dei non sit infinita. Omne enim infinitum est imperfectum, secundum philosophum, in III Physic. Sed potentia Dei non est imperfecta. Ergo non est infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0028","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0029","text":"Praeterea, omnis potentia manifestatur per effectum, alias frustra esset. Si igitur potentia Dei esset infinita, posset facere effectum infinitum. Quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0030","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0031","text":"Praeterea, philosophus probat in VIII Physic., quod si potentia alicuius corporis esset infinita, moveret in instanti. Deus autem non movet in instanti, sed movet creaturam spiritualem *per tempus, creaturam vero corporalem per locum et tempus*, secundum Augustinum, VIII super Genesim ad litteram. Non ergo est eius potentia infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0032","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0033","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Hilarius, VIII de Trin., quod Deus est immensae virtutis, vivens, potens. Omne autem immensum est infinitum. Ergo virtus divina est infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0034","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, secundum hoc potentia activa invenitur in Deo, secundum quod ipse actu est. Esse autem eius est infinitum, inquantum non est limitatum per aliquid recipiens; ut patet per ea quae supra dicta sunt, cum de infinitate divinae essentiae ageretur. Unde necesse est quod activa potentia Dei sit infinita. In omnibus enim agentibus hoc invenitur, quod quanto aliquod agens perfectius habet formam qua agit, tanto est maior eius potentia in agendo. Sicut quanto est aliquid magis calidum, tanto habet maiorem potentiam ad calefaciendum, et haberet utique potentiam infinitam ad calefaciendum, si eius calor esset infinitus. Unde, cum ipsa essentia divina, per quam Deus agit, sit infinita, sicut supra ostensum est, sequitur quod eius potentia sit infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0036","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0037","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de infinito quod est ex parte materiae non terminatae per formam; cuiusmodi est infinitum quod congruit quantitati. Sic autem non est infinita divina essentia, ut supra ostensum est; et per consequens nec eius potentia. Unde non sequitur quod sit imperfecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0038","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod potentia agentis univoci tota manifestatur in suo effectu, potentia enim generativa hominis nihil potest plus quam generare hominem. Sed potentia agentis non univoci non tota manifestatur in sui effectus productione, sicut potentia solis non tota manifestatur in productione alicuius animalis ex putrefactione generati. Manifestum est autem quod Deus non est agens univocum, nihil enim aliud potest cum eo convenire neque in specie, neque in genere, ut supra ostensum est. Unde relinquitur quod effectus eius semper est minor quam potentia eius. Non ergo oportet quod manifestetur infinita potentia Dei in hoc, quod producat effectum infinitum. Et tamen, etiam si nullum effectum produceret, non esset Dei potentia frustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040","text":"Quia frustra est quod ordinatur ad finem, quem non attingit, potentia autem Dei non ordinatur ad effectum sicut ad finem, sed magis ipsa est finis sui effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0041","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod philosophus in VIII Physic., probat, quod si aliquod corpus haberet potentiam infinitam, quod moveret in non tempore. Et tamen ostendit, quod potentia motoris caeli est infinita, quia movere potest tempore infinito. Relinquitur ergo secundum eius intentionem, quod potentia infinita corporis si esset, moveret in non tempore, non autem potentia incorporei motoris. Cuius ratio est, quia corpus movens aliud corpus, est agens univocum. Unde oportet quod tota potentia agentis manifestetur in motu. Quia igitur quanto moventis corporis potentia est maior, tanto velocius movet, necesse est quod si fuerit infinita, moveat improportionabiliter citius, quod est movere in non tempore. Sed movens incorporeum est agens non univocum. Unde non oportet, quod tota virtus eius manifestetur in motu ita, quod moveat in non tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0043","text":"Et praesertim, quia movet secundum dispositionem suae voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0045","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit omnipotens. Moveri enim et pati aliquid omnium est. Sed hoc Deus non potest, est enim immobilis, ut supra dictum est. Non igitur est omnipotens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0047","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0048","text":"Praeterea, peccare aliquid agere est. Sed Deus non potest peccare, neque seipsum negare, ut dicitur II Tim. II. Ergo Deus non est omnipotens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0049","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0050","text":"Praeterea, de Deo dicitur quod *omnipotentiam suam parcendo maxime et miserando manifestat*. Ultimum igitur quod potest divina potentia, est parcere et misereri. Aliquid autem est multo maius quam parcere et misereri; sicut creare alium mundum, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Ergo Deus non est omnipotens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0051","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0052","text":"Praeterea, super illud I Cor. I, *stultam fecit Deus sapientiam huius mundi*, dicit Glossa, *sapientiam huius mundi fecit Deus stultam, ostendendo possibile, quod illa impossibile iudicabat*. Unde videtur quod non sit aliquid iudicandum possibile vel impossibile secundum inferiores causas, prout sapientia huius mundi iudicat; sed secundum potentiam divinam. Si igitur Deus sit omnipotens, omnia erunt possibilia. Nihil ergo impossibile. Sublato autem impossibili, tollitur necessarium, nam quod necesse est esse, impossibile est non esse. Nihil ergo erit necessarium in rebus, si Deus est omnipotens. Hoc autem est impossibile. Ergo Deus non est omnipotens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0053","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0054","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Luc. I, *non erit impossibile apud Deum omne verbum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0055","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod communiter confitentur omnes Deum esse omnipotentem. Sed rationem omnipotentiae assignare videtur difficile. Dubium enim potest esse quid comprehendatur sub ista distributione, cum dicitur omnia posse Deum. Sed si quis recte consideret, cum potentia dicatur ad possibilia, cum Deus omnia posse dicitur, nihil rectius intelligitur quam quod possit omnia possibilia, et ob hoc omnipotens dicatur. Possibile autem dicitur dupliciter, secundum philosophum, in V Metaphys. Uno modo, per respectum ad aliquam potentiam, sicut quod subditur humanae potentiae, dicitur esse possibile homini. Non autem potest dici quod Deus dicatur omnipotens, quia potest omnia quae sunt possibilia naturae creatae, quia divina potentia in plura extenditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","text":"Si autem dicatur quod Deus sit omnipotens, quia potest omnia quae sunt possibilia suae potentiae, erit circulatio in manifestatione omnipotentiae, hoc enim non erit aliud quam dicere quod Deus est omnipotens, quia potest omnia quae potest. Relinquitur igitur quod Deus dicatur omnipotens, quia potest omnia possibilia absolute, quod est alter modus dicendi possibile. Dicitur autem aliquid possibile vel impossibile absolute, ex habitudine terminorum, possibile quidem, quia praedicatum non repugnat subiecto, ut Socratem sedere; impossibile vero absolute, quia praedicatum repugnat subiecto, ut hominem esse asinum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","text":"Est autem considerandum quod, cum unumquodque agens agat sibi simile, unicuique potentiae activae correspondet possibile ut obiectum proprium, secundum rationem illius actus in quo fundatur potentia activa, sicut potentia calefactiva refertur, ut ad proprium obiectum, ad esse calefactibile. Esse autem divinum, super quod ratio divinae potentiae fundatur, est esse infinitum, non limitatum ad aliquod genus entis, sed praehabens in se totius esse perfectionem. Unde quidquid potest habere rationem entis, continetur sub possibilibus absolutis, respectu quorum Deus dicitur omnipotens. Nihil autem opponitur rationi entis, nisi non ens. Hoc igitur repugnat rationi possibilis absoluti, quod subditur divinae omnipotentiae, quod implicat in se esse et non esse simul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","text":"Hoc enim omnipotentiae non subditur, non propter defectum divinae potentiae; sed quia non potest habere rationem factibilis neque possibilis. Quaecumque igitur contradictionem non implicant, sub illis possibilibus continentur, respectu quorum dicitur Deus omnipotens. Ea vero quae contradictionem implicant, sub divina omnipotentia non continentur, quia non possunt habere possibilium rationem. Unde convenientius dicitur quod non possunt fieri, quam quod Deus non potest ea facere. Neque hoc est contra verbum Angeli dicentis, *non erit impossibile apud Deum omne verbum*. Id enim quod contradictionem implicat, verbum esse non potest, quia nullus intellectus potest illud concipere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0060","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus dicitur omnipotens secundum potentiam activam, non secundum potentiam passivam, ut dictum est. Unde, quod non potest moveri et pati, non repugnat omnipotentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0062","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod peccare est deficere a perfecta actione, unde posse peccare est posse deficere in agendo, quod repugnat omnipotentiae. Et propter hoc, Deus peccare non potest, qui est omnipotens. Quamvis philosophus dicat, in IV Topic., quod potest Deus et studiosus prava agere. Sed hoc intelligitur vel sub conditione cuius antecedens sit impossibile, ut puta si dicamus quod potest Deus prava agere si velit, nihil enim prohibet conditionalem esse veram, cuius antecedens et consequens est impossibile; sicut si dicatur, si homo est asinus, habet quatuor pedes. Vel ut intelligatur quod Deus potest aliqua agere, quae nunc prava videntur; quae tamen si ageret, bona essent. Vel loquitur secundum communem opinionem gentilium, qui homines dicebant transferri in deos, ut Iovem vel Mercurium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0064","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0065","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Dei omnipotentia ostenditur maxime in parcendo et miserando, quia per hoc ostenditur Deum habere summam potestatem, quod libere peccata dimittit, eius enim qui superioris legi astringitur, non est libere peccata condonare. Vel quia, parcendo hominibus et miserando, perducit eos ad participationem infiniti boni, qui est ultimus effectus divinae virtutis. Vel quia, ut supra dictum est, effectus divinae misericordiae est fundamentum omnium divinorum operum, nihil enim debetur alicui nisi propter id quod est datum ei a Deo non debitum. In hoc autem maxime divina omnipotentia manifestatur, quod ad ipsam pertinet prima institutio omnium bonorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0066","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod possibile absolutum non dicitur neque secundum causas superiores, neque secundum causas inferiores sed secundum seipsum. Possibile vero quod dicitur secundum aliquam potentiam, nominatur possibile secundum proximam causam. Unde ea quae immediate nata sunt fieri a Deo solo, ut creare, iustificare, et huiusmodi, dicuntur possibilia secundum causam superiorem, quae autem nata sunt fieri a causis inferioribus, dicuntur possibilia secundum causas inferiores. Nam secundum conditionem causae proximae, effectus habet contingentiam vel necessitatem, ut supra dictum est. In hoc autem reputatur stulta mundi sapientia, quod ea quae sunt impossibilia naturae, etiam Deo impossibilia iudicabat. Et sic patet quod omnipotentia Dei impossibilitatem et necessitatem a rebus non excludit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0068","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0069","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0070","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus possit facere quod praeterita non fuerint. Quod enim est impossibile per se, magis est impossibile quam quod est impossibile per accidens. Sed Deus potest facere id quod est impossibile per se, ut caecum illuminare, vel mortuum resuscitare. Ergo multo magis potest Deus facere illud quod est impossibile per accidens. Sed praeterita non fuisse, est impossibile per accidens, accidit enim Socratem non currere esse impossibile, ex hoc quod praeteriit. Ergo Deus potest facere quod praeterita non fuerint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0071","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0072","text":"Praeterea, quidquid Deus facere potuit, potest, cum eius potentia non minuatur. Sed Deus potuit facere, antequam Socrates curreret, quod non curreret. Ergo, postquam cucurrit, potest Deus facere quod non cucurrerit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0073","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0074","text":"Praeterea, caritas est maior virtus quam virginitas. Sed Deus potest reparare caritatem amissam. Ergo et virginitatem. Ergo potest facere quod illa quae corrupta fuit, non fuerit corrupta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0075","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0076","text":"Sed contra est quod Hieronymus dicit, *cum Deus omnia possit, non potest de corrupta facere incorruptam*. Ergo eadem ratione non potest facere de quocumque alio praeterito quod non fuerit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0077","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, sub omnipotentia Dei non cadit aliquid quod contradictionem implicat. Praeterita autem non fuisse, contradictionem implicat. Sicut enim contradictionem implicat dicere quod Socrates sedet et non sedet, ita, quod sederit et non sederit. Dicere autem quod sederit, est dicere quod sit praeteritum, dicere autem quod non sederit, est dicere quod non fuerit. Unde praeterita non fuisse, non subiacet divinae potentiae. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit, contra Faustum, *quisquis ita dicit, si Deus omnipotens est, faciat ut quae facta sunt, facta non fuerint, non videt hoc se dicere, si Deus omnipotens est, faciat ut ea quae vera sunt, eo ipso quod vera sunt, falsa sint*. Et philosophus dicit, in VI Ethic., quod *hoc solo privatur Deus, ingenita facere quae sunt facta*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0079","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0080","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet praeterita non fuisse sit impossibile per accidens, si consideretur id quod est praeteritum, idest cursus Socratis; tamen, si consideretur praeteritum sub ratione praeteriti, ipsum non fuisse est impossibile non solum per se, sed absolute, contradictionem implicans. Et sic est magis impossibile quam mortuum resurgere, quod non implicat contradictionem, quod dicitur impossibile secundum aliquam potentiam, scilicet naturalem. Talia enim impossibilia divinae potentiae subduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0081","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sicut Deus, quantum est ad perfectionem divinae potentiae, omnia potest, sed quaedam non subiacent eius potentiae, quia deficiunt a ratione possibilium; ita, si attendatur immutabilitas divinae potentiae, quidquid Deus potuit, potest; aliqua tamen olim habuerunt rationem possibilium, dum erant fienda, quae iam deficiunt a ratione possibilium, dum sunt facta. Et sic dicitur Deus ea non posse, quia ea non possunt fieri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0083","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0084","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnem corruptionem mentis et corporis Deus auferre potest a muliere corrupta, hoc tamen ab ea removeri non poterit, quod corrupta non fuerit. Sicut etiam ab aliquo peccatore auferre non potest quod non peccaverit, et quod caritatem non amiserit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0085","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0086","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0087","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit facere nisi ea quae facit. Deus enim non potest facere quae non praescivit et praeordinavit se facturum. Sed non praescivit neque praeordinavit se facturum, nisi ea quae facit. Ergo non potest facere nisi ea quae facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0088","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0089","text":"Praeterea, Deus non potest facere nisi quod debet, et quod iustum est fieri. Sed Deus non debet facere quae non facit, nec iustum est ut faciat quae non facit. Ergo Deus non potest facere nisi quae facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0090","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0091","text":"Praeterea, Deus non potest facere nisi quod bonum est, et conveniens rebus factis. Sed rebus factis a Deo non est bonum nec conveniens aliter esse quam sint. Ergo Deus non potest facere nisi quae facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0092","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0093","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Matt. XXVI, *an non possum rogare patrem meum, et exhibebit mihi modo plus quam duodecim legiones Angelorum?* Neque autem ipse rogabat, neque pater exhibebat ad repugnandum Iudaeis. Ergo Deus potest facere quod non facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0094","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc quidam dupliciter erraverunt. Quidam enim posuerunt Deum agere quasi ex necessitate naturae; ut sicut ex actione rerum naturalium non possunt alia provenire nisi quae eveniunt, utpote ex semine hominis homo, ex semine olivae oliva; ita ex operatione divina non possint aliae res, vel alius ordo rerum effluere, nisi sicut nunc est. Sed supra ostendimus Deum non agere quasi ex necessitate naturae, sed voluntatem eius esse omnium rerum causam; neque etiam ipsam voluntatem naturaliter et ex necessitate determinari ad has res. Unde nullo modo iste cursus rerum sic ex necessitate a Deo provenit, quod alia provenire non possent. Alii vero dixerunt quod potentia divina determinatur ad hunc cursum rerum, propter ordinem sapientiae et iustitiae divinae, sine quo Deus nihil operatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","text":"Cum autem potentia Dei, quae est eius essentia, non sit aliud quam Dei sapientia, convenienter quidem dici potest quod nihil sit in Dei potentia, quod non sit in ordine divinae sapientiae, nam divina sapientia totum posse potentiae comprehendit. Sed tamen ordo a divina sapientia rebus inditus, in quo ratio iustitiae consistit, ut supra dictum est, non adaequat divinam sapientiam, sic ut divina sapientia limitetur ad hunc ordinem. Manifestum est enim quod tota ratio ordinis, quam sapiens rebus a se factis imponit, a fine sumitur. Quando igitur finis est proportionatus rebus propter finem factis, sapientia facientis limitatur ad aliquem determinatum ordinem. Sed divina bonitas est finis improportionabiliter excedens res creatas. Unde divina sapientia non determinatur ad aliquem certum ordinem rerum, ut non possit alius cursus rerum ab ipsa effluere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0097","text":"Unde dicendum est simpliciter quod Deus potest alia facere quam quae facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0098","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in nobis, in quibus est aliud potentia et essentia a voluntate et intellectu, et iterum intellectus aliud a sapientia, et voluntas aliud a iustitia, potest esse aliquid in potentia, quod non potest esse in voluntate iusta, vel in intellectu sapiente. Sed in Deo est idem potentia et essentia et voluntas et intellectus et sapientia et iustitia. Unde nihil potest esse in potentia divina, quod non possit esse in voluntate iusta ipsius, et in intellectu sapiente eius. Tamen, quia voluntas non determinatur ex necessitate ad haec vel illa, nisi forte ex suppositione, ut supra dictum est; neque sapientia Dei et iustitia determinantur ad hunc ordinem, ut supra dictum est; nihil prohibet esse aliquid in potentia Dei, quod non vult, et quod non continetur sub ordine quem statuit rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","text":"Et quia potentia intelligitur ut exequens, voluntas autem ut imperans, et intellectus et sapientia ut dirigens, quod attribuitur potentiae secundum se consideratae, dicitur Deus posse secundum potentiam absolutam. Et huiusmodi est omne illud in quo potest salvari ratio entis, ut supra dictum est. Quod autem attribuitur potentiae divinae secundum quod exequitur imperium voluntatis iustae, hoc dicitur Deus posse facere de potentia ordinata. Secundum hoc ergo, dicendum est quod Deus potest alia facere, de potentia absoluta, quam quae praescivit et praeordinavit se facturum, non tamen potest esse quod aliqua faciat, quae non praesciverit et praeordinaverit se facturum. Quia ipsum facere subiacet praescientiae et praeordinationi, non autem ipsum posse, quod est naturale. Ideo enim Deus aliquid facit, quia vult, non tamen ideo potest, quia vult, sed quia talis est in sua natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0101","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus non debet aliquid alicui nisi sibi. Unde, cum dicitur quod Deus non potest facere nisi quod debet nihil aliud significatur nisi quod Deus non potest facere nisi quod ei est conveniens et iustum. Sed hoc quod dico conveniens et iustum, potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, sic quod hoc quod dico conveniens et iustum, prius intelligatur coniungi cum hoc verbo est, ita quod restringatur ad standum pro praesentibus; et sic referatur ad potentiam. Et sic falsum est quod dicitur, est enim sensus, Deus non potest facere nisi quod modo conveniens est et iustum. Si vero prius coniungatur cum hoc verbo potest, quod habet vim ampliandi, et postmodum cum hoc verbo est, significabitur quoddam praesens confusum, et erit locutio vera, sub hoc sensu, Deus non potest facere nisi id quod, si faceret, esset conveniens et iustum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0103","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0104","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet iste cursus rerum sit determinatus istis rebus quae nunc sunt, non tamen ad hunc cursum limitatur divina sapientia et potestas. Unde, licet istis rebus quae nunc sunt, nullus alius cursus esset bonus et conveniens, tamen Deus posset alias res facere, et alium eis imponere ordinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0105","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0106","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0107","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit meliora facere ea quae facit. Quidquid enim Deus facit, potentissime et sapientissime facit. Sed tanto fit aliquid melius, quanto fit potentius et sapientius. Ergo Deus non potest aliquid facere melius quam facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0108","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0109","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus, contra Maximinum, sic argumentatur, *si Deus potuit, et noluit, gignere filium sibi aequalem, invidus fuit*. Eadem ratione, si Deus potuit res meliores facere quam fecerit, et noluit, invidus fuit. Sed invidia est omnino relegata a Deo. Ergo Deus unumquodque fecit optimum. Non ergo Deus potest aliquid facere melius quam fecit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0110","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0111","text":"Praeterea, id quod est maxime et valde bonum, non potest melius fieri, quia maximo nihil est maius. Sed, sicut Augustinus dicit in Enchirid., *bona sunt singula quae Deus fecit, sed simul universa valde bona, quia ex omnibus consistit universitatis admirabilis pulchritudo*. Ergo bonum universi non potest melius fieri a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0112","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0113","text":"Praeterea, homo Christus est plenus gratia et veritate, et spiritum habet non ad mensuram, et sic non potest esse melior. Beatitudo etiam creata dicitur esse summum bonum, et sic non potest esse melius. Beata etiam virgo Maria est super omnes choros Angelorum exaltata, et sic non potest esse melior. Non igitur omnia quae fecit Deus, potest facere meliora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0114","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0115","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur ad Ephes. III, quod *Deus potens est omnia facere abundantius quam petimus aut intelligimus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0116","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod bonitas alicuius rei est duplex. Una quidem, quae est de essentia rei; sicut esse rationale est de essentia hominis. Et quantum ad hoc bonum, Deus non potest facere aliquam rem meliorem quam ipsa sit, licet possit facere aliquam aliam ea meliorem. Sicut etiam non potest facere quaternarium maiorem, quia, si esset maior, iam non esset quaternarius, sed alius numerus. Sic enim se habet additio differentiae substantialis in definitionibus, sicut additio unitatis in numeris, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. Alia bonitas est, quae est extra essentiam rei; sicut bonum hominis est esse virtuosum vel sapientem. Et secundum tale bonum, potest Deus res a se factas facere meliores. Simpliciter autem loquendo, qualibet re a se facta potest Deus facere aliam meliorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0118","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur Deum posse aliquid facere melius quam facit, si ly melius sit nomen, verum est, qualibet enim re potest facere aliam meliorem. Eandem vero potest facere meliorem quodammodo, et quodammodo non, sicut dictum est. Si vero ly melius sit adverbium, et importet modum ex parte facientis, sic Deus non potest facere melius quam sicut facit, quia non potest facere ex maiori sapientia et bonitate. Si autem importet modum ex parte facti, sic potest facere melius, quia potest dare rebus a se factis meliorem modum essendi quantum ad accidentalia, licet non quantum ad essentialia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0120","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0121","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod de ratione filii est quod aequetur patri, cum ad perfectum venerit, non est autem de ratione creaturae alicuius, quod sit melior quam a Deo facta est. Unde non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0122","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod universum, suppositis istis rebus, non potest esse melius; propter decentissimum ordinem his rebus attributum a Deo, in quo bonum universi consistit. Quorum si unum aliquod esset melius, corrumperetur proportio ordinis, sicut, si una chorda plus debito intenderetur, corrumperetur citharae melodia. Posset tamen Deus alias res facere, vel alias addere istis rebus factis, et sic esset illud universum melius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0124","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0125","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod humanitas Christi ex hoc quod est unita Deo, et beatitudo creata ex hoc quod est fruitio Dei, et beata virgo ex hoc quod est mater Dei, habent quandam dignitatem infinitam, ex bono infinito quod est Deus. Et ex hac parte non potest aliquid fieri melius eis, sicut non potest aliquid melius esse Deo.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ccd8f3c9647075bc4b29c874eb7e866cb1f033bae1a593be5b4267991bb9f926","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140704213057Z_8ebe5c56","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 25","source_sha256":"ccd8f3c9647075bc4b29c874eb7e866cb1f033bae1a593be5b4267991bb9f926","sha256":"e53e9772c9a8c3d620633b0e45f624cceebd2301d6a34f2c14072e8fe7cd0e16","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:07:04.213094+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0002","text":"[I q. 25 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003","text":"After the consideration of the divine knowledge and will, and of those things which pertain to this, it remains to consider the divine power. And concerning this, six questions are asked. First, whether there is power in God. Second, whether His power is infinite. Third, whether He is omnipotent. Fourth, whether He can bring it about that things which are past have not been. Fifth, whether God can do things that He does not do, or omit things that He does. Sixth, whether He can make the things that He makes better.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0005","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that there is no power in God. For just as prime matter is related to potency, so God, who is the first agent, is related to act. But prime matter, considered in itself, is without every act. Therefore the first agent, which is God, is without potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0007","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0008","text":"Further, according to the Philosopher, in Metaphysics IX, the act of any potency is better than that potency; for form is better than matter, and action than active potency, for it is its end. But nothing is better than that which is in God, because whatever is in God is God, as was shown above. Therefore there is no power in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0009","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0010","text":"Further, power is a principle of operation. But the divine operation is His essence, since there is no accident in God. But the divine essence has no principle. Therefore the notion of power does not befit God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0011","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0012","text":"Further, it was shown above that God's knowledge and His will are the cause of things. But cause and principle are the same. Therefore it is not necessary to assign power to God, but only knowledge and will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0013","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0014","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Psalm LXXXVIII: *You are mighty, O Lord, and Your truth is round about You*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0015","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that power is twofold, namely passive, which is in no way in God; and active, which must be posited in God in the highest degree. For it is manifest that each thing, insofar as it is actual and perfect, is accordingly an active principle of something; whereas each thing undergoes [something], insofar as it is deficient and imperfect. But it was shown above that God is pure act, and perfect simply and universally; nor does any imperfection have a place in Him. Hence it belongs to Him most of all to be an active principle, and in no way to undergo [anything]. But the character of an active principle belongs to active power. For active power is a principle of acting upon another, whereas passive power is a principle of being acted upon by another, as the Philosopher says in Metaphysics V. It remains, therefore, that active power exists in God in the highest degree.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0017","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that active power is not divided against act, but is founded upon it; for each thing acts insofar as it is in act. Passive power, however, is divided against act; for each thing undergoes [something] insofar as it is in potency. Hence this power is excluded from God, but not active power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0019","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that whenever act is other than potency, act must be nobler than potency. But God's action is not other than His power; rather, both are the divine essence, because neither is His being other than His essence. Hence it is not necessary that anything be nobler than God's power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0021","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that power in created things is not only a principle of action, but also of an effect. Thus, therefore, the notion of power is preserved in God with respect to this, that it is a principle of an effect, but not with respect to this, that it is a principle of action, which is the divine essence. Unless perhaps according to the mode of understanding, insofar as the divine essence, which in itself precontains simply whatever perfection there is in created things, can be understood both under the notion of action and under the notion of power; just as it is also understood both under the notion of a supposit having a nature and under the notion of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0023","text":"[I q. 25 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that power is not posited in God as something differing in reality from knowledge and will, but only according to reason; namely, insofar as power conveys the notion of a principle executing that which the will commands and to which knowledge directs. These three belong to God according to the same [thing]. Or it may be said that divine knowledge or will itself, insofar as it is an effective principle, has the character of power. Hence the consideration of knowledge and will precedes in God the consideration of power, just as a cause precedes operation and effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0025","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0026","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0027","text":"We proceed to the second as follows. It seems that God's power is not infinite. For every infinite thing is imperfect, according to the Philosopher, in *Physics* III. But God's power is not imperfect. Therefore it is not infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0028","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0029","text":"Moreover, every power is manifested through an effect; otherwise it would be in vain. Therefore, if God's power were infinite, He could make an infinite effect. But this is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0030","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0031","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher proves in *Physics* VIII that, if the power of some body were infinite, it would move in an instant. But God does not move in an instant; rather, according to Augustine, in *The Literal Meaning of Genesis* VIII, He moves the spiritual creature *through time, but the bodily creature through place and time*. Therefore His power is not infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0032","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0033","text":"But on the contrary, Hilary says, in *On the Trinity* VIII, that God is of immeasurable strength, living, powerful. But everything immeasurable is infinite. Therefore the divine power is infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0034","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","text":"I answer that, as has already been said, active power is found in God insofar as He is actual. But His being is infinite, insofar as it is not limited by anything receiving it; as is clear from what was said above, when the infinity of the divine essence was treated. Hence it is necessary that God's active power be infinite. For in all agents this is found: that the more perfectly an agent has the form by which it acts, the greater is its power in acting. Thus, the hotter a thing is, the greater power it has for heating; and it would indeed have infinite power for heating if its heat were infinite. Hence, since the divine essence itself, through which God acts, is infinite, as was shown above, it follows that His power is infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0036","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0037","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher speaks of the infinite which is on the side of matter not terminated by form; of this sort is the infinite that belongs to quantity. But the divine essence is not infinite in this way, as was shown above; and consequently neither is its power. Hence it does not follow that it is imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0038","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","text":"To the second it must be said that the power of a univocal agent is wholly manifested in its effect; for the generative power of a human being can do nothing more than generate a human being. But the power of a non-univocal agent is not wholly manifested in the production of its effect, just as the power of the sun is not wholly manifested in the production of some animal generated from putrefaction. Now it is manifest that God is not a univocal agent, for nothing else can agree with Him either in species or in genus, as was shown above. Hence it remains that His effect is always less than His power. Therefore it is not necessary that God's infinite power be manifested in this, that He produces an infinite effect. And yet, even if He produced no effect, God's power would not be in vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040","text":"For that which is ordered to an end which it does not attain is in vain; but God's power is not ordered to an effect as to an end, but rather it itself is the end of its effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0041","text":"[I q. 25 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0042","text":"To the third it must be said that the Philosopher proves in *Physics* VIII that, if some body had infinite power, it would move in no time. Yet he shows that the power of the mover of the heaven is infinite, because it can move for an infinite time. Therefore, according to his intention, it follows that, if infinite power of a body existed, it would move in no time, but not the power of an incorporeal mover. The reason for this is that a body moving another body is a univocal agent. Hence the whole power of the agent must be manifested in the motion. Therefore, since the greater the power of a moving body is, the more swiftly it moves, it is necessary that, if it were infinite, it should move disproportionately more swiftly, which is to move in no time. But an incorporeal mover is a non-univocal agent. Hence it is not necessary that its whole power be manifested in motion in such a way that it moves in no time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0043","text":"And especially because it moves according to the disposition of its will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0045","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0046","text":"We proceed to the third as follows. It seems that God is not omnipotent. For to be moved and to undergo something belong to all things. But God cannot do this, for He is immovable, as was said above. Therefore He is not omnipotent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0047","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0048","text":"Moreover, to sin is to do something. But God cannot sin, nor deny Himself, as is said in II Timothy II. Therefore God is not omnipotent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0049","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0050","text":"Moreover, it is said of God that *he manifests his omnipotence most greatly by sparing and having mercy*. Therefore, the ultimate thing that the divine power can do is to spare and to have mercy. But something is much greater than to spare and to have mercy, such as to create another world, or something of this kind. Therefore God is not omnipotent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0051","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0052","text":"Moreover, on that passage, I Cor. I, *God has made foolish the wisdom of this world*, the Gloss says: *God made the wisdom of this world foolish by showing possible what it judged impossible*. Hence it seems that something ought not to be judged possible or impossible according to lower causes, as the wisdom of this world judges, but according to the divine power. Therefore, if God is omnipotent, all things will be possible. Therefore nothing is impossible. But when the impossible is removed, the necessary is removed; for that which necessarily is, is impossible not to be. Therefore, if God is omnipotent, nothing will be necessary among things. But this is impossible. Therefore God is not omnipotent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0053","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0054","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Luke I: *No word shall be impossible with God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0055","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","text":"I answer that, all commonly confess that God is omnipotent. But it seems difficult to assign the account of omnipotence. For there can be doubt as to what is comprehended under this distribution, when it is said that God can do all things. But if one considers rightly, since power is spoken of in relation to possibles, when God is said to be able to do all things, nothing is understood more rightly than that he can do all possible things, and for this reason is called omnipotent. Now “possible” is spoken of in two ways, according to the Philosopher, in Metaphysics V. In one way, in relation to some power, as that which is subject to human power is said to be possible for a human being. But God cannot be said to be omnipotent because he can do all things that are possible to created nature, because divine power extends to more things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","text":"But if it should be said that God is omnipotent because he can do all things that are possible to his power, there will be circularity in the manifestation of omnipotence; for this will be nothing other than to say that God is omnipotent because he can do all that he can do. It remains, therefore, that God is called omnipotent because he can do all things possible absolutely, which is the other way of speaking of the possible. Now something is called possible or impossible absolutely from the relation of the terms: possible indeed, because the predicate does not conflict with the subject, as that Socrates sits; but impossible absolutely, because the predicate conflicts with the subject, as that a human being is an ass.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","text":"But it must be considered that, since every agent acts so as to produce its like, to every active power there corresponds the possible as its proper object, according to the character of that act on which the active power is founded; just as the power of heating is referred, as to its proper object, to that which can be heated. But the divine being, upon which the notion of divine power is founded, is infinite being, not limited to any genus of being, but prepossessing in itself the perfection of all being. Hence whatever can have the character of being is contained under the absolutely possible things, with respect to which God is called omnipotent. But nothing is opposed to the character of being except non-being. Therefore this conflicts with the character of the absolutely possible, which is subject to divine omnipotence: that it involves being and non-being simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","text":"For this is not subject to omnipotence, not on account of a defect of divine power, but because it cannot have the character either of something makeable or of something possible. Therefore, whatever things do not involve a contradiction are contained under those possible things with respect to which God is called omnipotent. But those things which involve a contradiction are not contained under divine omnipotence, because they cannot have the character of possible things. Hence it is more fittingly said that they cannot be done than that God cannot do them. Nor is this contrary to the word of the Angel saying, *No word shall be impossible with God*. For that which involves a contradiction cannot be a word, because no intellect can conceive it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0060","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God is called omnipotent according to active power, not according to passive power, as has been said. Hence, that he cannot be moved and suffer does not conflict with omnipotence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0062","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","text":"To the second, it must be said that to sin is to fall short of perfect action; hence, to be able to sin is to be able to fall short in acting, which conflicts with omnipotence. And for this reason God, who is omnipotent, cannot sin. Although the Philosopher says, in Topics IV, that God and the virtuous man can do wicked things. But this is understood either under a condition whose antecedent is impossible, as if we should say that God can do wicked things if he wills; for nothing prevents a conditional from being true whose antecedent and consequent are impossible, as when it is said: if a human being is an ass, he has four feet. Or it is understood to mean that God can do certain things which now appear wicked, but which, if he did them, would be good. Or he speaks according to the common opinion of the gentiles, who said that human beings were transferred into gods, such as Jupiter or Mercury.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0064","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0065","text":"To the third, it must be said that God’s omnipotence is shown most greatly in sparing and having mercy, because by this it is shown that God has supreme power, in that he freely forgives sins; for it does not belong to one bound by the law of a superior freely to remit sins. Or because, by sparing human beings and having mercy on them, he leads them to a participation in the infinite good, which is the ultimate effect of divine power. Or because, as was said above, the effect of divine mercy is the foundation of all divine works; for nothing is due to anyone except on account of that which has been given to him by God without being due. But divine omnipotence is manifested most greatly in this, that the first establishment of all goods belongs to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0066","text":"[I q. 25 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the absolutely possible is not called such according to either higher causes or lower causes, but according to itself. But the possible that is spoken of according to some power is called possible according to its proximate cause. Hence those things which are naturally apt to be done immediately by God alone, such as to create, to justify, and things of this kind, are called possible according to the higher cause; whereas those which are naturally apt to be done by lower causes are called possible according to lower causes. For according to the condition of the proximate cause, the effect has contingency or necessity, as was said above. But the wisdom of the world is accounted foolish in this: that it judged those things which are impossible to nature to be impossible even to God. And thus it is clear that God’s omnipotence does not exclude impossibility and necessity from things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0068","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0069","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0070","text":"The fourth is approached as follows. It seems that God can bring it about that past things have not been. For what is impossible through itself is more impossible than what is impossible incidentally. But God can do that which is impossible through itself, such as to make a blind man see, or to raise the dead. Therefore, much more can God do that which is impossible incidentally. But that past things have not been is impossible incidentally; for it happens that it is impossible for Socrates not to have run, from the fact that it is past. Therefore God can bring it about that past things have not been.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0071","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0072","text":"Moreover, whatever God was able to do, he is able to do, since his power is not diminished. But before Socrates ran, God was able to bring it about that he would not run. Therefore, after he has run, God can bring it about that he has not run.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0073","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0074","text":"Moreover, charity is a greater virtue than virginity. But God can restore lost charity. Therefore, virginity as well. Therefore, He can make it the case that she who was corrupted was not corrupted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0075","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0076","text":"But on the contrary, Jerome says, *although God can do all things, He cannot make an incorrupt woman out of one who has been corrupted*. Therefore, by the same reasoning, He cannot make, of anything else that is past, that it was not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0077","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as stated above, that which implies a contradiction does not fall under God's omnipotence. But that past things should not have been implies a contradiction. For just as it implies a contradiction to say that Socrates is sitting and is not sitting, so too that he sat and did not sit. But to say that he sat is to say that it is past; and to say that he did not sit is to say that it was not. Hence, that past things should not have been is not subject to divine power. And this is what Augustine says, against Faustus: *whoever says thus, If God is omnipotent, let Him make the things that have been done not to have been done, does not see that he is saying this: If God is omnipotent, let Him make those things that are true, precisely insofar as they are true, to be false*. And the Philosopher says, in Ethics VI, that *in this alone is God deprived: to make ungenerated those things which have been done*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0079","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0080","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although that past things should not have been is impossible accidentally, if what is past is considered, namely, Socrates' running; nevertheless, if the past is considered under the aspect of pastness, its not having been is impossible not only through itself, but absolutely, as implying a contradiction. And thus it is more impossible than for a dead person to rise again, which does not imply a contradiction, and which is called impossible according to a certain power, namely, natural power. For such impossibilities are subject to divine power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0081","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082","text":"To the second, it must be said that just as God, as regards the perfection of divine power, can do all things, but certain things are not subject to His power because they fall short of the character of possibles; so, if the immutability of divine power is considered, whatever God was able to do, He is able to do. Yet some things formerly had the character of possibles, while they were to be done, which now fall short of the character of possibles, while they have been done. And thus God is said not to be able to do them, because they cannot be done.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0083","text":"[I q. 25 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0084","text":"To the third, it must be said that God can remove every corruption of mind and body from a corrupted woman; nevertheless, this cannot be removed from her: that she was not corrupted. Just as He likewise cannot remove from some sinner the fact that he did not sin, and that he did not lose charity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0085","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0086","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0087","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that God cannot do anything except what He does. For God cannot do what He did not foreknow and preordain that He would do. But He did not foreknow or preordain that He would do anything except what He does. Therefore, He cannot do anything except what He does.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0088","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0089","text":"Moreover, God cannot do except what He ought to do, and what it is just to be done. But God ought not to do the things He does not do, nor is it just that He do the things He does not do. Therefore, God cannot do anything except what He does.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0090","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0091","text":"Moreover, God cannot do except what is good, and fitting for the things made. But for the things made by God, it is neither good nor fitting to be otherwise than they are. Therefore, God cannot do anything except what He does.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0092","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0093","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Matthew XXVI: *Can I not ask my Father, and He will at once provide me with more than twelve legions of Angels?* Yet He Himself was not asking, nor was the Father providing them to resist the Jews. Therefore, God can do what He does not do.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0094","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","text":"I answer that it must be said that concerning this some have erred in two ways. For some posited that God acts as though from a necessity of nature: so that, just as from the action of natural things there cannot result things other than those which occur—for instance, from human seed, a human being; from olive seed, an olive—so from the divine operation other things, or another order of things, cannot flow forth than as it now is. But above we showed that God does not act as though from a necessity of nature, but that His will is the cause of all things; nor is that will itself naturally and necessarily determined to these things. Hence, this course of things in no way proceeds from God with such necessity that other things could not proceed. But others said that divine power is determined to this course of things by reason of the order of divine wisdom and justice, without which God does nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","text":"But since the power of God, which is His essence, is not other than God's wisdom, it can fittingly indeed be said that there is nothing in God's power which is not in the order of divine wisdom, for divine wisdom comprehends the whole power of power. Yet the order bestowed upon things by divine wisdom, in which the character of justice consists, as was said above, does not equal divine wisdom, such that divine wisdom is limited to this order. For it is manifest that the whole character of order which a wise man imposes upon the things made by him is taken from the end. Therefore, when the end is proportioned to the things made for the end, the wisdom of the maker is limited to some determinate order. But divine goodness is an end disproportionately exceeding created things. Hence divine wisdom is not determined to any certain order of things, such that another course of things could not flow forth from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0097","text":"Hence it must be said simply that God can make things other than those which He makes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0098","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that in us, in whom power and essence are one thing and will and intellect another, and again intellect is one thing and wisdom another, and will one thing and justice another, there can be something in power which cannot be in a just will or in a wise intellect. But in God, power and essence and will and intellect and wisdom and justice are the same. Hence nothing can be in the divine power which cannot be in His just will and His wise intellect. Nevertheless, because the will is not determined by necessity to these or those things, except perhaps by supposition, as was said above; nor are God's wisdom and justice determined to this order, as was said above; nothing prevents there being something in God's power which He does not will, and which is not contained under the order that He has established for things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","text":"And because power is understood as executing, whereas will as commanding, and intellect and wisdom as directing, what is attributed to power considered in itself is said to be what God can do according to absolute power. And of this sort is everything in which the account of being can be preserved, as was said above. But what is attributed to the divine power insofar as it executes the command of a just will is said to be what God can do by ordered power. Accordingly, it must be said that God can make, by absolute power, things other than those which He foreknew and preordained that He would make; yet it cannot be that He makes any things which He did not foreknow and preordain that He would make. For making itself is subject to foreknowledge and preordination, but being able is not, since this is natural. For God makes something because He wills it; yet He is not able because He wills, but because He is such in His nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0101","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102","text":"To the second, it must be said that God owes nothing to anyone except Himself. Hence, when it is said that God cannot do anything except what He ought, nothing else is signified than that God cannot do anything except what is fitting and just for Him. But what I call fitting and just can be understood in two ways. In one way, such that what I call fitting and just is understood first to be joined with the verb “is,” so that it is restricted to standing for present things; and thus it is referred to power. And thus what is said is false; for the sense is: God cannot do except what is now fitting and just. But if it is first joined with the verb “can,” which has an extending force, and afterwards with the verb “is,” a certain confused present will be signified, and the statement will be true, in this sense: God cannot do except that which, if He were to do it, would be fitting and just.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0103","text":"[I q. 25 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0104","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although this course of things is determined by these things which now exist, nevertheless divine wisdom and power are not limited to this course. Hence, although, for these things which now exist, no other course would be good and fitting, nevertheless God could make other things and impose another order upon them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0105","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0106","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0107","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth article. It seems that God cannot make better the things that He makes. For whatever God makes, He makes most powerfully and most wisely. But something is made better insofar as it is made more powerfully and more wisely. Therefore God cannot make anything better than He makes it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0108","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0109","text":"Further, Augustine, Against Maximinus, argues thus: *if God was able, and was unwilling, to beget a Son equal to Himself, He was envious*. By the same reasoning, if God was able to make things better than He made them, and was unwilling, He was envious. But envy is altogether excluded from God. Therefore God made each thing best. Therefore God cannot make anything better than He made it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0110","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0111","text":"Further, that which is supremely and very good cannot be made better, because there is nothing greater than the greatest. But, as Augustine says in the Enchiridion, *the individual things which God made are good, but all things together are very good, because from all of them consists the admirable beauty of the universe*. Therefore the good of the universe cannot be made better by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0112","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0113","text":"Further, the man Christ is full of grace and truth, and has the Spirit not by measure, and thus cannot be better. Created beatitude is also called the highest good, and thus cannot be better. The blessed Virgin Mary also is exalted above all the choirs of Angels, and thus cannot be better. Therefore God cannot make better all the things that He has made.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0114","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0115","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Ephesians III that *God is able to do all things more abundantly than we ask or understand*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0116","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","text":"I answer that the goodness of a thing is twofold. One kind is of the essence of the thing, just as being rational is of the essence of man. And as regards this good, God cannot make any thing better than it is, although He can make some other thing better than it. Thus He also cannot make a quaternary greater, because if it were greater, it would no longer be a quaternary, but another number. For the addition of a substantial difference in definitions is related as the addition of a unit in numbers, as is said in Metaphysics VIII. Another goodness is outside the essence of the thing, as the good of man is to be virtuous or wise. And according to such goodness, God can make the things made by Him better. But, speaking simply, God can make another thing better than any thing made by Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0118","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that, when it is said that God can make something better than He makes it, if “better” is a noun, it is true, for He can make another thing better than any thing. Yet He can make the same thing better in a certain respect, and in a certain respect not, as has been said. But if “better” is an adverb, and conveys a mode on the part of the maker, then God cannot make better than as He makes, because He cannot make from greater wisdom and goodness. But if it conveys a mode on the part of the thing made, then He can make better, because He can give to things made by Him a better mode of being as regards accidental features, though not as regards essential ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0120","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0121","text":"To the second, it must be said that it belongs to the nature of a son that, when he has come to perfection, he be equal to his father; but it does not belong to the nature of any creature that it be better than it was made by God. Hence the reasoning is not similar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0122","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","text":"To the third, it must be said that the universe, given these things, cannot be better, on account of the most fitting order attributed to these things by God, in which the good of the universe consists. If any one of these things were better, the proportion of the order would be corrupted, just as, if one string were tightened more than it ought to be, the melody of the lyre would be corrupted. God could nevertheless make other things, or add others to these things already made, and thus that universe would be better.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0124","text":"[I q. 25 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0125","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Christ's humanity, from the fact that it is united to God, and created beatitude, from the fact that it is the enjoyment of God, and the Blessed Virgin, from the fact that she is the Mother of God, have a certain infinite dignity from the infinite good that is God. And in this respect, nothing can be made better than they, just as nothing can be better than God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0125"]}],"source_digest":"49370edac0d5417cdaa495960727c12a09d8593e99f11c83ade5044d85112d0e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 25"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 25","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18","title":"First Part · Question 26","order":27,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_18","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 26 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29544)\n\nUltimo autem, post considerationem eorum quae ad divinae essentiae unitatem pertinent, considerandum est de divina beatitudine. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum beatitudo Deo competat. Secundo, secundum quid dicitur Deus esse beatus, utrum secundum actum intellectus. Tertio, utrum sit essentialiter beatitudo cuiuslibet beati. Quarto, utrum in eius beatitudine omnis beatitudo includatur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 26 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29545)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod beatitudo Deo non conveniat. *Beatitudo enim*, secundum Boetium, in III de Consol., *est status omnium bonorum aggregatione perfectus*. Sed aggregatio bonorum non habet locum in Deo, sicut nec compositio. Ergo Deo non convenit beatitudo.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29546)\n\nPraeterea, beatitudo, sive felicitas, est praemium virtutis, secundum philosophum, in I Ethic. Sed Deo non convenit praemium, sicut nec meritum. Ergo nec beatitudo.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29547)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit apostolus, I ad Tim. ultimo, *quem suis temporibus ostendet Deus beatus et solus potens, rex regum et dominus dominantium*.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29548)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod beatitudo maxime Deo competit. Nihil enim aliud sub nomine beatitudinis intelligitur, nisi bonum perfectum intellectualis naturae; cuius est suam sufficientiam cognoscere in bono quod habet; et cui competit ut ei contingat aliquid vel bene vel male, et sit suarum operationum domina. Utrumque autem istorum excellentissime Deo convenit, scilicet perfectum esse, et intelligentem. Unde beatitudo maxime convenit Deo.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29549)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aggregatio bonorum est in Deo non per modum compositionis, sed per modum simplicitatis, quia quae in creaturis multiplicia sunt, in Deo praeexistunt simpliciter et unite, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29550)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod esse praemium virtutis accidit beatitudini vel felicitati, inquantum aliquis beatitudinem acquirit, sicut esse terminum generationis accidit enti, inquantum exit de potentia in actum. Sicut igitur Deus habet esse, quamvis non generetur; ita habet beatitudinem, quamvis non mereatur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 26 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29551)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non dicatur beatus secundum intellectum. Beatitudo enim est summum bonum. Sed bonum dicitur in Deo secundum essentiam, quia bonum respicit esse, quod est secundum essentiam, secundum Boetium, in libro de Hebdomad. Ergo et beatitudo dicitur in Deo secundum essentiam, et non secundum intellectum.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29552)\n\nPraeterea, beatitudo habet rationem finis. Finis autem est obiectum voluntatis, sicut et bonum. Ergo beatitudo dicitur in Deo secundum voluntatem, et non secundum intellectum.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29553)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, XXXII Moralium, *ipse gloriosus est, qui, dum seipso perfruitur, accedentis laudis indigens non est*. Esse autem gloriosum significat esse beatum. Cum igitur Deo fruamur secundum intellectum, quia visio est tota merces, ut dicit Augustinus, videtur quod beatitudo dicatur in Deo secundum intellectum.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29554)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod beatitudo, sicut dictum est, significat bonum perfectum intellectualis naturae. Et inde est quod, sicut unaquaeque res appetit suam perfectionem, ita et intellectualis natura naturaliter appetit esse beata. Id autem quod est perfectissimum in qualibet intellectuali natura, est intellectualis operatio, secundum quam capit quodammodo omnia. Unde cuiuslibet intellectualis naturae creatae beatitudo consistit in intelligendo. In Deo autem non est aliud esse et intelligere secundum rem, sed tantum secundum intelligentiae rationem. Attribuenda ergo est Deo beatitudo secundum intellectum, sicut et aliis beatis, qui per assimilationem ad beatitudinem ipsius, beati dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29555)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ex illa ratione probatur quod Deus sit beatus secundum suam essentiam, non autem quod beatitudo ei conveniat secundum rationem essentiae, sed magis secundum rationem intellectus.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29556)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod beatitudo, cum sit bonum, est obiectum voluntatis. Obiectum autem praeintelligitur actui potentiae. Unde, secundum modum intelligendi, prius est beatitudo divina, quam actus voluntatis in ea requiescentis. Et hoc non potest esse nisi actus intellectus. Unde in actu intellectus attenditur beatitudo.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 26 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29557)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus sit beatitudo cuiuslibet beati. Deus enim est summum bonum, ut supra ostensum est. Impossibile est autem esse plura summa bona, ut etiam ex superioribus patet. Cum igitur de ratione beatitudinis sit, quod sit summum bonum, videtur quod beatitudo non sit aliud quam Deus.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29558)\n\nPraeterea, beatitudo est finis rationalis naturae ultimus. Sed esse ultimum finem rationalis naturae, soli Deo convenit. Ergo beatitudo cuiuslibet beati est solus Deus.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29559)\n\nSed contra, beatitudo unius est maior beatitudine alterius, secundum illud I Cor. XV, *stella differt a stella in claritate*. Sed Deo nihil est maius. Ergo beatitudo est aliquid aliud quam Deus.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29560)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod beatitudo intellectualis naturae consistit in actu intellectus. In quo duo possunt considerari, scilicet obiectum actus, quod est intelligibile; et ipse actus, qui est intelligere. Si igitur beatitudo consideretur ex parte ipsius obiecti, sic solus Deus est beatitudo, quia ex hoc solo est aliquis beatus, quod Deum intelligit; secundum illud Augustini, in V libro Confess., *beatus est qui te novit, etiam si alia ignoret*. Sed ex parte actus intelligentis, beatitudo est quid creatum in creaturis beatis, in Deo autem est etiam secundum hoc, aliquid increatum.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29561)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod beatitudo, quantum ad obiectum, est summum bonum simpliciter, sed quantum ad actum, in creaturis beatis, est summum bonum, non simpliciter, sed in genere bonorum participabilium a creatura.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29562)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod finis est duplex, scilicet cuius et quo, ut philosophus dicit, scilicet ipsa res, et usus rei, sicut avaro est finis pecunia, et acquisitio pecuniae. Creaturae igitur rationalis est quidem Deus finis ultimus ut res; beatitudo autem creata ut usus, vel magis fruitio, rei.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 26 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29563)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod beatitudo divina non complectatur omnes beatitudines. Sunt enim quaedam beatitudines falsae. Sed in Deo nihil potest esse falsum. Ergo divina beatitudo non complectitur omnem beatitudinem.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29564)\n\nPraeterea, quaedam beatitudo, secundum quosdam, consistit in rebus corporalibus, sicut in voluptatibus, divitiis, et huiusmodi, quae quidem Deo convenire non possunt, cum sit incorporeus. Ergo beatitudo eius non complectitur omnem beatitudinem.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29565)\n\nSed contra est quod beatitudo est perfectio quaedam. Divina autem perfectio complectitur omnem perfectionem, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo divina beatitudo complectitur omnem beatitudinem.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29566)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidquid est desiderabile in quacumque beatitudine, vel vera vel falsa, totum eminentius in divina beatitudine praeexistit. De contemplativa enim felicitate, habet continuam et certissimam contemplationem sui et omnium aliorum, de activa vero, gubernationem totius universi. De terrena vero felicitate, quae consistit in voluptate, divitiis, potestate, dignitate et fama, secundum Boetium, in III de Consol., habet gaudium de se et de omnibus aliis, pro delectatione, pro divitiis, habet omnimodam sufficientiam, quam divitiae promittunt, pro potestate, omnipotentiam, pro dignitate, omnium regimen, pro fama vero, admirationem totius creaturae.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29567)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod beatitudo aliqua secundum hoc est falsa, secundum quod deficit a ratione verae beatitudinis, et sic non est in Deo. Sed quidquid habet de similitudine, quantumcumque tenui, beatitudinis, totum praeexistit in divina beatitudine.\n\n[I q. 26 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29568)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bona quae sunt in corporalibus corporaliter, in Deo sunt spiritualiter, secundum modum suum. Et haec dicta sufficiant de his quae pertinent ad divinae essentiae unitatem.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0002","text":"[I q. 26 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0003","text":"Ultimo autem, post considerationem eorum quae ad divinae essentiae unitatem pertinent, considerandum est de divina beatitudine. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum beatitudo Deo competat. Secundo, secundum quid dicitur Deus esse beatus, utrum secundum actum intellectus. Tertio, utrum sit essentialiter beatitudo cuiuslibet beati. Quarto, utrum in eius beatitudine omnis beatitudo includatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0005","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod beatitudo Deo non conveniat. *Beatitudo enim*, secundum Boetium, in III de Consol., *est status omnium bonorum aggregatione perfectus*. Sed aggregatio bonorum non habet locum in Deo, sicut nec compositio. Ergo Deo non convenit beatitudo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0007","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0008","text":"Praeterea, beatitudo, sive felicitas, est praemium virtutis, secundum philosophum, in I Ethic. Sed Deo non convenit praemium, sicut nec meritum. Ergo nec beatitudo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0009","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0010","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit apostolus, I ad Tim. ultimo, *quem suis temporibus ostendet Deus beatus et solus potens, rex regum et dominus dominantium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0011","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod beatitudo maxime Deo competit. Nihil enim aliud sub nomine beatitudinis intelligitur, nisi bonum perfectum intellectualis naturae; cuius est suam sufficientiam cognoscere in bono quod habet; et cui competit ut ei contingat aliquid vel bene vel male, et sit suarum operationum domina. Utrumque autem istorum excellentissime Deo convenit, scilicet perfectum esse, et intelligentem. Unde beatitudo maxime convenit Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0013","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aggregatio bonorum est in Deo non per modum compositionis, sed per modum simplicitatis, quia quae in creaturis multiplicia sunt, in Deo praeexistunt simpliciter et unite, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0015","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod esse praemium virtutis accidit beatitudini vel felicitati, inquantum aliquis beatitudinem acquirit, sicut esse terminum generationis accidit enti, inquantum exit de potentia in actum. Sicut igitur Deus habet esse, quamvis non generetur; ita habet beatitudinem, quamvis non mereatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0017","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0018","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0019","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non dicatur beatus secundum intellectum. Beatitudo enim est summum bonum. Sed bonum dicitur in Deo secundum essentiam, quia bonum respicit esse, quod est secundum essentiam, secundum Boetium, in libro de Hebdomad. Ergo et beatitudo dicitur in Deo secundum essentiam, et non secundum intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0020","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0021","text":"Praeterea, beatitudo habet rationem finis. Finis autem est obiectum voluntatis, sicut et bonum. Ergo beatitudo dicitur in Deo secundum voluntatem, et non secundum intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0022","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0023","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, XXXII Moralium, *ipse gloriosus est, qui, dum seipso perfruitur, accedentis laudis indigens non est*. Esse autem gloriosum significat esse beatum. Cum igitur Deo fruamur secundum intellectum, quia visio est tota merces, ut dicit Augustinus, videtur quod beatitudo dicatur in Deo secundum intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0024","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod beatitudo, sicut dictum est, significat bonum perfectum intellectualis naturae. Et inde est quod, sicut unaquaeque res appetit suam perfectionem, ita et intellectualis natura naturaliter appetit esse beata. Id autem quod est perfectissimum in qualibet intellectuali natura, est intellectualis operatio, secundum quam capit quodammodo omnia. Unde cuiuslibet intellectualis naturae creatae beatitudo consistit in intelligendo. In Deo autem non est aliud esse et intelligere secundum rem, sed tantum secundum intelligentiae rationem. Attribuenda ergo est Deo beatitudo secundum intellectum, sicut et aliis beatis, qui per assimilationem ad beatitudinem ipsius, beati dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0026","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ex illa ratione probatur quod Deus sit beatus secundum suam essentiam, non autem quod beatitudo ei conveniat secundum rationem essentiae, sed magis secundum rationem intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0028","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod beatitudo, cum sit bonum, est obiectum voluntatis. Obiectum autem praeintelligitur actui potentiae. Unde, secundum modum intelligendi, prius est beatitudo divina, quam actus voluntatis in ea requiescentis. Et hoc non potest esse nisi actus intellectus. Unde in actu intellectus attenditur beatitudo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0030","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0031","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0032","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus sit beatitudo cuiuslibet beati. Deus enim est summum bonum, ut supra ostensum est. Impossibile est autem esse plura summa bona, ut etiam ex superioribus patet. Cum igitur de ratione beatitudinis sit, quod sit summum bonum, videtur quod beatitudo non sit aliud quam Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0033","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0034","text":"Praeterea, beatitudo est finis rationalis naturae ultimus. Sed esse ultimum finem rationalis naturae, soli Deo convenit. Ergo beatitudo cuiuslibet beati est solus Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0035","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0036","text":"Sed contra, beatitudo unius est maior beatitudine alterius, secundum illud I Cor. XV, *stella differt a stella in claritate*. Sed Deo nihil est maius. Ergo beatitudo est aliquid aliud quam Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0037","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod beatitudo intellectualis naturae consistit in actu intellectus. In quo duo possunt considerari, scilicet obiectum actus, quod est intelligibile; et ipse actus, qui est intelligere. Si igitur beatitudo consideretur ex parte ipsius obiecti, sic solus Deus est beatitudo, quia ex hoc solo est aliquis beatus, quod Deum intelligit; secundum illud Augustini, in V libro Confess., *beatus est qui te novit, etiam si alia ignoret*. Sed ex parte actus intelligentis, beatitudo est quid creatum in creaturis beatis, in Deo autem est etiam secundum hoc, aliquid increatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0039","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod beatitudo, quantum ad obiectum, est summum bonum simpliciter, sed quantum ad actum, in creaturis beatis, est summum bonum, non simpliciter, sed in genere bonorum participabilium a creatura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0041","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod finis est duplex, scilicet cuius et quo, ut philosophus dicit, scilicet ipsa res, et usus rei, sicut avaro est finis pecunia, et acquisitio pecuniae. Creaturae igitur rationalis est quidem Deus finis ultimus ut res; beatitudo autem creata ut usus, vel magis fruitio, rei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0043","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0044","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0045","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod beatitudo divina non complectatur omnes beatitudines. Sunt enim quaedam beatitudines falsae. Sed in Deo nihil potest esse falsum. Ergo divina beatitudo non complectitur omnem beatitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0046","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0047","text":"Praeterea, quaedam beatitudo, secundum quosdam, consistit in rebus corporalibus, sicut in voluptatibus, divitiis, et huiusmodi, quae quidem Deo convenire non possunt, cum sit incorporeus. Ergo beatitudo eius non complectitur omnem beatitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0048","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod beatitudo est perfectio quaedam. Divina autem perfectio complectitur omnem perfectionem, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo divina beatitudo complectitur omnem beatitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0050","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidquid est desiderabile in quacumque beatitudine, vel vera vel falsa, totum eminentius in divina beatitudine praeexistit. De contemplativa enim felicitate, habet continuam et certissimam contemplationem sui et omnium aliorum, de activa vero, gubernationem totius universi. De terrena vero felicitate, quae consistit in voluptate, divitiis, potestate, dignitate et fama, secundum Boetium, in III de Consol., habet gaudium de se et de omnibus aliis, pro delectatione, pro divitiis, habet omnimodam sufficientiam, quam divitiae promittunt, pro potestate, omnipotentiam, pro dignitate, omnium regimen, pro fama vero, admirationem totius creaturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0052","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod beatitudo aliqua secundum hoc est falsa, secundum quod deficit a ratione verae beatitudinis, et sic non est in Deo. Sed quidquid habet de similitudine, quantumcumque tenui, beatitudinis, totum praeexistit in divina beatitudine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0054","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bona quae sunt in corporalibus corporaliter, in Deo sunt spiritualiter, secundum modum suum. Et haec dicta sufficiant de his quae pertinent ad divinae essentiae unitatem.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"36816b4c627322a14a7041783c83f1c8aa22194a91afbc5e7e1fbed76683c082","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140736723187Z_63e2348d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 26","source_sha256":"36816b4c627322a14a7041783c83f1c8aa22194a91afbc5e7e1fbed76683c082","sha256":"1ad1c5467b0f75992b15105701a0b0ea298c572e8dded5c49a6410b692ebcb07","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:07:36.723227+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0002","text":"[I q. 26 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0003","text":"Lastly, after the consideration of those things that pertain to the unity of the divine essence, we must consider divine beatitude. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether beatitude belongs to God. Second, in what respect God is said to be blessed: whether according to an act of intellect. Third, whether He is essentially the beatitude of every blessed person. Fourth, whether every beatitude is included in His beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0005","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that beatitude does not befit God. For *beatitude*, according to Boethius, in Book III of *The Consolation*, *is a state made perfect by the aggregation of all goods*. But an aggregation of goods has no place in God, just as neither does composition. Therefore beatitude does not befit God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0007","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0008","text":"Further, beatitude, or happiness, is the reward of virtue, according to the Philosopher, in Book I of the *Ethics*. But reward does not befit God, just as neither does merit. Therefore neither does beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0009","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0010","text":"On the contrary, the Apostle says, at the end of I Timothy, *whom in His own times God will show forth, blessed and the only powerful one, King of kings and Lord of lords*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0011","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","text":"I answer that beatitude belongs most of all to God. For nothing other is understood under the name of beatitude than the perfect good of an intellectual nature; whose nature it is to know its own sufficiency in the good that it possesses, and to which it belongs that something may happen to it either well or ill, and that it be mistress of its own operations. Now both of these befit God most excellently, namely, to be perfect and to be intelligent. Hence beatitude most of all befits God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0013","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the aggregation of goods is in God not by way of composition, but by way of simplicity, because those things that are multiple in creatures pre-exist in God simply and unitedly, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0015","text":"[I q. 26 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","text":"To the second, it must be said that being the reward of virtue befalls beatitude or happiness insofar as someone acquires beatitude, just as being the terminus of generation befalls a being insofar as it proceeds from potency to act. Therefore, just as God has being, although He is not generated, so He has beatitude, although He does not merit it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0017","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0018","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0019","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God is not called blessed according to intellect. For beatitude is the highest good. But good is said of God according to essence, because good regards being, which is according to essence, according to Boethius, in the book *De Hebdomadibus*. Therefore beatitude too is said of God according to essence, and not according to intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0020","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0021","text":"Further, beatitude has the character of an end. But an end is the object of the will, as is also the good. Therefore beatitude is said of God according to will, and not according to intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0022","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0023","text":"On the contrary, Gregory says, in Book XXXII of the *Morals*, *He Himself is glorious, who, while enjoying Himself, has no need of praise that is added*. But to be glorious signifies to be blessed. Therefore, since we enjoy God according to intellect, because vision is the whole reward, as Augustine says, it seems that beatitude is said of God according to intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0024","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","text":"I answer that it must be said that beatitude, as has been said, signifies the perfect good of an intellectual nature. And hence it is that, just as each thing desires its own perfection, so too an intellectual nature naturally desires to be happy. But that which is most perfect in any intellectual nature is the intellectual operation, by which it in some way apprehends all things. Hence the beatitude of every created intellectual nature consists in understanding. In God, however, to be and to understand are not distinct in reality, but only according to the notion of understanding. Therefore beatitude must be attributed to God according to intellect, as also to the other blessed, who are called blessed through assimilation to His beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0026","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that from that argument it is proved that God is blessed according to His essence, but not that beatitude belongs to Him according to the notion of essence; rather, according to the notion of intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0028","text":"[I q. 26 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","text":"To the second it must be said that beatitude, since it is a good, is an object of the will. But the object is understood beforehand in relation to the act of a power. Hence, according to the mode of understanding, divine beatitude is prior to the act of the will resting in it. And this can only be an act of intellect. Hence beatitude is considered in the act of intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0030","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0031","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0032","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that God is the beatitude of everyone who is blessed. For God is the highest good, as was shown above. But it is impossible that there be several highest goods, as is also evident from what has preceded. Therefore, since it belongs to the notion of beatitude that it be the highest good, it seems that beatitude is nothing other than God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0033","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0034","text":"Moreover, beatitude is the ultimate end of rational nature. But to be the ultimate end of rational nature belongs to God alone. Therefore the beatitude of everyone who is blessed is God alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0035","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0036","text":"On the contrary, the beatitude of one is greater than the beatitude of another, according to that of I Cor. XV, *star differs from star in glory*. But nothing is greater than God. Therefore beatitude is something other than God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0037","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","text":"I answer that it must be said that the beatitude of an intellectual nature consists in an act of intellect. In this two things can be considered: namely, the object of the act, which is the intelligible; and the act itself, which is understanding. Therefore, if beatitude is considered on the part of the object itself, then God alone is beatitude, because someone is blessed solely by this, that he understands God; according to that saying of Augustine in Book V of the *Confessions*, *blessed is he who knows You, even if he is ignorant of other things*. But on the part of the act of understanding, beatitude is something created in blessed creatures; whereas in God, even in this respect, it is something uncreated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0039","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that beatitude, as regards the object, is the highest good simply; but as regards the act, in blessed creatures, it is the highest good, not simply, but in the genus of goods capable of being participated in by a creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0041","text":"[I q. 26 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","text":"To the second it must be said that an end is twofold, namely, that of which and that by which, as the Philosopher says: namely, the thing itself and the use of the thing; just as for the miser money is an end, and so is the acquisition of money. Therefore, for a rational creature, God is indeed the ultimate end as a thing; but created beatitude is the use, or rather the enjoyment, of the thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0043","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0044","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0045","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that divine beatitude does not encompass all beatitudes. For there are certain false beatitudes. But nothing false can be in God. Therefore divine beatitude does not encompass every beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0046","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0047","text":"Moreover, according to some, a certain beatitude consists in bodily things, such as pleasures, riches, and things of this sort; and these indeed cannot belong to God, since He is incorporeal. Therefore His beatitude does not encompass every beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0048","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0049","text":"On the contrary, beatitude is a certain perfection. But divine perfection embraces every perfection, as was shown above. Therefore divine beatitude embraces every beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0050","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","text":"I answer that whatever is desirable in any beatitude, whether true or false, pre-exists more eminently in divine beatitude in its entirety. For with respect to contemplative happiness, it has continuous and most certain contemplation of itself and of all other things; with respect to active happiness, the governance of the whole universe. But with respect to earthly happiness, which consists in pleasure, riches, power, dignity, and fame, according to Boethius, in Book III of the Consolation, it has joy in itself and in all other things in place of pleasure; in place of riches, it has complete sufficiency, which riches promise; in place of power, omnipotence; in place of dignity, the rule of all things; and in place of fame, the admiration of every creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0052","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a certain beatitude is false insofar as it falls short of the character of true beatitude, and thus it is not in God. But whatever it has of likeness, however slight, to beatitude, pre-exists in its entirety in divine beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0054","text":"[I q. 26 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","text":"To the second, it must be said that the goods which are in bodily things bodily are in God spiritually, according to his mode. And let what has been said suffice concerning those matters pertaining to the unity of the divine essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055"]}],"source_digest":"b75d6a5b59ca2f65fa85de90ec120a20963cb39bd9a519c08c964aead75c1c33","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 26"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 26","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19","title":"First Part · Question 27","order":28,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_19","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 27 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29569)\n\nConsideratis autem his quae ad divinae essentiae unitatem pertinent, restat considerare de his quae pertinent ad Trinitatem personarum in divinis. Et quia personae divinae secundum relationes originis distinguuntur, secundum ordinem doctrinae prius considerandum est de origine, sive de processione, secundo, de relationibus originis; tertio, de personis. Circa processionem quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum processio sit in divinis. Secundo, utrum aliqua processio in divinis generatio dici possit. Tertio, utrum praeter generationem aliqua alia processio possit esse in divinis. Quarto, utrum illa alia processio possit dici generatio. Quinto, utrum in divinis sint plures processiones quam duae.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29570)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non possit esse aliqua processio. Processio enim significat motum ad extra. Sed in divinis nihil est mobile, neque extraneum. Ergo neque processio.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29571)\n\nPraeterea, omne procedens est diversum ab eo a quo procedit. Sed in Deo non est aliqua diversitas, sed summa simplicitas. Ergo in Deo non est processio aliqua.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29572)\n\nPraeterea, procedere ab alio videtur rationi primi principii repugnare. Sed Deus est primum principium, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo in Deo processio locum non habet.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29573)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit dominus, Ioan. VIII, *ego ex Deo processi*.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29574)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod divina Scriptura, in rebus divinis, nominibus ad processionem pertinentibus utitur. Hanc autem processionem diversi diversimode acceperunt. Quidam enim acceperunt hanc processionem secundum quod effectus procedit a causa. Et sic accepit Arius, dicens filium procedere a patre sicut primam eius creaturam, et spiritum sanctum procedere a patre et filio sicut creaturam utriusque. Et secundum hoc, neque filius neque spiritus sanctus esset verus Deus. Quod est contra id quod dicitur de filio, I Ioan. ult., *ut simus in vero filio eius, hic est verus Deus*. Et de spiritu sancto dicitur, I Cor. VI, *nescitis quia membra vestra templum sunt spiritus sancti?* Templum autem habere solius Dei est. Alii vero hanc processionem acceperunt secundum quod causa dicitur procedere in effectum, inquantum vel movet ipsum, vel similitudinem suam ipsi imprimit. Et sic accepit Sabellius, dicens ipsum Deum patrem filium dici, secundum quod carnem assumpsit ex virgine. Et eundem dicit spiritum sanctum, secundum quod creaturam rationalem sanctificat, et ad vitam movet. Huic autem acceptioni repugnant verba domini de se dicentis, Ioan. V, *non potest facere a se filius quidquam*; et multa alia, per quae ostenditur quod non est ipse pater qui filius. Si quis autem diligenter consideret, uterque accepit processionem secundum quod est ad aliquid extra, unde neuter posuit processionem in ipso Deo. Sed, cum omnis processio sit secundum aliquam actionem, sicut secundum actionem quae tendit in exteriorem materiam, est aliqua processio ad extra; ita secundum actionem quae manet in ipso agente, attenditur processio quaedam ad intra. Et hoc maxime patet in intellectu, cuius actio, scilicet intelligere, manet in intelligente. Quicumque enim intelligit, ex hoc ipso quod intelligit, procedit aliquid intra ipsum, quod est conceptio rei intellectae, ex vi intellectiva proveniens, et ex eius notitia procedens. Quam quidem conceptionem vox significat, et dicitur verbum cordis, significatum verbo vocis. Cum autem Deus sit super omnia, ea quae in Deo dicuntur, non sunt intelligenda secundum modum infimarum creaturarum, quae sunt corpora; sed secundum similitudinem supremarum creaturarum, quae sunt intellectuales substantiae; a quibus etiam similitudo accepta deficit a repraesentatione divinorum. Non ergo accipienda est processio secundum quod est in corporalibus, vel per motum localem, vel per actionem alicuius causae in exteriorem effectum, ut calor a calefaciente in calefactum; sed secundum emanationem intelligibilem, utpote verbi intelligibilis a dicente, quod manet in ipso. Et sic fides Catholica processionem ponit in divinis.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29575)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de processione quae est motus localis, vel quae est secundum actionem tendentem in exteriorem materiam, vel in exteriorem effectum, talis autem processio non est in divinis, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29576)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod id quod procedit secundum processionem quae est ad extra, oportet esse diversum ab eo a quo procedit. Sed id quod procedit ad intra processu intelligibili, non oportet esse diversum, imo, quanto perfectius procedit, tanto magis est unum cum eo a quo procedit. Manifestum est enim quod quanto aliquid magis intelligitur, tanto conceptio intellectualis est magis intima intelligenti, et magis unum, nam intellectus secundum hoc quod actu intelligit, secundum hoc fit unum cum intellecto. Unde, cum divinum intelligere sit in fine perfectionis, ut supra dictum est, necesse est quod verbum divinum sit perfecte unum cum eo a quo procedit, absque omni diversitate.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29577)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod procedere a principio ut extraneum et diversum, repugnat rationi primi principii, sed procedere ut intimum et absque diversitate, per modum intelligibilem, includitur in ratione primi principii. Cum enim dicimus aedificatorem principium domus, in ratione huius principii includitur conceptio suae artis, et includeretur in ratione primi principii, si aedificator esset primum principium. Deus autem, qui est primum principium rerum, comparatur ad res creatas ut artifex ad artificiata.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29578)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod processio quae est in divinis, non possit dici generatio. Generatio enim est mutatio de non esse in esse, corruptioni opposita; et utriusque subiectum est materia. Sed nihil horum competit divinis. Ergo non potest generatio esse in divinis.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29579)\n\nPraeterea, in Deo est processio secundum modum intelligibilem, ut dictum est. Sed in nobis talis processio non dicitur generatio. Ergo neque in Deo.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29580)\n\nPraeterea, omne genitum accipit esse a generante. Esse ergo cuiuslibet geniti est esse receptum. Sed nullum esse receptum est per se subsistens. Cum igitur esse divinum sit esse per se subsistens, ut supra probatum est, sequitur quod nullius geniti esse sit esse divinum. Non est ergo generatio in divinis.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29581)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo II, *ego hodie genui te*.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29582)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod processio verbi in divinis dicitur generatio. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod nomine generationis dupliciter utimur. Uno modo, communiter ad omnia generabilia et corruptibilia, et sic generatio nihil aliud est quam mutatio de non esse ad esse. Alio modo, proprie in viventibus, et sic generatio significat originem alicuius viventis a principio vivente coniuncto. Et haec proprie dicitur nativitas. Non tamen omne huiusmodi dicitur genitum, sed proprie quod procedit secundum rationem similitudinis. Unde pilus vel capillus non habet rationem geniti et filii, sed solum quod procedit secundum rationem similitudinis, non cuiuscumque, nam vermes qui generantur in animalibus, non habent rationem generationis et filiationis, licet sit similitudo secundum genus, sed requiritur ad rationem talis generationis, quod procedat secundum rationem similitudinis in natura eiusdem speciei, sicut homo procedit ab homine, et equus ab equo. In viventibus autem quae de potentia in actum vitae procedunt, sicut sunt homines et animalia, generatio utramque generationem includit. Si autem sit aliquod vivens cuius vita non exeat de potentia in actum, processio, si qua in tali vivente invenitur, excludit omnino primam rationem generationis; sed potest habere rationem generationis quae est propria viventium. Sic igitur processio verbi in divinis habet rationem generationis. Procedit enim per modum intelligibilis actionis, quae est operatio vitae, et a principio coniuncto, ut supra iam dictum est, et secundum rationem similitudinis, quia conceptio intellectus est similitudo rei intellectae, et in eadem natura existens, quia in Deo idem est intelligere et esse, ut supra ostensum est. Unde processio verbi in divinis dicitur generatio, et ipsum verbum procedens dicitur filius.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29583)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de generatione secundum rationem primam, prout importat exitum de potentia in actum. Et sic non invenitur in divinis, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29584)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intelligere in nobis non est ipsa substantia intellectus, unde verbum quod secundum intelligibilem operationem procedit in nobis, non est eiusdem naturae cum eo a quo procedit. Unde non proprie et complete competit sibi ratio generationis. Sed intelligere divinum est ipsa substantia intelligentis, ut supra ostensum est, unde verbum procedens procedit ut eiusdem naturae subsistens. Et propter hoc proprie dicitur genitum et filius. Unde et his quae pertinent ad generationem viventium, utitur Scriptura ad significandam processionem divinae sapientiae, scilicet conceptione et partu, dicitur enim ex persona divinae sapientiae, Proverb. VIII, *nondum erant abyssi, et ego iam concepta eram; ante colles ego parturiebar*. Sed in intellectu nostro utimur nomine conceptionis, secundum quod in verbo nostri intellectus invenitur similitudo rei intellectae, licet non inveniatur naturae identitas.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29585)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non omne acceptum est receptum in aliquo subiecto, alioquin non posset dici quod tota substantia rei creatae sit accepta a Deo, cum totius substantiae non sit aliquod subiectum receptivum. Sic igitur id quod est genitum in divinis, accipit esse a generante, non tanquam illud esse sit receptum in aliqua materia vel subiecto (quod repugnat subsistentiae divini esse); sed secundum hoc dicitur esse acceptum, inquantum procedens ab alio habet esse divinum, non quasi aliud ab esse divino existens. In ipsa enim perfectione divini esse continetur et verbum intelligibiliter procedens, et principium verbi; sicut et quaecumque ad eius perfectionem pertinent, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29586)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit in divinis alia processio a generatione verbi. Eadem enim ratione erit aliqua alia processio ab illa alia processione, et sic procederetur in infinitum, quod est inconveniens. Standum est igitur in primo, ut sit una tantum processio in divinis.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29587)\n\nPraeterea, in omni natura invenitur tantum unus modus communicationis illius naturae, et hoc ideo est, quia operationes secundum terminos habent unitatem et diversitatem. Sed processio in divinis non est nisi secundum communicationem divinae naturae. Cum igitur sit una tantum natura divina, ut supra ostensum est, relinquitur quod una sit tantum processio in divinis.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29588)\n\nPraeterea, si sit in divinis alia processio ab intelligibili processione verbi, non erit nisi processio amoris, quae est secundum voluntatis operationem. Sed talis processio non potest esse alia a processione intellectus intelligibili, quia voluntas in Deo non est aliud ab intellectu, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo in Deo non est alia processio praeter processionem verbi.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29589)\n\nSed contra est quod spiritus sanctus procedit a patre, ut dicitur Ioan. XV. Ipse autem est alius a filio, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *rogabo patrem meum, et alium Paracletum dabit vobis*. Ergo in divinis est alia processio praeter processionem verbi.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29590)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in divinis sunt duae processiones, scilicet processio verbi, et quaedam alia. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod in divinis non est processio nisi secundum actionem quae non tendit in aliquid extrinsecum, sed manet in ipso agente. Huiusmodi autem actio in intellectuali natura est actio intellectus et actio voluntatis. Processio autem verbi attenditur secundum actionem intelligibilem. Secundum autem operationem voluntatis invenitur in nobis quaedam alia processio, scilicet processio amoris, secundum quam amatum est in amante, sicut per conceptionem verbi res dicta vel intellecta, est in intelligente. Unde et praeter processionem verbi, ponitur alia processio in divinis, quae est processio amoris.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29591)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod non est necessarium procedere in divinis processionibus in infinitum. Processio enim quae est ad intra in intellectuali natura, terminatur in processione voluntatis.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29592)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod quidquid est in Deo, est Deus, ut supra ostensum est, quod non contingit in aliis rebus. Et ideo per quamlibet processionem quae non est ad extra, communicatur divina natura, non autem aliae naturae.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29593)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet in Deo non sit aliud voluntas et intellectus, tamen de ratione voluntatis et intellectus est, quod processiones quae sunt secundum actionem utriusque, se habeant secundum quendam ordinem. Non enim est processio amoris nisi in ordine ad processionem verbi, nihil enim potest voluntate amari, nisi sit in intellectu conceptum. Sicut igitur attenditur quidam ordo verbi ad principium a quo procedit, licet in divinis sit eadem substantia intellectus et conceptio intellectus; ita, licet in Deo sit idem voluntas et intellectus, tamen, quia de ratione amoris est quod non procedat nisi a conceptione intellectus, habet ordinis distinctionem processio amoris a processione verbi in divinis.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29594)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod processio amoris in divinis sit generatio. Quod enim procedit in similitudine naturae in viventibus, dicitur generatum et nascens. Sed id quod procedit in divinis per modum amoris, procedit in similitudine naturae, alias esset extraneum a natura divina, et sic esset processio ad extra. Ergo quod procedit in divinis per modum amoris, procedit ut genitum et nascens.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29595)\n\nPraeterea, sicut similitudo est de ratione verbi, ita est etiam de ratione amoris, unde dicitur Eccli. XIII, quod *omne animal diligit simile sibi*. Si igitur ratione similitudinis verbo procedenti convenit generari et nasci, videtur etiam quod amori procedenti convenit generari.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29596)\n\nPraeterea, non est in genere quod non est in aliqua eius specie. Si igitur in divinis sit quaedam processio amoris, oportet quod, praeter hoc nomen commune, habeat aliquod nomen speciale. Sed non est aliud nomen dare nisi generatio. Ergo videtur quod processio amoris in divinis sit generatio.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29597)\n\nSed contra est quia secundum hoc sequeretur quod spiritus sanctus, qui procedit ut amor, procederet ut genitus. Quod est contra illud Athanasii, *spiritus sanctus a patre et filio non factus nec creatus nec genitus, sed procedens*.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29598)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod processio amoris in divinis non debet dici generatio. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod haec est differentia inter intellectum et voluntatem, quod intellectus fit in actu per hoc quod res intellecta est in intellectu secundum suam similitudinem, voluntas autem fit in actu, non per hoc quod aliqua similitudo voliti sit in voluntate, sed ex hoc quod voluntas habet quandam inclinationem in rem volitam. Processio igitur quae attenditur secundum rationem intellectus, est secundum rationem similitudinis, et intantum potest habere rationem generationis, quia omne generans generat sibi simile. Processio autem quae attenditur secundum rationem voluntatis, non consideratur secundum rationem similitudinis, sed magis secundum rationem impellentis et moventis in aliquid. Et ideo quod procedit in divinis per modum amoris, non procedit ut genitum vel ut filius, sed magis procedit ut spiritus, quo nomine quaedam vitalis motio et impulsio designatur, prout aliquis ex amore dicitur moveri vel impelli ad aliquid faciendum.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29599)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod quidquid est in divinis, est unum cum divina natura. Unde ex parte huius unitatis non potest accipi propria ratio huius processionis vel illius, secundum quam una distinguatur ab alia, sed oportet quod propria ratio huius vel illius processionis accipiatur secundum ordinem unius processionis ad aliam. Huiusmodi autem ordo attenditur secundum rationem voluntatis et intellectus. Unde secundum horum propriam rationem sortitur in divinis nomen utraque processio, quod imponitur ad propriam rationem rei significandam. Et inde est quod procedens per modum amoris et divinam naturam accipit, et tamen non dicitur natum.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29600)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod similitudo aliter pertinet ad verbum, et aliter ad amorem. Nam ad verbum pertinet inquantum ipsum est quaedam similitudo rei intellectae, sicut genitum est similitudo generantis, sed ad amorem pertinet, non quod ipse amor sit similitudo, sed inquantum similitudo est principium amandi. Unde non sequitur quod amor sit genitus, sed quod genitum sit principium amoris.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29601)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Deum nominare non possumus nisi ex creaturis, ut dictum est supra. Et quia in creaturis communicatio naturae non est nisi per generationem, processio in divinis non habet proprium vel speciale nomen nisi generationis. Unde processio quae non est generatio, remansit sine speciali nomine. Sed potest nominari spiratio, quia est processio spiritus.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29602)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sint plures processiones in divinis quam duae. Sicut enim scientia et voluntas attribuitur Deo, ita et potentia. Si igitur secundum intellectum et voluntatem accipiuntur in Deo duae processiones, videtur quod tertia sit accipienda secundum potentiam.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29603)\n\nPraeterea, bonitas maxime videtur esse principium processionis, cum bonum dicatur diffusivum sui esse. Videtur igitur quod secundum bonitatem aliqua processio in divinis accipi debeat.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29604)\n\nPraeterea, maior est fecunditatis virtus in Deo quam in nobis. Sed in nobis non est tantum una processio verbi, sed multae, quia ex uno verbo in nobis procedit aliud verbum; et similiter ex uno amore alius amor. Ergo et in Deo sunt plures processiones quam duae.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29605)\n\nSed contra est quod in Deo non sunt nisi duo procedentes, scilicet filius et spiritus sanctus. Ergo sunt ibi tantum duae processiones.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29606)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod processiones in divinis accipi non possunt nisi secundum actiones quae in agente manent. Huiusmodi autem actiones in natura intellectuali et divina non sunt nisi duae, scilicet intelligere et velle. Nam sentire, quod etiam videtur esse operatio in sentiente, est extra naturam intellectualem, neque totaliter est remotum a genere actionum quae sunt ad extra; nam sentire perficitur per actionem sensibilis in sensum. Relinquitur igitur quod nulla alia processio possit esse in Deo, nisi verbi et amoris.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29607)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod potentia est principium agendi in aliud, unde secundum potentiam accipitur actio ad extra. Et sic secundum attributum potentiae non accipitur processio divinae personae, sed solum processio creaturarum.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29608)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bonum, sicut dicit Boetius in libro de Hebd., pertinet ad essentiam et non ad operationem, nisi forte sicut obiectum voluntatis. Unde, cum processiones divinas secundum aliquas actiones necesse sit accipere, secundum bonitatem et huiusmodi alia attributa non accipiuntur aliae processiones nisi verbi et amoris, secundum quod Deus suam essentiam, veritatem et bonitatem intelligit et amat.\n\n[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29609)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra habitum est, Deus uno simplici actu omnia intelligit, et similiter omnia vult. Unde in eo non potest esse processio verbi ex verbo, neque amoris ex amore, sed est in eo solum unum verbum perfectum, et unus amor perfectus. Et in hoc eius perfecta fecunditas manifestatur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0002","text":"[I q. 27 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","text":"Consideratis autem his quae ad divinae essentiae unitatem pertinent, restat considerare de his quae pertinent ad Trinitatem personarum in divinis. Et quia personae divinae secundum relationes originis distinguuntur, secundum ordinem doctrinae prius considerandum est de origine, sive de processione, secundo, de relationibus originis; tertio, de personis. Circa processionem quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum processio sit in divinis. Secundo, utrum aliqua processio in divinis generatio dici possit. Tertio, utrum praeter generationem aliqua alia processio possit esse in divinis. Quarto, utrum illa alia processio possit dici generatio. Quinto, utrum in divinis sint plures processiones quam duae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0005","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non possit esse aliqua processio. Processio enim significat motum ad extra. Sed in divinis nihil est mobile, neque extraneum. Ergo neque processio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0007","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0008","text":"Praeterea, omne procedens est diversum ab eo a quo procedit. Sed in Deo non est aliqua diversitas, sed summa simplicitas. Ergo in Deo non est processio aliqua.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0009","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0010","text":"Praeterea, procedere ab alio videtur rationi primi principii repugnare. Sed Deus est primum principium, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo in Deo processio locum non habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0011","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit dominus, Ioan. VIII, *ego ex Deo processi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0013","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod divina Scriptura, in rebus divinis, nominibus ad processionem pertinentibus utitur. Hanc autem processionem diversi diversimode acceperunt. Quidam enim acceperunt hanc processionem secundum quod effectus procedit a causa. Et sic accepit Arius, dicens filium procedere a patre sicut primam eius creaturam, et spiritum sanctum procedere a patre et filio sicut creaturam utriusque. Et secundum hoc, neque filius neque spiritus sanctus esset verus Deus. Quod est contra id quod dicitur de filio, I Ioan. ult., *ut simus in vero filio eius, hic est verus Deus*. Et de spiritu sancto dicitur, I Cor. VI, *nescitis quia membra vestra templum sunt spiritus sancti?* Templum autem habere solius Dei est. Alii vero hanc processionem acceperunt secundum quod causa dicitur procedere in effectum, inquantum vel movet ipsum, vel similitudinem suam ipsi imprimit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","text":"Et sic accepit Sabellius, dicens ipsum Deum patrem filium dici, secundum quod carnem assumpsit ex virgine. Et eundem dicit spiritum sanctum, secundum quod creaturam rationalem sanctificat, et ad vitam movet. Huic autem acceptioni repugnant verba domini de se dicentis, Ioan. V, *non potest facere a se filius quidquam*; et multa alia, per quae ostenditur quod non est ipse pater qui filius. Si quis autem diligenter consideret, uterque accepit processionem secundum quod est ad aliquid extra, unde neuter posuit processionem in ipso Deo. Sed, cum omnis processio sit secundum aliquam actionem, sicut secundum actionem quae tendit in exteriorem materiam, est aliqua processio ad extra; ita secundum actionem quae manet in ipso agente, attenditur processio quaedam ad intra. Et hoc maxime patet in intellectu, cuius actio, scilicet intelligere, manet in intelligente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","text":"Quicumque enim intelligit, ex hoc ipso quod intelligit, procedit aliquid intra ipsum, quod est conceptio rei intellectae, ex vi intellectiva proveniens, et ex eius notitia procedens. Quam quidem conceptionem vox significat, et dicitur verbum cordis, significatum verbo vocis. Cum autem Deus sit super omnia, ea quae in Deo dicuntur, non sunt intelligenda secundum modum infimarum creaturarum, quae sunt corpora; sed secundum similitudinem supremarum creaturarum, quae sunt intellectuales substantiae; a quibus etiam similitudo accepta deficit a repraesentatione divinorum. Non ergo accipienda est processio secundum quod est in corporalibus, vel per motum localem, vel per actionem alicuius causae in exteriorem effectum, ut calor a calefaciente in calefactum; sed secundum emanationem intelligibilem, utpote verbi intelligibilis a dicente, quod manet in ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0017","text":"Et sic fides Catholica processionem ponit in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0018","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de processione quae est motus localis, vel quae est secundum actionem tendentem in exteriorem materiam, vel in exteriorem effectum, talis autem processio non est in divinis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0020","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod id quod procedit secundum processionem quae est ad extra, oportet esse diversum ab eo a quo procedit. Sed id quod procedit ad intra processu intelligibili, non oportet esse diversum, imo, quanto perfectius procedit, tanto magis est unum cum eo a quo procedit. Manifestum est enim quod quanto aliquid magis intelligitur, tanto conceptio intellectualis est magis intima intelligenti, et magis unum, nam intellectus secundum hoc quod actu intelligit, secundum hoc fit unum cum intellecto. Unde, cum divinum intelligere sit in fine perfectionis, ut supra dictum est, necesse est quod verbum divinum sit perfecte unum cum eo a quo procedit, absque omni diversitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0022","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod procedere a principio ut extraneum et diversum, repugnat rationi primi principii, sed procedere ut intimum et absque diversitate, per modum intelligibilem, includitur in ratione primi principii. Cum enim dicimus aedificatorem principium domus, in ratione huius principii includitur conceptio suae artis, et includeretur in ratione primi principii, si aedificator esset primum principium. Deus autem, qui est primum principium rerum, comparatur ad res creatas ut artifex ad artificiata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0025","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod processio quae est in divinis, non possit dici generatio. Generatio enim est mutatio de non esse in esse, corruptioni opposita; et utriusque subiectum est materia. Sed nihil horum competit divinis. Ergo non potest generatio esse in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0027","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0028","text":"Praeterea, in Deo est processio secundum modum intelligibilem, ut dictum est. Sed in nobis talis processio non dicitur generatio. Ergo neque in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0029","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0030","text":"Praeterea, omne genitum accipit esse a generante. Esse ergo cuiuslibet geniti est esse receptum. Sed nullum esse receptum est per se subsistens. Cum igitur esse divinum sit esse per se subsistens, ut supra probatum est, sequitur quod nullius geniti esse sit esse divinum. Non est ergo generatio in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0031","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo II, *ego hodie genui te*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0033","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod processio verbi in divinis dicitur generatio. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod nomine generationis dupliciter utimur. Uno modo, communiter ad omnia generabilia et corruptibilia, et sic generatio nihil aliud est quam mutatio de non esse ad esse. Alio modo, proprie in viventibus, et sic generatio significat originem alicuius viventis a principio vivente coniuncto. Et haec proprie dicitur nativitas. Non tamen omne huiusmodi dicitur genitum, sed proprie quod procedit secundum rationem similitudinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0035","text":"Unde pilus vel capillus non habet rationem geniti et filii, sed solum quod procedit secundum rationem similitudinis, non cuiuscumque, nam vermes qui generantur in animalibus, non habent rationem generationis et filiationis, licet sit similitudo secundum genus, sed requiritur ad rationem talis generationis, quod procedat secundum rationem similitudinis in natura eiusdem speciei, sicut homo procedit ab homine, et equus ab equo. In viventibus autem quae de potentia in actum vitae procedunt, sicut sunt homines et animalia, generatio utramque generationem includit. Si autem sit aliquod vivens cuius vita non exeat de potentia in actum, processio, si qua in tali vivente invenitur, excludit omnino primam rationem generationis; sed potest habere rationem generationis quae est propria viventium. Sic igitur processio verbi in divinis habet rationem generationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","text":"Procedit enim per modum intelligibilis actionis, quae est operatio vitae, et a principio coniuncto, ut supra iam dictum est, et secundum rationem similitudinis, quia conceptio intellectus est similitudo rei intellectae, et in eadem natura existens, quia in Deo idem est intelligere et esse, ut supra ostensum est. Unde processio verbi in divinis dicitur generatio, et ipsum verbum procedens dicitur filius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0037","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de generatione secundum rationem primam, prout importat exitum de potentia in actum. Et sic non invenitur in divinis, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0039","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intelligere in nobis non est ipsa substantia intellectus, unde verbum quod secundum intelligibilem operationem procedit in nobis, non est eiusdem naturae cum eo a quo procedit. Unde non proprie et complete competit sibi ratio generationis. Sed intelligere divinum est ipsa substantia intelligentis, ut supra ostensum est, unde verbum procedens procedit ut eiusdem naturae subsistens. Et propter hoc proprie dicitur genitum et filius. Unde et his quae pertinent ad generationem viventium, utitur Scriptura ad significandam processionem divinae sapientiae, scilicet conceptione et partu, dicitur enim ex persona divinae sapientiae, Proverb. VIII, *nondum erant abyssi, et ego iam concepta eram; ante colles ego parturiebar*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0041","text":"Sed in intellectu nostro utimur nomine conceptionis, secundum quod in verbo nostri intellectus invenitur similitudo rei intellectae, licet non inveniatur naturae identitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0042","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non omne acceptum est receptum in aliquo subiecto, alioquin non posset dici quod tota substantia rei creatae sit accepta a Deo, cum totius substantiae non sit aliquod subiectum receptivum. Sic igitur id quod est genitum in divinis, accipit esse a generante, non tanquam illud esse sit receptum in aliqua materia vel subiecto (quod repugnat subsistentiae divini esse); sed secundum hoc dicitur esse acceptum, inquantum procedens ab alio habet esse divinum, non quasi aliud ab esse divino existens. In ipsa enim perfectione divini esse continetur et verbum intelligibiliter procedens, et principium verbi; sicut et quaecumque ad eius perfectionem pertinent, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0045","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit in divinis alia processio a generatione verbi. Eadem enim ratione erit aliqua alia processio ab illa alia processione, et sic procederetur in infinitum, quod est inconveniens. Standum est igitur in primo, ut sit una tantum processio in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0047","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0048","text":"Praeterea, in omni natura invenitur tantum unus modus communicationis illius naturae, et hoc ideo est, quia operationes secundum terminos habent unitatem et diversitatem. Sed processio in divinis non est nisi secundum communicationem divinae naturae. Cum igitur sit una tantum natura divina, ut supra ostensum est, relinquitur quod una sit tantum processio in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0049","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0050","text":"Praeterea, si sit in divinis alia processio ab intelligibili processione verbi, non erit nisi processio amoris, quae est secundum voluntatis operationem. Sed talis processio non potest esse alia a processione intellectus intelligibili, quia voluntas in Deo non est aliud ab intellectu, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo in Deo non est alia processio praeter processionem verbi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0051","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0052","text":"Sed contra est quod spiritus sanctus procedit a patre, ut dicitur Ioan. XV. Ipse autem est alius a filio, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *rogabo patrem meum, et alium Paracletum dabit vobis*. Ergo in divinis est alia processio praeter processionem verbi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0053","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0054","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in divinis sunt duae processiones, scilicet processio verbi, et quaedam alia. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod in divinis non est processio nisi secundum actionem quae non tendit in aliquid extrinsecum, sed manet in ipso agente. Huiusmodi autem actio in intellectuali natura est actio intellectus et actio voluntatis. Processio autem verbi attenditur secundum actionem intelligibilem. Secundum autem operationem voluntatis invenitur in nobis quaedam alia processio, scilicet processio amoris, secundum quam amatum est in amante, sicut per conceptionem verbi res dicta vel intellecta, est in intelligente. Unde et praeter processionem verbi, ponitur alia processio in divinis, quae est processio amoris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0055","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0056","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod non est necessarium procedere in divinis processionibus in infinitum. Processio enim quae est ad intra in intellectuali natura, terminatur in processione voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0057","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod quidquid est in Deo, est Deus, ut supra ostensum est, quod non contingit in aliis rebus. Et ideo per quamlibet processionem quae non est ad extra, communicatur divina natura, non autem aliae naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0059","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet in Deo non sit aliud voluntas et intellectus, tamen de ratione voluntatis et intellectus est, quod processiones quae sunt secundum actionem utriusque, se habeant secundum quendam ordinem. Non enim est processio amoris nisi in ordine ad processionem verbi, nihil enim potest voluntate amari, nisi sit in intellectu conceptum. Sicut igitur attenditur quidam ordo verbi ad principium a quo procedit, licet in divinis sit eadem substantia intellectus et conceptio intellectus; ita, licet in Deo sit idem voluntas et intellectus, tamen, quia de ratione amoris est quod non procedat nisi a conceptione intellectus, habet ordinis distinctionem processio amoris a processione verbi in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0061","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0062","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0063","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod processio amoris in divinis sit generatio. Quod enim procedit in similitudine naturae in viventibus, dicitur generatum et nascens. Sed id quod procedit in divinis per modum amoris, procedit in similitudine naturae, alias esset extraneum a natura divina, et sic esset processio ad extra. Ergo quod procedit in divinis per modum amoris, procedit ut genitum et nascens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0064","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0065","text":"Praeterea, sicut similitudo est de ratione verbi, ita est etiam de ratione amoris, unde dicitur Eccli. XIII, quod *omne animal diligit simile sibi*. Si igitur ratione similitudinis verbo procedenti convenit generari et nasci, videtur etiam quod amori procedenti convenit generari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0066","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0067","text":"Praeterea, non est in genere quod non est in aliqua eius specie. Si igitur in divinis sit quaedam processio amoris, oportet quod, praeter hoc nomen commune, habeat aliquod nomen speciale. Sed non est aliud nomen dare nisi generatio. Ergo videtur quod processio amoris in divinis sit generatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0068","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0069","text":"Sed contra est quia secundum hoc sequeretur quod spiritus sanctus, qui procedit ut amor, procederet ut genitus. Quod est contra illud Athanasii, *spiritus sanctus a patre et filio non factus nec creatus nec genitus, sed procedens*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0070","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod processio amoris in divinis non debet dici generatio. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod haec est differentia inter intellectum et voluntatem, quod intellectus fit in actu per hoc quod res intellecta est in intellectu secundum suam similitudinem, voluntas autem fit in actu, non per hoc quod aliqua similitudo voliti sit in voluntate, sed ex hoc quod voluntas habet quandam inclinationem in rem volitam. Processio igitur quae attenditur secundum rationem intellectus, est secundum rationem similitudinis, et intantum potest habere rationem generationis, quia omne generans generat sibi simile. Processio autem quae attenditur secundum rationem voluntatis, non consideratur secundum rationem similitudinis, sed magis secundum rationem impellentis et moventis in aliquid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","text":"Et ideo quod procedit in divinis per modum amoris, non procedit ut genitum vel ut filius, sed magis procedit ut spiritus, quo nomine quaedam vitalis motio et impulsio designatur, prout aliquis ex amore dicitur moveri vel impelli ad aliquid faciendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0073","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod quidquid est in divinis, est unum cum divina natura. Unde ex parte huius unitatis non potest accipi propria ratio huius processionis vel illius, secundum quam una distinguatur ab alia, sed oportet quod propria ratio huius vel illius processionis accipiatur secundum ordinem unius processionis ad aliam. Huiusmodi autem ordo attenditur secundum rationem voluntatis et intellectus. Unde secundum horum propriam rationem sortitur in divinis nomen utraque processio, quod imponitur ad propriam rationem rei significandam. Et inde est quod procedens per modum amoris et divinam naturam accipit, et tamen non dicitur natum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0075","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0076","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod similitudo aliter pertinet ad verbum, et aliter ad amorem. Nam ad verbum pertinet inquantum ipsum est quaedam similitudo rei intellectae, sicut genitum est similitudo generantis, sed ad amorem pertinet, non quod ipse amor sit similitudo, sed inquantum similitudo est principium amandi. Unde non sequitur quod amor sit genitus, sed quod genitum sit principium amoris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0077","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Deum nominare non possumus nisi ex creaturis, ut dictum est supra. Et quia in creaturis communicatio naturae non est nisi per generationem, processio in divinis non habet proprium vel speciale nomen nisi generationis. Unde processio quae non est generatio, remansit sine speciali nomine. Sed potest nominari spiratio, quia est processio spiritus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0079","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0080","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0081","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sint plures processiones in divinis quam duae. Sicut enim scientia et voluntas attribuitur Deo, ita et potentia. Si igitur secundum intellectum et voluntatem accipiuntur in Deo duae processiones, videtur quod tertia sit accipienda secundum potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0082","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0083","text":"Praeterea, bonitas maxime videtur esse principium processionis, cum bonum dicatur diffusivum sui esse. Videtur igitur quod secundum bonitatem aliqua processio in divinis accipi debeat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0084","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0085","text":"Praeterea, maior est fecunditatis virtus in Deo quam in nobis. Sed in nobis non est tantum una processio verbi, sed multae, quia ex uno verbo in nobis procedit aliud verbum; et similiter ex uno amore alius amor. Ergo et in Deo sunt plures processiones quam duae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0086","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0087","text":"Sed contra est quod in Deo non sunt nisi duo procedentes, scilicet filius et spiritus sanctus. Ergo sunt ibi tantum duae processiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0088","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod processiones in divinis accipi non possunt nisi secundum actiones quae in agente manent. Huiusmodi autem actiones in natura intellectuali et divina non sunt nisi duae, scilicet intelligere et velle. Nam sentire, quod etiam videtur esse operatio in sentiente, est extra naturam intellectualem, neque totaliter est remotum a genere actionum quae sunt ad extra; nam sentire perficitur per actionem sensibilis in sensum. Relinquitur igitur quod nulla alia processio possit esse in Deo, nisi verbi et amoris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0090","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod potentia est principium agendi in aliud, unde secundum potentiam accipitur actio ad extra. Et sic secundum attributum potentiae non accipitur processio divinae personae, sed solum processio creaturarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0092","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bonum, sicut dicit Boetius in libro de Hebd., pertinet ad essentiam et non ad operationem, nisi forte sicut obiectum voluntatis. Unde, cum processiones divinas secundum aliquas actiones necesse sit accipere, secundum bonitatem et huiusmodi alia attributa non accipiuntur aliae processiones nisi verbi et amoris, secundum quod Deus suam essentiam, veritatem et bonitatem intelligit et amat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0094","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra habitum est, Deus uno simplici actu omnia intelligit, et similiter omnia vult. Unde in eo non potest esse processio verbi ex verbo, neque amoris ex amore, sed est in eo solum unum verbum perfectum, et unus amor perfectus. Et in hoc eius perfecta fecunditas manifestatur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9dd1c2ab15029b739bf9409464aff7e9c0796c59e744fda543ee452b99981399","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140835680820Z_ca4faa04","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 27","source_sha256":"9dd1c2ab15029b739bf9409464aff7e9c0796c59e744fda543ee452b99981399","sha256":"aa87d92c4a99ac633eabd954e91ae8a9d4bea9fe1ae7d1770ae036e8316cbc4c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:08:35.680836+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0002","text":"[I q. 27 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","text":"Having considered those matters that pertain to the unity of the divine essence, it remains to consider those that pertain to the Trinity of persons in God. And because the divine persons are distinguished according to relations of origin, according to the order of doctrine one must first consider origin, or procession; secondly, the relations of origin; thirdly, the persons. Concerning procession, five questions are asked. First, whether there is procession in God. Secondly, whether any procession in God can be called generation. Thirdly, whether, besides generation, there can be some other procession in God. Fourthly, whether that other procession can be called generation. Fifthly, whether there are more than two processions in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0005","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0006","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that there cannot be any procession in God. For procession signifies motion outward. But in divine things nothing is movable, nor extraneous. Therefore neither is there procession.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0007","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0008","text":"Moreover, everything proceeding is diverse from that from which it proceeds. But in God there is no diversity, but supreme simplicity. Therefore there is no procession in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0009","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0010","text":"Moreover, to proceed from another seems incompatible with the notion of a first principle. But God is the first principle, as was shown above. Therefore procession has no place in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0011","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the Lord says, John VIII, *I proceeded from God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0013","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that Sacred Scripture, in divine matters, uses names pertaining to procession. But different people have understood this procession in different ways. For some understood this procession according as an effect proceeds from a cause. And thus Arius understood it, saying that the Son proceeds from the Father as His first creature, and that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son as the creature of both. And according to this, neither the Son nor the Holy Spirit would be true God. This is contrary to what is said of the Son, 1 John, last chapter, *that we may be in His true Son; this is true God*. And of the Holy Spirit it is said, 1 Cor. VI, *do you not know that your members are a temple of the Holy Spirit?* But to have a temple belongs to God alone. Others, however, understood this procession according as a cause is said to proceed into an effect, insofar as it either moves it or impresses its likeness upon it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","text":"And thus Sabellius understood it, saying that God Himself is called Father and Son according as He assumed flesh from the Virgin. And he says that He is the Holy Spirit according as He sanctifies the rational creature and moves it toward life. But the words of the Lord speaking of Himself are opposed to this understanding, John V, *the Son cannot do anything of Himself*; and many others, through which it is shown that the Father Himself is not the Son. But if anyone considers carefully, each understood procession according as it is toward something external, whence neither posited procession in God Himself. But since every procession is according to some action, just as according to an action tending toward external matter there is a certain procession outward, so according to an action remaining in the agent itself there is considered a certain procession inward. And this is most evident in the intellect, whose action, namely understanding, remains in the one understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","text":"For whoever understands, by the very fact that he understands, something proceeds within him, which is the conception of the thing understood, arising from the intellective power and proceeding from its knowledge. This conception the voice signifies, and it is called the word of the heart, signified by the word of the voice. But since God is above all things, those things that are said of God are not to be understood according to the mode of the lowest creatures, which are bodies, but according to the likeness of the highest creatures, which are intellectual substances; from which even the likeness taken falls short of representing divine things. Therefore procession is not to be understood according as it is in bodily things, whether through local motion or through the action of some cause upon an external effect, as heat from that which heats into that which is heated; but according to intelligible emanation, as of an intelligible word from one speaking, which remains within him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0017","text":"And thus the Catholic faith posits procession in divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0018","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that objection proceeds from procession that is local motion, or that is according to an action tending toward external matter or toward an external effect; but such procession is not in divine things, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0020","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","text":"To the second it must be said that that which proceeds according to a procession that is outward must be diverse from that from which it proceeds. But that which proceeds inwardly by an intelligible process need not be diverse; indeed, the more perfectly it proceeds, the more it is one with that from which it proceeds. For it is evident that the more something is understood, the more intimate to the one understanding and the more one is the intellectual conception; for the intellect, according as it actually understands, becomes one with the thing understood. Hence, since divine understanding is at the summit of perfection, as was said above, it is necessary that the divine Word be perfectly one with that from which it proceeds, without any diversity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0022","text":"[I q. 27 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","text":"To the third it must be said that to proceed from a principle as something extraneous and diverse is incompatible with the notion of a first principle; but to proceed as something intimate and without diversity, in an intelligible mode, is included in the notion of a first principle. For when we call a builder the principle of a house, the conception of his art is included in the notion of this principle; and it would be included in the notion of the first principle if the builder were the first principle. But God, who is the first principle of things, is compared to created things as an artisan to artifacts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0025","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the procession which is in the divine things cannot be called generation. For generation is a change from non-being to being, opposed to corruption; and the subject of both is matter. But none of these belongs to the divine things. Therefore there cannot be generation in the divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0027","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0028","text":"Moreover, in God there is procession according to an intelligible mode, as has been said. But in us such a procession is not called generation. Therefore neither is it in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0029","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0030","text":"Moreover, everything generated receives being from that which generates it. Therefore the being of anything generated is received being. But no received being subsists through itself. Since, therefore, the divine being is being subsisting through itself, as was proved above, it follows that the being of no generated thing is divine being. Therefore there is no generation in the divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0031","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0032","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm II, *I have begotten you today*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0033","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","text":"I answer that the procession of the Word in the divine things is called generation. For the clarification of this, it must be known that we use the name generation in two ways. In one way, commonly, for all generable and corruptible things; and thus generation is nothing other than a change from non-being to being. In another way, properly, in living things; and thus generation signifies the origin of a living thing from a conjoined living principle. And this is properly called birth. Yet not every such thing is called generated, but properly that which proceeds according to the character of likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0035","text":"Hence a hair or a strand of hair does not have the character of something generated and of a son, but only that which proceeds according to the character of likeness—not any likeness whatsoever, for worms that are generated in animals do not have the character of generation and filiation, although there is likeness according to genus—but for the character of such generation it is required that it proceed according to the character of likeness in the nature of the same species, as man proceeds from man, and horse from horse. But in living things that proceed from potency to the act of life, such as men and animals, generation includes both kinds of generation. But if there is some living thing whose life does not pass from potency into act, any procession found in such a living thing excludes altogether the first character of generation; but it can have the character of generation that is proper to living things. Thus, therefore, the procession of the Word in the divine things has the character of generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","text":"For it proceeds by way of an intelligible action, which is an operation of life, and from a conjoined principle, as was already said above, and according to the character of likeness, because the conception of the intellect is a likeness of the thing understood, and existing in the same nature, because in God to understand and to be are the same, as was shown above. Hence the procession of the Word in the divine things is called generation, and the proceeding Word itself is called Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0037","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that objection proceeds concerning generation according to the first character, insofar as it implies a passing from potency to act. And thus it is not found in the divine things, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0039","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","text":"To the second it must be said that understanding in us is not the very substance of the intellect; hence the word which proceeds in us according to intelligible operation is not of the same nature as that from which it proceeds. Hence the character of generation does not properly and completely belong to it. But divine understanding is the very substance of the one understanding, as was shown above; hence the proceeding Word proceeds as subsisting in the same nature. And on this account it is properly called generated and Son. Hence Scripture also uses those things that pertain to the generation of living things, namely conception and birth, to signify the procession of divine wisdom; for it is said in the person of divine wisdom, Proverbs VIII, *the depths were not yet, and I was already conceived; before the hills I was brought forth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0041","text":"But in our intellect we use the name conception insofar as a likeness of the thing understood is found in the word of our intellect, although identity of nature is not found there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0042","text":"[I q. 27 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","text":"To the third it must be said that not everything received is received in some subject; otherwise it could not be said that the whole substance of a created thing is received from God, since there is no receptive subject of the whole substance. Thus, therefore, that which is generated in the divine things receives being from the generator, not as though that being were received in some matter or subject—which is incompatible with the subsistence of divine being—but it is called received in this respect: that, proceeding from another, it has divine being, not as though it existed as something other than divine being. For in the very perfection of divine being are contained both the Word proceeding intelligibly and the principle of the Word, as also whatever pertains to its perfection, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0045","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0046","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that there is no procession in the divine things other than the generation of the Word. For by the same reasoning there would be some other procession from that other procession, and thus one would proceed to infinity, which is unfitting. Therefore one must stop at the first, so that there is only one procession in the divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0047","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0048","text":"Moreover, in every nature only one mode of communicating that nature is found, and this is because operations have unity and diversity according to their termini. But procession in the divine things is only according to the communication of the divine nature. Therefore, since there is only one divine nature, as was shown above, it remains that there is only one procession in the divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0049","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0050","text":"Moreover, if there is in the divine another procession besides the intelligible procession of the Word, it can only be the procession of love, which is according to the operation of the will. But such a procession cannot be other than the intelligible procession of the intellect, because in God the will is not other than the intellect, as was shown above. Therefore in God there is no other procession besides the procession of the Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0051","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0052","text":"On the contrary, the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father, as is said in John XV. But He is other than the Son, according to that of John XIV, *I will ask my Father, and He will give you another Paraclete*. Therefore in the divine there is another procession besides the procession of the Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0053","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0054","text":"I answer that it must be said that in the divine there are two processions, namely, the procession of the Word and another one. For the elucidation of this, it must be considered that in the divine there is no procession except according to an action that does not tend toward something extrinsic, but remains in the agent itself. Now in an intellectual nature an action of this sort is the action of the intellect and the action of the will. The procession of the Word, however, is considered according to intelligible action. But according to the operation of the will, another procession is found in us, namely, the procession of love, according to which the beloved is in the lover, just as through the conception of the word the thing spoken or understood is in the one understanding. Hence, besides the procession of the Word, another procession is posited in the divine, which is the procession of love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0055","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0056","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that it is not necessary to proceed to infinity in the divine processions. For the procession that is inward in an intellectual nature is terminated in the procession of the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0057","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","text":"To the second, it must be said that whatever is in God is God, as was shown above; this does not occur in other things. And therefore, through every procession that is not outward, the divine nature is communicated, but not other natures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0059","text":"[I q. 27 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although in God the will and the intellect are not other, nevertheless it belongs to the nature of will and intellect that the processions which are according to the action of each should stand in a certain order. For there is no procession of love except in relation to the procession of the Word, since nothing can be loved by the will unless it has been conceived in the intellect. Therefore, just as a certain order of the Word to the principle from which it proceeds is considered, although in the divine the substance of the intellect and the conception of the intellect are the same; so, although in God will and intellect are the same, nevertheless, because it belongs to the nature of love that it proceed only from the conception of the intellect, the procession of love has a distinction of order from the procession of the Word in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0061","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0062","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0063","text":"The fourth point is approached thus. It seems that the procession of love in the divine is generation. For that which proceeds in a likeness of nature in living things is called generated and born. But that which proceeds in the divine by way of love proceeds in a likeness of nature; otherwise it would be foreign to the divine nature, and thus it would be a procession outward. Therefore that which proceeds in the divine by way of love proceeds as generated and born.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0064","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0065","text":"Moreover, just as likeness belongs to the nature of the Word, so also does it belong to the nature of love; hence it is said in Ecclesiasticus XIII that *every animal loves that which is like itself*. Therefore, if on account of likeness it belongs to the proceeding Word to be generated and born, it seems also that it belongs to proceeding love to be generated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0066","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0067","text":"Moreover, what is not in any of its species is not in the genus. Therefore, if there is a certain procession of love in the divine, it is necessary that, besides this common name, it have some special name. But no other name can be given except generation. Therefore it seems that the procession of love in the divine is generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0068","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0069","text":"On the contrary, because according to this it would follow that the Holy Spirit, who proceeds as love, would proceed as begotten. This is contrary to that of Athanasius: *the Holy Spirit, from the Father and the Son, not made nor created nor begotten, but proceeding*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0070","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","text":"I answer that it must be said that the procession of love in the divine ought not to be called generation. For the elucidation of this, it should be known that this is the difference between intellect and will: the intellect comes to be in act by the fact that the thing understood is in the intellect according to its likeness, whereas the will comes to be in act not by the fact that some likeness of the thing willed is in the will, but from the fact that the will has a certain inclination toward the thing willed. Therefore the procession that is considered according to the character of intellect is according to the character of likeness, and insofar as this is so it can have the character of generation, because every generator generates something like itself. But the procession that is considered according to the character of will is not considered according to the character of likeness, but rather according to the character of impelling and moving toward something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","text":"And therefore that which proceeds in the divine by way of love does not proceed as begotten or as Son, but rather proceeds as spirit, by which name a certain vital motion and impulsion is designated, insofar as someone is said to be moved or impelled by love to do something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0073","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that whatever is in God is one with the divine nature. Hence, on the side of this unity, the proper account of this procession or that cannot be taken, according to which one is distinguished from another; rather, the proper account of this or that procession must be taken according to the order of one procession to another. But such an order is considered according to the character of will and intellect. Hence, according to their proper character, each procession receives in God a name which is imposed to signify the proper character of the thing. And from this it follows that what proceeds by way of love receives the divine nature, and yet is not called begotten.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0075","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0076","text":"To the second it must be said that likeness pertains differently to the word and to love. For it pertains to the word insofar as the word itself is a certain likeness of the thing understood, just as what is begotten is a likeness of the begetter; but it pertains to love not because love itself is a likeness, but insofar as likeness is a principle of loving. Hence it does not follow that love is begotten, but that what is begotten is the principle of love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0077","text":"[I q. 27 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","text":"To the third it must be said that we cannot name God except from creatures, as was said above. And because in creatures the communication of nature occurs only through generation, procession in God has no proper or special name other than generation. Hence the procession which is not generation has remained without a special name. But it can be called spiration, because it is the procession of the Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0079","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0080","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0081","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that there are more than two processions in God. For just as knowledge and will are attributed to God, so too is power. Therefore, if two processions are posited in God according to intellect and will, it seems that a third should be posited according to power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0082","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0083","text":"Moreover, goodness seems especially to be a principle of procession, since the good is said to be diffusive of itself. Therefore, it seems that some procession in God should be posited according to goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0084","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0085","text":"Moreover, the power of fecundity is greater in God than in us. But in us there is not only one procession of the word, but many, because from one word another word proceeds in us; and likewise from one love another love. Therefore, in God too there are more than two processions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0086","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0087","text":"But on the contrary, in God there are only two proceeding persons, namely, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Therefore, there are only two processions there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0088","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","text":"I answer that it must be said that processions in God can be understood only according to actions that remain in the agent. But such actions in an intellectual and divine nature are only two, namely, to understand and to will. For to sense, which also appears to be an operation in the one sensing, lies outside intellectual nature, nor is it wholly removed from the genus of actions that are outward; for sensing is completed through the action of the sensible object upon the sense. It remains, therefore, that no other procession can be in God except that of the Word and of Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0090","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that power is a principle of acting upon another; hence, according to power, an action outward is understood. And thus, according to the attribute of power, the procession of a divine person is not understood, but only the procession of creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0092","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093","text":"To the second it must be said that the good, as Boethius says in the book De Hebdomadibus, pertains to essence and not to operation, except perhaps as the object of the will. Hence, since the divine processions must be understood according to certain actions, no processions other than those of the Word and Love are understood according to goodness and other such attributes, insofar as God understands and loves His own essence, truth, and goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0094","text":"[I q. 27 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html#29609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","text":"To the third it must be said that, as was treated above, God understands all things by one simple act, and likewise wills all things. Hence in Him there cannot be a procession of word from word, nor of love from love; rather, there is in Him only one perfect Word and one perfect Love. And in this His perfect fecundity is manifested.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095"]}],"source_digest":"cc80067281967410462cb350d952c18b23e3b62adc6019e209fb9e880921bb0b","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 27"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 27","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20","title":"First Part · Question 28","order":29,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_20","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 28 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29610)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de relationibus divinis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in Deo sint aliquae relationes reales. Secundo, utrum illae relationes sint ipsa essentia divina, vel sint extrinsecus affixae. Tertio, utrum possint esse in Deo plures relationes realiter distinctae ab invicem. Quarto, de numero harum relationum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29611)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sint aliquae relationes reales. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *cum quis praedicamenta in divinam vertit praedicationem, cuncta mutantur in substantiam quae praedicari possunt; ad aliquid vero omnino non potest praedicari*. Sed quidquid est realiter in Deo, de ipso praedicari potest. Ergo relatio non est realiter in Deo.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29612)\n\nPraeterea, dicit Boetius in eodem libro, quod *similis est relatio in Trinitate patris ad filium, et utriusque ad spiritum sanctum, ut eius quod est idem, ad id quod est idem*. Sed huiusmodi relatio est rationis tantum, quia omnis relatio realis exigit duo extrema realiter. Ergo relationes quae ponuntur in divinis, non sunt reales relationes, sed rationis tantum.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29613)\n\nPraeterea, relatio paternitatis est relatio principii. Sed cum dicitur, Deus est principium creaturarum, non importatur aliqua relatio realis, sed rationis tantum. Ergo nec paternitas in divinis est relatio realis. Et eadem ratione nec aliae relationes quae ponuntur ibi.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29614)\n\nPraeterea, generatio in divinis est secundum intelligibilis verbi processionem. Sed relationes quae consequuntur operationem intellectus, sunt relationes rationis. Ergo paternitas et filiatio, quae dicuntur in divinis secundum generationem, sunt relationes rationis tantum.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29615)\n\nSed contra est quod pater non dicitur nisi a paternitate, et filius a filiatione. Si igitur paternitas et filiatio non sunt in Deo realiter, sequitur quod Deus non sit realiter pater aut filius, sed secundum rationem intelligentiae tantum, quod est haeresis Sabelliana.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29616)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod relationes quaedam sunt in divinis realiter. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod solum in his quae dicuntur ad aliquid, inveniuntur aliqua secundum rationem tantum, et non secundum rem. Quod non est in aliis generibus, quia alia genera, ut quantitas et qualitas, secundum propriam rationem significant aliquid alicui inhaerens. Ea vero quae dicuntur ad aliquid, significant secundum propriam rationem solum respectum ad aliud. Qui quidem respectus aliquando est in ipsa natura rerum; utpote quando aliquae res secundum suam naturam ad invicem ordinatae sunt, et invicem inclinationem habent. Et huiusmodi relationes oportet esse reales. Sicut in corpore gravi est inclinatio et ordo ad locum medium, unde respectus quidam est in ipso gravi respectu loci medii. Et similiter est de aliis huiusmodi. Aliquando vero respectus significatus per ea quae dicuntur ad aliquid, est tantum in ipsa apprehensione rationis conferentis unum alteri, et tunc est relatio rationis tantum; sicut cum comparat ratio hominem animali, ut speciem ad genus. Cum autem aliquid procedit a principio eiusdem naturae, necesse est quod ambo, scilicet procedens et id a quo procedit, in eodem ordine conveniant, et sic oportet quod habeant reales respectus ad invicem. Cum igitur processiones in divinis sint in identitate naturae, ut ostensum est, necesse est quod relationes quae secundum processiones divinas accipiuntur, sint relationes reales.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29617)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ad aliquid dicitur omnino non praedicari in Deo, secundum propriam rationem eius quod dicitur ad aliquid; inquantum scilicet propria ratio eius quod ad aliquid dicitur, non accipitur per comparationem ad illud cui inest relatio, sed per respectum ad alterum. Non ergo per hoc excludere voluit quod relatio non esset in Deo, sed quod non praedicaretur per modum inhaerentis secundum propriam relationis rationem, sed magis per modum ad aliud se habentis.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29618)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod relatio quae importatur per hoc nomen idem, est relatio rationis tantum, si accipiatur simpliciter idem, quia huiusmodi relatio non potest consistere nisi in quodam ordine quem ratio adinvenit alicuius ad seipsum, secundum aliquas eius duas considerationes. Secus autem est, cum dicuntur aliqua eadem esse, non in numero, sed in natura generis sive speciei. Boetius igitur relationes quae sunt in divinis, assimilat relationi identitatis, non quantum ad omnia, sed quantum ad hoc solum, quod per huiusmodi relationes non diversificatur substantia, sicut nec per relationem identitatis.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29619)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum creatura procedat a Deo in diversitate naturae, Deus est extra ordinem totius creaturae, nec ex eius natura est eius habitudo ad creaturas. Non enim producit creaturas ex necessitate suae naturae, sed per intellectum et per voluntatem, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo in Deo non est realis relatio ad creaturas. Sed in creaturis est realis relatio ad Deum, quia creaturae continentur sub ordine divino, et in earum natura est quod dependeant a Deo. Sed processiones divinae sunt in eadem natura. Unde non est similis ratio.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29620)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod relationes quae consequuntur solam operationem intellectus in ipsis rebus intellectis, sunt relationes rationis tantum, quia scilicet eas ratio adinvenit inter duas res intellectas. Sed relationes quae consequuntur operationem intellectus, quae sunt inter verbum intellectualiter procedens et illud a quo procedit, non sunt relationes rationis tantum, sed rei, quia et ipse intellectus et ratio est quaedam res, et comparatur realiter ad id quod procedit intelligibiliter, sicut res corporalis ad id quod procedit corporaliter. Et sic paternitas et filiatio sunt relationes reales in divinis.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29621)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod relatio in Deo non sit idem quod sua essentia. Dicit enim Augustinus, in V de Trin., quod *non omne quod dicitur in Deo, dicitur secundum substantiam. Dicitur enim ad aliquid, sicut pater ad filium, sed haec non secundum substantiam dicuntur*. Ergo relatio non est divina essentia.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29622)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, VII de Trin., *omnis res quae relative dicitur, est etiam aliquid excepto relativo; sicut homo dominus, et homo servus*. Si igitur relationes aliquae sunt in Deo, oportet esse in Deo aliquid aliud praeter relationes. Sed hoc aliud non potest esse nisi essentia. Ergo essentia est aliud a relationibus.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29623)\n\nPraeterea, esse relativi est ad aliud se habere, ut dicitur in praedicamentis. Si igitur relatio sit ipsa divina essentia, sequitur quod esse divinae essentiae sit ad aliud se habere, quod repugnat perfectioni divini esse, quod est maxime absolutum et per se subsistens, ut supra ostensum est. Non igitur relatio est ipsa essentia divina.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29624)\n\nSed contra, omnis res quae non est divina essentia, est creatura. Sed relatio realiter competit Deo. Si ergo non est divina essentia, erit creatura, et ita ei non erit adoratio latriae exhibenda, contra quod in praefatione cantatur, *ut in personis proprietas, et in maiestate adoretur aequalitas*.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29625)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc dicitur Gilbertus Porretanus errasse, sed errorem suum postmodum in Remensi Concilio revocasse. Dixit enim quod relationes in divinis sunt assistentes, sive extrinsecus affixae. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod in quolibet novem generum accidentis est duo considerare. Quorum unum est esse quod competit unicuique ipsorum secundum quod est accidens. Et hoc communiter in omnibus est inesse subiecto, accidentis enim esse est inesse. Aliud quod potest considerari in unoquoque, est propria ratio uniuscuiusque illorum generum. Et in aliis quidem generibus a relatione, utpote quantitate et qualitate, etiam propria ratio generis accipitur secundum comparationem ad subiectum, nam quantitas dicitur mensura substantiae, qualitas vero dispositio substantiae. Sed ratio propria relationis non accipitur secundum comparationem ad illud in quo est, sed secundum comparationem ad aliquid extra. Si igitur consideremus, etiam in rebus creatis, relationes secundum id quod relationes sunt, sic inveniuntur esse assistentes, non intrinsecus affixae; quasi significantes respectum quodammodo contingentem ipsam rem relatam, prout ab ea tendit in alterum. Si vero consideretur relatio secundum quod est accidens, sic est inhaerens subiecto, et habens esse accidentale in ipso. Sed Gilbertus Porretanus consideravit relationem primo modo tantum. Quidquid autem in rebus creatis habet esse accidentale, secundum quod transfertur in Deum, habet esse substantiale, nihil enim est in Deo ut accidens in subiecto, sed quidquid est in Deo, est eius essentia. Sic igitur ex ea parte qua relatio in rebus creatis habet esse accidentale in subiecto, relatio realiter existens in Deo habet esse essentiae divinae, idem omnino ei existens. In hoc vero quod ad aliquid dicitur, non significatur aliqua habitudo ad essentiam, sed magis ad suum oppositum. Et sic manifestum est quod relatio realiter existens in Deo, est idem essentiae secundum rem; et non differt nisi secundum intelligentiae rationem, prout in relatione importatur respectus ad suum oppositum, qui non importatur in nomine essentiae. Patet ergo quod in Deo non est aliud esse relationis et esse essentiae, sed unum et idem.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29626)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verba illa Augustini non pertinent ad hoc, quod paternitas, vel alia relatio quae est in Deo, secundum esse suum non sit idem quod divina essentia; sed quod non praedicatur secundum modum substantiae, ut existens in eo de quo dicitur, sed ut ad alterum se habens. Et propter hoc dicuntur duo tantum esse praedicamenta in divinis. Quia alia praedicamenta important habitudinem ad id de quo dicuntur, tam secundum suum esse, quam secundum proprii generis rationem, nihil autem quod est in Deo, potest habere habitudinem ad id in quo est, vel de quo dicitur, nisi habitudinem identitatis, propter summam Dei simplicitatem.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29627)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut in rebus creatis, in illo quod dicitur relative, non solum est invenire respectum ad alterum, sed etiam aliquid absolutum, ita et in Deo, sed tamen aliter et aliter. Nam id quod invenitur in creatura praeter id quod continetur sub significatione nominis relativi, est alia res, in Deo autem non est alia res, sed una et eadem, quae non perfecte exprimitur relationis nomine, quasi sub significatione talis nominis comprehensa. Dictum est enim supra, cum de divinis nominibus agebatur, quod plus continetur in perfectione divinae essentiae, quam aliquo nomine significari possit. Unde non sequitur quod in Deo, praeter relationem, sit aliquid aliud secundum rem; sed solum considerata nominum ratione.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29628)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si in perfectione divina nihil plus contineretur quam quod significat nomen relativum, sequeretur quod esse eius esset imperfectum, utpote ad aliquid aliud se habens, sicut si non contineretur ibi plus quam quod nomine sapientiae significatur, non esset aliquid subsistens. Sed quia divinae essentiae perfectio est maior quam quod significatione alicuius nominis comprehendi possit, non sequitur, si nomen relativum, vel quodcumque aliud nomen dictum de Deo, non significat aliquid perfectum, quod divina essentia habeat esse imperfectum, quia divina essentia comprehendit in se omnium generum perfectionem, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29629)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod relationes quae sunt in Deo, realiter ab invicem non distinguantur. Quaecumque enim uni et eidem sunt eadem, sibi invicem sunt eadem. Sed omnis relatio in Deo existens est idem secundum rem cum divina essentia. Ergo relationes secundum rem ab invicem non distinguuntur.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29630)\n\nPraeterea, sicut paternitas et filiatio secundum nominis rationem distinguuntur ab essentia divina, ita et bonitas et potentia. Sed propter huiusmodi rationis distinctionem non est aliqua realis distinctio bonitatis et potentiae divinae. Ergo neque paternitatis et filiationis.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29631)\n\nPraeterea, in divinis non est distinctio realis nisi secundum originem. Sed una relatio non videtur oriri ex alia. Ergo relationes non distinguuntur realiter ab invicem.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29632)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *substantia in divinis continet unitatem, relatio multiplicat Trinitatem*. Si ergo relationes non distinguuntur ab invicem realiter, non erit in divinis Trinitas realis, sed rationis tantum, quod est Sabelliani erroris.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29633)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ex eo quod aliquid alicui attribuitur, oportet quod attribuantur ei omnia quae sunt de ratione illius, sicut cuicumque attribuitur homo, oportet quod attribuatur ei esse rationale. De ratione autem relationis est respectus unius ad alterum, secundum quem aliquid alteri opponitur relative. Cum igitur in Deo realiter sit relatio, ut dictum est, oportet quod realiter sit ibi oppositio. Relativa autem oppositio in sui ratione includit distinctionem. Unde oportet quod in Deo sit realis distinctio, non quidem secundum rem absolutam, quae est essentia, in qua est summa unitas et simplicitas; sed secundum rem relativam.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29634)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum in III Physic., argumentum illud tenet, quod quaecumque uni et eidem sunt eadem, sibi invicem sunt eadem, in his quae sunt idem re et ratione, sicut tunica et indumentum, non autem in his quae differunt ratione. Unde ibidem dicit quod, licet actio sit idem motui, similiter et passio, non tamen sequitur quod actio et passio sint idem, quia in actione importatur respectus ut a quo est motus in mobili, in passione vero ut qui est ab alio. Et similiter, licet paternitas sit idem secundum rem cum essentia divina, et similiter filiatio, tamen haec duo in suis propriis rationibus important oppositos respectus. Unde distinguuntur ab invicem.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29635)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod potentia et bonitas non important in suis rationibus aliquam oppositionem, unde non est similis ratio.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29636)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quamvis relationes, proprie loquendo, non oriantur vel procedant ab invicem, tamen accipiuntur per oppositum secundum processionem alicuius ab alio.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29637)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sint tantum quatuor relationes reales, scilicet paternitas, filiatio, spiratio et processio. Est enim considerare in Deo relationes intelligentis ad intellectum, et volentis ad volitum, quae videntur esse relationes reales, neque sub praedictis continentur. Non ergo sunt solum quatuor relationes reales in Deo.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29638)\n\nPraeterea, relationes reales accipiuntur in Deo secundum processionem intelligibilem verbi. Sed relationes intelligibiles multiplicantur in infinitum, ut Avicenna dicit. Ergo in Deo sunt infinitae relationes reales.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29639)\n\nPraeterea, ideae sunt in Deo ab aeterno, ut supra dictum est. Non autem distinguuntur ab invicem nisi secundum respectum ad res, ut supra dictum est. Ergo in Deo sunt multo plures relationes aeternae.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29640)\n\nPraeterea, aequalitas et similitudo et identitas sunt relationes quaedam; et sunt in Deo ab aeterno. Ergo plures relationes sunt ab aeterno in Deo, quam quae dictae sunt.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29641)\n\nSed contra, videtur quod sint pauciores. Quia secundum philosophum, in III Physic., *eadem via est de Athenis ad Thebas, et de Thebis ad Athenas*. Ergo videtur quod pari ratione eadem sit relatio de patre ad filium, quae dicitur paternitas, et de filio ad patrem, quae dicitur filiatio. Et sic non sunt quatuor relationes in Deo.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29642)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, in V Metaphys., relatio omnis fundatur vel supra quantitatem, ut duplum et dimidium; vel supra actionem et passionem, ut faciens et factum, pater et filius, dominus et servus, et huiusmodi. Cum autem quantitas non sit in Deo (est enim sine quantitate magnus, ut dicit Augustinus). Relinquitur ergo quod realis relatio in Deo esse non possit, nisi super actionem fundata. Non autem super actiones secundum quas procedit aliquid extrinsecum a Deo, quia relationes Dei ad creaturas non sunt realiter in ipso, ut supra dictum est. Unde relinquitur quod relationes reales in Deo non possunt accipi, nisi secundum actiones secundum quas est processio in Deo, non extra, sed intra. Huiusmodi autem processiones sunt duae tantum, ut supra dictum est, quarum una accipitur secundum actionem intellectus, quae est processio verbi; alia secundum actionem voluntatis, quae est processio amoris. Secundum quamlibet autem processionem oportet duas accipere relationes oppositas, quarum una sit procedentis a principio, et alia ipsius principii. Processio autem verbi dicitur generatio, secundum propriam rationem qua competit rebus viventibus. Relatio autem principii generationis in viventibus perfectis dicitur paternitas, relatio vero procedentis a principio dicitur filiatio. Processio vero amoris non habet nomen proprium, ut supra dictum est, unde neque relationes quae secundum ipsam accipiuntur. Sed vocatur relatio principii huius processionis spiratio; relatio autem procedentis, processio; quamvis haec duo nomina ad ipsas processiones vel origines pertineant, et non ad relationes.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29643)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in his in quibus differt intellectus et intellectum, volens et volitum, potest esse realis relatio et scientiae ad rem scitam, et volentis ad rem volitam. Sed in Deo est idem omnino intellectus et intellectum, quia intelligendo se intelligit omnia alia, et eadem ratione voluntas et volitum. Unde in Deo huiusmodi relationes non sunt reales, sicut neque relatio eiusdem ad idem. Sed tamen relatio ad verbum est realis, quia verbum intelligitur ut procedens per actionem intelligibilem, non autem ut res intellecta. Cum enim intelligimus lapidem, id quod ex re intellecta concipit intellectus, vocatur verbum.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29644)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in nobis relationes intelligibiles in infinitum multiplicantur, quia alio actu intelligit homo lapidem, et alio actu intelligit se intelligere lapidem, et alio etiam intelligit hoc intelligere, et sic in infinitum multiplicantur actus intelligendi, et per consequens relationes intellectae. Sed hoc in Deo non habet locum, quia uno actu tantum omnia intelligit.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29645)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod respectus ideales sunt ut intellecti a Deo. Unde ex eorum pluralitate non sequitur quod sint plures relationes in Deo, sed quod Deus cognoscat plures relationes.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29646)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod aequalitas et similitudo in Deo non sunt relationes reales, sed rationis tantum, ut infra patebit.\n\n[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29647)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod via est eadem ab uno termino ad alterum, et e converso; sed tamen respectus sunt diversi. Unde ex hoc non potest concludi quod eadem sit relatio patris ad filium, et e converso, sed posset hoc concludi de aliquo absoluto, si esset medium inter ea.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0002","text":"[I q. 28 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de relationibus divinis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in Deo sint aliquae relationes reales. Secundo, utrum illae relationes sint ipsa essentia divina, vel sint extrinsecus affixae. Tertio, utrum possint esse in Deo plures relationes realiter distinctae ab invicem. Quarto, de numero harum relationum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0005","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sint aliquae relationes reales. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *cum quis praedicamenta in divinam vertit praedicationem, cuncta mutantur in substantiam quae praedicari possunt; ad aliquid vero omnino non potest praedicari*. Sed quidquid est realiter in Deo, de ipso praedicari potest. Ergo relatio non est realiter in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0007","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0008","text":"Praeterea, dicit Boetius in eodem libro, quod *similis est relatio in Trinitate patris ad filium, et utriusque ad spiritum sanctum, ut eius quod est idem, ad id quod est idem*. Sed huiusmodi relatio est rationis tantum, quia omnis relatio realis exigit duo extrema realiter. Ergo relationes quae ponuntur in divinis, non sunt reales relationes, sed rationis tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0009","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0010","text":"Praeterea, relatio paternitatis est relatio principii. Sed cum dicitur, Deus est principium creaturarum, non importatur aliqua relatio realis, sed rationis tantum. Ergo nec paternitas in divinis est relatio realis. Et eadem ratione nec aliae relationes quae ponuntur ibi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0011","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0012","text":"Praeterea, generatio in divinis est secundum intelligibilis verbi processionem. Sed relationes quae consequuntur operationem intellectus, sunt relationes rationis. Ergo paternitas et filiatio, quae dicuntur in divinis secundum generationem, sunt relationes rationis tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0013","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod pater non dicitur nisi a paternitate, et filius a filiatione. Si igitur paternitas et filiatio non sunt in Deo realiter, sequitur quod Deus non sit realiter pater aut filius, sed secundum rationem intelligentiae tantum, quod est haeresis Sabelliana.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0015","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod relationes quaedam sunt in divinis realiter. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod solum in his quae dicuntur ad aliquid, inveniuntur aliqua secundum rationem tantum, et non secundum rem. Quod non est in aliis generibus, quia alia genera, ut quantitas et qualitas, secundum propriam rationem significant aliquid alicui inhaerens. Ea vero quae dicuntur ad aliquid, significant secundum propriam rationem solum respectum ad aliud. Qui quidem respectus aliquando est in ipsa natura rerum; utpote quando aliquae res secundum suam naturam ad invicem ordinatae sunt, et invicem inclinationem habent. Et huiusmodi relationes oportet esse reales. Sicut in corpore gravi est inclinatio et ordo ad locum medium, unde respectus quidam est in ipso gravi respectu loci medii. Et similiter est de aliis huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","text":"Aliquando vero respectus significatus per ea quae dicuntur ad aliquid, est tantum in ipsa apprehensione rationis conferentis unum alteri, et tunc est relatio rationis tantum; sicut cum comparat ratio hominem animali, ut speciem ad genus. Cum autem aliquid procedit a principio eiusdem naturae, necesse est quod ambo, scilicet procedens et id a quo procedit, in eodem ordine conveniant, et sic oportet quod habeant reales respectus ad invicem. Cum igitur processiones in divinis sint in identitate naturae, ut ostensum est, necesse est quod relationes quae secundum processiones divinas accipiuntur, sint relationes reales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0018","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ad aliquid dicitur omnino non praedicari in Deo, secundum propriam rationem eius quod dicitur ad aliquid; inquantum scilicet propria ratio eius quod ad aliquid dicitur, non accipitur per comparationem ad illud cui inest relatio, sed per respectum ad alterum. Non ergo per hoc excludere voluit quod relatio non esset in Deo, sed quod non praedicaretur per modum inhaerentis secundum propriam relationis rationem, sed magis per modum ad aliud se habentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0020","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod relatio quae importatur per hoc nomen idem, est relatio rationis tantum, si accipiatur simpliciter idem, quia huiusmodi relatio non potest consistere nisi in quodam ordine quem ratio adinvenit alicuius ad seipsum, secundum aliquas eius duas considerationes. Secus autem est, cum dicuntur aliqua eadem esse, non in numero, sed in natura generis sive speciei. Boetius igitur relationes quae sunt in divinis, assimilat relationi identitatis, non quantum ad omnia, sed quantum ad hoc solum, quod per huiusmodi relationes non diversificatur substantia, sicut nec per relationem identitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0022","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum creatura procedat a Deo in diversitate naturae, Deus est extra ordinem totius creaturae, nec ex eius natura est eius habitudo ad creaturas. Non enim producit creaturas ex necessitate suae naturae, sed per intellectum et per voluntatem, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo in Deo non est realis relatio ad creaturas. Sed in creaturis est realis relatio ad Deum, quia creaturae continentur sub ordine divino, et in earum natura est quod dependeant a Deo. Sed processiones divinae sunt in eadem natura. Unde non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0024","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod relationes quae consequuntur solam operationem intellectus in ipsis rebus intellectis, sunt relationes rationis tantum, quia scilicet eas ratio adinvenit inter duas res intellectas. Sed relationes quae consequuntur operationem intellectus, quae sunt inter verbum intellectualiter procedens et illud a quo procedit, non sunt relationes rationis tantum, sed rei, quia et ipse intellectus et ratio est quaedam res, et comparatur realiter ad id quod procedit intelligibiliter, sicut res corporalis ad id quod procedit corporaliter. Et sic paternitas et filiatio sunt relationes reales in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0027","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod relatio in Deo non sit idem quod sua essentia. Dicit enim Augustinus, in V de Trin., quod *non omne quod dicitur in Deo, dicitur secundum substantiam. Dicitur enim ad aliquid, sicut pater ad filium, sed haec non secundum substantiam dicuntur*. Ergo relatio non est divina essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0029","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0030","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, VII de Trin., *omnis res quae relative dicitur, est etiam aliquid excepto relativo; sicut homo dominus, et homo servus*. Si igitur relationes aliquae sunt in Deo, oportet esse in Deo aliquid aliud praeter relationes. Sed hoc aliud non potest esse nisi essentia. Ergo essentia est aliud a relationibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0031","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0032","text":"Praeterea, esse relativi est ad aliud se habere, ut dicitur in praedicamentis. Si igitur relatio sit ipsa divina essentia, sequitur quod esse divinae essentiae sit ad aliud se habere, quod repugnat perfectioni divini esse, quod est maxime absolutum et per se subsistens, ut supra ostensum est. Non igitur relatio est ipsa essentia divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0033","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0034","text":"Sed contra, omnis res quae non est divina essentia, est creatura. Sed relatio realiter competit Deo. Si ergo non est divina essentia, erit creatura, et ita ei non erit adoratio latriae exhibenda, contra quod in praefatione cantatur, *ut in personis proprietas, et in maiestate adoretur aequalitas*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0035","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc dicitur Gilbertus Porretanus errasse, sed errorem suum postmodum in Remensi Concilio revocasse. Dixit enim quod relationes in divinis sunt assistentes, sive extrinsecus affixae. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod in quolibet novem generum accidentis est duo considerare. Quorum unum est esse quod competit unicuique ipsorum secundum quod est accidens. Et hoc communiter in omnibus est inesse subiecto, accidentis enim esse est inesse. Aliud quod potest considerari in unoquoque, est propria ratio uniuscuiusque illorum generum. Et in aliis quidem generibus a relatione, utpote quantitate et qualitate, etiam propria ratio generis accipitur secundum comparationem ad subiectum, nam quantitas dicitur mensura substantiae, qualitas vero dispositio substantiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","text":"Sed ratio propria relationis non accipitur secundum comparationem ad illud in quo est, sed secundum comparationem ad aliquid extra. Si igitur consideremus, etiam in rebus creatis, relationes secundum id quod relationes sunt, sic inveniuntur esse assistentes, non intrinsecus affixae; quasi significantes respectum quodammodo contingentem ipsam rem relatam, prout ab ea tendit in alterum. Si vero consideretur relatio secundum quod est accidens, sic est inhaerens subiecto, et habens esse accidentale in ipso. Sed Gilbertus Porretanus consideravit relationem primo modo tantum. Quidquid autem in rebus creatis habet esse accidentale, secundum quod transfertur in Deum, habet esse substantiale, nihil enim est in Deo ut accidens in subiecto, sed quidquid est in Deo, est eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","text":"Sic igitur ex ea parte qua relatio in rebus creatis habet esse accidentale in subiecto, relatio realiter existens in Deo habet esse essentiae divinae, idem omnino ei existens. In hoc vero quod ad aliquid dicitur, non significatur aliqua habitudo ad essentiam, sed magis ad suum oppositum. Et sic manifestum est quod relatio realiter existens in Deo, est idem essentiae secundum rem; et non differt nisi secundum intelligentiae rationem, prout in relatione importatur respectus ad suum oppositum, qui non importatur in nomine essentiae. Patet ergo quod in Deo non est aliud esse relationis et esse essentiae, sed unum et idem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0039","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verba illa Augustini non pertinent ad hoc, quod paternitas, vel alia relatio quae est in Deo, secundum esse suum non sit idem quod divina essentia; sed quod non praedicatur secundum modum substantiae, ut existens in eo de quo dicitur, sed ut ad alterum se habens. Et propter hoc dicuntur duo tantum esse praedicamenta in divinis. Quia alia praedicamenta important habitudinem ad id de quo dicuntur, tam secundum suum esse, quam secundum proprii generis rationem, nihil autem quod est in Deo, potest habere habitudinem ad id in quo est, vel de quo dicitur, nisi habitudinem identitatis, propter summam Dei simplicitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0041","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut in rebus creatis, in illo quod dicitur relative, non solum est invenire respectum ad alterum, sed etiam aliquid absolutum, ita et in Deo, sed tamen aliter et aliter. Nam id quod invenitur in creatura praeter id quod continetur sub significatione nominis relativi, est alia res, in Deo autem non est alia res, sed una et eadem, quae non perfecte exprimitur relationis nomine, quasi sub significatione talis nominis comprehensa. Dictum est enim supra, cum de divinis nominibus agebatur, quod plus continetur in perfectione divinae essentiae, quam aliquo nomine significari possit. Unde non sequitur quod in Deo, praeter relationem, sit aliquid aliud secundum rem; sed solum considerata nominum ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0043","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0044","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si in perfectione divina nihil plus contineretur quam quod significat nomen relativum, sequeretur quod esse eius esset imperfectum, utpote ad aliquid aliud se habens, sicut si non contineretur ibi plus quam quod nomine sapientiae significatur, non esset aliquid subsistens. Sed quia divinae essentiae perfectio est maior quam quod significatione alicuius nominis comprehendi possit, non sequitur, si nomen relativum, vel quodcumque aliud nomen dictum de Deo, non significat aliquid perfectum, quod divina essentia habeat esse imperfectum, quia divina essentia comprehendit in se omnium generum perfectionem, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0045","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0046","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0047","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod relationes quae sunt in Deo, realiter ab invicem non distinguantur. Quaecumque enim uni et eidem sunt eadem, sibi invicem sunt eadem. Sed omnis relatio in Deo existens est idem secundum rem cum divina essentia. Ergo relationes secundum rem ab invicem non distinguuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0048","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0049","text":"Praeterea, sicut paternitas et filiatio secundum nominis rationem distinguuntur ab essentia divina, ita et bonitas et potentia. Sed propter huiusmodi rationis distinctionem non est aliqua realis distinctio bonitatis et potentiae divinae. Ergo neque paternitatis et filiationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0050","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0051","text":"Praeterea, in divinis non est distinctio realis nisi secundum originem. Sed una relatio non videtur oriri ex alia. Ergo relationes non distinguuntur realiter ab invicem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0052","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0053","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *substantia in divinis continet unitatem, relatio multiplicat Trinitatem*. Si ergo relationes non distinguuntur ab invicem realiter, non erit in divinis Trinitas realis, sed rationis tantum, quod est Sabelliani erroris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0054","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ex eo quod aliquid alicui attribuitur, oportet quod attribuantur ei omnia quae sunt de ratione illius, sicut cuicumque attribuitur homo, oportet quod attribuatur ei esse rationale. De ratione autem relationis est respectus unius ad alterum, secundum quem aliquid alteri opponitur relative. Cum igitur in Deo realiter sit relatio, ut dictum est, oportet quod realiter sit ibi oppositio. Relativa autem oppositio in sui ratione includit distinctionem. Unde oportet quod in Deo sit realis distinctio, non quidem secundum rem absolutam, quae est essentia, in qua est summa unitas et simplicitas; sed secundum rem relativam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0056","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum in III Physic., argumentum illud tenet, quod quaecumque uni et eidem sunt eadem, sibi invicem sunt eadem, in his quae sunt idem re et ratione, sicut tunica et indumentum, non autem in his quae differunt ratione. Unde ibidem dicit quod, licet actio sit idem motui, similiter et passio, non tamen sequitur quod actio et passio sint idem, quia in actione importatur respectus ut a quo est motus in mobili, in passione vero ut qui est ab alio. Et similiter, licet paternitas sit idem secundum rem cum essentia divina, et similiter filiatio, tamen haec duo in suis propriis rationibus important oppositos respectus. Unde distinguuntur ab invicem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0058","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0059","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod potentia et bonitas non important in suis rationibus aliquam oppositionem, unde non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0060","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0061","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quamvis relationes, proprie loquendo, non oriantur vel procedant ab invicem, tamen accipiuntur per oppositum secundum processionem alicuius ab alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0062","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0063","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0064","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo non sint tantum quatuor relationes reales, scilicet paternitas, filiatio, spiratio et processio. Est enim considerare in Deo relationes intelligentis ad intellectum, et volentis ad volitum, quae videntur esse relationes reales, neque sub praedictis continentur. Non ergo sunt solum quatuor relationes reales in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0065","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0066","text":"Praeterea, relationes reales accipiuntur in Deo secundum processionem intelligibilem verbi. Sed relationes intelligibiles multiplicantur in infinitum, ut Avicenna dicit. Ergo in Deo sunt infinitae relationes reales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0067","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0068","text":"Praeterea, ideae sunt in Deo ab aeterno, ut supra dictum est. Non autem distinguuntur ab invicem nisi secundum respectum ad res, ut supra dictum est. Ergo in Deo sunt multo plures relationes aeternae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0069","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0070","text":"Praeterea, aequalitas et similitudo et identitas sunt relationes quaedam; et sunt in Deo ab aeterno. Ergo plures relationes sunt ab aeterno in Deo, quam quae dictae sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0071","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0072","text":"Sed contra, videtur quod sint pauciores. Quia secundum philosophum, in III Physic., *eadem via est de Athenis ad Thebas, et de Thebis ad Athenas*. Ergo videtur quod pari ratione eadem sit relatio de patre ad filium, quae dicitur paternitas, et de filio ad patrem, quae dicitur filiatio. Et sic non sunt quatuor relationes in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0073","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, in V Metaphys., relatio omnis fundatur vel supra quantitatem, ut duplum et dimidium; vel supra actionem et passionem, ut faciens et factum, pater et filius, dominus et servus, et huiusmodi. Cum autem quantitas non sit in Deo (est enim sine quantitate magnus, ut dicit Augustinus). Relinquitur ergo quod realis relatio in Deo esse non possit, nisi super actionem fundata. Non autem super actiones secundum quas procedit aliquid extrinsecum a Deo, quia relationes Dei ad creaturas non sunt realiter in ipso, ut supra dictum est. Unde relinquitur quod relationes reales in Deo non possunt accipi, nisi secundum actiones secundum quas est processio in Deo, non extra, sed intra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","text":"Huiusmodi autem processiones sunt duae tantum, ut supra dictum est, quarum una accipitur secundum actionem intellectus, quae est processio verbi; alia secundum actionem voluntatis, quae est processio amoris. Secundum quamlibet autem processionem oportet duas accipere relationes oppositas, quarum una sit procedentis a principio, et alia ipsius principii. Processio autem verbi dicitur generatio, secundum propriam rationem qua competit rebus viventibus. Relatio autem principii generationis in viventibus perfectis dicitur paternitas, relatio vero procedentis a principio dicitur filiatio. Processio vero amoris non habet nomen proprium, ut supra dictum est, unde neque relationes quae secundum ipsam accipiuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","text":"Sed vocatur relatio principii huius processionis spiratio; relatio autem procedentis, processio; quamvis haec duo nomina ad ipsas processiones vel origines pertineant, et non ad relationes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0077","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in his in quibus differt intellectus et intellectum, volens et volitum, potest esse realis relatio et scientiae ad rem scitam, et volentis ad rem volitam. Sed in Deo est idem omnino intellectus et intellectum, quia intelligendo se intelligit omnia alia, et eadem ratione voluntas et volitum. Unde in Deo huiusmodi relationes non sunt reales, sicut neque relatio eiusdem ad idem. Sed tamen relatio ad verbum est realis, quia verbum intelligitur ut procedens per actionem intelligibilem, non autem ut res intellecta. Cum enim intelligimus lapidem, id quod ex re intellecta concipit intellectus, vocatur verbum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0079","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in nobis relationes intelligibiles in infinitum multiplicantur, quia alio actu intelligit homo lapidem, et alio actu intelligit se intelligere lapidem, et alio etiam intelligit hoc intelligere, et sic in infinitum multiplicantur actus intelligendi, et per consequens relationes intellectae. Sed hoc in Deo non habet locum, quia uno actu tantum omnia intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0081","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod respectus ideales sunt ut intellecti a Deo. Unde ex eorum pluralitate non sequitur quod sint plures relationes in Deo, sed quod Deus cognoscat plures relationes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0083","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod aequalitas et similitudo in Deo non sunt relationes reales, sed rationis tantum, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0085","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0086","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod via est eadem ab uno termino ad alterum, et e converso; sed tamen respectus sunt diversi. Unde ex hoc non potest concludi quod eadem sit relatio patris ad filium, et e converso, sed posset hoc concludi de aliquo absoluto, si esset medium inter ea.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"289fdc0dba3af183c2de883eed91f5abc77ca713a516f4efb7bd7e6542621646","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T140935619539Z_3de9dd9a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 28","source_sha256":"289fdc0dba3af183c2de883eed91f5abc77ca713a516f4efb7bd7e6542621646","sha256":"28fdfaea1e1375f78f52059fbed1a0e485fc1f47e07c1bda90706cbaba59a2ff","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:09:35.619606+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0002","text":"[I q. 28 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0003","text":"Next, the divine relations must be considered. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether there are any real relations in God. Second, whether those relations are the divine essence itself, or are affixed extrinsically. Third, whether there can be in God several relations really distinct from one another. Fourth, concerning the number of these relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0005","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that there are no real relations in God. For Boethius says, in the book *On the Trinity*, that *when one turns the categories to divine predication, all that can be predicated is changed into substance; but relation cannot be predicated at all*. But whatever is really in God can be predicated of Him. Therefore relation is not really in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0007","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0008","text":"Further, Boethius says in the same book that *the relation in the Trinity of Father to Son, and of both to the Holy Spirit, is like that of what is the same to what is the same*. But such a relation is a relation of reason only, because every real relation requires two real extremes. Therefore the relations posited in divine things are not real relations, but relations of reason only.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0009","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0010","text":"Further, the relation of paternity is a relation of principle. But when it is said, God is the principle of creatures, no real relation is implied, but one of reason only. Therefore neither is paternity in divine things a real relation. And by the same reasoning, neither are the other relations posited there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0011","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0012","text":"Further, generation in divine things is according to the procession of the intelligible word. But relations consequent upon the operation of the intellect are relations of reason. Therefore paternity and filiation, which are spoken of in divine things according to generation, are relations of reason only.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0013","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0014","text":"But on the contrary, Father is not called so except from paternity, nor Son from filiation. If, therefore, paternity and filiation are not really in God, it follows that God is not really Father or Son, but only according to the conception of the intellect, which is the Sabellian heresy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0015","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","text":"I answer that certain relations are really in divine things. For the evidence of this, it must be considered that only among those things which are said relative to something are some found to be according to reason only and not according to reality. This is not the case in the other genera, because the other genera, such as quantity and quality, according to their proper account signify something inhering in something. But those things which are said relative to something, according to their proper account signify only a respect to another. This respect is sometimes in the very nature of things; as when certain things are, according to their nature, ordered to one another and have an inclination toward one another. And relations of this sort must be real. Thus in a heavy body there is an inclination and order toward the middle place, whence there is a certain respect in the heavy thing itself with respect to the middle place. And it is similar in other such cases.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","text":"But sometimes the respect signified by those things which are said relative to something is only in the apprehension of reason, comparing one thing with another, and then it is a relation of reason only; as when reason compares man to animal, as species to genus. But when something proceeds from a principle of the same nature, it is necessary that both, namely the proceeding thing and that from which it proceeds, agree in the same order, and thus it is necessary that they have real respects toward one another. Therefore, since the processions in divine things are in identity of nature, as has been shown, it is necessary that the relations taken according to the divine processions be real relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0018","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that relation is said not to be predicated in God at all according to the proper account of that which is said relative to something; namely, insofar as the proper account of that which is said relative to something is not taken by comparison with that in which the relation exists, but by respect to another. Therefore, by this he did not intend to exclude that relation was in God, but that it was not predicated according to the mode of an inhering thing according to the proper account of relation, but rather according to the mode of something standing in relation to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0020","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that the relation implied by this name “same” is a relation of reason only, if “same” is taken simply, because a relation of this sort can consist only in a certain ordering which reason discovers of something to itself, according to two considerations of it. But it is otherwise when certain things are said to be the same, not in number, but in the nature of genus or species. Therefore Boethius likens the relations which are in divine things to the relation of identity, not in every respect, but only in this: that through such relations the substance is not diversified, just as neither is it through the relation of identity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0022","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that, since the creature proceeds from God in diversity of nature, God is outside the order of the whole of creation, nor is His relation to creatures from His nature. For He does not produce creatures from the necessity of His nature, but through intellect and will, as was said above. Therefore there is no real relation in God to creatures. But there is a real relation in creatures to God, because creatures are contained under the divine order, and it belongs to their nature that they depend upon God. But the divine processions are in the same nature. Hence the reasoning is not similar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0024","text":"[I q. 28 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that relations which follow the operation of the intellect alone in the things themselves understood are relations of reason only, because reason, namely, devises them between two things understood. But relations which follow the operation of the intellect, which are between the word proceeding intellectually and that from which it proceeds, are not relations of reason only, but of reality, because the intellect and reason itself is a certain thing, and is really related to that which proceeds intelligibly, just as a corporeal thing is related to that which proceeds corporeally. And thus paternity and filiation are real relations in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0027","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0028","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that relation in God is not the same as His essence. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* V, that *not everything that is said in God is said according to substance. For something is said relatively, as father to son, but these are not said according to substance*. Therefore relation is not the divine essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0029","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0030","text":"Further, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* VII, *every thing that is spoken of relatively is also something besides the relative; as man [is] master, and man [is] servant*. Therefore, if there are any relations in God, there must be in God something other than relations. But this other can only be the essence. Therefore the essence is other than the relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0031","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0032","text":"Further, the being of a relative is to be disposed toward another, as is said in the *Categories*. Therefore, if relation is the divine essence itself, it follows that the being of the divine essence is to be disposed toward another, which is repugnant to the perfection of the divine being, which is most absolute and subsisting through itself, as was shown above. Therefore relation is not the divine essence itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0033","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0034","text":"On the contrary, every thing that is not the divine essence is a creature. But relation belongs to God really. Therefore, if it is not the divine essence, it will be a creature, and thus worship of latria is not to be rendered to it, contrary to what is sung in the preface: *that in the persons the property, and in the majesty the equality, may be adored*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0035","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0036","text":"I answer that it must be said that Gilbert of Poitiers is said to have erred concerning this, but afterwards to have retracted his error at the Council of Rheims. For he said that relations in the divine are attendant, or externally affixed. To make this evident, it must be considered that in each of the nine genera of accidents there are two things to consider. One of these is the being that belongs to each of them insofar as it is an accident. And this, commonly in all, is to inhere in a subject; for the being of an accident is to inhere. The other thing which can be considered in each is the proper account of each of those genera. And indeed in the other genera besides relation, such as quantity and quality, even the proper account of the genus is taken according to comparison with the subject; for quantity is called the measure of substance, but quality the disposition of substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","text":"But the proper account of relation is not taken according to comparison with that in which it is, but according to comparison with something external. Therefore, if we consider relations, even in created things, according to what they are as relations, they are found to be attendant, not intrinsically affixed; as though signifying a respect in some way touching the thing related itself, insofar as it tends from it toward another. But if relation is considered insofar as it is an accident, then it inheres in the subject and has accidental being in it. But Gilbert of Poitiers considered relation in the first way only. Now whatever in created things has accidental being, insofar as it is transferred to God has substantial being, for nothing is in God as an accident in a subject, but whatever is in God is His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","text":"Thus, therefore, on the side on which relation in created things has accidental being in a subject, relation really existing in God has the being of the divine essence, being altogether the same as it. But in this, that it is said relatively, no relation to the essence is signified, but rather to its opposite. And thus it is manifest that relation really existing in God is the same as the essence in reality; and it differs only according to the account of understanding, insofar as in relation a respect to its opposite is implied, which is not implied in the name of essence. It is clear, therefore, that in God the being of relation and the being of essence are not other, but one and the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0039","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that those words of Augustine do not pertain to the point that paternity, or another relation which is in God, is not, according to its being, the same as the divine essence; but [to the point] that it is not predicated according to the mode of substance, as existing in that of which it is said, but as being disposed toward another. And for this reason only two predicaments are said to be in the divine. For the other predicaments imply a relation to that of which they are said, both according to their being and according to the account of their proper genus; but nothing that is in God can have a relation to that in which it is, or of which it is said, except a relation of identity, because of God's supreme simplicity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0041","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0042","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as in created things, in that which is spoken of relatively, there is to be found not only a respect to another, but also something absolute, so too in God, yet in different ways. For that which is found in a creature besides that which is contained under the signification of the relative name is another thing; but in God it is not another thing, but one and the same, which is not perfectly expressed by the name of relation, as though comprehended under the signification of such a name. For it was said above, when divine names were being treated, that more is contained in the perfection of the divine essence than can be signified by any name. Hence it does not follow that in God, besides relation, there is something else in reality, but only when the account of the names is considered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0043","text":"[I q. 28 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0044","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if nothing more were contained in the divine perfection than what the relative name signifies, it would follow that its being was imperfect, as being disposed toward something else; just as, if nothing more were contained there than what is signified by the name of wisdom, it would not be something subsisting. But because the perfection of the divine essence is greater than can be comprehended by the signification of any name, it does not follow that, if a relative name, or any other name said of God, does not signify something perfect, the divine essence has imperfect being, because the divine essence comprehends in itself the perfection of all genera, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0045","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0046","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0047","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the relations which are in God are not really distinguished from one another. For whatever things are the same as one and the same thing are the same as one another. But every relation existing in God is the same in reality as the divine essence. Therefore the relations are not distinguished from one another in reality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0048","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0049","text":"Moreover, just as paternity and filiation are distinguished from the divine essence according to the notion of the name, so too are goodness and power. But on account of a distinction of this sort in notion, there is no real distinction between divine goodness and power. Therefore neither is there one between paternity and filiation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0050","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0051","text":"Moreover, in divine things there is no real distinction except according to origin. But one relation does not seem to arise from another. Therefore relations are not really distinguished from one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0052","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0053","text":"But on the contrary, Boethius says, in the book *On the Trinity*, that *substance in divine things contains unity; relation multiplies the Trinity*. Therefore, if relations are not really distinguished from one another, there will not be a real Trinity in divine things, but only one of reason, which is the error of Sabellius.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0054","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","text":"I answer that it must be said that, from the fact that something is attributed to something, all the things that belong to the notion of that thing must be attributed to it; just as, to whomever “man” is attributed, being rational must be attributed. Now it belongs to the notion of relation to be a reference of one to another, according to which something is relatively opposed to another. Therefore, since relation is really in God, as has been said, opposition must really be there. But relative opposition includes distinction in its notion. Hence there must be a real distinction in God, not indeed according to the absolute reality, which is the essence, in which there is supreme unity and simplicity, but according to relative reality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0056","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, according to the Philosopher in *Physics* III, that argument holds—that whatever things are the same as one and the same thing are the same as one another—in those things that are the same in reality and in notion, such as a tunic and a garment, but not in those that differ in notion. Hence he says in the same place that, although action is the same as motion, and likewise passion, it nevertheless does not follow that action and passion are the same, because in action there is implied a reference as that from which motion is in the movable thing, whereas in passion, as that which is from another. And similarly, although paternity is the same in reality as the divine essence, and likewise filiation, nevertheless these two imply opposed references in their proper notions. Hence they are distinguished from one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0058","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0059","text":"To the second it must be said that power and goodness do not imply any opposition in their notions; hence the reasoning is not similar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0060","text":"[I q. 28 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0061","text":"To the third it must be said that, although relations, properly speaking, do not arise or proceed from one another, nevertheless they are taken as opposites according to the procession of one thing from another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0062","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0063","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0064","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that there are not only four real relations in God, namely, paternity, filiation, spiration, and procession. For in God one may consider the relations of the one understanding to the intellect, and of the one willing to the thing willed, which seem to be real relations and are not contained under those stated above. Therefore there are not only four real relations in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0065","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0066","text":"Moreover, real relations are taken in God according to the intelligible procession of the Word. But intelligible relations are multiplied to infinity, as Avicenna says. Therefore there are infinite real relations in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0067","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0068","text":"Moreover, ideas are in God from eternity, as was said above. But they are not distinguished from one another except according to their reference to things, as was said above. Therefore there are many more eternal relations in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0069","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0070","text":"Moreover, equality, likeness, and identity are certain relations; and they are in God from eternity. Therefore there are more relations from eternity in God than those that have been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0071","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0072","text":"But on the contrary, it seems that there are fewer. For according to the Philosopher, in *Physics* III, *the road from Athens to Thebes is the same as that from Thebes to Athens*. Therefore it seems that, by equal reasoning, the relation from father to son, which is called paternity, is the same as that from son to father, which is called filiation. And thus there are not four relations in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0073","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","text":"I answer that, according to the Philosopher, in Metaphysics V, every relation is founded either upon quantity, as double and half; or upon action and passion, as maker and thing made, father and son, master and servant, and the like. But since quantity is not in God (for He is great without quantity, as Augustine says), it remains therefore that a real relation cannot be in God unless it is founded upon action. Yet not upon actions according to which something proceeds externally from God, because God’s relations to creatures are not really in Him, as was said above. Hence it remains that real relations in God can be understood only according to actions according to which there is procession in God, not outside, but within.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","text":"But there are only two processions of this kind, as was said above, one of which is understood according to the action of the intellect, which is the procession of the Word; the other according to the action of the will, which is the procession of love. According to each procession, moreover, two opposite relations must be understood, one of the one proceeding from the principle, and the other of the principle itself. Now the procession of the Word is called generation, according to the proper notion by which it belongs to living things. But the relation of the principle of generation in perfect living things is called paternity, whereas the relation of the one proceeding from the principle is called filiation. The procession of love, however, has no proper name, as was said above; hence neither do the relations understood according to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","text":"But the relation of the principle of this procession is called spiration; while the relation of the one proceeding is called procession; although these two names pertain to the processions or origins themselves, and not to the relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0077","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in those things in which the intellect and the thing understood differ, and the one willing and the thing willed differ, there can be a real relation both of knowledge to the thing known and of the one willing to the thing willed. But in God, intellect and thing understood are altogether the same, because by understanding Himself He understands all other things; and, for the same reason, will and thing willed are the same. Hence in God such relations are not real, just as neither is the relation of the same to itself. Yet the relation to the Word is real, because the Word is understood as proceeding through an intelligible action, but not as a thing understood. For when we understand a stone, that which the intellect conceives from the thing understood is called a word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0079","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","text":"To the second, it must be said that in us intelligible relations are multiplied to infinity, because by one act a man understands a stone, by another act he understands that he understands a stone, and by yet another he understands this understanding; and thus acts of understanding, and consequently understood relations, are multiplied to infinity. But this has no place in God, because He understands all things by one act only.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0081","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","text":"To the third, it must be said that ideal respects are as understood by God. Hence from their plurality it does not follow that there are several relations in God, but that God knows several relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0083","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that equality and likeness in God are not real relations, but only relations of reason, as will be clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0085","text":"[I q. 28 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0086","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the way is the same from one terminus to the other, and conversely; yet the respects are diverse. Hence from this it cannot be concluded that the relation of father to son is the same, and conversely; but this could be concluded concerning something absolute, if there were a middle between them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0086"]}],"source_digest":"b16558274ee858bcb0d7e66ec9faf84e6fb7d6b589af50ffb8206242e642f163","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 28"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 28","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21","title":"First Part · Question 29","order":30,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_21","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 29 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29648)\n\nPraemissis autem his quae de processionibus et relationibus praecognoscenda videbantur, necessarium est aggredi de personis. Et primo, secundum considerationem absolutam; et deinde secundum comparativam considerationem. Oportet autem absolute de personis, primo quidem in communi considerare; deinde de singulis personis. Ad communem autem considerationem personarum quatuor pertinere videntur, primo quidem, significatio huius nominis persona; secundo vero, numerus personarum; tertio, ea quae consequuntur numerum personarum, vel ei opponuntur, ut diversitas et solitudo, et huiusmodi; quarto vero, ea quae pertinent ad notitiam personarum. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de definitione personae. Secundo, de comparatione personae ad essentiam, subsistentiam et hypostasim. Tertio, utrum nomen personae competat in divinis. Quarto, quid ibi significet.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29649)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod incompetens sit definitio personae quam Boetius assignat in libro de duabus naturis, quae talis est, *persona est rationalis naturae individua substantia*. Nullum enim singulare definitur. Sed persona significat quoddam singulare. Ergo persona inconvenienter definitur.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29650)\n\nPraeterea, substantia, prout ponitur in definitione personae, aut sumitur pro substantia prima, aut pro substantia secunda. Si pro substantia prima, superflue additur individua, quia substantia prima est substantia individua. Si vero stat pro substantia secunda, falso additur, et est oppositio in adiecto, nam secundae substantiae dicuntur genera vel species. Ergo definitio est male assignata.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29651)\n\nPraeterea, nomen intentionis non debet poni in definitione rei. Non enim esset bona assignatio, si quis diceret, homo est species animalis, homo enim est nomen rei, et species est nomen intentionis. Cum igitur persona sit nomen rei (significat enim substantiam quandam rationalis naturae), inconvenienter individuum, quod est nomen intentionis, in eius definitione ponitur.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29652)\n\nPraeterea, natura est principium motus et quietis in eo in quo est per se et non per accidens, ut dicitur in II Physic. Sed persona est in rebus immobilibus, sicut in Deo et in Angelis. Non ergo in definitione personae debuit poni natura, sed magis essentia.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29653)\n\nPraeterea, anima separata est rationalis naturae individua substantia. Non autem est persona. Inconvenienter ergo persona sic definitur.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29654)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, licet universale et particulare inveniantur in omnibus generibus, tamen speciali quodam modo individuum invenitur in genere substantiae. Substantia enim individuatur per seipsam, sed accidentia individuantur per subiectum, quod est substantia, dicitur enim haec albedo, inquantum est in hoc subiecto. Unde etiam convenienter individua substantiae habent aliquod speciale nomen prae aliis, dicuntur enim hypostases, vel primae substantiae. Sed adhuc quodam specialiori et perfectiori modo invenitur particulare et individuum in substantiis rationalibus, quae habent dominium sui actus, et non solum aguntur, sicut alia, sed per se agunt, actiones autem in singularibus sunt. Et ideo etiam inter ceteras substantias quoddam speciale nomen habent singularia rationalis naturae. Et hoc nomen est persona. Et ideo in praedicta definitione personae ponitur substantia individua, inquantum significat singulare in genere substantiae, additur autem rationalis naturae, inquantum significat singulare in rationalibus substantiis.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29655)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet hoc singulare vel illud definiri non possit, tamen id quod pertinet ad communem rationem singularitatis, definiri potest, et sic philosophus definit substantiam primam. Et hoc modo definit Boetius personam.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29656)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, secundum quosdam, substantia in definitione personae ponitur pro substantia prima, quae est hypostasis. Neque tamen superflue additur individua. Quia nomine hypostasis vel substantiae primae, excluditur ratio universalis et partis (non enim dicimus quod homo communis sit hypostasis, neque etiam manus, cum sit pars), sed per hoc quod additur individuum, excluditur a persona ratio assumptibilis; humana enim natura in Christo non est persona, quia est assumpta a digniori, scilicet a verbo Dei. Sed melius dicendum est quod substantia accipitur communiter, prout dividitur per primam et secundam, et per hoc quod additur individua, trahitur ad standum pro substantia prima.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29657)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quia substantiales differentiae non sunt nobis notae, vel etiam nominatae non sunt, oportet interdum uti differentiis accidentalibus loco substantialium, puta si quis diceret, ignis est corpus simplex, calidum et siccum, accidentia enim propria sunt effectus formarum substantialium, et manifestant eas. Et similiter nomina intentionum possunt accipi ad definiendum res, secundum quod accipiuntur pro aliquibus nominibus rerum quae non sunt posita. Et sic hoc nomen individuum ponitur in definitione personae, ad designandum modum subsistendi qui competit substantiis particularibus.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29658)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, in V Metaphys., nomen naturae primo impositum est ad significandam generationem viventium, quae dicitur nativitas. Et quia huiusmodi generatio est a principio intrinseco, extensum est hoc nomen ad significandum principium intrinsecum cuiuscumque motus. Et sic definitur natura in II Physic. Et quia huiusmodi principium est formale vel materiale, communiter tam materia quam forma dicitur natura. Et quia per formam completur essentia uniuscuiusque rei, communiter essentia uniuscuiusque rei, quam significat eius definitio, vocatur natura. Et sic accipitur hic natura. Unde Boetius in eodem libro dicit quod *natura est unumquodque informans specifica differentia*, specifica enim differentia est quae complet definitionem, et sumitur a propria forma rei. Et ideo convenientius fuit quod in definitione personae, quae est singulare alicuius generis determinati, uteretur nomine naturae, quam essentiae, quae sumitur ab esse, quod est communissimum.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29659)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod anima est pars humanae speciei, et ideo, licet sit separata, quia tamen retinet naturam unibilitatis, non potest dici substantia individua quae est hypostasis vel substantia prima; sicut nec manus, nec quaecumque alia partium hominis. Et sic non competit ei neque definitio personae, neque nomen.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29660)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod persona sit idem quod hypostasis, subsistentia et essentia. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Duab. Natur., quod *Graeci naturae rationalis individuam substantiam hypostaseos nomine vocaverunt*. Sed hoc etiam, apud nos, significat nomen personae. Ergo persona omnino idem est quod hypostasis.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29661)\n\nPraeterea, sicut in divinis dicimus tres personas, ita in divinis dicimus tres subsistentias, quod non esset, nisi persona et subsistentia idem significarent. Ergo idem significant persona et subsistentia.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29662)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius dicit, in commento praedicamentorum, quod usia, quod est idem quod essentia, significat compositum ex materia et forma. Id autem quod est compositum ex materia et forma, est individuum substantiae, quod et hypostasis et persona dicitur. Ergo omnia praedicta nomina idem significare videntur.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29663)\n\nSed contra est quod Boetius dicit, in libro de Duab. Natur., quod *genera et species subsistunt tantum; individua vero non modo subsistunt, verum etiam substant*. Sed a subsistendo dicuntur subsistentiae, sicut a substando substantiae vel hypostases. Cum igitur esse hypostases vel personas non conveniat generibus vel speciebus, hypostases vel personae non sunt idem quod subsistentiae.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29664)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius dicit, in commento praedicamentorum, quod hypostasis dicitur materia, usiosis autem, idest subsistentia, dicitur forma. Sed neque forma neque materia potest dici persona. Ergo persona differt a praedictis.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29665)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, in V Metaphys., substantia dicitur dupliciter. Uno modo dicitur substantia quidditas rei, quam significat definitio, secundum quod dicimus quod definitio significat substantiam rei, quam quidem substantiam Graeci usiam vocant, quod nos essentiam dicere possumus. Alio modo dicitur substantia subiectum vel suppositum quod subsistit in genere substantiae. Et hoc quidem, communiter accipiendo, nominari potest et nomine significante intentionem, et sic dicitur suppositum. Nominatur etiam tribus nominibus significantibus rem, quae quidem sunt res naturae, subsistentia et hypostasis, secundum triplicem considerationem substantiae sic dictae. Secundum enim quod per se existit et non in alio, vocatur subsistentia, illa enim subsistere dicimus, quae non in alio, sed in se existunt. Secundum vero quod supponitur alicui naturae communi, sic dicitur res naturae; sicut hic homo est res naturae humanae. Secundum vero quod supponitur accidentibus, dicitur hypostasis vel substantia. Quod autem haec tria nomina significant communiter in toto genere substantiarum, hoc nomen persona significat in genere rationalium substantiarum.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29666)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hypostasis, apud Graecos, ex propria significatione nominis habet quod significet quodcumque individuum substantiae, sed ex usu loquendi habet quod sumatur pro individuo rationalis naturae, ratione suae excellentiae.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29667)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut nos dicimus in divinis pluraliter tres personas et tres subsistentias, ita Graeci dicunt tres hypostases. Sed quia nomen substantiae, quod secundum proprietatem significationis respondet hypostasi, aequivocatur apud nos, cum quandoque significet essentiam, quandoque hypostasim; ne possit esse erroris occasio, maluerunt pro hypostasi transferre subsistentiam, quam substantiam.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29668)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod essentia proprie est id quod significatur per definitionem. Definitio autem complectitur principia speciei, non autem principia individualia. Unde in rebus compositis ex materia et forma, essentia significat non solum formam, nec solum materiam, sed compositum ex materia et forma communi, prout sunt principia speciei. Sed compositum ex hac materia et ex hac forma, habet rationem hypostasis et personae, anima enim et caro et os sunt de ratione hominis, sed haec anima et haec caro et hoc os sunt de ratione huius hominis. Et ideo hypostasis et persona addunt supra rationem essentiae principia individualia; neque sunt idem cum essentia in compositis ex materia et forma, ut supra dictum est, cum de simplicitate divina ageretur.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29669)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Boetius dicit genera et species subsistere, inquantum individuis aliquibus competit subsistere, ex eo quod sunt sub generibus et speciebus in praedicamento substantiae comprehensis, non quod ipsae species vel genera subsistant, nisi secundum opinionem Platonis, qui posuit species rerum separatim subsistere a singularibus. Substare vero competit eisdem individuis in ordine ad accidentia, quae sunt praeter rationem generum et specierum.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29670)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod individuum compositum ex materia et forma, habet quod substet accidenti, ex proprietate materiae. Unde et Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin., *forma simplex subiectum esse non potest*. Sed quod per se subsistat, habet ex proprietate suae formae, quae non advenit rei subsistenti, sed dat esse actuale materiae, ut sic individuum subsistere possit. Propter hoc ergo hypostasim attribuit materiae, et usiosim, sive subsistentiam, formae, quia materia est principium substandi, et forma est principium subsistendi.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29671)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomen personae non sit ponendum in divinis. Dicit enim Dionysius, in principio de Div. Nom. *universaliter non est audendum aliquid dicere nec cogitare de supersubstantiali occulta divinitate, praeter ea quae divinitus nobis ex sanctis eloquiis sunt expressa*. Sed nomen personae non exprimitur nobis in sacra Scriptura novi vel veteris testamenti. Ergo non est nomine personae utendum in divinis.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29672)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius dicit, in libro de Duab. Natur., *nomen personae videtur traductum ex his personis quae in comoediis tragoediisque homines repraesentabant; persona enim dicta est a personando, quia concavitate ipsa maior necesse est ut volvatur sonus. Graeci vero has personas prosopa vocant, ab eo quod ponantur in facie, atque ante oculos obtegant vultum*. Sed hoc non potest competere in divinis, nisi forte secundum metaphoram. Ergo nomen personae non dicitur de Deo nisi metaphorice.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29673)\n\nPraeterea, omnis persona est hypostasis. Sed nomen hypostasis non videtur Deo competere, cum, secundum Boetium, significet id quod subiicitur accidentibus, quae in Deo non sunt. Hieronymus etiam dicit quod in hoc nomine hypostasis, venenum latet sub melle. Ergo hoc nomen persona non est dicendum de Deo.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29674)\n\nPraeterea, a quocumque removetur definitio, et definitum. Sed definitio personae supra posita non videtur Deo competere. Tum quia ratio importat discursivam cognitionem, quae non competit Deo, ut supra ostensum est, et sic Deus non potest dici rationalis naturae. Tum etiam quia Deus dici non potest individua substantia, cum principium individuationis sit materia, Deus autem immaterialis est; neque etiam accidentibus substat, ut substantia dici possit. Nomen ergo personae Deo attribui non debet.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29675)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in symbolo Athanasii, *alia est persona patris, alia filii, alia spiritus sancti*.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29676)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod persona significat id quod est perfectissimum in tota natura, scilicet subsistens in rationali natura. Unde, cum omne illud quod est perfectionis, Deo sit attribuendum, eo quod eius essentia continet in se omnem perfectionem; conveniens est ut hoc nomen persona de Deo dicatur. Non tamen eodem modo quo dicitur de creaturis, sed excellentiori modo; sicut et alia nomina quae, creaturis a nobis imposita, Deo attribuuntur; sicut supra ostensum est, cum de divinis nominibus ageretur.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29677)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet nomen personae in Scriptura veteris vel novi testamenti non inveniatur dictum de Deo, tamen id quod nomen significat, multipliciter in sacra Scriptura invenitur assertum de Deo; scilicet quod est maxime per se ens, et perfectissime intelligens. Si autem oporteret de Deo dici solum illa, secundum vocem, quae sacra Scriptura de Deo tradit, sequeretur quod nunquam in alia lingua posset aliquis loqui de Deo, nisi in illa in qua primo tradita est Scriptura veteris vel novi testamenti. Ad inveniendum autem nova nomina, antiquam fidem de Deo significantia, coegit necessitas disputandi cum haereticis. Nec haec novitas vitanda est, cum non sit profana, utpote a Scripturarum sensu non discordans, docet autem apostolus profanas vocum novitates vitare, I ad Tim. ult.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29678)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quamvis hoc nomen persona non conveniat Deo quantum ad id a quo impositum est nomen, tamen quantum ad id ad quod significandum imponitur, maxime Deo convenit. Quia enim in comoediis et tragoediis repraesentabantur aliqui homines famosi, impositum est hoc nomen persona ad significandum aliquos dignitatem habentes. Unde consueverunt dici personae in Ecclesiis, quae habent aliquam dignitatem. Propter quod quidam definiunt personam, dicentes quod *persona est hypostasis proprietate distincta ad dignitatem pertinente*. Et quia magnae dignitatis est in rationali natura subsistere, ideo omne individuum rationalis naturae dicitur persona, ut dictum est. Sed dignitas divinae naturae excedit omnem dignitatem, et secundum hoc maxime competit Deo nomen personae.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29679)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nomen hypostasis non competit Deo quantum ad id a quo est impositum nomen, cum non substet accidentibus, competit autem ei quantum ad id, quod est impositum ad significandum rem subsistentem. Hieronymus autem dicit sub hoc nomine venenum latere, quia antequam significatio huius nominis esset plene nota apud Latinos, haeretici per hoc nomen simplices decipiebant, ut confiterentur plures essentias, sicut confitentur plures hypostases; propter hoc quod nomen substantiae, cui respondet in Graeco nomen hypostasis, communiter accipitur apud nos pro essentia.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29680)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Deus potest dici rationalis naturae, secundum quod ratio non importat discursum, sed communiter intellectualem naturam. Individuum autem Deo competere non potest quantum ad hoc quod individuationis principium est materia, sed solum secundum quod importat incommunicabilitatem. Substantia vero convenit Deo, secundum quod significat existere per se. Quidam tamen dicunt quod definitio superius a Boetio data, non est definitio personae secundum quod personas in Deo dicimus. Propter quod Ricardus de sancto Victore, corrigere volens hanc definitionem, dixit quod persona, secundum quod de Deo dicitur, est divinae naturae incommunicabilis existentia.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29681)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen persona non significet relationem, sed substantiam, in divinis. Dicit enim Augustinus, in VII de Trin., *cum dicimus personam patris, non aliud dicimus quam substantiam patris; ad se quippe dicitur persona, non ad filium*.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29682)\n\nPraeterea, quid quaerit de essentia. Sed, sicut dicit Augustinus in eodem loco, cum dicitur, *tres sunt qui testimonium dant in caelo, pater, verbum et spiritus* sanctus; et quaeritur, quid tres? Respondetur, tres personae. Ergo hoc nomen persona significat essentiam.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29683)\n\nPraeterea, secundum philosophum, IV Metaphys., id quod significatur per nomen, est eius definitio. Sed definitio personae est rationalis naturae individua substantia, ut dictum est. Ergo hoc nomen persona significat substantiam.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29684)\n\nPraeterea, persona in hominibus et Angelis non significat relationem, sed aliquid absolutum. Si igitur in Deo significaret relationem, diceretur aequivoce de Deo et hominibus et Angelis.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29685)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod omne nomen ad personas pertinens, relationem significat. Sed nullum nomen magis pertinet ad personas, quam hoc nomen persona. Ergo hoc nomen persona relationem significat.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29686)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa significationem huius nominis persona in divinis, difficultatem ingerit quod pluraliter de tribus praedicatur, praeter naturam essentialium nominum; neque etiam ad aliquid dicitur, sicut nomina quae relationem significant. Unde quibusdam visum est quod hoc nomen persona simpliciter, ex virtute vocabuli, essentiam significet in divinis, sicut hoc nomen Deus, et hoc nomen sapiens, sed propter instantiam haereticorum, est accommodatum, ex ordinatione Concilii, ut possit poni pro relativis; et praecipue in plurali, vel cum nomine partitivo, ut cum dicimus tres personas, vel alia est persona patris, alia filii. In singulari vero potest sumi pro absoluto, et pro relativo. Sed haec non videtur sufficiens ratio. Quia si hoc nomen persona, ex vi suae significationis, non habet quod significet nisi essentiam in divinis; ex hoc quod dictum est tres personas, non fuisset haereticorum quietata calumnia, sed maioris calumniae data esset eis occasio. Et ideo alii dixerunt quod hoc nomen persona in divinis significat simul essentiam et relationem. Quorum quidam dixerunt quod significat essentiam in recto, et relationem in obliquo. Quia persona dicitur quasi per se una, unitas autem pertinet ad essentiam. Quod autem dicitur per se, implicat relationem oblique, intelligitur enim pater per se esse, quasi relatione distinctus a filio. Quidam vero dixerunt e converso, quod significat relationem in recto, et essentiam in obliquo, quia in definitione personae, natura ponitur in obliquo. Et isti propinquius ad veritatem accesserunt. Ad evidentiam igitur huius quaestionis, considerandum est quod aliquid est de significatione minus communis, quod tamen non est de significatione magis communis, rationale enim includitur in significatione hominis, quod tamen non est de significatione animalis. Unde aliud est quaerere de significatione animalis, et aliud est quaerere de significatione animalis quod est homo. Similiter aliud est quaerere de significatione huius nominis persona in communi, et aliud de significatione personae divinae. Persona enim in communi significat substantiam individuam rationalis naturae, ut dictum est. Individuum autem est quod est in se indistinctum, ab aliis vero distinctum. Persona igitur, in quacumque natura, significat id quod est distinctum in natura illa sicut in humana natura significat has carnes et haec ossa et hanc animam, quae sunt principia individuantia hominem; quae quidem, licet non sint de significatione personae, sunt tamen de significatione personae humanae. Distinctio autem in divinis non fit nisi per relationes originis, ut dictum est supra. Relatio autem in divinis non est sicut accidens inhaerens subiecto, sed est ipsa divina essentia, unde est subsistens, sicut essentia divina subsistit. Sicut ergo deitas est Deus, ita paternitas divina est Deus pater, qui est persona divina. Persona igitur divina significat relationem ut subsistentem. Et hoc est significare relationem per modum substantiae quae est hypostasis subsistens in natura divina; licet subsistens in natura divina non sit aliud quam natura divina. Et secundum hoc, verum est quod hoc nomen persona significat relationem in recto, et essentiam in obliquo, non tamen relationem inquantum est relatio, sed inquantum significatur per modum hypostasis. Similiter etiam significat essentiam in recto, et relationem in obliquo, inquantum essentia idem est quod hypostasis; hypostasis autem significatur in divinis ut relatione distincta; et sic relatio, per modum relationis significata, cadit in ratione personae in obliquo. Et secundum hoc etiam dici potest, quod haec significatio huius nominis persona non erat percepta ante haereticorum calumniam, unde non erat in usu hoc nomen persona, nisi sicut unum aliorum absolutorum. Sed postmodum accommodatum est hoc nomen persona ad standum pro relativo, ex congruentia suae significationis, ut scilicet hoc quod stat pro relativo, non solum habeat ex usu, ut prima opinio dicebat, sed etiam ex significatione sua.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29687)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc nomen persona dicitur ad se, non ad alterum, quia significat relationem, non per modum relationis, sed per modum substantiae quae est hypostasis. Et secundum hoc Augustinus dicit quod significat essentiam, prout in Deo essentia est idem cum hypostasi, quia in Deo non differt quod est et quo est.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29688)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod quid quandoque quaerit de natura quam significat definitio; ut cum quaeritur, quid est homo? Et respondetur, animal rationale mortale. Quandoque vero quaerit suppositum; ut cum quaeritur, quid natat in mari? Et respondetur, piscis. Et sic quaerentibus quid tres? Responsum est, tres personae.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29689)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in intellectu substantiae individuae, idest distinctae vel incommunicabilis, intelligitur in divinis relatio, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29690)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod diversa ratio minus communium non facit aequivocationem in magis communi. Licet enim sit alia propria definitio equi et asini, tamen univocantur in nomine animalis, quia communis definitio animalis convenit utrique. Unde non sequitur quod, licet in significatione personae divinae contineatur relatio, non autem in significatione angelicae personae vel humanae, quod nomen personae aequivoce dicatur. Licet nec etiam dicatur univoce, cum nihil univoce de Deo dici possit et de creaturis, ut supra ostensum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0002","text":"[I q. 29 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0003","text":"Praemissis autem his quae de processionibus et relationibus praecognoscenda videbantur, necessarium est aggredi de personis. Et primo, secundum considerationem absolutam; et deinde secundum comparativam considerationem. Oportet autem absolute de personis, primo quidem in communi considerare; deinde de singulis personis. Ad communem autem considerationem personarum quatuor pertinere videntur, primo quidem, significatio huius nominis persona; secundo vero, numerus personarum; tertio, ea quae consequuntur numerum personarum, vel ei opponuntur, ut diversitas et solitudo, et huiusmodi; quarto vero, ea quae pertinent ad notitiam personarum. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de definitione personae. Secundo, de comparatione personae ad essentiam, subsistentiam et hypostasim. Tertio, utrum nomen personae competat in divinis. Quarto, quid ibi significet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0005","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod incompetens sit definitio personae quam Boetius assignat in libro de duabus naturis, quae talis est, *persona est rationalis naturae individua substantia*. Nullum enim singulare definitur. Sed persona significat quoddam singulare. Ergo persona inconvenienter definitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0007","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0008","text":"Praeterea, substantia, prout ponitur in definitione personae, aut sumitur pro substantia prima, aut pro substantia secunda. Si pro substantia prima, superflue additur individua, quia substantia prima est substantia individua. Si vero stat pro substantia secunda, falso additur, et est oppositio in adiecto, nam secundae substantiae dicuntur genera vel species. Ergo definitio est male assignata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0009","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0010","text":"Praeterea, nomen intentionis non debet poni in definitione rei. Non enim esset bona assignatio, si quis diceret, homo est species animalis, homo enim est nomen rei, et species est nomen intentionis. Cum igitur persona sit nomen rei (significat enim substantiam quandam rationalis naturae), inconvenienter individuum, quod est nomen intentionis, in eius definitione ponitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0011","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0012","text":"Praeterea, natura est principium motus et quietis in eo in quo est per se et non per accidens, ut dicitur in II Physic. Sed persona est in rebus immobilibus, sicut in Deo et in Angelis. Non ergo in definitione personae debuit poni natura, sed magis essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0013","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0014","text":"Praeterea, anima separata est rationalis naturae individua substantia. Non autem est persona. Inconvenienter ergo persona sic definitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0015","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, licet universale et particulare inveniantur in omnibus generibus, tamen speciali quodam modo individuum invenitur in genere substantiae. Substantia enim individuatur per seipsam, sed accidentia individuantur per subiectum, quod est substantia, dicitur enim haec albedo, inquantum est in hoc subiecto. Unde etiam convenienter individua substantiae habent aliquod speciale nomen prae aliis, dicuntur enim hypostases, vel primae substantiae. Sed adhuc quodam specialiori et perfectiori modo invenitur particulare et individuum in substantiis rationalibus, quae habent dominium sui actus, et non solum aguntur, sicut alia, sed per se agunt, actiones autem in singularibus sunt. Et ideo etiam inter ceteras substantias quoddam speciale nomen habent singularia rationalis naturae. Et hoc nomen est persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017","text":"Et ideo in praedicta definitione personae ponitur substantia individua, inquantum significat singulare in genere substantiae, additur autem rationalis naturae, inquantum significat singulare in rationalibus substantiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0018","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet hoc singulare vel illud definiri non possit, tamen id quod pertinet ad communem rationem singularitatis, definiri potest, et sic philosophus definit substantiam primam. Et hoc modo definit Boetius personam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0020","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, secundum quosdam, substantia in definitione personae ponitur pro substantia prima, quae est hypostasis. Neque tamen superflue additur individua. Quia nomine hypostasis vel substantiae primae, excluditur ratio universalis et partis (non enim dicimus quod homo communis sit hypostasis, neque etiam manus, cum sit pars), sed per hoc quod additur individuum, excluditur a persona ratio assumptibilis; humana enim natura in Christo non est persona, quia est assumpta a digniori, scilicet a verbo Dei. Sed melius dicendum est quod substantia accipitur communiter, prout dividitur per primam et secundam, et per hoc quod additur individua, trahitur ad standum pro substantia prima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0022","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quia substantiales differentiae non sunt nobis notae, vel etiam nominatae non sunt, oportet interdum uti differentiis accidentalibus loco substantialium, puta si quis diceret, ignis est corpus simplex, calidum et siccum, accidentia enim propria sunt effectus formarum substantialium, et manifestant eas. Et similiter nomina intentionum possunt accipi ad definiendum res, secundum quod accipiuntur pro aliquibus nominibus rerum quae non sunt posita. Et sic hoc nomen individuum ponitur in definitione personae, ad designandum modum subsistendi qui competit substantiis particularibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0024","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, in V Metaphys., nomen naturae primo impositum est ad significandam generationem viventium, quae dicitur nativitas. Et quia huiusmodi generatio est a principio intrinseco, extensum est hoc nomen ad significandum principium intrinsecum cuiuscumque motus. Et sic definitur natura in II Physic. Et quia huiusmodi principium est formale vel materiale, communiter tam materia quam forma dicitur natura. Et quia per formam completur essentia uniuscuiusque rei, communiter essentia uniuscuiusque rei, quam significat eius definitio, vocatur natura. Et sic accipitur hic natura. Unde Boetius in eodem libro dicit quod *natura est unumquodque informans specifica differentia*, specifica enim differentia est quae complet definitionem, et sumitur a propria forma rei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0026","text":"Et ideo convenientius fuit quod in definitione personae, quae est singulare alicuius generis determinati, uteretur nomine naturae, quam essentiae, quae sumitur ab esse, quod est communissimum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0027","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0028","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod anima est pars humanae speciei, et ideo, licet sit separata, quia tamen retinet naturam unibilitatis, non potest dici substantia individua quae est hypostasis vel substantia prima; sicut nec manus, nec quaecumque alia partium hominis. Et sic non competit ei neque definitio personae, neque nomen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0029","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0030","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0031","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod persona sit idem quod hypostasis, subsistentia et essentia. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Duab. Natur., quod *Graeci naturae rationalis individuam substantiam hypostaseos nomine vocaverunt*. Sed hoc etiam, apud nos, significat nomen personae. Ergo persona omnino idem est quod hypostasis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0032","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0033","text":"Praeterea, sicut in divinis dicimus tres personas, ita in divinis dicimus tres subsistentias, quod non esset, nisi persona et subsistentia idem significarent. Ergo idem significant persona et subsistentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0034","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0035","text":"Praeterea, Boetius dicit, in commento praedicamentorum, quod usia, quod est idem quod essentia, significat compositum ex materia et forma. Id autem quod est compositum ex materia et forma, est individuum substantiae, quod et hypostasis et persona dicitur. Ergo omnia praedicta nomina idem significare videntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0036","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0037","text":"Sed contra est quod Boetius dicit, in libro de Duab. Natur., quod *genera et species subsistunt tantum; individua vero non modo subsistunt, verum etiam substant*. Sed a subsistendo dicuntur subsistentiae, sicut a substando substantiae vel hypostases. Cum igitur esse hypostases vel personas non conveniat generibus vel speciebus, hypostases vel personae non sunt idem quod subsistentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0038","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0039","text":"Praeterea, Boetius dicit, in commento praedicamentorum, quod hypostasis dicitur materia, usiosis autem, idest subsistentia, dicitur forma. Sed neque forma neque materia potest dici persona. Ergo persona differt a praedictis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0040","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, in V Metaphys., substantia dicitur dupliciter. Uno modo dicitur substantia quidditas rei, quam significat definitio, secundum quod dicimus quod definitio significat substantiam rei, quam quidem substantiam Graeci usiam vocant, quod nos essentiam dicere possumus. Alio modo dicitur substantia subiectum vel suppositum quod subsistit in genere substantiae. Et hoc quidem, communiter accipiendo, nominari potest et nomine significante intentionem, et sic dicitur suppositum. Nominatur etiam tribus nominibus significantibus rem, quae quidem sunt res naturae, subsistentia et hypostasis, secundum triplicem considerationem substantiae sic dictae. Secundum enim quod per se existit et non in alio, vocatur subsistentia, illa enim subsistere dicimus, quae non in alio, sed in se existunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","text":"Secundum vero quod supponitur alicui naturae communi, sic dicitur res naturae; sicut hic homo est res naturae humanae. Secundum vero quod supponitur accidentibus, dicitur hypostasis vel substantia. Quod autem haec tria nomina significant communiter in toto genere substantiarum, hoc nomen persona significat in genere rationalium substantiarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0043","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0044","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hypostasis, apud Graecos, ex propria significatione nominis habet quod significet quodcumque individuum substantiae, sed ex usu loquendi habet quod sumatur pro individuo rationalis naturae, ratione suae excellentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0045","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0046","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut nos dicimus in divinis pluraliter tres personas et tres subsistentias, ita Graeci dicunt tres hypostases. Sed quia nomen substantiae, quod secundum proprietatem significationis respondet hypostasi, aequivocatur apud nos, cum quandoque significet essentiam, quandoque hypostasim; ne possit esse erroris occasio, maluerunt pro hypostasi transferre subsistentiam, quam substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0047","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod essentia proprie est id quod significatur per definitionem. Definitio autem complectitur principia speciei, non autem principia individualia. Unde in rebus compositis ex materia et forma, essentia significat non solum formam, nec solum materiam, sed compositum ex materia et forma communi, prout sunt principia speciei. Sed compositum ex hac materia et ex hac forma, habet rationem hypostasis et personae, anima enim et caro et os sunt de ratione hominis, sed haec anima et haec caro et hoc os sunt de ratione huius hominis. Et ideo hypostasis et persona addunt supra rationem essentiae principia individualia; neque sunt idem cum essentia in compositis ex materia et forma, ut supra dictum est, cum de simplicitate divina ageretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0049","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0050","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Boetius dicit genera et species subsistere, inquantum individuis aliquibus competit subsistere, ex eo quod sunt sub generibus et speciebus in praedicamento substantiae comprehensis, non quod ipsae species vel genera subsistant, nisi secundum opinionem Platonis, qui posuit species rerum separatim subsistere a singularibus. Substare vero competit eisdem individuis in ordine ad accidentia, quae sunt praeter rationem generum et specierum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0051","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0052","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod individuum compositum ex materia et forma, habet quod substet accidenti, ex proprietate materiae. Unde et Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin., *forma simplex subiectum esse non potest*. Sed quod per se subsistat, habet ex proprietate suae formae, quae non advenit rei subsistenti, sed dat esse actuale materiae, ut sic individuum subsistere possit. Propter hoc ergo hypostasim attribuit materiae, et usiosim, sive subsistentiam, formae, quia materia est principium substandi, et forma est principium subsistendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0053","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0054","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0055","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomen personae non sit ponendum in divinis. Dicit enim Dionysius, in principio de Div. Nom. *universaliter non est audendum aliquid dicere nec cogitare de supersubstantiali occulta divinitate, praeter ea quae divinitus nobis ex sanctis eloquiis sunt expressa*. Sed nomen personae non exprimitur nobis in sacra Scriptura novi vel veteris testamenti. Ergo non est nomine personae utendum in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0056","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0057","text":"Praeterea, Boetius dicit, in libro de Duab. Natur., *nomen personae videtur traductum ex his personis quae in comoediis tragoediisque homines repraesentabant; persona enim dicta est a personando, quia concavitate ipsa maior necesse est ut volvatur sonus. Graeci vero has personas prosopa vocant, ab eo quod ponantur in facie, atque ante oculos obtegant vultum*. Sed hoc non potest competere in divinis, nisi forte secundum metaphoram. Ergo nomen personae non dicitur de Deo nisi metaphorice.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0058","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0059","text":"Praeterea, omnis persona est hypostasis. Sed nomen hypostasis non videtur Deo competere, cum, secundum Boetium, significet id quod subiicitur accidentibus, quae in Deo non sunt. Hieronymus etiam dicit quod in hoc nomine hypostasis, venenum latet sub melle. Ergo hoc nomen persona non est dicendum de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0060","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0061","text":"Praeterea, a quocumque removetur definitio, et definitum. Sed definitio personae supra posita non videtur Deo competere. Tum quia ratio importat discursivam cognitionem, quae non competit Deo, ut supra ostensum est, et sic Deus non potest dici rationalis naturae. Tum etiam quia Deus dici non potest individua substantia, cum principium individuationis sit materia, Deus autem immaterialis est; neque etiam accidentibus substat, ut substantia dici possit. Nomen ergo personae Deo attribui non debet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0062","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0063","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in symbolo Athanasii, *alia est persona patris, alia filii, alia spiritus sancti*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0064","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod persona significat id quod est perfectissimum in tota natura, scilicet subsistens in rationali natura. Unde, cum omne illud quod est perfectionis, Deo sit attribuendum, eo quod eius essentia continet in se omnem perfectionem; conveniens est ut hoc nomen persona de Deo dicatur. Non tamen eodem modo quo dicitur de creaturis, sed excellentiori modo; sicut et alia nomina quae, creaturis a nobis imposita, Deo attribuuntur; sicut supra ostensum est, cum de divinis nominibus ageretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0066","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet nomen personae in Scriptura veteris vel novi testamenti non inveniatur dictum de Deo, tamen id quod nomen significat, multipliciter in sacra Scriptura invenitur assertum de Deo; scilicet quod est maxime per se ens, et perfectissime intelligens. Si autem oporteret de Deo dici solum illa, secundum vocem, quae sacra Scriptura de Deo tradit, sequeretur quod nunquam in alia lingua posset aliquis loqui de Deo, nisi in illa in qua primo tradita est Scriptura veteris vel novi testamenti. Ad inveniendum autem nova nomina, antiquam fidem de Deo significantia, coegit necessitas disputandi cum haereticis. Nec haec novitas vitanda est, cum non sit profana, utpote a Scripturarum sensu non discordans, docet autem apostolus profanas vocum novitates vitare, I ad Tim. ult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0068","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quamvis hoc nomen persona non conveniat Deo quantum ad id a quo impositum est nomen, tamen quantum ad id ad quod significandum imponitur, maxime Deo convenit. Quia enim in comoediis et tragoediis repraesentabantur aliqui homines famosi, impositum est hoc nomen persona ad significandum aliquos dignitatem habentes. Unde consueverunt dici personae in Ecclesiis, quae habent aliquam dignitatem. Propter quod quidam definiunt personam, dicentes quod *persona est hypostasis proprietate distincta ad dignitatem pertinente*. Et quia magnae dignitatis est in rationali natura subsistere, ideo omne individuum rationalis naturae dicitur persona, ut dictum est. Sed dignitas divinae naturae excedit omnem dignitatem, et secundum hoc maxime competit Deo nomen personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0070","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0071","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nomen hypostasis non competit Deo quantum ad id a quo est impositum nomen, cum non substet accidentibus, competit autem ei quantum ad id, quod est impositum ad significandum rem subsistentem. Hieronymus autem dicit sub hoc nomine venenum latere, quia antequam significatio huius nominis esset plene nota apud Latinos, haeretici per hoc nomen simplices decipiebant, ut confiterentur plures essentias, sicut confitentur plures hypostases; propter hoc quod nomen substantiae, cui respondet in Graeco nomen hypostasis, communiter accipitur apud nos pro essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0072","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Deus potest dici rationalis naturae, secundum quod ratio non importat discursum, sed communiter intellectualem naturam. Individuum autem Deo competere non potest quantum ad hoc quod individuationis principium est materia, sed solum secundum quod importat incommunicabilitatem. Substantia vero convenit Deo, secundum quod significat existere per se. Quidam tamen dicunt quod definitio superius a Boetio data, non est definitio personae secundum quod personas in Deo dicimus. Propter quod Ricardus de sancto Victore, corrigere volens hanc definitionem, dixit quod persona, secundum quod de Deo dicitur, est divinae naturae incommunicabilis existentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0074","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0075","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0076","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen persona non significet relationem, sed substantiam, in divinis. Dicit enim Augustinus, in VII de Trin., *cum dicimus personam patris, non aliud dicimus quam substantiam patris; ad se quippe dicitur persona, non ad filium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0077","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0078","text":"Praeterea, quid quaerit de essentia. Sed, sicut dicit Augustinus in eodem loco, cum dicitur, *tres sunt qui testimonium dant in caelo, pater, verbum et spiritus* sanctus; et quaeritur, quid tres? Respondetur, tres personae. Ergo hoc nomen persona significat essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0079","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0080","text":"Praeterea, secundum philosophum, IV Metaphys., id quod significatur per nomen, est eius definitio. Sed definitio personae est rationalis naturae individua substantia, ut dictum est. Ergo hoc nomen persona significat substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0081","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0082","text":"Praeterea, persona in hominibus et Angelis non significat relationem, sed aliquid absolutum. Si igitur in Deo significaret relationem, diceretur aequivoce de Deo et hominibus et Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0083","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0084","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod omne nomen ad personas pertinens, relationem significat. Sed nullum nomen magis pertinet ad personas, quam hoc nomen persona. Ergo hoc nomen persona relationem significat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0085","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0086","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa significationem huius nominis persona in divinis, difficultatem ingerit quod pluraliter de tribus praedicatur, praeter naturam essentialium nominum; neque etiam ad aliquid dicitur, sicut nomina quae relationem significant. Unde quibusdam visum est quod hoc nomen persona simpliciter, ex virtute vocabuli, essentiam significet in divinis, sicut hoc nomen Deus, et hoc nomen sapiens, sed propter instantiam haereticorum, est accommodatum, ex ordinatione Concilii, ut possit poni pro relativis; et praecipue in plurali, vel cum nomine partitivo, ut cum dicimus tres personas, vel alia est persona patris, alia filii. In singulari vero potest sumi pro absoluto, et pro relativo. Sed haec non videtur sufficiens ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0087","text":"Quia si hoc nomen persona, ex vi suae significationis, non habet quod significet nisi essentiam in divinis; ex hoc quod dictum est tres personas, non fuisset haereticorum quietata calumnia, sed maioris calumniae data esset eis occasio. Et ideo alii dixerunt quod hoc nomen persona in divinis significat simul essentiam et relationem. Quorum quidam dixerunt quod significat essentiam in recto, et relationem in obliquo. Quia persona dicitur quasi per se una, unitas autem pertinet ad essentiam. Quod autem dicitur per se, implicat relationem oblique, intelligitur enim pater per se esse, quasi relatione distinctus a filio. Quidam vero dixerunt e converso, quod significat relationem in recto, et essentiam in obliquo, quia in definitione personae, natura ponitur in obliquo. Et isti propinquius ad veritatem accesserunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","text":"Ad evidentiam igitur huius quaestionis, considerandum est quod aliquid est de significatione minus communis, quod tamen non est de significatione magis communis, rationale enim includitur in significatione hominis, quod tamen non est de significatione animalis. Unde aliud est quaerere de significatione animalis, et aliud est quaerere de significatione animalis quod est homo. Similiter aliud est quaerere de significatione huius nominis persona in communi, et aliud de significatione personae divinae. Persona enim in communi significat substantiam individuam rationalis naturae, ut dictum est. Individuum autem est quod est in se indistinctum, ab aliis vero distinctum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","text":"Persona igitur, in quacumque natura, significat id quod est distinctum in natura illa sicut in humana natura significat has carnes et haec ossa et hanc animam, quae sunt principia individuantia hominem; quae quidem, licet non sint de significatione personae, sunt tamen de significatione personae humanae. Distinctio autem in divinis non fit nisi per relationes originis, ut dictum est supra. Relatio autem in divinis non est sicut accidens inhaerens subiecto, sed est ipsa divina essentia, unde est subsistens, sicut essentia divina subsistit. Sicut ergo deitas est Deus, ita paternitas divina est Deus pater, qui est persona divina. Persona igitur divina significat relationem ut subsistentem. Et hoc est significare relationem per modum substantiae quae est hypostasis subsistens in natura divina; licet subsistens in natura divina non sit aliud quam natura divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0090","text":"Et secundum hoc, verum est quod hoc nomen persona significat relationem in recto, et essentiam in obliquo, non tamen relationem inquantum est relatio, sed inquantum significatur per modum hypostasis. Similiter etiam significat essentiam in recto, et relationem in obliquo, inquantum essentia idem est quod hypostasis; hypostasis autem significatur in divinis ut relatione distincta; et sic relatio, per modum relationis significata, cadit in ratione personae in obliquo. Et secundum hoc etiam dici potest, quod haec significatio huius nominis persona non erat percepta ante haereticorum calumniam, unde non erat in usu hoc nomen persona, nisi sicut unum aliorum absolutorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0091","text":"Sed postmodum accommodatum est hoc nomen persona ad standum pro relativo, ex congruentia suae significationis, ut scilicet hoc quod stat pro relativo, non solum habeat ex usu, ut prima opinio dicebat, sed etiam ex significatione sua.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0092","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc nomen persona dicitur ad se, non ad alterum, quia significat relationem, non per modum relationis, sed per modum substantiae quae est hypostasis. Et secundum hoc Augustinus dicit quod significat essentiam, prout in Deo essentia est idem cum hypostasi, quia in Deo non differt quod est et quo est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0094","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0095","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod quid quandoque quaerit de natura quam significat definitio; ut cum quaeritur, quid est homo? Et respondetur, animal rationale mortale. Quandoque vero quaerit suppositum; ut cum quaeritur, quid natat in mari? Et respondetur, piscis. Et sic quaerentibus quid tres? Responsum est, tres personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0096","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0097","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in intellectu substantiae individuae, idest distinctae vel incommunicabilis, intelligitur in divinis relatio, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0098","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod diversa ratio minus communium non facit aequivocationem in magis communi. Licet enim sit alia propria definitio equi et asini, tamen univocantur in nomine animalis, quia communis definitio animalis convenit utrique. Unde non sequitur quod, licet in significatione personae divinae contineatur relatio, non autem in significatione angelicae personae vel humanae, quod nomen personae aequivoce dicatur. Licet nec etiam dicatur univoce, cum nihil univoce de Deo dici possit et de creaturis, ut supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8db1c77700bbaeef790b9963f58ea0732aa2adb69ba3fbd6fe3c112ed807d06f","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141054953647Z_26c5abbf","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 29","source_sha256":"8db1c77700bbaeef790b9963f58ea0732aa2adb69ba3fbd6fe3c112ed807d06f","sha256":"a07d52f4a53aaba5bcb8eaa7f20cc2985ffad4a341d487548e63302c1df45085","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:10:54.953690+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0002","text":"[I q. 29 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0003","text":"Having premised those matters which seemed necessary to know beforehand concerning processions and relations, it is necessary to proceed to persons. First, according to an absolute consideration; and then, according to a comparative consideration. Now, concerning persons absolutely, one must consider first what is common, and then the individual persons. Four things seem to pertain to the common consideration of persons: first, the signification of this name, person; second, the number of persons; third, those things which follow upon the number of persons, or are opposed to it, such as diversity and solitude, and the like; fourth, those things which pertain to the knowledge of persons. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, concerning the definition of person. Second, concerning the comparison of person with essence, subsistence, and hypostasis. Third, whether the name person is fitting in divine things. Fourth, what it signifies there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0005","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the definition of person assigned by Boethius in the book *On the Two Natures* is unsuitable; namely, *a person is an individual substance of a rational nature*. For no singular is defined. But person signifies something singular. Therefore person is defined unsuitably.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0007","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0008","text":"Further, substance, insofar as it is set down in the definition of person, is taken either for first substance or for second substance. If for first substance, “individual” is added superfluously, since first substance is individual substance. But if it stands for second substance, it is added falsely, and there is a contradiction in the adjunct, for genera or species are called second substances. Therefore the definition is badly assigned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0009","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0010","text":"Further, a name of intention ought not to be placed in the definition of a thing. For it would not be a good assignment if someone were to say, man is a species of animal; for man is a name of a thing, and species is a name of intention. Therefore, since person is a name of a thing (for it signifies a certain substance of rational nature), individual, which is a name of intention, is unsuitably placed in its definition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0011","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0012","text":"Further, nature is a principle of motion and rest in that in which it is, through itself and not accidentally, as is said in *Physics* II. But person is found in immovable things, as in God and the angels. Therefore nature ought not to have been placed in the definition of person, but rather essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0013","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0014","text":"Further, a separated soul is an individual substance of a rational nature. Yet it is not a person. Therefore person is unsuitably so defined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0015","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","text":"I answer that, although universal and particular are found in all genera, nevertheless the individual is found in the genus of substance in a certain special mode. For substance is individuated through itself, whereas accidents are individuated through a subject, which is substance; for this whiteness is spoken of insofar as it is in this subject. Hence, too, individual substances fittingly have some special name beyond the others: they are called hypostases, or first substances. But particular and individual are found in rational substances in a still more special and more perfect mode, since they have dominion over their own act, and are not merely acted upon, as other things are, but act through themselves; and actions are in singulars. Therefore, among the other substances also, singulars of rational nature have a certain special name. And this name is person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017","text":"And therefore, in the aforesaid definition of person, individual substance is set down insofar as it signifies a singular in the genus of substance; but of rational nature is added insofar as it signifies a singular among rational substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0018","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although this singular or that cannot be defined, nevertheless that which pertains to the common account of singularity can be defined; and thus the Philosopher defines first substance. And in this way Boethius defines person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0020","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","text":"To the second it must be said that, according to some, substance in the definition of person is set down for first substance, which is hypostasis. Yet “individual” is not added superfluously. For by the name hypostasis or first substance, the notion of universal and of part is excluded (for we do not say that common man is a hypostasis, nor even a hand, since it is a part); but by the addition of individual, the notion of what can be assumed is excluded from person. For human nature in Christ is not a person, because it is assumed by something more dignified, namely, by the Word of God. But it is better to say that substance is taken commonly, insofar as it is divided into first and second; and by the addition of individual, it is drawn to stand for first substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0022","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0023","text":"To the third it must be said that, because substantial differences are not known to us, or are not even named, it is sometimes necessary to use accidental differences in place of substantial ones; for example, if someone were to say, fire is a simple body, hot and dry. For proper accidents are effects of substantial forms and make them manifest. Likewise, names of intentions can be taken for defining things, insofar as they are taken for certain names of things which have not been posited. And thus this name individual is placed in the definition of person, to designate the mode of subsisting which belongs to particular substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0024","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, according to the Philosopher, in Metaphysics V, the name nature was first imposed to signify the generation of living things, which is called birth. And because generation of this sort is from an intrinsic principle, this name was extended to signify the intrinsic principle of any motion. And thus nature is defined in Physics II. And because such a principle is formal or material, both matter and form are commonly called nature. And because through form the essence of each thing is completed, the essence of each thing, which its definition signifies, is commonly called nature. And nature is taken in this sense here. Hence Boethius says in the same book that *nature is the specific difference informing each thing*, for specific difference is what completes the definition, and it is taken from the proper form of the thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0026","text":"And therefore it was more fitting that, in the definition of person, which is the singular of a determinate genus, he should use the name nature rather than essence, which is taken from being, which is most common.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0027","text":"[I q. 29 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0028","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the soul is part of the human species, and therefore, although it is separated, nevertheless, because it retains the nature of being capable of union, it cannot be called an individual substance which is a hypostasis or first substance; just as neither can a hand, nor any other of the parts of man. And thus neither the definition of person nor the name belongs to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0029","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0030","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0031","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that person is the same as hypostasis, subsistence, and essence. For Boethius says, in the book On the Two Natures, that *the Greeks called an individual substance of rational nature by the name hypostasis*. But among us the name person also signifies this. Therefore person is altogether the same as hypostasis.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0032","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0033","text":"Moreover, just as in divine things we say three persons, so in divine things we say three subsistences; which would not be the case unless person and subsistence signified the same. Therefore person and subsistence signify the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0034","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0035","text":"Moreover, Boethius says, in his commentary on the Categories, that usia, which is the same as essence, signifies the composite of matter and form. But that which is composed of matter and form is an individual substance, which is also called hypostasis and person. Therefore all the aforesaid names seem to signify the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0036","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0037","text":"But on the contrary, Boethius says, in the book On the Two Natures, that *genera and species only subsist; but individuals not only subsist, but also stand under*. But they are called subsistences from subsisting, just as they are called substances or hypostases from standing under. Therefore, since being hypostases or persons does not belong to genera or species, hypostases or persons are not the same as subsistences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0038","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0039","text":"Moreover, Boethius says, in his commentary on the Categories, that matter is called hypostasis, whereas form is called usiosis, that is, subsistence. But neither form nor matter can be called a person. Therefore person differs from the aforesaid.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0040","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","text":"I answer that it must be said that, according to the Philosopher, in Metaphysics V, substance is spoken of in two ways. In one way, the substance of a thing is called its quiddity, which the definition signifies, inasmuch as we say that the definition signifies the substance of the thing; this substance the Greeks call usia, which we can call essence. In another way, substance is called the subject or supposit which subsists in the genus of substance. And this, taken commonly, can be named also by a name signifying an intention, and thus it is called a supposit. It is also named by three names signifying a thing, namely, thing of nature, subsistence, and hypostasis, according to a threefold consideration of substance so called. For insofar as it exists through itself and not in another, it is called subsistence; for we say that those things subsist which exist not in another but in themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","text":"But insofar as it is the supposit of some common nature, it is called a thing of nature; as this man is a thing of human nature. But insofar as it is the supposit of accidents, it is called hypostasis or substance. What these three names signify commonly in the whole genus of substances, the name person signifies in the genus of rational substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0043","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0044","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that hypostasis, among the Greeks, from the proper signification of the name has it that it signifies any individual of substance; but from usage in speech it has it that it is taken for an individual of rational nature, by reason of its excellence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0045","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0046","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as we say in divine things, in the plural, three persons and three subsistences, so the Greeks say three hypostases. But because the name substance, which according to the propriety of its signification corresponds to hypostasis, is equivocal among us, since it sometimes signifies essence and sometimes hypostasis, lest there be an occasion of error, they preferred to render subsistence rather than substance for hypostasis.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0047","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","text":"To the third, it must be said that essence properly is that which is signified by the definition. But the definition comprehends the principles of the species, not, however, individual principles. Hence, in things composed of matter and form, essence signifies not form alone, nor matter alone, but the composite of common matter and form, insofar as they are principles of the species. But the composite of this matter and this form has the character of hypostasis and person; for soul and flesh and bone belong to the account of man, but this soul and this flesh and this bone belong to the account of this man. And therefore hypostasis and person add individual principles beyond the account of essence; nor are they the same as essence in composites of matter and form, as was said above when treating of the divine simplicity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0049","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0050","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Boethius says that genera and species subsist insofar as it belongs to certain individuals to subsist, from the fact that they are contained under genera and species in the category of substance; not that the species or genera themselves subsist, except according to Plato's opinion, who posited that the species of things subsist separately from singulars. But to underlie belongs to those same individuals in relation to accidents, which are beyond the account of genera and species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0051","text":"[I q. 29 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0052","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the individual composed of matter and form has its underlying an accident from the property of matter. Hence Boethius also says, in the book On the Trinity, *a simple form cannot be a subject*. But that it subsists through itself, it has from the property of its form, which does not accrue to a subsisting thing, but gives actual being to matter, so that the individual may thus be able to subsist. Therefore, for this reason he attributes hypostasis to matter, and ousiosis, or subsistence, to form, because matter is the principle of underlying, and form is the principle of subsisting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0053","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0054","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0055","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the name person should not be posited in divine things. For Dionysius says, at the beginning of On the Divine Names: *universally, one must not dare to say or think anything concerning the supersubstantial hidden divinity, except those things which have been divinely expressed to us from the sacred utterances*. But the name person is not expressed to us in Sacred Scripture of the New or Old Testament. Therefore the name person is not to be used in divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0056","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0057","text":"Further, Boethius says, in the book On the Two Natures: *the name person seems to have been derived from those masks which represented men in comedies and tragedies; for persona is said from personare, because by its very concavity the sound necessarily reverberates more greatly. The Greeks, however, call these masks prosopa, from the fact that they are placed on the face and cover the countenance before the eyes*. But this cannot belong to divine things, except perhaps metaphorically. Therefore the name person is said of God only metaphorically.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0058","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0059","text":"Further, every person is a hypostasis. But the name hypostasis does not seem to belong to God, since, according to Boethius, it signifies that which is subject to accidents, which are not in God. Jerome also says that in this name hypostasis, poison lies hidden beneath honey. Therefore this name person is not to be said of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0060","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0061","text":"Further, from whatever the definition is removed, the defined thing is removed as well. But the definition of person set forth above does not seem to belong to God. First, because rationality implies discursive cognition, which does not belong to God, as was shown above; and thus God cannot be said to be of a rational nature. Second, because God cannot be called an individual substance, since the principle of individuation is matter, whereas God is immaterial; nor does He underlie accidents, so that He may be called a substance. Therefore the name person ought not to be attributed to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0062","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0063","text":"On the contrary, it is said in the Athanasian Creed: *the person of the Father is one, that of the Son another, that of the Holy Spirit another*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0064","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","text":"I answer that it must be said that person signifies that which is most perfect in the whole of nature, namely, a subsistent in a rational nature. Hence, since everything that belongs to perfection is to be attributed to God, because His essence contains within itself every perfection, it is fitting that this name person be said of God. Yet not in the same way as it is said of creatures, but in a more excellent way; just as are the other names which, imposed by us upon creatures, are attributed to God, as was shown above when the divine names were treated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0066","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although the name person is not found to be said of God in the Scripture of the Old or New Testament, nevertheless that which the name signifies is found to be asserted of God in Sacred Scripture in many ways; namely, that He is being most through Himself and understands most perfectly. But if it were necessary to say of God only those things, in wording, which Sacred Scripture hands down concerning God, it would follow that no one could ever speak of God in another language except that in which the Scripture of the Old or New Testament was first handed down. But the necessity of disputing with heretics compelled the discovery of new names signifying the ancient faith concerning God. Nor is this novelty to be avoided, since it is not profane, being not discordant with the sense of the Scriptures; yet the Apostle teaches us to avoid profane novelties of words, at the end of I Timothy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0068","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although this name person does not suit God with respect to that from which the name was imposed, nevertheless with respect to that for the signifying of which it is imposed, it suits God most greatly. For because certain famous men were represented in comedies and tragedies, this name person was imposed to signify those having dignity. Hence those who have some dignity are customarily called persons in churches. For this reason, some define person by saying that *a person is a hypostasis distinguished by a property pertaining to dignity*. And because it is of great dignity to subsist in a rational nature, therefore every individual of a rational nature is called a person, as has been said. But the dignity of the divine nature exceeds every dignity, and according to this the name person belongs most of all to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0070","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0071","text":"To the third, it must be said that the name hypostasis does not belong to God with respect to that from which the name is imposed, since He does not underlie accidents; but it belongs to Him with respect to that which it was imposed to signify, namely, a subsisting thing. Jerome, however, says that poison lies hidden beneath this name because, before the signification of this name was fully known among the Latins, heretics deceived the simple by means of this name, so that they might confess several essences, just as they confess several hypostases, on account of the fact that the name substance, to which the Greek name hypostasis corresponds, is commonly taken among us for essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0072","text":"[I q. 29 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that God can be called of rational nature, insofar as reason does not imply discursive reasoning, but intellectual nature in general. But individual cannot belong to God insofar as matter is the principle of individuation, but only insofar as it implies incommunicability. Substance, however, belongs to God insofar as it signifies existing through itself. Yet some say that the definition given above by Boethius is not a definition of person insofar as we speak of persons in God. Therefore Richard of Saint Victor, wishing to correct this definition, said that person, insofar as it is said of God, is an incommunicable existence of the divine nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0074","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0075","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0076","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that this name person does not signify relation, but substance, in divine things. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, VII, *when we say the person of the Father, we say nothing other than the substance of the Father; for person is said in relation to itself, not in relation to the Son*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0077","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0078","text":"Further, what asks concerning the essence. But, as Augustine says in the same place, when it is said, *there are three who bear witness in heaven: the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit*; and it is asked, what three? The answer is: three persons. Therefore this name person signifies essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0079","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0080","text":"Further, according to the Philosopher, in *Metaphysics* IV, that which is signified by a name is its definition. But the definition of person is an individual substance of a rational nature, as has been said. Therefore this name person signifies substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0081","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0082","text":"Further, person in human beings and angels does not signify relation, but something absolute. Therefore, if in God it signified relation, it would be said equivocally of God and of human beings and angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0083","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0084","text":"On the contrary, Boethius says, in the book *On the Trinity*, that every name pertaining to persons signifies relation. But no name pertains more to persons than this name person. Therefore this name person signifies relation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0085","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0086","text":"I answer that it must be said that, concerning the signification of this name person in divine things, a difficulty is occasioned by the fact that it is predicated plurally of three, contrary to the nature of essential names; nor is it said in relation to something, as are names that signify relation. Hence it seemed to some that this name person simply, by the force of the word, signifies essence in divine things, like this name God and this name wise; but, because of the insistence of heretics, it has been adapted, by ordinance of a Council, so that it may be used for relatives; especially in the plural, or with a partitive name, as when we say three persons, or the person of the Father is one, that of the Son another. In the singular, however, it can be taken for the absolute and for the relative. But this does not seem a sufficient account.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0087","text":"For if this name person, by the force of its signification, had nothing to signify in divine things except essence, then from the fact that three persons were said, the calumny of the heretics would not have been quieted, but an occasion for greater calumny would have been given them. And therefore others said that this name person in divine things signifies essence and relation at once. Some of these said that it signifies essence directly and relation indirectly. For person is said as though per se one; but unity pertains to essence. Yet what is said per se implies relation indirectly: for the Father is understood to exist per se, as though distinguished from the Son by relation. Others, however, said conversely that it signifies relation directly and essence indirectly, because in the definition of person nature is set down indirectly. And these came closer to the truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","text":"Therefore, for the clarification of this question, one must consider that something belongs to the signification of the less common which nevertheless does not belong to the signification of the more common: for rational is included in the signification of human being, but it does not belong to the signification of animal. Hence it is one thing to inquire into the signification of animal, and another to inquire into the signification of animal which is human being. Similarly, it is one thing to inquire into the signification of this name person in general, and another into the signification of divine person. For person in general signifies an individual substance of a rational nature, as has been said. But the individual is that which is undivided in itself, but distinct from others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","text":"Therefore person, in whatever nature, signifies that which is distinct in that nature; just as in human nature it signifies this flesh and these bones and this soul, which are the principles individuating a human being. Although these indeed are not of the signification of person, they are nevertheless of the signification of human person. But distinction in divine things is made only through relations of origin, as was said above. But relation in divine things is not like an accident inhering in a subject, but is the divine essence itself; hence it is subsistent, just as the divine essence subsists. Therefore, just as deity is God, so divine paternity is God the Father, who is a divine person. Therefore divine person signifies relation as subsisting. And this is to signify relation in the mode of substance, which is a hypostasis subsisting in the divine nature; although that which subsists in the divine nature is not other than the divine nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0090","text":"And according to this, it is true that this name person signifies relation directly and essence indirectly; yet not relation insofar as it is relation, but insofar as it is signified in the mode of hypostasis. Likewise, it also signifies essence directly and relation indirectly, insofar as essence is the same as hypostasis; but hypostasis is signified in divine things as distinct by relation; and thus relation, signified in the mode of relation, falls into the notion of person indirectly. And according to this it can also be said that this signification of the name person was not perceived before the calumny of the heretics; hence this name person was not in use except as one of the other absolutes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0091","text":"But afterward this name person was adapted to stand for a relative, by the congruity of its signification, so that what stands for a relative has this not only from usage, as the first opinion said, but also from its signification.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0092","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that this name person is said in relation to itself, not in relation to another, because it signifies relation not in the mode of relation, but in the mode of substance, which is hypostasis. And according to this Augustine says that it signifies essence, insofar as in God essence is the same as hypostasis, because in God that which is and that by which it is do not differ.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0094","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0095","text":"To the second, it must be said that what sometimes asks concerning the nature signified by the definition; as when it is asked, what is a human being? And the answer is: a rational mortal animal. But sometimes it asks concerning the suppositum; as when it is asked, what swims in the sea? And the answer is: a fish. And thus, to those asking what three? the answer was: three persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0096","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0097","text":"To the third, it must be said that in the understanding of an individual substance, that is, a distinct or incommunicable one, relation is understood in divine things, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0098","text":"[I q. 29 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the diverse notion of less common things does not make equivocation in that which is more common. For although the proper definition of a horse and of an ass is different, nevertheless they are predicated univocally under the name animal, because the common definition of animal belongs to each. Hence it does not follow that, although relation is contained in the signification of divine person, but not in the signification of angelic or human person, the name person is said equivocally. Nor indeed is it said univocally either, since nothing can be said univocally of God and creatures, as was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099"]}],"source_digest":"97d15dc0fdc228ec03efdc6ace62f497424332ef5c4c4690a62813a846803e41","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 29"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 29","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22","title":"First Part · Question 30","order":31,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_22","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 30 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29691)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de pluralitate personarum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum sint plures personae in divinis. Secundo, quot sunt. Tertio, quid significent termini numerales in divinis. Quarto, de communitate huius nominis persona.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29692)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit ponere plures personas in divinis. Persona enim est rationalis naturae individua substantia. Si ergo sunt plures personae in divinis, sequitur quod sint plures substantiae, quod videtur haereticum.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29693)\n\nPraeterea, pluralitas proprietatum absolutarum non facit distinctionem personarum, neque in Deo neque in nobis, multo igitur minus pluralitas relationum. Sed in Deo non est alia pluralitas nisi relationum, ut supra dictum est. Ergo non potest dici quod in Deo sint plures personae.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29694)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius dicit, de Deo loquens, quod hoc vere unum est, in quo nullus est numerus. Sed pluralitas importat numerum. Ergo non sunt plures personae in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29695)\n\nPraeterea, ubicumque est numerus, ibi est totum et pars. Si igitur in Deo sit numerus personarum, erit in Deo ponere totum et partem, quod simplicitati divinae repugnat.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29696)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Athanasius, *alia est persona patris, alia filii, alia spiritus sancti*. Ergo pater et filius et spiritus sanctus sunt plures personae.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29697)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod plures esse personas in divinis, sequitur ex praemissis. Ostensum est enim supra quod hoc nomen persona significat in divinis relationem, ut rem subsistentem in natura divina. Supra autem habitum est quod sunt plures relationes reales in divinis. Unde sequitur quod sint plures res subsistentes in divina natura. Et hoc est esse plures personas in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29698)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod substantia non ponitur in definitione personae secundum quod significat essentiam, sed secundum quod significat suppositum, quod patet ex hoc quod additur individua. Ad significandum autem substantiam sic dictam, habent Graeci nomen hypostasis, unde sicut nos dicimus tres personas, ita ipsi dicunt tres hypostases. Nos autem non consuevimus dicere tres substantias, ne intelligerentur tres essentiae, propter nominis aequivocationem.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29699)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod proprietates absolutae in divinis, ut bonitas et sapientia, non opponuntur ad invicem, unde neque realiter distinguuntur. Quamvis ergo eis conveniat subsistere, non tamen sunt plures res subsistentes, quod est esse plures personas. Proprietates autem absolutae in rebus creatis non subsistunt, licet realiter ab invicem distinguantur, ut albedo et dulcedo. Sed proprietates relativae in Deo et subsistunt, et realiter ab invicem distinguuntur, ut supra dictum est. Unde pluralitas talium proprietatum sufficit ad pluralitatem personarum in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29700)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod a Deo, propter summam unitatem et simplicitatem, excluditur omnis pluralitas absolute dictorum; non autem pluralitas relationum. Quia relationes praedicantur de aliquo ut ad alterum; et sic compositionem in ipso de quo dicuntur non important, ut Boetius in eodem libro docet.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29701)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod numerus est duplex, scilicet numerus simplex vel absolutus, ut duo et tria et quatuor; et numerus qui est in rebus numeratis, ut duo homines et duo equi. Si igitur in divinis accipiatur numerus absolute sive abstracte, nihil prohibet in eo esse totum et partem, et sic non est nisi in acceptione intellectus nostri; non enim numerus absolutus a rebus numeratis est nisi in intellectu. Si autem accipiamus numerum prout est in rebus numeratis, sic in rebus quidem creatis, unum est pars duorum, et duo trium, ut unus homo duorum, et duo trium, sed non est sic in Deo, quia tantus est pater quanta tota Trinitas, ut infra patebit.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29702)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo sint plures personae quam tres. Pluralitas enim personarum in divinis est secundum pluralitatem proprietatum relativarum, ut dictum est. Sed quatuor sunt relationes in divinis, ut supra dictum est, scilicet paternitas, filiatio, communis spiratio et processio. Ergo quatuor personae sunt in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29703)\n\nPraeterea, non plus differt natura a voluntate in Deo, quam natura ab intellectu. Sed in divinis est alia persona quae procedit per modum voluntatis, ut amor; et alia quae procedit per modum naturae, ut filius. Ergo est etiam alia quae procedit per modum intellectus, ut verbum; et alia quae procedit per modum naturae, ut filius. Et sic iterum sequitur quod non sunt tantum tres personae in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29704)\n\nPraeterea, in rebus creatis quod excellentius est, plures habet operationes intrinsecas, sicut homo supra alia animalia habet intelligere et velle. Sed Deus in infinitum excedit omnem creaturam. Ergo non solum est ibi persona procedens per modum voluntatis et per modum intellectus, sed infinitis aliis modis. Ergo sunt infinitae personae in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29705)\n\nPraeterea, ex infinita bonitate patris est, quod infinite seipsum communicet, producendo personam divinam. Sed etiam in spiritu sancto est infinita bonitas. Ergo spiritus sanctus producit divinam personam, et illa aliam, et sic in infinitum.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29706)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod continetur sub determinato numero, est mensuratum, numerus enim mensura quaedam est. Sed personae divinae sunt immensae, ut patet per Athanasium, *immensus pater, immensus filius, immensus spiritus sanctus*. Non ergo sub numero ternario continentur.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29707)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur I Ioan. ult., *tres sunt qui testimonium dant in caelo, pater, verbum et spiritus sanctus*. Quaerentibus autem, quid tres? Respondetur, tres personae, ut Augustinus dicit, in VII de Trin. Sunt igitur tres personae tantum in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29708)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum praemissa, necesse est ponere tantum tres personas in divinis. Ostensum est enim quod plures personae sunt plures relationes subsistentes, ab invicem realiter distinctae. Realis autem distinctio inter relationes divinas non est nisi in ratione oppositionis relativae. Ergo oportet duas relationes oppositas ad duas personas pertinere, si quae autem relationes oppositae non sunt, ad eandem personam necesse est eas pertinere. Paternitas ergo et filiatio, cum sint oppositae relationes, ad duas personas ex necessitate pertinent. Paternitas igitur subsistens est persona patris, et filiatio subsistens est persona filii. Aliae autem duae relationes ad neutram harum oppositionem habent, sed sibi invicem opponuntur. Impossibile est igitur quod ambae uni personae conveniant. Oportet ergo quod vel una earum conveniat utrique dictarum personarum, aut quod una uni, et alia alii. Non autem potest esse quod processio conveniat patri et filio, vel alteri eorum, quia sic sequeretur quod processio intellectus, quae est generatio in divinis, secundum quam accipitur paternitas et filiatio, prodiret ex processione amoris, secundum quam accipitur spiratio et processio, si persona generans et genita procederent a spirante, quod est contra praemissa. Relinquitur ergo quod spiratio conveniat et personae patris et personae filii, utpote nullam habens oppositionem relativam nec ad paternitatem nec ad filiationem. Et per consequens oportet quod conveniat processio alteri personae, quae dicitur persona spiritus sancti, quae per modum amoris procedit, ut supra habitum est. Relinquitur ergo tantum tres personas esse in divinis, scilicet patrem et filium et spiritum sanctum.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29709)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet sint quatuor relationes in divinis, tamen una earum, scilicet spiratio, non separatur a persona patris et filii, sed convenit utrique. Et sic, licet sit relatio, non tamen dicitur proprietas, quia non convenit uni tantum personae, neque est relatio personalis, idest constituens personam. Sed hae tres relationes, paternitas, filiatio et processio, dicuntur proprietates personales, quasi personas constituentes, nam paternitas est persona patris, filiatio persona filii, processio persona spiritus sancti procedentis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29710)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod id quod procedit per modum intellectus, ut verbum, procedit secundum rationem similitudinis, sicut etiam id quod procedit per modum naturae, et ideo supra dictum est quod processio verbi divini est ipsa generatio per modum naturae. Amor autem, inquantum huiusmodi, non procedit ut similitudo illius a quo procedit (licet in divinis amor sit coessentialis inquantum est divinus), et ideo processio amoris non dicitur generatio in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29711)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod homo, cum sit perfectior aliis animalibus, habet plures operationes intrinsecas quam alia animalia, quia eius perfectio est per modum compositionis. Unde in Angelis, qui sunt perfectiores et simpliciores, sunt pauciores operationes intrinsecae quam in homine, quia in eis non est imaginari, sentire, et huiusmodi. Sed in Deo, secundum rem, non est nisi una operatio, quae est sua essentia. Sed quomodo sunt duae processiones, supra ostensum est.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29712)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si spiritus sanctus haberet aliam numero bonitatem a bonitate patris, oporteret enim quod, sicut pater per suam bonitatem producit personam divinam, ita et spiritus sanctus. Sed una et eadem bonitas patris est et spiritus sancti. Neque etiam est distinctio nisi per relationes personarum. Unde bonitas convenit spiritui sancto quasi habita ab alio, patri autem, sicut a quo communicatur alteri. Oppositio autem relationis non permittit ut cum relatione spiritus sancti sit relatio principii respectu divinae personae, quia ipse procedit ab aliis personis quae in divinis esse possunt.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29713)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod numerus determinatus, si accipiatur numerus simplex, qui est tantum in acceptione intellectus, per unum mensuratur. Si vero accipiatur numerus rerum in divinis personis, sic non competit ibi ratio mensurati, quia eadem est magnitudo trium personarum, ut infra patebit; idem autem non mensuratur per idem.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29714)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod termini numerales ponant aliquid in divinis. Unitas enim divina est eius essentia. Sed omnis numerus est unitas repetita. Ergo omnis terminus numeralis in divinis significat essentiam. Ergo ponit aliquid in Deo.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29715)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid dicitur de Deo et creaturis, eminentius convenit Deo quam creaturis. Sed termini numerales in creaturis aliquid ponunt. Ergo multo magis in Deo.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29716)\n\nPraeterea, si termini numerales non ponunt aliquid in divinis, sed inducuntur ad removendum tantum, ut per pluralitatem removeatur unitas, et per unitatem pluralitas; sequitur quod sit circulatio in ratione, confundens intellectum et nihil certificans; quod est inconveniens. Relinquitur ergo quod termini numerales aliquid ponunt in divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29717)\n\nSed contra est quod Hilarius dicit, in IV de Trin., *sustulit singularitatis ac solitudinis intelligentiam professio consortii*, quod est professio pluralitatis. Et Ambrosius dicit, in libro de fide *cum unum Deum dicimus, unitas pluralitatem excludit deorum, non quantitatem in Deo ponimus*. Ex quibus videtur quod huiusmodi nomina sunt inducta in divinis ad removendum, non ad ponendum aliquid.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29718)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Magister, in sententiis, ponit quod termini numerales non ponunt aliquid in divinis, sed removent tantum. Alii vero dicunt contrarium. Ad evidentiam igitur huius, considerandum est quod omnis pluralitas consequitur aliquam divisionem. Est autem duplex divisio. Una materialis, quae fit secundum divisionem continui, et hanc consequitur numerus qui est species quantitatis. Unde talis numerus non est nisi in rebus materialibus habentibus quantitatem. Alia est divisio formalis, quae fit per oppositas vel diversas formas, et hanc divisionem sequitur multitudo quae non est in aliquo genere, sed est de transcendentibus, secundum quod ens dividitur per unum et multa. Et talem multitudinem solam contingit esse in rebus immaterialibus. Quidam igitur, non considerantes nisi multitudinem quae est species quantitatis discretae, quia videbant quod quantitas discreta non habet locum in divinis, posuerunt quod termini numerales non ponunt aliquid in Deo, sed removent tantum. Alii vero, eandem multitudinem considerantes, dixerunt quod, sicut scientia ponitur in Deo secundum rationem propriam scientiae, non autem secundum rationem sui generis, quia in Deo nulla est qualitas; ita numerus in Deo ponitur secundum propriam rationem numeri, non autem secundum rationem sui generis, quod est quantitas. Nos autem dicimus quod termini numerales, secundum quod veniunt in praedicationem divinam, non sumuntur a numero qui est species quantitatis; quia sic de Deo non dicerentur nisi metaphorice, sicut et aliae proprietates corporalium, sicut latitudo, longitudo, et similia, sed sumuntur a multitudine secundum quod est transcendens. Multitudo autem sic accepta hoc modo se habet ad multa de quibus praedicatur, sicut unum quod convertitur cum ente ad ens. Huiusmodi autem unum, sicut supra dictum est, cum de Dei unitate ageretur, non addit aliquid supra ens nisi negationem divisionis tantum, unum enim significat ens indivisum. Et ideo de quocumque dicatur unum, significatur illa res indivisa, sicut unum dictum de homine, significat naturam vel substantiam hominis non divisam. Et eadem ratione, cum dicuntur res multae, multitudo sic accepta significat res illas cum indivisione circa unamquamque earum. Numerus autem qui est species quantitatis, ponit quoddam accidens additum supra ens, et similiter unum quod est principium numeri. Termini ergo numerales significant in divinis illa de quibus dicuntur, et super hoc nihil addunt nisi negationem, ut dictum est, et quantum ad hoc, veritatem dixit Magister in sententiis. Ut, cum dicimus, essentia est una, unum significat essentiam indivisam, cum dicimus, persona est una, significat personam indivisam, cum dicimus, personae sunt plures, significantur illae personae, et indivisio circa unamquamque earum; quia de ratione multitudinis est, quod ex unitatibus constet.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29719)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod unum, cum sit de transcendentibus, est communius quam substantia et quam relatio, et similiter multitudo. Unde potest stare in divinis et pro substantia et pro relatione, secundum quod competit his quibus adiungitur. Et tamen per huiusmodi nomina, supra essentiam vel relationem, additur, ex eorum significatione propria, negatio quaedam divisionis, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29720)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod multitudo quae ponit aliquid in rebus creatis, est species quantitatis; quae non transumitur in divinam praedicationem; sed tantum multitudo transcendens, quae non addit supra ea de quibus dicitur, nisi indivisionem circa singula. Et talis multitudo dicitur de Deo.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29721)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod unum non est remotivum multitudinis, sed divisionis, quae est prior, secundum rationem, quam unum vel multitudo. Multitudo autem non removet unitatem, sed removet divisionem circa unumquodque eorum ex quibus constat multitudo. Et haec supra exposita sunt, cum de divina unitate ageretur. Sciendum tamen est quod auctoritates in oppositum inductae, non probant sufficienter propositum. Licet enim pluralitate excludatur solitudo, et unitate deorum pluralitas, non tamen sequitur quod his nominibus hoc solum significetur. Albedine enim excluditur nigredo, non tamen nomine albedinis significatur sola nigredinis exclusio.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29722)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen persona non possit esse commune tribus personis. Nihil enim est commune tribus personis nisi essentia. Sed hoc nomen persona non significat essentiam in recto. Ergo non est commune tribus.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29723)\n\nPraeterea, commune opponitur incommunicabili. Sed de ratione personae est quod sit incommunicabilis, ut patet ex definitione Ricardi de s. Victore supra posita. Ergo hoc nomen persona non est commune tribus.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29724)\n\nPraeterea, si est commune tribus, aut ista communitas attenditur secundum rem, aut secundum rationem. Sed non secundum rem, quia sic tres personae essent una persona. Nec iterum secundum rationem tantum, quia sic persona esset universale, in divinis autem non est universale et particulare, neque genus neque species, ut supra ostensum est. Non ergo hoc nomen persona est commune tribus.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29725)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, VII de Trin., quod cum quaereretur, quid tres? Responsum est, tres personae; quia commune est eis id quod est persona.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29726)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ipse modus loquendi ostendit hoc nomen persona tribus esse commune, cum dicimus tres personas, sicut cum dicimus tres homines, ostendimus hominem esse commune tribus. Manifestum est autem quod non est communitas rei, sicut una essentia communis est tribus, quia sic sequeretur unam esse personam trium, sicut essentia est una. Qualis autem sit communitas, investigantes diversimode locuti sunt. Quidam enim dixerunt quod est communitas negationis; propter hoc, quod in definitione personae ponitur incommunicabile. Quidam autem dixerunt quod est communitas intentionis, eo quod in definitione personae ponitur individuum; sicut si dicatur quod esse speciem est commune equo et bovi. Sed utrumque horum excluditur per hoc, quod hoc nomen persona non est nomen negationis neque intentionis, sed est nomen rei. Et ideo dicendum est quod etiam in rebus humanis hoc nomen persona est commune communitate rationis, non sicut genus vel species, sed sicut individuum vagum. Nomina enim generum vel specierum, ut homo vel animal, sunt imposita ad significandum ipsas naturas communes; non autem intentiones naturarum communium, quae significantur his nominibus genus vel species. Sed individuum vagum, ut aliquis homo, significat naturam communem cum determinato modo existendi qui competit singularibus, ut scilicet sit per se subsistens distinctum ab aliis. Sed in nomine singularis designati, significatur determinatum distinguens, sicut in nomine Socratis haec caro et hoc os. Hoc tamen interest, quod aliquis homo significat naturam, vel individuum ex parte naturae, cum modo existendi qui competit singularibus, hoc autem nomen persona non est impositum ad significandum individuum ex parte naturae, sed ad significandum rem subsistentem in tali natura. Hoc autem est commune secundum rationem omnibus personis divinis, ut unaquaeque earum subsistat in natura divina distincta ab aliis. Et sic hoc nomen persona, secundum rationem, est commune tribus personis divinis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29727)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de communitate rei.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29728)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet persona sit incommunicabilis, tamen ipse modus existendi incommunicabiliter, potest esse pluribus communis.\n\n[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29729)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet sit communitas rationis et non rei tamen non sequitur quod in divinis sit universale et particulare, vel genus vel species. Tum quia neque in rebus humanis communitas personae est communitas generis vel speciei. Tum quia personae divinae habent unum esse, genus autem et species, et quodlibet universale, praedicatur de pluribus secundum esse differentibus.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0002","text":"[I q. 30 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de pluralitate personarum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum sint plures personae in divinis. Secundo, quot sunt. Tertio, quid significent termini numerales in divinis. Quarto, de communitate huius nominis persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0005","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit ponere plures personas in divinis. Persona enim est rationalis naturae individua substantia. Si ergo sunt plures personae in divinis, sequitur quod sint plures substantiae, quod videtur haereticum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0007","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0008","text":"Praeterea, pluralitas proprietatum absolutarum non facit distinctionem personarum, neque in Deo neque in nobis, multo igitur minus pluralitas relationum. Sed in Deo non est alia pluralitas nisi relationum, ut supra dictum est. Ergo non potest dici quod in Deo sint plures personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0009","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0010","text":"Praeterea, Boetius dicit, de Deo loquens, quod hoc vere unum est, in quo nullus est numerus. Sed pluralitas importat numerum. Ergo non sunt plures personae in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0011","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0012","text":"Praeterea, ubicumque est numerus, ibi est totum et pars. Si igitur in Deo sit numerus personarum, erit in Deo ponere totum et partem, quod simplicitati divinae repugnat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0013","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Athanasius, *alia est persona patris, alia filii, alia spiritus sancti*. Ergo pater et filius et spiritus sanctus sunt plures personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0015","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod plures esse personas in divinis, sequitur ex praemissis. Ostensum est enim supra quod hoc nomen persona significat in divinis relationem, ut rem subsistentem in natura divina. Supra autem habitum est quod sunt plures relationes reales in divinis. Unde sequitur quod sint plures res subsistentes in divina natura. Et hoc est esse plures personas in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0017","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod substantia non ponitur in definitione personae secundum quod significat essentiam, sed secundum quod significat suppositum, quod patet ex hoc quod additur individua. Ad significandum autem substantiam sic dictam, habent Graeci nomen hypostasis, unde sicut nos dicimus tres personas, ita ipsi dicunt tres hypostases. Nos autem non consuevimus dicere tres substantias, ne intelligerentur tres essentiae, propter nominis aequivocationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0019","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod proprietates absolutae in divinis, ut bonitas et sapientia, non opponuntur ad invicem, unde neque realiter distinguuntur. Quamvis ergo eis conveniat subsistere, non tamen sunt plures res subsistentes, quod est esse plures personas. Proprietates autem absolutae in rebus creatis non subsistunt, licet realiter ab invicem distinguantur, ut albedo et dulcedo. Sed proprietates relativae in Deo et subsistunt, et realiter ab invicem distinguuntur, ut supra dictum est. Unde pluralitas talium proprietatum sufficit ad pluralitatem personarum in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0021","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod a Deo, propter summam unitatem et simplicitatem, excluditur omnis pluralitas absolute dictorum; non autem pluralitas relationum. Quia relationes praedicantur de aliquo ut ad alterum; et sic compositionem in ipso de quo dicuntur non important, ut Boetius in eodem libro docet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0023","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod numerus est duplex, scilicet numerus simplex vel absolutus, ut duo et tria et quatuor; et numerus qui est in rebus numeratis, ut duo homines et duo equi. Si igitur in divinis accipiatur numerus absolute sive abstracte, nihil prohibet in eo esse totum et partem, et sic non est nisi in acceptione intellectus nostri; non enim numerus absolutus a rebus numeratis est nisi in intellectu. Si autem accipiamus numerum prout est in rebus numeratis, sic in rebus quidem creatis, unum est pars duorum, et duo trium, ut unus homo duorum, et duo trium, sed non est sic in Deo, quia tantus est pater quanta tota Trinitas, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0025","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0026","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0027","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Deo sint plures personae quam tres. Pluralitas enim personarum in divinis est secundum pluralitatem proprietatum relativarum, ut dictum est. Sed quatuor sunt relationes in divinis, ut supra dictum est, scilicet paternitas, filiatio, communis spiratio et processio. Ergo quatuor personae sunt in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0028","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0029","text":"Praeterea, non plus differt natura a voluntate in Deo, quam natura ab intellectu. Sed in divinis est alia persona quae procedit per modum voluntatis, ut amor; et alia quae procedit per modum naturae, ut filius. Ergo est etiam alia quae procedit per modum intellectus, ut verbum; et alia quae procedit per modum naturae, ut filius. Et sic iterum sequitur quod non sunt tantum tres personae in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0030","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0031","text":"Praeterea, in rebus creatis quod excellentius est, plures habet operationes intrinsecas, sicut homo supra alia animalia habet intelligere et velle. Sed Deus in infinitum excedit omnem creaturam. Ergo non solum est ibi persona procedens per modum voluntatis et per modum intellectus, sed infinitis aliis modis. Ergo sunt infinitae personae in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0032","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0033","text":"Praeterea, ex infinita bonitate patris est, quod infinite seipsum communicet, producendo personam divinam. Sed etiam in spiritu sancto est infinita bonitas. Ergo spiritus sanctus producit divinam personam, et illa aliam, et sic in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0034","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0035","text":"Praeterea, omne quod continetur sub determinato numero, est mensuratum, numerus enim mensura quaedam est. Sed personae divinae sunt immensae, ut patet per Athanasium, *immensus pater, immensus filius, immensus spiritus sanctus*. Non ergo sub numero ternario continentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0036","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0037","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur I Ioan. ult., *tres sunt qui testimonium dant in caelo, pater, verbum et spiritus sanctus*. Quaerentibus autem, quid tres? Respondetur, tres personae, ut Augustinus dicit, in VII de Trin. Sunt igitur tres personae tantum in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0038","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum praemissa, necesse est ponere tantum tres personas in divinis. Ostensum est enim quod plures personae sunt plures relationes subsistentes, ab invicem realiter distinctae. Realis autem distinctio inter relationes divinas non est nisi in ratione oppositionis relativae. Ergo oportet duas relationes oppositas ad duas personas pertinere, si quae autem relationes oppositae non sunt, ad eandem personam necesse est eas pertinere. Paternitas ergo et filiatio, cum sint oppositae relationes, ad duas personas ex necessitate pertinent. Paternitas igitur subsistens est persona patris, et filiatio subsistens est persona filii. Aliae autem duae relationes ad neutram harum oppositionem habent, sed sibi invicem opponuntur. Impossibile est igitur quod ambae uni personae conveniant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","text":"Oportet ergo quod vel una earum conveniat utrique dictarum personarum, aut quod una uni, et alia alii. Non autem potest esse quod processio conveniat patri et filio, vel alteri eorum, quia sic sequeretur quod processio intellectus, quae est generatio in divinis, secundum quam accipitur paternitas et filiatio, prodiret ex processione amoris, secundum quam accipitur spiratio et processio, si persona generans et genita procederent a spirante, quod est contra praemissa. Relinquitur ergo quod spiratio conveniat et personae patris et personae filii, utpote nullam habens oppositionem relativam nec ad paternitatem nec ad filiationem. Et per consequens oportet quod conveniat processio alteri personae, quae dicitur persona spiritus sancti, quae per modum amoris procedit, ut supra habitum est. Relinquitur ergo tantum tres personas esse in divinis, scilicet patrem et filium et spiritum sanctum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0041","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet sint quatuor relationes in divinis, tamen una earum, scilicet spiratio, non separatur a persona patris et filii, sed convenit utrique. Et sic, licet sit relatio, non tamen dicitur proprietas, quia non convenit uni tantum personae, neque est relatio personalis, idest constituens personam. Sed hae tres relationes, paternitas, filiatio et processio, dicuntur proprietates personales, quasi personas constituentes, nam paternitas est persona patris, filiatio persona filii, processio persona spiritus sancti procedentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0043","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod id quod procedit per modum intellectus, ut verbum, procedit secundum rationem similitudinis, sicut etiam id quod procedit per modum naturae, et ideo supra dictum est quod processio verbi divini est ipsa generatio per modum naturae. Amor autem, inquantum huiusmodi, non procedit ut similitudo illius a quo procedit (licet in divinis amor sit coessentialis inquantum est divinus), et ideo processio amoris non dicitur generatio in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0045","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod homo, cum sit perfectior aliis animalibus, habet plures operationes intrinsecas quam alia animalia, quia eius perfectio est per modum compositionis. Unde in Angelis, qui sunt perfectiores et simpliciores, sunt pauciores operationes intrinsecae quam in homine, quia in eis non est imaginari, sentire, et huiusmodi. Sed in Deo, secundum rem, non est nisi una operatio, quae est sua essentia. Sed quomodo sunt duae processiones, supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0047","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si spiritus sanctus haberet aliam numero bonitatem a bonitate patris, oporteret enim quod, sicut pater per suam bonitatem producit personam divinam, ita et spiritus sanctus. Sed una et eadem bonitas patris est et spiritus sancti. Neque etiam est distinctio nisi per relationes personarum. Unde bonitas convenit spiritui sancto quasi habita ab alio, patri autem, sicut a quo communicatur alteri. Oppositio autem relationis non permittit ut cum relatione spiritus sancti sit relatio principii respectu divinae personae, quia ipse procedit ab aliis personis quae in divinis esse possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0049","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod numerus determinatus, si accipiatur numerus simplex, qui est tantum in acceptione intellectus, per unum mensuratur. Si vero accipiatur numerus rerum in divinis personis, sic non competit ibi ratio mensurati, quia eadem est magnitudo trium personarum, ut infra patebit; idem autem non mensuratur per idem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0051","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0052","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0053","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod termini numerales ponant aliquid in divinis. Unitas enim divina est eius essentia. Sed omnis numerus est unitas repetita. Ergo omnis terminus numeralis in divinis significat essentiam. Ergo ponit aliquid in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0054","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0055","text":"Praeterea, quidquid dicitur de Deo et creaturis, eminentius convenit Deo quam creaturis. Sed termini numerales in creaturis aliquid ponunt. Ergo multo magis in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0056","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0057","text":"Praeterea, si termini numerales non ponunt aliquid in divinis, sed inducuntur ad removendum tantum, ut per pluralitatem removeatur unitas, et per unitatem pluralitas; sequitur quod sit circulatio in ratione, confundens intellectum et nihil certificans; quod est inconveniens. Relinquitur ergo quod termini numerales aliquid ponunt in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0058","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0059","text":"Sed contra est quod Hilarius dicit, in IV de Trin., *sustulit singularitatis ac solitudinis intelligentiam professio consortii*, quod est professio pluralitatis. Et Ambrosius dicit, in libro de fide *cum unum Deum dicimus, unitas pluralitatem excludit deorum, non quantitatem in Deo ponimus*. Ex quibus videtur quod huiusmodi nomina sunt inducta in divinis ad removendum, non ad ponendum aliquid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0060","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Magister, in sententiis, ponit quod termini numerales non ponunt aliquid in divinis, sed removent tantum. Alii vero dicunt contrarium. Ad evidentiam igitur huius, considerandum est quod omnis pluralitas consequitur aliquam divisionem. Est autem duplex divisio. Una materialis, quae fit secundum divisionem continui, et hanc consequitur numerus qui est species quantitatis. Unde talis numerus non est nisi in rebus materialibus habentibus quantitatem. Alia est divisio formalis, quae fit per oppositas vel diversas formas, et hanc divisionem sequitur multitudo quae non est in aliquo genere, sed est de transcendentibus, secundum quod ens dividitur per unum et multa. Et talem multitudinem solam contingit esse in rebus immaterialibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","text":"Quidam igitur, non considerantes nisi multitudinem quae est species quantitatis discretae, quia videbant quod quantitas discreta non habet locum in divinis, posuerunt quod termini numerales non ponunt aliquid in Deo, sed removent tantum. Alii vero, eandem multitudinem considerantes, dixerunt quod, sicut scientia ponitur in Deo secundum rationem propriam scientiae, non autem secundum rationem sui generis, quia in Deo nulla est qualitas; ita numerus in Deo ponitur secundum propriam rationem numeri, non autem secundum rationem sui generis, quod est quantitas. Nos autem dicimus quod termini numerales, secundum quod veniunt in praedicationem divinam, non sumuntur a numero qui est species quantitatis; quia sic de Deo non dicerentur nisi metaphorice, sicut et aliae proprietates corporalium, sicut latitudo, longitudo, et similia, sed sumuntur a multitudine secundum quod est transcendens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","text":"Multitudo autem sic accepta hoc modo se habet ad multa de quibus praedicatur, sicut unum quod convertitur cum ente ad ens. Huiusmodi autem unum, sicut supra dictum est, cum de Dei unitate ageretur, non addit aliquid supra ens nisi negationem divisionis tantum, unum enim significat ens indivisum. Et ideo de quocumque dicatur unum, significatur illa res indivisa, sicut unum dictum de homine, significat naturam vel substantiam hominis non divisam. Et eadem ratione, cum dicuntur res multae, multitudo sic accepta significat res illas cum indivisione circa unamquamque earum. Numerus autem qui est species quantitatis, ponit quoddam accidens additum supra ens, et similiter unum quod est principium numeri. Termini ergo numerales significant in divinis illa de quibus dicuntur, et super hoc nihil addunt nisi negationem, ut dictum est, et quantum ad hoc, veritatem dixit Magister in sententiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0064","text":"Ut, cum dicimus, essentia est una, unum significat essentiam indivisam, cum dicimus, persona est una, significat personam indivisam, cum dicimus, personae sunt plures, significantur illae personae, et indivisio circa unamquamque earum; quia de ratione multitudinis est, quod ex unitatibus constet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0065","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0066","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod unum, cum sit de transcendentibus, est communius quam substantia et quam relatio, et similiter multitudo. Unde potest stare in divinis et pro substantia et pro relatione, secundum quod competit his quibus adiungitur. Et tamen per huiusmodi nomina, supra essentiam vel relationem, additur, ex eorum significatione propria, negatio quaedam divisionis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0067","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0068","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod multitudo quae ponit aliquid in rebus creatis, est species quantitatis; quae non transumitur in divinam praedicationem; sed tantum multitudo transcendens, quae non addit supra ea de quibus dicitur, nisi indivisionem circa singula. Et talis multitudo dicitur de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0069","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod unum non est remotivum multitudinis, sed divisionis, quae est prior, secundum rationem, quam unum vel multitudo. Multitudo autem non removet unitatem, sed removet divisionem circa unumquodque eorum ex quibus constat multitudo. Et haec supra exposita sunt, cum de divina unitate ageretur. Sciendum tamen est quod auctoritates in oppositum inductae, non probant sufficienter propositum. Licet enim pluralitate excludatur solitudo, et unitate deorum pluralitas, non tamen sequitur quod his nominibus hoc solum significetur. Albedine enim excluditur nigredo, non tamen nomine albedinis significatur sola nigredinis exclusio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0071","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0072","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0073","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen persona non possit esse commune tribus personis. Nihil enim est commune tribus personis nisi essentia. Sed hoc nomen persona non significat essentiam in recto. Ergo non est commune tribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0074","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0075","text":"Praeterea, commune opponitur incommunicabili. Sed de ratione personae est quod sit incommunicabilis, ut patet ex definitione Ricardi de s. Victore supra posita. Ergo hoc nomen persona non est commune tribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0076","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0077","text":"Praeterea, si est commune tribus, aut ista communitas attenditur secundum rem, aut secundum rationem. Sed non secundum rem, quia sic tres personae essent una persona. Nec iterum secundum rationem tantum, quia sic persona esset universale, in divinis autem non est universale et particulare, neque genus neque species, ut supra ostensum est. Non ergo hoc nomen persona est commune tribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0078","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0079","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, VII de Trin., quod cum quaereretur, quid tres? Responsum est, tres personae; quia commune est eis id quod est persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0080","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ipse modus loquendi ostendit hoc nomen persona tribus esse commune, cum dicimus tres personas, sicut cum dicimus tres homines, ostendimus hominem esse commune tribus. Manifestum est autem quod non est communitas rei, sicut una essentia communis est tribus, quia sic sequeretur unam esse personam trium, sicut essentia est una. Qualis autem sit communitas, investigantes diversimode locuti sunt. Quidam enim dixerunt quod est communitas negationis; propter hoc, quod in definitione personae ponitur incommunicabile. Quidam autem dixerunt quod est communitas intentionis, eo quod in definitione personae ponitur individuum; sicut si dicatur quod esse speciem est commune equo et bovi. Sed utrumque horum excluditur per hoc, quod hoc nomen persona non est nomen negationis neque intentionis, sed est nomen rei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","text":"Et ideo dicendum est quod etiam in rebus humanis hoc nomen persona est commune communitate rationis, non sicut genus vel species, sed sicut individuum vagum. Nomina enim generum vel specierum, ut homo vel animal, sunt imposita ad significandum ipsas naturas communes; non autem intentiones naturarum communium, quae significantur his nominibus genus vel species. Sed individuum vagum, ut aliquis homo, significat naturam communem cum determinato modo existendi qui competit singularibus, ut scilicet sit per se subsistens distinctum ab aliis. Sed in nomine singularis designati, significatur determinatum distinguens, sicut in nomine Socratis haec caro et hoc os.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","text":"Hoc tamen interest, quod aliquis homo significat naturam, vel individuum ex parte naturae, cum modo existendi qui competit singularibus, hoc autem nomen persona non est impositum ad significandum individuum ex parte naturae, sed ad significandum rem subsistentem in tali natura. Hoc autem est commune secundum rationem omnibus personis divinis, ut unaquaeque earum subsistat in natura divina distincta ab aliis. Et sic hoc nomen persona, secundum rationem, est commune tribus personis divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0084","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0085","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de communitate rei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0086","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0087","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet persona sit incommunicabilis, tamen ipse modus existendi incommunicabiliter, potest esse pluribus communis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0088","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet sit communitas rationis et non rei tamen non sequitur quod in divinis sit universale et particulare, vel genus vel species. Tum quia neque in rebus humanis communitas personae est communitas generis vel speciei. Tum quia personae divinae habent unum esse, genus autem et species, et quodlibet universale, praedicatur de pluribus secundum esse differentibus.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2a29fdd5aea77068192edef08bedf1450f7fd5d28ad6e34938e8bc62bf7cab9a","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141156575557Z_684bfe0b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 30","source_sha256":"2a29fdd5aea77068192edef08bedf1450f7fd5d28ad6e34938e8bc62bf7cab9a","sha256":"f5d6289d3e767cc7416157b72dbb7763b6593f84aff392345103a4add4f9fec7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:11:56.575585+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0002","text":"[I q. 30 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0003","text":"Next, the plurality of persons is inquired into. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether there are several persons in God. Second, how many there are. Third, what numerical terms signify in God. Fourth, concerning the commonality of this name, person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0005","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that several persons should not be posited in God. For a person is an individual substance of a rational nature. Therefore, if there are several persons in God, it follows that there are several substances, which seems heretical.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0007","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0008","text":"Further, a plurality of absolute properties does not make for a distinction of persons, either in God or in us; much less, therefore, does a plurality of relations. But in God there is no plurality other than that of relations, as was said above. Therefore it cannot be said that there are several persons in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0009","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0010","text":"Further, Boethius says, speaking of God, that that is truly one in which there is no number. But plurality implies number. Therefore there are not several persons in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0011","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0012","text":"Further, wherever there is number, there are whole and part. Therefore, if there is a number of persons in God, one will have to posit whole and part in God, which is incompatible with divine simplicity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0013","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0014","text":"On the contrary, Athanasius says: *the person of the Father is other, that of the Son other, that of the Holy Spirit other*. Therefore the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit are several persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0015","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that there are several persons in God follows from what has been set forth above. For it was shown above that this name, person, in God signifies a relation as a subsisting thing in the divine nature. But it was established above that there are several real relations in God. Hence it follows that there are several subsisting things in the divine nature. And this is for there to be several persons in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0017","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that substance is not placed in the definition of person insofar as it signifies essence, but insofar as it signifies a suppositum, as is clear from the addition of individual. Now the Greeks have the name hypostasis to signify substance so called; hence, just as we say three persons, so they say three hypostases. But we are not accustomed to say three substances, lest three essences be understood, on account of the equivocation of the name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0019","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that absolute properties in God, such as goodness and wisdom, are not opposed to one another, whence neither are they really distinct. Therefore, although it belongs to them to subsist, they are nevertheless not several subsisting things, which is to be several persons. But absolute properties in created things do not subsist, although they are really distinct from one another, as whiteness and sweetness. But relative properties in God both subsist and are really distinct from one another, as was said above. Hence the plurality of such properties suffices for a plurality of persons in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0021","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that, on account of supreme unity and simplicity, every plurality of things said absolutely is excluded from God; but not a plurality of relations. For relations are predicated of something as being toward another; and thus they do not imply composition in that of which they are said, as Boethius teaches in the same book.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0023","text":"[I q. 30 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that number is twofold: namely, simple or absolute number, such as two, three, and four; and number that is in numbered things, such as two men and two horses. Therefore, if number is taken in God absolutely or abstractly, nothing prevents whole and part from being in it; and thus it exists only in the apprehension of our intellect, for absolute number apart from numbered things exists only in the intellect. But if we take number as it is in numbered things, then indeed in created things one is a part of two, and two of three, as one man of two, and two of three; but it is not so in God, because the Father is as great as the whole Trinity, as will be clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0025","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0026","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0027","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there are more than three persons in God. For the plurality of persons in things divine is according to the plurality of relative properties, as has been said. But there are four relations in things divine, as was said above, namely, paternity, filiation, common spiration, and procession. Therefore there are four persons in things divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0028","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0029","text":"Moreover, nature differs no more from will in God than nature does from intellect. But in things divine there is one person who proceeds by way of will, as love; and another who proceeds by way of nature, as the Son. Therefore there is also another who proceeds by way of intellect, as the Word; and another who proceeds by way of nature, as the Son. And thus it follows again that there are not only three persons in things divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0030","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0031","text":"Moreover, among created things, what is more excellent has more intrinsic operations, just as man, above other animals, has understanding and willing. But God infinitely exceeds every creature. Therefore there is not only a person proceeding by way of will and by way of intellect, but in infinitely many other ways. Therefore there are infinite persons in things divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0032","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0033","text":"Moreover, it belongs to the infinite goodness of the Father that He communicate Himself infinitely, by producing a divine person. But infinite goodness is also in the Holy Spirit. Therefore the Holy Spirit produces a divine person, and that one another, and so on to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0034","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0035","text":"Moreover, everything contained under a determinate number is measured, for number is a certain measure. But the divine persons are immense, as is clear from Athanasius: *the Father immense, the Son immense, the Holy Spirit immense*. Therefore they are not contained under the number three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0036","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0037","text":"On the contrary, it is said in the last chapter of I John, *there are three who bear testimony in heaven: the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit*. But to those asking, “What three?” the answer is given: three persons, as Augustine says in Book VII of *On the Trinity*. Therefore there are only three persons in things divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0038","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","text":"I answer that, according to what has been set forth, it is necessary to posit only three persons in things divine. For it has been shown that several persons are several subsistent relations, really distinct from one another. Now real distinction among the divine relations exists only by reason of relative opposition. Therefore two opposed relations must belong to two persons; but if any relations are not opposed, they must belong to the same person. Paternity, therefore, and filiation, since they are opposed relations, necessarily belong to two persons. Therefore subsistent paternity is the person of the Father, and subsistent filiation is the person of the Son. But the other two relations have opposition to neither of these, but are opposed to one another. Therefore it is impossible for both to belong to one person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","text":"Therefore, either one of them must belong to each of the said persons, or one to one and the other to the other. But it cannot be that procession belongs to the Father and the Son, or to either one of them, because it would then follow that the procession of intellect, which is generation in things divine, according to which paternity and filiation are understood, would proceed from the procession of love, according to which spiration and procession are understood, if the generating and generated person proceeded from the spirating person; which is contrary to what has been set forth. It remains, therefore, that spiration belongs both to the person of the Father and to the person of the Son, since it has no relative opposition either to paternity or to filiation. And consequently procession must belong to the other person, who is called the person of the Holy Spirit, who proceeds by way of love, as was established above. Therefore it remains that there are only three persons in things divine: namely, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0041","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although there are four relations in things divine, one of them, namely spiration, is not separated from the person of the Father and of the Son, but belongs to both. And thus, although it is a relation, it is not called a property, because it does not belong to only one person; nor is it a personal relation, that is, one constituting a person. But these three relations—paternity, filiation, and procession—are called personal properties, as it were constituting the persons; for paternity is the person of the Father, filiation the person of the Son, procession the person of the proceeding Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0043","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","text":"To the second it must be said that that which proceeds by way of intellect, as the Word, proceeds according to the character of likeness, just as does that which proceeds by way of nature; and therefore it was said above that the procession of the divine Word is generation itself by way of nature. But love, as such, does not proceed as a likeness of that from which it proceeds (although in things divine love is coessential insofar as it is divine), and therefore the procession of love is not called generation in things divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0045","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","text":"To the third it must be said that man, since he is more perfect than other animals, has more intrinsic operations than other animals, because his perfection is by way of composition. Hence in angels, who are more perfect and simpler, there are fewer intrinsic operations than in man, because in them there is no imagining, sensing, and the like. But in God, in reality, there is only one operation, which is His essence. But how there are two processions was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0047","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","text":"To the fourth it must be said that that argument would proceed if the Holy Spirit had a goodness numerically other than the goodness of the Father; for it would be necessary that, just as the Father produces a divine person through His goodness, so also would the Holy Spirit. But the goodness of the Father and of the Holy Spirit is one and the same. Nor is there distinction except through the relations of the persons. Hence goodness belongs to the Holy Spirit as possessed from another, but to the Father as that from whom it is communicated to another. But the opposition of relation does not permit that, together with the relation of the Holy Spirit, there be a relation of principle with respect to a divine person, because He proceeds from the other persons that can be in things divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0049","text":"[I q. 30 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","text":"To the fifth it must be said that a determinate number, if it is taken as simple number, which exists only in the apprehension of the intellect, is measured by one. But if the number of things in the divine persons is taken, then the notion of what is measured does not apply there, because the magnitude of the three persons is the same, as will be clear below; but the same is not measured by the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0051","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0052","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0053","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that numerical terms posit something in divine things. For divine unity is His essence. But every number is a repeated unity. Therefore every numerical term in divine things signifies the essence. Therefore it posits something in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0054","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0055","text":"Further, whatever is said of God and creatures belongs more eminently to God than to creatures. But numerical terms posit something in creatures. Therefore much more so in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0056","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0057","text":"Further, if numerical terms do not posit something in divine things, but are introduced only in order to remove, so that by plurality unity is removed, and by unity plurality; it follows that there is a circularity in reasoning, confusing the intellect and establishing nothing; which is unfitting. Therefore numerical terms posit something in divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0058","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0059","text":"But on the contrary, Hilary says, in *On the Trinity*, IV, “the profession of fellowship, which is the profession of plurality, has taken away the understanding of singularity and solitude.” And Ambrose says, in the book *On the Faith*, “when we say one God, unity excludes a plurality of gods; we do not posit quantity in God.” From these it seems that names of this kind are introduced in divine things in order to remove, not to posit something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0060","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Master, in the *Sentences*, holds that numerical terms do not posit something in divine things, but only remove. Others, however, say the contrary. Therefore, for the clarification of this matter, it must be considered that every plurality follows upon some division. Now division is twofold. One is material, which comes about according to the division of a continuum, and number, which is a species of quantity, follows upon this. Hence such number is only in material things having quantity. The other is formal division, which comes about through opposed or diverse forms, and multitude follows upon this division; this multitude is not in any genus, but is among the transcendentals, insofar as being is divided by one and many. And only such a multitude can be in immaterial things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","text":"Therefore some, considering only the multitude that is a species of discrete quantity, because they saw that discrete quantity has no place in divine things, maintained that numerical terms do not posit something in God, but only remove. Others, however, considering the same multitude, said that, just as knowledge is posited in God according to the proper notion of knowledge, but not according to the notion of its genus, since there is no quality in God; so number is posited in God according to the proper notion of number, but not according to the notion of its genus, which is quantity. But we say that numerical terms, insofar as they occur in divine predication, are not taken from number which is a species of quantity; for thus they would not be said of God except metaphorically, just as the other properties of bodies, such as breadth, length, and the like; but they are taken from multitude insofar as it is transcendental.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","text":"But multitude so understood is related to the many things of which it is predicated in the same way as one, which is convertible with being, is related to being. Now such a one, as was said above when divine unity was under discussion, adds nothing to being except only the negation of division; for one signifies undivided being. And therefore, of whatever one is said, that thing is signified as undivided; just as one, said of a man, signifies the nature or substance of man as undivided. And by the same reasoning, when things are said to be many, multitude so understood signifies those things together with indivision in respect of each one of them. But number, which is a species of quantity, posits a certain accident added over and above being, and likewise one which is the principle of number. Therefore numerical terms signify in divine things those things of which they are said, and add nothing beyond this except negation, as has been said; and in this respect the Master spoke truly in the *Sentences*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0064","text":"Thus, when we say, “the essence is one,” one signifies the undivided essence; when we say, “the person is one,” it signifies the undivided person; when we say, “the persons are many,” those persons are signified, together with indivision in respect of each one of them; because it belongs to the notion of multitude that it consist of unities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0065","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0066","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that one, since it is among the transcendentals, is more common than substance and than relation, and likewise multitude. Hence it can stand in divine things both for substance and for relation, according as it belongs to those to which it is joined. And yet, by names of this kind, over and above essence or relation, there is added, from their proper signification, a certain negation of division, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0067","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0068","text":"To the second it must be said that the multitude which posits something in created things is a species of quantity; this is not transferred into divine predication. Rather, only transcendent multitude is so transferred, which adds nothing beyond the things of which it is said except indivision with respect to each. And such multitude is said of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0069","text":"[I q. 30 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","text":"To the third it must be said that one does not remove multitude, but division, which is prior in notion to either one or multitude. But multitude does not remove unity; rather, it removes division with respect to each one of those of which the multitude consists. And these matters were explained above, when divine unity was under discussion. It should nevertheless be known that the authorities adduced to the contrary do not sufficiently prove the point proposed. For although solitude is excluded by plurality, and plurality of gods by unity, it does not nevertheless follow that only this is signified by these names. For blackness is excluded by whiteness, yet by the name whiteness not merely the exclusion of blackness is signified.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0071","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0072","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0073","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that this name “person” cannot be common to the three persons. For nothing is common to the three persons except the essence. But this name “person” does not signify the essence directly. Therefore it is not common to the three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0074","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0075","text":"Further, what is common is opposed to what is incommunicable. But it belongs to the notion of person that it be incommunicable, as is clear from the definition of Richard of St. Victor set forth above. Therefore this name “person” is not common to the three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0076","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0077","text":"Further, if it is common to the three, this commonality is considered either according to the thing or according to reason. But not according to the thing, because then the three persons would be one person. Nor again according to reason alone, because then person would be a universal; but in divine things there is neither universal nor particular, neither genus nor species, as was shown above. Therefore this name “person” is not common to the three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0078","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0079","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in Book VII On the Trinity, that when it was asked, “three what?” the answer was, “three persons,” because that which is person is common to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0080","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","text":"I answer that it must be said that the very manner of speaking shows that this name “person” is common to the three: when we say “three persons,” just as when we say “three men,” we show that man is common to the three. Now it is evident that this is not a commonality of the thing, as the one essence is common to the three; for thus it would follow that the three are one person, just as the essence is one. But as to what sort of commonality it is, those investigating have spoken in different ways. For some said that it is a commonality of negation, on account of the fact that “incommunicable” is placed in the definition of person. Others, however, said that it is a commonality of intention, because “individual” is placed in the definition of person; as if one were to say that being a species is common to a horse and an ox. But both of these are excluded by the fact that this name “person” is neither a name of negation nor of intention, but is a name of a thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","text":"And therefore it must be said that, even in human things, this name “person” is common by a commonality of reason, not as a genus or species, but as a vague individual. For names of genera or species, such as “man” or “animal,” are imposed to signify the common natures themselves, but not the intentions of common natures, which are signified by these names “genus” or “species.” But a vague individual, such as “some man,” signifies the common nature with a determinate mode of existing that belongs to singulars, namely, that it is subsisting through itself and distinct from others. But in the name of a designated singular, the determinate distinguishing feature is signified, as in the name Socrates, this flesh and this bone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","text":"There is, however, this difference: “some man” signifies nature, or an individual on the part of nature, with the mode of existing that belongs to singulars; but this name “person” is not imposed to signify an individual on the part of nature, but to signify a subsisting thing in such a nature. Now this is common according to reason to all the divine persons: that each of them subsists in the divine nature as distinct from the others. And thus this name “person,” according to reason, is common to the three divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0084","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0085","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that argument proceeds from a commonality of the thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0086","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0087","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although a person is incommunicable, nevertheless the very mode of existing incommunicably can be common to several.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0088","text":"[I q. 30 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although there is a commonality of reason and not of the thing, it nevertheless does not follow that in divine things there is universal and particular, or genus and species. First, because not even in human things is the commonality of person a commonality of genus or species. Second, because the divine persons have one being, whereas genus and species, and every universal, are predicated of several things differing in being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089"]}],"source_digest":"9ee3a0fcede03267f92c0feea3f190ddf218ec26c910879f4cbc23b4545625d5","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 30"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 30","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23","title":"First Part · Question 31","order":32,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_23","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 31 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29730)\n\nPost haec considerandum est de his quae ad unitatem vel pluralitatem pertinent in divinis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de ipso nomine Trinitatis. Secundo, utrum possit dici, filius est alius a patre. Tertio, utrum dictio exclusiva, quae videtur alietatem excludere, possit adiungi nomini essentiali in divinis. Quarto, utrum possit adiungi termino personali.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29731)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit Trinitas in divinis. Omne enim nomen in divinis vel significat substantiam, vel relationem. Sed hoc nomen Trinitas non significat substantiam, praedicaretur enim de singulis personis. Neque significat relationem, quia non dicitur secundum nomen ad aliud. Ergo nomine Trinitatis non est utendum in divinis.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29732)\n\nPraeterea, hoc nomen Trinitas videtur esse nomen collectivum, cum significet multitudinem. Tale autem nomen non convenit in divinis, cum unitas importata per nomen collectivum sit minima unitas, in divinis autem est maxima unitas. Ergo hoc nomen Trinitas non convenit in divinis.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29733)\n\nPraeterea, omne trinum est triplex. Sed in Deo non est triplicitas, cum triplicitas sit species inaequalitatis. Ergo nec Trinitas.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29734)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid est in Deo, est in unitate essentiae divinae, quia Deus est sua essentia. Si igitur Trinitas est in Deo, erit in unitate essentiae divinae. Et sic in Deo erunt tres essentiales unitates, quod est haereticum.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29735)\n\nPraeterea, in omnibus quae dicuntur de Deo, concretum praedicatur de abstracto, deitas enim est Deus, et paternitas est pater. Sed Trinitas non potest dici trina, quia sic essent novem res in divinis, quod est erroneum. Ergo nomine Trinitatis non est utendum in divinis.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29736)\n\nSed contra est quod Athanasius dicit, quod *unitas in Trinitate, et Trinitas in unitate veneranda sit*.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29737)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nomen Trinitatis in divinis significat determinatum numerum personarum. Sicut igitur ponitur pluralitas personarum in divinis, ita utendum est nomine Trinitatis, quia hoc idem quod significat pluralitas indeterminate, significat hoc nomen Trinitas determinate.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29738)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc nomen Trinitas, secundum etymologiam vocabuli, videtur significare unam essentiam trium personarum, secundum quod dicitur Trinitas quasi trium unitas. Sed secundum proprietatem vocabuli, significat magis numerum personarum unius essentiae. Et propter hoc non possumus dicere quod pater sit Trinitas, quia non est tres personae. Non autem significat ipsas relationes personarum, sed magis numerum personarum ad invicem relatarum. Et inde est quod, secundum nomen, ad aliud non refertur.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29739)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nomen collectivum duo importat, scilicet pluralitatem suppositorum, et unitatem quandam, scilicet ordinis alicuius, populus enim est multitudo hominum sub aliquo ordine comprehensorum. Quantum ergo ad primum, hoc nomen Trinitas convenit cum nominibus collectivis, sed quantum ad secundum differt, quia in divina Trinitate non solum est unitas ordinis, sed cum hoc est etiam unitas essentiae.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29740)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Trinitas absolute dicitur, significat enim numerum ternarium personarum. Sed triplicitas significat proportionem inaequalitatis, est enim species proportionis inaequalis, sicut patet per Boetium in arithmetica. Et ideo non est in Deo triplicitas, sed Trinitas.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29741)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in Trinitate divina intelligitur et numerus, et personae numeratae. Cum ergo dicimus Trinitatem in unitate, non ponimus numerum in unitate essentiae, quasi sit ter una, sed personas numeratas ponimus in unitate naturae, sicut supposita alicuius naturae dicuntur esse in natura illa. E converso autem dicimus unitatem in Trinitate, sicut natura dicitur esse in suis suppositis.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29742)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, cum dicitur, Trinitas est trina, ratione numeri importati significatur multiplicatio eiusdem numeri in seipsum, cum hoc quod dico trinum, importet distinctionem in suppositis illius de quo dicitur. Et ideo non potest dici quod Trinitas sit trina, quia sequeretur, si Trinitas esset trina, quod tria essent supposita Trinitatis; sicut cum dicitur, Deus est trinus, sequitur quod sunt tria supposita deitatis.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29743)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filius non sit alius a patre. Alius enim est relativum diversitatis substantiae. Si igitur filius est alius a patre, videtur quod sit a patre diversus. Quod est contra Augustinum, VII de Trin., ubi dicit quod, cum dicimus tres personas, non diversitatem intelligere volumus.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29744)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque sunt alii ab invicem, aliquo modo ab invicem differunt. Si igitur filius est alius a patre, sequitur quod sit differens a patre. Quod est contra Ambrosium, in I de fide, ubi ait, *pater et filius deitate unum sunt, nec est ibi substantiae differentia, neque ulla diversitas*.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29745)\n\nPraeterea, ab alio alienum dicitur. Sed filius non est alienus a patre, dicit enim Hilarius, in VII de Trin., quod in divinis personis nihil est diversum, nihil alienum, nihil separabile. Ergo filius non est alius a patre.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29746)\n\nPraeterea, alius et aliud idem significant, sed sola generis consignificatione differunt. Si ergo filius est alius a patre, videtur sequi quod filius sit aliud a patre.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29747)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *una est enim essentia patris et filii et spiritus sancti, in qua non est aliud pater, aliud filius, aliud spiritus sanctus; quamvis personaliter sit alius pater, alius filius, alius spiritus sanctus*.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29748)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, quia ex verbis inordinate prolatis incurritur haeresis, ut Hieronymus dicit, ideo cum de Trinitate loquimur, cum cautela et modestia est agendum, quia, ut Augustinus dicit, in I de Trin., *nec periculosius alicubi erratur, nec laboriosius aliquid quaeritur, nec fructuosius aliquid invenitur*. Oportet autem in his quae de Trinitate loquimur, duos errores oppositos cavere, temperate inter utrumque procedentes, scilicet errorem Arii, qui posuit cum Trinitate personarum Trinitatem substantiarum; et errorem Sabellii, qui posuit cum unitate essentiae unitatem personae. Ad evitandum igitur errorem Arii, vitare debemus in divinis nomen diversitatis et differentiae, ne tollatur unitas essentiae, possumus autem uti nomine distinctionis, propter oppositionem relativam. Unde sicubi in aliqua Scriptura authentica diversitas vel differentia personarum invenitur, sumitur diversitas vel differentia pro distinctione. Ne autem tollatur simplicitas divinae essentiae, vitandum est nomen separationis et divisionis, quae est totius in partes. Ne autem tollatur aequalitas, vitandum est nomen disparitatis. Ne vero tollatur similitudo, vitandum est nomen alieni et discrepantis, dicit enim Ambrosius, in libro de fide, quod in patre et filio non est discrepans, sed una divinitas, et secundum Hilarium, ut dictum est, in divinis nihil est alienum, nihil separabile. Ad vitandum vero errorem Sabellii, vitare debemus singularitatem, ne tollatur communicabilitas essentiae divinae, unde Hilarius dicit, VII de Trin., *patrem et filium singularem Deum praedicare, sacrilegum est*. Debemus etiam vitare nomen unici, ne tollatur numerus personarum, unde Hilarius in eodem libro dicit quod a Deo excluditur singularis atque unici intelligentia. Dicimus tamen unicum filium, quia non sunt plures filii in divinis. Neque tamen dicimus unicum Deum, quia pluribus deitas est communis vitamus etiam nomen confusi, ne tollatur ordo naturae a personis, unde Ambrosius dicit, I de fide, *neque confusum est quod unum est, neque multiplex esse potest quod indifferens est*. Vitandum est etiam nomen solitarii, ne tollatur consortium trium personarum, dicit enim Hilarius, in IV de Trin., *nobis neque solitarius, neque diversus Deus est confitendus*. Hoc autem nomen alius, masculine sumptum, non importat nisi distinctionem suppositi. Unde convenienter dicere possumus quod filius est alius a patre, quia scilicet est aliud suppositum divinae naturae, sicut est alia persona, et alia hypostasis.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29749)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod alius, quia est sicut quoddam particulare nomen, tenet se ex parte suppositi, unde ad eius rationem sufficit distinctio substantiae quae est hypostasis vel persona. Sed diversitas requirit distinctionem substantiae quae est essentia. Et ideo non possumus dicere quod filius sit diversus a patre, licet sit alius.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29750)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod differentia importat distinctionem formae. Est autem tantum una forma in divinis, ut patet per id quod dicitur Philip. II, *qui cum in forma Dei esset*. Et ideo nomen differentis non proprie competit in divinis, ut patet per auctoritatem inductam. Utitur tamen Damascenus nomine differentiae in divinis personis, secundum quod proprietas relativa significatur per modum formae, unde dicit quod non differunt ab invicem hypostases secundum substantiam, sed secundum determinatas proprietates. Sed differentia sumitur pro distinctione, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29751)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod alienum est quod est extraneum et dissimile. Sed hoc non importatur cum dicitur alius. Et ideo dicimus filium alium a patre, licet non dicamus alienum.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29752)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod neutrum genus est informe, masculinum autem est formatum et distinctum, et similiter femininum. Et ideo convenienter per neutrum genus significatur essentia communis, per masculinum autem et femininum, aliquod suppositum determinatum in communi natura. Unde etiam in rebus humanis, si quaeratur, quis est iste? Respondetur, Socrates, quod nomen est suppositi, si autem quaeratur, quid est iste? Respondetur, animal rationale et mortale. Et ideo, quia in divinis distinctio est secundum personas, non autem secundum essentiam, dicimus quod pater est alius a filio, sed non aliud, et e converso dicimus quod sunt unum, sed non unus.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29753)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod dictio exclusiva solus non sit addenda termino essentiali in divinis. Quia secundum philosophum, in II Elench., *solus est qui cum alio non est*. Sed Deus est cum Angelis et sanctis animabus. Ergo non possumus dicere Deum solum.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29754)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid adiungitur termino essentiali in divinis, potest praedicari de qualibet persona per se, et de omnibus simul, quia enim convenienter dicitur sapiens Deus, possumus dicere, pater est sapiens Deus, et Trinitas est sapiens Deus. Sed Augustinus, in VI de Trin., dicit, *consideranda est illa sententia, qua dicitur non esse patrem verum Deum solum*. Ergo non potest dici solus Deus.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29755)\n\nPraeterea, si haec dictio solus adiungitur termino essentiali, aut hoc erit respectu praedicati personalis, aut respectu praedicati essentialis. Sed non respectu praedicati personalis, quia haec est falsa, solus Deus est pater, cum etiam homo sit pater. Neque etiam respectu praedicati essentialis. Quia si haec esset vera, solus Deus creat, videtur sequi quod haec esset vera, solus pater creat, quia quidquid dicitur de Deo, potest dici de patre. Haec autem est falsa, quia etiam filius est creator. Non ergo haec dictio solus potest in divinis adiungi termino essentiali.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29756)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur I ad Tim. I, *regi saeculorum immortali, invisibili, soli Deo*.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29757)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod haec dictio solus potest accipi ut categorematica vel syncategorematica. Dicitur autem dictio categorematica, quae absolute ponit rem significatam circa aliquod suppositum; ut albus circa hominem, cum dicitur homo albus. Si ergo sic accipiatur haec dictio solus, nullo modo potest adiungi alicui termino in divinis, quia poneret solitudinem circa terminum cui adiungeretur, et sic sequeretur Deum esse solitarium; quod est contra praedicta. Dictio vero syncategorematica dicitur, quae importat ordinem praedicati ad subiectum, sicut haec dictio omnis, vel nullus. Et similiter haec dictio solus, quia excludit omne aliud suppositum a consortio praedicati. Sicut, cum dicitur, solus Socrates scribit, non datur intelligi quod Socrates sit solitarius; sed quod nullus sit ei consors in scribendo, quamvis cum eo multis existentibus. Et per hunc modum nihil prohibet hanc dictionem solus adiungere alicui essentiali termino in divinis, inquantum excluduntur omnia alia a Deo a consortio praedicati, ut si dicamus, solus Deus est aeternus, quia nihil praeter Deum est aeternum.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29758)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet Angeli et animae sanctae semper sint cum Deo, tamen, si non esset pluralitas personarum in divinis, sequeretur, quod Deus esset solus vel solitarius. Non enim tollitur solitudo per associationem alicuius quod est extraneae naturae, dicitur enim aliquis solus esse in horto, quamvis sint ibi multae plantae et animalia. Et similiter diceretur Deus esse solus vel solitarius, Angelis et hominibus cum eo existentibus, si non essent in divinis personae plures. Consociatio igitur Angelorum et animarum non excludit solitudinem absolutam a divinis, et multo minus solitudinem respectivam, per comparationem ad aliquod praedicatum.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29759)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod haec dictio solus, proprie loquendo, non ponitur ex parte praedicati, quod sumitur formaliter, respicit enim suppositum, inquantum excludit aliud suppositum ab eo cui adiungitur. Sed hoc adverbium tantum, cum sit exclusivum, potest poni ex parte subiecti, et ex parte praedicati, possumus enim dicere, tantum Socrates currit, idest nullus alius; et, Socrates currit tantum, idest nihil aliud facit. Unde non proprie dici potest, pater est solus Deus, vel, Trinitas est solus Deus, nisi forte ex parte praedicati intelligatur aliqua implicatio, ut dicatur, Trinitas est Deus qui est solus Deus. Et secundum hoc etiam posset esse vera ista, pater est Deus qui est solus Deus, si relativum referret praedicatum, et non suppositum. Augustinus autem, cum dicit patrem non esse solum Deum, sed Trinitatem esse solum Deum, loquitur expositive, ac si diceret, cum dicitur, regi saeculorum, invisibili, soli Deo, non est exponendum de persona patris, sed de sola Trinitate.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29760)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod utroque modo potest haec dictio solus adiungi termino essentiali. Haec enim propositio, solus Deus est pater, est duplex. Quia ly pater potest praedicare personam patris, et sic est vera, non enim homo est illa persona. Vel potest praedicare relationem tantum, et sic est falsa, quia relatio paternitatis etiam in aliis invenitur, licet non univoce. Similiter haec est vera, solus Deus creat. Nec tamen sequitur, ergo solus pater, quia, ut sophistae dicunt, dictio exclusiva immobilitat terminum cui adiungitur, ut non possit fieri sub eo descensus pro aliquo suppositorum; non enim sequitur, solus homo est animal rationale mortale, ergo solus Socrates.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29761)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod dictio exclusiva possit adiungi termino personali, etiam si praedicatum sit commune. Dicit enim dominus, ad patrem loquens, Ioan. XVII, *ut cognoscant te, solum Deum verum*. Ergo solus pater est Deus verus.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29762)\n\nPraeterea, Matth. XI dicitur, nemo novit filium nisi pater; quod idem significat ac si diceretur, solus pater novit filium. Sed nosse filium est commune. Ergo idem quod prius.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29763)\n\nPraeterea, dictio exclusiva non excludit illud quod est de intellectu termini cui adiungitur, unde non excludit partem, neque universale, non enim sequitur, solus Socrates est albus, ergo manus eius non est alba; vel, ergo homo non est albus. Sed una persona est in intellectu alterius, sicut pater in intellectu filii, et e converso. Non ergo per hoc quod dicitur, solus pater est Deus, excluditur filius vel spiritus sanctus. Et sic videtur haec locutio esse vera.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29764)\n\nPraeterea, ab Ecclesia cantatur, *tu solus altissimus, Iesu Christe*.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29765)\n\nSed contra, haec locutio, solus pater est Deus, habet duas expositivas, scilicet, pater est Deus, et, nullus alius a patre est Deus. Sed haec secunda est falsa, quia filius alius est a patre, qui est Deus. Ergo et haec est falsa, solus pater est Deus. Et sic de similibus.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29766)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum dicimus, solus pater est Deus, haec propositio potest habere multiplicem intellectum. Si enim solus ponat solitudinem circa patrem, sic est falsa, secundum quod sumitur categorematice. Secundum vero quod sumitur syncategorematice, sic iterum potest intelligi multipliciter. Quia si excludat a forma subiecti, sic est vera, ut sit sensus, solus pater est Deus, idest, ille cum quo nullus alius est pater, est Deus. Et hoc modo exponit Augustinus, in VI de Trin., cum dicit, *solum patrem dicimus, non quia separatur a filio vel spiritu sancto; sed hoc dicentes, significamus quod illi simul cum eo non sunt pater*. Sed hic sensus non habetur ex consueto modo loquendi, nisi intellecta aliqua implicatione, ut si dicatur, ille qui solus dicitur pater, est Deus. Secundum vero proprium sensum, excludit a consortio praedicati. Et sic haec propositio est falsa, si excludit alium masculine, est autem vera, si excludit aliud neutraliter tantum, quia filius est alius a patre, non tamen aliud; similiter et spiritus sanctus. Sed quia haec dictio solus respicit proprie subiectum, ut dictum est, magis se habet ad excludendum alium quam aliud. Unde non est extendenda talis locutio; sed pie exponenda, sicubi inveniatur in authentica Scriptura.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29767)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicimus, te solum Deum verum, non intelligitur de persona patris, sed de tota Trinitate, ut Augustinus exponit. Vel, si intelligatur de persona patris, non excluduntur aliae personae, propter essentiae unitatem, prout ly solus excludit tantum aliud, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29768)\n\nEt similiter dicendum est ad secundum. Cum enim aliquid essentiale dicitur de patre, non excluditur filius vel spiritus sanctus, propter essentiae unitatem. Tamen sciendum est quod in auctoritate praedicta, haec dictio nemo non idem est quod nullus homo, quod videtur significare vocabulum (non enim posset excipi persona patris), sed sumitur, secundum usum loquendi, distributive pro quacumque rationali natura.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29769)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod dictio exclusiva non excludit illa quae sunt de intellectu termini cui adiungitur, si non differunt secundum suppositum, ut pars et universale. Sed filius differt supposito a patre, et ideo non est similis ratio.\n\n[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29770)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod non dicimus absolute quod solus filius sit altissimus, sed quod solus sit altissimus cum spiritu sancto, in gloria Dei patris.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0002","text":"[I q. 31 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0003","text":"Post haec considerandum est de his quae ad unitatem vel pluralitatem pertinent in divinis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de ipso nomine Trinitatis. Secundo, utrum possit dici, filius est alius a patre. Tertio, utrum dictio exclusiva, quae videtur alietatem excludere, possit adiungi nomini essentiali in divinis. Quarto, utrum possit adiungi termino personali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0005","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit Trinitas in divinis. Omne enim nomen in divinis vel significat substantiam, vel relationem. Sed hoc nomen Trinitas non significat substantiam, praedicaretur enim de singulis personis. Neque significat relationem, quia non dicitur secundum nomen ad aliud. Ergo nomine Trinitatis non est utendum in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0007","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0008","text":"Praeterea, hoc nomen Trinitas videtur esse nomen collectivum, cum significet multitudinem. Tale autem nomen non convenit in divinis, cum unitas importata per nomen collectivum sit minima unitas, in divinis autem est maxima unitas. Ergo hoc nomen Trinitas non convenit in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0009","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0010","text":"Praeterea, omne trinum est triplex. Sed in Deo non est triplicitas, cum triplicitas sit species inaequalitatis. Ergo nec Trinitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0011","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0012","text":"Praeterea, quidquid est in Deo, est in unitate essentiae divinae, quia Deus est sua essentia. Si igitur Trinitas est in Deo, erit in unitate essentiae divinae. Et sic in Deo erunt tres essentiales unitates, quod est haereticum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0013","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0014","text":"Praeterea, in omnibus quae dicuntur de Deo, concretum praedicatur de abstracto, deitas enim est Deus, et paternitas est pater. Sed Trinitas non potest dici trina, quia sic essent novem res in divinis, quod est erroneum. Ergo nomine Trinitatis non est utendum in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0015","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod Athanasius dicit, quod *unitas in Trinitate, et Trinitas in unitate veneranda sit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0017","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nomen Trinitatis in divinis significat determinatum numerum personarum. Sicut igitur ponitur pluralitas personarum in divinis, ita utendum est nomine Trinitatis, quia hoc idem quod significat pluralitas indeterminate, significat hoc nomen Trinitas determinate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0019","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc nomen Trinitas, secundum etymologiam vocabuli, videtur significare unam essentiam trium personarum, secundum quod dicitur Trinitas quasi trium unitas. Sed secundum proprietatem vocabuli, significat magis numerum personarum unius essentiae. Et propter hoc non possumus dicere quod pater sit Trinitas, quia non est tres personae. Non autem significat ipsas relationes personarum, sed magis numerum personarum ad invicem relatarum. Et inde est quod, secundum nomen, ad aliud non refertur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0021","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0022","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nomen collectivum duo importat, scilicet pluralitatem suppositorum, et unitatem quandam, scilicet ordinis alicuius, populus enim est multitudo hominum sub aliquo ordine comprehensorum. Quantum ergo ad primum, hoc nomen Trinitas convenit cum nominibus collectivis, sed quantum ad secundum differt, quia in divina Trinitate non solum est unitas ordinis, sed cum hoc est etiam unitas essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0023","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Trinitas absolute dicitur, significat enim numerum ternarium personarum. Sed triplicitas significat proportionem inaequalitatis, est enim species proportionis inaequalis, sicut patet per Boetium in arithmetica. Et ideo non est in Deo triplicitas, sed Trinitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0025","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in Trinitate divina intelligitur et numerus, et personae numeratae. Cum ergo dicimus Trinitatem in unitate, non ponimus numerum in unitate essentiae, quasi sit ter una, sed personas numeratas ponimus in unitate naturae, sicut supposita alicuius naturae dicuntur esse in natura illa. E converso autem dicimus unitatem in Trinitate, sicut natura dicitur esse in suis suppositis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0027","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0028","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, cum dicitur, Trinitas est trina, ratione numeri importati significatur multiplicatio eiusdem numeri in seipsum, cum hoc quod dico trinum, importet distinctionem in suppositis illius de quo dicitur. Et ideo non potest dici quod Trinitas sit trina, quia sequeretur, si Trinitas esset trina, quod tria essent supposita Trinitatis; sicut cum dicitur, Deus est trinus, sequitur quod sunt tria supposita deitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0029","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0030","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0031","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filius non sit alius a patre. Alius enim est relativum diversitatis substantiae. Si igitur filius est alius a patre, videtur quod sit a patre diversus. Quod est contra Augustinum, VII de Trin., ubi dicit quod, cum dicimus tres personas, non diversitatem intelligere volumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0032","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0033","text":"Praeterea, quicumque sunt alii ab invicem, aliquo modo ab invicem differunt. Si igitur filius est alius a patre, sequitur quod sit differens a patre. Quod est contra Ambrosium, in I de fide, ubi ait, *pater et filius deitate unum sunt, nec est ibi substantiae differentia, neque ulla diversitas*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0034","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0035","text":"Praeterea, ab alio alienum dicitur. Sed filius non est alienus a patre, dicit enim Hilarius, in VII de Trin., quod in divinis personis nihil est diversum, nihil alienum, nihil separabile. Ergo filius non est alius a patre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0036","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0037","text":"Praeterea, alius et aliud idem significant, sed sola generis consignificatione differunt. Si ergo filius est alius a patre, videtur sequi quod filius sit aliud a patre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0038","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0039","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *una est enim essentia patris et filii et spiritus sancti, in qua non est aliud pater, aliud filius, aliud spiritus sanctus; quamvis personaliter sit alius pater, alius filius, alius spiritus sanctus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0040","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, quia ex verbis inordinate prolatis incurritur haeresis, ut Hieronymus dicit, ideo cum de Trinitate loquimur, cum cautela et modestia est agendum, quia, ut Augustinus dicit, in I de Trin., *nec periculosius alicubi erratur, nec laboriosius aliquid quaeritur, nec fructuosius aliquid invenitur*. Oportet autem in his quae de Trinitate loquimur, duos errores oppositos cavere, temperate inter utrumque procedentes, scilicet errorem Arii, qui posuit cum Trinitate personarum Trinitatem substantiarum; et errorem Sabellii, qui posuit cum unitate essentiae unitatem personae. Ad evitandum igitur errorem Arii, vitare debemus in divinis nomen diversitatis et differentiae, ne tollatur unitas essentiae, possumus autem uti nomine distinctionis, propter oppositionem relativam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","text":"Unde sicubi in aliqua Scriptura authentica diversitas vel differentia personarum invenitur, sumitur diversitas vel differentia pro distinctione. Ne autem tollatur simplicitas divinae essentiae, vitandum est nomen separationis et divisionis, quae est totius in partes. Ne autem tollatur aequalitas, vitandum est nomen disparitatis. Ne vero tollatur similitudo, vitandum est nomen alieni et discrepantis, dicit enim Ambrosius, in libro de fide, quod in patre et filio non est discrepans, sed una divinitas, et secundum Hilarium, ut dictum est, in divinis nihil est alienum, nihil separabile. Ad vitandum vero errorem Sabellii, vitare debemus singularitatem, ne tollatur communicabilitas essentiae divinae, unde Hilarius dicit, VII de Trin., *patrem et filium singularem Deum praedicare, sacrilegum est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043","text":"Debemus etiam vitare nomen unici, ne tollatur numerus personarum, unde Hilarius in eodem libro dicit quod a Deo excluditur singularis atque unici intelligentia. Dicimus tamen unicum filium, quia non sunt plures filii in divinis. Neque tamen dicimus unicum Deum, quia pluribus deitas est communis vitamus etiam nomen confusi, ne tollatur ordo naturae a personis, unde Ambrosius dicit, I de fide, *neque confusum est quod unum est, neque multiplex esse potest quod indifferens est*. Vitandum est etiam nomen solitarii, ne tollatur consortium trium personarum, dicit enim Hilarius, in IV de Trin., *nobis neque solitarius, neque diversus Deus est confitendus*. Hoc autem nomen alius, masculine sumptum, non importat nisi distinctionem suppositi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","text":"Unde convenienter dicere possumus quod filius est alius a patre, quia scilicet est aliud suppositum divinae naturae, sicut est alia persona, et alia hypostasis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0045","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod alius, quia est sicut quoddam particulare nomen, tenet se ex parte suppositi, unde ad eius rationem sufficit distinctio substantiae quae est hypostasis vel persona. Sed diversitas requirit distinctionem substantiae quae est essentia. Et ideo non possumus dicere quod filius sit diversus a patre, licet sit alius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0047","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0048","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod differentia importat distinctionem formae. Est autem tantum una forma in divinis, ut patet per id quod dicitur Philip. II, *qui cum in forma Dei esset*. Et ideo nomen differentis non proprie competit in divinis, ut patet per auctoritatem inductam. Utitur tamen Damascenus nomine differentiae in divinis personis, secundum quod proprietas relativa significatur per modum formae, unde dicit quod non differunt ab invicem hypostases secundum substantiam, sed secundum determinatas proprietates. Sed differentia sumitur pro distinctione, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0049","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0050","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod alienum est quod est extraneum et dissimile. Sed hoc non importatur cum dicitur alius. Et ideo dicimus filium alium a patre, licet non dicamus alienum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0051","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod neutrum genus est informe, masculinum autem est formatum et distinctum, et similiter femininum. Et ideo convenienter per neutrum genus significatur essentia communis, per masculinum autem et femininum, aliquod suppositum determinatum in communi natura. Unde etiam in rebus humanis, si quaeratur, quis est iste? Respondetur, Socrates, quod nomen est suppositi, si autem quaeratur, quid est iste? Respondetur, animal rationale et mortale. Et ideo, quia in divinis distinctio est secundum personas, non autem secundum essentiam, dicimus quod pater est alius a filio, sed non aliud, et e converso dicimus quod sunt unum, sed non unus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0053","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0054","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0055","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod dictio exclusiva solus non sit addenda termino essentiali in divinis. Quia secundum philosophum, in II Elench., *solus est qui cum alio non est*. Sed Deus est cum Angelis et sanctis animabus. Ergo non possumus dicere Deum solum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0056","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0057","text":"Praeterea, quidquid adiungitur termino essentiali in divinis, potest praedicari de qualibet persona per se, et de omnibus simul, quia enim convenienter dicitur sapiens Deus, possumus dicere, pater est sapiens Deus, et Trinitas est sapiens Deus. Sed Augustinus, in VI de Trin., dicit, *consideranda est illa sententia, qua dicitur non esse patrem verum Deum solum*. Ergo non potest dici solus Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0058","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0059","text":"Praeterea, si haec dictio solus adiungitur termino essentiali, aut hoc erit respectu praedicati personalis, aut respectu praedicati essentialis. Sed non respectu praedicati personalis, quia haec est falsa, solus Deus est pater, cum etiam homo sit pater. Neque etiam respectu praedicati essentialis. Quia si haec esset vera, solus Deus creat, videtur sequi quod haec esset vera, solus pater creat, quia quidquid dicitur de Deo, potest dici de patre. Haec autem est falsa, quia etiam filius est creator. Non ergo haec dictio solus potest in divinis adiungi termino essentiali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0060","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0061","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur I ad Tim. I, *regi saeculorum immortali, invisibili, soli Deo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0062","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod haec dictio solus potest accipi ut categorematica vel syncategorematica. Dicitur autem dictio categorematica, quae absolute ponit rem significatam circa aliquod suppositum; ut albus circa hominem, cum dicitur homo albus. Si ergo sic accipiatur haec dictio solus, nullo modo potest adiungi alicui termino in divinis, quia poneret solitudinem circa terminum cui adiungeretur, et sic sequeretur Deum esse solitarium; quod est contra praedicta. Dictio vero syncategorematica dicitur, quae importat ordinem praedicati ad subiectum, sicut haec dictio omnis, vel nullus. Et similiter haec dictio solus, quia excludit omne aliud suppositum a consortio praedicati. Sicut, cum dicitur, solus Socrates scribit, non datur intelligi quod Socrates sit solitarius; sed quod nullus sit ei consors in scribendo, quamvis cum eo multis existentibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064","text":"Et per hunc modum nihil prohibet hanc dictionem solus adiungere alicui essentiali termino in divinis, inquantum excluduntur omnia alia a Deo a consortio praedicati, ut si dicamus, solus Deus est aeternus, quia nihil praeter Deum est aeternum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0065","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0066","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet Angeli et animae sanctae semper sint cum Deo, tamen, si non esset pluralitas personarum in divinis, sequeretur, quod Deus esset solus vel solitarius. Non enim tollitur solitudo per associationem alicuius quod est extraneae naturae, dicitur enim aliquis solus esse in horto, quamvis sint ibi multae plantae et animalia. Et similiter diceretur Deus esse solus vel solitarius, Angelis et hominibus cum eo existentibus, si non essent in divinis personae plures. Consociatio igitur Angelorum et animarum non excludit solitudinem absolutam a divinis, et multo minus solitudinem respectivam, per comparationem ad aliquod praedicatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0067","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod haec dictio solus, proprie loquendo, non ponitur ex parte praedicati, quod sumitur formaliter, respicit enim suppositum, inquantum excludit aliud suppositum ab eo cui adiungitur. Sed hoc adverbium tantum, cum sit exclusivum, potest poni ex parte subiecti, et ex parte praedicati, possumus enim dicere, tantum Socrates currit, idest nullus alius; et, Socrates currit tantum, idest nihil aliud facit. Unde non proprie dici potest, pater est solus Deus, vel, Trinitas est solus Deus, nisi forte ex parte praedicati intelligatur aliqua implicatio, ut dicatur, Trinitas est Deus qui est solus Deus. Et secundum hoc etiam posset esse vera ista, pater est Deus qui est solus Deus, si relativum referret praedicatum, et non suppositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0069","text":"Augustinus autem, cum dicit patrem non esse solum Deum, sed Trinitatem esse solum Deum, loquitur expositive, ac si diceret, cum dicitur, regi saeculorum, invisibili, soli Deo, non est exponendum de persona patris, sed de sola Trinitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0070","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod utroque modo potest haec dictio solus adiungi termino essentiali. Haec enim propositio, solus Deus est pater, est duplex. Quia ly pater potest praedicare personam patris, et sic est vera, non enim homo est illa persona. Vel potest praedicare relationem tantum, et sic est falsa, quia relatio paternitatis etiam in aliis invenitur, licet non univoce. Similiter haec est vera, solus Deus creat. Nec tamen sequitur, ergo solus pater, quia, ut sophistae dicunt, dictio exclusiva immobilitat terminum cui adiungitur, ut non possit fieri sub eo descensus pro aliquo suppositorum; non enim sequitur, solus homo est animal rationale mortale, ergo solus Socrates.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0072","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0073","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0074","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod dictio exclusiva possit adiungi termino personali, etiam si praedicatum sit commune. Dicit enim dominus, ad patrem loquens, Ioan. XVII, *ut cognoscant te, solum Deum verum*. Ergo solus pater est Deus verus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0075","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0076","text":"Praeterea, Matth. XI dicitur, nemo novit filium nisi pater; quod idem significat ac si diceretur, solus pater novit filium. Sed nosse filium est commune. Ergo idem quod prius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0077","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0078","text":"Praeterea, dictio exclusiva non excludit illud quod est de intellectu termini cui adiungitur, unde non excludit partem, neque universale, non enim sequitur, solus Socrates est albus, ergo manus eius non est alba; vel, ergo homo non est albus. Sed una persona est in intellectu alterius, sicut pater in intellectu filii, et e converso. Non ergo per hoc quod dicitur, solus pater est Deus, excluditur filius vel spiritus sanctus. Et sic videtur haec locutio esse vera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0079","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0080","text":"Praeterea, ab Ecclesia cantatur, *tu solus altissimus, Iesu Christe*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0081","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0082","text":"Sed contra, haec locutio, solus pater est Deus, habet duas expositivas, scilicet, pater est Deus, et, nullus alius a patre est Deus. Sed haec secunda est falsa, quia filius alius est a patre, qui est Deus. Ergo et haec est falsa, solus pater est Deus. Et sic de similibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0083","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum dicimus, solus pater est Deus, haec propositio potest habere multiplicem intellectum. Si enim solus ponat solitudinem circa patrem, sic est falsa, secundum quod sumitur categorematice. Secundum vero quod sumitur syncategorematice, sic iterum potest intelligi multipliciter. Quia si excludat a forma subiecti, sic est vera, ut sit sensus, solus pater est Deus, idest, ille cum quo nullus alius est pater, est Deus. Et hoc modo exponit Augustinus, in VI de Trin., cum dicit, *solum patrem dicimus, non quia separatur a filio vel spiritu sancto; sed hoc dicentes, significamus quod illi simul cum eo non sunt pater*. Sed hic sensus non habetur ex consueto modo loquendi, nisi intellecta aliqua implicatione, ut si dicatur, ille qui solus dicitur pater, est Deus. Secundum vero proprium sensum, excludit a consortio praedicati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","text":"Et sic haec propositio est falsa, si excludit alium masculine, est autem vera, si excludit aliud neutraliter tantum, quia filius est alius a patre, non tamen aliud; similiter et spiritus sanctus. Sed quia haec dictio solus respicit proprie subiectum, ut dictum est, magis se habet ad excludendum alium quam aliud. Unde non est extendenda talis locutio; sed pie exponenda, sicubi inveniatur in authentica Scriptura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0086","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0087","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicimus, te solum Deum verum, non intelligitur de persona patris, sed de tota Trinitate, ut Augustinus exponit. Vel, si intelligatur de persona patris, non excluduntur aliae personae, propter essentiae unitatem, prout ly solus excludit tantum aliud, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0088","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0089","text":"Et similiter dicendum est ad secundum. Cum enim aliquid essentiale dicitur de patre, non excluditur filius vel spiritus sanctus, propter essentiae unitatem. Tamen sciendum est quod in auctoritate praedicta, haec dictio nemo non idem est quod nullus homo, quod videtur significare vocabulum (non enim posset excipi persona patris), sed sumitur, secundum usum loquendi, distributive pro quacumque rationali natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0090","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0091","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod dictio exclusiva non excludit illa quae sunt de intellectu termini cui adiungitur, si non differunt secundum suppositum, ut pars et universale. Sed filius differt supposito a patre, et ideo non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0092","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0093","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod non dicimus absolute quod solus filius sit altissimus, sed quod solus sit altissimus cum spiritu sancto, in gloria Dei patris.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a3878557feb7e0c845c01b8eb7f521d701f449ced202eaf79955a46ad7d87fe8","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141301803487Z_af7870ed","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 31","source_sha256":"a3878557feb7e0c845c01b8eb7f521d701f449ced202eaf79955a46ad7d87fe8","sha256":"fedcef6b0b43b7cd26a09ab142852e9545ed36eda044f0d027a7762d064c65dd","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:13:01.803507+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0002","text":"[I q. 31 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0003","text":"After these matters, we must consider those things that pertain to unity or plurality in the divine. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, concerning the very name of Trinity. Second, whether it can be said: the Son is other than the Father. Third, whether the exclusive expression, which seems to exclude otherness, can be joined to an essential name in the divine. Fourth, whether it can be joined to a personal term.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0005","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that there is no Trinity in the divine. For every name in the divine signifies either substance or relation. But this name, Trinity, does not signify substance, for it would then be predicated of the individual persons. Nor does it signify relation, because it is not said according to a name in relation to another. Therefore the name Trinity is not to be used in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0007","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0008","text":"Further, this name Trinity seems to be a collective name, since it signifies a multitude. But such a name is not fitting in the divine, since the unity conveyed by a collective name is the least unity, whereas in the divine there is the greatest unity. Therefore this name Trinity is not fitting in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0009","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0010","text":"Further, everything threefold is triple. But in God there is no triplicity, since triplicity is a species of inequality. Therefore neither is there Trinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0011","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0012","text":"Further, whatever is in God is in the unity of the divine essence, because God is his essence. If, therefore, Trinity is in God, it will be in the unity of the divine essence. And thus there will be three essential unities in God, which is heretical.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0013","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0014","text":"Further, in all things said of God, the concrete is predicated of the abstract: for deity is God, and paternity is Father. But Trinity cannot be said to be triune, because there would thus be nine things in the divine, which is erroneous. Therefore the name Trinity is not to be used in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0015","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0016","text":"But on the contrary, Athanasius says that *unity in Trinity, and Trinity in unity, is to be venerated*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0017","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","text":"I answer that the name Trinity in the divine signifies a determinate number of persons. Therefore, just as a plurality of persons is posited in the divine, so the name Trinity must be used, because what plurality signifies indeterminately, this name Trinity signifies determinately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0019","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that this name Trinity, according to the etymology of the word, seems to signify one essence of three persons, insofar as Trinity is said as though the unity of three. But according to the proper meaning of the word, it signifies rather the number of persons of one essence. And on this account we cannot say that the Father is Trinity, because he is not three persons. Yet it does not signify the relations of the persons themselves, but rather the number of persons related to one another. Hence it is that, according to its name, it is not referred to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0021","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0022","text":"To the second it must be said that a collective name conveys two things: namely, a plurality of supposita, and a certain unity, namely, that of some order; for a people is a multitude of men comprehended under some order. Therefore, as regards the first, this name Trinity agrees with collective names; but as regards the second, it differs, because in the divine Trinity there is not only unity of order, but along with this there is also unity of essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0023","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024","text":"To the third it must be said that Trinity is said absolutely, for it signifies the ternary number of persons. But triplicity signifies a proportion of inequality; for it is a species of unequal proportion, as is clear from Boethius in the Arithmetic. And therefore there is no triplicity in God, but Trinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0025","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that in the divine Trinity both number and numbered persons are understood. Therefore, when we say Trinity in unity, we do not posit number in the unity of the essence, as though it were three times one; rather, we posit the numbered persons in the unity of nature, just as the supposita of some nature are said to be in that nature. Conversely, however, we say unity in Trinity, just as nature is said to be in its supposita.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0027","text":"[I q. 31 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0028","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, when it is said, “Trinity is triple,” by reason of the number implied, a multiplication of the same number by itself is signified, since what I call “triple” implies a distinction in the supposita of that of which it is said. And therefore it cannot be said that the Trinity is triple, because it would follow, if the Trinity were triple, that there are three supposita of the Trinity; just as, when it is said, “God is triple,” it follows that there are three supposita of the Godhead.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0029","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0030","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0031","text":"We proceed thus to the second article. It seems that the Son is not other than the Father. For *other* is relative to diversity of substance. Therefore, if the Son is other than the Father, he seems to be diverse from the Father. This is contrary to Augustine, *On the Trinity*, VII, where he says that, when we say three persons, we do not mean to understand diversity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0032","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0033","text":"Further, whoever are other than one another differ from one another in some way. Therefore, if the Son is other than the Father, it follows that he differs from the Father. This is contrary to Ambrose, in *On the Faith*, I, where he says: *“The Father and the Son are one in Godhead, and there is neither difference of substance there nor any diversity.”*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0034","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0035","text":"Further, that which is from another is called alien. But the Son is not alien from the Father; for Hilary says, *On the Trinity*, VII, that in the divine persons there is nothing diverse, nothing alien, nothing separable. Therefore, the Son is not other than the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0036","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0037","text":"Further, *alius* and *aliud* signify the same thing, but differ only in the connotation of gender. Therefore, if the Son is other than the Father, it seems to follow that the Son is something other than the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0038","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0039","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says in the book *On Faith to Peter*: *“For the essence of the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit is one, in which the Father is not one thing, the Son another, and the Holy Spirit another; although personally the Father is other, the Son other, and the Holy Spirit other.”*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0040","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","text":"I answer that, because heresy is incurred through words uttered without order, as Jerome says, therefore, when we speak of the Trinity, we must proceed with caution and moderation; because, as Augustine says, *On the Trinity*, I, *“nowhere is error more dangerous, nor is anything sought more laboriously, nor found more fruitfully.”* Now in those things which we say concerning the Trinity, we must avoid two opposite errors, proceeding with moderation between them: namely, the error of Arius, who posited, together with a Trinity of persons, a trinity of substances; and the error of Sabellius, who posited, together with unity of essence, unity of person. Therefore, in order to avoid the error of Arius, we must avoid in divine matters the name of diversity and difference, lest the unity of essence be removed; but we may use the name distinction, on account of relative opposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","text":"Hence, wherever diversity or difference of persons is found in any authentic Scripture, diversity or difference is taken for distinction. But lest the simplicity of the divine essence be removed, the name of separation and division—which is of a whole into parts—must be avoided. And lest equality be removed, the name of disparity must be avoided. But lest likeness be removed, the names alien and discrepant must be avoided; for Ambrose says, in the book *On the Faith*, that in the Father and the Son there is nothing discrepant, but one Godhead; and according to Hilary, as has been said, in divine things there is nothing alien, nothing separable. But in order to avoid the error of Sabellius, we must avoid singularity, lest the communicability of the divine essence be removed; hence Hilary says, *On the Trinity*, VII: *“To preach the Father and the Son as a singular God is sacrilegious.”*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043","text":"We must also avoid the name only, lest the number of persons be removed; hence Hilary says in the same book that the understanding of singular and only is excluded from God. Yet we say only Son, because there are not several sons in divine things. Nor, however, do we say only God, because the Godhead is common to several. We also avoid the name confused, lest the order of nature be removed from the persons; hence Ambrose says, *On the Faith*, I: *“That which is one is not confused, nor can that which is undifferentiated be multiple.”* The name solitary must also be avoided, lest the communion of the three persons be removed; for Hilary says, *On the Trinity*, IV: *“For us, God is to be confessed neither as solitary nor as diverse.”* But this name *alius*, taken in the masculine, signifies nothing but distinction of suppositum.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","text":"Hence we can fittingly say that the Son is other than the Father, because he is another suppositum of the divine nature, just as he is another person and another hypostasis.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0045","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that *alius*, because it is like a certain particular name, pertains to the side of suppositum; hence, for its notion, the distinction of substance which is hypostasis or person suffices. But diversity requires distinction of substance which is essence. And therefore we cannot say that the Son is diverse from the Father, although he is other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0047","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0048","text":"To the second, it must be said that difference implies distinction of form. But there is only one form in divine things, as is clear from what is said in Phil. II: *“Who, being in the form of God.”* And therefore the name differing does not properly belong in divine things, as is clear from the authority cited. Yet Damascene uses the name difference with respect to the divine persons, insofar as a relative property is signified after the manner of a form; hence he says that the hypostases do not differ from one another according to substance, but according to determinate properties. But difference is taken for distinction, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0049","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0050","text":"To the third, it must be said that “alien” is what is extraneous and dissimilar. But this is not implied when “other” is said. And therefore we say that the Son is other than the Father, although we do not say that he is alien.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0051","text":"[I q. 31 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the neuter gender is unformed, whereas the masculine is formed and distinct, and likewise the feminine. And therefore the common essence is fittingly signified by the neuter gender, but some determinate supposit in the common nature by the masculine and feminine. Hence also in human things, if it is asked, “Who is this?” one replies, “Socrates,” which is the name of a supposit; but if it is asked, “What is this?” one replies, “A rational and mortal animal.” And therefore, because in divine things distinction is according to persons, but not according to essence, we say that the Father is other than the Son, but not another thing; and conversely, we say that they are one, but not one person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0053","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0054","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0055","text":"We proceed to the third as follows. It seems that the exclusive expression “alone” should not be added to an essential term in divine things. For according to the Philosopher, in *II Elench.*, *he is alone who is not with another*. But God is with the angels and holy souls. Therefore we cannot say that God is alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0056","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0057","text":"Further, whatever is joined to an essential term in divine things can be predicated, in itself, of any person and of all together; for since “God is wise” is fittingly said, we can say, “The Father is wise God,” and “The Trinity is wise God.” But Augustine, in *VI De Trin.*, says, *the statement must be considered in which it is said that the Father is not God alone*. Therefore “God alone” cannot be said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0058","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0059","text":"Further, if this expression “alone” is joined to an essential term, this will be either with respect to a personal predicate or with respect to an essential predicate. But not with respect to a personal predicate, because this is false: “God alone is Father,” since a man also is a father. Nor with respect to an essential predicate either. For if this were true, “God alone creates,” it seems to follow that this would be true: “The Father alone creates,” because whatever is said of God can be said of the Father. But this is false, because the Son also is Creator. Therefore this expression “alone” cannot in divine things be joined to an essential term.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0060","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0061","text":"On the contrary, it is said in I Tim. I, *to the King of the ages, immortal, invisible, to God alone*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0062","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","text":"I answer that this expression “alone” can be taken either categorematically or syncategorematically. Now a categorematic expression is said to be one that posits absolutely the thing signified concerning some supposit; as “white” concerning a man, when “a white man” is said. Therefore, if this expression “alone” is taken in this way, it can by no means be joined to any term in divine things, because it would posit solitude concerning the term to which it was joined, and thus it would follow that God is solitary; which is contrary to what has been said above. But a syncategorematic expression is said to be one that implies an ordering of the predicate to the subject, as do the expressions “every” and “none.” And likewise this expression “alone,” because it excludes every other supposit from participation in the predicate. Thus, when it is said, “Socrates alone writes,” it is not given to be understood that Socrates is solitary, but that no one shares with him in writing, although many exist with him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064","text":"And in this way nothing prevents this expression “alone” from being joined to some essential term in divine things, insofar as all others are excluded from sharing with God in the predicate; as if we say, “God alone is eternal,” because nothing besides God is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0065","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0066","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although angels and holy souls are always with God, nevertheless, if there were not a plurality of persons in divine things, it would follow that God was alone or solitary. For solitude is not removed by association with something of an extraneous nature; for someone is said to be alone in a garden, although there are many plants and animals there. And similarly, God would be said to be alone or solitary, even if angels and men existed with him, if there were not several persons in divine things. Therefore, the association of angels and souls does not exclude absolute solitude from divine things, and much less relative solitude, by comparison with some predicate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0067","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","text":"To the second, it must be said that this expression “alone,” properly speaking, is not placed on the side of the predicate, which is taken formally; for it regards the supposit, insofar as it excludes another supposit from that to which it is joined. But the adverb “only,” since it is exclusive, can be placed both on the side of the subject and on the side of the predicate; for we can say, “Only Socrates runs,” that is, no one else does; and, “Socrates only runs,” that is, he does nothing else. Hence it cannot properly be said, “The Father is God alone,” or, “The Trinity is God alone,” unless perhaps some implication is understood on the side of the predicate, so that it is said, “The Trinity is God who is God alone.” And accordingly this too could be true: “The Father is God who is God alone,” if the relative referred to the predicate and not to the supposit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0069","text":"But Augustine, when he says that the Father is not God alone, but that the Trinity is God alone, speaks by way of exposition, as if to say: when it is said, “to the King of the ages, invisible, to God alone,” this is not to be expounded of the person of the Father, but of the Trinity alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0070","text":"[I q. 31 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","text":"To the third, it must be said that in both ways this expression “alone” can be joined to an essential term. For this proposition, “God alone is Father,” is twofold. For “Father” can predicate the person of the Father, and thus it is true, since no man is that person. Or it can predicate only the relation, and thus it is false, because the relation of paternity is found also in others, though not univocally. Likewise, this is true: “God alone creates.” Yet it does not follow, therefore, “The Father alone [creates],” because, as the sophists say, an exclusive expression renders immobile the term to which it is joined, so that no descent can be made under it to any of the supposits; for it does not follow: “Man alone is a rational mortal animal; therefore Socrates alone [is].”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0072","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0073","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0074","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that an exclusive term can be joined to a personal term, even if the predicate is common. For the Lord says, speaking to the Father, John 17, *that they may know thee, the only true God*. Therefore the Father alone is the true God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0075","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0076","text":"Further, Matthew 11 says, no one knows the Son except the Father; which signifies the same as if it were said, the Father alone knows the Son. But to know the Son is common. Therefore, the same conclusion as before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0077","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0078","text":"Further, an exclusive term does not exclude that which is included in the understanding of the term to which it is joined; hence it does not exclude a part, nor a universal. For it does not follow: Socrates alone is white; therefore his hand is not white; or, therefore man is not white. But one person is included in the understanding of another, as the Father in the understanding of the Son, and conversely. Therefore, by the statement, the Father alone is God, the Son or the Holy Spirit is not excluded. And thus this expression seems to be true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0079","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0080","text":"Further, it is sung by the Church, *thou alone art the Most High, Jesus Christ*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0081","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0082","text":"On the contrary, this expression, the Father alone is God, has two expositions, namely: the Father is God, and, no one other than the Father is God. But this second is false, because the Son is other than the Father, and is God. Therefore this too is false: the Father alone is God. And likewise in similar cases.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0083","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","text":"I answer that, when we say, the Father alone is God, this proposition can have several meanings. For if “alone” posits solitude with respect to the Father, then it is false, insofar as it is taken categorematically. But insofar as it is taken syncategorematically, it can again be understood in several ways. For if it excludes from the form of the subject, then it is true, so that the sense is: the Father alone is God, that is, he with whom no one else is Father is God. And Augustine expounds it in this way, in *On the Trinity*, VI, when he says: *we say the Father alone, not because he is separated from the Son or the Holy Spirit; but in saying this, we signify that they together with him are not Father*. But this sense is not obtained from the customary manner of speaking, unless some implication is understood, as if it were said: he who alone is called Father is God. But according to its proper sense, it excludes from participation in the predicate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","text":"And thus this proposition is false if it excludes another masculine; but it is true if it excludes only another thing neuter, because the Son is other than the Father, yet not another thing; and likewise the Holy Spirit. But because this term “alone” properly regards the subject, as has been said, it is more apt to exclude another [person] than another thing. Hence such an expression should not be used broadly; but should be piously expounded wherever it is found in authoritative Scripture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0086","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0087","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, when we say, thee the only true God, it is not understood of the person of the Father, but of the whole Trinity, as Augustine expounds it. Or, if it is understood of the person of the Father, the other persons are not excluded, on account of the unity of essence, insofar as the word “alone” excludes only another thing, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0088","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0089","text":"And the same must be said in reply to the second. For when something essential is said of the Father, the Son or the Holy Spirit is not excluded, on account of the unity of essence. Nevertheless, it should be known that in the authority cited above, this term “no one” is not the same as “no man,” which the word seems to signify (for the person of the Father could not then be excepted); rather, according to customary usage, it is taken distributively for any rational nature whatsoever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0090","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0091","text":"To the third it must be said that an exclusive term does not exclude those things which are included in the understanding of the term to which it is joined, if they do not differ in supposit, as a part and a universal. But the Son differs in supposit from the Father, and therefore the reasoning is not the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0092","text":"[I q. 31 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0093","text":"To the fourth it must be said that we do not say absolutely that the Son alone is the Most High, but that he alone is the Most High together with the Holy Spirit, in the glory of God the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0093"]}],"source_digest":"6dc200171ef24f7f59ed9ec7b1daf4ac7f70652abdac74c298a7a534cb7b2df0","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 31"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 31","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24","title":"First Part · Question 32","order":33,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_24","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 32 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29771)\n\nConsequenter inquirendum est de cognitione divinarum personarum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum per rationem naturalem possint cognosci divinae personae. Secundo, utrum sint aliquae notiones divinis personis attribuendae. Tertio, de numero notionum. Quarto, utrum liceat diversimode circa notiones opinari.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29772)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Trinitas divinarum personarum possit per naturalem rationem cognosci. Philosophi enim non devenerunt in Dei cognitionem nisi per rationem naturalem, inveniuntur autem a philosophis multa dicta de Trinitate personarum. Dicit enim Aristoteles, in I de caelo et mundo, per hunc numerum, scilicet ternarium, *adhibuimus nos ipsos magnificare Deum unum, eminentem proprietatibus eorum quae sunt creata*. Augustinus etiam dicit, VII Confes., ibi legi, scilicet in libris Platonicorum, *non quidem his verbis, sed hoc idem omnino, multis et multiplicibus suaderi rationibus, quod in principio erat verbum, et verbum erat apud Deum, et Deus erat verbum*, et huiusmodi quae ibi sequuntur, in quibus verbis distinctio divinarum personarum traditur. Dicitur etiam in Glossa Rom. I, et Exod. VIII, quod magi Pharaonis defecerunt in tertio signo, idest in notitia tertiae personae, scilicet spiritus sancti, et sic ad minus duas cognoverunt. Trismegistus etiam dixit, *monas genuit monadem, et in se suum reflexit ardorem*, per quod videtur generatio filii, et spiritus sancti processio intimari. Cognitio ergo divinarum personarum potest per rationem naturalem haberi.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29773)\n\nPraeterea, Ricardus de sancto Victore dicit, in libro de Trin., *credo sine dubio quod ad quamcumque explanationem veritatis, non modo probabilia, imo etiam necessaria argumenta non desint*. Unde etiam ad probandum Trinitatem personarum, aliqui induxerunt rationem ex infinitate bonitatis divinae, quae seipsam infinite communicat in processione divinarum personarum. Quidam vero per hoc, quod *nullius boni sine consortio potest esse iucunda possessio*. Augustinus vero procedit ad manifestandum Trinitatem personarum, ex processione verbi et amoris in mente nostra, quam viam supra secuti sumus. Ergo per rationem naturalem potest cognosci Trinitas personarum.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29774)\n\nPraeterea, superfluum videtur homini tradere quod humana ratione cognosci non potest. Sed non est dicendum quod traditio divina de cognitione Trinitatis sit superflua. Ergo Trinitas personarum ratione humana cognosci potest.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29775)\n\nSed contra est quod Hilarius dicit, in libro II de Trin., *non putet homo sua intelligentia generationis sacramentum posse consequi*. Ambrosius etiam dicit, *impossibile est generationis scire secretum, mens deficit, vox silet*. Sed per originem generationis et processionis distinguitur Trinitas in personis divinis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Cum ergo illud homo non possit scire et intelligentia consequi, ad quod ratio necessaria haberi non potest, sequitur quod Trinitas personarum per rationem cognosci non possit.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29776)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est per rationem naturalem ad cognitionem Trinitatis divinarum personarum pervenire. Ostensum est enim supra quod homo per rationem naturalem in cognitionem Dei pervenire non potest nisi ex creaturis. Creaturae autem ducunt in Dei cognitionem, sicut effectus in causam. Hoc igitur solum ratione naturali de Deo cognosci potest, quod competere ei necesse est secundum quod est omnium entium principium, et hoc fundamento usi sumus supra in consideratione Dei. Virtus autem creativa Dei est communis toti Trinitati, unde pertinet ad unitatem essentiae, non ad distinctionem personarum. Per rationem igitur naturalem cognosci possunt de Deo ea quae pertinent ad unitatem essentiae, non autem ea quae pertinent ad distinctionem personarum. Qui autem probare nititur Trinitatem personarum naturali ratione, fidei dupliciter derogat. Primo quidem, quantum ad dignitatem ipsius fidei, quae est ut sit de rebus invisibilibus, quae rationem humanam excedunt. Unde apostolus dicit, ad Heb. XI, quod fides est de non apparentibus. Et apostolus dicit, I Cor. II, *sapientiam loquimur inter perfectos, sapientiam vero non huius saeculi, neque principum huius saeculi; sed loquimur Dei sapientiam in mysterio, quae abscondita est*. Secundo, quantum ad utilitatem trahendi alios ad fidem. Cum enim aliquis ad probandam fidem inducit rationes quae non sunt cogentes, cedit in irrisionem infidelium, credunt enim quod huiusmodi rationibus innitamur, et propter eas credamus. Quae igitur fidei sunt, non sunt tentanda probare nisi per auctoritates, his qui auctoritates suscipiunt. Apud alios vero, sufficit defendere non esse impossibile quod praedicat fides. Unde Dionysius dicit, II cap. de Div. Nom., *si aliquis est qui totaliter eloquiis resistit, longe erit a nostra philosophia; si autem ad veritatem eloquiorum*, scilicet sacrorum, respicit, hoc et nos canone utimur.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29777)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophi non cognoverunt mysterium Trinitatis divinarum personarum per propria, quae sunt paternitas, filiatio et processio; secundum illud apostoli, I ad Cor. II, *loquimur Dei sapientiam, quam nemo principum huius saeculi cognovit*, idest philosophorum, secundum Glossam. Cognoverunt tamen quaedam essentialia attributa quae appropriantur personis, sicut potentia patri, sapientia filio, bonitas spiritui sancto, ut infra patebit. Quod ergo Aristoteles dicit, *per hunc numerum adhibuimus nos ipsos* etc., non est sic intelligendum, quod ipse poneret ternarium numerum in divinis, sed vult dicere quod antiqui utebantur ternario numero in sacrificiis et orationibus, propter quandam ternarii numeri perfectionem. In libris etiam Platonicorum invenitur *in principio erat verum*, non secundum quod verbum significat personam genitam in divinis, sed secundum quod per verbum intelligitur ratio idealis, per quam Deus omnia condidit, quae filio appropriatur. Et licet appropriata tribus personis cognoscerent, dicuntur tamen in tertio signo defecisse, idest in cognitione tertiae personae, quia a bonitate, quae spiritui sancto appropriatur, deviaverunt, dum cognoscentes Deum, non sicut Deum glorificaverunt, ut dicitur Rom. I. Vel, quia ponebant Platonici unum primum ens, quod etiam dicebant esse patrem totius universitatis rerum, consequenter ponebant aliam substantiam sub eo, quam vocabant mentem vel paternum intellectum, in qua erant rationes omnium rerum, sicut Macrobius recitat super somnium Scipionis, non autem ponebant aliquam substantiam tertiam separatam, quae videretur spiritui sancto respondere. Sic autem nos non ponimus patrem et filium, secundum substantiam differentes, sed hoc fuit error Origenis et Arii. Sequentium in hoc Platonicos. Quod vero Trismegistus dixit, *monas monadem genuit, et in se suum reflexit ardorem*, non est referendum ad generationem filii vel processionem spiritus sancti, sed ad productionem mundi, nam unus Deus produxit unum mundum propter sui ipsius amorem.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29778)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ad aliquam rem dupliciter inducitur ratio. Uno modo, ad probandum sufficienter aliquam radicem, sicut in scientia naturali inducitur ratio sufficiens ad probandum quod motus caeli semper sit uniformis velocitatis. Alio modo inducitur ratio, non quae sufficienter probet radicem, sed quae radici iam positae ostendat congruere consequentes effectus, sicut in astrologia ponitur ratio excentricorum et epicyclorum ex hoc quod, hac positione facta, possunt salvari apparentia sensibilia circa motus caelestes, non tamen ratio haec est sufficienter probans, quia etiam forte alia positione facta salvari possent. Primo ergo modo potest induci ratio ad probandum Deum esse unum, et similia. Sed secundo modo se habet ratio quae inducitur ad manifestationem Trinitatis, quia scilicet, Trinitate posita, congruunt huiusmodi rationes; non tamen ita quod per has rationes sufficienter probetur Trinitas personarum. Et hoc patet per singula. Bonitas enim infinita Dei manifestatur etiam in productione creaturarum, quia infinitae virtutis est ex nihilo producere. Non enim oportet, si infinita bonitate se communicat, quod aliquid infinitum a Deo procedat, sed secundum modum suum recipiat divinam bonitatem. Similiter etiam quod dicitur, quod *sine consortio non potest esse iucunda possessio alicuius boni*, locum habet quando in una persona non invenitur perfecta bonitas; unde indiget, ad plenam iucunditatis bonitatem, bono alicuius alterius consociati sibi. Similitudo autem intellectus nostri non sufficienter probat aliquid de Deo, propter hoc quod intellectus non univoce invenitur in Deo et in nobis. Et inde est quod Augustinus, super Ioan., dicit quod per fidem venitur ad cognitionem, et non e converso.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29779)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod cognitio divinarum personarum fuit necessaria nobis dupliciter. Uno modo, ad recte sentiendum de creatione rerum. Per hoc enim quod dicimus Deum omnia fecisse verbo suo, excluditur error ponentium Deum produxisse res ex necessitate naturae. Per hoc autem quod ponimus in eo processionem amoris, ostenditur quod Deus non propter aliquam indigentiam creaturas produxit, neque propter aliquam aliam causam extrinsecam; sed propter amorem suae bonitatis. Unde et Moyses, postquam dixerat, in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, subdit, dixit Deus, fiat lux, ad manifestationem divini verbi; et postea dixit, vidit Deus lucem, quod esset bona, ad ostendendum approbationem divini amoris; et similiter in aliis operibus. Alio modo, et principalius, ad recte sentiendum de salute generis humani, quae perficitur per filium incarnatum, et per donum spiritus sancti.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29780)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint ponendae notiones in divinis. Dicit enim Dionysius, in I cap. de Div. Nom., quod *non est audendum dicere aliquid de Deo, praeter ea quae nobis ex sacris eloquiis sunt expressa*. Sed de notionibus nulla fit mentio in eloquiis sacrae Scripturae. Ergo non sunt ponendae notiones in divinis.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29781)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid ponitur in divinis, aut pertinet ad unitatem essentiae, aut ad Trinitatem personarum. Sed notiones non pertinent ad unitatem essentiae, nec ad Trinitatem personarum. De notionibus enim neque praedicantur ea quae sunt essentiae, non enim dicimus quod paternitas sit sapiens vel creet, neque etiam ea quae sunt personae; non enim dicimus quod paternitas generet et filiatio generetur. Ergo non sunt ponendae notiones in divinis.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29782)\n\nPraeterea, in simplicibus non sunt ponenda aliqua abstracta, quae sint principia cognoscendi, quia cognoscuntur seipsis. Sed divinae personae sunt simplicissimae. Ergo non sunt ponendae in divinis personis notiones.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29783)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Ioannes Damascenus, differentiam hypostaseon, idest personarum, in tribus proprietatibus, *idest paternali et filiali et processionali, recognoscimus*. Sunt ergo ponendae proprietates et notiones in divinis.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29784)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Praepositivus, attendens simplicitatem personarum, dixit non esse ponendas proprietates et notiones in divinis, et sicubi inveniantur, exponit abstractum pro concreto, sicut enim consuevimus dicere, rogo benignitatem tuam, idest te benignum, ita cum dicitur in divinis paternitas, intelligitur Deus pater. Sed, sicut ostensum est supra, divinae simplicitati non praeiudicat quod in divinis utamur nominibus concretis et abstractis. Quia secundum quod intelligimus, sic nominamus. Intellectus autem noster non potest pertingere ad ipsam simplicitatem divinam, secundum quod in se est consideranda, et ideo secundum modum suum divina apprehendit et nominat, idest secundum quod invenitur in rebus sensibilibus, a quibus cognitionem accipit. In quibus, ad significandum simplices formas, nominibus abstractis utimur, ad significandum vero res subsistentes, utimur nominibus concretis. Unde et divina, sicut supra dictum est, ratione simplicitatis, per nomina abstracta significamus, ratione vero subsistentiae et complementi, per nomina concreta. Oportet autem non solum nomina essentialia in abstracto et in concreto significare, ut cum dicimus deitatem et Deum, vel sapientiam et sapientem; sed etiam personalia, ut dicamus paternitatem et patrem. Ad quod duo praecipue nos cogunt. Primo quidem, haereticorum instantia. Cum enim confiteamur patrem et filium et spiritum sanctum esse unum Deum et tres personas, quaerentibus quo sunt unus Deus, et quo sunt tres personae, sicut respondetur quod sunt essentia vel deitate unum, ita oportuit esse aliqua nomina abstracta, quibus responderi possit personas distingui. Et huiusmodi sunt proprietates vel notiones in abstracto significatae, ut paternitas et filiatio. Et ideo essentia significatur in divinis ut quid, persona vero ut quis, proprietas autem ut quo. Secundo, quia una persona invenitur in divinis referri ad duas personas, scilicet persona patris ad personam filii et personam spiritus sancti. Non autem una relatione, quia sic sequeretur quod etiam filius et spiritus sanctus una et eadem relatione referrentur ad patrem; et sic, cum sola relatio in divinis multiplicet Trinitatem, sequeretur quod filius et spiritus sanctus non essent duae personae. Neque potest dici, ut Praepositivus dicebat, quod sicut Deus uno modo se habet ad creaturas, cum tamen creaturae diversimode se habeant ad ipsum, sic pater una relatione refertur ad filium et ad spiritum sanctum, cum tamen illi duo duabus relationibus referantur ad patrem. Quia cum ratio specifica relativi consistat in hoc quod ad aliud se habet, necesse est dicere quod duae relationes non sunt diversae secundum speciem, si ex opposito una relatio eis correspondeat, oportet enim aliam speciem relationis esse domini et patris, secundum diversitatem filiationis et servitutis. Omnes autem creaturae sub una specie relationis referuntur ad Deum, ut sunt creaturae ipsius, filius autem et spiritus sanctus non secundum relationes unius rationis referuntur ad patrem, unde non est simile. Et iterum, in Deo non requiritur relatio realis ad creaturam, ut supra dictum est, relationes autem rationis in Deo multiplicare non est inconveniens. Sed in patre oportet esse relationem realem qua refertur ad filium et spiritum sanctum, unde secundum duas relationes filii et spiritus sancti quibus referuntur ad patrem, oportet intelligi duas relationes in patre, quibus referatur ad filium et spiritum sanctum. Unde, cum non sit nisi una patris persona, necesse fuit seorsum significari relationes in abstracto, quae dicuntur proprietates et notiones.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29785)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet de notionibus non fiat mentio in sacra Scriptura, fit tamen mentio de personis, in quibus intelliguntur notiones, sicut abstractum in concreto.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29786)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod notiones significantur in divinis, non ut res, sed ut rationes quaedam quibus cognoscuntur personae; licet ipsae notiones vel relationes realiter sint in Deo, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo ea quae habent ordinem aliquem ad actum aliquem essentialem vel personalem, non possunt dici de notionibus, quia hoc repugnat modo significandi ipsarum. Unde non possumus dicere quod paternitas generet vel creet, sit sapiens vel intelligens. Essentialia vero quae non habent ordinem ad aliquem actum, sed removent conditiones creaturae a Deo possunt praedicari de notionibus, possumus enim dicere quod paternitas est aeterna vel immensa, vel quodcumque huiusmodi. Et similiter, propter identitatem rei, possunt substantiva personalia et essentialia praedicari de notionibus, possumus enim dicere quod paternitas est Deus, et paternitas est pater.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29787)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet personae sint simplices, tamen absque praeiudicio simplicitatis possunt propriae rationes personarum in abstracto significari, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29788)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint quinque notiones. Propriae enim notiones personarum sunt relationes quibus distinguuntur. Sed relationes in divinis non sunt nisi quatuor, ut supra dictum est. Ergo et notiones sunt tantum quatuor.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29789)\n\nPraeterea, propter hoc quod in divinis est una essentia, dicitur Deus unus, propter hoc autem quod sunt tres personae, dicitur Deus trinus. Si ergo in divinis sunt quinque notiones, dicetur quinus, quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29790)\n\nPraeterea, si, tribus personis existentibus in divinis, sunt quinque notiones, oportet quod in aliqua personarum sint aliquae notiones duae vel plures; sicut in persona patris ponitur innascibilitas et paternitas et communis spiratio. Aut igitur istae tres notiones differunt re, aut non. Si differunt re, sequitur quod persona patris sit composita ex pluribus rebus. Si autem differunt ratione tantum, sequitur quod una earum possit de alia praedicari, ut dicamus quod, sicut bonitas divina est eius sapientia propter indifferentiam rei, ita communis spiratio sit paternitas, quod non conceditur. Igitur non sunt quinque notiones.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29791)\n\nSed contra, videtur quod sint plures. Quia sicut pater a nullo est, et secundum hoc accipitur notio quae dicitur innascibilitas, ita a spiritu sancto non est alia persona. Et secundum hoc oportebit accipere sextam notionem.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29792)\n\nPraeterea, sicut patri et filio commune est quod ab eis procedat spiritus sanctus, ita commune est filio et spiritui sancto quod procedant a patre. Ergo, sicut una notio ponitur communis patri et filio, ita debet poni una notio communis filio et spiritui sancto.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29793)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod notio dicitur id quod est propria ratio cognoscendi divinam personam. Divinae autem personae multiplicantur secundum originem. Ad originem autem pertinet a quo alius, et qui ab alio, et secundum hos duos modos potest innotescere persona. Igitur persona patris non potest innotescere per hoc quod sit ab alio, sed per hoc quod a nullo est, et sic ex hac parte eius notio est innascibilitas. Sed inquantum aliquis est ab eo, innotescit dupliciter. Quia inquantum filius est ab eo, innotescit notione paternitatis, inquantum autem spiritus sanctus est ab eo, innotescit notione communis spirationis. Filius autem potest innotescere per hoc quod est ab alio nascendo, et sic innotescit per filiationem. Et per hoc quod est alius ab eo, scilicet spiritus sanctus, et per hoc innotescit eodem modo sicut et pater, scilicet communi spiratione. Spiritus sanctus autem innotescere potest per hoc quod est ab alio vel ab aliis, et sic innotescit processione. Non autem per hoc quod alius sit ab eo, quia nulla divina persona procedit ab eo. Sunt igitur quinque notiones in divinis, scilicet innascibilitas, paternitas, filiatio, communis spiratio et processio. Harum autem tantum quatuor sunt relationes, nam innascibilitas non est relatio nisi per reductionem, ut infra dicetur. Quatuor autem tantum proprietates sunt, nam communis spiratio non est proprietas, quia convenit duabus personis. Tres autem sunt notiones personales, idest constituentes personas, scilicet paternitas, filiatio et processio, nam communis spiratio et innascibilitas dicuntur notiones personarum, non autem personales, ut infra magis patebit.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29794)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod praeter quatuor relationes oportet ponere aliam notionem, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29795)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod essentia in divinis significatur ut res quaedam; et similiter personae significantur ut res quaedam sed notiones significantur ut rationes notificantes personas. Et ideo, licet dicatur Deus unus propter unitatem essentiae, et trinus propter Trinitatem personarum; non tamen dicitur quinus propter quinque notiones.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29796)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum sola oppositio relativa faciat pluralitatem realem in divinis, plures proprietates unius personae, cum non opponantur ad invicem relative, non differunt realiter. Nec tamen de invicem praedicantur, quia significantur ut diversae rationes personarum. Sicut etiam non dicimus quod attributum potentiae sit attributum scientiae, licet dicamus quod scientia sit potentia.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29797)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, cum persona importet dignitatem, ut supra dictum est, non potest accipi notio aliqua spiritus sancti ex hoc quod nulla persona est ab ipso. Hoc enim non pertinet ad dignitatem ipsius; sicut pertinet ad auctoritatem patris quod sit a nullo.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29798)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod filius et spiritus sanctus non conveniunt in uno speciali modo existendi a patre; sicut pater et filius conveniunt in uno speciali modo producendi spiritum sanctum. Id autem quod est principium innotescendi, oportet esse aliquid speciale. Et ideo non est simile.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 32 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29799)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non liceat contrarie opinari de notionibus. Dicit enim Augustinus, in I de Trin., quod non erratur alicubi periculosius quam in materia Trinitatis, ad quam certum est notiones pertinere. Sed contrariae opiniones non possunt esse absque errore. Ergo contrarie opinari circa notiones non licet.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29800)\n\nPraeterea, per notiones cognoscuntur personae, ut dictum est. Sed circa personas non licet contrarie opinari. Ergo nec circa notiones.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29801)\n\nSed contra, articuli fidei non sunt de notionibus. Ergo circa notiones licet sic vel aliter opinari.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29802)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ad fidem pertinet aliquid dupliciter. Uno modo, directe; sicut ea quae nobis sunt principaliter divinitus tradita, ut Deum esse trinum et unum, filium Dei esse incarnatum, et huiusmodi. Et circa haec opinari falsum, hoc ipso inducit haeresim, maxime si pertinacia adiungatur. Indirecte vero ad fidem pertinent ea ex quibus consequitur aliquid contrarium fidei; sicut si quis diceret Samuelem non fuisse filium Elcanae; ex hoc enim sequitur Scripturam divinam esse falsam. Circa huiusmodi ergo absque periculo haeresis aliquis falsum potest opinari, antequam consideretur, vel determinatum sit, quod ex hoc sequitur aliquid contrarium fidei, et maxime si non pertinaciter adhaereat. Sed postquam manifestum est, et praecipue si sit per Ecclesiam determinatum, quod ex hoc sequitur aliquid contrarium fidei, in hoc errare non esset absque haeresi. Et propter hoc, multa nunc reputantur haeretica, quae prius non reputabantur, propter hoc quod nunc est magis manifestum quid ex eis sequatur. Sic igitur dicendum est quod circa notiones aliqui absque periculo haeresis contrarie sunt opinati, non intendentes sustinere aliquid contrarium fidei. Sed si quis falsum opinaretur circa notiones, considerans quod ex hoc sequatur aliquid contrarium fidei, in haeresim laberetur.\n\n[I q. 32 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29803)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0002","text":"[I q. 32 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0003","text":"Consequenter inquirendum est de cognitione divinarum personarum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum per rationem naturalem possint cognosci divinae personae. Secundo, utrum sint aliquae notiones divinis personis attribuendae. Tertio, de numero notionum. Quarto, utrum liceat diversimode circa notiones opinari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0005","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Trinitas divinarum personarum possit per naturalem rationem cognosci. Philosophi enim non devenerunt in Dei cognitionem nisi per rationem naturalem, inveniuntur autem a philosophis multa dicta de Trinitate personarum. Dicit enim Aristoteles, in I de caelo et mundo, per hunc numerum, scilicet ternarium, *adhibuimus nos ipsos magnificare Deum unum, eminentem proprietatibus eorum quae sunt creata*. Augustinus etiam dicit, VII Confes., ibi legi, scilicet in libris Platonicorum, *non quidem his verbis, sed hoc idem omnino, multis et multiplicibus suaderi rationibus, quod in principio erat verbum, et verbum erat apud Deum, et Deus erat verbum*, et huiusmodi quae ibi sequuntur, in quibus verbis distinctio divinarum personarum traditur. Dicitur etiam in Glossa Rom. I, et Exod.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0007","text":"VIII, quod magi Pharaonis defecerunt in tertio signo, idest in notitia tertiae personae, scilicet spiritus sancti, et sic ad minus duas cognoverunt. Trismegistus etiam dixit, *monas genuit monadem, et in se suum reflexit ardorem*, per quod videtur generatio filii, et spiritus sancti processio intimari. Cognitio ergo divinarum personarum potest per rationem naturalem haberi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0008","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0009","text":"Praeterea, Ricardus de sancto Victore dicit, in libro de Trin., *credo sine dubio quod ad quamcumque explanationem veritatis, non modo probabilia, imo etiam necessaria argumenta non desint*. Unde etiam ad probandum Trinitatem personarum, aliqui induxerunt rationem ex infinitate bonitatis divinae, quae seipsam infinite communicat in processione divinarum personarum. Quidam vero per hoc, quod *nullius boni sine consortio potest esse iucunda possessio*. Augustinus vero procedit ad manifestandum Trinitatem personarum, ex processione verbi et amoris in mente nostra, quam viam supra secuti sumus. Ergo per rationem naturalem potest cognosci Trinitas personarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0010","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0011","text":"Praeterea, superfluum videtur homini tradere quod humana ratione cognosci non potest. Sed non est dicendum quod traditio divina de cognitione Trinitatis sit superflua. Ergo Trinitas personarum ratione humana cognosci potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0012","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0013","text":"Sed contra est quod Hilarius dicit, in libro II de Trin., *non putet homo sua intelligentia generationis sacramentum posse consequi*. Ambrosius etiam dicit, *impossibile est generationis scire secretum, mens deficit, vox silet*. Sed per originem generationis et processionis distinguitur Trinitas in personis divinis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Cum ergo illud homo non possit scire et intelligentia consequi, ad quod ratio necessaria haberi non potest, sequitur quod Trinitas personarum per rationem cognosci non possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0014","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est per rationem naturalem ad cognitionem Trinitatis divinarum personarum pervenire. Ostensum est enim supra quod homo per rationem naturalem in cognitionem Dei pervenire non potest nisi ex creaturis. Creaturae autem ducunt in Dei cognitionem, sicut effectus in causam. Hoc igitur solum ratione naturali de Deo cognosci potest, quod competere ei necesse est secundum quod est omnium entium principium, et hoc fundamento usi sumus supra in consideratione Dei. Virtus autem creativa Dei est communis toti Trinitati, unde pertinet ad unitatem essentiae, non ad distinctionem personarum. Per rationem igitur naturalem cognosci possunt de Deo ea quae pertinent ad unitatem essentiae, non autem ea quae pertinent ad distinctionem personarum. Qui autem probare nititur Trinitatem personarum naturali ratione, fidei dupliciter derogat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","text":"Primo quidem, quantum ad dignitatem ipsius fidei, quae est ut sit de rebus invisibilibus, quae rationem humanam excedunt. Unde apostolus dicit, ad Heb. XI, quod fides est de non apparentibus. Et apostolus dicit, I Cor. II, *sapientiam loquimur inter perfectos, sapientiam vero non huius saeculi, neque principum huius saeculi; sed loquimur Dei sapientiam in mysterio, quae abscondita est*. Secundo, quantum ad utilitatem trahendi alios ad fidem. Cum enim aliquis ad probandam fidem inducit rationes quae non sunt cogentes, cedit in irrisionem infidelium, credunt enim quod huiusmodi rationibus innitamur, et propter eas credamus. Quae igitur fidei sunt, non sunt tentanda probare nisi per auctoritates, his qui auctoritates suscipiunt. Apud alios vero, sufficit defendere non esse impossibile quod praedicat fides. Unde Dionysius dicit, II cap. de Div.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0017","text":"Nom., *si aliquis est qui totaliter eloquiis resistit, longe erit a nostra philosophia; si autem ad veritatem eloquiorum*, scilicet sacrorum, respicit, hoc et nos canone utimur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0018","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophi non cognoverunt mysterium Trinitatis divinarum personarum per propria, quae sunt paternitas, filiatio et processio; secundum illud apostoli, I ad Cor. II, *loquimur Dei sapientiam, quam nemo principum huius saeculi cognovit*, idest philosophorum, secundum Glossam. Cognoverunt tamen quaedam essentialia attributa quae appropriantur personis, sicut potentia patri, sapientia filio, bonitas spiritui sancto, ut infra patebit. Quod ergo Aristoteles dicit, *per hunc numerum adhibuimus nos ipsos* etc., non est sic intelligendum, quod ipse poneret ternarium numerum in divinis, sed vult dicere quod antiqui utebantur ternario numero in sacrificiis et orationibus, propter quandam ternarii numeri perfectionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0020","text":"In libris etiam Platonicorum invenitur *in principio erat verum*, non secundum quod verbum significat personam genitam in divinis, sed secundum quod per verbum intelligitur ratio idealis, per quam Deus omnia condidit, quae filio appropriatur. Et licet appropriata tribus personis cognoscerent, dicuntur tamen in tertio signo defecisse, idest in cognitione tertiae personae, quia a bonitate, quae spiritui sancto appropriatur, deviaverunt, dum cognoscentes Deum, non sicut Deum glorificaverunt, ut dicitur Rom. I. Vel, quia ponebant Platonici unum primum ens, quod etiam dicebant esse patrem totius universitatis rerum, consequenter ponebant aliam substantiam sub eo, quam vocabant mentem vel paternum intellectum, in qua erant rationes omnium rerum, sicut Macrobius recitat super somnium Scipionis, non autem ponebant aliquam substantiam tertiam separatam, quae videretur spiritui sancto respondere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0021","text":"Sic autem nos non ponimus patrem et filium, secundum substantiam differentes, sed hoc fuit error Origenis et Arii. Sequentium in hoc Platonicos. Quod vero Trismegistus dixit, *monas monadem genuit, et in se suum reflexit ardorem*, non est referendum ad generationem filii vel processionem spiritus sancti, sed ad productionem mundi, nam unus Deus produxit unum mundum propter sui ipsius amorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0022","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ad aliquam rem dupliciter inducitur ratio. Uno modo, ad probandum sufficienter aliquam radicem, sicut in scientia naturali inducitur ratio sufficiens ad probandum quod motus caeli semper sit uniformis velocitatis. Alio modo inducitur ratio, non quae sufficienter probet radicem, sed quae radici iam positae ostendat congruere consequentes effectus, sicut in astrologia ponitur ratio excentricorum et epicyclorum ex hoc quod, hac positione facta, possunt salvari apparentia sensibilia circa motus caelestes, non tamen ratio haec est sufficienter probans, quia etiam forte alia positione facta salvari possent. Primo ergo modo potest induci ratio ad probandum Deum esse unum, et similia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","text":"Sed secundo modo se habet ratio quae inducitur ad manifestationem Trinitatis, quia scilicet, Trinitate posita, congruunt huiusmodi rationes; non tamen ita quod per has rationes sufficienter probetur Trinitas personarum. Et hoc patet per singula. Bonitas enim infinita Dei manifestatur etiam in productione creaturarum, quia infinitae virtutis est ex nihilo producere. Non enim oportet, si infinita bonitate se communicat, quod aliquid infinitum a Deo procedat, sed secundum modum suum recipiat divinam bonitatem. Similiter etiam quod dicitur, quod *sine consortio non potest esse iucunda possessio alicuius boni*, locum habet quando in una persona non invenitur perfecta bonitas; unde indiget, ad plenam iucunditatis bonitatem, bono alicuius alterius consociati sibi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0025","text":"Similitudo autem intellectus nostri non sufficienter probat aliquid de Deo, propter hoc quod intellectus non univoce invenitur in Deo et in nobis. Et inde est quod Augustinus, super Ioan., dicit quod per fidem venitur ad cognitionem, et non e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0026","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod cognitio divinarum personarum fuit necessaria nobis dupliciter. Uno modo, ad recte sentiendum de creatione rerum. Per hoc enim quod dicimus Deum omnia fecisse verbo suo, excluditur error ponentium Deum produxisse res ex necessitate naturae. Per hoc autem quod ponimus in eo processionem amoris, ostenditur quod Deus non propter aliquam indigentiam creaturas produxit, neque propter aliquam aliam causam extrinsecam; sed propter amorem suae bonitatis. Unde et Moyses, postquam dixerat, in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, subdit, dixit Deus, fiat lux, ad manifestationem divini verbi; et postea dixit, vidit Deus lucem, quod esset bona, ad ostendendum approbationem divini amoris; et similiter in aliis operibus. Alio modo, et principalius, ad recte sentiendum de salute generis humani, quae perficitur per filium incarnatum, et per donum spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0028","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0029","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0030","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint ponendae notiones in divinis. Dicit enim Dionysius, in I cap. de Div. Nom., quod *non est audendum dicere aliquid de Deo, praeter ea quae nobis ex sacris eloquiis sunt expressa*. Sed de notionibus nulla fit mentio in eloquiis sacrae Scripturae. Ergo non sunt ponendae notiones in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0031","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0032","text":"Praeterea, quidquid ponitur in divinis, aut pertinet ad unitatem essentiae, aut ad Trinitatem personarum. Sed notiones non pertinent ad unitatem essentiae, nec ad Trinitatem personarum. De notionibus enim neque praedicantur ea quae sunt essentiae, non enim dicimus quod paternitas sit sapiens vel creet, neque etiam ea quae sunt personae; non enim dicimus quod paternitas generet et filiatio generetur. Ergo non sunt ponendae notiones in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0033","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0034","text":"Praeterea, in simplicibus non sunt ponenda aliqua abstracta, quae sint principia cognoscendi, quia cognoscuntur seipsis. Sed divinae personae sunt simplicissimae. Ergo non sunt ponendae in divinis personis notiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0035","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0036","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Ioannes Damascenus, differentiam hypostaseon, idest personarum, in tribus proprietatibus, *idest paternali et filiali et processionali, recognoscimus*. Sunt ergo ponendae proprietates et notiones in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0037","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Praepositivus, attendens simplicitatem personarum, dixit non esse ponendas proprietates et notiones in divinis, et sicubi inveniantur, exponit abstractum pro concreto, sicut enim consuevimus dicere, rogo benignitatem tuam, idest te benignum, ita cum dicitur in divinis paternitas, intelligitur Deus pater. Sed, sicut ostensum est supra, divinae simplicitati non praeiudicat quod in divinis utamur nominibus concretis et abstractis. Quia secundum quod intelligimus, sic nominamus. Intellectus autem noster non potest pertingere ad ipsam simplicitatem divinam, secundum quod in se est consideranda, et ideo secundum modum suum divina apprehendit et nominat, idest secundum quod invenitur in rebus sensibilibus, a quibus cognitionem accipit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","text":"In quibus, ad significandum simplices formas, nominibus abstractis utimur, ad significandum vero res subsistentes, utimur nominibus concretis. Unde et divina, sicut supra dictum est, ratione simplicitatis, per nomina abstracta significamus, ratione vero subsistentiae et complementi, per nomina concreta. Oportet autem non solum nomina essentialia in abstracto et in concreto significare, ut cum dicimus deitatem et Deum, vel sapientiam et sapientem; sed etiam personalia, ut dicamus paternitatem et patrem. Ad quod duo praecipue nos cogunt. Primo quidem, haereticorum instantia. Cum enim confiteamur patrem et filium et spiritum sanctum esse unum Deum et tres personas, quaerentibus quo sunt unus Deus, et quo sunt tres personae, sicut respondetur quod sunt essentia vel deitate unum, ita oportuit esse aliqua nomina abstracta, quibus responderi possit personas distingui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","text":"Et huiusmodi sunt proprietates vel notiones in abstracto significatae, ut paternitas et filiatio. Et ideo essentia significatur in divinis ut quid, persona vero ut quis, proprietas autem ut quo. Secundo, quia una persona invenitur in divinis referri ad duas personas, scilicet persona patris ad personam filii et personam spiritus sancti. Non autem una relatione, quia sic sequeretur quod etiam filius et spiritus sanctus una et eadem relatione referrentur ad patrem; et sic, cum sola relatio in divinis multiplicet Trinitatem, sequeretur quod filius et spiritus sanctus non essent duae personae. Neque potest dici, ut Praepositivus dicebat, quod sicut Deus uno modo se habet ad creaturas, cum tamen creaturae diversimode se habeant ad ipsum, sic pater una relatione refertur ad filium et ad spiritum sanctum, cum tamen illi duo duabus relationibus referantur ad patrem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0041","text":"Quia cum ratio specifica relativi consistat in hoc quod ad aliud se habet, necesse est dicere quod duae relationes non sunt diversae secundum speciem, si ex opposito una relatio eis correspondeat, oportet enim aliam speciem relationis esse domini et patris, secundum diversitatem filiationis et servitutis. Omnes autem creaturae sub una specie relationis referuntur ad Deum, ut sunt creaturae ipsius, filius autem et spiritus sanctus non secundum relationes unius rationis referuntur ad patrem, unde non est simile. Et iterum, in Deo non requiritur relatio realis ad creaturam, ut supra dictum est, relationes autem rationis in Deo multiplicare non est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0042","text":"Sed in patre oportet esse relationem realem qua refertur ad filium et spiritum sanctum, unde secundum duas relationes filii et spiritus sancti quibus referuntur ad patrem, oportet intelligi duas relationes in patre, quibus referatur ad filium et spiritum sanctum. Unde, cum non sit nisi una patris persona, necesse fuit seorsum significari relationes in abstracto, quae dicuntur proprietates et notiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0043","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0044","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet de notionibus non fiat mentio in sacra Scriptura, fit tamen mentio de personis, in quibus intelliguntur notiones, sicut abstractum in concreto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0045","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod notiones significantur in divinis, non ut res, sed ut rationes quaedam quibus cognoscuntur personae; licet ipsae notiones vel relationes realiter sint in Deo, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo ea quae habent ordinem aliquem ad actum aliquem essentialem vel personalem, non possunt dici de notionibus, quia hoc repugnat modo significandi ipsarum. Unde non possumus dicere quod paternitas generet vel creet, sit sapiens vel intelligens. Essentialia vero quae non habent ordinem ad aliquem actum, sed removent conditiones creaturae a Deo possunt praedicari de notionibus, possumus enim dicere quod paternitas est aeterna vel immensa, vel quodcumque huiusmodi. Et similiter, propter identitatem rei, possunt substantiva personalia et essentialia praedicari de notionibus, possumus enim dicere quod paternitas est Deus, et paternitas est pater.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0047","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0048","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet personae sint simplices, tamen absque praeiudicio simplicitatis possunt propriae rationes personarum in abstracto significari, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0049","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0050","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0051","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint quinque notiones. Propriae enim notiones personarum sunt relationes quibus distinguuntur. Sed relationes in divinis non sunt nisi quatuor, ut supra dictum est. Ergo et notiones sunt tantum quatuor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0052","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0053","text":"Praeterea, propter hoc quod in divinis est una essentia, dicitur Deus unus, propter hoc autem quod sunt tres personae, dicitur Deus trinus. Si ergo in divinis sunt quinque notiones, dicetur quinus, quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0054","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0055","text":"Praeterea, si, tribus personis existentibus in divinis, sunt quinque notiones, oportet quod in aliqua personarum sint aliquae notiones duae vel plures; sicut in persona patris ponitur innascibilitas et paternitas et communis spiratio. Aut igitur istae tres notiones differunt re, aut non. Si differunt re, sequitur quod persona patris sit composita ex pluribus rebus. Si autem differunt ratione tantum, sequitur quod una earum possit de alia praedicari, ut dicamus quod, sicut bonitas divina est eius sapientia propter indifferentiam rei, ita communis spiratio sit paternitas, quod non conceditur. Igitur non sunt quinque notiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0056","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0057","text":"Sed contra, videtur quod sint plures. Quia sicut pater a nullo est, et secundum hoc accipitur notio quae dicitur innascibilitas, ita a spiritu sancto non est alia persona. Et secundum hoc oportebit accipere sextam notionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0058","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0059","text":"Praeterea, sicut patri et filio commune est quod ab eis procedat spiritus sanctus, ita commune est filio et spiritui sancto quod procedant a patre. Ergo, sicut una notio ponitur communis patri et filio, ita debet poni una notio communis filio et spiritui sancto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0060","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod notio dicitur id quod est propria ratio cognoscendi divinam personam. Divinae autem personae multiplicantur secundum originem. Ad originem autem pertinet a quo alius, et qui ab alio, et secundum hos duos modos potest innotescere persona. Igitur persona patris non potest innotescere per hoc quod sit ab alio, sed per hoc quod a nullo est, et sic ex hac parte eius notio est innascibilitas. Sed inquantum aliquis est ab eo, innotescit dupliciter. Quia inquantum filius est ab eo, innotescit notione paternitatis, inquantum autem spiritus sanctus est ab eo, innotescit notione communis spirationis. Filius autem potest innotescere per hoc quod est ab alio nascendo, et sic innotescit per filiationem. Et per hoc quod est alius ab eo, scilicet spiritus sanctus, et per hoc innotescit eodem modo sicut et pater, scilicet communi spiratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","text":"Spiritus sanctus autem innotescere potest per hoc quod est ab alio vel ab aliis, et sic innotescit processione. Non autem per hoc quod alius sit ab eo, quia nulla divina persona procedit ab eo. Sunt igitur quinque notiones in divinis, scilicet innascibilitas, paternitas, filiatio, communis spiratio et processio. Harum autem tantum quatuor sunt relationes, nam innascibilitas non est relatio nisi per reductionem, ut infra dicetur. Quatuor autem tantum proprietates sunt, nam communis spiratio non est proprietas, quia convenit duabus personis. Tres autem sunt notiones personales, idest constituentes personas, scilicet paternitas, filiatio et processio, nam communis spiratio et innascibilitas dicuntur notiones personarum, non autem personales, ut infra magis patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0063","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0064","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod praeter quatuor relationes oportet ponere aliam notionem, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0065","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod essentia in divinis significatur ut res quaedam; et similiter personae significantur ut res quaedam sed notiones significantur ut rationes notificantes personas. Et ideo, licet dicatur Deus unus propter unitatem essentiae, et trinus propter Trinitatem personarum; non tamen dicitur quinus propter quinque notiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0067","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum sola oppositio relativa faciat pluralitatem realem in divinis, plures proprietates unius personae, cum non opponantur ad invicem relative, non differunt realiter. Nec tamen de invicem praedicantur, quia significantur ut diversae rationes personarum. Sicut etiam non dicimus quod attributum potentiae sit attributum scientiae, licet dicamus quod scientia sit potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0069","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0070","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, cum persona importet dignitatem, ut supra dictum est, non potest accipi notio aliqua spiritus sancti ex hoc quod nulla persona est ab ipso. Hoc enim non pertinet ad dignitatem ipsius; sicut pertinet ad auctoritatem patris quod sit a nullo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0071","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0072","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod filius et spiritus sanctus non conveniunt in uno speciali modo existendi a patre; sicut pater et filius conveniunt in uno speciali modo producendi spiritum sanctum. Id autem quod est principium innotescendi, oportet esse aliquid speciale. Et ideo non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0073","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0074","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0075","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non liceat contrarie opinari de notionibus. Dicit enim Augustinus, in I de Trin., quod non erratur alicubi periculosius quam in materia Trinitatis, ad quam certum est notiones pertinere. Sed contrariae opiniones non possunt esse absque errore. Ergo contrarie opinari circa notiones non licet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0076","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0077","text":"Praeterea, per notiones cognoscuntur personae, ut dictum est. Sed circa personas non licet contrarie opinari. Ergo nec circa notiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0078","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0079","text":"Sed contra, articuli fidei non sunt de notionibus. Ergo circa notiones licet sic vel aliter opinari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0080","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ad fidem pertinet aliquid dupliciter. Uno modo, directe; sicut ea quae nobis sunt principaliter divinitus tradita, ut Deum esse trinum et unum, filium Dei esse incarnatum, et huiusmodi. Et circa haec opinari falsum, hoc ipso inducit haeresim, maxime si pertinacia adiungatur. Indirecte vero ad fidem pertinent ea ex quibus consequitur aliquid contrarium fidei; sicut si quis diceret Samuelem non fuisse filium Elcanae; ex hoc enim sequitur Scripturam divinam esse falsam. Circa huiusmodi ergo absque periculo haeresis aliquis falsum potest opinari, antequam consideretur, vel determinatum sit, quod ex hoc sequitur aliquid contrarium fidei, et maxime si non pertinaciter adhaereat. Sed postquam manifestum est, et praecipue si sit per Ecclesiam determinatum, quod ex hoc sequitur aliquid contrarium fidei, in hoc errare non esset absque haeresi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","text":"Et propter hoc, multa nunc reputantur haeretica, quae prius non reputabantur, propter hoc quod nunc est magis manifestum quid ex eis sequatur. Sic igitur dicendum est quod circa notiones aliqui absque periculo haeresis contrarie sunt opinati, non intendentes sustinere aliquid contrarium fidei. Sed si quis falsum opinaretur circa notiones, considerans quod ex hoc sequatur aliquid contrarium fidei, in haeresim laberetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0083","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0084","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d61f38b8ff8de1ea0757e8743f8ecc28682786c1bbe849d60f1148b690abce67","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141406890708Z_a74f7e46","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 32","source_sha256":"d61f38b8ff8de1ea0757e8743f8ecc28682786c1bbe849d60f1148b690abce67","sha256":"656bf49d6be2cff9279890c26f47318d8b06ca0a84960b88741c06529aba44d9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:14:06.890728+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0002","text":"[I q. 32 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0003","text":"Next, inquiry must be made concerning the knowledge of the divine persons. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether the divine persons can be known by natural reason. Second, whether there are any notions to be attributed to the divine persons. Third, concerning the number of notions. Fourth, whether it is permissible to hold different opinions concerning the notions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0005","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0006","text":"We proceed to the first as follows. It seems that the Trinity of divine persons can be known by natural reason. For philosophers did not arrive at the knowledge of God except by natural reason; yet many things are found to have been said by philosophers concerning the Trinity of persons. For Aristotle says, in I *On Heaven and the World*, “by this number, namely the ternary, we have employed ourselves to magnify the one God, surpassing in the properties of created things.” Augustine also says, *Confessions* VII: “there I read,” namely, in the books of the Platonists, “not indeed in these words, but that selfsame thing altogether, commended by many and manifold arguments: that in the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God,” and such things as follow there, in which words the distinction of the divine persons is conveyed. It is also said in the Gloss on Rom. I and Exod.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0007","text":"VIII, that Pharaoh's magicians failed at the third sign, that is, in knowledge of the third person, namely the Holy Spirit; and thus they knew at least two. Trismegistus also said, “the monad begot the monad, and reflected its own ardor upon itself,” by which the generation of the Son and the procession of the Holy Spirit seem to be intimated. Therefore, knowledge of the divine persons can be had by natural reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0008","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0009","text":"Further, Richard of Saint Victor says, in the book *On the Trinity*, “I believe without doubt that, for whatever explanation of truth, arguments not merely probable but even necessary will not be lacking.” Hence also, to prove the Trinity of persons, some have adduced an argument from the infinity of divine goodness, which communicates itself infinitely in the procession of the divine persons. Others, however, from the fact that “the possession of no good can be pleasant without companionship.” Augustine, moreover, proceeds to make manifest the Trinity of persons from the procession of word and love in our mind, a path which we have followed above. Therefore, the Trinity of persons can be known by natural reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0010","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0011","text":"Further, it seems superfluous to impart to man what cannot be known by human reason. But it must not be said that the divine teaching concerning the knowledge of the Trinity is superfluous. Therefore, the Trinity of persons can be known by human reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0012","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0013","text":"On the contrary, Hilary says, in Book II *On the Trinity*, “let man not think that by his own understanding he can attain the mystery of generation.” Ambrose also says, “it is impossible to know the secret of generation; the mind fails, the voice is silent.” But the Trinity is distinguished in the divine persons through the origin of generation and procession, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore, since man cannot know or attain by understanding that for which a necessary argument cannot be had, it follows that the Trinity of persons cannot be known by reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0014","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible to arrive at knowledge of the Trinity of divine persons by natural reason. For it was shown above that man cannot arrive at the knowledge of God by natural reason except from creatures. But creatures lead to the knowledge of God as effects to a cause. Therefore, by natural reason only that can be known of God which must belong to Him insofar as He is the principle of all beings; and we have employed this foundation above in the consideration of God. But God's creative power is common to the whole Trinity; hence it pertains to the unity of essence, not to the distinction of persons. Therefore, by natural reason those things concerning God which pertain to the unity of essence can be known, but not those which pertain to the distinction of persons. But whoever endeavors to prove the Trinity of persons by natural reason detracts from faith in two ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","text":"First, indeed, as to the dignity of faith itself, which is that it concerns invisible things that exceed human reason. Hence the Apostle says, in Heb. XI, that faith concerns things not appearing. And the Apostle says, I Cor. II, “we speak wisdom among the perfect; yet wisdom not of this age, nor of the rulers of this age; but we speak the wisdom of God in a mystery, which is hidden.” Second, as to the usefulness of drawing others to the faith. For when someone, in proving the faith, adduces arguments that are not compelling, he yields to the mockery of unbelievers; for they believe that we rely on such arguments and believe because of them. Therefore, matters of faith should not be attempted to be proved except by authorities, for those who accept authorities. But among others, it suffices to defend that what faith preaches is not impossible. Hence Dionysius says, chapter II of *On the Divine*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0017","text":"*Names*, “if there is anyone who wholly resists the sayings, he will be far from our philosophy; but if he looks to the truth of the sayings,” namely, the sacred sayings, “we too employ this as our canon.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0018","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the philosophers did not know the mystery of the Trinity of divine persons through the proper things, which are paternity, filiation, and procession; according to the saying of the Apostle, I Cor. II: “we speak the wisdom of God, which none of the rulers of this age knew,” that is, none of the philosophers, according to the Gloss. Yet they knew certain essential attributes which are appropriated to the persons, such as power to the Father, wisdom to the Son, and goodness to the Holy Spirit, as will appear below. Therefore, what Aristotle says, “by this number we have employed ourselves,” etc., is not to be understood as though he posited the ternary number in divine things; rather, he means that the ancients used the ternary number in sacrifices and prayers, on account of a certain perfection of the ternary number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0020","text":"In the books of the Platonists likewise is found “in the beginning was the true,” not insofar as “word” signifies a begotten person in divine things, but insofar as by “word” is understood the ideal reason through which God created all things, which is appropriated to the Son. And although they knew things appropriated to the three persons, they are nevertheless said to have failed at the third sign, that is, in knowledge of the third person, because they departed from goodness, which is appropriated to the Holy Spirit, when, knowing God, they did not glorify Him as God, as is said in Rom. I. Or, because the Platonists posited one first being, which they also called the father of the whole universe of things, they consequently posited another substance beneath it, which they called mind or paternal intellect, in which were the reasons of all things, as Macrobius recounts in his commentary on the *Dream of Scipio*; but they did not posit any third separate substance that might seem to correspond to the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0021","text":"But we do not posit Father and Son as differing in substance; rather, this was the error of Origen and Arius, who in this followed the Platonists. But what Trismegistus said, “the monad begot the monad, and reflected its own ardor upon itself,” is not to be referred to the generation of the Son or the procession of the Holy Spirit, but to the production of the world; for the one God produced one world out of love for Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0022","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","text":"To the second it must be said that an argument is adduced for some matter in two ways. In one way, to prove sufficiently some principle, as in natural science a sufficient argument is adduced to prove that the motion of the heaven is always of uniform speed. In another way, an argument is adduced not which sufficiently proves the principle, but which shows effects consequent upon an already posited principle to be fitting; as in astronomy an argument from eccentrics and epicycles is posited from the fact that, this position having been made, the sensible appearances concerning celestial motions can be saved. Yet this argument is not sufficiently probative, since perhaps they could also be saved if another position were made. Therefore, in the first way an argument can be adduced to prove that God is one, and similar things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","text":"But the argument adduced for the manifestation of the Trinity is of the second kind: namely, that, once the Trinity is posited, arguments of this sort are fitting; yet not such that the Trinity of persons is sufficiently proved through these arguments. And this is clear in each case. For the infinite goodness of God is manifested also in the production of creatures, because it belongs to infinite power to produce from nothing. For it is not necessary that, if He communicates Himself by infinite goodness, something infinite should proceed from God; rather, it receives the divine goodness according to its own mode. Likewise, too, what is said—that “without companionship the possession of any good cannot be pleasant”—holds when perfect goodness is not found in one person; hence such a person needs, for the full goodness of delight, the good of some other joined to him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0025","text":"But the likeness of our intellect does not sufficiently prove anything about God, because intellect is not found univocally in God and in us. And hence Augustine, on John, says that through faith one comes to knowledge, and not conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0026","text":"[I q. 32 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","text":"To the third, it must be said that knowledge of the divine persons was necessary for us in two ways. In one way, for thinking rightly about the creation of things. For by saying that God made all things by his Word, the error of those who maintain that God produced things from necessity of nature is excluded. But by positing in him a procession of love, it is shown that God produced creatures not from any need, nor on account of any other extrinsic cause, but because of love for his goodness. Hence Moses also, after he had said, in the beginning God created heaven and earth, adds, God said, let light be made, for the manifestation of the divine Word; and afterwards he said, God saw the light, that it was good, to show the approval of divine love; and similarly in the other works. In another way, and more principally, for thinking rightly about the salvation of the human race, which is accomplished through the incarnate Son and through the gift of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0028","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0029","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0030","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that notions should not be posited in the divine. For Dionysius says, in chapter 1 of *On the Divine Names*, that *one must not dare to say anything concerning God beyond those things which have been expressed to us in the sacred utterances*. But no mention of notions is made in the utterances of Sacred Scripture. Therefore notions should not be posited in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0031","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0032","text":"Further, whatever is posited in the divine either pertains to the unity of essence or to the Trinity of persons. But notions pertain neither to the unity of essence nor to the Trinity of persons. For neither are those things which belong to essence predicated of notions—for we do not say that paternity is wise or creates—nor even those things which belong to person—for we do not say that paternity begets and filiation is begotten. Therefore notions should not be posited in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0033","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0034","text":"Further, in simple things no abstract terms that are principles of knowing should be posited, because they are known through themselves. But the divine persons are most simple. Therefore notions should not be posited in the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0035","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0036","text":"On the contrary, John Damascene says: “We recognize the difference of the hypostases, that is, of the persons, in three properties, namely paternal, filial, and processional.” Therefore properties and notions must be posited in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0037","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","text":"I answer that Praepositivus, considering the simplicity of the persons, said that properties and notions should not be posited in the divine; and wherever they are found, he explains the abstract in place of the concrete. For just as we are accustomed to say, I ask your kindness, that is, you who are kind, so when paternity is spoken of in the divine, God the Father is understood. But, as was shown above, it does not prejudice divine simplicity that in the divine we use concrete and abstract names. For as we understand, so we name. But our intellect cannot attain to the divine simplicity itself, according as it is to be considered in itself; and therefore it apprehends and names divine things according to its own mode, that is, according to what is found in sensible things, from which it receives knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","text":"In these, in order to signify simple forms, we use abstract names; but in order to signify subsisting things, we use concrete names. Hence, as was said above, we signify divine things by abstract names on account of simplicity, but by concrete names on account of subsistence and completeness. Yet it is necessary to signify not only essential names in the abstract and concrete, as when we say deity and God, or wisdom and wise; but also personal names, so that we may say paternity and father. Two things especially compel us to this. First, indeed, the insistence of heretics. For since we confess that Father and Son and Holy Spirit are one God and three persons, when those asking by what they are one God and by what they are three persons are answered that they are one by essence or deity, so it was necessary that there be some abstract names by which it could be answered that the persons are distinguished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","text":"And such are the properties or notions signified abstractly, such as paternity and filiation. And therefore essence is signified in the divine as what, but person as who, and property as by what. Second, because one person is found in the divine to be related to two persons, namely, the person of the Father to the person of the Son and the person of the Holy Spirit. But not by one relation; for thus it would follow that the Son and the Holy Spirit also would be related to the Father by one and the same relation; and thus, since relation alone in the divine multiplies the Trinity, it would follow that the Son and the Holy Spirit were not two persons. Nor can it be said, as Praepositivus said, that just as God is related in one way to creatures, although creatures are related to him in diverse ways, so the Father is related by one relation to the Son and to the Holy Spirit, although those two are related to the Father by two relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0041","text":"For since the specific account of a relative consists in this, that it is related to another, it is necessary to say that two relations are not diverse in species if, conversely, one relation corresponds to them; for the relation of lord and that of father must be of another species, according to the diversity of filiation and servitude. But all creatures are related to God under one species of relation, insofar as they are his creatures; whereas the Son and the Holy Spirit are not related to the Father according to relations of one account. Hence there is no likeness. And again, a real relation to the creature is not required in God, as was said above; but it is not unfitting that relations of reason be multiplied in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0042","text":"But in the Father there must be a real relation by which he is related to the Son and the Holy Spirit. Hence, corresponding to the two relations of the Son and the Holy Spirit by which they are related to the Father, two relations must be understood in the Father, by which he is related to the Son and the Holy Spirit. Therefore, since there is only one person of the Father, it was necessary that the relations be separately signified in the abstract, and these are called properties and notions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0043","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0044","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although no mention of notions is made in Sacred Scripture, mention is nevertheless made of persons, in which notions are understood, as the abstract in the concrete.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0045","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","text":"To the second, it must be said that notions are signified in the divine not as things, but as certain accounts by which the persons are known; although the notions or relations themselves are really in God, as was said above. And therefore those things that have some order to some essential or personal act cannot be said of notions, because this is incompatible with their mode of signification. Hence we cannot say that paternity begets or creates, or is wise or understanding. But essential things that have no order to any act, but remove creaturely conditions from God, can be predicated of notions; for we can say that paternity is eternal or immense, or anything of this kind. And similarly, on account of identity of thing, personal and essential substantives can be predicated of notions; for we can say that paternity is God, and paternity is Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0047","text":"[I q. 32 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0048","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although the persons are simple, nevertheless, without prejudice to simplicity, the proper accounts of the persons can be signified abstractly, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0049","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0050","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0051","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that there are not five notions. For the proper notions of the persons are the relations by which they are distinguished. But there are only four relations in the divine, as stated above. Therefore there are only four notions as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0052","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0053","text":"Further, because there is one essence in the divine, God is called one; but because there are three persons, God is called threefold. Therefore, if there are five notions in the divine, he will be called fivefold, which is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0054","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0055","text":"Further, if, there being three persons in the divine, there are five notions, it must be that in some of the persons there are two or more notions; just as in the person of the Father unbegottenness, paternity, and common spiration are posited. Therefore either these three notions differ in reality, or they do not. If they differ in reality, it follows that the person of the Father is composed of several things. But if they differ only in reason, it follows that one of them can be predicated of another, so that we may say that, just as divine goodness is his wisdom on account of the indifference of the thing, so common spiration is paternity—which is not conceded. Therefore there are not five notions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0056","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0057","text":"But on the contrary, it seems that there are more. For just as the Father is from no one, and according to this the notion called unbegottenness is taken, so no other person is from the Holy Spirit. And according to this a sixth notion would have to be taken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0058","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0059","text":"Further, just as it is common to the Father and the Son that the Holy Spirit proceeds from them, so it is common to the Son and the Holy Spirit that they proceed from the Father. Therefore, just as one notion is posited as common to the Father and the Son, so one notion ought to be posited as common to the Son and the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0060","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","text":"I answer that a notion is called that which is the proper intelligible account for knowing a divine person. Now the divine persons are multiplied according to origin. But to origin pertain that from which another is, and that which is from another; and a person can be made known according to these two modes. Therefore the person of the Father cannot be made known by the fact that he is from another, but by the fact that he is from no one; and thus, on this side, his notion is unbegottenness. But insofar as someone is from him, he is made known in two ways. For insofar as the Son is from him, he is made known by the notion of paternity; but insofar as the Holy Spirit is from him, he is made known by the notion of common spiration. The Son, however, can be made known by the fact that he is from another by being born, and thus he is made known through filiation; and by the fact that another is from him, namely the Holy Spirit, and thereby he is made known in the same way as the Father, namely by common spiration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","text":"But the Holy Spirit can be made known by the fact that he is from another or from others, and thus he is made known by procession. But not by the fact that another is from him, because no divine person proceeds from him. Therefore there are five notions in the divine: namely, unbegottenness, paternity, filiation, common spiration, and procession. Of these, however, only four are relations, for unbegottenness is not a relation except by reduction, as will be said below. But there are only four properties, for common spiration is not a property, because it belongs to two persons. But there are three personal notions, that is, notions constituting persons: namely, paternity, filiation, and procession. For common spiration and unbegottenness are called notions of the persons, but not personal notions, as will become clearer below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0063","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0064","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that besides the four relations another notion must be posited, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0065","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","text":"To the second, it must be said that essence in the divine is signified as a certain thing; and likewise the persons are signified as certain things, but the notions are signified as intelligible accounts making the persons known. And therefore, although God is called one on account of the unity of essence, and threefold on account of the Trinity of persons, he is nevertheless not called fivefold on account of the five notions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0067","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","text":"To the third, it must be said that, since relative opposition alone produces real plurality in the divine, several properties of one person, since they are not relatively opposed to one another, do not differ really. Yet they are not predicated of one another, because they are signified as diverse intelligible accounts of the persons. Thus neither do we say that the attribute of power is the attribute of knowledge, although we say that knowledge is power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0069","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0070","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, since person imports dignity, as was said above, no notion of the Holy Spirit can be taken from the fact that no person is from him. For this does not pertain to his dignity, just as it pertains to the authority of the Father that he is from no one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0071","text":"[I q. 32 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0072","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the Son and the Holy Spirit do not agree in one special mode of existing from the Father, as the Father and the Son agree in one special mode of producing the Holy Spirit. But that which is a principle of being made known must be something special. And therefore the cases are not alike.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0073","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0074","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0075","text":"We proceed to the fourth as follows. It seems that it is not permissible to hold contrary opinions concerning the notions. For Augustine says, in Book I of *On the Trinity*, that nowhere does one err more dangerously than in the matter of the Trinity, to which the notions certainly pertain. But contrary opinions cannot exist without error. Therefore, it is not permissible to hold contrary opinions concerning the notions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0076","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0077","text":"Further, the persons are known through the notions, as has been said. But it is not permissible to hold contrary opinions concerning the persons. Therefore, neither is it permissible concerning the notions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0078","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0079","text":"On the contrary, the articles of faith are not about the notions. Therefore, concerning the notions, it is permissible to hold an opinion either in this way or otherwise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0080","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","text":"I answer that something pertains to faith in two ways. In one way, directly: as those things principally handed down to us by God, such as that God is three and one, that the Son of God was incarnate, and the like. And to hold a false opinion concerning these itself entails heresy, especially if obstinacy is joined to it. But things pertain to faith indirectly from which something contrary to faith follows; as if someone were to say that Samuel was not the son of Elkanah, for from this it follows that divine Scripture is false. Therefore, concerning such matters, someone can hold a false opinion without danger of heresy, before it is considered or determined that from this something contrary to faith follows, especially if he does not adhere obstinately. But after it is evident, and especially if it has been determined by the Church, that from this something contrary to faith follows, to err in this would not be without heresy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","text":"And because of this, many things are now considered heretical which were not previously considered so, because it is now more evident what follows from them. Thus, then, it must be said that some have held contrary opinions concerning the notions without danger of heresy, not intending to maintain anything contrary to faith. But if someone were to hold a false opinion concerning the notions, considering that something contrary to faith follows from this, he would fall into heresy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0083","text":"[I q. 32 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0084","text":"And through this the response to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0084"]}],"source_digest":"b6b5b5d2c1f89ca19c669be87cd81542f52caedc6e097a13f23603750c8bce53","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 32"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 32","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25","title":"First Part · Question 33","order":34,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_25","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 33 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29804)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de personis in speciali. Et primo de persona patris. Circa quam quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum patri competat esse principium. Secundo, utrum persona patris proprie significetur hoc nomine pater. Tertio, utrum per prius dicatur in divinis pater secundum quod sumitur personaliter, quam secundum quod sumitur essentialiter. Quarto, utrum sit proprium patri esse ingenitum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29805)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pater non possit dici principium filii vel spiritus sancti. Principium enim et causa idem sunt, secundum philosophum. Sed non dicimus patrem esse causam filii. Ergo non debet dici quod sit eius principium.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29806)\n\nPraeterea, principium dicitur respectu principiati. Si igitur pater est principium filii, sequitur filium esse principiatum, et per consequens esse creatum. Quod videtur esse erroneum.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29807)\n\nPraeterea, nomen principii a prioritate sumitur. Sed in divinis non est prius et posterius, ut Athanasius dicit. Ergo in divinis non debemus uti nomine principii.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29808)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in IV de Trin., *pater est principium totius deitatis*.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29809)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod hoc nomen principium nihil aliud significat quam id a quo aliquid procedit, omne enim a quo aliquid procedit quocumque modo, dicimus esse principium; et e converso. Cum ergo pater sit a quo procedit alius, sequitur quod pater est principium.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29810)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Graeci utuntur in divinis indifferenter nomine causae, sicut et nomine principii, sed Latini doctores non utuntur nomine causae, sed solum nomine principii. Cuius ratio est, quia principium communius est quam causa, sicut causa communius quam elementum, primus enim terminus, vel etiam prima pars rei dicitur principium, sed non causa. Quanto autem aliquod nomen est communius, tanto convenientius assumitur in divinis, ut supra dictum est, quia nomina, quanto magis specialia sunt, tanto magis determinant modum convenientem creaturae. Unde hoc nomen causa videtur importare diversitatem substantiae, et dependentiam alicuius ab altero; quam non importat nomen principii. In omnibus enim causae generibus, semper invenitur distantia inter causam et id cuius est causa, secundum aliquam perfectionem aut virtutem. Sed nomine principii utimur etiam in his quae nullam huiusmodi differentiam habent, sed solum secundum quendam ordinem, sicut cum dicimus punctum esse principium lineae, vel etiam cum dicimus primam partem lineae esse principium lineae.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29811)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod apud Graecos invenitur de filio vel spiritu sancto dici quod principientur. Sed hoc non est in usu doctorum nostrorum. Quia licet attribuamus patri aliquid auctoritatis ratione principii, nihil tamen ad subiectionem vel minorationem quocumque modo pertinens, attribuimus filio vel spiritui sancto, ut vitetur omnis erroris occasio. Secundum quem modum Hilarius dicit, IX de Trin., *donantis auctoritate pater maior est; sed minor non est filius, cui unum esse donatur*.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29812)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet hoc nomen principium, quantum ad id a quo imponitur ad significandum, videatur a prioritate sumptum; non tamen significat prioritatem, sed originem. Non enim idem est quod significat nomen, et a quo nomen imponitur, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29813)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen pater non sit proprie nomen divinae personae. Hoc enim nomen pater significat relationem. Persona autem est substantia individua. Non ergo hoc nomen pater est proprie nomen significativum personae.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29814)\n\nPraeterea, generans communius est quam pater, nam omnis pater est generans, sed non e converso. Sed nomen communius magis proprie dicitur in divinis, ut dictum est. Ergo magis proprium nomen est personae divinae generans et genitor, quam pater.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29815)\n\nPraeterea, nihil quod secundum metaphoram dicitur, potest esse nomen proprium alicuius. Sed verbum metaphorice apud nos dicitur genitum vel proles, et per consequens ille cuius est verbum, metaphorice dicitur pater. Non ergo principium verbi in divinis potest proprie dici pater.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29816)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod proprie dicitur in divinis, per prius dicitur de Deo quam de creaturis. Sed generatio per prius videtur dici de creaturis quam de Deo, verior enim ibi videtur esse generatio, ubi aliquid procedit ab alio distinctum non secundum relationem tantum, sed etiam secundum essentiam. Ergo nomen patris, quod a generatione sumitur, non videtur esse proprium alicuius divinae personae.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29817)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo, *ipse invocabit me, pater meus es tu*.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29818)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nomen proprium cuiuslibet personae significat id per quod illa persona distinguitur ab omnibus aliis. Sicut enim de ratione hominis est anima et corpus, ita de intellectu huius hominis est haec anima et hoc corpus, ut dicitur in VII Metaphys.; his autem hic homo ab omnibus aliis distinguitur. Id autem per quod distinguitur persona patris ab omnibus aliis, est paternitas. Unde proprium nomen personae patris est hoc nomen pater, quod significat paternitatem.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29819)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod apud nos relatio non est subsistens persona, et ideo hoc nomen pater, apud nos, non significat personam, sed relationem personae. Non autem est ita in divinis, ut quidam falso opinati sunt, nam relatio quam significat hoc nomen pater, est subsistens persona. Unde supra dictum est quod hoc nomen persona in divinis significat relationem ut subsistentem in divina natura.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29820)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, in II de anima, denominatio rei maxime debet fieri a perfectione et fine. Generatio autem significat ut in fieri, sed paternitas significat complementum generationis. Et ideo potius est nomen divinae personae pater, quam generans vel genitor.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29821)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod verbum non est aliquid subsistens in natura humana, unde non proprie potest dici genitum vel filius. Sed verbum divinum est aliquid subsistens in natura divina, unde proprie, et non metaphorice, dicitur filius, et eius principium, pater.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29822)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod nomen generationis et paternitatis, sicut et alia nomina quae proprie dicuntur in divinis, per prius dicuntur de Deo quam de creaturis, quantum ad rem significatam, licet non quantum ad modum significandi. Unde et apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. III, *flecto genua mea ad patrem domini nostri Iesu Christi, ex quo omnis paternitas in caelo et in terra nominatur*. Quod sic apparet. Manifestum est enim quod generatio accipit speciem a termino, qui est forma generati. Et quanto haec fuerit propinquior formae generantis, tanto verior et perfectior est generatio; sicut generatio univoca est perfectior quam non univoca, nam de ratione generantis est, quod generet sibi simile secundum formam. Unde hoc ipsum quod in generatione divina est eadem numero forma generantis et geniti, in rebus autem creatis non est eadem numero, sed specie tantum, ostendit quod generatio, et per consequens paternitas, per prius sit in Deo quam in creaturis. Unde hoc ipsum quod in divinis est distinctio geniti a generante secundum relationem tantum, ad veritatem divinae generationis et paternitatis pertinet.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29823)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen pater non dicatur in divinis per prius secundum quod personaliter sumitur. Commune enim, secundum intellectum, est prius proprio. Sed hoc nomen pater, secundum quod personaliter sumitur, est proprium personae patris, secundum vero quod sumitur essentialiter est commune toti Trinitati, nam toti Trinitati dicimus pater noster. Ergo per prius dicitur pater essentialiter sumptum, quam personaliter.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29824)\n\nPraeterea, in his quae sunt eiusdem rationis, non est praedicatio per prius et posterius. Sed paternitas et filiatio secundum unam rationem videntur dici secundum quod persona divina est pater filii, et secundum quod tota Trinitas est pater noster vel creaturae, cum, secundum Basilium, accipere sit commune creaturae et filio. Ergo non per prius dicitur pater in divinis secundum quod sumitur essentialiter, quam secundum quod sumitur personaliter.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29825)\n\nPraeterea, inter ea quae non dicuntur secundum rationem unam, non potest esse comparatio. Sed filius comparatur creaturae in ratione filiationis vel generationis, secundum illud Coloss. I, *qui est imago Dei invisibilis, primogenitus omnis creaturae*. Ergo non per prius dicitur in divinis paternitas personaliter sumpta, quam essentialiter; sed secundum rationem eandem.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29826)\n\nSed contra est quod aeternum prius est temporali. Ab aeterno autem Deus est pater filii, ex tempore autem pater est creaturae. Ergo per prius dicitur paternitas in Deo respectu filii, quam respectu creaturae.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29827)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod per prius dicitur nomen de illo in quo salvatur tota ratio nominis perfecte, quam de illo in quo salvatur secundum aliquid, de hoc enim dicitur quasi per similitudinem ad id in quo perfecte salvatur, quia omnia imperfecta sumuntur a perfectis. Et inde est quod hoc nomen leo per prius dicitur de animali in quo tota ratio leonis salvatur, quod proprie dicitur leo, quam de aliquo homine in quo invenitur aliquid de ratione leonis, ut puta audacia vel fortitudo, vel aliquid huiusmodi, de hoc enim per similitudinem dicitur. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod perfecta ratio paternitatis et filiationis invenitur in Deo patre et Deo filio, quia patris et filii una est natura et gloria. Sed in creatura filiatio invenitur respectu Dei, non secundum perfectam rationem, cum non sit una natura creatoris et creaturae; sed secundum aliqualem similitudinem. Quae quanto perfectior fuerit, tanto propinquius acceditur ad veram filiationis rationem. Dicitur enim Deus alicuius creaturae pater, propter similitudinem vestigii tantum, utpote irrationalium creaturarum; secundum illud Iob XXXVIII, *quis est pluviae pater? Aut quis genuit stillas roris?* Alicuius vero creaturae, scilicet rationalis, secundum similitudinem imaginis; secundum illud Deut. XXXII, *nonne ipse est pater tuus, qui possedit et fecit et creavit te?* Aliquorum vero est pater secundum similitudinem gratiae, qui etiam dicuntur filii adoptivi, secundum quod ordinantur ad haereditatem aeternae gloriae per munus gratiae acceptum; secundum illud Rom. VIII, *ipse spiritus reddit testimonium spiritui nostro, quod sumus filii Dei; si autem filii, et haeredes*. Aliquorum vero secundum similitudinem gloriae, prout iam gloriae haereditatem possident; secundum illud Rom. V, *gloriamur in spe gloriae filiorum Dei*. Sic igitur patet quod per prius paternitas dicitur in divinis secundum quod importatur respectus personae ad personam, quam secundum quod importatur respectus Dei ad creaturam.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29828)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod communia absolute dicta, secundum ordinem intellectus nostri, sunt priora quam propria, quia includuntur in intellectu propriorum, sed non e converso; in intellectu enim personae patris intelligitur Deus, sed non convertitur. Sed communia quae important respectum ad creaturam, per posterius dicuntur quam propria quae important respectus personales, quia persona procedens in divinis, procedit ut principium productionis creaturarum. Sicut enim verbum conceptum in mente artificis, per prius intelligitur procedere ab artifice quam artificiatum, quod producitur ad similitudinem verbi concepti in mente; ita per prius procedit filius a patre quam creatura, de qua nomen filiationis dicitur secundum quod aliquid participat de similitudine filii; ut patet per illud quod dicitur Rom. VIII, *quos praescivit, et praedestinavit fieri conformes imaginis filii eius*.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29829)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod accipere dicitur esse commune creaturae et filio, non secundum univocationem, sed secundum similitudinem quandam remotam, ratione cuius dicitur primogenitus creaturae. Unde in auctoritate inducta subditur, ut sit ipse primogenitus in multis fratribus, postquam dixerat conformes fieri aliquos imaginis filii Dei. Sed filius Dei naturaliter habet quoddam singulare prae aliis, scilicet habere per naturam id quod accipit; ut idem Basilius dicit. Et secundum hoc dicitur unigenitus, ut patet Ioan. I, *unigenitus, qui est in sinu patris, ipse nobis enarravit*.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29830)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad tertium.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29831)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse ingenitum non sit patri proprium. Omnis enim proprietas ponit aliquid in eo cuius est proprietas. Sed ingenitus nihil ponit in patre, sed removet tantum. Ergo non significat proprietatem patris.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29832)\n\nPraeterea, ingenitum aut dicitur privative, aut negative. Si negative, tunc quidquid non est genitum, potest dici ingenitum. Sed spiritus sanctus non est genitus, neque etiam essentia divina. Ergo ingenitum etiam eis convenit, et sic non est proprium patri. Si autem privative sumatur, cum omnis privatio significet imperfectionem in privato, sequitur quod persona patris sit imperfecta. Quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29833)\n\nPraeterea, ingenitus in divinis non significat relationem, quia non dicitur relative, significat ergo substantiam. Ingenitus igitur et genitus secundum substantiam differunt. Filius autem, qui est genitus, non differt a patre secundum substantiam. Pater ergo non debet dici ingenitus.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29834)\n\nPraeterea, proprium est quod uni soli convenit. Sed cum sint plures ab alio procedentes in divinis, nihil videtur prohibere quin etiam sint plures ab alio non existentes. Non igitur est proprium patri esse ingenitum.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29835)\n\nPraeterea, sicut pater est principium personae genitae, ita et personae procedentis. Si ergo propter oppositionem quam habet ad personam genitam, proprium patris ponitur esse quod sit ingenitus; etiam proprium eius debet poni quod sit improcessibilis.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29836)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Hilarius, IV de Trin.: *est unus ab uno*, scilicet ab ingenito genitus, *proprietate videlicet in unoquoque et innascibilitatis et originis*.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29837)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut in creaturis invenitur principium primum et principium secundum, ita in personis divinis, in quibus non est prius et posterius, invenitur principium non de principio, quod est pater, et principium a principio, quod est filius. In rebus autem creatis aliquod principium primum innotescit dupliciter, uno quidem modo, inquantum est principium primum per hoc quod habet relationem ad ea quae ab ipso sunt; alio modo, inquantum est primum principium per hoc quod non est ab alio. Sic igitur et pater innotescit quidem paternitate et communi spiratione, per respectum ad personas ab eo procedentes, inquantum autem est principium non de principio, innotescit per hoc, quod non est ab alio, quod pertinet ad proprietatem innascibilitatis, quam significat hoc nomen ingenitus.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29838)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod innascibilitas, quam significat hoc nomen ingenitus, secundum quod est proprietas patris, non dicitur tantum negative; sed importat vel utrumque simul, scilicet quod pater a nullo est, et quod est principium aliorum; vel importat universalem auctoritatem; vel etiam fontalem plenitudinem. Sed hoc non videtur verum. Quia sic innascibilitas non esset alia proprietas a paternitate et spiratione, sed includeret eas, sicut includitur proprium in communi, nam fontalitas et auctoritas nihil aliud significant in divinis quam principium originis. Et ideo dicendum est, secundum Augustinum, V de Trin., quod ingenitus negationem generationis passivae importat, dicit enim quod *tantum valet quod dicitur ingenitus, quantum valet quod dicitur non filius*. Nec propter hoc sequitur quod ingenitus non debeat poni propria notio patris, quia prima et simplicia per negationes notificantur; sicut dicimus punctum esse cuius pars non est.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29839)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ingenitum quandoque sumitur negative tantum. Et secundum hoc Hieronymus dicit spiritum sanctum esse ingenitum, idest non genitum. Alio modo potest dici ingenitum aliquo modo privative, non tamen aliquam imperfectionem importat. Multipliciter enim dicitur privatio. Uno modo, quando aliquid non habet quod natum est haberi ab alio, etiamsi ipsum non sit natum habere illud, sicut si lapis dicatur res mortua, quia caret vita, quam quaedam res natae sunt habere. Alio modo dicitur privatio, quando aliquid non habet quod natum est haberi ab aliquo sui generis; sicut si talpa dicatur caeca. Tertio modo, quando ipsum non habet quod natum est habere, et hoc modo privatio imperfectionem importat. Sic autem ingenitum non dicitur privative de patre, sed secundo modo, prout scilicet aliquod suppositum divinae naturae non est genitum, cuius tamen naturae aliquod suppositum est genitum. Sed secundum hanc rationem, etiam de spiritu sancto potest dici ingenitum. Unde ad hoc quod sit proprium soli patri, oportet ulterius in nomine ingeniti intelligere, quod conveniat alicui personae divinae quae sit principium alterius personae; ut sic intelligatur importare negationem in genere principii personaliter dicti in divinis. Vel, ut intelligatur in nomine ingeniti, quod omnino non sit ab alio, et non solum quod non sit ab alio per generationem. Sic enim nec spiritui sancto convenit esse ingenitum, qui est ab alio per processionem ut persona subsistens, nec etiam divinae essentiae, de qua potest dici quod est in filio vel in spiritu sancto ab alio, scilicet a patre.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29840)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, secundum Damascenum, ingenitum uno modo significat idem quod increatum, et sic secundum substantiam dicitur; per hoc enim differt substantia creata ab increata. Alio modo significat id quod non est genitum. Et sic relative dicitur, eo modo quo negatio reducitur ad genus affirmationis, sicut non homo ad genus substantiae, et non album ad genus qualitatis. Unde, cum genitum in divinis relationem importet, ingenitum etiam ad relationem pertinet. Et sic non sequitur quod pater ingenitus distinguatur a filio genito secundum substantiam; sed solum secundum relationem, inquantum scilicet relatio filii negatur de patre.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29841)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut in quolibet genere oportet ponere unum primum, ita in divina natura oportet ponere unum principium quod non sit ab alio, quod ingenitum dicitur. Ponere igitur duos innascibiles, est ponere duos deos, et duas naturas divinas. Unde Hilarius dicit, in libro de synodis, *cum unus Deus sit, duo innascibiles esse non possunt*. Et hoc praecipue quia, si essent duo innascibiles, unus eorum non esset ab alio, et sic non distinguerentur oppositione relativa oporteret igitur quod distinguerentur diversitate naturae.\n\n[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29842)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod proprietas patris prout non est ab alio, potius significatur per remotionem nativitatis filii, quam per remotionem processionis spiritus sancti. Tum quia processio spiritus sancti non habet nomen speciale, ut supra dictum est. Tum quia etiam ordine naturae praesupponit generationem filii. Unde, remoto a patre quod non sit genitus, cum tamen sit principium generationis, sequitur consequenter quod non sit procedens processione spiritus sancti, quia spiritus sanctus non est generationis principium, sed a genito procedens.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0002","text":"[I q. 33 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de personis in speciali. Et primo de persona patris. Circa quam quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum patri competat esse principium. Secundo, utrum persona patris proprie significetur hoc nomine pater. Tertio, utrum per prius dicatur in divinis pater secundum quod sumitur personaliter, quam secundum quod sumitur essentialiter. Quarto, utrum sit proprium patri esse ingenitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0005","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pater non possit dici principium filii vel spiritus sancti. Principium enim et causa idem sunt, secundum philosophum. Sed non dicimus patrem esse causam filii. Ergo non debet dici quod sit eius principium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0007","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0008","text":"Praeterea, principium dicitur respectu principiati. Si igitur pater est principium filii, sequitur filium esse principiatum, et per consequens esse creatum. Quod videtur esse erroneum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0009","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0010","text":"Praeterea, nomen principii a prioritate sumitur. Sed in divinis non est prius et posterius, ut Athanasius dicit. Ergo in divinis non debemus uti nomine principii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0011","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in IV de Trin., *pater est principium totius deitatis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0013","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod hoc nomen principium nihil aliud significat quam id a quo aliquid procedit, omne enim a quo aliquid procedit quocumque modo, dicimus esse principium; et e converso. Cum ergo pater sit a quo procedit alius, sequitur quod pater est principium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0015","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Graeci utuntur in divinis indifferenter nomine causae, sicut et nomine principii, sed Latini doctores non utuntur nomine causae, sed solum nomine principii. Cuius ratio est, quia principium communius est quam causa, sicut causa communius quam elementum, primus enim terminus, vel etiam prima pars rei dicitur principium, sed non causa. Quanto autem aliquod nomen est communius, tanto convenientius assumitur in divinis, ut supra dictum est, quia nomina, quanto magis specialia sunt, tanto magis determinant modum convenientem creaturae. Unde hoc nomen causa videtur importare diversitatem substantiae, et dependentiam alicuius ab altero; quam non importat nomen principii. In omnibus enim causae generibus, semper invenitur distantia inter causam et id cuius est causa, secundum aliquam perfectionem aut virtutem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","text":"Sed nomine principii utimur etiam in his quae nullam huiusmodi differentiam habent, sed solum secundum quendam ordinem, sicut cum dicimus punctum esse principium lineae, vel etiam cum dicimus primam partem lineae esse principium lineae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0018","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod apud Graecos invenitur de filio vel spiritu sancto dici quod principientur. Sed hoc non est in usu doctorum nostrorum. Quia licet attribuamus patri aliquid auctoritatis ratione principii, nihil tamen ad subiectionem vel minorationem quocumque modo pertinens, attribuimus filio vel spiritui sancto, ut vitetur omnis erroris occasio. Secundum quem modum Hilarius dicit, IX de Trin., *donantis auctoritate pater maior est; sed minor non est filius, cui unum esse donatur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0020","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet hoc nomen principium, quantum ad id a quo imponitur ad significandum, videatur a prioritate sumptum; non tamen significat prioritatem, sed originem. Non enim idem est quod significat nomen, et a quo nomen imponitur, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0023","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen pater non sit proprie nomen divinae personae. Hoc enim nomen pater significat relationem. Persona autem est substantia individua. Non ergo hoc nomen pater est proprie nomen significativum personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0025","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0026","text":"Praeterea, generans communius est quam pater, nam omnis pater est generans, sed non e converso. Sed nomen communius magis proprie dicitur in divinis, ut dictum est. Ergo magis proprium nomen est personae divinae generans et genitor, quam pater.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0027","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0028","text":"Praeterea, nihil quod secundum metaphoram dicitur, potest esse nomen proprium alicuius. Sed verbum metaphorice apud nos dicitur genitum vel proles, et per consequens ille cuius est verbum, metaphorice dicitur pater. Non ergo principium verbi in divinis potest proprie dici pater.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0029","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0030","text":"Praeterea, omne quod proprie dicitur in divinis, per prius dicitur de Deo quam de creaturis. Sed generatio per prius videtur dici de creaturis quam de Deo, verior enim ibi videtur esse generatio, ubi aliquid procedit ab alio distinctum non secundum relationem tantum, sed etiam secundum essentiam. Ergo nomen patris, quod a generatione sumitur, non videtur esse proprium alicuius divinae personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0031","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo, *ipse invocabit me, pater meus es tu*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0033","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nomen proprium cuiuslibet personae significat id per quod illa persona distinguitur ab omnibus aliis. Sicut enim de ratione hominis est anima et corpus, ita de intellectu huius hominis est haec anima et hoc corpus, ut dicitur in VII Metaphys.; his autem hic homo ab omnibus aliis distinguitur. Id autem per quod distinguitur persona patris ab omnibus aliis, est paternitas. Unde proprium nomen personae patris est hoc nomen pater, quod significat paternitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0035","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod apud nos relatio non est subsistens persona, et ideo hoc nomen pater, apud nos, non significat personam, sed relationem personae. Non autem est ita in divinis, ut quidam falso opinati sunt, nam relatio quam significat hoc nomen pater, est subsistens persona. Unde supra dictum est quod hoc nomen persona in divinis significat relationem ut subsistentem in divina natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0037","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, in II de anima, denominatio rei maxime debet fieri a perfectione et fine. Generatio autem significat ut in fieri, sed paternitas significat complementum generationis. Et ideo potius est nomen divinae personae pater, quam generans vel genitor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0039","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod verbum non est aliquid subsistens in natura humana, unde non proprie potest dici genitum vel filius. Sed verbum divinum est aliquid subsistens in natura divina, unde proprie, et non metaphorice, dicitur filius, et eius principium, pater.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0041","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod nomen generationis et paternitatis, sicut et alia nomina quae proprie dicuntur in divinis, per prius dicuntur de Deo quam de creaturis, quantum ad rem significatam, licet non quantum ad modum significandi. Unde et apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. III, *flecto genua mea ad patrem domini nostri Iesu Christi, ex quo omnis paternitas in caelo et in terra nominatur*. Quod sic apparet. Manifestum est enim quod generatio accipit speciem a termino, qui est forma generati. Et quanto haec fuerit propinquior formae generantis, tanto verior et perfectior est generatio; sicut generatio univoca est perfectior quam non univoca, nam de ratione generantis est, quod generet sibi simile secundum formam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","text":"Unde hoc ipsum quod in generatione divina est eadem numero forma generantis et geniti, in rebus autem creatis non est eadem numero, sed specie tantum, ostendit quod generatio, et per consequens paternitas, per prius sit in Deo quam in creaturis. Unde hoc ipsum quod in divinis est distinctio geniti a generante secundum relationem tantum, ad veritatem divinae generationis et paternitatis pertinet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0045","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen pater non dicatur in divinis per prius secundum quod personaliter sumitur. Commune enim, secundum intellectum, est prius proprio. Sed hoc nomen pater, secundum quod personaliter sumitur, est proprium personae patris, secundum vero quod sumitur essentialiter est commune toti Trinitati, nam toti Trinitati dicimus pater noster. Ergo per prius dicitur pater essentialiter sumptum, quam personaliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0047","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0048","text":"Praeterea, in his quae sunt eiusdem rationis, non est praedicatio per prius et posterius. Sed paternitas et filiatio secundum unam rationem videntur dici secundum quod persona divina est pater filii, et secundum quod tota Trinitas est pater noster vel creaturae, cum, secundum Basilium, accipere sit commune creaturae et filio. Ergo non per prius dicitur pater in divinis secundum quod sumitur essentialiter, quam secundum quod sumitur personaliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0049","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0050","text":"Praeterea, inter ea quae non dicuntur secundum rationem unam, non potest esse comparatio. Sed filius comparatur creaturae in ratione filiationis vel generationis, secundum illud Coloss. I, *qui est imago Dei invisibilis, primogenitus omnis creaturae*. Ergo non per prius dicitur in divinis paternitas personaliter sumpta, quam essentialiter; sed secundum rationem eandem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0051","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0052","text":"Sed contra est quod aeternum prius est temporali. Ab aeterno autem Deus est pater filii, ex tempore autem pater est creaturae. Ergo per prius dicitur paternitas in Deo respectu filii, quam respectu creaturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0053","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod per prius dicitur nomen de illo in quo salvatur tota ratio nominis perfecte, quam de illo in quo salvatur secundum aliquid, de hoc enim dicitur quasi per similitudinem ad id in quo perfecte salvatur, quia omnia imperfecta sumuntur a perfectis. Et inde est quod hoc nomen leo per prius dicitur de animali in quo tota ratio leonis salvatur, quod proprie dicitur leo, quam de aliquo homine in quo invenitur aliquid de ratione leonis, ut puta audacia vel fortitudo, vel aliquid huiusmodi, de hoc enim per similitudinem dicitur. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod perfecta ratio paternitatis et filiationis invenitur in Deo patre et Deo filio, quia patris et filii una est natura et gloria. Sed in creatura filiatio invenitur respectu Dei, non secundum perfectam rationem, cum non sit una natura creatoris et creaturae; sed secundum aliqualem similitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","text":"Quae quanto perfectior fuerit, tanto propinquius acceditur ad veram filiationis rationem. Dicitur enim Deus alicuius creaturae pater, propter similitudinem vestigii tantum, utpote irrationalium creaturarum; secundum illud Iob XXXVIII, *quis est pluviae pater? Aut quis genuit stillas roris?* Alicuius vero creaturae, scilicet rationalis, secundum similitudinem imaginis; secundum illud Deut. XXXII, *nonne ipse est pater tuus, qui possedit et fecit et creavit te?* Aliquorum vero est pater secundum similitudinem gratiae, qui etiam dicuntur filii adoptivi, secundum quod ordinantur ad haereditatem aeternae gloriae per munus gratiae acceptum; secundum illud Rom. VIII, *ipse spiritus reddit testimonium spiritui nostro, quod sumus filii Dei; si autem filii, et haeredes*. Aliquorum vero secundum similitudinem gloriae, prout iam gloriae haereditatem possident; secundum illud Rom.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","text":"V, *gloriamur in spe gloriae filiorum Dei*. Sic igitur patet quod per prius paternitas dicitur in divinis secundum quod importatur respectus personae ad personam, quam secundum quod importatur respectus Dei ad creaturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0057","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod communia absolute dicta, secundum ordinem intellectus nostri, sunt priora quam propria, quia includuntur in intellectu propriorum, sed non e converso; in intellectu enim personae patris intelligitur Deus, sed non convertitur. Sed communia quae important respectum ad creaturam, per posterius dicuntur quam propria quae important respectus personales, quia persona procedens in divinis, procedit ut principium productionis creaturarum. Sicut enim verbum conceptum in mente artificis, per prius intelligitur procedere ab artifice quam artificiatum, quod producitur ad similitudinem verbi concepti in mente; ita per prius procedit filius a patre quam creatura, de qua nomen filiationis dicitur secundum quod aliquid participat de similitudine filii; ut patet per illud quod dicitur Rom. VIII, *quos praescivit, et praedestinavit fieri conformes imaginis filii eius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0059","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod accipere dicitur esse commune creaturae et filio, non secundum univocationem, sed secundum similitudinem quandam remotam, ratione cuius dicitur primogenitus creaturae. Unde in auctoritate inducta subditur, ut sit ipse primogenitus in multis fratribus, postquam dixerat conformes fieri aliquos imaginis filii Dei. Sed filius Dei naturaliter habet quoddam singulare prae aliis, scilicet habere per naturam id quod accipit; ut idem Basilius dicit. Et secundum hoc dicitur unigenitus, ut patet Ioan. I, *unigenitus, qui est in sinu patris, ipse nobis enarravit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0061","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0062","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad tertium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0063","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0064","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0065","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod esse ingenitum non sit patri proprium. Omnis enim proprietas ponit aliquid in eo cuius est proprietas. Sed ingenitus nihil ponit in patre, sed removet tantum. Ergo non significat proprietatem patris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0066","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0067","text":"Praeterea, ingenitum aut dicitur privative, aut negative. Si negative, tunc quidquid non est genitum, potest dici ingenitum. Sed spiritus sanctus non est genitus, neque etiam essentia divina. Ergo ingenitum etiam eis convenit, et sic non est proprium patri. Si autem privative sumatur, cum omnis privatio significet imperfectionem in privato, sequitur quod persona patris sit imperfecta. Quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0068","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0069","text":"Praeterea, ingenitus in divinis non significat relationem, quia non dicitur relative, significat ergo substantiam. Ingenitus igitur et genitus secundum substantiam differunt. Filius autem, qui est genitus, non differt a patre secundum substantiam. Pater ergo non debet dici ingenitus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0070","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0071","text":"Praeterea, proprium est quod uni soli convenit. Sed cum sint plures ab alio procedentes in divinis, nihil videtur prohibere quin etiam sint plures ab alio non existentes. Non igitur est proprium patri esse ingenitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0072","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0073","text":"Praeterea, sicut pater est principium personae genitae, ita et personae procedentis. Si ergo propter oppositionem quam habet ad personam genitam, proprium patris ponitur esse quod sit ingenitus; etiam proprium eius debet poni quod sit improcessibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0074","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0075","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Hilarius, IV de Trin.: *est unus ab uno*, scilicet ab ingenito genitus, *proprietate videlicet in unoquoque et innascibilitatis et originis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0076","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut in creaturis invenitur principium primum et principium secundum, ita in personis divinis, in quibus non est prius et posterius, invenitur principium non de principio, quod est pater, et principium a principio, quod est filius. In rebus autem creatis aliquod principium primum innotescit dupliciter, uno quidem modo, inquantum est principium primum per hoc quod habet relationem ad ea quae ab ipso sunt; alio modo, inquantum est primum principium per hoc quod non est ab alio. Sic igitur et pater innotescit quidem paternitate et communi spiratione, per respectum ad personas ab eo procedentes, inquantum autem est principium non de principio, innotescit per hoc, quod non est ab alio, quod pertinet ad proprietatem innascibilitatis, quam significat hoc nomen ingenitus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0078","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod innascibilitas, quam significat hoc nomen ingenitus, secundum quod est proprietas patris, non dicitur tantum negative; sed importat vel utrumque simul, scilicet quod pater a nullo est, et quod est principium aliorum; vel importat universalem auctoritatem; vel etiam fontalem plenitudinem. Sed hoc non videtur verum. Quia sic innascibilitas non esset alia proprietas a paternitate et spiratione, sed includeret eas, sicut includitur proprium in communi, nam fontalitas et auctoritas nihil aliud significant in divinis quam principium originis. Et ideo dicendum est, secundum Augustinum, V de Trin., quod ingenitus negationem generationis passivae importat, dicit enim quod *tantum valet quod dicitur ingenitus, quantum valet quod dicitur non filius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","text":"Nec propter hoc sequitur quod ingenitus non debeat poni propria notio patris, quia prima et simplicia per negationes notificantur; sicut dicimus punctum esse cuius pars non est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0081","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0082","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ingenitum quandoque sumitur negative tantum. Et secundum hoc Hieronymus dicit spiritum sanctum esse ingenitum, idest non genitum. Alio modo potest dici ingenitum aliquo modo privative, non tamen aliquam imperfectionem importat. Multipliciter enim dicitur privatio. Uno modo, quando aliquid non habet quod natum est haberi ab alio, etiamsi ipsum non sit natum habere illud, sicut si lapis dicatur res mortua, quia caret vita, quam quaedam res natae sunt habere. Alio modo dicitur privatio, quando aliquid non habet quod natum est haberi ab aliquo sui generis; sicut si talpa dicatur caeca. Tertio modo, quando ipsum non habet quod natum est habere, et hoc modo privatio imperfectionem importat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","text":"Sic autem ingenitum non dicitur privative de patre, sed secundo modo, prout scilicet aliquod suppositum divinae naturae non est genitum, cuius tamen naturae aliquod suppositum est genitum. Sed secundum hanc rationem, etiam de spiritu sancto potest dici ingenitum. Unde ad hoc quod sit proprium soli patri, oportet ulterius in nomine ingeniti intelligere, quod conveniat alicui personae divinae quae sit principium alterius personae; ut sic intelligatur importare negationem in genere principii personaliter dicti in divinis. Vel, ut intelligatur in nomine ingeniti, quod omnino non sit ab alio, et non solum quod non sit ab alio per generationem. Sic enim nec spiritui sancto convenit esse ingenitum, qui est ab alio per processionem ut persona subsistens, nec etiam divinae essentiae, de qua potest dici quod est in filio vel in spiritu sancto ab alio, scilicet a patre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0084","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0085","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, secundum Damascenum, ingenitum uno modo significat idem quod increatum, et sic secundum substantiam dicitur; per hoc enim differt substantia creata ab increata. Alio modo significat id quod non est genitum. Et sic relative dicitur, eo modo quo negatio reducitur ad genus affirmationis, sicut non homo ad genus substantiae, et non album ad genus qualitatis. Unde, cum genitum in divinis relationem importet, ingenitum etiam ad relationem pertinet. Et sic non sequitur quod pater ingenitus distinguatur a filio genito secundum substantiam; sed solum secundum relationem, inquantum scilicet relatio filii negatur de patre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0086","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut in quolibet genere oportet ponere unum primum, ita in divina natura oportet ponere unum principium quod non sit ab alio, quod ingenitum dicitur. Ponere igitur duos innascibiles, est ponere duos deos, et duas naturas divinas. Unde Hilarius dicit, in libro de synodis, *cum unus Deus sit, duo innascibiles esse non possunt*. Et hoc praecipue quia, si essent duo innascibiles, unus eorum non esset ab alio, et sic non distinguerentur oppositione relativa oporteret igitur quod distinguerentur diversitate naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0088","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod proprietas patris prout non est ab alio, potius significatur per remotionem nativitatis filii, quam per remotionem processionis spiritus sancti. Tum quia processio spiritus sancti non habet nomen speciale, ut supra dictum est. Tum quia etiam ordine naturae praesupponit generationem filii. Unde, remoto a patre quod non sit genitus, cum tamen sit principium generationis, sequitur consequenter quod non sit procedens processione spiritus sancti, quia spiritus sanctus non est generationis principium, sed a genito procedens.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"62640d359435dbb07e681195cc81c189ec77d95e42b217bfaabbcb827352b0d1","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141506900797Z_b20deeb5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 33","source_sha256":"62640d359435dbb07e681195cc81c189ec77d95e42b217bfaabbcb827352b0d1","sha256":"1aa4c12a5bea1a281d93c5869316ff54f15ab9cef618c21fc1c93f2562dbbce1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:15:06.900814+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0002","text":"[I q. 33 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0003","text":"Consequently, we must consider the persons in particular. And first, the person of the Father. Concerning whom four questions are asked. First, whether it belongs to the Father to be a principle. Second, whether the person of the Father is properly signified by this name, Father. Third, whether in divine things Father is said primarily insofar as it is taken personally, rather than insofar as it is taken essentially. Fourth, whether it is proper to the Father to be unbegotten.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0005","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0006","text":"We proceed to the first as follows. It seems that the Father cannot be called the principle of the Son or of the Holy Spirit. For principle and cause are the same, according to the Philosopher. But we do not say that the Father is the cause of the Son. Therefore it should not be said that He is His principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0007","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0008","text":"Further, a principle is spoken of in relation to that which is principiated. Therefore, if the Father is the principle of the Son, it follows that the Son is principiated, and consequently created. This seems erroneous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0009","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0010","text":"Further, the name principle is taken from priority. But in divine things there is no before and after, as Athanasius says. Therefore, in divine things we ought not to use the name principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0011","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, IV: *the Father is the principle of the whole Godhead*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0013","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that this name, principle, signifies nothing other than that from which something proceeds; for everything from which something proceeds in whatever way, we call a principle; and conversely. Therefore, since the Father is that from whom another proceeds, it follows that the Father is a principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0015","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Greeks use in divine things the name cause indifferently, just as they do the name principle; but Latin doctors do not use the name cause, but only the name principle. The reason for this is that principle is more common than cause, just as cause is more common than element; for the first terminus, or even the first part of a thing, is called a principle, but not a cause. Now the more common any name is, the more suitably is it taken up in divine things, as was said above, because names, the more particular they are, the more they determine a mode appropriate to creatures. Hence the name cause seems to imply diversity of substance and the dependence of one thing upon another; which the name principle does not imply. For in all kinds of causes there is always found a distance between the cause and that of which it is the cause, according to some perfection or power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","text":"But we use the name principle even of things that have no such difference, but only a certain order, as when we say that a point is the principle of a line, or when we say that the first part of a line is the principle of the line.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0018","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that among the Greeks one finds it said of the Son or the Holy Spirit that they are principiated. But this is not in the usage of our doctors. For although we attribute to the Father a certain authority by reason of principle, nevertheless we attribute nothing to the Son or the Holy Spirit that pertains in any way to subjection or diminution, so that every occasion of error may be avoided. In this manner Hilary says, in *On the Trinity*, IX: *by the authority of the giver, the Father is greater; but the Son, to whom one being is given, is not lesser*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0020","text":"[I q. 33 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although this name principle, as to that from which it is imposed for signifying, seems to be taken from priority, nevertheless it does not signify priority, but origin. For what a name signifies is not the same as that from which the name is imposed, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0023","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0024","text":"We proceed to the second as follows. It seems that this name Father is not properly the name of a divine person. For this name Father signifies a relation. But a person is an individual substance. Therefore this name Father is not properly a name signifying a person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0025","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0026","text":"Moreover, generating is more common than father, for every father is generating, but not conversely. But the more common name is said more properly in divine matters, as has been stated. Therefore, generating and generator are more proper names of the divine person than father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0027","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0028","text":"Moreover, nothing that is said metaphorically can be a proper name of anything. But among us a word is called begotten or offspring metaphorically, and consequently he whose word it is is called father metaphorically. Therefore, the principle of the Word in divine matters cannot properly be called Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0029","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0030","text":"Moreover, everything that is said properly in divine matters is said of God before it is said of creatures. But generation seems to be said of creatures before it is said of God; for generation seems to be truer there, where something proceeds from another as distinct not only according to relation, but also according to essence. Therefore, the name Father, which is taken from generation, does not seem to be proper to any divine person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0031","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0032","text":"But against this is what is said in the Psalm: *he shall call upon me, Thou art my Father*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0033","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","text":"I answer that it must be said that the proper name of any person signifies that by which that person is distinguished from all others. For just as soul and body belong to the account of man, so this soul and this body belong to the understanding of this man, as is said in VII *Metaphysics*; and by these this man is distinguished from all others. Now that by which the person of the Father is distinguished from all others is paternity. Hence the proper name of the person of the Father is this name Father, which signifies paternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0035","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that among us relation is not a subsisting person, and therefore this name father, among us, does not signify a person, but the relation of a person. But it is not so in divine matters, as some have falsely thought; for the relation signified by this name Father is a subsisting person. Hence it was said above that this name person in divine matters signifies a relation as subsisting in the divine nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0037","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","text":"To the second it must be said that, according to the Philosopher, in II *On the Soul*, the naming of a thing ought especially to be made from its perfection and end. But generation signifies as being in the process of becoming, whereas paternity signifies the completion of generation. And therefore Father is more a name of the divine person than generating or generator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0039","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","text":"To the third it must be said that a word is not something subsisting in human nature; hence it cannot properly be called begotten or son. But the divine Word is something subsisting in the divine nature; hence it is properly, and not metaphorically, called Son, and its principle, Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0041","text":"[I q. 33 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the name of generation and paternity, like the other names that are properly said in divine matters, are said of God before they are said of creatures, with respect to the thing signified, though not with respect to the mode of signifying. Hence the Apostle also says, in Ephesians III: *I bend my knees to the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, from whom all paternity in heaven and on earth is named*. This is apparent as follows. For it is manifest that generation takes its species from the term, which is the form of the generated thing. And the nearer this is to the form of the generator, the truer and more perfect is the generation; just as univocal generation is more perfect than non-univocal generation, for it belongs to the nature of a generator that it generate something like itself according to form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","text":"Hence the very fact that in divine generation the form of the generator and the generated is numerically the same, whereas in created things it is not numerically the same but only specifically the same, shows that generation, and consequently paternity, is in God before it is in creatures. Hence the very fact that in divine matters there is a distinction of the generated from the generator only according to relation pertains to the truth of divine generation and paternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0045","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0046","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that this name Father is not said in divine matters primarily insofar as it is taken personally. For the common, according to understanding, is prior to the proper. But this name Father, insofar as it is taken personally, is proper to the person of the Father; whereas insofar as it is taken essentially, it is common to the whole Trinity, for we say to the whole Trinity, Our Father. Therefore, Father, taken essentially, is said primarily rather than personally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0047","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0048","text":"Moreover, in things that are of the same account, there is no predication according to prior and posterior. But paternity and filiation seem to be said according to one account, both insofar as the divine person is Father of the Son and insofar as the whole Trinity is our Father or the Father of the creature, since, according to Basil, to receive is common to the creature and the Son. Therefore, Father is not said in divine matters primarily insofar as it is taken essentially rather than insofar as it is taken personally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0049","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0050","text":"Moreover, among those things that are not spoken of according to one account, there cannot be comparison. But the Son is compared to the creature in the account of sonship or generation, according to Col. I, *who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature*. Therefore, paternity, taken personally, is not predicated in the divine [persons] before it is predicated essentially, but according to the same account.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0051","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0052","text":"But on the contrary, the eternal is prior to the temporal. Now God is Father of the Son from eternity, but He is Father of the creature in time. Therefore paternity is predicated in God before it is predicated with respect to the Son than with respect to the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0053","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","text":"I answer that it must be said that a name is predicated first of that in which the whole account of the name is preserved perfectly, rather than of that in which it is preserved in some respect; for it is predicated of the latter as if by likeness to that in which it is perfectly preserved, because all imperfect things are taken from perfect things. Hence this name “lion” is predicated first of the animal in which the whole account of lion is preserved, which is properly called a lion, rather than of some man in whom something of the account of lion is found, such as boldness or courage, or something of this sort; for it is predicated of the latter by likeness. But it is clear from what has been said above that the perfect account of paternity and sonship is found in God the Father and God the Son, because the Father and the Son have one nature and glory. But in the creature sonship is found in relation to God, not according to the perfect account, since the nature of Creator and creature is not one, but according to a certain likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","text":"The more perfect this likeness is, the more closely one approaches the true account of sonship. For God is called the Father of some creature because of the likeness of a trace only, as in the case of irrational creatures; according to Job XXXVIII, *Who is the father of the rain? Or who begot the drops of dew?* But He is the Father of another creature, namely, the rational creature, according to the likeness of an image; according to Deut. XXXII, *Is He not thy Father, who possessed and made and created thee?* But He is Father of some according to the likeness of grace, who are also called adopted sons, insofar as they are ordered to the inheritance of eternal glory through the gift of grace received; according to Rom. VIII, *the Spirit himself gives testimony to our spirit that we are sons of God; and if sons, heirs also*. But He is Father of some according to the likeness of glory, insofar as they already possess the inheritance of glory; according to Rom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","text":"V, *we glory in the hope of the glory of the sons of God*. Thus, therefore, it is clear that paternity is predicated in the divine [persons] first insofar as it implies a relation of person to person, rather than insofar as it implies a relation of God to the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0057","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that things common when predicated absolutely are, according to the order of our understanding, prior to proper things, because they are included in the understanding of proper things, but not conversely; for God is understood in the understanding of the person of the Father, but not conversely. But common things that imply a relation to the creature are predicated subsequently to proper things that imply personal relations, because a proceeding person in the divine [persons] proceeds as the principle of the production of creatures. For just as the word conceived in the mind of an artisan is understood to proceed from the artisan before the artifact, which is produced in the likeness of the word conceived in the mind, so the Son proceeds from the Father before the creature, of which the name of sonship is predicated insofar as it participates in some likeness of the Son; as is clear from what is said in Rom. VIII, *those whom He foreknew, He also predestined to be made conformable to the image of His Son*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0059","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0060","text":"To the second, it must be said that to receive is said to be common to the creature and the Son, not according to univocity, but according to a certain remote likeness, by reason of which He is called the firstborn of the creature. Hence in the authority cited there follows, *that He himself might be the firstborn among many brethren*, after it had said that some are made conformable to the image of the Son of God. But the Son of God naturally has something singular beyond others, namely, to have by nature that which He receives; as Basil himself says. And according to this He is called the only-begotten, as is clear in John I, *the only-begotten, who is in the bosom of the Father, He himself has declared Him to us*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0061","text":"[I q. 33 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0062","text":"And through this the answer to the third [objection] is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0063","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0064","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0065","text":"The fourth point is addressed thus. It seems that being unbegotten is not proper to the Father. For every property posits something in that of which it is a property. But “unbegotten” posits nothing in the Father, but only removes. Therefore it does not signify a property of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0066","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0067","text":"Moreover, “unbegotten” is said either privatively or negatively. If negatively, then whatever is not begotten can be called unbegotten. But the Holy Spirit is not begotten, nor is the divine essence. Therefore “unbegotten” also belongs to them, and thus is not proper to the Father. But if it is taken privatively, since every privation signifies an imperfection in that which is deprived, it follows that the person of the Father is imperfect. This is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0068","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0069","text":"Moreover, “unbegotten” in the divine [persons] does not signify a relation, because it is not said relatively; therefore it signifies substance. Therefore “unbegotten” and “begotten” differ with respect to substance. But the Son, who is begotten, does not differ from the Father with respect to substance. Therefore the Father ought not to be called unbegotten.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0070","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0071","text":"Moreover, what is proper is that which belongs to one alone. But since there are several who proceed from another in the divine [persons], nothing seems to prevent there also being several who are not from another. Therefore it is not proper to the Father to be unbegotten.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0072","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0073","text":"Moreover, just as the Father is the principle of the generated person, so too of the proceeding person. Therefore, if, on account of the opposition that he has to the generated person, it is held to be proper to the Father that he is unbegotten, it must likewise be held proper to him that he is non-proceeding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0074","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0075","text":"But on the contrary, Hilary says, *On the Trinity*, IV: *there is one from one*, namely, the begotten from the unbegotten, *namely by the property in each of both unbegottenness and origin*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0076","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","text":"I answer that it must be said that, just as among creatures a first principle and a second principle are found, so in the divine persons, in whom there is no before and after, there are found a principle not from a principle, which is the Father, and a principle from a principle, which is the Son. Now, in created things, a certain first principle is made known in two ways: in one way, insofar as it is a first principle through having a relation to those things which are from it; in another way, insofar as it is a first principle through not being from another. Thus, therefore, the Father is made known indeed by paternity and common spiration, by reference to the persons proceeding from him; but insofar as he is a principle not from a principle, he is made known through this, that he is not from another, which pertains to the property of unbegottenness, signified by this name, “unbegotten.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0078","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that some say that unbegottenness, which is signified by this name, “unbegotten,” insofar as it is a property of the Father, is not spoken only negatively; rather, it conveys either both at once—namely, that the Father is from no one and that he is the principle of others—or it conveys universal authority, or even fontal fullness. But this does not seem true. For in that case unbegottenness would not be a property other than paternity and spiration, but would include them, as the proper is included in the common; for fontality and authority signify nothing other in divine matters than a principle of origin. Therefore it must be said, according to Augustine, *On the Trinity*, V, that “unbegotten” conveys the negation of passive generation; for he says that *“unbegotten” means just as much as “not Son.”*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","text":"Nor does it follow from this that unbegotten should not be posited as a proper notion of the Father, because first and simple things are signified by negations; just as we say that a point is that of which there is no part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0081","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0082","text":"To the second it must be said that “unbegotten” is sometimes taken merely negatively. And in this sense Jerome says that the Holy Spirit is unbegotten, that is, not begotten. In another way, something can be called unbegotten in a certain privative manner, yet it does not convey any imperfection. For privation is spoken of in many ways. In one way, when something does not have what is naturally had by another, even if it itself is not naturally apt to have it; as if a stone is called a dead thing because it lacks life, which certain things are naturally apt to have. In another way, privation is spoken of when something does not have what is naturally had by something of its genus; as if a mole is called blind. In a third way, when it itself does not have what it is naturally apt to have; and in this way privation conveys imperfection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","text":"But “unbegotten” is not said privatively of the Father in this way, but in the second way, namely, insofar as a certain supposit of the divine nature is not begotten, although a supposit of that nature is begotten. But according to this account, the Holy Spirit too can be called unbegotten. Hence, in order that it may be proper to the Father alone, it is necessary further to understand in the name “unbegotten” that it belongs to a divine person who is the principle of another person; so that it may thus be understood to convey a negation in the genus of principle as personally predicated in divine matters. Or, so that it may be understood in the name “unbegotten” that he is in no way from another, and not merely that he is not from another through generation. For thus neither does it belong to the Holy Spirit to be unbegotten, since he is from another through procession as a subsisting person; nor even to the divine essence, of which it can be said that it is in the Son or in the Holy Spirit from another, namely, from the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0084","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0085","text":"To the third it must be said that, according to Damascene, “unbegotten” in one way signifies the same as “uncreated,” and thus is said with respect to substance; for by this a created substance differs from an uncreated one. In another way, it signifies that which is not begotten. And thus it is said relatively, in the way in which a negation is reduced to the genus of its affirmation, as “not-man” to the genus of substance, and “not-white” to the genus of quality. Hence, since “begotten” in divine matters conveys a relation, “unbegotten” also pertains to relation. And thus it does not follow that the unbegotten Father is distinguished from the begotten Son according to substance, but only according to relation, namely, insofar as the relation of the Son is denied of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0086","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","text":"To the fourth it must be said that, just as in every genus one first thing must be posited, so in the divine nature one principle must be posited which is not from another, and which is called unbegotten. Therefore, to posit two unbegottens is to posit two gods and two divine natures. Hence Hilary says, in the book *On the Synods*: *since there is one God, there cannot be two unbegottens.* And this especially because, if there were two unbegottens, neither of them would be from the other, and thus they would not be distinguished by relative opposition; it would therefore be necessary that they be distinguished by diversity of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0088","text":"[I q. 33 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","text":"To the fifth it must be said that the property of the Father, insofar as he is not from another, is signified more fittingly by the removal of the Son’s nativity than by the removal of the Holy Spirit’s procession. First, because the procession of the Holy Spirit has no special name, as was said above. Second, because it also presupposes, in the order of nature, the generation of the Son. Hence, once it is removed from the Father that he is begotten, although he is the principle of generation, it consequently follows that he is not proceeding by the procession of the Holy Spirit, because the Holy Spirit is not the principle of generation, but proceeds from the begotten one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089"]}],"source_digest":"e27e9521117a37831e893546b36713acbe583ef07e0974b2ed9cd7128528e911","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 33"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 33","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26","title":"First Part · Question 34","order":35,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_26","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 34 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29843)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de persona filii. Attribuuntur autem tria nomina filio, scilicet filius, verbum et imago. Sed ratio filii ex ratione patris consideratur. Unde restat considerandum de verbo et imagine. Circa verbum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum verbum dicatur essentialiter in divinis, vel personaliter. Secundo, utrum sit proprium nomen filii. Tertio, utrum in nomine verbi importetur respectus ad creaturas.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29844)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod verbum in divinis non sit nomen personale. Nomina enim personalia proprie dicuntur in divinis, ut pater et filius. Sed verbum metaphorice dicitur in divinis, ut Origenes dicit, super Ioannem. Ergo verbum non est personale in divinis.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29845)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Augustinum, in libro de Trin., verbum est notitia cum amore. Et secundum Anselmum, in Monol., *dicere summo spiritui nihil aliud est quam cogitando intueri*. Sed notitia et cogitatio et intuitus in divinis essentialiter dicuntur. Ergo verbum non dicitur personaliter in divinis.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29846)\n\nPraeterea, de ratione verbi est quod dicatur. Sed, secundum Anselmum, sicut pater est intelligens, et filius est intelligens, et spiritus sanctus est intelligens; ita pater est dicens, filius est dicens, et spiritus sanctus est dicens. Et similiter quilibet eorum dicitur. Ergo nomen verbi essentialiter dicitur in divinis, et non personaliter.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29847)\n\nPraeterea, nulla persona divina est facta. Sed verbum Dei est aliquid factum, dicitur enim in Psalmo CXLVIII, *ignis, grando, nix, glacies, spiritus procellarum, quae faciunt verbum eius*. Ergo verbum non est nomen personale in divinis.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29848)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in VII de Trin., *sicut filius refertur ad patrem, ita et verbum ad id cuius est verbum*. Sed filius est nomen personale, quia relative dicitur. Ergo et verbum.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29849)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nomen verbi in divinis, si proprie sumatur, est nomen personale, et nullo modo essentiale. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod verbum tripliciter quidem in nobis proprie dicitur, quarto autem modo, dicitur improprie sive figurative. Manifestius autem et communius in nobis dicitur verbum quod voce profertur. Quod quidem ab interiori procedit quantum ad duo quae in verbo exteriori inveniuntur, scilicet vox ipsa, et significatio vocis. Vox enim significat intellectus conceptum, secundum philosophum, in libro I Periherm., et iterum vox ex imaginatione procedit, ut in libro de anima dicitur. Vox autem quae non est significativa, verbum dici non potest. Ex hoc ergo dicitur verbum vox exterior, quia significat interiorem mentis conceptum. Sic igitur primo et principaliter interior mentis conceptus verbum dicitur, secundario vero, ipsa vox interioris conceptus significativa, tertio vero, ipsa imaginatio vocis verbum dicitur. Et hos tres modos verbi ponit Damascenus, in I libro, cap. XIII, dicens quod verbum dicitur naturalis intellectus motus, secundum quem movetur et intelligit et cogitat, velut lux et splendor, quantum ad primum, rursus verbum est quod non verbo profertur, sed in corde pronuntiatur, quantum ad tertium, rursus etiam verbum est Angelus, idest nuntius, intelligentiae, quantum ad secundum. Dicitur autem figurative quarto modo verbum, id quod verbo significatur vel efficitur, sicut consuevimus dicere, hoc est verbum quod dixi tibi, vel quod mandavit rex, demonstrato aliquo facto quod verbo significatum est vel simpliciter enuntiantis, vel etiam imperantis. Dicitur autem proprie verbum in Deo, secundum quod verbum significat conceptum intellectus. Unde Augustinus dicit, in XV de Trin., *quisquis potest intelligere verbum, non solum antequam sonet, verum etiam antequam sonorum eius imagines cogitatione involvantur, iam potest videre aliquam verbi illius similitudinem, de quo dictum est, in principio erat verbum*. Ipse autem conceptus cordis de ratione sua habet quod ab alio procedat, scilicet a notitia concipientis. Unde verbum, secundum quod proprie dicitur in divinis, significat aliquid ab alio procedens, quod pertinet ad rationem nominum personalium in divinis, eo quod personae divinae distinguuntur secundum originem, ut dictum est. Unde oportet quod nomen verbi, secundum quod proprie in divinis accipitur, non sumatur essentialiter, sed personaliter tantum.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29850)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Ariani, quorum fons Origenes invenitur, posuerunt filium alium a patre esse in diversitate substantiae. Unde conati sunt, cum filius Dei verbum dicitur, astruere non esse proprie dictum; ne, sub ratione verbi procedentis, cogerentur fateri filium Dei non esse extra substantiam patris; nam verbum interius sic a dicente procedit, quod in ipso manet. Sed necesse est, si ponitur verbum Dei metaphorice dictum, quod ponatur verbum Dei proprie dictum. Non enim potest aliquid metaphorice verbum dici, nisi ratione manifestationis, quia vel manifestat sicut verbum, vel est verbo manifestatum. Si autem est manifestatum verbo, oportet ponere verbum quo manifestetur. Si autem dicitur verbum quia exterius manifestat, ea quae exterius manifestant, non dicuntur verba nisi inquantum significant interiorem mentis conceptum, quem aliquis etiam per exteriora signa manifestat. Etsi ergo verbum aliquando dicatur metaphorice in divinis, tamen oportet ponere verbum proprie dictum, quod personaliter dicatur.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29851)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nihil eorum quae ad intellectum pertinent, personaliter dicitur in divinis, nisi solum verbum, solum enim verbum significat aliquid ab alio emanans. Id enim quod intellectus in concipiendo format, est verbum. Intellectus autem ipse, secundum quod est per speciem intelligibilem in actu, consideratur absolute. Et similiter intelligere, quod ita se habet ad intellectum in actu, sicut esse ad ens in actu, non enim intelligere significat actionem ab intelligente exeuntem, sed in intelligente manentem. Cum ergo dicitur quod verbum est notitia, non accipitur notitia pro actu intellectus cognoscentis, vel pro aliquo eius habitu, sed pro eo quod intellectus concipit cognoscendo. Unde et Augustinus dicit quod verbum est sapientia genita, quod nihil aliud est quam ipsa conceptio sapientis, quae etiam pari modo notitia genita dici potest. Et per eundem modum potest intelligi quod dicere Deo sit cogitando intueri, inquantum scilicet intuitu cogitationis divinae concipitur verbum Dei. Cogitationis tamen nomen Dei verbo proprie non convenit, dicit enim Augustinus, XV de Trin., *ita dicitur illud verbum Dei, ut cogitatio non dicatur; ne aliquid esse quasi volubile credatur in Deo, quod nunc accipiat formam ut verbum sit, eamque dimittere possit, atque informiter quodammodo volutari*. Cogitatio enim proprie in inquisitione veritatis consistit, quae in Deo locum non habet. Cum vero intellectus iam ad formam veritatis pertingit, non cogitat, sed perfecte veritatem contemplatur. Unde Anselmus improprie accipit cogitationem pro contemplatione.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29852)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut, proprie loquendo, verbum dicitur personaliter in divinis et non essentialiter, ita et dicere. Unde, sicut verbum non est commune patri et filio et spiritui sancto, ita non est verum quod pater et filius et spiritus sanctus sint unus dicens. Unde Augustinus dicit, VII de Trin., *dicens illo coaeterno verbo non singulus intelligitur in divinis*. Sed dici convenit cuilibet personae, dicitur enim non solum verbum sed res quae verbo intelligitur vel significatur. Sic ergo uni soli personae in divinis convenit dici eo modo quo dicitur verbum, eo vero modo quo dicitur res in verbo intellecta, cuilibet personae convenit dici. Pater enim, intelligendo se et filium et spiritum sanctum, et omnia alia quae eius scientia continentur, concipit verbum, ut sic tota Trinitas verbo dicatur, et etiam omnis creatura; sicut intellectus hominis verbo quod concipit intelligendo lapidem, lapidem dicit. Anselmus vero improprie accepit dicere pro intelligere. Quae tamen differunt. Nam intelligere importat solam habitudinem intelligentis ad rem intellectam; in qua nulla ratio originis importatur, sed solum informatio quaedam in intellectu nostro, prout intellectus noster fit in actu per formam rei intellectae. In Deo autem importat omnimodam identitatem, quia in Deo est omnino idem intellectus et intellectum, ut supra ostensum est. Sed dicere importat principaliter habitudinem ad verbum conceptum nihil enim est aliud dicere quam proferre verbum. Sed mediante verbo importat habitudinem ad rem intellectam, quae in verbo prolato manifestatur intelligenti. Et sic sola persona quae profert verbum, est dicens in divinis, cum tamen singula personarum sit intelligens et intellecta, et per consequens verbo dicta.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29853)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod verbum sumitur ibi figurative, prout significatum vel effectus verbi dicitur verbum. Sic enim creaturae dicuntur facere verbum Dei, inquantum exequuntur effectum aliquem, ad quem ordinantur ex verbo concepto divinae sapientiae, sicut aliquis dicitur facere verbum regis, dum facit opus ad quod ex verbo regis instigatur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29854)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod verbum non sit proprium nomen filii. Filius enim est persona subsistens in divinis. Sed verbum non significat rem subsistentem, ut in nobis patet. Ergo verbum non potest esse proprium nomen personae filii.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29855)\n\nPraeterea, verbum prolatione quadam procedit a dicente. Si ergo filius est proprie verbum, non procedit a patre nisi per modum prolationis. Quod est haeresis Valentini, ut patet per Augustinum, in libro de haeresibus.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29856)\n\nPraeterea, omne nomen proprium alicuius personae significat proprietatem aliquam eius. Si igitur verbum sit proprium nomen filii, significabit aliquam proprietatem eius. Et sic erunt plures proprietates in divinis quam supra enumeratae sunt.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29857)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque intelligit, intelligendo concipit verbum. Sed filius intelligit. Ergo filii est aliquod verbum. Et sic non est proprium filii esse verbum.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29858)\n\nPraeterea, Hebr. I dicitur de filio, portans omnia verbo virtutis suae, ex quo Basilius accipit quod spiritus sanctus sit verbum filii. Non est ergo proprium filii esse verbum.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29859)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., *verbum solus filius accipitur*.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29860)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod verbum proprie dictum in divinis personaliter accipitur, et est proprium nomen personae filii. Significat enim quandam emanationem intellectus, persona autem quae procedit in divinis secundum emanationem intellectus, dicitur filius, et huiusmodi processio dicitur generatio, ut supra ostensum est. Unde relinquitur quod solus filius proprie dicatur verbum in divinis.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29861)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in nobis non est idem esse et intelligere, unde illud quod habet in nobis esse intelligibile, non pertinet ad naturam nostram. Sed esse Dei est ipsum eius intelligere, unde verbum Dei non est aliquod accidens in ipso, vel aliquis effectus eius; sed pertinet ad ipsam naturam eius. Et ideo oportet quod sit aliquid subsistens, quia quidquid est in natura Dei, subsistit. Et ideo Damascenus dicit quod *verbum Dei est substantiale, et in hypostasi ens, reliqua vero verba*, scilicet nostra, *virtutes sunt animae*.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29862)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non propter hoc error Valentini est damnatus, quia filium dixit prolatione natum, ut Ariani calumniabantur, sicut Hilarius refert, VI de Trin., sed propter varium modum prolationis quem posuit, sicut patet per Augustinum in libro de haeresibus.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29863)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in nomine verbi eadem proprietas importatur quae in nomine filii, unde dicit Augustinus, *eo dicitur verbum, quo filius*. Ipsa enim nativitas filii, quae est proprietas personalis eius, diversis nominibus significatur, quae filio attribuuntur ad exprimendum diversimode perfectionem eius. Nam ut ostendatur connaturalis patri, dicitur filius; ut ostendatur coaeternus, dicitur splendor; ut ostendatur omnino similis, dicitur imago; ut ostendatur immaterialiter genitus, dicitur verbum. Non autem potuit unum nomen inveniri, per quod omnia ista designarentur.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29864)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod eo modo convenit filio esse intelligentem, quo convenit ei esse Deum, cum intelligere essentialiter dicatur in divinis, ut dictum est. Est autem filius Deus genitus, non autem generans Deus. Unde est quidem intelligens, non ut producens verbum, sed ut verbum procedens; prout scilicet in Deo verbum procedens secundum rem non differt ab intellectu divino, sed relatione sola distinguitur a principio verbi.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29865)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, cum de filio dicitur, portans omnia verbo virtutis suae, verbum figurate accipitur pro effectu verbi. Unde Glossa ibi dicit quod verbum sumitur pro imperio; inquantum scilicet ex effectu virtutis verbi est quod res conserventur in esse, sicut ex effectu virtutis verbi est quod res producantur in esse. Quod vero Basilius interpretatur verbum pro spiritu sancto, improprie et figurate locutus est, prout verbum alicuius dici potest omne illud quod est manifestativum eius, ut sic ea ratione dicatur spiritus sanctus verbum filii, quia manifestat filium.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29866)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in nomine verbi non importetur respectus ad creaturam. Omne enim nomen connotans effectum in creatura, essentialiter in divinis dicitur. Sed verbum non dicitur essentialiter, sed personaliter, ut dictum est. Ergo verbum non importat respectum ad creaturam.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29867)\n\nPraeterea, quae important respectum ad creaturas, dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, ut dominus et creator. Sed verbum dicitur de Deo ab aeterno. Ergo non importat respectum ad creaturam.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29868)\n\nPraeterea, verbum importat respectum ad id a quo procedit. Si ergo importat respectum ad creaturam, sequitur quod procedat a creatura.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29869)\n\nPraeterea, ideae sunt plures secundum diversos respectus ad creaturas. Si igitur verbum importat respectum ad creaturas, sequitur quod in Deo non sit unum verbum tantum, sed plura.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29870)\n\nPraeterea, si verbum importat respectum ad creaturam, hoc non est nisi inquantum creaturae cognoscuntur a Deo. Sed Deus non solum cognoscit entia, sed etiam non entia. Ergo in verbo importabitur respectus ad non entia, quod videtur falsum.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29871)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *in nomine verbi significatur non solum respectus ad patrem, sed etiam ad illa quae per verbum facta sunt operativa potentia*.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29872)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in verbo importatur respectus ad creaturam. Deus enim, cognoscendo se, cognoscit omnem creaturam. Verbum autem in mente conceptum, est repraesentativum omnis eius quod actu intelligitur. Unde in nobis sunt diversa verba, secundum diversa quae intelligimus. Sed quia Deus uno actu et se et omnia intelligit, unicum verbum eius est expressivum non solum patris, sed etiam creaturarum. Et sicut Dei scientia Dei quidem est cognoscitiva tantum, creaturarum autem cognoscitiva et factiva; ita verbum Dei eius quod in Deo patre est, est expressivum tantum, creaturarum vero est expressivum et operativum. Et propter hoc dicitur in Psalmo XXXII, *dixit, et facta sunt*; quia in verbo importatur ratio factiva eorum quae Deus facit.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29873)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in nomine personae includitur etiam natura oblique, nam persona est rationalis naturae individua substantia. In nomine igitur personae divinae, quantum ad relationem personalem, non importatur respectus ad creaturam, sed importatur in eo quod pertinet ad naturam. Nihil tamen prohibet, inquantum includitur in significatione eius essentia, quod importetur respectus ad creaturam, sicut enim proprium est filio quod sit filius, ita proprium est ei quod sit genitus Deus, vel genitus creator. Et per hunc modum importatur relatio ad creaturam in nomine verbi.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29874)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum relationes consequantur actiones, quaedam nomina important relationem Dei ad creaturam, quae consequitur actionem Dei in exteriorem effectum transeuntem, sicut creare et gubernare, et talia dicuntur de Deo ex tempore. Quaedam vero relationem quae consequitur actionem non transeuntem in exteriorem effectum, sed manentem in agente, ut scire et velle, et talia non dicuntur de Deo ex tempore. Et huiusmodi relatio ad creaturam importatur in nomine verbi. Nec est verum quod nomina importantia relationem Dei ad creaturas, omnia dicantur ex tempore, sed sola illa nomina quae important relationem consequentem actionem Dei in exteriorem effectum transeuntem, ex tempore dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29875)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod creaturae non cognoscuntur a Deo per scientiam a creaturis acceptam, sed per essentiam suam. Unde non oportet quod a creaturis procedat verbum, licet verbum sit expressivum creaturarum.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29876)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod nomen ideae principaliter est impositum ad significandum respectum ad creaturam, et ideo pluraliter dicitur in divinis, neque est personale. Sed nomen verbi principaliter impositum est ad significandam relationem ad dicentem, et ex consequenti ad creaturas, inquantum Deus, intelligendo se, intelligit omnem creaturam. Et propter hoc in divinis est unicum tantum verbum, et personaliter dictum.\n\n[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29877)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod eo modo quo scientia Dei est non entium, et verbum Dei est non entium, quia non est aliquid minus in verbo Dei quam in scientia Dei, ut Augustinus dicit. Sed tamen verbum est entium ut expressivum et factivum, non entium autem, ut expressivum et manifestativum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0002","text":"[I q. 34 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de persona filii. Attribuuntur autem tria nomina filio, scilicet filius, verbum et imago. Sed ratio filii ex ratione patris consideratur. Unde restat considerandum de verbo et imagine. Circa verbum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum verbum dicatur essentialiter in divinis, vel personaliter. Secundo, utrum sit proprium nomen filii. Tertio, utrum in nomine verbi importetur respectus ad creaturas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0005","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod verbum in divinis non sit nomen personale. Nomina enim personalia proprie dicuntur in divinis, ut pater et filius. Sed verbum metaphorice dicitur in divinis, ut Origenes dicit, super Ioannem. Ergo verbum non est personale in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0007","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0008","text":"Praeterea, secundum Augustinum, in libro de Trin., verbum est notitia cum amore. Et secundum Anselmum, in Monol., *dicere summo spiritui nihil aliud est quam cogitando intueri*. Sed notitia et cogitatio et intuitus in divinis essentialiter dicuntur. Ergo verbum non dicitur personaliter in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0009","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0010","text":"Praeterea, de ratione verbi est quod dicatur. Sed, secundum Anselmum, sicut pater est intelligens, et filius est intelligens, et spiritus sanctus est intelligens; ita pater est dicens, filius est dicens, et spiritus sanctus est dicens. Et similiter quilibet eorum dicitur. Ergo nomen verbi essentialiter dicitur in divinis, et non personaliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0011","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0012","text":"Praeterea, nulla persona divina est facta. Sed verbum Dei est aliquid factum, dicitur enim in Psalmo CXLVIII, *ignis, grando, nix, glacies, spiritus procellarum, quae faciunt verbum eius*. Ergo verbum non est nomen personale in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0013","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in VII de Trin., *sicut filius refertur ad patrem, ita et verbum ad id cuius est verbum*. Sed filius est nomen personale, quia relative dicitur. Ergo et verbum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0015","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nomen verbi in divinis, si proprie sumatur, est nomen personale, et nullo modo essentiale. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod verbum tripliciter quidem in nobis proprie dicitur, quarto autem modo, dicitur improprie sive figurative. Manifestius autem et communius in nobis dicitur verbum quod voce profertur. Quod quidem ab interiori procedit quantum ad duo quae in verbo exteriori inveniuntur, scilicet vox ipsa, et significatio vocis. Vox enim significat intellectus conceptum, secundum philosophum, in libro I Periherm., et iterum vox ex imaginatione procedit, ut in libro de anima dicitur. Vox autem quae non est significativa, verbum dici non potest. Ex hoc ergo dicitur verbum vox exterior, quia significat interiorem mentis conceptum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0017","text":"Sic igitur primo et principaliter interior mentis conceptus verbum dicitur, secundario vero, ipsa vox interioris conceptus significativa, tertio vero, ipsa imaginatio vocis verbum dicitur. Et hos tres modos verbi ponit Damascenus, in I libro, cap. XIII, dicens quod verbum dicitur naturalis intellectus motus, secundum quem movetur et intelligit et cogitat, velut lux et splendor, quantum ad primum, rursus verbum est quod non verbo profertur, sed in corde pronuntiatur, quantum ad tertium, rursus etiam verbum est Angelus, idest nuntius, intelligentiae, quantum ad secundum. Dicitur autem figurative quarto modo verbum, id quod verbo significatur vel efficitur, sicut consuevimus dicere, hoc est verbum quod dixi tibi, vel quod mandavit rex, demonstrato aliquo facto quod verbo significatum est vel simpliciter enuntiantis, vel etiam imperantis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","text":"Dicitur autem proprie verbum in Deo, secundum quod verbum significat conceptum intellectus. Unde Augustinus dicit, in XV de Trin., *quisquis potest intelligere verbum, non solum antequam sonet, verum etiam antequam sonorum eius imagines cogitatione involvantur, iam potest videre aliquam verbi illius similitudinem, de quo dictum est, in principio erat verbum*. Ipse autem conceptus cordis de ratione sua habet quod ab alio procedat, scilicet a notitia concipientis. Unde verbum, secundum quod proprie dicitur in divinis, significat aliquid ab alio procedens, quod pertinet ad rationem nominum personalium in divinis, eo quod personae divinae distinguuntur secundum originem, ut dictum est. Unde oportet quod nomen verbi, secundum quod proprie in divinis accipitur, non sumatur essentialiter, sed personaliter tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0019","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Ariani, quorum fons Origenes invenitur, posuerunt filium alium a patre esse in diversitate substantiae. Unde conati sunt, cum filius Dei verbum dicitur, astruere non esse proprie dictum; ne, sub ratione verbi procedentis, cogerentur fateri filium Dei non esse extra substantiam patris; nam verbum interius sic a dicente procedit, quod in ipso manet. Sed necesse est, si ponitur verbum Dei metaphorice dictum, quod ponatur verbum Dei proprie dictum. Non enim potest aliquid metaphorice verbum dici, nisi ratione manifestationis, quia vel manifestat sicut verbum, vel est verbo manifestatum. Si autem est manifestatum verbo, oportet ponere verbum quo manifestetur. Si autem dicitur verbum quia exterius manifestat, ea quae exterius manifestant, non dicuntur verba nisi inquantum significant interiorem mentis conceptum, quem aliquis etiam per exteriora signa manifestat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0021","text":"Etsi ergo verbum aliquando dicatur metaphorice in divinis, tamen oportet ponere verbum proprie dictum, quod personaliter dicatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0022","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0023","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nihil eorum quae ad intellectum pertinent, personaliter dicitur in divinis, nisi solum verbum, solum enim verbum significat aliquid ab alio emanans. Id enim quod intellectus in concipiendo format, est verbum. Intellectus autem ipse, secundum quod est per speciem intelligibilem in actu, consideratur absolute. Et similiter intelligere, quod ita se habet ad intellectum in actu, sicut esse ad ens in actu, non enim intelligere significat actionem ab intelligente exeuntem, sed in intelligente manentem. Cum ergo dicitur quod verbum est notitia, non accipitur notitia pro actu intellectus cognoscentis, vel pro aliquo eius habitu, sed pro eo quod intellectus concipit cognoscendo. Unde et Augustinus dicit quod verbum est sapientia genita, quod nihil aliud est quam ipsa conceptio sapientis, quae etiam pari modo notitia genita dici potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0024","text":"Et per eundem modum potest intelligi quod dicere Deo sit cogitando intueri, inquantum scilicet intuitu cogitationis divinae concipitur verbum Dei. Cogitationis tamen nomen Dei verbo proprie non convenit, dicit enim Augustinus, XV de Trin., *ita dicitur illud verbum Dei, ut cogitatio non dicatur; ne aliquid esse quasi volubile credatur in Deo, quod nunc accipiat formam ut verbum sit, eamque dimittere possit, atque informiter quodammodo volutari*. Cogitatio enim proprie in inquisitione veritatis consistit, quae in Deo locum non habet. Cum vero intellectus iam ad formam veritatis pertingit, non cogitat, sed perfecte veritatem contemplatur. Unde Anselmus improprie accipit cogitationem pro contemplatione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0025","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0026","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut, proprie loquendo, verbum dicitur personaliter in divinis et non essentialiter, ita et dicere. Unde, sicut verbum non est commune patri et filio et spiritui sancto, ita non est verum quod pater et filius et spiritus sanctus sint unus dicens. Unde Augustinus dicit, VII de Trin., *dicens illo coaeterno verbo non singulus intelligitur in divinis*. Sed dici convenit cuilibet personae, dicitur enim non solum verbum sed res quae verbo intelligitur vel significatur. Sic ergo uni soli personae in divinis convenit dici eo modo quo dicitur verbum, eo vero modo quo dicitur res in verbo intellecta, cuilibet personae convenit dici.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027","text":"Pater enim, intelligendo se et filium et spiritum sanctum, et omnia alia quae eius scientia continentur, concipit verbum, ut sic tota Trinitas verbo dicatur, et etiam omnis creatura; sicut intellectus hominis verbo quod concipit intelligendo lapidem, lapidem dicit. Anselmus vero improprie accepit dicere pro intelligere. Quae tamen differunt. Nam intelligere importat solam habitudinem intelligentis ad rem intellectam; in qua nulla ratio originis importatur, sed solum informatio quaedam in intellectu nostro, prout intellectus noster fit in actu per formam rei intellectae. In Deo autem importat omnimodam identitatem, quia in Deo est omnino idem intellectus et intellectum, ut supra ostensum est. Sed dicere importat principaliter habitudinem ad verbum conceptum nihil enim est aliud dicere quam proferre verbum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0028","text":"Sed mediante verbo importat habitudinem ad rem intellectam, quae in verbo prolato manifestatur intelligenti. Et sic sola persona quae profert verbum, est dicens in divinis, cum tamen singula personarum sit intelligens et intellecta, et per consequens verbo dicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0029","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0030","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod verbum sumitur ibi figurative, prout significatum vel effectus verbi dicitur verbum. Sic enim creaturae dicuntur facere verbum Dei, inquantum exequuntur effectum aliquem, ad quem ordinantur ex verbo concepto divinae sapientiae, sicut aliquis dicitur facere verbum regis, dum facit opus ad quod ex verbo regis instigatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0031","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0032","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0033","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod verbum non sit proprium nomen filii. Filius enim est persona subsistens in divinis. Sed verbum non significat rem subsistentem, ut in nobis patet. Ergo verbum non potest esse proprium nomen personae filii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0034","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0035","text":"Praeterea, verbum prolatione quadam procedit a dicente. Si ergo filius est proprie verbum, non procedit a patre nisi per modum prolationis. Quod est haeresis Valentini, ut patet per Augustinum, in libro de haeresibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0036","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0037","text":"Praeterea, omne nomen proprium alicuius personae significat proprietatem aliquam eius. Si igitur verbum sit proprium nomen filii, significabit aliquam proprietatem eius. Et sic erunt plures proprietates in divinis quam supra enumeratae sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0038","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0039","text":"Praeterea, quicumque intelligit, intelligendo concipit verbum. Sed filius intelligit. Ergo filii est aliquod verbum. Et sic non est proprium filii esse verbum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0040","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0041","text":"Praeterea, Hebr. I dicitur de filio, portans omnia verbo virtutis suae, ex quo Basilius accipit quod spiritus sanctus sit verbum filii. Non est ergo proprium filii esse verbum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0042","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0043","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., *verbum solus filius accipitur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0044","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0045","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod verbum proprie dictum in divinis personaliter accipitur, et est proprium nomen personae filii. Significat enim quandam emanationem intellectus, persona autem quae procedit in divinis secundum emanationem intellectus, dicitur filius, et huiusmodi processio dicitur generatio, ut supra ostensum est. Unde relinquitur quod solus filius proprie dicatur verbum in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0046","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0047","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in nobis non est idem esse et intelligere, unde illud quod habet in nobis esse intelligibile, non pertinet ad naturam nostram. Sed esse Dei est ipsum eius intelligere, unde verbum Dei non est aliquod accidens in ipso, vel aliquis effectus eius; sed pertinet ad ipsam naturam eius. Et ideo oportet quod sit aliquid subsistens, quia quidquid est in natura Dei, subsistit. Et ideo Damascenus dicit quod *verbum Dei est substantiale, et in hypostasi ens, reliqua vero verba*, scilicet nostra, *virtutes sunt animae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0048","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0049","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non propter hoc error Valentini est damnatus, quia filium dixit prolatione natum, ut Ariani calumniabantur, sicut Hilarius refert, VI de Trin., sed propter varium modum prolationis quem posuit, sicut patet per Augustinum in libro de haeresibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0050","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0051","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in nomine verbi eadem proprietas importatur quae in nomine filii, unde dicit Augustinus, *eo dicitur verbum, quo filius*. Ipsa enim nativitas filii, quae est proprietas personalis eius, diversis nominibus significatur, quae filio attribuuntur ad exprimendum diversimode perfectionem eius. Nam ut ostendatur connaturalis patri, dicitur filius; ut ostendatur coaeternus, dicitur splendor; ut ostendatur omnino similis, dicitur imago; ut ostendatur immaterialiter genitus, dicitur verbum. Non autem potuit unum nomen inveniri, per quod omnia ista designarentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0052","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0053","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod eo modo convenit filio esse intelligentem, quo convenit ei esse Deum, cum intelligere essentialiter dicatur in divinis, ut dictum est. Est autem filius Deus genitus, non autem generans Deus. Unde est quidem intelligens, non ut producens verbum, sed ut verbum procedens; prout scilicet in Deo verbum procedens secundum rem non differt ab intellectu divino, sed relatione sola distinguitur a principio verbi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0054","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0055","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, cum de filio dicitur, portans omnia verbo virtutis suae, verbum figurate accipitur pro effectu verbi. Unde Glossa ibi dicit quod verbum sumitur pro imperio; inquantum scilicet ex effectu virtutis verbi est quod res conserventur in esse, sicut ex effectu virtutis verbi est quod res producantur in esse. Quod vero Basilius interpretatur verbum pro spiritu sancto, improprie et figurate locutus est, prout verbum alicuius dici potest omne illud quod est manifestativum eius, ut sic ea ratione dicatur spiritus sanctus verbum filii, quia manifestat filium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0056","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0057","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0058","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in nomine verbi non importetur respectus ad creaturam. Omne enim nomen connotans effectum in creatura, essentialiter in divinis dicitur. Sed verbum non dicitur essentialiter, sed personaliter, ut dictum est. Ergo verbum non importat respectum ad creaturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0059","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0060","text":"Praeterea, quae important respectum ad creaturas, dicuntur de Deo ex tempore, ut dominus et creator. Sed verbum dicitur de Deo ab aeterno. Ergo non importat respectum ad creaturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0061","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0062","text":"Praeterea, verbum importat respectum ad id a quo procedit. Si ergo importat respectum ad creaturam, sequitur quod procedat a creatura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0063","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0064","text":"Praeterea, ideae sunt plures secundum diversos respectus ad creaturas. Si igitur verbum importat respectum ad creaturas, sequitur quod in Deo non sit unum verbum tantum, sed plura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0065","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0066","text":"Praeterea, si verbum importat respectum ad creaturam, hoc non est nisi inquantum creaturae cognoscuntur a Deo. Sed Deus non solum cognoscit entia, sed etiam non entia. Ergo in verbo importabitur respectus ad non entia, quod videtur falsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0067","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0068","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *in nomine verbi significatur non solum respectus ad patrem, sed etiam ad illa quae per verbum facta sunt operativa potentia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0069","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in verbo importatur respectus ad creaturam. Deus enim, cognoscendo se, cognoscit omnem creaturam. Verbum autem in mente conceptum, est repraesentativum omnis eius quod actu intelligitur. Unde in nobis sunt diversa verba, secundum diversa quae intelligimus. Sed quia Deus uno actu et se et omnia intelligit, unicum verbum eius est expressivum non solum patris, sed etiam creaturarum. Et sicut Dei scientia Dei quidem est cognoscitiva tantum, creaturarum autem cognoscitiva et factiva; ita verbum Dei eius quod in Deo patre est, est expressivum tantum, creaturarum vero est expressivum et operativum. Et propter hoc dicitur in Psalmo XXXII, *dixit, et facta sunt*; quia in verbo importatur ratio factiva eorum quae Deus facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0071","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0072","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in nomine personae includitur etiam natura oblique, nam persona est rationalis naturae individua substantia. In nomine igitur personae divinae, quantum ad relationem personalem, non importatur respectus ad creaturam, sed importatur in eo quod pertinet ad naturam. Nihil tamen prohibet, inquantum includitur in significatione eius essentia, quod importetur respectus ad creaturam, sicut enim proprium est filio quod sit filius, ita proprium est ei quod sit genitus Deus, vel genitus creator. Et per hunc modum importatur relatio ad creaturam in nomine verbi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0073","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0074","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum relationes consequantur actiones, quaedam nomina important relationem Dei ad creaturam, quae consequitur actionem Dei in exteriorem effectum transeuntem, sicut creare et gubernare, et talia dicuntur de Deo ex tempore. Quaedam vero relationem quae consequitur actionem non transeuntem in exteriorem effectum, sed manentem in agente, ut scire et velle, et talia non dicuntur de Deo ex tempore. Et huiusmodi relatio ad creaturam importatur in nomine verbi. Nec est verum quod nomina importantia relationem Dei ad creaturas, omnia dicantur ex tempore, sed sola illa nomina quae important relationem consequentem actionem Dei in exteriorem effectum transeuntem, ex tempore dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0075","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod creaturae non cognoscuntur a Deo per scientiam a creaturis acceptam, sed per essentiam suam. Unde non oportet quod a creaturis procedat verbum, licet verbum sit expressivum creaturarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0077","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0078","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod nomen ideae principaliter est impositum ad significandum respectum ad creaturam, et ideo pluraliter dicitur in divinis, neque est personale. Sed nomen verbi principaliter impositum est ad significandam relationem ad dicentem, et ex consequenti ad creaturas, inquantum Deus, intelligendo se, intelligit omnem creaturam. Et propter hoc in divinis est unicum tantum verbum, et personaliter dictum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0079","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0080","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod eo modo quo scientia Dei est non entium, et verbum Dei est non entium, quia non est aliquid minus in verbo Dei quam in scientia Dei, ut Augustinus dicit. Sed tamen verbum est entium ut expressivum et factivum, non entium autem, ut expressivum et manifestativum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8cf4badd9c92a07f656c19f613f9281a2ac180f3857b2c4591efa15c8454f1fe","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141609481520Z_8a16d233","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 34","source_sha256":"8cf4badd9c92a07f656c19f613f9281a2ac180f3857b2c4591efa15c8454f1fe","sha256":"4fd8bd8d50abe950c55c8d5eccca35a8008415175201571934376d9d7c0cb93c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:16:09.481538+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0002","text":"[I q. 34 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the person of the Son. Now three names are attributed to the Son, namely, Son, Word, and Image. But the account of Son is considered from the account of Father. Hence it remains to consider the Word and the Image. Concerning the Word, three questions are asked. First, whether Word is said essentially in divine things, or personally. Second, whether it is a proper name of the Son. Third, whether a relation to creatures is implied in the name Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0005","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that Word is not a personal name in divine things. For personal names are properly said in divine things, such as Father and Son. But Word is said metaphorically in divine things, as Origen says, on John. Therefore Word is not personal in divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0007","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0008","text":"Further, according to Augustine, in the book *On the Trinity*, the word is knowledge together with love. And according to Anselm, in the *Monologion*, *for the highest spirit to speak is nothing other than to behold by thinking*. But knowledge and thought and beholding are said essentially in divine things. Therefore Word is not said personally in divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0009","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0010","text":"Further, it belongs to the account of a word that it be spoken. But, according to Anselm, just as the Father is understanding, and the Son is understanding, and the Holy Spirit is understanding, so the Father is speaking, the Son is speaking, and the Holy Spirit is speaking. And similarly each of them is spoken. Therefore the name Word is said essentially in divine things, and not personally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0011","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0012","text":"Further, no divine person is made. But the word of God is something made, for it is said in Psalm CXLVIII, *fire, hail, snow, ice, stormy winds, which do his word*. Therefore Word is not a personal name in divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0013","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0014","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in VII *On the Trinity*, *as the Son is related to the Father, so also is the Word to that whose Word it is*. But Son is a personal name, because it is said relatively. Therefore so too is Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0015","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0016","text":"I answer that the name Word in divine things, if it is taken properly, is a personal name and in no way an essential one. To make this clear, one must know that word is properly said of us in three ways, but in a fourth way it is said improperly or figuratively. Now, most manifestly and commonly among us, word is said of that which is uttered by the voice. This proceeds from what is inward with respect to two things found in the exterior word, namely, the voice itself and the signification of the voice. For, according to the Philosopher, in book I of *Perihermeneias*, the voice signifies a concept of the intellect; and again, as is said in the book *On the Soul*, voice proceeds from imagination. But a voice that is not significant cannot be called a word. Therefore an exterior voice is called a word because it signifies an interior concept of the mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0017","text":"Thus, therefore, first and principally the interior concept of the mind is called a word; secondarily, the voice itself signifying the interior concept; thirdly, the imagination of the voice itself is called a word. And Damascene sets down these three modes of word in book I, chapter XIII, saying that word is the natural movement of the intellect, according to which it is moved and understands and thinks, like light and splendor, with respect to the first; again, word is that which is not uttered by a word, but is pronounced in the heart, with respect to the third; again, word is also the angel, that is, the messenger, of intelligence, with respect to the second. But, in a fourth way, that which is signified or effected by a word is called word figuratively, as we are accustomed to say, this is the word which I told you, or which the king commanded, while pointing to some deed that was signified by a word either of one simply declaring, or also of one commanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","text":"But word is properly said in God according as word signifies a concept of the intellect. Hence Augustine says, in XV *On the Trinity*, *whoever can understand a word, not only before it sounds, but even before images of its sounds are involved in thought, can already see some likeness of that Word of which it was said, in the beginning was the Word*. Now the very concept of the heart has, by its nature, that it proceeds from another, namely, from the knowledge of the one conceiving. Hence Word, according as it is properly said in divine things, signifies something proceeding from another, which belongs to the account of personal names in divine things, because the divine persons are distinguished according to origin, as has been said. Hence the name Word, according as it is properly taken in divine things, must not be taken essentially, but only personally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0019","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0020","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Arians, whose source is found in Origen, held that the Son is other than the Father in diversity of substance. Hence, since the Son of God is called Word, they strove to establish that this is not said properly, lest, under the account of a proceeding word, they be compelled to confess that the Son of God is not outside the substance of the Father; for the interior word so proceeds from the speaker that it remains in him. But it is necessary, if the Word of God is held to be said metaphorically, that the Word of God properly so called be held. For something cannot be called word metaphorically except by reason of manifestation, because either it manifests as a word does, or it is manifested by a word. But if it is manifested by a word, one must posit a word by which it is manifested. And if it is called word because it manifests outwardly, those things which manifest outwardly are not called words except insofar as they signify the interior concept of the mind, which someone also manifests through exterior signs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0021","text":"Therefore, although word is sometimes said metaphorically in divine things, one must nevertheless posit a word properly so called, which is said personally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0022","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0023","text":"To the second, it must be said that none of those things pertaining to intellect is said personally in divine things except Word alone, for Word alone signifies something emanating from another. For that which the intellect forms in conceiving is a word. But the intellect itself, insofar as it is actual through an intelligible species, is considered absolutely. And likewise understanding, which stands to the intellect in act as being stands to a being in act; for understanding does not signify an action going forth from the one understanding, but one remaining in the one understanding. Therefore, when it is said that word is knowledge, knowledge is not taken for the act of the knowing intellect, or for some habit of it, but for that which the intellect conceives by knowing. Hence Augustine also says that Word is begotten wisdom, which is nothing other than the very conception of the wise one, which likewise can be called begotten knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0024","text":"And in the same way it can be understood that for God to speak is to behold by thinking, namely, insofar as the Word of God is conceived by the beholding of divine thought. Nevertheless, the name thought does not properly befit God with respect to the Word; for Augustine says, in XV *On the Trinity*, *that Word of God is so called that it is not called thought, lest something as it were changeable be believed to be in God, which now receives a form in order to be a word, and can dismiss it, and in some manner be rolled about without form*. For thought properly consists in the inquiry after truth, which has no place in God. But when the intellect has already attained the form of truth, it does not think, but perfectly contemplates the truth. Hence Anselm takes thought improperly for contemplation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0025","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0026","text":"To the third, it must be said that, just as, properly speaking, Word is said personally in divine matters and not essentially, so also is speaking. Hence, just as Word is not common to the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, so it is not true that the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit are one speaker. Hence Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, VII: *by speaking that coeternal Word, He is not understood as singly in divine matters*. But to be said befits any person, for not only the word but also the thing understood or signified by the word is said. Thus, therefore, to one person alone in divine matters does being said belong in the manner in which Word is said; but in the manner in which a thing understood in the word is said, being said belongs to every person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027","text":"For the Father, by understanding Himself and the Son and the Holy Spirit, and all other things which are contained in His knowledge, conceives the Word, so that in this way the whole Trinity is said by the Word, and likewise every creature; just as the human intellect, by the word which it conceives in understanding a stone, says the stone. Anselm, however, took speaking improperly for understanding. Yet these differ. For understanding imports only the relation of the one understanding to the thing understood; in this no notion of origin is imported, but only a certain informing in our intellect, insofar as our intellect is made actual through the form of the thing understood. In God, however, it imports complete identity, because in God intellect and thing understood are altogether the same, as was shown above. But speaking principally imports a relation to the conceived word; for speaking is nothing other than uttering a word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0028","text":"But through the mediation of the word it imports a relation to the thing understood, which is manifested to the one understanding in the uttered word. And thus only the person who utters the Word is speaker in divine matters, although each of the persons is understanding and understood, and consequently said by the Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0029","text":"[I q. 34 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0030","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Word is taken there figuratively, insofar as the thing signified or the effect of a word is called word. For thus creatures are said to do the word of God, insofar as they carry out some effect to which they are ordered by the word conceived by divine wisdom, just as someone is said to do the word of a king when he performs the work to which he is prompted by the word of the king.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0031","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0032","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0033","text":"We proceed to the second thus. It seems that Word is not a proper name of the Son. For the Son is a subsisting person in divine matters. But word does not signify a subsisting thing, as is evident among us. Therefore Word cannot be the proper name of the person of the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0034","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0035","text":"Further, a word proceeds from one speaking by a certain utterance. Therefore, if the Son is properly Word, He proceeds from the Father only by way of utterance. This is the heresy of Valentinus, as is evident from Augustine in the book *On Heresies*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0036","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0037","text":"Further, every proper name of some person signifies some property of that person. Therefore, if Word is the proper name of the Son, it will signify some property of Him. And thus there will be more properties in divine matters than those enumerated above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0038","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0039","text":"Further, whoever understands, by understanding conceives a word. But the Son understands. Therefore the Son has some word. And thus it is not proper to the Son to be Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0040","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0041","text":"Further, in Heb. I it is said of the Son: bearing all things by the word of His power, from which Basil takes that the Holy Spirit is the word of the Son. Therefore it is not proper to the Son to be Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0042","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0043","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, VI: *Word is understood of the Son alone*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0044","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0045","text":"I answer that Word, properly so called in divine matters, is taken personally, and is the proper name of the person of the Son. For it signifies a certain emanation of intellect; but the person who proceeds in divine matters according to an emanation of intellect is called Son, and such a procession is called generation, as was shown above. Hence it follows that the Son alone is properly called Word in divine matters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0046","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0047","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in us being and understanding are not the same; hence that which has intelligible being in us does not belong to our nature. But the being of God is His very understanding; hence the Word of God is not some accident in Him, or some effect of Him, but belongs to His very nature. And therefore it must be something subsisting, because whatever is in the nature of God subsists. And therefore Damascene says that *the Word of God is substantial and existing in hypostasis, whereas the other words*, namely ours, *are powers of the soul*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0048","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0049","text":"To the second, it must be said that the error of Valentinus was condemned not because he said that the Son was born by utterance, as the Arians slanderously alleged, as Hilary reports in *On the Trinity*, VI, but because of the various mode of utterance that he posited, as is clear from Augustine in the book *On Heresies*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0050","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0051","text":"To the third, it must be said that in the name Word the same property is conveyed as in the name Son; hence Augustine says, *by that by which he is called Word, he is called Son*. For the very birth of the Son, which is his personal property, is signified by diverse names, which are attributed to the Son in order to express his perfection in different ways. For to show that he is connatural with the Father, he is called Son; to show that he is coeternal, he is called Splendor; to show that he is altogether similar, he is called Image; to show that he is generated immaterially, he is called Word. But no one name could be found through which all these things would be designated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0052","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0053","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that it belongs to the Son to be intelligent in the same way as it belongs to him to be God, since in divine matters to understand is said essentially, as has been said. But the Son is God begotten, not God begetting. Hence he is indeed intelligent, not as producing the Word, but as the proceeding Word; namely, insofar as in God the Word proceeding does not differ in reality from the divine intellect, but is distinguished from the principle of the Word by relation alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0054","text":"[I q. 34 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0055","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, when it is said of the Son, “bearing all things by the word of his power,” word is taken figuratively for the effect of the word. Hence the Gloss there says that word is taken for command; namely, insofar as it is from the effect of the power of the word that things are preserved in being, just as it is from the effect of the power of the word that things are brought forth into being. But when Basil interprets word as the Holy Spirit, he speaks improperly and figuratively, insofar as anything whatsoever that manifests someone can be called that person's word; thus, for this reason the Holy Spirit is called the Word of the Son, because he manifests the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0056","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0057","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0058","text":"We proceed to the third as follows. It seems that in the name Word no relation to the creature is conveyed. For every name connoting an effect in a creature is said essentially in divine matters. But Word is not said essentially, but personally, as has been said. Therefore Word does not convey a relation to the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0059","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0060","text":"Further, things that convey a relation to creatures are said of God in time, such as Lord and Creator. But Word is said of God from eternity. Therefore it does not convey a relation to the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0061","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0062","text":"Further, Word conveys a relation to that from which it proceeds. Therefore, if it conveys a relation to the creature, it follows that it proceeds from the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0063","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0064","text":"Further, ideas are multiple according to diverse relations to creatures. Therefore, if Word conveys a relation to creatures, it follows that there is not only one Word in God, but several.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0065","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0066","text":"Further, if Word conveys a relation to the creature, this is only insofar as creatures are known by God. But God knows not only beings, but also non-beings. Therefore a relation to non-beings will be conveyed in the Word, which seems false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0067","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0068","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions* that *in the name Word is signified not only a relation to the Father, but also to those things which were made through the Word by operative power*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0069","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","text":"I answer that it must be said that in the Word a relation to the creature is conveyed. For God, by knowing himself, knows every creature. But the Word conceived in the mind is representative of everything that is actually understood. Hence in us there are diverse words, according to the diverse things that we understand. But because God understands both himself and all things by one act, his one Word is expressive not only of the Father, but also of creatures. And just as God's knowledge of God is only cognitive, whereas his knowledge of creatures is cognitive and productive, so the Word of God is only expressive of that which is in God the Father, whereas of creatures it is expressive and operative. And on this account it is said in Psalm XXXII, *he spoke, and they were made*; because in the Word is conveyed the productive intelligible account of those things which God makes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0071","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0072","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the name person also includes nature obliquely, for a person is an individual substance of a rational nature. Therefore, in the name of a divine person, as regards the personal relation, a relation to the creature is not conveyed, but it is conveyed in that which pertains to nature. Nevertheless, nothing prevents, insofar as the essence is included in its signification, a relation to the creature from being conveyed; for just as it is proper to the Son that he be Son, so it is proper to him that he be God begotten, or Creator begotten. And in this way a relation to the creature is conveyed in the name Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0073","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0074","text":"To the second, it must be said that, since relations follow upon actions, certain names signify the relation of God to the creature, which follows upon God's action passing over into an external effect, such as to create and to govern; and such things are said of God in time. But certain [names signify] the relation that follows upon an action not passing over into an external effect, but remaining in the agent, such as to know and to will; and such things are not said of God in time. And a relation of this kind to the creature is signified in the name Word. Nor is it true that all names signifying the relation of God to creatures are said in time; rather, only those names that signify the relation following upon God's action passing over into an external effect are said in time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0075","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0076","text":"To the third, it must be said that creatures are not known by God through knowledge received from creatures, but through His essence. Hence it is not necessary that the Word proceed from creatures, although the Word is expressive of creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0077","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0078","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the name idea is principally imposed to signify a respect to the creature, and therefore it is said in the plural in divine matters, nor is it personal. But the name Word is principally imposed to signify the relation to the speaker, and consequently to creatures, insofar as God, by understanding Himself, understands every creature. And on this account there is only one Word in divine matters, and it is said personally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0079","text":"[I q. 34 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0080","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that in the same way in which God's knowledge is of non-beings, God's Word is also of non-beings, because there is not anything less in God's Word than in God's knowledge, as Augustine says. Yet the Word is of beings as expressive and productive, but of non-beings as expressive and manifestative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0080"]}],"source_digest":"6859caa63dd693ad7f25805f05f22870c58e0b3520b2b73604975eba1affb5a6","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 34"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 34","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27","title":"First Part · Question 35","order":36,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_27","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 35 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29878)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de imagine. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum imago in divinis dicatur personaliter. Secundo, utrum sit proprium filii.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29879)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago non dicatur personaliter in divinis. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *una est sanctae Trinitatis divinitas et imago, ad quam factus est homo*. Igitur imago dicitur essentialiter, et non personaliter.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29880)\n\nPraeterea, Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., quod *imago est eius rei ad quam imaginatur, species indifferens*. Sed species, sive forma, in divinis dicitur essentialiter. Ergo et imago.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29881)\n\nPraeterea, imago ab imitando dicitur, in quo importatur prius et posterius. Sed in divinis personis nihil est prius et posterius ergo imago non potest esse nomen personale in divinis.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29882)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, *quid est absurdius quam imaginem ad se dici?* Ergo imago in divinis relative dicitur. Et sic est nomen personale.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29883)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod de ratione imaginis est similitudo. Non tamen quaecumque similitudo sufficit ad rationem imaginis; sed similitudo quae est in specie rei, vel saltem in aliquo signo speciei. Signum autem speciei in rebus corporeis maxime videtur esse figura, videmus enim quod diversorum animalium secundum speciem, sunt diversae figurae, non autem diversi colores. Unde, si depingatur color alicuius rei in pariete, non dicitur esse imago, nisi depingatur figura. Sed neque ipsa similitudo speciei sufficit vel figurae; sed requiritur ad rationem imaginis origo, quia, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., unum ovum non est imago alterius, quia non est de illo expressum. Ad hoc ergo quod vere aliquid sit imago, requiritur quod ex alio procedat simile ei in specie, vel saltem in signo speciei. Ea vero quae processionem sive originem important in divinis, sunt personalia. Unde hoc nomen imago est nomen personale.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29884)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod imago proprie dicitur quod procedit ad similitudinem alterius. Illud autem ad cuius similitudinem aliquid procedit, proprie dicitur exemplar, improprie vero imago. Sic tamen Augustinus utitur nomine imaginis, cum dicit divinitatem sanctae Trinitatis esse imaginem ad quam factus est homo.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29885)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod species, prout ponitur ab Hilario in definitione imaginis, importat formam deductam in aliquo ab alio. Hoc enim modo imago dicitur esse species alicuius, sicuti id quod assimilatur alicui, dicitur forma eius, inquantum habet formam illi similem.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29886)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod imitatio in divinis personis non significat posterioritatem, sed solam assimilationem.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29887)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomen imaginis non sit proprium filio. Quia, ut dicit Damascenus, spiritus sanctus est imago filii. Non est ergo proprium filii.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29888)\n\nPraeterea, de ratione imaginis est similitudo cum expressione, ut Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest. Sed hoc convenit spiritui sancto, procedit enim ab alio secundum modum similitudinis. Ergo spiritus sanctus est imago. Et ita non est proprium filii quod sit imago.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29889)\n\nPraeterea, homo etiam dicitur imago Dei, secundum illud I ad Cor. XI, *vir non debet velare caput suum, quoniam imago et gloria Dei est*. Ergo non est proprium filio.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29890)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod *solus filius est imago patris*.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29891)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod doctores Graecorum communiter dicunt spiritum sanctum esse imaginem patris et filii. Sed doctores Latini soli filio attribuunt nomen imaginis, non enim invenitur in canonica Scriptura nisi de filio. Dicitur enim Coloss. I, *qui est imago Dei invisibilis, primogenitus creaturae*; et ad Hebr. I, *qui cum sit splendor gloriae, et figura substantiae eius*. Huius autem rationem assignant quidam ex hoc, quod filius convenit cum patre non solum in natura, sed etiam in notione principii, spiritus autem sanctus non convenit cum filio nec cum patre in aliqua notione. Sed hoc non videtur sufficere. Quia sicut secundum relationes non attenditur in divinis neque aequalitas neque inaequalitas, ut Augustinus dicit; ita neque similitudo, quae requiritur ad rationem imaginis. Unde alii dicunt quod spiritus sanctus non potest dici imago filii, quia imaginis non est imago. Neque etiam imago patris, quia etiam imago refertur immediate ad id cuius est imago; spiritus sanctus autem refertur ad patrem per filium. Neque etiam est imago patris et filii, quia sic esset una imago duorum, quod videtur impossibile. Unde relinquitur quod spiritus sanctus nullo modo sit imago. Sed hoc nihil est. Quia pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, ut infra dicetur, unde nihil prohibet sic patris et filii, inquantum sunt unum, esse unam imaginem; cum etiam homo totius Trinitatis sit una imago. Et ideo aliter dicendum est quod, sicut spiritus sanctus, quamvis sua processione accipiat naturam patris, sicut et filius, non tamen dicitur natus; ita, licet accipiat speciem similem patris, non dicitur imago. Quia filius procedit ut verbum, de cuius ratione est similitudo speciei ad id a quo procedit; non autem de ratione amoris; quamvis hoc conveniat amori qui est spiritus sanctus, inquantum est amor divinus.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29892)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Damascenus et alii doctores Graecorum communiter utuntur nomine imaginis pro perfecta similitudine.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29893)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet spiritus sanctus sit similis patri et filio, non tamen sequitur quod sit imago, ratione iam dicta.\n\n[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29894)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod imago alicuius dupliciter in aliquo invenitur. Uno modo, in re eiusdem naturae secundum speciem, ut imago regis invenitur in filio suo. Alio modo, in re alterius naturae, sicut imago regis invenitur in denario. Primo autem modo, filius est imago patris, secundo autem modo dicitur homo imago Dei. Et ideo ad designandam in homine imperfectionem imaginis, homo non solum dicitur imago, sed ad imaginem, per quod motus quidam tendentis in perfectionem designatur. Sed de filio Dei non potest dici quod sit ad imaginem, quia est perfecta patris imago.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0002","text":"[I q. 35 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de imagine. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum imago in divinis dicatur personaliter. Secundo, utrum sit proprium filii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0005","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago non dicatur personaliter in divinis. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *una est sanctae Trinitatis divinitas et imago, ad quam factus est homo*. Igitur imago dicitur essentialiter, et non personaliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0007","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., quod *imago est eius rei ad quam imaginatur, species indifferens*. Sed species, sive forma, in divinis dicitur essentialiter. Ergo et imago.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0009","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0010","text":"Praeterea, imago ab imitando dicitur, in quo importatur prius et posterius. Sed in divinis personis nihil est prius et posterius ergo imago non potest esse nomen personale in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0011","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, *quid est absurdius quam imaginem ad se dici?* Ergo imago in divinis relative dicitur. Et sic est nomen personale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0013","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod de ratione imaginis est similitudo. Non tamen quaecumque similitudo sufficit ad rationem imaginis; sed similitudo quae est in specie rei, vel saltem in aliquo signo speciei. Signum autem speciei in rebus corporeis maxime videtur esse figura, videmus enim quod diversorum animalium secundum speciem, sunt diversae figurae, non autem diversi colores. Unde, si depingatur color alicuius rei in pariete, non dicitur esse imago, nisi depingatur figura. Sed neque ipsa similitudo speciei sufficit vel figurae; sed requiritur ad rationem imaginis origo, quia, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., unum ovum non est imago alterius, quia non est de illo expressum. Ad hoc ergo quod vere aliquid sit imago, requiritur quod ex alio procedat simile ei in specie, vel saltem in signo speciei. Ea vero quae processionem sive originem important in divinis, sunt personalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0015","text":"Unde hoc nomen imago est nomen personale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0016","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod imago proprie dicitur quod procedit ad similitudinem alterius. Illud autem ad cuius similitudinem aliquid procedit, proprie dicitur exemplar, improprie vero imago. Sic tamen Augustinus utitur nomine imaginis, cum dicit divinitatem sanctae Trinitatis esse imaginem ad quam factus est homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0018","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod species, prout ponitur ab Hilario in definitione imaginis, importat formam deductam in aliquo ab alio. Hoc enim modo imago dicitur esse species alicuius, sicuti id quod assimilatur alicui, dicitur forma eius, inquantum habet formam illi similem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0020","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod imitatio in divinis personis non significat posterioritatem, sed solam assimilationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0023","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomen imaginis non sit proprium filio. Quia, ut dicit Damascenus, spiritus sanctus est imago filii. Non est ergo proprium filii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0025","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0026","text":"Praeterea, de ratione imaginis est similitudo cum expressione, ut Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest. Sed hoc convenit spiritui sancto, procedit enim ab alio secundum modum similitudinis. Ergo spiritus sanctus est imago. Et ita non est proprium filii quod sit imago.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0027","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0028","text":"Praeterea, homo etiam dicitur imago Dei, secundum illud I ad Cor. XI, *vir non debet velare caput suum, quoniam imago et gloria Dei est*. Ergo non est proprium filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0029","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod *solus filius est imago patris*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0031","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod doctores Graecorum communiter dicunt spiritum sanctum esse imaginem patris et filii. Sed doctores Latini soli filio attribuunt nomen imaginis, non enim invenitur in canonica Scriptura nisi de filio. Dicitur enim Coloss. I, *qui est imago Dei invisibilis, primogenitus creaturae*; et ad Hebr. I, *qui cum sit splendor gloriae, et figura substantiae eius*. Huius autem rationem assignant quidam ex hoc, quod filius convenit cum patre non solum in natura, sed etiam in notione principii, spiritus autem sanctus non convenit cum filio nec cum patre in aliqua notione. Sed hoc non videtur sufficere. Quia sicut secundum relationes non attenditur in divinis neque aequalitas neque inaequalitas, ut Augustinus dicit; ita neque similitudo, quae requiritur ad rationem imaginis. Unde alii dicunt quod spiritus sanctus non potest dici imago filii, quia imaginis non est imago.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0033","text":"Neque etiam imago patris, quia etiam imago refertur immediate ad id cuius est imago; spiritus sanctus autem refertur ad patrem per filium. Neque etiam est imago patris et filii, quia sic esset una imago duorum, quod videtur impossibile. Unde relinquitur quod spiritus sanctus nullo modo sit imago. Sed hoc nihil est. Quia pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, ut infra dicetur, unde nihil prohibet sic patris et filii, inquantum sunt unum, esse unam imaginem; cum etiam homo totius Trinitatis sit una imago. Et ideo aliter dicendum est quod, sicut spiritus sanctus, quamvis sua processione accipiat naturam patris, sicut et filius, non tamen dicitur natus; ita, licet accipiat speciem similem patris, non dicitur imago.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0034","text":"Quia filius procedit ut verbum, de cuius ratione est similitudo speciei ad id a quo procedit; non autem de ratione amoris; quamvis hoc conveniat amori qui est spiritus sanctus, inquantum est amor divinus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0035","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Damascenus et alii doctores Graecorum communiter utuntur nomine imaginis pro perfecta similitudine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0037","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet spiritus sanctus sit similis patri et filio, non tamen sequitur quod sit imago, ratione iam dicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0039","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod imago alicuius dupliciter in aliquo invenitur. Uno modo, in re eiusdem naturae secundum speciem, ut imago regis invenitur in filio suo. Alio modo, in re alterius naturae, sicut imago regis invenitur in denario. Primo autem modo, filius est imago patris, secundo autem modo dicitur homo imago Dei. Et ideo ad designandam in homine imperfectionem imaginis, homo non solum dicitur imago, sed ad imaginem, per quod motus quidam tendentis in perfectionem designatur. Sed de filio Dei non potest dici quod sit ad imaginem, quia est perfecta patris imago.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2875d585b41043e158ea6fcceae58f593d9c388dd37606d1820df952bd04e57d","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141632328852Z_1849301d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 35","source_sha256":"2875d585b41043e158ea6fcceae58f593d9c388dd37606d1820df952bd04e57d","sha256":"56e1822de6e8a3e332ea8397d34a34345bba403b5db6401d3295ba31a07ad295","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:16:32.328863+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0002","text":"[I q. 35 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0003","text":"Next, there is inquiry concerning image. And concerning this, two questions are asked. First, whether image is said personally in divine things. Second, whether it is proper to the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0005","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0006","text":"The first is proceeded with thus. It seems that image is not said personally in divine things. For Augustine says, in the book *On Faith to Peter*, “the divinity and image of the Holy Trinity is one, according to which man was made.” Therefore, image is said essentially and not personally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0007","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0008","text":"Further, Hilary says, in the book *On the Synods*, that “an image is a species indistinguishable from that thing according to which it is imaged.” But species, or form, is said essentially in divine things. Therefore, so also is image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0009","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0010","text":"Further, image is so called from imitating, in which prior and posterior are implied. But in the divine persons there is nothing prior or posterior; therefore image cannot be a personal name in divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0011","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, “what is more absurd than for an image to be said with reference to itself?” Therefore image is said relatively in divine things. And thus it is a personal name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0013","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that likeness belongs to the notion of image. Yet not every likeness suffices for the notion of image, but a likeness that is in the species of a thing, or at least in some sign of the species. Now, in corporeal things, figure seems especially to be a sign of species; for we see that animals diverse according to species have diverse figures, but not diverse colors. Hence, if the color of some thing is painted upon a wall, it is not called an image unless its figure is painted. But neither does likeness itself in species or figure suffice; rather, origin is required for the notion of image, because, as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, one egg is not the image of another, because it is not expressed from it. Therefore, for something truly to be an image, it is required that it proceed from another, like to it in species, or at least in a sign of the species. But those things that imply procession or origin in divine things are personal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0015","text":"Hence this name image is a personal name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0016","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that image is properly said of that which proceeds unto the likeness of another. But that to the likeness of which something proceeds is properly called an exemplar, but improperly an image. Augustine nevertheless uses the name image in this way when he says that the divinity of the Holy Trinity is the image according to which man was made.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0018","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that species, insofar as it is set down by Hilary in the definition of image, implies a form derived in something from another. For in this way an image is said to be the species of something, just as that which is made like something is called its form, insofar as it has a form like to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0020","text":"[I q. 35 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that imitation in the divine persons does not signify posteriority, but likeness alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0023","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0024","text":"The second is proceeded with thus. It seems that the name image is not proper to the Son. For, as Damascene says, the Holy Spirit is the image of the Son. Therefore it is not proper to the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0025","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0026","text":"Further, it belongs to the notion of an image that there be likeness with expression, as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*. But this belongs to the Holy Spirit, for he proceeds from another according to the mode of likeness. Therefore the Holy Spirit is an image. And thus it is not proper to the Son that he be an image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0027","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0028","text":"Further, man too is called the image of God, according to I Corinthians 11: *a man ought not to cover his head, since he is the image and glory of God*. Therefore this is not proper to the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0029","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in VI *On the Trinity*, that *the Son alone is the image of the Father*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0031","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Greek doctors commonly say that the Holy Spirit is the image of the Father and the Son. But the Latin doctors attribute the name of image to the Son alone; for it is not found in canonical Scripture except concerning the Son. For it is said in Colossians 1: *who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature*; and in Hebrews 1: *who, being the brightness of glory and the figure of his substance*. Some assign the reason for this from the fact that the Son agrees with the Father not only in nature, but also in the notion of principle, whereas the Holy Spirit agrees neither with the Son nor with the Father in any notion. But this does not seem sufficient. For just as, according to the relations, neither equality nor inequality is considered in divine things, as Augustine says, so neither is likeness, which is required for the notion of image. Hence others say that the Holy Spirit cannot be called the image of the Son, because an image is not the image of an image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0033","text":"Nor is he the image of the Father, because an image too is referred immediately to that of which it is the image; but the Holy Spirit is referred to the Father through the Son. Nor again is he the image of the Father and the Son, because in that case he would be one image of two, which seems impossible. Hence it remains that the Holy Spirit is in no way an image. But this is of no account. For the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit, as will be said below; hence nothing prevents there being one image of the Father and the Son insofar as they are one, since man too is one image of the whole Trinity. And therefore it must be said otherwise: that, just as the Holy Spirit, although by his procession he receives the nature of the Father, as does the Son, is nevertheless not said to be begotten, so too, although he receives a form like the Father, he is not called an image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0034","text":"For the Son proceeds as Word, to whose notion belongs the likeness of species to that from which it proceeds; but this does not belong to the notion of love, although it belongs to the love that is the Holy Spirit, insofar as he is divine love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0035","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Damascene and the other Greek doctors commonly use the name of image for perfect likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0037","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0038","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although the Holy Spirit is like the Father and the Son, it nevertheless does not follow that he is an image, for the reason already given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0039","text":"[I q. 35 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that the image of something is found in something else in two ways. In one way, in a thing of the same nature according to species, as the image of a king is found in his son. In another way, in a thing of another nature, as the image of a king is found on a coin. In the first way, the Son is the image of the Father; but in the second way man is called the image of God. And therefore, in order to designate the imperfection of the image in man, man is not only called an image, but also [made] to the image, by which a certain movement of one tending toward perfection is designated. But of the Son of God it cannot be said that he is to the image, because he is the perfect image of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_27:P0040"]}],"source_digest":"dd98afad8c4f1db0696f217af2f4999fa871aa8dabd96e7a417fb274d64aca69","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 35"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 35","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28","title":"First Part · Question 36","order":37,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_28","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 36 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29895)\n\nPost haec considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad personam spiritus sancti. Qui quidem non solum dicitur spiritus sanctus, sed etiam amor et donum Dei. Circa nomen ergo spiritus sancti quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum hoc nomen spiritus sanctus sit proprium alicuius divinae personae. Secundo, utrum illa persona divina quae spiritus sanctus dicitur, procedat a patre et filio. Tertio, utrum procedat a patre per filium. Quarto, utrum pater et filius sint unum principium spiritus sancti.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29896)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen spiritus sanctus non sit proprium nomen alicuius divinae personae. Nullum enim nomen commune tribus personis, est proprium alicuius personae. Sed hoc nomen spiritus sanctus est commune tribus personis. Ostendit enim Hilarius, VIII de Trin., in spiritu Dei aliquando significari patrem, ut cum dicitur, spiritus domini super me; aliquando significari filium, ut cum dicit filius, in spiritu Dei eiicio Daemonia, naturae suae potestate eiicere se Daemonia demonstrans; aliquando spiritum sanctum, ut ibi, effundam de spiritu meo super omnem carnem. Ergo hoc nomen spiritus sanctus non est proprium alicuius divinae personae.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29897)\n\nPraeterea, nomina divinarum personarum ad aliquid dicuntur, ut Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin. Sed hoc nomen spiritus sanctus non dicitur ad aliquid. Ergo hoc nomen non est proprium divinae personae.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29898)\n\nPraeterea, quia filius est nomen alicuius divinae personae, non potest dici filius huius vel illius. Dicitur autem spiritus huius vel illius hominis. Ut enim habetur Num. XI, *dixit dominus ad Moysen, auferam de spiritu tuo, tradamque eis*; et IV Reg. II, *requievit spiritus Eliae super Elisaeum*. Ergo spiritus sanctus non videtur esse proprium nomen alicuius divinae personae.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29899)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur I Ioan. ult., *tres sunt qui testimonium dant in caelo, pater, verbum et spiritus sanctus*. Ut autem Augustinus dicit, VII de Trin., cum quaeritur, quid tres? Dicimus, tres personae. Ergo spiritus sanctus est nomen divinae personae.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29900)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum sint duae processiones in divinis, altera earum, quae est per modum amoris, non habet proprium nomen, ut supra dictum est. Unde et relationes quae secundum huiusmodi processionem accipiuntur, innominatae sunt, ut etiam supra dictum est. Propter quod et nomen personae hoc modo procedentis, eadem ratione, non habet proprium nomen. Sed sicut sunt accommodata aliqua nomina, ex usu loquentium, ad significandum praedictas relationes, cum nominamus eas nomine processionis et spirationis, quae, secundum proprietatem significationis, magis videntur significare actus notionales quam relationes; ita ad significandum divinam personam quae procedit per modum amoris, accommodatum est, ex usu Scripturae, hoc nomen spiritus sanctus. Et huius quidem convenientiae ratio sumi potest ex duobus. Primo quidem, ex ipsa communitate eius quod dicitur spiritus sanctus. Ut enim Augustinus dicit, XV de Trin., *quia spiritus sanctus communis est ambobus, id vocatur ipse proprie quod ambo communiter, nam et pater est spiritus, et filius est spiritus; et pater est sanctus, et filius est sanctus*. Secundo vero, ex propria significatione. Nam nomen spiritus, in rebus corporeis, impulsionem quandam et motionem significare videtur, nam flatum et ventum spiritum nominamus. Est autem proprium amoris, quod moveat et impellat voluntatem amantis in amatum. Sanctitas vero illis rebus attribuitur, quae in Deum ordinantur. Quia igitur persona divina procedit per modum amoris quo Deus amatur, convenienter spiritus sanctus nominatur.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29901)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc quod dico spiritus sanctus, prout sumitur in virtute duarum dictionum, commune est toti Trinitati. Quia nomine spiritus significatur immaterialitas divinae substantiae, spiritus enim corporeus invisibilis est, et parum habet de materia; unde omnibus substantiis immaterialibus et invisibilibus hoc nomen attribuimus. Per hoc vero quod dicitur sanctus, significatur puritas divinae bonitatis. Si autem accipiatur hoc quod dico spiritus sanctus, in vi unius dictionis, sic ex usu Ecclesiae est accommodatum ad significandam unam trium personarum, scilicet quae procedit per modum amoris, ratione iam dicta.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29902)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet hoc quod dico spiritus sanctus, relative non dicatur, tamen pro relativo ponitur, inquantum est accommodatum ad significandam personam sola relatione ab aliis distinctam. Potest tamen intelligi etiam in nomine aliqua relatio, si spiritus intelligatur quasi spiratus.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29903)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in nomine filii intelligitur sola relatio eius qui est a principio, ad principium, sed in nomine patris intelligitur relatio principii; et similiter in nomine spiritus, prout importat quandam vim motivam. Nulli autem creaturae competit esse principium respectu alicuius divinae personae, sed e converso. Et ideo potest dici pater noster, et spiritus noster, non tamen potest dici filius noster.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29904)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod spiritus sanctus non procedat a filio. Quia secundum Dionysium, *non est audendum dicere aliquid de substantiali divinitate, praeter ea quae divinitus nobis ex sacris eloquiis sunt expressa*. Sed in Scriptura sacra non exprimitur quod spiritus sanctus a filio procedat, sed solum quod procedat a patre; ut patet Ioann. XV, *spiritum veritatis, qui a patre procedit*. Ergo spiritus sanctus non procedit a filio.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29905)\n\nPraeterea, in symbolo Constantinopolitanae synodi sic legitur, *credimus in spiritum sanctum, dominum et vivificantem, ex patre procedentem, cum patre et filio adorandum et glorificandum*. Nullo igitur modo debuit addi in symbolo nostro quod spiritus sanctus procedat a filio, sed videntur esse anathematis rei, qui hoc addiderunt.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29906)\n\nPraeterea, Damascenus dicit, *spiritum sanctum ex patre dicimus, et spiritum patris nominamus, ex filio autem spiritum sanctum non dicimus, spiritum vero filii nominamus*. Ergo spiritus sanctus non procedit a filio.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29907)\n\nPraeterea, nihil procedit ab eo in quo quiescit. Sed spiritus sanctus quiescit in filio. Dicitur enim in legenda beati Andreae, *pax vobis, et universis qui credunt in unum Deum patrem, et in unum filium eius, unicum dominum nostrum Iesum Christum, et in unum spiritum sanctum, procedentem ex patre, et in filio permanentem*. Ergo spiritus sanctus non procedit a filio.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29908)\n\nPraeterea, filius procedit ut verbum. Sed spiritus noster in nobis non videtur procedere a verbo nostro. Ergo nec spiritus sanctus procedit a filio.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29909)\n\nPraeterea, spiritus sanctus perfecte procedit a patre. Ergo superfluum est dicere quod procedit a filio.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29910)\n\nPraeterea, in perpetuis non differt esse et posse, ut dicitur in III Physic.; et multo minus in divinis. Sed spiritus sanctus potest distingui a filio, etiam si ab eo non procedat. Dicit enim Anselmus, in libro de processione spiritus sancti, *habent utique a patre esse filius et spiritus sanctus, sed diverso modo, quia alter nascendo, et alter procedendo, ut alii sint per hoc ab invicem*. Et postea subdit, *nam si per aliud non essent plures filius et spiritus sanctus, per hoc solum essent diversi*. Ergo spiritus sanctus distinguitur a filio, ab eo non existens.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29911)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Athanasius, *spiritus sanctus a patre et filio, non factus, nec creatus, nec genitus, sed procedens*.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29912)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere spiritum sanctum a filio esse. Si enim non esset ab eo, nullo modo posset ab eo personaliter distingui. Quod ex supra dictis patet. Non enim est possibile dicere quod secundum aliquid absolutum divinae personae ab invicem distinguantur, quia sequeretur quod non esset trium una essentia; quidquid enim in divinis absolute dicitur, ad unitatem essentiae pertinet. Relinquitur ergo quod solum relationibus divinae personae ab invicem distinguantur. Relationes autem personas distinguere non possunt, nisi secundum quod sunt oppositae. Quod ex hoc patet, quia pater habet duas relationes, quarum una refertur ad filium, et alia ad spiritum sanctum; quae tamen, quia non sunt oppositae, non constituunt duas personas, sed ad unam personam patris tantum pertinent. Si ergo in filio et in spiritu sancto non esset invenire nisi duas relationes quibus uterque refertur ad patrem, illae relationes non essent ad invicem oppositae; sicut neque duae relationes quibus pater refertur ad illos. Unde, sicut persona patris est una, ita sequeretur quod persona filii et spiritus sancti esset una, habens duas relationes oppositas duabus relationibus patris. Hoc autem est haereticum, cum tollat fidem Trinitatis. Oportet ergo quod filius et spiritus sanctus ad invicem referantur oppositis relationibus. Non autem possunt esse in divinis aliae relationes oppositae nisi relationes originis, ut supra probatum est. Oppositae autem relationes originis accipiuntur secundum principium, et secundum quod est a principio. Relinquitur ergo quod necesse est dicere vel filium esse a spiritu sancto, quod nullus dicit, vel spiritum sanctum esse a filio, quod nos confitemur. Et huic quidem consonat ratio processionis utriusque. Dictum enim est supra quod filius procedit per modum intellectus, ut verbum; spiritus sanctus autem per modum voluntatis, ut amor. Necesse est autem quod amor a verbo procedat, non enim aliquid amamus, nisi secundum quod conceptione mentis apprehendimus. Unde et secundum hoc manifestum est quod spiritus sanctus procedit a filio. Ipse etiam ordo rerum hoc docet. Nusquam enim hoc invenimus, quod ab uno procedant plura absque ordine, nisi in illis solum quae materialiter differunt; sicut unus faber producit multos cultellos materialiter ab invicem distinctos, nullum ordinem habentes ad invicem. Sed in rebus in quibus non est sola materialis distinctio, semper invenitur in multitudine productorum aliquis ordo. Unde etiam in ordine creaturarum productarum, decor divinae sapientiae manifestatur. Si ergo ab una persona patris procedunt duae personae, scilicet filius et spiritus sanctus, oportet esse aliquem ordinem eorum ad invicem. Nec potest aliquis ordo alius assignari, nisi ordo naturae, quo alius est ex alio. Non est igitur possibile dicere quod filius et spiritus sanctus sic procedant a patre, quod neuter eorum procedat ab alio, nisi quis poneret in eis materialem distinctionem, quod est impossibile. Unde etiam ipsi Graeci processionem spiritus sancti aliquem ordinem habere ad filium intelligunt. Concedunt enim spiritum sanctum esse spiritum filii, et esse a patre per filium. Et quidam eorum dicuntur concedere quod sit a filio, vel profluat ab eo, non tamen quod procedat. Quod videtur vel ex ignorantia, vel ex protervia esse. Quia si quis recte consideret, inveniet processionis verbum inter omnia quae ad originem qualemcumque pertinent, communissimum esse. Utimur enim eo ad designandum qualemcumque originem; sicut quod linea procedit a puncto, radius a sole, rivus a fonte; et similiter in quibuscumque aliis. Unde ex quocumque alio ad originem pertinente, potest concludi quod spiritus sanctus procedit a filio.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29913)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod de Deo dicere non debemus quod in sacra Scriptura non invenitur vel per verba, vel per sensum. Licet autem per verba non inveniatur in sacra Scriptura quod spiritus sanctus procedit a filio, invenitur tamen quantum ad sensum; et praecipue ubi dicit filius, Ioan. XVI, de spiritu sancto loquens, *ille me clarificabit, quia de meo accipiet*. Regulariter etiam in sacra Scriptura tenendum est, quod id quod de patre dicitur, oportet de filio intelligi, etiam si dictio exclusiva addatur, nisi solum in illis in quibus pater et filius secundum oppositas relationes distinguuntur. Cum enim dominus, Matth. XI, dicit, *nemo novit filium nisi pater*, non excluditur quin filius seipsum cognoscat. Sic igitur cum dicitur quod spiritus sanctus a patre procedit, etiam si adderetur quod a solo patre procedit, non excluderetur inde filius, quia quantum ad hoc quod est esse principium spiritus sancti, non opponuntur pater et filius; sed solum quantum ad hoc, quod hic est pater et ille filius.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29914)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in quolibet Concilio institutum fuit symbolum aliquod, propter errorem aliquem qui in Concilio damnabatur. Unde sequens Concilium non faciebat aliud symbolum quam primum, sed id quod implicite continebatur in primo symbolo, per aliqua addita explanabatur contra haereses insurgentes. Unde in determinatione Chalcedonensis synodi dicitur, quod illi qui fuerunt congregati in Concilio Constantinopolitano, doctrinam de spiritu sancto tradiderunt, non quod minus esset in praecedentibus (qui apud Nicaeam congregati sunt), inferentes; *sed intellectum eorum adversus haereticos declarantes*. Quia igitur in tempore antiquorum Conciliorum nondum exortus fuerat error dicentium spiritum sanctum non procedere a filio; non fuit necessarium quod hoc explicite poneretur. Sed postea, insurgente errore quorundam, in quodam Concilio in Occidentalibus partibus congregato, expressum fuit auctoritate Romani pontificis; cuius auctoritate etiam antiqua Concilia congregabantur et confirmabantur. Continebatur tamen implicite in hoc ipso quod dicebatur spiritus sanctus a patre procedere.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29915)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod spiritum sanctum non procedere a filio, primo fuit a Nestorianis introductum; ut patet in quodam symbolo Nestorianorum damnato in Ephesina synodo. Et hunc errorem secutus fuit Theodoretus Nestorianus, et plures post ipsum; inter quos fuit etiam Damascenus. Unde in hoc eius sententiae non est standum. Quamvis a quibusdam dicatur quod Damascenus, sicut non confitetur spiritum sanctum esse a filio, ita etiam non negat, ex vi illorum verborum.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29916)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod per hoc quod spiritus sanctus dicitur quiescere vel manere in filio, non excluditur quin ab eo procedat, quia et filius in patre manere dicitur, cum tamen a patre procedat. Dicitur etiam spiritus sanctus in filio quiescere, vel sicut amor amantis quiescit in amato; vel quantum ad humanam naturam Christi, propter id quod scriptum est, Ioan. I, *super quem videris spiritum descendentem, et manentem super eum, hic est qui baptizat*.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29917)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod verbum in divinis non accipitur secundum similitudinem verbi vocalis, a quo non procedit spiritus, quia sic tantum metaphorice diceretur, sed secundum similitudinem verbi mentalis, a quo amor procedit.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29918)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod per hoc quod spiritus sanctus perfecte procedit a patre, non solum non superfluum est dicere quod spiritus sanctus procedat a filio; sed omnino necessarium. Quia una virtus est patris et filii; et quidquid est a patre, necesse est esse a filio, nisi proprietati filiationis repugnet. Non enim filius est a seipso, licet sit a patre.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29919)\n\nAd septimum dicendum quod spiritus sanctus distinguitur personaliter a filio in hoc, quod origo unius distinguitur ab origine alterius. Sed ipsa differentia originis est per hoc, quod filius est solum a patre, spiritus sanctus vero a patre et filio. Non enim aliter processiones distinguerentur, sicut supra ostensum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29920)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod spiritus sanctus non procedat a patre per filium. Quod enim procedit ab aliquo per aliquem, non procedit ab eo immediate. Si igitur spiritus sanctus procedit a patre per filium, non procedit a patre immediate. Quod videtur inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29921)\n\nPraeterea, si spiritus sanctus procedit a patre per filium, non procedit a filio nisi propter patrem. Sed propter quod unumquodque, et illud magis. Ergo magis procedit a patre quam a filio.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29922)\n\nPraeterea, filius habet esse per generationem. Si igitur spiritus sanctus est a patre per filium, sequitur quod prius generetur filius, et postea procedat spiritus sanctus. Et sic processio spiritus sancti non est aeterna. Quod est haereticum.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29923)\n\nPraeterea, cum aliquis dicitur per aliquem operari, potest e converso dici, sicut enim dicimus quod rex operatur per ballivum, ita potest dici quod ballivus operatur per regem. Sed nullo modo dicimus quod filius spiret spiritum sanctum per patrem. Ergo nullo modo potest dici quod pater spiret spiritum sanctum per filium.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29924)\n\nSed contra est quod Hilarius dicit, in libro de Trin., *conserva hanc, oro, fidei meae religionem, ut semper obtineam patrem, scilicet te; et filium tuum una tecum adorem; et spiritum sanctum tuum, qui est per unigenitum tuum, promerear*.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29925)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in omnibus locutionibus in quibus dicitur aliquis per aliquem operari, haec praepositio per designat in causali aliquam causam seu principium illius actus. Sed cum actio sit media inter faciens et factum, quandoque illud causale cui adiungitur haec praepositio per, est causa actionis secundum quod exit ab agente. Et tunc est causa agenti quod agat; sive sit causa finalis, sive formalis, sive effectiva vel motiva, finalis quidem, ut si dicamus quod artifex operatur per cupiditatem lucri; formalis vero, ut si dicamus quod operatur per artem suam; motiva vero, si dicamus quod operatur per imperium alterius. Quandoque vero dictio causalis cui adiungitur haec praepositio per, est causa actionis secundum quod terminatur ad factum; ut cum dicimus, artifex operatur per martellum. Non enim significatur quod martellus sit causa artifici quod agat, sed quod sit causa artificiato ut ab artifice procedat; et quod hoc ipsum habeat ab artifice. Et hoc est quod quidam dicunt, quod haec praepositio per quandoque notat auctoritatem in recto, ut cum dicitur, rex operatur per ballivum, quandoque autem in obliquo, ut cum dicitur, ballivus operatur per regem. Quia igitur filius habet a patre quod ab eo procedat spiritus sanctus, potest dici quod pater per filium spirat spiritum sanctum; vel quod spiritus sanctus procedat a patre per filium, quod idem est.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29926)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in qualibet actione est duo considerare, scilicet suppositum agens, et virtutem qua agit; sicut ignis calefacit calore. Si igitur in patre et filio consideretur virtus qua spirant spiritum sanctum, non cadit ibi aliquod medium, quia haec virtus est una et eadem. Si autem considerentur ipsae personae spirantes, sic, cum spiritus sanctus communiter procedat a patre et filio, invenitur spiritus sanctus immediate a patre procedere, inquantum est ab eo; et mediate, inquantum est a filio. Et sic dicitur procedere a patre per filium. Sicut etiam Abel processit immediate ab Adam, inquantum Adam fuit pater eius; et mediate, inquantum Eva fuit mater eius, quae processit ab Adam; licet hoc exemplum materialis processionis ineptum videatur ad significandam immaterialem processionem divinarum personarum.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29927)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, si filius acciperet a patre aliam virtutem numero ad spirandum spiritum sanctum, sequeretur quod esset sicut causa secunda et instrumentalis, et sic magis procederet a patre quam a filio. Sed una et eadem numero virtus spirativa est in patre et filio, et ideo aequaliter procedit ab utroque. Licet aliquando dicatur principaliter vel proprie procedere de patre, propter hoc quod filius habet hanc virtutem a patre.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29928)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut generatio filii est coaeterna generanti, unde non prius fuit pater quam gigneret filium; ita processio spiritus sancti est coaeterna suo principio. Unde non fuit prius filius genitus, quam spiritus sanctus procederet, sed utrumque aeternum est.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29929)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, cum aliquis dicitur per aliquid operari, non semper recipitur conversio, non enim dicimus quod martellus operetur per fabrum. Dicimus autem quod ballivus operatur per regem, quia ballivi est agere, cum sit dominus sui actus. Martelli autem non est agere, sed solum agi, unde non designatur nisi ut instrumentum. Dicitur autem ballivus operari per regem, quamvis haec praepositio per denotet medium, quia, quanto suppositum est prius in agendo, tanto virtus eius est immediatior effectui, quia virtus causae primae coniungit causam secundam suo effectui, unde et prima principia dicuntur immediata in demonstrativis scientiis. Sic igitur, inquantum ballivus est medius secundum ordinem suppositorum agentium, dicitur rex operari per ballivum, secundum ordinem vero virtutum, dicitur ballivus operari per regem, quia virtus regis facit quod actio ballivi consequatur effectum. Ordo autem non attenditur inter patrem et filium quantum ad virtutem; sed solum quantum ad supposita. Et ideo dicitur quod pater spirat per filium, et non e converso.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29930)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pater et filius non sint unum principium spiritus sancti. Quia spiritus sanctus non videtur a patre et filio procedere inquantum sunt unum, neque in natura, quia spiritus sanctus sic etiam procederet a seipso, qui est unum cum eis in natura; neque etiam inquantum sunt unum in aliqua proprietate, quia una proprietas non potest esse duorum suppositorum, ut videtur. Ergo spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio ut sunt plures. Non ergo pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29931)\n\nPraeterea, cum dicitur, pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, non potest ibi designari unitas personalis, quia sic pater et filius essent una persona. Neque etiam unitas proprietatis, quia si propter unam proprietatem pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, pari ratione, propter duas proprietates pater videtur esse duo principia filii et spiritus sancti; quod est inconveniens. Non ergo pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29932)\n\nPraeterea, filius non magis convenit cum patre quam spiritus sanctus. Sed spiritus sanctus et pater non sunt unum principium respectu alicuius divinae personae. Ergo neque pater et filius.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29933)\n\nPraeterea, si pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti aut unum quod est pater; aut unum quod non est pater. Sed neutrum est dare, quia si unum quod est pater, sequitur quod filius sit pater; si unum quod non est pater, sequitur quod pater non est pater. Non ergo dicendum est quod pater et filius sint unum principium spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29934)\n\nPraeterea, si pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti videtur e converso dicendum quod unum principium spiritus sancti sit pater et filius. Sed haec videtur esse falsa, quia hoc quod dico principium, oportet quod supponat vel pro persona patris, vel pro persona filii; et utroque modo est falsa. Ergo etiam haec est falsa, pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29935)\n\nPraeterea, unum in substantia facit idem. Si igitur pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, sequitur quod sint idem principium. Sed hoc a multis negatur. Ergo non est concedendum quod pater et filius sint unum principium spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29936)\n\nPraeterea, pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, quia sunt unum principium creaturae, dicuntur esse unus creator. Sed pater et filius non sunt unus spirator, sed duo spiratores, ut a multis dicitur. Quod etiam consonat dictis Hilarii, qui dicit, in II de Trin., quod spiritus sanctus a patre et filio auctoribus confitendus est. Ergo pater et filius non sunt unum principium spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29937)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in V de Trin., quod pater et filius non sunt duo principia, sed unum principium spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29938)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod pater et filius in omnibus unum sunt, in quibus non distinguit inter eos relationis oppositio. Unde, cum in hoc quod est esse principium spiritus sancti, non opponantur relative, sequitur quod pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti. Quidam tamen dicunt hanc esse impropriam, pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti. Quia cum hoc nomen principium, singulariter acceptum, non significet personam, sed proprietatem, dicunt quod sumitur adiective, et quia adiectivum non determinatur per adiectivum, non potest convenienter dici quod pater et filius sint unum principium spiritus sancti, nisi unum intelligatur quasi adverbialiter positum, ut sit sensus, sunt unum principium, idest uno modo. Sed simili ratione posset dici pater duo principia filii et spiritus sancti, idest duobus modis. Dicendum est ergo quod, licet hoc nomen principium significet proprietatem, tamen significat eam per modum substantivi, sicut hoc nomen pater vel filius etiam in rebus creatis. Unde numerum accipit a forma significata, sicut et alia substantiva. Sicut igitur pater et filius sunt unus Deus, propter unitatem formae significatae per hoc nomen Deus; ita sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, propter unitatem proprietatis significatae in hoc nomine principium.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29939)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, si attendatur virtus spirativa, spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio inquantum sunt unum in virtute spirativa, quae quodammodo significat naturam cum proprietate, ut infra dicetur. Neque est inconveniens unam proprietatem esse in duobus suppositis, quorum est una natura. Si vero considerentur supposita spirationis, sic spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio ut sunt plures, procedit enim ab eis ut amor unitivus duorum.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29940)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur, pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, designatur una proprietas, quae est forma significata per nomen. Non tamen sequitur quod propter plures proprietates possit dici pater plura principia, quia implicaretur pluralitas suppositorum.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29941)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod secundum relativas proprietates non attenditur in divinis similitudo vel dissimilitudo, sed secundum essentiam. Unde, sicut pater non est similior sibi quam filio, ita nec filius similior patri quam spiritus sanctus.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29942)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod haec duo, scilicet, pater et filius sunt unum principium quod est pater, aut, unum principium quod non est pater, non sunt contradictorie opposita. Unde non est necesse alterum eorum dare. Cum enim dicimus, pater et filius sunt unum principium, hoc quod dico principium, non habet determinatam suppositionem, imo confusam pro duabus personis simul. Unde in processu est fallacia figurae dictionis, a confusa suppositione ad determinatam.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29943)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod haec etiam est vera, unum principium spiritus sancti est pater et filius. Quia hoc quod dico principium non supponit pro una persona tantum, sed indistincte pro duabus, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29944)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod convenienter potest dici quod pater et filius sunt idem principium, secundum quod ly principium supponit confuse et indistincte pro duabus personis simul.\n\n[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29945)\n\nAd septimum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod pater et filius, licet sint unum principium spiritus sancti, sunt tamen duo spiratores, propter distinctionem suppositorum, sicut etiam duo spirantes, quia actus referuntur ad supposita. Nec est eadem ratio de hoc nomine creator. Quia spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio ut sunt duae personae distinctae, ut dictum est, non autem creatura procedit a tribus personis ut sunt personae distinctae, sed ut sunt unum in essentia. Sed videtur melius dicendum quod, quia spirans adiectivum est, spirator vero substantivum, possumus dicere quod pater et filius sunt duo spirantes, propter pluralitatem suppositorum; non autem duo spiratores, propter unam spirationem. Nam adiectiva nomina habent numerum secundum supposita, substantiva vero a seipsis, secundum formam significatam. Quod vero Hilarius dicit, quod spiritus sanctus est a patre et filio auctoribus, exponendum est quod ponitur substantivum pro adiectivo.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0002","text":"[I q. 36 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0003","text":"Post haec considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad personam spiritus sancti. Qui quidem non solum dicitur spiritus sanctus, sed etiam amor et donum Dei. Circa nomen ergo spiritus sancti quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum hoc nomen spiritus sanctus sit proprium alicuius divinae personae. Secundo, utrum illa persona divina quae spiritus sanctus dicitur, procedat a patre et filio. Tertio, utrum procedat a patre per filium. Quarto, utrum pater et filius sint unum principium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0005","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod hoc nomen spiritus sanctus non sit proprium nomen alicuius divinae personae. Nullum enim nomen commune tribus personis, est proprium alicuius personae. Sed hoc nomen spiritus sanctus est commune tribus personis. Ostendit enim Hilarius, VIII de Trin., in spiritu Dei aliquando significari patrem, ut cum dicitur, spiritus domini super me; aliquando significari filium, ut cum dicit filius, in spiritu Dei eiicio Daemonia, naturae suae potestate eiicere se Daemonia demonstrans; aliquando spiritum sanctum, ut ibi, effundam de spiritu meo super omnem carnem. Ergo hoc nomen spiritus sanctus non est proprium alicuius divinae personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0007","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0008","text":"Praeterea, nomina divinarum personarum ad aliquid dicuntur, ut Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin. Sed hoc nomen spiritus sanctus non dicitur ad aliquid. Ergo hoc nomen non est proprium divinae personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0009","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quia filius est nomen alicuius divinae personae, non potest dici filius huius vel illius. Dicitur autem spiritus huius vel illius hominis. Ut enim habetur Num. XI, *dixit dominus ad Moysen, auferam de spiritu tuo, tradamque eis*; et IV Reg. II, *requievit spiritus Eliae super Elisaeum*. Ergo spiritus sanctus non videtur esse proprium nomen alicuius divinae personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0011","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur I Ioan. ult., *tres sunt qui testimonium dant in caelo, pater, verbum et spiritus sanctus*. Ut autem Augustinus dicit, VII de Trin., cum quaeritur, quid tres? Dicimus, tres personae. Ergo spiritus sanctus est nomen divinae personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0013","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum sint duae processiones in divinis, altera earum, quae est per modum amoris, non habet proprium nomen, ut supra dictum est. Unde et relationes quae secundum huiusmodi processionem accipiuntur, innominatae sunt, ut etiam supra dictum est. Propter quod et nomen personae hoc modo procedentis, eadem ratione, non habet proprium nomen. Sed sicut sunt accommodata aliqua nomina, ex usu loquentium, ad significandum praedictas relationes, cum nominamus eas nomine processionis et spirationis, quae, secundum proprietatem significationis, magis videntur significare actus notionales quam relationes; ita ad significandum divinam personam quae procedit per modum amoris, accommodatum est, ex usu Scripturae, hoc nomen spiritus sanctus. Et huius quidem convenientiae ratio sumi potest ex duobus. Primo quidem, ex ipsa communitate eius quod dicitur spiritus sanctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0015","text":"Ut enim Augustinus dicit, XV de Trin., *quia spiritus sanctus communis est ambobus, id vocatur ipse proprie quod ambo communiter, nam et pater est spiritus, et filius est spiritus; et pater est sanctus, et filius est sanctus*. Secundo vero, ex propria significatione. Nam nomen spiritus, in rebus corporeis, impulsionem quandam et motionem significare videtur, nam flatum et ventum spiritum nominamus. Est autem proprium amoris, quod moveat et impellat voluntatem amantis in amatum. Sanctitas vero illis rebus attribuitur, quae in Deum ordinantur. Quia igitur persona divina procedit per modum amoris quo Deus amatur, convenienter spiritus sanctus nominatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0016","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc quod dico spiritus sanctus, prout sumitur in virtute duarum dictionum, commune est toti Trinitati. Quia nomine spiritus significatur immaterialitas divinae substantiae, spiritus enim corporeus invisibilis est, et parum habet de materia; unde omnibus substantiis immaterialibus et invisibilibus hoc nomen attribuimus. Per hoc vero quod dicitur sanctus, significatur puritas divinae bonitatis. Si autem accipiatur hoc quod dico spiritus sanctus, in vi unius dictionis, sic ex usu Ecclesiae est accommodatum ad significandam unam trium personarum, scilicet quae procedit per modum amoris, ratione iam dicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0018","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet hoc quod dico spiritus sanctus, relative non dicatur, tamen pro relativo ponitur, inquantum est accommodatum ad significandam personam sola relatione ab aliis distinctam. Potest tamen intelligi etiam in nomine aliqua relatio, si spiritus intelligatur quasi spiratus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0020","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in nomine filii intelligitur sola relatio eius qui est a principio, ad principium, sed in nomine patris intelligitur relatio principii; et similiter in nomine spiritus, prout importat quandam vim motivam. Nulli autem creaturae competit esse principium respectu alicuius divinae personae, sed e converso. Et ideo potest dici pater noster, et spiritus noster, non tamen potest dici filius noster.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0023","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod spiritus sanctus non procedat a filio. Quia secundum Dionysium, *non est audendum dicere aliquid de substantiali divinitate, praeter ea quae divinitus nobis ex sacris eloquiis sunt expressa*. Sed in Scriptura sacra non exprimitur quod spiritus sanctus a filio procedat, sed solum quod procedat a patre; ut patet Ioann. XV, *spiritum veritatis, qui a patre procedit*. Ergo spiritus sanctus non procedit a filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0025","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0026","text":"Praeterea, in symbolo Constantinopolitanae synodi sic legitur, *credimus in spiritum sanctum, dominum et vivificantem, ex patre procedentem, cum patre et filio adorandum et glorificandum*. Nullo igitur modo debuit addi in symbolo nostro quod spiritus sanctus procedat a filio, sed videntur esse anathematis rei, qui hoc addiderunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0027","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0028","text":"Praeterea, Damascenus dicit, *spiritum sanctum ex patre dicimus, et spiritum patris nominamus, ex filio autem spiritum sanctum non dicimus, spiritum vero filii nominamus*. Ergo spiritus sanctus non procedit a filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0029","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0030","text":"Praeterea, nihil procedit ab eo in quo quiescit. Sed spiritus sanctus quiescit in filio. Dicitur enim in legenda beati Andreae, *pax vobis, et universis qui credunt in unum Deum patrem, et in unum filium eius, unicum dominum nostrum Iesum Christum, et in unum spiritum sanctum, procedentem ex patre, et in filio permanentem*. Ergo spiritus sanctus non procedit a filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0031","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0032","text":"Praeterea, filius procedit ut verbum. Sed spiritus noster in nobis non videtur procedere a verbo nostro. Ergo nec spiritus sanctus procedit a filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0033","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0034","text":"Praeterea, spiritus sanctus perfecte procedit a patre. Ergo superfluum est dicere quod procedit a filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0035","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0036","text":"Praeterea, in perpetuis non differt esse et posse, ut dicitur in III Physic.; et multo minus in divinis. Sed spiritus sanctus potest distingui a filio, etiam si ab eo non procedat. Dicit enim Anselmus, in libro de processione spiritus sancti, *habent utique a patre esse filius et spiritus sanctus, sed diverso modo, quia alter nascendo, et alter procedendo, ut alii sint per hoc ab invicem*. Et postea subdit, *nam si per aliud non essent plures filius et spiritus sanctus, per hoc solum essent diversi*. Ergo spiritus sanctus distinguitur a filio, ab eo non existens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0037","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0038","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Athanasius, *spiritus sanctus a patre et filio, non factus, nec creatus, nec genitus, sed procedens*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0039","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0040","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere spiritum sanctum a filio esse. Si enim non esset ab eo, nullo modo posset ab eo personaliter distingui. Quod ex supra dictis patet. Non enim est possibile dicere quod secundum aliquid absolutum divinae personae ab invicem distinguantur, quia sequeretur quod non esset trium una essentia; quidquid enim in divinis absolute dicitur, ad unitatem essentiae pertinet. Relinquitur ergo quod solum relationibus divinae personae ab invicem distinguantur. Relationes autem personas distinguere non possunt, nisi secundum quod sunt oppositae. Quod ex hoc patet, quia pater habet duas relationes, quarum una refertur ad filium, et alia ad spiritum sanctum; quae tamen, quia non sunt oppositae, non constituunt duas personas, sed ad unam personam patris tantum pertinent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0041","text":"Si ergo in filio et in spiritu sancto non esset invenire nisi duas relationes quibus uterque refertur ad patrem, illae relationes non essent ad invicem oppositae; sicut neque duae relationes quibus pater refertur ad illos. Unde, sicut persona patris est una, ita sequeretur quod persona filii et spiritus sancti esset una, habens duas relationes oppositas duabus relationibus patris. Hoc autem est haereticum, cum tollat fidem Trinitatis. Oportet ergo quod filius et spiritus sanctus ad invicem referantur oppositis relationibus. Non autem possunt esse in divinis aliae relationes oppositae nisi relationes originis, ut supra probatum est. Oppositae autem relationes originis accipiuntur secundum principium, et secundum quod est a principio. Relinquitur ergo quod necesse est dicere vel filium esse a spiritu sancto, quod nullus dicit, vel spiritum sanctum esse a filio, quod nos confitemur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0042","text":"Et huic quidem consonat ratio processionis utriusque. Dictum enim est supra quod filius procedit per modum intellectus, ut verbum; spiritus sanctus autem per modum voluntatis, ut amor. Necesse est autem quod amor a verbo procedat, non enim aliquid amamus, nisi secundum quod conceptione mentis apprehendimus. Unde et secundum hoc manifestum est quod spiritus sanctus procedit a filio. Ipse etiam ordo rerum hoc docet. Nusquam enim hoc invenimus, quod ab uno procedant plura absque ordine, nisi in illis solum quae materialiter differunt; sicut unus faber producit multos cultellos materialiter ab invicem distinctos, nullum ordinem habentes ad invicem. Sed in rebus in quibus non est sola materialis distinctio, semper invenitur in multitudine productorum aliquis ordo. Unde etiam in ordine creaturarum productarum, decor divinae sapientiae manifestatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0043","text":"Si ergo ab una persona patris procedunt duae personae, scilicet filius et spiritus sanctus, oportet esse aliquem ordinem eorum ad invicem. Nec potest aliquis ordo alius assignari, nisi ordo naturae, quo alius est ex alio. Non est igitur possibile dicere quod filius et spiritus sanctus sic procedant a patre, quod neuter eorum procedat ab alio, nisi quis poneret in eis materialem distinctionem, quod est impossibile. Unde etiam ipsi Graeci processionem spiritus sancti aliquem ordinem habere ad filium intelligunt. Concedunt enim spiritum sanctum esse spiritum filii, et esse a patre per filium. Et quidam eorum dicuntur concedere quod sit a filio, vel profluat ab eo, non tamen quod procedat. Quod videtur vel ex ignorantia, vel ex protervia esse. Quia si quis recte consideret, inveniet processionis verbum inter omnia quae ad originem qualemcumque pertinent, communissimum esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0044","text":"Utimur enim eo ad designandum qualemcumque originem; sicut quod linea procedit a puncto, radius a sole, rivus a fonte; et similiter in quibuscumque aliis. Unde ex quocumque alio ad originem pertinente, potest concludi quod spiritus sanctus procedit a filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0045","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0046","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod de Deo dicere non debemus quod in sacra Scriptura non invenitur vel per verba, vel per sensum. Licet autem per verba non inveniatur in sacra Scriptura quod spiritus sanctus procedit a filio, invenitur tamen quantum ad sensum; et praecipue ubi dicit filius, Ioan. XVI, de spiritu sancto loquens, *ille me clarificabit, quia de meo accipiet*. Regulariter etiam in sacra Scriptura tenendum est, quod id quod de patre dicitur, oportet de filio intelligi, etiam si dictio exclusiva addatur, nisi solum in illis in quibus pater et filius secundum oppositas relationes distinguuntur. Cum enim dominus, Matth. XI, dicit, *nemo novit filium nisi pater*, non excluditur quin filius seipsum cognoscat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0047","text":"Sic igitur cum dicitur quod spiritus sanctus a patre procedit, etiam si adderetur quod a solo patre procedit, non excluderetur inde filius, quia quantum ad hoc quod est esse principium spiritus sancti, non opponuntur pater et filius; sed solum quantum ad hoc, quod hic est pater et ille filius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0048","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0049","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in quolibet Concilio institutum fuit symbolum aliquod, propter errorem aliquem qui in Concilio damnabatur. Unde sequens Concilium non faciebat aliud symbolum quam primum, sed id quod implicite continebatur in primo symbolo, per aliqua addita explanabatur contra haereses insurgentes. Unde in determinatione Chalcedonensis synodi dicitur, quod illi qui fuerunt congregati in Concilio Constantinopolitano, doctrinam de spiritu sancto tradiderunt, non quod minus esset in praecedentibus (qui apud Nicaeam congregati sunt), inferentes; *sed intellectum eorum adversus haereticos declarantes*. Quia igitur in tempore antiquorum Conciliorum nondum exortus fuerat error dicentium spiritum sanctum non procedere a filio; non fuit necessarium quod hoc explicite poneretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0050","text":"Sed postea, insurgente errore quorundam, in quodam Concilio in Occidentalibus partibus congregato, expressum fuit auctoritate Romani pontificis; cuius auctoritate etiam antiqua Concilia congregabantur et confirmabantur. Continebatur tamen implicite in hoc ipso quod dicebatur spiritus sanctus a patre procedere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0051","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0052","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod spiritum sanctum non procedere a filio, primo fuit a Nestorianis introductum; ut patet in quodam symbolo Nestorianorum damnato in Ephesina synodo. Et hunc errorem secutus fuit Theodoretus Nestorianus, et plures post ipsum; inter quos fuit etiam Damascenus. Unde in hoc eius sententiae non est standum. Quamvis a quibusdam dicatur quod Damascenus, sicut non confitetur spiritum sanctum esse a filio, ita etiam non negat, ex vi illorum verborum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0053","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0054","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod per hoc quod spiritus sanctus dicitur quiescere vel manere in filio, non excluditur quin ab eo procedat, quia et filius in patre manere dicitur, cum tamen a patre procedat. Dicitur etiam spiritus sanctus in filio quiescere, vel sicut amor amantis quiescit in amato; vel quantum ad humanam naturam Christi, propter id quod scriptum est, Ioan. I, *super quem videris spiritum descendentem, et manentem super eum, hic est qui baptizat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0055","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0056","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod verbum in divinis non accipitur secundum similitudinem verbi vocalis, a quo non procedit spiritus, quia sic tantum metaphorice diceretur, sed secundum similitudinem verbi mentalis, a quo amor procedit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0057","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0058","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod per hoc quod spiritus sanctus perfecte procedit a patre, non solum non superfluum est dicere quod spiritus sanctus procedat a filio; sed omnino necessarium. Quia una virtus est patris et filii; et quidquid est a patre, necesse est esse a filio, nisi proprietati filiationis repugnet. Non enim filius est a seipso, licet sit a patre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0059","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0060","text":"Ad septimum dicendum quod spiritus sanctus distinguitur personaliter a filio in hoc, quod origo unius distinguitur ab origine alterius. Sed ipsa differentia originis est per hoc, quod filius est solum a patre, spiritus sanctus vero a patre et filio. Non enim aliter processiones distinguerentur, sicut supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0061","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0062","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0063","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod spiritus sanctus non procedat a patre per filium. Quod enim procedit ab aliquo per aliquem, non procedit ab eo immediate. Si igitur spiritus sanctus procedit a patre per filium, non procedit a patre immediate. Quod videtur inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0064","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0065","text":"Praeterea, si spiritus sanctus procedit a patre per filium, non procedit a filio nisi propter patrem. Sed propter quod unumquodque, et illud magis. Ergo magis procedit a patre quam a filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0066","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0067","text":"Praeterea, filius habet esse per generationem. Si igitur spiritus sanctus est a patre per filium, sequitur quod prius generetur filius, et postea procedat spiritus sanctus. Et sic processio spiritus sancti non est aeterna. Quod est haereticum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0068","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0069","text":"Praeterea, cum aliquis dicitur per aliquem operari, potest e converso dici, sicut enim dicimus quod rex operatur per ballivum, ita potest dici quod ballivus operatur per regem. Sed nullo modo dicimus quod filius spiret spiritum sanctum per patrem. Ergo nullo modo potest dici quod pater spiret spiritum sanctum per filium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0070","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0071","text":"Sed contra est quod Hilarius dicit, in libro de Trin., *conserva hanc, oro, fidei meae religionem, ut semper obtineam patrem, scilicet te; et filium tuum una tecum adorem; et spiritum sanctum tuum, qui est per unigenitum tuum, promerear*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0072","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0073","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in omnibus locutionibus in quibus dicitur aliquis per aliquem operari, haec praepositio per designat in causali aliquam causam seu principium illius actus. Sed cum actio sit media inter faciens et factum, quandoque illud causale cui adiungitur haec praepositio per, est causa actionis secundum quod exit ab agente. Et tunc est causa agenti quod agat; sive sit causa finalis, sive formalis, sive effectiva vel motiva, finalis quidem, ut si dicamus quod artifex operatur per cupiditatem lucri; formalis vero, ut si dicamus quod operatur per artem suam; motiva vero, si dicamus quod operatur per imperium alterius. Quandoque vero dictio causalis cui adiungitur haec praepositio per, est causa actionis secundum quod terminatur ad factum; ut cum dicimus, artifex operatur per martellum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0074","text":"Non enim significatur quod martellus sit causa artifici quod agat, sed quod sit causa artificiato ut ab artifice procedat; et quod hoc ipsum habeat ab artifice. Et hoc est quod quidam dicunt, quod haec praepositio per quandoque notat auctoritatem in recto, ut cum dicitur, rex operatur per ballivum, quandoque autem in obliquo, ut cum dicitur, ballivus operatur per regem. Quia igitur filius habet a patre quod ab eo procedat spiritus sanctus, potest dici quod pater per filium spirat spiritum sanctum; vel quod spiritus sanctus procedat a patre per filium, quod idem est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0075","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0076","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in qualibet actione est duo considerare, scilicet suppositum agens, et virtutem qua agit; sicut ignis calefacit calore. Si igitur in patre et filio consideretur virtus qua spirant spiritum sanctum, non cadit ibi aliquod medium, quia haec virtus est una et eadem. Si autem considerentur ipsae personae spirantes, sic, cum spiritus sanctus communiter procedat a patre et filio, invenitur spiritus sanctus immediate a patre procedere, inquantum est ab eo; et mediate, inquantum est a filio. Et sic dicitur procedere a patre per filium. Sicut etiam Abel processit immediate ab Adam, inquantum Adam fuit pater eius; et mediate, inquantum Eva fuit mater eius, quae processit ab Adam; licet hoc exemplum materialis processionis ineptum videatur ad significandam immaterialem processionem divinarum personarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0077","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0078","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, si filius acciperet a patre aliam virtutem numero ad spirandum spiritum sanctum, sequeretur quod esset sicut causa secunda et instrumentalis, et sic magis procederet a patre quam a filio. Sed una et eadem numero virtus spirativa est in patre et filio, et ideo aequaliter procedit ab utroque. Licet aliquando dicatur principaliter vel proprie procedere de patre, propter hoc quod filius habet hanc virtutem a patre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0079","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0080","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut generatio filii est coaeterna generanti, unde non prius fuit pater quam gigneret filium; ita processio spiritus sancti est coaeterna suo principio. Unde non fuit prius filius genitus, quam spiritus sanctus procederet, sed utrumque aeternum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0081","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0082","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, cum aliquis dicitur per aliquid operari, non semper recipitur conversio, non enim dicimus quod martellus operetur per fabrum. Dicimus autem quod ballivus operatur per regem, quia ballivi est agere, cum sit dominus sui actus. Martelli autem non est agere, sed solum agi, unde non designatur nisi ut instrumentum. Dicitur autem ballivus operari per regem, quamvis haec praepositio per denotet medium, quia, quanto suppositum est prius in agendo, tanto virtus eius est immediatior effectui, quia virtus causae primae coniungit causam secundam suo effectui, unde et prima principia dicuntur immediata in demonstrativis scientiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0083","text":"Sic igitur, inquantum ballivus est medius secundum ordinem suppositorum agentium, dicitur rex operari per ballivum, secundum ordinem vero virtutum, dicitur ballivus operari per regem, quia virtus regis facit quod actio ballivi consequatur effectum. Ordo autem non attenditur inter patrem et filium quantum ad virtutem; sed solum quantum ad supposita. Et ideo dicitur quod pater spirat per filium, et non e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0084","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0085","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0086","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pater et filius non sint unum principium spiritus sancti. Quia spiritus sanctus non videtur a patre et filio procedere inquantum sunt unum, neque in natura, quia spiritus sanctus sic etiam procederet a seipso, qui est unum cum eis in natura; neque etiam inquantum sunt unum in aliqua proprietate, quia una proprietas non potest esse duorum suppositorum, ut videtur. Ergo spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio ut sunt plures. Non ergo pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0087","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0088","text":"Praeterea, cum dicitur, pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, non potest ibi designari unitas personalis, quia sic pater et filius essent una persona. Neque etiam unitas proprietatis, quia si propter unam proprietatem pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, pari ratione, propter duas proprietates pater videtur esse duo principia filii et spiritus sancti; quod est inconveniens. Non ergo pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0089","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0090","text":"Praeterea, filius non magis convenit cum patre quam spiritus sanctus. Sed spiritus sanctus et pater non sunt unum principium respectu alicuius divinae personae. Ergo neque pater et filius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0091","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0092","text":"Praeterea, si pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti aut unum quod est pater; aut unum quod non est pater. Sed neutrum est dare, quia si unum quod est pater, sequitur quod filius sit pater; si unum quod non est pater, sequitur quod pater non est pater. Non ergo dicendum est quod pater et filius sint unum principium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0093","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0094","text":"Praeterea, si pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti videtur e converso dicendum quod unum principium spiritus sancti sit pater et filius. Sed haec videtur esse falsa, quia hoc quod dico principium, oportet quod supponat vel pro persona patris, vel pro persona filii; et utroque modo est falsa. Ergo etiam haec est falsa, pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0095","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0096","text":"Praeterea, unum in substantia facit idem. Si igitur pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, sequitur quod sint idem principium. Sed hoc a multis negatur. Ergo non est concedendum quod pater et filius sint unum principium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0097","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0098","text":"Praeterea, pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, quia sunt unum principium creaturae, dicuntur esse unus creator. Sed pater et filius non sunt unus spirator, sed duo spiratores, ut a multis dicitur. Quod etiam consonat dictis Hilarii, qui dicit, in II de Trin., quod spiritus sanctus a patre et filio auctoribus confitendus est. Ergo pater et filius non sunt unum principium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0099","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0100","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in V de Trin., quod pater et filius non sunt duo principia, sed unum principium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0101","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0102","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod pater et filius in omnibus unum sunt, in quibus non distinguit inter eos relationis oppositio. Unde, cum in hoc quod est esse principium spiritus sancti, non opponantur relative, sequitur quod pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti. Quidam tamen dicunt hanc esse impropriam, pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti. Quia cum hoc nomen principium, singulariter acceptum, non significet personam, sed proprietatem, dicunt quod sumitur adiective, et quia adiectivum non determinatur per adiectivum, non potest convenienter dici quod pater et filius sint unum principium spiritus sancti, nisi unum intelligatur quasi adverbialiter positum, ut sit sensus, sunt unum principium, idest uno modo. Sed simili ratione posset dici pater duo principia filii et spiritus sancti, idest duobus modis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0103","text":"Dicendum est ergo quod, licet hoc nomen principium significet proprietatem, tamen significat eam per modum substantivi, sicut hoc nomen pater vel filius etiam in rebus creatis. Unde numerum accipit a forma significata, sicut et alia substantiva. Sicut igitur pater et filius sunt unus Deus, propter unitatem formae significatae per hoc nomen Deus; ita sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, propter unitatem proprietatis significatae in hoc nomine principium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0104","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0105","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, si attendatur virtus spirativa, spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio inquantum sunt unum in virtute spirativa, quae quodammodo significat naturam cum proprietate, ut infra dicetur. Neque est inconveniens unam proprietatem esse in duobus suppositis, quorum est una natura. Si vero considerentur supposita spirationis, sic spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio ut sunt plures, procedit enim ab eis ut amor unitivus duorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0106","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0107","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur, pater et filius sunt unum principium spiritus sancti, designatur una proprietas, quae est forma significata per nomen. Non tamen sequitur quod propter plures proprietates possit dici pater plura principia, quia implicaretur pluralitas suppositorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0108","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0109","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod secundum relativas proprietates non attenditur in divinis similitudo vel dissimilitudo, sed secundum essentiam. Unde, sicut pater non est similior sibi quam filio, ita nec filius similior patri quam spiritus sanctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0110","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0111","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod haec duo, scilicet, pater et filius sunt unum principium quod est pater, aut, unum principium quod non est pater, non sunt contradictorie opposita. Unde non est necesse alterum eorum dare. Cum enim dicimus, pater et filius sunt unum principium, hoc quod dico principium, non habet determinatam suppositionem, imo confusam pro duabus personis simul. Unde in processu est fallacia figurae dictionis, a confusa suppositione ad determinatam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0112","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0113","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod haec etiam est vera, unum principium spiritus sancti est pater et filius. Quia hoc quod dico principium non supponit pro una persona tantum, sed indistincte pro duabus, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0114","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0115","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod convenienter potest dici quod pater et filius sunt idem principium, secundum quod ly principium supponit confuse et indistincte pro duabus personis simul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0116","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0117","text":"Ad septimum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod pater et filius, licet sint unum principium spiritus sancti, sunt tamen duo spiratores, propter distinctionem suppositorum, sicut etiam duo spirantes, quia actus referuntur ad supposita. Nec est eadem ratio de hoc nomine creator. Quia spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio ut sunt duae personae distinctae, ut dictum est, non autem creatura procedit a tribus personis ut sunt personae distinctae, sed ut sunt unum in essentia. Sed videtur melius dicendum quod, quia spirans adiectivum est, spirator vero substantivum, possumus dicere quod pater et filius sunt duo spirantes, propter pluralitatem suppositorum; non autem duo spiratores, propter unam spirationem. Nam adiectiva nomina habent numerum secundum supposita, substantiva vero a seipsis, secundum formam significatam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0118","text":"Quod vero Hilarius dicit, quod spiritus sanctus est a patre et filio auctoribus, exponendum est quod ponitur substantivum pro adiectivo.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"18a775225f2d250f99b6abd3b82c17ef85953a27bcc0940733499292f409a495","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141802262533Z_cefcf556","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 36","source_sha256":"18a775225f2d250f99b6abd3b82c17ef85953a27bcc0940733499292f409a495","sha256":"146aab198b9d1be2fc33dce8c443156fe39a3f001e2e4839872335fce8c4982f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:18:02.262555+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0002","text":"[I q. 36 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0003","text":"After this, we must consider those matters that pertain to the person of the Holy Spirit. He is called not only the Holy Spirit, but also the Love and Gift of God. Therefore, concerning the name of the Holy Spirit, four questions are asked. First, whether this name, Holy Spirit, is proper to any divine person. Second, whether that divine person who is called the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son. Third, whether He proceeds from the Father through the Son. Fourth, whether the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0005","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that this name, Holy Spirit, is not the proper name of any divine person. For no name common to the three persons is proper to any one person. But this name, Holy Spirit, is common to the three persons. For Hilary shows, in Book VIII On the Trinity, that by the Spirit of God the Father is sometimes signified, as when it is said, “the Spirit of the Lord is upon me”; sometimes the Son is signified, as when the Son says, “in the Spirit of God I cast out demons,” showing that He casts out demons by the power of His own nature; sometimes the Holy Spirit, as there, “I will pour out of my Spirit upon all flesh.” Therefore this name, Holy Spirit, is not proper to any divine person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0007","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0008","text":"Further, the names of the divine persons are said relatively, as Boethius says in the book On the Trinity. But this name, Holy Spirit, is not said relatively. Therefore this name is not proper to a divine person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0009","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0010","text":"Further, because Son is the name of a divine person, He cannot be called the son of this or that person. But one speaks of the spirit of this or that man. For as is found in Num. XI, *the Lord said to Moses, “I will take of your spirit and give it to them”*; and in IV Kings II, *the spirit of Elijah rested upon Elisha*. Therefore the Holy Spirit does not seem to be the proper name of any divine person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0011","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0012","text":"On the contrary, it is said in the last chapter of I John, *there are three who bear testimony in heaven: the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit*. But, as Augustine says, in Book VII On the Trinity, when it is asked, “three what?” we say, “three persons.” Therefore the Holy Spirit is the name of a divine person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0013","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0014","text":"I answer that, since there are two processions in the divine, the one of them which is by the mode of love has no proper name, as was said above. Hence the relations that are understood according to such a procession are also unnamed, as was likewise said above. For this reason, the name of the person proceeding in this manner likewise has no proper name. But just as certain names have been appropriated, from the usage of speakers, for signifying the aforesaid relations, when we name them by the name of procession and spiration, which, according to the propriety of their signification, seem rather to signify notional acts than relations; so too, for signifying the divine person who proceeds by the mode of love, this name, Holy Spirit, has been appropriated from the usage of Scripture. And the reason for this suitability can be taken from two things. First, from the very commonness of that which is called Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0015","text":"For as Augustine says, in Book XV On the Trinity, *because the Holy Spirit is common to both, He Himself is properly called that which both are commonly: for the Father too is spirit, and the Son is spirit; and the Father is holy, and the Son is holy*. Second, from its proper signification. For the name spirit, in corporeal things, seems to signify a certain impulse and motion; for we call breath and wind spirit. But it is proper to love that it move and impel the will of the lover toward the beloved. Holiness, moreover, is attributed to those things which are ordered to God. Therefore, since the divine person proceeds by the mode of love by which God is loved, He is fittingly called the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0016","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0017","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that “Holy Spirit,” insofar as it is taken in the force of two words, is common to the whole Trinity. For by the name spirit the immateriality of the divine substance is signified; for a bodily spirit is invisible and has little matter. Hence we attribute this name to all immaterial and invisible substances. But by the term holy is signified the purity of divine goodness. If, however, “Holy Spirit” is taken in the force of one word, then by the usage of the Church it has been appropriated to signify one of the three persons, namely, the one who proceeds by the mode of love, for the reason already given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0018","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0019","text":"To the second it must be said that, although “Holy Spirit” is not said relatively, nevertheless it is put for a relative, insofar as it has been appropriated to signify a person distinguished from the others by relation alone. Yet some relation can also be understood in the name, if spirit is understood as though spirated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0020","text":"[I q. 36 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0021","text":"To the third it must be said that in the name Son is understood only the relation of one who is from a principle to the principle; but in the name Father is understood the relation of a principle; and similarly in the name spirit, insofar as it conveys a certain moving power. But it belongs to no creature to be a principle in relation to any divine person, but rather the converse. And therefore He can be called our Father and our Spirit, but He cannot be called our Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0023","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the Holy Spirit does not proceed from the Son. For according to Dionysius, *one must not dare to say anything concerning the substantial divinity beyond those things which have been divinely expressed to us in the sacred utterances*. But Sacred Scripture does not express that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Son, but only that He proceeds from the Father; as is clear in John XV: *the Spirit of truth, who proceeds from the Father*. Therefore the Holy Spirit does not proceed from the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0025","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0026","text":"Moreover, in the creed of the Council of Constantinople it is thus read: *we believe in the Holy Spirit, the Lord and giver of life, who proceeds from the Father, who is to be adored and glorified together with the Father and the Son*. Therefore, in no way ought it to have been added to our creed that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Son; rather, those who added this appear to be liable to anathema.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0027","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0028","text":"Moreover, Damascene says: *we say that the Holy Spirit is from the Father, and we name him the Spirit of the Father; but we do not say that the Holy Spirit is from the Son, though we name him the Spirit of the Son*. Therefore, the Holy Spirit does not proceed from the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0029","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0030","text":"Moreover, nothing proceeds from that in which it rests. But the Holy Spirit rests in the Son. For it is said in the legend of blessed Andrew: *Peace be with you, and with all who believe in one God the Father, and in one Son of his, our only Lord Jesus Christ, and in one Holy Spirit, proceeding from the Father and remaining in the Son*. Therefore, the Holy Spirit does not proceed from the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0031","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0032","text":"Moreover, the Son proceeds as the Word. But our spirit within us does not appear to proceed from our word. Therefore neither does the Holy Spirit proceed from the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0033","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0034","text":"Moreover, the Holy Spirit proceeds perfectly from the Father. Therefore it is superfluous to say that he proceeds from the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0035","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0036","text":"Moreover, in perpetual things, to be and to be able do not differ, as is said in *Physics* III; and much less so in divine things. But the Holy Spirit can be distinguished from the Son even if he does not proceed from him. For Anselm says, in the book *On the Procession of the Holy Spirit*: *the Son and the Holy Spirit indeed have being from the Father, but in a different manner, because one does so by being born, and the other by proceeding, so that by this they may be distinct from one another*. And afterward he adds: *for if the Son and the Holy Spirit were not more than one by something else, they would be different by this alone*. Therefore, the Holy Spirit is distinguished from the Son without being from him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0037","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0038","text":"But to the contrary is what Athanasius says: *the Holy Spirit is from the Father and the Son, not made, nor created, nor begotten, but proceeding*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0039","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0040","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Holy Spirit is from the Son. For if he were not from him, he could in no way be personally distinguished from him. This is clear from what has been said above. For it is not possible to say that the divine persons are distinguished from one another according to something absolute, because it would follow that there was not one essence of the three; for whatever is said absolutely in divine things pertains to the unity of essence. It remains, therefore, that the divine persons are distinguished from one another only by relations. But relations cannot distinguish persons except insofar as they are opposed. This is evident from the fact that the Father has two relations, one of which is referred to the Son and the other to the Holy Spirit; yet, because these are not opposed, they do not constitute two persons, but pertain only to the one person of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0041","text":"Therefore, if in the Son and the Holy Spirit there were found only the two relations by which each is referred to the Father, those relations would not be opposed to one another, just as neither are the two relations by which the Father is referred to them. Hence, just as the person of the Father is one, so it would follow that the person of the Son and the Holy Spirit was one, having two relations opposed to the two relations of the Father. But this is heretical, since it removes the faith of the Trinity. Therefore the Son and the Holy Spirit must be referred to one another by opposed relations. But there can be no other opposed relations in divine things except relations of origin, as was proved above. Now opposed relations of origin are understood according to a principle and according to what is from a principle. It remains, therefore, that it must be said either that the Son is from the Holy Spirit—which no one says—or that the Holy Spirit is from the Son, which we confess.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0042","text":"And the account of the procession of each accords with this. For it was said above that the Son proceeds by way of intellect, as Word; whereas the Holy Spirit proceeds by way of will, as love. Now love must proceed from the Word, for we do not love anything except insofar as we apprehend it by a conception of the mind. Hence in this way also it is manifest that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Son. The very order of things likewise teaches this. For nowhere do we find that several things proceed from one without order, except only among things that differ materially; as one craftsman produces many knives, materially distinct from one another, having no order among themselves. But in things in which there is not merely a material distinction, some order is always found in the multitude of things produced. Hence, even in the order of created things, the beauty of divine wisdom is manifested.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0043","text":"Therefore, if two persons, namely the Son and the Holy Spirit, proceed from the one person of the Father, there must be some order of them to one another. Nor can any order other than an order of nature be assigned, by which one is from another. It is therefore not possible to say that the Son and the Holy Spirit proceed from the Father in such a way that neither of them proceeds from the other, unless one were to posit a material distinction in them, which is impossible. Hence the Greeks themselves also understand the procession of the Holy Spirit to have some order with respect to the Son. For they concede that the Holy Spirit is the Spirit of the Son and is from the Father through the Son. And some of them are said to concede that he is from the Son, or flows forth from him, but not that he proceeds. This seems to arise either from ignorance or from obstinacy. For if anyone considers rightly, he will find that the word “procession,” among all things pertaining to any kind of origin, is the most common.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0044","text":"For we use it to signify any kind of origin: as a line proceeds from a point, a ray from the sun, a stream from a spring; and likewise in any other cases. Hence, from whatever else pertains to origin, it can be concluded that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0045","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0046","text":"Therefore, to the first objection it must be said that we ought not say of God what is not found in Sacred Scripture either in words or in sense. Although it is not found in Sacred Scripture in words that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Son, it is nevertheless found there in sense; and especially where the Son says, speaking of the Holy Spirit in John 16: *He will glorify me, because he will receive of what is mine*. It must also be held as a rule in Sacred Scripture that what is said of the Father must be understood of the Son as well, even if an exclusive expression is added, except only in those matters in which Father and Son are distinguished by opposed relations. For when the Lord says, in Matthew 11, *no one knows the Son except the Father*, this does not exclude the Son's knowing himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0047","text":"Thus, therefore, when it is said that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father, even if it were added that he proceeds from the Father alone, the Son would not thereby be excluded; because with respect to being the principle of the Holy Spirit, the Father and the Son are not opposed, but only with respect to the fact that this one is Father and that one Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0048","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0049","text":"To the second, it must be said that in every Council some symbol was instituted, on account of some error that was condemned in the Council. Hence the following Council did not make a symbol other than the first, but what was contained implicitly in the first symbol was explained through certain additions against the heresies arising. Hence in the determination of the Synod of Chalcedon it is said that those who were assembled in the Council of Constantinople handed down the doctrine concerning the Holy Spirit, not introducing anything deficient in those preceding (who had assembled at Nicaea), *but declaring their meaning against heretics*. Therefore, because in the time of the ancient Councils the error of those saying that the Holy Spirit does not proceed from the Son had not yet arisen, it was not necessary that this be stated explicitly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0050","text":"But afterward, when the error of certain persons arose, it was expressed in a certain Council assembled in the Western regions by the authority of the Roman Pontiff, by whose authority the ancient Councils also were assembled and confirmed. Yet it was contained implicitly in this very fact, that it was said that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0051","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0052","text":"To the third, it must be said that the claim that the Holy Spirit does not proceed from the Son was first introduced by the Nestorians, as is apparent in a certain symbol of the Nestorians condemned in the Synod of Ephesus. And this error was followed by the Nestorian Theodoret, and many after him, among whom was also Damascene. Hence his opinion is not to be followed in this matter. Although some say that Damascene, just as he does not confess that the Holy Spirit is from the Son, so neither does he deny it, by force of those words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0053","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0054","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that by the fact that the Holy Spirit is said to rest or remain in the Son, it is not excluded that He proceeds from Him, because the Son too is said to remain in the Father, although He proceeds from the Father. The Holy Spirit is also said to rest in the Son, either as the love of the lover rests in the beloved, or with respect to Christ's human nature, on account of what is written, John 1: *upon whom you shall see the Spirit descending and remaining upon him, this is he who baptizes*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0055","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0056","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that word in divine things is not understood according to the likeness of a spoken word, from which no spirit proceeds—for in that case it would be said only metaphorically—but according to the likeness of a mental word, from which love proceeds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0057","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0058","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that, from the fact that the Holy Spirit proceeds perfectly from the Father, not only is it not superfluous to say that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Son, but it is altogether necessary. For the power of the Father and Son is one; and whatever is from the Father must be from the Son, unless it conflicts with the property of filiation. For the Son is not from Himself, although He is from the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0059","text":"[I q. 36 a. 2 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0060","text":"To the seventh, it must be said that the Holy Spirit is personally distinguished from the Son in this, that the origin of the one is distinguished from the origin of the other. But the very difference of origin is through this: that the Son is from the Father alone, whereas the Holy Spirit is from the Father and the Son. For otherwise the processions would not be distinguished, as was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0061","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0062","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0063","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the Holy Spirit does not proceed from the Father through the Son. For what proceeds from someone through another does not proceed from him immediately. Therefore, if the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father through the Son, He does not proceed immediately from the Father. This seems unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0064","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0065","text":"Moreover, if the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father through the Son, He proceeds from the Son only on account of the Father. But that on account of which each thing is, is more so. Therefore He proceeds more from the Father than from the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0066","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0067","text":"Moreover, the Son has being through generation. Therefore, if the Holy Spirit is from the Father through the Son, it follows that the Son is generated first, and afterward the Holy Spirit proceeds. And thus the procession of the Holy Spirit is not eternal. This is heretical.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0068","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0069","text":"Moreover, when someone is said to act through another, it can be said conversely, for just as we say that the king acts through the bailiff, so it can be said that the bailiff acts through the king. But in no way do we say that the Son breathes forth the Holy Spirit through the Father. Therefore in no way can it be said that the Father breathes forth the Holy Spirit through the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0070","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0071","text":"But on the contrary, Hilary says, in the book *On the Trinity*: *Preserve, I pray, this devotion of my faith, that I may always hold fast to the Father, namely, to you; and may adore your Son together with you; and may merit your Holy Spirit, who is through your Only-begotten*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0072","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0073","text":"I answer that it must be said that in all expressions in which someone is said to act through someone, this preposition through designates, in a causal expression, some cause or principle of that act. But since action is intermediate between the doer and the thing done, sometimes that causal expression to which this preposition through is joined is the cause of the action insofar as it proceeds from the agent. And then it is a cause to the agent that he acts; whether it is a final, formal, efficient, or motive cause: final indeed, as if we say that an artisan works through desire for gain; formal, however, as if we say that he works through his art; motive, if we say that he works through another's command. But sometimes the causal expression to which this preposition through is joined is the cause of the action insofar as it is terminated in the thing made; as when we say that an artisan works through a hammer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0074","text":"For it is not signified that the hammer is a cause to the artisan that he acts, but that it is a cause to the thing made that it proceeds from the artisan; and that it has this very thing from the artisan. And this is what some say: that this preposition through sometimes denotes authority directly, as when it is said, “the king acts through the bailiff,” but sometimes indirectly, as when it is said, “the bailiff acts through the king.” Therefore, since the Son has from the Father that the Holy Spirit proceeds from him, it can be said that the Father breathes forth the Holy Spirit through the Son; or that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father through the Son, which is the same thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0075","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0076","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in every action there are two things to consider, namely, the acting suppositum and the power by which it acts; just as fire heats by heat. If, therefore, in the Father and the Son the power by which they breathe forth the Holy Spirit is considered, no intermediate falls there, because this power is one and the same. But if the persons themselves breathing forth are considered, then, since the Holy Spirit proceeds commonly from the Father and the Son, the Holy Spirit is found to proceed immediately from the Father, insofar as he is from him; and mediately, insofar as he is from the Son. And thus he is said to proceed from the Father through the Son. So too Abel proceeded immediately from Adam, insofar as Adam was his father; and mediately, insofar as Eve was his mother, who proceeded from Adam; although this example of material procession seems unsuitable for signifying the immaterial procession of the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0077","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0078","text":"To the second it must be said that, if the Son received from the Father another power, numerically distinct, for breathing forth the Holy Spirit, it would follow that he would be as a secondary and instrumental cause, and thus the Holy Spirit would proceed more from the Father than from the Son. But one and the same numerically spirative power is in the Father and the Son, and therefore he proceeds equally from both. Although he is sometimes said to proceed principally or properly from the Father, because the Son has this power from the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0079","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0080","text":"To the third it must be said that, just as the generation of the Son is coeternal with the begetter, whence the Father was not prior to begetting the Son; so the procession of the Holy Spirit is coeternal with its principle. Hence the Son was not begotten prior to the Holy Spirit's proceeding, but both are eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0081","text":"[I q. 36 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0082","text":"To the fourth it must be said that, when someone is said to act through something, conversion is not always admissible; for we do not say that a hammer acts through a smith. But we do say that a bailiff acts through the king, because it belongs to the bailiff to act, since he is master of his own act. But it does not belong to the hammer to act, but only to be acted upon; whence it is designated only as an instrument. Now the bailiff is said to act through the king, although this preposition through denotes an intermediate, because the prior a suppositum is in acting, the more immediate is its power to the effect; for the power of the first cause conjoins the second cause to its effect, whence first principles too are called immediate in demonstrative sciences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0083","text":"Thus, therefore, insofar as the bailiff is intermediate according to the order of acting supposita, the king is said to act through the bailiff; but according to the order of powers, the bailiff is said to act through the king, because the king's power brings it about that the bailiff's action attains its effect. But no order is considered between the Father and the Son with respect to power, but only with respect to supposita. And therefore it is said that the Father breathes forth through the Son, and not conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0084","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0085","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0086","text":"We proceed to the fourth thus. It seems that the Father and the Son are not one principle of the Holy Spirit. For the Holy Spirit does not seem to proceed from the Father and the Son insofar as they are one: not in nature, because in that way the Holy Spirit would also proceed from himself, who is one with them in nature; nor even insofar as they are one in some property, because one property cannot belong to two supposita, as seems evident. Therefore the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son as they are several. Therefore the Father and the Son are not one principle of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0087","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0088","text":"Moreover, when it is said, “the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit,” personal unity cannot be designated there, because then the Father and the Son would be one person. Nor can unity of property be designated, because if, by reason of one property, the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit, by equal reasoning, by reason of two properties the Father seems to be two principles of the Son and the Holy Spirit; which is unfitting. Therefore the Father and the Son are not one principle of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0089","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0090","text":"Moreover, the Son does not agree with the Father more than does the Holy Spirit. But the Holy Spirit and the Father are not one principle with respect to any divine person. Therefore neither are the Father and the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0091","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0092","text":"Moreover, if the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit, they are either one which is the Father, or one which is not the Father. But neither is possible: for if they are one which is the Father, it follows that the Son is the Father; if they are one which is not the Father, it follows that the Father is not the Father. Therefore it must not be said that the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0093","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0094","text":"Moreover, if the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit, it seems that conversely one should say that the one principle of the Holy Spirit is the Father and the Son. But this seems to be false, because what I call “principle” must stand either for the person of the Father or for the person of the Son; and in either way it is false. Therefore this too is false: “the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0095","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0096","text":"Moreover, one in substance makes the same. Therefore, if the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit, it follows that they are the same principle. But this is denied by many. Therefore it must not be conceded that the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0097","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0098","text":"Moreover, the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, because they are one principle of the creature, are said to be one creator. But the Father and the Son are not one spirator, but two spirators, as is said by many. This also accords with the words of Hilary, who says, in Book II On the Trinity, that the Holy Spirit must be confessed as from the Father and the Son as authors. Therefore the Father and the Son are not one principle of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0099","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0100","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in Book V On the Trinity, that the Father and the Son are not two principles, but one principle of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0101","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0102","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Father and the Son are one in all things in which opposition of relation does not distinguish between them. Hence, since in being the principle of the Holy Spirit they are not relatively opposed, it follows that the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit. Some, however, say that this statement, “the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit,” is improper. For since this name “principle,” taken in the singular, does not signify a person but a property, they say that it is taken adjectivally; and because an adjective is not determined by an adjective, it cannot fittingly be said that the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit, unless “one” is understood as if placed adverbially, so that the sense is: they are one principle, that is, in one manner. But by similar reasoning it could be said that the Father is two principles of the Son and the Holy Spirit, that is, in two manners.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0103","text":"Therefore it must be said that, although this name “principle” signifies a property, nevertheless it signifies it in the manner of a substantive, just as the name “father” or “son” does also in created things. Hence it takes its number from the form signified, as do other substantives. Therefore, just as the Father and the Son are one God, on account of the unity of the form signified by this name “God,” so they are one principle of the Holy Spirit, on account of the unity of the property signified in this name “principle.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0104","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0105","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, if the spirative power is considered, the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son insofar as they are one in spirative power, which in some way signifies nature together with property, as will be said below. Nor is it unfitting for one property to be in two supposita, whose nature is one. But if the supposita of the spiration are considered, then the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son as they are several; for he proceeds from them as the unitive love of two.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0106","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0107","text":"To the second it must be said that, when it is said, “the Father and the Son are one principle of the Holy Spirit,” one property is designated, which is the form signified by the name. Yet it does not follow that, on account of several properties, the Father can be called several principles, because a plurality of supposita would be implied.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0108","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0109","text":"To the third it must be said that likeness or unlikeness in divine things is not considered according to relative properties, but according to essence. Hence, just as the Father is not more like himself than like the Son, so neither is the Son more like the Father than the Holy Spirit is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0110","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0111","text":"To the fourth it must be said that these two propositions, namely, “the Father and the Son are one principle which is the Father,” or “one principle which is not the Father,” are not contradictorily opposed. Hence it is not necessary to grant either of them. For when we say, “the Father and the Son are one principle,” this word “principle” does not have determinate supposition, but rather confused supposition for the two persons together. Hence in the argument there is the fallacy of figure of speech, from confused supposition to determinate supposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0112","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0113","text":"To the fifth it must be said that this too is true: “the one principle of the Holy Spirit is the Father and the Son.” For this word “principle” does not stand for one person only, but indistinctly for the two, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0114","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0115","text":"To the sixth it must be said that it can fittingly be said that the Father and the Son are the same principle, insofar as “principle” stands confusedly and indistinctly for the two persons together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0116","text":"[I q. 36 a. 4 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0117","text":"To the seventh it must be said that some say that the Father and the Son, although they are one principle of the Holy Spirit, are nevertheless two spirators, on account of the distinction of supposita, and likewise two spirating persons, because acts are referred to supposita. Nor is the reasoning the same concerning this name “creator.” For the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son as they are two distinct persons, as has been said; but a creature does not proceed from the three persons as they are distinct persons, but as they are one in essence. But it seems better to say that, because “spirating” is an adjective whereas “spirator” is a substantive, we can say that the Father and the Son are two spirating persons, on account of the plurality of supposita; but not two spirators, on account of the one spiration. For adjectival names have number according to the supposita, whereas substantives have it from themselves, according to the form signified.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0118","text":"But what Hilary says, namely that the Holy Spirit is from the Father and the Son as authors, must be explained as a substantive being used in place of an adjective.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_28:P0118"]}],"source_digest":"df7c7d19644471d06e701ddc7d4b96b8a07e99ac52dc3f9d5b6641c80be9fe79","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 36"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 36","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29","title":"First Part · Question 37","order":38,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_29","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 37 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29946)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de nomine amoris. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum sit proprium nomen spiritus sancti. Secundo, utrum pater et filius diligant se spiritu sancto.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29947)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod amor non sit proprium nomen spiritus sancti. Dicit enim Augustinus, XV de Trin., *nescio cur, sicut sapientia dicitur et pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, et simul omnes non tres sed una sapientia, non ita et caritas dicatur pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, et simul omnes una caritas*. Sed nullum nomen quod de singulis personis praedicatur et de omnibus in communi singulariter, est nomen proprium alicuius personae. Ergo hoc nomen amor non est proprium spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29948)\n\nPraeterea, spiritus sanctus est persona subsistens. Sed amor non significatur ut persona subsistens, sed ut actio quaedam ab amante transiens in amatum. Ergo amor non est proprium nomen spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29949)\n\nPraeterea, amor est nexus amantium, quia secundum Dionysium, IV cap. de Div. Nom., est quaedam vis unitiva. Sed nexus est medium inter ea quae connectit, non autem aliquid ab eis procedens. Cum igitur spiritus sanctus procedat a patre et filio, sicut ostensum est, videtur quod non sit amor aut nexus patris et filii.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29950)\n\nPraeterea, cuiuslibet amantis est aliquis amor. Sed spiritus sanctus est amans. Ergo eius est aliquis amor. Si igitur spiritus sanctus est amor, erit amor amoris, et spiritus a spiritu. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29951)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, in homilia Pentecostes, *ipse spiritus sanctus est amor*.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29952)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nomen amoris in divinis sumi potest et essentialiter et personaliter. Et secundum quod personaliter sumitur, est proprium nomen spiritus sancti; sicut verbum est proprium nomen filii. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod, cum in divinis, ut supra ostensum est, sint duae processiones, una per modum intellectus, quae est processio verbi; alia per modum voluntatis, quae est processio amoris, quia prima est nobis magis nota, ad singula significanda quae in ea considerari possunt, sunt magis propria nomina adinventa; non autem in processione voluntatis. Unde et quibusdam circumlocutionibus utimur ad significandam personam procedentem, et relationes etiam quae accipiuntur secundum hanc processionem, et processionis et spirationis nominibus nominantur, ut supra dictum est, quae tamen sunt magis nomina originis quam relationis, secundum proprietatem vocabuli. Et tamen similiter utramque processionem considerari oportet. Sicut enim ex hoc quod aliquis rem aliquam intelligit, provenit quaedam intellectualis conceptio rei intellectae in intelligente, quae dicitur verbum; ita ex hoc quod aliquis rem aliquam amat, provenit quaedam impressio, ut ita loquar, rei amatae in affectu amantis, secundum quam amatum dicitur esse in amante, sicut et intellectum in intelligente. Ita quod, cum aliquis seipsum intelligit et amat, est in seipso non solum per identitatem rei, sed etiam ut intellectum in intelligente, et amatum in amante. Sed ex parte intellectus, sunt vocabula adinventa ad significandum respectum intelligentis ad rem intellectam, ut patet in hoc quod dico intelligere, et sunt etiam alia vocabula adinventa ad significandum processum intellectualis conceptionis, scilicet ipsum dicere, et verbum. Unde in divinis intelligere solum essentialiter dicitur, quia non importat habitudinem ad verbum procedens, sed verbum personaliter dicitur, quia significat id quod procedit, ipsum vero dicere dicitur notionaliter, quia importat habitudinem principii verbi ad verbum ipsum. Ex parte autem voluntatis, praeter diligere et amare, quae important habitudinem amantis ad rem amatam, non sunt aliqua vocabula imposita, quae importent habitudinem ipsius impressionis vel affectionis rei amatae, quae provenit in amante ex hoc quod amat, ad suum principium, aut e converso. Et ideo, propter vocabulorum inopiam, huiusmodi habitudines significamus vocabulis amoris et dilectionis; sicut si verbum nominaremus intelligentiam conceptam, vel sapientiam genitam. Sic igitur, inquantum in amore vel dilectione non importatur nisi habitudo amantis ad rem amatam, amor et diligere essentialiter dicuntur, sicut intelligentia et intelligere. Inquantum vero his vocabulis utimur ad exprimendam habitudinem eius rei quae procedit per modum amoris, ad suum principium, et e converso; ita quod per amorem intelligatur amor procedens, et per diligere intelligatur spirare amorem procedentem, sic amor est nomen personae, et diligere vel amare est verbum notionale, sicut dicere vel generare.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29953)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de caritate, secundum quod essentialiter sumitur in divinis, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29954)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intelligere et velle et amare, licet significentur per modum actionum transeuntium in obiecta, sunt tamen actiones manentes in agentibus, ut supra dictum est; ita tamen quod in ipso agente important habitudinem quandam ad obiectum. Unde amor, etiam in nobis, est aliquid manens in amante, et verbum cordis manens in dicente; tamen cum habitudine ad rem verbo expressam, vel amatam. Sed in Deo, in quo nullum est accidens, plus habet, quia tam verbum quam amor est subsistens. Cum ergo dicitur quod spiritus sanctus est amor patris in filium, vel in quidquam aliud, non significatur aliquid transiens in alium; sed solum habitudo amoris ad rem amatam; sicut et in verbo importatur habitudo verbi ad rem verbo expressam.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29955)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod spiritus sanctus dicitur esse nexus patris et filii, inquantum est amor, quia, cum pater amet unica dilectione se et filium, et e converso, importatur in spiritu sancto, prout est amor, habitudo patris ad filium, et e converso, ut amantis ad amatum. Sed ex hoc ipso quod pater et filius se mutuo amant, oportet quod mutuus amor, qui est spiritus sanctus, ab utroque procedat. Secundum igitur originem, spiritus sanctus non est medius, sed tertia in Trinitate persona. Secundum vero praedictam habitudinem, est medius nexus duorum, ab utroque procedens.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29956)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut filio, licet intelligat, non tamen sibi competit producere verbum, quia intelligere convenit ei ut verbo procedenti; ita, licet spiritus sanctus amet, essentialiter accipiendo, non tamen convenit ei quod spiret amorem, quod est diligere notionaliter sumptum; quia sic diligit essentialiter ut amor procedens, non ut a quo procedit amor.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29957)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pater et filius non diligant se spiritu sancto. Augustinus enim, in VII de Trin., probat quod pater non est sapiens sapientia genita. Sed sicut filius est sapientia genita, ita spiritus sanctus est amor procedens, ut dictum est. Ergo pater et filius non diligunt se amore procedente, qui est spiritus sanctus.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29958)\n\nPraeterea, cum dicitur, pater et filius diligunt se spiritu sancto, hoc verbum diligere aut sumitur essentialiter, aut notionaliter. Sed non potest esse vera secundum quod sumitur essentialiter, quia pari ratione posset dici quod pater intelligit filio. Neque etiam secundum quod sumitur notionaliter, quia pari ratione posset dici quod pater et filius spirant spiritu sancto, vel quod pater generat filio. Ergo nullo modo haec est vera, pater et filius diligunt se spiritu sancto.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29959)\n\nPraeterea, eodem amore pater diligit filium, et se, et nos. Sed pater non diligit se spiritu sancto. Quia nullus actus notionalis reflectitur super principium actus, non enim potest dici quod pater generat se, vel spirat se. Ergo etiam non potest dici quod diligat se spiritu sancto, secundum quod diligere sumitur notionaliter. Item, amor quo diligit nos, non videtur esse spiritus sanctus, quia importatur respectus ad creaturam, et ita ad essentiam pertinet. Ergo et haec est falsa, pater diligit filium spiritu sancto.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29960)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod *spiritus sanctus est quo genitus a generante diligitur, genitoremque suum diligit*.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29961)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hanc quaestionem difficultatem affert quod, cum dicitur, pater diligit filium spiritu sancto, cum ablativus construatur in habitudine alicuius causae, videtur quod spiritus sanctus sit principium diligendi patri et filio; quod est omnino impossibile. Et ideo quidam dixerunt hanc esse falsam, pater et filius diligunt se spiritu sancto. Et dicunt hanc esse retractatam ab Augustino in suo simili, cum scilicet retractavit istam, pater est sapiens sapientia genita. Quidam vero dicunt quod est propositio impropria; et est sic exponenda, pater diligit filium spiritu sancto, idest amore essentiali, qui appropriatur spiritui sancto. Quidam vero dixerunt quod ablativus iste construitur in habitudine signi, ut sit sensus, spiritus sanctus est signum quod pater diligat filium, inquantum scilicet procedit ab eis ut amor. Quidam vero dixerunt quod ablativus iste construitur in habitudine causae formalis, quia spiritus sanctus est amor, quo formaliter pater et filius se invicem diligunt. Quidam vero dixerunt quod construitur in habitudine effectus formalis. Et isti propinquius ad veritatem accesserunt. Unde ad huius evidentiam, sciendum est quod, cum res communiter denominentur a suis formis, sicut album ab albedine, et homo ab humanitate; omne illud a quo aliquid denominatur, quantum ad hoc habet habitudinem formae. Ut si dicam, iste est indutus vestimento, iste ablativus construitur in habitudine causae formalis, quamvis non sit forma. Contingit autem aliquid denominari per id quod ab ipso procedit, non solum sicut agens actione; sed etiam sicut ipso termino actionis, qui est effectus, quando ipse effectus in intellectu actionis includitur. Dicimus enim quod ignis est calefaciens calefactione, quamvis calefactio non sit calor, qui est forma ignis, sed actio ab igne procedens, et dicimus quod arbor est florens floribus, quamvis flores non sint forma arboris, sed quidam effectus ab ipsa procedentes. Secundum hoc ergo dicendum quod, cum diligere in divinis dupliciter sumatur, essentialiter scilicet et notionaliter; secundum quod essentialiter sumitur, sic pater et filius non diligunt se spiritu sancto, sed essentia sua. Unde Augustinus dicit, in XV de Trin., *quis audet dicere patrem nec se nec filium nec spiritum sanctum diligere nisi per spiritum sanctum?* Et secundum hoc procedunt primae opiniones. Secundum vero quod notionaliter sumitur, sic diligere nihil est aliud quam spirare amorem; sicut dicere est producere verbum, et florere est producere flores. Sicut ergo dicitur arbor florens floribus, ita dicitur pater dicens verbo vel filio, se et creaturam, et pater et filius dicuntur diligentes spiritu sancto, vel amore procedente, et se et nos.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29962)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod esse sapientem vel intelligentem in divinis non sumitur nisi essentialiter, et ideo non potest dici quod pater sit sapiens vel intelligens filio. Sed diligere sumitur non solum essentialiter, sed etiam notionaliter. Et secundum hoc, possumus dicere quod pater et filius diligunt se spiritu sancto, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29963)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quando in intellectu alicuius actionis importatur determinatus effectus, potest denominari principium actionis et ab actione et ab effectu; sicut possumus dicere quod arbor est florens floritione, et floribus. Sed quando in actione non includitur determinatus effectus, tunc non potest principium actionis denominari ab effectu, sed solum ab actione, non enim dicimus quod arbor producit florem flore, sed productione floris. In hoc igitur quod dico spirat vel generat, importatur actus notionalis tantum. Unde non possumus dicere quod pater spiret spiritu sancto, vel generet filio. Possumus autem dicere quod pater dicit verbo, tanquam persona procedente, et dicit dictione, tanquam actu notionali, quia dicere importat determinatam personam procedentem, cum dicere sit producere verbum. Et similiter diligere, prout notionaliter sumitur, est producere amorem. Et ideo potest dici quod pater diligit filium spiritu sancto, tanquam persona procedente, et ipsa dilectione, tanquam actu notionali.\n\n[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29964)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod pater non solum filium, sed etiam se et nos diligit spiritu sancto. Quia, ut dictum est diligere, prout notionaliter sumitur, non solum importat productionem divinae personae, sed etiam personam productam per modum amoris, qui habet habitudinem ad rem dilectam. Unde, sicut pater dicit se et omnem creaturam verbo quod genuit, inquantum verbum genitum sufficienter repraesentat patrem et omnem creaturam; ita diligit se et omnem creaturam spiritu sancto, inquantum spiritus sanctus procedit ut amor bonitatis primae, secundum quam pater amat se et omnem creaturam. Et sic etiam patet quod respectus importatur ad creaturam et in verbo et in amore procedente, quasi secundario; inquantum scilicet veritas et bonitas divina est principium intelligendi et amandi omnem creaturam.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0002","text":"[I q. 37 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de nomine amoris. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum sit proprium nomen spiritus sancti. Secundo, utrum pater et filius diligant se spiritu sancto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0005","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod amor non sit proprium nomen spiritus sancti. Dicit enim Augustinus, XV de Trin., *nescio cur, sicut sapientia dicitur et pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, et simul omnes non tres sed una sapientia, non ita et caritas dicatur pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, et simul omnes una caritas*. Sed nullum nomen quod de singulis personis praedicatur et de omnibus in communi singulariter, est nomen proprium alicuius personae. Ergo hoc nomen amor non est proprium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0007","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0008","text":"Praeterea, spiritus sanctus est persona subsistens. Sed amor non significatur ut persona subsistens, sed ut actio quaedam ab amante transiens in amatum. Ergo amor non est proprium nomen spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0009","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0010","text":"Praeterea, amor est nexus amantium, quia secundum Dionysium, IV cap. de Div. Nom., est quaedam vis unitiva. Sed nexus est medium inter ea quae connectit, non autem aliquid ab eis procedens. Cum igitur spiritus sanctus procedat a patre et filio, sicut ostensum est, videtur quod non sit amor aut nexus patris et filii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0011","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0012","text":"Praeterea, cuiuslibet amantis est aliquis amor. Sed spiritus sanctus est amans. Ergo eius est aliquis amor. Si igitur spiritus sanctus est amor, erit amor amoris, et spiritus a spiritu. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0013","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, in homilia Pentecostes, *ipse spiritus sanctus est amor*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0015","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nomen amoris in divinis sumi potest et essentialiter et personaliter. Et secundum quod personaliter sumitur, est proprium nomen spiritus sancti; sicut verbum est proprium nomen filii. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod, cum in divinis, ut supra ostensum est, sint duae processiones, una per modum intellectus, quae est processio verbi; alia per modum voluntatis, quae est processio amoris, quia prima est nobis magis nota, ad singula significanda quae in ea considerari possunt, sunt magis propria nomina adinventa; non autem in processione voluntatis. Unde et quibusdam circumlocutionibus utimur ad significandam personam procedentem, et relationes etiam quae accipiuntur secundum hanc processionem, et processionis et spirationis nominibus nominantur, ut supra dictum est, quae tamen sunt magis nomina originis quam relationis, secundum proprietatem vocabuli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0017","text":"Et tamen similiter utramque processionem considerari oportet. Sicut enim ex hoc quod aliquis rem aliquam intelligit, provenit quaedam intellectualis conceptio rei intellectae in intelligente, quae dicitur verbum; ita ex hoc quod aliquis rem aliquam amat, provenit quaedam impressio, ut ita loquar, rei amatae in affectu amantis, secundum quam amatum dicitur esse in amante, sicut et intellectum in intelligente. Ita quod, cum aliquis seipsum intelligit et amat, est in seipso non solum per identitatem rei, sed etiam ut intellectum in intelligente, et amatum in amante. Sed ex parte intellectus, sunt vocabula adinventa ad significandum respectum intelligentis ad rem intellectam, ut patet in hoc quod dico intelligere, et sunt etiam alia vocabula adinventa ad significandum processum intellectualis conceptionis, scilicet ipsum dicere, et verbum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0018","text":"Unde in divinis intelligere solum essentialiter dicitur, quia non importat habitudinem ad verbum procedens, sed verbum personaliter dicitur, quia significat id quod procedit, ipsum vero dicere dicitur notionaliter, quia importat habitudinem principii verbi ad verbum ipsum. Ex parte autem voluntatis, praeter diligere et amare, quae important habitudinem amantis ad rem amatam, non sunt aliqua vocabula imposita, quae importent habitudinem ipsius impressionis vel affectionis rei amatae, quae provenit in amante ex hoc quod amat, ad suum principium, aut e converso. Et ideo, propter vocabulorum inopiam, huiusmodi habitudines significamus vocabulis amoris et dilectionis; sicut si verbum nominaremus intelligentiam conceptam, vel sapientiam genitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0019","text":"Sic igitur, inquantum in amore vel dilectione non importatur nisi habitudo amantis ad rem amatam, amor et diligere essentialiter dicuntur, sicut intelligentia et intelligere. Inquantum vero his vocabulis utimur ad exprimendam habitudinem eius rei quae procedit per modum amoris, ad suum principium, et e converso; ita quod per amorem intelligatur amor procedens, et per diligere intelligatur spirare amorem procedentem, sic amor est nomen personae, et diligere vel amare est verbum notionale, sicut dicere vel generare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0020","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0021","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de caritate, secundum quod essentialiter sumitur in divinis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0022","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0023","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intelligere et velle et amare, licet significentur per modum actionum transeuntium in obiecta, sunt tamen actiones manentes in agentibus, ut supra dictum est; ita tamen quod in ipso agente important habitudinem quandam ad obiectum. Unde amor, etiam in nobis, est aliquid manens in amante, et verbum cordis manens in dicente; tamen cum habitudine ad rem verbo expressam, vel amatam. Sed in Deo, in quo nullum est accidens, plus habet, quia tam verbum quam amor est subsistens. Cum ergo dicitur quod spiritus sanctus est amor patris in filium, vel in quidquam aliud, non significatur aliquid transiens in alium; sed solum habitudo amoris ad rem amatam; sicut et in verbo importatur habitudo verbi ad rem verbo expressam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0024","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0025","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod spiritus sanctus dicitur esse nexus patris et filii, inquantum est amor, quia, cum pater amet unica dilectione se et filium, et e converso, importatur in spiritu sancto, prout est amor, habitudo patris ad filium, et e converso, ut amantis ad amatum. Sed ex hoc ipso quod pater et filius se mutuo amant, oportet quod mutuus amor, qui est spiritus sanctus, ab utroque procedat. Secundum igitur originem, spiritus sanctus non est medius, sed tertia in Trinitate persona. Secundum vero praedictam habitudinem, est medius nexus duorum, ab utroque procedens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0026","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0027","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut filio, licet intelligat, non tamen sibi competit producere verbum, quia intelligere convenit ei ut verbo procedenti; ita, licet spiritus sanctus amet, essentialiter accipiendo, non tamen convenit ei quod spiret amorem, quod est diligere notionaliter sumptum; quia sic diligit essentialiter ut amor procedens, non ut a quo procedit amor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0028","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0029","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0030","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pater et filius non diligant se spiritu sancto. Augustinus enim, in VII de Trin., probat quod pater non est sapiens sapientia genita. Sed sicut filius est sapientia genita, ita spiritus sanctus est amor procedens, ut dictum est. Ergo pater et filius non diligunt se amore procedente, qui est spiritus sanctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0031","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0032","text":"Praeterea, cum dicitur, pater et filius diligunt se spiritu sancto, hoc verbum diligere aut sumitur essentialiter, aut notionaliter. Sed non potest esse vera secundum quod sumitur essentialiter, quia pari ratione posset dici quod pater intelligit filio. Neque etiam secundum quod sumitur notionaliter, quia pari ratione posset dici quod pater et filius spirant spiritu sancto, vel quod pater generat filio. Ergo nullo modo haec est vera, pater et filius diligunt se spiritu sancto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0033","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0034","text":"Praeterea, eodem amore pater diligit filium, et se, et nos. Sed pater non diligit se spiritu sancto. Quia nullus actus notionalis reflectitur super principium actus, non enim potest dici quod pater generat se, vel spirat se. Ergo etiam non potest dici quod diligat se spiritu sancto, secundum quod diligere sumitur notionaliter. Item, amor quo diligit nos, non videtur esse spiritus sanctus, quia importatur respectus ad creaturam, et ita ad essentiam pertinet. Ergo et haec est falsa, pater diligit filium spiritu sancto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0035","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0036","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod *spiritus sanctus est quo genitus a generante diligitur, genitoremque suum diligit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0037","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0038","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hanc quaestionem difficultatem affert quod, cum dicitur, pater diligit filium spiritu sancto, cum ablativus construatur in habitudine alicuius causae, videtur quod spiritus sanctus sit principium diligendi patri et filio; quod est omnino impossibile. Et ideo quidam dixerunt hanc esse falsam, pater et filius diligunt se spiritu sancto. Et dicunt hanc esse retractatam ab Augustino in suo simili, cum scilicet retractavit istam, pater est sapiens sapientia genita. Quidam vero dicunt quod est propositio impropria; et est sic exponenda, pater diligit filium spiritu sancto, idest amore essentiali, qui appropriatur spiritui sancto. Quidam vero dixerunt quod ablativus iste construitur in habitudine signi, ut sit sensus, spiritus sanctus est signum quod pater diligat filium, inquantum scilicet procedit ab eis ut amor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0039","text":"Quidam vero dixerunt quod ablativus iste construitur in habitudine causae formalis, quia spiritus sanctus est amor, quo formaliter pater et filius se invicem diligunt. Quidam vero dixerunt quod construitur in habitudine effectus formalis. Et isti propinquius ad veritatem accesserunt. Unde ad huius evidentiam, sciendum est quod, cum res communiter denominentur a suis formis, sicut album ab albedine, et homo ab humanitate; omne illud a quo aliquid denominatur, quantum ad hoc habet habitudinem formae. Ut si dicam, iste est indutus vestimento, iste ablativus construitur in habitudine causae formalis, quamvis non sit forma. Contingit autem aliquid denominari per id quod ab ipso procedit, non solum sicut agens actione; sed etiam sicut ipso termino actionis, qui est effectus, quando ipse effectus in intellectu actionis includitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0040","text":"Dicimus enim quod ignis est calefaciens calefactione, quamvis calefactio non sit calor, qui est forma ignis, sed actio ab igne procedens, et dicimus quod arbor est florens floribus, quamvis flores non sint forma arboris, sed quidam effectus ab ipsa procedentes. Secundum hoc ergo dicendum quod, cum diligere in divinis dupliciter sumatur, essentialiter scilicet et notionaliter; secundum quod essentialiter sumitur, sic pater et filius non diligunt se spiritu sancto, sed essentia sua. Unde Augustinus dicit, in XV de Trin., *quis audet dicere patrem nec se nec filium nec spiritum sanctum diligere nisi per spiritum sanctum?* Et secundum hoc procedunt primae opiniones. Secundum vero quod notionaliter sumitur, sic diligere nihil est aliud quam spirare amorem; sicut dicere est producere verbum, et florere est producere flores.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0041","text":"Sicut ergo dicitur arbor florens floribus, ita dicitur pater dicens verbo vel filio, se et creaturam, et pater et filius dicuntur diligentes spiritu sancto, vel amore procedente, et se et nos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0042","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0043","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod esse sapientem vel intelligentem in divinis non sumitur nisi essentialiter, et ideo non potest dici quod pater sit sapiens vel intelligens filio. Sed diligere sumitur non solum essentialiter, sed etiam notionaliter. Et secundum hoc, possumus dicere quod pater et filius diligunt se spiritu sancto, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0044","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0045","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quando in intellectu alicuius actionis importatur determinatus effectus, potest denominari principium actionis et ab actione et ab effectu; sicut possumus dicere quod arbor est florens floritione, et floribus. Sed quando in actione non includitur determinatus effectus, tunc non potest principium actionis denominari ab effectu, sed solum ab actione, non enim dicimus quod arbor producit florem flore, sed productione floris. In hoc igitur quod dico spirat vel generat, importatur actus notionalis tantum. Unde non possumus dicere quod pater spiret spiritu sancto, vel generet filio. Possumus autem dicere quod pater dicit verbo, tanquam persona procedente, et dicit dictione, tanquam actu notionali, quia dicere importat determinatam personam procedentem, cum dicere sit producere verbum. Et similiter diligere, prout notionaliter sumitur, est producere amorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0046","text":"Et ideo potest dici quod pater diligit filium spiritu sancto, tanquam persona procedente, et ipsa dilectione, tanquam actu notionali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0047","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0048","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod pater non solum filium, sed etiam se et nos diligit spiritu sancto. Quia, ut dictum est diligere, prout notionaliter sumitur, non solum importat productionem divinae personae, sed etiam personam productam per modum amoris, qui habet habitudinem ad rem dilectam. Unde, sicut pater dicit se et omnem creaturam verbo quod genuit, inquantum verbum genitum sufficienter repraesentat patrem et omnem creaturam; ita diligit se et omnem creaturam spiritu sancto, inquantum spiritus sanctus procedit ut amor bonitatis primae, secundum quam pater amat se et omnem creaturam. Et sic etiam patet quod respectus importatur ad creaturam et in verbo et in amore procedente, quasi secundario; inquantum scilicet veritas et bonitas divina est principium intelligendi et amandi omnem creaturam.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fa3065c3b53a5e1152a727111dfaa69abbb9df47317baa87e49498ba90106bb5","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141839940958Z_504f8581","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 37","source_sha256":"fa3065c3b53a5e1152a727111dfaa69abbb9df47317baa87e49498ba90106bb5","sha256":"2031b66f50b3a96d869d541b09dbbd83bc9f2c197d35daab068baccf7d7c4cc6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:18:39.940970+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0002","text":"[I q. 37 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0003","text":"Next, the name of love is considered. And concerning this, two questions are asked. First, whether it is a proper name of the Holy Spirit. Second, whether the Father and the Son love themselves by the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0005","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0006","text":"To the first, one proceeds as follows. It seems that love is not a proper name of the Holy Spirit. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XV: *I do not know why, just as wisdom is said of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit, and of all together—not three wisdoms but one wisdom—so charity is not said of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit, and of all together one charity.* But no name which is predicated of the individual persons and, in common, singularly of all, is the proper name of any person. Therefore this name, love, is not proper to the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0007","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0008","text":"Further, the Holy Spirit is a subsisting person. But love is not signified as a subsisting person, but as a certain action passing from the lover into the beloved. Therefore love is not a proper name of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0009","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0010","text":"Further, love is the bond of lovers, because, according to Dionysius, chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, it is a certain unitive power. But a bond is intermediate between the things that it connects, and is not something proceeding from them. Therefore, since the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son, as has been shown, it seems that he is not the love or bond of the Father and the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0011","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0012","text":"Further, every lover has some love. But the Holy Spirit is a lover. Therefore he has some love. If, then, the Holy Spirit is love, there will be love of love, and spirit from spirit. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0013","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0014","text":"On the contrary, Gregory says, in his homily on Pentecost: *the Holy Spirit himself is love*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0015","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0016","text":"I answer that the name of love can be taken in divine things both essentially and personally. And insofar as it is taken personally, it is a proper name of the Holy Spirit, just as Word is a proper name of the Son. For the evidence of this, it should be known that, since in divine things, as was shown above, there are two processions, one according to the mode of intellect, which is the procession of the Word, and the other according to the mode of will, which is the procession of love, because the first is better known to us, more proper names have been devised for signifying the individual things that can be considered in it, but not in the procession of the will. Hence we also use certain circumlocutions to signify the proceeding person, and even the relations that are understood according to this procession are named by the names of procession and spiration, as was said above; although, according to the proper meaning of the term, these are names of origin rather than of relation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0017","text":"Yet both processions must be considered in a similar way. For just as from the fact that someone understands some thing, there results in the one understanding a certain intellectual conception of the thing understood, which is called a word; so from the fact that someone loves some thing, there results, so to speak, a certain impression of the thing loved in the affection of the lover, according to which the beloved is said to be in the lover, just as the understood is in the one understanding. Thus, when someone understands and loves himself, he is in himself not only through the identity of the thing, but also as the understood in the one understanding and as the beloved in the lover. But on the part of the intellect, terms have been devised to signify the relation of the one understanding to the thing understood, as is clear in what I call understanding; and other terms have also been devised to signify the process of the intellectual conception, namely, speaking itself and word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0018","text":"Hence in divine things understanding alone is said essentially, because it does not imply a relation to the proceeding Word; but Word is said personally, because it signifies that which proceeds; whereas speaking is said notionally, because it implies the relation of the principle of the Word to the Word itself. But on the part of the will, besides loving and to love, which imply the relation of the lover to the thing loved, no terms have been imposed which imply the relation to its principle, or conversely, of the very impression or affection of the thing loved which results in the lover from the fact that he loves. And therefore, because of the lack of terms, we signify such relations by the terms love and affection; just as if we were to call the Word conceived understanding or begotten wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0019","text":"Thus, therefore, insofar as in love or affection nothing is implied except the relation of the lover to the thing loved, love and loving are said essentially, just as understanding and to understand. But insofar as we use these terms to express the relation of that thing which proceeds according to the mode of love to its principle, and conversely—so that by love proceeding love is understood, and by loving the spirating of proceeding love is understood—then love is the name of a person, and loving or to love is a notional verb, just as speaking or begetting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0020","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0021","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine speaks of charity insofar as it is taken essentially in divine things, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0022","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0023","text":"To the second, it must be said that to understand, to will, and to love, although they are signified after the manner of actions passing into objects, are nevertheless actions remaining in the agents, as was said above; yet in the agent itself they imply a certain relation to the object. Hence love, even in us, is something remaining in the lover, and the word of the heart remains in the speaker; nevertheless, with a relation to the thing expressed by the word or loved. But in God, in whom there is no accident, this has more force, because both Word and love are subsistent. Therefore, when it is said that the Holy Spirit is the love of the Father for the Son, or for anything else, something passing into another is not signified; but only the relation of love to the thing loved, just as in the Word there is implied the relation of the Word to the thing expressed by the Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0024","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0025","text":"To the third, it must be said that the Holy Spirit is called the bond of the Father and the Son, insofar as He is Love; because, since the Father loves Himself and the Son with one love, and conversely, in the Holy Spirit, insofar as He is Love, is implied the relation of the Father to the Son, and conversely, as of lover to beloved. But from the very fact that the Father and the Son mutually love one another, the mutual Love, which is the Holy Spirit, must proceed from both. Therefore, according to origin, the Holy Spirit is not intermediate, but is the third Person in the Trinity. But according to the aforesaid relation, He is the intermediate bond of the two, proceeding from both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0026","text":"[I q. 37 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0027","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, just as it belongs to the Son to understand, but not to produce the Word, because understanding belongs to Him as the proceeding Word; so too, although the Holy Spirit loves, taking this essentially, it nevertheless does not belong to Him to breathe forth Love, which is to love taken notionally. For thus He loves essentially as proceeding Love, not as that from which Love proceeds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0028","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0029","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0030","text":"We proceed to the second as follows. It seems that the Father and the Son do not love one another by the Holy Spirit. For Augustine, in *On the Trinity*, VII, proves that the Father is not wise by begotten wisdom. But just as the Son is begotten Wisdom, so the Holy Spirit is proceeding Love, as has been said. Therefore the Father and the Son do not love one another by proceeding Love, which is the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0031","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0032","text":"Further, when it is said, “the Father and the Son love one another by the Holy Spirit,” this verb “to love” is taken either essentially or notionally. But it cannot be true insofar as it is taken essentially, because by the same reasoning it could be said that the Father understands by the Son. Nor can it be true insofar as it is taken notionally, because by the same reasoning it could be said that the Father and the Son breathe forth by the Holy Spirit, or that the Father begets by the Son. Therefore in no way is this true: the Father and the Son love one another by the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0033","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0034","text":"Further, by the same love the Father loves the Son, Himself, and us. But the Father does not love Himself by the Holy Spirit, because no notional act is reflected back upon the principle of the act; for one cannot say that the Father begets Himself, or breathes forth Himself. Therefore neither can it be said that He loves Himself by the Holy Spirit, insofar as loving is taken notionally. Likewise, the love by which He loves us does not seem to be the Holy Spirit, because a relation to a creature is implied, and thus it pertains to the essence. Therefore this too is false: the Father loves the Son by the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0035","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0036","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, VI, that *the Holy Spirit is that by which the Begotten is loved by the Begetter, and loves His Begetter*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0037","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0038","text":"I answer that it must be said that this question presents a difficulty because, when it is said, “the Father loves the Son by the Holy Spirit,” since the ablative is construed in the relation of some cause, it seems that the Holy Spirit is a principle of loving for the Father and the Son; which is altogether impossible. And therefore some have said that this is false: the Father and the Son love one another by the Holy Spirit. And they say that Augustine retracted this in his *Retractions*, when, namely, he retracted this statement: “the Father is wise by begotten wisdom.” Others, however, say that it is an improper proposition; and it should be expounded thus: the Father loves the Son by the Holy Spirit, that is, by essential love, which is appropriated to the Holy Spirit. Others have said that this ablative is construed in the relation of a sign, so that the sense is: the Holy Spirit is a sign that the Father loves the Son, insofar, namely, as He proceeds from them as Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0039","text":"Others, however, have said that this ablative is construed in the relation of formal cause, because the Holy Spirit is the Love by which the Father and the Son formally love one another. Others, however, have said that it is construed in the relation of formal effect. And these came closer to the truth. Hence, for the elucidation of this, one must know that, since things are commonly denominated from their forms, as white from whiteness, and man from humanity, everything from which something is denominated has, in this respect, the relation of a form. Thus, if I say, “this man is clothed with a garment,” this ablative is construed in the relation of formal cause, although it is not a form. Now something may be denominated from that which proceeds from it, not only as an agent from an action, but also from the very term of the action, which is an effect, when the effect itself is included in the notion of the action.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0040","text":"For we say that fire is heating by heating, although heating is not heat, which is the form of fire, but an action proceeding from fire; and we say that a tree is flowering with flowers, although flowers are not the form of the tree, but certain effects proceeding from it. Accordingly, it must be said that, since loving in divine matters is taken in two ways, namely essentially and notionally: insofar as it is taken essentially, the Father and the Son do not love one another by the Holy Spirit, but by Their essence. Hence Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XV: *who dares to say that the Father loves neither Himself nor the Son nor the Holy Spirit except through the Holy Spirit?* And according to this, the first opinions proceed. But insofar as it is taken notionally, to love is nothing other than to breathe forth Love, just as to speak is to produce a word, and to flower is to produce flowers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0041","text":"Therefore, just as a tree is said to flower with flowers, so the Father is said to speak Himself and the creature by the Word or Son, and the Father and the Son are said to love both themselves and us by the Holy Spirit, or by proceeding Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0042","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0043","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that being wise or understanding in divine matters is taken only essentially, and therefore it cannot be said that the Father is wise or understanding by the Son. But loving is taken not only essentially, but also notionally. And according to this, we can say that the Father and the Son love one another by the Holy Spirit, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0044","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0045","text":"To the second, it must be said that, when a determinate effect is implied in the notion of an action, the principle of the action can be denominated both from the action and from the effect; just as we can say that a tree is flowering by flowering and by flowers. But when a determinate effect is not included in the action, then the principle of the action cannot be denominated from the effect, but only from the action; for we do not say that a tree produces a flower by a flower, but by the production of a flower. Therefore, in saying “breathes forth” or “begets,” only a notional act is implied. Hence we cannot say that the Father breathes forth by the Holy Spirit, or begets by the Son. But we can say that the Father speaks by the Word, as by the proceeding person, and speaks by speaking, as by the notional act, because speaking implies a determinate proceeding person, since to speak is to produce a word. And similarly, loving, insofar as it is taken notionally, is to produce Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0046","text":"And therefore it can be said that the Father loves the Son by the Holy Spirit, as by the proceeding person, and by loving itself, as by the notional act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0047","text":"[I q. 37 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0048","text":"To the third, it must be said that the Father loves not only the Son, but also Himself and us, by the Holy Spirit. For, as has been said, loving, insofar as it is taken notionally, implies not only the production of a divine person, but also the person produced in the mode of Love, which has a relation to the thing loved. Hence, just as the Father speaks Himself and every creature by the Word which He has begotten, insofar as the begotten Word sufficiently represents the Father and every creature; so He loves Himself and every creature by the Holy Spirit, insofar as the Holy Spirit proceeds as Love of the first goodness, according to which the Father loves Himself and every creature. And thus it is also clear that a relation to the creature is implied both in the Word and in proceeding Love, as it were secondarily; namely, insofar as the divine truth and goodness are the principle of understanding and loving every creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_29:P0048"]}],"source_digest":"0a5ea7647be4bd9d7413698d3b4f99cc025e0eaf8bafbf06123487d96c7bda3d","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 37"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 37","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30","title":"First Part · Question 38","order":39,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_30","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 38 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29965)\n\nConsequenter quaeritur de dono. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum donum possit esse nomen personale. Secundo, utrum sit proprium spiritus sancti.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29966)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod donum non sit nomen personale. Omne enim nomen personale importat aliquam distinctionem in divinis. Sed nomen doni non importat aliquam distinctionem in divinis, dicit enim Augustinus, XV de Trin., quod *spiritus sanctus ita datur sicut Dei donum, ut etiam seipsum det sicut Deus*. Ergo donum non est nomen personale.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29967)\n\nPraeterea, nullum nomen personale convenit essentiae divinae. Sed essentia divina est donum quod pater dat filio, ut patet per Hilarium, IX de Trin. Ergo donum non est nomen personale.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29968)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Damascenum, nihil est subiectum aut serviens in divinis personis. Sed donum importat quandam subiectionem et ad eum cui datur, et ad eum a quo datur. Ergo donum non est nomen personale.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29969)\n\nPraeterea, donum importat respectum ad creaturam, et ita videtur de Deo dici ex tempore. Sed nomina personalia dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno, ut pater et filius. Ergo donum non est nomen personale.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29970)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XV de Trin., *sicut corpus carnis nihil aliud est quam caro, sic donum spiritus sancti nihil aliud est quam spiritus sanctus*. Sed spiritus sanctus est nomen personale. Ergo et donum.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29971)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in nomine doni importatur aptitudo ad hoc quod donetur. Quod autem donatur, habet habitudinem et ad id a quo datur, et ad id cui datur, non enim daretur ab aliquo nisi esset eius; et ad hoc alicui datur, ut eius sit. Persona autem divina dicitur esse alicuius, vel secundum originem, sicut filius est patris; vel inquantum ab aliquo habetur. Habere autem dicimur id quo libere possumus uti vel frui, ut volumus. Et per hunc modum divina persona non potest haberi nisi a rationali creatura Deo coniuncta. Aliae autem creaturae moveri quidem possunt a divina persona; non tamen sic quod in potestate earum sit frui divina persona, et uti effectu eius. Ad quod quandoque pertingit rationalis creatura; ut puta cum sic fit particeps divini verbi et procedentis amoris, ut possit libere Deum vere cognoscere et recte amare. Unde sola creatura rationalis potest habere divinam personam. Sed ad hoc quod sic eam habeat, non potest propria virtute pervenire, unde oportet quod hoc ei desuper detur; hoc enim dari nobis dicitur, quod aliunde habemus. Et sic divinae personae competit dari, et esse donum.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29972)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod nomen doni importat distinctionem personalem, secundum quod donum dicitur esse alicuius per originem. Et tamen spiritus sanctus dat seipsum, inquantum est sui ipsius, ut potens se uti, vel potius frui; sicut et homo liber dicitur esse sui ipsius. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit, super Ioan., *quid tam tuum est quam tu?* Vel dicendum, et melius, quod donum oportet esse aliquo modo dantis. Sed hoc esse huius dicitur multipliciter. Uno modo, per modum identitatis, sicut dicit Augustinus super Ioan., et sic donum non distinguitur a dante, sed ab eo cui datur. Et sic dicitur quod spiritus sanctus dat se. Alio modo dicitur aliquid esse alicuius ut possessio vel servus, et sic oportet quod donum essentialiter distinguatur a dante. Et sic donum Dei est aliquid creatum. Tertio modo dicitur hoc esse huius per originem tantum, et sic filius est patris, et spiritus sanctus utriusque. Inquantum ergo donum hoc modo dicitur esse dantis, sic distinguitur a dante personaliter, et est nomen personale.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29973)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod essentia dicitur esse donum patris primo modo, quia essentia est patris per modum identitatis.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29974)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod donum, secundum quod est nomen personale in divinis, non importat subiectionem, sed originem tantum, in comparatione ad dantem. In comparatione vero ad eum cui datur, importat liberum usum vel fruitionem, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29975)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod donum non dicitur ex eo quod actu datur, sed inquantum habet aptitudinem ut possit dari. Unde ab aeterno divina persona dicitur donum, licet ex tempore detur. Nec tamen per hoc quod importatur respectus ad creaturam, oportet quod sit essentiale, sed quod aliquid essentiale in suo intellectu includatur, sicut essentia includitur in intellectu personae, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29976)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod donum non sit proprium nomen spiritus sancti. Donum enim dicitur ex eo quod datur. Sed, sicut dicitur Isa. IX, *filius datus est nobis*. Ergo esse donum convenit filio, sicut spiritui sancto.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29977)\n\nPraeterea, omne nomen proprium alicuius personae significat aliquam eius proprietatem. Sed hoc nomen donum non significat proprietatem aliquam spiritus sancti. Ergo donum non est proprium nomen spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29978)\n\nPraeterea, spiritus sanctus potest dici spiritus alicuius hominis, ut supra dictum est. Sed non potest dici donum alicuius hominis, sed solum donum Dei. Ergo donum non est proprium nomen spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29979)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in IV de Trin., *sicut natum esse est filio a patre esse, ita spiritui sancto donum Dei esse est a patre et filio procedere*. Sed spiritus sanctus sortitur proprium nomen inquantum procedit a patre et filio. Ergo et donum est proprium nomen spiritus sancti.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29980)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod donum, secundum quod personaliter sumitur in divinis, est proprium nomen spiritus sancti. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod donum proprie est datio irreddibilis, secundum philosophum, idest quod non datur intentione retributionis, et sic importat gratuitam donationem. Ratio autem gratuitae donationis est amor, ideo enim damus gratis alicui aliquid, quia volumus ei bonum. Primum ergo quod damus ei, est amor quo volumus ei bonum. Unde manifestum est quod amor habet rationem primi doni, per quod omnia dona gratuita donantur. Unde, cum spiritus sanctus procedat ut amor, sicut iam dictum est, procedit in ratione doni primi. Unde dicit Augustinus, XV de Trin., quod *per donum quod est spiritus sanctus, multa propria dona dividuntur membris Christi*.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29981)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut filius, quia procedit per modum verbi, quod de ratione sua habet quod sit similitudo sui principii dicitur proprie imago, licet etiam spiritus sanctus sit similis patri; ita etiam spiritus sanctus, quia a patre procedit ut amor, dicitur proprie donum, licet etiam filius detur. Hoc enim ipsum quod filius datur, est ex patris amore, secundum illud Ioan. III, *sic Deus dilexit mundum, ut filium suum unigenitum daret*.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29982)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in nomine doni importatur quod sit dantis per originem. Et sic importatur proprietas originis spiritus sancti, quae est processio.\n\n[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29983)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod donum, antequam detur, est tantum dantis, sed postquam datur, est eius cui datur. Quia igitur donum non importat dationem in actu, non potest dici quod sit donum hominis; sed donum Dei dantis. Cum autem iam datum est, tunc hominis est vel spiritus vel datum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0002","text":"[I q. 38 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0003","text":"Consequenter quaeritur de dono. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum donum possit esse nomen personale. Secundo, utrum sit proprium spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0005","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod donum non sit nomen personale. Omne enim nomen personale importat aliquam distinctionem in divinis. Sed nomen doni non importat aliquam distinctionem in divinis, dicit enim Augustinus, XV de Trin., quod *spiritus sanctus ita datur sicut Dei donum, ut etiam seipsum det sicut Deus*. Ergo donum non est nomen personale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0007","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0008","text":"Praeterea, nullum nomen personale convenit essentiae divinae. Sed essentia divina est donum quod pater dat filio, ut patet per Hilarium, IX de Trin. Ergo donum non est nomen personale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0009","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0010","text":"Praeterea, secundum Damascenum, nihil est subiectum aut serviens in divinis personis. Sed donum importat quandam subiectionem et ad eum cui datur, et ad eum a quo datur. Ergo donum non est nomen personale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0011","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0012","text":"Praeterea, donum importat respectum ad creaturam, et ita videtur de Deo dici ex tempore. Sed nomina personalia dicuntur de Deo ab aeterno, ut pater et filius. Ergo donum non est nomen personale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0013","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XV de Trin., *sicut corpus carnis nihil aliud est quam caro, sic donum spiritus sancti nihil aliud est quam spiritus sanctus*. Sed spiritus sanctus est nomen personale. Ergo et donum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0015","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in nomine doni importatur aptitudo ad hoc quod donetur. Quod autem donatur, habet habitudinem et ad id a quo datur, et ad id cui datur, non enim daretur ab aliquo nisi esset eius; et ad hoc alicui datur, ut eius sit. Persona autem divina dicitur esse alicuius, vel secundum originem, sicut filius est patris; vel inquantum ab aliquo habetur. Habere autem dicimur id quo libere possumus uti vel frui, ut volumus. Et per hunc modum divina persona non potest haberi nisi a rationali creatura Deo coniuncta. Aliae autem creaturae moveri quidem possunt a divina persona; non tamen sic quod in potestate earum sit frui divina persona, et uti effectu eius. Ad quod quandoque pertingit rationalis creatura; ut puta cum sic fit particeps divini verbi et procedentis amoris, ut possit libere Deum vere cognoscere et recte amare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0017","text":"Unde sola creatura rationalis potest habere divinam personam. Sed ad hoc quod sic eam habeat, non potest propria virtute pervenire, unde oportet quod hoc ei desuper detur; hoc enim dari nobis dicitur, quod aliunde habemus. Et sic divinae personae competit dari, et esse donum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0018","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod nomen doni importat distinctionem personalem, secundum quod donum dicitur esse alicuius per originem. Et tamen spiritus sanctus dat seipsum, inquantum est sui ipsius, ut potens se uti, vel potius frui; sicut et homo liber dicitur esse sui ipsius. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit, super Ioan., *quid tam tuum est quam tu?* Vel dicendum, et melius, quod donum oportet esse aliquo modo dantis. Sed hoc esse huius dicitur multipliciter. Uno modo, per modum identitatis, sicut dicit Augustinus super Ioan., et sic donum non distinguitur a dante, sed ab eo cui datur. Et sic dicitur quod spiritus sanctus dat se. Alio modo dicitur aliquid esse alicuius ut possessio vel servus, et sic oportet quod donum essentialiter distinguatur a dante. Et sic donum Dei est aliquid creatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0020","text":"Tertio modo dicitur hoc esse huius per originem tantum, et sic filius est patris, et spiritus sanctus utriusque. Inquantum ergo donum hoc modo dicitur esse dantis, sic distinguitur a dante personaliter, et est nomen personale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0021","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0022","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod essentia dicitur esse donum patris primo modo, quia essentia est patris per modum identitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0023","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0024","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod donum, secundum quod est nomen personale in divinis, non importat subiectionem, sed originem tantum, in comparatione ad dantem. In comparatione vero ad eum cui datur, importat liberum usum vel fruitionem, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0025","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0026","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod donum non dicitur ex eo quod actu datur, sed inquantum habet aptitudinem ut possit dari. Unde ab aeterno divina persona dicitur donum, licet ex tempore detur. Nec tamen per hoc quod importatur respectus ad creaturam, oportet quod sit essentiale, sed quod aliquid essentiale in suo intellectu includatur, sicut essentia includitur in intellectu personae, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0027","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0028","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0029","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod donum non sit proprium nomen spiritus sancti. Donum enim dicitur ex eo quod datur. Sed, sicut dicitur Isa. IX, *filius datus est nobis*. Ergo esse donum convenit filio, sicut spiritui sancto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0030","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0031","text":"Praeterea, omne nomen proprium alicuius personae significat aliquam eius proprietatem. Sed hoc nomen donum non significat proprietatem aliquam spiritus sancti. Ergo donum non est proprium nomen spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0032","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0033","text":"Praeterea, spiritus sanctus potest dici spiritus alicuius hominis, ut supra dictum est. Sed non potest dici donum alicuius hominis, sed solum donum Dei. Ergo donum non est proprium nomen spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0034","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0035","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in IV de Trin., *sicut natum esse est filio a patre esse, ita spiritui sancto donum Dei esse est a patre et filio procedere*. Sed spiritus sanctus sortitur proprium nomen inquantum procedit a patre et filio. Ergo et donum est proprium nomen spiritus sancti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0036","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0037","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod donum, secundum quod personaliter sumitur in divinis, est proprium nomen spiritus sancti. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod donum proprie est datio irreddibilis, secundum philosophum, idest quod non datur intentione retributionis, et sic importat gratuitam donationem. Ratio autem gratuitae donationis est amor, ideo enim damus gratis alicui aliquid, quia volumus ei bonum. Primum ergo quod damus ei, est amor quo volumus ei bonum. Unde manifestum est quod amor habet rationem primi doni, per quod omnia dona gratuita donantur. Unde, cum spiritus sanctus procedat ut amor, sicut iam dictum est, procedit in ratione doni primi. Unde dicit Augustinus, XV de Trin., quod *per donum quod est spiritus sanctus, multa propria dona dividuntur membris Christi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0038","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0039","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut filius, quia procedit per modum verbi, quod de ratione sua habet quod sit similitudo sui principii dicitur proprie imago, licet etiam spiritus sanctus sit similis patri; ita etiam spiritus sanctus, quia a patre procedit ut amor, dicitur proprie donum, licet etiam filius detur. Hoc enim ipsum quod filius datur, est ex patris amore, secundum illud Ioan. III, *sic Deus dilexit mundum, ut filium suum unigenitum daret*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0040","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0041","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in nomine doni importatur quod sit dantis per originem. Et sic importatur proprietas originis spiritus sancti, quae est processio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0042","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0043","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod donum, antequam detur, est tantum dantis, sed postquam datur, est eius cui datur. Quia igitur donum non importat dationem in actu, non potest dici quod sit donum hominis; sed donum Dei dantis. Cum autem iam datum est, tunc hominis est vel spiritus vel datum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"07ad112e3d151a0d96c8c8f2e47f827aa651e66ce1306c1955db5fdd46f1a295","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T141921496096Z_184a08c4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 38","source_sha256":"07ad112e3d151a0d96c8c8f2e47f827aa651e66ce1306c1955db5fdd46f1a295","sha256":"b7d5c02d24d6f1493a5d61a70d14ec1d0128c14a5eb19ff8cc941db0cb2c0f96","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:19:21.496121+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0002","text":"[I q. 38 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0003","text":"Next, the gift is considered. And concerning this, two questions are asked. First, whether “gift” can be a personal name. Second, whether it is proper to the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0005","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that gift is not a personal name. For every personal name implies some distinction in the divine [persons]. But the name of gift does not imply any distinction in the divine [persons]; for Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XV, that *the Holy Spirit is given as the gift of God, so that he also gives himself as God*. Therefore gift is not a personal name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0007","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0008","text":"Further, no personal name belongs to the divine essence. But the divine essence is the gift which the Father gives to the Son, as is clear from Hilary, *On the Trinity*, IX. Therefore gift is not a personal name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0009","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0010","text":"Further, according to Damascene, there is nothing subject or serving in the divine persons. But gift implies a certain subjection, both to him to whom it is given and to him by whom it is given. Therefore gift is not a personal name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0011","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0012","text":"Further, gift implies a relation to a creature, and thus seems to be said of God in time. But personal names are said of God from eternity, as Father and Son. Therefore gift is not a personal name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0013","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0014","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XV: *as the body of flesh is nothing other than flesh, so the gift of the Holy Spirit is nothing other than the Holy Spirit*. But the Holy Spirit is a personal name. Therefore so too is gift.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0015","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that in the name gift there is implied an aptitude for being given. Now that which is given has a relation both to that by whom it is given and to that to whom it is given; for it would not be given by someone unless it were his; and it is given to someone in order that it may be his. But a divine person is said to be someone’s either according to origin, as the Son is the Father’s; or insofar as it is possessed by someone. Now we are said to possess that which we can freely use or enjoy as we will. And in this way a divine person cannot be possessed except by a rational creature united to God. Other creatures, indeed, can be moved by a divine person; yet not in such a way that it is in their power to enjoy the divine person and to use its effect. To this, however, the rational creature sometimes attains; for example, when it is thus made a participant in the divine Word and in the proceeding love, so that it can freely know God truly and love [him] rightly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0017","text":"Hence the rational creature alone can possess a divine person. But it cannot attain by its own power to possessing it thus; hence it is necessary that this be given to it from above. For that is said to be given to us which we have from another source. And thus it belongs to a divine person to be given and to be a gift.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0018","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the name gift implies a personal distinction, insofar as a gift is said to be someone’s through origin. And yet the Holy Spirit gives himself, insofar as he is his own, as being able to use himself, or rather to enjoy himself; just as a free man too is said to be his own. And this is what Augustine says, commenting on John: *what is so yours as you are?* Or it must be said, and better, that a gift must in some way be the giver’s. But this “being his” is said in many ways. In one way, by the mode of identity, as Augustine says, commenting on John; and thus the gift is not distinguished from the giver, but from him to whom it is given. And in this way the Holy Spirit is said to give himself. In another way, something is said to be someone’s as a possession or a slave; and thus it is necessary that the gift be essentially distinguished from the giver. And thus God’s gift is something created.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0020","text":"In a third way, this is said to be his through origin alone, and thus the Son is the Father’s, and the Holy Spirit is both [theirs]. Therefore, insofar as a gift is said to be the giver’s in this way, it is personally distinguished from the giver and is a personal name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0021","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0022","text":"To the second, it must be said that the essence is called the Father’s gift in the first way, because the essence is the Father’s by the mode of identity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0023","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0024","text":"To the third, it must be said that gift, insofar as it is a personal name in the divine [persons], does not imply subjection, but origin alone, in comparison with the giver. But in comparison with him to whom it is given, it implies free use or enjoyment, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0025","text":"[I q. 38 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0026","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that gift is not so called from the fact that it is actually given, but insofar as it has the aptitude to be able to be given. Hence, from eternity a divine person is called gift, although it is given in time. Yet from the fact that a relation to a creature is implied, it does not have to be essential; rather, something essential must be included in its intelligibility, just as essence is included in the intelligibility of person, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0027","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0028","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0029","text":"We proceed to the second as follows. It seems that Gift is not a proper name of the Holy Spirit. For gift is so called from the fact that it is given. But, as is said in Isa. IX, *a son has been given to us*. Therefore, to be gift befits the Son just as it does the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0030","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0031","text":"Moreover, every proper name of a person signifies some property of that person. But this name, gift, does not signify any property of the Holy Spirit. Therefore Gift is not a proper name of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0032","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0033","text":"Moreover, the Holy Spirit can be called the spirit of some man, as was said above. But he cannot be called the gift of some man, but only the gift of God. Therefore Gift is not a proper name of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0034","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0035","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in IV *On the Trinity*, *just as for the Son to be born is to be from the Father, so for the Holy Spirit to be the gift of God is to proceed from the Father and the Son*. But the Holy Spirit receives a proper name insofar as he proceeds from the Father and the Son. Therefore Gift too is a proper name of the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0036","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0037","text":"I answer that it must be said that Gift, insofar as it is taken personally in divine matters, is a proper name of the Holy Spirit. For the clarification of this, it should be known that a gift properly is an irrecompensable giving, according to the Philosopher; that is, what is not given with the intention of recompense, and thus it implies a gratuitous bestowal. Now the reason for gratuitous bestowal is love; for we give something to someone freely because we will good to that person. Therefore, the first thing that we give to that person is the love by which we will good to that person. Hence it is manifest that love has the character of the first gift, through which all gratuitous gifts are given. Hence, since the Holy Spirit proceeds as love, as has already been said, he proceeds under the aspect of the first gift. Hence Augustine says, in XV *On the Trinity*, that *through the gift which is the Holy Spirit, many proper gifts are distributed to the members of Christ*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0038","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0039","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, just as the Son, because he proceeds in the mode of a word, which by its very nature is a likeness of its principle, is properly called Image, although the Holy Spirit too is like the Father; so also the Holy Spirit, because he proceeds from the Father as love, is properly called Gift, although the Son too is given. For the very fact that the Son is given is from the Father's love, according to that of John III, *God so loved the world that he gave his only-begotten Son*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0040","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0041","text":"To the second, it must be said that in the name gift it is implied that it is of the giver by origin. And thus the property of origin of the Holy Spirit is implied, which is procession.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0042","text":"[I q. 38 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0043","text":"To the third, it must be said that a gift, before it is given, belongs only to the giver; but after it is given, it belongs to the one to whom it is given. Therefore, since gift does not imply giving in act, it cannot be said to be a man's gift, but the gift of God who gives. But when it has already been given, then it is a man's either as spirit or as something given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_30:P0043"]}],"source_digest":"1dc3bcac8a774afcd51f03b12aeb967a0970898ed5b06d11e31dc46e385fb52e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 38"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 38","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31","title":"First Part · Question 39","order":40,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_31","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 39 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29984)\n\nPost ea quae de personis divinis absolute tractata sunt, considerandum restat de personis in comparatione ad essentiam, et ad proprietates, et ad actus notionales; et de comparatione ipsarum ad invicem. Quantum igitur ad primum horum, octo quaeruntur. Primo, utrum essentia in divinis sit idem quod persona. Secundo, utrum dicendum sit quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae. Tertio, utrum nomina essentialia praedicanda sint de personis in plurali vel in singulari. Quarto, utrum adiectiva notionalia, aut verba vel participia, praedicari possint de nominibus essentialibus concretive acceptis. Quinto, utrum praedicari possint de nominibus essentialibus in abstracto acceptis. Sexto, utrum nomina personarum praedicari possint de nominibus essentialibus concretis. Septimo, utrum essentialia attributa sint approprianda personis. Octavo, quod attributum cuique personae debeat appropriari.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29985)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in divinis essentia non sit idem quod persona. In quibuscumque enim essentia est idem quod persona seu suppositum, oportet quod sit tantum unum suppositum unius naturae, ut patet in omnibus substantiis separatis, eorum enim quae sunt idem re, unum multiplicari non potest, quin multiplicetur et reliquum. Sed in divinis est una essentia et tres personae, ut ex supra dictis patet. Ergo essentia non est idem quod persona.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29986)\n\nPraeterea, affirmatio et negatio simul et semel non verificantur de eodem. Sed affirmatio et negatio verificantur de essentia et persona, nam persona est distincta, essentia vero non est distincta. Ergo persona et essentia non sunt idem.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29987)\n\nPraeterea, nihil subiicitur sibi ipsi. Sed persona subiicitur essentiae, unde suppositum vel hypostasis nominatur. Ergo persona non est idem quod essentia.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29988)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VII de Trin., *cum dicimus personam patris, non aliud dicimus quam substantiam patris*.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29989)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod considerantibus divinam simplicitatem, quaestio ista in manifesto habet veritatem. Ostensum est enim supra quod divina simplicitas hoc requirit, quod in Deo sit idem essentia et suppositum; quod in substantiis intellectualibus nihil est aliud quam persona. Sed difficultatem videtur ingerere quod, multiplicatis personis divinis, essentia retinet unitatem. Et quia, ut Boetius dicit, relatio multiplicat personarum Trinitatem, posuerunt aliqui hoc modo in divinis differre essentiam et personam, quo et relationes dicebant esse assistentes, considerantes in relationibus solum quod ad alterum sunt, et non quod res sunt. Sed, sicut supra ostensum est, sicut relationes in rebus creatis accidentaliter insunt, ita in Deo sunt ipsa essentia divina. Ex quo sequitur quod in Deo non sit aliud essentia quam persona secundum rem; et tamen quod personae realiter ab invicem distinguantur. Persona enim, ut dictum est supra, significat relationem, prout est subsistens in natura divina. Relatio autem, ad essentiam comparata, non differt re, sed ratione tantum, comparata autem ad oppositam relationem, habet, virtute oppositionis, realem distinctionem. Et sic remanet una essentia, et tres personae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29990)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in creaturis non potest esse distinctio suppositorum per relationes, sed oportet quod sit per essentialia principia, quia relationes non sunt subsistentes in creaturis. In divinis autem relationes sunt subsistentes, et ideo, secundum quod habent oppositionem ad invicem, possunt distinguere supposita. Neque tamen distinguitur essentia, quia relationes ipsae non distinguuntur ab invicem secundum quod sunt realiter idem cum essentia.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29991)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, inquantum essentia et persona in divinis differunt secundum intelligentiae rationem, sequitur quod aliquid possit affirmari de uno, quod negatur de altero, et per consequens quod, supposito uno, non supponatur alterum.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29992)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod rebus divinis nomina imponimus secundum modum rerum creatarum, ut supra dictum est. Et quia naturae rerum creatarum individuantur per materiam, quae subiicitur naturae speciei, inde est quod individua dicuntur subiecta, vel supposita, vel hypostases. Et propter hoc etiam divinae personae supposita vel hypostases nominantur, non quod ibi sit aliqua suppositio vel subiectio secundum rem.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29993)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit dicendum tres personas esse unius essentiae. Dicit enim Hilarius, in libro de Synod., quod pater et filius et spiritus sanctus *sunt quidem per substantiam tria, per consonantiam vero unum*. Sed substantia Dei est eius essentia. Ergo tres personae non sunt unius essentiae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29994)\n\nPraeterea, non est affirmandum aliquid de divinis, quod auctoritate Scripturae sacrae non est expressum, ut patet per Dionysium, I cap. de Div. Nom. Sed nunquam in Scriptura sacra exprimitur quod pater et filius et spiritus sanctus sunt unius essentiae. Ergo hoc non est asserendum.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29995)\n\nPraeterea, natura divina est idem quod essentia. Sufficeret ergo dicere quod tres personae sunt unius naturae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29996)\n\nPraeterea, non consuevit dici quod persona sit essentiae, sed magis quod essentia sit personae. Ergo neque convenienter videtur dici quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29997)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit quod non dicimus tres personas esse ex una essentia, ne intelligatur in divinis aliud esse essentia et persona. Sed sicut praepositiones sunt transitivae, ita et obliqui. Ergo, pari ratione, non est dicendum quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29998)\n\nPraeterea, id quod potest esse erroris occasio, non est in divinis dicendum. Sed cum dicuntur tres personae unius essentiae vel substantiae datur erroris occasio. Quia, ut Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., *una substantia patris et filii praedicata, aut unum qui duas nuncupationes habeat, subsistentem significat; aut divisam unam substantiam duas imperfectas fecisse substantias; aut tertiam priorem substantiam, quae a duobus et usurpata sit et assumpta*. Non est ergo dicendum tres personas esse unius substantiae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29999)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro II contra Maximinum, quod hoc nomen homousion, quod in Concilio Nicaeno adversus Arianos firmatum est, idem significat quod tres personas esse unius essentiae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30000)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, intellectus noster res divinas nominat, non secundum modum earum, quia sic eas cognoscere non potest; sed secundum modum in rebus creatis inventum. Et quia in rebus sensibilibus, a quibus intellectus noster scientiam capit, natura alicuius speciei per materiam individuatur; et sic natura se habet ut forma, individuum autem ut suppositum formae, propter hoc etiam in divinis, quantum ad modum significandi, essentia significatur ut forma trium personarum. Dicimus autem in rebus creatis formam quamcumque esse eius cuius est forma; sicut sanitatem vel pulchritudinem hominis alicuius. Rem autem habentem formam non dicimus esse formae, nisi cum adiectione alicuius adiectivi, quod designat illam formam, ut cum dicimus, ista mulier est egregiae formae, iste homo est perfectae virtutis. Et similiter, quia in divinis, multiplicatis personis, non multiplicatur essentia, dicimus unam essentiam esse trium personarum; et tres personas unius essentiae, ut intelligantur isti genitivi construi in designatione formae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30001)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod substantia sumitur pro hypostasi; et non pro essentia.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30002)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet tres personas esse unius essentiae non inveniatur in sacra Scriptura per haec verba, invenitur tamen quantum ad hunc sensum, sicut ibi, *ego et pater unum sumus; et, ego in patre, et pater in me est*. Et per multa alia haberi potest idem.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30003)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quia natura designat principium actus, essentia vero ab essendo dicitur, possunt dici aliqua unius naturae, quae conveniunt in aliquo actu, sicut omnia calefacientia, sed unius essentiae dici non possunt, nisi quorum est unum esse. Et ideo magis exprimitur unitas divina per hoc quod dicitur quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae, quam si diceretur quod sunt unius naturae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30004)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod forma, absolute accepta, consuevit significari ut eius cuius est forma, ut virtus Petri. E converso autem, res habens formam aliquam non consuevit significari ut eius, nisi cum volumus determinare sive designare formam. Et tunc requiruntur duo genitivi, quorum unus significet formam, et alius determinationem formae, ut si dicatur, Petrus est magnae virtutis, vel etiam requiritur unus genitivus habens vim duorum genitivorum, ut cum dicitur, vir sanguinum est iste, idest effusor multi sanguinis. Quia igitur essentia divina significatur ut forma respectu personae, convenienter essentia personae dicitur. Non autem e converso, nisi aliquid addatur ad designationem essentiae; ut si dicatur quod pater est persona divinae essentiae, vel quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30005)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod haec praepositio ex vel de non designat habitudinem causae formalis, sed magis habitudinem causae efficientis vel materialis. Quae quidem causae in omnibus distinguuntur ab his quorum sunt causae, nihil enim est sua materia, neque aliquid est suum principium activum. Aliquid tamen est sua forma, ut patet in omnibus rebus immaterialibus. Et ideo per hoc quod dicimus tres personas unius essentiae, significando essentiam in habitudine formae, non ostenditur aliud esse essentia quam persona, quod ostenderetur, si diceremus tres personas ex eadem essentia.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30006)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod, sicut Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., *male sanctis rebus praeiudicatur, si, quia non sanctae a quibusdam habentur, esse non debeant. Sic, si male intelligitur homousion, quid ad me bene intelligentem? Sit ergo una substantia ex naturae genitae proprietate, non sit autem ex portione, aut ex unione, aut ex communione*.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30007)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina essentialia, ut hoc nomen Deus, non praedicentur singulariter de tribus personis, sed pluraliter. Sicut enim homo significatur ut habens humanitatem, ita Deus significatur ut habens deitatem. Sed tres personae sunt tres habentes deitatem. Ergo tres personae sunt tres dii.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30008)\n\nPraeterea, Gen. I, ubi dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*, Hebraica veritas habet Elohim, quod potest interpretari dii, sive iudices. Et hoc dicitur propter pluralitatem personarum. Ergo tres personae sunt plures dii, et non unus Deus.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30009)\n\nPraeterea, hoc nomen res, cum absolute dicatur, videtur ad substantiam pertinere. Sed hoc nomen pluraliter praedicatur de tribus personis, dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de Doctr. Christ., *res quibus fruendum est, sunt pater et filius et spiritus sanctus*. Ergo et alia nomina essentialia pluraliter praedicari possunt de tribus personis.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30010)\n\nPraeterea, sicut hoc nomen Deus significat habentem deitatem, ita hoc nomen persona significat subsistentem in natura aliqua intellectuali. Sed dicimus tres personas. Ergo, eadem ratione, dicere possumus tres deos.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30011)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Deut. VI, *audi, Israel, dominus Deus tuus, Deus unus est*.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30012)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nominum essentialium quaedam significant essentiam substantive, quaedam vero adiective. Ea quidem quae substantive essentiam significant, praedicantur de tribus personis singulariter tantum, et non pluraliter, quae vero adiective essentiam significant, praedicantur de tribus personis in plurali. Cuius ratio est, quia nomina substantiva significant aliquid per modum substantiae, nomina vero adiectiva significant aliquid per modum accidentis, quod inhaeret subiecto. Substantia autem, sicut per se habet esse, ita per se habet unitatem vel multitudinem, unde et singularitas vel pluralitas nominis substantivi attenditur secundum formam significatam per nomen. Accidentia autem, sicut esse habent in subiecto, ita ex subiecto suscipiunt unitatem et multitudinem, et ideo in adiectivis attenditur singularitas et pluralitas secundum supposita. In creaturis autem non invenitur una forma in pluribus suppositis nisi unitate ordinis, ut forma multitudinis ordinatae. Unde nomina significantia talem formam, si sint substantiva, praedicantur de pluribus in singulari, non autem si sint adiectiva. Dicimus enim quod multi homines sunt collegium vel exercitus aut populus, dicimus tamen quod plures homines sunt collegiati. In divinis autem essentia divina significatur per modum formae, ut dictum est quae quidem simplex est et maxime una, ut supra ostensum est. Unde nomina significantia divinam essentiam substantive, singulariter, et non pluraliter, de tribus personis praedicantur. Haec igitur est ratio quare Socratem et Platonem et Ciceronem dicimus tres homines; patrem autem et filium et spiritum sanctum non dicimus tres deos, sed unum Deum, quia in tribus suppositis humanae naturae sunt tres humanitates; in tribus autem personis est una divina essentia. Ea vero quae significant essentiam adiective, praedicantur pluraliter de tribus, propter pluralitatem suppositorum. Dicimus enim tres existentes vel tres sapientes, aut tres aeternos et increatos et immensos, si adiective sumantur. Si vero substantive sumantur, dicimus unum increatum, immensum et aeternum, ut Athanasius dicit.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30013)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet Deus significet habentem deitatem, est tamen alius modus significandi, nam Deus dicitur substantive, sed habens deitatem dicitur adiective. Unde, licet sint tres habentes deitatem, non tamen sequitur quod sint tres dii.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30014)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod diversae linguae habent diversum modum loquendi. Unde, sicut propter pluralitatem suppositorum Graeci dicunt tres hypostases, ita et in Hebraeo dicitur pluraliter Elohim. Nos autem non dicimus pluraliter neque deos neque substantias, ne pluralitas ad substantiam referatur.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30015)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod hoc nomen res est de transcendentibus. Unde, secundum quod pertinet ad relationem, pluraliter praedicatur in divinis, secundum vero quod pertinet ad substantiam, singulariter praedicatur. Unde Augustinus dicit ibidem quod *eadem Trinitas quaedam summa res est*.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30016)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod forma significata per hoc nomen persona, non est essentia vel natura, sed personalitas. Unde, cum sint tres personalitates, idest tres personales proprietates, in patre et filio et spiritu sancto, non singulariter, sed pluraliter praedicatur de tribus.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30017)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina essentialia concretiva non possunt supponere pro persona, ita quod haec sit vera, Deus genuit Deum. Quia, ut sophistae dicunt, terminus singularis idem significat et supponit. Sed hoc nomen Deus videtur esse terminus singularis, cum pluraliter praedicari non possit, ut dictum est. Ergo, cum significet essentiam, videtur quod supponat pro essentia, et non pro persona.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30018)\n\nPraeterea, terminus in subiecto positus non restringitur per terminum positum in praedicato, ratione significationis; sed solum ratione temporis consignificati. Sed cum dico, Deus creat, hoc nomen Deus supponit pro essentia. Ergo cum dicitur, Deus genuit, non potest iste terminus Deus, ratione praedicati notionalis, supponere pro persona.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30019)\n\nPraeterea, si haec est vera, Deus genuit, quia pater generat; pari ratione haec erit vera, Deus non generat, quia filius non generat. Ergo est Deus generans, et Deus non generans. Et ita videtur sequi quod sint duo dii.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30020)\n\nPraeterea, si Deus genuit Deum, aut se Deum, aut alium Deum. Sed non se Deum, quia, ut Augustinus dicit, in I de Trin., *nulla res generat seipsam*. Neque alium Deum, quia non est nisi unus Deus. Ergo haec est falsa, Deus genuit Deum.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30021)\n\nPraeterea, si Deus genuit Deum, aut Deum qui est Deus pater, aut Deum qui non est Deus pater. Si Deum qui est Deus pater, ergo Deus pater est genitus. Si Deum qui non est Deus pater, ergo Deus est qui non est Deus pater, quod est falsum. Non ergo potest dici quod Deus genuit Deum.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30022)\n\nSed contra est quod in symbolo dicitur Deum de Deo.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30023)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod hoc nomen Deus, et similia, proprie secundum suam naturam supponunt pro essentia, sed ex adiuncto notionali trahuntur ad supponendum pro persona. Et haec opinio processisse videtur ex consideratione divinae simplicitatis, quae requirit quod in Deo idem sit habens et quod habetur, et sic habens deitatem, quod significat hoc nomen Deus, est idem quod deitas. Sed in proprietatibus locutionum, non tantum attendenda est res significata; sed etiam modus significandi. Et ideo, quia hoc nomen Deus significat divinam essentiam ut in habente ipsam, sicut hoc nomen homo humanitatem significat in supposito; alii melius dixerunt quod hoc nomen Deus ex modo significandi habet ut proprie possit supponere pro persona, sicut et hoc nomen homo. Quandoque ergo hoc nomen Deus supponit pro essentia, ut cum dicitur, Deus creat, quia hoc praedicatum competit subiecto ratione formae significatae, quae est deitas. Quandoque vero supponit personam, vel unam tantum, ut cum dicitur, Deus generat; vel duas, ut cum dicitur Deus spirat; vel tres, ut cum dicitur, *regi saeculorum immortali, invisibili, soli Deo* etc., I Tim. I.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30024)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc nomen Deus, licet conveniat cum terminis singularibus in hoc, quod forma significata non multiplicatur; convenit tamen cum terminis communibus in hoc, quod forma significata invenitur in pluribus suppositis. Unde non oportet quod semper supponat pro essentia quam significat.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30025)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit contra illos qui dicebant quod hoc nomen Deus non habet naturalem suppositionem pro persona.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30026)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aliter se habet hoc nomen Deus ad supponendum pro persona, et hoc nomen homo. Quia enim forma significata per hoc nomen homo, idest humanitas, realiter dividitur in diversis suppositis, per se supponit pro persona; etiamsi nihil addatur quod determinet ipsum ad personam, quae est suppositum distinctum. Unitas autem sive communitas humanae naturae non est secundum rem, sed solum secundum considerationem, unde iste terminus homo non supponit pro natura communi, nisi propter exigentiam alicuius additi, ut cum dicitur, homo est species. Sed forma significata per hoc nomen Deus, scilicet essentia divina, est una et communis secundum rem. Unde per se supponit pro natura communi, sed ex adiuncto determinatur eius suppositio ad personam. Unde cum dicitur, Deus generat, ratione actus notionalis supponit hoc nomen Deus pro persona patris. Sed cum dicitur, Deus non generat, nihil additur quod determinet hoc nomen ad personam filii, unde datur intelligi quod generatio repugnet divinae naturae. Sed si addatur aliquid pertinens ad personam filii, vera erit locutio; ut si dicatur, Deus genitus non generat. Unde etiam non sequitur, est Deus generans et est Deus non generans, nisi ponatur aliquid pertinens ad personas; ut puta si dicamus, pater est Deus generans, et filius est Deus non generans. Et ita non sequitur quod sint plures dii, quia pater et filius sunt unus Deus, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30027)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod haec est falsa, pater genuit se Deum, quia ly se, cum sit reciprocum, refert idem suppositum. Neque est contrarium quod Augustinus dicit, ad maximum, quod Deus pater genuit alterum se. Quia ly se vel est casus ablativi; ut sit sensus, genuit alterum a se. Vel facit relationem simplicem, et sic refert identitatem naturae, sed est impropria vel emphatica locutio, ut sit sensus, genuit alterum simillimum sibi. Similiter et haec est falsa, genuit alium Deum. Quia licet filius sit alius a patre, ut supra dictum est, non tamen est dicendum quod sit alius Deus, quia intelligeretur quod hoc adiectivum alius poneret rem suam circa substantivum quod est Deus; et sic significaretur distinctio deitatis. Quidam tamen concedunt istam, genuit alium Deum, ita quod ly alius sit substantivum, et ly Deus appositive construatur cum eo. Sed hic est improprius modus loquendi, et evitandus, ne detur occasio erroris.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30028)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod haec est falsa, Deus genuit Deum qui est Deus pater, quia, cum ly pater appositive construatur cum ly Deus, restringit ipsum ad standum pro persona patris; ut sit sensus, genuit Deum qui est ipse pater, et sic pater esset genitus, quod est falsum. Unde negativa est vera, genuit Deum qui non est Deus pater. Si tamen intelligeretur constructio non esse appositiva, sed aliquid esse interponendum; tunc e converso affirmativa esset vera, et negativa falsa; ut sit sensus, genuit Deum qui est Deus qui est pater. Sed haec est extorta expositio. Unde melius est quod simpliciter affirmativa negetur, et negativa concedatur. Praepositivus tamen dixit quod tam negativa quam affirmativa est falsa. Quia hoc relativum qui in affirmativa potest referre suppositum, sed in negativa refert et significatum et suppositum. Unde sensus affirmativae est, quod esse Deum patrem conveniat personae filii. Negativae vero sensus est, quod esse Deum patrem non tantum removeatur a persona filii, sed etiam a divinitate eius sed hoc irrationabile videtur, cum, secundum philosophum, de eodem de quo est affirmatio, possit etiam esse negatio.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30029)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina essentialia in abstracto significata possint supponere pro persona, ita quod haec sit vera, essentia generat essentiam. Dicit enim Augustinus, VII de Trin., *pater et filius sunt una sapientia, quia una essentia; et singillatim sapientia de sapientia, sicut essentia de essentia*.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30030)\n\nPraeterea, generatis nobis vel corruptis, generantur vel corrumpuntur ea quae in nobis sunt. Sed filius generatur. Ergo, cum essentia divina sit in filio, videtur quod essentia divina generetur.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30031)\n\nPraeterea, idem est Deus et essentia divina, ut ex supra dictis patet. Sed haec est vera, Deus generat Deum, sicut dictum est. Ergo haec est vera, essentia generat essentiam.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30032)\n\nPraeterea, de quocumque praedicatur aliquid, potest supponere pro illo. Sed essentia divina est pater. Ergo essentia potest supponere pro persona patris. Et sic essentia generat.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30033)\n\nPraeterea, essentia est res generans, quia est pater, qui est generans. Si igitur essentia non sit generans, erit essentia res generans et non generans, quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30034)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in IV de Trin., *pater est principium totius deitatis*. Sed non est principium nisi generando vel spirando. Ergo pater generat vel spirat deitatem.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30035)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in I de Trin., quod *nulla res generat seipsam*. Sed si essentia generat essentiam, non generat nisi seipsam, cum nihil sit in Deo, quod distinguatur a divina essentia. Ergo essentia non generat essentiam.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30036)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc erravit abbas Ioachim, asserens quod, sicut dicitur, Deus genuit Deum, ita potest dici quod essentia genuit essentiam; considerans quod, propter divinam simplicitatem, non est aliud Deus quam divina essentia. Sed in hoc deceptus fuit, quia ad veritatem locutionum, non solum oportet considerare res significatas, sed etiam modum significandi ut dictum est. Licet autem, secundum re, sit idem Deus quod deitas, non tamen est idem modus significandi utrobique. Nam hoc nomen Deus, quia significat divinam essentiam ut in habente, ex modo suae significationis naturaliter habet quod possit supponere pro persona, et sic ea quae sunt propria personarum, possunt praedicari de hoc nomine Deus, ut dicatur quod Deus est genitus vel generans, sicut dictum est. Sed hoc nomen essentia non habet ex modo suae significationis quod supponat pro persona, quia significat essentiam ut formam abstractam. Et ideo ea quae sunt propria personarum, quibus ab invicem distinguuntur, non possunt essentiae attribui, significaretur enim quod esset distinctio in essentia divina, sicut est distinctio in suppositis.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30037)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, ad exprimendam unitatem essentiae et personae, sancti doctores aliquando expressius locuti sunt quam proprietas locutionis patiatur. Unde huiusmodi locutiones non sunt extendendae, sed exponendae, ut scilicet nomina abstracta exponantur per concreta, vel etiam per nomina personalia, ut, cum dicitur, essentia de essentia, vel sapientia de sapientia, sit sensus, filius, qui est essentia et sapientia, est de patre, qui est essentia et sapientia. In his tamen nominibus abstractis est quidam ordo attendendus, quia ea quae pertinent ad actum, magis propinque se habent ad personas, quia actus sunt suppositorum. Unde minus impropria est ista, natura de natura, vel sapientia de sapientia, quam essentia de essentia.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30038)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in creaturis generatum non accipit naturam eandem numero quam generans habet, sed aliam numero, quae incipit in eo esse per generationem de novo, et desinit esse per corruptionem, et ideo generatur et corrumpitur per accidens. Sed Deus genitus eandem naturam numero accipit quam generans habet. Et ideo natura divina in filio non generatur, neque per se neque per accidens.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30039)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet Deus et divina essentia sint idem secundum rem, tamen, ratione alterius modi significandi, oportet loqui diversimode de utroque.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30040)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod essentia divina praedicatur de patre per modum identitatis, propter divinam simplicitatem, nec tamen sequitur quod possit supponere pro patre, propter diversum modum significandi. Ratio autem procederet in illis, quorum unum praedicatur de altero sicut universale de particulari.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30041)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod haec est differentia inter nomina substantiva et adiectiva, quia nomina substantiva ferunt suum suppositum, adiectiva vero non, sed rem significatam ponunt circa substantivum. Unde sophistae dicunt quod nomina substantiva supponunt; adiectiva vero non supponunt, sed copulant. Nomina igitur personalia substantiva possunt de essentia praedicari, propter identitatem rei, neque sequitur quod proprietas personalis distinctam determinet essentiam; sed ponitur circa suppositum importatum per nomen substantivum. Sed notionalia et personalia adiectiva non possunt praedicari de essentia, nisi aliquo substantivo adiuncto. Unde non possumus dicere quod essentia est generans. Possumus tamen dicere quod essentia est res generans, vel Deus generans, si res et Deus supponant pro persona, non autem si supponant pro essentia. Unde non est contradictio, si dicatur quod essentia est res generans, et res non generans, quia primo res tenetur pro persona, secundo pro essentia.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30042)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod deitas, inquantum est una in pluribus suppositis, habet quandam convenientiam cum forma nominis collectivi. Unde cum dicitur, pater est principium totius deitatis, potest sumi pro universitate personarum; inquantum scilicet, in omnibus personis divinis, ipse est principium. Nec oportet quod sit principium sui ipsius, sicut aliquis de populo dicitur rector totius populi, non tamen sui ipsius. Vel potest dici quod est principium totius deitatis, non quia eam generet et spiret, sed quia eam, generando et spirando, communicat.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 39 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30043)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod personae non possint praedicari de nominibus essentialibus concretis, ut dicatur, Deus est tres personae, vel est Trinitas. Haec enim est falsa, homo est omnis homo, quia pro nullo suppositorum verificari potest, neque enim Socrates est omnis homo, neque Plato, neque aliquis alius. Sed similiter ista, Deus est Trinitas, pro nullo suppositorum naturae divinae verificari potest, neque enim pater est Trinitas, neque filius, neque spiritus sanctus. Ergo haec est falsa, Deus est Trinitas.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30044)\n\nPraeterea, inferiora non praedicantur de suis superioribus nisi accidentali praedicatione, ut cum dico, animal est homo, accidit enim animali esse hominem. Sed hoc nomen Deus se habet ad tres personas sicut commune ad inferiora, ut Damascenus dicit. Ergo videtur quod nomina personarum non possint praedicari de hoc nomine Deus, nisi accidentaliter.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30045)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in sermone de fide, *credimus unum Deum unam esse divini nominis Trinitatem*.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30046)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, licet nomina personalia vel notionalia adiectiva non possint praedicari de essentia; tamen substantiva possunt, propter realem identitatem essentiae et personae. Essentia autem divina non solum idem est realiter cum una persona, sed cum tribus. Unde et una persona, et duae, et tres possunt de essentia praedicari; ut si dicamus, essentia est pater et filius et spiritus sanctus. Et quia hoc nomen Deus per se habet quod supponat pro essentia, ut dictum est, ideo, sicut haec est vera, essentia est tres personae, ita haec est vera, Deus est tres personae.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30047)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, hoc nomen homo per se habet supponere pro persona; sed ex adiuncto habet quod stet pro natura communi. Et ideo haec est falsa, homo est omnis homo, quia pro nullo supposito verificari potest. Sed hoc nomen Deus per se habet quod stet pro essentia. Unde, licet pro nullo suppositorum divinae naturae haec sit vera, Deus est Trinitas, est tamen vera pro essentia. Quod non attendens, Porretanus eam negavit.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30048)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur, Deus vel divina essentia est pater, est praedicatio per identitatem, non autem sicut inferioris de superiori, quia in divinis non est universale et singulare. Unde, sicut est per se ista, pater est Deus, ita et ista, Deus est pater; et nullo modo per accidens.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30049)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina essentialia non sint approprianda personis. Quod enim potest vergere in errorem fidei, vitandum est in divinis, quia, ut Hieronymus dicit, *ex verbis inordinate prolatis incurritur haeresis*. Sed ea quae sunt communia tribus personis appropriare alicui, potest vergere in errorem fidei, quia potest intelligi quod vel illi tantum personae conveniant cui appropriantur; vel quod magis conveniant ei quam aliis. Ergo essentialia attributa non sunt approprianda personis.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30050)\n\nPraeterea, essentialia attributa, in abstracto significata, significant per modum formae. Sed una persona non se habet ad aliam ut forma, cum forma ab eo cuius est forma, supposito non distinguatur. Ergo essentialia attributa, maxime in abstracto significata, non debent appropriari personis.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30051)\n\nPraeterea, proprium prius est appropriato, proprium enim est de ratione appropriati. Sed essentialia attributa, secundum modum intelligendi, sunt priora personis, sicut commune est prius proprio. Ergo essentialia attributa non debent esse appropriata.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30052)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit, I Cor. I, *Christum, Dei virtutem et Dei sapientiam*.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30053)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, ad manifestationem fidei, conveniens fuit essentialia attributa personis appropriari. Licet enim Trinitas personarum demonstratione probari non possit, ut supra dictum est, convenit tamen ut per aliqua magis manifesta declaretur. Essentialia vero attributa sunt nobis magis manifesta secundum rationem, quam propria personarum, quia ex creaturis, ex quibus cognitionem accipimus, possumus per certitudinem devenire in cognitionem essentialium attributorum; non autem in cognitionem personalium proprietatum, ut supra dictum est. Sicut igitur similitudine vestigii vel imaginis in creaturis inventa utimur ad manifestationem divinarum personarum, ita et essentialibus attributis. Et haec manifestatio personarum per essentialia attributa, appropriatio nominatur. Possunt autem manifestari personae divinae per essentialia attributa dupliciter. Uno modo, per viam similitudinis, sicut ea quae pertinent ad intellectum, appropriantur filio, qui procedit per modum intellectus ut verbum. Alio modo, per modum dissimilitudinis, sicut potentia appropriatur patri, ut Augustinus dicit, quia apud nos patres solent esse propter senectutem infirmi; ne tale aliquid suspicemur in Deo.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30054)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod essentialia attributa non sic appropriantur personis ut eis esse propria asserantur, sed ad manifestandum personas per viam similitudinis vel dissimilitudinis, ut dictum est. Unde nullus error fidei sequitur, sed magis manifestatio veritatis.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30055)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, si sic appropriarentur essentialia attributa personis, quod essent eis propria, sequeretur quod una persona se haberet ad aliam in habitudine formae. Quod excludit Augustinus, in VII de Trin., ostendens quod pater non est sapiens sapientia quam genuit, quasi solus filius sit sapientia; ut sic pater et filius simul tantum possint dici sapiens, non autem pater sine filio. Sed filius dicitur sapientia patris, quia est sapientia de patre sapientia, uterque enim per se est sapientia, et simul ambo una sapientia. Unde pater non est sapiens sapientia quam genuit, sed sapientia quae est sua essentia.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30056)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet essentiale attributum, secundum rationem propriam, sit prius quam persona, secundum, modum intelligendi; tamen, inquantum habet rationem appropriati, nihil prohibet proprium personae esse prius quam appropriatum. Sicut color posterior est corpore, inquantum est corpus, prius tamen est naturaliter corpore albo, inquantum est album.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30057)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter a sacris doctoribus sint essentialia personis attributa. Dicit enim Hilarius, in II de Trin., *aeternitas est in patre, species in imagine, usus in munere*. In quibus verbis ponit tria nomina propria personarum, scilicet nomen patris; et nomen imaginis, quod est proprium filio, ut supra dictum est; et nomen muneris, sive doni, quod est proprium spiritus sancti, ut supra habitum est. Ponit etiam tria appropriata, nam aeternitatem appropriat patri, speciem filio, usum spiritui sancto. Et videtur quod irrationabiliter. Nam aeternitas importat durationem essendi, species vero est essendi principium, usus vero ad operationem pertinere videtur. Sed essentia et operatio nulli personae appropriari inveniuntur. Ergo inconvenienter videntur ista appropriata personis.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30058)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus in I de Doctr. Christ., sic dicit, *in patre est unitas, in filio aequalitas, in spiritu sancto unitatis aequalitatisque concordia*. Et videtur quod inconvenienter. Quia una persona non denominatur formaliter per id quod appropriatur alteri, non enim est sapiens pater sapientia genita, ut dictum est. Sed, sicut ibidem subditur, *tria haec unum omnia sunt propter patrem, aequalia omnia propter filium, connexa omnia propter spiritum sanctum*. Non ergo convenienter appropriantur personis.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30059)\n\nItem, secundum Augustinum, patri attribuitur potentia, filio sapientia, spiritui sancto bonitas. Et videtur hoc esse inconveniens. Nam virtus ad potentiam pertinet. Virtus autem invenitur appropriari filio, secundum illud I ad Cor. I, *Christum, Dei virtutem*; et etiam spiritui sancto, secundum illud Luc. VI, *virtus de illo exibat, et sanabat omnes*. Non ergo potentia patri est approprianda.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30060)\n\nItem, Augustinus, in libro de Trin., dicit, *non confuse accipiendum est quod ait apostolus, ex ipso, et per ipsum, et in ipso - ex ipso dicens propter patrem; per ipsum propter filium; in ipso propter spiritum sanctum*. Sed videtur quod inconvenienter. Quia per hoc quod dicit in ipso, videtur importari habitudo causae finalis, quae est prima causarum. Ergo ista habitudo causae deberet appropriari patri, qui est principium non de principio.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30061)\n\nItem, invenitur veritas appropriari filio, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *ego sum via, veritas et vita*. Et similiter liber vitae, secundum illud Psalmi XXXIX, *in capite libri scriptum est de me*, Glossa, idest apud patrem, qui est caput meum. Et similiter hoc quod dico, qui est, quia super illud Isa. LXV, *ecce ego, ad gentes*, dicit Glossa, *filius loquitur, qui dixit Moysi, ego sum qui sum*. Sed videtur quod propria sint filii, et non appropriata. *Nam veritas*, secundum Augustinum, in libro de vera religione, *est summa similitudo principii, absque omni dissimilitudine*, et sic videtur quod proprie conveniat filio, qui habet principium. Liber etiam vitae videtur proprium aliquid esse, quia significat ens ab alio, omnis enim liber ab aliquo scribitur. Hoc etiam ipsum qui est videtur esse proprium filio. Quia si, cum Moysi dicitur, ego sum qui sum, loquitur Trinitas, ergo Moyses poterat dicere, ille qui est pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, misit me ad vos. Ergo et ulterius dicere poterat, ille qui est pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, misit me ad vos, demonstrando certam personam. Hoc autem est falsum, quia nulla persona est pater et filius et spiritus sanctus. Non ergo potest esse commune Trinitati, sed est proprium filii.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30062)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod intellectus noster, qui ex creaturis in Dei cognitionem manuducitur, oportet quod Deum consideret secundum modum quem ex creaturis assumit. In consideratione autem alicuius creaturae, quatuor per ordinem nobis occurrunt. Nam primo, consideratur res ipsa absolute, inquantum est ens quoddam. Secunda autem consideratio rei est, inquantum est una. Tertia consideratio rei est, secundum quod inest ei virtus ad operandum et ad causandum. Quarta autem consideratio rei est, secundum habitudinem quam habet ad causata. Unde haec etiam quadruplex consideratio circa Deum nobis occurrit. Secundum igitur primam considerationem, qua consideratur absolute Deus secundum esse suum, sic sumitur appropriatio Hilarii, secundum quam aeternitas appropriatur patri, species filio, usus spiritui sancto. Aeternitas enim, inquantum significat esse non principiatum, similitudinem habet cum proprio patris, qui est principium non de principio. Species autem, sive pulchritudo, habet similitudinem cum propriis filii. Nam ad pulchritudinem tria requiruntur. Primo quidem, integritas sive perfectio, quae enim diminuta sunt, hoc ipso turpia sunt. Et debita proportio sive consonantia. Et iterum claritas, unde quae habent colorem nitidum, pulchra esse dicuntur. Quantum igitur ad primum, similitudinem habet cum proprio filii, inquantum est filius habens in se vere et perfecte naturam patris. Unde, ad hoc innuendum, Augustinus in sua expositione dicit, ubi, scilicet in filio, summa et prima vita est, et cetera. Quantum vero ad secundum, convenit cum proprio filii, inquantum est imago expressa patris. Unde videmus quod aliqua imago dicitur esse pulchra, si perfecte repraesentat rem, quamvis turpem. Et hoc tetigit Augustinus cum dicit, ubi est tanta convenientia, et prima aequalitas, et cetera. Quantum vero ad tertium, convenit cum proprio filii, inquantum est verbum, quod quidem lux est, et splendor intellectus, ut Damascenus dicit. Et hoc tangit Augustinus cum dicit, *tanquam verbum perfectum cui non desit aliquid, et ars quaedam omnipotentis Dei,* et cetera. Usus autem habet similitudinem cum propriis spiritus sancti, largo modo accipiendo usum, secundum quod uti comprehendit sub se etiam frui; prout uti est assumere aliquid in facultatem voluntatis, et frui est cum gaudio uti, ut Augustinus, X de Trin., dicit. Usus ergo quo pater et filius se invicem fruuntur, convenit cum proprio spiritus sancti, inquantum est amor. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit, *illa dilectio, delectatio, felicitas vel beatitudo, usus ab illo appellatus est*. Usus vero quo nos fruimur Deo, similitudinem habet cum proprio spiritus sancti, inquantum est donum. Et hoc ostendit Augustinus cum dicit, *est in Trinitate spiritus sanctus, genitoris genitique suavitas, ingenti largitate atque ubertate nos perfundens*. Et sic patet quare aeternitas, species et usus personis attribuantur vel approprientur, non autem essentia vel operatio. Quia in ratione horum, propter sui communitatem, non invenitur aliquid similitudinem habens cum propriis personarum. Secunda vero consideratio Dei est, inquantum consideratur ut unus. Et sic Augustinus patri appropriat unitatem, filio aequalitatem, spiritui sancto concordiam sive connexionem. Quae quidem tria unitatem importare manifestum est, sed differenter. Nam unitas dicitur absolute, non praesupponens aliquid aliud. Et ideo appropriatur patri, qui non praesupponit aliquam personam, cum sit principium non de principio. Aequalitas autem importat unitatem in respectu ad alterum, nam aequale est quod habet unam quantitatem cum alio. Et ideo aequalitas appropriatur filio, qui est principium de principio. Connexio autem importat unitatem aliquorum duorum. Unde appropriatur spiritui sancto, inquantum est a duobus. Ex quo etiam intelligi potest quod dicit Augustinus, *tria esse unum propter patrem, aequalia propter filium, connexa propter spiritum sanctum*. Manifestum est enim quod illi attribuitur unumquodque, in quo primo invenitur, sicut omnia inferiora dicuntur vivere propter animam vegetabilem, in qua primo invenitur ratio vitae in istis inferioribus. Unitas autem statim invenitur in persona patris, etiam, per impossibile, remotis aliis personis. Et ideo aliae personae a patre habent unitatem. Sed remotis aliis personis, non invenitur aequalitas in patre, sed statim, posito filio, invenitur aequalitas. Et ideo dicuntur omnia aequalia propter filium, non quod filius sit principium aequalitatis patri; sed quia, nisi esset patri aequalis filius, pater aequalis non posset dici. Aequalitas enim eius primo consideratur ad filium, hoc enim ipsum quod spiritus sanctus patri aequalis est, a filio habet. Similiter, excluso spiritu sancto, qui est duorum nexus, non posset intelligi unitas connexionis inter patrem et filium. Et ideo dicuntur omnia esse connexa propter spiritum sanctum, quia, posito spiritu sancto, invenitur unde pater et filius possint dici connexi. Secundum vero tertiam considerationem, qua in Deo sufficiens virtus consideratur ad causandum, sumitur tertia appropriatio, scilicet potentiae, sapientiae et bonitatis. Quae quidem appropriatio fit et secundum rationem similitudinis, si consideretur quod in divinis personis est, et secundum rationem dissimilitudinis, si consideretur quod in creaturis est. Potentia enim habet rationem principii. Unde habet similitudinem cum patre caelesti, qui est principium totius divinitatis. Deficit autem interdum patri terreno, propter senectutem. Sapientia vero similitudinem habet cum filio caelesti, inquantum est verbum, quod nihil aliud est quam conceptus sapientiae. Deficit autem interdum filio terreno, propter temporis paucitatem. Bonitas autem, cum sit ratio et obiectum amoris, habet similitudinem cum spiritu divino, qui est amor. Sed repugnantiam habere videtur ad spiritum terrenum, secundum quod importat violentam quandam impulsionem; prout dicitur Isa. XXV, *spiritus robustorum quasi turbo impellens parietem*. Virtus autem appropriatur filio et spiritui sancto, non secundum quod virtus dicitur ipsa potentia rei, sed secundum quod interdum virtus dicitur id quod a potentia rei procedit, prout dicimus aliquod virtuosum factum esse virtutem alicuius agentis. Secundum vero quartam considerationem, prout consideratur Deus in habitudine ad suos effectus, sumitur illa appropriatio ex quo, per quem, et in quo. Haec enim praepositio ex importat quandoque quidem habitudinem causae materialis, quae locum non habet in divinis, aliquando vero habitudinem causae efficientis. Quae quidem competit Deo ratione suae potentiae activae, unde et appropriatur patri, sicut et potentia. Haec vero praepositio per designat quidem quandoque causam mediam; sicut dicimus quod faber operatur per martellum. Et sic ly per quandoque non est appropriatum, sed proprium filii, secundum illud Ioan. I, *omnia per ipsum facta sunt*; non quia filius sit instrumentum, sed quia ipse est principium de principio. Quandoque vero designat habitudinem formae per quam agens operatur; sicut dicimus quod artifex operatur per artem. Unde, sicut sapientia et ars appropriantur filio, ita et ly per quem. Haec vero praepositio in denotat proprie habitudinem continentis. Continet autem Deus res dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum suas similitudines; prout scilicet res dicuntur esse in Deo, inquantum sunt in eius scientia. Et sic hoc quod dico in ipso, esset appropriandum filio. Alio vero modo continentur res a Deo, inquantum Deus sua bonitate eas conservat et gubernat, ad finem convenientem adducendo. Et sic ly in quo appropriatur spiritui sancto, sicut et bonitas. Nec oportet quod habitudo causae finalis, quamvis sit prima causarum, approprietur patri, qui est principium non de principio, quia personae divinae, quarum pater est principium, non procedunt ut ad finem, cum quaelibet illarum sit ultimus finis; sed naturali processione, quae magis ad rationem naturalis potentiae pertinere videtur.\n\n[I q. 39 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30063)\n\nAd illud vero quod de aliis quaeritur, dicendum quod veritas, cum pertineat ad intellectum, ut supra dictum est, appropriatur filio, non tamen est proprium eius. Quia veritas, ut supra dictum est, considerari potest prout est in intellectu, vel prout est in re. Sicut igitur intellectus et res essentialiter sumpta sunt essentialia et non personalia, ita et veritas. Definitio autem Augustini inducta, datur de veritate secundum quod appropriatur filio. Liber autem vitae in recto quidem importat notitiam, sed in obliquo vitam, est enim, ut supra dictum est, notitia Dei de his qui habituri sunt vitam aeternam. Unde appropriatur filio, licet vita approprietur spiritui sancto, inquantum importat quendam interiorem motum, et sic convenit cum proprio spiritus sancti, inquantum est amor. Esse autem scriptum ab alio, non est de ratione libri inquantum est liber; sed inquantum est quoddam artificiatum. Unde non importat originem, neque est personale, sed appropriatum personae. Ipsum autem qui est appropriatur personae filii, non secundum propriam rationem, sed ratione adiuncti, inquantum scilicet in locutione Dei ad Moysen, praefigurabatur liberatio humani generis, quae facta est per filium. Sed tamen, secundum quod ly qui sumitur relative, posset referre interdum personam filii, et sic sumeretur personaliter, ut puta si dicatur, filius est genitus qui est; sicut et Deus genitus personale est. Sed infinite sumptum est essentiale. Et licet hoc pronomen iste, grammatice loquendo, ad aliquam certam personam videatur pertinere; tamen quaelibet res demonstrabilis, grammatice loquendo, persona dici potest, licet secundum rei naturam non sit persona; dicimus enim iste lapis, et iste asinus. Unde et, grammatice loquendo, essentia divina, secundum quod significatur et supponitur per hoc nomen Deus, potest demonstrari hoc pronomine iste; secundum illud Exod. XV, *iste Deus meus, et glorificabo eum*.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0002","text":"[I q. 39 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0003","text":"Post ea quae de personis divinis absolute tractata sunt, considerandum restat de personis in comparatione ad essentiam, et ad proprietates, et ad actus notionales; et de comparatione ipsarum ad invicem. Quantum igitur ad primum horum, octo quaeruntur. Primo, utrum essentia in divinis sit idem quod persona. Secundo, utrum dicendum sit quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae. Tertio, utrum nomina essentialia praedicanda sint de personis in plurali vel in singulari. Quarto, utrum adiectiva notionalia, aut verba vel participia, praedicari possint de nominibus essentialibus concretive acceptis. Quinto, utrum praedicari possint de nominibus essentialibus in abstracto acceptis. Sexto, utrum nomina personarum praedicari possint de nominibus essentialibus concretis. Septimo, utrum essentialia attributa sint approprianda personis. Octavo, quod attributum cuique personae debeat appropriari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0005","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in divinis essentia non sit idem quod persona. In quibuscumque enim essentia est idem quod persona seu suppositum, oportet quod sit tantum unum suppositum unius naturae, ut patet in omnibus substantiis separatis, eorum enim quae sunt idem re, unum multiplicari non potest, quin multiplicetur et reliquum. Sed in divinis est una essentia et tres personae, ut ex supra dictis patet. Ergo essentia non est idem quod persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0007","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0008","text":"Praeterea, affirmatio et negatio simul et semel non verificantur de eodem. Sed affirmatio et negatio verificantur de essentia et persona, nam persona est distincta, essentia vero non est distincta. Ergo persona et essentia non sunt idem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0009","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0010","text":"Praeterea, nihil subiicitur sibi ipsi. Sed persona subiicitur essentiae, unde suppositum vel hypostasis nominatur. Ergo persona non est idem quod essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0011","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VII de Trin., *cum dicimus personam patris, non aliud dicimus quam substantiam patris*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0013","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod considerantibus divinam simplicitatem, quaestio ista in manifesto habet veritatem. Ostensum est enim supra quod divina simplicitas hoc requirit, quod in Deo sit idem essentia et suppositum; quod in substantiis intellectualibus nihil est aliud quam persona. Sed difficultatem videtur ingerere quod, multiplicatis personis divinis, essentia retinet unitatem. Et quia, ut Boetius dicit, relatio multiplicat personarum Trinitatem, posuerunt aliqui hoc modo in divinis differre essentiam et personam, quo et relationes dicebant esse assistentes, considerantes in relationibus solum quod ad alterum sunt, et non quod res sunt. Sed, sicut supra ostensum est, sicut relationes in rebus creatis accidentaliter insunt, ita in Deo sunt ipsa essentia divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0015","text":"Ex quo sequitur quod in Deo non sit aliud essentia quam persona secundum rem; et tamen quod personae realiter ab invicem distinguantur. Persona enim, ut dictum est supra, significat relationem, prout est subsistens in natura divina. Relatio autem, ad essentiam comparata, non differt re, sed ratione tantum, comparata autem ad oppositam relationem, habet, virtute oppositionis, realem distinctionem. Et sic remanet una essentia, et tres personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0016","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in creaturis non potest esse distinctio suppositorum per relationes, sed oportet quod sit per essentialia principia, quia relationes non sunt subsistentes in creaturis. In divinis autem relationes sunt subsistentes, et ideo, secundum quod habent oppositionem ad invicem, possunt distinguere supposita. Neque tamen distinguitur essentia, quia relationes ipsae non distinguuntur ab invicem secundum quod sunt realiter idem cum essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0018","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, inquantum essentia et persona in divinis differunt secundum intelligentiae rationem, sequitur quod aliquid possit affirmari de uno, quod negatur de altero, et per consequens quod, supposito uno, non supponatur alterum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0020","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod rebus divinis nomina imponimus secundum modum rerum creatarum, ut supra dictum est. Et quia naturae rerum creatarum individuantur per materiam, quae subiicitur naturae speciei, inde est quod individua dicuntur subiecta, vel supposita, vel hypostases. Et propter hoc etiam divinae personae supposita vel hypostases nominantur, non quod ibi sit aliqua suppositio vel subiectio secundum rem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0023","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit dicendum tres personas esse unius essentiae. Dicit enim Hilarius, in libro de Synod., quod pater et filius et spiritus sanctus *sunt quidem per substantiam tria, per consonantiam vero unum*. Sed substantia Dei est eius essentia. Ergo tres personae non sunt unius essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0025","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0026","text":"Praeterea, non est affirmandum aliquid de divinis, quod auctoritate Scripturae sacrae non est expressum, ut patet per Dionysium, I cap. de Div. Nom. Sed nunquam in Scriptura sacra exprimitur quod pater et filius et spiritus sanctus sunt unius essentiae. Ergo hoc non est asserendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0027","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0028","text":"Praeterea, natura divina est idem quod essentia. Sufficeret ergo dicere quod tres personae sunt unius naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0029","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0030","text":"Praeterea, non consuevit dici quod persona sit essentiae, sed magis quod essentia sit personae. Ergo neque convenienter videtur dici quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0031","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0032","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit quod non dicimus tres personas esse ex una essentia, ne intelligatur in divinis aliud esse essentia et persona. Sed sicut praepositiones sunt transitivae, ita et obliqui. Ergo, pari ratione, non est dicendum quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0033","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0034","text":"Praeterea, id quod potest esse erroris occasio, non est in divinis dicendum. Sed cum dicuntur tres personae unius essentiae vel substantiae datur erroris occasio. Quia, ut Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., *una substantia patris et filii praedicata, aut unum qui duas nuncupationes habeat, subsistentem significat; aut divisam unam substantiam duas imperfectas fecisse substantias; aut tertiam priorem substantiam, quae a duobus et usurpata sit et assumpta*. Non est ergo dicendum tres personas esse unius substantiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0035","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0036","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro II contra Maximinum, quod hoc nomen homousion, quod in Concilio Nicaeno adversus Arianos firmatum est, idem significat quod tres personas esse unius essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0037","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0038","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, intellectus noster res divinas nominat, non secundum modum earum, quia sic eas cognoscere non potest; sed secundum modum in rebus creatis inventum. Et quia in rebus sensibilibus, a quibus intellectus noster scientiam capit, natura alicuius speciei per materiam individuatur; et sic natura se habet ut forma, individuum autem ut suppositum formae, propter hoc etiam in divinis, quantum ad modum significandi, essentia significatur ut forma trium personarum. Dicimus autem in rebus creatis formam quamcumque esse eius cuius est forma; sicut sanitatem vel pulchritudinem hominis alicuius. Rem autem habentem formam non dicimus esse formae, nisi cum adiectione alicuius adiectivi, quod designat illam formam, ut cum dicimus, ista mulier est egregiae formae, iste homo est perfectae virtutis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0039","text":"Et similiter, quia in divinis, multiplicatis personis, non multiplicatur essentia, dicimus unam essentiam esse trium personarum; et tres personas unius essentiae, ut intelligantur isti genitivi construi in designatione formae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0040","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0041","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod substantia sumitur pro hypostasi; et non pro essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0042","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0043","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet tres personas esse unius essentiae non inveniatur in sacra Scriptura per haec verba, invenitur tamen quantum ad hunc sensum, sicut ibi, *ego et pater unum sumus; et, ego in patre, et pater in me est*. Et per multa alia haberi potest idem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0044","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0045","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quia natura designat principium actus, essentia vero ab essendo dicitur, possunt dici aliqua unius naturae, quae conveniunt in aliquo actu, sicut omnia calefacientia, sed unius essentiae dici non possunt, nisi quorum est unum esse. Et ideo magis exprimitur unitas divina per hoc quod dicitur quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae, quam si diceretur quod sunt unius naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0046","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0047","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod forma, absolute accepta, consuevit significari ut eius cuius est forma, ut virtus Petri. E converso autem, res habens formam aliquam non consuevit significari ut eius, nisi cum volumus determinare sive designare formam. Et tunc requiruntur duo genitivi, quorum unus significet formam, et alius determinationem formae, ut si dicatur, Petrus est magnae virtutis, vel etiam requiritur unus genitivus habens vim duorum genitivorum, ut cum dicitur, vir sanguinum est iste, idest effusor multi sanguinis. Quia igitur essentia divina significatur ut forma respectu personae, convenienter essentia personae dicitur. Non autem e converso, nisi aliquid addatur ad designationem essentiae; ut si dicatur quod pater est persona divinae essentiae, vel quod tres personae sunt unius essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0048","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0049","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod haec praepositio ex vel de non designat habitudinem causae formalis, sed magis habitudinem causae efficientis vel materialis. Quae quidem causae in omnibus distinguuntur ab his quorum sunt causae, nihil enim est sua materia, neque aliquid est suum principium activum. Aliquid tamen est sua forma, ut patet in omnibus rebus immaterialibus. Et ideo per hoc quod dicimus tres personas unius essentiae, significando essentiam in habitudine formae, non ostenditur aliud esse essentia quam persona, quod ostenderetur, si diceremus tres personas ex eadem essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0050","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0051","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod, sicut Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., *male sanctis rebus praeiudicatur, si, quia non sanctae a quibusdam habentur, esse non debeant. Sic, si male intelligitur homousion, quid ad me bene intelligentem? Sit ergo una substantia ex naturae genitae proprietate, non sit autem ex portione, aut ex unione, aut ex communione*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0052","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0053","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0054","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina essentialia, ut hoc nomen Deus, non praedicentur singulariter de tribus personis, sed pluraliter. Sicut enim homo significatur ut habens humanitatem, ita Deus significatur ut habens deitatem. Sed tres personae sunt tres habentes deitatem. Ergo tres personae sunt tres dii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0055","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0056","text":"Praeterea, Gen. I, ubi dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*, Hebraica veritas habet Elohim, quod potest interpretari dii, sive iudices. Et hoc dicitur propter pluralitatem personarum. Ergo tres personae sunt plures dii, et non unus Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0057","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0058","text":"Praeterea, hoc nomen res, cum absolute dicatur, videtur ad substantiam pertinere. Sed hoc nomen pluraliter praedicatur de tribus personis, dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de Doctr. Christ., *res quibus fruendum est, sunt pater et filius et spiritus sanctus*. Ergo et alia nomina essentialia pluraliter praedicari possunt de tribus personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0059","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0060","text":"Praeterea, sicut hoc nomen Deus significat habentem deitatem, ita hoc nomen persona significat subsistentem in natura aliqua intellectuali. Sed dicimus tres personas. Ergo, eadem ratione, dicere possumus tres deos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0061","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0062","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Deut. VI, *audi, Israel, dominus Deus tuus, Deus unus est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0063","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0064","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nominum essentialium quaedam significant essentiam substantive, quaedam vero adiective. Ea quidem quae substantive essentiam significant, praedicantur de tribus personis singulariter tantum, et non pluraliter, quae vero adiective essentiam significant, praedicantur de tribus personis in plurali. Cuius ratio est, quia nomina substantiva significant aliquid per modum substantiae, nomina vero adiectiva significant aliquid per modum accidentis, quod inhaeret subiecto. Substantia autem, sicut per se habet esse, ita per se habet unitatem vel multitudinem, unde et singularitas vel pluralitas nominis substantivi attenditur secundum formam significatam per nomen. Accidentia autem, sicut esse habent in subiecto, ita ex subiecto suscipiunt unitatem et multitudinem, et ideo in adiectivis attenditur singularitas et pluralitas secundum supposita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0065","text":"In creaturis autem non invenitur una forma in pluribus suppositis nisi unitate ordinis, ut forma multitudinis ordinatae. Unde nomina significantia talem formam, si sint substantiva, praedicantur de pluribus in singulari, non autem si sint adiectiva. Dicimus enim quod multi homines sunt collegium vel exercitus aut populus, dicimus tamen quod plures homines sunt collegiati. In divinis autem essentia divina significatur per modum formae, ut dictum est quae quidem simplex est et maxime una, ut supra ostensum est. Unde nomina significantia divinam essentiam substantive, singulariter, et non pluraliter, de tribus personis praedicantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0066","text":"Haec igitur est ratio quare Socratem et Platonem et Ciceronem dicimus tres homines; patrem autem et filium et spiritum sanctum non dicimus tres deos, sed unum Deum, quia in tribus suppositis humanae naturae sunt tres humanitates; in tribus autem personis est una divina essentia. Ea vero quae significant essentiam adiective, praedicantur pluraliter de tribus, propter pluralitatem suppositorum. Dicimus enim tres existentes vel tres sapientes, aut tres aeternos et increatos et immensos, si adiective sumantur. Si vero substantive sumantur, dicimus unum increatum, immensum et aeternum, ut Athanasius dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0067","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0068","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet Deus significet habentem deitatem, est tamen alius modus significandi, nam Deus dicitur substantive, sed habens deitatem dicitur adiective. Unde, licet sint tres habentes deitatem, non tamen sequitur quod sint tres dii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0069","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0070","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod diversae linguae habent diversum modum loquendi. Unde, sicut propter pluralitatem suppositorum Graeci dicunt tres hypostases, ita et in Hebraeo dicitur pluraliter Elohim. Nos autem non dicimus pluraliter neque deos neque substantias, ne pluralitas ad substantiam referatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0071","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0072","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod hoc nomen res est de transcendentibus. Unde, secundum quod pertinet ad relationem, pluraliter praedicatur in divinis, secundum vero quod pertinet ad substantiam, singulariter praedicatur. Unde Augustinus dicit ibidem quod *eadem Trinitas quaedam summa res est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0073","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0074","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod forma significata per hoc nomen persona, non est essentia vel natura, sed personalitas. Unde, cum sint tres personalitates, idest tres personales proprietates, in patre et filio et spiritu sancto, non singulariter, sed pluraliter praedicatur de tribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0075","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0076","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0077","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina essentialia concretiva non possunt supponere pro persona, ita quod haec sit vera, Deus genuit Deum. Quia, ut sophistae dicunt, terminus singularis idem significat et supponit. Sed hoc nomen Deus videtur esse terminus singularis, cum pluraliter praedicari non possit, ut dictum est. Ergo, cum significet essentiam, videtur quod supponat pro essentia, et non pro persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0078","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0079","text":"Praeterea, terminus in subiecto positus non restringitur per terminum positum in praedicato, ratione significationis; sed solum ratione temporis consignificati. Sed cum dico, Deus creat, hoc nomen Deus supponit pro essentia. Ergo cum dicitur, Deus genuit, non potest iste terminus Deus, ratione praedicati notionalis, supponere pro persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0080","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0081","text":"Praeterea, si haec est vera, Deus genuit, quia pater generat; pari ratione haec erit vera, Deus non generat, quia filius non generat. Ergo est Deus generans, et Deus non generans. Et ita videtur sequi quod sint duo dii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0082","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0083","text":"Praeterea, si Deus genuit Deum, aut se Deum, aut alium Deum. Sed non se Deum, quia, ut Augustinus dicit, in I de Trin., *nulla res generat seipsam*. Neque alium Deum, quia non est nisi unus Deus. Ergo haec est falsa, Deus genuit Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0084","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0085","text":"Praeterea, si Deus genuit Deum, aut Deum qui est Deus pater, aut Deum qui non est Deus pater. Si Deum qui est Deus pater, ergo Deus pater est genitus. Si Deum qui non est Deus pater, ergo Deus est qui non est Deus pater, quod est falsum. Non ergo potest dici quod Deus genuit Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0086","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0087","text":"Sed contra est quod in symbolo dicitur Deum de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0088","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0089","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod hoc nomen Deus, et similia, proprie secundum suam naturam supponunt pro essentia, sed ex adiuncto notionali trahuntur ad supponendum pro persona. Et haec opinio processisse videtur ex consideratione divinae simplicitatis, quae requirit quod in Deo idem sit habens et quod habetur, et sic habens deitatem, quod significat hoc nomen Deus, est idem quod deitas. Sed in proprietatibus locutionum, non tantum attendenda est res significata; sed etiam modus significandi. Et ideo, quia hoc nomen Deus significat divinam essentiam ut in habente ipsam, sicut hoc nomen homo humanitatem significat in supposito; alii melius dixerunt quod hoc nomen Deus ex modo significandi habet ut proprie possit supponere pro persona, sicut et hoc nomen homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0090","text":"Quandoque ergo hoc nomen Deus supponit pro essentia, ut cum dicitur, Deus creat, quia hoc praedicatum competit subiecto ratione formae significatae, quae est deitas. Quandoque vero supponit personam, vel unam tantum, ut cum dicitur, Deus generat; vel duas, ut cum dicitur Deus spirat; vel tres, ut cum dicitur, *regi saeculorum immortali, invisibili, soli Deo* etc., I Tim. I.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0091","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0092","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc nomen Deus, licet conveniat cum terminis singularibus in hoc, quod forma significata non multiplicatur; convenit tamen cum terminis communibus in hoc, quod forma significata invenitur in pluribus suppositis. Unde non oportet quod semper supponat pro essentia quam significat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0093","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0094","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit contra illos qui dicebant quod hoc nomen Deus non habet naturalem suppositionem pro persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0095","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0096","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aliter se habet hoc nomen Deus ad supponendum pro persona, et hoc nomen homo. Quia enim forma significata per hoc nomen homo, idest humanitas, realiter dividitur in diversis suppositis, per se supponit pro persona; etiamsi nihil addatur quod determinet ipsum ad personam, quae est suppositum distinctum. Unitas autem sive communitas humanae naturae non est secundum rem, sed solum secundum considerationem, unde iste terminus homo non supponit pro natura communi, nisi propter exigentiam alicuius additi, ut cum dicitur, homo est species. Sed forma significata per hoc nomen Deus, scilicet essentia divina, est una et communis secundum rem. Unde per se supponit pro natura communi, sed ex adiuncto determinatur eius suppositio ad personam. Unde cum dicitur, Deus generat, ratione actus notionalis supponit hoc nomen Deus pro persona patris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0097","text":"Sed cum dicitur, Deus non generat, nihil additur quod determinet hoc nomen ad personam filii, unde datur intelligi quod generatio repugnet divinae naturae. Sed si addatur aliquid pertinens ad personam filii, vera erit locutio; ut si dicatur, Deus genitus non generat. Unde etiam non sequitur, est Deus generans et est Deus non generans, nisi ponatur aliquid pertinens ad personas; ut puta si dicamus, pater est Deus generans, et filius est Deus non generans. Et ita non sequitur quod sint plures dii, quia pater et filius sunt unus Deus, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0098","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0099","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod haec est falsa, pater genuit se Deum, quia ly se, cum sit reciprocum, refert idem suppositum. Neque est contrarium quod Augustinus dicit, ad maximum, quod Deus pater genuit alterum se. Quia ly se vel est casus ablativi; ut sit sensus, genuit alterum a se. Vel facit relationem simplicem, et sic refert identitatem naturae, sed est impropria vel emphatica locutio, ut sit sensus, genuit alterum simillimum sibi. Similiter et haec est falsa, genuit alium Deum. Quia licet filius sit alius a patre, ut supra dictum est, non tamen est dicendum quod sit alius Deus, quia intelligeretur quod hoc adiectivum alius poneret rem suam circa substantivum quod est Deus; et sic significaretur distinctio deitatis. Quidam tamen concedunt istam, genuit alium Deum, ita quod ly alius sit substantivum, et ly Deus appositive construatur cum eo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0100","text":"Sed hic est improprius modus loquendi, et evitandus, ne detur occasio erroris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0101","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0102","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod haec est falsa, Deus genuit Deum qui est Deus pater, quia, cum ly pater appositive construatur cum ly Deus, restringit ipsum ad standum pro persona patris; ut sit sensus, genuit Deum qui est ipse pater, et sic pater esset genitus, quod est falsum. Unde negativa est vera, genuit Deum qui non est Deus pater. Si tamen intelligeretur constructio non esse appositiva, sed aliquid esse interponendum; tunc e converso affirmativa esset vera, et negativa falsa; ut sit sensus, genuit Deum qui est Deus qui est pater. Sed haec est extorta expositio. Unde melius est quod simpliciter affirmativa negetur, et negativa concedatur. Praepositivus tamen dixit quod tam negativa quam affirmativa est falsa. Quia hoc relativum qui in affirmativa potest referre suppositum, sed in negativa refert et significatum et suppositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0103","text":"Unde sensus affirmativae est, quod esse Deum patrem conveniat personae filii. Negativae vero sensus est, quod esse Deum patrem non tantum removeatur a persona filii, sed etiam a divinitate eius sed hoc irrationabile videtur, cum, secundum philosophum, de eodem de quo est affirmatio, possit etiam esse negatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0104","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0105","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0106","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina essentialia in abstracto significata possint supponere pro persona, ita quod haec sit vera, essentia generat essentiam. Dicit enim Augustinus, VII de Trin., *pater et filius sunt una sapientia, quia una essentia; et singillatim sapientia de sapientia, sicut essentia de essentia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0107","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0108","text":"Praeterea, generatis nobis vel corruptis, generantur vel corrumpuntur ea quae in nobis sunt. Sed filius generatur. Ergo, cum essentia divina sit in filio, videtur quod essentia divina generetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0109","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0110","text":"Praeterea, idem est Deus et essentia divina, ut ex supra dictis patet. Sed haec est vera, Deus generat Deum, sicut dictum est. Ergo haec est vera, essentia generat essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0111","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0112","text":"Praeterea, de quocumque praedicatur aliquid, potest supponere pro illo. Sed essentia divina est pater. Ergo essentia potest supponere pro persona patris. Et sic essentia generat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0113","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0114","text":"Praeterea, essentia est res generans, quia est pater, qui est generans. Si igitur essentia non sit generans, erit essentia res generans et non generans, quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0115","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0116","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in IV de Trin., *pater est principium totius deitatis*. Sed non est principium nisi generando vel spirando. Ergo pater generat vel spirat deitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0117","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0118","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in I de Trin., quod *nulla res generat seipsam*. Sed si essentia generat essentiam, non generat nisi seipsam, cum nihil sit in Deo, quod distinguatur a divina essentia. Ergo essentia non generat essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0119","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0120","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc erravit abbas Ioachim, asserens quod, sicut dicitur, Deus genuit Deum, ita potest dici quod essentia genuit essentiam; considerans quod, propter divinam simplicitatem, non est aliud Deus quam divina essentia. Sed in hoc deceptus fuit, quia ad veritatem locutionum, non solum oportet considerare res significatas, sed etiam modum significandi ut dictum est. Licet autem, secundum re, sit idem Deus quod deitas, non tamen est idem modus significandi utrobique. Nam hoc nomen Deus, quia significat divinam essentiam ut in habente, ex modo suae significationis naturaliter habet quod possit supponere pro persona, et sic ea quae sunt propria personarum, possunt praedicari de hoc nomine Deus, ut dicatur quod Deus est genitus vel generans, sicut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0121","text":"Sed hoc nomen essentia non habet ex modo suae significationis quod supponat pro persona, quia significat essentiam ut formam abstractam. Et ideo ea quae sunt propria personarum, quibus ab invicem distinguuntur, non possunt essentiae attribui, significaretur enim quod esset distinctio in essentia divina, sicut est distinctio in suppositis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0122","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0123","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, ad exprimendam unitatem essentiae et personae, sancti doctores aliquando expressius locuti sunt quam proprietas locutionis patiatur. Unde huiusmodi locutiones non sunt extendendae, sed exponendae, ut scilicet nomina abstracta exponantur per concreta, vel etiam per nomina personalia, ut, cum dicitur, essentia de essentia, vel sapientia de sapientia, sit sensus, filius, qui est essentia et sapientia, est de patre, qui est essentia et sapientia. In his tamen nominibus abstractis est quidam ordo attendendus, quia ea quae pertinent ad actum, magis propinque se habent ad personas, quia actus sunt suppositorum. Unde minus impropria est ista, natura de natura, vel sapientia de sapientia, quam essentia de essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0124","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0125","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in creaturis generatum non accipit naturam eandem numero quam generans habet, sed aliam numero, quae incipit in eo esse per generationem de novo, et desinit esse per corruptionem, et ideo generatur et corrumpitur per accidens. Sed Deus genitus eandem naturam numero accipit quam generans habet. Et ideo natura divina in filio non generatur, neque per se neque per accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0126","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0127","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet Deus et divina essentia sint idem secundum rem, tamen, ratione alterius modi significandi, oportet loqui diversimode de utroque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0128","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0129","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod essentia divina praedicatur de patre per modum identitatis, propter divinam simplicitatem, nec tamen sequitur quod possit supponere pro patre, propter diversum modum significandi. Ratio autem procederet in illis, quorum unum praedicatur de altero sicut universale de particulari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0130","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0131","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod haec est differentia inter nomina substantiva et adiectiva, quia nomina substantiva ferunt suum suppositum, adiectiva vero non, sed rem significatam ponunt circa substantivum. Unde sophistae dicunt quod nomina substantiva supponunt; adiectiva vero non supponunt, sed copulant. Nomina igitur personalia substantiva possunt de essentia praedicari, propter identitatem rei, neque sequitur quod proprietas personalis distinctam determinet essentiam; sed ponitur circa suppositum importatum per nomen substantivum. Sed notionalia et personalia adiectiva non possunt praedicari de essentia, nisi aliquo substantivo adiuncto. Unde non possumus dicere quod essentia est generans. Possumus tamen dicere quod essentia est res generans, vel Deus generans, si res et Deus supponant pro persona, non autem si supponant pro essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0132","text":"Unde non est contradictio, si dicatur quod essentia est res generans, et res non generans, quia primo res tenetur pro persona, secundo pro essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0133","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0134","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod deitas, inquantum est una in pluribus suppositis, habet quandam convenientiam cum forma nominis collectivi. Unde cum dicitur, pater est principium totius deitatis, potest sumi pro universitate personarum; inquantum scilicet, in omnibus personis divinis, ipse est principium. Nec oportet quod sit principium sui ipsius, sicut aliquis de populo dicitur rector totius populi, non tamen sui ipsius. Vel potest dici quod est principium totius deitatis, non quia eam generet et spiret, sed quia eam, generando et spirando, communicat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0135","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0136","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0137","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod personae non possint praedicari de nominibus essentialibus concretis, ut dicatur, Deus est tres personae, vel est Trinitas. Haec enim est falsa, homo est omnis homo, quia pro nullo suppositorum verificari potest, neque enim Socrates est omnis homo, neque Plato, neque aliquis alius. Sed similiter ista, Deus est Trinitas, pro nullo suppositorum naturae divinae verificari potest, neque enim pater est Trinitas, neque filius, neque spiritus sanctus. Ergo haec est falsa, Deus est Trinitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0138","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0139","text":"Praeterea, inferiora non praedicantur de suis superioribus nisi accidentali praedicatione, ut cum dico, animal est homo, accidit enim animali esse hominem. Sed hoc nomen Deus se habet ad tres personas sicut commune ad inferiora, ut Damascenus dicit. Ergo videtur quod nomina personarum non possint praedicari de hoc nomine Deus, nisi accidentaliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0140","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0141","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in sermone de fide, *credimus unum Deum unam esse divini nominis Trinitatem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0142","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0143","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, licet nomina personalia vel notionalia adiectiva non possint praedicari de essentia; tamen substantiva possunt, propter realem identitatem essentiae et personae. Essentia autem divina non solum idem est realiter cum una persona, sed cum tribus. Unde et una persona, et duae, et tres possunt de essentia praedicari; ut si dicamus, essentia est pater et filius et spiritus sanctus. Et quia hoc nomen Deus per se habet quod supponat pro essentia, ut dictum est, ideo, sicut haec est vera, essentia est tres personae, ita haec est vera, Deus est tres personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0144","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0145","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, hoc nomen homo per se habet supponere pro persona; sed ex adiuncto habet quod stet pro natura communi. Et ideo haec est falsa, homo est omnis homo, quia pro nullo supposito verificari potest. Sed hoc nomen Deus per se habet quod stet pro essentia. Unde, licet pro nullo suppositorum divinae naturae haec sit vera, Deus est Trinitas, est tamen vera pro essentia. Quod non attendens, Porretanus eam negavit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0146","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0147","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur, Deus vel divina essentia est pater, est praedicatio per identitatem, non autem sicut inferioris de superiori, quia in divinis non est universale et singulare. Unde, sicut est per se ista, pater est Deus, ita et ista, Deus est pater; et nullo modo per accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0148","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0149","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0150","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nomina essentialia non sint approprianda personis. Quod enim potest vergere in errorem fidei, vitandum est in divinis, quia, ut Hieronymus dicit, *ex verbis inordinate prolatis incurritur haeresis*. Sed ea quae sunt communia tribus personis appropriare alicui, potest vergere in errorem fidei, quia potest intelligi quod vel illi tantum personae conveniant cui appropriantur; vel quod magis conveniant ei quam aliis. Ergo essentialia attributa non sunt approprianda personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0151","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0152","text":"Praeterea, essentialia attributa, in abstracto significata, significant per modum formae. Sed una persona non se habet ad aliam ut forma, cum forma ab eo cuius est forma, supposito non distinguatur. Ergo essentialia attributa, maxime in abstracto significata, non debent appropriari personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0153","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0154","text":"Praeterea, proprium prius est appropriato, proprium enim est de ratione appropriati. Sed essentialia attributa, secundum modum intelligendi, sunt priora personis, sicut commune est prius proprio. Ergo essentialia attributa non debent esse appropriata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0155","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0156","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit, I Cor. I, *Christum, Dei virtutem et Dei sapientiam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0157","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0158","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, ad manifestationem fidei, conveniens fuit essentialia attributa personis appropriari. Licet enim Trinitas personarum demonstratione probari non possit, ut supra dictum est, convenit tamen ut per aliqua magis manifesta declaretur. Essentialia vero attributa sunt nobis magis manifesta secundum rationem, quam propria personarum, quia ex creaturis, ex quibus cognitionem accipimus, possumus per certitudinem devenire in cognitionem essentialium attributorum; non autem in cognitionem personalium proprietatum, ut supra dictum est. Sicut igitur similitudine vestigii vel imaginis in creaturis inventa utimur ad manifestationem divinarum personarum, ita et essentialibus attributis. Et haec manifestatio personarum per essentialia attributa, appropriatio nominatur. Possunt autem manifestari personae divinae per essentialia attributa dupliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0159","text":"Uno modo, per viam similitudinis, sicut ea quae pertinent ad intellectum, appropriantur filio, qui procedit per modum intellectus ut verbum. Alio modo, per modum dissimilitudinis, sicut potentia appropriatur patri, ut Augustinus dicit, quia apud nos patres solent esse propter senectutem infirmi; ne tale aliquid suspicemur in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0160","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0161","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod essentialia attributa non sic appropriantur personis ut eis esse propria asserantur, sed ad manifestandum personas per viam similitudinis vel dissimilitudinis, ut dictum est. Unde nullus error fidei sequitur, sed magis manifestatio veritatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0162","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0163","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, si sic appropriarentur essentialia attributa personis, quod essent eis propria, sequeretur quod una persona se haberet ad aliam in habitudine formae. Quod excludit Augustinus, in VII de Trin., ostendens quod pater non est sapiens sapientia quam genuit, quasi solus filius sit sapientia; ut sic pater et filius simul tantum possint dici sapiens, non autem pater sine filio. Sed filius dicitur sapientia patris, quia est sapientia de patre sapientia, uterque enim per se est sapientia, et simul ambo una sapientia. Unde pater non est sapiens sapientia quam genuit, sed sapientia quae est sua essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0164","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0165","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet essentiale attributum, secundum rationem propriam, sit prius quam persona, secundum, modum intelligendi; tamen, inquantum habet rationem appropriati, nihil prohibet proprium personae esse prius quam appropriatum. Sicut color posterior est corpore, inquantum est corpus, prius tamen est naturaliter corpore albo, inquantum est album.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0166","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0167","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0168","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter a sacris doctoribus sint essentialia personis attributa. Dicit enim Hilarius, in II de Trin., *aeternitas est in patre, species in imagine, usus in munere*. In quibus verbis ponit tria nomina propria personarum, scilicet nomen patris; et nomen imaginis, quod est proprium filio, ut supra dictum est; et nomen muneris, sive doni, quod est proprium spiritus sancti, ut supra habitum est. Ponit etiam tria appropriata, nam aeternitatem appropriat patri, speciem filio, usum spiritui sancto. Et videtur quod irrationabiliter. Nam aeternitas importat durationem essendi, species vero est essendi principium, usus vero ad operationem pertinere videtur. Sed essentia et operatio nulli personae appropriari inveniuntur. Ergo inconvenienter videntur ista appropriata personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0169","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0170","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus in I de Doctr. Christ., sic dicit, *in patre est unitas, in filio aequalitas, in spiritu sancto unitatis aequalitatisque concordia*. Et videtur quod inconvenienter. Quia una persona non denominatur formaliter per id quod appropriatur alteri, non enim est sapiens pater sapientia genita, ut dictum est. Sed, sicut ibidem subditur, *tria haec unum omnia sunt propter patrem, aequalia omnia propter filium, connexa omnia propter spiritum sanctum*. Non ergo convenienter appropriantur personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0171","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0172","text":"Item, secundum Augustinum, patri attribuitur potentia, filio sapientia, spiritui sancto bonitas. Et videtur hoc esse inconveniens. Nam virtus ad potentiam pertinet. Virtus autem invenitur appropriari filio, secundum illud I ad Cor. I, *Christum, Dei virtutem*; et etiam spiritui sancto, secundum illud Luc. VI, *virtus de illo exibat, et sanabat omnes*. Non ergo potentia patri est approprianda.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0173","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0174","text":"Item, Augustinus, in libro de Trin., dicit, *non confuse accipiendum est quod ait apostolus, ex ipso, et per ipsum, et in ipso - ex ipso dicens propter patrem; per ipsum propter filium; in ipso propter spiritum sanctum*. Sed videtur quod inconvenienter. Quia per hoc quod dicit in ipso, videtur importari habitudo causae finalis, quae est prima causarum. Ergo ista habitudo causae deberet appropriari patri, qui est principium non de principio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0175","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0176","text":"Item, invenitur veritas appropriari filio, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *ego sum via, veritas et vita*. Et similiter liber vitae, secundum illud Psalmi XXXIX, *in capite libri scriptum est de me*, Glossa, idest apud patrem, qui est caput meum. Et similiter hoc quod dico, qui est, quia super illud Isa. LXV, *ecce ego, ad gentes*, dicit Glossa, *filius loquitur, qui dixit Moysi, ego sum qui sum*. Sed videtur quod propria sint filii, et non appropriata. *Nam veritas*, secundum Augustinum, in libro de vera religione, *est summa similitudo principii, absque omni dissimilitudine*, et sic videtur quod proprie conveniat filio, qui habet principium. Liber etiam vitae videtur proprium aliquid esse, quia significat ens ab alio, omnis enim liber ab aliquo scribitur. Hoc etiam ipsum qui est videtur esse proprium filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0177","text":"Quia si, cum Moysi dicitur, ego sum qui sum, loquitur Trinitas, ergo Moyses poterat dicere, ille qui est pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, misit me ad vos. Ergo et ulterius dicere poterat, ille qui est pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, misit me ad vos, demonstrando certam personam. Hoc autem est falsum, quia nulla persona est pater et filius et spiritus sanctus. Non ergo potest esse commune Trinitati, sed est proprium filii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0178","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0179","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod intellectus noster, qui ex creaturis in Dei cognitionem manuducitur, oportet quod Deum consideret secundum modum quem ex creaturis assumit. In consideratione autem alicuius creaturae, quatuor per ordinem nobis occurrunt. Nam primo, consideratur res ipsa absolute, inquantum est ens quoddam. Secunda autem consideratio rei est, inquantum est una. Tertia consideratio rei est, secundum quod inest ei virtus ad operandum et ad causandum. Quarta autem consideratio rei est, secundum habitudinem quam habet ad causata. Unde haec etiam quadruplex consideratio circa Deum nobis occurrit. Secundum igitur primam considerationem, qua consideratur absolute Deus secundum esse suum, sic sumitur appropriatio Hilarii, secundum quam aeternitas appropriatur patri, species filio, usus spiritui sancto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0180","text":"Aeternitas enim, inquantum significat esse non principiatum, similitudinem habet cum proprio patris, qui est principium non de principio. Species autem, sive pulchritudo, habet similitudinem cum propriis filii. Nam ad pulchritudinem tria requiruntur. Primo quidem, integritas sive perfectio, quae enim diminuta sunt, hoc ipso turpia sunt. Et debita proportio sive consonantia. Et iterum claritas, unde quae habent colorem nitidum, pulchra esse dicuntur. Quantum igitur ad primum, similitudinem habet cum proprio filii, inquantum est filius habens in se vere et perfecte naturam patris. Unde, ad hoc innuendum, Augustinus in sua expositione dicit, ubi, scilicet in filio, summa et prima vita est, et cetera. Quantum vero ad secundum, convenit cum proprio filii, inquantum est imago expressa patris. Unde videmus quod aliqua imago dicitur esse pulchra, si perfecte repraesentat rem, quamvis turpem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0181","text":"Et hoc tetigit Augustinus cum dicit, ubi est tanta convenientia, et prima aequalitas, et cetera. Quantum vero ad tertium, convenit cum proprio filii, inquantum est verbum, quod quidem lux est, et splendor intellectus, ut Damascenus dicit. Et hoc tangit Augustinus cum dicit, *tanquam verbum perfectum cui non desit aliquid, et ars quaedam omnipotentis Dei,* et cetera. Usus autem habet similitudinem cum propriis spiritus sancti, largo modo accipiendo usum, secundum quod uti comprehendit sub se etiam frui; prout uti est assumere aliquid in facultatem voluntatis, et frui est cum gaudio uti, ut Augustinus, X de Trin., dicit. Usus ergo quo pater et filius se invicem fruuntur, convenit cum proprio spiritus sancti, inquantum est amor. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit, *illa dilectio, delectatio, felicitas vel beatitudo, usus ab illo appellatus est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0182","text":"Usus vero quo nos fruimur Deo, similitudinem habet cum proprio spiritus sancti, inquantum est donum. Et hoc ostendit Augustinus cum dicit, *est in Trinitate spiritus sanctus, genitoris genitique suavitas, ingenti largitate atque ubertate nos perfundens*. Et sic patet quare aeternitas, species et usus personis attribuantur vel approprientur, non autem essentia vel operatio. Quia in ratione horum, propter sui communitatem, non invenitur aliquid similitudinem habens cum propriis personarum. Secunda vero consideratio Dei est, inquantum consideratur ut unus. Et sic Augustinus patri appropriat unitatem, filio aequalitatem, spiritui sancto concordiam sive connexionem. Quae quidem tria unitatem importare manifestum est, sed differenter. Nam unitas dicitur absolute, non praesupponens aliquid aliud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0183","text":"Et ideo appropriatur patri, qui non praesupponit aliquam personam, cum sit principium non de principio. Aequalitas autem importat unitatem in respectu ad alterum, nam aequale est quod habet unam quantitatem cum alio. Et ideo aequalitas appropriatur filio, qui est principium de principio. Connexio autem importat unitatem aliquorum duorum. Unde appropriatur spiritui sancto, inquantum est a duobus. Ex quo etiam intelligi potest quod dicit Augustinus, *tria esse unum propter patrem, aequalia propter filium, connexa propter spiritum sanctum*. Manifestum est enim quod illi attribuitur unumquodque, in quo primo invenitur, sicut omnia inferiora dicuntur vivere propter animam vegetabilem, in qua primo invenitur ratio vitae in istis inferioribus. Unitas autem statim invenitur in persona patris, etiam, per impossibile, remotis aliis personis. Et ideo aliae personae a patre habent unitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0184","text":"Sed remotis aliis personis, non invenitur aequalitas in patre, sed statim, posito filio, invenitur aequalitas. Et ideo dicuntur omnia aequalia propter filium, non quod filius sit principium aequalitatis patri; sed quia, nisi esset patri aequalis filius, pater aequalis non posset dici. Aequalitas enim eius primo consideratur ad filium, hoc enim ipsum quod spiritus sanctus patri aequalis est, a filio habet. Similiter, excluso spiritu sancto, qui est duorum nexus, non posset intelligi unitas connexionis inter patrem et filium. Et ideo dicuntur omnia esse connexa propter spiritum sanctum, quia, posito spiritu sancto, invenitur unde pater et filius possint dici connexi. Secundum vero tertiam considerationem, qua in Deo sufficiens virtus consideratur ad causandum, sumitur tertia appropriatio, scilicet potentiae, sapientiae et bonitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0185","text":"Quae quidem appropriatio fit et secundum rationem similitudinis, si consideretur quod in divinis personis est, et secundum rationem dissimilitudinis, si consideretur quod in creaturis est. Potentia enim habet rationem principii. Unde habet similitudinem cum patre caelesti, qui est principium totius divinitatis. Deficit autem interdum patri terreno, propter senectutem. Sapientia vero similitudinem habet cum filio caelesti, inquantum est verbum, quod nihil aliud est quam conceptus sapientiae. Deficit autem interdum filio terreno, propter temporis paucitatem. Bonitas autem, cum sit ratio et obiectum amoris, habet similitudinem cum spiritu divino, qui est amor. Sed repugnantiam habere videtur ad spiritum terrenum, secundum quod importat violentam quandam impulsionem; prout dicitur Isa. XXV, *spiritus robustorum quasi turbo impellens parietem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0186","text":"Virtus autem appropriatur filio et spiritui sancto, non secundum quod virtus dicitur ipsa potentia rei, sed secundum quod interdum virtus dicitur id quod a potentia rei procedit, prout dicimus aliquod virtuosum factum esse virtutem alicuius agentis. Secundum vero quartam considerationem, prout consideratur Deus in habitudine ad suos effectus, sumitur illa appropriatio ex quo, per quem, et in quo. Haec enim praepositio ex importat quandoque quidem habitudinem causae materialis, quae locum non habet in divinis, aliquando vero habitudinem causae efficientis. Quae quidem competit Deo ratione suae potentiae activae, unde et appropriatur patri, sicut et potentia. Haec vero praepositio per designat quidem quandoque causam mediam; sicut dicimus quod faber operatur per martellum. Et sic ly per quandoque non est appropriatum, sed proprium filii, secundum illud Ioan.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0187","text":"I, *omnia per ipsum facta sunt*; non quia filius sit instrumentum, sed quia ipse est principium de principio. Quandoque vero designat habitudinem formae per quam agens operatur; sicut dicimus quod artifex operatur per artem. Unde, sicut sapientia et ars appropriantur filio, ita et ly per quem. Haec vero praepositio in denotat proprie habitudinem continentis. Continet autem Deus res dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum suas similitudines; prout scilicet res dicuntur esse in Deo, inquantum sunt in eius scientia. Et sic hoc quod dico in ipso, esset appropriandum filio. Alio vero modo continentur res a Deo, inquantum Deus sua bonitate eas conservat et gubernat, ad finem convenientem adducendo. Et sic ly in quo appropriatur spiritui sancto, sicut et bonitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0188","text":"Nec oportet quod habitudo causae finalis, quamvis sit prima causarum, approprietur patri, qui est principium non de principio, quia personae divinae, quarum pater est principium, non procedunt ut ad finem, cum quaelibet illarum sit ultimus finis; sed naturali processione, quae magis ad rationem naturalis potentiae pertinere videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0189","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0190","text":"Ad illud vero quod de aliis quaeritur, dicendum quod veritas, cum pertineat ad intellectum, ut supra dictum est, appropriatur filio, non tamen est proprium eius. Quia veritas, ut supra dictum est, considerari potest prout est in intellectu, vel prout est in re. Sicut igitur intellectus et res essentialiter sumpta sunt essentialia et non personalia, ita et veritas. Definitio autem Augustini inducta, datur de veritate secundum quod appropriatur filio. Liber autem vitae in recto quidem importat notitiam, sed in obliquo vitam, est enim, ut supra dictum est, notitia Dei de his qui habituri sunt vitam aeternam. Unde appropriatur filio, licet vita approprietur spiritui sancto, inquantum importat quendam interiorem motum, et sic convenit cum proprio spiritus sancti, inquantum est amor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0191","text":"Esse autem scriptum ab alio, non est de ratione libri inquantum est liber; sed inquantum est quoddam artificiatum. Unde non importat originem, neque est personale, sed appropriatum personae. Ipsum autem qui est appropriatur personae filii, non secundum propriam rationem, sed ratione adiuncti, inquantum scilicet in locutione Dei ad Moysen, praefigurabatur liberatio humani generis, quae facta est per filium. Sed tamen, secundum quod ly qui sumitur relative, posset referre interdum personam filii, et sic sumeretur personaliter, ut puta si dicatur, filius est genitus qui est; sicut et Deus genitus personale est. Sed infinite sumptum est essentiale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0192","text":"Et licet hoc pronomen iste, grammatice loquendo, ad aliquam certam personam videatur pertinere; tamen quaelibet res demonstrabilis, grammatice loquendo, persona dici potest, licet secundum rei naturam non sit persona; dicimus enim iste lapis, et iste asinus. Unde et, grammatice loquendo, essentia divina, secundum quod significatur et supponitur per hoc nomen Deus, potest demonstrari hoc pronomine iste; secundum illud Exod. XV, *iste Deus meus, et glorificabo eum*.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"cdfb29716ab3d68cd1d43c0517e1fd20612992c55b7946319695e9e0756caa42","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T142148985454Z_34af0db9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 39","source_sha256":"cdfb29716ab3d68cd1d43c0517e1fd20612992c55b7946319695e9e0756caa42","sha256":"fd986f837ef0b8e1dd1482dc7837dd6c211288d09582b9578dd8c7fc510154f3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:21:48.985476+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0002","text":"[I q. 39 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0003","text":"After those matters which have been treated concerning the divine persons absolutely, it remains to consider the persons in comparison with the essence, with the properties, and with the notional acts; and their comparison with one another. Accordingly, with regard to the first of these, eight questions are asked. First, whether in divine matters essence is the same as person. Second, whether it should be said that the three persons are of one essence. Third, whether essential names ought to be predicated of the persons in the plural or in the singular. Fourth, whether notional adjectives, or verbs or participles, can be predicated of essential names taken concretely. Fifth, whether they can be predicated of essential names taken abstractly. Sixth, whether names of persons can be predicated of concrete essential names. Seventh, whether essential attributes are to be appropriated to the persons. Eighth, which attribute ought to be appropriated to each person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0005","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that in divine matters essence is not the same as person. For wherever essence is the same as person or supposit, there must be only one supposit of one nature, as is clear in all separate substances; for of things that are the same in reality, one cannot be multiplied without the other also being multiplied. But in divine matters there is one essence and three persons, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore essence is not the same as person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0007","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0008","text":"Moreover, affirmation and negation are not verified of the same thing simultaneously and in the same respect. But affirmation and negation are verified of essence and person, for person is distinct, whereas essence is not distinct. Therefore person and essence are not the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0009","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0010","text":"Moreover, nothing is subjected to itself. But person is subjected to essence, whence it is named supposit or hypostasis. Therefore person is not the same as essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0011","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in VII *On the Trinity*, *when we say the person of the Father, we say nothing other than the substance of the Father*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0013","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0014","text":"I answer that, for those considering the divine simplicity, this question has its truth manifestly. For it was shown above that divine simplicity requires this: that in God essence and supposit are the same; and in intellectual substances the latter is nothing other than person. But the fact that, when the divine persons are multiplied, the essence retains unity seems to introduce a difficulty. And because, as Boethius says, relation multiplies the Trinity of persons, some held that essence and person differ in divine matters in the way in which they also said relations are assistant, considering in relations only that they are toward another, and not that they are things. But, as was shown above, just as relations are present accidentally in created things, so in God they are the divine essence itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0015","text":"From this it follows that in God essence is not other than person in reality; and yet that the persons are really distinguished from one another. For person, as was said above, signifies relation insofar as it subsists in the divine nature. Relation, however, when compared with essence, does not differ in reality but only in account; but when compared with an opposed relation, it has, by the force of opposition, a real distinction. And thus one essence and three persons remain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0016","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in creatures there cannot be a distinction of supposits through relations, but it must be through essential principles, because relations are not subsistent in creatures. In divine matters, however, relations are subsistent, and therefore, insofar as they have opposition to one another, they can distinguish supposits. Yet the essence is not distinguished, because the relations themselves are not distinguished from one another insofar as they are really the same as the essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0018","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0019","text":"To the second it must be said that, insofar as essence and person in divine matters differ according to the account of understanding, it follows that something can be affirmed of one which is denied of the other, and consequently that, upon one being supposed, the other is not supposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0020","text":"[I q. 39 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0021","text":"To the third it must be said that we impose names upon divine things according to the mode of created things, as was said above. And because the natures of created things are individuated through matter, which is subjected to the nature of the species, individuals are therefore called subjects, or supposits, or hypostases. And for this reason the divine persons too are called supposits or hypostases, not because there is there any supposition or subjection in reality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0023","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that it should not be said that the three persons are of one essence. For Hilary says, in the book *On the Synods*, that the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit *are indeed three according to substance, but one according to concord*. But the substance of God is His essence. Therefore the three persons are not of one essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0025","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0026","text":"Moreover, nothing is to be affirmed concerning divine things which is not expressed by the authority of Sacred Scripture, as is clear from Dionysius, chapter 1 of *On the Divine Names*. But it is never expressed in Sacred Scripture that the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit are of one essence. Therefore, this is not to be asserted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0027","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0028","text":"Moreover, the divine nature is the same as the essence. Therefore, it would suffice to say that the three persons are of one nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0029","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0030","text":"Moreover, it is not customary to say that a person is of an essence, but rather that an essence is of a person. Therefore, neither does it seem fittingly said that the three persons are of one essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0031","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0032","text":"Moreover, Augustine says that we do not say that three persons are from one essence, lest it be understood that in divine things essence and person are different. But just as prepositions are transitive, so also are oblique cases. Therefore, by the same reasoning, it should not be said that the three persons are of one essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0033","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0034","text":"Moreover, that which can be an occasion of error ought not to be said of divine things. But when the three persons are said to be of one essence or substance, an occasion of error is given. For, as Hilary says, in the book *On the Synods*, *one substance of Father and Son, when proclaimed, signifies either one subsisting person who has two appellations; or that one divided substance has made two imperfect substances; or a third, prior substance, which has been both appropriated and assumed by the two*. Therefore, it should not be said that the three persons are of one substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0035","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#29999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0036","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in book II *Against Maximinus*, that this term *homousion*, which was established at the Council of Nicaea against the Arians, signifies the same thing as that the three persons are of one essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0037","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0038","text":"I answer that, as was said above, our intellect names divine things not according to their mode—for it cannot know them in that way—but according to the mode found in created things. And because in sensible things, from which our intellect derives knowledge, the nature of a species is individuated through matter, and thus nature is related as form, while the individual is related as the supposit of the form, for this reason also in divine things, as regards the mode of signifying, essence is signified as the form of the three persons. Now in created things we say that any form whatsoever is of that of which it is the form, as the health or beauty of some man. But we do not say that a thing having a form is of the form, unless with the addition of some adjective designating that form, as when we say, this woman is of excellent form; this man is of perfect virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0039","text":"And similarly, because in divine things, when the persons are multiplied, the essence is not multiplied, we say that one essence is of the three persons, and that the three persons are of one essence, so that these genitives are understood to be construed in the designation of form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0040","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0041","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that substance is taken for hypostasis, and not for essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0042","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0043","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although that the three persons are of one essence is not found in Sacred Scripture in these words, it is nevertheless found as to this meaning, as in the passage, *I and the Father are one*; and, *I am in the Father, and the Father is in me*. And the same can be obtained through many other passages.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0044","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0045","text":"To the third, it must be said that, because nature designates a principle of action, whereas essence is so called from being, some things which agree in some action can be called of one nature, as all things heating; but they cannot be called of one essence unless theirs is one being. And therefore divine unity is expressed more fully by saying that the three persons are of one essence than if it were said that they are of one nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0046","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0047","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that form, taken absolutely, is customarily signified as of that of which it is the form, as Peter's virtue. Conversely, however, a thing having some form is not customarily signified as of it, except when we wish to determine or designate the form. And then two genitives are required, one of which signifies the form and the other the determination of the form, as if it is said, Peter is of great virtue; or else one genitive having the force of two genitives is required, as when it is said, this man is a man of bloods, that is, a shedder of much blood. Therefore, because the divine essence is signified as form in relation to person, the essence of a person is fittingly said. But not conversely, unless something be added for the designation of the essence; as if it were said that the Father is a person of the divine essence, or that the three persons are of one essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0048","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0049","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that this preposition *from* or *of* does not designate the relation of a formal cause, but rather the relation of an efficient or material cause. These causes are in every case distinguished from those of which they are causes, for nothing is its own matter, nor is anything its own active principle. Yet something is its own form, as is clear in all immaterial things. And therefore, by saying that the three persons are of one essence, signifying the essence under the relation of form, it is not shown that essence is other than person; which would be shown if we were to say that the three persons are from the same essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0050","text":"[I q. 39 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0051","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that, as Hilary says in the book *On the Synods*, *holy things are wrongly prejudged if, because they are not held holy by some, they ought not to be. Thus, if homoousion is badly understood, what is that to me who understand it well? Let there therefore be one substance from the property of the begotten nature, but not from proportion, or union, or communion*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0052","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0053","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0054","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that essential names, such as this name God, are not predicated singularly of the three persons, but plurally. For just as man is signified as having humanity, so God is signified as having deity. But the three persons are three having deity. Therefore the three persons are three gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0055","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0056","text":"Further, in Gen. I, where it is said, *In the beginning God created heaven and earth*, the Hebrew text has Elohim, which can be interpreted gods, or judges. And this is said on account of the plurality of persons. Therefore the three persons are several gods, and not one God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0057","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0058","text":"Further, this name thing, when it is said absolutely, seems to pertain to substance. But this name is predicated plurally of the three persons; for Augustine says, in the book *On Christian Doctrine*, *the things to be enjoyed are the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit*. Therefore other essential names too can be predicated plurally of the three persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0059","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0060","text":"Further, just as this name God signifies one having deity, so this name person signifies one subsisting in some intellectual nature. But we say three persons. Therefore, for the same reason, we can say three gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0061","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0062","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Deut. VI, *Hear, O Israel, the Lord thy God, God is one*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0063","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0064","text":"I answer that among essential names, some signify the essence substantively, while others signify it adjectivally. Those indeed which signify the essence substantively are predicated of the three persons only in the singular, and not in the plural; whereas those which signify the essence adjectivally are predicated of the three persons in the plural. The reason for this is that substantive names signify something in the manner of a substance, whereas adjective names signify something in the manner of an accident, which inheres in a subject. But substance, just as it has being through itself, so it has unity or multitude through itself; whence also the singularity or plurality of a substantive name is considered according to the form signified by the name. Accidents, however, just as they have being in a subject, so they receive unity and multitude from the subject; and therefore in adjectives singularity and plurality are considered according to the supposita.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0065","text":"But in creatures, one form is not found in several supposita except by a unity of order, as the form of an ordered multitude. Hence names signifying such a form, if they are substantive, are predicated of several in the singular, but not if they are adjective. For we say that many men are a college, or an army, or a people; yet we say that several men are collegiates. But in divine things the divine essence is signified in the manner of a form, as has been said; and it is simple and most one, as was shown above. Hence names signifying the divine essence substantively are predicated of the three persons in the singular, and not in the plural.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0066","text":"This, then, is the reason why we call Socrates and Plato and Cicero three men; but we do not call the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit three gods, but one God: because in the three supposita of human nature there are three humanities, whereas in the three persons there is one divine essence. But those which signify the essence adjectivally are predicated plurally of the three, on account of the plurality of supposita. For we say three existing, or three wise, or three eternal and uncreated and immense, if these are taken adjectivally. But if they are taken substantively, we say one uncreated, immense, and eternal, as Athanasius says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0067","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0068","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although God signifies one having deity, nevertheless there is a different mode of signifying: for God is said substantively, but having deity is said adjectivally. Hence, although there are three having deity, it nevertheless does not follow that there are three gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0069","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0070","text":"To the second, it must be said that different languages have different manners of speaking. Hence, just as on account of the plurality of supposita the Greeks say three hypostases, so also in Hebrew Elohim is said in the plural. But we do not say either gods or substances in the plural, lest plurality be referred to substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0071","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0072","text":"To the third, it must be said that this name thing is among the transcendentals. Hence, insofar as it pertains to relation, it is predicated plurally in divine things; but insofar as it pertains to substance, it is predicated singularly. Hence Augustine says in the same place that *the same Trinity is a certain supreme thing*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0073","text":"[I q. 39 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0074","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the form signified by this name “person” is not essence or nature, but personality. Hence, since there are three personalities, that is, three personal properties, in the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit, it is predicated of the three not in the singular but in the plural.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0075","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0076","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0077","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that concrete essential names cannot stand for a person, such that this is true: God begot God. For, as the sophists say, a singular term signifies and stands for the same thing. But this name “God” seems to be a singular term, since it cannot be predicated in the plural, as has been said. Therefore, since it signifies the essence, it seems that it stands for the essence and not for a person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0078","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0079","text":"Further, a term placed in the subject is not restricted by a term placed in the predicate, by reason of signification, but only by reason of the time connoted. But when I say, “God creates,” this name “God” stands for the essence. Therefore, when it is said, “God begot,” this term “God” cannot, by reason of the notional predicate, stand for a person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0080","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0081","text":"Further, if this is true, “God begot,” because the Father begets, by the same reasoning this will be true, “God does not beget,” because the Son does not beget. Therefore, there is a God begetting and a God not begetting. And thus it seems to follow that there are two gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0082","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0083","text":"Further, if God begot God, then he begot either himself as God, or another God. But not himself as God, because, as Augustine says in On the Trinity I, *no thing generates itself*. Nor another God, because there is only one God. Therefore this is false: God begot God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0084","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0085","text":"Further, if God begot God, then he begot either God who is God the Father, or God who is not God the Father. If God who is God the Father, then God the Father is begotten. If God who is not God the Father, then there is God who is not God the Father, which is false. Therefore it cannot be said that God begot God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0086","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0087","text":"On the contrary, in the Creed it is said: God from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0088","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0089","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have said that this name “God,” and similar names, properly, according to their nature, stand for the essence, but are drawn by an added notional term to stand for a person. And this opinion seems to have proceeded from consideration of the divine simplicity, which requires that in God that which has and that which is had are the same; and thus that which has deity, which this name “God” signifies, is the same as deity. But in the properties of expressions, not only the thing signified must be considered, but also the mode of signifying. And therefore, because this name “God” signifies the divine essence as in one possessing it, just as this name “man” signifies humanity in a supposit; others have said better that this name “God,” from its mode of signifying, is such that it can properly stand for a person, just as this name “man” can.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0090","text":"Therefore, sometimes this name “God” stands for the essence, as when it is said, “God creates,” because this predicate belongs to the subject by reason of the signified form, which is deity. But sometimes it stands for a person: for one person only, as when it is said, “God begets”; or for two, as when it is said, “God spirates”; or for three, as when it is said, *to the King of the ages, immortal, invisible, to the only God*, etc., 1 Tim. 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0091","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0092","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that this name “God,” although it agrees with singular terms in this, that the signified form is not multiplied, nevertheless agrees with common terms in this, that the signified form is found in several supposits. Hence it need not always stand for the essence which it signifies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0093","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0094","text":"To the second, it must be said that that objection proceeds against those who said that this name “God” does not have a natural supposition for a person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0095","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0096","text":"To the third, it must be said that this name “God” and this name “man” are related differently with respect to standing for a person. For since the form signified by this name “man,” that is, humanity, is really divided among diverse supposits, it stands of itself for a person, even if nothing is added that determines it to a person, which is a distinct supposit. But the unity or community of human nature is not according to reality, but only according to consideration; hence this term “man” does not stand for the common nature except because of the requirement of something added, as when it is said, “Man is a species.” But the form signified by this name “God,” namely, the divine essence, is one and common according to reality. Hence it stands of itself for the common nature, but by something added its supposition is determined to a person. Hence when it is said, “God begets,” by reason of the notional act this name “God” stands for the person of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0097","text":"But when it is said, “God does not generate,” nothing is added that determines this name to the person of the Son, whence it is given to understand that generation is repugnant to the divine nature. But if something pertaining to the person of the Son is added, the statement will be true; as if it is said, “God begotten does not generate.” Hence neither does it follow that there is a generating God and a non-generating God, unless something pertaining to the persons is posited; for example, if we say, “the Father is generating God, and the Son is non-generating God.” And thus it does not follow that there are several gods, because the Father and the Son are one God, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0098","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0099","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that this is false: “the Father begot himself God,” because *himself*, being reciprocal, refers to the same supposit. Nor is what Augustine says, namely, that God the Father begot another himself, contrary to this. For *himself* is either in the ablative case, so that the sense is: he begot another from himself. Or it makes a simple relation, and thus refers to identity of nature; but it is an improper or emphatic expression, so that the sense is: he begot another most like himself. Similarly, this too is false: “he begot another God.” For although the Son is other than the Father, as was said above, nevertheless it should not be said that he is another God, because it would be understood that this adjective *another* posits its own thing concerning the substantive “God”; and thus a distinction of deity would be signified. Some nevertheless concede this statement, “he begot another God,” such that *another* is a substantive and *God* is construed appositively with it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0100","text":"But this is an improper manner of speaking, and is to be avoided, lest an occasion of error be given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0101","text":"[I q. 39 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0102","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that this is false: “God begot God who is God the Father,” because, since *Father* is construed appositively with *God*, it restricts it to standing for the person of the Father; so that the sense is: he begot God who is the Father himself, and thus the Father would be begotten, which is false. Hence the negative is true: “he begot God who is not God the Father.” If, however, the construction were understood not to be appositive, but that something must be interposed, then conversely the affirmative would be true and the negative false; so that the sense is: he begot God who is God who is Father. But this is a forced exposition. Hence it is better that the affirmative simply be denied and the negative granted. Praepositinus, however, said that both the negative and the affirmative are false. For this relative *who* in the affirmative can refer to the supposit, but in the negative it refers both to the signified and to the supposit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0103","text":"Hence the sense of the affirmative is that being God the Father belongs to the person of the Son. But the sense of the negative is that being God the Father is removed not only from the person of the Son, but also from his divinity; but this seems unreasonable, since, according to the Philosopher, there can also be a negation concerning the same thing concerning which there is an affirmation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0104","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0105","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0106","text":"We proceed to the fifth thus. It seems that essential names signified in the abstract can stand for a person, such that this is true: “essence generates essence.” For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, VII: *the Father and the Son are one wisdom, because [they are] one essence; and, taken singly, wisdom from wisdom, just as essence from essence*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0107","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0108","text":"Further, when we are generated or corrupted, those things which are in us are generated or corrupted. But the Son is generated. Therefore, since the divine essence is in the Son, it seems that the divine essence is generated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0109","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0110","text":"Further, God and the divine essence are the same, as is clear from what has been said above. But this is true: “God generates God,” as has been said. Therefore this is true: “essence generates essence.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0111","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0112","text":"Further, whatever something is predicated of, it can stand for that thing. But the divine essence is the Father. Therefore essence can stand for the person of the Father. And thus essence generates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0113","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0114","text":"Further, essence is a generating thing, because it is the Father, who generates. If, therefore, essence is not generating, essence will be a generating and non-generating thing, which is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0115","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0116","text":"Further, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, IV: *the Father is the principle of the whole deity*. But he is not a principle except by generating or spirating. Therefore the Father generates or spirates the deity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0117","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0118","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, I, that *no thing generates itself*. But if essence generates essence, it generates nothing but itself, since there is nothing in God that is distinct from the divine essence. Therefore essence does not generate essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0119","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0120","text":"I answer that it must be said that Abbot Joachim erred concerning this, asserting that, just as it is said, “God begot God,” so it can be said that essence begot essence; considering that, on account of the divine simplicity, God is not other than the divine essence. But in this he was deceived, because for the truth of expressions one must consider not only the things signified, but also the mode of signifying, as has been said. Although, in reality, God is the same as deity, nevertheless the mode of signifying is not the same in both cases. For this name “God,” because it signifies the divine essence as in one possessing it, naturally has from the mode of its signification that it can stand for a person; and thus those things which are proper to the persons can be predicated of this name “God,” so that it is said that God is begotten or generating, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0121","text":"But the name essence does not, by the mode of its signification, have it that it stands for a person, because it signifies essence as an abstract form. And therefore those things which are proper to the persons, by which they are distinguished from one another, cannot be attributed to the essence; for it would be signified that there is a distinction in the divine essence, just as there is a distinction in the supposita.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0122","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0123","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, in order to express the unity of essence and person, the holy doctors have sometimes spoken more expressly than the propriety of speech permits. Hence expressions of this kind are not to be extended, but explained: namely, so that abstract names are explained through concrete names, or even through personal names; thus, when it is said, essence from essence, or wisdom from wisdom, the sense is: the Son, who is essence and wisdom, is from the Father, who is essence and wisdom. Yet among these abstract names a certain order must be observed, because those which pertain to act are more closely related to the persons, since acts belong to supposita. Hence, nature from nature, or wisdom from wisdom, is less improper than essence from essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0124","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0125","text":"To the second it must be said that, in creatures, that which is generated does not receive the same nature in number as that which generates has, but one other in number, which begins to be in it anew through generation and ceases to be through corruption; and therefore it is generated and corrupted accidentally. But God begotten receives the same nature in number as that which begets has. And therefore the divine nature in the Son is generated neither per se nor accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0126","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0127","text":"To the third it must be said that, although God and the divine essence are the same in reality, nevertheless, by reason of a different mode of signifying, one must speak differently concerning each.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0128","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0129","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the divine essence is predicated of the Father by way of identity, on account of divine simplicity; yet it does not follow that it can stand for the Father, on account of the different mode of signifying. But the argument would proceed in those things of which one is predicated of the other as a universal of a particular.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0130","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0131","text":"To the fifth it must be said that this is the difference between substantive and adjective names: substantive names carry their suppositum, whereas adjectives do not, but place the thing signified around a substantive. Hence the sophists say that substantive names supposit; adjectives, however, do not supposit but conjoin. Therefore substantive personal names can be predicated of the essence, on account of identity of thing; nor does it follow that a personal property determines a distinct essence, but it is placed around the suppositum conveyed by the substantive name. But notional and personal adjectives cannot be predicated of the essence unless some substantive is added. Hence we cannot say that essence is generating. We can, however, say that essence is a generating thing, or God generating, if thing and God stand for a person, but not if they stand for the essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0132","text":"Hence there is no contradiction if it is said that essence is a generating thing, and a non-generating thing, because in the first instance thing is taken for a person, in the second for essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0133","text":"[I q. 39 a. 5 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0134","text":"To the sixth it must be said that deity, insofar as it is one in several supposita, has a certain likeness to the form of a collective noun. Hence, when it is said, the Father is the principle of the whole deity, it can be taken for the totality of the persons; namely, insofar as, in all the divine persons, he is the principle. Nor need he be the principle of himself, just as someone from the people is called ruler of the whole people, yet not of himself. Or it can be said that he is the principle of the whole deity, not because he generates and spirates it, but because, by generating and spirating, he communicates it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0135","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0136","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0137","text":"The sixth is approached thus. It seems that the persons cannot be predicated of concrete essential names, so that it may be said, God is three persons, or is the Trinity. For this is false: man is every man, because it cannot be verified of any of the supposita; for neither is Socrates every man, nor Plato, nor anyone else. But likewise this, God is the Trinity, cannot be verified of any of the supposita of the divine nature; for neither is the Father the Trinity, nor the Son, nor the Holy Spirit. Therefore this is false: God is the Trinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0138","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0139","text":"Moreover, inferiors are not predicated of their superiors except by accidental predication, as when I say, an animal is a man; for it is accidental to an animal to be a man. But this name God is related to the three persons as a common term to inferiors, as Damascene says. Therefore it seems that the names of the persons cannot be predicated of this name God except accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0140","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0141","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in his sermon on faith, *we believe that one God is one Trinity of the divine name*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0142","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0143","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as has already been said, although personal names or notional adjectives cannot be predicated of the essence, nevertheless substantives can be, on account of the real identity of essence and person. Now the divine essence is not only really the same as one person, but as three. Hence both one person, and two, and three can be predicated of the essence; as if we were to say: the essence is Father and Son and Holy Spirit. And because this name God of itself has it that it stands for the essence, as has been said, therefore, just as this is true, the essence is three persons, so this is true, God is three persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0144","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0145","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, as was stated above, this name “man” of itself has supposition for a person; but from something added it has that it stands for the common nature. And therefore this is false: man is every man, because it can be verified of no supposit. But this name “God” of itself has that it stands for the essence. Hence, although this is true of none of the supposit(s) of the divine nature, God is Trinity, it is nevertheless true of the essence. Porretanus, not attending to this, denied it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0146","text":"[I q. 39 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0147","text":"To the second it must be said that, when it is said, “God,” or “the divine essence,” “is Father,” the predication is through identity, and not as that of an inferior of a superior, because in divine things there is no universal and singular. Hence, just as this proposition, “the Father is God,” is per se, so also is this, “God is Father”; and in no way accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0148","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0149","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0150","text":"The seventh is approached thus. It seems that essential names should not be appropriated to the persons. For that which can tend toward an error of faith must be avoided in divine matters, because, as Jerome says, *heresy is incurred from words uttered without order*. But to appropriate to one person things that are common to the three persons can tend toward an error of faith, because it can be understood either that they belong only to that person to whom they are appropriated, or that they belong to it more than to the others. Therefore essential attributes should not be appropriated to the persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0151","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0152","text":"Moreover, essential attributes, when signified abstractly, signify in the mode of a form. But one person is not related to another as form, since a form is not distinguished from that of which it is the form, namely, the supposit. Therefore essential attributes, especially when signified abstractly, ought not to be appropriated to the persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0153","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0154","text":"Moreover, what is proper is prior to what is appropriated, for what is proper belongs to the account of what is appropriated. But essential attributes, according to the mode of understanding, are prior to the persons, just as the common is prior to the proper. Therefore essential attributes ought not to be appropriated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0155","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0156","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, 1 Cor. 1, *Christ, the power of God and the wisdom of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0157","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0158","text":"I answer that it was fitting, for the manifestation of the faith, that essential attributes be appropriated to the persons. For although the Trinity of persons cannot be proved by demonstration, as was stated above, it is nevertheless fitting that it be declared through certain more manifest things. Now essential attributes are more manifest to us according to reason than are the properties of the persons, because from creatures, from which we receive knowledge, we can attain with certainty to knowledge of the essential attributes, but not to knowledge of the personal properties, as was stated above. Therefore, just as we use the likeness of a trace or image found in creatures for the manifestation of the divine persons, so also we use essential attributes. And this manifestation of the persons through essential attributes is called appropriation. Now the divine persons can be manifested through essential attributes in two ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0159","text":"In one way, by way of likeness: thus things pertaining to intellect are appropriated to the Son, who proceeds in the mode of intellect as Word. In another way, by way of unlikeness: thus power is appropriated to the Father, as Augustine says, because among us fathers are customarily infirm on account of old age, lest we suspect anything such in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0160","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0161","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that essential attributes are not so appropriated to the persons as to be asserted to be proper to them, but in order to manifest the persons by way of likeness or unlikeness, as has been said. Hence no error of faith follows, but rather a manifestation of the truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0162","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0163","text":"To the second it must be said that, if essential attributes were so appropriated to the persons that they were proper to them, it would follow that one person was related to another in the relation of form. Augustine excludes this in *On the Trinity*, VII, showing that the Father is not wise by the wisdom that he begot, as though the Son alone were wisdom, such that Father and Son together alone could be called wise, but not the Father without the Son. But the Son is called the wisdom of the Father because he is wisdom from the Father who is wisdom; for each is wisdom through himself, and together both are one wisdom. Hence the Father is not wise by the wisdom that he begot, but by the wisdom that is his essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0164","text":"[I q. 39 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0165","text":"To the third it must be said that, although an essential attribute, according to its proper account, is prior to a person according to the mode of understanding, nevertheless, insofar as it has the account of something appropriated, nothing prevents what is proper to a person from being prior to what is appropriated. Thus color is posterior to body insofar as it is body, yet it is naturally prior to a white body insofar as it is white.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0166","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0167","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0168","text":"The eighth is approached thus. It seems that essential attributes have been unsuitably attributed to the persons by the sacred doctors. For Hilary says, in *On the Trinity*, II: *eternity is in the Father, form in the image, use in the gift*. In these words he sets down three names proper to the persons: namely, the name Father; and the name Image, which is proper to the Son, as was stated above; and the name Gift, or Don, which is proper to the Holy Spirit, as was treated above. He also sets down three appropriated [attributes], for he appropriates eternity to the Father, form to the Son, and use to the Holy Spirit. And this seems unreasonable. For eternity implies duration of being, whereas form is a principle of being, and use seems to pertain to operation. But essence and operation are found to be appropriated to no person. Therefore these things seem unsuitably appropriated to the persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0169","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0170","text":"Moreover, Augustine, in Book I of *On Christian Doctrine*, says thus: *in the Father is unity, in the Son equality, in the Holy Spirit the harmony of unity and equality.* And this seems unsuitable. For one person is not formally denominated by that which is appropriated to another; for the Father is not wise by the begotten wisdom, as has been stated. But, as he adds in the same place, *these three are all one on account of the Father, all equal on account of the Son, all connected on account of the Holy Spirit.* Therefore they are not suitably appropriated to the persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0171","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0172","text":"Likewise, according to Augustine, power is attributed to the Father, wisdom to the Son, goodness to the Holy Spirit. And this seems unsuitable. For virtue pertains to power. But virtue is found to be appropriated to the Son, according to I Cor. I, *Christ, the power of God*; and also to the Holy Spirit, according to Luke VI, *power went out from him and healed all.* Therefore power is not to be appropriated to the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0173","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0174","text":"Likewise, Augustine, in the book *On the Trinity*, says: *It must not be understood confusedly what the Apostle says, from him, and through him, and in him—saying from him on account of the Father; through him on account of the Son; in him on account of the Holy Spirit.* But this seems unsuitable. For by his saying “in him,” a relation of final cause seems to be implied, which is the first of causes. Therefore this relation of cause ought to be appropriated to the Father, who is principle not from a principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0175","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0176","text":"Likewise, truth is found to be appropriated to the Son, according to John XIV, *I am the way, the truth, and the life.* Likewise the book of life, according to Ps. XXXIX, *at the head of the book it is written concerning me*—the Gloss: that is, with the Father, who is my head. And likewise this expression, “he who is,” because on Isa. LXV, *behold, I [am] to the nations*, the Gloss says: *the Son speaks, who said to Moses, I am who I am.* But these seem to be proper to the Son, and not appropriated. For *truth*, according to Augustine in the book *On True Religion*, *is the supreme likeness of the principle, without any dissimilarity*; and thus it seems properly to belong to the Son, who has a principle. The book of life likewise seems to be something proper, because it signifies being from another; for every book is written by someone. This very expression, “he who is,” likewise seems proper to the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0177","text":"For if, when it is said to Moses, “I am who I am,” the Trinity speaks, then Moses could say, “He who is Father and Son and Holy Spirit has sent me to you.” Therefore he could further say, “He who is Father and Son and Holy Spirit has sent me to you,” while pointing to a definite person. But this is false, because no person is Father and Son and Holy Spirit. Therefore it cannot be common to the Trinity, but is proper to the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0178","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0179","text":"I answer that it must be said that our intellect, which is led by creatures into the knowledge of God, must consider God according to the mode that it takes from creatures. But in the consideration of any creature, four things occur to us in order. For first, the thing itself is considered absolutely, insofar as it is a certain being. The second consideration of the thing is insofar as it is one. The third consideration of the thing is according as there is in it a power for acting and causing. The fourth consideration of the thing is according to the relation that it has to things caused. Hence this fourfold consideration also occurs to us concerning God. Therefore, according to the first consideration, by which God is considered absolutely according to his being, Hilary's appropriation is taken, according to which eternity is appropriated to the Father, species to the Son, and use to the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0180","text":"For eternity, insofar as it signifies unoriginated being, has a likeness to what is proper to the Father, who is principle not from a principle. But species, or beauty, has a likeness to what is proper to the Son. For three things are required for beauty. First, integrity or perfection; for things that are diminished are, by that very fact, ugly. And due proportion or harmony. And again, clarity; hence those which have a shining color are said to be beautiful. Therefore, as to the first, it has a likeness to what is proper to the Son, insofar as the Son has in himself the nature of the Father truly and perfectly. Hence, to indicate this, Augustine in his exposition says, where, namely in the Son, is supreme and first life, and so forth. But as to the second, it agrees with what is proper to the Son insofar as he is the expressive image of the Father. Hence we see that an image is said to be beautiful if it perfectly represents the thing, even though that thing is ugly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0181","text":"And Augustine touched upon this when he says, where there is so great conformity and first equality, and so forth. But as to the third, it agrees with what is proper to the Son insofar as he is the Word, which indeed is light and the splendor of the intellect, as Damascene says. And Augustine touches upon this when he says, *as a perfect word to which nothing is lacking, and a certain art of almighty God,* and so forth. But use has a likeness to what is proper to the Holy Spirit, taking “use” in a broad sense, according as using includes under itself even enjoying; insofar as to use is to take something into the faculty of the will, and to enjoy is to use with joy, as Augustine says in Book X of *On the Trinity*. Therefore, the use by which the Father and the Son enjoy one another agrees with what is proper to the Holy Spirit, insofar as he is love. And this is what Augustine says: *that love, delight, happiness or blessedness, was called use by him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0182","text":"But the use by which we enjoy God has a likeness to what is proper to the Holy Spirit, insofar as he is gift. And Augustine shows this when he says: *there is in the Trinity the Holy Spirit, the sweetness of begetter and begotten, bathing us in immense generosity and abundance.* And thus it is clear why eternity, species, and use are attributed or appropriated to the persons, but not essence or operation. For in the notion of these, because of their commonness, nothing is found that has a likeness to what is proper to the persons. But the second consideration of God is insofar as he is considered as one. And thus Augustine appropriates unity to the Father, equality to the Son, and harmony or connection to the Holy Spirit. These three plainly signify unity, but differently. For unity is said absolutely, not presupposing something else.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0183","text":"And therefore it is appropriated to the Father, who does not presuppose any person, since he is principle not from a principle. But equality implies unity in relation to another; for equal is that which has one quantity with another. And therefore equality is appropriated to the Son, who is principle from a principle. But connection implies the unity of two things. Hence it is appropriated to the Holy Spirit, insofar as he is from two. From this one may also understand what Augustine says: *the three are one on account of the Father, equal on account of the Son, connected on account of the Holy Spirit.* For it is clear that each thing is attributed to that in which it is first found, just as all lower things are said to live on account of the vegetative soul, in which the notion of life is first found in these lower things. But unity is found at once in the person of the Father, even if, per impossibile, the other persons are removed. And therefore the other persons have unity from the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0184","text":"But if the other persons are removed, equality is not found in the Father; rather, as soon as the Son is posited, equality is found. And therefore all are said to be equal on account of the Son, not because the Son is the principle of equality to the Father, but because, unless there were a Son equal to the Father, the Father could not be called equal. For his equality is first considered in relation to the Son; indeed, the very fact that the Holy Spirit is equal to the Father he has from the Son. Similarly, if the Holy Spirit, who is the bond of the two, were excluded, the unity of connection between the Father and Son could not be understood. And therefore all are said to be connected on account of the Holy Spirit, because, once the Holy Spirit is posited, there is that by which the Father and Son can be called connected. But according to the third consideration, by which sufficient power in God for causing is considered, the third appropriation is taken: namely, of power, wisdom, and goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0185","text":"This appropriation is made both according to the notion of likeness, if what is in the divine persons is considered, and according to the notion of unlikeness, if what is in creatures is considered. For power has the character of a principle. Hence it has a likeness to the heavenly Father, who is the principle of all divinity. But it sometimes fails in the earthly father, on account of old age. Wisdom, however, has a likeness to the heavenly Son, insofar as he is the Word, which is nothing other than the concept of wisdom. But it sometimes fails in the earthly son, on account of the shortness of time. Goodness, however, since it is the principle and object of love, has a likeness to the divine Spirit, who is love. But it seems to have an incompatibility with the earthly spirit, insofar as it implies a certain violent impulse; as it is said in Isa. XXV, *the spirit of the strong is as a whirlwind driving against a wall*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0186","text":"But virtue is appropriated to the Son and the Holy Spirit, not insofar as virtue is called the very power of a thing, but insofar as virtue is sometimes called that which proceeds from the power of a thing, as when we say that some virtuous deed is the virtue of an agent. But according to the fourth consideration, insofar as God is considered in relation to his effects, that appropriation is taken: from whom, through whom, and in whom. For this preposition *from* sometimes implies a relation of material cause, which has no place in divine things; but sometimes a relation of efficient cause. This belongs to God by reason of his active power; hence it is appropriated to the Father, as power also is. But this preposition *through* sometimes designates an intermediate cause, as when we say that a craftsman works through a hammer. And thus *through* is sometimes not appropriated, but proper to the Son, according to John","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0187","text":"I: *all things were made through him*; not because the Son is an instrument, but because he is principle from a principle. But sometimes it designates a relation of the form through which the agent works, as when we say that an artisan works through art. Hence, just as wisdom and art are appropriated to the Son, so too is *through whom*. But this preposition *in* properly denotes a relation of what contains. Now God contains things in two ways. In one way, according to their likenesses; namely, insofar as things are said to be in God, inasmuch as they are in his knowledge. And thus the expression *in him* would be appropriated to the Son. In another way, things are contained by God insofar as God, by his goodness, preserves and governs them, bringing them to a fitting end. And thus *in whom* is appropriated to the Holy Spirit, as goodness also is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0188","text":"Nor need the relation of final cause, although it is the first of causes, be appropriated to the Father, who is principle not from a principle, because the divine persons, of whom the Father is the principle, do not proceed as toward an end, since each of them is the ultimate end; but by a natural procession, which seems rather to pertain to the notion of natural power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0189","text":"[I q. 39 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0190","text":"But as to that which is asked concerning the other matters, it must be said that truth, since it pertains to intellect, as stated above, is appropriated to the Son, yet is not proper to him. For truth, as stated above, can be considered insofar as it is in the intellect, or insofar as it is in the thing. Therefore, just as intellect and thing, taken essentially, are essential and not personal, so also is truth. But Augustine's definition cited is given of truth insofar as it is appropriated to the Son. The book of life, however, directly implies knowledge, but indirectly life; for it is, as stated above, God's knowledge of those who will have eternal life. Hence it is appropriated to the Son, although life is appropriated to the Holy Spirit, insofar as it implies a certain interior motion, and thus agrees with what is proper to the Holy Spirit, insofar as he is love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0191","text":"But being written by another does not belong to the notion of a book insofar as it is a book, but insofar as it is an artifact. Hence it does not imply origin, nor is it personal, but appropriated to a person. But “he who is” itself is appropriated to the person of the Son, not according to its proper notion, but by reason of something joined to it, namely, insofar as in God's speech to Moses the liberation of the human race, which was accomplished through the Son, was prefigured. Yet insofar as *who* is taken relatively, it could sometimes refer to the person of the Son, and thus would be taken personally; for example, if it is said, “the Son is begotten who is”; just as “begotten God” is personal. But when taken indefinitely, it is essential.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0192","text":"And although this pronoun *this*, grammatically speaking, seems to pertain to some definite person, nevertheless anything demonstrable, grammatically speaking, can be called a person, although according to the nature of the thing it is not a person; for we say, “this stone” and “this ass.” Hence, grammatically speaking, the divine essence, insofar as it is signified and stands for by this name God, can be pointed out by this pronoun *this*, according to Exod. XV: *this is my God, and I shall glorify him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_31:P0192"]}],"source_digest":"45adf80eac456cf4bc316191cd16345aa2b1c84aa8a64a6101e8907e1fb68852","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 39"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 39","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32","title":"First Part · Question 40","order":41,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_32","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 40 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30064)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de personis in comparatione ad relationes sive proprietates. Et quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum relatio sit idem quod persona. Secundo, utrum relationes distinguant et constituant personas. Tertio, utrum, abstractis per intellectum relationibus a personis, remaneant hypostases distinctae. Quarto, utrum relationes, secundum intellectum, praesupponant actus personarum, vel e converso.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30065)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in divinis non sit idem relatio quod persona. Quaecumque enim sunt idem, multiplicato uno eorum, multiplicatur et aliud. Sed contingit in una persona esse plures relationes, sicut in persona patris est paternitas et communis spiratio, et iterum unam relationem in duabus personis esse, sicut communis spiratio est in patre et filio. Ergo relatio non est idem quod persona.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30066)\n\nPraeterea, nihil est in seipso, secundum philosophum, in IV Physic. Sed relatio est in persona. Nec potest dici quod ratione identitatis, quia sic esset etiam in essentia. Ergo relatio sive proprietas et persona non sunt idem in divinis.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30067)\n\nPraeterea, quaecumque sunt idem, ita se habent, quod quidquid praedicatur de uno, praedicatur et de alio. Non autem quidquid praedicatur de persona, praedicatur de proprietate. Dicimus enim quod pater generat, sed non dicimus quod paternitas sit generans. Ergo proprietas non est idem quod persona in divinis.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30068)\n\nSed contra, in divinis non differt quod est et quo est, ut habetur a Boetio in libro de Hebd. Sed pater paternitate est pater. Ergo pater idem est quod paternitas. Et eadem ratione aliae proprietates idem sunt cum personis.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30069)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc aliqui diversimode opinati sunt. Quidam enim dixerunt proprietates neque esse personas, neque in personis. Qui fuerunt moti ex modo significandi relationum, quae quidem non significant ut in aliquo, sed magis ut ad aliquid. Unde dixerunt relationes esse assistentes, sicut supra expositum est. Sed quia relatio, secundum quod est quaedam res in divinis, est ipsa essentia; essentia autem idem est quod persona, ut ex dictis patet; oportet quod relatio sit idem quod persona. Hanc igitur identitatem alii considerantes, dixerunt proprietates quidem esse personas, non autem in personis, quia non ponebant proprietates in divinis nisi secundum modum loquendi, ut supra dictum est. Necesse est autem ponere proprietates in divinis, ut supra ostendimus. Quae quidem significantur in abstracto, ut quaedam formae personarum. Unde, cum de ratione formae sit, quod sit in eo cuius est forma, oportet dicere proprietates esse in personis, et eas tamen esse personas; sicut essentiam esse in Deo dicimus, quae tamen est Deus.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30070)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod persona et proprietas sunt idem re, differunt tamen secundum rationem. Unde non oportet quod, multiplicato uno, multiplicetur reliquum. Considerandum tamen est quod, propter divinam simplicitatem, consideratur duplex realis identitas in divinis eorum quae differunt in rebus creatis. Quia enim divina simplicitas excludit compositionem formae et materiae, sequitur quod in divinis idem est abstractum et concretum, ut deitas et Deus. Quia vero divina simplicitas excludit compositionem subiecti et accidentis, sequitur quod quidquid attribuitur Deo, est eius essentia, et propter hoc sapientia et virtus idem sunt in Deo, quia ambo sunt in divina essentia. Et secundum hanc duplicem rationem identitatis, proprietas in divinis est idem cum persona. Nam proprietates personales sunt idem cum personis, ea ratione qua abstractum est idem cum concreto. Sunt enim ipsae personae subsistentes; ut paternitas est ipse pater, et filiatio filius, et processio spiritus sanctus. Proprietates autem non personales sunt idem cum personis secundum aliam rationem identitatis, qua omne illud quod attribuitur Deo, est eius essentia. Sic igitur communis spiratio est idem cum persona patris et cum persona filii, non quod sit una persona per se subsistens; sed, sicut una essentia est in duabus personis, ita et una proprietas, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30071)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod proprietates dicuntur esse in essentia, per modum identitatis tantum. In personis autem dicuntur esse per modum identitatis, non quidem secundum rem tantum, sed quantum ad modum significandi, sicut forma in supposito. Et ideo proprietates determinant et distinguunt personas, non autem essentiam.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30072)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod participia et verba notionalia significant actus notionales. Actus autem suppositorum sunt. Proprietates autem non significantur ut supposita, sed ut formae suppositorum. Et ideo modus significandi repugnat, ut participia et verba notionalia de proprietatibus praedicentur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30073)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod personae non distinguantur per relationes. Simplicia enim seipsis distinguuntur. Sed personae divinae sunt maxime simplices. Ergo distinguuntur seipsis, et non relationibus.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30074)\n\nPraeterea, nulla forma distinguitur nisi secundum suum genus, non enim album a nigro distinguitur nisi secundum qualitatem. Sed hypostasis significat individuum in genere substantiae. Non ergo relationibus hypostases distingui possint.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30075)\n\nPraeterea, absolutum est prius quam relativum. Sed prima distinctio est distinctio divinarum personarum. Ergo divinae personae non distinguuntur relationibus.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30076)\n\nPraeterea, id quod praesupponit distinctionem, non potest esse primum distinctionis principium. Sed relatio praesupponit distinctionem, cum in eius definitione ponatur, esse enim relativi est ad aliud se habere. Ergo primum principium distinctivum in divinis non potest esse relatio.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30077)\n\nSed contra est quod Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin., quod sola relatio multiplicat Trinitatem divinarum personarum.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30078)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in quibuscumque pluribus invenitur aliquid commune, oportet quaerere aliquid distinctivum. Unde, cum tres personae conveniant secundum essentiae unitatem, necesse est quaerere aliquid quo distinguantur, ad hoc quod plures sint. Inveniuntur autem in divinis personis duo secundum quae differunt, scilicet origo, et relatio. Quae quidem quamvis re non differant, differunt tamen secundum modum significandi, nam origo significatur per modum actus, ut generatio; relatio vero per modum formae, ut paternitas. Quidam igitur, attendentes quod relatio consequitur actum, dixerunt quod hypostases in divinis distinguuntur per originem; ut dicamus quod pater distinguitur a filio, inquantum ille generat et hic est genitus. Relationes autem sive proprietates manifestant consequenter hypostasum sive personarum distinctiones, sicut et in creaturis proprietates manifestant distinctiones individuorum, quae fiunt per materialia principia. Sed hoc non potest stare, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia ad hoc quod aliqua duo distincta intelligantur, necesse est eorum distinctionem intelligi per aliquid intrinsecum utrique; sicut in rebus creatis vel per materiam, vel per formam. Origo autem alicuius rei non significatur ut aliquid intrinsecum, sed ut via quaedam a re vel ad rem, sicut generatio significatur ut via quaedam ad rem genitam, et ut progrediens a generante. Unde non potest esse quod res genita et generans distinguantur sola generatione, sed oportet intelligere tam in generante quam in genito ea quibus ab invicem distinguuntur. In persona autem divina non est aliud intelligere nisi essentiam et relationem sive proprietatem. Unde, cum in essentia conveniant, relinquitur quod per relationes personae ab invicem distinguantur. Secundo, quia distinctio in divinis personis non est sic intelligenda, quasi aliquid commune dividatur, quia essentia communis remanet indivisa, sed oportet quod ipsa distinguentia constituant res distinctas. Sic autem relationes vel proprietates distinguunt vel constituunt hypostases vel personas, inquantum sunt ipsae personae subsistentes, sicut paternitas est pater, et filiatio est filius, eo quod in divinis non differt abstractum et concretum. Sed contra rationem originis est, quod constituat hypostasim vel personam. Quia origo active significata, significatur ut progrediens a persona subsistente, unde praesupponit eam. Origo autem passive significata, ut nativitas, significatur ut via ad personam subsistentem; et nondum ut eam constituens. Unde melius dicitur quod personae seu hypostases distinguantur relationibus, quam per originem. Licet enim distinguantur utroque modo, tamen prius et principalius per relationes, secundum modum intelligendi. Unde hoc nomen pater non solum significat proprietatem, sed etiam hypostasim, sed hoc nomen genitor, vel generans, significat tantum proprietatem. Quia hoc nomen pater significat relationem, quae est distinctiva et constitutiva hypostasis, hoc autem nomen generans, vel genitus, significat originem, quae non est distinctiva et constitutiva hypostasis.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30079)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod personae sunt ipsae relationes subsistentes. Unde non repugnat simplicitati divinarum personarum, quod relationibus distinguantur.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30080)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod personae divinae non distinguuntur in esse in quo subsistunt, neque in aliquo absoluto, sed solum secundum id quod ad aliquid dicuntur. Unde ad earum distinctionem sufficit relatio.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30081)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quanto distinctio prior est, tanto propinquior est unitati. Et ideo debet esse minima. Et ideo distinctio personarum non debet esse nisi per id quod minimum distinguit, scilicet per relationem.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30082)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod relatio praesupponit distinctionem suppositorum, quando est accidens, sed si relatio sit subsistens, non praesupponit, sed secum fert distinctionem. Cum enim dicitur quod relativi esse est ad aliud se habere, per ly aliud intelligitur correlativum, quod non est prius, sed simul natura.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30083)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod, abstractis per intellectum proprietatibus seu relationibus a personis, adhuc remaneant hypostases. Id enim ad quod aliquid se habet ex additione, potest intelligi remoto eo quod sibi additur, sicut homo se habet ad animal ex additione, et potest intelligi animal remoto rationali. Sed persona se habet ex additione ad hypostasim, est enim persona hypostasis proprietate distincta ad dignitatem pertinente. Ergo, remota proprietate personali a persona, intelligitur hypostasis.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30084)\n\nPraeterea, pater non ab eodem habet quod sit pater, et quod sit aliquis. Cum enim paternitate sit pater, si paternitate esset aliquis, sequeretur quod filius, in quo non est paternitas, non esset aliquis. Remota ergo per intellectum paternitate a patre, adhuc remanet quod sit aliquis; quod est esse hypostasim. Ergo, remota proprietate a persona, remanet hypostasis.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30085)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, V de Trin., *non hoc est dicere ingenitum, quod est dicere patrem, quia etsi filium non genuisset, nihil prohiberet eum dicere ingenitum*. Sed si filium non genuisset, non inesset ei paternitas. Ergo, remota paternitate, adhuc remanet hypostasis patris ut ingenita.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30086)\n\nSed contra est quod Hilarius dicit, IV de Trin., *nihil habet filius nisi natum*. Nativitate autem est filius. Ergo, remota filiatione, non remanet hypostasis filii. Et eadem ratio est de aliis personis.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30087)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod duplex fit abstractio per intellectum. Una quidem, secundum quod universale abstrahitur a particulari, ut animal ab homine. Alia vero, secundum quod forma abstrahitur a materia; sicut forma circuli abstrahitur per intellectum ab omni materia sensibili. Inter has autem abstractiones haec est differentia, quod in abstractione quae fit secundum universale et particulare, non remanet id a quo fit abstractio, remota enim ab homine differentia rationali, non remanet in intellectu homo, sed solum animal. In abstractione vero quae attenditur secundum formam et materiam, utrumque manet in intellectu, abstrahendo enim formam circuli ab aere, remanet seorsum in intellectu nostro et intellectus circuli et intellectus aeris. Quamvis autem in divinis non sit universale neque particulare, nec forma et materia, secundum rem; tamen, secundum modum significandi, invenitur aliqua similitudo horum in divinis; secundum quem modum Damascenus dicit quod *commune est substantia, particulare vero hypostasis*. Si igitur loquamur de abstractione quae fit secundum universale et particulare, remotis proprietatibus, remanet in intellectu essentia communis, non autem hypostasis patris, quae est quasi particulare. Si vero loquamur secundum modum abstractionis formae a materia, remotis proprietatibus non personalibus, remanet intellectus hypostasum et personarum, sicut, remoto per intellectum a patre quod sit ingenitus vel spirans, remanet hypostasis vel persona patris. Sed remota proprietate personali per intellectum, tollitur intellectus hypostasis. Non enim proprietates personales sic intelliguntur advenire hypostasibus divinis, sicut forma subiecto praeexistenti, sed ferunt secum sua supposita, inquantum sunt ipsae personae subsistentes, sicut paternitas est ipse pater, hypostasis enim significat aliquid distinctum in divinis, cum hypostasis sit substantia individua. Cum igitur relatio sit quae distinguit hypostases et constituit eas, ut dictum est, relinquitur quod, relationibus personalibus remotis per intellectum, non remaneant hypostases. Sed, sicut dictum est, aliqui dicunt quod hypostases in divinis non distinguuntur per relationes, sed per solam originem; ut intelligatur pater esse hypostasis quaedam per hoc, quod non est ab alio; filius autem per hoc, quod est ab alio per generationem. Sed relationes advenientes quasi proprietates ad dignitatem pertinentes, constituunt rationem personae, unde et personalitates dicuntur. Unde, remotis huiusmodi relationibus per intellectum, remanent quidem hypostases, sed non personae. Sed hoc non potest esse, propter duo. Primo, quia relationes distinguunt et constituunt hypostases, ut ostensum est. Secundo, quia omnis hypostasis naturae rationalis est persona, ut patet per definitionem Boetii, dicentis quod persona est rationalis naturae individua substantia. Unde, ad hoc quod esset hypostasis et non persona, oporteret abstrahi ex parte naturae rationalitatem; non autem ex parte personae proprietatem.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30088)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod persona non addit supra hypostasim proprietatem distinguentem absolute, sed proprietatem distinguentem ad dignitatem pertinentem, totum enim hoc est accipiendum loco unius differentiae. Ad dignitatem autem pertinet proprietas distinguens, secundum quod intelligitur subsistens in natura rationali. Unde, remota proprietate distinguente a persona, non remanet hypostasis, sed remaneret, si tolleretur rationalitas naturae. Tam enim persona quam hypostasis est substantia individua, unde in divinis de ratione utriusque est relatio distinguens.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30089)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod paternitate pater non solum est pater, sed est persona, et est quis sive hypostasis. Nec tamen sequitur quod filius non sit quis sive hypostasis; sicut non sequitur quod non sit persona.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30090)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod intentio Augustini non fuit dicere quod hypostasis patris remaneat ingenita, remota paternitate, quasi innascibilitas constituat et distinguat hypostasim patris, hoc enim esse non potest, cum ingenitum nihil ponat, sed negative dicatur, ut ipsemet dicit. Sed loquitur in communi, quia non omne ingenitum est pater. Remota ergo paternitate, non remanet in divinis hypostasis patris, ut distinguitur ab aliis personis; sed ut distinguitur a creaturis, sicut Iudaei intelligunt.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30091)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionales praeintelligantur proprietatibus. Dicit enim Magister, XXVII dist. I Sent., quod *semper pater est, quia genuit semper filium*. Et ita videtur quod generatio, secundum intellectum, praecedat paternitatem.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30092)\n\nPraeterea, omnis relatio praesupponit, in intellectu, id supra quod fundatur; sicut aequalitas quantitatem. Sed paternitas est relatio fundata super actione quae est generatio. Ergo paternitas praesupponit generationem.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30093)\n\nPraeterea, sicut se habet generatio activa ad paternitatem, ita se habet nativitas ad filiationem. Sed filiatio praesupponit nativitatem, ideo enim filius est, quia natus est. Ergo et paternitas praesupponit generationem.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30094)\n\nSed contra, generatio est operatio personae patris. Sed paternitas constituit personam patris. Ergo prius est, secundum intellectum, paternitas quam generatio.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30095)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum illos qui dicunt quod proprietates non distinguunt et constituunt hypostases, sed manifestant hypostases distinctas et constitutas, absolute dicendum est quod relationes, secundum modum intelligendi, consequuntur actus notionales; ut dici possit simpliciter quod quia generat, est pater. Sed supponendo quod relationes distinguant et constituant hypostases in divinis, oportet distinctione uti. Quia origo significatur in divinis active et passive, active quidem, sicut generatio attribuitur patri, et spiratio, sumpta pro actu notionali, attribuitur patri et filio; passive autem, sicut nativitas attribuitur filio, et processio spiritui sancto. Origines enim passive significatae, simpliciter praecedunt, secundum intellectum, proprietates personarum procedentium, etiam personales, quia origo passive significata, significatur ut via ad personam proprietate constitutam. Similiter et origo active significata, prior est, secundum intellectum, quam relatio personae originantis quae non est personalis, sicut actus notionalis spirationis, secundum intellectum, praecedit proprietatem relativam innominatam communem patri et filio. Sed proprietas personalis patris potest considerari dupliciter. Uno modo, ut est relatio, et sic iterum, secundum intellectum, praesupponit actum notionalem; quia relatio, inquantum huiusmodi, fundatur super actum. Alio modo, secundum quod est constitutiva personae, et sic oportet quod praeintelligatur relatio actui notionali, sicut persona agens praeintelligitur actioni.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30096)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum Magister dicit quod quia generat est pater, accipit nomen patris secundum quod designat relationem tantum, non autem secundum quod significat personam subsistentem. Sic enim oporteret e converso dicere quod quia pater est, generat.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30097)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de paternitate, secundum quod est relatio, et non secundum quod est constitutiva personae.\n\n[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30098)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nativitas est via ad personam filii, et ideo, secundum intellectum, praecedit filiationem, etiam secundum quod est constitutiva personae filii. Sed generatio activa significatur ut progrediens a persona patris, et ideo praesupponit proprietatem personalem patris.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0002","text":"[I q. 40 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de personis in comparatione ad relationes sive proprietates. Et quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum relatio sit idem quod persona. Secundo, utrum relationes distinguant et constituant personas. Tertio, utrum, abstractis per intellectum relationibus a personis, remaneant hypostases distinctae. Quarto, utrum relationes, secundum intellectum, praesupponant actus personarum, vel e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0005","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in divinis non sit idem relatio quod persona. Quaecumque enim sunt idem, multiplicato uno eorum, multiplicatur et aliud. Sed contingit in una persona esse plures relationes, sicut in persona patris est paternitas et communis spiratio, et iterum unam relationem in duabus personis esse, sicut communis spiratio est in patre et filio. Ergo relatio non est idem quod persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0007","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0008","text":"Praeterea, nihil est in seipso, secundum philosophum, in IV Physic. Sed relatio est in persona. Nec potest dici quod ratione identitatis, quia sic esset etiam in essentia. Ergo relatio sive proprietas et persona non sunt idem in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0009","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quaecumque sunt idem, ita se habent, quod quidquid praedicatur de uno, praedicatur et de alio. Non autem quidquid praedicatur de persona, praedicatur de proprietate. Dicimus enim quod pater generat, sed non dicimus quod paternitas sit generans. Ergo proprietas non est idem quod persona in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0011","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0012","text":"Sed contra, in divinis non differt quod est et quo est, ut habetur a Boetio in libro de Hebd. Sed pater paternitate est pater. Ergo pater idem est quod paternitas. Et eadem ratione aliae proprietates idem sunt cum personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0013","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc aliqui diversimode opinati sunt. Quidam enim dixerunt proprietates neque esse personas, neque in personis. Qui fuerunt moti ex modo significandi relationum, quae quidem non significant ut in aliquo, sed magis ut ad aliquid. Unde dixerunt relationes esse assistentes, sicut supra expositum est. Sed quia relatio, secundum quod est quaedam res in divinis, est ipsa essentia; essentia autem idem est quod persona, ut ex dictis patet; oportet quod relatio sit idem quod persona. Hanc igitur identitatem alii considerantes, dixerunt proprietates quidem esse personas, non autem in personis, quia non ponebant proprietates in divinis nisi secundum modum loquendi, ut supra dictum est. Necesse est autem ponere proprietates in divinis, ut supra ostendimus. Quae quidem significantur in abstracto, ut quaedam formae personarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0015","text":"Unde, cum de ratione formae sit, quod sit in eo cuius est forma, oportet dicere proprietates esse in personis, et eas tamen esse personas; sicut essentiam esse in Deo dicimus, quae tamen est Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0016","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod persona et proprietas sunt idem re, differunt tamen secundum rationem. Unde non oportet quod, multiplicato uno, multiplicetur reliquum. Considerandum tamen est quod, propter divinam simplicitatem, consideratur duplex realis identitas in divinis eorum quae differunt in rebus creatis. Quia enim divina simplicitas excludit compositionem formae et materiae, sequitur quod in divinis idem est abstractum et concretum, ut deitas et Deus. Quia vero divina simplicitas excludit compositionem subiecti et accidentis, sequitur quod quidquid attribuitur Deo, est eius essentia, et propter hoc sapientia et virtus idem sunt in Deo, quia ambo sunt in divina essentia. Et secundum hanc duplicem rationem identitatis, proprietas in divinis est idem cum persona. Nam proprietates personales sunt idem cum personis, ea ratione qua abstractum est idem cum concreto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0018","text":"Sunt enim ipsae personae subsistentes; ut paternitas est ipse pater, et filiatio filius, et processio spiritus sanctus. Proprietates autem non personales sunt idem cum personis secundum aliam rationem identitatis, qua omne illud quod attribuitur Deo, est eius essentia. Sic igitur communis spiratio est idem cum persona patris et cum persona filii, non quod sit una persona per se subsistens; sed, sicut una essentia est in duabus personis, ita et una proprietas, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0019","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod proprietates dicuntur esse in essentia, per modum identitatis tantum. In personis autem dicuntur esse per modum identitatis, non quidem secundum rem tantum, sed quantum ad modum significandi, sicut forma in supposito. Et ideo proprietates determinant et distinguunt personas, non autem essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0021","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod participia et verba notionalia significant actus notionales. Actus autem suppositorum sunt. Proprietates autem non significantur ut supposita, sed ut formae suppositorum. Et ideo modus significandi repugnat, ut participia et verba notionalia de proprietatibus praedicentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0024","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod personae non distinguantur per relationes. Simplicia enim seipsis distinguuntur. Sed personae divinae sunt maxime simplices. Ergo distinguuntur seipsis, et non relationibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0026","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0027","text":"Praeterea, nulla forma distinguitur nisi secundum suum genus, non enim album a nigro distinguitur nisi secundum qualitatem. Sed hypostasis significat individuum in genere substantiae. Non ergo relationibus hypostases distingui possint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0028","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0029","text":"Praeterea, absolutum est prius quam relativum. Sed prima distinctio est distinctio divinarum personarum. Ergo divinae personae non distinguuntur relationibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0030","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0031","text":"Praeterea, id quod praesupponit distinctionem, non potest esse primum distinctionis principium. Sed relatio praesupponit distinctionem, cum in eius definitione ponatur, esse enim relativi est ad aliud se habere. Ergo primum principium distinctivum in divinis non potest esse relatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0032","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0033","text":"Sed contra est quod Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin., quod sola relatio multiplicat Trinitatem divinarum personarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0034","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in quibuscumque pluribus invenitur aliquid commune, oportet quaerere aliquid distinctivum. Unde, cum tres personae conveniant secundum essentiae unitatem, necesse est quaerere aliquid quo distinguantur, ad hoc quod plures sint. Inveniuntur autem in divinis personis duo secundum quae differunt, scilicet origo, et relatio. Quae quidem quamvis re non differant, differunt tamen secundum modum significandi, nam origo significatur per modum actus, ut generatio; relatio vero per modum formae, ut paternitas. Quidam igitur, attendentes quod relatio consequitur actum, dixerunt quod hypostases in divinis distinguuntur per originem; ut dicamus quod pater distinguitur a filio, inquantum ille generat et hic est genitus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0036","text":"Relationes autem sive proprietates manifestant consequenter hypostasum sive personarum distinctiones, sicut et in creaturis proprietates manifestant distinctiones individuorum, quae fiunt per materialia principia. Sed hoc non potest stare, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia ad hoc quod aliqua duo distincta intelligantur, necesse est eorum distinctionem intelligi per aliquid intrinsecum utrique; sicut in rebus creatis vel per materiam, vel per formam. Origo autem alicuius rei non significatur ut aliquid intrinsecum, sed ut via quaedam a re vel ad rem, sicut generatio significatur ut via quaedam ad rem genitam, et ut progrediens a generante. Unde non potest esse quod res genita et generans distinguantur sola generatione, sed oportet intelligere tam in generante quam in genito ea quibus ab invicem distinguuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0037","text":"In persona autem divina non est aliud intelligere nisi essentiam et relationem sive proprietatem. Unde, cum in essentia conveniant, relinquitur quod per relationes personae ab invicem distinguantur. Secundo, quia distinctio in divinis personis non est sic intelligenda, quasi aliquid commune dividatur, quia essentia communis remanet indivisa, sed oportet quod ipsa distinguentia constituant res distinctas. Sic autem relationes vel proprietates distinguunt vel constituunt hypostases vel personas, inquantum sunt ipsae personae subsistentes, sicut paternitas est pater, et filiatio est filius, eo quod in divinis non differt abstractum et concretum. Sed contra rationem originis est, quod constituat hypostasim vel personam. Quia origo active significata, significatur ut progrediens a persona subsistente, unde praesupponit eam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0038","text":"Origo autem passive significata, ut nativitas, significatur ut via ad personam subsistentem; et nondum ut eam constituens. Unde melius dicitur quod personae seu hypostases distinguantur relationibus, quam per originem. Licet enim distinguantur utroque modo, tamen prius et principalius per relationes, secundum modum intelligendi. Unde hoc nomen pater non solum significat proprietatem, sed etiam hypostasim, sed hoc nomen genitor, vel generans, significat tantum proprietatem. Quia hoc nomen pater significat relationem, quae est distinctiva et constitutiva hypostasis, hoc autem nomen generans, vel genitus, significat originem, quae non est distinctiva et constitutiva hypostasis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0039","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod personae sunt ipsae relationes subsistentes. Unde non repugnat simplicitati divinarum personarum, quod relationibus distinguantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0041","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod personae divinae non distinguuntur in esse in quo subsistunt, neque in aliquo absoluto, sed solum secundum id quod ad aliquid dicuntur. Unde ad earum distinctionem sufficit relatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0043","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0044","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quanto distinctio prior est, tanto propinquior est unitati. Et ideo debet esse minima. Et ideo distinctio personarum non debet esse nisi per id quod minimum distinguit, scilicet per relationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0045","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0046","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod relatio praesupponit distinctionem suppositorum, quando est accidens, sed si relatio sit subsistens, non praesupponit, sed secum fert distinctionem. Cum enim dicitur quod relativi esse est ad aliud se habere, per ly aliud intelligitur correlativum, quod non est prius, sed simul natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0047","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0048","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0049","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod, abstractis per intellectum proprietatibus seu relationibus a personis, adhuc remaneant hypostases. Id enim ad quod aliquid se habet ex additione, potest intelligi remoto eo quod sibi additur, sicut homo se habet ad animal ex additione, et potest intelligi animal remoto rationali. Sed persona se habet ex additione ad hypostasim, est enim persona hypostasis proprietate distincta ad dignitatem pertinente. Ergo, remota proprietate personali a persona, intelligitur hypostasis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0050","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0051","text":"Praeterea, pater non ab eodem habet quod sit pater, et quod sit aliquis. Cum enim paternitate sit pater, si paternitate esset aliquis, sequeretur quod filius, in quo non est paternitas, non esset aliquis. Remota ergo per intellectum paternitate a patre, adhuc remanet quod sit aliquis; quod est esse hypostasim. Ergo, remota proprietate a persona, remanet hypostasis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0052","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0053","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, V de Trin., *non hoc est dicere ingenitum, quod est dicere patrem, quia etsi filium non genuisset, nihil prohiberet eum dicere ingenitum*. Sed si filium non genuisset, non inesset ei paternitas. Ergo, remota paternitate, adhuc remanet hypostasis patris ut ingenita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0054","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0055","text":"Sed contra est quod Hilarius dicit, IV de Trin., *nihil habet filius nisi natum*. Nativitate autem est filius. Ergo, remota filiatione, non remanet hypostasis filii. Et eadem ratio est de aliis personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0056","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0057","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod duplex fit abstractio per intellectum. Una quidem, secundum quod universale abstrahitur a particulari, ut animal ab homine. Alia vero, secundum quod forma abstrahitur a materia; sicut forma circuli abstrahitur per intellectum ab omni materia sensibili. Inter has autem abstractiones haec est differentia, quod in abstractione quae fit secundum universale et particulare, non remanet id a quo fit abstractio, remota enim ab homine differentia rationali, non remanet in intellectu homo, sed solum animal. In abstractione vero quae attenditur secundum formam et materiam, utrumque manet in intellectu, abstrahendo enim formam circuli ab aere, remanet seorsum in intellectu nostro et intellectus circuli et intellectus aeris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0058","text":"Quamvis autem in divinis non sit universale neque particulare, nec forma et materia, secundum rem; tamen, secundum modum significandi, invenitur aliqua similitudo horum in divinis; secundum quem modum Damascenus dicit quod *commune est substantia, particulare vero hypostasis*. Si igitur loquamur de abstractione quae fit secundum universale et particulare, remotis proprietatibus, remanet in intellectu essentia communis, non autem hypostasis patris, quae est quasi particulare. Si vero loquamur secundum modum abstractionis formae a materia, remotis proprietatibus non personalibus, remanet intellectus hypostasum et personarum, sicut, remoto per intellectum a patre quod sit ingenitus vel spirans, remanet hypostasis vel persona patris. Sed remota proprietate personali per intellectum, tollitur intellectus hypostasis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0059","text":"Non enim proprietates personales sic intelliguntur advenire hypostasibus divinis, sicut forma subiecto praeexistenti, sed ferunt secum sua supposita, inquantum sunt ipsae personae subsistentes, sicut paternitas est ipse pater, hypostasis enim significat aliquid distinctum in divinis, cum hypostasis sit substantia individua. Cum igitur relatio sit quae distinguit hypostases et constituit eas, ut dictum est, relinquitur quod, relationibus personalibus remotis per intellectum, non remaneant hypostases. Sed, sicut dictum est, aliqui dicunt quod hypostases in divinis non distinguuntur per relationes, sed per solam originem; ut intelligatur pater esse hypostasis quaedam per hoc, quod non est ab alio; filius autem per hoc, quod est ab alio per generationem. Sed relationes advenientes quasi proprietates ad dignitatem pertinentes, constituunt rationem personae, unde et personalitates dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0060","text":"Unde, remotis huiusmodi relationibus per intellectum, remanent quidem hypostases, sed non personae. Sed hoc non potest esse, propter duo. Primo, quia relationes distinguunt et constituunt hypostases, ut ostensum est. Secundo, quia omnis hypostasis naturae rationalis est persona, ut patet per definitionem Boetii, dicentis quod persona est rationalis naturae individua substantia. Unde, ad hoc quod esset hypostasis et non persona, oporteret abstrahi ex parte naturae rationalitatem; non autem ex parte personae proprietatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0061","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0062","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod persona non addit supra hypostasim proprietatem distinguentem absolute, sed proprietatem distinguentem ad dignitatem pertinentem, totum enim hoc est accipiendum loco unius differentiae. Ad dignitatem autem pertinet proprietas distinguens, secundum quod intelligitur subsistens in natura rationali. Unde, remota proprietate distinguente a persona, non remanet hypostasis, sed remaneret, si tolleretur rationalitas naturae. Tam enim persona quam hypostasis est substantia individua, unde in divinis de ratione utriusque est relatio distinguens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0063","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0064","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod paternitate pater non solum est pater, sed est persona, et est quis sive hypostasis. Nec tamen sequitur quod filius non sit quis sive hypostasis; sicut non sequitur quod non sit persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0065","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0066","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod intentio Augustini non fuit dicere quod hypostasis patris remaneat ingenita, remota paternitate, quasi innascibilitas constituat et distinguat hypostasim patris, hoc enim esse non potest, cum ingenitum nihil ponat, sed negative dicatur, ut ipsemet dicit. Sed loquitur in communi, quia non omne ingenitum est pater. Remota ergo paternitate, non remanet in divinis hypostasis patris, ut distinguitur ab aliis personis; sed ut distinguitur a creaturis, sicut Iudaei intelligunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0067","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0068","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0069","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionales praeintelligantur proprietatibus. Dicit enim Magister, XXVII dist. I Sent., quod *semper pater est, quia genuit semper filium*. Et ita videtur quod generatio, secundum intellectum, praecedat paternitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0070","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0071","text":"Praeterea, omnis relatio praesupponit, in intellectu, id supra quod fundatur; sicut aequalitas quantitatem. Sed paternitas est relatio fundata super actione quae est generatio. Ergo paternitas praesupponit generationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0072","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0073","text":"Praeterea, sicut se habet generatio activa ad paternitatem, ita se habet nativitas ad filiationem. Sed filiatio praesupponit nativitatem, ideo enim filius est, quia natus est. Ergo et paternitas praesupponit generationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0074","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0075","text":"Sed contra, generatio est operatio personae patris. Sed paternitas constituit personam patris. Ergo prius est, secundum intellectum, paternitas quam generatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0076","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum illos qui dicunt quod proprietates non distinguunt et constituunt hypostases, sed manifestant hypostases distinctas et constitutas, absolute dicendum est quod relationes, secundum modum intelligendi, consequuntur actus notionales; ut dici possit simpliciter quod quia generat, est pater. Sed supponendo quod relationes distinguant et constituant hypostases in divinis, oportet distinctione uti. Quia origo significatur in divinis active et passive, active quidem, sicut generatio attribuitur patri, et spiratio, sumpta pro actu notionali, attribuitur patri et filio; passive autem, sicut nativitas attribuitur filio, et processio spiritui sancto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0078","text":"Origines enim passive significatae, simpliciter praecedunt, secundum intellectum, proprietates personarum procedentium, etiam personales, quia origo passive significata, significatur ut via ad personam proprietate constitutam. Similiter et origo active significata, prior est, secundum intellectum, quam relatio personae originantis quae non est personalis, sicut actus notionalis spirationis, secundum intellectum, praecedit proprietatem relativam innominatam communem patri et filio. Sed proprietas personalis patris potest considerari dupliciter. Uno modo, ut est relatio, et sic iterum, secundum intellectum, praesupponit actum notionalem; quia relatio, inquantum huiusmodi, fundatur super actum. Alio modo, secundum quod est constitutiva personae, et sic oportet quod praeintelligatur relatio actui notionali, sicut persona agens praeintelligitur actioni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0079","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0080","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum Magister dicit quod quia generat est pater, accipit nomen patris secundum quod designat relationem tantum, non autem secundum quod significat personam subsistentem. Sic enim oporteret e converso dicere quod quia pater est, generat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0081","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0082","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de paternitate, secundum quod est relatio, et non secundum quod est constitutiva personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0083","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0084","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nativitas est via ad personam filii, et ideo, secundum intellectum, praecedit filiationem, etiam secundum quod est constitutiva personae filii. Sed generatio activa significatur ut progrediens a persona patris, et ideo praesupponit proprietatem personalem patris.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8e1c46c619f0008045fccb85386ac64b5b02c45043c15f0b7aaeff32faafb159","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T142245020832Z_b2a4d0b1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 40","source_sha256":"8e1c46c619f0008045fccb85386ac64b5b02c45043c15f0b7aaeff32faafb159","sha256":"e28bc7c09770c77dc989da84074fea4fc44a7dd6730362afd4e63ee755d1352b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:22:45.020850+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0002","text":"[I q. 40 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0003","text":"Next, there is inquiry concerning the persons in comparison with the relations or properties. And four questions are asked. First, whether relation is the same as person. Second, whether relations distinguish and constitute persons. Third, whether, if relations are abstracted from persons by the intellect, distinct hypostases remain. Fourth, whether relations, according to the intellect, presuppose the acts of the persons, or conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0005","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first point. It seems that in God relation is not the same as person. For whatever things are the same, if one of them is multiplied, the other is multiplied as well. But it can happen that there are several relations in one person, as in the person of the Father there are paternity and common spiration; and again, that one relation is in two persons, as common spiration is in the Father and the Son. Therefore relation is not the same as person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0007","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0008","text":"Further, according to the Philosopher, in Physics IV, nothing is in itself. But relation is in a person. Nor can it be said that this is by reason of identity, because in that case it would also be in the essence. Therefore relation or property and person are not the same in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0009","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0010","text":"Further, whatever things are the same are so related that whatever is predicated of one is predicated of the other. But not whatever is predicated of a person is predicated of a property. For we say that the Father generates, but we do not say that paternity is generating. Therefore property is not the same as person in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0011","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0012","text":"On the contrary, in God that which is and that by which it is do not differ, as is held by Boethius in the book On the Hebdomads. But the Father is Father by paternity. Therefore the Father is the same as paternity. And by the same reasoning the other properties are the same as the persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0013","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have held different opinions on this matter. For some said that properties are neither persons nor in persons. They were moved by the mode of signifying of relations, which indeed signify not as being in something, but rather as being toward something. Hence they said that relations are assisting, as was explained above. But because relation, insofar as it is a certain reality in God, is the essence itself; and the essence is the same as person, as is clear from what has been said; relation must be the same as person. Others, therefore, considering this identity, said that properties are indeed persons, but not in persons, because they posited properties in God only according to a mode of speaking, as was said above. But it is necessary to posit properties in God, as we showed above. For they are signified in the abstract, as certain forms of persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0015","text":"Hence, since it belongs to the nature of form that it be in that of which it is the form, one must say that properties are in persons, and yet that they are persons; just as we say that the essence is in God, though it is nevertheless God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0016","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that person and property are the same in reality, yet they differ in notion. Hence it need not be that, if one is multiplied, the other is multiplied. Nevertheless, it must be considered that, on account of divine simplicity, there is considered in God a twofold real identity of things which differ in created things. For since divine simplicity excludes composition of form and matter, it follows that in God the abstract and the concrete are the same, as deity and God. And since divine simplicity excludes composition of subject and accident, it follows that whatever is attributed to God is his essence, and for this reason wisdom and power are the same in God, because both are in the divine essence. And according to this twofold ground of identity, property in God is the same as person. For personal properties are the same as persons on the ground that the abstract is the same as the concrete.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0018","text":"For the persons themselves are subsisting: thus paternity is the Father himself, filiation the Son, and procession the Holy Spirit. Non-personal properties, however, are the same as persons according to the other ground of identity, by which everything attributed to God is his essence. Thus common spiration is the same as the person of the Father and as the person of the Son, not because it is one person subsisting through itself; but, just as one essence is in two persons, so too is one property, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0019","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that properties are said to be in the essence only by the mode of identity. But they are said to be in the persons by the mode of identity, not indeed according to reality alone, but also with respect to the mode of signifying, as form is in a supposit. And therefore properties determine and distinguish persons, but not the essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0021","text":"[I q. 40 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that notional participles and verbs signify notional acts. But acts belong to supposits. Properties, however, are not signified as supposits, but as forms of supposits. And therefore the mode of signifying is incompatible with notional participles and verbs being predicated of properties.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0024","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the persons are not distinguished by relations. For simple things are distinguished by themselves. But the divine persons are supremely simple. Therefore they are distinguished by themselves, and not by relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0026","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0027","text":"Further, no form is distinguished except according to its genus; for white is not distinguished from black except according to quality. But hypostasis signifies an individual in the genus of substance. Therefore hypostases cannot be distinguished by relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0028","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0029","text":"Further, the absolute is prior to the relative. But the first distinction is the distinction of the divine persons. Therefore the divine persons are not distinguished by relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0030","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0031","text":"Further, that which presupposes distinction cannot be the first principle of distinction. But relation presupposes distinction, since in its definition it is posited; for to be of a relative is to be disposed toward another. Therefore relation cannot be the first distinguishing principle in the divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0032","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0033","text":"But on the contrary, Boethius says, in the book On the Trinity, that relation alone multiplies the Trinity of divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0034","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0035","text":"I answer that it must be said that wherever something common is found in several things, it is necessary to seek something distinguishing. Hence, since the three persons agree in the unity of essence, it is necessary to seek that by which they are distinguished, so that they may be several. Now two things are found in the divine persons according to which they differ, namely origin and relation. Although these do not differ in reality, they nevertheless differ according to their mode of signifying; for origin is signified after the manner of an act, as generation; but relation after the manner of a form, as paternity. Therefore some, considering that relation follows upon act, said that hypostases in divine things are distinguished by origin; so that we may say that the Father is distinguished from the Son insofar as the former generates and the latter is begotten.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0036","text":"But relations or properties consequently manifest the distinctions of hypostases or persons, just as in creatures properties manifest the distinctions of individuals, which come about through material principles. But this cannot stand, for two reasons. First, because in order that two distinct things be understood, it is necessary that their distinction be understood through something intrinsic to each; as in created things, either through matter or through form. But the origin of a thing is not signified as something intrinsic, but as a certain way from a thing or toward a thing; just as generation is signified as a certain way toward the generated thing, and as proceeding from the generator. Hence it cannot be that the generated thing and the generator are distinguished by generation alone, but it is necessary to understand in both the generator and the generated those things by which they are distinguished from one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0037","text":"But in the divine person there is nothing else to be understood except essence and relation or property. Hence, since they agree in essence, it remains that the persons are distinguished from one another by relations. Second, because distinction in the divine persons is not to be understood as though something common were divided, since the common essence remains undivided; rather, the distinguishing things themselves must constitute distinct things. Now relations or properties distinguish or constitute hypostases or persons in this way, insofar as they are themselves subsisting persons, just as paternity is the Father, and filiation is the Son, because in divine things the abstract and the concrete do not differ. But it is contrary to the notion of origin that it should constitute a hypostasis or person. For origin actively signified is signified as proceeding from a subsisting person, and thus presupposes it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0038","text":"But origin passively signified, as birth, is signified as a way toward a subsisting person, and not yet as constituting it. Hence it is said better that persons or hypostases are distinguished by relations than by origin. For although they are distinguished in both ways, nevertheless they are distinguished first and principally by relations, according to the mode of understanding. Hence this name Father signifies not only a property, but also a hypostasis; but this name begetter, or generating, signifies only a property. For this name Father signifies a relation that is distinctive and constitutive of a hypostasis; but this name generating, or begotten, signifies an origin that is not distinctive and constitutive of a hypostasis.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0039","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0040","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the persons are the subsisting relations themselves. Hence it is not incompatible with the simplicity of the divine persons that they are distinguished by relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0041","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0042","text":"To the second it must be said that the divine persons are not distinguished in the being in which they subsist, nor in anything absolute, but only according to that which is said in relation to another. Hence relation suffices for their distinction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0043","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0044","text":"To the third it must be said that the prior a distinction is, the nearer it is to unity. And therefore it ought to be minimal. And therefore the distinction of the persons ought to be only through that which distinguishes minimally, namely, through relation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0045","text":"[I q. 40 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0046","text":"To the fourth it must be said that relation presupposes a distinction of supposita when it is an accident; but if relation is subsisting, it does not presuppose it, but carries distinction along with itself. For when it is said that the being of a relative is to be disposed toward another, by “another” is understood the correlative, which is not prior, but simultaneous by nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0047","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0048","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0049","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that, once the properties or relations have been abstracted by the intellect from the persons, the hypostases still remain. For that which something has by addition can be understood when that which is added to it is removed, just as man is related to animal by addition, and animal can be understood when rational is removed. But person is related by addition to hypostasis, for person is a hypostasis distinguished by a property pertaining to dignity. Therefore, when the personal property is removed from person, hypostasis is understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0050","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0051","text":"Further, the Father does not have from the same thing both that he is Father and that he is someone. For since he is Father by paternity, if he were someone by paternity, it would follow that the Son, in whom there is no paternity, would not be someone. Therefore, when paternity is removed by the intellect from the Father, there still remains that he is someone, which is to be a hypostasis. Therefore, when the property is removed from the person, hypostasis remains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0052","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0053","text":"Further, Augustine says, *On the Trinity*, V: “to say unbegotten is not the same as to say Father, because even if he had not begotten the Son, nothing would prevent his being called unbegotten.” But if he had not begotten the Son, paternity would not be in him. Therefore, when paternity is removed, the hypostasis of the Father remains as unbegotten.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0054","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0055","text":"On the contrary, Hilary says, *On the Trinity*, IV: “the Son has nothing except what is born.” But he is Son by nativity. Therefore, when filiation is removed, the hypostasis of the Son does not remain. And the same reasoning applies to the other persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0056","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0057","text":"I answer that it must be said that a twofold abstraction is made by the intellect. One, indeed, according as the universal is abstracted from the particular, as animal from man. The other, however, according as form is abstracted from matter, just as the form of a circle is abstracted by the intellect from all sensible matter. Now between these abstractions there is this difference: that in the abstraction which is made according to universal and particular, that from which the abstraction is made does not remain; for, when the rational difference is removed from man, man does not remain in the intellect, but animal alone. But in the abstraction considered according to form and matter, both remain in the intellect; for, when the form of a circle is abstracted from bronze, there remain separately in our intellect both the concept of circle and the concept of bronze.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0058","text":"Although, however, in divine things there is neither universal nor particular, nor form and matter, in reality, nevertheless, according to the mode of signifying, a certain likeness of these is found in divine things; according to which mode Damascene says that “substance is common, but hypostasis particular.” If, therefore, we speak of the abstraction which is made according to universal and particular, when the properties are removed, the common essence remains in the intellect, but not the hypostasis of the Father, which is as it were a particular. But if we speak according to the mode of abstraction of form from matter, when the non-personal properties are removed, the concept of hypostases and persons remains, just as, when it is removed from the Father by the intellect that he is unbegotten or spirating, the hypostasis or person of the Father remains. But when the personal property is removed by the intellect, the concept of hypostasis is taken away.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0059","text":"For the personal properties are not understood to accrue to the divine hypostases as form accrues to a pre-existing subject, but they carry their supposita with them, insofar as they themselves are subsisting persons, just as paternity is the Father himself; for hypostasis signifies something distinct in divine things, since hypostasis is an individual substance. Therefore, since relation is that which distinguishes and constitutes the hypostases, as has been said, it follows that, once the personal relations are removed by the intellect, the hypostases do not remain. But, as has been said, some say that the hypostases in divine things are distinguished not by relations, but by origin alone; so that the Father is understood to be a certain hypostasis because he is not from another, but the Son because he is from another by generation. But relations accruing as properties pertaining to dignity constitute the notion of person, whence they are also called personalities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0060","text":"Hence, when relations of this sort are removed by the intellect, hypostases indeed remain, but not persons. But this cannot be, for two reasons. First, because relations distinguish and constitute hypostases, as has been shown. Second, because every hypostasis of a rational nature is a person, as is clear from the definition of Boethius, who says that person is an individual substance of a rational nature. Hence, for there to be a hypostasis and not a person, rationality would have to be abstracted on the part of the nature, not a property on the part of the person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0061","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0062","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that person does not add to hypostasis a distinguishing property absolutely, but a distinguishing property pertaining to dignity; for all of this must be taken in place of one difference. Now a distinguishing property pertains to dignity according as it is understood as subsisting in a rational nature. Hence, when the distinguishing property is removed from person, hypostasis does not remain; but it would remain if the rationality of the nature were removed. For both person and hypostasis are individual substance; hence in divine things a distinguishing relation belongs to the notion of each.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0063","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0064","text":"To the second, it must be said that by paternity the Father not only is Father, but is a person and is someone, or a hypostasis. Yet it does not follow that the Son is not someone, or a hypostasis, just as it does not follow that he is not a person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0065","text":"[I q. 40 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0066","text":"To the third, it must be said that Augustine’s intention was not to say that the hypostasis of the Father remains unbegotten when paternity is removed, as though unbegottenness constituted and distinguished the hypostasis of the Father; for this cannot be, since “unbegotten” posits nothing, but is said negatively, as he himself says. Rather, he speaks in general, because not every unbegotten one is Father. Therefore, when paternity is removed, the hypostasis of the Father does not remain in divine things as distinguished from the other persons; but it remains as distinguished from creatures, as the Jews understand it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0067","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0068","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0069","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that notional acts are understood beforehand in relation to properties. For the Master says, *Sentences*, I, distinction 27, that “the Father is always Father, because he has always begotten the Son.” And thus it seems that generation, according to the intellect, precedes paternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0070","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0071","text":"Further, every relation presupposes, in the intellect, that upon which it is founded, just as equality presupposes quantity. But paternity is a relation founded upon the action which is generation. Therefore, paternity presupposes generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0072","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0073","text":"Moreover, just as active generation is related to paternity, so nativity is related to filiation. But filiation presupposes nativity, for he is a son because he has been born. Therefore paternity likewise presupposes generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0074","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0075","text":"But on the contrary, generation is an operation of the person of the Father. But paternity constitutes the person of the Father. Therefore, according to understanding, paternity is prior to generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0076","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0077","text":"I answer that, according to those who say that the properties do not distinguish and constitute hypostases, but manifest hypostases as distinct and constituted, it must be said absolutely that the relations, according to the mode of understanding, follow the notional acts; so that one may say simply that because he generates, he is Father. But assuming that relations distinguish and constitute hypostases in the divine, a distinction must be made. For origin is signified in the divine both actively and passively: actively indeed, as generation is attributed to the Father, and spiration, taken for a notional act, is attributed to the Father and the Son; but passively, as nativity is attributed to the Son, and procession to the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0078","text":"For origins signified passively simply precede, according to understanding, the properties of the proceeding persons, even the personal ones, because origin signified passively is signified as a way to a person constituted by a property. Likewise, origin signified actively is prior, according to understanding, to the relation of the originating person which is not personal; just as the notional act of spiration, according to understanding, precedes the unnamed relative property common to the Father and the Son. But the personal property of the Father can be considered in two ways. In one way, as it is a relation; and thus again, according to understanding, it presupposes the notional act, because relation, as such, is founded upon an act. In another way, according as it is constitutive of the person; and thus the relation must be understood beforehand in relation to the notional act, just as the acting person is understood beforehand in relation to the action.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0079","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0080","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, when the Master says that because he generates he is Father, he takes the name Father insofar as it designates a relation only, and not insofar as it signifies a subsisting person. For thus it would be necessary to say conversely that because he is Father, he generates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0081","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0082","text":"To the second, it must be said that that objection proceeds concerning paternity insofar as it is a relation, and not insofar as it is constitutive of the person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0083","text":"[I q. 40 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0084","text":"To the third, it must be said that nativity is a way to the person of the Son, and therefore, according to understanding, it precedes filiation, even insofar as this is constitutive of the person of the Son. But active generation is signified as proceeding from the person of the Father, and therefore presupposes the personal property of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_32:P0084"]}],"source_digest":"15e86fad7ff179e502bcee188122bda3fb2448c9e8ef3b22210df0fb30c08dfb","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 40"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 40","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33","title":"First Part · Question 41","order":42,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_33","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 41 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30099)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de personis in comparatione ad actus notionales. Et circa hoc quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum actus notionales sint attribuendi personis. Secundo, utrum huiusmodi actus sint necessarii vel voluntarii. Tertio, utrum, secundum huiusmodi actus, persona procedat de nihilo, vel de aliquo. Quarto, utrum in divinis sit ponere potentiam respectu actuum notionalium. Quinto, quid significet huiusmodi potentia. Sexto, utrum actus notionalis ad plures personas terminari possit.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30100)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionales non sint personis attribuendi. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *omnia genera, cum quis in divinam vertit praedicationem, in divinam mutantur substantiam, exceptis relativis*. Sed actio est unum de decem generibus. Si igitur actio aliqua Deo attribuitur, ad eius essentiam pertinebit, et non ad notionem.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30101)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, V de Trin., omne quod de Deo dicitur, aut dicitur secundum substantiam, aut secundum relationem. Sed ea quae ad substantiam pertinent, significantur per essentialia attributa, quae vero ad relationem, per nomina personarum et per nomina proprietatum. Non sunt ergo, praeter haec, attribuendi personis notionales actus.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30102)\n\nPraeterea, proprium actionis est ex se passionem inferre. Sed in divinis non ponimus passiones. Ergo neque actus notionales ibi ponendi sunt.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30103)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *proprium patris est, quod filium genuit*. Sed generatio actus quidam est. Ergo actus notionales ponendi sunt in divinis.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30104)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in divinis personis attenditur distinctio secundum originem. Origo autem convenienter designari non potest nisi per aliquos actus. Ad designandum igitur ordinem originis in divinis personis, necessarium fuit attribuere personis actus notionales.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30105)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod omnis origo designatur per aliquem actum. Duplex autem ordo originis attribui Deo potest. Unus quidem, secundum quod creatura ab eo progreditur, et hoc commune est tribus personis. Et ideo actiones quae attribuuntur Deo ad designandum processum creaturarum ab ipso, ad essentiam pertinent. Alius autem ordo originis in divinis attenditur secundum processionem personae a persona. Unde actus designantes huius originis ordinem, notionales dicuntur, quia notiones personarum sunt personarum habitudines ad invicem, ut ex dictis patet.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30106)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod actus notionales secundum modum significandi tantum differunt a relationibus personarum; sed re sunt omnino idem. Unde Magister dicit, in I Sent., XXVI dist., quod generatio et nativitas aliis nominibus dicuntur paternitas et filiatio. Ad cuius evidentiam, attendendum est quod primo coniicere potuimus originem alicuius ab alio, ex motu, quod enim aliqua res a sua dispositione removeretur per motum, manifestum fuit hoc ab aliqua causa accidere. Et ideo actio, secundum primam nominis impositionem, importat originem motus, sicut enim motus, prout est in mobili ab aliquo, dicitur passio; ita origo ipsius motus, secundum quod incipit ab alio et terminatur in id quod movetur, vocatur actio. Remoto igitur motu, actio nihil aliud importat quam ordinem originis, secundum quod a causa aliqua vel principio procedit in id quod est a principio. Unde, cum in divinis non sit motus, actio personalis producentis personam, nihil aliud est quam habitudo principii ad personam quae est a principio. Quae quidem habitudines sunt ipsae relationes vel notiones. Quia tamen de divinis et intelligibilibus rebus loqui non possumus nisi secundum modum rerum sensibilium, a quibus cognitionem accipimus; et in quibus actiones et passiones, inquantum motum implicant, aliud sunt a relationibus quae ex actionibus et passionibus consequuntur, oportuit seorsum significari habitudines personarum per modum actus, et seorsum per modum relationum. Et sic patet quod sunt idem secundum rem, sed differunt solum secundum modum significandi.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30107)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod actio, secundum quod importat originem motus, infert ex se passionem, sic autem non ponitur actio in divinis personis. Unde non ponuntur ibi passiones, nisi solum grammatice loquendo, quantum ad modum significandi; sicut patri attribuimus generare, et filio generari.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30108)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionales sint voluntarii. Dicit enim Hilarius, in libro de Synod., *non naturali necessitate ductus, pater genuit filium*.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30109)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus, Coloss. I, *transtulit nos in regnum filii dilectionis suae*. Dilectio autem voluntatis est. Ergo filius genitus est a patre, voluntate.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30110)\n\nPraeterea, nihil magis est voluntarium quam amor. Sed spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio ut amor. Ergo procedit voluntarie.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30111)\n\nPraeterea, filius procedit per modum intellectus, ut verbum. Sed omne verbum procedit a dicente per voluntatem. Ergo filius procedit a patre per voluntatem, et non per naturam.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30112)\n\nPraeterea, quod non est voluntarium, est necessarium. Si igitur pater non genuit filium voluntate, videtur sequi quod necessitate genuerit. Quod est contra Augustinum, in libro ad Orosium.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30113)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in eodem libro, quod *neque voluntate genuit pater filium, neque necessitate*.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30114)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum dicitur aliquid esse vel fieri voluntate, dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo, ut ablativus designet concomitantiam tantum, sicut possum dicere quod ego sum homo mea voluntate, quia scilicet volo me esse hominem. Et hoc modo potest dici quod pater genuit filium voluntate, sicut et est voluntate Deus, quia vult se esse Deum, et vult se generare filium. Alio modo sic, quod ablativus importet habitudinem principii, sicut dicitur quod artifex operatur voluntate, quia voluntas est principium operis. Et secundum hunc modum, dicendum est quod Deus pater non genuit filium voluntate; sed voluntate produxit creaturam. Unde in libro de Synod. dicitur, *si quis voluntate Dei, tanquam unum aliquid de creaturis, filium factum dicat, anathema sit*. Et huius ratio est, quia voluntas et natura secundum hoc differunt in causando, quia natura determinata est ad unum; sed voluntas non est determinata ad unum. Cuius ratio est, quia effectus assimilatur formae agentis per quam agit. Manifestum est autem quod unius rei non est nisi una forma naturalis, per quam res habet esse, unde quale ipsum est, tale facit. Sed forma per quam voluntas agit, non est una tantum, sed sunt plures, secundum quod sunt plures rationes intellectae, unde quod voluntate agitur, non est tale quale est agens, sed quale vult et intelligit illud esse agens. Eorum igitur voluntas principium est, quae possunt sic vel aliter esse. Eorum autem quae non possunt nisi sic esse, principium natura est. Quod autem potest sic vel aliter esse, longe est a natura divina, sed hoc pertinet ad rationem creaturae, quia Deus est per se necesse esse, creatura autem est facta ex nihilo. Et ideo Ariani, volentes ad hoc deducere quod filius sit creatura, dixerunt quod pater genuit filium voluntate, secundum quod voluntas designat principium. Nobis autem dicendum est quod pater genuit filium non voluntate, sed natura. Unde Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., *omnibus creaturis substantiam Dei voluntas attulit; sed naturam filio dedit ex impassibili ac non nata substantia perfecta nativitas. Talia enim cuncta creata sunt, qualia Deus esse voluit, filius autem, natus ex Deo, talis subsistit, qualis et Deus est*.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30115)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod auctoritas illa inducitur contra illos qui a generatione filii etiam concomitantiam paternae voluntatis removebant, dicentes sic eum natura genuisse filium, ut inde voluntas generandi ei non adesset, sicut nos multa naturali necessitate contra voluntatem patimur, ut mortem, senectutem, et huiusmodi defectus. Et hoc patet per praecedentia et subsequentia. Sic enim ibi dicitur, *non enim, nolente patre, vel coactus pater, vel naturali necessitate inductus cum nollet, genuit filium*.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30116)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod apostolus nominat Christum filium dilectionis Dei, inquantum est a Deo superabundanter dilectus, non quod dilectio sit principium generationis filii.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30117)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod etiam voluntas, inquantum est natura quaedam, aliquid naturaliter vult; sicut voluntas hominis naturaliter tendit ad beatitudinem. Et similiter Deus naturaliter vult et amat seipsum. Sed circa alia a se, voluntas Dei se habet ad utrumque quodammodo, ut dictum est. Spiritus autem sanctus procedit ut amor, inquantum Deus amat seipsum. Unde naturaliter procedit, quamvis per modum voluntatis procedat.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30118)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod etiam in conceptionibus intellectualibus fit reductio ad prima, quae naturaliter intelliguntur. Deus autem naturaliter intelligit seipsum. Et secundum hoc, conceptio verbi divini est naturalis.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30119)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod necessarium dicitur aliquid per se, et per aliud. Per aliud quidem dupliciter. Uno modo, sicut per causam agentem et cogentem, et sic necessarium dicitur quod est violentum. Alio modo, sicut per causam finalem, sicut dicitur aliquid esse necessarium in his quae sunt ad finem, inquantum sine hoc non potest esse finis, vel bene esse. Et neutro istorum modorum divina generatio est necessaria, quia Deus non est propter finem, neque coactio cadit in ipsum. Per se autem dicitur aliquid necessarium, quod non potest non esse. Et sic Deum esse est necessarium. Et hoc modo patrem generare filium est necessarium.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30120)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionales non sint de aliquo. Quia si pater generat filium de aliquo, aut de seipso, aut de aliquo alio. Si de aliquo alio, cum id de quo aliquid generatur, sit in eo quod generatur, sequitur quod aliquid alienum a patre sit in filio. Quod est contra Hilarium, VII de Trin., ubi dicit, *nihil in his diversum est vel alienum*. Si autem filium generat pater de seipso, id autem de quo aliquid generatur, si sit permanens, recipit eius praedicationem quod generatur; sicut dicimus quod homo est albus, quia homo permanet, cum de non albo fit albus, sequitur igitur quod pater vel non permaneat, genito filio, vel quod pater sit filius, quod est falsum. Non ergo pater generat filium de aliquo, sed de nihilo.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30121)\n\nPraeterea, id de quo aliquid generatur, est principium eius quod generatur. Si ergo pater generat filium de essentia vel natura sua sequitur quod essentia vel natura patris sit principium filii. Sed non principium materiale, quia materia locum in divinis non habet. Ergo est principium quasi activum, sicut generans est principium geniti. Et ita sequitur quod essentia generet, quod supra improbatum est.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30122)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit quod tres personae non sunt ex eadem essentia, quia non est aliud essentia et persona. Sed persona filii non est aliud ab essentia patris. Ergo filius non est de essentia patris.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30123)\n\nPraeterea, omnis creatura est ex nihilo. Sed filius in Scripturis dicitur creatura, dicitur enim Eccli. XXIV, ex ore sapientiae genitae, *ego ex ore altissimi prodii, primogenita ante omnem creaturam*; et postea ex ore eiusdem sapientiae dicitur, *ab initio, et ante saecula, creata sum*. Ergo filius non est genitus ex aliquo, sed ex nihilo. Et similiter potest obiici de spiritu sancto, propter hoc quod dicitur, Zac. XII *dixit dominus, extendens caelum et fundans terram, et creans spiritum hominis in eo*; et Amos IV, secundum aliam litteram, *ego formans montes, et creans spiritum*.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30124)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *pater Deus de sua natura sine initio genuit filium sibi aequalem*.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30125)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod filius non est genitus de nihilo, sed de substantia patris. Ostensum est enim supra quod paternitas, et filiatio, et nativitas, vere et proprie est in divinis. Hoc autem interest inter generationem veram, per quam aliquis procedit ut filius, et factionem, quod faciens facit aliquid de exteriori materia, sicut scamnum facit artifex de ligno; homo autem generat filium de seipso. Sicut autem artifex creatus facit aliquid ex materia, ita Deus facit ex nihilo, ut infra ostendetur, non quod nihilum cedat in substantiam rei, sed quia ab ipso tota substantia rei producitur, nullo alio praesupposito. Si ergo filius procederet a patre ut de nihilo existens, hoc modo se haberet ad patrem ut artificiatum ad artificem, quod manifestum est nomen filiationis proprie habere non posse, sed solum secundum aliquam similitudinem. Unde relinquitur quod, si filius Dei procederet a patre quasi existens ex nihilo, non esset vere et proprie filius. Cuius contrarium dicitur I Ioan. ult., *ut simus in vero filio eius Iesu Christo*. Filius igitur Dei verus non est ex nihilo, nec factus, sed tantum genitus. Si qui autem ex nihilo a Deo facti filii Dei dicantur, hoc erit metaphorice, secundum aliqualem assimilationem ad eum qui vere filius est. Unde, inquantum solus est verus et naturalis Dei filius, dicitur unigenitus, secundum illud Ioan. I *unigenitus, qui est in sinu patris, ipse enarravit*. Inquantum vero per assimilationem ad ipsum alii dicuntur filii adoptivi, quasi metaphorice dicitur esse primogenitus, secundum illud Rom. VIII, *quos praescivit, et praedestinavit conformes fieri imaginis filii sui, ut sit ipse primogenitus in multis fratribus*. Relinquitur ergo quod Dei filius sit genitus de substantia patris. Aliter tamen quam filius hominis. Pars enim substantiae hominis generantis transit in substantiam geniti. Sed divina natura impartibilis est. Unde necesse est quod pater, generando filium, non partem naturae in ipsum transfuderit, sed totam naturam ei communicaverit, remanente distinctione solum secundum originem, ut ex dictis patet.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30126)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum filius dicitur natus de patre, haec praepositio de significat principium generans consubstantiale; non autem principium materiale. Quod enim producitur de materia, fit per transmutationem illius de quo producitur, in aliquam formam; divina autem essentia non est transmutabilis, neque alterius formae susceptiva.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30127)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur filius genitus de essentia patris, secundum expositionem Magistri, V dist. I Sent., designat habitudinem principii quasi activi, ubi sic exponit, *filius est genitus de essentia patris, idest de patre essentia*; propter hoc quod Augustinus, XV libro de Trin., dicit, *tale est quod dico, de patre essentia, ac si expressius dicerem, de patris essentia*. Sed hoc non videtur sufficere ad sensum huiusmodi locutionis. Possumus enim dicere quod creatura est ex Deo essentia, non tamen quod sit ex essentia Dei. Unde aliter dici potest quod haec praepositio de semper denotat consubstantialitatem. Unde non dicimus quod domus sit de aedificatore, cum non sit causa consubstantialis. Possumus autem dicere quod aliquid sit de aliquo, quocumque modo illud significetur ut principium consubstantiale, sive illud sit principium activum, sicut filius dicitur esse de patre; sive sit principium materiale, sicut cultellus dicitur esse de ferro; sive sit principium formale, in his dumtaxat in quibus ipsae formae sunt subsistentes, et non advenientes alteri; possumus enim dicere quod Angelus aliquis est de natura intellectuali. Et per hunc modum dicimus quod filius est genitus de essentia patris; inquantum essentia patris, filio per generationem communicata, in eo subsistit.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30128)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur, filius est genitus de essentia patris, additur aliquid respectu cuius potest salvari distinctio. Sed cum dicitur quod tres personae sunt de essentia divina, non ponitur aliquid respectu cuius possit importari distinctio per praepositionem significata. Et ideo non est simile.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30129)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, cum dicitur, sapientia est creata, potest intelligi, non de sapientia quae est filius Dei, sed de sapientia creata, quam Deus indidit creaturis, dicitur enim Eccli. I, *ipse creavit eam*, scilicet sapientiam, *spiritu sancto, et effudit illam super omnia opera sua*. Neque est inconveniens quod in uno contextu locutionis loquatur Scriptura de sapientia genita et creata, quia sapientia creata est participatio quaedam sapientiae increatae. Vel potest referri ad naturam creatam assumptam a filio, ut sit sensus, ab initio et ante saecula creata sum, idest, praevisa sum creaturae uniri. Vel, per hoc quod sapientia creata et genita nuncupatur, modus divinae generationis nobis insinuatur. In generatione enim, quod generatur accipit naturam generantis, quod perfectionis est, in creatione vero, creans non mutatur, sed creatum non recipit naturam creantis. Dicitur ergo filius simul creatus et genitus, ut ex creatione accipiatur immutabilitas patris, et ex generatione unitas naturae in patre et filio. Et sic exponitur intellectus huius Scripturae ab Hilario, in libro de Synod. Auctoritates autem inductae non loquuntur de spiritu sancto, sed de spiritu creato; qui quandoque dicitur ventus, quandoque aer, quandoque flatus hominis, quandoque etiam anima, vel quaecumque substantia invisibilis.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30130)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in divinis non sit potentia respectu actuum notionalium. Omnis enim potentia est vel activa, vel passiva. Sed neutra hic competere potest, potentia enim passiva in Deo non est, ut supra ostensum est; potentia vero activa non competit uni personae respectu alterius, cum personae divinae non sint factae, ut ostensum est. Ergo in divinis non est potentia ad actus notionales.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30131)\n\nPraeterea, potentia dicitur ad possibile. Sed divinae personae non sunt de numero possibilium, sed de numero necessariorum. Ergo respectu actuum notionalium, quibus divinae personae procedunt, non debet poni potentia in divinis.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30132)\n\nPraeterea, filius procedit ut verbum, quod est conceptio intellectus, spiritus autem sanctus procedit ut amor, qui pertinet ad voluntatem. Sed potentia in Deo dicitur per comparationem ad effectus, non autem per comparationem ad intelligere et velle, ut supra habitum est. Ergo in divinis non debet dici potentia per comparationem ad actus notionales.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30133)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, contra Maximinum haereticum, *si Deus pater non potuit generare filium sibi aequalem, ubi est omnipotentia Dei patris?* Est ergo in divinis potentia respectu actuum notionalium.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30134)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ponuntur actus notionales in divinis, ita necesse est ibi ponere potentiam respectu huiusmodi actuum, cum potentia nihil aliud significet quam principium alicuius actus. Unde, cum patrem intelligamus ut principium generationis, et patrem et filium ut principium spirationis, necesse est quod patri attribuamus potentiam generandi, et patri et filio potentiam spirandi. Quia potentia generandi significat id quo generans generat, omne autem generans generat aliquo, unde in omni generante oportet ponere potentiam generandi, et in spirante potentiam spirandi.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30135)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut secundum actus notionales non procedit aliqua persona ut facta, ita neque potentia ad actus notionales dicitur in divinis per respectum ad aliquam personam factam, sed solum per respectum ad personam procedentem.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30136)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod possibile, secundum quod necessario opponitur, sequitur potentiam passivam, quae non est in divinis. Unde neque in divinis est aliquid possibile per modum istum, sed solum secundum quod possibile continetur sub necessario. Sic autem dici potest quod, sicut Deum esse est possibile, sic filium generari est possibile.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30137)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod potentia significat principium. Principium autem distinctionem importat ab eo cuius est principium. Consideratur autem duplex distinctio in his quae dicuntur de Deo, una secundum rem, alia secundum rationem tantum. Secundum rem quidem, Deus distinguitur per essentiam a rebus quarum est per creationem principium, sicut una persona distinguitur ab alia, cuius est principium, secundum actum notionalem. Sed actio ab agente non distinguitur in Deo nisi secundum rationem tantum, alioquin actio esset accidens in Deo. Et ideo respectu illarum actionum secundum quas aliquae res procedunt distinctae a Deo, vel essentialiter vel personaliter, potest Deo attribui potentia, secundum propriam rationem principii. Et ideo, sicut potentiam ponimus creandi in Deo, ita possumus ponere potentiam generandi vel spirandi. Sed intelligere et velle non sunt tales actus qui designent processionem alicuius rei a Deo distinctae, vel essentialiter vel personaliter. Unde respectu horum actuum, non potest salvari ratio potentiae in Deo, nisi secundum modum intelligendi et significandi tantum; prout diversimode significatur in Deo intellectus et intelligere, cum tamen ipsum intelligere Dei sit eius essentia, non habens principium.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30138)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod potentia generandi vel spirandi significet relationem, et non essentiam. Potentia enim significat principium, ut ex eius definitione patet, dicitur enim potentia activa esse principium agendi, ut patet in V Metaphys. Sed principium in divinis respectu personae dicitur notionaliter. Ergo potentia in divinis non significat essentiam, sed relationem.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30139)\n\nPraeterea, in divinis non differt posse et agere. Sed generatio in divinis significat relationem. Ergo et potentia generandi.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30140)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae significant essentiam in divinis, communia sunt tribus personis. Sed potentia generandi non est communis tribus personis, sed propria patri. Ergo non significat essentiam.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30141)\n\nSed contra est quod, sicut Deus potest generare filium, ita et vult. Sed voluntas generandi significat essentiam. Ergo et potentia generandi.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30142)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod potentia generandi significat relationem in divinis. Sed hoc esse non potest. Nam illud proprie dicitur potentia in quocumque agente, quo agens agit. Omne autem producens aliquid per suam actionem, producit sibi simile quantum ad formam qua agit sicut homo genitus est similis generanti in natura humana, cuius virtute pater potest generare hominem. Illud ergo est potentia generativa in aliquo generante, in quo genitum similatur generanti. Filius autem Dei similatur patri gignenti in natura divina. Unde natura divina in patre, est potentia generandi in ipso. Unde et Hilarius dicit, in V de Trin., *nativitas Dei non potest eam ex qua profecta est, non tenere naturam; nec enim aliud quam Deus subsistit, quod non aliunde quam de Deo subsistit*. Sic igitur dicendum est quod potentia generandi principaliter significat divinam essentiam, ut Magister dicit, VII dist. I Sent.; non autem tantum relationem. Nec etiam essentiam inquantum est idem relationi, ut significet ex aequo utrumque. Licet enim paternitas ut forma patris significetur, est tamen proprietas personalis, habens se ad personam patris, ut forma individualis ad aliquod individuum creatum. Forma autem individualis, in rebus creatis, constituit personam generantem, non autem est quo generans generat, alioquin Socrates generaret Socratem. Unde neque paternitas potest intelligi ut quo pater generat, sed ut constituens personam generantis, alioquin pater generaret patrem. Sed id quo pater generat, est natura divina, in qua sibi filius assimilatur. Et secundum hoc Damascenus dicit quod generatio est opus naturae, non sicut generantis, sed sicut eius quo generans generat. Et ideo potentia generandi significat in recto naturam divinam, sed in obliquo relationem.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30143)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod potentia non significat ipsam relationem principii, alioquin esset in genere relationis, sed significat id quod est principium; non quidem sicut agens dicitur principium, sed sicut id quo agens agit, dicitur principium agens autem distinguitur a facto, et generans a generato, sed id quo generans generat, est commune genito et generanti; et tanto perfectius, quanto perfectior fuerit generatio. Unde, cum divina generatio sit perfectissima, id quo generans generat, est commune genito et generanti, et idem numero, non solum specie, sicut in rebus creatis. Per hoc ergo quod dicimus quod essentia divina est principium quo generans generat, non sequitur quod essentia divina distinguatur; sicut sequeretur, si diceretur quod essentia divina generat.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30144)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sic est idem in divinis potentia generandi cum generatione, sicut essentia divina cum generatione et paternitate est idem re, sed non ratione.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30145)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum dico potentiam generandi, potentia significatur in recto, et generatio in obliquo; sicut si dicerem essentiam patris. Unde quantum ad essentiam quae significatur, potentia generandi communis est tribus personis, sed quantum ad notionem quae connotatur, propria est personae patris.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30146)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionalis ad plures personas terminari possit, ita quod sint plures personae genitae vel spiratae in divinis. Cuicumque enim inest potentia generandi, potest generare. Sed filio inest potentia generandi. Ergo potest generare. Non autem seipsum. Ergo alium filium. Ergo possunt esse plures filii in divinis.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30147)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, contra Maximinum, *filius non genuit creatorem. Neque enim non potuit, sed non oportuit*.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30148)\n\nPraeterea, Deus pater est potentior ad generandum quam pater creatus. Sed unus homo potest generare plures filios. Ergo et Deus, praecipue cum potentia patris, uno filio generato, non diminuatur.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30149)\n\nSed contra est quod in divinis non differt esse et posse. Si igitur in divinis possent esse plures filii, essent plures filii. Et ita essent plures personae quam tres in divinis, quod est haereticum.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30150)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Athanasius dicit, *in divinis est tantum unus pater, unus filius, unus spiritus sanctus*. Cuius quidem ratio quadruplex assignari potest. Prima quidem ex parte relationum, quibus solum personae distinguuntur. Cum enim personae divinae sint ipsae relationes subsistentes, non possent esse plures patres vel plures filii in divinis, nisi essent plures paternitates et plures filiationes. Quod quidem esse non posset nisi secundum materialem rerum distinctionem, formae enim unius speciei non multiplicantur nisi secundum materiam, quae in divinis non est. Unde in divinis non potest esse nisi una tantum filiatio subsistens; sicut et albedo subsistens non posset esse nisi una. Secunda vero ex modo processionum. Quia Deus omnia intelligit et vult uno et simplici actu. Unde non potest esse nisi una persona procedens per modum verbi, quae est filius; et una tantum per modum amoris, quae est spiritus sanctus. Tertia ratio sumitur ex modo procedendi. Quia personae ipsae procedunt naturaliter, ut dictum est, natura autem determinatur ad unum. Quarta ex perfectione divinarum personarum. Ex hoc enim est perfectus filius, quod tota filiatio divina in eo continetur, et quod est tantum unus filius. Et similiter dicendum est de aliis personis.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30151)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, quamvis simpliciter concedendum sit quod potentiam quam habet pater, habeat filius; non tamen concedendum est quod filius habeat potentiam generandi, si generandi sit gerundivum verbi activi, ut sit sensus quod filius habeat potentiam ad generandum. Sicut, licet idem esse sit patris et filii, non tamen convenit filio esse patrem, propter notionale adiunctum. Si tamen hoc quod dico generandi, sit gerundivum verbi passivi, potentia generandi est in filio, idest ut generetur. Et similiter si sit gerundivum verbi impersonalis, ut sit sensus, potentia generandi, idest qua ab aliqua persona generatur.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30152)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Augustinus in verbis illis non intendit dicere quod filius posset generare filium, sed quod hoc non est ex impotentia filii, quod non generet, ut infra patebit.\n\n[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30153)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod immaterialitas et perfectio divina requirit ut non possint esse plures filii in divinis, sicut dictum est. Unde quod non sint plures filii, non est ex impotentia patris ad generandum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0002","text":"[I q. 41 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de personis in comparatione ad actus notionales. Et circa hoc quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum actus notionales sint attribuendi personis. Secundo, utrum huiusmodi actus sint necessarii vel voluntarii. Tertio, utrum, secundum huiusmodi actus, persona procedat de nihilo, vel de aliquo. Quarto, utrum in divinis sit ponere potentiam respectu actuum notionalium. Quinto, quid significet huiusmodi potentia. Sexto, utrum actus notionalis ad plures personas terminari possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0005","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionales non sint personis attribuendi. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *omnia genera, cum quis in divinam vertit praedicationem, in divinam mutantur substantiam, exceptis relativis*. Sed actio est unum de decem generibus. Si igitur actio aliqua Deo attribuitur, ad eius essentiam pertinebit, et non ad notionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0007","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, V de Trin., omne quod de Deo dicitur, aut dicitur secundum substantiam, aut secundum relationem. Sed ea quae ad substantiam pertinent, significantur per essentialia attributa, quae vero ad relationem, per nomina personarum et per nomina proprietatum. Non sunt ergo, praeter haec, attribuendi personis notionales actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0009","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0010","text":"Praeterea, proprium actionis est ex se passionem inferre. Sed in divinis non ponimus passiones. Ergo neque actus notionales ibi ponendi sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0011","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *proprium patris est, quod filium genuit*. Sed generatio actus quidam est. Ergo actus notionales ponendi sunt in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0013","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in divinis personis attenditur distinctio secundum originem. Origo autem convenienter designari non potest nisi per aliquos actus. Ad designandum igitur ordinem originis in divinis personis, necessarium fuit attribuere personis actus notionales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0015","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod omnis origo designatur per aliquem actum. Duplex autem ordo originis attribui Deo potest. Unus quidem, secundum quod creatura ab eo progreditur, et hoc commune est tribus personis. Et ideo actiones quae attribuuntur Deo ad designandum processum creaturarum ab ipso, ad essentiam pertinent. Alius autem ordo originis in divinis attenditur secundum processionem personae a persona. Unde actus designantes huius originis ordinem, notionales dicuntur, quia notiones personarum sunt personarum habitudines ad invicem, ut ex dictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0017","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod actus notionales secundum modum significandi tantum differunt a relationibus personarum; sed re sunt omnino idem. Unde Magister dicit, in I Sent., XXVI dist., quod generatio et nativitas aliis nominibus dicuntur paternitas et filiatio. Ad cuius evidentiam, attendendum est quod primo coniicere potuimus originem alicuius ab alio, ex motu, quod enim aliqua res a sua dispositione removeretur per motum, manifestum fuit hoc ab aliqua causa accidere. Et ideo actio, secundum primam nominis impositionem, importat originem motus, sicut enim motus, prout est in mobili ab aliquo, dicitur passio; ita origo ipsius motus, secundum quod incipit ab alio et terminatur in id quod movetur, vocatur actio. Remoto igitur motu, actio nihil aliud importat quam ordinem originis, secundum quod a causa aliqua vel principio procedit in id quod est a principio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0019","text":"Unde, cum in divinis non sit motus, actio personalis producentis personam, nihil aliud est quam habitudo principii ad personam quae est a principio. Quae quidem habitudines sunt ipsae relationes vel notiones. Quia tamen de divinis et intelligibilibus rebus loqui non possumus nisi secundum modum rerum sensibilium, a quibus cognitionem accipimus; et in quibus actiones et passiones, inquantum motum implicant, aliud sunt a relationibus quae ex actionibus et passionibus consequuntur, oportuit seorsum significari habitudines personarum per modum actus, et seorsum per modum relationum. Et sic patet quod sunt idem secundum rem, sed differunt solum secundum modum significandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0020","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod actio, secundum quod importat originem motus, infert ex se passionem, sic autem non ponitur actio in divinis personis. Unde non ponuntur ibi passiones, nisi solum grammatice loquendo, quantum ad modum significandi; sicut patri attribuimus generare, et filio generari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0023","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionales sint voluntarii. Dicit enim Hilarius, in libro de Synod., *non naturali necessitate ductus, pater genuit filium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0025","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0026","text":"Praeterea, apostolus, Coloss. I, *transtulit nos in regnum filii dilectionis suae*. Dilectio autem voluntatis est. Ergo filius genitus est a patre, voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0027","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0028","text":"Praeterea, nihil magis est voluntarium quam amor. Sed spiritus sanctus procedit a patre et filio ut amor. Ergo procedit voluntarie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0029","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0030","text":"Praeterea, filius procedit per modum intellectus, ut verbum. Sed omne verbum procedit a dicente per voluntatem. Ergo filius procedit a patre per voluntatem, et non per naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0031","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0032","text":"Praeterea, quod non est voluntarium, est necessarium. Si igitur pater non genuit filium voluntate, videtur sequi quod necessitate genuerit. Quod est contra Augustinum, in libro ad Orosium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0033","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0034","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in eodem libro, quod *neque voluntate genuit pater filium, neque necessitate*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0035","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum dicitur aliquid esse vel fieri voluntate, dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo, ut ablativus designet concomitantiam tantum, sicut possum dicere quod ego sum homo mea voluntate, quia scilicet volo me esse hominem. Et hoc modo potest dici quod pater genuit filium voluntate, sicut et est voluntate Deus, quia vult se esse Deum, et vult se generare filium. Alio modo sic, quod ablativus importet habitudinem principii, sicut dicitur quod artifex operatur voluntate, quia voluntas est principium operis. Et secundum hunc modum, dicendum est quod Deus pater non genuit filium voluntate; sed voluntate produxit creaturam. Unde in libro de Synod. dicitur, *si quis voluntate Dei, tanquam unum aliquid de creaturis, filium factum dicat, anathema sit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0037","text":"Et huius ratio est, quia voluntas et natura secundum hoc differunt in causando, quia natura determinata est ad unum; sed voluntas non est determinata ad unum. Cuius ratio est, quia effectus assimilatur formae agentis per quam agit. Manifestum est autem quod unius rei non est nisi una forma naturalis, per quam res habet esse, unde quale ipsum est, tale facit. Sed forma per quam voluntas agit, non est una tantum, sed sunt plures, secundum quod sunt plures rationes intellectae, unde quod voluntate agitur, non est tale quale est agens, sed quale vult et intelligit illud esse agens. Eorum igitur voluntas principium est, quae possunt sic vel aliter esse. Eorum autem quae non possunt nisi sic esse, principium natura est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0038","text":"Quod autem potest sic vel aliter esse, longe est a natura divina, sed hoc pertinet ad rationem creaturae, quia Deus est per se necesse esse, creatura autem est facta ex nihilo. Et ideo Ariani, volentes ad hoc deducere quod filius sit creatura, dixerunt quod pater genuit filium voluntate, secundum quod voluntas designat principium. Nobis autem dicendum est quod pater genuit filium non voluntate, sed natura. Unde Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., *omnibus creaturis substantiam Dei voluntas attulit; sed naturam filio dedit ex impassibili ac non nata substantia perfecta nativitas. Talia enim cuncta creata sunt, qualia Deus esse voluit, filius autem, natus ex Deo, talis subsistit, qualis et Deus est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0039","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod auctoritas illa inducitur contra illos qui a generatione filii etiam concomitantiam paternae voluntatis removebant, dicentes sic eum natura genuisse filium, ut inde voluntas generandi ei non adesset, sicut nos multa naturali necessitate contra voluntatem patimur, ut mortem, senectutem, et huiusmodi defectus. Et hoc patet per praecedentia et subsequentia. Sic enim ibi dicitur, *non enim, nolente patre, vel coactus pater, vel naturali necessitate inductus cum nollet, genuit filium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0041","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod apostolus nominat Christum filium dilectionis Dei, inquantum est a Deo superabundanter dilectus, non quod dilectio sit principium generationis filii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0043","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0044","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod etiam voluntas, inquantum est natura quaedam, aliquid naturaliter vult; sicut voluntas hominis naturaliter tendit ad beatitudinem. Et similiter Deus naturaliter vult et amat seipsum. Sed circa alia a se, voluntas Dei se habet ad utrumque quodammodo, ut dictum est. Spiritus autem sanctus procedit ut amor, inquantum Deus amat seipsum. Unde naturaliter procedit, quamvis per modum voluntatis procedat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0045","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0046","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod etiam in conceptionibus intellectualibus fit reductio ad prima, quae naturaliter intelliguntur. Deus autem naturaliter intelligit seipsum. Et secundum hoc, conceptio verbi divini est naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0047","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0048","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod necessarium dicitur aliquid per se, et per aliud. Per aliud quidem dupliciter. Uno modo, sicut per causam agentem et cogentem, et sic necessarium dicitur quod est violentum. Alio modo, sicut per causam finalem, sicut dicitur aliquid esse necessarium in his quae sunt ad finem, inquantum sine hoc non potest esse finis, vel bene esse. Et neutro istorum modorum divina generatio est necessaria, quia Deus non est propter finem, neque coactio cadit in ipsum. Per se autem dicitur aliquid necessarium, quod non potest non esse. Et sic Deum esse est necessarium. Et hoc modo patrem generare filium est necessarium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0049","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0050","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0051","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionales non sint de aliquo. Quia si pater generat filium de aliquo, aut de seipso, aut de aliquo alio. Si de aliquo alio, cum id de quo aliquid generatur, sit in eo quod generatur, sequitur quod aliquid alienum a patre sit in filio. Quod est contra Hilarium, VII de Trin., ubi dicit, *nihil in his diversum est vel alienum*. Si autem filium generat pater de seipso, id autem de quo aliquid generatur, si sit permanens, recipit eius praedicationem quod generatur; sicut dicimus quod homo est albus, quia homo permanet, cum de non albo fit albus, sequitur igitur quod pater vel non permaneat, genito filio, vel quod pater sit filius, quod est falsum. Non ergo pater generat filium de aliquo, sed de nihilo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0052","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0053","text":"Praeterea, id de quo aliquid generatur, est principium eius quod generatur. Si ergo pater generat filium de essentia vel natura sua sequitur quod essentia vel natura patris sit principium filii. Sed non principium materiale, quia materia locum in divinis non habet. Ergo est principium quasi activum, sicut generans est principium geniti. Et ita sequitur quod essentia generet, quod supra improbatum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0054","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0055","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit quod tres personae non sunt ex eadem essentia, quia non est aliud essentia et persona. Sed persona filii non est aliud ab essentia patris. Ergo filius non est de essentia patris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0056","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0057","text":"Praeterea, omnis creatura est ex nihilo. Sed filius in Scripturis dicitur creatura, dicitur enim Eccli. XXIV, ex ore sapientiae genitae, *ego ex ore altissimi prodii, primogenita ante omnem creaturam*; et postea ex ore eiusdem sapientiae dicitur, *ab initio, et ante saecula, creata sum*. Ergo filius non est genitus ex aliquo, sed ex nihilo. Et similiter potest obiici de spiritu sancto, propter hoc quod dicitur, Zac. XII *dixit dominus, extendens caelum et fundans terram, et creans spiritum hominis in eo*; et Amos IV, secundum aliam litteram, *ego formans montes, et creans spiritum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0058","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0059","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *pater Deus de sua natura sine initio genuit filium sibi aequalem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0060","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0061","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod filius non est genitus de nihilo, sed de substantia patris. Ostensum est enim supra quod paternitas, et filiatio, et nativitas, vere et proprie est in divinis. Hoc autem interest inter generationem veram, per quam aliquis procedit ut filius, et factionem, quod faciens facit aliquid de exteriori materia, sicut scamnum facit artifex de ligno; homo autem generat filium de seipso. Sicut autem artifex creatus facit aliquid ex materia, ita Deus facit ex nihilo, ut infra ostendetur, non quod nihilum cedat in substantiam rei, sed quia ab ipso tota substantia rei producitur, nullo alio praesupposito. Si ergo filius procederet a patre ut de nihilo existens, hoc modo se haberet ad patrem ut artificiatum ad artificem, quod manifestum est nomen filiationis proprie habere non posse, sed solum secundum aliquam similitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0062","text":"Unde relinquitur quod, si filius Dei procederet a patre quasi existens ex nihilo, non esset vere et proprie filius. Cuius contrarium dicitur I Ioan. ult., *ut simus in vero filio eius Iesu Christo*. Filius igitur Dei verus non est ex nihilo, nec factus, sed tantum genitus. Si qui autem ex nihilo a Deo facti filii Dei dicantur, hoc erit metaphorice, secundum aliqualem assimilationem ad eum qui vere filius est. Unde, inquantum solus est verus et naturalis Dei filius, dicitur unigenitus, secundum illud Ioan. I *unigenitus, qui est in sinu patris, ipse enarravit*. Inquantum vero per assimilationem ad ipsum alii dicuntur filii adoptivi, quasi metaphorice dicitur esse primogenitus, secundum illud Rom. VIII, *quos praescivit, et praedestinavit conformes fieri imaginis filii sui, ut sit ipse primogenitus in multis fratribus*. Relinquitur ergo quod Dei filius sit genitus de substantia patris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0063","text":"Aliter tamen quam filius hominis. Pars enim substantiae hominis generantis transit in substantiam geniti. Sed divina natura impartibilis est. Unde necesse est quod pater, generando filium, non partem naturae in ipsum transfuderit, sed totam naturam ei communicaverit, remanente distinctione solum secundum originem, ut ex dictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0064","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0065","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum filius dicitur natus de patre, haec praepositio de significat principium generans consubstantiale; non autem principium materiale. Quod enim producitur de materia, fit per transmutationem illius de quo producitur, in aliquam formam; divina autem essentia non est transmutabilis, neque alterius formae susceptiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0066","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0067","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur filius genitus de essentia patris, secundum expositionem Magistri, V dist. I Sent., designat habitudinem principii quasi activi, ubi sic exponit, *filius est genitus de essentia patris, idest de patre essentia*; propter hoc quod Augustinus, XV libro de Trin., dicit, *tale est quod dico, de patre essentia, ac si expressius dicerem, de patris essentia*. Sed hoc non videtur sufficere ad sensum huiusmodi locutionis. Possumus enim dicere quod creatura est ex Deo essentia, non tamen quod sit ex essentia Dei. Unde aliter dici potest quod haec praepositio de semper denotat consubstantialitatem. Unde non dicimus quod domus sit de aedificatore, cum non sit causa consubstantialis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0068","text":"Possumus autem dicere quod aliquid sit de aliquo, quocumque modo illud significetur ut principium consubstantiale, sive illud sit principium activum, sicut filius dicitur esse de patre; sive sit principium materiale, sicut cultellus dicitur esse de ferro; sive sit principium formale, in his dumtaxat in quibus ipsae formae sunt subsistentes, et non advenientes alteri; possumus enim dicere quod Angelus aliquis est de natura intellectuali. Et per hunc modum dicimus quod filius est genitus de essentia patris; inquantum essentia patris, filio per generationem communicata, in eo subsistit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0069","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0070","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur, filius est genitus de essentia patris, additur aliquid respectu cuius potest salvari distinctio. Sed cum dicitur quod tres personae sunt de essentia divina, non ponitur aliquid respectu cuius possit importari distinctio per praepositionem significata. Et ideo non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0071","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0072","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, cum dicitur, sapientia est creata, potest intelligi, non de sapientia quae est filius Dei, sed de sapientia creata, quam Deus indidit creaturis, dicitur enim Eccli. I, *ipse creavit eam*, scilicet sapientiam, *spiritu sancto, et effudit illam super omnia opera sua*. Neque est inconveniens quod in uno contextu locutionis loquatur Scriptura de sapientia genita et creata, quia sapientia creata est participatio quaedam sapientiae increatae. Vel potest referri ad naturam creatam assumptam a filio, ut sit sensus, ab initio et ante saecula creata sum, idest, praevisa sum creaturae uniri. Vel, per hoc quod sapientia creata et genita nuncupatur, modus divinae generationis nobis insinuatur. In generatione enim, quod generatur accipit naturam generantis, quod perfectionis est, in creatione vero, creans non mutatur, sed creatum non recipit naturam creantis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0073","text":"Dicitur ergo filius simul creatus et genitus, ut ex creatione accipiatur immutabilitas patris, et ex generatione unitas naturae in patre et filio. Et sic exponitur intellectus huius Scripturae ab Hilario, in libro de Synod. Auctoritates autem inductae non loquuntur de spiritu sancto, sed de spiritu creato; qui quandoque dicitur ventus, quandoque aer, quandoque flatus hominis, quandoque etiam anima, vel quaecumque substantia invisibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0074","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0075","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0076","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in divinis non sit potentia respectu actuum notionalium. Omnis enim potentia est vel activa, vel passiva. Sed neutra hic competere potest, potentia enim passiva in Deo non est, ut supra ostensum est; potentia vero activa non competit uni personae respectu alterius, cum personae divinae non sint factae, ut ostensum est. Ergo in divinis non est potentia ad actus notionales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0077","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0078","text":"Praeterea, potentia dicitur ad possibile. Sed divinae personae non sunt de numero possibilium, sed de numero necessariorum. Ergo respectu actuum notionalium, quibus divinae personae procedunt, non debet poni potentia in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0079","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0080","text":"Praeterea, filius procedit ut verbum, quod est conceptio intellectus, spiritus autem sanctus procedit ut amor, qui pertinet ad voluntatem. Sed potentia in Deo dicitur per comparationem ad effectus, non autem per comparationem ad intelligere et velle, ut supra habitum est. Ergo in divinis non debet dici potentia per comparationem ad actus notionales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0081","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0082","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, contra Maximinum haereticum, *si Deus pater non potuit generare filium sibi aequalem, ubi est omnipotentia Dei patris?* Est ergo in divinis potentia respectu actuum notionalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0083","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0084","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ponuntur actus notionales in divinis, ita necesse est ibi ponere potentiam respectu huiusmodi actuum, cum potentia nihil aliud significet quam principium alicuius actus. Unde, cum patrem intelligamus ut principium generationis, et patrem et filium ut principium spirationis, necesse est quod patri attribuamus potentiam generandi, et patri et filio potentiam spirandi. Quia potentia generandi significat id quo generans generat, omne autem generans generat aliquo, unde in omni generante oportet ponere potentiam generandi, et in spirante potentiam spirandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0085","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0086","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut secundum actus notionales non procedit aliqua persona ut facta, ita neque potentia ad actus notionales dicitur in divinis per respectum ad aliquam personam factam, sed solum per respectum ad personam procedentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0087","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0088","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod possibile, secundum quod necessario opponitur, sequitur potentiam passivam, quae non est in divinis. Unde neque in divinis est aliquid possibile per modum istum, sed solum secundum quod possibile continetur sub necessario. Sic autem dici potest quod, sicut Deum esse est possibile, sic filium generari est possibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0089","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0090","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod potentia significat principium. Principium autem distinctionem importat ab eo cuius est principium. Consideratur autem duplex distinctio in his quae dicuntur de Deo, una secundum rem, alia secundum rationem tantum. Secundum rem quidem, Deus distinguitur per essentiam a rebus quarum est per creationem principium, sicut una persona distinguitur ab alia, cuius est principium, secundum actum notionalem. Sed actio ab agente non distinguitur in Deo nisi secundum rationem tantum, alioquin actio esset accidens in Deo. Et ideo respectu illarum actionum secundum quas aliquae res procedunt distinctae a Deo, vel essentialiter vel personaliter, potest Deo attribui potentia, secundum propriam rationem principii. Et ideo, sicut potentiam ponimus creandi in Deo, ita possumus ponere potentiam generandi vel spirandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0091","text":"Sed intelligere et velle non sunt tales actus qui designent processionem alicuius rei a Deo distinctae, vel essentialiter vel personaliter. Unde respectu horum actuum, non potest salvari ratio potentiae in Deo, nisi secundum modum intelligendi et significandi tantum; prout diversimode significatur in Deo intellectus et intelligere, cum tamen ipsum intelligere Dei sit eius essentia, non habens principium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0092","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0093","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0094","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod potentia generandi vel spirandi significet relationem, et non essentiam. Potentia enim significat principium, ut ex eius definitione patet, dicitur enim potentia activa esse principium agendi, ut patet in V Metaphys. Sed principium in divinis respectu personae dicitur notionaliter. Ergo potentia in divinis non significat essentiam, sed relationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0095","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0096","text":"Praeterea, in divinis non differt posse et agere. Sed generatio in divinis significat relationem. Ergo et potentia generandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0097","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0098","text":"Praeterea, ea quae significant essentiam in divinis, communia sunt tribus personis. Sed potentia generandi non est communis tribus personis, sed propria patri. Ergo non significat essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0099","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0100","text":"Sed contra est quod, sicut Deus potest generare filium, ita et vult. Sed voluntas generandi significat essentiam. Ergo et potentia generandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0101","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0102","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod potentia generandi significat relationem in divinis. Sed hoc esse non potest. Nam illud proprie dicitur potentia in quocumque agente, quo agens agit. Omne autem producens aliquid per suam actionem, producit sibi simile quantum ad formam qua agit sicut homo genitus est similis generanti in natura humana, cuius virtute pater potest generare hominem. Illud ergo est potentia generativa in aliquo generante, in quo genitum similatur generanti. Filius autem Dei similatur patri gignenti in natura divina. Unde natura divina in patre, est potentia generandi in ipso. Unde et Hilarius dicit, in V de Trin., *nativitas Dei non potest eam ex qua profecta est, non tenere naturam; nec enim aliud quam Deus subsistit, quod non aliunde quam de Deo subsistit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0103","text":"Sic igitur dicendum est quod potentia generandi principaliter significat divinam essentiam, ut Magister dicit, VII dist. I Sent.; non autem tantum relationem. Nec etiam essentiam inquantum est idem relationi, ut significet ex aequo utrumque. Licet enim paternitas ut forma patris significetur, est tamen proprietas personalis, habens se ad personam patris, ut forma individualis ad aliquod individuum creatum. Forma autem individualis, in rebus creatis, constituit personam generantem, non autem est quo generans generat, alioquin Socrates generaret Socratem. Unde neque paternitas potest intelligi ut quo pater generat, sed ut constituens personam generantis, alioquin pater generaret patrem. Sed id quo pater generat, est natura divina, in qua sibi filius assimilatur. Et secundum hoc Damascenus dicit quod generatio est opus naturae, non sicut generantis, sed sicut eius quo generans generat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0104","text":"Et ideo potentia generandi significat in recto naturam divinam, sed in obliquo relationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0105","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0106","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod potentia non significat ipsam relationem principii, alioquin esset in genere relationis, sed significat id quod est principium; non quidem sicut agens dicitur principium, sed sicut id quo agens agit, dicitur principium agens autem distinguitur a facto, et generans a generato, sed id quo generans generat, est commune genito et generanti; et tanto perfectius, quanto perfectior fuerit generatio. Unde, cum divina generatio sit perfectissima, id quo generans generat, est commune genito et generanti, et idem numero, non solum specie, sicut in rebus creatis. Per hoc ergo quod dicimus quod essentia divina est principium quo generans generat, non sequitur quod essentia divina distinguatur; sicut sequeretur, si diceretur quod essentia divina generat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0107","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0108","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sic est idem in divinis potentia generandi cum generatione, sicut essentia divina cum generatione et paternitate est idem re, sed non ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0109","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0110","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum dico potentiam generandi, potentia significatur in recto, et generatio in obliquo; sicut si dicerem essentiam patris. Unde quantum ad essentiam quae significatur, potentia generandi communis est tribus personis, sed quantum ad notionem quae connotatur, propria est personae patris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0111","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0112","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0113","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus notionalis ad plures personas terminari possit, ita quod sint plures personae genitae vel spiratae in divinis. Cuicumque enim inest potentia generandi, potest generare. Sed filio inest potentia generandi. Ergo potest generare. Non autem seipsum. Ergo alium filium. Ergo possunt esse plures filii in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0114","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0115","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, contra Maximinum, *filius non genuit creatorem. Neque enim non potuit, sed non oportuit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0116","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0117","text":"Praeterea, Deus pater est potentior ad generandum quam pater creatus. Sed unus homo potest generare plures filios. Ergo et Deus, praecipue cum potentia patris, uno filio generato, non diminuatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0118","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0119","text":"Sed contra est quod in divinis non differt esse et posse. Si igitur in divinis possent esse plures filii, essent plures filii. Et ita essent plures personae quam tres in divinis, quod est haereticum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0120","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0121","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Athanasius dicit, *in divinis est tantum unus pater, unus filius, unus spiritus sanctus*. Cuius quidem ratio quadruplex assignari potest. Prima quidem ex parte relationum, quibus solum personae distinguuntur. Cum enim personae divinae sint ipsae relationes subsistentes, non possent esse plures patres vel plures filii in divinis, nisi essent plures paternitates et plures filiationes. Quod quidem esse non posset nisi secundum materialem rerum distinctionem, formae enim unius speciei non multiplicantur nisi secundum materiam, quae in divinis non est. Unde in divinis non potest esse nisi una tantum filiatio subsistens; sicut et albedo subsistens non posset esse nisi una. Secunda vero ex modo processionum. Quia Deus omnia intelligit et vult uno et simplici actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0122","text":"Unde non potest esse nisi una persona procedens per modum verbi, quae est filius; et una tantum per modum amoris, quae est spiritus sanctus. Tertia ratio sumitur ex modo procedendi. Quia personae ipsae procedunt naturaliter, ut dictum est, natura autem determinatur ad unum. Quarta ex perfectione divinarum personarum. Ex hoc enim est perfectus filius, quod tota filiatio divina in eo continetur, et quod est tantum unus filius. Et similiter dicendum est de aliis personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0123","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0124","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, quamvis simpliciter concedendum sit quod potentiam quam habet pater, habeat filius; non tamen concedendum est quod filius habeat potentiam generandi, si generandi sit gerundivum verbi activi, ut sit sensus quod filius habeat potentiam ad generandum. Sicut, licet idem esse sit patris et filii, non tamen convenit filio esse patrem, propter notionale adiunctum. Si tamen hoc quod dico generandi, sit gerundivum verbi passivi, potentia generandi est in filio, idest ut generetur. Et similiter si sit gerundivum verbi impersonalis, ut sit sensus, potentia generandi, idest qua ab aliqua persona generatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0125","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0126","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Augustinus in verbis illis non intendit dicere quod filius posset generare filium, sed quod hoc non est ex impotentia filii, quod non generet, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0127","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0128","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod immaterialitas et perfectio divina requirit ut non possint esse plures filii in divinis, sicut dictum est. Unde quod non sint plures filii, non est ex impotentia patris ad generandum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bf0be463cfda77df4ebf1686727d3d0e063a5ff289b81719afb6859303f86b7f","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T142416247923Z_d5709aef","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 41","source_sha256":"bf0be463cfda77df4ebf1686727d3d0e063a5ff289b81719afb6859303f86b7f","sha256":"4dc9cd5c87c800ab2f9370758c97e4e8cdf6477dd3edfd4760051308a0f69511","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:24:16.247944+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0002","text":"[I q. 41 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the persons in comparison with the notional acts. Concerning this, six questions are asked. First, whether notional acts are to be attributed to the persons. Second, whether acts of this kind are necessary or voluntary. Third, whether, according to acts of this kind, a person proceeds from nothing, or from something. Fourth, whether in the divine there is to be posited a power with respect to notional acts. Fifth, what such power signifies. Sixth, whether a notional act can terminate in several persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0005","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0006","text":"We proceed to the first as follows. It seems that notional acts are not to be attributed to the persons. For Boethius says, in the book *On the Trinity*, that *all genera, when one turns their predication toward the divine, are changed into divine substance, except relatives*. But action is one of the ten genera. Therefore, if any action is attributed to God, it will pertain to His essence, and not to a notion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0007","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0008","text":"Further, Augustine says, *On the Trinity*, V, that everything said of God is said either according to substance or according to relation. But those things which pertain to substance are signified by the essential attributes, whereas those which pertain to relation are signified by the names of the persons and by the names of the properties. Therefore, besides these, notional acts are not to be attributed to the persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0009","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0010","text":"Further, it is proper to action of itself to bring about passion. But we do not posit passions in the divine. Therefore, neither are notional acts to be posited there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0011","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *On Faith to Peter*, *it is proper to the Father that He begot the Son*. But generation is a certain act. Therefore, notional acts are to be posited in the divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0013","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that in the divine persons distinction is considered according to origin. But origin cannot fittingly be designated except through certain acts. Therefore, in order to designate the order of origin in the divine persons, it was necessary to attribute notional acts to the persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0015","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0016","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that every origin is designated through some act. Now a twofold order of origin can be attributed to God. One, indeed, according as the creature proceeds from Him, and this is common to the three persons. Therefore, the actions attributed to God in order to designate the procession of creatures from Him pertain to the essence. But another order of origin in the divine is considered according to the procession of person from person. Hence the acts designating the order of this origin are called notional, because the notions of the persons are the relations of the persons to one another, as is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0017","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0018","text":"To the second it must be said that notional acts differ from the relations of the persons only according to the mode of signifying; but in reality they are altogether the same. Hence the Master says, in *Sentences*, I, distinction XXVI, that generation and birth are called, by other names, paternity and filiation. For the clarification of this, it should be noted that we were first able to infer the origin of one thing from another from motion: for the fact that some thing was removed from its disposition through motion was manifestly seen to happen from some cause. And therefore action, according to the first imposition of the name, implies the origin of motion; for just as motion, insofar as it is in the movable thing from another, is called passion, so the origin of motion itself, insofar as it begins from another and terminates in that which is moved, is called action. Therefore, once motion is removed, action implies nothing other than an order of origin, according as it proceeds from some cause or principle into that which is from the principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0019","text":"Hence, since there is no motion in the divine, the personal action of the person producing a person is nothing other than the relation of a principle to the person who is from the principle. And these relations are the relations or notions themselves. Yet because we cannot speak of divine and intelligible things except according to the mode of sensible things, from which we receive knowledge—and in which actions and passions, insofar as they imply motion, are other than the relations that follow from actions and passions—it was necessary that the relations of the persons be signified separately in the mode of acts, and separately in the mode of relations. And thus it is clear that they are the same in reality, but differ only according to the mode of signifying.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0020","text":"[I q. 41 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0021","text":"To the third it must be said that action, insofar as it implies the origin of motion, of itself entails passion; but action is not posited in this way in the divine persons. Hence passions are not posited there, except only in grammatical speech, as regards the mode of signifying; just as we attribute to the Father begetting, and to the Son being begotten.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0023","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0024","text":"We proceed to the second as follows. It seems that notional acts are voluntary. For Hilary says, in the book *On the Synods*, *the Father begot the Son, not led by natural necessity*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0025","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0026","text":"Furthermore, the Apostle, in Colossians I, says, *he has transferred us into the kingdom of the Son of his love*. But love belongs to the will. Therefore the Son is begotten of the Father by will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0027","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0028","text":"Furthermore, nothing is more voluntary than love. But the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son as love. Therefore he proceeds voluntarily.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0029","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0030","text":"Furthermore, the Son proceeds by way of intellect, as the Word. But every word proceeds from the speaker by will. Therefore the Son proceeds from the Father by will, and not by nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0031","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0032","text":"Furthermore, what is not voluntary is necessary. If, therefore, the Father did not beget the Son by will, it seems to follow that he begot him by necessity. This is contrary to Augustine, in the book addressed to Orosius.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0033","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0034","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says in the same book that *the Father begot the Son neither by will nor by necessity*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0035","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0036","text":"I answer that, when something is said to be or to come to be by will, this can be understood in two ways. In one way, such that the ablative indicates mere concomitance, as I can say that I am a man by my will, namely, because I will myself to be a man. And in this way it can be said that the Father begot the Son by will, just as he is God by will, because he wills himself to be God, and wills himself to generate the Son. In another way, such that the ablative imports the relation of a principle, as an artisan is said to work by will, because the will is the principle of the work. And according to this mode, it must be said that God the Father did not beget the Son by will; rather, by will he produced the creature. Hence in the book *On the Synods* it is said: *If anyone says that the Son was made by the will of God, as though one thing among creatures, let him be anathema*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0037","text":"And the reason for this is that will and nature differ in causing in this respect: nature is determined to one thing, whereas will is not determined to one thing. The reason for this is that an effect is assimilated to the form of the agent through which it acts. Now it is manifest that of one thing there is only one natural form, through which a thing has being; hence, as it itself is, so it makes. But the form through which the will acts is not only one, but there are many, according as there are many understood intelligible accounts; hence, what is done by will is not such as the agent is, but such as the agent wills and understands it to be. Therefore, the will is the principle of those things that can be thus or otherwise. But of those things which cannot be otherwise than thus, nature is the principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0038","text":"But that which can be thus or otherwise is far from the divine nature; rather, this pertains to the notion of a creature, because God is necessary being through himself, whereas the creature is made from nothing. And therefore the Arians, wishing to lead to the conclusion that the Son is a creature, said that the Father begot the Son by will, insofar as will designates a principle. But we must say that the Father begot the Son not by will, but by nature. Hence Hilary says, in the book *On the Synods*: *Will bestowed the substance of God upon all creatures; but perfect birth gave nature to the Son from the impassible and unbegotten substance. For all created things were made such as God willed them to be; but the Son, born from God, subsists such as God also is*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0039","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that authority is adduced against those who removed even the concomitance of the paternal will from the generation of the Son, saying that he so begot the Son by nature that the will to generate was not present to him thereby, just as we undergo many things by natural necessity against our will, such as death, old age, and defects of this sort. And this is clear from what precedes and follows. For it is there said: *For the Father did not beget the Son unwilling, or as a Father compelled, or induced by natural necessity when he did not will it*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0041","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0042","text":"To the second, it must be said that the Apostle calls Christ the Son of God's love insofar as he is loved by God superabundantly, not because love is the principle of the generation of the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0043","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0044","text":"To the third, it must be said that even the will, insofar as it is a certain nature, naturally wills something; just as the human will naturally tends toward beatitude. And similarly God naturally wills and loves himself. But with respect to things other than himself, the will of God is disposed toward either in a certain way, as has been said. But the Holy Spirit proceeds as love, insofar as God loves himself. Hence he proceeds naturally, although he proceeds by way of will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0045","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0046","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that even in intellectual conceptions there is a reduction to first things, which are naturally understood. But God naturally understands himself. And according to this, the conception of the divine Word is natural.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0047","text":"[I q. 41 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0048","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that something is called necessary through itself and through another. Through another, indeed, in two ways. In one way, as through an efficient and compelling cause, and thus that which is violent is called necessary. In another way, as through a final cause, just as something is said to be necessary among things that are for an end, insofar as without it the end, or well-being, cannot be. And divine generation is not necessary in either of these ways, because God is not for the sake of an end, nor does compulsion apply to him. But something is called necessary through itself which cannot not be. And thus God's being is necessary. And in this way the Father's generating the Son is necessary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0049","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0050","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0051","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that notional acts are not from anything. For if the Father generates the Son from something, then either from himself or from something else. If from something else, since that from which something is generated is in that which is generated, it follows that something foreign to the Father is in the Son. This is contrary to Hilary, *On the Trinity*, VII, where he says, *there is nothing diverse or alien in them*. But if the Father generates the Son from himself, then that from which something is generated, if it remains, receives the predication of that which is generated; just as we say that a man is white, because the man remains when from non-white he becomes white. Therefore it follows that either the Father does not remain when the Son is generated, or that the Father is the Son, which is false. Therefore the Father does not generate the Son from anything, but from nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0052","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0053","text":"Further, that from which something is generated is the principle of that which is generated. Therefore, if the Father generates the Son from his essence or nature, it follows that the essence or nature of the Father is the principle of the Son. But it is not a material principle, because matter has no place in divine things. Therefore it is a principle as it were active, just as the generator is the principle of the generated. And thus it follows that the essence generates, which was disproved above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0054","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0055","text":"Further, Augustine says that the three persons are not from the same essence, because essence and person are not other than one another. But the person of the Son is not other than the essence of the Father. Therefore the Son is not from the essence of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0056","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0057","text":"Further, every creature is from nothing. But the Son is called a creature in the Scriptures; for it is said in Sirach XXIV, from the mouth of begotten Wisdom, *I came forth from the mouth of the Most High, the firstborn before every creature*; and afterwards it is said from the mouth of the same Wisdom, *from the beginning, and before the ages, I was created*. Therefore the Son is not begotten from anything, but from nothing. And similarly an objection can be made concerning the Holy Spirit, on account of what is said in Zechariah XII: *the Lord says, stretching out heaven and founding earth, and creating the spirit of man within him*; and in Amos IV, according to another reading: *I form the mountains, and create the spirit*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0058","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0059","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says in the book *On Faith to Peter*: *God the Father begot from his own nature, without beginning, a Son equal to himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0060","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0061","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Son is begotten not from nothing, but from the substance of the Father. For it has been shown above that fatherhood, sonship, and nativity are truly and properly in divine things. Now there is this difference between true generation, by which someone proceeds as a son, and making: that a maker makes something from exterior matter, as an artisan makes a bench from wood; but a man generates a son from himself. And just as a created artisan makes something from matter, so God makes from nothing, as will be shown below—not because nothing passes over into the substance of a thing, but because the entire substance of the thing is produced by him, with nothing else presupposed. Therefore, if the Son proceeded from the Father as existing from nothing, he would stand in relation to the Father as an artifact to an artisan; and it is manifest that he could not properly have the name of sonship, but only according to some likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0062","text":"Hence it remains that, if the Son of God proceeded from the Father as though existing from nothing, he would not be truly and properly a Son. The contrary of this is said in 1 John, at the end: *that we may be in his true Son, Jesus Christ*. Therefore the true Son of God is not from nothing, nor made, but only begotten. But if any who are made from nothing by God are called sons of God, this will be metaphorically, according to some assimilation to him who is truly Son. Hence, insofar as he alone is the true and natural Son of God, he is called the only-begotten, according to John I: *the only-begotten, who is in the bosom of the Father, he has declared him*. But insofar as others, by assimilation to him, are called adoptive sons, he is said, as it were metaphorically, to be the firstborn, according to Romans VIII: *those whom he foreknew, he also predestined to be made conformable to the image of his Son, that he himself might be the firstborn among many brethren*. Therefore it remains that the Son of God is begotten from the substance of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0063","text":"Yet otherwise than a son of man. For a part of the substance of the man generating passes into the substance of the one generated. But the divine nature is indivisible. Hence it is necessary that the Father, in generating the Son, has not transfused a part of the nature into him, but has communicated the whole nature to him, with distinction remaining only according to origin, as is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0064","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0065","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, when the Son is said to be born from the Father, this preposition *from* signifies a consubstantial generating principle, not a material principle. For what is produced from matter comes to be through the transformation of that from which it is produced into some form; but the divine essence is not changeable, nor receptive of another form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0066","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0067","text":"To the second it must be said that, when the Son is said to be begotten from the essence of the Father, according to the exposition of the Master, *Sentences*, I, distinction 5, it designates a relation to a principle as it were active, where he expounds thus: *the Son is begotten from the essence of the Father, that is, from the Father who is essence*; on account of the fact that Augustine, in book XV *On the Trinity*, says: *what I say, from the Father who is essence, is the same as if I were to say more expressly, from the essence of the Father*. But this does not seem sufficient for the sense of such an expression. For we can say that a creature is from God who is essence, yet not that it is from the essence of God. Hence it can be said otherwise that this preposition *from* always denotes consubstantiality. Hence we do not say that a house is from its builder, since he is not a consubstantial cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0068","text":"But we can say that something is from something, in whatever way that thing is signified as a consubstantial principle, whether it be an active principle, as the Son is said to be from the Father; or a material principle, as a knife is said to be from iron; or a formal principle, but only in those things in which the forms themselves are subsistent and do not accrue to another; for we can say that an angel is from an intellectual nature. And in this way we say that the Son is begotten from the essence of the Father, insofar as the essence of the Father, communicated to the Son through generation, subsists in him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0069","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0070","text":"To the third it must be said that, when it is said, “the Son is begotten from the essence of the Father,” something is added in relation to which distinction can be preserved. But when it is said that the three persons are from the divine essence, nothing is posited in relation to which distinction can be conveyed by the preposition. And therefore the cases are not alike.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0071","text":"[I q. 41 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0072","text":"To the fourth it must be said that, when it is said, “Wisdom was created,” this can be understood not of the Wisdom which is the Son of God, but of created wisdom, which God implanted in creatures; for it is said in Sirach I: *he created her*, namely wisdom, *by the Holy Spirit, and poured her out over all his works*. Nor is it unfitting that in one context of speech Scripture speaks of begotten and created wisdom, because created wisdom is a certain participation in uncreated Wisdom. Or it can be referred to the created nature assumed by the Son, so that the sense is: *from the beginning and before the ages I was created*, that is, I was foreseen to be united to a creature. Or, by the fact that Wisdom is called created and begotten, the mode of divine generation is suggested to us. For in generation, that which is generated receives the nature of the generator, which belongs to perfection; whereas in creation, the creator is not changed, but the creature does not receive the nature of the creator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0073","text":"Therefore, the Son is said to be created and begotten simultaneously, so that from creation the immutability of the Father may be understood, and from generation the unity of nature in the Father and the Son. And thus the meaning of this Scripture is expounded by Hilary, in the book On the Synods. But the authorities adduced do not speak of the Holy Spirit, but of created spirit; which is sometimes called wind, sometimes air, sometimes the breath of man, sometimes also soul, or whatever invisible substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0074","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0075","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0076","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that in God there is no power with respect to notional acts. For every power is either active or passive. But neither can belong here: for passive power is not in God, as was shown above; while active power does not belong to one person with respect to another, since the divine persons are not made, as has been shown. Therefore, in God there is no power for notional acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0077","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0078","text":"Further, power is spoken of in relation to the possible. But the divine persons are not among the possible, but among the necessary. Therefore, with respect to the notional acts by which the divine persons proceed, power ought not to be posited in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0079","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0080","text":"Further, the Son proceeds as Word, which is a conception of the intellect; but the Holy Spirit proceeds as Love, which pertains to the will. But power in God is spoken of in comparison with effects, not in comparison with understanding and willing, as was held above. Therefore, in God power ought not to be spoken of in comparison with notional acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0081","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0082","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, against the heretic Maximinus: *if God the Father could not beget a Son equal to himself, where is the omnipotence of God the Father?* Therefore, in God there is power with respect to notional acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0083","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0084","text":"I answer that, just as notional acts are posited in God, so it is necessary to posit there power with respect to acts of this kind, since power signifies nothing other than the principle of some act. Hence, since we understand the Father as the principle of generation, and the Father and the Son as the principle of spiration, it is necessary that we attribute to the Father the power of generating, and to the Father and the Son the power of spirating. For the power of generating signifies that by which the generator generates; but every generator generates by something. Hence in every generator it is necessary to posit a power of generating, and in one who spirates a power of spirating.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0085","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0086","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, just as according to notional acts no person proceeds as made, so neither is power for notional acts spoken of in God by reference to some made person, but only by reference to a proceeding person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0087","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0088","text":"To the second it must be said that the possible, insofar as it is opposed to the necessary, follows passive power, which is not in God. Hence neither is there anything possible in God in this manner, but only insofar as the possible is contained under the necessary. In this way it can be said that, just as it is possible that God exists, so it is possible that the Son is begotten.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0089","text":"[I q. 41 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0090","text":"To the third it must be said that power signifies a principle. But a principle implies distinction from that of which it is the principle. Now a twofold distinction is considered in those things that are said of God: one according to reality, the other according to reason alone. According to reality, indeed, God is distinguished in essence from the things of which he is the principle through creation, just as one person is distinguished from another of whom he is the principle according to a notional act. But action is not distinguished from the agent in God except according to reason alone; otherwise action would be an accident in God. And therefore, with respect to those actions according to which certain things proceed distinct from God, either essentially or personally, power can be attributed to God according to the proper character of a principle. And therefore, just as we posit in God the power to create, so we can posit the power to generate or to spirate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0091","text":"But understanding and willing are not such acts as designate the procession of something distinct from God, either essentially or personally. Hence, with respect to these acts, the character of power cannot be preserved in God, except only according to the mode of understanding and signifying; insofar as intellect and understanding are signified diversely in God, although God's very act of understanding is his essence, having no principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0092","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0093","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0094","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that the power of generating or spirating signifies relation, and not essence. For power signifies a principle, as is clear from its definition; for active power is said to be the principle of acting, as is clear in Metaphysics V. But principle in God, with respect to person, is spoken of notionally. Therefore, power in God does not signify essence, but relation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0095","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0096","text":"Further, in God, to be able and to act do not differ. But generation in God signifies relation. Therefore, so too does the power of generating.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0097","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0098","text":"Moreover, those things that signify the essence in divine matters are common to the three persons. But the power of generating is not common to the three persons, but is proper to the Father. Therefore, it does not signify the essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0099","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0100","text":"On the contrary, just as God can generate the Son, so too He wills [to generate Him]. But the will of generating signifies the essence. Therefore, so does the power of generating.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0101","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0102","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have said that the power of generating signifies relation in divine matters. But this cannot be so. For that by which an agent acts is properly called a power in any agent whatsoever. Now everything producing something through its action produces something like itself with respect to the form by which it acts, just as a man begotten is like the generator in human nature, by whose power the father can generate a man. Therefore, that in a generator in which the begotten is likened to the generator is the generative power. But the Son of God is likened to the Father begetting in the divine nature. Hence the divine nature in the Father is the power of generating in Him. Thus Hilary also says, in Book V *On the Trinity*: *the nativity of God cannot fail to retain the nature from which it has proceeded; for nothing other than God subsists which does not subsist from God alone*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0103","text":"Thus, therefore, it must be said that the power of generating principally signifies the divine essence, as the Master says, *Sentences*, distinction I, Book VII; but not relation alone. Nor does it signify the essence insofar as it is the same as relation, so as to signify both equally. For although paternity is signified as the form of the Father, it is nevertheless a personal property, related to the person of the Father as an individual form to some created individual. But an individual form, in created things, constitutes a generating person, and is not that by which the generator generates; otherwise Socrates would generate Socrates. Hence neither can paternity be understood as that by which the Father generates, but as constituting the person of the generator; otherwise the Father would generate the Father. But that by which the Father generates is the divine nature, in which the Son is assimilated to Him. Accordingly Damascene says that generation is a work of nature, not as belonging to the generator, but as belonging to that by which the generator generates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0104","text":"And therefore the power of generating signifies the divine nature directly, but relation indirectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0105","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0106","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that power does not signify the relation itself of a principle, otherwise it would be in the genus of relation; rather, it signifies that which is a principle—not indeed as an agent is called a principle, but as that by which an agent acts is called a principle. But an agent is distinguished from what is made, and a generator from the generated; whereas that by which a generator generates is common to the generated and the generator, and the more perfectly so, the more perfect the generation is. Hence, since the divine generation is most perfect, that by which the generator generates is common to the generated and the generator, and is the same in number, not merely in species, as in created things. Therefore, from our saying that the divine essence is the principle by which the generator generates, it does not follow that the divine essence is distinguished; as it would follow if it were said that the divine essence generates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0107","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0108","text":"To the second it must be said that in divine matters the power of generating is the same as generation, just as the divine essence is the same in reality as generation and paternity, but not in account.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0109","text":"[I q. 41 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0110","text":"To the third it must be said that, when I say “power of generating,” power is signified directly, and generation indirectly; just as if I were to say “the essence of the Father.” Hence, with respect to the essence that is signified, the power of generating is common to the three persons; but with respect to the notion that is connoted, it is proper to the person of the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0111","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0112","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0113","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that a notional act can terminate in several persons, so that there may be several persons begotten or spirated in divine matters. For whoever possesses the power of generating can generate. But the Son possesses the power of generating. Therefore, He can generate. But not Himself. Therefore, another Son. Therefore, there can be several Sons in divine matters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0114","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0115","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, against Maximinus: *the Son did not beget a Creator. For it was not because He could not, but because it was not fitting*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0116","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0117","text":"Moreover, God the Father is more powerful in generating than a created father. But one man can generate several sons. Therefore God also can do so, especially since the power of the Father is not diminished once one Son has been generated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0118","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0119","text":"On the contrary, in divine matters being and being able do not differ. Therefore, if several Sons could exist in divine matters, several Sons would exist. And thus there would be more than three persons in divine matters, which is heretical.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0120","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0121","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as Athanasius says, *in the Godhead there is only one Father, one Son, one Holy Spirit*. A fourfold reason for this can be assigned. The first is on the part of the relations, by which alone the persons are distinguished. For since the divine persons are the subsisting relations themselves, there could not be several fathers or several sons in the Godhead unless there were several paternities and several filiations. But this could not be except according to the material distinction of things; for forms of one species are multiplied only according to matter, which is not in the Godhead. Hence in the Godhead there can be only one subsisting filiation; just as subsisting whiteness also could be only one. The second is from the mode of processions. For God understands and wills all things by one simple act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0122","text":"Hence there can be only one person proceeding in the manner of a word, who is the Son; and only one in the manner of love, who is the Holy Spirit. The third reason is taken from the mode of proceeding. For the persons themselves proceed naturally, as has been said; but nature is determined to one. The fourth is from the perfection of the divine persons. For the Son is perfect from the fact that the whole divine filiation is contained in him, and that there is only one Son. And the same must be said of the other persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0123","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0124","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although it should simply be granted that the Son has the power that the Father has, nevertheless it should not be granted that the Son has the power of generating, if “of generating” is a gerundive of the active verb, so that the sense is that the Son has the power to generate. Just as, although the being of the Father and the Son is the same, it nevertheless does not belong to the Son to be Father, on account of the notional adjunct. But if this expression, “of generating,” is a gerundive of the passive verb, the power of generating is in the Son, that is, that he be generated. And similarly, if it is a gerundive of an impersonal verb, so that the sense is: the power of generating, that is, by which he is generated by some person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0125","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0126","text":"To the second, it must be said that Augustine, in those words, does not intend to say that the Son could generate a son, but that the fact that he does not generate is not due to the Son's impotence, as will be clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0127","text":"[I q. 41 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0128","text":"To the third, it must be said that divine immateriality and perfection require that there cannot be several sons in the Godhead, as has been said. Hence the fact that there are not several sons is not due to the Father's impotence to generate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_33:P0128"]}],"source_digest":"59c3739829c534301f772ee8dffa89609e274fe27dbc95a348cc60818fd911cc","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 41"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 41","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34","title":"First Part · Question 42","order":43,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_34","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 42 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30154)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de comparatione personarum ad invicem. Et primo, quantum ad aequalitatem et similitudinem; secundo, quantum ad missionem. Circa primum quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum aequalitas locum habeat in divinis personis. Secundo, utrum persona procedens sit aequalis ei a qua procedit, secundum aeternitatem. Tertio, utrum sit aliquis ordo in divinis personis. Quarto utrum personae divinae sint aequales secundum magnitudinem. Quinto, utrum una earum sit in alia. Sexto, utrum sint aequales secundum potentiam.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30155)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aequalitas non competat divinis personis. Aequalitas enim attenditur secundum unum in quantitate, ut patet per philosophum, V Metaphys. In divinis autem personis non invenitur neque quantitas continua intrinseca, quae dicitur magnitudo; neque quantitas continua extrinseca, quae dicitur locus et tempus; neque secundum quantitatem discretam invenitur in eis aequalitas, quia duae personae sunt plures quam una. Ergo divinis personis non convenit aequalitas.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30156)\n\nPraeterea, divinae personae sunt unius essentiae, ut supra dictum est. Essentia autem significatur per modum formae. Convenientia autem in forma non facit aequalitatem, sed similitudinem. Ergo in divinis personis est dicenda similitudo, et non aequalitas.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30157)\n\nPraeterea, in quibuscumque invenitur aequalitas, illa sunt sibi invicem aequalia, quia aequale dicitur aequali aequale. Sed divinae personae non possunt sibi invicem dici aequales. Quia, ut Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., *imago, si perfecte implet illud cuius est imago, ipsa coaequatur ei, non illud imagini suae*. Imago autem patris est filius, et sic pater non est aequalis filio. Non ergo in divinis personis invenitur aequalitas.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30158)\n\nPraeterea, aequalitas relatio quaedam est. Sed nulla relatio est communis omnibus personis, cum secundum relationes personae ab invicem distinguantur. Non ergo aequalitas divinis personis convenit.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30159)\n\nSed contra est quod Athanasius dicit, quod *tres personae coaeternae sibi sunt et coaequales*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30160)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere aequalitatem in divinis personis. Quia secundum philosophum, in X Metaphys., aequale dicitur quasi per negationem minoris et maioris. Non autem possumus in divinis personis ponere aliquid maius et minus, quia, ut Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin., eos differentia, scilicet deitatis, comitatur, *qui vel augent vel minuunt, ut Ariani, qui gradibus meritorum Trinitatem variantes distrahunt, atque in pluralitatem deducunt*. Cuius ratio est, quia inaequalium non potest esse una quantitas numero. Quantitas autem in divinis non est aliud quam eius essentia. Unde relinquitur quod, si esset aliqua inaequalitas in divinis personis, quod non esset in eis una essentia, et sic non essent tres personae unus Deus, quod est impossibile. Oportet igitur aequalitatem ponere in divinis personis.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30161)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod duplex est quantitas. Una scilicet quae dicitur quantitas molis, vel quantitas dimensiva, quae in solis rebus corporalibus est, unde in divinis personis locum non habet. Sed alia est quantitas virtutis, quae attenditur secundum perfectionem alicuius naturae vel formae, quae quidem quantitas designatur secundum quod dicitur aliquid magis vel minus calidum, inquantum est perfectius vel minus perfectum in caliditate. Huiusmodi autem quantitas virtualis attenditur primo quidem in radice, idest in ipsa perfectione formae vel naturae, et sic dicitur magnitudo spiritualis, sicut dicitur magnus calor propter suam intensionem et perfectionem. Et ideo dicit Augustinus, VI de Trin., quod *in his quae non mole magna sunt, hoc est maius esse, quod est melius esse*, nam melius dicitur quod perfectius est. Secundo autem attenditur quantitas virtualis in effectibus formae. Primus autem effectus formae est esse, nam omnis res habet esse secundum suam formam. Secundus autem effectus est operatio, nam omne agens agit per suam formam. Attenditur igitur quantitas virtualis et secundum esse, et secundum operationem, secundum esse quidem, inquantum ea quae sunt perfectioris naturae, sunt maioris durationis; secundum operationem vero, inquantum ea quae sunt perfectioris naturae, sunt magis potentia ad agendum. Sic igitur, ut Augustinus dicit, in libro de fide ad Petrum, aequalitas intelligitur in patre et filio et spiritu sancto, *inquantum nullus horum aut praecedit aeternitate, aut excedit magnitudine, aut superat potestate*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30162)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ubi attenditur aequalitas secundum quantitatem virtualem, aequalitas includit in se similitudinem, et aliquid plus, quia excludit excessum. Quaecumque enim communicant in una forma, possunt dici similia, etiamsi inaequaliter illam formam participant, sicut si dicatur aer esse similis igni in calore, sed non possunt dici aequalia, si unum altero perfectius formam illam participet. Et quia non solum una est natura patris et filii, sed etiam aeque perfecte est in utroque, ideo non solum dicimus filium esse similem patri, ut excludatur error Eunomii; sed etiam dicimus aequalem, ut excludatur error Arii.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30163)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aequalitas vel similitudo dupliciter potest significari in divinis, scilicet per nomina et per verba. Secundum quidem quod significatur per nomina, mutua aequalitas dicitur in divinis personis et similitudo, filius enim est aequalis et similis patri, et e converso. Et hoc ideo, quia essentia divina non magis est patris quam filii, unde, sicut filius habet magnitudinem patris, quod est esse eum aequalem patri, ita pater habet magnitudinem filii, quod est esse eum aequalem filio. Sed quantum ad creaturas, ut Dionysius dicit, IX cap. de Div. Nom., *non recipitur conversio aequalitatis et similitudinis*. Dicuntur enim causata similia causis, inquantum habent formam causarum, sed non e converso, quia forma principaliter est in causa, et secundario in causato. Sed verba significant aequalitatem cum motu. Et licet motus non sit in divinis, est tamen ibi accipere. Quia igitur filius accipit a patre unde est aequalis ei, et non e converso, propter hoc dicimus quod filius coaequatur patri, et non e converso.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30164)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in divinis personis nihil est considerare nisi essentiam, in qua communicant, et relationes, in quibus distinguuntur. Aequalitas autem utrumque importat, scilicet distinctionem personarum, quia nihil sibi ipsi dicitur aequale; et unitatem essentiae, quia ex hoc personae sunt sibi invicem aequales, quod sunt unius magnitudinis et essentiae. Manifestum est autem quod idem ad seipsum non refertur aliqua relatione reali. Nec iterum una relatio refertur ad aliam per aliquam aliam relationem, cum enim dicimus quod paternitas opponitur filiationi, oppositio non est relatio media inter paternitatem et filiationem. Quia utroque modo relatio multiplicaretur in infinitum. Et ideo aequalitas et similitudo in divinis personis non est aliqua realis relatio distincta a relationibus personalibus, sed in suo intellectu includit et relationes distinguentes personas, et essentiae unitatem. Et propterea Magister dicit, in XXXI dist. I Sent., quod in his appellatio tantum est relativa.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30165)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod persona procedens non sit coaeterna suo principio, ut filius patri. Arius enim duodecim modos generationis assignat. Primus modus est iuxta fluxum lineae a puncto, ubi deest aequalitas simplicitatis. Secundus modus est iuxta emissionem radiorum a sole, ubi deest aequalitas naturae. Tertius modus est iuxta characterem, seu impressionem a sigillo, ubi deest consubstantialitas et potentiae efficientia. Quartus modus est iuxta immissionem bonae voluntatis a Deo, ubi etiam deest consubstantialitas. Quintus modus est iuxta exitum accidentis a substantia, sed accidenti deest subsistentia. Sextus modus est iuxta abstractionem speciei a materia, sicut sensus accipit speciem a re sensibili, ubi deest aequalitas simplicitatis spiritualis. Septimus modus est iuxta excitationem voluntatis a cogitatione, quae quidem excitatio temporalis est. Octavus modus est iuxta transfigurationem, ut ex aere fit imago, quae materialis est. Nonus modus est motus a movente, et hic etiam ponitur effectus et causa. Decimus modus est iuxta eductionem specierum a genere, qui non competit in divinis, quia pater non praedicatur de filio sicut genus de specie. Undecimus modus est iuxta ideationem, ut arca exterior ab ea quae est in mente. Duodecimus modus est iuxta nascentiam, ut homo est a patre, ubi est prius et posterius secundum tempus. Patet ergo quod in omni modo quo aliquid est ex altero, aut deest aequalitas naturae, aut aequalitas durationis. Si igitur filius est a patre, oportet dicere vel eum esse minorem patre, aut posteriorem, aut utrumque.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30166)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est ex altero, habet principium. Sed nullum aeternum habet principium. Ergo filius non est aeternus, neque spiritus sanctus.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30167)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod corrumpitur, desinit esse. Ergo omne quod generatur, incipit esse, ad hoc enim generatur, ut sit. Sed filius est genitus a patre. Ergo incipit esse, et non est coaeternus patri.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30168)\n\nPraeterea, si filius genitus est a patre, aut semper generatur, aut est dare aliquod instans suae generationis. Si semper generatur; dum autem aliquid est in generari, est imperfectum, sicut patet in successivis, quae sunt semper in fieri, ut tempus et motus, sequitur quod filius semper sit imperfectus; quod est inconveniens. Est ergo dare aliquod instans generationis filii. Ante illud ergo instans filius non erat.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30169)\n\nSed contra est quod Athanasius dicit, quod *totae tres personae coaeternae sibi sunt*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30170)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere filium esse coaeternum patri. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod aliquid ex principio existens posterius esse suo principio, potest contingere ex duobus, uno modo, ex parte agentis; alio modo, ex parte actionis. Ex parte agentis quidem, aliter in agentibus voluntariis, aliter in agentibus naturalibus. In agentibus quidem voluntariis, propter electionem temporis, sicut enim in agentis voluntarii potestate est eligere formam quam effectui conferat, ut supra dictum est, ita in eius potestate est eligere tempus in quo effectum producat. In agentibus autem naturalibus hoc contingit, quia agens aliquod non a principio habet perfectionem virtutis naturaliter ad agendum, sed ei advenit post aliquod tempus; sicut homo non a principio generare potest. Ex parte autem actionis, impeditur ne id quod est a principio simul sit cum suo principio, propter hoc quod actio est successiva. Unde, dato quod aliquod agens tali actione agere inciperet statim cum est, non statim eodem instanti esset effectus, sed in instanti ad quod terminatur actio. Manifestum est autem secundum praemissa, quod pater non generat filium voluntate, sed natura. Et iterum, quod natura patris ab aeterno perfecta fuit. Et iterum, quod actio qua pater producit filium, non est successiva, quia sic filius Dei successive generaretur, et esset eius generatio materialis et cum motu, quod est impossibile. Relinquitur ergo quod filius fuit, quandocumque fuit pater. Et sic filius est coaeternus patri, et similiter spiritus sanctus utrique.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30171)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, in libro de verbis domini, nullus modus processionis alicuius creaturae perfecte repraesentat divinam generationem, unde oportet ex multis modis colligere similitudinem, ut quod deest ex uno, aliqualiter suppleatur ex altero. Et propter hoc dicitur in synodo Ephesina, *coexistere semper coaeternum patri filium, splendor tibi denuntiet, impassibilitatem nativitatis ostendat verbum; consubstantialitatem filii nomen insinuet*. Inter omnia tamen expressius repraesentat processio verbi ab intellectu, quod quidem non est posterius eo a quo procedit; nisi sit talis intellectus qui exeat de potentia in actum, quod in Deo dici non potest.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30172)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aeternitas excludit principium durationis, sed non principium originis.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30173)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnis corruptio est mutatio quaedam, et ideo omne quod corrumpitur, incipit non esse, et desinit esse. Sed generatio divina non est transmutatio, ut dictum est supra. Unde filius semper generatur, et pater semper generat.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30174)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in tempore aliud est quod est indivisibile, scilicet instans; et aliud est quod est durans, scilicet tempus. Sed in aeternitate ipsum nunc indivisibile est semper stans, ut supra dictum est. Generatio vero filii non est in nunc temporis, aut in tempore, sed in aeternitate. Et ideo, ad significandum praesentialitatem et permanentiam aeternitatis, potest dici quod semper nascitur, ut Origenes dixit. Sed, ut Gregorius et Augustinus dicunt, melius est quod dicatur semper natus, ut ly semper designet permanentiam aeternitatis, et ly natus perfectionem geniti. Sic ergo filius nec imperfectus est, neque erat quando non erat, ut Arius dixit.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30175)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in divinis personis non sit ordo naturae. Quidquid enim in divinis est, vel est essentia vel persona vel notio. Sed ordo naturae non significat essentiam, neque est aliqua personarum aut notionum. Ergo ordo naturae non est in divinis.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30176)\n\nPraeterea, in quibuscumque est ordo naturae, unum est prius altero, saltem secundum naturam et intellectum. Sed in divinis personis nihil est prius et posterius, ut Athanasius dicit. Ergo in divinis personis non est ordo naturae.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30177)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid ordinatur, distinguitur. Sed natura in divinis non distinguitur. Ergo non ordinatur. Ergo non est ibi ordo naturae.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30178)\n\nPraeterea, natura divina est eius essentia. Sed non dicitur in divinis ordo essentiae. Ergo neque ordo naturae.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30179)\n\nSed contra, ubicumque est pluralitas sine ordine, ibi est confusio. Sed in divinis personis non est confusio, ut Athanasius dicit. Ergo est ibi ordo.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30180)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ordo semper dicitur per comparationem ad aliquod principium. Unde sicut dicitur principium multipliciter, scilicet secundum situm, ut punctus, secundum intellectum, ut principium demonstrationis, et secundum causas singulas; ita etiam dicitur ordo. In divinis autem dicitur principium secundum originem, absque prioritate, ut supra dictum est. Unde oportet ibi esse ordinem secundum originem, absque prioritate. Et hic vocatur ordo naturae, secundum Augustinum, *non quo alter sit prius altero, sed quo alter est ex altero*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30181)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ordo naturae significat notionem originis in communi, non autem in speciali.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30182)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in rebus creatis, etiam cum id quod est a principio sit suo principio coaevum secundum durationem, tamen principium est prius secundum naturam et intellectum, si consideretur id quod est principium. Sed si considerentur ipsae relationes causae et causati, et principii et principiati, manifestum est quod relativa sunt simul natura et intellectu, inquantum unum est in definitione alterius. Sed in divinis ipsae relationes sunt subsistentes personae in una natura. Unde neque ex parte naturae, neque ex parte relationum, una persona potest esse prior alia, neque etiam secundum naturam et intellectum.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30183)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ordo naturae dicitur, non quod ipsa natura ordinetur, sed quod ordo in divinis personis attenditur secundum naturalem originem.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30184)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod natura quodammodo importat rationem principii, non autem essentia. Et ideo ordo originis melius nominatur ordo naturae, quam ordo essentiae.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30185)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filius non sit aequalis patri in magnitudine. Dicit enim ipse, Ioan. XIV, *pater maior me est*; et apostolus, I Cor. XV, *ipse filius subiectus erit illi qui sibi subiecit omnia*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30186)\n\nPraeterea, paternitas pertinet ad dignitatem patris. Sed paternitas non convenit filio. Ergo non quidquid dignitatis habet pater, habet filius. Ergo non est aequalis patri in magnitudine.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30187)\n\nPraeterea, ubicumque est totum et pars, plures partes sunt aliquid maius quam una tantum vel pauciores; sicut tres homines sunt aliquid maius quam duo vel unus. Sed in divinis videtur esse totum universale et pars, nam sub relatione vel notione plures notiones continentur. Cum igitur in patre sint tres notiones, in filio autem tantum duae, videtur quod filius non sit aequalis patri.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30188)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Philip. II, *non rapinam arbitratus est esse se aequalem Deo*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30189)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere filium esse aequalem patri in magnitudine. Magnitudo enim Dei non est aliud quam perfectio naturae ipsius. Hoc autem est de ratione paternitatis et filiationis, quod filius per generationem pertingat ad habendam perfectionem naturae quae est in patre, sicut et pater. Sed quia in hominibus generatio est transmutatio quaedam exeuntis de potentia in actum, non statim a principio homo filius est aequalis patri generanti; sed per debitum incrementum ad aequalitatem perducitur, nisi aliter eveniat propter defectum principii generationis. Manifestum est autem ex dictis quod in divinis est proprie et vere paternitas et filiatio. Nec potest dici quod virtus Dei patris fuerit defectiva in generando; neque quod Dei filius successive et per transmutationem ad perfectionem pervenerit. Unde necesse est dicere quod ab aeterno fuerit patri aequalis in magnitudine. Unde et Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., *tolle corporum infirmitates, tolle conceptus initium, tolle dolores et omnem humanam necessitatem, omnis filius secundum naturalem nativitatem aequalitas patris est, quia est et similitudo naturae*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30190)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verba illa intelliguntur dicta de Christo secundum humanam naturam, in qua minor est patre, et ei subiectus. Sed secundum naturam divinam, aequalis est patri. Et hoc est quod Athanasius dicit; *aequalis patri secundum divinitatem, minor patre secundum humanitatem*. Vel, secundum Hilarium, in IX libro de Trin., *donantis auctoritate pater maior est, sed minor non est cui unum esse donatur*. Et in libro de Synod. dicit quod subiectio filii naturae pietas est, idest recognitio auctoritatis paternae, subiectio autem ceterorum, creationis infirmitas.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30191)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aequalitas attenditur secundum magnitudinem. Magnitudo autem in divinis significat perfectionem naturae, ut dictum est, et ad essentiam pertinet. Et ideo aequalitas in divinis, et similitudo, secundum essentialia attenditur, nec potest secundum distinctionem relationum inaequalitas vel dissimilitudo dici. Unde Augustinus dicit, contra Maximinum, *originis quaestio est quid de quo sit; aequalitatis autem, qualis aut quantus sit*. Paternitas igitur est dignitas patris, sicut et essentia patris, nam dignitas absolutum est, et ad essentiam pertinet. Sicut igitur eadem essentia quae in patre est paternitas, in filio est filiatio; ita eadem dignitas quae in patre est paternitas, in filio est filiatio. Vere ergo dicitur quod quidquid dignitatis habet pater, habet filius. Nec sequitur, paternitatem habet pater, ergo paternitatem habet filius. Mutatur enim quid in ad aliquid, eadem enim est essentia et dignitas patris et filii, sed in patre est secundum relationem dantis, in filio secundum relationem accipientis.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30192)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod relatio in divinis non est totum universale, quamvis de pluribus relationibus praedicetur, quia omnes relationes sunt unum secundum essentiam et esse, quod repugnat rationi universalis, cuius partes secundum esse distinguuntur. Et similiter persona, ut supra dictum est, non est universale in divinis. Unde neque omnes relationes sunt maius aliquid quam una tantum; nec omnes personae maius aliquid quam una tantum; quia tota perfectio divinae naturae est in qualibet personarum.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30193)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filius non sit in patre, et e converso. Philosophus enim, in IV Physic., ponit octo modos essendi aliquid in aliquo; et secundum nullum horum filius est in patre, aut e converso, ut patet discurrenti per singulos modos. Ergo filius non est in patre, nec e converso.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30194)\n\nPraeterea, nihil quod exivit ab aliquo, est in eo. Sed filius ab aeterno exivit a patre, secundum illud Micheae V, *egressus eius ab initio, a diebus aeternitatis*. Ergo filius non est in patre.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30195)\n\nPraeterea, unum oppositorum non est in altero. Sed filius et pater opponuntur relative. Ergo unus non potest esse in alio.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30196)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. XIV, *ego in patre, et pater in me est*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30197)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in patre et filio tria est considerare, scilicet essentiam, relationem et originem; et secundum quodlibet istorum filius est in patre, et e converso. Secundum essentiam enim pater est in filio, quia pater est sua essentia, et communicat suam essentiam filio, non per aliquam suam transmutationem, unde sequitur quod, cum essentia patris sit in filio, quod in filio sit pater. Et similiter, cum filius sit sua essentia, sequitur quod sit in patre, in quo est eius essentia. Et hoc est quod Hilarius dicit, V de Trin., *naturam suam, ut ita dicam, sequitur immutabilis Deus, immutabilem gignens Deum. Subsistentem ergo in eo Dei naturam intelligimus, cum in Deo Deus insit*. Secundum etiam relationes, manifestum est quod unum oppositorum relative est in altero secundum intellectum. Secundum originem etiam manifestum est quod processio verbi intelligibilis non est ad extra, sed manet in dicente. Id etiam quod verbo dicitur, in verbo continetur. Et eadem ratio est de spiritu sancto.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30198)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ea quae in creaturis sunt, non sufficienter repraesentant ea quae Dei sunt. Et ideo secundum nullum eorum modorum quos philosophus enumerat, filius est in patre, aut e converso. Accedit tamen magis ad hoc modus ille, secundum quem aliquid dicitur esse in principio originante, nisi quod deest unitas essentiae, in rebus creatis, inter principium et id quod est a principio.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30199)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod exitus filii a patre est secundum modum processionis interioris, prout verbum exit a corde, et manet in eo. Unde exitus iste in divinis est secundum solam distinctionem relationum, non secundum essentialem aliquam distantiam.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30200)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod pater et filius opponuntur secundum relationes, non autem secundum essentiam. Et tamen oppositorum relative unum est in altero, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30201)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filius non sit aequalis patri secundum potentiam. Dicitur enim Ioan. V, *non potest filius a se facere quidquam, nisi quod viderit patrem facientem*. Pater autem a se potest facere. Ergo pater maior est filio secundum potentiam.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30202)\n\nPraeterea, maior est potentia eius qui praecipit et docet, quam eius qui obedit et audit. Sed pater mandat filio, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *sicut mandatum dedit mihi pater, sic facio*. Pater etiam docet filium, secundum illud Ioan. V, *pater diligit filium, et omnia demonstrat ei quae ipse facit*. Similiter et filius audit, secundum illud Ioan. V, *sicut audio, iudico*. Ergo pater est maioris potentiae quam filius.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30203)\n\nPraeterea, ad omnipotentiam patris pertinet quod possit filium generare sibi aequalem, dicit enim Augustinus, in libro contra Maximin., *si non potuit generare sibi aequalem, ubi est omnipotentia Dei patris?* Sed filius non potest generare filium, ut supra ostensum est. Non ergo quidquid pertinet ad omnipotentiam patris, potest filius. Et ita non est ei in potestate aequalis.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30204)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. V, *quaecumque pater facit, haec et filius similiter facit*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30205)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere quod filius est aequalis patri in potestate. Potentia enim agendi consequitur perfectionem naturae, videmus enim in creaturis quod quanto aliquid habet perfectiorem naturam, tanto est maioris virtutis in agendo. Ostensum est autem supra quod ipsa ratio divinae paternitatis et filiationis exigit quod filius sit aequalis patri in magnitudine, idest in perfectione naturae. Unde relinquitur quod filius sit aequalis patri in potestate. Et eadem ratio est de spiritu sancto respectu utriusque.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30206)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in hoc quod dicitur quod filius non potest a se facere quidquam, non subtrahitur filio aliqua potestas quam habeat pater; cum statim subdatur quod quaecumque pater facit, filius similiter facit. Sed ostenditur quod filius habet potestatem a patre, a quo habet naturam. Unde dicit Hilarius, IX de Trin., *naturae divinae haec unitas est, ut ita per se agat filius, quod non a se agat*.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30207)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in demonstratione patris et auditione filii, non intelligitur nisi quod pater communicat scientiam filio, sicut et essentiam. Et ad idem potest referri mandatum patris, per hoc quod ab aeterno dedit ei scientiam et voluntatem agendorum, eum generando. Vel potius referendum est ad Christum secundum humanam naturam.\n\n[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30208)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut eadem essentia quae in patre est paternitas, in filio est filiatio; ita eadem est potentia qua pater generat, et qua filius generatur. Unde manifestum est quod quidquid potest pater, potest filius. Non tamen sequitur quod possit generare, sed mutatur quid in ad aliquid, nam generatio significat relationem in divinis. Habet ergo filius eandem omnipotentiam quam pater, sed cum alia relatione. Quia pater habet eam ut dans, et hoc significatur, cum dicitur quod potest generare. Filius autem habet eam ut accipiens, et hoc significatur, cum dicitur quod potest generari.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0002","text":"[I q. 42 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de comparatione personarum ad invicem. Et primo, quantum ad aequalitatem et similitudinem; secundo, quantum ad missionem. Circa primum quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum aequalitas locum habeat in divinis personis. Secundo, utrum persona procedens sit aequalis ei a qua procedit, secundum aeternitatem. Tertio, utrum sit aliquis ordo in divinis personis. Quarto utrum personae divinae sint aequales secundum magnitudinem. Quinto, utrum una earum sit in alia. Sexto, utrum sint aequales secundum potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0005","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aequalitas non competat divinis personis. Aequalitas enim attenditur secundum unum in quantitate, ut patet per philosophum, V Metaphys. In divinis autem personis non invenitur neque quantitas continua intrinseca, quae dicitur magnitudo; neque quantitas continua extrinseca, quae dicitur locus et tempus; neque secundum quantitatem discretam invenitur in eis aequalitas, quia duae personae sunt plures quam una. Ergo divinis personis non convenit aequalitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0007","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0008","text":"Praeterea, divinae personae sunt unius essentiae, ut supra dictum est. Essentia autem significatur per modum formae. Convenientia autem in forma non facit aequalitatem, sed similitudinem. Ergo in divinis personis est dicenda similitudo, et non aequalitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0009","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0010","text":"Praeterea, in quibuscumque invenitur aequalitas, illa sunt sibi invicem aequalia, quia aequale dicitur aequali aequale. Sed divinae personae non possunt sibi invicem dici aequales. Quia, ut Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., *imago, si perfecte implet illud cuius est imago, ipsa coaequatur ei, non illud imagini suae*. Imago autem patris est filius, et sic pater non est aequalis filio. Non ergo in divinis personis invenitur aequalitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0011","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0012","text":"Praeterea, aequalitas relatio quaedam est. Sed nulla relatio est communis omnibus personis, cum secundum relationes personae ab invicem distinguantur. Non ergo aequalitas divinis personis convenit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0013","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod Athanasius dicit, quod *tres personae coaeternae sibi sunt et coaequales*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0015","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere aequalitatem in divinis personis. Quia secundum philosophum, in X Metaphys., aequale dicitur quasi per negationem minoris et maioris. Non autem possumus in divinis personis ponere aliquid maius et minus, quia, ut Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin., eos differentia, scilicet deitatis, comitatur, *qui vel augent vel minuunt, ut Ariani, qui gradibus meritorum Trinitatem variantes distrahunt, atque in pluralitatem deducunt*. Cuius ratio est, quia inaequalium non potest esse una quantitas numero. Quantitas autem in divinis non est aliud quam eius essentia. Unde relinquitur quod, si esset aliqua inaequalitas in divinis personis, quod non esset in eis una essentia, et sic non essent tres personae unus Deus, quod est impossibile. Oportet igitur aequalitatem ponere in divinis personis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0017","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod duplex est quantitas. Una scilicet quae dicitur quantitas molis, vel quantitas dimensiva, quae in solis rebus corporalibus est, unde in divinis personis locum non habet. Sed alia est quantitas virtutis, quae attenditur secundum perfectionem alicuius naturae vel formae, quae quidem quantitas designatur secundum quod dicitur aliquid magis vel minus calidum, inquantum est perfectius vel minus perfectum in caliditate. Huiusmodi autem quantitas virtualis attenditur primo quidem in radice, idest in ipsa perfectione formae vel naturae, et sic dicitur magnitudo spiritualis, sicut dicitur magnus calor propter suam intensionem et perfectionem. Et ideo dicit Augustinus, VI de Trin., quod *in his quae non mole magna sunt, hoc est maius esse, quod est melius esse*, nam melius dicitur quod perfectius est. Secundo autem attenditur quantitas virtualis in effectibus formae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0019","text":"Primus autem effectus formae est esse, nam omnis res habet esse secundum suam formam. Secundus autem effectus est operatio, nam omne agens agit per suam formam. Attenditur igitur quantitas virtualis et secundum esse, et secundum operationem, secundum esse quidem, inquantum ea quae sunt perfectioris naturae, sunt maioris durationis; secundum operationem vero, inquantum ea quae sunt perfectioris naturae, sunt magis potentia ad agendum. Sic igitur, ut Augustinus dicit, in libro de fide ad Petrum, aequalitas intelligitur in patre et filio et spiritu sancto, *inquantum nullus horum aut praecedit aeternitate, aut excedit magnitudine, aut superat potestate*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0020","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ubi attenditur aequalitas secundum quantitatem virtualem, aequalitas includit in se similitudinem, et aliquid plus, quia excludit excessum. Quaecumque enim communicant in una forma, possunt dici similia, etiamsi inaequaliter illam formam participant, sicut si dicatur aer esse similis igni in calore, sed non possunt dici aequalia, si unum altero perfectius formam illam participet. Et quia non solum una est natura patris et filii, sed etiam aeque perfecte est in utroque, ideo non solum dicimus filium esse similem patri, ut excludatur error Eunomii; sed etiam dicimus aequalem, ut excludatur error Arii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0022","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aequalitas vel similitudo dupliciter potest significari in divinis, scilicet per nomina et per verba. Secundum quidem quod significatur per nomina, mutua aequalitas dicitur in divinis personis et similitudo, filius enim est aequalis et similis patri, et e converso. Et hoc ideo, quia essentia divina non magis est patris quam filii, unde, sicut filius habet magnitudinem patris, quod est esse eum aequalem patri, ita pater habet magnitudinem filii, quod est esse eum aequalem filio. Sed quantum ad creaturas, ut Dionysius dicit, IX cap. de Div. Nom., *non recipitur conversio aequalitatis et similitudinis*. Dicuntur enim causata similia causis, inquantum habent formam causarum, sed non e converso, quia forma principaliter est in causa, et secundario in causato. Sed verba significant aequalitatem cum motu. Et licet motus non sit in divinis, est tamen ibi accipere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0024","text":"Quia igitur filius accipit a patre unde est aequalis ei, et non e converso, propter hoc dicimus quod filius coaequatur patri, et non e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0025","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0026","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in divinis personis nihil est considerare nisi essentiam, in qua communicant, et relationes, in quibus distinguuntur. Aequalitas autem utrumque importat, scilicet distinctionem personarum, quia nihil sibi ipsi dicitur aequale; et unitatem essentiae, quia ex hoc personae sunt sibi invicem aequales, quod sunt unius magnitudinis et essentiae. Manifestum est autem quod idem ad seipsum non refertur aliqua relatione reali. Nec iterum una relatio refertur ad aliam per aliquam aliam relationem, cum enim dicimus quod paternitas opponitur filiationi, oppositio non est relatio media inter paternitatem et filiationem. Quia utroque modo relatio multiplicaretur in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0027","text":"Et ideo aequalitas et similitudo in divinis personis non est aliqua realis relatio distincta a relationibus personalibus, sed in suo intellectu includit et relationes distinguentes personas, et essentiae unitatem. Et propterea Magister dicit, in XXXI dist. I Sent., quod in his appellatio tantum est relativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0028","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0029","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0030","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod persona procedens non sit coaeterna suo principio, ut filius patri. Arius enim duodecim modos generationis assignat. Primus modus est iuxta fluxum lineae a puncto, ubi deest aequalitas simplicitatis. Secundus modus est iuxta emissionem radiorum a sole, ubi deest aequalitas naturae. Tertius modus est iuxta characterem, seu impressionem a sigillo, ubi deest consubstantialitas et potentiae efficientia. Quartus modus est iuxta immissionem bonae voluntatis a Deo, ubi etiam deest consubstantialitas. Quintus modus est iuxta exitum accidentis a substantia, sed accidenti deest subsistentia. Sextus modus est iuxta abstractionem speciei a materia, sicut sensus accipit speciem a re sensibili, ubi deest aequalitas simplicitatis spiritualis. Septimus modus est iuxta excitationem voluntatis a cogitatione, quae quidem excitatio temporalis est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0031","text":"Octavus modus est iuxta transfigurationem, ut ex aere fit imago, quae materialis est. Nonus modus est motus a movente, et hic etiam ponitur effectus et causa. Decimus modus est iuxta eductionem specierum a genere, qui non competit in divinis, quia pater non praedicatur de filio sicut genus de specie. Undecimus modus est iuxta ideationem, ut arca exterior ab ea quae est in mente. Duodecimus modus est iuxta nascentiam, ut homo est a patre, ubi est prius et posterius secundum tempus. Patet ergo quod in omni modo quo aliquid est ex altero, aut deest aequalitas naturae, aut aequalitas durationis. Si igitur filius est a patre, oportet dicere vel eum esse minorem patre, aut posteriorem, aut utrumque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0032","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0033","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est ex altero, habet principium. Sed nullum aeternum habet principium. Ergo filius non est aeternus, neque spiritus sanctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0034","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0035","text":"Praeterea, omne quod corrumpitur, desinit esse. Ergo omne quod generatur, incipit esse, ad hoc enim generatur, ut sit. Sed filius est genitus a patre. Ergo incipit esse, et non est coaeternus patri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0036","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0037","text":"Praeterea, si filius genitus est a patre, aut semper generatur, aut est dare aliquod instans suae generationis. Si semper generatur; dum autem aliquid est in generari, est imperfectum, sicut patet in successivis, quae sunt semper in fieri, ut tempus et motus, sequitur quod filius semper sit imperfectus; quod est inconveniens. Est ergo dare aliquod instans generationis filii. Ante illud ergo instans filius non erat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0038","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0039","text":"Sed contra est quod Athanasius dicit, quod *totae tres personae coaeternae sibi sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0040","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0041","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere filium esse coaeternum patri. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod aliquid ex principio existens posterius esse suo principio, potest contingere ex duobus, uno modo, ex parte agentis; alio modo, ex parte actionis. Ex parte agentis quidem, aliter in agentibus voluntariis, aliter in agentibus naturalibus. In agentibus quidem voluntariis, propter electionem temporis, sicut enim in agentis voluntarii potestate est eligere formam quam effectui conferat, ut supra dictum est, ita in eius potestate est eligere tempus in quo effectum producat. In agentibus autem naturalibus hoc contingit, quia agens aliquod non a principio habet perfectionem virtutis naturaliter ad agendum, sed ei advenit post aliquod tempus; sicut homo non a principio generare potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0042","text":"Ex parte autem actionis, impeditur ne id quod est a principio simul sit cum suo principio, propter hoc quod actio est successiva. Unde, dato quod aliquod agens tali actione agere inciperet statim cum est, non statim eodem instanti esset effectus, sed in instanti ad quod terminatur actio. Manifestum est autem secundum praemissa, quod pater non generat filium voluntate, sed natura. Et iterum, quod natura patris ab aeterno perfecta fuit. Et iterum, quod actio qua pater producit filium, non est successiva, quia sic filius Dei successive generaretur, et esset eius generatio materialis et cum motu, quod est impossibile. Relinquitur ergo quod filius fuit, quandocumque fuit pater. Et sic filius est coaeternus patri, et similiter spiritus sanctus utrique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0043","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0044","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, in libro de verbis domini, nullus modus processionis alicuius creaturae perfecte repraesentat divinam generationem, unde oportet ex multis modis colligere similitudinem, ut quod deest ex uno, aliqualiter suppleatur ex altero. Et propter hoc dicitur in synodo Ephesina, *coexistere semper coaeternum patri filium, splendor tibi denuntiet, impassibilitatem nativitatis ostendat verbum; consubstantialitatem filii nomen insinuet*. Inter omnia tamen expressius repraesentat processio verbi ab intellectu, quod quidem non est posterius eo a quo procedit; nisi sit talis intellectus qui exeat de potentia in actum, quod in Deo dici non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0045","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0046","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aeternitas excludit principium durationis, sed non principium originis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0047","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0048","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnis corruptio est mutatio quaedam, et ideo omne quod corrumpitur, incipit non esse, et desinit esse. Sed generatio divina non est transmutatio, ut dictum est supra. Unde filius semper generatur, et pater semper generat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0049","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0050","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in tempore aliud est quod est indivisibile, scilicet instans; et aliud est quod est durans, scilicet tempus. Sed in aeternitate ipsum nunc indivisibile est semper stans, ut supra dictum est. Generatio vero filii non est in nunc temporis, aut in tempore, sed in aeternitate. Et ideo, ad significandum praesentialitatem et permanentiam aeternitatis, potest dici quod semper nascitur, ut Origenes dixit. Sed, ut Gregorius et Augustinus dicunt, melius est quod dicatur semper natus, ut ly semper designet permanentiam aeternitatis, et ly natus perfectionem geniti. Sic ergo filius nec imperfectus est, neque erat quando non erat, ut Arius dixit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0051","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0052","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0053","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in divinis personis non sit ordo naturae. Quidquid enim in divinis est, vel est essentia vel persona vel notio. Sed ordo naturae non significat essentiam, neque est aliqua personarum aut notionum. Ergo ordo naturae non est in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0054","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0055","text":"Praeterea, in quibuscumque est ordo naturae, unum est prius altero, saltem secundum naturam et intellectum. Sed in divinis personis nihil est prius et posterius, ut Athanasius dicit. Ergo in divinis personis non est ordo naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0056","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0057","text":"Praeterea, quidquid ordinatur, distinguitur. Sed natura in divinis non distinguitur. Ergo non ordinatur. Ergo non est ibi ordo naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0058","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0059","text":"Praeterea, natura divina est eius essentia. Sed non dicitur in divinis ordo essentiae. Ergo neque ordo naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0060","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0061","text":"Sed contra, ubicumque est pluralitas sine ordine, ibi est confusio. Sed in divinis personis non est confusio, ut Athanasius dicit. Ergo est ibi ordo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0062","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0063","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ordo semper dicitur per comparationem ad aliquod principium. Unde sicut dicitur principium multipliciter, scilicet secundum situm, ut punctus, secundum intellectum, ut principium demonstrationis, et secundum causas singulas; ita etiam dicitur ordo. In divinis autem dicitur principium secundum originem, absque prioritate, ut supra dictum est. Unde oportet ibi esse ordinem secundum originem, absque prioritate. Et hic vocatur ordo naturae, secundum Augustinum, *non quo alter sit prius altero, sed quo alter est ex altero*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0064","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0065","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ordo naturae significat notionem originis in communi, non autem in speciali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0066","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0067","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in rebus creatis, etiam cum id quod est a principio sit suo principio coaevum secundum durationem, tamen principium est prius secundum naturam et intellectum, si consideretur id quod est principium. Sed si considerentur ipsae relationes causae et causati, et principii et principiati, manifestum est quod relativa sunt simul natura et intellectu, inquantum unum est in definitione alterius. Sed in divinis ipsae relationes sunt subsistentes personae in una natura. Unde neque ex parte naturae, neque ex parte relationum, una persona potest esse prior alia, neque etiam secundum naturam et intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0068","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0069","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ordo naturae dicitur, non quod ipsa natura ordinetur, sed quod ordo in divinis personis attenditur secundum naturalem originem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0070","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0071","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod natura quodammodo importat rationem principii, non autem essentia. Et ideo ordo originis melius nominatur ordo naturae, quam ordo essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0072","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0073","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0074","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filius non sit aequalis patri in magnitudine. Dicit enim ipse, Ioan. XIV, *pater maior me est*; et apostolus, I Cor. XV, *ipse filius subiectus erit illi qui sibi subiecit omnia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0075","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0076","text":"Praeterea, paternitas pertinet ad dignitatem patris. Sed paternitas non convenit filio. Ergo non quidquid dignitatis habet pater, habet filius. Ergo non est aequalis patri in magnitudine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0077","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0078","text":"Praeterea, ubicumque est totum et pars, plures partes sunt aliquid maius quam una tantum vel pauciores; sicut tres homines sunt aliquid maius quam duo vel unus. Sed in divinis videtur esse totum universale et pars, nam sub relatione vel notione plures notiones continentur. Cum igitur in patre sint tres notiones, in filio autem tantum duae, videtur quod filius non sit aequalis patri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0079","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0080","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Philip. II, *non rapinam arbitratus est esse se aequalem Deo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0081","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0082","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere filium esse aequalem patri in magnitudine. Magnitudo enim Dei non est aliud quam perfectio naturae ipsius. Hoc autem est de ratione paternitatis et filiationis, quod filius per generationem pertingat ad habendam perfectionem naturae quae est in patre, sicut et pater. Sed quia in hominibus generatio est transmutatio quaedam exeuntis de potentia in actum, non statim a principio homo filius est aequalis patri generanti; sed per debitum incrementum ad aequalitatem perducitur, nisi aliter eveniat propter defectum principii generationis. Manifestum est autem ex dictis quod in divinis est proprie et vere paternitas et filiatio. Nec potest dici quod virtus Dei patris fuerit defectiva in generando; neque quod Dei filius successive et per transmutationem ad perfectionem pervenerit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0083","text":"Unde necesse est dicere quod ab aeterno fuerit patri aequalis in magnitudine. Unde et Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., *tolle corporum infirmitates, tolle conceptus initium, tolle dolores et omnem humanam necessitatem, omnis filius secundum naturalem nativitatem aequalitas patris est, quia est et similitudo naturae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0084","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0085","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verba illa intelliguntur dicta de Christo secundum humanam naturam, in qua minor est patre, et ei subiectus. Sed secundum naturam divinam, aequalis est patri. Et hoc est quod Athanasius dicit; *aequalis patri secundum divinitatem, minor patre secundum humanitatem*. Vel, secundum Hilarium, in IX libro de Trin., *donantis auctoritate pater maior est, sed minor non est cui unum esse donatur*. Et in libro de Synod. dicit quod subiectio filii naturae pietas est, idest recognitio auctoritatis paternae, subiectio autem ceterorum, creationis infirmitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0086","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0087","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aequalitas attenditur secundum magnitudinem. Magnitudo autem in divinis significat perfectionem naturae, ut dictum est, et ad essentiam pertinet. Et ideo aequalitas in divinis, et similitudo, secundum essentialia attenditur, nec potest secundum distinctionem relationum inaequalitas vel dissimilitudo dici. Unde Augustinus dicit, contra Maximinum, *originis quaestio est quid de quo sit; aequalitatis autem, qualis aut quantus sit*. Paternitas igitur est dignitas patris, sicut et essentia patris, nam dignitas absolutum est, et ad essentiam pertinet. Sicut igitur eadem essentia quae in patre est paternitas, in filio est filiatio; ita eadem dignitas quae in patre est paternitas, in filio est filiatio. Vere ergo dicitur quod quidquid dignitatis habet pater, habet filius. Nec sequitur, paternitatem habet pater, ergo paternitatem habet filius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0088","text":"Mutatur enim quid in ad aliquid, eadem enim est essentia et dignitas patris et filii, sed in patre est secundum relationem dantis, in filio secundum relationem accipientis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0089","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0090","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod relatio in divinis non est totum universale, quamvis de pluribus relationibus praedicetur, quia omnes relationes sunt unum secundum essentiam et esse, quod repugnat rationi universalis, cuius partes secundum esse distinguuntur. Et similiter persona, ut supra dictum est, non est universale in divinis. Unde neque omnes relationes sunt maius aliquid quam una tantum; nec omnes personae maius aliquid quam una tantum; quia tota perfectio divinae naturae est in qualibet personarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0091","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0092","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0093","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filius non sit in patre, et e converso. Philosophus enim, in IV Physic., ponit octo modos essendi aliquid in aliquo; et secundum nullum horum filius est in patre, aut e converso, ut patet discurrenti per singulos modos. Ergo filius non est in patre, nec e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0094","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0095","text":"Praeterea, nihil quod exivit ab aliquo, est in eo. Sed filius ab aeterno exivit a patre, secundum illud Micheae V, *egressus eius ab initio, a diebus aeternitatis*. Ergo filius non est in patre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0096","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0097","text":"Praeterea, unum oppositorum non est in altero. Sed filius et pater opponuntur relative. Ergo unus non potest esse in alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0098","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0099","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. XIV, *ego in patre, et pater in me est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0100","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0101","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in patre et filio tria est considerare, scilicet essentiam, relationem et originem; et secundum quodlibet istorum filius est in patre, et e converso. Secundum essentiam enim pater est in filio, quia pater est sua essentia, et communicat suam essentiam filio, non per aliquam suam transmutationem, unde sequitur quod, cum essentia patris sit in filio, quod in filio sit pater. Et similiter, cum filius sit sua essentia, sequitur quod sit in patre, in quo est eius essentia. Et hoc est quod Hilarius dicit, V de Trin., *naturam suam, ut ita dicam, sequitur immutabilis Deus, immutabilem gignens Deum. Subsistentem ergo in eo Dei naturam intelligimus, cum in Deo Deus insit*. Secundum etiam relationes, manifestum est quod unum oppositorum relative est in altero secundum intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0102","text":"Secundum originem etiam manifestum est quod processio verbi intelligibilis non est ad extra, sed manet in dicente. Id etiam quod verbo dicitur, in verbo continetur. Et eadem ratio est de spiritu sancto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0103","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0104","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ea quae in creaturis sunt, non sufficienter repraesentant ea quae Dei sunt. Et ideo secundum nullum eorum modorum quos philosophus enumerat, filius est in patre, aut e converso. Accedit tamen magis ad hoc modus ille, secundum quem aliquid dicitur esse in principio originante, nisi quod deest unitas essentiae, in rebus creatis, inter principium et id quod est a principio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0105","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0106","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod exitus filii a patre est secundum modum processionis interioris, prout verbum exit a corde, et manet in eo. Unde exitus iste in divinis est secundum solam distinctionem relationum, non secundum essentialem aliquam distantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0107","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0108","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod pater et filius opponuntur secundum relationes, non autem secundum essentiam. Et tamen oppositorum relative unum est in altero, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0109","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0110","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0111","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filius non sit aequalis patri secundum potentiam. Dicitur enim Ioan. V, *non potest filius a se facere quidquam, nisi quod viderit patrem facientem*. Pater autem a se potest facere. Ergo pater maior est filio secundum potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0112","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0113","text":"Praeterea, maior est potentia eius qui praecipit et docet, quam eius qui obedit et audit. Sed pater mandat filio, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *sicut mandatum dedit mihi pater, sic facio*. Pater etiam docet filium, secundum illud Ioan. V, *pater diligit filium, et omnia demonstrat ei quae ipse facit*. Similiter et filius audit, secundum illud Ioan. V, *sicut audio, iudico*. Ergo pater est maioris potentiae quam filius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0114","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0115","text":"Praeterea, ad omnipotentiam patris pertinet quod possit filium generare sibi aequalem, dicit enim Augustinus, in libro contra Maximin., *si non potuit generare sibi aequalem, ubi est omnipotentia Dei patris?* Sed filius non potest generare filium, ut supra ostensum est. Non ergo quidquid pertinet ad omnipotentiam patris, potest filius. Et ita non est ei in potestate aequalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0116","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0117","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. V, *quaecumque pater facit, haec et filius similiter facit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0118","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0119","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere quod filius est aequalis patri in potestate. Potentia enim agendi consequitur perfectionem naturae, videmus enim in creaturis quod quanto aliquid habet perfectiorem naturam, tanto est maioris virtutis in agendo. Ostensum est autem supra quod ipsa ratio divinae paternitatis et filiationis exigit quod filius sit aequalis patri in magnitudine, idest in perfectione naturae. Unde relinquitur quod filius sit aequalis patri in potestate. Et eadem ratio est de spiritu sancto respectu utriusque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0120","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0121","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in hoc quod dicitur quod filius non potest a se facere quidquam, non subtrahitur filio aliqua potestas quam habeat pater; cum statim subdatur quod quaecumque pater facit, filius similiter facit. Sed ostenditur quod filius habet potestatem a patre, a quo habet naturam. Unde dicit Hilarius, IX de Trin., *naturae divinae haec unitas est, ut ita per se agat filius, quod non a se agat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0122","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0123","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in demonstratione patris et auditione filii, non intelligitur nisi quod pater communicat scientiam filio, sicut et essentiam. Et ad idem potest referri mandatum patris, per hoc quod ab aeterno dedit ei scientiam et voluntatem agendorum, eum generando. Vel potius referendum est ad Christum secundum humanam naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0124","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0125","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut eadem essentia quae in patre est paternitas, in filio est filiatio; ita eadem est potentia qua pater generat, et qua filius generatur. Unde manifestum est quod quidquid potest pater, potest filius. Non tamen sequitur quod possit generare, sed mutatur quid in ad aliquid, nam generatio significat relationem in divinis. Habet ergo filius eandem omnipotentiam quam pater, sed cum alia relatione. Quia pater habet eam ut dans, et hoc significatur, cum dicitur quod potest generare. Filius autem habet eam ut accipiens, et hoc significatur, cum dicitur quod potest generari.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4e0180a3ed5033c9778c42dee8b6c514bd0fd7d1c9286f4a1c0b2836024847e9","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T142539148555Z_bc199b43","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 42","source_sha256":"4e0180a3ed5033c9778c42dee8b6c514bd0fd7d1c9286f4a1c0b2836024847e9","sha256":"7a966e7f790258f1354b29a5d78e971ead99ea3248cd8145193c1dd0c8e2b2a5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:25:39.148587+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0002","text":"[I q. 42 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the comparison of the persons with one another. First, as regards equality and likeness; second, as regards mission. Concerning the first, six questions are asked. First, whether equality has a place in the divine persons. Second, whether the proceeding person is equal in eternity to the one from whom it proceeds. Third, whether there is any order among the divine persons. Fourth, whether the divine persons are equal in magnitude. Fifth, whether one of them is in another. Sixth, whether they are equal in power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0005","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that equality does not belong to the divine persons. For equality is considered according to unity in quantity, as is clear from the Philosopher, Metaphysics V. But in the divine persons there is found neither intrinsic continuous quantity, which is called magnitude; nor extrinsic continuous quantity, which is called place and time; nor is equality found in them according to discrete quantity, because two persons are more than one. Therefore equality does not befit the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0007","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0008","text":"Further, the divine persons are of one essence, as was said above. But essence is signified after the manner of form. Now agreement in form does not produce equality, but likeness. Therefore likeness, and not equality, is to be predicated of the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0009","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0010","text":"Further, wherever equality is found, those things are equal to one another, because an equal is said to be equal to an equal. But the divine persons cannot be said to be equal to one another. For, as Augustine says, in On the Trinity VI, *an image, if it perfectly fulfills that of which it is an image, is itself made equal to it, not it to its image*. But the image of the Father is the Son, and thus the Father is not equal to the Son. Therefore equality is not found in the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0011","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0012","text":"Further, equality is a certain relation. But no relation is common to all the persons, since the persons are distinguished from one another according to relations. Therefore equality does not belong to the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0013","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0014","text":"On the contrary, Athanasius says that *the three persons are coeternal with one another and coequal*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0015","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0016","text":"I answer that it is necessary to posit equality in the divine persons. For according to the Philosopher, in Metaphysics X, equal is said, as it were, through the negation of lesser and greater. But we cannot posit anything greater or lesser in the divine persons, because, as Boethius says in the book On the Trinity, difference, namely of deity, accompanies those *who either increase or diminish, as the Arians do, who, varying the Trinity by degrees of merits, tear it asunder and lead it into plurality*. The reason for this is that there cannot be one quantity numerically in unequal things. But quantity in the divine is nothing other than its essence. Hence it follows that, if there were any inequality in the divine persons, there would not be one essence in them, and thus the three persons would not be one God, which is impossible. Therefore equality must be posited in the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0017","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that quantity is twofold. One is called quantity of bulk, or dimensional quantity, which exists only in corporeal things; hence it has no place in the divine persons. But another is quantity of power, which is considered according to the perfection of some nature or form; this quantity is indicated insofar as something is called more or less hot, inasmuch as it is more perfect or less perfect in heat. Now such virtual quantity is considered first in its root, that is, in the very perfection of the form or nature, and thus it is called spiritual magnitude, just as heat is called great on account of its intensity and perfection. And therefore Augustine says, in On the Trinity VI, that *in those things which are not great by bulk, to be greater is to be better*, for that is called better which is more perfect. Secondly, however, virtual quantity is considered in the effects of form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0019","text":"But the first effect of form is being, for every thing has being according to its form. The second effect is operation, for every agent acts through its form. Therefore virtual quantity is considered both according to being and according to operation: according to being, indeed, insofar as those things which are of a more perfect nature are of greater duration; but according to operation, insofar as those things which are of a more perfect nature are more powerful for acting. Thus, therefore, as Augustine says in the book On Faith to Peter, equality is understood in the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, *inasmuch as none of these either precedes in eternity, or exceeds in magnitude, or surpasses in power*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0020","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0021","text":"To the second it must be said that where equality is considered according to virtual quantity, equality includes likeness within itself, and something more, because it excludes excess. For whatever things share in one form can be called like, even if they participate unequally in that form, as when air is said to be like fire in heat; but they cannot be called equal if one participates in that form more perfectly than the other. And because not only is the nature of the Father and the Son one, but it is also equally perfect in each, we therefore not only say that the Son is like the Father, so that the error of Eunomius may be excluded; but we also say that he is equal, so that the error of Arius may be excluded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0022","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0023","text":"To the third it must be said that equality or likeness can be signified in two ways in the divine, namely, by nouns and by verbs. Insofar as it is signified by nouns, mutual equality and likeness are predicated of the divine persons: for the Son is equal and like to the Father, and conversely. And this is because the divine essence belongs no more to the Father than to the Son; hence, just as the Son has the magnitude of the Father, which is for him to be equal to the Father, so the Father has the magnitude of the Son, which is for him to be equal to the Son. But with respect to creatures, as Dionysius says in chapter IX of On the Divine Names, *the conversion of equality and likeness is not admitted*. For effects are said to be like their causes insofar as they have the forms of the causes, but not conversely, because the form is principally in the cause and secondarily in the effect. But verbs signify equality with motion. And although there is no motion in the divine, it can nevertheless be taken there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0024","text":"Therefore, because the Son receives from the Father that whereby he is equal to him, and not conversely, on this account we say that the Son is made coequal to the Father, and not conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0025","text":"[I q. 42 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0026","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that in the divine persons there is nothing to be considered except the essence, in which they communicate, and the relations, in which they are distinguished. Equality, however, imports both: namely, the distinction of persons, because nothing is said to be equal to itself; and the unity of essence, because the persons are equal to one another from the fact that they are of one magnitude and essence. Now it is manifest that the same thing is not related to itself by any real relation. Nor, again, is one relation related to another through any other relation; for when we say that paternity is opposed to filiation, opposition is not a relation intermediate between paternity and filiation. For in either way relation would be multiplied to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0027","text":"And therefore equality and likeness in the divine persons are not any real relation distinct from the personal relations, but in their intelligible account they include both the relations distinguishing the persons and the unity of the essence. And therefore the Master says, in distinction XXXI of the First Book of the Sentences, that in these there is only a relative appellation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0028","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0029","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0030","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the proceeding person is not coeternal with his principle, as the Son is with the Father. For Arius assigns twelve modes of generation. The first mode is according to the flowing of a line from a point, where equality of simplicity is lacking. The second mode is according to the emission of rays from the sun, where equality of nature is lacking. The third mode is according to a character, or impression, from a seal, where consubstantiality and the efficacy of power are lacking. The fourth mode is according to the imparting of good will from God, where consubstantiality is likewise lacking. The fifth mode is according to the issuing forth of an accident from a substance, but an accident lacks subsistence. The sixth mode is according to the abstraction of a species from matter, as sense receives a species from a sensible thing, where equality of spiritual simplicity is lacking. The seventh mode is according to the arousing of the will by thought, which arousing is temporal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0031","text":"The eighth mode is according to transfiguration, as an image comes to be from air, which is material. The ninth mode is motion from a mover, and here too an effect and a cause are posited. The tenth mode is according to the drawing forth of species from genus, which does not befit divine things, because the Father is not predicated of the Son as genus of species. The eleventh mode is according to ideation, as an outer chest is from the one which is in the mind. The twelfth mode is according to birth, as a man is from a father, where there is before and after according to time. Therefore it is clear that in every mode by which something is from another, either equality of nature or equality of duration is lacking. Therefore, if the Son is from the Father, it is necessary to say either that he is less than the Father, or later, or both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0032","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0033","text":"Moreover, everything that is from another has a principle. But nothing eternal has a principle. Therefore the Son is not eternal, nor is the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0034","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0035","text":"Moreover, everything that is corrupted ceases to be. Therefore everything that is generated begins to be, for it is generated in order that it may be. But the Son is begotten by the Father. Therefore he begins to be and is not coeternal with the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0036","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0037","text":"Moreover, if the Son is begotten by the Father, either he is always being generated, or some instant of his generation must be posited. If he is always being generated—while something is in being generated, it is imperfect, as is evident in successive things, which are always in coming-to-be, such as time and motion—it follows that the Son is always imperfect; which is unfitting. Therefore some instant of the Son's generation must be posited. Before that instant, therefore, the Son was not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0038","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0039","text":"But on the contrary, Athanasius says that *all three persons are coeternal with one another*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0040","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0041","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Son is coeternal with the Father. For the clarification of this, it must be considered that something existing from a principle can be later than its principle in two ways: in one way, on the part of the agent; in another way, on the part of the action. On the part of the agent, however, it is otherwise in voluntary agents and otherwise in natural agents. In voluntary agents it is so because of the choice of time: for just as it is in the power of a voluntary agent to choose the form which he may confer upon the effect, as was said above, so it is in his power to choose the time at which he may produce the effect. But in natural agents this occurs because some agent does not from the beginning have the perfection of the natural power for acting, but it comes to him after some time; just as a man cannot generate from the beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0042","text":"On the part of the action, however, that which is from a principle is prevented from existing simultaneously with its principle because the action is successive. Hence, granted that some agent began to act by such an action as soon as it exists, the effect would not exist immediately in that same instant, but in the instant at which the action is terminated. But it is manifest from what has been said that the Father does not generate the Son by will, but by nature. And again, that the nature of the Father was perfect from eternity. And again, that the action by which the Father produces the Son is not successive, because in that case the Son of God would be generated successively, and his generation would be material and with motion, which is impossible. It remains, therefore, that the Son existed whenever the Father existed. And thus the Son is coeternal with the Father, and likewise the Holy Spirit with both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0043","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0044","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Augustine says in the book On the Words of the Lord, no mode of procession of any creature perfectly represents the divine generation; hence it is necessary to gather a likeness from many modes, so that what is lacking in one may in some way be supplied from another. And for this reason it is said in the Synod of Ephesus: *Let the splendor proclaim to you that the Son coexists ever coeternal with the Father; let the Word show the impassibility of the nativity; let the name Son indicate consubstantiality.* Yet among all, the procession of the word from the intellect represents it more expressly; and the word is not later than that from which it proceeds, unless it is an intellect such as passes from potency into act, which cannot be said in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0045","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0046","text":"To the second, it must be said that eternity excludes a principle of duration, but not a principle of origin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0047","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0048","text":"To the third, it must be said that every corruption is a certain change, and therefore everything that is corrupted begins not to be and ceases to be. But divine generation is not a transmutation, as was said above. Hence the Son is always being generated, and the Father always generates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0049","text":"[I q. 42 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0050","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that in time one thing is indivisible, namely, the instant; and another is enduring, namely, time. But in eternity the indivisible now itself is always standing, as was said above. But the generation of the Son is not in the now of time, or in time, but in eternity. And therefore, to signify the presence and permanence of eternity, it can be said that he is always being born, as Origen said. But, as Gregory and Augustine say, it is better that he be said to be always born, so that “always” designates the permanence of eternity, and “born” the perfection of the begotten. Thus, therefore, the Son is neither imperfect, nor was there a time when he was not, as Arius said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0051","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0052","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0053","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that there is no order of nature in the divine persons. For whatever is in God is either essence or person or notion. But the order of nature does not signify essence, nor is it any of the persons or notions. Therefore, the order of nature is not in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0054","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0055","text":"Further, in whatever things there is an order of nature, one is prior to another, at least according to nature and understanding. But in the divine persons there is nothing prior or posterior, as Athanasius says. Therefore, there is no order of nature in the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0056","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0057","text":"Further, whatever is ordered is distinguished. But nature in God is not distinguished. Therefore, it is not ordered. Therefore, there is no order of nature there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0058","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0059","text":"Further, the divine nature is his essence. But an order of essence is not spoken of in God. Therefore, neither is an order of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0060","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0061","text":"On the contrary, wherever there is plurality without order, there is confusion. But in the divine persons there is no confusion, as Athanasius says. Therefore, there is order there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0062","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0063","text":"I answer that it must be said that order is always spoken of by comparison to some principle. Hence, just as “principle” is spoken of in many ways—namely, according to position, as a point; according to understanding, as the principle of a demonstration; and according to the individual causes—so too is “order” spoken of. But in God, principle is spoken of according to origin, without priority, as was said above. Hence there must be order there according to origin, without priority. And this is called the order of nature, according to Augustine, *not because one is prior to another, but because one is from another*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0064","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0065","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the order of nature signifies the notion of origin in general, but not in particular.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0066","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0067","text":"To the second, it must be said that in created things, even when that which is from a principle is coeval with its principle in duration, nevertheless the principle is prior according to nature and understanding, if that which is a principle is considered. But if the relations themselves of cause and caused, and of principle and what proceeds from a principle, are considered, it is manifest that relatives are simultaneous in nature and understanding, insofar as one is in the definition of the other. But in God the relations themselves are subsistent persons in one nature. Hence, neither on the part of nature nor on the part of the relations can one person be prior to another, not even according to nature and understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0068","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0069","text":"To the third, it must be said that it is called the order of nature, not because nature itself is ordered, but because the order in the divine persons is considered according to natural origin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0070","text":"[I q. 42 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0071","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that nature in some way conveys the character of a principle, but essence does not. And therefore the order of origin is more fittingly called the order of nature than the order of essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0072","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0073","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0074","text":"The fourth point is approached thus. It seems that the Son is not equal to the Father in greatness. For he himself says, John XIV, *the Father is greater than I*; and the Apostle, I Cor. XV, *the Son himself will be subject to him who subjected all things to him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0075","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0076","text":"Moreover, paternity pertains to the dignity of the Father. But paternity does not belong to the Son. Therefore the Son does not possess everything of dignity that the Father possesses. Therefore he is not equal to the Father in greatness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0077","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0078","text":"Moreover, wherever there are a whole and a part, several parts are something greater than only one or fewer; just as three men are something greater than two or one. But in divine things there seems to be a universal whole and a part, for under relation or notion several notions are contained. Therefore, since there are three notions in the Father, but only two in the Son, it seems that the Son is not equal to the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0079","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0080","text":"On the contrary, it is said, Phil. II, *he did not deem equality with God a thing to be seized*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0081","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0082","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Son is equal to the Father in greatness. For the greatness of God is nothing other than the perfection of his nature itself. Now it belongs to the character of paternity and filiation that the Son, through generation, attain to possessing the perfection of nature that is in the Father, as does the Father. But because in human beings generation is a certain change of one going from potency to act, a man who is a son is not equal from the very beginning to his generating father; rather, through due growth, he is brought to equality, unless something else occurs because of a defect in the principle of generation. But it is manifest from what has been said that in divine things there are properly and truly paternity and filiation. Nor can it be said that the power of God the Father was defective in generating, or that the Son of God attained perfection successively and through change.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0083","text":"Hence it must be said that from eternity he has been equal to the Father in greatness. Thus Hilary also says, in the book *On the Synods*, *remove the infirmities of bodies, remove the beginning of conception, remove pains and every human necessity: every son, according to natural birth, is the equality of the father, because he is also the likeness of the nature*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0084","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0085","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that those words are understood as spoken of Christ according to his human nature, in which he is less than the Father and subject to him. But according to the divine nature, he is equal to the Father. And this is what Athanasius says: *equal to the Father according to divinity, less than the Father according to humanity*. Or, according to Hilary, in Book IX of *On the Trinity*, *by the authority of the giver the Father is greater, but he to whom one being is given is not less*. And in the book *On the Synods* he says that the subjection of the Son is the piety of nature, that is, recognition of paternal authority; whereas the subjection of the rest is the weakness of creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0086","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0087","text":"To the second it must be said that equality is considered according to greatness. But greatness in divine things signifies the perfection of nature, as has been said, and pertains to essence. Therefore equality in divine things, and likeness, are considered according to what is essential; nor can inequality or unlikeness be said according to the distinction of relations. Hence Augustine says, against Maximinus, *the question of origin is what is from what; but that of equality, what sort it is or how great it is*. Paternity, therefore, is the dignity of the Father, just as the essence of the Father is, for dignity is absolute and pertains to essence. Therefore, just as the same essence which in the Father is paternity is filiation in the Son, so the same dignity which in the Father is paternity is filiation in the Son. Therefore it is truly said that whatever dignity the Father has, the Son has. Nor does it follow: the Father has paternity; therefore, the Son has paternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0088","text":"For the *what* is changed into a *toward-something*; for the essence and dignity of the Father and the Son are the same, but in the Father they are according to the relation of giver, and in the Son according to the relation of receiver.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0089","text":"[I q. 42 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0090","text":"To the third it must be said that relation in divine things is not a universal whole, although it is predicated of several relations, because all relations are one according to essence and being, which is incompatible with the character of a universal, whose parts are distinguished according to being. Likewise, as was said above, person is not a universal in divine things. Hence neither are all relations something greater than only one, nor are all persons something greater than only one, because the whole perfection of the divine nature is in each of the persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0091","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0092","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0093","text":"The fifth point is approached thus. It seems that the Son is not in the Father, and conversely. For the Philosopher, in *Physics* IV, posits eight modes of something's being in something; and according to none of these is the Son in the Father, or conversely, as is clear to one who runs through the individual modes. Therefore the Son is not in the Father, nor conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0094","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0095","text":"Moreover, nothing that has gone forth from something is in it. But the Son has gone forth from the Father from eternity, according to Micah V, *his going forth is from the beginning, from the days of eternity*. Therefore the Son is not in the Father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0096","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0097","text":"Moreover, one of opposites is not in the other. But Son and Father are relatively opposed. Therefore, one cannot be in the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0098","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0099","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in John XIV, *I am in the Father, and the Father is in me*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0100","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0101","text":"I answer that it must be said that three things are to be considered in the Father and the Son, namely, essence, relation, and origin; and according to each of these the Son is in the Father, and conversely. For according to essence the Father is in the Son, because the Father is his essence, and communicates his essence to the Son, not through any change in himself; whence it follows that, since the essence of the Father is in the Son, the Father is in the Son. And similarly, since the Son is his essence, it follows that he is in the Father, in whom his essence is. And this is what Hilary says, *On the Trinity*, V: *The unchangeable God, begetting unchangeable God, follows, so to speak, his own nature. Therefore, when God is in God, we understand the subsisting nature of God to be in him*. According to the relations also, it is manifest that one of relatively opposed things is in the other according to understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0102","text":"According to origin also it is manifest that the procession of the intelligible word is not outward, but remains in the speaker. That which is spoken by the word is also contained in the word. And the same reasoning applies to the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0103","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0104","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that those things which are in creatures do not sufficiently represent those things which are in God. And therefore, according to none of the modes enumerated by the Philosopher is the Son in the Father, or conversely. Yet that mode comes closer to this according to which something is said to be in its originating principle, except that in created things unity of essence is lacking between the principle and that which is from the principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0105","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0106","text":"To the second, it must be said that the coming forth of the Son from the Father is according to the mode of an interior procession, as a word comes forth from the heart and remains in it. Hence this coming forth in divine things is according only to the distinction of relations, not according to any essential distance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0107","text":"[I q. 42 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0108","text":"To the third, it must be said that Father and Son are opposed according to relations, but not according to essence. And yet, of relatively opposed things, one is in the other, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0109","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0110","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0111","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth article. It seems that the Son is not equal to the Father in power. For it is said in John V, *the Son cannot do anything of himself, unless he sees the Father doing it*. But the Father can act of himself. Therefore, the Father is greater than the Son in power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0112","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0113","text":"Moreover, the power of one who commands and teaches is greater than that of one who obeys and hears. But the Father commands the Son, according to John XIV, *as the Father gave me commandment, so I do*. The Father also teaches the Son, according to John V, *the Father loves the Son, and shows him all things that he himself does*. Likewise, the Son hears, according to John V, *as I hear, I judge*. Therefore, the Father is of greater power than the Son.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0114","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0115","text":"Moreover, it belongs to the omnipotence of the Father that he can generate a Son equal to himself; for Augustine says, in the book *Against Maximinus*, *if he could not generate one equal to himself, where is the omnipotence of God the Father?* But the Son cannot generate a Son, as was shown above. Therefore, the Son cannot do whatever belongs to the omnipotence of the Father. And thus he is not equal to him in power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0116","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0117","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in John V, *whatever the Father does, these things the Son also does in like manner*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0118","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0119","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Son is necessarily equal to the Father in power. For the power of acting follows the perfection of nature; indeed, we see in creatures that the more perfect a nature something has, the greater its power in acting. But it was shown above that the very character of divine fatherhood and sonship requires that the Son be equal to the Father in greatness, that is, in perfection of nature. Hence it remains that the Son is equal to the Father in power. And the same reasoning applies to the Holy Spirit with respect to both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0120","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0121","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in the statement that the Son cannot do anything of himself, no power that the Father has is taken away from the Son; since it is immediately added that whatever the Father does, the Son likewise does. Rather, it is shown that the Son has power from the Father, from whom he has his nature. Hence Hilary says, *On the Trinity*, IX: “This is the unity of the divine nature: that the Son so acts through himself that he does not act from himself.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0122","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0123","text":"To the second, it must be said that in the Father's showing and the Son's hearing, nothing is understood except that the Father communicates knowledge to the Son, just as he communicates essence. And the Father's command can be referred to the same thing, namely, that from eternity, in generating him, he gave him knowledge and will concerning the things to be done. Or, rather, it should be referred to Christ according to his human nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0124","text":"[I q. 42 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0125","text":"To the third, it must be said that, just as the same essence which in the Father is paternity is in the Son filiation, so too the power by which the Father generates and by which the Son is generated is the same. Hence it is manifest that whatever the Father can do, the Son can do. Yet it does not follow that he can generate; rather, “what” is changed into “relative to something,” for generation signifies a relation in divine things. Therefore, the Son has the same omnipotence as the Father, but with another relation. For the Father has it as giving, and this is signified when it is said that he can generate. But the Son has it as receiving, and this is signified when it is said that he can be generated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_34:P0125"]}],"source_digest":"2250fe2b91ac1ab4400083d45604f103d6b31d6e42d5534ec42b45010930281e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 42"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 42","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35","title":"First Part · Question 43","order":44,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_35","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 43 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30209)\n\nDeinde considerandum de missione divinarum personarum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum alicui divinae personae conveniat mitti. Secundo, utrum missio sit aeterna, vel temporalis tantum. Tertio, secundum quid divina persona invisibiliter mittatur. Quarto, utrum cuilibet personae conveniat mitti. Quinto, utrum invisibiliter mittatur tam filius, quam spiritus sanctus. Sexto, ad quos fiat missio invisibilis. Septimo, de missione visibili. Octavo, utrum aliqua persona mittat seipsam, visibiliter aut invisibiliter.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30210)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod personae divinae non conveniat mitti. Missus enim minor est mittente. Sed una persona divina non est minor alia. Ergo una persona non mittitur ab alia.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30211)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod mittitur, separatur a mittente, unde Hieronymus dicit, super Ezechiel., *quod coniunctum est, et in corpore uno copulatum, mitti non potest*. Sed in divinis personis nihil est separabile, ut Hilarius dicit. Ergo una persona non mittitur ab alia.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30212)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque mittitur, ab aliquo loco discedit, et ad aliquem locum de novo vadit. Hoc autem divinae personae non convenit, cum ubique sit. Ergo divinae personae non convenit mitti.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30213)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. VIII, *non sum ego solus, sed ego et qui misit me, pater*.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30214)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in ratione missionis duo importantur, quorum unum est habitudo missi ad eum a quo mittitur; aliud est habitudo missi ad terminum ad quem mittitur. Per hoc autem quod aliquis mittitur, ostenditur processio quaedam missi a mittente; vel secundum imperium, sicut dominus mittit servum; vel secundum consilium, ut si consiliarius mittere dicatur regem ad bellandum; vel secundum originem, ut si dicatur quod flos emittitur ab arbore. Ostenditur etiam habitudo ad terminum ad quem mittitur, ut aliquo modo ibi esse incipiat; vel quia prius ibi omnino non erat quo mittitur; vel quia incipit ibi aliquo modo esse, quo prius non erat. Missio igitur divinae personae convenire potest, secundum quod importat ex una parte processionem originis a mittente; et secundum quod importat ex alia parte novum modum existendi in aliquo. Sicut filius dicitur esse missus a patre in mundum, secundum quod incoepit esse in mundo visibiliter per carnem assumptam, et tamen ante in mundo erat, ut dicitur Ioan. I.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30215)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod missio importat minorationem in eo qui mittitur, secundum quod importat processionem a principio mittente aut secundum imperium, aut secundum consilium, quia imperans est maior, et consilians est sapientior. Sed in divinis non importat nisi processionem originis; quae est secundum aequalitatem, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30216)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illud quod sic mittitur ut incipiat esse ubi prius nullo modo erat, sua missione localiter movetur, unde oportet quod loco separetur a mittente. Sed hoc non accidit in missione divinae personae, quia persona divina missa, sicut non incipit esse ubi prius non fuerat, ita nec desinit esse ubi fuerat. Unde talis missio est sine separatione; sed habet solam distinctionem originis.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30217)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de missione quae fit secundum motum localem; quae non habet locum in divinis.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30218)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod missio possit esse aeterna. Dicit enim Gregorius, eo mittitur filius, quo generatur. Sed generatio filii est aeterna. Ergo et missio.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30219)\n\nPraeterea, cuicumque convenit aliquid temporaliter, illud mutatur. Sed persona divina non mutatur. Ergo missio divinae personae non est temporalis, sed aeterna.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30220)\n\nPraeterea, missio processionem importat. Sed processio divinarum personarum est aeterna. Ergo et missio.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30221)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Galat. IV, *cum venit plenitudo temporis, misit Deus filium suum*.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30222)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in his quae important originem divinarum personarum, est quaedam differentia attendenda. Quaedam enim in sui significatione important solam habitudinem ad principium, ut processio et exitus. Quaedam vero, cum habitudine ad principium, determinant processionis terminum. Quorum quaedam determinant terminum aeternum, sicut generatio et spiratio, nam generatio est processio divinae personae in naturam divinam; et spiratio, passive accepta, importat processionem amoris subsistentis. Quaedam vero, cum habitudine ad principium, important terminum temporalem, sicut missio et datio, mittitur enim aliquid ad hoc ut sit in aliquo, et datur ad hoc quod habeatur; personam autem divinam haberi ab aliqua creatura, vel esse novo modo existendi in ea, est quoddam temporale. Unde missio et datio in divinis dicuntur temporaliter tantum. Generatio autem et spiratio solum ab aeterno. Processio autem et exitus dicuntur in divinis et aeternaliter et temporaliter, nam filius ab aeterno processit ut sit Deus; temporaliter autem ut etiam sit homo, secundum missionem visibilem; vel etiam ut sit in homine, secundum invisibilem missionem.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30223)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Gregorius loquitur de generatione temporali filii, non a patre, sed a matre. Vel, quia ex hoc ipso filius habet quod possit mitti, quod est ab aeterno genitus.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30224)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod divinam personam esse novo modo in aliquo, vel ab aliquo haberi temporaliter, non est propter mutationem divinae personae, sed propter mutationem creaturae, sicut et Deus temporaliter dicitur dominus, propter mutationem creaturae.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30225)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod missio non solum importat processionem a principio, sed determinat processionis terminum temporalem. Unde missio solum est temporalis. Vel, missio includit processionem aeternam, et aliquid addit, scilicet temporalem effectum, habitudo enim divinae personae ad suum principium non est nisi ab aeterno. Unde gemina dicitur processio, aeterna scilicet et temporalis, non propter hoc quod habitudo ad principium geminetur, sed geminatio est ex parte termini temporalis et aeterni.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30226)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod missio invisibilis divinae personae non sit solum secundum donum gratiae gratum facientis. Divinam enim personam mitti, est ipsam donari. Si igitur divina persona mittitur solum secundum dona gratiae gratum facientis, non donabitur ipsa persona divina, sed solum dona eius. Quod est error dicentium spiritum sanctum non dari, sed eius dona.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30227)\n\nPraeterea, haec praepositio secundum denotat habitudinem alicuius causae. Sed persona divina est causa quod habeatur donum gratiae gratum facientis, et non e converso; secundum illud Rom. V, *caritas Dei diffusa est in cordibus nostris per spiritum sanctum, qui datus est nobis*. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur quod persona divina secundum dona gratiae gratum facientis mittatur.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30228)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, IV de Trin., quod *filius, cum ex tempore mente percipitur, mitti dicitur*. Sed filius cognoscitur non solum per gratiam gratum facientem, sed etiam per gratiam gratis datam, sicut per fidem et per scientiam. Non ergo persona divina mittitur secundum solam gratiam gratum facientem.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30229)\n\nPraeterea, Rabanus dicit quod spiritus sanctus datus est apostolis ad operationem miraculorum. Hoc autem non est donum gratiae gratum facientis, sed gratiae gratis datae. Ergo persona divina non solum datur secundum gratiam gratum facientem.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30230)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XV de Trin., quod spiritus sanctus procedit temporaliter ad sanctificandam creaturam. Missio autem est temporalis processio. Cum igitur sanctificatio creaturae non sit nisi per gratiam gratum facientem, sequitur quod missio divinae personae non sit nisi per gratiam gratum facientem.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30231)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod divinae personae convenit mitti, secundum quod novo modo existit in aliquo; dari autem, secundum quod habetur ab aliquo. Neutrum autem horum est nisi secundum gratiam gratum facientem. Est enim unus communis modus quo Deus est in omnibus rebus per essentiam, potentiam et praesentiam, sicut causa in effectibus participantibus bonitatem ipsius. Super istum modum autem communem, est unus specialis, qui convenit creaturae rationali, in qua Deus dicitur esse sicut cognitum in cognoscente et amatum in amante. Et quia, cognoscendo et amando, creatura rationalis sua operatione attingit ad ipsum Deum, secundum istum specialem modum Deus non solum dicitur esse in creatura rationali, sed etiam habitare in ea sicut in templo suo. Sic igitur nullus alius effectus potest esse ratio quod divina persona sit novo modo in rationali creatura, nisi gratia gratum faciens. Unde secundum solam gratiam gratum facientem, mittitur et procedit temporaliter persona divina. Similiter illud solum habere dicimur, quo libere possumus uti vel frui. Habere autem potestatem fruendi divina persona, est solum secundum gratiam gratum facientem. Sed tamen in ipso dono gratiae gratum facientis, spiritus sanctus habetur, et inhabitat hominem. Unde ipsemet spiritus sanctus datur et mittitur.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30232)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod per donum gratiae gratum facientis perficitur creatura rationalis, ad hoc quod libere non solum ipso dono creato utatur, sed ut ipsa divina persona fruatur. Et ideo missio invisibilis fit secundum donum gratiae gratum facientis, et tamen ipsa persona divina datur.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30233)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod gratia gratum faciens disponit animam ad habendam divinam personam, et significatur hoc, cum dicitur quod spiritus sanctus datur secundum donum gratiae. Sed tamen ipsum donum gratiae est a spiritu sancto, et hoc significatur, cum dicitur quod caritas Dei diffunditur in cordibus nostris per spiritum sanctum.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30234)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet per aliquos effectus filius cognosci possit a nobis, non tamen per aliquos effectus nos inhabitat, vel etiam habetur a nobis.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30235)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod operatio miraculorum est manifestativa gratiae gratum facientis, sicut et donum prophetiae, et quaelibet gratia gratis data. Unde I Cor. XII, gratia gratis data nominatur manifestatio spiritus. Sic igitur apostolis dicitur datus spiritus sanctus ad operationem miraculorum, quia data est eis gratia gratum faciens cum signo manifestante. Si autem daretur solum signum gratiae gratum facientis sine gratia, non diceretur dari simpliciter spiritus sanctus; nisi forte cum aliqua determinatione, secundum quod dicitur quod alicui datur spiritus propheticus vel miraculorum, inquantum a spiritu sancto habet virtutem prophetandi vel miracula faciendi.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30236)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod etiam patri conveniat mitti. Mitti enim divinam personam est ipsam dari. Sed pater dat seipsum, cum haberi non possit, nisi se ipso donante. Ergo potest dici quod pater mittat seipsum.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30237)\n\nPraeterea, persona divina mittitur secundum inhabitationem gratiae. Sed per gratiam tota Trinitas inhabitat in nobis, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *ad eum veniemus, et mansionem apud eum faciemus*. Ergo quaelibet divinarum personarum mittitur.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30238)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid convenit alicui personae, convenit omnibus, praeter notiones et personas. Sed missio non significat aliquam personam, neque etiam notionem, cum sint tantum quinque notiones, ut supra dictum est. Ergo cuilibet personae divinae convenit mitti.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30239)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in II libro de Trin., quod *solus pater nunquam legitur missus*.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30240)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod missio in sui ratione importat processionem ab alio; et in divinis, secundum originem, ut supra dictum est. Unde, cum pater non sit ab alio, nullo modo convenit sibi mitti; sed solum filio et spiritui sancto, quibus convenit esse ab alio.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30241)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod si dare importet liberalem communicationem alicuius, sic pater dat seipsum, inquantum se liberaliter communicat creaturae ad fruendum. Si vero importet auctoritatem dantis respectu eius quod datur, sic non convenit dari in divinis nisi personae quae est ab alio; sicut nec mitti.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30242)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet effectus gratiae sit etiam a patre, qui inhabitat per gratiam, sicut et filius et spiritus sanctus; quia tamen non est ab alio, non dicitur mitti. Et hoc est quod dicit Augustinus, IV de Trin., quod *pater, cum in tempore a quoquam cognoscitur, non dicitur missus, non enim habet de quo sit, aut ex quo procedat*.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30243)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod missio, inquantum importat processionem a mittente, includit in sui significatione notionem, non quidem in speciali, sed in generali, prout esse ab alio est commune duabus notionibus.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30244)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filio non conveniat invisibiliter mitti. Missio enim invisibilis divinae personae attenditur secundum dona gratiae. Sed omnia dona gratiae pertinent ad spiritum sanctum, secundum illud I Cor. XII, *omnia operatur unus atque idem spiritus*. Ergo invisibiliter non mittitur nisi spiritus sanctus.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30245)\n\nPraeterea, missio divinae personae fit secundum gratiam gratum facientem. Sed dona quae pertinent ad perfectionem intellectus, non sunt dona gratiae gratum facientis, cum sine caritate possint haberi, secundum illud I ad Cor. XIII, *si habuero prophetiam, et noverim mysteria omnia, et omnem scientiam, et si habuero omnem fidem, ita ut montes transferam, caritatem autem non habeam, nihil sum*. Cum ergo filius procedat ut verbum intellectus, videtur quod non conveniat sibi invisibiliter mitti.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30246)\n\nPraeterea, missio divinae personae est quaedam processio, ut dictum est. Sed alia est processio filii, alia spiritus sancti. Ergo et alia missio, si uterque mittitur. Et sic altera earum superflueret, cum una sit sufficiens ad sanctificandam creaturam.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30247)\n\nSed contra est quod Sap. IX dicitur de divina sapientia, *mitte illam de caelis sanctis tuis, et a sede magnitudinis tuae*.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30248)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod per gratiam gratum facientem tota Trinitas inhabitat mentem, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *ad eum veniemus, et mansionem apud eum faciemus*. Mitti autem personam divinam ad aliquem per invisibilem gratiam, significat novum modum inhabitandi illius personae, et originem eius ab alia. Unde, cum tam filio quam spiritui sancto conveniat et inhabitare per gratiam et ab alio esse, utrique convenit invisibiliter mitti. Patri autem licet conveniat inhabitare per gratiam, non tamen sibi convenit ab alio esse; et per consequens nec mitti.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30249)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet omnia dona, inquantum dona sunt, attribuantur spiritui sancto, quia habet rationem primi doni, secundum quod est amor, ut supra dictum est; aliqua tamen dona, secundum proprias rationes, attribuuntur per quandam appropriationem filio, scilicet illa quae pertinent ad intellectum et secundum illa dona attenditur missio filii. Unde Augustinus dicit, IV de Trin., quod *tunc invisibiliter filius cuiquam mittitur, cum a quoquam cognoscitur atque percipitur*.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30250)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod anima per gratiam conformatur Deo. Unde ad hoc quod aliqua persona divina mittatur ad aliquem per gratiam, oportet quod fiat assimilatio illius ad divinam personam quae mittitur per aliquod gratiae donum. Et quia spiritus sanctus est amor, per donum caritatis anima spiritui sancto assimilatur, unde secundum donum caritatis attenditur missio spiritus sancti. Filius autem est verbum, non qualecumque, sed spirans amorem, unde Augustinus dicit, in IX libro de Trin., *verbum quod insinuare intendimus, cum amore notitia est*. Non igitur secundum quamlibet perfectionem intellectus mittitur filius, sed secundum talem instructionem intellectus, qua prorumpat in affectum amoris, ut dicitur Ioan. VI, *omnis qui audivit a patre, et didicit, venit ad me*; et in Psalm., *in meditatione mea exardescet ignis*. Et ideo signanter dicit Augustinus quod filius mittitur, cum a quoquam cognoscitur atque percipitur, perceptio enim experimentalem quandam notitiam significat. Et haec proprie dicitur sapientia, quasi sapida scientia, secundum illud Eccli. VI, *sapientia doctrinae secundum nomen eius est*.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30251)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum missio importet originem personae missae et inhabitationem per gratiam, ut supra dictum est, si loquamur de missione quantum ad originem, sic missio filii distinguitur a missione spiritus sancti, sicut et generatio a processione. Si autem quantum ad effectum gratiae, sic communicant duae missiones in radice gratiae, sed distinguuntur in effectibus gratiae, qui sunt illuminatio intellectus, et inflammatio affectus. Et sic manifestum est quod una non potest esse sine alia, quia neutra est sine gratia gratum faciente, nec una persona separatur ab alia.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30252)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod missio invisibilis non fiat ad omnes qui sunt participes gratiae. Patres enim veteris testamenti gratiae participes fuerunt. Sed ad illos non videtur fuisse facta missio invisibilis, dicitur enim Ioan. VII, *nondum erat spiritus datus, quia nondum erat Iesus glorificatus*. Ergo missio invisibilis non fit ad omnes qui sunt participes gratiae.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30253)\n\nPraeterea, profectus in virtute non est nisi per gratiam. Sed missio invisibilis non videtur attendi secundum profectum virtutis, quia profectus virtutis videtur esse continuus, cum caritas semper aut proficiat aut deficiat; et sic missio esset continua. Ergo missio invisibilis non fit ad omnes participes gratiae.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30254)\n\nPraeterea, Christus et beati plenissime habent gratiam. Sed ad eos non videtur fieri missio, quia missio fit ad aliquid distans; Christus autem, secundum quod homo, et omnes beati perfecte sunt uniti Deo. Non ergo ad omnes participes gratiae fit missio invisibilis.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30255)\n\nPraeterea, sacramenta novae legis continent gratiam. Nec tamen ad ea dicitur fieri missio invisibilis. Non ergo ad omnia quae habent gratiam, fit missio invisibilis.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30256)\n\nSed contra est quod, secundum Augustinum, missio invisibilis fit ad sanctificandam creaturam. Omnis autem creatura habens gratiam sanctificatur. Ergo ad omnem creaturam huiusmodi fit missio invisibilis.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30257)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, missio de sui ratione importat quod ille qui mittitur vel incipiat esse ubi prius non fuit, sicut accidit in rebus creatis; vel incipiat esse ubi prius fuit, sed quodam modo novo, secundum quod missio attribuitur divinis personis. Sic ergo in eo ad quem fit missio, oportet duo considerare, scilicet inhabitationem gratiae, et innovationem quandam per gratiam. Ad omnes ergo fit missio invisibilis, in quibus haec duo inveniuntur.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30258)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod missio invisibilis est facta ad patres veteris testamenti. Unde dicit Augustinus, IV de Trin., quod secundum quod filius mittitur invisibiliter, *fit in hominibus aut cum hominibus; hoc autem antea factum est in patribus et prophetis*. Quod ergo dicitur, nondum erat datus spiritus, intelligitur de illa datione cum signo visibili, quae facta est in die Pentecostes.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30259)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod etiam secundum profectum virtutis, aut augmentum gratiae, fit missio invisibilis. Unde Augustinus dicit, IV de Trin., quod *tunc cuiquam mittitur filius, cum a quoquam cognoscitur atque percipitur, quantum cognosci et percipi potest pro captu vel proficientis in Deum, vel perfectae in Deo animae rationalis*. Sed tamen secundum illud augmentum gratiae praecipue missio invisibilis attenditur, quando aliquis proficit in aliquem novum actum, vel novum statum gratiae, ut puta cum aliquis proficit in gratiam miraculorum aut prophetiae, vel in hoc quod ex fervore caritatis exponit se martyrio, aut abrenuntiat his quae possidet, aut quodcumque opus arduum aggreditur.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30260)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ad beatos est facta missio invisibilis in ipso principio beatitudinis. Postmodum autem ad eos fit missio invisibilis, non secundum intensionem gratiae, sed secundum quod aliqua mysteria eis revelantur de novo, quod est usque ad diem iudicii. Quod quidem augmentum attenditur secundum extensionem gratiae ad plura se extendentis. Ad Christum autem fuit facta invisibilis missio in principio suae conceptionis, non autem postea, cum a principio suae conceptionis fuerit plenus omni sapientia et gratia.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30261)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod gratia est in sacramentis novae legis instrumentaliter, sicut forma artificiati est in instrumentis artis, secundum quendam decursum ab agente in patiens. Missio autem non dicitur fieri nisi respectu termini. Unde missio divinae personae non fit ad sacramenta, sed ad eos qui per sacramenta gratiam suscipiunt.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30262)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod spiritui sancto non conveniat visibiliter mitti. Filius enim, secundum quod visibiliter missus est in mundum, dicitur esse minor patre. Sed nunquam legitur spiritus sanctus minor patre. Ergo spiritui sancto non convenit visibiliter mitti.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30263)\n\nPraeterea, missio visibilis attenditur secundum aliquam creaturam visibilem assumptam, sicut missio filii secundum carnem. Sed spiritus sanctus non assumpsit aliquam creaturam visibilem. Unde non potest dici quod in aliquibus creaturis visibilibus sit alio modo quam in aliis, nisi forte sicut in signo; sicut est etiam in sacramentis, et in omnibus figuris legalibus. Non ergo spiritus sanctus visibiliter mittitur, vel oportet dicere quod secundum omnia huiusmodi, eius missio visibilis attenditur.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30264)\n\nPraeterea, quaelibet creatura visibilis est effectus demonstrans totam Trinitatem. Non ergo per illas creaturas visibiles magis mittitur spiritus sanctus quam alia persona.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30265)\n\nPraeterea, filius visibiliter est missus secundum dignissimam visibilium creaturarum, scilicet secundum naturam humanam. Si igitur spiritus sanctus visibiliter mittitur, debuit mitti secundum aliquas creaturas rationales.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30266)\n\nPraeterea, quae visibiliter fiunt divinitus, dispensantur per ministerium Angelorum, ut Augustinus dicit, III de Trin. Si ergo aliquae species visibiles apparuerunt, hoc factum fuit per Angelos. Et sic ipsi Angeli mittuntur, et non spiritus sanctus.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30267)\n\nPraeterea, si spiritus sanctus visibiliter mittatur, hoc non est nisi ad manifestandum invisibilem missionem, quia invisibilia per visibilia manifestantur. Ergo ad quem missio invisibilis facta non fuit, nec missio visibilis fieri debuit, et ad omnes ad quos fit missio invisibilis, sive in novo sive in veteri testamento, missio visibilis fieri debet, quod patet esse falsum. Non ergo spiritus sanctus visibiliter mittitur.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30268)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Matth. III, quod spiritus sanctus descendit super dominum baptizatum in specie columbae.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30269)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus providet omnibus secundum uniuscuiusque modum. Est autem modus connaturalis hominis, ut per visibilia ad invisibilia manuducatur, ut ex supra dictis patet, et ideo invisibilia Dei oportuit homini per visibilia manifestari. Sicut igitur seipsum Deus, et processiones aeternas personarum, per creaturas visibiles, secundum aliqua indicia, hominibus quodammodo demonstravit; ita conveniens fuit ut etiam invisibiles missiones divinarum personarum secundum aliquas visibiles creaturas manifestarentur. Aliter tamen filius et spiritus sanctus. Nam spiritui sancto, inquantum procedit ut amor, competit esse sanctificationis donum, filio autem, inquantum est spiritus sancti principium, competit esse sanctificationis huius auctorem. Et ideo filius visibiliter missus est tanquam sanctificationis auctor, sed spiritus sanctus tanquam sanctificationis indicium.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30270)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod filius creaturam visibilem in qua apparuit, in unitatem personae assumpsit, sic ut quod de illa creatura dicitur, de filio Dei dici possit. Et sic, ratione naturae assumptae, filius dicitur minor patre. Sed spiritus sanctus non assumpsit creaturam visibilem in qua apparuit, in unitatem personae, ut quod illi convenit, de illo praedicetur. Unde non potest dici minor patre propter visibilem creaturam.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30271)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod missio visibilis spiritus sancti non attenditur secundum visionem imaginariam, quae est visio prophetica. Quia, ut Augustinus dicit, II de Trin., *visio prophetica non est exhibita corporeis oculis per formas corporeas, sed in spiritu per spirituales corporum imagines, columbam vero illam et ignem oculis viderunt quicumque viderunt. Neque iterum sic se habuit spiritus sanctus ad huiusmodi species, sicut filius ad petram (quia dicitur, petra erat Christus). Illa enim petra iam erat in creatura, et per actionis modum nuncupata est nomine Christi, quem significabat, sed illa columba et ignis ad haec tantum significanda repente extiterunt. Sed videntur esse similia flammae illi quae in rubo apparuit Moysi, et illi columnae quam populus in eremo sequebatur, et fulgoribus ac tonitruis quae fiebant cum lex daretur in monte, ad hoc enim rerum illarum corporalis extitit species, ut aliquid significaret, atque praeteriret*. Sic igitur patet quod missio visibilis neque attenditur secundum visiones propheticas, quae fuerunt imaginariae, et non corporales, neque secundum signa sacramentalia veteris et novi testamenti, in quibus quaedam res praeexistentes assumuntur ad aliquid significandum. Sed spiritus sanctus visibiliter dicitur esse missus, inquantum fuit monstratus in quibusdam creaturis, sicut in signis, ad hoc specialiter factis.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30272)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet illas creaturas visibiles tota Trinitas operata sit, tamen factae sunt ad demonstrandum specialiter hanc vel illam personam. Sicut enim diversis nominibus significantur pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, ita etiam diversis rebus significari potuerunt; quamvis inter eos nulla sit separatio aut diversitas.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30273)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod personam filii declarari oportuit ut sanctificationis auctorem, ut dictum est, et ideo oportuit quod missio visibilis filii fieret secundum naturam rationalem, cuius est agere, et cui potest competere sanctificare. Indicium autem sanctificationis esse potuit quaecumque alia creatura. Neque oportuit quod creatura visibilis ad hoc formata, esset assumpta a spiritu sancto in unitatem personae, cum non assumeretur ad aliquid agendum, sed ad indicandum tantum. Et propter hoc etiam non oportuit quod duraret, nisi quandiu perageret officium suum.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30274)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod illae creaturae visibiles formatae sunt ministerio Angelorum, non tamen ad significandum personam Angeli, sed ad significandam personam spiritus sancti. Quia igitur spiritus sanctus erat in illis creaturis visibilibus sicut signatum in signo, propter hoc secundum eas spiritus sanctus visibiliter mitti dicitur, et non Angelus.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30275)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod non est de necessitate invisibilis missionis, ut semper manifestetur per aliquod signum visibile exterius, sed, sicut dicitur I Cor. XII, *manifestatio spiritus datur alicui ad utilitatem*, scilicet Ecclesiae. Quae quidem utilitas est, ut per huiusmodi visibilia signa fides confirmetur et propagetur. Quod quidem principaliter factum est per Christum et per apostolos, secundum illud Hebr. II, *cum initium accepisset enarrari per dominum, ab eis qui audierunt in nos confirmata est*. Et ideo specialiter debuit fieri missio visibilis spiritus sancti ad Christum et ad apostolos, et ad aliquos primitivos sanctos, in quibus quodammodo Ecclesia fundabatur, ita tamen quod visibilis missio facta ad Christum, demonstraret missionem invisibilem non tunc, sed in principio suae conceptionis, ad eum factam. Facta autem est missio visibilis ad Christum, in Baptismo quidem sub specie columbae, quod est animal fecundum, ad ostendendum in Christo auctoritatem donandi gratiam per spiritualem regenerationem, unde vox patris intonuit, *hic est filius meus dilectus*, ut ad similitudinem unigeniti alii regenerarentur. In transfiguratione vero, sub specie nubis lucidae, ad ostendendam exuberantiam doctrinae, unde dictum est, *ipsum audite*. Ad apostolos autem, sub specie flatus, ad ostendendam potestatem ministerii in dispensatione sacramentorum, unde dictum est eis, *quorum remiseritis peccata, remittuntur eis*. Sed sub linguis igneis, ad ostendendum officium doctrinae, unde dicitur quod *coeperunt loqui variis linguis*. Ad patres autem veteris testamenti, missio visibilis spiritus sancti fieri non debuit quia prius debuit perfici missio visibilis filii quam spiritus sancti, cum spiritus sanctus manifestet filium, sicut filius patrem. Fuerunt tamen factae visibiles apparitiones divinarum personarum patribus veteris testamenti. Quae quidem missiones visibiles dici non possunt, quia non fuerunt factae, secundum Augustinum, ad designandum inhabitationem divinae personae per gratiam, sed ad aliquid aliud manifestandum.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30276)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nulla persona divina mittatur nisi ab ea a qua procedit aeternaliter. Quia, sicut dicit Augustinus, IV de Trin., *pater a nullo mittitur, quia a nullo est*. Si ergo aliqua persona divina mittitur ab alia, oportet quod sit ab illa.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30277)\n\nPraeterea, mittens habet auctoritatem respectu missi. Sed respectu divinae personae non potest haberi auctoritas nisi secundum originem. Ergo oportet quod divina persona quae mittitur, sit a persona mittente.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30278)\n\nPraeterea, si persona divina potest mitti ab eo a quo non est, nihil prohibebit dicere quod spiritus sanctus detur ab homine, quamvis non sit ab eo. Quod est contra Augustinum, XV de Trin. Ergo divina persona non mittitur nisi ab ea a qua est.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30279)\n\nSed contra est quod filius mittitur a spiritu sancto, secundum illud Isaiae XLVIII, *et nunc misit me dominus Deus, et spiritus eius*. Filius autem non est a spiritu sancto. Ergo persona divina mittitur ab ea a qua non est.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30280)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc inveniuntur aliqui diversimode locuti esse. Secundum quosdam enim, persona divina non mittitur nisi ab eo a quo est aeternaliter. Et secundum hoc, cum dicitur filius Dei missus a spiritu sancto, referendum est hoc ad humanam naturam, secundum quam missus est ad praedicandum a spiritu sancto. Augustinus autem dicit, II de Trin., quod filius mittitur et a se et a spiritu sancto; et spiritus sanctus etiam mittitur et a se et a filio, ut sic mitti in divinis non conveniat cuilibet personae, sed solum personae ab alio existenti; mittere autem conveniat cuilibet personae. Utrumque autem habet aliquo modo veritatem. Quia cum dicitur aliqua persona mitti, designatur et ipsa persona ab alio existens, et effectus visibilis aut invisibilis, secundum quem missio divinae personae attenditur. Si igitur mittens designetur ut principium personae quae mittitur, sic non quaelibet persona mittit, sed solum illa cui convenit esse principium illius personae. Et sic filius mittitur tantum a patre, spiritus sanctus autem a patre et filio. Si vero persona mittens intelligatur esse principium effectus secundum quem attenditur missio, sic tota Trinitas mittit personam missam. Non autem propter hoc homo dat spiritum sanctum, quia nec effectum gratiae potest causare.\n\n[I q. 43 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30281)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0002","text":"[I q. 43 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum de missione divinarum personarum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum alicui divinae personae conveniat mitti. Secundo, utrum missio sit aeterna, vel temporalis tantum. Tertio, secundum quid divina persona invisibiliter mittatur. Quarto, utrum cuilibet personae conveniat mitti. Quinto, utrum invisibiliter mittatur tam filius, quam spiritus sanctus. Sexto, ad quos fiat missio invisibilis. Septimo, de missione visibili. Octavo, utrum aliqua persona mittat seipsam, visibiliter aut invisibiliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0005","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod personae divinae non conveniat mitti. Missus enim minor est mittente. Sed una persona divina non est minor alia. Ergo una persona non mittitur ab alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0007","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0008","text":"Praeterea, omne quod mittitur, separatur a mittente, unde Hieronymus dicit, super Ezechiel., *quod coniunctum est, et in corpore uno copulatum, mitti non potest*. Sed in divinis personis nihil est separabile, ut Hilarius dicit. Ergo una persona non mittitur ab alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0009","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quicumque mittitur, ab aliquo loco discedit, et ad aliquem locum de novo vadit. Hoc autem divinae personae non convenit, cum ubique sit. Ergo divinae personae non convenit mitti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0011","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. VIII, *non sum ego solus, sed ego et qui misit me, pater*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0013","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in ratione missionis duo importantur, quorum unum est habitudo missi ad eum a quo mittitur; aliud est habitudo missi ad terminum ad quem mittitur. Per hoc autem quod aliquis mittitur, ostenditur processio quaedam missi a mittente; vel secundum imperium, sicut dominus mittit servum; vel secundum consilium, ut si consiliarius mittere dicatur regem ad bellandum; vel secundum originem, ut si dicatur quod flos emittitur ab arbore. Ostenditur etiam habitudo ad terminum ad quem mittitur, ut aliquo modo ibi esse incipiat; vel quia prius ibi omnino non erat quo mittitur; vel quia incipit ibi aliquo modo esse, quo prius non erat. Missio igitur divinae personae convenire potest, secundum quod importat ex una parte processionem originis a mittente; et secundum quod importat ex alia parte novum modum existendi in aliquo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0015","text":"Sicut filius dicitur esse missus a patre in mundum, secundum quod incoepit esse in mundo visibiliter per carnem assumptam, et tamen ante in mundo erat, ut dicitur Ioan. I.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0016","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod missio importat minorationem in eo qui mittitur, secundum quod importat processionem a principio mittente aut secundum imperium, aut secundum consilium, quia imperans est maior, et consilians est sapientior. Sed in divinis non importat nisi processionem originis; quae est secundum aequalitatem, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0018","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illud quod sic mittitur ut incipiat esse ubi prius nullo modo erat, sua missione localiter movetur, unde oportet quod loco separetur a mittente. Sed hoc non accidit in missione divinae personae, quia persona divina missa, sicut non incipit esse ubi prius non fuerat, ita nec desinit esse ubi fuerat. Unde talis missio est sine separatione; sed habet solam distinctionem originis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0020","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de missione quae fit secundum motum localem; quae non habet locum in divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0023","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod missio possit esse aeterna. Dicit enim Gregorius, eo mittitur filius, quo generatur. Sed generatio filii est aeterna. Ergo et missio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0025","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0026","text":"Praeterea, cuicumque convenit aliquid temporaliter, illud mutatur. Sed persona divina non mutatur. Ergo missio divinae personae non est temporalis, sed aeterna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0027","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0028","text":"Praeterea, missio processionem importat. Sed processio divinarum personarum est aeterna. Ergo et missio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0029","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Galat. IV, *cum venit plenitudo temporis, misit Deus filium suum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0031","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in his quae important originem divinarum personarum, est quaedam differentia attendenda. Quaedam enim in sui significatione important solam habitudinem ad principium, ut processio et exitus. Quaedam vero, cum habitudine ad principium, determinant processionis terminum. Quorum quaedam determinant terminum aeternum, sicut generatio et spiratio, nam generatio est processio divinae personae in naturam divinam; et spiratio, passive accepta, importat processionem amoris subsistentis. Quaedam vero, cum habitudine ad principium, important terminum temporalem, sicut missio et datio, mittitur enim aliquid ad hoc ut sit in aliquo, et datur ad hoc quod habeatur; personam autem divinam haberi ab aliqua creatura, vel esse novo modo existendi in ea, est quoddam temporale. Unde missio et datio in divinis dicuntur temporaliter tantum. Generatio autem et spiratio solum ab aeterno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0033","text":"Processio autem et exitus dicuntur in divinis et aeternaliter et temporaliter, nam filius ab aeterno processit ut sit Deus; temporaliter autem ut etiam sit homo, secundum missionem visibilem; vel etiam ut sit in homine, secundum invisibilem missionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0034","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Gregorius loquitur de generatione temporali filii, non a patre, sed a matre. Vel, quia ex hoc ipso filius habet quod possit mitti, quod est ab aeterno genitus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0036","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod divinam personam esse novo modo in aliquo, vel ab aliquo haberi temporaliter, non est propter mutationem divinae personae, sed propter mutationem creaturae, sicut et Deus temporaliter dicitur dominus, propter mutationem creaturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0038","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod missio non solum importat processionem a principio, sed determinat processionis terminum temporalem. Unde missio solum est temporalis. Vel, missio includit processionem aeternam, et aliquid addit, scilicet temporalem effectum, habitudo enim divinae personae ad suum principium non est nisi ab aeterno. Unde gemina dicitur processio, aeterna scilicet et temporalis, non propter hoc quod habitudo ad principium geminetur, sed geminatio est ex parte termini temporalis et aeterni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0041","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod missio invisibilis divinae personae non sit solum secundum donum gratiae gratum facientis. Divinam enim personam mitti, est ipsam donari. Si igitur divina persona mittitur solum secundum dona gratiae gratum facientis, non donabitur ipsa persona divina, sed solum dona eius. Quod est error dicentium spiritum sanctum non dari, sed eius dona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0043","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0044","text":"Praeterea, haec praepositio secundum denotat habitudinem alicuius causae. Sed persona divina est causa quod habeatur donum gratiae gratum facientis, et non e converso; secundum illud Rom. V, *caritas Dei diffusa est in cordibus nostris per spiritum sanctum, qui datus est nobis*. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur quod persona divina secundum dona gratiae gratum facientis mittatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0045","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0046","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, IV de Trin., quod *filius, cum ex tempore mente percipitur, mitti dicitur*. Sed filius cognoscitur non solum per gratiam gratum facientem, sed etiam per gratiam gratis datam, sicut per fidem et per scientiam. Non ergo persona divina mittitur secundum solam gratiam gratum facientem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0047","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0048","text":"Praeterea, Rabanus dicit quod spiritus sanctus datus est apostolis ad operationem miraculorum. Hoc autem non est donum gratiae gratum facientis, sed gratiae gratis datae. Ergo persona divina non solum datur secundum gratiam gratum facientem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0049","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0050","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XV de Trin., quod spiritus sanctus procedit temporaliter ad sanctificandam creaturam. Missio autem est temporalis processio. Cum igitur sanctificatio creaturae non sit nisi per gratiam gratum facientem, sequitur quod missio divinae personae non sit nisi per gratiam gratum facientem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0051","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0052","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod divinae personae convenit mitti, secundum quod novo modo existit in aliquo; dari autem, secundum quod habetur ab aliquo. Neutrum autem horum est nisi secundum gratiam gratum facientem. Est enim unus communis modus quo Deus est in omnibus rebus per essentiam, potentiam et praesentiam, sicut causa in effectibus participantibus bonitatem ipsius. Super istum modum autem communem, est unus specialis, qui convenit creaturae rationali, in qua Deus dicitur esse sicut cognitum in cognoscente et amatum in amante. Et quia, cognoscendo et amando, creatura rationalis sua operatione attingit ad ipsum Deum, secundum istum specialem modum Deus non solum dicitur esse in creatura rationali, sed etiam habitare in ea sicut in templo suo. Sic igitur nullus alius effectus potest esse ratio quod divina persona sit novo modo in rationali creatura, nisi gratia gratum faciens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0053","text":"Unde secundum solam gratiam gratum facientem, mittitur et procedit temporaliter persona divina. Similiter illud solum habere dicimur, quo libere possumus uti vel frui. Habere autem potestatem fruendi divina persona, est solum secundum gratiam gratum facientem. Sed tamen in ipso dono gratiae gratum facientis, spiritus sanctus habetur, et inhabitat hominem. Unde ipsemet spiritus sanctus datur et mittitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0054","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0055","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod per donum gratiae gratum facientis perficitur creatura rationalis, ad hoc quod libere non solum ipso dono creato utatur, sed ut ipsa divina persona fruatur. Et ideo missio invisibilis fit secundum donum gratiae gratum facientis, et tamen ipsa persona divina datur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0056","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0057","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod gratia gratum faciens disponit animam ad habendam divinam personam, et significatur hoc, cum dicitur quod spiritus sanctus datur secundum donum gratiae. Sed tamen ipsum donum gratiae est a spiritu sancto, et hoc significatur, cum dicitur quod caritas Dei diffunditur in cordibus nostris per spiritum sanctum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0058","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0059","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet per aliquos effectus filius cognosci possit a nobis, non tamen per aliquos effectus nos inhabitat, vel etiam habetur a nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0060","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0061","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod operatio miraculorum est manifestativa gratiae gratum facientis, sicut et donum prophetiae, et quaelibet gratia gratis data. Unde I Cor. XII, gratia gratis data nominatur manifestatio spiritus. Sic igitur apostolis dicitur datus spiritus sanctus ad operationem miraculorum, quia data est eis gratia gratum faciens cum signo manifestante. Si autem daretur solum signum gratiae gratum facientis sine gratia, non diceretur dari simpliciter spiritus sanctus; nisi forte cum aliqua determinatione, secundum quod dicitur quod alicui datur spiritus propheticus vel miraculorum, inquantum a spiritu sancto habet virtutem prophetandi vel miracula faciendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0062","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0063","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0064","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod etiam patri conveniat mitti. Mitti enim divinam personam est ipsam dari. Sed pater dat seipsum, cum haberi non possit, nisi se ipso donante. Ergo potest dici quod pater mittat seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0065","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0066","text":"Praeterea, persona divina mittitur secundum inhabitationem gratiae. Sed per gratiam tota Trinitas inhabitat in nobis, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *ad eum veniemus, et mansionem apud eum faciemus*. Ergo quaelibet divinarum personarum mittitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0067","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0068","text":"Praeterea, quidquid convenit alicui personae, convenit omnibus, praeter notiones et personas. Sed missio non significat aliquam personam, neque etiam notionem, cum sint tantum quinque notiones, ut supra dictum est. Ergo cuilibet personae divinae convenit mitti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0069","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0070","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in II libro de Trin., quod *solus pater nunquam legitur missus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0071","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0072","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod missio in sui ratione importat processionem ab alio; et in divinis, secundum originem, ut supra dictum est. Unde, cum pater non sit ab alio, nullo modo convenit sibi mitti; sed solum filio et spiritui sancto, quibus convenit esse ab alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0073","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0074","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod si dare importet liberalem communicationem alicuius, sic pater dat seipsum, inquantum se liberaliter communicat creaturae ad fruendum. Si vero importet auctoritatem dantis respectu eius quod datur, sic non convenit dari in divinis nisi personae quae est ab alio; sicut nec mitti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0075","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0076","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet effectus gratiae sit etiam a patre, qui inhabitat per gratiam, sicut et filius et spiritus sanctus; quia tamen non est ab alio, non dicitur mitti. Et hoc est quod dicit Augustinus, IV de Trin., quod *pater, cum in tempore a quoquam cognoscitur, non dicitur missus, non enim habet de quo sit, aut ex quo procedat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0077","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0078","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod missio, inquantum importat processionem a mittente, includit in sui significatione notionem, non quidem in speciali, sed in generali, prout esse ab alio est commune duabus notionibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0079","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0080","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0081","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod filio non conveniat invisibiliter mitti. Missio enim invisibilis divinae personae attenditur secundum dona gratiae. Sed omnia dona gratiae pertinent ad spiritum sanctum, secundum illud I Cor. XII, *omnia operatur unus atque idem spiritus*. Ergo invisibiliter non mittitur nisi spiritus sanctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0082","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0083","text":"Praeterea, missio divinae personae fit secundum gratiam gratum facientem. Sed dona quae pertinent ad perfectionem intellectus, non sunt dona gratiae gratum facientis, cum sine caritate possint haberi, secundum illud I ad Cor. XIII, *si habuero prophetiam, et noverim mysteria omnia, et omnem scientiam, et si habuero omnem fidem, ita ut montes transferam, caritatem autem non habeam, nihil sum*. Cum ergo filius procedat ut verbum intellectus, videtur quod non conveniat sibi invisibiliter mitti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0084","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0085","text":"Praeterea, missio divinae personae est quaedam processio, ut dictum est. Sed alia est processio filii, alia spiritus sancti. Ergo et alia missio, si uterque mittitur. Et sic altera earum superflueret, cum una sit sufficiens ad sanctificandam creaturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0086","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0087","text":"Sed contra est quod Sap. IX dicitur de divina sapientia, *mitte illam de caelis sanctis tuis, et a sede magnitudinis tuae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0088","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0089","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod per gratiam gratum facientem tota Trinitas inhabitat mentem, secundum illud Ioan. XIV, *ad eum veniemus, et mansionem apud eum faciemus*. Mitti autem personam divinam ad aliquem per invisibilem gratiam, significat novum modum inhabitandi illius personae, et originem eius ab alia. Unde, cum tam filio quam spiritui sancto conveniat et inhabitare per gratiam et ab alio esse, utrique convenit invisibiliter mitti. Patri autem licet conveniat inhabitare per gratiam, non tamen sibi convenit ab alio esse; et per consequens nec mitti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0090","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0091","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet omnia dona, inquantum dona sunt, attribuantur spiritui sancto, quia habet rationem primi doni, secundum quod est amor, ut supra dictum est; aliqua tamen dona, secundum proprias rationes, attribuuntur per quandam appropriationem filio, scilicet illa quae pertinent ad intellectum et secundum illa dona attenditur missio filii. Unde Augustinus dicit, IV de Trin., quod *tunc invisibiliter filius cuiquam mittitur, cum a quoquam cognoscitur atque percipitur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0092","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0093","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod anima per gratiam conformatur Deo. Unde ad hoc quod aliqua persona divina mittatur ad aliquem per gratiam, oportet quod fiat assimilatio illius ad divinam personam quae mittitur per aliquod gratiae donum. Et quia spiritus sanctus est amor, per donum caritatis anima spiritui sancto assimilatur, unde secundum donum caritatis attenditur missio spiritus sancti. Filius autem est verbum, non qualecumque, sed spirans amorem, unde Augustinus dicit, in IX libro de Trin., *verbum quod insinuare intendimus, cum amore notitia est*. Non igitur secundum quamlibet perfectionem intellectus mittitur filius, sed secundum talem instructionem intellectus, qua prorumpat in affectum amoris, ut dicitur Ioan. VI, *omnis qui audivit a patre, et didicit, venit ad me*; et in Psalm., *in meditatione mea exardescet ignis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0094","text":"Et ideo signanter dicit Augustinus quod filius mittitur, cum a quoquam cognoscitur atque percipitur, perceptio enim experimentalem quandam notitiam significat. Et haec proprie dicitur sapientia, quasi sapida scientia, secundum illud Eccli. VI, *sapientia doctrinae secundum nomen eius est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0095","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0096","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum missio importet originem personae missae et inhabitationem per gratiam, ut supra dictum est, si loquamur de missione quantum ad originem, sic missio filii distinguitur a missione spiritus sancti, sicut et generatio a processione. Si autem quantum ad effectum gratiae, sic communicant duae missiones in radice gratiae, sed distinguuntur in effectibus gratiae, qui sunt illuminatio intellectus, et inflammatio affectus. Et sic manifestum est quod una non potest esse sine alia, quia neutra est sine gratia gratum faciente, nec una persona separatur ab alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0097","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0098","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0099","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod missio invisibilis non fiat ad omnes qui sunt participes gratiae. Patres enim veteris testamenti gratiae participes fuerunt. Sed ad illos non videtur fuisse facta missio invisibilis, dicitur enim Ioan. VII, *nondum erat spiritus datus, quia nondum erat Iesus glorificatus*. Ergo missio invisibilis non fit ad omnes qui sunt participes gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0100","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0101","text":"Praeterea, profectus in virtute non est nisi per gratiam. Sed missio invisibilis non videtur attendi secundum profectum virtutis, quia profectus virtutis videtur esse continuus, cum caritas semper aut proficiat aut deficiat; et sic missio esset continua. Ergo missio invisibilis non fit ad omnes participes gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0102","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0103","text":"Praeterea, Christus et beati plenissime habent gratiam. Sed ad eos non videtur fieri missio, quia missio fit ad aliquid distans; Christus autem, secundum quod homo, et omnes beati perfecte sunt uniti Deo. Non ergo ad omnes participes gratiae fit missio invisibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0104","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0105","text":"Praeterea, sacramenta novae legis continent gratiam. Nec tamen ad ea dicitur fieri missio invisibilis. Non ergo ad omnia quae habent gratiam, fit missio invisibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0106","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0107","text":"Sed contra est quod, secundum Augustinum, missio invisibilis fit ad sanctificandam creaturam. Omnis autem creatura habens gratiam sanctificatur. Ergo ad omnem creaturam huiusmodi fit missio invisibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0108","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0109","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, missio de sui ratione importat quod ille qui mittitur vel incipiat esse ubi prius non fuit, sicut accidit in rebus creatis; vel incipiat esse ubi prius fuit, sed quodam modo novo, secundum quod missio attribuitur divinis personis. Sic ergo in eo ad quem fit missio, oportet duo considerare, scilicet inhabitationem gratiae, et innovationem quandam per gratiam. Ad omnes ergo fit missio invisibilis, in quibus haec duo inveniuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0110","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0111","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod missio invisibilis est facta ad patres veteris testamenti. Unde dicit Augustinus, IV de Trin., quod secundum quod filius mittitur invisibiliter, *fit in hominibus aut cum hominibus; hoc autem antea factum est in patribus et prophetis*. Quod ergo dicitur, nondum erat datus spiritus, intelligitur de illa datione cum signo visibili, quae facta est in die Pentecostes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0112","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0113","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod etiam secundum profectum virtutis, aut augmentum gratiae, fit missio invisibilis. Unde Augustinus dicit, IV de Trin., quod *tunc cuiquam mittitur filius, cum a quoquam cognoscitur atque percipitur, quantum cognosci et percipi potest pro captu vel proficientis in Deum, vel perfectae in Deo animae rationalis*. Sed tamen secundum illud augmentum gratiae praecipue missio invisibilis attenditur, quando aliquis proficit in aliquem novum actum, vel novum statum gratiae, ut puta cum aliquis proficit in gratiam miraculorum aut prophetiae, vel in hoc quod ex fervore caritatis exponit se martyrio, aut abrenuntiat his quae possidet, aut quodcumque opus arduum aggreditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0114","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0115","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ad beatos est facta missio invisibilis in ipso principio beatitudinis. Postmodum autem ad eos fit missio invisibilis, non secundum intensionem gratiae, sed secundum quod aliqua mysteria eis revelantur de novo, quod est usque ad diem iudicii. Quod quidem augmentum attenditur secundum extensionem gratiae ad plura se extendentis. Ad Christum autem fuit facta invisibilis missio in principio suae conceptionis, non autem postea, cum a principio suae conceptionis fuerit plenus omni sapientia et gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0116","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0117","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod gratia est in sacramentis novae legis instrumentaliter, sicut forma artificiati est in instrumentis artis, secundum quendam decursum ab agente in patiens. Missio autem non dicitur fieri nisi respectu termini. Unde missio divinae personae non fit ad sacramenta, sed ad eos qui per sacramenta gratiam suscipiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0118","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0119","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0120","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod spiritui sancto non conveniat visibiliter mitti. Filius enim, secundum quod visibiliter missus est in mundum, dicitur esse minor patre. Sed nunquam legitur spiritus sanctus minor patre. Ergo spiritui sancto non convenit visibiliter mitti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0121","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0122","text":"Praeterea, missio visibilis attenditur secundum aliquam creaturam visibilem assumptam, sicut missio filii secundum carnem. Sed spiritus sanctus non assumpsit aliquam creaturam visibilem. Unde non potest dici quod in aliquibus creaturis visibilibus sit alio modo quam in aliis, nisi forte sicut in signo; sicut est etiam in sacramentis, et in omnibus figuris legalibus. Non ergo spiritus sanctus visibiliter mittitur, vel oportet dicere quod secundum omnia huiusmodi, eius missio visibilis attenditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0123","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0124","text":"Praeterea, quaelibet creatura visibilis est effectus demonstrans totam Trinitatem. Non ergo per illas creaturas visibiles magis mittitur spiritus sanctus quam alia persona.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0125","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0126","text":"Praeterea, filius visibiliter est missus secundum dignissimam visibilium creaturarum, scilicet secundum naturam humanam. Si igitur spiritus sanctus visibiliter mittitur, debuit mitti secundum aliquas creaturas rationales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0127","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0128","text":"Praeterea, quae visibiliter fiunt divinitus, dispensantur per ministerium Angelorum, ut Augustinus dicit, III de Trin. Si ergo aliquae species visibiles apparuerunt, hoc factum fuit per Angelos. Et sic ipsi Angeli mittuntur, et non spiritus sanctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0129","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0130","text":"Praeterea, si spiritus sanctus visibiliter mittatur, hoc non est nisi ad manifestandum invisibilem missionem, quia invisibilia per visibilia manifestantur. Ergo ad quem missio invisibilis facta non fuit, nec missio visibilis fieri debuit, et ad omnes ad quos fit missio invisibilis, sive in novo sive in veteri testamento, missio visibilis fieri debet, quod patet esse falsum. Non ergo spiritus sanctus visibiliter mittitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0131","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0132","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Matth. III, quod spiritus sanctus descendit super dominum baptizatum in specie columbae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0133","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0134","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus providet omnibus secundum uniuscuiusque modum. Est autem modus connaturalis hominis, ut per visibilia ad invisibilia manuducatur, ut ex supra dictis patet, et ideo invisibilia Dei oportuit homini per visibilia manifestari. Sicut igitur seipsum Deus, et processiones aeternas personarum, per creaturas visibiles, secundum aliqua indicia, hominibus quodammodo demonstravit; ita conveniens fuit ut etiam invisibiles missiones divinarum personarum secundum aliquas visibiles creaturas manifestarentur. Aliter tamen filius et spiritus sanctus. Nam spiritui sancto, inquantum procedit ut amor, competit esse sanctificationis donum, filio autem, inquantum est spiritus sancti principium, competit esse sanctificationis huius auctorem. Et ideo filius visibiliter missus est tanquam sanctificationis auctor, sed spiritus sanctus tanquam sanctificationis indicium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0135","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0136","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod filius creaturam visibilem in qua apparuit, in unitatem personae assumpsit, sic ut quod de illa creatura dicitur, de filio Dei dici possit. Et sic, ratione naturae assumptae, filius dicitur minor patre. Sed spiritus sanctus non assumpsit creaturam visibilem in qua apparuit, in unitatem personae, ut quod illi convenit, de illo praedicetur. Unde non potest dici minor patre propter visibilem creaturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0137","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0138","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod missio visibilis spiritus sancti non attenditur secundum visionem imaginariam, quae est visio prophetica. Quia, ut Augustinus dicit, II de Trin., *visio prophetica non est exhibita corporeis oculis per formas corporeas, sed in spiritu per spirituales corporum imagines, columbam vero illam et ignem oculis viderunt quicumque viderunt. Neque iterum sic se habuit spiritus sanctus ad huiusmodi species, sicut filius ad petram (quia dicitur, petra erat Christus). Illa enim petra iam erat in creatura, et per actionis modum nuncupata est nomine Christi, quem significabat, sed illa columba et ignis ad haec tantum significanda repente extiterunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0139","text":"Sed videntur esse similia flammae illi quae in rubo apparuit Moysi, et illi columnae quam populus in eremo sequebatur, et fulgoribus ac tonitruis quae fiebant cum lex daretur in monte, ad hoc enim rerum illarum corporalis extitit species, ut aliquid significaret, atque praeteriret*. Sic igitur patet quod missio visibilis neque attenditur secundum visiones propheticas, quae fuerunt imaginariae, et non corporales, neque secundum signa sacramentalia veteris et novi testamenti, in quibus quaedam res praeexistentes assumuntur ad aliquid significandum. Sed spiritus sanctus visibiliter dicitur esse missus, inquantum fuit monstratus in quibusdam creaturis, sicut in signis, ad hoc specialiter factis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0140","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0141","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet illas creaturas visibiles tota Trinitas operata sit, tamen factae sunt ad demonstrandum specialiter hanc vel illam personam. Sicut enim diversis nominibus significantur pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, ita etiam diversis rebus significari potuerunt; quamvis inter eos nulla sit separatio aut diversitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0142","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0143","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod personam filii declarari oportuit ut sanctificationis auctorem, ut dictum est, et ideo oportuit quod missio visibilis filii fieret secundum naturam rationalem, cuius est agere, et cui potest competere sanctificare. Indicium autem sanctificationis esse potuit quaecumque alia creatura. Neque oportuit quod creatura visibilis ad hoc formata, esset assumpta a spiritu sancto in unitatem personae, cum non assumeretur ad aliquid agendum, sed ad indicandum tantum. Et propter hoc etiam non oportuit quod duraret, nisi quandiu perageret officium suum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0144","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0145","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod illae creaturae visibiles formatae sunt ministerio Angelorum, non tamen ad significandum personam Angeli, sed ad significandam personam spiritus sancti. Quia igitur spiritus sanctus erat in illis creaturis visibilibus sicut signatum in signo, propter hoc secundum eas spiritus sanctus visibiliter mitti dicitur, et non Angelus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0146","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0147","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod non est de necessitate invisibilis missionis, ut semper manifestetur per aliquod signum visibile exterius, sed, sicut dicitur I Cor. XII, *manifestatio spiritus datur alicui ad utilitatem*, scilicet Ecclesiae. Quae quidem utilitas est, ut per huiusmodi visibilia signa fides confirmetur et propagetur. Quod quidem principaliter factum est per Christum et per apostolos, secundum illud Hebr. II, *cum initium accepisset enarrari per dominum, ab eis qui audierunt in nos confirmata est*. Et ideo specialiter debuit fieri missio visibilis spiritus sancti ad Christum et ad apostolos, et ad aliquos primitivos sanctos, in quibus quodammodo Ecclesia fundabatur, ita tamen quod visibilis missio facta ad Christum, demonstraret missionem invisibilem non tunc, sed in principio suae conceptionis, ad eum factam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0148","text":"Facta autem est missio visibilis ad Christum, in Baptismo quidem sub specie columbae, quod est animal fecundum, ad ostendendum in Christo auctoritatem donandi gratiam per spiritualem regenerationem, unde vox patris intonuit, *hic est filius meus dilectus*, ut ad similitudinem unigeniti alii regenerarentur. In transfiguratione vero, sub specie nubis lucidae, ad ostendendam exuberantiam doctrinae, unde dictum est, *ipsum audite*. Ad apostolos autem, sub specie flatus, ad ostendendam potestatem ministerii in dispensatione sacramentorum, unde dictum est eis, *quorum remiseritis peccata, remittuntur eis*. Sed sub linguis igneis, ad ostendendum officium doctrinae, unde dicitur quod *coeperunt loqui variis linguis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0149","text":"Ad patres autem veteris testamenti, missio visibilis spiritus sancti fieri non debuit quia prius debuit perfici missio visibilis filii quam spiritus sancti, cum spiritus sanctus manifestet filium, sicut filius patrem. Fuerunt tamen factae visibiles apparitiones divinarum personarum patribus veteris testamenti. Quae quidem missiones visibiles dici non possunt, quia non fuerunt factae, secundum Augustinum, ad designandum inhabitationem divinae personae per gratiam, sed ad aliquid aliud manifestandum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0150","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0151","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0152","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nulla persona divina mittatur nisi ab ea a qua procedit aeternaliter. Quia, sicut dicit Augustinus, IV de Trin., *pater a nullo mittitur, quia a nullo est*. Si ergo aliqua persona divina mittitur ab alia, oportet quod sit ab illa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0153","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0154","text":"Praeterea, mittens habet auctoritatem respectu missi. Sed respectu divinae personae non potest haberi auctoritas nisi secundum originem. Ergo oportet quod divina persona quae mittitur, sit a persona mittente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0155","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0156","text":"Praeterea, si persona divina potest mitti ab eo a quo non est, nihil prohibebit dicere quod spiritus sanctus detur ab homine, quamvis non sit ab eo. Quod est contra Augustinum, XV de Trin. Ergo divina persona non mittitur nisi ab ea a qua est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0157","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0158","text":"Sed contra est quod filius mittitur a spiritu sancto, secundum illud Isaiae XLVIII, *et nunc misit me dominus Deus, et spiritus eius*. Filius autem non est a spiritu sancto. Ergo persona divina mittitur ab ea a qua non est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0159","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0160","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc inveniuntur aliqui diversimode locuti esse. Secundum quosdam enim, persona divina non mittitur nisi ab eo a quo est aeternaliter. Et secundum hoc, cum dicitur filius Dei missus a spiritu sancto, referendum est hoc ad humanam naturam, secundum quam missus est ad praedicandum a spiritu sancto. Augustinus autem dicit, II de Trin., quod filius mittitur et a se et a spiritu sancto; et spiritus sanctus etiam mittitur et a se et a filio, ut sic mitti in divinis non conveniat cuilibet personae, sed solum personae ab alio existenti; mittere autem conveniat cuilibet personae. Utrumque autem habet aliquo modo veritatem. Quia cum dicitur aliqua persona mitti, designatur et ipsa persona ab alio existens, et effectus visibilis aut invisibilis, secundum quem missio divinae personae attenditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0161","text":"Si igitur mittens designetur ut principium personae quae mittitur, sic non quaelibet persona mittit, sed solum illa cui convenit esse principium illius personae. Et sic filius mittitur tantum a patre, spiritus sanctus autem a patre et filio. Si vero persona mittens intelligatur esse principium effectus secundum quem attenditur missio, sic tota Trinitas mittit personam missam. Non autem propter hoc homo dat spiritum sanctum, quia nec effectum gratiae potest causare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0162","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0163","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"af2cc5ec7f52e4949a641a2063333e3d6efd8f12f3674b4f6b8738f0ec3df9ab","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T142724679104Z_ee611453","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 43","source_sha256":"af2cc5ec7f52e4949a641a2063333e3d6efd8f12f3674b4f6b8738f0ec3df9ab","sha256":"54e66d12e4dba709c0c620c76be1603789a312f69e32a1f8e7396195a5f9577d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:27:24.679128+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0002","text":"[I q. 43 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0003","text":"Next, the mission of the divine persons must be considered. And concerning this, eight questions are asked. First, whether it befits any divine person to be sent. Second, whether mission is eternal, or temporal only. Third, in what respect a divine person is sent invisibly. Fourth, whether being sent befits every person. Fifth, whether both the Son and the Holy Spirit are sent invisibly. Sixth, to whom the invisible mission takes place. Seventh, concerning the visible mission. Eighth, whether any person sends himself, visibly or invisibly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0005","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that being sent does not befit a divine person. For one who is sent is lesser than the sender. But one divine person is not lesser than another. Therefore one person is not sent by another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0007","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0008","text":"Further, everything that is sent is separated from the sender; hence Jerome says, commenting on Ezekiel, *what is joined together and coupled in one body cannot be sent*. But among the divine persons nothing is separable, as Hilary says. Therefore one person is not sent by another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0009","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0010","text":"Further, whoever is sent departs from some place and goes anew to some place. But this does not befit a divine person, since it is everywhere. Therefore being sent does not befit a divine person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0011","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0012","text":"On the contrary, it is said in John VIII, *I am not alone, but I and the Father who sent me*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0013","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0014","text":"I answer that, in the notion of mission two things are implied. One is the relation of the one sent to him by whom he is sent; the other is the relation of the one sent to the terminus to which he is sent. Now, by the fact that someone is sent, a certain procession of the one sent from the sender is shown: either according to command, as a master sends a servant; or according to counsel, as when a counsellor is said to send a king to wage war; or according to origin, as when a flower is said to be sent forth by a tree. The relation to the terminus to which he is sent is also shown, so that he begins somehow to be there: either because previously he was not at all there whither he is sent, or because he begins somehow to be there in a way in which he was not there previously. Therefore mission can befit a divine person, insofar as on the one hand it implies a procession of origin from the sender, and insofar as on the other hand it implies a new mode of existing in something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0015","text":"Thus the Son is said to have been sent by the Father into the world, insofar as he began to be in the world visibly through assumed flesh; and yet he was previously in the world, as is said in John I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0016","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0017","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that mission implies inferiority in the one who is sent insofar as it implies procession from a sending principle either according to command or according to counsel, because one commanding is greater and one counselling is wiser. But in divine things it implies only procession of origin, which is according to equality, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0018","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that what is so sent as to begin to be where previously it was in no way, is moved locally by its mission; hence it must be separated in place from the sender. But this does not occur in the mission of a divine person, because the divine person sent, just as it does not begin to be where it had not previously been, so neither does it cease to be where it had been. Hence such a mission is without separation, but has only a distinction of origin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0020","text":"[I q. 43 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that that objection proceeds concerning a mission that occurs by local motion; this has no place in divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0023","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that mission can be eternal. For Gregory says: in that he is begotten, the Son is sent. But the generation of the Son is eternal. Therefore so too is his mission.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0025","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0026","text":"Moreover, whatever belongs to someone temporally is changed. But a divine person is not changed. Therefore, the mission of a divine person is not temporal, but eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0027","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0028","text":"Moreover, mission implies procession. But the procession of the divine persons is eternal. Therefore, so is mission.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0029","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Galatians IV, *when the fullness of time came, God sent his Son*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0031","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that, in those things which imply the origin of the divine persons, a certain difference must be observed. For some, in their signification, imply only a relation to a principle, as procession and going forth. Others, however, together with a relation to a principle, determine the term of the procession. Of these, some determine an eternal term, as generation and spiration; for generation is the procession of a divine person into the divine nature, and spiration, taken passively, implies the procession of subsisting love. Others, however, together with a relation to a principle, imply a temporal term, as mission and giving; for something is sent in order that it may be in something, and is given in order that it may be possessed. But for a divine person to be possessed by a creature, or to be in it according to a new mode of existing, is something temporal. Hence mission and giving are said of the divine only temporally. But generation and spiration only eternally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0033","text":"But procession and going forth are said of the divine both eternally and temporally; for the Son proceeded eternally so as to be God, but temporally so as also to be man, according to the visible mission; or also so as to be in man, according to the invisible mission.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0034","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Gregory speaks of the temporal generation of the Son, not from the Father, but from the mother. Or because from the very fact that he is eternally begotten, the Son has that he can be sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0036","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0037","text":"To the second, it must be said that a divine person’s being in something according to a new mode, or being possessed temporally by someone, is not due to a change in the divine person, but to a change in the creature; just as God too is said to be Lord temporally, on account of a change in the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0038","text":"[I q. 43 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0039","text":"To the third, it must be said that mission not only implies procession from a principle, but determines a temporal term of the procession. Hence mission is temporal only. Or, mission includes eternal procession and adds something, namely, a temporal effect; for the relation of a divine person to its principle is only eternal. Hence the procession is called twofold, namely eternal and temporal, not because the relation to the principle is doubled, but the doubling is on the part of the temporal and eternal term.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0041","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0042","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the invisible mission of a divine person is not only according to the gift of grace that makes one pleasing. For for a divine person to be sent is for that person itself to be given. Therefore, if a divine person is sent only according to the gifts of grace that makes one pleasing, the divine person itself will not be given, but only its gifts. This is the error of those who say that the Holy Spirit is not given, but his gifts are.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0043","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0044","text":"Moreover, this preposition, “according to,” denotes a relation of some cause. But the divine person is the cause that the gift of grace that makes one pleasing is possessed, and not conversely; according to Romans V, *the charity of God has been poured forth in our hearts through the Holy Spirit, who has been given to us*. Therefore, it is unfittingly said that a divine person is sent according to the gifts of grace that makes one pleasing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0045","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0046","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, in Book IV of *On the Trinity*, that *the Son, when he is perceived by the mind in time, is said to be sent*. But the Son is known not only through grace that makes one pleasing, but also through grace freely given, as through faith and through knowledge. Therefore, a divine person is not sent according to grace that makes one pleasing alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0047","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0048","text":"Moreover, Rabanus says that the Holy Spirit was given to the apostles for the working of miracles. But this is not a gift of grace that makes one pleasing, but of grace freely given. Therefore, a divine person is not given only according to grace that makes one pleasing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0049","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0050","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XV, that the Holy Spirit proceeds temporally in order to sanctify the creature. But mission is a temporal procession. Therefore, since the sanctification of the creature is only through grace that makes pleasing, it follows that the mission of a divine person is only through grace that makes pleasing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0051","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0052","text":"I answer that it must be said that it belongs to a divine person to be sent, insofar as it exists in someone in a new way; but to be given, insofar as it is possessed by someone. But neither of these occurs except according to grace that makes pleasing. For there is one common manner in which God is in all things through essence, power, and presence, as a cause is in effects participating in his goodness. Beyond this common manner, however, there is a special one, which belongs to the rational creature, in which God is said to be as the known in the knower and the beloved in the lover. And because, by knowing and loving, the rational creature attains God himself by its operation, according to this special manner God is said not only to be in the rational creature, but also to dwell in it as in his temple. Thus, therefore, no other effect can be the reason why a divine person is in a rational creature in a new way, except grace that makes pleasing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0053","text":"Hence, according to grace that makes pleasing alone, a divine person is sent and proceeds temporally. Likewise, we are said to possess only that which we can freely use or enjoy. But to have the power of enjoying a divine person is only according to grace that makes pleasing. Yet in the very gift of grace that makes pleasing, the Holy Spirit is possessed and dwells in a human being. Hence the Holy Spirit himself is given and sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0054","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0055","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that by the gift of grace that makes pleasing the rational creature is perfected so that it may freely not only use the created gift itself, but enjoy the divine person itself. And therefore the invisible mission takes place according to the gift of grace that makes pleasing, and yet the divine person itself is given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0056","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0057","text":"To the second, it must be said that grace that makes pleasing disposes the soul to possess a divine person, and this is signified when it is said that the Holy Spirit is given according to the gift of grace. Yet the gift of grace itself is from the Holy Spirit, and this is signified when it is said that the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts through the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0058","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0059","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although through certain effects the Son can be known by us, nevertheless through no effects does he dwell in us, or even is possessed by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0060","text":"[I q. 43 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0061","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the working of miracles is a manifestation of grace that makes pleasing, as are also the gift of prophecy and every grace freely given. Hence in I Corinthians XII, grace freely given is called a manifestation of the Spirit. Thus, therefore, the Holy Spirit is said to have been given to the apostles for the working of miracles, because grace that makes pleasing was given to them with a manifesting sign. But if only the sign of grace that makes pleasing were given without grace, the Holy Spirit would not be said to be given simply, unless perhaps with some qualification, as when it is said that the prophetic or miraculous Spirit is given to someone, insofar as he has from the Holy Spirit the power of prophesying or working miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0062","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0063","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0064","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that it also belongs to the Father to be sent. For to send a divine person is to give that person. But the Father gives himself, since he cannot be possessed unless he himself gives himself. Therefore it can be said that the Father sends himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0065","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0066","text":"Further, a divine person is sent according to the indwelling of grace. But through grace the whole Trinity dwells in us, according to John XIV: *We will come to him and make our dwelling with him*. Therefore each of the divine persons is sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0067","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0068","text":"Further, whatever belongs to one person belongs to all, except notions and persons. But mission signifies neither a person nor a notion, since there are only five notions, as was said above. Therefore it belongs to every divine person to be sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0069","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0070","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book II of *On the Trinity*, that *the Father alone is never read to have been sent*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0071","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0072","text":"I answer that it must be said that mission, in its notion, entails procession from another; and in divine things, according to origin, as was said above. Hence, since the Father is not from another, being sent in no way belongs to him; but only to the Son and the Holy Spirit, to whom it belongs to be from another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0073","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0074","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that if giving implies the liberal communication of something to someone, then the Father gives Himself, insofar as He liberally communicates Himself to the creature for enjoyment. But if it implies the authority of the giver with respect to that which is given, then in divine matters it does not befit anyone to be given except a person who is from another; just as neither does it befit anyone to be sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0075","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0076","text":"To the second it must be said that, although the effect of grace is also from the Father, who dwells by grace, just as do the Son and the Holy Spirit, nevertheless, because He is not from another, He is not said to be sent. And this is what Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, IV: “when the Father is known in time by anyone, He is not said to be sent; for He does not have one from whom He is, or from whom He proceeds.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0077","text":"[I q. 43 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0078","text":"To the third it must be said that mission, insofar as it implies procession from the sender, includes in its signification a notion, not indeed a special one, but a general one, insofar as being from another is common to two notions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0079","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0080","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0081","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that it does not befit the Son to be sent invisibly. For the invisible mission of a divine person is considered according to the gifts of grace. But all gifts of grace pertain to the Holy Spirit, according to 1 Cor. XII: “one and the same Spirit works all these things.” Therefore, no one but the Holy Spirit is sent invisibly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0082","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0083","text":"Further, the mission of a divine person takes place according to sanctifying grace. But the gifts pertaining to the perfection of the intellect are not gifts of sanctifying grace, since they can be had without charity, according to 1 Cor. XIII: “if I have prophecy, and know all mysteries and all knowledge, and if I have all faith, so as to remove mountains, but do not have charity, I am nothing.” Therefore, since the Son proceeds as the word of the intellect, it seems that it does not befit Him to be sent invisibly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0084","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0085","text":"Further, the mission of a divine person is a certain procession, as has been said. But the procession of the Son is one thing, and that of the Holy Spirit another. Therefore their mission too is different, if each is sent. And thus one of them would be superfluous, since one is sufficient to sanctify the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0086","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0087","text":"But on the contrary, it is said of divine wisdom in Wis. IX: “send her from your holy heavens, and from the throne of your majesty.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0088","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0089","text":"I answer that it must be said that through sanctifying grace the whole Trinity dwells in the mind, according to John XIV: “we will come to him, and make our dwelling with him.” But for a divine person to be sent to someone through invisible grace signifies a new mode of indwelling of that person, and His origin from another. Hence, since both the Son and the Holy Spirit are suited both to dwell by grace and to be from another, it befits each to be sent invisibly. But although it befits the Father to dwell by grace, it does not befit Him to be from another; and consequently neither to be sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0090","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0091","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, although all gifts, insofar as they are gifts, are attributed to the Holy Spirit, because He has the character of the first gift, insofar as He is love, as was said above; nevertheless, certain gifts, according to their proper characters, are attributed to the Son by a certain appropriation—namely, those which pertain to the intellect—and the mission of the Son is considered according to those gifts. Hence Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, IV, that “then the Son is invisibly sent to someone when He is known and perceived by someone.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0092","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0093","text":"To the second it must be said that the soul is conformed to God through grace. Hence, in order that some divine person be sent to someone through grace, it is necessary that an assimilation of that person to the divine person who is sent take place through some gift of grace. And because the Holy Spirit is love, through the gift of charity the soul is assimilated to the Holy Spirit; hence the mission of the Holy Spirit is considered according to the gift of charity. But the Son is the Word, not any sort of word, but one breathing forth love; hence Augustine says, in book IX of *On the Trinity*: “the word which we intend to indicate is knowledge with love.” Therefore, the Son is not sent according to every perfection of the intellect, but according to such an instruction of the intellect as bursts forth into an affection of love, as is said in John VI: “everyone who has heard from the Father and learned comes to me”; and in the Psalm: “in my meditation fire will blaze forth.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0094","text":"And therefore Augustine significantly says that the Son is sent when He is known and perceived by someone, for perception signifies a certain experiential knowledge. And this is properly called wisdom, as it were, savory knowledge, according to Ecclus. VI: “wisdom of teaching is according to its name.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0095","text":"[I q. 43 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0096","text":"To the third it must be said that, since mission implies the origin of the person sent and indwelling through grace, as was said above, if we speak of mission with respect to origin, then the mission of the Son is distinguished from the mission of the Holy Spirit, just as generation is distinguished from procession. But if with respect to the effect of grace, then the two missions communicate in the root of grace, but are distinguished in the effects of grace, which are the illumination of the intellect and the inflaming of the affection. And thus it is clear that one cannot be without the other, because neither is without sanctifying grace, nor is one person separated from another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0097","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0098","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0099","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that an invisible mission is not made to all who partake of grace. For the fathers of the Old Testament partook of grace. But an invisible mission does not seem to have been made to them, for it is said in John VII, *the Spirit had not yet been given, because Jesus was not yet glorified*. Therefore, an invisible mission is not made to all who partake of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0100","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0101","text":"Further, progress in virtue is only through grace. But an invisible mission does not seem to be considered according to progress in virtue, because progress in virtue seems to be continuous, since charity always either progresses or declines; and thus mission would be continuous. Therefore, an invisible mission is not made to all partakers of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0102","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0103","text":"Further, Christ and the blessed have grace most fully. But mission does not seem to be made to them, because mission is made to something distant; whereas Christ, insofar as he is man, and all the blessed are perfectly united to God. Therefore, an invisible mission is not made to all partakers of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0104","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0105","text":"Further, the sacraments of the new law contain grace. Yet an invisible mission is not said to be made to them. Therefore, an invisible mission is not made to all things that have grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0106","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0107","text":"But on the contrary, according to Augustine, an invisible mission is made in order to sanctify the creature. Now every creature having grace is sanctified. Therefore, an invisible mission is made to every such creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0108","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0109","text":"I answer that, as was said above, mission by its very notion implies that he who is sent either begins to be where he was not previously, as happens in created things; or begins to be where he was previously, but in some new manner, according as mission is attributed to the divine persons. Therefore, in the one to whom mission is made, two things must be considered, namely the indwelling of grace, and a certain renewal through grace. Therefore, an invisible mission is made to all in whom these two things are found.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0110","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0111","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an invisible mission was made to the fathers of the Old Testament. Hence Augustine says, in IV *On the Trinity*, that insofar as the Son is sent invisibly, *this takes place in men or with men; and this was done before in the fathers and prophets*. Therefore, the statement, the Spirit had not yet been given, is understood concerning that giving with a visible sign, which took place on the day of Pentecost.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0112","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0113","text":"To the second, it must be said that an invisible mission also takes place according to progress in virtue, or an increase of grace. Hence Augustine says, in IV *On the Trinity*, that *then the Son is sent to someone when he is known and perceived by someone, as much as he can be known and perceived according to the capacity either of a rational soul progressing toward God, or perfected in God*. Yet an invisible mission is considered especially according to that increase of grace when someone progresses to some new act, or to a new state of grace—for example, when someone progresses to the grace of miracles or prophecy, or when, out of the fervor of charity, he exposes himself to martyrdom, or renounces what he possesses, or undertakes any arduous work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0114","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0115","text":"To the third, it must be said that an invisible mission was made to the blessed at the very beginning of beatitude. But afterward an invisible mission is made to them, not according to an intensification of grace, but insofar as certain mysteries are newly revealed to them, and this continues until the day of judgment. This increase is considered according to the extension of grace as it extends to more things. But an invisible mission was made to Christ at the beginning of his conception, but not afterward, since from the beginning of his conception he was full of all wisdom and grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0116","text":"[I q. 43 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0117","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that grace is in the sacraments of the new law instrumentally, just as the form of an artefact is in the instruments of an art, according to a certain transmission from agent to patient. But mission is said to take place only in relation to a terminus. Hence the mission of a divine person is not made to the sacraments, but to those who receive grace through the sacraments.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0118","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0119","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0120","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that it is not fitting for the Holy Spirit to be sent visibly. For the Son, insofar as he was visibly sent into the world, is said to be less than the Father. But the Holy Spirit is never read to be less than the Father. Therefore, it is not fitting for the Holy Spirit to be sent visibly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0121","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0122","text":"Moreover, a visible mission is considered according to some visible creature assumed, as the mission of the Son according to the flesh. But the Holy Spirit did not assume any visible creature. Hence it cannot be said that He is in certain visible creatures in a manner other than in others, except perhaps as in a sign; as He is also in the sacraments and in all the figures of the Law. Therefore, the Holy Spirit is not sent visibly, or one must say that His visible mission is considered according to all such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0123","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0124","text":"Moreover, every visible creature is an effect demonstrating the whole Trinity. Therefore, through those visible creatures the Holy Spirit is not sent more than another person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0125","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0126","text":"Moreover, the Son was visibly sent according to the most excellent of visible creatures, namely, according to human nature. Therefore, if the Holy Spirit is visibly sent, He ought to have been sent according to some rational creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0127","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0128","text":"Moreover, those things which are visibly done by God are dispensed through the ministry of angels, as Augustine says, *On the Trinity*, III. Therefore, if any visible appearances appeared, this was done through angels. And thus the angels themselves are sent, and not the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0129","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0130","text":"Moreover, if the Holy Spirit is visibly sent, this is only in order to manifest the invisible mission, since invisible things are manifested through visible things. Therefore, to one to whom the invisible mission was not made, neither ought the visible mission to have been made; and to all to whom the invisible mission is made, whether in the New or in the Old Testament, the visible mission ought to be made—which is evidently false. Therefore, the Holy Spirit is not visibly sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0131","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0132","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Matthew III that the Holy Spirit descended upon the Lord when baptized in the form of a dove.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0133","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0134","text":"I answer that it must be said that God provides for all things according to the mode of each. Now it is connatural to man to be led by visible things to invisible things, as is clear from what has been said above; and therefore the invisible things of God had to be manifested to man through visible things. Therefore, just as God in some manner showed Himself and the eternal processions of the persons to men through visible creatures, according to certain indications, so it was fitting that the invisible missions of the divine persons too should be manifested through certain visible creatures. Yet the Son and the Holy Spirit are otherwise situated. For it belongs to the Holy Spirit, insofar as He proceeds as love, to be the gift of sanctification; but it belongs to the Son, insofar as He is the principle of the Holy Spirit, to be the author of this sanctification. And therefore the Son was visibly sent as the author of sanctification, but the Holy Spirit as an indication of sanctification.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0135","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0136","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Son assumed into the unity of His person the visible creature in which He appeared, so that what is said of that creature can be said of the Son of God. And thus, by reason of the nature assumed, the Son is said to be less than the Father. But the Holy Spirit did not assume into the unity of His person the visible creature in which He appeared, such that what belongs to it is predicated of Him. Hence He cannot be said to be less than the Father on account of the visible creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0137","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0138","text":"To the second, it must be said that the visible mission of the Holy Spirit is not considered according to an imaginary vision, which is a prophetic vision. For, as Augustine says, *On the Trinity*, II, *the prophetic vision was not presented to bodily eyes through bodily forms, but in the spirit through spiritual images of bodies; but that dove and fire were seen with the eyes by whoever saw them. Nor again did the Holy Spirit stand in the same relation to such appearances as the Son did to the rock (for it is said, “the rock was Christ”). For that rock already existed in creation, and was called by the name of Christ by way of action, whom it signified; but that dove and fire suddenly came into being solely to signify these things.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0139","text":"But they seem similar to that flame which appeared to Moses in the bush, and to that pillar which the people followed in the wilderness, and to the flashes of lightning and thunder which occurred when the law was given on the mountain; for the bodily appearance of those things came into being for this purpose, that it might signify something and pass away. Thus, therefore, it is clear that the visible mission is considered neither according to prophetic visions, which were imaginary and not bodily, nor according to the sacramental signs of the Old and New Testaments, in which certain pre-existing things are assumed to signify something. But the Holy Spirit is said to be visibly sent insofar as He was shown in certain creatures, as in signs specially made for this purpose.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0140","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0141","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although the whole Trinity wrought those visible creatures, nevertheless they were made to demonstrate especially this or that person. For just as the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit are signified by diverse names, so too they could be signified by diverse things, although there is no separation or diversity among them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0142","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0143","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the person of the Son had to be declared as the author of sanctification, as has been said; and therefore the visible mission of the Son had to take place according to a rational nature, whose office it is to act and to which it can belong to sanctify. But any other creature could be an indication of sanctification. Nor was it necessary that the visible creature formed for this purpose should be assumed by the Holy Spirit into the unity of person, since it was not assumed in order to do something, but only to indicate. And for this reason it was also unnecessary that it should endure, except for as long as it performed its office.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0144","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0145","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that those visible creatures were formed through the ministry of the angels, yet not to signify the person of an angel, but to signify the person of the Holy Spirit. Therefore, because the Holy Spirit was in those visible creatures as the thing signified in the sign, for this reason the Holy Spirit is said to be sent visibly according to them, and not an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0146","text":"[I q. 43 a. 7 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0147","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that it is not necessary for an invisible mission always to be manifested through some outward visible sign; but, as is said in I Cor. XII, *the manifestation of the Spirit is given to someone for usefulness*, namely, that of the Church. This usefulness indeed is that faith may be confirmed and propagated through visible signs of this kind. This was accomplished principally through Christ and through the apostles, according to Heb. II: *when it had begun to be declared through the Lord, it was confirmed to us by those who heard*. And therefore a visible mission of the Holy Spirit especially ought to have been made to Christ and to the apostles, and to certain primitive saints, in whom the Church was in some manner being founded; yet in such a way that the visible mission made to Christ would demonstrate the invisible mission made to him, not then, but at the beginning of his conception.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0148","text":"But a visible mission was made to Christ: indeed, at Baptism under the appearance of a dove, which is a fruitful animal, in order to show in Christ the authority of granting grace through spiritual regeneration; hence the voice of the Father sounded forth: *this is my beloved Son*, so that others might be regenerated into the likeness of the Only-Begotten. But at the Transfiguration, under the appearance of a bright cloud, in order to show the superabundance of doctrine; hence it was said: *hear him*. But to the apostles, under the appearance of a breath, in order to show the power of ministry in the dispensation of the sacraments; hence it was said to them: *whose sins you forgive, they are forgiven them*. But under fiery tongues, in order to show the office of teaching; hence it is said that *they began to speak in diverse tongues*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0149","text":"But to the fathers of the Old Testament, a visible mission of the Holy Spirit ought not to have been made, because the visible mission of the Son had first to be accomplished before that of the Holy Spirit, since the Holy Spirit manifests the Son, just as the Son manifests the Father. Nevertheless, visible apparitions of the divine persons were made to the fathers of the Old Testament. These, however, cannot be called visible missions, because they were not made, according to Augustine, in order to designate the indwelling of a divine person through grace, but in order to manifest something else.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0150","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0151","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0152","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth article. It seems that no divine person is sent except by the one from whom he proceeds eternally. For, as Augustine says, in IV *On the Trinity*, *the Father is sent by no one, because he is from no one*. Therefore, if one divine person is sent by another, it must be from that other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0153","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0154","text":"Further, the sender has authority with respect to the one sent. But authority with respect to a divine person cannot be had except according to origin. Therefore, the divine person who is sent must be from the sending person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0155","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0156","text":"Further, if a divine person can be sent by one from whom he is not, nothing would prevent saying that the Holy Spirit is given by a man, although he is not from him. This is contrary to Augustine, XV *On the Trinity*. Therefore, a divine person is sent only by the one from whom he is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0157","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0158","text":"But on the contrary, the Son is sent by the Holy Spirit, according to Isaiah XLVIII: *and now the Lord God has sent me, and his Spirit*. But the Son is not from the Holy Spirit. Therefore, a divine person is sent by one from whom he is not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0159","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0160","text":"I answer that it must be said that, concerning this matter, some are found to have spoken in different ways. For according to some, a divine person is sent only by one from whom he is eternally. And according to this, when the Son of God is said to be sent by the Holy Spirit, this must be referred to the human nature, according to which he was sent by the Holy Spirit to preach. But Augustine says, in II *On the Trinity*, that the Son is sent both by himself and by the Holy Spirit; and that the Holy Spirit likewise is sent both by himself and by the Son. Thus, to be sent in divine matters does not belong to every person, but only to a person existing from another; whereas to send belongs to every person. But each position has truth in some respect. For when some person is said to be sent, both the person himself existing from another and the visible or invisible effect according to which the mission of a divine person is considered are designated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0161","text":"Therefore, if the sender is designated as the principle of the person who is sent, then not every person sends, but only the one to whom it belongs to be the principle of that person. Thus the Son is sent only by the Father, whereas the Holy Spirit is sent by the Father and the Son. But if the sending person is understood to be the principle of the effect according to which the mission is considered, then the whole Trinity sends the person sent. Yet it does not follow from this that man gives the Holy Spirit, because neither can he cause the effect of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0162","text":"[I q. 43 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1028.html#30281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0163","text":"And through this the solution to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_35:P0163"]}],"source_digest":"d9b53c97d32439c0387fc8790b075b927921dd00a35ed1272d5d5ec7e3c49d83","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 43"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 43","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36","title":"First Part · Question 44","order":45,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_36","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 44 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30282)\n\nPost considerationem divinarum personarum, considerandum restat de processione creaturarum a Deo. Erit autem haec consideratio tripartita, ut primo consideretur de productione creaturarum; secundo, de earum distinctione; tertio, de conservatione et gubernatione. Circa primum tria sunt consideranda, primo quidem, quae sit prima causa entium; secundo, de modo procedendi creaturarum a prima causa; tertio vero, de principio durationis rerum. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Deus sit causa efficiens omnium entium. Secundo, utrum materia prima sit creata a Deo, vel sit principium ex aequo coordinatum ei. Tertio, utrum Deus sit causa exemplaris rerum, vel sint alia exemplaria praeter ipsum. Quarto, utrum ipse sit causa finalis rerum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30283)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit necessarium omne ens esse creatum a Deo. Nihil enim prohibet inveniri rem sine eo quod non est de ratione rei, sicut hominem sine albedine. Sed habitudo causati ad causam non videtur esse de ratione entium quia sine hac possunt aliqua entia intelligi. Ergo sine hac possunt esse. Ergo nihil prohibet esse aliqua entia non creata a Deo.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30284)\n\nPraeterea, ad hoc aliquid indiget causa efficiente, ut sit. Ergo quod non potest non esse, non indiget causa efficiente. Sed nullum necessarium potest non esse, quia quod necesse est esse, non potest non esse. Cum igitur multa sint necessaria in rebus, videtur quod non omnia entia sint a Deo.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30285)\n\nPraeterea, quorumcumque est aliqua causa, in his potest fieri demonstratio per causam illam. Sed in mathematicis non fit demonstratio per causam agentem, ut per philosophum patet, in III Metaphys. Non igitur omnia entia sunt a Deo sicut a causa agente.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30286)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Rom. XI, *ex ipso, et per ipsum, et in ipso sunt omnia*.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30287)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere omne quod quocumque modo est, a Deo esse. Si enim aliquid invenitur in aliquo per participationem, necesse est quod causetur in ipso ab eo cui essentialiter convenit; sicut ferrum fit ignitum ab igne. Ostensum est autem supra, cum de divina simplicitate ageretur, quod Deus est ipsum esse per se subsistens. Et iterum ostensum est quod esse subsistens non potest esse nisi unum, sicut si albedo esset subsistens, non posset esse nisi una, cum albedines multiplicentur secundum recipientia. Relinquitur ergo quod omnia alia a Deo non sint suum esse, sed participant esse. Necesse est igitur omnia quae diversificantur secundum diversam participationem essendi, ut sint perfectius vel minus perfecte, causari ab uno primo ente, quod perfectissime est. Unde et Plato dixit quod necesse est ante omnem multitudinem ponere unitatem. Et Aristoteles dicit, in II Metaphys., quod id quod est maxime ens et maxime verum, est causa omnis entis et omnis veri, sicut id quod maxime calidum est, est causa omnis caliditatis.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30288)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet habitudo ad causam non intret definitionem entis quod est causatum, tamen sequitur ad ea qua sunt de eius ratione, quia ex hoc quod aliquid per participationem est ens, sequitur quod sit causatum ab alio. Unde huiusmodi ens non potest esse, quin sit causatum; sicut nec homo, quin sit risibile. Sed quia esse causatum non est de ratione entis simpliciter, propter hoc invenitur aliquod ens non causatum.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30289)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ex hac ratione quidam moti fuerunt ad ponendum quod id quod est necessarium non habeat causam, ut dicitur in VIII Physic. Sed hoc manifeste falsum apparet in scientiis demonstrativis, in quibus principia necessaria sunt causae conclusionum necessariarum. Et ideo dicit Aristoteles, in V Metaphys., quod sunt quaedam necessaria quae habent causam suae necessitatis. Non ergo propter hoc solum requiritur causa agens, quia effectus potest non esse, sed quia effectus non esset, si causa non esset. Haec enim conditionalis est vera, sive antecedens et consequens sint possibilia, sive impossibilia.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30290)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod mathematica accipiuntur ut abstracta secundum rationem, cum tamen non sint abstracta secundum esse. Unicuique autem competit habere causam agentem, secundum quod habet esse. Licet igitur ea quae sunt mathematica habeant causam agentem, non tamen secundum habitudinem quam habent ad causam agentem, cadunt sub consideratione mathematici. Et ideo in scientiis mathematicis non demonstratur aliquid per causam agentem.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30291)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod materia prima non sit creata a Deo. Omne enim quod fit, componitur ex subiecto et ex aliquo alio, ut dicitur in I Physic. Sed materiae primae non est aliquod subiectum. Ergo materia prima non potest esse facta a Deo.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30292)\n\nPraeterea, actio et passio dividuntur contra se invicem. Sed sicut primum principium activum est Deus, ita primum principium passivum est materia. Ergo Deus et materia prima sunt duo principia contra se invicem divisa, quorum neutrum est ab alio.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30293)\n\nPraeterea, omne agens agit sibi simile, et sic, cum omne agens agat inquantum est actu, sequitur quod omne factum aliquo modo sit in actu. Sed materia prima est tantum in potentia, inquantum huiusmodi. Ergo contra rationem materiae primae est, quod sit facta.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30294)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XII Confess., duo fecisti, domine, unum prope te, scilicet Angelum, aliud prope nihil, scilicet materiam primam.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30295)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod antiqui philosophi paulatim, et quasi pedetentim, intraverunt in cognitionem veritatis. A principio enim, quasi grossiores existentes, non existimabant esse entia nisi corpora sensibilia. Quorum qui ponebant in eis motum, non considerabant motum nisi secundum aliqua accidentia, ut puta secundum raritatem et densitatem, congregationem et segregationem. Et supponentes ipsam substantiam corporum increatam, assignabant aliquas causas huiusmodi accidentalium transmutationum, ut puta amicitiam, litem, intellectum, aut aliquid huiusmodi. Ulterius vero procedentes, distinxerunt per intellectum inter formam substantialem et materiam, quam ponebant increatam; et perceperunt transmutationem fieri in corporibus secundum formas essentiales. Quarum transmutationum quasdam causas universaliores ponebant, ut obliquum circulum, secundum Aristotelem, vel ideas, secundum Platonem. Sed considerandum est quod materia per formam contrahitur ad determinatam speciem; sicut substantia alicuius speciei per accidens ei adveniens contrahitur ad determinatum modum essendi, ut homo contrahitur per album. Utrique igitur consideraverunt ens particulari quadam consideratione, vel inquantum est hoc ens, vel inquantum est tale ens. Et sic rebus causas agentes particulares assignaverunt. Et ulterius aliqui erexerunt se ad considerandum ens inquantum est ens, et consideraverunt causam rerum, non solum secundum quod sunt haec vel talia, sed secundum quod sunt entia. Hoc igitur quod est causa rerum inquantum sunt entia, oportet esse causam rerum, non solum secundum quod sunt talia per formas accidentales, nec secundum quod sunt haec per formas substantiales, sed etiam secundum omne illud quod pertinet ad esse illorum quocumque modo. Et sic oportet ponere etiam materiam primam creatam ab universali causa entium.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30296)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus in I Physic. loquitur de fieri particulari, quod est de forma in formam, sive accidentalem sive substantialem nunc autem loquimur de rebus secundum emanationem earum ab universali principio essendi. A qua quidem emanatione nec materia excluditur, licet a primo modo factionis excludatur.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30297)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod passio est effectus actionis. Unde et rationabile est quod primum principium passivum sit effectus primi principii activi, nam omne imperfectum causatur a perfecto. Oportet enim primum principium esse perfectissimum, ut dicit Aristoteles, in XII Metaphys.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30298)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa non ostendit quod materia non sit creata, sed quod non sit creata sine forma. Licet enim omne creatum sit in actu, non tamen est actus purus. Unde oportet quod etiam illud quod se habet ex parte potentiae, sit creatum, si totum quod ad esse ipsius pertinet, creatum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30299)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod causa exemplaris sit aliquid praeter Deum. Exemplatum enim habet similitudinem exemplaris. Sed creaturae longe sunt a divina similitudine. Non ergo Deus est causa exemplaris earum.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30300)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est per participationem, reducitur ad aliquid per se existens, ut ignitum ad ignem, sicut iam dictum est. Sed quaecumque sunt in sensibilibus rebus, sunt solum per participationem alicuius speciei, quod ex hoc patet, quod in nullo sensibilium invenitur solum id quod ad rationem speciei pertinet, sed adiunguntur principiis speciei principia individuantia. Oportet ergo ponere ipsas species per se existentes, ut per se hominem, et per se equum, et huiusmodi. Et haec dicuntur exemplaria. Sunt igitur exemplaria res quaedam extra Deum.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30301)\n\nPraeterea, scientiae et definitiones sunt de ipsis speciebus, non secundum quod sunt in particularibus, quia particularium non est scientia nec definitio. Ergo sunt quaedam entia, quae sunt entia vel species non in singularibus. Et haec dicuntur exemplaria. Ergo idem quod prius.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30302)\n\nPraeterea, hoc idem videtur per Dionysium, qui dicit, V cap. de Div. Nom., quod *ipsum secundum se esse, prius est eo quod est per se vitam esse, et eo quod est per se sapientiam esse*.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30303)\n\nSed contra est quod exemplar est idem quod idea. Sed ideae, secundum quod Augustinus libro octoginta trium quaest. dicit, *sunt formae principales, quae divina intelligentia continentur*. Ergo exemplaria rerum non sunt extra Deum.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30304)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus est prima causa exemplaris omnium rerum. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod ad productionem alicuius rei ideo necessarium est exemplar, ut effectus determinatam formam consequatur, artifex enim producit determinatam formam in materia, propter exemplar ad quod inspicit, sive illud sit exemplar ad quod extra intuetur, sive sit exemplar interius mente conceptum. Manifestum est autem quod ea quae naturaliter fiunt, determinatas formas consequuntur. Haec autem formarum determinatio oportet quod reducatur, sicut in primum principium, in divinam sapientiam, quae ordinem universi excogitavit, qui in rerum distinctione consistit. Et ideo oportet dicere quod in divina sapientia sunt rationes omnium rerum, quas supra diximus ideas, id est formas exemplares in mente divina existentes. Quae quidem licet multiplicentur secundum respectum ad res, tamen non sunt realiter aliud a divina essentia, prout eius similitudo a diversis participari potest diversimode. Sic igitur ipse Deus est primum exemplar omnium. Possunt etiam in rebus creatis quaedam aliorum exemplaria dici, secundum quod quaedam sunt ad similitudinem aliorum, vel secundum eandem speciem, vel secundum analogiam alicuius imitationis.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30305)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet creaturae non pertingant ad hoc quod sint similes Deo secundum suam naturam, similitudine speciei, ut homo genitus homini generanti; attingunt tamen ad eius similitudinem secundum repraesentationem rationis intellectae a Deo, ut domus quae est in materia, domui quae est in mente artificis.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30306)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod de ratione hominis est quod sit in materia, et sic non potest inveniri homo sine materia. Licet igitur hic homo sit per participationem speciei, non tamen potest reduci ad aliquid existens per se in eadem specie; sed ad speciem superexcedentem, sicut sunt substantiae separatae. Et eadem ratio est de aliis sensibilibus.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30307)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet quaelibet scientia et definitio sit solum entium, non tamen oportet quod res eundem modum habeant in essendo, quem intellectus habet in intelligendo. Nos enim, per virtutem intellectus agentis, abstrahimus species universales a particularibus conditionibus, non tamen oportet quod universalia praeter particularia subsistant, ut particularium exemplaria.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30308)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Dionysius, XI cap. de Div. Nom., per se vitam et per se sapientiam quandoque nominat ipsum Deum, quandoque virtutes ipsis rebus datas, non autem quasdam subsistentes res, sicut antiqui posuerunt.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30309)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit causa finalis omnium. Agere enim propter finem videtur esse alicuius indigentis fine. Sed Deus nullo est indigens. Ergo non competit sibi agere propter finem.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30310)\n\nPraeterea, finis generationis et forma generati et agens non incidunt in idem numero, ut dicitur in II Physic., quia finis generationis est forma generati. Sed Deus est primum agens omnium. Non ergo est causa finalis omnium.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30311)\n\nPraeterea, finem omnia appetunt. Sed Deum non omnia appetunt, quia neque omnia ipsum cognoscunt. Deus ergo non est omnium finis.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30312)\n\nPraeterea, finalis causa est prima causarum. Si igitur Deus sit causa agens et causa finalis, sequitur quod in eo sit prius et posterius. Quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30313)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Prov. XVI, *universa propter semetipsum operatus est dominus*.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30314)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod omne agens agit propter finem, alioquin ex actione agentis non magis sequeretur hoc quam illud, nisi a casu. Est autem idem finis agentis et patientis, inquantum huiusmodi, sed aliter et aliter, unum enim et idem est quod agens intendit imprimere, et quod patiens intendit recipere. Sunt autem quaedam quae simul agunt et patiuntur, quae sunt agentia imperfecta, et his convenit quod etiam in agendo intendant aliquid acquirere. Sed primo agenti, qui est agens tantum, non convenit agere propter acquisitionem alicuius finis; sed intendit solum communicare suam perfectionem, quae est eius bonitas. Et unaquaeque creatura intendit consequi suam perfectionem, quae est similitudo perfectionis et bonitatis divinae. Sic ergo divina bonitas est finis rerum omnium.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30315)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod agere propter indigentiam non est nisi agentis imperfecti, quod natum est agere et pati. Sed hoc Deo non competit. Et ideo ipse solus est maxime liberalis, quia non agit propter suam utilitatem, sed solum propter suam bonitatem.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30316)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod forma generati non est finis generationis nisi inquantum est similitudo formae generantis, quod suam similitudinem communicare intendit. Alioquin forma generati esset nobilior generante, cum finis sit nobilior his quae sunt ad finem.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30317)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnia appetunt Deum ut finem, appetendo quodcumque bonum, sive appetitu intelligibili, sive sensibili, sive naturali, qui est sine cognitione, quia nihil habet rationem boni et appetibilis, nisi secundum quod participat Dei similitudinem.\n\n[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30318)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, cum Deus sit causa efficiens, exemplaris et finalis omnium rerum, et materia prima sit ab ipso, sequitur quod primum principium omnium rerum sit unum tantum secundum rem. Nihil tamen prohibet in eo considerari multa secundum rationem, quorum quaedam prius cadunt in intellectu nostro quam alia.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0002","text":"[I q. 44 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0003","text":"Post considerationem divinarum personarum, considerandum restat de processione creaturarum a Deo. Erit autem haec consideratio tripartita, ut primo consideretur de productione creaturarum; secundo, de earum distinctione; tertio, de conservatione et gubernatione. Circa primum tria sunt consideranda, primo quidem, quae sit prima causa entium; secundo, de modo procedendi creaturarum a prima causa; tertio vero, de principio durationis rerum. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Deus sit causa efficiens omnium entium. Secundo, utrum materia prima sit creata a Deo, vel sit principium ex aequo coordinatum ei. Tertio, utrum Deus sit causa exemplaris rerum, vel sint alia exemplaria praeter ipsum. Quarto, utrum ipse sit causa finalis rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0005","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit necessarium omne ens esse creatum a Deo. Nihil enim prohibet inveniri rem sine eo quod non est de ratione rei, sicut hominem sine albedine. Sed habitudo causati ad causam non videtur esse de ratione entium quia sine hac possunt aliqua entia intelligi. Ergo sine hac possunt esse. Ergo nihil prohibet esse aliqua entia non creata a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0007","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0008","text":"Praeterea, ad hoc aliquid indiget causa efficiente, ut sit. Ergo quod non potest non esse, non indiget causa efficiente. Sed nullum necessarium potest non esse, quia quod necesse est esse, non potest non esse. Cum igitur multa sint necessaria in rebus, videtur quod non omnia entia sint a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0009","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quorumcumque est aliqua causa, in his potest fieri demonstratio per causam illam. Sed in mathematicis non fit demonstratio per causam agentem, ut per philosophum patet, in III Metaphys. Non igitur omnia entia sunt a Deo sicut a causa agente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0011","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Rom. XI, *ex ipso, et per ipsum, et in ipso sunt omnia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0013","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere omne quod quocumque modo est, a Deo esse. Si enim aliquid invenitur in aliquo per participationem, necesse est quod causetur in ipso ab eo cui essentialiter convenit; sicut ferrum fit ignitum ab igne. Ostensum est autem supra, cum de divina simplicitate ageretur, quod Deus est ipsum esse per se subsistens. Et iterum ostensum est quod esse subsistens non potest esse nisi unum, sicut si albedo esset subsistens, non posset esse nisi una, cum albedines multiplicentur secundum recipientia. Relinquitur ergo quod omnia alia a Deo non sint suum esse, sed participant esse. Necesse est igitur omnia quae diversificantur secundum diversam participationem essendi, ut sint perfectius vel minus perfecte, causari ab uno primo ente, quod perfectissime est. Unde et Plato dixit quod necesse est ante omnem multitudinem ponere unitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0015","text":"Et Aristoteles dicit, in II Metaphys., quod id quod est maxime ens et maxime verum, est causa omnis entis et omnis veri, sicut id quod maxime calidum est, est causa omnis caliditatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0016","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet habitudo ad causam non intret definitionem entis quod est causatum, tamen sequitur ad ea qua sunt de eius ratione, quia ex hoc quod aliquid per participationem est ens, sequitur quod sit causatum ab alio. Unde huiusmodi ens non potest esse, quin sit causatum; sicut nec homo, quin sit risibile. Sed quia esse causatum non est de ratione entis simpliciter, propter hoc invenitur aliquod ens non causatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0018","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ex hac ratione quidam moti fuerunt ad ponendum quod id quod est necessarium non habeat causam, ut dicitur in VIII Physic. Sed hoc manifeste falsum apparet in scientiis demonstrativis, in quibus principia necessaria sunt causae conclusionum necessariarum. Et ideo dicit Aristoteles, in V Metaphys., quod sunt quaedam necessaria quae habent causam suae necessitatis. Non ergo propter hoc solum requiritur causa agens, quia effectus potest non esse, sed quia effectus non esset, si causa non esset. Haec enim conditionalis est vera, sive antecedens et consequens sint possibilia, sive impossibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0020","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod mathematica accipiuntur ut abstracta secundum rationem, cum tamen non sint abstracta secundum esse. Unicuique autem competit habere causam agentem, secundum quod habet esse. Licet igitur ea quae sunt mathematica habeant causam agentem, non tamen secundum habitudinem quam habent ad causam agentem, cadunt sub consideratione mathematici. Et ideo in scientiis mathematicis non demonstratur aliquid per causam agentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0023","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod materia prima non sit creata a Deo. Omne enim quod fit, componitur ex subiecto et ex aliquo alio, ut dicitur in I Physic. Sed materiae primae non est aliquod subiectum. Ergo materia prima non potest esse facta a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0025","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0026","text":"Praeterea, actio et passio dividuntur contra se invicem. Sed sicut primum principium activum est Deus, ita primum principium passivum est materia. Ergo Deus et materia prima sunt duo principia contra se invicem divisa, quorum neutrum est ab alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0027","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0028","text":"Praeterea, omne agens agit sibi simile, et sic, cum omne agens agat inquantum est actu, sequitur quod omne factum aliquo modo sit in actu. Sed materia prima est tantum in potentia, inquantum huiusmodi. Ergo contra rationem materiae primae est, quod sit facta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0029","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XII Confess., duo fecisti, domine, unum prope te, scilicet Angelum, aliud prope nihil, scilicet materiam primam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0031","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod antiqui philosophi paulatim, et quasi pedetentim, intraverunt in cognitionem veritatis. A principio enim, quasi grossiores existentes, non existimabant esse entia nisi corpora sensibilia. Quorum qui ponebant in eis motum, non considerabant motum nisi secundum aliqua accidentia, ut puta secundum raritatem et densitatem, congregationem et segregationem. Et supponentes ipsam substantiam corporum increatam, assignabant aliquas causas huiusmodi accidentalium transmutationum, ut puta amicitiam, litem, intellectum, aut aliquid huiusmodi. Ulterius vero procedentes, distinxerunt per intellectum inter formam substantialem et materiam, quam ponebant increatam; et perceperunt transmutationem fieri in corporibus secundum formas essentiales. Quarum transmutationum quasdam causas universaliores ponebant, ut obliquum circulum, secundum Aristotelem, vel ideas, secundum Platonem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0033","text":"Sed considerandum est quod materia per formam contrahitur ad determinatam speciem; sicut substantia alicuius speciei per accidens ei adveniens contrahitur ad determinatum modum essendi, ut homo contrahitur per album. Utrique igitur consideraverunt ens particulari quadam consideratione, vel inquantum est hoc ens, vel inquantum est tale ens. Et sic rebus causas agentes particulares assignaverunt. Et ulterius aliqui erexerunt se ad considerandum ens inquantum est ens, et consideraverunt causam rerum, non solum secundum quod sunt haec vel talia, sed secundum quod sunt entia. Hoc igitur quod est causa rerum inquantum sunt entia, oportet esse causam rerum, non solum secundum quod sunt talia per formas accidentales, nec secundum quod sunt haec per formas substantiales, sed etiam secundum omne illud quod pertinet ad esse illorum quocumque modo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0034","text":"Et sic oportet ponere etiam materiam primam creatam ab universali causa entium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0035","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus in I Physic. loquitur de fieri particulari, quod est de forma in formam, sive accidentalem sive substantialem nunc autem loquimur de rebus secundum emanationem earum ab universali principio essendi. A qua quidem emanatione nec materia excluditur, licet a primo modo factionis excludatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0037","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod passio est effectus actionis. Unde et rationabile est quod primum principium passivum sit effectus primi principii activi, nam omne imperfectum causatur a perfecto. Oportet enim primum principium esse perfectissimum, ut dicit Aristoteles, in XII Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0039","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa non ostendit quod materia non sit creata, sed quod non sit creata sine forma. Licet enim omne creatum sit in actu, non tamen est actus purus. Unde oportet quod etiam illud quod se habet ex parte potentiae, sit creatum, si totum quod ad esse ipsius pertinet, creatum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0042","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod causa exemplaris sit aliquid praeter Deum. Exemplatum enim habet similitudinem exemplaris. Sed creaturae longe sunt a divina similitudine. Non ergo Deus est causa exemplaris earum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0044","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0045","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est per participationem, reducitur ad aliquid per se existens, ut ignitum ad ignem, sicut iam dictum est. Sed quaecumque sunt in sensibilibus rebus, sunt solum per participationem alicuius speciei, quod ex hoc patet, quod in nullo sensibilium invenitur solum id quod ad rationem speciei pertinet, sed adiunguntur principiis speciei principia individuantia. Oportet ergo ponere ipsas species per se existentes, ut per se hominem, et per se equum, et huiusmodi. Et haec dicuntur exemplaria. Sunt igitur exemplaria res quaedam extra Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0046","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0047","text":"Praeterea, scientiae et definitiones sunt de ipsis speciebus, non secundum quod sunt in particularibus, quia particularium non est scientia nec definitio. Ergo sunt quaedam entia, quae sunt entia vel species non in singularibus. Et haec dicuntur exemplaria. Ergo idem quod prius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0048","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0049","text":"Praeterea, hoc idem videtur per Dionysium, qui dicit, V cap. de Div. Nom., quod *ipsum secundum se esse, prius est eo quod est per se vitam esse, et eo quod est per se sapientiam esse*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0050","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0051","text":"Sed contra est quod exemplar est idem quod idea. Sed ideae, secundum quod Augustinus libro octoginta trium quaest. dicit, *sunt formae principales, quae divina intelligentia continentur*. Ergo exemplaria rerum non sunt extra Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0052","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0053","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus est prima causa exemplaris omnium rerum. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod ad productionem alicuius rei ideo necessarium est exemplar, ut effectus determinatam formam consequatur, artifex enim producit determinatam formam in materia, propter exemplar ad quod inspicit, sive illud sit exemplar ad quod extra intuetur, sive sit exemplar interius mente conceptum. Manifestum est autem quod ea quae naturaliter fiunt, determinatas formas consequuntur. Haec autem formarum determinatio oportet quod reducatur, sicut in primum principium, in divinam sapientiam, quae ordinem universi excogitavit, qui in rerum distinctione consistit. Et ideo oportet dicere quod in divina sapientia sunt rationes omnium rerum, quas supra diximus ideas, id est formas exemplares in mente divina existentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0054","text":"Quae quidem licet multiplicentur secundum respectum ad res, tamen non sunt realiter aliud a divina essentia, prout eius similitudo a diversis participari potest diversimode. Sic igitur ipse Deus est primum exemplar omnium. Possunt etiam in rebus creatis quaedam aliorum exemplaria dici, secundum quod quaedam sunt ad similitudinem aliorum, vel secundum eandem speciem, vel secundum analogiam alicuius imitationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0055","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0056","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet creaturae non pertingant ad hoc quod sint similes Deo secundum suam naturam, similitudine speciei, ut homo genitus homini generanti; attingunt tamen ad eius similitudinem secundum repraesentationem rationis intellectae a Deo, ut domus quae est in materia, domui quae est in mente artificis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0057","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0058","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod de ratione hominis est quod sit in materia, et sic non potest inveniri homo sine materia. Licet igitur hic homo sit per participationem speciei, non tamen potest reduci ad aliquid existens per se in eadem specie; sed ad speciem superexcedentem, sicut sunt substantiae separatae. Et eadem ratio est de aliis sensibilibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0059","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0060","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet quaelibet scientia et definitio sit solum entium, non tamen oportet quod res eundem modum habeant in essendo, quem intellectus habet in intelligendo. Nos enim, per virtutem intellectus agentis, abstrahimus species universales a particularibus conditionibus, non tamen oportet quod universalia praeter particularia subsistant, ut particularium exemplaria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0061","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0062","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Dionysius, XI cap. de Div. Nom., per se vitam et per se sapientiam quandoque nominat ipsum Deum, quandoque virtutes ipsis rebus datas, non autem quasdam subsistentes res, sicut antiqui posuerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0063","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0064","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0065","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non sit causa finalis omnium. Agere enim propter finem videtur esse alicuius indigentis fine. Sed Deus nullo est indigens. Ergo non competit sibi agere propter finem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0066","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0067","text":"Praeterea, finis generationis et forma generati et agens non incidunt in idem numero, ut dicitur in II Physic., quia finis generationis est forma generati. Sed Deus est primum agens omnium. Non ergo est causa finalis omnium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0068","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0069","text":"Praeterea, finem omnia appetunt. Sed Deum non omnia appetunt, quia neque omnia ipsum cognoscunt. Deus ergo non est omnium finis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0070","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0071","text":"Praeterea, finalis causa est prima causarum. Si igitur Deus sit causa agens et causa finalis, sequitur quod in eo sit prius et posterius. Quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0072","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Prov. XVI, *universa propter semetipsum operatus est dominus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0074","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod omne agens agit propter finem, alioquin ex actione agentis non magis sequeretur hoc quam illud, nisi a casu. Est autem idem finis agentis et patientis, inquantum huiusmodi, sed aliter et aliter, unum enim et idem est quod agens intendit imprimere, et quod patiens intendit recipere. Sunt autem quaedam quae simul agunt et patiuntur, quae sunt agentia imperfecta, et his convenit quod etiam in agendo intendant aliquid acquirere. Sed primo agenti, qui est agens tantum, non convenit agere propter acquisitionem alicuius finis; sed intendit solum communicare suam perfectionem, quae est eius bonitas. Et unaquaeque creatura intendit consequi suam perfectionem, quae est similitudo perfectionis et bonitatis divinae. Sic ergo divina bonitas est finis rerum omnium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0076","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0077","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod agere propter indigentiam non est nisi agentis imperfecti, quod natum est agere et pati. Sed hoc Deo non competit. Et ideo ipse solus est maxime liberalis, quia non agit propter suam utilitatem, sed solum propter suam bonitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0078","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0079","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod forma generati non est finis generationis nisi inquantum est similitudo formae generantis, quod suam similitudinem communicare intendit. Alioquin forma generati esset nobilior generante, cum finis sit nobilior his quae sunt ad finem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0080","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0081","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnia appetunt Deum ut finem, appetendo quodcumque bonum, sive appetitu intelligibili, sive sensibili, sive naturali, qui est sine cognitione, quia nihil habet rationem boni et appetibilis, nisi secundum quod participat Dei similitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0082","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0083","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, cum Deus sit causa efficiens, exemplaris et finalis omnium rerum, et materia prima sit ab ipso, sequitur quod primum principium omnium rerum sit unum tantum secundum rem. Nihil tamen prohibet in eo considerari multa secundum rationem, quorum quaedam prius cadunt in intellectu nostro quam alia.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"96dec965d650d7bb31f1ef764731882a46befac372bd7f77fa41d1c0ccf1adb0","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T142813319812Z_cf668e8a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 44","source_sha256":"96dec965d650d7bb31f1ef764731882a46befac372bd7f77fa41d1c0ccf1adb0","sha256":"9d492a526314ad5e9fef3cdc93841fded8770516a20b4ff83fac66f92eeeeb62","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:28:13.319829+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0002","text":"[I q. 44 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0003","text":"After the consideration of the divine persons, it remains to consider the procession of creatures from God. This consideration will be threefold: first, concerning the production of creatures; second, concerning their distinction; third, concerning their preservation and governance. Concerning the first, three things must be considered: first, what is the first cause of beings; second, concerning the manner in which creatures proceed from the first cause; third, concerning the principle of the duration of things. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether God is the efficient cause of all beings. Second, whether prime matter is created by God, or is a principle co-ordinated with Him on equal terms. Third, whether God is the exemplary cause of things, or whether there are other exemplars besides Him. Fourth, whether He Himself is the final cause of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0005","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0006","text":"The first point is approached thus. It seems that it is not necessary for every being to be created by God. For nothing prevents a thing from being found without that which does not belong to the essence of the thing, as a human being without whiteness. But the relation of the caused thing to its cause does not seem to belong to the essence of beings, because certain beings can be understood without this relation. Therefore, they can exist without it. Therefore, nothing prevents there being some beings not created by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0007","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0008","text":"Further, a thing needs an efficient cause in order that it may be. Therefore, that which cannot not be does not need an efficient cause. But no necessary thing can not be, because what necessarily is cannot not be. Therefore, since there are many necessary things among things, it seems that not all beings are from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0009","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0010","text":"Further, in the case of whatever things have some cause, a demonstration can be made through that cause. But in mathematics no demonstration is made through an agent cause, as is clear from the Philosopher in *Metaphysics* III. Therefore, not all beings are from God as from an agent cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0011","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Romans XI: *For from Him, and through Him, and in Him are all things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0013","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that everything which in any way is, is from God. For if something is found in something by participation, it must be caused in it by that to which it belongs essentially; just as iron becomes fiery through fire. Now it was shown above, when divine simplicity was being treated, that God is subsistent being itself. And again it was shown that subsistent being can be only one; just as, if whiteness were subsistent, there could be only one whiteness, since whitenesses are multiplied according to their recipients. It remains, therefore, that all things other than God are not their own being, but participate in being. Therefore, all things which are diversified according to a diverse participation of being, so as to be more or less perfectly, must be caused by one first being, which is most perfectly. Hence Plato too said that before every multitude it is necessary to posit unity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0015","text":"And Aristotle says, in *Metaphysics* II, that what is most being and most true is the cause of every being and of every truth, just as what is hottest is the cause of all heat.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0016","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although relation to a cause does not enter into the definition of a being which is caused, nevertheless it follows upon those things which belong to its essence, because from the fact that something is a being by participation, it follows that it is caused by another. Hence a being of this sort cannot exist without being caused, just as neither can a human being exist without being capable of laughter. But because being caused is not of the essence of being simply, for this reason some uncaused being is found.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0018","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that some, moved by this argument, were led to maintain that what is necessary has no cause, as is said in *Physics* VIII. But this plainly appears false in the demonstrative sciences, in which necessary principles are causes of necessary conclusions. And therefore Aristotle says, in *Metaphysics* V, that there are certain necessary things which have a cause of their necessity. Therefore, an agent cause is not required solely because the effect can fail to be, but because the effect would not be if the cause were not. For this conditional is true whether the antecedent and consequent are possible or impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0020","text":"[I q. 44 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that mathematical objects are taken as abstract according to reason, although they are not abstract according to being. But it belongs to each thing to have an agent cause insofar as it has being. Therefore, although mathematical objects have an agent cause, they nevertheless do not fall under the mathematician's consideration according to the relation which they have to an agent cause. And therefore in the mathematical sciences something is not demonstrated through an agent cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0023","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0024","text":"The second point is approached thus. It seems that prime matter is not created by God. For everything that is made is composed from a subject and from something else, as is said in *Physics* I. But prime matter has no subject. Therefore, prime matter cannot be made by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0025","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0026","text":"Moreover, action and passion are divided in opposition to one another. But just as God is the first active principle, so matter is the first passive principle. Therefore God and prime matter are two principles divided in opposition to one another, neither of which is from the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0027","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0028","text":"Moreover, every agent acts so as to produce something like itself, and thus, since every agent acts insofar as it is in act, it follows that everything made is in some way in act. But prime matter is only in potency, as such. Therefore it is contrary to the nature of prime matter that it should be made.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0029","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0030","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in Book XII of the Confessions: “You have made two things, Lord: one near to you, namely the angel; the other near to nothing, namely prime matter.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0031","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that the ancient philosophers entered gradually, and as it were step by step, into the knowledge of truth. For at the beginning, being as it were rather crude, they did not judge anything to be beings except sensible bodies. Of these, those who posited motion in them considered motion only according to certain accidents, for example according to rarity and density, aggregation and segregation. And, supposing the very substance of bodies to be uncreated, they assigned certain causes of such accidental changes, for example friendship, strife, intellect, or something of this sort. But proceeding further, they distinguished by intellect between substantial form and matter, which they posited to be uncreated; and they perceived that change takes place in bodies according to essential forms. And they posited certain more universal causes of these changes, such as the oblique circle, according to Aristotle, or ideas, according to Plato.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0033","text":"But it must be considered that matter is contracted by form to a determinate species; just as the substance of some species is contracted by an accident accruing to it to a determinate mode of being, as man is contracted by white. Both, therefore, considered being under a certain particular consideration, either insofar as it is this being, or insofar as it is such a being. And thus they assigned particular agent causes to things. And further, some raised themselves to consider being insofar as it is being, and considered the cause of things not only according as they are these or such, but according as they are beings. Therefore, that which is the cause of things insofar as they are beings must be the cause of things not only according as they are such through accidental forms, nor according as they are these through substantial forms, but also according to everything that pertains to their being in whatever way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0034","text":"And thus it is necessary to posit that even prime matter was created by the universal cause of beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0035","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher, in Book I of the Physics, speaks of particular coming-to-be, which is from form to form, whether accidental or substantial; but now we are speaking of things according to their emanation from the universal principle of being. From this emanation matter is not excluded, although it is excluded from the first mode of making.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0037","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0038","text":"To the second, it must be said that passion is the effect of action. Hence it is also reasonable that the first passive principle should be the effect of the first active principle, for every imperfect thing is caused by what is perfect. For the first principle must be most perfect, as Aristotle says in Book XII of the Metaphysics.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0039","text":"[I q. 44 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that that argument does not show that matter is not created, but that it is not created without form. For although everything created is in act, it is nevertheless not pure act. Hence that which stands on the side of potency must also be created, if everything that pertains to its being is created.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0042","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0043","text":"The third is approached thus. It seems that the exemplary cause is something besides God. For the thing exemplified has a likeness to the exemplar. But creatures are far removed from the divine likeness. Therefore God is not their exemplary cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0044","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0045","text":"Moreover, everything that exists by participation is reduced to something existing through itself, as what is ignited is reduced to fire, as has already been said. But whatever things are in sensible things are only by participation in some species; this is clear from the fact that in none of the sensible things is found only that which pertains to the nature of the species, but individuating principles are joined to the principles of the species. Therefore it is necessary to posit the species themselves as existing through themselves, such as man-in-itself and horse-in-itself, and the like. And these are called exemplars. Therefore exemplars are certain things outside God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0046","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0047","text":"Moreover, sciences and definitions concern the species themselves, not according as they are in particulars, because there is no science or definition of particulars. Therefore there are certain beings that are beings or species not in singulars. And these are called exemplars. Therefore the same as before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0048","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0049","text":"Moreover, this same point is evident through Dionysius, who says, in chapter V of *On the Divine Names*, that *being itself through itself is prior to living itself through itself, and to being wisdom itself through itself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0050","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0051","text":"On the contrary, an exemplar is the same as an idea. But ideas, as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *are principal forms, which are contained in the divine intelligence*. Therefore, the exemplars of things are not outside God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0052","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0053","text":"I answer that it must be said that God is the first exemplary cause of all things. To make this evident, it must be considered that an exemplar is necessary for the production of a thing in order that the effect may attain a determinate form; for an artisan produces a determinate form in matter on account of the exemplar at which he looks, whether it be an exemplar upon which he looks externally or an exemplar inwardly conceived in the mind. Now it is manifest that things which come to be naturally attain determinate forms. But this determination of forms must be reduced, as to its first principle, to divine wisdom, which devised the order of the universe, consisting in the distinction of things. Therefore, it must be said that in divine wisdom there are the intelligible accounts of all things, which we above called ideas, that is, exemplary forms existing in the divine mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0054","text":"Although these are multiplied according to their relation to things, they are nevertheless not really other than the divine essence, insofar as its likeness can be participated in diversely by diverse things. Thus, therefore, God himself is the first exemplar of all things. Certain things in created reality can also be called exemplars of others, insofar as some are according to the likeness of others, either according to the same species or according to an analogy of some imitation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0055","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0056","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although creatures do not attain to being like God according to His nature, by a likeness of species, as a man begotten is like the man begetting him, they nevertheless attain to His likeness according to the representation of the intelligible account understood by God, as the house that is in matter is like the house that is in the mind of the artisan.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0057","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0058","text":"To the second, it must be said that it belongs to the intelligible account of man that he be in matter, and thus a man cannot be found without matter. Therefore, although this man exists through participation in the species, he nevertheless cannot be reduced to something existing through itself in the same species, but to an exceeding species, such as the separate substances. And the same reasoning applies to other sensible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0059","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0060","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although every science and definition is only of beings, it is nevertheless not necessary that things have the same mode in being that the intellect has in understanding. For by the power of the agent intellect, we abstract universal species from particular conditions; nevertheless, it is not necessary that universals subsist apart from particulars as exemplars of particulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0061","text":"[I q. 44 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0062","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, as Dionysius says in chapter XI of *On the Divine Names*, by “life itself through itself” and “wisdom itself through itself” he sometimes designates God himself, sometimes powers given to things themselves, but not certain subsisting things, as the ancients held.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0063","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0064","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0065","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that God is not the final cause of all things. For to act for the sake of an end seems to belong to one who is in need of the end. But God is in need of nothing. Therefore, it does not belong to Him to act for the sake of an end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0066","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0067","text":"Moreover, the end of generation, the form of the thing generated, and the agent do not coincide in the same thing numerically, as is said in *Physics* II, because the end of generation is the form of the thing generated. But God is the first agent of all things. Therefore, He is not the final cause of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0068","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0069","text":"Moreover, all things desire an end. But not all things desire God, since neither do all things know Him. Therefore, God is not the end of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0070","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0071","text":"Moreover, the final cause is the first of causes. If, therefore, God is an efficient cause and a final cause, it follows that in Him there is prior and posterior. This is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0072","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0073","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Prov. XVI: *the Lord has made all things for himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0074","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0075","text":"I answer that it must be said that every agent acts for an end; otherwise, from the action of the agent this would follow no more than that, except by chance. Now the end of the agent and of the patient, insofar as such, is the same, but in different ways: for one and the same thing is what the agent intends to impress and what the patient intends to receive. But there are certain things which act and are acted upon at the same time; these are imperfect agents, and it belongs to them that, even in acting, they intend to acquire something. But it does not belong to the first agent, which is an agent only, to act for the acquisition of some end; rather, it intends only to communicate its perfection, which is its goodness. And each creature intends to attain its own perfection, which is a likeness of the divine perfection and goodness. Thus, therefore, the divine goodness is the end of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0076","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0077","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to act on account of need belongs only to an imperfect agent, which is naturally suited to act and to be acted upon. But this does not belong to God. And therefore he alone is most liberal, because he does not act for his own benefit, but solely on account of his goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0078","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0079","text":"To the second, it must be said that the form of the generated thing is not the end of generation except insofar as it is a likeness of the form of the generator, which intends to communicate its likeness. Otherwise, the form of the generated thing would be nobler than the generator, since the end is nobler than those things that are for the end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0080","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0081","text":"To the third, it must be said that all things desire God as their end, by desiring whatever good, whether by an intelligible, or a sensible, or a natural appetite—which is without cognition—because nothing has the character of good and desirable except insofar as it participates in a likeness of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0082","text":"[I q. 44 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0083","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, since God is the efficient, exemplary, and final cause of all things, and prime matter is from him, it follows that the first principle of all things is only one according to reality. Nevertheless, nothing prevents many things from being considered in it according to reason, some of which fall into our intellect prior to others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_36:P0083"]}],"source_digest":"26ebeb812f7a3e5763cb66556b8556000188a158d3363cec6f7d3c2270965d7f","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 44"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 44","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37","title":"First Part · Question 45","order":46,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_37","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 45 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30319)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de modo emanationis rerum a primo principio, qui dicitur creatio. De qua quaeruntur octo. Primo, quid sit creatio. Secundo, utrum Deus possit aliquid creare. Tertio, utrum creatio sit aliquod ens in rerum natura. Quarto, cui competit creari. Quinto, utrum solius Dei sit creare. Sexto, utrum commune sit toti Trinitati, aut proprium alicuius personae. Septimo, utrum vestigium aliquod Trinitatis sit in rebus creatis. Octavo, utrum opus creationis admisceatur in operibus naturae et voluntatis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30320)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creare non sit ex nihilo aliquid facere. Dicit enim Augustinus, contra adversarium legis et prophetarum, *facere est quod omnino non erat, creare vero est ex eo quod iam erat educendo aliquid constituere*.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30321)\n\nPraeterea, nobilitas actionis et motus ex terminis consideratur. Nobilior igitur est actio quae ex bono in bonum est, et ex ente in ens, quam quae est ex nihilo in aliquid. Sed creatio videtur esse nobilissima actio, et prima inter omnes actiones. Ergo non est ex nihilo in aliquid, sed magis ex ente in ens.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30322)\n\nPraeterea, haec praepositio ex importat habitudinem alicuius causae, et maxime materialis; sicut cum dicimus quod statua fit ex aere. Sed nihil non potest esse materia entis, nec aliquo modo causa eius. Ergo creare non est ex nihilo aliquid facere.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30323)\n\nSed contra est quod super illud Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum* etc., dicit Glossa quod creare est aliquid ex nihilo facere.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30324)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, non solum oportet considerare emanationem alicuius entis particularis ab aliquo particulari agente, sed etiam emanationem totius entis a causa universali, quae est Deus, et hanc quidem emanationem designamus nomine creationis. Quod autem procedit secundum emanationem particularem, non praesupponitur emanationi, sicut, si generatur homo, non fuit prius homo, sed homo fit ex non homine, et album ex non albo. Unde, si consideretur emanatio totius entis universalis a primo principio, impossibile est quod aliquod ens praesupponatur huic emanationi. Idem autem est nihil quod nullum ens. Sicut igitur generatio hominis est ex non ente quod est non homo, ita creatio, quae est emanatio totius esse, est ex non ente quod est nihil.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30325)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus aequivoce utitur nomine creationis, secundum quod creari dicuntur ea quae in melius reformantur, ut cum dicitur aliquis creari in episcopum. Sic autem non loquimur hic de creatione, sed sicut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30326)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod mutationes accipiunt speciem et dignitatem non a termino a quo, sed a termino ad quem. Tanto ergo perfectior et prior est aliqua mutatio, quanto terminus ad quem illius mutationis est nobilior et prior; licet terminus a quo, qui opponitur termino ad quem, sit imperfectior. Sicut generatio simpliciter est nobilior et prior quam alteratio, propter hoc quod forma substantialis est nobilior quam forma accidentalis, tamen privatio substantialis formae, quae est terminus a quo in generatione, est imperfectior quam contrarium, quod est terminus a quo in alteratione. Et similiter creatio est perfectior et prior quam generatio et alteratio, quia terminus ad quem est tota substantia rei. Id autem quod intelligitur ut terminus a quo, est simpliciter non ens.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30327)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur aliquid ex nihilo fieri, haec praepositio ex non designat causam materialem, sed ordinem tantum; sicut cum dicitur, ex mane fit meridies, idest, post mane fit meridies. Sed intelligendum est quod haec praepositio ex potest includere negationem importatam in hoc quod dico nihil, vel includi ab ea. Si primo modo, tunc ordo remanet affirmatus, et ostenditur ordo eius, quod est ad non esse praecedens. Si vero negatio includat praepositionem, tunc ordo negatur, et est sensus, fit ex nihilo, idest non fit ex aliquo; sicut si dicatur, iste loquitur de nihilo, quia non loquitur de aliquo. Et utroque modo verificatur, cum dicitur ex nihilo aliquid fieri. Sed primo modo, haec praepositio ex importat ordinem, ut dictum est, secundo modo, importat habitudinem causae materialis, quae negatur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30328)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit aliquid creare. Quia secundum philosophum, I Physic., antiqui philosophi acceperunt ut communem conceptionem animi, ex nihilo nihil fieri. Sed potentia Dei non se extendit ad contraria primorum principiorum; utpote quod Deus faciat quod totum non sit maius sua parte, vel quod affirmatio et negatio sint simul vera. Ergo Deus non potest aliquid ex nihilo facere, vel creare.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30329)\n\nPraeterea, si creare est aliquid ex nihilo facere, ergo creari est aliquid fieri. Sed omne fieri est mutari. Ergo creatio est mutatio. Sed omnis mutatio est ex subiecto aliquo, ut patet per definitionem motus, nam motus est actus existentis in potentia. Ergo est impossibile aliquid a Deo ex nihilo fieri.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30330)\n\nPraeterea, quod factum est, necesse est aliquando fieri. Sed non potest dici quod illud quod creatur, simul fiat et factum sit, quia in permanentibus, quod fit, non est, quod autem factum est, iam est; simul ergo aliquid esset et non esset. Ergo, si aliquid fit, fieri eius praecedit factum esse. Sed hoc non potest esse, nisi praeexistat subiectum in quo sustentetur ipsum fieri. Ergo impossibile est aliquid fieri ex nihilo.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30331)\n\nPraeterea, infinitam distantiam non est pertransire. Sed infinita distantia est inter ens et nihil. Ergo non contingit ex nihilo aliquid fieri.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30332)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30333)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod non solum non est impossibile a Deo aliquid creari, sed necesse est ponere a Deo omnia creata esse, ut ex praemissis habetur. Quicumque enim facit aliquid ex aliquo, illud ex quo facit praesupponitur actioni eius, et non producitur per ipsam actionem, sicut artifex operatur ex rebus naturalibus, ut ex ligno et aere, quae per artis actionem non causantur, sed causantur per actionem naturae. Sed et ipsa natura causat res naturales quantum ad formam, sed praesupponit materiam. Si ergo Deus non ageret nisi ex aliquo praesupposito, sequeretur quod illud praesuppositum non esset causatum ab ipso. Ostensum est autem supra quod nihil potest esse in entibus quod non sit a Deo, qui est causa universalis totius esse. Unde necesse est dicere quod Deus ex nihilo res in esse producit.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30334)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod antiqui philosophi, sicut supra dictum est, non consideraverunt nisi emanationem effectuum particularium a causis particularibus, quas necesse est praesupponere aliquid in sua actione, et secundum hoc erat eorum communis opinio, ex nihilo nihil fieri. Sed tamen hoc locum non habet in prima emanatione ab universali rerum principio.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30335)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod creatio non est mutatio nisi secundum modum intelligendi tantum. Nam de ratione mutationis est, quod aliquid idem se habeat aliter nunc et prius, nam quandoque est idem ens actu, aliter se habens nunc et prius, sicut in motibus secundum quantitatem et qualitatem et ubi; quandoque vero est idem ens in potentia tantum, sicut in mutatione secundum substantiam, cuius subiectum est materia. Sed in creatione, per quam producitur tota substantia rerum, non potest accipi aliquid idem aliter se habens nunc et prius, nisi secundum intellectum tantum; sicut si intelligatur aliqua res prius non fuisse totaliter, et postea esse. Sed cum actio et passio conveniant in substantia motus, et differant solum secundum habitudines diversas, ut dicitur in III Physic., oportet quod, subtracto motu, non remaneant nisi diversae habitudines in creante et creato. Sed quia modus significandi sequitur modum intelligendi, ut dictum est, creatio significatur per modum mutationis, et propter hoc dicitur quod creare est ex nihilo aliquid facere. Quamvis facere et fieri magis in hoc conveniant quam mutare et mutari, quia facere et fieri important habitudinem causae ad effectum et effectus ad causam, sed mutationem ex consequenti.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30336)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in his quae fiunt sine motu, simul est fieri et factum esse, sive talis factio sit terminus motus, sicut illuminatio (nam simul aliquid illuminatur et illuminatum est); sive non sit terminus motus, sicut simul formatur verbum in corde et formatum est. Et in his, quod fit, est, sed cum dicitur fieri, significatur ab alio esse, et prius non fuisse. Unde, cum creatio sit sine motu, simul aliquid creatur et creatum est.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30337)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit ex falsa imaginatione, ac si sit aliquod infinitum medium inter nihilum et ens, quod patet esse falsum. Procedit autem falsa haec imaginatio ex eo quod creatio significatur ut quaedam mutatio inter duos terminos existens.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30338)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatio non sit aliquid in creatura. Sicut enim creatio passive accepta attribuitur creaturae, ita creatio active accepta attribuitur creatori. Sed creatio active accepta non est aliquid in creatore, quia sic sequeretur quod in Deo esset aliquid temporale. Ergo creatio passive accepta non est aliquid in creatura.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30339)\n\nPraeterea, nihil est medium inter creatorem et creaturam. Sed creatio significatur ut medium inter utrumque, non enim est creator, cum non sit aeterna; neque creatura, quia oporteret eadem ratione aliam ponere creationem qua ipsa crearetur, et sic in infinitum. Creatio ergo non est aliquid.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30340)\n\nPraeterea, si creatio est aliquid praeter substantiam creatam, oportet quod sit accidens eius. Omne autem accidens est in subiecto. Ergo res creata esset subiectum creationis. Et sic idem esset subiectum creationis et terminus. Quod est impossibile, quia subiectum prius est accidente, et conservat accidens; terminus autem posterius est actione et passione cuius est terminus, et eo existente cessat actio et passio. Igitur ipsa creatio non est aliqua res.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30341)\n\nSed contra, maius est fieri aliquid secundum totam substantiam, quam secundum formam substantialem vel accidentalem. Sed generatio simpliciter vel secundum quid, qua fit aliquid secundum formam substantialem vel accidentalem, est aliquid in generato. Ergo multo magis creatio, qua fit aliquid secundum totam substantiam, est aliquid in creato.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30342)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod creatio ponit aliquid in creato secundum relationem tantum. Quia quod creatur, non fit per motum vel per mutationem. Quod enim fit per motum vel mutationem, fit ex aliquo praeexistenti, quod quidem contingit in productionibus particularibus aliquorum entium; non autem potest hoc contingere in productione totius esse a causa universali omnium entium, quae est Deus. Unde Deus, creando, producit res sine motu. Subtracto autem motu ab actione et passione, nihil remanet nisi relatio, ut dictum est. Unde relinquitur quod creatio in creatura non sit nisi relatio quaedam ad creatorem, ut ad principium sui esse; sicut in passione quae est cum motu, importatur relatio ad principium motus.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30343)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod creatio active significata significat actionem divinam, quae est eius essentia cum relatione ad creaturam. Sed relatio in Deo ad creaturam non est realis, sed secundum rationem tantum. Relatio vero creaturae ad Deum est relatio realis, ut supra dictum est, cum de divinis nominibus ageretur.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30344)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quia creatio significatur ut mutatio, sicut dictum est; mutatio autem media quodammodo est inter movens et motum, ideo etiam creatio significatur ut media inter creatorem et creaturam. Tamen creatio passive accepta est in creatura, et est creatura. Neque tamen oportet quod alia creatione creetur, quia relationes, cum hoc ipsum quod sunt, ad aliquid dicantur, non referuntur per aliquas alias relationes, sed per seipsas; sicut etiam supra dictum est, cum de aequalitate personarum ageretur.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30345)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod creationis, secundum quod significatur ut mutatio, creatura est terminus, sed secundum quod vere est relatio, creatura est eius subiectum, et prius ea in esse, sicut subiectum accidente. Sed habet quandam rationem prioritatis ex parte obiecti ad quod dicitur, quod est principium creaturae. Neque tamen oportet quod, quandiu creatura sit, dicatur creari, quia creatio importat habitudinem creaturae ad creatorem cum quadam novitate seu incoeptione.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30346)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creari non sit proprium compositorum et subsistentium. Dicitur enim in libro de causis, prima rerum creatarum est esse. Sed esse rei creatae non est subsistens. Ergo creatio proprie non est subsistentis et compositi.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30347)\n\nPraeterea, quod creatur est ex nihilo. Composita autem non sunt ex nihilo, sed ex suis componentibus. Ergo compositis non convenit creari.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30348)\n\nPraeterea, illud proprie producitur per primam emanationem, quod supponitur in secunda, sicut res naturalis per generationem naturalem, quae supponitur in operatione artis. Sed illud quod supponitur in generatione naturali, est materia. Ergo materia est quae proprie creatur, et non compositum.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30349)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Caelum autem et terra sunt res compositae subsistentes. Ergo horum proprie est creatio.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30350)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod creari est quoddam fieri, ut dictum est. Fieri autem ordinatur ad esse rei. Unde illis proprie convenit fieri et creari, quibus convenit esse. Quod quidem convenit proprie subsistentibus, sive sint simplicia, sicut substantiae separatae; sive sint composita, sicut substantiae materiales. Illi enim proprie convenit esse, quod habet esse; et hoc est subsistens in suo esse. Formae autem et accidentia, et alia huiusmodi, non dicuntur entia quasi ipsa sint, sed quia eis aliquid est; ut albedo ea ratione dicitur ens, quia ea subiectum est album. Unde, secundum philosophum, accidens magis proprie dicitur entis quam ens. Sicut igitur accidentia et formae, et huiusmodi, quae non subsistunt, magis sunt coexistentia quam entia; ita magis debent dici concreata quam creata. Proprie vero creata sunt subsistentia.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30351)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur, prima rerum creatarum est esse, ly esse non importat subiectum creatum; sed importat propriam rationem obiecti creationis. Nam ex eo dicitur aliquid creatum, quod est ens, non ex eo quod est hoc ens, cum creatio sit emanatio totius esse ab ente universali, ut dictum est. Et est similis modus loquendi, sicut si diceretur quod primum visibile est color, quamvis illud quod proprie videtur, sit coloratum.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30352)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod creatio non dicit constitutionem rei compositae ex principiis praeexistentibus, sed compositum sic dicitur creari, quod simul cum omnibus suis principiis in esse producitur.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30353)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa non probat quod sola materia creetur; sed quod materia non sit nisi ex creatione. Nam creatio est productio totius esse, et non solum materiae.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30354)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non solius Dei sit creare. Quia secundum philosophum, perfectum est quod potest sibi simile facere. Sed creaturae immateriales sunt perfectiores creaturis materialibus, quae faciunt sibi simile, ignis enim generat ignem, et homo generat hominem. Ergo substantia immaterialis potest facere substantiam sibi similem. Sed substantia immaterialis non potest fieri nisi per creationem, cum non habeat materiam ex qua fiat. Ergo aliqua creatura potest creare.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30355)\n\nPraeterea, quanto maior est resistentia ex parte facti, tanto maior virtus requiritur in faciente. Sed plus resistit contrarium quam nihil. Ergo maioris virtutis est aliquid facere ex contrario, quod tamen creatura facit; quam aliquid facere ex nihilo. Multo magis igitur creatura hoc facere potest.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30356)\n\nPraeterea, virtus facientis consideratur secundum mensuram eius quod fit. Sed ens creatum est finitum, ut supra probatum est, cum de Dei infinitate ageretur. Ergo ad producendum per creationem aliquid creatum, non requiritur nisi virtus finita. Sed habere virtutem finitam non est contra rationem creaturae. Ergo non est impossibile creaturam creare.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30357)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in III de Trin., quod neque boni neque mali Angeli possunt esse creatores alicuius rei. Multo minus igitur aliae creaturae.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30358)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod satis apparet in primo aspectu, secundum praemissa, quod creare non potest esse propria actio nisi solius Dei. Oportet enim universaliores effectus in universaliores et priores causas reducere. Inter omnes autem effectus, universalissimum est ipsum esse. Unde oportet quod sit proprius effectus primae et universalissimae causae, quae est Deus. Unde etiam dicitur libro de causis, quod neque intelligentia vel anima nobilis dat esse, nisi inquantum operatur operatione divina. Producere autem esse absolute, non inquantum est hoc vel tale, pertinet ad rationem creationis. Unde manifestum est quod creatio est propria actio ipsius Dei. Contingit autem quod aliquid participet actionem propriam alicuius alterius, non virtute propria, sed instrumentaliter, inquantum agit in virtute alterius; sicut aer per virtutem ignis habet calefacere et ignire. Et secundum hoc, aliqui opinati sunt quod, licet creatio sit propria actio universalis causae, tamen aliqua inferiorum causarum inquantum agit in virtute primae causae, potest creare. Et sic posuit Avicenna quod prima substantia separata, creata a Deo, creat aliam post se, et substantiam orbis, et animam eius; et quod substantia orbis creat materiam inferiorum corporum. Et secundum hunc etiam modum Magister dicit, in V dist. IV Sent., quod Deus potest creaturae communicare potentiam creandi, ut creet per ministerium, non propria auctoritate. Sed hoc esse non potest. Quia causa secunda instrumentalis non participat actionem causae superioris, nisi inquantum per aliquid sibi proprium dispositive operatur ad effectum principalis agentis. Si igitur nihil ibi ageret secundum illud quod est sibi proprium, frustra adhiberetur ad agendum, nec oporteret esse determinata instrumenta determinatarum actionum. Sic enim videmus quod securis, scindendo lignum, quod habet ex proprietate suae formae, producit scamni formam, quae est effectus proprius principalis agentis. Illud autem quod est proprius effectus Dei creantis, est illud quod praesupponitur omnibus aliis, scilicet esse absolute. Unde non potest aliquid operari dispositive et instrumentaliter ad hunc effectum, cum creatio non sit ex aliquo praesupposito, quod possit disponi per actionem instrumentalis agentis. Sic igitur impossibile est quod alicui creaturae conveniat creare, neque virtute propria, neque instrumentaliter sive per ministerium. Et hoc praecipue inconveniens est dici de aliquo corpore, quod creet, cum nullum corpus agat nisi tangendo vel movendo; et sic requirit in sua actione aliquid praeexistens, quod possit tangi et moveri; quod est contra rationem creationis.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30359)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aliquod perfectum participans aliquam naturam, facit sibi simile, non quidem producendo absolute illam naturam, sed applicando eam ad aliquid. Non enim hic homo potest esse causa naturae humanae absolute, quia sic esset causa sui ipsius, sed est causa quod natura humana sit in hoc homine generato. Et sic praesupponit in sua actione determinatam materiam per quam est hic homo. Sed sicut hic homo participat humanam naturam, ita quodcumque ens creatum participat, ut ita dixerim, naturam essendi, quia solus Deus est suum esse, ut supra dictum est. Nullum igitur ens creatum potest producere aliquod ens absolute, nisi inquantum esse causat in hoc, et sic oportet quod praeintelligatur id per quod aliquid est hoc, actioni qua facit sibi simile. In substantia autem immateriali non potest praeintelligi aliquid per quod sit haec, quia est haec per suam formam, per quam habet esse, cum sint formae subsistentes. Igitur substantia immaterialis non potest producere aliam substantiam immaterialem sibi similem, quantum ad esse eius; sed quantum ad perfectionem aliquam superadditam; sicut si dicamus quod superior Angelus illuminat inferiorem, ut Dionysius dicit. Secundum quem modum etiam in caelestibus est paternitas, ut ex verbis apostoli patet, Ephes. III, *ex quo omnis paternitas in caelo et in terra nominatur*. Et ex hoc etiam evidenter apparet quod nullum ens creatum potest causare aliquid, nisi praesupposito aliquo. Quod repugnat rationi creationis.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30360)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ex contrario fit aliquid per accidens, ut dicitur in I Physic., per se autem fit aliquid ex subiecto, quod est in potentia. Contrarium igitur resistit agenti, inquantum impedit potentiam ab actu in quem intendit reducere agens, sicut ignis intendit reducere materiam aquae in actum sibi similem, sed impeditur per formam et dispositiones contrarias, quibus quasi ligatur potentia ne reducatur in actum. Et quanto magis fuerit potentia ligata, tanto requiritur maior virtus in agente ad reducendam materiam in actum. Unde multo maior potentia requiritur in agente, si nulla potentia praeexistat. Sic ergo patet quod multo maioris virtutis est facere aliquid ex nihilo, quam ex contrario.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30361)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod virtus facientis non solum consideratur ex substantia facti, sed etiam ex modo faciendi, maior enim calor non solum magis, sed etiam citius calefacit. Quamvis igitur creare aliquem effectum finitum non demonstret potentiam infinitam, tamen creare ipsum ex nihilo demonstrat potentiam infinitam. Quod ex praedictis patet. Si enim tanto maior virtus requiritur in agente, quanto potentia est magis remota ab actu, oportet quod virtus agentis ex nulla praesupposita potentia, quale agens est creans, sit infinita, quia nulla proportio est nullius potentiae ad aliquam potentiam, quam praesupponit virtus agentis naturalis, sicut et non entis ad ens. Et quia nulla creatura habet simpliciter potentiam infinitam, sicut neque esse infinitum, ut supra probatum est, relinquitur quod nulla creatura possit creare.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30362)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creare sit proprium alicuius personae. Quod enim est prius, est causa eius quod est post; et perfectum imperfecti. Sed processio divinae personae est prior quam processio creaturae, et magis perfecta, quia divina persona procedit in perfecta similitudine sui principii, creatura vero in imperfecta. Ergo processiones divinarum personarum sunt causa processionis rerum. Et sic creare est proprium personae.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30363)\n\nPraeterea, personae divinae non distinguuntur ab invicem nisi per suas processiones et relationes. Quidquid igitur differenter attribuitur divinis personis, hoc convenit eis secundum processiones et relationes personarum. Sed causalitas creaturarum diversimode attribuitur divinis personis, nam in symbolo fidei patri attribuitur quod sit creator omnium visibilium et invisibilium; filio autem attribuitur quod per eum omnia facta sunt; sed spiritui sancto, quod sit dominus et vivificator. Causalitas ergo creaturarum convenit personis secundum processiones et relationes.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30364)\n\nPraeterea, si dicatur quod causalitas creaturae attenditur secundum aliquod attributum essentiale quod appropriatur alicui personae, hoc non videtur sufficiens. Quia quilibet effectus divinus causatur a quolibet attributo essentiali, scilicet potentia, bonitate et sapientia, et sic non magis pertinet ad unum quam ad aliud. Non deberet ergo aliquis determinatus modus causalitatis attribui uni personae magis quam alii, nisi distinguerentur in creando secundum relationes et processiones.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30365)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, II cap. de Div. Nom., quod communia totius divinitatis sunt omnia causalia.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30366)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod creare est proprie causare sive producere esse rerum. Cum autem omne agens agat sibi simile, principium actionis considerari potest ex actionis effectu, ignis enim est qui generat ignem. Et ideo creare convenit Deo secundum suum esse, quod est eius essentia, quae est communis tribus personis. Unde creare non est proprium alicui personae, sed commune toti Trinitati. Sed tamen divinae personae secundum rationem suae processionis habent causalitatem respectu creationis rerum. Ut enim supra ostensum est, cum de Dei scientia et voluntate ageretur, Deus est causa rerum per suum intellectum et voluntatem, sicut artifex rerum artificiatarum. Artifex autem per verbum in intellectu conceptum, et per amorem suae voluntatis ad aliquid relatum, operatur. Unde et Deus pater operatus est creaturam per suum verbum, quod est filius; et per suum amorem, qui est spiritus sanctus. Et secundum hoc processiones personarum sunt rationes productionis creaturarum, inquantum includunt essentialia attributa, quae sunt scientia et voluntas.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30367)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod processiones divinarum personarum sunt causa creationis sicut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30368)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut natura divina, licet sit communis tribus personis, ordine tamen quodam eis convenit, inquantum filius accipit naturam divinam a patre, et spiritus sanctus ab utroque; ita etiam et virtus creandi, licet sit communis tribus personis, ordine tamen quodam eis convenit; nam filius habet eam a patre, et spiritus sanctus ab utroque. Unde creatorem esse attribuitur patri, ut ei qui non habet virtutem creandi ab alio. De filio autem dicitur per quem omnia facta sunt, inquantum habet eandem virtutem, sed ab alio, nam haec praepositio per solet denotare causam mediam, sive principium de principio. Sed spiritui sancto, qui habet eandem virtutem ab utroque, attribuitur quod dominando gubernet, et vivificet quae sunt creata a patre per filium. Potest etiam huius attributionis communis ratio accipi ex appropriatione essentialium attributorum. Nam, sicut supra dictum est, patri appropriatur potentia, quae maxime manifestatur in creatione, et ideo attribuitur patri creatorem esse. Filio autem appropriatur sapientia, per quam agens per intellectum operatur, et ideo dicitur de filio, per quem omnia facta sunt. Spiritui sancto autem appropriatur bonitas, ad quam pertinet gubernatio deducens res in debitos fines, et vivificatio, nam vita in interiori quodam motu consistit, primum autem movens est finis et bonitas.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30369)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet quilibet effectus Dei procedat ex quolibet attributorum, tamen reducitur unusquisque effectus ad illud attributum, cum quo habet convenientiam secundum propriam rationem, sicut ordinatio rerum ad sapientiam, et iustificatio impii ad misericordiam et bonitatem se superabundanter diffundentem. Creatio vero, quae est productio ipsius substantiae rei, reducitur ad potentiam.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30370)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in creaturis non sit necesse inveniri vestigium Trinitatis. Per sua enim vestigia unumquodque investigari potest. Sed Trinitas personarum non potest investigari ex creaturis, ut supra habitum est. Ergo vestigia Trinitatis non sunt in creatura.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30371)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid in creatura est, creatum est. Si igitur vestigium Trinitatis invenitur in creatura secundum aliquas proprietates suas, et omne creatum habet vestigium Trinitatis, oportet in unaquaque illarum inveniri etiam vestigium Trinitatis, et sic in infinitum.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30372)\n\nPraeterea, effectus non repraesentat nisi suam causam. Sed causalitas creaturarum pertinet ad naturam communem, non autem ad relationes, quibus personae distinguuntur et numerantur. Ergo in creatura non invenitur vestigium Trinitatis, sed solum unitatis essentiae.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30373)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod *Trinitatis vestigium in creatura apparet*.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30374)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod omnis effectus aliqualiter repraesentat suam causam, sed diversimode. Nam aliquis effectus repraesentat solam causalitatem causae, non autem formam eius, sicut fumus repraesentat ignem, et talis repraesentatio dicitur esse repraesentatio vestigii; vestigium enim demonstrat motum alicuius transeuntis, sed non qualis sit. Aliquis autem effectus repraesentat causam quantum ad similitudinem formae eius, sicut ignis generatus ignem generantem, et statua Mercurii Mercurium, et haec est repraesentatio imaginis. Processiones autem divinarum personarum attenduntur secundum actus intellectus et voluntatis, sicut supra dictum est, nam filius procedit ut verbum intellectus, spiritus sanctus ut amor voluntatis. In creaturis igitur rationalibus, in quibus est intellectus et voluntas, invenitur repraesentatio Trinitatis per modum imaginis, inquantum invenitur in eis verbum conceptum et amor procedens. Sed in creaturis omnibus invenitur repraesentatio Trinitatis per modum vestigii, inquantum in qualibet creatura inveniuntur aliqua quae necesse est reducere in divinas personas sicut in causam. Quaelibet enim creatura subsistit in suo esse, et habet formam per quam determinatur ad speciem, et habet ordinem ad aliquid aliud. Secundum igitur quod est quaedam substantia creata, repraesentat causam et principium, et sic demonstrat personam patris, qui est principium non de principio. Secundum autem quod habet quandam formam et speciem, repraesentat verbum; secundum quod forma artificiati est ex conceptione artificis. Secundum autem quod habet ordinem, repraesentat spiritum sanctum, inquantum est amor, quia ordo effectus ad aliquid alterum est ex voluntate creantis. Et ideo dicit Augustinus, in VI Lib. de Trin., quod vestigium Trinitatis invenitur in unaquaque creatura, secundum quod unum aliquid est, et secundum quod aliqua specie formatur, et secundum quod quendam ordinem tenet. Et ad haec etiam reducuntur illa tria, numerus, pondus et mensura, quae ponuntur Sap. XI, nam mensura refertur ad substantiam rei limitatam suis principiis, numerus ad speciem, pondus ad ordinem. Et ad haec etiam reducuntur alia tria quae ponit Augustinus, modus species et ordo. Et ea quae ponit in libro octoginta trium quaest. quod constat, quod discernitur, quod congruit, constat enim aliquid per suam substantiam, discernitur per formam, congruit per ordinem. Et in idem de facili reduci possunt quaecumque sic dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30375)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod repraesentatio vestigii attenditur secundum appropriata, per quem modum ex creaturis in Trinitatem divinarum personarum veniri potest, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30376)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod creatura est res proprie subsistens, in qua est praedicta tria invenire. Neque oportet quod in quolibet eorum quae ei insunt, haec tria inveniantur, sed secundum ea vestigium rei subsistenti attribuitur.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30377)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod etiam processiones personarum sunt causa et ratio creationis aliquo modo, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30378)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatio admisceatur in operibus naturae et artis. In qualibet enim operatione naturae et artis producitur aliqua forma. Sed non producitur ex aliquo, cum non habeat materiam partem sui. Ergo producitur ex nihilo. Et sic in qualibet operatione naturae et artis est creatio.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30379)\n\nPraeterea, effectus non est potior sua causa. Sed in rebus naturalibus non invenitur aliquid agens nisi forma accidentalis, quae est forma activa vel passiva. Non ergo per operationem naturae producitur forma substantialis. Relinquitur igitur quod sit per creationem.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30380)\n\nPraeterea, natura facit sibi simile. Sed quaedam inveniuntur generata in natura non ab aliquo sibi simili, sicut patet in animalibus generatis per putrefactionem. Ergo eorum forma non est a natura, sed a creatione. Et eadem ratio est de aliis.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30381)\n\nPraeterea, quod non creatur, non est creatura. Si igitur in his quae sunt a natura non adiungatur creatio, sequitur quod ea quae sunt a natura, non sunt creaturae. Quod est haereticum.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30382)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus, super Gen. ad Lit., distinguit opus propagationis, quod est opus naturae, ab opere creationis.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30383)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod haec dubitatio inducitur propter formas. Quas quidam posuerunt non incipere per actionem naturae, sed prius in materia extitisse, ponentes latitationem formarum. Et hoc accidit eis ex ignorantia materiae, quia nesciebant distinguere inter potentiam et actum, quia enim formae praeexistunt in materia in potentia, posuerunt eas simpliciter praeexistere. Alii vero posuerunt formas dari vel causari ab agente separato, per modum creationis. Et secundum hoc cuilibet operationi naturae adiungitur creatio. Sed hoc accidit eis ex ignorantia formae. Non enim considerabant quod forma naturalis corporis non est subsistens, sed quo aliquid est, et ideo, cum fieri et creari non conveniat proprie nisi rei subsistenti, sicut supra dictum est, formarum non est fieri neque creari, sed concreata esse. Quod autem proprie fit ab agente naturali, est compositum, quod fit ex materia. Unde in operibus naturae non admiscetur creatio, sed praesupponitur ad operationem naturae.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30384)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod formae incipiunt esse in actu, compositis factis, non quod ipsae fiant per se, sed per accidens tantum.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30385)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod qualitates activae in natura agunt in virtute formarum substantialium. Et ideo agens naturale non solum producit sibi simile secundum qualitatem, sed secundum speciem.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30386)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ad generationem animalium imperfectorum sufficit agens universale, quod est virtus caelestis, cui assimilantur non secundum speciem, sed secundum analogiam quandam, neque oportet dicere quod eorum formae creantur ab agente separato. Ad generationem vero animalium perfectorum non sufficit agens universale, sed requiritur agens proprium, quod est generans univocum.\n\n[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30387)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod operatio naturae non est nisi ex praesuppositione principiorum creatorum, et sic ea quae per naturam fiunt, creaturae dicuntur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0002","text":"[I q. 45 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de modo emanationis rerum a primo principio, qui dicitur creatio. De qua quaeruntur octo. Primo, quid sit creatio. Secundo, utrum Deus possit aliquid creare. Tertio, utrum creatio sit aliquod ens in rerum natura. Quarto, cui competit creari. Quinto, utrum solius Dei sit creare. Sexto, utrum commune sit toti Trinitati, aut proprium alicuius personae. Septimo, utrum vestigium aliquod Trinitatis sit in rebus creatis. Octavo, utrum opus creationis admisceatur in operibus naturae et voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0005","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creare non sit ex nihilo aliquid facere. Dicit enim Augustinus, contra adversarium legis et prophetarum, *facere est quod omnino non erat, creare vero est ex eo quod iam erat educendo aliquid constituere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0007","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0008","text":"Praeterea, nobilitas actionis et motus ex terminis consideratur. Nobilior igitur est actio quae ex bono in bonum est, et ex ente in ens, quam quae est ex nihilo in aliquid. Sed creatio videtur esse nobilissima actio, et prima inter omnes actiones. Ergo non est ex nihilo in aliquid, sed magis ex ente in ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0009","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0010","text":"Praeterea, haec praepositio ex importat habitudinem alicuius causae, et maxime materialis; sicut cum dicimus quod statua fit ex aere. Sed nihil non potest esse materia entis, nec aliquo modo causa eius. Ergo creare non est ex nihilo aliquid facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0011","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod super illud Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum* etc., dicit Glossa quod creare est aliquid ex nihilo facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0013","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, non solum oportet considerare emanationem alicuius entis particularis ab aliquo particulari agente, sed etiam emanationem totius entis a causa universali, quae est Deus, et hanc quidem emanationem designamus nomine creationis. Quod autem procedit secundum emanationem particularem, non praesupponitur emanationi, sicut, si generatur homo, non fuit prius homo, sed homo fit ex non homine, et album ex non albo. Unde, si consideretur emanatio totius entis universalis a primo principio, impossibile est quod aliquod ens praesupponatur huic emanationi. Idem autem est nihil quod nullum ens. Sicut igitur generatio hominis est ex non ente quod est non homo, ita creatio, quae est emanatio totius esse, est ex non ente quod est nihil.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0015","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus aequivoce utitur nomine creationis, secundum quod creari dicuntur ea quae in melius reformantur, ut cum dicitur aliquis creari in episcopum. Sic autem non loquimur hic de creatione, sed sicut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0017","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod mutationes accipiunt speciem et dignitatem non a termino a quo, sed a termino ad quem. Tanto ergo perfectior et prior est aliqua mutatio, quanto terminus ad quem illius mutationis est nobilior et prior; licet terminus a quo, qui opponitur termino ad quem, sit imperfectior. Sicut generatio simpliciter est nobilior et prior quam alteratio, propter hoc quod forma substantialis est nobilior quam forma accidentalis, tamen privatio substantialis formae, quae est terminus a quo in generatione, est imperfectior quam contrarium, quod est terminus a quo in alteratione. Et similiter creatio est perfectior et prior quam generatio et alteratio, quia terminus ad quem est tota substantia rei. Id autem quod intelligitur ut terminus a quo, est simpliciter non ens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0019","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0020","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur aliquid ex nihilo fieri, haec praepositio ex non designat causam materialem, sed ordinem tantum; sicut cum dicitur, ex mane fit meridies, idest, post mane fit meridies. Sed intelligendum est quod haec praepositio ex potest includere negationem importatam in hoc quod dico nihil, vel includi ab ea. Si primo modo, tunc ordo remanet affirmatus, et ostenditur ordo eius, quod est ad non esse praecedens. Si vero negatio includat praepositionem, tunc ordo negatur, et est sensus, fit ex nihilo, idest non fit ex aliquo; sicut si dicatur, iste loquitur de nihilo, quia non loquitur de aliquo. Et utroque modo verificatur, cum dicitur ex nihilo aliquid fieri. Sed primo modo, haec praepositio ex importat ordinem, ut dictum est, secundo modo, importat habitudinem causae materialis, quae negatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0022","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit aliquid creare. Quia secundum philosophum, I Physic., antiqui philosophi acceperunt ut communem conceptionem animi, ex nihilo nihil fieri. Sed potentia Dei non se extendit ad contraria primorum principiorum; utpote quod Deus faciat quod totum non sit maius sua parte, vel quod affirmatio et negatio sint simul vera. Ergo Deus non potest aliquid ex nihilo facere, vel creare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0024","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0025","text":"Praeterea, si creare est aliquid ex nihilo facere, ergo creari est aliquid fieri. Sed omne fieri est mutari. Ergo creatio est mutatio. Sed omnis mutatio est ex subiecto aliquo, ut patet per definitionem motus, nam motus est actus existentis in potentia. Ergo est impossibile aliquid a Deo ex nihilo fieri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0026","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0027","text":"Praeterea, quod factum est, necesse est aliquando fieri. Sed non potest dici quod illud quod creatur, simul fiat et factum sit, quia in permanentibus, quod fit, non est, quod autem factum est, iam est; simul ergo aliquid esset et non esset. Ergo, si aliquid fit, fieri eius praecedit factum esse. Sed hoc non potest esse, nisi praeexistat subiectum in quo sustentetur ipsum fieri. Ergo impossibile est aliquid fieri ex nihilo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0028","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0029","text":"Praeterea, infinitam distantiam non est pertransire. Sed infinita distantia est inter ens et nihil. Ergo non contingit ex nihilo aliquid fieri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0030","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0032","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod non solum non est impossibile a Deo aliquid creari, sed necesse est ponere a Deo omnia creata esse, ut ex praemissis habetur. Quicumque enim facit aliquid ex aliquo, illud ex quo facit praesupponitur actioni eius, et non producitur per ipsam actionem, sicut artifex operatur ex rebus naturalibus, ut ex ligno et aere, quae per artis actionem non causantur, sed causantur per actionem naturae. Sed et ipsa natura causat res naturales quantum ad formam, sed praesupponit materiam. Si ergo Deus non ageret nisi ex aliquo praesupposito, sequeretur quod illud praesuppositum non esset causatum ab ipso. Ostensum est autem supra quod nihil potest esse in entibus quod non sit a Deo, qui est causa universalis totius esse. Unde necesse est dicere quod Deus ex nihilo res in esse producit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0034","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod antiqui philosophi, sicut supra dictum est, non consideraverunt nisi emanationem effectuum particularium a causis particularibus, quas necesse est praesupponere aliquid in sua actione, et secundum hoc erat eorum communis opinio, ex nihilo nihil fieri. Sed tamen hoc locum non habet in prima emanatione ab universali rerum principio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0036","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod creatio non est mutatio nisi secundum modum intelligendi tantum. Nam de ratione mutationis est, quod aliquid idem se habeat aliter nunc et prius, nam quandoque est idem ens actu, aliter se habens nunc et prius, sicut in motibus secundum quantitatem et qualitatem et ubi; quandoque vero est idem ens in potentia tantum, sicut in mutatione secundum substantiam, cuius subiectum est materia. Sed in creatione, per quam producitur tota substantia rerum, non potest accipi aliquid idem aliter se habens nunc et prius, nisi secundum intellectum tantum; sicut si intelligatur aliqua res prius non fuisse totaliter, et postea esse. Sed cum actio et passio conveniant in substantia motus, et differant solum secundum habitudines diversas, ut dicitur in III Physic., oportet quod, subtracto motu, non remaneant nisi diversae habitudines in creante et creato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0038","text":"Sed quia modus significandi sequitur modum intelligendi, ut dictum est, creatio significatur per modum mutationis, et propter hoc dicitur quod creare est ex nihilo aliquid facere. Quamvis facere et fieri magis in hoc conveniant quam mutare et mutari, quia facere et fieri important habitudinem causae ad effectum et effectus ad causam, sed mutationem ex consequenti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0039","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in his quae fiunt sine motu, simul est fieri et factum esse, sive talis factio sit terminus motus, sicut illuminatio (nam simul aliquid illuminatur et illuminatum est); sive non sit terminus motus, sicut simul formatur verbum in corde et formatum est. Et in his, quod fit, est, sed cum dicitur fieri, significatur ab alio esse, et prius non fuisse. Unde, cum creatio sit sine motu, simul aliquid creatur et creatum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0041","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0042","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit ex falsa imaginatione, ac si sit aliquod infinitum medium inter nihilum et ens, quod patet esse falsum. Procedit autem falsa haec imaginatio ex eo quod creatio significatur ut quaedam mutatio inter duos terminos existens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0043","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0044","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0045","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatio non sit aliquid in creatura. Sicut enim creatio passive accepta attribuitur creaturae, ita creatio active accepta attribuitur creatori. Sed creatio active accepta non est aliquid in creatore, quia sic sequeretur quod in Deo esset aliquid temporale. Ergo creatio passive accepta non est aliquid in creatura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0046","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0047","text":"Praeterea, nihil est medium inter creatorem et creaturam. Sed creatio significatur ut medium inter utrumque, non enim est creator, cum non sit aeterna; neque creatura, quia oporteret eadem ratione aliam ponere creationem qua ipsa crearetur, et sic in infinitum. Creatio ergo non est aliquid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0048","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0049","text":"Praeterea, si creatio est aliquid praeter substantiam creatam, oportet quod sit accidens eius. Omne autem accidens est in subiecto. Ergo res creata esset subiectum creationis. Et sic idem esset subiectum creationis et terminus. Quod est impossibile, quia subiectum prius est accidente, et conservat accidens; terminus autem posterius est actione et passione cuius est terminus, et eo existente cessat actio et passio. Igitur ipsa creatio non est aliqua res.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0050","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0051","text":"Sed contra, maius est fieri aliquid secundum totam substantiam, quam secundum formam substantialem vel accidentalem. Sed generatio simpliciter vel secundum quid, qua fit aliquid secundum formam substantialem vel accidentalem, est aliquid in generato. Ergo multo magis creatio, qua fit aliquid secundum totam substantiam, est aliquid in creato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0052","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0053","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod creatio ponit aliquid in creato secundum relationem tantum. Quia quod creatur, non fit per motum vel per mutationem. Quod enim fit per motum vel mutationem, fit ex aliquo praeexistenti, quod quidem contingit in productionibus particularibus aliquorum entium; non autem potest hoc contingere in productione totius esse a causa universali omnium entium, quae est Deus. Unde Deus, creando, producit res sine motu. Subtracto autem motu ab actione et passione, nihil remanet nisi relatio, ut dictum est. Unde relinquitur quod creatio in creatura non sit nisi relatio quaedam ad creatorem, ut ad principium sui esse; sicut in passione quae est cum motu, importatur relatio ad principium motus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0054","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0055","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod creatio active significata significat actionem divinam, quae est eius essentia cum relatione ad creaturam. Sed relatio in Deo ad creaturam non est realis, sed secundum rationem tantum. Relatio vero creaturae ad Deum est relatio realis, ut supra dictum est, cum de divinis nominibus ageretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0056","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0057","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quia creatio significatur ut mutatio, sicut dictum est; mutatio autem media quodammodo est inter movens et motum, ideo etiam creatio significatur ut media inter creatorem et creaturam. Tamen creatio passive accepta est in creatura, et est creatura. Neque tamen oportet quod alia creatione creetur, quia relationes, cum hoc ipsum quod sunt, ad aliquid dicantur, non referuntur per aliquas alias relationes, sed per seipsas; sicut etiam supra dictum est, cum de aequalitate personarum ageretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0058","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0059","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod creationis, secundum quod significatur ut mutatio, creatura est terminus, sed secundum quod vere est relatio, creatura est eius subiectum, et prius ea in esse, sicut subiectum accidente. Sed habet quandam rationem prioritatis ex parte obiecti ad quod dicitur, quod est principium creaturae. Neque tamen oportet quod, quandiu creatura sit, dicatur creari, quia creatio importat habitudinem creaturae ad creatorem cum quadam novitate seu incoeptione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0060","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0061","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0062","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creari non sit proprium compositorum et subsistentium. Dicitur enim in libro de causis, prima rerum creatarum est esse. Sed esse rei creatae non est subsistens. Ergo creatio proprie non est subsistentis et compositi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0063","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0064","text":"Praeterea, quod creatur est ex nihilo. Composita autem non sunt ex nihilo, sed ex suis componentibus. Ergo compositis non convenit creari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0065","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0066","text":"Praeterea, illud proprie producitur per primam emanationem, quod supponitur in secunda, sicut res naturalis per generationem naturalem, quae supponitur in operatione artis. Sed illud quod supponitur in generatione naturali, est materia. Ergo materia est quae proprie creatur, et non compositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0067","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0068","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Caelum autem et terra sunt res compositae subsistentes. Ergo horum proprie est creatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0069","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0070","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod creari est quoddam fieri, ut dictum est. Fieri autem ordinatur ad esse rei. Unde illis proprie convenit fieri et creari, quibus convenit esse. Quod quidem convenit proprie subsistentibus, sive sint simplicia, sicut substantiae separatae; sive sint composita, sicut substantiae materiales. Illi enim proprie convenit esse, quod habet esse; et hoc est subsistens in suo esse. Formae autem et accidentia, et alia huiusmodi, non dicuntur entia quasi ipsa sint, sed quia eis aliquid est; ut albedo ea ratione dicitur ens, quia ea subiectum est album. Unde, secundum philosophum, accidens magis proprie dicitur entis quam ens. Sicut igitur accidentia et formae, et huiusmodi, quae non subsistunt, magis sunt coexistentia quam entia; ita magis debent dici concreata quam creata. Proprie vero creata sunt subsistentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0071","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0072","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur, prima rerum creatarum est esse, ly esse non importat subiectum creatum; sed importat propriam rationem obiecti creationis. Nam ex eo dicitur aliquid creatum, quod est ens, non ex eo quod est hoc ens, cum creatio sit emanatio totius esse ab ente universali, ut dictum est. Et est similis modus loquendi, sicut si diceretur quod primum visibile est color, quamvis illud quod proprie videtur, sit coloratum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0073","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0074","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod creatio non dicit constitutionem rei compositae ex principiis praeexistentibus, sed compositum sic dicitur creari, quod simul cum omnibus suis principiis in esse producitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0075","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa non probat quod sola materia creetur; sed quod materia non sit nisi ex creatione. Nam creatio est productio totius esse, et non solum materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0077","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0078","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0079","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non solius Dei sit creare. Quia secundum philosophum, perfectum est quod potest sibi simile facere. Sed creaturae immateriales sunt perfectiores creaturis materialibus, quae faciunt sibi simile, ignis enim generat ignem, et homo generat hominem. Ergo substantia immaterialis potest facere substantiam sibi similem. Sed substantia immaterialis non potest fieri nisi per creationem, cum non habeat materiam ex qua fiat. Ergo aliqua creatura potest creare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0080","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0081","text":"Praeterea, quanto maior est resistentia ex parte facti, tanto maior virtus requiritur in faciente. Sed plus resistit contrarium quam nihil. Ergo maioris virtutis est aliquid facere ex contrario, quod tamen creatura facit; quam aliquid facere ex nihilo. Multo magis igitur creatura hoc facere potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0082","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0083","text":"Praeterea, virtus facientis consideratur secundum mensuram eius quod fit. Sed ens creatum est finitum, ut supra probatum est, cum de Dei infinitate ageretur. Ergo ad producendum per creationem aliquid creatum, non requiritur nisi virtus finita. Sed habere virtutem finitam non est contra rationem creaturae. Ergo non est impossibile creaturam creare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0084","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0085","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in III de Trin., quod neque boni neque mali Angeli possunt esse creatores alicuius rei. Multo minus igitur aliae creaturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0086","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0087","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod satis apparet in primo aspectu, secundum praemissa, quod creare non potest esse propria actio nisi solius Dei. Oportet enim universaliores effectus in universaliores et priores causas reducere. Inter omnes autem effectus, universalissimum est ipsum esse. Unde oportet quod sit proprius effectus primae et universalissimae causae, quae est Deus. Unde etiam dicitur libro de causis, quod neque intelligentia vel anima nobilis dat esse, nisi inquantum operatur operatione divina. Producere autem esse absolute, non inquantum est hoc vel tale, pertinet ad rationem creationis. Unde manifestum est quod creatio est propria actio ipsius Dei. Contingit autem quod aliquid participet actionem propriam alicuius alterius, non virtute propria, sed instrumentaliter, inquantum agit in virtute alterius; sicut aer per virtutem ignis habet calefacere et ignire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0088","text":"Et secundum hoc, aliqui opinati sunt quod, licet creatio sit propria actio universalis causae, tamen aliqua inferiorum causarum inquantum agit in virtute primae causae, potest creare. Et sic posuit Avicenna quod prima substantia separata, creata a Deo, creat aliam post se, et substantiam orbis, et animam eius; et quod substantia orbis creat materiam inferiorum corporum. Et secundum hunc etiam modum Magister dicit, in V dist. IV Sent., quod Deus potest creaturae communicare potentiam creandi, ut creet per ministerium, non propria auctoritate. Sed hoc esse non potest. Quia causa secunda instrumentalis non participat actionem causae superioris, nisi inquantum per aliquid sibi proprium dispositive operatur ad effectum principalis agentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0089","text":"Si igitur nihil ibi ageret secundum illud quod est sibi proprium, frustra adhiberetur ad agendum, nec oporteret esse determinata instrumenta determinatarum actionum. Sic enim videmus quod securis, scindendo lignum, quod habet ex proprietate suae formae, producit scamni formam, quae est effectus proprius principalis agentis. Illud autem quod est proprius effectus Dei creantis, est illud quod praesupponitur omnibus aliis, scilicet esse absolute. Unde non potest aliquid operari dispositive et instrumentaliter ad hunc effectum, cum creatio non sit ex aliquo praesupposito, quod possit disponi per actionem instrumentalis agentis. Sic igitur impossibile est quod alicui creaturae conveniat creare, neque virtute propria, neque instrumentaliter sive per ministerium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0090","text":"Et hoc praecipue inconveniens est dici de aliquo corpore, quod creet, cum nullum corpus agat nisi tangendo vel movendo; et sic requirit in sua actione aliquid praeexistens, quod possit tangi et moveri; quod est contra rationem creationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0091","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0092","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aliquod perfectum participans aliquam naturam, facit sibi simile, non quidem producendo absolute illam naturam, sed applicando eam ad aliquid. Non enim hic homo potest esse causa naturae humanae absolute, quia sic esset causa sui ipsius, sed est causa quod natura humana sit in hoc homine generato. Et sic praesupponit in sua actione determinatam materiam per quam est hic homo. Sed sicut hic homo participat humanam naturam, ita quodcumque ens creatum participat, ut ita dixerim, naturam essendi, quia solus Deus est suum esse, ut supra dictum est. Nullum igitur ens creatum potest producere aliquod ens absolute, nisi inquantum esse causat in hoc, et sic oportet quod praeintelligatur id per quod aliquid est hoc, actioni qua facit sibi simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0093","text":"In substantia autem immateriali non potest praeintelligi aliquid per quod sit haec, quia est haec per suam formam, per quam habet esse, cum sint formae subsistentes. Igitur substantia immaterialis non potest producere aliam substantiam immaterialem sibi similem, quantum ad esse eius; sed quantum ad perfectionem aliquam superadditam; sicut si dicamus quod superior Angelus illuminat inferiorem, ut Dionysius dicit. Secundum quem modum etiam in caelestibus est paternitas, ut ex verbis apostoli patet, Ephes. III, *ex quo omnis paternitas in caelo et in terra nominatur*. Et ex hoc etiam evidenter apparet quod nullum ens creatum potest causare aliquid, nisi praesupposito aliquo. Quod repugnat rationi creationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0094","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0095","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ex contrario fit aliquid per accidens, ut dicitur in I Physic., per se autem fit aliquid ex subiecto, quod est in potentia. Contrarium igitur resistit agenti, inquantum impedit potentiam ab actu in quem intendit reducere agens, sicut ignis intendit reducere materiam aquae in actum sibi similem, sed impeditur per formam et dispositiones contrarias, quibus quasi ligatur potentia ne reducatur in actum. Et quanto magis fuerit potentia ligata, tanto requiritur maior virtus in agente ad reducendam materiam in actum. Unde multo maior potentia requiritur in agente, si nulla potentia praeexistat. Sic ergo patet quod multo maioris virtutis est facere aliquid ex nihilo, quam ex contrario.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0096","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0097","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod virtus facientis non solum consideratur ex substantia facti, sed etiam ex modo faciendi, maior enim calor non solum magis, sed etiam citius calefacit. Quamvis igitur creare aliquem effectum finitum non demonstret potentiam infinitam, tamen creare ipsum ex nihilo demonstrat potentiam infinitam. Quod ex praedictis patet. Si enim tanto maior virtus requiritur in agente, quanto potentia est magis remota ab actu, oportet quod virtus agentis ex nulla praesupposita potentia, quale agens est creans, sit infinita, quia nulla proportio est nullius potentiae ad aliquam potentiam, quam praesupponit virtus agentis naturalis, sicut et non entis ad ens. Et quia nulla creatura habet simpliciter potentiam infinitam, sicut neque esse infinitum, ut supra probatum est, relinquitur quod nulla creatura possit creare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0098","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0099","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0100","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creare sit proprium alicuius personae. Quod enim est prius, est causa eius quod est post; et perfectum imperfecti. Sed processio divinae personae est prior quam processio creaturae, et magis perfecta, quia divina persona procedit in perfecta similitudine sui principii, creatura vero in imperfecta. Ergo processiones divinarum personarum sunt causa processionis rerum. Et sic creare est proprium personae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0101","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0102","text":"Praeterea, personae divinae non distinguuntur ab invicem nisi per suas processiones et relationes. Quidquid igitur differenter attribuitur divinis personis, hoc convenit eis secundum processiones et relationes personarum. Sed causalitas creaturarum diversimode attribuitur divinis personis, nam in symbolo fidei patri attribuitur quod sit creator omnium visibilium et invisibilium; filio autem attribuitur quod per eum omnia facta sunt; sed spiritui sancto, quod sit dominus et vivificator. Causalitas ergo creaturarum convenit personis secundum processiones et relationes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0103","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0104","text":"Praeterea, si dicatur quod causalitas creaturae attenditur secundum aliquod attributum essentiale quod appropriatur alicui personae, hoc non videtur sufficiens. Quia quilibet effectus divinus causatur a quolibet attributo essentiali, scilicet potentia, bonitate et sapientia, et sic non magis pertinet ad unum quam ad aliud. Non deberet ergo aliquis determinatus modus causalitatis attribui uni personae magis quam alii, nisi distinguerentur in creando secundum relationes et processiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0105","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0106","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, II cap. de Div. Nom., quod communia totius divinitatis sunt omnia causalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0107","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0108","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod creare est proprie causare sive producere esse rerum. Cum autem omne agens agat sibi simile, principium actionis considerari potest ex actionis effectu, ignis enim est qui generat ignem. Et ideo creare convenit Deo secundum suum esse, quod est eius essentia, quae est communis tribus personis. Unde creare non est proprium alicui personae, sed commune toti Trinitati. Sed tamen divinae personae secundum rationem suae processionis habent causalitatem respectu creationis rerum. Ut enim supra ostensum est, cum de Dei scientia et voluntate ageretur, Deus est causa rerum per suum intellectum et voluntatem, sicut artifex rerum artificiatarum. Artifex autem per verbum in intellectu conceptum, et per amorem suae voluntatis ad aliquid relatum, operatur. Unde et Deus pater operatus est creaturam per suum verbum, quod est filius; et per suum amorem, qui est spiritus sanctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0109","text":"Et secundum hoc processiones personarum sunt rationes productionis creaturarum, inquantum includunt essentialia attributa, quae sunt scientia et voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0110","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0111","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod processiones divinarum personarum sunt causa creationis sicut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0112","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0113","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut natura divina, licet sit communis tribus personis, ordine tamen quodam eis convenit, inquantum filius accipit naturam divinam a patre, et spiritus sanctus ab utroque; ita etiam et virtus creandi, licet sit communis tribus personis, ordine tamen quodam eis convenit; nam filius habet eam a patre, et spiritus sanctus ab utroque. Unde creatorem esse attribuitur patri, ut ei qui non habet virtutem creandi ab alio. De filio autem dicitur per quem omnia facta sunt, inquantum habet eandem virtutem, sed ab alio, nam haec praepositio per solet denotare causam mediam, sive principium de principio. Sed spiritui sancto, qui habet eandem virtutem ab utroque, attribuitur quod dominando gubernet, et vivificet quae sunt creata a patre per filium. Potest etiam huius attributionis communis ratio accipi ex appropriatione essentialium attributorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0114","text":"Nam, sicut supra dictum est, patri appropriatur potentia, quae maxime manifestatur in creatione, et ideo attribuitur patri creatorem esse. Filio autem appropriatur sapientia, per quam agens per intellectum operatur, et ideo dicitur de filio, per quem omnia facta sunt. Spiritui sancto autem appropriatur bonitas, ad quam pertinet gubernatio deducens res in debitos fines, et vivificatio, nam vita in interiori quodam motu consistit, primum autem movens est finis et bonitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0115","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0116","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet quilibet effectus Dei procedat ex quolibet attributorum, tamen reducitur unusquisque effectus ad illud attributum, cum quo habet convenientiam secundum propriam rationem, sicut ordinatio rerum ad sapientiam, et iustificatio impii ad misericordiam et bonitatem se superabundanter diffundentem. Creatio vero, quae est productio ipsius substantiae rei, reducitur ad potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0117","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0118","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0119","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in creaturis non sit necesse inveniri vestigium Trinitatis. Per sua enim vestigia unumquodque investigari potest. Sed Trinitas personarum non potest investigari ex creaturis, ut supra habitum est. Ergo vestigia Trinitatis non sunt in creatura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0120","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0121","text":"Praeterea, quidquid in creatura est, creatum est. Si igitur vestigium Trinitatis invenitur in creatura secundum aliquas proprietates suas, et omne creatum habet vestigium Trinitatis, oportet in unaquaque illarum inveniri etiam vestigium Trinitatis, et sic in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0122","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0123","text":"Praeterea, effectus non repraesentat nisi suam causam. Sed causalitas creaturarum pertinet ad naturam communem, non autem ad relationes, quibus personae distinguuntur et numerantur. Ergo in creatura non invenitur vestigium Trinitatis, sed solum unitatis essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0124","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0125","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod *Trinitatis vestigium in creatura apparet*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0126","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0127","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod omnis effectus aliqualiter repraesentat suam causam, sed diversimode. Nam aliquis effectus repraesentat solam causalitatem causae, non autem formam eius, sicut fumus repraesentat ignem, et talis repraesentatio dicitur esse repraesentatio vestigii; vestigium enim demonstrat motum alicuius transeuntis, sed non qualis sit. Aliquis autem effectus repraesentat causam quantum ad similitudinem formae eius, sicut ignis generatus ignem generantem, et statua Mercurii Mercurium, et haec est repraesentatio imaginis. Processiones autem divinarum personarum attenduntur secundum actus intellectus et voluntatis, sicut supra dictum est, nam filius procedit ut verbum intellectus, spiritus sanctus ut amor voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0128","text":"In creaturis igitur rationalibus, in quibus est intellectus et voluntas, invenitur repraesentatio Trinitatis per modum imaginis, inquantum invenitur in eis verbum conceptum et amor procedens. Sed in creaturis omnibus invenitur repraesentatio Trinitatis per modum vestigii, inquantum in qualibet creatura inveniuntur aliqua quae necesse est reducere in divinas personas sicut in causam. Quaelibet enim creatura subsistit in suo esse, et habet formam per quam determinatur ad speciem, et habet ordinem ad aliquid aliud. Secundum igitur quod est quaedam substantia creata, repraesentat causam et principium, et sic demonstrat personam patris, qui est principium non de principio. Secundum autem quod habet quandam formam et speciem, repraesentat verbum; secundum quod forma artificiati est ex conceptione artificis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0129","text":"Secundum autem quod habet ordinem, repraesentat spiritum sanctum, inquantum est amor, quia ordo effectus ad aliquid alterum est ex voluntate creantis. Et ideo dicit Augustinus, in VI Lib. de Trin., quod vestigium Trinitatis invenitur in unaquaque creatura, secundum quod unum aliquid est, et secundum quod aliqua specie formatur, et secundum quod quendam ordinem tenet. Et ad haec etiam reducuntur illa tria, numerus, pondus et mensura, quae ponuntur Sap. XI, nam mensura refertur ad substantiam rei limitatam suis principiis, numerus ad speciem, pondus ad ordinem. Et ad haec etiam reducuntur alia tria quae ponit Augustinus, modus species et ordo. Et ea quae ponit in libro octoginta trium quaest. quod constat, quod discernitur, quod congruit, constat enim aliquid per suam substantiam, discernitur per formam, congruit per ordinem. Et in idem de facili reduci possunt quaecumque sic dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0130","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0131","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod repraesentatio vestigii attenditur secundum appropriata, per quem modum ex creaturis in Trinitatem divinarum personarum veniri potest, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0132","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0133","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod creatura est res proprie subsistens, in qua est praedicta tria invenire. Neque oportet quod in quolibet eorum quae ei insunt, haec tria inveniantur, sed secundum ea vestigium rei subsistenti attribuitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0134","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0135","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod etiam processiones personarum sunt causa et ratio creationis aliquo modo, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0136","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0137","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0138","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatio admisceatur in operibus naturae et artis. In qualibet enim operatione naturae et artis producitur aliqua forma. Sed non producitur ex aliquo, cum non habeat materiam partem sui. Ergo producitur ex nihilo. Et sic in qualibet operatione naturae et artis est creatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0139","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0140","text":"Praeterea, effectus non est potior sua causa. Sed in rebus naturalibus non invenitur aliquid agens nisi forma accidentalis, quae est forma activa vel passiva. Non ergo per operationem naturae producitur forma substantialis. Relinquitur igitur quod sit per creationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0141","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0142","text":"Praeterea, natura facit sibi simile. Sed quaedam inveniuntur generata in natura non ab aliquo sibi simili, sicut patet in animalibus generatis per putrefactionem. Ergo eorum forma non est a natura, sed a creatione. Et eadem ratio est de aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0143","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0144","text":"Praeterea, quod non creatur, non est creatura. Si igitur in his quae sunt a natura non adiungatur creatio, sequitur quod ea quae sunt a natura, non sunt creaturae. Quod est haereticum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0145","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0146","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus, super Gen. ad Lit., distinguit opus propagationis, quod est opus naturae, ab opere creationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0147","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0148","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod haec dubitatio inducitur propter formas. Quas quidam posuerunt non incipere per actionem naturae, sed prius in materia extitisse, ponentes latitationem formarum. Et hoc accidit eis ex ignorantia materiae, quia nesciebant distinguere inter potentiam et actum, quia enim formae praeexistunt in materia in potentia, posuerunt eas simpliciter praeexistere. Alii vero posuerunt formas dari vel causari ab agente separato, per modum creationis. Et secundum hoc cuilibet operationi naturae adiungitur creatio. Sed hoc accidit eis ex ignorantia formae. Non enim considerabant quod forma naturalis corporis non est subsistens, sed quo aliquid est, et ideo, cum fieri et creari non conveniat proprie nisi rei subsistenti, sicut supra dictum est, formarum non est fieri neque creari, sed concreata esse. Quod autem proprie fit ab agente naturali, est compositum, quod fit ex materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0149","text":"Unde in operibus naturae non admiscetur creatio, sed praesupponitur ad operationem naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0150","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0151","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod formae incipiunt esse in actu, compositis factis, non quod ipsae fiant per se, sed per accidens tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0152","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0153","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod qualitates activae in natura agunt in virtute formarum substantialium. Et ideo agens naturale non solum producit sibi simile secundum qualitatem, sed secundum speciem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0154","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0155","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ad generationem animalium imperfectorum sufficit agens universale, quod est virtus caelestis, cui assimilantur non secundum speciem, sed secundum analogiam quandam, neque oportet dicere quod eorum formae creantur ab agente separato. Ad generationem vero animalium perfectorum non sufficit agens universale, sed requiritur agens proprium, quod est generans univocum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0156","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0157","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod operatio naturae non est nisi ex praesuppositione principiorum creatorum, et sic ea quae per naturam fiunt, creaturae dicuntur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"defb49b24a9e4ea0fbe3be05b1c4611d47b0efebc7ba8c9f59dcc31be671557b","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143014342204Z_1cc0b234","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 45","source_sha256":"defb49b24a9e4ea0fbe3be05b1c4611d47b0efebc7ba8c9f59dcc31be671557b","sha256":"d549fbc20ac929d18b519dd1df7b88867460cbba0d9d7c0457b664e5b00fe665","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:30:14.342230+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0002","text":"[I q. 45 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0003","text":"Next, there is inquiry concerning the mode of the emanation of things from the first principle, which is called creation. Concerning this, eight questions are asked. First, what creation is. Second, whether God can create anything. Third, whether creation is some being in the nature of things. Fourth, to what it belongs to be created. Fifth, whether to create belongs to God alone. Sixth, whether it is common to the whole Trinity, or proper to some person. Seventh, whether there is some trace of the Trinity in created things. Eighth, whether the work of creation is mingled into the works of nature and of will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0005","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that to create is not to make something out of nothing. For Augustine says, against the adversary of the law and the prophets: *to make is [to make] that which was wholly not; but to create is, by bringing forth something from that which already was, to establish it*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0007","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0008","text":"Further, the nobility of an action and a motion is considered from their termini. Therefore, an action which is from good to good, and from being to being, is nobler than one which is from nothing to something. But creation seems to be the noblest action, and first among all actions. Therefore it is not from nothing to something, but rather from being to being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0009","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0010","text":"Further, this preposition *from* conveys a relation to some cause, and especially to a material cause; as when we say that a statue is made from bronze. But nothing cannot be the matter of a being, nor in any way its cause. Therefore to create is not to make something from nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0011","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, the Gloss says, concerning that passage of Gen. I, *In the beginning God created heaven*, etc., that to create is to make something from nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0013","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0014","text":"I answer that, as was said above, not only must the emanation of some particular being from some particular agent be considered, but also the emanation of the whole of being from the universal cause, which is God; and this emanation we designate by the name of creation. Now, that which proceeds according to a particular emanation is not presupposed to the emanation; just as, if a man is generated, there was not previously a man, but a man comes to be from a non-man, and a white thing from a non-white thing. Hence, if the emanation of the whole of universal being from the first principle is considered, it is impossible that any being be presupposed to this emanation. But nothing is the same as no being. Therefore, just as the generation of a man is from a non-being which is a non-man, so creation, which is the emanation of the whole of being, is from a non-being which is nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0015","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine uses the name of creation equivocally, according as those things are said to be created which are reformed for the better, as when someone is said to be created a bishop. But we are not speaking here of creation in this way, but as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0017","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that changes receive their species and dignity not from the terminus from which, but from the terminus to which. Therefore a change is so much the more perfect and prior as the terminus to which that change is directed is nobler and prior, although the terminus from which, which is opposed to the terminus to which, is more imperfect. Thus generation simply speaking is nobler and prior than alteration, because substantial form is nobler than accidental form; yet the privation of substantial form, which is the terminus from which in generation, is more imperfect than the contrary, which is the terminus from which in alteration. Likewise, creation is more perfect and prior than generation and alteration, because the terminus to which is the whole substance of the thing. But that which is understood as the terminus from which is simply non-being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0019","text":"[I q. 45 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0020","text":"To the third, it must be said that, when something is said to be made from nothing, this preposition *from* does not designate a material cause, but only an order; as when it is said, from morning comes midday, that is, after morning comes midday. But it must be understood that this preposition *from* can include the negation conveyed in what I call nothing, or be included by it. If in the first way, then the order remains affirmed, and its order is shown, namely, to preceding non-being. But if the negation includes the preposition, then the order is denied, and the sense is: it is made from nothing, that is, it is not made from something; as if it were said, this man speaks of nothing, because he does not speak of something. And in both ways the statement that something is made from nothing is verified. But in the first way, this preposition *from* conveys an order, as has been said; in the second way, it conveys a relation to a material cause, which is denied.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0022","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God cannot create anything. For according to the Philosopher, in *Physics* I, the ancient philosophers took as a common conception of the mind that nothing comes to be from nothing. But God's power does not extend to contraries of first principles; for example, that God should make the whole not greater than its part, or that affirmation and negation should be true at the same time. Therefore God cannot make anything from nothing, or create.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0024","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0025","text":"Moreover, if to create is to make something out of nothing, then to be created is for something to come to be. But every coming-to-be is a change. Therefore creation is a change. But every change is from some subject, as is clear from the definition of motion; for motion is the actuality of that which exists in potency. Therefore it is impossible for something to be made by God out of nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0026","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0027","text":"Moreover, what has been made must at some time come to be. But it cannot be said that that which is created comes to be and has been made simultaneously, because in permanent things, what is coming to be is not, whereas what has been made already is; therefore, something would simultaneously be and not be. Therefore, if something comes to be, its coming-to-be precedes its having been made. But this cannot be unless there pre-exists a subject in which the coming-to-be itself is sustained. Therefore it is impossible for something to come to be out of nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0028","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0029","text":"Moreover, an infinite distance cannot be traversed. But there is an infinite distance between being and nothing. Therefore it does not happen that something comes to be out of nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0030","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0031","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. I: *In the beginning God created heaven and earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0032","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0033","text":"I answer that it must be said that not only is it not impossible for something to be created by God, but it is necessary to hold that all things have been created by God, as is clear from what has been set forth above. For whoever makes something from something presupposes that from which he makes it to his action, and it is not produced by that action itself, just as an artisan works from natural things, such as wood and bronze, which are not caused by the action of art, but are caused by the action of nature. But nature itself causes natural things as regards their form, while presupposing matter. Therefore, if God did not act except from something presupposed, it would follow that that presupposed thing was not caused by Him. But it was shown above that nothing can exist among beings that is not from God, who is the universal cause of all being. Hence it is necessary to say that God produces things into being out of nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0034","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the ancient philosophers, as was said above, considered only the emanation of particular effects from particular causes, which must presuppose something in their action; and accordingly their common opinion was that nothing comes to be from nothing. Yet this has no place in the first emanation from the universal principle of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0036","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0037","text":"To the second it must be said that creation is a change only according to the mode of understanding. For it belongs to the nature of change that one and the same thing be otherwise now and before: for sometimes it is the same being in act, disposed otherwise now and before, as in motions according to quantity, quality, and place; but sometimes it is the same being only in potency, as in change according to substance, the subject of which is matter. But in creation, through which the whole substance of things is produced, one cannot take something the same, disposed otherwise now and before, except according to the intellect alone; as if one understood some thing previously not to have existed at all, and afterward to exist. But since action and passion agree in the substance of motion and differ only according to diverse relations, as is said in Physics III, it is necessary that, when motion is removed, nothing remain except diverse relations in the creator and the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0038","text":"But because the mode of signifying follows the mode of understanding, as has been said, creation is signified in the manner of a change, and on this account it is said that to create is to make something out of nothing. Although making and coming-to-be agree in this more than changing and being changed do, because making and coming-to-be imply a relation of cause to effect and of effect to cause, but change consequently.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0039","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0040","text":"To the third it must be said that in those things which come to be without motion, coming-to-be and having been made are simultaneous, whether such making is the terminus of motion, as illumination is (for something is illuminated and has been illuminated simultaneously); or whether it is not the terminus of motion, as a word is simultaneously formed in the heart and has been formed. And in these cases, what comes to be exists; but when it is said to come to be, its being from another and its previously not having been are signified. Hence, since creation is without motion, something is created and has been created simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0041","text":"[I q. 45 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0042","text":"To the fourth it must be said that that objection proceeds from a false imagination, as though there were some infinite intermediate between nothing and being, which is plainly false. This false imagination proceeds from the fact that creation is signified as a certain change existing between two termini.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0043","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0044","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0045","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that creation is not something in the creature. For just as creation taken passively is attributed to the creature, so creation taken actively is attributed to the creator. But creation taken actively is not something in the creator, because it would then follow that there was something temporal in God. Therefore creation taken passively is not something in the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0046","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0047","text":"Moreover, there is nothing intermediate between the creator and the creature. But creation is signified as an intermediate between the two, for it is not the creator, since it is not eternal; nor is it the creature, because for the same reason one would have to posit another creation by which it itself was created, and so on to infinity. Therefore creation is not something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0048","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0049","text":"Moreover, if creation is something besides the created substance, it must be an accident of it. But every accident is in a subject. Therefore, the created thing would be the subject of creation. And thus the same thing would be the subject of creation and its terminus. This is impossible, because the subject is prior to the accident and preserves the accident; whereas the terminus is posterior to the action and passion of which it is the terminus, and when it exists the action and passion cease. Therefore, creation itself is not any thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0050","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0051","text":"On the contrary, it is greater for something to come to be according to its whole substance than according to a substantial or accidental form. But generation simply, or in a certain respect, by which something comes to be according to a substantial or accidental form, is something in that which is generated. Therefore, much more is creation, by which something comes to be according to its whole substance, something in the created thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0052","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0053","text":"I answer that it must be said that creation posits something in the created thing only according to relation. For what is created does not come to be through motion or change. For what comes to be through motion or change comes to be from something pre-existing; and this indeed occurs in the particular productions of certain beings, but it cannot occur in the production of the whole of being by the universal cause of all beings, which is God. Hence God, in creating, produces things without motion. But when motion is removed from action and passion, nothing remains except relation, as has been said. Hence it follows that creation in the creature is nothing but a certain relation to the Creator, as to the principle of its being; just as in passion accompanied by motion there is implied a relation to the principle of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0054","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0055","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that creation, actively signified, signifies the divine action, which is His essence together with a relation to the creature. But the relation in God to the creature is not real, but only according to reason. The relation of the creature to God, however, is a real relation, as was said above when the divine names were being considered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0056","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0057","text":"To the second it must be said that, because creation is signified as a change, as has been said, and change is in some way intermediate between mover and moved, creation too is therefore signified as intermediate between Creator and creature. Nevertheless, creation taken passively is in the creature, and is the creature. Yet it need not be created by another creation, because relations, since in their very being they are said to be toward something, are not referred by any other relations, but by themselves; as was likewise said above, when the equality of the persons was being considered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0058","text":"[I q. 45 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0059","text":"To the third it must be said that, insofar as creation is signified as a change, the creature is its terminus; but insofar as it is truly a relation, the creature is its subject and is prior to it in being, as a subject is prior to an accident. Yet it has a certain character of priority on the part of the object to which it is said, which is the principle of the creature. Nor need it be the case that, as long as the creature exists, it is said to be created, because creation implies the creature's relation to the Creator together with a certain newness or beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0060","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0061","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0062","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that to be created is not proper to composites and subsistents. For it is said in the Book of Causes that the first of created things is being. But the being of a created thing is not subsistent. Therefore, creation is not properly of a subsistent and composite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0063","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0064","text":"Moreover, what is created is from nothing. But composites are not from nothing, but from their components. Therefore, it does not belong to composites to be created.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0065","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0066","text":"Moreover, that is properly produced by the first emanation which is presupposed in the second, as a natural thing is by natural generation, which is presupposed in the operation of art. But what is presupposed in natural generation is matter. Therefore, matter is what is properly created, and not the composite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0067","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0068","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. I, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*. But heaven and earth are composite subsisting things. Therefore, creation properly belongs to these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0069","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0070","text":"I answer that it must be said that to be created is a certain coming-to-be, as has been said. But coming-to-be is ordered to the being of a thing. Hence coming-to-be and being created properly belong to those to which being belongs. And this properly belongs to subsistents, whether they are simple, as separate substances, or composite, as material substances. For being properly belongs to that which has being; and this is what subsists in its being. But forms and accidents, and other such things, are not called beings as though they themselves are, but because something is by them; thus whiteness is called a being on the ground that by it the subject is white. Hence, according to the Philosopher, an accident is more properly called \"of a being\" than \"a being.\" Therefore, just as accidents and forms, and things of this sort, which do not subsist, are more coexistent than beings, so they ought rather to be called concreated than created. But subsistents are properly created.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0071","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0072","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that when it is said, “the first of created things is being,” the word “being” does not imply a created subject, but implies the proper intelligible account of the object of creation. For something is called created from the fact that it is a being, not from the fact that it is this being, since creation is an emanation of the whole of being from the universal being, as has been said. And the manner of speaking is similar to saying that the first visible is color, although that which is properly seen is the colored thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0073","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0074","text":"To the second, it must be said that creation does not signify the constituting of a composite thing from pre-existing principles, but the composite is said to be created in such a way that it is brought forth into being together with all its principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0075","text":"[I q. 45 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0076","text":"To the third, it must be said that that argument does not prove that matter alone is created, but that matter exists only from creation. For creation is the production of the whole of being, and not of matter alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0077","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0078","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0079","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that to create does not belong to God alone. For according to the Philosopher, that is perfect which can make something like itself. But immaterial creatures are more perfect than material creatures, which make something like themselves; for fire generates fire, and man generates man. Therefore an immaterial substance can make a substance like itself. But an immaterial substance can come to be only through creation, since it has no matter from which it may come to be. Therefore some creature can create.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0080","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0081","text":"Further, the greater the resistance on the part of the thing made, the greater power is required in the maker. But a contrary resists more than nothing. Therefore it belongs to a greater power to make something from a contrary—which nevertheless a creature does—than to make something from nothing. Much more, therefore, can a creature do this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0082","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0083","text":"Further, the power of the maker is considered according to the measure of that which is made. But created being is finite, as was proved above when God's infinity was being treated. Therefore, to produce something created through creation requires only finite power. But to possess finite power is not contrary to the nature of a creature. Therefore it is not impossible for a creature to create.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0084","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0085","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in III *On the Trinity*, that neither good nor evil angels can be creators of any thing. Much less, therefore, can other creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0086","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0087","text":"I answer that it must be said that, from what has been set forth, it sufficiently appears at first sight that to create can be the proper action of God alone. For more universal effects must be reduced to more universal and prior causes. Now among all effects, the most universal is being itself. Hence it must be the proper effect of the first and most universal cause, which is God. Hence it is also said in the *Book of Causes* that neither an intelligence nor a noble soul gives being, except insofar as it operates by a divine operation. But to produce being absolutely, not insofar as it is this or such, belongs to the nature of creation. Hence it is manifest that creation is the proper action of God himself. Now it happens that something participates in the proper action of another, not by its own power, but instrumentally, insofar as it acts by the power of another; just as air, by the power of fire, has the capacity to heat and to ignite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0088","text":"And accordingly, some have held that, although creation is the proper action of the universal cause, nevertheless some of the lower causes, insofar as they act by the power of the first cause, can create. Thus Avicenna held that the first separate substance, created by God, creates another after itself, and the substance of the sphere, and its soul; and that the substance of the sphere creates the matter of lower bodies. And in this manner also the Master says, in V, distinction IV of the *Sentences*, that God can communicate to a creature the power of creating, so that it creates by ministry, not by its own authority. But this cannot be. For the instrumental secondary cause does not participate in the action of the higher cause except insofar as it operates dispositively toward the effect of the principal agent through something proper to itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0089","text":"Therefore, if it did nothing there according to what is proper to itself, it would be employed in acting in vain, nor would there have to be determinate instruments for determinate actions. For thus we see that an axe, by splitting wood—which it has from the property of its own form—produces the form of a bench, which is the proper effect of the principal agent. But that which is the proper effect of God creating is that which is presupposed by all other things, namely, being absolutely. Hence nothing can operate dispositively and instrumentally toward this effect, since creation is not from something presupposed which could be disposed by the action of an instrumental agent. Thus, therefore, it is impossible that it belong to any creature to create, neither by its own power nor instrumentally or by ministry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0090","text":"And it is especially unfitting to say this of any body, namely, that it creates, since no body acts except by touching or moving; and thus in its action it requires something pre-existing which can be touched and moved. This is contrary to the nature of creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0091","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0092","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that some perfect thing participating in a certain nature makes something like itself, not indeed by producing that nature absolutely, but by applying it to something. For this man cannot be the cause of human nature absolutely, because in that way he would be the cause of himself; but he is the cause that human nature is in this generated man. And thus he presupposes in his action determinate matter through which this is a man. But just as this man participates in human nature, so every created being participates, so to speak, in the nature of being, because God alone is his own being, as was said above. Therefore no created being can produce any being absolutely, except insofar as it causes being in this; and thus that through which something is this must be pre-understood in the action by which it makes something like itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0093","text":"But in an immaterial substance, nothing through which it is this can be pre-understood, because it is this through its form, through which it has being, since they are subsistent forms. Therefore an immaterial substance cannot produce another immaterial substance like itself, as regards its being; but it can do so as regards some superadded perfection, as if we were to say that a higher angel illuminates a lower one, as Dionysius says. In this manner there is also fatherhood in the heavenly beings, as is clear from the words of the Apostle, Eph. III, *from whom every fatherhood in heaven and on earth is named*. And from this it also evidently appears that no created being can cause anything unless something is presupposed. This is repugnant to the nature of creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0094","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0095","text":"To the second, it must be said that something comes to be from a contrary accidentally, as is said in I *Physics*; but per se something comes to be from a subject which is in potency. Therefore the contrary resists the agent insofar as it prevents the potency from the act to which the agent intends to reduce it, just as fire intends to reduce the matter of water to an act like itself, but is impeded by the contrary form and dispositions, by which the potency is, as it were, bound so that it is not reduced to act. And the more the potency is bound, the greater power is required in the agent to reduce matter to act. Hence much greater power is required in the agent if no potency pre-exists. Thus, therefore, it is clear that it belongs to a much greater power to make something from nothing than from a contrary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0096","text":"[I q. 45 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0097","text":"To the third it must be said that the power of the maker is considered not only from the substance of what is made, but also from the mode of making; for greater heat not only heats more, but also more quickly. Therefore, although to create some finite effect does not demonstrate infinite power, nevertheless to create it from nothing demonstrates infinite power. This is clear from what has been said. For if so much the greater power is required in the agent as the potency is more remote from act, the power of an agent from no presupposed potency—such an agent as one that creates—is necessarily infinite, because there is no proportion of no potency to any potency, which the power of a natural agent presupposes, just as there is none of non-being to being. And because no creature has absolutely infinite power, just as neither does it have infinite being, as was proved above, it follows that no creature can create.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0098","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0099","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0100","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that to create is proper to some person. For what is prior is the cause of what is subsequent; and what is perfect, of what is imperfect. But the procession of a divine person is prior to the procession of a creature, and more perfect, because a divine person proceeds in perfect likeness to its principle, whereas a creature does so imperfectly. Therefore the processions of the divine persons are the cause of the procession of things. And thus to create is proper to a person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0101","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0102","text":"Further, the divine persons are distinguished from one another only by their processions and relations. Therefore, whatever is attributed differently to the divine persons belongs to them according to the processions and relations of the persons. But the causality of creatures is attributed in diverse ways to the divine persons: for in the creed of faith it is attributed to the Father that he is Creator of all things visible and invisible; to the Son, however, that through him all things were made; but to the Holy Spirit, that he is Lord and giver of life. Therefore the causality of creatures belongs to the persons according to processions and relations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0103","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0104","text":"Further, if it be said that the causality of the creature is considered according to some essential attribute which is appropriated to a person, this does not seem sufficient. For every divine effect is caused by every essential attribute, namely power, goodness, and wisdom, and thus it pertains no more to one than to another. Therefore some determinate mode of causality ought not to be attributed to one person more than to another, unless they were distinguished in creating according to relations and processions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0105","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0106","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter 2 of On the Divine Names, that all causal things are common to the whole divinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0107","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0108","text":"I answer that it must be said that to create is properly to cause or to produce the being of things. Since, however, every agent acts so as to produce something like itself, the principle of an action can be considered from the effect of the action; for it is fire that generates fire. And therefore to create belongs to God according to his being, which is his essence, and which is common to the three persons. Hence to create is not proper to any one person, but is common to the whole Trinity. Yet the divine persons, according to the character of their procession, have causality with respect to the creation of things. For, as was shown above when God's knowledge and will were being treated, God is the cause of things through his intellect and will, as an artisan is the cause of artifacts. But an artisan works through the word conceived in the intellect and through the love of his will directed toward something. Hence God the Father also worked the creature through his Word, which is the Son, and through his love, which is the Holy Spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0109","text":"And according to this, the processions of the persons are the accounts of the production of creatures, insofar as they include the essential attributes, which are knowledge and will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0110","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0111","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the processions of the divine persons are the cause of creation, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0112","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0113","text":"To the second it must be said that, just as the divine nature, although it is common to the three persons, nevertheless belongs to them in a certain order, insofar as the Son receives the divine nature from the Father, and the Holy Spirit from both; so also the power of creating, although it is common to the three persons, nevertheless belongs to them in a certain order. For the Son has it from the Father, and the Holy Spirit from both. Hence to be Creator is attributed to the Father, as to him who does not have the power of creating from another. But of the Son it is said, through whom all things were made, insofar as he has the same power, but from another; for this preposition through customarily denotes a middle cause, or a principle from a principle. But to the Holy Spirit, who has the same power from both, it is attributed that he govern by ruling and give life to those things which were created by the Father through the Son. A common reason for this attribution can also be taken from the appropriation of the essential attributes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0114","text":"For, as was said above, power is appropriated to the Father, which is most especially manifested in creation, and therefore being Creator is attributed to the Father. But wisdom is appropriated to the Son, through which an agent acting through intellect works, and therefore it is said of the Son, through whom all things were made. But goodness is appropriated to the Holy Spirit, to which pertains governance leading things to their due ends, and vivification; for life consists in a certain interior motion, but the first mover is the end and goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0115","text":"[I q. 45 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0116","text":"To the third it must be said that, although every effect of God proceeds from every one of the attributes, nevertheless each effect is referred back to that attribute with which it has agreement according to its proper account, as the ordering of things to wisdom, and the justification of the ungodly to mercy and to goodness diffusing itself superabundantly. But creation, which is the production of the very substance of a thing, is referred to power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0117","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0118","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0119","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that it is not necessary for a trace of the Trinity to be found in creatures. For each thing can be investigated through its traces. But the Trinity of persons cannot be investigated from creatures, as was held above. Therefore traces of the Trinity are not in the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0120","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0121","text":"Moreover, whatever is in a creature is created. Therefore, if a trace of the Trinity is found in a creature according to some of its properties, and everything created has a trace of the Trinity, a trace of the Trinity must also be found in each one of those properties, and thus to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0122","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0123","text":"Moreover, an effect represents only its cause. But the causality of creatures pertains to the common nature, and not to the relations by which the persons are distinguished and numbered. Therefore, a trace of the Trinity is not found in the creature, but only of the unity of the essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0124","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0125","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book VI of *On the Trinity*, that *the trace of the Trinity appears in the creature*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0126","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0127","text":"I answer that it must be said that every effect in some way represents its cause, but in diverse ways. For one effect represents only the causality of the cause, but not its form, as smoke represents fire; and such a representation is called a representation by trace. For a trace indicates the movement of someone passing by, but not what sort of being he is. But another effect represents the cause as to the likeness of its form, as generated fire represents generating fire, and a statue of Mercury, Mercury; and this is a representation by image. Now the processions of the divine persons are considered according to the acts of intellect and will, as was said above; for the Son proceeds as the word of the intellect, the Holy Spirit as the love of the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0128","text":"Therefore, in rational creatures, in which there are intellect and will, a representation of the Trinity is found by way of image, insofar as a conceived word and proceeding love are found in them. But in all creatures a representation of the Trinity is found by way of trace, insofar as in every creature there are certain things which must be referred back to the divine persons as to a cause. For every creature subsists in its being, and has a form by which it is determined to a species, and has an ordering toward something else. Therefore, insofar as it is a certain created substance, it represents a cause and principle, and thus manifests the person of the Father, who is principle not from a principle. But insofar as it has a certain form and species, it represents the Word, according as the form of an artifact is from the conception of the artisan.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0129","text":"But insofar as it has an order, it represents the Holy Spirit, insofar as He is love, because the order of an effect toward something other is from the will of the creator. And therefore Augustine says, in Book VI of *On the Trinity*, that the trace of the Trinity is found in every creature, insofar as something is one, and insofar as it is formed by some species, and insofar as it holds a certain order. And to these are also reduced those three, number, weight, and measure, which are set down in Wisdom XI; for measure is referred to the substance of a thing limited by its principles, number to species, weight to order. And to these are likewise reduced the other three which Augustine sets down: mode, species, and order; and those which he sets down in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*: that which subsists, that by which it is distinguished, that which is fitting. For something subsists through its substance, is distinguished through its form, is fitting through its order. And whatever things are spoken of in this way can easily be reduced to the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0130","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0131","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the representation by trace is considered according to appropriations, in which way one can come from creatures to the Trinity of the divine persons, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0132","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0133","text":"To the second it must be said that a creature is a thing properly subsisting, in which the aforesaid three are to be found. Nor is it necessary that these three be found in each of the things that belong to it; rather, the trace is attributed to the subsisting thing according to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0134","text":"[I q. 45 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0135","text":"To the third it must be said that even the processions of the persons are in some way the cause and rationale of creation, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0136","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0137","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0138","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth article. It seems that creation is mingled in the works of nature and art. For in every operation of nature and art some form is produced. But it is not produced from something, since it does not have matter as a part of itself. Therefore it is produced from nothing. And thus in every operation of nature and art there is creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0139","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0140","text":"Moreover, an effect is not more powerful than its cause. But in natural things nothing acting is found except an accidental form, which is an active or passive form. Therefore, through the operation of nature a substantial form is not produced. It remains, therefore, that it is through creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0141","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0142","text":"Moreover, nature makes what is like itself. But certain things are found generated in nature not from something like themselves, as is clear in animals generated through putrefaction. Therefore their form is not from nature, but from creation. And the same reasoning applies to the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0143","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0144","text":"Moreover, what is not created is not a creature. Therefore, if creation is not joined to those things which are from nature, it follows that those things which are from nature are not creatures. This is heretical.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0145","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0146","text":"On the contrary, Augustine, in his *Literal Commentary on Genesis*, distinguishes the work of propagation, which is a work of nature, from the work of creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0147","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0148","text":"I answer that this doubt is introduced on account of forms. Some held that they do not begin through the action of nature, but existed previously in matter, maintaining the hiddenness of forms. This befell them from ignorance of matter, because they did not know how to distinguish between potency and act: for, since forms pre-exist in matter potentially, they held that they pre-exist simply. Others, however, held that forms are given or caused by a separate agent, by way of creation. And accordingly creation is joined to every operation of nature. But this befell them from ignorance of form. For they did not consider that the form of a natural body is not subsistent, but is that by which something is; and therefore, since to be made and to be created properly belongs only to a subsistent thing, as was said above, it does not belong to forms to be made or created, but to be created together [with the composite]. But what is properly made by a natural agent is the composite, which is made from matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0149","text":"Hence, in the works of nature creation is not mingled in, but is presupposed for the operation of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0150","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0151","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that forms begin to be in act when the composites have been made, not because they themselves are made through themselves, but only accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0152","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0153","text":"To the second it must be said that active qualities in nature act by the power of substantial forms. And therefore a natural agent produces not only something like itself according to quality, but according to species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0154","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0155","text":"To the third it must be said that, for the generation of imperfect animals, a universal agent suffices, namely the celestial power, to which they are assimilated not according to species but according to a certain analogy; nor is it necessary to say that their forms are created by a separate agent. But for the generation of perfect animals, a universal agent does not suffice; rather, a proper agent is required, namely a univocal generator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0156","text":"[I q. 45 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0157","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the operation of nature exists only upon the presupposition of created principles, and thus those things that come to be through nature are called creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_37:P0157"]}],"source_digest":"1de75cd3d610f4e28d9a46a94cca88c644f50f2b18ffcdf1aa7c227de7bcd7e8","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 45"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 45","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38","title":"First Part · Question 46","order":47,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_38","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 46 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30388)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de principio durationis rerum creatarum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum creaturae semper fuerint. Secundo, utrum eas incoepisse sit articulus fidei. Tertio, quomodo Deus dicatur in principio caelum et terram creasse.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30389)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod universitas creaturarum, quae mundi nomine nuncupatur, non incoeperit, sed fuerit ab aeterno. Omne enim quod incoepit esse, antequam fuerit, possibile fuit ipsum esse, alioquin impossibile fuisset ipsum fieri. Si ergo mundus incoepit esse, antequam inciperet, possibile fuit ipsum esse. Sed quod possibile est esse, est materia, quae est in potentia ad esse, quod est per formam, et ad non esse, quod est per privationem. Si ergo mundus incoepit esse, ante mundum fuit materia. Sed non potest esse materia sine forma, materia autem mundi cum forma, est mundus. Fuit ergo mundus antequam esse inciperet, quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30390)\n\nPraeterea, nihil quod habet virtutem ut sit semper, quandoque est et quandoque non est, quia ad quantum se extendit virtus alicuius rei, tandiu est. Sed omne incorruptibile habet virtutem ut sit semper, non enim virtutem habet ad determinatum durationis tempus. Nullum ergo incorruptibile quandoque est et quandoque non est. Sed omne quod incipit esse, quandoque est et quandoque non est. Nullum ergo incorruptibile incipit esse. Sed multa sunt in mundo incorruptibilia, ut corpora caelestia, et omnes substantiae intellectuales. Ergo mundus non incoepit esse.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30391)\n\nPraeterea, nullum ingenitum incoepit esse. Sed philosophus probat in I Physic., quod materia est ingenita; et in I de caelo et mundo, quod caelum est ingenitum. Non ergo universitas rerum incoepit esse.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30392)\n\nPraeterea, vacuum est ubi non est corpus, sed possibile est esse. Sed si mundus incoepit esse, ubi nunc est corpus mundi, prius non fuit aliquod corpus, et tamen poterat ibi esse, alioquin nunc ibi non esset. Ergo ante mundum fuit vacuum, quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30393)\n\nPraeterea, nihil de novo incipit moveri, nisi per hoc quod movens vel mobile aliter se habet nunc quam prius. Sed quod aliter se habet nunc quam prius, movetur. Ergo ante omnem motum de novo incipientem, fuit aliquis motus. Motus ergo semper fuit. Ergo et mobile, quia motus non est nisi in mobili.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30394)\n\nPraeterea, omne movens aut est naturale, aut est voluntarium. Sed neutrum incipit movere, nisi aliquo motu praeexistente. Natura enim semper eodem modo operatur. Unde, nisi praecedat aliqua immutatio vel in natura moventis vel in mobili, non incipiet a movente naturali esse motus, qui non fuit prius. Voluntas autem absque sui immutatione retardat facere quod proponit, sed hoc non est nisi per aliquam immutationem quam imaginatur, ad minus ex parte ipsius temporis. Sicut qui vult facere domum cras, et non hodie, expectat aliquid futurum cras, quod hodie non est; et ad minus expectat quod dies hodiernus transeat, et crastinus adveniat; quod sine mutatione non est, quia tempus est numerus motus. Relinquitur ergo quod ante omnem motum de novo incipientem, fuit alius motus. Et sic idem quod prius.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30395)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid est semper in principio et semper in fine, nec incipere nec desinere potest, quia quod incipit, non est in suo fine; quod autem desinit, non est in suo principio. Sed tempus semper est in suo principio et fine, quia nihil est temporis nisi nunc, quod est finis praeteriti, et principium futuri. Ergo tempus nec incipere nec desinere potest. Et per consequens nec motus, cuius numerus tempus est.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30396)\n\nPraeterea, Deus aut est prior mundo natura tantum, aut duratione. Si natura tantum, ergo, cum Deus sit ab aeterno, et mundus est ab aeterno. Si autem est prior duratione; prius autem et posterius in duratione constituunt tempus, ergo ante mundum fuit tempus; quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 9](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30397)\n\nPraeterea, posita causa sufficienti, ponitur effectus, causa enim ad quam non sequitur effectus, est causa imperfecta, indigens alio ad hoc quod effectus sequatur. Sed Deus est sufficiens causa mundi; et finalis, ratione suae bonitatis; et exemplaris, ratione suae sapientiae; et effectiva, ratione suae potentiae; ut ex superioribus patet. Cum ergo Deus sit ab aeterno, et mundus fuit ab aeterno.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 10](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30398)\n\nPraeterea, cuius actio est aeterna, et effectus aeternus. Sed actio Dei est eius substantia, quae est aeterna. Ergo et mundus est aeternus.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30399)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. XVII, *clarifica me, pater, apud temetipsum, claritate quam habui priusquam mundus fieret*; et Proverb. VIII, *dominus possedit me in initio viarum suarum, antequam quidquam faceret a principio*.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30400)\n\nRespondeo dicendum nihil praeter Deum ab aeterno fuisse. Et hoc quidem ponere non est impossibile. Ostensum est enim supra quod voluntas Dei est causa rerum. Sic ergo aliqua necesse est esse, sicut necesse est Deum velle illa, cum necessitas effectus ex necessitate causae dependeat, ut dicitur in V Metaphys. Ostensum est autem supra quod, absolute loquendo, non est necesse Deum velle aliquid nisi seipsum. Non est ergo necessarium Deum velle quod mundus fuerit semper. Sed eatenus mundus est, quatenus Deus vult illum esse, cum esse mundi ex voluntate Dei dependeat sicut ex sua causa. Non est igitur necessarium mundum semper esse. Unde nec demonstrative probari potest. Nec rationes quas ad hoc Aristoteles inducit, sunt demonstrativae simpliciter, sed secundum quid, scilicet ad contradicendum rationibus antiquorum, ponentium mundum incipere secundum quosdam modos in veritate impossibiles. Et hoc apparet ex tribus. Primo quidem, quia tam in VIII Physic. quam in I de caelo, praemittit quasdam opiniones, ut Anaxagorae et Empedoclis et Platonis, contra quos rationes contradictorias inducit. Secundo, quia, ubicumque de hac materia loquitur, inducit testimonia antiquorum, quod non est demonstratoris, sed probabiliter persuadentis. Tertio, quia expresse dicit in I Lib. Topic., quod quaedam sunt problemata dialectica, de quibus rationes non habemus, ut utrum mundus sit aeternus.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30401)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, antequam mundus esset, possibile fuit mundum esse, non quidem secundum potentiam passivam, quae est materia; sed secundum potentiam activam Dei. Et etiam secundum quod dicitur aliquid absolute possibile, non secundum aliquam potentiam sed ex sola habitudine terminorum, qui sibi non repugnant; secundum quod possibile opponitur impossibili, ut patet per philosophum, in V Metaphys.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30402)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illud quod habet virtutem ut sit semper ex quo habet illam virtutem, non quandoque est et quandoque non est, sed antequam haberet illam virtutem, non fuit. Unde haec ratio, quae ponitur ab Aristotele in I de caelo, non concludit simpliciter quod incorruptibilia non incoeperunt esse, sed quod non incoeperunt esse per modum naturalem, quo generabilia et corruptibilia incipiunt esse.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30403)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Aristoteles, in I Physic., probat materiam esse ingenitam, per hoc quod non habet subiectum de quo sit. In I autem de caelo et mundo, probat caelum ingenitum, quia non habet contrarium ex quo generetur. Unde patet quod per utrumque non concluditur nisi quod materia et caelum non incoeperunt per generationem, ut quidam ponebant, praecipue de caelo. Nos autem dicimus quod materia et coelum producta sunt in esse per creationem, ut ex dictis patet.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30404)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod ad rationem vacui non sufficit in quo nihil est, sed requiritur quod sit spatium capax corporis, in quo non sit corpus, ut patet per Aristotelem, in IV Physic. Nos autem dicimus non fuisse locum aut spatium ante mundum.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30405)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod primus motor semper eodem modo se habuit primum autem mobile non semper eodem modo se habuit, quia incoepit esse, cum prius non fuisset. Sed hoc non fuit per mutationem, sed per creationem, quae non est mutatio, ut supra dictum est. Unde patet quod haec ratio, quam ponit Aristoteles in VIII Physic., procedit contra eos qui ponebant mobilia aeterna, sed motum non aeternum; ut patet ex opinionibus Anaxagorae et Empedoclis. Nos autem ponimus, ex quo mobilia incoeperunt, semper fuisse motum.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30406)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod primum agens est agens voluntarium. Et quamvis habuit voluntatem aeternam producendi aliquem effectum, non tamen produxit aeternum effectum. Nec est necesse quod praesupponatur aliqua mutatio, nec etiam propter imaginationem temporis. Aliter enim est intelligendum de agente particulari, quod praesupponit aliquid, et causat alterum, et aliter de agente universali, quod producit totum. Sicut agens particulare producit formam, et praesupponit materiam, unde oportet quod formam inducat secundum proportionem ad debitam materiam. Unde rationabiliter in ipso consideratur quod inducit formam in talem materiam et non in aliam, ex differentia materiae ad materiam. Sed hoc non rationabiliter consideratur in Deo, qui simul producit formam et materiam, sed consideratur rationabiliter in eo, quod ipse producit materiam congruam formae et fini. Agens autem particulare praesupponit tempus, sicut et materiam. Unde rationabiliter consideratur in eo, quod agit in tempore posteriori et non in priori, secundum imaginationem successionis temporis post tempus. Sed in agente universali, quod producit rem et tempus, non est considerare quod agat nunc et non prius, secundum imaginationem temporis post tempus, quasi tempus praesupponatur eius actioni, sed considerandum est in eo, quod dedit effectui suo tempus quantum voluit, et secundum quod conveniens fuit ad suam potentiam demonstrandam. Manifestius enim mundus ducit in cognitionem divinae potentiae creantis, si mundus non semper fuit, quam si semper fuisset, omne enim quod non semper fuit, manifestum est habere causam; sed non ita manifestum est de eo quod semper fuit.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30407)\n\nAd septimum dicendum quod, sicut dicitur in IV Physic., prius et posterius est in tempore, secundum quod prius et posterius est in motu. Unde principium et finis accipienda sunt in tempore, sicut et in motu. Supposita autem aeternitate motus, necesse est quod quodlibet momentum in motu acceptum sit principium et terminus motus, quod non oportet, si motus incipiat. Et eadem ratio est de nunc temporis. Et sic patet quod ratio illa instantis nunc, quod semper sit principium et finis temporis, praesupponit aeternitatem temporis et motus. Unde Aristoteles hanc rationem inducit, in VIII Physic., contra eos qui ponebant aeternitatem temporis, sed negabant aeternitatem motus.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30408)\n\nAd octavum dicendum quod Deus est prior mundo duratione. Sed ly prius non designat prioritatem temporis, sed aeternitatis. Vel dicendum quod designat aeternitatem temporis imaginati, et non realiter existentis. Sicut, cum dicitur, supra caelum nihil est, ly supra designat locum imaginatum tantum, secundum quod possibile est imaginari dimensionibus caelestis corporis dimensiones alias superaddi.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 9](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30409)\n\nAd nonum dicendum quod, sicut effectus sequitur a causa agente naturaliter secundum modum suae formae, ita sequitur ab agente per voluntatem secundum formam ab eo praeconceptam et definitam, ut ex superioribus patet. Licet igitur Deus ab aeterno fuerit sufficiens causa mundi, non tamen oportet quod ponatur mundus ab eo productus, nisi secundum quod est in praedefinitione suae voluntatis; ut scilicet habeat esse post non esse, ut manifestius declaret suum auctorem.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 10](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30410)\n\nAd decimum dicendum quod, posita actione, sequitur effectus secundum exigentiam formae quae est principium actionis. In agentibus autem per voluntatem, quod conceptum est et praedefinitum, accipitur ut forma quae est principium actionis. Ex actione igitur Dei aeterna non sequitur effectus aeternus, sed qualem Deus voluit, ut scilicet haberet esse post non esse.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30411)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mundum incoepisse non sit articulus fidei, sed conclusio demonstrabilis. Omne enim factum habet principium suae durationis. Sed demonstrative probari potest quod Deus sit causa effectiva mundi, et hoc etiam probabiliores philosophi posuerunt. Ergo demonstrative probari potest quod mundus incoeperit.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30412)\n\nPraeterea, si necesse est dicere quod mundus factus est a Deo, aut ergo ex nihilo, aut ex aliquo. Sed non ex aliquo, quia sic materia mundi praecessisset mundum; contra quod procedunt rationes Aristotelis ponentis caelum ingenitum. Ergo oportet dicere quod mundus sit factus ex nihilo. Et sic habet esse post non esse. Ergo oportet quod esse incoeperit.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30413)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod operatur per intellectum, a quodam principio operatur, ut patet in omnibus artificibus. Sed Deus est agens per intellectum. Ergo a quodam principio operatur mundus igitur, qui est eius effectus, non fuit semper.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30414)\n\nPraeterea, manifeste apparet artes aliquas, et habitationes regionum, ex determinatis temporibus incoepisse. Sed hoc non esset, si mundus semper fuisset. Mundum igitur non semper fuisse manifestum est.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30415)\n\nPraeterea, certum est nihil Deo aequari posse. Sed si mundus semper fuisset, aequipararetur Deo in duratione. Ergo certum est non semper mundum fuisse.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30416)\n\nPraeterea, si mundus semper fuit, infiniti dies praecesserunt diem istum. Sed infinita non est pertransire. Ergo nunquam fuisset perventum ad hunc diem, quod est manifeste falsum.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30417)\n\nPraeterea, si mundus fuit aeternus, et generatio fuit ab aeterno. Ergo unus homo genitus est ab alio in infinitum. Sed pater est causa efficiens filii, ut dicitur in II Physic. Ergo in causis efficientibus est procedere in infinitum, quod improbatur in II Metaphys.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30418)\n\nPraeterea, si mundus et generatio semper fuit, infiniti homines praecesserunt. Sed anima hominis est immortalis. Ergo infinitae animae humanae nunc essent actu, quod est impossibile. Ergo ex necessitate sciri potest quod mundus incoeperit; et non sola fide tenetur.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30419)\n\nSed contra, fidei articuli demonstrative probari non possunt, *quia fides de non apparentibus est*, ut dicitur ad Hebr. XI. Sed Deum esse creatorem mundi, sic quod mundus incoeperit esse, est articulus fidei, dicimus enim, credo in unum Deum et cetera. Et iterum, Gregorius dicit, in Homil. I in Ezech., quod Moyses prophetizavit de praeterito, dicens in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram; in quo novitas mundi traditur. Ergo novitas mundi habetur tantum per revelationem. Et ideo non potest probari demonstrative.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30420)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod mundum non semper fuisse, sola fide tenetur, et demonstrative probari non potest, sicut et supra de mysterio Trinitatis dictum est. Et huius ratio est, quia novitas mundi non potest demonstrationem recipere ex parte ipsius mundi. Demonstrationis enim principium est quod quid est. Unumquodque autem, secundum rationem suae speciei, abstrahit ab hic et nunc, propter quod dicitur quod universalia sunt ubique et semper. Unde demonstrari non potest quod homo, aut caelum, aut lapis non semper fuit. Similiter etiam neque ex parte causae agentis, quae agit per voluntatem. Voluntas enim Dei ratione investigari non potest, nisi circa ea quae absolute necesse est Deum velle, talia autem non sunt quae circa creaturas vult, ut dictum est. Potest autem voluntas divina homini manifestari per revelationem, cui fides innititur. Unde mundum incoepisse est credibile, non autem demonstrabile vel scibile. Et hoc utile est ut consideretur, ne forte aliquis, quod fidei est demonstrare praesumens, rationes non necessarias inducat, quae praebeant materiam irridendi infidelibus, existimantibus nos propter huiusmodi rationes credere quae fidei sunt.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30421)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus, XI de Civ. Dei, philosophorum ponentium aeternitatem mundi, duplex fuit opinio. Quidam enim posuerunt quod substantia mundi non sit a Deo. Et horum est intollerabilis error; et ideo ex necessitate refellitur. Quidam autem sic posuerunt mundum aeternum, quod tamen mundum a Deo factum dixerunt. *Non enim mundum temporis volunt habere, sed suae creationis initium, ut quodam modo vix intelligibili semper sit factus. Id autem quomodo intelligant, invenerunt*, ut idem dicit in X de Civ. Dei. *Sicut enim, inquiunt, si pes ex aeternitate semper fuisset in pulvere, semper subesset vestigium, quod a calcante factum nemo dubitaret; sic et mundus semper fuit, semper existente qui fecit*. Et ad hoc intelligendum, considerandum est quod causa efficiens quae agit per motum, de necessitate praecedit tempore suum effectum, quia effectus non est nisi in termino actionis, agens autem omne oportet esse principium actionis. Sed si actio sit instantanea, et non successiva, non est necessarium faciens esse prius facto duratione; sicut patet in illuminatione. Unde dicunt quod non sequitur ex necessitate, si Deus est causa activa mundi, quod sit prior mundo duratione, quia creatio, qua mundum produxit, non est mutatio successiva, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30422)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illi qui ponerent mundum aeternum, dicerent mundum factum a Deo ex nihilo, non quod factus sit post nihilum, secundum quod nos intelligimus per nomen creationis; sed quia non est factus de aliquo. Et sic etiam non recusant aliqui eorum creationis nomen, ut patet ex Avicenna in sua metaphysica.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30423)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod illa est ratio Anaxagorae, quae ponitur in III Physic. Sed non de necessitate concludit, nisi de intellectu qui deliberando investigat quid agendum sit, quod est simile motui. Talis autem est intellectus humanus, sed non divinus, ut supra patet.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30424)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod ponentes aeternitatem mundi, ponunt aliquam regionem infinities esse mutatam de inhabitabili in habitabilem, et e converso. Et similiter ponunt quod artes, propter diversas corruptiones et accidentia, infinities fuerunt inventae, et iterum corruptae. Unde Aristoteles dicit, in libro Meteor., quod ridiculum est ex huiusmodi particularibus mutationibus opinionem accipere de novitate mundi totius.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30425)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, etsi mundus semper fuisset, non tamen parificaretur Deo in aeternitate, ut dicit Boetius, in fine de Consolat., quia esse divinum est esse totum simul, absque successione; non autem sic est de mundo.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30426)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod transitus semper intelligitur a termino in terminum. Quaecumque autem praeterita dies signetur, ab illa usque ad istam sunt finiti dies, qui pertransiri poterunt. Obiectio autem procedit ac si, positis extremis, sint media infinita.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30427)\n\nAd septimum dicendum quod in causis efficientibus impossibile est procedere in infinitum per se; ut puta si causae quae per se requiruntur ad aliquem effectum, multiplicarentur in infinitum; sicut si lapis moveretur a baculo, et baculus a manu, et hoc in infinitum. Sed per accidens in infinitum procedere in causis agentibus non reputatur impossibile; ut puta si omnes causae quae in infinitum multiplicantur, non teneant ordinem nisi unius causae, sed earum multiplicatio sit per accidens; sicut artifex agit multis martellis per accidens, quia unus post unum frangitur. Accidit ergo huic martello, quod agat post actionem alterius martelli. Et similiter accidit huic homini, inquantum generat, quod sit generatus ab alio, generat enim inquantum homo, et non inquantum est filius alterius hominis; omnes enim homines generantes habent gradum unum in causis efficientibus, scilicet gradum particularis generantis. Unde non est impossibile quod homo generetur ab homine in infinitum. Esset autem impossibile, si generatio huius hominis dependeret ab hoc homine, et a corpore elementari, et a sole, et sic in infinitum.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30428)\n\nAd octavum dicendum quod hanc rationem ponentes aeternitatem mundi multipliciter effugiunt quidam enim non reputant impossibile esse infinitas animas actu; ut patet in metaphysica Algazelis, dicentis hoc esse infinitum per accidens. Sed hoc improbatum est superius. Quidam vero dicunt animam corrumpi cum corpore. Quidam vero quod ex omnibus animabus remanet una tantum. Alii vero, ut Augustinus dicit, posuerunt propter hoc circuitum animarum; ut scilicet animae separatae a corporibus, post determinata temporum curricula, iterum redirent ad corpora. De quibus omnibus in sequentibus est agendum. Considerandum tamen quod haec ratio particularis est. Unde posset dicere aliquis quod mundus fuit aeternus, vel saltem aliqua creatura, ut Angelus; non autem homo. Nos autem intendimus universaliter, an aliqua creatura fuerit ab aeterno.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30429)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatio rerum non fuit in principio temporis. Quod enim non est in tempore, non est in aliquo temporis. Sed creatio rerum non fuit in tempore, per creationem enim rerum substantia in esse producta est; tempus autem non mensurat substantiam rerum, et praecipue incorporalium. Ergo creatio non fuit in principio temporis.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30430)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus probat quod omne quod fit, fiebat, et sic omne fieri habet prius et posterius. In principio autem temporis, cum sit indivisibile, non est prius et posterius. Ergo, cum creari sit quoddam fieri, videtur quod res non sint creatae in principio temporis.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30431)\n\nPraeterea, ipsum etiam tempus creatum est. Sed non potest creari in principio temporis, cum tempus sit divisibile, principium autem temporis indivisibile. Non ergo creatio rerum fuit in principio temporis.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30432)\n\nSed contra est quod Gen. I dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30433)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod illud verbum Genes. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*, tripliciter exponitur, ad excludendum tres errores. Quidam enim posuerunt mundum semper fuisse, et tempus non habere principium. Et ad hoc excludendum, exponitur, in principio, scilicet temporis. Quidam vero posuerunt duo esse creationis principia, unum bonorum, aliud malorum. Et ad hoc excludendum, exponitur, in principio, idest in filio. Sicut enim principium effectivum appropriatur patri, propter potentiam, ita principium exemplare appropriatur filio, propter sapientiam, ut sicut dicitur, omnia in sapientia fecisti, ita intelligatur Deum omnia fecisse in principio, idest in filio; secundum illud apostoli ad Coloss. I, in ipso, scilicet filio, condita sunt universa. Alii vero dixerunt corporalia esse creata a Deo mediantibus creaturis spiritualibus. Et ad hoc excludendum, exponitur, in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, idest ante omnia. Quatuor enim ponuntur simul creata, scilicet caelum Empyreum, materia corporalis (quae nomine terrae intelligitur), tempus, et natura angelica.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30434)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod non dicuntur in principio temporis res esse creatae, quasi principium temporis sit creationis mensura sed quia simul cum tempore caelum et terra creata sunt.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30435)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod verbum illud philosophi intelligitur de fieri quod est per motum, vel quod est terminus motus. Quia cum in quolibet motu sit accipere prius et posterius, ante quodcumque signum in motu signato, dum scilicet aliquid est in moveri et fieri, est accipere prius, et etiam aliquid post ipsum, quia quod est in principio motus, vel in termino, non est in moveri. Creatio autem neque est motus neque terminus motus, ut supra dictum est. Unde sic aliquid creatur, quod non prius creabatur.\n\n[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30436)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nihil fit nisi secundum quod est. Nihil autem est temporis nisi nunc. Unde non potest fieri nisi secundum aliquod nunc, non quia in ipso primo nunc sit tempus, sed quia ab eo incipit tempus.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0002","text":"[I q. 46 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de principio durationis rerum creatarum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum creaturae semper fuerint. Secundo, utrum eas incoepisse sit articulus fidei. Tertio, quomodo Deus dicatur in principio caelum et terram creasse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0005","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod universitas creaturarum, quae mundi nomine nuncupatur, non incoeperit, sed fuerit ab aeterno. Omne enim quod incoepit esse, antequam fuerit, possibile fuit ipsum esse, alioquin impossibile fuisset ipsum fieri. Si ergo mundus incoepit esse, antequam inciperet, possibile fuit ipsum esse. Sed quod possibile est esse, est materia, quae est in potentia ad esse, quod est per formam, et ad non esse, quod est per privationem. Si ergo mundus incoepit esse, ante mundum fuit materia. Sed non potest esse materia sine forma, materia autem mundi cum forma, est mundus. Fuit ergo mundus antequam esse inciperet, quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0007","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0008","text":"Praeterea, nihil quod habet virtutem ut sit semper, quandoque est et quandoque non est, quia ad quantum se extendit virtus alicuius rei, tandiu est. Sed omne incorruptibile habet virtutem ut sit semper, non enim virtutem habet ad determinatum durationis tempus. Nullum ergo incorruptibile quandoque est et quandoque non est. Sed omne quod incipit esse, quandoque est et quandoque non est. Nullum ergo incorruptibile incipit esse. Sed multa sunt in mundo incorruptibilia, ut corpora caelestia, et omnes substantiae intellectuales. Ergo mundus non incoepit esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0009","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0010","text":"Praeterea, nullum ingenitum incoepit esse. Sed philosophus probat in I Physic., quod materia est ingenita; et in I de caelo et mundo, quod caelum est ingenitum. Non ergo universitas rerum incoepit esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0011","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0012","text":"Praeterea, vacuum est ubi non est corpus, sed possibile est esse. Sed si mundus incoepit esse, ubi nunc est corpus mundi, prius non fuit aliquod corpus, et tamen poterat ibi esse, alioquin nunc ibi non esset. Ergo ante mundum fuit vacuum, quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0013","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0014","text":"Praeterea, nihil de novo incipit moveri, nisi per hoc quod movens vel mobile aliter se habet nunc quam prius. Sed quod aliter se habet nunc quam prius, movetur. Ergo ante omnem motum de novo incipientem, fuit aliquis motus. Motus ergo semper fuit. Ergo et mobile, quia motus non est nisi in mobili.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0015","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0016","text":"Praeterea, omne movens aut est naturale, aut est voluntarium. Sed neutrum incipit movere, nisi aliquo motu praeexistente. Natura enim semper eodem modo operatur. Unde, nisi praecedat aliqua immutatio vel in natura moventis vel in mobili, non incipiet a movente naturali esse motus, qui non fuit prius. Voluntas autem absque sui immutatione retardat facere quod proponit, sed hoc non est nisi per aliquam immutationem quam imaginatur, ad minus ex parte ipsius temporis. Sicut qui vult facere domum cras, et non hodie, expectat aliquid futurum cras, quod hodie non est; et ad minus expectat quod dies hodiernus transeat, et crastinus adveniat; quod sine mutatione non est, quia tempus est numerus motus. Relinquitur ergo quod ante omnem motum de novo incipientem, fuit alius motus. Et sic idem quod prius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0017","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0018","text":"Praeterea, quidquid est semper in principio et semper in fine, nec incipere nec desinere potest, quia quod incipit, non est in suo fine; quod autem desinit, non est in suo principio. Sed tempus semper est in suo principio et fine, quia nihil est temporis nisi nunc, quod est finis praeteriti, et principium futuri. Ergo tempus nec incipere nec desinere potest. Et per consequens nec motus, cuius numerus tempus est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0019","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0020","text":"Praeterea, Deus aut est prior mundo natura tantum, aut duratione. Si natura tantum, ergo, cum Deus sit ab aeterno, et mundus est ab aeterno. Si autem est prior duratione; prius autem et posterius in duratione constituunt tempus, ergo ante mundum fuit tempus; quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0021","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 9](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0022","text":"Praeterea, posita causa sufficienti, ponitur effectus, causa enim ad quam non sequitur effectus, est causa imperfecta, indigens alio ad hoc quod effectus sequatur. Sed Deus est sufficiens causa mundi; et finalis, ratione suae bonitatis; et exemplaris, ratione suae sapientiae; et effectiva, ratione suae potentiae; ut ex superioribus patet. Cum ergo Deus sit ab aeterno, et mundus fuit ab aeterno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0023","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 10](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0024","text":"Praeterea, cuius actio est aeterna, et effectus aeternus. Sed actio Dei est eius substantia, quae est aeterna. Ergo et mundus est aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0025","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0026","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. XVII, *clarifica me, pater, apud temetipsum, claritate quam habui priusquam mundus fieret*; et Proverb. VIII, *dominus possedit me in initio viarum suarum, antequam quidquam faceret a principio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0027","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0028","text":"Respondeo dicendum nihil praeter Deum ab aeterno fuisse. Et hoc quidem ponere non est impossibile. Ostensum est enim supra quod voluntas Dei est causa rerum. Sic ergo aliqua necesse est esse, sicut necesse est Deum velle illa, cum necessitas effectus ex necessitate causae dependeat, ut dicitur in V Metaphys. Ostensum est autem supra quod, absolute loquendo, non est necesse Deum velle aliquid nisi seipsum. Non est ergo necessarium Deum velle quod mundus fuerit semper. Sed eatenus mundus est, quatenus Deus vult illum esse, cum esse mundi ex voluntate Dei dependeat sicut ex sua causa. Non est igitur necessarium mundum semper esse. Unde nec demonstrative probari potest. Nec rationes quas ad hoc Aristoteles inducit, sunt demonstrativae simpliciter, sed secundum quid, scilicet ad contradicendum rationibus antiquorum, ponentium mundum incipere secundum quosdam modos in veritate impossibiles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0029","text":"Et hoc apparet ex tribus. Primo quidem, quia tam in VIII Physic. quam in I de caelo, praemittit quasdam opiniones, ut Anaxagorae et Empedoclis et Platonis, contra quos rationes contradictorias inducit. Secundo, quia, ubicumque de hac materia loquitur, inducit testimonia antiquorum, quod non est demonstratoris, sed probabiliter persuadentis. Tertio, quia expresse dicit in I Lib. Topic., quod quaedam sunt problemata dialectica, de quibus rationes non habemus, ut utrum mundus sit aeternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0030","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0031","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, antequam mundus esset, possibile fuit mundum esse, non quidem secundum potentiam passivam, quae est materia; sed secundum potentiam activam Dei. Et etiam secundum quod dicitur aliquid absolute possibile, non secundum aliquam potentiam sed ex sola habitudine terminorum, qui sibi non repugnant; secundum quod possibile opponitur impossibili, ut patet per philosophum, in V Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0032","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0033","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illud quod habet virtutem ut sit semper ex quo habet illam virtutem, non quandoque est et quandoque non est, sed antequam haberet illam virtutem, non fuit. Unde haec ratio, quae ponitur ab Aristotele in I de caelo, non concludit simpliciter quod incorruptibilia non incoeperunt esse, sed quod non incoeperunt esse per modum naturalem, quo generabilia et corruptibilia incipiunt esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0034","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0035","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Aristoteles, in I Physic., probat materiam esse ingenitam, per hoc quod non habet subiectum de quo sit. In I autem de caelo et mundo, probat caelum ingenitum, quia non habet contrarium ex quo generetur. Unde patet quod per utrumque non concluditur nisi quod materia et caelum non incoeperunt per generationem, ut quidam ponebant, praecipue de caelo. Nos autem dicimus quod materia et coelum producta sunt in esse per creationem, ut ex dictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0036","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0037","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod ad rationem vacui non sufficit in quo nihil est, sed requiritur quod sit spatium capax corporis, in quo non sit corpus, ut patet per Aristotelem, in IV Physic. Nos autem dicimus non fuisse locum aut spatium ante mundum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0038","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0039","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod primus motor semper eodem modo se habuit primum autem mobile non semper eodem modo se habuit, quia incoepit esse, cum prius non fuisset. Sed hoc non fuit per mutationem, sed per creationem, quae non est mutatio, ut supra dictum est. Unde patet quod haec ratio, quam ponit Aristoteles in VIII Physic., procedit contra eos qui ponebant mobilia aeterna, sed motum non aeternum; ut patet ex opinionibus Anaxagorae et Empedoclis. Nos autem ponimus, ex quo mobilia incoeperunt, semper fuisse motum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0040","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0041","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod primum agens est agens voluntarium. Et quamvis habuit voluntatem aeternam producendi aliquem effectum, non tamen produxit aeternum effectum. Nec est necesse quod praesupponatur aliqua mutatio, nec etiam propter imaginationem temporis. Aliter enim est intelligendum de agente particulari, quod praesupponit aliquid, et causat alterum, et aliter de agente universali, quod producit totum. Sicut agens particulare producit formam, et praesupponit materiam, unde oportet quod formam inducat secundum proportionem ad debitam materiam. Unde rationabiliter in ipso consideratur quod inducit formam in talem materiam et non in aliam, ex differentia materiae ad materiam. Sed hoc non rationabiliter consideratur in Deo, qui simul producit formam et materiam, sed consideratur rationabiliter in eo, quod ipse producit materiam congruam formae et fini.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0042","text":"Agens autem particulare praesupponit tempus, sicut et materiam. Unde rationabiliter consideratur in eo, quod agit in tempore posteriori et non in priori, secundum imaginationem successionis temporis post tempus. Sed in agente universali, quod producit rem et tempus, non est considerare quod agat nunc et non prius, secundum imaginationem temporis post tempus, quasi tempus praesupponatur eius actioni, sed considerandum est in eo, quod dedit effectui suo tempus quantum voluit, et secundum quod conveniens fuit ad suam potentiam demonstrandam. Manifestius enim mundus ducit in cognitionem divinae potentiae creantis, si mundus non semper fuit, quam si semper fuisset, omne enim quod non semper fuit, manifestum est habere causam; sed non ita manifestum est de eo quod semper fuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0043","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0044","text":"Ad septimum dicendum quod, sicut dicitur in IV Physic., prius et posterius est in tempore, secundum quod prius et posterius est in motu. Unde principium et finis accipienda sunt in tempore, sicut et in motu. Supposita autem aeternitate motus, necesse est quod quodlibet momentum in motu acceptum sit principium et terminus motus, quod non oportet, si motus incipiat. Et eadem ratio est de nunc temporis. Et sic patet quod ratio illa instantis nunc, quod semper sit principium et finis temporis, praesupponit aeternitatem temporis et motus. Unde Aristoteles hanc rationem inducit, in VIII Physic., contra eos qui ponebant aeternitatem temporis, sed negabant aeternitatem motus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0045","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0046","text":"Ad octavum dicendum quod Deus est prior mundo duratione. Sed ly prius non designat prioritatem temporis, sed aeternitatis. Vel dicendum quod designat aeternitatem temporis imaginati, et non realiter existentis. Sicut, cum dicitur, supra caelum nihil est, ly supra designat locum imaginatum tantum, secundum quod possibile est imaginari dimensionibus caelestis corporis dimensiones alias superaddi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0047","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 9](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0048","text":"Ad nonum dicendum quod, sicut effectus sequitur a causa agente naturaliter secundum modum suae formae, ita sequitur ab agente per voluntatem secundum formam ab eo praeconceptam et definitam, ut ex superioribus patet. Licet igitur Deus ab aeterno fuerit sufficiens causa mundi, non tamen oportet quod ponatur mundus ab eo productus, nisi secundum quod est in praedefinitione suae voluntatis; ut scilicet habeat esse post non esse, ut manifestius declaret suum auctorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0049","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 10](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0050","text":"Ad decimum dicendum quod, posita actione, sequitur effectus secundum exigentiam formae quae est principium actionis. In agentibus autem per voluntatem, quod conceptum est et praedefinitum, accipitur ut forma quae est principium actionis. Ex actione igitur Dei aeterna non sequitur effectus aeternus, sed qualem Deus voluit, ut scilicet haberet esse post non esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0051","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0052","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0053","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mundum incoepisse non sit articulus fidei, sed conclusio demonstrabilis. Omne enim factum habet principium suae durationis. Sed demonstrative probari potest quod Deus sit causa effectiva mundi, et hoc etiam probabiliores philosophi posuerunt. Ergo demonstrative probari potest quod mundus incoeperit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0054","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0055","text":"Praeterea, si necesse est dicere quod mundus factus est a Deo, aut ergo ex nihilo, aut ex aliquo. Sed non ex aliquo, quia sic materia mundi praecessisset mundum; contra quod procedunt rationes Aristotelis ponentis caelum ingenitum. Ergo oportet dicere quod mundus sit factus ex nihilo. Et sic habet esse post non esse. Ergo oportet quod esse incoeperit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0056","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0057","text":"Praeterea, omne quod operatur per intellectum, a quodam principio operatur, ut patet in omnibus artificibus. Sed Deus est agens per intellectum. Ergo a quodam principio operatur mundus igitur, qui est eius effectus, non fuit semper.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0058","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0059","text":"Praeterea, manifeste apparet artes aliquas, et habitationes regionum, ex determinatis temporibus incoepisse. Sed hoc non esset, si mundus semper fuisset. Mundum igitur non semper fuisse manifestum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0060","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0061","text":"Praeterea, certum est nihil Deo aequari posse. Sed si mundus semper fuisset, aequipararetur Deo in duratione. Ergo certum est non semper mundum fuisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0062","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0063","text":"Praeterea, si mundus semper fuit, infiniti dies praecesserunt diem istum. Sed infinita non est pertransire. Ergo nunquam fuisset perventum ad hunc diem, quod est manifeste falsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0064","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0065","text":"Praeterea, si mundus fuit aeternus, et generatio fuit ab aeterno. Ergo unus homo genitus est ab alio in infinitum. Sed pater est causa efficiens filii, ut dicitur in II Physic. Ergo in causis efficientibus est procedere in infinitum, quod improbatur in II Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0066","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0067","text":"Praeterea, si mundus et generatio semper fuit, infiniti homines praecesserunt. Sed anima hominis est immortalis. Ergo infinitae animae humanae nunc essent actu, quod est impossibile. Ergo ex necessitate sciri potest quod mundus incoeperit; et non sola fide tenetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0068","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0069","text":"Sed contra, fidei articuli demonstrative probari non possunt, *quia fides de non apparentibus est*, ut dicitur ad Hebr. XI. Sed Deum esse creatorem mundi, sic quod mundus incoeperit esse, est articulus fidei, dicimus enim, credo in unum Deum et cetera. Et iterum, Gregorius dicit, in Homil. I in Ezech., quod Moyses prophetizavit de praeterito, dicens in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram; in quo novitas mundi traditur. Ergo novitas mundi habetur tantum per revelationem. Et ideo non potest probari demonstrative.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0070","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0071","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod mundum non semper fuisse, sola fide tenetur, et demonstrative probari non potest, sicut et supra de mysterio Trinitatis dictum est. Et huius ratio est, quia novitas mundi non potest demonstrationem recipere ex parte ipsius mundi. Demonstrationis enim principium est quod quid est. Unumquodque autem, secundum rationem suae speciei, abstrahit ab hic et nunc, propter quod dicitur quod universalia sunt ubique et semper. Unde demonstrari non potest quod homo, aut caelum, aut lapis non semper fuit. Similiter etiam neque ex parte causae agentis, quae agit per voluntatem. Voluntas enim Dei ratione investigari non potest, nisi circa ea quae absolute necesse est Deum velle, talia autem non sunt quae circa creaturas vult, ut dictum est. Potest autem voluntas divina homini manifestari per revelationem, cui fides innititur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0072","text":"Unde mundum incoepisse est credibile, non autem demonstrabile vel scibile. Et hoc utile est ut consideretur, ne forte aliquis, quod fidei est demonstrare praesumens, rationes non necessarias inducat, quae praebeant materiam irridendi infidelibus, existimantibus nos propter huiusmodi rationes credere quae fidei sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0073","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0074","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus, XI de Civ. Dei, philosophorum ponentium aeternitatem mundi, duplex fuit opinio. Quidam enim posuerunt quod substantia mundi non sit a Deo. Et horum est intollerabilis error; et ideo ex necessitate refellitur. Quidam autem sic posuerunt mundum aeternum, quod tamen mundum a Deo factum dixerunt. *Non enim mundum temporis volunt habere, sed suae creationis initium, ut quodam modo vix intelligibili semper sit factus. Id autem quomodo intelligant, invenerunt*, ut idem dicit in X de Civ. Dei. *Sicut enim, inquiunt, si pes ex aeternitate semper fuisset in pulvere, semper subesset vestigium, quod a calcante factum nemo dubitaret; sic et mundus semper fuit, semper existente qui fecit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0075","text":"Et ad hoc intelligendum, considerandum est quod causa efficiens quae agit per motum, de necessitate praecedit tempore suum effectum, quia effectus non est nisi in termino actionis, agens autem omne oportet esse principium actionis. Sed si actio sit instantanea, et non successiva, non est necessarium faciens esse prius facto duratione; sicut patet in illuminatione. Unde dicunt quod non sequitur ex necessitate, si Deus est causa activa mundi, quod sit prior mundo duratione, quia creatio, qua mundum produxit, non est mutatio successiva, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0076","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0077","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illi qui ponerent mundum aeternum, dicerent mundum factum a Deo ex nihilo, non quod factus sit post nihilum, secundum quod nos intelligimus per nomen creationis; sed quia non est factus de aliquo. Et sic etiam non recusant aliqui eorum creationis nomen, ut patet ex Avicenna in sua metaphysica.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0078","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0079","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod illa est ratio Anaxagorae, quae ponitur in III Physic. Sed non de necessitate concludit, nisi de intellectu qui deliberando investigat quid agendum sit, quod est simile motui. Talis autem est intellectus humanus, sed non divinus, ut supra patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0080","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0081","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod ponentes aeternitatem mundi, ponunt aliquam regionem infinities esse mutatam de inhabitabili in habitabilem, et e converso. Et similiter ponunt quod artes, propter diversas corruptiones et accidentia, infinities fuerunt inventae, et iterum corruptae. Unde Aristoteles dicit, in libro Meteor., quod ridiculum est ex huiusmodi particularibus mutationibus opinionem accipere de novitate mundi totius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0082","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0083","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, etsi mundus semper fuisset, non tamen parificaretur Deo in aeternitate, ut dicit Boetius, in fine de Consolat., quia esse divinum est esse totum simul, absque successione; non autem sic est de mundo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0084","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0085","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod transitus semper intelligitur a termino in terminum. Quaecumque autem praeterita dies signetur, ab illa usque ad istam sunt finiti dies, qui pertransiri poterunt. Obiectio autem procedit ac si, positis extremis, sint media infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0086","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0087","text":"Ad septimum dicendum quod in causis efficientibus impossibile est procedere in infinitum per se; ut puta si causae quae per se requiruntur ad aliquem effectum, multiplicarentur in infinitum; sicut si lapis moveretur a baculo, et baculus a manu, et hoc in infinitum. Sed per accidens in infinitum procedere in causis agentibus non reputatur impossibile; ut puta si omnes causae quae in infinitum multiplicantur, non teneant ordinem nisi unius causae, sed earum multiplicatio sit per accidens; sicut artifex agit multis martellis per accidens, quia unus post unum frangitur. Accidit ergo huic martello, quod agat post actionem alterius martelli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0088","text":"Et similiter accidit huic homini, inquantum generat, quod sit generatus ab alio, generat enim inquantum homo, et non inquantum est filius alterius hominis; omnes enim homines generantes habent gradum unum in causis efficientibus, scilicet gradum particularis generantis. Unde non est impossibile quod homo generetur ab homine in infinitum. Esset autem impossibile, si generatio huius hominis dependeret ab hoc homine, et a corpore elementari, et a sole, et sic in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0089","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0090","text":"Ad octavum dicendum quod hanc rationem ponentes aeternitatem mundi multipliciter effugiunt quidam enim non reputant impossibile esse infinitas animas actu; ut patet in metaphysica Algazelis, dicentis hoc esse infinitum per accidens. Sed hoc improbatum est superius. Quidam vero dicunt animam corrumpi cum corpore. Quidam vero quod ex omnibus animabus remanet una tantum. Alii vero, ut Augustinus dicit, posuerunt propter hoc circuitum animarum; ut scilicet animae separatae a corporibus, post determinata temporum curricula, iterum redirent ad corpora. De quibus omnibus in sequentibus est agendum. Considerandum tamen quod haec ratio particularis est. Unde posset dicere aliquis quod mundus fuit aeternus, vel saltem aliqua creatura, ut Angelus; non autem homo. Nos autem intendimus universaliter, an aliqua creatura fuerit ab aeterno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0091","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0092","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0093","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatio rerum non fuit in principio temporis. Quod enim non est in tempore, non est in aliquo temporis. Sed creatio rerum non fuit in tempore, per creationem enim rerum substantia in esse producta est; tempus autem non mensurat substantiam rerum, et praecipue incorporalium. Ergo creatio non fuit in principio temporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0094","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0095","text":"Praeterea, philosophus probat quod omne quod fit, fiebat, et sic omne fieri habet prius et posterius. In principio autem temporis, cum sit indivisibile, non est prius et posterius. Ergo, cum creari sit quoddam fieri, videtur quod res non sint creatae in principio temporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0096","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0097","text":"Praeterea, ipsum etiam tempus creatum est. Sed non potest creari in principio temporis, cum tempus sit divisibile, principium autem temporis indivisibile. Non ergo creatio rerum fuit in principio temporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0098","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0099","text":"Sed contra est quod Gen. I dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0100","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0101","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod illud verbum Genes. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*, tripliciter exponitur, ad excludendum tres errores. Quidam enim posuerunt mundum semper fuisse, et tempus non habere principium. Et ad hoc excludendum, exponitur, in principio, scilicet temporis. Quidam vero posuerunt duo esse creationis principia, unum bonorum, aliud malorum. Et ad hoc excludendum, exponitur, in principio, idest in filio. Sicut enim principium effectivum appropriatur patri, propter potentiam, ita principium exemplare appropriatur filio, propter sapientiam, ut sicut dicitur, omnia in sapientia fecisti, ita intelligatur Deum omnia fecisse in principio, idest in filio; secundum illud apostoli ad Coloss. I, in ipso, scilicet filio, condita sunt universa. Alii vero dixerunt corporalia esse creata a Deo mediantibus creaturis spiritualibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0102","text":"Et ad hoc excludendum, exponitur, in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, idest ante omnia. Quatuor enim ponuntur simul creata, scilicet caelum Empyreum, materia corporalis (quae nomine terrae intelligitur), tempus, et natura angelica.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0103","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0104","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod non dicuntur in principio temporis res esse creatae, quasi principium temporis sit creationis mensura sed quia simul cum tempore caelum et terra creata sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0105","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0106","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod verbum illud philosophi intelligitur de fieri quod est per motum, vel quod est terminus motus. Quia cum in quolibet motu sit accipere prius et posterius, ante quodcumque signum in motu signato, dum scilicet aliquid est in moveri et fieri, est accipere prius, et etiam aliquid post ipsum, quia quod est in principio motus, vel in termino, non est in moveri. Creatio autem neque est motus neque terminus motus, ut supra dictum est. Unde sic aliquid creatur, quod non prius creabatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0107","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0108","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nihil fit nisi secundum quod est. Nihil autem est temporis nisi nunc. Unde non potest fieri nisi secundum aliquod nunc, non quia in ipso primo nunc sit tempus, sed quia ab eo incipit tempus.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"08610a898aa4c6f06f59cc30dbd2f31525f5e13c48ee4a52249fde9e6b4b0aef","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143132458283Z_684cda1f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 46","source_sha256":"08610a898aa4c6f06f59cc30dbd2f31525f5e13c48ee4a52249fde9e6b4b0aef","sha256":"fdc12d9144b8c50dea36e606733779dca878357a729478f56c15146cea8ae4e5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:31:32.458720+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0002","text":"[I q. 46 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the beginning of the duration of created things. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, whether creatures have always existed. Second, whether their having begun is an article of faith. Third, how God is said to have created heaven and earth in the beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0005","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the universe of creatures, which is called by the name of world, did not begin, but has existed from eternity. For everything that began to be, before it was, was possible to be; otherwise it would have been impossible for it to come to be. If, therefore, the world began to be, before it began, it was possible for it to be. But that which is possible to be is matter, which is in potency to being, which is through form, and to non-being, which is through privation. Therefore, if the world began to be, matter existed before the world. But matter cannot exist without form; yet the matter of the world together with form is the world. Therefore the world existed before it began to be, which is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0007","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0008","text":"Further, nothing that has the power to exist always sometimes is and sometimes is not, because for as long as the power of a thing extends, so long does it exist. But everything incorruptible has the power to exist always, for it does not have power for a determinate period of duration. Therefore, no incorruptible thing sometimes is and sometimes is not. But everything that begins to be sometimes is and sometimes is not. Therefore, no incorruptible thing begins to be. But many things in the world are incorruptible, such as the heavenly bodies and all intellectual substances. Therefore, the world did not begin to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0009","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0010","text":"Further, nothing ungenerated began to be. But the Philosopher proves in Physics I that matter is ungenerated; and in On the Heavens and the World I, that heaven is ungenerated. Therefore, the universe of things did not begin to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0011","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0012","text":"Further, a vacuum is where there is no body, but where it is possible for there to be one. But if the world began to be, where the body of the world now is, there was previously no body, and yet one could be there; otherwise it would not now be there. Therefore, before the world there was a vacuum, which is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0013","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0014","text":"Further, nothing begins to be moved anew unless, through this, the mover or the movable is disposed otherwise now than before. But what is disposed otherwise now than before is moved. Therefore, before every motion beginning anew, there was some motion. Therefore, motion has always existed. Therefore, so too has the movable, because motion exists only in a movable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0015","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0016","text":"Further, every mover is either natural or voluntary. But neither begins to move unless some pre-existing motion is present. For nature always operates in the same way. Hence, unless some change precedes either in the nature of the mover or in the movable, there will not begin from a natural mover a motion that did not exist before. But the will delays, without a change in itself, doing what it proposes; yet this is only through some change which it imagines, at least on the part of time itself. Thus, one who wishes to build a house tomorrow and not today awaits something that will be tomorrow and is not today; and at least awaits that the present day pass and the following day arrive. But this does not occur without change, because time is the number of motion. It remains, therefore, that before every motion beginning anew, there was another motion. And thus the same as before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0017","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0018","text":"Further, whatever is always at the beginning and always at the end can neither begin nor cease, because what begins is not at its end, whereas what ceases is not at its beginning. But time is always at its beginning and end, because nothing of time exists except the now, which is the end of the past and the beginning of the future. Therefore, time can neither begin nor cease. And consequently neither can motion, of which time is the number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0019","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0020","text":"Further, God is prior to the world either by nature alone or by duration. If by nature alone, then, since God is from eternity, the world too is from eternity. But if He is prior by duration—and prior and posterior in duration constitute time—then there was time before the world, which is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0021","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 9](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0022","text":"Further, when a sufficient cause is posited, the effect is posited; for a cause from which no effect follows is an imperfect cause, needing something else in order that the effect may follow. But God is a sufficient cause of the world: final, by reason of His goodness; exemplary, by reason of His wisdom; and efficient, by reason of His power, as is clear from what has gone before. Therefore, since God is from eternity, the world too has existed from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0023","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 arg. 10](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0024","text":"Further, the effect of that whose action is eternal is also eternal. But God's action is His substance, which is eternal. Therefore, the world too is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0025","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0026","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in John XVII: *Glorify me, Father, with yourself, with the glory which I had before the world was made*; and in Proverbs VIII: *The Lord possessed me in the beginning of his ways, before he made anything from the beginning*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0027","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0028","text":"I answer that it must be said that nothing besides God existed from eternity. And indeed, to maintain this is not impossible. For it was shown above that the will of God is the cause of things. Therefore, things must be as God necessarily wills them to be, since the necessity of an effect depends upon the necessity of its cause, as is said in V Metaphysics. But it was shown above that, speaking absolutely, it is not necessary for God to will anything except himself. Therefore, it is not necessary for God to will that the world should always have existed. But the world exists only insofar as God wills it to exist, since the being of the world depends upon the will of God as upon its cause. Therefore, it is not necessary that the world always exist. Hence neither can this be proved demonstratively. Nor are the arguments that Aristotle adduces for this demonstrative simply, but only in a certain respect—namely, for contradicting the arguments of the ancients, who held that the world began in certain modes that are in truth impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0029","text":"And this is apparent from three points. First, because both in VIII Physics and in I On the Heavens, he sets forth certain opinions beforehand, such as those of Anaxagoras, Empedocles, and Plato, against whom he adduces contradictory arguments. Second, because, wherever he speaks concerning this matter, he adduces the testimonies of the ancients, which is not the procedure of one who demonstrates, but of one who persuades probabilistically. Third, because he expressly says in I of the Topics that some problems are dialectical, concerning which we do not have arguments, such as whether the world is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0030","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0031","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, before the world existed, it was possible for the world to exist, not indeed according to passive power, which belongs to matter, but according to God's active power. And also according as something is called absolutely possible, not according to some power, but from the mere relation of the terms, which are not repugnant to one another; in which sense the possible is opposed to the impossible, as is clear from the Philosopher in V Metaphysics.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0032","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0033","text":"To the second, it must be said that what has the power to exist always, from the time when it has that power, is not at one time and not at another; but before it had that power, it did not exist. Hence this argument, which Aristotle gives in I On the Heavens, does not conclude simply that incorruptible things did not begin to exist, but that they did not begin to exist by the natural mode by which generable and corruptible things begin to exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0034","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0035","text":"To the third, it must be said that Aristotle, in I Physics, proves that matter is ungenerated by the fact that it has no subject from which it might come to be. But in I On the Heavens and the World, he proves that heaven is ungenerated because it has no contrary from which it may be generated. Hence it is clear that from both arguments there follows only that matter and heaven did not begin by generation, as some held, especially concerning heaven. But we say that matter and heaven were brought forth into being by creation, as is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0036","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0037","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, for the notion of a vacuum, it does not suffice that there be that in which nothing is; rather, it is required that there be a space capable of a body, in which there is no body, as is clear from Aristotle in IV Physics. But we say that there was no place or space before the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0038","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0039","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the first mover always was disposed in the same way; but the first movable was not always disposed in the same way, because it began to exist when previously it had not existed. But this was not through change, but through creation, which is not a change, as was said above. Hence it is clear that this argument, which Aristotle sets forth in VIII Physics, proceeds against those who held movable things to be eternal, but motion not eternal, as is evident from the opinions of Anaxagoras and Empedocles. But we maintain that, from the time movable things began, motion has always existed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0040","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0041","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that the first agent is an agent by will. And although it had an eternal will to produce some effect, it nevertheless did not produce an eternal effect. Nor need any change be presupposed, nor even on account of the imagination of time. For one must understand differently of a particular agent, which presupposes one thing and causes another, and of a universal agent, which produces the whole. Thus a particular agent produces a form and presupposes matter; hence it must introduce the form in proportion to suitable matter. Therefore, it is reasonably considered in it why it introduces a form into this matter and not another, by reason of the difference of matter from matter. But this is not reasonably considered in God, who produces form and matter simultaneously; rather, it is reasonably considered in him that he produces matter suited to the form and the end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0042","text":"But a particular agent presupposes time, just as it presupposes matter. Hence it is reasonably considered in it why it acts at a later time and not at an earlier, according to the imagination of the succession of time after time. But in the universal agent, which produces the thing and time, one must not consider why it acts now and not before, according to the imagination of time after time, as though time were presupposed to its action; rather, one must consider in it that it gave its effect such time as it willed, and as was fitting for the manifestation of its power. For the world leads more clearly to knowledge of the divine creative power if the world has not always existed than if it had always existed; for everything that has not always existed is manifestly seen to have a cause, but this is not so manifest concerning that which has always existed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0043","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0044","text":"To the seventh, it must be said that, as is said in IV Physics, prior and posterior are in time according as prior and posterior are in motion. Hence beginning and end must be understood in time as also in motion. But if the eternity of motion is presupposed, it is necessary that every instant taken in motion be a beginning and a terminus of motion; this need not be so if motion begins. And the same reasoning applies to the now of time. Thus it is clear that the argument concerning the instant now, namely, that it is always the beginning and end of time, presupposes the eternity of time and motion. Hence Aristotle adduces this argument in VIII Physics against those who held the eternity of time but denied the eternity of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0045","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0046","text":"To the eighth, it must be said that God is prior to the world in duration. But the word *prior* does not designate priority of time, but of eternity. Or it may be said that it designates the eternity of imagined time, and not of time really existing. Thus, when it is said that there is nothing above heaven, the word *above* designates only an imagined place, insofar as it is possible to imagine dimensions other than those of the heavenly body added beyond its dimensions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0047","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 9](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0048","text":"To the ninth, it must be said that, just as an effect follows from a naturally acting cause according to the mode of its form, so it follows from an agent acting through will according to the form preconceived and determined by it, as is clear from the foregoing. Therefore, although God was from eternity a sufficient cause of the world, it need not be held that the world was produced by him except according as it is in the predetermination of his will: namely, that it should have being after non-being, so that it may declare its author more manifestly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0049","text":"[I q. 46 a. 1 ad 10](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0050","text":"To the tenth, it must be said that, once an action is posited, an effect follows according to the requirement of the form that is the principle of the action. But in agents acting through will, that which is conceived and preordained is taken as the form that is the principle of the action. Therefore, from God's eternal action there does not follow an eternal effect, but such an effect as God willed, namely, that it should have being after not-being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0051","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0052","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0053","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the world's having begun is not an article of faith, but a demonstrable conclusion. For everything made has a beginning of its duration. But it can be demonstrated that God is the efficient cause of the world, and even the more probable philosophers have maintained this. Therefore it can be demonstrated that the world began.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0054","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0055","text":"Further, if it is necessary to say that the world was made by God, then it was made either from nothing or from something. But not from something, because in that case the matter of the world would have preceded the world; against which proceed Aristotle's arguments maintaining that the heaven is ungenerated. Therefore one must say that the world was made from nothing. And thus it has being after not-being. Therefore it must have begun to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0056","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0057","text":"Further, everything that works through intellect works from some beginning, as is evident in all craftsmen. But God is an agent through intellect. Therefore the world, which is his effect, did not always exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0058","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0059","text":"Further, it is manifestly apparent that certain arts and the habitation of regions began at determinate times. But this would not be so if the world had always existed. Therefore it is manifest that the world has not always existed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0060","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0061","text":"Further, it is certain that nothing can be equated with God. But if the world had always existed, it would be made equal to God in duration. Therefore it is certain that the world has not always existed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0062","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0063","text":"Further, if the world has always existed, infinite days preceded this day. But an infinite cannot be traversed. Therefore one would never have arrived at this day, which is manifestly false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0064","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0065","text":"Further, if the world was eternal, generation too was from eternity. Therefore one man has been begotten by another to infinity. But a father is the efficient cause of his son, as is said in *Physics* II. Therefore there is an infinite regress in efficient causes, which is disproved in *Metaphysics* II.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0066","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 arg. 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0067","text":"Further, if the world and generation have always existed, infinitely many men have preceded. But the human soul is immortal. Therefore infinitely many human souls would now exist actually, which is impossible. Therefore it can be known of necessity that the world began; and it is not held by faith alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0068","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0069","text":"On the contrary, articles of faith cannot be demonstratively proved, *because faith is concerning things not apparent*, as is said in Hebrews XI. But that God is the creator of the world, such that the world began to be, is an article of faith; for we say, I believe in one God, and so forth. And again, Gregory says, in Homily I on Ezekiel, that Moses prophesied concerning the past, saying: In the beginning God created heaven and earth; in which the newness of the world is handed down. Therefore the newness of the world is held only through revelation. And therefore it cannot be demonstratively proved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0070","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0071","text":"I answer that it must be said that the world has not always existed is held by faith alone, and cannot be demonstratively proved, just as was said above concerning the mystery of the Trinity. The reason for this is that the newness of the world cannot admit of demonstration from the side of the world itself. For the principle of demonstration is what a thing is. But each thing, according to the account of its species, abstracts from here and now; on account of which it is said that universals are everywhere and always. Hence it cannot be demonstrated that man, or heaven, or stone has not always existed. Likewise, neither can it be demonstrated from the side of the efficient cause, which acts through will. For God's will cannot be investigated by reason except concerning those things which it is absolutely necessary for God to will; but such are not the things that he wills concerning creatures, as has been said. Yet the divine will can be made known to man through revelation, on which faith relies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0072","text":"Hence, that the world began is believable, but not demonstrable or knowable. And it is useful to consider this, lest perhaps someone, presuming to demonstrate what belongs to faith, adduce non-necessary arguments that furnish matter for unbelievers to mock, since they suppose that we believe the matters of faith on account of arguments of this sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0073","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0074","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, as Augustine says in XI *On the City of God*, there was a twofold opinion among philosophers who posited the eternity of the world. For some held that the substance of the world is not from God. And theirs is an intolerable error; and therefore it is necessarily refuted. But others so held the world to be eternal that nevertheless they said the world was made by God. *For they do not wish the world to have a beginning in time, but a beginning of its creation, so that in some manner scarcely intelligible it may always have been made. But how they understand this, they have discovered*, as he says in X *On the City of God*. *For, they say, just as if a foot had always from eternity been in the dust, a footprint would always lie beneath it, which no one would doubt had been made by the one treading; so too the world has always existed, since the one who made it has always existed*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0075","text":"And for understanding this, it must be considered that an efficient cause which acts through motion necessarily precedes its effect in time, because the effect exists only at the terminus of the action, whereas every agent must be the principle of the action. But if the action is instantaneous and not successive, it is not necessary that the maker be prior to the thing made in duration; as is clear in illumination. Hence they say that it does not follow necessarily, if God is the active cause of the world, that He is prior to the world in duration, because creation, by which He produced the world, is not a successive change, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0076","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0077","text":"To the second it must be said that those who posited the world to be eternal would say that the world was made by God from nothing, not because it was made after nothing, according to what we understand by the name of creation, but because it was not made from anything. And thus some of them do not reject even the name of creation, as is clear from Avicenna in his *Metaphysics*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0078","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0079","text":"To the third it must be said that this is the argument of Anaxagoras, which is set forth in III *Physics*. But it does not necessarily conclude except concerning an intellect that, by deliberating, investigates what is to be done, which is similar to motion. Now such is the human intellect, but not the divine, as is clear above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0080","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0081","text":"To the fourth it must be said that those who posit the eternity of the world hold that some region has infinitely often been changed from uninhabitable to habitable, and conversely. And likewise they hold that the arts, owing to various destructions and accidents, have infinitely often been discovered and again destroyed. Hence Aristotle says, in the book *Meteorology*, that it is ridiculous to derive an opinion about the newness of the whole world from particular changes of this kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0082","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpustomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0083","text":"To the fifth it must be said that, even if the world had always existed, it would nevertheless not be made equal to God in eternity, as Boethius says at the end of *On Consolation*, because divine being is being wholly simultaneous, without succession; but this is not so of the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0084","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0085","text":"To the sixth it must be said that passage is always understood from a terminus to a terminus. But whatever past day be designated, from that day up to this one there are finite days, which can be traversed. The objection, however, proceeds as if, with the extremes posited, the intermediates were infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0086","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0087","text":"To the seventh it must be said that in efficient causes it is impossible to proceed to infinity *per se*; as, for example, if the causes that are required *per se* for some effect were multiplied to infinity, as if a stone were moved by a stick, and the stick by a hand, and so on to infinity. But to proceed to infinity *per accidens* in agent causes is not held to be impossible; as, for example, if all the causes that are multiplied to infinity hold the order only of one cause, but their multiplication is *per accidens*; as an artisan acts with many hammers *per accidens*, because one breaks after another. Therefore, it is accidental to this hammer that it acts after the action of another hammer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0088","text":"And similarly, it is accidental to this man, insofar as he generates, that he was generated by another; for he generates insofar as he is a man, and not insofar as he is the son of another man. For all generating men have one rank in efficient causes, namely, the rank of a particular generator. Hence it is not impossible that man be generated by man to infinity. But it would be impossible if the generation of this man depended upon this man, and upon an elemental body, and upon the sun, and so on to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0089","text":"[I q. 46 a. 2 ad 8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0090","text":"To the eighth it must be said that those who posit the eternity of the world evade this argument in many ways. For some do not hold it impossible that there be infinitely many souls actually; as is clear in the *Metaphysics* of Algazel, who says that this is infinite *per accidens*. But this was disproved above. Others indeed say that the soul is corrupted with the body. Others, however, say that of all souls only one remains. Others, as Augustine says, posited for this reason a cycle of souls: namely, that souls separated from bodies, after determinate cycles of time, would return again to bodies. All of these matters must be treated in what follows. Nevertheless, it must be considered that this argument is particular. Hence someone could say that the world was eternal, or at least some creature, such as an angel, but not man. But we intend universally to ask whether any creature has existed from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0091","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0092","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0093","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the creation of things was not at the beginning of time. For what is not in time is not in any part of time. But the creation of things was not in time, for through the creation of things their substance was brought forth into being; but time does not measure the substance of things, and especially not of incorporeal things. Therefore, creation was not at the beginning of time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0094","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0095","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher proves that everything which comes to be was coming to be, and thus every coming-to-be has a before and an after. But at the beginning of time, since it is indivisible, there is no before and after. Therefore, since to be created is a certain coming-to-be, it seems that things were not created at the beginning of time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0096","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0097","text":"Moreover, time itself was also created. But it cannot be created at the beginning of time, since time is divisible, whereas the beginning of time is indivisible. Therefore, the creation of things was not at the beginning of time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0098","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0099","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. I, *In the beginning God created heaven and earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0100","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0101","text":"I answer that, the phrase in Gen. I, *In the beginning God created heaven and earth*, is explained in three ways, in order to exclude three errors. For some held that the world had always existed and that time had no beginning. And in order to exclude this, “in the beginning” is explained as, namely, of time. Others, however, held that there are two principles of creation, one of good things, the other of evil things. And in order to exclude this, “in the beginning” is explained as, that is, in the Son. For just as the effective principle is appropriated to the Father on account of power, so the exemplary principle is appropriated to the Son on account of wisdom, so that, just as it is said, “You have made all things in wisdom,” so it may be understood that God made all things in the beginning, that is, in the Son; according to that saying of the Apostle in Col. I: “In him,” namely the Son, “all things were created.” Others, moreover, said that corporeal things were created by God through the mediation of spiritual creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0102","text":"And in order to exclude this, “In the beginning God created heaven and earth” is explained as, that is, before all things. For four things are posited as created simultaneously: namely, the Empyrean heaven, corporeal matter (which is understood by the name of earth), time, and the angelic nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0103","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0104","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that things are not said to have been created in the beginning of time as though the beginning of time were the measure of creation, but because heaven and earth were created simultaneously with time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0105","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0106","text":"To the second, it must be said that that statement of the Philosopher is understood concerning coming-to-be which is through motion, or which is the terminus of motion. For since in every motion there is a before and an after, before any designated point in a designated motion, while, namely, something is in being moved and in coming-to-be, there is a before to be taken, and also something after it; because what is at the beginning of motion, or at its terminus, is not in being moved. But creation is neither motion nor the terminus of motion, as was said above. Hence something is so created that it was not previously being created.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0107","text":"[I q. 46 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0108","text":"To the third, it must be said that nothing comes to be except insofar as it is. But nothing of time exists except the now. Hence it cannot come to be except according to some now, not because time is in that first now itself, but because time begins from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_38:P0108"]}],"source_digest":"3a557c1295aea5d72a7cf76ba007f8b3a253179b7dff7d82ed85e3122055670d","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 46"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 46","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39","title":"First Part · Question 47","order":48,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_39","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 47 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30437)\n\nPost productionem creaturarum in esse, considerandum est de distinctione earum. Erit autem haec consideratio tripartita. Nam primo considerabimus de distinctione rerum in communi; secundo, de distinctione boni et mali; tertio, de distinctione spiritualis et corporalis creaturae. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, de ipsa rerum multitudine seu distinctione. Secundo, de earum inaequalitate. Tertio, de unitate mundi.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30438)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod rerum multitudo et distinctio non sit a Deo. Unum enim semper natum est unum facere. Sed Deus est maxime unus, ut ex praemissis patet. Ergo non producit nisi unum effectum.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30439)\n\nPraeterea, exemplatum assimilatur suo exemplari. Sed Deus est causa exemplaris sui effectus, ut supra dictum est. Ergo, cum Deus sit unus, effectus eius est unus tantum, et non distinctus.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30440)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae sunt ad finem, proportionantur fini. Sed finis creaturae est unus, scilicet divina bonitas, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo effectus Dei non est nisi unus.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30441)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, quod *Deus distinxit lucem a tenebris, et divisit aquas ab aquis*. Ergo distinctio et multitudo rerum est a Deo.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30442)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod causam distinctionis rerum multipliciter aliqui assignaverunt. Quidam enim attribuerunt eam materiae, vel soli, vel simul cum agente. Soli quidem materiae, sicut Democritus, et omnes antiqui naturales, ponentes solam causam materialem, secundum quos distinctio rerum provenit a casu, secundum motum materiae. Materiae vero et agenti simul distinctionem et multitudinem rerum attribuit Anaxagoras, qui posuit intellectum distinguentem res, extrahendo quod erat permixtum in materia. Sed hoc non potest stare propter duo. Primo quidem, quia supra ostensum est quod etiam ipsa materia a Deo creata est. Unde oportet et distinctionem, si qua est ex parte materiae, in altiorem causam reducere. Secundo, quia materia est propter formam, et non e converso. Distinctio autem rerum est per formas proprias. Non ergo distinctio est in rebus propter materiam, sed potius e converso in materia creata est difformitas, ut esset diversis formis accommodata. Quidam vero attribuerunt distinctionem rerum secundis agentibus. Sicut Avicenna, qui dixit quod Deus, intelligendo se, produxit intelligentiam primam, in qua, quia non est suum esse, ex necessitate incidit compositio potentiae et actus, ut infra patebit. Sic igitur prima intelligentia, inquantum intelligit causam primam, produxit secundam intelligentiam; inquantum autem intelligit se secundum quod est in potentia, produxit corpus caeli, quod movet; inquantum vero intelligit se secundum illud quod habet de actu, produxit animam caeli. Sed hoc non potest stare propter duo. Primo quidem, quia supra ostensum est quod creare solius Dei est. Unde ea quae non possunt causari nisi per creationem, a solo Deo producuntur, et haec sunt omnia quae non subiacent generationi et corruptioni. Secundo, quia secundum hanc positionem, non proveniret ex intentione primi agentis universitas rerum, sed ex concursu multarum causarum agentium. Tale autem dicimus provenire a casu. Sic igitur complementum universi, quod in diversitate rerum consistit, esset a casu, quod est impossibile. Unde dicendum est quod distinctio rerum et multitudo est ex intentione primi agentis, quod est Deus. Produxit enim res in esse propter suam bonitatem communicandam creaturis, et per eas repraesentandam. Et quia per unam creaturam sufficienter repraesentari non potest, produxit multas creaturas et diversas, ut quod deest uni ad repraesentandam divinam bonitatem, suppleatur ex alia, nam bonitas quae in Deo est simpliciter et uniformiter, in creaturis est multipliciter et divisim. Unde perfectius participat divinam bonitatem, et repraesentat eam, totum universum, quam alia quaecumque creatura. Et quia ex divina sapientia est causa distinctionis rerum, ideo Moyses dicit res esse distinctas verbo Dei, quod est conceptio sapientiae. Et hoc est quod dicitur Gen. I, *dixit Deus, fiat lux. Et divisit lucem a tenebris*.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30443)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod agens per naturam agit per formam per quam est, quae unius tantum est una, et ideo non agit nisi unum. Agens autem voluntarium, quale est Deus, ut supra ostensum est, agit per formam intellectam. Cum igitur Deum multa intelligere non repugnet unitati et simplicitati ipsius, ut supra ostensum est, relinquitur quod, licet sit unus, possit multa facere.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30444)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio illa teneret de exemplato quod perfecte repraesentat exemplar, quod non multiplicatur nisi materialiter. Unde imago increata, quae est perfecta, est una tantum. Sed nulla creatura repraesentat perfecte exemplar primum, quod est divina essentia. Et ideo potest per multa repraesentari. Et tamen secundum quod ideae dicuntur exemplaria, pluralitati rerum correspondet in mente divina pluralitas idearum.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30445)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in speculativis medium demonstrationis, quod perfecte demonstrat conclusionem, est unum tantum, sed media probabilia sunt multa. Et similiter in operativis, quando id quod est ad finem adaequat, ut ita dixerim, finem, non requiritur quod sit nisi unum tantum. Sed creatura non sic se habet ad finem qui est Deus. Unde oportuit creaturas multiplicari.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30446)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inaequalitas rerum non sit a Deo. Optimi enim est optima adducere. Sed inter optima unum non est maius altero. Ergo Dei, qui est optimus, est omnia aequalia facere.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30447)\n\nPraeterea, aequalitas est effectus unitatis, ut dicitur in V Metaphys. Sed Deus est unus. Ergo fecit omnia aequalia.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30448)\n\nPraeterea, iustitiae est inaequalia inaequalibus dare. Sed Deus est iustus in omnibus operibus suis. Cum ergo operationi eius, qua esse rebus communicat, non praesupponatur aliqua inaequalitas rerum, videtur quod fecerit omnia aequalia.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30449)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Eccli. XXXIII, *quare dies diem superat, et iterum lux lucem, et annus annum, sol solem? A domini scientia separata sunt*.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30450)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Origenes, volens excludere positionem ponentium distinctionem in rebus ex contrarietate principiorum boni et mali, posuit a Deo a principio omnia creata esse aequalia. Dicit enim quod Deus primo creavit creaturas rationales tantum, et omnes aequales, in quibus primo exorta est inaequalitas ex libero arbitrio, quibusdam conversis in Deum secundum magis et minus, quibusdam etiam secundum magis et minus a Deo aversis. Illae igitur rationales creaturae quae ad Deum per liberum arbitrium conversae sunt, promotae sunt ad diversos ordines Angelorum, pro diversitate meritorum. Illae autem quae aversae sunt a Deo, sunt corporibus alligatae diversis, secundum diversitatem peccati, et hanc causam dicit esse creationis et diversitatis corporum. Sed secundum hoc, universitas corporalium creaturarum non esset propter bonitatem Dei communicandam creaturis, sed ad puniendum peccatum. Quod est contra illud quod dicitur Gen. I, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*. Et ut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, *quid stultius dici potest, quam istum solem, ut in uno mundo unus esset, non decori pulchritudinis, vel saluti rerum corporalium consuluisse artificem Deum; sed hoc potius evenisse, quia una anima sic peccaverat? Ac per hoc, si centum animae peccassent, centum soles haberet hic mundus*. Et ideo dicendum est quod, sicut sapientia Dei est causa distinctionis rerum, ita et inaequalitatis. Quod sic patet. Duplex enim distinctio invenitur in rebus, una formalis, in his quae differunt specie; alia vero materialis, in his quae differunt numero tantum. Cum autem materia sit propter formam, distinctio materialis est propter formalem. Unde videmus quod in rebus incorruptibilibus non est nisi unum individuum unius speciei, quia species sufficienter conservatur in uno, in generabilibus autem et corruptibilibus, sunt multa individua unius speciei, ad conservationem speciei. Ex quo patet quod principalior est distinctio formalis quam materialis. Distinctio autem formalis semper requirit inaequalitatem, quia, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys., formae rerum sunt sicut numeri, in quibus species variantur per additionem vel subtractionem unitatis. Unde in rebus naturalibus gradatim species ordinatae esse videntur, sicut mixta perfectiora sunt elementis, et plantae corporibus mineralibus, et animalia plantis, et homines aliis animalibus; et in singulis horum una species perfectior aliis invenitur. Sicut ergo divina sapientia causa est distinctionis rerum propter perfectionem universi, ita et inaequalitatis. Non enim esset perfectum universum, si tantum unus gradus bonitatis inveniretur in rebus.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30451)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod optimi agentis est producere totum effectum suum optimum, non tamen quod quamlibet partem totius faciat optimam simpliciter, sed optimam secundum proportionem ad totum, tolleretur enim bonitas animalis, si quaelibet pars eius oculi haberet dignitatem. Sic igitur et Deus totum universum constituit optimum, secundum modum creaturae, non autem singulas creaturas, sed unam alia meliorem. Et ideo de singulis creaturis dicitur Gen. I, *vidit Deus lucem quod esset bona*, et similiter de singulis, sed de omnibus simul dicitur, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30452)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod primum quod procedit ab unitate, est aequalitas; et deinde procedit multiplicitas. Et ideo a patre, cui, secundum Augustinum, appropriatur unitas, processit filius, cui appropriatur aequalitas; et deinde creatura, cui competit inaequalitas. Sed tamen etiam a creaturis participatur quaedam aequalitas, scilicet proportionis.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30453)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa est quae movit Origenem, sed non habet locum nisi in retributione praemiorum, quorum inaequalitas debetur inaequalibus meritis. Sed in constitutione rerum non est inaequalitas partium per quamcumque inaequalitatem praecedentem vel meritorum vel etiam dispositionis materiae; sed propter perfectionem totius. Ut patet etiam in operibus artis, non enim propter hoc differt tectum a fundamento, quia habet diversam materiam; sed ut sit domus perfecta ex diversis partibus, quaerit artifex diversam materiam, et faceret eam si posset.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30454)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit unus mundus tantum, sed plures. Quia, ut Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest., inconveniens est dicere quod Deus sine ratione res creavit. Sed ea ratione qua creavit unum, potuit creare multos, cum eius potentia non sit limitata ad unius mundi creationem, sed est infinita, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo Deus plures mundos produxit.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30455)\n\nPraeterea, natura facit quod melius est, et multo magis Deus. Sed melius esset esse plures mundos quam unum, quia plura bona paucioribus meliora sunt. Ergo plures mundi facti sunt a Deo.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30456)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod habet formam in materia, potest multiplicari secundum numerum, manente eadem specie, quia multiplicatio secundum numerum est ex materia. Sed mundus habet formam in materia, sicut enim cum dico homo, significo formam, cum autem dico hic homo, significo formam in materia; ita, cum dicitur mundus, significatur forma, cum autem dicitur hic mundus, significatur forma in materia. Ergo nihil prohibet esse plures mundos.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30457)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. I, *mundus per ipsum factus est*; ubi singulariter mundum nominavit, quasi uno solo mundo existente.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30458)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ipse ordo in rebus sic a Deo creatis existens, unitatem mundi manifestat. Mundus enim iste unus dicitur unitate ordinis, secundum quod quaedam ad alia ordinantur. Quaecumque autem sunt a Deo, ordinem habent ad invicem et ad ipsum Deum, ut supra ostensum est. Unde necesse est quod omnia ad unum mundum pertineant. Et ideo illi potuerunt ponere plures mundos, qui causam mundi non posuerunt aliquam sapientiam ordinantem, sed casum; ut Democritus, qui dixit ex concursu atomorum factum esse hunc mundum, et alios infinitos.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30459)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod haec ratio est quare mundus est unus, quia debent omnia esse ordinata uno ordine, et ad unum. Propter quod Aristoteles, in XII Metaphys., ex unitate ordinis in rebus existentis concludit unitatem Dei gubernantis. Et Plato ex unitate exemplaris probat unitatem mundi, quasi exemplati.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30460)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nullum agens intendit pluralitatem materialem ut finem, quia materialis multitudo non habet certum terminum, sed de se tendit in infinitum; infinitum autem repugnat rationi finis. Cum autem dicitur plures mundos esse meliores quam unum, hoc dicitur secundum multitudinem materialem. Tale autem melius non est de intentione Dei agentis, quia eadem ratione dici posset quod, si fecisset duos, melius esset quod essent tres; et sic in infinitum.\n\n[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30461)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod mundus constat ex sua tota materia. Non enim est possibile esse aliam terram quam istam, quia omnis terra ferretur naturaliter ad hoc medium, ubicumque esset. Et eadem ratio est de aliis corporibus quae sunt partes mundi.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0002","text":"[I q. 47 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0003","text":"Post productionem creaturarum in esse, considerandum est de distinctione earum. Erit autem haec consideratio tripartita. Nam primo considerabimus de distinctione rerum in communi; secundo, de distinctione boni et mali; tertio, de distinctione spiritualis et corporalis creaturae. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, de ipsa rerum multitudine seu distinctione. Secundo, de earum inaequalitate. Tertio, de unitate mundi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0005","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod rerum multitudo et distinctio non sit a Deo. Unum enim semper natum est unum facere. Sed Deus est maxime unus, ut ex praemissis patet. Ergo non producit nisi unum effectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0007","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0008","text":"Praeterea, exemplatum assimilatur suo exemplari. Sed Deus est causa exemplaris sui effectus, ut supra dictum est. Ergo, cum Deus sit unus, effectus eius est unus tantum, et non distinctus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0009","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0010","text":"Praeterea, ea quae sunt ad finem, proportionantur fini. Sed finis creaturae est unus, scilicet divina bonitas, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo effectus Dei non est nisi unus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0011","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, quod *Deus distinxit lucem a tenebris, et divisit aquas ab aquis*. Ergo distinctio et multitudo rerum est a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0013","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod causam distinctionis rerum multipliciter aliqui assignaverunt. Quidam enim attribuerunt eam materiae, vel soli, vel simul cum agente. Soli quidem materiae, sicut Democritus, et omnes antiqui naturales, ponentes solam causam materialem, secundum quos distinctio rerum provenit a casu, secundum motum materiae. Materiae vero et agenti simul distinctionem et multitudinem rerum attribuit Anaxagoras, qui posuit intellectum distinguentem res, extrahendo quod erat permixtum in materia. Sed hoc non potest stare propter duo. Primo quidem, quia supra ostensum est quod etiam ipsa materia a Deo creata est. Unde oportet et distinctionem, si qua est ex parte materiae, in altiorem causam reducere. Secundo, quia materia est propter formam, et non e converso. Distinctio autem rerum est per formas proprias.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0015","text":"Non ergo distinctio est in rebus propter materiam, sed potius e converso in materia creata est difformitas, ut esset diversis formis accommodata. Quidam vero attribuerunt distinctionem rerum secundis agentibus. Sicut Avicenna, qui dixit quod Deus, intelligendo se, produxit intelligentiam primam, in qua, quia non est suum esse, ex necessitate incidit compositio potentiae et actus, ut infra patebit. Sic igitur prima intelligentia, inquantum intelligit causam primam, produxit secundam intelligentiam; inquantum autem intelligit se secundum quod est in potentia, produxit corpus caeli, quod movet; inquantum vero intelligit se secundum illud quod habet de actu, produxit animam caeli. Sed hoc non potest stare propter duo. Primo quidem, quia supra ostensum est quod creare solius Dei est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0016","text":"Unde ea quae non possunt causari nisi per creationem, a solo Deo producuntur, et haec sunt omnia quae non subiacent generationi et corruptioni. Secundo, quia secundum hanc positionem, non proveniret ex intentione primi agentis universitas rerum, sed ex concursu multarum causarum agentium. Tale autem dicimus provenire a casu. Sic igitur complementum universi, quod in diversitate rerum consistit, esset a casu, quod est impossibile. Unde dicendum est quod distinctio rerum et multitudo est ex intentione primi agentis, quod est Deus. Produxit enim res in esse propter suam bonitatem communicandam creaturis, et per eas repraesentandam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0017","text":"Et quia per unam creaturam sufficienter repraesentari non potest, produxit multas creaturas et diversas, ut quod deest uni ad repraesentandam divinam bonitatem, suppleatur ex alia, nam bonitas quae in Deo est simpliciter et uniformiter, in creaturis est multipliciter et divisim. Unde perfectius participat divinam bonitatem, et repraesentat eam, totum universum, quam alia quaecumque creatura. Et quia ex divina sapientia est causa distinctionis rerum, ideo Moyses dicit res esse distinctas verbo Dei, quod est conceptio sapientiae. Et hoc est quod dicitur Gen. I, *dixit Deus, fiat lux. Et divisit lucem a tenebris*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0018","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod agens per naturam agit per formam per quam est, quae unius tantum est una, et ideo non agit nisi unum. Agens autem voluntarium, quale est Deus, ut supra ostensum est, agit per formam intellectam. Cum igitur Deum multa intelligere non repugnet unitati et simplicitati ipsius, ut supra ostensum est, relinquitur quod, licet sit unus, possit multa facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0020","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio illa teneret de exemplato quod perfecte repraesentat exemplar, quod non multiplicatur nisi materialiter. Unde imago increata, quae est perfecta, est una tantum. Sed nulla creatura repraesentat perfecte exemplar primum, quod est divina essentia. Et ideo potest per multa repraesentari. Et tamen secundum quod ideae dicuntur exemplaria, pluralitati rerum correspondet in mente divina pluralitas idearum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0022","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in speculativis medium demonstrationis, quod perfecte demonstrat conclusionem, est unum tantum, sed media probabilia sunt multa. Et similiter in operativis, quando id quod est ad finem adaequat, ut ita dixerim, finem, non requiritur quod sit nisi unum tantum. Sed creatura non sic se habet ad finem qui est Deus. Unde oportuit creaturas multiplicari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0025","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inaequalitas rerum non sit a Deo. Optimi enim est optima adducere. Sed inter optima unum non est maius altero. Ergo Dei, qui est optimus, est omnia aequalia facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0027","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0028","text":"Praeterea, aequalitas est effectus unitatis, ut dicitur in V Metaphys. Sed Deus est unus. Ergo fecit omnia aequalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0029","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0030","text":"Praeterea, iustitiae est inaequalia inaequalibus dare. Sed Deus est iustus in omnibus operibus suis. Cum ergo operationi eius, qua esse rebus communicat, non praesupponatur aliqua inaequalitas rerum, videtur quod fecerit omnia aequalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0031","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Eccli. XXXIII, *quare dies diem superat, et iterum lux lucem, et annus annum, sol solem? A domini scientia separata sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0033","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Origenes, volens excludere positionem ponentium distinctionem in rebus ex contrarietate principiorum boni et mali, posuit a Deo a principio omnia creata esse aequalia. Dicit enim quod Deus primo creavit creaturas rationales tantum, et omnes aequales, in quibus primo exorta est inaequalitas ex libero arbitrio, quibusdam conversis in Deum secundum magis et minus, quibusdam etiam secundum magis et minus a Deo aversis. Illae igitur rationales creaturae quae ad Deum per liberum arbitrium conversae sunt, promotae sunt ad diversos ordines Angelorum, pro diversitate meritorum. Illae autem quae aversae sunt a Deo, sunt corporibus alligatae diversis, secundum diversitatem peccati, et hanc causam dicit esse creationis et diversitatis corporum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0035","text":"Sed secundum hoc, universitas corporalium creaturarum non esset propter bonitatem Dei communicandam creaturis, sed ad puniendum peccatum. Quod est contra illud quod dicitur Gen. I, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*. Et ut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, *quid stultius dici potest, quam istum solem, ut in uno mundo unus esset, non decori pulchritudinis, vel saluti rerum corporalium consuluisse artificem Deum; sed hoc potius evenisse, quia una anima sic peccaverat? Ac per hoc, si centum animae peccassent, centum soles haberet hic mundus*. Et ideo dicendum est quod, sicut sapientia Dei est causa distinctionis rerum, ita et inaequalitatis. Quod sic patet. Duplex enim distinctio invenitur in rebus, una formalis, in his quae differunt specie; alia vero materialis, in his quae differunt numero tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0036","text":"Cum autem materia sit propter formam, distinctio materialis est propter formalem. Unde videmus quod in rebus incorruptibilibus non est nisi unum individuum unius speciei, quia species sufficienter conservatur in uno, in generabilibus autem et corruptibilibus, sunt multa individua unius speciei, ad conservationem speciei. Ex quo patet quod principalior est distinctio formalis quam materialis. Distinctio autem formalis semper requirit inaequalitatem, quia, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys., formae rerum sunt sicut numeri, in quibus species variantur per additionem vel subtractionem unitatis. Unde in rebus naturalibus gradatim species ordinatae esse videntur, sicut mixta perfectiora sunt elementis, et plantae corporibus mineralibus, et animalia plantis, et homines aliis animalibus; et in singulis horum una species perfectior aliis invenitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0037","text":"Sicut ergo divina sapientia causa est distinctionis rerum propter perfectionem universi, ita et inaequalitatis. Non enim esset perfectum universum, si tantum unus gradus bonitatis inveniretur in rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0038","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0039","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod optimi agentis est producere totum effectum suum optimum, non tamen quod quamlibet partem totius faciat optimam simpliciter, sed optimam secundum proportionem ad totum, tolleretur enim bonitas animalis, si quaelibet pars eius oculi haberet dignitatem. Sic igitur et Deus totum universum constituit optimum, secundum modum creaturae, non autem singulas creaturas, sed unam alia meliorem. Et ideo de singulis creaturis dicitur Gen. I, *vidit Deus lucem quod esset bona*, et similiter de singulis, sed de omnibus simul dicitur, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0040","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0041","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod primum quod procedit ab unitate, est aequalitas; et deinde procedit multiplicitas. Et ideo a patre, cui, secundum Augustinum, appropriatur unitas, processit filius, cui appropriatur aequalitas; et deinde creatura, cui competit inaequalitas. Sed tamen etiam a creaturis participatur quaedam aequalitas, scilicet proportionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0042","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0043","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa est quae movit Origenem, sed non habet locum nisi in retributione praemiorum, quorum inaequalitas debetur inaequalibus meritis. Sed in constitutione rerum non est inaequalitas partium per quamcumque inaequalitatem praecedentem vel meritorum vel etiam dispositionis materiae; sed propter perfectionem totius. Ut patet etiam in operibus artis, non enim propter hoc differt tectum a fundamento, quia habet diversam materiam; sed ut sit domus perfecta ex diversis partibus, quaerit artifex diversam materiam, et faceret eam si posset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0045","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit unus mundus tantum, sed plures. Quia, ut Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest., inconveniens est dicere quod Deus sine ratione res creavit. Sed ea ratione qua creavit unum, potuit creare multos, cum eius potentia non sit limitata ad unius mundi creationem, sed est infinita, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo Deus plures mundos produxit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0047","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0048","text":"Praeterea, natura facit quod melius est, et multo magis Deus. Sed melius esset esse plures mundos quam unum, quia plura bona paucioribus meliora sunt. Ergo plures mundi facti sunt a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0049","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0050","text":"Praeterea, omne quod habet formam in materia, potest multiplicari secundum numerum, manente eadem specie, quia multiplicatio secundum numerum est ex materia. Sed mundus habet formam in materia, sicut enim cum dico homo, significo formam, cum autem dico hic homo, significo formam in materia; ita, cum dicitur mundus, significatur forma, cum autem dicitur hic mundus, significatur forma in materia. Ergo nihil prohibet esse plures mundos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0051","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0052","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. I, *mundus per ipsum factus est*; ubi singulariter mundum nominavit, quasi uno solo mundo existente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0053","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0054","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ipse ordo in rebus sic a Deo creatis existens, unitatem mundi manifestat. Mundus enim iste unus dicitur unitate ordinis, secundum quod quaedam ad alia ordinantur. Quaecumque autem sunt a Deo, ordinem habent ad invicem et ad ipsum Deum, ut supra ostensum est. Unde necesse est quod omnia ad unum mundum pertineant. Et ideo illi potuerunt ponere plures mundos, qui causam mundi non posuerunt aliquam sapientiam ordinantem, sed casum; ut Democritus, qui dixit ex concursu atomorum factum esse hunc mundum, et alios infinitos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0055","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0056","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod haec ratio est quare mundus est unus, quia debent omnia esse ordinata uno ordine, et ad unum. Propter quod Aristoteles, in XII Metaphys., ex unitate ordinis in rebus existentis concludit unitatem Dei gubernantis. Et Plato ex unitate exemplaris probat unitatem mundi, quasi exemplati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0057","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0058","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nullum agens intendit pluralitatem materialem ut finem, quia materialis multitudo non habet certum terminum, sed de se tendit in infinitum; infinitum autem repugnat rationi finis. Cum autem dicitur plures mundos esse meliores quam unum, hoc dicitur secundum multitudinem materialem. Tale autem melius non est de intentione Dei agentis, quia eadem ratione dici posset quod, si fecisset duos, melius esset quod essent tres; et sic in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0059","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0060","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod mundus constat ex sua tota materia. Non enim est possibile esse aliam terram quam istam, quia omnis terra ferretur naturaliter ad hoc medium, ubicumque esset. Et eadem ratio est de aliis corporibus quae sunt partes mundi.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e95c47f45e0e11c7c9626d5e8f77d80fbb21ae2c30466e55e9d3b339c13fb856","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143222274559Z_aaa6978e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 47","source_sha256":"e95c47f45e0e11c7c9626d5e8f77d80fbb21ae2c30466e55e9d3b339c13fb856","sha256":"9c685b8b554c39086e7257582fe386da1ff78b953435c9a3ebe0e7f6b56e477a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:32:22.274589+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0002","text":"[I q. 47 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0003","text":"After considering the production of creatures in being, we must consider their distinction. This consideration will be threefold. For first we shall consider the distinction of things in general; second, the distinction of good and evil; third, the distinction of spiritual and corporeal creature. Concerning the first, three questions are asked. First, concerning the multitude or distinction of things itself. Second, concerning their inequality. Third, concerning the unity of the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0005","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the multitude and distinction of things are not from God. For one is always naturally disposed to make one. But God is most one, as is clear from what has preceded. Therefore He produces only one effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0007","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0008","text":"Moreover, that which is patterned is assimilated to its exemplar. But God is the exemplar cause of His effect, as was said above. Therefore, since God is one, His effect is only one, and not distinct.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0009","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0010","text":"Moreover, things that are for an end are proportioned to the end. But the end of the creature is one, namely, the divine goodness, as was shown above. Therefore God's effect is only one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0011","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. I that *God distinguished light from darkness, and divided the waters from the waters*. Therefore the distinction and multitude of things are from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0013","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have assigned the cause of the distinction of things in various ways. For some attributed it to matter, either alone or together with an agent. To matter alone indeed, as Democritus and all the ancient natural philosophers, who posited matter alone as a cause, according to whom the distinction of things proceeds by chance, according to the motion of matter. But Anaxagoras attributed the distinction and multitude of things to matter and agent together, holding that intellect distinguishes things by drawing out what was mixed together in matter. But this cannot stand for two reasons. First, because it was shown above that even matter itself was created by God. Hence it is necessary also to reduce distinction, if any exists on the part of matter, to a higher cause. Second, because matter is for the sake of form, and not conversely. But the distinction of things is through their proper forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0015","text":"Therefore distinction is not in things on account of matter; rather, conversely, dissimilarity was created in matter so that it might be accommodated to diverse forms. But some attributed the distinction of things to secondary agents. Thus Avicenna, who said that God, by understanding Himself, produced the first intelligence, in which, because its being is not itself, there necessarily occurs a composition of potency and act, as will appear below. Thus, therefore, the first intelligence, insofar as it understands the first cause, produced the second intelligence; but insofar as it understands itself according as it is in potency, it produced the body of heaven, which it moves; while insofar as it understands itself according to that which it has of act, it produced the soul of heaven. But this cannot stand for two reasons. First, because it was shown above that to create belongs to God alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0016","text":"Hence those things which can be caused only through creation are produced by God alone; and these are all things that are not subject to generation and corruption. Second, because according to this position, the totality of things would not result from the intention of the first agent, but from the concurrence of many agent causes. But we say that such a thing results by chance. Thus the completion of the universe, which consists in the diversity of things, would be by chance, which is impossible. Hence it must be said that the distinction and multitude of things are from the intention of the first agent, which is God. For He produced things in being for the sake of communicating His goodness to creatures, and representing it through them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0017","text":"And because it cannot be sufficiently represented by one creature, He produced many and diverse creatures, so that what is lacking to one for representing the divine goodness might be supplied by another; for the goodness that is in God simply and uniformly is in creatures multiply and dividedly. Hence the whole universe participates in the divine goodness and represents it more perfectly than any other creature whatsoever. And because the divine wisdom is the cause of the distinction of things, Moses therefore says that things are distinguished by the word of God, which is a conception of wisdom. And this is what is said in Gen. I: *God said, Let light be made. And He divided the light from the darkness*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0018","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an agent acting by nature acts through the form by which it is, which is one only of one thing, and therefore it acts only one effect. But a voluntary agent, such as God is, as was shown above, acts through an understood form. Therefore, since understanding many things is not repugnant to God's unity and simplicity, as was shown above, it follows that, although He is one, He can make many things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0020","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that that argument would hold concerning what is patterned which perfectly represents the exemplar, which is multiplied only materially. Hence the uncreated image, which is perfect, is only one. But no creature perfectly represents the first exemplar, which is the divine essence. And therefore it can be represented through many things. Yet insofar as ideas are called exemplars, a plurality of ideas in the divine mind corresponds to the plurality of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0022","text":"[I q. 47 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that in speculative matters the middle term of demonstration, which perfectly demonstrates the conclusion, is only one; but probable middle terms are many. And similarly in practical matters, when that which is for an end equals, so to speak, the end, it is not required that it be more than one only. But the creature is not thus related to the end which is God. Hence creatures had to be multiplied.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0025","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second point. It seems that the inequality of things is not from God. For it belongs to the best to bring forth the best. But among the best, one is not greater than another. Therefore, it belongs to God, who is best, to make all things equal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0027","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0028","text":"Further, equality is an effect of unity, as is said in Metaphysics V. But God is one. Therefore, He made all things equal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0029","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0030","text":"Further, it belongs to justice to give unequal things to unequal persons. But God is just in all His works. Therefore, since no inequality of things is presupposed to His operation by which He communicates being to things, it seems that He made all things equal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0031","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0032","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Ecclesiasticus XXXIII: *why does day surpass day, and again light light, and year year, sun sun? They have been separated by the knowledge of the Lord*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0033","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0034","text":"I answer that Origen, wishing to exclude the position of those who posit a distinction among things from the contrariety of the principles of good and evil, held that in the beginning all things were created by God equal. For he says that God first created rational creatures only, and all equal, among whom inequality first arose from free choice: some being turned toward God more or less, and some also turned away from God more or less. Therefore, those rational creatures which were turned toward God through free choice were advanced to the diverse orders of Angels according to the diversity of their merits. But those which were turned away from God were bound to diverse bodies according to the diversity of their sin; and he says that this is the cause of the creation and diversity of bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0035","text":"But according to this, the whole universe of bodily creatures would not exist for the sake of communicating God's goodness to creatures, but for punishing sin. This is contrary to what is said in Genesis I: *God saw all things that He had made, and they were very good*. And as Augustine says, in City of God XI: *what more foolish thing can be said than that the divine Craftsman took thought that this sun should be one in one world, not for the beauty of its adornment or the welfare of bodily things, but that this happened rather because one soul had sinned in this way? And consequently, if a hundred souls had sinned, this world would have a hundred suns*. And therefore it must be said that, just as the wisdom of God is the cause of the distinction of things, so also is it the cause of inequality. This is evident as follows. For a twofold distinction is found in things: one formal, in those which differ in species; the other material, in those which differ only in number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0036","text":"But since matter exists for the sake of form, material distinction exists for the sake of formal distinction. Hence we see that among incorruptible things there is only one individual of one species, because the species is sufficiently preserved in one; but among generable and corruptible things there are many individuals of one species, for the preservation of the species. From this it is clear that formal distinction is more principal than material distinction. But formal distinction always requires inequality, because, as is said in Metaphysics VIII, the forms of things are like numbers, in which species vary through the addition or subtraction of unity. Hence among natural things species seem to be arranged by degrees: just as mixed bodies are more perfect than elements, plants than mineral bodies, animals than plants, and human beings than other animals; and in each of these, one species is found more perfect than the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0037","text":"Therefore, just as divine wisdom is the cause of the distinction of things for the perfection of the universe, so also it is the cause of inequality. For the universe would not be perfect if only one grade of goodness were found in things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0038","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0039","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that it belongs to the best agent to produce its whole effect as best, not, however, so that it makes every part of the whole best absolutely, but best according to its proportion to the whole; for the goodness of an animal would be removed if each of its parts had the dignity of an eye. Thus, therefore, God established the whole universe as best, according to the mode of a creature, but not each creature; rather, He made one better than another. Therefore, of individual creatures it is said in Genesis I, *God saw the light, that it was good*, and similarly of the individual things; but of all things together it is said, *God saw all things that He had made, and they were very good*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0040","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0041","text":"To the second, it must be said that the first thing that proceeds from unity is equality; and then multiplicity proceeds. Therefore, from the Father, to whom, according to Augustine, unity is appropriated, proceeded the Son, to whom equality is appropriated; and then the creature, to which inequality belongs. Yet a certain equality is also participated by creatures, namely, that of proportion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0042","text":"[I q. 47 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0043","text":"To the third, it must be said that this is the argument that moved Origen, but it has force only in the recompense of rewards, whose inequality is due to unequal merits. But in the establishment of things there is no inequality of parts through any preceding inequality, whether of merits or even of the disposition of matter, but on account of the perfection of the whole. This is also clear in works of art: for a roof differs from a foundation not because it has different matter, but, in order that there may be a perfect house from diverse parts, the craftsman seeks diverse matter, and would make it if he could.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0045","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0046","text":"We proceed thus to the third point. It seems that there is not only one world, but several. For, as Augustine says in the book Eighty-Three Questions, it is unfitting to say that God created things without reason. But by the reason by which He created one, He could create many, since His power is not limited to the creation of one world, but is infinite, as was shown above. Therefore, God produced several worlds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0047","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0048","text":"Further, nature makes what is better, and God much more so. But it would be better for there to be several worlds than one, because more goods are better than fewer. Therefore, several worlds were made by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0049","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0050","text":"Moreover, everything that has a form in matter can be multiplied according to number, while the same species remains, because multiplication according to number is from matter. But the world has a form in matter: for just as, when I say “man,” I signify a form, whereas when I say “this man,” I signify a form in matter; so, when “world” is said, a form is signified, whereas when “this world” is said, a form in matter is signified. Therefore, nothing prevents there being several worlds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0051","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0052","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in John 1, *the world was made through him*; where he named the world in the singular, as though only one world existed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0053","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0054","text":"I answer that it must be said that the very order existing in things thus created by God manifests the unity of the world. For this world is called one by the unity of order, insofar as some things are ordered to others. But whatever things are from God have an order among themselves and to God himself, as was shown above. Hence it is necessary that all things belong to one world. And therefore those were able to posit several worlds who posited as the cause of the world not some ordering wisdom, but chance; as Democritus, who said that this world, and infinitely many others, came to be from the concurrence of atoms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0055","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0056","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that this is the reason why the world is one: because all things ought to be ordered by one order and to one. On account of this, Aristotle, in Metaphysics XII, concludes from the unity of the order existing in things the unity of the governing God. And Plato proves from the unity of the exemplar the unity of the world, as of that which is exemplified.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0057","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0058","text":"To the second, it must be said that no agent intends material plurality as an end, because material multitude has no determinate limit, but of itself tends to infinity; but the infinite is incompatible with the notion of an end. Now, when it is said that several worlds are better than one, this is said with respect to material multitude. But such a better is not within the intention of God as agent, because by the same reasoning it could be said that, if he had made two, it would be better if there were three; and so on to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0059","text":"[I q. 47 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0060","text":"To the third, it must be said that the world consists of its whole matter. For it is not possible for there to be another earth than this one, because all earth would naturally be borne toward this center, wherever it might be. And the same reasoning holds concerning the other bodies that are parts of the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_39:P0060"]}],"source_digest":"3abc8c2544366953c7873530eb959e7e254871f52f63b35ffdb281fa1e03f024","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 47"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 47","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40","title":"First Part · Question 48","order":49,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_40","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 48 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30462)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de distinctione rerum in speciali. Et primo, de distinctione boni et mali; deinde de distinctione spiritualis et corporalis creaturae. Circa primum, quaerendum est de malo; et de causa mali. Circa malum quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum malum sit natura aliqua. Secundo, utrum malum inveniatur in rebus. Tertio, utrum bonum sit subiectum mali. Quarto, utrum malum totaliter corrumpat bonum. Quinto, de divisione mali per poenam et culpam. Sexto, quid habeat plus de ratione mali, utrum poena vel culpa.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30463)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum sit natura quaedam. Quia omne genus est natura quaedam. Sed malum est quoddam genus, dicitur enim in praedicamentis, quod *bonum et malum non sunt in genere, sed sunt genera aliorum*. Ergo malum est natura quaedam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30464)\n\nPraeterea, omnis differentia constitutiva alicuius speciei est natura quaedam. Malum autem est differentia constitutiva in moralibus, differt enim specie malus habitus a bono, ut liberalitas ab illiberalitate. Ergo malum significat naturam quandam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30465)\n\nPraeterea, utrumque contrariorum est natura quaedam. Sed malum et bonum non opponuntur ut privatio et habitus, sed ut contraria, ut probat philosophus, in praedicamentis, per hoc quod inter bonum et malum est aliquid medium, et a malo potest fieri reditus ad bonum. Ergo malum significat naturam quandam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30466)\n\nPraeterea, quod non est, non agit. Sed malum agit, quia corrumpit bonum. Ergo malum est quoddam ens, et natura quaedam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30467)\n\nPraeterea, ad perfectionem universitatis rerum non pertinet nisi quod est ens et natura quaedam. Sed malum pertinet ad perfectionem universitatis rerum, dicit enim Augustinus, in Enchirid., quod *ex omnibus consistit universitatis admirabilis pulchritudo; in qua etiam illud quod malum dicitur, bene ordinatum, et suo loco positum, eminentius commendat bona*. Ergo malum est natura quaedam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30468)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *malum non est existens neque bonum*.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30469)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod unum oppositorum cognoscitur per alterum, sicut per lucem tenebra. Unde et quid sit malum, oportet ex ratione boni accipere. Diximus autem supra quod bonum est omne id quod est appetibile, et sic, cum omnis natura appetat suum esse et suam perfectionem, necesse est dicere quod esse et perfectio cuiuscumque naturae rationem habeat bonitatis. Unde non potest esse quod malum significet quoddam esse, aut quandam formam seu naturam. Relinquitur ergo quod nomine mali significetur quaedam absentia boni. Et pro tanto dicitur quod malum neque est existens nec bonum, quia cum ens, inquantum huiusmodi, sit bonum, eadem est remotio utrorumque.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30470)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Aristoteles ibi loquitur secundum opinionem Pythagoricorum, qui malum existimabant esse naturam quandam, et ideo ponebant bonum et malum genera. Consuevit enim Aristoteles, et praecipue in libris logicalibus, ponere exempla quae probabilia erant suo tempore, secundum opinionem aliquorum philosophorum. Vel dicendum, sicut dicit philosophus in X Metaphys., quod *prima contrarietas est habitus et privatio*, quia scilicet in omnibus contrariis salvatur, cum semper unum contrariorum sit imperfectum respectu alterius, ut nigrum respectu albi, et amarum respectu dulcis. Et pro tanto bonum et malum dicuntur genera, non simpliciter, sed contrariorum, quia sicut omnis forma habet rationem boni, ita omnis privatio, inquantum huiusmodi, habet rationem mali.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30471)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bonum et malum non sunt differentiae constitutivae nisi in moralibus, quae recipiunt speciem ex fine, qui est obiectum voluntatis, a qua moralia dependent. Et quia bonum habet rationem finis, ideo bonum et malum sunt differentiae specificae in moralibus; bonum per se, sed malum inquantum est remotio debiti finis. Nec tamen remotio debiti finis constituit speciem in moralibus, nisi secundum quod adiungitur fini indebito, sicut neque in naturalibus invenitur privatio formae substantialis, nisi adiuncta alteri formae. Sic igitur malum quod est differentia constitutiva in moralibus, est quoddam bonum adiunctum privationi alterius boni, sicut finis intemperati est, non quidem carere bono rationis, sed delectabile sensus absque ordine rationis. Unde malum, inquantum malum, non est differentia constitutiva; sed ratione boni adiuncti.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30472)\n\nEt per hoc etiam patet responsio ad tertium. Nam ibi philosophus loquitur de bono et malo, secundum quod inveniuntur in moralibus. Sic enim inter bonum et malum invenitur medium, prout bonum dicitur quod est ordinatum; malum autem, quod non solum est deordinatum, sed etiam nocivum alteri. Unde dicit philosophus in IV Ethic., quod prodigus vanus quidem est, sed non malus. Ab hoc etiam malo quod est secundum morem, contingit fieri reditum ad bonum; non autem ex quocumque malo. Non enim ex caecitate fit reditus ad visionem, cum tamen caecitas sit malum quoddam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30473)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod aliquid agere dicitur tripliciter. Uno modo, formaliter, eo modo loquendi quo dicitur albedo facere album. Et sic malum, etiam ratione ipsius privationis, dicitur corrumpere bonum, quia est ipsa corruptio vel privatio boni. Alio modo dicitur aliquid agere effective, sicut pictor dicitur facere album parietem. Tertio modo, per modum causae finalis, sicut finis dicitur efficere, movendo efficientem. His autem duobus modis malum non agit aliquid per se, idest secundum quod est privatio quaedam, sed secundum quod ei bonum adiungitur, nam omnis actio est ab aliqua forma, et omne quod desideratur ut finis, est perfectio aliqua. Et ideo, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., malum non agit neque desideratur nisi virtute boni adiuncti; per se autem est infinitum, et praeter voluntatem et intentionem.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30474)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, partes universi habent ordinem ad invicem, secundum quod una agit in alteram, et est finis alterius et exemplar. Haec autem, ut dictum est, non possunt convenire malo, nisi ratione boni adiuncti. Unde malum neque ad perfectionem universi pertinet, neque sub ordine universi concluditur, nisi per accidens, idest ratione boni adiuncti.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30475)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum non inveniatur in rebus. Quidquid enim invenitur in rebus, vel est ens aliquod, vel privatio entis alicuius, quod est non ens. Sed Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod malum distat ab existente, et adhuc plus distat a non existente. Ergo malum nullo modo invenitur in rebus.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30476)\n\nPraeterea, ens et res convertuntur. Si ergo malum est ens in rebus, sequitur quod malum sit res quaedam. Quod est contra praedicta.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30477)\n\n*Praeterea, albius est quod est nigro impermixtius*, ut dicitur in III libro Topic. Aristotelis. Ergo et melius est quod est malo impermixtius. Sed Deus facit semper quod melius est, multo magis quam natura. Ergo in rebus a Deo conditis nihil malum invenitur.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30478)\n\nSed contra est quod secundum hoc removerentur omnes prohibitiones et poenae, quae non sunt nisi malorum.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30479)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, perfectio universi requirit inaequalitatem esse in rebus, ut omnes bonitatis gradus impleantur. Est autem unus gradus bonitatis ut aliquid ita bonum sit, quod nunquam deficere possit. Alius autem gradus bonitatis est, ut sic aliquid bonum sit, quod a bono deficere possit. Qui etiam gradus in ipso esse inveniuntur, quaedam enim sunt, quae suum esse amittere non possunt, ut incorporalia; quaedam vero sunt, quae amittere possunt, ut corporalia. Sicut igitur perfectio universitatis rerum requirit ut non solum sint entia incorruptibilia, sed etiam corruptibilia; ita perfectio universi requirit ut sint quaedam quae a bonitate deficere possint; ad quod sequitur ea interdum deficere. In hoc autem consistit ratio mali, ut scilicet aliquid deficiat a bono. Unde manifestum est quod in rebus malum invenitur, sicut et corruptio, nam et ipsa corruptio malum quoddam est.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30480)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod malum distat et ab ente simpliciter, et non ente simpliciter, quia neque est sicut habitus, neque sicut pura negatio, sed sicut privatio.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30481)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut dicitur in V Metaphys., ens dupliciter dicitur. Uno modo, secundum quod significat entitatem rei, prout dividitur per decem praedicamenta, et sic convertitur cum re. Et hoc modo, nulla privatio est ens, unde nec malum. Alio modo dicitur ens, quod significat veritatem propositionis, quae in compositione consistit, cuius nota est hoc verbum est, et hoc est ens quo respondetur ad quaestionem an est. Et sic caecitatem dicimus esse in oculo, vel quamcumque aliam privationem. Et hoc modo etiam malum dicitur ens. Propter huius autem distinctionis ignorantiam, aliqui, considerantes quod aliquae res dicuntur malae, vel quod malum dicitur esse in rebus, crediderunt quod malum esset res quaedam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30482)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Deus et natura, et quodcumque agens, facit quod melius est in toto; sed non quod melius est in unaquaque parte, nisi per ordinem ad totum, ut supra dictum est. Ipsum autem totum quod est universitas creaturarum, melius et perfectius est, si in eo sint quaedam quae a bono deficere possunt, quae interdum deficiunt, Deo hoc non impediente. Tum quia providentiae non est naturam destruere, sed salvare, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., ipsa autem natura rerum hoc habet, ut quae deficere possunt, quandoque deficiant. Tum quia, ut dicit Augustinus in Enchirid., Deus est adeo potens, quod etiam potest bene facere de malis. Unde multa bona tollerentur, si Deus nullum malum permitteret esse. Non enim generaretur ignis, nisi corrumperetur aer; neque conservaretur vita leonis, nisi occideretur asinus; neque etiam laudaretur iustitia vindicans, et patientia sufferens, si non esset iniquitas.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30483)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum non sit in bono sicut in subiecto. Omnia enim bona sunt existentia. Sed Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod malum non est existens, neque in existentibus. Ergo malum non est in bono sicut in subiecto.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30484)\n\nPraeterea, malum non est ens, bonum vero est ens. Sed non ens non requirit ens, in quo sit sicut in subiecto. Ergo nec malum requirit bonum, in quo sit sicut in subiecto.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30485)\n\nPraeterea, unum contrariorum non est subiectum alterius. Sed bonum et malum sunt contraria. Ergo malum non est in bono sicut in subiecto.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30486)\n\nPraeterea, id in quo est albedo sicut in subiecto, dicitur esse album. Ergo et id in quo est malum sicut in subiecto, est malum. Si ergo malum sit in bono sicut in subiecto, sequitur quod bonum sit malum, contra id quod dicitur Isai. V, *vae, qui dicitis malum bonum, et bonum malum*.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30487)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., quod malum non est nisi in bono.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30488)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, malum importat remotionem boni. Non autem quaelibet remotio boni malum dicitur. Potest enim accipi remotio boni et privative, et negative. Remotio igitur boni negative accepta, mali rationem non habet, alioquin sequeretur quod ea quae nullo modo sunt, mala essent; et iterum quod quaelibet res esset mala, ex hoc quod non habet bonum alterius rei, utpote quod homo esset malus, quia non habet velocitatem capreae, vel fortitudinem leonis. Sed remotio boni privative accepta, malum dicitur, sicut privatio visus caecitas dicitur. Subiectum autem privationis et formae est unum et idem, scilicet ens in potentia, sive sit ens in potentia simpliciter, sicut materia prima, quae est subiectum formae substantialis et privationis oppositae; sive sit ens in potentia secundum quid et in actu simpliciter, ut corpus diaphanum, quod est subiectum tenebrarum et lucis. Manifestum est autem quod forma per quam aliquid est actu, perfectio quaedam est, et bonum quoddam, et sic omne ens in actu, bonum quoddam est. Et similiter omne ens in potentia, inquantum huiusmodi, bonum quoddam est, secundum quod habet ordinem ad bonum, sicut enim est ens in potentia, ita et bonum in potentia. Relinquitur ergo quod subiectum mali sit bonum.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30489)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius intelligit malum non esse in existentibus sicut partem, aut sicut proprietatem naturalem alicuius existentis.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30490)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non ens negative acceptum non requirit subiectum. Sed privatio est negatio in subiecto, ut dicitur in IV Metaphys., et tale non ens est malum.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30491)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod malum non est sicut in subiecto in bono quod ei opponitur, sed in quodam alio bono, subiectum enim caecitatis non est visus, sed animal. Videtur tamen, ut Augustinus dicit, hic fallere dialecticorum regula, quae dicit contraria simul esse non posse. Hoc tamen intelligendum est secundum communem acceptionem boni et mali, non autem secundum quod specialiter accipitur hoc bonum et hoc malum. Album autem et nigrum, dulce et amarum, et huiusmodi contraria, non accipiuntur nisi specialiter, quia sunt in quibusdam generibus determinatis. Sed bonum circuit omnia genera, unde unum bonum potest simul esse cum privatione alterius boni.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30492)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod propheta imprecatur vae illis qui dicunt id quod est bonum, secundum quod est bonum, esse malum. Hoc autem non sequitur ex praemissis, ut per praedicta patet.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 48 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30493)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum corrumpat totum bonum. Unum enim contrariorum totaliter corrumpitur per alterum. Sed bonum et malum sunt contraria. Ergo malum potest corrumpere totum bonum.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30494)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., quod malum nocet inquantum adimit bonum. Sed bonum est sibi simile et uniforme. Ergo totaliter tollitur per malum.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30495)\n\nPraeterea, malum, quandiu est, nocet et aufert bonum. Sed illud a quo semper aliquid aufertur, quandoque consumitur, nisi sit infinitum; quod non potest dici de aliquo bono creato. Ergo malum consumit totaliter bonum.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30496)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., quod malum non potest totaliter consumere bonum.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30497)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod malum non potest totaliter consumere bonum. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod est triplex bonum. Quoddam, quod per malum totaliter tollitur, et hoc est bonum oppositum malo; sicut lumen totaliter per tenebras tollitur, et visus per caecitatem. Quoddam vero bonum est, quod nec totaliter tollitur per malum, nec diminuitur, scilicet bonum quod est subiectum mali; non enim per tenebras aliquid de substantia aeris diminuitur. Quoddam vero bonum est, quod diminuitur quidem per malum, sed non totaliter tollitur, et hoc bonum est habilitas subiecti ad actum. Diminutio autem huius boni non est accipienda per subtractionem, sicut est diminutio in quantitatibus, sed per remissionem, sicut est diminutio in qualitatibus et formis. Remissio autem huius habilitatis est accipienda e contrario intensioni ipsius. Intenditur enim huiusmodi habilitas per dispositiones quibus materia praeparatur ad actum; quae quanto magis multiplicantur in subiecto, tanto habilius est ad recipiendum perfectionem et formam. Et e contrario remittitur per dispositiones contrarias; quae quanto magis multiplicatae sunt in materia, et magis intensae, tanto magis remittitur potentia ad actum. Si igitur contrariae dispositiones in infinitum multiplicari et intendi non possunt, sed usque ad certum terminum, neque habilitas praedicta in infinitum diminuitur vel remittitur. Sicut patet in qualitatibus activis et passivis elementorum, frigiditas enim et humiditas, per quae diminuitur sive remittitur habilitas materiae ad formam ignis, non possunt multiplicari in infinitum. Si vero dispositiones contrariae in infinitum multiplicari possunt, et habilitas praedicta in infinitum diminuitur vel remittitur. Non tamen totaliter tollitur, quia semper manet in sua radice, quae est substantia subiecti. Sicut si in infinitum interponantur corpora opaca inter solem et aerem, in infinitum diminuetur habilitas aeris ad lumen, nunquam tamen totaliter tollitur, manente aere, qui secundum naturam suam est diaphanus. Similiter in infinitum potest fieri additio in peccatis, per quae semper magis ac magis minuitur habilitas animae ad gratiam, quae quidem peccata sunt quasi obstacula interposita inter nos et Deum secundum illud Isaiae LIX, *peccata nostra diviserunt inter nos et Deum*. Neque tamen tollitur totaliter ab anima praedicta habilitas, quia consequitur naturam ipsius.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30498)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod bonum quod opponitur malo, totaliter tollitur, sed alia bona non totaliter tolluntur, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30499)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod habilitas praedicta est media inter subiectum et actum. Unde ex ea parte qua attingit actum, diminuitur per malum, sed ex ea parte qua tenet se cum subiecto, remanet. Ergo, licet bonum in se sit simile, tamen, propter comparationem eius ad diversa, non totaliter tollitur, sed in parte.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30500)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quidam, imaginantes diminutionem boni praedicti ad similitudinem diminutionis quantitatis, dixerunt quod, sicut continuum dividitur in infinitum, facta divisione secundum eandem proportionem (ut puta quod accipiatur medium medii, vel tertium tertii), sic in proposito accidit. Sed haec ratio hic locum non habet. Quia in divisione in qua semper servatur eadem proportio, semper subtrahitur minus et minus, minus enim est medium medii quam medium totius. Sed secundum peccatum non de necessitate minus diminuit de habilitate praedicta, quam praecedens, sed forte aut aequaliter, aut magis. Dicendum est ergo quod, licet ista habilitas sit quoddam finitum, diminuitur tamen in infinitum, non per se, sed per accidens, secundum quod contrariae dispositiones etiam in infinitum augentur, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30501)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum insufficienter dividatur per poenam et culpam. Omnis enim defectus malum quoddam esse videtur. Sed in omnibus creaturis est quidam defectus, quod se in esse conservare non possunt, qui tamen nec poena nec culpa est. Non ergo sufficienter malum dividitur per poenam et culpam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30502)\n\nPraeterea, in rebus irrationalibus non invenitur culpa nec poena. Invenitur tamen in eis corruptio et defectus, quae ad rationem mali pertinent. Ergo non omne malum est poena vel culpa.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30503)\n\nPraeterea, tentatio quoddam malum est. Nec tamen est culpa, quia tentatio cui non consentitur, *non est peccatum, sed materia exercendae virtutis*, ut dicitur in Glossa II Cor. XII. Nec etiam poena, quia tentatio praecedit culpam, poena autem subsequitur. Insufficienter ergo malum dividitur per poenam et culpam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30504)\n\nSed contra, videtur quod divisio sit superflua. Ut enim Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., malum dicitur quia nocet. Quod autem nocet, poenale est. Omne ergo malum sub poena continetur.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30505)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod malum, sicut supra dictum est, est privatio boni, quod in perfectione et actu consistit principaliter et per se. Actus autem est duplex, primus, et secundus. Actus quidem primus est forma et integritas rei, actus autem secundus est operatio. Contingit ergo malum esse dupliciter. Uno modo, per subtractionem formae, aut alicuius partis, quae requiritur ad integritatem rei; sicut caecitas malum est, et carere membro. Alio modo, per subtractionem debitae operationis; vel quia omnino non est; vel quia debitum modum et ordinem non habet. Quia vero bonum simpliciter est obiectum voluntatis, malum, quod est privatio boni, secundum specialem rationem invenitur in creaturis rationalibus habentibus voluntatem. Malum igitur quod est per subtractionem formae vel integritatis rei, habet rationem poenae; et praecipue supposito quod omnia divinae providentiae et iustitiae subdantur, ut supra ostensum est, de ratione enim poenae est, quod sit contraria voluntati. Malum autem quod consistit in subtractione debitae operationis in rebus voluntariis, habet rationem culpae. Hoc enim imputatur alicui in culpam, cum deficit a perfecta actione, cuius dominus est secundum voluntatem. Sic igitur omne malum in rebus voluntariis consideratum vel est poena vel culpa.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30506)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, quia malum privatio est boni, et non negatio pura, ut dictum est supra; non omnis defectus boni est malum, sed defectus boni quod natum est et debet haberi. Defectus enim visionis non est malum in lapide, sed in animali, quia contra rationem lapidis est, quod visum habeat. Similiter etiam contra rationem creaturae est, quod in esse conservetur a seipsa, quia idem dat esse et conservat. Unde iste defectus non est malum creaturae.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30507)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod poena et culpa non dividunt malum simpliciter; sed malum in rebus voluntariis.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30508)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod tentatio, prout importat provocationem ad malum, semper malum culpae est in tentante. Sed in eo qui tentatur, non est proprie, nisi secundum quod aliqualiter immutatur, sic enim actio agentis est in patiente. Secundum autem quod tentatus immutatur ad malum a tentante, incidit in culpam.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30509)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod de ratione poenae est, quod noceat agenti in seipso. Sed de ratione culpae est, quod noceat agenti in sua actione. Et sic utrumque sub malo continetur, secundum quod habet rationem nocumenti.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30510)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod habeat plus de ratione mali poena quam culpa. Culpa enim se habet ad poenam, ut meritum ad praemium. Sed praemium habet plus de ratione boni quam meritum, cum sit finis eius. Ergo poena plus habet de ratione mali quam culpa.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30511)\n\nPraeterea, illud est maius malum, quod opponitur maiori bono. Sed poena, sicut dictum est, opponitur bono agentis, culpa autem bono actionis. Cum ergo melius sit agens quam actio, videtur quod peius sit poena quam culpa.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30512)\n\nPraeterea, ipsa privatio finis poena quaedam est, quae dicitur carentia visionis divinae. Malum autem culpae est per privationem ordinis ad finem. Ergo poena est maius malum quam culpa.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30513)\n\nSed contra, sapiens artifex inducit minus malum ad vitandum maius; sicut medicus praecidit membrum, ne corrumpatur corpus. Sed Dei sapientia infert poenam ad vitandam culpam. Ergo culpa est maius malum quam poena.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30514)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod culpa habet plus de ratione mali quam poena, et non solum quam poena sensibilis, quae consistit in privatione corporalium bonorum, cuiusmodi poenas plures intelligunt; sed etiam universaliter accipiendo poenam, secundum quod privatio gratiae vel gloriae poenae quaedam sunt. Cuius est duplex ratio. Prima quidem est, quia ex malo culpae fit aliquis malus, non autem ex malo poenae; secundum illud Dionysii, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *puniri non est malum, sed fieri poena dignum*. Et hoc ideo est quia, cum bonum simpliciter consistat in actu, et non in potentia, ultimus autem actus est operatio, vel usus quarumcumque rerum habitarum; bonum hominis simpliciter consideratur in bona operatione, vel bono usu rerum habitarum. Utimur autem rebus omnibus per voluntatem. Unde ex bona voluntate, qua homo bene utitur rebus habitis, dicitur homo bonus; et ex mala, malus. Potest enim qui habet malam voluntatem, etiam bono quod habet, male uti; sicut si grammaticus voluntarie incongrue loquatur. Quia ergo culpa consistit in deordinato actu voluntatis, poena vero in privatione alicuius eorum quibus utitur voluntas; perfectius habet rationem mali culpa quam poena. Secunda ratio sumi potest ex hoc, quod Deus est auctor mali poenae, non autem mali culpae. Cuius ratio est, quia malum poenae privat bonum creaturae, sive accipiatur bonum creaturae aliquid creatum, sicut caecitas privat visum; sive sit bonum increatum, sicut per carentiam visionis divinae tollitur creaturae bonum increatum. Malum vero culpae opponitur proprie ipsi bono increato, contrariatur enim impletioni divinae voluntatis, et divino amori quo bonum divinum in seipso amatur; et non solum secundum quod participatur a creatura. Sic igitur patet quod culpa habet plus de ratione mali quam poena.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30515)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet culpa terminetur ad poenam, sicut meritum ad praemium, tamen culpa non intenditur propter poenam, sicut meritum propter praemium, sed potius e converso poena inducitur ut vitetur culpa. Et sic culpa est peius quam poena.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30516)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ordo actionis, qui tollitur per culpam, est perfectius bonum agentis, cum sit perfectio secunda, quam bonum quod tollitur per poenam, quod est perfectio prima.\n\n[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30517)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non est comparatio culpae ad poenam sicut finis et ordinis ad finem, quia utrumque potest privari aliquo modo et per culpam, et per poenam. Sed per poenam quidem, secundum quod ipse homo removetur a fine, et ab ordine ad finem, per culpam vero, secundum quod ista privatio pertinet ad actionem, quae non ordinatur ad finem debitum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0002","text":"[I q. 48 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de distinctione rerum in speciali. Et primo, de distinctione boni et mali; deinde de distinctione spiritualis et corporalis creaturae. Circa primum, quaerendum est de malo; et de causa mali. Circa malum quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum malum sit natura aliqua. Secundo, utrum malum inveniatur in rebus. Tertio, utrum bonum sit subiectum mali. Quarto, utrum malum totaliter corrumpat bonum. Quinto, de divisione mali per poenam et culpam. Sexto, quid habeat plus de ratione mali, utrum poena vel culpa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0005","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum sit natura quaedam. Quia omne genus est natura quaedam. Sed malum est quoddam genus, dicitur enim in praedicamentis, quod *bonum et malum non sunt in genere, sed sunt genera aliorum*. Ergo malum est natura quaedam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0007","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0008","text":"Praeterea, omnis differentia constitutiva alicuius speciei est natura quaedam. Malum autem est differentia constitutiva in moralibus, differt enim specie malus habitus a bono, ut liberalitas ab illiberalitate. Ergo malum significat naturam quandam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0009","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0010","text":"Praeterea, utrumque contrariorum est natura quaedam. Sed malum et bonum non opponuntur ut privatio et habitus, sed ut contraria, ut probat philosophus, in praedicamentis, per hoc quod inter bonum et malum est aliquid medium, et a malo potest fieri reditus ad bonum. Ergo malum significat naturam quandam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0011","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0012","text":"Praeterea, quod non est, non agit. Sed malum agit, quia corrumpit bonum. Ergo malum est quoddam ens, et natura quaedam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0013","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0014","text":"Praeterea, ad perfectionem universitatis rerum non pertinet nisi quod est ens et natura quaedam. Sed malum pertinet ad perfectionem universitatis rerum, dicit enim Augustinus, in Enchirid., quod *ex omnibus consistit universitatis admirabilis pulchritudo; in qua etiam illud quod malum dicitur, bene ordinatum, et suo loco positum, eminentius commendat bona*. Ergo malum est natura quaedam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0015","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *malum non est existens neque bonum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0017","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod unum oppositorum cognoscitur per alterum, sicut per lucem tenebra. Unde et quid sit malum, oportet ex ratione boni accipere. Diximus autem supra quod bonum est omne id quod est appetibile, et sic, cum omnis natura appetat suum esse et suam perfectionem, necesse est dicere quod esse et perfectio cuiuscumque naturae rationem habeat bonitatis. Unde non potest esse quod malum significet quoddam esse, aut quandam formam seu naturam. Relinquitur ergo quod nomine mali significetur quaedam absentia boni. Et pro tanto dicitur quod malum neque est existens nec bonum, quia cum ens, inquantum huiusmodi, sit bonum, eadem est remotio utrorumque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0019","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Aristoteles ibi loquitur secundum opinionem Pythagoricorum, qui malum existimabant esse naturam quandam, et ideo ponebant bonum et malum genera. Consuevit enim Aristoteles, et praecipue in libris logicalibus, ponere exempla quae probabilia erant suo tempore, secundum opinionem aliquorum philosophorum. Vel dicendum, sicut dicit philosophus in X Metaphys., quod *prima contrarietas est habitus et privatio*, quia scilicet in omnibus contrariis salvatur, cum semper unum contrariorum sit imperfectum respectu alterius, ut nigrum respectu albi, et amarum respectu dulcis. Et pro tanto bonum et malum dicuntur genera, non simpliciter, sed contrariorum, quia sicut omnis forma habet rationem boni, ita omnis privatio, inquantum huiusmodi, habet rationem mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0021","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0022","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bonum et malum non sunt differentiae constitutivae nisi in moralibus, quae recipiunt speciem ex fine, qui est obiectum voluntatis, a qua moralia dependent. Et quia bonum habet rationem finis, ideo bonum et malum sunt differentiae specificae in moralibus; bonum per se, sed malum inquantum est remotio debiti finis. Nec tamen remotio debiti finis constituit speciem in moralibus, nisi secundum quod adiungitur fini indebito, sicut neque in naturalibus invenitur privatio formae substantialis, nisi adiuncta alteri formae. Sic igitur malum quod est differentia constitutiva in moralibus, est quoddam bonum adiunctum privationi alterius boni, sicut finis intemperati est, non quidem carere bono rationis, sed delectabile sensus absque ordine rationis. Unde malum, inquantum malum, non est differentia constitutiva; sed ratione boni adiuncti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0023","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0024","text":"Et per hoc etiam patet responsio ad tertium. Nam ibi philosophus loquitur de bono et malo, secundum quod inveniuntur in moralibus. Sic enim inter bonum et malum invenitur medium, prout bonum dicitur quod est ordinatum; malum autem, quod non solum est deordinatum, sed etiam nocivum alteri. Unde dicit philosophus in IV Ethic., quod prodigus vanus quidem est, sed non malus. Ab hoc etiam malo quod est secundum morem, contingit fieri reditum ad bonum; non autem ex quocumque malo. Non enim ex caecitate fit reditus ad visionem, cum tamen caecitas sit malum quoddam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0025","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0026","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod aliquid agere dicitur tripliciter. Uno modo, formaliter, eo modo loquendi quo dicitur albedo facere album. Et sic malum, etiam ratione ipsius privationis, dicitur corrumpere bonum, quia est ipsa corruptio vel privatio boni. Alio modo dicitur aliquid agere effective, sicut pictor dicitur facere album parietem. Tertio modo, per modum causae finalis, sicut finis dicitur efficere, movendo efficientem. His autem duobus modis malum non agit aliquid per se, idest secundum quod est privatio quaedam, sed secundum quod ei bonum adiungitur, nam omnis actio est ab aliqua forma, et omne quod desideratur ut finis, est perfectio aliqua. Et ideo, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., malum non agit neque desideratur nisi virtute boni adiuncti; per se autem est infinitum, et praeter voluntatem et intentionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0027","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0028","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, partes universi habent ordinem ad invicem, secundum quod una agit in alteram, et est finis alterius et exemplar. Haec autem, ut dictum est, non possunt convenire malo, nisi ratione boni adiuncti. Unde malum neque ad perfectionem universi pertinet, neque sub ordine universi concluditur, nisi per accidens, idest ratione boni adiuncti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0029","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0030","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0031","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum non inveniatur in rebus. Quidquid enim invenitur in rebus, vel est ens aliquod, vel privatio entis alicuius, quod est non ens. Sed Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod malum distat ab existente, et adhuc plus distat a non existente. Ergo malum nullo modo invenitur in rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0032","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0033","text":"Praeterea, ens et res convertuntur. Si ergo malum est ens in rebus, sequitur quod malum sit res quaedam. Quod est contra praedicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0034","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0035","text":"*Praeterea, albius est quod est nigro impermixtius*, ut dicitur in III libro Topic. Aristotelis. Ergo et melius est quod est malo impermixtius. Sed Deus facit semper quod melius est, multo magis quam natura. Ergo in rebus a Deo conditis nihil malum invenitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0036","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0037","text":"Sed contra est quod secundum hoc removerentur omnes prohibitiones et poenae, quae non sunt nisi malorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0038","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0039","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, perfectio universi requirit inaequalitatem esse in rebus, ut omnes bonitatis gradus impleantur. Est autem unus gradus bonitatis ut aliquid ita bonum sit, quod nunquam deficere possit. Alius autem gradus bonitatis est, ut sic aliquid bonum sit, quod a bono deficere possit. Qui etiam gradus in ipso esse inveniuntur, quaedam enim sunt, quae suum esse amittere non possunt, ut incorporalia; quaedam vero sunt, quae amittere possunt, ut corporalia. Sicut igitur perfectio universitatis rerum requirit ut non solum sint entia incorruptibilia, sed etiam corruptibilia; ita perfectio universi requirit ut sint quaedam quae a bonitate deficere possint; ad quod sequitur ea interdum deficere. In hoc autem consistit ratio mali, ut scilicet aliquid deficiat a bono.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0040","text":"Unde manifestum est quod in rebus malum invenitur, sicut et corruptio, nam et ipsa corruptio malum quoddam est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0041","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0042","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod malum distat et ab ente simpliciter, et non ente simpliciter, quia neque est sicut habitus, neque sicut pura negatio, sed sicut privatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0043","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0044","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut dicitur in V Metaphys., ens dupliciter dicitur. Uno modo, secundum quod significat entitatem rei, prout dividitur per decem praedicamenta, et sic convertitur cum re. Et hoc modo, nulla privatio est ens, unde nec malum. Alio modo dicitur ens, quod significat veritatem propositionis, quae in compositione consistit, cuius nota est hoc verbum est, et hoc est ens quo respondetur ad quaestionem an est. Et sic caecitatem dicimus esse in oculo, vel quamcumque aliam privationem. Et hoc modo etiam malum dicitur ens. Propter huius autem distinctionis ignorantiam, aliqui, considerantes quod aliquae res dicuntur malae, vel quod malum dicitur esse in rebus, crediderunt quod malum esset res quaedam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0045","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0046","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Deus et natura, et quodcumque agens, facit quod melius est in toto; sed non quod melius est in unaquaque parte, nisi per ordinem ad totum, ut supra dictum est. Ipsum autem totum quod est universitas creaturarum, melius et perfectius est, si in eo sint quaedam quae a bono deficere possunt, quae interdum deficiunt, Deo hoc non impediente. Tum quia providentiae non est naturam destruere, sed salvare, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., ipsa autem natura rerum hoc habet, ut quae deficere possunt, quandoque deficiant. Tum quia, ut dicit Augustinus in Enchirid., Deus est adeo potens, quod etiam potest bene facere de malis. Unde multa bona tollerentur, si Deus nullum malum permitteret esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0047","text":"Non enim generaretur ignis, nisi corrumperetur aer; neque conservaretur vita leonis, nisi occideretur asinus; neque etiam laudaretur iustitia vindicans, et patientia sufferens, si non esset iniquitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0048","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0049","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0050","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum non sit in bono sicut in subiecto. Omnia enim bona sunt existentia. Sed Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod malum non est existens, neque in existentibus. Ergo malum non est in bono sicut in subiecto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0051","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0052","text":"Praeterea, malum non est ens, bonum vero est ens. Sed non ens non requirit ens, in quo sit sicut in subiecto. Ergo nec malum requirit bonum, in quo sit sicut in subiecto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0053","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0054","text":"Praeterea, unum contrariorum non est subiectum alterius. Sed bonum et malum sunt contraria. Ergo malum non est in bono sicut in subiecto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0055","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0056","text":"Praeterea, id in quo est albedo sicut in subiecto, dicitur esse album. Ergo et id in quo est malum sicut in subiecto, est malum. Si ergo malum sit in bono sicut in subiecto, sequitur quod bonum sit malum, contra id quod dicitur Isai. V, *vae, qui dicitis malum bonum, et bonum malum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0057","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0058","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., quod malum non est nisi in bono.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0059","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0060","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, malum importat remotionem boni. Non autem quaelibet remotio boni malum dicitur. Potest enim accipi remotio boni et privative, et negative. Remotio igitur boni negative accepta, mali rationem non habet, alioquin sequeretur quod ea quae nullo modo sunt, mala essent; et iterum quod quaelibet res esset mala, ex hoc quod non habet bonum alterius rei, utpote quod homo esset malus, quia non habet velocitatem capreae, vel fortitudinem leonis. Sed remotio boni privative accepta, malum dicitur, sicut privatio visus caecitas dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0061","text":"Subiectum autem privationis et formae est unum et idem, scilicet ens in potentia, sive sit ens in potentia simpliciter, sicut materia prima, quae est subiectum formae substantialis et privationis oppositae; sive sit ens in potentia secundum quid et in actu simpliciter, ut corpus diaphanum, quod est subiectum tenebrarum et lucis. Manifestum est autem quod forma per quam aliquid est actu, perfectio quaedam est, et bonum quoddam, et sic omne ens in actu, bonum quoddam est. Et similiter omne ens in potentia, inquantum huiusmodi, bonum quoddam est, secundum quod habet ordinem ad bonum, sicut enim est ens in potentia, ita et bonum in potentia. Relinquitur ergo quod subiectum mali sit bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0062","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0063","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius intelligit malum non esse in existentibus sicut partem, aut sicut proprietatem naturalem alicuius existentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0064","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0065","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non ens negative acceptum non requirit subiectum. Sed privatio est negatio in subiecto, ut dicitur in IV Metaphys., et tale non ens est malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0066","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0067","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod malum non est sicut in subiecto in bono quod ei opponitur, sed in quodam alio bono, subiectum enim caecitatis non est visus, sed animal. Videtur tamen, ut Augustinus dicit, hic fallere dialecticorum regula, quae dicit contraria simul esse non posse. Hoc tamen intelligendum est secundum communem acceptionem boni et mali, non autem secundum quod specialiter accipitur hoc bonum et hoc malum. Album autem et nigrum, dulce et amarum, et huiusmodi contraria, non accipiuntur nisi specialiter, quia sunt in quibusdam generibus determinatis. Sed bonum circuit omnia genera, unde unum bonum potest simul esse cum privatione alterius boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0068","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0069","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod propheta imprecatur vae illis qui dicunt id quod est bonum, secundum quod est bonum, esse malum. Hoc autem non sequitur ex praemissis, ut per praedicta patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0070","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0071","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0072","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum corrumpat totum bonum. Unum enim contrariorum totaliter corrumpitur per alterum. Sed bonum et malum sunt contraria. Ergo malum potest corrumpere totum bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0073","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0074","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., quod malum nocet inquantum adimit bonum. Sed bonum est sibi simile et uniforme. Ergo totaliter tollitur per malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0075","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0076","text":"Praeterea, malum, quandiu est, nocet et aufert bonum. Sed illud a quo semper aliquid aufertur, quandoque consumitur, nisi sit infinitum; quod non potest dici de aliquo bono creato. Ergo malum consumit totaliter bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0077","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0078","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., quod malum non potest totaliter consumere bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0079","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0080","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod malum non potest totaliter consumere bonum. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod est triplex bonum. Quoddam, quod per malum totaliter tollitur, et hoc est bonum oppositum malo; sicut lumen totaliter per tenebras tollitur, et visus per caecitatem. Quoddam vero bonum est, quod nec totaliter tollitur per malum, nec diminuitur, scilicet bonum quod est subiectum mali; non enim per tenebras aliquid de substantia aeris diminuitur. Quoddam vero bonum est, quod diminuitur quidem per malum, sed non totaliter tollitur, et hoc bonum est habilitas subiecti ad actum. Diminutio autem huius boni non est accipienda per subtractionem, sicut est diminutio in quantitatibus, sed per remissionem, sicut est diminutio in qualitatibus et formis. Remissio autem huius habilitatis est accipienda e contrario intensioni ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0081","text":"Intenditur enim huiusmodi habilitas per dispositiones quibus materia praeparatur ad actum; quae quanto magis multiplicantur in subiecto, tanto habilius est ad recipiendum perfectionem et formam. Et e contrario remittitur per dispositiones contrarias; quae quanto magis multiplicatae sunt in materia, et magis intensae, tanto magis remittitur potentia ad actum. Si igitur contrariae dispositiones in infinitum multiplicari et intendi non possunt, sed usque ad certum terminum, neque habilitas praedicta in infinitum diminuitur vel remittitur. Sicut patet in qualitatibus activis et passivis elementorum, frigiditas enim et humiditas, per quae diminuitur sive remittitur habilitas materiae ad formam ignis, non possunt multiplicari in infinitum. Si vero dispositiones contrariae in infinitum multiplicari possunt, et habilitas praedicta in infinitum diminuitur vel remittitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0082","text":"Non tamen totaliter tollitur, quia semper manet in sua radice, quae est substantia subiecti. Sicut si in infinitum interponantur corpora opaca inter solem et aerem, in infinitum diminuetur habilitas aeris ad lumen, nunquam tamen totaliter tollitur, manente aere, qui secundum naturam suam est diaphanus. Similiter in infinitum potest fieri additio in peccatis, per quae semper magis ac magis minuitur habilitas animae ad gratiam, quae quidem peccata sunt quasi obstacula interposita inter nos et Deum secundum illud Isaiae LIX, *peccata nostra diviserunt inter nos et Deum*. Neque tamen tollitur totaliter ab anima praedicta habilitas, quia consequitur naturam ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0083","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0084","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod bonum quod opponitur malo, totaliter tollitur, sed alia bona non totaliter tolluntur, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0085","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0086","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod habilitas praedicta est media inter subiectum et actum. Unde ex ea parte qua attingit actum, diminuitur per malum, sed ex ea parte qua tenet se cum subiecto, remanet. Ergo, licet bonum in se sit simile, tamen, propter comparationem eius ad diversa, non totaliter tollitur, sed in parte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0087","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0088","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quidam, imaginantes diminutionem boni praedicti ad similitudinem diminutionis quantitatis, dixerunt quod, sicut continuum dividitur in infinitum, facta divisione secundum eandem proportionem (ut puta quod accipiatur medium medii, vel tertium tertii), sic in proposito accidit. Sed haec ratio hic locum non habet. Quia in divisione in qua semper servatur eadem proportio, semper subtrahitur minus et minus, minus enim est medium medii quam medium totius. Sed secundum peccatum non de necessitate minus diminuit de habilitate praedicta, quam praecedens, sed forte aut aequaliter, aut magis. Dicendum est ergo quod, licet ista habilitas sit quoddam finitum, diminuitur tamen in infinitum, non per se, sed per accidens, secundum quod contrariae dispositiones etiam in infinitum augentur, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0089","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0090","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0091","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum insufficienter dividatur per poenam et culpam. Omnis enim defectus malum quoddam esse videtur. Sed in omnibus creaturis est quidam defectus, quod se in esse conservare non possunt, qui tamen nec poena nec culpa est. Non ergo sufficienter malum dividitur per poenam et culpam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0092","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0093","text":"Praeterea, in rebus irrationalibus non invenitur culpa nec poena. Invenitur tamen in eis corruptio et defectus, quae ad rationem mali pertinent. Ergo non omne malum est poena vel culpa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0094","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0095","text":"Praeterea, tentatio quoddam malum est. Nec tamen est culpa, quia tentatio cui non consentitur, *non est peccatum, sed materia exercendae virtutis*, ut dicitur in Glossa II Cor. XII. Nec etiam poena, quia tentatio praecedit culpam, poena autem subsequitur. Insufficienter ergo malum dividitur per poenam et culpam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0096","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0097","text":"Sed contra, videtur quod divisio sit superflua. Ut enim Augustinus dicit, in Enchirid., malum dicitur quia nocet. Quod autem nocet, poenale est. Omne ergo malum sub poena continetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0098","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0099","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod malum, sicut supra dictum est, est privatio boni, quod in perfectione et actu consistit principaliter et per se. Actus autem est duplex, primus, et secundus. Actus quidem primus est forma et integritas rei, actus autem secundus est operatio. Contingit ergo malum esse dupliciter. Uno modo, per subtractionem formae, aut alicuius partis, quae requiritur ad integritatem rei; sicut caecitas malum est, et carere membro. Alio modo, per subtractionem debitae operationis; vel quia omnino non est; vel quia debitum modum et ordinem non habet. Quia vero bonum simpliciter est obiectum voluntatis, malum, quod est privatio boni, secundum specialem rationem invenitur in creaturis rationalibus habentibus voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0100","text":"Malum igitur quod est per subtractionem formae vel integritatis rei, habet rationem poenae; et praecipue supposito quod omnia divinae providentiae et iustitiae subdantur, ut supra ostensum est, de ratione enim poenae est, quod sit contraria voluntati. Malum autem quod consistit in subtractione debitae operationis in rebus voluntariis, habet rationem culpae. Hoc enim imputatur alicui in culpam, cum deficit a perfecta actione, cuius dominus est secundum voluntatem. Sic igitur omne malum in rebus voluntariis consideratum vel est poena vel culpa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0101","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0102","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, quia malum privatio est boni, et non negatio pura, ut dictum est supra; non omnis defectus boni est malum, sed defectus boni quod natum est et debet haberi. Defectus enim visionis non est malum in lapide, sed in animali, quia contra rationem lapidis est, quod visum habeat. Similiter etiam contra rationem creaturae est, quod in esse conservetur a seipsa, quia idem dat esse et conservat. Unde iste defectus non est malum creaturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0103","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0104","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod poena et culpa non dividunt malum simpliciter; sed malum in rebus voluntariis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0105","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0106","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod tentatio, prout importat provocationem ad malum, semper malum culpae est in tentante. Sed in eo qui tentatur, non est proprie, nisi secundum quod aliqualiter immutatur, sic enim actio agentis est in patiente. Secundum autem quod tentatus immutatur ad malum a tentante, incidit in culpam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0107","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0108","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod de ratione poenae est, quod noceat agenti in seipso. Sed de ratione culpae est, quod noceat agenti in sua actione. Et sic utrumque sub malo continetur, secundum quod habet rationem nocumenti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0109","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0110","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0111","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod habeat plus de ratione mali poena quam culpa. Culpa enim se habet ad poenam, ut meritum ad praemium. Sed praemium habet plus de ratione boni quam meritum, cum sit finis eius. Ergo poena plus habet de ratione mali quam culpa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0112","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0113","text":"Praeterea, illud est maius malum, quod opponitur maiori bono. Sed poena, sicut dictum est, opponitur bono agentis, culpa autem bono actionis. Cum ergo melius sit agens quam actio, videtur quod peius sit poena quam culpa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0114","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0115","text":"Praeterea, ipsa privatio finis poena quaedam est, quae dicitur carentia visionis divinae. Malum autem culpae est per privationem ordinis ad finem. Ergo poena est maius malum quam culpa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0116","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0117","text":"Sed contra, sapiens artifex inducit minus malum ad vitandum maius; sicut medicus praecidit membrum, ne corrumpatur corpus. Sed Dei sapientia infert poenam ad vitandam culpam. Ergo culpa est maius malum quam poena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0118","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0119","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod culpa habet plus de ratione mali quam poena, et non solum quam poena sensibilis, quae consistit in privatione corporalium bonorum, cuiusmodi poenas plures intelligunt; sed etiam universaliter accipiendo poenam, secundum quod privatio gratiae vel gloriae poenae quaedam sunt. Cuius est duplex ratio. Prima quidem est, quia ex malo culpae fit aliquis malus, non autem ex malo poenae; secundum illud Dionysii, IV cap. de Div. Nom., *puniri non est malum, sed fieri poena dignum*. Et hoc ideo est quia, cum bonum simpliciter consistat in actu, et non in potentia, ultimus autem actus est operatio, vel usus quarumcumque rerum habitarum; bonum hominis simpliciter consideratur in bona operatione, vel bono usu rerum habitarum. Utimur autem rebus omnibus per voluntatem. Unde ex bona voluntate, qua homo bene utitur rebus habitis, dicitur homo bonus; et ex mala, malus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0120","text":"Potest enim qui habet malam voluntatem, etiam bono quod habet, male uti; sicut si grammaticus voluntarie incongrue loquatur. Quia ergo culpa consistit in deordinato actu voluntatis, poena vero in privatione alicuius eorum quibus utitur voluntas; perfectius habet rationem mali culpa quam poena. Secunda ratio sumi potest ex hoc, quod Deus est auctor mali poenae, non autem mali culpae. Cuius ratio est, quia malum poenae privat bonum creaturae, sive accipiatur bonum creaturae aliquid creatum, sicut caecitas privat visum; sive sit bonum increatum, sicut per carentiam visionis divinae tollitur creaturae bonum increatum. Malum vero culpae opponitur proprie ipsi bono increato, contrariatur enim impletioni divinae voluntatis, et divino amori quo bonum divinum in seipso amatur; et non solum secundum quod participatur a creatura. Sic igitur patet quod culpa habet plus de ratione mali quam poena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0121","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0122","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet culpa terminetur ad poenam, sicut meritum ad praemium, tamen culpa non intenditur propter poenam, sicut meritum propter praemium, sed potius e converso poena inducitur ut vitetur culpa. Et sic culpa est peius quam poena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0123","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0124","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ordo actionis, qui tollitur per culpam, est perfectius bonum agentis, cum sit perfectio secunda, quam bonum quod tollitur per poenam, quod est perfectio prima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0125","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0126","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non est comparatio culpae ad poenam sicut finis et ordinis ad finem, quia utrumque potest privari aliquo modo et per culpam, et per poenam. Sed per poenam quidem, secundum quod ipse homo removetur a fine, et ab ordine ad finem, per culpam vero, secundum quod ista privatio pertinet ad actionem, quae non ordinatur ad finem debitum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d23c1f964be7e495cc70906b00046ff41ea6803ac4c2e7e61668f800172fa281","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143345976275Z_e8bc313c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 48","source_sha256":"d23c1f964be7e495cc70906b00046ff41ea6803ac4c2e7e61668f800172fa281","sha256":"342ce0f2dd10f9da52175ff9beaec5262c0c7571dfb17e56ba6b8f07f22dbfd1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:33:45.976299+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0002","text":"[I q. 48 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the distinction of things in particular. First, the distinction between good and evil; then, the distinction between spiritual and corporeal creature. Concerning the first, inquiry must be made about evil, and about the cause of evil. Concerning evil, six questions are asked. First, whether evil is some nature. Second, whether evil is found in things. Third, whether good is the subject of evil. Fourth, whether evil wholly corrupts good. Fifth, concerning the division of evil into punishment and fault. Sixth, whether punishment or fault has more of the character of evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0005","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that evil is a certain nature. For every genus is a certain nature. But evil is a genus; for it is said in the Categories that *good and evil are not in a genus, but are genera of other things*. Therefore evil is a certain nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0007","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0008","text":"Further, every difference constituting some species is a certain nature. But evil is a constitutive difference in moral matters, for an evil habit differs specifically from a good one, as liberality from illiberality. Therefore evil signifies a certain nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0009","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0010","text":"Further, each of two contraries is a certain nature. But evil and good are not opposed as privation and habit, but as contraries, as the Philosopher proves in the Categories, from the fact that there is something intermediate between good and evil, and that a return from evil to good can occur. Therefore evil signifies a certain nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0011","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0012","text":"Further, what is not does not act. But evil acts, because it corrupts good. Therefore evil is a certain being, and a certain nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0013","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0014","text":"Further, only that which is a being and a certain nature pertains to the perfection of the universe of things. But evil pertains to the perfection of the universe of things; for Augustine says in the Enchiridion that *the wonderful beauty of the universe consists of all things; in which even that which is called evil, when well ordered and placed in its own place, commends goods more eminently*. Therefore evil is a certain nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0015","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0016","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says in chapter IV of On the Divine Names: *evil is neither an existing thing nor good*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0017","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0018","text":"I answer that it must be said that one of two opposites is known through the other, as darkness through light. Hence what evil is must be understood from the notion of good. Now we said above that good is everything that is appetible; and thus, since every nature desires its own being and its own perfection, it must be said that the being and perfection of every nature have the character of goodness. Hence it cannot be that evil signifies some being, or some form or nature. It remains, therefore, that by the name of evil a certain absence of good is signified. And to this extent it is said that evil is neither an existing thing nor good, because, since being, as such, is good, the removal of both is the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0019","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0020","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Aristotle there speaks according to the opinion of the Pythagoreans, who thought evil to be a certain nature, and therefore posited good and evil as genera. For Aristotle was accustomed, especially in his logical books, to set forth examples that were probable in his time, according to the opinion of certain philosophers. Or it must be said, as the Philosopher says in Metaphysics X, that *the first contrariety is habit and privation*, because it is preserved in all contraries, since one contrary is always imperfect in relation to the other, as black in relation to white, and bitter in relation to sweet. And to this extent good and evil are called genera, not simply, but of contraries, because just as every form has the character of good, so every privation, as such, has the character of evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0021","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0022","text":"To the second it must be said that good and evil are constitutive differences only in moral matters, which receive their species from the end, which is the object of the will, upon which moral matters depend. And because good has the character of an end, good and evil are therefore specific differences in moral matters: good through itself, but evil insofar as it is the removal of a due end. Yet the removal of a due end does not constitute a species in moral matters except insofar as it is joined to an undue end, just as in natural things privation of substantial form is not found except joined to another form. Thus, therefore, the evil that is a constitutive difference in moral matters is a certain good joined to the privation of another good, just as the end of the intemperate person is, indeed, not to lack the good of reason, but sensible delight without the order of reason. Hence evil, insofar as it is evil, is not a constitutive difference, but is so by reason of the good joined to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0023","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0024","text":"And through this the response to the third is also clear. For there the Philosopher speaks of good and evil insofar as they are found in moral matters. For in this way an intermediate is found between good and evil, insofar as good is called that which is ordered, while evil is that which is not only disordered but also harmful to another. Hence the Philosopher says in Ethics IV that the prodigal person is vain indeed, but not evil. A return to good can also occur from this evil that is according to morals, but not from every evil. For there is no return from blindness to sight, although blindness is a certain evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0025","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0026","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that something is said to act in three ways. In one way, formally, according to that manner of speaking in which whiteness is said to make [something] white. And thus evil, even by reason of privation itself, is said to corrupt good, because it is itself the corruption or privation of good. In another way, something is said to act effectively, as a painter is said to make a wall white. In a third way, by way of final cause, as an end is said to effect, by moving the efficient [cause]. But in these two ways evil does not act anything through itself, that is, insofar as it is a certain privation, but insofar as good is joined to it; for every action is from some form, and everything desired as an end is some perfection. And therefore, as Dionysius says in chapter IV of On the Divine Names, evil neither acts nor is desired except by the power of the good joined to it; but through itself it is infinite, and beyond will and intention.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0027","text":"[I q. 48 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0028","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, as was said above, the parts of the universe have an order toward one another, insofar as one acts upon another, and is the end and exemplar of another. But these, as has been said, cannot belong to evil except by reason of the good joined to it. Hence evil neither pertains to the perfection of the universe nor is included under the order of the universe, except accidentally, that is, by reason of the good joined to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0029","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0030","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0031","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that evil is not found in things. For whatever is found in things is either some being or the privation of some being, which is non-being. But Dionysius says, in chapter IV of On the Divine Names, that evil is distant from being, and is still more distant from non-being. Therefore evil is found in things in no way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0032","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0033","text":"Further, being and thing are convertible. Therefore, if evil is a being in things, it follows that evil is some thing. This is contrary to what was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0034","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0035","text":"*The whiter is that which is more unmixed with black*, as is said in Book III of Aristotle's Topics. Therefore the better too is that which is more unmixed with evil. But God always makes what is better, much more so than nature. Therefore, in things created by God, no evil is found.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0036","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0037","text":"But to the contrary, according to this all prohibitions and punishments would be removed, and these concern only evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0038","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0039","text":"I answer that, as was said above, the perfection of the universe requires that there be inequality in things, so that all grades of goodness may be fulfilled. Now one grade of goodness is that something is so good that it can never fail. But another grade of goodness is that something is so good that it can fail from the good. These grades are also found in being itself: for some things are such that they cannot lose their being, as incorporeal things; while some are such that they can lose it, as corporeal things. Therefore, just as the perfection of the universe of things requires that there be not only incorruptible beings but also corruptible ones, so the perfection of the universe requires that there be some things which can fail from goodness; and it follows from this that they sometimes fail. But the notion of evil consists in this, namely, that something fails from good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0040","text":"Hence it is manifest that evil is found in things, just as corruption is; for corruption itself is also a certain evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0041","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0042","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that evil is distant both from being simply and from non-being simply, because it is neither as a habit nor as a pure negation, but as a privation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0043","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0044","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as is said in Metaphysics V, being is spoken of in two ways. In one way, insofar as it signifies the entity of a thing, as it is divided through the ten categories; and thus it is convertible with thing. And in this way no privation is a being, and hence neither is evil. In another way, being is spoken of as signifying the truth of a proposition, which consists in composition, the sign of which is this verb “is”; and this is the being by which one answers the question whether [something] is. And thus we say that blindness is in an eye, or any other privation. And in this way evil too is called a being. But owing to ignorance of this distinction, some, considering that some things are called evil, or that evil is said to be in things, believed that evil was some thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0045","text":"[I q. 48 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0046","text":"To the third, it must be said that God and nature, and every agent whatsoever, make what is better in the whole; but not what is better in each individual part, except in relation to the whole, as was said above. But the whole itself which is the universe of creatures is better and more perfect if there are in it some things that can fail from the good, and that sometimes do fail, God not preventing this. First, because it belongs not to providence to destroy nature, but to preserve it, as Dionysius says in chapter IV of On the Divine Names; but the nature of things itself has this, that those which can fail sometimes do fail. Second, because, as Augustine says in the Enchiridion, God is so powerful that He can even make good out of evils. Hence many goods would be removed if God permitted no evil to exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0047","text":"For fire would not be generated unless air were corrupted; nor would the life of a lion be preserved unless an ass were killed; nor indeed would avenging justice and enduring patience be praised if there were no injustice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0048","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0049","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0050","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that evil is not in good as in a subject. For all beings are good. But Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that evil is not an existent, nor is it in existents. Therefore evil is not in good as in a subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0051","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0052","text":"Further, evil is not a being, whereas good is a being. But non-being does not require a being in which it may be as in a subject. Therefore neither does evil require good in which it may be as in a subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0053","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0054","text":"Further, one of contraries is not the subject of the other. But good and evil are contraries. Therefore evil is not in good as in a subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0055","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0056","text":"Further, that in which whiteness is as in a subject is said to be white. Therefore that also in which evil is as in a subject is evil. If, therefore, evil is in good as in a subject, it follows that good is evil, contrary to what is said in Isaiah V: *Woe to you who call evil good, and good evil*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0057","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0058","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says in the *Enchiridion* that evil is only in good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0059","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0060","text":"I answer that, as has been said, evil signifies the removal of good. Yet not every removal of good is called evil. For the removal of good can be taken either privatively or negatively. Therefore, the removal of good taken negatively does not have the character of evil; otherwise it would follow that things which in no way exist would be evil, and again that every thing would be evil because it does not have the good of another thing—for example, that a man would be evil because he does not have the speed of a deer or the strength of a lion. But the removal of good taken privatively is called evil, just as the privation of sight is called blindness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0061","text":"Now the subject of privation and of form is one and the same, namely, a being in potency: whether it is a being in potency simply, as prime matter, which is the subject of substantial form and of the opposed privation; or a being in potency in a certain respect and in act simply, as a transparent body, which is the subject of darkness and light. But it is clear that the form through which something is in act is a certain perfection and a certain good, and thus every being in act is a certain good. Likewise every being in potency, insofar as it is such, is a certain good, according as it has an ordering toward good; for just as it is a being in potency, so too it is good in potency. It remains, therefore, that the subject of evil is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0062","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0063","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that Dionysius understands evil not to be in existents as a part, or as a natural property of any existent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0064","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0065","text":"To the second, it must be said that non-being taken negatively does not require a subject. But privation is a negation in a subject, as is said in *Metaphysics* IV, and such non-being is evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0066","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0067","text":"To the third, it must be said that evil is not in good which is opposed to it as in a subject, but in some other good; for the subject of blindness is not sight, but an animal. Yet, as Augustine says, the rule of the dialecticians which says that contraries cannot exist simultaneously seems to fail here. This, however, must be understood according to the common acceptance of good and evil, and not according as this good and this evil are taken specially. For white and black, sweet and bitter, and contraries of this sort, are not taken except specially, because they are in certain determinate genera. But good extends over all genera; hence one good can exist at the same time as the privation of another good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0068","text":"[I q. 48 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0069","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the prophet pronounces woe upon those who call that which is good, insofar as it is good, evil. But this does not follow from the foregoing, as is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0070","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0071","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0072","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that evil corrupts the whole good. For one of contraries is totally corrupted by the other. But good and evil are contraries. Therefore evil can corrupt the whole good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0073","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 obj. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0074","text":"Moreover, Augustine says in the *Enchiridion* that evil harms insofar as it removes good. But good is like itself and uniform. Therefore, it is wholly taken away by evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0075","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 obj. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0076","text":"Moreover, evil, as long as it exists, harms and takes away good. But that from which something is always taken away is at some time consumed, unless it is infinite; which cannot be said of any created good. Therefore evil wholly consumes good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0077","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 on the contrary](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0078","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says in the *Enchiridion* that evil cannot wholly consume good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0079","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 body](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0080","text":"I answer that evil cannot wholly consume good. For the clarification of this, it must be considered that there is a threefold good. One sort is wholly removed by evil, and this is the good opposed to evil; just as light is wholly removed by darkness, and sight by blindness. But there is another good that is neither wholly removed by evil nor diminished, namely, the good that is the subject of evil; for nothing of the substance of air is diminished by darkness. But there is another good that is indeed diminished by evil, but not wholly removed, and this good is the aptitude of the subject for act. The diminution of this good, however, is not to be understood by subtraction, as diminution is in quantities, but by remission, as diminution is in qualities and forms. But the remission of this aptitude is to be understood as the contrary of its intensification.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0081","text":"For an aptitude of this kind is intensified by dispositions through which matter is prepared for act; and the more these are multiplied in the subject, the more apt it is to receive perfection and form. Conversely, it is remitted by contrary dispositions; and the more these are multiplied in matter, and the more intense they are, the more the potency for act is remitted. Therefore, if contrary dispositions cannot be multiplied and intensified to infinity, but only up to a certain limit, neither is the aforesaid aptitude diminished or remitted to infinity. This is evident in the active and passive qualities of the elements: for coldness and moisture, through which the aptitude of matter for the form of fire is diminished or remitted, cannot be multiplied to infinity. But if contrary dispositions can be multiplied to infinity, then the aforesaid aptitude is diminished or remitted to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0082","text":"Yet it is not wholly removed, because it always remains in its root, which is the substance of the subject. Thus, if opaque bodies are interposed to infinity between the sun and the air, the aptitude of the air for light will be diminished to infinity; yet it is never wholly removed, since the air remains, which by its nature is transparent. Likewise, an addition can be made to infinity in sins, through which the aptitude of the soul for grace is always more and more diminished; these sins are, as it were, obstacles interposed between us and God, according to Isaiah 59: *our sins have divided between us and God*. Yet the aforesaid aptitude is not wholly removed from the soul, because it follows upon its nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0083","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 reply to obj. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0084","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the good opposed to evil is wholly removed, but the other goods are not wholly removed, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0085","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 reply to obj. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0086","text":"To the second, it must be said that the aforesaid aptitude is intermediate between subject and act. Hence, insofar as it reaches to act, it is diminished by evil; but insofar as it adheres to the subject, it remains. Therefore, although good is in itself similar, nevertheless, on account of its relation to different things, it is not wholly removed, but only in part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0087","text":"[I q. 48 a. 4 reply to obj. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0088","text":"To the third, it must be said that certain people, imagining the diminution of the aforesaid good after the likeness of diminution of quantity, said that, just as a continuum is divided to infinity, with a division made according to the same proportion—for example, when half of a half, or a third of a third, is taken—so it happens in the matter at hand. But this reasoning has no place here. For in a division in which the same proportion is always preserved, less and less is always subtracted; for half of a half is less than half of the whole. But a subsequent sin does not necessarily diminish the aforesaid aptitude less than the preceding one, but perhaps equally, or more. It must therefore be said that, although this aptitude is something finite, it is nevertheless diminished to infinity, not through itself, but accidentally, insofar as contrary dispositions are likewise increased to infinity, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0089","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0090","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 obj. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0091","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that evil is insufficiently divided into punishment and fault. For every defect seems to be a kind of evil. But in all creatures there is a certain defect, namely, that they cannot preserve themselves in being, which nevertheless is neither punishment nor fault. Therefore evil is not sufficiently divided into punishment and fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0092","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 obj. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0093","text":"Moreover, in irrational things neither fault nor punishment is found. Yet corruption and defect, which pertain to the notion of evil, are found in them. Therefore not every evil is punishment or fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0094","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 obj. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0095","text":"Moreover, temptation is a certain evil. Yet it is not fault, because temptation to which one does not consent, “is not sin, but material for the exercise of virtue,” as is said in the Gloss on 2 Corinthians 12. Nor is it punishment, because temptation precedes fault, whereas punishment follows it. Therefore evil is insufficiently divided into punishment and fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0096","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 on the contrary](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0097","text":"But on the contrary, it seems that the division is superfluous. For as Augustine says in the Enchiridion, evil is so called because it harms. But that which harms is penal. Therefore every evil is contained under punishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0098","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0099","text":"I answer that it must be said that evil, as was said above, is the privation of good, which consists principally and per se in perfection and act. Now act is twofold: first and second. First act is the form and integrity of a thing, whereas second act is operation. Therefore evil can occur in two ways. In one way, through the removal of a form, or of some part required for the integrity of the thing; as blindness is an evil, and the lack of a limb. In another way, through the removal of due operation: either because it is altogether absent, or because it does not have its due mode and order. But since good simply speaking is the object of the will, evil, which is the privation of good, is found under a special aspect in rational creatures possessing will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0100","text":"Therefore evil which is through the removal of the form or integrity of a thing has the character of punishment; and especially granted that all things are subject to divine providence and justice, as was shown above; for it belongs to the character of punishment that it be contrary to the will. But evil which consists in the removal of due operation in voluntary things has the character of fault. For this is imputed to someone as fault, when he falls short of the perfect action of which he is master according to his will. Thus every evil considered in voluntary things is either punishment or fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0101","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0102","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, because evil is the privation of good and not pure negation, as was said above, not every lack of good is evil, but only the lack of a good which is naturally apt to be and ought to be possessed. For the lack of sight is not an evil in a stone, but in an animal, because it is contrary to the nature of a stone that it should have sight. Likewise, it is contrary to the nature of a creature that it should be preserved in being by itself, because the same [cause] gives being and preserves it. Hence this defect is not an evil of the creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0103","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0104","text":"To the second, it must be said that punishment and fault do not divide evil simply speaking, but evil in voluntary things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0105","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0106","text":"To the third, it must be said that temptation, insofar as it implies provocation to evil, is always an evil of fault in the one tempting. But in the one who is tempted, it is not properly so, except insofar as he is altered in some way; for thus the action of the agent is in the patient. But insofar as the one tempted is changed to evil by the tempter, he falls into fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0107","text":"[I q. 48 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0108","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that it belongs to the character of punishment that it harm the agent in himself. But it belongs to the character of fault that it harm the agent in his action. And thus both are contained under evil, insofar as each has the character of harm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0109","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0110","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0111","text":"We proceed to the sixth thus. It seems that punishment has more of the character of evil than fault. For fault is related to punishment as merit is to reward. But reward has more of the character of good than merit, since it is its end. Therefore punishment has more of the character of evil than fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0112","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0113","text":"Further, that is the greater evil which is opposed to the greater good. But punishment, as has been said, is opposed to the good of the agent, whereas fault is opposed to the good of the action. Therefore, since the agent is better than the action, punishment seems worse than fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0114","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0115","text":"Further, the very privation of the end is a certain punishment, which is called the lack of the divine vision. But the evil of fault is through the privation of order to the end. Therefore punishment is a greater evil than fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0116","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0117","text":"But on the contrary, a wise craftsman introduces a lesser evil in order to avoid a greater one; just as a physician cuts off a limb lest the body be corrupted. But God's wisdom inflicts punishment in order to avoid fault. Therefore fault is a greater evil than punishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0118","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0119","text":"I answer that it must be said that fault has more of the character of evil than punishment, and not only than sensible punishment, which consists in the privation of bodily goods, the sort of punishments which many understand; but also taking punishment universally, insofar as the privation of grace or glory are certain punishments. There is a twofold reason for this. The first is that someone is made evil by the evil of fault, but not by the evil of punishment; according to that saying of Dionysius, chapter IV of On the Divine Names, *to be punished is not evil, but to become worthy of punishment is evil*. And this is because, since good simply speaking consists in act and not in potency, while ultimate act is operation, or the use of whatever things are possessed, the good of man is considered simply speaking in good operation, or in the good use of things possessed. Now we use all things through the will. Hence from a good will, by which a man makes good use of the things possessed, a man is called good; and from an evil will, evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0120","text":"For one who has an evil will can also use badly the good that he possesses; as when a grammarian voluntarily speaks ungrammatically. Therefore, since fault consists in a disordered act of the will, whereas punishment consists in the privation of some one of those things which the will uses, fault has more perfectly the character of evil than punishment. The second reason can be taken from the fact that God is the author of the evil of punishment, but not of the evil of fault. The reason for this is that the evil of punishment deprives a creature of a good of the creature, whether the good of the creature be taken as something created, as blindness deprives one of sight; or whether it be an uncreated good, as through the lack of divine vision an uncreated good is removed from the creature. But the evil of fault is properly opposed to uncreated good itself, for it is contrary to the fulfillment of the divine will and to the divine love by which the divine good is loved in itself, and not only insofar as it is participated in by the creature. Thus it is clear that fault has more of the character of evil than punishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0121","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0122","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, although fault is ordered to punishment, as merit to reward, nevertheless fault is not intended on account of punishment, as merit is on account of reward; rather, conversely, punishment is inflicted in order that fault may be avoided. And thus fault is worse than punishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0123","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0124","text":"To the second it must be said that the order of action, which is taken away through fault, is a more perfect good of the agent, since it is a second perfection, than the good which is taken away through punishment, which is a first perfection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0125","text":"[I q. 48 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0126","text":"To the third it must be said that there is not a comparison of fault to punishment as of an end and an ordering to the end, because each can in some way be deprived both through fault and through punishment. But through punishment, indeed, insofar as the man himself is removed from the end and from the order to the end; whereas through fault, insofar as this privation pertains to the action, which is not ordered to the due end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_40:P0126"]}],"source_digest":"24db55c4f123fa40df4dc70981c60730fef29ee301ee0374d6a6f1d75960fcd1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 48"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 48","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41","title":"First Part · Question 49","order":50,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_41","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 49 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30518)\n\nConsequenter quaeritur de causa mali. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum bonum possit esse causa mali. Secundo, utrum summum bonum, quod est Deus, sit causa mali. Tertio, utrum sit aliquod summum malum, quod sit prima causa omnium malorum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30519)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum non possit esse causa mali. Dicitur enim Matth. VII, *non potest arbor bona malos fructus facere*.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30520)\n\nPraeterea, unum contrariorum non potest esse causa alterius. Malum autem est contrarium bono. Ergo bonum non potest esse causa mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30521)\n\nPraeterea, effectus deficiens non procedit nisi a causa deficiente. Sed malum, si causam habeat, est effectus deficiens. Ergo habet causam deficientem. Sed omne deficiens malum est. Ergo causa mali non est nisi malum.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30522)\n\nPraeterea, Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod malum non habet causam. Ergo bonum non est causa mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30523)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, contra Iulianum, *non fuit omnino unde oriri posset malum, nisi ex bono*.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30524)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere quod omne malum aliqualiter causam habeat. Malum enim est defectus boni quod natum est et debet haberi. Quod autem aliquid deficiat a sua naturali et debita dispositione, non potest provenire nisi ex aliqua causa trahente rem extra suam dispositionem, non enim grave movetur sursum nisi ab aliquo impellente, nec agens deficit in sua actione nisi propter aliquod impedimentum. Esse autem causam non potest convenire nisi bono, quia nihil potest esse causa nisi inquantum est ens; omne autem ens, inquantum huiusmodi, bonum est. Et si consideremus speciales rationes causarum, agens et forma et finis perfectionem quandam important, quae pertinet ad rationem boni, sed et materia, inquantum est potentia ad bonum, habet rationem boni. Et quidem quod bonum sit causa mali per modum causae materialis, iam ex praemissis patet, ostensum est enim quod bonum est subiectum mali. Causam autem formalem malum non habet, sed est magis privatio formae. Et similiter nec causam finalem, sed magis est privatio ordinis ad finem debitum; non solum enim finis habet rationem boni, sed etiam utile, quod ordinatur ad finem. Causam autem per modum agentis habet malum, non autem per se, sed per accidens. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod aliter causatur malum in actione, et aliter in effectu. In actione quidem causatur malum propter defectum alicuius principiorum actionis, vel principalis agentis, vel instrumentalis, sicut defectus in motu animalis potest contingere vel propter debilitatem virtutis motivae, ut in pueris; vel propter solam ineptitudinem instrumenti, ut in claudis. Malum autem in re aliqua, non tamen in proprio effectu agentis, causatur quandoque ex virtute agentis; quandoque autem ex defectu ipsius, vel materiae. Ex virtute quidem vel perfectione agentis, quando ad formam intentam ab agente sequitur ex necessitate alterius formae privatio; sicut ad formam ignis sequitur privatio formae aeris vel aquae. Sicut ergo, quanto ignis fuerit perfectior in virtute, tanto perfectius imprimit formam suam, ita etiam tanto perfectius corrumpit contrarium, unde malum et corruptio aeris et aquae, est ex perfectione ignis. Sed hoc est per accidens, quia ignis non intendit privare formam aquae, sed inducere formam propriam; sed hoc faciendo, causat et illud per accidens. Sed si sit defectus in effectu proprio ignis, puta quod deficiat a calefaciendo, hoc est vel propter defectum actionis, qui redundat in defectum alicuius principii, ut dictum est; vel ex indispositione materiae, quae non recipit actionem ignis agentis. Sed et hoc ipsum quod est esse deficiens, accidit bono, cui per se competit agere. Unde verum est quod malum secundum nullum modum habet causam nisi per accidens. Sic autem bonum est causa mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30525)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, contra Iulian., *arborem malam appellat dominus voluntatem malam, et arborem bonam, voluntatem bonam*. Ex voluntate autem bona non producitur actus moralis malus, cum ex ipsa voluntate bona iudicetur actus moralis bonus. Sed tamen ipse motus malae voluntatis causatur a creatura rationali, quae bona est. Et sic est causa mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30526)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bonum non causat illud malum quod est sibi contrarium, sed quoddam aliud, sicut bonitas ignis causat malum aquae; et homo bonus secundum suam naturam, causat malum actum secundum morem. Et hoc ipsum per accidens est, ut dictum est. Invenitur autem quod etiam unum contrariorum causat aliud per accidens, sicut frigidum exterius ambiens calefacit, inquantum calor retrahitur ad interiora.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30527)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod malum habet causam deficientem aliter in rebus voluntariis, et naturalibus. Agens enim naturale producit effectum suum talem quale ipsum est, nisi impediatur ab aliquo extrinseco, et hoc ipsum est quidam defectus eius. Unde nunquam sequitur malum in effectu, nisi praeexistat aliquod aliud malum in agente vel materia, sicut dictum est. Sed in rebus voluntariis, defectus actionis a voluntate actu deficiente procedit, inquantum non subiicit se actu suae regulae. Qui tamen defectus non est culpa, sed sequitur culpa ex hoc quod cum tali defectu operatur.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30528)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod malum non habet causam per se, sed per accidens tantum, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30529)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod summum bonum, quod est Deus, sit causa mali. Dicitur enim Isai. XLV, *ego dominus, et non est alter Deus, formans lucem et creans tenebras, faciens pacem et creans malum*. Et Amos III, *si erit malum in civitate, quod dominus non fecerit*.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30530)\n\nPraeterea, effectus causae secundae reducitur in causam primam. Bonum autem est causa mali, ut dictum est. Cum igitur omnis boni causa sit Deus, ut supra ostensum est, sequitur quod etiam omne malum sit a Deo.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30531)\n\nPraeterea, sicut dicitur in II Physic., idem est causa salutis navis, et periculi. Sed Deus est causa salutis omnium rerum. Ergo est ipse causa omnis perditionis et mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30532)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *Deus non est auctor mali, quia non est causa tendendi ad non esse*.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30533)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex dictis patet, malum quod in defectu actionis consistit, semper causatur ex defectu agentis. In Deo autem nullus defectus est, sed summa perfectio, ut supra ostensum est. Unde malum quod in defectu actionis consistit, vel quod ex defectu agentis causatur, non reducitur in Deum sicut in causam. Sed malum quod in corruptione rerum aliquarum consistit, reducitur in Deum sicut in causam. Et hoc patet tam in naturalibus quam in voluntariis. Dictum est enim quod aliquod agens, inquantum sua virtute producit aliquam formam ad quam sequitur corruptio et defectus, causat sua virtute illam corruptionem et defectum. Manifestum est autem quod forma quam principaliter Deus intendit in rebus creatis, est bonum ordinis universi. Ordo autem universi requirit, ut supra dictum est, quod quaedam sint quae deficere possint, et interdum deficiant. Et sic Deus, in rebus causando bonum ordinis universi, ex consequenti, et quasi per accidens, causat corruptiones rerum; secundum illud quod dicitur I Reg. II, *dominus mortificat et vivificat*. Sed quod dicitur Sap. I, quod *Deus mortem non fecit*, intelligitur quasi per se intentam. Ad ordinem autem universi pertinet etiam ordo iustitiae, qui requirit ut peccatoribus poena inferatur. Et secundum hoc, Deus est auctor mali quod est poena, non autem mali quod est culpa, ratione supra dicta.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30534)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod auctoritates illae loquuntur de malo poenae, non autem de malo culpae.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30535)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod effectus causae secundae deficientis reducitur in causam primam non deficientem, quantum ad id quod habet entitatis et perfectionis, non autem quantum ad id quod habet de defectu. Sicut quidquid est motus in claudicatione, causatur a virtute motiva; sed quod est obliquitatis in ea, non est ex virtute motiva, sed ex curvitate cruris. Et similiter quidquid est entitatis et actionis in actione mala, reducitur in Deum sicut in causam, sed quod est ibi defectus, non causatur a Deo, sed ex causa secunda deficiente.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30536)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod submersio navis attribuitur nautae ut causae, ex eo quod non agit quod requiritur ad salutem navis. Sed Deus non deficit ab agendo quod est necessarium ad salutem. Unde non est simile.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30537)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod sit unum summum malum, quod sit causa omnis mali. Contrariorum enim effectuum contrariae sunt causae. Sed in rebus invenitur contrarietas, secundum illud Eccli. XXXIII, *contra malum bonum est, et contra vitam mors; sic et contra virum iustum peccator*. Ergo sunt contraria principia, unum boni, et aliud mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30538)\n\nPraeterea, si unum contrariorum est in rerum natura, et reliquum, ut dicitur in II de caelo et mundo. Sed summum bonum est in rerum natura, quod est causa omnis boni, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo est et summum malum ei oppositum, causa omnis mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30539)\n\nPraeterea, sicut in rebus invenitur bonum et melius, ita malum et peius. Sed bonum et melius dicuntur per respectum ad optimum. Ergo malum et peius dicuntur per respectum ad aliquod summum malum.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30540)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est per participationem, reducitur ad illud quod est per essentiam. Sed res quae sunt malae apud nos, non sunt malae per essentiam, sed per participationem. Ergo est invenire aliquod summum malum per essentiam, quod est causa omnis mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30541)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est per accidens, reducitur ad illud quod est per se. Sed bonum est causa mali per accidens. Ergo oportet ponere aliquod summum malum, quod sit causa malorum per se. Neque potest dici quod malum non habeat causam per se, sed per accidens tantum, quia sequeretur quod malum non esset ut in pluribus, sed ut in paucioribus.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30542)\n\nPraeterea, malum effectus reducitur ad malum causae, quia effectus deficiens est a causa deficiente, sicut supra dictum est. Sed hoc non est procedere in infinitum. Ergo oportet ponere unum primum malum, quod sit causa omnis mali.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30543)\n\nSed contra est quod summum bonum est causa omnis entis, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo non potest esse aliquod principium ei oppositum, quod sit causa malorum.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30544)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ex praedictis patet non esse unum primum principium malorum, sicut est unum primum principium bonorum. Primo quidem, quia primum principium bonorum est per essentiam bonum, ut supra ostensum est. Nihil autem potest esse per suam essentiam malum, ostensum est enim quod omne ens, inquantum est ens, bonum est; et quod malum non est nisi in bono ut in subiecto. Secundo, quia primum bonorum principium est summum et perfectum bonum, quod praehabet in se omnem bonitatem, ut supra ostensum est. Summum autem malum esse non potest, quia, sicut ostensum est, etsi malum semper diminuat bonum, nunquam tamen illud potest totaliter consumere; et sic, semper remanente bono, non potest esse aliquid integre et perfecte malum. Propter quod philosophus dicit, in IV Ethic., quod *si malum integrum sit, seipsum destruet*, quia destructo omni bono (quod requiritur ad integritatem mali), subtrahitur etiam ipsum malum, cuius subiectum est bonum. Tertio, quia ratio mali repugnat rationi primi principii. Tum quia omne malum causatur ex bono, ut supra ostensum est. Tum quia malum non potest esse causa nisi per accidens, et sic non potest esse prima causa, quia causa per accidens est posterior ea quae est per se, ut patet in II Physic. Qui autem posuerunt duo prima principia, unum bonum et alterum malum, ex eadem radice in hunc errorem inciderunt, ex qua et aliae extraneae positiones antiquorum ortum habuerunt, quia scilicet non consideraverunt causam universalem totius entis, sed particulares tantum causas particularium effectuum. Propter hoc enim, si aliquid invenerunt esse nocivum alicui rei per virtutem suae naturae, aestimaverunt naturam illius rei esse malam, puta si quis dicat naturam ignis esse malam, quia combussit domum alicuius pauperis. Iudicium autem de bonitate alicuius rei non est accipiendum secundum ordinem ad aliquid particulare; sed secundum seipsum, et secundum ordinem ad totum universum, in quo quaelibet res suum locum ordinatissime tenet, ut ex dictis patet. Similiter etiam, quia invenerunt duorum particularium effectuum contrariorum duas causas particulares contrarias, nesciverunt reducere causas particulares contrarias in causam universalem communem. Et ideo usque ad prima principia contrarietatem in causis esse iudicaverunt. Sed cum omnia contraria conveniant in uno communi, necesse est in eis, supra causas contrarias proprias, inveniri unam causam communem, sicut supra qualitates contrarias elementorum invenitur virtus corporis caelestis. Et similiter supra omnia quae quocumque modo sunt, invenitur unum primum principium essendi, ut supra ostensum est.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30545)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod contraria conveniunt in genere uno, et etiam conveniunt in ratione essendi. Et ideo, licet habeant causas particulares contrarias, tamen oportet devenire ad unam primam causam communem.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30546)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod privatio et habitus nata sunt fieri circa idem. Subiectum autem privationis est ens in potentia, ut dictum est. Unde, cum malum sit privatio boni, ut ex dictis patet, illi bono opponitur cui adiungitur potentia, non autem summo bono, quod est actus purus.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30547)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod unumquodque intenditur secundum propriam rationem. Sicut autem forma est perfectio quaedam, ita privatio est quaedam remotio. Unde omnis forma et perfectio et bonum per accessum ad terminum perfectum intenditur, privatio autem et malum per recessum a termino. Unde non dicitur malum et peius per accessum ad summum malum, sicut dicitur bonum et melius per accessum ad summum bonum.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30548)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod nullum ens dicitur malum per participationem, sed per privationem participationis. Unde non oportet fieri reductionem ad aliquid quod sit per essentiam malum.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30549)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod malum non potest habere causam nisi per accidens, ut supra ostensum est. Unde impossibile est fieri reductionem ad aliquid quod sit per se causa mali. Quod autem dicitur, quod malum est ut in pluribus, simpliciter falsum est. Nam generabilia et corruptibilia, in quibus solum contingit esse malum naturae, sunt modica pars totius universi. Et iterum in unaquaque specie defectus naturae accidit ut in paucioribus. In solis autem hominibus malum videtur esse ut in pluribus, quia bonum hominis secundum sensum non est hominis inquantum homo, idest secundum rationem; plures autem sequuntur sensum quam rationem.\n\n[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30550)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod in causis mali non est procedere in infinitum, sed est reducere omnia mala in aliquam causam bonam, ex qua sequitur malum per accidens.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0002","text":"[I q. 49 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0003","text":"Consequenter quaeritur de causa mali. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum bonum possit esse causa mali. Secundo, utrum summum bonum, quod est Deus, sit causa mali. Tertio, utrum sit aliquod summum malum, quod sit prima causa omnium malorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0005","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod bonum non possit esse causa mali. Dicitur enim Matth. VII, *non potest arbor bona malos fructus facere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0007","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0008","text":"Praeterea, unum contrariorum non potest esse causa alterius. Malum autem est contrarium bono. Ergo bonum non potest esse causa mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0009","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0010","text":"Praeterea, effectus deficiens non procedit nisi a causa deficiente. Sed malum, si causam habeat, est effectus deficiens. Ergo habet causam deficientem. Sed omne deficiens malum est. Ergo causa mali non est nisi malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0011","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0012","text":"Praeterea, Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod malum non habet causam. Ergo bonum non est causa mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0013","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, contra Iulianum, *non fuit omnino unde oriri posset malum, nisi ex bono*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0015","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere quod omne malum aliqualiter causam habeat. Malum enim est defectus boni quod natum est et debet haberi. Quod autem aliquid deficiat a sua naturali et debita dispositione, non potest provenire nisi ex aliqua causa trahente rem extra suam dispositionem, non enim grave movetur sursum nisi ab aliquo impellente, nec agens deficit in sua actione nisi propter aliquod impedimentum. Esse autem causam non potest convenire nisi bono, quia nihil potest esse causa nisi inquantum est ens; omne autem ens, inquantum huiusmodi, bonum est. Et si consideremus speciales rationes causarum, agens et forma et finis perfectionem quandam important, quae pertinet ad rationem boni, sed et materia, inquantum est potentia ad bonum, habet rationem boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0017","text":"Et quidem quod bonum sit causa mali per modum causae materialis, iam ex praemissis patet, ostensum est enim quod bonum est subiectum mali. Causam autem formalem malum non habet, sed est magis privatio formae. Et similiter nec causam finalem, sed magis est privatio ordinis ad finem debitum; non solum enim finis habet rationem boni, sed etiam utile, quod ordinatur ad finem. Causam autem per modum agentis habet malum, non autem per se, sed per accidens. Ad cuius evidentiam, sciendum est quod aliter causatur malum in actione, et aliter in effectu. In actione quidem causatur malum propter defectum alicuius principiorum actionis, vel principalis agentis, vel instrumentalis, sicut defectus in motu animalis potest contingere vel propter debilitatem virtutis motivae, ut in pueris; vel propter solam ineptitudinem instrumenti, ut in claudis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0018","text":"Malum autem in re aliqua, non tamen in proprio effectu agentis, causatur quandoque ex virtute agentis; quandoque autem ex defectu ipsius, vel materiae. Ex virtute quidem vel perfectione agentis, quando ad formam intentam ab agente sequitur ex necessitate alterius formae privatio; sicut ad formam ignis sequitur privatio formae aeris vel aquae. Sicut ergo, quanto ignis fuerit perfectior in virtute, tanto perfectius imprimit formam suam, ita etiam tanto perfectius corrumpit contrarium, unde malum et corruptio aeris et aquae, est ex perfectione ignis. Sed hoc est per accidens, quia ignis non intendit privare formam aquae, sed inducere formam propriam; sed hoc faciendo, causat et illud per accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0019","text":"Sed si sit defectus in effectu proprio ignis, puta quod deficiat a calefaciendo, hoc est vel propter defectum actionis, qui redundat in defectum alicuius principii, ut dictum est; vel ex indispositione materiae, quae non recipit actionem ignis agentis. Sed et hoc ipsum quod est esse deficiens, accidit bono, cui per se competit agere. Unde verum est quod malum secundum nullum modum habet causam nisi per accidens. Sic autem bonum est causa mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0020","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0021","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, contra Iulian., *arborem malam appellat dominus voluntatem malam, et arborem bonam, voluntatem bonam*. Ex voluntate autem bona non producitur actus moralis malus, cum ex ipsa voluntate bona iudicetur actus moralis bonus. Sed tamen ipse motus malae voluntatis causatur a creatura rationali, quae bona est. Et sic est causa mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0022","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0023","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bonum non causat illud malum quod est sibi contrarium, sed quoddam aliud, sicut bonitas ignis causat malum aquae; et homo bonus secundum suam naturam, causat malum actum secundum morem. Et hoc ipsum per accidens est, ut dictum est. Invenitur autem quod etiam unum contrariorum causat aliud per accidens, sicut frigidum exterius ambiens calefacit, inquantum calor retrahitur ad interiora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0024","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0025","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod malum habet causam deficientem aliter in rebus voluntariis, et naturalibus. Agens enim naturale producit effectum suum talem quale ipsum est, nisi impediatur ab aliquo extrinseco, et hoc ipsum est quidam defectus eius. Unde nunquam sequitur malum in effectu, nisi praeexistat aliquod aliud malum in agente vel materia, sicut dictum est. Sed in rebus voluntariis, defectus actionis a voluntate actu deficiente procedit, inquantum non subiicit se actu suae regulae. Qui tamen defectus non est culpa, sed sequitur culpa ex hoc quod cum tali defectu operatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0026","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0027","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod malum non habet causam per se, sed per accidens tantum, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0028","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0029","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0030","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod summum bonum, quod est Deus, sit causa mali. Dicitur enim Isai. XLV, *ego dominus, et non est alter Deus, formans lucem et creans tenebras, faciens pacem et creans malum*. Et Amos III, *si erit malum in civitate, quod dominus non fecerit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0031","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0032","text":"Praeterea, effectus causae secundae reducitur in causam primam. Bonum autem est causa mali, ut dictum est. Cum igitur omnis boni causa sit Deus, ut supra ostensum est, sequitur quod etiam omne malum sit a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0033","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0034","text":"Praeterea, sicut dicitur in II Physic., idem est causa salutis navis, et periculi. Sed Deus est causa salutis omnium rerum. Ergo est ipse causa omnis perditionis et mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0035","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0036","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *Deus non est auctor mali, quia non est causa tendendi ad non esse*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0037","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0038","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex dictis patet, malum quod in defectu actionis consistit, semper causatur ex defectu agentis. In Deo autem nullus defectus est, sed summa perfectio, ut supra ostensum est. Unde malum quod in defectu actionis consistit, vel quod ex defectu agentis causatur, non reducitur in Deum sicut in causam. Sed malum quod in corruptione rerum aliquarum consistit, reducitur in Deum sicut in causam. Et hoc patet tam in naturalibus quam in voluntariis. Dictum est enim quod aliquod agens, inquantum sua virtute producit aliquam formam ad quam sequitur corruptio et defectus, causat sua virtute illam corruptionem et defectum. Manifestum est autem quod forma quam principaliter Deus intendit in rebus creatis, est bonum ordinis universi. Ordo autem universi requirit, ut supra dictum est, quod quaedam sint quae deficere possint, et interdum deficiant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0039","text":"Et sic Deus, in rebus causando bonum ordinis universi, ex consequenti, et quasi per accidens, causat corruptiones rerum; secundum illud quod dicitur I Reg. II, *dominus mortificat et vivificat*. Sed quod dicitur Sap. I, quod *Deus mortem non fecit*, intelligitur quasi per se intentam. Ad ordinem autem universi pertinet etiam ordo iustitiae, qui requirit ut peccatoribus poena inferatur. Et secundum hoc, Deus est auctor mali quod est poena, non autem mali quod est culpa, ratione supra dicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0040","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0041","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod auctoritates illae loquuntur de malo poenae, non autem de malo culpae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0042","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0043","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod effectus causae secundae deficientis reducitur in causam primam non deficientem, quantum ad id quod habet entitatis et perfectionis, non autem quantum ad id quod habet de defectu. Sicut quidquid est motus in claudicatione, causatur a virtute motiva; sed quod est obliquitatis in ea, non est ex virtute motiva, sed ex curvitate cruris. Et similiter quidquid est entitatis et actionis in actione mala, reducitur in Deum sicut in causam, sed quod est ibi defectus, non causatur a Deo, sed ex causa secunda deficiente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0044","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0045","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod submersio navis attribuitur nautae ut causae, ex eo quod non agit quod requiritur ad salutem navis. Sed Deus non deficit ab agendo quod est necessarium ad salutem. Unde non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0046","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0047","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0048","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod sit unum summum malum, quod sit causa omnis mali. Contrariorum enim effectuum contrariae sunt causae. Sed in rebus invenitur contrarietas, secundum illud Eccli. XXXIII, *contra malum bonum est, et contra vitam mors; sic et contra virum iustum peccator*. Ergo sunt contraria principia, unum boni, et aliud mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0049","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0050","text":"Praeterea, si unum contrariorum est in rerum natura, et reliquum, ut dicitur in II de caelo et mundo. Sed summum bonum est in rerum natura, quod est causa omnis boni, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo est et summum malum ei oppositum, causa omnis mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0051","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0052","text":"Praeterea, sicut in rebus invenitur bonum et melius, ita malum et peius. Sed bonum et melius dicuntur per respectum ad optimum. Ergo malum et peius dicuntur per respectum ad aliquod summum malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0053","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0054","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est per participationem, reducitur ad illud quod est per essentiam. Sed res quae sunt malae apud nos, non sunt malae per essentiam, sed per participationem. Ergo est invenire aliquod summum malum per essentiam, quod est causa omnis mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0055","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0056","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est per accidens, reducitur ad illud quod est per se. Sed bonum est causa mali per accidens. Ergo oportet ponere aliquod summum malum, quod sit causa malorum per se. Neque potest dici quod malum non habeat causam per se, sed per accidens tantum, quia sequeretur quod malum non esset ut in pluribus, sed ut in paucioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0057","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0058","text":"Praeterea, malum effectus reducitur ad malum causae, quia effectus deficiens est a causa deficiente, sicut supra dictum est. Sed hoc non est procedere in infinitum. Ergo oportet ponere unum primum malum, quod sit causa omnis mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0059","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0060","text":"Sed contra est quod summum bonum est causa omnis entis, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo non potest esse aliquod principium ei oppositum, quod sit causa malorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0061","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0062","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ex praedictis patet non esse unum primum principium malorum, sicut est unum primum principium bonorum. Primo quidem, quia primum principium bonorum est per essentiam bonum, ut supra ostensum est. Nihil autem potest esse per suam essentiam malum, ostensum est enim quod omne ens, inquantum est ens, bonum est; et quod malum non est nisi in bono ut in subiecto. Secundo, quia primum bonorum principium est summum et perfectum bonum, quod praehabet in se omnem bonitatem, ut supra ostensum est. Summum autem malum esse non potest, quia, sicut ostensum est, etsi malum semper diminuat bonum, nunquam tamen illud potest totaliter consumere; et sic, semper remanente bono, non potest esse aliquid integre et perfecte malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0063","text":"Propter quod philosophus dicit, in IV Ethic., quod *si malum integrum sit, seipsum destruet*, quia destructo omni bono (quod requiritur ad integritatem mali), subtrahitur etiam ipsum malum, cuius subiectum est bonum. Tertio, quia ratio mali repugnat rationi primi principii. Tum quia omne malum causatur ex bono, ut supra ostensum est. Tum quia malum non potest esse causa nisi per accidens, et sic non potest esse prima causa, quia causa per accidens est posterior ea quae est per se, ut patet in II Physic. Qui autem posuerunt duo prima principia, unum bonum et alterum malum, ex eadem radice in hunc errorem inciderunt, ex qua et aliae extraneae positiones antiquorum ortum habuerunt, quia scilicet non consideraverunt causam universalem totius entis, sed particulares tantum causas particularium effectuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0064","text":"Propter hoc enim, si aliquid invenerunt esse nocivum alicui rei per virtutem suae naturae, aestimaverunt naturam illius rei esse malam, puta si quis dicat naturam ignis esse malam, quia combussit domum alicuius pauperis. Iudicium autem de bonitate alicuius rei non est accipiendum secundum ordinem ad aliquid particulare; sed secundum seipsum, et secundum ordinem ad totum universum, in quo quaelibet res suum locum ordinatissime tenet, ut ex dictis patet. Similiter etiam, quia invenerunt duorum particularium effectuum contrariorum duas causas particulares contrarias, nesciverunt reducere causas particulares contrarias in causam universalem communem. Et ideo usque ad prima principia contrarietatem in causis esse iudicaverunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0065","text":"Sed cum omnia contraria conveniant in uno communi, necesse est in eis, supra causas contrarias proprias, inveniri unam causam communem, sicut supra qualitates contrarias elementorum invenitur virtus corporis caelestis. Et similiter supra omnia quae quocumque modo sunt, invenitur unum primum principium essendi, ut supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0066","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0067","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod contraria conveniunt in genere uno, et etiam conveniunt in ratione essendi. Et ideo, licet habeant causas particulares contrarias, tamen oportet devenire ad unam primam causam communem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0068","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0069","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod privatio et habitus nata sunt fieri circa idem. Subiectum autem privationis est ens in potentia, ut dictum est. Unde, cum malum sit privatio boni, ut ex dictis patet, illi bono opponitur cui adiungitur potentia, non autem summo bono, quod est actus purus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0070","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0071","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod unumquodque intenditur secundum propriam rationem. Sicut autem forma est perfectio quaedam, ita privatio est quaedam remotio. Unde omnis forma et perfectio et bonum per accessum ad terminum perfectum intenditur, privatio autem et malum per recessum a termino. Unde non dicitur malum et peius per accessum ad summum malum, sicut dicitur bonum et melius per accessum ad summum bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0072","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0073","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod nullum ens dicitur malum per participationem, sed per privationem participationis. Unde non oportet fieri reductionem ad aliquid quod sit per essentiam malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0074","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0075","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod malum non potest habere causam nisi per accidens, ut supra ostensum est. Unde impossibile est fieri reductionem ad aliquid quod sit per se causa mali. Quod autem dicitur, quod malum est ut in pluribus, simpliciter falsum est. Nam generabilia et corruptibilia, in quibus solum contingit esse malum naturae, sunt modica pars totius universi. Et iterum in unaquaque specie defectus naturae accidit ut in paucioribus. In solis autem hominibus malum videtur esse ut in pluribus, quia bonum hominis secundum sensum non est hominis inquantum homo, idest secundum rationem; plures autem sequuntur sensum quam rationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0076","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0077","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod in causis mali non est procedere in infinitum, sed est reducere omnia mala in aliquam causam bonam, ex qua sequitur malum per accidens.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9dc05a76a9db66655d5a9bf50cd74d62bfdee68497a2061e6e37d8f2e58fb119","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143435578716Z_5fd6b0ee","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 49","source_sha256":"9dc05a76a9db66655d5a9bf50cd74d62bfdee68497a2061e6e37d8f2e58fb119","sha256":"c2b50dfdba3ad3c954a10df1b7b9f1dd8ef8eac46f5e309c1b4c0a2d2f9bf5d0","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:34:35.578733+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0002","text":"[I q. 49 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0003","text":"Consequently, the cause of evil is considered. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, whether good can be a cause of evil. Second, whether the highest good, which is God, is the cause of evil. Third, whether there is some highest evil which is the first cause of all evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0005","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0006","text":"We proceed to the first as follows. It seems that good cannot be a cause of evil. For it is said in Matthew VII, *a good tree cannot bear evil fruits*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0007","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0008","text":"Further, one of two contraries cannot be the cause of the other. But evil is contrary to good. Therefore good cannot be the cause of evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0009","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0010","text":"Further, a deficient effect proceeds only from a deficient cause. But evil, if it has a cause, is a deficient effect. Therefore it has a deficient cause. But every deficient thing is evil. Therefore the cause of evil is nothing but evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0011","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0012","text":"Further, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that evil has no cause. Therefore good is not the cause of evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0013","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0014","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, against Julian, *there was absolutely no source from which evil could arise except from good*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0015","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that every evil has a cause in some way. For evil is the defect of a good which is naturally suited and ought to be possessed. But that something should fall short of its natural and due disposition cannot come about except through some cause drawing the thing outside its disposition; for a heavy thing is not moved upward except by something impelling it, nor does an agent fail in its action except on account of some impediment. Now to be a cause can belong only to good, because nothing can be a cause except insofar as it is a being; but every being, insofar as it is such, is good. And if we consider the particular accounts of causes, agent, form, and end imply a certain perfection, which pertains to the notion of good; but matter also, insofar as it is potentiality for good, has the notion of good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0017","text":"Indeed, that good is the cause of evil in the manner of a material cause is already clear from what has been said above, for it has been shown that good is the subject of evil. But evil has no formal cause, but is rather a privation of form. Similarly, it has no final cause, but is rather a privation of order to the due end; for not only the end has the notion of good, but also the useful, which is ordered to the end. Yet evil has a cause in the manner of an agent, not per se, but per accidens. For an understanding of this, it should be known that evil is caused differently in an action and differently in an effect. In an action, evil is caused through a defect in one of the principles of action, whether of the principal agent or of the instrument; just as a defect in the movement of an animal can occur either through weakness of the motive power, as in children, or through the mere unsuitability of the instrument, as in the lame.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0018","text":"But evil in some thing—though not in the proper effect of the agent—is caused sometimes by the power of the agent, and sometimes by a defect in it or in the matter. It is caused by the power or perfection of the agent when, upon the form intended by the agent, the privation of another form follows of necessity; just as upon the form of fire follows the privation of the form of air or water. Therefore, just as the more perfect fire is in power, the more perfectly it impresses its form, so also the more perfectly it corrupts the contrary; whence the evil and corruption of air and water are from the perfection of fire. But this is per accidens, because fire does not intend to deprive water of its form, but to induce its own form; yet in doing this, it also causes that other thing per accidens.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0019","text":"But if there is a defect in the proper effect of fire, for example, that it falls short in heating, this is either through a defect of the action, which is traceable to a defect in some principle, as has been said; or from the indisposition of the matter, which does not receive the action of the acting fire. But this very fact of being deficient also happens to good, to which acting belongs per se. Hence it is true that evil in no way has a cause except per accidens. In this way, then, good is the cause of evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0020","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0021","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Augustine says, against Julian, *the Lord calls the evil will an evil tree, and the good will a good tree*. But from a good will a morally evil act is not produced, since a moral act is judged good from the good will itself. Nevertheless, the very movement of the evil will is caused by a rational creature, which is good. And thus it is a cause of evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0022","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0023","text":"To the second, it must be said that good does not cause the evil which is contrary to itself, but some other evil; just as the goodness of fire causes the evil of water, and a man good according to his nature causes an evil act according to morals. And this itself is per accidens, as has been said. Yet it is found that even one of two contraries causes the other per accidens, as the surrounding external cold heats insofar as heat is drawn back to the interior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0024","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0025","text":"To the third, it must be said that evil has a deficient cause differently in voluntary and in natural things. For a natural agent produces its effect such as it itself is, unless it is impeded by something extrinsic, and this itself is a certain defect of it. Hence evil in the effect never follows unless some other evil pre-exists in the agent or the matter, as has been said. But in voluntary things, the defect of the action proceeds from the will actually failing, insofar as it does not actually subject itself to its rule. Yet this defect is not guilt; rather, guilt follows from the fact that it acts with such a defect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0026","text":"[I q. 49 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0027","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that evil does not have a cause per se, but only per accidens, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0028","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0029","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0030","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the highest good, which is God, is the cause of evil. For it is said in Isaiah XLV: *I am the Lord, and there is no other God; forming light and creating darkness, making peace and creating evil.* And Amos III: *Shall there be evil in a city which the Lord has not done?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0031","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0032","text":"Further, the effect of a secondary cause is reduced to a first cause. But good is the cause of evil, as has been said. Therefore, since God is the cause of every good, as was shown above, it follows that every evil also is from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0033","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0034","text":"Further, as is said in Physics II, the same thing is the cause of a ship's safety and of its peril. But God is the cause of the safety of all things. Therefore, He Himself is the cause of every destruction and evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0035","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0036","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in the book of Eighty-Three Questions, that *God is not the author of evil, because He is not the cause of tending toward non-being*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0037","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0038","text":"I answer that, as is clear from what has been said, evil which consists in a defect of action is always caused by a defect of the agent. But in God there is no defect, but the highest perfection, as was shown above. Hence evil which consists in a defect of action, or which is caused by a defect of the agent, is not reduced to God as its cause. But evil which consists in the corruption of certain things is reduced to God as its cause. And this is clear both in natural and in voluntary matters. For it has been said that an agent, insofar as by its power it produces some form upon which corruption and defect follow, causes by its power that corruption and defect. Now it is manifest that the form principally intended by God in created things is the good of the order of the universe. But the order of the universe requires, as was said above, that there be some things that can fail, and sometimes do fail.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0039","text":"And thus God, in causing in things the good of the order of the universe, consequently, and as it were per accidens, causes corruptions of things; according to what is said in I Kings II: *the Lord kills and makes alive.* But what is said in Wisdom I, that *God did not make death*, is understood of death as intended per se. Moreover, the order of justice also belongs to the order of the universe, and this requires that punishment be inflicted upon sinners. And in this respect God is the author of the evil that is punishment, but not of the evil that is guilt, for the reason given above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0040","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0041","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that those authorities speak of the evil of punishment, not of the evil of guilt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0042","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0043","text":"To the second, it must be said that the effect of a deficient secondary cause is reduced to the non-deficient first cause as regards that which it has of being and perfection, but not as regards that which it has of defect. Thus, whatever motion there is in limping is caused by the motive power; but what there is of crookedness in it is not from the motive power, but from the curvature of the leg. And similarly, whatever there is of being and action in an evil action is reduced to God as cause, but the defect that is there is not caused by God, but by a deficient secondary cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0044","text":"[I q. 49 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0045","text":"To the third, it must be said that the sinking of a ship is attributed to the sailor as its cause, because he does not do what is required for the ship's safety. But God does not fail to do what is necessary for salvation. Hence the likeness does not hold.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0046","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0047","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0048","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that there is one highest evil, which is the cause of every evil. For contrary effects have contrary causes. But contrariety is found in things, according to Ecclesiasticus XXXIII: *against evil is good, and against life death; so also against a just man, a sinner.* Therefore there are contrary principles, one of good and the other of evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0049","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0050","text":"Moreover, if one of two contraries is in the nature of things, so too is the other, as is said in *On the Heavens and the World*, II. But the highest good is in the nature of things, and it is the cause of every good, as was shown above. Therefore there is also a highest evil opposed to it, the cause of every evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0051","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0052","text":"Moreover, just as good and better are found among things, so too are evil and worse. But good and better are spoken of in relation to the best. Therefore evil and worse are spoken of in relation to some highest evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0053","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0054","text":"Moreover, everything which is by participation is reduced to that which is by essence. But things that are evil among us are not evil by essence, but by participation. Therefore some highest evil by essence is to be found, which is the cause of every evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0055","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0056","text":"Moreover, everything which is accidental is reduced to that which is per se. But good is the cause of evil accidentally. Therefore one must posit some highest evil which is the per se cause of evils. Nor can it be said that evil has no per se cause, but only an accidental one, because it would follow that evil would not be in most cases, but in few cases.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0057","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0058","text":"Moreover, the evil of an effect is reduced to the evil of a cause, because a defective effect is from a defective cause, as was said above. But this does not proceed to infinity. Therefore one must posit one first evil, which is the cause of every evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0059","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0060","text":"But on the contrary, the highest good is the cause of every being, as was shown above. Therefore there cannot be any principle opposed to it which is the cause of evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0061","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0062","text":"I answer that it must be said that from the foregoing it is clear that there is not one first principle of evils, as there is one first principle of goods. First, indeed, because the first principle of goods is good by essence, as was shown above. But nothing can be evil through its own essence; for it has been shown that every being, insofar as it is a being, is good, and that evil exists only in good as in a subject. Second, because the first principle of goods is the highest and perfect good, which precontains in itself every goodness, as was shown above. But there cannot be a highest evil, because, as was shown, although evil always diminishes good, it can nevertheless never consume it totally; and thus, since good always remains, nothing can be wholly and perfectly evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0063","text":"For this reason the Philosopher says, in *Ethics* IV, that *if evil were integral, it would destroy itself*, because, when every good is destroyed (which is required for the integrality of evil), evil itself also is removed, for its subject is good. Third, because the notion of evil is repugnant to the notion of a first principle. Both because every evil is caused by good, as was shown above; and because evil cannot be a cause except accidentally, and thus cannot be a first cause, since an accidental cause is subsequent to that which is per se, as is clear in *Physics* II. But those who posited two first principles, one good and the other evil, fell into this error from the same root from which the other strange positions of the ancients also arose: namely, because they did not consider the universal cause of the whole of being, but only particular causes of particular effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0064","text":"For this reason, if they found something to be harmful to some thing by the power of its nature, they judged the nature of that thing to be evil—for example, if someone were to say that the nature of fire is evil because it burned the house of some poor man. But judgment concerning the goodness of a thing ought not to be taken according to its relation to something particular, but according to itself and according to its order to the whole universe, in which every thing holds its place in the most orderly manner, as is clear from what has been said. Likewise, because they found, for two contrary particular effects, two contrary particular causes, they did not know how to reduce contrary particular causes to a common universal cause. And therefore they judged that there is contrariety in causes all the way up to the first principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0065","text":"But since all contraries agree in one common thing, it is necessary that in them, above their proper contrary causes, one common cause be found, just as above the contrary qualities of the elements the power of the heavenly body is found. And likewise, above all things that in any manner are, one first principle of being is found, as was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0066","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0067","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that contraries agree in one genus, and also agree in the notion of being. Therefore, although they have contrary particular causes, it is nevertheless necessary to arrive at one first common cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0068","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0069","text":"To the second, it must be said that privation and habit are naturally apt to occur in the same thing. But the subject of privation is a being in potency, as was said. Hence, since evil is the privation of good, as is clear from what has been said, it is opposed to that good to which potency is joined, but not to the highest good, which is pure act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0070","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0071","text":"To the third, it must be said that each thing is intensified according to its proper notion. Now, just as form is a certain perfection, so privation is a certain removal. Hence every form and perfection and good is intensified by approach to the perfect terminus, whereas privation and evil are intensified by recession from the terminus. Therefore evil and worse are not spoken of by approach to a highest evil, as good and better are spoken of by approach to the highest good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0072","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0073","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that no being is called evil through participation, but through the privation of participation. Hence it is not necessary to make a reduction to something that is evil through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0074","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0075","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that evil cannot have a cause except accidentally, as was shown above. Hence it is impossible to make a reduction to something that is per se the cause of evil. But the statement that evil exists for the most part is simply false. For generable and corruptible things, in which alone evil of nature can occur, are a small part of the whole universe. And again, in each species a defect of nature occurs in the fewer cases. But in human beings alone evil seems to exist for the most part, because the good of man according to sense is not the good of man insofar as he is man, that is, according to reason; moreover, more follow sense than reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0076","text":"[I q. 49 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1044.html#30550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0077","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that in the causes of evil one must not proceed to infinity, but all evils must be reduced to some good cause, from which evil follows accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_41:P0077"]}],"source_digest":"0d956980fcca6be91f7467434a921d14c825b0091a0a2de203c99cd0cc05e341","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 49"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 49","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42","title":"First Part · Question 50","order":51,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_42","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 50 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30551)\n\nPost haec considerandum est de distinctione corporalis et spiritualis creaturae. Et primo, de creatura pure spirituali, quae in Scriptura sacra Angelus nominatur; secundo, de creatura pure corporali; tertio, de creatura composita ex corporali et spirituali, quae est homo. Circa vero Angelos, considerandum est primo de his quae pertinent ad eorum substantiam; secundo, de his quae pertinent ad eorum intellectum; tertio, de his quae pertinent ad eorum voluntatem; quarto, de his quae pertinent ad eorum creationem. De substantia autem eorum considerandum est et absolute, et per comparationem ad corporalia. Circa substantiam vero eorum absolute, quinque quaeruntur. Primo, utrum sit aliqua creatura omnino spiritualis, et penitus incorporea. Secundo, supposito quod Angelus sit talis, quaeritur utrum Angelus sit compositus ex materia et forma. Tertio, quaeritur de multitudine eorum. Quarto, de differentia ipsorum ab invicem. Quinto, de immortalitate, seu incorruptibilitate ipsorum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30552)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non sit omnino incorporeus. Illud enim quod est incorporeum solum quoad nos, et non quoad Deum, non est incorporeum simpliciter. Sed Damascenus dicit, in libro II, quod *Angelus incorporeus et immaterialis dicitur quantum ad nos, sed comparatus ad Deum, corporeus et materialis invenitur*. Non ergo est incorporeus simpliciter.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30553)\n\nPraeterea, nihil movetur nisi corpus, ut probatur in VI Physic. Sed Damascenus dicit ibidem quod Angelus est substantia intellectualis semper mobilis. Angelus ergo est substantia corporea.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30554)\n\nPraeterea, Ambrosius dicit, in libro de spiritu sancto, *omnis creatura certis suae naturae circumscripta est limitibus*. Circumscribi autem proprium est corporum. Ergo omnis creatura est corporea. Angeli autem sunt Dei creaturae, ut patet in Psalmo CXLVIII, laudate dominum, omnes Angeli eius; et postea subditur, *quoniam ipse dixit, et facta sunt, ipse mandavit, et creata sunt*. Ergo Angeli sunt corporei.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30555)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CIII, *qui facit Angelos suos spiritus*.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30556)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere aliquas creaturas incorporeas. Id enim quod praecipue in rebus creatis Deus intendit est bonum quod consistit in assimilatione ad Deum. Perfecta autem assimilatio effectus ad causam attenditur, quando effectus imitatur causam secundum illud per quod causa producit effectum; sicut calidum facit calidum. Deus autem creaturam producit per intellectum et voluntatem, ut supra ostensum est. Unde ad perfectionem universi requiritur quod sint aliquae creaturae intellectuales. Intelligere autem non potest esse actus corporis, nec alicuius virtutis corporeae, quia omne corpus determinatur ad hic et nunc. Unde necesse est ponere, ad hoc quod universum sit perfectum, quod sit aliqua incorporea creatura. Antiqui autem, ignorantes vim intelligendi, et non distinguentes inter sensum et intellectum, nihil esse existimaverunt in mundo, nisi quod sensu et imaginatione apprehendi potest. Et quia sub imaginatione non cadit nisi corpus, existimaverunt quod nullum ens esset nisi corpus; ut philosophus dicit in IV Physic. Et ex his processit Sadducaeorum error, dicentium non esse spiritum. Sed hoc ipsum quod intellectus est altior sensu, rationabiliter ostendit esse aliquas res incorporeas, a solo intellectu comprehensibiles.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30557)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod substantiae incorporeae medium sunt inter Deum et creaturas corporeas. Medium autem comparatum ad unum extremum, videtur alterum extremum; sicut tepidum comparatum calido, videtur frigidum. Et hac ratione dicitur quod Angeli, Deo comparati, sunt materiales et corporei, non quod in eis sit aliquid de natura corporea.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30558)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod motus ibi accipitur prout intelligere et velle motus quidam dicuntur. Dicitur ergo Angelus substantia semper mobilis, quia semper est actu intelligens, non quandoque actu et quandoque potentia, sicut nos. Unde patet quod ratio procedit ex aequivoco.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30559)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod circumscribi terminis localibus est proprium corporum, sed circumscribi terminis essentialibus est commune cuilibet creaturae, tam corporali quam spirituali. Unde dicit Ambrosius, in libro de Spir. Sanct., quod licet quaedam locis corporalibus non contineantur, circumscriptione tamen substantiae non carent.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30560)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus sit compositus ex materia et forma. Omne enim quod continetur sub aliquo genere, est compositum ex genere et differentia, quae, adveniens generi, constituit speciem. Sed genus sumitur ex materia, differentia vero ex forma, ut patet in VIII Metaphys. Ergo omne quod est in genere, est compositum ex materia et forma. Sed Angelus est in genere substantiae. Ergo est compositus ex materia et forma.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30561)\n\nPraeterea, in quocumque inveniuntur proprietates materiae, ibi invenitur materia. Proprietates autem materiae sunt recipere et substare; unde dicit Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *forma simplex subiectum esse non potest*. Haec autem inveniuntur in Angelo. Ergo Angelus est compositus ex materia et forma.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30562)\n\nPraeterea, forma est actus. Quod ergo est forma tantum, est actus purus. Sed Angelus non est actus purus, hoc enim solius Dei est. Ergo non est forma tantum, sed habet formam in materia.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30563)\n\nPraeterea, forma proprie limitatur et finitur per materiam. Forma ergo quae non est in materia, est forma infinita. Sed forma Angeli non est infinita, quia omnis creatura finita est. Ergo forma Angeli est in materia.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30564)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *primae creaturae sicut incorporales et immateriales intelliguntur*.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30565)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam ponunt Angelos esse compositos ex materia et forma. Et hanc opinionem astruere nititur Avicebron in libro fontis vitae. Supponit enim quod quaecumque distinguuntur secundum intellectum, sint etiam in rebus distincta. In substantia autem incorporea intellectus apprehendit aliquid per quod distinguitur a substantia corporea, et aliquid per quod cum ea convenit. Unde ex hoc vult concludere quod illud per quod differt substantia incorporea a corporea, sit ei quasi forma, et illud quod subiicitur huic formae distinguenti quasi commune, sit materia eius. Et propter hoc ponit quod eadem est materia universalis spiritualium et corporalium, ut intelligatur quod forma incorporeae substantiae sic sit impressa in materia spiritualium, sicut forma quantitatis est impressa in materia corporalium. Sed primo aspectu apparet esse impossibile unam esse materiam spiritualium et corporalium. Non enim est possibile quod forma spiritualis et corporalis recipiatur in una parte materiae, quia sic una et eadem res numero esset corporalis et spiritualis. Unde relinquitur quod alia pars materiae sit quae recipit formam corporalem, et alia quae recipit formam spiritualem. Materiam autem dividi in partes non contingit nisi secundum quod intelligitur sub quantitate, qua remota, remanet substantia indivisibilis, ut dicitur in I Physic. Sic igitur relinquitur quod materia spiritualium sit subiecta quantitati, quod est impossibile. Impossibile est ergo quod una sit materia corporalium et spiritualium. Sed adhuc ulterius impossibile est quod substantia intellectualis habeat qualemcumque materiam. Operatio enim cuiuslibet rei est secundum modum substantiae eius. Intelligere autem est operatio penitus immaterialis. Quod ex eius obiecto apparet, a quo actus quilibet recipit speciem et rationem, sic enim unumquodque intelligitur, inquantum a materia abstrahitur; quia formae in materia sunt individuales formae, quas intellectus non apprehendit secundum quod huiusmodi. Unde relinquitur quod omnis substantia intellectualis est omnino immaterialis. Non est autem necessarium quod ea quae distinguuntur secundum intellectum, sint distincta in rebus, quia intellectus non apprehendit res secundum modum rerum, sed secundum modum suum. Unde res materiales, quae sunt infra intellectum nostrum, simpliciori modo sunt in intellectu nostro, quam sint in seipsis. Substantiae autem angelicae sunt supra intellectum nostrum. Unde intellectus noster non potest attingere ad apprehendendum eas secundum quod sunt in seipsis; sed per modum suum, secundum quod apprehendit res compositas. Et sic etiam apprehendit Deum, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30566)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod differentia est quae constituit speciem. Unumquodque autem constituitur in specie, secundum quod determinatur ad aliquem specialem gradum in entibus, quia species rerum sunt sicut numeri, qui differunt per additionem et subtractionem unitatis, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. In rebus autem materialibus aliud est quod determinat ad specialem gradum, scilicet forma, et aliud quod determinatur, scilicet materia, unde ab alio sumitur genus, et ab alio differentia. Sed in rebus immaterialibus non est aliud determinans et determinatum, sed unaquaeque earum secundum seipsam tenet determinatum gradum in entibus. Et ideo genus et differentia in eis non accipitur secundum aliud et aliud, sed secundum unum et idem. Quod tamen differt secundum considerationem nostram, inquantum enim intellectus noster considerat illam rem ut indeterminate, accipitur in eis ratio generis; inquantum vero considerat ut determinate, accipitur ratio differentiae.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30567)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio illa ponitur in libro fontis vitae. Et esset necessaria, si idem esset modus quo recipit intellectus, et quo recipit materia. Sed hoc patet esse falsum. Materia enim recipit formam, ut secundum ipsam constituatur in esse alicuius speciei, vel aeris, vel ignis, vel cuiuscumque alterius. Sic autem intellectus non recipit formam, alioquin verificaretur opinio Empedoclis, qui posuit quod terram terra cognoscimus, et ignem igne. Sed forma intelligibilis est in intellectu secundum ipsam rationem formae, sic enim cognoscitur ab intellectu. Unde talis receptio non est receptio materiae, sed est receptio substantiae immaterialis.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30568)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet in Angelo non sit compositio formae et materiae, est tamen in eo actus et potentia. Quod quidem manifestum potest esse ex consideratione rerum materialium, in quibus invenitur duplex compositio. Prima quidem formae et materiae, ex quibus constituitur natura aliqua. Natura autem sic composita non est suum esse, sed esse est actus eius. Unde ipsa natura comparatur ad suum esse sicut potentia ad actum. Subtracta ergo materia, et posito quod ipsa forma subsistat non in materia, adhuc remanet comparatio formae ad ipsum esse ut potentiae ad actum. Et talis compositio intelligenda est in Angelis. Et hoc est quod a quibusdam dicitur, quod Angelus est compositus ex quo est et quod est, vel ex esse et quod est, ut Boetius dicit, nam quod est est ipsa forma subsistens; ipsum autem esse est quo substantia est, sicut cursus est quo currens currit. Sed in Deo non est aliud esse et quod est, ut supra ostensum est. Unde solus Deus est actus purus.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30569)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod omnis creatura est finita simpliciter, inquantum esse eius non est absolutum subsistens, sed limitatur ad naturam aliquam cui advenit. Sed nihil prohibet aliquam creaturam esse secundum quid infinitam. Creaturae autem materiales habent infinitatem ex parte materiae, sed finitatem ex parte formae, quae limitatur per materiam in qua recipitur. Substantiae autem immateriales creatae sunt finitae secundum suum esse, sed infinitae secundum quod eorum formae non sunt receptae in alio. Sicut si diceremus albedinem separatam existentem esse infinitam quantum ad rationem albedinis, quia non contrahitur ad aliquod subiectum; esse tamen eius esset finitum, quia determinatur ad aliquam naturam specialem. Et propter hoc dicitur in libro de causis, quod intelligentia est finita superius, inquantum scilicet recipit esse a suo superiori; sed est infinita inferius, inquantum non recipitur in aliqua materia.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30570)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint in aliquo magno numero. Numerus enim species quantitatis est, et sequitur divisionem continui. Hoc autem non potest esse in Angelis cum sint incorporei, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo Angeli non possunt esse in aliquo magno numero.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30571)\n\nPraeterea, quanto aliquid est magis propinquum uni, tanto minus est multiplicatum, ut in numeris apparet. Natura autem angelica inter alias naturas creatas est Deo propinquior. Cum ergo Deus sit maxime unus, videtur quod in natura angelica inveniatur minimum de multitudine.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30572)\n\nPraeterea, proprius effectus separatarum substantiarum videtur esse motus corporum caelestium. Sed motus corporum caelestium sunt secundum aliquem determinatum numerum paucum, qui a nobis comprehendi potest. Ergo Angeli non sunt in maiori multitudine, quam motus corporum caelestium.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30573)\n\nPraeterea, Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *propter radios divinae bonitatis subsistunt intelligibiles et intellectuales omnes substantiae*. Sed radius non multiplicatur nisi secundum diversitatem recipientium. Non autem potest dici quod materia sit receptiva intelligibilis radii, cum substantiae intellectuales sint immateriales, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo videtur quod multiplicatio substantiarum intellectualium non possit esse nisi secundum exigentiam primorum corporum, scilicet caelestium, ut ad ea quodammodo processus praedictorum radiorum terminetur. Et sic idem quod prius.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30574)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Dan. VII, *millia millium ministrabant ei, et decies millies centena millia assistebant ei*.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30575)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa numerum substantiarum separatarum, diversi diversis viis processerunt. Plato enim posuit substantias separatas esse species rerum sensibilium, utpote si poneremus ipsam naturam humanam esse separatam. Et secundum hoc oportebat dicere quod substantiae separatae sint secundum numerum specierum sensibilium. Sed hanc positionem improbat Aristoteles, ex eo quod materia est de ratione speciei horum sensibilium. Unde substantiae separatae non possunt esse species exemplares horum sensibilium, sed habent quasdam naturas altiores naturis rerum sensibilium. Posuit tamen Aristoteles quod illae naturae perfectiores habent ordinem ad sensibilia ista, secundum rationem moventis et finis. Et ideo secundum numerum primorum motuum, conatus est adinvenire numerum substantiarum separatarum. Sed quia hoc videtur repugnare documentis sacrae Scripturae, Rabbi Moyses, Iudaeus, volens utrumque concordare, posuit quod Angeli, secundum quod dicuntur substantiae immateriales, multiplicantur secundum numerum motuum vel corporum caelestium, secundum Aristotelem. Sed posuit quod Angeli in Scriptura dicuntur etiam homines divina annuntiantes; et iterum virtutes rerum naturalium, quae Dei omnipotentiam manifestant. Sed hoc est alienum a consuetudine Scripturae, quod virtutes rerum irrationabilium Angeli nominentur. Unde dicendum est quod etiam Angeli secundum quod sunt immateriales substantiae, in quadam multitudine maxima sunt, omnem materialem multitudinem excedentes. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, XIV cap. Caelest. Hierarch., *multi sunt beati exercitus supernarum mentium, infirmam et constrictam excedentes nostrorum materialium numerorum commensurationem*. Et huius ratio est quia, cum perfectio universi sit illud quod praecipue Deus intendit in creatione rerum, quanto aliqua sunt magis perfecta tanto in maiori excessu sunt creata a Deo. Sicut autem in corporibus attenditur excessus secundum magnitudinem, ita in rebus incorporeis potest attendi excessus secundum multitudinem. Videmus autem quod corpora incorruptibilia, quae sunt perfectiora inter corpora, excedunt quasi incomparabiliter secundum magnitudinem corpora corruptibilia, nam tota sphaera activorum et passivorum est aliquid modicum respectu corporum caelestium. Unde rationabile est quod substantiae immateriales excedant secundum multitudinem substantias materiales, quasi incomparabiliter.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30576)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in Angelis non est numerus qui est quantitas discreta, causatus ex divisione continui, sed causatus ex distinctione formarum, prout multitudo est de transcendentibus, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30577)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ex hoc quod natura angelica est Deo propinqua, oportet quod habeat minimum de multitudine in sui compositione, non autem ita quod in paucis salvetur.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30578)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa est Aristotelis in XII Metaphys. Et ex necessitate concluderet, si substantiae separatae essent propter substantias corporales, sic enim frustra essent immateriales substantiae, nisi ex eis aliquis motus in rebus corporalibus appareret. Non est autem hoc verum, quod substantiae immateriales sint propter corporales, quia finis nobilior est his quae sunt ad finem. Unde etiam Aristoteles dicit ibidem quod haec ratio non est necessaria, sed probabilis. Coactus autem fuit hac ratione uti, quia ad cognoscendum intelligibilia non possumus pervenire nisi per sensibilia.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30579)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod ratio illa procedit secundum opinionem eorum qui causam distinctionis rerum ponebant esse materiam. Hoc autem improbatum est. Unde multiplicatio Angelorum neque secundum materiam, neque secundum corpora est accipienda, sed secundum divinam sapientiam, diversos ordines immaterialium substantiarum excogitantem.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30580)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non differant specie. Cum enim differentia sit nobilior genere, quaecumque conveniunt secundum id quod est nobilissimum in eis, conveniunt in ultima differentia constitutiva; et ita sunt eadem secundum speciem. Sed omnes Angeli conveniunt in eo quod est nobilissimum in eis, scilicet in intellectualitate. Ergo omnes Angeli sunt unius speciei.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30581)\n\nPraeterea, magis et minus non diversificant speciem. Sed Angeli non videntur differre ad invicem nisi secundum magis et minus; prout scilicet unus alio est simplicior, et perspicacioris intellectus. Ergo Angeli non differunt specie.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30582)\n\nPraeterea, anima et Angelus ex opposito dividuntur. Sed omnes animae sunt unius speciei. Ergo et Angeli.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30583)\n\nPraeterea, quanto aliquid est perfectius in natura, tanto magis debet multiplicari. Hoc autem non esset, si in una specie esset unum tantum individuum. Ergo multi Angeli sunt unius speciei.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30584)\n\nSed contra est quod in his quae sunt unius speciei, non est invenire prius et posterius, ut dicitur in III Metaphys. Sed in Angelis, etiam unius ordinis, sunt primi et medii et ultimi, ut dicit Dionysius, X cap. Ang. Hier. Ergo Angeli non sunt eiusdem speciei.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30585)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt omnes substantias spirituales esse unius speciei, etiam animas. Alii vero quod omnes Angeli sunt unius speciei, sed non animae. Quidam vero quod omnes Angeli unius hierarchiae, aut etiam unius ordinis. Sed hoc est impossibile. Ea enim quae conveniunt specie et differunt numero, conveniunt in forma, et distinguuntur materialiter. Si ergo Angeli non sunt compositi ex materia et forma, ut dictum est supra, sequitur quod impossibile sit esse duos Angelos unius speciei. Sicut etiam impossibile esset dicere quod essent plures albedines separatae, aut plures humanitates; cum albedines non sint plures nisi secundum quod sunt in pluribus substantiis. Si tamen Angeli haberent materiam, nec sic possent esse plures Angeli unius speciei. Sic enim oporteret quod principium distinctionis unius ab alio esset materia, non quidem secundum divisionem quantitatis, cum sint incorporei, sed secundum diversitatem potentiarum. Quae quidem diversitas materiae causat diversitatem non solum speciei, sed generis.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30586)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod differentia est nobilior genere, sicut determinatum indeterminato et proprium communi; non autem sicut alia et alia natura. Alioquin oporteret quod omnia animalia irrationalia essent unius speciei; vel quod esset in eis aliqua alia perfectior forma quam anima sensibilis. Differunt ergo specie animalia irrationalia secundum diversos gradus determinatos naturae sensitivae. Et similiter omnes Angeli differunt specie secundum diversos gradus naturae intellectivae.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30587)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod magis et minus, secundum quod causantur ex intensione et remissione unius formae, non diversificant speciem. Sed secundum quod causantur ex formis diversorum graduum, sic diversificant speciem, sicut si dicamus quod ignis est perfectior aere. Et hoc modo Angeli diversificantur secundum magis et minus.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30588)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod bonum speciei praeponderat bono individui. Unde multo melius est quod multiplicentur species in Angelis, quam quod multiplicentur individua in una specie.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30589)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod multiplicatio secundum numerum, cum in infinitum protendi possit, non intenditur ab agente, sed sola multiplicatio secundum speciem, ut supra dictum est. Unde perfectio naturae angelicae requirit multiplicationem specierum, non autem multiplicationem individuorum in una specie.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30590)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint incorruptibiles. Dicit enim Damascenus de Angelo, quod *est substantia intellectualis, gratia et non natura immortalitatem suscipiens*.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30591)\n\nPraeterea, Plato dicit, in Timaeo, *o dii deorum, quorum opifex idem paterque ego, opera siquidem vos mea, dissolubilia natura, me tamen ita volente indissolubilia*. Hos autem deos non aliud quam Angelos intelligere potest. Ergo Angeli natura sua sunt corruptibiles.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30592)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Gregorium, *omnia in nihilum deciderent, nisi ea manus omnipotentis conservaret*. Sed quod in nihilum redigi potest, est corruptibile. Ergo, cum Angeli sint a Deo facti, videtur quod sint corruptibiles secundum suam naturam.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30593)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod intellectuales substantiae *vitam habent indeficientem, ab universa corruptione, morte et materia et generatione mundae existentes*.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30594)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere Angelos secundum suam naturam esse incorruptibiles. Cuius ratio est, quia nihil corrumpitur nisi per hoc, quod forma eius a materia separatur, unde, cum Angelus sit ipsa forma subsistens, ut ex dictis patet, impossibile est quod eius substantia sit corruptibilis. Quod enim convenit alicui secundum se, nunquam ab eo separari potest, ab eo autem cui convenit per aliud, potest separari, separato eo secundum quod ei conveniebat. Rotunditas enim a circulo separari non potest, quia convenit ei secundum seipsum, sed aeneus circulus potest amittere rotunditatem per hoc, quod circularis figura separatur ab aere. Esse autem secundum se competit formae, unumquodque enim est ens actu secundum quod habet formam. Materia vero est ens actu per formam. Compositum igitur ex materia et forma desinit esse actu per hoc, quod forma separatur a materia. Sed si ipsa forma subsistat in suo esse, sicut est in Angelis, ut dictum est, non potest amittere esse. Ipsa igitur immaterialitas Angeli est ratio quare Angelus est incorruptibilis secundum suam naturam. Et huius incorruptibilitatis signum accipi potest ex eius intellectuali operatione, quia enim unumquodque operatur secundum quod est actu, operatio rei indicat modum esse ipsius. Species autem et ratio operationis ex obiecto comprehenditur. Obiectum autem intelligibile, cum sit supra tempus, est sempiternum. Unde omnis substantia intellectualis est incorruptibilis secundum suam naturam.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30595)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Damascenus accipit immortalitatem perfectam, quae includit omnimodam immutabilitatem, quia omnis mutatio est quaedam mors, ut Augustinus dicit. Perfectam autem immutabilitatem Angeli non nisi per gratiam assequuntur, ut infra patebit.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30596)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Plato per deos intelligit corpora caelestia, quae existimabat esse ex elementis composita, et ideo secundum suam naturam dissolubilia, sed voluntate divina semper conservantur in esse.\n\n[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30597)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, quoddam necessarium est quod habet causam suae necessitatis. Unde non repugnat necessario nec incorruptibili, quod esse eius dependeat ab alio sicut a causa. Per hoc ergo quod dicitur quod omnia deciderent in nihilum nisi continerentur a Deo, et etiam Angeli, non datur intelligi quod in Angelis sit aliquod corruptionis principium, sed quod esse Angeli dependeat a Deo sicut a causa. Non autem dicitur aliquid esse corruptibile, per hoc quod Deus possit illud in non esse redigere, subtrahendo suam conservationem, sed per hoc quod in seipso aliquod principium corruptionis habet, vel contrarietatem vel saltem potentiam materiae.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0002","text":"[I q. 50 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0003","text":"Post haec considerandum est de distinctione corporalis et spiritualis creaturae. Et primo, de creatura pure spirituali, quae in Scriptura sacra Angelus nominatur; secundo, de creatura pure corporali; tertio, de creatura composita ex corporali et spirituali, quae est homo. Circa vero Angelos, considerandum est primo de his quae pertinent ad eorum substantiam; secundo, de his quae pertinent ad eorum intellectum; tertio, de his quae pertinent ad eorum voluntatem; quarto, de his quae pertinent ad eorum creationem. De substantia autem eorum considerandum est et absolute, et per comparationem ad corporalia. Circa substantiam vero eorum absolute, quinque quaeruntur. Primo, utrum sit aliqua creatura omnino spiritualis, et penitus incorporea. Secundo, supposito quod Angelus sit talis, quaeritur utrum Angelus sit compositus ex materia et forma. Tertio, quaeritur de multitudine eorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0004","text":"Quarto, de differentia ipsorum ab invicem. Quinto, de immortalitate, seu incorruptibilitate ipsorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0006","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non sit omnino incorporeus. Illud enim quod est incorporeum solum quoad nos, et non quoad Deum, non est incorporeum simpliciter. Sed Damascenus dicit, in libro II, quod *Angelus incorporeus et immaterialis dicitur quantum ad nos, sed comparatus ad Deum, corporeus et materialis invenitur*. Non ergo est incorporeus simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0008","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0009","text":"Praeterea, nihil movetur nisi corpus, ut probatur in VI Physic. Sed Damascenus dicit ibidem quod Angelus est substantia intellectualis semper mobilis. Angelus ergo est substantia corporea.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0010","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0011","text":"Praeterea, Ambrosius dicit, in libro de spiritu sancto, *omnis creatura certis suae naturae circumscripta est limitibus*. Circumscribi autem proprium est corporum. Ergo omnis creatura est corporea. Angeli autem sunt Dei creaturae, ut patet in Psalmo CXLVIII, laudate dominum, omnes Angeli eius; et postea subditur, *quoniam ipse dixit, et facta sunt, ipse mandavit, et creata sunt*. Ergo Angeli sunt corporei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0012","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0013","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CIII, *qui facit Angelos suos spiritus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0014","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0015","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere aliquas creaturas incorporeas. Id enim quod praecipue in rebus creatis Deus intendit est bonum quod consistit in assimilatione ad Deum. Perfecta autem assimilatio effectus ad causam attenditur, quando effectus imitatur causam secundum illud per quod causa producit effectum; sicut calidum facit calidum. Deus autem creaturam producit per intellectum et voluntatem, ut supra ostensum est. Unde ad perfectionem universi requiritur quod sint aliquae creaturae intellectuales. Intelligere autem non potest esse actus corporis, nec alicuius virtutis corporeae, quia omne corpus determinatur ad hic et nunc. Unde necesse est ponere, ad hoc quod universum sit perfectum, quod sit aliqua incorporea creatura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0016","text":"Antiqui autem, ignorantes vim intelligendi, et non distinguentes inter sensum et intellectum, nihil esse existimaverunt in mundo, nisi quod sensu et imaginatione apprehendi potest. Et quia sub imaginatione non cadit nisi corpus, existimaverunt quod nullum ens esset nisi corpus; ut philosophus dicit in IV Physic. Et ex his processit Sadducaeorum error, dicentium non esse spiritum. Sed hoc ipsum quod intellectus est altior sensu, rationabiliter ostendit esse aliquas res incorporeas, a solo intellectu comprehensibiles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0017","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod substantiae incorporeae medium sunt inter Deum et creaturas corporeas. Medium autem comparatum ad unum extremum, videtur alterum extremum; sicut tepidum comparatum calido, videtur frigidum. Et hac ratione dicitur quod Angeli, Deo comparati, sunt materiales et corporei, non quod in eis sit aliquid de natura corporea.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0019","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod motus ibi accipitur prout intelligere et velle motus quidam dicuntur. Dicitur ergo Angelus substantia semper mobilis, quia semper est actu intelligens, non quandoque actu et quandoque potentia, sicut nos. Unde patet quod ratio procedit ex aequivoco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0021","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod circumscribi terminis localibus est proprium corporum, sed circumscribi terminis essentialibus est commune cuilibet creaturae, tam corporali quam spirituali. Unde dicit Ambrosius, in libro de Spir. Sanct., quod licet quaedam locis corporalibus non contineantur, circumscriptione tamen substantiae non carent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0024","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus sit compositus ex materia et forma. Omne enim quod continetur sub aliquo genere, est compositum ex genere et differentia, quae, adveniens generi, constituit speciem. Sed genus sumitur ex materia, differentia vero ex forma, ut patet in VIII Metaphys. Ergo omne quod est in genere, est compositum ex materia et forma. Sed Angelus est in genere substantiae. Ergo est compositus ex materia et forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0026","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0027","text":"Praeterea, in quocumque inveniuntur proprietates materiae, ibi invenitur materia. Proprietates autem materiae sunt recipere et substare; unde dicit Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *forma simplex subiectum esse non potest*. Haec autem inveniuntur in Angelo. Ergo Angelus est compositus ex materia et forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0028","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0029","text":"Praeterea, forma est actus. Quod ergo est forma tantum, est actus purus. Sed Angelus non est actus purus, hoc enim solius Dei est. Ergo non est forma tantum, sed habet formam in materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0030","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0031","text":"Praeterea, forma proprie limitatur et finitur per materiam. Forma ergo quae non est in materia, est forma infinita. Sed forma Angeli non est infinita, quia omnis creatura finita est. Ergo forma Angeli est in materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0032","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0033","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *primae creaturae sicut incorporales et immateriales intelliguntur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0034","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam ponunt Angelos esse compositos ex materia et forma. Et hanc opinionem astruere nititur Avicebron in libro fontis vitae. Supponit enim quod quaecumque distinguuntur secundum intellectum, sint etiam in rebus distincta. In substantia autem incorporea intellectus apprehendit aliquid per quod distinguitur a substantia corporea, et aliquid per quod cum ea convenit. Unde ex hoc vult concludere quod illud per quod differt substantia incorporea a corporea, sit ei quasi forma, et illud quod subiicitur huic formae distinguenti quasi commune, sit materia eius. Et propter hoc ponit quod eadem est materia universalis spiritualium et corporalium, ut intelligatur quod forma incorporeae substantiae sic sit impressa in materia spiritualium, sicut forma quantitatis est impressa in materia corporalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0036","text":"Sed primo aspectu apparet esse impossibile unam esse materiam spiritualium et corporalium. Non enim est possibile quod forma spiritualis et corporalis recipiatur in una parte materiae, quia sic una et eadem res numero esset corporalis et spiritualis. Unde relinquitur quod alia pars materiae sit quae recipit formam corporalem, et alia quae recipit formam spiritualem. Materiam autem dividi in partes non contingit nisi secundum quod intelligitur sub quantitate, qua remota, remanet substantia indivisibilis, ut dicitur in I Physic. Sic igitur relinquitur quod materia spiritualium sit subiecta quantitati, quod est impossibile. Impossibile est ergo quod una sit materia corporalium et spiritualium. Sed adhuc ulterius impossibile est quod substantia intellectualis habeat qualemcumque materiam. Operatio enim cuiuslibet rei est secundum modum substantiae eius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0037","text":"Intelligere autem est operatio penitus immaterialis. Quod ex eius obiecto apparet, a quo actus quilibet recipit speciem et rationem, sic enim unumquodque intelligitur, inquantum a materia abstrahitur; quia formae in materia sunt individuales formae, quas intellectus non apprehendit secundum quod huiusmodi. Unde relinquitur quod omnis substantia intellectualis est omnino immaterialis. Non est autem necessarium quod ea quae distinguuntur secundum intellectum, sint distincta in rebus, quia intellectus non apprehendit res secundum modum rerum, sed secundum modum suum. Unde res materiales, quae sunt infra intellectum nostrum, simpliciori modo sunt in intellectu nostro, quam sint in seipsis. Substantiae autem angelicae sunt supra intellectum nostrum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0038","text":"Unde intellectus noster non potest attingere ad apprehendendum eas secundum quod sunt in seipsis; sed per modum suum, secundum quod apprehendit res compositas. Et sic etiam apprehendit Deum, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0039","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod differentia est quae constituit speciem. Unumquodque autem constituitur in specie, secundum quod determinatur ad aliquem specialem gradum in entibus, quia species rerum sunt sicut numeri, qui differunt per additionem et subtractionem unitatis, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. In rebus autem materialibus aliud est quod determinat ad specialem gradum, scilicet forma, et aliud quod determinatur, scilicet materia, unde ab alio sumitur genus, et ab alio differentia. Sed in rebus immaterialibus non est aliud determinans et determinatum, sed unaquaeque earum secundum seipsam tenet determinatum gradum in entibus. Et ideo genus et differentia in eis non accipitur secundum aliud et aliud, sed secundum unum et idem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0041","text":"Quod tamen differt secundum considerationem nostram, inquantum enim intellectus noster considerat illam rem ut indeterminate, accipitur in eis ratio generis; inquantum vero considerat ut determinate, accipitur ratio differentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0042","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0043","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio illa ponitur in libro fontis vitae. Et esset necessaria, si idem esset modus quo recipit intellectus, et quo recipit materia. Sed hoc patet esse falsum. Materia enim recipit formam, ut secundum ipsam constituatur in esse alicuius speciei, vel aeris, vel ignis, vel cuiuscumque alterius. Sic autem intellectus non recipit formam, alioquin verificaretur opinio Empedoclis, qui posuit quod terram terra cognoscimus, et ignem igne. Sed forma intelligibilis est in intellectu secundum ipsam rationem formae, sic enim cognoscitur ab intellectu. Unde talis receptio non est receptio materiae, sed est receptio substantiae immaterialis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0044","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0045","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet in Angelo non sit compositio formae et materiae, est tamen in eo actus et potentia. Quod quidem manifestum potest esse ex consideratione rerum materialium, in quibus invenitur duplex compositio. Prima quidem formae et materiae, ex quibus constituitur natura aliqua. Natura autem sic composita non est suum esse, sed esse est actus eius. Unde ipsa natura comparatur ad suum esse sicut potentia ad actum. Subtracta ergo materia, et posito quod ipsa forma subsistat non in materia, adhuc remanet comparatio formae ad ipsum esse ut potentiae ad actum. Et talis compositio intelligenda est in Angelis. Et hoc est quod a quibusdam dicitur, quod Angelus est compositus ex quo est et quod est, vel ex esse et quod est, ut Boetius dicit, nam quod est est ipsa forma subsistens; ipsum autem esse est quo substantia est, sicut cursus est quo currens currit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0046","text":"Sed in Deo non est aliud esse et quod est, ut supra ostensum est. Unde solus Deus est actus purus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0047","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0048","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod omnis creatura est finita simpliciter, inquantum esse eius non est absolutum subsistens, sed limitatur ad naturam aliquam cui advenit. Sed nihil prohibet aliquam creaturam esse secundum quid infinitam. Creaturae autem materiales habent infinitatem ex parte materiae, sed finitatem ex parte formae, quae limitatur per materiam in qua recipitur. Substantiae autem immateriales creatae sunt finitae secundum suum esse, sed infinitae secundum quod eorum formae non sunt receptae in alio. Sicut si diceremus albedinem separatam existentem esse infinitam quantum ad rationem albedinis, quia non contrahitur ad aliquod subiectum; esse tamen eius esset finitum, quia determinatur ad aliquam naturam specialem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0049","text":"Et propter hoc dicitur in libro de causis, quod intelligentia est finita superius, inquantum scilicet recipit esse a suo superiori; sed est infinita inferius, inquantum non recipitur in aliqua materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0050","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0051","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0052","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint in aliquo magno numero. Numerus enim species quantitatis est, et sequitur divisionem continui. Hoc autem non potest esse in Angelis cum sint incorporei, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo Angeli non possunt esse in aliquo magno numero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0053","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0054","text":"Praeterea, quanto aliquid est magis propinquum uni, tanto minus est multiplicatum, ut in numeris apparet. Natura autem angelica inter alias naturas creatas est Deo propinquior. Cum ergo Deus sit maxime unus, videtur quod in natura angelica inveniatur minimum de multitudine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0055","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0056","text":"Praeterea, proprius effectus separatarum substantiarum videtur esse motus corporum caelestium. Sed motus corporum caelestium sunt secundum aliquem determinatum numerum paucum, qui a nobis comprehendi potest. Ergo Angeli non sunt in maiori multitudine, quam motus corporum caelestium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0057","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0058","text":"Praeterea, Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *propter radios divinae bonitatis subsistunt intelligibiles et intellectuales omnes substantiae*. Sed radius non multiplicatur nisi secundum diversitatem recipientium. Non autem potest dici quod materia sit receptiva intelligibilis radii, cum substantiae intellectuales sint immateriales, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo videtur quod multiplicatio substantiarum intellectualium non possit esse nisi secundum exigentiam primorum corporum, scilicet caelestium, ut ad ea quodammodo processus praedictorum radiorum terminetur. Et sic idem quod prius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0059","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0060","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Dan. VII, *millia millium ministrabant ei, et decies millies centena millia assistebant ei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0061","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0062","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa numerum substantiarum separatarum, diversi diversis viis processerunt. Plato enim posuit substantias separatas esse species rerum sensibilium, utpote si poneremus ipsam naturam humanam esse separatam. Et secundum hoc oportebat dicere quod substantiae separatae sint secundum numerum specierum sensibilium. Sed hanc positionem improbat Aristoteles, ex eo quod materia est de ratione speciei horum sensibilium. Unde substantiae separatae non possunt esse species exemplares horum sensibilium, sed habent quasdam naturas altiores naturis rerum sensibilium. Posuit tamen Aristoteles quod illae naturae perfectiores habent ordinem ad sensibilia ista, secundum rationem moventis et finis. Et ideo secundum numerum primorum motuum, conatus est adinvenire numerum substantiarum separatarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0063","text":"Sed quia hoc videtur repugnare documentis sacrae Scripturae, Rabbi Moyses, Iudaeus, volens utrumque concordare, posuit quod Angeli, secundum quod dicuntur substantiae immateriales, multiplicantur secundum numerum motuum vel corporum caelestium, secundum Aristotelem. Sed posuit quod Angeli in Scriptura dicuntur etiam homines divina annuntiantes; et iterum virtutes rerum naturalium, quae Dei omnipotentiam manifestant. Sed hoc est alienum a consuetudine Scripturae, quod virtutes rerum irrationabilium Angeli nominentur. Unde dicendum est quod etiam Angeli secundum quod sunt immateriales substantiae, in quadam multitudine maxima sunt, omnem materialem multitudinem excedentes. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, XIV cap. Caelest. Hierarch., *multi sunt beati exercitus supernarum mentium, infirmam et constrictam excedentes nostrorum materialium numerorum commensurationem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0064","text":"Et huius ratio est quia, cum perfectio universi sit illud quod praecipue Deus intendit in creatione rerum, quanto aliqua sunt magis perfecta tanto in maiori excessu sunt creata a Deo. Sicut autem in corporibus attenditur excessus secundum magnitudinem, ita in rebus incorporeis potest attendi excessus secundum multitudinem. Videmus autem quod corpora incorruptibilia, quae sunt perfectiora inter corpora, excedunt quasi incomparabiliter secundum magnitudinem corpora corruptibilia, nam tota sphaera activorum et passivorum est aliquid modicum respectu corporum caelestium. Unde rationabile est quod substantiae immateriales excedant secundum multitudinem substantias materiales, quasi incomparabiliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0065","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0066","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in Angelis non est numerus qui est quantitas discreta, causatus ex divisione continui, sed causatus ex distinctione formarum, prout multitudo est de transcendentibus, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0067","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0068","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ex hoc quod natura angelica est Deo propinqua, oportet quod habeat minimum de multitudine in sui compositione, non autem ita quod in paucis salvetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0069","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0070","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa est Aristotelis in XII Metaphys. Et ex necessitate concluderet, si substantiae separatae essent propter substantias corporales, sic enim frustra essent immateriales substantiae, nisi ex eis aliquis motus in rebus corporalibus appareret. Non est autem hoc verum, quod substantiae immateriales sint propter corporales, quia finis nobilior est his quae sunt ad finem. Unde etiam Aristoteles dicit ibidem quod haec ratio non est necessaria, sed probabilis. Coactus autem fuit hac ratione uti, quia ad cognoscendum intelligibilia non possumus pervenire nisi per sensibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0071","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0072","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod ratio illa procedit secundum opinionem eorum qui causam distinctionis rerum ponebant esse materiam. Hoc autem improbatum est. Unde multiplicatio Angelorum neque secundum materiam, neque secundum corpora est accipienda, sed secundum divinam sapientiam, diversos ordines immaterialium substantiarum excogitantem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0073","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0074","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0075","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non differant specie. Cum enim differentia sit nobilior genere, quaecumque conveniunt secundum id quod est nobilissimum in eis, conveniunt in ultima differentia constitutiva; et ita sunt eadem secundum speciem. Sed omnes Angeli conveniunt in eo quod est nobilissimum in eis, scilicet in intellectualitate. Ergo omnes Angeli sunt unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0076","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0077","text":"Praeterea, magis et minus non diversificant speciem. Sed Angeli non videntur differre ad invicem nisi secundum magis et minus; prout scilicet unus alio est simplicior, et perspicacioris intellectus. Ergo Angeli non differunt specie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0078","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0079","text":"Praeterea, anima et Angelus ex opposito dividuntur. Sed omnes animae sunt unius speciei. Ergo et Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0080","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0081","text":"Praeterea, quanto aliquid est perfectius in natura, tanto magis debet multiplicari. Hoc autem non esset, si in una specie esset unum tantum individuum. Ergo multi Angeli sunt unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0082","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0083","text":"Sed contra est quod in his quae sunt unius speciei, non est invenire prius et posterius, ut dicitur in III Metaphys. Sed in Angelis, etiam unius ordinis, sunt primi et medii et ultimi, ut dicit Dionysius, X cap. Ang. Hier. Ergo Angeli non sunt eiusdem speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0084","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0085","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt omnes substantias spirituales esse unius speciei, etiam animas. Alii vero quod omnes Angeli sunt unius speciei, sed non animae. Quidam vero quod omnes Angeli unius hierarchiae, aut etiam unius ordinis. Sed hoc est impossibile. Ea enim quae conveniunt specie et differunt numero, conveniunt in forma, et distinguuntur materialiter. Si ergo Angeli non sunt compositi ex materia et forma, ut dictum est supra, sequitur quod impossibile sit esse duos Angelos unius speciei. Sicut etiam impossibile esset dicere quod essent plures albedines separatae, aut plures humanitates; cum albedines non sint plures nisi secundum quod sunt in pluribus substantiis. Si tamen Angeli haberent materiam, nec sic possent esse plures Angeli unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0086","text":"Sic enim oporteret quod principium distinctionis unius ab alio esset materia, non quidem secundum divisionem quantitatis, cum sint incorporei, sed secundum diversitatem potentiarum. Quae quidem diversitas materiae causat diversitatem non solum speciei, sed generis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0087","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0088","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod differentia est nobilior genere, sicut determinatum indeterminato et proprium communi; non autem sicut alia et alia natura. Alioquin oporteret quod omnia animalia irrationalia essent unius speciei; vel quod esset in eis aliqua alia perfectior forma quam anima sensibilis. Differunt ergo specie animalia irrationalia secundum diversos gradus determinatos naturae sensitivae. Et similiter omnes Angeli differunt specie secundum diversos gradus naturae intellectivae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0089","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0090","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod magis et minus, secundum quod causantur ex intensione et remissione unius formae, non diversificant speciem. Sed secundum quod causantur ex formis diversorum graduum, sic diversificant speciem, sicut si dicamus quod ignis est perfectior aere. Et hoc modo Angeli diversificantur secundum magis et minus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0091","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0092","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod bonum speciei praeponderat bono individui. Unde multo melius est quod multiplicentur species in Angelis, quam quod multiplicentur individua in una specie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0093","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0094","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod multiplicatio secundum numerum, cum in infinitum protendi possit, non intenditur ab agente, sed sola multiplicatio secundum speciem, ut supra dictum est. Unde perfectio naturae angelicae requirit multiplicationem specierum, non autem multiplicationem individuorum in una specie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0095","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0096","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0097","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint incorruptibiles. Dicit enim Damascenus de Angelo, quod *est substantia intellectualis, gratia et non natura immortalitatem suscipiens*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0098","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0099","text":"Praeterea, Plato dicit, in Timaeo, *o dii deorum, quorum opifex idem paterque ego, opera siquidem vos mea, dissolubilia natura, me tamen ita volente indissolubilia*. Hos autem deos non aliud quam Angelos intelligere potest. Ergo Angeli natura sua sunt corruptibiles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0100","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0101","text":"Praeterea, secundum Gregorium, *omnia in nihilum deciderent, nisi ea manus omnipotentis conservaret*. Sed quod in nihilum redigi potest, est corruptibile. Ergo, cum Angeli sint a Deo facti, videtur quod sint corruptibiles secundum suam naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0102","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0103","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod intellectuales substantiae *vitam habent indeficientem, ab universa corruptione, morte et materia et generatione mundae existentes*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0104","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0105","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere Angelos secundum suam naturam esse incorruptibiles. Cuius ratio est, quia nihil corrumpitur nisi per hoc, quod forma eius a materia separatur, unde, cum Angelus sit ipsa forma subsistens, ut ex dictis patet, impossibile est quod eius substantia sit corruptibilis. Quod enim convenit alicui secundum se, nunquam ab eo separari potest, ab eo autem cui convenit per aliud, potest separari, separato eo secundum quod ei conveniebat. Rotunditas enim a circulo separari non potest, quia convenit ei secundum seipsum, sed aeneus circulus potest amittere rotunditatem per hoc, quod circularis figura separatur ab aere. Esse autem secundum se competit formae, unumquodque enim est ens actu secundum quod habet formam. Materia vero est ens actu per formam. Compositum igitur ex materia et forma desinit esse actu per hoc, quod forma separatur a materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0106","text":"Sed si ipsa forma subsistat in suo esse, sicut est in Angelis, ut dictum est, non potest amittere esse. Ipsa igitur immaterialitas Angeli est ratio quare Angelus est incorruptibilis secundum suam naturam. Et huius incorruptibilitatis signum accipi potest ex eius intellectuali operatione, quia enim unumquodque operatur secundum quod est actu, operatio rei indicat modum esse ipsius. Species autem et ratio operationis ex obiecto comprehenditur. Obiectum autem intelligibile, cum sit supra tempus, est sempiternum. Unde omnis substantia intellectualis est incorruptibilis secundum suam naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0107","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0108","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Damascenus accipit immortalitatem perfectam, quae includit omnimodam immutabilitatem, quia omnis mutatio est quaedam mors, ut Augustinus dicit. Perfectam autem immutabilitatem Angeli non nisi per gratiam assequuntur, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0109","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0110","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Plato per deos intelligit corpora caelestia, quae existimabat esse ex elementis composita, et ideo secundum suam naturam dissolubilia, sed voluntate divina semper conservantur in esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0111","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0112","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, quoddam necessarium est quod habet causam suae necessitatis. Unde non repugnat necessario nec incorruptibili, quod esse eius dependeat ab alio sicut a causa. Per hoc ergo quod dicitur quod omnia deciderent in nihilum nisi continerentur a Deo, et etiam Angeli, non datur intelligi quod in Angelis sit aliquod corruptionis principium, sed quod esse Angeli dependeat a Deo sicut a causa. Non autem dicitur aliquid esse corruptibile, per hoc quod Deus possit illud in non esse redigere, subtrahendo suam conservationem, sed per hoc quod in seipso aliquod principium corruptionis habet, vel contrarietatem vel saltem potentiam materiae.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"dc076dd7138bc7ca2bd3da68b30b367cdaede4c9852b9ad6a82863d72e968f46","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143552932933Z_e2571477","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 50","source_sha256":"dc076dd7138bc7ca2bd3da68b30b367cdaede4c9852b9ad6a82863d72e968f46","sha256":"b02ef04566b5309cd21ca2d4d2326ba620cbd3d5fe188436c6a8dbe150ea8613","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:35:52.932948+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0002","text":"[I q. 50 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0003","text":"After these matters, we must consider the distinction between corporeal and spiritual creature. And first, concerning the purely spiritual creature, which in Sacred Scripture is called an Angel; second, concerning the purely corporeal creature; third, concerning the creature composed of corporeal and spiritual, which is man. Concerning the Angels, however, we must consider first those things pertaining to their substance; second, those pertaining to their intellect; third, those pertaining to their will; fourth, those pertaining to their creation. Now concerning their substance, we must consider both absolutely and in comparison with corporeal things. Concerning their substance absolutely, five questions are asked. First, whether there is any creature altogether spiritual and entirely incorporeal. Second, granted that an Angel is such, it is asked whether an Angel is composed of matter and form. Third, the question concerns their multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0004","text":"Fourth, concerning their difference from one another. Fifth, concerning their immortality, or incorruptibility.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0006","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0007","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that an Angel is not altogether incorporeal. For that which is incorporeal only with respect to us, and not with respect to God, is not simply incorporeal. But Damascene says, in Book II, that *an Angel is called incorporeal and immaterial in relation to us, but when compared to God, is found to be corporeal and material*. Therefore, he is not simply incorporeal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0008","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0009","text":"Moreover, nothing is moved except a body, as is proved in Physics VI. But Damascene says in the same place that an Angel is an intellectual substance always in motion. Therefore, an Angel is a corporeal substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0010","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0011","text":"Moreover, Ambrose says, in the book On the Holy Spirit, *every creature is circumscribed by the definite limits of its nature*. But to be circumscribed is proper to bodies. Therefore every creature is corporeal. Now Angels are creatures of God, as is clear in Psalm CXLVIII: Praise the Lord, all his Angels; and afterwards it is added, *for he spoke, and they were made; he commanded, and they were created*. Therefore Angels are corporeal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0012","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0013","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm CIII, *who makes his Angels spirits*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0014","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0015","text":"I answer that it must be held that there are certain incorporeal creatures. For that which God principally intends in created things is the good which consists in assimilation to God. Now a perfect assimilation of an effect to its cause is considered when the effect imitates the cause according to that by which the cause produces the effect; just as what is hot makes what is hot. But God produces the creature through intellect and will, as was shown above. Hence, for the perfection of the universe it is required that there be certain intellectual creatures. But understanding cannot be an act of a body, nor of any corporeal power, because every body is determined to here and now. Hence it must be held, in order that the universe may be perfect, that there is some incorporeal creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0016","text":"The ancients, however, being ignorant of the power of understanding, and not distinguishing between sense and intellect, thought that there was nothing in the world except what can be apprehended by sense and imagination. And because nothing falls under imagination except body, they thought that no being existed except body, as the Philosopher says in Physics IV. And from these arose the error of the Sadducees, who said that there is no spirit. But this very fact that intellect is higher than sense reasonably shows that there are certain incorporeal things, comprehensible by intellect alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0017","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that incorporeal substances are intermediate between God and corporeal creatures. But what is intermediate, when compared to one extreme, appears to be the other extreme; just as what is lukewarm, compared to what is hot, appears cold. And for this reason it is said that Angels, compared with God, are material and corporeal, not because there is in them anything of corporeal nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0019","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that motion there is taken insofar as understanding and willing are called certain motions. Therefore an Angel is called a substance always in motion because he is always actually understanding, not at one time in act and at another in potency, as we are. Hence it is clear that the argument proceeds from equivocation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0021","text":"[I q. 50 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that to be circumscribed by local limits is proper to bodies, but to be circumscribed by essential limits is common to every creature, both corporeal and spiritual. Hence Ambrose says, in the book On the Holy Spirit, that although certain things are not contained by corporeal places, nevertheless they do not lack circumscription of substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0024","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second point. It seems that an angel is composed of matter and form. For everything contained under some genus is composed of genus and difference, which, coming to the genus, constitutes the species. But genus is taken from matter, whereas difference is taken from form, as is clear in Metaphysics VIII. Therefore everything that is in a genus is composed of matter and form. But an angel is in the genus of substance. Therefore it is composed of matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0026","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0027","text":"Moreover, wherever the properties of matter are found, matter is found there. But the properties of matter are to receive and to underlie; hence Boethius says, in the book On the Trinity, that *a simple form cannot be a subject*. But these are found in an angel. Therefore an angel is composed of matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0028","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0029","text":"Moreover, form is act. Therefore that which is form alone is pure act. But an angel is not pure act, for this belongs to God alone. Therefore it is not form alone, but has form in matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0030","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0031","text":"Moreover, form is properly limited and bounded by matter. Therefore a form which is not in matter is an infinite form. But an angel's form is not infinite, because every creature is finite. Therefore an angel's form is in matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0032","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0033","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says in chapter IV of On the Divine Names that *the first creatures are understood as incorporeal and immaterial*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0034","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0035","text":"I answer that some posit that angels are composed of matter and form. And Avicebron endeavors to establish this opinion in the book Fountain of Life. For he supposes that whatever things are distinguished according to the intellect are also distinct in reality. Now in an incorporeal substance the intellect apprehends something by which it is distinguished from corporeal substance, and something by which it agrees with it. Hence from this he wishes to conclude that that by which an incorporeal substance differs from a corporeal one is, as it were, its form, and that what is subjected to this distinguishing form as something common is its matter. And on this account he posits that the universal matter of spiritual and corporeal things is the same, so that the form of incorporeal substance may be understood to be impressed upon the matter of spiritual things just as the form of quantity is impressed upon corporeal matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0036","text":"But at first sight it appears impossible that the matter of spiritual and corporeal things should be one. For it is not possible that spiritual and corporeal form be received in one part of matter, because thus one and the same thing numerically would be corporeal and spiritual. Hence it remains that one part of matter receives corporeal form, and another receives spiritual form. But matter cannot be divided into parts except insofar as it is understood under quantity; once this is removed, an indivisible substance remains, as is said in Physics I. Thus, then, it remains that the matter of spiritual things is subject to quantity, which is impossible. Therefore it is impossible that the matter of corporeal and spiritual things be one. But further still, it is impossible that an intellectual substance have any matter whatsoever. For the operation of any thing is according to the mode of its substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0037","text":"But understanding is an operation wholly immaterial. This is apparent from its object, from which every act receives its species and account; for thus each thing is understood, insofar as it is abstracted from matter, because forms in matter are individual forms, which the intellect does not apprehend as such. Hence it follows that every intellectual substance is altogether immaterial. But it is not necessary that things distinguished according to the intellect be distinct in reality, because the intellect does not apprehend things according to the mode of things, but according to its own mode. Hence material things, which are below our intellect, are in our intellect in a simpler mode than they are in themselves. But angelic substances are above our intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0038","text":"Hence our intellect cannot attain to apprehend them as they are in themselves, but according to its own mode, according as it apprehends composite things. And thus it also apprehends God, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0039","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that difference is that which constitutes the species. But each thing is constituted in a species insofar as it is determined to some special grade among beings, because the species of things are like numbers, which differ by the addition and subtraction of unity, as is said in Metaphysics VIII. Now in material things, that which determines to a special grade, namely form, is one thing, and that which is determined, namely matter, is another; hence genus is taken from one thing and difference from another. But in immaterial things, the determining and the determined are not different things; rather, each of them through itself holds a determinate grade among beings. And therefore genus and difference in them are not taken according to one thing and another, but according to one and the same thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0041","text":"Yet this differs according to our consideration: insofar as our intellect considers that thing as indeterminate, the notion of genus is taken in them; but insofar as it considers it as determinate, the notion of difference is taken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0042","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0043","text":"To the second, it must be said that this argument is set forth in the book Fountain of Life. And it would be necessary if the mode by which the intellect receives and that by which matter receives were the same. But this is evidently false. For matter receives form so that through it it may be constituted in the being of some species, whether of air or fire or anything else. But the intellect does not receive form in this way; otherwise the opinion of Empedocles would be verified, who held that we know earth by earth and fire by fire. But intelligible form is in the intellect according to the very account of form, for it is thus that it is known by the intellect. Hence such reception is not the reception of matter, but the reception of an immaterial substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0044","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0045","text":"To the third, it must be said that although in an angel there is no composition of form and matter, nevertheless there are act and potency in it. This can indeed be made clear from the consideration of material things, in which a twofold composition is found. The first is that of form and matter, from which some nature is constituted. But a nature thus composed is not its own being; rather, being is its act. Hence nature itself is compared to its being as potency to act. Therefore, if matter is removed and it is posited that form itself subsists not in matter, there still remains a relation of form to being itself as potency to act. And such a composition is to be understood in angels. And this is what is said by some: that an angel is composed of that by which it is and that which it is, or of being and that which is, as Boethius says. For that which is is the subsisting form itself; but being itself is that by which a substance is, just as running is that by which a runner runs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0046","text":"But in God, being and that which is are not distinct, as was shown above. Hence God alone is pure act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0047","text":"[I q. 50 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0048","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that every creature is finite simply speaking, insofar as its being is not absolute subsisting being, but is limited to some nature to which it comes. But nothing prevents some creature from being infinite in a certain respect. Now material creatures have infinity on the part of matter, but finitude on the part of form, which is limited by the matter in which it is received. But created immaterial substances are finite according to their being, but infinite insofar as their forms are not received in another. Thus, if we were to say that separately existing whiteness is infinite as regards the notion of whiteness, because it is not contracted to any subject, nevertheless its being would be finite, because it is determined to some special nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0049","text":"And on this account it is said in the Book of Causes that an intelligence is finite above, namely insofar as it receives being from what is above it; but it is infinite below, insofar as it is not received in any matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0050","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0051","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0052","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that angels are not in any great number. For number is a species of quantity, and follows the division of a continuum. But this cannot be in angels, since they are incorporeal, as was shown above. Therefore angels cannot be in any great number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0053","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0054","text":"Moreover, the nearer something is to unity, the less it is multiplied, as appears in numbers. But angelic nature is nearer to God than other created natures. Therefore, since God is supremely one, it seems that in angelic nature there is the least of multitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0055","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0056","text":"Moreover, the proper effect of separate substances seems to be the motion of the heavenly bodies. But the motions of the heavenly bodies are according to some determinate small number, which can be comprehended by us. Therefore angels are not in a greater multitude than the motions of the heavenly bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0057","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0058","text":"Moreover, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *The Divine Names*, that *all intelligible and intellectual substances subsist by reason of the rays of divine goodness*. But a ray is multiplied only according to the diversity of recipients. Now it cannot be said that matter is receptive of an intelligible ray, since intellectual substances are immaterial, as was shown above. Therefore it seems that the multiplication of intellectual substances can be only according to the requirement of the first bodies, namely the heavenly bodies, so that in some manner the procession of the aforesaid rays may terminate in them. And thus the same as before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0059","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0060","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Dan. VII: *thousands of thousands ministered to him, and ten thousand times a hundred thousand stood before him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0061","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0062","text":"I answer that, concerning the number of separate substances, different persons have proceeded by different ways. For Plato held that separate substances are the species of sensible things, as if we were to posit human nature itself as separate. And according to this, it was necessary to say that separate substances correspond in number to the species of sensible things. But Aristotle disproves this position, on the ground that matter belongs to the account of the species of these sensible things. Hence separate substances cannot be the exemplar species of these sensible things, but have certain natures higher than the natures of sensible things. Yet Aristotle held that those more perfect natures have an ordering to these sensible things, according to the character of mover and end. And therefore, according to the number of the first motions, he endeavored to discover the number of separate substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0063","text":"But because this seems to conflict with the teachings of Sacred Scripture, Rabbi Moses, a Jew, wishing to reconcile both, held that angels, insofar as they are called immaterial substances, are multiplied according to the number of motions or heavenly bodies, in accordance with Aristotle. But he held that angels in Scripture are also called men announcing divine things; and likewise the powers of natural things, which manifest God's omnipotence. But it is foreign to the usage of Scripture that the powers of irrational things should be called angels. Hence it must be said that even angels insofar as they are immaterial substances are in a certain very great multitude, exceeding every material multitude. And this is what Dionysius says, in chapter XIV of *The Celestial Hierarchy*: *the blessed hosts of the supernal minds are many, exceeding the feeble and constricted commensuration of our material numbers*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0064","text":"And the reason for this is that, since the perfection of the universe is that which God chiefly intends in the creation of things, the more perfect certain things are, the more abundantly they have been created by God. Now, just as in bodies abundance is considered according to magnitude, so in incorporeal things abundance can be considered according to multitude. But we see that incorruptible bodies, which are more perfect among bodies, exceed corruptible bodies almost incomparably in magnitude; for the whole sphere of active and passive things is something small in comparison with the heavenly bodies. Hence it is reasonable that immaterial substances should exceed material substances in multitude, almost incomparably.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0065","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0066","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in angels there is not number which is discrete quantity, caused by the division of a continuum, but number caused by the distinction of forms, insofar as multitude is among the transcendentals, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0067","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0068","text":"To the second it must be said that, from the fact that angelic nature is close to God, it is necessary that it have the least of multitude in its composition, but not that it be preserved in a few.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0069","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0070","text":"To the third it must be said that this argument is Aristotle's in Metaphysics XII. And it would conclude necessarily if separate substances existed for the sake of corporeal substances; for in that case immaterial substances would exist in vain unless some motion in corporeal things appeared from them. But it is not true that immaterial substances exist for the sake of corporeal ones, because the end is nobler than the things that are for the end. Hence Aristotle himself also says in the same place that this argument is not necessary, but probable. He was, however, compelled to use this argument because we cannot arrive at knowledge of intelligible things except through sensible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0071","text":"[I q. 50 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0072","text":"To the fourth it must be said that this argument proceeds according to the opinion of those who held that matter is the cause of the distinction of things. But this has been disproved. Hence the multiplication of angels is to be understood neither according to matter nor according to bodies, but according to divine wisdom, devising diverse orders of immaterial substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0073","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0074","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0075","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that Angels do not differ in species. For since difference is nobler than genus, whatever things agree according to that which is most noble in them agree in their ultimate constitutive difference; and thus they are the same according to species. But all Angels agree in that which is most noble in them, namely, in intellectuality. Therefore all Angels are of one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0076","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0077","text":"Further, more and less do not diversify species. But Angels do not seem to differ from one another except according to more and less; namely, insofar as one is simpler than another, and of more penetrating intellect. Therefore Angels do not differ in species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0078","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0079","text":"Further, soul and Angel are divided in opposition. But all souls are of one species. Therefore Angels are as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0080","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0081","text":"Further, the more perfect something is in nature, the more it ought to be multiplied. But this would not be so if there were only one individual in one species. Therefore many Angels are of one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0082","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0083","text":"But on the contrary, among those things which are of one species, prior and posterior are not to be found, as is said in Metaphysics III. But among Angels, even of one order, there are first, middle, and last, as Dionysius says in chapter 10 of The Angelic Hierarchy. Therefore Angels are not of the same species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0084","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0085","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have held all spiritual substances to be of one species, including souls. Others, however, held that all Angels are of one species, but not souls. Others again, that all Angels of one hierarchy, or even of one order, are [of one species]. But this is impossible. For things that agree in species and differ in number agree in form and are distinguished materially. Therefore, if Angels are not composed of matter and form, as was said above, it follows that it is impossible for there to be two Angels of one species. Just as it would also be impossible to say that there were several separate whitenesses, or several humanities, since whitenesses are not several except insofar as they are in several substances. Yet if Angels had matter, neither thus could there be several Angels of one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0086","text":"For thus it would be necessary that the principle of distinction of one from another be matter, not indeed according to the division of quantity, since they are incorporeal, but according to diversity of powers. This diversity of matter, moreover, causes diversity not only of species but also of genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0087","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0088","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that difference is nobler than genus, as the determinate than the indeterminate and the proper than the common, but not as one nature than another nature. Otherwise it would be necessary that all irrational animals be of one species, or that there be in them some other form more perfect than the sensitive soul. Therefore irrational animals differ in species according to diverse determinate grades of sensitive nature. And similarly all Angels differ in species according to diverse grades of intellectual nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0089","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0090","text":"To the second it must be said that more and less, insofar as they are caused by the intensification and remission of one form, do not diversify species. But insofar as they are caused by forms of diverse grades, they do diversify species, as if we were to say that fire is more perfect than air. And in this way Angels are diversified according to more and less.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0091","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0092","text":"To the third it must be said that the good of the species outweighs the good of the individual. Hence it is much better that species be multiplied among Angels than that individuals be multiplied in one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0093","text":"[I q. 50 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0094","text":"To the fourth it must be said that multiplication according to number, since it can be extended to infinity, is not intended by the agent, but only multiplication according to species, as was said above. Hence the perfection of the angelic nature requires multiplication of species, but not multiplication of individuals in one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0095","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0096","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0097","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth point. It seems that angels are not incorruptible. For Damascene says of an angel that it “is an intellectual substance, receiving immortality by grace and not by nature.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0098","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0099","text":"Further, Plato says in the *Timaeus*, “O gods of gods, whose maker and father am I myself: since you are my works, dissoluble by nature, yet, by my will, indissoluble.” Now by these gods he can understand nothing other than angels. Therefore, angels are corruptible by their nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0100","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0101","text":"Further, according to Gregory, “all things would fall into nothing unless the hand of the Almighty preserved them.” But that which can be reduced to nothing is corruptible. Therefore, since angels were made by God, it seems that they are corruptible according to their nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0102","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0103","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter 4 of *On the Divine Names*, that intellectual substances “have unfailing life, existing clean from all corruption, death, matter, and generation.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0104","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0105","text":"I answer that it must be said that angels are incorruptible according to their nature. The reason for this is that nothing is corrupted except through its form being separated from matter; hence, since an angel is a subsisting form itself, as is clear from what has been said, it is impossible for its substance to be corruptible. For what belongs to something through itself can never be separated from it; but what belongs to it through another can be separated from it, once that through which it belonged to it has been removed. For roundness cannot be separated from a circle, because it belongs to it through itself; but a bronze circle can lose its roundness through the circular figure being separated from the bronze. But being belongs through itself to form, for each thing is actually a being insofar as it has form. Matter, however, is actually a being through form. Therefore, the composite of matter and form ceases to be actual through the form’s being separated from matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0106","text":"But if the form itself subsists in its being, as it does in angels, as has been said, it cannot lose being. Therefore, the very immateriality of an angel is the reason why an angel is incorruptible according to its nature. And a sign of this incorruptibility may be taken from its intellectual operation: for, since each thing operates insofar as it is actual, the operation of a thing indicates its mode of being. But the species and nature of an operation are comprehended from its object. Now the intelligible object, since it is above time, is everlasting. Hence every intellectual substance is incorruptible according to its nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0107","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0108","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Damascene understands perfect immortality, which includes complete immutability, because every change is a kind of death, as Augustine says. But angels attain perfect immutability only through grace, as will become clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0109","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0110","text":"To the second, it must be said that Plato understands by gods the heavenly bodies, which he thought to be composed of the elements and therefore dissoluble according to their nature, but which are always preserved in being by the divine will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0111","text":"[I q. 50 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0112","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as was said above, something necessary has a cause of its necessity. Hence it is not incompatible with what is necessary or incorruptible that its being depend on another as on a cause. Therefore, from the statement that all things, including angels, would fall into nothing unless they were sustained by God, it is not to be understood that there is in angels some principle of corruption, but that the being of an angel depends on God as on a cause. Nor is something said to be corruptible because God can reduce it to non-being by withdrawing His preservation, but because it has in itself some principle of corruption, either contrariety or at least the potentiality of matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_42:P0112"]}],"source_digest":"4575b471accb0cec75a1337135c9a64fe1c22723278556c7c94d47c1a14485ed","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 50"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 50","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43","title":"First Part · Question 51","order":52,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_43","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 51 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30598)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de Angelis per comparationem ad corporalia. Et primo, de comparatione Angelorum ad corpora; secundo, de comparatione Angelorum ad loca corporalia; tertio, de comparatione Angelorum ad motum localem. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angeli habeant corpora naturaliter sibi unita. Secundo, utrum assumant corpora. Tertio, utrum in corporibus assumptis exerceant opera vitae.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30599)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli habeant corpora naturaliter sibi unita. Dicit enim Origenes, in libro peri archon, *solius Dei, idest patris et filii et spiritus sancti, naturae illud proprium est, ut sine materiali substantia et absque ulla corporeae adiectionis societate, intelligatur existere*. Bernardus etiam dicit, in VI homilia super Cant., *demus Deo soli, sicut immortalitatem sic incorporeitatem, cuius natura sola neque propter se neque propter alium, solatio indiget instrumenti corporei. Liquet autem omnem spiritum creatum corporeo indigere solatio*. Augustinus etiam dicit, super Gen. ad Litt., *Daemones aerea dicuntur animalia, quia corporum aereorum natura vigent*. Eadem autem est natura Daemonis et Angeli. Ergo Angeli habent corpora naturaliter sibi unita.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30600)\n\nPraeterea, Gregorius, in homilia Epiphaniae, nominat Angelum rationale animal. Omne autem animal componitur ex corpore et anima. Ergo Angeli habent corpora naturaliter sibi unita.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30601)\n\nPraeterea, perfectior est vita in Angelis quam in animabus. Sed anima non solum vivit, sed etiam vivificat corpus. Ergo Angeli vivificant corpora naturaliter sibi unita.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30602)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli sicut incorporales intelliguntur*.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30603)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angeli non habent corpora sibi naturaliter unita. Quod enim accidit alicui naturae, non invenitur universaliter in natura illa, sicut habere alas, quia non est de ratione animalis, non convenit omni animali. Cum autem intelligere non sit actus corporis nec alicuius virtutis corporeae, ut infra patebit, habere corpus unitum non est de ratione substantiae intellectualis inquantum huiusmodi, sed accidit alicui substantiae intellectuali propter aliquid aliud; sicut humanae animae competit uniri corpori, quia est imperfecta et in potentia existens in genere intellectualium substantiarum, non habens in sui natura plenitudinem scientiae, sed acquirens eam per sensus corporeos a sensibilibus rebus, ut infra dicetur. In quocumque autem genere invenitur aliquid imperfectum, oportet praeexistere aliquid perfectum in genere illo. Sunt igitur aliquae substantiae perfectae intellectuales in natura intellectuali, non indigentes acquirere scientiam a sensibilibus rebus. Non igitur omnes substantiae intellectuales sunt unitae corporibus; sed aliquae sunt a corporibus separatae. Et has dicimus Angelos.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30604)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, quorundam opinio fuit quod omne ens esset corpus. Et ex hac existimatione derivatum videtur, quod aliqui existimaverunt nullas substantias incorporeas esse nisi corporibus unitas; adeo quod quidam etiam posuerunt Deum esse animam mundi, ut Augustinus narrat in VII de Civ. Dei. Sed quia hoc fidei Catholicae repugnat, quae ponit Deum super omnia exaltatum, secundum illud Psalmi VIII, *elevata est magnificentia tua super caelos*, Origenes, hoc de Deo dicere recusans, de aliis secutus est aliorum opinionem; sicut et in multis aliis deceptus fuit, sequens antiquorum philosophorum opiniones. Verbum autem Bernardi potest exponi, quod spiritus creati indigeant corporali instrumento, non naturaliter unito, sed ad aliquid assumpto, ut infra dicetur. Augustinus autem loquitur non asserendo, sed opinione Platonicorum utens, qui ponebant esse quaedam animalia aerea, quae Daemones nominabant.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30605)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Gregorius nominat Angelum rationale animal metaphorice, propter similitudinem rationis.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30606)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod vivificare effective simpliciter perfectionis est. Unde et Deo convenit secundum illud I Reg. II, *dominus mortificat et vivificat*. Sed vivificare formaliter est substantiae quae est pars alicuius naturae, et non habentis in se integram naturam speciei. Unde substantia intellectualis quae non est unita corpori, est perfectior quam ea quae est corpori unita.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30607)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non assumant corpora. In opere enim Angeli nihil est superfluum; sicut neque in opere naturae. Sed superfluum esset quod Angeli corpora assumerent, Angelus enim non indiget corpore, cum eius virtus omnem virtutem corporis excedat. Ergo Angelus non assumit corpus.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30608)\n\nPraeterea, omnis assumptio ad aliquam unionem terminatur, quia assumere dicitur quasi ad se sumere. Sed corpus non unitur Angelo ut formae, sicut dictum est. Ex eo autem quod unitur sibi ut motori, non dicitur assumi, alioquin sequeretur quod omnia corpora mota ab Angelis, essent ab eis assumpta. Ergo Angeli non assumunt corpora.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30609)\n\nPraeterea, Angeli non assumunt corpora de terra vel aqua, quia non subito disparerent; neque iterum de igne, quia comburerent ea quae contingerent; neque iterum ex aere, quia aer infigurabilis est et incolorabilis. Ergo Angeli corpora non assumunt.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30610)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XVI de Civ. Dei, quod Angeli in assumptis corporibus Abrahae apparuerunt.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30611)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt Angelos nunquam corpora assumere, sed omnia quae in Scripturis divinis leguntur de apparitionibus Angelorum, contigisse in visione prophetiae, hoc est secundum imaginationem. Sed hoc repugnat intentioni Scripturae. Illud enim quod imaginaria visione videtur, est in sola imaginatione videntis, unde non videtur indifferenter ab omnibus. Scriptura autem divina sic introducit interdum Angelos apparentes, ut communiter ab omnibus viderentur; sicut Angeli apparentes Abrahae, visi sunt ab eo et tota familia eius, et a Loth, et a civibus Sodomorum. Similiter Angelus qui apparuit Tobiae, ab omnibus videbatur. Ex quo manifestum fit huiusmodi contigisse secundum corpoream visionem, qua videtur id quod positum est extra videntem, unde ab omnibus videri potest. Tali autem visione non videtur nisi corpus. Cum igitur Angeli neque corpora sint, neque habeant corpora naturaliter sibi unita, ut ex dictis patet, relinquitur quod interdum corpora assumant.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30612)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Angeli non indigent corpore assumpto propter seipsos, sed propter nos; ut familiariter cum hominibus conversando, demonstrent intelligibilem societatem quam homines expectant cum eis habendam in futura vita. Hoc etiam quod Angeli corpora assumpserunt in veteri testamento, fuit quoddam figurale indicium quod verbum Dei assumpturum esset corpus humanum, omnes enim apparitiones veteris testamenti ad illam apparitionem ordinatae fuerunt, qua filius Dei apparuit in carne.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30613)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod corpus assumptum unitur Angelo, non quidem ut formae, neque solum ut motori; sed sicut motori repraesentato per corpus mobile assumptum. Sicut enim in sacra Scriptura proprietates rerum intelligibilium sub similitudinibus rerum sensibilium describuntur, ita corpora sensibilia divina virtute sic formantur ab Angelis, ut congruant ad repraesentandum Angeli intelligibiles proprietates. Et hoc est Angelum assumere corpus.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30614)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet aer, in sua raritate manens, non retineat figuram neque colorem; quando tamen condensatur, et figurari et colorari potest, sicut patet in nubibus. Et sic Angeli assumunt corpora ex aere, condensando ipsum virtute divina, quantum necesse est ad corporis assumendi formationem.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30615)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli in corporibus assumptis opera vitae exerceant. Angelos enim veritatis non decet aliqua fictio. Esset autem fictio, si corpus ab eis assumptum, quod vivum videtur et opera vitae habens, non haberet huiusmodi. Ergo Angeli in assumpto corpore opera vitae exercent.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30616)\n\nPraeterea, in operibus Angeli non sunt aliqua frustra. Frustra autem in corpore assumpto per Angelum formarentur oculi et nares et alia sensuum instrumenta, nisi per ea Angelus sentiret. Ergo Angelus sentit per corpus assumptum. Quod est propriissimum opus vitae.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30617)\n\nPraeterea, moveri motu processivo est unum de operibus vitae, ut patet in II de anima. Manifeste autem Angeli apparent in assumptis corporibus moveri. Dicitur enim Gen. XVIII, quod Abraham simul gradiebatur, deducens Angelos qui ei apparuerant. Et Angelus Tobiae quaerenti, nosti viam quae ducit in civitatem Medorum? Respondit, *novi, et omnia itinera eius frequenter ambulavi*. Ergo Angeli in corporibus assumptis frequenter exercent opera vitae.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30618)\n\nPraeterea, locutio est opus viventis, fit enim per vocem, quae est sonus ab ore animalis prolatus ut dicitur in II de anima. Manifestum est autem ex multis locis Scripturae, Angelos in assumptis corporibus locutos fuisse. Ergo in corporibus assumptis exercent opera vitae.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30619)\n\nPraeterea, comedere est proprium opus animalis, unde dominus post resurrectionem, in argumentum resumptae vitae, cum discipulis manducavit, ut habetur Lucae ultimo. Sed Angeli in assumptis corporibus apparentes comederunt, et Abraham eis cibos obtulit, quos tamen prius adoraverat, ut habetur Gen. XVIII. Ergo Angeli in assumptis corporibus exercent opera vitae.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30620)\n\nPraeterea, generare hominem est actus vitae. Sed hoc competit Angelis in assumptis corporibus dicitur enim Gen. VI, *postquam ingressi sunt filii Dei ad filias hominum, illaeque genuerunt, isti sunt potentes a saeculo viri famosi*. Ergo Angeli exercent opera vitae in corporibus assumptis.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30621)\n\nSed contra, corpora assumpta ab Angelis non vivunt, ut supra dictum est. Ergo nec opera vitae per ea exerceri possunt.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30622)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quaedam opera viventium habent aliquid commune cum aliis operibus, ut locutio, quae est opus viventis, convenit cum aliis sonis inanimatorum, inquantum est sonus; et progressio cum aliis motibus, inquantum est motus. Quantum ergo ad id quod est commune utrisque operibus, possunt opera vitae fieri ab Angelis per corpora assumpta. Non autem quantum ad id quod est proprium viventium, quia secundum philosophum, in libro de Somn. et Vig., cuius est potentia, eius est actio; unde nihil potest habere opus vitae, quod non habet vitam, quae est potentiale principium talis actionis.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30623)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut non est contra veritatem quod in Scriptura intelligibilia sub figuris sensibilibus describuntur, quia hoc non dicitur ad adstruendum quod intelligibilia sint sensibilia, sed per figuras sensibilium proprietates intelligibilium secundum similitudinem quandam dantur intelligi; ita non repugnat veritati sanctorum Angelorum quod corpora ab eis assumpta videntur homines viventes, licet non sint. Non enim assumuntur nisi ut per proprietates hominis et operum hominis. Spirituales proprietates Angelorum et eorum spiritualia opera designentur. Quod non ita congrue fieret, si veros homines assumerent, quia proprietates eorum ducerent in ipsos homines, non in Angelos.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30624)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sentire est totaliter opus vitae, unde nullo modo est dicendum quod Angeli per organa assumptorum corporum sentiant. Nec tamen superflue sunt formata. Non enim ad hoc sunt formata, ut per ea sentiatur, sed ad hoc ut per huiusmodi organa virtutes spirituales Angelorum designentur; sicut per oculum designatur virtus cognitiva Angeli, et per alia membra aliae eius virtutes, ut Dionysius docet, ult. cap. Cael. Hier.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30625)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod motus qui est a motore coniuncto, est proprium opus vitae. Sic autem non moventur corpora assumpta ab eis, quia Angeli non sunt eorum formae. Moventur tamen Angeli per accidens, motis huiusmodi corporibus, cum sint in eis sicut motores in mobilibus, et ita sunt hic quod non alibi, quod de Deo dici non potest. Unde licet Deus non moveatur, motis his in quibus est, quia ubique est; Angeli tamen moventur per accidens ad motum corporum assumptorum. Non autem ad motum corporum caelestium, etiamsi sint in eis sicut motores in mobilibus, quia corpora caelestia non recedunt de loco secundum totum; nec determinatur spiritui moventi orbem locus secundum aliquam determinatam partem substantiae orbis, quae nunc est in oriente, nunc in occidente; sed secundum determinatum situm, quia semper est in oriente virtus movens, ut dicitur in VIII Physic.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30626)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Angeli proprie non loquuntur per corpora assumpta, sed est aliquid simile locutioni, inquantum formant sonos in aere similes vocibus humanis.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30627)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod nec etiam comedere, proprie loquendo, Angelis convenit, quia comestio importat sumptionem cibi convertibilis in substantiam comedentis. Et quamvis in corpus Christi post resurrectionem cibus non converteretur, sed resolveretur in praeiacentem materiam, tamen Christus habebat corpus talis naturae in quod posset cibus converti, unde fuit vera comestio. Sed cibus assumptus ab Angelis neque convertebatur in corpus assumptum, neque corpus illud talis erat naturae in quod posset alimentum converti, unde non fuit vera comestio, sed figurativa spiritualis comestionis. Et hoc est quod Angelus dixit, Tob. XII, *cum essem vobiscum, videbar quidem manducare et bibere, sed ego potu invisibili et cibo utor*. Abraham autem obtulit eis cibos, existimans eos homines esse; in quibus tamen Deum venerabatur, sicut solet Deus esse in prophetis, ut Augustinus dicit, XVI de Civ. Dei.\n\n[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30628)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, XV de Civ. Dei, *multi se expertos, vel ab expertis audisse confirmant, Silvanos et Faunos, quos vulgus incubos vocat, improbos saepe extitisse mulieribus et earum expetisse atque peregisse concubitum. Unde hoc negare impudentiae videtur. Sed Angeli Dei sancti nullo modo sic labi ante diluvium potuerunt. Unde per filios Dei intelliguntur filii Seth, qui boni erant, filias autem hominum nominat Scriptura eas quae natae erant de stirpe Cain. Neque mirandum est quod de eis gigantes nasci potuerunt, neque enim omnes gigantes fuerunt, sed multo plures ante diluvium quam post*. Si tamen ex coitu Daemonum aliqui interdum nascuntur, hoc non est per semen ab eis decisum, aut a corporibus assumptis, sed per semen alicuius hominis ad hoc acceptum, utpote quod idem Daemon qui est succubus ad virum, fiat incubus ad mulierem; sicut et aliarum rerum semina assumunt ad aliquarum rerum generationem, ut Augustinus dicit, III de Trin.; ut sic ille qui nascitur non sit filius Daemonis, sed illius hominis cuius est semen acceptum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0002","text":"[I q. 51 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de Angelis per comparationem ad corporalia. Et primo, de comparatione Angelorum ad corpora; secundo, de comparatione Angelorum ad loca corporalia; tertio, de comparatione Angelorum ad motum localem. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angeli habeant corpora naturaliter sibi unita. Secundo, utrum assumant corpora. Tertio, utrum in corporibus assumptis exerceant opera vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0005","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli habeant corpora naturaliter sibi unita. Dicit enim Origenes, in libro peri archon, *solius Dei, idest patris et filii et spiritus sancti, naturae illud proprium est, ut sine materiali substantia et absque ulla corporeae adiectionis societate, intelligatur existere*. Bernardus etiam dicit, in VI homilia super Cant., *demus Deo soli, sicut immortalitatem sic incorporeitatem, cuius natura sola neque propter se neque propter alium, solatio indiget instrumenti corporei. Liquet autem omnem spiritum creatum corporeo indigere solatio*. Augustinus etiam dicit, super Gen. ad Litt., *Daemones aerea dicuntur animalia, quia corporum aereorum natura vigent*. Eadem autem est natura Daemonis et Angeli. Ergo Angeli habent corpora naturaliter sibi unita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0007","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Gregorius, in homilia Epiphaniae, nominat Angelum rationale animal. Omne autem animal componitur ex corpore et anima. Ergo Angeli habent corpora naturaliter sibi unita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0009","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0010","text":"Praeterea, perfectior est vita in Angelis quam in animabus. Sed anima non solum vivit, sed etiam vivificat corpus. Ergo Angeli vivificant corpora naturaliter sibi unita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0011","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli sicut incorporales intelliguntur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0013","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angeli non habent corpora sibi naturaliter unita. Quod enim accidit alicui naturae, non invenitur universaliter in natura illa, sicut habere alas, quia non est de ratione animalis, non convenit omni animali. Cum autem intelligere non sit actus corporis nec alicuius virtutis corporeae, ut infra patebit, habere corpus unitum non est de ratione substantiae intellectualis inquantum huiusmodi, sed accidit alicui substantiae intellectuali propter aliquid aliud; sicut humanae animae competit uniri corpori, quia est imperfecta et in potentia existens in genere intellectualium substantiarum, non habens in sui natura plenitudinem scientiae, sed acquirens eam per sensus corporeos a sensibilibus rebus, ut infra dicetur. In quocumque autem genere invenitur aliquid imperfectum, oportet praeexistere aliquid perfectum in genere illo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0015","text":"Sunt igitur aliquae substantiae perfectae intellectuales in natura intellectuali, non indigentes acquirere scientiam a sensibilibus rebus. Non igitur omnes substantiae intellectuales sunt unitae corporibus; sed aliquae sunt a corporibus separatae. Et has dicimus Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0016","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, quorundam opinio fuit quod omne ens esset corpus. Et ex hac existimatione derivatum videtur, quod aliqui existimaverunt nullas substantias incorporeas esse nisi corporibus unitas; adeo quod quidam etiam posuerunt Deum esse animam mundi, ut Augustinus narrat in VII de Civ. Dei. Sed quia hoc fidei Catholicae repugnat, quae ponit Deum super omnia exaltatum, secundum illud Psalmi VIII, *elevata est magnificentia tua super caelos*, Origenes, hoc de Deo dicere recusans, de aliis secutus est aliorum opinionem; sicut et in multis aliis deceptus fuit, sequens antiquorum philosophorum opiniones. Verbum autem Bernardi potest exponi, quod spiritus creati indigeant corporali instrumento, non naturaliter unito, sed ad aliquid assumpto, ut infra dicetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0018","text":"Augustinus autem loquitur non asserendo, sed opinione Platonicorum utens, qui ponebant esse quaedam animalia aerea, quae Daemones nominabant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0019","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Gregorius nominat Angelum rationale animal metaphorice, propter similitudinem rationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0021","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod vivificare effective simpliciter perfectionis est. Unde et Deo convenit secundum illud I Reg. II, *dominus mortificat et vivificat*. Sed vivificare formaliter est substantiae quae est pars alicuius naturae, et non habentis in se integram naturam speciei. Unde substantia intellectualis quae non est unita corpori, est perfectior quam ea quae est corpori unita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0024","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non assumant corpora. In opere enim Angeli nihil est superfluum; sicut neque in opere naturae. Sed superfluum esset quod Angeli corpora assumerent, Angelus enim non indiget corpore, cum eius virtus omnem virtutem corporis excedat. Ergo Angelus non assumit corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0026","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0027","text":"Praeterea, omnis assumptio ad aliquam unionem terminatur, quia assumere dicitur quasi ad se sumere. Sed corpus non unitur Angelo ut formae, sicut dictum est. Ex eo autem quod unitur sibi ut motori, non dicitur assumi, alioquin sequeretur quod omnia corpora mota ab Angelis, essent ab eis assumpta. Ergo Angeli non assumunt corpora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0028","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0029","text":"Praeterea, Angeli non assumunt corpora de terra vel aqua, quia non subito disparerent; neque iterum de igne, quia comburerent ea quae contingerent; neque iterum ex aere, quia aer infigurabilis est et incolorabilis. Ergo Angeli corpora non assumunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0030","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XVI de Civ. Dei, quod Angeli in assumptis corporibus Abrahae apparuerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0032","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt Angelos nunquam corpora assumere, sed omnia quae in Scripturis divinis leguntur de apparitionibus Angelorum, contigisse in visione prophetiae, hoc est secundum imaginationem. Sed hoc repugnat intentioni Scripturae. Illud enim quod imaginaria visione videtur, est in sola imaginatione videntis, unde non videtur indifferenter ab omnibus. Scriptura autem divina sic introducit interdum Angelos apparentes, ut communiter ab omnibus viderentur; sicut Angeli apparentes Abrahae, visi sunt ab eo et tota familia eius, et a Loth, et a civibus Sodomorum. Similiter Angelus qui apparuit Tobiae, ab omnibus videbatur. Ex quo manifestum fit huiusmodi contigisse secundum corpoream visionem, qua videtur id quod positum est extra videntem, unde ab omnibus videri potest. Tali autem visione non videtur nisi corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0034","text":"Cum igitur Angeli neque corpora sint, neque habeant corpora naturaliter sibi unita, ut ex dictis patet, relinquitur quod interdum corpora assumant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0035","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Angeli non indigent corpore assumpto propter seipsos, sed propter nos; ut familiariter cum hominibus conversando, demonstrent intelligibilem societatem quam homines expectant cum eis habendam in futura vita. Hoc etiam quod Angeli corpora assumpserunt in veteri testamento, fuit quoddam figurale indicium quod verbum Dei assumpturum esset corpus humanum, omnes enim apparitiones veteris testamenti ad illam apparitionem ordinatae fuerunt, qua filius Dei apparuit in carne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0037","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod corpus assumptum unitur Angelo, non quidem ut formae, neque solum ut motori; sed sicut motori repraesentato per corpus mobile assumptum. Sicut enim in sacra Scriptura proprietates rerum intelligibilium sub similitudinibus rerum sensibilium describuntur, ita corpora sensibilia divina virtute sic formantur ab Angelis, ut congruant ad repraesentandum Angeli intelligibiles proprietates. Et hoc est Angelum assumere corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0039","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet aer, in sua raritate manens, non retineat figuram neque colorem; quando tamen condensatur, et figurari et colorari potest, sicut patet in nubibus. Et sic Angeli assumunt corpora ex aere, condensando ipsum virtute divina, quantum necesse est ad corporis assumendi formationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0042","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli in corporibus assumptis opera vitae exerceant. Angelos enim veritatis non decet aliqua fictio. Esset autem fictio, si corpus ab eis assumptum, quod vivum videtur et opera vitae habens, non haberet huiusmodi. Ergo Angeli in assumpto corpore opera vitae exercent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0044","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0045","text":"Praeterea, in operibus Angeli non sunt aliqua frustra. Frustra autem in corpore assumpto per Angelum formarentur oculi et nares et alia sensuum instrumenta, nisi per ea Angelus sentiret. Ergo Angelus sentit per corpus assumptum. Quod est propriissimum opus vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0046","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0047","text":"Praeterea, moveri motu processivo est unum de operibus vitae, ut patet in II de anima. Manifeste autem Angeli apparent in assumptis corporibus moveri. Dicitur enim Gen. XVIII, quod Abraham simul gradiebatur, deducens Angelos qui ei apparuerant. Et Angelus Tobiae quaerenti, nosti viam quae ducit in civitatem Medorum? Respondit, *novi, et omnia itinera eius frequenter ambulavi*. Ergo Angeli in corporibus assumptis frequenter exercent opera vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0048","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0049","text":"Praeterea, locutio est opus viventis, fit enim per vocem, quae est sonus ab ore animalis prolatus ut dicitur in II de anima. Manifestum est autem ex multis locis Scripturae, Angelos in assumptis corporibus locutos fuisse. Ergo in corporibus assumptis exercent opera vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0050","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0051","text":"Praeterea, comedere est proprium opus animalis, unde dominus post resurrectionem, in argumentum resumptae vitae, cum discipulis manducavit, ut habetur Lucae ultimo. Sed Angeli in assumptis corporibus apparentes comederunt, et Abraham eis cibos obtulit, quos tamen prius adoraverat, ut habetur Gen. XVIII. Ergo Angeli in assumptis corporibus exercent opera vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0052","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0053","text":"Praeterea, generare hominem est actus vitae. Sed hoc competit Angelis in assumptis corporibus dicitur enim Gen. VI, *postquam ingressi sunt filii Dei ad filias hominum, illaeque genuerunt, isti sunt potentes a saeculo viri famosi*. Ergo Angeli exercent opera vitae in corporibus assumptis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0054","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0055","text":"Sed contra, corpora assumpta ab Angelis non vivunt, ut supra dictum est. Ergo nec opera vitae per ea exerceri possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0056","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0057","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quaedam opera viventium habent aliquid commune cum aliis operibus, ut locutio, quae est opus viventis, convenit cum aliis sonis inanimatorum, inquantum est sonus; et progressio cum aliis motibus, inquantum est motus. Quantum ergo ad id quod est commune utrisque operibus, possunt opera vitae fieri ab Angelis per corpora assumpta. Non autem quantum ad id quod est proprium viventium, quia secundum philosophum, in libro de Somn. et Vig., cuius est potentia, eius est actio; unde nihil potest habere opus vitae, quod non habet vitam, quae est potentiale principium talis actionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0058","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0059","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut non est contra veritatem quod in Scriptura intelligibilia sub figuris sensibilibus describuntur, quia hoc non dicitur ad adstruendum quod intelligibilia sint sensibilia, sed per figuras sensibilium proprietates intelligibilium secundum similitudinem quandam dantur intelligi; ita non repugnat veritati sanctorum Angelorum quod corpora ab eis assumpta videntur homines viventes, licet non sint. Non enim assumuntur nisi ut per proprietates hominis et operum hominis. Spirituales proprietates Angelorum et eorum spiritualia opera designentur. Quod non ita congrue fieret, si veros homines assumerent, quia proprietates eorum ducerent in ipsos homines, non in Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0060","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0061","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sentire est totaliter opus vitae, unde nullo modo est dicendum quod Angeli per organa assumptorum corporum sentiant. Nec tamen superflue sunt formata. Non enim ad hoc sunt formata, ut per ea sentiatur, sed ad hoc ut per huiusmodi organa virtutes spirituales Angelorum designentur; sicut per oculum designatur virtus cognitiva Angeli, et per alia membra aliae eius virtutes, ut Dionysius docet, ult. cap. Cael. Hier.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0062","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0063","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod motus qui est a motore coniuncto, est proprium opus vitae. Sic autem non moventur corpora assumpta ab eis, quia Angeli non sunt eorum formae. Moventur tamen Angeli per accidens, motis huiusmodi corporibus, cum sint in eis sicut motores in mobilibus, et ita sunt hic quod non alibi, quod de Deo dici non potest. Unde licet Deus non moveatur, motis his in quibus est, quia ubique est; Angeli tamen moventur per accidens ad motum corporum assumptorum. Non autem ad motum corporum caelestium, etiamsi sint in eis sicut motores in mobilibus, quia corpora caelestia non recedunt de loco secundum totum; nec determinatur spiritui moventi orbem locus secundum aliquam determinatam partem substantiae orbis, quae nunc est in oriente, nunc in occidente; sed secundum determinatum situm, quia semper est in oriente virtus movens, ut dicitur in VIII Physic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0064","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0065","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Angeli proprie non loquuntur per corpora assumpta, sed est aliquid simile locutioni, inquantum formant sonos in aere similes vocibus humanis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0066","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0067","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod nec etiam comedere, proprie loquendo, Angelis convenit, quia comestio importat sumptionem cibi convertibilis in substantiam comedentis. Et quamvis in corpus Christi post resurrectionem cibus non converteretur, sed resolveretur in praeiacentem materiam, tamen Christus habebat corpus talis naturae in quod posset cibus converti, unde fuit vera comestio. Sed cibus assumptus ab Angelis neque convertebatur in corpus assumptum, neque corpus illud talis erat naturae in quod posset alimentum converti, unde non fuit vera comestio, sed figurativa spiritualis comestionis. Et hoc est quod Angelus dixit, Tob. XII, *cum essem vobiscum, videbar quidem manducare et bibere, sed ego potu invisibili et cibo utor*. Abraham autem obtulit eis cibos, existimans eos homines esse; in quibus tamen Deum venerabatur, sicut solet Deus esse in prophetis, ut Augustinus dicit, XVI de Civ. Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0068","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0069","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, XV de Civ. Dei, *multi se expertos, vel ab expertis audisse confirmant, Silvanos et Faunos, quos vulgus incubos vocat, improbos saepe extitisse mulieribus et earum expetisse atque peregisse concubitum. Unde hoc negare impudentiae videtur. Sed Angeli Dei sancti nullo modo sic labi ante diluvium potuerunt. Unde per filios Dei intelliguntur filii Seth, qui boni erant, filias autem hominum nominat Scriptura eas quae natae erant de stirpe Cain. Neque mirandum est quod de eis gigantes nasci potuerunt, neque enim omnes gigantes fuerunt, sed multo plures ante diluvium quam post*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0070","text":"Si tamen ex coitu Daemonum aliqui interdum nascuntur, hoc non est per semen ab eis decisum, aut a corporibus assumptis, sed per semen alicuius hominis ad hoc acceptum, utpote quod idem Daemon qui est succubus ad virum, fiat incubus ad mulierem; sicut et aliarum rerum semina assumunt ad aliquarum rerum generationem, ut Augustinus dicit, III de Trin.; ut sic ille qui nascitur non sit filius Daemonis, sed illius hominis cuius est semen acceptum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6cda71694d097ecbe3eb0c256b0b960bda5b07dd10f50a16b9597a6156464a3f","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143646321458Z_11079a98","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 51","source_sha256":"6cda71694d097ecbe3eb0c256b0b960bda5b07dd10f50a16b9597a6156464a3f","sha256":"ad60572b4c7270932f712058fe43b4182067424b4e52ed57e538eb801c902cf2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:36:46.321472+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0002","text":"[I q. 51 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0003","text":"Next, the Angels are considered in comparison with bodily things. First, concerning the comparison of Angels with bodies; second, concerning the comparison of Angels with bodily places; third, concerning the comparison of Angels with local motion. Concerning the first, three questions are asked. First, whether Angels have bodies naturally united to them. Second, whether they assume bodies. Third, whether, in assumed bodies, they perform the operations of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0005","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0006","text":"The first point is approached thus. It seems that Angels have bodies naturally united to them. For Origen says, in the book *Peri Archon*, “It is proper to the nature of God alone, that is, of the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, to be understood to exist without material substance and without any association of bodily addition.” Bernard likewise says, in his sixth homily on the Song of Songs, “Let us grant to God alone, just as immortality, so incorporeality, whose nature alone needs no assistance of a bodily instrument, either for its own sake or for another's sake. But it is clear that every created spirit needs bodily assistance.” Augustine likewise says, On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, “Demons are called aerial animals because they flourish by the nature of aerial bodies.” But the nature of demon and Angel is the same. Therefore Angels have bodies naturally united to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0007","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0008","text":"Moreover, Gregory, in a homily for the Epiphany, calls an Angel a rational animal. But every animal is composed of body and soul. Therefore Angels have bodies naturally united to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0009","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0010","text":"Moreover, life in Angels is more perfect than in souls. But a soul not only lives, but also gives life to a body. Therefore Angels give life to bodies naturally united to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0011","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that “Angels are understood as incorporeal.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0013","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0014","text":"I answer that Angels do not have bodies naturally united to them. For what belongs accidentally to some nature is not found universally in that nature; thus, having wings, since it is not of the essence of an animal, does not belong to every animal. Since, moreover, understanding is not an act of a body nor of any bodily power, as will appear below, having a united body is not of the essence of an intellectual substance as such, but belongs accidentally to an intellectual substance on account of something else; just as it belongs to the human soul to be united to a body because it is imperfect and exists in potency in the genus of intellectual substances, not having in its nature the fullness of knowledge, but acquiring it through bodily senses from sensible things, as will be said below. But wherever something imperfect is found in a genus, something perfect must pre-exist in that genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0015","text":"Therefore, there are certain intellectual substances perfect in intellectual nature, not needing to acquire knowledge from sensible things. Therefore not all intellectual substances are united to bodies; but some are separated from bodies. And these we call Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0016","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as was said above, some held the opinion that every being was a body. And from this estimation it seems to have been derived that some thought there were no incorporeal substances except those united to bodies; so much so that some even posited God to be the soul of the world, as Augustine relates in VII of *The City of God*. But because this is opposed to the Catholic faith, which posits God as exalted above all things, according to that passage of Psalm VIII, “thy magnificence is raised above the heavens,” Origen, refusing to say this of God, followed the opinion of others concerning other things; just as in many other matters he was deceived, following the opinions of the ancient philosophers. Bernard's words, however, can be explained as meaning that created spirits need a bodily instrument, not naturally united to them, but assumed for some purpose, as will be said below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0018","text":"Augustine, however, speaks not by asserting, but by employing the opinion of the Platonists, who posited certain aerial animals, which they called demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0019","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that Gregory calls an Angel a rational animal metaphorically, on account of the likeness of reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0021","text":"[I q. 51 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that to give life effectively is simply a perfection. Hence it belongs also to God, according to that passage of I Kings II, “the Lord kills and gives life.” But to give life formally belongs to a substance that is part of some nature and does not have within itself the complete nature of the species. Hence an intellectual substance not united to a body is more perfect than one united to a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0024","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that angels do not assume bodies. For in the work of an angel there is nothing superfluous, just as neither is there in the work of nature. But it would be superfluous for angels to assume bodies; for an angel does not need a body, since its power exceeds every power of a body. Therefore an angel does not assume a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0026","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0027","text":"Moreover, every assumption terminates in some union, because to assume is said as if to take to oneself. But a body is not united to an angel as to a form, as has been said. Yet from the fact that it is united to it as to a mover, it is not said to be assumed; otherwise it would follow that all bodies moved by angels were assumed by them. Therefore angels do not assume bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0028","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0029","text":"Moreover, angels do not assume bodies from earth or water, because they would not suddenly disappear; nor again from fire, because they would burn the things they touched; nor again from air, because air is incapable of being formed and colored. Therefore angels do not assume bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0030","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0031","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in Book XVI of the City of God, that angels appeared to Abraham in assumed bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0032","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0033","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have said that angels never assume bodies, but that everything read in the divine Scriptures concerning the appearances of angels occurred in a prophetic vision, that is, according to the imagination. But this is contrary to the intention of Scripture. For that which is seen in an imaginary vision is in the imagination of the one seeing alone; hence it is not seen indiscriminately by all. But divine Scripture sometimes introduces angels as appearing in such a way that they were seen commonly by all; as the angels appearing to Abraham were seen by him and his entire household, and by Lot, and by the citizens of Sodom. Likewise, the angel who appeared to Tobias was seen by all. From this it is clear that such things occurred according to bodily vision, by which that which is placed outside the one seeing is seen, and hence can be seen by all. But by such vision nothing but a body is seen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0034","text":"Therefore, since angels are neither bodies nor have bodies naturally united to them, as is clear from what has been said, it remains that they sometimes assume bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0035","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that angels do not need an assumed body for their own sake, but for our sake: so that, by conversing familiarly with human beings, they may show the intelligible society which human beings await to have with them in the future life. This also, that angels assumed bodies in the Old Testament, was a certain figurative indication that the Word of God would assume a human body; for all the appearances of the Old Testament were ordered to that appearance in which the Son of God appeared in the flesh.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0037","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0038","text":"To the second, it must be said that an assumed body is united to an angel, not indeed as to a form, nor merely as to a mover, but as to a mover represented through the assumed mobile body. For just as in Sacred Scripture the properties of intelligible things are described under likenesses of sensible things, so sensible bodies are formed by angels through divine power in such a way that they are fitting for representing the intelligible properties of an angel. And this is for an angel to assume a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0039","text":"[I q. 51 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although air, while remaining in its rarity, does not retain shape or color, nevertheless, when it is condensed, it can both be shaped and colored, as is clear in clouds. And thus angels assume bodies from air, condensing it by divine power as much as is necessary for the formation of the body to be assumed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0042","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that angels exercise works of life in assumed bodies. For no falsehood befits angels of truth. But it would be a falsehood if a body assumed by them, which seems to be living and to have works of life, did not have such things. Therefore angels exercise works of life in an assumed body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0044","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0045","text":"Moreover, there is nothing futile in the works of an angel. But eyes and nostrils and the other instruments of the senses would be formed futilely in a body assumed by an angel unless the angel sensed through them. Therefore an angel senses through an assumed body. This is a most proper work of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0046","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0047","text":"Moreover, to be moved with progressive motion is one of the works of life, as is clear in Book II of On the Soul. But angels plainly appear to be moved in assumed bodies. For it is said in Genesis XVIII that Abraham walked along with them, conducting the angels who had appeared to him. And to Tobias, asking, “Do you know the road that leads to the city of the Medes?” the angel answered, *“I know it, and have frequently travelled all its routes.”* Therefore angels frequently exercise works of life in assumed bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0048","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0049","text":"Moreover, speech is an operation of a living being; for it is produced through a voice, which is a sound uttered from the mouth of an animal, as is said in Book II of *On the Soul*. But it is evident from many places in Scripture that Angels spoke in assumed bodies. Therefore, in assumed bodies they exercise operations of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0050","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0051","text":"Moreover, eating is a proper operation of an animal; hence the Lord, after the resurrection, ate with the disciples as an argument for the life resumed, as is related in the last chapter of Luke. But Angels appearing in assumed bodies ate, and Abraham offered them food, though he had first adored them, as is related in Genesis XVIII. Therefore Angels, in assumed bodies, exercise operations of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0052","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0053","text":"Moreover, to generate a human being is an act of life. But this is said to belong to Angels in assumed bodies, for Genesis VI says, *after the sons of God went in to the daughters of men, and they bore children, these are the mighty men of old, men of renown*. Therefore Angels exercise operations of life in assumed bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0054","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0055","text":"On the contrary, bodies assumed by Angels do not live, as was said above. Therefore neither can operations of life be exercised through them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0056","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0057","text":"I answer that it must be said that certain operations of living beings have something in common with other operations: thus speech, which is an operation of a living being, agrees with other sounds of inanimate things insofar as it is a sound; and locomotion agrees with other motions insofar as it is motion. Therefore, with respect to what is common to both kinds of operations, operations of life can be performed by Angels through assumed bodies. But not with respect to what is proper to living beings, because, according to the Philosopher, in the book *On Sleep and Waking*, the action belongs to that to which the power belongs. Hence nothing can have an operation of life which does not have life, which is the potential principle of such an action.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0058","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0059","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, just as it is not contrary to truth that intelligible things are described in Scripture under sensible figures—for this is not said in order to establish that intelligible things are sensible, but so that, through figures of sensible things, the properties of intelligible things may be understood by a certain likeness—so neither is it repugnant to the truth concerning the holy Angels that the bodies assumed by them appear to be living human beings, although they are not. For they are assumed only so that, through the properties of man and of human operations, the spiritual properties of Angels and their spiritual operations may be signified. This would not be done so fittingly if they assumed true human beings, because their properties would lead to the human beings themselves, not to the Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0060","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0061","text":"To the second, it must be said that sensing is wholly an operation of life; hence it must in no way be said that Angels sense through the organs of assumed bodies. Yet these are not fashioned superfluously. For they are not fashioned in order that sensation may occur through them, but so that through such organs the spiritual powers of Angels may be signified: just as through the eye the cognitive power of an Angel is signified, and through the other members his other powers, as Dionysius teaches in the last chapter of *The Celestial Hierarchy*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0062","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0063","text":"To the third, it must be said that motion which is from a conjoined mover is a proper operation of life. But assumed bodies are not moved by them in this way, because Angels are not their forms. Nevertheless, Angels are moved accidentally when such bodies are moved, since they are in them as movers in movable things; and thus they are here and not elsewhere, which cannot be said of God. Hence, although God is not moved when those things in which He is are moved, because He is everywhere, Angels nevertheless are moved accidentally according to the motion of assumed bodies. But they are not so moved by the motion of heavenly bodies, even if they are in them as movers in movable things, because heavenly bodies do not depart from place as a whole; nor is a place determined for the spirit moving an orb according to some determined part of the substance of the orb, which is now in the east and now in the west, but according to a determined position, because the moving power is always in the east, as is said in Book VIII of the *Physics*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0064","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0065","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Angels do not properly speak through assumed bodies, but there is something like speech insofar as they form sounds in the air resembling human voices.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0066","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0067","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, properly speaking, eating does not belong even to Angels, because eating implies the taking of food capable of being converted into the substance of the eater. And although in Christ's body after the resurrection food was not converted, but was dissolved into pre-existing matter, nevertheless Christ had a body of such a nature that food could be converted into it; hence it was true eating. But the food taken by Angels was neither converted into the assumed body, nor was that body of such a nature that nourishment could be converted into it; hence it was not true eating, but a figurative eating signifying spiritual eating. And this is what the Angel said in Tobias XII: *when I was with you, I seemed indeed to eat and drink, but I use invisible drink and food*. But Abraham offered them food, believing them to be human beings; in them, however, he venerated God, as God is accustomed to be in prophets, as Augustine says in Book XVI of *The City of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0068","text":"[I q. 51 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0069","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that, as Augustine says in Book XV of *The City of God*, *many affirm that they have experienced themselves, or have heard from those who experienced it, that Silvans and Fauns, whom the common people call incubi, have often proved wicked toward women and have sought and accomplished intercourse with them. Hence to deny this seems impudent. But the holy Angels of God could in no way have so fallen before the flood. Therefore by the sons of God are understood the sons of Seth, who were good; but Scripture calls daughters of men those who were born from the stock of Cain. Nor is it surprising that giants could be born from them; for not all were giants, but there were many more before the flood than after*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0070","text":"Yet if some are at times born from intercourse with demons, this is not through seed emitted by them, or by their assumed bodies, but through the seed of some man taken for this purpose: namely, so that the same demon who is a succubus to a man may become an incubus to a woman. Thus they also take seeds of other things for the generation of certain things, as Augustine says in Book III of *On the Trinity*; so that the one who is born may not be the son of a demon, but of that man whose seed was taken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_43:P0070"]}],"source_digest":"67f0417aa9300f9277898c5b1328a75d3b83c6da9602a9ca3a990e535f795fcf","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 51"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 51","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44","title":"First Part · Question 52","order":53,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_44","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 52 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30629)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de loco Angeli. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angelus sit in loco. Secundo, utrum possit esse in pluribus locis simul. Tertio, utrum plures Angeli possint esse in eodem loco.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30630)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non sit in loco. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Hebd., *communis animi conceptio apud sapientes est, incorporalia in loco non esse*. Et Aristoteles, in IV Physic., dicit quod *non omne quod est, est in loco, sed mobile corpus*. Sed Angelus non est corpus, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo Angelus non est in loco.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30631)\n\nPraeterea, locus est quantitas positionem habens. Omne ergo quod est in loco, habet aliquem situm. Sed habere situm non potest convenire Angelo, cum substantia sua sit immunis a quantitate, cuius propria differentia est positionem habere. Ergo Angelus non est in loco.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30632)\n\nPraeterea, esse in loco est mensurari loco et contineri a loco, ut patet per philosophum in IV Physic. Sed Angelus non potest mensurari neque contineri a loco, quia continens est formalius contento, sicut aer aqua, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Ergo Angelus non est in loco.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30633)\n\nSed contra est quod in collecta dicitur, *Angeli tui sancti, habitantes in ea, nos in pace custodiant*.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30634)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angelo convenit esse in loco, aequivoce tamen dicitur Angelus esse in loco, et corpus. Corpus enim est in loco per hoc, quod applicatur loco secundum contactum dimensivae quantitatis. Quae quidem in Angelis non est; sed est in eis quantitas virtualis. Per applicationem igitur virtutis angelicae ad aliquem locum qualitercumque, dicitur Angelus esse in loco corporeo. Et secundum hoc patet quod non oportet dicere quod Angelus commensuretur loco; vel quod habeat situm in continuo. Haec enim conveniunt corpori locato, prout est quantum quantitate dimensiva. Similiter etiam non oportet propter hoc, quod contineatur a loco. Nam substantia incorporea sua virtute contingens rem corpoream, continet ipsam, et non continetur ab ea, anima enim est in corpore ut continens, et non ut contenta. Et similiter Angelus dicitur esse in loco corporeo, non ut contentum, sed ut continens aliquo modo.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 1 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30635)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30636)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus possit esse in pluribus locis simul. Angelus enim non est minoris virtutis quam anima. Sed anima est simul in pluribus locis, quia est tota in qualibet parte corporis, ut Augustinus dicit. Ergo Angelus potest esse in pluribus locis simul.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30637)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus est in corpore assumpto; et cum assumat corpus continuum, videtur quod sit in qualibet eius parte. Sed secundum partes eius considerantur diversa loca. Ergo Angelus est simul in pluribus locis.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30638)\n\nPraeterea, Damascenus dicit quod ubi Angelus operatur, ibi est. Sed aliquando operatur simul in pluribus locis, ut patet de Angelo subvertente Sodomam, Gen. XIX. Ergo Angelus potest esse in pluribus locis simul.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30639)\n\nSed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, quod *Angeli, dum sunt in caelo, non sunt in terra*.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30640)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angelus est virtutis et essentiae finitae. Divina autem virtus et essentia infinita est, et est universalis causa omnium, et ideo sua virtute omnia contingit, et non solum in pluribus locis est, sed ubique. Virtus autem Angeli, quia finita est, non se extendit ad omnia, sed ad aliquid unum determinatum. Oportet enim quidquid comparatur ad unam virtutem, ut unum aliquid comparari ad ipsam. Sicut igitur universum ens comparatur ut unum aliquid ad universalem Dei virtutem, ita et aliquod particulare ens comparatur ut aliquid unum ad Angeli virtutem. Unde cum Angelus sit in loco per applicationem virtutis suae ad locum, sequitur quod non sit ubique, nec in pluribus locis, sed in uno loco tantum. Circa hoc tamen aliqui decepti sunt. Quidam enim, imaginationem transcendere non valentes, cogitaverunt indivisibilitatem Angeli ad modum indivisibilitatis puncti, et inde crediderunt quod Angelus non posset esse nisi in loco punctali. Sed manifeste decepti sunt. Nam punctum est indivisibile habens situm, sed Angelus est indivisibile extra genus quantitatis et situs existens. Unde non est necesse quod determinetur ei unus locus indivisibilis secundum situm; sed vel divisibilis vel indivisibilis, vel maior vel minor, secundum quod voluntarie applicat suam virtutem ad corpus maius vel minus. Et sic totum corpus cui per suam virtutem applicatur, correspondet ei ut unus locus. Nec tamen oportet quod si aliquis Angelus movet caelum, quod sit ubique. Primo quidem, quia non applicatur virtus eius nisi ad id quod primo ab ipso movetur, una autem pars caeli est in qua primo est motus, scilicet pars orientis, unde etiam philosophus, in VIII Physic., virtutem motoris caelorum attribuit parti orientis. Secundo, quia non ponitur a philosophis quod una substantia separata moveat omnes orbes immediate. Unde non oportet quod sit ubique. Sic igitur patet quod diversimode esse in loco convenit corpori, et Angelo, et Deo. Nam corpus est in loco circumscriptive, quia commensuratur loco. Angelus autem non circumscriptive, cum non commensuretur loco, sed definitive, quia ita est in uno loco, quod non in alio. Deus autem neque circumscriptive neque definitive, quia est ubique.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30641)\n\nEt per hoc patet de facili responsio ad obiecta, quia totum illud cui immediate applicatur virtus Angeli, reputatur ut unus locus eius, licet sit continuum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30642)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod plures Angeli possint simul esse in eodem loco. Plura enim corpora non possunt esse simul in eodem loco, quia replent locum. Sed Angeli non replent locum, quia solum corpus replet locum, ut non sit vacuum, ut patet per philosophum, in IV Physic. Ergo plures Angeli possunt esse in uno loco.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30643)\n\nPraeterea, plus differt Angelus et corpus quam duo Angeli. Sed Angelus et corpus sunt simul in eodem loco, quia nullus locus est qui non sit plenus sensibili corpore, ut probatur in IV Physic. Ergo multo magis duo Angeli possunt esse in eodem loco.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30644)\n\nPraeterea, anima est in qualibet parte corporis, secundum Augustinum. Sed Daemones, licet non illabantur mentibus, illabuntur tamen interdum corporibus, et sic anima et Daemon sunt simul in eodem loco. Ergo, eadem ratione, quaecumque aliae spirituales substantiae.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30645)\n\nSed contra, duae animae non sunt in eodem corpore. Ergo, pari ratione, neque duo Angeli in eodem loco.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30646)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod duo Angeli non sunt simul in eodem loco. Et ratio huius est, quia impossibile est quod duae causae completae sint immediatae unius et eiusdem rei. Quod patet in omni genere causarum, una enim est forma proxima unius rei, et unum est proximum movens, licet possint esse plures motores remoti. Nec habet instantiam de pluribus trahentibus navem, quia nullus eorum est perfectus motor, cum virtus uniuscuiusque sit insufficiens ad movendum; sed omnes simul sunt in loco unius motoris, inquantum omnes virtutes eorum aggregantur ad unum motum faciendum. Unde cum Angelus dicatur esse in loco per hoc quod virtus eius immediate contingit locum per modum continentis perfecti, ut dictum est, non potest esse nisi unus Angelus in uno loco.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30647)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod plures Angelos esse in uno loco non impeditur propter impletionem loci, sed propter aliam causam, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30648)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angelus et corpus non eodem modo sunt in loco, unde ratio non sequitur.\n\n[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30649)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nec etiam Daemon et anima comparantur ad corpus secundum eandem habitudinem causae; cum anima sit forma, non autem Daemon. Unde ratio non sequitur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0002","text":"[I q. 52 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de loco Angeli. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angelus sit in loco. Secundo, utrum possit esse in pluribus locis simul. Tertio, utrum plures Angeli possint esse in eodem loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0005","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non sit in loco. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Hebd., *communis animi conceptio apud sapientes est, incorporalia in loco non esse*. Et Aristoteles, in IV Physic., dicit quod *non omne quod est, est in loco, sed mobile corpus*. Sed Angelus non est corpus, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo Angelus non est in loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0007","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0008","text":"Praeterea, locus est quantitas positionem habens. Omne ergo quod est in loco, habet aliquem situm. Sed habere situm non potest convenire Angelo, cum substantia sua sit immunis a quantitate, cuius propria differentia est positionem habere. Ergo Angelus non est in loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0009","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0010","text":"Praeterea, esse in loco est mensurari loco et contineri a loco, ut patet per philosophum in IV Physic. Sed Angelus non potest mensurari neque contineri a loco, quia continens est formalius contento, sicut aer aqua, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Ergo Angelus non est in loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0011","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod in collecta dicitur, *Angeli tui sancti, habitantes in ea, nos in pace custodiant*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0013","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angelo convenit esse in loco, aequivoce tamen dicitur Angelus esse in loco, et corpus. Corpus enim est in loco per hoc, quod applicatur loco secundum contactum dimensivae quantitatis. Quae quidem in Angelis non est; sed est in eis quantitas virtualis. Per applicationem igitur virtutis angelicae ad aliquem locum qualitercumque, dicitur Angelus esse in loco corporeo. Et secundum hoc patet quod non oportet dicere quod Angelus commensuretur loco; vel quod habeat situm in continuo. Haec enim conveniunt corpori locato, prout est quantum quantitate dimensiva. Similiter etiam non oportet propter hoc, quod contineatur a loco. Nam substantia incorporea sua virtute contingens rem corpoream, continet ipsam, et non continetur ab ea, anima enim est in corpore ut continens, et non ut contenta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0015","text":"Et similiter Angelus dicitur esse in loco corporeo, non ut contentum, sed ut continens aliquo modo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0016","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0017","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0018","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0019","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0020","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus possit esse in pluribus locis simul. Angelus enim non est minoris virtutis quam anima. Sed anima est simul in pluribus locis, quia est tota in qualibet parte corporis, ut Augustinus dicit. Ergo Angelus potest esse in pluribus locis simul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0021","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0022","text":"Praeterea, Angelus est in corpore assumpto; et cum assumat corpus continuum, videtur quod sit in qualibet eius parte. Sed secundum partes eius considerantur diversa loca. Ergo Angelus est simul in pluribus locis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0023","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0024","text":"Praeterea, Damascenus dicit quod ubi Angelus operatur, ibi est. Sed aliquando operatur simul in pluribus locis, ut patet de Angelo subvertente Sodomam, Gen. XIX. Ergo Angelus potest esse in pluribus locis simul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0025","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0026","text":"Sed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, quod *Angeli, dum sunt in caelo, non sunt in terra*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0027","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0028","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angelus est virtutis et essentiae finitae. Divina autem virtus et essentia infinita est, et est universalis causa omnium, et ideo sua virtute omnia contingit, et non solum in pluribus locis est, sed ubique. Virtus autem Angeli, quia finita est, non se extendit ad omnia, sed ad aliquid unum determinatum. Oportet enim quidquid comparatur ad unam virtutem, ut unum aliquid comparari ad ipsam. Sicut igitur universum ens comparatur ut unum aliquid ad universalem Dei virtutem, ita et aliquod particulare ens comparatur ut aliquid unum ad Angeli virtutem. Unde cum Angelus sit in loco per applicationem virtutis suae ad locum, sequitur quod non sit ubique, nec in pluribus locis, sed in uno loco tantum. Circa hoc tamen aliqui decepti sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0029","text":"Quidam enim, imaginationem transcendere non valentes, cogitaverunt indivisibilitatem Angeli ad modum indivisibilitatis puncti, et inde crediderunt quod Angelus non posset esse nisi in loco punctali. Sed manifeste decepti sunt. Nam punctum est indivisibile habens situm, sed Angelus est indivisibile extra genus quantitatis et situs existens. Unde non est necesse quod determinetur ei unus locus indivisibilis secundum situm; sed vel divisibilis vel indivisibilis, vel maior vel minor, secundum quod voluntarie applicat suam virtutem ad corpus maius vel minus. Et sic totum corpus cui per suam virtutem applicatur, correspondet ei ut unus locus. Nec tamen oportet quod si aliquis Angelus movet caelum, quod sit ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0030","text":"Primo quidem, quia non applicatur virtus eius nisi ad id quod primo ab ipso movetur, una autem pars caeli est in qua primo est motus, scilicet pars orientis, unde etiam philosophus, in VIII Physic., virtutem motoris caelorum attribuit parti orientis. Secundo, quia non ponitur a philosophis quod una substantia separata moveat omnes orbes immediate. Unde non oportet quod sit ubique. Sic igitur patet quod diversimode esse in loco convenit corpori, et Angelo, et Deo. Nam corpus est in loco circumscriptive, quia commensuratur loco. Angelus autem non circumscriptive, cum non commensuretur loco, sed definitive, quia ita est in uno loco, quod non in alio. Deus autem neque circumscriptive neque definitive, quia est ubique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0031","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0032","text":"Et per hoc patet de facili responsio ad obiecta, quia totum illud cui immediate applicatur virtus Angeli, reputatur ut unus locus eius, licet sit continuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0033","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0034","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0035","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod plures Angeli possint simul esse in eodem loco. Plura enim corpora non possunt esse simul in eodem loco, quia replent locum. Sed Angeli non replent locum, quia solum corpus replet locum, ut non sit vacuum, ut patet per philosophum, in IV Physic. Ergo plures Angeli possunt esse in uno loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0036","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0037","text":"Praeterea, plus differt Angelus et corpus quam duo Angeli. Sed Angelus et corpus sunt simul in eodem loco, quia nullus locus est qui non sit plenus sensibili corpore, ut probatur in IV Physic. Ergo multo magis duo Angeli possunt esse in eodem loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0038","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0039","text":"Praeterea, anima est in qualibet parte corporis, secundum Augustinum. Sed Daemones, licet non illabantur mentibus, illabuntur tamen interdum corporibus, et sic anima et Daemon sunt simul in eodem loco. Ergo, eadem ratione, quaecumque aliae spirituales substantiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0040","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0041","text":"Sed contra, duae animae non sunt in eodem corpore. Ergo, pari ratione, neque duo Angeli in eodem loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0042","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0043","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod duo Angeli non sunt simul in eodem loco. Et ratio huius est, quia impossibile est quod duae causae completae sint immediatae unius et eiusdem rei. Quod patet in omni genere causarum, una enim est forma proxima unius rei, et unum est proximum movens, licet possint esse plures motores remoti. Nec habet instantiam de pluribus trahentibus navem, quia nullus eorum est perfectus motor, cum virtus uniuscuiusque sit insufficiens ad movendum; sed omnes simul sunt in loco unius motoris, inquantum omnes virtutes eorum aggregantur ad unum motum faciendum. Unde cum Angelus dicatur esse in loco per hoc quod virtus eius immediate contingit locum per modum continentis perfecti, ut dictum est, non potest esse nisi unus Angelus in uno loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0044","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0045","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod plures Angelos esse in uno loco non impeditur propter impletionem loci, sed propter aliam causam, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0046","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0047","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angelus et corpus non eodem modo sunt in loco, unde ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0048","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0049","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nec etiam Daemon et anima comparantur ad corpus secundum eandem habitudinem causae; cum anima sit forma, non autem Daemon. Unde ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"370c67dbd30706ad4d016801f90e4e3e7490bcbe9d0c6853b7e0e5e3bdcd4e01","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143716722356Z_5594a8ad","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 52","source_sha256":"370c67dbd30706ad4d016801f90e4e3e7490bcbe9d0c6853b7e0e5e3bdcd4e01","sha256":"78adc8742edc0e6a9adbae1bc2a9398cd64f8fd10da4093bff929ef206037588","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:37:16.722396+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0002","text":"[I q. 52 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0003","text":"Next, there is inquiry concerning the place of an angel. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, whether an angel is in a place. Second, whether it can be in several places at once. Third, whether several angels can be in the same place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0005","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that an angel is not in a place. For Boethius says, in the book *On the Hebdomads*, “among the wise, it is a common conception of the mind that incorporeal things are not in a place.” And Aristotle, in *Physics* IV, says that “not everything that is is in a place, but a mobile body.” But an angel is not a body, as was shown above. Therefore, an angel is not in a place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0007","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0008","text":"Further, place is a quantity having position. Therefore, everything that is in a place has some position. But to have position cannot belong to an angel, since its substance is exempt from quantity, of which having position is the proper difference. Therefore, an angel is not in a place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0009","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0010","text":"Further, to be in a place is to be measured by place and contained by place, as is clear from the Philosopher in *Physics* IV. But an angel cannot be measured or contained by place, because that which contains is more formal than that which is contained, as air is than water, as is said in *Physics* IV. Therefore, an angel is not in a place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0011","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0012","text":"But to the contrary, it is said in the collect, “May thy holy angels, dwelling therein, keep us in peace.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0013","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that it belongs to an angel to be in a place; yet an angel and a body are said equivocally to be in a place. For a body is in a place through being applied to the place by contact of dimensive quantity. Such quantity indeed is not in angels; but virtual quantity is in them. Therefore, through the application of angelic power to some place in whatever manner, an angel is said to be in a corporeal place. And accordingly it is clear that one need not say that an angel is commensurated with place, or that it has position in a continuum. For these belong to a body situated in a place, insofar as it is quantified by dimensive quantity. Likewise, neither need one say, on this account, that it is contained by place. For an incorporeal substance, by its power touching a corporeal thing, contains that thing and is not contained by it; for the soul is in the body as containing, and not as contained.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0015","text":"And likewise an angel is said to be in a corporeal place, not as something contained, but as in some manner containing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0016","text":"[I q. 52 a. 1 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0017","text":"And through this, the reply to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0018","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0019","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0020","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that an angel can be in several places at once. For an angel is not of lesser power than a soul. But a soul is at once in several places, because it is whole in every part of the body, as Augustine says. Therefore, an angel can be in several places at once.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0021","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0022","text":"Further, an angel is in an assumed body; and since it assumes a continuous body, it seems that it is in every part of it. But different places are considered according to its parts. Therefore, an angel is at once in several places.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0023","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0024","text":"Further, Damascene says that where an angel operates, there it is. But sometimes it operates at once in several places, as is clear concerning the angel overthrowing Sodom, Gen. XIX. Therefore, an angel can be in several places at once.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0025","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0026","text":"On the contrary, Damascene says that *angels, while they are in heaven, are not on earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0027","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0028","text":"I answer that it must be said that an angel is of finite power and essence. But the divine power and essence are infinite, and are the universal cause of all things; and therefore by His power He touches all things, and is not only in many places, but everywhere. But an angel's power, since it is finite, does not extend to all things, but to some one determinate thing. For whatever is compared to one power must be compared to it as one thing. Therefore, just as the whole of being is compared as one thing to the universal power of God, so too some particular being is compared as one thing to an angel's power. Hence, since an angel is in a place through the application of his power to the place, it follows that he is not everywhere, nor in many places, but only in one place. Yet some have been deceived concerning this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0029","text":"For some, being unable to transcend imagination, conceived the indivisibility of an angel after the manner of the indivisibility of a point, and thence believed that an angel could be only in a point-like place. But they are manifestly deceived. For a point is an indivisible having position, but an angel is an indivisible existing outside the genus of quantity and position. Hence it is not necessary that one indivisible place according to position be assigned to him; rather, it may be divisible or indivisible, greater or lesser, according as he voluntarily applies his power to a greater or lesser body. And thus the whole body to which he is applied by his power corresponds to him as one place. Yet it is not necessary that, if an angel moves the heaven, he be everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0030","text":"First, indeed, because his power is applied only to that which is first moved by him; but there is one part of the heaven in which motion is first found, namely, the eastern part. Hence the Philosopher also, in *Physics* VIII, attributes the power of the mover of the heavens to the eastern part. Second, because the philosophers do not posit that one separate substance immediately moves all the spheres. Hence it is not necessary that it be everywhere. Thus, therefore, it is clear that to be in a place belongs in different ways to a body, an angel, and God. For a body is in a place circumscriptively, because it is commensurate with the place. But an angel is not [there] circumscriptively, since he is not commensurate with the place, but definitively, because he is so in one place that he is not in another. But God is neither [there] circumscriptively nor definitively, because He is everywhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0031","text":"[I q. 52 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0032","text":"And through this the response to the objections is easily clear: because the whole to which an angel's power is immediately applied is regarded as his one place, although it is continuous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0033","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0034","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0035","text":"We proceed thus to the third [question]. It seems that several angels can be in the same place at the same time. For several bodies cannot be in the same place at the same time, because they fill the place. But angels do not fill a place, because only a body fills a place, so that there may not be a vacuum, as is clear from the Philosopher, in *Physics* IV. Therefore several angels can be in one place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0036","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0037","text":"Further, an angel and a body differ more than two angels. But an angel and a body are at the same time in the same place, because there is no place that is not full of sensible body, as is proved in *Physics* IV. Therefore, much more can two angels be in the same place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0038","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0039","text":"Further, the soul is in every part of the body, according to Augustine. But demons, although they do not enter into minds, nevertheless sometimes enter into bodies; and thus a soul and a demon are at the same time in the same place. Therefore, by the same reasoning, so too any other spiritual substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0040","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0041","text":"On the contrary, two souls are not in the same body. Therefore, by equal reasoning, neither are two angels in the same place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0042","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0043","text":"I answer that it must be said that two angels are not at the same time in the same place. And the reason for this is that it is impossible for two complete causes to be immediate [causes] of one and the same thing. This is evident in every genus of causes: for there is one proximate form of one thing, and one proximate mover, although there can be several remote movers. Nor is there a counter-instance in several persons pulling a ship, because none of them is a perfect mover, since the power of each is insufficient to move it; but all together take the place of one mover, insofar as all their powers are gathered together to produce one motion. Hence, since an angel is said to be in a place by the fact that his power immediately touches the place in the manner of a perfect container, as has been said, there can be only one angel in one place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0044","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0045","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that several angels being in one place is not prevented on account of the filling of the place, but on account of another cause, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0046","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0047","text":"To the second, it must be said that an angel and a body are not in a place in the same way; hence the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0048","text":"[I q. 52 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0049","text":"To the third it must be said that neither are the demon and the soul related to the body according to the same relation of cause; since the soul is a form, whereas the demon is not. Hence the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_44:P0049"]}],"source_digest":"c9b3c1503c025d3a9edb88ea25a4a6e3ae29b827966ba0f4ce69eb586280683d","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 52"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 52","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45","title":"First Part · Question 53","order":54,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_45","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 53 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30650)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de motu locali Angelorum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angelus possit moveri localiter. Secundo, utrum moveatur de loco ad locum, pertranseundo medium. Tertio, utrum motus Angeli sit in tempore vel in instanti.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30651)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit moveri localiter. Ut enim probat philosophus in VI Physic., nullum impartibile movetur, quia dum aliquid est in termino a quo, non movetur; nec etiam dum est in termino ad quem, sed tunc mutatum est, unde relinquitur quod omne quod movetur, dum movetur, partim est in termino a quo, et partim in termino ad quem. Sed Angelus est impartibilis. Ergo Angelus non potest moveri localiter.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30652)\n\nPraeterea, motus est actus imperfecti, ut dicitur in III Physic. Sed Angelus beatus non est imperfectus. Ergo Angelus beatus non movetur localiter.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30653)\n\nPraeterea, motus non est nisi propter indigentiam. Sed sanctorum Angelorum nulla est indigentia. Ergo sancti Angeli localiter non moventur.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30654)\n\nSed contra, eiusdem rationis est Angelum beatum moveri, et animam beatam moveri. Sed necesse est dicere animam beatam localiter moveri, cum sit articulus fidei quod Christus secundum animam, descendit ad Inferos. Ergo Angelus beatus movetur localiter.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30655)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angelus beatus potest moveri localiter. Sed sicut esse in loco aequivoce convenit corpori et Angelo, ita etiam et moveri secundum locum. Corpus enim est in loco, inquantum continetur sub loco, et commensuratur loco. Unde oportet quod etiam motus corporis secundum locum, commensuretur loco, et sit secundum exigentiam eius. Et inde est quod secundum continuitatem magnitudinis est continuitas motus; et secundum prius et posterius in magnitudine, est prius et posterius in motu locali corporis, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Sed Angelus non est in loco ut commensuratus et contentus, sed magis ut continens. Unde motus Angeli in loco, non oportet quod commensuretur loco, nec quod sit secundum exigentiam eius, ut habeat continuitatem ex loco; sed est motus non continuus. Quia enim Angelus non est in loco nisi secundum contactum virtutis, ut dictum est, necesse est quod motus Angeli in loco nihil aliud sit quam diversi contactus diversorum locorum successive et non simul, quia Angelus non potest simul esse in pluribus locis, ut supra dictum est. Huiusmodi autem contactus non est necessarium esse continuos. Potest tamen in huiusmodi contactibus continuitas quaedam inveniri. Quia, ut dictum est, nihil prohibet Angelo assignare locum divisibilem, per contactum suae virtutis; sicut corpori assignatur locus divisibilis, per contactum suae magnitudinis. Unde sicut corpus successive, et non simul, dimittit locum in quo prius erat, et ex hoc causatur continuitas in motu locali eius; ita etiam Angelus potest dimittere successive locum divisibilem in quo prius erat, et sic motus eius erit continuus. Et potest etiam totum locum simul dimittere, et toti alteri loco simul se applicare, et sic motus eius non erit continuus.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30656)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod illa ratio dupliciter deficit in proposito. Primo quidem, quia demonstratio Aristotelis procedit de indivisibili secundum quantitatem, cui respondet locus de necessitate indivisibilis. Quod non potest dici de Angelo. Secundo, quia demonstratio Aristotelis procedit de motu continuo. Si enim motus non esset continuus, posset dici quod aliquid movetur dum est in termino a quo, et dum est in termino ad quem, quia ipsa successio diversorum ubi circa eandem rem, motus diceretur; unde in quolibet illorum ubi res esset, illa posset dici moveri. Sed continuitas motus hoc impedit, quia nullum continuum est in termino suo, ut patet, quia linea non est in puncto. Et ideo oportet quod illud quod movetur, non sit totaliter in altero terminorum, dum movetur; sed partim in uno, et partim in altero. Secundum ergo quod motus Angeli non est continuus, demonstratio Aristotelis non procedit in proposito. Sed secundum quod motus Angeli ponitur continuus, sic concedi potest quod Angelus, dum movetur, partim est in termino a quo, et partim in termino ad quem (ut tamen partialitas non referatur ad substantiam Angeli, sed ad locum), quia in principio sui motus continui, Angelus est in toto loco divisibili a quo incipit moveri; sed dum est in ipso moveri, est in parte primi loci quem deserit, et in parte secundi loci quem occupat. Et hoc quidem quod possit occupare partes duorum locorum, competit Angelo ex hoc quod potest occupare locum divisibilem per applicationem suae virtutis sicut corpus per applicationem magnitudinis. Unde sequitur de corpore mobili secundum locum, quod sit divisibile secundum magnitudinem de Angelo autem, quod virtus eius possit applicari alicui divisibili.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30657)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod motus existentis in potentia, est actus imperfecti. Sed motus qui est secundum applicationem virtutis, est existentis in actu, quia virtus rei est secundum quod actu est.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30658)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod motus existentis in potentia, est propter indigentiam suam, sed motus existentis in actu, non est propter indigentiam suam, sed propter indigentiam alterius. Et hoc modo Angelus, propter indigentiam nostram, localiter movetur, secundum illud Heb. I, *omnes sunt administratorii spiritus, in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 53 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30659)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non transeat per medium. Omne enim quod pertransit medium, prius pertransit locum sibi aequalem, quam maiorem. Locus autem aequalis Angeli, qui est indivisibilis, est locus punctalis. Si ergo Angelus in suo motu pertransit medium, oportet quod numeret puncta infinita suo motu, quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30660)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus est simplicioris substantiae quam anima nostra. Sed anima nostra sua cogitatione potest transire de uno extremo in aliud, non pertranseundo medium, possum enim cogitare Galliam et postea Syriam, nihil cogitando de Italia, quae est in medio. Ergo multo magis Angelus potest de uno extremo transire ad aliud, non per medium.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30661)\n\nSed contra, si Angelus movetur de uno loco ad alium, quando est in termino ad quem, non movetur, sed mutatus est. Sed ante omne mutatum esse praecedit mutari, ergo alicubi existens movebatur. Sed non movebatur dum erat in termino a quo. Movebatur ergo dum erat in medio. Et ita oportet quod pertranseat medium.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30662)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, motus localis Angeli potest esse continuus, et non continuus. Si ergo sit continuus, non potest Angelus moveri de uno extremo in alterum, quin transeat per medium, quia, ut dicitur in V Physic., medium est in quod prius venit quod continue mutatur, quam in quod mutatur ultimum; ordo enim prioris et posterioris in motu continuo, est secundum ordinem prioris et posterioris in magnitudine, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Si autem motus Angeli non sit continuus, possibile est quod pertranseat de aliquo extremo in aliud, non pertransito medio. Quod sic patet. Inter quaelibet enim duo extrema loca sunt infinita loca media; sive accipiantur loca divisibilia, sive indivisibilia. Et de indivisibilibus quidem manifestum est, quia inter quaelibet duo puncta sunt infinita puncta media, cum nulla duo puncta consequantur se invicem sine medio, ut in VI Physic. probatur. De locis autem divisibilibus necesse est etiam hoc dicere. Et hoc demonstratur ex motu continuo alicuius corporis. Corpus enim non movetur de loco ad locum nisi in tempore. In toto autem tempore mensurante motum corporis, non est accipere duo nunc, in quibus corpus quod movetur non sit in alio et alio loco, quia si in uno et eodem loco esset in duobus nunc, sequeretur quod ibi quiesceret; cum nihil aliud sit quiescere quam in loco eodem esse nunc et prius. Cum igitur inter primum nunc et ultimum temporis mensurantis motum, sint infinita nunc, oportet quod inter primum locum, a quo incipit moveri, et ultimum locum, ad quem terminatur motus, sint infinita loca. Et hoc sic etiam sensibiliter apparet. Sit enim unum corpus unius palmi, et sit via per quam transit, duorum palmorum, manifestum est quod locus primus, a quo incipit motus, est unius palmi; et locus, ad quem terminatur motus, est alterius palmi. Manifestum est autem quod, quando incipit moveri, paulatim deserit primum palmum, et subintrat secundum. Secundum ergo quod dividitur magnitudo palmi, secundum hoc multiplicantur loca media, quia quodlibet punctum signatum in magnitudine primi palmi, est principium unius loci; et punctum signatum in magnitudine alterius palmi, est terminus eiusdem. Unde cum magnitudo sit divisibilis in infinitum, et puncta sint etiam infinita in potentia in qualibet magnitudine; sequitur quod inter quaelibet duo loca sint infinita loca media. Mobile autem infinitatem mediorum locorum non consumit nisi per continuitatem motus, quia sicut loca media sunt infinita in potentia, ita et in motu continuo est accipere infinita quaedam in potentia. Si ergo motus non sit continuus, omnes partes motus erunt numeratae in actu. Si ergo mobile quodcumque moveatur motu non continuo, sequitur quod vel non transeat omnia media, vel quod actu numeret media infinita, quod est impossibile. Sic igitur secundum quod motus Angeli non est continuus, non pertransit omnia media. Hoc autem, scilicet moveri de extremo in extremum et non per medium, potest convenire Angelo sed non corpori. Quia corpus mensuratur et continetur sub loco, unde oportet quod sequatur leges loci in suo motu. Sed substantia Angeli non est subdita loco ut contenta, sed est superior eo ut continens, unde in potestate eius est applicare se loco prout vult, vel per medium vel sine medio.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30663)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod locus Angeli non accipitur ei aequalis secundum magnitudinem, sed secundum contactum virtutis, et sic locus Angeli potest esse divisibilis, et non semper punctalis. Sed tamen loca media etiam divisibilia, sunt infinita, ut dictum est, sed consumuntur per continuitatem motus, ut patet ex praedictis.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30664)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angelus dum movetur localiter, applicatur eius essentia diversis locis, animae autem essentia non applicatur rebus quas cogitat, sed potius res cogitatae sunt in ipsa. Et ideo non est simile.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30665)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in motu continuo mutatum esse non est pars moveri, sed terminus unde oportet quod moveri sit ante mutatum esse. Et ideo oportet quod talis motus sit per medium. Sed in motu non continuo mutatum esse est pars, sicut unitas est pars numeri, unde successio diversorum locorum, etiam sine medio, constituit talem motum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30666)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod motus Angeli sit in instanti. Quanto enim virtus motoris fuerit fortior, et mobile minus resistens motori, tanto motus est velocior. Sed virtus Angeli moventis seipsum, improportionabiliter excedit virtutem moventem aliquod corpus. Proportio autem velocitatum est secundum minorationem temporis. Omne autem tempus omni tempori proportionabile est. Si igitur aliquod corpus movetur in tempore, Angelus movetur in instanti.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30667)\n\nPraeterea, motus Angeli simplicior est quam aliqua mutatio corporalis. Sed aliqua mutatio corporalis est in instanti, ut illuminatio, tum quia non illuminatur aliquid successive, sicut calefit successive; tum quia radius non prius pertingit ad propinquum quam ad remotum. Ergo multo magis motus Angeli est in instanti.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30668)\n\nPraeterea, si Angelus movetur in tempore de loco ad locum, manifestum est quod in ultimo instanti illius temporis est in termino ad quem, in toto autem tempore praecedenti, aut est in loco immediate praecedenti, qui accipitur ut terminus a quo; aut partim in uno et partim in alio. Si autem partim in uno et partim in alio, sequitur quod sit partibilis, quod est impossibile. Ergo in toto tempore praecedenti est in termino a quo. Ergo quiescit ibi, cum quiescere sit in eodem esse nunc et prius, ut dictum est. Et sic sequitur quod non moveatur nisi in ultimo instanti temporis.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30669)\n\nSed contra, in omni mutatione est prius et posterius. Sed prius et posterius motus numeratur secundum tempus. Ergo omnis motus est in tempore, etiam motus Angeli; cum in eo sit prius et posterius.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30670)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt motum localem Angeli esse in instanti. Dicebant enim quod, cum Angelus movetur de uno loco ad alium, in toto tempore praecedenti Angelus est in termino a quo in ultimo autem instanti illius temporis est in termino ad quem. Nec oportet esse aliquod medium inter duos terminos; sicut non est aliquod medium inter tempus et terminum temporis. Inter duo autem nunc temporis, est tempus medium, unde dicunt quod non est dare ultimum nunc in quo fuit in termino a quo. Sicut in illuminatione, et in generatione substantiali ignis, non est dare ultimum instans in quo aer fuit tenebrosus, vel in quo materia fuit sub privatione formae ignis, sed est dare ultimum tempus, ita quod in ultimo illius temporis est vel lumen in aere, vel forma ignis in materia. Et sic illuminatio et generatio substantialis dicuntur motus instantanei. Sed hoc non habet locum in proposito. Quod sic ostenditur. De ratione enim quietis est quod quiescens non aliter se habeat nunc et prius, et ideo in quolibet nunc temporis mensurantis quietem, quiescens est in eodem et in primo, et in medio, et in ultimo. Sed de ratione motus est quod id quod movetur, aliter se habeat nunc et prius, et ideo in quolibet nunc temporis mensurantis motum, mobile se habet in alia et alia dispositione, unde oportet quod in ultimo nunc habeat formam quam prius non habebat. Et sic patet quod quiescere in toto tempore in aliquo, puta in albedine, est esse in illo in quolibet instanti illius temporis, unde non est possibile ut aliquid in toto tempore praecedenti quiescat in uno termino et postea in ultimo instanti illius temporis sit in alio termino. Sed hoc est possibile in motu, quia moveri in toto aliquo tempore, non est esse in eadem dispositione in quolibet instanti illius temporis. Igitur omnes huiusmodi mutationes instantaneae sunt termini motus continui, sicut generatio est terminus alterationis materiae, et illuminatio terminus motus localis corporis illuminantis. Motus autem localis Angeli non est terminus alicuius alterius motus continui, sed est per seipsum, a nullo alio motu dependens. Unde impossibile est dicere quod in toto tempore sit in aliquo loco, et in ultimo nunc sit in alio loco. Sed oportet assignare nunc in quo ultimo fuit in loco praecedenti. Ubi autem sunt multa nunc sibi succedentia, ibi de necessitate est tempus, cum tempus nihil aliud sit quam numeratio prioris et posterioris in motu. Unde relinquitur quod motus Angeli sit in tempore. In continuo quidem tempore, si sit motus eius continuus; in non continuo autem, si motus sit non continuus (utroque enim modo contingit esse motum Angeli, ut dictum est), continuitas enim temporis est ex continuitate motus, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Sed istud tempus, sive sit tempus continuum sive non, non est idem cum tempore quod mensurat motum caeli, et quo mensurantur omnia corporalia, quae habent mutabilitatem ex motu caeli. Motus enim Angeli non dependet ex motu caeli.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30671)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, si tempus motus Angeli non sit continuum, sed successio quaedam ipsorum nunc, non habebit proportionem ad tempus quod mensurat motum corporalium, quod est continuum, cum non sit eiusdem rationis. Si vero sit continuum, est quidem proportionabile, non quidem propter proportionem moventis et mobilis sed propter proportionem magnitudinum in quibus est motus. Et praeterea, velocitas motus Angeli non est secundum quantitatem suae virtutis; sed secundum determinationem suae voluntatis.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30672)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illuminatio est terminus motus; et est alteratio, non motus localis ut intelligatur lumen moveri prius ad propinquum, quam ad remotum. Motus autem Angeli est localis, et non est terminus motus. Unde non est simile.\n\n[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30673)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de tempore continuo. Tempus autem motus Angeli potest esse non continuum. Et sic Angelus in uno instanti potest esse in uno loco, et in alio instanti in alio loco, nullo tempore intermedio existente. Si autem tempus motus Angeli sit continuum, Angelus in toto tempore praecedenti ultimum nunc, variatur per infinita loca, ut prius expositum est. Est tamen partim in uno locorum continuorum et partim in alio, non quod substantia illius sit partibilis; sed quia virtus sua applicatur ad partem primi loci et ad partem secundi, ut etiam supra dictum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0002","text":"[I q. 53 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de motu locali Angelorum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angelus possit moveri localiter. Secundo, utrum moveatur de loco ad locum, pertranseundo medium. Tertio, utrum motus Angeli sit in tempore vel in instanti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0005","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit moveri localiter. Ut enim probat philosophus in VI Physic., nullum impartibile movetur, quia dum aliquid est in termino a quo, non movetur; nec etiam dum est in termino ad quem, sed tunc mutatum est, unde relinquitur quod omne quod movetur, dum movetur, partim est in termino a quo, et partim in termino ad quem. Sed Angelus est impartibilis. Ergo Angelus non potest moveri localiter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0007","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0008","text":"Praeterea, motus est actus imperfecti, ut dicitur in III Physic. Sed Angelus beatus non est imperfectus. Ergo Angelus beatus non movetur localiter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0009","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0010","text":"Praeterea, motus non est nisi propter indigentiam. Sed sanctorum Angelorum nulla est indigentia. Ergo sancti Angeli localiter non moventur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0011","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0012","text":"Sed contra, eiusdem rationis est Angelum beatum moveri, et animam beatam moveri. Sed necesse est dicere animam beatam localiter moveri, cum sit articulus fidei quod Christus secundum animam, descendit ad Inferos. Ergo Angelus beatus movetur localiter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0013","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angelus beatus potest moveri localiter. Sed sicut esse in loco aequivoce convenit corpori et Angelo, ita etiam et moveri secundum locum. Corpus enim est in loco, inquantum continetur sub loco, et commensuratur loco. Unde oportet quod etiam motus corporis secundum locum, commensuretur loco, et sit secundum exigentiam eius. Et inde est quod secundum continuitatem magnitudinis est continuitas motus; et secundum prius et posterius in magnitudine, est prius et posterius in motu locali corporis, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Sed Angelus non est in loco ut commensuratus et contentus, sed magis ut continens. Unde motus Angeli in loco, non oportet quod commensuretur loco, nec quod sit secundum exigentiam eius, ut habeat continuitatem ex loco; sed est motus non continuus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0015","text":"Quia enim Angelus non est in loco nisi secundum contactum virtutis, ut dictum est, necesse est quod motus Angeli in loco nihil aliud sit quam diversi contactus diversorum locorum successive et non simul, quia Angelus non potest simul esse in pluribus locis, ut supra dictum est. Huiusmodi autem contactus non est necessarium esse continuos. Potest tamen in huiusmodi contactibus continuitas quaedam inveniri. Quia, ut dictum est, nihil prohibet Angelo assignare locum divisibilem, per contactum suae virtutis; sicut corpori assignatur locus divisibilis, per contactum suae magnitudinis. Unde sicut corpus successive, et non simul, dimittit locum in quo prius erat, et ex hoc causatur continuitas in motu locali eius; ita etiam Angelus potest dimittere successive locum divisibilem in quo prius erat, et sic motus eius erit continuus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0016","text":"Et potest etiam totum locum simul dimittere, et toti alteri loco simul se applicare, et sic motus eius non erit continuus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0017","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod illa ratio dupliciter deficit in proposito. Primo quidem, quia demonstratio Aristotelis procedit de indivisibili secundum quantitatem, cui respondet locus de necessitate indivisibilis. Quod non potest dici de Angelo. Secundo, quia demonstratio Aristotelis procedit de motu continuo. Si enim motus non esset continuus, posset dici quod aliquid movetur dum est in termino a quo, et dum est in termino ad quem, quia ipsa successio diversorum ubi circa eandem rem, motus diceretur; unde in quolibet illorum ubi res esset, illa posset dici moveri. Sed continuitas motus hoc impedit, quia nullum continuum est in termino suo, ut patet, quia linea non est in puncto. Et ideo oportet quod illud quod movetur, non sit totaliter in altero terminorum, dum movetur; sed partim in uno, et partim in altero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0019","text":"Secundum ergo quod motus Angeli non est continuus, demonstratio Aristotelis non procedit in proposito. Sed secundum quod motus Angeli ponitur continuus, sic concedi potest quod Angelus, dum movetur, partim est in termino a quo, et partim in termino ad quem (ut tamen partialitas non referatur ad substantiam Angeli, sed ad locum), quia in principio sui motus continui, Angelus est in toto loco divisibili a quo incipit moveri; sed dum est in ipso moveri, est in parte primi loci quem deserit, et in parte secundi loci quem occupat. Et hoc quidem quod possit occupare partes duorum locorum, competit Angelo ex hoc quod potest occupare locum divisibilem per applicationem suae virtutis sicut corpus per applicationem magnitudinis. Unde sequitur de corpore mobili secundum locum, quod sit divisibile secundum magnitudinem de Angelo autem, quod virtus eius possit applicari alicui divisibili.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0020","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod motus existentis in potentia, est actus imperfecti. Sed motus qui est secundum applicationem virtutis, est existentis in actu, quia virtus rei est secundum quod actu est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0022","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod motus existentis in potentia, est propter indigentiam suam, sed motus existentis in actu, non est propter indigentiam suam, sed propter indigentiam alterius. Et hoc modo Angelus, propter indigentiam nostram, localiter movetur, secundum illud Heb. I, *omnes sunt administratorii spiritus, in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0025","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non transeat per medium. Omne enim quod pertransit medium, prius pertransit locum sibi aequalem, quam maiorem. Locus autem aequalis Angeli, qui est indivisibilis, est locus punctalis. Si ergo Angelus in suo motu pertransit medium, oportet quod numeret puncta infinita suo motu, quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0027","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0028","text":"Praeterea, Angelus est simplicioris substantiae quam anima nostra. Sed anima nostra sua cogitatione potest transire de uno extremo in aliud, non pertranseundo medium, possum enim cogitare Galliam et postea Syriam, nihil cogitando de Italia, quae est in medio. Ergo multo magis Angelus potest de uno extremo transire ad aliud, non per medium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0029","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0030","text":"Sed contra, si Angelus movetur de uno loco ad alium, quando est in termino ad quem, non movetur, sed mutatus est. Sed ante omne mutatum esse praecedit mutari, ergo alicubi existens movebatur. Sed non movebatur dum erat in termino a quo. Movebatur ergo dum erat in medio. Et ita oportet quod pertranseat medium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0031","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, motus localis Angeli potest esse continuus, et non continuus. Si ergo sit continuus, non potest Angelus moveri de uno extremo in alterum, quin transeat per medium, quia, ut dicitur in V Physic., medium est in quod prius venit quod continue mutatur, quam in quod mutatur ultimum; ordo enim prioris et posterioris in motu continuo, est secundum ordinem prioris et posterioris in magnitudine, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Si autem motus Angeli non sit continuus, possibile est quod pertranseat de aliquo extremo in aliud, non pertransito medio. Quod sic patet. Inter quaelibet enim duo extrema loca sunt infinita loca media; sive accipiantur loca divisibilia, sive indivisibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0033","text":"Et de indivisibilibus quidem manifestum est, quia inter quaelibet duo puncta sunt infinita puncta media, cum nulla duo puncta consequantur se invicem sine medio, ut in VI Physic. probatur. De locis autem divisibilibus necesse est etiam hoc dicere. Et hoc demonstratur ex motu continuo alicuius corporis. Corpus enim non movetur de loco ad locum nisi in tempore. In toto autem tempore mensurante motum corporis, non est accipere duo nunc, in quibus corpus quod movetur non sit in alio et alio loco, quia si in uno et eodem loco esset in duobus nunc, sequeretur quod ibi quiesceret; cum nihil aliud sit quiescere quam in loco eodem esse nunc et prius. Cum igitur inter primum nunc et ultimum temporis mensurantis motum, sint infinita nunc, oportet quod inter primum locum, a quo incipit moveri, et ultimum locum, ad quem terminatur motus, sint infinita loca. Et hoc sic etiam sensibiliter apparet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0034","text":"Sit enim unum corpus unius palmi, et sit via per quam transit, duorum palmorum, manifestum est quod locus primus, a quo incipit motus, est unius palmi; et locus, ad quem terminatur motus, est alterius palmi. Manifestum est autem quod, quando incipit moveri, paulatim deserit primum palmum, et subintrat secundum. Secundum ergo quod dividitur magnitudo palmi, secundum hoc multiplicantur loca media, quia quodlibet punctum signatum in magnitudine primi palmi, est principium unius loci; et punctum signatum in magnitudine alterius palmi, est terminus eiusdem. Unde cum magnitudo sit divisibilis in infinitum, et puncta sint etiam infinita in potentia in qualibet magnitudine; sequitur quod inter quaelibet duo loca sint infinita loca media.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0035","text":"Mobile autem infinitatem mediorum locorum non consumit nisi per continuitatem motus, quia sicut loca media sunt infinita in potentia, ita et in motu continuo est accipere infinita quaedam in potentia. Si ergo motus non sit continuus, omnes partes motus erunt numeratae in actu. Si ergo mobile quodcumque moveatur motu non continuo, sequitur quod vel non transeat omnia media, vel quod actu numeret media infinita, quod est impossibile. Sic igitur secundum quod motus Angeli non est continuus, non pertransit omnia media. Hoc autem, scilicet moveri de extremo in extremum et non per medium, potest convenire Angelo sed non corpori. Quia corpus mensuratur et continetur sub loco, unde oportet quod sequatur leges loci in suo motu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0036","text":"Sed substantia Angeli non est subdita loco ut contenta, sed est superior eo ut continens, unde in potestate eius est applicare se loco prout vult, vel per medium vel sine medio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0037","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod locus Angeli non accipitur ei aequalis secundum magnitudinem, sed secundum contactum virtutis, et sic locus Angeli potest esse divisibilis, et non semper punctalis. Sed tamen loca media etiam divisibilia, sunt infinita, ut dictum est, sed consumuntur per continuitatem motus, ut patet ex praedictis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0039","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angelus dum movetur localiter, applicatur eius essentia diversis locis, animae autem essentia non applicatur rebus quas cogitat, sed potius res cogitatae sunt in ipsa. Et ideo non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0041","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in motu continuo mutatum esse non est pars moveri, sed terminus unde oportet quod moveri sit ante mutatum esse. Et ideo oportet quod talis motus sit per medium. Sed in motu non continuo mutatum esse est pars, sicut unitas est pars numeri, unde successio diversorum locorum, etiam sine medio, constituit talem motum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0043","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0044","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0045","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod motus Angeli sit in instanti. Quanto enim virtus motoris fuerit fortior, et mobile minus resistens motori, tanto motus est velocior. Sed virtus Angeli moventis seipsum, improportionabiliter excedit virtutem moventem aliquod corpus. Proportio autem velocitatum est secundum minorationem temporis. Omne autem tempus omni tempori proportionabile est. Si igitur aliquod corpus movetur in tempore, Angelus movetur in instanti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0046","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0047","text":"Praeterea, motus Angeli simplicior est quam aliqua mutatio corporalis. Sed aliqua mutatio corporalis est in instanti, ut illuminatio, tum quia non illuminatur aliquid successive, sicut calefit successive; tum quia radius non prius pertingit ad propinquum quam ad remotum. Ergo multo magis motus Angeli est in instanti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0048","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0049","text":"Praeterea, si Angelus movetur in tempore de loco ad locum, manifestum est quod in ultimo instanti illius temporis est in termino ad quem, in toto autem tempore praecedenti, aut est in loco immediate praecedenti, qui accipitur ut terminus a quo; aut partim in uno et partim in alio. Si autem partim in uno et partim in alio, sequitur quod sit partibilis, quod est impossibile. Ergo in toto tempore praecedenti est in termino a quo. Ergo quiescit ibi, cum quiescere sit in eodem esse nunc et prius, ut dictum est. Et sic sequitur quod non moveatur nisi in ultimo instanti temporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0050","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0051","text":"Sed contra, in omni mutatione est prius et posterius. Sed prius et posterius motus numeratur secundum tempus. Ergo omnis motus est in tempore, etiam motus Angeli; cum in eo sit prius et posterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0052","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0053","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt motum localem Angeli esse in instanti. Dicebant enim quod, cum Angelus movetur de uno loco ad alium, in toto tempore praecedenti Angelus est in termino a quo in ultimo autem instanti illius temporis est in termino ad quem. Nec oportet esse aliquod medium inter duos terminos; sicut non est aliquod medium inter tempus et terminum temporis. Inter duo autem nunc temporis, est tempus medium, unde dicunt quod non est dare ultimum nunc in quo fuit in termino a quo. Sicut in illuminatione, et in generatione substantiali ignis, non est dare ultimum instans in quo aer fuit tenebrosus, vel in quo materia fuit sub privatione formae ignis, sed est dare ultimum tempus, ita quod in ultimo illius temporis est vel lumen in aere, vel forma ignis in materia. Et sic illuminatio et generatio substantialis dicuntur motus instantanei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0054","text":"Sed hoc non habet locum in proposito. Quod sic ostenditur. De ratione enim quietis est quod quiescens non aliter se habeat nunc et prius, et ideo in quolibet nunc temporis mensurantis quietem, quiescens est in eodem et in primo, et in medio, et in ultimo. Sed de ratione motus est quod id quod movetur, aliter se habeat nunc et prius, et ideo in quolibet nunc temporis mensurantis motum, mobile se habet in alia et alia dispositione, unde oportet quod in ultimo nunc habeat formam quam prius non habebat. Et sic patet quod quiescere in toto tempore in aliquo, puta in albedine, est esse in illo in quolibet instanti illius temporis, unde non est possibile ut aliquid in toto tempore praecedenti quiescat in uno termino et postea in ultimo instanti illius temporis sit in alio termino.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0055","text":"Sed hoc est possibile in motu, quia moveri in toto aliquo tempore, non est esse in eadem dispositione in quolibet instanti illius temporis. Igitur omnes huiusmodi mutationes instantaneae sunt termini motus continui, sicut generatio est terminus alterationis materiae, et illuminatio terminus motus localis corporis illuminantis. Motus autem localis Angeli non est terminus alicuius alterius motus continui, sed est per seipsum, a nullo alio motu dependens. Unde impossibile est dicere quod in toto tempore sit in aliquo loco, et in ultimo nunc sit in alio loco. Sed oportet assignare nunc in quo ultimo fuit in loco praecedenti. Ubi autem sunt multa nunc sibi succedentia, ibi de necessitate est tempus, cum tempus nihil aliud sit quam numeratio prioris et posterioris in motu. Unde relinquitur quod motus Angeli sit in tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0056","text":"In continuo quidem tempore, si sit motus eius continuus; in non continuo autem, si motus sit non continuus (utroque enim modo contingit esse motum Angeli, ut dictum est), continuitas enim temporis est ex continuitate motus, ut dicitur in IV Physic. Sed istud tempus, sive sit tempus continuum sive non, non est idem cum tempore quod mensurat motum caeli, et quo mensurantur omnia corporalia, quae habent mutabilitatem ex motu caeli. Motus enim Angeli non dependet ex motu caeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0057","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, si tempus motus Angeli non sit continuum, sed successio quaedam ipsorum nunc, non habebit proportionem ad tempus quod mensurat motum corporalium, quod est continuum, cum non sit eiusdem rationis. Si vero sit continuum, est quidem proportionabile, non quidem propter proportionem moventis et mobilis sed propter proportionem magnitudinum in quibus est motus. Et praeterea, velocitas motus Angeli non est secundum quantitatem suae virtutis; sed secundum determinationem suae voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0059","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illuminatio est terminus motus; et est alteratio, non motus localis ut intelligatur lumen moveri prius ad propinquum, quam ad remotum. Motus autem Angeli est localis, et non est terminus motus. Unde non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0061","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de tempore continuo. Tempus autem motus Angeli potest esse non continuum. Et sic Angelus in uno instanti potest esse in uno loco, et in alio instanti in alio loco, nullo tempore intermedio existente. Si autem tempus motus Angeli sit continuum, Angelus in toto tempore praecedenti ultimum nunc, variatur per infinita loca, ut prius expositum est. Est tamen partim in uno locorum continuorum et partim in alio, non quod substantia illius sit partibilis; sed quia virtus sua applicatur ad partem primi loci et ad partem secundi, ut etiam supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6441c64553312d3eca78f9b7240cab57bc9c7a994f937de42ea22a4f67166a51","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143801713632Z_1de642cf","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 53","source_sha256":"6441c64553312d3eca78f9b7240cab57bc9c7a994f937de42ea22a4f67166a51","sha256":"b2d784de98b055cd5b8e7625226f01ccfa9e5c952469b4166b55057aa3d67130","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:38:01.713685+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0002","text":"[I q. 53 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0003","text":"Consequently, we must consider the local motion of angels. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, whether an angel can be moved locally. Second, whether it moves from place to place by passing through the intermediate [space]. Third, whether an angel's motion is in time or in an instant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0005","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first [question]. It seems that an angel cannot be moved locally. For, as the Philosopher proves in Physics VI, no indivisible thing is moved, because while something is at the terminus from which, it is not moved; nor is it [moved] while it is at the terminus to which, but then it has been changed. Hence it remains that everything which is moved, while it is being moved, is partly at the terminus from which and partly at the terminus to which. But an angel is indivisible. Therefore an angel cannot be moved locally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0007","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0008","text":"Further, motion is the act of what is imperfect, as is said in Physics III. But a blessed angel is not imperfect. Therefore a blessed angel is not moved locally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0009","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0010","text":"Further, motion exists only on account of need. But holy angels have no need. Therefore holy angels are not moved locally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0011","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the reason is the same for a blessed angel to be moved and for a blessed soul to be moved. But it is necessary to say that a blessed soul is moved locally, since it is an article of faith that Christ, according to his soul, descended into Hell. Therefore a blessed angel is moved locally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0013","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that a blessed angel can be moved locally. But just as to be in a place belongs equivocally to a body and to an angel, so also does to be moved according to place. For a body is in a place insofar as it is contained under the place and is commensurated with the place. Hence the motion of a body according to place must also be commensurated with the place and be according to its requirements. And from this it follows that, according to the continuity of magnitude, there is continuity of motion; and according to before and after in magnitude, there is before and after in the local motion of a body, as is said in Physics IV. But an angel is not in a place as commensurated and contained, but rather as containing. Hence an angel's motion in a place need not be commensurated with the place, nor be according to its requirements, so as to have continuity from the place; rather, it is a non-continuous motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0015","text":"For since an angel is in a place only by contact of power, as has been said, an angel's motion in a place must be nothing other than different contacts of different places successively and not simultaneously, because an angel cannot be in several places simultaneously, as was said above. Yet such contacts need not be continuous. Nevertheless, a certain continuity can be found in such contacts. For, as has been said, nothing prevents assigning a divisible place to an angel by the contact of its power, just as a divisible place is assigned to a body by the contact of its magnitude. Hence, just as a body successively, and not simultaneously, leaves the place in which it was before, and from this continuity is caused in its local motion, so also an angel can successively leave the divisible place in which it was before, and thus its motion will be continuous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0016","text":"And it can also leave the whole place simultaneously and apply itself simultaneously to another whole place, and thus its motion will not be continuous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0017","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0018","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that that argument fails in the present matter in two ways. First, because Aristotle's demonstration proceeds concerning what is indivisible according to quantity, to which a necessarily indivisible place corresponds. This cannot be said of an angel. Second, because Aristotle's demonstration proceeds concerning continuous motion. For if motion were not continuous, one could say that something is moved while it is at the terminus from which and while it is at the terminus to which, because the very succession of different wheres concerning the same thing would be called motion; hence, in each of those wheres in which the thing was, it could be said to be moved. But the continuity of motion prevents this, because no continuum is at its terminus, as is evident, since a line is not in a point. And therefore that which is moved must not be wholly in either terminus while it is being moved, but partly in one and partly in the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0019","text":"Therefore, insofar as an angel's motion is not continuous, Aristotle's demonstration does not apply in the present matter. But insofar as an angel's motion is held to be continuous, it can be conceded that an angel, while it is moved, is partly at the terminus from which and partly at the terminus to which (provided, however, that partiality is referred not to the angel's substance but to the place), because at the beginning of its continuous motion the angel is in the whole divisible place from which it begins to be moved; but while it is in the very being-moved, it is in part of the first place which it leaves and in part of the second place which it occupies. And indeed, its being able to occupy parts of two places belongs to an angel from the fact that it can occupy a divisible place by the application of its power, just as a body [does] by the application of magnitude. Hence it follows of a body movable according to place that it is divisible according to magnitude; but of an angel, that its power can be applied to some divisible thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0020","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that the motion of something existing in potency is the act of what is imperfect. But motion that is according to the application of power belongs to something existing in act, because the power of a thing is according as it exists in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0022","text":"[I q. 53 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that the motion of something existing in potency is on account of its own need; but the motion of something existing in act is not on account of its own need, but on account of another's need. And in this way an angel, on account of our need, is moved locally, according to Hebrews I: *Are they not all ministering spirits, sent for ministry on behalf of those who receive the inheritance of salvation?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0025","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that an angel does not pass through the middle. For everything that passes through the middle first passes through a place equal to itself before [passing through] a greater one. But the place equal to an angel, which is indivisible, is a point-place. Therefore, if an angel in its motion passes through the middle, it must number infinitely many points by its motion, which is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0027","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0028","text":"Further, an angel is of a simpler substance than our soul. But our soul can by its thought pass from one extreme to another without passing through the middle; for I can think of Gaul and afterward Syria, thinking nothing of Italy, which lies in between. Therefore, much more can an angel pass from one extreme to another, not through the middle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0029","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0030","text":"On the contrary, if an angel is moved from one place to another, when it is at the terminus to which [it moves], it is not being moved, but has been changed. But before every having-been-changed, being-changed precedes; therefore, while existing somewhere, it was being moved. But it was not being moved while it was at the terminus from which [it moved]. Therefore, it was being moved while it was in the middle. And thus it must pass through the middle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0031","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was stated above, an angel's local motion can be continuous and non-continuous. Therefore, if it is continuous, an angel cannot be moved from one extreme to the other without passing through the middle, because, as is said in Physics V, the middle is that at which what is changed continuously arrives before that at which it is changed last; for the order of prior and posterior in continuous motion is according to the order of prior and posterior in magnitude, as is said in Physics IV. But if an angel's motion is not continuous, it is possible for it to pass from one extreme to another without passing through the middle. This is clear as follows. For between any two extreme places there are infinitely many intermediate places, whether divisible or indivisible places are taken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0033","text":"And concerning indivisible [places], this is manifest, because between any two points there are infinitely many intermediate points, since no two points follow one another without a middle, as is proved in Physics VI. But concerning divisible places, it is necessary to say this as well. And this is demonstrated from the continuous motion of a body. For a body is not moved from place to place except in time. But in the whole time measuring a body's motion, it is not possible to take two nows in which the body that is moved is not in different places; because if it were in one and the same place at two nows, it would follow that it rested there, since to rest is nothing other than to be in the same place now and before. Therefore, since between the first now and the last [now] of the time measuring the motion there are infinitely many nows, there must be infinitely many places between the first place from which it begins to be moved and the last place at which the motion terminates. And this also appears perceptibly in the following way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0034","text":"For let there be one body of one palm, and let the path through which it passes be of two palms. It is manifest that the first place, from which the motion begins, is of one palm; and the place at which the motion terminates is of another palm. But it is manifest that, when it begins to be moved, it gradually leaves the first palm and enters upon the second. Therefore, according as the magnitude of the palm is divided, intermediate places are multiplied accordingly, because every point marked in the magnitude of the first palm is the beginning of one place, and a point marked in the magnitude of the other palm is the terminus of that same [place]. Hence, since magnitude is divisible to infinity, and points are also infinite in potency in every magnitude, it follows that between any two places there are infinitely many intermediate places.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0035","text":"But a movable does not exhaust the infinity of intermediate places except through continuity of motion, because, just as the intermediate places are infinite in potency, so also in continuous motion certain infinitely many [things] can be taken in potency. Therefore, if motion is not continuous, all the parts of the motion will be numbered in act. Therefore, if any movable is moved with non-continuous motion, it follows either that it does not pass through all the intermediates, or that it actually numbers infinite intermediates, which is impossible. Thus, insofar as an angel's motion is not continuous, it does not pass through all the intermediates. But this, namely, to be moved from extreme to extreme and not through the middle, can belong to an angel but not to a body. For a body is measured and contained under place, whence it must follow the laws of place in its motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0036","text":"But an angel's substance is not subject to place as contained, but is superior to it as containing; whence it is in its power to apply itself to a place as it wills, either through the middle or without a middle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0037","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an angel's place is not taken as equal to it according to magnitude, but according to contact of power; and thus an angel's place can be divisible, and is not always point-like. Yet even divisible intermediate places are infinite, as has been said, but they are exhausted through continuity of motion, as is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0039","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0040","text":"To the second, it must be said that, while an angel is moved locally, its essence is applied to diverse places; but the essence of the soul is not applied to the things that it thinks, but rather the things thought are in it. And therefore there is no likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0041","text":"[I q. 53 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0042","text":"To the third, it must be said that in continuous motion, having-been-changed is not a part of being-moved, but its terminus, whence being-moved must be before having-been-changed. And therefore such motion must be through the middle. But in non-continuous motion, having-been-changed is a part, just as unity is a part of number; whence the succession of diverse places, even without a middle, constitutes such motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0043","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0044","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0045","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that an angel's motion is instantaneous. For the stronger the power of the mover, and the less resistant the movable is to the mover, the swifter is the motion. But the power of an angel moving itself exceeds without proportion the power moving some body. Now the proportion of velocities is according to the diminution of time. But every time is proportionable to every time. Therefore, if some body is moved in time, an angel is moved in an instant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0046","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0047","text":"Further, an angel's motion is simpler than any bodily change. But some bodily change occurs in an instant, as illumination: both because something is not illuminated successively, as it is heated successively; and because a ray does not reach the nearer [thing] before the more distant. Therefore, much more is an angel's motion instantaneous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0048","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0049","text":"Moreover, if an Angel is moved in time from place to place, it is manifest that at the last instant of that time he is at the terminus to which, whereas throughout the preceding time he is either in the immediately preceding place, which is taken as the terminus from which, or partly in one and partly in the other. But if partly in one and partly in the other, it follows that he is divisible, which is impossible. Therefore, throughout the preceding time he is at the terminus from which. Therefore he is at rest there, since to be at rest is to be in the same [state] now and before, as has been said. And thus it follows that he is not moved except at the last instant of time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0050","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0051","text":"But on the contrary, in every change there is prior and posterior. But the prior and posterior of motion is numbered according to time. Therefore every motion is in time, including the motion of an Angel, since in it there is prior and posterior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0052","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0053","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have maintained that the local motion of an Angel is in an instant. For they said that, when an Angel is moved from one place to another, throughout the preceding time the Angel is at the terminus from which, but at the last instant of that time he is at the terminus to which. Nor need there be any intermediate between the two termini, just as there is no intermediate between time and the terminus of time. But between two nows of time there is an intermediate time; hence they say that no last now can be given in which he was at the terminus from which. Just as in illumination and in the substantial generation of fire, no last instant can be given in which the air was dark, or in which matter was under the privation of the form of fire; but a last time can be given, such that at its end there is either light in the air or the form of fire in matter. And thus illumination and substantial generation are called instantaneous motions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0054","text":"But this does not apply to the matter at hand. This is shown as follows. For it belongs to the nature of rest that what is at rest be not otherwise disposed now and before; and therefore, at every now of the time measuring rest, what is at rest is in the same [state], at the first, the middle, and the last. But it belongs to the nature of motion that what is moved be otherwise disposed now and before; and therefore, at every now of the time measuring motion, the movable is in different and different dispositions, whence it must be that at the last now it has a form that it did not have before. Thus it is clear that to be at rest throughout a whole time in something, for example in whiteness, is to be in it at every instant of that time; whence it is not possible for something to be at rest throughout the preceding time in one terminus and then, at the last instant of that time, to be in another terminus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0055","text":"But this is possible in motion, because to be moved throughout some whole time is not to be in the same disposition at every instant of that time. Therefore all changes of this sort that are instantaneous are termini of continuous motion, just as generation is the terminus of the alteration of matter, and illumination the terminus of the local motion of the illuminating body. But the local motion of an Angel is not the terminus of some other continuous motion, but is through itself, depending on no other motion. Hence it is impossible to say that throughout the whole time he is in some place and at the last now is in another place. Rather, a now must be assigned in which he was last in the preceding place. But where there are many nows succeeding one another, there is necessarily time, since time is nothing other than the numbering of prior and posterior in motion. Hence it remains that the motion of an Angel is in time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0056","text":"Indeed, in continuous time, if his motion is continuous; but in non-continuous [time], if the motion is non-continuous (for the motion of an Angel can occur in both ways, as has been said). For the continuity of time derives from the continuity of motion, as is said in Physics IV. But this time, whether it be continuous time or not, is not the same as the time that measures the motion of the heavens and by which all bodily things are measured, which have mutability from the motion of the heavens. For the motion of an Angel does not depend on the motion of the heavens.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0057","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0058","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that if the time of an Angel's motion is not continuous, but a certain succession of nows themselves, it will not have a proportion to the time that measures the motion of bodies, which is continuous, since it is not of the same nature. But if it is continuous, it is proportionable, not indeed on account of a proportion of mover and movable, but on account of a proportion of the magnitudes in which the motion occurs. And furthermore, the speed of an Angel's motion is not according to the quantity of his power, but according to the determination of his will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0059","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0060","text":"To the second, it must be said that illumination is the terminus of motion, and is alteration, not local motion such that light is understood to move first to what is near and then to what is remote. But the motion of an Angel is local and is not the terminus of motion. Hence there is no likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0061","text":"[I q. 53 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0062","text":"To the third, it must be said that that objection proceeds concerning continuous time. But the time of an Angel's motion can be non-continuous. And thus an Angel can be in one place in one instant and in another place in another instant, with no intermediate time existing. But if the time of an Angel's motion is continuous, throughout the whole time preceding the last now the Angel varies through infinitely many places, as was explained above. Yet he is partly in one of the continuous places and partly in another, not because his substance is divisible, but because his power is applied to part of the first place and part of the second, as was also said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_45:P0062"]}],"source_digest":"7d789da24944e78bd578fa333ac4349ba46c39782ec4b9506a6dabd03250d5cc","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 53"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 53","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46","title":"First Part · Question 54","order":55,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_46","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 54 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30674)\n\nConsideratis his quae ad substantiam Angeli pertinent, procedendum est ad cognitionem ipsius. Haec autem consideratio erit quadripartita, nam primo considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad virtutem cognoscitivam Angeli; secundo, de his quae pertinent ad medium cognoscendi ipsius; tertio, de his quae ab eo cognoscuntur; quarto, de modo cognitionis ipsorum. Circa primum quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum intelligere Angeli sit sua substantia. Secundo, utrum eius esse sit suum intelligere. Tertio, utrum eius substantia sit sua virtus intellectiva. Quarto, utrum in Angelis sit intellectus agens et possibilis. Quinto, utrum in eis sit aliqua alia potentia cognoscitiva quam intellectus.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30675)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intelligere Angeli sit eius substantia. Angelus enim est sublimior et simplicior quam intellectus agens animae. Sed substantia intellectus agentis est sua actio; ut patet in III de anima per Aristotelem, et eius Commentatorem. Ergo, multo fortius, substantia Angeli est sua actio, quae est intelligere.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30676)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in XII Metaphys., quod actio intellectus est vita. Sed cum vivere sit esse viventibus, ut dicitur in II de anima, videtur quod vita sit essentia. Ergo actio intellectus est essentia intelligentis Angeli.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30677)\n\nPraeterea, si extrema sunt unum, medium non differt ab eis, quia extremum magis distat ab extremo, quam medium. Sed in Angelo idem est intellectus et intellectum, ad minus inquantum intelligit essentiam suam. Ergo intelligere, quod cadit medium inter intellectum et rem intellectam, est idem cum substantia Angeli intelligentis.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30678)\n\nSed contra, plus differt actio rei a substantia eius, quam ipsum esse eius. Sed nullius creati suum esse est sua substantia, hoc enim solius Dei proprium est, ut ex superioribus patet. Ergo neque Angeli, neque alterius creaturae, sua actio est eius substantia.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30679)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est quod actio Angeli, vel cuiuscumque alterius creaturae, sit eius substantia. Actio enim est proprie actualitas virtutis; sicut esse est actualitas substantiae vel essentiae. Impossibile est autem quod aliquid quod non est purus actus, sed aliquid habet de potentia admixtum, sit sua actualitas, quia actualitas potentialitati repugnat. Solus autem Deus est actus purus. Unde in solo Deo sua substantia est suum esse et suum agere. Praeterea, si intelligere Angeli esset sua substantia, oporteret quod intelligere Angeli esset subsistens. Intelligere autem subsistens non potest esse nisi unum; sicut nec aliquod abstractum subsistens. Unde unius Angeli substantia non distingueretur neque a substantia Dei, quae est ipsum intelligere subsistens; neque a substantia alterius Angeli. Si etiam Angelus ipse esset suum intelligere, non possent esse gradus in intelligendo perfectius et minus perfecte, cum hoc contingat propter diversam participationem ipsius intelligere.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30680)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur quod intellectus agens est sua actio, est praedicatio non per essentiam, sed per concomitantiam, quia cum sit in actu eius substantia, statim quantum est in se, concomitatur ipsam actio. Quod non est de intellectu possibili, qui non habet actiones nisi postquam fuerit factus in actu.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30681)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod vita non hoc modo se habet ad vivere, sicut essentia ad esse; sed sicut cursus ad currere, quorum unum significat actum in abstracto, aliud in concreto. Unde non sequitur si vivere sit esse, quod vita sit essentia. Quamvis etiam quandoque vita pro essentia ponatur; secundum quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de Trin., quod *memoria et intelligentia et voluntas sunt una essentia, una vita*. Sed sic non accipitur a philosopho cum dicit quod *actio intellectus est vita*.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30682)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod actio quae transit in aliquid extrinsecum, est realiter media inter agens et subiectum recipiens actionem. Sed actio quae manet in agente, non est realiter medium inter agens et obiectum, sed secundum modum significandi tantum, realiter vero consequitur unionem obiecti cum agente. Ex hoc enim quod intellectum fit unum cum intelligente, consequitur intelligere, quasi quidam effectus differens ab utroque.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 54 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30683)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intelligere Angeli sit eius esse. Vivere enim viventibus est esse, ut dicitur in II de anima. Sed intelligere est quoddam vivere, ut in eodem dicitur. Ergo intelligere Angeli est eius esse.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30684)\n\nPraeterea, sicut se habet causa ad causam, ita effectus ad effectum. Sed forma per quam Angelus est, est eadem cum forma per quam intelligit ad minus seipsum. Ergo eius intelligere est idem cum suo esse.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30685)\n\nSed contra, intelligere Angeli est motus eius; ut patet per Dionysium, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Sed esse non est motus. Ergo esse Angeli non est intelligere eius.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30686)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod actio Angeli non est eius esse, neque actio alicuius creaturae. Duplex enim est actionis genus, ut dicitur IX Metaphys. Una scilicet actio est quae transit in aliquid exterius, inferens ei passionem, sicut urere et secare. Alia vero actio est quae non transit in rem exteriorem, sed manet in ipso agente, sicut sentire, intelligere et velle, per huiusmodi enim actionem non immutatur aliquid extrinsecum, sed totum in ipso agente agitur. De prima ergo actione manifestum est quod non potest esse ipsum esse agentis, nam esse agentis significatur intra ipsum, actio autem talis est effluxus in actum ab agente. Secunda autem actio de sui ratione habet infinitatem, vel simpliciter, vel secundum quid. Simpliciter quidem, sicut intelligere, cuius obiectum est verum, et velle, cuius obiectum est bonum, quorum utrumque convertitur cum ente; et ita intelligere et velle, quantum est de se, habent se ad omnia; et utrumque recipit speciem ab obiecto. Secundum quid autem infinitum est sentire, quod se habet ad omnia sensibilia, sicut visus ad omnia visibilia. Esse autem cuiuslibet creaturae est determinatum ad unum secundum genus et speciem, esse autem solius Dei est simpliciter infinitum, in se omnia comprehendens, ut dicit Dionysius, V cap. de Div. Nom. Unde solum esse divinum est suum intelligere et suum velle.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30687)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod vivere quandoque sumitur pro ipso esse viventis, quandoque vero pro operatione vitae, idest per quam demonstratur aliquid esse vivens. Et hoc modo philosophus dicit quod intelligere est vivere quoddam, ibi enim distinguit diversos gradus viventium secundum diversa opera vitae.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30688)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ipsa essentia Angeli est ratio totius sui esse, non autem est ratio totius sui intelligere, quia non omnia intelligere potest per suam essentiam. Et ideo secundum propriam rationem, inquantum est talis essentia, comparatur ad ipsum esse Angeli. Sed ad eius intelligere comparatur secundum rationem universalioris obiecti, scilicet veri vel entis. Et sic patet quod, licet sit eadem forma, non tamen secundum eandem rationem est principium essendi et intelligendi. Et propter hoc non sequitur quod in Angelo sit idem esse et intelligere.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30689)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod virtus vel potentia intellectiva in Angelo non sit aliud quam sua essentia. Mens enim et intellectus nominant potentiam intellectivam. Sed Dionysius in pluribus locis suorum librorum, nominat ipsos Angelos intellectus et mentes. Ergo Angelus est sua potentia intellectiva.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30690)\n\nPraeterea, si potentia intellectiva in Angelo est aliquid praeter eius essentiam, oportet quod sit accidens, hoc enim dicimus esse accidens alicuius, quod est praeter eius essentiam. *Sed forma simplex subiectum esse non potest*, ut Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin. Ergo Angelus non esset forma simplex, quod est contra praemissa.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30691)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII Confess., quod Deus fecit angelicam naturam prope se, materiam autem primam prope nihil, ex quo videtur quod Angelus sit simplicior quam materia prima, utpote Deo propinquior. Sed materia prima est sua potentia. Ergo multo magis Angelus est sua potentia intellectiva.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30692)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, XI cap. Angel. Hier., quod *Angeli dividuntur in substantiam, virtutem et operationem*. Ergo aliud est in eis substantia, et aliud virtus, et aliud operatio.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30693)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nec in Angelo nec in aliqua creatura, virtus vel potentia operativa est idem quod sua essentia. Quod sic patet. Cum enim potentia dicatur ad actum, oportet quod secundum diversitatem actuum sit diversitas potentiarum, propter quod dicitur quod proprius actus respondet propriae potentiae. In omni autem creato essentia differt a suo esse, et comparatur ad ipsum sicut potentia ad actum, ut ex supra dictis patet. Actus autem ad quem comparatur potentia operativa, est operatio. In Angelo autem non est idem intelligere et esse, nec aliqua alia operatio aut in ipso aut in quocumque alio creato, est idem quod eius esse. Unde essentia Angeli non est eius potentia intellectiva, nec alicuius creati essentia est eius operativa potentia.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30694)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Angelus dicitur intellectus et mens, quia tota eius cognitio est intellectualis. Cognitio autem animae partim est intellectualis, et partim sensitiva.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30695)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod forma simplex quae est actus purus, nullius accidentis potest esse subiectum, quia subiectum comparatur ad accidens ut potentia ad actum. Et huiusmodi est solus Deus. Et de tali forma loquitur ibi Boetius. Forma autem simplex quae non est suum esse, sed comparatur ad ipsum ut potentia ad actum, potest esse subiectum accidentis, et praecipue eius quod consequitur speciem, huiusmodi enim accidens pertinet ad formam (accidens vero quod est individui, non consequens totam speciem, consequitur materiam, quae est individuationis principium). Et talis forma simplex est Angelus.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30696)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod potentia materiae est ad ipsum esse substantiale, et non potentia operativa, sed ad esse accidentale. Unde non est simile.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 54 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30697)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelo sit intellectus agens et possibilis. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod *sicut in omni natura est aliquid quo est omnia fieri, et aliquid quo est omnia facere, ita etiam in anima*. Sed Angelus est natura quaedam. Ergo in eo est intellectus agens et possibilis.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30698)\n\nPraeterea, recipere est proprium intellectus possibilis, illuminare autem est proprium intellectus agentis, ut patet in III de anima. Sed Angelus recipit illuminationem a superiori, et illuminat inferiorem. Ergo in eo est intellectus agens et possibilis.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30699)\n\nSed contra est quod in nobis intellectus agens et possibilis est per comparationem ad phantasmata; quae quidem comparantur ad intellectum possibilem ut colores ad visum, ad intellectum autem agentem ut colores ad lumen, ut patet ex III de anima. Sed hoc non est in Angelo. Ergo in Angelo non est intellectus agens et possibilis.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30700)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necessitas ponendi intellectum possibilem in nobis, fuit propter hoc, quod nos invenimur quandoque intelligentes in potentia et non in actu, unde oportet esse quandam virtutem, quae sit in potentia ad intelligibilia ante ipsum intelligere, sed reducitur in actum eorum cum fit sciens, et ulterius cum fit considerans. Et haec virtus vocatur intellectus possibilis. Necessitas autem ponendi intellectum agentem fuit, quia naturae rerum materialium, quas nos intelligimus, non subsistunt extra animam immateriales et intelligibiles in actu, sed sunt solum intelligibiles in potentia, extra animam existentes, et ideo oportuit esse aliquam virtutem, quae faceret illas naturas intelligibiles actu. Et haec virtus dicitur intellectus agens in nobis. Utraque autem necessitas deest in Angelis. Quia neque sunt quandoque intelligentes in potentia tantum, respectu eorum quae naturaliter intelligunt, neque intelligibilia eorum sunt intelligibilia in potentia, sed in actu; intelligunt enim primo et principaliter res immateriales, ut infra patebit. Et ideo non potest in eis esse intellectus agens et possibilis, nisi aequivoce.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30701)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus intelligit ista duo esse in omni natura in qua contingit esse generari vel fieri, ut ipsa verba demonstrant. In Angelo autem non generatur scientia, sed naturaliter adest. Unde non oportet ponere in eis agens et possibile.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30702)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus agentis est illuminare non quidem alium intelligentem, sed intelligibilia in potentia, inquantum per abstractionem facit ea intelligibilia actu. Ad intellectum autem possibilem pertinet esse in potentia respectu naturalium cognoscibilium, et quandoque fieri actu. Unde quod Angelus illuminat Angelum, non pertinet ad rationem intellectus agentis. Neque ad rationem intellectus possibilis pertinet, quod illuminatur de supernaturalibus mysteriis, ad quae cognoscenda quandoque est in potentia. Si quis autem velit haec vocare intellectum agentem et possibilem, aequivoce dicet, nec de nominibus est curandum.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30703)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit sola intellectiva cognitio. Dicit enim Augustinus, VIII de Civ. Dei, quod in Angelis est vita quae intelligit et sentit. Ergo in eis est potentia sensitiva.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30704)\n\nPraeterea, Isidorus dicit quod Angeli multa noverunt per experientiam. Experientia autem fit ex multis memoriis, ut dicitur in I Metaphys. Ergo in eis est etiam memorativa potentia.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30705)\n\nPraeterea, Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod in Daemonibus est phantasia proterva. Phantasia autem ad vim imaginativam pertinet. Ergo in Daemonibus est vis imaginativa. Et eadem ratione in Angelis, quia sunt eiusdem naturae.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30706)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, in homilia de ascensione, quod *homo sentit cum pecoribus, et intelligit cum Angelis*.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30707)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in anima nostra sunt quaedam vires, quarum operationes per organa corporea exercentur, et huiusmodi vires sunt actus quarundam partium corporis, sicut est visus in oculo, et auditus in aure. Quaedam vero vires animae nostrae sunt, quarum operationes per organa corporea non exercentur, ut intellectus et voluntas, et huiusmodi non sunt actus aliquarum partium corporis. Angeli autem non habent corpora sibi naturaliter unita, ut ex supra dictis patet. Unde de viribus animae non possunt eis competere nisi intellectus et voluntas. Et hoc etiam Commentator dicit, XII Metaphys., quod substantiae separatae dividuntur in intellectum et voluntatem. Et hoc convenit ordini universi, ut suprema creatura intellectualis sit totaliter intellectiva; et non secundum partem, ut anima nostra. Et propter hoc etiam Angeli vocantur intellectus et mentes, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 54 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30708)\n\nAd ea vero quae in contrarium obiiciuntur, potest dupliciter responderi. Uno modo, quod auctoritates illae loquuntur secundum opinionem illorum qui posuerunt Angelos et Daemones habere corpora naturaliter sibi unita. Qua opinione frequenter Augustinus in libris suis utitur, licet eam asserere non intendat, unde dicit, XXI de Civ. Dei, quod *super hac inquisitione non est multum laborandum*. Alio modo potest dici, quod auctoritates illae, et consimiles, sunt intelligendae per quandam similitudinem. Quia cum sensus certam apprehensionem habeat de proprio sensibili, est in usu loquentium ut etiam secundum certam apprehensionem intellectus aliquid sentire dicamur. Unde etiam sententia nominatur. Experientia vero Angelis attribui potest per similitudinem cognitorum, etsi non per similitudinem virtutis cognoscitivae. Est enim in nobis experientia, dum singularia per sensum cognoscimus, Angeli autem singularia cognoscunt, ut infra patebit, sed non per sensum. Sed tamen memoria in Angelis potest poni, secundum quod ab Augustino ponitur in mente; licet non possit eis competere secundum quod ponitur pars animae sensitivae. Similiter dicendum quod phantasia proterva attribuitur Daemonibus, ex eo quod habent falsam practicam existimationem de vero bono, deceptio autem in nobis proprie fit secundum phantasiam, per quam interdum similitudinibus rerum inhaeremus sicut rebus ipsis, ut patet in dormientibus et amentibus.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0002","text":"[I q. 54 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0003","text":"Consideratis his quae ad substantiam Angeli pertinent, procedendum est ad cognitionem ipsius. Haec autem consideratio erit quadripartita, nam primo considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad virtutem cognoscitivam Angeli; secundo, de his quae pertinent ad medium cognoscendi ipsius; tertio, de his quae ab eo cognoscuntur; quarto, de modo cognitionis ipsorum. Circa primum quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum intelligere Angeli sit sua substantia. Secundo, utrum eius esse sit suum intelligere. Tertio, utrum eius substantia sit sua virtus intellectiva. Quarto, utrum in Angelis sit intellectus agens et possibilis. Quinto, utrum in eis sit aliqua alia potentia cognoscitiva quam intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0005","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intelligere Angeli sit eius substantia. Angelus enim est sublimior et simplicior quam intellectus agens animae. Sed substantia intellectus agentis est sua actio; ut patet in III de anima per Aristotelem, et eius Commentatorem. Ergo, multo fortius, substantia Angeli est sua actio, quae est intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0007","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0008","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in XII Metaphys., quod actio intellectus est vita. Sed cum vivere sit esse viventibus, ut dicitur in II de anima, videtur quod vita sit essentia. Ergo actio intellectus est essentia intelligentis Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0009","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0010","text":"Praeterea, si extrema sunt unum, medium non differt ab eis, quia extremum magis distat ab extremo, quam medium. Sed in Angelo idem est intellectus et intellectum, ad minus inquantum intelligit essentiam suam. Ergo intelligere, quod cadit medium inter intellectum et rem intellectam, est idem cum substantia Angeli intelligentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0011","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0012","text":"Sed contra, plus differt actio rei a substantia eius, quam ipsum esse eius. Sed nullius creati suum esse est sua substantia, hoc enim solius Dei proprium est, ut ex superioribus patet. Ergo neque Angeli, neque alterius creaturae, sua actio est eius substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0013","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est quod actio Angeli, vel cuiuscumque alterius creaturae, sit eius substantia. Actio enim est proprie actualitas virtutis; sicut esse est actualitas substantiae vel essentiae. Impossibile est autem quod aliquid quod non est purus actus, sed aliquid habet de potentia admixtum, sit sua actualitas, quia actualitas potentialitati repugnat. Solus autem Deus est actus purus. Unde in solo Deo sua substantia est suum esse et suum agere. Praeterea, si intelligere Angeli esset sua substantia, oporteret quod intelligere Angeli esset subsistens. Intelligere autem subsistens non potest esse nisi unum; sicut nec aliquod abstractum subsistens. Unde unius Angeli substantia non distingueretur neque a substantia Dei, quae est ipsum intelligere subsistens; neque a substantia alterius Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0015","text":"Si etiam Angelus ipse esset suum intelligere, non possent esse gradus in intelligendo perfectius et minus perfecte, cum hoc contingat propter diversam participationem ipsius intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0016","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur quod intellectus agens est sua actio, est praedicatio non per essentiam, sed per concomitantiam, quia cum sit in actu eius substantia, statim quantum est in se, concomitatur ipsam actio. Quod non est de intellectu possibili, qui non habet actiones nisi postquam fuerit factus in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0018","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod vita non hoc modo se habet ad vivere, sicut essentia ad esse; sed sicut cursus ad currere, quorum unum significat actum in abstracto, aliud in concreto. Unde non sequitur si vivere sit esse, quod vita sit essentia. Quamvis etiam quandoque vita pro essentia ponatur; secundum quod Augustinus dicit, in libro de Trin., quod *memoria et intelligentia et voluntas sunt una essentia, una vita*. Sed sic non accipitur a philosopho cum dicit quod *actio intellectus est vita*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0020","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod actio quae transit in aliquid extrinsecum, est realiter media inter agens et subiectum recipiens actionem. Sed actio quae manet in agente, non est realiter medium inter agens et obiectum, sed secundum modum significandi tantum, realiter vero consequitur unionem obiecti cum agente. Ex hoc enim quod intellectum fit unum cum intelligente, consequitur intelligere, quasi quidam effectus differens ab utroque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0023","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intelligere Angeli sit eius esse. Vivere enim viventibus est esse, ut dicitur in II de anima. Sed intelligere est quoddam vivere, ut in eodem dicitur. Ergo intelligere Angeli est eius esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0025","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0026","text":"Praeterea, sicut se habet causa ad causam, ita effectus ad effectum. Sed forma per quam Angelus est, est eadem cum forma per quam intelligit ad minus seipsum. Ergo eius intelligere est idem cum suo esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0027","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0028","text":"Sed contra, intelligere Angeli est motus eius; ut patet per Dionysium, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Sed esse non est motus. Ergo esse Angeli non est intelligere eius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0029","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0030","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod actio Angeli non est eius esse, neque actio alicuius creaturae. Duplex enim est actionis genus, ut dicitur IX Metaphys. Una scilicet actio est quae transit in aliquid exterius, inferens ei passionem, sicut urere et secare. Alia vero actio est quae non transit in rem exteriorem, sed manet in ipso agente, sicut sentire, intelligere et velle, per huiusmodi enim actionem non immutatur aliquid extrinsecum, sed totum in ipso agente agitur. De prima ergo actione manifestum est quod non potest esse ipsum esse agentis, nam esse agentis significatur intra ipsum, actio autem talis est effluxus in actum ab agente. Secunda autem actio de sui ratione habet infinitatem, vel simpliciter, vel secundum quid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0031","text":"Simpliciter quidem, sicut intelligere, cuius obiectum est verum, et velle, cuius obiectum est bonum, quorum utrumque convertitur cum ente; et ita intelligere et velle, quantum est de se, habent se ad omnia; et utrumque recipit speciem ab obiecto. Secundum quid autem infinitum est sentire, quod se habet ad omnia sensibilia, sicut visus ad omnia visibilia. Esse autem cuiuslibet creaturae est determinatum ad unum secundum genus et speciem, esse autem solius Dei est simpliciter infinitum, in se omnia comprehendens, ut dicit Dionysius, V cap. de Div. Nom. Unde solum esse divinum est suum intelligere et suum velle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0032","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0033","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod vivere quandoque sumitur pro ipso esse viventis, quandoque vero pro operatione vitae, idest per quam demonstratur aliquid esse vivens. Et hoc modo philosophus dicit quod intelligere est vivere quoddam, ibi enim distinguit diversos gradus viventium secundum diversa opera vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0034","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0035","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ipsa essentia Angeli est ratio totius sui esse, non autem est ratio totius sui intelligere, quia non omnia intelligere potest per suam essentiam. Et ideo secundum propriam rationem, inquantum est talis essentia, comparatur ad ipsum esse Angeli. Sed ad eius intelligere comparatur secundum rationem universalioris obiecti, scilicet veri vel entis. Et sic patet quod, licet sit eadem forma, non tamen secundum eandem rationem est principium essendi et intelligendi. Et propter hoc non sequitur quod in Angelo sit idem esse et intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0036","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0037","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0038","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod virtus vel potentia intellectiva in Angelo non sit aliud quam sua essentia. Mens enim et intellectus nominant potentiam intellectivam. Sed Dionysius in pluribus locis suorum librorum, nominat ipsos Angelos intellectus et mentes. Ergo Angelus est sua potentia intellectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0039","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0040","text":"Praeterea, si potentia intellectiva in Angelo est aliquid praeter eius essentiam, oportet quod sit accidens, hoc enim dicimus esse accidens alicuius, quod est praeter eius essentiam. *Sed forma simplex subiectum esse non potest*, ut Boetius dicit, in libro de Trin. Ergo Angelus non esset forma simplex, quod est contra praemissa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0041","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0042","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII Confess., quod Deus fecit angelicam naturam prope se, materiam autem primam prope nihil, ex quo videtur quod Angelus sit simplicior quam materia prima, utpote Deo propinquior. Sed materia prima est sua potentia. Ergo multo magis Angelus est sua potentia intellectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0043","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0044","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, XI cap. Angel. Hier., quod *Angeli dividuntur in substantiam, virtutem et operationem*. Ergo aliud est in eis substantia, et aliud virtus, et aliud operatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0045","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0046","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nec in Angelo nec in aliqua creatura, virtus vel potentia operativa est idem quod sua essentia. Quod sic patet. Cum enim potentia dicatur ad actum, oportet quod secundum diversitatem actuum sit diversitas potentiarum, propter quod dicitur quod proprius actus respondet propriae potentiae. In omni autem creato essentia differt a suo esse, et comparatur ad ipsum sicut potentia ad actum, ut ex supra dictis patet. Actus autem ad quem comparatur potentia operativa, est operatio. In Angelo autem non est idem intelligere et esse, nec aliqua alia operatio aut in ipso aut in quocumque alio creato, est idem quod eius esse. Unde essentia Angeli non est eius potentia intellectiva, nec alicuius creati essentia est eius operativa potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0047","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0048","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Angelus dicitur intellectus et mens, quia tota eius cognitio est intellectualis. Cognitio autem animae partim est intellectualis, et partim sensitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0049","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0050","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod forma simplex quae est actus purus, nullius accidentis potest esse subiectum, quia subiectum comparatur ad accidens ut potentia ad actum. Et huiusmodi est solus Deus. Et de tali forma loquitur ibi Boetius. Forma autem simplex quae non est suum esse, sed comparatur ad ipsum ut potentia ad actum, potest esse subiectum accidentis, et praecipue eius quod consequitur speciem, huiusmodi enim accidens pertinet ad formam (accidens vero quod est individui, non consequens totam speciem, consequitur materiam, quae est individuationis principium). Et talis forma simplex est Angelus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0051","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0052","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod potentia materiae est ad ipsum esse substantiale, et non potentia operativa, sed ad esse accidentale. Unde non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0053","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0054","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0055","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelo sit intellectus agens et possibilis. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod *sicut in omni natura est aliquid quo est omnia fieri, et aliquid quo est omnia facere, ita etiam in anima*. Sed Angelus est natura quaedam. Ergo in eo est intellectus agens et possibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0056","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0057","text":"Praeterea, recipere est proprium intellectus possibilis, illuminare autem est proprium intellectus agentis, ut patet in III de anima. Sed Angelus recipit illuminationem a superiori, et illuminat inferiorem. Ergo in eo est intellectus agens et possibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0058","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0059","text":"Sed contra est quod in nobis intellectus agens et possibilis est per comparationem ad phantasmata; quae quidem comparantur ad intellectum possibilem ut colores ad visum, ad intellectum autem agentem ut colores ad lumen, ut patet ex III de anima. Sed hoc non est in Angelo. Ergo in Angelo non est intellectus agens et possibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0060","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0061","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necessitas ponendi intellectum possibilem in nobis, fuit propter hoc, quod nos invenimur quandoque intelligentes in potentia et non in actu, unde oportet esse quandam virtutem, quae sit in potentia ad intelligibilia ante ipsum intelligere, sed reducitur in actum eorum cum fit sciens, et ulterius cum fit considerans. Et haec virtus vocatur intellectus possibilis. Necessitas autem ponendi intellectum agentem fuit, quia naturae rerum materialium, quas nos intelligimus, non subsistunt extra animam immateriales et intelligibiles in actu, sed sunt solum intelligibiles in potentia, extra animam existentes, et ideo oportuit esse aliquam virtutem, quae faceret illas naturas intelligibiles actu. Et haec virtus dicitur intellectus agens in nobis. Utraque autem necessitas deest in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0062","text":"Quia neque sunt quandoque intelligentes in potentia tantum, respectu eorum quae naturaliter intelligunt, neque intelligibilia eorum sunt intelligibilia in potentia, sed in actu; intelligunt enim primo et principaliter res immateriales, ut infra patebit. Et ideo non potest in eis esse intellectus agens et possibilis, nisi aequivoce.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0063","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0064","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus intelligit ista duo esse in omni natura in qua contingit esse generari vel fieri, ut ipsa verba demonstrant. In Angelo autem non generatur scientia, sed naturaliter adest. Unde non oportet ponere in eis agens et possibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0065","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0066","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus agentis est illuminare non quidem alium intelligentem, sed intelligibilia in potentia, inquantum per abstractionem facit ea intelligibilia actu. Ad intellectum autem possibilem pertinet esse in potentia respectu naturalium cognoscibilium, et quandoque fieri actu. Unde quod Angelus illuminat Angelum, non pertinet ad rationem intellectus agentis. Neque ad rationem intellectus possibilis pertinet, quod illuminatur de supernaturalibus mysteriis, ad quae cognoscenda quandoque est in potentia. Si quis autem velit haec vocare intellectum agentem et possibilem, aequivoce dicet, nec de nominibus est curandum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0067","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0068","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0069","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit sola intellectiva cognitio. Dicit enim Augustinus, VIII de Civ. Dei, quod in Angelis est vita quae intelligit et sentit. Ergo in eis est potentia sensitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0070","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0071","text":"Praeterea, Isidorus dicit quod Angeli multa noverunt per experientiam. Experientia autem fit ex multis memoriis, ut dicitur in I Metaphys. Ergo in eis est etiam memorativa potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0072","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0073","text":"Praeterea, Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod in Daemonibus est phantasia proterva. Phantasia autem ad vim imaginativam pertinet. Ergo in Daemonibus est vis imaginativa. Et eadem ratione in Angelis, quia sunt eiusdem naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0074","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0075","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, in homilia de ascensione, quod *homo sentit cum pecoribus, et intelligit cum Angelis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0076","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in anima nostra sunt quaedam vires, quarum operationes per organa corporea exercentur, et huiusmodi vires sunt actus quarundam partium corporis, sicut est visus in oculo, et auditus in aure. Quaedam vero vires animae nostrae sunt, quarum operationes per organa corporea non exercentur, ut intellectus et voluntas, et huiusmodi non sunt actus aliquarum partium corporis. Angeli autem non habent corpora sibi naturaliter unita, ut ex supra dictis patet. Unde de viribus animae non possunt eis competere nisi intellectus et voluntas. Et hoc etiam Commentator dicit, XII Metaphys., quod substantiae separatae dividuntur in intellectum et voluntatem. Et hoc convenit ordini universi, ut suprema creatura intellectualis sit totaliter intellectiva; et non secundum partem, ut anima nostra. Et propter hoc etiam Angeli vocantur intellectus et mentes, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0078","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0079","text":"Ad ea vero quae in contrarium obiiciuntur, potest dupliciter responderi. Uno modo, quod auctoritates illae loquuntur secundum opinionem illorum qui posuerunt Angelos et Daemones habere corpora naturaliter sibi unita. Qua opinione frequenter Augustinus in libris suis utitur, licet eam asserere non intendat, unde dicit, XXI de Civ. Dei, quod *super hac inquisitione non est multum laborandum*. Alio modo potest dici, quod auctoritates illae, et consimiles, sunt intelligendae per quandam similitudinem. Quia cum sensus certam apprehensionem habeat de proprio sensibili, est in usu loquentium ut etiam secundum certam apprehensionem intellectus aliquid sentire dicamur. Unde etiam sententia nominatur. Experientia vero Angelis attribui potest per similitudinem cognitorum, etsi non per similitudinem virtutis cognoscitivae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0080","text":"Est enim in nobis experientia, dum singularia per sensum cognoscimus, Angeli autem singularia cognoscunt, ut infra patebit, sed non per sensum. Sed tamen memoria in Angelis potest poni, secundum quod ab Augustino ponitur in mente; licet non possit eis competere secundum quod ponitur pars animae sensitivae. Similiter dicendum quod phantasia proterva attribuitur Daemonibus, ex eo quod habent falsam practicam existimationem de vero bono, deceptio autem in nobis proprie fit secundum phantasiam, per quam interdum similitudinibus rerum inhaeremus sicut rebus ipsis, ut patet in dormientibus et amentibus.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"71e9ba6a41732220eb6d9687db049cf3b47e6e227ebdcbcc95cd86fdbcd7c8de","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143856949121Z_b9139cac","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 54","source_sha256":"71e9ba6a41732220eb6d9687db049cf3b47e6e227ebdcbcc95cd86fdbcd7c8de","sha256":"16fb4ca8f4381a249a877d324863d10bf252dc22b3fc5df7bb8088d8a83ccdeb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:38:56.949150+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0002","text":"[I q. 54 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0003","text":"Having considered those things that pertain to the substance of the angel, we must proceed to knowledge of it. This consideration will be fourfold: for first, those things that pertain to the angel’s cognitive power must be considered; second, those that pertain to its means of knowing; third, those things that are known by it; fourth, the mode of knowing them. Concerning the first, five questions are asked. First, whether an angel’s understanding is its substance. Second, whether its being is its understanding. Third, whether its substance is its intellectual power. Fourth, whether in angels there are an active and a possible intellect. Fifth, whether there is in them any cognitive power other than intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0005","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that an angel’s understanding is its substance. For an angel is higher and simpler than the active intellect of the soul. But the substance of the active intellect is its action, as is clear in Aristotle and his Commentator, *On the Soul* III. Therefore, much more strongly, the substance of an angel is its action, which is understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0007","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0008","text":"Further, the Philosopher says, in *Metaphysics* XII, that the action of intellect is life. But since for living things to live is to be, as is said in *On the Soul* II, it seems that life is essence. Therefore the action of intellect is the essence of the understanding angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0009","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0010","text":"Further, if the extremes are one, the middle does not differ from them, because an extreme is more distant from an extreme than the middle is. But in an angel intellect and the understood are the same, at least insofar as it understands its own essence. Therefore understanding, which falls between intellect and the thing understood, is the same as the substance of the understanding angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0011","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the action of a thing differs more from its substance than its very being does. But the being of no created thing is its substance; for this is proper to God alone, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore neither an angel’s action nor that of any other creature is its substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0013","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible for the action of an angel, or of any other creature whatsoever, to be its substance. For action is properly the actuality of a power, just as being is the actuality of substance or essence. But it is impossible for anything which is not pure act, but has something of potentiality mixed with it, to be its own actuality, because actuality is opposed to potentiality. Now God alone is pure act. Hence, in God alone, His substance is His being and His acting. Further, if an angel’s understanding were its substance, the angel’s understanding would have to be subsistent. But subsistent understanding can be only one, just as neither can any subsistent abstract thing. Hence the substance of one angel would be distinguished neither from the substance of God, which is subsistent understanding itself, nor from the substance of another angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0015","text":"Moreover, if the angel itself were its own understanding, there could not be degrees in understanding more perfectly and less perfectly, since this comes about through a diverse participation in understanding itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0016","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, when it is said that the active intellect is its action, this is predication not through essence but through concomitance, because, since its substance is in act, action immediately accompanies it, so far as it is in itself. This is not so of the possible intellect, which has no actions except after it has been made actual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0018","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0019","text":"To the second it must be said that life is not related to living in the way essence is related to being, but as running is to run: one of these signifies the act in the abstract, the other in the concrete. Hence it does not follow that, if to live is to be, life is essence. Although life is sometimes also posited in place of essence, according as Augustine says, in the book *On the Trinity*, that *memory and intelligence and will are one essence, one life*. But the Philosopher does not take it in this way when he says that *the action of intellect is life*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0020","text":"[I q. 54 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0021","text":"To the third it must be said that an action which passes into something extrinsic is really intermediate between the agent and the subject receiving the action. But an action which remains in the agent is not really intermediate between the agent and the object, but only according to the mode of signifying; in reality, however, it follows upon the union of the object with the agent. For from the fact that the understood becomes one with the one understanding, understanding follows, as a certain effect differing from both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0023","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that an angel’s understanding is its being. For for living things to live is to be, as is said in *On the Soul* II. But understanding is a kind of living, as is said in the same place. Therefore an angel’s understanding is its being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0025","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0026","text":"Moreover, as cause is related to cause, so effect is related to effect. But the form by which an angel exists is the same as the form by which it understands, at least itself. Therefore its understanding is the same as its being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0027","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0028","text":"On the contrary, an angel's understanding is its motion, as is clear from Dionysius, chapter 4 of *On the Divine Names*. But being is not motion. Therefore an angel's being is not its understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0029","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0030","text":"I answer that an angel's action is not its being, nor is the action of any creature. For there are two kinds of action, as is said in *Metaphysics* IX. One action, namely, passes into something external, bringing about a passion in it, as burning and cutting. But another action does not pass into an external thing, but remains in the agent itself, as sensing, understanding, and willing; for by such an action nothing extrinsic is changed, but the whole is done in the agent itself. Therefore, regarding the first action, it is manifest that it cannot be the very being of the agent, for the being of the agent is signified as within it, whereas such an action is an efflux into act from the agent. But the second action has infinity by its very nature, either simply or in a certain respect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0031","text":"Simply, indeed, as understanding, whose object is the true, and willing, whose object is the good, both of which are convertible with being; and thus understanding and willing, so far as they are concerned, are related to all things, and each receives its species from the object. But sensing is infinite in a certain respect, since it is related to all sensible things, as sight to all visible things. But the being of every creature is determined to one thing according to genus and species, whereas the being of God alone is simply infinite, comprehending all things in itself, as Dionysius says in chapter 5 of *On the Divine Names*. Hence only the divine being is its own understanding and its own willing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0032","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0033","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that living is sometimes taken for the very being of the living thing, but sometimes for the operation of life, that is, that through which something is shown to be living. And in this way the Philosopher says that understanding is a certain kind of living; for there he distinguishes the different grades of living things according to the different works of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0034","text":"[I q. 54 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0035","text":"To the second, it must be said that the very essence of an angel is the principle of its whole being, but it is not the principle of its whole understanding, because it cannot understand all things through its essence. And therefore, according to its proper account, insofar as it is such an essence, it is related to the angel's very being. But it is related to its understanding according to the account of a more universal object, namely, the true or being. Thus it is clear that, although it is the same form, nevertheless it is not according to the same account the principle of being and of understanding. And on this account it does not follow that in an angel being and understanding are the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0036","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0037","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0038","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the intellectual power or potency in an angel is not other than its essence. For mind and intellect name the intellectual power. But Dionysius, in several places in his books, calls the angels themselves intellects and minds. Therefore an angel is its intellectual power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0039","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0040","text":"Moreover, if the intellectual power in an angel is something besides its essence, it must be an accident; for that which is besides a thing's essence, we say, is its accident. *But a simple form cannot be a subject*, as Boethius says in the book *On the Trinity*. Therefore an angel would not be a simple form, which is contrary to what was set forth above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0041","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0042","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, in *Confessions* XII, that God made the angelic nature close to Himself, but prime matter close to nothing; from which it seems that an angel is simpler than prime matter, as being nearer to God. But prime matter is its own potency. Therefore, much more is an angel its own intellectual power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0043","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0044","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter 11 of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, that *angels are divided into substance, power, and operation*. Therefore in them substance is one thing, power another, and operation another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0045","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0046","text":"I answer that neither in an angel nor in any creature is operative power or potency the same as its essence. This is clear as follows. For since potency is spoken of in relation to act, there must, according to the diversity of acts, be a diversity of potencies; on which account it is said that a proper act corresponds to a proper potency. But in every created thing essence differs from its being, and is related to it as potency to act, as is clear from what has been said above. But the act to which an operative potency is related is operation. Now in an angel understanding and being are not the same, nor is any other operation, whether in it or in any other created thing, the same as its being. Hence an angel's essence is not its intellectual potency, nor is the essence of any created thing its operative potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0047","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0048","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an angel is called intellect and mind because all its cognition is intellectual. But the cognition of the soul is partly intellectual and partly sensitive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0049","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0050","text":"To the second, it must be said that a simple form which is pure act cannot be the subject of any accident, because a subject is related to an accident as potency to act. And only God is such. And Boethius is speaking there of such a form. But a simple form which is not its own being, but is related to being itself as potency to act, can be the subject of an accident, and especially of that which follows upon the species; for an accident of this sort belongs to the form (whereas an accident which belongs to the individual, not following upon the whole species, follows matter, which is the principle of individuation). And an angel is such a simple form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0051","text":"[I q. 54 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0052","text":"To the third, it must be said that the potency of matter is toward substantial being itself, and not operative potency, but [is] toward accidental being. Hence there is no likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0053","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0054","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0055","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that in an angel there are an agent and a possible intellect. For the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that *as in every nature there is something by which all things come to be, and something by which all things are made, so also in the soul*. But an angel is a certain nature. Therefore, in it there are an agent and a possible intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0056","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0057","text":"Moreover, receiving is proper to the possible intellect, whereas illuminating is proper to the agent intellect, as is clear in *On the Soul* III. But an angel receives illumination from a superior [angel], and illuminates an inferior [angel]. Therefore, in it there are an agent and a possible intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0058","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0059","text":"But on the contrary, in us the agent and possible intellect are [so] in relation to phantasms; these are related to the possible intellect as colors to sight, but to the agent intellect as colors to light, as is clear from *On the Soul* III. But this is not so in an angel. Therefore, in an angel there are no agent and possible intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0060","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0061","text":"I answer that it must be said that the necessity of positing a possible intellect in us arose from this: that we are found sometimes to understand potentially and not actually. Hence there must be a certain power which is in potency to intelligibles before actual understanding itself, but is reduced to their act when it becomes knowing, and further when it becomes considering. And this power is called the possible intellect. But the necessity of positing an agent intellect arose because the natures of material things, which we understand, do not subsist outside the soul as immaterial and actually intelligible, but, existing outside the soul, are intelligible only potentially; and therefore there had to be some power which would make those natures actually intelligible. And this power is called the agent intellect in us. But both necessities are absent in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0062","text":"For neither are they sometimes understanding only potentially with respect to those things which they understand naturally, nor are their intelligibles intelligible potentially, but actually; for they understand immaterial things first and principally, as will be clear below. And therefore there cannot be in them an agent and a possible intellect, except equivocally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0063","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0064","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher understands these two to be in every nature in which it can happen that there is generation or coming-to-be, as the words themselves show. But in an angel knowledge is not generated, but is naturally present. Hence there is no need to posit in them an agent and a possible [intellect].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0065","text":"[I q. 54 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0066","text":"To the second, it must be said that it belongs to the agent intellect to illuminate, not indeed another intellect, but intelligibles in potency, insofar as by abstraction it makes them actually intelligible. But it belongs to the possible intellect to be in potency with respect to things naturally knowable, and sometimes to become actual. Hence the fact that an angel illuminates an angel does not belong to the character of the agent intellect. Nor does the fact that it is illuminated concerning supernatural mysteries, for knowing which it is sometimes in potency, belong to the character of the possible intellect. But if anyone wishes to call these the agent and possible intellect, he will speak equivocally; and there is no need to be concerned about names.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0067","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0068","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0069","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that in angels there is not only intellectual cognition. For Augustine says, in *The City of God* VIII, that in angels there is a life which understands and senses. Therefore, there is in them a sensitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0070","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0071","text":"Moreover, Isidore says that angels know many things through experience. But experience comes about from many memories, as is said in *Metaphysics* I. Therefore, there is also in them a memorative power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0072","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0073","text":"Moreover, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that in demons there is perverse imagination. But imagination pertains to the imaginative power. Therefore, in demons there is an imaginative power. And by the same reasoning in angels, because they are of the same nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0074","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0075","text":"But on the contrary, Gregory says, in the homily on the Ascension, that *man senses with the beasts and understands with the angels*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0076","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0077","text":"I answer that it must be said that in our soul there are certain powers whose operations are exercised through bodily organs, and powers of this sort are acts of certain parts of the body, as sight is in the eye and hearing in the ear. But there are certain powers of our soul whose operations are not exercised through bodily organs, such as intellect and will; and powers of this sort are not acts of any parts of the body. Angels, however, do not have bodies naturally united to them, as is clear from what has been said above. Hence, of the powers of the soul, only intellect and will can belong to them. And the Commentator also says this, in XII *Metaphysics*, namely, that separate substances are divided into intellect and will. And this befits the order of the universe: that the highest intellectual creature should be wholly intellectual, and not intellectual only in part, as our soul is. And for this reason angels are also called intellects and minds, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0078","text":"[I q. 54 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0079","text":"To those things, however, which are objected to the contrary, a twofold answer can be given. In one way, that those authorities speak according to the opinion of those who held that angels and demons have bodies naturally united to them. Augustine frequently employs this opinion in his books, although he does not intend to assert it; hence he says, in XXI *On the City of God*, that *one should not labor much over this inquiry*. In another way, it can be said that those authorities, and similar ones, are to be understood by a certain likeness. For since sense has a determinate apprehension of its proper sensible object, it is customary in speech that we too are said to sense something according to a determinate apprehension of the intellect. Hence, a judgment is also called a “sensing.” Experience, moreover, can be attributed to angels by a likeness in the things known, although not by a likeness in the cognitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0080","text":"For in us there is experience when we know singulars through sense; angels, however, know singulars, as will be clear below, but not through sense. Yet memory can be posited in angels according as it is posited by Augustine in the mind, although it cannot belong to them according as it is posited as a part of the sensitive soul. Likewise, it must be said that perverse imagination is attributed to demons from the fact that they have a false practical estimation concerning the true good; but deception in us properly occurs according to imagination, through which we sometimes cling to likenesses of things as though to the things themselves, as is clear in sleepers and the insane.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_46:P0080"]}],"source_digest":"beddf82da295d478285cab09bbb74f652a5ee6a978bc3cb588e928d143cfa109","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 54"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 54","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47","title":"First Part · Question 55","order":56,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_47","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 55 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30709)\n\nConsequenter quaeritur de medio cognitionis angelicae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angeli cognoscant omnia per suam substantiam, vel per aliquas species. Secundo, si per species, utrum per species connaturales, vel per species a rebus acceptas. Tertio, utrum Angeli superiores cognoscant per species magis universales, quam inferiores.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30710)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli cognoscant omnia per suam substantiam. Dicit enim Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli sciunt ea quae sunt in terra, secundum propriam naturam mentis*. Sed natura Angeli est eius essentia. Ergo Angelus per suam essentiam res cognoscit.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30711)\n\nPraeterea, secundum philosophum, in XII Metaphys., et in III de anima, *in his quae sunt sine materia, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur*. Id autem quod intelligitur est idem intelligenti ratione eius quo intelligitur. Ergo in his quae sunt sine materia, sicut sunt Angeli, id quo intelligitur est ipsa substantia intelligentis.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30712)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est in altero, est in eo per modum eius in quo est. Sed Angelus habet naturam intellectualem. Ergo quidquid est in ipso, est in eo per modum intelligibilem. Sed omnia sunt in eo, quia inferiora in entibus sunt in superioribus essentialiter, superiora vero sunt in inferioribus participative; et ideo dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod Deus tota in totis congregat, idest omnia in omnibus. Ergo Angelus omnia in sua substantia cognoscit.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30713)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, in eodem capite, quod Angeli illuminantur rationibus rerum. Ergo cognoscunt per rationes rerum, et non per propriam substantiam.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30714)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod illud quo intellectus intelligit, comparatur ad intellectum intelligentem ut forma eius, quia forma est quo agens agit. Oportet autem, ad hoc quod potentia perfecte compleatur per formam, quod omnia contineantur sub forma, ad quae potentia se extendit. Et inde est quod in rebus corruptibilibus forma non perfecte complet potentiam materiae, quia potentia materiae ad plura se extendit quam sit continentia formae huius vel illius. Potentia autem intellectiva Angeli se extendit ad intelligendum omnia, quia obiectum intellectus est ens vel verum commune. Ipsa autem essentia Angeli non comprehendit in se omnia, cum sit essentia determinata ad genus et ad speciem. Hoc autem proprium est essentiae divinae, quae infinita est, ut in se simpliciter omnia comprehendat perfecte. Et ideo solus Deus cognoscit omnia per suam essentiam. Angelus autem per suam essentiam non potest omnia cognoscere; sed oportet intellectum eius aliquibus speciebus perfici ad res cognoscendas.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30715)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur Angelum secundum suam naturam res cognoscere, ly secundum non determinat medium cognitionis, quod est similitudo cogniti; sed virtutem cognoscitivam, quae convenit Angelo secundum suam naturam.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30716)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut sensus in actu est sensibile in actu, ut dicitur in III de anima, non ita quod ipsa vis sensitiva sit ipsa similitudo sensibilis quae est in sensu, sed quia ex utroque fit unum sicut ex actu et potentia; ita et intellectus in actu dicitur esse intellectum in actu, non quod substantia intellectus sit ipsa similitudo per quam intelligit, sed quia illa similitudo est forma eius. Idem est autem quod dicitur, in his quae sunt sine materia, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur, ac si diceretur quod intellectus in actu est intellectum in actu, ex hoc enim aliquid est intellectum in actu quod est immateriale.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30717)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ea quae sunt infra Angelum, et ea quae sunt supra ipsum, sunt quodammodo in substantia eius, non quidem perfecte, neque secundum propriam rationem, cum Angeli essentia, finita existens, secundum propriam rationem ab aliis distinguatur; sed secundum quandam rationem communem. In essentia autem Dei sunt omnia perfecte et secundum propriam rationem, sicut in prima et universali virtute operativa, a qua procedit quidquid est in quacumque re vel proprium vel commune. Et ideo Deus per essentiam suam habet propriam cognitionem de rebus omnibus, non autem Angelus, sed solam communem.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30718)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli intelligant per species a rebus acceptas. Omne enim quod intelligitur, per aliquam sui similitudinem in intelligente intelligitur. Similitudo autem alicuius in altero existens, aut est ibi per modum exemplaris, ita quod illa similitudo sit causa rei, aut est ibi per modum imaginis, ita quod sit causata a re. Oportet igitur quod omnis scientia intelligentis vel sit causa rei intellectae, vel causata a re. Sed scientia Angeli non est causa rerum existentium in natura, sed sola divina scientia. Ergo oportet quod species per quas intelligit intellectus angelicus, sint a rebus acceptae.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30719)\n\nPraeterea, lumen angelicum est fortius quam lumen intellectus agentis in anima. Sed lumen intellectus agentis abstrahit species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus. Ergo lumen intellectus angelici potest abstrahere species etiam ab ipsis rebus sensibilibus. Et ita nihil prohibet dicere quod Angelus intelligat per species a rebus acceptas.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30720)\n\nPraeterea, species quae sunt in intellectu, indifferenter se habent ad praesens et distans, nisi quatenus a rebus sensibilibus accipiuntur. Si ergo Angelus non intelligit per species a rebus acceptas, eius cognitio indifferenter se haberet ad propinqua et distantia, et ita frustra secundum locum moveretur.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30721)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli non congregant divinam cognitionem a rebus divisibilibus, aut a sensibilibus*.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30722)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod species per quas Angeli intelligunt, non sunt a rebus acceptae, sed eis connaturales. Sic enim oportet intelligere distinctionem et ordinem spiritualium substantiarum, sicut est distinctio et ordo corporalium. Suprema autem corpora habent potentiam in sui natura totaliter perfectam per formam, in corporibus autem inferioribus potentia materiae non totaliter perficitur per formam, sed accipit nunc unam, nunc aliam formam, ab aliquo agente. Similiter et inferiores substantiae intellectivae, scilicet animae humanae, habent potentiam intellectivam non completam naturaliter; sed completur in eis successive, per hoc quod accipiunt species intelligibiles a rebus. Potentia vero intellectiva in substantiis spiritualibus superioribus, idest in Angelis, naturaliter completa est per species intelligibiles, inquantum habent species intelligibiles connaturales ad omnia intelligenda quae naturaliter cognoscere possunt. Et hoc etiam ex ipso modo essendi huiusmodi substantiarum apparet. Substantiae enim spirituales inferiores, scilicet animae, habent esse affine corpori, inquantum sunt corporum formae, et ideo ex ipso modo essendi competit eis ut a corporibus, et per corpora suam perfectionem intelligibilem consequantur, alioquin frustra corporibus unirentur. Substantiae vero superiores, idest Angeli, sunt a corporibus totaliter absolutae, immaterialiter et in esse intelligibili subsistentes, et ideo suam perfectionem intelligibilem consequuntur per intelligibilem effluxum, quo a Deo species rerum cognitarum acceperunt simul cum intellectuali natura. Unde Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt., quod *cetera, quae infra Angelos sunt, ita creantur, ut prius fiant in cognitione rationalis creaturae, ac deinde in genere suo*.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30723)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in mente Angeli sunt similitudines creaturarum, non quidem ab ipsis creaturis acceptae, sed a Deo, qui est creaturarum causa, et in quo primo similitudines rerum existunt. Unde Augustinus dicit, in eodem libro, quod *sicut ratio qua creatura conditur, prius est in verbo Dei quam ipsa creatura quae conditur, sic et eiusdem rationis cognitio prius fit in creatura intellectuali, ac deinde est ipsa conditio creaturae*.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30724)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod de extremo ad extremum non pervenitur nisi per medium. Esse autem formae in imaginatione, quod est quidem sine materia, non tamen sine materialibus conditionibus, medium est inter esse formae quae est in materia, et esse formae quae est in intellectu per abstractionem a materia et a conditionibus materialibus. Unde quantumcumque sit potens intellectus angelicus, non posset formas materiales reducere ad esse intelligibile, nisi prius reduceret eas ad esse formarum imaginatarum. Quod est impossibile, cum careat imaginatione, ut dictum est. Dato etiam quod posset abstrahere species intelligibiles a rebus materialibus, non tamen abstraheret, quia non indigeret eis, cum habeat species intelligibiles connaturales.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30725)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod cognitio Angeli indifferenter se habet ad distans et propinquum secundum locum. Non tamen propter hoc motus eius localis est frustra, non enim movetur localiter ad cognitionem accipiendam, sed ad operandum aliquid in loco.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30726)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod superiores Angeli non intelligant per species magis universales quam inferiores. Universale enim esse videtur quod a particularibus abstrahitur. Sed Angeli non intelligunt per species a rebus abstractas. Ergo non potest dici quod species intellectus angelici sint magis vel minus universales.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30727)\n\nPraeterea, quod cognoscitur in speciali, perfectius cognoscitur quam quod cognoscitur in universali, quia cognoscere aliquid in universali est quodammodo medium inter potentiam et actum. Si ergo Angeli superiores cognoscunt per formas magis universales quam inferiores, sequitur quod Angeli superiores habeant scientiam magis imperfectam quam inferiores. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30728)\n\nPraeterea, idem non potest esse propria ratio multorum. Sed si Angelus superior cognoscat per unam formam universalem diversa, quae inferior Angelus cognoscit per plures formas speciales, sequitur quod Angelus superior utitur una forma universali ad cognoscendum diversa. Ergo non poterit habere propriam cognitionem de utroque. Quod videtur inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30729)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, XII cap. Angel. Hier., quod superiores Angeli participant scientiam magis in universali quam inferiores. Et in libro de causis dicitur quod Angeli superiores habent formas magis universales.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30730)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ex hoc sunt in rebus aliqua superiora, quod sunt uni primo, quod est Deus, propinquiora et similiora. In Deo autem tota plenitudo intellectualis cognitionis continetur in uno, scilicet in essentia divina, per quam Deus omnia cognoscit. Quae quidem intelligibilis plenitudo in intellectibus creatis inferiori modo et minus simpliciter invenitur. Unde oportet quod ea quae Deus cognoscit per unum, inferiores intellectus cognoscant per multa, et tanto amplius per plura, quanto amplius intellectus inferior fuerit. Sic igitur quanto Angelus fuerit superior, tanto per pauciores species universitatem intelligibilium apprehendere poterit. Et ideo oportet quod eius formae sint universaliores, quasi ad plura se extendentes unaquaeque earum. Et de hoc exemplum aliqualiter in nobis perspici potest. Sunt enim quidam, qui veritatem intelligibilem capere non possunt, nisi eis particulatim per singula explicetur, et hoc quidem ex debilitate intellectus eorum contingit. Alii vero, qui sunt fortioris intellectus, ex paucis multa capere possunt.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30731)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod accidit universali ut a singularibus abstrahatur, inquantum intellectus illud cognoscens a rebus cognitionem accipit. Si vero sit aliquis intellectus a rebus cognitionem non accipiens, universale ab eo cognitum non erit abstractum a rebus, sed quodammodo ante res praeexistens, vel secundum ordinem causae, sicut universales rerum rationes sunt in verbo Dei; vel saltem ordine naturae, sicut universales rerum rationes sunt in intellectu angelico.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30732)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod cognoscere aliquid in universali, dicitur dupliciter. Uno modo, ex parte rei cognitae, ut scilicet cognoscatur solum universalis natura rei. Et sic cognoscere aliquid in universali est imperfectius, imperfecte enim cognosceret hominem, qui cognosceret de eo solum quod est animal. Alio modo, ex parte medii cognoscendi. Et sic perfectius est cognoscere aliquid in universali, perfectior enim est intellectus qui per unum universale medium potest singula propria cognoscere, quam qui non potest.\n\n[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30733)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod idem non potest esse plurium propria ratio adaequata. Sed si sit excellens, potest idem accipi ut propria ratio et similitudo diversorum. Sicut in homine est universalis prudentia quantum ad omnes actus virtutum; et potest accipi ut propria ratio et similitudo particularis prudentiae quae est in leone ad actus magnanimitatis, et eius quae est in vulpe ad actus cautelae, et sic de aliis. Similiter essentia divina accipitur, propter sui excellentiam, ut propria ratio singulorum, quia est in ea unde sibi singula similentur secundum proprias rationes. Et eodem modo dicendum est de ratione universali quae est in mente Angeli, quod per eam, propter eius excellentiam, multa cognosci possunt propria cognitione.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0002","text":"[I q. 55 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0003","text":"Consequenter quaeritur de medio cognitionis angelicae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angeli cognoscant omnia per suam substantiam, vel per aliquas species. Secundo, si per species, utrum per species connaturales, vel per species a rebus acceptas. Tertio, utrum Angeli superiores cognoscant per species magis universales, quam inferiores.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0005","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli cognoscant omnia per suam substantiam. Dicit enim Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli sciunt ea quae sunt in terra, secundum propriam naturam mentis*. Sed natura Angeli est eius essentia. Ergo Angelus per suam essentiam res cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0007","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0008","text":"Praeterea, secundum philosophum, in XII Metaphys., et in III de anima, *in his quae sunt sine materia, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur*. Id autem quod intelligitur est idem intelligenti ratione eius quo intelligitur. Ergo in his quae sunt sine materia, sicut sunt Angeli, id quo intelligitur est ipsa substantia intelligentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0009","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0010","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est in altero, est in eo per modum eius in quo est. Sed Angelus habet naturam intellectualem. Ergo quidquid est in ipso, est in eo per modum intelligibilem. Sed omnia sunt in eo, quia inferiora in entibus sunt in superioribus essentialiter, superiora vero sunt in inferioribus participative; et ideo dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod Deus tota in totis congregat, idest omnia in omnibus. Ergo Angelus omnia in sua substantia cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0011","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, in eodem capite, quod Angeli illuminantur rationibus rerum. Ergo cognoscunt per rationes rerum, et non per propriam substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0013","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod illud quo intellectus intelligit, comparatur ad intellectum intelligentem ut forma eius, quia forma est quo agens agit. Oportet autem, ad hoc quod potentia perfecte compleatur per formam, quod omnia contineantur sub forma, ad quae potentia se extendit. Et inde est quod in rebus corruptibilibus forma non perfecte complet potentiam materiae, quia potentia materiae ad plura se extendit quam sit continentia formae huius vel illius. Potentia autem intellectiva Angeli se extendit ad intelligendum omnia, quia obiectum intellectus est ens vel verum commune. Ipsa autem essentia Angeli non comprehendit in se omnia, cum sit essentia determinata ad genus et ad speciem. Hoc autem proprium est essentiae divinae, quae infinita est, ut in se simpliciter omnia comprehendat perfecte. Et ideo solus Deus cognoscit omnia per suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0015","text":"Angelus autem per suam essentiam non potest omnia cognoscere; sed oportet intellectum eius aliquibus speciebus perfici ad res cognoscendas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0016","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum dicitur Angelum secundum suam naturam res cognoscere, ly secundum non determinat medium cognitionis, quod est similitudo cogniti; sed virtutem cognoscitivam, quae convenit Angelo secundum suam naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0018","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut sensus in actu est sensibile in actu, ut dicitur in III de anima, non ita quod ipsa vis sensitiva sit ipsa similitudo sensibilis quae est in sensu, sed quia ex utroque fit unum sicut ex actu et potentia; ita et intellectus in actu dicitur esse intellectum in actu, non quod substantia intellectus sit ipsa similitudo per quam intelligit, sed quia illa similitudo est forma eius. Idem est autem quod dicitur, in his quae sunt sine materia, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur, ac si diceretur quod intellectus in actu est intellectum in actu, ex hoc enim aliquid est intellectum in actu quod est immateriale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0020","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ea quae sunt infra Angelum, et ea quae sunt supra ipsum, sunt quodammodo in substantia eius, non quidem perfecte, neque secundum propriam rationem, cum Angeli essentia, finita existens, secundum propriam rationem ab aliis distinguatur; sed secundum quandam rationem communem. In essentia autem Dei sunt omnia perfecte et secundum propriam rationem, sicut in prima et universali virtute operativa, a qua procedit quidquid est in quacumque re vel proprium vel commune. Et ideo Deus per essentiam suam habet propriam cognitionem de rebus omnibus, non autem Angelus, sed solam communem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0023","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli intelligant per species a rebus acceptas. Omne enim quod intelligitur, per aliquam sui similitudinem in intelligente intelligitur. Similitudo autem alicuius in altero existens, aut est ibi per modum exemplaris, ita quod illa similitudo sit causa rei, aut est ibi per modum imaginis, ita quod sit causata a re. Oportet igitur quod omnis scientia intelligentis vel sit causa rei intellectae, vel causata a re. Sed scientia Angeli non est causa rerum existentium in natura, sed sola divina scientia. Ergo oportet quod species per quas intelligit intellectus angelicus, sint a rebus acceptae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0025","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0026","text":"Praeterea, lumen angelicum est fortius quam lumen intellectus agentis in anima. Sed lumen intellectus agentis abstrahit species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus. Ergo lumen intellectus angelici potest abstrahere species etiam ab ipsis rebus sensibilibus. Et ita nihil prohibet dicere quod Angelus intelligat per species a rebus acceptas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0027","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0028","text":"Praeterea, species quae sunt in intellectu, indifferenter se habent ad praesens et distans, nisi quatenus a rebus sensibilibus accipiuntur. Si ergo Angelus non intelligit per species a rebus acceptas, eius cognitio indifferenter se haberet ad propinqua et distantia, et ita frustra secundum locum moveretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0029","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli non congregant divinam cognitionem a rebus divisibilibus, aut a sensibilibus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0031","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod species per quas Angeli intelligunt, non sunt a rebus acceptae, sed eis connaturales. Sic enim oportet intelligere distinctionem et ordinem spiritualium substantiarum, sicut est distinctio et ordo corporalium. Suprema autem corpora habent potentiam in sui natura totaliter perfectam per formam, in corporibus autem inferioribus potentia materiae non totaliter perficitur per formam, sed accipit nunc unam, nunc aliam formam, ab aliquo agente. Similiter et inferiores substantiae intellectivae, scilicet animae humanae, habent potentiam intellectivam non completam naturaliter; sed completur in eis successive, per hoc quod accipiunt species intelligibiles a rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0033","text":"Potentia vero intellectiva in substantiis spiritualibus superioribus, idest in Angelis, naturaliter completa est per species intelligibiles, inquantum habent species intelligibiles connaturales ad omnia intelligenda quae naturaliter cognoscere possunt. Et hoc etiam ex ipso modo essendi huiusmodi substantiarum apparet. Substantiae enim spirituales inferiores, scilicet animae, habent esse affine corpori, inquantum sunt corporum formae, et ideo ex ipso modo essendi competit eis ut a corporibus, et per corpora suam perfectionem intelligibilem consequantur, alioquin frustra corporibus unirentur. Substantiae vero superiores, idest Angeli, sunt a corporibus totaliter absolutae, immaterialiter et in esse intelligibili subsistentes, et ideo suam perfectionem intelligibilem consequuntur per intelligibilem effluxum, quo a Deo species rerum cognitarum acceperunt simul cum intellectuali natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0034","text":"Unde Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt., quod *cetera, quae infra Angelos sunt, ita creantur, ut prius fiant in cognitione rationalis creaturae, ac deinde in genere suo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0035","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in mente Angeli sunt similitudines creaturarum, non quidem ab ipsis creaturis acceptae, sed a Deo, qui est creaturarum causa, et in quo primo similitudines rerum existunt. Unde Augustinus dicit, in eodem libro, quod *sicut ratio qua creatura conditur, prius est in verbo Dei quam ipsa creatura quae conditur, sic et eiusdem rationis cognitio prius fit in creatura intellectuali, ac deinde est ipsa conditio creaturae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0037","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod de extremo ad extremum non pervenitur nisi per medium. Esse autem formae in imaginatione, quod est quidem sine materia, non tamen sine materialibus conditionibus, medium est inter esse formae quae est in materia, et esse formae quae est in intellectu per abstractionem a materia et a conditionibus materialibus. Unde quantumcumque sit potens intellectus angelicus, non posset formas materiales reducere ad esse intelligibile, nisi prius reduceret eas ad esse formarum imaginatarum. Quod est impossibile, cum careat imaginatione, ut dictum est. Dato etiam quod posset abstrahere species intelligibiles a rebus materialibus, non tamen abstraheret, quia non indigeret eis, cum habeat species intelligibiles connaturales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0039","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod cognitio Angeli indifferenter se habet ad distans et propinquum secundum locum. Non tamen propter hoc motus eius localis est frustra, non enim movetur localiter ad cognitionem accipiendam, sed ad operandum aliquid in loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0042","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod superiores Angeli non intelligant per species magis universales quam inferiores. Universale enim esse videtur quod a particularibus abstrahitur. Sed Angeli non intelligunt per species a rebus abstractas. Ergo non potest dici quod species intellectus angelici sint magis vel minus universales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0044","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0045","text":"Praeterea, quod cognoscitur in speciali, perfectius cognoscitur quam quod cognoscitur in universali, quia cognoscere aliquid in universali est quodammodo medium inter potentiam et actum. Si ergo Angeli superiores cognoscunt per formas magis universales quam inferiores, sequitur quod Angeli superiores habeant scientiam magis imperfectam quam inferiores. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0046","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0047","text":"Praeterea, idem non potest esse propria ratio multorum. Sed si Angelus superior cognoscat per unam formam universalem diversa, quae inferior Angelus cognoscit per plures formas speciales, sequitur quod Angelus superior utitur una forma universali ad cognoscendum diversa. Ergo non poterit habere propriam cognitionem de utroque. Quod videtur inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0048","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, XII cap. Angel. Hier., quod superiores Angeli participant scientiam magis in universali quam inferiores. Et in libro de causis dicitur quod Angeli superiores habent formas magis universales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0050","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ex hoc sunt in rebus aliqua superiora, quod sunt uni primo, quod est Deus, propinquiora et similiora. In Deo autem tota plenitudo intellectualis cognitionis continetur in uno, scilicet in essentia divina, per quam Deus omnia cognoscit. Quae quidem intelligibilis plenitudo in intellectibus creatis inferiori modo et minus simpliciter invenitur. Unde oportet quod ea quae Deus cognoscit per unum, inferiores intellectus cognoscant per multa, et tanto amplius per plura, quanto amplius intellectus inferior fuerit. Sic igitur quanto Angelus fuerit superior, tanto per pauciores species universitatem intelligibilium apprehendere poterit. Et ideo oportet quod eius formae sint universaliores, quasi ad plura se extendentes unaquaeque earum. Et de hoc exemplum aliqualiter in nobis perspici potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0052","text":"Sunt enim quidam, qui veritatem intelligibilem capere non possunt, nisi eis particulatim per singula explicetur, et hoc quidem ex debilitate intellectus eorum contingit. Alii vero, qui sunt fortioris intellectus, ex paucis multa capere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0053","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0054","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod accidit universali ut a singularibus abstrahatur, inquantum intellectus illud cognoscens a rebus cognitionem accipit. Si vero sit aliquis intellectus a rebus cognitionem non accipiens, universale ab eo cognitum non erit abstractum a rebus, sed quodammodo ante res praeexistens, vel secundum ordinem causae, sicut universales rerum rationes sunt in verbo Dei; vel saltem ordine naturae, sicut universales rerum rationes sunt in intellectu angelico.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0055","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0056","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod cognoscere aliquid in universali, dicitur dupliciter. Uno modo, ex parte rei cognitae, ut scilicet cognoscatur solum universalis natura rei. Et sic cognoscere aliquid in universali est imperfectius, imperfecte enim cognosceret hominem, qui cognosceret de eo solum quod est animal. Alio modo, ex parte medii cognoscendi. Et sic perfectius est cognoscere aliquid in universali, perfectior enim est intellectus qui per unum universale medium potest singula propria cognoscere, quam qui non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0057","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0058","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod idem non potest esse plurium propria ratio adaequata. Sed si sit excellens, potest idem accipi ut propria ratio et similitudo diversorum. Sicut in homine est universalis prudentia quantum ad omnes actus virtutum; et potest accipi ut propria ratio et similitudo particularis prudentiae quae est in leone ad actus magnanimitatis, et eius quae est in vulpe ad actus cautelae, et sic de aliis. Similiter essentia divina accipitur, propter sui excellentiam, ut propria ratio singulorum, quia est in ea unde sibi singula similentur secundum proprias rationes. Et eodem modo dicendum est de ratione universali quae est in mente Angeli, quod per eam, propter eius excellentiam, multa cognosci possunt propria cognitione.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fae39d1dc6566f4aaa75b1cc4b3ddd2154be3edd3e906d46a8ea06d7ad4e542b","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T143938142616Z_dafa288b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 55","source_sha256":"fae39d1dc6566f4aaa75b1cc4b3ddd2154be3edd3e906d46a8ea06d7ad4e542b","sha256":"f49e69807db29eaa0753ce153ee722fb942eda76a2ba46978320ecafdc302912","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:39:38.142722+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0002","text":"[I q. 55 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0003","text":"Next, the medium of angelic cognition is considered. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, whether angels know all things through their substance, or through certain species. Second, if through species, whether through connatural species, or through species received from things. Third, whether the higher angels know through more universal species than the lower.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0005","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0006","text":"The first point is approached as follows. It seems that angels know all things through their substance. For Dionysius says, in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*, that *angels know the things that are on earth according to the proper nature of their mind*. But an angel's nature is its essence. Therefore, an angel knows things through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0007","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0008","text":"Moreover, according to the Philosopher, in XII *Metaphysics* and in III *On the Soul*, *in things without matter, intellect and that which is understood are the same*. But that which is understood is the same as the one understanding by reason of that through which it is understood. Therefore, in things without matter, such as angels, that through which one understands is the very substance of the one understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0009","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0010","text":"Moreover, everything that is in another is in it according to the mode of that in which it is. But an angel has an intellectual nature. Therefore, whatever is in it is in it in an intelligible mode. But all things are in it, because lower things among beings are in higher things essentially, whereas higher things are in lower things participatively; and therefore Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that God gathers all things together in all things, that is, all things in everything. Therefore, an angel knows all things in its substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0011","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says in the same chapter that angels are illuminated by the intelligible principles of things. Therefore, they know through the intelligible principles of things, and not through their own substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0013","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that that through which the intellect understands is related to the understanding intellect as its form, because form is that through which an agent acts. But in order that a potency may be perfectly completed through a form, all the things to which the potency extends must be contained under the form. Thus it is that, in corruptible things, form does not perfectly complete the potency of matter, because the potency of matter extends to more things than are contained by this or that form. But the intellective potency of an angel extends to understanding all things, because the object of intellect is being or the common true. Yet the very essence of an angel does not comprehend all things in itself, since it is an essence determined to genus and species. But it is proper to the divine essence, which is infinite, to comprehend all things in itself simply and perfectly. And therefore God alone knows all things through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0015","text":"But an angel cannot know all things through its essence; rather, its intellect must be perfected by certain species in order to know things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0016","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, when it is said that an angel knows things according to its nature, the term *according to* does not determine the medium of cognition, which is the likeness of the thing known, but the cognitive power, which belongs to an angel according to its nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0018","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as sense in act is the sensible in act, as is said in III *On the Soul*, not in such a way that the sensitive power itself is the very likeness of the sensible which is in sense, but because from both there comes to be one thing, as from act and potency; so too intellect in act is said to be the understood in act, not because the substance of the intellect is itself the likeness through which it understands, but because that likeness is its form. Now, the statement that in things without matter intellect and that which is understood are the same is equivalent to saying that intellect in act is the understood in act; for it is by being immaterial that something is understood in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0020","text":"[I q. 55 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that those things which are below an angel and those which are above it are in some way in its substance, though not perfectly, nor according to their proper intelligible account, since an angel's essence, being finite, is distinguished from others according to its proper intelligible account; but according to a certain common intelligible account. But in the essence of God all things are perfectly and according to their proper intelligible account, as in the first and universal operative power, from which proceeds whatever is either proper or common in any thing whatsoever. And therefore God has, through His essence, proper cognition of all things, but an angel does not, having only common cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0023","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0024","text":"The second point is approached as follows. It seems that angels understand through species received from things. For everything that is understood is understood through some likeness of itself in the one understanding. But the likeness of something existing in another is either there in the manner of an exemplar, so that that likeness is the cause of the thing, or it is there in the manner of an image, so that it is caused by the thing. Therefore, all knowledge of one understanding is either the cause of the thing understood or caused by the thing. But an angel's knowledge is not the cause of things existing in nature, but divine knowledge alone is. Therefore, the species through which the angelic intellect understands must be received from things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0025","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0026","text":"Further, angelic light is stronger than the light of the agent intellect in the soul. But the light of the agent intellect abstracts intelligible species from phantasms. Therefore, the light of the angelic intellect can abstract species even from sensible things themselves. And thus nothing prevents one from saying that an Angel understands through species received from things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0027","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0028","text":"Further, the species that are in the intellect are disposed indifferently toward what is present and what is distant, except insofar as they are received from sensible things. If, therefore, an Angel does not understand through species received from things, his knowledge would be disposed indifferently toward things near and distant, and thus he would be moved locally in vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0029","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*, that *Angels do not gather divine knowledge from divisible things, or from sensible things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0031","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that the species through which Angels understand are not received from things, but are connatural to them. For the distinction and order of spiritual substances must be understood in the same way as there is a distinction and order among bodily things. Now the highest bodies have a power in their nature wholly perfected through form; but in lower bodies the potentiality of matter is not wholly perfected through form, but receives now one, now another form, from some agent. Likewise, the lower intellectual substances, namely human souls, have an intellectual power that is not naturally complete; rather, it is completed in them successively, by their receiving intelligible species from things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0033","text":"But the intellectual power in the higher spiritual substances, that is, in Angels, is naturally complete through intelligible species, insofar as they have intelligible species connatural to all the things that they can naturally know. And this also appears from the very mode of being of such substances. For the lower spiritual substances, namely souls, have a being akin to body, insofar as they are forms of bodies; and therefore it befits them, from their very mode of being, to attain their intelligible perfection from bodies and through bodies; otherwise they would be united to bodies in vain. But the higher substances, that is, Angels, are wholly separate from bodies, subsisting immaterially and in intelligible being; and therefore they attain their intelligible perfection through an intelligible efflux, by which they received from God the species of things known together with their intellectual nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0034","text":"Hence Augustine says, in Book II of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that *the other things, which are below Angels, are so created that they are first made in the knowledge of the rational creature, and then in their own genus*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0035","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in the mind of an Angel there are likenesses of creatures, not indeed received from the creatures themselves, but from God, who is the cause of creatures, and in whom the likenesses of things exist first. Hence Augustine says, in the same book, that *just as the intelligible account by which a creature is created is in the Word of God before the creature itself that is created, so too the knowledge of that same intelligible account first comes to be in the intellectual creature, and afterwards the creature itself is created*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0037","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0038","text":"To the second, it must be said that one does not arrive from one extreme to another except through a middle. Now the being of a form in the imagination, which is indeed without matter, though not without material conditions, is a middle between the being of a form that is in matter and the being of a form that is in the intellect through abstraction from matter and from material conditions. Hence, however powerful the angelic intellect may be, it could not reduce material forms to intelligible being unless it first reduced them to the being of imagined forms. But this is impossible, since it lacks imagination, as was said. Even if it were granted that it could abstract intelligible species from material things, it nevertheless would not abstract them, because it would not need them, since it has connatural intelligible species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0039","text":"[I q. 55 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that the knowledge of an Angel is disposed indifferently toward what is distant and what is near in place. Nevertheless, for this reason its local movement is not in vain, for it is not moved locally in order to receive knowledge, but in order to do something in a place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0042","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third point. It seems that the higher Angels do not understand through more universal species than the lower Angels. For the universal seems to be that which is abstracted from particulars. But Angels do not understand through species abstracted from things. Therefore, it cannot be said that the species of the angelic intellect are more or less universal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0044","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0045","text":"Further, what is known in particular is known more perfectly than what is known universally, because to know something universally is in some way intermediate between potency and act. If, therefore, the higher Angels know through forms more universal than those of the lower Angels, it follows that the higher Angels have knowledge more imperfect than the lower Angels. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0046","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0047","text":"Further, the same thing cannot be the proper intelligible account of many things. But if a higher Angel knows diverse things through one universal form, which a lower Angel knows through several special forms, it follows that the higher Angel uses one universal form for knowing diverse things. Therefore, he will not be able to have proper knowledge of each of the two. This seems unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0048","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0049","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter XII of the Angelic Hierarchy, that the higher angels participate in knowledge more universally than the lower ones. And in the Book of Causes it is said that the higher angels have more universal forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0050","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0051","text":"I answer that it must be said that there are some things higher in beings because they are nearer and more like to the one first principle, which is God. But in God the whole fullness of intellectual knowledge is contained in one thing, namely, in the divine essence, through which God knows all things. This intelligible fullness is found in created intellects in a lower and less simple manner. Hence it is necessary that what God knows through one thing, lower intellects know through many, and through all the more things the lower the intellect is. Thus, therefore, the higher an angel is, the fewer species he can use to apprehend the universality of intelligibles. And therefore it is necessary that his forms be more universal, as though each of them extended to more things. And an example of this can in some measure be perceived in us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0052","text":"For there are some who cannot grasp intelligible truth unless it is set forth to them bit by bit, through individual points; and this occurs because of the weakness of their intellect. Others, however, who are of stronger intellect, can grasp many things from a few.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0053","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0054","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that it is accidental to the universal that it be abstracted from singulars, insofar as an intellect knowing it receives knowledge from things. But if there is an intellect that does not receive knowledge from things, the universal known by it will not be abstracted from things, but will in some manner pre-exist things, either according to the order of cause, as the universal notions of things are in the Word of God; or at least according to the order of nature, as the universal notions of things are in the angelic intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0055","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0056","text":"To the second it must be said that to know something universally is said in two ways. In one way, on the part of the thing known, namely, insofar as only the universal nature of the thing is known. And thus to know something universally is more imperfect; for one would know man imperfectly who knew of him only that he is an animal. In another way, on the part of the medium of knowing. And thus it is more perfect to know something universally; for the intellect that can know particular proper things through one universal medium is more perfect than one that cannot.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0057","text":"[I q. 55 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0058","text":"To the third it must be said that the same thing cannot be the proper and adequate notion of several things. But if it is excellent, the same thing can be taken as the proper notion and likeness of different things. Thus in man there is universal prudence with respect to all the acts of the virtues; and it can be taken as the proper notion and likeness of the particular prudence that is in the lion with respect to acts of magnanimity, and of that which is in the fox with respect to acts of caution, and so of the others. Similarly, the divine essence, on account of its excellence, is taken as the proper notion of singular things, because in it there is that by which individual things are likened to it according to their proper notions. And in the same way it must be said of the universal notion that is in the mind of an angel, that through it, on account of its excellence, many things can be known with proper knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_47:P0058"]}],"source_digest":"a8a8fb013ac3343b87387b07b4edafab3fccf45e2ec0e52594943294ca94d574","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 55"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 55","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48","title":"First Part · Question 56","order":57,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_48","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 56 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30734)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de cognitione Angelorum ex parte rerum quas cognoscunt. Et primo, de cognitione rerum immaterialium; secundo, de cognitione rerum materialium. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angelus cognoscat seipsum. Secundo, utrum unus cognoscat alium. Tertio, utrum Angelus per sua naturalia cognoscat Deum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30735)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus seipsum non cognoscat. Dicit enim Dionysius, VI cap. Angel. Hier., quod Angeli ignorant proprias virtutes. Cognita autem substantia, cognoscitur virtus. Ergo Angelus non cognoscit suam essentiam.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30736)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus est quaedam substantia singularis, alioquin non ageret, cum actus sint singularium subsistentium. Sed nullum singulare est intelligibile. Ergo non potest intelligi. Et ita, cum Angelo non adsit nisi intellectiva cognitio, non poterit aliquis Angelus cognoscere seipsum.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30737)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus movetur ab intelligibili, quia intelligere est quoddam pati, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed nihil movetur aut patitur a seipso; ut in rebus corporalibus apparet. Ergo Angelus non potest intelligere seipsum.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30738)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Angelus in ipsa sua conformatione, hoc est illustratione veritatis, cognovit seipsum*.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30739)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex supra dictis patet, obiectum aliter se habet in actione quae manet in agente, et in actione quae transit in aliquid exterius. Nam in actione quae transit in aliquid exterius, obiectum sive materia in quam transit actus, est separata ab agente, sicut calefactum a calefaciente, et aedificatum ab aedificante. Sed in actione quae manet in agente, oportet ad hoc quod procedat actio, quod obiectum uniatur agenti, sicut oportet quod sensibile uniatur sensui, ad hoc quod sentiat actu. Et ita se habet obiectum unitum potentiae ad huiusmodi actionem, sicut forma quae est principium actionis in aliis agentibus, sicut enim calor est principium formale calefactionis in igne, ita species rei visae est principium formale visionis in oculo. Sed considerandum est quod huiusmodi species obiecti quandoque est in potentia tantum in cognoscitiva virtute, et tunc est cognoscens in potentia tantum; et ad hoc quod actu cognoscat, requiritur quod potentia cognoscitiva reducatur in actum speciei. Si autem semper eam actu habeat, nihilominus per eam cognoscere potest absque aliqua mutatione vel receptione praecedenti. Ex quo patet quod moveri ab obiecto non est de ratione cognoscentis inquantum est cognoscens, sed inquantum est potentia cognoscens. Nihil autem differt, ad hoc quod forma sit principium actionis, quod ipsa forma sit alii inhaerens, et quod sit per se subsistens, non enim minus calor calefaceret si esset per se subsistens, quam calefacit inhaerens. Sic igitur et si aliquid in genere intelligibilium se habeat ut forma intelligibilis subsistens, intelliget seipsum. Angelus autem, cum sit immaterialis, est quaedam forma subsistens, et per hoc intelligibilis actu. Unde sequitur quod per suam formam, quae est sua substantia, seipsum intelligat.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30740)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod littera illa est antiquae translationis, quae corrigitur per novam, in qua dicitur, praeterea et ipsos, scilicet Angelos, cognovisse proprias virtutes; loco cuius habebatur in alia translatione, et adhuc et eos ignorare proprias virtutes. Quamvis etiam littera antiquae translationis salvari possit quantum ad hoc, quod Angeli non perfecte cognoscunt suam virtutem, secundum quod procedit ab ordine divinae sapientiae, quae est Angelis incomprehensibilis.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30741)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod singularium quae sunt in rebus corporalibus, non est intellectus, apud nos, non ratione singularitatis, sed ratione materiae, quae est in eis individuationis principium. Unde si aliqua singularia sunt sine materia subsistentia, sicut sunt Angeli, illa nihil prohibet intelligibilia esse actu.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30742)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod moveri et pati convenit intellectui secundum quod est in potentia. Unde non habet locum in intellectu angelico; maxime quantum ad hoc quod intelligit seipsum. Actio etiam intellectus non est eiusdem rationis cum actione quae in corporalibus invenitur, in aliam materiam transeunte.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30743)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus alium non cognoscat. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod si intellectus humanus haberet in se aliquam naturam de numero naturarum rerum sensibilium, illa natura interius existens prohiberet apparere extranea, sicut etiam si pupilla esset colorata aliquo colore, non posset videre omnem colorem. Sed sicut se habet intellectus humanus ad cognoscendas res corporeas, ita se habet intellectus angelicus ad cognoscendas res immateriales. Cum igitur intellectus angelicus habeat in se aliquam naturam determinatam de numero illarum naturarum, videtur quod alias cognoscere non possit.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30744)\n\nPraeterea, in libro de causis dicitur quod *omnis intelligentia sit quod est supra se, inquantum est causata ab eo; et quod est sub se, inquantum est causa eius*. Sed unus Angelus non est causa alterius. Ergo unus Angelus non cognoscit alium.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30745)\n\nPraeterea, unus Angelus non potest cognoscere alium per essentiam ipsius Angeli cognoscentis, cum omnis cognitio sit secundum rationem similitudinis, essentia autem Angeli cognoscentis non est similis essentiae Angeli cogniti nisi in genere, ut ex supra dictis patet; unde sequeretur quod unus Angelus non haberet de alio cognitionem propriam, sed generalem tantum. Similiter etiam non potest dici quod unus Angelus cognoscat alium per essentiam Angeli cogniti, quia illud quo intellectus intelligit, est intrinsecum intellectui; sola autem Trinitas illabitur menti. Similiter etiam dici non potest quod unus cognoscat alium per speciem, quia illa species non differret ab Angelo intellecto, cum utrumque sit immateriale. Nullo igitur modo videtur quod unus Angelus possit intelligere alium.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30746)\n\nPraeterea, si unus Angelus intelligit alium, aut hoc esset per speciem innatam, et sic sequeretur quod, si Deus nunc de novo crearet aliquem Angelum, quod non posset cognosci ab his qui nunc sunt. Aut per speciem acquisitam a rebus, et sic sequeretur quod Angeli superiores non possent cognoscere inferiores, a quibus nihil accipiunt. Nullo igitur modo videtur quod unus Angelus alium cognoscat.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30747)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro de causis, quod *omnis intelligentia scit res quae non corrumpuntur*.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30748)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt., ea quae in verbo Dei ab aeterno praeextiterunt, dupliciter ab eo effluxerunt, uno modo, in intellectum angelicum; alio modo, ut subsisterent in propriis naturis. In intellectum autem angelicum processerunt per hoc, quod Deus menti angelicae impressit rerum similitudines, quas in esse naturali produxit. In verbo autem Dei ab aeterno extiterunt non solum rationes rerum corporalium, sed etiam rationes omnium spiritualium creaturarum. Sic igitur unicuique spiritualium creaturarum a verbo Dei impressae sunt omnes rationes rerum omnium, tam corporalium quam spiritualium. Ita tamen quod unicuique Angelo impressa est ratio suae speciei secundum esse naturale et intelligibile simul, ita scilicet quod in natura suae speciei subsisteret, et per eam se intelligeret, aliarum vero naturarum, tam spiritualium quam corporalium, rationes sunt ei impressae secundum esse intelligibile tantum, ut videlicet per huiusmodi species impressas, tam creaturas corporales quam spirituales cognosceret.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30749)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod naturae spirituales Angelorum ab invicem distinguuntur ordine quodam, sicut supra dictum est. Et sic natura unius Angeli non prohibet intellectum ipsius a cognoscendis aliis naturis Angelorum, cum tam superiores quam inferiores habeant affinitatem cum natura eius, differentia existente tantum secundum diversos gradus perfectionis.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30750)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio causae et causati non facit ad hoc quod unus Angelus alium cognoscat, nisi ratione similitudinis, inquantum causa et causatum sunt similia. Et ideo, si inter Angelos ponatur similitudo absque causalitate, remanebit in uno cognitio alterius.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30751)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod unus Angelus cognoscit alium per speciem eius in intellectu suo existentem, quae differt ab Angelo cuius similitudo est, non secundum esse materiale et immateriale, sed secundum esse naturale et intentionale. Nam ipse Angelus est forma subsistens in esse naturali, non autem species eius quae est in intellectu alterius Angeli, sed habet ibi esse intelligibile tantum. Sicut etiam et forma coloris in pariete habet esse naturale, in medio autem deferente habet esse intentionale tantum.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30752)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Deus unamquamque creaturam fecit proportionatam universo quod facere disposuit. Et ideo, si Deus instituisset facere plures Angelos vel plures naturas rerum, plures species intelligibiles mentibus angelicis impressisset. Sicut si aedificator voluisset facere maiorem domum, fecisset maius fundamentum. Unde eiusdem rationis est quod Deus adderet aliquam creaturam universo, et aliquam speciem intelligibilem Angelo.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30753)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli per sua naturalia Deum cognoscere non possint. Dicit enim Dionysius, I cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Deus est super omnes caelestes mentes incomprehensibili virtute collocatus*. Et postea subdit quod, *quia est supra omnem substantiam, ab omni cognitione est segregatus*.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30754)\n\nPraeterea, Deus in infinitum distat ab intellectu Angeli. Sed in infinitum distantia non possunt attingi. Ergo videtur quod Angelus per sua naturalia non possit Deum cognoscere.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30755)\n\nPraeterea, I Cor. XIII dicitur, *videmus nunc per speculum in aenigmate, tunc autem facie ad faciem*. Ex quo videtur quod sit duplex Dei cognitio, una, qua videtur per sui essentiam, secundum quam dicitur videri facie ad faciem; alia, secundum quod videtur in speculo creaturarum. Sed primam Dei cognitionem Angelus habere non potuit per sua naturalia, ut supra ostensum est. Visio autem specularis Angelis non convenit, quia non accipiunt divinam cognitionem e rebus sensibilibus, ut dicit Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo Angeli per sua naturalia Deum cognoscere non possunt.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30756)\n\nSed contra, Angeli sunt potentiores in cognoscendo quam homines. Sed homines per sua naturalia Deum cognoscere possunt; secundum illud Rom. I, *quod notum est Dei, manifestum est in illis*. Ergo multo magis Angeli.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30757)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angeli aliquam cognitionem de Deo habere possunt per sua naturalia. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod aliquid tripliciter cognoscitur. Uno modo, per praesentiam suae essentiae in cognoscente, sicut si lux videatur in oculo, et sic dictum est quod Angelus intelligit seipsum. Alio modo, per praesentiam suae similitudinis in potentia cognoscitiva, sicut lapis videtur ab oculo per hoc quod similitudo eius resultat in oculo. Tertio modo, per hoc quod similitudo rei cognitae non accipitur immediate ab ipsa re cognita, sed a re alia, in qua resultat, sicut cum videmus hominem in speculo. Primae igitur cognitioni assimilatur divina cognitio, qua per essentiam suam videtur. Et haec cognitio Dei non potest adesse creaturae alicui per sua naturalia, ut supra dictum est. Tertiae autem cognitioni assimilatur cognitio qua nos cognoscimus Deum in via, per similitudinem eius in creaturis resultantem; secundum illud Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*. Unde et dicimur Deum videre in speculo. Cognitio autem qua Angelus per sua naturalia cognoscit Deum, media est inter has duas; et similatur illi cognitioni qua videtur res per speciem ab ea acceptam. Quia enim imago Dei est in ipsa natura Angeli impressa per suam essentiam, Angelus Deum cognoscit, inquantum est similitudo Dei. Non tamen ipsam essentiam Dei videt, quia nulla similitudo creata est sufficiens ad repraesentandam divinam essentiam. Unde magis ista cognitio tenet se cum speculari, quia et ipsa natura angelica est quoddam speculum divinam similitudinem repraesentans.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30758)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius loquitur de cognitione comprehensionis, ut expresse eius verba ostendunt. Et sic a nullo intellectu creato cognoscitur.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30759)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod propter hoc quod intellectus et essentia Angeli in infinitum distant a Deo, sequitur quod non possit ipsum comprehendere, nec per suam naturam eius essentiam videre. Non tamen sequitur propter hoc, quod nullam eius cognitionem habere possit, quia sicut Deus in infinitum distat ab Angelo, ita cognitio quam Deus habet de seipso, in infinitum distat a cognitione quam Angelus habet de eo.\n\n[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30760)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod cognitio quam naturaliter Angelus habet de Deo, est media inter utramque cognitionem, et tamen magis se tenet cum una, ut supra dictum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0002","text":"[I q. 56 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de cognitione Angelorum ex parte rerum quas cognoscunt. Et primo, de cognitione rerum immaterialium; secundo, de cognitione rerum materialium. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum Angelus cognoscat seipsum. Secundo, utrum unus cognoscat alium. Tertio, utrum Angelus per sua naturalia cognoscat Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0005","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus seipsum non cognoscat. Dicit enim Dionysius, VI cap. Angel. Hier., quod Angeli ignorant proprias virtutes. Cognita autem substantia, cognoscitur virtus. Ergo Angelus non cognoscit suam essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0007","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Angelus est quaedam substantia singularis, alioquin non ageret, cum actus sint singularium subsistentium. Sed nullum singulare est intelligibile. Ergo non potest intelligi. Et ita, cum Angelo non adsit nisi intellectiva cognitio, non poterit aliquis Angelus cognoscere seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0009","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0010","text":"Praeterea, intellectus movetur ab intelligibili, quia intelligere est quoddam pati, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed nihil movetur aut patitur a seipso; ut in rebus corporalibus apparet. Ergo Angelus non potest intelligere seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0011","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Angelus in ipsa sua conformatione, hoc est illustratione veritatis, cognovit seipsum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0013","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex supra dictis patet, obiectum aliter se habet in actione quae manet in agente, et in actione quae transit in aliquid exterius. Nam in actione quae transit in aliquid exterius, obiectum sive materia in quam transit actus, est separata ab agente, sicut calefactum a calefaciente, et aedificatum ab aedificante. Sed in actione quae manet in agente, oportet ad hoc quod procedat actio, quod obiectum uniatur agenti, sicut oportet quod sensibile uniatur sensui, ad hoc quod sentiat actu. Et ita se habet obiectum unitum potentiae ad huiusmodi actionem, sicut forma quae est principium actionis in aliis agentibus, sicut enim calor est principium formale calefactionis in igne, ita species rei visae est principium formale visionis in oculo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0015","text":"Sed considerandum est quod huiusmodi species obiecti quandoque est in potentia tantum in cognoscitiva virtute, et tunc est cognoscens in potentia tantum; et ad hoc quod actu cognoscat, requiritur quod potentia cognoscitiva reducatur in actum speciei. Si autem semper eam actu habeat, nihilominus per eam cognoscere potest absque aliqua mutatione vel receptione praecedenti. Ex quo patet quod moveri ab obiecto non est de ratione cognoscentis inquantum est cognoscens, sed inquantum est potentia cognoscens. Nihil autem differt, ad hoc quod forma sit principium actionis, quod ipsa forma sit alii inhaerens, et quod sit per se subsistens, non enim minus calor calefaceret si esset per se subsistens, quam calefacit inhaerens. Sic igitur et si aliquid in genere intelligibilium se habeat ut forma intelligibilis subsistens, intelliget seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0016","text":"Angelus autem, cum sit immaterialis, est quaedam forma subsistens, et per hoc intelligibilis actu. Unde sequitur quod per suam formam, quae est sua substantia, seipsum intelligat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0017","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod littera illa est antiquae translationis, quae corrigitur per novam, in qua dicitur, praeterea et ipsos, scilicet Angelos, cognovisse proprias virtutes; loco cuius habebatur in alia translatione, et adhuc et eos ignorare proprias virtutes. Quamvis etiam littera antiquae translationis salvari possit quantum ad hoc, quod Angeli non perfecte cognoscunt suam virtutem, secundum quod procedit ab ordine divinae sapientiae, quae est Angelis incomprehensibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0019","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod singularium quae sunt in rebus corporalibus, non est intellectus, apud nos, non ratione singularitatis, sed ratione materiae, quae est in eis individuationis principium. Unde si aliqua singularia sunt sine materia subsistentia, sicut sunt Angeli, illa nihil prohibet intelligibilia esse actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0021","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod moveri et pati convenit intellectui secundum quod est in potentia. Unde non habet locum in intellectu angelico; maxime quantum ad hoc quod intelligit seipsum. Actio etiam intellectus non est eiusdem rationis cum actione quae in corporalibus invenitur, in aliam materiam transeunte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0024","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus alium non cognoscat. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod si intellectus humanus haberet in se aliquam naturam de numero naturarum rerum sensibilium, illa natura interius existens prohiberet apparere extranea, sicut etiam si pupilla esset colorata aliquo colore, non posset videre omnem colorem. Sed sicut se habet intellectus humanus ad cognoscendas res corporeas, ita se habet intellectus angelicus ad cognoscendas res immateriales. Cum igitur intellectus angelicus habeat in se aliquam naturam determinatam de numero illarum naturarum, videtur quod alias cognoscere non possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0026","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0027","text":"Praeterea, in libro de causis dicitur quod *omnis intelligentia sit quod est supra se, inquantum est causata ab eo; et quod est sub se, inquantum est causa eius*. Sed unus Angelus non est causa alterius. Ergo unus Angelus non cognoscit alium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0028","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0029","text":"Praeterea, unus Angelus non potest cognoscere alium per essentiam ipsius Angeli cognoscentis, cum omnis cognitio sit secundum rationem similitudinis, essentia autem Angeli cognoscentis non est similis essentiae Angeli cogniti nisi in genere, ut ex supra dictis patet; unde sequeretur quod unus Angelus non haberet de alio cognitionem propriam, sed generalem tantum. Similiter etiam non potest dici quod unus Angelus cognoscat alium per essentiam Angeli cogniti, quia illud quo intellectus intelligit, est intrinsecum intellectui; sola autem Trinitas illabitur menti. Similiter etiam dici non potest quod unus cognoscat alium per speciem, quia illa species non differret ab Angelo intellecto, cum utrumque sit immateriale. Nullo igitur modo videtur quod unus Angelus possit intelligere alium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0030","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0031","text":"Praeterea, si unus Angelus intelligit alium, aut hoc esset per speciem innatam, et sic sequeretur quod, si Deus nunc de novo crearet aliquem Angelum, quod non posset cognosci ab his qui nunc sunt. Aut per speciem acquisitam a rebus, et sic sequeretur quod Angeli superiores non possent cognoscere inferiores, a quibus nihil accipiunt. Nullo igitur modo videtur quod unus Angelus alium cognoscat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0032","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0033","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro de causis, quod *omnis intelligentia scit res quae non corrumpuntur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0034","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt., ea quae in verbo Dei ab aeterno praeextiterunt, dupliciter ab eo effluxerunt, uno modo, in intellectum angelicum; alio modo, ut subsisterent in propriis naturis. In intellectum autem angelicum processerunt per hoc, quod Deus menti angelicae impressit rerum similitudines, quas in esse naturali produxit. In verbo autem Dei ab aeterno extiterunt non solum rationes rerum corporalium, sed etiam rationes omnium spiritualium creaturarum. Sic igitur unicuique spiritualium creaturarum a verbo Dei impressae sunt omnes rationes rerum omnium, tam corporalium quam spiritualium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0036","text":"Ita tamen quod unicuique Angelo impressa est ratio suae speciei secundum esse naturale et intelligibile simul, ita scilicet quod in natura suae speciei subsisteret, et per eam se intelligeret, aliarum vero naturarum, tam spiritualium quam corporalium, rationes sunt ei impressae secundum esse intelligibile tantum, ut videlicet per huiusmodi species impressas, tam creaturas corporales quam spirituales cognosceret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0037","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod naturae spirituales Angelorum ab invicem distinguuntur ordine quodam, sicut supra dictum est. Et sic natura unius Angeli non prohibet intellectum ipsius a cognoscendis aliis naturis Angelorum, cum tam superiores quam inferiores habeant affinitatem cum natura eius, differentia existente tantum secundum diversos gradus perfectionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0039","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio causae et causati non facit ad hoc quod unus Angelus alium cognoscat, nisi ratione similitudinis, inquantum causa et causatum sunt similia. Et ideo, si inter Angelos ponatur similitudo absque causalitate, remanebit in uno cognitio alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0041","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod unus Angelus cognoscit alium per speciem eius in intellectu suo existentem, quae differt ab Angelo cuius similitudo est, non secundum esse materiale et immateriale, sed secundum esse naturale et intentionale. Nam ipse Angelus est forma subsistens in esse naturali, non autem species eius quae est in intellectu alterius Angeli, sed habet ibi esse intelligibile tantum. Sicut etiam et forma coloris in pariete habet esse naturale, in medio autem deferente habet esse intentionale tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0043","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0044","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Deus unamquamque creaturam fecit proportionatam universo quod facere disposuit. Et ideo, si Deus instituisset facere plures Angelos vel plures naturas rerum, plures species intelligibiles mentibus angelicis impressisset. Sicut si aedificator voluisset facere maiorem domum, fecisset maius fundamentum. Unde eiusdem rationis est quod Deus adderet aliquam creaturam universo, et aliquam speciem intelligibilem Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0045","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0046","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0047","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli per sua naturalia Deum cognoscere non possint. Dicit enim Dionysius, I cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Deus est super omnes caelestes mentes incomprehensibili virtute collocatus*. Et postea subdit quod, *quia est supra omnem substantiam, ab omni cognitione est segregatus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0048","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0049","text":"Praeterea, Deus in infinitum distat ab intellectu Angeli. Sed in infinitum distantia non possunt attingi. Ergo videtur quod Angelus per sua naturalia non possit Deum cognoscere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0050","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0051","text":"Praeterea, I Cor. XIII dicitur, *videmus nunc per speculum in aenigmate, tunc autem facie ad faciem*. Ex quo videtur quod sit duplex Dei cognitio, una, qua videtur per sui essentiam, secundum quam dicitur videri facie ad faciem; alia, secundum quod videtur in speculo creaturarum. Sed primam Dei cognitionem Angelus habere non potuit per sua naturalia, ut supra ostensum est. Visio autem specularis Angelis non convenit, quia non accipiunt divinam cognitionem e rebus sensibilibus, ut dicit Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo Angeli per sua naturalia Deum cognoscere non possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0052","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0053","text":"Sed contra, Angeli sunt potentiores in cognoscendo quam homines. Sed homines per sua naturalia Deum cognoscere possunt; secundum illud Rom. I, *quod notum est Dei, manifestum est in illis*. Ergo multo magis Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0054","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0055","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angeli aliquam cognitionem de Deo habere possunt per sua naturalia. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod aliquid tripliciter cognoscitur. Uno modo, per praesentiam suae essentiae in cognoscente, sicut si lux videatur in oculo, et sic dictum est quod Angelus intelligit seipsum. Alio modo, per praesentiam suae similitudinis in potentia cognoscitiva, sicut lapis videtur ab oculo per hoc quod similitudo eius resultat in oculo. Tertio modo, per hoc quod similitudo rei cognitae non accipitur immediate ab ipsa re cognita, sed a re alia, in qua resultat, sicut cum videmus hominem in speculo. Primae igitur cognitioni assimilatur divina cognitio, qua per essentiam suam videtur. Et haec cognitio Dei non potest adesse creaturae alicui per sua naturalia, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0056","text":"Tertiae autem cognitioni assimilatur cognitio qua nos cognoscimus Deum in via, per similitudinem eius in creaturis resultantem; secundum illud Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*. Unde et dicimur Deum videre in speculo. Cognitio autem qua Angelus per sua naturalia cognoscit Deum, media est inter has duas; et similatur illi cognitioni qua videtur res per speciem ab ea acceptam. Quia enim imago Dei est in ipsa natura Angeli impressa per suam essentiam, Angelus Deum cognoscit, inquantum est similitudo Dei. Non tamen ipsam essentiam Dei videt, quia nulla similitudo creata est sufficiens ad repraesentandam divinam essentiam. Unde magis ista cognitio tenet se cum speculari, quia et ipsa natura angelica est quoddam speculum divinam similitudinem repraesentans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0057","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Dionysius loquitur de cognitione comprehensionis, ut expresse eius verba ostendunt. Et sic a nullo intellectu creato cognoscitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0059","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod propter hoc quod intellectus et essentia Angeli in infinitum distant a Deo, sequitur quod non possit ipsum comprehendere, nec per suam naturam eius essentiam videre. Non tamen sequitur propter hoc, quod nullam eius cognitionem habere possit, quia sicut Deus in infinitum distat ab Angelo, ita cognitio quam Deus habet de seipso, in infinitum distat a cognitione quam Angelus habet de eo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0061","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod cognitio quam naturaliter Angelus habet de Deo, est media inter utramque cognitionem, et tamen magis se tenet cum una, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"94df1e95f30a9d453735f63693b0e4e68636df1354641d5529cd48040d9afc97","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T144017104773Z_9ea479ff","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 56","source_sha256":"94df1e95f30a9d453735f63693b0e4e68636df1354641d5529cd48040d9afc97","sha256":"ec610e609956608acc7f1b575d3dcf1f52148603c534ff43d37ce4eb2af1f37f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:40:17.104786+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0002","text":"[I q. 56 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0003","text":"Next, inquiry is made concerning the angels' knowledge on the part of the things that they know. First, concerning their knowledge of immaterial things; second, concerning their knowledge of material things. Concerning the first, three questions are asked. First, whether an angel knows himself. Second, whether one knows another. Third, whether an angel knows God through his natural powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0005","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0006","text":"The first is approached as follows. It seems that an angel does not know himself. For Dionysius says, in chapter VI of the *Angelic Hierarchy*, that angels are ignorant of their own powers. But when a substance is known, its power is known. Therefore an angel does not know his essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0007","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0008","text":"Moreover, an angel is a certain singular substance; otherwise he would not act, since acts belong to singular subsistent things. But no singular is intelligible. Therefore he cannot be understood. And thus, since no cognition but intellectual cognition belongs to an angel, no angel will be able to know himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0009","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0010","text":"Moreover, the intellect is moved by the intelligible, because to understand is a kind of being acted upon, as is said in *On the Soul* III. But nothing is moved or acted upon by itself, as is apparent in bodily things. Therefore an angel cannot understand himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0011","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in II *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that *the angel, in his very formation—that is, in the illumination of truth—knew himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0013","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as is clear from what was said above, an object is related differently in an action that remains in the agent and in an action that passes over into something external. For in an action that passes over into something external, the object, or matter, into which the action passes is separate from the agent, as the heated thing is from the heater, and the built thing from the builder. But in an action that remains in the agent, in order for the action to proceed, the object must be united to the agent, just as the sensible thing must be united to the sense in order that it may actually sense. And thus the object united to a power is related to an action of this kind as the form that is the principle of action in other agents; for just as heat is the formal principle of heating in fire, so the species of the thing seen is the formal principle of vision in the eye.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0015","text":"But it must be considered that a species of the object of this kind is sometimes only potentially in the cognitive power, and then the knower is only potentially knowing; and in order that it may know actually, it is required that the cognitive power be reduced to the act of the species. But if it always has it actually, it can nevertheless know through it without any preceding change or reception. From this it is clear that to be moved by an object does not belong to the nature of a knower insofar as it is a knower, but insofar as it is a cognitive power. Now, for a form to be a principle of action, it makes no difference whether that form inheres in another or subsists through itself; for heat would not heat less if it subsisted through itself than it heats while inhering. Thus, therefore, if something in the genus of intelligible things is related as a subsisting intelligible form, it will understand itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0016","text":"But an angel, since he is immaterial, is a subsisting form, and thereby actually intelligible. Hence it follows that through his form, which is his substance, he understands himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0017","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that text is from the old translation, which is corrected by the new one, in which it is said: moreover, that they themselves, namely the angels, knew their own powers; in place of which the other translation had: and that they too are ignorant of their own powers. Although the text of the old translation can also be upheld insofar as angels do not know their power perfectly, according as it proceeds from the order of divine wisdom, which is incomprehensible to angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0019","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that singular things which are in bodily things are not objects of intellect for us, not by reason of their singularity, but by reason of the matter that is in them, which is the principle of individuation. Hence, if there are any singular things subsisting without matter, as angels do, nothing prevents them from being actually intelligible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0021","text":"[I q. 56 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that to be moved and to be acted upon belongs to the intellect insofar as it is in potency. Hence it has no place in the angelic intellect, especially as regards its understanding itself. Moreover, the action of the intellect is not of the same account as the action found in bodily things, which passes over into another matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0024","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that one angel does not know another. For the Philosopher says, in III *On the Soul*, that if the human intellect had within itself some nature among the number of the natures of sensible things, that nature existing within would prevent an external thing from appearing, just as, if the pupil were colored with some color, it could not see every color. But as the human intellect is related to knowing corporeal things, so the angelic intellect is related to knowing immaterial things. Therefore, since the angelic intellect has within itself some determinate nature among the number of those natures, it seems that it cannot know the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0026","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0027","text":"Moreover, it is said in the *Book of Causes* that *every intelligence is that which is above itself, insofar as it is caused by it; and that which is below itself, insofar as it is its cause*. But one angel is not the cause of another. Therefore, one angel does not know another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0028","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0029","text":"Moreover, one angel cannot know another through the essence of the knowing angel itself, since all knowledge is according to the notion of likeness; but the essence of the knowing angel is not like the essence of the known angel except in genus, as is clear from what was said above. Hence it would follow that one angel would not have proper knowledge of another, but only general knowledge. Likewise, neither can it be said that one angel knows another through the essence of the known angel, because that by which the intellect understands is intrinsic to the intellect; but the Trinity alone enters into the mind. Likewise, neither can it be said that one knows another through a species, because that species would not differ from the angel understood, since both are immaterial. Therefore, in no way does it seem that one angel can understand another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0030","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0031","text":"Moreover, if one angel understands another, this would be either through an innate species, and thus it would follow that, if God were now to create some angel anew, it could not be known by those who now exist; or through a species acquired from things, and thus it would follow that the higher angels could not know the lower, from whom they receive nothing. Therefore, in no way does it seem that one angel knows another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0032","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0033","text":"On the contrary, it is said in the *Book of Causes* that *every intelligence knows things that do not perish*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0034","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0035","text":"I answer that, as Augustine says in II *On Genesis according to the Letter*, those things which preexisted eternally in the Word of God flowed forth from it in two ways: in one way, into the angelic intellect; in another way, so as to subsist in their own natures. Now they proceeded into the angelic intellect through this, that God impressed upon the angelic mind likenesses of the things which He produced in natural being. But in the Word of God there existed eternally not only the notions of corporeal things, but also the notions of all spiritual creatures. Thus, therefore, upon each of the spiritual creatures there were impressed by the Word of God all the notions of all things, both corporeal and spiritual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0036","text":"Yet in such a way that upon each angel the notion of its own species was impressed according to natural and intelligible being simultaneously—namely, so that it might subsist in the nature of its species and understand itself through it—whereas the notions of other natures, both spiritual and corporeal, are impressed upon it according to intelligible being only, so that by such impressed species it might know both corporeal and spiritual creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0037","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the spiritual natures of angels are distinguished from one another by a certain order, as was said above. And thus the nature of one angel does not prevent its intellect from knowing the other natures of angels, since both the higher and the lower have an affinity with its nature, the difference being only according to diverse degrees of perfection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0039","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0040","text":"To the second, it must be said that the character of cause and caused does not bear upon one angel's knowing another, except by reason of likeness, insofar as cause and caused are alike. And therefore, if likeness without causality is posited among angels, knowledge of the one by the other will remain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0041","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0042","text":"To the third, it must be said that one angel knows another through its species existing in his intellect; this differs from the angel whose likeness it is, not according to material and immaterial being, but according to natural and intentional being. For the angel itself is a subsisting form in natural being, whereas its species, which is in the intellect of another angel, is not; rather, it has there intelligible being only. Just as the form of color in a wall has natural being, but in the transmitting medium it has intentional being only.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0043","text":"[I q. 56 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0044","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that God made each creature proportionate to the universe that He resolved to make. And therefore, if God had ordained to make more angels or more natures of things, He would have impressed more intelligible species upon angelic minds. Just as, if a builder had wished to make a larger house, he would have made a larger foundation. Hence it is of the same account that God should add some creature to the universe and some intelligible species to an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0045","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0046","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0047","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that angels cannot know God through their natural powers. For Dionysius says, in chapter I of *On the Divine Names*, that *God is established above all heavenly minds by an incomprehensible power*. And afterward he adds that, *because He is above every substance, He is removed from all knowledge*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0048","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0049","text":"Moreover, God is infinitely distant from the intellect of an angel. But things infinitely distant cannot be attained. Therefore it seems that an angel cannot know God through its natural powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0050","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0051","text":"Moreover, it is said in I Cor. XIII, *we see now through a glass in an enigma, but then face to face*. From this it seems that there is a twofold knowledge of God: one by which He is seen through His essence, according to which He is said to be seen face to face; another according to which He is seen in the mirror of creatures. But the angel could not have the first knowledge of God through its natural powers, as was shown above. But mirror-vision does not befit angels, because they do not receive divine knowledge from sensible things, as Dionysius says, ch. VII of *On the Divine Names*. Therefore angels cannot know God through their natural powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0052","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0053","text":"But on the contrary, angels are more powerful in knowing than human beings. But human beings can know God through their natural powers, according to Rom. I: *what is known of God is manifest in them*. Therefore much more so angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0054","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0055","text":"I answer that angels can have some knowledge of God through their natural powers. For the clarification of this, one must consider that a thing is known in three ways. In one way, through the presence of its essence in the knower, as when light is seen in the eye; and thus it was said that an angel understands itself. In another way, through the presence of its likeness in the cognitive power, as a stone is seen by the eye through the fact that its likeness is reflected in the eye. In a third way, because the likeness of the known thing is not received immediately from the known thing itself, but from another thing in which it is reflected, as when we see a man in a mirror. Therefore the divine knowledge by which He is seen through His essence is analogous to the first kind of knowledge. And this knowledge of God cannot belong to any creature through its natural powers, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0056","text":"But the knowledge by which we know God in the wayfarer-state, through His likeness reflected in creatures, is analogous to the third kind of knowledge; according to Rom. I: *the invisible things of God are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made*. Hence we are also said to see God in a mirror. But the knowledge by which an angel knows God through its natural powers is intermediate between these two; and it is analogous to that knowledge by which a thing is seen through a species received from it. For since the image of God is impressed upon the very nature of an angel through its essence, an angel knows God insofar as it is a likeness of God. Yet it does not see the very essence of God, because no created likeness is sufficient to represent the divine essence. Hence this knowledge is more akin to mirror-knowledge, because the angelic nature itself is a kind of mirror representing the divine likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0057","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0058","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Dionysius speaks of comprehensive knowledge, as his words expressly show. And thus God is known by no created intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0059","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0060","text":"To the second it must be said that, from the fact that the intellect and essence of an angel are infinitely distant from God, it follows that it cannot comprehend Him, nor see His essence through its own nature. Yet it does not follow from this that it can have no knowledge of Him, because just as God is infinitely distant from an angel, so the knowledge that God has of Himself is infinitely distant from the knowledge that an angel has of Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0061","text":"[I q. 56 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0062","text":"To the third it must be said that the knowledge which an angel naturally has of God is intermediate between the two kinds of knowledge, and yet is more akin to one of them, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_48:P0062"]}],"source_digest":"6cb10adf49e66fb016e834713b18b42bb1366e6e0235081a6436f2daed3f9f1b","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 56"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 56","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49","title":"First Part · Question 57","order":58,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_49","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 57 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30761)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de his materialibus quae ab Angelis cognoscuntur. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum Angeli cognoscant naturas rerum materialium. Secundo, utrum cognoscant singularia. Tertio, utrum cognoscant futura. Quarto, utrum cognoscant cogitationes cordium. Quinto, utrum cognoscant omnia mysteria gratiae.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30762)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non cognoscant res materiales. Intellectum enim est perfectio intelligentis. Res autem materiales non possunt esse perfectiones Angelorum, cum sint infra ipsos. Ergo Angeli non cognoscunt res materiales.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30763)\n\nPraeterea, visio intellectualis est eorum quae sunt in anima per sui essentiam, ut dicitur in Glossa, II ad Cor. XII. Sed res materiales non possunt esse in anima hominis, vel in mente Angeli, per suas essentias. Ergo non possunt intellectuali visione cognosci, sed solum imaginaria, qua apprehenduntur similitudines corporum; et sensibili, quae est de ipsis corporibus. In Angelis autem non est visio imaginaria et sensibilis, sed solum intellectualis. Ergo Angeli materialia cognoscere non possunt.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30764)\n\nPraeterea, res materiales non sunt intelligibiles in actu, sed sunt cognoscibiles apprehensione sensus et imaginationis; quae non est in Angelis. Ergo Angeli materialia non cognoscunt.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30765)\n\nSed contra, quidquid potest inferior virtus, potest virtus superior. Sed intellectus hominis, qui est ordine naturae infra intellectum Angeli, potest cognoscere res materiales. Ergo multo fortius intellectus Angeli.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30766)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod talis est ordo in rebus, quod superiora in entibus sunt perfectiora inferioribus, et quod in inferioribus continetur deficienter et partialiter et multipliciter, in superioribus continetur eminenter et per quandam totalitatem et simplicitatem. Et ideo in Deo, sicut in summo rerum vertice, omnia supersubstantialiter praeexistunt secundum ipsum suum simplex esse, ut Dionysius dicit, in libro de Div. Nom. Angeli autem inter ceteras creaturas sunt Deo propinquiores et similiores, unde et plura participant ex bonitate divina, et perfectius, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. Cael. Hier. Sic igitur omnia materialia in ipsis Angelis praeexistunt, simplicius quidem et immaterialius quam in ipsis rebus; multiplicius autem et imperfectius quam in Deo. Omne autem quod est in aliquo; est in eo per modum eius in quo est. Angeli autem secundum suam naturam sunt intellectuales. Et ideo, sicut Deus per suam essentiam materialia cognoscit, ita Angeli ea cognoscunt per hoc quod sunt in eis per suas intelligibiles species.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30767)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectum est perfectio intelligentis secundum speciem intelligibilem quam habet in intellectu. Et sic species intelligibiles quae sunt in intellectu Angeli, sunt perfectiones et actus intellectus angelici.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30768)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sensus non apprehendit essentias rerum, sed exteriora accidentia tantum. Similiter neque imaginatio, sed apprehendit solas similitudines corporum. Intellectus autem solus apprehendit essentias rerum. Unde in III de anima dicitur quod obiectum intellectus est quod quid est, circa quod non errat, sicut neque sensus circa proprium sensibile. Sic ergo essentiae rerum materialium sunt in intellectu hominis vel Angeli, ut intellectum est in intelligente, et non secundum esse suum reale. Quaedam vero sunt quae sunt in intellectu vel in anima secundum utrumque esse. Et utrorumque est visio intellectualis.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30769)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si Angelus acciperet cognitionem rerum materialium ab ipsis rebus materialibus, oporteret quod faceret eas intelligibiles actu, abstrahendo eas. Non autem accipit cognitionem earum a rebus materialibus, sed per species actu intelligibiles rerum sibi connaturales, rerum materialium notitiam habet; sicut intellectus noster secundum species quas intelligibiles facit abstrahendo.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30770)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus singularia non cognoscat. Dicit enim philosophus, in I Physic., quod sensus est singularium, ratio vero (vel intellectus) universalium. In Angelis autem non est vis cognoscitiva nisi intellectiva, ut ex superioribus patet. Ergo singularia non cognoscunt.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30771)\n\nPraeterea, omnis cognitio est per assimilationem aliquam cognoscentis ad cognitum. Sed non videtur quod possit esse aliqua assimilatio Angeli ad singulare inquantum est singulare, cum Angelus sit immaterialis, ut supra dictum est, singularitatis vero principium sit materia. Ergo Angelus non potest cognoscere singularia.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30772)\n\nPraeterea, si Angelus scit singularia, aut per species singulares, aut per species universales. Non per singulares, quia sic oporteret quod haberet species infinitas. Neque per universales, quia universale non est sufficiens principium cognoscendi singulare inquantum est singulare, cum in universali singularia non cognoscantur nisi in potentia. Ergo Angelus non cognoscit singularia.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30773)\n\nSed contra, nullus potest custodire quod non cognoscit. Sed Angeli custodiunt homines singulares, secundum illud Psalmi XC, *Angelis suis mandavit de te,* et cetera. Ergo Angeli cognoscunt singularia.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30774)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam totaliter subtraxerunt Angelis singularium cognitionem. Sed hoc primo quidem derogat Catholicae fidei, quae ponit haec inferiora administrari per Angelos, secundum illud Heb. I, *omnes sunt administratorii spiritus*. Si autem singularium notitiam non haberent, nullam providentiam habere possent de his quae in hoc mundo aguntur; cum actus singularium sint. Et hoc est contra illud quod dicitur Eccle. V, *ne dicas coram Angelo, non est providentia*. Secundo, etiam derogat philosophiae documentis, secundum quae ponuntur Angeli motores caelestium orbium, et quod eos moveant secundum intellectum et voluntatem. Et ideo alii dixerunt quod Angelus habet quidem cognitionem singularium, sed in causis universalibus, ad quas reducuntur particulares omnes effectus, sicut si astrologus iudicet de aliqua eclipsi futura, per dispositiones caelestium motuum. Sed haec positio praedicta inconvenientia non evadit, quia sic cognoscere singulare in causis universalibus, non est cognoscere ipsum ut est singulare, hoc est ut est hic et nunc. Astrologus enim cognoscens eclipsim futuram per computationem caelestium motuum, scit eam in universali; et non prout est hic et nunc, nisi per sensum accipiat. Administratio autem et providentia et motus sunt singularium, prout sunt hic et nunc. Et ideo aliter dicendum est quod, sicut homo cognoscit diversis viribus cognitivis omnia rerum genera, intellectu quidem universalia et immaterialia, sensu autem singularia et corporalia; ita Angelus per unam intellectivam virtutem utraque cognoscit. Hoc enim rerum ordo habet, quod quanto aliquid est superius, tanto habeat virtutem magis unitam et ad plura se extendentem, sicut in ipso homine patet quod sensus communis, qui est superior quam sensus proprius, licet sit unica potentia, omnia cognoscit quae quinque sensibus exterioribus cognoscuntur, et quaedam alia quae nullus sensus exterior cognoscit, scilicet differentiam albi et dulcis. Et simile etiam est in aliis considerare. Unde cum Angelus naturae ordine sit supra hominem, inconveniens est dicere quod homo quacumque sua potentia cognoscat aliquid, quod Angelus per unam vim suam cognoscitivam, scilicet intellectum, non cognoscat. Unde Aristoteles pro inconvenienti habet ut litem, quam nos scimus, Deus ignoret; ut patet in I de anima, et in III Metaphys. Modus autem quo intellectus Angeli singularia cognoscit, ex hoc considerari potest quod, sicut a Deo effluunt res ut subsistant in propriis naturis, ita etiam ut sint in cognitione angelica. Manifestum est autem quod a Deo effluit in rebus non solum illud quod ad naturam universalem pertinet, sed etiam ea quae sunt individuationis principia, est enim causa totius substantiae rei, et quantum ad materiam et quantum ad formam. Et secundum quod causat, sic et cognoscit, quia scientia eius est causa rei, ut supra ostensum est. Sicut igitur Deus per essentiam suam, per quam omnia causat, est similitudo omnium, et per eam omnia cognoscit non solum quantum ad naturas universales, sed etiam quantum ad singularitatem; ita Angeli per species a Deo inditas, res cognoscunt non solum quantum ad naturam universalem, sed etiam secundum earum singularitatem, inquantum sunt quaedam repraesentationes multiplicatae illius unicae et simplicis essentiae.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30775)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de intellectu nostro, qui non intelligit res nisi abstrahendo; et per ipsam abstractionem a materialibus conditionibus, id quod abstrahitur, fit universale. Hic autem modus intelligendi non convenit Angelis, ut supra dictum est, et ideo non est eadem ratio.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30776)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod secundum suam naturam Angeli non assimilantur rebus materialibus sicut assimilatur aliquid alicui secundum convenientiam in genere vel in specie, aut in accidente; sed sicut superius habet similitudinem cum inferiori, ut sol cum igne. Et per hunc etiam modum in Deo est similitudo omnium, et quantum ad formam et quantum ad materiam, inquantum in ipso praeexistit ut in causa quidquid in rebus invenitur. Et eadem ratione species intellectus Angeli, quae sunt quaedam derivatae similitudines a divina essentia, sunt similitudines rerum non solum quantum ad formam, sed etiam quantum ad materiam.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30777)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Angeli cognoscunt singularia per formas universales, quae tamen sunt similitudines rerum et quantum ad principia universalia, et quantum ad individuationis principia. Quomodo autem per eandem speciem possint multa cognosci iam supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30778)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli cognoscant futura. Angeli enim potentiores sunt in cognoscendo quam homines. Sed homines aliqui cognoscunt multa futura. Ergo multo fortius Angeli.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30779)\n\nPraeterea, praesens et futurum sunt differentiae temporis. Sed intellectus Angeli est supra tempus, parificatur enim intelligentia aeternitati, idest aevo, ut dicitur in libro de causis. Ergo quantum ad intellectum Angeli, non differunt praeteritum et futurum; sed indifferenter cognoscit utrumque.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30780)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus non cognoscit per species acceptas a rebus, sed per species innatas universales. Sed species universales aequaliter se habent ad praesens, praeteritum et futurum. Ergo videtur quod Angeli indifferenter cognoscant praeterita et praesentia et futura.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30781)\n\nPraeterea, sicut aliquid dicitur distans secundum tempus, ita secundum locum. Sed Angeli cognoscunt distantia secundum locum. Ergo etiam cognoscunt distantia secundum tempus futurum.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30782)\n\nSed contra, id quod est proprium signum divinitatis, non convenit Angelis. Sed cognoscere futura est proprium signum divinitatis; secundum illud Isaiae XLI, *annuntiate quae ventura sunt in futurum, et sciemus quod dii estis vos*. Ergo Angeli non cognoscunt futura.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30783)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod futurum dupliciter potest cognosci. Uno modo, in causa sua. Et sic futura quae ex necessitate ex causis suis proveniunt, per certam scientiam cognoscuntur, ut solem oriri cras. Quae vero ex suis causis proveniunt ut in pluribus, cognoscuntur non per certitudinem, sed per coniecturam; sicut medicus praecognoscit sanitatem infirmi. Et iste modus cognoscendi futura adest Angelis; et tanto magis quam nobis, quanto magis rerum causas et universalius et perfectius cognoscunt; sicut medici qui acutius vident causas, melius de futuro statu aegritudinis prognosticantur. Quae vero proveniunt ex causis suis ut in paucioribus, penitus sunt ignota, sicut casualia et fortuita. Alio modo cognoscuntur futura in seipsis. Et sic solius Dei est futura cognoscere, non solum quae ex necessitate proveniunt, vel ut in pluribus, sed etiam casualia et fortuita, quia Deus videt omnia in sua aeternitate, quae, cum sit simplex, toti tempori adest, et ipsum concludit. Et ideo unus Dei intuitus fertur in omnia quae aguntur per totum tempus sicut in praesentia, et videt omnia ut in seipsis sunt; sicut supra dictum est cum de Dei scientia ageretur. Angelicus autem intellectus, et quilibet intellectus creatus, deficit ab aeternitate divina. Unde non potest ab aliquo intellectu creato cognosci futurum, ut est in suo esse.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30784)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod homines non cognoscunt futura nisi in causis suis, vel Deo revelante. Et sic Angeli multo subtilius futura cognoscunt.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30785)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet intellectus Angeli sit supra tempus quo mensurantur corporales motus, est tamen in intellectu Angeli tempus secundum successionem intelligibilium conceptionum; secundum quod dicit Augustinus, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Deus movet spiritualem creaturam per tempus*. Et ita, cum sit successio in intellectu Angeli, non omnia quae aguntur per totum tempus, sunt ei praesentia.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30786)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet species quae sunt in intellectu Angeli, quantum est de se, aequaliter se habeant ad praesentia, praeterita et futura; tamen praesentia, praeterita et futura non aequaliter se habent ad rationes. Quia ea quae praesentia sunt, habent naturam per quam assimilantur speciebus quae sunt in mente Angeli, et sic per eas cognosci possunt. Sed quae futura sunt, nondum habent naturam per quam illis assimilentur, unde per eas cognosci non possunt.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30787)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod distantia secundum locum sunt iam in rerum natura, et participant aliquam speciem, cuius similitudo est in Angelo, quod non est verum de futuris, ut dictum est. Et ideo non est simile.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30788)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli cognoscant cogitationes cordium. Dicit enim Gregorius, in moralibus, super illud Iob XXVIII, *non aequabitur ei aurum vel vitrum*, quod tunc, scilicet in beatitudine resurgentium, unus *erit perspicabilis alteri sicut ipse sibi, et cum uniuscuiusque intellectus attenditur, simul conscientia penetratur*. Sed resurgentes erunt similes Angelis, sicut habetur Matth. XXII. Ergo unus Angelus potest videre id quod est in conscientia alterius.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30789)\n\nPraeterea, sicut se habent figurae ad corpora, ita se habent species intelligibiles ad intellectum. Sed viso corpore, videtur eius figura. Ergo visa substantia intellectuali, videtur species intelligibilis quae est in ipsa. Ergo, cum Angelus videat alium Angelum, et etiam animam, videtur quod possit videre cogitationem utriusque.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30790)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae sunt in intellectu nostro, sunt similiora Angelo quam ea quae sunt in phantasia, cum haec sint intellecta in actu, illa vero in potentia tantum. Sed ea quae sunt in phantasia, possunt cognosci ab Angelo sicut corporalia, cum phantasia sit virtus corporis. Ergo videtur quod Angelus possit cognoscere cogitationes intellectus.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30791)\n\nSed contra, quod est proprium Dei, non convenit Angelis. Sed cognoscere cogitationes cordium est proprium Dei, secundum illud Ierem. XVII, *pravum est cor hominis et inscrutabile, quis cognoscet illud? Ego, dominus, scrutans corda*. Ergo Angeli non cognoscunt secreta cordium.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30792)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod cogitatio cordis dupliciter potest cognosci. Uno modo, in suo effectu. Et sic non solum ab Angelo, sed etiam ab homine cognosci potest; et tanto subtilius, quanto effectus huiusmodi fuerit magis occultus. Cognoscitur enim cogitatio interdum non solum per actum exteriorem, sed etiam per immutationem vultus, et etiam medici aliquas affectiones animi per pulsum cognoscere possunt. Et multo magis Angeli, vel etiam Daemones, quanto subtilius huiusmodi immutationes occultas corporales perpendunt. Unde Augustinus dicit, in libro de divinatione Daemonum, quod *aliquando hominum dispositiones, non solum voce prolatas, verum etiam cogitatione conceptas, cum signa quaedam in corpore exprimuntur ex animo, tota facilitate perdiscunt*, quamvis in libro Retract. hoc dicat non esse asserendum quomodo fiat. Alio modo possunt cognosci cogitationes, prout sunt in intellectu; et affectiones, prout sunt in voluntate. Et sic solus Deus cogitationes cordium et affectiones voluntatum cognoscere potest. Cuius ratio est, quia voluntas rationalis creaturae soli Deo subiacet; et ipse solus in eam operari potest, qui est principale eius obiectum, ut ultimus finis; et hoc magis infra patebit. Et ideo ea quae in voluntate sunt, vel quae ex voluntate sola dependent, soli Deo sunt nota. Manifestum est autem quod ex sola voluntate dependet quod aliquis actu aliqua consideret, quia cum aliquis habet habitum scientiae, vel species intelligibiles in eo existentes, utitur eis cum vult. Et ideo dicit apostolus, I Cor. II, quod *quae sunt hominis, nemo novit nisi spiritus hominis, qui in ipso est*.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30793)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod modo cogitatio unius hominis non cognoscitur ab alio, propter duplex impedimentum, scilicet propter grossitiem corporis, et propter voluntatem claudentem sua secreta. Primum autem obstaculum tolletur in resurrectione, nec est in Angelis. Sed secundum impedimentum manebit post resurrectionem, et est modo in Angelis. Et tamen qualitatem mentis, quantum ad quantitatem gratiae et gloriae, repraesentabit claritas corporis. Et sic unus mentem alterius videre poterit.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30794)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, etsi unus Angelus, species intelligibiles alterius videat, per hoc quod modus intelligibilium specierum, secundum maiorem et minorem universalitatem, proportionatur nobilitati substantiarum; non tamen sequitur quod unus cognoscat quomodo alius illis intelligibilibus speciebus utitur actualiter considerando.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30795)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod appetitus brutalis non est dominus sui actus, sed sequitur impressionem alterius causae corporalis vel spiritualis. Quia igitur Angeli cognoscunt res corporales et dispositiones earum, possunt per haec cognoscere quod est in appetitu et in apprehensione phantastica brutorum animalium; et etiam hominum, secundum quod in eis quandoque appetitus sensitivus procedit in actum, sequens aliquam impressionem corporalem, sicut in brutis semper est. Non tamen oportet quod Angeli cognoscant motum appetitus sensitivi et apprehensionem phantasticam hominis, secundum quod moventur a voluntate et ratione, quia etiam inferior pars animae participat aliqualiter rationem, sicut obediens imperanti, ut dicitur in I Ethic. Nec tamen sequitur quod, si Angelus cognoscit quod est in appetitu sensitivo vel phantasia hominis, quod cognoscat id quod est in cogitatione vel voluntate, quia intellectus vel voluntas non subiacet appetitui sensitivo et phantasiae, sed potest eis diversimode uti.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30796)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli mysteria gratiae cognoscant. Quia inter omnia mysteria excellentius est mysterium incarnationis Christi. Sed hoc Angeli cognoverunt a principio, dicit enim Augustinus, V super Gen. ad Litt., quod *sic fuit hoc mysterium absconditum a saeculis in Deo, ut tamen innotesceret principibus et potestatibus in caelestibus*. Et dicit apostolus, I ad Tim. III, quod *apparuit Angelis illud magnum sacramentum pietatis*. Ergo Angeli mysteria gratiae cognoscunt.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30797)\n\nPraeterea, rationes omnium mysteriorum gratiae in divina sapientia continentur. Sed Angeli vident ipsam Dei sapientiam, quae est eius essentia. Ergo Angeli mysteria gratiae cognoscunt.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30798)\n\nPraeterea, prophetae per Angelos instruuntur, ut patet per Dionysium, IV cap. Angel. Hier. Sed prophetae mysteria gratiae cognoverunt, dicitur enim Amos III, *non faciet dominus verbum, nisi revelaverit secretum ad servos suos, prophetas*. Ergo Angeli mysteria gratiae cognoscunt.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30799)\n\nSed contra est quod nullus discit illud quod cognoscit. Sed Angeli, etiam supremi, quaerunt de divinis mysteriis gratiae, et ea discunt, dicitur enim VII cap. Cael. Hier., quod *sacra Scriptura inducit quasdam caelestes essentias ad ipsum Iesum quaestionem facientes, et addiscentes scientiam divinae eius operationis pro nobis, et Iesum eas sine medio docentem*; ut patet Isaiae LXIII, ubi quaerentibus Angelis, quis est iste qui venit de Edom? Respondit Iesus, ego, qui loquor iustitiam. Ergo Angeli non cognoscunt mysteria gratiae.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30800)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in Angelis est cognitio duplex. Una quidem naturalis, secundum quam cognoscunt res tum per essentiam suam, tum etiam per species innatas. Et hac cognitione mysteria gratiae Angeli cognoscere non possunt. Haec enim mysteria ex pura Dei voluntate dependent, si autem unus Angelus non potest cognoscere cogitationes alterius ex voluntate eius dependentes, multo minus potest cognoscere ea quae ex sola Dei voluntate dependent. Et sic argumentatur apostolus, I Cor. II, *quae sunt hominis, nemo novit nisi spiritus hominis, qui in ipso est. Ita et quae sunt Dei, nemo novit nisi spiritus Dei*. Est autem alia Angelorum cognitio, quae eos beatos facit, qua vident verbum et res in verbo. Et hac quidem visione cognoscunt mysteria gratiae, non quidem omnia, nec aequaliter omnes sed secundum quod Deus voluerit eis revelare; secundum illud apostoli, I Cor. II, *nobis autem revelavit Deus per spiritum suum*. Ita tamen quod superiores Angeli, perspicacius divinam sapientiam contemplantes, plura mysteria et altiora in ipsa Dei visione cognoscunt, quae inferioribus manifestant, eos illuminando. Et horum etiam mysteriorum quaedam a principio suae creationis cognoverunt; quaedam vero postmodum, secundum quod eorum officiis congruit, edocentur.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30801)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod de mysterio incarnationis Christi dupliciter contingit loqui. Uno modo, in generali, et sic omnibus revelatum est a principio suae beatitudinis. Cuius ratio est, quia hoc est quoddam generale principium, ad quod omnia eorum officia ordinantur, *omnes enim sunt administratorii spiritus*, ut dicitur Heb. I, *in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*; quod quidem fit per incarnationis mysterium. Unde oportuit de hoc mysterio omnes a principio communiter edoceri. Alio modo possumus loqui de mysterio incarnationis quantum ad speciales conditiones. Et sic non omnes Angeli a principio de omnibus sunt edocti, immo quidam, etiam superiores Angeli, postmodum didicerunt, ut patet per auctoritatem Dionysii inductam.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30802)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet Angeli beati divinam sapientiam contemplentur, non tamen eam comprehendunt. Et ideo non oportet quod cognoscant quidquid in ea latet.\n\n[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30803)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quidquid prophetae cognoverunt de mysteriis gratiae per revelationem divinam, multo excellentius est Angelis revelatum. Et licet prophetis ea quae Deus facturus erat circa salutem humani generis, in generali revelaverit; quaedam tamen specialia apostoli circa hoc cognoverunt, quae prophetae non cognoverant; secundum illud Ephes. III, *potestis, legentes, intelligere prudentiam meam in mysterio Christi, quod aliis generationibus non est agnitum, sicut nunc revelatum est sanctis apostolis eius*. Inter ipsos etiam prophetas, posteriores cognoverunt quod priores non cognoverant; secundum illud Psalmi CXVIII, *super senes intellexi*. Et Gregorius dicit quod per successiones temporum, crevit divinae cognitionis augmentum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0002","text":"[I q. 57 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de his materialibus quae ab Angelis cognoscuntur. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum Angeli cognoscant naturas rerum materialium. Secundo, utrum cognoscant singularia. Tertio, utrum cognoscant futura. Quarto, utrum cognoscant cogitationes cordium. Quinto, utrum cognoscant omnia mysteria gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0005","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non cognoscant res materiales. Intellectum enim est perfectio intelligentis. Res autem materiales non possunt esse perfectiones Angelorum, cum sint infra ipsos. Ergo Angeli non cognoscunt res materiales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0007","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0008","text":"Praeterea, visio intellectualis est eorum quae sunt in anima per sui essentiam, ut dicitur in Glossa, II ad Cor. XII. Sed res materiales non possunt esse in anima hominis, vel in mente Angeli, per suas essentias. Ergo non possunt intellectuali visione cognosci, sed solum imaginaria, qua apprehenduntur similitudines corporum; et sensibili, quae est de ipsis corporibus. In Angelis autem non est visio imaginaria et sensibilis, sed solum intellectualis. Ergo Angeli materialia cognoscere non possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0009","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0010","text":"Praeterea, res materiales non sunt intelligibiles in actu, sed sunt cognoscibiles apprehensione sensus et imaginationis; quae non est in Angelis. Ergo Angeli materialia non cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0011","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0012","text":"Sed contra, quidquid potest inferior virtus, potest virtus superior. Sed intellectus hominis, qui est ordine naturae infra intellectum Angeli, potest cognoscere res materiales. Ergo multo fortius intellectus Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0013","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod talis est ordo in rebus, quod superiora in entibus sunt perfectiora inferioribus, et quod in inferioribus continetur deficienter et partialiter et multipliciter, in superioribus continetur eminenter et per quandam totalitatem et simplicitatem. Et ideo in Deo, sicut in summo rerum vertice, omnia supersubstantialiter praeexistunt secundum ipsum suum simplex esse, ut Dionysius dicit, in libro de Div. Nom. Angeli autem inter ceteras creaturas sunt Deo propinquiores et similiores, unde et plura participant ex bonitate divina, et perfectius, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. Cael. Hier. Sic igitur omnia materialia in ipsis Angelis praeexistunt, simplicius quidem et immaterialius quam in ipsis rebus; multiplicius autem et imperfectius quam in Deo. Omne autem quod est in aliquo; est in eo per modum eius in quo est. Angeli autem secundum suam naturam sunt intellectuales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0015","text":"Et ideo, sicut Deus per suam essentiam materialia cognoscit, ita Angeli ea cognoscunt per hoc quod sunt in eis per suas intelligibiles species.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0016","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectum est perfectio intelligentis secundum speciem intelligibilem quam habet in intellectu. Et sic species intelligibiles quae sunt in intellectu Angeli, sunt perfectiones et actus intellectus angelici.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0018","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sensus non apprehendit essentias rerum, sed exteriora accidentia tantum. Similiter neque imaginatio, sed apprehendit solas similitudines corporum. Intellectus autem solus apprehendit essentias rerum. Unde in III de anima dicitur quod obiectum intellectus est quod quid est, circa quod non errat, sicut neque sensus circa proprium sensibile. Sic ergo essentiae rerum materialium sunt in intellectu hominis vel Angeli, ut intellectum est in intelligente, et non secundum esse suum reale. Quaedam vero sunt quae sunt in intellectu vel in anima secundum utrumque esse. Et utrorumque est visio intellectualis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0020","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si Angelus acciperet cognitionem rerum materialium ab ipsis rebus materialibus, oporteret quod faceret eas intelligibiles actu, abstrahendo eas. Non autem accipit cognitionem earum a rebus materialibus, sed per species actu intelligibiles rerum sibi connaturales, rerum materialium notitiam habet; sicut intellectus noster secundum species quas intelligibiles facit abstrahendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0023","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus singularia non cognoscat. Dicit enim philosophus, in I Physic., quod sensus est singularium, ratio vero (vel intellectus) universalium. In Angelis autem non est vis cognoscitiva nisi intellectiva, ut ex superioribus patet. Ergo singularia non cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0025","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0026","text":"Praeterea, omnis cognitio est per assimilationem aliquam cognoscentis ad cognitum. Sed non videtur quod possit esse aliqua assimilatio Angeli ad singulare inquantum est singulare, cum Angelus sit immaterialis, ut supra dictum est, singularitatis vero principium sit materia. Ergo Angelus non potest cognoscere singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0027","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0028","text":"Praeterea, si Angelus scit singularia, aut per species singulares, aut per species universales. Non per singulares, quia sic oporteret quod haberet species infinitas. Neque per universales, quia universale non est sufficiens principium cognoscendi singulare inquantum est singulare, cum in universali singularia non cognoscantur nisi in potentia. Ergo Angelus non cognoscit singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0029","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0030","text":"Sed contra, nullus potest custodire quod non cognoscit. Sed Angeli custodiunt homines singulares, secundum illud Psalmi XC, *Angelis suis mandavit de te,* et cetera. Ergo Angeli cognoscunt singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0031","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam totaliter subtraxerunt Angelis singularium cognitionem. Sed hoc primo quidem derogat Catholicae fidei, quae ponit haec inferiora administrari per Angelos, secundum illud Heb. I, *omnes sunt administratorii spiritus*. Si autem singularium notitiam non haberent, nullam providentiam habere possent de his quae in hoc mundo aguntur; cum actus singularium sint. Et hoc est contra illud quod dicitur Eccle. V, *ne dicas coram Angelo, non est providentia*. Secundo, etiam derogat philosophiae documentis, secundum quae ponuntur Angeli motores caelestium orbium, et quod eos moveant secundum intellectum et voluntatem. Et ideo alii dixerunt quod Angelus habet quidem cognitionem singularium, sed in causis universalibus, ad quas reducuntur particulares omnes effectus, sicut si astrologus iudicet de aliqua eclipsi futura, per dispositiones caelestium motuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0033","text":"Sed haec positio praedicta inconvenientia non evadit, quia sic cognoscere singulare in causis universalibus, non est cognoscere ipsum ut est singulare, hoc est ut est hic et nunc. Astrologus enim cognoscens eclipsim futuram per computationem caelestium motuum, scit eam in universali; et non prout est hic et nunc, nisi per sensum accipiat. Administratio autem et providentia et motus sunt singularium, prout sunt hic et nunc. Et ideo aliter dicendum est quod, sicut homo cognoscit diversis viribus cognitivis omnia rerum genera, intellectu quidem universalia et immaterialia, sensu autem singularia et corporalia; ita Angelus per unam intellectivam virtutem utraque cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0034","text":"Hoc enim rerum ordo habet, quod quanto aliquid est superius, tanto habeat virtutem magis unitam et ad plura se extendentem, sicut in ipso homine patet quod sensus communis, qui est superior quam sensus proprius, licet sit unica potentia, omnia cognoscit quae quinque sensibus exterioribus cognoscuntur, et quaedam alia quae nullus sensus exterior cognoscit, scilicet differentiam albi et dulcis. Et simile etiam est in aliis considerare. Unde cum Angelus naturae ordine sit supra hominem, inconveniens est dicere quod homo quacumque sua potentia cognoscat aliquid, quod Angelus per unam vim suam cognoscitivam, scilicet intellectum, non cognoscat. Unde Aristoteles pro inconvenienti habet ut litem, quam nos scimus, Deus ignoret; ut patet in I de anima, et in III Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0035","text":"Modus autem quo intellectus Angeli singularia cognoscit, ex hoc considerari potest quod, sicut a Deo effluunt res ut subsistant in propriis naturis, ita etiam ut sint in cognitione angelica. Manifestum est autem quod a Deo effluit in rebus non solum illud quod ad naturam universalem pertinet, sed etiam ea quae sunt individuationis principia, est enim causa totius substantiae rei, et quantum ad materiam et quantum ad formam. Et secundum quod causat, sic et cognoscit, quia scientia eius est causa rei, ut supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0036","text":"Sicut igitur Deus per essentiam suam, per quam omnia causat, est similitudo omnium, et per eam omnia cognoscit non solum quantum ad naturas universales, sed etiam quantum ad singularitatem; ita Angeli per species a Deo inditas, res cognoscunt non solum quantum ad naturam universalem, sed etiam secundum earum singularitatem, inquantum sunt quaedam repraesentationes multiplicatae illius unicae et simplicis essentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0037","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de intellectu nostro, qui non intelligit res nisi abstrahendo; et per ipsam abstractionem a materialibus conditionibus, id quod abstrahitur, fit universale. Hic autem modus intelligendi non convenit Angelis, ut supra dictum est, et ideo non est eadem ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0039","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod secundum suam naturam Angeli non assimilantur rebus materialibus sicut assimilatur aliquid alicui secundum convenientiam in genere vel in specie, aut in accidente; sed sicut superius habet similitudinem cum inferiori, ut sol cum igne. Et per hunc etiam modum in Deo est similitudo omnium, et quantum ad formam et quantum ad materiam, inquantum in ipso praeexistit ut in causa quidquid in rebus invenitur. Et eadem ratione species intellectus Angeli, quae sunt quaedam derivatae similitudines a divina essentia, sunt similitudines rerum non solum quantum ad formam, sed etiam quantum ad materiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0041","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Angeli cognoscunt singularia per formas universales, quae tamen sunt similitudines rerum et quantum ad principia universalia, et quantum ad individuationis principia. Quomodo autem per eandem speciem possint multa cognosci iam supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0043","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0044","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0045","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli cognoscant futura. Angeli enim potentiores sunt in cognoscendo quam homines. Sed homines aliqui cognoscunt multa futura. Ergo multo fortius Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0046","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0047","text":"Praeterea, praesens et futurum sunt differentiae temporis. Sed intellectus Angeli est supra tempus, parificatur enim intelligentia aeternitati, idest aevo, ut dicitur in libro de causis. Ergo quantum ad intellectum Angeli, non differunt praeteritum et futurum; sed indifferenter cognoscit utrumque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0048","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0049","text":"Praeterea, Angelus non cognoscit per species acceptas a rebus, sed per species innatas universales. Sed species universales aequaliter se habent ad praesens, praeteritum et futurum. Ergo videtur quod Angeli indifferenter cognoscant praeterita et praesentia et futura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0050","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0051","text":"Praeterea, sicut aliquid dicitur distans secundum tempus, ita secundum locum. Sed Angeli cognoscunt distantia secundum locum. Ergo etiam cognoscunt distantia secundum tempus futurum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0052","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0053","text":"Sed contra, id quod est proprium signum divinitatis, non convenit Angelis. Sed cognoscere futura est proprium signum divinitatis; secundum illud Isaiae XLI, *annuntiate quae ventura sunt in futurum, et sciemus quod dii estis vos*. Ergo Angeli non cognoscunt futura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0054","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0055","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod futurum dupliciter potest cognosci. Uno modo, in causa sua. Et sic futura quae ex necessitate ex causis suis proveniunt, per certam scientiam cognoscuntur, ut solem oriri cras. Quae vero ex suis causis proveniunt ut in pluribus, cognoscuntur non per certitudinem, sed per coniecturam; sicut medicus praecognoscit sanitatem infirmi. Et iste modus cognoscendi futura adest Angelis; et tanto magis quam nobis, quanto magis rerum causas et universalius et perfectius cognoscunt; sicut medici qui acutius vident causas, melius de futuro statu aegritudinis prognosticantur. Quae vero proveniunt ex causis suis ut in paucioribus, penitus sunt ignota, sicut casualia et fortuita. Alio modo cognoscuntur futura in seipsis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0056","text":"Et sic solius Dei est futura cognoscere, non solum quae ex necessitate proveniunt, vel ut in pluribus, sed etiam casualia et fortuita, quia Deus videt omnia in sua aeternitate, quae, cum sit simplex, toti tempori adest, et ipsum concludit. Et ideo unus Dei intuitus fertur in omnia quae aguntur per totum tempus sicut in praesentia, et videt omnia ut in seipsis sunt; sicut supra dictum est cum de Dei scientia ageretur. Angelicus autem intellectus, et quilibet intellectus creatus, deficit ab aeternitate divina. Unde non potest ab aliquo intellectu creato cognosci futurum, ut est in suo esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0057","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod homines non cognoscunt futura nisi in causis suis, vel Deo revelante. Et sic Angeli multo subtilius futura cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0059","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet intellectus Angeli sit supra tempus quo mensurantur corporales motus, est tamen in intellectu Angeli tempus secundum successionem intelligibilium conceptionum; secundum quod dicit Augustinus, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Deus movet spiritualem creaturam per tempus*. Et ita, cum sit successio in intellectu Angeli, non omnia quae aguntur per totum tempus, sunt ei praesentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0061","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet species quae sunt in intellectu Angeli, quantum est de se, aequaliter se habeant ad praesentia, praeterita et futura; tamen praesentia, praeterita et futura non aequaliter se habent ad rationes. Quia ea quae praesentia sunt, habent naturam per quam assimilantur speciebus quae sunt in mente Angeli, et sic per eas cognosci possunt. Sed quae futura sunt, nondum habent naturam per quam illis assimilentur, unde per eas cognosci non possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0063","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0064","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod distantia secundum locum sunt iam in rerum natura, et participant aliquam speciem, cuius similitudo est in Angelo, quod non est verum de futuris, ut dictum est. Et ideo non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0065","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0066","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0067","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli cognoscant cogitationes cordium. Dicit enim Gregorius, in moralibus, super illud Iob XXVIII, *non aequabitur ei aurum vel vitrum*, quod tunc, scilicet in beatitudine resurgentium, unus *erit perspicabilis alteri sicut ipse sibi, et cum uniuscuiusque intellectus attenditur, simul conscientia penetratur*. Sed resurgentes erunt similes Angelis, sicut habetur Matth. XXII. Ergo unus Angelus potest videre id quod est in conscientia alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0068","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0069","text":"Praeterea, sicut se habent figurae ad corpora, ita se habent species intelligibiles ad intellectum. Sed viso corpore, videtur eius figura. Ergo visa substantia intellectuali, videtur species intelligibilis quae est in ipsa. Ergo, cum Angelus videat alium Angelum, et etiam animam, videtur quod possit videre cogitationem utriusque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0070","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0071","text":"Praeterea, ea quae sunt in intellectu nostro, sunt similiora Angelo quam ea quae sunt in phantasia, cum haec sint intellecta in actu, illa vero in potentia tantum. Sed ea quae sunt in phantasia, possunt cognosci ab Angelo sicut corporalia, cum phantasia sit virtus corporis. Ergo videtur quod Angelus possit cognoscere cogitationes intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0072","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0073","text":"Sed contra, quod est proprium Dei, non convenit Angelis. Sed cognoscere cogitationes cordium est proprium Dei, secundum illud Ierem. XVII, *pravum est cor hominis et inscrutabile, quis cognoscet illud? Ego, dominus, scrutans corda*. Ergo Angeli non cognoscunt secreta cordium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0074","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod cogitatio cordis dupliciter potest cognosci. Uno modo, in suo effectu. Et sic non solum ab Angelo, sed etiam ab homine cognosci potest; et tanto subtilius, quanto effectus huiusmodi fuerit magis occultus. Cognoscitur enim cogitatio interdum non solum per actum exteriorem, sed etiam per immutationem vultus, et etiam medici aliquas affectiones animi per pulsum cognoscere possunt. Et multo magis Angeli, vel etiam Daemones, quanto subtilius huiusmodi immutationes occultas corporales perpendunt. Unde Augustinus dicit, in libro de divinatione Daemonum, quod *aliquando hominum dispositiones, non solum voce prolatas, verum etiam cogitatione conceptas, cum signa quaedam in corpore exprimuntur ex animo, tota facilitate perdiscunt*, quamvis in libro Retract. hoc dicat non esse asserendum quomodo fiat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0076","text":"Alio modo possunt cognosci cogitationes, prout sunt in intellectu; et affectiones, prout sunt in voluntate. Et sic solus Deus cogitationes cordium et affectiones voluntatum cognoscere potest. Cuius ratio est, quia voluntas rationalis creaturae soli Deo subiacet; et ipse solus in eam operari potest, qui est principale eius obiectum, ut ultimus finis; et hoc magis infra patebit. Et ideo ea quae in voluntate sunt, vel quae ex voluntate sola dependent, soli Deo sunt nota. Manifestum est autem quod ex sola voluntate dependet quod aliquis actu aliqua consideret, quia cum aliquis habet habitum scientiae, vel species intelligibiles in eo existentes, utitur eis cum vult. Et ideo dicit apostolus, I Cor. II, quod *quae sunt hominis, nemo novit nisi spiritus hominis, qui in ipso est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0077","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0078","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod modo cogitatio unius hominis non cognoscitur ab alio, propter duplex impedimentum, scilicet propter grossitiem corporis, et propter voluntatem claudentem sua secreta. Primum autem obstaculum tolletur in resurrectione, nec est in Angelis. Sed secundum impedimentum manebit post resurrectionem, et est modo in Angelis. Et tamen qualitatem mentis, quantum ad quantitatem gratiae et gloriae, repraesentabit claritas corporis. Et sic unus mentem alterius videre poterit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0079","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0080","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, etsi unus Angelus, species intelligibiles alterius videat, per hoc quod modus intelligibilium specierum, secundum maiorem et minorem universalitatem, proportionatur nobilitati substantiarum; non tamen sequitur quod unus cognoscat quomodo alius illis intelligibilibus speciebus utitur actualiter considerando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0081","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0082","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod appetitus brutalis non est dominus sui actus, sed sequitur impressionem alterius causae corporalis vel spiritualis. Quia igitur Angeli cognoscunt res corporales et dispositiones earum, possunt per haec cognoscere quod est in appetitu et in apprehensione phantastica brutorum animalium; et etiam hominum, secundum quod in eis quandoque appetitus sensitivus procedit in actum, sequens aliquam impressionem corporalem, sicut in brutis semper est. Non tamen oportet quod Angeli cognoscant motum appetitus sensitivi et apprehensionem phantasticam hominis, secundum quod moventur a voluntate et ratione, quia etiam inferior pars animae participat aliqualiter rationem, sicut obediens imperanti, ut dicitur in I Ethic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0083","text":"Nec tamen sequitur quod, si Angelus cognoscit quod est in appetitu sensitivo vel phantasia hominis, quod cognoscat id quod est in cogitatione vel voluntate, quia intellectus vel voluntas non subiacet appetitui sensitivo et phantasiae, sed potest eis diversimode uti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0084","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0085","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0086","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli mysteria gratiae cognoscant. Quia inter omnia mysteria excellentius est mysterium incarnationis Christi. Sed hoc Angeli cognoverunt a principio, dicit enim Augustinus, V super Gen. ad Litt., quod *sic fuit hoc mysterium absconditum a saeculis in Deo, ut tamen innotesceret principibus et potestatibus in caelestibus*. Et dicit apostolus, I ad Tim. III, quod *apparuit Angelis illud magnum sacramentum pietatis*. Ergo Angeli mysteria gratiae cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0087","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0088","text":"Praeterea, rationes omnium mysteriorum gratiae in divina sapientia continentur. Sed Angeli vident ipsam Dei sapientiam, quae est eius essentia. Ergo Angeli mysteria gratiae cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0089","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0090","text":"Praeterea, prophetae per Angelos instruuntur, ut patet per Dionysium, IV cap. Angel. Hier. Sed prophetae mysteria gratiae cognoverunt, dicitur enim Amos III, *non faciet dominus verbum, nisi revelaverit secretum ad servos suos, prophetas*. Ergo Angeli mysteria gratiae cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0091","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0092","text":"Sed contra est quod nullus discit illud quod cognoscit. Sed Angeli, etiam supremi, quaerunt de divinis mysteriis gratiae, et ea discunt, dicitur enim VII cap. Cael. Hier., quod *sacra Scriptura inducit quasdam caelestes essentias ad ipsum Iesum quaestionem facientes, et addiscentes scientiam divinae eius operationis pro nobis, et Iesum eas sine medio docentem*; ut patet Isaiae LXIII, ubi quaerentibus Angelis, quis est iste qui venit de Edom? Respondit Iesus, ego, qui loquor iustitiam. Ergo Angeli non cognoscunt mysteria gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0093","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0094","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in Angelis est cognitio duplex. Una quidem naturalis, secundum quam cognoscunt res tum per essentiam suam, tum etiam per species innatas. Et hac cognitione mysteria gratiae Angeli cognoscere non possunt. Haec enim mysteria ex pura Dei voluntate dependent, si autem unus Angelus non potest cognoscere cogitationes alterius ex voluntate eius dependentes, multo minus potest cognoscere ea quae ex sola Dei voluntate dependent. Et sic argumentatur apostolus, I Cor. II, *quae sunt hominis, nemo novit nisi spiritus hominis, qui in ipso est. Ita et quae sunt Dei, nemo novit nisi spiritus Dei*. Est autem alia Angelorum cognitio, quae eos beatos facit, qua vident verbum et res in verbo. Et hac quidem visione cognoscunt mysteria gratiae, non quidem omnia, nec aequaliter omnes sed secundum quod Deus voluerit eis revelare; secundum illud apostoli, I Cor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0095","text":"II, *nobis autem revelavit Deus per spiritum suum*. Ita tamen quod superiores Angeli, perspicacius divinam sapientiam contemplantes, plura mysteria et altiora in ipsa Dei visione cognoscunt, quae inferioribus manifestant, eos illuminando. Et horum etiam mysteriorum quaedam a principio suae creationis cognoverunt; quaedam vero postmodum, secundum quod eorum officiis congruit, edocentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0096","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0097","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod de mysterio incarnationis Christi dupliciter contingit loqui. Uno modo, in generali, et sic omnibus revelatum est a principio suae beatitudinis. Cuius ratio est, quia hoc est quoddam generale principium, ad quod omnia eorum officia ordinantur, *omnes enim sunt administratorii spiritus*, ut dicitur Heb. I, *in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*; quod quidem fit per incarnationis mysterium. Unde oportuit de hoc mysterio omnes a principio communiter edoceri. Alio modo possumus loqui de mysterio incarnationis quantum ad speciales conditiones. Et sic non omnes Angeli a principio de omnibus sunt edocti, immo quidam, etiam superiores Angeli, postmodum didicerunt, ut patet per auctoritatem Dionysii inductam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0098","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0099","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet Angeli beati divinam sapientiam contemplentur, non tamen eam comprehendunt. Et ideo non oportet quod cognoscant quidquid in ea latet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0100","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0101","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quidquid prophetae cognoverunt de mysteriis gratiae per revelationem divinam, multo excellentius est Angelis revelatum. Et licet prophetis ea quae Deus facturus erat circa salutem humani generis, in generali revelaverit; quaedam tamen specialia apostoli circa hoc cognoverunt, quae prophetae non cognoverant; secundum illud Ephes. III, *potestis, legentes, intelligere prudentiam meam in mysterio Christi, quod aliis generationibus non est agnitum, sicut nunc revelatum est sanctis apostolis eius*. Inter ipsos etiam prophetas, posteriores cognoverunt quod priores non cognoverant; secundum illud Psalmi CXVIII, *super senes intellexi*. Et Gregorius dicit quod per successiones temporum, crevit divinae cognitionis augmentum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fbb85820dc8f02a3d6a1dbe609347158851ec659de45fa28a502130c7fe961b2","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T144137163975Z_4fa18e80","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 57","source_sha256":"fbb85820dc8f02a3d6a1dbe609347158851ec659de45fa28a502130c7fe961b2","sha256":"fa787154ded65a3f140dc72faca1a8b55904b603071b498252acde7cf198bbb8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:41:37.164023+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0002","text":"[I q. 57 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0003","text":"Next, inquiry is made concerning those material things which are known by the Angels. And concerning this, five questions are asked. First, whether Angels know the natures of material things. Second, whether they know singulars. Third, whether they know future things. Fourth, whether they know the thoughts of hearts. Fifth, whether they know all the mysteries of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0005","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that Angels do not know material things. For the thing understood is a perfection of the one understanding. But material things cannot be perfections of Angels, since they are below them. Therefore Angels do not know material things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0007","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0008","text":"Further, intellectual vision is of those things which are in the soul through their essence, as is said in the Gloss on 2 Cor. XII. But material things cannot be in the human soul, or in the angelic mind, through their essences. Therefore they cannot be known by intellectual vision, but only by imaginary vision, by which likenesses of bodies are apprehended; and by sensible vision, which is of the bodies themselves. But in Angels there is no imaginary or sensible vision, but only intellectual vision. Therefore Angels cannot know material things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0009","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0010","text":"Further, material things are not actually intelligible, but are knowable by the apprehension of sense and imagination; which is not in Angels. Therefore Angels do not know material things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0011","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0012","text":"On the contrary, whatever a lower power can do, a higher power can do. But the human intellect, which in the order of nature is below the angelic intellect, can know material things. Therefore much more can the angelic intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0013","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that such is the order in things that higher beings are more perfect than lower beings, and what is contained deficiently and partially and multiply in lower beings is contained eminently and with a certain totality and simplicity in higher beings. And therefore in God, as at the highest summit of things, all things pre-exist super-substantially according to their own simple being, as Dionysius says in the book On the Divine Names. But Angels, among the other creatures, are nearer and more like to God; hence they participate more in the divine goodness, and more perfectly, as Dionysius says in chapter IV of the Celestial Hierarchy. Thus, therefore, all material things pre-exist in the Angels more simply indeed and more immaterially than in the things themselves, but more multiply and imperfectly than in God. But everything which is in something is in it according to the mode of that in which it is. Now Angels are intellectual according to their nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0015","text":"And therefore, just as God knows material things through His essence, so Angels know them through the fact that they are in them by their intelligible species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0016","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the thing understood is a perfection of the one understanding according to the intelligible species which he has in his intellect. And thus the intelligible species which are in the angelic intellect are perfections and acts of the angelic intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0018","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that sense does not apprehend the essences of things, but only their exterior accidents. Likewise, neither does imagination, but it apprehends only likenesses of bodies. But intellect alone apprehends the essences of things. Hence it is said in III On the Soul that the object of intellect is the what-it-is, concerning which it does not err, just as neither does sense concerning its proper sensible. Thus, therefore, the essences of material things are in the intellect of a human being or an Angel, as the thing understood is in the one understanding, and not according to their real being. But some things are in the intellect or in the soul according to both kinds of being. And there is intellectual vision of both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0020","text":"[I q. 57 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if an Angel received knowledge of material things from the material things themselves, it would be necessary that he make them actually intelligible by abstracting them. But he does not receive knowledge of them from material things; rather, through actually intelligible species of things connatural to himself, he has knowledge of material things, just as our intellect has it according to the species which it makes intelligible by abstraction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0023","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that an Angel does not know singulars. For the Philosopher says, in I Physics, that sense is of singulars, whereas reason (or intellect) is of universals. But in Angels there is no cognitive power except an intellectual one, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore they do not know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0025","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0026","text":"Further, all knowledge is through some assimilation of the knower to the known. But it does not seem that there can be any assimilation of an angel to a singular thing insofar as it is singular, since an angel is immaterial, as stated above, whereas the principle of singularity is matter. Therefore an angel cannot know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0027","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0028","text":"Further, if an angel knows singulars, he does so either through singular species or through universal species. Not through singular species, because then he would have to possess infinitely many species. Nor through universal species, because the universal is not a sufficient principle for knowing the singular insofar as it is singular, since singulars are known in the universal only potentially. Therefore an angel does not know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0029","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0030","text":"On the contrary, no one can guard what he does not know. But angels guard individual human beings, according to that passage of Psalm XC, *He has commanded His angels concerning you,* and so forth. Therefore angels know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0031","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0032","text":"I answer that some have entirely denied to angels knowledge of singulars. But this, first, detracts from the Catholic faith, which holds that these lower things are administered by angels, according to Hebrews I: *they are all ministering spirits*. But if they had no knowledge of singulars, they could have no providence over those things that take place in this world, since acts belong to singulars. And this is contrary to what is said in Ecclesiastes V: *do not say before the angel, “There is no providence.”* Secondly, it also detracts from the teachings of philosophy, according to which angels are held to be movers of the celestial spheres, and to move them according to intellect and will. Therefore others have said that an angel does indeed have knowledge of singulars, but in universal causes, to which all particular effects are reduced; as when an astronomer judges of some future eclipse through the dispositions of the celestial motions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0033","text":"But the aforesaid position does not escape the stated difficulties, because to know a singular in universal causes in this way is not to know it as it is singular, that is, as it is here and now. For an astronomer who knows a future eclipse through a calculation of the celestial motions knows it universally, and not as it is here and now, unless he receives it through sense. Administration, however, providence, and motion concern singulars as they are here and now. Therefore one must say otherwise: just as man knows all kinds of things through diverse cognitive powers—universals and immaterial things by intellect, but singulars and bodily things by sense—so an angel knows both through one intellective power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0034","text":"For the order of things is such that, the higher something is, the more unified is its power and the more things it extends to; as is evident in man himself, since common sense, which is higher than the particular sense, although it is one power, knows everything known by the five external senses, and certain other things that no external sense knows, namely, the difference between white and sweet. The same may likewise be considered in other cases. Hence, since by the order of nature an angel is above man, it is unfitting to say that man knows something by any one of his powers which an angel does not know through his one cognitive power, namely, intellect. Hence Aristotle considers it unfitting that God should be ignorant of a dispute which we know, as is clear in *On the Soul* I and *Metaphysics* III.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0035","text":"The manner in which an angel's intellect knows singulars may be considered from this: that, just as things flow forth from God so as to subsist in their own natures, so too they do so as to be in angelic cognition. Now it is clear that from God there flows forth into things not only that which pertains to universal nature, but also those things which are principles of individuation; for He is the cause of the whole substance of a thing, both as to matter and as to form. And as He causes, so He also knows, because His knowledge is the cause of the thing, as was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0036","text":"Therefore, just as God, through His essence, by which He causes all things, is the likeness of all things, and through it knows all things not only as to universal natures but also as to singularity, so angels, through species bestowed by God, know things not only as to their universal nature but also according to their singularity, insofar as they are certain multiplied representations of that one and simple essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0037","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher speaks of our intellect, which understands things only by abstracting; and through that very abstraction from material conditions, what is abstracted becomes universal. But this mode of understanding does not befit angels, as stated above; and therefore the reasoning is not the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0039","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0040","text":"To the second, it must be said that according to their nature angels are not assimilated to material things as one thing is assimilated to another by agreement in genus or species, or in an accident; but as what is higher has a likeness to what is lower, as the sun to fire. And in this manner there is also in God a likeness of all things, both as to form and as to matter, insofar as whatever is found in things preexists in Him as in a cause. And for the same reason, the species of an angel's intellect, which are certain likenesses derived from the divine essence, are likenesses of things not only as to form but also as to matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0041","text":"[I q. 57 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0042","text":"To the third, it must be said that angels know singulars through universal forms, which nevertheless are likenesses of things both as to universal principles and as to principles of individuation. But how many things can be known through the same species has already been stated above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0043","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0044","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0045","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that angels know future things. For angels are more powerful in knowing than men. But some men know many future things. Therefore angels do so much more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0046","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0047","text":"Further, present and future are differences of time. But an angel's intellect is above time; for intelligence is equated with eternity, that is, with the aevum, as is said in the *Book of Causes*. Therefore, as regards an angel's intellect, past and future do not differ; rather, it knows each indifferently.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0048","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0049","text":"Moreover, an angel does not know through species received from things, but through innate universal species. But universal species stand equally in relation to the present, the past, and the future. Therefore, it seems that angels know past, present, and future things indiscriminately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0050","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0051","text":"Moreover, just as something is said to be distant according to time, so also according to place. But angels know things distant according to place. Therefore, they also know things distant according to future time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0052","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0053","text":"On the contrary, that which is a proper sign of divinity does not belong to angels. But to know future things is a proper sign of divinity; according to that passage of Isaiah 41, *declare the things that are to come in the future, and we shall know that you are gods*. Therefore, angels do not know future things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0054","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0055","text":"I answer that it must be said that the future can be known in two ways. In one way, in its cause. And thus future things that proceed necessarily from their causes are known with certain knowledge, as that the sun will rise tomorrow. But those which proceed from their causes for the most part are known not with certainty, but by conjecture; as a physician foreknows the recovery of a sick person. And this mode of knowing future things is present to angels, and so much more than to us as they know the causes of things more universally and more perfectly; just as physicians who discern causes more keenly make better prognoses about the future state of an illness. But those things which proceed from their causes in the minority of cases are wholly unknown, such as chance and fortuitous things. In another way, future things are known in themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0056","text":"And thus it belongs to God alone to know future things, not only those which proceed necessarily, or for the most part, but also chance and fortuitous things, because God sees all things in His eternity, which, since it is simple, is present to the whole of time and encompasses it. And therefore the one gaze of God is directed toward all things that are done throughout all time as present, and sees all things as they are in themselves; as was said above when God's knowledge was treated. But the angelic intellect, and every created intellect, falls short of the divine eternity. Hence the future, as it is in its being, cannot be known by any created intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0057","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0058","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that men do not know future things except in their causes, or by God's revealing. And thus angels know future things much more subtly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0059","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0060","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although the intellect of an angel is above the time by which bodily motions are measured, nevertheless in an angel's intellect there is time according to the succession of intelligible conceptions; according to which Augustine says, in Book VIII of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that *God moves the spiritual creature through time*. And thus, since there is succession in an angel's intellect, not all things that are done throughout all time are present to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0061","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0062","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although the species that are in an angel's intellect, considered in themselves, stand equally in relation to present, past, and future things, nevertheless present, past, and future things do not stand equally in relation to the intelligible principles. For things that are present have a nature through which they are assimilated to the species that are in the angel's mind, and thus can be known through them. But things that are future do not yet have a nature through which they may be assimilated to those species; hence they cannot be known through them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0063","text":"[I q. 57 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0064","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that things distant according to place are already in the nature of things, and participate in some species, the likeness of which is in the angel; this is not true of future things, as has been said. And therefore the comparison does not hold.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0065","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0066","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0067","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that angels know the thoughts of hearts. For Gregory says, in the *Morals*, on the words of Job 28, *gold or glass shall not be equal to it*, that then, namely in the blessedness of those rising again, one *will be transparent to another as to himself, and when the intellect of each is observed, conscience too is penetrated at the same time*. But those who rise again will be like angels, as is found in Matthew 22. Therefore, one angel can see what is in another's conscience.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0068","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0069","text":"Moreover, as shapes stand in relation to bodies, so intelligible species stand in relation to the intellect. But when a body is seen, its shape is seen. Therefore, when an intellectual substance is seen, the intelligible species that is in it is seen. Therefore, since an angel sees another angel, and also a soul, it seems that he can see the thought of each.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0070","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0071","text":"Moreover, those things that are in our intellect are more like an angel than those that are in the imagination, since the former are understood in act, whereas the latter are only in potency. But those things that are in the imagination can be known by an angel as bodily things, since the imagination is a power of the body. Therefore, it seems that an angel can know the thoughts of the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0072","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0073","text":"On the contrary, what is proper to God does not belong to the angels. But to know the thoughts of hearts is proper to God, according to Jeremiah 17: *the heart of man is perverse and inscrutable; who shall know it? I, the Lord, searching hearts*. Therefore angels do not know the secrets of hearts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0074","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0075","text":"I answer that it must be said that the thought of the heart can be known in two ways. In one way, in its effect. And thus it can be known not only by an angel, but also by a human being; and the more subtly, the more hidden such an effect is. For thought is sometimes known not only through an outward act, but also through a change of countenance; and physicians can even know certain affections of the soul through the pulse. And much more can angels, or even demons, insofar as they discern such hidden bodily changes more subtly. Hence Augustine says, in the book *On the Divination of Demons*, that *they sometimes learn with complete ease men's dispositions, not only when uttered in speech, but even when conceived in thought, when certain signs are expressed in the body from the soul*; although in the book *Retractations* he says that it should not be asserted how this comes about.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0076","text":"In another way, thoughts can be known insofar as they are in the intellect, and affections insofar as they are in the will. And thus God alone can know the thoughts of hearts and the affections of wills. The reason for this is that the will of a rational creature is subject to God alone; and He alone can act upon it, who is its principal object, as its ultimate end; and this will become clearer below. And therefore those things which are in the will, or which depend on the will alone, are known to God alone. Now it is evident that it depends on the will alone that someone actually consider certain things, because when someone has a habit of knowledge, or intelligible species existing in him, he uses them when he wills. And therefore the Apostle says, in 1 Cor. 2, that *no one knows the things of a man except the spirit of the man which is in him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0077","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0078","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that at present the thought of one human being is not known by another because of a twofold impediment: namely, because of the grossness of the body and because of the will closing off its secrets. The first obstacle, however, will be removed in the resurrection, and is not present in angels. But the second impediment will remain after the resurrection, and is present now in angels. Nevertheless, the clarity of the body will represent the quality of the mind, as regards the measure of grace and glory. And thus one will be able to see the mind of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0079","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0080","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although one angel sees the intelligible species of another, because the mode of intelligible species, according to greater and lesser universality, is proportioned to the nobility of substances, it does not nevertheless follow that one knows how another uses those intelligible species in actually considering.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0081","text":"[I q. 57 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0082","text":"To the third, it must be said that the brutish appetite is not master of its own act, but follows the impression of another bodily or spiritual cause. Therefore, since angels know bodily things and their dispositions, through these they can know what is in the appetite and in the phantasmal apprehension of brute animals; and also of human beings, insofar as in them the sensitive appetite sometimes proceeds into act, following some bodily impression, as is always the case in brute animals. Yet it need not be that angels know the movement of the sensitive appetite and the phantasmal apprehension of a human being, insofar as they are moved by will and reason, because even the lower part of the soul participates in reason in some manner, as one obeying one who commands, as is said in the first book of the *Ethics*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0083","text":"Nor does it follow that, if an angel knows what is in a human being's sensitive appetite or phantasy, he knows what is in thought or will, because the intellect or will is not subject to the sensitive appetite and phantasy, but can use them in diverse ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0084","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0085","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0086","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that angels know the mysteries of grace. For among all mysteries, the mystery of Christ's incarnation is the most excellent. But the angels knew this from the beginning, for Augustine says, in Book V of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that *this mystery was so hidden from the ages in God that it nevertheless became known to the principalities and powers in the heavens*. And the Apostle says, in 1 Tim. 3, that *that great sacrament of piety appeared to angels*. Therefore angels know the mysteries of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0087","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0088","text":"Further, the reasons of all the mysteries of grace are contained in divine wisdom. But angels see God's wisdom itself, which is His essence. Therefore angels know the mysteries of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0089","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0090","text":"Further, the prophets are instructed by angels, as is evident from Dionysius, chapter 4 of *The Celestial Hierarchy*. But the prophets knew the mysteries of grace, for it is said in Amos 3: *the Lord will not do a word unless He has revealed His secret to His servants, the prophets*. Therefore angels know the mysteries of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0091","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0092","text":"But on the contrary, no one learns what he knows. But angels, even the highest, inquire concerning the divine mysteries of grace and learn them; for it is said in chapter 7 of *The Celestial Hierarchy* that *sacred Scripture introduces certain celestial essences questioning Jesus Himself, and learning the knowledge of His divine operation for us, and Jesus teaching them without an intermediary*; as is evident in Isaiah 63, where, to the angels asking, Who is this who comes from Edom? Jesus replied, I, who speak justice. Therefore angels do not know the mysteries of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0093","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0094","text":"I answer that it must be said that in angels there is a twofold knowledge. One is natural, by which they know things both through their essence and through innate species. And by this knowledge angels cannot know the mysteries of grace. For these mysteries depend on the pure will of God; but if one angel cannot know the thoughts of another that depend on his will, much less can he know those things that depend on the will of God alone. And thus the Apostle argues in 1 Cor. 2: *no one knows the things of a man except the spirit of the man which is in him. So also no one knows the things of God except the Spirit of God*. But there is another knowledge of angels, which makes them blessed, by which they see the Word and things in the Word. And by this vision they know the mysteries of grace, though not all of them, nor all equally, but according as God wills to reveal them to them; according to the saying of the Apostle, 1 Cor.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0095","text":"2: *but God has revealed them to us through His Spirit*. Yet in such a way that the higher angels, contemplating divine wisdom more penetratingly, know more numerous and loftier mysteries in the very vision of God, which they manifest to the lower angels by illuminating them. And some of these mysteries they knew from the beginning of their creation; but others they are taught afterwards, according as is fitting to their offices.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0096","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0097","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that one can speak of the mystery of Christ's Incarnation in two ways. In one way, in general; and thus it was revealed to all from the beginning of their beatitude. The reason for this is that this is a certain general principle to which all their offices are ordered; for “all are ministering spirits,” as is said in Hebrews 1, “sent for ministry on behalf of those who shall receive the inheritance of salvation”; and this indeed comes about through the mystery of the Incarnation. Hence it was necessary that all be commonly instructed concerning this mystery from the beginning. In another way, we can speak of the mystery of the Incarnation as to its special conditions. And thus not all the Angels were instructed from the beginning concerning all things; indeed, some, even higher Angels, learned them afterward, as is clear from the authority of Dionysius cited.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0098","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0099","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although the blessed Angels contemplate divine wisdom, they nevertheless do not comprehend it. And therefore it is not necessary that they know whatever lies hidden in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0100","text":"[I q. 57 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0101","text":"To the third, it must be said that whatever the prophets knew concerning the mysteries of grace through divine revelation was revealed to the Angels much more excellently. And although God revealed to the prophets, in general, those things that He was to do concerning the salvation of the human race, nevertheless the apostles knew certain special matters concerning this which the prophets had not known; according to Ephesians 3: “you are able, in reading, to understand my understanding in the mystery of Christ, which was not made known to other generations as it has now been revealed to His holy apostles.” Even among the prophets themselves, the later ones knew what the earlier had not known; according to Psalm 118: “I have understood above the aged.” And Gregory says that through the succession of times, an increase of divine knowledge grew.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_49:P0101"]}],"source_digest":"b1c8b76bcfcaff36e0521467bb914453659406ca9a4e1e819eb1b716e65ee664","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 57"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 57","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50","title":"First Part · Question 58","order":59,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_50","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 58 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30804)\n\nPost haec considerandum est de modo angelicae cognitionis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur septem. Primo, utrum intellectus Angeli quandoque sit in potentia, quandoque in actu. Secundo, utrum Angelus possit simul intelligere multa. Tertio, utrum intelligat discurrendo. Quarto, utrum intelligat componendo et dividendo. Quinto, utrum in intellectu Angeli possit esse falsitas. Sexto, utrum cognitio Angeli possit dici matutina et vespertina. Septimo, utrum sit eadem cognitio matutina et vespertina, vel diversae.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30805)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus Angeli quandoque sit in potentia. Motus enim est actus existentis in potentia, ut dicitur III Physic. Sed mentes angelicae intelligendo moventur, ut dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo mentes angelicae quandoque sunt in potentia.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30806)\n\nPraeterea, cum desiderium sit rei non habitae, possibilis tamen haberi, quicumque desiderat aliquid intelligere, est in potentia ad illud. Sed I Petri I, dicitur, *in quem desiderant Angeli prospicere*. Ergo intellectus Angeli quandoque est in potentia.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30807)\n\nPraeterea, in libro de causis dicitur quod intelligentia intelligit secundum modum suae substantiae. Sed substantia Angeli habet aliquid de potentia permixtum. Ergo quandoque intelligit in potentia.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30808)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Angeli, ex quo creati sunt, ipsa verbi aeternitate, sancta et pia contemplatione perfruuntur*. Sed intellectus contemplans non est in potentia, sed in actu. Ergo intellectus Angeli non est in potentia.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30809)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut philosophus dicit, in III de anima et in VIII Physic., intellectus dupliciter est in potentia, uno modo, sicut ante addiscere vel invenire, idest antequam habeat habitum scientiae; alio modo dicitur esse in potentia, sicut cum iam habet habitum scientiae, sed non considerat. Primo igitur modo, intellectus Angeli nunquam est in potentia respectu eorum ad quae eius cognitio naturalis se extendere potest. Sicut enim corpora superiora, scilicet caelestia, non habent potentiam ad esse, quae non sit completa per actum; ita caelestes intellectus, scilicet Angeli, non habent aliquam intelligibilem potentiam, quae non sit totaliter completa per species intelligibiles connaturales eis. Sed quantum ad ea quae eis divinitus revelantur, nihil prohibet intellectus eorum esse in potentia, quia sic etiam corpora caelestia sunt in potentia quandoque ut illuminentur a sole. Secundo vero modo, intellectus Angeli potest esse in potentia ad ea quae cognoscit naturali cognitione, non enim omnia quae naturali cognitione cognoscit, semper actu considerat. Sed ad cognitionem verbi, et eorum quae in verbo videt, nunquam hoc modo est in potentia, quia semper actu intuetur verbum, et ea quae in verbo videt. In hac enim visione eorum beatitudo consistit, beatitudo autem non consistit in habitu, sed in actu, ut dicit philosophus, in I Ethic.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30810)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod motus ibi non sumitur secundum quod est actus imperfecti, idest existentis in potentia; sed secundum quod est actus perfecti, idest existentis in actu. Sic enim intelligere et sentire dicuntur motus, ut dicitur in III de anima.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30811)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod desiderium illud Angelorum non excludit rem desideratam, sed eius fastidium. Vel dicuntur desiderare Dei visionem, quantum ad novas revelationes, quas pro opportunitate negotiorum a Deo recipiunt.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30812)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in substantia Angeli non est aliqua potentia denudata ab actu. Et similiter nec intellectus Angeli sic est in potentia, quod sit absque actu.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30813)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit simul multa intelligere. Dicit enim philosophus, II Topic., quod *contingit multa scire, sed unum tantum intelligere*.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30814)\n\nPraeterea, nihil intelligitur nisi secundum quod intellectus formatur per speciem intelligibilem, sicut corpus formatur per figuram. Sed unum corpus non potest formari diversis figuris. Ergo unus intellectus non potest simul intelligere diversa intelligibilia.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30815)\n\nPraeterea, intelligere est motus quidam. Nullus autem motus terminatur ad diversos terminos. Ergo non contingit simul multa intelligere.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30816)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, IV Sup. Gen. ad Litt., *potentia spiritualis mentis angelicae cuncta quae voluerit, facillime simul comprehendit*.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30817)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ad unitatem motus requiritur unitas termini, ita ad unitatem operationis requiritur unitas obiecti. Contingit autem aliqua accipi ut plura, et ut unum; sicut partes alicuius continui. Si enim unaquaeque per se accipiatur, plures sunt, unde et non una operatione, nec simul accipiuntur per sensum et intellectum. Alio modo accipiuntur secundum quod sunt unum in toto, et sic simul et una operatione cognoscuntur tam per sensum quam per intellectum, dum totum continuum consideratur, ut dicitur in III de anima. Et sic etiam intellectus noster simul intelligit subiectum et praedicatum, prout sunt partes unius propositionis; et duo comparata, secundum quod conveniunt in una comparatione. Ex quo patet quod multa, secundum quod sunt distincta, non possunt simul intelligi; sed secundum quod uniuntur in uno intelligibili, sic simul intelliguntur. Unumquodque autem est intelligibile in actu, secundum quod eius similitudo est in intellectu. Quaecumque igitur per unam speciem intelligibilem cognosci possunt, cognoscuntur ut unum intelligibile; et ideo simul cognoscuntur. Quae vero per diversas species intelligibiles cognoscuntur, ut diversa intelligibilia capiuntur. Angeli igitur ea cognitione qua cognoscunt res per verbum, omnia cognoscunt una intelligibili specie, quae est essentia divina. Et ideo quantum ad talem cognitionem, omnia simul cognoscunt, sicut et in patria *non erunt volubiles nostrae cogitationes, ab aliis in alia euntes atque redeuntes, sed omnem scientiam nostram simul uno conspectu videbimus*, ut Augustinus dicit in XV de Trin. Ea vero cognitione qua cognoscunt res per species innatas, omnia illa simul possunt intelligere, quae una specie cognoscuntur; non autem illa quae diversis.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30818)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intelligere multa ut unum, est quodammodo unum intelligere.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30819)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus formatur per intelligibilem speciem quam apud se habet. Et ideo sic potest una specie intelligibili multa simul intelligibilia intueri, sicut unum corpus per unam figuram potest simul multis corporibus assimilari.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30820)\n\nAd tertium dicendum sicut ad primum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30821)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus cognoscat discurrendo. Discursus enim intellectus attenditur secundum hoc, quod unum per aliud cognoscitur. Sed Angeli cognoscunt unum per aliud, cognoscunt enim creaturas per verbum. Ergo intellectus Angeli cognoscit discurrendo.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30822)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid potest virtus inferior, potest et virtus superior. Sed intellectus humanus potest syllogizare, et in effectibus causas cognoscere, secundum quae discursus attenditur. Ergo intellectus Angeli, qui superior est ordine naturae, multo magis hoc potest.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30823)\n\nPraeterea, Isidorus dicit quod Daemones per experientiam multa cognoscunt. Sed experimentalis cognitio est discursiva, *ex multis enim memoriis fit unum experimentum, et ex multis experimentis fit unum universale*, ut dicitur in fine Poster., et in principio Metaphys. Ergo cognitio Angelorum est discursiva.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30824)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli non congregant divinam cognitionem a sermonibus diffusis, neque ab aliquo communi ad ista specialia simul aguntur*.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30825)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut saepius dictum est, Angeli illum gradum tenent in substantiis spiritualibus, quem corpora caelestia in substantiis corporeis, nam et caelestes mentes a Dionysio dicuntur. Est autem haec differentia inter caelestia et terrena corpora, quod corpora terrena per mutationem et motum adipiscuntur suam ultimam perfectionem, corpora vero caelestia statim, ex ipsa sua natura, suam ultimam perfectionem habent. Sic igitur et inferiores intellectus, scilicet hominum, per quendam motum et discursum intellectualis operationis perfectionem in cognitione veritatis adipiscuntur; dum scilicet ex uno cognito in aliud cognitum procedunt. Si autem statim in ipsa cognitione principii noti, inspicerent quasi notas omnes conclusiones consequentes, in eis discursus locum non haberet. Et hoc est in Angelis, quia statim in illis quae primo naturaliter cognoscunt, inspiciunt omnia quaecumque in eis cognosci possunt. Et ideo dicuntur intellectuales, quia etiam apud nos, ea quae statim naturaliter apprehenduntur, intelligi dicuntur; unde intellectus dicitur habitus primorum principiorum. Animae vero humanae, quae veritatis notitiam per quendam discursum acquirunt, rationales vocantur. Quod quidem contingit ex debilitate intellectualis luminis in eis. Si enim haberent plenitudinem intellectualis luminis, sicut Angeli, statim in primo aspectu principiorum totam virtutem eorum comprehenderent, intuendo quidquid ex eis syllogizari posset.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30826)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod discursus quendam motum nominat. Omnis autem motus est de uno priori in aliud posterius. Unde discursiva cognitio attenditur secundum quod ex aliquo prius noto devenitur in cognitionem alterius posterius noti, quod prius erat ignotum. Si autem in uno inspecto simul aliud inspiciatur, sicut in speculo inspicitur simul imago rei et res; non est propter hoc cognitio discursiva. Et hoc modo cognoscunt Angeli res in verbo.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30827)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angeli syllogizare possunt, tanquam syllogismum cognoscentes; et in causis effectus vident, et in effectibus causas, non tamen ita quod cognitionem veritatis ignotae acquirant syllogizando ex causis in causata, et ex causatis in causas.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30828)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod experientia in Angelis et Daemonibus dicitur secundum quandam similitudinem, prout scilicet cognoscunt sensibilia praesentia; tamen absque omni discursu.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30829)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli intelligant componendo et dividendo. Ubi enim est multitudo intellectuum, ibi est compositio intellectuum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed in intellectu Angeli est multitudo intellectuum, cum per diversas species diversa intelligat, et non omnia simul. Ergo in intellectu Angeli est compositio et divisio.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30830)\n\nPraeterea, plus distat negatio ab affirmatione, quam quaecumque duae naturae oppositae, quia prima distinctio est per affirmationem et negationem. Sed aliquas naturas distantes Angelus non cognoscit per unum, sed per diversas species, ut ex dictis patet. Ergo oportet quod affirmationem et negationem cognoscat per diversa. Et ita videtur quod Angelus intelligat componendo et dividendo.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30831)\n\nPraeterea, locutio est signum intellectus. Sed Angeli hominibus loquentes, proferunt affirmativas et negativas enuntiationes, quae sunt signa compositionis et divisionis in intellectu; ut ex multis locis sacrae Scripturae apparet. Ergo videtur quod Angelus intelligat componendo et dividendo.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30832)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *virtus intellectualis Angelorum resplendet conspicaci divinorum intellectuum simplicitate*. Sed simplex intelligentia est sine compositione et divisione, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo Angelus intelligit sine compositione et divisione.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30833)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut in intellectu ratiocinante comparatur conclusio ad principium, ita in intellectu componente et dividente comparatur praedicatum ad subiectum. Si enim intellectus statim in ipso principio videret conclusionis veritatem, nunquam intelligeret discurrendo vel ratiocinando. Similiter si intellectus statim in apprehensione quidditatis subiecti, haberet notitiam de omnibus quae possunt attribui subiecto vel removeri ab eo, nunquam intelligeret componendo et dividendo, sed solum intelligendo quod quid est. Sic igitur patet quod ex eodem provenit quod intellectus noster intelligit discurrendo, et componendo et dividendo, ex hoc scilicet, quod non statim in prima apprehensione alicuius primi apprehensi, potest inspicere quidquid in eo virtute continetur. Quod contingit ex debilitate luminis intellectualis in nobis, sicut dictum est. Unde cum in Angelo sit lumen intellectuale perfectum, cum sit speculum purum et clarissimum, ut dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom.; relinquitur quod Angelus, sicut non intelligit ratiocinando, ita non intelligit componendo et dividendo. Nihilominus tamen compositionem et divisionem enuntiationum intelligit, sicut et ratiocinationem syllogismorum, intelligit enim composita simpliciter, et mobilia immobiliter, et materialia immaterialiter.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30834)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod non qualiscumque multitudo intellectuum compositionem causat, sed multitudo illorum intellectuum quorum unum attribuitur alteri, vel removetur ab altero. Angelus autem, intelligendo quidditatem alicuius rei, simul intelligit quidquid ei attribui potest vel removeri ab ea. Unde intelligendo quod quid est, intelligit quidquid nos intelligere possumus et componendo et dividendo, per unum suum simplicem intellectum.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30835)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod diversae quidditates rerum minus differunt, quantum ad rationem existendi, quam affirmatio et negatio. Tamen quantum ad rationem cognoscendi, affirmatio et negatio magis conveniunt, quia statim per hoc quod cognoscitur veritas affirmationis, cognoscitur falsitas negationis oppositae.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30836)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod hoc quod Angeli loquuntur enuntiationes affirmativas et negativas, manifestat quod Angeli cognoscunt compositionem et divisionem, non autem quod cognoscant componendo et dividendo, sed simpliciter cognoscendo quod quid est.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30837)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in intellectu Angeli possit esse falsitas. Protervitas enim ad falsitatem pertinet. Sed in Daemonibus est phantasia proterva, ut dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo videtur quod in Angelorum intellectu possit esse falsitas.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30838)\n\nPraeterea, nescientia est causa falsae aestimationis. Sed in Angelis potest esse nescientia, ut Dionysius dicit, VI cap. Eccles. Hier. Ergo videtur quod in eis possit esse falsitas.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30839)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod cadit a veritate sapientiae, et habet rationem depravatam, habet falsitatem vel errorem in suo intellectu. Sed hoc Dionysius dicit de Daemonibus, VII cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo videtur quod in intellectu Angelorum possit esse falsitas.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30840)\n\nSed contra, philosophus dicit, III de anima, quod *intellectus semper verus est*. Augustinus etiam dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *nihil intelligitur nisi verum*. Sed Angeli non cognoscunt aliquid nisi intelligendo. Ergo in Angeli cognitione non potest esse deceptio et falsitas.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30841)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod huius quaestionis veritas aliquatenus ex praemissa dependet. Dictum est enim quod Angelus non intelligit componendo et dividendo, sed intelligendo quod quid est. Intellectus autem circa quod quid est semper verus est, sicut et sensus circa proprium obiectum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed per accidens in nobis accidit deceptio et falsitas intelligendo quod quid est, scilicet secundum rationem alicuius compositionis, vel cum definitionem unius rei accipimus ut definitionem alterius; vel cum partes definitionis sibi non cohaerent, sicut si accipiatur pro definitione alicuius rei, animal quadrupes volatile (nullum enim animal tale est); et hoc quidem accidit in compositis, quorum definitio ex diversis sumitur, quorum unum est materiale ad aliud. Sed intelligendo quidditates simplices, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys., non est falsitas, quia vel totaliter non attinguntur, et nihil intelligimus de eis; vel cognoscuntur ut sunt. Sic igitur per se non potest esse falsitas aut error aut deceptio in intellectu alicuius Angeli; sed per accidens contingit. Alio tamen modo quam in nobis. Nam nos componendo et dividendo quandoque ad intellectum quidditatis pervenimus, sicut cum dividendo vel demonstrando definitionem investigamus. Quod quidem in Angelis non contingit; sed per quod quid est rei cognoscunt omnes enuntiationes ad illam rem pertinentes. Manifestum est autem quod quidditas rei potest esse principium cognoscendi respectu eorum quae naturaliter conveniunt rei vel ab ea removentur, non autem eorum quae a supernaturali Dei ordinatione dependent. Angeli igitur boni, habentes rectam voluntatem, per cognitionem quidditatis rei non iudicant de his quae naturaliter ad rem pertinent, nisi salva ordinatione divina. Unde in eis non potest esse falsitas aut error. Daemones vero, per voluntatem perversam subducentes intellectum a divina sapientia, absolute interdum de rebus iudicant secundum naturalem conditionem. Et in his quae naturaliter ad rem pertinent, non decipiuntur. Sed decipi possunt quantum ad ea quae supernaturalia sunt, sicut si considerans hominem mortuum, iudicet eum non resurrecturum; et si videns hominem Christum, iudicet eum non esse Deum.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30842)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad ea quae utrinque obiiciuntur. Nam protervitas Daemonum est secundum quod non subduntur divinae sapientiae. Nescientia autem est in Angelis, non respectu naturalium cognoscibilium, sed supernaturalium. Patet etiam quod intellectus eius quod quid est semper est verus, nisi per accidens, secundum quod indebite ordinatur ad aliquam compositionem vel divisionem.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30843)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit vespertina neque matutina cognitio. Vespere enim et mane admixtionem tenebrarum habent. Sed in cognitione Angeli non est aliqua tenebrositas; cum non sit ibi error vel falsitas. Ergo cognitio Angeli non debet dici matutina vel vespertina.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30844)\n\nPraeterea, inter vespere et mane cadit nox; et inter mane et vespere cadit meridies. Si igitur in Angelis cadit cognitio matutina et vespertina, pari ratione videtur quod in eis debeat esse meridiana et nocturna cognitio.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30845)\n\nPraeterea, cognitio distinguitur secundum differentiam cognitorum, unde in III de anima dicit philosophus quod *scientiae secantur quemadmodum et res*. Triplex autem est esse rerum, scilicet in verbo, in propria natura, et in intelligentia angelica, ut Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo, si ponatur cognitio matutina in Angelis et vespertina, propter esse rerum in verbo et in propria natura; debet etiam in eis poni tertia cognitio, propter esse rerum in intelligentia angelica.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30846)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus, IV super Gen. ad Litt., et XI de Civ. Dei, distinguit cognitionem Angelorum per matutinam et vespertinam.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30847)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod hoc quod dicitur de cognitione matutina et vespertina in Angelis, introductum est ab Augustino, qui sex dies in quibus Deus legitur fecisse cuncta, Gen. I, intelligi vult non hos usitatos dies qui solis circuitu peraguntur, cum sol quarto die factus legatur; sed unum diem, scilicet cognitionem angelicam sex rerum generibus praesentatam. Sicut autem in die consueto mane est principium diei, vespere autem terminus, ita cognitio ipsius primordialis esse rerum, dicitur cognitio matutina, et haec est secundum quod res sunt in verbo. Cognitio autem ipsius esse rei creatae secundum quod in propria natura consistit, dicitur cognitio vespertina, nam esse rerum fluit a verbo sicut a quodam primordiali principio, et hic effluxus terminatur ad esse rerum quod in propria natura habent.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30848)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod vespere et mane non accipiuntur in cognitione angelica secundum similitudinem ad admixtionem tenebrarum; sed secundum similitudinem principii et termini. Vel dicendum quod nihil prohibet, ut dicit Augustinus IV super Gen. ad Litt., aliquid in comparatione ad unum dici lux, et in comparatione ad aliud dici tenebra. Sicut vita fidelium et iustorum, in comparatione ad impios, dicitur lux, secundum illud Ephes. V, *fuistis aliquando tenebrae, nunc autem lux in domino*; quae tamen vita fidelium, in comparatione ad vitam gloriae, tenebrosa dicitur, secundum illud II Petri I, *habetis propheticum sermonem, cui bene facitis attendentes quasi lucernae lucenti in caliginoso loco*. Sic igitur cognitio Angeli qua cognoscit res in propria natura, dies est per comparationem ad ignorantiam vel errorem, sed obscura est per comparationem ad visionem verbi.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30849)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod matutina et vespertina cognitio ad diem pertinet, idest ad Angelos illuminatos, qui sunt distincti a tenebris, idest a malis Angelis. Angeli autem boni, cognoscentes creaturam, non in ea figuntur, quod esset tenebrescere et noctem fieri; sed hoc ipsum referunt ad laudem Dei, in quo sicut in principio omnia cognoscunt. Et ideo post vesperam non ponitur nox, sed mane, ita quod mane sit finis praecedentis diei et principium sequentis, inquantum Angeli cognitionem praecedentis operis ad laudem Dei referunt. Meridies autem sub nomine diei comprehenditur, quasi medium inter duo extrema. Vel potest meridies referri ad cognitionem ipsius Dei, qui non habet principium nec finem.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30850)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod etiam ipsi Angeli creaturae sunt. Unde esse rerum in intelligentia angelica comprehenditur sub vespertina cognitione, sicut et esse rerum in propria natura.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30851)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod una sit cognitio vespertina et matutina. Dicitur enim Gen. I, *factum est vespere et mane dies unus*. Sed per diem intelligitur cognitio angelica, ut Augustinus dicit. Ergo una et eadem est cognitio in Angelis matutina et vespertina.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30852)\n\nPraeterea, impossibile est unam potentiam simul duas operationes habere. Sed Angeli semper sunt in actu cognitionis matutinae, quia semper vident Deum et res in Deo, secundum illud Matth. XVIII, *Angeli eorum semper vident faciem patris mei* et cetera. Ergo, si cognitio vespertina esset alia a matutina, nullo modo Angelus posset esse in actu cognitionis vespertinae.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30853)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus dicit, I Cor. XIII, *cum venerit quod perfectum est, evacuabitur quod ex parte est*. Sed si cognitio vespertina sit alia a matutina, comparatur ad ipsam sicut imperfectum ad perfectum. Ergo non poterit simul vespertina cognitio esse cum matutina.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30854)\n\nIn contrarium est quod dicit Augustinus, IV super Gen. ad Litt., *quod multum interest inter cognitionem rei cuiuscumque in verbo Dei, et cognitionem eius in natura eius, ut illud merito pertineat ad diem, hoc ad vesperam*.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30855)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, cognitio vespertina dicitur, qua Angeli cognoscunt res in propria natura. Quod non potest ita intelligi quasi ex propria rerum natura cognitionem accipiant, ut haec praepositio in indicet habitudinem principii, quia non accipiunt Angeli cognitionem a rebus, ut supra habitum est. Relinquitur igitur quod hoc quod dicitur in propria natura, accipiatur secundum rationem cogniti, secundum quod subest cognitioni; ut scilicet cognitio vespertina in Angelis dicatur secundum quod cognoscunt esse rerum quod habent res in propria natura. Quod quidem per duplex medium cognoscunt, scilicet per species innatas, et per rationes rerum in verbo existentes. Non enim, videndo verbum, cognoscunt solum illud esse rerum quod habent in verbo; sed illud esse quod habent in propria natura; sicut Deus per hoc quod videt se, cognoscit esse rerum quod habent in propria natura. Si ergo dicatur cognitio vespertina secundum quod cognoscunt esse rerum quod habent in propria natura, videndo verbum; sic una et eadem secundum essentiam est cognitio vespertina et matutina, differens solum secundum cognita. Si vero cognitio vespertina dicatur secundum quod Angeli cognoscunt esse rerum quod habent in propria natura, per formas innatas; sic alia est cognitio vespertina et matutina. Et ita videtur intelligere Augustinus, cum unam ponat imperfectam respectu alterius.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30856)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut numerus sex dierum, secundum intellectum Augustini, accipitur secundum sex genera rerum quae cognoscuntur ab Angelis; ita unitas diei accipitur secundum unitatem rei cognitae, quae tamen diversis cognitionibus cognosci potest.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30857)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod duae operationes possunt simul esse unius potentiae, quarum una ad aliam refertur; ut patet cum voluntas simul vult et finem et ea quae sunt ad finem, et intellectus simul intelligit principia et conclusiones per principia, quando iam scientiam acquisivit. Cognitio autem vespertina in Angelis refertur ad matutinam, ut Augustinus dicit. Unde nihil prohibet utramque simul esse in Angelis.\n\n[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30858)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, veniente perfecto, evacuatur imperfectum quod ei opponitur, sicut fides, quae est eorum quae non videntur, evacuatur visione veniente. Sed imperfectio vespertinae cognitionis non opponitur perfectioni matutinae. Quod enim cognoscatur aliquid in seipso, non est oppositum ei quod cognoscatur in sua causa. Nec iterum quod aliquid cognoscatur per duo media, quorum unum est perfectius et aliud imperfectius, aliquid repugnans habet, sicut ad eandem conclusionem habere possumus et medium demonstrativum et dialecticum. Et similiter eadem res potest sciri ab Angelo per verbum increatum, et per speciem innatam.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0002","text":"[I q. 58 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0003","text":"Post haec considerandum est de modo angelicae cognitionis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur septem. Primo, utrum intellectus Angeli quandoque sit in potentia, quandoque in actu. Secundo, utrum Angelus possit simul intelligere multa. Tertio, utrum intelligat discurrendo. Quarto, utrum intelligat componendo et dividendo. Quinto, utrum in intellectu Angeli possit esse falsitas. Sexto, utrum cognitio Angeli possit dici matutina et vespertina. Septimo, utrum sit eadem cognitio matutina et vespertina, vel diversae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0005","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus Angeli quandoque sit in potentia. Motus enim est actus existentis in potentia, ut dicitur III Physic. Sed mentes angelicae intelligendo moventur, ut dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo mentes angelicae quandoque sunt in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0007","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0008","text":"Praeterea, cum desiderium sit rei non habitae, possibilis tamen haberi, quicumque desiderat aliquid intelligere, est in potentia ad illud. Sed I Petri I, dicitur, *in quem desiderant Angeli prospicere*. Ergo intellectus Angeli quandoque est in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0009","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0010","text":"Praeterea, in libro de causis dicitur quod intelligentia intelligit secundum modum suae substantiae. Sed substantia Angeli habet aliquid de potentia permixtum. Ergo quandoque intelligit in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0011","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Angeli, ex quo creati sunt, ipsa verbi aeternitate, sancta et pia contemplatione perfruuntur*. Sed intellectus contemplans non est in potentia, sed in actu. Ergo intellectus Angeli non est in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0013","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut philosophus dicit, in III de anima et in VIII Physic., intellectus dupliciter est in potentia, uno modo, sicut ante addiscere vel invenire, idest antequam habeat habitum scientiae; alio modo dicitur esse in potentia, sicut cum iam habet habitum scientiae, sed non considerat. Primo igitur modo, intellectus Angeli nunquam est in potentia respectu eorum ad quae eius cognitio naturalis se extendere potest. Sicut enim corpora superiora, scilicet caelestia, non habent potentiam ad esse, quae non sit completa per actum; ita caelestes intellectus, scilicet Angeli, non habent aliquam intelligibilem potentiam, quae non sit totaliter completa per species intelligibiles connaturales eis. Sed quantum ad ea quae eis divinitus revelantur, nihil prohibet intellectus eorum esse in potentia, quia sic etiam corpora caelestia sunt in potentia quandoque ut illuminentur a sole.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0015","text":"Secundo vero modo, intellectus Angeli potest esse in potentia ad ea quae cognoscit naturali cognitione, non enim omnia quae naturali cognitione cognoscit, semper actu considerat. Sed ad cognitionem verbi, et eorum quae in verbo videt, nunquam hoc modo est in potentia, quia semper actu intuetur verbum, et ea quae in verbo videt. In hac enim visione eorum beatitudo consistit, beatitudo autem non consistit in habitu, sed in actu, ut dicit philosophus, in I Ethic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0016","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod motus ibi non sumitur secundum quod est actus imperfecti, idest existentis in potentia; sed secundum quod est actus perfecti, idest existentis in actu. Sic enim intelligere et sentire dicuntur motus, ut dicitur in III de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0018","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod desiderium illud Angelorum non excludit rem desideratam, sed eius fastidium. Vel dicuntur desiderare Dei visionem, quantum ad novas revelationes, quas pro opportunitate negotiorum a Deo recipiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0020","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in substantia Angeli non est aliqua potentia denudata ab actu. Et similiter nec intellectus Angeli sic est in potentia, quod sit absque actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0023","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit simul multa intelligere. Dicit enim philosophus, II Topic., quod *contingit multa scire, sed unum tantum intelligere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0025","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0026","text":"Praeterea, nihil intelligitur nisi secundum quod intellectus formatur per speciem intelligibilem, sicut corpus formatur per figuram. Sed unum corpus non potest formari diversis figuris. Ergo unus intellectus non potest simul intelligere diversa intelligibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0027","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0028","text":"Praeterea, intelligere est motus quidam. Nullus autem motus terminatur ad diversos terminos. Ergo non contingit simul multa intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0029","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, IV Sup. Gen. ad Litt., *potentia spiritualis mentis angelicae cuncta quae voluerit, facillime simul comprehendit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0031","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ad unitatem motus requiritur unitas termini, ita ad unitatem operationis requiritur unitas obiecti. Contingit autem aliqua accipi ut plura, et ut unum; sicut partes alicuius continui. Si enim unaquaeque per se accipiatur, plures sunt, unde et non una operatione, nec simul accipiuntur per sensum et intellectum. Alio modo accipiuntur secundum quod sunt unum in toto, et sic simul et una operatione cognoscuntur tam per sensum quam per intellectum, dum totum continuum consideratur, ut dicitur in III de anima. Et sic etiam intellectus noster simul intelligit subiectum et praedicatum, prout sunt partes unius propositionis; et duo comparata, secundum quod conveniunt in una comparatione. Ex quo patet quod multa, secundum quod sunt distincta, non possunt simul intelligi; sed secundum quod uniuntur in uno intelligibili, sic simul intelliguntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0033","text":"Unumquodque autem est intelligibile in actu, secundum quod eius similitudo est in intellectu. Quaecumque igitur per unam speciem intelligibilem cognosci possunt, cognoscuntur ut unum intelligibile; et ideo simul cognoscuntur. Quae vero per diversas species intelligibiles cognoscuntur, ut diversa intelligibilia capiuntur. Angeli igitur ea cognitione qua cognoscunt res per verbum, omnia cognoscunt una intelligibili specie, quae est essentia divina. Et ideo quantum ad talem cognitionem, omnia simul cognoscunt, sicut et in patria *non erunt volubiles nostrae cogitationes, ab aliis in alia euntes atque redeuntes, sed omnem scientiam nostram simul uno conspectu videbimus*, ut Augustinus dicit in XV de Trin. Ea vero cognitione qua cognoscunt res per species innatas, omnia illa simul possunt intelligere, quae una specie cognoscuntur; non autem illa quae diversis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0034","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intelligere multa ut unum, est quodammodo unum intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0036","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus formatur per intelligibilem speciem quam apud se habet. Et ideo sic potest una specie intelligibili multa simul intelligibilia intueri, sicut unum corpus per unam figuram potest simul multis corporibus assimilari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0038","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum sicut ad primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0041","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus cognoscat discurrendo. Discursus enim intellectus attenditur secundum hoc, quod unum per aliud cognoscitur. Sed Angeli cognoscunt unum per aliud, cognoscunt enim creaturas per verbum. Ergo intellectus Angeli cognoscit discurrendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0043","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0044","text":"Praeterea, quidquid potest virtus inferior, potest et virtus superior. Sed intellectus humanus potest syllogizare, et in effectibus causas cognoscere, secundum quae discursus attenditur. Ergo intellectus Angeli, qui superior est ordine naturae, multo magis hoc potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0045","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0046","text":"Praeterea, Isidorus dicit quod Daemones per experientiam multa cognoscunt. Sed experimentalis cognitio est discursiva, *ex multis enim memoriis fit unum experimentum, et ex multis experimentis fit unum universale*, ut dicitur in fine Poster., et in principio Metaphys. Ergo cognitio Angelorum est discursiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0047","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0048","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli non congregant divinam cognitionem a sermonibus diffusis, neque ab aliquo communi ad ista specialia simul aguntur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0049","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0050","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut saepius dictum est, Angeli illum gradum tenent in substantiis spiritualibus, quem corpora caelestia in substantiis corporeis, nam et caelestes mentes a Dionysio dicuntur. Est autem haec differentia inter caelestia et terrena corpora, quod corpora terrena per mutationem et motum adipiscuntur suam ultimam perfectionem, corpora vero caelestia statim, ex ipsa sua natura, suam ultimam perfectionem habent. Sic igitur et inferiores intellectus, scilicet hominum, per quendam motum et discursum intellectualis operationis perfectionem in cognitione veritatis adipiscuntur; dum scilicet ex uno cognito in aliud cognitum procedunt. Si autem statim in ipsa cognitione principii noti, inspicerent quasi notas omnes conclusiones consequentes, in eis discursus locum non haberet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0051","text":"Et hoc est in Angelis, quia statim in illis quae primo naturaliter cognoscunt, inspiciunt omnia quaecumque in eis cognosci possunt. Et ideo dicuntur intellectuales, quia etiam apud nos, ea quae statim naturaliter apprehenduntur, intelligi dicuntur; unde intellectus dicitur habitus primorum principiorum. Animae vero humanae, quae veritatis notitiam per quendam discursum acquirunt, rationales vocantur. Quod quidem contingit ex debilitate intellectualis luminis in eis. Si enim haberent plenitudinem intellectualis luminis, sicut Angeli, statim in primo aspectu principiorum totam virtutem eorum comprehenderent, intuendo quidquid ex eis syllogizari posset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0052","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod discursus quendam motum nominat. Omnis autem motus est de uno priori in aliud posterius. Unde discursiva cognitio attenditur secundum quod ex aliquo prius noto devenitur in cognitionem alterius posterius noti, quod prius erat ignotum. Si autem in uno inspecto simul aliud inspiciatur, sicut in speculo inspicitur simul imago rei et res; non est propter hoc cognitio discursiva. Et hoc modo cognoscunt Angeli res in verbo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0054","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angeli syllogizare possunt, tanquam syllogismum cognoscentes; et in causis effectus vident, et in effectibus causas, non tamen ita quod cognitionem veritatis ignotae acquirant syllogizando ex causis in causata, et ex causatis in causas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0056","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0057","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod experientia in Angelis et Daemonibus dicitur secundum quandam similitudinem, prout scilicet cognoscunt sensibilia praesentia; tamen absque omni discursu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0058","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0059","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0060","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli intelligant componendo et dividendo. Ubi enim est multitudo intellectuum, ibi est compositio intellectuum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed in intellectu Angeli est multitudo intellectuum, cum per diversas species diversa intelligat, et non omnia simul. Ergo in intellectu Angeli est compositio et divisio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0061","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0062","text":"Praeterea, plus distat negatio ab affirmatione, quam quaecumque duae naturae oppositae, quia prima distinctio est per affirmationem et negationem. Sed aliquas naturas distantes Angelus non cognoscit per unum, sed per diversas species, ut ex dictis patet. Ergo oportet quod affirmationem et negationem cognoscat per diversa. Et ita videtur quod Angelus intelligat componendo et dividendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0063","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0064","text":"Praeterea, locutio est signum intellectus. Sed Angeli hominibus loquentes, proferunt affirmativas et negativas enuntiationes, quae sunt signa compositionis et divisionis in intellectu; ut ex multis locis sacrae Scripturae apparet. Ergo videtur quod Angelus intelligat componendo et dividendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0065","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0066","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom., quod *virtus intellectualis Angelorum resplendet conspicaci divinorum intellectuum simplicitate*. Sed simplex intelligentia est sine compositione et divisione, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo Angelus intelligit sine compositione et divisione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0067","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0068","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut in intellectu ratiocinante comparatur conclusio ad principium, ita in intellectu componente et dividente comparatur praedicatum ad subiectum. Si enim intellectus statim in ipso principio videret conclusionis veritatem, nunquam intelligeret discurrendo vel ratiocinando. Similiter si intellectus statim in apprehensione quidditatis subiecti, haberet notitiam de omnibus quae possunt attribui subiecto vel removeri ab eo, nunquam intelligeret componendo et dividendo, sed solum intelligendo quod quid est. Sic igitur patet quod ex eodem provenit quod intellectus noster intelligit discurrendo, et componendo et dividendo, ex hoc scilicet, quod non statim in prima apprehensione alicuius primi apprehensi, potest inspicere quidquid in eo virtute continetur. Quod contingit ex debilitate luminis intellectualis in nobis, sicut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0069","text":"Unde cum in Angelo sit lumen intellectuale perfectum, cum sit speculum purum et clarissimum, ut dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom.; relinquitur quod Angelus, sicut non intelligit ratiocinando, ita non intelligit componendo et dividendo. Nihilominus tamen compositionem et divisionem enuntiationum intelligit, sicut et ratiocinationem syllogismorum, intelligit enim composita simpliciter, et mobilia immobiliter, et materialia immaterialiter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0070","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0071","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod non qualiscumque multitudo intellectuum compositionem causat, sed multitudo illorum intellectuum quorum unum attribuitur alteri, vel removetur ab altero. Angelus autem, intelligendo quidditatem alicuius rei, simul intelligit quidquid ei attribui potest vel removeri ab ea. Unde intelligendo quod quid est, intelligit quidquid nos intelligere possumus et componendo et dividendo, per unum suum simplicem intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0072","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0073","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod diversae quidditates rerum minus differunt, quantum ad rationem existendi, quam affirmatio et negatio. Tamen quantum ad rationem cognoscendi, affirmatio et negatio magis conveniunt, quia statim per hoc quod cognoscitur veritas affirmationis, cognoscitur falsitas negationis oppositae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0074","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0075","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod hoc quod Angeli loquuntur enuntiationes affirmativas et negativas, manifestat quod Angeli cognoscunt compositionem et divisionem, non autem quod cognoscant componendo et dividendo, sed simpliciter cognoscendo quod quid est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0076","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0077","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0078","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in intellectu Angeli possit esse falsitas. Protervitas enim ad falsitatem pertinet. Sed in Daemonibus est phantasia proterva, ut dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo videtur quod in Angelorum intellectu possit esse falsitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0079","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0080","text":"Praeterea, nescientia est causa falsae aestimationis. Sed in Angelis potest esse nescientia, ut Dionysius dicit, VI cap. Eccles. Hier. Ergo videtur quod in eis possit esse falsitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0081","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0082","text":"Praeterea, omne quod cadit a veritate sapientiae, et habet rationem depravatam, habet falsitatem vel errorem in suo intellectu. Sed hoc Dionysius dicit de Daemonibus, VII cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo videtur quod in intellectu Angelorum possit esse falsitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0083","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0084","text":"Sed contra, philosophus dicit, III de anima, quod *intellectus semper verus est*. Augustinus etiam dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *nihil intelligitur nisi verum*. Sed Angeli non cognoscunt aliquid nisi intelligendo. Ergo in Angeli cognitione non potest esse deceptio et falsitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0085","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0086","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod huius quaestionis veritas aliquatenus ex praemissa dependet. Dictum est enim quod Angelus non intelligit componendo et dividendo, sed intelligendo quod quid est. Intellectus autem circa quod quid est semper verus est, sicut et sensus circa proprium obiectum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed per accidens in nobis accidit deceptio et falsitas intelligendo quod quid est, scilicet secundum rationem alicuius compositionis, vel cum definitionem unius rei accipimus ut definitionem alterius; vel cum partes definitionis sibi non cohaerent, sicut si accipiatur pro definitione alicuius rei, animal quadrupes volatile (nullum enim animal tale est); et hoc quidem accidit in compositis, quorum definitio ex diversis sumitur, quorum unum est materiale ad aliud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0087","text":"Sed intelligendo quidditates simplices, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys., non est falsitas, quia vel totaliter non attinguntur, et nihil intelligimus de eis; vel cognoscuntur ut sunt. Sic igitur per se non potest esse falsitas aut error aut deceptio in intellectu alicuius Angeli; sed per accidens contingit. Alio tamen modo quam in nobis. Nam nos componendo et dividendo quandoque ad intellectum quidditatis pervenimus, sicut cum dividendo vel demonstrando definitionem investigamus. Quod quidem in Angelis non contingit; sed per quod quid est rei cognoscunt omnes enuntiationes ad illam rem pertinentes. Manifestum est autem quod quidditas rei potest esse principium cognoscendi respectu eorum quae naturaliter conveniunt rei vel ab ea removentur, non autem eorum quae a supernaturali Dei ordinatione dependent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0088","text":"Angeli igitur boni, habentes rectam voluntatem, per cognitionem quidditatis rei non iudicant de his quae naturaliter ad rem pertinent, nisi salva ordinatione divina. Unde in eis non potest esse falsitas aut error. Daemones vero, per voluntatem perversam subducentes intellectum a divina sapientia, absolute interdum de rebus iudicant secundum naturalem conditionem. Et in his quae naturaliter ad rem pertinent, non decipiuntur. Sed decipi possunt quantum ad ea quae supernaturalia sunt, sicut si considerans hominem mortuum, iudicet eum non resurrecturum; et si videns hominem Christum, iudicet eum non esse Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0089","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0090","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad ea quae utrinque obiiciuntur. Nam protervitas Daemonum est secundum quod non subduntur divinae sapientiae. Nescientia autem est in Angelis, non respectu naturalium cognoscibilium, sed supernaturalium. Patet etiam quod intellectus eius quod quid est semper est verus, nisi per accidens, secundum quod indebite ordinatur ad aliquam compositionem vel divisionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0091","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0092","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0093","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit vespertina neque matutina cognitio. Vespere enim et mane admixtionem tenebrarum habent. Sed in cognitione Angeli non est aliqua tenebrositas; cum non sit ibi error vel falsitas. Ergo cognitio Angeli non debet dici matutina vel vespertina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0094","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0095","text":"Praeterea, inter vespere et mane cadit nox; et inter mane et vespere cadit meridies. Si igitur in Angelis cadit cognitio matutina et vespertina, pari ratione videtur quod in eis debeat esse meridiana et nocturna cognitio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0096","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0097","text":"Praeterea, cognitio distinguitur secundum differentiam cognitorum, unde in III de anima dicit philosophus quod *scientiae secantur quemadmodum et res*. Triplex autem est esse rerum, scilicet in verbo, in propria natura, et in intelligentia angelica, ut Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo, si ponatur cognitio matutina in Angelis et vespertina, propter esse rerum in verbo et in propria natura; debet etiam in eis poni tertia cognitio, propter esse rerum in intelligentia angelica.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0098","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0099","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus, IV super Gen. ad Litt., et XI de Civ. Dei, distinguit cognitionem Angelorum per matutinam et vespertinam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0100","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0101","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod hoc quod dicitur de cognitione matutina et vespertina in Angelis, introductum est ab Augustino, qui sex dies in quibus Deus legitur fecisse cuncta, Gen. I, intelligi vult non hos usitatos dies qui solis circuitu peraguntur, cum sol quarto die factus legatur; sed unum diem, scilicet cognitionem angelicam sex rerum generibus praesentatam. Sicut autem in die consueto mane est principium diei, vespere autem terminus, ita cognitio ipsius primordialis esse rerum, dicitur cognitio matutina, et haec est secundum quod res sunt in verbo. Cognitio autem ipsius esse rei creatae secundum quod in propria natura consistit, dicitur cognitio vespertina, nam esse rerum fluit a verbo sicut a quodam primordiali principio, et hic effluxus terminatur ad esse rerum quod in propria natura habent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0102","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0103","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod vespere et mane non accipiuntur in cognitione angelica secundum similitudinem ad admixtionem tenebrarum; sed secundum similitudinem principii et termini. Vel dicendum quod nihil prohibet, ut dicit Augustinus IV super Gen. ad Litt., aliquid in comparatione ad unum dici lux, et in comparatione ad aliud dici tenebra. Sicut vita fidelium et iustorum, in comparatione ad impios, dicitur lux, secundum illud Ephes. V, *fuistis aliquando tenebrae, nunc autem lux in domino*; quae tamen vita fidelium, in comparatione ad vitam gloriae, tenebrosa dicitur, secundum illud II Petri I, *habetis propheticum sermonem, cui bene facitis attendentes quasi lucernae lucenti in caliginoso loco*. Sic igitur cognitio Angeli qua cognoscit res in propria natura, dies est per comparationem ad ignorantiam vel errorem, sed obscura est per comparationem ad visionem verbi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0104","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0105","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod matutina et vespertina cognitio ad diem pertinet, idest ad Angelos illuminatos, qui sunt distincti a tenebris, idest a malis Angelis. Angeli autem boni, cognoscentes creaturam, non in ea figuntur, quod esset tenebrescere et noctem fieri; sed hoc ipsum referunt ad laudem Dei, in quo sicut in principio omnia cognoscunt. Et ideo post vesperam non ponitur nox, sed mane, ita quod mane sit finis praecedentis diei et principium sequentis, inquantum Angeli cognitionem praecedentis operis ad laudem Dei referunt. Meridies autem sub nomine diei comprehenditur, quasi medium inter duo extrema. Vel potest meridies referri ad cognitionem ipsius Dei, qui non habet principium nec finem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0106","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0107","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod etiam ipsi Angeli creaturae sunt. Unde esse rerum in intelligentia angelica comprehenditur sub vespertina cognitione, sicut et esse rerum in propria natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0108","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0109","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0110","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod una sit cognitio vespertina et matutina. Dicitur enim Gen. I, *factum est vespere et mane dies unus*. Sed per diem intelligitur cognitio angelica, ut Augustinus dicit. Ergo una et eadem est cognitio in Angelis matutina et vespertina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0111","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0112","text":"Praeterea, impossibile est unam potentiam simul duas operationes habere. Sed Angeli semper sunt in actu cognitionis matutinae, quia semper vident Deum et res in Deo, secundum illud Matth. XVIII, *Angeli eorum semper vident faciem patris mei* et cetera. Ergo, si cognitio vespertina esset alia a matutina, nullo modo Angelus posset esse in actu cognitionis vespertinae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0113","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0114","text":"Praeterea, apostolus dicit, I Cor. XIII, *cum venerit quod perfectum est, evacuabitur quod ex parte est*. Sed si cognitio vespertina sit alia a matutina, comparatur ad ipsam sicut imperfectum ad perfectum. Ergo non poterit simul vespertina cognitio esse cum matutina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0115","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0116","text":"In contrarium est quod dicit Augustinus, IV super Gen. ad Litt., *quod multum interest inter cognitionem rei cuiuscumque in verbo Dei, et cognitionem eius in natura eius, ut illud merito pertineat ad diem, hoc ad vesperam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0117","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0118","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, cognitio vespertina dicitur, qua Angeli cognoscunt res in propria natura. Quod non potest ita intelligi quasi ex propria rerum natura cognitionem accipiant, ut haec praepositio in indicet habitudinem principii, quia non accipiunt Angeli cognitionem a rebus, ut supra habitum est. Relinquitur igitur quod hoc quod dicitur in propria natura, accipiatur secundum rationem cogniti, secundum quod subest cognitioni; ut scilicet cognitio vespertina in Angelis dicatur secundum quod cognoscunt esse rerum quod habent res in propria natura. Quod quidem per duplex medium cognoscunt, scilicet per species innatas, et per rationes rerum in verbo existentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0119","text":"Non enim, videndo verbum, cognoscunt solum illud esse rerum quod habent in verbo; sed illud esse quod habent in propria natura; sicut Deus per hoc quod videt se, cognoscit esse rerum quod habent in propria natura. Si ergo dicatur cognitio vespertina secundum quod cognoscunt esse rerum quod habent in propria natura, videndo verbum; sic una et eadem secundum essentiam est cognitio vespertina et matutina, differens solum secundum cognita. Si vero cognitio vespertina dicatur secundum quod Angeli cognoscunt esse rerum quod habent in propria natura, per formas innatas; sic alia est cognitio vespertina et matutina. Et ita videtur intelligere Augustinus, cum unam ponat imperfectam respectu alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0120","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0121","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut numerus sex dierum, secundum intellectum Augustini, accipitur secundum sex genera rerum quae cognoscuntur ab Angelis; ita unitas diei accipitur secundum unitatem rei cognitae, quae tamen diversis cognitionibus cognosci potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0122","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0123","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod duae operationes possunt simul esse unius potentiae, quarum una ad aliam refertur; ut patet cum voluntas simul vult et finem et ea quae sunt ad finem, et intellectus simul intelligit principia et conclusiones per principia, quando iam scientiam acquisivit. Cognitio autem vespertina in Angelis refertur ad matutinam, ut Augustinus dicit. Unde nihil prohibet utramque simul esse in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0124","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0125","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, veniente perfecto, evacuatur imperfectum quod ei opponitur, sicut fides, quae est eorum quae non videntur, evacuatur visione veniente. Sed imperfectio vespertinae cognitionis non opponitur perfectioni matutinae. Quod enim cognoscatur aliquid in seipso, non est oppositum ei quod cognoscatur in sua causa. Nec iterum quod aliquid cognoscatur per duo media, quorum unum est perfectius et aliud imperfectius, aliquid repugnans habet, sicut ad eandem conclusionem habere possumus et medium demonstrativum et dialecticum. Et similiter eadem res potest sciri ab Angelo per verbum increatum, et per speciem innatam.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"529e11ea6f759570add0b1191a61eac986f60aec5e7682eba0695d3bbbe8f4bb","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T144302609022Z_e87344f2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 58","source_sha256":"529e11ea6f759570add0b1191a61eac986f60aec5e7682eba0695d3bbbe8f4bb","sha256":"72a16c95cb2305f928ec0440701161a89b5eb4c38a07a3c5b850d555668c67fe","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:43:02.609043+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0002","text":"[I q. 58 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0003","text":"After this, we must consider the mode of angelic cognition. And concerning this, seven questions are asked. First, whether the intellect of an angel is sometimes in potency and sometimes in act. Second, whether an angel can understand many things simultaneously. Third, whether it understands by discursive reasoning. Fourth, whether it understands by composing and dividing. Fifth, whether falsity can exist in an angel's intellect. Sixth, whether an angel's cognition can be called morning and evening. Seventh, whether morning and evening cognition are the same or different.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0005","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0006","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that the intellect of an angel is sometimes in potency. For motion is the act of that which exists in potency, as is said in Physics III. But angelic minds are moved in understanding, as Dionysius says, chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*. Therefore angelic minds are sometimes in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0007","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0008","text":"Further, since desire is for a thing not possessed, though capable of being possessed, whoever desires to understand something is in potency toward it. But it is said in I Peter 1, *into which angels desire to look*. Therefore the intellect of an angel is sometimes in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0009","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0010","text":"Further, it is said in the *Book of Causes* that an intelligence understands according to the mode of its substance. But the substance of an angel has something of potency mixed into it. Therefore it sometimes understands in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0011","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in II *On Genesis according to the Letter*, that *from the moment they were created, angels enjoy the very eternity of the Word in holy and pious contemplation*. But an intellect that contemplates is not in potency, but in act. Therefore the intellect of an angel is not in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0013","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0014","text":"I answer that, as the Philosopher says, in III *On the Soul* and VIII *Physics*, the intellect is in potency in two ways: in one way, as before learning or discovering, that is, before it possesses the habit of knowledge; in another way, it is said to be in potency as when it already possesses the habit of knowledge but does not consider it. Therefore, in the first way, the intellect of an angel is never in potency with respect to those things to which its natural cognition can extend. For just as the higher bodies, namely the heavenly bodies, do not have a potency for being that is not completed by act, so the heavenly intellects, namely the angels, do not have any intelligible potency that is not wholly completed by intelligible species connatural to them. But with respect to those things which are divinely revealed to them, nothing prevents their intellects from being in potency, since in this way even heavenly bodies are sometimes in potency to be illuminated by the sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0015","text":"But in the second way, the intellect of an angel can be in potency toward those things which it knows by natural cognition; for it does not always actually consider all the things it knows by natural cognition. But with respect to cognition of the Word, and of those things which it sees in the Word, it is never in potency in this way, because it always actually beholds the Word and those things which it sees in the Word. For their beatitude consists in this vision, and beatitude does not consist in a habit, but in act, as the Philosopher says in I *Ethics*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0016","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that motion is not taken there insofar as it is the act of the imperfect, that is, of that which exists in potency; but insofar as it is the act of the perfect, that is, of that which exists in act. For in this way understanding and sensing are called motions, as is said in III *On the Soul*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0018","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that this desire of the angels does not exclude the thing desired, but excludes weariness with it. Or they are said to desire the vision of God with respect to the new revelations which they receive from God as the exigency of affairs requires.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0020","text":"[I q. 58 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that in the substance of an angel there is no potency stripped of act. And likewise, neither is the intellect of an angel in potency in such a way that it is without act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0023","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0024","text":"The second is approached thus. It seems that an angel cannot understand many things simultaneously. For the Philosopher says, in II *Topics*, that *it is possible to know many things, but to understand only one*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0025","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0026","text":"Moreover, nothing is understood except insofar as the intellect is informed by an intelligible species, just as a body is informed by a figure. But one body cannot be informed by diverse figures. Therefore, one intellect cannot understand diverse intelligibles simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0027","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0028","text":"Moreover, understanding is a certain motion. But no motion is terminated at diverse termini. Therefore, it is not possible to understand many things simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0029","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in IV On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, “the spiritual power of the angelic mind most easily comprehends simultaneously all things that it wills.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0031","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0032","text":"I answer that, just as unity of terminus is required for the unity of motion, so unity of object is required for the unity of an operation. Now some things can be taken as many, and as one; as the parts of some continuum. For if each is taken by itself, they are many, whence they are not apprehended by sense and intellect in one operation, nor simultaneously. In another way, they are taken insofar as they are one in the whole, and thus they are known simultaneously and by one operation both by sense and by intellect, while the whole continuum is considered, as is said in III On the Soul. And likewise our intellect simultaneously understands subject and predicate, insofar as they are parts of one proposition; and two things compared, insofar as they agree in one comparison. From this it is clear that many things, insofar as they are distinct, cannot be understood simultaneously; but insofar as they are united in one intelligible, they are thus understood simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0033","text":"But each thing is intelligible in act insofar as its likeness is in the intellect. Therefore, whatever things can be known through one intelligible species are known as one intelligible; and therefore they are known simultaneously. But those things which are known through diverse intelligible species are apprehended as diverse intelligibles. Angels, therefore, by that cognition whereby they know things through the Word, know all things through one intelligible species, which is the divine essence. And therefore, as to such cognition, they know all things simultaneously, just as also in the fatherland “our thoughts will not be changeable, going and returning from some things to others, but we shall see all our knowledge simultaneously in one glance,” as Augustine says in XV On the Trinity. But by that cognition whereby they know things through innate species, they can understand simultaneously all those things which are known through one species, but not those which are known through diverse species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0034","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to understand many things as one is in a certain way to understand one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0036","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0037","text":"To the second, it must be said that the intellect is informed by the intelligible species which it has within itself. And therefore it can, by one intelligible species, behold many intelligibles simultaneously, just as one body can, by one figure, be simultaneously assimilated to many bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0038","text":"[I q. 58 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0039","text":"To the third, it must be said as to the first.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0041","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0042","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that an angel knows by discursion. For the discursion of the intellect is considered according to this, that one thing is known through another. But angels know one thing through another, for they know creatures through the Word. Therefore, an angel's intellect knows by discursion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0043","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0044","text":"Moreover, whatever an inferior power can do, a superior power can also do. But the human intellect can syllogize, and know causes in effects, according to which discursion is considered. Therefore, the intellect of an angel, which is superior in the order of nature, can do this much more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0045","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0046","text":"Moreover, Isidore says that demons know many things through experience. But experiential cognition is discursive, for “from many memories one experience comes to be, and from many experiences one universal comes to be,” as is said at the end of the Posterior Analytics and at the beginning of the Metaphysics. Therefore, the cognition of angels is discursive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0047","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0048","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter VII of On the Divine Names, that “angels do not gather divine cognition from dispersed discourses, nor are they at the same time led from anything common to these particular things.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0049","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0050","text":"I answer that, as has often been said, angels hold that grade among spiritual substances which heavenly bodies hold among corporeal substances; for they are also called by Dionysius “heavenly minds.” Now there is this difference between heavenly and earthly bodies: earthly bodies attain their ultimate perfection through change and motion, whereas heavenly bodies have their ultimate perfection at once, from their very nature. So too the lower intellects, namely those of human beings, attain perfection in the knowledge of truth through a certain motion and discursion of intellectual operation; namely, while they proceed from one thing known to another thing known. But if, at once in the very cognition of a known principle, they were to behold as known all the consequent conclusions, there would be no place in them for discursion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0051","text":"And this is so in angels, because at once, in those things which they first know naturally, they behold all things whatsoever that can be known in them. And therefore they are called intellectual, because even among us those things which are at once naturally apprehended are said to be understood; hence intellect is called the habit of first principles. But human souls, which acquire knowledge of truth through a certain discursion, are called rational. And this occurs because of the weakness of the intellectual light in them. For if they possessed the fullness of intellectual light, as angels do, then at once, at the first glance at the principles, they would comprehend their entire force, intuiting whatever could be syllogized from them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0052","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that discursion denotes a certain motion. But every motion is from one prior thing to another subsequent thing. Hence discursive cognition is understood insofar as, from something previously known, one arrives at cognition of another thing subsequently known, which was previously unknown. But if, upon beholding one thing, another is beheld at the same time—as in a mirror the image of a thing and the thing itself are beheld at the same time—cognition is not on that account discursive. And in this way angels know things in the Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0054","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0055","text":"To the second, it must be said that angels can syllogize, insofar as they know a syllogism; and in causes they see effects, and in effects causes, yet not in such a way that they acquire knowledge of an unknown truth by syllogizing from causes to things caused, and from things caused to causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0056","text":"[I q. 58 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0057","text":"To the third, it must be said that experience is said of angels and demons according to a certain likeness, insofar namely as they know present sensible things; yet without any discursion whatsoever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0058","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0059","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0060","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that angels understand by composing and dividing. For where there is a multitude of understandings, there is composition of understandings, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. But in an angel’s intellect there is a multitude of understandings, since by diverse species it understands diverse things, and not all things at once. Therefore, in an angel’s intellect there is composition and division.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0061","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0062","text":"Moreover, negation is more distant from affirmation than any two opposed natures are, because the first distinction is through affirmation and negation. But an angel does not know certain distant natures through one [species], but through diverse species, as is clear from what has been said. Therefore it must know affirmation and negation through diverse [species]. And thus it seems that an angel understands by composing and dividing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0063","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0064","text":"Moreover, speech is a sign of intellect. But angels speaking to human beings utter affirmative and negative propositions, which are signs of composition and division in the intellect; as appears from many places in Sacred Scripture. Therefore it seems that an angel understands by composing and dividing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0065","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0066","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter VII of *The Divine Names*, that *the intellectual power of angels shines forth with the perspicuous simplicity of divine understandings*. But simple understanding is without composition and division, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. Therefore an angel understands without composition and division.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0067","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0068","text":"I answer that, just as in the reasoning intellect a conclusion is related to a principle, so in the intellect that composes and divides a predicate is related to a subject. For if the intellect were to see the truth of the conclusion at once in the principle itself, it would never understand by discursing or reasoning. Likewise, if the intellect, at once in the apprehension of the quiddity of the subject, had knowledge of all things that can be attributed to the subject or removed from it, it would never understand by composing and dividing, but only by understanding what a thing is. Thus, therefore, it is clear that our intellect understands by discursing, and by composing and dividing, for the same reason: namely, because at once in the first apprehension of some first apprehended thing, it cannot behold whatever is contained in it virtually. This happens because of the weakness of the intellectual light in us, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0069","text":"Hence, since there is in an angel perfect intellectual light, since it is a pure and most clear mirror, as Dionysius says in chapter IV of *The Divine Names*, it follows that, just as an angel does not understand by reasoning, so neither does it understand by composing and dividing. Nevertheless, it understands the composition and division of propositions, just as it understands the reasoning of syllogisms; for it understands composite things simply, mobile things immovably, and material things immaterially.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0070","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0071","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that not just any multitude of understandings causes composition, but a multitude of those understandings of which one is attributed to another or removed from another. But an angel, in understanding the quiddity of some thing, understands at the same time whatever can be attributed to it or removed from it. Hence, by understanding what a thing is, it understands, through its one simple understanding, whatever we can understand even by composing and dividing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0072","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0073","text":"To the second, it must be said that the diverse quiddities of things differ less, as regards the account of existing, than affirmation and negation. Yet, as regards the account of knowing, affirmation and negation agree more, because as soon as the truth of an affirmation is known, the falsity of the opposed negation is known.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0074","text":"[I q. 58 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0075","text":"To the third, it must be said that the fact that angels speak affirmative and negative propositions shows that angels know composition and division, but not that they know by composing and dividing; rather, by simply knowing what a thing is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0076","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0077","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0078","text":"The fifth is approached as follows. It seems that there can be falsity in an angel’s intellect. For perversity pertains to falsity. But in demons there is a perverse imagination, as Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*. Therefore, it seems that falsity can be in the intellect of angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0079","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0080","text":"Further, ignorance is a cause of false estimation. But ignorance can be in angels, as Dionysius says, in chapter VI of *The Ecclesiastical Hierarchy*. Therefore, it seems that falsity can be in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0081","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0082","text":"Further, everything that falls away from the truth of wisdom and has a depraved reason has falsity or error in its intellect. But Dionysius says this of demons, in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*. Therefore, it seems that falsity can be in the intellect of angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0083","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0084","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in III *On the Soul*, that *the intellect is always true*. Augustine also says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, that *nothing is understood except what is true*. But angels know nothing except by understanding. Therefore, there cannot be deception and falsity in an angel’s cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0085","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0086","text":"I answer that the truth of this question depends in some measure on what has been set forth above. For it has been said that an angel does not understand by composing and dividing, but by understanding what a thing is. Now the intellect concerning what a thing is is always true, just as sense is concerning its proper object, as is said in III *On the Soul*. But in us deception and falsity occur accidentally in understanding what a thing is, namely, according to the character of some composition: either when we take the definition of one thing as the definition of another; or when the parts of a definition do not cohere with one another, as if “a four-footed flying animal” were taken as the definition of some thing (for no animal is such). And this indeed occurs in composites, whose definition is taken from diverse things, one of which is material in relation to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0087","text":"But in understanding simple quiddities, as is said in IX *Metaphysics*, there is no falsity, because either they are not attained at all, and we understand nothing about them; or they are known as they are. Thus, therefore, falsity or error or deception cannot be in itself in the intellect of any angel; but it occurs accidentally. Yet in another way than in us. For we sometimes arrive at understanding a quiddity by composing and dividing, as when, by dividing or demonstrating, we investigate a definition. This does not occur in angels; rather, through what a thing is they know all the propositions pertaining to that thing. Now it is clear that a thing’s quiddity can be a principle of knowing with respect to those things that naturally belong to the thing or are removed from it, but not with respect to those things that depend upon God’s supernatural ordering.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0088","text":"Good angels, therefore, having a right will, do not judge through their knowledge of a thing’s quiddity concerning those things that naturally pertain to the thing except with the divine ordering preserved. Hence falsity or error cannot be in them. Demons, however, through a perverse will withdrawing their intellect from divine wisdom, sometimes judge about things absolutely according to their natural condition. And in those things that naturally pertain to a thing, they are not deceived. But they can be deceived with respect to those things that are supernatural, as when, considering a dead man, one judges that he will not rise again; and when, seeing the man Christ, one judges that he is not God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0089","text":"[I q. 58 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0090","text":"And through this the answer to the points objected on both sides is clear. For the perversity of demons consists in their not being subject to divine wisdom. Ignorance, moreover, is in angels not with respect to naturally knowable things, but to supernatural things. It is also clear that the intellect of what a thing is is always true, unless accidentally, insofar as it is improperly ordered to some composition or division.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0091","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0092","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0093","text":"The sixth is approached as follows. It seems that there is neither evening nor morning cognition in angels. For evening and morning have an admixture of darkness. But there is no darkness in an angel’s cognition, since there is no error or falsity there. Therefore, an angel’s cognition ought not to be called morning or evening.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0094","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0095","text":"Further, night falls between evening and morning, and midday falls between morning and evening. Therefore, if morning and evening cognition occur in angels, by the same reasoning it seems that there ought to be midday and night cognition in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0096","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0097","text":"Moreover, cognition is distinguished according to the difference of the things known; hence the Philosopher says in *On the Soul* III that *the sciences are divided in the same way as things are*. Now things have a threefold being: namely, in the Word, in their own nature, and in angelic intelligence, as Augustine says in *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis* II. Therefore, if morning and evening cognition are posited in angels on account of the being of things in the Word and in their own nature, a third cognition should also be posited in them, on account of the being of things in angelic intelligence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0098","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0099","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine, in *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis* IV and *The City of God* XI, distinguishes the cognition of angels into morning and evening.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0100","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0101","text":"I answer that it must be said that what is said concerning morning and evening cognition in angels was introduced by Augustine, who wishes the six days in which God is read to have made all things, in Genesis I, to be understood not as these customary days which are completed by the circuit of the sun, since the sun is read to have been made on the fourth day, but as one day, namely, angelic cognition presented with the six genera of things. Now, just as in an ordinary day morning is the beginning of the day, while evening is its end, so cognition of the primordial being itself of things is called morning cognition, and this is according as things are in the Word. But cognition of the being itself of a created thing, according as it subsists in its own nature, is called evening cognition; for the being of things flows from the Word as from a kind of primordial principle, and this outflow is terminated in the being of things which they have in their own nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0102","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0103","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that evening and morning are not taken in angelic cognition according to a likeness to the admixture of darkness, but according to a likeness of beginning and end. Or it must be said that nothing prevents, as Augustine says in *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis* IV, something from being called light in comparison with one thing and darkness in comparison with another. Thus the life of the faithful and just, in comparison with the impious, is called light, according to Ephesians V, *you were once darkness, but now light in the Lord*; yet this life of the faithful, in comparison with the life of glory, is called dark, according to II Peter I, *you have the prophetic word, to which you do well to attend as to a lamp shining in a dark place*. Thus, therefore, the cognition by which an angel knows things in their own nature is day in comparison with ignorance or error, but is obscure in comparison with the vision of the Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0104","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0105","text":"To the second it must be said that morning and evening cognition belong to day, that is, to the illuminated angels, who are distinct from darkness, that is, from the evil angels. But good angels, when knowing the creature, do not fix themselves upon it, which would be to grow dark and for night to come to be; rather, they refer this very thing to the praise of God, in whom they know all things as in their principle. And therefore night is not placed after evening, but morning, so that morning is the end of the preceding day and the beginning of the following one, insofar as the angels refer their cognition of the preceding work to the praise of God. Noon, however, is comprehended under the name of day, as being the middle between two extremes. Or noon can be referred to the cognition of God himself, who has neither beginning nor end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0106","text":"[I q. 58 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0107","text":"To the third it must be said that the angels themselves too are creatures. Hence the being of things in angelic intelligence is included under evening cognition, as is also the being of things in their own nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0108","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0109","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0110","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh article. It seems that evening and morning cognition are one. For it is said in Genesis I, *there came to be evening and morning, one day*. But by day angelic cognition is understood, as Augustine says. Therefore morning and evening cognition in angels are one and the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0111","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0112","text":"Moreover, it is impossible for one power to have two operations at the same time. But angels are always in the act of morning cognition, because they always see God and things in God, according to Matthew XVIII, *their angels always see the face of my Father*, and so forth. Therefore, if evening cognition were other than morning cognition, an angel could in no way be in the act of evening cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0113","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0114","text":"Moreover, the Apostle says, I Corinthians XIII, *when that which is perfect has come, that which is in part will be done away with*. But if evening cognition is other than morning cognition, it is compared to it as the imperfect to the perfect. Therefore evening cognition will not be able to exist at the same time as morning cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0115","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0116","text":"On the contrary is what Augustine says in *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis* IV: *there is a great difference between the cognition of any thing whatsoever in the Word of God and its cognition in its nature, so that the former deservedly belongs to day, the latter to evening*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0117","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0118","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as has been stated, evening cognition is called that by which angels know things in their own nature. This cannot be understood as though they receive cognition from the nature of things themselves, such that this preposition *in* indicates the relation of a principle, because angels do not receive cognition from things, as was established above. It remains, therefore, that what is said, “in their own nature,” be understood according to the account of the known thing, according as it is subject to cognition; namely, so that evening cognition in angels is called such according as they know the being of things which things have in their own nature. They know this through a twofold medium: namely, through innate species and through the intelligible accounts of things existing in the Word.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0119","text":"For by seeing the Word, they know not only that being of things which they have in the Word, but also that being which they have in their own nature; just as God, by seeing himself, knows the being of things which they have in their own nature. Therefore, if evening cognition is called such according as they know the being of things which they have in their own nature by seeing the Word, then evening and morning cognition are one and the same in essence, differing only according to the things known. But if evening cognition is called such according as angels know the being of things which they have in their own nature through innate forms, then evening cognition is other than morning cognition. And Augustine seems to understand it in this way, when he posits one as imperfect in relation to the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0120","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0121","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, just as the number of six days, according to Augustine’s understanding, is taken according to the six kinds of things that are known by the angels, so the unity of the day is taken according to the unity of the thing known, which nevertheless can be known by diverse cognitions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0122","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0123","text":"To the second, it must be said that two operations can be simultaneous in one power, one of which is referred to the other; as is clear when the will simultaneously wills both the end and the things that are for the end, and the intellect simultaneously understands principles and conclusions through the principles, when it has already acquired knowledge. But evening cognition in the angels is referred to morning cognition, as Augustine says. Hence nothing prevents both from being simultaneous in the angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0124","text":"[I q. 58 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0125","text":"To the third, it must be said that, when the perfect comes, the imperfect which is opposed to it is done away with, just as faith, which concerns things not seen, is done away with when vision comes. But the imperfection of evening cognition is not opposed to the perfection of morning cognition. For that something is known in itself is not opposed to its being known in its cause. Nor again is there anything incompatible in something being known through two media, one of which is more perfect and the other less perfect, just as we can have for the same conclusion both a demonstrative and a dialectical middle term. And similarly, the same thing can be known by an angel through the uncreated Word and through an innate species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_50:P0125"]}],"source_digest":"e88a3e2452ba73c2935da935b7ef68ea62d9746577bd02928f1df43e5090af6b","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 58"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 58","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51","title":"First Part · Question 59","order":60,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_51","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 59 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30859)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad voluntatem Angelorum. Et primo considerabimus de ipsa voluntate secundo, de motu eius, qui est amor sive dilectio. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in Angelis sit voluntas. Secundo, utrum voluntas Angeli sit ipsa natura eorum, vel etiam ipse intellectus eorum. Tertio, utrum in Angelis sit liberum arbitrium. Quarto, utrum in eis sit irascibilis et concupiscibilis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30860)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit voluntas. Quia, ut dicit philosophus, in III de anima, *voluntas in ratione est*. Sed in Angelis non est ratio, sed aliquid superius ratione. Ergo in Angelis non est voluntas, sed aliquid superius voluntate.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30861)\n\nPraeterea, voluntas sub appetitu continetur, ut patet per philosophum, in III de anima. Sed appetitus est imperfecti, est enim eius quod nondum habetur. Cum igitur in Angelis, maxime in beatis, non sit aliqua imperfectio, videtur quod non sit in eis voluntas.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30862)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod voluntas est movens motum, movetur enim ab appetibili intellecto. Sed Angeli sunt immobiles; cum sint incorporei. Ergo in Angelis non est voluntas.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30863)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X Lib. de Trin., quod imago Trinitatis invenitur in mente secundum memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem. Imago autem Dei invenitur non solum in mente humana, sed etiam in mente angelica; cum etiam mens angelica sit capax Dei. Ergo in Angelis est voluntas.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30864)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere in Angelis voluntatem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum omnia procedant ex voluntate divina, omnia suo modo per appetitum inclinantur in bonum, sed diversimode. Quaedam enim inclinantur in bonum, per solam naturalem habitudinem, absque cognitione, sicut plantae et corpora inanimata. Et talis inclinatio ad bonum vocatur appetitus naturalis. Quaedam vero ad bonum inclinantur cum aliqua cognitione; non quidem sic quod cognoscant ipsam rationem boni, sed cognoscunt aliquod bonum particulare; sicut sensus, qui cognoscit dulce et album et aliquid huiusmodi. Inclinatio autem hanc cognitionem sequens, dicitur appetitus sensitivus. Quaedam vero inclinantur ad bonum cum cognitione qua cognoscunt ipsam boni rationem; quod est proprium intellectus. Et haec perfectissime inclinantur in bonum; non quidem quasi ab alio solummodo directa in bonum, sicut ea quae cognitione carent; neque in bonum particulariter tantum, sicut ea in quibus est sola sensitiva cognitio; sed quasi inclinata in ipsum universale bonum. Et haec inclinatio dicitur voluntas. Unde cum Angeli per intellectum cognoscant ipsam universalem rationem boni, manifestum est quod in eis sit voluntas.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30865)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aliter ratio transcendit sensum, et aliter intellectus rationem. Ratio enim transcendit sensum, secundum diversitatem cognitorum, nam sensus est particularium, ratio vero universalium. Et ideo oportet quod sit alius appetitus tendens in bonum universale, qui debetur rationi; et alius tendens in bonum particulare, qui debetur sensui. Sed intellectus et ratio differunt quantum ad modum cognoscendi, quia scilicet intellectus cognoscit simplici intuitu, ratio vero discurrendo de uno in aliud. Sed tamen ratio per discursum pervenit ad cognoscendum illud, quod intellectus sine discursu cognoscit, scilicet universale. Idem est ergo obiectum quod appetitivae proponitur et ex parte rationis, et ex parte intellectus. Unde in Angelis, qui sunt intellectuales tantum, non est appetitus superior voluntate.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30866)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, licet nomen appetitivae partis sit sumptum ab appetendo ea quae non habentur, tamen appetitiva pars non solum ad haec se extendit, sed etiam ad multa alia. Sicut et nomen lapidis sumptum est a laesione pedis, cum tamen lapidi non hoc solum conveniat. Similiter irascibilis potentia denominatur ab ira; cum tamen in ea sint plures aliae passiones, ut spes et audacia et huiusmodi.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30867)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod voluntas dicitur movens motum, secundum quod velle est motus quidam, et intelligere; cuiusmodi motum nihil prohibet in Angelis esse, quia talis motus est actus perfecti, ut dicitur in III de anima.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30868)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non differat voluntas ab intellectu et natura. Angelus enim est simplicior quam corpus naturale. Sed corpus naturale per suam formam inclinatur in suum finem, qui est eius bonum. Ergo multo magis Angelus. Forma autem Angeli est vel natura ipsa in qua subsistit, vel species quae est in intellectu eius. Ergo Angelus inclinatur in bonum per naturam suam, et per speciem intelligibilem. Haec autem inclinatio ad bonum pertinet ad voluntatem. Voluntas igitur Angeli non est aliud quam eius natura vel intellectus.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30869)\n\nPraeterea, obiectum intellectus est verum, voluntatis autem bonum. Bonum autem et verum non differunt realiter, sed secundum rationem tantum. Ergo voluntas et intellectus non differunt realiter.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30870)\n\nPraeterea, distinctio communis et proprii non diversificat potentias, eadem enim potentia visiva est coloris et albedinis. Sed bonum et verum videntur se habere sicut commune et proprium, nam verum est quoddam bonum, scilicet intellectus. Ergo voluntas, cuius obiectum est bonum, non differt ab intellectu, cuius obiectum est verum.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30871)\n\nSed contra, voluntas in Angelis est bonorum tantum. Intellectus autem est bonorum et malorum, cognoscunt enim utrumque. Ergo voluntas in Angelis est aliud quam eius intellectus.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30872)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod voluntas in Angelis est quaedam virtus vel potentia, quae nec est ipsa eorum natura, nec eorum intellectus. Et quod non sit eorum natura, apparet ex hoc, quod natura vel essentia alicuius rei intra ipsam rem comprehenditur, quidquid ergo se extendit ad id quod est extra rem, non est rei essentia. Unde videmus in corporibus naturalibus, quod inclinatio quae est ad esse rei, non est per aliquid superadditum essentiae; sed per materiam, quae appetit esse antequam illud habeat, et per formam, quae tenet rem in esse postquam fuerit. Sed inclinatio ad aliquid extrinsecum, est per aliquid essentiae superadditum, sicut inclinatio ad locum est per gravitatem vel levitatem, inclinatio autem ad faciendum sibi simile est per qualitates activas. Voluntas autem habet inclinationem in bonum naturaliter. Unde ibi solum est idem essentia et voluntas, ubi totaliter bonum continetur in essentia volentis; scilicet in Deo, qui nihil vult extra se nisi ratione suae bonitatis. Quod de nulla creatura potest dici; cum bonum infinitum sit extra essentiam cuiuslibet creati. Unde nec voluntas Angeli, nec alterius creaturae, potest esse idem quod eius essentia. Similiter nec potest esse idem quod intellectus Angeli vel hominis. Nam cognitio fit per hoc quod cognitum est in cognoscente, unde ea ratione se extendit eius intellectus in id quod est extra se, secundum quod illud quod extra ipsum est per essentiam, natum est aliquo modo in eo esse. Voluntas vero se extendit in id quod extra se est, secundum quod quadam inclinatione quodammodo tendit in rem exteriorem. Alterius autem virtutis est, quod aliquid habeat in se quod est extra se, et quod ipsum tendat in rem exteriorem. Et ideo oportet quod in qualibet creatura sit aliud intellectus et voluntas. Non autem in Deo, qui habet et ens universale et bonum universale in seipso. Unde tam voluntas quam intellectus est eius essentia.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30873)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod corpus naturale per formam substantialem inclinatur in esse suum, sed in exterius inclinatur per aliquid additum, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30874)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod potentiae non diversificantur secundum materialem distinctionem obiectorum, sed secundum formalem distinctionem, quae attenditur secundum rationem obiecti. Et ideo diversitas secundum rationem boni et veri, sufficit ad diversitatem intellectus et voluntatis.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30875)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quia bonum et verum convertuntur secundum rem, inde est quod et bonum ab intellectu intelligitur sub ratione veri, et verum a voluntate appetitur sub ratione boni. Sed tamen diversitas rationum ad diversificandum potentias sufficit, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30876)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit liberum arbitrium. Actus enim liberi arbitrii est eligere. Sed electio non potest esse in Angelis, cum electio sit appetitus praeconsiliati, consilium autem est inquisitio quaedam ut dicitur in III Ethic.; Angeli autem non cognoscunt inquirendo, quia hoc pertinet ad discursum rationis. Ergo videtur quod in Angelis non sit liberum arbitrium.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30877)\n\nPraeterea, liberum arbitrium se habet ad utrumlibet. Sed ex parte intellectus non est aliquid se habens ad utrumlibet in Angelis, quia intellectus eorum non fallitur in naturalibus intelligibilibus, ut dictum est. Ergo nec ex parte appetitus liberum arbitrium in eis esse potest.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30878)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae sunt naturalia in Angelis, conveniunt eis secundum magis et minus, quia in superioribus Angelis natura intellectualis est perfectior quam in inferioribus. Liberum autem arbitrium non recipit magis et minus. Ergo in Angelis non est liberum arbitrium.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30879)\n\nSed contra, libertas arbitrii ad dignitatem hominis pertinet. Sed Angeli digniores sunt hominibus. Ergo libertas arbitrii, cum sit in hominibus, multo magis est in Angelis.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30880)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quaedam sunt quae non agunt ex aliquo arbitrio, sed quasi ab aliis acta et mota, sicut sagitta a sagittante movetur ad finem. Quaedam vero agunt quodam arbitrio, sed non libero, sicut animalia irrationalia, ovis enim fugit lupum ex quodam iudicio, quo existimat eum sibi noxium; sed hoc iudicium non est sibi liberum, sed a natura inditum. Sed solum id quod habet intellectum, potest agere iudicio libero, inquantum cognoscit universalem rationem boni, ex qua potest iudicare hoc vel illud esse bonum. Unde ubicumque est intellectus, est liberum arbitrium. Et sic patet liberum arbitrium esse in Angelis etiam excellentius quam in hominibus, sicut et intellectum.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30881)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de electione secundum quod est hominis. Sicut autem aestimatio hominis in speculativis differt ab aestimatione Angeli in hoc, quod una est absque inquisitione, alia vero per inquisitionem; ita et in operativis. Unde in Angelis est electio; non tamen cum inquisitiva deliberatione consilii, sed per subitam acceptionem veritatis.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30882)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, cognitio fit per hoc quod cognita sunt in cognoscente. Ad imperfectionem autem alicuius rei pertinet, si non sit in ea id quod natum est in ea esse. Unde Angelus non esset perfectus in sua natura, si intellectus eius non esset determinatus ad omnem veritatem quam naturaliter cognoscere potest. Sed actus appetitivae virtutis est per hoc quod affectus inclinatur ad rem exteriorem. Non autem dependet perfectio rei ex omni re ad quam inclinatur, sed solum ex superiori. Et ideo non pertinet ad imperfectionem Angeli, si non habet voluntatem determinatam respectu eorum quae infra ipsum sunt. Pertineret autem ad imperfectionem eius, si indeterminate se haberet ad illud quod supra ipsum est.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30883)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod liberum arbitrium nobiliori modo est in superioribus Angelis, quam in inferioribus, sicut et iudicium intellectus. Tamen verum est quod ipsa libertas, secundum quod in ea consideratur quaedam remotio coactionis, non suscipit magis et minus, quia privationes et negationes non remittuntur nec intenduntur per se, sed solum per suam causam, vel secundum aliquam affirmationem adiunctam.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30884)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis sit irascibilis et concupiscibilis. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod in Daemonibus est furor irrationabilis et concupiscentia amens. Sed Daemones eiusdem naturae sunt cum Angelis, quia peccatum non mutavit in eis naturam. Ergo in Angelis est irascibilis et concupiscibilis.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30885)\n\nPraeterea, amor et gaudium in concupiscibili sunt; ira vero, spes et timor in irascibili. Sed haec attribuuntur Angelis bonis et malis in Scripturis. Ergo in Angelis est irascibilis et concupiscibilis.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30886)\n\nPraeterea, virtutes quaedam dicuntur esse in irascibili et concupiscibili; sicut caritas et temperantia videntur esse in concupiscibili, spes autem et fortitudo in irascibili. Sed virtutes hae sunt in Angelis. Ergo in Angelis est concupiscibilis et irascibilis.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30887)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis sunt in parte sensitiva; quae non est in Angelis. Ergo in eis non est irascibilis et concupiscibilis.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30888)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod intellectivus appetitus non dividitur per irascibilem et concupiscibilem, sed solum appetitus sensitivus. Cuius ratio est quia cum potentiae non distinguantur secundum distinctionem materialem obiectorum, sed solum secundum rationem formalem obiecti; si alicui potentiae respondeat aliquod obiectum secundum rationem communem, non erit distinctio potentiarum secundum diversitatem propriorum quae sub illo communi continentur. Sicut si proprium obiectum potentiae visivae est color secundum rationem coloris, non distinguuntur plures potentiae visivae secundum differentiam albi et nigri, sed si proprium obiectum alicuius potentiae esset album inquantum album, distingueretur potentia visiva albi a potentia visiva nigri. Manifestum est autem ex dictis quod obiectum appetitus intellectivi, qui voluntas dicitur, est bonum secundum communem boni rationem, nec potest esse aliquis appetitus nisi boni. Unde in parte intellectiva appetitus non dividitur secundum distinctionem aliquorum particularium bonorum; sicut dividitur appetitus sensitivus, qui non respicit bonum secundum communem rationem, sed quoddam particulare bonum. Unde, cum in Angelis non sit nisi appetitus intellectivus, eorum appetitus non distinguitur per irascibilem et concupiscibilem, sed remanet indivisus; et vocatur voluntas.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30889)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod furor et concupiscentia metaphorice dicuntur esse in Daemonibus, sicut et ira quandoque Deo attribuitur, propter similitudinem effectus.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30890)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod amor et gaudium, secundum quod sunt passiones, sunt in concupiscibili, sed secundum quod nominant simplicem voluntatis actum, sic sunt in intellectiva parte; prout amare est velle bonum alicui, et gaudere est quiescere voluntatem in aliquo bono habito. Et universaliter nihil horum dicitur de Angelis secundum passionem, ut Augustinus dicit, IX de Civ. Dei.\n\n[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30891)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod caritas, secundum quod est virtus, non est in concupiscibili, sed in voluntate. Nam obiectum concupiscibilis est bonum delectabile secundum sensum, huiusmodi autem non est bonum divinum, quod est obiectum caritatis. Et eadem ratione dicendum est quod spes non est in irascibili, quia obiectum irascibilis est quoddam arduum quod est sensibile, circa quod non est spes quae est virtus, sed circa arduum divinum. Temperantia autem, secundum quod est virtus humana, est circa concupiscentias delectabilium sensibilium, quae pertinent ad vim concupiscibilem. Et similiter fortitudo est circa audacias et timores quae sunt in irascibili. Et ideo temperantia, secundum quod est virtus humana, est in concupiscibili, et fortitudo in irascibili. Sed hoc modo non sunt in Angelis. Non enim in eis sunt passiones concupiscentiarum, vel timoris et audaciae, quas oporteat per temperantiam et fortitudinem regulare. Sed temperantia in eis dicitur, secundum quod moderate suam voluntatem exhibent secundum regulam divinae voluntatis. Et fortitudo in eis dicitur, secundum quod voluntatem divinam firmiter exequuntur. Quod totum fit per voluntatem; et non per irascibilem et concupiscibilem.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0002","text":"[I q. 59 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad voluntatem Angelorum. Et primo considerabimus de ipsa voluntate secundo, de motu eius, qui est amor sive dilectio. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in Angelis sit voluntas. Secundo, utrum voluntas Angeli sit ipsa natura eorum, vel etiam ipse intellectus eorum. Tertio, utrum in Angelis sit liberum arbitrium. Quarto, utrum in eis sit irascibilis et concupiscibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0005","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit voluntas. Quia, ut dicit philosophus, in III de anima, *voluntas in ratione est*. Sed in Angelis non est ratio, sed aliquid superius ratione. Ergo in Angelis non est voluntas, sed aliquid superius voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0007","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0008","text":"Praeterea, voluntas sub appetitu continetur, ut patet per philosophum, in III de anima. Sed appetitus est imperfecti, est enim eius quod nondum habetur. Cum igitur in Angelis, maxime in beatis, non sit aliqua imperfectio, videtur quod non sit in eis voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0009","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0010","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod voluntas est movens motum, movetur enim ab appetibili intellecto. Sed Angeli sunt immobiles; cum sint incorporei. Ergo in Angelis non est voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0011","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X Lib. de Trin., quod imago Trinitatis invenitur in mente secundum memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem. Imago autem Dei invenitur non solum in mente humana, sed etiam in mente angelica; cum etiam mens angelica sit capax Dei. Ergo in Angelis est voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0013","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere in Angelis voluntatem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum omnia procedant ex voluntate divina, omnia suo modo per appetitum inclinantur in bonum, sed diversimode. Quaedam enim inclinantur in bonum, per solam naturalem habitudinem, absque cognitione, sicut plantae et corpora inanimata. Et talis inclinatio ad bonum vocatur appetitus naturalis. Quaedam vero ad bonum inclinantur cum aliqua cognitione; non quidem sic quod cognoscant ipsam rationem boni, sed cognoscunt aliquod bonum particulare; sicut sensus, qui cognoscit dulce et album et aliquid huiusmodi. Inclinatio autem hanc cognitionem sequens, dicitur appetitus sensitivus. Quaedam vero inclinantur ad bonum cum cognitione qua cognoscunt ipsam boni rationem; quod est proprium intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0015","text":"Et haec perfectissime inclinantur in bonum; non quidem quasi ab alio solummodo directa in bonum, sicut ea quae cognitione carent; neque in bonum particulariter tantum, sicut ea in quibus est sola sensitiva cognitio; sed quasi inclinata in ipsum universale bonum. Et haec inclinatio dicitur voluntas. Unde cum Angeli per intellectum cognoscant ipsam universalem rationem boni, manifestum est quod in eis sit voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0016","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aliter ratio transcendit sensum, et aliter intellectus rationem. Ratio enim transcendit sensum, secundum diversitatem cognitorum, nam sensus est particularium, ratio vero universalium. Et ideo oportet quod sit alius appetitus tendens in bonum universale, qui debetur rationi; et alius tendens in bonum particulare, qui debetur sensui. Sed intellectus et ratio differunt quantum ad modum cognoscendi, quia scilicet intellectus cognoscit simplici intuitu, ratio vero discurrendo de uno in aliud. Sed tamen ratio per discursum pervenit ad cognoscendum illud, quod intellectus sine discursu cognoscit, scilicet universale. Idem est ergo obiectum quod appetitivae proponitur et ex parte rationis, et ex parte intellectus. Unde in Angelis, qui sunt intellectuales tantum, non est appetitus superior voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0018","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, licet nomen appetitivae partis sit sumptum ab appetendo ea quae non habentur, tamen appetitiva pars non solum ad haec se extendit, sed etiam ad multa alia. Sicut et nomen lapidis sumptum est a laesione pedis, cum tamen lapidi non hoc solum conveniat. Similiter irascibilis potentia denominatur ab ira; cum tamen in ea sint plures aliae passiones, ut spes et audacia et huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0020","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod voluntas dicitur movens motum, secundum quod velle est motus quidam, et intelligere; cuiusmodi motum nihil prohibet in Angelis esse, quia talis motus est actus perfecti, ut dicitur in III de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0023","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non differat voluntas ab intellectu et natura. Angelus enim est simplicior quam corpus naturale. Sed corpus naturale per suam formam inclinatur in suum finem, qui est eius bonum. Ergo multo magis Angelus. Forma autem Angeli est vel natura ipsa in qua subsistit, vel species quae est in intellectu eius. Ergo Angelus inclinatur in bonum per naturam suam, et per speciem intelligibilem. Haec autem inclinatio ad bonum pertinet ad voluntatem. Voluntas igitur Angeli non est aliud quam eius natura vel intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0025","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0026","text":"Praeterea, obiectum intellectus est verum, voluntatis autem bonum. Bonum autem et verum non differunt realiter, sed secundum rationem tantum. Ergo voluntas et intellectus non differunt realiter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0027","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0028","text":"Praeterea, distinctio communis et proprii non diversificat potentias, eadem enim potentia visiva est coloris et albedinis. Sed bonum et verum videntur se habere sicut commune et proprium, nam verum est quoddam bonum, scilicet intellectus. Ergo voluntas, cuius obiectum est bonum, non differt ab intellectu, cuius obiectum est verum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0029","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0030","text":"Sed contra, voluntas in Angelis est bonorum tantum. Intellectus autem est bonorum et malorum, cognoscunt enim utrumque. Ergo voluntas in Angelis est aliud quam eius intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0031","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod voluntas in Angelis est quaedam virtus vel potentia, quae nec est ipsa eorum natura, nec eorum intellectus. Et quod non sit eorum natura, apparet ex hoc, quod natura vel essentia alicuius rei intra ipsam rem comprehenditur, quidquid ergo se extendit ad id quod est extra rem, non est rei essentia. Unde videmus in corporibus naturalibus, quod inclinatio quae est ad esse rei, non est per aliquid superadditum essentiae; sed per materiam, quae appetit esse antequam illud habeat, et per formam, quae tenet rem in esse postquam fuerit. Sed inclinatio ad aliquid extrinsecum, est per aliquid essentiae superadditum, sicut inclinatio ad locum est per gravitatem vel levitatem, inclinatio autem ad faciendum sibi simile est per qualitates activas. Voluntas autem habet inclinationem in bonum naturaliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0033","text":"Unde ibi solum est idem essentia et voluntas, ubi totaliter bonum continetur in essentia volentis; scilicet in Deo, qui nihil vult extra se nisi ratione suae bonitatis. Quod de nulla creatura potest dici; cum bonum infinitum sit extra essentiam cuiuslibet creati. Unde nec voluntas Angeli, nec alterius creaturae, potest esse idem quod eius essentia. Similiter nec potest esse idem quod intellectus Angeli vel hominis. Nam cognitio fit per hoc quod cognitum est in cognoscente, unde ea ratione se extendit eius intellectus in id quod est extra se, secundum quod illud quod extra ipsum est per essentiam, natum est aliquo modo in eo esse. Voluntas vero se extendit in id quod extra se est, secundum quod quadam inclinatione quodammodo tendit in rem exteriorem. Alterius autem virtutis est, quod aliquid habeat in se quod est extra se, et quod ipsum tendat in rem exteriorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0034","text":"Et ideo oportet quod in qualibet creatura sit aliud intellectus et voluntas. Non autem in Deo, qui habet et ens universale et bonum universale in seipso. Unde tam voluntas quam intellectus est eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0035","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod corpus naturale per formam substantialem inclinatur in esse suum, sed in exterius inclinatur per aliquid additum, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0037","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod potentiae non diversificantur secundum materialem distinctionem obiectorum, sed secundum formalem distinctionem, quae attenditur secundum rationem obiecti. Et ideo diversitas secundum rationem boni et veri, sufficit ad diversitatem intellectus et voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0039","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quia bonum et verum convertuntur secundum rem, inde est quod et bonum ab intellectu intelligitur sub ratione veri, et verum a voluntate appetitur sub ratione boni. Sed tamen diversitas rationum ad diversificandum potentias sufficit, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0042","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit liberum arbitrium. Actus enim liberi arbitrii est eligere. Sed electio non potest esse in Angelis, cum electio sit appetitus praeconsiliati, consilium autem est inquisitio quaedam ut dicitur in III Ethic.; Angeli autem non cognoscunt inquirendo, quia hoc pertinet ad discursum rationis. Ergo videtur quod in Angelis non sit liberum arbitrium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0044","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0045","text":"Praeterea, liberum arbitrium se habet ad utrumlibet. Sed ex parte intellectus non est aliquid se habens ad utrumlibet in Angelis, quia intellectus eorum non fallitur in naturalibus intelligibilibus, ut dictum est. Ergo nec ex parte appetitus liberum arbitrium in eis esse potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0046","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0047","text":"Praeterea, ea quae sunt naturalia in Angelis, conveniunt eis secundum magis et minus, quia in superioribus Angelis natura intellectualis est perfectior quam in inferioribus. Liberum autem arbitrium non recipit magis et minus. Ergo in Angelis non est liberum arbitrium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0048","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0049","text":"Sed contra, libertas arbitrii ad dignitatem hominis pertinet. Sed Angeli digniores sunt hominibus. Ergo libertas arbitrii, cum sit in hominibus, multo magis est in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0050","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quaedam sunt quae non agunt ex aliquo arbitrio, sed quasi ab aliis acta et mota, sicut sagitta a sagittante movetur ad finem. Quaedam vero agunt quodam arbitrio, sed non libero, sicut animalia irrationalia, ovis enim fugit lupum ex quodam iudicio, quo existimat eum sibi noxium; sed hoc iudicium non est sibi liberum, sed a natura inditum. Sed solum id quod habet intellectum, potest agere iudicio libero, inquantum cognoscit universalem rationem boni, ex qua potest iudicare hoc vel illud esse bonum. Unde ubicumque est intellectus, est liberum arbitrium. Et sic patet liberum arbitrium esse in Angelis etiam excellentius quam in hominibus, sicut et intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0052","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de electione secundum quod est hominis. Sicut autem aestimatio hominis in speculativis differt ab aestimatione Angeli in hoc, quod una est absque inquisitione, alia vero per inquisitionem; ita et in operativis. Unde in Angelis est electio; non tamen cum inquisitiva deliberatione consilii, sed per subitam acceptionem veritatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0054","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, cognitio fit per hoc quod cognita sunt in cognoscente. Ad imperfectionem autem alicuius rei pertinet, si non sit in ea id quod natum est in ea esse. Unde Angelus non esset perfectus in sua natura, si intellectus eius non esset determinatus ad omnem veritatem quam naturaliter cognoscere potest. Sed actus appetitivae virtutis est per hoc quod affectus inclinatur ad rem exteriorem. Non autem dependet perfectio rei ex omni re ad quam inclinatur, sed solum ex superiori. Et ideo non pertinet ad imperfectionem Angeli, si non habet voluntatem determinatam respectu eorum quae infra ipsum sunt. Pertineret autem ad imperfectionem eius, si indeterminate se haberet ad illud quod supra ipsum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0056","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0057","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod liberum arbitrium nobiliori modo est in superioribus Angelis, quam in inferioribus, sicut et iudicium intellectus. Tamen verum est quod ipsa libertas, secundum quod in ea consideratur quaedam remotio coactionis, non suscipit magis et minus, quia privationes et negationes non remittuntur nec intenduntur per se, sed solum per suam causam, vel secundum aliquam affirmationem adiunctam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0058","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0059","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0060","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis sit irascibilis et concupiscibilis. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod in Daemonibus est furor irrationabilis et concupiscentia amens. Sed Daemones eiusdem naturae sunt cum Angelis, quia peccatum non mutavit in eis naturam. Ergo in Angelis est irascibilis et concupiscibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0061","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0062","text":"Praeterea, amor et gaudium in concupiscibili sunt; ira vero, spes et timor in irascibili. Sed haec attribuuntur Angelis bonis et malis in Scripturis. Ergo in Angelis est irascibilis et concupiscibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0063","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0064","text":"Praeterea, virtutes quaedam dicuntur esse in irascibili et concupiscibili; sicut caritas et temperantia videntur esse in concupiscibili, spes autem et fortitudo in irascibili. Sed virtutes hae sunt in Angelis. Ergo in Angelis est concupiscibilis et irascibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0065","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0066","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis sunt in parte sensitiva; quae non est in Angelis. Ergo in eis non est irascibilis et concupiscibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0067","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0068","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod intellectivus appetitus non dividitur per irascibilem et concupiscibilem, sed solum appetitus sensitivus. Cuius ratio est quia cum potentiae non distinguantur secundum distinctionem materialem obiectorum, sed solum secundum rationem formalem obiecti; si alicui potentiae respondeat aliquod obiectum secundum rationem communem, non erit distinctio potentiarum secundum diversitatem propriorum quae sub illo communi continentur. Sicut si proprium obiectum potentiae visivae est color secundum rationem coloris, non distinguuntur plures potentiae visivae secundum differentiam albi et nigri, sed si proprium obiectum alicuius potentiae esset album inquantum album, distingueretur potentia visiva albi a potentia visiva nigri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0069","text":"Manifestum est autem ex dictis quod obiectum appetitus intellectivi, qui voluntas dicitur, est bonum secundum communem boni rationem, nec potest esse aliquis appetitus nisi boni. Unde in parte intellectiva appetitus non dividitur secundum distinctionem aliquorum particularium bonorum; sicut dividitur appetitus sensitivus, qui non respicit bonum secundum communem rationem, sed quoddam particulare bonum. Unde, cum in Angelis non sit nisi appetitus intellectivus, eorum appetitus non distinguitur per irascibilem et concupiscibilem, sed remanet indivisus; et vocatur voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0070","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0071","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod furor et concupiscentia metaphorice dicuntur esse in Daemonibus, sicut et ira quandoque Deo attribuitur, propter similitudinem effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0072","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0073","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod amor et gaudium, secundum quod sunt passiones, sunt in concupiscibili, sed secundum quod nominant simplicem voluntatis actum, sic sunt in intellectiva parte; prout amare est velle bonum alicui, et gaudere est quiescere voluntatem in aliquo bono habito. Et universaliter nihil horum dicitur de Angelis secundum passionem, ut Augustinus dicit, IX de Civ. Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0074","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0075","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod caritas, secundum quod est virtus, non est in concupiscibili, sed in voluntate. Nam obiectum concupiscibilis est bonum delectabile secundum sensum, huiusmodi autem non est bonum divinum, quod est obiectum caritatis. Et eadem ratione dicendum est quod spes non est in irascibili, quia obiectum irascibilis est quoddam arduum quod est sensibile, circa quod non est spes quae est virtus, sed circa arduum divinum. Temperantia autem, secundum quod est virtus humana, est circa concupiscentias delectabilium sensibilium, quae pertinent ad vim concupiscibilem. Et similiter fortitudo est circa audacias et timores quae sunt in irascibili. Et ideo temperantia, secundum quod est virtus humana, est in concupiscibili, et fortitudo in irascibili. Sed hoc modo non sunt in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0076","text":"Non enim in eis sunt passiones concupiscentiarum, vel timoris et audaciae, quas oporteat per temperantiam et fortitudinem regulare. Sed temperantia in eis dicitur, secundum quod moderate suam voluntatem exhibent secundum regulam divinae voluntatis. Et fortitudo in eis dicitur, secundum quod voluntatem divinam firmiter exequuntur. Quod totum fit per voluntatem; et non per irascibilem et concupiscibilem.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"88320880ac9c73855d88a7c8d0c1ff765c1b15aec23ec0165831af2d881f5faa","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T144353806071Z_c69464d3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 59","source_sha256":"88320880ac9c73855d88a7c8d0c1ff765c1b15aec23ec0165831af2d881f5faa","sha256":"f98be6ce79e1716233ee1c186a0b580cd99a35efc81473836379c1d109a6c306","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:43:53.806088+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0002","text":"[I q. 59 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0003","text":"It remains consequently to consider those things that pertain to the will of the angels. And first we shall consider the will itself; second, its movement, which is love or dilection. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether there is will in angels. Second, whether an angel's will is their very nature, or even their very intellect. Third, whether there is free choice in angels. Fourth, whether there are in them the irascible and concupiscible powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0005","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that there is no will in angels. For, as the Philosopher says in *On the Soul*, III, *the will is in reason*. But in angels there is not reason, but something higher than reason. Therefore, in angels there is no will, but something higher than will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0007","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0008","text":"Further, the will is contained under appetite, as is clear from the Philosopher in *On the Soul*, III. But appetite belongs to what is imperfect, for it is of that which is not yet possessed. Therefore, since there is no imperfection in angels, especially in the blessed, it seems that there is no will in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0009","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0010","text":"Further, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul*, III, that the will is a moved mover, for it is moved by the intelligibly apprehended appetible. But angels are immovable, since they are incorporeal. Therefore, there is no will in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0011","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in Book X of *On the Trinity*, that the image of the Trinity is found in the mind according to memory, understanding, and will. But the image of God is found not only in the human mind, but also in the angelic mind, since the angelic mind too is capable of God. Therefore, there is will in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0013","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be held that there is will in angels. For the evidence of this, it should be considered that, since all things proceed from the divine will, all things are inclined in their own way toward the good through appetite, but in different ways. For some are inclined toward the good through a merely natural disposition, without cognition, as plants and inanimate bodies. And such an inclination toward the good is called natural appetite. But some are inclined toward the good with some cognition; not indeed so that they know the very notion of the good, but they know some particular good, as sense knows sweet and white and something of this sort. But the inclination following this cognition is called sensitive appetite. Yet some are inclined toward the good with a cognition by which they know the very notion of the good; and this is proper to intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0015","text":"And these are inclined most perfectly toward the good; not indeed as though directed toward the good solely by another, like those that lack cognition; nor only toward a particular good, like those in which there is only sensitive cognition; but as inclined toward the universal good itself. And this inclination is called will. Hence, since angels know through intellect the universal notion of the good itself, it is manifest that there is will in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0016","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it should be said that reason transcends sense in one way, and intellect transcends reason in another. For reason transcends sense according to the diversity of things known, since sense is of particulars, whereas reason is of universals. And therefore there must be one appetite tending toward the universal good, which belongs to reason; and another tending toward a particular good, which belongs to sense. But intellect and reason differ with respect to the mode of knowing: namely, because intellect knows by simple intuition, whereas reason knows by discoursing from one thing to another. Yet reason through discourse arrives at knowing that which intellect knows without discourse, namely, the universal. Therefore, the same object is proposed to the appetitive power both on the part of reason and on the part of intellect. Hence, in angels, who are intellectual alone, there is no appetite higher than will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0018","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0019","text":"To the second it should be said that, although the name of the appetitive part is taken from desiring things that are not possessed, nevertheless the appetitive part extends not only to these, but also to many other things. Just as the name of a stone is taken from injuring the foot, although this alone does not belong to a stone. Similarly, the irascible power is named from anger, although there are in it several other passions, such as hope and daring and the like.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0020","text":"[I q. 59 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0021","text":"To the third it should be said that the will is called a moved mover, insofar as willing is a certain movement, as is understanding; and nothing prevents movement of this sort from being in angels, because such movement is the act of what is perfect, as is said in *On the Soul*, III.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0023","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that in angels will does not differ from intellect and nature. For an angel is simpler than a natural body. But a natural body is inclined toward its end, which is its good, through its form. Therefore, much more is an angel. But an angel's form is either the very nature in which it subsists, or the species that is in its intellect. Therefore, an angel is inclined toward the good through its nature and through the intelligible species. But this inclination toward the good pertains to the will. Therefore, an angel's will is nothing other than its nature or intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0025","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0026","text":"Moreover, the object of the intellect is the true, whereas that of the will is the good. But the good and the true do not differ really, but only conceptually. Therefore, the will and the intellect do not differ really.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0027","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0028","text":"Moreover, the distinction between common and proper does not diversify powers; for the same power of sight is of color and of whiteness. But the good and the true seem to be related as common and proper, for the true is a certain good, namely, that of the intellect. Therefore, the will, whose object is the good, does not differ from the intellect, whose object is the true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0029","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0030","text":"On the contrary, the will in angels is concerned only with goods. But the intellect is concerned with goods and evils, for they know both. Therefore, the will in angels is other than their intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0031","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0032","text":"I answer that the will in angels is a certain faculty or power, which is neither their very nature nor their intellect. And that it is not their nature is apparent from this: the nature or essence of anything is contained within the thing itself; therefore, whatever extends to that which is outside the thing is not the essence of the thing. Hence we see in natural bodies that the inclination toward the being of a thing is not through something added to the essence, but through matter, which desires being before it has it, and through form, which keeps a thing in being after it has come to be. But the inclination toward something extrinsic is through something added to the essence: thus the inclination toward a place is through heaviness or lightness, while the inclination to produce something like itself is through active qualities. Now the will has an inclination toward the good naturally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0033","text":"Hence essence and will are the same only where the good is wholly contained in the essence of the one willing; namely, in God, who wills nothing outside Himself except by reason of His goodness. This cannot be said of any creature, since infinite good is outside the essence of every creature. Hence neither the will of an angel nor that of any other creature can be the same as its essence. Likewise, neither can it be the same as the intellect of an angel or a human being. For cognition occurs through the known's being in the knower; hence its intellect extends to that which is outside itself insofar as that which is outside it in essence is naturally apt to be in it in some way. But the will extends to that which is outside itself insofar as, by a certain inclination, it in some way tends toward the external thing. Now it belongs to a different power for something to have in itself that which is outside itself, and for it itself to tend toward an external thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0034","text":"And therefore, in every creature, intellect and will must be distinct. But not in God, who has both universal being and universal good in Himself. Hence both will and intellect are His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0035","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a natural body is inclined toward its own being through its substantial form, but is inclined toward what is external through something added, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0037","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0038","text":"To the second, it must be said that powers are not diversified according to the material distinction of objects, but according to their formal distinction, which is considered according to the intelligible account of the object. And therefore, a diversity in the account of the good and the true suffices for the diversity of intellect and will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0039","text":"[I q. 59 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that, because the good and the true are convertible in reality, it follows that the good is understood by the intellect under the aspect of the true, and the true is desired by the will under the aspect of the good. Nevertheless, the diversity of the aspects suffices to diversify the powers, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0042","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0043","text":"The third point is approached thus. It seems that there is no free choice in angels. For the act of free choice is to choose. But choice cannot be in angels, since choice is an appetite following prior deliberation, while deliberation is a kind of inquiry, as is said in Ethics III; but angels do not know by inquiring, because this pertains to the discursive process of reason. Therefore, it seems that there is no free choice in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0044","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0045","text":"Moreover, free choice is open to either alternative. But on the part of intellect there is nothing open to either alternative in angels, because their intellect is not deceived in natural intelligibles, as has been said. Therefore, neither on the part of appetite can free choice be in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0046","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0047","text":"Moreover, those things which are natural in angels belong to them according to more and less, because in the higher angels the intellectual nature is more perfect than in the lower. But free choice does not admit of more and less. Therefore, there is no free choice in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0048","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0049","text":"On the contrary, freedom of choice pertains to the dignity of man. But Angels are more dignified than men. Therefore, since freedom of choice is in men, it is much more in Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0050","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0051","text":"I answer that, it must be said that there are certain things which do not act from any choice, but as though acted upon and moved by others, just as an arrow is moved by the archer toward its end. But certain things act by a certain choice, though not a free one, as irrational animals do: for a sheep flees a wolf by a certain judgment, by which it judges him harmful to itself; but this judgment is not free to it, but implanted by nature. But only that which has intellect can act by free judgment, insofar as it knows the universal character of the good, from which it can judge this or that to be good. Hence, wherever there is intellect, there is free choice. And thus it is clear that free choice is in Angels even more excellently than in men, just as intellect is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0052","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher speaks of choice insofar as it belongs to man. But just as man's estimation in speculative matters differs from an Angel's estimation in this, that the one is without inquiry whereas the other is through inquiry, so also in practical matters. Hence there is choice in Angels; not, however, with the inquiring deliberation of counsel, but through a sudden apprehension of truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0054","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0055","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as has been said, cognition occurs through the known things being in the knower. But it pertains to the imperfection of something if that which is naturally apt to be in it is not in it. Hence an Angel would not be perfect in his nature if his intellect were not determined to every truth that he can naturally know. But the act of the appetitive power occurs through the affect being inclined toward an exterior thing. Yet the perfection of a thing does not depend on every thing toward which it is inclined, but only on what is higher. And therefore it does not pertain to the imperfection of an Angel if he does not have a will determined with respect to those things that are below him. But it would pertain to his imperfection if he were indeterminate with respect to that which is above him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0056","text":"[I q. 59 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0057","text":"To the third, it must be said that free choice is in the higher Angels in a nobler mode than in the lower, just as is the judgment of intellect. Nevertheless, it is true that freedom itself, insofar as a certain removal of compulsion is considered in it, does not admit of more and less, because privations and negations are not diminished or increased through themselves, but only through their cause or according to some accompanying affirmation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0058","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0059","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0060","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that in Angels there are the irascible and concupiscible powers. For Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that in demons there are irrational fury and insane concupiscence. But demons are of the same nature as Angels, because sin did not change their nature. Therefore, in Angels there are the irascible and concupiscible powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0061","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0062","text":"Moreover, love and joy are in the concupiscible power; but anger, hope, and fear are in the irascible power. But these are attributed in the Scriptures to good and evil Angels. Therefore, in Angels there are the irascible and concupiscible powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0063","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0064","text":"Moreover, certain virtues are said to be in the irascible and concupiscible powers; thus charity and temperance seem to be in the concupiscible power, whereas hope and fortitude are in the irascible power. But these virtues are in Angels. Therefore, in Angels there are the concupiscible and irascible powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0065","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0066","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in III *On the Soul*, that the irascible and concupiscible powers are in the sensitive part, which is not in Angels. Therefore, in them there are not the irascible and concupiscible powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0067","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0068","text":"I answer that, it must be said that the intellectual appetite is not divided into the irascible and concupiscible powers, but only the sensitive appetite is. The reason for this is that, since powers are distinguished not according to the material distinction of objects but only according to the formal character of the object, if an object corresponds to some power according to a common character, there will not be a distinction of powers according to the diversity of the particular objects contained under that common character. Thus, if the proper object of the visual power is color according to the character of color, several visual powers are not distinguished according to the difference between white and black. But if the proper object of some power were white insofar as it is white, the visual power of white would be distinguished from the visual power of black.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0069","text":"But it is clear from what has been said that the object of the intellectual appetite, which is called the will, is the good according to the common character of the good, and there can be no appetite except for the good. Hence, in the intellective part, appetite is not divided according to the distinction of certain particular goods, as the sensitive appetite is divided, which does not regard the good according to its common character but a certain particular good. Hence, since in Angels there is only intellectual appetite, their appetite is not distinguished by the irascible and concupiscible powers, but remains undivided; and it is called will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0070","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0071","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that fury and concupiscence are said to be in demons metaphorically, just as anger is sometimes attributed to God, on account of a similarity of effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0072","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0073","text":"To the second, it must be said that love and joy, insofar as they are passions, are in the concupiscible power; but insofar as they name a simple act of the will, they are in the intellectual part: inasmuch as to love is to will some good to someone, and to rejoice is for the will to rest in some possessed good. And universally, none of these is said of the angels according to passion, as Augustine says in City of God, IX.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0074","text":"[I q. 59 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0075","text":"To the third, it must be said that charity, insofar as it is a virtue, is not in the concupiscible power, but in the will. For the object of the concupiscible power is a good delightful according to sense; but the divine good, which is the object of charity, is not such a good. And for the same reason it must be said that hope is not in the irascible power, because the object of the irascible power is something arduous that is sensible, whereas the hope that is a virtue is not concerned with this, but with the divine arduous. Temperance, however, insofar as it is a human virtue, concerns concupiscences for sensible delights, which pertain to the concupiscible power. And similarly fortitude concerns boldnesses and fears that are in the irascible power. And therefore temperance, insofar as it is a human virtue, is in the concupiscible power, and fortitude in the irascible power. But in this way they are not in the angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0076","text":"For there are not in them passions of concupiscence, or of fear and boldness, which would need to be regulated through temperance and fortitude. But temperance is said to be in them insofar as they moderately dispose their will according to the rule of the divine will. And fortitude is said to be in them insofar as they firmly carry out the divine will. All of which takes place through the will, and not through the irascible and concupiscible powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_51:P0076"]}],"source_digest":"580ff0d2c346fd7f450fa8c92c253a5344d82283306e4a836120aeb8a3c67c23","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 59"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 59","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52","title":"First Part · Question 60","order":61,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_52","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 60 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30892)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de actu voluntatis, qui est amor sive dilectio, nam omnis actus appetitivae virtutis ex amore seu dilectione derivatur. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum in Angelis sit dilectio naturalis. Secundo, utrum in eis sit dilectio electiva. Tertio, utrum Angelus diligat seipsum dilectione naturali an electiva. Quarto, utrum unus Angelus diligat alium dilectione naturali sicut seipsum. Quinto, utrum Angelus naturali dilectione diligat Deum plus quam seipsum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30893)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit amor vel dilectio naturalis. Amor enim naturalis dividitur contra intellectualem; ut patet per Dionysium, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Sed amor Angeli est intellectualis. Ergo non est naturalis.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30894)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae amant amore naturali, magis aguntur quam agant, nihil enim habet dominium suae naturae. Sed Angeli non aguntur, sed agunt; cum sint liberi arbitrii, ut ostensum est. Ergo in Angelis non est amor seu dilectio naturalis.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30895)\n\nPraeterea, omnis dilectio aut est recta, aut non recta. Dilectio autem recta pertinet ad caritatem, dilectio autem non recta pertinet ad iniquitatem. Neutrum autem horum pertinet ad naturam, quia caritas est supra naturam, iniquitas autem est contra naturam. Ergo nulla dilectio naturalis est in Angelis.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30896)\n\nSed contra est quod dilectio sequitur cognitionem, nihil enim amatur nisi cognitum, ut Augustinus dicit, X de Trin. Sed in Angelis est cognitio naturalis. Ergo et dilectio naturalis.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30897)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est in Angelis ponere dilectionem naturalem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod semper prius salvatur in posteriori. Natura autem prior est quam intellectus, quia natura cuiuscumque rei est essentia eius. Unde id quod est naturae, oportet salvari etiam in habentibus intellectum. Est autem hoc commune omni naturae, ut habeat aliquam inclinationem, quae est appetitus naturalis vel amor. Quae tamen inclinatio diversimode invenitur in diversis naturis, in unaquaque secundum modum eius. Unde in natura intellectuali invenitur inclinatio naturalis secundum voluntatem; in natura autem sensitiva, secundum appetitum sensitivum, in natura vero carente cognitione, secundum solum ordinem naturae in aliquid. Unde cum Angelus sit natura intellectualis, oportet quod in voluntate eius sit naturalis dilectio.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30898)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectualis amor dividitur contra naturalem qui est solum naturalis, inquantum est naturae quae non addit supra rationem naturae perfectionem sensus aut intellectus.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30899)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod omnia quae sunt in toto mundo, aguntur ab aliquo, praeter primum agens, quod ita agit quod nullo modo ab alio agitur, in quo est idem natura et voluntas. Et ideo non est inconveniens si Angelus agatur, inquantum inclinatio naturalis est sibi indita ab auctore suae naturae. Non tamen sic agitur quod non agat; cum habeat liberam voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30900)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut cognitio naturalis semper est vera ita dilectio naturalis semper est recta, cum amor naturalis nihil aliud sit quam inclinatio naturae indita ab auctore naturae. Dicere ergo quod inclinatio naturalis non sit recta, est derogare auctori naturae. Alia tamen est rectitudo naturalis dilectionis, et alia est rectitudo caritatis et virtutis, quia una rectitudo est perfectiva alterius. Sicut etiam alia est veritas naturalis cognitionis; et alia est veritas cognitionis infusae vel acquisitae.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 60 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30901)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit dilectio electiva. Dilectio enim electiva videtur esse amor rationalis, cum electio sequatur consilium, quod in inquisitione consistit, ut dicitur in III Ethic. Sed rationalis amor dividitur contra intellectualem (qui est proprius Angelorum); ut dicitur IV cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo in Angelis non est dilectio electiva.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30902)\n\nPraeterea, in Angelis non est nisi cognitio naturalis, praeter cognitionem infusam, quia non discurrunt de principiis ad acquirendum conclusiones. Et sic ad omnia quae naturaliter cognoscere possunt, sic se habent sicut intellectus noster ad prima principia quae naturaliter cognoscere potest. Sed dilectio sequitur cognitionem, ut dictum est. Ergo in Angelis, praeter dilectionem gratuitam, non est nisi dilectio naturalis. Non ergo electiva.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30903)\n\nSed contra, naturalibus neque meremur neque demeremur. Sed Angeli sua dilectione aliqua merentur, vel demerentur. Ergo in eis est aliqua dilectio electiva.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30904)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in Angelis est quaedam dilectio naturalis et quaedam electiva. Et naturalis dilectio in eis est principium electivae, quia semper id quod pertinet ad prius, habet rationem principii; unde, cum natura sit primum quod est in unoquoque, oportet quod id quod ad naturam pertinet, sit principium in quolibet. Et hoc apparet in homine et quantum ad intellectum, et quantum ad voluntatem. Intellectus enim cognoscit principia naturaliter, et ex hac cognitione causatur in homine scientia conclusionum, quae non cognoscuntur naturaliter ab homine, sed per inventionem vel doctrinam. Similiter in voluntate finis hoc modo se habet, sicut principium in intellectu, ut dicitur in II Physic. Unde voluntas naturaliter tendit in suum finem ultimum, omnis enim homo naturaliter vult beatitudinem. Et ex hac naturali voluntate causantur omnes aliae voluntates, cum quidquid homo vult, velit propter finem. Dilectio igitur boni quod homo naturaliter vult sicut finem, est dilectio naturalis, dilectio autem ab hac derivata, quae est boni quod diligitur propter finem, est dilectio electiva. Hoc tamen differenter se habet ex parte intellectus, et voluntatis. Quia, sicut supra dictum est, cognitio intellectus fit secundum quod res cognitae sunt in cognoscente. Est autem ex imperfectione intellectualis naturae in homine, quod non statim eius intellectus naturaliter habet omnia intelligibilia, sed quaedam, a quibus in alia quodammodo movetur. Sed actus appetitivae virtutis est, e converso, secundum ordinem appetentis ad res. Quarum quaedam sunt secundum se bona, et ideo secundum se appetibilia, quaedam vero habent rationem bonitatis ex ordine ad aliud, et sunt appetibilia propter aliud. Unde non est ex imperfectione appetentis, quod aliquid appetat naturaliter ut finem, et aliquid per electionem, ut ordinatur in finem. Quia igitur natura intellectualis in Angelis perfecta est, invenitur in eis sola cognitio naturalis, non autem ratiocinativa, sed invenitur in eis dilectio et naturalis et electiva. Haec autem dicta sunt, praetermissis his quae supra naturam sunt, horum enim natura non est principium sufficiens. De his autem infra dicetur.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30905)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod non omnis dilectio electiva est amor rationalis, secundum quod rationalis amor dividitur contra intellectualem. Dicitur enim sic amor rationalis, qui sequitur cognitionem ratiocinativam, non omnis autem electio consequitur discursum rationis, ut supra dictum est, cum de libero arbitrio ageretur; sed solum electio hominis. Unde ratio non sequitur.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30906)\n\nAd secundum patet responsio ex dictis.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30907)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non diligat seipsum dilectione naturali et electiva. Dilectio enim naturalis est ipsius finis, sicut dictum est; dilectio autem electiva, eorum quae sunt ad finem. Sed idem non potest esse finis et ad finem, respectu eiusdem. Ergo non potest esse eiusdem dilectio naturalis et electiva.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30908)\n\nPraeterea, amor est virtus unitiva et concretiva, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Sed unitio et concretio est diversorum in unum reductorum. Ergo non potest Angelus diligere seipsum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30909)\n\nPraeterea, dilectio est quidam motus. Sed omnis motus in alterum tendit. Ergo videtur quod Angelus non possit amare seipsum dilectione naturali, nec electiva.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30910)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, IX Ethic., quod *amicabilia quae sunt ad alterum, veniunt ex amicabilibus quae sunt ad seipsum*.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30911)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum amor sit boni, bonum autem sit et in substantia et in accidente, ut patet I Ethic., dupliciter aliquid amatur, uno modo, ut bonum subsistens; alio modo, ut bonum accidentale sive inhaerens. Illud quidem amatur ut bonum subsistens, quod sic amatur ut ei aliquis velit bonum. Ut bonum vero accidentale seu inhaerens amatur id quod desideratur alteri, sicut amatur scientia, non ut ipsa sit bona, sed ut habeatur. Et hunc modum amoris quidam nominaverunt concupiscentiam, primum vero amicitiam. Manifestum est autem quod in rebus cognitione carentibus, unumquodque naturaliter appetit consequi id quod est sibi bonum; sicut ignis locum sursum. Unde et Angelus et homo naturaliter appetunt suum bonum et suam perfectionem. Et hoc est amare seipsum. Unde naturaliter tam Angelus quam homo diligit seipsum, inquantum aliquod bonum naturali appetitu sibi desiderat. Inquantum vero sibi desiderat aliquod bonum per electionem, intantum amat seipsum dilectione electiva.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30912)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Angelus aut homo non diligit se dilectione naturali et electiva secundum idem; sed secundum diversa, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30913)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut plus est esse unum quam uniri, ita amor magis est unus ad seipsum, quam ad diversa quae ei uniuntur. Sed ideo Dionysius usus fuit nomine unitionis et concretionis, ut ostenderet derivationem amoris a se in alia, sicut ab uno derivatur unitio.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30914)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut amor est actio manens in agente, ita est motus manens in amante, non autem tendens in aliquid aliud ex necessitate; sed potest reflecti super amantem, ut amet seipsum, sicut et cognitio reflectitur in cognoscentem, ut cognoscat seipsum.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30915)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus non diligat naturali dilectione alium sicut seipsum. Dilectio enim sequitur cognitionem. Sed unus Angelus non cognoscit alium sicut seipsum, quia seipsum cognoscit per suam essentiam, alium vero per eius similitudinem, ut supra dictum est. Ergo videtur quod unus Angelus non diligat alium sicut seipsum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30916)\n\nPraeterea, causa est potior causato, et principium eo quod ex principio derivatur. Sed dilectio quae est ad alium, derivatur ab ea quae est ad seipsum; sicut dicit philosophus, IX Ethic. Ergo Angelus non diligit alium sicut seipsum, sed seipsum magis.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30917)\n\nPraeterea, dilectio naturalis est alicuius tanquam finis; et non potest removeri. Sed unus Angelus non est finis alterius; et iterum haec dilectio potest removeri, ut patet in Daemonibus, qui non diligunt bonos Angelos. Ergo unus Angelus non diligit alium naturali dilectione sicut seipsum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30918)\n\nSed contra est, quia illud quod invenitur in omnibus, etiam ratione carentibus, videtur esse naturale. Sed sicut dicitur Eccli. XIII, omne animal diligit sibi simile. Ergo Angelus diligit naturaliter alium sicut seipsum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30919)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, Angelus et homo naturaliter seipsum diligit. Illud autem quod est unum cum aliquo, est ipsummet, unde unumquodque diligit id quod est unum sibi. Et si quidem sit unum sibi unione naturali, diligit illud dilectione naturali, si vero sit unum secum unione non naturali, diligit ipsum dilectione non naturali. Sicut homo diligit civem suum dilectione politicae virtutis; consanguineum autem suum dilectione naturali, inquantum est unum cum eo in principio generationis naturalis. Manifestum est autem quod id quod est unum cum aliquo, genere vel specie, est unum per naturam. Et ideo dilectione naturali quaelibet res diligit id quod est secum unum secundum speciem, inquantum diligit speciem suam. Et hoc etiam apparet in his quae cognitione carent, nam ignis naturalem inclinationem habet ut communicet alteri suam formam, quod est bonum eius; sicut naturaliter inclinatur ad hoc quod quaerat bonum suum, ut esse sursum. Sic ergo dicendum est quod unus Angelus diligit alium naturali dilectione, inquantum convenit cum eo in natura. Sed inquantum convenit cum eo in aliquibus aliis vel etiam inquantum differt ab eo secundum quaedam alia, non diligit eum naturali dilectione.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30920)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc quod dico sicut seipsum, potest uno modo determinare cognitionem seu dilectionem ex parte cogniti et dilecti. Et sic cognoscit alium sicut seipsum, quia cognoscit alium esse, sicut cognoscit seipsum esse. Alio modo potest determinare cognitionem et dilectionem ex parte diligentis et cognoscentis. Et sic non cognoscit alium sicut seipsum, quia se cognoscit per suam essentiam, alium autem non per eius essentiam. Et similiter non diligit alium sicut seipsum, quia seipsum diligit per suam voluntatem, alium autem non diligit per eius voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30921)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ly sicut non designat aequalitatem, sed similitudinem. Cum enim dilectio naturalis super unitatem naturalem fundetur, illud quod est minus unum cum eo, naturaliter minus diligit. Unde naturaliter plus diligit quod est unum numero, quam quod est unum specie vel genere. Sed naturale est quod similem dilectionem habeat ad alium sicut ad seipsum, quantum ad hoc, quod sicut seipsum diligit inquantum vult sibi bonum, ita alium diligat inquantum vult eius bonum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30922)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod dilectio naturalis dicitur esse ipsius finis, non tanquam cui aliquis velit bonum; sed tanquam bonum quod quis vult sibi, et per consequens alii, inquantum est unum sibi. Nec ista dilectio naturalis removeri potest etiam ab Angelis malis, quin dilectionem naturalem habeant ad alios Angelos, inquantum cum eis communicant in natura. Sed odiunt eos, inquantum diversificantur secundum iustitiam et iniustitiam.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30923)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus naturali dilectione non diligat Deum plus quam seipsum. Quia, ut dictum est, dilectio naturalis fundatur super unione naturali. Sed natura divina maxime distat a natura Angeli. Ergo naturali dilectione Angelus minus diligit Deum quam se, vel etiam alium Angelum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30924)\n\nPraeterea, propter quod unumquodque, et illud magis. Sed naturali dilectione quilibet diligit alium propter se, unumquodque enim diligit aliquid inquantum est bonum sibi. Ergo dilectione naturali Angelus non diligit Deum plus quam seipsum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30925)\n\nPraeterea, natura reflectitur in seipsam, videmus enim quod omne agens naturaliter agit ad conservationem sui. Non autem reflecteretur in seipsam natura, si tenderet in aliud plus quam in seipsam. Non ergo naturali dilectione diligit Angelus Deum plus quam se.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30926)\n\nPraeterea, hoc videtur esse proprium caritatis, ut aliquis Deum plus quam seipsum diligat. Sed dilectio caritatis non est naturalis in Angelis, sed diffunditur in cordibus eorum per spiritum sanctum, qui datus est eis, ut dicit Augustinus, XII de Civ. Dei. Ergo non diligunt Deum Angeli dilectione naturali plus quam seipsos.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30927)\n\nPraeterea, dilectio naturalis semper manet, manente natura. Sed diligere Deum plus quam seipsum non manet in peccante Angelo vel homine, quia, ut Augustinus dicit, XIV de Civ. Dei, *fecerunt civitates duas amores duo, terrenam scilicet amor sui usque ad Dei contemptum, caelestem vero amor Dei usque ad contemptum sui*. Ergo diligere Deum supra seipsum non est naturale.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30928)\n\nSed contra, omnia moralia legis praecepta sunt de lege naturae. Sed praeceptum de diligendo Deum supra seipsum, est praeceptum morale legis. Ergo est de lege naturae. Ergo dilectione naturali Angelus diligit Deum supra seipsum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30929)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod Angelus naturali dilectione diligit Deum plus quam se, amore concupiscentiae, quia scilicet plus appetit sibi bonum divinum quam bonum suum. Et quodammodo amore amicitiae, inquantum scilicet Deo vult naturaliter Angelus maius bonum quam sibi, vult enim naturaliter Deum esse Deum, se autem vult habere naturam propriam. Sed simpliciter loquendo, naturali dilectione plus diligit se quam Deum, quia intensius et principalius naturaliter diligit se quam Deum. Sed falsitas huius opinionis manifeste apparet, si quis in rebus naturalibus consideret ad quid res naturaliter moveatur, inclinatio enim naturalis in his quae sunt sine ratione, demonstrat inclinationem naturalem in voluntate intellectualis naturae. Unumquodque autem in rebus naturalibus, quod secundum naturam hoc ipsum quod est, alterius est, principalius et magis inclinatur in id cuius est, quam in seipsum. Et haec inclinatio naturalis demonstratur ex his quae naturaliter aguntur, quia *unumquodque, sicut agitur naturaliter, sic aptum natum est agi*, ut dicitur in II Physic. Videmus enim quod naturaliter pars se exponit, ad conservationem totius, sicut manus exponitur ictui, absque deliberatione, ad conservationem totius corporis. Et quia ratio imitatur naturam, huiusmodi inclinationem invenimus in virtutibus politicis, est enim virtuosi civis, ut se exponat mortis periculo pro totius reipublicae conservatione; et si homo esset naturalis pars huius civitatis, haec inclinatio esset ei naturalis. Quia igitur bonum universale est ipse Deus, et sub hoc bono continetur etiam Angelus et homo et omnis creatura, quia omnis creatura naturaliter, secundum id quod est, Dei est; sequitur quod naturali dilectione etiam Angelus et homo plus et principalius diligat Deum quam seipsum. Alioquin, si naturaliter plus seipsum diligeret quam Deum, sequeretur quod naturalis dilectio esset perversa; et quod non perficeretur per caritatem, sed destrueretur.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30930)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit in his quae ex aequo dividuntur, quorum unum non est alteri ratio existendi et bonitatis, in talibus enim unumquodque diligit naturaliter magis seipsum quam alterum, inquantum est magis sibi ipsi unum quam alteri. Sed in illis quorum unum est tota ratio existendi et bonitatis alii, magis diligitur naturaliter tale alterum quam ipsum; sicut dictum est quod unaquaeque pars diligit naturaliter totum plus quam se. Et quodlibet singulare naturaliter diligit plus bonum suae speciei, quam bonum suum singulare. Deus autem non solum est bonum unius speciei, sed est ipsum universale bonum simpliciter. Unde unumquodque suo modo naturaliter diligit Deum plus quam seipsum.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30931)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur quod Deus diligitur ab Angelo inquantum est ei bonus, si ly inquantum dicat finem, sic falsum est, non enim diligit naturaliter Deum propter bonum suum, sed propter ipsum Deum. Si vero dicat rationem amoris ex parte amantis, sic verum est, non enim esset in natura alicuius quod amaret Deum, nisi ex eo quod unumquodque dependet a bono quod est Deus.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30932)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod natura reflectitur in seipsam non solum quantum ad id quod est ei singulare, sed multo magis quantum ad commune, inclinatur enim unumquodque ad conservandum non solum suum individuum, sed etiam suam speciem. Et multo magis habet naturalem inclinationem unumquodque in id quod est bonum universale simpliciter.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30933)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Deus, secundum quod est universale bonum, a quo dependet omne bonum naturale, diligitur naturali dilectione ab unoquoque. Inquantum vero est bonum beatificans naturaliter omnes supernaturali beatitudine, sic diligitur dilectione caritatis.\n\n[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30934)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, cum in Deo sit unum et idem eius substantia et bonum commune, omnes qui vident ipsam Dei essentiam, eodem motu dilectionis moventur in ipsam Dei essentiam prout est ab aliis distincta, et secundum quod est quoddam bonum commune. Et quia inquantum est bonum commune, naturaliter amatur ab omnibus; quicumque videt eum per essentiam, impossibile est quin diligat ipsum. Sed illi qui non vident essentiam eius, cognoscunt eum per aliquos particulares effectus, qui interdum eorum voluntati contrariantur. Et sic hoc modo dicuntur odio habere Deum, cum tamen, inquantum est bonum commune omnium, unumquodque naturaliter diligat plus Deum quam seipsum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0002","text":"[I q. 60 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de actu voluntatis, qui est amor sive dilectio, nam omnis actus appetitivae virtutis ex amore seu dilectione derivatur. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum in Angelis sit dilectio naturalis. Secundo, utrum in eis sit dilectio electiva. Tertio, utrum Angelus diligat seipsum dilectione naturali an electiva. Quarto, utrum unus Angelus diligat alium dilectione naturali sicut seipsum. Quinto, utrum Angelus naturali dilectione diligat Deum plus quam seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0005","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit amor vel dilectio naturalis. Amor enim naturalis dividitur contra intellectualem; ut patet per Dionysium, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Sed amor Angeli est intellectualis. Ergo non est naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0007","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0008","text":"Praeterea, ea quae amant amore naturali, magis aguntur quam agant, nihil enim habet dominium suae naturae. Sed Angeli non aguntur, sed agunt; cum sint liberi arbitrii, ut ostensum est. Ergo in Angelis non est amor seu dilectio naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0009","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0010","text":"Praeterea, omnis dilectio aut est recta, aut non recta. Dilectio autem recta pertinet ad caritatem, dilectio autem non recta pertinet ad iniquitatem. Neutrum autem horum pertinet ad naturam, quia caritas est supra naturam, iniquitas autem est contra naturam. Ergo nulla dilectio naturalis est in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0011","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dilectio sequitur cognitionem, nihil enim amatur nisi cognitum, ut Augustinus dicit, X de Trin. Sed in Angelis est cognitio naturalis. Ergo et dilectio naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0013","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est in Angelis ponere dilectionem naturalem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod semper prius salvatur in posteriori. Natura autem prior est quam intellectus, quia natura cuiuscumque rei est essentia eius. Unde id quod est naturae, oportet salvari etiam in habentibus intellectum. Est autem hoc commune omni naturae, ut habeat aliquam inclinationem, quae est appetitus naturalis vel amor. Quae tamen inclinatio diversimode invenitur in diversis naturis, in unaquaque secundum modum eius. Unde in natura intellectuali invenitur inclinatio naturalis secundum voluntatem; in natura autem sensitiva, secundum appetitum sensitivum, in natura vero carente cognitione, secundum solum ordinem naturae in aliquid. Unde cum Angelus sit natura intellectualis, oportet quod in voluntate eius sit naturalis dilectio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0015","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectualis amor dividitur contra naturalem qui est solum naturalis, inquantum est naturae quae non addit supra rationem naturae perfectionem sensus aut intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0017","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod omnia quae sunt in toto mundo, aguntur ab aliquo, praeter primum agens, quod ita agit quod nullo modo ab alio agitur, in quo est idem natura et voluntas. Et ideo non est inconveniens si Angelus agatur, inquantum inclinatio naturalis est sibi indita ab auctore suae naturae. Non tamen sic agitur quod non agat; cum habeat liberam voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0019","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0020","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut cognitio naturalis semper est vera ita dilectio naturalis semper est recta, cum amor naturalis nihil aliud sit quam inclinatio naturae indita ab auctore naturae. Dicere ergo quod inclinatio naturalis non sit recta, est derogare auctori naturae. Alia tamen est rectitudo naturalis dilectionis, et alia est rectitudo caritatis et virtutis, quia una rectitudo est perfectiva alterius. Sicut etiam alia est veritas naturalis cognitionis; et alia est veritas cognitionis infusae vel acquisitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0022","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non sit dilectio electiva. Dilectio enim electiva videtur esse amor rationalis, cum electio sequatur consilium, quod in inquisitione consistit, ut dicitur in III Ethic. Sed rationalis amor dividitur contra intellectualem (qui est proprius Angelorum); ut dicitur IV cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo in Angelis non est dilectio electiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0024","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0025","text":"Praeterea, in Angelis non est nisi cognitio naturalis, praeter cognitionem infusam, quia non discurrunt de principiis ad acquirendum conclusiones. Et sic ad omnia quae naturaliter cognoscere possunt, sic se habent sicut intellectus noster ad prima principia quae naturaliter cognoscere potest. Sed dilectio sequitur cognitionem, ut dictum est. Ergo in Angelis, praeter dilectionem gratuitam, non est nisi dilectio naturalis. Non ergo electiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0026","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0027","text":"Sed contra, naturalibus neque meremur neque demeremur. Sed Angeli sua dilectione aliqua merentur, vel demerentur. Ergo in eis est aliqua dilectio electiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0028","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0029","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in Angelis est quaedam dilectio naturalis et quaedam electiva. Et naturalis dilectio in eis est principium electivae, quia semper id quod pertinet ad prius, habet rationem principii; unde, cum natura sit primum quod est in unoquoque, oportet quod id quod ad naturam pertinet, sit principium in quolibet. Et hoc apparet in homine et quantum ad intellectum, et quantum ad voluntatem. Intellectus enim cognoscit principia naturaliter, et ex hac cognitione causatur in homine scientia conclusionum, quae non cognoscuntur naturaliter ab homine, sed per inventionem vel doctrinam. Similiter in voluntate finis hoc modo se habet, sicut principium in intellectu, ut dicitur in II Physic. Unde voluntas naturaliter tendit in suum finem ultimum, omnis enim homo naturaliter vult beatitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0030","text":"Et ex hac naturali voluntate causantur omnes aliae voluntates, cum quidquid homo vult, velit propter finem. Dilectio igitur boni quod homo naturaliter vult sicut finem, est dilectio naturalis, dilectio autem ab hac derivata, quae est boni quod diligitur propter finem, est dilectio electiva. Hoc tamen differenter se habet ex parte intellectus, et voluntatis. Quia, sicut supra dictum est, cognitio intellectus fit secundum quod res cognitae sunt in cognoscente. Est autem ex imperfectione intellectualis naturae in homine, quod non statim eius intellectus naturaliter habet omnia intelligibilia, sed quaedam, a quibus in alia quodammodo movetur. Sed actus appetitivae virtutis est, e converso, secundum ordinem appetentis ad res. Quarum quaedam sunt secundum se bona, et ideo secundum se appetibilia, quaedam vero habent rationem bonitatis ex ordine ad aliud, et sunt appetibilia propter aliud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0031","text":"Unde non est ex imperfectione appetentis, quod aliquid appetat naturaliter ut finem, et aliquid per electionem, ut ordinatur in finem. Quia igitur natura intellectualis in Angelis perfecta est, invenitur in eis sola cognitio naturalis, non autem ratiocinativa, sed invenitur in eis dilectio et naturalis et electiva. Haec autem dicta sunt, praetermissis his quae supra naturam sunt, horum enim natura non est principium sufficiens. De his autem infra dicetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0032","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0033","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod non omnis dilectio electiva est amor rationalis, secundum quod rationalis amor dividitur contra intellectualem. Dicitur enim sic amor rationalis, qui sequitur cognitionem ratiocinativam, non omnis autem electio consequitur discursum rationis, ut supra dictum est, cum de libero arbitrio ageretur; sed solum electio hominis. Unde ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0034","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0035","text":"Ad secundum patet responsio ex dictis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0036","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0037","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0038","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non diligat seipsum dilectione naturali et electiva. Dilectio enim naturalis est ipsius finis, sicut dictum est; dilectio autem electiva, eorum quae sunt ad finem. Sed idem non potest esse finis et ad finem, respectu eiusdem. Ergo non potest esse eiusdem dilectio naturalis et electiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0039","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0040","text":"Praeterea, amor est virtus unitiva et concretiva, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Sed unitio et concretio est diversorum in unum reductorum. Ergo non potest Angelus diligere seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0041","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0042","text":"Praeterea, dilectio est quidam motus. Sed omnis motus in alterum tendit. Ergo videtur quod Angelus non possit amare seipsum dilectione naturali, nec electiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0043","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0044","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, IX Ethic., quod *amicabilia quae sunt ad alterum, veniunt ex amicabilibus quae sunt ad seipsum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0045","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0046","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum amor sit boni, bonum autem sit et in substantia et in accidente, ut patet I Ethic., dupliciter aliquid amatur, uno modo, ut bonum subsistens; alio modo, ut bonum accidentale sive inhaerens. Illud quidem amatur ut bonum subsistens, quod sic amatur ut ei aliquis velit bonum. Ut bonum vero accidentale seu inhaerens amatur id quod desideratur alteri, sicut amatur scientia, non ut ipsa sit bona, sed ut habeatur. Et hunc modum amoris quidam nominaverunt concupiscentiam, primum vero amicitiam. Manifestum est autem quod in rebus cognitione carentibus, unumquodque naturaliter appetit consequi id quod est sibi bonum; sicut ignis locum sursum. Unde et Angelus et homo naturaliter appetunt suum bonum et suam perfectionem. Et hoc est amare seipsum. Unde naturaliter tam Angelus quam homo diligit seipsum, inquantum aliquod bonum naturali appetitu sibi desiderat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0047","text":"Inquantum vero sibi desiderat aliquod bonum per electionem, intantum amat seipsum dilectione electiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0048","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0049","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Angelus aut homo non diligit se dilectione naturali et electiva secundum idem; sed secundum diversa, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0050","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0051","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut plus est esse unum quam uniri, ita amor magis est unus ad seipsum, quam ad diversa quae ei uniuntur. Sed ideo Dionysius usus fuit nomine unitionis et concretionis, ut ostenderet derivationem amoris a se in alia, sicut ab uno derivatur unitio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0052","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0053","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut amor est actio manens in agente, ita est motus manens in amante, non autem tendens in aliquid aliud ex necessitate; sed potest reflecti super amantem, ut amet seipsum, sicut et cognitio reflectitur in cognoscentem, ut cognoscat seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0054","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0055","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0056","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus non diligat naturali dilectione alium sicut seipsum. Dilectio enim sequitur cognitionem. Sed unus Angelus non cognoscit alium sicut seipsum, quia seipsum cognoscit per suam essentiam, alium vero per eius similitudinem, ut supra dictum est. Ergo videtur quod unus Angelus non diligat alium sicut seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0057","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0058","text":"Praeterea, causa est potior causato, et principium eo quod ex principio derivatur. Sed dilectio quae est ad alium, derivatur ab ea quae est ad seipsum; sicut dicit philosophus, IX Ethic. Ergo Angelus non diligit alium sicut seipsum, sed seipsum magis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0059","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0060","text":"Praeterea, dilectio naturalis est alicuius tanquam finis; et non potest removeri. Sed unus Angelus non est finis alterius; et iterum haec dilectio potest removeri, ut patet in Daemonibus, qui non diligunt bonos Angelos. Ergo unus Angelus non diligit alium naturali dilectione sicut seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0061","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0062","text":"Sed contra est, quia illud quod invenitur in omnibus, etiam ratione carentibus, videtur esse naturale. Sed sicut dicitur Eccli. XIII, omne animal diligit sibi simile. Ergo Angelus diligit naturaliter alium sicut seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0063","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0064","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, Angelus et homo naturaliter seipsum diligit. Illud autem quod est unum cum aliquo, est ipsummet, unde unumquodque diligit id quod est unum sibi. Et si quidem sit unum sibi unione naturali, diligit illud dilectione naturali, si vero sit unum secum unione non naturali, diligit ipsum dilectione non naturali. Sicut homo diligit civem suum dilectione politicae virtutis; consanguineum autem suum dilectione naturali, inquantum est unum cum eo in principio generationis naturalis. Manifestum est autem quod id quod est unum cum aliquo, genere vel specie, est unum per naturam. Et ideo dilectione naturali quaelibet res diligit id quod est secum unum secundum speciem, inquantum diligit speciem suam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0065","text":"Et hoc etiam apparet in his quae cognitione carent, nam ignis naturalem inclinationem habet ut communicet alteri suam formam, quod est bonum eius; sicut naturaliter inclinatur ad hoc quod quaerat bonum suum, ut esse sursum. Sic ergo dicendum est quod unus Angelus diligit alium naturali dilectione, inquantum convenit cum eo in natura. Sed inquantum convenit cum eo in aliquibus aliis vel etiam inquantum differt ab eo secundum quaedam alia, non diligit eum naturali dilectione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0066","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0067","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc quod dico sicut seipsum, potest uno modo determinare cognitionem seu dilectionem ex parte cogniti et dilecti. Et sic cognoscit alium sicut seipsum, quia cognoscit alium esse, sicut cognoscit seipsum esse. Alio modo potest determinare cognitionem et dilectionem ex parte diligentis et cognoscentis. Et sic non cognoscit alium sicut seipsum, quia se cognoscit per suam essentiam, alium autem non per eius essentiam. Et similiter non diligit alium sicut seipsum, quia seipsum diligit per suam voluntatem, alium autem non diligit per eius voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0068","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0069","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ly sicut non designat aequalitatem, sed similitudinem. Cum enim dilectio naturalis super unitatem naturalem fundetur, illud quod est minus unum cum eo, naturaliter minus diligit. Unde naturaliter plus diligit quod est unum numero, quam quod est unum specie vel genere. Sed naturale est quod similem dilectionem habeat ad alium sicut ad seipsum, quantum ad hoc, quod sicut seipsum diligit inquantum vult sibi bonum, ita alium diligat inquantum vult eius bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0070","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0071","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod dilectio naturalis dicitur esse ipsius finis, non tanquam cui aliquis velit bonum; sed tanquam bonum quod quis vult sibi, et per consequens alii, inquantum est unum sibi. Nec ista dilectio naturalis removeri potest etiam ab Angelis malis, quin dilectionem naturalem habeant ad alios Angelos, inquantum cum eis communicant in natura. Sed odiunt eos, inquantum diversificantur secundum iustitiam et iniustitiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0072","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0073","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0074","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus naturali dilectione non diligat Deum plus quam seipsum. Quia, ut dictum est, dilectio naturalis fundatur super unione naturali. Sed natura divina maxime distat a natura Angeli. Ergo naturali dilectione Angelus minus diligit Deum quam se, vel etiam alium Angelum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0075","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0076","text":"Praeterea, propter quod unumquodque, et illud magis. Sed naturali dilectione quilibet diligit alium propter se, unumquodque enim diligit aliquid inquantum est bonum sibi. Ergo dilectione naturali Angelus non diligit Deum plus quam seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0077","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0078","text":"Praeterea, natura reflectitur in seipsam, videmus enim quod omne agens naturaliter agit ad conservationem sui. Non autem reflecteretur in seipsam natura, si tenderet in aliud plus quam in seipsam. Non ergo naturali dilectione diligit Angelus Deum plus quam se.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0079","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0080","text":"Praeterea, hoc videtur esse proprium caritatis, ut aliquis Deum plus quam seipsum diligat. Sed dilectio caritatis non est naturalis in Angelis, sed diffunditur in cordibus eorum per spiritum sanctum, qui datus est eis, ut dicit Augustinus, XII de Civ. Dei. Ergo non diligunt Deum Angeli dilectione naturali plus quam seipsos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0081","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0082","text":"Praeterea, dilectio naturalis semper manet, manente natura. Sed diligere Deum plus quam seipsum non manet in peccante Angelo vel homine, quia, ut Augustinus dicit, XIV de Civ. Dei, *fecerunt civitates duas amores duo, terrenam scilicet amor sui usque ad Dei contemptum, caelestem vero amor Dei usque ad contemptum sui*. Ergo diligere Deum supra seipsum non est naturale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0083","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0084","text":"Sed contra, omnia moralia legis praecepta sunt de lege naturae. Sed praeceptum de diligendo Deum supra seipsum, est praeceptum morale legis. Ergo est de lege naturae. Ergo dilectione naturali Angelus diligit Deum supra seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0085","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0086","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod Angelus naturali dilectione diligit Deum plus quam se, amore concupiscentiae, quia scilicet plus appetit sibi bonum divinum quam bonum suum. Et quodammodo amore amicitiae, inquantum scilicet Deo vult naturaliter Angelus maius bonum quam sibi, vult enim naturaliter Deum esse Deum, se autem vult habere naturam propriam. Sed simpliciter loquendo, naturali dilectione plus diligit se quam Deum, quia intensius et principalius naturaliter diligit se quam Deum. Sed falsitas huius opinionis manifeste apparet, si quis in rebus naturalibus consideret ad quid res naturaliter moveatur, inclinatio enim naturalis in his quae sunt sine ratione, demonstrat inclinationem naturalem in voluntate intellectualis naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0087","text":"Unumquodque autem in rebus naturalibus, quod secundum naturam hoc ipsum quod est, alterius est, principalius et magis inclinatur in id cuius est, quam in seipsum. Et haec inclinatio naturalis demonstratur ex his quae naturaliter aguntur, quia *unumquodque, sicut agitur naturaliter, sic aptum natum est agi*, ut dicitur in II Physic. Videmus enim quod naturaliter pars se exponit, ad conservationem totius, sicut manus exponitur ictui, absque deliberatione, ad conservationem totius corporis. Et quia ratio imitatur naturam, huiusmodi inclinationem invenimus in virtutibus politicis, est enim virtuosi civis, ut se exponat mortis periculo pro totius reipublicae conservatione; et si homo esset naturalis pars huius civitatis, haec inclinatio esset ei naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0088","text":"Quia igitur bonum universale est ipse Deus, et sub hoc bono continetur etiam Angelus et homo et omnis creatura, quia omnis creatura naturaliter, secundum id quod est, Dei est; sequitur quod naturali dilectione etiam Angelus et homo plus et principalius diligat Deum quam seipsum. Alioquin, si naturaliter plus seipsum diligeret quam Deum, sequeretur quod naturalis dilectio esset perversa; et quod non perficeretur per caritatem, sed destrueretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0089","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0090","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit in his quae ex aequo dividuntur, quorum unum non est alteri ratio existendi et bonitatis, in talibus enim unumquodque diligit naturaliter magis seipsum quam alterum, inquantum est magis sibi ipsi unum quam alteri. Sed in illis quorum unum est tota ratio existendi et bonitatis alii, magis diligitur naturaliter tale alterum quam ipsum; sicut dictum est quod unaquaeque pars diligit naturaliter totum plus quam se. Et quodlibet singulare naturaliter diligit plus bonum suae speciei, quam bonum suum singulare. Deus autem non solum est bonum unius speciei, sed est ipsum universale bonum simpliciter. Unde unumquodque suo modo naturaliter diligit Deum plus quam seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0091","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0092","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur quod Deus diligitur ab Angelo inquantum est ei bonus, si ly inquantum dicat finem, sic falsum est, non enim diligit naturaliter Deum propter bonum suum, sed propter ipsum Deum. Si vero dicat rationem amoris ex parte amantis, sic verum est, non enim esset in natura alicuius quod amaret Deum, nisi ex eo quod unumquodque dependet a bono quod est Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0093","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0094","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod natura reflectitur in seipsam non solum quantum ad id quod est ei singulare, sed multo magis quantum ad commune, inclinatur enim unumquodque ad conservandum non solum suum individuum, sed etiam suam speciem. Et multo magis habet naturalem inclinationem unumquodque in id quod est bonum universale simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0095","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0096","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Deus, secundum quod est universale bonum, a quo dependet omne bonum naturale, diligitur naturali dilectione ab unoquoque. Inquantum vero est bonum beatificans naturaliter omnes supernaturali beatitudine, sic diligitur dilectione caritatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0097","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0098","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, cum in Deo sit unum et idem eius substantia et bonum commune, omnes qui vident ipsam Dei essentiam, eodem motu dilectionis moventur in ipsam Dei essentiam prout est ab aliis distincta, et secundum quod est quoddam bonum commune. Et quia inquantum est bonum commune, naturaliter amatur ab omnibus; quicumque videt eum per essentiam, impossibile est quin diligat ipsum. Sed illi qui non vident essentiam eius, cognoscunt eum per aliquos particulares effectus, qui interdum eorum voluntati contrariantur. Et sic hoc modo dicuntur odio habere Deum, cum tamen, inquantum est bonum commune omnium, unumquodque naturaliter diligat plus Deum quam seipsum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6ad08d6c2913479ffb935ab2600827713a415153ba897162625da9dc8d99e1af","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T144509590076Z_5b362327","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 60","source_sha256":"6ad08d6c2913479ffb935ab2600827713a415153ba897162625da9dc8d99e1af","sha256":"906712425a58950b87b19dd39ba0a7669ec17f60354acd51419203eaaab6f7fc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:45:09.590096+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0002","text":"[I q. 60 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the act of the will, which is love or dilection, for every act of the appetitive power derives from love or dilection. And concerning this, five questions are asked. First, whether there is natural dilection in angels. Second, whether there is elective dilection in them. Third, whether an angel loves himself with natural or elective dilection. Fourth, whether one angel loves another with natural dilection as himself. Fifth, whether an angel loves God with natural dilection more than himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0005","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that there is no natural love or dilection in angels. For natural love is divided against intellectual love, as is clear from Dionysius, chapter 4 of *On the Divine Names*. But the love of an angel is intellectual. Therefore it is not natural.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0007","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0008","text":"Further, those things which love by natural love are acted upon more than they act, for nothing has dominion over its own nature. But angels are not acted upon, but act, since they possess free choice, as has been shown. Therefore there is no natural love or dilection in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0009","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0010","text":"Further, every dilection is either right or not right. But right dilection belongs to charity, whereas not-right dilection belongs to iniquity. Yet neither of these belongs to nature, because charity is above nature, whereas iniquity is against nature. Therefore no natural dilection is in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0011","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, dilection follows cognition, for nothing is loved unless known, as Augustine says in book X of *On the Trinity*. But natural cognition is in angels. Therefore natural dilection is as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0013","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that natural dilection must be posited in angels. For the evidence of this, it should be considered that what is prior is always preserved in what is posterior. Now nature is prior to intellect, because the nature of each thing is its essence. Hence that which belongs to nature must be preserved even in those having intellect. But it is common to every nature that it have some inclination, which is natural appetite or love. Yet this inclination is found in diverse ways in diverse natures, in each according to its mode. Hence, in an intellectual nature natural inclination is found according to the will; but in a sensitive nature, according to the sensitive appetite; and in a nature lacking cognition, according to the order of nature alone toward something. Hence, since an angel is an intellectual nature, there must be natural dilection in his will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0015","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that intellectual love is divided against natural love that is merely natural, insofar as it belongs to a nature which does not add, over and above the character of nature, the perfection of sense or intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0017","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that all things which are in the whole world are acted upon by something, except the first agent, which acts in such a way that it is in no way acted upon by another, and in which nature and will are the same. And therefore it is not unfitting if an angel is acted upon, insofar as natural inclination is bestowed upon him by the author of his nature. Yet he is not so acted upon that he does not act, since he has free will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0019","text":"[I q. 60 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0020","text":"To the third, it must be said that, just as natural cognition is always true, so natural dilection is always right, since natural love is nothing other than an inclination of nature bestowed by the author of nature. Therefore, to say that natural inclination is not right is to derogate from the author of nature. Yet the rectitude of natural dilection is one thing, and the rectitude of charity and virtue another, because one rectitude perfects the other. Likewise, the truth of natural cognition is one thing, and the truth of infused or acquired cognition another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0022","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there is no elective dilection in angels. For elective dilection seems to be rational love, since choice follows counsel, which consists in inquiry, as is said in book III of the *Ethics*. But rational love is divided against intellectual love (which is proper to angels), as is said in chapter 4 of *On the Divine Names*. Therefore there is no elective dilection in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0024","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0025","text":"Moreover, in angels there is only natural cognition, apart from infused cognition, because they do not reason from principles in order to acquire conclusions. And thus, with respect to all the things that they can know naturally, they are related as our intellect is to the first principles that it can know naturally. But love follows cognition, as has been said. Therefore, in angels, apart from gratuitous love, there is only natural love. Therefore, not elective love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0026","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0027","text":"On the contrary, by natural things we neither merit nor demerit. But angels, by some love of theirs, merit or demerit. Therefore, there is in them some elective love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0028","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0029","text":"I answer that it must be said that in angels there is a certain natural love and a certain elective love. And natural love in them is the principle of elective love, because that which pertains to what is prior always has the character of a principle; hence, since nature is the first thing in each thing, that which pertains to nature must be a principle in every case. And this is apparent in man both with respect to the intellect and with respect to the will. For the intellect knows principles naturally, and from this cognition there is caused in man knowledge of conclusions, which are not naturally known by man, but through discovery or instruction. Similarly, the end is related in the will in the same way as a principle is in the intellect, as is said in Physics II. Hence the will naturally tends toward its ultimate end, for every man naturally wills happiness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0030","text":"And from this natural willing all other willings are caused, since whatever a man wills, he wills for the sake of an end. Therefore, love of the good which a man naturally wills as an end is natural love; but the love derived from this, which is of the good loved for the sake of an end, is elective love. Yet this stands differently on the part of the intellect and of the will. For, as was said above, cognition of the intellect takes place according as the things known are in the knower. Now it is owing to the imperfection of intellectual nature in man that his intellect does not immediately have naturally all intelligibles, but only certain ones, from which it is in some way moved to others. But the act of the appetitive power is, conversely, according to the ordering of the one who desires toward things. Of these, some are good in themselves, and therefore desirable in themselves; but others have the character of goodness from their ordering to another, and are desirable for the sake of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0031","text":"Hence it is not owing to an imperfection in the one who desires that he desires something naturally as an end and something by choice, as ordered to the end. Therefore, since intellectual nature in angels is perfect, there is found in them natural cognition alone, and not ratiocinative cognition; but there is found in them both natural and elective love. These things, however, have been said with omission of those things which are above nature, for nature is not a sufficient principle of these. But these will be discussed below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0032","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0033","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that not every elective love is rational love, according to the sense in which rational love is distinguished from intellectual love. For rational love is so called when it follows ratiocinative cognition; but not every choice follows a discursion of reason, as was said above when free choice was being treated; rather, only human choice does so. Hence the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0034","text":"[I q. 60 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0035","text":"The answer to the second is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0036","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0037","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0038","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that an angel does not love himself with natural and elective love. For natural love is of the end itself, as has been said; but elective love is of those things that are for the end. But the same thing cannot be both end and for the end, in respect of the same thing. Therefore, there cannot be natural and elective love of the same thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0039","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0040","text":"Moreover, love is a unitive and binding force, as Dionysius says in chapter IV of On the Divine Names. But union and binding are the reduction of diverse things into one. Therefore, an angel cannot love himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0041","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0042","text":"Moreover, love is a certain motion. But every motion tends toward another. Therefore, it seems that an angel cannot love himself with either natural or elective love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0043","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0044","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in Ethics IX, that *the friendly things that are directed toward another arise from the friendly things that are directed toward oneself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0045","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0046","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since love is of the good, and good exists both in substance and in accident, as is clear in Ethics I, something is loved in two ways: in one way, as a subsisting good; in another way, as an accidental or inhering good. That is loved as a subsisting good which is so loved that someone wills good to it. But that which is desired for another is loved as an accidental or inhering good, as knowledge is loved, not that it itself may be good, but that it may be possessed. And some have called this mode of love concupiscence, while they have called the first friendship. Now it is evident that in things lacking cognition, each thing naturally desires to attain what is good for itself, as fire desires a place above. Hence both angel and man naturally desire their own good and their own perfection. And this is to love oneself. Hence both angel and man naturally love themselves, insofar as each desires some good for himself by natural appetite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0047","text":"But insofar as he desires some good for himself by choice, to that extent he loves himself with elective love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0048","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0049","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an angel or a human being does not love himself by natural and elective love in the same respect, but in different respects, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0050","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0051","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as it is more to be one than to be united, so love is more one toward itself than toward diverse things that are united to it. But Dionysius used the term union and concretion in order to show the derivation of love from itself into other things, just as union is derived from unity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0052","text":"[I q. 60 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0053","text":"To the third, it must be said that, just as love is an action remaining in the agent, so it is a motion remaining in the lover, and not necessarily tending toward something else; rather, it can be reflected back upon the lover, so that he loves himself, just as cognition too is reflected upon the knower, so that he knows himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0054","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0055","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0056","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that one angel does not love another by natural love as himself. For love follows cognition. But one angel does not know another as himself, because he knows himself through his essence, but another through the latter's likeness, as was said above. Therefore it seems that one angel does not love another as himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0057","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0058","text":"Further, a cause is more powerful than its effect, and a principle than that which is derived from the principle. But the love that is toward another is derived from that which is toward oneself, as the Philosopher says in Ethics IX. Therefore an angel does not love another as himself, but loves himself more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0059","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0060","text":"Further, natural love is for something as an end, and cannot be removed. But one angel is not the end of another; and moreover this love can be removed, as is evident in the demons, who do not love the good angels. Therefore one angel does not love another by natural love as himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0061","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0062","text":"On the contrary, because that which is found in all things, even those lacking reason, seems to be natural. But, as is said in Ecclesiasticus XIII, every animal loves what is like itself. Therefore an angel naturally loves another as himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0063","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0064","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as has been stated, an angel and a human being naturally love themselves. Now that which is one with something is that very thing itself; hence each thing loves that which is one with it. And if it is one with it by a natural union, it loves it by natural love; but if it is one with it by a non-natural union, it loves it by non-natural love. Thus a human being loves his fellow citizen by the love of political virtue; but his blood relative by natural love, insofar as he is one with him in the principle of natural generation. Now it is evident that what is one with something in genus or species is one by nature. And therefore, by natural love every thing loves that which is one with it according to species, insofar as it loves its own species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0065","text":"And this is also evident in things that lack cognition, for fire has a natural inclination to communicate its form to another, which is its good; just as it is naturally inclined to seek its own good, namely, to be upward. Thus, therefore, it must be said that one angel loves another by natural love, insofar as he agrees with him in nature. But insofar as he agrees with him in certain other things, or even insofar as he differs from him in certain other respects, he does not love him by natural love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0066","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0067","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the phrase “as himself” can in one way qualify cognition or love on the part of the thing known and loved. And thus he knows another as himself, because he knows that another exists, just as he knows that he himself exists. In another way it can qualify cognition and love on the part of the one loving and knowing. And thus he does not know another as himself, because he knows himself through his own essence, but another not through the latter's essence. And similarly, he does not love another as himself, because he loves himself through his own will, but does not love another through the latter's will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0068","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0069","text":"To the second, it must be said that the word “as” does not indicate equality, but similarity. For since natural love is founded upon natural unity, that which is less one with it is naturally loved less. Hence it naturally loves more that which is one in number than that which is one in species or genus. But it is natural that it have a similar love for another as for itself with respect to this: that just as it loves itself insofar as it wills good to itself, so it loves another insofar as it wills that one's good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0070","text":"[I q. 60 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0071","text":"To the third, it must be said that natural love is said to be of the end, not as that to which someone wills good, but as the good that one wills for oneself and, consequently, for another, insofar as that other is one with oneself. Nor can this natural love be removed even from evil angels, so that they do not have natural love for other angels insofar as they share nature with them. But they hate them insofar as they differ according to justice and injustice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0072","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0073","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0074","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth [article]. It seems that an angel does not love God more than himself by natural love. For, as has been said, natural love is founded upon natural union. But the divine nature is at the greatest distance from the nature of an angel. Therefore, by natural love an angel loves God less than himself, or even less than another angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0075","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0076","text":"Further, that for the sake of which each thing is [loved], is loved more. But by natural love everyone loves another for his own sake; for each thing loves something insofar as it is good for itself. Therefore, by natural love an angel does not love God more than himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0077","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0078","text":"Further, nature reflects upon itself; for we see that every natural agent acts for its own preservation. But nature would not reflect upon itself if it tended toward another more than toward itself. Therefore, by natural love an angel does not love God more than himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0079","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0080","text":"Further, it seems proper to charity that someone love God more than himself. But the love of charity is not natural in angels, but is poured forth into their hearts through the Holy Spirit, who has been given to them, as Augustine says in XII *On the City of God*. Therefore, angels do not love God more than themselves by natural love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0081","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0082","text":"Further, natural love always remains so long as nature remains. But loving God more than oneself does not remain in a sinning angel or human being; because, as Augustine says in XIV *On the City of God*, *two loves made two cities: the earthly, namely love of self even to contempt of God; the heavenly, however, love of God even to contempt of self*. Therefore, loving God above oneself is not natural.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0083","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0084","text":"On the contrary, all the moral precepts of the law belong to the natural law. But the precept concerning loving God above oneself is a moral precept of the law. Therefore, it belongs to the natural law. Therefore, by natural love an angel loves God above himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0085","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0086","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have said that an angel loves God more than himself by natural love, with the love of concupiscence, namely because he desires the divine good for himself more than his own good. And in a certain way with the love of friendship, insofar as an angel naturally wills a greater good to God than to himself: for he naturally wills God to be God, but wills himself to have his own nature. But, speaking simply, by natural love he loves himself more than God, because he naturally loves himself more intensely and principally than God. But the falsity of this opinion clearly appears if one considers, in natural things, toward what a thing is naturally moved; for natural inclination in things without reason manifests natural inclination in the will of an intellectual nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0087","text":"Now every thing among natural things which, according to what it is, belongs to another, is more principally and more strongly inclined toward that to which it belongs than toward itself. And this natural inclination is shown from things that occur naturally, because *each thing, as it is naturally acted upon, so is it naturally fitted to be acted upon*, as is said in II *Physics*. For we see that a part naturally exposes itself for the preservation of the whole, just as a hand exposes itself to a blow, without deliberation, for the preservation of the whole body. And because reason imitates nature, we find such an inclination in the political virtues: for it belongs to the virtuous citizen to expose himself to the danger of death for the preservation of the whole commonwealth; and if a human being were a natural part of this city, this inclination would be natural to him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0088","text":"Therefore, since the universal good is God himself, and under this good are contained also angel and human being and every creature, because every creature naturally, according to what it is, belongs to God; it follows that by natural love both angel and human being love God more and more principally than themselves. Otherwise, if one naturally loved oneself more than God, it would follow that natural love was perverse, and that it would not be perfected by charity but destroyed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0089","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0090","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that argument proceeds concerning things which are divided equally, of which one is not to the other the reason of existence and goodness. For in such things each naturally loves itself more than another, insofar as it is more one with itself than with another. But among those things of which one is the entire reason of the other's existence and goodness, such another is naturally loved more than itself; as was said, each part naturally loves the whole more than itself. And every singular thing naturally loves the good of its species more than its own singular good. But God is not only the good of one species, but is simply the universal good itself. Hence each thing, in its own way, naturally loves God more than itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0091","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0092","text":"To the second it must be said that, when it is said that God is loved by an angel insofar as He is good to him, if the particle *insofar as* indicates an end, then this is false; for he does not naturally love God for the sake of his own good, but for the sake of God himself. But if it indicates the reason for love on the part of the lover, then this is true; for it would not be in the nature of anything to love God except from the fact that each thing depends upon the good that is God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0093","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0094","text":"To the third it must be said that nature reflects upon itself not only with respect to what is singular to it, but much more with respect to what is common; for each thing is inclined to preserve not only its own individual, but also its species. And still more does each thing have a natural inclination toward that which is simply the universal good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0095","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0096","text":"To the fourth it must be said that God, insofar as He is the universal good upon which every natural good depends, is loved by each thing with natural love. But insofar as He is the good beatifying all naturally with supernatural beatitude, He is loved with the love of charity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0097","text":"[I q. 60 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0098","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, since in God his substance and the common good are one and the same, all who see the very essence of God are moved by the same movement of love toward the essence of God itself, insofar as it is distinct from others, and insofar as it is a certain common good. And because, insofar as it is a common good, it is naturally loved by all, whoever sees him through his essence cannot but love him. But those who do not see his essence know him through certain particular effects, which sometimes oppose their will. And thus in this way they are said to hate God, although, insofar as he is the common good of all, each thing naturally loves God more than itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_52:P0098"]}],"source_digest":"495e28736b4d259976271eab59bbd50f3ea7ab6c6ef231ee1b7c93d2643ffa1f","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 60"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 60","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53","title":"First Part · Question 61","order":62,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_53","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 61 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30935)\n\nPost ea quae praemissa sunt de natura Angelorum, et cognitione et voluntate eorum, restat considerandum de eorum creatione, sive universaliter de eorum exordio. Et haec consideratio est tripartita. Nam primo considerabimus quomodo producti sunt in esse naturae; secundo, quomodo perfecti sunt in gratia vel gloria; tertio, quomodo aliqui ex eis facti sunt mali. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Angelus habeat causam sui esse. Secundo, utrum Angelus sit ab aeterno. Tertio, utrum Angelus sit creatus ante corporalem creaturam. Quarto, utrum Angeli fuerint creati in caelo Empyreo.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30936)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non habeant causam sui esse. De his enim quae sunt a Deo creata, agitur Genesis I. Sed nulla mentio fit ibi de Angelis. Ergo Angeli non sunt creati a Deo.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30937)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in VIII Metaphys., quod si aliqua substantia sit forma sine materia, *statim per seipsam est ens et unum, et non habet causam quae faciat eam ens et unum*. Sed Angeli sunt formae immateriales, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo non habent causam sui esse.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30938)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod fit ab aliquo agente, per hoc quod fit, accipit formam ab eo. Sed Angeli, cum sint formae, non accipiunt formam ab aliquo agente. Ergo Angeli non habent causam agentem.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30939)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CXLVIII, *laudate eum, omnes Angeli eius*. Et postea subdit, *quoniam ipse dixit, et facta sunt*.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30940)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere et Angelos, et omne id quod praeter Deum est, a Deo factum esse. Solus enim Deus est suum esse, in omnibus autem aliis differt essentia rei et esse eius, ut ex superioribus patet. Et ex hoc manifestum est quod solus Deus est ens per suam essentiam, omnia vero alia sunt entia per participationem. Omne autem quod est per participationem causatur ab eo quod est per essentiam, sicut omne ignitum causatur ab igne. Unde necesse est Angelos a Deo creatos esse.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30941)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus dicit, in XI de Civ. Dei, quod Angeli non sunt praetermissi in illa prima rerum creatione, sed significantur nomine caeli, aut etiam lucis. Ideo autem vel praetermissi sunt, vel nominibus rerum corporalium significati, quia Moyses rudi populo loquebatur, qui nondum capere poterat incorpoream naturam; et si eis fuisset expressum aliquas res esse super omnem naturam corpoream, fuisset eis occasio idololatriae, ad quam proni erant, et a qua Moyses eos praecipue revocare intendebat.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30942)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod substantiae quae sunt formae subsistentes, non habent causam aliquam formalem sui esse et suae unitatis, nec causam agentem per transmutationem materiae de potentia in actum, sed habent causam producentem totam substantiam.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30943)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad tertium.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30944)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus sit productus a Deo ab aeterno. Deus enim est causa Angeli per suum esse, non enim agit per aliquid additum suae essentiae. Sed esse eius est aeternum. Ergo ab aeterno Angelos produxit.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30945)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod quandoque est et quandoque non est, subiacet tempori. Sed Angelus est supra tempus, ut dicitur in libro de causis. Ergo Angelus non quandoque est, et quandoque non est, sed semper.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30946)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus probat incorruptibilitatem animae per hoc, quod per intellectum est capax veritatis. Sed sicut veritas est incorruptibilis, ita est aeterna. Ergo natura intellectualis et animae et Angeli, non solum est incorruptibilis, sed etiam aeterna.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30947)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Prov. VIII, ex persona sapientiae genitae, *dominus possedit me ab initio viarum suarum, antequam quidquam faceret a principio*. Sed Angeli sunt facti a Deo, ut ostensum est. Ergo Angeli aliquando non fuerunt.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30948)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod solus Deus, pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, est ab aeterno. Hoc enim fides Catholica indubitanter tenet; et omne contrarium est sicut haereticum refutandum. Sic enim Deus creaturas produxit, quod eas ex nihilo fecit, idest postquam nihil fuerat.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30949)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod esse Dei est ipsum eius velle. Per hoc ergo quod Deus produxit Angelos et alias creaturas per suum esse, non excluditur quin eas produxerit per suam voluntatem. Voluntas autem Dei non de necessitate se habet ad productionem creaturarum, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo produxit et quae voluit, et quando voluit.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30950)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angelus est supra tempus quod est numerus motus caeli, quia est supra omnem motum corporalis naturae. Non tamen est supra tempus quod est numerus successionis esse eius post non esse, et etiam quod est numerus successionis quae est in operationibus eius. Unde Augustinus dicit, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Deus movet creaturam spiritualem per tempus*.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30951)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Angeli et animae intellectivae, ex hoc ipso quod habent naturam per quam sunt capaces veritatis, sunt incorruptibiles. Sed hanc naturam non habuerunt ab aeterno; sed data fuit eis a Deo quando ipse voluit. Unde non sequitur quod Angeli sint ab aeterno.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30952)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli fuerint creati ante mundum corporeum. Dicit enim Hieronymus, super epistolam ad Titum, *sex millia nondum nostri temporis complentur annorum; et quanta tempora, quantasque saeculorum origines fuisse arbitrandum est, in quibus Angeli, throni, dominationes, ceterique ordines Deo servierunt?* Damascenus etiam dicit, in II libro, *quidam dicunt quod ante omnem creationem geniti sunt Angeli; ut theologus dicit Gregorius, primum quidem excogitavit angelicas virtutes et caelestes, et excogitatio opus eius fuit*.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30953)\n\nPraeterea, angelica natura est media inter naturam divinam et naturam corpoream. Sed natura divina est ab aeterno, natura autem corporea ex tempore. Ergo natura angelica facta est ante creationem temporis, et post aeternitatem.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30954)\n\nPraeterea, plus distat natura angelica a natura corporali, quam una natura corporalis ab alia. Sed una natura corporalis fuit facta ante aliam, unde et sex dies productionis rerum in principio Genesis describuntur. Ergo multo magis natura angelica facta est ante omnem naturam corporalem.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30955)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Non autem hoc esset verum, si aliquid creasset antea. Ergo Angeli non sunt ante naturam corpoream creati.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30956)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc invenitur duplex sanctorum doctorum sententia, illa tamen probabilior videtur, quod Angeli simul cum creatura corporea sunt creati. Angeli enim sunt quaedam pars universi, non enim constituunt per se unum universum, sed tam ipsi quam creatura corporea in constitutionem unius universi conveniunt. Quod apparet ex ordine unius creaturae ad aliam, ordo enim rerum ad invicem est bonum universi. Nulla autem pars perfecta est a suo toto separata. Non est igitur probabile ut Deus, cuius perfecta sunt opera, ut dicitur Deut. XXXII, creaturam angelicam seorsum ante alias creaturas creaverit. Quamvis contrarium non sit reputandum erroneum; praecipue propter sententiam Gregorii Nazianzeni, cuius tanta est in doctrina Christiana auctoritas, ut nullus unquam eius dictis calumniam inferre praesumpserit, sicut nec Athanasii documentis, ut Hieronymus dicit.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30957)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Hieronymus loquitur secundum sententiam doctorum Graecorum, qui omnes hoc concorditer sentiunt, quod Angeli sunt ante mundum corporeum creati.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30958)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus non est aliqua pars universi, sed est supra totum universum, praehabens in se eminentiori modo totam universi perfectionem. Angelus autem est pars universi. Unde non est eadem ratio.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30959)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod creaturae corporeae omnes sunt unum in materia, sed Angeli non conveniunt in materia cum creatura corporea. Unde, creata materia corporalis creaturae, omnia quodammodo sunt creata, non autem, creatis Angelis, esset ipsum universum creatum. Si vero contrarium teneatur, quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram* exponendum est, in principio, idest in filio, vel in principio temporis, non autem in principio, idest ante quod nihil, nisi dicatur, ante quod nihil in genere corporalium creaturarum.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30960)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint creati in caelo Empyreo. Angeli enim sunt substantiae incorporeae. Sed substantia incorporea non dependet a corpore secundum suum esse, et per consequens neque secundum suum fieri. Ergo Angeli non sunt creati in loco corporeo.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30961)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, III super Gen. ad Litt., quod Angeli fuerunt creati in superiori parte aeris. Non ergo in caelo Empyreo.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30962)\n\nPraeterea, caelum Empyreum dicitur esse caelum supremum. Si igitur Angeli creati fuissent in caelo Empyreo, non convenisset eis in superius caelum ascendere. Quod est contra id quod ex persona Angeli peccantis dicitur Isaiae XIV, *ascendam in caelum*.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30963)\n\nSed contra est quod Strabus dicit, super illud, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, caelum non visibile firmamentum hic appellat, sed Empyreum, idest igneum vel intellectuale, quod non ab ardore, sed a splendore dicitur, quod statim factum, Angelis est repletum*.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30964)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, ex creaturis corporalibus et spiritualibus unum universum constituitur. Unde sic creatae sunt spirituales creaturae, quod ad creaturam corporalem aliquem ordinem habent, et toti creaturae corporali praesident. Unde conveniens fuit quod Angeli in supremo corpore crearentur, tanquam toti naturae corporeae praesidentes; sive id dicatur caelum Empyreum, sive qualitercumque nominetur. Unde Isidorus dicit quod supremum caelum est caelum Angelorum, super illud Deut. X, *domini Dei tui est caelum, et caelum caeli*.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30965)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Angeli non sunt creati in loco corporeo, quasi dependentes a corpore secundum suum esse vel secundum suum fieri, potuisset enim Deus Angelos ante totam creaturam corporalem creasse, ut multi sancti doctores tenent. Sed facti sunt in loco corporeo, ad ostendendum ordinem eorum ad naturam corpoream, et quod sua virtute corpora contingunt.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30966)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Augustinus forte per supremam partem aeris intelligit supremam partem caeli, cum quo aer quandam convenientiam habet propter suam subtilitatem et diaphaneitatem. Vel loquitur non de omnibus Angelis, sed de illis qui peccaverunt, qui secundum quosdam fuerunt de inferioribus ordinibus. Nihil autem prohibet dicere quod superiores Angeli, habentes virtutem elevatam et universalem supra omnia corpora, sint in supremo creaturae corporeae creati; alii vero, habentes virtutes magis particulares, sint creati in inferioribus corporibus.\n\n[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30967)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod loquitur ibi non de caelo aliquo corporeo, sed de caelo sanctae Trinitatis, in quod Angelus peccans ascendere voluit, dum voluit aliquo modo Deo aequiparari, ut infra patebit.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0002","text":"[I q. 61 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0003","text":"Post ea quae praemissa sunt de natura Angelorum, et cognitione et voluntate eorum, restat considerandum de eorum creatione, sive universaliter de eorum exordio. Et haec consideratio est tripartita. Nam primo considerabimus quomodo producti sunt in esse naturae; secundo, quomodo perfecti sunt in gratia vel gloria; tertio, quomodo aliqui ex eis facti sunt mali. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Angelus habeat causam sui esse. Secundo, utrum Angelus sit ab aeterno. Tertio, utrum Angelus sit creatus ante corporalem creaturam. Quarto, utrum Angeli fuerint creati in caelo Empyreo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0005","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non habeant causam sui esse. De his enim quae sunt a Deo creata, agitur Genesis I. Sed nulla mentio fit ibi de Angelis. Ergo Angeli non sunt creati a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0007","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0008","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in VIII Metaphys., quod si aliqua substantia sit forma sine materia, *statim per seipsam est ens et unum, et non habet causam quae faciat eam ens et unum*. Sed Angeli sunt formae immateriales, ut supra ostensum est. Ergo non habent causam sui esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0009","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0010","text":"Praeterea, omne quod fit ab aliquo agente, per hoc quod fit, accipit formam ab eo. Sed Angeli, cum sint formae, non accipiunt formam ab aliquo agente. Ergo Angeli non habent causam agentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0011","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CXLVIII, *laudate eum, omnes Angeli eius*. Et postea subdit, *quoniam ipse dixit, et facta sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0013","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere et Angelos, et omne id quod praeter Deum est, a Deo factum esse. Solus enim Deus est suum esse, in omnibus autem aliis differt essentia rei et esse eius, ut ex superioribus patet. Et ex hoc manifestum est quod solus Deus est ens per suam essentiam, omnia vero alia sunt entia per participationem. Omne autem quod est per participationem causatur ab eo quod est per essentiam, sicut omne ignitum causatur ab igne. Unde necesse est Angelos a Deo creatos esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0015","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus dicit, in XI de Civ. Dei, quod Angeli non sunt praetermissi in illa prima rerum creatione, sed significantur nomine caeli, aut etiam lucis. Ideo autem vel praetermissi sunt, vel nominibus rerum corporalium significati, quia Moyses rudi populo loquebatur, qui nondum capere poterat incorpoream naturam; et si eis fuisset expressum aliquas res esse super omnem naturam corpoream, fuisset eis occasio idololatriae, ad quam proni erant, et a qua Moyses eos praecipue revocare intendebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0017","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod substantiae quae sunt formae subsistentes, non habent causam aliquam formalem sui esse et suae unitatis, nec causam agentem per transmutationem materiae de potentia in actum, sed habent causam producentem totam substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0019","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0020","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad tertium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0022","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus sit productus a Deo ab aeterno. Deus enim est causa Angeli per suum esse, non enim agit per aliquid additum suae essentiae. Sed esse eius est aeternum. Ergo ab aeterno Angelos produxit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0024","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0025","text":"Praeterea, omne quod quandoque est et quandoque non est, subiacet tempori. Sed Angelus est supra tempus, ut dicitur in libro de causis. Ergo Angelus non quandoque est, et quandoque non est, sed semper.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0026","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0027","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus probat incorruptibilitatem animae per hoc, quod per intellectum est capax veritatis. Sed sicut veritas est incorruptibilis, ita est aeterna. Ergo natura intellectualis et animae et Angeli, non solum est incorruptibilis, sed etiam aeterna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0028","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0029","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Prov. VIII, ex persona sapientiae genitae, *dominus possedit me ab initio viarum suarum, antequam quidquam faceret a principio*. Sed Angeli sunt facti a Deo, ut ostensum est. Ergo Angeli aliquando non fuerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0030","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0031","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod solus Deus, pater et filius et spiritus sanctus, est ab aeterno. Hoc enim fides Catholica indubitanter tenet; et omne contrarium est sicut haereticum refutandum. Sic enim Deus creaturas produxit, quod eas ex nihilo fecit, idest postquam nihil fuerat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0032","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0033","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod esse Dei est ipsum eius velle. Per hoc ergo quod Deus produxit Angelos et alias creaturas per suum esse, non excluditur quin eas produxerit per suam voluntatem. Voluntas autem Dei non de necessitate se habet ad productionem creaturarum, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo produxit et quae voluit, et quando voluit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0034","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0035","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angelus est supra tempus quod est numerus motus caeli, quia est supra omnem motum corporalis naturae. Non tamen est supra tempus quod est numerus successionis esse eius post non esse, et etiam quod est numerus successionis quae est in operationibus eius. Unde Augustinus dicit, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Deus movet creaturam spiritualem per tempus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0036","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0037","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Angeli et animae intellectivae, ex hoc ipso quod habent naturam per quam sunt capaces veritatis, sunt incorruptibiles. Sed hanc naturam non habuerunt ab aeterno; sed data fuit eis a Deo quando ipse voluit. Unde non sequitur quod Angeli sint ab aeterno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0038","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0039","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0040","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli fuerint creati ante mundum corporeum. Dicit enim Hieronymus, super epistolam ad Titum, *sex millia nondum nostri temporis complentur annorum; et quanta tempora, quantasque saeculorum origines fuisse arbitrandum est, in quibus Angeli, throni, dominationes, ceterique ordines Deo servierunt?* Damascenus etiam dicit, in II libro, *quidam dicunt quod ante omnem creationem geniti sunt Angeli; ut theologus dicit Gregorius, primum quidem excogitavit angelicas virtutes et caelestes, et excogitatio opus eius fuit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0041","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0042","text":"Praeterea, angelica natura est media inter naturam divinam et naturam corpoream. Sed natura divina est ab aeterno, natura autem corporea ex tempore. Ergo natura angelica facta est ante creationem temporis, et post aeternitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0043","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0044","text":"Praeterea, plus distat natura angelica a natura corporali, quam una natura corporalis ab alia. Sed una natura corporalis fuit facta ante aliam, unde et sex dies productionis rerum in principio Genesis describuntur. Ergo multo magis natura angelica facta est ante omnem naturam corporalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0045","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0046","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Non autem hoc esset verum, si aliquid creasset antea. Ergo Angeli non sunt ante naturam corpoream creati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0047","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0048","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc invenitur duplex sanctorum doctorum sententia, illa tamen probabilior videtur, quod Angeli simul cum creatura corporea sunt creati. Angeli enim sunt quaedam pars universi, non enim constituunt per se unum universum, sed tam ipsi quam creatura corporea in constitutionem unius universi conveniunt. Quod apparet ex ordine unius creaturae ad aliam, ordo enim rerum ad invicem est bonum universi. Nulla autem pars perfecta est a suo toto separata. Non est igitur probabile ut Deus, cuius perfecta sunt opera, ut dicitur Deut. XXXII, creaturam angelicam seorsum ante alias creaturas creaverit. Quamvis contrarium non sit reputandum erroneum; praecipue propter sententiam Gregorii Nazianzeni, cuius tanta est in doctrina Christiana auctoritas, ut nullus unquam eius dictis calumniam inferre praesumpserit, sicut nec Athanasii documentis, ut Hieronymus dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0049","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0050","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Hieronymus loquitur secundum sententiam doctorum Graecorum, qui omnes hoc concorditer sentiunt, quod Angeli sunt ante mundum corporeum creati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0051","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0052","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus non est aliqua pars universi, sed est supra totum universum, praehabens in se eminentiori modo totam universi perfectionem. Angelus autem est pars universi. Unde non est eadem ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0053","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0054","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod creaturae corporeae omnes sunt unum in materia, sed Angeli non conveniunt in materia cum creatura corporea. Unde, creata materia corporalis creaturae, omnia quodammodo sunt creata, non autem, creatis Angelis, esset ipsum universum creatum. Si vero contrarium teneatur, quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram* exponendum est, in principio, idest in filio, vel in principio temporis, non autem in principio, idest ante quod nihil, nisi dicatur, ante quod nihil in genere corporalium creaturarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0055","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0056","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0057","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint creati in caelo Empyreo. Angeli enim sunt substantiae incorporeae. Sed substantia incorporea non dependet a corpore secundum suum esse, et per consequens neque secundum suum fieri. Ergo Angeli non sunt creati in loco corporeo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0058","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0059","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, III super Gen. ad Litt., quod Angeli fuerunt creati in superiori parte aeris. Non ergo in caelo Empyreo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0060","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0061","text":"Praeterea, caelum Empyreum dicitur esse caelum supremum. Si igitur Angeli creati fuissent in caelo Empyreo, non convenisset eis in superius caelum ascendere. Quod est contra id quod ex persona Angeli peccantis dicitur Isaiae XIV, *ascendam in caelum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0062","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0063","text":"Sed contra est quod Strabus dicit, super illud, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, caelum non visibile firmamentum hic appellat, sed Empyreum, idest igneum vel intellectuale, quod non ab ardore, sed a splendore dicitur, quod statim factum, Angelis est repletum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0064","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0065","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, ex creaturis corporalibus et spiritualibus unum universum constituitur. Unde sic creatae sunt spirituales creaturae, quod ad creaturam corporalem aliquem ordinem habent, et toti creaturae corporali praesident. Unde conveniens fuit quod Angeli in supremo corpore crearentur, tanquam toti naturae corporeae praesidentes; sive id dicatur caelum Empyreum, sive qualitercumque nominetur. Unde Isidorus dicit quod supremum caelum est caelum Angelorum, super illud Deut. X, *domini Dei tui est caelum, et caelum caeli*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0066","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0067","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Angeli non sunt creati in loco corporeo, quasi dependentes a corpore secundum suum esse vel secundum suum fieri, potuisset enim Deus Angelos ante totam creaturam corporalem creasse, ut multi sancti doctores tenent. Sed facti sunt in loco corporeo, ad ostendendum ordinem eorum ad naturam corpoream, et quod sua virtute corpora contingunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0068","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0069","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Augustinus forte per supremam partem aeris intelligit supremam partem caeli, cum quo aer quandam convenientiam habet propter suam subtilitatem et diaphaneitatem. Vel loquitur non de omnibus Angelis, sed de illis qui peccaverunt, qui secundum quosdam fuerunt de inferioribus ordinibus. Nihil autem prohibet dicere quod superiores Angeli, habentes virtutem elevatam et universalem supra omnia corpora, sint in supremo creaturae corporeae creati; alii vero, habentes virtutes magis particulares, sint creati in inferioribus corporibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0070","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0071","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod loquitur ibi non de caelo aliquo corporeo, sed de caelo sanctae Trinitatis, in quod Angelus peccans ascendere voluit, dum voluit aliquo modo Deo aequiparari, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4fd5ce1da33a84f5b97bfdfd4d194df48828ba940d50efa01eee15e82a8791a8","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T144604977177Z_2beb5163","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 61","source_sha256":"4fd5ce1da33a84f5b97bfdfd4d194df48828ba940d50efa01eee15e82a8791a8","sha256":"97d96af553bb5b787995c18c4146ffb58efbd745b28494dad15b4ed5f8bfb352","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:46:04.977216+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0002","text":"[I q. 61 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0003","text":"After those matters that have been set forth concerning the nature of the angels, and their knowledge and will, it remains to consider their creation, or universally, their origin. And this consideration is threefold. For first we shall consider how they were produced in natural being; second, how they were perfected in grace or glory; third, how some among them became evil. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether an angel has a cause of its being. Second, whether an angel is from eternity. Third, whether an angel was created before corporeal creatures. Fourth, whether the angels were created in the Empyrean heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0005","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that angels do not have a cause of their being. For Genesis I treats of those things that were created by God. But no mention is made there of angels. Therefore angels were not created by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0007","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0008","text":"Further, the Philosopher says, in Metaphysics VIII, that if some substance is a form without matter, *then immediately through itself it is a being and one, and does not have a cause that makes it a being and one*. But angels are immaterial forms, as was shown above. Therefore they do not have a cause of their being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0009","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0010","text":"Further, everything that is made by some agent, by the very fact that it is made, receives form from it. But angels, since they are forms, do not receive form from any agent. Therefore angels do not have an efficient cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0011","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm CXLVIII, *Praise him, all his angels*. And afterward it adds, *because he spoke, and they were made*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0013","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that both the angels and everything that exists besides God were made by God. For God alone is his own being; but in all other things the essence of the thing and its being differ, as is clear from what has preceded. And from this it is manifest that God alone is being through his essence, whereas all other things are beings by participation. But everything that is by participation is caused by that which is by essence, just as everything ignited is caused by fire. Hence it is necessary that the angels were created by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0015","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine says, in XI of *The City of God*, that angels were not omitted in that first creation of things, but are signified by the name of heaven, or even of light. But they were either omitted or signified by the names of corporeal things because Moses was speaking to a rude people, who could not yet grasp incorporeal nature; and if it had been expressly stated to them that certain things existed above every corporeal nature, this would have been for them an occasion of idolatry, toward which they were inclined and from which Moses chiefly intended to call them back.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0017","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that substances which are subsistent forms do not have any formal cause of their being and their unity, nor an efficient cause through the transmutation of matter from potency into act; but they do have a cause producing the whole substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0019","text":"[I q. 61 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0020","text":"And through this the solution to the third is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0022","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that an angel was produced by God from eternity. For God is the cause of an angel through his being, since he does not act through anything added to his essence. But his being is eternal. Therefore he produced angels from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0024","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0025","text":"Moreover, everything which at one time is and at another time is not is subject to time. But an angel is above time, as is said in the Book of Causes. Therefore, an angel does not at one time exist and at another time not exist, but always.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0026","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0027","text":"Moreover, Augustine proves the incorruptibility of the soul from the fact that through intellect it is capable of truth. But just as truth is incorruptible, so too it is eternal. Therefore, the intellectual nature both of the soul and of the angel is not only incorruptible but also eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0028","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0029","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Proverbs VIII, in the person of begotten wisdom, *the Lord possessed me from the beginning of his ways, before he made anything from the beginning*. But angels were made by God, as has been shown. Therefore, angels at one time were not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0030","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0031","text":"I answer that it must be said that God alone—the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit—is from eternity. For this the Catholic faith holds without doubt, and every contrary position must be rejected as heretical. For God so produced creatures that he made them from nothing, that is, after nothing had been.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0032","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0033","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God's being is his very willing. Therefore, from the fact that God produced angels and other creatures through his being, it is not excluded that he produced them through his will. But the will of God does not stand in a necessary relation to the production of creatures, as was said above. And therefore he produced both what he willed and when he willed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0034","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0035","text":"To the second, it must be said that an angel is above time which is the number of the motion of the heavens, because it is above every motion of corporeal nature. Yet it is not above time which is the number of the succession of its being after not-being, and also which is the number of the succession that is in its operations. Hence Augustine says, in Book VIII on the Literal Meaning of Genesis, that *God moves the spiritual creature through time*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0036","text":"[I q. 61 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0037","text":"To the third, it must be said that angels and intellectual souls, from the very fact that they have a nature through which they are capable of truth, are incorruptible. But they did not have this nature from eternity; rather, it was given to them by God when he himself willed. Hence it does not follow that angels are from eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0038","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0039","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0040","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that angels were created before the corporeal world. For Jerome says, on the Epistle to Titus, *not yet six thousand years of our time have been completed; and what times, and what beginnings of ages, must be thought to have existed, in which angels, thrones, dominions, and the other orders served God?* Damascene likewise says, in Book II, *some say that angels were begotten before every creation; as Gregory the Theologian says, first indeed he conceived the angelic and heavenly powers, and his conception was his work*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0041","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0042","text":"Moreover, the angelic nature is intermediate between the divine nature and corporeal nature. But the divine nature is from eternity, whereas corporeal nature is from time. Therefore, angelic nature was made before the creation of time and after eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0043","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0044","text":"Moreover, angelic nature is more distant from corporeal nature than one corporeal nature is from another. But one corporeal nature was made before another; hence the six days of the production of things are described at the beginning of Genesis. Therefore, much more was angelic nature made before every corporeal nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0045","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0046","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Genesis I, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*. But this would not be true if he had created anything before. Therefore, angels were not created before corporeal nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0047","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0048","text":"I answer that it must be said that a twofold opinion of the holy doctors is found concerning this; nevertheless, that opinion seems more probable according to which angels were created at the same time as corporeal creatures. For angels are a certain part of the universe: they do not by themselves constitute one universe, but both they and corporeal creatures come together in the constitution of one universe. This is apparent from the order of one creature to another, for the order of things among themselves is the good of the universe. But no part is perfect when separated from its whole. Therefore, it is not probable that God, whose works are perfect, as is said in Deuteronomy XXXII, created the angelic creature separately before other creatures. Although the contrary should not be considered erroneous, especially on account of the opinion of Gregory Nazianzen, whose authority in Christian doctrine is so great that no one has ever dared to cast reproach upon his sayings, just as neither upon the teachings of Athanasius, as Jerome says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0049","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0050","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that Jerome speaks according to the opinion of the Greek doctors, who all agree in this: that the Angels were created before the corporeal world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0051","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0052","text":"To the second it must be said that God is not any part of the universe, but is above the whole universe, possessing beforehand within Himself, in a more eminent mode, the whole perfection of the universe. But an Angel is a part of the universe. Hence the reasoning is not the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0053","text":"[I q. 61 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0054","text":"To the third it must be said that all corporeal creatures are one in matter, but the Angels do not share matter with corporeal creatures. Hence, when the matter of corporeal creatures was created, all things were in some manner created; but, when Angels were created, the universe itself would not thereby have been created. But if the contrary be maintained, then what is said in Gen. I, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*, must be expounded as: in the beginning, that is, in the Son, or in the beginning of time; but not in the beginning, that is, before which there was nothing—unless it be said, before which there was nothing in the genus of corporeal creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0055","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0056","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0057","text":"The fourth point is approached thus. It seems that the Angels were not created in the Empyrean heaven. For Angels are incorporeal substances. But an incorporeal substance does not depend on a body with respect to its being, and consequently neither with respect to its coming-to-be. Therefore Angels are not created in a corporeal place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0058","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0059","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, in III *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that the Angels were created in the upper region of the air. Therefore, not in the Empyrean heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0060","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0061","text":"Moreover, the Empyrean heaven is said to be the highest heaven. Therefore, if the Angels had been created in the Empyrean heaven, it would not have been fitting for them to ascend into a higher heaven. This is contrary to what is said in the person of the sinning Angel in Isaiah XIV: *I shall ascend into heaven*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0062","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0063","text":"But on the contrary, Strabus says, on the words, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*: “Here he calls heaven not the visible firmament, but the Empyrean, that is, fiery or intellectual, which is so called not from heat but from brightness, and which, as soon as it was made, was filled with Angels.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0064","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0065","text":"I answer that, as has been said, one universe is constituted from corporeal and spiritual creatures. Hence spiritual creatures were so created that they have some order to corporeal creatures, and preside over the whole corporeal creation. Hence it was fitting that the Angels should be created in the highest body, as presiding over the whole corporeal nature; whether this be called the Empyrean heaven or be named in whatever manner. Hence Isidore says that the highest heaven is the heaven of the Angels, on the words of Deut. X: *the heaven, and the heaven of heaven, belongs to the Lord thy God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0066","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0067","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the Angels were not created in a corporeal place as though depending on a body with respect to their being or their coming-to-be; for God could have created the Angels before the whole corporeal creation, as many holy doctors maintain. But they were made in a corporeal place in order to show their order to corporeal nature, and that by their power they touch bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0068","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0069","text":"To the second it must be said that Augustine perhaps understands by the highest region of the air the highest region of heaven, with which air has a certain affinity by reason of its subtlety and diaphaneity. Or he speaks not of all the Angels, but of those who sinned, who according to some were from the lower orders. But nothing prevents one from saying that the higher Angels, having an elevated and universal power over all bodies, were created in the highest body of corporeal creation; whereas others, having more particular powers, were created in lower bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0070","text":"[I q. 61 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0071","text":"To the third it must be said that he speaks there not of any corporeal heaven, but of the heaven of the Holy Trinity, into which the sinning Angel wished to ascend, while wishing in some manner to make himself equal to God, as will be clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_53:P0071"]}],"source_digest":"db767b62b623511878a3a34c5fcc0cb8a29c946aaedd5bf71cb2ba7299324806","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 61"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 61","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54","title":"First Part · Question 62","order":63,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_54","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 62 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30968)\n\nConsequenter investigandum est quomodo Angeli facti sunt in esse gratiae vel gloriae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur novem. Primo, utrum Angeli fuerint in sua creatione beati. Secundo, utrum indiguerint gratia ad hoc quod ad Deum converterentur. Tertio, utrum fuerint creati in gratia. Quarto, utrum suam beatitudinem meruerint. Quinto, utrum statim post meritum beatitudinem adepti fuerint. Sexto, utrum gratiam et gloriam secundum capacitatem suorum naturalium receperint. Septimo, utrum post consecutionem gloriae remanserit in eis dilectio et cognitio naturalis. Octavo, utrum postmodum potuerint peccare. Nono, utrum post adeptionem gloriae potuerint proficere.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30969)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli fuerint creati beati. Dicitur enim in libro de ecclesiasticis dogmatibus, quod *Angeli qui in illa in qua creati sunt beatitudine perseverant, non natura possident bonum quod habent*. Sunt ergo Angeli creati in beatitudine.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30970)\n\nPraeterea, natura angelica est nobilior quam creatura corporalis. Sed creatura corporalis statim in principio suae creationis fuit creata formata et perfecta; nec informitas praecessit in ea formationem tempore, sed natura tantum, ut Augustinus dicit, I super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo nec naturam angelicam creavit Deus informem et imperfectam. Sed eius formatio et perfectio est per beatitudinem, secundum quod fruitur Deo. Ergo fuit creata beata.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30971)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Augustinum, super Gen. ad Litt., ea quae leguntur facta in operibus sex dierum, simul facta fuerunt, et sic oportet quod statim a principio creationis rerum fuerint omnes illi sex dies. Sed in illis sex diebus, secundum eius expositionem, mane fuit cognitio angelica secundum quam cognoverunt verbum et res in verbo. Ergo statim a principio creationis cognoverunt verbum et res in verbo. Sed Angeli beati sunt per hoc quod verbum vident. Ergo statim a principio suae creationis Angeli fuerunt beati.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30972)\n\nSed contra, de ratione beatitudinis est stabilitas sive confirmatio in bono. Sed Angeli non statim ut creati sunt, fuerunt confirmati in bono, quod casus quorundam ostendit. Non ergo Angeli in sua creatione fuerunt beati.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30973)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nomine beatitudinis intelligitur ultima perfectio rationalis seu intellectualis naturae, et inde est quod naturaliter desideratur, quia unumquodque naturaliter desiderat suam ultimam perfectionem. Ultima autem perfectio rationalis seu intellectualis naturae est duplex. Una quidem, quam potest assequi virtute suae naturae, et haec quodammodo beatitudo vel felicitas dicitur. Unde et Aristoteles perfectissimam hominis contemplationem, qua optimum intelligibile, quod est Deus, contemplari potest in hac vita, dicit esse ultimam hominis felicitatem. Sed super hanc felicitatem est alia felicitas, quam in futuro expectamus, qua videbimus Deum sicuti est. Quod quidem est supra cuiuslibet intellectus creati naturam, ut supra ostensum est. Sic igitur dicendum est quod, quantum ad primam beatitudinem, quam Angelus assequi virtute suae naturae potuit, fuit creatus beatus. Quia perfectionem huiusmodi Angelus non acquirit per aliquem motum discursivum, sicut homo, sed statim ei adest propter suae naturae dignitatem, ut supra dictum est. Sed ultimam beatitudinem, quae facultatem naturae excedit, Angeli non statim in principio suae creationis habuerunt, quia haec beatitudo non est aliquid naturae, sed naturae finis; et ideo non statim eam a principio debuerunt habere.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30974)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod beatitudo ibi accipitur pro illa perfectione naturali quam Angelus habuit in statu innocentiae.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30975)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod creatura corporalis statim in principio suae creationis habere non potuit perfectionem ad quam per suam operationem perducitur, unde, secundum Augustinum, germinatio plantarum ex terra non statim fuit in primis operibus, in quibus virtus sola germinativa plantarum data est terrae. Et similiter creatura angelica in principio suae creationis habuit perfectionem suae naturae; non autem perfectionem ad quam per suam operationem pervenire debebat.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30976)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Angelus duplicem habet verbi cognitionem, unam naturalem, et aliam gloriae, naturalem quidem, qua cognoscit verbum per eius similitudinem in sua natura relucentem; cognitionem vero gloriae, qua cognoscit verbum per suam essentiam. Et utraque cognoscit Angelus res in verbo, sed naturali quidem cognitione imperfecte, cognitione vero gloriae perfecte. Prima ergo cognitio rerum in verbo affuit Angelo a principio suae creationis, secunda vero non, sed quando facti sunt beati per conversionem ad bonum. Et haec proprie dicitur cognitio matutina.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30977)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non indiguerit gratia ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum. Ad ea enim quae naturaliter possumus, gratia non indigemus. Sed naturaliter Angelus convertitur in Deum, quia naturaliter Deum diligit, ut ex supra dictis patet. Ergo Angelus non indiguit gratia ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30978)\n\nPraeterea, ad ea tantum videmur indigere auxilio, quae sunt difficilia. Sed converti ad Deum non erat difficile Angelo; cum nihil esset in eo quod huic conversioni repugnaret. Ergo Angelus non indiguit auxilio gratiae ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30979)\n\nPraeterea, converti ad Deum est se ad gratiam praeparare, unde Zach. I, dicitur, *convertimini ad me, et ego convertar ad vos*. Sed nos non indigemus gratia ad hoc quod nos ad gratiam praeparemus, quia sic esset abire in infinitum. Ergo non indiguit gratia Angelus ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30980)\n\nSed contra, per conversionem ad Deum Angelus pervenit ad beatitudinem. Si igitur non indiguisset gratia ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum, sequeretur quod non indigeret gratia ad habendam vitam aeternam. Quod est contra illud apostoli, Rom. VI, *gratia Dei vita aeterna*.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30981)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angeli indiguerunt gratia ad hoc quod converterentur in Deum, prout est obiectum beatitudinis. Sicut enim superius dictum est, naturalis motus voluntatis est principium omnium eorum quae volumus. Naturalis autem inclinatio voluntatis est ad id quod est conveniens secundum naturam. Et ideo, si aliquid sit supra naturam, voluntas in id ferri non potest, nisi ab aliquo alio supernaturali principio adiuta. Sicut patet quod ignis habet naturalem inclinationem ad calefaciendum, et ad generandum ignem, sed generare carnem est supra naturalem virtutem ignis, unde ignis ad hoc nullam inclinationem habet, nisi secundum quod movetur ut instrumentum ab anima nutritiva. Ostensum est autem supra, cum de Dei cognitione ageretur, quod videre Deum per essentiam, in quo ultima beatitudo rationalis creaturae consistit, est supra naturam cuiuslibet intellectus creati. Unde nulla creatura rationalis potest habere motum voluntatis ordinatum ad illam beatitudinem, nisi mota a supernaturali agente. Et hoc dicimus auxilium gratiae. Et ideo dicendum est quod Angelus in illam beatitudinem voluntate converti non potuit, nisi per auxilium gratiae.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30982)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Angelus naturaliter diligit Deum, inquantum est principium naturalis esse. Hic autem loquimur de conversione ad Deum, inquantum est beatificans per suae essentiae visionem.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30983)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod difficile est quod transcendit potentiam. Sed hoc contingit esse dupliciter. Uno modo, quia transcendit potentiam secundum suum naturalem ordinem. Et tunc, si ad hoc possit pervenire aliquo auxilio, dicitur difficile; si autem nullo modo, dicitur impossibile, sicut impossibile est hominem volare. Alio modo transcendit aliquid potentiam, non secundum ordinem naturalem potentiae, sed propter aliquod impedimentum potentiae adiunctum. Sicut ascendere non est contra naturalem ordinem potentiae animae motivae, quia anima, quantum est de se, nata est movere in quamlibet partem, sed impeditur ab hoc propter corporis gravitatem; unde difficile est homini ascendere. Converti autem ad beatitudinem ultimam, homini quidem est difficile et quia est supra naturam, et quia habet impedimentum ex corruptione corporis et infectione peccati. Sed Angelo est difficile propter hoc solum quod est supernaturale.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30984)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quilibet motus voluntatis in Deum, potest dici conversio in ipsum. Et ideo triplex est conversio in Deum. Una quidem per dilectionem perfectam, quae est creaturae iam Deo fruentis. Et ad hanc conversionem requiritur gratia consummata. Alia conversio est, quae est meritum beatitudinis. Et ad hanc requiritur habitualis gratia, quae est merendi principium. Tertia conversio est, per quam aliquis praeparat se ad gratiam habendam. Et ad hanc non exigitur aliqua habitualis gratia, sed operatio Dei ad se animam convertentis, secundum illud Thren. ult., *converte nos, domine, ad te, et convertemur*. Unde patet quod non est procedere in infinitum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30985)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint creati in gratia. Dicit enim Augustinus, II super Gen. ad Litt., quod angelica natura primo erat informiter creata, et caelum dicta, postmodum vero formata est, et lux appellata. Sed haec formatio est per gratiam. Ergo non sunt creati in gratia.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthom